\iffalse

\begin{book}
  \emph{A Benchmark approach to quantitative finance}\\
  E. Platen and D. Heath\\
  (Springer, 2006)
\end{book}

The first part of the book covers stochastic processes,
with a few exercises.
The second part discusses the ``benchmark approach'':
applications of 
the \d{growth-optimal portfolio} (GOP),
\ie, of the Kelly principle.

Options can be priced using the real-world probability, 
as $E[ \text{Payoff} / \text{GOP} ]$
(this is the GOP on two assets: 
the underlyer and the risk-free asset), \ie,
the GOP is a deflator (stochastic discount factor) -- 
things are actually more complicated: 
$\text{Payoff} / \text{GOP}$ is only a local martingale.
In case the payoff is independent of the GOP, 
this reduces to the actuary's present value pricing.
More generally, if $S$ is an asset,
$B$ a portfolio, 
and if there exists a measure $Q$ under which 
$S/B$ is a martingale, 
then $B$ is called a \d{numeraire} for $S$:
the risk-free asset is a numeraire (wrt the risk-free measure),
and so is the GOP (wrt the real-world measure).

The risk-premium of an asset of price $S$ is 
\begin{align*}
  p(t) 
  &= \lim_{h \to 0^+} 
     \dfrac1h 
     E_t \left[ \dfrac{S(t+h) - S(t)}{h} \right] 
     - r_t \\
  &= \dfrac{d}{dt} [ \ln(S), \ln(\text{GOP}) ]_t
\end{align*}
and the beta of a portfolio is 
the ratio of its risk premium with that of the GOP.
The ICAPM model links 
expected returns (between $t$ and $t+h$), 
risk premium and beta.

