%% Multivariate extreme values and causality

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal discovery in heavy-tailed models}\\
  N. Gnecco et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Extreme observations can be viewed as natural experiments --
they facilitate causal analysis.
The (positive) \emph{causal tail coefficient} between random variables $X_1$ and $X_2$ is
\[ \Gamma_{12} = \lim_{ u \to 1^- } \E\bigl[ F_2(X_2) \mid F_1(X_2) < u \bigr]. \]
It is close to 1 id $X_1$ causes $X_2$ (positively).

It can be estimated as
\begin{align*}
  \hat F_2(x) &= \dfrac1n \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf 1_{ X_{ i2 \leq x } } \\
  \hat\Gamma_{12} &= \dfrac 1k \sum_{ i=1 } ^n \hat F_2( X_{i2} ) \mathbf 1 _{ X_{i1} > X_{n-k,1} }
\end{align*}

Extremal ancestor search (EASE, R implementation in \texttt{causalXtreme})
recovers a causal order on the variables by identifying the root as the node minimizing the score
\[ M_i = \max_{ j \neq i } \Gamma_{ji} . \]

To account for both positive and negative relations, use
\begin{align*}
  \psi_{12} &= \lim_{ u \to 1^- } \E\bigl[
              \sigma\bigl( F_2(X_2) \bigr) \mid \sigma\bigl( F_1(X_1)\bigr) > u 
              \bigr] \\
  \sigma(x) &= \va{ 2x - 1 } \\
  \psi_{12} &= \dfrac12 ( \Gamma_{1,2} + \Gamma_{-1,2} )
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Decomposition of dependence\\ for high-dimensional extremes}\\
  D. Cooley and E. Thibaud (2018)
\end{book}
A random vector $X$, with values in the positive orthant,
is \emph{multivariate regularly varying}
if its joint tail decays like a power function.
\begin{align*}
  & n \mathrm{P}[ b_n^{-1} X \in \bigcdot ] \overset{v}{\underset{n\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}} \nu_X(\bigcdot) \\
  & b_n = L(n) n^{1/\alpha}, \ L\ \text{slowly varying} \\
  & C(r,B) = \left\{ x \ :\ \n x \geq r , \ \dfrac{ x }{ \n x } \in B \right\}
    , \ B \subset \mathbf S_{p-1}^+ \\
  & \nu_X \text{: angular measure} \\
  & \nu_X\bigl( C(r,b) \bigr) = r^{-\alpha} H_X(B), \ H_X \text{ measure on } \mathbf S_{p-1}^+ \\
  & \nu_X( dr \times dw ) = \alpha r^{ -\alpha-1} dr \, dH_X(w)
\end{align*}

The \emph{tail pairwise dependence matrix} of the random variable $X$ is
\begin{align*}
  \Sigma_{ij} &= \int w_i w_j d H_X(w) \\
  \hat\Sigma_{ij} &= \dfrac{ r_0^2 }{ N } \sum_{t=1}^N w_{ti} w_{tj} \mathbf 1_{ r_t > r_0 }.
\end{align*}

$(\RR_+^\times)^p$ can be made into a vector space, with \emph{transformed linear} operations.
\begin{align*}
  x_1 \oplus x_2 &= t( t^{-1} x_1 + ^{-1} x_2 ) \\
  a \circ x_1 &= t \bigl( a t^{-1}(x_1) \bigr) \\
  A \circ \mathbf x &= t\bigl( A t^{-1}(x) \bigr) \quad A \in \RR^{p \times q } \\
  \langle x_1, x_2 \rangle _{ (\RR_+^\times)^p } &= \bigl\langle t^{-1}(x_1), t^{-1}(x_2) \bigr\rangle_{ \RR^p } \\
  t(u) &= \log( 1 + e^u )
\end{align*}
Regular variation is preserved by transformed linear operations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Separation-based causal discovery for extremes}\\
  J. Jiang et al.
\end{book}
To focus on extreme events, replace the linear operations in a structural causal model (SCM)
with transformed linear ones;
run the PC algorithm using tests on the (partial) tail pairwise dependence matrix.





%% Finance

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{9th annual global quantitative\\ and macro investment conference}\\
  Wolfe Research (2025)
\end{book}
For some companies, there is a misalignment between the claimed AI involvement in 10K filings and actual AI job postings and hiring,
in both directions (AI washing, AI masking).

Time series language models (Moirai, TimesFM)
outperform AR(1) models but are brittle:
combine BVAR and TSLM (BVAR on the data, TSLM on the BVAR residuals).

In presence of weak signals, prefer ridge regression to the lasso (or boosting),
which tend to drop weak but informative predictors.

Collusion between RL-based trading agents can emerge without communication or intent.

AI-generated content is beneficial on curated social media,
but detrimental (it increases speculation) on unmoderated social media.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Asset embeddings}\\
  X. Gabaix et al. (2025)
\end{book}
Compute asset embeddings from holdings data:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Recommender systems:
  \begin{align*}
    & \log w_{ui} \approx \langle \phi_u, \phi_i \rangle \\
    & u \text{: user (investor)} \\
    & i \text{: item (asset)} \\
    & w \text{: weight in portfolio} \\
    & \phi_u, \phi_i \text{: embeddings} 
  \end{align*}
\item Word2vec, BERT, GPT, after transforming the holdings into a sequence of tokens, sorting the assets by decreasing weight.
\end{compactitem}

This is more informative (at forecasting relative valuation and comovement) than text-based embeddings (10-K, earnings calls, news).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dual interpretation\\ of machine learning forecasts}\\
  P. Goulet Colombe et al.
\end{book}
The output of many models ((kernel) ridge regression, neural nets, random forests, boosting)
can be expressed as linear combinations of training observations:
\[ \hat y_j = \sum_{ i \in \text{Train} } w_{ij} y_i . \]
Those weights can be interpreted as the weights of a ``data portfolio''.
We can compute and plot:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The contributions $c_{ji} = w_{ji} y_i$, and their cummulated sums;
\item The concentration of $w_{\bigcdot j }$;
\item The sum of the negative weights (short positions);
\item The sum of the weights (not necessarily 1 -- but tree-based models are long-only, fully-invested);
\item The turnover $\n{ w_{ \bigcdot, j} - w_{ \bigcdot, j-1} }_1$;
\item The overall historical importance
  $\n{ w_{ i \bigcdot } }_1$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The popularity asset pricing model}\\
  T.M. Idzorek et al.
\end{book}
Most ``market anomalies'' can be explained by heterogeneous expectations (disagreement) and/or investor preferences (taste).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Domesticating the factor zoo\\ with economic theory}\\
  T.M. Idzorek et al. (2024)  
\end{book}
The factor zoo can be tamed by separating \emph{price factors} from \emph{attribution factors}.
Price factors are small in number, long-lived, caused by excess demand resulting from investors' preferences -- risk, illiquidity, low beta, bad ESG, reputation discount.
Attribution factors have zero expected returns and are backward-looking;
they are described by the PAPM (popularity asset pricing model) equilibrium model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identifying small mean-reverting portfolios}\\
  A. d'Aspremont (2007)
\end{book}
\textbf{1.}
We assume asset prices are modeled by a stationary structural VAR model.
\begin{align*}
  S_t &= A S_{t-1} + \epsilon_t \\
  \Gamma &= \var[ S ]
\end{align*}
``Stationary'' means that the eigenvalues of $A$ are all less than 1 in absolute value.
``Structural'' means that $A$ (and $\Gamma$) are sparse.

We estimate $A$ with lasso regressions.
We estimate $\Gamma$ with the graphical lasso (a sparsity penalty on $\Gamma^{-1}$, which tends to be sparser than $\Gamma$).

\textbf{2.} We want to find mean-reverting portfolios $w$. We can define ``mean-reverting'' in two ways:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The portfolio value $p_t = w' S_t$ should follow an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process,
  \[ p_t - p_{t-1} = \lambda ( \bar p - p_{t-1} ) + \epsilon_t \]
  with a large value of $\lambda$;
\item The portfolio value $p_t = w' S_t$ should not be predictable; the predictability can be defined as
  \[ \nu = \dfrac{ \var[ \hat p ] }{ \var [p] }. \]
\end{compactitem}
Those quantities can be computed either empirically, from $p = w ' S$,
or theoretically, from the VAR model.
\begin{align*}
  \lambda &= 1 - \beta \\
          &= 1 - \dfrac{ \cov[ p_t , p_{t-1} ] }{ \var[ p_{t-1} ] } \\
          &= 1 - \dfrac{ w' A \Gamma w }{ w' \Gamma w }\\[1mm]
  \nu &= \dfrac{ \var[ \hat p ] }{ \var[p] } \\
          &= \dfrac{ \var[ w' \hat S_t ] }{ \var[ w' S_t ] } \\
          &= \dfrac{ \var[ w' A S_{t-1} ] }{ \var[ w' S_t ] } \\
          &= \dfrac{ w' A \Gamma A' w }{ w' \Gamma w }
\end{align*}

\textbf{3.} Optimizing the theoretical quantities is a sparse generalized eigenvalue problem.
It can be solved in three ways:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Greedily: iteratively select the index of the variable that maximizes the ratio; 
\item Exactly: exhaustive search for small matrices (up to 15$\times$15);
\item SDP (semidefinite programming) relaxation (only for the predictability: for the mean reversion, $A\Gamma$ is not symmetric).
\end{compactitem}

Instead of looking for mean-reverting portfolios, we can look for momentum portfolios, by minimizing the mean reversion $\lambda$ or
maximizing the predictability $\nu$.

% The paper uses row vectors; I use column vectors: their A is my A'
% They use a continuous time OU process: there is a log in their λ, which is not always defined. 

