  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[shift={(5.2,0)}]
    \node (IX) at (0,1) {$I_X$};
    \node (IY) at (2,1) {$I_Y$};
    \node (X)  at (0,0) {$X$};
    \node (Y)  at (2,0) {$Y$};
    \node[circle,draw,densely dotted,inner sep=0pt] (W) at (1,1) {$W$};
    \draw[->] (IX) -- (X);
    \draw[->] (IY) -- (Y);
    \draw[->] (W) -- (X);
    \draw[->] (W) -- (Y);
    \node at (1,-1.1) {\footnotesize latent confounder};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
with conditional independence tests.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepSeek-R1: incentivizing reasoning capability in LLMs via reinforcement learning}
\end{book}
DeepSeek-R1-Zero uses RL (GRPO) on DeepSeek-V3-Base to enhance its reasoning capabilities:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Prompts: unspecified (math, leetcode, etc.);
\item Reward: only accuracy (correct final answer) and format, as specified by the prompt:\\
  \texttt{<think>...</think>} and \texttt{<answer>...</answer>}.
\end{compactitem}
During training, the model spends more and more time thinking,
but the \texttt{<think>} section becomes less and less readable
(it mixes Chinese and English).

DeepSeek-R1 uses 4 steps:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item SUpervised fine-tuning (SFT)
  with high-quality data, e.g., readable answers from DeepSeek-R1-Zero;
\item RL, as above;
\item SFT, with 600k reasoning samples (ask the model to generate several answers,
  and have DeepSeek-V3 select the best)
  and 200k non-reasoning samples from (DeepSeek-V3, unspecified prompts);
\item RL, for alignment (unspecified prompts and rewards).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepSeekMath: pushing the limits\\ of mathematical reasoning\\ in open language models}\\
  Z. Shao et al.
\end{book}
Start with a code model, and fine-tune it on a large mathematical corpus:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Train a FastText classifier on OpenWebMath; 
\item Apply it to CommonCrawl; 
\item Use the results to identify websites with math contents; 
\item Add the corresponding pages to the FastText training data
  (adding arxiv papers does not bring any improvement);
  re-train;
\item To avoid benchmark contamination, exclude pages containing a 10-gram from one of the benchmarks; 
\item Apply the new model to CommonCrawl.
\end{compactitem}

Also evaluate on miniF2F (convert an informal proof into a formal one, checked with Isabelle)
and on GSM8K (math with tool use: Python, SymPy).

In PPO,
\[ J = \E_{\substack{
    q \sim \text{queries} \\ o \sim \pi_{\text{old}}( \bigcdot|q) }}
\operatorname*{Mean}_{ 1 \leq t \leq \va o }
\dfrac{
  \pi_\theta ( o_t | q , o_{<t} )
}{
  \pi_{\text{old}} ( o_t | q , o_{<t} )
} - \beta \text{KL}( \pi_\theta \| \pi_{\text{ref}} )
\]
GRPO (group relative polici optimization)
replaces the advantage $A$ with the average reward from $\pi_{\text{old}}$,
\[ A_i = \dfrac{ r_i - \text{mean}( \mathbf r ) }{ \text{std}( \mathbf r ) } \]
and uses
\[ \text{KL} ( \pi_\theta \| \pi_{\text{ref}} ) =
\dfrac{ \pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdots) }{ \pi_\theta( \cdots ) } - \log
\dfrac{ \pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdots) }{ \pi_\theta( \cdots ) } - 1. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepSeek-V3 technical report}\\
\end{book}
DeepSeek-V3 combines:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item MoE (top-k and shared experts); 
\item No auxilliary loss to ensure load balancing between experts replaced by a bias term for each expert;
\item Sequence-wise auxilliary loss; 
\item Multi-head laent attentio (MLA): joint low-rank compression of $K$ and $V$ to reduce the size of the KV cache;
\item Multi-token predictin; 
\item FP8 mixed precision (FP8, BF16, FP32);
\item Context length extension (first 32k, then 128k);
\item Many parallelism and communication improvements).
\end{compactitem}
Training required 2 months on 2048 GPUs (\$5m).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Softmax is not enough\\ (for sharp out-of-distribution)}\\
  P. Veli\v ckovi\'c et al.
\end{book}
``Softmax'' should have been called ``softargmax''.

A function is \emph{sharp} if it only depends on a constant number of inputs, e.g., max.
Softmax cannot approximate shaprness with increasing problem size:
use a temperature-adjusted softmax instead,
with the temperature depending on the entropy of the initial probability distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data-driven discovery of synamical systems\\ in pharmacology using large language models}\\
  S. Holt et al.
\end{book}
LLM-based, CMA-ES-like optimization to find the ODE (Python code) best fitting the data;
the LLM can also request more features (from a list).
Iterate:
\begin{align*}
  f &= \text{LLM}( \text{descriptions}, \text{past attempts} ) \\
  \ell &= \min_\theta \text{loss} \bigl[ f(\text{Data}; \theta ) \bigr].
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rethinking early stopping:\\ refine, then calibrate}\\
  E. Berta et al.
\end{book}
(Multiclass) classifiers minimize two quantities:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Refinement error (whether the classes are in the correct order); 
\item Calibration error (whether the probabilities are correct).
\end{compactitem}
They are not simultaneously minimized during training.
Stop training when the refinement loss stops decreasing,
\[ \min_g \operatorname*{Mean}_{ (X,Y) \sim \text{Data} }
\text{loss} \bigl[ g\bigl( f(X) , Y \bigr) \bigr]  \]
where $g$ is the relalibration -- temperature scaling is good enough.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards safe reinforcement learning\\ via constraining conditional value at risk}\\
  C. Ying et al.
\end{book}
CVaR-PPO (aka CPPO)
adds a CVaR constraint to the PPO loss,
transforms it using the formulation of CVaR as an optimization problem,
and replaces the hard constraint
with its Lagrangian relaxation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FinRL-DeepSeek: LLM-infused risk-sensitive reinforcement learning for trading agents}\\
  M. Benhenda
\end{book}
RL trading agent, using technical indicators and LLM-derived signals
(trade recommendation from news,
confidence in this recommendation, risk assessment,
each as a numeric score in $\llbracket 0, 10 \rrbracket$,
trained with CVaR-PPO.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On causal discovery\\ in presence of deterministic relations}\\
  L. Li et al.
\end{book}
Deterministic relations create independence
and wreak havoc in constraint-based causal discovery algorithms:
for instance,
if $X \to Y$ is deterministic,
then $y$ is constant when conditioned on $X$, and therefore independent from everything:
$ * \indep Y \,|\, X$.
Similarly,
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (H) at (0,1) {height};
  \node (W) at (2,1) {weight};
  \node (B) at (1,0) {BMI};
  \draw[->] (H) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (B);
  \node[anchor=west] at (3,1)  {$*\indep \text{height} \ |\  \text{MBI}, \text{weight}$};
  \node[anchor=west] at (3,.5) {$*\indep \text{weight} \ |\  \text{MBI}, \text{height}$};
  \node[anchor=west] at (3,0)  {$*\indep \text{BMI} \ |\  \text{height}, \text{weight}$.};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
GES can be modified to account for deterministic relations:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Identify deterministic variables (by looking at the variance of the residuals of regressions)
  and ignore independence relations coming from determinism;
\item Use a (RKHS) ridge-adjusted BIC as score
  (because the variance matrix is singular in presence of determinism).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalized score functions\\ for causal discovery}\\
  B. Huang et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Using linear models in GES can introduce spurious relations, e.g., in
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.1,yscale=.8,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (x1) at (0,2) {$X_1$};
  \node (x2) at (0,1) {$X_2$};
  \node (x3) at (0,0) {$X_3$};
  \draw[->] (x1) -- (x2);
  \draw[->] (x2) -- (x3);
  \draw[->,densely dotted] (x1) to[out=-60,in=60] (x3);
  \begin{scope}[shift={(-4,0)}]
    \node[anchor=west] at (0,2) {$X_1 = \epsilon_1$};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0,1) {$X_2 = \smash{X_1 + X_1^2 + \epsilon_2}$};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0,0) {$X_3 = \smash{X_2 + X_2^2 + \epsilon_3}$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
the influence of $X_1$ on $X_3$ cannot be blocked by a linear function of $X_2$,
and can miss relations, e.g.,
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.1,yscale=.8,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (x1) at (0,1) {$X_1$};
  \node (x2) at (0,0) {$X_2$};
  \draw[->] (x1) -- (x2);
  \begin{scope}[shift={(-4,0)}]
    \node[anchor=west] at (0,1) {$X_1 = \epsilon_1$};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0,0) {$X_2 = \smash{(\sin X_1 + \epsilon_2)^2}$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
Use a kernel conditional independence test
to rule out potential parents of a node.

For the score function, use the
(ridge-regularized) CV-log-likelihood or marginal likelihood of an RKHS regression
(regression on
\[ \begin{bmatrix} \kappa(x_1,x) \\ \vdots \\ \kappa( x_n, x ) \end{bmatrix} \in \RR^n \]
instead of $\phi(x) \in \mathscr H$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Universality, characteristic kernels\\ and RKHS embeddings of measures}\\
  B.K. Sriperumbudur et al.
\end{book}
A kernel on $X$ is a positive definite function
$\kappa : X \times X \to \RR$.
I defines a subspace
\[ \text{Span} \{ \kappa(\bigcdot , x ) , \ x \in X \} \subset \mathscr F( X , \RR ). \]
It is \emph{universal} if
\[ \mathscr H_0 = \overline{
  \text{Span} \{ \kappa(\bigcdot, x) , \ x \in X_0 \}
} = \mathscr F, \]
for some $X_0 \subset X$
and some Hilbert space $(\mathscr F , \n\cdot)$ of functions on $X$.

It is \emph{characteristic} if
\[ \left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \text{Borel}(X) & \longrightarrow & \mathscr H \\
  P & \longmapsto & \displaystyle \int_X \kappa( \bigcdot , x ) d P(x) 
\end{array}
\right. \]
is injective.

Examples include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\mathscr C^0(X)$ and $\n\cdot _\infty$;
\item $\mathscr C^0(X)$ and compact convergence; 
\item $\mathscr C_0(X)$ (continuous functions vanishing at infinity) and $\n\cdot _\infty$;
\item $L^0$ and $\n\cdot _p$
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DoWhy-GCM: an extension of DoWhy\\ for causal inference in graphical causal models}\\
  P. Bl\"obaum et al.
\end{book}
\texttt{dowhy} was limited to effect estimation
\[ \E\bigl[ Y \,|\, \text{do}(T=1) \bigr]; \]
with \texttt{dowhy-gcm}, it can answer causal queries:
counterfactuals, direct and indirect effects,
causal strength, root cause analysis, etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Can large language models\\ infer causation from correlation?}\\
  Z. Jin et al.
\end{book}
Can an LLM learn the PC algorithm, \ie, infer causation from correlations
(independence and conditional independence relations, expressed as text)?
No. Fine-tuning does not help much.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal inference using LLM-guided discovery}\\
  A. Vashishtha et al.
\end{book}
Do not ask the LLM to find the whole causal graph
(it would truggle to distinguish direct from indirect relations)
but only the partial order it induces (the \emph{transitive reduction},
aka Hasse diagram it induces).

You can compare DAGs with the $L^1$ distance betwen their transitive reductions.

Use a triplet prompt to infer this order:
provide 3 variables and ask for their causal graph.

Applications include
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Post-processing the output of the PC algorithm, to orient the remaining unoriented edges; 
\item Constraints (transitive closure) for the PC or GES algorithms.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal order: the key to leveraging\\ imperfect experts in causal inference}\\
  A. Vashishtha et al. (2024)
\end{book}
Building a causal graph by asking pairwise questions to an LLM creates spurious edges --
with pairwise queries alone, the LLM cannot distinguish between direct and indirect effects.

The not build a DAG, but just a \emph{partial order}: it can be used
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item To obtain a valid adjustment set (the ancestors of a node in a topological order);
\item As constraints or prior for classical causal discovery algorithms.
\end{compactitem}

Do not use pairwise queries, but triplet queries (either all triplets,
or $k=10$ triplets for each pair if the graph is large),
aggregated with a majority vote:
this creates fewer cycles.

The \emph{topological divergence} between a partial order $\preccurlyeq$
and a DAG defined by its adjacency matrix
is the number of edges incompatible with the order,
\[ D( {\preccurlyeq} , A ) = \sum_{ i \not\preccurlyeq j } A_{ij}. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{From causal to concept-based\\ representation learning}\\
  G. Rajendran et al.
\end{book}
With no additional assumptions or data, we cannot recover
latent factors $Z$ from observed data $X = f(Z)$.
If $Z$ is a causal representaion , and if we have interventions an all latent variables $Z_j$,
it is identifiable -- but that is a lot of interventions.
A \emph{concept} is a projection $AZ$ of the latent factors;
concepts are identifiable through conditioning.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CLadder: assessing causal reasoning\\ in language models}\\
  Z. Jin et al.
\end{book}
Dataset to check if LLMs already know do-calculus
and can answer queries on the three rungs of teh causality ladder
(association, intervention, counterfactuals),
without resorting to commonsense.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep proxy causal learning and its application to confounded bandit policy evaluation}\\
  L. Xu et al. (2024)
\end{book}
PCL estimates causal effects in presence of unobserved confounders using proxies.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (Z) at (-1,1) {$Z$};
  \node (W) at (3,1) {$W$};
  \node (A)  at (0,0) {$X$};
  \node (Y)  at (2,0) {$Y$};
  \node[circle,draw,densely dotted,inner sep=0pt] (U) at (1,1) {$U$};
