\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On information transfer\\ in control dynamical systems}\\
  S. Sinha and U. Vaidya (2018)
\end{book}
The directed information is
\[ I(X\to Y) = H(Y_1,\dots,Y_T) - \sum_{ 1 \leq t \leq T } H( Y_t | Y_{<t} , X_{\leq t} ). \]
The transfer entropy is
\[ T_{X \to Y } = H( Y_t | Y_{<t} ) - H( Y_t | Y_{<t}, X_{<t} ). \]
A dynamical system
\begin{align*}
  x_{t+1} &= f(x_t, y_t ) + \text{noise} \\
  y_{t+1} &= g(x_t, y_t ) + \text{noise}
\end{align*}
defines a conditional probability distribution
$\rho( y_{t+1} | y_t )$.
Similarly, if we freeze $x$,
\begin{align*}
  x_{t+1} &= x_t \\
  y_{t+1} &= g(x_t , y_t ) + \text{noise} 
\end{align*}
defines $\rho_{\not x} ( y_{t+1} | y_t )$.
The information transfer is
\[ \left. T_{x \to y} \right|_t^{t+1} =
H\bigl( \rho( y_{t+1} | y_t ) \bigr) -
H\bigl( \rho_{\not x}( y_{t+1} | y_t ) \bigr).
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Context is key: a benchmark for forecasting with essential textual information}\\
  A.R. Williams et al.
\end{book}
Cik is a benchmark for \emph{multimodal}
probabilistic time series forecasting models
(models also provided with a textual context for each time series),
such as UniTime,
Time-LLM, LagLlama, Chronos, Moira, TimeGEN.

The CRPS (continuous ranked probability score))
\begin{align*}
  \text{CRPS}( F, x ) &= \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \bigl( F(y) = \mathbf1_{y\geq x} \bigr)^2 dy
  \qquad (\text{dim}=1) \\
  &= \E_{ X \sim F } \va{ X - x } - \dfrac12 \E_{ X, Y \sim F } \va{ X-Y}
\end{align*}
\[
<<python-CRPS,engine='python',results='hide',echo=FALSE,out.width='.8\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import scipy
xs = np.linspace( -5, 5 )
ys = scipy.stats.norm.cdf(xs)
i = ( 3*len(xs) ) // 5
fig, ax = plt.subplots( figsize = (4,1.5) )
ax.fill_between( xs[:i+1], 0*ys[:i+1], ys[:i+1], color = 'tab:orange', alpha = .3 )
ax.fill_between( xs[i:], ys[i:], 1+0*ys[i:], color = 'tab:orange', alpha = .3 )
ax.plot(xs, ys, linewidth = 3)
ax.axhline( 0, color = 'black', linewidth = 1 )
ax.axhline( 1, color = 'black', linewidth = 1, linestyle = ':' )
ax.set_xlim(-5, 5)
ax.axis('off')
plt.show()
@
\]
(the energy score uses $\n{\cdot}_2^p$ insead of $\va\cdot$)
is a proper scoring rule generalizing the MAE
to assess \emph{probabilistic} forecasts --
cross-entropy
\begin{align*}
  - \operatorname*{Mean}_x \log f(x) &= H( \text{data}, F ) \\
  H(p,q) &= - \E_{ X \sim p } \log q(x) 
\end{align*}
is often a better choice if the forecasted density is available.

The RCRPS (region-of-interest CRPS) is a weighted and penalized CRPS:
to give more weight to some forecasting horizons and penalize (problem-specific) constraint violations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Regions of reliability in the evaluation\\ of multivariate probabilistic forecasts}\\
  \'E. Marcotte et al. (2023)
\end{book}
A \emph{scoring rule} is a function $S$ measuring how unlikely an event $y \sim \text{Data}$ is,
wrt a forecasted distribution $F$; it is \emph{proper} if
\[ \E_{ y \sim \text{Data} } S(y,F) \]
is minimal for $F = \text{Data}$.

In practice, however, we do not have a ground truth distribution, but only a finite sample from it:
for some combinations of
\begin{align*}
  d &: \text{ dimension} \\
  n &: \text{ground truth sample size} \\
  m &: \text{ forecast sample size} 
\end{align*}
the statistical power of most scoring rules to detect
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Differences in mean; 
\item Positive correlations;
\item Skewness; 
\item Difference in marginal variance;
\item etc.
\end{compactitem}
is low -- you need to increase $n$ and $m$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time-MoE: billion-scale time series foundation models with mixture of experts}\\
  X. Shi et al.
\end{book}
Transformer (with SwiGLU, RMSNorm, no biases, ROPE, multi-resolution forecasting, MoE),
trained on 300B data points.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards transparent time series forecasting}\\
  K. Kacprzyk et al.
\end{book}
TO make time series forecasting (from static features)
``transparent'',
let the user interactively see the impact of changing some of the inputs on:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The shape of the forecast (a sequence of segments, labeled
  increasing/constant/decreasing and convex/linear/concave);
\item Some properties of the forecast (mean, slope, maximum, minimum).
\end{compactitem}
Fit a model with clubic splines (piecewise polynomials)
$\RR^k \to \RR^T$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecast evaluation for data scientists: common pitfalls and best practices}\\
  H. Hewamalage et al.
\end{book}
Long list of error measures for time series forecasting, with their suggested use and pitfalls.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use a simple baseline (naive, seasonal naive);
\item Forecast plots $t,\hat y \sim \text{time}$ are misleading;
\item Beware of non-stationarity (seasonality, trend, unit root (stochastic trend), heteroskedasticity, structural breaks)
  and non-normality (symmetry, tails, outliers, intermittency (zero-inflation));
\item Beware of ddata leakage.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Chronos: learning the language of time series}\\
  A.F. Ansari et al.
\end{book}
T5-based foundation time series forecasting model, processing time series
that have been mean-scaled (divide by the average absolute value) and quantized (uniform binning),
trained with data augmentation:
TSMixup, KernelSynth (Gaussian processes with kernels from the automated statistician).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The effectiveness of discretization\\ in forecasting: an empirical study\\ on neural time series models}\\
  S. Rabanset et al.
\end{book}
Binning input and output data tends to improve the performance of deep learning time series forecasting models (MLP, CNN, RNN).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum entropy bootstrap for time series: the meboot R package}\\
  H.D. Vinod and J. L\'opez-de-Lacalle 
\end{book}
The \emph{maximum entropy bootstrap}, for potentially non-stationary time series,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Separates the order of the observations from their distribution; 
\item Resamples the observations, using a piecewise uniform distribution (more precisely, a piecewise maximum entropy distribution, with a pre-specified mean on each interval, to allow values slightly more extreme than observed);
\item Keep the order (sic).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum entropy ensembles for time series inference in economics}\\
  H.D. Vinod (2006)
\end{book}
% Initial paper on the ME bootstrap

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The uncertainty of machine learning predictions in asset pricing}\\
  Y. Liao et al. (2025)
\end{book}
There are many types of bootstraps:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Classical: resampling with replacement;
\item Residual: fit a model and esample the residuals;
\item Wild: idem, after multiplying the residuals by $N(0,1)$
  (for heteroskedastic data);
\item Blocks: blocks of constant sizes;
\item Stationary: blocks of random length, from a geometric (exponential) distribution; 
\item Parametric: $X \sim F_{ \hat\theta}$;
\item Smooth: $X \sim \text{kde}$;
\item etc.
\end{compactitem}
To compute prediction confidence intervals for deep learning models on panel data,
use a variant of the wild bootstrap:
\begin{align*}
  \hat y_{it} &= \hat g( x_{i, t-1} ) && \text{forecast} \\
  \epsilon_{it} &= y_{it} - \hat y_{it} && \text{residual} \\
  \eta_t &\sim N(0,1) && \eta_t \text{, not } \eta_{it} \text{ or } \eta_i \\
  y_{it}^* = \hat y_{it} + \eta_t \epsilon_{it} 
\end{align*}
and fine-tune the model (starting from the trained one) for $k$ epochs.

Applications include worst-case (uncertainty-aware) portfolio optimization
\[ \operatorname{Maximize}_w \min_{ \mu \in \text{CI} } w' \mu - \dfrac\lambda2 w' V w. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The stationary bootstrap}\\
  D.N. Politis and J.P. Romano
\end{book}
Resample blocks of random lengths, from a geometric distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Proceedings of the 7th annual\\ AI in finance conference}\\
  Wolfe, 2025
\end{book}
LLM applications in finance include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Comparison of ChatGPT-generated answers with human ones in conference calls -- differences correlate with abnormal volumes; 
\item Denoising reported earnings; 
\item Human-free surveys, with synthetic personas (mimicking human participants); 
\item Making discretionary investing systematic; 
\item Identifying (and betting against) retain investor biases (technical analysis, from StockTwits).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Regimes}\\
  A. Mulliner et al. (2025)
\end{book}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take a few macro-economic variables
  (S\&P 500, 10y-3m, 3m, oil, copper, VIX, stock-bond correlation);
\item Compute the (Euclidean) distance between today's state and past states;
\item Buy (sell) assets with positive (negative) average returns in similar past situations.
\end{compactitem}
One can also look at the most dissimilar past states, and take the opposite actions.
[It works better with the Mahalanobis distance;
  a learned Mahalanobis distance gives similar results out-of-sample but overfits in-sample.]

