\iffalse

\textbf{1.}
Deterministic models (Kermack-McKendrick): compartmental models,
basic reproduction number $R_0$.
Reduction of contact as the epidemic progresses:
$\beta S I \to \beta S f(I)$.
The time spent in each compactment is exponential, but this can be generalized.
Stochastic models: compartments do not model well the begining of an epidemic, when there are too few people.
Galton-Watson process, epidemics on nwtworks, Reed-Frost model.
Endemic disease models: stable and unstable equilibria.
Heterogeneous mixing: care groups, super-spreaders, age.
Vector-borne diseases.

\textbf{2.} Compartmental models and basic reproduction number.
SIS: two equilibria, disease-free, and endemic.
\begin{align*}
  \dot S &= - \beta S I + \alpha I \\
  \dot I &= \beta S I - \alpha I \\
  R_0 = \beta N / \alpha \\
  N &= S + I = \text{const}
\end{align*}
Viruses tend to be SIR, bacteria SIS.
Adding births and deaths.

TODO: PLOT

\begin{align*}
  \dot S &= - \beta \dfrac IN S \\
  \dot I &= \beta \dfrac IN S - \gamma I \\
  \dot R &= \gamma I \\
  1/\gamma&:\text{ average infection period} \\
  \beta &: \text{ transmission rate} \\
  R_0 &= \beta / \gamma
\end{align*}

Other examples: two competing strains;
two groups of people, with slow diffusion between them, at different rates for $S$ and $I$.

\textbf{3.} Endemic disease models
There are many variants:


\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item SEIR (E=non-contagious);
\item Vertical transmission (mother-child);
  TODO: PLOT
\item Group of vaccinated people
  TODO: PLOT (vaccination)
\item SITR: some people can be treated, reducing transmission, mortality, length;
\item Treatment with limited capacity
  TODO: PLOT
\item Temporary immunity (SIRS), with an exponentially-distributed immunity period;
\item Temporary immunity, with a constant period (delay ODE);
\item Isolation.
\end{compactitem}
Some pf those systems of ODEs have several equilibria:
a disease-free equilibrium and one or severa endemic ones,
some stable, some not; some systems may exhibit oscillations.

Herd immunity: $p > 1 - R_0$.

$R_0$ can be computed from the life expectancy and the average age of the infectin:
$R_0 = 1 + L/A$ (assuming exponential distributions).

There may be several stable equilibriua;
the disease can be endemic even if $R_0 < 1$.
A \emph{bifurcation} is a change in equilibrium behaviour.

TODO: PLOTS (forward and backward bifurcations)

The disease stages are exponentially distributes,
but a Gamma distribution is more realistic.
In general, this leads to a system of \emph{integro-differential equations},
but a Gamma($n$) stage can be replaced by $n$ Exp(1) substages -- a larger system of ODEs.

\textbf{4.} Epidemic models.

Compartment models do not adequately describe the begining of an epidemic:
use a branching process instead,
to describe the epidemic on a graph.
The random contacts of an individual with others can be described by a random graph.
If $G_0$ is the generating function of the degree distribution,
then
\begin{align*}
  \langle k \rangle &= G'_0(1) \\
  G_1(z) = \dfrac{ G_0'(z) }{ \langle k \rangle } \\
  R_0 &= G_1'(1) = \dfrac{ G''_0(1) }{ G_0'(1) }
\end{align*}
More SIR variants:

