\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Automatic posterior transformation\\ for likelihood-free inference}\\
  D.S. Greenberg et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The Bayesian approach to statistics estimates the posterior $p(\theta|x)$
from the prior $p(\theta)$ and the likelihood $p(x|\theta)$.

\emph{Approximate Bayesian computation} (ABC, aka likelihood-free inference)
only assumes that we know the prior $p(\theta)$ and that we can sample
from $p(x|\theta)$ -- but we do not know the likelihood.

One can model the posterior as $q_{ F(x,\phi)} (\theta) \approx p(\theta|x)$,
where $q_\psi$ is a family of tractable distributions and $F$ is a neural net, trained with generated pairs
$(\theta, x)$,
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_\phi \sum_i \log q_{ F(x_i , \phi ) } ( \theta_i ). \]

Since we are interested in the posterior $p(\theta|x_0)$ at a specific point $x_0$,
we may want to restrict the $\theta$ samples to ``informative'' values
(sampling $\theta$ from $q_{ F(x_0,\phi_{n-1}) } (\theta)$
instead of $p(\theta)$ to estimate $\phi_n$) -- this gives a biased posterior,
but it can be fixed.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep symbolic regression:\\ recovering mathematical expressions from data\\ via risk-seeking policy gradients}\\
  B.K. Petersen et al. 
\end{book}
Symbolic regression usually uses genetic programming.
Instead, use RL to train an RNN model,
to generate a symbolic expression tree in pre-order traversal (depth-first, left-to-right).
The RNN is not only fed the previous token,
but also the parent and sibling nodes.
The reward is the normalized root mean square error.
The constant token is replaced with the constant maximizing the reward (using BFGS).
The \textsc{reinforce} policy gradient
(with baseline, for variance reduction) would maximize the expextec \emph{sum} of rewards --
instead, we want to maximize the maximum reward:
this can be achieved by maximizing the conditional expected reward
\[ \E_{ \tau \sim p_\theta } \bigl[ R(\tau) \,|\, R_\tau \geq R_\epsilon(\theta) \bigr]. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Why do tree-based models still outperform deep learning on tabular data?}\\
  L. Grinsztajn et al.
\end{book}
Neural networks:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Are biased towards overly smooth functions;
\item Are more affected (than tree-based methods) by uninformative features; 
\item Are rotation-invariant (rotating the input does not affect training).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{KwikBucks: correlation clustering with cheap-weak and expensive-strong signals}\\
  S. Silwal et al. (2023)
\end{book}
Given a graph $(V,E)$,
\emph{correlation clustering} finds a clustering $C$ minimizing
\[ \sum_{ (i,j) \not\in E } C_{ij} + \sum_{ (i,j) \in E } (1-C_{ij}). \]
KwikCluster picks a vertex at random,
forms a cluster by adding its neighbours, removes it, and iterates.
Alternatively, find a \emph{maximum independent set}
and assign (in parallel) the other nodes to their first neighbour in that set --
these are 3-approximations.

This can be generalized to budgeted correlation clustering --
we have a limited number of queries to the exact graph,
and an unlimited number to an approximation of the graph.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spectral sparsification of graphs:\\ theory and algorithms}\\
  J. Baston et al. (2009)
\end{book}
The \emph{Laplacian quadratic form} of a weighted graph $(V,w)$ is
\[ Q_G(x) = \sum_{ (u,v) \in E } w_{uv} ( x_u - x_v ) ^ 2. \]
The \emph{cut} of a subset of nodes $S \subset V$ is
\[ \text{Cut}_G(S) = \sum_{\substack{ u \in S \\ v \not\in S }} w_{uv} = Q(\mathbf{1}_S ). \]
Two weighted graphs are \emph{cut-similar},
resp. \emph{spectrally similar} if
\[ \begin{array}{lr@{\,}c@{\,}l}
  \forall S & \sigma^{-1} \cdot \text{Cut}_{\tilde G} (S) \leq & \text{Cut}_G(S) & \leq \sigma \cdot \text{Cut}_{\tilde G} (S) \\
  \forall S & \sigma^{-1} \cdot Q_{\tilde G} (S)          \leq & Q_G(S)          & \leq \sigma \cdot Q_{\tilde G} (S).
\end{array} \]
The \emph{effective resistance} between two nodes $u$, $v$ is
\[ R_{uv} = \biggl( \min_{ x: \ x_u=1,\, x_v=0 } Q_G(x) \biggr)^{\!\!-1} \]
(the equivalent resistance if you replace each edge with a resistor of resistance $1/w$).

The hypercube is a spectral approximation
(a spectral sparsifier) of the complete graph on $2^n$ vertices
(set the weights on the hypercube to $n/2\sqrt{\log n}$).

One can obtain spectral sparsifiers:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item By sampling $q$ edges (with replacement)
  with probabilities $p_e \propto w_e R_e$
  and assigning them weights $w_e / q p_e$;
\item Or as a union of random spanning trees.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spectral sparsification of graphs}\\
  D.A. Spielman and S.H. Teng (2010)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gemma Scope: open sparse autoencoders everywhere all at once on Gemma 2}\\
  T. Lieberum et al.
\end{book}
To explain an LLM,
train a \emph{sparse autoencoder} (SAE, sparse and nonnegative)
on its activations
(after the MLP, or the activations on the residual stream -- for all layers)
\begin{align*}
  & x \mapsto y = \sigma( W_1 x + b_1 ) \mapsto \hat x = W_2 y + b_2 \\
  & \sigma(z) = \text{JumpReLU}_\theta(z) = z \odot \mathbf{1}_{ z \geq \theta } =
  \smash{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.5,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=-2]
  \draw[->] (-1,0) -- (1.5,0);
  \draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,1.5);
  \draw[densely dotted] (-1,-1) -- (1.5,1.5);
  \draw[ultra thick,blue!50] (-1,0) -- (.75,0);
  \draw[ultra thick,blue!50] (.75,.75) -- (1.5,1.5);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}
  \\
  & \theta = 10^{-3}
\end{align*}
\emph{Transcoders} (which reconstruct the output of an MLP from its input,
using a sparse hidden layer) do not work that well.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Jumping ahead: improving reconstruction fidelity with JumpReLU sparse autoencoders}\\
  S. Rajamanoharan et al.
\end{book}
To train an SAE,
with discontinuous JumpReLU activations
and $\ell^0$ sparsity penalty,
use straight-through estimation for the gradients
\[
\dfrac{ \partial \text{JumpReLU} }{ \partial \theta } * k_\epsilon ,
\quad
\dfrac{ \partial \mathbf 1 _{ z \geq \theta } }{ \partial \theta } * k_\epsilon
\]
where $k_\epsilon$ is a (rectangle) kernel of bandwidth $\epsilon$;
for the $\ell^0$ penalty, use the pre-activations:
\[ \n{y} _0 = \mathbf 1 _{ W_1 x + b_1 \geq 0 }. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scaling and evaluating sparse auto-encoders}\\
  L. Gao et al.
\end{book}
The SAE with top-$k$ activations ($k=32$)
does not require an $\ell^0$ or $\ell^1$ penalty,
but may suffer from a large number of dead neurons:
add an auxiliary loss, the reconstruction error from the top $k_{\text{aux}}$ (512).
You can use several values of $k$,
e.g., $\ell(k) + \ell(4k)/8$, and change $k$ at test time.

% TODO: WHAT IS $\ell$?

\begin{align*}
  y &= \sigma \bigl( W_1 (x-b_2) + b_1 \bigr) \\
  \hat x &= W_2 y + b_2
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving dictionary learning\\ with gated sparse autoencoders}\\
  S. Rajamanoharan et al.
\end{book}
The $L^1$ penalty in SAE
\begin{align*}
  x &= x - b_2 \\
  y &= \text{ReLU}( W_1 x + b_1 ) \\
  \hat x &= W_2 y + b_2 
\end{align*}
causes shrinkage.
Instead, \emph{gated SAE}s use
\begin{align*}
  x &= x - b_2 \\
  g &= W_0 x + b_0 \\
  y &= \mathbf 1 _{g\geq0} \odot \text{ReLU}( W_1 x + b_1 ) \\
  \hat x &= W_2 y + b_2 \\
  (W_1)_{ij} &= e^{ r_i } \cdot ( W_0 )_{ij}.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Transcoders find\\ interpretable LLM feature circuits}\\
  J. Dunefsky et al. 
\end{book}
\emph{Circuit analysis} looks for sparse subgraphs corresponding to specific behaviours.
Transcoders approximate a densely activating MLP layer with a wider, sparsely activating layer.
\begin{align*}
  z &= \text{ReLU}( W_1 x + b_1 ) \\
  \hat y &= W_2 z + b_2.
\end{align*}
Train with a faithfulness loss
$\n{ \hat y - y }^2$
and a sparsity penalty $\n z _1 $.

%TODO: FIGURE 1?

To find interpretable circuits,
pick the top $k$ activations;
among their children, pick the top $k$ activations;
among the resulting $k^2$ graphs, pick the top $k$ ones;
and continue.

