\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A Wigner-Eckart theorem\\ for group equivariant convolution kernels}\\
  L. Lang and M. Weiler (2021)
\end{book}
The Peter-Weyl theorem states that if $G$ is a compact group and $X$ a homogeneous space,
then $L^2(X,K)$ ($K=\RR$ or $\CC$) is a direct sum
of unitary irreducible representations,
each with multiplicity at most its dimension;
for $X=G$ and $K=\CC$, the multiplicity is the dimension.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Coordinate-independent\\ convolutional networks}\\
  M. Weiler et al.
\end{book}
% 300 pages; not read

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nomic Embed: training a reproducible\\ long context text embedder}\\
  Z. Nussbaum et al.
\end{book}
Open-source 8192-context-length embedding model,
with \emph{task-specific prefixes},
to break the symmetry of the encoder (we want a different model
for questions and answers, such that the embeddings match).
Tricks include: RoPE, SwiGLU, Flash Attention, AdamW, gradient accumulation, etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Self-guiding exploration\\ for combinatorial problems}\\
  Z. Iklassov et al.
\end{book}
To solve combinaorial problems (e.g., TSP) with an LLM:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item ``List all possible methods to solve this problem. Return them separated by newlines'';
\item For each method, ``list all the steps to use this method'';
\item For each step, ``Is the problem easily solvable? Return `yes' or `no`'';
  if not, go to the previous step;
\item ``Give feedback on the proposed solution``;
\item ``Integrate all previous findings and provide the final answer''.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stealing part of a production language model}\\
  N. Carlini et al.
\end{book}
With access to the logits of an LLM,
one can recover the dimension of the last weight matrix.
The final layer is a projection from the hidden latent dimension to a \emph{higher} dimensional logit vector:
the dimension of the span of the logit vectors is the dimension of the last layer;
estimate it by looking at the singular value of the matrix $Q$ of logits.
The SVD, $Q = U \Sigma V^\top$
also gives the weight matrix, $W = U \Sigma$ (up to symmetries).
The attack still works without direct access to the logits, but is more expensive.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Let's do a thought experiment: using counterfactuals to improve moral reasoning}\\
  X. Ma et al. (2023)
\end{book}
To help LLMs to better in moral reasoning tasks:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Ask for moral counterfactual questions for each scenario;
\item Answer; discuss moral implications; highlight moral conflicts;
\item Summarize and conclude.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tree of thoughts: deliberate problem solving with large language models}\\
  S. Yao et al.
\end{book}
Don't ask for the next step, as in CoT (chain of thoughts),
but for several possible next steps;
explore the tree with BFS
(ask the LLM to pick the best $k$ children  among those generated;
some tasks (e.g., creative writing) are fine with $k=1$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ChatDB: augmenting LLMs\\ with databases as their symbolic memory}\\
  C. Hu et al.
\end{book}
Give your LLM access to an SQL database,
where it can store (and later retrieve) information.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to tokenize for general retrieval}\\
  W. Sun et al.
\end{book}
Sparse retrieval relies on inverted indices (TF-IDF, BM25, etc.).
Dense retrieval compares queries and document embeddings.
Generative retrieval replaces the dense embeddings with discrete ones -- lists of ``tokens''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Retrieval augmented generation\\ for large language models: a survey}\\
  Y. Gao et al. (2023)
\end{book}
RAG can suffer from low precision, low recall, irrelevance, hallucinations:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Preprocess the chunks: remove irrelevant or duplicate information
  (e.g., boilerplate text, special characters);
  rewrite to eliminate ambiguity; adjust chunk size;
\item Align query and documents:
  given a document,
  ask for corresponding queries and use them to compute the embedding;
  conversely, given a query, ask for (hallicinated) documents answering it,
  and query the vector database with them;
\item Add metadata to the retrieved documents;
\item Use a customized (domain-specific) embedding;
\item Re-rank;
\item Reduce noise in the retrieved documents and compress them (reduce the context length):
  remove irrelevant context, highlight pivotal paragraphs;
  ask the LLM to rewrite the context;
\item Hybrid search (vector + keywords);
\item Recursive retrieval: first retrieve small chunks, to capture key information, then larger blocks for more context; 
\item Ask the LLM to ``step back and reason about the general underlying concepts or principles'';
\item Let the LLM populate its own database;
\item Ask the LLM to assess the relevance of the retrieved documents;
\item Rewrite the query; try several formulations;
\item Compute the embeddings on small chunks, but retrieve larger chunks (e.g., abstracts vs full papers);
\item etc.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time is encoded in the weights\\ of finetuned language models}\\
  K. Nylund et al.
\end{book}
The time vectors are $W_t - W$, where $W$ are the model weights and $W_t$ the weights after finetuning on data from period $t$.
Use them to shift the model's attention to another period, e.g., $W + (W_{t_1} - W_{t_0})$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{``Do anything now'': characterizing and\\ evaluating in-the-wild jailbreak prompts\\ on large language models}\\
  X. Shen et al. et al. (2023)
\end{book}
Get your jailbreak prompts from Reddit and Discord.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Patch n' Pack: NaBiT, a vision transformer for any aspect ratio and resolution}\\
  M. Dehghani et al.
\end{book}
Instead of resizing images before giving them to a ViT,
pack patches from several images, and have the network output a sequence of labels, one for each input image.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Domain adaptation for time series\\ under feature and label shifts}\\
  H. He et al.
\end{book}
Align the two domains (feature shift: time and frequency features, Sinkhorn divergence),
then correct (label shift).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Baldur: whole-proof generation and repair with large language models}\\
  E. First et al.
\end{book}
LLM to generate formal proofs (software verification with Isabelle)
and fix them in case of errors.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Prompt programming for large language models: beyond the few-shot paradigm}\\
  L. Reynolds and K. McDonell
\end{book}
Few shot prompts help locate already learnt tasks:
well-crafted 0-shot prompts can do better.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Safety alignment should be made\\ more than just a few tokens deep}\\
  X. Qi et al.
\end{book}
Safety alignment of LLMs with RLHF is shallow: it just trains the model to identify harmful requests and to \emph{start} the answer with a refusal (``I cannot'', etc.).
Those models are suceptible to \emph{prefilling attacks}
(provide the begining of the answers, e.g., ``Sure, here is'' and let the model complete it).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{JaxPruner:\\ a concise library for sparsity research}\\
  J.H. Lee et al. (2024)
\end{book}
Implementation of pruning and sparse training algorithms.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A* search without expansions: learning heurisic functions with deep Q-networks}\\
  F. Agostinelli et al.
\end{book}
Deep Q nework as heuristic for $A^*$ search (aka best-first search).



%%%%% MAB

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic clustering\\ of contextual multi-armed bandits}\\
  T.T. Nguyen and H.W. Lauw (2014)
\end{book}
DynUCB:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fits a LinUCB model for each user;
\item Clusters the coefficients of those models ($k$-means);
\item Selects the action using the average model in each cluster.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonparametric bandits with covariates}\\
  P. Rigolet and A. Zeevi (2018)
\end{book}
For the (non-contextual) 2-arm bandit, UCB is asymptotically optimal and its regret is $O( \log n )$.
For a contextual 2-arm bandit, bin the context vectors (using a grid) and use UCB on each cell;
the regret is polynomial -- there exist bandits for which this cannot be improved.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonparametric stochastic contextual bandits}\\
  M.Y. Guan and H. Jiang (2018)
\end{book}
(Under reasonable assumptions), $k$-NN-UCB has sublinear regret.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A practical method for solving contextual bandit problems using decision trees}\\
  A.N. Elmachtoub et al.
\end{book}
For each possible action, fit a decision tree on a bootstrap sample, 
and use it as a Thompson sample:
predict the reward, and pick the action with the highest reward.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The epoch-greedy algorithm\\ for contextual multi-armed bandits}\\
  J. Langford and T. Zhang 
\end{book}
Iterate:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item One exploration step (random arm);
\item $\lceil \epsilon_n \rceil$ exploitation steps, where $\epsilon_n$ is an average regret bound for each exploration step,
  for the policy optimal (from a finite set $\mathscr H$ of candidate policies)
  on the exploration data only.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gambling in a rigged casino:\\ the adversarial multi-armed bandit problem}\\
  P Auer et al. (1998)
\end{book}
\emph{Exp3} solves the fixed-horizon multi-arm bandit (MAB) problem with $k$ arms as
\begin{align*}
  p_i(t) &\propto \exp \eta \sum_{ \tau < t } \hat r_{i \tau } \\
  \hat r_{it} &= \begin{cases}
    \dfrac{ r_{it} }{ \hat p_{it} } & \text{if arm $i$ was chosen at time $t$} \\
    0 & \text{otherwise} 
  \end{cases} \\
  \hat p_{it} &= (1- \epsilon) p_{it} + \dfrac{ \epsilon }{ k } 
\end{align*}

\emph{Exp4} uses the same idea to choose between experts (each providing a probability distribution on arms).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Approximate planning in large POMDPs\\ via reusable trajectories}\\
  M. Kearns et al. 
\end{book}
Given a POMDP you can sample from,
with a finite action space, build \emph{trajectory trees}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Nodes: state, observation pairs
\item Children for all actions, with the edge labeled by the corresponding reward;
\item Depth $H$ (there is an exponential number of nodes in each tree).
\end{compactitem}
Use those trees to evaluate policies:
a deterministic (resp. stochastic) policy defines
a (distribution on) path(s).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sequential testing}\\
  R. Nowak (2011)
\end{book}
Consider the null and alternative hypotheses
\begin{align*}
  H_0 &: X_i \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} p_0 \\
  H_1 &: X_i \sim p_1
\end{align*}
(they have to be simple);
choose $\alpha$ and $\beta$,
the desired type I and type II error rates;
compute the thresholds
\begin{align*}
  a &= \log \dfrac{ \beta }{ 1 - \alpha } < 0\\
  b &= \log \dfrac{ 1 - \beta }{ \alpha } > 0\
\end{align*}
and the cummulated sums
\[ \Lambda_k = \sum_{ i = 1 } ^k \log \dfrac{ p_1(x_i) }{ p_0(x_i) }. \]
The \emph{sequential probability ratio test} (SRPT)
accepts $H_p$ (resp $H_0$)
as soon as $\Lambda_k > b$
(resp $\Lambda_k < a$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Doubly robust policy evaluation\\ and optimization}\\
  M. Dud\'ik et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The \emph{direct method} (DM, aka controlling for confounders, backdoor adjustment)
estimates the value of a (MAB) policy
from an estimate $\hat r(s,a)$ of the reward conditioned on the state (
state = context = exogenous variables) $s$ and action $a$
\[ V = \operatorname*{Mean} _t \sum_a \pi( a, s_t ) \hat r( s_t , a ) ; \]
it has low variance but potentially high bias.

\emph{Importance sampling} (IS) uses the (known) data collection policy $\pi_0$
\[ V = \operatorname*{Mean} _t \dfrac{ \pi( a_t, s_t ) }{ \pi_0( a_t, s_t ) }  r_t \]
(there is no bias, but the variance is high if $\pi$ and $\pi_0$ are very different).

The \emph{inverse propensity} score (IPS, or inverse propensity weighting, IPW)
method uses some estimator $\hat \pi_0$ of the collection policy,
\[ V = \operatorname*{Mean} _t \dfrac{ \pi( a_t, s_t ) }{ \hat \pi_0( a_t, s_t ) }  r_t. \]

The \emph{doubly robust} (DR) estimator uses $\hat r$ (DM) as a baseline,
and corrects it with the propensity score
\[ V = \operatorname*{Mean} _t \sum_a \pi(a, s_t) \hat r(s_t, a) +
\dfrac{ \pi( a_t, s_t ) }{ \hat \pi_0( a_t, s_t ) }  ( r_t - \hat r_t(s_t, a_t)  ).
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Open bandit dataset and pipeline: towards realistic and reproducible off-policy valuation}\\
  Y. Saito et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Besides DM, IPW, DR, \emph{off-policy evaluation} (OPE) of contextual MAB policies include
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Self-normalized IPW
  \[ V = \dfrac{
    \operatorname*{Mean}_t w(a_t,s_r) r_t
  }{
    \operatorname*{Mean}_t w(a_t,s_r)
  }, \qquad
  w(a,s) = \dfrac{ \pi(a,s) }{ \hat\pi_0(a,s) } \] 
\item Switch estimator: DR when $w \leq \tau$, DM when the importance weights are too large;
\item DR with optimistic shrinkage
  \begin{align*}
    V &= \operatorname*{Mean}_t \hat r(s_t,\pi) + \hat w( a_t, s_t, \lambda) \bigl[ r_t - \hat r(a_t, s_t ) \bigr] \\
    \!\!\hat w(a, s, \lambda) &= \dfrac{ \lambda }{ w(a,s) ^ + \lambda } w(a,s)
  \end{align*}
\item Cross-fitting.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TimesNet: temporal 2D-variation modeling\\ for general time series analysis}\\
  H. Xu et al.
\end{book}
To account for inter- and intra-period variations, and multi-periodicity,
stack (with residual connection) several TimesBlocks:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the FFT of the 1D input (if there are several channels, average the amplitudes of each frequency)
  to identify the top $k$ frequencies
\item For eah of those frequencies, reshape the 1D input to 2D
  (\ie, stack them),
  %TODO: PLOT
  with 0-padding if needed;
\item Apply an inception block;
\item Reshape to 1D.
\end{compactitem}

