\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A geometric approach\\ to asset allocation with investor views}\\
  A.V. Antonov et al. (2024)
\end{book}
Given a prior on the distribution of asset returns
\[ x \sim p_{\text{prior}} = N( \mu_0, V_0 ) \]
and investor views
$ x \sim p_{\text{views}} = N(\mu_1,V_1)$,
compute a posterior distribution
\[ \operatorname*{Argmin}_{ p = N(\mu,V) } d( p, p_{\text{prior}} ) \text{\ \ such that\ \ } d( p, p_{\text{view}} ) \leq d_0 \]
or
\[ \operatorname*{Argmin}_{ p = N(\mu,V) } d( p, p_{\text{prior}} ) + \lambda  d( p, p_{\text{view}} ) \leq d_0. \]
This readily generalizes to degenerate views.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Markowitz portfolio construction at seventy}\\
  S. Boyd et al. (2024)
\end{book}
Long list of constraints and penalties you may want to add to your portfolio optimization
(turnover, risk budget, costs, etc.).

Replace the hard constraints with penalties to ensure the problem is always feasible
(but report constraint violations);
fine-tune the scale of the penalties on historical data.

Use robust optimization to account for the uncertainty in $\mu$ and $\Sigma$.

Some constraints are not convex:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Integral number of shares (lot shares);
\item Maximum number of assets;
\item Minimum position size;
\end{compactitem}

Do not write the target volatility constraint as
$ w' \Sigma w \leq \sigma^2$,
but $\n{ L^\top w }_2 \leq \sigma$,
where $\Sigma = L^\top L$ is the Choleski decomposition.
If $\Sigma$ comes from a factor model, $\Sigma = F v F^\top + \Delta$,
use
\begin{align*}
  w' \Sigma w &= \n{ \ell^\top w }_2^2 + \n{ \Delta^{1/2} w }_2^2 \\
  &= \n{ ( \ell^\top  w , \Delta^{1/2} w ) }_2^2
\end{align*}
where $v = \ell^\top \ell$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Constrained max drawdown: a fast and robust portfolio optimization approach}\\
  A. Dorador
\end{book}
The Markowitz portfolio optimization problem
(minimizing risk with a minimum return constraint)
can be linearized:
replace the risk with
\[ \sum_t \va{ \sum_i ( r_{it} - \bar r_i ) w_i } \]
or
\[ \min_t \sum_i r_{it} w_i \]
(add $0 \leq w \leq \tfrac12$ to avoid the 1-asset portfolio).
To limit the number of assets (e.g.,
$\forall i\ w_i \neq 0 \implies w_i \leq 0.05$),
use the big-M transform (with $M=0.5$).

