\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Can large language models\\ infer causation from correlation}\\
  Z. Jin et al.
\end{book}
Ask an LLM to discover and run the PC algorithm, \ie, discover and use
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $A \indep B \,|\, C \implies $ no $A$--$B$ edge;
\item $A \indep B \text{ and } A \not\indep B \,|\, C \implies
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.8,yscale=.17,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline={(0,.06)}]
  \node (A) at (0,2) {$A$};
  \node (B) at (0,0) {$B$};
  \node (C) at (1,1) {$C$;};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
  $
\item All colliders can be found in that way.
\end{compactitem}
(given all the relevant conditional independence statements, with no noise).
The performance is bad.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Full parameter fine-tuning for\\ large language models with limited resources}\\
  K. Lv et al.
\end{book}
% LOMO
To reduce memory usage when training large models, replace the traditional
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Forward pass, keeping all the activations; 
\item Backward pass, keeping all the gradients; 
\item Parameter update
\end{compactitem}
with
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Forward pass
\item For each layer, starting from the last:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Compute the gradients; 
  \item Update the parameters; 
  \item Free all the layers after the current one (they are no longer needed).
  \end{compactitem}
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Language models can solve computer tasks}\\
  G. Kim et al. (2023)
\end{book}
When asking an LLM to generate keyboard and mouse actions, iterate:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item ``Review your previous answer and find problems with it'';
\item ``Based on the problems found, improve your answer''.
\end{compactitem}
[This is similar to LLM-based optimization.]

