\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GPT is becoming a Turing machine:\\ here are some ways to program it}\\
  A. Jojic et al.
\end{book}
Simulate iterative behaviour (loops)
by providing a repetitive execution path as example.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Faithful chain-of-thought reasoning}\\
  Q. Lyu et al.
\end{book}
CoT is not faithful: the explanations need not entail the conclusion.
Instead, ask the LLM to generate the reasoning and the answer in a formal language
(Python, Datalog, PDDL, depending on the task:
math, relational inference, robot planning) and verify~it.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scaling down to scale up:\\ a guide to parameter-efficient fine-tuning}\\
  V. Lialin et al.
\end{book}
There are many parameter efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Adapters} are small fully-connected layers (or MoE layers),
  added (for instance) after transformers; 
\item \emph{Soft prompts} are prompt prefixes added,
  not as input tokens,
  but in latent space (either only for the input layer,
  or for all layers);
\item Selective methods only fine-tune some of the parameters,
  e.g., only the last layers, or only the biases,
  or a sparse subset of parameters (subset of important rows and columns, learned mask);
\item Reparametrization methods alter the weights $W$,
  e.g., $W \leftarrow W + AB'$ (LoRA),
  $W = W + A \otimes B$ or
  $W = W + \text{fastfood}(A)$.
\end{compactitem}
Those can be combined.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fastfood -- approximating kernel expansions\\ in loglinear time}\\
  Q. Le et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The \emph{fastfood transform}
\[ V = \dfrac1{ \sigma \sqrt d } D H_d G \Pi H_d B \]
where
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\Pi \in \{0,1\}^{d \times d}$ is a permutation matrix; 
\item $H_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}$,
  $H_{2\ell} = \begin{bmatrix} H_\ell & H_\ell \\ H_\ell & - H_\ell \end{bmatrix}$;
\item $B$ is diagonal with $\pm1$ entries;
\item $G$ is diagonal, Gaussian;
\item $D$ is diagonal;
\end{compactitem}
approximates Gaussian matrices
$Z \sim N( 0_{n\times d}, \sigma^2 ).$

