\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph learning}\\
  (ICML 2024 Tutorial)
\end{book}

Early graph learning methods relied on node embeddings:
DeepWalk, Node2Vec (a biased random walk, between depth-first search and breadth-first search).
The first graph neural nets relied on message passing (MPNN),
which can be written with matrix multiplications (GCN),
and augmented with a gating mechanism (GAT, graph attention);
if there are high-degree nodes, use sampling
(GraphSAGE samples the 2-hop neighbourhoods)

\emph{Spectral graph theory} studies the graph Laplacian.
The first eigenvectors (``eigenfunctions'') minimize the variability of the signal.
The Cheeger constant is the minimum (normalized) cut (NP hard)
\[ h_G = \min_{ S \subset V } \dfrac{
  \va{ \partial S } 
}{
  \min \bigl\{
  \text{vol}(S), \text{vol}(\bar S)
  \bigr\}
}
\]
(the volume is the sum of the degrees);
it is related to the second eigenvalue
\[ \dfrac{ \lambda_2 }2 \leq h_G \leq \sqrt{ 2 \lambda_2 }. \]
The commute time CT (of a node $u$) is
the expected number of steps needed for a random walk starting at $u$ to come back to $u$.
The effective resistance is
\[ R_{uv} = \dfrac{ \text{CT}_{uv} }{ \text{vol}(G) }. \]

A \emph{graph transformer} is a transformer (not a GNN)
whose positional encoding comes from a graph:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Laplacian eigenvectors (which generalize sines and cosines);
\item Learned positional embeddings (LPE), \ie, learned transformations of
  \[ \begin{pmatrix}
    \lambda_1 & \phi_{ij} \\
    \vdots & \vdots \\
    \lambda_m & \phi_{mj}
  \end{pmatrix}
  \]
\item Centrality encoding
  $ h_i = x_i + z^-_{ \deg^- (v_i) } + z^+_{ \deg^+ (v_i) } $;
\item Spacial encoding.
\end{compactitem}
The attention matrix can be sparse:
exphormer uses the original graph, an expander graph, and a global sink (not unlike BigBird).
GraphGPS combines MPNN layers, attention layers, and positional/structural encodings.

The expressivity of GNNs is limited by the WL isomorphism test --
but 1-WL cannot distinguish $d$-regular graphs...
To enhance expressivity, one can:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Add features: random features, substructures,
  affinity measures (effective resistance, hitting time, etc.);
\item Modulate message passing (e.g., with gating, in GAT);
\item Modify the graph, e.g., higher-order GNNs for message passing
  on $k$-tuples of nodes instead of nodes. 
\end{compactitem}

The Dirichlet energy measures \emph{oversmoothing}
\[ E(H) = \tr( H' L H ) = \dfrac12
\sum_{ (u,v) \in E } \n{
  \dfrac{ h_u }{ \sqrt{ d_u } } - 
  \dfrac{ h_v }{ \sqrt{ d_v } }
  }^2 \]
(the Rayleigh coefficient, used to define the eigenvalues,
is a normalized Dirichlet energy).
The rate of convergence of a random walk
$x_t = P^t x_0$, resp. the heat kernel,
$x_t = e^{-tL}$ to the stationary (resp. uniform) distribution is $\lambda_2$:
convergence is faster if the total effective resistance is low.
To limit oversmoothing: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Normalize the node embeddings;
\item Sparsify the graph (this reduces the spectral gap);
\item Regularize the weight matrix;
\item Add skip connections;
\item Change the GNN dynamics   % ???
  (GraphSAGE, GAT, PIGNN, AdaptiveGNN). 
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Oversquashing} (the exponential growth of the perceptive field) can be measured by the Cheeger constant,
the effective resistance, the curvature, the Hessian.
\[ \text{sensitivity}( u \to v ) = \n{
  \dfrac{ \partial h_v^{(r)} }{ \partial h_u^{(0)} }
  } \]
The symmetric Jacobian obstruction
\begin{align*}
& \left(\dfrac1{d_v}
\dfrac{ \partial h_v^{(m)} }{ \partial h_v^{(k)} } -
\dfrac1{\sqrt{d_u d_v }} \dfrac{ \partial h_v^{(m)} }{ \partial h_u^{(k)} }
\right)
+ \\
% Idem after switching u and v
& \qquad
\left(\dfrac1{d_u}
\dfrac{ \partial h_u^{(m)} }{ \partial h_u^{(k)} } -
\dfrac1{\sqrt{d_v d_u }} \dfrac{ \partial h_u^{(m)} }{ \partial h_v^{(k)} }
\right)
\end{align*}
is related to the (total) effective resistance.

Solutions include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Graph rewiring:
  spacial (SDRF, curvature) or spectral (DiffWire);
\item Virtual nodes;
\item Advanced architectures: % ?
  AdaptiveGNN.
\end{compactitem}
There is a trade-off between oversmoothing (OSM) and oversquashing (OSQ):
high $R$, low $\lambda_2$ gives more OSQ,
low $R$, high $\lambda_2$ gives more OSM.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data-efficient machine learning}\\
  (ICML 2024 Tutorial)
\end{book}

Data-efficient ML looks for a small subset of the training data,
such that a model trained on that subset has a generalization error similar
to that of a model trained on the whole data.
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{ S \subset V } F(S) \text{ st } \va S \leq k \]

\textbf{1.} For supervised learning, look for a subset with similar gradients.
For a single point $w \in \mathscr W$, take the medoids of
$\{ \nabla f_i(w) , \ i \in V \}$,
weighted with the cluster sizes.
It is a submodular problem (diminishing returns):
it can be solved (approximately) with a greedy algorithm
\[ F(D^*) = \sum_{ i \in V } \min_{ j \in S^* } \n{ \nabla f_i(w) - \nabla f_j(w) }. \]
This depends on $w$, but we can take the worst case:
\[ F(S^*) \leq \sum_{ i \in V } \min_{ j \in S^* }
\underbrace{\max_{ w \in \mathscr W} \n{ \nabla f_i(w) - \nabla f_j(w) }}_{ d_{ij} }. \]
If $f$ is convex,
\[ d_{ij} \leq \text{const} \cdot \n{ x_i - x_j } \]
where $x$ are the feature vectors:
it suffices to cluster the features.
If $f$ is not convex (e.g., a neural net), we still expect that
\[ d_{ij} \leq \text{const} \cdot 
\n{ \nabla_{\!\!z_i^{(L)}} f_i(w) - \nabla_{\!\!z_j^{(L)}} f_j(w) }. \]
(gradients wrt the last layer):
for classification, we would cluster the log-probabilities;
since they depend on $w$, we need to update the clusters from time to time.

To decide when to update the coresets,
model the loss as a piecewise convex function,
and change the coreset when the model moves to a different convex region:
check if the current convex approximation remains valid.
\begin{align*}
  & \tilde \ell (\delta ) = \ell(w) + g_s \delta + \tfrac12 \delta^\top H_s \delta \\
  & \dfrac{ \bigl| \tilde\ell (\delta ) - \ell( w + \delta ) \bigr| }{ \ell( w + \delta ) }
  \leq \tau
\end{align*}

To ensure SGD gets unbiased gradient estimates,
select multiple random subsets of size $r\gg 1$,
and form a coreset (minibatch) of size $m$ for each of them;
check the convex approximation on the union of the minibatches.
As training progresses, this selects increasingly difficult examples.

There are simple heuristics, looking for difficult-to-learn examples, but they are affected by noisy labels:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Drop unforgettable samples (a ``forgetting event''
  occurs when a sample, which was correctly classified earlier in training,
  becomes incorrectly classified);
\item Train several models, for only 20 epochs,
  and keep samples with the largest dLoss/dLastLayer;
\item Cartography the dataset into:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item High confidence, low variability: easy -- discard;
  \item Low confidence, low variability: potentially mislabeled -- discard;
  \item High variability (ambiguous), \ie, the true class probability fluctuates during training -- keep.
  \end{compactitem}
\end{compactitem}
Those heuristics allow the removal of up to 30\% of the data; if you want to remove more,
keep the easy examples instead of the hard ones.
Those heuristics do not take similarity into account;
submodular optimization does, and yields a curriculum:
it starts with easy examples (but not the easiest),
and progressively increases the difficulty (measured by ``forgettability'').

\textbf{2.} For self-supervised contrastive learning, this is trickier:
the loss
\[ \ell_{ij} = - \log \dfrac{
  \exp \text{sim}(z_i,z_j) / \tau
}{
  \sum\limits_{k\neq i} \exp \text{sim}(z_i,z_k) / \tau
} \]
(and its gradient)
for an observation depends on all the observations --
it tries to
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Align augmentations of the same example (``alignment'');
\item Push apart augmentations of different examples (or examples from different classes) (``divergence'').
\end{compactitem}
The coreset should preserve alignment and divergence.

Define the expected alignment distance as
\[ d_{ij} = \E_{\substack{ x \in A(x_i) \\ x' \in A(x_j) }} \n{ x - x' } \]
from a cheap proxy model.

To preserve alignment, ensure that, for each discarded example,
there is another with similar augmentations
\[ \forall u \in V_k \setminus S_k \ \min_{ j \in S_k } d_{ij} \leq \delta, \]
\ie, we select a diverse set of examples in each latent class.

The latent classes are, for instance, $k$-means clusters on a cheap proxy model,
or a foundational model (CLIP).

To preserve divergence, find a subset preserving the centers of the latent classes
(\ie, select the most central examples in each latent class)
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{\substack{ S \subset V \\ \va S \leq r }}
\sum_{ i \in V_k \setminus S_k }
\sum_{ j \in S_k } d_{ij}.
\]
(This is a submodular optimization problem.)
Contrary to supervised learning, where we were discarding easy examples
and keeping those near decision boundaries,
for SLL, we train on easy examples (cluster centers).

\textbf{3a.} Multimodal foundational models (CLIP, contrastive language-image pretraining)
% CLIP
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.25,inner sep=3pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \foreach \i in {0,2,4,6,8,10} {
    \draw (0,\i) -- (10,\i);
    \draw (\i,0) -- (\i,10);
  }
  \node[anchor=east,inner sep=1pt] (t) at (-7,12) {text};
  \node[anchor=east,inner sep=1pt] (i) at (-7,5) {image};
  \node[draw] (e1) at (-4,12) {enc};
  \node[draw] (e2) at (-4,5) {enc};
  \draw[thick,->] (t) -- (e1);
  \draw[thick,->] (i) -- (e2);
  \foreach \i in {1,3,5,7,9} 
    \draw[thick,->] (e1) -| (\i,10);
  \foreach \i in {1,3,5,7,9} 
    \draw[thick,->] (e2) -- (-1,5) |- (0,\i);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
are similar: they pull together matching image-caption pairs,
and push apart other pairs,
but they are even more data-hungry (400 times what supervised learning requires, versus 10 times).

For diversity (alignment of modalities),
find a subset preserving the cross-covariance in each latent class
\[ C = \dfrac1{ \va V } \sum_{ i \in V }
( x_{\text{vision}}^i - \mu_{\text{vision}} )
( x_{\text{language}}^i - \mu_{\text{language}} ).
\]

For centrality, preserve the latent class centers.
Define the latent classes with the cross-modality similarity (from a proxy model)
\[
\text{sim}( \text{caption}_1 , \text{image}_2 ) +
\text{sim}( \text{caption}_2 , \text{image}_1 ). \]
The corresponding coresets can be
\begin{align*}
  &\operatorname*{Maximize}_{ S_k \subset V_k } \sum_{ i \in S_k } \text{csim}( \underset{\text{text}}{i} , \underset{\text{image}}{i} ) \\
  &\operatorname*{Maximize}_{ S_k \subset V_k } \sum_{ j \in S_k } \sum_{ i \in V_k} \text{csim}(i,j) \\
  &\operatorname*{Maximize}_{ S_k \subset V_k } \sum_{\substack{ i \in V_k \\ j \in S_k }} \text{csim}(i,j) +  \sum_{ i \in S_k } \text{csim}(i,i)\\
\end{align*}
(these are non-monotone submodular optimization problems).

Heuristics include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Deduplication: cluster the CLIP embeddings, discard samples when the similarity is above $1-\epsilon$;
\item Focus on examples that are learnable but not easy to learn
  \[
  \text{CLIP}_{\substack{\rlap{\scriptsize\text{fully}}\hphantom{\text{trained}}\\\text{trained}}} (i,i)
  -
  \text{CLIP}_{\substack{
      \text{partially}
      \\
      \rlap{\scriptsize\text{trained}}\hphantom{\text{partially}}
  }} (i,i)
  \]
\end{compactitem}
(on a clean dataset, those heuristics do not improve on a random subset).

\textbf{3b.} For LLMs, we cannot match the gradients:
they are too high-dimensional, even for the last layer,
even for LoRA (for fine-tuning).
If the curvature is small (during fine-tuning, it is),
examples with similar loss trajectories
(similar values for a few points -- with a smaller model)
have similar gradients:
can can sample from loss-trajectory clusters.

If you want to actually match the gradients:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use the $V$ projection (from the transformer's $Q$, $K$, $V$ matrices) at the last layer;
\item Use zeroth order gradients (gradients as an expectation)
%  TODO: FORMULA?
\item Sparsify (keep 1000 or 2000 dimensions with the largest magnitude).
\end{compactitem}

Heuristics include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Perplexity (prefer examples with average perplexity);
\item Memorization ranking;  % ? 
\item Deduplication;
\item Data selection with another LLM;
\item Centroids of hidden states;
\item Influence functions (if there is a target class);
\item Data models.  % ?
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural operator learning}\\
  (ICML 2024 tutorial)
\end{book}
The first layer of a neural net can be written
\begin{align*}
  && b_i &= \sigma \left( \dfrac1n \sum_{j} k_{ij} a_i \right) \\
  &\text{or} & v(y_i) &= \sigma \left( \sum \kappa( y_i, x_i ) a(x_j) \Delta x_j \right) \\
  &\text{\ie,} & v(y) &= \sigma \left( \int \kappa(y,x) a(x) dx \right);
\end{align*}
it is a linear integral operator.
We can add bias and skip connection
\[ v(y) = \sigma\left( \int \kappa(y,x) a(x) dx + W a(y) + b(y) \right). \]
The output function can be evaluated at any point,
and the input can be provided at any discretization.

The graph neural operator (GNO) implements $\kappa$ as a neural net.

The Fourier neural operator uses basis functions
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=1.5,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (a) at (.3,0) {$a$};
  \node[fill=blue!10, align=center, text width = 7mm, inner sep=3pt] (F) at (1,0) {$\mathscr F$};
  \node[fill=blue!10, align=center, text width = 7mm, inner sep=3pt] (k) at (2,0) {$\kappa$};
  \node[fill=blue!10, align=center, text width = 7mm, inner sep=3pt] (G) at (3,0) {$\mathscr F^{-1}$};
  \node (v) at (3.7,0) {$v$};
  \draw[->] (a) -- (F);
  \draw[->] (F) -- (k);
  \draw[->] (k) -- (G);
  \draw[->] (G) -- (v);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
(on a regular grid, use the FFT).

The model can be trained on a coarse grid and evaluated on a finer one.

The integral can also be computed with:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Gaussian quadrature;
\item The multipole method (UNO: U-shaped NO, similar to UNet).
\end{compactitem}
This also works for other network architectures:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Transformers (just replace $\sum$ with $\int$);
\item CNNs (e.g., derivatives).
\end{compactitem}

Physics-informed neural operators (PINO)
use both data and a PDE (the PDE can have a higher resolution).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mixtures of experts in the era of LLMs}\\
  (ICML 2024 tutorial)
\end{book}
(Sparse-gated) mixtures of experts (MoE)
can suffer from:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Imbalanced routing (some experts are used too often, or too rarely);
\item Redundant experts.
\end{compactitem}
Many design choices are possible:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Top-2 or fine-grained;
\item Shared expert (an expert that is always used);
\item Experts after each layer, with more experts in the last layers;
\item Loss function.
\end{compactitem}
To convert a dense LLM into a MoE,
you can:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Copy the feed-forward part to form experts (this increases the number of parameters);
\item Split the feed-forward part
  (using $k$-means on the neuron activations).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distribution-free\\ predictive uncertainty quantification:\\
  strength and limits of conformal prediction}\\
  (ICML 2024 tutorial)
\end{book}
Given observations
$(X_1,Y_1),\dots,(X_n,Y_n)$,
conformal prediction aims to find $C_\alpha$ such that
\[ \mathrm P\bigl[ Y_{n+1} \in C_\alpha(X_{n+1}) \bigr] \geq 1-\alpha. \]

\d{Split conformal prediction} (SCP) proceeds as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Split the data into training
  (75\% to 90\%), calibration and test ($X_{n+1}$);
\item Fit a model $\hat \mu$ on the training set;
\item Compute ``conformity scores'' on the calibration set
  \[ S_{\text{Cal}} = \bigl\{
  \va{\text{residuals}} \text{ on Cal}
  \bigr\} \union \{ \infty \} \]
\item Use the corresponding quantiles
  \[ \hat C_\alpha ( X_{n+1} ) = \bigl[ \hat \mu( X_{n+1} ) \pm q_{1-\alpha}( S_{\text{Cal}} ) \bigr]. \]
\end{compactitem}
If the data is exchangeable, we have marginal validity
\[ 1 - \alpha \leq \E_{ X_{n+1} } \mathrm{P} \bigl[
Y_{n+1} \in \hat C_\alpha (X_{n+1} )
\bigr] \leq 1 - \alpha + \dfrac1{ \#\text{Cal} + 1 } \]
but SCP is not adaptive (it does not recognize heteroskedasticity).

\d{Conformalized quantile regrssion} (CQR)
fits two quantile models, e.g., 10\% and 90\%,
and uses the signed distance to the closest quantile (negative if inside, positive if outside).
\[ \hat C_\alpha( X_{n+1} ) = \bigl[
  \widehat{\text{QR}}_\ell (X_{n+1}) - q_{1-\alpha} ( S_{\text{Cal}} ) ,
  \widehat{\text{QR}}_u   (X_{n+1}) + q_{1-\alpha} ( S_{\text{Cal}} ) 
  \bigr]
\]
More generally, if
$ S = \bigl\{
s(x_i,y_i),
i \in \text{Cal} 
\bigr\} \union \{\infty\}$,
\[\hat C_\alpha (X_{n+1}) = \bigl\{ y \ :\ s(X_{n+1}, y ) \leq _{1-\alpha}(S) \bigr\}. \]

\d{Locally weighted SCP} uses
\begin{align*}
  s(X,Y) &= \dfrac{ \va{ \hat\mu(X) - Y } }{ \hat\rho(X) } \\
  \hat C_\alpha(X) &= \bigl[ \hat\mu(X) \pm q_{1-\alpha}(S) \hat\rho(S) \bigr] 
\end{align*}

\d{Full conformal prediction} avoids data splitting it
fit models $\hat A_y$ on all the data plus $(X_{n+1},y)$, for all possible values of $y$, and uses
\begin{align*}
  S_y &= \bigl\{ s(X_i,Y_i,\hat A_y ) \bigr\} \union \{ s(X_{n+1}, y, \hat A_y ) \bigr\} \\
  \hat C_\alpha(X_{n+1}) &= \bigl\{ y \ :\ s(X_{n+1} , y, \hat A_y ) \leq q_{1-\alpha} ( S_y ) \bigr\}
\end{align*}

The \emph{jackknife} uses the leave-out-one residuals
(but assumes the algorithm is ``stable'').
\begin{align*}
  S &= \bigl\{ \bigl| \hat A_{-i} (X_i) - Y_i \bigr| \bigr\} \union \{\infty\} \\
  \hat C &= \bigl[ \hat A(X_{n+1}) \pm q_{1-\alpha}(S) \bigr]
\end{align*}

The \emph{Jackknife+} does not make that assumption
\begin{align*}
  s_i &= \va{ Y_i - \hat\mu_{-i}(X_i) } \\
  u_i &= \hat \mu_{-i}(X_i) + s_i \\
  \ell_i &= \hat \mu_{-i}(X_i) - s_i \\
  U &= \{u_i\} \union \{ \infty \} \\
  L &= \{\ell_i\} \union \{ \infty \} \\
  C &= \bigl[ q_\alpha(L) , q_{1-\alpha}(U) \bigr]
\end{align*}
(the coverage is $\geq 1-2\alpha$, not $1-\alpha$,
unless the algorithm is stable).

\emph{Cross-Validation+}
is between SCP and Jackknife+.

In a \emph{covariate shift}, $\mathscr L_X$ changes but not $\mathscr L_{Y|X}$.
In a \emph{label shift}, $\mathscr L_Y$ changes, but not $\mathscr L_{X|Y}$.
To deal with covariate shift, give more importance to calibration points closer to the test point.
\begin{align*}
  & (X_1,Y_1), \dots, (X_n,Y_n) \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} P_X \times P_{Y|X} \\
  & (X_{n+1},Y_{n+1}) \sim \tilde P_X \times P_{Y|X} \\
  &w_i \propto \dfrac{ d \tilde P_X(x_i) }{ d P_X(x) } \\
  &C_\alpha = \bigl\{
  y \,: \,
  s( X_{n+1} , y, \hat A ) \leq q_{1-\alpha} \bigl( \sum w_i \delta_{ s_i } + w_{n+1} \delta_\infty \bigr)
  \bigr\}
\end{align*}
Use a similar idea for label shift.

For time series data,
ACI (adaptive conformal inference)
keeps track of the miscoverage ate (with an EWMA)
and adjusts the quantile accordingly;
AgACI uses several half-lives, combined with dynamic weights, and processes the upper and lower bounds seperately.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convex analysis at infinity:\\ introduction to astral space}\\
  (ICML 2024 tutorial)
\end{book}
The \emph{astral space} is a compactification of $\RR^n$
for convex analysis.

We would like convex functions on $\RR^n$ to always have a minimum:
to this end, we can add ``points at infinity''.
In dimension 1, we can just set $\bar \RR = [ - \infty ,  \infty ]$ --
the exponential function now has a minimum.

In dimension 2, we would like
\[ f(x_1,x_2) = e^{-x_1} + ( x_2 - x_1 )^2 \]
to have a minimum:
we can add ``points at infinity'' for each direction and offset.
But this is not sufficient:
we also want
\[ f(x_1,x_2) = e^{-x_1} + e^{ -x_2 + x_1 / 2 } \]
to have a minimum, but the sequence $x_t = (t,t^2)$
does not correspond to a straight ray...

The \emph{astral space} $\overline{\RR^n}$
is obtained by adding to $\RR^n$ the sequences $(x_t)_t$ such that
\[ \forall u \in \RR^n \ \lim (x_t \cdot u ) \text{ exists (in $\bar\RR$)} \]
and identifying two sequences, $x_t \sim x'_t$
if $\forall u \ \lim (x_t \cdot u) = \lim (x'_t \cdot u)$.
This is a compact (but not metric) topological space.

For $\bar x \in \overline{\RR^n}$, the \emph{coupling function} is
\[ \bar x \cdot u = \lim( x_t \cdot u ). \]
It does not have all the properties of a scalar product, but
\begin{align*}
  \bar x = \bar x'    &\text{\  iff \ } \forall u \ \ \bar x \cdot u = \bar x ' \cdot u \\
  \bar x \in \RR^n    &\text{\  iff \ } \forall u \ \ \bar x \cdot u \in \RR \\
  \bar x_t \to \bar x &\text{\  iff \ } \forall u \ \ \bar x_t \cdot u \to \bar x \cdot u
\end{align*}
An \emph{astron} is an element of the form
$x_t = tv$, for $v \in \RR^n$ (a straight ray);
it will be written $\omega v = \lim tv$.
The elements of $\overline{\RR^2}$ are of the form
\[ x_t = t^2 v_1 + t v_2 + q, \qquad v_1,v_2 \in \RR^2 \]
\def\lplus{\mathbin{{\bigcdot}\!{+}}}%
and are written $\bar x = \omega v_1 \lplus \omega v_2 \lplus q $
where $\lplus$ is the \emph{leftward addition}.
On $\bar \RR$:
\begin{align*}
  (+\infty) \lplus (-\infty) &= +\infty \\
  (-\infty) \lplus (+\infty) &= -\infty \\
  x \lplus y &= x + y \text{ otherwise}. 
\end{align*}
On $\overline{\RR^n}$:
$ (\bar x \lplus \bar y ) \cdot u = ( \bar x \cdot u ) + ( \bar y \cdot u ) $.

In dimension $n$, every astral point can be written
\[ \bar x = \underbrace{ \omega v_1 \lplus \cdots \lplus \omega v_k}_{\text{astrons}}
\lplus \underbrace{q}_{\clap{\scriptsize finite part}} \]
where $v_1,\dots,v_k,q$ are orthogonal;
$k$ is the \emph{astral rank} of $\bar x$.

A $\mathscr C^0$ function $f: \RR^n \to \bar \RR$ can
be extended to $\bar f : \overline{\RR^n} \to \RR$ as
\[ \bar f( \bar x ) = \min_{ x_t \to \bar x } \lim f(x_t). \]
For linear functions, $f(x) = x \cdot u$, the limit does not depend on the choice of the sequence,
and $\bar f(\bar x ) = \bar x \cdot u$, but, in general,
$\bar f$ is not continuous: for instance, with
\[ f(x_1, x_2 ) = e^{ - x_1 } + e^{ - x_2 + x^2 /2 } \]
$\bar f$ is minimized at $\bar x = \omega e_2 \lplus \omega e_1$,
but $\bar f$ is not continuous at $\bar x$:
$x_t=(t,\tfrac12 t^2) \to \bar x$, but
$f( x_t) \to 1 \neq 0$.

A convex function $f : \RR^n \to \bar R$ has a minimum at $x$ iff
$0 \in \partial f(x)$.
This generalizes the subgradient at infinity:
\begin{align*}
  u \in \partial \bar f(x) \ \text{ iff } \ \exists b \ \ 
  & \forall \bar y \in \overline{\RR^n} \ \ \bar f(\bar y) \geq \bar y \cdot u + b \\
  & \exists x_t \to \bar x \ \ f(x_t) - f( x_t \cdot u + b) \to 0
\end{align*}

For constrained optimization
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ u \in \RR^n } g(u) \ \text{ st }\  a \cdot u = b \]
a sufficient condition is
$a \cdot u = b$, $ \nabla g(u) = \lambda a$.
We want to allow vertical tangents, as with
\[ g(u) = \text{entropy}( u_1 , u_2, 1 - u_1, - u_2 ) \]
(it is finite on the triangle, including its boundary,
but it is not differentiable on the boundary --
it is infinite outside the boundary).
For $\bar x \in \overline{ \RR^n }$,
the dual linear function is $\phi(u) = \bar x \cdot u$.
The \emph{dual subgradient} of a convex function
$g : \RR^n \to \bar \RR$ is
\begin{align*}
  \bar x \in \bar\partial g(u) \ \text{ iff }\ 
  & g(u) \in \RR \\
  & \forall v \in \RR^n \ \ g(v) \geq \bar x \cdot (v-u) + g(u) 
\end{align*}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=1,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \draw[very thick] (A) to[out=-90,in=-90,looseness=2] (B);
  \color{blue!50!black}
  \draw[densely dotted] (-.5,.4) -| (0,-1) -- (1.3,-1);
  \node[anchor=east] at (-.5,.4) {\footnotesize$+\infty$};
  \node[anchor=north] at (0,-1) {\footnotesize$\bar\partial = \{-\infty\}$};
  \node[anchor=west] at (1.3,-1) {\footnotesize$-\infty$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=1,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (-1,0) {};
  \draw[very thick] (A) to[out=-90,in=-90,looseness=2] (B);
  \color{blue!50!black}
  \draw[densely dotted] (.5,.4) -| (0,-1) -- (-1.3,-1);
  \node[anchor=west] at (.5,.4) {\footnotesize$+\infty$};
  \node[anchor=north] at (0,-1) {\footnotesize$\bar\partial = \{+\infty\}$};
  \node[anchor=east] at (-1.3,-1) {\footnotesize$-\infty$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
``Conjugacy inverts the subgradient map'':
if $f$ is closed, proper, convex,
\[ u \in \partial f(x) \iff x \in \partial f^*(u). \]
More generally:
\[ u \in \partial \bar f(\bar x ) \iff \bar x \in \bar\partial f^*(u). \]

On $\RR^n$, if $f$ is convex,
$\nabla f x = 0 \Rightarrow x$ is a minimizer.
On $\overline{ \RR^n }$, if $f$ is convex,
$x_t \to \bar x$, and $\bar f$ is continuous at $\bar x$
(this is not always the case, but most loss functions have a continuous extension), then
\[ \nabla f(x_t) \to 0 \ \implies\  f(x_t) \to \inf f. \]

If $f$ is convex and $\bar f$ continuous,
if the progress condition is satisfied
\begin{align*}
  & f(x_{t+1}) \leq f(x_t) - \alpha_t h(\n{ u_t} ) \\
  & \alpha_t \geq 0 , \quad \sum \alpha_t = \infty \\
  & u_t \in \partial f(x_t) \\
  & h \text{ non-decreasing},\ h(0)=0, \ h(s) > 0
\end{align*}
then $f(x_t) \to \inf f$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Strategic ML: learning with data that behaves}\\
  (ICML 2024 Tutorial)
\end{book}
Strategic machine learning is a generalization of adversarial learning,
in which subjects can change their features, to some extent.
Assuming they know the model, it is a nested min-argmax problem
(adversarial learning is a minimax problem).
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & h, x' \\
  \text{To minimize} & \E \mathbf 1_{ y \neq h(x') } \\
  \text{Where} & x' = \argmax\limits_{ x' } \underset{\text{utility}}{h(x')} - \underset{\text{cost}}{c(x,x')}
\end{array} \]

It is a Stackelberg game: the solution is an equilibrium.
Adversarial learning assumes an (exogenous) distribution shift
\[
