If the subject is close to the decision boundary,
it will move to be exactly on it, but if it is too far away ,
it will not move.

Replace the hinge loss with a strategic hinge
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.4,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=8]
  \draw[->] (-2,0) -- (2.5,0);
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3);
  \draw[densely dotted] (-2,3) -- (1,0);
  \draw[very thick,blue!50] (-2,3) -- (-1,2);
  \draw[very thick,blue!50] (-1,1) -- (0,1) -- (1,0) -- (2,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad
\qquad
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.4,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=8]
  \draw[->] (-2,0) -- (2.5,0);
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3);
  \draw[densely dotted] (-2,3) -- (1,0);
  \draw[very thick,blue!50] (-2,3) -- (-1,2);
  \draw[very thick,blue!50] (-1,1) -- (0,1) -- (1,0) -- (2,0);
  \draw[very thick,red!50] (-2,1) -- (-1,0) -- (2,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
which penalizes points, not where they are, but where they could be.

Strategic ML is not necessarily adversarial:
for instance, in a recommendation system,
both users and the system want the user to be correctly classified.

If the users do not know the model exactly, their response is smooth.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.7,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=8]
  \draw[->] (-2,0) -- (2,0);
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,1.5);
