  B. Ustun and C. Rudin 
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Interpretable machine learning\\ based on functional Anova framework:\\ algorithms and comparison}\\
  L. Hu et al. (2023)
\end{book}
There are many generalizations of GAM:
\begin{align*}
  &\text{GAM} & g(x) &= g_1(x_1) + \cdots + g_n(x_n) \\
  &\text{AIM} & g(x) &= g_1(\beta_1 ' x) + \cdots + g_n(\beta_n'x) \\
  &\text{GA$^2$M} & g(x) &= \sum g_i(x_i) + \sum g_{ij}(x_i,x_j)
\end{align*}
(GA$^2$M is also known as fANOVA).

Explainable boosting machines (EBM) are similar to GA$^2$M, but the $g_i$'s and $g_{ij}$'s are decision trees:
one first fits the first order effects $g_i$,
and only then the second order effects $g_{ij}$ on the residuals,
but only for the top $k$ interactions,
identified by comparing 4 quadrants.

GAMI-Lin-T (GAM with interactions and linear trees)
are similar to EBM, but:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The leaves contain linear functions;
\item For the interactions, one variable is used only for splitting, and the other one is only used in the linear models, so we have two different terms $g_{ij}$ and $g_{ji}$;
\item The top-$k$ interactions are selected by comparing the predictive power (on the residuals) of the model trees;
\item There is an additional ``purifying'' step, to ensure orthogonality.
\end{compactitem}
\begin{align*}
  \bigl\langle
  g_{ij}(x_i,x_j) , g_i(x_i)
  \bigr\rangle &= 0 \\
  \bigl\langle
  g_{ij}(x_i,x_j) , g_j(x_j)
  \bigr\rangle &= 0
\end{align*}

GAMINet is similar but uses neural nets for $g_i$ and $g_{ij}$,
with a ``clarity'' penalty to ensure orthogonality
\[ \Omega_{jk} = \va{
  \dfrac1N \sum_{i \in \text{Observations}} g_j(x_{ij}) g_{jk}( x_{ij}, x_{ik} )
} \]
(and a monotonicity penalty if desired).

