\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Linear iterative feature embedding:\\ an ensemble framework for interpretable model}\\
  A. Sudjianto et al. 
\end{book}
A wide, single-layer neural net can be seen as an ensemble
of narrow single-layer nets:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take $k$ random neural nets
  \[ \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl} \RR^d & \longrightarrow & \RR \\ x & \longmapsto & \hat y \end{array} \right. \]
\item Use them to define samples of the data
  $\{ i : \hat y > a \}$
  (or $\hat y < a$);
  discard samples too large or too small;
\item Train new neural nets on those samples (to learn features);
\item Iterate a few times
\item Combine the features thus learned (elastic net).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An optimization approach\\ to learning falling rule lists}\\
  C. Chen and C. Rudin (2018)
\end{book}
Monte Carlo search on frequent itemsets.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Counterexample guided learning\\ of monotonic neural networks}\\
  A. Sivaraman et al.
\end{book}
For each input sample $x$, find (with an SMT solver)
the worst monotonicity counterexamples
in each direction:
\begin{align*}
  & x_\ell \geq x \ \text{ with } \ f(x_\ell) \leq f(x) \\
  & x_u   \leq x \ \text{ with } \ f(x_u) \geq f(x)  
\end{align*}
These define the lower and upper envelopes of $f$.

Use them during training:
(every other iteration)
replace each sample $(x_i,y_i)$ with
$(x_i,\hat y_i)$,
$(x_{i\ell},\hat y_i)$,
$(x_{iu},\hat y_i)$,
where $\hat y_i = \tfrac12 \bigl[
f(x_{i\ell}) + f(x_{iu})
\bigr]$.

