  \emph{On inductive biases for machine learning\\ in data-constrained settings}\\
  G. Mialon (2022)
\end{book}
To approximate a kernel $K$, use
\begin{align*}
  K(x,x') &\approx \bigl\langle \psi(x) , \psi(x') \bigr\rangle \\
  \psi(x) &= K(Z,Z)^{-1/2} K(Z,x)
\end{align*}
where $Z$ is a set of ``anchoring points''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A trainable optimal transport embedding\\ for feature aggregation and\\ its relationship to attention}\\
  G. Mialon et al. (2021)
\end{book}
To get a representation of (varying length) sequences $x$,
encoded as sets of $k$-mers with positional embeddings,
compute the optimal transport $P$
(wrt some kernel) to a learned fixed-length sequence $z$, and use the optimal transport as weights:
$ (x_i)_i \mapsto ( \sum_i P_{ij} z_j )_j. $

You could use several reference sequences.
This is a pooling operation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GraphiT:\\ encoding graph structure in transformers}\\
  G. Mialon et al. (2021)
\end{book}
To use transformers with graphs:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use a masked transformer (so that each node only attends to its $k$-hop neighbours) -- or not;
\item Use the eigenvectors of the Laplacian as positional encoding, $L = \sum \lambda_i u_i u_i'$;
\item Use a graph kernel to compute the positional encoding;
  \begin{align*}
    k_r &= \sum r(\lambda) u_i u_i' \\
    r(\lambda) &= e^{ - \beta \lambda } && \text{diffusion} \\
    r(\lambda) &= (1-\gamma\lambda)^p && \text{$p$-step random walk}
  \end{align*}
\item Rescale the attention weights: $\exp( Q Q' / \sqrt d ) \odot k_r$.
\end{compactitem}

