\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pretaining and the lasso}\\
  E. Craig et al.
\end{book}
Pretrain the lasso on a large dataset
\[ y = C \beta_1' + \epsilon \qquad \beta_1 \text{ sparse} \]
then, fine-tune it on the subset of interest (stratification)
or on a different, smaller dataset
\[ y - X \beta_1' = X \beta_2 ' + \eta \qquad \beta_2 \text{ sparse} \]
where the lhs, the residuals of the first model,
can be replaced by $y - (1-\alpha) X \beta_1'$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation and inference of heterogeneous treatment effects using random forests}\\
  S. Wager and S. Athey (2017)
\end{book}
Under unconfoundedness
$\{ Y_i^{(0)} , Y_i^{(1)} \} \indep T_i \,|\, X_i $,
\ie, $X$ are the only confounders
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.6,yscale=.7,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (X) at (1,1) {$X$};
  \node (T) at (0,0) {$T$};
  \node (Y) at (2,0) {$Y$};
  \draw[->] (X) -- (T);
  \draw[->] (X) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (T) -- (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

the treatment effect
$\tau(X) = \E [ Y_i^{(1)} - Y_i^{(0)} | X_i = x ] $
can be computed as
\[ \tau(x) = \E \left[
  Y_i \left( \dfrac{ T_i }{ e(x) } - \dfrac{ 1 - T_i }{ 1 - e(x) } \right) \middle| X_i = x
  \right] \]
where $e(x) = E[ T_i | X_i = x ]$
is the propensity of receiving the treatment.

\emph{Causal forests} directly estimate $\tau$
without estimating $e$ first,
by noticing that if the leaves of an (honest) tree are small enough,
they look like randomized experiments
and the treatment effect
can be estimated with unweighted averages
\[ \hat \tau(x) =
\operatorname*{Mean}_{\substack{ i \in L \\ T_i = 1 }} Y_i -
\operatorname*{Mean}_{\substack{ i \in L \\ T_i = 0 }} Y_i 
\]
where $L$ is the leaf containing $x$.

There are two ways of building honest trees:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A \emph{propensity tree} is built without using the outcome $Y$,
  to predict the treatment $T$, ensuring that each leaf contains at least $k$ observations for each treatment;
\item A \emph{double sample tree} is built using half the data,
  to forecast the outcome $Y$,
  but the responses are computed using the other half.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HEBO: Heteroskedastic evolutionary\\ Bayesian optimization}\\
  A.I. Cowen-Rivers et al.
\end{book}
For Bayesian optimization on heteroskedastic non-stationary data:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use an input-warped
  $\Psi(x) = 1 - ( 1 - x^a )^{b-1} $ GP
  (linear + Mat\'ern 3/2, from \texttt{GPy})
\item Power-transform (Box-Cox or Yeo-Johnson, in \texttt{sk\-learn}) the output;
\item Add noise to the posterior mean;
\item Use multiple acquisition functions
  (NSGA-II, from \texttt{pymoo}).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unsupervised learning of visual features\\ by contrasting cluster assignments}\\
  M. Caron et al.
\end{book}
SwAV computes image features by minimizing
$\ell( z_t, q_s ) + \ell( z_s, q_t )$, where
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $x_t$ and $x_s$ are augmentations of the same image;
\item $z = f_\theta(x) / \n{ f_\theta(x) } $ are the corresponding features; 
\item $c_1,\dots,c_k$ are (trainable) prototype vectors;
\item $q = \argmax\limits_{ c \in \{c_1,\dots,c_k\} } \langle c, z \rangle$;
\end{compactitem}

