\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Models tell you what to discard:\\ adaptive KV cache compression for LLMs}\\
  S. Ge et al. (2024)
\end{book}
The KV cache mechanism stores previous key-value vectors to avoid recomputing them each time.
Recognize the (head-specific) attention patterns:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Local context;
  special tokens (punctuation, etc.); 
\item Column-sparse;
\item Dense.
\end{compactitem}
and discard unneeded tokens.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Vision transformers need registers}\\
  T. Darcet et al.
\end{book}
Attention maps of ViT are not informative: tokens in low-information regions (e.g., sky)
appear important -- they are used for internal computations.
Provide additional tokens (``registers''),
neither in the input nor in the output (contrary to the \textsc{cls} token): the attention maps become interpretable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Beyond Weisfeiler-Lehman: a quantitative framework for GNN expressiveness}\\
  B. Zhang et al. (2024)
\end{book}
Given a GNN model $\mathscr M$,
computing graph representations
$G \mapsto \mathscr M(G)$,
and a substructure (a graph) $F$,
$\mathscr M$ can count $F$ under homomorphism if
\[ \forall G, H \ \mathscr M(G) = \mathscr M(H)
\Leftrightarrow
\bigl| \hom(F,G) \bigr| =
\bigl| \hom(F,H) \bigr|. \]

The homomorphism expressivity of $\mathscr M$
is the set $\mathscr F ( \mathscr M)$
of all substructures it can count.
For instance:
\begin{align*}
  &\mathscr F( \text{MPNN} ) = \{ \text{forests} \} \\
  &\mathscr F( \text{subgraph GNN} ) = \{ F \, :\, F \text{ has an end-point-shared NED} \} \\
  &\mathscr F( \text{local 2-GNN}  ) = \{ F \, :\, F \text{ has a strong NED} \} \\
  &\mathscr F( \text{local 2-FGNN} ) = \{ F \, :\, F \text{ has an almost strong NED} \} \\
  &\mathscr F( \text{2-FGNN}       ) = \{ F \, :\, F \text{ has a NED} \}
\end{align*}

A \emph{nested ear decomposition} (NED) is:
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=4,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0,
        every node/.style={circle,fill,black}
  ]
\node (A) at (0,0) {};
\node (B) at (1,0) {};
\node (E) at (.5,.1) {};
\draw[thick,red!80!black] (A) to node[pos=.3] (C) {} node[pos=.9] (D) {} node[anchor=north,fill=none]{\footnotesize\color{red!80!black}1} (1,0);
\draw[thick,blue!80!black] (A) to[out=90,in=90, looseness=1.8] node[anchor=south,fill=none]{\footnotesize\color{blue!80!black}2}(C);
\draw[thick,blue!80!black] (D) to[out=90,in=60,looseness=1.5] node[pos=.4] (F) {} node [pos=.75] (G) {} node[anchor=north,fill=none,pos=.575]{\footnotesize\color{blue!80!black}3} (E);
\draw[thick,green!60!black] (A) to[out=90,in=90,looseness=1.8] node[pos=.45] (H) {} node[pos=.55] (I) {} node[anchor=east,pos=.2,inner sep=2pt,fill=none]{\footnotesize\color{green!60!black}4} (B);
\draw[thick,red!80!black] (E) to[out=140,in=125,looseness=1.8] node[anchor=south,fill=none]{\footnotesize\color{red!80!black}5}(G);
\draw[thick,green!60!black] (G) to[out=100,in=70,looseness=1.5] node[anchor=south,fill=none]{\footnotesize\color{green!60!black}6} (F);
\draw[thick,black] (H) to[out=100,in=80,looseness=1.5] node[anchor=south,fill=none]{\footnotesize 7} (I);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

A \emph{subgraph GNN} applies a MPNN to all marked subgraphs
$\{\!\{ G^u \ : \ u \in V_G \}\!\}$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GNNCert: deterministic certification\\ of graph neural networks\\ against adversarial perturbations}\\
  Z. Xia et al.
\end{book}
Divide the graph (the set of edges and/or features) into (non-overlapping) subgraphs;
use a graph classifier on each of them, and take the majority vote:
this is robust to a bounded number of edge or feature perturbations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph neural networks for learning equivariant representations of neural networks}\\
  M. Kofinas et al.
\end{book}
Neural networks are computation graphs:
they can be processed with GNNs or transformers,
which automatically account for their permutation symetries.

Applications:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Implicit neural representation (INR) classification;
\item Predicting the generalization performance of a CNN classifier (example: models from the small CNN zoo -- same architecture (not required), different weights);
\item Improving the weights of a neural net (learning to optimize),
  given weights, gradients, and momentum, at several scales.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Self-RAG: learning to retrieve, generate,\\ and critique through self-reflection}\\
  A. Asai et al.
\end{book}
Ask the LLM if they need RAG,
if the retrieved documents are relevant,
if the generated answer is consistent with these documents.
Use special tokens,
\textsc{Retrieve}, \textsc{IsRelevant}, \textsc{Supported}, etc.
(ask an LLM to add those tokens to the data).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Knowledge card: filling LLM's knowledge gaps with plugin specialized language models}\\
  S. Feng et al. (2024)
\end{book}
Train several small, domain-specific LLMs,
and use the text they generate instead of RAG:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bottom-up: generate text from the query for all of them, refine those texts,
  and ask an LLM to use them to answer the query;
\item Top-down: first, ask the LLM if it needs more data, and which specialized LLMs to use.
\end{compactitem}
The documents generated by those specialized LLMs are preprocessed for relevance, brevity and factuality:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Check relevance with the cosine similarity with the query, only keeping the top $k$;
\item Ensure brevity by summarizing the documents
\item Check the sumary is consistent with the original text;
\item Check that it is factually correct using RAG.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Self-alignment\\ with instruction back-translation}\\
  X. Li et al.
\end{book}
To generate instructions to fine-tune an LLM, start with the answers (good quality web pages)
and ask the LLM to produce instructions that would lead to those answers;
also ask the LLM to select high-quality examples. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Amortizing intractable inference\\ in large language models}\\
  E.J. Hu et al.
\end{book}
Given an LLM, we may want to sample from related distributions, e.g.:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Infilling: $q( B | A, C ) \propto p_{\text{LM}} (ABC )$;
\item Tempered sampling: $q(B|A) \propto p_{\text{LM}}(AB)^{1/T}$;
\item Constrained generation: $q(A) \propto p_{\text{LM}}(A) c(A)$.
\end{compactitem}
This can be done with \emph{GFlowNets},
a diversity-seeking reinforcement learning algorithm,
training a policy to sample tokens from an unnormalized density.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved techniques\\ for training consistency models}\\
  Y. Song and P. Dhariwal (2024)
\end{book}
Diffusion models learn a denoising function
\[ ( x_{ \sigma + \Delta \sigma } , \sigma + \Delta \sigma ) \mapsto x_\sigma . \]
Consistency models directly learn
\[ f_\theta: (x_\sigma, \sigma) \mapsto x_0 \]
by minimizing (a weighted expectation of)
\[ d \bigl[ f_\theta( x_{\sigma_{i+1}} ) , f_{\theta^-}( \breve x_{ \sigma_i } ) \bigr] \]
where $\theta^- = \text{EWMA}( \theta )$,
$\breve x = x - \sigma \cdot \Delta \sigma \cdot \nabla_{\!x} \log p_\sigma(x)$
and the score function $\nabla_{\!x} \log p_\sigma(x)$
comes from a diffusion model
(consistency distillation).
Instead, consistency training uses
$\breve x = x + \sigma z$, 
$z \sim N(0,I)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{W\"urstchen: an efficient architecture\\ for large-scale text-to-image diffusion models}\\
  P. Pertinas et al. (2024)
\end{book}
3-stage latent diffusion model (LDM):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A first LDM computes a very low-dimensional latent representation of the image,
  conditioned on the input text;
\item A second LDM computes a low-dimensional latent representation of the image,
  conditioned on the output of the previous stage;
\item Finally, a VQGAN decoder generates the full-resolution image.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lipschitz singularities in diffusion models}\\
  Z. Yang et al.
\end{book}
Diffusion models are not Lipschitz at $\sigma=0$.
E-TSDM replaces
$f: (x_t,t) \mapsto x_{t - \Delta t}$ with
\[ \tilde f: (x_t, t) \longmapsto
\begin{cases}
  f( x_t,t) & \text{if }  t > \tilde t \\
  f( x_t, \tilde t ) & \text{if } t \leq \tilde t
\end{cases}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalization in diffusion models arises from geometry-adaptive harmonic representations}\\
  Z. Kadkhodaie et al.
\end{book}
Diffusion models trained on sufficiently large ($10^5$) non-overlapping datasets
learn the same score function and generate similar images:
they do not memorize their training data.

They have a GAHB bias.
A (bias-free) denoiser is locally linear:
$f(y) \approx \nabla f \cdot y$;
its Jacobian is approximately symmetric (and psd),
so it has an eigen decomposition
$f(y) \approx \sum_k \lambda_k(y) \langle y , e_k(y) \rangle e_k(y). $
The denoiser performs some shrinkage in that (data-dependent) basis;
this basis shows oscillating patterns.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving convergence and generalization using parameter symmetries}\\
  B. Zhao et al.
\end{book}
In the optimization problem
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ g \in \RR^d } \ell(w), \]
if the loss $\ell$ is invariant under the action of some group $G$,
\[ \forall g \in G \quad \forall w \in \RR^d \quad \ell( g \cdot w ) = \ell(w) , \]
\emph{symmetry teleportation} is a variant of gradient descent looking for the point
with the steepest gradient in the current orbit,
\begin{align*}
  w &\leftarrow g \cdot w \\
  g &= \argmax_g \va{ \nabla \ell (g \cdot w ) }
\end{align*}
Teleporting towards larger curvatures may improve generalization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Symmetry teleportation\\ for accelerated optimization }\\
  B. Zhao et al.
\end{book}
<<python-optimization-teleportation,engine='python',results='hide',echo=FALSE,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from matplotlib.patches import Ellipse

def add_ellipse( V, mu, radius = 1, ax = None, color = 'tab:blue', linewidth = 3 ):
  if isinstance( V, pd.DataFrame ):
    V = V.values
  if isinstance( mu, pd.Series ):
    mu = mu.values
  else:
    mu = np.array( mu )
  D, U = np.linalg.eigh(V)
  D = np.clip( D, 0, np.inf )
  sigma = np.sqrt(D)
  theta = - np.arctan2( U[1,0], U[0,0] )
  e = Ellipse(
    xy = mu,
    width  = radius * sigma[0],
    height = radius * sigma[1],
    angle = - np.rad2deg( theta ),
  )
  e.set_color( color )
  e.set_linewidth( linewidth )
  e.set_facecolor( 'none' )
  if ax is not None:
    ax.add_artist(e)
  return e

V = np.array([[4,0],[0,.5]])
mu = np.zeros(shape=2)

def f(x):
  x = x.reshape(-1,1)
  return np.squeeze( x.T @ np.linalg.inv(V) @ x )

def g(f, x0, epsilon=1e-6):
  e1 = np.array([epsilon,0])
  e2 = np.array([0,epsilon])
  g1 = ( f(x0+e1) - f(x0-e1) ) / ( 2*epsilon )
  g2 = ( f(x0+e2) - f(x0-e2) ) / ( 2*epsilon )
  return np.array( [ g1, g2 ] )

scale = .8
fig, axs = plt.subplots( 1, 3, figsize = (scale*12,scale*1.8), layout = 'constrained' )
for ax in axs:
  for radius in np.linspace(.5,3,6):
    add_ellipse( V, mu, 2*radius, ax=ax, linewidth=2 )
  ax.set_aspect(1)
  ax.set_xlim(-6.1,6.1)
  ax.set_ylim(-2.3,2.3)
  ax.axis('off')

ax = axs[0]
C = np.linalg.cholesky(np.linalg.inv(V))
u = np.array([-3,3])
u = u / np.sqrt( np.sum(u**2) )
x0 = 3 * np.linalg.solve( C.T, u )
ax.scatter( x0[0], x0[1], color = 'tab:orange' )
for _ in range(10):
  g0 = g(f, x0)
  x1 = x0 - .4 * g0
  ax.scatter( x1[0], x1[1], color = 'tab:orange' )
  ax.plot( [x0[0],x1[0]], [x0[1],x1[1]], color = 'tab:orange', linewidth = 3 )
  x0 = x1

ax = axs[1]
u = np.array([-3,3])
u = u / np.sqrt( np.sum(u**2) )
x0 = 3 * np.linalg.solve( C.T, u )
ax.scatter( x0[0], x0[1], color = 'tab:orange' )
x1 = np.zeros(shape=2)
ax.scatter( x1[0], x1[1], color = 'tab:orange' )
ax.plot( [x0[0],x1[0]], [x0[1],x1[1]], color = 'tab:orange', linewidth = 3 )

ax = axs[2]
u = np.array([-3,3])
u = u / np.sqrt( np.sum(u**2) )
x0 = 3 * np.linalg.solve( C.T, u )
ax.scatter( x0[0], x0[1], color = 'tab:orange' )
u = np.array([0,1])
x1 = 3 * np.linalg.solve( C.T, u )
ax.scatter( x1[0], x1[1], color = 'tab:orange' )
ax.plot( [x0[0],x1[0]], [x0[1],x1[1]], color = 'tab:orange', linestyle = ':', linewidth = 3 )
x0 = x1
for _ in range(3):
  g0 = g(f, x0)
  x1 = x0 - .2 * g0
  ax.scatter( x1[0], x1[1], color = 'tab:orange' )
  ax.plot( [x0[0],x1[0]], [x0[1],x1[1]], color = 'tab:orange', linewidth = 3 )
  x0 = x1

fig.savefig("tmp/python-optimization-teleportation.pdf")
plt.show()
@
\hbox to \hsize{\hss \clap{\footnotesize Gradient} \hss \hss \clap{\footnotesize Newton} \hss \hss \clap{\footnotesize Teleportation} \hss}

Linear neural nets are invariant under an action of $\text{GL}_{d_m}$:
\begin{align*}
  & g \cdot W_m = W_m g^{-1} \\
  & g \cdot W_{m-1} = g W_{m-1} \\
  & g \cdot W_k = W_k \ \text{ if } k \not\in \{ m, m-1 \}.
\end{align*}
This can be generalized to neural nets with invertible nonlinearities (e.g., leaky ReLU),
\begin{align*}
  & g \cdot W_{m-1}  = \sigma^{-1} \bigl[ g \sigma ( W_{m-1} h_{m-2} ) \bigr] h_{m-2}^{-1} \\
  & h_{m-1} \mapsto W_m h_{m-1} \mapsto \sigma( W_m h_{m-1} )
\end{align*}
(if $h_{m-2}$ is square and invertible);
note that the action depends on the data.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Symmetries, flat minima,\\ and the conserved quantities of gradient flow}\\
  B. Zhao et al.
\end{book}
Given a 2-layer neural net
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.9,yscale=.9,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (x) at (0,0) {$x$};
  \node[draw] (V) at (1,0) {$V$};
  \node[draw] (s) at (2,0) {$\sigma\vphantom{V}$};
  \node[draw] (U) at (3,0) {$U$};
  \node (F) at (4.2,0) {$F(x)$};
  \draw[->] (x) -- (V);
  \draw[->] (V) -> (s);
  \draw[->] (s) -> (U);
  \draw[->] (U) -> (F);
  \node (a) at (0,-.4) {\footnotesize$\RR^n$};
  \node (b) at (2,-.4) {\footnotesize$\RR^h$};
  \node (c) at (4,-.4) {\footnotesize$\RR^m$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
(for which $\forall z\ \sigma(z) \neq0$, e.g., $\sigma=\text{sigmoid}$),
there is a (data-dependent) action of
$\text{GL}_h$ on $\text{Param}\times\RR^n$
\[ g \cdot ( U, V, x ) = ( U R_{ \sigma(Vx) } R^{-1} _{ \sigma( g V x ) } , g V , x ) \]
where
\[ ( R_z )_{ij} = \begin{cases}
  z_i \cos ( \alpha_{j-1} ) \left( \prod_{ k < j } \sin \alpha_k \right) & \text{if } j \leq i \\
  - r \sin \alpha_i &\text{if } j = i+1 \\
  0 &\text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
and $(r, \alpha_1, \dots,\alpha_{h-1})$ are the spherical coordinates of $z$
\[ z_i = r \cos \alpha_i \prod_{k<i} \sin \alpha_i . \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{InfoBatch: lossless training speed up\\ by unbiased dynamic data pruning}\\
  Z. Qin et al.
\end{book}
Data pruning (filtering out samples contributing little to training)
leads to biased gradients.
Use dynamic data pruning instead:
keep track of the loss of each sample, randomly drop some proportion of the low-loss samples (anew for each epoch)
and rescale the gradients of the remaining low-loss samples;
for the last few epochs, use the whole dataset.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards a statistical theory of data selection under weak supervision}\\
  G. Kolossov et al.
\end{book}
To select on which data to train:
\begin{align*}
  \pi_i &= \min\{ c \cdot ( \var \hat Y_i ) ^\alpha ; 1 \} \\
  w_i &= 1 \text{ or } 1/\pi_i
\end{align*}
where
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit a model on a small subset of the data
  and compute $\hat Y_i$ from this surrogate model
  (better surrogates need not be useful);
\item $\alpha<0$ prefers easy samples (better if you want a small number of samples);
\item $\alpha>0$ prefers hard samples; 
\item $c$ is chosen to have the desired number of samples;
\item $w_i = 1/\pi_1$ leads to unbiased (but possibly worse) results.
\end{compactitem}
Use cross-validation to choose the surrogate model, $\alpha$ and $w$ --
it will depend on the dimensionality of the problem, on the model,
and on the proportion of the data you want to keep.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Meta continual learning revisited:\\ implicitly enhancing\\ online Hessian approximation\\ via variance reduction}\\
  Y. Wu et al.
\end{book}
Regularization-based continual learning uses updates of the form
\[ \theta \leftarrow \theta - \alpha ( H^1 + \cdots + H^{k-1} )^{-1}
\nabla_\theta \mathscr L ^k (\theta) \]
where $H^i$ is the Hessian of the loss at the end of raining for task $i$
(e.g., the diagonal Fisher information matrix)
to keep the weights that mattered for task $i$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Topological data analysis\\ on noisy quantum computers}\\
  I.Y. Akhalwaya et al.
\end{book}
Quantum computers do linear algebra in exponentially large vector spaces.
This is what TDA needs (computing the rank of an exponentially large matrix).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cameras as rays:\\ pose estimation via ray diffusion}\\
  J.Y. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
A camera is typically parametrized as $(R,t,k) \in \text{SO}(3) \times \RR^3 \times \RR^{3\times3}$
\[ \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl}
  \text{World} & \longrightarrow & \text{Pixels} \\
  x & \longmapsto & u = k[ R | T ] x.
\end{array} \right.
\]
Instead, model a camera as a collection of rays
$(r_1,\dots,r_m)$
associated to pixel coordinates
$(u_1,\dots,u_m)$ (patches).
The ray in direction $d \in \RR^3$ through point $p \in \RR^3$
can be represented with Pl\"ucker coordinates
\[ (d, m = p \times d ) \in \RR^3 \times \RR^3  \]
(actually $\PP(\RR^6)$: the Pl\"ucker embedding is
$\text{Gr}_k V \hookrightarrow \PP( \Lambda^k V )$,
here with $k=2$, $\dim V = 4$ -- lines in 3-dimensional projective space
are 2-dimensional linear subspaces in 4-dimensional space).
To convert from cameras to rays:
\begin{align*}
  d &= R' K^{-1} u \\
  m &= (-R' t) \times d
\end{align*}
To convert from rays to cameras:
first, find the camera center,
$ c = \argmin_p \sum \n{ p \times d - m } ^ 2$,
then the camera transformation
$ P = \argmin_{ \n H = 1 } \sum \n{ Hd \times u } $.
Train a transformer (with positional encoding) to predict rays from patch features or,
better if there is ambiguity because the data is too sparse, a diffusion model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Accelerating distributed stochastic optimization via self-repellent random walks}\\
  J. Hu et al.
\end{book}

Given a time-reversible Markov chain with transition kernel $P$
and stationary distribution $\mu$,
the \emph{self-repellent random walk} (SRRW)
\[ K_{ij}(x) \propto P_{ij} \left( \dfrac{ x_i }{ \mu_j } \right)^{-\alpha}, \]
where $x_j$ is the number of visits to state $j$, has a smaller asymptotic variance
and converges faster.

The \emph{token algorithm} is a decentralized SGD,
implemented using a random walk (e.g., SRRW) on a graph,
each node updating $\theta$ using its local gradient and sending the new value to one of its neighbours. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Self-repellent random walks on general graphs: achieving minimal sampling variance\\ via nonlinear Markov chains}\\
  V. Doshi et al. (2023)
\end{book}
% Original paper on SRRW

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic heavy ball}\\
  S. Gadat et al. (2016)
\end{book}
To find the minimum of a function $f$, the (deterministic)
\emph{heavy ball} method (HBF, heavy ball with friction)
considers a ball, moving on the graph of $f$, subject to both damping $\gamma$ and acceleration
\[ \ddot x_t + \gamma_t \dot x_t + \nabla f(x_t ) = 0. \]
It converges towards a minimum of $f$ if
\begin{align*}
  &\int_0^\infty \gamma_s ds = \infty \\
  &\int_0^\infty \exp \left( - \int_0^t \gamma_s ds \right) dt < \infty ,
\end{align*}
e.g., if
$\gamma_t = r/t$ ($r>1$)
or
$\gamma_t = \gamma > 0$.

It can be discretized
\begin{align*}
  y_{t+1} &= (1-\gamma_t) y_t - \nabla f(x_t) \\
  x_{t+1} &= x_t + y_{t+1} 
\end{align*}
and one can replace the gradient $\nabla f(x_t)$ with an approximation
(computated on a different minibatch each time).
% The formulas in the paper are different.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding in-context learning\\ in transformers and LLMs\\ by learning to learn discrete functions}\\
  S. Bhattamishra et al.
\end{book}
In-context learning (ICL) is the task of forecasting $y$ from $x$ after seeing a handful of examples.
\[ (x_1,y_1,x_2,y_2,\dots,x_n,y_n,x_{n+1}) \mapsto y_{n+1} \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ReLU strikes back: exploiting activation sparsity in large language models}\\
  I. Mirazadeh et al.
\end{book}
ReLU (contrary to the trendier GELU or SiLU)
activation functions lead to sparse activations,
and a significant reduction of CPU-GPU data transfers,
with a negligible impact on convergence and performance.
For pretrained models, replace their activation functions with ReLU and fine-tune them.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unprocessing seven years\\ of algorithmic fairness}\\
  A.F. Cruz and M. Hardt
\end{book}
Experimental evidence suggests that
processing a model to equalize the error rates in different demographic groups
is Pareto-optimal:
varying the group-specific acceptance threshold yields the fairness-accuracy frontier.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the joint interaction\\ of models, data and features}\\
  Y. Jiang et al. (2024)
\end{book}
According to the GDE (generalization disagreement equality),
the (expected) agreement in a deep ensemble
(an ensemble of randonly initialized networks,
on unlabeled data)
equals its (expected) test accuracy -- but this only holds for \emph{calibrated} ensembles.

The agreement can be modeled by a tensor
\[ \Omega \in \{0,1\} ^{ \# \text{models} \times \# \text{samples} \times \# \text{features} } \]
built as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit $M$ classification models;
\item For each of them, define ``features'' as the principal components of its penultimate layer;
\item Cluster the features (greedy algorithm for $k$-partite matching) attemping to put in the same cluster
  principal components whose correlation is above some threshold;
\item Match features to observations.
\end{compactitem}
Experiments suggest that the agreement,
computed from that tensor,
is very close to the test accuracy,
even for non-calibrated ensembles. 
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Assessing generalization via disagreement}\\
  Y. Jiang et al.
\end{book}
Given a well-calibrated ensemble of classification models
(same model, trained with a stochastic algorithm and different random seeds),
the disagreement rate
(on unseen, unlabeled data)
equals the test error (in expectation).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistically optimal $k$-means clustering\\ via nonnegative low-rank\\ semidefinite programming}\\
  Y. Zhuang et al.
\end{book}
The $k$-means problem
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ \beta_1,\dots,\beta_k \in \RR^p }
\sum_{i=1}^n \min_{ k \in \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket } \n{ X_i - \beta_k }_2^2 \]
is NP-complete and often solved with heuristics or relaxations:
Lloyd's algorithm, spectral clustering, NMF, SDP.
The problem can be reformulated as
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{ G_1 \sqcup \cdots \sqcup G_k = \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket}
\sum_k \dfrac1{ \va{ G_k } } \sum_{ i,j \in G_k } \langle X_i, X_j \rangle 
\]
or
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_{\substack{
    H \in \{0,1\} ^{ n \times k } \\
    H \mathbf 1_k = \mathbf 1_n
}} \langle A , H B H^\top \rangle
\]
where
\begin{align*}
  A &= - X' X \\
  B &= \text{diag} \bigl( \va{G_1}^{-1} , \dots , \va{ G_k }^{-1} \bigr) 
\end{align*}

The SDP relaxation looks for $Z = H B H^\top$ instead:
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ Z \succcurlyeq 0 } \langle A, Z \rangle
\ \text{ st }\ 
\tr Z = k, \ Z \mathbf 1_n = \mathbf 1_n, \ Z \geq 0 . \]
Further relax it by assuming $Z$ is low-rank,
$Z = U U'$
(and replacing $UU^\top \geq 0$ with the stronger $U \geq 0$)
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ U \in \RR^{n \times r} }
\langle A , U U^\top \rangle \text{ st }
\n{ U }_F^2 \!=\! k , \, U U^\top \!\mathbf 1_n \!=\! \mathbf 1_n, \, U \geq 0 \]
(this keeps most of the theoretical properties of the SDP relaxation).
The NMF relaxation did not have those equality constraints:
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ U \in \RR^{n\times r} } \n{ A + U U^\top }_F^2 \ \text{ st }\ U \geq 0. \]

