\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the humanity of conversational AI: evaluating the psychological portrayal of LLMs}\\
  J.T. Huang et al.
\end{book}
Comparison of the results of psychological tests for humans and LLMs:
LLMs lie more, and are more self-confident.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What data benefits my classifier?\\
  Enhancing model performance and interpretability through influence-based\\ data selection}\\
  A. Chhabra et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{influence} of a sample $(x_j,y_j)$
on some quantity of interest $f$ (loss, fairness, robustness, etc.)
is
\[ I(x_j) = \nabla_\theta f(\hat\theta) ^\top
H_{\hat\theta}^{-1}
\nabla_\theta\ell ( x_j, y_j; \hat\theta ) \]
where
\begin{align*}
  H_{\hat\theta} &= \dfrac1n \sum_i \nabla_\theta^2 \ell( x_i, y_i ; \hat\theta ) \\
  \hat\theta &= \argmin_\theta \dfrac1n \sum_i \ell( x_i, y_i; \theta ).
\end{align*}
To interpret it, train a decision tree
\[ (x_j, y_j) \longmapsto I(x_j). \]
Use \emph{hierarchical shrinkage} on the resulting tree.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hierarchical shrinkage:\\ improving the accuracy and\\ interpretability of tree-based methods}\\
  A. Agarwal et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Consider a decision tree $h$:
\begin{align*}
  & q \text{: input} \\
  & t_0 \text{: root} \\
  & t_n \text{: leaf containing } q \\
  & t_n \subset t_{n-1} \subset \cdots \subset t_0 \text{: leaf-to-root path} \\
  & \phi(t) \text{: number of sampled in node } t \\
  & \xi(q,t) \text{: output for $q$ in node $t$}.
\end{align*}
Its output
\[ h(q) = \xi(q,t_0) + \sum_{ j \geq 1 } \xi(q, t_j) - \xi(q,t_{j-1}) \]
can be shrunk to
\[ \hat h(q) = \xi(q,t_0) + \sum_{ j \geq 1 }
\dfrac{
  \xi(q, t_j) - \xi(q,t_{j-1})
}{
  1 + \lambda / \phi(t_{j-1})
}. \]
This alternative to pruning also applies to random forests:
it often improves accuracy and simplifies the decision boundaries.

Code in \texttt{imodels}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Never train from scratch: fair comparison\\ of long-sequence models\\ requires data-driven priors}\\
  I. Amos et al.
\end{book}
Do not train your models from a random initialization:
pretrain them, on the downstream data (\emph{self-pretraining}), with some denoising objective.

