Another causal discovery review paper.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic causal programming\\ for bounding treatment effects}\\
  K. Padh et al.
\end{book}
In presence of unobserved confounders,
auxilliary variables can help identify causal effects.
\[
\tikzset{
  observed/.style={circle,draw,text width=1em,align=center},
  hidden/.style={circle,draw,densely dotted},
}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.8,yscale=.8,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node at (1,-.5) {\footnotesize instrumental variable};
  \node at (4.5,-.5) {\footnotesize mediator};
  \node at (8,-.5) {\footnotesize proxy};
  \begin{scope}[every node/.style={observed}]
    \node (X) at (0,0) {$X$};
    \node (Y) at (2,0) {$Y$};
    \node (I) at (0,1) {$I$};
    \node[hidden] (Z) at (1,1) {$Z$};
    \draw[->] (I) -- (X);
    \draw[->] (Z) -- (X);
    \draw[->] (Z) -- (Y);
    \draw[->] (X) -- (Y);
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[
      every node/.style={observed},
      shift={(3.5,0)},
    ]
    \node (X) at (0,0) {$X$};
    \node (Y) at (2,0) {$Y$};
    \node (M) at (1,0) {$M$};
    \node[hidden] (Z) at (1,1) {$Z$};
    \draw[->] (Z) -- (X);
    \draw[->] (Z) -- (Y);
    \draw[->] (X) -- (M);
    \draw[->] (M) -- (Y);
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[
      every node/.style={observed},
      shift={(7,0)},
    ]
    \node (X) at (0,0) {$X$};
    \node (Y) at (2,0) {$Y$};
    \node (U) at (2,1) {$U$};
    \node[hidden] (Z) at (1,1) {$Z$};
    \draw[->] (Z) -- (X);
    \draw[->] (Z) -- (Y);
    \draw[->] (X) -- (Y);
    \draw[->] (Z) -- (U);
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Stochastic causal programming (SCP)
does not model the distribution of the unobserved confounders but,
for each variable $V$
\[
\tikzset{
  observed/.style={circle,draw,text width=1em,align=center},
  hidden/.style={circle,draw,densely dotted},
}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.3,yscale=.8,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node[observed,ellipse,text width = 2em] (P) at (0,0) {$\text{Pa}\,V\mathstrut$};
  \node[observed] (V) at (1,0) {$V$};
  \node[hidden] (Z) at (1,1) {$Z$};
  \draw[->] (P) -- (V);
  \draw[->] (Z) -- (V);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
the distribution over function $f_{z,V}$
\[ V = f_{z,V} ( \text{Pa}\, V ) \]
with basis functions,
and constraints (smoothness, maximum number of inflection points, etc.).
We can then compute the maximum and minimum of
$\E\bigl[ Y \,|\, \text{do}( X = x ) \bigr]$
over all plausible models.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A versatile causal discovery framework\\ to allow causally related hidden variables}\\
  X. Dong et al.
\end{book}
D-separation can be inferred by conditional independence tests.
\emph{T-separation} can be inferred by the rank of the (cross)covariance matrix
\[ \text{rank}\, \Sigma_{A,B} = \min\bigl\{
\va{ C_A} + \va{ C_B } \ :\ A \indep_{\!\!t} B \,|\, C_A , C_B 
\bigr\}. \]
It subsumes d-separation, and is more informative in presence of latent variables.
\[ A \indep_{\!\!d} B \,|\, C \text{\ \ iff \ \ } \text{rank}\, \Sigma_{A\union C, B \union C} = \va{C} \]
A \emph{trek} $X \overset{(P_1,P_2)}{\longrightarrow} Y$
is a pair of paths with the same source, and $X$ and $Y$ as respective sinks.
$A$ and $B$ are $t$-separated given $(C_A,C_B)$
if, for every trek $(P_1,P_2)$ from a node in $A$ to a node in $B$,
either $P_1 \inter C_A \neq \nothing$
or $P_2 \inter C_B \neq \nothing$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal inference for time series analysis: problems, methods and evaluation}\\
  R. Moraffah et al.
\end{book}
Causal discovery for time series often relies on (generalizations of) Granger causality,
the PC algorithm (PCMCI) or structural equation models (LiNGAM on VAR residuals).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Review of causal discovery methods\\ based on graphical models}\\
  C. Glymour  et al.
\end{book}
Clear explanation of the PC, FCI and LiNGAM algorithms.
% GES is mentioned with insufficient details

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to the foundations\\ of causal discovery}\\
  F. Eberhardt (2016)
\end{book}
Use a MaxSAT solver to combine background knowledge with the output of a causal discovery algorithm.

A random variable $(X_1,\dots,X_n)$ is \emph{Markov} wrt a graph $G$
if $\forall X \ X \!\indep\! \text{NonDesc}(X) \,|\,\text{Pa}(X)$;
this implies
\[ X \indep_{\!\!d} Y \,|\, C \implies X \indep Y \,|\, C. \]
It is \emph{faithful} to the graph if the converse holds:
\[ X \indep Y \,|\, C \implies X \indep_{\!\!d} Y \,|\, C \]
(\ie, the graph has no extra edges, and there are no cancellations).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal discovery on high-dimensional data}\\
  Z. Hao et al. (2014)
\end{book}
% CDHD
To find a causal graph in high dimension:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For each variable $y$, find its potential neighbours
  by using the max relevance min redundancy criterion
  \[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_S \sum_{ x \in S } I(y ; x ) - \dfrac1{\va X}
  \sum_{x, x' \in S } I(x; x'); \]
  greedily:
  \begin{align*}
    x_{k+1} &= \argmax_{ x \in X \setminus S_k } I(y;x) - \dfrac1k \sum_{ x' \in S_k } I(x;x') \\
    S_{k+1} &= S_k \union \{ x_{k+1} \}.
  \end{align*}
\item Use conditional independence tests ($\chi^2$, $G$ or kernel-based)
  to prune $S$ and only keep direct causes and consequences of $y$;
\item Orient the edges with entropy-based IGCI:
  if $x$ is more random than $y$, \ie, if $H(x) > H(y)$,
  then choose the direction $x \to y$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A causal feature selection algorithm\\ for stock prediction modeling}\\
  Z. Zhang et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Replace feature selection (lasso, etc.)
with \emph{causal feature selection}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Greedily add variables $x$ with large $I(x;y)$,
  unless they are independent of $x$ conditionally on the variables already selected;
\item Identify the causes of $y$ by noticing that $y$ is a collider for them.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CausalTime: realistically generated time series for benchmarking of causal discovery}\\
  Y. Cgeng et al.
\end{book}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit a non-linear VAR (NAR) (with noise modeled with a normalizing flow);
\item Define the parents of a variable $X_i$ as the variables
  with the largest feature importance (DeepShap);
\item Split the NAR into 3 terms: causal (from the identified parents), residual and noise
  \[ x_i = f_i( x \odot H ) + \bigl[ f_i(x) - f_i(x \odot H ) \bigr] + \epsilon_i  \]
  where $H$ is the adjacency matrix of the discovered parents;
\item Use the model to generate new data, of dimension $2N$,
  keeping (separately) both the causal and residual terms, with adjacency matrix
  \[ \begin{bmatrix} H & \mathbf 1 \\ I & 0 \end{bmatrix}. \]
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Beware of the simulated DAG! Causal discovery benchmarks may be easy to game}\\
  A.G. Reisach et al.
\end{book}
Sorting the variables by increasing variance
can recover a topological order of the causal graphs;
use the lasso (regressing each variable on its potential parents) to recover the graph.

If the data has been normalized, correlations tend to increase (decrease) in the causal (anti-causal) direction:
\[ i \to j \to k \qquad \cor( X_i,X_j) \leq \cor( X_j, X_k ). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Approximate kernel-based\\ conditional independence tests\\ for fast nonparametric causal discovery}\\
  E.V. Strobl et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{kernel conditional independence test} (KCIT)
can be approximated with random features
(randomized conditional independence test, RCIT):
\begin{align*}
  & H_0 : X \indep Y \,|\, Z \\
  & \ddot X = (X,Z) \\
  & \ddot A = \bigl[ f_1(\ddot X) , \dots , f_m(\ddot X) \bigr] \\
  & \ddot B = \bigl[ h_1(Y) , \dots , h_q(Y) \bigr] \\
  & \ddot C = \bigl[ g_1(Z) , \dots , g_d(Z) \bigr] \\
  & g_1(Z) =  \sqrt 2 \cos( W^\top Z + B ) \\
  & W \sim N(0,I) \\
  & B \sim \text{Unif}( 0, 2\pi ) \\
  &\text{idem for the $f_i$'s and $h_i$'s} \\
  & \hat \Sigma_{ \ddot A B \cdot C } =
  \hat\Sigma_{\ddot AB} - \hat\Sigma_{AC} \bigl( \hat\Sigma_{CC} + \gamma I \bigr)^{-1} \hat\Sigma_{CB} \\
  &\hphantom{ \hat \Sigma_{ \ddot A B \cdot C } } =
  \cov \bigl[  \text{res}( \ddot A \sim C ) , \text{res}( B \sim C ) \bigr] 
\end{align*}
(using ridge regression).

The \emph{randomized conditional correlation test} (RCoT) uses $X$ instead of $\ddot X$.
\[ \text{test statistic} = n \n{ \hat\Sigma_{ \ddot A B \cdot C } }_F^2 \]
(the distribution of the test statistic under $H_0$ is complicated:
linear combination of $\chi^2$ variables, with weights given by the eigenvalues of some covariance matrix).

% R implementation in RCIT: github, unmaintained, never on CRAN.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conditional independence testing\\ based on a nearest neighbour estimator\\ of conditional mutual information}\\
  J. Runge 
\end{book}
Conditional mutual information
\[ I(X ; Y | Z ) = H(X,Z) + H(Y,Z) - H(Z) - H(X,Y,Z) \]
can be estimated with the KL (Kozachenko-Leonenko) entropy estimator.
To estimate its (finite-sample) distribution under
\[ H_0: X \indep Y \,|\, Z \]
use random permutations, taking care to only destroy the relation between $X$ and $Y$,
while preserving that between $X$ and $Z$.

Python implementation in \texttt{tigramite}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Better simulations\\ for validating causal discovery\\ with the DAG-adaptation of the onion method}\\
  B. Andrews and E. Kummerfeld
\end{book}
The onion method samples from the space of correlation matrices
by building correlation matrices one dimension at a time.
\[ R_{i+1} = \begin{pmatrix} R_1 & r_{i+1} \\ r_{i+1} ' & 1 \end{pmatrix}
\qquad 1 - r_{i+1} ' R_i ^{-1} r_{i+1} < 1 \]
It can be modified to sample from correlation matrices compatible with a given DAG.
% Code available (MIT)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal discovery\\ from heterogeneous/nonstationary data}\\
  B. Huang et al.
\end{book}
CD-NOD is a PC-like causal discovery algorithm,
which deals with heterogeneous and/or nonstationary
data by adding a ``domain'' and/or ``time'' variable;
variables connected to them have changing causal mechanisms.
Assuming that the distribution shifts are due to a small number of variables
also helps identify some edge directions:
in the situation
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.6,yscale=.7,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (X) at (0,0) {$X$};
  \node (C) at (1,1) {$C$};
  \node (Y) at (2,0) {$Y,$};
  \draw[->] (C) -- (X);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (X) -- (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
if we see changes in
$P(X|Y)$ and $P(Y)$ and ($P(X)$ xor $P(Y|X)$),
we conclude that $X \to Y$.

% Implementation in causal-learn

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Amortized causal discovery: learning\\ to infer causal graphs from time series data}\\
  S. L\"owe et al.
\end{book}
Neurons (in different patients $s$) have the same dynamics, but different connectivity
(causal graphs) $G_s$
\[ \underline x _s^{t+1} = g( \underline x_s^{ \leq t } , G_s ). \]
Model them, jointly, as
\begin{align*}
  \hat G_s &= f_\phi( \underline x _s ) \\
  \underline x_s^{t+1} &\approx f_\theta ( \underline x_s^{\leq t } , \hat G_s ).
\end{align*}
Baselines:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Linear Granger causality;
\item Neural Granger causality, with MLP, LSTM, or eSRU (economy statistical recurrent unit);
\item MPIR (minimum predictive information regularization);
\item Transfer entropy;
\item Mutual information.
\end{compactitem}
Data: particles; phase-coupled oscillators; simulated fMRI (netsim).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural Granger causality}\\
  A. Tank et al.
\end{book}
Nonlinear Granger causality,
with a sparse-input MLP or an LSTM.
% Data: simulated gene expression data; motion capture

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Economy statistical recurrent units\\ for inferring nonlinear Granger causality}\\
  S. Khanna and V.Y.F. Tan
\end{book}
The statistical recurrent unit (SRU) is yet another RNN/GRU/LSTM variant,
with feedback.
Use it, componentwise, for nonlinear Granger causality testing:
the causal relations can be extracted from the model parameters.
Reduce the number of parameters by reducing the dimension of (some of) the latent space(s)
with sketching (fixed random projections), and add sparsifying penalties.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Discovering nonlinear relations with minimum predictive information regularization}\\
  T. Wu et al. 
\end{book}
To test for Granger causality with neural nets,
there is no need to try the variables one by one:
allow each $X_{j,t-1}$ to have learnable corruption $\tilde X_{j,t-1}$,
as much as possible (measure it with the mutual information between
$\tilde X_{j,t-1}$ and $X_{j,t-1}$)
while maintaining good prediction of $X_{it}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal structure learning\\ supervised by large language model}\\
  T. Ban et al.
\end{book}
Use some (data-based) causal learning algorithm;
then ask an LLM if the edges are correct,
and add a constraint to remove the incorrect edges;
iterate until convergence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The DeCAMFounder: nonlinear causal discovery in the presence of hidden variables}\\
  R. Agrawal et al.
\end{book}
% Not read
To estimate a causal graph in presence of \emph{pervasive confounding}
(unobserved confounders affecting many observed variables),
use PCA to recover (sufficient statistics) of the unobserved variables.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ABIDES-Economist: agent-based simulation of economic systems with learning agents}\\
  K. Dwarakanath et al.
\end{book}
Multi-agent system, with households (labour, consumption, saving),
firms (wages, prices, production, inventory),
central bank (interest rate)
and government (tax rate, tax credits).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RiskMiner: discovering formulaic alphas\\ via risk-seeking Monte Carlo tree search}\\
  T. Ren et al.
\end{book}
Alpha mining, not with a genetic algorithm (\texttt{gplearn}),
but with reinforcement learning (MCTS),
with \begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\cor( \alpha, \text{returns} ) - \operatorname*{Mean}_{\alpha'} \cor(\alpha, \alpha')$
  as intermediate rewards, to ensure diversity;
\item $\cor(\alpha, \text{returns}) = \text{IC}$ as final reward.
\end{compactitem}
Do not optimize the expected cummulated reward, but some quantile of it.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DiffsFormer: a diffusion transformer\\ on stock factor augmentation}\\
  Y. Gao et al.
\end{book}
Diffusion models (DDPM) progressively add noise to their input
\[ x_t = \sqrt{ 1 - \beta_t } x_{t-1} + \sqrt{ \beta_t} \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim N(0,I), \]
equivalently,
\[ x_t = \sqrt{ \bar\alpha_t } x_0 + \sqrt{ 1 - \bar \alpha_t} \epsilon , \qquad \epsilon \sim (,I), \]
and try to reverse the process
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\theta \E_{\substack{
    x_0 \sim \text{data} \\
    \epsilon \sim N(0,I) \\
    t \sim \text{Uniform}
}} \n{ \epsilon - \epsilon_\theta( x_t , t ) }^2. \]
Do not denoise pure noise, but noisy inputs (diffusion-based augmentation).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ABCs (and Ds) of understanding VARs}\\
  J. Fern\'andez-Villaverde et al.
\end{book}
In a state space model
\begin{align*}
  x_{t+1} &= A x_t + B w_{t+1} \\
  y_{t+1} &= C x_t + D w_{t+1}
\end{align*}
where $x$ is hidden and $w$ are stocks, when can we recover the shocks $w_{t+1}$ by comparing $y_{t+1}$
and $\E_t[y_t]$?
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.6,yscale=.7,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (A) {DSGE};
  \node (B) [right=5mm of A] {SSM};
  \node (C) [right=5mm of B] {VAR};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DSGE models}\\
  A. Mikusheva (2007)
\end{book}
A DSGE model considers agents (firms, households, banks, state, etc.),
each maximizing its utility, subject to a few balance equations.
Writing the first order equations and linearizing everything
gives a linear SSM, amenable to the Kalman filter
(or a particle filter, if you do not linearize).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Properties of the entropic risk measure EVaR in relation to selected distributions}\\
  Y. Mishura et al.
\end{book}
Explicit computation of the entropic value at risk
\begin{align*}
  \text{EVaR}_\alpha X &= \inf_{ t > 0 } \dfrac 1t \log \dfrac{ m_X(t) }{ 1 - \alpha } \\
  m_X(t) &= \E[ e^{tX} ] \quad \text{ moment generating function}
\end{align*}
for a few distributions (using the Lambert $W$ function,
$W(x) e^{ W(x) } = x$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ploutos: towards interpretable stock movement prediction with financial large language model}\\
  H. Tong et al.
\end{book}
Have several models (technical analysis, sentiment, financial ratios, etc.)
output their forecasts as text, with an explanation, and use another LLM to aggregate them,
with an explanation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal text-based time series indices}\\
  D. Ardia and K. Bluteau (2024)
\end{book}
To convert text (news) into macroeconomic time series,
one typically uses some (trained) sentiment indicator,
computed from heuristically selected documents
(based on the presence of keywords) --
instead, the selection process should be part of the optimization process.

Application to EPU (economic policy uncertainty).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling financial time series\\ with generative adversarial networks}\\
  S. Takahashi et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Stylized facts of financial time series include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Linear unpredictability (no correlation);
\item Fat tails, $3 < \alpha < 5$;
\item Volatility clustering
  $\cor \bigl( \va{ r_t } , \va{ r_{t+k} } \bigr) \propto k^{-\beta} $;
\item Leverage:
  $\cor( r_t, \sigma_{t+k} ) < 0$;
\item Coarse-fine volatility correlation
  \begin{align*}
    \text{coarse}_t &= \va{ \sum_{ 1 \leq i \leq \tau } r_{t-i} } \\
    \text{fine}_t &= \sum \va{ r_{t-i} } \\
    \rho(k) &= \cor( \text{coarse}_{t+k} , \text{fine}_t ) \\
    \Delta \rho(k) &= \rho(k) - \rho(-k) < 0
  \end{align*}
\item Gain-loss asymmetry: the waiting time for a +10\% price change is larger than that for a $-10$\% change
  (prices drop faster than they rise).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Macroeconomic regimes}\\
  L. Baele et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Model inflation, output and interest rate as
\begin{align*}
  \pi_t &= \delta \hat\pi_t + (1-\delta) \pi_{t-1} + \lambda y_t + \text{noise} \\
  y_t &= \mu \hat y_t + (1-\mu) y_{t-1} - \phi( i_t - \hat \pi_t ) + \text{noise} \\
  i_t &= p \hat \imath _t + (1-\rho) ( \beta \hat\pi_t + \gamma y_t ) + \text{noise} 
\end{align*}
with regime switching for
$(\beta,\gamma)$, $\var \epsilon_\pi$, $\var \epsilon_y$, $\var \epsilon_i$.

