\emph{Unsupervised} learning can be seen as (interpretable) data compression.

% 2. Probability: univariate models
\textbf{2.}
Summary statistics have limitations (Anscombe quartet, datasaurus dozen).

% TODO: PLOT?

In medicine, to compute $P[\text{infected} | \text{test}]$
with Bayes's rule, we need sensitivity, specificity and prevalence.
\[
\footnotesize
\dfrac{
  \text{sensitivity} \times \text{prevalence}
}{
  \text{sensitivity} \times \text{prevalence} +
  (1-\text{specificity}) \times ( 1 - \text{prevalence} )
}
\]
\begin{align*}
  y &\sim \text{Bernoulli}\bigl( \sigma( w' x + b ) \bigr) & \text{\footnotesize logistic regression}\\
  y &\sim \text{Cat}\bigl( \text{softmax}( Wx + b ) \bigr) & \text{\footnotesize multinomial logistic regression}
\end{align*}

The \emph{log-sum-exp trick} is
\[ \log \sum e^{ a_i } = m + \log \sum e^{ a_i - m }; \]
with $m = \max a_i$, the $a_i - m$ are negative, and the exponential does not overflow.

Common distributions include Gaussian, Student, Laplace, Cauchy, beta, gamma, exponential, chi squared,
inverse gamma.
\begin{align*}
  \text{Exp}(\lambda) &= \text{Gamma}(  1, \lambda ) \\
  \chi^2(\nu) &= \text{Gamma}\left( \dfrac\nu2, \dfrac12 \right)
\end{align*}

If $X \sim p_X$ and $Y=f(X)$, then $Y\sim p_Y$, where the change-of-variable formula is
\[ p_Y(y) = p_X\bigl( f^{-1}(y) \bigr) \va{ \text{det} \dfrac{ d f^{-1}(y) }{ dy } }. \]
If $X \indep Y$, then $p_{X+Y} = p_X * p_Y$.

% 3. Probability: multivariate models
\textbf{3.}
\emph{Simpson's paradox} says that a statistical trend that appears in different
groups can disappear or reverse sign when those groups are combined.

The \emph{conditional Gaussian distribution} is
\begin{align*}
  \begin{pmatrix} Y_1 \\ Y_2 \end{pmatrix} &\sim
  N\left( \begin{pmatrix} \mu_1 \\ \mu_2 \end{pmatrix} ,
  \begin{pmatrix} \Sigma_{11} & \Sigma_{12} \\ \Sigma_{21} & \Sigma_{22} \end{pmatrix}
  \right) \\
  Y_1 \,|\, Y_2 = y_1 &\sim N( \mu_{1|2} , \Sigma_{1|2} ) \\
  \mu_{1|2} &= \mu_1 + \Sigma_{12} \Sigma_{22}^{-1} ( y_2 - \mu_2 ) \\
  \Sigma_{1|2} &= \Sigma_{11} - \Sigma_{12} \Sigma_{22}^{-1} \Sigma_{21} = \Lambda_{11}^{-1} \\
  \Lambda &= \Sigma^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} \Lambda_{11} & \Lambda_{12} \\ \Lambda_{21} & \Lambda_{22} \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}

The \emph{Bayes rule for Gaussians} is:
if
\begin{align*}
  z &\sim N(\mu_z, \Sigma_z) \\
  y|z & \sim N( Wz + b, \Sigma_y ) 
\end{align*}
then $z|y \sim N(\mu_{z|y}, \Sigma_{z|y})$, where
\begin{align*}
  \mu_{z|y} &= \Sigma_{z|y} \bigl[ W' \Sigma_y^{-1} ( y - b ) + \Sigma_z^{-1} \mu_z \bigr] \\
  \Sigma_{z|y} ^{-1} &= \Sigma_z^{-1} + W' \Sigma_y^{-1} W
\end{align*}
(the Gaussian distribution is  conjugate prior for the Gaussian likelihood).

The ``completing the square'' trick is not limited to scalars:
\begin{align*}
  f(x) &= x'Ax + x'b + x \\
  &= (x-h)'A(x-h) + k \\
  h &= -\dfrac12 A^{-1} b \\
  k &= c - \dfrac14 b' A^{-1} b
\end{align*}
      
The \emph{exponential family} density is 
\begin{align*}
  p(y) &= h(y( \cdot \exp \bigl[ \eta ' T(y) - A(\eta) \bigr] \\
  h &\text{: scale, e.g., 1} \\
  \eta &\text{: natural parameters, $t$} \\
  T &\text{: sufficient statistic, e.g., id} \\
  A &\text{: log-partition function (convex)}.
\end{align*}

These are maximum entropy distributions, solutions of
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & p \\
  \text{To maximize} & H(p) \text{ (or } -\text{KL}(p\|h) ) \\
  \text{Such that} & \E\limits_{y \sim p} \bigl[ T(y) \bigr] = t.
\end{array} \]

The log-partition function $A$ is the cummulant generating function
\begin{align*}
  K(t) &= \log \E[ e^{tX} ] \\
  \kappa_n &= K^{(n)} (0) .
\end{align*}
In particular,
\begin{align*}
  \nabla A(\eta) &= \E\bigl[ T(y) \bigr] \\
  \nabla^2 A(\eta) &= \cov \bigl[ T(y) \bigr] \succcurlyeq 0
\end{align*}
and $A$ is convex.

