% 4. Statistics
\textbf{4.}
The \emph{maximum likelihood estimator} (MLE) is
\begin{align*}
  \hat\theta_{\text{MLE}}
  &= \argmax \text{KL}( \text{data} \| p_\theta ) \hspace*{-2cm} \\
  &= \argmax H(\text{data}, p_\theta) &&\text{(cross-entropy)} \\
  &= \argmin \text{NNL}(\theta) &&\text{(negative log-likelihood)}.
\end{align*}
For binary classification, use a \emph{surrogate loss function}
(convex, decreasing, and above $\textbf{1}_{[ z \leq 0 ]}$)
such as
\begin{align*}
  \text{hinge} &: \text{ReLU}(1-m) \\
  \text{log} &: \log( 1 + e^{-m} ) \\
  \text{exp} &: \exp(-m)
\end{align*}
where $m = \hat y y$, $y \in \{\pm1\}$, $\hat y \in \RR$.

The \emph{method of moments} is an alternative to MLE;
it may give invalid results.

Regularization with $\log_{\text{prior}}$ is an equivalent to MAP (maximum a posteriori).
Estimate the strength of the regularization with cross-validation.

The ``one standard error rule'' suggests to take the simplest model
whose risk is no more than one standard deviation above that of the best model.

The \emph{Bayesian} approach views probability in terms of \emph{information},
rather than \emph{repeated trials}.

Mixtures of conjugate distributions are still conjugate.

``Uninformative'' priors are informative: call them ``diffuse'' instead.

To estimate the parameters and hyperparameters, one can use MLE, MAP, or a Bayesian approach --
there is a hierarchy of increasingly Bayesian approaches:
\begin{tabular}{lll}
  Method & Parameters & Hyper-parameters \\
  \hline
  ML & ML & none \\
  MAP & MAP & fixed \\
  ML-I\!I (empirical Bayes) & Bayes & ML \\
  MAP-I\!I & Bayes & MAP \\
  Full Bayes & Bayes & Bayes
\end{tabular}

To summarize the posterior distribution,
provide a (centered) \emph{credible interval} or the \emph{highest posterior density} (HPD) region
(highest density interval, HDI).

If exact posterior inference is not possible,
use approximations such as
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Laplace approximation
  \begin{align*}
    & \int f(\theta) d\theta \approx \dfrac{ ( 2\pi )^{n/2} }{ \sqrt{ \det H } } f(\theta_{\max}) \\
    & \theta_{\max} = \argmax f \\
    & H = - \left . \nabla^2 \log f \right|_{ \theta = \theta_{\max} }^{\mathstrut}
  \end{align*}
  obtained by approximating $f$ with a Gaussian,
  \ie, using a Taylor expansion of $f$ at its mode;
  use a change of variable to make $f$ more symmetric and closer to a Gaussian;
\item Variational inference;
\item MCMC (HMC).
\end{compactitem}

In frequentist statistics (the data is a random variable, the parameters are fixed),
the MLE is asymptotically Gaussian
\[ \hat\theta_{\text{MLE}} \overset{d}{\longrightarrow} N \bigl( \theta^* , ( N F(\theta^*))^{-1} \bigr) \]
where the \emph{Fisher information matrix} is
\begin{align*}
  F(\theta) &= \E_{ X \in p_\theta } \bigl[
    \bigl( \nabla \log p_\theta(X) \bigr)
    \bigl( \nabla \log p_\theta(X) \bigr) ^\top
    \bigr] \\
  &= - \E_{ X \sim p_\theta } \bigl[ \nabla^2 \log p_\theta(X) \bigr] \\
  &= \text{Hessian of the NNL}.
\end{align*}

% Bootstrap

There is a trade-off between bias and variance. 
\begin{align*}
  \text{bias} &= \E[ \hat\theta ] - \theta^* \\
  \text{V}[\hat\theta] &= \E[ \hat\theta^2 ] - \E[ \hat\theta ]^2 \\
  \E[\cdot] &= \E_{ \mathscr D \sim p_{\!\mathscr D}^{\mathstrut} } [ \cdot ]
\end{align*}

% 5. Decision theory
\textbf{5.}
The loss function we minimize should include the cost of incorrect output
(and the reward of correct ones).
For instance, classification problems could have a \emph{reject option}
(the algorithm is allowed to output ``I do not know'').
\begin{align*}
  &(p_1,\dots,p_n) \sim \text{Dirichlet}(1,\dots,1) \\
  &j \sim \text{Cat}\bigl( [1,\dots,n], [p_1,\dots,p_n] \bigr) \\
  &i = \argmax_i p_i \\
  &i = p_i > \lambda \ ?\  i : 0 \\
  &\text{cost} = \begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if } i = j \\
    \lambda_{\text{reject}} & \text{if } i = 0 \\
    \lambda_{\text{error}} & \text{otherwise}
  \end{cases}
\end{align*}
The threshold $\lambda$ minimizing $\E[\text{cost}]$ is
\[ 1 - \dfrac{ \lambda_{\text{reject}} }{ \lambda_{\text{error}} }. \]
In case of class imbalance, prefer the precision-recall curve to the ROC curve.

The \emph{interpolated precision} for a given recall level $\alpha$
is the maximim precision of a recall level at least $\alpha$
(the precision is not necessarily decreassing with $\alpha$).
The \emph{average precision} is the average of the interpolaated precisions.

The $F_\beta$ score is the weighted harmonic mean of precision and recall, with weights 1 and $\beta^2$;
if recall (resp. precision) is more important, try $\beta = 2$ (resp. $\beta=1/2$).

To choose between two models, in a Bayesian way, one can look at
the \emph{Bayesian factor} (for Bayesian hypothesis testing):
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ P(M_1|D) }{ P(M_2|D) }
  &= \dfrac{ P(M_1,D) / P(D) }{ P(M_2,D) / P(D) } \\
  &= \dfrac{ P(D|M_1) P(M_1) }{ P(D|M_2) P(M_2) } \\
  B &= \dfrac{ P(D|M_1) }{ P(D|M_2) } \text{ if } P(M_1) = P(M_2) = \tfrac12.
\end{align*}

These are \emph{marginal likkelihoods} (or ``evidence''):
the parameters have been integrated out,
\[ p(D|M) = \int p(D|M,\theta) p(\theta|M) d\theta . \]
The marginal likelihood can be difficult to compute:
by approximating the posterior with a Gaussian
(Laplace approximation)
\begin{align*}
  & \log p(D|M) \approx \log p(D|\hat\theta) + \log p(\hat\theta) - \tfrac12 \log \va H \\
  & H = - \nabla^2 \left. \log p(D,\theta) \right|_{ \theta = \hat\theta }^{\mathstrut},
\end{align*}
choosing a uniform prior $p(\theta) \propto 1$,
and approximating the Occam factor
\begin{align*}
  H &= \sum_i \nabla^2 \log p(D_i|\theta) \\
  &= \sum_i H_i \\
  &\approx N \cdot \hat H \\
  \log \va H
  &\approx \log \va{ N \hat H } \\
  &= D \log N + \log \va{\hat H} \\
  &\approx D \log N
\end{align*}
(because $\log \va{ \hat H }$ is constant and becomes negligible when $D \log N \to \infty$),
we get the Bayesian information criterion (BIC)
\[ \log p(d|m) \approx \log p(D,\hat\theta, m) - \dfrac{ D_m }{2} \log N \]
(it is sometimes multiplied by $-2$ to get a loss).
Alternatives to the BIC include:
\begin{align*}
  \text{AIC} &= - 2 \log p(D|\hat\theta,m) + 2 D \\
  \text{MDL} &=  - \log p(D|\hat\theta,m) = \log p(m) 
\end{align*}
where $p(m)$ is the number of bits needed to specify which model $m$ it is.

Point null hypotheses, $H_0: \mu = 0$, are irrelevant:
eeplace $0$ with a \emph{region of practical equivalence} (ROPE)
$H_0: \mu \in [ - \epsilon, \epsilon]$;
we can then compute $P(H_0 | \text{Data})$ (Bayesian $T$ test).

The (frequentist) risk of an estimator $\hat\theta$ is
\[ \text{Risk}(\theta, \hat\theta) = \E_{ X \sim p_\theta } \bigl[
  \ell\bigl( \theta, \hat\theta(X) \bigr)
  \bigr].
\]
The \emph{Bayesian risk} integrates out the (unknown) true parameter $\theta$ with a prior.

In supervised learning, the population risk is
\[ \text{Risk}( f, p^* ) = \E_{ x, y \sim p^* } \bigl[ \ell\bigl( y, f(x) \bigr) \bigr] \]
where $(x,y)$ are sampled from the true (unknown) joint distribution $p^*$.
The empirical risk replaces $p^*$ with the empirical distribution of the observed data
\[ \text{Risk}( f, \text{Data} ) =
\E_{ x, y \sim \text{Data} } \bigl[ \ell\bigl( y , f(x) \bigr) \bigr]. \]

The empirical risk can be decomposed into
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The approximation error (risk of the best model in the class considered);
\item The estimation error (generalization gap), stemming from the difference
  between the population distribution and the empirical distribution.
\end{compactitem}
Add a regularization to the risk
\[ \text{Risk}( f, \text{Data} ) + \lambda C(f) \]
and choose $\lambda$ to minimize the LOO-CV risk.

Frequentist hypothesis testing is based on a fallacy:
\begin{quote}\itshape
  If $H_0$ were true, this statistic would probably not occur.\\
  This statistic did occur.\\
  Therefore, $H_0$ is probably false.
\end{quote}
\begin{quote}\itshape
  If a person is American, he is probably not a member of congress.\\
  This person is a member of congress.\\
  Therefore, he is probably not American.
\end{quote}

% 6. Information theory
\textbf{6.}
Joint entropy, mutual information, conditional entropy behave like
set union, intersection and difference.
\begin{align*}
  H(p) &= \E_{ X \sim p } - \log_2 p(x) && \text{entropy} \\
  H_{\text{ce}}(p,q) &= \E_{ X \sim p } - \log_2 q(x) && \text{cross-entropy} \\
  H(X,Y) &= - \sum_{x,y} p(x,y) \log_2 p(x,y) && \text{joint entropy} \\
  H(Y|X) &= \E_{ x \sim p_X} H(Y|X=x) && \text{conditional entropy} \\
  &= H(X,Y) - H(X) \\
  H(X_1,\cdots,X_n) &= \sum H(X_i | X_1,\dots, X_{i-1} ) && \text{chain rule} \\
  \text{perplexity}(p) &= 2^{ H(p) } && \text{average branching factor} \\
  \text{KL}(p\|q) &= \E_{ X \sim p } \left[ \log \dfrac{ p(x) }{ q(x) } \right]
  && \text{relative entropy} \\
  &= - H(p) + H_{\text{ce}}(p,q) \\
  I(X;Y) &= \text{KL}\bigl( p(x,y) \| p(x) p(y) \bigr)
  && \text{mutual information} \\
  &= H(X) - H(X|Y) \\
  &= H(X,Y) - H(X|Y) - H(Y|X) \hspace*{-4cm} \\
  &= H(X) + H(Y) - H(X,Y) \hspace*{-4cm} 
\end{align*}

\[
