\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{gCastle: a Python toolbox for causal discovery}\\
  N. Zhang et al. (2021)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal discovery in financial markets:\\ a framework for nonstationary time\,series data}\\
  A. Sadeghi et al. (2024)
\end{book}
Adapt the PC algorithm,
for causal discovery with nonstationary time series by:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Adding a ``time'' variable; 
\item Considering several lags;
\item Computing conditional independence tests: KCIT, RCoT, CMIknn, ParCorr
\item Orienting edges with known directions
  ($\text{Time}_t \to X_t$, $X_{t-k} \to Y_t$);
\item Orienting the other edges, whenever possible (as in the original PC algorithm).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Discovering causal models with optimization: confounders, cycles and instrument validity}\\
  F. Eberhardt et al.
\end{book}
Build a causal graph from causal independence relations by reducing the problem
to a MIP (mixed integer program).
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find}
  & x \in \{0,1\}^{ \tilde E} \\
  & y \in \{ 0,1 \}^{ P(\tilde E, \zeta) } \\
  & z \\
  \text{Where}
  & \tilde E \text{ is a set of candidate edges} \\
  & P(\tilde E, \zeta) \text{ is the set of simple paths} \\
  &
  \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.3,yscale=.8,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node (C) at (2,0) {};
  \node (D) at (3,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (E) at (4,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (F) at (5,0) {};
  \draw (A) -- (B);
  \draw (B) -- (C);
  \draw[densely dotted] (C) -- (D);
  \draw (D) -- (E);
  \draw (E) -- (F);  
\end{tikzpicture}
  %\\ &
  \text{ or extended paths} \\
  &
  \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (C) at (2,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (D) at (3,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (E) at (4,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (F) at (5,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (G) at (6,0) {};

  \node[circle,fill] (H) at (3,-1) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (I) at (3,-2) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (J) at (3,-3) {};
  \draw (A) -- (B);
  \draw[densely dotted] (B) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (D);
  \draw[<-] (D) -- (E);
  \draw[densely dotted] (E) -- (F);  
  \draw (F) -- (G);
  \draw[->] (D) -- (H);
  \draw[densely dotted] (H) -- (I);
  \draw[->] (I) -- (J);  
\end{tikzpicture}
  \\
  \text{Such that}
  & \text{$x$ and $y$ are consistent} \\
  & \text{$y$ recognizes d-separation: } \\
  & \qquad i \indep j | C \text{ with error } z_{ijC}\\
  & \text{$y$ recognizes d-connection: } \\
  & \qquad i \not\indep j | C \text{ with error } z_{ijC}\\
  \text{To minimize}
  & \sum_{ijC} z_{ijC}
\end{array} \]

The set of candidate edges $\tilde E$
is grown progressively,
by adding the smallest number of edges to account for all potential
colliders
(\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (C) at (2,0) {};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (B);  
\end{tikzpicture})
and 
non-colliders
(\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (C) at (2,0) {};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (C);  
\end{tikzpicture}
or
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (C) at (2,0) {};
  \draw[->] (B) -- (A);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (C);  
\end{tikzpicture})
not yet explained; only include one edge for each such triple.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Structural intervention distance (SID)\\ for evaluating causal graphs}\\
  J. Peters and P. B\"uhlmann
\end{book}
Given a ground-truth causal graph $g$, and an estimated graph $\hat g$,
the SID is the number of pairs of nodes $(X,Y)$ for which the parents of $X$ in $\hat g$
are not a sufficient conditioning set for $X \to Y$ in $g$.

This is not a distance: it is not symmetric, and
$\text{SID}(g,\hat g) = 0 \rlap{\ensuremath{\quad\not}}\implies g = \hat g$.

The \emph{structural Hamming distance} (SHD) between DAGs
is the $L^1$ distance between their adjacency matrices.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Canonical portfolios:\\ optimal asset and signal combination}\\
  N. Firoozye et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Look for an easy-to-forecast portfolio, with CCA (canonical correlation analysis)
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{a,b} \cor( a' X, b' R ) \]
where
\begin{align*}
  X_{it} &\text{: signal} \\
  R_{it} &\text{: return} \\
  i &\text{: asset} \\
  t &\text{: time.}  
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Disciplined saddle programming}\\
  P. Schiele et al. (2023)
\end{book}
A convex-concave \emph{saddle function}
$f: \mathscr X \times \mathscr Y \to \RR$
is convex in $x$ (for $y$ fixed)
and concave in $y$ (for $x$ fixed).
A \emph{saddle point} $(x^**,y^*)$ satisfies
\[ \forall x \forall y \quad
f(x^*,y) \leq f(x^**,y^**) \leq f(x,y^*), \]
\ie,
\begin{align*}
  x^* &= \argmin_x f(x,y^*) \\
  y^* &= \argmax_y f(x^*,y).
\end{align*}
The \emph{saddle extremum functions} are
\begin{align*}
  G(x) &= \sup_{ y \in \mathscr Y } f(x,y) \\
  H(y) &= \inf_{ x \in \mathscr X} f(x,y).
\end{align*}
Problems with saddle point extremum constraints
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x \phi(x) \text{ st } G(x) \leq 0 \]
are ``semi-infinite constraint'' problems:
there is an infinite number of constraints
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x \phi(x) \text{ st } \forall y \ f(x,y) \leq 0. \]
Robust optimization problems are saddlepoint problems.

To solve the minimax problem
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x \max_y f(x,y) \]
replace $\max_y f(x,y)$ with its dual
to have a minimin (convex optimization) problem.
This can be automated for ``conically representable saddle functions'';
Python implementation in \texttt{dsp}.
\begin{align*}
  f(x,y) &= x' y \\
  f(x,y) &= \text{convex}(x) ' \text{concave}(y) \\
  f(x,y) &= \bigl( \sum y_i x_i^2 \bigr)^{1/2} \\
  f(x,y) &= \log \sum y_i \exp x_i \\
  f(x,y) &= \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} ' \begin{pmatrix} P & * \\ * & -Q \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}
  && P, Q \text{ psd} \\
  f(x,Y) &= x' Y x && Y \text{ psd}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Synthetic data applications in finance}\\
  V.K. Potluru et al.
\end{book}
To generate synthetic tabular datasets,
try SDV;
also look at the t-SNE scatterplot of the synthetic and real data.

Synthetic data generation is often unsupervised (agnostic to the downstream task),
but it can be supervised:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bayesian optimization to select the model hyperparameters;
\item Weights of a mixture of several data generation processes.
\end{compactitem}

For event data, try Hawkes processes and their generalizations
(Cox processes), and automated planning
(agents have a state (accounts, balance, rent, taxes, utility bills)
and a (random) goal).

For time series, try parametric models (SDE), non-parametric models
(TimeGAN, QuantGAN, TimeVAE, neural SDE,
implicit neural representations (hypernetworks)
and agent-based models (\textsc{Abides}))
% Check: rddlsim, pyRDDLGym (RDDL is a description of a multi-agent MDP)

