To extend the context size,
do not extrapolate
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.05,yscale=.2,inner sep=.6pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2] 
\a\c
\end{tikzpicture}
\longrightarrow
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.05,yscale=.2,inner sep=.6pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2] 
\b\d
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
but interpolate the positional embedding
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.05,yscale=.2,inner sep=.6pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2] 
\a\c
\end{tikzpicture}
\longrightarrow
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.05,yscale=.2,inner sep=.6pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2] 
\a\e
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\endgroup

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Textbooks are all you need}\\
  S. Gunasekar et al.
\end{book}
Data quality matters and impacts the scaling laws
(textbook quality web data,
GPT-generated textbooks about Python coding).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Emergence of autopoietic vesicles\\ able to grow, repair and reproduce\\ in a minimalist particle system}\\
  T. Cabaret
\end{book}
Life-like particle system,
with 4 types of particles: precursor, catalyst, intermediary, surfactant.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio construction\\ when regimes are ambiguous}\\
  M. Kritzman et al.
\end{book}
Do not define all-or-nothing regimes
\[ r_t = \mathbf1_{ x_t \geq \text{threshold} } \]
but
\[ r_t = 
- \underbrace{ \left(\dfrac{x_t - x_0 }{ \sigma } \right)^2 }_{\text{similarity}}
+ \underbrace{
  \left( \dfrac{ x_t - \bar x }{ \sigma } \right)^2
  +
  \left( \dfrac{ x_0 - \bar x }{ \sigma } \right)^2
}_{\text{informativeness}}
\]
(use the Mahalanobis distance, $(x-y)'\Sigma^{-1}(x-y)$,
if the indicator $x$ is multivariate).
Use the relevance $r_t$ (truncated, to discard less relevant observations)
as weights to compute regime-specific expected returns and asset covariances.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust statistics\\ for portfolio construction and analysis}\\
  R.D. Martin et al.
\end{book}
Replace winsorization with \emph{adaptive winsorization}:
clip observations $k\cdot\text{MAD}$ away from the median;
upper and lower MAD for skewed data.

\emph{M-estimators} minimize
\[ \sum_i \rho\left( \dfrac{ \epsilon_1(\theta) }{ \hat s } \right) \]
where
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\rho$ is a replacement for the square loss;
\item $\epsilon_i(\theta)$ are the model residuals;
\item $\hat s$ is a robust scale, computed before the optimization.
\end{compactitem}
Rho functions include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Huber (quadratic for small values, linear for large values);
\item mOpt (constant for large values).
\end{compactitem}
For linear regression,
$\epsilon_i(\theta) = y_i - x_i^\top \theta$
and the first order condition is
\[ x_i \rho' \left( \dfrac{ \epsilon_i(\theta) }{ \hat s } \right) = 0. \]
For mOpt, $\rho'$ is
\[ \psi(x) = \begin{cases}
  x & \text{if } \va x \leq 1 \\
  \dfrac{ \phi(1) }{ \phi(1) - a } \left( x - \text{sign}(x) \dfrac a{\phi(x)} \right)
  & \text{if } 1 \leq \va x \leq c\\
  0 & \text{if } \va x \geq c,
\end{cases} \]
with $a=0.0132$, $c=3.00$.
It can be implemented with IRLS, with weights $w(x) = \psi(x) / x$.

The \emph{MM estimator} computes a robust covariance by iterating
\begin{align*}
  & \sum w_i (x_i - \mu ) = 0 && \text{weighted mean} \\
  & \dfrac1N \sum w_i ( x_i - \mu ) ( x_i - \mu ) ' = C && \text{weighted covariance}
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
  d & = (x_i - \mu )T^\top C^{-1} (x_i - \mu) = \n{ C^{-1/2} (x_i - \mu ) }^2 \\
  w_i &= W( d_i / cs )
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
  W(x) &= \begin{cases}
    1 &\text{if } x \leq 4 \\
    q(x) &\text{if } 4 \leq x \leq 9 \\
    0 &\text{if } x > 9
  \end{cases} \\
  q(x) &= \text{polynomial of degree 3} \\
  s &\text{: robust scale} \\
  c &\text{: tuning parameter.}
\end{align*}

In R, check \texttt{RobStatTM}, \texttt{PCRA} (risk analysis)
and \texttt{facmodCS} (cross-sectional factor models).

