\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Exact combinatorial optimization\\ with graph convolutional neural networks}\\
  M. Gasse et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Imitation learning of branch-and-bound selection policies,
with a GCN on the variable-constraint bipartite graph.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural combinatorial optimization\\ with reinforcement learning}\\
  I. Bello et al. (2017)
\end{book}
A \emph{pointer network} can generate a permutation
$\sigma \in \mathfrak S_n$ of a set of points $x_1,\dots,x_n$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item First, an encoder processes the points in sequence, with an LSTM;
\item Then, a decoder generates the $\sigma(i)$'s, one at a time:
  at each step, it generates a distribution on the $x_i$'s (attention),
  picks a point from that distribution, and gives it to the next step.
\end{compactitem}
Solve the TSP with RL (A3C, with policy gradient,
and \textsc{reinforce} to compute the gradient),
with a pointer network for the policy (actor).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph neural networks\\ inspired by classical algorithms}\\
  Y. Yang et al.
\end{book}
The energy
\[ \ell(Y) = \n{ Y - f_w(X) }^2 + \lambda \tr( Y' L Y ) \]
where
$Y$ are candicate node embeddings,
$X$ are node features,
and $L = D - A$ is the Laplacian,
is minimized by
\[ Y^*(w) = ( I + \lambda L )^{-1} f_w(X); \]
it is an approximation of $f_w(X)$ smoothed by the graph structure.

It can be used for node classification
\[ \text{loss}(\theta, w) = \sum_i D \bigl[ g_\theta\bigl( y_i^*(w) \bigr) , t_i \bigr] . \]

Instead of inverting $I + \lambda L$,
do a few gradient steps on $\ell$;
they can be interpreted as GNN layers.

The penalty $\tr(Y'LY) = \sum_{ij \in E} \n{y_i - y_j}^2$
can be generalized to $\sum_{ij\in E} \rho( \n{y_i - y_j} ^2 )$
to increase robustness (to adversarial edges).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the ability of graph neural networks\\ to model interactions between vertices}\\
  N. Razin et al. 
\end{book}
The \emph{walk index} of a graph $(V,E)$ wrt a partition
$V = I \union I^c$
is the number of length-$L$ walks starting on the boundary of the partition.
The \emph{walk index edge sparsification} algorithm
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Starts with a set of partitions across which we want to keep interactions
\item Computes the walk indices wrt each of those partitions
  if we remove one edge, for each edge; 
\item Removes the edge with the maximum walk indices (aggregated with max, min or mean).
\end{compactitem}

