\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Training spiking neural networks\\ using lessons from deep learning}\\
  J.K. Eshraghian et al.
\end{book}
The leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) neuron takes as input a spike train
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.2,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
\draw[thick,blue!50!black] ( 0.897 ,0)--( 0.897,1 );
\draw[thick,blue!50!black] ( 0.266 ,0)--( 0.266,1 );
\draw[thick,blue!50!black] ( 0.372 ,0)--( 0.372,1 );
\draw[thick,blue!50!black] ( 0.573 ,0)--( 0.573,1 );
\draw[thick,blue!50!black] ( 0.908 ,0)--( 0.908,1 );
\draw[thick,blue!50!black] ( 0.202 ,0)--( 0.202,1 );
\draw (0,0) -- (1,0);
\end{tikzpicture},
smooths it into a ``potential', e.g., with exponential decay,
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.1,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \draw[thick,blue!50!black] (0,0)--(0.01,0)--(0.02,0)--(0.03,0)--(0.04,0)--(0.051,0)--(0.061,0)--(0.071,0)--(0.081,0)--(0.091,0)--(0.101,0)--(0.111,0)--(0.121,0)--(0.131,0)--(0.141,0)--(0.152,0)--(0.162,0)--(0.172,0)--(0.182,0)--(0.192,0)--(0.202,1)--(0.212,0.922)--(0.222,0.851)--(0.232,0.785)--(0.242,0.724)--(0.253,0.668)--(0.263,0.616)--(0.273,1.515)--(0.283,1.398)--(0.293,1.289)--(0.303,1.189)--(0.313,1.097)--(0.323,1.012)--(0.333,0.933)--(0.343,0.861)--(0.354,0.794)--(0.364,0.732)--(0.374,1.662)--(0.384,1.533)--(0.394,1.414)--(0.404,1.304)--(0.414,1.203)--(0.424,1.109)--(0.434,1.023)--(0.444,0.944)--(0.455,0.871)--(0.465,0.803)--(0.475,0.741)--(0.485,0.683)--(0.495,0.63)--(0.505,0.581)--(0.515,0.536)--(0.525,0.494)--(0.535,0.456)--(0.545,0.421)--(0.556,0.388)--(0.566,0.358)--(0.576,1.308)--(0.586,1.207)--(0.596,1.113)--(0.606,1.027)--(0.616,0.947)--(0.626,0.873)--(0.636,0.806)--(0.646,0.743)--(0.657,0.685)--(0.667,0.632)--(0.677,0.583)--(0.687,0.538)--(0.697,0.496)--(0.707,0.458)--(0.717,0.422)--(0.727,0.389)--(0.737,0.359)--(0.747,0.331)--(0.758,0.305)--(0.768,0.282)--(0.778,0.26)--(0.788,0.24)--(0.798,0.221)--(0.808,0.204)--(0.818,0.188)--(0.828,0.174)--(0.838,0.16)--(0.848,0.148)--(0.859,0.136)--(0.869,0.126)--(0.879,0.116)--(0.889,0.107)--(0.899,1.083)--(0.909,1.99)--(0.919,1.836)--(0.929,1.693)--(0.939,1.562)--(0.949,1.44)--(0.96,1.329)--(0.97,1.225)--(0.98,1.13)--(0.99,1.043)--(1,0.962);
\draw (0,0) -- (1,0);
\end{tikzpicture},
and, each time it exceeds a prescribed threshold,
emits a spike, each emission resetting the potential.
\begin{align*}
  X_t &: \text{ input} \\
  U_t &= \beta U_{t-1} + W x_t - \theta S_{t-1} \text{ latent state} \\
  S_t &= \mathbf 1_{ U_t \geq 0 } \text{ output}
\end{align*}

The input needs to be converted into a spike train, e.g., as the firing rate (rate coding),
or the time to the first spike (latency coding).

Spiking neural nets (SSN) can be trained with
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Shadow training (SSN in the forward pass, ANN in the backward pass);
\item Back-propagation through time (BPTT);
\item Local learning rules.
\end{compactitem}

