\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Large non-stationary noisy covariance matrices: a cross-validation approach}\\
  V.W.C. Tan and S. Zohren (2020)
\end{book}
The sample covariance matrix with exponential weights
\begin{align*}
  \hat E &= \dfrac{
    \displaystyle
    \sum_{ t=0 }^{ T-1 } \beta^t x_{ \tau - t }  x'_{ \tau - t } 
  }{
    \sum \beta^t
  } \\
  &= \dfrac1T C' B X \\
  B &= T \text{diag} \left( \dfrac{ \beta^t }{ \sum \beta^s } \right)_t  \\
  &= T \dfrac{ 1 - \beta }{ 1 - \beta^T } \text{diag}( \beta^t )_t \\
  \hat E &= \cov \tilde X \\
  \tilde X &= X B^{1/2} \\
  N/T &\longrightarrow q  \\
  N(1-\beta) &\longrightarrow q_e
\end{align*}
can account for non-stationarity,
but its limiting spectral distribution
is slightly different from the Marchenko-Pastur one:
the eigenvalues of $\hat E$ are biased estimators of the true eigenvalues.
Keep the eigenvectors, but use cross-validation (on $\tilde X$) to remove the bias of the eigenvalues.

Let $\rho$ be the asymptotic distribution of the eigenvalues of a random matrix;
its \emph{Stieltjes transform} is
\[ G(z) = \int_{\RR} \dfrac{ \rho(t) }{ z - t } dt =
\lim_{ N \to \infty } \dfrac1N \E \tr \bigl[ (X-zI)^{-1} \bigr ]. \]
The \emph{R-transform} is defined by
\[ R \bigl( G(z) \bigr) + \dfrac1{ G(z) } = z \]
and satisfies
$R_{A+B} = R_A + R_B$
(assuming the eigenvectors are in ``general position'').
To compute the spectrum of $A+B$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute $G_A$, $G_B$, $R_A$, $R_B$;
\item Compute $R_{A+B} = R_A + R_B$;
\item Compute $G_{A+B}$;
\item Compute $\rho_{A+B}$
\end{compactitem}
  
