\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximally machine-learnable portfolios}\\
  P.G. Coulombe et al. (2022)
\end{book}
ACE (alternating conditional expectation)
finds and easy-to-forecast transformation of the target variable
\[ g(Y) = f(X) + \epsilon \]
(iteratively estimate $g$ fixing $f$, and $f$ fixing $g$,
with backfitting polynomials).
With time series, this becomes
\[ g( Y_{t+h} ) = f(X_t) + \epsilon_{ t+h } . \]

This can be generalized to portfolios (MACE, multivariate ACE):
find a portfolio $w$ whose returns
\[ g( Y_{t+h} ) = w' Y_{t+h} \]
are easy to forecast with a random forest
(from market variables,
macro variables, or the characteristics (returns, earnings yield, etc.) of the portfolio itself).
\begin{align*}
  \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ w , f }\  & \sum_t \bigl[ w' r_{t+h} - f(X_t) \bigr] + \lambda \n w ^2 \\
  \operatorname{such\ that}\  & w \geq 0 , \ w' \mathbf 1 = 1.
\end{align*}
The model can be estimated by alternatingly fitting a random forest to get $f$,
and a ridge regression to get~$w$.

