\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GFlowNet Foundations}\\
  Y. Bengio et al.
\end{book}
Contrary to MCMC, GFlowNets can efficiently sample from multimodal distributions,
on discrete objects that can be contructed incrementally, e.g.,
set, graphs or molecules.
They can be trained, not from data, but from an unnormalized probability distribution
(the \emph{reward}).

A \emph{flow network} is a DAG,
with a single source and a single sink,
with a non-negative function
$F : \mathscr T \to \RR_+$
on the set of complete trajectories.
This defines:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item State flow: $\displaystyle F(s) = \sum_{ \tau \,:\, s \in \mathscr T} F(\tau)$;
\item Edge flow: $\displaystyle F(s \to s') = \sum_{ \tau\,:\,(s\to s')\in \mathscr T} F(\tau)$;
\item Total flow: $\displaystyle Z = \sum_\tau F(\tau)$;
\item Forward transition probability:
  \[ P_F(s'|s) = \dfrac{ F(s \to s') }{ F(s) }; \]
\item Backward transition probability:
  \[ P_B(s|s') = \dfrac{ F(s \to s') }{ F(s') }. \]
\end{compactitem}

State and edge flows define a flow if the satisfy the \emph{flow matching conditions}
\begin{align*}
  \hat F(s') &= \sum_{ s \to s'} \hat F( s \to s' ) \\
  \hat F(s') &= \sum_{s' \to s''} \hat F( s' \to s'').
\end{align*}

State flow and forward and backward transition probabilities correspond
to a flow if they satisfy the \emph{detailed balance conditions}
\[ \hat F(s) \hat P_G(s'|s) = \hat F(s') \hat P_B(s|s'). \]

A flow is \emph{Markovian} if
\[ P( s \to s' | s_0 \to \cdots \to s ) = P( s \to s' | x ) = P_F(s'|s). \]
Each flow function (on paths) is equivalent to
(has the same edge flow as) a unique Markovian flow.
The edge flows determine a unique Markovian flow.

A GFlowNet parametrized by edge flows $\hat F(s \to s')$
can be trained with the \emph{flow matching loss}
\[
\sum_{ s' } \left[
  \log \dfrac{
    \displaystyle
    \delta + \sum_{
      s \in \text{Par}(s') 
    }
    \hat F( s \to s' )    
  }{
    \displaystyle
    \delta + R(s') +
    \sum_{\substack{
        s'' \in \text{Child}(s') \\
        s'' \neq \text{final}
    }}
    \hat F( s' \to s'' )
  }
  \right] ^2
\]

A GFlowNet parametrized by vertex flows
$\hat F(s)$ and transition probabilities
$\hat P_F(s'|s)$, $\hat P_B(s|s')$
can be trained with the \emph{detailed balance loss}
\[
\sum_{ s \to s' } \left[
  \log \dfrac{
    \delta + \hat F(s) \hat P_F(s'|s) 
  }{
    \delta + \hat F(s') \hat P_B(s|s')
  }
  \right] ^2
\]
(replace the denominator with $\delta + R(s)$ if $s'=\text{final}$).

A GFlowNet parametrized by total flow $\hat Z$
and transition probabilities $\hat P_F(s'|s)$,
$\hat P_B(s|s')$ can be trained with the
\emph{trajectory balance loss} (preferred)
\[ \sum_\tau \left[
  \log \dfrac{
    \displaystyle
    \hat Z \, \prod_t \hat P_F(s_t|s_{t-1})
  }{
    \displaystyle
    R(s_T) \prod_t \hat P_B( s_{t-1} | s_t )
  }
  \right]^2.
\]

