\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient and robust\\ approximate nearest neighbor search\\ using hierarchical navigable small world graphs}\\
  Y.A. Malkov and D.A. Yashunin
\end{book}
NSW (navigable small world) adds the points, in a random order,
linking each one to its $M$ closest (approximate) neighbours
(greedy search with multiple random entry nodes).
The construction can be parallelized.
The first nodes are better connected than later ones.

HNSW (hierarchical NSW) uses $n$ subgraphs,
containing an increasing fraction of the nodes:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start the search at the top (smallest) one,
at a high degree node;
\item Greedily find the node closest to the query;
\item Start a new search in the next graph, from this node; 
\item Continue down the stack of graphs.
\end{compactitem}

This is similar to the \emph{probability skip list}
(a stack of sorted linked lists).
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=1,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0,
    every node/.style={text width = 4mm, text height = 2mm, text depth = 2mm, draw, align=center}
]
  \node (A0) at (0,0) {};
  \node[above=0pt of A0] (A1) {};
  \node[above=0pt of A1] (A2) {};
  \node[above=0pt of A2] (A3) {};

  \node[right=4mm of A0] (B0) {};
  \draw[->] (A0) -- (B0);

  \node[right=4mm of B0] (C0) {};
  \node[above=0pt of C0] (C1) {};
  \node[above=0pt of C1] (C2) {};
  \node[above=0pt of C2] (C3) {};
  \draw[->] (B0) -- (C0);
  \draw[->] (A1) -- (C1);
  \draw[->] (A2) -- (C2);
  \draw[->] (A3) -- (C3);

  \node[right=4mm of C0] (D0) {};
  \draw[->] (C0) -- (D0);

  \node[right=4mm of D0] (E0) {};
  \node[above=0pt of E0] (E1) {};
  \draw[->] (D0) -- (E0);
  \draw[->] (C1) -- (E1);

  \node[right=4mm of E0] (F0) {};
  \draw[->] (E0) -- (F0);

  \node[right=4mm of F0] (G0) {};
  \node[above=0pt of G0] (G1) {};
  \node[above=0pt of G1] (G2) {};
  \draw[->] (F0) -- (G0);
  \draw[->] (E1) -- (G1);
  \draw[->] (C2) -- (G2);

