\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Powerful $p$-value combination methods\\ to detect incomplete association}\\
  S. Yoon et al. (2021)
\end{book}
To combine $p$-values,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{Fisher} method converts them to $\chi^2$:
  \begin{align*}
    p_i &\overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} U(0,1) \\
    T &= - 2 \sum \log p_i \sim \chi^2( 2 n );
  \end{align*}
\item The \emph{Lancaster} method uses the sample sizes $s_i$ as weights
  \[ L = \sum F_{ \chi^2(s_i) } ^{ -1 } ( p_i ) \sim \chi^2( \sum s_i ) \]
  but the excessive degrees of freedom decrease the power;
\item The \emph{weighted Fisher} method rescales the sample sizes
  to keep the same degrees of freedom,
  using $\chi^2(\text{df} = 2n ) = \Gamma( k = n, \theta = 2 )$;
\item The \emph{roP method} uses the $r$-th ordered (smallest) $p$-value
  \[ p_{(r)} \sim \text{Beta}( r, n - r + 1 ) \]
  but it is very sensitive to the choice of $r$;
\item \emph{OrdMeta} uses $x = \min_r F_{(r)}( p_{(r)} )$ as statistic
  \[ p = 1 - n!
  \int_{ F_{(n)}^{-1} } ^ 1
  \cdots
  \int_{ F_{(2)}^{-1} } ^ {t_3}
  \int_{ F_{(1)}^{-1} } ^ {t_2}
  dt_1 \dots dt_n.
  \]
\end{compactitem}
R implementation in \texttt{metapro},
with \texttt{SymPy} for the integral.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Combining dependent $p$-values with an empirical adaptation of Brown's method}\\
  W. Poole et al. (2016)
\end{book}
To combine $p$-values, the Fisher method uses
\begin{align*}
  p_i & \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} U(0,1) \\
  \Psi &= \sum - 2 \log p_i \sim \chi^2( 2k ).
\end{align*}
If the $p$-values are not independent,
the Brown (Kost) method posits
$\Psi \sim c\cdot \chi^2( 2f )$,
estimates $c$ and $f$ by matching the first two moments
\begin{align*}
  f &= \dfrac{ \E[ \Psi ] ^ 2 }{ \var [ \Psi ] } \\
  c &= \dfrac{ \var[ \Psi ] }{ 2 \E[ \Psi ] } \\
  \E[\Psi] &= 2k \\
  \var[ \Psi ] &= 4k + 2 \sum_{ i < j } \cov [ - 2 \log p_i, - 2 \log p_j ]
\end{align*}
and approximates the covariance as
\begin{align*}
  & \cov[ - 2 \log p_i , - 2 \log p_j ] = a \rho_{ij} + b \rho_{ij}^2 + c \rho_{ij}^3 \\
  & a = 3.263 \quad b = -.719 \quad c = 0.027 \\
  & \rho_{ij} = \cor( X_i , X_j ) \\
  & X_i = \Phi^{-1} ( p_i )
\end{align*}
(in particular, this also assumes a Gaussian copula).

Instead, approximate the covariance with the ecdf.
% ?

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How good are convex hull algorithms?}\\
  D. Avis et al. (1996)
\end{book}
There are several convex hull algorithm, each library implementing only one of them:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Double description: \texttt{cddlib}, \texttt{porta},
\item Reverse search: \texttt{lrslib},
\item Quick hull: \texttt{qhull}.
\end{compactitem}

Each of them has trouble dealing with some type of polytopes:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fat lattice: \#facets${}\gg{}$size;
\item Intricate: \#triangles${}\gg{}$size;
\item Dwarfed: $P = Q \inter H$,
  $\#\text{facets}_P < \#\text{facets}_Q$, $\text{size}_Q\gg\text{size}_P$,
  where the size is the space required to store both representations of the polytope
  (set of vertices, set of half-spaces)
  and $\gg$ means ``super-polynomial''.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quasi-stable coloring for graph compression}\\
  M. Kayali and D. Suciu
\end{book}
The Weisfeler-Leman (WL) algorithm (aka colour refinement)
produces a \emph{stable colouring},
which can be used for graph reduction
(merge all the nodes with the same colour),
but the reduction is rather modest.
Quasi-stable colourings allow two nodes to have the same
colour if the number of neighbours they have in each colour is at most $q$.
Applying the WL algorithm until there are $n$ colours is a good heuristic.

Applications include betweenness centrality, maximum flow.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive hierarchical regular\\ binning of data point features}\\
  D. Floros et al.
\end{book}
Efficient construction of a k-d-tree
from the dyadic encoding of the point coordinates.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{QUBO.jl: a Julia ecosystem for quadratic unconstrained binary optimization}\\
  P.M. Xavier et al.
\end{book}
Many optimization problems can be converted (automatically) to QUBO form
(quadratic unconstrained binary optimization),
which can be solved by many quantum computers.
% Julia is still behind Python (Qiskit) for quantum computing, but this may be changing

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning QUBO models for quantum annealing: a constraint-based approach}\\
  F. Richoux et al.
\end{book}
To convert a combinatorial optimization problem to QUBO form,
we need to convert the constraints into quadratic terms in the objective function.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For the 1-hot constraint,
  since $\sum x_i = 1 \Leftrightarrow ( \sum x_i - 1 ) ^ 2 = 0$,
  we can add $(\sum x_i - 1 )^2$;
\item Permutation constraints are handled similarly.
  \[ \forall \sigma \in \{0,1\}^{n \times n}
  \ \sigma \in \mathfrak S _n
  \Leftrightarrow \sigma \mathbf 1 = \mathbf 1 \wedge \sigma ' \mathbf 1 = 1\]
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-fidelity covariance estimation\\ in the log-Euclidean geometry}\\
  A. Maurais et al. (2023)
\end{book}
The Euclidean multi-fidelity covariance estimator
\[ \hat \Sigma_n^{(0)} + \sum_\ell \alpha_\ell \bigl(
\hat \Sigma_{n_\ell}^{(\ell)} -
\hat \Sigma_{n_{\ell - 1}}^{(\ell)}
\bigr) \]
is not guaranteed to be positive definite.
Replace the Euclidean geometry
with the log-Euclidean one
(\ie, use $\operatorname{Log} \Sigma$ instead of $\Sigma$,
and Fr\'echet average);
the Bures-Wasserstein geometry,
or the log-Cholesky geometry are alternative choices.
(One can compute the optimal sizes $n^*$ and the optimal weights $\alpha^*$
to maximize the MSE with a constraint on the total cost.)

The generalized variance and correlation are
\begin{align*}
  \sigma_X^2 &= \tr \cov [ X X' ] \\
  \rho_{XY} &= \dfrac{
    \tr \cov [ X X' , Y Y' ]
  }{
    \sigma_X \sigma_Y
  }.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation matrix clustering\\ for statistical arbitrage portfolios}\\
  A. Cartea et al. (2023)
\end{book}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take daily returns, for 600 stocks, over 4 years;
\item Remove the market (adjusting for the CAPM beta);
\item Compute the correlation matrix $C$;
\item View it as a weighted graph, with potentially negative weights;
  compute the Laplacians
\begin{align*}
  \bar A_{ij} &= \va{ A_{ij} } \\
  \bar D_{ii} &= \sum_j \va{ A_{ij} }\\
  D_{ii} &= \sum_j A_{ij} \\
  L &= \bar D - \bar A \\
  \bar L_{\text{rw}} &= I - \bar D ^{-1} A \\
  \bar L_{\text{sym}} &= I - \bar D^{-1/2} A \bar D^{-1/2} \\
  A &= A^+ - A^- \\
  D_{ii}^+ &= \sum_j A_{ij}^+, \quad D_{ii}^- = \sum_j \bar A_{ij} \\
  L^{\pm} &= D^\pm - A^\pm \\
  L_{\text{sym}}^\pm &= (D^\pm)^{-1/2}  L^\pm (D^\pm)^{-1/2}  \\
  (L_1,L_2) &= ( L^+ + \tau D^-, L^- + \tau D^+ ), \quad \tau>0\\
  (L_{1,\text{sym}},L_{2,\text{sym}}) &= ( L_{\text{sym}}^+ + \tau I, L_{\text{sym}}^- + \tau I ) \\ 
\end{align*}
\item Reduce the dimension using the $k$ smallest (generalized) eigenvalues of
$\bar L$ or $\bar L_{\text{rw}}$ or $\bar L_{\text{sym}}$
or $(L_1,L_2)$ or $(L_{1,\text{sym}},L_{2,\text{sym}})$;
\item Cluster the resulting points using $k$-means (with the same $k$);
\item Choose $k$ using random matrix theory (RMT), as the number of eigenvalues of $C$
  beyond $\lambda_+ = ( 1 + \sqrt{N/T} )^2$,
  or the proportion of variance explained by the top eigenvalues of $C$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Is there a replication crisis in finance?}\\
  T. Engerslev et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Apparently, no:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Do not use returns, but risk-adjusted returns (CAPM alpha);
\item Adjust for multiple testing, either with BY or, better, a Bayesian model,
  to account for correlation between factors
  (set the prior expected alpha to zero,
  and select the other with emprical Bayes,
  using only out-of-sample data to overcome the puplication bias);
\item Use that Bayesian model to compute the FDR.  
\end{compactitem}
% Bayesian approach to multiple testing
% Data (aggregated by country, 100 countries, 150 factors): jkfactors.com

