\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data-driven subgroup identification\\ for linear regression}\\
  Z. Izzo et al. (2023)
\end{book}
To find a region where the relation $x \mapsto y$ is almost linear,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit a linear model on the $k$ nearest neighbours of each point;
\item Pick the model with the lowest training error
\item Enlarge the neighbourhood to include all points with a low error for that model
\item Pick the largest ball (or polytope) containing only points in that subset.
\end{compactitem}
% Also check: k-NN in weight space; linear model trees

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{XTab: cross-table pretraining\\ for tabular transformers}\\
  B. Zhu et al. (2023)
\end{book}
Self-supervised learning for tabular data can use the following losses:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Reconstruction: reconstruct a corrupted row
  (60\% of the entries have been replaced by resampling from the corresponding column)
\item Contrastive loss: the postive pairs are $(x,\tilde x)$, where $x$ is a row and $\tilde x$
  a corrupted version of it;
  the negative pairs are different rows.
\item Supervised loss: predict the value of a column.
\end{compactitem}
You can pretrain a single model and fine-tune it on different tables and tasks:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Featurize a row into a sequence of ``tokens''
  (embeddings), ending with \textsc{cls}:
  for qualitative columns, learn an embedding,
  for quantitative columns, learn an affine transformation $\RR \to \RR^d$;
\item Feed it to an FT-transformer (or some other transformer variant);
\item Finish with a projection head.
\end{compactitem}
The transformer is shared across tasks and frozen after pretraining;
the featurizers and projection heads are task-specific and have to be re-trained.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TransTab: learning transferable tabular transformers across tables}\\
  Z. Wang and J. Sun
\end{book}
Earlier paper with a similar idea and a \emph{gated transformer}, \ie,
a transformer followed by a gated feedforward layer:
$x \longmapsto W_1 ( W_2 \odot x \oplus W_3 x )$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Revisiting deep learning models\\ for tabular data}\\
  Y. Gorishniy et al.
\end{book}
The FT-Transformer 
tokenizes the rows (\ie, converts them to sequences of embeddings)
before feeding them to the transformer:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For qualitative columns, learn an embedding of the possible values; 
\item For quantitative columns, learn an affine transform $\RR \to \RR^d$;
\item Append a \textsc{cls} token,
\end{compactitem}
Also try a ResNet.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fastformer:\\ additive attention can be all you need}\\
  C. Wu et al.
\end{book}
Efficient transformer variants use:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Hashing (Reformer)
\item Low-rank factorizations (Linformer)
\item A mixture of local and global attention (Longformer, BigBird)
\end{compactitem}
The FastFormer reduces the dimension of the attention matrix.
