\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SAINT: improved neural networks\\ for tabular data via row attention\\ and contrastive pre-training}\\
  G. Somepalli et al.
\end{book}
Transformer on tabular data, alternating between:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Within-row attention,
\item Between-row attention, concatenating the embeddings of all the columns
  (limited to rows in the current batch).
\end{compactitem}
Pre-train in a self-supervised way (contrastive learning with the InfoNCE loss
and/or denoising).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CoDi: co-evolving contrastive diffusion models
  for mixed type tabular synthesis}\\
  C. Lee et al. (2023)
\end{book}
The \emph{diffusion probabilistic model}
can be defined for continuous
\begin{align*}
  q( x_t | x_{t-1} ) &= N \bigl( x_t ; \sqrt{ 1 - \beta_t } x_{t-1} , \beta_t I \bigr) \\
  p_\theta( x_{t-1} | x_t ) &= N \bigl( x_{t-1} ; \mu_\theta(x_t,t), \Sigma_\theta(x_t,t) \bigr)
\end{align*}
or discrete variables
\begin{align*}
  q( x_t | x_{t-1} ) &= (1-\beta_t) \mathbf1_{ x_t = x_{t-1} } + \beta_t \text{Unif}(x_t) \\
  p_\theta( x_{t-1} | x_t ) &= \sum_{x_0} q( x_{t-1} | x_t, x_0 ) p_\theta( x_0, x_t ) \\
  q( x_{t-1} | x_t, x_0 ) &= \text{(Bayes)}.
\end{align*}
Both minimize
\[ \text{KL} \bigl(
  q( x_{t-1} | x_t , x_0 ) \,\|\, p_\theta( x_{t-1} | x_t )
\bigr). \]
For tabular data, use two models, one continuous, one discrete,
each conditioned on the other one at each step.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TabDDPM: modeling tabular data\\ with diffusion models}\\
  A. Kotelnikov
\end{book}
Similar idea, with a quantile transformation of quantitative columns.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Compressing tabular data\\ via latent variable estimation}\\
  A. Montanari and E. Weiner (2023)
\end{book}
To compress tabular data, first cluster the rows and columns ($k$-means on the SVD).

