\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning deep time-index models\\ for time series forecasting}\\
  G. Woo et al. (2023)
\end{book}
Learn a ``time-index'' model, \ie, a 1-dimensional implicit neural representation
(INR, NeRF -- these generalize the classical structural time series models
decomposing a time series into trend, cycle and holiday components)
\[ \hat y_t = f_{\theta, \phi} \bigl( \tau(t) \bigr) \]
where $\tau$ are random Fourier features
and the parameters are separated into
curve-fitting $\theta$
and forecasting/extrapolating (inductive bias) $\phi$:
\begin{align*}
  \theta &\leftarrow \argmin_\theta \text{fitting\,error}(\theta, \phi) &&\text{on } [t-h,t] \\
  \phi &\leftarrow \argmin_\phi \text{forecasting\,error}(\theta, \phi) &&\text{on } [t,t+h].
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-autoregressive conditional diffusion models for time series prediction}\\
  L. Shen and J.T. Kwok (2023)
\end{book}
TimeDiff forecasts a time series using a diffusion model using, as conditioners:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item An AR model of the past, computed as
  \[\sum w_i \odot x_{t-i} + b;\]
\item A mixture of the past, $x_{\leq t}$, transformed with a conv\-net,
  and the future, $x_{>t}$ (a kind of teacher forcing).
\end{compactitem}
The sigmoid-weighted linear unit is
\[ \text{SiLU}(z) = \sigma(z) \odot z . \]
Also check: CSDI, SSSD, FedFormer, NBeats.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CSDI: conditional score-based diffusion models for probabilistic time series imputation}\\
  Y. Tashiro et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Diffusion model to impute the missing values in a time series,
conditioning on the observed values, trained with self-supervised learning.

% TODO: PLOT

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficiently modeling long sequences\\ with structured state spaces}\\
  A. Gu et al. 
\end{book}
The three ways of representing a state space model (SSM),
continuous time, recurrent, and convolutional, are related.
\begin{align*}
  x' &= Ax + Bu \\
  y &= C x \\[2mm]
  x_k &= \bar A x_{k-1} + \bar B u_k \\
  y_k &= C x_k \\
  \bar A &= ( I - \tfrac12 h A )^{-1} ( I + \tfrac12 h A ) \\
  \bar B &= ( I - \tfrac12 h A )^{-1}  h B \\[2mm]
  y &= K * u \\
  K &= ( C \bar A ^0 \bar B , C \bar A ^1 \bar B , \dots ,
  C \bar A ^{n-1} \bar B , C \bar A ^n \bar B )
\end{align*}

With the HiPPO matrix
\[ A_{nk} = - \begin{cases}
  (2n+1) ^ {1/2} (2k+1) ^{1/2} & \text{ if } n > k \\
  n & \text{ if } n = k \\
  0 & \text{ otherwise}
\end{cases} \]
the SSM (S4) memorizes its input $u$.

It is possible to efficiently compute the convolution with $K$
if $A$ is normal ($AA^*=A^*A$;
equivalently, $A=UDU^*$, $D$ diagonal, $UU^*=I$).
The HiPPO matrix is the sum of a normal and a low-rank matrix;
it is still possible to efficiently compute the convolution
with $K$.
With a change of basis, we can replace the normal matrix with a diagonal one.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HiPPO: recurrent memory\\ with optimal polynomial projections}\\
  A. Gu et al. (2020)
\end{book}
To compress the information contained in a function
$f|_{[0,t]}$, online (\ie, progressively, updating the compressed
information as $t$ increases),
project it on a finite-dimensional subspace, e.g., that of polynomials
of degree less than $N$, for some measure $\mu_t$ on $[0,t]$,
\[ g_t = \argmin_{\substack{
    g \in \RR[X] \\ \deg g < N }}
\n{ f_{ \leq t} ^{\mathstrut} - g } _{ L^2(\mu_t) }.
\]
The measure $\mu_t$ defines orthogonal polynomials
$(g_n)_{n < N}$;
the coefficients of $g_t$ in this basis are
\[ c_k(t) = \bigl\langle f_{\leq t} ^{\mathstrut} , g_k \bigr\rangle _{ \mu_t } . \]
They are solution of an ODE.
\[ \dfrac{ d c(t) }{ dt } = A_t c(t) + B_t f(t) \] 
For instance:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Legendre (LMU, Legendre memory unit)
  \begin{align*}
    \mu_t &= \dfrac1{\theta} \mathbf{1} _{ t - \theta, t } \\
    A_{nk} &= \dfrac1\theta \begin{cases}
      (-1)^{n-k} (2n+1) & \text{if } n \geq k \\
      (2n+1) & \text{if } n \leq k
    \end{cases} \\
    B_n &= \dfrac1\theta (2n+1) (-1)^n
  \end{align*}
\item Laguerre
  \begin{align*}
    \mu_t(x) &= e^{ - (t-x) } \mathbf 1_{ x \leq t } \\
    A_{nk} &= \mathbf{1} _{ n \geq k } \\
    B_n &= 1
  \end{align*}
\item Scaled Legendre
  \begin{align*}
    \mu_t &= \dfrac1t \mathbf{1}_{[0,t]} \\
    A_{nk} &= \begin{cases}
      (2n+1)^{1/2} (2k+1)^{1/2} &\text{if } n > k \\
      n+1 &\text{if } n = k \\
      0 &\text{if } n < k
    \end{cases} \\
    B_n &= (2n+1)^{1/2}    
  \end{align*}
\item Fourier recurrent unit (FRU): orthogonal polynomials on the unit circle
  $\{ z : \va z = 1 \}$.
\end{compactitem}
This can be used inside an RNN, to increase memory.   
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{It's raw!\\ Audio generation with state space models}\\
  K. Goel et al.
\end{book}
UNet with S4 blocks for audio generation;
can be bidirectional.

S4 is a state-space model,
not computed iteratively, but all at once,
with a convolution -- this can be done efficiently if the matrix is diagonal-plus-low-rank (1 or 3);
it is initialized with a HiPPO matrix.
Parametrizing the matrix as $\Lambda - pp^*$, $\Lambda$ diagonal,
$p \in \RR^{N\times r}$, instead of $\Lambda + p q^*$,
ensures it is Hurwitz (its eigenvalues have a negative part), \ie,
the corresponding SSM is stable.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diffusion-based time series imputation and forecasting with structured state space models}\\
  J.M. Lopez and N. Strodthoff 
\end{book}
SSSD is a diffusion model with S4 blocks
for missing value imputation in time series.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep latent state space models\\ for time series generation}\\
  L. Zhou et al.
\end{book}
Variant of S4 with an extra latent variable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Simple hardware-efficient long convolutions for sequence modeling}\\
  D.Y. Fu et al. (2023)
\end{book}
The \emph{butterfly decomposition} decomposes the FFT on $n=n_1 n_2$ values
into FFTs on $n_1$ and $n_2$ values
(by reshaping the input as an $n_1 \times n_2$ matrix and applying the FFT on the rows and columns --
you can also fine-tune the FFT coefficients, keeping the same sparsity patterns,
to get an FFT-like transformation).
To improve the performance of the long convolution (S4),
tweak the kernel:
dropout, smoothing (moving average), quashing of small values. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural continuous-discrete state space models for irregularly-sampled time series}\\
  A.F. Ansari et al.
\end{book}
If the observations, in a state space model (SSM)
(observed at irregular intervals)
are high-dimensional, use lower-dimensional auxilliary variables.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1, yscale=.8,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (z0) at (0,2) {$z_{t_0}$};
  \node (z1) at (1,2) {$z_{t_1}$};
  \node (z2) at (2,2) {$z_{t_2}$};
  \node (z3) at (3,2) {$z_{t_3}$};
  \node (z4) at (4,2) {$\cdots$};
  \node (a0) at (0,1) {$a_0$};
  \node (a1) at (1,1) {$a_1$};
  \node (a2) at (2,1) {$a_2$};
  \node (a3) at (3,1) {$a_3$};
  \node (a4) at (4,1) {$\cdots$}; 
  \node (y0) at (0,0) {$y_0$};
  \node (y1) at (1,0) {$y_1$};
  \node (y2) at (2,0) {$y_2$};
  \node (y3) at (3,0) {$y_3$};
  \node (y4) at (4,0) {$\cdots$};
  \draw[->] (z0) -- (z1);
  \draw[->] (z1) -- (z2);
  \draw[->] (z2) -- (z3);
  \draw[->] (z3) -- (z4);
  \draw[->] (z0) -- (a0);
  \draw[->] (z1) -- (a1);
  \draw[->] (z2) -- (a2);
  \draw[->] (z3) -- (a3);
  \draw[->] (a0) -- (y0);
  \draw[->] (a1) -- (y1);
  \draw[->] (a2) -- (y2);
  \draw[->] (a3) -- (y3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Use linear of locally linear dynamics,
which interpolate between SSMs.
\begin{align*}
  dz &= f(z,t) dt + G(z,t) dB \\
  f(z,t) &= F(z) z \\
  G(z,t) &= I \\
  F(z) &= \sum \alpha_j(z) F_j \\
  \alpha(z) &= \text{softmax}\bigl( g(z) \bigr) \\
  g &\text{: neural net} \\
  F_j &\text{: basis matrices}
\end{align*}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Feature programming\\ for multivariate time series prediction}\\
  A. Reneau et al. (2023)
\end{book}
Automatically generate features for multivariate time series by combining operators
$\text{Diff}(x_i,x_j) = x_i - x_j$,
Lag, $\Delta$, $\Delta^2$, MA.

The Ising model
\begin{align*}
P(\sigma) &\propto
\exp - \beta \left(
\sum_{ (i,j) \in E } J_{ij} \sigma_i \sigma_j + \sum_{ i \in V } h_i \sigma_i 
\right) \\
\sigma_i & \in \{\pm1\}
\end{align*}
can be turned into a dynamic process (Glauber dynamics).
\begin{align*}
  P( \sigma_{i,t+1} ) &\propto \exp( \sigma_{ i, t+1 } \gamma_{it} ) \\
  \gamma_{it} &= \sum_j J_{ij} \sigma_j + h_i 
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning perturbations\\ to explain time series predictions}\\
  J. Enguehard (2023)
\end{book}
To explain a model $f$,
one can use:
a local linear approximation,
the gradient of the output wrt the features,
or a perturbation: modify the input on a mask, as small/large as possible,
to decrease/preserve the performance as much as possible.
Instead of a fixed perturbation (average, moving average, Gaussian blur, etc.)
use a neural network:
\[ \!\!\!\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & m, \text{NN} \\
  \text{To minimize} & d \bigl[
    f(x) , f\bigl( \Phi(x,m) \bigr)
    \bigr] + \n{ m }_1 + \n{ \text{NN}(x) }_1 \\
  \text{Such that} & 0 \leq m \leq 1 \\
  \text{Where} & \Phi(x,m) = m \odot x + (1-m) \odot \text{NN}(x)
\end{array} \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FaDIn: fast discretized inference for Hawkes processes with general parametric kernels}\\
  G. Staerman et al. (2023)
\end{book}
To speed up the estimation of multivariate Hawkes processes with parametric (non-exponential) kernels
(which is quadratic in general), assume the kernel has bounded support
(limit on the influence of a past event)
and discretize the temporal point process.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FEDformer: frequency-enhanced decomposed transformer for long-term series forecasting}\\
  T. Zhou et al.
\end{book}
Use a transformer in Fourier (or wavelet) space,
but only keep a random subset of the Fourier components.
For the wavelets, process the different scales separately.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1, yscale=.8,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (x) at (0,2) {$x$};
  \node[fill=blue!15] (lin) at (1,2) {lin};
  \node[fill=blue!15] (F) at (2,2) {$\mathscr F$};
  \node[fill=blue!15] (s) at (2,1) {select};
  \node[fill=blue!15,align=center] (A) at (3.5,0) {attention\\ or linear};
  \node[fill=blue!15] (p) at (5,1) {pad};
  \node[fill=blue!15] (F1) at (5,2) {$\mathscr F^{-1}$};
  \node (y) at (6,2) {};
  \draw[->] (x) -- (lin);
  \draw[->] (lin) -- (F);
  \draw[->] (F) -- (s);
  \draw[->] (s) -- (A);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (p);
  \draw[->] (p) -- (F1);
  \draw[->] (F1) -- (y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
To extract the trend, use average filters of different sizes, and combine them.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.7, yscale=.5,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (x) at (.2,1) {$x$};
  \node[fill=blue!15] (t) at (1,2) {trends};
  \node[fill=blue!15] (l) at (1,0) {linear};
  \node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (p) at (2,2) {\footnotesize$\times$};
  \node[fill=blue!15] (s) at (2,0) {softmax};
  \node (y) at (2.5,2) {};
  \draw[->] (x) -- (t);
  \draw[->] (x) -- (l);
  \draw[->] (t) -- (p);
  \draw[->] (s) -- (p);
  \draw[->] (l) -- (s);
  \draw[->] (p) -- (y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
  
