In dimension 2, $\text{Ric}(g) = k g$, where $k$ is the Gauss curvature.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diffusion improves graph learning}\\
  J. Gasteiger et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The generalized diffusion matrix is
\[ S = \sum_{ k \geq 0 } \theta_k T^k, \]
for instance PPR (personalized page rank),
$T=AD^{-1}$, $\theta_k  = \alpha(1-\alpha)^k$,
or heat diffusion,
$T=AD^{-1}$, $\theta_k = e^{-t} t^k / k!$
(add weighted self-loops to the adjacency matrix $A$;
you can also use the symmetric transition matrix $T_{\text{sym}} = D^{-1/2} A D^{-1/2} $
instead of the random walk one $T_{\text{rw}} = A D^{-1}$).

Truncate it (keeping the top $k$ entries in each column,
or the entries above some threshold)
and use the resulting (weighted) graph
instead of the original one
(graph diffusion convolution, GDC, DIGL).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DRew: dynamically rewired\\ message passing with delay}\\
  B. Gutteridge et al. (2023)
\end{book}
To limit oversquashing in GNNs, have each node at layer $k$ directly receive input from nodes $k$ hops away
(instead of via its immediate neighbours)
either their current (DRew) or initial ($\nu$DRew) state.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Expander graph propagation}\\
  A. Deac et al. (2022)
\end{book}
The \emph{Cheeger constant} of a connected graph $G = (V,E)$ is
\[ h(G) = \min_{\substack{ A \subset V \\ 0 < \va A < \frac12 \va V }}
\dfrac{ \va{ \partial A } }{ \va A }. \]

Let $0 = \lambda_0 < \lambda_1 < \lambda_2 < \cdots$
be the eigenvalues of the Laplacian of a graph $G$.

An \emph{expander family} is a family of graphs
$(G_i)_{i \in I}$
such that $\forall i \ \lambda_1(G_i) \geq c$, for some $c>0$;
equivalently, $\forall i \ h(G_i) \geq \epsilon$, for some $\epsilon > 0$.
In an expander family with bounded degree,
\[ \text{diam}(G_i) = O\bigl( \log \va{ V_i } \bigr). \]

The \emph{Cayley graph} of a group $G$ wrt a generating set $S$
has the elements of $G$ as nodes, and edges $g \to gs$
for $g \in G$, $s \in S$.
\[ G_n = \text{Cayley} \left(
\text{SL}_2 ( \ZZ / n ) , \left\{
\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
,
\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
\right\}
\right) \]
is an expander family with $O(n^3)$ nodes.
$G_n$ is locally tree-like, up to distance $O(\log n)$.

% TODO: PLOT [cayley graph]

All the edges have negative curvature,
but not enough to produce oversquashing --
in any case, expansion, sparsity and non-negative curvature
are incompatible: if there is a bound on the degree and the Cheeger constant,
there are only finitely many such graphs.

To address oversquashing, interleave
a GNN on the input graph
with a GNN on the Cayley graph
(build it breadth-first, and only keep the first $\va{V}$ nodes -- it is connected.

Other ways of addressing oversquashing include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Adding a master node;
\item Feature augmentation;
\item Graph rewiring (personalized page-rank, stochastic discrete Ricci flow).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Relevant walk search\\ for explaining graph neural networks}\\
  P. Xiong et al. (2023)
\end{book}
With the max-product algorithm, layerwise relevance propagation (LRP)
can return the top-$k$ most relevant walks in polynomial time.
% ALso check: LRP; GMMExplainer, PGExplainer (mask unimportant edges/features)
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Brauer's group equivariant neural network}\\
  E. Pearce-Crump (2023)
\end{book}
Description of a generating set of
\[ \hom_G \bigl(
(\RR^n)^{\tens k} , (\RR^n)^{\tens \ell}
\bigr) \]
for $G=\text{O}(n)$, $\text{SO}(n)$ or $\text{Sp}(n)$,
where the action of $G$ on $(\RR^n)^{\tens k}$ is
\[ g \cdot ( x_1 \tens \cdots \tens x_k ) =
(g x_1 ) \tens \cdots \tens (g x_k). \]

In an equivariant neural net,
the layers are representations (rather than just vector spaces)
and the maps between them are equivatiant (represenattion morphisms).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural execution of graph algorithms}\\
  P. Veli\v ckovi\'c et al.
\end{book}
Train a graph neural net to learn,
simultaneously, several graph algorithms
(e.g., breadth-first search and Bellman-Ford shortest paths)
with a shared ``processor'' and task-specific encoders and decoders;
reuse this processor for other graph tasks.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.4,yscale=.8,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (i1) at (0,2) {input\strut};  
  \node (i2) at (0,0) {input\strut};
  \node[inner sep = 2pt,fill=blue!15] (e1) at (1,2) {enc1\strut};
  \node[inner sep = 2pt,fill=blue!15] (e2) at (1,0) {enc2\strut};
  \node[inner sep = 2pt,fill=blue!15] (p) at (2,1) {processor\strut};
  \node[inner sep = 2pt,fill=blue!15] (d1) at (3,2) {dec1\strut};
  \node[inner sep = 2pt,fill=blue!15] (d2) at (3,0) {dec2\strut};
  \node (o1) at (4,2) {output\strut};
  \node (o2) at (4,0) {output\strut};
  \node at (5,2) {(task1)\strut};
  \node at (5,0) {(task2)\strut};
  \draw[->] (i1) -- (e1);
  \draw[->] (e1) -- (p);
  \draw[->] (p) -- (d1);
  \draw[->] (d1) -- (o1);
  \draw[->] (i2) -- (e2);
  \draw[->] (e2) -- (p);
  \draw[->] (p) -- (d2);
  \draw[->] (d2) -- (o2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

The \emph{Pr\"ufer sequence} of a tree with nodes labeled 1 to $n$ is obtained as follows:
in step $i$, remove the leaf with the smallest label and store the label of its neighbour in position $i$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural priority queues\\ for graph neural networks}\\
  R. Jain et al. (2023)
\end{book}
Differentiable priority queue.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Recursive algorithmic reasoning}\\
  D. Jayalath et al.
\end{book}
Augment GNNs with a stack, for recursive tasks, e.g., depth-first search.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to boost training by periodic nowcasting near future weights}\\
  J. Jang et al. (2023)
\end{book}
The weight nowcaster network forecasts future (short-term) weights and allows to intermittently skip a few epochs.

% TODO: PLOT? (what is figure 8?)
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A unifying causal framework for analyzing dataset shift-stable learning algorithms}\\
  A. Subbaswamy et al. (2022)
\end{book}
When using a causal graph, pay attention to ``unstable edges'' --
edges whose conditional probability distribution may change.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Breaking the curse of depth\\ in graph convolutional networks\\ via refined initialization strategy}\\
  S. Wang et al. (2023)
\end{book}
To check if the initialization of a (graph) neural net is satisfactory, look at
forward signal propagation, backward signal propagation, and diversity propagation,
\[
\dfrac{ \E \n{ H^{(L)} }_F^2 }{ \E \n X _F^2 }
\qquad
\E \n{ \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial W^{(1)} } }
\qquad
\E \dfrac{ \operatorname{Dir} H^{(L)} }{ \n{ H^{(L)} }_F^2 }
\]
where the Dirichlet energy is
$\operatorname{Dir} H = \tr( H' L H )$
and $L$ is the normalized Laplacian.

We do not want those quantities to vanish or explode as $L \to \infty$;
with traditional initialization schemes (Kaiming, Xavier), they do.

MetaInit and SPoGInit scale the weights, layerwise, to improve propagation
(make the signal propagations close to 1, and the diversity propagation large).

Additing residual connections, initialized at (0,1), also helps (ReZero, ReZeroGCN)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model soups: averaging weights of multiple fine-tuned models improves accuracy\\ without increasing inference time}\\
  M. Wortsman et al. (2022)
\end{book}
When fine-tuning, instead of selecting the best model after hyperparameter search,
or averaging the predictions of all the models (ensembling),
average the weights, either of all the models (uniform soup),
or by adding the models to the soup only if they improve the test set accuracy (greedy soup).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust fine-tuning of zero-shot models}\\
  M. Wortsman et al.
\end{book}
Fine-tuning improves accuracy on the target distribution,
but reduces robustness to distribution shifts.
Instead, ensemble the weights of the zero-shot (not fine-tuned)
and fine-tuned models
(WiSE-FT).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Categorizing variants of Goodhart's law}\\
  D. Manheim and S. Garrabrant (2019)
\end{book}
There are several categories of Goodhart's law:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The metric is a noisy version of the intended goal -- the optimization may be misled by the noise;
\item The metric is a good approximation of the intended goal,
  but only in a small region of the search space -- the optimizer may leave that region;
\item The causal relation between the metric and the intended goal is not direct,
  and someone (the regulator, or the agent --
  in particular if his own goal is different (cobra effect))
  manipulates $X$.
\end{compactitem}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node[circle,draw] (X) at (0,1) {$X$};
  \node[ellipse,draw] (m) at (-1,0) {metric};
  \node[ellipse,draw] (g) at (1,0) {goal};
  \draw[->] (X) -- (m);
  \draw[->] (X) -- (g);
  \begin{scope}[shift={(4,-.5)}]  
    \node[circle,draw] (X) at (0,1) {$X$};
    \node[ellipse,draw] (m) at (0,0) {metric};
    \node[ellipse,draw] (g) at (0,2) {goal};
    \draw[->] (g) -- (X);
    \draw[->] (X) -- (m);
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TRAK: Attributing model behaviour at scale}\\
  S.M. Park et al. (2023)
\end{book}
\emph{Data attribution} decomposes the output of a model
$z \mapsto f(z, \theta)$
into a sum of contributions of the training samples $S$.
\[ f\bigl( x, \theta^*(S') \bigr) \approx \tau ( z, S ) \cdot \mathbf 1 _{ S'}
\qquad \text{for } S' \subset S \]
This can be done with a lasso linear regression (datamodel)
\[ \tau( z, S ) = \argmin_{ \beta \in \RR^n } \sum_i \bigl[
  \beta ' \mathbf 1_{ S_i } - f\bigl( z, \theta^*(S_i ) \bigr)   
  \bigr] ^2 + \lambda \n \beta _1
\]
for random subsets $S_1,\dots,S_n \subset S$
of sizes $\alpha \va S$, $\alpha \in S$,
but this requires fitting $n$ models (thousands).

Instead:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Linearize the model; 
\item Reduce the dimension (random projection)
  \[ \phi( z_i ) = P' \nabla_{\!\theta}  f\bigl( z_i , \theta^* (S) \bigr) ; \]
\item The model has become a logistic regression:
  we can directly compute the approximate leave-out-one influence; 
\item Ensemble (over random subsets $S_i \subset S$);
\item Soft-threshold.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Eigen memory trees}\\
  M. Rucker et al. (2022)
\end{book}
High-dimensional variant of quad-trees:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The data is stored in the leaves of a binary tree;
\item Routing is done by testing if
  \[ \langle x , \texttt{node.router} \rangle \geq \texttt{node.boundary}; \]
\item When a leaf has become too large, it is split using the first principal component of the points it contains.
\end{compactitem}
Application: memory for episodic replay in reinforcement learning (contextual bandits).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Automated search for conjectures\\ on mathematical constants\\ using analysis of integer sequences}\\
  O. Razon et al.
\end{book}
Continued fractions can be written
\begin{align*}
  c &=
  a_0 + \dfrac{ b_1 }{
    a_1 + \dfrac{ b_2 }{
      a_2 + \dfrac{ b_3 }{
        a_3 + \cdots
      }
    }
  } \\
  &= a_0 + \operatorname*{K}_{ i=1 }^{ \infty } \dfrac{ b_i }{ a_i } \\
  &= a_0 +
  \dfrac{ b_1 }{ a_1 + }
  \dfrac{ b_2 }{ a_2 + }
  \dfrac{ b_3 }{ a_3 + }
  \cdots
\end{align*}

In a sign-interlaced continued fraction,
$(b_i)_{i\geq1}$ is periodic and $\forall i\ b_i \in \{\pm1\}$.

The non-terminating Euclidean algorithm for $(\alpha,1)$
computes the simple ($\forall i \ b_i = 1$) continued fraction of $\alpha$.
It can be extended to allow for sign variation ($\forall i\ b_i \in \{\pm1\}$),
\ie, negative remainders.

The Berlekamp-Massey algorithm finds the minimal linear recurrence sequence with integer coefficients
producing a given integer sequence.

Given a constant $\alpha$ (e.g., $\tan 1$),
generate its sign-interlaced continued fractions
(for all sign patterns up to period $T$)
and look for a linear recurrence for the $a_i$'s.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Refining generative process with discriminator guidance in score-based diffusion models}\\
  D. Kim et al. (2023)
\end{book}
Given a pre-trained diffusion model,
train a discriminator $d_\phi(x,t)$
to distinguish between real images and generated ones,
at all noise levels $t$.
Adjust the learned score function by adding
\[ \nabla \log \dfrac{
  d_\phi( x_t, t )
}{
  1 - d_\phi( x_t, t )
}. \]
  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding deep generative models\\ with generalized empirical likelihoods}\\
  S. Ravuri et al.
\end{book}
Given $n$ independent samples
$x_1,\dots,x_n \sim p$,
the \emph{empirical likelihood} method checks if their mean is $c \in \RR^d$
\[ \E_{ x \sim p } [x] \overset?= c \]
using the weighted empirical distribution
$\hat p_\pi = \sum \pi_i \delta_{ x_i } $
maximizing $\text{KL}( \hat p_n \| p )$ with the prescribed mean,
where $\hat p_n = \sum_i \frac1n \delta_{ x_i }$.
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \pi \in \RR^n \\
  \text{To maximize} & \sum_i \log \pi_i \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ \pi_i \geq 0 \\
  & \sum \pi_i = 1 \\
  & \sum \pi_i x_i = c
\end{array} \]
The \emph{generalized empirical likelihood}
uses a moment condition instead,
\[ \E_{ x \sim p_\pi } \bigl[ m(x,c) \bigr] = 0 \]
and a Cressie-Read divergence instead of the KL divergence.

Such divergences include
\[ \begin{array}{l@{\quad}l}
  \text{KL}( \hat p_n \| p_\pi ) &  \displaystyle\operatorname{Maximize} \prod \pi_i \\[1mm]
  \text{KL}( p_\pi \| \hat p_n ) & \displaystyle \operatorname{Maximize} - \sum \pi_i \log \pi_i \\[1mm]
  \displaystyle\dfrac12 \sum \biggl( \pi_i - \dfrac1n \biggr) ^2 &
  \operatorname{Minimize} \biggl( \pi_i - \dfrac1n \biggr) ^2.
\end{array}
\]

Moments include
\[ \begin{array}{l@{\quad}l}
  x - c & \text{mean} \\
  \nabla_{\!\theta} \log p_\theta(x) & \text{score function} \\
  \bigl[ \phi(x) , \phi(x) \phi(x)' \bigr] - c & \text{Fr\'echet inception distance} \\
  k(x,t_i) - k(y,t_i) & \text{mean embedding.}
\end{array} \]

The mean embedding, which minimizes
\[ \sum_i \left[
  \E_{ x \sim p } k(x,t_i) - \E_{ x \sim q } k(x, t_i) 
  \right] ^2 \]
for ``witness points'' $t_i \sim r$, for some distribution $r$,
is related to the \emph{maximum mean discrepancy} (MMD)
\[
\E_{ x_1, x_2 \sim p } \bigl[ k(x_1,x_2) \bigr] +
\E_{ y_1, y_2 \sim q } \bigl[ k(y_1,y_2) \bigr]
- 2 \E_{\substack{ x \sim p \\ y \sim q }} \bigl[ k(x,y) \bigr].
\]

Try the mean embedding, with the exponential kernel
$k(x,y) = \exp( x' y / d )$
and the reverse KL divergence (exponential tilting).

With inception, VGG16 or BYOL features $\phi$,
you can compare images from a generative model with those from the training data,
and highlight those unlikely in the other set (e.g., missing modes).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DreamFusion: text-to-3D using 2D diffusion}\\
  B. Poole et al.
\end{book}
Generate a 3D object as a NeRF
(neural radiance field) from text
using a pretrained text-to-image diffusion model (CLIP);
the model is optimized anew for each new text.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=1,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
