\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Expectile risk quadrangle and applications}\\
  V. Kuzmenko et al. (2023)
\end{book}
A \emph{quadrangle} associated to a statistic is a set of 4 functionals, e.g.,
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Statistic}  & S(x) &= \text{VaR}_\alpha(X) \\
  &\text{Risk}       & R(X) &= \text{CVaR}_\alpha(S) \\
  &\text{Deviation}  & D(X) &= \text{CVaR}_\alpha[ X - \E X ] \\
  &\text{Regret}     & V(X) &= (1-\alpha)^{-1} \E[ X^+ ] \\
  &\text{Error}      & E(X) &= \E\left[ \dfrac{\alpha}{1-\alpha} X^+ + X^- \right].
\end{align*}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.3,yscale=.8,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \node (R) at (0,2) {Risk $R$};
  \node (D) at (2,2) {Deviation $D$};
  \node (V) at (0,1) {Regret $R$};
  \node (E) at (2,1) {Error $E$};
  \node (S) at (1,0) {Statistic $S$};
  \draw[->] (V) -- (R);
  \draw[->] (V) -- (S);
  \draw[->] (E) -- (S);
  \draw[->] (E) -- (D);
  \draw[->,transform canvas={shift={(0pt,+1.5pt)}}] (R) -- (D);
  \draw[->,transform canvas={shift={(0pt,-1.5pt)}}] (D) -- (R);
  \draw[->,transform canvas={shift={(0pt,+1.5pt)}}] (V) -- (E);
  \draw[->,transform canvas={shift={(0pt,-1.5pt)}}] (E) -- (V);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

One can define quadrangles (they are not unique) for the \emph{expectile}
\[ e_q(X) = \argmin_{ c \in \RR } \E_q[ X - c ] \]
where the asymmetric quadratic error is
\[ \E_q[X] = q \E\bigl[ (X^+)^2 \bigr] + (1-q) \E\bigl[ (X^-)^2 \bigr]. \]
It is the solution of (first order condition)
\[ q \E\bigl[ (X-c)^+ \bigr] = (1-q) \E \bigl[ (X-c)^- \bigr]. \]
This leads to estimators of expectile as solutions of linear programs.

