\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Latent factor analysis in short panels}\\
  A.P. Fortin et al. (2023)
\end{book}
Test for the number of factors using the norm and eigenvalues of $\hat S$,
\begin{align*}
  \hat S &= \Sigma^{-1/2} M ( V - \Sigma ) M' \Sigma^{-1/2} \\
  V &= \dfrac1n Y Y' \quad \text{($Y$ centered)} \\
  \Sigma &= \dfrac1n \epsilon \epsilon'
\end{align*}
where the factor model is
\[ y_i = \mu + F \beta_i + \epsilon_i . \]
It can be estimated using
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\operatorname{diag} V = \text{diag}( F F' + \Sigma )$;
\item $F$ contains the first $k$ eigenvectors of $V \Sigma^{-1}$,
  with eigenvalues $1 + \hat \gamma_j$, normalized so that
  $F' \Sigma^{-1} F = \text{diag} ( \hat \gamma_1 , \dots , \hat \gamma_k )$.
\end{compactitem}
% Ref: An introduction to multivariate statistical analysis (T.W> Anderson, 2003), chapters 11 and 14
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple method for predicting\\ covariance matrices of financial returns}\\
  K. Johansson et al. (2023)
\end{book}
The iterated EWMA estimator of the covariance matrix combines an EWMA
for the volatilities and one for the correlation matrix, with a smaller half-life
for the former.
Combine (the Choleski factors of the precision matrices of)
several such estimators
for different pairs of half-lives (or other estimators)
to maximize the log-likelihood -- the weights are the solution
of a convex optimization problem
(implementation: \texttt{cvxgrp/cov\_pred\_finance}).

Use the regret (difference in log-likelihood with the best (in-sample) constant variance matrix)
or the performance of some portfolio (minimum variance, maximum diversity, risk parity, etc.)
to compare estimators.

