\textbf{4.} Deep neural networks are easier to train
and generalize better than shallow ones.

\textbf{5.} The cross-entropy loss is equal, up to an additive constant,
to the negative log-likelihood.
\begin{align*}
  \text{KL}( q \,\|\, p ) &= \E_{ z \sim q } \biggl[ \log \dfrac{ q(z) }{ p(z) } \biggr] \\
  \hat\theta &= \argmin_\theta \text{KL} ( \text{data} \| \text{model}_\theta ) \\
  &= \argmin_\theta \E_{ z \sim q_{\text{data}} } \biggl[ \log \dfrac{ q_{\text{data}} (z) }{ p_\theta(z) } \biggr] \\
  &= \argmin_\theta \E_{ z \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ - \log  p_\theta(z)  \bigr]  
\end{align*}
Many other losses can be used, e.g.,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Negative log-likelihood from other models;
\item Pinball loss (quantile regression);
\item Focal loss (downweight well-calssified examples);
\item Hinge loss (SVM);
\item Exponential loss (AdaBoost);
\item Learning-to-rank;
\item etc.
\end{compactitem}

\text{6.}
Adam combines momentum and RMSProp
(parameter-specific learning rates);
it often makes rapid initial progress,
but you may want to switch to SGD after a while.
Learning rate warm-up may be needed.

\textbf{7.}
To avoid vanishing or exploding gradients,
initialize the weights so that the activations have mean 0 and variance 1,
e.g., with He initialization
\[ \sigma^2 = \dfrac{ 4 }{ d_{\text{in}} + d_{\text{out}} } \]
(initially derived for linear layers)
or Glorot initialization (multiply by 2, to account for
ReLU activation functions).

\textbf{8.}
There are three sources of error:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Noise: the data generation process is stochastic; 
\item Bias: the data generation process is not in the family of models considered;
\item Variance: the model is estimated from a finite sample.
\end{compactitem}

The \emph{double descent} phenomenon depends on the loss,
the initialization, the optimization algorithm
(which provides implicit regularization);
it is more visible with noisy training data.

Adding more data can have a negative impact on performance --
the model is less over-parametrized.

