% Page 135: good definition of the "curse of dimensionality"

Data drift can manifest itself as:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Change in $x$: covariate shift;
\item Change in $y$: prior shift;
\item Change in $y|x$: concept shift.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{9.} The discreteness of the time steps in gradient descent are
equivalent to an implicit regularization:
% Derivation pages 156-157
\begin{align*}
  \text{loss}_{\text{GD}} (\phi) &= \text{loss}(\phi) + \dfrac\alpha4 \n{ \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial \phi } }^2 \\
  \phi_{t+1} &= \phi_t - \alpha \dfrac{ \partial \text{loss} }{ \partial \phi },
\end{align*}
\ie, the trajectory avoids places with a steep gradient
(and the regularization becomes stronger with larger step sizes $\alpha$).

The minibatches in SGD introduce another implicit regularization
\[ \dfrac{ \alpha }{ 4B } \sum_{ b = 1 } ^B
\n{ \dfrac{ \partial \text{loss}_b }{ \partial \phi } -
  \dfrac{ \partial \text{loss} }{ \partial \phi }
}^2 \]
\ie, the trajectory prefers places where gradients
do not differ too much between batches.

To improve performance, try early stopping,
ensembling, dropout,
noise (in the inputs (adversarial training), the weights,
or the labels (label smoothing)),
pretraining, augmentation,
self-supervised learning.

\textbf{11.}
Very deep networks suffer from \emph{shattered gradients}:
small changes in the input can lead to large changes in the gradient of the loss.
Residual (skip) connections address that problem.
A ResNet can be seen as an ensemble of smaller networks:
