Batch normalization helps with exploding/vanishing gradients.

Variations of ResNets include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item UNet;
\item Stacked hourglass networks (stacked UNets);
\item DenseNet (concatenates the outputs of all previous layers to feed the current layer).
\end{compactitem}

% 12. Transformers

% 13. GNN

\textbf{13.}
An \emph{inductive} model sees labeled data at training time
and unlabeled data at test time.
A \emph{transductive} model sees both labeled and unlabeled data
at training time (semi-supervised learning) --
node classification models are often transductive.

Graph attention
\begin{align*}
  S_{mn} &= a \left[ \phi' \begin{pmatrix} h_m \\ h_n \end{pmatrix} \right] \\
  H &\leftarrow a \bigl[
    H' \cdot \text{Softmask}( S, A+I )
    \bigr]
\end{align*}
differs from traditional attention:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $Q = K = V = H$;
\item There is a mask, $A+I$;
\item $S \neq K' Q$.
\end{compactitem}

For large graphs, minibatch training is tricky:
try adding a subset of the nodes in the receptive field at each stage,
or sample a subgraph with random walks.

\textbf{14.} Unsupervised learning includes
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item GANs;
\item VAEs;
\item Normalizing flows;
\item Diffusion models.
\end{compactitem}

% TODO: DIAGRAM PAGE 269?

The inception score (of an image generation model)
is the Fr\'echet distance between the latent representations
(penultimate activations of an inception model)
of real and generated data,
after Gaussian approximation.
\[ \n{ \mu_X - \mu_Y }^2 + \tr \bigl[
  \Sigma_X + \Sigma_Y - 2 ( \Sigma_X \Sigma_Y )^{1/2}
  \bigr] \]

\textbf{15.}
\d{GANs} are difficult to train and suffer from \emph{mode dropping},
or even \emph{mode collapse}.
\emph{Minibatch discrimination} adds a penalty to ensure diversity in each minibatch.

If the discriminator is too good
(in particular, if it recognizes that real and generated data are disjoint),
the gradients are uninformative and the generator cannot improve.
The \emph{Wasserstein loss} does not have that problem: the gradients
remain informative.

GAN variants include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Conditional GAN: generator and discriminator are given the class of the input; 
\item Classifier GAN: the generator is given the class,
  and the discriminator tries to recover it;
\item InfoGAN: the discriminator tries to recover part of the noise given to the generator (this helps disentangle the latent space);
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.6, yscale=.3,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \node (c) at (0,0) {$c$};
  \node (z) at (0,2) {$z$};
  \node[inner sep = 2pt,fill=blue!15] (g) at (1,1) {\footnotesize generator};
  \node (x2) at (2,1) {$x^*$};
  \node (x1) at (2,3) {$x$};
  \node[inner sep = 2pt,fill=blue!15] (d) at (3,2) {\footnotesize discriminator};
  \node[anchor=west] (p) at (4,3) {$P[\text{real}]$};
  \node[anchor=west] (c2) at (4,1) {$\hat c$};
  \draw[->] (z) -- (g);
  \draw[->] (c) -- (g);
  \draw[->] (g) -- (x2);
  \draw[->] (x1) -- (d);
  \draw[->] (x2) -- (d);
  \draw[->] (d) -- (p);
  \draw[->] (d) -- (c2);
  \node[below=0pt of x2] {\footnotesize generated};
  \node[above=0pt of x1] {\footnotesize real};
  \node[above=0pt of z] {\footnotesize latent};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\item Superresolution GAN;
\item CycleGAN;
\item StyleGAN:
  add the latent variable (and the noise) at several points in the network, \ie,
  at different scales;
\item MADGAN: several generators, and the discriminator tries to guess which one was used.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{16.}
\d{Normalizing flows}
are invertible layers (or networks)
whose Jacobians have an easy-to-compute determinant.
For linear layers, this is easy to obtain with the LU decomposition
\begin{align*}
  f(h) &= \beta + \Omega h \\
  \Omega &= PL ( U + D ) \\
  P &\in \mathfrak S_n \\
  L &=
\left[\!\!\!  
