\textbf{17.} % VAE
To model $p(x)$, latent variable models
model $p(x,z)$,
often as $p(x,z) = p(x|z) p(z)$,
and marginalize~$z$:
$p(x) = \E_z p(x,z)$.
Examples include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Gaussian mixture;
  \begin{align*}
    z &\sim \text{Discrete} \\
    x | z &\sim N( \mu_z , \sigma_z^2 ) 
  \end{align*}
\item Nonlinear Gaussian mixtures
  \begin{align*}
    z &\sim N(0,I) \\
    x | z &\sim N\bigl( f_\phi(z) , \sigma^2 I )
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}
The expectation $\E_z p(x,z)$ is intractable,
but amenable to variational inference.
\begin{align*}
  \log p(x) &= \log \int p(x,z) dz \\
  &= \log \int q(z) \dfrac{ p(x,z) }{ q(z) } dz && \text{($q$ arbitrary)} \\
  &\geq \int q(z) \log \dfrac{ p(x,z) }{ q(z) } dz && \text{(Jensen)} \\
  &= \text{ELBO}(q,p)
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
  \text{ELBO}(q,p)
  &= \int q(z) \log \dfrac{ p(x,z) }{ q(z) } dz \\
  &= \int q(z) \log \dfrac{ p(z|x) p(x) }{ q(z) } dz \\
  &= \int q(z) \log p(x) dz +
  \int q(z) \log \dfrac{ p(z|x) }{ q(z) } dz \\
  &= \log p(x) + \text{KL} \bigl( q(z) \,\|\, p(z|x) \bigr) 
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
  \text{ELBO}(q,p)
  &= \int q(z) \log \dfrac{ p(x,z) }{ q(z) } dz \\
  &= \int q(z) \log \dfrac{ p(x|z) p(z) }{ q(z) } dz \\
  &= \int q(z) \log p(x|z) dz +
  \int q(z) \dfrac{ p(z) }{ q(z) } dz \\
  &= \int q(z) \log p(x|z) dz + \text{KL}\bigl( q(z) \,\|\, p(z) \bigr) \\
  &= \text{reconstruction loss} + \text{distance to prior}
\end{align*}
This is the loss used by VAEs.

For the distributions, use
\begin{align*}
  p_\phi(x|z) &= \text{Norm}_x\bigl( f(z,\phi) , \sigma^2 I ) \\
  q_{\theta,x}(z) &= \text{Norm}_z\bigl( g_\mu^{\mathstrut}(x,\theta) , g_\Sigma^{\mathstrut}(x,\theta) \bigr) \\
  p(z) &= \text{Norm}_z( 0, I ).
\end{align*}

The first term is intractable, but can be approximated with Monte Carlo, by sampling
$z \sim q_{\theta,x}$ (one sample may be good enough).
The second term is the KL divergence between two Gaussians,
\[ \text{KL} \bigl( N(\mu,\Sigma) \| N(0,I) \bigr) =
\tfrac12 \bigl( \tr \Sigma + \mu' \mu - n - \log \det \Sigma \bigr).
\]

The $\beta$-VAE rescales the second term (KL penalty)
\[ \text{loss} = \log p_\phi(x,z^*) - \beta \cdot \text{KL}
\bigl( q_{\theta,x} (z) \| p(z) \bigr) \]
where $z^*$ is a single sample.

% p. 338: reparametrization trick

\textbf{18.}
\d{Diffusion models} progressively add noise to an image
\begin{align*}
  z_0 &= x \\
  z_t &= \sqrt{ 1 - \beta_t } z_{t-1} + \sqrt{ \beta_t } \epsilon_t
\end{align*}
and try to reverse this process.
If we knew $x$, we could compute $q( z_{t-1} | z_t, x )$ -- it is Gaussian.
Approximate $q( z_{t-1} | z_t )$ with Gaussian distributions
\[ z_{t-1} | z_t \sim N\bigl( f_t( z_t , \phi_t ) , \sigma_t^2 I \bigr) \]
and use variational inference.
% Details; clear pseudo-code

StableDiffision does this in a latent space (smaller than pixel space).

% 19. Reinforcement learning [not read]

\textbf{20.}
It is not clear why deep learning works:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item It is not because the dataset is easy to learn: one can fit random data almost as easily; 
\item It is not because of (explicit or implicit) regularization; 
\item It is not because SGD, with small batches, moves easily between valleys --
  large batches work equally well;
\item Activation functions are important: they should provide informative gradients; 
\item Initialization is only important for deep networks;
\item Inductive bias (CNNs for grid data, transformers for $\mathfrak S_n$-invariance)
  is important;
\item Overparametrization and depth are important
  (lottery ticket hypothesis: a large network can be seen as an ensemble of smaller subnetworks, one of which may have been initialized near a minimum);
  overparametrization also allows the model to be smoother between data points; 
\item Regularization can explain double descent and \emph{grokking}
  (sudden improvement in generalizarion long after the training error reached zero)
  % The norm of the weights is related to the Lipschitz constant
\end{compactitem}

The loss surface has interesting properties:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item There are many global minima;
  they seem to be connected by low-loss regions;
\item The optimization path lies in a low-dimensional subspace;
\item There are few or no bad local minima (but there are many saddle points).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple explanation of partial least squares}\\
  K.S. Ng (2013)
\end{book}
The principal components of $X$ (assuming the data is centered)
can be computed from the eigen decomposition of $X'X$.
\begin{align*}
  & X'X = P D P' && \text{eigen decomposition} \\
  & P && \text{principal components} \\
  & T = XP && \text{scores} \\
  & X = T P' && \text{(because $P^{-1} = P'$)}
\end{align*}
Often, we only keep the first $k$ principal components
$P_{\leq k}$.
\[ T_{ \leq k } = X P_{ \leq k } \]
The principal components can also be computed from the
singular value decomposition (SVD).
\[ X = (U \Sigma) P' = T P' \]
The \textsc{Nipals} algorithm uses power iterations to find the principal components one by one,
\[ \begin{array}{l}
  \text{Repeat} \\
  \hspace{5mm} t = \text{random} \\
  \hspace{5mm} \text{Repeat} \\
  \hspace{1cm} p \propto X' t \\
  \hspace{1cm} t = Xp \\
  \hspace{5mm} X \leftarrow X - t p'
\end{array} \]

Given two datasets, $X$ and $Y$, consider the \textsc{Nipals} algorithms for them, but swap the scores of $X$ and $Y$, and interleave the updates.
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{PCA} \hspace*{3cm} & \text{PLS} \\[2mm]
  \text{Repeat} &   \text{Repeat} \\
  \hspace{5mm} t = \text{random} & \hspace{5mm} t = \text{random} \\
  \hspace{5mm} u = \text{random} & \hspace{5mm} u = \text{random} \\
  \hspace{5mm} \text{Repeat} & \hspace{5mm} \text{Repeat} \\
  \hspace{1cm} p \propto X' \color{red}t & \hspace{1cm} p \propto X' \color{red}u \\
  \hspace{1cm} t = Xp         & \hspace{1cm} t = Xp         \\
  \hspace{1cm} q \propto U' \color{red}u & \hspace{1cm} q \propto U' \color{red}t \\
  \hspace{1cm} y = Yq         & \hspace{1cm} y = Yq         \\
  \hspace{5mm} X \leftarrow X - t p' & \hspace{5mm} X \leftarrow X - t p' \\
  \hspace{5mm} X \leftarrow Y - u q' & \hspace{5mm} X \leftarrow Y - u q'
\end{array} \]
This gives decompositions
\begin{align*}
  X &= T P' \\
  Y &= U Q'.
\end{align*}

PLS regression estimates a regression between the scores (latent)
$ U \sim T$, giving $U \approx T \beta$,
and uses it to express $Y$ from $X$:
\begin{align*}
  Y &= U Q' \\
  &\approx T \beta Q' \\
  &= X P \beta Q' \\
  &= X B_{\text{PLS}}.
\end{align*}

For $p$, this is the power iteration
\[ p \propto X' u \propto X' Y q \propto X'YY't \propto X'YY'Xp \]
to compute the largest eigenvalue of $X'YY'X$, \ie,
\begin{align*}
  p
  &= \argmax_{ \n p = 1 } p' X'YY'X p \\
  &= \argmax_{ \n p = 1 } (Y'Xp) Y'X p \\
  &= \argmax_{ \n p = 1 } \cov(Y,Xp)' \cov(Y,Xp) \\
  &= \argmax_{ \n p = 1 } \n{ \cov( Y, Xp ) }.  
\end{align*}
Note that $p$ and $q$ are the right and left singular vectors of $X'Y$:
\[ p, q = \argmax_{ \n p = \n q = 1 } \cov( Xp, Xq ). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A survey of partial least squares (PLS) methods,
  with emphasis on the 2-block case}\\
  J.A. Wegelin (2000)
\end{book}
There are (at least) 3 variants of the PLS algorithm.
They all use a rank-1 approximation of $A=X'Y$ (from the SVD)
\begin{align*}
  A &\approx d p q' \\
  t &= X p \\
  u &= Y q
\end{align*}
but differ on how $X$ and $Y$ (or $A$) are updated:
\begin{align*}
  & \text{PLS-W2A} &
  X &\leftarrow X - t ( t' t )^{-1} t' X \\
  && Y &\leftarrow Y - u (u'u)^{-1} u' Y \\
  & \text{PLS-SVD} &
  A &\leftarrow A - d p q' \\
  & \text{PLS2} &
  X &\leftarrow X - t ( t' t )^{-1} t' X \\
  && Y &\leftarrow Y - (t't)^{-1} (t'u) tu'
\end{align*}

PLS1 is the special case of PLS2 where $Y$ only has one variable.

CCA (canonical correlation analysis) replaces the covariance with the correlation:
\[ p, q = \argmax_{ \n p \n q = 1 } \cor( Xp, Xq ). \]
This requires inverting $X'X$ and $Y'Y$, which can lead to numeric instabilities.

PLS can be generalized to more than two blocks (PLS-SEM -- not recommended)
if we posit the relations between the latent variables (path diagram).

