\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Partial least squares methods:\\ partial least squares correlation\\ and partial least squares regression}\\
  H. Abdi and L.J. Williams (2013)
\end{book}
Confusingly, PLS can refer to two things:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item PLS correlation, finding latent factors common to two datasets;
\item PLS regression, predicting one dataset from another.
\end{compactitem}
Given two data matrices
$X \in \RR^{I \times J}$,
$Y \in \RR^{I \times K}$
(with centered and normalized columns),
PLSC finds the unit vectors $u_1 \in \RR^K$, $v_1 \in \RR^J$
maximizing the covariance $(X v_1)' (Y u_1)$,
then the unit vectors, orthogonal to $u_1$ and $v_1$,
maximizing $(X v_2)' (Y u_2)$, and so on.
Those vectors can be computed in one go from the SVD
\begin{align*}
  Y' X &= U \Delta V' \\
  U &\!: \text{ loadings of $Y$} \\
  V &\!: \text{ loadings of $X$} \\
  XV &\!: \text{ factor scores} \\
  YU &\!: \text{ factor scores} 
\end{align*}
To assess significance, compute the sum of the
(retained) singular values (inertia)
and compare with that obtained after shuffling the rows of $X$
(bootstrap distribution of the inertia).

PLS regression computes latent factors an $X$,
and regresses $Y$ on those factors:
\begin{align*}
  X &= T P' \\
  \hat Y &= Y B C' = (P')^\dagger B C'.
\end{align*}
It can be computed iteratively:
\begin{align*}
  X' Y &= W \Delta C' && \text{(SVD)} \\
  &\approx w \delta c' && \text{(rank 1)} \\
  t &= X w && \text{$X$ latent} \\
  p &= X' t && \text{$X$ loadings} \\
  u &= Y c \\
  \hat X &= t p' \\
  \hat Y &= u c' \\
  &= tt'uc' \\
  &= t b c' \\
  b &= t' u && \text{slope} \\
  X &\leftarrow X - \hat X \\
  Y &\leftarrow Y - \hat Y.
\end{align*}
After $L=\rank X$ iterations:
\begin{align*}
  X &= T P' \\
  \hat Y
  &= T B C' \\
  &= X (P')^\dagger B C'
\end{align*}
This solves the optimization problem
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w \\
  \text{To maximize} & \n{ \cov(Xw,Y) }^2 \\
  \text{Such that} & \n w = 1.
\end{array} \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Supervised linear dimension reduction methods:
  review, extensions and comparisons}\\
  S. Xu et al.
\end{book}
Supervised dimension reduction methods include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item PCA on the $\ell$ variables most correlated with $y$
  (choose $\ell$ to get the best performance when predicting $y$
  from the top $k$ principal components, $k$ fixed);
\item Idem, but iteratively, extracting one principal component at a time;
\item $k$ principal components most correlated with $y$
  (instead of the top $k$);
\item PLS-W2A
  \begin{align*}
    u &= \argmax_{ \n u = 1 } u' X y y' X u \\
    z &= X u \\
    X &\leftarrow X - z u' \\
    y &\leftarrow y - \dfrac{ \langle y, z \rangle }{ \n z ^2 } z;
  \end{align*}
\item Extended PLS-W2A, \ie, with a PCA penalty,
  \[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{ \n u = 1 }
  u' X y y' X u - \lambda \n{ X - X u u' } ^2
  \]
\item PLS-SVD
  \[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{ U' U = I }
  \n{ \cov( XU , y ) }
  \]
\item Extended PLS-SVD
  \[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{ U' U = I }
  \n{ \cov( XU , y ) } - \lambda \n{ X - X U U' }^2
  \]
\item LSPCA (least-squares PCA, no closed-form solution)
  \[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ \beta,\ U'U = I }
  \n{ y - X U \beta } ^ 2 - \lambda \n{ X - X U U' }^2
  \]
\item Supervised probabilistic PCA (EM algorithm)
  \begin{align*}
    x &= U z + \epsilon \\
    y &= v' z + \eta
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}

Prefer PLS-W2A and LSPCA.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Supervised dimension reduction for big data}\\
  J.T.Vogelstein et al.
\end{book}
PCA can be adapted to include a target qualitative variable.
In the case of two classes:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the mean of each class;
\item The difference between the means is the first component;
\item Subtract the mean;
\item Perform PCA from the covariance matrix of the centered data.
\end{compactitem}
This can be generalized to more than 2 classes,
and to robust alternatives of the mean and variance.
    
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SLISEMAP: supervised dimensionality reduction through local explanations}\\
  A. Bj\"orklund et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Dimension reduction (supervised UMAP),
not on the features,
but on LIME explanations.
\begin{align*}
  z_i &: \text{ embedding of observation $i$ (unknown)} \\
  d_{ij} &= d( z_1, z_j ) \\
  w_{ij} &\propto e^{ d_{ij} } \qquad \sum_j w_{ij} = 1 \\
  L_{ij} &= \text{loss}\bigl( g_i(x_j), y_j \bigr)
\end{align*}
where $L_{ij}$ is the loss of the local model $g_i$ for observation $i$,
applied to observation $j$.
The latent embeddings are
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_z
\sum_{ij} w_{ij} L_{ij}
\ \ \text{ s.t. }\ \ 
\bigl\langle \n{ z_i }^2 \bigr\rangle = r^2.
\]
    
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Supervised discriminative PCA with adaptive neighbours for dimensionality reduction}\\
  Z. Shi et al.
\end{book}
PCA can be written
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_W \tr( X' X' X W ) \text{ s.t. } W' W = I_k \]
or
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_W \n{ X - X W W' }^2 \text{ s.t. } W' W = I_k. \]

The variant
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_Q \tr( Q' X X' Q ) \text{ s.t. } Q' Q = I_k \]
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_Q \n{ X - X X' X }^2 \text{ s.t. } W' W = I_k \]
is equivalent if the data has been standardized
(the projections are respectively
$W_{\text{PCA}} = V_{1:k}$ and
$W_{\text{vPCA}} = V_{1:k} \Sigma_{1:k}$,
where $X=U\Sigma V'$ is the SVD).

SDSPCA adds a target variable,
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{\substack{W, G, Q \\ Q' Q = I_k}}
\n{ X - Q W' }^2 + \alpha \n{ Y - Q G }^2 + \beta \n{ Q }_2
\]
(it can be computed from the eigenvectors of
$-XX' - \alpha YY' + \beta D$).

PCAN is a PCA variant preserving neighbourhood information
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & W, F, S \\
  \text{To minimize} &
  \displaystyle
  \sum\limits_{ij} \n{ W' x_i - W' x_j }^2 S_{ij} + \gamma_i S_{ij}^2 + {} \\
  & {} + \lambda \n{ f_i - f_j }^2 S_{ij} \\
  \text{Such that}
  & S \mathbf 1 = \mathbf 1 \\
  & W' X' X W = I_k \\
  & F' F = I_c
\end{array} \]

One can combine SDSPCA and PCAN.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial and macroeconomic data through\\ the lens of a nonlinear dynamic factor model}\\
  P.A. Guerr\'on et al.
\end{book}
The dynamic factor model
\begin{align*}
  Y_t &= \Lambda F_t + \epsilon_t \\
  F_t &= \Psi(L) F_{t-1} + \eta_t
\end{align*}
can be made non-linear
\begin{align*}
  y_t &= \mathscr G( f_t ) + \epsilon_t \\
  f_t &= \mathscr H( f_{t-1} ) + \eta_t.
\end{align*}
The pruned second order state space model
\begin{align*}
  y_t &= G f_t + \epsilon_t \\
  f_t &= x + a_t + b_t \\
  a_t &= \alpha a-{t-1} + \eta_t \\
  b_t &= \beta b_{t-1} + \gamma a_{t-1}^2
\end{align*}
can be estimated with a particle filter
(you may need hundreds of thousands of particles).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Predicting trends in the quality\\ of state-of-the-art neural networks\\ without access to training or testing data}\\
  C.H. Martin et al.
\end{book}
For each weight matrix $W$\!, compute
(with \texttt{weight\-watcher}):
the eigenvalues of $W'W$
(they tend to look like the eigenvalues of a random matrix,
or the eigenvalues of a random matrix with a few outliers,
or a heavy-tail distribution),
their power law exponent $\alpha$, and
\begin{align*}
  & \log \sum \lambda_i \\
  & \log \lambda_{\text{max}} \\
  & \alpha \log \lambda_{\text{max}} \\
  & \log \sum \lambda_i ^ \alpha
\end{align*}
and aggregate those quantities across layers
\begin{align*}
  & \sum_\ell \log \sum \lambda_{i\ell} \\
  & \sum_\ell \log \lambda_{\text{max},\ell} \\
  & \dfrac1L \sum_\ell \alpha_\ell \log \lambda_{\text{max},\ell} \\
  & \sum_\ell \log \sum \lambda_{i\ell} ^ {\alpha_\ell} \\
  & \dfrac1L \sum_\ell \alpha_\ell.
\end{align*}
Trained networks with good accruracy tend to have lower values of those metrics.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tree-ring watermarks: fingerprints for diffusion images that are invisible and robust}\\
  Y. Wen et al.
\end{book}
Embed the watermark into the initial noise vector,
in Fourier space,
as rings of constant value.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural set function extensions: learning\\ with discrete functions in high dimensions}\\
  N. Karalias et al. (2022)
\end{book}
A set function $f : \{0,1\}^{[n]} \to \RR$ can be extended to
\[ \tilde f : \left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  [0,1]^n & \longrightarrow & \RR \\
  x & \longmapsto & \displaystyle \tilde f(x) = \sum_{ S \subset [n] } p_x(S) f(S)
\end{array}
\right. \]
where $p_x$ satisfies
\begin{align*}
  & \sum_{ S \subset [n] } p_x(S) \mathbf 1_S = x \\
  & \sum_{ S \subset [n] } p_x(S) = 1 \\
  & \forall S \subset [n] \  \ \ p_x(S) \geq 0.
\end{align*}
It can further be extended to positive semidefinite matrices, $\SS_+^n$, e.g.,
\[ \tilde f(X) = \sum_{ S, T \subset [n] }
\sum_i \lambda_i \, p_{x_i}(S) \, p_{x_i}(T) \, f(S \inter T)
\]
where $X = \sum \lambda_i x_i s_i '$ is the eigen decomposition of $X$
and $p_x$ is as above.
More generally,
\[ \tilde f(X) = \sum_{ S, T \subset [n] } p_X(S,T) f(S \inter T) \]
where $p_X$ satisfies
\begin{align*}
  & X \preccurlyeq  \tfrac12 p_X(S,T) ( \mathbf1_S \mathbf1_T' + \mathbf1_T \mathbf1_S' ) \\
  & \sum_{S,T} p_X(S,T) = 1 \\
  & \forall S, T \ \ p_X(S,T) \geq 0.
\end{align*}

Lifting opimization problems to higher dimensions can make them better-behaved
(can help overcome the low-dimensional bottleneck).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The big data paradox in clinical practice}\\
  P. Msaouel (2022)
\end{book}
The more data you have, the less likely your confidence intervals are to
contain the true value: more data reduces variance (and confidence intervals),
but the bias remains. Try to:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Improve data quality;
\item Model subject heterogeneity (missing counfounders, e.g.,
  in the linear regressions to analyze RCT results);
\item Include the bias in the confidence intervals.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fine-tuning language models\\ with just forward passes}\\
  S. Malladi et al.
\end{book}
Backpropagation of large models requires an inordinate amount of memory
(with an 80GB GPU, you can use a 30B model, but only train a 3B one).
Use a stochastic gradient estimator
(SPSA, ZO-SGD).
\begin{align*}
  z &\sim N(0,I) \\
  \hat\nabla \ell(\theta) &= \dfrac{
    \ell( \theta + \epsilon z ) - \ell( \theta - \epsilon z )
  }{ 2 \epsilon }
  z \\
  & \approx zz' \nabla_{\!\theta} \ell(\theta)
\end{align*}
To reduce memory further, do not store $z$, but only its random seed,
and re-generate it each time it is needed (MeZO).

Keeping gradient history does not require much additional memory
($\Delta \ell$ and the seed for $z$ for each timestamp).
For more precision, use several vectors $z$.

Also try variance reduction,
or layerwise gradients
(if the layer gradients have different scales).

