\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The little book of deep learning}\\
  F. Fleuret (2023)
\end{book}
Non-technical (but broad) introduction to deep learning
with clear diagrams for the most common architectures
(ResNet, UNet, Transformer, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Descent steps of a relation-aware energy produce heterogeneous  graph neural networks}\\
  H. Ahn et al. (2022)
\end{book}
One can learn a latent representation $Y$ of the nodes of a graph as
\[ Y_w(X) = \argmin_Y \n{ Y - f_w(X) }^2 + \lambda \tr( Y' L Y ), \]
\ie, nodes with similar features $X$ have a similar representation (first term),
and nodes linked by an edge have a similar representation (second term).
The weights $w$ of the neural network $f_w$ are chosen wrt some classification or prediction task
\[ \theta, w = \argmin_{\theta,w} \sum_i \text{loss}
\bigl( g_\theta \bigl( Y_w(x_i) \bigl) , y_i \bigl). \]
For heterogeneous graphs, consider
\begin{align*}
  Y_w(X) = \argmin_Y \sum_s \bigl[
    &
    \n{ Y_s - f_{w_s}(X_s) }^2 \\
    &
    + 
    \lambda \sum_{s'} \sum_t \sum_{ij\in E_t}
    \n{ Y_i H_t - Y_j }^2
    \bigr]
\end{align*}
where
$s$ is the node type,
$t$ the edge type,
$H_t$ compatibility matrices (to allow heterophily).
  
\nextbook
