\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TrivialAugment: tuning-free yet state-of-the-art data augmnentation}\\
  S.G. M\"uller and F. Hutter
\end{book}
For each image, pick one (instead of several) augmentation,
uniformly at random (instead of from a learned distribution),
and apply it with a random (instead of learned) strength.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A study on the evaluation of generative models}\\
  E. Betzalel et al.
\end{book}
Do not use the inception score and ImageNet to assess generative models:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use NotImageNet32 instead of ImageNet;
\item Use CLIP instead of the inception model
\item Use several metrics: FID$_\infty$ (unbiased FID), KID, ClearFID.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Elucidating the design space\\ of diffusion-based generative models}\\
  T. Karras et al. (2022)
\end{book}
% Technical. Not read.
A denoising diffusion model can be written as an ODE
\[ \dot x = - \dot\sigma_t \sigma_t \nabla_{\!x} \log p(x, \sigma_t) \]
(we have many trajectories $(x_t)_t$ from which we want to learn
the score function $\nabla \log p$).
To sample, use higher-order methods (Euler-Heun, Runge-Kutta).
Stochastic samplimg (Langevin diffusion) gives better results.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MaskGIT:\\ masked generative image transformer}\\
  H. Chang et al.
\end{book}
Vision transformers (ViT) generate image patches sequentially,
in raster scan order.
Instead, learn to predict randomly masked patches (\`a la BERT),
generate the image all at once, and progressively refine it.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the importance of noise scheduling\\ for diffusion models}\\
  T. Chen
\end{book}
% Not read
The optimal noise schedule depends on the image size:
there is more redundancy in large images.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Texture generation\\ with neural cellular automata}\\
  A. Mordvintsev et al.
\end{book}
Train a neural cellular automaton (NCA)
(all cells have the same differentiable update rule)
to produce a (tilable) pattern given an example,
using a style transfer loss
(Gram matrix of the latent representation of a classifier)
or the inception distance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ensemble deep learning: a review}\\
  M.A. Ganaie
\end{book}
Ensemble learning is not limited to equal weighted averages of a few models:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bagging;
\item Boosting;
\item Stacking;
\item Negative correlation learning, \ie,
  adding a penalty to make the base learners more diverse
  (they should not all make the same errors);
\item Implicit ensembles (dropout, drop-connect, stochastic depth)
\item Homogeneous ensembles (same models, but different training datasets,
  augmentations, initializations, regularizations, hyperparameters),
  heterogeneous ensembles;
\item Weighted average, with learned weights (mixture of experts),
  or weights chosen by cross-validation
  (SuperLearner)
\item Voting rules (Condorcet, etc.)
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Loss surfaces, mode connectivity,\\ and fast ensembling of DNNs}\\
  T. Garipov et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The optima of loss functions are connected by simple curves over which training and test accuracy are nearly constant:
learn those curves (e.g., B\'ezier) $\phi_\theta$
by minimizing
\[ \E_{ t \sim U(0,1) } \bigl[ \text{loss} \bigl( \phi_\theta(t) \bigr) \bigr] . \]
\emph{Fast geometric ensembling} (FGE) starts with a single solution
and looks for a nearby solution (sufficiently away from it)
with a similar loss --
this can be done using a sawtooth learning rate schedule.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A convergent ADMM framework\\ for efficient neural network training}\\
  J. Wang et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The neural network training problem can be written in ADMM form
\[ \begin{array}{lll}
  \text{Find} & w_1,\dots,w_L & \text{weights} \\
  & a_1, \dots,a_L & \text{activations} \\
  \text{To minimize} & \text{loss}(a_L,y) + \sum_\ell r_\ell(w_\ell) \\
  \text{Such that} &
  \forall \ell \in \llbracket 1 , L \rrbracket \ \ a_\ell = g_\ell( w_\ell, a_{\ell-1} )
  \hspace*{-2cm}
\end{array} \]
or, by replacing the non-linear constraints with a penalty, minimizing 
\[
\text{loss}(a_L,y) + \sum_\ell r_\ell(w_\ell)
+ \beta \sum_\ell \n{ 
  a_\ell - g_\ell( w_\ell, a_{\ell-1} )
}_2^2.
\]
Update the parameters first backwards
\[ a_L \to w_L \to a_{L-1} \to w_{L-1} \to \cdots \to a_1 \to w_1 \]
then forwards
\[ w_1 \to a_1 \to w_2 \to a_2 \to \cdots \to w_L \to a_L. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast and flexible ADMM algorithms\\ for trend filtering}\\
  A. Ramdas and R.J. Tibshirani
\end{book}
Trend filtering solves the problem
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\beta \tfrac12 \n{ y - \beta }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ D^{(k+1)} \beta }_1. \]
The standard ADMM approach rewrites the problem as
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \beta, \alpha \\
  \text{To minimize} & \tfrac12 \n{ y - \beta }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ \alpha }_1 \\\
  \text{Such that} & \alpha = D^{(k+1)} \beta .
\end{array} \]
Instead, rewrite it as
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \beta, \alpha \\
  \text{To minimize} & \tfrac12  \n{ y - \beta }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ D^{(1)} \alpha }_1 \\
  \text{Such that} & \alpha = D^{(k)}\beta.
\end{array} \]
The 0th order trend filtering problem, $k+1=1$,
can be solved in linear time,
with dynamic programming, or the taut string principle.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Gromov-Wasserstein distance between networks and stable network invariants}\\
  S. Chowdhury and F. M\'emoli
\end{book}
Given two metric measure spaces
$(X,d_X,p_X)$, $(Y,d_Y,p_Y)$,
their Gromov-Wasserstein distance is the minimum distorsion of a coupling
between $p_X$ and $p_Y$.
\[
%\dfrac12
\inf_{\substack{
    \pi_{1\#} p = p_X \\
    \pi_{2\#} p = p_Y \\    
}}
\!\!\!\!
\iint\limits_{
  \vrule width 0pt height 1em
  X\times Y\times X \times Y
}
\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!
\va{
  d_X(x,x') - d_Y(x,y')
} ^ q
\!
p( dx, dy ) p( dx'\!, dy' )
\]
It can be generalized to \emph{networks}
$(X,\omega_X, p_X)$, where $X$ is a Polish space, $\omega_X$ a measurable function on $X\times X$
(it need not satisfy the triangle inequality, be symmetrix, or have zeros on the diagonal).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Weisfeiler-Lehman meets Gromov-Wasserstein}\\
  S. Chen et al.
\end{book}
A \emph{labeled measure Markov chain} (LMMC) is the datum of:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $X$, a finite set (states);
\item $m: X \to \mathscr P(X)$ (Markov kernel, \ie, transition matrix,
  where $\mathscr P(X)$ is the set of probability distributions on $X$);
\item $\mu \in \mathscr P(X)$ a stationary distribution for $m$;
\item $\ell: X \to Z$ (label, for some metrix space $Z$.
\end{compactitem}
A labeled graph, and $q \in [0,1)$,
defines an LMMC on the set of nodes,
with transition probabilities
\[ m(u,v) = \begin{cases}
  1 & \text{if } u = v \text{ and } \mathscr N_u = \nothing \\
  q & \text{if } u = v \text{ and } \mathscr N_u \neq \nothing \\
  \dfrac{1-q}{\operatorname{deg} u} & \text{if } v \in \mathscr N_u \\
  0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases} \]
and stationary distribution $\mu(v) \propto \max\{ 1 , \operatorname{deg} v \}.$

The multiset labels in the WL test can be interpreted as probability distributions
$\mathscr L^1, \mathscr L^2, \dots, \mathscr L^k$ on $\mathscr P(Z)$,
$\mathscr P\mathscr P(Z), \dots, \mathscr P \cdots \mathscr P(Z) = \mathscr P ^{\circ k} (Z)$.
The WL distance between $X$ and $Y$ is
\[ d^k(X,Y) = d_W( \mathscr L_X^k ,  \mathscr L_Y^k ) \]
(Wasserstein distance on $\mathscr P^{\circ k}(Z)$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The open algebraic path problem}\\
  J. Master
\end{book}
The shortest path problem on a graph computes the transitve closure
of the weight matrix with coefficients in the \emph{rig}
$([0,\infty], \min, +)$.
The algebraic shortest path generalizes this to an arbitrary rig or even a commutative quantale
(monoidal closed poset with all joins).
\[ \begin{array}{lccc}
  \relax [0,\infty] & \geq & \inf & + \\
  \relax [0,\infty] & \leq & \sup & \inf \\
  \relax [0,1] & \leq & \sup & \times \\
  \{ \text{T},\text{F} \} && \textsc{or} & \textsc{and} \\
  \mathscr P(\Sigma^*) & \subset & \union & \textsc{concat}
\end{array} \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Uncertainty in deep learning}\\
  Y. Gal (2016)
\end{book}
To get a probabilistic forecast (as opposed to a point forecast)
from a neural net trained with droupout (or some other stochastic regularization techniaue),
use the network at test time as you did during training,
to sample from the distribution of $y^*$ and estimate $\E[y^*]$ and $\var[y^*]$.
  
\nextbook
