\]
\[ \Delta X_t = \mu - \mathbf 1 ' \begin{bmatrix} c_{t-1} \\ \vdots \\ c_{t-p} \end{bmatrix} + \epsilon_t \]
and estimated with a Kalman filter.

The \emph{variance ratio} statistic
\begin{align*}
  R_k &= V_k / V_1 \\
  V_k &= \dfrac1k \var[ X_{t+k} - X_t - k \mu ] \\
  \mu &= \E[ \Delta X_t ]
\end{align*}
measures the fraction of variation due to permanent shocks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian vector autoregressions}\\
  T. Wo\'zniak
\end{book}
A VAR($p$) model
\[ t_t = \mu + A_1 y_{t-1} + \cdots + A_k y_{t-k} + u_t
\quad u_t \sim N(0,\Sigma) \]
can be written, by transposing the equation and vertically stacking the resulting row vectors,
\[ Y = X A + U \qquad U \sim \text{MN}(0,\Sigma, I). \]
With a normal-Widhart prior
\begin{align*}
  \Sigma &\sim \text{IW}( \underline S , \underline \nu ) \\
  A | \Sigma &\sim \text{MN} ( \underline A , \Sigma, \underline V ),
\end{align*}
the posterior is also normal-Wishart
\begin{align*}
  \Sigma &\sim \text{IW}( \overline S , \overline \nu ) \\
  A | \Sigma &\sim \text{MN} ( \overline A , \Sigma, \overline V ),
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
  \overline V &= ( \underline V ^{-1} + X ' X ) ^{-1} \\
  \overline A &= \overline V ( \underline V ^{-1} \underline A + X' Y ) \\
  \overline \nu &= \underline \nu + T \\
  \overline S &= \underline S + Y' Y + \underline A ' \underline V ^{-1} \underline A -
  \overline A ' \overline V ^{-1} \overline A.
\end{align*}

The Minnesota prior is
\[
A = \left[\!\!\!  
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline={([yshift=-.55ex]current bounding box.center)},outer sep=0pt]
  %\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=(current bounding box.center)]
%\clip[draw] (-.7,-2.5) rectangle (.7,2.5);  
\clip (-.7,-2.5) rectangle (.7,2.5);  
\matrix (m) [
  matrix of math nodes,
  nodes in empty cells,
  row sep={3ex,between origins},column sep={3ex,between origins}
]{
  0 & & 0 \\
  1 \\ \\ & & 1 \\
  0 \\ \\ & & 0 \\
  & & \\
  0 \\ \\ & & 0 \\
};
\draw (m-1-1) -- (m-1-3);
\draw (m-2-1) -- (m-4-3);
\draw (m-5-1) -- (m-7-3);
\draw (m-9-1) -- (m-11-3);
\draw[thick, loosely dotted] (m-8-1.center) -- (m-8-3.center);
\end{tikzpicture}
\!\!\!\right]
\]

\[ \underline V = \text{diag} \biggl(
\lambda_0 ,
\dfrac{ \lambda_1 }{ 1^2 \hat \sigma_1 ^2 } ,
\dots ,
\dfrac{ \lambda_1 }{ 1^2 \hat \sigma_n ^2 } ,
\dfrac{ \lambda_1 }{ 2^2 \hat \sigma_2 ^2 } ,
\dots ,
\biggr) \]
where $\hat \sigma _i ^2$
is the error term variance of a univariate AR($k$) model on variable $i$.
For the other hyperparameters, try
\begin{align*}
  \underline S &= ( Y - XA )' (Y - XA) \\
  \underline \nu &= T - K - N - 1.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nowcasting\\ with large Bayesian vector autoregressions}\\
  J. Cimadomo (2020)
\end{book}
A \emph{state space BVAR} is a VAR written in state space form. To sample from it, iterate:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sample the missing values given $A$ and $\Sigma$,
  using a Kalman simulation smoother;
\item Sample the Minnesota hyperparameters given the data;
\item Sample the parameters $A$, $\Sigma$ given the data and the hyperparameters.
\end{compactitem}

A \emph{stacking BVAR} deals with mixed frequency data by adding the lags of the high-frequency variables.

The \emph{cube root BVAR} deals with monthly and quarterly data
by fitting a VAR($p$) on quarterly data, writing it in companion form,
$Y_t = A Y_{t-1} + \epsilon_t$
and converting it to a monthly VAR,
$ Y_t = B Y_{t-1} + \eta_t$,
wnere $B^3=A$.
There are many cubic roots of $A$:
for the real eigenvalues, take the real cubic root;
for the complex one, take that with the lowest argument
(\ie, that leading to the least oscillatory behaviour).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Large Bayesian vector autoregressions}\\
  M. Ba\'nbura et al. (2007)
\end{book}
The FAVAR model is a VAR model with a small number of variables,
augmented with a few principal components from a larger number of variables.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating and accounting for the output gap with large Bayesian vector autoregressions}\\
  J. Morley and B. Wong (2019)
\end{book}
To choose the variables to include in a BVAR (reduce their number from 138 to 7),
look at the contribution of each to the Beveridge-Nelson cycle
(adding (removing) irrelevant (relevant) variables does not (does) change the cycle)
and remove the variables one by one, starting with those whose contribution has the lowest variance.

Use a Minnesota prior
\begin{align*}
  \E[ \phi_k^{ik} ] &= 0 \\
  \var[ \phi_k^{ij} ] &= \dfrac{ \lambda ^2 }{ k^2 } \text{ if } i=j \\
  \var[ \phi_k^{ij} ] &= \dfrac{ \lambda ^2 }{ k^2 } \dfrac{ \sigma_i ^2 }{ \sigma_j ^2 } \text{ if } i\neq j
\end{align*}
where $\lambda$ is selected to minimize the 1-step ahead RMSE on an expanding window.

The contributions can be computed from
\begin{align*}
  ( \Delta X_t - \mu ) &= F ( \Delta X_{t-1} - \mu ) + H e_t \\
  \text{Cycle}_t &= - F ( I-F)^{-1} ( \Delta X_t - \mu ) \\
  &= \cdots \\
  &= - \sum_{ I \geq 0} F^{i+1} ( I-F) ^{-1} H e_{t-i}.
\end{align*}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Vector autoregressions: forecasting and reality}\\
  J.C. Robertson and E.W. Tallman (1999)
\end{book}
The VAR(1) model (e.g., a BVAR with a Minnesota prior)
\( X_t = A X_{t-1} + \epsilon_t \), \(\epsilon_t \sim N(0,\Sigma) \)
can be written
\( X_t \sim N( AX_{t-1}, \Sigma ). \)
If some of the coordinates are not observed,
their distribution given the observed coordinates is known (\emph{conditional forecasting})
(from the usual formula for the conditional Gaussian distribution).
\begin{align*}
  & \E[ X_t^{\text{mis}} | X_t^{\text{obs}} ] = \\
  & \hspace*{1cm}
  ( A X_{t-1} )^{\text{mis}} +
  \Sigma_{ \text{mis}, \text{obs} } ^{\vphantom{-1}}
  \Sigma_{ \text{obs} , \text{obs} } ^{-1} \bigl[
    X_t ^{\text{obs}} - ( A X_t )^{\text{obs}}  \bigr]
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Large Bayesian vector autoregression}\\
  J.C.C. Chan
\end{book}
The BVAR with errors $U \sim \text{MN}(0,\Sigma,\Omega)$
can be non-Gaussian and/or have autocorrelated errors:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Gaussian: $\Omega=I$;
\item Common stochastic volatility:
  \begin{align*}
    \Omega &= \text{diag}( e^{h_1} , \dots , e^{h_T} ) \\
    h_t &= \rho h_{t-1} + \epsilon_t 
  \end{align*}
\item Fat tails (Student T):
  \begin{align*}
    \Omega &= \text{diag}( \lambda_1, \dots , \lambda_T ) \\
    \lambda_t &\sim \text{InverseGamma}
  \end{align*}
\item Serially dependent errors, e.g., $u_t \sim \text{MA}(2)$, \ie,
  \[ \Omega =
\left(\!\!\!  
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline={([yshift=-.8ex]current bounding box.center)},outer sep=0pt]
