\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to $g$ methods}\\
  A.I. Naimi et al. (2016)
\end{book}
G methods (G-formula, marginal structural models,
and structural nested models)
compute
\[
E[ Y \,|\, \text{do}(A_0 = A_1 = 1) ] -
E[ Y \,|\, \text{do}(A_0 = A_1 = 0) ]
\]
in the following situation
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=1,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \node (A0) at (0,0) {$A_0$};
  \node (Z1) at (1,0) {$Z_1$};
  \node (A1) at (2,0) {$A_1$};
  \node (Y)  at (3,0) {$Y$};
  \node (U)  at (2,1) {$U$};
  \draw[->] (A0) -- (Z1);
  \draw[->] (Z1) -- (A1);
  \draw[->] (A1) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (U) -- (Z1);
  \draw[->] (U) -- (Y);
  \node[above = 0pt of U] {\footnotesize unobserved confounder};
  \node[below = 0pt of A0] {\footnotesize treatment};
  \node[below = 0pt of A1] {\footnotesize treatment};
  \node[below = 0pt of Y]  {\footnotesize outcome};
  \node at (0,-.7) {\scriptsize\color{black!70} HIV drug};
  \node at (1,-.7) {\scriptsize\color{black!70} viral load};
  \node at (2,-.7) {\scriptsize\color{black!70} HIV drug};
  \node at (3,-.7) {\scriptsize\color{black!70} CD4 count};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

If we only wanted do($A_1$),
we would condition on the confounder $Z_1$,
but if we also want do($A_0$),
we cannot condition on $Z_1$ because it is a collider.
The \emph{G-formula} is obtained by writing down the joint probability.
\begin{align*}
  & P( y ,\! a_1 ,\! z_1,\! a_0 ) \!=\!
  P( y | a_1,\!z_1,\!z_0) P(a_1|z_1,\!a_0) P(z_1|a_0) P(a_0) \\
  & \E[Y] = \!\!\sum_{a_1,z_1,a_0}\!\! E[ Y|a_1,z_1,a_0] P(a_1|z_1,a_0) P(z_1|a_0) P(a_0) \\
  & \E[Y | \text{do}(a_0,a_1)] = \sum_{z_1} E[ Y|a_1,z_1,a_0] P(z_1|a_0)
\end{align*}

\emph{Marginal structural models}
use inverse probability weighting to fit the regression $ y \sim a_1 + a_2 $.
\begin{align*}
  P[ a_0, a_1 | z_1 ] &=
  P[ a_0 | a_1,z_1 ] P[ a_1|z_1] \\
  &= P[a_0] P[a_1|z_1] \\
  &= \tfrac12 P[a_1|z_1] \\
  P[a_1,a_2] &= \tfrac12 P[a_1] \\
  w_{a_0,a_1,z_1} &= \dfrac{ P[a_0,a_1] }{ P[a_1,a_1|z_1] }
\end{align*}
