Neural network to estimate (MLE) the kernel of a Hawkes process.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Feature selection with annealing\\ for computer vision and big data learning}\\
  A. Barbu et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Recursive feature elimination} (RFE)
alternates between
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Training an SVM model;
\item Discarding the features with the smallest coefficients.
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Feature selection with annealing} (FSA)
alternates between
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Gradient descent step;
\item Discarding the features with the smallest coefficients.
\end{compactitem}

Also check Boruta.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantile-parametrized distributions}\\
  T.W. Keelin and B.W. Powley (2011)
\end{book}
A \emph{quantile-parametrized distribution} (QPD)
is defined by
\[ F^{-1}(y) = \sum_{i=1}^n a_i g_i(y), \quad y \in (0,1). \]
For instance, with $n=4$ and (\emph{Q-normal})
\begin{align*}
  g_1(y) &= 1 \\
  g_2(y) &= \Phi^{-1}(y) \\
  g_3(y) &= y \Phi^{-1}(y) \\
  g_4(y) &= y.
\end{align*}
Then,
$f(x) = \bigl( \sum a_i g_i'(y) \bigr)^{-1}$
where $x = F^{-1}(y)$.

Find $a$ from $n$ probability-value pairs $(x_i,y_i)_{1\leq i \leq n}$
as $a = Y^{-1} x$,
$Y_{ij} = g_j(y_i)$,
provided $Y$ is invertible
and $\forall y \in (0,1) \ \sum a_i g_i'(y) > 0$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ontology development 101:\\ a guide to creating your first ontology}\\
  N.F. Noy and D.L. McGuinness
\end{book}
An \emph{ontology} is a formal specification of the terms used in a domain,
their relations, and properties.
It consists of:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Classes (\emph{concepts}),
\item Hierarchical relations between those classes,
\item \emph{Instances} of those classes,
\item Properties (\emph{slots}) of those classes, or of the instances,
  each with a set of allowable values
  (\emph{facets}: class, instance, symbol, free text, list, etc.).
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Axiomatic characterization\\ of pointwise Shapley decompositions}\\
  M.C. Christansen (2023)
\end{book}
The Shapley values decompose a function $F: \{0,1\}^d \to \RR$
into a sum of contributions of each input variable.
It can be generalized to functions with real arguments
(\emph{average sequential decomposition}, aka \emph{poinwise Shapley values})
by writing (assuming $F(0)=0$)
\begin{align*}
  F(x) &= F(x) - F(0) \\
  & = F(x_1,\dots,x_d) - F(x_1,\dots,x_{d-1},0) + {}\\
  & \hphantom{{}={}} F(x_1,\dots,x_{d-1},0) - F(x_1,\dots,x_{d-2},0,0) + {}\\
  & \hphantom{{}={}} \cdots
\end{align*}
and averaging over all permutations of $(x_1,\dots,x_d)$.

