\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep adaptive input normalization\\ for time series forecasting}\\
  N. Passalis et al.
\end{book}
Before processing a time series with a neural net,
DAIN normalizes it, adaptively.
\begin{align*}
  a &\leftarrow \text{LSTM}(x) \\
  x &\leftarrow x - W a \\
  b &\leftarrow \text{LSTM}(x) \\
  x &\leftarrow x \oslash Wb \\
  c,d &\leftarrow \text{LSTM}(x) \\
  x &\leftarrow x \odot \text{sigm}( Wc + d )
\end{align*}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Expectile hidden Markov regression models\\ for analyzing cryptocurrency returns}\\
  B. Foroni et al. (2023)
\end{book}
\emph{Expectile regression} replaces the squares in the loss function
with
\[ \omega_\tau(u) = \begin{cases}
  \tau u^2 & \text{ if } u \geq 0 \\
  (1-\tau) u^2 & \text{ if } u < 0,
\end{cases} \]
where $\tau \in (0,1)$.
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\beta \E \bigl[ \omega_\tau ( y - X' \beta ) \bigr] \]
It is often estimated with IRLS (iteratively reweighted least squares).

The \emph{asymmetric normal} distribution has density
\[ f(y) \propto \exp - \omega_\tau \left( \dfrac{ y - \mu }{ \sigma } \right). \]
The asymmetric normal hidden Markov model (HMM) can be estimated
with the expectation-maximization (EM) algorithm.

  
