Given $x \in \{0,1\}^N$ such that $x_i \equiv i a \pmod 2$,
the \emph{Bernstein-Vazirani} algorithm finds $a$.

Given $x \in \{0,1\}^N$ such that $x_i = x_j$ iff $i=j$ or $i=j\oplus s$,
for some $s \in \{0,1\}^n$,
the \emph{Simon algorthm} finds $s$.

The \emph{Fourier transform}
\[ ( F_N )_{ jk} = \dfrac{ 1 }{ \sqrt N }
e^{2\pi i jk/N } \]
generalizes the Hadamard transform $H = F_2$.
The Fast Fourier transform (FFT) computes
$F_N v$ efficiently by recursively separating even and odd terms.
The \emph{Quantum Fourier transform} only requires
$O\bigl( (\log N) ^2 \bigr)$ gates,  instead of $O( N \log N)$.
\[ F_N \ket k = \bigotimes_{ \ell = 1 }^{ n } \dfrac{ 1 }{ \sqrt 2 }
\left( \ket 0 + e^{ 2\pi i k /2\ell } \right)
\]
