\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph-regularized tensor regression:\\
  a domain-aware framework for interpretable\\ multi-way financial modelling}\\
  Y.L. Xu et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Tensor regression} is a generalization of linear regression
$ y = \langle W, X \rangle + b$,
for instance
\begin{align*}
  y^t &= w_{ij\tau} x_{ij\tau}^t + b \\
  i &:\text{ stock} \\
  j &:\text{ feature} \\
  \tau &:\text{ lag} \\
  t &:\text{ time}
\end{align*}
where $x_{ij\tau}^t$ is the value of feature $j$ for stock $i$ at time $t-\tau$.
We can make the model more parsimonious with a CP decomposition
\[ w_{ij\tau} = \sum_c u^1_{ic} u^2_{jc} u^3_{\tau c} . \]
Given a graph on one of the dimensions, e.g., sector membership for the stocks
(or a chain graph
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.25,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=-2.5]
  \node[circle,fill] at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] at (2,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] at (3,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] at (4,0) {};
  \draw (0,0) -- (4,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
for the lags, or some a priori relation between the features),
with Laplacian $L$, one can add a penalty
$\tr( u^1_{i\bigcdot} {}' L u^1_{i\bigcdot} )$
to make the signal smooth.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quasi-Monte Carlo methods for computing derivatives sensitivities on the GPU}\\
  P. Bilokon et al. (2022)
\end{book}
The \emph{pathwise method} computes
\[ \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial S_0 }
\E_{ S \sim \text{rw}(\mu,\sigma^2) }
\bigl[ f(S_T) \bigr],
\]
with Monte Carlo simulations, as
\[ \E
\biggl[
  \dfrac{ \partial f(S_T) }{ \partial S_T }
  \dfrac{ \partial S_T }{ \partial S_0 }
\biggr]
\]
where $\dfrac{ \partial S_T }{ \partial S_0 } = \dfrac{ S_T }{ S_0 }$.
  
