\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Moment sum-of-squares approach for fast\\ risk estimation in uncertain environments}\\
  A.M. Jasour et al.
\end{book}
Given $x \in \RR^n$ (e.g., robot position),
we want to know if $x \in \chi$,
where $\chi \subset \RR^n$
is a semi-algebraic set.
Both $x$ and $\chi$ are only known approximately,
but we know the moments of their distributions
(for $\chi$, the moments of the distributions of the coefficients of the polynomials defining it).
The resulting semi-definite program
becomes intractable as the size of the problem increases,
but one can efficiently compute bounds of the moments.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Randomized geometric tools\\ for anomaly detection in stock markets}\\
  C. Bachelard et al.
\end{book}
Uniformly sampling long-only fully-invested portfolios with a prescribed volatility
is equivalent to sampling from
$K = \SS^{n-1} \inter \Delta$
for some simplex $\Delta$. It is not connected, but one can:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Identify the connected components by looking at how the 1-simplex
  $\Delta$ intersects the sphere $\SS^{n-1}$;
\item Compute the volume of each component.
\end{compactitem}
We can sample, uniformly, from a component $K_i$,
with Monte Carlo, starting from a point $p$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Great cycle walk:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Pick a geodesic (great circle) $\ell$ going through $p$;
  \item Compute the intersection $\ell \inter K_i$; 
  \item Pick a point at random on $\ell \inter K_i$;
  \end{compactitem}
\item Reflective great circle walk:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Pick a geodesic;
  \item Pick a length, $L = -\tau \log \eta$, $\eta \sim U(0,1)$;
  \item Follow the geodesic for a length $L$, reflecting on $\partial \Delta$
    whenever we reach it.
  \end{compactitem}  
\end{compactitem}
