  T. Jaisson (2022)
\end{book}
Differentiable reinforcement learning (RL), for a single asset ($n=1$), on simulated data
with a 2-scale alpha
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_t &= \alpha_t^{\text{slow}} + \alpha_t^{\text{fast}} \\
  \alpha_t^{\text{slow}} &= \rho^{\text{slow}} \alpha^{\text{slow}}_{t-1} + \text{noise} \\
  \alpha_t^{\text{fast}} &= \rho^{\text{fast}} \alpha^{\text{fast}}_{t-1} + \text{noise} \\
  \rho^{\text{fast}} &= 0, \quad \rho^{\text{slow}} > 0
\end{align*}

%TODO: PLOT

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distributionally robust\\ end-to-end portfolio construction}\\
  G. Costa and G.NB. Iyengar (2022)
\end{book}
In the portfolio optimization
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{\substack{
    w \geq 0 \\ w' \mathbf 1 1 = 1 }}
\text{Risk}(w) - \gamma w' y,
\]
use, as risk, the worst \emph{deviation risk measure}
\begin{align*}
  \text{Risk}(w) &= \max_{\substack{
      p \geq 0 \\ p' \mathbf 1 = 1 \\ D_\phi(p, \text{Unif}) \leq \delta
  }}
  f_\epsilon( w, p ) \\
  f_\epsilon(w,p) &= \min_c \sum p_j R( w' \epsilon_j - c ) \\
  D_\phi &\text{: $\phi$-divergence} \\
  \epsilon &\text{: past prediction errors} \\
  R &\text{: even function}
\end{align*}
The minimax problem can be reformulated with convex duality, and solved end-to-end
(to maximize the Sharpe ratio), for 20 assets, 15 years, weekly returns, 17 predcitors
(code available).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Supervised portfolios}\\
  G. Chevalier et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Instead of forecasting returns (or uniformized returns,
residual returns, volatility-adjusted returns, etc.)
forecast the weights of the optimal portfolio
(using realized returns as alpha).
This adjusts for:
factor exposures,
volatility,
investor preferences (utility, constraints).
Example with 25 assets, 14 features.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Alternatives to deep neural networks in finance}\\
  A.V. Antonov and V.V. Piterbarg (2022)
\end{book}
To approximate functions
$f : \RR^k \to \RR$, try
\[ \tilde f(x) = \sum \alpha_n s( x - z_n ) . \]
For the Fourier transform, the $z_n$'s are on a grid,
and $\alpha_n = \hat f(z_n)$ -- but we could use quasi-random points
for the $z_n$'s, estimate the $\alpha_n$'s with a linear regression,
and fine-tune the position of the $z_n$'s
(\emph{stochastic sampling}).

For \emph{image rendering}, $s$ is sinc or its Lanczos generalization
\[ s(x) = \dfrac{ \sin( \pi x ) }{ \pi x }
\ \ \text{or} \ \ 
s(x) = \dfrac{ \sin( \pi x ) }{ \pi x }
\dfrac{ \sin( \pi x / a ) }{ \pi x / a }
\mathbf 1_{ x \in [ -a, a ] }. \]

\emph{Generalized stochastic sampling} uses
\[ \tilde f(x) = \sum_n \alpha_n \prod_d \phi \bigl( (x_d - z_{nd} ) \beta_d \bigr) \]
for a fixed activation function $\phi$ (e.g., sinc),
quasi-random $z_n$'s, and
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\beta_d = \lambda_d \cdot \eta$,
  $\lambda^2_d = \E \bigl( \nabla_{\!d} f(x) \bigr)^2$,
  $\eta$ optimized;
\item or $\beta_d$ optimized;
\item or $\beta_{d,n}$ optimized.
\end{compactitem}

The tensor train decomposition
\begin{align*}
  %\text{TODO: PLOT} \\
  A_{ i_1, \dots, i_D } &=
  G^1_{ i_1 k_1 } 
  G^2_{ k_1 i_2 k_2 }
  \cdots
  G^{D-1}_{ k_{D-2} i_{D-1} k_{D-1} }
  G^D_{ k_{D-1} i_D } \\
  A(i_1,\dots,i_D) &=
  \underbracket[.6pt]{g_1(i_1)}_{\substack{\text{row}\\\text{vector}}}
  \underbracket[.6pt]{g_2(i_2) \cdots g_{D-1}(i_{D-1})}_{\text{matrices}}
  \underbracket[.6pt]{g_D(i_D)}_{\substack{\text{column}\\\text{vector}}}
\end{align*}
can be made functional (fTT)
\[ \tilde f(x) =
\underbracket[.6pt]{g_1(x_1)}_{\substack{\text{row}\\\text{vector}}}
\underbracket[.6pt]{g_2(x_2) \cdots g_{D-1}( x_{D-1} )}_{\text{matrices}}
\underbracket[.6pt]{g_D( x_D )}_{\substack{\text{column}\\\text{vector}}}
\]
where the $g_d$'s are learned
(with alternating least squares, from basis functions --
note that this depends on the order of the $x_d$'s).

Classical approaches include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fourier decomposition;
\item Chebychev decomposition (up to dimension 7: $7^7 < 10^6$);
\item Natural neighbour interpolation (in dimension 2 or 3)
  or other linear interpolations (barycenters).
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cryptocurrency bubble detection:\\ a new stock market dataset, financial task\\ and hyperbolic models}\\
  R. Sawhney et al.
\end{book}
Identify bubbles in cryptocurrencies (9 exchanges $\times$ 50 currencies, 5 years of daily OHLC prices)
with the \textsc{psy} model, and train a hyperbolic GRU on both text
(tweets with currency tickers, e.g., \texttt{\$DOGE})
and prices, to predict the probability that a bubble starts or ends at time $t$.

In the Poincar\'e ball:
\begin{align*}
  g_x &= \dfrac{ 2 }{ 1 - \n x ^2 } g_{\text{Euclidean}} \\
  x \oplus y &= \dfrac{
    \bigl( 1 + 2 \langle x , y \rangle + \n y ^2 \bigr) x +
    \bigl( 1 - \n x ^2 \bigr) y
  }{
    1 + 2 \langle x, y \rangle + \n x ^2 \n y ^2
  } \\
  \exp_x(v) &= x \oplus \biggl(
  \tanh \biggl(
  \dfrac{ \n v }{ 1 - \n x ^2 }
  \biggr)
  \dfrac{ v }{ \n v }
  \biggr) \\
  \log_x(y) &= \bigl( 1 - \n x ^2 \bigr)
  \tanh^{-1} \bigl( \n{ -x \oplus y } \bigr)
  \dfrac{ - x \oplus y }{ \n{ - x \oplus y } } \\
  W \otimes x &= \exp_0 \bigl( W \log_0 (x) \bigr)
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Testing for multiple bubbles:\\ historical episodes of exuberance\\ and collapse in the S\&P 500}\\
  P.C.B. Phillips et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To detect a (single) price bubble, try the following test statistics:
\begin{align*}
  \text{PYW:}&& \text{SADF}(r_0) &= \sup_{\substack{ r_0 \leq r_2 \leq 1 }} \text{ADF}_{[0,r_2]} \\
  \text{PSY:}&& \text{GSADF}(r_0) &= \sup_{\substack{ r_0 \leq r_2 \leq 1 \\ 0 \leq r_1 \leq r_2 - r_0 }}
  \text{ADF}_{[r_1, r_2]}.
\end{align*}
They can be adapted to the detection of multiple bubbles.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep multiple instance learning for forecasting stock trends using financial news}\\
  Y. Deng and S.M. Yiu
\end{book}
In \emph{multiple instance learning}.
training instances are arranged in bags, and only bags are labeled, not instances.

Forecast the sign of the next day's returns from news:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Word embedding, pretrained with Glove;
\item BiLSTM;
\item Instance-level classifiers;
\item Aggregation;
\item Final classifier.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The RIFT (representation of inter-related time series) model and its applications}\\
  A. Sokolov et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Compute a neural representation of financial time series by applying
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A TCN on stock returns; 
\item A transformer on industry and market returns;
\item An industry embedding 
\end{compactitem}
in a Siamese network to forecast future correlations.
[Instead, forecast reversion or divergence of pairs, or cointegration, or mutual information.]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Decomposing cross-sectional volatility}\\
  J. Menchero and A. Morozov (2010)
\end{book}
Given a risk model
\[ X_i = \sum_k \beta_{ik} F_k + u_i, \]
the cross-sectional volatility can be decomposed as
\[ \sigma(X_{\bigcdot}) = \sum_k F_k \sigma( \beta_{ \bigcdot k } ) \rho( \beta_{\bigcdot k} , X_{\bigcdot} ) +
\sigma( u_{\bigcdot} ) \rho( u_{\bigcdot} , X_{\bigcdot} ).
\]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{AdaptSPEC: adaptive spectral estimation\\ for nonstationary time series}\\
  O. Rosen et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Model (oscillating) non-stationary time series by recursively splitting them and estimating their spectrum on each segment, with reversible jump Markov chain Monte Carlo (RJMCMC).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical indetence of lead-lag at various timescales between asynchronous time series from $p$-values of transfer entropy}\\
  C. Bongiorno and D. Challet (2022)
\end{book}
Asymptotic distribution of the test statistic
\begin{align*}
  H_0 &:\ \text{TE}(B\to A) = \text{TE}(C\to B) \\
  H_1 &:\ \text{TE}(B\to A) > \text{TE}(C\to B)
\end{align*}
where the transfer entropy 
\[ \text{TE}( B \to A ) = H( A^+ | A ) - H( A^+ | A, B ) \]
($A^+$ denoted the future of $A$)
is a generalization of Granger causality,
often approximated by discretizing the data.
(If you are patient, you can also compute bootstrap $p$-values.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Advances in domain independent\\ linear text segmentation}\\
  F.Y.Y. Choi 
\end{book}
To segment a text:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Split it into sentences;
\item Remove stopwords, step the words, count the words;
\item Compute the matrix of similarities between sentences;
\item Replace each value with its rank in a local region; % , e.g., $\n x$;
\item Compute $s_{ij}$, the sum of the values of
  $\text{sim}_{ \llbracket i, j \rrbracket \times \llbracket i , j \rrbracket }$
  (start along the diagonal and move outwards);
\item Recursively split $\llbracket 1 , N \rrbracket$,
  maximizing the density $\sum s_k / \sum a_k$,
  where $a_k$ if the area of $k \times k$, $k=\llbracket i , j \rrbracket$;
\item To decide when to stop, compute the differences
  $\delta D_n - D_n - D_{n-1}$,
  smooth them, compute their mean $\mu$
  and standard deviation $\sigma$;
  stop when $\delta D > \mu + 1.2 \sigma$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Self-attention between datapoints:\\ going beyond individual input-output pairs\\ in deep learning}\\
  J. Kossen et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Do not use attention only between attributes,
but also between datapoints
(samples -- this assumes you put the whole dataset in each minibatch or, at least, a representative part of it).
Try on tabular data.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Decision transformer:\\ reinforcement learning via sequence modeling}\\
  L. Chen et al. 
\end{book}
To learn, offine,
from suboptimal trajectories,
train a GPT model to predict the next token in a sequence of
return-to-go, state, action. For instance, one can find shortest paths on a graph by training on random walks.
\begin{minted}{python}
  t = embed_t(t) # Positional embedding
  s = embed_s(s)
  a = enbed_a(a)
  R = embed_R(R)
  return transformer( s+t, a+t, R+t ).action
\end{minted}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CrossViT: cross-attention multi-scale vision transformer for image classification}\\
  C.F. Chen et al.
\end{book}
Process both small and large image patches with a ViT;
to combine them, use \emph{cross-attention}, \ie, attention between the \textsc{cls} token for one patch size
and the image patch tokens for the other size --
the \textsc{cls} tokens can be seen as ``inducing points'' (or tokens):
the computations only require linear time.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient and modular implicit differentiation}\\
  M. Blondel et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Implicit differentiation (computing the gradient of the solution
of an optimization problem) in JAX
(for optimization layers, or bilevel problems:
hyperparameter optimization, meta-learning):
separately specify the optimality conditions and implement the optimization.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evaluating robustness of neural networks\\ with mixed integer programming}\\
  V. Tjeng et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Neural networks can be verified (their robustness to adversarial examples can be measured)
efficiently, up to 100,000 ReLU, with mixed integer programming
(\texttt{MIPVerify.jp}).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random dot product graph models\\ for social networks}\\
  S.J. Young and E.R. Scheinerman
\end{book}
The dot product random graph model generates graphs by sampling a vector $X_u$ (from some probability distribution)
and adding an edge $u{-}v$ with probability
$\langle X_u , X_v \rangle$. For directed graphs, use two distributions
$p(u{-}v) = \langle X_u, Y_v \rangle$.
Other random graph models include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Configuration model (prescribed degree distribution);
\item Prefered attachment (Barab\'asi-Albert); 
\item Copying model.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Duplication models for biological networks}\\
  F. Chung et al. (2003)
\end{book}
The degree distribution of biological graphs follows a power law with exponent $\beta \in (1,2)$,
in contrast with non-biological networks, for which $\beta \in (2,4)$.
This can be modeled by a duplication process:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a graph $G_0$;
\item Pick a node at random; 
\item Duplicate it, keeping each edge with probability $p$;
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}
Asymptotically, the power law exponent $\beta$
satisfies
$p (\beta-1) = 1 - p^{\beta-1}$;
in particular,
if $p \in (\tfrac12,1)$, then $\beta<2$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modernizing PHCpack through phcpy}\\
  J. Verschelde (2014)
\end{book}
PHCpack is an (old, file and menu-based) package to solve systems of polynomial equations (over $\CC^n$)
$f(x) = 0$ using a system with known solutions $g(x)=0$
by keeping track of the solutions of $h_t(x)=0$ where
\[ h_t(x) = \gamma (1-t) g(x) + t f(x), \quad t \in (0,1) \]
and $\gamma \in \CC$ is random.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate backtests and copulas\\ for risk evaluation}\\
  B. David and G. Zumbach (2022)
\end{book}
Fit a bivariate Student copula to LM-GARCH innovations
(to remove heteroskedasticity), in-sample;
then apply the \emph{Rosenblatt transform} to out-of-sample
\[ \left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \text{Student copula} & \longrightarrow & \text{Uniform copula} \\
  ( u_1, u_2 ) & \longmapsto & \bigl( u_1 , P [ U_2 \leq u_2 \mid U_1 = u_1 ] \bigr)
\end{array}
\right. \]
and test if the result is indeed uniform.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Polynomial voting rules}\\
  W. Tang and D.D. Yao (2022)
\end{book}
In a proof of stake (PoS) system, agents have a voting power proportional to
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Their stake;
\item The square root of their stake; 
\item Their randomly fluctuating stake: each bidder receives a new stake with probability proportional
  to some power of its current stake.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On finding the community\\ with maximum persistence probability}\\
  A. Avellone et al.
\end{book}
The persistence probability of a subgraph $C \subset V$ of a graph $G=(V,E)$
is \[ \alpha = \dfrac{ \sum\limits_{i,j \in C } a_{ij} }{ \sum\limits_{i \in C, j \in V} a_{ij} }. \]
For small graphs, it can be computed with a mixed integer program (MIP);
for arger graphs, heuristics are available.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Compromise-free Bayesian neural networks}\\
  K. Javid et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{Bayesian evidence} (aka \emph{marginal likelihood})
is the average of the likelihood function
over the parameter space, weighted by the prior distribution.
It is often a proxy for the out-of-sample performance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Randomized Nystr\"om preconditioning}\\
  Z. Frangella et al.
\end{book}
To solve $(A+\mu I) x = b$, with $A$ positive semi-definite, $\mu \geq 0$,
consider a randomized Nystr\"om approximation
\begin{align*}
  \Omega &\sim N( 0_{n \times \ell} I ) \\
  \hat A &= (A\Omega) (\Omega ' A \Omega) ^\dagger (A \Omega)' ,
\end{align*}
compute its eigendecomposition $\hat A = U \Lambda U'$
and use the preconditioner
\[ P = \dfrac{ U ( \Lambda + \mu I ) U' }{ \lambda_\ell + \mu } + ( I - U U' ) \]
\ie, replace $(A+\mu I)$ with $P^{-1}(A+\mu I)$ (it is easy to solve $Py=0$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Binarsity: a penalization for one-hot encoded features in linear supervised learning}\\
  M.Z. Alaya et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Discretize (binarize) continuous variables into $b$ bins
and fit a linear model with a fused lasso (total variation) penalty
to make the transformation locally constant, and a constraint to have the sum of the weights
sum to zero for each preductor.

To total variation proximal operator can be computed efficiently,
and applied separately from the constraint.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantifying the impact of ecological memory on the dynamics of interacting communities}\\
  M. Khalighi et al. (2021)
\end{book}
To add memory to the Lotka-Volterra model, replace $d/dt$
with the fractional derivative $\mathfrak D^\mu$,
where $\mu \in (0,1)$ measures the memory.
\[ \mathfrak D ^\mu g(t) = \dfrac1{\Gamma(1-\mu)} \int_{t_0}^t \dfrac{ g'(\tau) d\tau }{ ( t - \tau )^\mu } \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Three-species Lotka-Volterra model\\ with respect to Caputo and\\ Caputo-Fabrizio fractional operators}\\
  M. Khalighi et al. (2021)  
\end{book}
The fractional derivative is not unique;
popular definitions include (Caputo, Caputo-Fabrizio)
\begin{align*}
  \mathfrak D^\alpha f(t) &= \dfrac1{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \int_0^t \dfrac{ f'(\tau) d\tau }{ ( t - \tau )^\alpha } \\
  \mathfrak D^\alpha f(t) &= \dfrac1{1-\alpha} \int_0^t \exp \biggl[
    - \dfrac{ \alpha (t - \tau ) }{ 1 - \alpha } \biggr] f'(\tau) d\tau.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conic optimization via operator splitting\\ and homogeneous self-dual embedding}\\
  B. O'Donoghue et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The pair of primal and dual problems
\[
\begin{array}{@{}l@{\ }l}
  \text{Find} & x, s \\
  \text{To minimize} & c' x \\
  \text{Such that} & Ax + s = b \\ & x \in \RR^n \\ & s \in K
\end{array}
\ 
\begin{array}{l@{\ }l}
  \text{Find} & y, r \\
  \text{To maximize} & - b' y \\
  \text{Such that} & - A' y + r = c \\ & r = 0 \\ & y \in K^*
\end{array}
\]
can be converted into a feasibility problem
\[ \begin{pmatrix} r \\ s \\ s \end{pmatrix} =
\begin{pmatrix} 0 & A' \\ -A & 0 \\ c' & b' \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} +
\begin{pmatrix} c \\ b \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
\quad
\begin{matrix}
  \rlap{\text{dual constraint}}\hphantom{\text{primal constraint}} \\
  \text{primal constraint} \\
  \rlap{\text{duality gap}}\hphantom{\text{primal constraint}}
\end{matrix} 
\]
or, after introducing scaling factors $\tau, \kappa \geq 0$
to detect primal or dual infeasibility
(\emph{homogeneous self-dual embedding})
\[
\begin{pmatrix} r \\ s \\ \kappa \end{pmatrix} =
\begin{pmatrix}
   0  &  A' & c \\
  -A  &  0  & b \\
  -c' & -b' & 0 
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ \tau \end{pmatrix}.
\]
This problem is self-dual:
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & u, v \\
  \text{Such that} & v = Q u \\
  & (u,v) \in C \times C^*.
\end{array}
\]
It can be solved with the ADMM algorithm.
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & u, v, \tilde u, \tilde v \\
  \text{To minimize} & I_{C \times C^*} (u,v) + I_{ Qu = v } ( \tilde u, \tilde v ) \\
  \text{Such that} & (u,v) = (\tilde u, \tilde v)
\end{array} \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An agent-based model\\ with realistic financial time series:\\
  a method for agent-based models validation}\\
  L.G. de Faria
\end{book}
Long list of stylized facts,
for log-returns
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fat tails, which disappear at lower frequencies;
\item Heavy tails (tail index), even after correction for volatility (GARCH);
\item Equity premium: $\E[r] > E[ r_{\text{rf}} ]$;
\item Excess volatility: $\sigma(\text{returns}) > \sigma(\text{fundamentals})$;
\item Leverage: $\cor( r_{\text{past}} , \sigma_{\text{future}} ) < 0$;
\item No autocorrelation;
\item Long memory: $\cor( \va{ r_t }, \va{ r_s } )$;
\item Power law of returns (''inverse cubic law'') and of volatility;
\item Volatility clustering: $\cor( \sigma_t, \sigma{t+1} )$;
\item $\cor( \sigma, \text{volume} ) > 0$
\end{compactitem}
volume:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Power law;
\item Long memory
\end{compactitem}
inter-trade duration:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Clustering; 
\item Long memory; 
\item Over-dispersion
\end{compactitem}
transaction size:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Power law
\end{compactitem}
spreads:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\cor( \text{spread}, \sigma ) > 0 $;
\item $\cor( \text{spread}, \text{volume} ) < 0$.
\end{compactitem}

