\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{E(n) equivariant graph neural network}\\
  V.G. Satorras et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Replace the GNN layer with
\begin{align*}
  m_{ij} &= \phi_2( h_i^\ell, h_j^\ell, a_{ij}) \\
  m_i &= \sum_{ j \in N(i) } m_{ij} \\
  h_i^{\ell+1} &= \phi_h( h_i^\ell, m_i ) 
\end{align*}
with
\begin{align*}
  m_{ij} &= \phi_2( h_i^\ell, h_j^\ell, \n{ x_i^\ell - x_j^\ell } ^2 , a_{ij} ) \\
  x_i^{\ell+1} &= x_i^\ell + \operatorname*{Mean}_{ j \neq i } ( x_i^\ell - x_j^\ell ) \phi_x( m_{ij} ) \\
  m_i &= \text{unchanged} \\
  h_i^{\ell+1} &= \text{unchanged}
\end{align*}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Vector neurons: a general framework\\ for SO(3)-equivariant networks}\\
  C. Deng et al.
\end{book}
Replace 1-dimensional (scalar) neurons with 3-dimensional ones:
linear transformations
\[ f_W: \left\{
\begin{array}{r@{\,}c@{\,}l}
  \RR^{c \times 3} & \longrightarrow & \RR^{c' \times 3} \\
  V & \longmapsto & WV
\end{array}
\right. \]
are natually equivariant,
$f_W(V) R = (WV) R = W(VR) = f_W(VR)$,
but the non-linearities have to be changed, e.g., to $V \mapsto V'$, where
\begin{align*}
  & V = ( v_1,\dots,v_c) \in \RR^{c\times 3} \\
  & W_c, U_c \in \RR^{1 \times C} \text{ for each } c \in \llbracket 1 , C \rrbracket \\
  & q_c = W_c V, \quad k_c = U_c V \in \RR^{1 \times 3} \\
  & v' = \begin{cases}
    q_c & \text{if } \langle q_c, k_c \rangle = 0 \\
    q_c - \left\langle q_c , \dfrac{ k_c }{ \n{ k_c } } \right\rangle \dfrac{ k_c }{ \n{ k_c } }
    & \text{ otherwise.}
  \end{cases}
\end{align*}
(The normalization layers are easy to make equivariant.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the equivalence between temporal and static equivariant graph representations}\\
  J. Gao et B. Ribeiro (2022)
\end{book}
Instead of time-and-graph representations
(compute node embeddings for each graph $G_t$ in the sequence, and then model the evolution of those embeddings),
try time-then-graph: compute representations of the time series of node features (e.g., with an LSTM),
and then embed them.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Inductive representation learning\\ on temporal graphs}\\
  D. Xu et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Dynamic graphs can be seen as graphs with another type of edge, for the time evolution;
temporal graph attention (TGAT) layers use the attention mechanism in the time dimension
(in the space dimension, use a GCN, GAT, etc.)
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Temporal graph networks\\ for deep learning on dynamic graphs}\\
  E. Rossi et al.
\end{book}
To process dynamic graphs
(in discrete or continuous time, \ie, sequences of graphs, or time-stamped lists of graph events),
replace the node (or edge) features with time series (LSTM, attention, etc.).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cycle representation learning\\ for inductive relation prediction}\\
  Z. Yan et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Given a graph $(V,E)$, its incidence matrix $\partial \in \FF_2^{ \va V \times \va E }$
defines a map $\partial: \FF_2^{\va E} \to \FF_2 ^{ \va V }$;
its kernel is the set of cycles, $Z$.

To get a basis of $Z$, pick a node $p$
and build its shortest path tree $T_p$:
the edges not in $T_p$ define cycles,
which form a basis of $Z$,
with $\beta_1 = \va E - \va V + 1 $ elements.

(To have shorter cycles, repeat for several points, e.g.,
the cluster centers from spectral clustering.)

The cycle incidence matrix of this basis is
$C_{T_p} \in \FF_2^{ \va E \times \beta_1 }$.

Use a bidirectional LSTM to compute cycle features.

Build a new graph, with cycles as nodes, and edge weights equal to the number of edges the cycles have in common.

To test if an edge should be in the graph, add it to the graph, and use a GNN on the cycle graph to compute its ``confidence''.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GNNRank: learning global rankings\\ from pairwise comparisons\\ via directed graph neural networks}\\
  Y. He et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Given a set of pairwise comparisons between $n$ elements
as the (weighted) adjacency matrix $A$ of a directed graph,
\emph{serial rank} computes the binary comparison matrix
$C_{ij} = \text{sign}(A_{ij} - A_{ji})$,
then the similarity matrix
$S = \tfrac12 ( n \mathbf 1 \mathbf 1 ' + C C' )$,
and finally its Fiedler vector
(eigenvector for the second largest eigenvalue --
the first one is $\mathbf 1$),
whose coordinate define the desired order.

GNNRank replaces the computation of the similarity matrix $A \mapsto S$ with a GNN,
and the computation of the Fiedler vector
with a few proximal gradient steps for the constrained optimization problem
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \\
  \text{To minimize} & x' S x \\
  \text{Such that} & \n x _2 = 1 \\ & x' \mathbf 1 = 1
\end{array} \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{G$^2$CN: graph Gaussian convolution networks with concentrated graph filters}\\
  M. Li et al.
\end{book}
The action of a GNN on node features can be written as $x \mapsto g(L) x$,
where $L$ is the normalized Laplacian.
Look at the maximum response, the center, and the bandwidth.
\begin{align*}
  R &= \max_{ \lambda \in [0,2] } g(\lambda) \\
  b &= \argmax_{ \lambda \in [0,2] } g(\lambda) \\
  \text{bw} &= \int_0^2 \mathbf1 \bigl( g(\lambda) \geq R / \sqrt2 \bigr) d \lambda
\end{align*}
Try $g(\lambda) = e^{ - T ( \lambda - b ) ^ 2 }$.
 
