(You can stack several rerankers.)

\textbf{3.} BERT is a transformer-based encoder-only network
(GPT is decoder-only; the original transformer is a sequence-to-sequence model)
providing \emph{contextual embeddings}.

MonoBERT uses BERT to predict if a document is relevant to a query
\[ P\bigl[ \text{relevant} \,|\, \texttt{[CLS]} q \texttt{[SEP]} d \texttt{[SEP]} \bigr] \]
from the contextual embedding of the \texttt{[CLS]} token.

To overcome the input length limit:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Birch trains BERT on short passages (e.g., tweets),
  and uses aggregated sentence scores;
\item VERT-MaxP trans BERT on overlapping passages, assuming that they are all relevant
  (or irrelevant) if the document is,
  and uses aggregated (max) passage relevance at inference time;
\item CEDR feeds BERT embeddings to an interaction model;
\item Parade uses BERT to compute a vector representation of each sentence,
  then another BERT to turn that sequence of sentence embeddings into a document embedding.
\end{compactitem}

The Reformer (LSH attention) and the Longformer (sparse attention) also deal with long documents,

DuoBERT compares the relevance of two documents.

\textbf{4.} The vocabulary used in a query may not match that in the document (e.g., ``car'', vs ``automobile''):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The query can be expanded:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item First, retrieve documents with the query words;
  \item Assume the first results are relevant;
  \item Add words from those socuments to the query;
  \item Retrieve more documents;
  \end{compactitem}
  Those words can be chose by training a model
  \[ P[ \text{term} | \text{query} , \text{document} ] \]
  measuring how relevant a term is (CEQE);
\item The query can be reformulated to make it more human-like
  (BERT works better with those);
\item The document themselves can be turned into queries (doc2query, trained on MS MARCO);
\item The query terms can be re-weighted, by modeling
  \[
  \begin{array}{c}
    \text{representation}\\[-2pt]
    \text{of document $d$} \\
    \text{term $t$}
  \end{array}
  \longmapsto
  \dfrac{
    \text{\# queries relevant for $d$ with $t$}
  }{
    \text{\# queries relevant for $d$}
  }
  \]
\item Expansion and reweighting can be combined.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{5.} Document retrieval, with approximate neighbour (ANN) search,
from pretrained dense representations,
is worse than retrieval from average pooling of GloVe embeddings --
fine-tuning is necessary, and careful selection of negative samples
improves performance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{InPars: data augmentation for intormation retrieval using large language models}\\
  L. Bonifacio et al.
\end{book}
Given a (small) set of (query, document) pairs,
generate plausible queries for all the documents
(few chot learning,
with GPT3 and the MS MARCO dataset, providing triplets
text / good question / bad question);
keep the highest probability pairs,
and use them for reranking
(T5, after \texttt{pyserini}).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pyserini: a Python toolkit for reproducible information retrieval research with sparse and dense representations}\\
  J. Lin et al.
\end{book}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ToxiGen: a large-scale machine-generated dataset for adversarial and implicit hate speech detection}\\
  T. Hartvigsen et al.
\end{book}
Toxic language detection syste,s often incorrectly flag texts merely because they mention
some minority group (often targeted by online hate): use a language to generate subtly toxic and benign text about those minorities.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Zero-shot neural passage retrieval via domain-targeted synthetic question generation}\\
  J. Ma et al.
\end{book}
Synthetic question generation, to compute question embeddings (instead of document embeddings),
for first stage information retrieval (nearest neighbour).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SwinIR: image restoration using Swin transformer}\\
  J. Liang et al.
\end{book}
Transformers for image restoration (upscaling, denoising, artefact removal):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a $3\times 3$ convolution, to extract shallow features;
\item Apply 6 residual swin transformer blocks (RSTB), followed by a convolution; 
\item Each RSTB has 6 swin transformer layers (STL), followed by a convolution; 
\item Each STL has a LayerNorm,
  a multihead self-attention (MSA) on non-overlapping $M\times M$ windows,
  another LayerNorm, an MLP;
\item The network ends with a convolution,
  to reconstruct the image from the shallow and deep features
  (there are skip-connections everywhere).
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalized cross-entropy loss for training deep neural networks with noisy labels}\\
  Z. Zhang and M.R. Sabuncu
\end{book}
To increase robustness to noisy labels,
try the negative Box-Cox transformation
$\text{loss}(j,p) = ( 1 - p_j^q ) / q$,
$q \in (0,1)$,
where $j$ is the label ($y$) and $p$ the probabilities output by the model ($\hat y$).
The cross-entropy
$\ell = \log p_j$
and the MAE
$\ell = \n{ e_j - p }_1 = 2 - 2 p_j$
are limiting cases,
for $q\to 0$ or $1$.
Truncate by replacing $p_j$ with $\min(p_j,k)$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling tabular data using conditional GAN}\\
  L. Xu et al. (2019)
\end{book}
To account for multimodal continuous columns, estimate the number of modes
with a \emph{variational Gaussian mixture} (VGM)
and fit a Gaussian mixture;
then, separately sample the mixture component and the value within that component.
To account for the imbalance in discrete columns,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Learn conditional generators, conditioned on the value of one of the discrete columns,
\item Sample the discrete values according to the log-frewquency of each category.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Opacus: user-friendly differential privacy library in pytorch}\\
  A. Yousefpour et al. (2021)
\end{book}
DP-SGD (differentially private stochastic gradient descent)
makes SGD differnetially private
(intuitively, removing one sample from the batch does not change the update)
by computing the per-sample gradients, clipping them, aggregating them, and adding noise.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Supervised contrastive learning}\\
  P. Khosla et al. (2020)
\end{book}
The \emph{triplet loss} uses one positive sample (augmentation)
and one negative sample,
but requires negative mining.
\emph{Contrastive learning} uses one positive sample and many negative samples
\begin{align*}
  \text{loss} &= \sum_i - \log \dfrac{
    \exp ( z_i \cdot z_{j(i)} / \tau )
  }{
    \sum\limits_{ a \in N(i) }
    \exp ( z_i \cdot z_a / \tau )
  } \\
  i &:\text{ samples} \\
  j(i) &:\text{ positive sample} \\
  N(i) &:\text{ negative samples} \\
  z_i \in \SS^{d-1}&:\text{ features.}
\end{align*}
Supervised contrastive learning uses many positives
(augmentations and samples from the same class)
and many negative samples
\[
\text{loss} = \sum_i -
\operatorname*{Mean}_{ p \in P(i) }
\log \dfrac{
    \exp ( z_i \cdot z_p / \tau )
  }{
    \sum_{ a \in N(i) }
    \exp ( z_i \cdot z_a / \tau )
  } 
\]
(putting the mean outside the log rather than inside works better).

Generate features in 2048 dimensions, normalize them,
reduce the dimension to 128 (MLP),
and normalize again;
use scalar products instead of Euclidean distance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Energy-latency attacks via sponge poisoning}\\
  A.E. Cin\`a (2022)
\end{book}
Mount a DoS attack on a deep learning model, running on sparsity-based ASIC accelerators
adopting zero-skipping strategies to avoid multiplications when the input is zero,
by adding a penalty ($\ell_0$ relaxation)
\[ \hat \ell_0(x) = \sum \dfrac{ x_i^2 }{ x_i^2 + \epsilon } \]
for non-zero activations.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A new correlation coefficient between categorical, ordinal and interval variables with Pearson characteristics}\\
  M. Baak et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The $\phi_k$ correlation coefficient (implementation in \texttt{phik})
is the correlation for which a Gaussian variable would have the same $\chi^2$
(after binning the quantitative variables into 10 bins).

The \emph{global correlation coefficient} is the highest possible correlation between variable $k$
and a linear combination of the other variables.
\[ g_k = \sqrt{ 1 - \bigl[ V_{kk} \cdot ( V^{-1} )_{kk} \bigr] ^{-1} } \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Decision tree learning with spatial modal logics}\\
  G. Pagliarini and G. Sciavicco
\end{book}
\emph{Modal logic} extends propositional logic with
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{\rotatebox[origin=c]{45}{$\square$}}
  P & \text{possibly } P \\
  \square P & \text{necessarily } P.
\end{array} \]
It is less expressive than first order logic (FOL, which allows
quantifiers, $\forall$, $\exists$).

\emph{Allen's interval algebra}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.1,yscale=.1,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \begin{scope}
    \draw (0,0) -- (6,0) -- (6,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle;
    \draw[ultra thick] (1,2) -- (3,2);
