\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistence images: a stable vector representation of persistent homology}\\
  H. Adams et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Persistence diagrams can be fed directly to distance-based machine learning algorithms
(with the bottleneck distance, or the Wasserstein distance),
but not to arbitrary machine learning algorithms (which often require coordinates).
The \emph{persistence image} is a vector representation
for a persistence diagram (PD) obtained by:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Rotating the (birth, death) PD into (birth, persistence),
  where $\text{persistence} = \text{death} - \text{birth}$;
\item Replacing each point with a Gaussian distribution (you need to choose its variance);
\item Considering a weighted sum of those distributions,
  with weights increasing with persistence (it should be zero on the diagonal)
  \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.2,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
    \draw (0,0) -- (2,0);
    \draw (0,0) -- (0,1);
    \draw[very thick] (0,0) -- (1,1) -- (2,1);
  \end{tikzpicture}
\item Rasterising the data (for $H_0$, the birth coordinate is always zero: it is a 1-dimensional image).
\end{compactitem}

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=\hsize]{2022-07-17_tda.pdf}
\end{center}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistence diagrams\\ with linear machine learning models}\\
  I. Obayashi and Y. Hiraoka
\end{book}
Linear models built on the \emph{persistence image}
are interpretable: large positive or negative coefficients correspond to desired or undesirable
regions of the persistence diagram.

Add a regularization term to smooth the weights for higher resolution persistence images.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistence weighted Gaussian kernel\\ for topological data analysis}\\
  G. Kusano et al.
\end{book}
Map persistence diagrams $D$ to functions
\[ D \mapsto \sum_{x\in D} w(x) k(x,\cdot) \]
where $k$ is a kernel and $w$ a weight function decreasing with persistence.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistent entropy for separating topological features from noise in Vietoris-Rips complexes}\\
  N. Atienza et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Given a persistence diagram $(b_i,d_i)_i$,
the \emph{persistent entropy} is the entropy $H$ of the persistences
$p_i \propto d_i - b_i$;
it can help prune non-signficant barcodes
(intuitively, $\exp H$ is the number of significant barcodes).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tropical coordinates\\ on the space of persistence barcodes}\\
  S. Kali\v snik
\end{book}
The \emph{tropical semiring} is $(\bar\RR, \min, +, \infty, 0)$;
the \emph{arctic semiring} is $(\RR \union \{-\infty\}, \max, +, -\infty, 0)$.
Max-plus polynomials up to functional equivalence
(identify polynomials if they always take the same values)
form a semiring $\text{MaxPlus}[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.
Similarly, Min-plus polynomials, and rationals tropical functions,
form semirings.

Symmetric tropical rational functions can be used as coordinates on the barcode space.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The ring of algebraic functions\\ on persistence barcodes}\\
  A. Adcock et al. (2013)
\end{book}
We could use symmetric polynomials as coordinates
on the space of barcodes:
$k[x_1,y_1,\dots,x_n,y_n]^{\mathfrak S_n}$
is complicated (there are non-trivial relations between the symmetric polynomilas -- syzygies),
but $\indlim k[x_1,y_1,\dots,x_n,y_n]^{\mathfrak S_n}$
is freely generated.
However, we do not only want
$(x_1,y_1,\dots,x_n,y_n) \sim (x_1,y_1,\dots,x_n,y_n,0,0)$,
but
$(x_1,y_1,\dots,x_n,y_n) \sim (x_1,y_1,\dots,x_n,y_n,x_{n+1},x_{n+1})$:
the corresponding subring of $k[x_1,y_1,\dots,x_n,y_n]^{\mathfrak S_n}$
is that of polynomials $f$ such that
\[ \left(
\dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial x_i } + \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial y_i }
\right) f \in ( y_i - x_i ) \]
and the limit of those subrings is freely generated by the
\[ \sum_i x_i^{a+1} y_i ^b - x_i ^a y_i^{b+1} . \]

(Those coordinates are not Lipschitz wrt the bottleneck or Wasserstein distance.)
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stable topological signatures\\ for points on 3D shapes}\\
  M. Carri\`ere et al.
\end{book}
To identify a point on a surface,
consider their persistence diagram, \ie,
the 1-holes of the geodesic balls centered on this point.

% TODO: PLOT

To get a vector embedding, compute the pairwise distances between the points
in the persistence diagram,
sort them, and pad them with zeroes,
\[ m(x,y) = \min\bigl\{
d(x,y),
d(x,\text{diag}),
d(y,\text{diag})
\bigr\}.
\]

% TODO: PLOT
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A stable multi-scale kernel\\ for topological machine learning}\\
  J. Reininghaus et al.
\end{book}
Define a feature map
$\Phi_\sigma: \text{PD} \to L^2$
on the space of persistence diagrams
by replacing each point $p$
with a Gaussian density
and subtracting another Gaussian $\bar p$ on the other side of the diagonal.
\begin{align*}
  & \Phi_\sigma(D) : x \longmapsto \sum_{ p \in D }
  \phi_\sigma\bigl( \n{ x -      p } \bigr) -
  \phi_\sigma\bigl( \n{ x - \bar p } \bigr) \\
  & \phi_\sigma(t) = e^{ - t^2 / \sigma^2 }
\end{align*}

The corresponding kernel is
\[ k_\sigma(F,G) = \sum_{\substack{ p \in F \\ q \in G }}
\phi_\sigma\bigl( \n{ p - q } \bigr) -
\phi_\sigma\bigl( \n{ p - \bar q } \bigr).
\]

% TODO: PLOT (fig2)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical analysis\\ of persistence intensity functions}\\
  Y.C. Chen et al.
\end{book}
Replace persistence diagrams with weighted sums of Gaussians,
one for each point,
with weights proportional to the persistence,
$w = \text{death} - \text{birth}$:
you can then easily average them, cluster them, etc.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A topology layer for machine learning}\\
  R. Br\"uel-Gabrielsson et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Persistence diagrams as a layer:
you can use
\[ \xi( p, q, i_0, \text{PD}_k ) =
\sum_{i\geq i_0} \va{ d_i - b_i }^p
\left( \dfrac{ b_i + d_i }2 \right)^q
\]
in the loss, \ie, increase or decrease
$\beta_0$, $\beta_1$,
$(\beta_0-1)_+$,
$(\beta_1-1)_+$,
etc.

Use as a regularizer (image reconstruction)
or a prior (generative models).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Bayesian learning rule}\\
  M.E. Khan and H. Rue
\end{book}
Empirical risk minimization
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\theta \sum_i \ell\bigl(
y_i , f_\theta(x_i) \bigr) + R(\theta) \]
can be generalized to probability distributions
\begin{align*}
  & \operatorname*{Minimize}_q \E_{ \theta \sim q }
  \biggl[ \sum_i \ell \bigl( y_i , f_\theta(x_i) \bigr) \biggr] +
  \text{KL} \bigl( q(\theta) \bigm\| p(\theta) \bigr) \\
  & p(\theta) \propto \exp - R(\theta).
\end{align*}
The \emph{Bayesian learning rule} looks for a probability
distribution in an exponential family
\begin{align*}
  q(\theta)  &= h(\theta) \exp\bigl[ \langle \lambda , T(\theta) \rangle - A(\lambda) \bigr] \\
  \lambda &: \text{ natural parameters} \\
  A &: \text{ log-partition (cummulant) function} \\
  T(\theta) &: \text{ sufficient statistics} \\
  h &: \text{ base measure} \\
  \mu &= \E_{ \theta \sim q } \bigl[ T(\theta) \bigr] \text{ expectation parameters}
\end{align*}
by iterating
\[ \lambda_{t+1} \leftarrow \lambda_t - \rho_t \tilde\nabla_{\!t} \biggl[
  \E_{\theta \sim q_t} \bar\ell(\theta) - H(q_t) \biggr]
\]
where
\begin{align*}
  \bar\ell(\theta) &= \sum_i \ell\bigl( y_i , f_\theta(x_i) \bigr) \\
  H(q_t) &= \E_{ \theta \sim q_t } \bigl[ - \log q_t(\theta) \bigr] \text{ entropy} \\
  \tilde\nabla_{\!t} \E_{ \theta \sim q_t } [ \bigcdot ] &=
  F(\lambda_t)^{-1} \nabla_{\!t} \E_{ \theta \sim q_t } [ \bigcdot ] \text{ natural gradient} \\
  &= \left. \nabla_{\!\!\mu} \E_{ \theta \sim q_t } [ \bigcdot ] \right|
  _{ \mu = \nabla_{\!\!\lambda} A(\lambda_t) } \\
  F(\lambda) &= \nabla^2_{\!\!\lambda} A(\lambda) \text{ Fisher information matrix}.
\end{align*}
For instance, the exponential families
$N(m,I)$ (unknown mean, known variance) and
$N(m,S^{-1})$ (unknown mean and variance)
give gradient descent and Newton's method.
   
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Do $t$-statistic hurdles need to be raised?}\\
  A.Y. Chen (2022)
\end{book}
Calls to lower the 5\% significance rate for publications account for false positives
but ignores (unobserved) false negatives.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MDD aggregated two-sample test}\\
  A. Schrab et al. (2021)
\end{book}
To test if two samples come from the same distribution
(non-parametrically, not only in dimension 1, contrary to the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test)
use the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) test
(an integral probability metric)
\[ \text{MMD}_k(p,q) = \sup_{\substack{
    f \in \mathscr H \\ \n{ f }_{ \mathscr H_k } \leq 1 }}
\va{
  \E_{ X \sim p } f(X) - \E_{ Y \sim q } f(Y) 
}
\]
where $\mathscr H_k$ is the RKHS associated to a kernel $k$
\begin{align*}
  \widehat{ \text{MMD} }_a &=
  \operatorname*{Mean}_{i, i'} k( x_i , x_{i'} ) +
  \operatorname*{Mean}_{j, j'} k( x_j, x_{j'} ) \\
  &\hphantom{={}}
  - 2 \operatorname*{Mean}_{ij} k( x_i, y_j )
\end{align*}
or
\begin{align*}
  & \widehat{\text{MMD}} _b =
  \operatorname*{Mean}_{i,j} h( X_i, X_j, Y_i Y_j ) \\
  & h( x, x', y, y' ) = k( x,x') + k(y,y') - k(x,y') - k(x',y)
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{JKONet: proximal optimal transport modeling of population dynamics}\\
  C. Bunne et al. (2021)
\end{book}
The \emph{JKO flow} is an analogue of proximal descent for probability distributions
\[ \rho_{t+1} = \argmin_\rho J(\rho) + \lambda W^2( \rho_t , \rho ) \]
where $\rho_t$ is a probability distribution and $W$ the 2-Wasserstein distance.

The optimal transport map is the gradient of a convex function
(if $\rho_t$ has a density -- Brenier's theorem),
which can be modeled with an \emph{input-convex neural net} (ICNN).
\begin{align*}
  T &= \nabla \psi_\theta \\
  \rho_{t+1} &= ( \nabla \psi_{\theta^*} ) _\# \rho_t \\
  \theta^* &= \argmin_\theta
  J \bigl( (\nabla \psi_\theta )_\# \rho_t \bigr) \!+
  \!\lambda\!\! \int\! \n{ x - \nabla \psi_\theta(x) }^2 \! d \rho_t(x)
\end{align*}

We want to find $J$ such that the JKO flow describes the evolution of a cloud of points
\[ J_\xi(\rho) = \int \E_\xi(x) d\rho(x) . \]

Implementation in \textsc{jax}, with \texttt{ott}.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fully parallel hyperparameter search:\\ reshaped space-filling}\\
  M.L. Cauwet et al.
\end{book}
Transform low disccrepancy sequences in $[0,1]^d$ as
$x \mapsto \Phi\bigl( \lambda \cdot \Phi^{-1}(x) \bigr) $
or
$x \mapsto \Phi\bigl( \lambda \cdot \Phi_{\text{Cauchy}}^{-1}(x) \bigr) $
(remove the outer $\Phi$ if the parameters are unbounded).
\[
<<CauchyRecentering,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=4.5,fig.height=1.5,out.width='.8\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
library(DBQR)
n <- 500
set.seed(1)
x <- runif(n)
y <- runif(n)
f <- function(x, lambda=.3) pnorm( lambda * qnorm(x) )
g <- function(x, lambda=.3) pnorm( lambda * qcauchy(x) )
op <- par( mfrow=c(1,3), mar = c(.1,1,2,1) )
plot( x, y, col = dbColours[1], pch = 16, axes = FALSE, main = "Original" )
box()
plot( f(x), f(y), col = dbColours[1], pch = 16, axes = FALSE, main = "Gaussian recentering" )
box()
plot( g(x), g(y), col = dbColours[1], pch = 16, axes = FALSE, main = "Cauchy recentering" )
box()
par(op)
@
\]

The \emph{Hammersley sequence} is the same as the Halton sequence,
except for the first dimension, in which the points are equidistant
(the Halton and Hammersley sequences have a ``scrambled'' version,
with better discrepancy guarantees).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evolution under strong noise: a self-adaptive evolution strategy can reach the lower performance bound -- the pcCMSA-ES}\\
  M. Hellwig and H.G. Beyer
\end{book}
Adaptively change the population size in CMSA-ES,
increasing it if the optimization stalls because of noise.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Covariance matrix adaptation revisited:\\ the CMSA evolution strategy}\\
  H.G. Beyer and B. Sendhoff
\end{book}
% Not read
CMSA-ES is a simplification of the CMA-ES optimization algorithm
with fewer hyperparameters, scaling to larger populations.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parametric estimate of intensity inhomogeneoties applied to MRI}\\
  M. Styner et al. (2000)
\end{book}
In ``(1+1) evolution strategy'',
both the population size and the number of children are equal to 1.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio performance attribution\\ via Shapley value}\\
  N. Moehle et al. (2021)
\end{book}
The Shapley attribution can be defined as
\[ a = \dfrac1{n!} \sum_{ \pi \in \mathfrak S_n } a_\pi \]
where
\[ a_{\pi,i} =
f( e_{\pi(1)} + \cdots + e_{\pi(i)} ) - 
f( e_{\pi(1)} + \cdots + e_{\pi(i-1)} )
\]
and approximated by sampling permutations $\pi \in \mathfrak S_n$
(with replacement) but we end up evaluating the same $f(x)$ many times.
Instead, use
\[ a_i = \sum_{ x \,:\,x_i = 0 }
\dfrac{
  (\mathbf 1 ' x ) ! ( n - \mathbf 1 ' x - 1 )
}{
  n !
}
\bigl( f(x + e_i) - f(x) \bigr)
\]
(where we assume, wlg, $f(0) = 0$)
and approximate it
\begin{align*}
  a_i &= \E_{ x \sim p } \bigl[ f(x+e_i) - f(x) \bigr] \\
  p(x) &=
\dfrac{
  (\mathbf 1 ' x ) ! ( n - \mathbf 1 ' x - 1 )
}{
  n !
}
\end{align*}
by sampling the number of active features $\mathbf 1 ' x$
from a multinomial distribution
\[ p_k = \dfrac{ k! ( n - k - 1 ) ! }{ n ! } \]
and then sampling $\mathbf 1' x$ features at random.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Does non-linear factorization of financial returns help build better and stabler portfolios?}\\
  B. Spilak and W.K. H\"ardle (2022)
\end{book}
Statistical factor portfolios
can be computed with a low rank factorization
\begin{align*}
  X &\approx W F \\
  X &: \text{ asset returns} \\
  F &: \text{ factor returns}.
\end{align*}

Convex non-negative matrix factorization adds constraints
\[ W \geq 0, \quad F = H' X , \quad H \geq 0 \]
(the assets are non-negative linear combinations of the factors,
and conversely).

Non-linear statistical factors can be computed with an autoencoder
\[ x \mapsto z = H x + b \mapsto W \sigma(z) + \epsilon \]
with constraints $H \geq 0$, $W \geq 0$
and penalties $\n{ W ' W - I }_2^2$
and $\n{ \Sigma_z \odot ( \mathbf 1 - I ) }^2$.

Use risk parity, hierarchical risk parity, etc.
to combine the factor portfolios.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast high-dimensional integration\\ using tensor networks}\\
  S. Cassel
\end{book}
The tensor train decomposition generalizes the approximation
\[ f(x,y) \approx \dfrac{ f( x, \bar y ) f( \bar x, y ) }{ f(\bar x , \bar y) }; \]
it interpolates a function from its restrictions on a few 1-dimensional axis-aligned subspaces
\begin{align*}
  f(x,y) &\approx \sum_{ij} f(x, b_j ) ( Q^{-1} )_{ij} f( a_{ij}, y ) \\
  Q_{k\ell} &= f(a_k , b_\ell)
\end{align*}
In dimension $n$:
\[ f(x_1,\dots,x_q) = F(x_1) Q_{12}^{-1} F_2(x_2) \cdots Q_{d-1,d}^{-1} F_d(x_d). \]

Appplication include high-dimensional integration;
it can be combined with Aitken extrapolation.

Given a sequence $(\psi_n)_n$, Aitken extrapolation writes it as a telescopic series.
\begin{align*}
  \psi_n &= \psi_0 + \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} ( \psi_{i+1} - \psi_i )
  && \text{telescopic series} \\
  &= \psi_0 + \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \bigl( g(\psi_i) - \psi_i \bigr) \\
  g(\psi_\infty) &= \psi_\infty && \text{fixed point} \\
  \dfrac{
    g(\psi_i)
  }{
    \psi_i - \psi_\infty
  } & \approx g'( \psi_i ) && \text{secant method} \\
  \psi_\infty &\approx \psi_i - \dfrac{ g(\psi_i) }{ g'(\psi_i) }
\end{align*}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Reciprocity in machine learning}\\
  M. Sundararajan and W. Krichene (2022)
\end{book}
Compare the average over $i$ and the average over $j$ of the influence of
observation $i$ on prediction $j$ (the latter is Cook's distance):
they tend to be similar.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bridging level-$k$ to Nash equilibrium}\\
  D. Levin and L. Zhang (2022)
\end{book}
Nash equilibrium assumes that each player is equally sophisticated.
A level 0 player plays at random;
a level $n+1$ player plays the best strategy assuming the opponent is level $n$.
An NLK$_1$ player plays the optimal strategy
assuming the opponent is level 0 with probability $\lambda$ and NLK$_1$
with probability $1-\lambda$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generative art using neural visual grammars and dual encoders}\\
  C. Fernando et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Given a text prompt, generate an image with a hierarchical neural $L$-system
and evaluate them with an image/text dual encoder.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Planting undetectable backdoors\\ in machine learning models}\\
  S. Goldwasser et al.
\end{book}
Given a natural training algorithm
\[ \text{train}: \text{data} \longmapsto \text{classifier}, \]
a \emph{backdoor} is a pair of algorithms
\[
\begin{array}{lrcl}
  \text{Backdoor}: & \text{data} & \longmapsto & \text{classifier, key} \\
  \text{Activate}: & \text{input}, \text{key} & \longmapsto & \text{new input} 
\end{array}
\]
such that
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The classifier is indistinguishable from the natural one
\item The new input is close to the old, but ends up in the other class.
\end{compactitem}
Undetectable backdoors exist, for dimension reasons
(the same arguments proves the existence of adversarial examples)
and can easily be built, e.g., for models using random Fourier features.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Manipulating SGD with data ordering attacks}\\
  I. Shumailov et al. (2021)
\end{book}
You can poison a model by tampering with the order of the samples used to train it
(rank the samples with a surrogate loss).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A generalist agent}\\
  S. Reed et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Compress (behaviour cloning) 600 reinforcement learning, language and image models
into a single model, with a transformer:
convert all the data into discrete sentences
(break down images into 16$\times$16 patches, in raster order;
mu-law encode
\[
F(x) =
\text{sign}(x) \dfrac{
  \log\bigl( 1 + \mu \va x \bigr)
}{
  \log\bigl( 1 + \mu M)
}  
\]
continuous values into $[-1,1]$, and discretize them into 24 bins).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model cards for model reporting}\\
  M. Mitchell et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{model card} should include:
data, model type, model architecture,
training algorithm, loss,
intended use,
out-of-scope use,
performance metrics,
performance across different factors or subpopulations.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep signature models\\ for financial equity time series prediction}\\
  M. Noguer i Alonso et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Forecast time series (daily returns, for 10 US stocks, from 14 days of data),
by stacking \emph{signets}.
\[
