\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding the quintile portfolio}\\
  R. Zhou and D.P. Palomar (2020)
\end{book}
The long-only quintile portfolio
(more generally, the top $k$\% portfolio)
is the solution of the robust optimization problem
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w \\
  \text{To maximize} & \min\limits_{\mu \,:\, \n{ \mu - \mu_0 }_1 \leq \epsilon } w' \mu \\
  \text{Such that} & w \geq 0 \\
  & w' \mathbf 1 = 1
\end{array}
\]
(for some value of $\epsilon$);
for larger values of $\epsilon$,
it is the $1/N$ portfolio.

For the long-short quintile portfolio,
replace the constraints with $\n w _1 - 1$, $w' \mathbf 1 = 0$.

For inverse-volatility weights, replace the uncertainty set with
\( \{ \mu_0 + e \,:\, \n{ e \oslash \sigma }_1 \leq \epsilon \} \)
where $\oslash$ is the elementwise division.

The results generalize to market exposure
$w' \beta = 0$ or sector exposures.

