\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A factor model for option returns}\\
  M. B\"uchner and B. Kelly 
\end{book}
\emph{Instrumented PCA} allows the loadings to depend on exogenous variables
(e.g., moneyness, time to maturity, implied volatility, embedded leverage, Greeks).
\begin{align*}
  r_{it} &: \text{ asset returns} \\
  z_{it} &: \text{ exogenous variables} \\
  \beta_{it} &= z_{it} ' \Gamma + \text{noise} \\
  r_{i, t+1} &= \beta_{it} f_{t+1} + \text{noise} \\
  f_t &: \text{ factor returns} 
\end{align*}
For options on the S\&P 500
(monthly holding period, delta-hedged daily),
3 factors are needed;
they can be interpreted as
volatility surface level,
term structure slope of the volatility surface,
moneyness skew of the volatility surface.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Drawdown beta and portfolio optimization}\\
  R. Ding and S. Uryasev (2021)
\end{book}
The \emph{drawdown at risk} (DaR) is the $\alpha$ quantile of the distribution of drawdowns,
The \emph{conditional drawdown at risk} (CDaR)
is the average of the drawdowns beyond the DaR.
The \emph{expected regret of drawdown} (ERoD)
is the average of the drawdowns beyond some fixed threshold (instead of a quantile).
The \emph{CDaR beta} (resp., ERoD beta)
is the average asset return, divided by the market CDaR (resp. ERoD),
with the averages in the numerator and denominator are computed over matching periods.

You want to invest in stocks with $\beta \approx 0$ but $\beta_{\text{CDaR}} < 0$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Capital asset pricing model (CAPM)\\ with drawdown measure}\\
  M. Zabarankin et al. (2013)
\end{book}
In the CAPM, replace the 1-period returns with returns over market drawdown periods.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sensitivity to large losses and $\rho$-arbitrage\\ for convex risk measures}\\
  M. Herdegen and N. Khan (2022)
\end{book}
A risk measure $\rho$ is suceptible to \emph{$\rho$-arbitrage} if it is possible
to have arbitrary high returns with arbitrarily low risk.
If $\rho$ is
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Monotone: $X \leq Y$ a.s. $\implies \rho(X) \geq \rho(Y)$,
\item Normalized:  $\rho(0) = 0$,
\item Star-shaped: $\forall X \ \forall \lambda \geq 1 \ \rho(\lambda X) \geq \lambda \rho(X)$,
\item Sensitive to large losses:
  \[ P[ X < 0 ] > 0 \implies \exists \lambda > 0 \ \rho(\lambda X) > 0, \]
\end{compactitem}
there is no $\rho$-arbitrage.

