  G. Genovese et al. (2022)
\end{book}
% Clear description of the algorithms to train and sample from an RBM
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probability equivalent level of value at risk\\ and higher order expected shortfalls}\\
  M. Barczy et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{probability equivalent level of VaR and ES},
$\text{PELVE}_\alpha X$ is the $c \geq 1$
such that
$\text{VaR}_{1-\alpha} X = \text{ES}_{1-c\alpha} X$.
It is motivated by the Basel move from $\text{VaR}_{.99}$ to $\text{ES}_{.975}$ (c=2.5).
The PELVE can be generalized from (VaR,\,ES) to $(\rho, \tilde \rho)$,
for families of risk measures
$(\rho_p)_p$ and
\[ \tilde \rho_p = \dfrac1{1-p} \int_p^1 \rho_s(X) ds. \]
The \emph{$n$th order expected shortfall} is
\[ \text{ES}_{p,n} = \dfrac n{1-p} \int_p^1 \left( \dfrac{ s-p }{ 1-p } \right )^{\!\!n-1}
\text{VaR}_s(X) ds. \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple method for measuring inequality}\\
  T. Sitthiyot and K. Holasut (2020)
\end{book}
Instead of using only the Gini index,
look at several measures:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Gini index;
\item Income share held by the top 10\%: $T_{10}$;
\item Income share held by the bottom 10\%: $B_{10}$
  (or their ratio, $B_{10} / T_{10}$);
\item Entropy $H$ of the income distribution.
\end{compactitem}
Combine those measures as
\[
\sqrt{ \dfrac{ \text{Gini}^2 + H^2 }{ 2 } }
\text{ or }
\sqrt{ \dfrac{ \text{Gini}^2 +
    \sqrt{ 1 - \dfrac{ B_{10} }{ T_{10} } }
  }{ 2 } }
\]
since $H \approx ( 1 - B_{10} / T_{10} ) ^ {1/4}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Missing financial data}\\
  S. Bryzgalova et al.
\end{book}
To fill in missing data in panel data $C_{i\ell t}$
\begin{align*}
  i &:\text{ asset} \\
  \ell &:\text{ feature} \\
  t &:\text{ time}
\end{align*}
estimate a cross-sectional factor model (PCA with missing values,
for each $t$, but with $\Lambda_t = \Lambda$ constant)
\[
\underset{I\times L}{C_t} =
\underset{I\times K}{F_t}
\underset{K\times L}{\Lambda\vphantom{C_t}}
+ \text{noise} \]
and use it in a time series model
\[ C_{it} \leftarrow \text{coalesce} \bigl(
C_{it} , f(C_{i,t-1}, F_{it}) \bigr)
\]
(or $f(C_{i,t-1}, C_{i,t+1}, F_{it})$ if you are willing to use future data)
where $f$ is linear.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Vulnerability-CoVaR:\\ investigating the crypto market}\\
  M. Waltz et al. (2022)
\end{book}
There are many generalizations of value at risk (VaR) to several assets:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item CoVaR$^{j|i}$: $\alpha$ quantile of $X_j$ conditioned on $X_i$ being below its $\alpha$ quantile;
\item MCoVaR$^j$: $\alpha$ quantile of $X_j$ conditioned on all the $X_i$ being below their $\alpha$ quantile;
\item VCoVaR$^j$: $\alpha$ quantile of $X_j$ conditioned on at least one $X_i$ being below its $\alpha$ quantile;
\item SCoVaR$^{j|i}$: $\alpha$ quantile of $X_j$ conditioned on $\sum_{i\neq j} X_i$ being below its $\alpha$ quantile;
\item Vulnerability index (VI): probability that $X_j$ is below its $\alpha$ quantile given that
  at least one $X_i$ is below its $\alpha$ quantile;
\item $\Delta$CoVaR: difference between CoVaR$^{j|i}$ and the $\alpha$ quantile of $X_i$
  being at its 50\% quantile.
\end{compactitem}
  
