\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms for optimization}\\
  M.J. Kochenderfer and T.A. Wheeler (2019)
\end{book}

\textbf{3.}
Given a univariate function $f$, three points $a<b<c$
are said to \d{bracket} a minimum if $f(b) < f(a)$
and $f(b) < f(c)$.
\emph{Fibonacci search} splits an interval in an optimal way
(to obtain the smallest bracket after $n$ iterations);
it is often approximated with a \emph{golden section search}.
\emph{Quadratic fit search} (often faster)
improves a bracket by approximating the function with a quadratic and taking its minimum.

For a Lipschitz continuous function,
the \emph{Schubert-Piyavskii method}
keeps track of a lower bound on the function
and progressively improves it.
\[
<<SchubertPiyavskii,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=2,out.width='.8\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
library(DBQR)
op <- par( mar=c(0,0,0,0) )
set.seed(1)
f <- function(x) sin(pi*x)
a <- 1.1*pi
xi <- 2 * runif(5) - 1
xs <- seq(-1,1,length=1000)
xs <- sort( c(xs,xi) )
plot(xs, f(xs), type='l', lwd = 3, col=dbColours[1], axes=FALSE, ylim = c(-1.5,1.1))
yi <- 0 * xs - Inf
for( x in xi ){
  y1 <- f(x) + a * (xs - x) 
  y2 <- f(x) - a * (xs - x)
  y3 <- ifelse( xs <= x, y1, y2 )
  yi <- pmax( yi, y3 )
}
lines( xs, yi, col=dbColours[3], lwd = 3 )
lines(xs, f(xs), lwd = 5, col=dbColours[1] )
points( xi, f(xi), pch=16, cex=2 )
par(op)
@
\]

If the derivative of the objective $f$ is known,
the bissection method can solve $f'(x)=0$.

\textbf{4.}
\emph{Line search} starts with a point $x$,
a direction $d$,
and solves the 1-dimensional problem
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\alpha f(x+\alpha d). \]
Approximate line search looks for a step size providing a ``sufficient decrease''
(Armijo condition)
\[ f(x + \alpha d ) \leq f(x) + \beta \alpha \nabla_{\!d} f(x) \qquad \beta = 10^{-4}. \]
The Wolfe conditions also include
\[ \nabla_{\!d} f(x + \alpha d ) \geq \sigma \nabla_{\! d} f(x). \]
\emph{Trust region} methods forbid excessively large step sizes
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x \hat f(x) \text{ such that } \n{ x - x_0 } \leq \delta ; \]
the radius $\delta$ can be adjusted by looking at the local model performance
\[ \eta = \dfrac{
  \text{actual improvement}
}{
  \text{predicted improvement}
} = \dfrac{
  f(x) - f(x_0)
}{
  \hat f(x) - f(x_0)
}.
\]

\textbf{5.} Gradient descent can perform poorly in narrow valleys.
\emph{Conjugate gradient} minimizes a quadratic function
$f(x) = \tfrac12 x' A x + b' x + c$
in $n$ mutually conjugate steps
$ \forall i \neq j \ d_i ' A d_j = 0 $.
\begin{align*}
  d_k &= - g_k + \beta_k d_{k-1} \\
  \beta_k &= \dfrac{ g_k' A d_{k-1} }{ d_{k-1} ' A d_{k-1} }
\end{align*}
We do not know $A$, but we can use approximations.
\[
\beta_k = \dfrac{ g_k ' g_k }{ g_{k-1} ' g_{k-1} }
\text{ or }
\beta_k = \dfrac{ g_k ' ( g_k - g_{k-1} ) }{ g_{k-1} ' g_{k-1} }
\]
Gradient descent can be improved with:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Momentum
\item Nesterov momentum (momentum at the next point)
\item An adaptive learning rate for each coordinate,
  using $\sum_k g_{ki}^2$ (AdaGrad, but the learning rate decreases too much)
  or an exponential moving average of the $g_{ki}^2$ (RMSProp,
  which has inconsistent units, or AdaDelta);
\item Adam, which combines momentum and adaptive learning rate.
\end{compactitem}
Hypergradient descent also optimizes the learning rate.

\textbf{6.}
\emph{Newton's method} approximates the objective function with a quadratic function,
$ x \leftarrow x - H^{-1} g$;
it can fail (oscillations, overshoot, or $f''<0$).
The \emph{secant method} uses an approximation of the second derivative with the first (in dimension 1).
\emph{Quasi-Newton methods} use an approximation of the Hessian (DFP, BFGS, L-BLFGS).
% Complicated formulas

\textbf{7.}
\emph{Coordinate descent} (aka cyclic coordinate search)
optimizes one coordinate at a time.
\emph{Powel's method} is similar, but progressively changes the directions,
replacing the last one with $x_{n+1}-x_1$
(occasionally reset the search direction lest they become colinear).

The \emph{Hooke-Jeeves} method evaluates $f(x)$ and $f(x\pm\alpha e_i)$.
\emph{Generalized pattern search} is similar but evaluates $f(x)$ and $f(x+\alpha d)$,
$d \in \mathscr D$, where $\mathscr D$ is a \emph{positive spanning set},
which can have as few as $n+1$ elements.

The \emph{Nelder-Mead method} uses a simplex to explore the space.

The \emph{divided rectangle} (\textsc{direct}) method uses Lipschitz bounds,
but without specifying the Lipschitz constant (it considers all possible values),
and dividing the search space recursively, into rectangles (otherwise, the Lipschitz lower bound
is a (rather complex) intersection of cones);
rectangles for which there is a Lipschitz constant for which their lower bound would be a minimum
are split into thirds along their largest dimension.

\textbf{8.}
To avoid plateaus and saddle points, try noisy descent
(adding Gaussian noise to the gradient),
\emph{mesh adaptive direct search} (random positive spanning directions)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.5,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
      every path/.style={thick}
  ]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (90:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-135:8mm);
\begin{scope}[shift={(2,0)}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-90:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (135:8mm);
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(4,0)}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-180:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (60:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-60:8mm);
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(6,0)}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (90:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-30:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-150:8mm);
\end{scope}
% Row 2
\begin{scope}[shift={(0,-2)}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-180:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-90:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (45:8mm);
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(2,-2)}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-180:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (90:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-45:8mm);
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(4,-2)}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-120:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (120:8mm);
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(6,-2)}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (-180:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (135:8mm);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (45:8mm);
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
or, even, simulated annealing.

The \emph{cross-entropy method}
does not follow a point
but a distribution;
at each step, it samples from that distribution,
and fits a distribution to the best samples.

\emph{Natural evolution strategies}
also solve
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\theta \E_{ x \sim p_\theta } \bigl[ f(x) \bigr] \]
but uses gradient descent and the log-derivative trick.

CMA-ES differs from CEM:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item After estimating $\mu$, $\sigma$, it samples from $N(\mu, \sigma^2\Sigma)$;
\item Instead of selecting the best samples, it weighs them
  (some weights are negative)
  \[ w_i = \log \dfrac{ m+1 }{ 2 } - \log i \qquad i \in \llbracket 1, m \rrbracket ; \]
\item The step size is updated (in a complicated way).
\end{compactitem}


\textbf{9.}
Initialize population methods with a uniform, Gaussian or Cauchy:
genetic algorithms (cross-over and mutations), \emph{differential evolution}
\[ x = x_i + w ( x_j - x_k ), \]
particle swarm optimization (momentum),
firefly algorithm (the attractive power of each particle depends on its fitness),
etc. -- there is an excessive proliferation of nature-inspired methods.

\emph{Hybrid methods} combine population search with local search.

\textbf{10.}
Transforming a constrained optimization problem can sometimes help remove constraints.
\[
<<ConstrainedOptimWheeler,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=3,fig.height=3,out.width='.5\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
wheeler <- function(x,y,a=1.5)
  - exp( - ( x * y - a ) ^ 2 - ( y - a ) ^ 2 )
xs <- seq(-.1,3, length=100)
ys <- seq(0,3.1, length=100)
z <- outer( xs, ys, FUN=wheeler )
op <- par( mar=c(0,0,0,0) )
contour(xs,ys,z, drawlabels=FALSE, axes=FALSE, lwd=2 )
xs <- seq(0,3, length=100)
lines( xs, sqrt(xs-.47), lwd = 5, col = dbColours[2] )
par(op)
@
\]

Lagrange multipliers deal with equality constraints
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x f(x) \text{ such that } h(x) = 0. \]
The first-order conditions
\begin{align*}
  h(x) &= 0 \\
  \nabla f(x) &= \lambda \nabla h(x)
\end{align*}
can be written with the Lagrangian
\begin{align*}
  \mathscr L ( x , \lambda ) &= f(x) - \lambda h(x) \\
  \nabla_{\!x} \mathscr L &= 0 \\
  \nabla_{\!\lambda} \mathscr L &= 0.
\end{align*}

With inequality constraints,
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(x) \\
  \text{Such that} & g(x) \leq 0 \\
  & h(x) = 0
\end{array} \]
things are more complicated.
The \emph{KKT conditions} are
\begin{align*}
  & g \leq 0 \\
  & h = 0 \\
  & \mu \geq 0 \\
  & \mu \odot g = 0 \\
  & \nabla f + \mu' \nabla g + \lambda' h(x) = 0.
\end{align*}
With the generalized Lagragian
\[ \mathscr L(x,\mu,\lambda) = f(x) + \mu' g(x) + \lambda' h(x), \]
the primal and dual problems are
\begin{align*}
  \text{primal} \qquad &
  \operatorname*{Minimize}_x \max_{\substack{ \mu \geq 0 \\ \lambda }} \mathscr L( x, \mu, \lambda ) \\
  \text{dual} \qquad &
  \operatorname*{Maximize}_{\substack{ \mu \geq 0 \\ \lambda }} \min_x \mathscr L( x, \mu, \lambda ).
\end{align*}
The max-min inequality is
\[ \max_a \min_b f(a,b) \leq \min_b \max(a) f(a,b). \]

To solve constrained optimization problems, try adding a quadratic penalty
\[ \sum_i \max \bigl( g_i(x) , 0 ) ^ 2 + \sum_j h_j(x) ^2. \]

For equality-constrained problems, the \emph{augmented Lagrange method}
uses both a quadratic and a linear penalty
\begin{align*}
  p(x) &= \tfrac12 \rho \n{ h(x) }^2 - \lambda ' h(x) \\
  \lambda_{k+1} &= \lambda_k - \rho h(x) .
\end{align*}
\emph{Interior point methods} use a \emph{barrier penalty}
(infinite for infeasible points), e.g.,
\[ p(x) = - \sum \dfrac1{ g_i(x) } \text{ or } p(x) = - \sum \log\bigl( - g_i(x) \bigr)
\mathbf1_{ g_i(x) \geq -1 } \]
(to find a feasible point, use a quadratic penalty).

\textbf{11.}
The \emph{simplex algorithm} moves from vertex to vertex of the feasible set.

To find a feasible solution of
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x c' x \text{ such that } Ax = b, \ x \geq 0 , \]
add extra variables $z$ and try to zero them out
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x, z \\
  \text{To minimize} & \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} ' \begin{pmatrix} x \\ z \end{pmatrix} \\
  \text{Such that} & \begin{pmatrix} A & Z \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ z \end{pmatrix} = b \\
  & x, z \geq 0
\end{array} \]
where $Z = \text{diag} \bigr( \text{sign}(b) \bigl).$

\textbf{12.}
To solve multiobjective optimization problems:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Constrain all but one of the objectives;
\item Rank the objectives, and maximize them one by one, adding constraints to fix the previous ones;
\end{compactitem}
or combine the objectives:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $ w' f(x) $ 
\item $ \n{ f(x) - y_{\text{goal}} }_p $ (goal programming)
\item $\sum w_i \bigl( f_i(x) - y_i^{\text{goal}} ) ^ p$
\item $\max w_i \bigl( f_i(x) - y_i^{\text{goal}} ) ^ p$
\item $\sum ( e^{p w_i} - 1 ) e^{ p f_i(x) }$.
\end{compactitem}

NSGA-II is a population method keeping track of the level of the individuals
(level-1 are non-dominated, level $k+1$ are non-dominated after removing levels 1 to $k$).
To preserve diversity, reinject good points into the population,
or penalize points with the density of their neighbourhood.

To choose $w$ in the weighted model $w' f(x)$,
ask experts if they prefer $a$ or $b$, and pick $w$ such that $w'a < w'b$;
repeat with other pairs; prefer the $w$ that best separates $w'a$ and $w'b$.
Choose $a$ and $b$ to cut the feasible space $\mathscr W$ into two equal parts (Q-eval).

Robust optimization does not choose one $w \in \mathscr W$:
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x \max_{ w \in \mathscr W} w' f(x) . \]
The \emph{minimax regret} approach solves
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x \max_{ w \in \mathscr W} \max_y w' f(x) - w' f(y). \]

\textbf{13.}
Grid search (full factorial design)
does not scale to high dimensions: try random sampling.
A \emph{uniform projection plan} is a sampling plan over a grid with uniform margins
(latin hypercube sampling).
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} {
  \draw[thick] (0,\i) -- (4,\i);
  \draw[thick] (\i,0) -- (\i,4);
}
\node[circle,fill] at (.5,2.5) {};
\node[circle,fill] at (1.5,3.5) {};
\node[circle,fill] at (2.5,1.5) {};
\node[circle,fill] at (3.5,0.5) {};
\begin{scope}[
          every path/.style={thick,densely dotted}
]
\draw (.5,2.5) -- (-.3,2.5);
\draw (.5,2.5) -- (.5,-.3);
\draw (1.5,3.5) -- (-.3,3.5);
\draw (1.5,3.5) -- (1.5,-.3);
\draw (2.5,1.5) -- (-.3,1.5);
\draw (2.5,1.5) -- (2.5,-.3);
\draw (3.5,0.5) -- (-.3,0.5);
\draw (3.5,0.5) -- (3.5,-.3);
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

To measure how space-filling a sampling plan is, look at
\begin{align*}
  \text{discrepancy}(X) &= \sup_{ H \text{ hyperrectangle}} \va{
    \dfrac{ \# X \inter H }{ \# X }
    - \text{vol}(H)
  } \\
  d(X) &= \min_{i\neq j} d(x_i, x_j) \\
  \Phi_q(X) &= \biggl(\sum_{i\neq j} d(x_i, x_j)^{-q} \biggr)^{\!\!1/q} \\
  \Phi(X) &= \max_{ q \in \{ 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100\}} \Phi_q(X)  
\end{align*}
Quasi-Monte Carlo (with quasi-random sequences, such as
additive recurrences, Halton or Sobol sequences)
has an error $O(1/m)$ instead of $O(1/\sqrt m)$.

\textbf{14.}
One can build a \emph{surrogate model} with basis functions
(sines, polynomials, radial basis functions)
and assess it with cross-validation
or \emph{0.632 bootstrap} (linear combination of leave-out-one bootstrap
and bootstrap, to avoid bias).

\textbf{15.} Gaussian processes (GP) are probabilistic surrogate models.

\textbf{16.}
To choose where next to evaluate the objective function,
look at the uncertainty $\hat \sigma(x)$,
a lower confidence bound $\hat \mu(x) - \alpha \hat\sigma(x)$,
the probability of improvement, or the expected improvement.

To ensure safe exploration, add a ``safety score'' to the GP model.

\textbf{17.}
To deal with noise, e.g.,
$f(x,z)=f(x)+z$ or $f(x,z)=f(x+z)$, with an uncertainty set,
use the minimax
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x \max_{ \n z \leq \epsilon } f(x,z) \]
or the information gap
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x, \epsilon \\
  \text{To maximize} & \epsilon \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall z \ \n z \leq \epsilon \ \Rightarrow\  (x,z) \text{ feasible} \\
  \text{and} & f(x,z) \leq y_{\text{max}}.
\end{array} \]

Probabilistic uncertainty uses distributions over the noize $z$:
look at $\E_z f(x,z)$, $\var_z f(x,z)$,
$\text{VaR}_z^{1-\alpha} f(x,z)$,
$\text{CVaR}_z^{1-\alpha} f(x,z)$.

\textbf{18.}
Given a distribution $ z \sim p$,
we can estimate the distribution of $f(z)$ (in particular $\E f(z)$ and $\var f(z)$) using:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A Taylor expansion of $f$;
\item Monte Carlo samples;
\item Polynomial chaos: if $f$ is a polynomial (product of orthogonal polynomials),
  we can easily compute $\E f(z)$ and $\var f(z)$;
\item Gaussian processes:
  $\E_z f(z) \approx \E_{ \hat f } \E_z \hat f(z) = \E_z \E_{\hat f} \hat f(z) = \E_z \mu(z)$
  (it is a little more complicated for $\var f(z)$).
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{19.}
For an integer program
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_x c' x \text{ such that } Ax=b,\ x\geq0 \]
with a \emph{totally unimodular} matrix $A$ (the determinant of all submatrices is $0$, $+1$ or $-1$),
the simplex algorithm returns an integer solution.

The \emph{cutting plane} method solves the problem without the integrality constraint,
and adds constraints to remove the non-integral parts of the solution.
\emph{Branch and bound} recursively splits the problem by adding constraints:
$x_i \leq \lfloor x_i^* \rfloor$
and $x_i \geq \lceil x_i^* \rceil$.

% Ant colony optimization can solve the traveling salesman problem.

% 20. Expression optimization
\textbf{20.}
To explore a search space described by a grammar, try genetic programming
(cross-over swaps two random subtrees).
A probabilistic grammar, with probabilities progressively updated
to focus on derivation rules improving the objective, may help.

\textbf{21.}
Multi-disciplinary optimization studies problems of the form
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(x) \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ F_i(x) = x
\end{array} \]
where $F_i$ only modifies coordinate $i$ of $x$.
Try (cf projected gradient)
\begin{align*}
  x &\leftarrow \textsc{mda}(x) \\
  x &\leftarrow \text{improve}(x)
\end{align*}
where \textsc{mda} returns a compatible assignment.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms for decision making}\\
  M.J. Kochenderfer et al. (2022)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.}
Decision making algorithms can rely on:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Explicit programming (hard-coded policies);
\item Supervised learning (behavioural cloning);
\item Optimization (search in policy space);
\item Planning (reduction of the problem to dynamic programming);
\item Reinforcement learning (if the model of the environment is not known).
\end{compactitem}

% Part 1: probabilistic reasoming (utility, bandits, PGMs)

\textbf{2.}
Probability distributions on a large number of variables can be represented
by Bayesian networks
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.2,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  every text node part/.style={align=center}
  ]
  \node[text width=2cm] (B) at (0,2) {Battery failure};
  \node[text width=2cm] (S) at (2,2) {Solar panel failure};
  \node[text width=3cm] (E) at (1,1) {No electricity};
  \node[text width=2cm] (T) at (0,0) {Trajectory deviation};
  \node[text width=2cm] (C) at (2,0) {Communication loss};
  \draw[->] (B) -- (E);
  \draw[->] (S) -- (E);
  \draw[->] (E) -- (T);
  \draw[->] (E) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\[
\text{explaining away: }
B \not \indep S \,|\,E
\]

\textbf{3.}
Inference on Bayesian networks can use:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sum-product variable elimination (marginalizing out variables as early as possible);
\item Junction tree algorithm (belief propagation on a tree of subsets of variables);
\item Loopy belief propagation.
\end{compactitem}
Exact inference is NP-hard:
a 3-SAT problem can be converted to a Bayesian network.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=1,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\begin{scope}[  
  every text node part/.style={align=center, text width=4.3mm},
  every node/.style={circle,draw},
]  
\node (X1) at (0,2) {$X_1$};  
\node (X2) at (2,2) {$X_2$};  
\node (X3) at (4,2) {$X_3$};  
\node (X4) at (6,2) {$X_4$};
\node (C1) at (1,1) {$C_1$};
\node (C2) at (3,1) {$C_2$};
\node (C3) at (5,1) {$C_3$};
\node (D1) at (1,0) {};
\node (D2) at (3,0) {};
\node[very thick] (D3) at (5,0) {$Y$};
\end{scope}
\draw[->] (X1) -- (C1);
\draw[->] (X2) -- (C1);
\draw[->] (X3) -- (C1);
\draw[->] (X1) -- (C2);
\draw[->] (X2) -- (C2);
\draw[->] (X3) -- (C2);
\draw[->] (X2) -- (C3);
\draw[->] (X3) -- (C3);
\draw[->] (X4) -- (C3);
\draw[->] (C1) -- (D1);
\draw[->] (C2) -- (D2);
\draw[->] (C3) -- (D3);
\draw[->] (D1) -- (D2);
\draw[->] (D2) -- (D3);
\node[anchor=west] at (7,2) {$X_i \sim \text{Unif}(\text{T},\text{F})$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7,1) {clauses};
\node[anchor=west,text width = 3cm, align=left] at (7,.2) {cummulated\\[-1mm] clauses};
\node[anchor=east] at (-.5,1) {$\begin{array}{rrr}
    1 & 2 & 3 \\
    -1 & -2 & 3 \\
    2 & -3 & 4 
  \end{array}$};
\node at (1,-.5) {\small $C_1$};
\node at (3,-.5) {\small $C_1 \!\wedge\! C_2$};
\node at (5,-.5) {\small $C_1$\rlap{${}\!\wedge\! C_2 \!\wedge\! C_3$}};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Approximate inference can also be done via:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Direct sampling (use a topological order on the nodes);
\item Likelihood-weighted sampling;
\item Gibbs sampling.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{4.} The parameters of a Bayesian network can be learned via
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Maximum likelihood;
\item Bayesian methods (with beta or Dirichlet priors);
\item Expectation maximization (EM), if some of the data is missing.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{5.}
To learn the structure of a Bayesian network,
compute the score (penalized log-likelihood) of candidates, using local search
or \emph{K2 search} (iterate over the nodes in a prespecified order,
adding parents if this increases the score, with a limit on the number of parents).
It may be more efficient to search the space of essential graphs (CPDAG) instead of all DAGs.

\textbf{6.}
Under some reasonable assumptions on an agent's preferences,
rational decisions are those maximizing the expected utility, for some utility function.

A \emph{decision network} (influence diagram)
has
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Chance, decision and utility nodes;
\item Conditional, informational and functional nodes (ending in chance, decision, and utility nodes).
\end{compactitem}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
  \node[inner sep=2pt,draw] (T) at (1,2) {$T$};
  \node[inner sep=1pt,circle,draw] (D) at (1,1) {$D$};
  \node[inner sep=1pt,diamond,draw] (U) at (2,1) {$U$};
  \node[circle,draw] (O1) at (0,0) {$O_1$};
  \node[circle,draw] (O2) at (1,0) {$O_2$};
  \node[circle,draw] (O3) at (2,0) {$O_3$};
  \draw[->] (T) -- (U);
  \draw[->] (D) -- (U);
  \draw[->] (D) -- (O1);
  \draw[->] (D) -- (O2);
  \draw[->] (D) -- (O3);
  \draw[->,thick,densely dotted] (O1) to[out=150,in=120,looseness=1.2] (T);
  \node[anchor=west] at (1.3,2) {\small treatment};
  \node[anchor=east] at (.75,1) {\small disease?};
  \node at (1,-.5) {\small diagnostic tests};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

The \emph{value of information} is
\[ \text{VOI}( O_2 | O_1 ) = \E_{ O_2 } \bigl[ \text{EU}( O_1, O_2 ) \bigr] - \text{EU}( O_1 ) \]
where $\text{EU}(O)$ is the expected utility of an optimal decision given observation $O$.

% Part II: Sequential problems (MDP, planning)

\textbf{7.}
Many problems require a sequence of decisions;
with a Markov assumption, they can be modeled as an MDP.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.2,yscale=1,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\node[inner sep = 1pt,draw] (A1) at (0,2) {$A_1$};
\node[inner sep = 1pt,draw] (A2) at (1,2) {$A_2$};
\node[inner sep = 1pt,draw] (A3) at (2,2) {$A_3$};
\node[diamond,draw] (R1) at (0,1) {$R_1$};
\node[diamond,draw] (R2) at (1,1) {$R_2$};
\node[diamond,draw] (R3) at (2,1) {$R_3$};
\node[circle,draw] (S1) at (0,0) {$S_1$};
\node[circle,draw] (S2) at (1,0) {$S_2$};
\node[circle,draw] (S3) at (2,0) {$S_3$};
\draw[->] (A1) -- (R1);
\draw[->] (A2) -- (R2);
\draw[->] (A3) -- (R3);
\draw[->] (S1) -- (R1);
\draw[->] (S2) -- (R2);
\draw[->] (S3) -- (R3);
\draw[->] (A1) -- (S2);
\draw[->] (A2) -- (S3);
\draw[->] (S1) -- (S2);
\draw[->] (S2) -- (S3);
\node at (1,-.6) {MDP};
\begin{scope}[shift={(4,0)}]
\node[inner sep = 1pt,draw] (A1) at (0,2) {$A_t$};
\node[diamond,draw] (R1) at (0,1) {$R_t$};
\node[circle,draw] (S1) at (0,0) {$S_t$};
\node[ellipse,draw] (S2) at (1,0) {$S_{t+1}$};
\draw[->] (A1) -- (R1);
\draw[->] (S1) -- (R1);
\draw[->] (A1) -- (S2);
\draw[->] (S1) -- (S2);
\node at (.5,-.6) {Stationary MDP};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\emph{Policy evaluation} computes the value (expected return) of a state
(if we follow the policy $\pi$) by iterating
\[ U(s) \leftarrow R + \gamma \E_{ s' } \bigl[ U(s) \bigr]. \]

\emph{Policy iteration} computes an optimal policy by iterating between policy ecaluation
and policy improvement (replacing the current policy with the greedy policy wrt the value function).

\emph{Value iteration} computes an optimal policy by iterating Bellman's equation
\[ U(s) \leftarrow \max_a \bigl( R(s,a) + \gamma \E_{s'} [ U(s') ] \bigr); \]
the updates can be done asynchronously.

The optimal policy is also solution of the linear program
\[\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & U \\
\text{To minimize} & \sum_s U(s) \\
\text{Such that} & \forall s \ U(s) \geq \max_a \bigl( R(s,a) + \gamma \E_{s'} [ U(s') ] \bigr)
\end{array}\]
(the minimization turns the inequalities into equalities).

% TODO: LQR p144-147

\textbf{8.}
If the state space is large (or continuous), approximate the value function:
$k$-nearest neighbour,
kernel smoothing,
linear interpolation,
simplex interpolation (only uses $d+1$ neighbours in dimension $d$, instead of $2^d$
for linear interpolation),
linear regression,
neural networks.
Many of those can be written
$U_\theta(s) = \theta ' \beta(s)$
for some basis functions
$(\beta_i)_{1 \leq i \leq m}$.

\textbf{9.}
The reachable state space is often smaller than the full state space:
\emph{receding horizon planning} solves a subset of the full MDP by only looking $d$ steps ahead.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Expand the whole search tree (forward search);
\item It may be possible to prune the search tree using a lower bound on the state value function
  $\ubar U(s)$
  and an upper bound on the state action value function $\bar Q(s,a)$;
\item Sample the next states using a rollout policy and average;
\item Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS) not only samples
  but also updates estimates of the state action value function $Q(s,a)$,
  often using the UCB1 exploration heuristic
  \[ \text{UCB}_1 = Q(s,a) + c \sqrt{ \dfrac{ \log N(s) }{ N(s,a) } } ; \]
  limit (but progressively increase) the number of actions considered
  in a state, and the number of states reachable after an action,
  to $\theta_1 N(s) ^{\theta_2}$ and $\theta_3 N(s,a) ^{\theta_4}$;
\item Heuristic search samples from a greedy policy for value function $U$,
  initialized to an upper bound, and progressively refined.
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Openloop} planning (committing to the next $d$ actions,
\ie, not revising the plan when new observations arrive),
reduces to an optimization problem; there are robust variants.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\node[anchor=east] (O) at (0,0) {Observe};
\node[anchor=west] (A) at (1,0) {Act};
\draw[->] (O.north east) to[out=30,in=150] (A.north west);  
\draw[->] (A.south west) to[out=-150,in=-30] (O.south east);
\node at (.5,-.6) {closed loop};
\begin{scope}[shift={(4,0)}]
\node[anchor=east] (O) at (0,0) {Observe};
\node[anchor=west] (A) at (1,0) {Act};
\draw[->] (O) -- (A);
\draw[->] (A.south east) to[out=-30,in=30,looseness=4] (A.north east);
\node at (.5,-.6) {open loop};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\textbf{10.}
\emph{Policy search} directly searches the space of policies to maximize the expected discounted return
$U(\theta)$, often approximated using $m$ strategy rollouts.

The \emph{Hooke-Jeeves method} is a local search taking steps of size $\pm\alpha$ in each coordinate direction.

\emph{Genetic search} keeps track of a population of policies, progressively perturbed and pruned.

The \emph{cross-entropy method} (CEM) updates a distribution rather than a set of points
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{\psi} \E_{ \theta \sim p_\psi} U(\theta)  \]
where $U(\theta)$ is the expected discounted return under policy $\pi_\theta$;
the expectation is approximated with samples from $p_\psi$,
and the distribution is updated by fitting it to the best samples.

\emph{Evolution strategies} optimize the same objective function,
by using a gradient step,
computed with the \emph{log-derivative trick}
\[ \nabla_{\!\psi} \E_ { \theta \sim p_\psi } U(\theta) = \cdots =
\E_{ p_\psi} \bigl[ U(\theta) \nabla_{\!\psi} \log p_\psi(\theta) \bigr]. \]

\emph{Rank shaping} replace the utilities $U(\theta)$ (which are noisy)
with their rank, or some transformation thereof.

\textbf{11.}
To estimate the gradient $\nabla U(\theta)$, one could use:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Finite differences;
\item Regression, after estimating $U(\theta + \epsilon)$ for random offsets $\epsilon$
  (instead of fixed-size, axis-aligned offsets);
\item The log-derivative trick; the variance can be reduced by using the reward-to-go,
  and by subtracting a baseline.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{12.}
\emph{Gradient ascent} takes a step in the gradient direction;
the gradient can be \emph{clipped} (coordinate-wise)
or rescaled (to cap its norm).
Rescaling the gradient to set its norm (\emph{restricted gradient})
is equivalent to maximizing a linear approximation of the objective inside a ball;
the \emph{natural gradient} defines that ball with the Fisher information matrix;
the \emph{trust region method} (TRPO)
uses a line search in the gradient direction within the Fisher information ball.

% TODO: PPO + clamping

\textbf{13.}
\emph{Actor-critic} methods jointly optimize the policy (actor)
and refine an approximation of the value function (critic):
by providing a better baseline, the critic helps lower the variance of the policy gradient.

\textbf{14.}
To evaluate the final policy:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use importance sampling, to focus on rare events;
\item Use robust dynamic programming, or adversarial analysis;
\item Compute the Pareto frontier, if there are several competing performance measures.
\end{compactitem}

% Part III Model uncertainly

\textbf{15.} To balance exploration and exploitation,
for binary \emph{bandits} (single-state MDP, with binary rewards)
one can take random actions once in a while ($\epsilon$-greedy).

\emph{Optimism under uncertainty} keepd track of an estimate of the (distribution of the) value of each action,
and chooses the action (``arm'') with the highest $\alpha$-quantile,
or some other upper confidence bound, e.g.,
\[ \text{UCB}_1 (a) = \rho_a + c \sqrt{ \dfrac{ \log N }{ N(a) } }. \]
Dynamic programming on belief states (number of tries, number of wins, for each arm -- there are exponentially many beliefs)
can be solved efficiently with the \emph{Gittins allocation index} [no details].

\textbf{16.}
The MLE of the MDP can be obtained from the counts,
and solved at each step;
instead of re-solving it each time, a few Bellman updates may be sufficient, e.g., focusing on states
known to lead to the current state.
One can bias the estimated MDP to encourage exploration
(increasing the reward of insufficiently-visited states).

\emph{Bayes-adaptive} MDPs extend the state space $S$ to $S \times B$ where $B$ is the set of possible beliefs on the model parameters.

\emph{Posterior sampling} (Thompson sampling) samples from the posterior model parameters,
instead of using the MAP.

\textbf{17.}
Model-free methods model the state action value function $Q$ directly,
without estimating the MDP.
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Bellman} & Q(s,a) &= \E_{r,s'} \bigl[ r + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s', a') \bigr] \\
  &\text{Update} & Q(s,a) &\leftarrow Q(s,a) + \alpha \bigl[ \text{target} - Q(s,a) \bigr]
\end{align*}
\vspace{-8mm}
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Q-learning} & \text{target} &= r + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s',a') && \text{off-policy} \\
  &\text{SARSA} & \text{target} &= r + Q(s' , a' ) && \text{on-policy}
\end{align*}
With sparse rewards, learning can be slow.

\emph{Eligibility traces} apply the update
\begin{align*}
  Q(s,a) &\leftarrow Q(s,a) + \alpha \delta \\
  \delta &= \text{target} - Q(s,a) 
\end{align*}
to all state-action pairs, with an exponentially decaying coefficient $\lambda$,
increased each time the pair is visited
\[ Q(s,a) \leftarrow Q(s,a) + \lambda (s,a) \cdot \alpha \cdot \delta \]
(it may not work well with off-policy updates, such as $Q$-learning).

\emph{Experience replay} can help avoid \emph{catastrophic forgetting}.

\textbf{18.}
\emph{Behavioural cloning} uses supervised learning to reproduce expert decisions,
but suffers from cascading errors: small errors compound
and eventually lead the agent to regions of the state space never visited by the expert.

\emph{Inverse reinforcement learning} learns a reward function (not a policy)
from trajectories, by matching policy features.
The problem is underspecified: also maximize the entropy of the distribution over trajectories.

\emph{Generative adversarial imitation learning} (GAIL) uses a GAN to generate expert-like trajectories.

% Part IV State uncertainty (POMDP)

\textbf{19.}
An MDP is a conditional distribution $r,s'|s,a$.
A POMDP is a conditional distribution $r,s',o|s,a$.
% POMDP
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.2,yscale=1,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\node[inner sep = 1pt,draw] (A1) at (0,2) {$A_t$};
\node[diamond,draw] (R1) at (0,1) {$R_t$};
\node[circle,draw,densely dotted,thick] (S1) at (0,0) {$S_t$};
\node[ellipse,draw,densely dotted,thick] (S2) at (1,0) {$S_{t+1}$};
\node[circle,draw] (O1) at (0,-1) {$O_t$};
\draw[->] (A1) -- (R1);
\draw[->] (S1) -- (R1);
\draw[->] (A1) -- (S2);
\draw[->] (S1) -- (S2);
\draw[->] (S1) -- (O1);
\node[anchor=west] at (2,2) {action};
\node[anchor=west] at (2,1) {reward};
\node[anchor=west] at (2,0) {state (hidden)};
\node[anchor=west] at (2,-1) {obeservation};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

The \emph{Kalman filter} progressively updates beliefs about the state,
assuming the model is linear and Gaussian.

The \emph{extended Kalman filter} (EKF) linearizes non-linear models (with Gaussian noise).

The \emph{unscented Kalman filter} (UKF) is derivative-free,
and uses deterministic sampling (``delta points'')
to approximate non-linear functions (``unscented transform'').
\[ \mu, \Sigma \mapsto
s_i^\pm = \mu \pm \sqrt{ \Sigma_i } \mapsto
f(s_i^\pm) \mapsto \mu' , \Sigma' \]

\emph{Particle filters} (sequential Monte Carlo, SCM)
can deal with nonlinear, non-Gaussian models, but can fail because of
\emph{particle deprivation}.
\emph{Particle injection} tried to remedy this by occsionally injecting particles from a broad distribution
(e.g., uniform), when the mean particle weight becomes too high.

\textbf{20.} A POMDP can be seen as an MDP with beliefs as states;
for planning, only use the $k$-step-ahead sub-MDP.
% I do not understand what they are doing with alpha vectors.

\textbf{21.}
\emph{QMDP} updates a set of \emph{alpha vectors}, one for each action.
\[ Q(b,a) = \sum_s Q(s,a) b(s) = \alpha ' _a b \]
\[ \alpha_a(s) = R(s,a) + \gamma \sum_{ s' } T( s' | s, a) \max_{a'} \alpha_{ a' } (s') \]

The \emph{fast informed bound} is similar,
but accounts for the observation model.
For lower bounds, try best action worst state, or single action forever.

\emph{Point-based value iteration} uses a set of alpha vectors,
associated to different belief points, $U(b) = \max_{ \alpha \in \Gamma } \alpha ' b $.

\textbf{22.}
MCTS can be extended to POMDPs.
% Not clear

\textbf{23.}
A \emph{controller} (finite state controller, FSA) is a policy with an internal state.

% Part V Multi-agent systems

\textbf{24.} In a \emph{simple game} (single-state, multi-agent MDP),
a \emph{dominant strategy} is a best response against all possible agent policies;
a \emph{dominant strategy equilibrium} need not exist.

Games with a finite state space have a Nash equilibrium: each agent follows a best-response strategy.
Computing a Nash equilibrium is PPAD-complete.

A \emph{correlated equilibrium} is a joint policy (the agent's actions need not be independent)
in which no agent can change its action to increase its expected utility.

\emph{Hierarchical softmax} models human agents:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Level 0 agents play at random;
\item Level $k+1$ agents assume the other players are level~$k$,
  and select actions with a softmax with precision~$\lambda$.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{25.}
A \emph{Markov game} (MG) is a multi-agent MDP.
Policy evaluation, best response, softmax response
\[ \pi_i(a_i | s ) \propto \exp \lambda Q(s,a_i), \]
Nash equilibrium can be generalized to MGs.
To improve the strategy, try gradient ascent with fictitious play, or Nash Q-learning.

\textbf{26.}
A partially-observable Markov game (POMG) is a multi-agent POMDP.

\textbf{27.}
In a \emph{decentralized POMDP}, agents share the same reward.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian optimization}\\
  R. Garnett (2021)
\end{book}

% TODO: Notations?

\textbf{1.}
\emph{Sequential optimization} iterates
\begin{align*}
  x &\leftarrow \text{policy}( \mathscr D ) \\
  y &\leftarrow \text{observe}( x ) \\
  \mathscr D &\leftarrow \mathscr D \union \bigl\{ (x,y) \bigr\}.
\end{align*}
The new observation, $x$, is often selected by maximizing some function (\emph{acquisition} function).

\textbf{2.}
\emph{Gaussian processes} are distributions on functions whose restrictions on finite sets
are Gaussian -- they are ``very high-dimensional'' Gaussian distributions:
$f \sim \text{GP}(\mu, k)$ means
(writing $\phi$ instead of $f(\mathbf x)$, to simplify the notations)
\begin{align*}
  \mathbf{\phi} & \sim N( \mathbf \mu , \Sigma ) \\
  \mathbf\mu &= \E[ \mathbf \phi ] = \mu(\mathbf x ) \\
  \Sigma &= \cov[ \mathbf \phi ] = k(\mathbf x , \mathbf x ).
\end{align*}
Values are often observed with noise:
$\mathbf y \sim N( \mathbf \mu , \Sigma + N )$.

The posterior is a conditional Gaussian:
\begin{align*}
  \mu_{\mathscr D} &= \mu(a) + k(x,\mathbf x ) ( \Sigma + N )^{-1} ( \mathbf y - \mathbf \mu ) \\
  k_{\mathscr D} (x,x') &= k(x,x') -kx(x,\mathbf x) ( \Sigma + N )^{-1} k( \mathbf x , x' ).
\end{align*}

If $L$ is linear and $X$ Gaussian. then $(X,LX)$ is Gaussian.
Since $\nabla$ or $h=\text{vech}\nabla \nabla^\top$ are linear,
if $f$ is a GP, then so if $(f,\nabla f, h f)$.
\[ (f, \nabla f, hf) \sim \text{GP} \left(
\begin{pmatrix}
  \mu \\ \nabla \mu \\ h \mu 
\end{pmatrix} ,
\begin{pmatrix}
  k & k \nabla^\top & k h^\top \\
  \nabla k & \nabla k \nabla^\top & \nabla k h ^\top \\
  h k & h k \nabla^\top & h k h^\top
\end{pmatrix}
\right) \]

If $f \sim \text{GP}(\mu \equiv 0, k)$ is sampled from a GP,
defined on a compact space $X$, with
$\forall x \neq x' \ \var[\phi - \phi'] = k(x,x') - 2 k(x,x') + x(x',x') \neq 0,$
then $f$ a.s.\ has a unique maximum on $X$.

Adding constraints to a GP, e.g., ``$f$ has a local maximum at $x_0$'',
or ``$f'(x_0)=0$, $f''(x_0) \leq 0$'',
can make the process non-Gaussian (with a GP, the distribution of $f''(x_0)$ is unbounded).
In this case, use factor graphs.

\textbf{3.}
The prior on the mean only affects the extrapolation regime.
\[
<<GauProExtrapolationMean,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=2,out.width='.8\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
library(GauPro)
x <- c(.3,.5,.8)
y <- c(.6,.5,.7)
x <- c(.3,.7)
y <- c(.6,.5)
s <- .5
#gp <- GauPro(x, y, parallel=FALSE)
k <- Matern52$new(beta=0)
trend.0 <- trend_0$new(m=0, m_est=FALSE)
gp <- GauPro_kernel_model$new(
  matrix(x, ncol=1),
  y,
  kernel=k,
  trend=trend.0, parallel=FALSE
)

k <- Matern52$new(beta=0)
trend.0 <- trend_0$new(m=0, m_est=FALSE)
gp1 <- GauPro_kernel_model$new(
  matrix(x, ncol=1),
  y-1,
  kernel=k,
  trend=trend.0, parallel=FALSE
)

op <- par( mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
plot(x, y, xlim = c(-2,3), ylim = c(-.1,1.1), axes=FALSE)

xs <- seq(-2.5,3.5,length=100)
polygon(
  c(xs, rev(xs)),
  c(gp$predict(xs)+s*gp$predict(xs, se=T)$se,
    rev(gp$predict(xs)-s*gp$predict(xs, se=T)$se)),
  border = NA,
  col=paste0(dbColours[1],"44")
  #col=dbColours[1]
)
polygon(
  c(xs, rev(xs)),
  1+c(gp1$predict(xs)+s*gp1$predict(xs, se=T)$se,
    rev(gp1$predict(xs)-s*gp1$predict(xs, se=T)$se)),
  border = NA,
  col=paste0(dbColours[3],"44")
  #col=dbColours[1]
)
lines(xs, gp$predict(xs), col=dbColours[1], lwd = 3)
lines(xs, 1+gp1$predict(xs), col=dbColours[3], lwd = 3)

abline( h=0, col = dbColours[1], lwd = 2, lty = 2 )
abline( h=1, col = dbColours[3], lwd = 2, lty = 2 )
points(x,y, pch = 16)
par(op)
@
\]

For the prior on the mean, use a constant or a linear combination of basis functions,
$\mu(x) = \mathbf \beta ' \mathbf \psi (x) $.
\emph{Stationary} covariance functions are of the form
$k(x,x') = \kappa(x-x')$;
\emph{isotropic} ones of the form
$k(x,x') = \kappa( \n{x'-x} )$.

Stationary covariance functions are characterized by their Fourier  transform $\nu$
(Bochner's theorem)
\[ \kappa(\mathbf x) =  \int \exp( 2\pi i \mathbf x ' \mathbf \xi ) d\nu(\mathbf\xi) \]
where $\nu$ is a finite, positive, symmetric, Borel measure on $\RR^n$ (spectral measure).

Common covariance functions include ($d=\n{x'-x}$):
\begin{align*}
  k_{\text{M \!\!\nicefrac12}} &= \exp( -d ) \\
  k_{\text{M \!\!\nicefrac32}} &= (1+\sqrt3 d) \exp( - \sqrt3 d ) \\
  k_{\text{M \!\!\nicefrac52}} &= (1 + \sqrt5 d + \tfrac53 d^2 ) \exp( - \sqrt5 d ) \\  
  k_{\text{SE}} &= \exp( -\tfrac12 d^2 ) \\
\end{align*}
Their sample paths are a.s.
$\mathscr C^0$, 
$\mathscr C^1$, 
$\mathscr C^2$, 
$\mathscr C^\infty$.

The \emph{spectral mixture} covariance is defined by
\begin{align*} 
  k(\xi) &= \sum_i w_i N( \xi ; \mu_i, \Sigma_i ) \\
  \kappa(\xi) &= \tfrac12 \bigl[ k(\xi) + k(-\xi) \bigr] \\
  K_{\text{SM}} (x,x') &= \sum w_i
  \cdot \exp \bigl[ - 2 \pi^2 (x-x')^\top \Sigma_i (x-x') \bigr] \\
  &\hphantom{{}= \sum w_i}
  \cdot \cos \bigl[ 2\pi (x-x')^\top \mu_i \bigr].
\end{align*}

The \emph{linear} covariance function corresponds to
\begin{align*}
  f(x) &= \beta_0 + \mathbf\beta^\top \mathbf x \\
  \beta &\sim N(a,B) \\
  k_{\text{LIN}} (x,x') &= b^2 + x^\top B x 
\end{align*}
We can rescale the input and the output of the covariance function:
$ \lambda^2 k(d/\ell) $.

\emph{Automatic relevance determination} (ARD) estimates those length and scale parameters from data.

\emph{Beta warping} transforms the data with
\[ \left\{
\begin{array}{r@{\,\,}c@{\,\,}l}
  \RR & \longrightarrow & [0,1] \\
  x & \longmapsto & \text{Beta}(x ; a,b )
\end{array}
\right. \]
to focus around zero.
\[
<<GPBetaWarping,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width='.8\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
library(mvtnorm)
set.seed(1)
xs <- seq(0,1,length=500)
C <- exp(-abs(15*outer(xs, xs, `-`))^2)
ys <- rmvnorm(4,sigma=C)
op <- par( mar=c(0,0,0,0) )
matplot(qbeta(xs,3,3), t(ys), col=dbColours, axes=FALSE)
par(op)
@
\]

Covariance functions can be combined:
\begin{align*}
  \cov[f+g] &= k_f + k_g \\
  \cov[fg] &= k_f k_g.
\end{align*}

\textbf{4.}
To select the GP family, the input and output scales, and the observation noise,
use the MAP estimator,
or model averaging (hyperparameter marginalization, perhaps via MCMC),
or search through a space of covariance structures (e.g., described with a grammar),
perhaps with Bayesian optimization.

\textbf{5.}
(One-step) \emph{Bayesian decision theory} suggests to select the action maximizing the expected utility
\begin{align*}
  & \mathscr D : \text{observed action} \\
  & \psi : \text{unobserved data} \\
  & a \in \mathscr A : \text{actions} \\
  & u(a,\psi,\mathscr D) : \text{utility} \\
  & \operatorname*{Maximize}_a \E\bigl[ u(a,\psi,\mathscr D) \bigm| a , \mathscr D \bigr]
\end{align*}
For $T=1$, we maximize
\[ \alpha_1(x,\mathscr D) = \E[ u(\mathscr D_1) | x ] - u(\mathscr D). \]
For $T=2$, we would like to maximize
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_2(x,\mathscr D)
  &= \E[ u(\mathscr D_2) - u(\mathscr D) | x_1 ] \\
  &= \E[ u(\mathscr D_2) - u(\mathscr D_1) + u(\mathscr D_1) - u(\mathscr D) | x_1 ] \\
  &= \E[ \alpha_1(x_2|\mathscr D_1) | x_1=x ] + \alpha_1(x,\mathscr D)
\end{align*}
but we know that, at the next step, we will choose $x_2$ to maximize $\alpha$,
so
\[\alpha_2(x,\mathscr D) = \E[ \alpha_1^*(\mathscr D_1) | x_1=x] + \alpha_1(x,\mathscr D). \]
There is an optimization, $\alpha_1^*$, parametrized by $x_1$, inside an expectation -- and,
if we increase $T$, there will be even more nesting.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0,
  ]
  \begin{scope}[inner sep=2pt]
  \node[draw] (x1) at (0,1) {$x_1$};
  \node[draw] (x2) at (2,1) {$x_1$};
  \node[draw] (xT) at (5,1) {$x_T$};
  \end{scope}
  \node[circle,draw] (y1) at (1,1) {$y_1$};
  \node[circle,draw] (y2) at (3,1) {$y_2$};
  \node[circle,draw] (yT) at (6,1) {$y_T$};
  \node[outer sep=1pt] (u) at (7,1) {$u(\mathscr D_T)$};
  \node (d) at (4,1) {$\cdots$};
  \node[anchor=base] at (0,0) {\footnotesize $\argmax\limits_{x_1}$};
  \node[anchor=base] at (1,0) {\footnotesize $\E\limits_{y_1}$};
  \node[anchor=base] at (2,0) {\footnotesize $\argmax\limits_{s_2}$};
  \node[anchor=base] at (3,0) {\footnotesize $\E\limits_{y_2}$};
  \node[anchor=base] at (6,0) {\footnotesize $\E\limits_{y_T}$};
  \draw (x1) -- (y1);
  \draw (y1) -- (x2);
  \draw (x2) -- (y2);
  \draw (y2) -- (d);
  \draw (d) -- (xT);
  \draw (xT) -- (yT);
  \draw (yT) -- (u);
  \draw (x1) -- ($(x1)+(60:7mm)$);
  \draw (x1) -- ($(x1)+(-60:7mm)$);
  \draw (y1) -- ($(y1)+(60:7mm)$);
  \draw (y1) -- ($(y1)+(-60:7mm)$);
  \draw (x2) -- ($(x2)+(60:7mm)$);
  \draw (x2) -- ($(x2)+(-60:7mm)$);
  \draw (y2) -- ($(y2)+(60:7mm)$);
  \draw (y2) -- ($(y2)+(-60:7mm)$);
  \draw (xT) -- ($(xT)+(60:7mm)$);
  \draw (xT) -- ($(xT)+(-60:7mm)$);
  \draw (yT) -- ($(yT)+(60:7mm)$);
  \draw (yT) -- ($(yT)+(-60:7mm)$);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\begin{align*}
  \alpha_T &= \alpha_1 + \E[ \alpha^*_{T-1} ] \\
  &= \alpha_1 + \E[ \max \alpha_{T-1} ] \\
  &= \alpha_1 + \E[ \max \E[ \max \alpha_{T-2} ] ] \\
  &\cdots
\end{align*}

Instead, we can use an optimal but truncated path ($T=1$ or 2),
or a suboptimal but complete path (using a heuristic to select $x_t$).

\textbf{6.}
For Bayesian optimization, the final action is a bet on the location of the extremum. The action space can be:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Only the points examined;
\item Points close to the points examined;
\item All the points in the domain $\mathscr X$ of $f$.
\end{compactitem}

For linear (risk-neutral) utility:
\begin{align*}
  u(\mathscr D) &= \max_{ (x,y) \in \mathscr D } \mu_{\mathscr D}(x) &&\text{single reward} \\
  u(\mathscr D) &= \max_{ (x,y) \in \mathscr X } \mu_{\mathscr D}(x) &&\text{global reward}
\end{align*}
(do not use $\smash{\max\limits_{(x,y) \in \mathscr D} y}$:
it includes observation noise).

For risk-averse or risk-seeking behaviour,
replace $\mu$ with $\mu + \beta\sigma$, $\beta<0$ or $\beta>0$.

Optimal control is slightly different: we want each $y_i$ to be large, \ie,
we are maximizing $\sum y_i$.

The information gain, $\E[\Delta H]$, or $\E[\Delta \text{KL} ]$,
is another utility function.

\textbf{7.}
The \emph{expected gain utility}
\[ \alpha(x,\mathscr D) = \E[ u(\mathscr D') | x,\mathscr D] - u(\mathscr D) \]
can be used as an activation function.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
  Utility & Activation \\
  \midrule
  single reward & expected improvement \\
  global reward & knowledge gradient \\
  unit for improving & probability of improvement \\[-1mm]
  single reward \\
  information gain & mutual information \\
  cummulated reward & posterior mean
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Bayesian optimization is based on either Bayesian decision theory,
or multi-arm bandits (infinite-arm bandits),
for instance, using an upper bound (e.g., the 99.9\% quantile)
of $y=f(x)$;
it is equivalent to maximizing the probability of improvement beyond some threshold.

\emph{Thompson sampling} maximizes a random acquisition function, sampled from the posterior.

Two-step lookahead works better.

\textbf{8.}
For Gaussian processes, without noise, the acquisition functions without noise are
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_{\text{EI}}(x) &= ( \mu - \phi^*) \Phi\left( \dfrac{ \mu-\phi^*}\sigma \right) +
  \sigma \phi\left( \dfrac{ \mu-\phi^*}\sigma \right)  \\
  \alpha _{\text{PI}} &= \Phi\left(\dfrac{ \mu-\tau}\sigma \right) 
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
  \mu &= \mu(x) \\
  \sigma &= \sigma(x) \\
  \phi^* &: \text{best value of $f(x)$ so far} \\
  \Phi, \phi&: \text{Gaussian cdf and pdf} \\
  \tau &: \text{improvement threshold}
\end{align*}

\textbf{9.}
For the implementation, use the Coolesky decomposition, and update it sequentially.

Given observations $z$, use \emph{inducing values} $v$
\[ \begin{pmatrix} v \\ z \end{pmatrix} \sim
N \left(
\begin{pmatrix} \mu \\ m \end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix} \Sigma & k' \\ k & C + N \end{pmatrix}
\right)
\]
and compute an approximation of their conditional distribution
\[ v | z \sim N( \tilde \mu, \tilde \Sigma ) \]
and marginalize them
\[ p(f|z) = \int p(f|z) q(v|z) dz. \]

% 10. Theoretical analysis

% 11. Extensions
\textbf{11.}
We assumed that each observation had a constant cost;
if it varies, and is known in advance, estimate it (with a GP, jointly).

For optimal batch selection, try an $n$-dimensional acquisition function.

\emph{Multifidelity} optimization also uses several
surrogates of an expensive objective function
(e.g., early stopping -- freeze-thaw, hyperband).

For multi-objective optimization, use the volume under an estimate of the Pareto frontier as utility
($S$-metric) and maximize the expected hypervolume improvement (EHVI).

\nextbook
