Estimating a robust gradient
$\nabla_{\!\theta}\ell$ is time-consuming:
instead, use robust estimates of the partial derivatives
$ \E[ \partial\ell / \partial \theta_i ]$,
with coordinate descent.
Choose which coordinate to update with importance sampling.

Robust estimators of the mean of $x_1,\dots,x_n$ include the \emph{median-of-means} (MOM)
(median of the blockwise means),
the \emph{trimmed mean},
and the \emph{Catoni-Holland estimator}: $\mu$ such that
\[ \sum \psi \left( \dfrac{ x_i - \mu }{\sigma} \right) = 0, \]
where $\psi$ is, e.g., the sigmoid
\[ \psi(x) = 2 \text{Arctan}( e^x) - \dfrac\pi2 \]
and $\sigma$ satisfies
\[ \sum \chi\left( \dfrac{ x_i - \bar x }{ \sigma } \right) = 0 \]
where $\bar x$ is the sample mean,
and \[ \chi(u) = \dfrac{ u^2 }{ 1 + u^2 } - c, \]
where $c$ is such that
$\E_{ Z \sim N(0,1) } [ \chi(Z) ] =0$.
Both $\mu$ and $\sigma$ can be computed with fixed point iterations:
\begin{align*}
  \mu &\leftarrow \mu + \dfrac{ \sigma }{ \mu } \sum \psi \left( \dfrac{ x_i - \mu }{ \sigma } \right) \\
  \sigma &\leftarrow \sigma \left( 1 - \dfrac{ \chi(0) }{n} \sum \chi\left( \dfrac{ x_i - \bar x }\sigma \right) \right).
\end{align*}

Alternatively, one could use traditional empirical risk minimization (ERM) on disjoint subsets,
and aggregate them with a median-of-mean.

