\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mean estimation and regression under heavy-tailed distributions -- a survey}\\
  G. Lugosi and S. Mendelson (2019)
\end{book}
The quality of a (univariate) mean estimator $\hat \mu_n$
is often measured with the mean square error
$\E\bigl[ (\hat\mu_n - \mu )^2 \bigr]$
but, if the differences $\va{ \hat\mu_n - \mu}$ are not sufficiently concentrated,
the expectation need not reflect the typical error.
Instead, prefer estimators $\hat\mu_n$ that are close to $\mu$ with high probability
\[ \va{ \hat\mu_n - \mu } \leq \epsilon \ \,\text{w.p. } 1 - \delta. \]
For Gaussian data, the sample mean satisfies ($L$-sub-Gaussian)
\[ \va{ \hat\mu_n - \mu } \leq \dfrac{ L \sigma \sqrt{ \log(2/\delta) } }{ \sqrt n } \quad\text{w.p. } 1-\delta. \]
We want an estimator with a similar inequality,
but for fat tail distributions
(the estimators will depend on $\delta$, but will only require finite variance;
with more assumptions, e.g., finite third moment, they need no longer depend on $\delta$):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{median-of-means} estimator splits the data into $k$ groups, computes their means, and the median of those means;
\item \emph{Catoni's} estimator generalizes the characterization of the mean as the solution $\hat \mu_n$ of
  \( \sum (x_i - \hat\mu_n) = 0 \)
  to \[ \sum \psi\left( \dfrac{ x_i - \hat\mu_n }{s} \right) = 0 \]
  for
  \[ \phi(x) = \begin{cases}
    \log\left( 1 + x + \dfrac{ x^\alpha }\alpha \right) & \text{if } x \geq 0 \\
    - \log\biggl( 1 - x + \dfrac{ x^\alpha }\alpha \biggr) & \text{if } x < 0
  \end{cases},\]
  $s = \sqrt{ n/2 } \sigma$,
  and some estimator of $\sigma$;
\item The \emph{trimmed mean}
  splits the data in two, uses the first half to compute the quantiles, and the second half
  (without the observations beyond the quantiles) to compute the mean.
\end{compactitem}

For multivariate sub-Gaussian data,
\begin{align*}
\n{ \hat\mu_n - \mu } &\leq
\sqrt{ \dfrac{ \tr \Sigma } n } +
\sqrt{ \dfrac{ 2 \lambda_{\text{max}} \log(1/\delta) }{ n } }
\ \text{w.p.}\, 1 - \delta \\
\E \n{ \hat\mu_n - \mu }^2 &= \dfrac{ \tr \Sigma }{n}
\end{align*}
Traditional multivariate medians
(geometric (spatial), Tukey (half-space),
Liu (simplicial),
Oja)
do not give the desired bounds. Neither do the following medians:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The point $\hat \mu_n$ such that the Euclidean ball centered at $\hat\mu_n$
  that contains half the data has minimal radius;
\item Rescale the $x_i$'s such that $\n{ x_i } \leq 1/\alpha$
  (\ie, shrink them towards zero),
  perhaps after centering the data (use independent batches to estimate the center (mean)
  and to compute the shrunk mean).
\end{compactitem}
The following (\emph{median of means tournament}) works:
let $T_a$, $a \in \RR^n$,
be the set of points $x \in \RR^n$ at least as close to $z_1,\dots,z_k$ as $a$,
and set $\hat \mu_n = \argmin_a \operatorname{radius}(T_a)$,
where $z_1,\dots,z_k$ are block means
(a semi-definite relaxation can be computed efficiently).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust sparse voting}\\
  Y. Allouah et al. (2022)
\end{book}
The \emph{quadratically regularized weighted median} is
\[ \text{QrMed}_W(w,x) = \argmin_z \tfrac12 W \! z^2 + \sum_i w_i \va{ z - x_i }. \]
Contrary to the median, it is $W$-Byzantine resilient
(an attacker can change the median by choosing to be on one end or the other,
and its impact is unbounded).

The \emph{Byzantine resilient mean estimator} is a clipped mean centered on the QrMed:
\begin{align*}
  &\text{BrMean}_W (w,x) = \\
  &\qquad\qquad \text{ClipMean}\left( w, x ; \text{QrMed}_{4W}(w,x), \dfrac{ \n{w}_1 }{ 4W } \right) \\
  &\text{ClipMean}( w,  x ; \mu,  \Delta ) = \\
  &\qquad\qquad\dfrac1{\n{w}_1} \sum_i w_i \text{Clip}( x_i, \mu-\Delta, \mu+\Delta ).
\end{align*}

To aggregate complete rankings, use the majority judgement, or the randomized Condorcet voting system.

If the voters only provide sparse scores,
on arbitrary scales, the following is sparsely unanimous
and $W$-Byzantine resilient (but not independent of irrelevant alternatives).
\begin{align*}
  n&: \text{voter} \\
  a &: \text{alternative} \\
  \theta_{na} &: \text{scores} \\
  w_n &: \text{voting rights} \\
  \theta_a &\leftarrow \dfrac{ \theta_a - \min \theta_{am} }{ \max \theta_{am} - \min \theta_{am} } \\
  s_{nm} &= \operatorname*{Mean}_{a,b} \dfrac{ \theta_{ma} - \theta_{mb} }{ \theta_{na} - \theta_{nb} } \\
  s_n &= 1 + \operatorname*{BrMean}_{m}( s_{nm} - 1 ) \\
  \tau_{nm} &= \operatorname*{Mean}_a s_m \theta_{ma} - s_n \theta_{na} \\
  \tau_m &= \operatorname*{BrMean}_m \tau_{nm} \\
  \operatorname{Mehestan}(w,\theta) &= \operatorname*{QrMed}_n s_n \theta_n + \tau_n
\end{align*}
(if you do not insist on resilience and scale invariance, the weighted mean is better).

Individually scaling the preferences cannot lead to sparse unanimity.

