  T. Kesselheim (2020)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.} In a \emph{network congestion game},
$n$ players have to find a path from their respective start nodes to their target node,
on a graph,
and incur a cost (delay) for each edge used;
the per-user cost depends on the number of users using this edge.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.6,yscale=.7,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\node[circle,draw] (s1) at (0,2) {$s_1$};  
\node[circle,draw] (s2) at (1,1) {$s_2$};  
\node[circle,draw] (s3) at (0,0) {$s_3$};
\node[circle,draw] (t1) at (5,2) {$t_1$};
\node[circle,draw] (t2) at (4,1) {$t_2$};
\node[circle,draw] (t3) at (5,0) {$t_3$};
\draw[->] (s1) -- node[pos=.25,right=1.5pt]{\scriptsize 0,0,0} (s2); 
\draw[->] (s3) -- node[pos=.25,right=1.5pt]{\scriptsize 0,0,0} (s2);
\draw[->] (s1) -- node[pos=.5,above=0pt]{\scriptsize 4,4,4} (t1);
\draw[->] (s2) -- node[pos=.5,above=0pt]{\scriptsize 1,2,3} (t2);
\draw[->] (t2) -- node[pos=.25,right=1.5pt]{\scriptsize 0,0,0} (t1);
\draw[->] (t2) -- node[pos=.25,right=1.5pt]{\scriptsize 0,0,0} (t3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
Each player wants to minimize its cost,
but society wants to minimize the total cost.

A \emph{congestion game} is the datum of
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A set of players $\llbracket 1, n \rrbracket$;
\item A set of resources $R = \llbracket 1, m \rrbracket$;
\item A strategy space for each player $\Sigma_i \subset 2^R$;
\item A delay function for each resource $d_r : \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket \to \ZZ$.
\end{compactitem}
The cost of a state $s = (s_1,\dots,s_n) \in \Sigma_1 \times \cdots \Sigma_n$
for player $i$ is
$c_i(s) = \sum_{ r \in S_i } d_r \bigl( n_r(s) \bigr)$,
where $n_r(s) = \#\{ i : r \in S_i \}$
is the number of players using resource~$r$.

In a congestion game, every sequence of best response improvement steps is finite:
there is a pure Nash equilibrium.
This can be seen by showing that the \emph{Rosenthal potential}
\[ \Phi(s) = \sum_r \sum_{ k=1 }^{ n_r(s) } d_r(k) \]
decreases.

\textbf{2.} This sequence can be exponentially long but,
in a \emph{singleton} congestion game ($\forall i\ \forall R \in s_i \ \va R = 1$),
it is at most polynomial.

Pure Nash equilibria are exactly the local minima of the Rosenthal potential.

In symmetric network congestion games (all players have the same source and target nodes),
the Nash equilibria can be computed in polynomial time
(by reduction to a min-cost flow problem).

\textbf{3.}
A (cost-minimization) \emph{normal form game} is defined by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A set of players $\llbracket 1 , n \rrbracket$;
\item For each player $i$, a set of pure strategies $S_i$;
\item For each player, a cost function
  \[ c_i : S = \prod_j S_j \longrightarrow \RR. \]
\end{compactitem}
\emph{Bimatrix games} are a special case.
% Bimatrix game
\[
