\textbf{4.} Pure Nash equilibria do not always exist,
but every finite normal form game has a
\emph{mixed Nash equilibrium}
(a distribution on strategies, for each player);
the proof uses Brouwer's fixed point theorem.
For bimatrix games,
one can find a mixed Nash equilibrium
with a simplex-like algorithm.

\textbf{5.}
NP is a complexity class for decision problems,
but Nash equilibrium
is a search problem.
A search problem is FP if we can find a solution in polynomial time;
it is FNP if we can verify a solution in polynomial time.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.3,yscale=.7,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\node at (1,0) {FP};
\node at (0,0) {PPAD};
\node at (2,0) {PLS};
\node at (1,1) {FNP};
\draw (1,0) ellipse (3mm and 3mm);
\draw (0.5,0) ellipse (10mm and 6mm);
\draw (1.5,0) ellipse (10mm and 6mm);
\draw (1,.25) ellipse (18mm and 12mm);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Finding a mixed Nash equilibrium in a normal form game is PPAD complete;
finding a pure Nash equilibrium in a congestion game is PLS complete.

A local search problem is in PLS (polynomial local search)
if there are polynomial algorithms to
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Find a feasible solution;
\item Compute the cost of a feasible solution;
\item Improve a feasible solution or, if it is already optimal, prove it.
\end{compactitem}
Max-Cut is PLS-complete,
and can be reduced to the problem of finding a Nash equilibrium
in a congestion game.

\textbf{6.} A \emph{correlated equilibrium} is a probability distribution on states
(the players are no longer independent) such that
$\forall i \in N \ \ 
\forall s_i \in S_i \ \ 
\forall s_i' \in S_i$
\[
\E_{ s \sim p } \bigl[ c_i(s) | s_i \bigr]
\leq
\E_{ s \sim p } \bigl[ c_i(s_i',s_{-i}) | s_i \bigr]
,\]
\ie,
whenever $p$ tells player $i$ to play $s_i$, it is the best thing to do.

A \emph{coarse correlated equilibrium} only demands
\[ 
\E_{ s \sim p } \bigl[ c_i(s) \bigr]
\leq
\E_{ s \sim p } \bigl[ c_i(s_i',s_{-i}) \bigr]
,\]
\ie, player $i$ is better off following $p$
than always choosing $s_i'$.

A correlated equilibrium can be computed in polynomial time (via linear programming).

To find an (approximate) coarse equilibrium,
the players can follow a \emph{no-regret} algorithm.
With two players, it would be:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The player picks a distribution $p_t$ over strategies; 
\item The adversary picks a cost vector $\ell_t$;
\item The player picks an action from $p_t$, incurs the corresponding loss,
  and gets to know the cost vector.
\end{compactitem}
The player tries to minimize the (external, expected) regret
\[
\E \biggl[ \sum_t \ell_t( a_t ) - \min_a \sum_t \ell(a) \biggr]
\]
(we compare the player's action with the best constant action in hindsight --
the best action sequence in hindsight, $\sum_t \min_a \ell_t(a)$,
would be too strong).
For multiple players, each player chooses a distribution,
and the loss vector is determined by the decisions of the other players

\textbf{7.}
The \emph{multiplicative weights algorithm}
progressively updates the probabilities by increasing those with low cost
and decreasing those with high cost:
\begin{align*}
  w_1 &= (1,\dots,1) \\
  p_t(i) &\propto w_t(i) \\
  w_{t+1}(i) &\leftarrow w_t(i) (1-\eta) ^ { \ell_t(i) }.
\end{align*}
With $\eta = \sqrt{ \log M / T }$,
the regret is at most
$2\sqrt{ T \log M }$.

\textbf{8.} The \emph{social cost} is the sum of the players' costs
\[ \text{SC}(S) = \sum_i c_i(S) . \]
The \d{price of anarchy} for pure Nash equilibria (PNE)
compares the worst Nash equilibrium with the best societal outcome.
\[ \text{PoA}_{ \text{PNE} } = \dfrac{
  \max\limits_{ S \in \text{PNE} } \text{SC}(S)
}{
  \min\limits_S \text{SC}(S)
} \]
In a congestion game with affine delay functions,
$\text{PoA} \leq 5/2$.
Other equilibrium concepts have a higher price of anarchy.
\[ 1 \leq
\text{PoA}_{\text{PNE}} \leq
\text{PoA}_{\text{MNE}} \leq
\text{PoA}_{\text{CE}} \leq
\text{PoA}_{\text{CCE}}
\]

A game is \emph{$(\lambda,\mu)$-smooth}
($\lambda > 0$, $\mu < 1$) if
\[ \forall s, s* \ \ \sum_i c_i( s_i^* , s_{-i} ) \leq
\lambda \text{SC} (s^*) + \mu \text{SC}(s); \]
the price of anarchy for coarse correlated equilibria is then at most $\lambda / (1-\mu)$.

\textbf{9.}
The \emph{price of stability}
compares the best equilibrium (the PoA used the worst) with the best societal outcome.
\[ \text{PoS}_{\text{Eq}} = \dfrac{
  \min\limits_{ s \in \text{Eq} } \text{SC}(s) 
}{
  \min\limits_{ s } \text{SC}(s) 
} \]


A \emph{cost-sharing game} is a congestion game with
$d_r(x) = c_r / x$;
the societal cost is then $\text{SC}(s) =
\sum_{ r : n_r(s) \geq 1 } c_r$.
The price of stability of a cost-sharing game is at most
\[ H_n = 1 + \dfrac12 + \dfrac13 + \cdots + \dfrac1n . \]
For a symmetric cost sharing game
(all players have the same strategies), it is 1,
but the price of anarchy can be large.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.3,yscale=.7,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\node[circle,draw] (s) at (0,0) {$s$};
\node[circle,draw] (t) at (1,0) {$t$};
\draw[->] (s) to[out=30,in=150] node[pos=.5,above=0pt] {\scriptsize $1+\epsilon$} (t);
\draw[->] (s) to[out=-30,in=-150] node[pos=.5,below=0pt] {\scriptsize $n$} (t);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

A \emph{market sharing game} is a (utility maximization) congestion game with
$S_i \subset \llbracket 1 , m \rrbracket$
and $u_i(s) = v_{ s_i } / n_{ s_i } (s)$,
where $v_j$ is the demand in market $j$,
shared among the players entering it.
The \emph{social welfare} is then
\[ \text{SW}(s) = \sum_{ j\ :\ n_j(s) \geq 1 } v_j . \]
The price of anarchy of a utility-maximization game is
\[ \text{PoA}_{\text{Eq}} = \dfrac{
  \max\limits_{ s \in S } \text{SW}(s)
}{
  \min\limits_{ p \in \text{Eq} } \text{SW}(p)
}. \]
A utility-maximization game is
$(\lambda,\mu)$-smooth ($\lambda>0$, $\mu\geq0$) if
\[
\forall s, s' \ \
\sum_i u_i( s_i^* , s_{-i} ) \geq
\lambda \text{SW}( s^* ) - \mu \text{SW}(s) ;
\]
its PoA$_{\text{CCE}}$ is at most $(1+\mu)/\lambda$.
The market-sharing game is (1,1)-smooth: $\text{PoA}_{\text{CCE}} \leq 2$.

\textbf{10.}
In a \emph{first-price auction},
where each player has a value $v_i$
for the item, makes a bid $b_i$,
and has utility $u_i( b , v_i ) = v_i - b_i $
if he wins and zero otherwise, there is a Nash equilibrium:
the highest value player bids the second highest value,
and the others bid their value.

In a \emph{second price auction} (Vickrey auction),
$u_i(b,v_i) = v_i - \max_{ j \neq i } b_i$;
it is a dominant strategy to bid truthfully.

Given a set of players $\llbracket 1 , n \rrbracket$,
a set of outcomes $X$,
valuation functions $v_i : X \to \RR$, and possible bids
$B = B_1 \times \cdots \times B_n$,
a \emph{mechanism} is the datum of
an outcome rule $f : B \to X$
and a payment rule $p : B \to \RR^n$.
The players' utilities are
\[ u_i(b) = v_i \bigl( f(b) \bigr) - p_i(b). \]
If the bids are valuation functions, the mechanism is \emph{direct}.
A mechanism is \d{truthful} (dominant strategy incentive compatible, DSIC)
if bidding the true value is a weakly dominant strategy.

\textbf{11.} In a \emph{single-parameter mechanism},
$X \subset \RR_{\geq0}^n$
and $v_i(x) = v_i \cdot x_i$.
In this case, if the allocation rule $f$ is monotone,
\ie, the $z \mapsto f_i(x, b_{-i})$ are non-decreasing,
setting the payment rule to
\[ p_i(b_i, b_{-i}) = b_i f_i( b_i, b_{-i} )  -
\int_0^{b_1} f_i( t , b_{-i} ) dt
\]
makes the mechanism truthful (\d{Myerson's lemma}).

\textbf{12.} In a \emph{combinatorial auction},
there are $n$ items and each bidder has a value for each subset of items.
If bidders are \emph{single-minded} (they only care whether they get all their desired items or not),
if their preferences are public (but their valuations private),
the \emph{greedy-by-value} mechanism (give bidders what they want, if possible,
starting bidders with the largest bids)
is a $d$-approximation, where $d$ is the size of the largest bundle.
For large bundle sizes, prefer greedy-my-sqrt-value-density (order the bidders by
$b_i / \sqrt{ \va {S_i^*} }$): it is an $\sqrt m$-approximation.

\textbf{13.}
A \d{VCG mechanism} 
is a mechanism whose allocation rule $f:V\to X$ maximizes social welfare,
\ie, $f(b) \in \argmax_x \sum_i b_i(x)$,
and whose payment rule is
\[ p_i(b) = \max_x \sum_{j\neq i} b_j(x) - \sum b_j\bigl( f(b) \bigr) \]
(player $i$'s externality).
It is DSIC.
Examples include
second-price single-item auction, sponsored search auctions
($v_i(x) = v_i \alpha_j$ if agent $i$ gets slot $j$),
unit-demand combinatorial auctions ($v_i(S) = \max_{j\in S} v_{ij}$).

VCG requires solving a welfare maximization problem:
an approximation is not enough -- for instance, VCG payments
with a greedy allocation for unit-demand combinatorial auctions (a 2-approximation)
do not give a truthful mechanism.

\textbf{14.} Given a (non-truthful) mechanism,
with incomplete information (the bidders' values
are drawn from a known distribution $v_i \sim \mathscr D_i $),
a \d{Bayes-Nash equilibrium} is a profile of bidding functions
$\beta_i : V_i \to B_i$ such that
$ \forall i \ \ \forall v_i \in V_i \ \ \forall b_i' \in B_i$
\[
\E_{ v_{-i} \sim \mathscr D_{-i} } u_i\bigl( \beta(v) , v_i \bigr) \geq
\E_{ v_{-i} \sim \mathscr D_{-i} } u_i\bigl( (b_i',\beta_{-i}(v)) , v_i \bigr)
.\]

\textbf{15.} A mechanism is $(\lambda,\mu)$-smooth if
\begin{align*}
  &
  \forall v \in V \ \ 
  \forall i \ \ 
  \forall b_i^* \ \ 
  \forall b \in B
  \\
  &
  \sum_i u_i \bigl( (b_i^* , b_{-i} ) , v_i \bigr) \geq
  \lambda \cdot \text{OPT}(v) - \mu \sum_i p_i(b);
\end{align*}
the price of anarchy is then bounded
(same bound for pure Nash equilibria, (coarse) correlated equilibria,
and Bayes-Nash equilibria):
\[ \text{PoA} \leq \dfrac{ \max (\mu, 1 ) }{ \lambda }. \]

Single-item first-price auction is ($\tfrac12$,1)-smooth;
single-item all-pay auction is ($\tfrac12$,2)-smooth.

\textbf{16.} Combinatorial auction with unit-demand valuations and first-price payments is
($\tfrac12$,1)-smooth.
First-price greedy-by-sqrt-value-density for multi-minded combinatorial auctions is
($\tfrac12$,$\sqrt{2m}$)-smooth.

\textbf{17.} A \d{Walrasian equilibrium} (in a combinatorial auction)
is a \emph{price vector} (Nash equilibria were about players' bids, not prices)
$q \in \RR_{\geq0}^m$
and an allocation $S$ such that each bidder gets a bundle maximizing his utility
\[ \forall S_i' \quad v_i(S_i) - \sum_{ j \in S_i } q_i \geq
v_i( S_i') - \sum _{ j \in S_i' } q_i . \]
Walrasian equilibria (if they exist) maximize social welfare.
Unit-demand VCG prices are a Walrasian equilibrium.

\textbf{18.} In the \emph{posted prices} mechanism,
buyers come one by one, and buy the set of unsold items maximizing their utility.
For unit-demand, the expected social welfare of the posted prices mechanism
is half the expected optimal welfare --
set the prices to (the expectation of) half
the value for the buyer getting it in the optimal welfare allocation.

\textbf{19.}
If the values are drawn from distributions $\mathscr D_i$,
the \emph{virtual values} are
\[ \phi_i(t) = t - \dfrac{ 1 - F_i(t) }{ f_i(t) }. \]
For a truthful single-parameter mechanism,
the expected revenue is the expected virtual welfare:
\[ \E_v \sum_i p_i(v) = \E_v \sum_i \phi_i(v_i) x_i(v) . \]
The \emph{virtual welfare maximizing mechanism}
(with Myerson's payments)
is truthful and maximizes expected revenue among truthful payments.

\textbf{20.} The second-price auction maximizes
the expected revenue among all truthful mechanisms
(if valuations are drawn from the same distribution, if the item is always allocated).
Posting a price
\[ p^* = \max \bigl\{
\phi^{-1}(0) , F^{-1} ( 1 - \tfrac1n ) \bigr\}
\]
gives a $1-(1-\tfrac1n)^n\geq(1-1/e)$-approximation of the optimal expected revenue.
(Surprisingly, truthfulness is not a restriction.)

\textbf{21.} To match agents to houses,
when agents have different preferences (without money),
start by assigning the houses at random,
then build a directed graph with edges from each agent to the owner of the house he prefers best among the remaining ones,
find a cycle,
reallocate the houses in it, and remove the corresponding nodes;
iterate until the graph is empty.
The result allocation is \emph{stable}.

\textbf{22.} The \d{Gale-Shapley} algorithm generalizes this to situations with preferences
from both agents and items.

\textbf{23.} To cut a cake $[0,1]$ between $n$ agents with valuations
\[ V_i(X) = \int_{ x \in X } v_i(x) dx
\qquad X \subset [0,1]
\qquad V_i([0,1]) = 1,
\]
we may want:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Proportionality: $\forall i \quad V_i(A_i) \geq 1/n$;
\item Envy-freeness: $\forall i, j \quad V_i(A_i) \geq V_i(A_j)$;
\item Equitability: $\forall i, j \quad V_i(A_i) = V_i(A_j)$.
\end{compactitem}
For two agents, the cut-and-choose mechanism is proportional, envy-free,
but not necessarily equitable.
For $n$ agents, the \emph{Dubins-Spanier algorithm} is proportional.
Ensuring envy-freeness is more complicated.
None of those algorithms is truthful.

\textbf{24.} A \emph{ranking rule}
for three or more candidates satisfying unanimity and independence of irrelevant alternatives
is a dictatorship.

\textbf{25.} \d{Shapley values} formalize cost-sharing.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mechanics for mathematicians}\\
  J. Wunsch (2020)
\end{book}

\textbf{2.}
Newton's law, $F=ma$, describes the evolution of a physical system
as a second-order differential equation.
Examples include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Gravitation (1d): $\ddot x = -1$;
\item Spring: $\ddot x = - \omega^2 x$;
\item Magnetism: $\ddot x = \dot x \times B$;
\item Gravity: $\ddot x = - \hat x / \n{ x }^2$.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{3.} The initial value problem has a unique solution
(Picard-Lindel\"of);
it may not have a closed form solution:
try Taylor expansions or numeric methods (Newton).

\textbf{4.} In dimension 1,
if the force does not depend on time, it comes from a \d{potential}:
\[ \ddot x = F(x)
\ \leadsto\ 
\ddot x \dot x = F(x) \dot x
\ \leadsto\ 
\dfrac{d}{dt} \biggl( \dfrac12 \dot x ^2 + V(x) \biggr) = 0
\]
where $V'(x) = - F(x)$.
The energy $E=\tfrac12 \dot x^2 + V(x)$ is constant.

\textbf{5.}
Plot the level curves of the energy in the phase plane to identify periodic motion
and distinguish between stable and unstable equilibria
($V(x_0)>0$ and $V(x_0)<0$).

\textbf{6.} In $\RR^n$, we would also like to write $F(x) = - \nabla V(x)$,
but that is only possible if $\operatorname{curl} F=0$, or, more generally, if
the vector field $F$ is conservative, \ie, 
$\int_\gamma F(x) \cdot dx$ only depends on the endpoints of the path~$\gamma$.

\textbf{7a.} The \d{Lagrangian} is the difference between kinetic $T$ and potential $V$ energy.
\[ L = T - V \qquad L(x,\dot x) = \tfrac12 \va{ \dot x }^2 - V(x) \]
The \emph{action} of a trajectory $\gamma: [0,T] \to \RR^3$ is
\[ S(\gamma) = \int_0^T L\bigl( \gamma(t) , \dot \gamma(t) \bigr) dt. \]
The \emph{principle of least action} states that the action is stationary, \ie,
its directional derivatives are zero:
\[ \left. \dfrac{ d }{ ds } S( \gamma + s \phi ) \right|_{ s=0 } = 0 \]
for all paths $\phi$ with $\phi(0) = \phi(T) = 0$.

For a functional of the form
\[ S(\gamma) = \int_0^T L \bigl( \gamma(t) , \dot \gamma(t) , t \bigr) dt, \]
the stationarity condition becomes
\[ \int_0^T \biggl[ \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial x } \cdot \phi(t) +
  \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \cdot \phi'(t)
  \biggr] dt = 0
\]
and, after integrating the second term by parts (and using $\phi(0)=\phi(T)=0$),
\[ \int_0^T \biggl(
\dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial x }
-
\dfrac{ d }{ dt }
\dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v }
\biggl)
\cdot \phi(t) dt = 0
\]
giving the \d{Euler-Lagrange equation}
\[ 
\dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial x }
=
\dfrac{ d }{ dt }
\dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v }.
\]

\textbf{7b.}
If a coordinate $x_i$ does not appear in $L$, then
\[ 
\dfrac{ d }{ dt }
\dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v_i }
=
\dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial x_i }
= 0,
\]
\ie, $\partial L / \partial v_i$ is a conserved quantity.

There is a similar result for time:
if $\partial L / \partial t = 0$,
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ dL }{ dt } &=
  \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial x } \cdot \dot \gamma +
  \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \cdot \ddot \gamma +
  \cancel{
    \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial t }
  } \\
  &= \biggl( \dfrac{d}{dt} \dfrac{\partial L}{\partial v} \biggr) \cdot \dot \gamma +
  \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \cdot \ddot \gamma \quad \text{(Euler-Lagrange)} \\
  &= \dfrac{ d }{ dt } \biggl( \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \cdot \dot \gamma \biggr)
  \quad \text{(product rule)}
\end{align*}
hence
\[ \dfrac{ d }{ dt } \biggl(
- L + \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \cdot \dot \gamma
\biggr) = 0 \]
\ie, the \emph{Hamiltonian}
\[ H(x,v) = - L(x,v) + \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \cdot v \]
is conserved.
(In simple cases, $H=T+V$.)

The Lagrangian approach facilitates changes of coordinates
and easily allows for constraints (e.g., with a reparametrization).

\textbf{7c.} More generally, a continuous symmetry
\begin{align*}
  & F^a : \RR^n \longrightarrow \RR^n \\
  & F^a_* (x,v) = \bigl( F(x) , \nabla F(x) \cdot v \bigr) \\
  & L\bigl( F_*^a(x,v) , t \bigr) = L(X,v,t) 
\end{align*}
gives rise to a conservation law (\d{Noether's theorem})
\[ p = \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \cdot W
\quad \text{where }
W(x) = \left.
\dfrac{ d }{ da } F^a(x) 
\right|_{a=0}.
\]
Examples include momentum and angular momentum.

\textbf{11.}
The Hamiltonian is defined as
\[ H = \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \cdot v - L. \]
With the change of variables
\begin{align*}
  q &= x \\
  p &= \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v }
\end{align*}
it becomes $ H = p v - L(x,v)$. We then have
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ \partial H }{ \partial p } &=
  v + \cancel{ p \dfrac{ \partial v }{ \partial p } }
  - \dcancel{
    \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial x }
    \smash{ \underset{=0}{ \dfrac{ \partial x }{ \partial p } } }
  }
  - \cancel{
    \smash{ \underset{=p}{\dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v }} }
    \dfrac{ \partial v }{ \partial p }
  } \\
  &= v \\&= \dot x \\&= \dot q \\
  \dfrac{ \partial H }{ \partial q } &=
  \cancel{ p \dfrac{ \partial v }{ \partial p } }
  - \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial x }
  \smash{ \underset{=1}{\dfrac{ \partial x }{ \partial p }} }
  - \cancel{ 
    \smash{ \underset{=p}{\dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v }} }
    \dfrac{ \partial v }{ \partial p }
  } \\
  &= - \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial x } \\
  &= - \dfrac{ d }{ dt } \dfrac{ \partial L }{ \partial v } \qquad \text{(Euler-Lagrange)} \\
  &= - \dot p  
\end{align*}
Hamilton's equations of motion are
\begin{align*}
  \dot q &= \dfrac{ \partial H }{ \partial p } \\
  \dot p &= - \dfrac{ \partial H }{ \partial q }.
\end{align*}
They define a vector field
\[ v_H = \begin{pmatrix} \partial H / \partial p \\ - \partial H / \partial q \end{pmatrix} \]
and a flow $(\Phi_t)_t$.

The Hamiltonian vector field has vanishing divergence,
$\nabla \cdot v_H = 0$;
its flow is therefore volume-preserving
(Liou\-ville's theorem).

For a function defined on phase space, $a(q,p)$,
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ da }{ dt } &=
  \dfrac{ \partial a }{ \partial q } \cdot \dot q +
  \dfrac{ \partial a }{ \partial p } \cdot \dot p \\
  &= \dfrac{ \partial a }{ \partial q} \cdot \dfrac{ \partial H }{ \partial p }
  - \dfrac{ \partial a }{ \partial p } \cdot \dfrac{ \partial H }{ \partial q } \\
  &= \{ a , H \} \qquad \text{(Poisson bracket)} \\
  &= ( v_H \cdot \nabla ) a.
\end{align*}
The flow is a simplectomorphism,
\[
(\nabla \Phi_t ) J (\nabla \Phi_t )' = J
\]
where $J = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & I \\ -I & 0 \end{pmatrix}$.






\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio construction with Gaussian mixture returns and exponential utility\\
  via convex optimization}\\
  E. Luxenberg and S. Boyd (2022)
\end{book}
The expected utility of a portfolio whose constituent returns are a Gaussian mixture has a simple closed form
\begin{align*}
  & r \sim \text{GM} \bigl( (\mu_i, \Sigma_i, \pi_i )_{ 1 \leq i \leq k } \bigr) \\
  & R = w' r \\
  & U(r) = 1 - e^ { - \gamma R } \\
  & \E\bigl[ U(R) \bigr] = 1 - M(w, -\gamma) \\
  & M( w, t ) = \E[ e^tR ] =
  \sum \pi_i \exp \Bigl[ t \mu_i ' w + \dfrac12 w' \Sigma_i w \Bigr]
\end{align*}
and maximizing the \emph{entropic value at risk}
\[ \text{EVaR}_\alpha(R) = \inf_{ \lambda > 0 } \dfrac{
  \log M(w, -\lambda) - \log \alpha }{ \lambda }
\]
and can also be formulated as a convex problem.
[Implementation in a few lines of \texttt{cvxpy}.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Entropic portfolio optimization: a disciplined convex programming framework}\\
  D. Cajas (2021)
\end{book}
The EVaR (and the EDaR, entropic drawdown at risk)
can also be optimized using samples.
Implementation in \texttt{Riskfolio-Lib}.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{OWA portfolio optimization: a disciplined convex programming framework}\\
  D. Cajas (2021)
\end{book}
The \emph{ordered weighted average} (OWA)
of $(y_i)_{1 \leq i \leq T}$
is a weighted average of order statistics,
$\sum w_i y_{(i)}$.
Examples include the \emph{Gini mean deviation}
\[ \text{GMD} = \operatorname*{Mean}_{ i \neq j } \va{ y_i - y_j } \]
or the $L$-moments ($\lambda_2 = \tfrac12 \text{GMD}$).
They can be formulated with a linear program.
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w, \alpha, \beta, y \\
  \text{To maximize} & w' \mu - \lambda  \sum_i ( \alpha_i + \beta_i ) \\
  \text{Such that}
  & w' \mathbf 1 = 1 \\
  & w \neq 0 \\
  & w' r = y \\
  & \forall i, j \quad \alpha_i + \beta_j \geq w_i y_{(j)}
\end{array} \]
where $r_{ki}$ is the return of asset $k$ in scenario $i$.

% TODO: $w$ should be one of the inputs?

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal matrix completion}\\
  A. Agarwal et al. 
\end{book}
To solve the matrix completion problem
\begin{align*}
  & A \in \RR^{m\times n} \qquad \text{latent} \\
  & \rlap{\ensuremath{ \epsilon \in \RR^{m \times n}}}
  \hphantom{A \in \RR^{m\times n}}
  \qquad \text{noise} \\
  & \E[\epsilon] = 0 \\
  & \tilde Y = A + \epsilon \\
  &
  \rlap{\ensuremath{P \in [0,1]^{m\times n}}}
  \hphantom{D \in \{0,1\}^{m \times n}}
  \qquad \text{propensity score} \\
  & D \in \{0,1\}^{m \times n} \qquad \text{missingness mask} \\
  & \E[ D ] = P \\
  & Y_{ij} = \begin{cases} \tilde Y_{ij} & \text{if } D_{ij} = 1 \\ * & \text{otherwise} 
  \end{cases}
\end{align*}
one can try
\begin{align*}
  & \text{USVT} && \operatorname*{Minimize}\limits_{ Q \, :\, \text{rank}\,Q \leq \mu }
  \n{ ( Y - Q ) \odot D } _2^2 \\
  & \text{softImpute} && \operatorname*{Minimize}\limits_{Q}
  \n{ ( Y - Q ) \odot D } _2^2 + \lambda \n{Q}_* \\
\end{align*}
(but they may not perform well if the data is not MCAR)
or inverse propensity weighting
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_Q
\sum_{i,j\,:\, D_{ij}=1} \dfrac1{P_{ij}} d( Y_{ij} , Q_{ij} ) + \lambda R(Q).
\]

To fill in a missing value $Y_{ij}$,
\emph{synthetic nearest neighbours}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Look for several fully-observed blocks $S_k$, with non-overlapping rows
(e.g., \emph{maximial bicliques}),
\item Approximate the row $\text{AC}_k$ from $S_k$,
  with a principal component regression,
\item Compute the corresponding prediction for $Y_{ij}$,
\item And average those predictions.
\end{compactitem}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.5,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \draw (0,0) -- (11,0) -- (11,9) -- (0,9) -- cycle;
  \draw[fill=black!20] (1,9) -- (9,9) -- (9,8) -- (1,8) -- cycle;
  \draw[ultra thick] (10,8) -- (11,8) -- (11,9) -- (10,9) -- cycle;
  \draw[fill=black!20] (3,5) -- (3,7) -- (7,7) -- (7,5) -- cycle;
  \draw[fill=black!20] (10,5) -- (11,5) -- (11,7) -- (10,7) -- cycle;
  \draw[fill=black!20] (5,3) -- (5,4) -- (9,4) -- (9,3) -- cycle;
  \draw[fill=black!20] (1,1) -- (1,2) -- (6,2) -- (6,1) -- cycle;
  \draw[fill=black!20] (10,1) -- (10,2) -- (11,2) -- (11,1) -- cycle;
  \draw[fill=black!20] (10,3) -- (10,4) -- (11,4) -- (11,3) -- cycle;
  \node at (-.5,8.5) {$i$};
  \node at (10.5,-.5) {$j$};
  \node at (5,6) {$S_1$};
  \node at (7,3.5) {$S_2$};
  \node at (3.5,1.5) {$S_3$};
  \node[anchor=west] at (11,6) {\footnotesize AR$_1$};
  \node[anchor=west] at (11,3.5) {\footnotesize AR$_2$};
  \node[anchor=west] at (11,1.5) {\footnotesize AR$_3$};
  \draw[ultra thick] (3,9.3) -- (7,9.3);
  \draw[ultra thick] (5,9.6) -- (9,9.6);
  \draw[ultra thick] (1,9.9) -- (6,9.9);
  \node[anchor=east] at (3,9.3) {\footnotesize AC$_1$};
  \node[anchor=west] at (9,9.6) {\footnotesize AC$_2$};
  \node[anchor=east] at (1,9.9) {\footnotesize AC$_3$};
  \draw[densely dotted] (0,8) -- (11,8);
  \draw[densely dotted] (10,0) -- (10,9);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Matrix estimation\\ by universal singular value thresholing}\\
  S. Chatterjee (2015)
\end{book}
Given a matrix $X \in [-1,1]^{m\times n}$, $m\geq n$,
with missing values,
USVT completes it as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fill in the missing values with 0;
\item Compute the SVD;
\item Only keep the singular values above $2\sqrt{ np}$,
  where $p$ is the proportion of observed values;
\item Reconstruct the matrix;
\item Clip the entries to $[-1,1]$.
\end{compactitem}
Applications include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Low-rank matrices;
\item Latent space models
  $x_{ij} = f(\beta_i,\beta_j) + \epsilon_{ij}$;
\item Bradley-Terry models
  \begin{align*}
    p_{ij} &= P[ \text{team $i$ wins over $j$} ] = \dfrac{ a_i }{ a_i + a_j } \\
    X_{ij} & \sim \text{Bernoulli}(p_{ij})
  \end{align*}
  and its non-linear generalizations ($p$ monotonic);
\item Stochastic block models
\item Graphons (estimation of a graphon from a single graph sampled from  it);
\item Distance matrices;
\item Positive semi-definite matrices. 
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Matrix completion and low-rank SVD\\ via fast alternating least squares}\\
  T. Hastie et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The matrix completion problem
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_M \dfrac12
\n{ (X-M) \odot O }_F^2 + \lambda \n{ M }_*
\]
where $\n{\cdot}_*$ is the nuclear norm,
the sum of the singular values (a convex relazation of the rank)
can be solved with the \emph{softImpute} algorithm, iterating:
\begin{align*}
  & \hat X = \text{coalesce}(X,0) \\
  & \hat X \overset{\text{SVD}}{=} U D V' \\
  & \hat M = U S_\lambda(D) V' \\
  & \hat X = \text{coalesce}( X, \hat M )
\end{align*}
where the soft thresholding is
$ S_\lambda(D_{ii}) = ( D_{ii} - \lambda )_+ $;
an efficient implementation can use a reduced rank SVD,
and write $\hat X$ as the sum of a sparse and a low-rank matrix,
$\hat X = \text{coalesce}(X,\hat M) = ( X-\hat M) + \hat M$.

The \emph{maximum margin matrix factorization} (MMMF) problem
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{A,B} \dfrac12 \n{ (X-AB') \odot O }_F^2 +
\dfrac\lambda2 \left( \n A _F^2 + \n B _F^2 \right) \]
is biconvex and can be solved with alternating least squares (ALS);
the solution is
\begin{align*}
  \hat A &= U_r S_\lambda(D_r)^{1/2} \\
  \hat B &= V_r S_\lambda(D_r)^{1/2}.
\end{align*}

The \emph{softImpute-ALS} algorithm combines both approaches.
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{A,B} \dfrac12 \n{ \hat X - A B' }_F^2 +
\dfrac\lambda2 \left( \n A _F^2 + \n B _F^2 \right). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A swiss army infinitesinal jackknife}\\
  R. Giordano et al. (2020)
\end{book}
To compute approximate bootstrap, cross-validation or jackknife
samples for an estimator $\theta$ defined by
\[ \dfrac1N \sum_n w_n g_n(\theta) = 0 \]
(the $g_n$'s could be the gradients of a loss function --
use automatic differentiation to compute them)
use a first order approximation.
\begin{align*}
  G(\theta,w) &= \dfrac1N w' g \\
  H(\theta,w) &= \dfrac1N w' \nabla_{\!\theta} g \\
  G(\theta(w), w) &= 0 \\
  \dfrac{\partial G}{ \partial \theta }
  \dfrac{ \partial \theta }{ \partial w } +
  \dfrac{ \partial G }{ \partial w } &= 0 \\
  \dfrac{ \partial \theta }{ \partial w} &= -
  \left( \dfrac{ \partial G }{ \partial \theta } \right)^{-1}
    \dfrac{ \partial G }{ \partial w } \\
    &= - H^{-1} \left( \dfrac1N g \right) \\
    \hat\theta_{IJ}(w) &= \hat\theta_{\mathbf1} +
    \left. \dfrac{ \partial \theta }{ \partial w } \right|_{ w = \mathbf 1 }
    ( w - \mathbf 1 ) \\
    &= \hat\theta_{\mathbf1} - H_{\mathbf1}^{-1} G( \hat\theta_{\mathbf1} , w - \mathbf 1 )  
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Uncertainty prediction for deep sequential regression using meta models}\\
  J. Navr\'atil et al.
\end{book}
A \emph{metamodel} predicts the error of a base model:
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Base model:} &
  \displaystyle \operatorname*{Minimize}_\phi \E\bigl[ \text{loss}(\hat y, y) \bigr] \\
  \text{Meta model:} &
  \displaystyle \operatorname*{Minimize}_\gamma \E\bigl[ \text{loss}(\hat z, z) \bigr]   
\end{array} \]
where $z = \text{loss}(\hat y, y)$ (those three loss functions can be different).

% The bootstrap only provides an estimate of the aleatoric noise (due to the data),
% not of the epistemic noise (inherent in the model). (wrong?)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning prediction intervals\\ for model performance}\\
  B. Elder et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Use a meta model to predict the performance (e.g., accuracy) of the base model,
and a meta-meta model to predict a confidence interval on it.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Confidence scoring using whitebox meta-models with linear classifiers}\\
  T. Chen et al.
\end{book}
Build a meta model from linear features on the various layers of the base model.

% TODO: PLOT?

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph similarity learning for change point detection in dynamic networks}\\
  D. Sulem et al. (2022)
\end{book}
To detect changepoints in time-varying networks $(G_t)_{t\geq0}$,
train a Siamese GNN
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.6,yscale=.5,inner sep=2pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
  \node (G1) at (.3,2) {$G_1$};
  \node (G2) at (.3,0) {$G_2$};
  \node[draw] (GNN1) at (1,2) {\textsc{gnn}};
  \node[draw] (GNN2) at (1,0) {\textsc{gnn}};
  \node[draw] (d) at (2,1) {distance};
  \node[draw] (s) at (3.15,1) {sort-$k$};
  \node[draw] (fc) at (4,1) {\textsc{fc}};
  \node (o) at (4.6,1) {$s$};
  \draw[->] (G1) -- (GNN1);
  \draw[->] (G2) -- (GNN2);
  \draw[->] (GNN1) -- (d);
  \draw[->] (GNN2) -- (d);
  \draw[->] (d) -- (s);
  \draw[->] (s) -- (fc);
  \draw[->] (fc) -- (o);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
and use its output as a similarity measure, e.g.,
$\operatorname*{Mean}\limits_{i \in \llbracket 1, L \rrbracket} s( G_t, G_{t-i} )$
or
$\operatorname*{Mean}\limits_{
  \substack{i,j \in \llbracket 1, L \rrbracket \\ i<j}
} s( G_{t-i}, G_{t-y} )$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sorted pooling in convolutional networks\\ for one-shot learning}\\
  H. Andr\'as (2020)
\end{book}
Max pooling can be replaced by:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $k$-Max pooling, returning the $k$-th largest value in each patch;
\item Sort-$k$ pooling, returning a weighted average of the top-$k$ values,
  with learned weights.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rough volatility: fact or artefact}\\
  R. Cont and P. Das (2022)
\end{book}
Volatility clustering suggests $H>\tfrac12$,
even though the roughness of the realized volatility suggests $H < \tfrac12$.

The $p$-variation of a function $x: [0,T] \to \RR$ is the limit
\[ \sum \va{ x( t_{i+1} ) - x(t) } ^p \longrightarrow [x]^p \]
when the partition $(0,t_0,t_1,\dots,t_N = T)$ gets finer.
The \emph{variation index} and the \emph{roughness index} are then
\begin{align*}
  p(x) &= \inf \{\, p \geq 1 \, : \, [x]^p < \infty \,\} \\
  H(x) &= 1/p(x) .
\end{align*}
If $x$ is a fractional Brownian motion with Hurst exponent $H$,
then $H(x) = H$ a.s..
To estimate the Hurst exponent from a finite sample,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute
  \[ W_p = \sum_i \dfrac{ \va{ \Delta_{Ki} x }^p }{ \displaystyle\sum\limits
    _{ j:\, [t^L_{j-1}, t^L_j] \inter [t^K_{i-1}, t^K_{i}] \neq \nothing }
    \va{ \Delta_{L j} x }^p } \]
  where the partition $\pi_L$ is a refinement of $\pi_K$,
\item Find $\hat p$ such that $W_{\hat p} = T$,
\item And let $\hat H = 1 / \hat p$.
\end{compactitem}
Realized volatility is rough ($H<\tfrac12$), even if instantaneous volatility
is not ($H=\tfrac12$):
roughness comes from market microstructure noise.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Error-correcting output codes with ensemble diversity for robust learning in neural networks}\\
  Y. Song et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Jointly train (end-to-end) a set of binary classifiers and the ECOC code matrix
to combine their outputs, maximizing
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The distance between rows (codewords)
\item And the shared information distance between columns (binary tasks),
  viz the variation of information
  \[ V(X,Y) = 2 H(X \wedge Y) - H(X) - H(Y) \]
\end{compactitem}
to mitigate adversarial attacks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time-aware language models\\ as temporal knowledge bases}\\
  B. Dhingra et al.
\end{book}
To make your language model aware of time
(some facts have an expiration date, e.g., the name of the president),
model $P_\theta[ \text{mask} | \text{context} , \text{time} ]$,
e.g., by prefixing each input with the date, ``\texttt{year: 2022}''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Density-preserving data visualization\\
  unveils dynamic patterns\\
  of single-cell transcriptomic variability}\\
  A. Narayam et al. (2020)
\end{book}
With $t$-SNE and UMAP,
the cluster sizes correspond to the number of observations
and ignore the density;
\emph{denSNE} and \emph{densMAP}
augment the objective with a differentiable measure of the total density,
to preserve the average distance to the nearest neighbours
\[ \dfrac{
  \sum_j p_{ij} \n{ x_i - x_j }^2 
}{
  \sum_j p_{ij}
}. \]
Available in \texttt{umap-learn}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Comparing clusterings -- an overview}\\
  S. Wagner and D. Wagner (2007)
\end{book}
Use an information-based measure, e.g., the normalized mutual information
\[ \text{NMI}_1( C_1, C_2 ) = \dfrac{ I( C_1 , C_2 ) }{ \sqrt{ H(C_1) H(C_2) } }. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The stochastic collocation Monte Carlo sampler:
  highly efficient sampling\\ from expensive distributions}\\
  L.A. Grzelak et al. (2016)
\end{book}
To sample from a distribution $F_Y$, one could use
\begin{align*}
  u & \sim \text{Unif} \\
  y &= F_Y^{-1}(u)
\end{align*}
or, more generally, for an auxilliary distribution $F_X$ (e.g., Gaussian)
\begin{align*}
  x &\sim F_X \\
  y &= F_Y^{-1} \bigl( F_X(x) \bigr) .
\end{align*}
If $F_Y^{-1}$ is expensive to compute,
stochastic collocation Monte Carlo (SCMC) approximates
$F_Y^{-1} \circ F_X$ with \emph{Lagrange polynomials}
on \emph{Gauss quadrature points}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Predicting value at risk for cryptocurrencies using generalized random forests}\\
  K. G\"orgen et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Random forests can be used for quantile regression:
change the splitting criterion to classify the observations wrt the quantile(s) of interest.
(Quantile regression forests only use quantile regression in the leaves,
without adjusting the splitting criterion.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A generalized precision matrix for t-Student distributions in portfolio optimization}\\
  K. Bax et al. (2022)
\end{book}
With a Gaussian random variable, the \emph{precision matrix}
(the inverse of the variance matrix) describes the conditional dependence structure;
with non-Gaussian data, this is no longer the case.
Alternatives to the precision matrix $\Omega = \Sigma^{-1}$ include:
\begin{align*}
  \Omega_{ij} &= - \E_x \left[ \dfrac{ \partial^2 }{ \partial x_i \partial x_j } \log f(x) \right] \\
  \Omega_{ij} &= - \E_{x_i, x_j} \left[
    \E_{ x_{\setminus ij} } \left[ \dfrac{ \partial^2 }{ \partial x_i \partial x_j } \log f(x) \right]
    \mathbf 1_{ x_i^2 + x_j^2 \geq t }
    \right]\\
  \Omega_{ij} &= \E_x \va{ \dfrac{ \partial^2 }{ \partial x_i \partial x_j } \log f(x) }
  % There is a fourth one, but the formula is too long
\end{align*}
Try them instead of $\Sigma^{-1}$ in portfolio optimization.
[Also check local Gaussian correlation.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Community detection\\ and portfolio optimization}\\
  L. Zhao et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Compute the correlation matrix from daily returns,
on a 500-day moving window;
build the PMFG (planar maximally filtered graph);
compute communities (infomap);
pick a stock at random (sic) in each community;
build an optimized portfolio from those stocks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Network diversification\\ for a robust portfolio allocation}\\
  M. Jaeger and D. Marinelli
\end{book}
Use the inverse of the degree (or eigen-centrality) on the PMFG as portfolio weights.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{LoCoV: low dimension covariance voting algorithm for portfolio optimization}\\
  J. Duan and I. Popescu
\end{book}
Compute the optimal portfolio on all subsets of $k$ assets,
and average the weights.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lazy network: a word embedding based temporal financial network to avoid economic shocks in asset pricing models}\\
  G. Adosoglou et al. (2022)
\end{book}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the similarity between the companies' 10K filings for year $t$;
\item Compute the absolute value of the difference between the similarities in years $t$ and $t-1$;
\item Invest in the periphery of the corresponding graph,
  \ie, companies with idiosyncratic changes.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A picture is worth a thousand words: measuring investor sentiment by combining machine learning and photos from news}\\
  K. Obaid and K. Pukthuanthong  
\end{book}
News sentiment can be extracted from news photos;
it contains the same information as the sentiment extracted from text.
Try a pretrained Inceptionv3, with a head fine-tuned on DeepSent (1000 images)
on data from the WSJ:
it can precit return and volume at 1~day and 1~week (with different signs).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Realized semibetas:\\ disentangling good and bad downside risks}\\
  T. Bollerslev et al.
\end{book}
Consider \emph{four} semibetas,
depending on the signs of the market and asset returns.
Only
\smash{$\beta_{\substack{
    \text{market} < 0 \\
    \rlap{$\scriptstyle\text{asset} > 0$}
    \hphantom{\text{market} < 0}
}}$}
and
$\beta_{\substack{
    \text{market} < 0 \\
    \rlap{$\scriptstyle\text{asset} < 0$}
    \hphantom{\text{market} < 0}
}}$
are priced.

The semibetas are defined from the decomposition
\begin{align*}
  \beta &= \dfrac{ \cov( r_{\text{market}} , r ) }{ \var r_{\text{market}} } \\
  &= \dfrac{ c_{++} + c_{--} - c_{+-} - c_{-+} }{ \var r_{\text{market}} } \\
  &= \beta_{++} + \beta_{--} - \beta_{+-} - \beta_{-+}.
\end{align*}
% statistical tests

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Investor attention and stock returns}\\
  J. Chen et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Combine 12 proxies of investor attention (aggregated over all stocks):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Abnormal trading volume (1m vs 12m);
\item Extreme returns (1m vs 12m);
\item Past returns (12m);
\item Nearness to the Dow 52-week high, and historical high;
\item Analyst coverage;
\item Change in advertising expenses;
\item Mutual fund inflow, and outflow;
\item Media coverage;
\item Google search volume;
\item Edgar search volume.
\end{compactitem}

Aggregate with
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item PCA,
\item \emph{scaled PCA} (sPCA)
  \begin{align*}
    \beta_i &= \beta( y \sim x_i ) \\
    A &= \text{PC}_1( \beta_1 x_1, \dots , \beta_n x_n ) \\
    y &: \text{future returns} \\
    x &: \text{proxies}
  \end{align*}
\item or \emph{partial least squares} (PLS)
  \begin{align*}
    \pi_i &= \beta( x_1 \sim y ) && \text{time series regressions} \\
    A_t &= \beta( x_t \sim \pi ) && \text{cross-sectional regressions.}
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Some applications of TDA\\ on financial markets}\\
  M.A. Ruiz-Ortiz et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Using
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Daily returns of 4 indices, for 15 days (15 points in 4-dimensional space),
\item or 4-dimensional delay embeddings of cryptocurrency returns, for 50 days (50 points)
\end{compactitem}
compute the persistence landscape $\lambda_t$
and $\var \n{ \lambda_t }_1$
or $\n{ \lambda_t }_1 + \va{ \n{ \lambda_t }_1 - \n{ \lambda_{t-1} }_1 }$
to detect crashes.

The persistent homology turbulence index (PHTI) is obtained by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Taking 60 days of industry log-returns (60 points in $k$-dimensional space),
  or a delay embedding of a single time series;
\item Computing the persistence diagram $P_t$,
  then the Wasserstein distances between $P_t$ and $P_{t-1}$,
  then a 60-day moving average,
  then the quantile over a 5-year window,
\item or, alternatively, by computing the persistence landscape
  and $\n{ \lambda_{t-\tau} - \lambda_t }_2$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tail-GAN: non-parametric scenario generation for tail risk estimation}\\
  R. Cont et al. (2022)
\end{book}
To generate scenarios to compute the VaR or the ES, train a GAN,
whose discriminator evaluates the quality of VaR and ES estimates (sorting is a.e. differentiable)
using a \emph{scoring function}
(the pair (VaR,ES) is \emph{elicitable} -- the formula is complicated)
on a set of static and \emph{dynamic} trading strategies.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sensitivity measures\\ based on scoring functions}\\
  T. Fissler and S.M. Pesenti (2022)
\end{book}
A statistic $T$ is \emph{elicitable} if there is a scoring function $S$ such that
$T(X) = \argmin_t \E\bigl[ S(t,X) \bigr]$.
For instance,
mean $S(\mu,x) = (\mu-x)^2$,
median $S(m,x) = \va{m-x}$,
VaR$_\alpha$,
or the pair $(\text{VaR}_\alpha, \text{ES}_\alpha)$ are elicitable;
for each of those, there is actually a (known) family of scoring functions.

The sensitivity of $T$ wrt information $Y$ is
\[ \dfrac{ \E[S] - \E[ S | Y ] }{ \E[ S ] }. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-objective reward generalization:\\
  improving performance of deep reinforcement learning for selected applications\\ in stock and cryptocurrency trading}\\
  S. Cornalba and C. Disselkamp 
\end{book}
If the reward is a vector rather than a scalar
(e.g., $(\mu, \mu/\sigma)$),
optimize a random positive linear combination of rewards, resampled at each step.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Weisfeiler and Lehman go cellular:\\ CW networks}\\
\end{book}
The Weisfeiler-Lehman isomorphism test can be generalized to simplicial of CW complexes
(\ie, cells with arbitrary shapes). A cell has 4 types of neighbours:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Boundary adjacent: cells on the boundary, one dimension smaller;
\item Coboundary adjacent: cells on the boundary, one dimension larger;
\item Lower adjacent cells:
  cells of the same dimension, sharing a lower-dimensional cell on their boundary;
\item Upper adjacent cells: cells of the same dimension, on the boundary of the same higher dimensional cell.
\end{compactitem}
Coboundary and lower adjacencies can be omitted without affecting the expressive power of the test.

A graph can be lifted to a CW complex in many ways,
e.g., the \emph{clique complex}
(each $(k+1)$-clique becomes a $k$-cell),
limited to cliques of size at most $n$,
or the \emph{cycle complex}.

Message-passing GNNs can be generalized to those lifted graphs.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A reverse expected shortfall\\ optimization formula}\\
  Y. Guan et al. (2022)
\end{book}
To minimize the expected shortfall
\begin{align*}
  \text{ES}_\alpha &= \dfrac1{ 1 - \alpha } \int_\alpha^1 \text{VaR}_\beta(X) d\beta\\
  &= \min_{t\in\RR} t + \dfrac{ 1 }{ 1 - \alpha }\E \bigl[ (X-t)_+ \bigr],
\end{align*}
it suffices to minimize
a linear function plus the excess mean loss (for each $t$).

Conversely,
\[ \E \bigl[ (X-t)_+ \bigr] =
\max_{ \alpha \in [0,1] }
(1-\alpha) \bigl[ \text{ES}_\alpha(X) - t \bigr]
\]
is useful in worst-case (\ie, robust) optimization.

These formulas generalize to optimized certainty equivalent (OCE) risk measures of the form
\[ R[X] = \int_{t\in\RR} t + \dfrac1\beta \E\bigl[ v(X-t) \bigr] . \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved iterative methods\\ for solving risk parity portfolio}\\
  J. Choi and R. Chen (2022)
\end{book}
% Clear, straightforward to implement

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Doubly truncated moment risk measures\\ for elliptical distributions}\\
  B. Zuo and C. Yin (2022)
\end{book}
The \emph{tail conditional moments} are
\begin{align*}
  \text{TCM}_{q,n}(X) &= \E \bigl[ (X-\text{TCE}_q X )^n \bigm| X > x_q \bigr] \\
  \text{TCE}_q(X) &= \E\bigl[ X \bigm| X > x_q \bigr] \\
  x_q &= \text{$q$-th quantile}.
\end{align*}
For the doubly truncated moments, replace the condition
$X > x_q$ with
$x_p < X < x_q$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate doubly truncated moments\\
  for generalized skew-elliptical distributions\\
  with applications to multivariate\\
  tail conditional risk measures}\\
  B. Zuo and C. Yin (2022)
\end{book}
For many multivariate distributions, one can compute
$\E[ Y | a \leq Y \leq b ]$
and $\E[ YY' | a \leq Y \leq b ]$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RPS: portfolio asset selection using graph-based representation learning}\\
  M.A. Fazli et al.
\end{book}
In portfolio optimization, replace the correlation matrix
with a similarity matrix computed from the distance matrix of a node embedding (Node2Vec(
computed from the complete graph with correlations as weights.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=.5,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  ]
\node (C) at (0,1) {correlation};
\node (D) at (-1,0) {distances};
\node (E) at (0,-1) {embedding};
\node (G) at (1,0) {graph};
\draw[->] (C.east)  to[out=0,    in=120] (G.north);
\draw[->] (G.south) to[out=-120, in=0  ] (E.east);
\draw[->] (E.west)  to[out=180,  in=-60] (D.south);
\draw[->] (D.north) to[out=60,   in=180] (C.west);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Connecting Sharpe ratio and Student $t$-statistic, and beyond}\\
  E. Benhamou 
\end{book}
If $X \sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)$,
the $\widehat{\text{SR}} = \hat \mu / \hat \sigma$ is (non-centered) Student.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The statistics of Sharpe ratios}\\
  A.W.  Lo (2002)
\end{book}
For iid returns,
\[ \sigma( \widehat{\text{SR}} ) = \sqrt{ \dfrac{ 1 + \tfrac12 \text{SR} }{ T } }. \]
For non-iid returns,
annual and monthly Sharpe ratios are related by ($q=12$)
\[ \dfrac{ \text{SR}(q) }{ \text{SR} } = \dfrac{ q }{
  \sqrt{
    q + 2 \sum\limits_{k=1}^{q-1} (q-k) \rho_k
  }
}. \]
In particular, for AR(1) returns,
\[ \dfrac{ \text{SR}(q) }{ \text{SR} } =
\sqrt q
\left[
  1 + \dfrac{ 2 \rho }{ 1 - \rho }
  \left(
  1 - \dfrac{ 1 - \rho^q }{ q (1-\rho) }
  \right)
\right] ^{-1/2}.
\]
The variance of $\widehat{\text{SR}}$ increases by
\[
\dfrac{
  \var \widehat{\text{SR}}(q)
}{
  \var \widehat{\text{SR}}
} = 1 + \dfrac{
  2 \sum\limits_{ j=1 }^{ q-1 } \left( 1 - j/q \right)^2
}{
  1 + 2 / \text{SR}^2
}. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-portfolio internal rebalancing processes}\\
  K. Francis-Staite (2022)
\end{book}
The (internal) rebalancing problem
\begin{align*}
  i   &\text{: asset class} \\
  j   &\text{: portfolio} \\
  a_i &\text{: value of asset class $i$} \\
  p_j &\text{: value of portfolio $j$} \\
  M_{ij} &\text{: desired proportion of asset class $i$ in portfolio $j$} \\
\end{align*}
where $\sum_i M_{ij} = 1$, $\sum a_i = \sum p_j$,
looks for $A$, as close to $M$ as possible (in some sense),
such that $\sum_i A_{ij} = 1$ and $Ap = a$
(one may add a constraint $\forall ij \ M_{ij}=0 \Rightarrow A_{ij}=0$).

In \emph{banker rebalancing}, all the portfolios except one (the banker) are given
their target allocation;
the banker receives the remaining funds.
In \emph{linear rebalancing},
each asset class is considered separately,
and the over-/under-weights are distributed equally
(not in proportion of the weight of the portfolio).
\emph{Market-invariant rebalancing} is more equitable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling bid and ask price dynamics\\
  with an extended Hawkes process\\
  and its empirical applications\\
  for high-frequency stock market data}\\
  K. Lee and B.K. Seo
\end{book}
Four-dimensional Hawkes process,
for the up and down bid and ask movements
\[ \lambda_t = \mu_t + \int_{-\infty}^t e^{ - \beta ( t - u ) } A_u d N_u \]
where $\mu_t$ and $A_t$ are observed (or $A \in \RR^{4\times4}$ constant).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-asset spot and option market simulation}\\
  M. Wiese et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Do not directly generate a grid of call prices (for various expiries and strikes)
but a grid of \emph{discrete local volatilities} (DLV)
\begin{align*}
  \sigma_{tk} &= \sqrt{ \dfrac{ \theta_{tk} }{ \tfrac12 k^2 \Delta t \Gamma_{tk} } } \\
  \theta_{tk} &= C_{ t + \Delta t , k } - C_{t,k} \approx \partial _t C_{tk} \\
  \Gamma_{tk} &\approx \partial_{kk} C_{tk}
\end{align*}
to enforce the ``no static arbitrage'' constraint.

Reduce the dimension of the DLV grid (from 26 to 3)
with an auto-encoder
$\sigma \mapsto x \mapsto \sigma$.

Train a conditional model $x_{t+1} | x_t, x_{t-1}$
with a normalizing flow
\begin{align*}
  u_{t+1} &\sim U(0,1)^3 \\
  x_{t+1} &\leftarrow f( u_{t+1} ; x_t , x_{t-1} ).
\end{align*}

The result still has a drift: it is not a martingale.

For several assets, sample $u_{t+1}$ from a Gaussian copula
with a block structure (or a non-parametric, flow-based copula).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk-neutral market simulation}\\
  M. Wiese and P. Murray (2022)
\end{book}
Remove the drift by reweighting the samples
(the weights are the solution of a convex optimization problem).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lazy prices}\\
  L. Cohen et al (2019)
\end{book}
Changes in 10K reports have a (negative) predictive power
(earnings, profitability, news, bankruptcies),
especially the ``risk factor'' section
and statements about the executive team (CEO, CFO) and litigation.

