\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to causal inference}\\
  B. Neal (2020)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.} Simpson's paradox shows that omitting important variables can lead to
the opposite conclusion:
for instance, if two treatments are available for some medical condition,
a non-invasive and an invasive one, the former used mostly for mild conditions
and the latter for more severe ones, the invasive one can appear worse
on the whole population, even though it is better on both sub-populations,
simply because it tends to be used mostly on difficult patients.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.5,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \node (T) at (0,0) {Treatment};
  \node (Y) at (2,0) {Outcome};
  \node (W) at (1,1) {Condition};
  \draw[->] (T) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (T);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

But a different causal graph, e.g.,
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0]
  \node (T) at (-.5,1) {Treatment};
  \node (Y) at (2,0) {Outcome};
  \node (W) at (1,1) {Condition};
  \draw[->] (T) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (T) -- (W);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
(if the invasive treatment is administered with delays,
letting the patient's condition worsen),
the same numbers lead to the opposite conclusion.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.5,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0,
  every text node part/.style={align=center}]
  \node (T) at (0,0) {Sleeping with\\your shoes on};
  \node (Y) at (2,0) {Waking up with\\a headache};
  \node (W) at (1,1) {Binge drinking};
  \draw[->] (T) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (T);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

% 2. Potential outcomes
\textbf{2.} The \d{potential outcomes} $Y_i(0)$ and $Y_i(1)$
are the values of the outcome $Y$ for subject $i$ if it does not ($T=0$)
or does ($T=1$) receive the treatment.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.8,yscale=.8,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0, text width=1em,
    every node/.style={circle,draw},
  every text node part/.style={align=center}
  ]
  \node[draw] (T) at (0,0) {T};
  \node (Y) at (2,0) {Y};
  \node (W) at (1,1) {X};
  \draw[->] (T) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (T);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

We only observe one of them,
\[
\begin{array}{cccccc}
  i & T & Y & Y(1) & Y(0) & Y(1)-Y(0) \\
  \hline
  1 & 0 & 0 & ? & 0 & ? \\
  2 & 1 & 1 & 1 & ? & ? \\
  3 & 1 & 0 & 0 & ? & ? \\
  4 & 0 & 0 & ? & 0 & ? \\
  5 & 0 & 1 & ? & 1 & ? \\
  6 & 1 & 1 & 1 & ? & ?
\end{array}
\]
and we are interested in their differences,
the \emph{indidual treatment effect}
\[ \text{ITE}_i = Y_i(1) - Y_i(0) \]
or the \emph{average treatment effect}
\[ \text{ATE}_i = \E[ Y_i(1) - Y_i(0) ]. \]
In general, it is different from the \emph{associational difference},
which is easy to compute.
\[ \text{ATE} \neq \E[ Y | T = 1 ] - \E[ Y | T = 0 ] \]
They are equal if the \emph{ignorability} (or \emph{exchangeability}) assumption is satisfied:
\[ Y(0), Y(1) \indep T. \]

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.8,yscale=.8,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0, text width=1em,
    every node/.style={circle,draw},
  every text node part/.style={align=center}
  ]
  \node[draw] (T) at (0,0) {T};
  \node (Y) at (2,0) {Y};
  \node (W) at (1,1) {X};
  \draw[->] (T) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

(We can ignore how the subjects were selected for the treatment;
the treated and the control groups are comparable).
This is the case for \emph{randomized control trials} (RCT).
More generally, if the \emph{unconfoundedness} assumption is satisfied
\[ Y(0) , Y(1) \indep T \,|\, X, \]

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.8,yscale=.8,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0, text width=1em,
  every node/.style={circle,draw},
  every text node part/.style={align=center}
  ]
  \node (T) at (0,0) {T};
  \node (Y) at (2,0) {Y};
  \node[fill=black!25] (W) at (1,1) {X};
  \draw[->] (T) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (W) -- (T);
  \node[above = 3pt of W,draw=none,rectangle,inner sep=-2pt] (b) {\color{blue!90!black}\vrule width 2pt height 1.5ex};
  \draw[dashed,very thick,blue!90!black] (T) to[out=90,in=210] (b.west);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
we can compute the ATE with the \d{adjustment formula}
\begin{align*}
  \text{ATE}
  &= \E[ Y(1) - Y(0) ] \\
  &= \E\nolimits_X \bigl[ \, \E[ Y \,|\, T=1, X ] - \E[ Y \,|\, T=0 , X ] \, \bigr].
\end{align*}
A few more assumptions are actually needed:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Unconfoundedness;
\item Positivity (overlap):
  the supports of $X\,|\,T=0$ and $X\,|\,T=1$ are the same, \ie,
  all subgroups of data appear both in $[T=1]$ and $[T=0]$;
\item Consistency: no multiple versions of the treatment;
\item No interference: $Y_i(t_i)$ does not depend on $t_j$, $j\neq i$.
\end{compactitem}

Causal inference has two steps:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Identification}, \ie, converting a \emph{causal estimand}
  (e.g., the ATE) into a \emph{statistical estimand} (e.g., the adjustment formula);
\item \emph{Estimation}, \ie, computing the statistical estimand from the data
  (this often involves some statistical of machine learning model).
\end{compactitem}

% 3. Flow of association and causation
\textbf{3.} A \emph{Bayesian network} is a DAG
encoding a joint probability distribution as
\[ P(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \prod_i P( x_i \,|\, \text{pa}_i ). \]
In particular, $X_i$ is independent of its non-descendants given its parents
(\emph{local Markov assumption}).
If adjacent nodes are dependent, the DAG is \emph{minimal}.

Association flows along \emph{chains}, \emph{forks}
and conditioned \d{immoralities} (\emph{colliders}, \emph{$v$-structures})
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.8,yscale=.8,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0, text width=.5em,
  every node/.style={circle,draw},
  every text node part/.style={align=center}
  ]
