GNNs can be generalized to multirelational graphs, \ie,
graph with several edge types (or edge features).

Graph pooling is needed for graph-level predictions:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sum or mean aggregation of the node embeddings
\item Attention with LSTM:
  \begin{align*}
    q &\leftarrow \textsc{lstm} ( o, q ) \\
    e_v &\leftarrow f( z_v , q ) \\
    a_v &\propto \exp e_v \\
    o &= \sum a_v z_v
  \end{align*}
\item Graph coarsening
  \begin{align*}
    A &\leftarrow S' A S \\
    X &\leftarrow S' X
  \end{align*}
  for some cluster assignment matrix $S$.
\end{compactitem}

% 6. GNNs in practice
\textbf{6.}
Pretraining with neighbourhood reconstruction loss does not work well.
Try to maximize the mutual information between node embeddings and graph embeddings:
\[ - \E_{\text{data}} \log D( z_u, z_G ) -
\E_{\substack{\text{corrupted}\\\text{data}}} \log \bigl(
1 - D( \tilde z_u , z_G ) \bigr) . \]
For deep networks, use gated updates.

% 7. Theoretical motivations
\textbf{7.}
A GNN layer is a convolution:
it can be expressed without message passing,
with the adjacency matrix $A$,
or $A_{\text{sym}} = D^{-1/2} A D^{-1/2}$,
or some Laplacian,
$L=D-A$,
$L_{\text{sym}} = D^{-1/2} L D^{-1/2}$
\begin{align*}
  Z &= \textsc{mlp} \bigl( f(A) \, \textsc{mlp}(X) \bigr) \\
  \intertext{with, e.g., }
  f(A) &= \sum \bigl( I - \alpha \tilde A \bigr)^k \\
  \tilde A &= ( D + I)^{-1/2} (A+I) (D+I)^{-1/2}.
\end{align*}

GNNs reproduce the operations in the Weisfieler-Lehman graph isomorphism test
(which cannot distinguish between
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.25,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0,
    every node/.style={circle,fill},
]
  \node (A) at (0,1) {};
  \node (B) at (1,1) {};
  \node (C) at (2,1) {};
  \node (D) at (2,0) {};
  \node (E) at (1,0) {};
  \node (F) at (0,0) {};
  \draw (A) -- (C) -- (D) -- (F) -- (A);
\end{tikzpicture}
and
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.25,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=0,
    every node/.style={circle,fill},
]
  \node (A) at (0,1) {};
  \node (B) at (1,1) {};
  \node (C) at (2,1) {};
  \node (D) at (2,0) {};
  \node (E) at (1,0) {};
  \node (F) at (0,0) {};
  \draw (A) -- (B) -- (F) -- (A);
  \draw (C) -- (D) -- (E) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
-- this is a strong limitation,
and breaking it is an open problem).

% 8. Traditional graph generation: Erdös-Rényi, SBM, preferential attachment

% 9. Deep generative models
\textbf{9.}
Variational graph autoencoders generate graphs
from node embeddings with a dot product decoder:
\begin{align*}
  \mu, \sigma &= \text{GNN}(A, X) \\
  z &\sim N(\mu, \sigma) \\
  P[ A_{ij} - 1 ] &= \sigma( z_i ' z_j ).
\end{align*}
They are good for reconstruction,
but not for graph generation.
A graph-level VAE uses a graph (not node) embedding
and generates an adjacency matrix in one go:
$ A = \sigma\bigl( \textsc{mlp}( z_G ) \bigr)$;
the nodes of the generated graph need to be matched to the original ones,
e.g., with a heuristic ordering.

Autoregressive decoders generate the rows (of the lower triangular part) of $A$
one by one, each conditioned on the previous ones,
as with the preferential attachment model
