Predictors include carbon markets, equity index, FX, energy (oil, etc.),
futures, industrial indices, air quality.
% They use the *levels* of those variables!

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cryptocurrency valuation:\\ an explainable AI approach}\\
  Y. Liu and L. Zhang
\end{book}
``Fundamental ratios'' for crypto currencies, such as miners' earnings/price,
market value/traded value, market value/number of users$^2$,
are bad predictors of future returns.
Instead, the utility/price ratio has some predictive power on long-term returns.
\begin{align*}
  \text{utility} &= \dfrac{
    \text{velocity} \times \text{staking ratio}
  }{
    \text{volatility} \times \text{dilution rate}
  } \\
  \text{velocity} &= \dfrac{
    \text{tokens traded (past day)}
  }{
    \text{tokens available}
  } \\
  \text{staking ratio} &= \dfrac{
    \text{inactive tokens (past year)}
  }{
    \text{tokens available}
  } \\
  \text{dilution rate} &=
  \text{token supply annual growth rate}  
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Price impact of order flow imbalance: multi-level, cross-sectional and forecasting}\\
  R. Cont et al. (2021)
\end{book}
The order flow imbalance (OFI) is the difference between:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The change in bid volume at level $m$ and
\item The change in ask volume at level $m$;
\end{compactitem}
normalize by dividing with the volume in levels up to $m$;
aggregate by taking the first principal component of
ofi$_1,\dots,$ofi$_{10}$;
use to explain contemporaneous returns,
or forecast future returns;
also try to use the ofi$_m$ or PC$_1$(ofi) of other stocks (lasso).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Analysis of a five-factor capital market model}\\
  S.F. Jarner and M. Preisel (2017)
\end{book}
The Munk capital market model is
\begin{align*}
  dr &= \kappa ( \bar r - r ) dt + \sigma dW && \text{short interest rate} \\
  dS/S &= (r+x) dt + \sigma dW && \text{stock index} \\
  dx &= \alpha (\bar x - x ) dt + \sigma dW && \text{equity risk premium} \\
  dI/I &= \pi dt + \sigma dW && \text{price index (inflation)} \\
  d\pi &= \beta( \bar \pi - \pi ) dt + \sigma dW && \text{expected inflation}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal portfolio choice and stock centrality for tail risk events}\\
  C. Katsouris (2021)
\end{book}
Replace the variance matrix with the VaR matrix:
\begin{align*}
  & P[ R_i \leq \text{VaR}_i^\alpha ] = \alpha \\
  & P [ R_i \leq \text{CoVaR}_{ij}^\alpha \,|\, R_j \leq \text{VaR}_j^\alpha ] = \alpha \\
  & \Delta \text{CoVaR}_{ij} = \text{CoVaR}_{ij} - \text{VaR}_{ij} \\
  & V_{ii} = \text{VaR}^+_i \\
  & V_{ij} = \sqrt{ \text{VaR}_i^+ \text{VaR}_j^+ } \Delta \text{CoVaR}_{ij} \\
  & \tilde V = \tfrac12 ( V + V' ) 
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal trend following portfolios}\\
  S. Valeyre (2022)
\end{book}
To estimate the trend, also use inter-asset cross-correlations.
The maximum Sharpe portfolio is then
$ w \approx C^{-1} ( \alpha C_\xi + \beta \mu \mu' ) C^{-1} , $
where $C$ is the variance matrix of the asset returns, and $C_\xi$ that of the stochastic trend.
\begin{align*}
  X_{it} &= \mu_i + \sum_{s<t} S_{t-s} \xi_{is} + \epsilon_{it} \\
  C_t &= \var X_{\bigcdot , t } \qquad C_{\xi t} = \var \xi_{\bigcdot , t}
\end{align*}
The agnostic risk parity portfolio is
the risk parity portfolio on the first eigenvectors of the variance matrix.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{From tether to libra: stablecoins,\\ digital currency and the future of money}\\
  A. Lipton et al.
\end{book}
Stable coins are often classified according to their collateral
(fiat-, commodity-, crypto- or un-collateralized).
Instead, look if the ability to redeem the coin is guaranteed by
a legal framework, technology, or not guaranteed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Combining reinforcement learning\\ and inverse reinforcement learning\\ for asset allocation recommendations}\\
  I. Halperin et al.
\end{book}
IRL learns a reward function from trajectories.
T-REX is an IRL algorithm learning the reward function
from non-optimal trajectories:
for this, it relies on extra information, viz a ranking of all trajectories.

Combine RL and IRL:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Learn the reward function of a group of portfolio managers
  (following the same type of strategy);
\item Solve the corresponding RL problem, to improve their decisions.
\end{compactitem}
To deal with the paucity of data, aggressively reduce the dimension
to sector weights
(for the benchmark and the strategies).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Limiting spectral distribution of high-dimensional Hayashi-Yoshida estimator of integrated covariance matrix}\\
  A. Chakrabarti and R. Sen (2022)
\end{book}
The Hayashi-Yoshida estimator of the integrated covariance matrix
\[ \Sigma_{HY}^{X,Y} = \sum_{ij} \Delta X_i \cdot \Delta Y_j \cdot \mathbf 1_{ I_i^X \inter I_j^Y \neq \nothing } \]
is inconsistent and unreliable in high dimension.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tone at the top?\\ Quantifying management presentation}\\
  Wolfe Research (2018)
\end{book}
Extract the following features from call transcripts:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Readability
\item Tone (Harvard-IV, Loughran-McDonald, Vader)
\item POS (proportion of adjectives, adverbs (subjectivity), first-person singular vs plural pronouns)
\item Ratio of digits to letters
\item Number of analysts participating
\item Topics (LDA), after removing sector-specific words or phrases
  (otherwise, the topics just identify the sectors)
\item Change in those features.
\end{compactitem}
and forecast future returns or earnings surprises.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Text mining form 8-K disclosures}\\
  Wolfe Research (2020)
\end{book}
Companies need to report ``material information''
withing 4 days in SEC form 8-K.
Forecast future returns (event study) with:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Filing frequency;
\item Positive and negative sentiment;
\item Readability;
\item Proportion of numbers in the text;
\item Filing delay;
\item Event abnormal return;
\item Event type (SEC classification).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Network of economically-linked firms}\\
  Wolfe Research (2021)
\end{book}
Use the average momentum ($k$-month, or $k$-month minus 1-month)
of nearby firms to forecast future returns,
where ``nearby'' is determined by:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Business segment
\item Geographic exposure (US, CA, EU, UK, ANZ, AxJ, JP, CN, EMEA, LATAM, rest);
\item Number of sell-side analysts in common;
\item Key executives
\item Patent citations;
\item Supply chain (bipartite graph of landlords and tenants for REITs in the Russell 3000);
\item Shipping network.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{NLP 5G Evolution:\\ text mining global corporate filings}\\
  Wolfe Research (2019)
\end{book}
Compute the following features on 10K/10Q filings:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Conformity to the SEC's requirements;
\item YoY or QoQ similarity with the previous one
  (cosine similarity on bags of words,
  [try (sliced) Wasserstein distance]);
\item POS (adjectives, adverbs, pronouns).
\end{compactitem}
Forecast future returns with an ensemble of elastic net and xgboost.

For international companies, build a multilingual sentiment lexicon.

For languages with no spaces:
\texttt{jieba} (Chinese), \texttt{tinysegmenter} (Japanese),
\texttt{konlpy} (Korean).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Patent, innovation and alpha}\\
  Wolfe Research (2019)
\end{book}
Innovative industries (more than 50\% of the companies have patents)
outperform.

Define the R\&D intensity as
R\&D/Sales, or log(R\&D/MCap),
or the residuals of log(R\&D)$\sim$log(MCap).

Compute features from patents:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Innovation intensity: number of patents in a 3-year window;
\item Originality: (effective) number of patent classes or subclasses;
\item Number of citations, back-citations, number of unique assignees in citations or back-citations,
  divided by the number of patents;
\item Number of foreign applications;
\item Maintenance fee non-payment;
\item HITS, PageRank.
\end{compactitem}
Adjust those factors for size (regress against log(MCap)).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal expected utility risk measures}\\
  S. Geissel et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Risk measures defined from a utility function include:
\begin{align*}
  \rho^{\text{SR}}(X) &= \inf \{ \, \eta \,:\, \E[u(X+\eta)] \geq q \, \} \\
  \rho^{\text{CE}}(X) &= - \text{CE}[X] = - u^{-1} \E[ u(X) ] \\
  \rho^{\text{OCE}}(X) &= - \sup_\eta - \eta + \E[ u(X+\eta) ] \\
  \rho^{\text{OEU}}(X) &= - \sup_\eta -\eta + \alpha \text{CE}[ X+\eta ] \qquad \alpha \in (0,1)
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep quantile and\\ deep composite model regression}\\
  T. Fissler et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Quantile regression, for several quantiles,
can be fitted with a neural network,
with constraints to ensure the quantiles are in the correct order.
This can be generalized to ``ES regression'':
the expected shortfall (ES) is not elicitable
(not an M-estimator),
but the triplet (quantile, lower ES, upper UE) is.
\[ \biggl(
F^{-1}(\tau) ,\ 
\dfrac1\tau \int_0^\tau \! F^{-1} ,\ 
\dfrac1{1-\tau} \int_\tau^1 \! F^{-1}
\biggr) \]
% R code available

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{2T-POT Hawkes model\\ for dynamic left- and right-tail\\ quantile forecasts of financial returns
  %: out-of-sample validation of self-exciting extremes versus conditional volatility
  }\\
  M.F. Tomlinson et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Model quantiles of a time series with a (2-tailed) peaks-over-threshold Hawkes model
(Hawkes model with generalized Pareto excess magnitudes --
the GPD parameters are constant)
rather than a GARCH model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multinomial backtesting\\ of distortion risk measures}\\
  S. Bettels et al. (2022)
\end{book}
The \emph{distorsion risk measure}
defined by a decreasing $g: [0,1] \twoheadrightarrow [0,1]$ is
\begin{align*}
  \rho_g(X) &=
  \int_{-\infty} ^ 0 \bigl( g(P[X>x]) - 1 \bigr) dx
  + \int_0^\infty g ( P[X>x] ) dx \\
  &= \int_0^1 q_X^+(1-u) d g(u) \\
  &= \int_0^1 F_X^{-1} (1-u) dg(u) \\
  q_X^+(u) &= \sup \{ x \,:\, F_X(u) \leq u \}.
\end{align*}
Examples include:
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.25,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  %every node/.style={circle,draw},
  every text node part/.style={align=center}
  ]
  \draw[->] (-.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
  \draw[->] (0,-.5) -- (0,3.5);
  \draw[ultra thick,blue!80!black] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (1,3) -- (3,3);
  \node at (1.5,-1.5) {VaR};
  \begin{scope}[shift={(8,0)}]
    \draw[->] (-.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
    \draw[->] (0,-.5) -- (0,3.5);
    \draw[ultra thick,blue!80!black] (0,0) -- (1,3) -- (3,3);
    \node at (1.5,-1.5) {AVaR};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[shift={(16,0)}]
    \draw[->] (-.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
    \draw[->] (0,-.5) -- (0,3.5);
    \draw[ultra thick,blue!80!black] (0,0) -- (.8,1.5) -- (2.2,2) |- (3,3);
    \node at (1.5,-1.5) {GlueVaR};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\begin{align*}
  g(u) &= 1 - (1-u)^n && \text{MinVaR: } \E[\max(X_1,\dots,X_n)] \\
  g(u) &= u^{1/n} && \text{MaxVaR} \\
  g(u) &= 1 - ( 1 - u^{1/n} ) ^n && \text{MinMaxVaR} \\
  g(u) &= \bigl( 1 - (1-u) ^n \bigr )^{1/n} && \text{MaxMinVaR} \\
  &\text{etc.}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Decomposable sums and their implications\\ on naturally quasiconvex risk measures}\\
  \c C. Ararat et al. (2022)
\end{book}
A risk measure is \emph{naturally convex} if
\[ \forall X,Y \quad \forall \lambda \quad \exists \mu \quad
\rho\bigl( \lambda X + (1-\lambda) Y \bigr) \leq \mu \rho(X) + (1-\mu) \rho(Y).
\]
The \emph{convexity index} of $f: \RR^m \to \bar\RR$
is
\begin{align*}
  c(f) &= \sup \{ \lambda < 0    \ :\ r_\lambda \text{ convex} \} \\
  \text{or }
  c(f) &= \sup \{ \lambda \geq 0 \ :\ r_\lambda \text{ concave} \}
\end{align*}
where $r_\lambda = e^{ - \lambda f(x) }$.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5, yscale=.25,inner sep=0pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0,
  %every node/.style={circle,draw},
  every text node part/.style={align=center}
  ]
  \draw[->] (-6,0) -- (3,0);
  \draw[line width=4pt, black!20] (-6,.5) -- (-4,.5);
  \draw[line width=4pt, black!20] (-2,.5) -- (-.1,.5);
  \node at (-5,1.3) {\scriptsize convex};
  \node at (-1,1.3) {\scriptsize not convex};
  \draw (0,-.5) -- (0,.5);
  \node[anchor=north] at (0,-.5) {\scriptsize 0};
  \node[anchor=south west] at (3,0) {\scriptsize $\lambda$};
  \begin{scope}[shift={(0,-4)}]
    \draw[->] (-6,0) -- (3,0);
    \draw[line width=4pt, black!20] (-6,.5) -- (-.1,.5);
    \draw[line width=4pt, black!20] (.1,.5) -- (2,.5);
    \node at (1,1.3) {\scriptsize concave};
    \node at (-3,1.3) {\scriptsize convex};
    \draw (0,-.5) -- (0,.5);
    \node[anchor=north] at (0,-.5) {\scriptsize 0};
    \node[anchor=south west] at (3,0) {\scriptsize $\lambda$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

A function of the form
\[ s(x_1,\dots,x_n) = f_1(x_1) + \cdots + f_n(x_n) \]
is quasi-convex iff
\[ c(f_1) + \cdots + c(f_n) \geq 0, \]
iff either all the $f_i$'s are convex,
or at most one is non-convex and
\[ \dfrac1{ c(f_1) } + \cdots + \dfrac1{ c(f_n) } \leq 0. \]
If the $f_i$'s are convex,
\[ \dfrac1{c(s)} = \sum \dfrac1{ c(f_i) }. \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model aggregation for risk evaluation\\ and robust optimization}\\
  T. Mao et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Given a risk measure
$\rho : \mathscr M \to \RR$
and an uncertainty set $\mathscr F \subset \mathscr M$,
the worst case risk is
\[ \rho^{\text{WR}} = \sup _{ F \in \mathscr F } \rho(F). \]
Instead, given an order relation $\preccurlyeq$ on $\mathscr M$,
e.g., first or second order dominance,
\begin{align*}
  F \preccurlyeq_1 G & \text{ iff } \forall u \text{ increasing } \int u dF \leq \int u dG \\
  F \preccurlyeq_2 G & \text{ iff } \forall u \text{ increasing convex } \int u dF \leq \int u dG
\end{align*}
one can consider the \emph{model aggregation risk}
\[ \rho^{\text{MA}}( \mathscr F ) = \rho \biggl( \bigvee_{F \in \mathscr F} F \biggr). \]
For $\preccurlyeq_1$ and $\preccurlyeq_2$, the supremum is easy to compute:
\begin{align*}
  \bigvee\nolimits_{\!\!1} \mathscr F &= \inf_{ F \in \mathscr F } F && \text{cdf} \\
  \biggl( \bigvee\nolimits_{\!\!1} \mathscr F \biggr)^{-1} &=
  \sup_{ F \in \mathscr F } F^{-1} && \text{icdf} \\
  \pi_{ \bigvee\nolimits_{\!2} \mathscr F } &=
  \sup_{ F \in \mathscr F } \pi_F && \hspace*{-1cm}\text{integrated survival function} \\
  \bigvee\nolimits_{\!\!2} \mathscr F &= 1 + \biggl( \sup_{ F \in \mathscr F } \pi_F \biggr)^{\!\!\prime}_+ 
\end{align*}
where the integrated survival function is
\[ \pi_F = \E_{ X \sim F } \bigl[ (X-x) _+ \bigr] = \int_x^\infty \bigl( 1 - F(t) \bigr) dt. \]

Under reasonable assumptions on $\mathscr F$,
\begin{align*}
  \text{VaR}_\alpha^{ \text{MA}, \preccurlyeq_1} &= \text{VaR}_\alpha^{\text{WR}} \\
  \text{ES}_\alpha^{ \text{MA}, \preccurlyeq_2} &= \text{ES}_\alpha^{\text{WR}}.
\end{align*}
Other risk measures include
\begin{align*}
  \text{RVaR}_{\alpha,\beta}(F) &= \dfrac1{\alpha-\beta} \int_\alpha^\beta \text{VaR}_s(F) ds
  &&\text{range VaR} \\
  \text{PD}_k(F) &= \int_0^1 k s^{k-1} \text{VaR}_s(F) ds && \text{power distorsion} 
\end{align*}
or the \emph{expectile}, defined as the unique solution $t = \text{ex}_\alpha(F)$ of
\[ \alpha \E_{ X \sim F } \bigl[ (X-t)_+ \bigr] = (1-\alpha) \E_{ X \sim F } \bigl[ (X-t)_- \bigr]. \]

Common uncertainty sets include
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The Wasserstein ball around $F_0$:
  \begin{align*}
    \!\!\!\! W_p(F,F_0) &= \biggl( \int_0^1 \va{ F^{-1}(s) - F_0^{-1}(s) }^p ds \biggr)^{1/p} \ \ \text{dim=1} \\
    \!\!\!\! W_p(F,F_0) &= \inf_{\substack{ X \sim F \\ Y \sim F_0 }} \bigl( \E \n{ X - Y }_a^p \bigr)^{1/p}
  \end{align*}
\item The mean-variance uncertainty set
  \[ \mathscr F_{\mu, \sigma} = \{ F \in \mathscr M \ :\
  \E[F] = \mu \text{ and } \var[F] = \sigma^2 \}
  \]
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Influence functions\\ for risk and performance estimators}\\
  S. Zhang et al. (2020)
\end{book}
The functional of an estimator $\hat \theta$ is
\[ \theta(F) = \lim_{\substack{ n \to \infty \\ r_i \sim F }} \hat\theta_n( r_1, \dots, r_n ) . \]
Its influence function is
\begin{align*}
  & \text{IF}(r, \theta,  F) =
  \left.
  \dfrac{d}{d\gamma} \theta(F_\gamma)
  \right|_{\gamma=0} \\
  & F_\gamma = (1-\gamma) F + \gamma \delta_r
\end{align*}
It satisfies
\begin{align*}
  & \E_{ r \sim F } \bigl[ \text{IF} ( r, \theta, F ) \bigr] = 0 \\
  & \hat \theta_n - \theta(F) = \dfrac1n \sum \text{IF}( r_i , \theta , F )
  + \text{remainder}
\end{align*}
hence
\[
\var[\hat \theta_n] \approx \dfrac1n \sum \text{IF}^2 ( r_i , \theta , F_n )
\]
(the variance of the estimator is the variance if the IF-transformed returns).

We can compute the IF of most performance measures
(Sharpe, Sortino, Rachev, Omega ratios, etc.)
and therefore estimate their standard deviation (assuming iid returns).

R implementation in \texttt{RPEIF} and \texttt{RPESE}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Standard errors of risk and performance estimators for serially correlated returns}\\
  X. Chen and R.D. Martin (2019)
\end{book}
If the returns are not iid, the variance should be corrected with the ACF,
\[ \var[\hat\theta_n] = \E\bigl[ \text{IF}^2(r) \bigr] + 2
\sum_{k\geq1} \E\bigl[ \text{IF}(r_0) \text{IF}(r_k) \bigr]
\]
which can be computed from the periodogram,
which can be smoothed with a GLM model for exponential distributions
(polynomial regression with a lasso penalty to control the degree).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PerformanceAnalytics:\\ estimation of higher order moments}\\
  D. Cornilly and B.G. Peterson (2020)
\end{book}
Factor model for the skewness and kurtosis tensors.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dependence model assessment\\ and selection with DecoupleNets}\\
  M. Hofert et al. (2022)
\end{book}
Learn a transformation from a known copula in dimension $d\geq2$
to the uniform copula in dimension 2,
and use it for gof tests
(try several copulas, and keep that for whhich the result is closes to uniform).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Continuous-time stochastic gradient descent for optimizing over the stationary distribution of stochastic differential equations}\\
  Z. Wang and J. Sirignano (2022)
\end{book}
To find the parameters $\theta$ of an SDE
\[ dX = \mu_\theta(X) dt + \sigma_\theta(X) dW \]
to minimize
\[ F(\theta) = \n{ \E_{ Y \sim \pi_\theta } \bigl[ f(Y) - \beta \bigr] }^2
\]
where $E\bigl[f(Y)\bigr]$
are some generalized moments of the invariant distribution $\pi_\theta$:
\begin{align*}
  \partial_t \theta &= - 2 \alpha \bigl[ f(\bar X) - \beta \bigr] \tilde X ' \nabla_x f(X) \\
  d \tilde X &= ( \tilde X \partial_x \mu + \partial_\theta \mu ) dt +
  ( \tilde X \partial_x \sigma + \partial_\theta \sigma ) dW \\
  dX &= \mu dt + \sigma dW \\
  d\bar X &= \mu dt + \sigma d\bar W
\end{align*}
($\tilde X$ estimates $\partial_\theta X$, $\alpha$ is the learning rate).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate matrix-exponential affine mixtures and their applications in risk theory}\\
  E.C.K. Cheung et al. (2021)
\end{book}
The matrix-exponential distribution on $[0,\infty)$,
$X \sim \text{ME}(\alpha, T, t)$
has density $f(x) = \alpha e^{Tx} t$,
$\alpha \in \RR^{1 \times p}$,
$T \in \RR^{p\times p}$,
$t \in \RR^{p\times 1}$
and cdf
$F(x) = 1 + \alpha e^{Tx} T^{-1} t$.
These are the distributions whose Laplace transform is rational.
They are stable by mixtures and affine mixtures
(mixtures $f = \sum c_i f_i$, with potentially negative weights $c_i$ ad long as $f\geq0$).

Multivariate matrix-exponential affine mixtures have density
\[ f(x_1,\dots,c_M) =
\sum_{ 1 \leq i_1, \dots,i_M \leq L }
p_{i_1,\dots,i_M}
f_{i_1}(x_1) \cdots f_{i_M}(x_M).
\]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph neural networks for asset management}\\
  G. Pacreau et al.
\end{book}
Feed pricing data (and your model's alpha) to an LSTM,
and the result to a GCN or GAT (or the hypergraph equivalent)
to forecast future returns (or their signs),
with a penalty to get the correct order (learning to rank)
\[ \lambda \sum_{ij} \bigl[ (\hat y_i - \hat y_j ) (y_i - y_j) \bigr]_+. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio selection models based on interval-valued conditional value at risk (ICVaR) and empirical analysis}\\
  J. Zhang and K. Zhang (2022)
\end{book}
To extend portfolio construction to return intervals
$[x^L,x^U]$, use the elxicographic order of (mean, left)
for the objective and replace the constraint
$Ax\leq B$ with
$a^U x \leq b^U$, $\Gamma(B,Ax) \leq \gamma$,
where
\begin{align*}
  \Gamma(A,B) &= \dfrac{ m(B) - m(A) }{ w(B) + w(A) } \\
  A &= [a^L, a^U] \\
  B &= [b^L, b^U] \\
  m(A) &= \dfrac12 ( a^L + a^U ) \\
  w(A) &= a^U - a^L \\
  \gamma &\in [0,1] \text{ fixed}.
\end{align*}
VaR anc CVaR can be similartly generalized to interval-valued random variables.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The growth of relative wealth\\ and the Kelly criterion}\\
  A.W. Lo et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The Kelly portfolio $f$, for two assets $a$ and $b$ with ratio returns $X_a$ and $X_b$,
maximizes
\[ \mu(f) = \E \bigl[ \log( 1 + f X_a + (1-f) X_b ) \bigr] \]
and satisfies
\[\E \left[ \dfrac{ X_a - X_b }{ 1 + f X_a + (1-f)X_b } \right] = 0 \]
(unless $\E[ (1+X_b)/(1+X_a) ] < 1$ or $\E[ (1+X_a)/(1+X_b) ] < 1$,
in which case $f=1$ or $f=0$.

Alternatively, given two investors $f$ and $g$,
we can look for $f$ maximizing the \emph{relative} final wealth
$W_f / (W_f + W_g)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fat tails and optimal liability driven portfolios}\\
  J. Rosenzweig
\end{book}
In portfolio optimization, replace the variance with some other even moment
(larger exponents give an approximation of the minimum return).

