%% 2022-03-12
%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%
%% 2022-01-16
%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Traversing the local polytopes\\ of ReLU neural networks:\\
  a unified approach for network verification}\\
  S. Xu et al.
\end{book}
A ReLU network defines a piecewise linear function.
Each neuron splits the space along a hyperplane:
this partitions the space into polytopes, each defined by a set of inequalities;
non-redundant inequalities (flipping them gives a non-empty polytope)
define the faces of that polytope.
Neighbouring polytopes (they differ by the direction of one non-redundant inequality)
form a \emph{polytope graph},
which can be explored with breadth-first search (BFS).
This graph, with BFS traversal, can be used to prove properties of the network,
e.g., existence of local adversarial attacks, or to generate counterfactual samples.
Previous approaches used mixed integer programming (MIP),
but the polytope graph can be explored with linear programming (LP) alone
(to check if a set of linear inequalities defines the empty set).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Explainable neural networks\\ based on additive index models}\\
  J. Vaughan et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The explainable neural network (xNN) is an \emph{additive index model}
\[ f(x) = \mu + \sum \gamma_i h_i( \beta_i x ) \]
where the $h_i$'s are scalar functions defined by neural nets.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Enhancing explainability of neural networks through architecture constraints}\\
  Z. Yang et al.
\end{book}
The generalized additive index model (GAIM, aka projection pursuit learning)
\[ f \bigl( \E[ y | x ] \bigr) = \mu + \sum h_i( w_i ' x ) \]
can be estimated with neural networks $h_i$,
\[ f \bigl( \E[ y | x ] \bigr) = \mu + \sum \beta_i h_i( w_i ' x ), \]
with an $L^1$ penalty on both $\beta$ and $w$, a smooth penalty on $h_i$,
$\operatorname{Mean}_x h_i''( w_i x ) ^2$,
a normalization constraint for the $h_i$'s,
\[
\E\bigl[ h_i(z) \bigr] = 0, \qquad
\E\bigl[ h_i(z)^2 \bigr] = 1,
\]
imposed by a batchnorm layer,
and an orthogonality constraint
$W'W = I_k$, \ie, $W \in \text{Stiefel}(p,k)$,
imposed with the \emph{Cayley transform},
\ie, by replacing the gradient step
\[ W \leftarrow W - \tau G, \qquad G = \nabla_{\!W} \mathscr L \]
with
\begin{align*}
  A &= G W' - W G' \\
  W &\leftarrow \biggl( I  +\dfrac\tau2 A \biggr)^{-1} \biggl( I - \dfrac\tau2 A \biggr) W .
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GAMI-Net: an explainable neural network based on generalized additive models\\ with structured interactions}\\
  Z. Yang et al.
\end{book}
One can estimate a GAM, with neural networks instead of (1- and 2-dimensional) splines, and with:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pruning of less important main effects (proportion of explained variance);
\item Pairwise interactions, estimated on the residuals of the main effects model,
  only for pairs for which one variable has already been selected,
  chosen by looking at the proportion of variance explained by forests of shallow trees on each pair,
  pruned as above;
\item An orthogonality penalty
  \[ \sum_{ij} \operatorname{Mean} f(x_i) f(x_i , x_j)  \]
  to clearly separate the main effects from the interactions.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unwrapping the black box\\ of deep ReLU networks: interpretability,\\ diagnostics and simplification}\\
  A. Sudjianto et al.
\end{book}
A neural network with ReLU activations is a locally linear model: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Look at the parallel plot of the coefficients of those linear functions;
\item For each feature $x_i$, plot the segments $\beta_i x_i \sim x_i$,
  one for each cell (``activation region'').
\end{compactitem}
To simplify the model, merge nearby cells with similar coefficients
(not unlike the fused lasso).

Python implementation: \texttt{aletheia-dnn}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive explainable neural networks (AxNNs)}\\
  J. Chen et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Fit an AIM (additive index model)
on the residuals of a GAM (generalized additive model),
both estimated with neural networks:
the GAM estimates the main effects, the AIM the residual interactions.
Train with AdaBoost, with 1-variable GAMs and 2-variable AIMs as weak learners.

