\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Surrogate locally-interpretable models\\ with supervised machine learning algorithms}\\
  L. Hu et al. (2020)
\end{book}
A regression tree (with linear models with an $L^1$ penalty, or GAMs, in the leaves),
trained on the output of a neural net,
gives an interpretable surrogate model,
not unlike LIME (a linear model on a neighbourhood of each observation)
or KLIME (a linear model on each $k$-means cluster -- $k$-means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm,
while a regression tree is a supervised one).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Designing inherently\\ interpretable machine learning models}\\
  A. Sudjianto and A. Zhang (2021)
\end{book}
To measure the interpretability of a model,
look
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item
  if constraints such as
  additivity, sparsity, linearity, smoothness, monotonicity can be imposed,
  globally or locally, at least approximately,
\item if the model can be visualized,
\item if it uses an (orthogonal) projection of the input features,
\item if it segments the input space in a small number of regions.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{General pitfalls\\ of model-agnostic interpretation methods\\ for machine learning models}\\
  C. Molnar et al.
\end{book}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Confusion between feature effect (Shapley score)
  and feature importance (e.g., permutation feature importance, PFI): use both;
\item Interpreting a model that does not generalize well:
  this gives an interpretation of the model, not of the data generation process;
\item Non-intrinsically interpretable models -- try GAM boosting;
\item Ignoring feature dependence:
  most methods assume features are independent;
\item Using only correlation to measure dependence:
  check KCCA (kernel canonical correlation),
  HSIC (Hilbert-Schmidt independence criterion),
  MI (mutual information -- more difficult to estimate);
\item Ignoring interactions (when aggregated, significant effects can cancel out):
  look at ICE curves;
\item Ignoring uncertainty: use the bootstrap;
\item Drawing causal conclusions.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning Wasserstein embeddings}\\
  N. Courty et al. (2018)
\end{book}
A \emph{Deep Wasserstein embedding} is a mapping
(of probability distributions, or images -- the examples given are MNIST and doodles)
transforming the Wasserstein distance into the Euclidean distance,
trained on data
($x_i=(x_i^1,x_i^2)$, $y_i = d_W(x_i^1,x_i^2)$),
with a Siamese network, regularized with a decoder and a reconstruction loss.

Applications include Wasserstein barycenter (interpolation)
and principal geodesic analysis.

