\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Set representation learning\\ with generalized sliced-Wasserstein embeddings}\\
  N. Naderializadeh et al.
\end{book}
To process sets with a neural net, one usually uses permutation equivariant modules
(e.g., self-attention, or, more generally, processing each point in the same way)
and permutation invariance modules (multi-head attention, pooling):
DeepSets, SetTransformers.
Instead:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item View the elements of a set as samples from a probability distribution $\mu_i$;
\item Project them on random 1-dimensional subspaces,
  $\nu_{i\theta} = g_{\theta\#} \mu_i$;
\item Compute the Monge map between each $\nu_{i\theta}$ and some reference distribution $\nu_{0\theta}$:
  $F_{\nu_{i \theta}} ^{-1} \circ F_{ \nu_{0 \theta} }$;
\item Use the cummulative distribution transforms
  $F_{\nu_{i \theta}} ^{-1} \circ F_{ \nu_{0 \theta} } - \text{Id}$ as representation of the $\mu_i$.
\end{compactitem}
The projection $g_\theta$, can be learned from data, using contrastive learning (SimCLR, SiamSiam).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wasserstein embedding for graph learning}\\
  S. Kolouri et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Represent a graph with its node embedding, \ie, a set of points in $\RR^d$, and then, the linear Wasserstein embedding.

Do not compute all pairwise distances, but only distances to some reference graph.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalized sliced distances\\ for probability distributions}\\
  S. Kolouri et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{Radon transform} recovers a distribution $p$
from its slices $p_f$
along hyperplanes
\begin{align*}
  p_{ f_\theta } (t) &= \int_{ \RR^d } p(x) \delta\bigl( t - \langle x , \theta \rangle \bigr) dx \\
  p(x) &= \int_{ \SS^{d-1} } \bigl( p_{ f_\theta } * \eta \bigr) \bigl( \langle x , \theta \rangle \bigr) d\theta
\end{align*}
where $\hat \eta(\omega) = c \va{ \omega }^{d-1}$.

The \emph{generalized Radon transform} uses a family of hypersurfaces
$H = \{ x \in \RR^d \ :\ f_\theta(x) = t \}$ instead,
where the $f_\theta$ are called ``defining functions''.
A metric $\xi$ between 1-dimensional probability measures
can be extended to higher dimensions
\[ \zeta( p, q ) = \left( \int \xi( p_{f_\theta}, q_{f_\theta} )^r d\theta \right) ^{1/r},
\qquad r \geq 1 \]
(generalized sliced probability metric)
or \[ \zeta^*(p,q) = \sup_\theta \xi( p_{f_\theta}, q_{f_\theta} ), \]
For instance:
\begin{align*}
  \zeta^2(p,q) &= \int \n{ A p_{f_\theta} - A q_{f_\theta} } d\theta \\
  A &= \text{Id} \\
  Ap(t) &=  \int_{-\infty}^t p(s) ds \\
  Ap(t) &= \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} p(t) k(t,s) ds.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parameter prediction\\ for unseen deep architecture}\\
  B. Knyazev et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Hypernetworks learn to compute the parameters of another neural network from training data
in a single forward pass:
\[ \text{training data} \longmapsto \text{weights} \]
but they require the neural net to have a fixed architecture.
Graph neural nets (GNN) allow an arbitrary architecture (for a fixed set of node types):
\[ \text{training data},\  \text{architecture} \longmapsto \text{weights}. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Squeeze-and-excitation networks}\\
  J. Hu et al.
\end{book}
A squeeze-and-excite block combines:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A convolution layer, outputting $C$ channels;
\item Global averaging, separately for each channel, outputting a vector of length $C$;
\item A simple transformation,
  \[ z \mapsto s = \sigma\bigl( w_2 \text{ReLU}( w_1 z ) \bigr), \]
  to compute the channel-specific scale, outputting a vector of length $C$;
\item Multiplication of the convolution output and the scale.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning in high dimension\\ always amounts to extrapolation}\\
  R. Balestriero
\end{book}
\emph{Interpolation} occurs when a new sample is in the convex hull of the training data;
in high dimension, this is unlikely to happen.
\begin{align*}
  x_1,\dots,x_N &\sim \text{Unif} (B_d) \\
  x &\sim \text{Unif} (B_d) \\
  P \bigl[ x \in \text{chull}(x_1,\dots,x_N) \bigr] &
  \xrightarrow[\substack{
      d, N \to \infty \\
      N < 2^{d/2} \!/d
  }]{} 0
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Task-based end-to-end model learning\\ in stochastic optimization}\\
  P.L. Donti et al.
\end{book}
Traditional machine learning, for
\begin{align*}
  x &\text{: features}  & f(x,y,z)&\text{: loss} \\
  y &\text{: predictions} \\
  z &\text{: policy} \\
\end{align*}
is often a 2-step process
\begin{align*}
  \theta &= \argmin_\theta \E_{ x,y \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ - \log p( y | x ; \theta ) \bigr ] \\
  z(x) &= \argmin_x \E_{ y \sim p(y | x ; \theta) } \bigl[ f(x,y,z) \bigr].
\end{align*}

Black-box, end-to-end optimization is not very data-efficient:
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\theta \E_{ x,y \sim \text{Data} } \bigl[ f\bigl(x,y, z(x;\theta)\bigr) \bigr]. \]

Modeling the data distribution (instead of the policy)
in an end-to-end fashion (\ie, with the correct loss function, instead of the log-likelihood)
is more data-efficient.
Iterate:
\begin{align*}
  x, y & \sim \text{Data} \\
  z^*(x,\theta) &= \argmin_z \E_{ y \sim p(y|x;\theta) } f(x,y,z) \\
  \theta &\leftarrow \theta - \alpha \nabla_{\!\theta} f\bigl(x,y, z^*(x,\theta)\bigr).
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{N-Beats: neural basis expansion analysis\\ for interpretable time series forecasting}\\
  B.N. Oreshkin et al. (2020)
\end{book}
To forecast time series, stack blocks with two outputs,
to forecast/backcast the next/previous periods,
taking the residual of the previous block as input;
the nonlinearities are given by basis function expansions
$\sum \theta_i \mathbf v_i$,
to extract the trend (first stack)
and the seasonallity (second stack).

