  \emph{Market impact decay and capacity}\\
  H. Chan (2021)
\end{book}
When a trade is split over several days,
subsequent days still feel the market impact of previous days -- market impact does not immediately
drop to zero but decays (it drops to zero after a month).
\begin{align*}
  w_{it}^* &\text{: target weights} \\
  w_{it} &= (1-\tau) w_{i,t-1} + \tau w_{it}^* \\
  \tau &\text{: trading speed} \\
  \text{Impact} &= \rlap{$\alpha \sqrt{ \dfrac{ \text{size} }{ \text{ADV} } },$} \hspace{3cm} \alpha=100\,\text{bp} \\
  \text{Decay}_t &= \rlap{$\gamma^t,$} \hspace{3cm} \gamma = 0.85 \\
  \text{Cost}_{it} &= C \Delta_{it} \sum_{s \leq t } \text{sign}_{is} \text{Decay}_{t-s} \text{Impact}_{is} \\
  C &\text{: Capital} \\
  \text{Capacity} &= \max \{ \, C \ :\ \max_\tau \text{Sharpe}_\tau \geq \text{threshold} \,\}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The correlation risk premium:\\ international evidence}\\
  G. Faria et al.
\end{book}
The correlation risk is the difference between the implied correlation
\begin{align*}
  \text{IC} &= \dfrac{
    \text{SV}_{\text{index}} - \sum_i w_i^2 \text{SV}_i 
  }{
    \sum\limits_{i\neq j} w_i w_j \sqrt{ \text{SV}_i \text{SV}_j }
  } \\
  \text{SV} &= \text{variance swap rates}
\end{align*}
and the realized correlation
\begin{align*}
  \text{RC} &=
  \int_{S_t}^\infty \dfrac{2}{K^2} \biggl( 1 - \log \dfrac{ K }{ S_t } \biggr) \text{Call}_{[t,T]}(K) dK \\
  &+ \int_0^{S_t} \dfrac{2}{K^2} \left( 1 + \log \dfrac{ K }{ S_t } \right) \text{Put}_{[t,T]}(K) dK
\end{align*}
Similarly, the variance risk factor is the difference between the implied and the realized variance.

