\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine learning optimization algorithms\\ and portfolio allocation}\\
  S. Perrin and T. Roncalli (2019)
\end{book}
Four machine learning algorithms are applicable to portfolio optimization
(e.g., with a Herfindahl penalty to increase diversification):
coordinate descent, ADMM, proximal gradient,
and Dykstra's algorithm.

\d{Coordinate descent} optimizes one variable at a time, keeping the others fixed;
those 1-dimensional optimization problems often have an explicit solution
(e.g., soft-thresholding, for the lasso)
or can be solved with line search.

\d{ADMM} solves problems of the form
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x, y \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(x) + g(y) \\
  \text{Such that} & Ax + By = c
\end{array}
\]
by iterating
\begin{align*}
  x & \leftarrow \argmin_x f(x) + \lambda \n{ Ax + By - c + u } _2^2 \\
  y & \leftarrow \argmin_y g(y) + \lambda \n{ Ax + By - c + u } _2^2 \\
  u & \leftarrow u + ( A x + B y - c ).
\end{align*}
Finding a good value of $\lambda$ is tricky:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Increase $\lambda$ if $\n r \gg \n s$,
\item Decrease $\lambda$ if $\n r \ll \n s$,
\end{compactitem}
where $r = Ax + By - c$ and $s = 2 \lambda A' B \Delta y.$

Convergence to a moderately accurate solution is fast;
convergence to a more accurate solution much slower.

To put an optimization problem in ADMM form, consider the following ``tricks'':
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item To minimize $f(x) + g(x)$, minimize $f(x) + g(y)$ subject to $x=y$;
\item To minimize $f(x)$ subject to $x \in \Omega$, minimize $f(x) + \textbf{1}_\Omega(y)$
  subject to $x=y$;
\item To minimize $f(x) + g(x)$ subject to $x \in \Omega_1 \inter \Omega_2$,
  minimize $f(x) + \mathbf{1}_{\Omega_1}(x) + g(y) + \mathbf{1}_{\Omega_2}(y)$
  subject to $x=y$;
\item To minimize $f(x) + g(Ax+b)$, minimize $f(x) + g(y)$ subject to $y=Ax+y$.
\end{compactitem}

The \d{proximal operator} is
\[ \operatorname{prox}_f(v) = \argmin_x f(x) + \tfrac12 \n{ x - v }_2^2; \]
it generalizes the Euclidean projection (for $f = \mathbf 1_\Omega$)
and can often be computed explicitly.

There is no formula for $\text{prox}_{f_1+f_2}$,
but it can be computed by iterating
\begin{align*}
  x &\leftarrow \text{prox}_{f_1} ( y + p ) \\
  p &\leftarrow y + p - x \\
  y &\leftarrow \text{prox}_{f_2} ( x + q ) \\
  q &\leftarrow x + q - y 
\end{align*}
starting with $x=y=v$ and $p=q=0$.
\d{Dykstra's algorithm} generalizes this to a sum of $m$ terms.
It can be used to project on an intersection:
$\mathbf1_{ \Omega_1 \inter \cdots \inter \Omega_m } = \mathbf1_{ \Omega_1 } +
\cdots \mathbf1_{\Omega_m}$, e.g., an intersection of half-spaces
(\ie, general inequality constraints, $Ax=b$, $Cx\leq d$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dykstra's algorithm, ADMM and coordinate descent: connections, insights, and extensions}\\
  R.J. Tibshirani (2017)
\end{book}
The best approximation problem
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & u \in \RR^n \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ y - u } ^2 \\
  \text{Such that} & u \in C_1 \inter \cdots \inter C_d
\end{array}
\]
can be solved with Dikstra's algorithm, iterating
\begin{align*}
  u &\leftarrow P_{C_k} ( u + z_k ) \\
  z_k &\leftarrow z_k - \Delta u
\end{align*}
with a different $k$ at each iteration
(use a different dual variable for each $C_k$).

Its dual is the regularized regression problem
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w \in \RR^p \\
  \text{To minimize} & \tfrac12 \n{ y - X w }^2 + \sum\limits_i h_i(w_i)
\end{array}
\]
and the coordinate descent algorithm is equivalent to Dykstra's.

