\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scenario-based risk evaluation}\\
  R. Wang and J.F. Ziegel
\end{book}
The conditional value at risk (VaR) and expected shortfall (ES) of $X$ given the market state $S$ are
\begin{align*}
  \text{CoVaR}_S X &= \text{VaR}[ X \,|\, S = \text{VaR}\, S ] \\
  \text{ES}_S X &= \E[ X \,|\, X \leq \text{CoVaR}_S X ].
\end{align*}
More generally, given scenarios $Q_1,\dots,Q_n$, one can define
\begin{align*}
  \operatorname{MES} X &= \max_i \text{ES}^{Q_i} X \\
  \operatorname{MVaR} X &= \max_i \text{VaR}^{Q_i} X \\
  \operatorname{AES} X &= \operatorname{Mean}_i \text{ES}^{Q_i} X \\
  \operatorname{iMES} X &= \operatorname*{Mean}_{q \in [p,1]} \text{MVaR}_q X \\
  \operatorname{rMES} X &= \text{ES} \bigl[  \max_i X_i \bigr],
  \qquad X_i \!\!\underset{\text{indep.}}{\sim}\!\! Q_i.
\end{align*}
AES and eMES are coherent and comonotonic additive.

Basel III and IV require a \emph{stress adjustment},
ES calibrated on the worst 12 months (MES),
and a \emph{dependence adjustment},
average of the ES calibrated on the worst 12 months and that
calibrated on the worst possible dependence structure
(comonotonic risk factors:
compute an ES esparately for each category of risk factors and add them).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A framework\\ for measures of risk under uncertainty}\\
  T. Fadina et al.
\end{book}
(Coherent) risk measures are computed (in the case of a portfolio)
from portfolio weights and a distribution of asset retruns.
A generalized risk measure uses, instead, a \emph{set} of distributions
on asset returns, \ie, a set of scenarios.
One can them compute the worst case (or average, etc.) VaR, ES, etc.

