\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The adoption of blockchain-based\\ decentralized exchanges}\\
  A. Capponi and R. Jia (2021)
\end{book}
The pricing function $F: \RR^2 \to \RR^2$ of an automated market maker (AMM) satisfies
\begin{align*}
  & F_x, F_y > 0 \\
  & F_{xx}, F_{yy} < 0 \\
  & F_{xy} > 0 \\
  & F( \lambda x, \lambda y ) = \lambda^k F(x,y),\ k>0 \\
  & F_x / F_y \xrightarrow[x\to0]{} \infty \\
  & F_x / F_y \xrightarrow[x\to\infty]{} 0 \\
  & F_x / F_y \xrightarrow[y\to0]{} 0 \\
  & F_x / F_y \xrightarrow[y\to\infty]{} \infty.
\end{align*}
The number of assets in the AMM (usually 2), and the volatility of their exchange rates,
increase arbitrage opportunities and lowers the incentives for liquidity providers.
Higher curvature mitigates arbitrage,
but benefits more investors than liquidity providers.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
