\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-asymptotic estimation of risk measures using stochastic gradient Langevin dynamics}\\
  J. Chu and L. Tangpi (2021)
\end{book}
The expected shortfall can be computed as the solution of an optimization problem,
\[ \text{ES}_u X = \inf_{ q \in \RR } \dfrac{ 1 }{ 1 - u } \E \bigl[ (X-q)^+ \bigr] + q \]
For $X = f(r,S)$, where $S = (X_0,\dots, X_n)$ is a set of risk factors
and $r$ the parameters of the strategy $f$, e.g.,
$f(r,S) = \sum r_i S_i$ for a portfolio, it can be computed with gradient descent.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Heterotic risk models}\\
  Z. Kakushadze (2015)
\end{book}
Hierarchical industry classifications define nested factor models
(for the correlation matrix, not the variance)
\begin{align*}
  V_0 &= \beta_{01} V_1 \beta_{01}' + \Delta_0 \\
  V_1 &= \beta_{12} V_2 \beta_{12}' + \Delta_1 \\
  V_2 &= \beta_{23} V_3 \beta_{23}' + \Delta_2
\end{align*}
(where 0, 1, 2, 3 are stocks, subindustries, industries, sectors).

