  Z. Kakushadze and W. Yu (2021)
\end{book}
To compute the variance matrix of ETFs, estimate a ``heterotic risk model'' after building an ETF taxonomy:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Largest sector, industry, subindustry (if one dominates, othersize ``broad'');
\item Largest capbin;
\item Largest region or country;
\item ``Value'' or ``growth'' or ``mixed'';
\item Bond type
\item Duration bin;
\item Credit rating'
\item Commodity type;
\item ``Leveraged'', ``inverse'', ``normal''.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical industry classification}\\
  Z. Kakushadze and W. Yu (2016)
\end{book}
Cluster \emph{normalized returns} (not raw returns, or correlations),
after ``smoothing'' stocks with an unreasonably small volatility.

Use the \emph{effective rank} as the number of clusters.

If you already have an industry classification, it is likely better: only apply the clustering inside
large subindustries.

