  Z. Kakushadze (2018)
\end{book}
(1-day) momentum, intraday volatility, and size --
volume has little effect.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{4-factor model for overnight returns}\\
  Z. Kakushadze (2015)
\end{book}
Size, intraday volatility (or $(H-L)/C$, averaged over 21 days), momentum, liquidity (volume).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Momentum residual networks}\\
  M.E. Sander et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Residual networks are discretizations of first order differential equations,
$ x_{n+1} = x_n + f_\theta(x_n) $.
Momentum residual networks are discretizations of second-order ODEs.
\begin{align*}
  v_{n+1} &= \gamma v_n + ( 1 - \gamma ) f(x_n) \\
  x_{n+1} &= x_n + v_{n+1}
\end{align*}
\[ \epsilon \ddot x + \dot x = f(x) , \qquad \epsilon = \dfrac1{1-\gamma} \]
They are invertible.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Loss surface simplexes\\ for mode-connecting volumes\\ and fast ensembling}\\
  G.W. Benton et al.
\end{book}
Global minima are believed to be connected by multidimensional submanifolds.
Search for simplicial complexes
$K = \text{chull}(x_1, \dots, x_n) $
minimizing
\[ \E _{ x \sim \text{Unif}(K) } \bigl[ \ell(x) \bigr] - \lambda \log \text{volume}(K) \]
where the expected loss in the simplex
is approximated from 5 random points in it,
and the number of vertices, $n$, progressively increases.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GRAND: graph neural diffusion}\\
  B.P. Chamberlain et al. (2021)
\end{book}
GNNs are equivalent to the explicit, single-step Euler discretization of a diffusion PDE:
multi-step (Runge-Kutta) and implicit schemes
perform better.
\begin{align*}
  x &: \text{ node features (scalar field)} \\
  \mathscr X &:\text{ edge features (vector field)} \\
  \langle x, y \rangle &= \sum_i x_i y_i \\
  \langle\!\langle \mathscr X, \mathscr Y \rangle\!\rangle &= \sum_{i>j} w_{ij} \mathscr X_{ij} \mathscr Y_{ij}
  \quad w_{ij} \in \{0,1\} \\
  \mathscr X_{ij} &= - \mathscr X_{ji} \qquad \text{alternating edge field} \\
  ( \nabla x )_{ij} &= x_j - x_i \\
  ( \div \mathscr X )_i &= \sum_{ j\,:\,(i,j)\in E } \mathscr X_{ij} = \sum_j w_{ij} \mathscr X_{ij} \\
  \langle\!\langle \nabla x, \mathscr X \rangle\!\rangle &= \langle x, \div \mathscr X \rangle
  \qquad \text{adjunction}
\end{align*}
The diffusion equation
\[ \dfrac{ \partial x }{ \partial t } = \div \bigl( G(x) \nabla x \bigr),
\qquad G_{ij,ij} = a(x_i,x_j), \]
for some attention function $a$, is of the form
\[ \dfrac{ \partial x_t }{ \partial t } =
\bigl( A(x_t) - I \bigr) x_t = \bar A(x_t) x_t . \]
The explicit scheme is
\[ x_i \leftarrow x_i + \sum_j a_{ij} ( x_j - x_i ), \]
\ie, $\mathbf x_{k+1} = (I + \tau A_k) \mathbf x_k$.
The implicit scheme is
$(I-\tau A_k) \mathbf x_{k+1} = x_k$
(it requires solving a linear system).
Multi-step schemes are of the form
\[ \sum_{j=0}^s \alpha_j x_{k+j} = \tau \sum_{s=0}^s \beta_j \bar A(x_{k+j}) x_{k+j}. \]
There are also adaptive schemes.
\[
