\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The fast kernel transform}\\
  J.P. Ryan et al. 
\end{book}
Kernel methods require matrix-vector products
$x \mapsto K x$, where the kernel $K$ may be large.
To speed up computations, one can use a low-rank approximation of $K$
(the Nystr\"om method uses a random sample of the columns).
Fast multipole methods use a low-rank approximation of the off-diagonal blocks,
but they require a kernel-specific (Laurent series) expansion of the kernel.

\[
