\]

Modern automatic differentialtion (AD)
tools can automatically compute those expansions.

Implementation in \texttt{fastKernelTransform.jl}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A short course on fast multipole methods}\\
  R. Beatson and L. Greengard
\end{book}
We want to compute
$u(x_i) = \sum_j w_j k(x_i,x_j)$
for some specific kernel $k$
(it is a matrix-vector product $u=kw$)
using a low-rank approximation
\[ k(x,y) = \sum_{k=1}^p \phi_k(x) \psi_k(y) \]
(at least for off-diagonal blocks of $k$).

For instance, in dimension 1, for
\[ u(x) = \sum_j w_j \sqrt{ ( x-x_j)^2 + c^2 }, \]
one can use a Laurent expansion in $x$
around (away from) $x_j$;
it can be used with a hierarchical decomposition of $\RR$
into ``panels'' $T$:
use the exact function
\[ s_T(x) = \sum_{j\in T} w_j \sqrt{ (x-x_j)^2 + c^2 } \]
for the panel containing $x$ and nearby panels,
and the Laurent expansion for distant ones.

For the \emph{Gauss transform} in dimension 2,
\[ U(x) = \sum_{i=1}^N w_i e^{ - \n{ x - x_i }^2 } \]
use the expansion into Hermite functions
\begin{align*}
  e^{-\n{x-x_i}^2} &= \sum_{n_1,n_2\geq0} \Phi_{n_1,n_2}(x-c) \Psi_{n_1,n_2}(x_i-c) \\
  \Phi_{n_1,n_2}(\mathbf x) &= h_{n_1}(x) h_{n_2}(y) \\
  \Psi_{n_1,n_2}(\mathbf x) &= \dfrac{ x^{n_1} y^{n_2} }{ n_1! n_2! }  
\end{align*}

The \emph{multipole expansion}, in dimension 2 ($z\in\CC$) is
\begin{align*}
  \phi(z)
  &= \sum_i q_i \log( z - z_i ) \\
  &= \biggl( \sum_i q_i \biggr) \log z + \sum_{k\geq1} \dfrac1k
  \dfrac{ \sum_i q_i z_i^k }{ z^k }.
\end{align*}
This gives an $O(N \log N)$ algorithm and,
with more work (formula to translate a multipole expansion
and/or convert it to a Taylor expansion) $O(N)$.

This generalizes to dimension 3 (but the expansion is more complicated).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Do vision transformers see\\ like convolutional neural networks?}\\
  M. Raghu et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{centered kernel alignment} (CKA)
compares neural network layers (activations) $X\in\RR^{m\times p_1}$,
$Y \in \RR^{m\times p_2}$ on $m$ samples (with $p_1$ and $p_2$ neurons)
using the Gram matrices $K=XX'$, $L=YY'$.
\begin{align*}
  \text{CKA}(K,L) &= \dfrac{
    \text{HSIC}(K,L)
  }{
    \sqrt{
    \text{HSIC}(K,K)
    \text{HSIC}(L,L)
    }
  } \\
  H &= I_n - \tfrac1n \mathbf 1 \mathbf 1' \\
  K_c &= HKH \\
  L_c &= HLH \\
  \text{HSIC}(K,L) &= \dfrac{
    \text{vec}(K_c) \cdot \text{vec}(L_c)
  }{
    (m-1)^2
  }
\end{align*}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Text generation with efficient (soft) Q-learning}\\
  H. Guo et al.
\end{book}
Policy-gradient is on-policy:
it is not data-efficient.
$Q$-learning is off-policy,
but noisy, and it converges slowly.
Try the following variants (soft $Q$-learning):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $Q$-learning with an entropy penalty;
\item In the Bellman equation, replace
  $\max_a Q(s_{t+1},a)$ with
  $\log \sum_a \exp Q(s_{t+1},a)$;
\item Instead of the state action value $Q$,
  path consistency learning (PCL)
  uses the state value function $V$ and
  \( V(s_t) - \gamma V(s_{t+1}) = r_t - \log \pi( a_t | s_t ). \)
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Curiosities and counterexamples\\ in smooth convex optimization}\\
  J. Bolte and E. Pauwels (2020)
\end{book}
Given an increasing sequence of compact convex subsets of $\RR^2$ with positively curved boundaries
$(C_n)_{n\in\ZZ}$, there is a $\mathscr C^k$ convex function with the $C_n$'s as level sets,
and a positive definite Hessian outside
$\argmin f = \bigcap_{n \in \ZZ} C_n$.
The proof uses:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The parametrization of $\partial C_n$ by its normals, $c_n: \RR / 2\pi\ZZ \to \partial C_n$
\item The boundary of the Minkowski sum $\lambda C_n + (1-\lambda) C_{n+1}$:
  $\lambda c_n + (1-\lambda) c_{n+1}$;
\item Bernstein interpolation to smooth piecewise linear functions.
\end{compactitem}
This can be used to build counter-examples, corresponding to paths of infinite length,
jiggling or spiralling
(e.g., the Newton flow need not converge,
the Tikhonov path may have infinite length, etc.)

<<CounterExamplesCOnvexOptimization,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
op <- par( mfrow=c(1,3), mar = c(0,0,0,0)+.5 )
x <- seq(0, 20*2*pi, length=1000)
y <- sin(x)
x <- x/max(x)
plot(x^5,y, type='l', lwd = 2, axes = FALSE)
box()

x <- seq(0,1,length=1000)[-1]
x <- x^2
y1 <- x * sin(10/x)
plot(x,y1, type = "l", lwd=2, axes = FALSE)
box()

theta <- seq(0,100,length=1000)[-1]
r <- 1 + 10/theta
x <- r * cos(theta)
y <- r * sin(theta)
a <- 3
plot(x,y, type = "l", lwd = 2, xlim = c(-a,a), ylim = c(-a,a), axes = FALSE)
box()
par(op)
@

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{VICReg: variance-invariance-covariance regularization for self-supervised learning}\\
  A. Bardes et al.
\end{book}
Siamese networks learn image representations by maximizing the agreement between embeddings of different views of the same image.
To avoid collapse problems,
use a loss made of three terms:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Invariance: $s(z,z') = \tfrac1n \sum \n{ z_{i\bigcdot} - z_{i\bigcdot}' }_2^2 $;
\item Variance: $\dfrac1d \sum \text{hinge}_\gamma \sqrt{ \var z_{\bigcdot j} }$;
\item Covariance: $c(z) = \dfrac1d \sum\limits_{ j_1 \neq j_2 } \cov( z )^2_{j_1,j_2}$.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Compositional processing emerges\\ in neural networks solving math problems}\\
  J. Russin et al.
\end{book}
Tensor product (TP) transformers replace the attention mechanism
\[ \text{Att}( Q,K,V) = \text{softmax} \left( \dfrac{ Q K' }{ \sqrt d } \right) V \]
with
\[ \text{TP-Att}( Q,K,V,R) = \text{softmax} \left( \dfrac{ Q K' }{ \sqrt d } \right) V \odot R. \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The devil is in the detail:\\ simple tricks improve\\ systematic generalization of transformers}\\
  R. Csord\'as et al.
\end{book}
Tests on human language to code translation datasets suggest the following advice.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item
  Use \emph{universal transformers}, \ie, transformers with weights shared between layers:
  they are less sensitive to the order of the operations seen during training.
\item 
Prefer relative (vs absolute) positional embedding,
to help generalization to longer output lengths.
\item 
Avoid early stopping for model selection.
\item 
Have validation and test sets for both the iid and generalizaation splits.
\item 
Use accuracy (not loss) for model selection.
\item 
Try different scaling: $N(0,1)$, Glorot multiplied by $\sqrt d$, Kaiming divided by $\sqrt d$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal design generation and power evaluation in T: the skpr package}\\
  T. Morgan-Wall and G. Khoury (2021)
\end{book}
There are many notions of ``optimal design'' (choice of a binary model matrix $X$):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item D-optimal: maximize $\det(X'X)$
\item I-optimal: minimize $\tr[ (X'X)^{-1} M ]$
\item A-optimal: minimize $\tr(X'X)^{-1}$
\item G-optimal: minimize $\tr(X'X)^{-1}$
\item E-optimal: maximize $\sigma_{\text{min}}(X'X)$
\item T-optimal: maximize $\tr(X'X)$
\item Alias-optimal: minimize $\tr(A'A)$ where $A=(X'X)^{-1} X' X_2$
  where $X_2$ is the model matrix for the interaction terms.
\end{compactitem}

In R:
\texttt{skpr},
\texttt{AlgDesign},
\texttt{DoEbase},
\texttt{FrF2},
\texttt{conf.design},
\texttt{planor},
\texttt{rsm}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{svars: an R package\\ for data-driven identification\\ in multivariate time series analysis}\\
  A. Lange et al. (2021)
\end{book}
VAR models are of the form
\begin{align*}
  y_t &= \mu + A_1 y_{t-1} + \cdots + A_p y_{t-p} + u_t \\
  u_t &\sim N(0,\Sigma).
\end{align*}
Structural VAR (SVAR) models further decompose the innovations $u_t$ as
\begin{align*}
  u_t &= B \epsilon_t \\
  \epsilon_t &\sim N(0,1) 
\end{align*}
where $B$ is invertible and $\Sigma = BB'$;
it is not identifiable:
$B$ can be replaced by $BO$, for $O \in O_n$.
To identify it, make additional assumptions, such as: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Assume $\Sigma$ changes with time, e.g.,
  $\Sigma_1 = BB'$ until time $t_0$,
  and $\Sigma_2 = B \Lambda B'$,
  $\Lambda$ diagonal, after;
\item Assume $\Sigma$ smoothly transitions from $\Sigma_1$ to $\Sigma_2$,
  \[ \Sigma_t = (1-\lambda_t) \Sigma_1 + \lambda_t \Sigma_2 \]
  with a logistic transition function
  \[ \lambda_t = \dfrac{ 1 }{ 1 + e^{-\gamma (s_t - c)} } \]
  for some state $s_t$;
\item Assume $\Sigma_t = B \, \text{diag}( \text{GARCH}_t ) \, B'$;
\item Use independent component analysis (ICA) to find $O\in O_n$,
  parametrized as a product of $n(n-1)/2$ Givens rotations, using:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\bigcdot}]
  \item The Cramer-von Mises distance between the empirical and the independence copulas;
  \item FastICA, steadyICA;
  \item or a Student likelihood.
  \end{compactitem}
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DC3: a learning method\\ for optimization with hard constraints}\\
  P.L. Donti et al. (2021)
\end{book}
To solve (constrained) optimization problems with deep learning:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Train a neural net, on solved problems, to produce partial solutions;
item Complete them so satisfy the equality constraints (this is differentiable, thanks to the implicit function theorem);
\item Perform a few gradient steps, along the manifold defined by the equality constraints,
  to enforce the inequality constraints (still differentiable).
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A unifying modeling abstraction\\ for infinite-dimensional optimization}\\
  J.L. Pulsipher et al.
\end{book}
\texttt{InfiniteOpt.jl} solves infinite dimensional optimization problems,
\ie, problems involving functions, probability distributions, differential operators, expectations;
it automatically discretizes the problem.
    
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A text-based analysis of corporate innovation}\\
  G. Bellstam et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Fit a latent Dirichlet allocation (LDA) model, with 15 topics, to 800,000 analyst reports
for S\&P companies;
identify the ``innovation'' topic as the closest
(for the KL divergence)
to the word distribution of a textbook on innovation
(first Google hit for ``innovation textbook pdf'');
combine with the Loughran-McDonald sentiment lexicon.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Language and domain specificity:\\ a Chinese financial sentiment dictionary}\\
  Z.Du et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Using news articles from \texttt{finance.sina.com.cn}
and manually selected words in 3 sentiment categories (positive, negative, and
a China-specific ``politically positive'',
from 500 articles),
train a word2vec model to find similar words and extend the lexicon. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Factor models for Chinese A-shares}\\
  M.X. Hanauer et al. (2021)
\end{book}
For China, instead of the Fama-French factors,
use market, size, E/P (not B/P), and perhaps ``abnormal turnover''.
\begin{align*}
  \text{Turnover} &= \dfrac{ \text{shares traded} }{ \text{shares outstanding} } \\
  \text{Abnormal turnover} &= \dfrac{
    \text{MA}( \text{turnover} , \text{20 days} )
  }{
    \text{MA}( \text{turnover} , \text{250 days} )
  }
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of global factor models}\\
  M.X. Hanauer (2019)
\end{book}
Replace the Fama-French factors with
market, size, E/P, cash-based profitability, change in assets, momentum.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spectral factor models}\\
  F.M. Bandi (2020)
\end{book}
In the 1-factor model (CAPM), $y_t = \alpha + \beta x_t + \epsilon_t$,
decompose the factor returns $(x_t)_t$ into high and low frequency components
(Wold decomposition)
\[ y_t = \alpha + \beta^{\text{LF}} x_t^{\text{LF}} + \beta^{\text{HF}} x_t^{\text{HF}} + \epsilon_t \]
The classical model imposes $\beta^{\text{LF}} = \beta^{\text{HF}}$).
Those spectral betas can be obtained from the high and low frequency components of $y$:
\begin{align*}
  y_t ^{\text{LF}} &= \alpha + \beta^{\text{LF}} x_t^{\text{LF}} + \epsilon_t^{\text{LF}} \\
  y_t ^{\text{HF}} &= 0 + \beta^{\text{HF}} x_t^{\text{HF}} + \epsilon_t^{\text{HF}}.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Weak supervision and Black-Litterman\\ for automated ESG portfolio construction}\\
  A. Sokolov et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Train a BERT classifier (on New York Times news -- they are already tagged)
to recognize ESG articles,
and use it as a Black-Litterman view to tilt the portfolio
away from companies with a lot of ESG press coverage
(alternatively, use this ESG score to adjust your model's alpha).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A reinforcement learning approach\\ to optimal execution}\\
  C.C. Moallemi and M. Wang (2021)
\end{book}
To decide when to trade (a single share),
predict future prices with an RNN and trade, at time $t$,
if the predicted price change on $[t,T]$ is negative --
otherwise, wait.

The loss $\displaystyle\dfrac1T \sum\limits_{i=1}^T \bigl( r_i - \textsc{rnn}_i(x_0) \bigr)^2 $
can be replaced with
\[
%\dfrac1T
%\left[
  \bigl(r_1 - \textsc{rnn}_1(x_0)\bigr)^2 +
  \sum_{i=2}^T \bigl( \textsc{rnn}_{i-1}(x_1) - \textsc{rnn}_i(x_0) \bigr)^2 
%\right]
\]
or
\[
%\dfrac1T
%\left[
  \sum_{i=1}^m \bigl(r_i - \textsc{rnn}_i(x_0)\bigr)^2 +
  \sum_{i=m}^T \bigl( \textsc{rnn}_{i-m}(x_m) - \textsc{rnn}_i(x_0) \bigr)^2 
%\right]
\]
(temporal difference supervised learning).

Instead of the (unconditional) predicted price change,
reinforcement learning uses the \emph{continuation value}
\[ C_{[t,T]} (x) = \sup_\pi \E_\pi \left[ \sum_{i=t}^{\tau_\pi} \biggm| x_t = x \right], \]
\ie, it takes early stopping into account.

For the state $x$, use:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Time of day;
\item Spread, normalized by volatility, or price;
\item Depth (level1, level5, imbalance $(1-5)/(1+5)$);
\item Number of trades, number of price changes;
\item Trade size (exponentially weighted).
\end{compactitem}
Normalize the prices (and the price changes) by the spread.

The reinforcement learning approach is slightly better,
but not much,
and it is more data-hungry.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio selection:\\ a target-distribution approach}\\
  N. Lassance and F. Vrins (2021)
\end{book}
Replace the portfolio optimization problem
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w \\
  \text{To maximize} & w' \E[X] - \tfrac12 \lambda w' \var[X] w 
\end{array}
\]
with
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w \\
  \text{To minimize} & \text{KL} ( w'X \,\|\, \text{target} ) 
\end{array}
\]
where the target distribution has the same first two moments as an efficient portfolio,
but with skewness and kurtosis reflecting the investor's preferences, e.g.,
a generalized Gaussian
\[ f(x) \propto \exp - \tfrac12 \left( \dfrac{ \va{x-\alpha} }{ \beta } \right) ^ \gamma \]
or a shifted normal.

If the target distribution has a smaller support, use the ``conditionnal KL divergence''
\[ \text{CKL}(f\,\|\,g) = \E_{X\sim f} \left[ \log \dfrac{ f(X) }{ g(X) } \biggm| X \in \operatorname{Supp} g \right]
+ \dfrac{ p }{ 1-p } \]
where $p = \operatorname*{P}\limits_{X \sim f} [ X \not\in \operatorname{Supp} g ]$.

When the asset returns are Gaussian (and the target mean and variances on or above the efficient frontier),
then the optimal portfolio is mean efficient.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hidden errors in regression-based attribution}\\
  L. Sneddon
\end{book}
If the return forecasts are a linear combination of ``alphas'',
$\mu = \sum \alpha_i$,
the (unconstrained) optimal portfolio is
\begin{align*}
  w &= \argmax_w w' \mu - \dfrac12 \lambda w' V w \\
  &= ( \lambda V)^{-1} \mu \\
  &= \sum ( \lambda V)^{-1} \alpha_i
\end{align*}
which gives an exact decomposition of the returns
\[ r' w = \sum r' (\lambda V)^{-1} \alpha_i. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation of a high-dimensional\\ counting process without penalty\\ for high-frequency events}\\
  L. Mucciante and A. Sancetta (2021)
\end{book}
The explanatory variances $X_t$ for the intensity $\lambda_t$
of a counting process $N_t$ (here, high-frequency trades)
often measure some form of ``activity'': they are positive and have a positive impact on the number
of events -- use a sign constraint in the model.
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \beta \\
  \text{To minimize} & \displaystyle - 2 \int_0^T X_t' \beta \,dN_t + \int_0^T ( X_t ' \beta )^2 dt \\
  \text{Such that} & \beta \geq 0 \\
  \text{Where} & dN_t = \lambda_t dt \\
  & \lambda_t = X_t ' \beta \\
  & X_t \geq 0 \\
  & \lambda_t > 0
\end{array}
\]
Predictors include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Volume imbalance, for levels 1 to 5,
  \[
  \dfrac{
    \text{bid size} - \text{ask size}
  }{
    \text{bid size} + \text{ask size}
  };
  \]
\item Trade imbalance;
\item Spread;
\item Duration.
\end{compactitem}
and their exponentially weighted moving average.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The calculus of M-estimation in R with geex}\\
  B.C. Saul and M.G. Hudgens (2020)
\end{book}
Estimators defined by \emph{estimating equations}
\[ \sum \psi( y_i , \hat \theta ) = 0 \]
are asymptotically normal and their asymptotic variance can be computed with the sandwich estimator.

The \texttt{geex} package computes user-defined estimators and their asymptotic variance
(with numerical derivatives, not automatic differentiation).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The calculus of M-estimation}\\
  L.A. Stefanski and D.D. Boos (2001)
\end{book}
\d{M-estimators} (or generalized estimating equations, GEE)
are solutions of $\sum_i \psi(y_i,\theta)=0$ or, more generally,
$\sum_i \psi(y_i,\theta)=c_i$ with $c_n/\sqrt n \smash{\overset{p}{\to}}0$.

Examples include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Mean: $\psi(y,\theta) = y - \theta$;
\item Mean and MAD:
  \[
  \psi(y,\theta) = \begin{pmatrix}
    \va{y-\theta_2} - \theta_1 \\ y - \theta_2 \end{pmatrix};
  \]
\item Any maximum likelihood estimator (MLE), with the score function
  \[
  \psi(y,\theta) = \dfrac{ \partial \log f_\theta(y) }{ \partial \theta };
  \]
\item Mean and variance:
  \[
  \psi(y,\theta) = \begin{pmatrix}
  y - \theta_1 \\ ( y-\theta_1 ) ^ 2 - \theta_2 
  \end{pmatrix}
  \]
  (it is not the same $\psi$ function as for the MLE, but it gives the same estimator);
\item Ratio $\bar Y / \bar X$:
  \[ \psi(y,\theta) = y - \theta x
  \ \ \text{ or }\ \ 
  \psi(y,\theta) = \begin{pmatrix}
    y - \theta_1 \\ x - \theta_2 \\ \theta_1 - \theta_3 \theta_2 
  \end{pmatrix};
  \]
\item $\mu$, $\sigma^2$, $\sigma$, $\log \sigma$:
  \[\psi(y,\theta) = \begin{pmatrix}
  y- \theta_1 \\ ( y-\theta_1)^2 - \theta_2 \\
  \sqrt{ \theta_2 } - \theta_2 \\ \log \theta_2 - \theta_4
  \end{pmatrix};\]
\item Instrumental variables
  $\beta_{\text{IV}} = \beta_{Y|T} / \beta_{W|T}$:
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.9,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt]
  \node[circle,draw] (T) at (0,2) {$T$};
  \node[circle,densely dotted,draw] (X) at (0,1) {$X$};
  \node[circle,draw] (W) at (0,0) {$W$};
  \node[circle,draw] (Y) at (1,1) {$Y$};
  \draw[->,thick] (T) -- (X);
  \draw[->,thick] (X) -- (W);
  \draw[->,thick] (X) -- (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
  \begin{align*}
  \psi(y,\theta) &= \begin{pmatrix}
    \theta_1 - t \\ ( y - \theta_2 w) ( \theta_1 - t ) 
  \end{pmatrix} \\
  \text{or }
  \psi(y,\theta) &= \begin{pmatrix}
    \theta_1 - t \\ \theta_2 - w \\
    ( y - \theta_3 w )( \theta_2 - w ) \\
    ( y - \theta_4 w )( \theta_1 - t )
  \end{pmatrix};
  \end{align*}
\item Robust location
  $\psi(y,\theta) = h_k(y-\theta)$
  where $h_k =
    \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.3,yscale=.2,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt,outer sep = 0pt,baseline=-3pt]
    \draw (-1.1,0) -- (1.1,0);
    \draw (0,-1.1) -- (0,1.1);
    \draw[ultra thick] (-1,1) -- (-.5,1) -- (0,0) -- (.5,1) -- (1,1);
  \end{tikzpicture}
  $;
\item Sample quantile:
    $\psi(y,\theta) = p - \mathbf{1}_{ y \leq \theta }$
\item Positive mean deviation from the median:
  \[
  \psi(y,\theta) = \begin{pmatrix}
  2 ( y - \theta_2 ) \mathbf1_{ y > \theta_2 } - \theta_1 \\ \tfrac12 - \mathbf1_{y\leq\theta_2}
  \end{pmatrix};
  \]
\item Non-linear least squares,
  $y = g(x,\beta)+\epsilon$:
  \[ \psi(y,x,\theta) = \bigl[ y - g(x,\beta) \bigr] g'(x,\beta); \]
\item Linear regression with robust loss (truncated $L^1$):
  \[ \psi(y,x,\theta) = h_k(y-x'\beta)x; \]
\item Generalized linear models (GLM).
\end{compactitem}

Under reasonable assumptions,
\[ \hat\theta \sim \text{AMN}\biggl( \theta_0 , \dfrac{ V(\theta_0) }{ n } \biggr) \]
(asymptotically multivariate normal),
where
\begin{align*}
  \theta_0 &: \text{ true parameter (unknown)} \\ 
  \hat\theta &: \text{ estimator} \\
  A(\theta_0) &= \E_{ Y \sim F } \biggl[ - \left. \dfrac{ \partial \psi(Y,\theta) }{ \partial \theta } \right|_{\theta=\theta_0} \biggr] \\
  B(\theta_0) &= \E_{ Y \sim F } \bigl[ \psi(Y,\theta_0) \psi(Y,\theta_0)' \bigr] \\
  V(\theta_0) &= A(\theta_0)^{-1} B(\theta_0) A(\theta_0)^{-\top}
  \text{ (sandwich estimator)} 
\end{align*}
For the MLE, $A=B=I$ is the information matrix and $V=I^{-1}$,
but the general formula remains valid even under model misspecification.
Use a CAS to compute the matrix products: often, you only want one of the diagonal elements of $V$
(your estimator alone is not an M-estimator, but it is a component of one).

The variance matrix can also be used for Wald tests:
$ (\hat\theta - \theta_0 ) ' V_n(\hat\theta)^{-1} (\hat\theta - \theta_0 ) $.

For non-smooth functions, swap $\partial_\theta$ and $\E$:
\[ A(\theta_0) = - \left. \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial \theta } \E_{ Y \sim F } \bigl[ \psi(Y,\theta) \bigr] \right|_{\theta=\theta_0}. \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An axiomatization of $\Lambda$-quantiles}\\
  F. Bellini and I. Peri (2021)
\end{book}
The \emph{$\Lambda$-quantiles} of a distribution $F$ are
\[ \Lambda\text{-VaR}(R) = - \inf \bigl\{ x \in \RR \ :\ F(x) > \Lambda(x) \bigr\} \]
where $\Lambda: \RR \to (0,1)$ is non-decreasing;
they are characterized by the \emph{locality} property:
changing $F$ above or below $\Lambda\text{-VaR}(F)$ does not change $\Lambda\text{-VaR}(F)$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonparametric extrema analysis\\ in time series for envelope extraction,\\ peak detection and clustering}\\
  K. Gokcesu and H. Gokcesu
\end{book}
To detect peaks (or bursts) in a signal $(x_t)_t$,
compute its envelope:
%signals $(a_t)_t$, $(b_t)_t$ such that
signals $a$, $b$, such that
$\forall t \ a_t \leq x_t \leq b(t)$ and $\forall t \ x_t \in \{ a_t, b_t \}$,
minimizing
\[ \sum \va{ a_{t+1} - a_t } + \sum \va{ b_{t+1} - b_t } . \]
If $a$ and $b$ are defined using constant interpolation,
\begin{align*}
  a_t &= s_t x_t + (1-s_t) a_{t-1} \\
  b_t &= (1-s_t) x_t + s_t b_{t-1} \\
  s_t &\in \{0,1\}.
\end{align*}
The problem can be solved efficiently, in $O(T \log T)$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Iterated and exponentially weighted moving principal component analysis}\\
  P. Bilokon and D. Finkelstein (2021)
\end{book}
When computing principal components on an expanding or moving window,
the principal components can flip sign:
instead of recomputing them anew each time,
use an iterative algorithm (Ogita-Aishima)
to refine the previous ones.

To account for non-stationarity, use exponential weights.

(Python implementation available.)
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Iterative refinement\\ for symmetric eigenvalue decomposition}\\
  T. Ogita and K. Aishima (2018)
\end{book}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Principal component analysis:\\ a review and recent developments}\\
  I.T. Jolliffe and J. Cadima (2016)
\end{book}
PCA can be computed from the eigendecomposition of the variance matrix
$S = \var X$: the unit eigenvectors $a$ satisfy
$\var Xa = a' S a = \lambda a a' = \lambda$,
or from the SVD of the column-centered data matrix $X^* = U L A'$,
\[ (n-1) S = X^*{}' X^* = (ULA')'(ULA') = A L^2 A', \]
which also gives the \emph{biplot}:
the rows of $U$ represent the observations,
those of $AL$ the variables.

Variants include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Varimax: rotate the principal components to make the loadings more concentrated;
\item Sparse PCA (SCoTLASS);
\item Robust PCA,
  \[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{L,S} \n{L}_* + \lambda \n{S}_1 \text{ such that } X = L + S; \]
\item Functional PCA;
\item Symbolic PCA (PCA for intervals, histograms, etc.).
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evaluation of the importance of criteria\\ for the selection of cryptocurrencies}\\
  N.A. van Heerden et al.
\end{book}
To combine predictors $x_1,\dots,x_m$ of $y$, use a linear combination with weights:
\begin{align*}
  w_j &= 1/m \\
  w_j &\propto \sigma_j \\
  w_j &\propto \sigma_j \sum_i (1-\rho_{ij}) \\
  w_j &\propto H_j \text{ (entropy)} \\
  w_j &\propto \dfrac{ d_j^- }{ d_j^- + d_j^+ } \\
  \text{where }
  d_j^+ &= d(x_j,y) \\
  d_j^- &= d(x_j,-y).
\end{align*}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning-based robust optimization:\\ procedures and statistical guarantees}\\
  L.J. Hong et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The chance-constrained program
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(x) \\
  \text{Such that} & \operatorname*{P}\limits_{ \xi \sim P } \bigl[ f(x, \xi) \in A \bigr ] \geq 1-\epsilon 
\end{array}
\]
can be relaxed to a robust optimization problem
by replacing the constraint with
\[ \forall \xi \in U \ g(x,\xi) \in A \]
for some set $U$ (uncertainty set) such that
\[ \operatorname*{P}_{ \xi \in P } \bigl[ \xi \in U ] \geq 1 - \epsilon. \]
If $P$ is only known from a sample, split the data in two:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use the first part to define the shape of $U$
  (ellipsoid, convex hull, union of ellipsoids or polytopes);
\item Use the second to calibrate the size of $U$, such that
  \[
  \operatorname*{P}_{ \text{data} \sim P^n }
  \biggl[
    \operatorname*{P}_{\xi \sim P } \bigl[ \xi \in U(\text{data}) \bigr] \geq 1-\epsilon
  \biggr]
  \geq 1-\delta.
  \]
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{From data to decisions:\\ distributionally robust optimization is optimal}\\
  B.P.G. van Parys et al.
\end{book}
For the optimization problem
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \\
  \text{To minimize} & c(x,P) \\
  \text{Where} & c(x,P) = \E\limits_{ \xi \sim P } \gamma(x,\xi) 
\end{array}
\]
use the distributionally robust predictor
\[ \hat c ( c, \hat P ) = \sup_{ \text{KL}( \hat P \| P ) \leq r } c(x,P)  \]
where $\hat P$ is the empirical distribution:
the out-of-sample disappointment
$P\bigl[ c(x,P) > \hat c(x,\hat P) \bigr] $
decays exponentially (uniformally) for all $x$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fractional growth portfolio investment}\\
  A.E. Brockwell (2021)
\end{book}
The Kelly portfolio, investing fractions $k=\Sigma^{-1} ( \mu - r \mathbf 1 )$
of the wealth in each asset,
maximizes the expected log-return
\[ L = \E \biggl[ \delta^{-1} \log \dfrac{ A_{t+\delta} }{ A_t } \biggr] , \]
but the variance
\[ V = \var \biggl[ \delta^{-1} \log \dfrac{ A_{t+\delta} }{ A_t } \biggr] \]
can be very high.
The \emph{fractional Kelly portfolio} invests a fraction $\alpha k$, $\alpha \in [0,1]$,
and has log-return expectation and variance
\begin{align*}
  L &= r + ( \alpha + \tfrac12 \alpha^2 ) S^2 \\
  V &= \alpha^2 S^2   
\end{align*}
where $S = \sqrt{
  (\mu - r \mathbf 1) ' \Sigma^{-1} (\mu - r \mathbf 1)
}$
is the Sharpe ratio of the Kelly portfolio.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Arbitrage-free implied volatility surface generation with variational autoencoders}\\
  B.X. Ning et al.
\end{book}
To generate arbitrage-free synthetic implied volatility (IV) surfaces:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit a stochastic differential equation (SDE) to the volatility surface, for each day;
\item Train a VAR on the SDE parameters;
\item Sample from the VAE;
\item Compute the IV surface from the corresponding SDE.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Regularization is all you need:\\ simple neural nets can excel on tabular data}\\
  A. Kadra et al. 
\end{book}
A simple MLP with a cocktail of regularizers,
with suitable hyperparameter tuning (BOHB, 4 days),
outperforms traditional machine learning (gradient boosted trees) on tabular data:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Batchnorm;
\item Stochastic weight averaging, \ie, averaging the local optima encountered on the optimization path;
\item Lookahead optimizer; % ?
\item Snapshot ensembles: ensembles of minima obtained after restarts;
\item Weight decay ($L^2$);
\item Dropout (various shapes);
\item Skip connections (shakedrop, shake-shake);
\item Mixup: data augmentation with convex combinations of training samples
\item Cutout: mask subsets of the input; 
\item Cutmix: Mixup + Cutout;
\item FGSM (adversarial training).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Examining the dynamic asset market linkages under the covid-19 global pandemic}\\
  A. Noda (2021)
\end{book}
To test for market efficiency (between 3 markets,
S\&P 500, Bitcoin, gold),
estimate a VAR
\[ x_t = \mu + A_1 x_{t-1} + \cdots + A_q x_{t-q} + \epsilon_t \]
with time-varying coefficients $A_i$,
and compute the ``joint degree of market efficiency'' $\zeta$
\begin{align*}
  \Phi &= ( I - A_1 - \cdots - A_q )^{-1} \\
  \zeta &= \max \sqrt{ (\Phi-I)' (\Phi-I) }.
\end{align*}
Under the efficient market hypothesis (EMH),
$A_1 = \cdots = A_q = 0$ and $\zeta=0$.

Those markets are getting less efficient.

