\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistent clustering\\ and a theorem of J. Kleinberg}\\
  G. Carlsson and F. M\'emoli (2008)
\end{book}
Single linkage clustering is the only functor from the category of finite metric spaces
and non-decreasing maps to the category of persistent sets
(multiscale clusterings $(X,\theta)$, there $\theta(r)$ is interpreted as the partition of $X$ at scale $r$)
that
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Preserves the underlying set
\item Gives the expected persistent set for 2-point spaces $\Delta_2(\delta)$:
  $\theta(r)$ is $\{\{*\},\{*\}\}$ is $r<\delta$ and $\{\{*,*\}\}$ otherwise;
\item Gives the discrete partition for $r \ll 1$.
\end{compactitem}
Coarser categories allow more such multiscale clustering functors:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Increasing monic maps: density-based clustering;
\item Isometries: anything (too general).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Classifying clustering schemes}\\
  G. Carlsson and F. M\'emoli (2010)
\end{book}
There is no clustering scheme $(X,d) \mapsto P \in \text{Partitions}(X)$
satisfying Kleinberg's axioms: scale invariance,
surjectivity (all clusterings are possible, if we change the metric),
consistence (reducing/increasing intra/inter-cluster distances does not change the clustering) --
we need multi-scale clusterings.

Single-linkage clustering can be defined from 2-simplices $\Delta_2(\delta)$, $\delta>0$,
and morphisms $\Delta_2(\delta) \to X$. The 2-simplex $\Delta_2(\delta)$ can be replaced by other
simplices ($\Delta_m(\delta)$ is very similar to \textsc{dbscan})
or an arbitrary family of metric spaces.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Characterization, stability and convergence\\ of hierarchical clustering methods}\\
  G. Carlsson and F. M\'emoli (2010)  
\end{book}
A dendrogram $(X,\theta)$ defines an ultrametric
$(x,y) \mapsto \min\{ r \geq 0 \,:\,x$ and $y$ belong to the same component of $\theta(r)\}$.
Conversely, an ultrametric defines a dendrogram.
Single linkage clustering corresponds to the maximal subdominant ultrametric.

The distorsion of a map of metric spaces $f : (X,d) \to (Y,d)$ is
\[ \text{dis}(f) = \max_{ x, x' \in X } \va{ d(x,x') - d( fx, fx' ) }. \]
The joint distorsion of $f:X\to Y$ and $g:Y\to X$ measures the degree to which they are inverses,
\[ \text{dis}(f,g) = \max_{\substack{ x \in X \\ y \in Y }} \va{ d(x,gy) - d(fx,y) }. \]
The \emph{Gromov-Hausdorff distance} between $(X,d)$ and $(Y,d)$ is
\[ \tfrac12 \min_{f,g} \max \bigl\{
\operatorname{dis} f, \operatorname{dis} g, \operatorname{dis} (f,g) 
\bigr\}. \]

Single linkage hierarchical clustering $(X,d) \to (X,u)$
is a contraction for the Gromov-Hausdorff distance.
Average linkage and complete linkage are not continuous.

