\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Building probabilistic causal models\\ using collective intelligence}\\
  O. Laudy et al. (2021)
\end{book}
CausalityLink (commercial) extracts data from text:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Numeric indicators (GDP, etc.), from a large ontology, with timestamps and duration;
\item Trends: changes in those indicators over some time span;
\item Events, with start and end date;
\item Causal links: indicator/trend/event $\to$ indicator/\allowbreak trend.
\end{compactitem}

Use those links to build a Bayesian network:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with the nodes you are interested in;
\item Add edges between them, starting with the strongest ones, but only if you do not introduce a cycle.
\end{compactitem}
Use two states (up, down);
estimate the root note probabilities from the past 90 days (with a 14-day half-life);
estimate the single-cause probabilities $P(B|A)$ from count data.
For multiple causes, combine $P(B|A_i)$ into $P(B|A_1,\dots,A_k)$
using a log-linear model
\begin{align*}
  & P(A_1,\dots,A_k,B) \propto \\
  & \qquad\qquad \exp \Bigl[
  \mu + \sum \lambda_{ A_\ell = i_\ell } + \lambda_{B=j} + \sum \lambda_{\substack{A_\ell - i_\ell \\ B = j}}
  \Bigr],
\end{align*}
where $i_\ell, j \in \{ \textsc{up}, \textsc{down} \}.$

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Foundations of reinforcement learning\\ with applications in finance}\\
  A. Rao and T. Jelvis (2021)
\end{book}
% Incomplete book draft: the end (advanced RL algorithms) is missing

\emph{Reinforcement learning} (RL),
or \emph{stochastic control} is the study of sequential optimal decisions under uncertainty.
The environment is described by a \emph{Markov decision process} (MDP),
\ie, the conditional distribution of reward $r$ and next state $s'$ given current state $s$ and action $a$,
\[ P(r,s' | s,a); \]
we want to find the \emph{policy} $\pi(a|s)$ maximizing the (expected) return, \ie,
the discounted sum of future rewards
$G_t = \sum_{ s \geq t} \gamma^{s-t} R_t$.

One can distinguish between
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Prediction}: Computing the value function
  $V^\pi(s) - \E[ G_0 | S_0 = s ]$,
  for a given policy $\pi$;
\item \emph{Control}: computing the optimal value function $V^*$, and the corresponding optimal policy $\pi^*$;
\item \emph{Planning}: solving a known MDP (often, with dynamic programming);
\item \emph{Learning}: solving an unknown MDP.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{1.} \emph{Inventory management} can be modeled as an MDP:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item State: number of items on inventory, constrained to be at most $N$;
\item Action: number of items to order (there might be a (fixed) delivery delay);
\item Stochastics: $\text{demand} \sim \text{Poisson}(\lambda)$.
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Dynamic pricing for end-of-season products} can also be modeled as an MDP''
assume the demand is $\text{Poisson}(\lambda)$, with $\lambda$ a known function of price.

\textbf{3.} The \emph{Bellman policy operator}
$B^\pi : V \mapsto R + \gamma P V$
is a contraction: it has a fixed point.
\emph{Policy iteration} oterates it to compute $V^\pi$.
\emph{Value iteration} iterates the \emph{Bellman optimality operator}
$B^* : V \mapsto \max_a ( R + \gamma P V )$: it alternates two steps,
\begin{align*}
  V &\leftarrow V^\pi && \text{policy evaluation} \\
  \pi &\leftarrow \text{greedy}(\pi) && \text{policy improvement}.
\end{align*}

\textbf{6.} \emph{Merton's portfolio problem} is an analytic solution
to the dynamic asset allocation and comsumption problem.

\textbf{7.} The \emph{optimal exercise} of an Americal ooption in the binomial tree model
can be computed with dynamic programming;
this generalizes to more general \emph{optimal stopping} problems.

In a (no-arbitrage) incomplete market,
option prices are not uniquely defined.
\emph{Superhedging} computes upper and lower bounds, but they are often too faar apart.
Instead, one can compute the price maximizing some expected utility, e.g., CARA
(expected utility \emph{indifference pricing}).

\textbf{8.} \emph{Optimal execution} can be modeled as an MDP:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item State: price, number of shares left to sell, time left;
\item Action: number of shares sold;
\item Reward: utility of the sales proceeds, after correcting the price with the temporary price impact
  (``eating into the book'');
\item Stochastics: the price follows a random walk, corrected with the permanent price impact.
\end{compactitem}
For (unrealistic) linear price impacts,
we can solve the Bellman equation.

\textbf{8.} \emph{Optimal market making} can be modeled as an MDP:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item State: mid-price, current P\&L, inventory;
\item Actions: bid and ask prices and quantities;
\item Reward: utility of P\&L and inventory value;
\item Stochastics: random walk for the mid-price; random number of shares bought and sold.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{9.} Many RL prediction algorithms are based on the update rule
\[ V(S_t) \leftarrow V(S_t) + \alpha \bigl( \text{target} - V(S_t) \bigr) \]
for different targets:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Monte Carlo: $G_t$;
\item TD (temporal difference):
  $R_{t+1} + \gamma V(S_{t+1})$;
\item $n$-step TD,
  $G_{t,2} = R_{t+1} + \gamma R_{t+2} + \gamma^2 V(S_{t+2})$,
  or, more generally, $G_{t,k}$;
\item TD($\lambda$), an exponentially-weighted average of the $G_{t,k}$, which can be efficiently
  computed (without waiting for the end of the episode) with \emph{eligibility traces}.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{10.} For control, if the MDP is unknown, we need to replace the state value function $V$
with the state-action value funtion $Q$, abd alternate
policy evaluation (Monte Carlo, TD, TD($\lambda$) -- for $Q$, TD is called SARSA),
and policy improvement ($\epsilon$-greedy rather than greedy, to explore enough).

But this is \emph{on-policy}.
If the target and behaviour policies, $\pi$ and $\mu$ are different (\emph{off-policy}):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Q-learning uses $R_{t+1} + \gamma \max_a Q(S_{t+1},a)$ as target;
\item Importance sampling multiplies the SARSA updates
  with $\pi(S_t,A_t) / \mu(S_t,A_t)$ (for one step).
\end{compactitem}

Convergence is not guaranteed if we combine bootstrapping (using $V$ itself in the target),
off-policy learning, and function approximation.

