Do not optimize a sum of squared residuals, but a utility function.
\begin{align*}
  & \sum_t \dfrac{ ( 1 + r_{\text{port},t} ) ^ { 1 - \gamma } }{ 1 - \gamma } && \text{utility}\\
  & r_{\text{port},t} = \sum w_{i,t-1} r_{i,t} && \text{portfolio returns} \\
  & w_{i,t} = f_\theta( x_{i,t} ) && \text{portfolio weights} \\
  & x_{i,t} && \text{stock characteristics}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Constructing long-short stock portfolios \\ with a new listwise learn-to-rank algorithm}\\
  X. Zhang et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Learning-to-rank models for information retrieval
\[ \text{Loss} = \sum \underset{\text{relevance}}{f_k} \cdot
\underset{\substack{\text{discount}\\\text{factor}}}{ \dfrac{ 1 }{ \log(1+k) } } \]
focus on best-ranked items:
adapt them for stock selection by focusing on both the top and bottom items
bny generalizing the Placket--Luce probability distribution on $\mathfrak S_n$
(ListMLE)
\[ P[\sigma] = \prod_i \dfrac{ \theta_{ \sigma^{-1}(i) } }{
  \sum_{ k \geq i } \theta_ { \sigma^{-1} ( k ) }
}
\]
to consider long-short pairs (instead of long-only items)
$ \theta_i \rightsquigarrow \theta_{2n+1-i}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Imputation, estimation\\ and missing data in finance}\\
  G. DiCesare (2006)
\end{book}
Comparisons of estimators of the parameters of a
partially-pbserved multivariate Brownian motion:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item EM algorithm;
\item Impute-solve:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Simulate $X_{\text{mis}} | X_{\text{obs}}, \theta_k$;
  \item Estimate $\hat \theta$ from the completed dataset;
  \item Update $\theta_{k+1} = \theta_k + \gamma_k ( \hat \theta - \theta_k )$;
  \end{compactitem}
\item Impute-posterior: 
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Sample $X_{\text{mis}} | X_{\text{obs}}, \theta_k$;
  \item Sample $\theta | X_{\text{obs}}, X_{\text{mis}}$.
  \end{compactitem}
\end{compactitem}

The EM algorithm can be implemented with the \emph{sweep operator}
\[
\text{sweep}_k : \left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \RR^{p\times p} & \longrightarrow & \RR^{p\times p} \\
  G & \longmapsto & H :
  \smash{
  \begin{aligned}[t]
    h_{kk} & = - 1 / g_{kk} \\
    h_{jk} & = h_{kj} = g_{jk} / g_{kk}
    % \text{ if } j \neq k
    \\
    h_{j\ell} & = h_{\ell j} = g_{j\ell} - \dfrac{ g_{jk} g_{j\ell} }{ g_{kk} } \\
  \end{aligned}
  }
\end{array}
\right.
\vrule width 0pt depth 1.5cm
\]
\[ \text{sweep}_1 \cdots \text{sweep}_p G = - G^{-1} \det G \]
on the sufficient statistic
\[ T = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \mathbf 1 ' Y \\ Y' \mathbf 1 & Y' Y \end{pmatrix}
\qquad
\Theta = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & \mu \\ \mu \Sigma \end{pmatrix}.
\]

Some of those computations can be generalized to SDEs:
for instance, after Euler discretization, one can sample from
$X_i | X_{i-1}, X_{i+1}$ with Metropolis-Hastings.

From the Markovian property,
we can cut the data into blocks along dates for which all the variables are observed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic style analysis and applications}\\
  M. Markov et al. (2004)
\end{book}
Return-based sstyle analysis is a constrained regressionof a fund's returns against benchmarks,
\[ R_t = \alpha + \sum_i \beta_i r_{it} + \epsilon_t, \quad \beta \geq 0, \ \beta' \mathbf 1 = 1. \]
Allow for time-changing exposures $\beta_{it}$
and add a ``smoothness'' penalty on $\sum \n{ \beta_{it} - \beta_{i,t-1} }^2$
(smoothness would be a penalty on $\Delta^2 \beta$, not $\Delta \beta$;
one could also use a Kalman filter).

Choose the scale of the penalty to maximize $R^2$.

To find the positionof the most likekely breakpoint,
compare all the models with a penalty on
\[ \sum_{\substack{i, t \\ t \neq t_0 }} \n{ \beta_{i,t} - \beta_{i,t-1} }^2, \]
for all values of $t_0$
(one could try the fused lasso instead).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conditional distribution in portfolio theory}\\
  E. Qian and S. Gorman (2001)
\end{book}
The simplified Black-Litterman framework can be generalized for views on returns, volatilities and correlations.
In the case of two assets, $r_1$, $r_2$, and views
$\tilde \mu_2 = E[r_2]$ and $\tilde \sigma_2 ^2 = \var[ r_2 ]$,
regress $r_1 \sim r_2$:
\begin{align*}
  r_1 &= \mu_1 + \rho \dfrac{ \sigma_1 }{ \sigma_2 } ( r_2 - \mu_2 ) + \epsilon \\
  \epsilon &\sim N\bigl( 0, \sigma_2^2 ( 1 - \rho^2) \bigr)
\end{align*}
and compute $E[r_1]$, $\var[r_1]$, $\cov[r_1,r_2]$
as a function of $\rho$, $\mu_1$, $\mu_2$, $\sigma_1$, $\sigma_2$, $\tilde \mu_2$, $\tilde \sigma_2$.

For a view on $d = r_1 - r_2$, regress $r_1$ and $r_2$ against $d$.

In the general case $r \sim N(\mu,\Sigma)$ with views on $v = Pr$,
regress $r \sim v$:
\begin{align*}
  r &= \mu + \Sigma P' \Sigma_v ^{-1} ( r - \mu_v ) + \epsilon \\
  \epsilon & \sim N( 0, \Sigma - \Sigma P' \Sigma_v^{-1} P \Sigma ) \\
  \mu_v &= P\mu \\
  \Sigma_v &= P \Sigma P'.
\end{align*}
The posterior is then
\begin{align*}
  \tilde \mu &= \mu + \Sigma P' \Sigma_v ^{-1} ( \tilde \mu _v - \mu_v ) \\
  \tilde \Sigma &= \Sigma + \Sigma P' ( \Sigma_v^{-1} \tilde \Sigma_v \Sigma_v^{-1} - \Sigma_v^{-1} ) P \Sigma .
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Global portfolio optimization revisited: a least discrimination alternative to Black-Litterman}\\
  J. P\'ezier (2007)
\end{book}
% Not clear: imprecise language, bad notation
Given a prior distribution $p$ (e.g., $X \sim N(\mu,\Sigma)$)
and constraints on a posterior distribution $\tilde p$
(e.g., $E[PX]=v$, as in Black-Litterman,
or $PX=v$, or $\var[PX] = V$),
look for the posterior distribution $\tilde p$,
satisfying those constraints,
and minimizing the difference in certainty equivalent of the optimal (dynamic) strategy
(Black-Litterman only considers static strategies, \ie, portfolios)
\[ \text{CE} \bigl( f_{\tilde p} (x) \mid \tilde p \bigr) -
\text{CE} \bigl( f_p(X) \mid p \bigr).
\]
For a payoff (random variable) $Y$ and a utility function $u$,
the certainty equivalent is defined as
$ \E \bigl[ u(Y) \bigr] = u\bigl( \text{CE}[Y] \bigr). $

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fully flexible views: theory and practice}\\
  A. Meucci (2008)
\end{book}
Given a prior distribution on asset returns (and/or risk factors),
and views, \ie, constraints on some of the generalized moments,
blend them:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take $N$ samples from the prior;
  see them as a uniform distribution $p$
  (you could also use importance sampling);
\item Solve the optimization problem
  \[
  \hspace*{-5mm}
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \text{Find} & \text{$q$, probability distribution} \\ & \text{on the samples} \\
    \text{To minimize} & \text{the relative entropy } \mathscr E(q, p ) = \displaystyle \int q \log \dfrac{ q }{ p } \\
    \text{Such that} & \text{the constraints be satisfied}
  \end{array}
  \]
\item If there are confidence levels on the views, use the mixture
  $(1-c) p + c q$
  (or $\sum c_i q_i$ of there are several views);
\item Use for portfolio construction.
\end{compactitem}

In the Gaussian case:
\begin{align*}
& X \sim N(\mu,\Sigma) \\
& E[ QX ] = \tilde \mu \\
& \operatorname{Var}[ GX ] = \tilde \Sigma \\[2mm]
&X \sim N(\bar \mu, \bar \Sigma ) \\[2mm]
&\bar \mu = \mu + \Sigma Q' ( Q \Sigma Q' )^{-1} ( \tilde \mu - Q \mu ) \\
&\bar \Sigma = \Sigma G' \bigl[ ( Q \Sigma Q' )^{-1} \tilde \Sigma ( Q \Sigma Q' )^{-1} - ( Q \Sigma Q' )^{-1} \bigr] G \Sigma.
\end{align*}

In the general case, to impose constraints on expectations, volatilities and correlations, use
\begin{align*}
  \sum_j q_j X_{jk} &= m_k \\
  \sum_j q_j X_{jk} ^2 &= \hat m_k ^2 + \sigma_k^2 \\
  \sum_j q_j X_{jk} X_{j\ell} &= \hat m_k \hat m_\ell + \hat \sigma_k \hat \sigma_\ell c_{k\ell}
\end{align*}
(this is not quite right, because we use $\hat m$ and $\hat \sigma$ from the prior,
but it makes the constraints linear and the problem convex).

