\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Macroeconomic forecasting\\ with statistically validated knowledge graphs}\\
  S. Tilly and G. Livan (2021)
\end{book}
To forecast/nowcast industrial production:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use the \emph{global database of events, language and tone}
  (GDELT, available via Google BigQuery) to build theme co-occurrence graphs;
\item Filter the themes relevant to economic growth with an LSTM, trained on 1000 manually labeled events (4000 nodes, 1 million edges);
\item Compute its backbone using a \emph{disparity filter}:
  normalize the weights of the edges connected to a given node
  so they sum up to 1 and apply the Benjamini-Hochberg (BH) procedure;
\item Compute the \emph{portrait divergence} to compare the graph with the previous month's;
\item Compute the eigenvector centralities of all the nodes;
\item Reduce the dimension of the centralities with PLS;
\item Add the Baltik Dry index (a shipping index)
  and crude oil price to the predictors;
\item Conclude with Granger cauisality, and a factor-augmented AR model.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An information-theoretic,\\ all-scales approach to comparing networks}\\
  J.P. Bagrow and E.M. Bollt (2019)
\end{book}
The \d{network portrait} of a graph is a matrix
$B_{\ell,k} = $number of nodes having $k$ nodes at distance $\ell$.
The 0th row contains the number of nodes;
the first row is the degree distribution;
each row defines a probability distribution.

<<NetworkPortrait,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Network portrait
## Real data: https://figshare.com/articles/dataset/American_College_Football_Network_Files/93179
# g <- read_graph("footballTSEinput.gml", format='gml')
library(DBQR)
library(igraph)
library(tidyverse)

network_portrait <- function(g, plot=TRUE, ...) { 
  d <- distances(g)
  f <- function(u) {
    u <- table(u)
    u <- data.frame( 
        distance = as.numeric( names(u) ),
        size     = as.numeric(u)
    )
    u
  }
  d <- apply(d, 1, f)
  d <- do.call( rbind, d )
  d <- d %>% group_by(distance, size) %>% count
  d <- as.data.frame(d)
  d$distance[ d$distance == Inf ] <- max( d$distance[d$distance != Inf] ) + 1
  nr <- max(d$distance) + 1
  nc <- max(d$size)
  r <- matrix(0, nr=nr, nc=nc)
  dimnames(r) <- list(
    distance = 0:max(d$distance),
    size     = 1:max(d$size)
  )
  r[ cbind( 1+d$distance, d$size ) ] <- d$n
  if( plot ) {
    #myimage( as.numeric(colnames(r)), as.numeric(rownames(r)), t(r[nr:1,]), ylab='distance', xlab='size', ylim = c(nr-.5,-.5) )
    myimage( as.numeric(colnames(r)), as.numeric(rownames(r)), t((1+log(r))[nr:1,]), ylab='distance', xlab='size', ylim = c(nr-.5,-.5), ... )
    return( invisible(r) )
  } else {
    return( r )
  }
}

op <- par( mfrow=c(1,3), las=1 )
g <- sample_gnp(500,.01)
network_portrait(g, main = 'Erdos-Renyi', xlim=c(0,300))

g <- sample_pa(500)
network_portrait(g, main = 'Barabasi-Albert')

#g <- sample_forestfire(500, .1)
#network_portrait(g, main = 'Forest fire')

#pm <- cbind( c(.1, .001), c(.001, .05) )
#g <- sample_sbm(1000, pref.matrix=pm, block.sizes=c(300,700))
#network_portrait(g, main='SBM')

#g <- sample_smallworld(2, 30, 1, 0)
#network_portrait(g, main='Grid')

g <- sample_smallworld(2, 30, 1, .02)
network_portrait(g, main='Small world')

par(op)
@

\relax

For weighted graphs, compute all shortest paths and bin their lengths

The \emph{network portrait divergence}
compares graphs using the Jensen-Shanon divergence between the distributions
$P(k,\ell) \propto k B_{\ell,k}$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portraits of complex networks}\\
  J.P. Bagrow et al. (2007)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Comparing methods for comparing networks}\\
  T. Tantardini et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Distances between networks include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Distance between the similarity matrices $S = (I + \epsilon^2 D - \epsilon A)^{-1}$,
  where $A$ is the adjacency matrix and $D$ the degree;
\item Distances between graphlet counts;
  one can also compute them on each egonet, aggregate them (NetDis), and use PCA;
\item Distance between the graphlet correlation matrices, obtained by counting, for each node, in how many graphlets of each type it is, and computing the correlation matrix between those counts;
\item Distance between the spectra of the Laplacians, e.g., via the heat signature trace
  $ h(t) = \sum_i e^{ - \lambda_i t };$
\item Graph kernels, TDA, portrait divergence, etc.
\end{compactitem}
The graphlet correlation distance (GCD) gives the best results.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
