List of 300+ investment signals. Unusable code (Stata) and data (commercial).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The most general methodology\\ to create a valid correlation matrix\\ for risk management and option pricing}\\
  R. Rebonato and P. J\"ackel (1999)
\end{book}
Correlation matrices can be parametrized as $C = BB'$, where the rows of $B$ are on the sphere $\SS^{n-1}$.
\begin{align*}
  b_{ij} &= \cos \theta_{ij} \prod_{k=1}^{j-1} \sin \theta_{ik} \qquad 1 \leq j \leq n-1 \\
  b_{in} &= \prod_{k=1}^{n-1} \sin \theta_{ik} \\
  \theta &\in \RR^{ n \times (n-1) }
\end{align*}

The closest correlation matrix can be obtained by solving
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\theta \n{ \hat C - C }_F ^2. \]
As a starting point for the optimization:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Eigenvalue decomposition $C = S \Lambda S'$;
\item Zero out the negative eigenvalues: $\Lambda_+$;
\item Compute $B_0 = S \sqrt{ \Lambda_+ }$;
\item Normalize the rows of $B_0$: $B$.
\end{compactitem}
If $C$ was a valid correlation, we have $C = BB'$.

