\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Matrix evolutions: synthetic correlations\\ and explainable machine learning\\ for constructing robust investment portfolios}\\
  J. Papenbrock et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Financial correlation matrices have the following properties:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The distribution of correlations is positively skewed;
\item Eigenvalues follow the Marchenko-Pastur distribution but for
  a very large eigenvalue (market) and a couple of large eigenvalues (industries);
\item The first eigenvector has positive entries (Perron-Frobenius);
\item The correlation has a ``hierarchical structure'',
  \ie, the distances from the correlation $\sqrt{1-c_{ij}}$ are very close to the distances
  on the (single linkage) dendrogram $d_{ij}$;
  this can be measured by the \emph{cophenetic correlation}
  $\cor( \sqrt{1-c}, d )$;
\item The minimum-spanning tree is scale-free, \ie, it has a power law degree distribution.
\end{compactitem}

To generate real-looking correlation matrices,
pick matrices at random in some neighbourhood of the empirical correlation
and use multiobjective optimization (why?) to match
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The average correlation;
\item The Gini coefficient of the eigenvalues;
\item The single-linkage cophenetic correlation;
\item The sum of the negative coefficients of the first eigenvector.
\end{compactitem}

Those matrices can then be used to compare investment strategies,
e.g., risk parity and hierarchical risk parity.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MOEA/D: A multi-objective evolutionary algorithm based on decomposition}\\
  Q. Zhang and H. Li (2007)
\end{book}
To solve the multiobjective optimization problem
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{ x \in \Omega } f_1(x), \dots , f_n(x) , \]
reduce it to a set of 1-dimensional problems
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_{ x \in \Omega } \sum_i \lambda_i f_i(x) \]
(where $\lambda$ is fixed)
and solve them sequentially, starting each optimization with the solution of the closest $\lambda$.

Instead, MOEA/D solves the problems simultaneously:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $P_j:\ \operatorname*{Maximize}\limits_{x \in \Omega} \sum_i \lambda_{ij} f_i(x) $ subproblems;
\item $x_{jt}$: best solution to $P_j$ after $t$ steps;
\item $x_{j,t+1}$: computed from the $x_{j',t}$ for the subproblems
  where $\lambda_{\cdot j'}$ is close to $\lambda_{\cdot j}$
\end{compactitem}
Contrary to domination-based multiobjective optimization,
decomposition-based algorithms can deal with higher-dimensional problems
(if there are many objectives, most candidate solutions are non-dominated).

