\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Imputation of multivariate time series data: performance benchmark for multiple imputation and spectral techniques}\\
  J. Bauer et al. (2013)
\end{book}
First look for missingness patterns:
proportion of missing data, run lengths, etc.
(CDS data, 1000 tickers, 10 maturities).

Data imputation with empirical orthogonal functions (DINEOF -- EOF is another term
for left and right singular vectors)
uses the SVD to recover the missing data,
progressively increasing the number of singular values retained.

Singular spectrun analysis (SSA) adds lags
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
  x_1 & x_2 & \cdots & \\
  \vdots & \vdots & & \vdots \\
  x_L & x_{L+1} & \cdots & x_N 
\end{pmatrix}
\]
and reconstructs the signal by averaging over the anti-diagonals.
It can be generalized to multivariate SSA (MSSA).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Temporal regularized matrix factorization\\ for high-dimensional time series prediction }\\
  H.F. Yu and N. Rao (2016)
\end{book}
Matrix completion can be used to model time series,
in high dimension, with missing values: $ Y \approx F X$.
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_{F,X} \sum_{int} ( y_{it} - f_i ' x_t )^2 + \lambda_1 R(F) + \lambda_2 R(X)
\]
The regularizer $R(X)$ could come from a graph
\[ R(X) = \sum_t \n{ x_t }^2 + \sum_{s,t} w_{st} \n{ x_t - x_s }^2 \]
if the weights are chosen beforehand.
Instead, use the negative log-likelihood of some time series model
\[ R(X) = - \log P(x_1, \dots, x_T | \theta ), \]
e.g., an AR model
\[ R(X) = \sum_{ t > L } \n{ x_t - \sum_{\ell = 1 }^L \theta_\ell x_{t-\ell} }^2 + \sum_t \n{ x_t }^2 \]
and a penalty (prior) for $\theta$.

Applications include missing value imputation and forecasting.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic sequential matrix factorization}\\
  O.D. Akyildiz et al. (2019)
\end{book}
In the matrix factorization problem
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{C,X} \n{ Y - CX }, \]
add a prior on $C$ (matrix variate Gaussian)
and $X$ (nonlinear state space model).
\begin{align*}
  s & \sim \text{IG} \\
  C & \sim \text{MN}( C_0, I, s V_0 ) \\
  x_0 & \sim \text{N}(\mu_0, s P_0 ) \\
  x_k & \sim \text{N}( f_\theta(x_{k-1}), s Q_0 )\\
  y_k & \sim \text{N}(C x_k , s R_0)
\end{align*}
(For $s$ constant, it is Gaussian, but if we marginalize it, we get more robust Student distributions.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mixtures, EM and missing data}\\
  B. Stewart (2017)
\end{book}
We want to estimate $\mu = \E[X]$ and $\Sigma = \var[ X ]$
in presence of missing data.

0. Start with initial estimates, e.g, means and variances (diagonal $\Sigma$) from available observations.

1. For each missingness pattern, estimate a regression
$ X_{\text{mis}} = X_{\text{obs}} \beta + \epsilon$.
We can compute $\beta$ and $\sigma^2 = \var \epsilon$ from $\mu$, $\Sigma$.
We this replace each missing value with a distribution
$ X_{\text{mis},i} \sim N( X_{\text{obs},i} \beta, \sigma^2 )$.
More precisely, we will need the expectations
$\E[ X_{\text{mis},i} ] $ and $\E[ X_{\text{mis},i} X_{\text{mis},i} ' ]$
(E-step).

2. Using the expectations $\E[X]$ and $\E[XX']$,
re-estimate $\mu,\Sigma$, with MLE (M-step).

3. Iterate until convergence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Amelia II: a program for missing data}\\
  J. Honaker et al. (2011)
\end{book}
To impute missing data, the EMB (EM with bootstrap) algorithm:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bootstraps missing data $m=5$ times
\item Models each bootstrap dataset as a Gaussian $N(\mu,\Sigma)$,
  using the EM algorithm for MLE with missing values;
\item Imputes the missing values with their conditional expectation ($m$ times).
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What to do about missing values\\ in time series cross-section data}\\
  J. Honaker and G. King (2010)
\end{book}
To allow smooth interpolation when needed,
add basis functions to the data (e.g., with cubic splines);
also add lags and leads of the variables.
Implementation in \texttt{Amelia}.

