%\end{book}
%No.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{mice:\\ multiple imputation by chained equations in R}\\
  S. van Buuren and K. Groothuis-Oudshooin (2011)
\end{book}
Iterate $T$ times:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sample $\theta_1$ from $\theta_1 | Y_1^{\text{obs}} , Y_2, \dots, Y_p$;
\item Sample $Y_1^{\text{mis}}$ from $Y_1^{\text{mis}} | \theta_1, Y_1^{\text{obs}} , Y_2, \dots, Y_p$;
\item \dots
\item Sample $\theta_p$;
\item Sample $Y_p^{\text{mis}}$.
\end{compactitem}
Those conditional models (often, regression or GLMs)
need not be compatible:
they may not come from a well-defined joint distribution -- this does not seem to be a problem.
Generate 5 such implied datasets;
fit your model on each of them; pool those models.
The \texttt{VIM} package provides a few more plots.

