\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=1.7,inner sep = 3pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0]
  \node (I) at (0,1) {input};
  \node[fill=green!30] (E) at (1,1) {Encoder};
  \node (L) at (2,1) {latent};
  \node[fill=blue!20] (D) at (3,1) {Decoder};
  \node (O) at (4,1) {output};
  \node (P) at (1.5,0) {prior};
  \node[fill=red!20] (Di) at (3,0) {Discriminator};
  \draw[->,thick] (I) -- (E);
  \draw[->,thick] (E) -- (L);
  \draw[->,thick] (L) -- (D);
  \draw[->,thick] (D) -- (O);
  \draw[->,thick] ([yshift=-.5mm] P.east) -- ([yshift=-.5mm] Di.west);
  \draw[->,thick] (L) to[out=-90, in=180] ([yshift=1mm] Di.west);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning sampling in financial statement audits using VQ autoencoding neural networks}
  M. Schreyer (2020)\\
\end{book}
The \emph{VQ-VAE}
\[ x \overset{q_\theta}{\longmapsto} z_e \longmapsto z_q \overset{p_\phi}{\longmapsto} \tilde x \]
adds a ``quantize'' operation to the autoencoder,
which maps the latent representation $z_e$ to the closest vector $z_q$
among a (learned) codebook $e_\psi$.
The loss has 4 terms:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Reconstruction from $z_q$ [depends on $\psi$, $\phi$];
\item Reconstruction from $z_e$, skipping the reconstruction step [$\theta$, $\phi$];
\item $\n{ z_e - \bar z_q }$ to move the latent representation towards the codebook vectors [$\theta$];
\item $\n{ \bar z_e - z_q }$ to move the codebook vectors towards the latent representation [$\psi$];
\end{compactitem}
where $\bar \cdot$ is the \emph{stop-gradient} operation:
identity in the forward pass, zero in the backward pass -- \ie, the quantity is considered constant.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Counterexample-guided learning\\ of monotonic neural networks}\\
  A. Sivaraman et al. (2020)
\end{book}
To ensure that a trained model is monotonic,
compute its (upper and lower) \emph{monotonic envelope}:
$\bar f(x_0)$ comes from the maximum monotonic violation for $x \leq x_0$
and can be computed with an SMT solver -- more precisely,
an \emph{OMT} (optimization modulo theories) solver
such as OptiMathSAT proprietary.

One can also include those examples at training time,
adding (to the next batch)
counterexamples
for each sample in the current batch,
and assigning them the average label.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Automatically learning compact quality aware surrogates for optimization problems}\\
  K. Wang et al. (2020)
\end{book}
In the \emph{predict-then-optimize} framework
\[ \xi \longmapsto \theta \longmapsto \argmin_x f_\theta(x) \]
simplify the optimizatyion layer
by replacing $x \in \RR^n$ with $x=Py$, $y \in \RR^m$, $m \ll n$,
where $P$ is \emph{learned} (rather than random).

[Implementation with \texttt{cvxpylayers} -- \texttt{qpth} is older and limited to quadratic programming.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generative minimization networks:\\ training GANs without competition}\\
  P. Grnarova et al. (2021)
\end{book}
To find the Nash equilibria of a min-max game
\begin{align*}
  \text{Player 1} &:\ \min_u \max_v M(u,v) \\
  \text{Player 2} &:\ \max_v \min_u M(u,v)
\end{align*}
the often-used gradient descent ascent (GDA) need not converge.
Instead, minimize the \emph{duality gap}
\[ \text{DG}(u,v) = \max_{ v' } M(u,v') - \min_{u'} M(u',v). \]
For GANs,
\[
M(u,v) = \E_{\substack{
    z_1 \sim \text{data} \\
    z_2 \sim \text{noise}
}}
\bigl[
  \log D_v( z_1) + \log( 1 + D_v G_u z_2 ) 
\bigr].
\]
To estimate the duality gap,
run $k$ steps of gradient descent
to find
$u_{\text{worst}}$ and $v_{\text{worst}}$.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ultra data-efficient GAN training: drawing\\ a lottery ticker first, then training it toughly}\\
  T. Chen et al. (2021)
\end{book}
To train a GAN with little data:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Look for a lottery ticket, with \emph{iterative magnitude pruning}
  (train for $t$ epochs and 100 samples,
  prune the smallest weights rewind);
\item Train with adversarial augmentation: perturbations to the intermediate features.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bootstrap your own latent:\\ a new approach to self-supervised learning}\\
  J.B. Grill (2020)
\end{book}
BYOL performs self-supervised learning (SSL) without negative pairs by training two networks:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item An online network, which predicts the target representation of an augmented version of the input;
\item A target network, moving average of the online network.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Exploring simple Siamese\\ representation learning}\\
  X. Chen and K. He (2020)
\end{book}
To learn a latent representation,
use simple siamese networks (SimSiam):
feed two augmentattions of the same image
to two copies of the same network,
keeping one frozen (``stop-gradient'')
and use the latent representation of one to predict that of the frozen one
(that is BYOL without the moving average).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding self-supervised learning dynamics without contrastive pairs}\\
  Y. Tian
\end{book}
In BYOL/SimSiam, the stop gradient and the predictor are important;
the moving average, weight decay, predictor optimality, less so.

DirectPred does not train the predictor, but sets its wights using the PCA of its input.
  
