\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometric attribution\\ and the interaction effect}\\
  A.E. Weber (2018)
\end{book}
There are many variants of Brinson attribution: arithmetic or geometric,
with or without interaction.
\begin{align*}
  r &= \sum w_i w_i \\
  \bar r &= \sum \bar w_i \bar r_i \\
  \r_A &= \sum w_i \bar r_i \\
  \r_S &= \sum \bar w_i r_i
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
  r - \bar r &= \sum( w_i - \bar w_i) ( \bar r_i - \bar r ) + \sum \bar w_i ( r_i - \bar r_i ) + \sum I_i \\
  r = \bar r &= \sum( w_i - \bar w_i) ( \bar r_i - \bar r ) + \sum w_i ( r_i - \bar r_i ) \\
  \dfrac{ 1 + r }{ 1 + \bar r } &=
  \dfrac{ 1 + r_A }{ 1 + \bar r } \cdot \dfrac{ 1 + r }{ 1 + r_A } \\
  \dfrac{ 1 + r }{ 1 + \bar r } &=
  \dfrac{ 1 + r_A }{ 1 + \bar r } \cdot \dfrac{ 1 + r }{ 1 + r_S } \cdot
  \dfrac{ (1+\bar r)(1+r) }{ (1+r_A)(1+r_S) }
\end{align*}
(For the geometric ones, write each factor as 1+contribution.)
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk-adjusted performance attribution}\\
  J.D. Fisher and J. D'Alessandro (2018)
\end{book}
The CAPM $R_P = \alpha + \beta R_M = \alpha + R_B + (1-\beta) R_B$
suggests adding a risk term to Brinson analysis
(it is easier to outperform by taking on more risk):
\[ R_P - R_B = \alpha + (1-\beta) R_B. \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk-adjusted performance attribution}\\   % Yes, same paper title...
  D. Spaulding (2016)
\end{book}
Brinson analysis can be adjusted for risk:
replace the returns $\mu$ with
\[ \text{MM} = \dfrac{ \sigma_{\text{Benchmark}} }{ \sigma_{\text{Portfolio}} } \cdot \mu . \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Attribution hears a Who!}\\
  A. Muralidhar (2016)
\end{book}
Replace MM with
\[ M^3 = \sqrt T \dfrac{
  ( \mu_P - \tfrac12 \sigma_P^2 ) - ( \mu_B - \tfrac12 \sigma_B^2 ) 
}{
  \sqrt{
    \sigma_P^2 - \sigma_B^2 - 2 \sigma_P \sigma_B \rho
  }
} \]
where $T$ is the number of years of data.

