\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Absolute return equity risk attribution\\ and forecasting}\\
  R.A. Cooper and T. Li (2012)
\end{book}
Return attribution $\mu = \sum \mu_i$
defines a volatility and information ratio attribution:
\begin{align*}
  \sigma &= \dfrac{ \sigma^2}{ \sigma } =
  \dfrac{ \var \sum X_i }{ \sigma }
  = \dfrac{ \sum \cov(X_i,X) }{ \sigma }
  = \sum \rho_i \sigma_i \\
  \text{IR} &= \dfrac{ \mu }{ \sigma}
  = \dfrac{ \sum \mu_i }{ \sigma }
  = \sum \dfrac{ \rho_i \sigma_i }{ \sigma } \cdot \dfrac{ \mu_i }{ \rho_i \sigma_i }
  = \sum \rho_i \dfrac{ \sigma_i }{ \sigma } \text{IR}_i
\end{align*}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Effective return: a brealthrough in cummulative performance attribution}\\
  R. Surz (2010)
\end{book}
For multi-period return attribution:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Replace the time-changing returns $r_{ijt}$ with constant returns $r_{ij}$
  leading to the came cummulated returns;
\item weak them ever so slightly so that $r_i$, $\bar r_i$ and $\bar r$
  have the correct values;
\item Then, use 1-period Brinson analysis.
\end{compactitem}
  
