  Z. Chen et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Look at double-sorted portfolios,
wrt both stock characteristics and beta (for this characteristic).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Smart beta multifactor methodology:\\ mixing vs integrating}\\
  T. Chow et al. (2017)
\end{book}
To combine investment factors,
one could invest in stocks with a good overall score,
or combine
single factor portfolios --
the former has better performance, but higher turnover and concentration.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Downsampling time series\\ for visual representation}\\
  S. Steinarsson (2013)
\end{book}
Split the data into (equal-size) buckets, with the first and last points alone in their bucket.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Keeping only the median (or the mode) in each bucket tends to remove local extrema.
\item The longest line algorithm picks a point in each bucket to maximize the length of the resulting line
  (dynamic programming).
\item The \d{Douglas-Peucker} algorithm picks the point farthest away from the line from the first to the last point, and iterates until the points are sufficiently close.
\item The \emph{Visvalingam-Whyatt} algorithm measures the importance of a point as the area of the triangle it forms with its adjacent points.
\item The \emph{largest triangle} algorithm picks the point in each bucket with the largest area with the point selected in the previous bucket and the average point in the next one.
\end{compactitem}
We can use dynamic bucket sizes (to keep outliers):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with equal-sized buckets;
\item Fit a linear regression in each bucket (also include one point before and one point after);
\item Split the bucket with the largest residuals, and merge the pair with the smallest residuals.
\end{compactitem}

