\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep factor model}\\
  K. Nakagawa et al. (2018)  
\end{book}
Deep factor models (17 factors to predict the returns of 2000 Topix stocks with a fully-connected neural net)
can be interpreted with \emph{layerwise relevance propagation} (LRP)
[yes, this ignores the nonlinearity].
\begin{align*}
  \ell &:\text{ layer} \\
  i,j&: \text{ neurons} \\
  x &: \text{ activations (before non-linearity)} \\
  z &: \text{ activations (after)} \\
  g &: \text{ non-linearity} \\
  R_i^\ell &:\text{ contribution of neuron $i$} \\
  R^n &: \text{ output} \\
  R_i^\ell &= \sum_j \dfrac{ z_{ij} }{ z_{\bullet j } } R_j^{\ell+1} \\
  z_{ij} &= w_{ij}^\ell x_i^\ell \\
  x_i^{\ell+1} &=  g( z_{i\bullet} ) = g( \sum s_{ij}^\ell x_i^\ell )
\end{align*}
To improve numeric stability, replace $ z_{ij} / z_{\bullet j} $ with 
\[ 
\dfrac{ z_{ij} }{ z_{\bullet j} + \epsilon \text{sign}( z_{\bullet j} ) }
\text{ or }
\dfrac{ z_{ij}^+ }{ z_{\bullet j}^+ } - \dfrac{ z_{ij}^- }{ z_{\bullet j} ^- }
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep recurrent factor model:\\ interpretable non-linear\\ and time-varying multi-factor model}\\
  K. Nakagawa et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Layerwise relevance propagation to interpret LSTM-based factor models (500 stocks, 17 factors);
the contributions are comparable to those of a linear model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Interpreting deep learning models\\ with marginal attribution\\ by conditioning on quantiles}\\
  M. Merz et al. (2021)
\end{book}
The first- and second-order \emph{marginal attribution by conditioning on quantiles} (MACQ)
of a model $x \mapsto \mu(x)$ are
\begin{align*}
  S_j &= \E \biggl[ X_j \dfrac{ \partial \mu(X) }{ \partial X_j }
    \biggm| \mu(X) = \mu_\alpha \biggr] \\
  T_{ij} &= \E \biggl[
    X_i X_j \dfrac{ \partial^2 \mu(X) }{ \partial X_i \partial X_j }
    \biggm| \mu(X) = \mu_\alpha \biggr]
\end{align*}
where $\mu_\alpha$ is the $\alpha$ quantile of $\mu(X)$.
If the origin $0$ is chosen in the middle of the data,
\[ \mu_\alpha \approx \mu(0) + \sum_i S_i - \dfrac12 \sum_{ij} T_{ij}. \]
  
%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Monotone and partially monotone neural nets}\\
  H. Daniels and M. Velikova (2010)
\end{book}
Neural networks with just one hidden layer cannot approximate
arbitrary monotonic functions,
but with two, they can, for instance, with a \emph{min-max network}
(but it will not work well in practice).
\[ x \longmapsto \min_i \max_j \sigma \biggl( \sum w_{ijk} x_k \biggr) \]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Certified monotonic neural networks}\\
  X. Liu et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Add a penalty
\[
\E_{ x \sim \text{Unif} }
\sum_i \max \bigl\{ b, - \partial_{ x_i } f(x) \bigr\} ^2
\]
to ensure that the derivatives of $f$ are at least $b \geq 0$.

Proving that a ReLU network is monotonic can be formulated as a mixed integer program.
To speed up computations,
enforce and test monotonicity for pairs of consecutive layers
(instead of the whole network).

One can also look for monotonicity violations around a given observation.

