\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A biclustering method\\ for time series data analysis}\\
  J. Lee et al. (2009)
\end{book}
The \d{plaid model} for biclustering is
\[ Z_{ij} = \mu_0 + \sum_k ( \mu_k + \alpha_{ik} + \beta_{kj} ) \rho_{ik} \kappa_{kj} + \epsilon_{ij}, \]
where $\rho$, $\kappa$ are binary variables indicating cluster membership
(of cluster $k$, for row $i$ and column $j$),
and $\mu_k + \alpha_{ik} + \beta_{kj}$ describes cluster $k$.
The \emph{layers} (biclusters) can be extracted one by one, by alternatingly  estimating %(\mu,\alpha,\beta)$
and $(\rho,\kappa)$.

For time series, ensure that the clusters in the time dimension are convex by filling in gaps of size at most $C$,
and only keeping the largest convex component
(there is also a pruning step, limited to the interval extremities in the time dimension).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved biclustering\\ of microarray data demonstrated through\\ systematic performance tests}\\
  H. Turner et al. (2005)
\end{book}
The original plaid algorithm used alternating least squares (3 steps: $\rho$, $\kappa$ and $\mu+\alpha+\beta$);
instead, use \emph{binary least squares}
\[ \rho = \argmin_{ \rho \in \{0,1\}^n } \sum_{ij} ( z_{ij} - \rho_i x_j ) ^ 2 \]
(the objective is separable).
    
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Plaid models for gene expression data}\\
  L. Lazzeroni and A. Owen (2002)
\end{book}
(First paper on the plaid model.)
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Biclustering of expression data}\\
  Y. Cheng and G.M. Church (2000)
\end{book}
To find a $\delta$-bicluster, \ie, a short subset of rows $I$ and columns $J$
such that the \emph{mean square residue}
\[ H(I,J) = \operatorname*{Mean}_{i \in I } ( a_{ij} - a_{iJ} - a_{Ij} + a_{IJ} ) ^2 \]
be below $\delta$,
one could start with the full matrix and greedily remove the row or column reducing $H(I,J)$ the most,
until we reach $\delta$ (or no row or column reduces $H$).
Instead,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Remove the rows (resp. columns) with
  \[ d(i) = \operatorname*{Mean}_{j\in J} ( a_{ij} - a_{iJ} - a_{Ij} + a_{IJ} )^2 > \alpha H(I,J) ;\]
\item Remove the rows (columns) with the largest $d(i)$;
\item Add back rows (columns) with $d(i) \geq H(I,J)$.
\end{compactitem}
(Also look at the row variance \[ V(I,J) = \operatorname*{Mean}_{j\in J} (a_{ij} - a_{Ij})^2 \]
to reject trivial clusters.)
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Gibbs-plaid biclustering model}\\
  T. Chekouo et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Plaid model with a prior on the gene labels $\rho_{ik}$ and the condition labels $\kappa_{jk}$,
from similarities from the gene ontology (GO) annotations
and proximity in time respectively.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extracting conserved gene expression motifs from gene expression data}\\
  T.M. Murali and S. Kasif (2003)
\end{book}
A conserved motif (\emph{xMotif}) is a pair $(I,J)$ of genes and samples such that
\( \forall i \in I \ \ \forall j \in J \ \ a_i \leq x_j b_i. \)
To find them:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pick $k$ samples at random (seed);
\item Find the genes on which they agree;
\item Find the samples on which those genes agree;
\item Discard if unsatisfactory;
\item Start again.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spectral biclustering of microarray data: coclustering genes and conditions}\\
  Y. Kuger et al. (2003)
\end{book}
SVD to uncover checkerboard patterns.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{QuBic: a qualitative biclustering algorithm\\ for gene expression data}\\
  G. Lie et al. (2009)
\end{book}
The QuBic qualitative clustering algorithm
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Starts with a graph, with genes as vertices, and gene similarity as edge weights;
\item Picks the heaviest edge;
\item Builds a cluster around it, progressively adding genes, such that the proportion of samples with the same gene expression (terciles) remains above 95\%.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A systematic comparison and evaluation of clustering methods for gene expression data}\\
  A. Preli\'c et al. (2006)
\end{book}
The \emph{bimax} algorithm, for binary data:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Picks a row as a template, and divides the columns into two groups: 1s in the template, and 0s in the template; 
\item Divides the rows into three groups:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item With 0s where there are 0s in the template;
  \item With 0s where there are 1s in the template;
  \item The rest;
  \end{compactitem}
\item Proceeds recursively (the two blocks of zeroes can be ignored).
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A linear time biclustering algorithm\\ for time series gene expression data}\\
  S.C. Madeira and A.L. Oliveira (2005)
\end{book}
Discretize the data and look for biclusters with the same value in each column (date), from the \emph{suffix tree} (Ukkonen) of the rows.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.5,xscale=.5,inner sep = 1pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0]
  \fill[blue!20] (.5,3.5) -- (3.5,3.5) -- (3.5,1.5) -- (.5,1.5) -- cycle;
  \fill[blue!20] (.5,0.5) -- (3.5,0.5) -- (3.5,-.5) -- (.5,-.5) -- cycle;
  \node at (0,0) {U$_1$};
  \node at (1,0) {U$_2$};
  \node at (2,0) {D$_3$};
  \node at (3,0) {U$_4$};
  \node at (4,0) {U$_5$};

  \node at (0,1) {N$_1$};
  \node at (1,1) {N$_2$};
  \node at (2,1) {N$_3$};
  \node at (3,1) {U$_4$};
  \node at (4,1) {N$_5$};

  \node at (0,2) {D$_1$};
  \node at (1,2) {U$_2$};
  \node at (2,2) {D$_3$};
  \node at (3,2) {U$_4$};
  \node at (4,2) {D$_5$};

  \node at (0,3) {N$_1$};
  \node at (1,3) {U$_2$};
  \node at (2,3) {D$_3$};
  \node at (3,3) {U$_4$};
  \node at (4,3) {N$_5$};  
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Biclustering gene expression using\\ factor graphs and the max-sum algorithm}\\
  M. Denitto et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The biclustering problem can be solved iteratively, by looking for the largest bicluster,
replacing with with noise, and iterating.
The problem of finding the largest bicluster can be modeled with a \emph{factor graph} and binary variables $x_{ij}$ for cluster membership:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $a_{ij} x_{ij}$ to promote high-value clusters (high gene expression);
\item $d(a_{ij},a_{kl}) x_{ij} x_{kl}$ to promote coherent clusters;
\item A factor for each pair of rows $i,k$ (resp. columns),
  zero if either is zero or if they have the same (column) pattern
  \[ I\biggl\{ \sum_j x_{ij} = 0 \text{ or } \sum_\ell x_{k\ell} = 0 \text{ or } \sum_j( x_{ij} - x_{kj} )^2 = 0 \biggr\} \]
    to get submatrices
\end{compactitem}
(Prior approaches used exemplars and had a quartic number of factors.)
The problem can be efficiently solved with the MaxSum message passing algorithm (the messages can be computed explicitly).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A biclustering approach based on\\ factor graphs and the max-sum algorithm}\\
  M. Denitto et al. (2017)
\end{book}
More detailed version of the same paper.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Biclustering of time series data\\ using factor graphs}\\
  M. Denitto et al. (2017)
\end{book}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HOP-MAP: efficient message passing\\ with high-order potentials}\\
  D. Tarlow et al. (2010)
\end{book}
In a factor graph (with binary variables), with high-order potentials such as
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Cardinality: $\theta(h_1,\dots,h_N) = f(h_1+\cdots+h_N)$,
\item Convexity: $\theta(h_1,\cdots,h_N) = \mathbf{1}_{\{i\ :\ h_i=1 \} \text{ convex}}$,
\item Ordering: $\theta(h_x,h_y)=1$ if exactly one coordinate, $i$, of $h_x$ is 1,
  exactly one coordinate, $j$, of $h_y$, is 1,
  and $i<j$,
\end{compactitem}
the messages can be computed efficiently with dynamic programming.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Factor graphs, message passing,\\ loopy belief propagation}\\
\end{book}
A \d{factor graph} is a factorization of an (unnormalized) probability distribution
as $p(x) \propto \prod_f \phi_f (x_f )$,
where each $\phi_f$ only depends on a subset $x_f$ of the variables $x_1,\dots,x_N$.
It can be represented as a bipartite graph, with nodes for variables $x_i$ and factors $\phi_f$,
and edges whenever a variable is used in a factor.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=1,inner sep = 1pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0,thick]
  \node[draw,circle] (x1) at (150:1) {$x_1$};
  \node[draw,circle] (x2) at (-150:1) {$x_2$};
  \node[draw]        (f1) at (0,0) {$f_1$};
  \node[draw,circle] (x3) at (1,0) {$x_3$};
  \node[draw]        (f2) at (2,0) {$f_2$};
  \node[draw,circle] (x4) at (3,0) {$x_4$};
  \draw (f1) -- (x1);
  \draw (f1) -- (x2);
  \draw (f1) -- (x3);
  \draw (f2) -- (x4);
  \draw (f2) -- (x3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\[ p(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4) \propto \phi(x_1,x_2,x_3) \phi(x_3,x_4) \]

In the case of trees, the probability can be maximized by \emph{message passing},
\ie, exchanging messages between nodes (each message to/from a variable is a function of its possible values -- for a discrete variable with $k$ values, it is a dimension-$k$ vector).
\begin{align*}
  \mu_{x\to f}(x) &= 0 \\
  \mu_{f \to x} (x) &= \log f(x) \\
  \mu_{f \to x} (x) &= \!\! \max_{x_1 \cdots x_M} \log f(x,x_1,\dots,x_M) + \sum_m \mu^{\mathstrut}_{x_m \to f} (x_m) \\
 \mu_{x \to f} (x) &= \sum_{\ell \in \mathscr N(x) } \mu^{\mathstrut}_{ f_\ell \to x } (x).
\end{align*}
Empirically, the \d{max-sum} algorithm still gives good results for non-trees
(loopy belief propagation).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Simultaneous supervised clustering\\ and feature selection over a graph}\\
  X. Shen et al. (2012)
\end{book}
The \emph{fused lasso}, a penalty on $\sum \va{ \beta_{i+1} - \beta_i }$,
clusters the predictors of a linear regression
when there is an ordering on them.
This ordering can be represented by a graph
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=.2,inner sep = 1pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=-3]
  \node[circle,fill] at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] at (2,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] at (3,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] at (4,0) {};
  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (4,0);
\end{tikzpicture}\,: the fused lasso can be generalized to an arbitrary (e.g., complete) graph.
The $L^1$ norm in the penalties $\sum\va{\beta_i}$ (for irrelevant predictors)
and $\sum\va{\beta_i - \beta_j}$ can be replaced with $\min\{\va\beta,1\}$:
the objective is no longer convex, but a difference of convex functions,
amenable to \emph{difference convex programming}
(or minimization-majorization, MM).
\[
\underset{ \min\{\va\beta,1\} }{
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.2,xscale=.2,inner sep = 1pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0]
  \draw (-2,0) -- (2,0);
  \draw[very thick,blue!70!black] (-2,1) -- (-1,1) -- (0,0) -- (1,1) -- (2,1);
\end{tikzpicture}
}
=
\underset{ \va\beta }{
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.2,xscale=.2,inner sep = 1pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0]
  \draw (-2,0) -- (2,0);
  \draw[very thick,blue!70!black] (-2,2) -- (0,0) -- (2,2);
\end{tikzpicture}
}
-
\underset{ (\va\beta-1)_+ }{
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.2,xscale=.2,inner sep = 1pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0]
  \draw (-2,0) -- (2,0);
  \draw[very thick,blue!70!black] (-2,1) -- (-1,0) -- (1,0) -- (2,1);
\end{tikzpicture}
}
\]

