  Y. Hilpisch (2015)
\end{book}

% Chapter 2. Market-based valuation
\textbf{2.} An option-pricing framework should account for different sources of risk:
level, volatility, jump, and interest rate.

Perfect hedging is only possible with continuous rebalancing and in complete markets --
in particular, without jumps.

% Chapter 3. Market stylized facts
\textbf{3.}
For options, market stylized facts include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Returns have fat tails, prices present jumps; 
\item Volalility is stochastic, tends to cluster, mean-reverts,
  is negatively correlated with returns (leverage);
\item Implied volatility has a smile, which is flatter for longer-term options (term structure);
\item Interest rates are stochastic, mean-reverting, and often positive.
\end{compactitem}

A market model should include stochastic volatility, jumps and stochastic short rates.

% Chapter 4. Risk-neutral valuation [clear]
\textbf{4.}
There is no arbitrage (in continuous time, ``no free lunch with vanishing risk'')
iff there is an equivalent martingale measure $Q$.
It is unique iff the market is complete.

% Chapter 5. Complete market models: CRR, BSM (Black-Scholes)

% Chapter 6. Fourier-based option pricing [interesting - important]
\textbf{6a.}
If the risk-neutral density (RND) $q$ is known, the price of a European option with payoff $C_T$
can be computed as
\[ C_0 = e^{ - r T } \int_0 ^\infty C_T(s) q(s) ds. \]
The characteristic function (Fourier transform) $\hat q(u) = \E_{ X \sim q} [ e^{ i u X } ]$
is often known: the option price can be computed using Parseval's identity
(I ignore signs, normalization factors, discounting factor),
\[ C_0 = \langle C_T, q \rangle = \langle \hat C_T , \hat q \rangle, \]
leading to the \d{Lewis formula}
\[
C_0 = S_0 - \dfrac{ \sqrt{ S_0 K } e^{-rT/2} }{ \pi }
\!\int_0^\infty \!\! \text{Re}[ e^{-izk} \phi(-z-i/2)] \dfrac{ dz }{ z^2 + \tfrac14 }.
\]

\textbf{6b.} The \d{Carr-Madan formulas} compute $\hat C_0$ (where $C_0$ is a function of $k=\log K$)
using Fubini's theorem (simple closed-form formula); the option price can then be computed
with the (inverse) fast-Fourier transform (FFT).
% Separate formulas for ITM and OTM calls

% Chapter 7. Valuation of American options by simulation
\textbf{7.}
For American options, the option value $V$ and its continuation value $C$ can be computed as (primal approach)
\begin{align*}
  V_t &= \sup_\tau \E\nolimits_0^Q \bigl[ B_0(\tau) h_\tau( S_\tau) \bigr] \\
  C_t &= \E\nolimits_t^Q \bigl[ e^{ -r \Delta t } V_{t + \Delta_t} ( S_{ t + \Delta_t} ) | S_t = s \bigr] \\
  V_t(s) &= \max \bigl\{ h_t(s) , C_t(s) \bigr\}.
\end{align*}
This is a lower bound on the option value. The dual approach gives an upper bound.
\begin{align*}
  V_0 &= \sum_\tau \E\nolimits_0^Q \bigl[ B_0(\tau) h_\tau(S_\tau) \bigr] \\
  &= \E\nolimits_0^Q \max_t B_0(t) \bigl( h_t(S_t) - Q_t \bigr) \\
  Q_t &= \sum_{u \leq t } \bigl( V_u( S_u ) - \E\nolimits_{u-\Delta t} ^0 V_u(S_u) \bigr).
\end{align*}
Both rely on least squares Monte Carlo (LSM, Longstaff-Schwartz).

% Chapter 8. Market-based valuation: example
\textbf{8.}
The following models extend the Black-Scholes model (BSM73):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Merton (M76): jumps;
\item Heston (H93): stochastic volatility;
\item Bates (B96): jumps and stochastic volatility;
\item BCC97: jumps, stochastic volatility and stochastic rates.
\end{compactitem}
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{dS}S &= (r-r_J) dt + v_t dZ_1 + J dN \\
  dv &= \kappa (\theta - v) dt + \sigma \sqrt v dZ_2 \\
  dr &= \kappa (\theta - r) dt + \sigma \sqrt r dZ_3 \quad \text{(CIR)} 
\end{align*}

For a single CIR process, one can explicitly compute $B_0(T)$.

The characteristic function of the BCC97 model is the product of those of H93 and M76J
(the stochastic rate only intervenes through $B_0(T)$).

Option valuation can be done by solving a PDE
(the formula is similar to the Black-Scholes one, but involves an integral),
Fourier transform (Lewis or Carr-Madan) or (for arbitrary options) Monte Carlo simulations
(replace the drift with $r$, carefully discretize the SDE, simulate,
and compute the average discounted payoff).

% Chapter 10. Monte Carlo simulations
\textbf{10.} For Monte Carlo simulations, the price can be discretized with the Euler or log-Euler scheme.
For the volatility, naive discretization is not exact:
it can be discretized exactly, but this involves a non-central $\chi^2$ distribution,
which is slow; instead, one can truncate (or reflect -- there are many variants) the naive discretization;
this also easily accounts for the correlation between the volatility and price innovations.

Use all the Monte Carlo variance reduction tricks you can think of:
antithetic variables, moment matching, control variates (European option prices,
which can be computed exactly, when pricing Americal options).

% Chapter 11. Model calibration
\textbf{11.}
The Black-Scholes model is complete;
the Heston model (stochastic volatility) is not, but any option can be replicated with the underlying and one option.
The Bates model (with jumps) is not complete: one would need an infinite number of options to replicate any payoff.
Consequently, the martingale measure $Q$ is not unique: several prices are compatible with the absence of arbitrage.
However, market prices correspond to one of those measures: \emph{calibration} estimates it.

Calibrate the model parameters to liquid vanilla options
(e.g., \textsc{euro stoxx} 50, 3 maturities less than 1 year, 5 strikes, Eonia and Euribor up to 1 year),
using, as loss function,
the MSE of prices (for pricing),
relative prices,
or implied volatilites (for hedging), 
perhaps with some weights (e.g., vega-weighted implied volatility).

The CIR model can be calibrated from the forward rates;
% explicit formula
the forward rates can be computed from the yields
\[ Y_T = - \dfrac{ \log B_0(T) }{ T } \qquad f_T = Y_T + \dfrac{ \partial Y_T }{ \partial T } T. \]

Optimize in several steps: $r$, $\sigma$, $J$, and finally $\sigma+J$.
There may be multiple local optimal: 
add an $L^2$ penalty to stay close to the previous-day solution.

% Chapter 12. Simulation and valuation in the general model framework
% Chapter 10 + jumps

% Chapter 13. Dynamic hedging
\textbf{13.}
Delta hedging is ony exact if it is continuous, if the underlying is the only source of risk,
if the price paths are continuous,
and if volatility is constamt.

Jump risk cannot be hedged away but, if there are options on many underlyiers,
it can be diversified away.

For a single option, delta heding is insufficient: try to add options to the replicating portfolio.
% No details...

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation of risk-neutral densities\\ using positive convolution approximation}\\
  O. Bondarenko (2003)
\end{book}
The risk-neutral density (RND) $f$ satisfies
\[ \text{Option price} = \int_0^{+\infty} \text{Payoff}(F) f(F) dF \]
and can be estimated from puts and calls
\[ f(F) =
\left. \dfrac{ \partial ^2 P(k) }{ \partial K^2 } \right|_{ K=F } =
\left. \dfrac{ \partial ^2 C(k) }{ \partial K^2 } \right|_{ K=F }
.
\]
Intuitively,$f(x) dx$ is the price of an option paying \$1 if the spot $S_T$ is in $[x,x+dx]$
and zero otherwise.


The \emph{positive convolution approximation} (PCA) looks for a density of the form
$f = \phi_h * u $,
where $\phi_h$ is a fixed kernel (its shape is not that important, but its bandwidth is)
and $u$ an arbitrary density, to recover the put prices.
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & u \\
  \text{To minimize} & \sum \bigl[ P_i - D^{_2} f(K_i) \bigr] ^2 \\
  \text{Such that} & u \geq 0, \quad \int u = 1 \\
  \text{Where} & f = \phi_h * u \\
  & D^{-2} f(x) = \int_{-\infty}^x \int_{-\infty}^y f(z) dz dy
\end{array}
\]
After discretization,
$f(x) = \sum a_j \phi(x - z_j)$, $\Delta z = 0.5 h$,
this becomes a quadratic optimization problem.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple and reliable way to compute option-based risk neutral distributions}\\
  A.M. Malz (2014)
\end{book}
To estimate the risk-neutral density (RND) from the
\emph{Breeden-Litzenberger} formula
\[
f(F) = 
\left. \dfrac{ \partial ^2 P(k) }{ \partial K^2 } \right|_{ K=F } =
\left. \dfrac{ \partial ^2 C(k) }{ \partial K^2 } \right|_{ K=F }
\]
fit the implied volatilitiess with a clamped cubic splines
(set the first derivative to zero at the boundaries)
and differentiate the Black-Scholes call prices.
Check for no-arbitrage violations:
the call price should be decreasing and convex.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Exploring return dynamics\\ via corridor-implied volatility}\\
  T.G. Andersen et al. (2015)
\end{book}
If there are no jumps, the realized variance is
\[ \E\nolimits_0^\QQ[ \text{RV} ] = 2 \int_0^\infty \dfrac{ M(K) }{ K^2 } dK \]
where $M(K) = \min \bigl\{ P(K), \ C(K) \bigr\}$
and the realized variance is computed from the squared ratio- or log-returns
(in the absence of jumps, they give the same result).
With jumps, the result remains if we replace the squared returns with the difference
\[ 2 ( \text{ratio-returns} - \text{log-returns} )  \]
(which is approximately 2/3 of the squared ratio-returns and 1/3 of the log-returns).

The CBOE VIX estimates the \emph{market-free implied volatility} (MFIV)
\[ \text{MFIV} = \dfrac{ 2 e^{rT} }{ T } \left[ \int_0^F \dfrac{ P(K) }{ K^2 } dK + \int_F^{\infty} \dfrac{ C(K) }{ K^2 } dK \right] \]
and is computed as
\begin{align*}
  \sigma^2 &= \dfrac{ 2 e^{rT} }{ T } \sum \dfrac{ \Delta K_i }{ K_i ^2 } M(K_i) - \dfrac1T \left( \dfrac{ F }{ K_f } - 1 \right )^2 \\
  \Delta K_i &= \tfrac12 ( K_{i+1} - K_{i-1} ) \\
  \Delta K_1 &= K_2 - K_1 \\
  \Delta K_N &= K_N - K_{N-1} \\
  K_f &= \text{first strike below $F$}
\end{align*}
where only liquid strikes are used -- but the corridor of strikes used,
$[K_1,K_N]$, can change and create spurious jumps.

The \emph{corridor implied volatility}
uses the same formula for a more stable corridor, defined from the $[3\%,97\%]$ quantile range of the distribution defined by the cdf
\[ R(K) = \dfrac{ P(K) }{ P(K) + C(K) }. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Construction and interpretation\\ of model-free implied volatility}\\
  T.G. Andersen and O. Bondarenko (2007)
\end{book}
Earlier paper on the corridor implied volatility (CIV),
where the corridor is defined from the PCA (positive convolution approximation) risk-neutral density estimator.

There is a negative variance risk premium in the VIX.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model-free implied volatility\\ and its information content}\\
  G.J. Jiang and Y.S. Tian (2003)
\end{book}
Derivation of the MFIV estimator from the Britten-Jones-Neuberger formula
\[
\E\nolimits_0^\QQ \left[ \int_{T_1}^{T_2} \!\!\left( \dfrac{ dS_t }{ S_t } \right)^2 \right] =
2 \! \int_0^\infty \! \dfrac{ C(T_2,K) - C(T_1,K) }{ K^2 } dK
\]
by adding a risk-free rate, setting $T_1=0$ so that the second term is a call payoff, either zero, 
or leading to a put price via the put-call parity.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Option prices, implied price processes\\ and stochastic volatility}\\
  M. Britten-Jones and A. Neugerger (2000)
\end{book}
(The formula is derived as the limit of a discrete model.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards a theory of volatility trading}\\
  P. Carr and D. Madan (2002)
\end{book}
The model-free implied volatility (MFIV) can be computed as follows.
\begin{align*}
  f(y) &= f(x) + f'(x) \bigl[ (y-x)_+ + (x-y)_+ \bigr]  \\
  &\hphantom{={}} + \!\int_0^x \!\! f''(t) (t-y)_+ dt + \!\int_x^\infty \!\! f''(t) (y-t)_+ dt \\
  \E\nolimits_0^\QQ\bigl[ f(F) \bigr] &=
  f(K) B_0 + f'(K) \bigl[ C(K) - P(K) \bigr] \\
  &\hphantom{={}}
  + \int_0^K \! f''(k) P(k) dk + \int_K^\infty \! f''(k) C(k) dk
\end{align*}
Letting $f(F) = \log( F_T/F_0)$ and $K=F_0$,
\begin{align*}
  \E\nolimits_0^\QQ\left[ \log \dfrac{ F_T }{ F_0 } \right] &=
  - \int_0^{F)0} \dfrac{ P(k) }{ k^2 } dk - \int_{F_0}^\infty \dfrac{ C(k) }{ k^2 }dk \\
  \E\nolimits_0^\QQ\left[ \log \dfrac{ F_T }{ F_0 } \right]
  &\overset{\text{\clap{It\=o}}}{=}
  -\dfrac12 \E\nolimits_0^\QQ \int_o^T \sigma_t^2 dt.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Prices of state contingent claims\\ implicit in option prices}\\
  D. Breeden and R. Litzenberger (1978)
\end{book}
Notice that
\[ \dfrac{ d }{ dx } \int_x^\infty f(x,y) dy = \int_x^\infty \dfrac{ \partial f(x,y) }{ \partial x } dy - f(x,x) \]
and apply it to the price of a call option
\[ C(K) = \int_0^\infty (S-K)_+ f(S) dS = \int_K^\infty (S-K) f(S) dS; \]
this gives
\[ \dfrac{ dC(K) }{ dK } = \int_K^\infty -f(S) dS - 0 \]
and finally
\[ \dfrac{ d^2 C(K) }{ dK^2 } = f(K) . \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Is implied correlation worth calculating?\\ Evidence from foreign exchange options\\ and historical data}\\
  C. Walter and J.A. Lopez (2000)
\end{book}
The correlation between (the log-returns of) two currency pairs A/C and B/C satisfies
\[ \rho_{ A, B / C } = \dfrac{
  \sigma_{A/C}^2 + \sigma_{B/C}^2 - \sigma_{A/B}^2
}{
  2 \sigma_{A/C} \sigma_{B/B}
}. \]
Computing it from implied volatilities has sometimes (not always) some predictive power on future correlation.
  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Implied correlation\\ for pricing multi-FX options}\\
  P.V. Shecvhenko (2004)
\end{book}
For unrelated currency pairs, A/B and C/D,
\[
\rho_{A/B,C/D} = \dfrac{
  \sigma_{C/B}^2 + \sigma_{A/D}^2 - \sigma_{D/B}^2 - \sigma_{C/A}^2
}{
  2 \sigma_{A/B}^2 \sigma_{C/D}^2
}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Implied correlation:\\ a new measure of diversification}\\
  V.D. Skintzi and A.P.N. Refenes (2005)
\end{book}
The implied correlation is defined from the implied volatilities of an index and its components as
\[ \rho = \dfrac{
  \sigma^2 - \sum w_i^2 \sigma_i^2
}{
  \sum\limits_{i\neq j} w_i w_j \sigma_i \sigma_j
}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring equity risk\\ with option implied correlations}\\
  A. Buss and G. Vilkov (2012)
\end{book}
Adjust the historical correlations
\[ \rho_{ij} = (1-\lambda) \hat\rho_{ij} + \lambda \]
such that % the implied volatilities satisfy
$ \sigma^2 = \sum w_i w_j \sigma_i \sigma_j \hat \rho_{ij} $.
\[ \lambda = \dfrac{
  \sigma^2 - \sum w_i w_j \sigma_i \sigma_j \hat \rho_{ij}
}{
  \sum w_i w_j \sigma_i \sigma_j (1-\hat\rho_{ij})
}.
\]
We can then define option implied betas
\[ \beta_i = \dfrac{
  \sigma_i \sum w_j \sigma_j \rho_{ij}
}{
  \sigma^2
}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating realistic implied\\ correlation matrix from option prices}\\
  K.N. Numpacharoen (2013)
\end{book}
If $\sigma^2 > \hat \sigma^2$,
replace $\rho_{ij} = (1-\lambda) \hat \rho_{ij} + \lambda $
with
\[ \rho_{ij} = (1-\lambda) \hat \rho_{ij} - \dfrac{ \lambda }{ n-1 }. \]

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine learning fund characterizations}\\
  D. Mehta et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Approaches to cluster funds (with $k$-means, hclust, fuzzy-C-means, SOM)
often uses performance and risk measures as features (performance ratios, betas).
Some use the bipartite graph of the top 10 holdings, projected, with spectral clustering.

Use aggregate data (proportion of assets in a given asset class, country, sector, capitalization bin, etc.)
with a decision tree, random forest, or a neural net (FC, 3 layers, 512/256/128/ReLu, softmax)
to reproduce the Morningstar classification
(not clustering, but classification).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sequential deep learning for credit risk monitoring with tabilar financial data}\\
  J.M. Clements et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Gradient boosted trees, commonly used to assess credit risk (from credit card data)
do not leverage the time dimension of the data (without clever feature engineering):
try temporal convolutions (TCN).
Other baselines include MLP, TabNet (attention for tabular data) and LSTMs.

Winsorize using the boosted tree cutoffs.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Covid-19 spreading in financial networks:\\ a semiparametric matrix regression model}\\
  M. Billio et al. (2021)
\end{book}
Model the time-varying adjacency matrix of the financial network as
\begin{align*}
  & A_t = \sum_r B_r f_{rt} + E_t \\
  & E_t \sim \text{MN}(0,I,\Sigma) \\
  & \Sigma \text{ diagonal} \\
  & A_t, B_r, E_t \text{ matrices} \\
  & f_{it} \text{ scalars (risk factors)}
\end{align*}
where the \emph{matrix normal distribution} is
\begin{align*}
  X &\sim \text{MN}( M, U, V ) \\
  M &\text{: mean} \\
  U &\text{: variance among rows} \\
  V &\text{: variance among columns} \\
  \operatorname{vec} X &\sim N( \operatorname{vec} M, V \tens U ).
\end{align*}
Use both stock returns and volatility,
and separately model
$A_{\text{ret},\text{ret}}$,
$A_{\text{ret},\text{vol}}$,
$A_{\text{vol},\text{ret}}$,
$A_{\text{vol},\text{vol}}$.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=1.5,inner sep = 2pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=-7]
  \node (A) at (0,0) {returns};
  \node (B) at (2,0) {volatility};
  \draw[thick,->] (A) to [out=15, in=165, looseness=1] node[auto,above=0pt,pos=.5] {\footnotesize leverage} (B);
  \draw[thick,->] (B) to [out=-165, in=-15, looseness=1] node[auto,below=0pt,pos=.5] {\footnotesize risk premium} (A);
  \draw[thick,->] (A) to [out=165,in=-165, looseness=3] (A);
  \draw[thick,->] (B) to [out=15,in=-15, looseness=3] (B);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Application: Bayesian inference, 500 European firms, pairwise Granger causality

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep portfolio optimization via distributional prediction and residual factors}\\
  K. Imajo et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Do not forecast returns, but idiosyncratic returns -- residuals, $\epsilon$, of a factor model.

Forecast the return distribution using quantile regression:
a neural network forecasting the quantiles of
$\epsilon_{i,t} | \epsilon_{i,<t}$
(assume $\Sigma = \var \epsilon$ is diagonal).

Account for inductive biases:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Amplitude invariance (volatility clustering):
  $(x_t)_t$ and $(\lambda x_t)_t$
  are equally likely,
  using homogeneous function
  $x \mapsto \n{x} \phi( x / \n x )$;
\item Time-scale invariance:
  $(x_t)_t$ and $(x_{\lambda t})_t$
  are equally likely,
  using shared weights at different scales.
\end{compactitem}
Maximize the Sharpe ratio of the optimal portfolio
$ w = \lambda ^{-1} \Sigma^{-1} \mu $.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms for learning graphs\\ in financial markets}\\
  J.V.M. Cardoso et al. (2020)
\end{book}
The graphical lasso models financial returns as
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ \Sigma^{-1} \succcurlyeq 0 } \text{tr}( \Sigma^{-1} S ) - \text{log\,det}^* ( \Sigma^{-1} ) + \alpha \n{ \Sigma^{-1} }_1.\]

Add a few constrains:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The precision matrix $\Sigma^{-1}$ is a graph Laplacian,
  $\Sigma^{-1} = \mathscr L W = \text{diag}( W \mathbf 1 ) - W$;
\item The graph has $k$ connected components,m \ie,
  $\operatorname{rank} \Sigma^{-1} = p-k$;
\item To avoid isolated nodes, each node has degree $d$,
  $\operatorname{diag} W = d \mathbf 1$.
\end{compactitem}

The problem can be solved by alternatingly estimating
$\Sigma^{-1}$ and $W$ (with ADMM).
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & W \geq 0, \ \Theta \succcurlyeq 0 \\
  \text{To minimize} & \text{trace}( S \mathscr L W) - \text{log\,det}^* \Theta \\
    \text{Such that} & \Theta = \mathscr L W \\
    & \operatorname{rank} \Theta = p - k \\
    & \operatorname{rank}\mathscr L W = p - k \\
    & \operatorname{diag} W = d \mathbf 1
\end{array}
\]
The rank constraint can be enforced
with \smash{$\sum\limits_{i=1}^k \lambda_i = 0$}, using
\[
\sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i(A) = \min_{\substack{
    V \in \RR^{p \times k} \\ V' V = I
}} \text{tr}( V' A V )
\]

For robustness to outliers, replace the Gaussian distribution with a Student
(the problem is no longer convex, but still amenable to MM).

ADMM solves
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x, z \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(x) + g(z) \\
  \text{Such that} & Ax + Bz = c
\end{array}
\]
by iterating
\begin{align*}
  x & \leftarrow \argmin L(x,z,y) \\
  z & \leftarrow \argmin L(x,z,y) \\
  y & \leftarrow y + \lambda( A x + B z - c )
\end{align*}
where
$ L(x,z,y) $ is
\[f(x) + g(z) + y' (Ax + Bz - c )
+ \tfrac12 \n{ Ax + Bz - c }^2.
\]

R implementation in \texttt{fingraph}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning high-dimensional Gaussian\\ graphical models under total positivity\\ without adjustment of tuning parameters}\\
  Y. Wang et al. (2020)
\end{book}
A Gaussian distribution is multivariate totally positive of order 2 (MTP$_2$, attractive Gaussian random field)
if all partial correlations are positive.

A density $f$ on $\RR^p$ is MTP$_2$ if
\[ \forall x, y \in \RR^p \quad f(x) f(y) \leq f(x \wedge y ) f(x \vee y ) \]
(where $\wedge$ and $\vee$ denote the elementwise min and max).

The \textsc{cmit} algorithm estimates a sparse graphical model,
starting with a complete graph,
and removing edge $i$--$j$ if $\va{ \rho_{ij|S} } \leq \lambda$
for some $S \subset \llbracket 1, p \rrbracket$, $\va S \leq \eta$ (max degree).

It can be modified for MTP$_2$ models: remove edge $i$--$j$ if
$\hat \rho_{ij | S \cup \{ k \} } < 0$
for some $S \subset \mathscr N(i) \setminus \{j\}$, $\va S \leq \ell$,
and some $k \not\in S \cup \{i,j\}$;
progressively increase $\ell$;
only estimate $\hat \rho$ on a minibatch.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{cgam: an R package for the constrained generalized additive model}\\
  C. Liao and M.C. Meyer
\end{book}
Monotonicity and convexity constraints for GAMs;
allows qualitative (ordered) variables.

%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Disparity of clustering coefficients\\ in the Holme-Kim network model}\\
  R.I. Oliveira (2016)
\end{book}
The \d{Holme-Kim} random graph model extends the Barabasi-Albert one
with a triad formation step (with some fixed probability)
after each preferential attachment.


The \emph{local clustering coefficient} of a node is the proportion of neighbours that are connected.
The local clustering coefficient of a graph is the average of the local clustering coefficients of its nodes.

The \emph{global clustering coefficient} of a graph is the proportion of paths of length 2
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.25,xscale=.25,inner sep = 1.2pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (C) at (60:1) {};
  \draw[thick] (A) -- (C) -- (B);
\end{tikzpicture}
that can be completed in a triangle
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.25,xscale=.25,inner sep = 1.2pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (C) at (60:1) {};
  \draw[thick] (A) -- (C) -- (B) -- (A);
\end{tikzpicture}.
Those coefficients are different:
\smash{\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.15,xscale=.75,inner sep = 1.2pt, outer sep = 0pt,baseline=0]
  \node[circle,fill] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \foreach \i in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {
    \node[circle,fill] (C-\i) at (.5,\i) {};
    \draw[thick] (A) -- (C-\i) -- (B);
  }
\end{tikzpicture}}.

The local clustering coefficient of the Holme-Kim random graph remains bounded away from zero,
but the global one slowly ($1/\log n$) tends to zero.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The atlas for the aspiring network scientist}\\
  M. Coscia (2020)
\end{book}
760-page illustrated book

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{fastnet: an R package for fast simulation\\ and analysis of large scale social networks}\\
  X. Dong et al. (2020)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The H-index of a network\\ and its relation to degree and coreness}\\
  L. L\"u et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The core decomposition of a graph is obtained by removing all notes of degree 1
(repeat until there are none left -- new ones may be created),
then all nodes of degree at most 2, and so on --
the \emph{coreness} of a node is the step at which it was removed.
The H-index of a node is the largest $h$ such that there are at least $h$ neighbours with degree at least $h$.
Iterating the H-index operator, starting with the degree,
converges to the coreness;
the updates can be asynchronous.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Characterization of complex networks:\\ a survey of measurements}\\
  L.F. Costa et al. (2006)
\end{book}
List of graph metrics

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph evolution:\\ densification and shrinking diameters}\\
  J. Leskovec et al. (2007)
\end{book}
Real-world grapjs grow with time and exhibit:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item densification:
  $(\# \text{edges} ) \propto ( \# \text{nodes} )^a$, $a>1$;
\item  shrinking: the effective diameter (90\% quantile of the distance distribution)
  decreases.
\end{compactitem}

The \emph{community-guided attachment model} builds a graph on the leaves of a complete $b$-ary
tree of depth $t$ (representing nested communities),
by adding an edge $i$--$j$ with probability proportional to $c^{d(i,j)}$,
where the distance is measured on the tree;
for a dynamic model,
one can use all the nodes instead of just the leaves.
It exhibits densification, but not shrinking.

The \emph{forest fire model}
adds nodes one by one,
linking them to a random existing node,
then to a random subset of its neighbours,
then to a random subset of these neighbours' neighbours,
and so on (citation networks arise like that).
For a narrow range of parameters,
it exhibits a heavy tail degree distribution, densification, shrinking, communities.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ANF: a fast and scalable tool\\ for data mining in massive graphs}\\
  C.R. Palmer et al. (2002)
\end{book}
To approimately compute the size of neighbourhoods,
\[ \mathscr N(x,h) = \{ y \in V \ :\ d(x,y) \leq h \} \]
iterate (for all $x$, and increasing $h$)
\[ \mathscr N(x,h) = \bigcup_{ x\text{--}y } \mathscr N(y,h-1) \]
using HyperLogLog structure (to count distinct elements).
Use approximate neighbours to speed computations further.

