  K.R. Coombes et al. (2019)
\end{book}
If you need large palettes (up to 36 colours).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{vsgoftest: and R package for goodness of fit testing based on KL divergence}\\
  J. Lequesne and P. Regnault (2020)
\end{book}
The Vasicek normality test relies on the Gaussian distribution maximizing entropy (for a fixed variance).
The Vasicek-Song gof test generalizes it to arbitrary (continuous) distributions,
by using the KL divergence
between the sample and the null distributions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Flexible regression models for count data based on renewal processes: the Countr package}\\
  T. Kharat et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Count data is often modeled as a Poisson distribution (or, sometimes, Geometric or Binomial),
but this imposes $\E Y = \var Y$, has no memory, and constant hazard function.
Instead, consider renewal processes with other inter-event distributions
(Weibull, Gamma, Burr).
\begin{verbatim}
  r <- renewalCount(y~1, d, "weibull")
\end{verbatim}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evaluating probabilistic forecasts\\ with scoring rules}\\
  A. Jordan et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Scoring rules compare probabilistic forecasts:
let $F$ be the forecast (a probability distribution, parametric or given by samples),
$y$ the outcome,
and $S(F,y)$ the score (lower is better).

A \emph{proper scoring rule} satisfies
\[ \forall F,G \ \E_{ Y \sim G } S(G,Y) \leq \E_{ Y \sim G } S(F,Y), \]
with equality iff $F=G$.
Examples include the logarithmic score
and the \emph{continuous ranked probability score}
(CRPS)
\begin{align*}
  \text{LogS}(F,y) &= - \log f(y) \\
  \text{CRPS}(F,y) &=
  \int \bigr[ F(z) - \mathbf1_{ y \leq z } \bigr] ^2 dz \\
  &= \E_{ X \sim F } \va{ X - y } - \E_{ X_1,X_2 \sim F } \va{ X_1 - X_2 }.
\end{align*}
If the forecast is provided as a sample,
the CRPS can be efficiently computed drom the order statistics.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian and non-Bayesian cause-specific competing risk analysis for parametric\\ and non-parametric survival functions:\\ the R package CFC}\\
  A.S. Mahani and M.T.A. Sharabiani
\end{book}
Cause-specific competing risk survival analysis is survival analysis with multiple, mutually-exclusive end-points:
$\dot F_k(t) = \bigl[ 1 - \sum F_\ell(t) \bigr] \lambda_k(t).$
By letting $E(t) = 1 = \sum F_k(t)$,
we get $\dot E/E = - \sum \lambda_k$ and
\begin{align*}
  E &= \prod S_k \\
  S_k(t) &= \exp - \int_0^t \lambda_k(s) ds \\
  F_k(t) &= \int_0^t \prod S_\ell(s) \lambda_k(s) ds.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Randomized matrix decompositions using R}\\
  N.B. Erichson et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The \texttt{rsvd} package provides
randomized (for big data) SVD, PCA, robust PCA, CUR,
interpolative decomposition.
Also check \texttt{irlba}, \texttt{RSpectra}, \texttt{svd::propack.svd}, \texttt{svd}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ManifoldOptim:\\ an R interface to the ROPTLIB library\\ for Riemannian manifold optimization}\\
  S.R. Martin et al. (2020)
\end{book}
The \emph{minimum average deviance estimation} (MADE) method
for sufficient dimension reduction (SDR)
looks for $B$ such that the local regression
(with weights from a kernel in the original space, and bandwidth from cross-validation)
$y \sim B' X$ gives the best fit:
it is an optimization on the Grassmanian.

In a linear model $Y = \mu + \beta X + \epsilon$,
$X \in \RR^p$, $Y \in \RR^r$,
$\epsilon \sim N(0,\Sigma)$,
not all of $Y$ contains relevant information:
the \emph{$\Sigma$-envelope} of $\operatorname{Span} \beta$ is the smallest subspace $S \supset \operatorname{Span} \beta$
such that $\Sigma = \Sigma_S + \Sigma_{S^\perp}$.
Finding such a subspace can be formulated as an optimization problem on the Grassmanian.

Also check \texttt{Pymanopt} (Python),
\texttt{GrassmanOptim} (R),
\texttt{ROPTLIB} (C++, in Julia, or R: \texttt{ManifoldOptim}, \texttt{ldr} (likelihood based dimension reduction)).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to the augmented inverse propensity weighted estimator}\\
  A.N. Glynn and K.M. Quinn (2009)
\end{book}
The average treatment effect (ATE) is
$\E[ Y(1) - Y(0) ]$.
There are many nore acronyms:
ATT, ATC (average treatment effect on treated/control),
SATE, PATE (sample/population ATE),
CATE (conditional ATE -- for a subgroup).

\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Propensity matching} is not data-efficient;
\item The \emph{regression estimator} fits a model
  $Y \sim T + Z$,
  \[ \widehat{\text{ATE}} = \operatorname*{Mean}_i
  \bigl[
    \hat Y ( T=1, Z=z_i ) - \hat Y ( T=0, Z=z_i )
  \bigr]
  \]
\item The \emph{inverse propensity weighting} (IPW) estimator models the probability of treatment $\pi(z)$,
  \[ \widehat{\text{ATE}} =
  \operatorname*{Mean}_i
  \left[
    \dfrac{ T_i }{ \pi(Z_i) } Y_i - \dfrac{ 1 - T_i }{ 1 - \pi(Z_i) } Y_i
  \right]
  \]
  It has poor small-sample properties ($\pi$ can be close to zero)
  and can even be outside the possible range for $Y(1)-Y(0)$.
\item IPW* normalizes the weights to ensure they sum to~1.
  \begin{align*}
    \widehat{\text{ATE}} &=
    \left( \sum \dfrac{ T_i }{ \pi(Z_i) } \right)^{\!\!-1}
    \sum \dfrac{ T_i }{ \pi(Z_i) } Y_i \\
    &\hphantom{={}} -
    \left( \sum \dfrac{ 1 - T_i }{ 1 - \pi(Z_i) } \right)^{\!\!-1}
    \sum \dfrac{ 1 - T_i }{ 1 - \pi(Z_i) } Y_i    
  \end{align*}
\item The covariate $Z$ contains information, not only about $T$, but also about the outcome $Y$.
  The \emph{augmented IPW} (AIPW) adjusts the ATE accordingly.
  % Big formula
  It is \emph{doubly robust}: it is consisten if either $\pi$ or $\hat Y$ is well specified.
  R implementation in \texttt{CausalGAM}.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Debiased inverse propensity score weighting for estimation of average treatment effects with high dimensional confounders}\\
  Y. Wang and  R.D. Shah (2020)
\end{book}
The oracle estiamtor
\begin{align*}
  \text{ATE} &=
  \operatorname*{Mean}_i \dfrac{ T_i }{ \pi(Z_i) } Y_i -
  \operatorname*{Mean}_i \dfrac{ 1 - T_i }{ 1 - \pi(Z_i) } Y_i  
\end{align*}
is unbiased, but there is a bias of the propensity score is estimated from data.

If $\mu \indep T | Z $,
\begin{align*}
  \text{ATE} &=
  \operatorname*{Mean}_i \dfrac{ T_i }{ \pi(Z_i) } (Y_i \mu_i )-
  \operatorname*{Mean}_i \dfrac{ 1 - T_i }{ 1 - \pi(Z_i) } ( Y_i - \mu_i )
\end{align*}
has the same properties, and we can choose $\mu$ to reduce the bias, e.g., with
\[
\begin{array}{@{}ll}
  \text{Find} & \mu \\
  \text{To minimize} & \sum \bigl( \hat{ \tilde Y } ( Z = Z_i ) - \mu_i \bigr) ^2 \vrule width 0pt depth .8em\\
  \text{Such that} &
  \n{
    \dfrac1n Z' ( \tilde Y \!-\! \tilde f(Z)) - \dfrac1n Z' ( \mu \!-\! \tilde f(Z) )
  }_\infty \!\!\! \leq \eta
\end{array}
\]
where
\[ \tilde Y = \dfrac{ T ( 1-\hat \pi ) }{ \hat \pi } Y + \dfrac{ (1-T) \hat \pi }{ 1 - \hat \pi } Y \]
(to ensure $\mu \indep T | Z$, split the data in 3: to estimate $\hat \pi$ and $\tilde f = \hat{\tilde Y}$, for the constraint, and for the rest).

Other methods include: IPW, AIPW, ARN (approximate residual rebalancing),
TMLE (targeted MLE).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Zigzag expanded navigation plots in R:\\ the R package zenplots}\\
\end{book}
Zigzag plots are pairs plots where adjacent axes share the same variable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Latent space approaches\\ to social network analysis}\\
  P.D. Hoff et al. (2002)
\end{book}
In a latent space network model,
nodes have both features $x_i$ and latent coordinates $z_i$,
and the edge probabilities are
\begin{align*}
  & z_i \sim N(\mu,\Sigma) \\
  & \operatorname{logit} y_{ij} = \beta x_i + \beta x_j + d(z_i,z_j)
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GNAR: Generalized\\ network autoregressive process in R}\\
  M. Knight et al. (2020)
\end{book}
In GNARs, $X_{it}$ is a linear combination of its past values (AR)
and those of its neighbours (GCN), with weights depending on the lag and the distance (number of hops).

