\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The real numbers -- a survey of construction}\\
  I. Weiss (2015)
\end{book}
Cauchy sequences and Dedekind cuts are not the only constructions of $\RR$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Decimal expansions (but arithmetic is tricky);
  $\sum a_n / 2^n$ with no restriction on $a_n$ (up to equivalence);
\item Nested families of intervals; Cauchy filters;
\item $\sum_{s\in S} s$, where $S$ is a multiset of elements of the form $1/n$;
  $\sum_{n\in A} 1/n$, $\sum_{n\in A} a_n$ for a fixed sequence $a$ such that $a_n \to 0$, $\sum a_n = \infty$;
\item Continued fractions; producst $\prod ( 1 + 1/a_n)$;
  $a_0 + 1/a_1 + 1/a_2 + \dots$;
  $a_0 + 1/a_1 + 1/a_1a_2 + 1/a_1a_2a_3 + \dots$;
\item Approximate endomorphisms, \ie $f: \ZZ \to \ZZ$
  such that $\{ f(x+y) - f(x) - f(y) \}$ be finite,
  up to equivalence $f \sim g$ if $\{ f(x) - g(x) \}$ is finite;
\item The universal property of the completion of $\QQ$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fisher information distance:\\ a geometric reading}\\
  S.I.R. Costa et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Formulas for computations on the Riemannian manifold of Gaussian distributions $N(\mu, \sigma^2)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Self-normalizing flows}\\
  T.A. Keller et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Normalizing flows require the Jacobian determinant,
which is expensive to compute,
unless it is triangular or easy to approximate --
instead, have the network learn both the flow at each layer and its inverse
[cf.~synthetic gradients].

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Explaining by removing:\\ a unified framework for model explanation}\\
  I.C. Covert et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Most model explanation methods (SHAP, LIME, etc.) can be unified and presented as a 3-step process:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Removal: remove part of the image (one or several circular regions, or regions from a segmentation algorithm)
  and replace it  (with noise, a constant value, a generative model, blur, etc.);
\item Behaviour: compute the effect of the removal (on theoutput, the loss, the average loss);
\item Aggragation: aggregate those effects (smallest destroying subset, smallest sufficient subset,
  mean after inclusion/removal, Shapley value, linear model, etc.).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Reservoir computing meets\\ recurrent kernels and structured transforms}\\
  J. Dong et al. (2020)
\end{book}
A function $f: \RR \to \RR^k$ and random points $w_i$ in $\RR^p$
(sampled from some distribution)
define random features
\[ \phi(u) = \dfrac{ 1 }{ \sqrt N } \bigl[ f(\langle w_1,u\rangle), \dots, f(\langle w_N,u\rangle) \bigr] \]
and a kernel $k(u,v) = \bigl\langle \phi(u) , \phi(v) \bigr\rangle$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{C-space tunnel discovery\\ for puzzle path planning}\\
  X. Zhang et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Rigid body disentanglement puzzles rely on narrow tunnels in configuration space.
Find those tunnels by aligning the features (gap-gap or gap-notch) of the pieces:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Euclidean-to-geodesic ratio pairs of points on a surface where the EGR is a \emph{local} minimum
  are around gaps or notches;
  \[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{u,v} \dfrac{ d_E(u,v) }{ d_G(u,v) } + \alpha d_E(u,v) \]
  use the \emph{heat kernel method} to compute the geodesics;
\item Medial axis notch detection:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Compute the medial axis (a surface);
  \item Use the \emph{mean curvature flow} to reduce it to a curve, the skeleton;
  \item For each point on the skeleton, find the closest point on the surface,
    and compute the distance to the opposite point;
  \item Notches are local minima for this distance;
  \end{compactitem}
\item Neural network, trained on data from simple puxzles (solved y a traditional planner),
  taking an image of the puzzle as input (5 channels: luminance, depth, normal)
  and predicting where the tunnels are.
\end{compactitem}
This does not work on puzzles not relying on gap-gap or gap-notch alignment
(e.g., Hanamaya Elk).

% Other types of puzzles: interlocking . burr / twisty  interlocking 3D jigsaw puzzles

