\emph{Nodewise regression} estimates the precision matrix (inverse of the variance)
of $x_1,\dots,x_n$ with a regression for each column,
$\beta_{ij} = \operatorname{coef}( x_i \sim s_{-i} )$,
as
\[
C = 
\left(\!\!\!  
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline={([yshift=-.8ex]current bounding box.center)}]
%\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=(current bounding box.center)]
\matrix (m) [
  matrix of math nodes,
  nodes in empty cells,
  row sep={4ex,between origins},column sep={5ex,between origins}
]{
1 & -\beta_{12} &  & {} \\
-\beta_{21}  &   &   & \\
 &   &   &  {} \\
{} & & {} & 1 \\
};
\draw (m-1-1)-- (m-4-4);
\draw[thick,loosely dotted] (m-1-2)-- (m-1-4) -- (m-3-4) -- (m-1-2);
\draw[thick,loosely dotted] (m-2-1)-- (m-4-1) -- (m-4-3) -- (m-2-1);
\end{tikzpicture}
\!\!\!\right)
\]
with a lasso penalty to encourage sparsity,
rescaled with the variance of the residuals
$\Theta = \Sigma^{-2} C$,
$\Sigma = \operatorname{diag}( \sigma_1^2 , \dots , \sigma_n )$;
symmetrize (with $\min(C,C')$) and discard negative eigenvalues.
If there are many variables, first use a low-rank factorzation $X = BF + E$,
then sparse nodewise regression on the residuals,
and finally Woodbury's formula to recover the precision of~$X$.


