\begin{book}
  \emph{An operational measure of riskiness}\\
  D.P. Foster and S. Hart (2009)
\end{book}
Given a discrete random variable $X$ modeling the outcome of a gamble,
\ie, sich that $\E X>0$ and $P[X<0]>0$,
its Foster-Hart risk $r$ is the positive solution of
\[ \E \log \left( 1 + \dfrac Xr \right) = 0; \]
it is greater than the maximum loss and guarantees no-bankruptcy.
For continuous distributions, it need not be defined:
use the maximum loss instead.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parametric UMAP: learning embeddings\\ with deep neural networks for representation and semi-supervised learning}\\
  T. Sainburg et al. (2020)
\end{book}
UMAP learns an embedding of the data points that preserves the graph structure
of the data;
parametric UMAP uses a neural network to compute that embedding
(instead of looking for the coordinates of each point in the dataset),
which allows adding new points.
Contrary to t-SNE, the loss function uses negative sampling and does not require normalization.
\begin{align*}
  p_{j|i} ^{\text{t-SNE}} &\propto \exp - \dfrac{ d(x_i, x_j) }{ 2 \sigma_i^2 } \\
  p_{j|i}^{\text{UMAP}} &= \exp - \dfrac{ d(x_i, x_j ) - \rho_i }{ \sigma_i } \\
  q_{ij}^{\text{t-SNE}} &\propto \bigl( 1 + \n{ z_i - z_j }^2 \bigr)^{-1} \\
  q_{ij}^{\text{UMAP}} &= \bigl( 1 + a \n{ z_i - z_j } ^{2b }\bigr)^{-1} \\
  \text{Loss}^{\text{t-SNE}} &= \text{KL}(p,q) = \sum_{i \neq j } p_{ij} \log \dfrac{ p_{ij} }{ q_{ij} } \\
  \text{Loss}^{\text{UMAP}} &= \text{CE}(p,q) \\ &= \sum_{i\neq j} p_{ij} \log \dfrac{p_{ij}}{q_{ij}} + (1-p_{ij}) \log \dfrac{ 1 - p_{ij} }{ 1 - q_{ij} }
\end{align*}
The UMAP loss can be used as a regularization to learn a latent representation
preserving the structure of the data,
in autoencoders or for semi-supervised learning.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{From trees to continuous embeddings and back: hyperbolic hierarchical clustering}\\
  I. Chami et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Hierarchical clustering algorithms are often heuristics.
More rigorously, one can minimize the DasGupta cost
\begin{align*}
  \text{Cost}(T) &= \sum_{ij} w_{ij} \va{ \operatorname{leaves} T( i \vee j ) } \\
  &= \sum_{ijk} ( w_{ij} + w_{ik} + w_{jk} - w_{ijk} ) + 2 \sum_{ij} w_{ij} 
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
  i, j &\text{: leaves} \\
  i \vee j &\text{: lowest common ancestor (LCA)} \\
  T(i \vee j) &\text{: tree below $i \vee j$} \\
  w_{ij} &\text{: similarity of $i$ and $j$} \\
  w_{ijk} &= \begin{pmatrix} w_{ij} \\ w_{ik} \\ w_{jk} \end{pmatrix} \cdot
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \mathbf 1_{ \{ i,j | k \} } \\
    \mathbf 1_{ \{ i,k | j \} } \\
    \mathbf 1_{ \{ j,k | i \} }
  \end{pmatrix} \\
  \{i,j|k\} &\text{: $i\vee j$ is a descendant of $i \vee j \vee k$}.
\end{align*}
This combinatorial optimization problem can be relaxed by looking for a hyperbolic embedding,
replacing $i \vee j$ with the point on the geodesic between $i$ and $j$
closest to the origin, and
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
    \mathbf 1_{ \{ i,j | k \} } \\
    \mathbf 1_{ \{ i,k | j \} } \\
    \mathbf 1_{ \{ j,k | i \} }
\end{pmatrix}
\qquad \text{with} \qquad
\operatorname{softmax}_\tau
\begin{pmatrix}
  d( i \vee j, 0 ) \\
  d( i \vee k, 0 ) \\
  d( j \vee k, 0 )
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
To reconstruct the tree, use
$d(i \vee j, 0)$.
(To speed up the $O(n^3)$ sum,
use all $(i,j)$ pairs,
but sample a single $k$ at random.)

This can be used as a (differentiable) step in a longer ML pipeline (end-to-end training).

