\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast and simple PCA\\ via convex optimization }\\
  D. Garber and E. Hazan (2015)
\end{book}
To find the largest eigenvalue of a positive definite matrix,
use the power method on $M^{-1} = (\lambda I - X)^{-1}$
for $\lambda > \lambda_1(X)$:
it has the same eigenvectors,
but its condition number
$\lambda_ 1 / ( \lambda_1 - \lambda_2 )$
can be lower.
The product $M^{-1} w$ required by the power method
can be efficiently computed
by solving a convex problem,
$M^{-1} w = \argmin_z \tfrac12 z' M z - w' z$.

Use variance-reduced stochastic gradient (SVRG)
and progressively reduce $\lambda$ to bring it closer to $\lambda_1(X)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sharpness minimization\\ for efficiently improving generalization}\\
  P. Foret et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Simultaneously minimize loss and loss sharpness
by replacing $\text{Loss}(w) + \lambda \n w _2^2$
with
\[ \max_{ \n{\epsilon}_p \leq \rho } \text{Loss}(w + \epsilon) + \lambda \n w _2^2. \]
Use the first-order expansion of the loss to approximate the solution of the inner maximization problem
and compute the gradient).
Only compute the sharpness,
$ \max_\epsilon \text{Loss}( w + \epsilon ) - \text{Loss}(w), $
on minibatches. 

