\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A meta-transfer objective\\ for learning to disentangle causal mechanisms}\\
  Y. Bengio et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Latent representations that best encode causal relations are better at transfer learning:
optimize for fast transfer learning and robustness to distributional changes.

For instance the following two models, for discrete variables, are equivalent,
\begin{align*}
  P(A,B) &= P(A) P(B|A) \\
  P(A,B) &= P(B) P(A|B)
\end{align*}
but if one undergoes a sparse change (e.g., an unknown intervention in a correct causal model),
it will adapt faster.

Instead of considering separate models,
one can consider a mixture.
\begin{align*}
  R &= \log \bigl[ \sigma(\gamma) \cdot \text{Lik}_{A \to B} +
  \bigl( 1 - \sigma(\gamma) \bigr) \cdot \text{Lik}_{B \to A} \bigr] \\
  \dfrac{ \partial R }{ \partial\gamma } &= \sigma(\gamma) - \sigma(\gamma + \Delta) \\
  \Delta &= \log \dfrac{
    \text{Lik}_{A \to B} ( \text{new data} )
  }{
    \text{Lik}_{B \to A} ( \text{new data} )
  }
\end{align*}

The variables $A$ and $B$ need not be observed:
they can be a latent representation.

