% 2. Assumptions for causal inference
\textbf{2.} A joint probability on $(X,Y)$ is consistent
with both $X \to Y$ and $X \leftarrow Y$:
\[ p(x,y) = p(x) p(y|x) = p(y) p(x|y). \]
To infer the direction of causality, we can rely on:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Interventions: change $x$ and check if $y$ changes;
\item The ``independence'' of cause $p_X$ and mechanism $p_{Y|X}$ --
  for a notion of independence to be defined;
\item The independence of the noises in the \d{structural causal model} (SCM):
  \begin{align*}
  & X = N_X \\
  & Y = f(X, N_Y) \\
  & N_X \indep N_Y.
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}
(The influence of time is not always clear:
in psychology, cause and effect tend to be simultaneous;
at the microscopic scale, the laws of physics are invertible.)

% 3. Cause-effect models
\textbf{3.}
To model an \emph{intervention} with a SCM,
just change the corresponding assignment.

To study \emph{counterfactuals} with an SCM
\begin{align*}
  & X = N_X \\
  & Y = f(X,N_Y),
\end{align*}
observe $(x,y)$, compute $N_X$ and $N_Y$,
then change the value of $X$ (equivalently, $N_X$) and see how it affects $Y$.
Counterfactual statements are not transitive
($X=2 \Rightarrow Y=5$, $Z=2 \Rightarrow Y=10$, $X=2 \not\Rightarrow Z=10$).

Do not confuse conditional probabilities, interventions (do-calculus) and counterfactuals:
even if the interventional probabilities are the same, the counterfactual statemens can differ.


% 4. Learning cause-effect models
\textbf{4.}
Causal inference is possible with just two variables, but requires additional assumptions.
\[ \underset{\text{cause}}{C} \longrightarrow \underset{\text{effect}}{E} \]
Indeed, every joint distribution $(X,Y)$ can be written
$Y = f(X,N_Y)$, $X \indep N_Y$:
just set $f(x,n_Y) = F_{Y|X}^{-1}( n_Y )$, $N_Y \sim U(0,1)$.

One can restrict the class of models, so that they can only be written in one direction
(and hope that this direction is the ``causal'' one).
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Linear models with non-Gaussian additive noise (LiNGAM),
  $ E = \alpha C + N_E$, $N_E \indep C$;
\item Non-linear additive noise model (ANM),
  $Y = f(X) + N_Y$, $N_Y \indep X$;
\item Discrete additive noise;
\item Post-nonlinear model, $Y = g\bigl( f(X) + N_Y \bigr)$, $N_Y \index X$.
\end{compactitem}

One can choose the direction in which $P_C$ and $P_{E|C}$ are ``independent'', in some sense.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For a deterministic relation,
  \[ Y=f(X), \quad f : [0,1] \to [0,1], \quad f(0)=0, \ f(1)=1, \]
  \d{IGCI} (information geometric causal inference) independence is defined as
  $\cor[ \log f', p_X ] = 0$ (where $\log f'$ and $p_X$ are seen as probability densities).
  We then have $\cor[ \log (f^{-1})' , p_Y ] \geq 0$,
  with equality if $f=\text{id}$
  (the peaks of $p_Y$ are regions where $f$ has small slope and $f^{-1}$ large slope:
  $p_Y$ contains information on $f^{-1}$).
\item In high dimension, the model $Y = AX + N_X$, $N_X \indep X$, $A \in \RR^{e\times d}$
  satisfies the \emph{trace condition} if
  $\tau_e( A \Sigma_{XX} A' ) = \tau_d( \Sigma_{XX} ) \tau_e( A A' ) $,
  where $\tau_k(B) = \text{trace}(B)/k$
  (it measures the ``independence'' of $A$ and $P_X$).
  If $A$ is invertible and $N_X=0$, then
  $\tau( A^{-1} \Sigma_{YY} A^{-1\prime}) \leq \tau(\Sigma_{YY}) \tau( A^{-1} A^{-1\prime} ) $.
  with equality if all the singular values of $A$ have the same absolute value.
\item $P_C$ and $P_{E|C}$ are \emph{algorithmically independent}
if the algorithmic mutual information
\[ I(P_C : P_{E|C} ) := K(P_c) + K(P_{E|C}) - K(P_{C,E}) \]
is zero,
where $K$ is the Kolmogorov complexity. This implies
$K(P_C) + K(P_{E|C}) \leq K(P_E) + K(P_{E|C})$
(it is easier to describe $C \to E$ than $E \to C$.
\end{compactitem}

Implementation:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Regression with subsequent independence tests (RESIT),
  for additive noise models,
  estimates $y \sim x$ and $x \sim y$,
  and tests $ x\indep y - \hat y$ and $y \indep x - \hat x$,
  with an independence test looking beyond correlation, e.g., HSIC
  (in R, use \verb|mgcv::gam(y~x)| and \texttt{dHSIC::dhsic.test}).
  Alternatively, compute the log-likelihoods
  \[ L_{X\to Y} = - \log \var X - \log \var ( y - \hat y ). \]
\item For IGCI, estimate $C_{X\to Y} = \int_0^1 \log f'(x) p(x) dx$ as
  \[ \hat C_{X\to Y} = \dfrac1{N-1} \sum_{j=1}^{N-1} \log \va{ \dfrac{ y_{j+1} - y_j  }{ x_{j+1} - x_j } }, \]
  where $x_1 < x_2 < \cdots < x_N$;
  it should be smaller in the causal direction;
  one could also use $H(X)$ instead of $C_{X\to Y}$
\item For the trce method, compute
\[ r_{X \to Y} = \dfrac{ \tau( A_Y \Sigma_{XX} A_Y' ) }{ \tau(A_Y A_Y' ) \tau( \Sigma_{XX} ) }\]
  it is closer to 1 in the causal direction.
\end{compactitem}

One could train a machine learning model, using datasets (or \emph{dataset features}) as inputs.


% 5. Connections to machine learning I
\textbf{5.}
\emph{Semi-supervised learning} does not work well if $X \to Y$
(but even in this case, knowing $P_X$ can help build a classifier with lower loss: the loss involves $\hat p_X$).

\emph{Covariate shifts} can help,
if a change in $P_{X,Y}$ is better explained by a change in $P_X$ (with $P_{Y|X}$ unchanged)
than by a change in $P_Y$.


% 6. Multivariate causal models
\textbf{6.}
SCMs satisfy the \emph{Markov property}, equivalently:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $d$-separation implies conditional independence;
\item Any $X$ is independent of its non-descendants given its parents;
\item $P(X) = \prod P\bigl(X_i | \text{Pa}(X_i) \bigr)$.
\end{compactitem}

Two graphs are in the same Markov equivalence class
if they have the same skeleton and the same v-structures
(or ``immoralities'': two non-directly-linked parents).

The \emph{completed partially directed acyclic graph} (CPDAG) of a Markov equivalence class
contains a directed edge $i\to j$ iif one of the DAGs in the equivalence class has it.

The \emph{Markov blanket} of a node contains its parents, its children, and it children's parents.

A probability distribution is \emph{faithful} wrt a graph if conditional independence implied $d$-separation.

A causal effect is \emph{confounded} if
\[ P^{\text{do}\,X=x}(y) \neq P(y \,|\, X=x). \]
In the kidney stone example
($Z$ is the size of the stone, large or small, $T$ the treatment, invasive or not, and $R$, the outcome),
% Kidney stone example: Z=size, T=treatment (invasive+efficient, less so), R=recovery
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=1,inner sep = 1pt]
  \node (T) at (0,0) {$T$};
  \node (Z) at (60:1) {$Z$};
  \node (R) at (0:1) {$R$};
  \draw[->,thick] (Z) -- (T);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z) -- (R);
  \draw[->,thick] (T) -- (R);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
the average causal effect (\d{ACE}) is
\begin{align*}
  & P^{\text{do}\,T=A}(R=1) - P^{\text{do}\,T=B}(R=1) \\
  &\qquad \neq P(R=1|T=A) - P(R=1|T=B)
\end{align*}
(the signs can even be different: Simpson's paradox),
but we can adjust for $Z$:
\[
P^{\text{do}\,T=A}(R=1)  = \sum_z \! P(R=1 | T=A, Z=z) P(Z=z).
\]
When those equalities hold, $Z$ a a \emph{valid adjustment set} of $T\to R$.

The parents of $X$ form a valid adjustment set for $Y$ (if $Y \not\in\text{Pa}(x)$)
More generally, $Z$ is a valid adjustment set if it blocks all non-directed paths from $X$ to $Y$
and contains no descendant of any node on a directed path from $X$ to $Y$.

In a linear Gaussian model, the effeect of $X$ on $Y$ can be summarized by a single number,
\[ \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial x } \E\nolimits^{\text{do}\, X = x } [ Y ]. \]

The \emph{propensity score} compresses the influence of several confounders $Z$
into a lower-dimensional variable $L$.
% Propensity score
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.8,xscale=1.2,inner sep = 1pt]
  \begin{scope}
  \node (Z1) at (0,2) {$Z_1$};
  \node (Z2) at (1,2) {$Z_2$};
  \node (Z3) at (2,2) {$Z_3$};
  \node (X)  at (0,0) {$X$};
  \node (Y)  at (2,0) {$Y$};
  \draw[->,thick] (Z1) -- (X);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z2) -- (X);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z3) -- (X);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z1) -- (Y);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z2) -- (Y);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z3) -- (Y);
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=4cm]
  \node (Z1) at (0,2) {$Z_1$};
  \node (Z2) at (1,2) {$Z_2$};
  \node (Z3) at (2,2) {$Z_3$};
  \node[circle,draw,thick] (L)  at (0,1) {$L$};
  \node (X)  at (0,0) {$X$};
  \node (Y)  at (2,0) {$Y$};
  \draw[->,thick] (Z1) -- (L);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z2) -- (L);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z3) -- (L);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z1) -- (Y);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z2) -- (Y);
  \draw[->,thick] (Z3) -- (Y);
  \draw[->,thick] (L)  -- (X);
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Pearl's \emph{do-calculus}
is a list of (three) rules
saying when we can add or remove a condition, or a ``do'' action,
or replace an action by a condition.

To check \emph{interventional equivalence}, it suffices to consider
interventions on single variables, replacing them with noise.
% (if all densities have full support)

The \emph{unit-level causal effect} is
$B_u(t=1) - B_u(t=0)$. The \emph{average causal effect} is
\[ \text{CE} = \operatorname*{Mean}_u B_u(t=1) - B_u(t=0). \]
For a randomized experiment, it can be estimated as
\[
\widehat{\text{CE}} = \dfrac1{\#U_1} \sum_{ u \in U_1} B_u(t=1) -
\dfrac1{\#U_0} \sum_{ u \in U_0} B_u(t=0).
\]


SCMs can be generalized to non-statistical information measures, non-probabilist notions of independence.
Let $R : 2^V \to \RR_+$ be a non-decreasing function, measuring the information in a subset $x \subset V$.
The conditional information is $R(x,y)-R(y) \geq 0$.
The conditional mutual information is
\[ I(x:y|z) = R(x,z) + R(y,z) - R(x,y,z) - R(z); \]
it is non-negative if $R$ is modular.
We can write $x \indep_R y | z$ when it is zero.
For a graph such that $R(x_j, \text{Pa}_j, n_j) = R(\text{Pa}_j,n_j)$
(a node $x_j$ does not contain more information than its parents and its unobserved node $n_j$)
and $R(n_1,\dots,n_d)=\sum R(n_j)$ (the unobserved nodes are independent),
$d$-separation implies conditional independence (wrt $R$).
Examples of such an $R$ include the number of words in a text, or the multi-information,
\[ I(x_1,\dots,x_d) = K(x_1,\dots,x_d) - \sum_j K(x_j), \]
where $K$ is the Kolmogorov complexity.
The algorithmic independence of conditionals,
\[ I( P_{X_1 | \text{Pa}_1}, \dots, P_{X_d | \text{Pa}_d} ) = 0 \]
is equivalent to
\[ K( P_{X_1,\dots,X_d} ) = \sum K( P_{X_j | \text{Pa}_j} ). \]

% 7. Learning multivariate causal models
\textbf{7.}
The DAG is identifiable in the following cases:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Linear Gaussian with equal error variances;
\item Linear non-Gaussian acyclic model (LiNGAM),
  \[ X_i = \sum_{k \in \text{Pa}_i } \beta_{ik} X_k + N_i; \]
\item Nonlinear Gaussian additive noise model
  \[ X_i = f_i( \text{Pa}_i) + N_i, \]
  and its special case, the causal additive model (CAM),
  \[ X_i = \sum_{ k \in \text{Pa}_i } f_{ik}(X_k) + N_i. \]
\end{compactitem}

Known interventions can also help identify the graph.

There are two types of algorithms to infer the structure of an SCM.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Some (IC (inductive causation), PC, SGS)
  test for conditional indepence, \ie, $d$-separation:
  we can detect v-structures; the CPDAG is identifiable.
  The conditional independence tests can be based on partial correlation,
  or be nonlinear: $ X \indep Y | Z$ if $X - \E[X|Z] \indep Y$ or $Y - \E[Y|Z] \indep X$.
\item Score=based methods assume that the correct graph gives a better fit to the data
  and maximize the BIC, or the Bayesian Dirichlet (BD) score
  (for discrete variables, following a multinomial distribution, with a Dirichlet prior),
  or the BDe (BD equivalent, accounting for Markov-equivalence).
  (Greedy equivalence search (GES) uses the BIC, starts with an empty graph,
  greedily adds edges until a local maximum, then tries to remove some of them, and stops there.) 
\end{compactitem}
It is possible to combine score- and independence-based approaches (MaxMin hill climbing).
The following can also help infer the structure of the graph:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Intervention with known targets;
\item Intervention with unknown targets (models with a smaller number of intervened variables are more likely);
\item Environment changes (we sometimes observe the same SCM, with different parameters).
\end{compactitem}

R software to estimate SCMs include
\texttt{pcalg}, \texttt{bnlearn},
\texttt{CompareCausalNetworks},
\texttt{InvariantCausal\-Pre\-diction},
\texttt{daggity}.

% 8. Connections to machine learning II
\textbf{8.}
Half-sibling regression was used for exoplanet search.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=1.5,inner sep = 1pt]
  \node[thick,circle,draw,dotted] (Q) at (0,1) {$Q$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw,dotted] (N) at (1,1) {$N$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw]        (Y) at (0,0) {$Y$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw]        (X) at (2,0) {$X$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw,dotted] (R) at (2,1) {$R$};
  \draw[->] (Q) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (N) -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (N) -- (X);
  \draw[->] (R) -- (X);
  \node at (0,1.5) {\footnotesize signal};
  \node at (1,1.5) {\footnotesize noise};
  \node at (2,1.5) {\footnotesize signal};
  \node at (0,-.5) {\footnotesize measurement of interest};
  \node at (2,-.5) {\footnotesize measurement};
  
\end{tikzpicture}
\]


% 9. Hidden variables
\textbf{9.}
Simpson's paradox shows that ignoring hidden variables can lead to incorrect causal conclusions --
causal reasoning is very sensitive to model mis-specification.

A set of random variables is \emph{causally sufficient} if there are no hidden causes influencing more than one variable.

An \emph{instrumental variable} $Z$ allows us to find the ACE (average causal effect) $\alpha$
in $ Y = \alpha X + \delta H + N_Y$.
% Instrumental variables
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=.7,inner sep = 1pt]
  \node[thick,circle,draw,dotted] (H) at (3,1) {$H$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw]        (Z) at (0,0) {$Z$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw]        (X) at (2,0) {$X$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw]        (Y) at (4,0) {$Y$};
  \draw[->,thick] (Z) -- node[auto,above=0pt,pos=.5] {\footnotesize $\beta$} (X);
  \draw[->,thick] (X) -- node[auto,below=1pt,pos=.5] {\footnotesize $\alpha$} (Y);
  \draw[->,thick] (H) -- node[auto,above=3pt,pos=.7] {\footnotesize $\gamma$} (X);
  \draw[->,thick] (H) -- node[auto,above=3pt,pos=.7] {\footnotesize $\delta$} (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

If you ignore the hidden variables (and apply the PC algorithm),
you can get incorrect causal conclusions.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=.6,inner sep = 1pt]
  \node[thick,circle,draw,dotted] (H) at (3,1) {$H$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw]        (A) at (0,0) {$A$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw]        (B) at (2,0) {$B$};
  \node[thick,circle,draw]        (C) at (4,0) {$C$};
