\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine learning in finance}\\
  M.F. Dixon et al. (2020)
\end{book}

The inconsistent notations make the book needlessly difficult to read.

\textbf{1.} % Introduction
Statistics assumes the data generation process is known,
while machine learning does not, but the models are very similar:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item ARIMA and RNN (\ie, NARX) model stationary time series; 
\item GARCH and GRNN model stationary, heteroskedastic time series; 
\item CNN generalizes exponential smoothing;
\item Auto-encoders generalize PCA.
\end{compactitem}

In supervised learning, a ``teacher'' provides the right answer;
in reinforcement learning (RL), the teacher only provides a ``reward''.

The optimal investment problem can be written
\begin{align*}
  S_{t+1} &= S_t \cdot ( 1 + \phi_t )                  &&\text{asset price}\\
  W_{t+1} &= ( 1-u_t) W_t + u_t W_t ( 1 + \phi_t )     &&\text{wealth}\\
  r_t &= \dfrac{ W_{t+1} - W_t }{ W_t } = u_t \phi_t   &&\text{portfolio returns}\\
  R_t &= r_t - \lambda \var[ r_t ]                   &&\text{reward.}\\
\end{align*}
The policy $u_t$ maximizing $\E \sum R_t$ is
\[      u_t^* = \dfrac{ \E[ \phi_t ] }{ 2 \lambda \var [ \phi_t ] } \]

Other examples include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Algorithmic trading, where the state is the 5-day returns (momentum of all 500 stocks in the S\&P 500)
  and the action whether to buy the S\&P500 for 5 days or not; 
\item Execution: the input is the limit order book (LOB), the output, the next mid-price; 
\item Mortgage: the output is the mortgage state, the input the previous state and explanatory variables.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{2.} % Probabilistic modeling (=Bayesian methods)
Neural networks are well-suited to point-wise estimation -- we also want to estimate uncertainty.

For frequentists, the source of uncertainty is that we only have a sample, not the whole population
($\theta$ is fixed, and $\hat\theta$ is a random variable, which depends on the sample).
\begin{align*}
  \text{MSE} ( \hat\theta ) &:= \E \n{ \hat\theta - \theta }_2^2  \\
  \text{bias}( \hat\theta, \theta ) &:= \E[ \hat\theta - \theta ] = \E[\hat\theta] - \theta \\
  \text{MSE}( \hat\theta, \theta ) & = \tr \var [ \hat \theta ] + \n{ \text{bias}( \hat \theta, \theta ) }_2^2
\end{align*}

%% Bayesian examples: 
%%   - coin toss, uniform prior, beta posterior
%%   - idem, beta prior, conjugate distribution
%%   - Sequential updates

\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item If $n$ is large and $p$ small, the frequentist and \d{Bayesian approach}es are (asymptotically) equivalent.
\item If $p$ is large or the likelihood intractable, the Bayesian approach is easier.
\item If $n$ is small, prefer the Bayesian approach: frequentist results are often only asymptotic.
\end{compactitem}

For model selection, frequentist tests and AIC assume the models are nested.

If the priors are equal, the Bayes factor is equal to the posterior odds.
\begin{align*}
  B &= \dfrac{ P( \text{data} | \text{model}_1 ) }{ P( \text{data} | \text{model}_2 ) } \\  
  P( \text{data} | \text{model} ) &= \int_\theta p(x | M, \theta ) p(\theta | M ) d\theta \\
  \dfrac{ P( \text{model}_1 | \text{data} ) }{ P( \text{model}_2 | \text{data} ) } & = 
  \dfrac{ P( \text{model}_1 ) P( \text{data} | \text{model}_1 ) }{ P(\text{model}_2) P( \text{data} | \text{model}_2 ) }
\end{align*}

%% Example: coin flip, $M_1:$ p=0.5$, $M_2:p\in(0,1)$.

The Bayesian approach prevents overfitting: if the model is too complex, $P(\text{data}|\text{model})$ is low.

\emph{Bayesian model averaging} uses $P(\text{model}|\text{data})$ as weights.

Logistic regression is a \emph{discriminative model} estimating $P(y|x)$.
The naive Bayes model is the corresponding \emph{generative model}:
\[ P(x,y) = p(y) \prod p(x_i | y ). \]

%% Directed: bayesian network
%% Undirected = Markov network = Markov random field
%%   example: RBM (factor analysis and mixture models are RBM)
%% Gaussian mixture model (EM algorithm)

\textbf{3.} % Bayesian regression and Gaussian processes
Bayesian linear regression is a \d{Gaussian process}:
\begin{align*}
  \theta_0, \theta_1 &\sim N(0,1) \\
  y &= \theta_0 + \theta_1 x \\
  \E[ y_i y_j | x_i, x_j ] &= 1 + x_i x_j
\end{align*}

An AR($p$) model is a discrete equivalent of GP regression with a Matern kernel.

Gaussian process regression is available in \texttt{sklearn}, \texttt{GpyTorch} or \texttt{Stan}.

In finance, GP regression can be used for option pricing (train on a grid): 
\[ \text{parameters} \mapsto \text{price, greeks, precision}. \]

%% Example: RBF = kernel that only depends on $\n{x-y}$, e.g., squared exponential (SE)
%% Evidence = $\log p(y|x, \lambda) = - [ y' K^{-1} y + \log \det K ] - \dfrac n2 \log \pi 
%%          = model fit + penalty
%% $K$ depends on $\lambda$

For large amounts of data, use \d{structured kernel interpolation} (SKI):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Choose a subset of points $U$;
\item Use local cubic interpolation: $K_{X,U} \approx W_X K_{U,U} $, with $W_X$ sparse;
\item Note that $K_{X,Z} \approx W_X K_{U,U} W_Z '$.
\end{compactitem}
If the inducing points $U$ form a grid and $K$ is an RBF kernel, 
$K_{U,U}$ usa Kronecker product of Toeplitz matrices: $K_{U,U} = T_1 \tens \cdots \tens T_p$.
Use conjugate gradient (CG) to solve $K^{-1} y$, and compute $\log \det K$ with an approximate diagonalization
(Toeplitz matrices are almost circulant, and circulant matrices are diagonalizable with a Fourier transform); 
rescale to $[0,1]^n$ for numeric stability.
For extrapolation, combine squared exponential (SE) and linear kernels.

The GP also provides analytic derivatives (Greeks): 
$ \partial_{x^*} \E[ f | x, y, x^*] = \partial _{ x^*} \mu_{x^*} + \partial _{x^*}  K_{x^*,x} ( K_{x,x} + \sigma^2 I)^{-1} y $

Gaussian processes can have a multi-dimensional response:
\begin{align*}
  f &\sim MGP( m \mu, K, \Omega) \\
  \text{vec} [ f(x_1),\dots,f(x_n) ] &\sim N(\operatorname{vec} M, \Sigma \tens \Omega)
\end{align*}

\textbf{4.} % Feed-forward networks (only toy financial examples)
The VC dimension of a \d{feed-forward network} is
\[ \text{VC dim} \geq W \times L \times \log \dfrac WL \]
where $L$ is the number of layers and $W$ their width.

If the payoff of an option, the option price is a convex function of the spot price:
to ensure convexity in a neural net, have the weights of all the layers but the first be positive.

Neural networks can be used in option pricing, e.g.,
to compute the option price $V$ from the spot price $S$
(with constraints: $V \geq 0$, convex, $0 \leq \Delta \leq 1$)
or to predict the implied volatility $\sigma$ from the moneyness $M = S/K$
(with constraints: $\sigma > 0$, convexity) --
more constraints may be needed to to avoid arbitrage (e.g., option price increasing wrt $T$, convex wrt $K$).

Bayesian neural nets add noise to the weights.

% No details: RBM, SG-MCMC, PX-MCMC, VI

\textbf{5.} To interpret a neural net, compute its \d{sensitivities},
$\partial y / \partial x$, and $\partial^2 y / \partial x_i \partial x_j$ for the interactions

%% No details: 
%% - Garson's algorithm
%% - Olden's algorithm

For instance, the linear risk model $r = Bf + \epsilon$
can be generalized into a nonlinear risk model
$r_t = F_t( B_t) + \epsilon_t$;
the sensitivities can be interpreted as (non-linear) factor returns.

