The max can be replaced with the \emph{mellow max}
\[ \text{mm}(\mathbf x) = \dfrac 1\omega \log \left( \dfrac1n \sum e^{\omega x_i } \right). \]

\textbf{10.} % RL applications: optimal trading, option pricing, G-learning
Reinforcement learning is trickier in finance, because of the higher dimension and the lower signal/noise ratio.

In option pricing, RL can provide replicating portfolios, 
minimizing the risk-adjusted cost of hedging:
\begin{align*}
  & H_T(S_T)                 &&\hspace*{-3cm}\text{ payoff} \\
  & \Pi_t = u_t S_t + B_t    && \hspace*{-3cm}\text{ hedge portfolio} \\
  & u_T = 0 \\
  & \Pi_T = B_T = H_T(S_T) \\
  & u_t S_{t+1} + e^{ r \Delta t} B_t = u_{t+1} S_{t+1} + B_{t+1}  \\
  & B_t = e^{ - r \Delta t} [ B_{t+1} + ( u_{t+1} - u_t ) S_{t+1} ]
\end{align*}
%% Sample paths from $S$; 
%% compute the corresponding distribution of $\Pi_0$ (it depends on $u$)
%% [analogy with Longstaff-Schwartz American option pricing]
\[
u^*_t(S_t) = \argmin_u \var[ \Pi_t | \mathscr F_t ] = \dfrac{ 
  \cov( \Pi_{t+1}, \Delta S_t | \mathscr F_t )
}{ 
  \var( \Delta S_t | \mathscr F_t )
}
\]
