%%   X_t &= - ( \mu - \sigma^2 / 2 ) t + \log S_t  \\
%%   d X_t &= \sigma dW_t
%% \end{align*}
%% Actions: $ X_t \mapsto a_t(X_t)  $  depends on both (detrended) price and time
%% (...)
%% (...) [horribly messy]

In higher dimensions, prefer probabilistic methods to Q-learning.
\begin{align*}
  &x_t  && \text{portfolio values} \\
  &b_t && \text{cash} \\
  &v_t = \mathbf 1 ' x_t + b_t  &&\text{portfolio value} \\
  &x_t^+ = x_t + u_t  &&\text{investment decision} \\
  &\mathbf 1 ' u_t + b_t^+ - b_t  = 0  &&\text{self-financing, $v_t = v_t^+$} \\
  &p_{it}  &&\text{asset prices} \\
  &r_t  &&\text{asset returns} \\
  &x_{t+1} = (\mathbf 1 + r_t) \odot x_t^+ \\
  &r_t - r_f \mathbf 1  = W z_t + M' u_t + \epsilon_t &&\text{asset return model} \\
  &z_t  &&\text{predictors} \\
  &M' u_t && \text{market impact} \\
  &z_{t+1}= (I-\Phi) \odot z_t + \epsilon^z_t     &&\text{OU process} \\
  &y = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ z \end{pmatrix}   &&\text{state} \\
  &R^0 = ( r_t - r_f \mathbf 1) ' (x_t + u_t ) &&\text{wealth gain} \\
  &R = R^0 - \lambda \var[R^0] - \text{impact} \rlap{\ensuremath{{}- \text{fee}}}     &&\text{\qquad\qquad reward}
\end{align*}

The Bellman equation can be written 
\begin{align*}
  V^*(y) &= \max_a R(y,a) + \gamma \E[ V^*(y')] \\
         &= \max_\pi \E_{ a \sim \pi } [ R(y,a) + \gamma \E[ V^*(y')] ].
\end{align*}
One can then add a penalty
\[  \dfrac 1\beta \log \dfrac{ \pi(a|y) }{ \pi_0(a|y) } \]
for each time step
\[  \dfrac1\beta \sum_{ t' \geq t } \gamma^{t'-t} \log \dfrac{ \pi( a_{t'} | y_{t'} ) }{ \pi_0( a_{t'} | y_{t'} ) }. \]

\d{G-learning} is Q-learning with a stochastic policy and an entropy penalty, for noisy environments
(the G-function is the Q-function with an entropy penalty).
F-learning is the analogue for the the state value function $V$.
\emph{Soft-Q-learning} is G-learning with a uniform prior $\pi_0$.

% Linear dynamics + quadratic rewards + deterministic policy: linear quadratic regulator (LQR)
% LQR with entropy regularization -- tractable if there is no market impact
% With market impact: nonlinear dynamics
% Deterministic policies: IQR (iterated quadratic regulator) -- iteratively linearize the dynamics (as with the extended Kalman filter)

RL also applies to wealth management, 
\[  W_{t+1} = (W_t - c_t) [ (1 - \alpha_t) R  + \alpha_t R_f ] \]
where the policy is $ (c_t,\alpha_t)_{0 \leq t < T} $
and the reward CRRA utility.
% [bad notations]

% LSPI    G-learning: high-dimension, arbitrary utility function
For instance, for a defined-contribution retirement plan,
\begin{align*}
  x_t &: \text{asset values (including cash)} \\
  u_t &: \text{changes} \\
  r_t&: \text{returns} \\
  \bar r_t&: \text{average returns} \\
  \Sigma_t \\
  c_t&: \text{cash inflow}  \\
  -c_t&: \text{consumption}
\end{align*}
the reward is
\[ -c_t - \lambda \E[ ( \text{target} - (1+r_t)(x_t+u_t) )_+ ] - u'_t \Omega u_t, \]
where the second term is a penalty for missing a target portfolio value (replace it with a quadratic penalty for tractability),
and the last term is a regularizer.

% "semi-analytic formulation of G-learning with Gaussian time-varying policies (GTVP)"

\textbf{11.} % Inverse RL (IRL) and imitation learning (IL)
\emph{Behavioural cloning} does not generalize well: the model does not understand the dynamics of the system.
Instead, learn the reward function.

In \d{inverse reinforcement learning} (IRL), the data is $(s,a,s')$, not $(s,a,r,s')$,
and the goal is to find both a reward function $r$ and a policy $\pi$.
The reward function is not well-specified: the optimal policy is unchanged if the reward is transformed as 
\[  \tilde r ( a, a, s' ) = r(s,a,s') + \gamma \Phi(s') - \Phi(s). \]

% MaxEnt IRL: $  \pi_\theta(a|s) = \dfrac{ \exp \hat r_\theta(s,a) }{ Z_\theta(s) } $
% Causal entropy
% Maximal causal entropy optimization
% Dual problem
% G-learning = soft Q-learning = Max Causal Entropy
% (...)
%
% Example: Learn the preferences and price sensitivities of customers of a recurrent service
%   Rem: noise is easily mistaken for agent heterogeneity
%   Assume that the reward (customer utility) is a linear combination of features (quota, price, consumption, etc. -- for a mobile data plan),
%   possibly with quadratic and interaction terms
%   Relative entropy IRL: a generalization of IRL, with a non-uniform prior
% (...)

GAIL (generative adversarial imitation learning)
% IRL:
calibrates the reward function so that the solution of the RL problem looks like the demonstration.

% GP and inverse RL
% Reward = linear combination of features
% If you do not have features, use a Gaussian process
% Bayesian IRL: the reward is stochastic; use a GP prior
% GP-IRL: like GP regression, but the function is not observed

We can also learn from failed trajectories (labeled as such) -- they are actions to avoid.
IRLF (IRL from failure) looks for a policy leading to trajectories close to the successful ones and far from the failed ones.

T-REX (Trajectory-ranked Reward EXtrapolation)
learns preferences: not the absolute values $r(s,a)$, but just whether $r(s,a) > r(s',a')$.
% Learn from two sets of trajectories, "good", and "bad".

% D-REX (Disturbance-based Reward EXtrapolation): adding noise to a policy learned with behavioural cloning makes it worse.

%Example: financial cliff-walking

Financial applications
Infer the reward function of a single agent: 
  - high-frequency trading strategy identification (e.g., for fraud detection by regulators): 
    cluster the strategies using the learned reward functions, rather than arbitrary features, which could be uninformative
  - reward function of a risk-averse option trader: assuming the trader uses the QLBS model, there is only one parameter to find, the risk aversion λ
  - reward function of a portfolio investor (...)
Infer the reward function of the "average" investor:
  - Investor sentiment
      State: market data
      Actions: sentiment (discretized into 3 levels), from news
      Reward: to estimate -- it will yield "useful" features; dimension reduction of market data
  - Inflow/outflow for a single stock
      Idem, but the actions are the inflow/outflow for a single stock

% \textbf{12.} Market dynamics, IRL, physics ("quantum equilibrium-disequilibrium (QED) model"), hierarchical representations, renormalization group, tensor networks (tensor train, etc.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine learning for factor investing}\\
  G. Coqueret and T. Guida (2020)
\end{book}

The authors provide data: 100 characteristics, uniformized, for 1000 stocks (in the US), monthly, over 20 years.

\textbf{4.} \emph{Fama-MacBeth regressions} proceed in two steps:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For each stock, regress the returns against the factor returns,
  yielding the exposure $\beta_{ik}$ of stock $i$ to factor $k$;
\item For each date, regress the stock returns against the exposures,
  yielding the premium $\gamma_{tk}$ of factor $k$ at time $t$.
\end{compactitem}

Note the difference between \emph{characteristics} (e.g., $E/P$)
and \emph{exposures} (e.g., $\beta$ to the ``high $E/P$ minus low $E/P$'' portfolio).

The factor portfolios are often cryptically called
SMB (size), HML (value), WML (momentum), RMW (profitability), CMS (investment), BAB (low risk).

\textbf{5.}
The features are autocorrelated but the label (the variable to predict) is not: try to remove the autocorrelation.

Removing noisy observations (not only outliers, but also 60\% of the values in the center of the distribution) may help.

% Conditional expectations: similar to my "median per quantile" plots
% Add interactions between macroeconomic variables and features
% Regime-dependent thresholds, for the label definitions: multiply them by exp(\pm( VIX - average VIX ))
% \textbf{8.} Applications of GANs and AEs. [not clear]

\textbf{10.} The \texttt{BART} package provides Bayesian additive regression trees
(regression trees with a prior which, after Monte Carlo sampling, can be used as ensemble).

\textbf{12.} Combine several models with ensembles,
forecast combinations
(find weights $w$, nonnegative and summing to 1, to combine forecasts from several models --
the nonnegative constraint has a sparsifying effect)
and stacked ensembles.

Use decision trees to forecast the absolute error of a model from macro-economic variables;
then, use it as a regime indicator.

\textbf{13.} In portfolio optimization,
try to add a diversifying constraint $w'w \leq \delta$ (or a penalty) on the Herfindahl index.

Try to forecast returns scaled by volatility, $r / e^\sigma$, instead of returns.

\textbf{14.} To interpret models, check the \texttt{iml}, \texttt{lime}, \texttt{breakDown} packages.
% Interpretability: iml, variable importance (drop in performance after shuffling a variable), PDP, lime, shapley, breakDown

\textbf{15.} To infer a causal graph from data, check the \texttt{pcalg}, \texttt{CAM} or \texttt{baycn} package.

The \texttt{CausalImpact} and \texttt{bsts} packages fit structural time series models, to detect or test for the presence of regime changes.

The \texttt{ReinforcementLearning} package provides $Q$-learning on SARS data (state, action, reward, next state), for discrete state and action spaces. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards a definition\\ of disentangled representations}\\
  I. Higgins et al. (2018)
\end{book}
A latent representation is disentangled wrt a symmetry group
of the set of world states,
decomposed as a direct product $G = G_1 \times \cdots \times G_n$, if:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The action of $G$ on the world states extends to an action on the latent representations,
  making the map
  \[ \text{World states} \longmapsto \text{Latent representations} \]
  equivariant;
\item The space of latent representations decomposes as $V = V_1 \times \cdots V_n$,
  such that each $G_i$ acts on $V_i$ and leaves the other $V_j$'s invariant.
\end{compactitem}
Note that:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The decomposition of $G$ is not unique (e.g., for translations in the plane,
  any basis gives a decomposition $\RR^2 \approx \RR \times \RR$);
\item There may be no non-trivial decompositions of $G$ (e.g., rotations, $\text{SO}(3)$).
\end{compactitem}
If $V$ is a vector space and the action linear, we can further cceompose the latent space
into irreducible representations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Disentangling by subspace diffusion}\\
  D. Pfau et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Let $M$ be an orientable Riemannian manifold.
The parallel transport along a loop
$\gamma : [0,1] \to M$, $\gamma(0) = \gamma(1) = x$
at a point $x \in m$,
defines a linear map
$H_\gamma: T_x M \to T_x M$ --
the \emph{holonomy} of $\gamma$.
The \d{holonomy group}
at $x$, $\text{Hol}_x(M)$,
is the set of all such maps.
If $M = M_1 \times \cdots \times M_n$,
then $T_x M = \bigoplus T_x M_i$
(orthogonal decomposition)
and the action of each $\text{Hol}_x(M)$
leaves each $T_x M_i$ invariant.
If $M$ is simply connected and geodesically complete,
the converse is true (\emph{deRham decomposition theorem}):
if a proper subspace $U \subsetneq T_x M$
is invariant under $\text{Hol}_x M$ for some $x$,
then $M = M_1 \times M_2$, $U=T_xM_1$, $U^\perp = T_xM_2$.

The holonomy group requires all loops:
instead, we can get the same information from random walks,
and the corresponding diffusion
\[ \dfrac{ \partial p(x,t) }{ \partial t } = \Delta^0 p(x,t), \]
where $\Delta^0$ is the Laplace-Beltrame operator on functions on $M$.
The Laplacian can be generalized to a Laplacian $\Delta^1$ on $TM$,
and a ``second-order connection Laplacian''
$\Delta^2$ on $T^*M \tens TM$.
If $M = M_1 \times \cdots \times M_n$,
then the projections $T_x M \to T_x M_i$ are in the kernel of $\Delta^2$
(eigenfunctions for $\lambda=0$).

The Geomancer (genmetric manifold component estimator) algorithm
estimates $\Delta^2$ from points in $M$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Build the symmetric nearest neighbour graph;
\item For each $i$, perform PCA on the $x_j - x_i$, $j \in N(i)$,
  to get local coordinates $U_i$ and local tangent vectors
  $v_j$: $x_j - x_i \approx U_i v_j$;
\item Compute the graph Laplacian
  \begin{align*}
    \Delta^{\!0} f &= \biggl( \sum_{ j \in N(i) } f_i - f_j \biggr)_{\!i} \\
    \Delta^{\!1} v &= \biggl( \sum_{ j \in N(i) } v_i - Q_{ij}' v_j \biggr)_{\!i} \\
    \Delta^{\!2} \Sigma &= \biggl( \sum_{ j \in N(i) } \Sigma_i - Q_{ij}' \Sigma_j Q_{ij}\biggr)_{\!i};
  \end{align*}
\item The parallel transport $Q_{ij}$ induced by the ambient (Euclidean) space
  can be estimated from the local coordinates $Q_{ij} = U_{ij} V_{ij}'$,
  where $U_j ' U_i = U_{ij} \Sigma_{ij} V_{ij}' $
  is the SVD decomposition;
\item To avoid spurious eigenfunctions
  (skew symmetric matrices, and small eigenvalues from $\Delta^0$),
  consider the action of $\Delta^2$ on symmetric, zero-trace matrices --
  the eigenfunctions are linear combinations of the desired projections.
\end{compactitem}

