If the ``generative factors'' $G_i$ are known
(orentation, position, shape, colour, etc.),
one can try to minimixe $\text{MI}(G_i, Z_i)$
and minimize $\text{MI}(G_i, Z_j)$, $i \neq j$.

We can allow for confounding in the latent factors $Z_i$
by asking that $Z_{\setminus i}$ be a \emph{valid adjustment set}
for $Z_i \to X$.

