\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Path signature on Lie groups}\\
  D. Lee and R. Ghrist (2020)
\end{book}
The path signature can be generalized to Lie-group-valued time series.

Let $G$ be a Lie group, $\mathfrak g$ its Lie algebra,
$PG$ the set of paths in $G$.
A path in $G$, $\gamma : [0,1] \to G$,
defines a path in $\mathfrak g$, $\gamma ' : [0,1] \to \mathfrak g$;
conversely, a path in $\mathfrak g$, $f : [0,1] \to \mathfrak g$,
defines a path in $G$ via the ODE
\begin{align*}
  \gamma_t ' &= f_t ( \gamma_t ) \\
  \gamma_0 &= g 
\end{align*}
A time serie s$(\hat \gamma_i)_i$ in $G$ defines a path in $G$,
via the discrete derivative and linear interpolation
\begin{align*}
  \hat \gamma_i ' &= \log ( \hat \gamma_i ^{_1} \hat \gamma_{i+1} ) \in \mathfrak g \\
  \gamma_y &= \hat \gamma_i \exp \bigl[ (t-\tau) \hat \gamma_i ' \bigr] , \quad t \in [i,i+1).    
\end{align*}
Note that:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The logarithm is not always defined: the points should be sufficiently close;
\item The exponential is not always surjective (for compact Lie groups, it is);
\item The Lie and Riemann exponentioals differ
  (but they coincide for biinvariant metrics; compact Lie groups have such a metric).
\end{compactitem}\

The \emph{path signature} of a path $\gamma \in PG$
is a formal power series in $\mathfrak g$,
$S(\gamma) \in T(\!( \mathfrak g )\!)$
(it can also be defined with iterated integrals).
\begin{align*}
  & I = (i_1, \dots, i_m ) && \text{multi-index} \\
  & N = \dim G \\
  & 1 \leq i_k \leq N \\
  & \Delta_m     && \text{simplex} \\
  & \omega_1,\dots,\omega_N && \text{basis of $\mathfrak g^*$} \\
  & S(\gamma) =  \int_{\Delta_m}  \rlap{\ensuremath{\omega_{i_1} ( \gamma_{t_1} ') \cdots \omega_{ i_m }( \gamma_{t_m} ' ) dt_1 \cdots dt_m \in \RR }}
\end{align*}

As in Euclidean spaces, the signature characterizes paths up-to tree-like equivalence.

To detect a lead-lag relation between two paths, $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$,
check the sign of
$S^{1,2}(\gamma)$ and $S^{2,1}(\gamma)$ ,
where $\gamma = ( \gamma_1 , \gamma_2 )$.

You may want to transform the time series, e.g.:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Replacing $\gamma_t$ with $(t,\gamma_t)$,
  which removes the reparametrization invariance;
\item Prepending a path from $0$ to $\gamma_0$,
  which removes translation invariance; 
\item Replacing $\gamma_t$ with
  $(\gamma_t, \gamma_{t-\tau}, \dots, \gamma_{t-m\tau} )$.
\end{compactitem}

The signature does not use the Lie algebra structure of $\mathfrak g$.
There is a signature-preserving bijection between paths in $G$
and paths in $\RR^N$: we can apply Euclidean data analysis techniques
to Lie-group-valued time series.

The (normalized) signature transform is a \emph{universal}
and \emph{characteristic} feature map
(those properties are equivalent).
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item[(i)] Any continuous bounded function
  $ f : OG \longrightarrow \RR$
  can be approximated by a linear function;
  \[ f(.) \approx \langle \ell, S(\cdot) \rangle \]
\item[(ii)] The map
  \[
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{rcl}
    \mathscr M (PG ) & \longrightarrow & T(\!( \mathfrak g )\!) \\
    \mu & \longmapsto & \E_\mu[S],
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \]
  where $\mathscr M(PG)$ is the set of finite Borel measures on $PG$,
  is injective.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast differentiable sorting and ranking}\\
  M. Blondel et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Let $\sigma(\theta)$, $s(\theta)$ , $r(\theta)$ denote the arg sort, sort and rank of a vector $\theta \in \RR^n$.
Argsort and rank can be defined as
\begin{align*}
  \sigma(\theta) &= \argmax_\sigma \langle \theta_\sigma , \rho \rangle \\
    r(\theta) &= \argmax_\pi \langle \theta , \rho_\pi \rangle
\end{align*}
where $\rho = (n,n-1,\dots,1)$
and $\theta_\sigma$ is $\theta$ permuted by $\sigma$.
The \emph{permutahedron} of a vector $x \in \RR^n$
is the convex hull of its permutations.
\[ P(w) = \text{Conv} \{ w_\sigma, \sigma \in \mathfrak S_n \} \subset \RR^n \]
Sorting and ranking are soutions of linear programs,
\begin{align*}
  s(\theta) &= \argmax_{ y \in P(\theta) } \langle y , \rho \rangle \\
  t(\theta) &= \argmax_{ y \in P(\rho) } \langle y , - \theta \rangle
\end{align*}
The optimization problem
\[ \argmax_{ \mu \in P(w) } \langle \mu, z \rangle \]
can be regularized with
$Q(\mu) = \tfrac12 \n \mu ^2 $,
which gives \[ \argmin_{ \mu \in P(w)} \tfrac12 \n{ \mu - z }^2 \]
(projection onto the permutahedron)
or the entropic regularization
$E(\mu) = \langle \mu, \log \mu - 1 \rangle$,
which gives
\[ \log \argmax_{ \mu \in P(e^w) } \langle z ,\mu \rangle - E(\mu) = \log \argmin_{ \mu \in P(e^w) } \text{KL}( \mu, e^z ). \]
To control the regularization strength, multiply $Q$ or $E$ by $\epsilon$ or, equivalently, divide $z$ by $\epsilon$.
Fine-tuning $\epsilon$ is important for some applications (top-$k$ classification), but irrelevant for others.
The solution can be computed as
$ z - v ( z_{\sigma(z) }, w )_{ \sigma^{-1}(z) } $,
where
\begin{align*}
  v_Q(s,w) &= \argmin_{ v_1 \geq \cdots \geq v_n } \tfrac12 \n{ v - (s-w) } ^ 2 \\ % \text{ (isotonic regression)} \\
  e_E(s,w) &= \argmin_{ v_1 \geq \cdots \geq v_n }\langle e^{s-v} , 1 \rangle + \langle e^w , v \rangle .
\end{align*}

These isotonic regressions can be computed efficiently with the ``pool adjacent violator'' (PAV) algorithm.
The Jacobian is easy to compute.
The whole algorithm is $O(n \log n)$.
Code available (Numpy, Jax, PyTorch, TensorFlow): \texttt{fast-soft-sort}.

Applications include rank correlation and trimmed least squares.

