Self-supervised pretraining works well if
\[ \text{input} \indep \text{pretext task} \,|\, \text{downstream task} \]

Approximate conditional independence can be quantified with partial correlation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PDE-constrained optimization\\ and the adjoint method}\\
  A.M. Bradley (2010)
\end{book}
The adjoint method computes the gradient,
$df\bigl( x(p) \bigr) / dp$,
of an implicit function $x(p)$ defined by $g(x,p) = 0$,
using the Lagrangian $L(x,p,\lambda) = f(x) + \lambda ' g(x,p) $.
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ df }{ dp }
  &= \dfrac{ d }{ dp } L\bigl( x(p), p, \lambda \bigr) \\
  &= \dfrac{ d }{ dp } \Bigl[ f(x) + \lambda ' g \bigl( x)p) , p \bigr) \Bigr] \\
  &= \dfrac{df}{dx} \dfrac{dx}{dp} + \lambda ' \Bigl( \dfrac{ \partial g}{ \partial x } \dfrac{ dx }{ dp } + \dfrac{ \partial g }{ \partial p } \Bigr) \\
  &= \Bigl( \dfrac{df}{dx} + \lambda ' \dfrac{\partial g}{ \partial x} \Bigr) \dfrac{dx}{dp} + \lambda ' \dfrac{ \partial g}{ \partial p} \\
  \intertext{If $dx/dp$ is difficult to compute, we can choose $\lambda$ to make the first term disappear.}
  &= - \Bigl( \dfrac{\partial g}{\partial x} \Bigr)^{-1} \dfrac{ df }{ dx } \dfrac{ \partial g }{ \partial p }
\end{align*}
We can get the same result directly by differentiating $g(x,p)=0$,
but this is useful for PDE-constrained optimization,
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & p, x \\
  \text{To minimize} & \displaystyle F(x,p) = \int_0^T f\bigl( x(t) , p, t \bigr) dt \\
  \text{Such that} & h(x, \dot x, p, t ) = 0 \\
  & g\bigl( x(0) , p \bigr) = 0 
\end{array}
\]
where directly computing
\[ \dfrac{ dF}{dp} = \int_0^T \Bigl( \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x} \dfrac{dx}{dp} + \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p} \Bigr) dt \]
is difficult because of $dx/dp$.
The Lagrangian is
\[ L = \int_0^T (f + \lambda h ) + \mu' g, \]
and we can choose $\lambda$ (a function) and $\mu$ (a vector)
to make the terms in $dx/dp$ disappear:
we are left with a differential equation for $\lambda$.

Applications of PDE-constrained optimization include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Finding the parameters of a PDE given observations;
\item Design optimization (e.g., airplane wing).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural CDEs for long time series\\ via the log-ODE method}\\
  J. Morill et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Neural ODEs are a continuous analogue of ResNets.
Neural controlled differential equations are continuous analogues of RNNs.

A \emph{controlled ODE} (CDE)
is an equation of the form
\[ Z_t = Z_a + \int_a^t f(Z_s) d X_s \]
where $X : [a,b] \to \RR^n$ and $f$ is unknown.

In a neural CDE, $X$ is known (linear interpolation of an irregularly sampled time series),
$X+Z$ is a latent (\ie, hidden) variable,
and $Y_t = \ell( Z_t )$
is observed;
given $X$ and $Y$,
we want to estimate $f$ and $\ell$.
As for RNNs, estimation for long time series is challenging.
The \emph{log-ODE} method uses
\[ Z_t = Z_a + \int_a^t \hat f( Z_s ) \text{LogSig} _{a,b} ^N (X) ds, \]
where $\text{LogSig}_{a,b}^N$ is the depth-$N$ log-signature anf $\hat f$
extends $f$ to the Lie algebra
where $\text{LogSig}_{a,b}^N$ lives
(but the implementation directly estimated $f$ and ignores the Lie algebra structure).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient transformers: a survey}\\
  Y. Tay et al (2020)
\end{book}
Transformers combine vector embedding, positional encoding, self- (and cross-)attention,
layer normalizartion, and residual connections.

The attention matrix is too large for the model to efficiently deal with large sequences,
but it can be approximated with fixed patterns
(blocks, strides, connected blocks),
learnable patterns (e.g., $k$-means clustering of the tokens),
side memory, low-rank factorizations, kernels.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tensor programs I:\\ wide feedforward or recurrent networks\\ of any architecture are Gaussian processes}\\
  G. Yang (2019)
\end{book}
Random, shallow, infinitely wide neural nets are Gaussian processes.
This generalizes to ``tensor programs'',
involving three types,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $G$: vector, asymptotically Gaussian,
\item $H$: vector,
\item $A$: matrix, asymptotically Gaussian,
\end{compactitem}
and three operations,
\begin{align*}
  \text{MatMul} &: \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl} A \times G & \longrightarrow & G \\ (A,x) & \longmapsto & Ax \end{array} \right. \\
  \text{LinComb} &: \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl} G^k & \longrightarrow & G \\ (x_1\dots,x_n) & \longmapsto & \sum a_i x_i \end{array} \right. \\
  \text{NonLin} &: \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl} G^k & \longrightarrow & H \\ (x_1,\dots,x_n) & \longmapsto & \phi(x_1,\dots,x_n) \end{array} \right. .\\
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tensor programs II:\\ neural tangent kernel for any architecture}\\
  G. Yang (2020)  
\end{book}
``Neural tangent kernel'' refers to two results,
corresponding to the initialization and the training of a neural net.
\begin{compactitem}
\item[(i)] For a shallow network $f$
  with random weights,
  $\Theta(x,y) = \langle \nabla_{\!\theta} f_\theta(x) , \nabla_{\!\theta} f_\theta(y) \rangle$
  converges to a deterministic kernel as the width of $f$ tends to infinity.
\item[(ii)] During gradient descent, the kernel remains constant and describes the evolution of the neural net:
  \[ f_t - f_{\text{data}} = e^{ - \eta t \Theta } ( f_0 - f_{\text{data}} ). \]
\end{compactitem}

