Original NTK paper.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generative language modeling\\ for automated theorem proving}\\
  S. Polu and I. Sutskever (2020)
\end{book}
A transformer model (GPT-like, 36 layers) can generate proof for MetaMath:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Predict a proof step from a goal;
\item Pretrain on text (arxiv, Math StachExchange, Github);
\item Train on synthetic data ($n$-digit arithmetic, ring algebra)
  and rteal data (\texttt{set.mm}: 38k theorems, 3m steps);
\item Also learn a value function
\end{compactitem}

\emph{MetaMath} is a proof assistant based on string substitution --
it is simpler than human-friendly alternatives (Coq, HOL Light, Lean),
but its \emph{de Bruijin factor},
the ratio of the length of a formal proof to that of a textbook,
is $10\sim20$, instead of $1\sim3$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{KarateClub: an API-oriented\\ open-source Python framework\\ for unsupervised learning on graphs}\\
  B. Rozembergczhi et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Sklearn-like API for community detection, wholegraph embedding, node and graph classification;
built on Numpy (dense matrices),
Scipy (sparse metrices),
gensim (matrix factorization),
PyGSP (signal porocessing on graphs), NetworkX.
GraphTool (ciommunity detection) and \textsc{snap} are more limited.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficiently sampling functions\\ from Gaussian process posteriors}\\
  J.T. Wilson et al. (2020)
\end{book}
One can sample from a Gaussian process
\[ y | X  \sim  N(0,K_{XX} ) \]
as follows
\begin{align*}
  \mu_* &= K_* ( K_{XX} + \sigma^2 I) ^{-1} y \\
  \Sigma_* &= K_{**} - K_{*X} ( K_{XX} + \sigma^2 I ) ^{-1} K_{X*} .
\end{align*}
The \emph{Nystr\"om approximation}
approximates $K_{XX}$ using a subset $Z \subset X$ of the data,
\[ K \approx K_Z K_{ZZ}^{-1} K_Z'. \]
The \emph{fully independent training conditional} (FITC) method
uses an arbitrary set of points (not necessarily among the observations),
\[ K \approx K_Z K_{ZZ}^{-1} K_Z' + \text{diag}( K_{XX} - K_{XZ} K_{ZZ}^{-1} K_{ZX} ) + \sigma^2 I \]
(it is obtained by integrating those points out
\[ p(y|x) = \int_u p(y,u | x,z) = \int_u p(y|u,x,z) p(u|z) \]
and assuming that the variance of the first factor is diagonal).
The computations can be simplified using \emph{Woodbury's formula}.

Besides those \emph{function space approximations},
the \emph{weight space approximation} expresses $f$
as a linear combination of random Fourier features.
\begin{align*}
  \phi_i(x) &= \cos( \theta_i x + \tau_i) \\
  f &= \sum w_i \phi_i \\
  w | x,y &\sim N(\mu,\Sigma) \\
  \mu &= ( \Phi' \Phi + \sigma^2 I) ^{-1} \Phi' y \\
  \Sigma &= ( \Phi ' \Phi + \sigma^2 I )^{-1} \sigma^2
\end{align*}

If $(a,b)$ is Gaussian, then (\emph{Matheron's rule} -- equality in distribution)
\[ (a | b = \beta) \overset{\text{d}}{=} a + \cov(a,b) \cov(b,b)^{-1} (\beta-b) \]
can be used to incrementally update a sample from a Gaussian proccess.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards an API for the real numbers}\\
  H.J. Boehm (2020)
\end{book}
\d{Recursive real} arithmetic replaces real numbers $x \in \RR$
with (computable) functions
\( f_x : \RR_+^\times \to \QQ \)
where $f_x(e)$ is a rational approximation of $x$ with error bounded by $e$:
$\va{ x - f_x(e) } \leq e$.
Equality of recursive reals in undecidable (halting problem),
except in some special cases (often, when the computations only involve integers, the 4 operations, log, exp, trigonometric functions and their inverses).

Applications include calculators
(Google's Android calculator,
for users who do not understand IEEE floating point arithmetic)
and testing the accuracy claims of floating point implementations.

Also check F. Johansson's \texttt{Calcium}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Traditional and heavy-tailed regularization\\ in neural network models}\\
  C.H. Martin and M.W. Mahoney (2019)
\end{book}
As the optimization progresses, the spectral density of the weight matrices
(distribution of the singular values) changes:
Marchenko-Pastur (MP), corresponding to Gaussian weights;
MP with outliers, corresponding to Gaussian weights plus a low-rank matrix;
power law distribution.
Code \href{https://github.com/CalculatedContent/WeightWatcher}{available}.
