\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Compact representation of uncertainty\\ in hierarchical clustering}\\
  C.S. Greenberg et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Hierarchical clusterings are often built with greedy algorithms,
beam search, or sequential Monte Carlo.
Consider a (posterior) probability distribution on hierarchies $H$,
\begin{align*}
  &\text{linkage function} && E: \mathscr P(X) \times \mathscr P(X)\longrightarrow \RR_+ \\
  &\text{energy of a tree} &&
  E(H) = \prod_{
  \hspace*{-1mm}
  \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.15,yscale=.2,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=1pt]
    \node[circle,fill] (A) at (1,1) {};
    \node[circle,fill] (B) at (0,0) {};
    \node[circle,fill] (C) at (2,0) {};
    \draw (B) -- (A) -- (C);
    \node[anchor=north,inner sep=2.5pt] at (-.2,0) {\scriptsize $X_i$};
    \node[anchor=north,inner sep=2.5pt] at (2.4,0) {\scriptsize $X_j$};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \hspace*{-2mm}
\in H } E(X_i,X_j).
\end{align*}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.2,yscale=.5,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt,
  every node/.style={circle,fill}
]
  \node (A1) at (0,0) {};
  \node (A2) at (1,0) {};
  \node (A3) at (2,0) {};
  \node (A4) at (3,0) {};
  \node (B1) at (4,0) {};
  \node (B2) at (5,0) {};
  \node (B3) at (6,0) {};
  \node (B4) at (7,0) {};
  \node (B5) at (8,0) {};
  \node (C1) at (9,0) {};
  \node (C2) at (10,0) {};
  \node (D1) at (11,0) {};
  \node (D2) at (12,0) {};
  \node (E) at (13,0) {};
  \node (A) at (1.5,1) {};
  \node (B) at (6,1) {};
  \node (C) at (9.5,1) {};
  \node (D) at (11.5,1) {};
  \node (ABC) at (6,2) {};
  \node (ABCD) at (11.5,3) {};
  \node (ABCDE) at (13,3.333) {};
  \draw (A1) -- (A);
  \draw (A2) -- (A);
  \draw (A3) -- (A);
  \draw (A4) -- (A);
  \draw (B1) -- (B);
  \draw (B2) -- (B);
  \draw (B3) -- (B);
  \draw (B4) -- (B);
  \draw (B5) -- (B);
  \draw (C1) -- (C);  
  \draw (C2) -- (C);  
  \draw (D1) -- (D);  
  \draw (D2) -- (D);
  \draw (A) -- (ABC);
  \draw (B) -- (ABC);
  \draw (C) -- (ABC);
  \draw (ABC) -- (ABCD);
  \draw (D) -- (ABCD);
  \draw (ABCD) -- (ABCDE);
  \draw (E) -- (ABCDE);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

The partition function can be computed, exactly, with \emph{dynamic programming}, using
\[
Z(X) =
\sum_H E(H) =
\!\!\!\!
\sum_{\substack{
    Y \subset X \text{ st} \\
    x \in X \\
    \va X > 1
}}
\!\!\!\!
E( X_i, X \setminus X_i )
\cdot
Z(X_i)
\cdot
Z(X \setminus X_i ).
\]
The MAP hierarachical clustering can be computed similarly,
using $\max$ instead of $\sum$
(the complexity is ``only'' $O(2^{2m})$,
much less than the number of hierarchies, $(2n-3)!!$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable nearest neighbour search\\ for optimal transport}\\
  A. Backurs et al. (2020)
\end{book}
The Wasserstein-1 distance on a finite set $X \subset \RR^n$
can use used, for instance, to compare documents,
seen as distributions on words,
after word2vec or glove embedding,
but it is expensive to compute.
Approximations include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Mapping each point in the support of $\mu$ to the nearest point in the support of $\nu$;
\item Building a random quad-tree (k-d tree) on $X$ and using
  \[ W_1(\mu,\nu) \approx \sum_{x \in X} 2^{ \ell(x) } \va{ \mu(x) - \nu(x) } \]
  where $\ell(x)$ is the level of $x$ in the tree.
\end{compactitem}
Instead, actually compute the optimal transport on the tree,
\[ f = \argmin_{ \substack{
    f \text{ s.t.} \\
    \operatorname{pr}_1 f = \mu \\
    \operatorname{pr}_2 f = \nu
}}
\sum_{x,y \in X }
f(x,y) t(x,y)
\]
where $t$ is the distance on the tree,
and use it with the actual Euclidean distance instead
\[ W_1(\mu,\nu) \approx \sum_{x,y \in X} f(x,y) \n{ x - y }. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the limitations\\ of representing functions on sets}\\
  E. Wagstaff et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Representing arbitrary permutation-invariant functions with a neural net (deep set)
requires a latent representation layer at least the input dimension --
dimension reduction would require discontinuous functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evolving normalization-activation layers}\\
  H. Liu et al. (2020)
\end{book}
\emph{Layer search} (as oppsed to architecture search)
for a replacement for the BatchNorm-ReLU (or BatchNorm-Swish) combination commonly used
as a computation graph made of elementary operations
($+$, $\times$, $/$, max, neg, $\sigma$, tanh, exp, log, abs, $\cdot^2$, $\sqrt\cdot$)
and aggregations on some dimensions of the input tensor
(batch/channel/instance/group mean/sd)
leads to a combination of batch and instance normalization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Enhance social recommendation\\ with adversarial graph convolutional networks}\\
  J. Yu et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Social recommendation systems do not work that well
because the relations between users are noisy, heterogeneous and too few.
Use an autoencoder to generate
\emph{alternate neighbourhoods}
(intuitively, informative neighbourhoods, and/or users with similar taste)
by applying a GCN (with skip-layer connections)
to motif-induced adjacency matrices
