\]

In a Bayesian context,
the point null hypothesis $H_0: \beta = 0$
has probability zero and should be replaced by a \emph{region of practical equivalence} (ROPE),
e.g., $H_0: \beta \in [ - 0.1\sigma, +0.1\sigma]$.
The null hypothesis can be rejected if
$P_{\text{posterior}} (H_0) \leq \alpha$
(do not use it if the parameters are highly correlated).

The package relies on \texttt{rstanarm} and also computes (and plots)
MAP estimates, credible intervals and Bayes factors.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{AutoML-Zero: evolving machine learning algorithms from scratch}\\
  E. Real et al. (2020)
\end{book}
AutoML often limits the search to one aspect of the model,
e.g., architecture search using hand-designed block, optimization algorithms, LSTM-like gating mechanisms, data augmentation.
A more flexible search space considers three functions,
\texttt{setup}, \texttt{predict}, \texttt{learn};
\texttt{predict} and \texttt{learn} are called in a loop;
they are made up of assembler-like operations on scalars (s), vectors (v) and matrices (m),
of the same size as the input, with no control structures, e.g.,
\begin{align*}
  m_1 &= s_2 \times m_2 \\
  s_0 &= \text{mean}(v_1) \\
  s_3 &= \sin(s_4) \\
  s_1 &= \text{runif}().
\end{align*}
The search space is too large for random search, but evolutionary strategies fare better.
To speed up the search, use small datasets, and detect equivalent algorithms by hashing their outputs on 10 inputs.

The search rediscovers gradient descent, multiplicative interactions, normalized gradient,
weight averaging, dropout, learning rate decay.

This approach can also be used to improve existing algorithms to adapt them to different situations
(e.g., little data, fast training, more classes, etc.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scaling laws for neural language models}\\
  J. Kaplan et al. (2020)
\end{book}
The loss of a transformer model depends on compute time, dataset size, model size.
\[
  \text{Loss} \propto \text{Compute} ^ {-0.05} \times \text{Data} ^ {-0.10} \times \text{Size} ^ {-0.10} 
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fawkes: protecting personal privacy against unauthorized deep learning models}\\
  S. Shan et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Modify your pictures before distributing them (imperceptibly, in feature space)
to prevent their use to train face recognition systems.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FixMatch: simplifying semisupervised learning with consystency and confidence}\\
  K. Sohn et al. (2020)
\end{book}
FixMatch performs semi-supervised learning
using \emph{pseudolabels}
(forecasts of the model, when the codel is sufficiently confident)
and consistency regularization
(similar inputs should give similar outputs).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stable neural flows}\\
  S. Massaroli et al. (2020)
\end{book}
To learn a mapping $u \mapsto v$,
\emph{neural ODEs} find a vector field $f$ whose integration
gives the desired function.
\begin{align*}
  \dot x &= f(x) \\
  x(0) &= u \\
  x(1) &= v
\end{align*}
More generally, one can add affine maps
\[ u \overset{g}{\longmapsto} x(0) \longmapsto x(1) \overset{h}{\longmapsto} v \]
if the dimensions do not match.
\begin{align*}
  \dot x &= f(u,x) \\
  x(0) &= g(u) \\
  h(x(1)) &= v
\end{align*}
The resulting ODE may have stiff dynamics and chaotic behaviour
(sensitivity to small perturbations in the input lead to adversarial attacks).
Replace
$\dot x = f(u,x)$
with $\dot x = - \partial_x \epsilon(u,x)$
(or, more generally, a Hamiltonian model).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dissecting neural ODEs}\\
  S. Massaroli et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Vanilla neural ODEs (continuous-depth neural nets)
are not universal approximators.
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ dh }{ ds } &= f_\theta(s,h) \\
  h(0) &= x \\
  h(1) &= y
\end{align*}
They can be augmented,
\[
\begin{aligned}
  \dfrac{ d }{ ds } \begin{pmatrix} h \\ a \end{pmatrix} &= f_\theta(s, h, a ) \\
  h(0) &= x \\
  a(0) &= 0 \\
  h(1) &= y 
\end{aligned}
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
\begin{aligned}
  \dfrac{ d }{ ds } \begin{pmatrix} h \\ a \end{pmatrix} &= f_\theta(s, h, a ) \\
  \begin{pmatrix} h(0) \\ a(0) \end{pmatrix} &= g(x) \\
  h(1) &= y.
\end{aligned}
\]
(Higher-order ODEs can also be formulated as augmented neural ODEs.)

\emph{Data-controlled} neural ODEs incorporate the input $x$ in the vector field,
\[ \dfrac{ dh }{ ds } = f_\theta( s, h, x ). \]

\emph{adaptive depth} neural ODEs use a data-rependent integration interval
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ dh }{ ds } &= f_\theta(s, h) \\
  h(0) &= 0 \\
  h( s(x)) &= y.
\end{align*}
[The implementation uses \texttt{torchdiffeq}.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Representation learning\\ through latent canonicalizations}\\
  O. Litany et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Approaches to learn from simulated data include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pretraining on simulated data and fine-tuning on real data;
\item Domain adaptation, if there is a lot of unlabeled real data;
\item Domain randomization.
\end{compactitem}
To learn a ``disentangled'' representation (this often just means ``isotropic''),
train an autoencoder and linear transformations of the latent space which reset
one or two parameters of the (generated) input
(e.g., colour, background colour, size, rotation, font) to a default value.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (I) at (0,0) {input};
  \node (L) at (1,0) {latent};
  \node (O) at (2,0) {output};
  \draw[->] (I) -- (L);
  \draw[->] (L) -- (O);
  \draw[->] (L) to[out=-110,in=-70, looseness=20] node[anchor=north,align=center]{\footnotesize $C_j$: canonicalizer\\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize for feature $j$} (L);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\begin{align*}
  p(x,z) &= p(x|z) p(z) \qquad \text{(generative model)} \\
  p(x,z) &= p(x|z) \underbrace{p(z_1) \cdots p(z_n)}_{\text{disentangled}}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Meta-pseudo labels}\\
  H. Pham et al. (2020)
\end{book}
A classifier can be trained with the true labels (e.g., binary),
smoothed labels,
with the labels from a teacher network trained independently
(log-probabilities, also available for non-labeled data),
or ``meta-labels'', still from a teacher network, not trained to produce good forecasts,
to help train the student.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random projections for manifold learning}\\
  C. Hegde et al. (2007)
\end{book}
The \emph{Grassberger-Procaccia algorithm}
computes the \emph{intrinsic dimension} of a cloud of points from pairwise distances
using the ``scale-dependent correlation dimension''
\begin{align*}
  C(r) &= \operatorname*{Mean}_{ i \neq j } \mathbf 1 _{ \n{ x_i - x_j } \leq r } \\
  \hat k = D(r_1,r_2) &= \dfrac{
    \log C(r_1) - \log C(r_2) 
  }{
    \log r_1 - \log r_2
  }
\end{align*}
where $r_2$, $r_2$ are chosen so that $C$ be linear on $[r_1,r_2]$
amd $[r_1,r_2]$ be large.
It can be computed from a random projection
$\RR^N \to \RR^M$, with $ M \propto K \log N$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rethinking batch normalization\\ in transformers}\\
  S. Shen et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Since batch statistics, in natural language processing,
have much higher variance than in computer vision,
layer-norm is often preferred.
BatchNorm can be used,
but with running statistics instead of batch statistics (``powernorm'').

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{NeRF: representing scenes\\ as neural radiance fields for view synthesis}\\
  M. Mildenhall et al. (2020)
\end{book}
A scene can be represented as a non-convolutional, fully-connected neural net
\[
\left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \RR^5 & \longrightarrow & \RR^4 \\
  (x,y,z,\theta,\phi) & \longmapsto & (R,G,B,A)
\end{array}
\right.
\]
computing the radiance emitted at a point $(x,y,z)$ in direction $(\theta,\phi)$
and turned into an image using volumetric rendering, which is differentiable.
It can be learnt from several (100) views of the same scene
(processed with \textsc{colmap} (SfM)).
The density (alpha) only depends on the position,
but the colour also depends on the direction,
to account for specuar effects.

To capture high-frequency details (neural nets are biased towards low-frequency functions,
map each coordinate to a high-dimensional space
\[
\left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  [-1,1] & \longrightarrow & [-1,1] ^{2L} \\
  x & \longmapsto & ( \sin 2^0 \pi x, \cos 2^0 \pi x , \dots ,
  \sin 2^{L-1} \pi x , \cos 2^{L-1} \pi x )
\end{array}
\right.
\]
(this is the Transformer's positional embedding).

Do not use columetric rendering with fixed points (deterministic quadrature),
but with $N$ points sampled at random in evenly-spaced bind (stratified sampling).

Use two networks, a coarse one, with stratified sampling, to estimate the density (alpha channel),
as a piecewise constant pdf, and a finer one, for density and colour,
using points sampled from this pdf insterad of stratified sampling (hierarchical sampling strategy).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the spectral bias of neural networks}\\
  N. Rahaman et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The Fourier transform of ReLU networks can be computed explicitly:
lower frequencies are learned first,
and they are more robust to random perturbations of the parameters.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Structure from motion}\\
  J.L. Sch\"onberger et al. (CVPR 2016)
\end{book}
Structure-from-motion (SfM) reconstructs a 3D scene
from an unordered image collection, in several steps:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Feature extraction: SIFT or learned features;
\item Matching: identification of potentially overlapping images;
\item Geometric verification and triangulation;
\item Adjustments, to avoid error accumulation and downplay outliers.
\end{compactitem}
\textsc{Colmap} is an open-source implementation, with a series of improvements to increase robustness.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Volatility is rough}\\
  T. Gatheral et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Log-volatility looks like a fractional Brownian motion
\[
m(q, \Delta) = \langle \va{ \log \sigma_{t+\Delta} - \log \sigma_t } ^ q \rangle
\propto \Delta^{qH},
\quad H \approx 0.1
\]
(data from \url{http://realized.oxford-man.ox.ac.uk}).
This explains the power law seen in the skew,
\[
  \left. \dfrac{ \partial \sigma }{ \partial K } \right|_{K=0} \sim \tau^{ - (\frac12-H) }.
\]
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Buy rough, sell smooth}\\
  P. Glasserman and P. He (2018)
\end{book}
Buy stocks whose implied volatility roughness is high.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The market generator}\\
  A. Kondratyev and C. Schwarz
\end{book}
A \emph{restricted Boltzman machine} (RBM) has two layers of binary variables,
visible $v$ and hidden $h$.
\begin{align*}
  E(v,h) &= - a' v - b' h - v' w h \\
  p(v,h) &\propto \exp - E(v,h)  \\
  w &\leftarrow w + \eta \bigl[ \langle v' h \rangle_{\text{data}} - \langle v'h \rangle_{\text{model}} \bigr] \\
  a &\leftarrow a + \eta \bigl[ \langle v \rangle_{\text{data}} - \langle v \rangle_{\text{model}} \bigr] \\
  b &\leftarrow b + \eta \bigl[ \langle h \rangle_{\text{data}} - \langle h \rangle_{\text{model}} \bigr] \\
\end{align*}
To get an unbiased estimate of $\langle v'h \rangle_{\text{model}}$, one could iteratily sample from
$h|v$ and $v|h$, for long enough ($10^3$ times).
\begin{align*}
  p( h_j = 1 | v ) &= \sigma( b_j + w_{ \bigcdot\, j } ' v ) \\
  p( h_i = 1 | h ) &= \sigma( a_i + w_{ i \, \bigcdot } ' h ) 
\end{align*}
Instead, $k$-step \emph{contrastive divergence} (CD) iterates for $k$ steps only:
\begin{align*}
  \Delta w_{ij} &= \eta \bigl[
    p( h_j = 1 | v^{(0)} ) v_i^{(0)} - p( h_j = 1 | v^{(k)} ) v_i^{(k)}
    \bigr] \\
  \Delta a_i &= \eta [ v_i^{(0)} - v_i^{(k)} ] \\
  \Delta b_j &= \eta \bigl[ p( h_j = 1 | v^{(0)} ) - p( h_j = 1 | v^{(k)}) \bigr]
\end{align*}
To deal with real (non-binary) data,
round number in $[x_{\min}, x_{\max}]$ to 16-bit binary numbers.

Experiments on 4 time series of FX returns amd 30 hidden units can reproduce the marginal distributions and the correlation structure.

To generate autocorrelated samples,
initialize the RBM with the previous values $S_{t-1}$
and sample $h$ and $v$ a small number of times ($\ll 10^3$).
To account for non-stationarity (volatility clustering),
add a binary indicator for each input time series,
indicating of the volatility is high or low (3-month vs long-term), and keep them fixed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Handling risk-on/risk-off dynamics with correlation regimes and correlation netwoeks}\\
  J. Papenbrock and P. Schwendner (2015)
\end{book}
Cluster correlation matrices (estimated at different points in time) to identify regimes:
25 assets (equities, bonds, commodities, FX), daily returns, 1-month window,
filtered with average linkage clustering (why not a minimum spanning tree?), $k$-means, $k=5$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep generative models}\\
  A. Soleimany (2020)
\end{book}
VAEs can help increase diversity:
do not use $N(0,1)$ to sample in the latent space but soemthing more dispersed
(larger variance or fatter tails).

CycleGANs have two generators and two discriminators;
they can be applied, not only to images,
but also to sounds (to change the voice from one person to another, via spectrograms).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BANANAS: Bayesian optimization\\ with neural architectures for NAS}\\
  C. White et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Cell-based search spaces for network architecture search (e.g., from NASBench)
assume the network architecture is fixed,
with ``cells'' to be filled in, from a few building blocks;
each cell can be encoded by listing the paths in it
(there is an exponential number of possible paths, but if the length is limited, it remains reasonable).

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (I) at (1,0) {};
  \node (A1) at (0,1) {$1\times1$};
  \node (A2) at (2,1) {$3\times3$};
  \node (A3) at (1,2) {$3\times3$};
  \node (MP1) at (0,2.5) {MP};
  \node (MP2) at (1,3) {MP};
  \node (O) at (1,4) {};
  \node (O2) at (1,3.5) {};
  \draw[->] (I) to[out=90,in=-90] (A1);
  \draw[->] (I) to[out=90,in=-90] (A2);
  \draw[->] (A1) to[out=90,in=-90] (A3);
  \draw[->] (A2) to[out=90,in=-90] (A3);
  \draw[->] (A1) -- (MP1);  
  \draw[->] (A3) -- (MP2);
  \draw[->] (MP1) to[out=90,in=180] (O2);  
  \draw[->] (MP2) -- (O);  
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

For Bayesian optimization, use a neural net
(of an ensemble of neural nets)
instead of a Gaussian process,
to predict (the mean and variance of)
the accuracy of an architecture,
and Thompson sampling.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiplicative interactions\\ and where to find them}\\
  S.M. Jayakumar et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Try replacing concatenation with multiplication -- gating, hypernetworks, dynamic convolutions, attention, etc.
Replace linear maps $(x,z) \mapsto W [x;z] + b$
with bilinear ones $(x,z) \mapsto z' W x + z' U + V x + b$ ($W$ is then a 3-dimensional tensor).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Big Bird: transformers for longer sequences}\\
  M. Zaheer et al. (2020)
\end{book}
The quadratic size of the attention matrix does not allow BERT to scale to large texts.
Instead of a dense matrix, use a sparse one, combining:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Random links: with several layers, random sparse matrices can approximate dense ones;
\item A window, \ie, non-zero elements close to the diagonal;
\item Bidirectional inks between important tokens (e.g., \textsc{cls}) and all other tokens.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bootstrap your own latent:\\ a new approach to self-supervised learning}\\
  J.B. Grill et al. (2020)
\end{book}
SimCLR performs self-supervised learning by combining augmentations and negative samples.
Negative samples are not really needed: use two neural networks,
the trained one (``online'') and an exponentially weighted moving average (``target''),
the online network predicting the latent representation of the target one under a different augmentation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Direct feedback alignment scales to\\ modern deep learning tasks and architectures}\\
  J. Launay et al. (2020)
\end{book}
Given a neural network
\[
\cdots \to h_{i-1}
\overset{W_i}{\longrightarrow} a_i
\overset{f_i}{\longrightarrow} h_i
\overset{W_{i+1}}{\longrightarrow} a_{i+1}
\to \cdots,
%\to h_N = \hat y \to \mathscr L( \hat y , y )
\]
back-propagation computes 
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{ \partial \mathscr L }{ \partial W_i } &=
\dfrac{ \partial \mathscr L }{ \partial a_{i+1} } 
\dfrac{ \partial a_{i+1} }{ \partial h_i } 
\dfrac{ \partial h_u }{ \partial a_i } 
\dfrac{ \partial a_i }{ \partial W_i } \\
&=
\dfrac{ \partial \mathscr L }{ \partial a_{i+1} } 
W_{i+1} f'_i(a_i) W_i.
\end{align*}
Direct feedback alignment (DFA) replaces the first two factors
(since they come from the next layer, they are not biologically plausible,
and they prevent parallelization)
with
\[
\dfrac{ \partial \mathscr L }{ \partial a_{N} } B_i \]
where $B_i$ is a fixed random matrix,
randomly projecting the global error.

DFA works well for fully-connected architectures (not convolutional ones).

%% 2020-10
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep unsupervised learning}\\
  P. Abbeel et al. (2020)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.} Deep unsupervised learning includes
\emph{generative models} which sample from the data manifold,
and \emph{self-supervised learning} (e.g., guessing the angle of a rotated image).
Those models provide one or more of:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Probability density;
\item Sampling;
\item Latent representation.
\end{compactitem}

Applications include (conditional) data generation,
anomaly detection,
compression (WaveOne is better than \textsc{jpeg}2000),
pretraining for text (GPT2, BERT) and vision (CPC, MoCo).

\textbf{2.}
\emph{Likelihood-based models}
allow us to sample $x \sim p$
and to compute probabilities
$p(x)$.
For discrete distributions, we could use a \emph{histogram},
but this does not scale (a $28\times28$ binary image would require $2^{28\times28}$ bins)
and generalizes poorly.
Instead, look for a parametric distribution,
estimated using maximum likelihood or,
equivalently,
\[ \text{KL}( \text{data} \| p_\theta ) = \E_{ x \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ - \log p_\theta(x) \bigr]
- H(\text{data}) . \]
If the probability $p_\theta$ is modeled by a neural net, we need to ensure that
\begin{align*}
  & \forall x \quad p_\theta(x) \in [0,1] \\
  & \sum_x p_\theta(x) = 1
\end{align*}
This can be done with a \emph{Bayesian net},
\[ \log p_\theta(x) = \sum_i \log p_\theta\bigl( x_i | \text{parents}(x_i) \bigr), \]
e.g., an \d{autoregressive model}
\[ \log p_\theta(x) = \sum_i \log p_\theta( x_i | x_{1 : i-1} ) \]
where 
$p(x_1)$ is given by a histogram
and $p(x_i | x_{1:i-1})$ by a neural network with a softmax.
\emph{Recurrent networks} allow some parameter sharing --
they even work for MNIST, but try to add skip connections
$(i-1,j) \to (i,j)$ and/or use positional encoding
\[ (i,j, \text{previous state}) \longmapsto (\text{output}, \text{next state}). \]

\emph{Masking-based models}, such as MADE
(masked autoencoder for distribution estimation)
take a neural net and remove connections (apply a mask)
such that output $i$ only depends on inputs $1:i-1$.

% Masking-based model
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.5,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3}
\foreach \j in {0,1,2,3}
  \node[circle,draw] (A-\i-\j) at (\i,\j) {};
\foreach \j in {0,1,2,3} {
  \draw (A-1-\j) -- (A-2-\j);
  \draw (A-2-\j) -- (A-3-\j);
}
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \ii using int(\i+1)] \i in {0,1,2} {
  \foreach \j in {2,1,0}
    \draw (A-\i-3) -- (A-\ii-\j);
  \foreach \j in {1,0}
    \draw (A-\i-2) -- (A-\ii-\j);
  \draw (A-\i-1) -- (A-\ii-0);
}
\node[left=0pt of A-0-3] {\footnotesize$x_1$};
\node[left=0pt of A-0-2] {\footnotesize$x_2$};
\node[left=0pt of A-0-1] {\footnotesize$x_3$};
\node[left=0pt of A-0-0] {\footnotesize$x_4$};
\node[right=0pt of A-3-3] {\footnotesize$p(x_1)$};
\node[right=0pt of A-3-2] {\footnotesize$p(x2|x_1)$};
\node[right=0pt of A-3-1] {\footnotesize$p(x3|x_{1:2})$};
\node[right=0pt of A-3-0] {\footnotesize$p(x4|x_{1:3})$};
\node at (.5,-.5)  {\footnotesize A};
\node at (1.5,-.5) {\footnotesize B};
\node at (2.5,-.5) {\footnotesize B};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

There are two types of layers, A and B,
containing only connections from $i$ to $j$
where $i<j$ (A) or $i \leq j$ (B);
use at least one layer of type A, anywhere.
The mask depends on the ordering; you can use several orderings (masks)
on the same underlying network (but not too many).

\emph{Causal convolutions} naturally provide the autoregressive structure,
with shared parameters, but they have a limited receptive field.
% Causal convolution, limited receptive field  
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.5,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4}
\foreach \j in {0,1,2,3}
  \node[circle,draw] (A-\i-\j) at (\i,\j) {};

\foreach[evaluate=\j as \jj using int(\j+1)] \j in {0,1,2}
  \foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4}
    \draw[->,gray] (A-\i-\j) -- (A-\i-\jj);

\foreach[evaluate=\i as \ii using int(\i+1)] \i in {0,1,2,3} {
  \foreach[evaluate=\j as \jj using int(\j+1)] \j in {0,1,2} {
    \draw[->,gray] (A-\i-\j) -- (A-\ii-\jj);
  }
}

\draw[->] (A-1-0) -- (A-2-1);  
\draw[->] (A-2-0) -- (A-2-1);  
\draw[->] (A-2-0) -- (A-3-1);  
\draw[->] (A-3-0) -- (A-3-1);  
\draw[->] (A-3-0) -- (A-4-1);  
\draw[->] (A-4-0) -- (A-4-1);
\draw[->] (A-2-1) -- (A-3-2);  
\draw[->] (A-3-1) -- (A-3-2);  
\draw[->] (A-3-1) -- (A-4-2);  
\draw[->] (A-4-1) -- (A-4-2);
\draw[->] (A-3-2) -- (A-4-3);  
\draw[->] (A-4-2) -- (A-4-3);
\node[left=0pt of A-0-3] {\footnotesize output};
\node[left=0pt of A-0-0] {\footnotesize input};
\node[above=0pt of A-4-3] {\footnotesize \rlap{\hspace*{-1mm}$p( x_i | x_{<i} )$}};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\emph{WaveNet} uses dilated convolutions, gated residual blocks and skip connections.
% Dilated convolution
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.5,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\foreach \i in {0,...,7}
\foreach \j in {0,1,2,3}
  \node[circle,draw] (A-\i-\j) at (\i,\j) {};
\draw (A-0-0) -- (A-1-1);
\draw (A-1-0) -- (A-1-1);
\draw (A-2-0) -- (A-3-1);
\draw (A-3-0) -- (A-3-1);
\draw (A-4-0) -- (A-5-1);
\draw (A-5-0) -- (A-5-1);
\draw (A-6-0) -- (A-7-1);
\draw (A-7-0) -- (A-7-1);

\draw (A-1-1) -- (A-3-2);
\draw (A-3-1) -- (A-3-2);
\draw (A-5-1) -- (A-7-2);
\draw (A-7-1) -- (A-7-2);

\draw (A-3-2) -- (A-7-3);
\draw (A-7-2) -- (A-7-3);

\end{tikzpicture}
\]

% Gated residual block
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.7,yscale=.6,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
\node[draw] (DC)   at (0,.7) {\footnotesize dilated convolution\strut};
\node[draw] (th)   at (-1,2) {\footnotesize tanh\strut};
\node[draw] (si)   at (+1,2) {\footnotesize $\sigma$\strut};
\node[draw] (conv) at (0,4) {\footnotesize $1\times1$\strut};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (t) at (0,3) {\footnotesize $\times$};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (p) at (0,5) {\footnotesize $+$};
\draw[->] (0,-.5) -- (DC);
\draw[->] (DC.north) -| (th);
\draw[->] (DC.north) -| (si);
\draw[->] (th.north) -- (t);
\draw[->] (si.north) -- (t);
\draw[->] (t) -- (conv);
\draw[->] (conv) -- (p);
\draw[->] (p) -- (0,5.7);
\draw[->] (0,-.1) -- (-2.5,-.1) |- (p);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

A 2-dimensional generalization of WaveNet would have a blind spot.

% 2-dimensional wavenet
\vspace{-4ex}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.3,yscale=.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \ii using int(\i+1)] \i in {0,1,2} {
  \foreach[evaluate=\j as \jj using int(\j+1)] \j in {0,1,2} {
    \draw (\i,\j) -- (\ii,\j) -- (\ii,\jj) -- (\i,\jj) -- cycle;
  }
}
\node[circle,fill] (A) at (1.5,1.5) {};
\draw[->,thick] (.4,1.5) -- (A);
\draw[->,thick] (.4,2.6) -- (A);
\draw[->,thick] (1.5,2.6) -- (A);
\draw[->,thick] (2.6,2.6) -- (A);
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad
\raisebox{-2em}{%
<<WaveNet2DBlindSpot,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=3,fig.height=3,out.width='.3\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
n <- 8
par( mar = .1 * c(1,1,1,1) )
plot( NA, xlim = c(0,n), ylim = c(0,n), axes = FALSE, xlab="", ylab="" )
segments(rep(0,n+1), 0:n, rep(n,n+1), 0:n)
segments(0:n, rep(0,n+1), 0:n, rep(n,n+1))
i0 <- 3
j0 <- 2
for( i in 1:n ) {
  for( j in 1:n ) {
    if( j < j0 || ( j == j0 && i >= i0 ) ) next
    k <- max( abs(i-i0), abs(j-j0) )
    col <- colorRampPalette(c("black", "white"), space="Lab")(7)[k]
    col <- c("grey30", "grey40", "grey50", "grey60", "grey70", "grey80")[k]
    if( j > j0 && i > i0 && j-j0 < i-i0 )
      col <- "pink"
    polygon( c(i,i,i-1,i-1), c(j,j-1,j-1,j), col = col )
  }
}
polygon( c(i0,i0,i0-1,i0-1), c(j0,j0-1,j0-1,j0), lwd = 10 )
@
}
\]

\vspace{-3ex}
\emph{PixelCNN} uses filters of the form
\(
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.3,yscale=.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \ii using int(\i+1)] \i in {0,1,2} {
  \foreach[evaluate=\j as \jj using int(\j+1)] \j in {0,1} {
    \draw (\i,\j) -- (\ii,\j) -- (\ii,\jj) -- (\i,\jj) -- cycle;
  }
}
\node[circle,fill] (A) at (1.5,.5) {};
\draw[->,thick] (.4,.5) -- (A);
\draw[->,thick] (.4,1.6) -- (A);
\draw[->,thick] (1.5,1.6) -- (A);
\draw[->,thick] (2.6,1.6) -- (A);
\draw[->,thick] (2.6,.5) -- (A);
\end{tikzpicture}
\)
but only above the pixel of interest (``vertical stack''),

\vspace{-4ex}
\[
<<WaveNet2DBlindSpot2,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=1.9,fig.height=1.9,out.width='.1875\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
n <- 5
par( mar = .1 * c(1,1,1,1) )
plot( NA, xlim = c(0,n), ylim = c(0,n), axes = FALSE, xlab="", ylab="" )
i0 <- 3
j0 <- 2
for( i in 1:n ) {
  for( j in 1:n ) {
    if( j < j0 ) next
    k <- max( abs(i-i0), abs(j-j0) )
    col <- colorRampPalette(c("black", "white"), space="Lab")(7)[k+1]
    col <- c("grey30", "grey50", "grey70", "grey90")[k+1]
    polygon( c(i,i,i-1,i-1), c(j,j-1,j-1,j), col = col )
  }
}
polygon( c(i0-1,i0-1,0,0), c(j0-1,j0-2,j0-2,j0-1), col = "pink" )
polygon( c(i0,i0,i0-1,i0-1), c(j0,j0-1,j0-1,j0)-1, lwd = 10 )
segments(rep(0,n+1), 0:n, rep(n,n+1), 0:n)
segments(0:n, rep(0,n+1), 0:n, rep(n,n+1))
@
\]

\vspace{-4ex}
combined with a 1-dimensional network to address the blindspot
on the left (``horizontal stack'');
the vertical stack is fed to the horizontal stack.

Using a multinomial distribution (softmax) for pixel values is suboptimal:
similar pixel values have similar probabilities.
PixelCNN++ uses a mixture of logistic distributions (the cdf is a sigmoid),
with downsampling and (UNet) skip connections.

Convolutions have a limited receptive field: PixelSNAIL uses CNNs and self-attention,
\[ A(q,k,V) = \sum_i \dfrac{
  e^{ q \cdot k_i }
}{
  \sum\limits_j e^{ q \cdot k_j }
} v_i
\]
(if the query $q$ is close to the 1-hot vector for coordinate $i$,
the output is close to $v_i$)
with masking; different orders are possible.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.3,yscale=.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \ii using int(\i+1)] \i in {0,...,4} {
  \foreach[evaluate=\j as \jj using int(\j+1)] \j in {0,...,4} {
    \draw[gray] (\i,\j) -- (\ii,\j) -- (\ii,\jj) -- (\i,\jj) -- cycle;
  }
}
\foreach \i in {1,...,5}
  \foreach \j in {1,...,5}
    \node[circle,fill] (A-\i-\j) at (\i-.5,\j-.5) {};
\draw[thick] (A-1-5) -- (A-5-5) --
      (A-1-4) -- (A-5-4) --
      (A-1-3) -- (A-5-3) --
      (A-1-2) -- (A-5-2) --
      (A-1-1) -- (A-5-1);
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.3,yscale=.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \ii using int(\i+1)] \i in {0,...,4} {
  \foreach[evaluate=\j as \jj using int(\j+1)] \j in {0,...,4} {
    \draw[gray] (\i,\j) -- (\ii,\j) -- (\ii,\jj) -- (\i,\jj) -- cycle;
  }
}
\foreach \i in {1,...,5}
  \foreach \j in {1,...,5}
    \node[circle,fill] (A-\i-\j) at (\i-.5,\j-.5) {};
\draw[thick]
      (A-1-5) -- (A-2-5) --
      (A-1-4) -- (A-3-5) --
      (A-1-3) -- (A-4-5) --
      (A-1-2) -- (A-5-5) --
      (A-1-1) -- (A-5-4) --
      (A-2-1) -- (A-5-3) --
      (A-3-1) -- (A-5-2) --
      (A-4-1) -- (A-5-1);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Extensions include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Conditional PixelCNN;
\item Hierarchical AR image models, which progressively increase the resolution;
\item Generating a greyscale image first, before adding colour.
\end{compactitem}

AR models are very slow: they generate one pixel at a time.
The can be sped up by breaking the AR pattern
(\emph{scaling AR video models}).
