\]

\emph{DCGAN} (deep convolutional GAN) uses mostly convolutions;
it allows for vector arithmetic (pose, smile, glasses, gender).
When interpolating using a GAN, do not use a straight line (it would go close to the origin, which is a muddy image), but a half-circle away from the origin.

\emph{Improved training of GANs} introduced a few ideas:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Feature matching: for some feature $f$, add
  \[ \n{ \E_{ x \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ f(x) \bigr] - \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } \bigl[ f(Gz) ] }^2 \]
  to the loss;
\item Minibatch discrimination: feed minibatches, rather than individual images, to the discriminator,
  to spot mode collapse
  (of just add $\exp - \n{f x_i - f x_j }_1$ to the loss, or to the input, for some feature $f$);
\item Historical averaging: regularize by adding
  $\n{ \theta - \tfrac1T \sum _t \theta_i }^2$
  to the loss (cf. Cesaro (usually at text time only), TRPO);
\item  One-sided label smoothing (only for the real data);
\item Virtual batch normalization: natch-norm procudes correlated samples: compute the batch statistics using a virtual batch made of the current observation and a fixed reference batch;
\item  Semi-supervised learning: have the discriminator predict labels, if available, instead of just real vs fake;
\item Inception score.
\end{compactitem}

The \emph{Wasserstein GAN} (WGAN) replaces the JSD with the Wasserstein distance
\begin{align*}
  W( p_{\text{real}}, p_{\text{gen}})
  &= \inf_{ \gamma \in \Pi(p_r, p_g) }
  \E_{ (x,y) \sim \gamma } \n{x-y} \\
  &= \sum_{ \n f _L \leq 1 }
  \E_{ x \sim p_r } \bigl[ f(X) \bigr] - 
  \E_{ x \sim p_g } \bigl[ f(X) \bigr].
\end{align*}
To enforce the Lipschitz condition, just clip the weights.
There is no classifier: the Lipschits function is just a ``critic''.
The gradient is more meaningful, and the model more robust
(no need for all those tricks).

WGAN-GP replaces the weight clipping with a gradient penalty
$ \bigl( \n{ \nabla_{\!x} D(x) }_2 - 1 \bigr)^2$,
evaluated at a rendom point between a real and a fake sample;
there is no batch-norm in the discriminator.

ProGAN progressively increases the resolution.

SNGAN (spectral normalization) uses a \emph{hinge loss}

\begin{align*}
  L_D &= \E_{ x \sim \text{data} } ( Dx - 1 ) _- + \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } ( - DGz - 1 )_- \\
  L_G &= - \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } [ DGz ],
\end{align*}
\emph{conditional batchnorm},
and enforces the Lipschitz condition using \emph{spectral normalization}
of the weight matrices,
$W \leftarrow W / \sigma(W)$;
the spectral norm (largest singular value)
can be approximated with power iteration (one iteration is enough).

The discriminator can use labels in different ways:
\[
