\]

SAGAN uses \emph{self-attention}, spectral normalization (in both discriminator and generator)
and hinge loss.

BigGAN scales everythingm adds \emph{orthogonal regularization}
$\n{W' W - I}_F^2$, but only for the off-diagonal elements
$\n{W'W \odot (\mathbf 1 - I ) }_F^2$, use the \emph{truncation trick}
(truncate the Gaussian $N(0,I)$ at test time to $[-c,c]$)
and ``standing statistics'' (?) for batch-norm.

StyleGAN uses the noise in a different way:
the generator starts with a constant input and progressively upscales it, adding noise at each step;
there are two types of noises:
the style noise, preprocessed by another network,
is incorporated at each step;
additional Gaussian noise,
different at each step, is also added.
As with style transfer,
StyleGAN uses (adaptive) \emph{instance normalization}.

StyleGAN2 addresses some artefacts in StyleGAN
(e.g., water-droplet-like artefacts, very visible in the activations, but less so in the final images)
and can compute the latent representation of an image.

The \emph{variational information bottleneck} (VIB)
add another encoder before the discriminator,
% VIB
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=.2,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (G) at (0,1) {\footnotesize generator};
  \node (R) at (0,-1) {\footnotesize data};
  \node[draw] (E) at (1,0) {\footnotesize\strut Encoder};
  \node[draw] (D) at (2.1,0) {\footnotesize\strut Discriminator};
  \node (O) at (3.2,0) {\footnotesize fake or real};
  \draw[->] (G) -| (.4,0) -- (E);
  \draw[->] (R) -| (.4,0) -- node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize $x$} (E);
  \draw[->] (E) -- node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize $z$} (D);
  \draw[->] (D) -- (O);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
to reduce the information it receives, with a constraint on the mutual information
$I(X;Z) \leq I_c$ or a variational bound on the mutual information
\[ \E_x \bigl[
  \text{KL} \bigl( p(z|x) \bigm\| r(z) \bigr)
\bigr] \leq I_c. \]

Pix2Pix is a \emph{conditional GAN} (cGAN) used, e.g., to colour black-and-white images,
in which the discriminator takes pairs of images to ensure the output is related to the input;
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.7,yscale=.2,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (I) at (-.1,1) {\footnotesize B\&W image};
  \node (N) at (-.1,-1) {\footnotesize noise};
  \node[draw] (G) at (1,0) {\footnotesize generator};
  \node[align=center] (C) at (1.95,.95) {\footnotesize color \\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize image};
  \node[draw] (D) at (3,2) {\footnotesize discriminator};
  \node[align=center] (O) at (4,2) {\footnotesize real \\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize or \\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize  fake};
  \draw[->] (I.east) -- (G);
  \draw[->] (N.east) -- (G);
  \draw[->] (G) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (D);
  \draw[->] (I) to[bend left=45] (D);
  \draw[->] (D) -- (O);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

it also uses an autoencoder loss
$\text{B\&W} \to \text{colour} \to \text{B\&W}$.
To reduce the capacity of the discriminator,
have it work on patches.
Examples include
colouring images,
turning contours into images,
sharpening,
turning sketches into photorealistic images (GauGAN),
controlling drawing software (learning-to-paint),
video (video2video:
pose detection to change the person moving or dancing, deep fake
adding frames, increasing resolution, etc.)

GANs can also be used for \emph{representation learning}.
The \emph{InfoGAN} generator takes as input a code $c$ and noise $z$ to generate an image $x$;
to ensure that the code is actually used,
maximize the mutual information $I(c,x)$ or a variational lower bound.
\begin{align*}
  I(c;x) &= I( c ; G(z,c)) \\
  &= H(x) - H(c | G(z,c)) \\
  &= H(c) + \E_{ x \sim G(z,c) } \E_{ c' \sim p(c' | x) } \log p(c' | x ) \\
  &\geq H(c) + \E_{ x \sim G(z,c) } \E_{ c' \sim p(c' | x) } \log q(c' | x )
\end{align*}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (R) at (0,2) {\footnotesize real};
  \node (N) at (0,1) {\footnotesize noise};
  \node (C) at (0,0) {\footnotesize code};
  \node[draw,inner sep = 2pt] (G) at (1,.5) {\footnotesize $G$};
  \node[draw,inner sep = 2pt] (q) at (2,0) {\footnotesize $q$};
  \node[draw,inner sep = 2pt] (D) at (2,2) {\footnotesize $D$};
  \node[align=center] (O1) at (3,2) {\footnotesize real \\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize or \\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize  fake};
  \node (O2) at (3,0) {\footnotesize code};
  \draw[->] (R) -- (D);
  \draw[->] (N) -- (G);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (G);
  \draw[->] (G) -- (D);
  \draw[->] (D) -- (O1);
  \draw[->] (G) -- (q);
  \draw[->] (q) -- (O2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

It can also be used for unsupervised category discovery (with a discrete code).

BiGAN (bidirectional GAN) and BigBiGAN learn ``generators'' in both direction,
between image and latent representation;
the discriminator learns to distinguish
encoder pairs $(x,\hat z)$ from generator pairs $(\hat x, z)$.
% BiGAN
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
