  \node[draw,minimum width=1ex,minimum height=1ex] (A) at (1,2) {};
  \node[draw,minimum width=1ex,minimum height=1ex] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[draw,minimum width=1ex,minimum height=1ex] (C) at (2,1) {};
  \node[circle,draw,inner sep=-.5pt] (p) at (3.15,1) {\footnotesize $+$};
  \node[anchor=west] (L) at (4,1) {\footnotesize loss};
  \draw[->] (x) -- (A);
  \draw[->] (A) -| (C);
  \draw[->] (A) -| (p);
  \draw[->] (z) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (B) -| (C);
  \draw[->] (B) -| (p);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (p);  
  \draw[->] (p) -- (L);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\emph{Energy-based models}
\[ p(x) = \dfrac1Z e^{ - E(x) } \]
are estimated by maximizing
\[
\E_{ x \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ \log p_\theta(x) \bigr]
 = \E \bigl[ - E_\theta(x) \bigr]- \log Z(\theta).
\]
The partition function $Z$ can be replaced by a variational lower bound.
\begin{align*}
  \log Z &= \log \sum_x e^{ - E_\theta(x) } \\
  &= \log \sum_x \dfrac{ q(x) }{ q(x) } e^{- E_\theta(x) } \\
  &= \log \E_{ x \sim q } \biggl[ \dfrac{ e^{ -E_\theta(x) }}{ q(x) } \biggr] \\
  &\geq \max_\phi \E_{ x \sim q_\phi } \biggl[ \log \dfrac{ e^{-E_\theta(x)} }{ q_\phi(x) } \biggr] \\
  &\geq \max_\phi \E_{ x \sim q_\phi } [ - E_\theta ] + H[ q_\phi].
\end{align*}
The objective is then an \emph{entropy-regularized GAN}
(the entropy helps prevent mode collapse).
\[
\max_\theta \min_\phi
\E_{ x \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ - E_\theta(x) \bigr] +
\E_{ x \sim q_\phi } \bigl[ E_\theta(x) \bigr] - H(q_\phi)
\]
The entropy is usually difficult to compute (except for some models, e.g., PixelCNN).

The WGAN optimizes the dual;
\emph{optimal transport} GAN directly optimized the primal,
e.g., using \emph{implicit MLE}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Generate $n$ samples from $p_\theta$;
\item Pick $n$ real images;
\item For each real image, pick the ``closest'' generated image, form some measure of distance
  (since each real image is matched, we avoid mode collapse);
\item Move $\theta$ to make them closer.
\end{compactitem}

Adversarial losses also have applications in \emph{transfer learning},
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node[align=center, anchor=east] (LI) at (.3,2) {\footnotesize labeled images \\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize (simulated)};
  \node[align=center, anchor=east] (UI) at (.3,0) {\footnotesize unlabeled images \\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize (real)};
  \node[minimum width = 2ex, minimum height = 2ex, draw] (A) at (1,2) {};
  \node[minimum width = 2ex, minimum height = 2ex, draw] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[minimum width = 2ex, minimum height = 2ex, draw] (C) at (2,2) {};
  \node[anchor=west] (AL) at (2,1) {\footnotesize adversarial loss};
  \node[anchor=west] (CL) at (2.7,2) {\footnotesize classification loss};
  \draw[->] (LI) -- (A);
  \draw[->] ([yshift=-5pt]A.east) -| (1.5,1) -- (AL);
  \draw[->] (B) -| (1.5,1) -- (AL);
  \draw[->] (UI) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (CL);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
fairness,
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.25,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (D) at (-.1,0) {\footnotesize data};
  \node[minimum width = 2ex, minimum height = 2ex, draw] (A) at (1,0) {};
  \node[minimum width = 2ex, minimum height = 2ex, draw] (B) at (2,1) {};
  \node[minimum width = 2ex, minimum height = 2ex, draw] (C) at (2,-1) {};
  \node[anchor=west] (O1) at (2.8,1)  {\footnotesize quantity of interest};
  \node[anchor=west] (O2) at (2.8,-1) {\footnotesize sensitive attributes};
  \draw[->] (D) -- (A);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (O1);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (O2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\[
\operatorname*{Maximize}_\theta \text{Loss}(\theta) \text{ such that } \text{Loss}_{\text{sensitive}} (\theta) \leq K 
\]
or imitation learning (GAIL)
\[
\E_\pi \bigl[ \log D(s,a) \bigr] + \E_{ \pi_{\text{expert}} } \bigl[ \log( 1 - D(s,a) ) \bigr] - \lambda H(\pi),
\]
which works better with a variational information bottleneck (VAIL).

\textbf{7.}
\d{Self-supervised learning} is a form of unsupervised learning which holds part of the data and
tasks a neural network to recover it.

\emph{Denoising autoencoders} add different types of noise
(Gaussian, zero, zero-or-one);
they make the output constant in directions orthogonal to the data manifold.
They can be stacked.

\emph{Impainting} removes the central region, a random block,
or an element (segmentation mask);
use a GAN loss in addition to the reconstruction loss to avoid blurry images.

The \emph{split-brain autoencoder} predicts one view of an image from another
(e.g., colour from greyscale, $L \mapsto (a,b)$);
it works better with quantized colours (pixelwise classification problem).
% Split-brain autoencoder
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node[draw, minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (X) at (.2,0) {\footnotesize $x$};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (A1) at (1.2,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1a) at (2,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1b) at (2.25,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1c) at (2.5,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1d) at (2.75,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1e) at (3,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (C1) at (3.8,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (H) at (4.8,0) {\footnotesize $\hat x$};

  \node[draw, minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (A2) at (1.2,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2a) at (2,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2b) at (2.25,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2c) at (2.5,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2d) at (2.75,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2e) at (3,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (C2) at (3.8,-1) {};

  \draw[->] (X) -- (A1);
  \draw[->] (X) -- (A2);
  \draw[->] (A1) -- (B1a);
  \draw[->] (A2) -- (B2a);
  \draw[->] (B1e) -- (C1);
  \draw[->] (B2e) -- (C2);
  \draw[->] (C1) -- (H);
  \draw[->] (C2) -- (H);

  \begin{scope}[outer sep = 0pt, line join=bevel]
    \node[minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (A1) at (1.2,1) {};
    \node[minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (C2) at (3.8,-1) {};
    \draw[fill=red!20] (A1.north west) -- (A1.north east) -- (A1.south west) -- cycle;
    \draw[fill=red!20] (C2.north west) -- (C2.north east) -- (C2.south west) -- cycle;
    
    \node[minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (A2) at (1.2,-1) {};
    \node[minimum width = 1em, minimum height = 1em] (C1) at (3.8,1) {};
    \draw[fill=blue!20] (A2.south west) -- (A2.south east) -- (A2.north east) -- cycle;
    \draw[fill=blue!20] (C1.south west) -- (C1.south east) -- (C1.north east) -- cycle;
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

One can try to predict the relative position of two patches (include gaps,
jitter the patches; chromatic aberration can be a problem: it points to the center of the image),
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node[draw, minimum width = 3cm, minimum height = 3cm] at (0,0) {};

  % Center
  \node[draw, minimum width = 7mm, minimum height = 7mm, very thick, red!50!black] at (0,0) {};

  \begin{scope}[thick, blue!50!black]
  % Top row
  \node[draw, minimum width = 5mm, minimum height = 7mm] at (-.8,.9) {};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 7mm, minimum height = 4mm] at (-.05,1.2) {};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 5mm, minimum height = 9mm] at (1,1) {};

  % Middle row
  \node[draw, minimum width = 6mm, minimum height = 5mm] at (-1.1,0.05) {};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 4mm, minimum height = 4mm] at (1.2,0.1) {};

  % Bottom row
  \node[draw, minimum width = 5mm, minimum height = 5mm] at (-.8,-.8) {};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 7mm, minimum height = 5mm] at (0,-.8) {};
  \node[draw, minimum width = 5mm, minimum height = 8mm] at (.8,-1) {};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
or reorder them , as a jigsaw problem,
or predict by how much an image was rotated (4 rotations work better than more).

Word embeddings predict the center of a window (CBOW) or the context of a word (skipgram, with negative sampling).

\emph{contrastive predictive coding} (CPC) looks for an encoder $E$ such that the mutual information
$I( E x_1 ; E x_2 )$ be high if $x_1$ and $x_2$ are patches from the same image,
and low otherwise.
Applications include sound, iamges. language, reinforcement learning;
data augmentation is important.

Even if you have data,
unsupervised pretraining followed by supervised fine-tuning
outperforms supervised training.

\emph{Instance discrimination} looks for a latent representation
in which augmented versions (cropped, flipped, etc.)
of the same image are close and different images are far apart:
MoCo (keep a buffer of negative examples), % NOT CLEAR
SimCLR, MoCo-v2.

\textbf{8.}
Use AR models if you need a density -- but sampling is slow.

Use VAEs if you need both a latent representation and samples.

Use GANs if you want good quality samples (sharp images), fast-training,
or image-to-image translation.

\textbf{9a.}
Supervised learning solves
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_\theta \E_{ (x,y) \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ \log p_\theta( y | x ) \bigr]. \]
\d{Semi-supervised learning} leverages unlabeled data.

\emph{Entropy minimization} ensures the classifier is confident on unlabeled data.
\emph{Pseudo-labels} are confident predictions, on the unlabeled data,
progressively added to the training data.

\d{Label consistency} asks that predictions be similar for different augmentations of any given input, e.g., with the \emph{$\pi$-model}
% pi-model: label consistency after data augmentation
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.9,yscale=.5,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (y) at (0,2) {\footnotesize $y$};  
  \node (x) at (0,0) {\footnotesize $x$};
  \node[draw,inner sep=2pt] (DA) at (1,0) {\footnotesize DA};  % Data augmentation
  
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1a) at (2,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1b) at (2.25,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1c) at (2.5,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1d) at (2.75,1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B1e) at (3,1) {};

  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2a) at (2,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2b) at (2.25,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2c) at (2.5,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2d) at (2.75,-1) {};
  \node[draw, minimum height = 1em, minimum width = .4em] (B2e) at (3,-1) {};

  \node[draw,inner sep=2pt,minimum width = 2.7cm] (CE) at (5.3,2) {\footnotesize cross-entropy};
  \node[draw,inner sep=2pt,minimum width = 2.7cm] (SD) at (5.3,0) {\footnotesize squared difference};
  \node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (p) at (7.3,1) {\footnotesize +};
  \node (w) at (7.8,2) {\footnotesize $w$};
  \node (L) at (8.3,1) {\footnotesize loss};
  \draw[->] (y) -- (CE);
  \draw[->] (CE) -| (p);
  \draw[->] (w) -- (p);
  \draw[->] (p) -- (L);
  \draw[->] (x) -- (DA);
  \draw[->] (DA) -- (B1a);
  \draw[->] (DA) -- (B2a);
  \draw[->] (B1e) -- (CE.south west);
  \draw[->] (B1e) -- (SD.north west);
  \draw[->] (B2e) -- (SD.south west);
  \draw[->] (SD) -| (p);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

or \emph{temporal ensembling}
(the squared difference is with a moving average of past latent representations of the input)
or \emph{mean teacher}
(instead of keeping a moving average for each input, just keep a moving average of the weights,
and compare the latent representations computed with the current and average weights).

An adversarial example $x+r$ can be found as
$r = \epsilon g / \n g$, where $g = \nabla_{\!x} \log p(y|x; \hat \theta)$, or
\[ r = \argmax_{ \n r \leq \epsilon } \text{KL} \bigl(
  p(\cdot | x ; \hat \theta) \bigm\| p(\cdot | x + r ; \hat \theta)
\bigr) \]
(linearize the Kullback-Leibler divergence and use power iteration).

\emph{Virtual adversarial training} (VAT) makes the forecasts for $x$ and $x+r$, for unlabeled data, close;
it performs best.

\emph{Unsupervised data augmentation} (UDA)
(back-translation, image augmentation)
improves performance more than random perturbations.
\emph{Training signal annealing} (TSA)
helps prevent overfitting on labeled data
by masking out high-confidence samples
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\theta \operatorname*{Mean}_{x,y} - \log p_\theta( y | x )
\mathbf 1_{ p_\theta(y|x)  \leq \eta_t }. \]

\emph{MixUp} creates new, synthetic observations by combining existing ones.
\begin{align*}
  \lambda &\sim \text{Beta}(\alpha, \alpha) \\
  \lambda ' &= \max( \lambda, \lambda' ) \\
  x' &= \lambda ' x_1 + (1-\lambda') x_2
\end{align*}
\emph{MixMatch} uses MixUp to mix labeled and unlabeled observations,
computes the labels by running the classifier on several augmentations of the input,
averaging the outputs,
and shapening (temperature) the average.

The \emph{noisy student} trains progressively larger models:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Train a teacher models;
\item Train a student model on the original data, and teacher forecasts for unlabeled data, with both input noise (data augmentation) and model noise (dropout, stochastic depth);
\item Iterate with a larger student.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{9b.} 
\d{Unsupervised distribution alignment} tries to find a mapping betwwen two domains, $A$ and $B$,
either as conditional probabilities, $p(a|b)$ and $p(b|a)$, or as maps
$A \leftrightarrows B$,
using only unpaired data from $A$ and $B$.
Examples include daylight and night-time images, black-and-white and colour images, segmentation mask and photograph, English and French sentences, image and caption, painting and photograph, etc.

\d{CycleGAN} combines \emph{marginal matching},
which approximates the marginals
$p(a|b) \approx q_\theta(a|b)$, $p(b|a) \approx q_\theta(b|a)$
such that
\begin{align*}
  q(b) &= \E_{ a \sim p } \bigl[ q(b|a) \bigr] \approx p(b) \\
  q(a) &= \E_{ b \sim p } \bigl[ q(a|b) \bigr] \approx p(a)
\end{align*}
and \emph{cycle consistency}.
It can cheat the cycle consistency condition by hiding information
(steganography).

\d{DualGAN} uses a stochastic mapping
$G_{AB} : A \times X \to B$ instead of $A \to B$,
but the cycle consistency
$G{)BA} \bigl( G_{AB} (a,z) , z' \bigr) = a$
makes it ignore $z$ and $z'$.
Instead, the \emph{augmented CycleGAN} learns mappings between $A \times Z$ and $B \times Z$.

\emph{Word2vec} embedings for dfferent languages can be aligned with a rotation:
approximate marginal matching with adversarial training followed by a refinement of the rotation by
solving the exact alignment problem
for the top pairs.

\emph{Unsupervised machine translation} combines
word-level alignment,
monolingual models (marginal matching),
back-translation (cycle consistency).

\textbf{10. Compression.}
The \emph{Shannon entropy} is the number of bits needed to transmit information,
\[ H(X) = \sum_i p(x_i) \log \dfrac1{ p(x_i) }. \]

For any uniquely decodable code $C$,
\[ \sum_{ (s,w) \in C } 2^{ - \ell(w) } \leq 1 \]
(Kraft-McMillan inequality);
conversely, for any lengths satisfying this ineqaulity, there exists a \emph{prefix} code.

The length of a code is at least the entropy,
$H(X) \leq \langle \ell \rangle$;
there exists a code with average length $\langle \ell \rangle \leq H(X) + 1 $.
\emph{Huffman coding} (a trie, built bottom up)
has length $\ell_i = \lceil - \operatorname{lg} p(x_i) \rceil$;
it is optimal.

If we only have an approximation $\hat p$ of $p$, the average length is
$\text{KL}(\hat p \| p ) + H(p)$.
If the entropy is too high, try conditioning:
$H(X|C) \leq H(X)$, e.g., with AR models.
The ``$+1$'' in $\langle \ell \rangle \leq H(X) + 1$ matters if $H(X)$ is small:
use larger chunks.
The entropy of the English language is around 1 bit per character.

\d{Arithmetic coding} encodes a message as a subinterval of $[0,1]$:
for each new character, split the current interval into subintervals
of sizes proportional to the probabilities of each symbol,
and pick that curresponding to the new character.
To send the final interval, use any number in it.

\d{Asymmetric numeral systems} are an alternative to arithmetic coding
using integers instead of intervals:
partition $\NN$ into subsets $S_a$, $S_b$, etc., one for each symbol,
such that ``$S_a$ contains a proportion $p(a)$ of integers'',
and progressively update a state $s \in \NN$, starting at $s=0$,
by setting $\text{enc}(s,a)$ to the $s$th element of $S_a$.

High-dimensional (discretized) continuous data can be modeled
as a mixture of simple distributions, $p(x) = \sum_i p(i) p(x|i)$, using
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Max-mode coding: send $i = \argmax p(i|x)$ encoded with $p$,
  then $x$ encoded with $p(\cdot|i)$;
  it costs $H(X) + \text{KL}( \text{true} \| \text{mixture} )$;
\item Posterior sampling: use $i$ sampled from $p(\cdot | x)$ instead;
\item \d{Bits-back coding}.
\end{compactitem}

The \d{Lempel-Ziv} (LZ) compression algorithm looks,
in the text seen so far,
for the longest match to the string starting at the current position,
and outputs its location and length.

% NLP
\textbf{11. NLP.}
The \emph{perplexity} of a language model is the average number of possible next words, \ie,
the branching factor -- it is between 5 and 10 for free speech,
and more constrained, 3 or 4, for translation.

Here are some milestones in the history of language models:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item GloVe, a factorization of the pointwise mutual information matrix,
\[ \text{PMI}(x,y) = \log \dfrac{ p(x,y) }{ p(x) p(y) }, \]
obtained from the co-occurrence matrix;
\item Word2vec, another vector embedding, predicting a word from its context (CBOW, continuous bag of words) or the opposite (skipgram);
\item Byte pair encoding (BPE, usually up to 32,000 tokens);
\item $n$-gram models with smoothing;
\item NPLM, an $n$-gram model, with vector embeddings and a neural net;
\item Skip-thought, skipgram with sentences instead of words (contrary to the word embeddings,
the sentence embeddings are not model parameters but are computed from the word embeddings);
\item ELMO, contextual word representations from RNNs;
\item GPT, contextual word representations with a transformer-based generative model;
\item BERT (masking), RoBERTa;
\item Electra: BERT-like model (masking) with a discriminator to distinguish real from generated sentences;
\item T5, which combines all of the above.
\end{compactitem}

