or split the parameters into task-specific and shared (or soft-shared: $\lambda \sum \n{ \theta_t - \theta_{t'} }$).

There can be \emph{negative transfer} -- independent networks may work better.

\textbf{3.} There are three types of metalearning algorithms: black-box, optimization-based and non-parametric.

In black-box algorithms, a first network processes the training data and outputs parameters for the second network, which performs the task.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.7,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node at (-1,1) {\footnotesize LSTM:};
  \node at (1,1.5) {\footnotesize $f_\theta$};
  \node[draw, inner sep=2pt, minimum width=3mm] (N1) at (0,1) {};
  \node[draw, inner sep=2pt, minimum width=3mm] (N2) at (1,1) {};
  \node[draw, inner sep=2pt, minimum width=3mm] (N3) at (2,1) {};
  \node (x1) at (0,0) {\footnotesize $(x_1,y_1)$};
  \node (x2) at (1,0) {\footnotesize $(x_2,y_2)$};
  \node (x3) at (2,0) {\footnotesize $(x_3,y_3)$};
  \node (f) at (3,1) {\footnotesize $\phi_i$};
  \draw[->] (x1) -- (N1);
  \draw[->] (x2) -- (N2);
  \draw[->] (x3) -- (N3);
  \draw[->] (N1) -- (N2);
  \draw[->] (N2) -- (N3);
  \draw[->] (N3) -- (f);
  \node (x) at (4,0) {\footnotesize $x$};
  \node[fill=blue!30] (g) at (4,1) {\footnotesize $g_{\phi_i}$};
  \node (y) at (4,2) {\footnotesize $y$};
  \draw[->] (x) -- (g);
  \draw[->] (g) -- (y);
  \node at (1,-.8) {\footnotesize $\mathscr D_i$};
  \node at (4,-.8) {\footnotesize $\mathscr D_i^{\text{test}}$};
  \draw [decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=5pt},yshift=-5pt] (-.5,0) -- (2.5,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Optimization-based algorithms, e.g., \d{MAML} (model-agnostic meta-learning) learn parameters $\theta$ such hat starting the optimization at $\theta$
gives the best performance on the new tasks.
\[
  \operatorname*{Minimize}_\theta
  \sum_{\text{task } i }
  \text{Loss} \bigl(
    \theta - \alpha \nabla_{\!\theta} \text{Loss}(\theta, \mathscr D_i^{\text{train}} ) ,
    \mathscr D_i^{\text{test}}
  \bigr)
\]

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \draw[thick,->] plot [smooth, tension=1] coordinates { (0,0) (1,1) (2.5,0)};
  \node[outer sep=0pt] (B) at (2.5,0) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (f1) at (3,.1) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (f2) at (2.7,-.5) {};
  \node[circle,fill] (f3) at (2.3,-.5) {};
  \draw[->, thick, blue, densely dotted] (B) -- (f1);
  \draw[->, thick, blue, densely dotted] (B) -- (f2);
  \draw[->, thick, blue, densely dotted] (B) -- (f3);
  \node[right=0pt of f1] {\footnotesize$\phi_1^*$};
  \node[right=0pt of f2] {\footnotesize$\phi_2^*$};
  \node[left=0pt of f3] {\footnotesize$\phi_3^*$};
  \node[circle,fill,outer sep=0pt] (A) at (1.4,.9) {};
  \node (l1) at (2,1.5) {\footnotesize$\nabla \mathscr L_1$};
  \node (l2) at (2.5,.8) {\footnotesize$\nabla \mathscr L_2$};
  \node (l3) at (1.3,0) {\footnotesize$\nabla \mathscr L_3$};
  \draw[->, thick, blue] (A) -- (l1);
  \draw[->, thick, blue] (A) -- (l2);
  \draw[->, thick, blue] (A) -- (l3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\textbf{4.}
MiniImageNet is a metalearning benchmark for one-shot 5-class image classification.

A \emph{siamese network} checks if two images are of the same class -- but training a binary classification model for $n$-way classification may not be optimal.
\emph{Matching networks} learn an embedding on which $k$-NN performs well.
If there are several samples per class, a \emph{prototypical network} embeds them all, computes their averages, and uses $k$-NN on those averages,
\[ p(y = k | x ) \propto \exp - d( f_\theta(x), c_k ). \]
One can also learn the distance (relation networks)
or allow for several prototypes per class (infinite mixture of prototypes, IMP).

\textbf{5.}
MAML ensembles are an example of Bayesian metalearning algorithm.

\textbf{6.}
Since MAML and black-box meta-learning only need gradients,
they can be used with policy gradient -- but the variance is high, and it is on-policy.
Value-based reinforcement learning (off-policy) is not gradient-based: it is difficult to combine it with MAML.

% \textbf{7.} % My notes are not clear

\textbf{8.}
Model-based reinfircement learning learns the dynamics (usually, the same across tasks);
\emph{model-predictive control} (MPC) replans after each step, because we may end up in a state slightly different from what the model predicted.
Learn either in latent space or in image space (video prediction)
\[
(\text{observation}_n , \text{action}_n ) \longmapsto \text{observation}_n.
\]

\textbf{9.} % Life-long learning
In \d{life-long learning}, the agent is asked to learn a first task, then a second, and so on,
instead of all at the same time.
Task order can be known, predictable, or random;
task boundaries may be known or not;
they can change abruptly or progressively shift.

We want to minimize the \emph{regret}.

There can be positive/negative forward/backward transfer:
the current task can make the performance on future/previous tasaks better/worse.

The \emph{follow-the-leader} algorithm stores all the data seen so far and trains on it.
The \emph{catastrophic forgetting} algorithm takes a gradient step on the current observation and forgets it.

To avoid forgetting previous tasks,
keep a small amount of data for each task
and add a constraint that the loss on the previus tasks does not get worse.
% Paper: gradient episodic memory for continual learning

\textbf{10.} % Jeff Clune
\emph{Generative teaching networks} (GTN) learn how to generate data to train on,
to help select the network architecture -- once it is chosen, train on actual data, long enough.

\emph{Weight normalizaion} suggests to parametrize the weights as
$ \mathbf w = g \cdot \mathbf v / \n{ \mathbf v }$, $g \in \RR_+$
and learn $\mathbf v$ and $g$ seprately.

\emph{Learning to RL} (LRL -- RL$^2$ is similar)
learns a RNN $(s,t,a_t) \mapsto a_t$;
it is amenable to differential Hebbian learning (``neurons that fire together wire together'')
and neuromodulated differentiable Hebbian learning (with eligibility traces).

To avoid catastrophic forgetting when ``learning to continuouslty learn'',
ANML uses  second network to identify the task and zero out the wrights irrelevant for it.

% ANML
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.7,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (I) at (0,0) {input};
  \node[fill=blue!20,minimum width=4cm] (A) at (3,1) {neuromodulation network\strut};
  \node[fill=blue!20,minimum width=4cm] (B) at (3,0) {prediction network\strut};
  \node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (T) at (5.8,0) {\footnotesize$\times$};
  \node (O) at (7,0) {output};
  \draw[->] (I) -- (A.west);
