\textsc{Poet} and Go-Explore use \emph{goal switching} and automatically generate tasks.

\textbf{11.} % Sergey Levine
\emph{Mutual information} is a measure of dependence
\begin{align*}
  I(x;y) &= \text{KL} \bigl( p(x,y) \bigm\| p(x) p(y) \bigr) \\
  &= \underset{\substack{\text{\vphantom{lp}how hard it}\\\text{\vphantom{lp}is to guess $y$}}}{ H\bigl( p(y) \bigr) } -
  \underset{\substack{\text{\vphantom{lp}how hard it}\\\text{\vphantom{lp}is to guess $y$}\\\text{\vphantom{lp}knowing $x$}}}{ H\bigl( p(y|x) \bigr) }
\end{align*}
For instance, the \emph{empowerment} is
\[ I(s_{t+1} ; a_t ) = H( s_{t+1} ) - H( s_{t+1} | a_t ). \]

Some variants of $Q$-learning
\begin{align*}
Q(s,a) &\leftarrow s(s,a) + \max_{ a' } Q( s' , a' ) \\
\intertext{increase exploration (soft $Q$-learning, SAC)}
Q(s,a) &\leftarrow s(s,a) + \log \int \exp Q( s' , a' ) da' \\
Q(s,a) &\leftarrow s(s,a) + \E_{ s', a' } \bigl[ Q( s' , a' ) - \underset{\text{entropy}}{\log \pi(a' | s' )} \bigr]
\end{align*}
Soft actor critic (SAC) updates the policy as
\[ \pi \leftarrow \argmin_\pi \text{KL} \bigl( \pi(\cdot|s) \bigm\| \exp Q^{\pi^{\text{old}}} (s,\cdot) \bigr). \]

To learn without a reward function, train a VAE to represent the state of the world,
pick a state at random, and use it as a goal.
To get diverse goals, update the VAE using \emph{weighted MLE},
maximizing $\E\bigl[ w(x) \log p(x) \bigr]$
instead of $\E\bigl[ \log p(x) \bigr]$,
e.g., with $w(x) = p(x) ^ \alpha$,
$\alpha \in [-1,0)$,
to increase entropy.
Learning $\pi(a|s,\text{goal})$ is equivalent to maximizing $I(s;\text{goal})$.

\emph{Intrinsic motivation} adds an exploration bonus
$-\log p_\pi(s)$ to the reward -- but check \emph{state marginal matching} (SMM) instead.

Instead of specifying a goal, give the policy a ``skill'' $z$ (an integer)
and have a discriminator try to guess it from the subsequent state(s). Policy and discriminator cooperate.
This maximizes the mutual information $I(z;s)$.
(In hierarchical reinforcement learning, once the skills have been learnt,
one can learn a policy that switches between them.)

\textbf{12.}
To avoid memorization, add a penalty on the information stored in the parameter (\emph{meta-regularization}).

If there is no explicit boundary between tasks, use the performance of the current model to detect changes or,
more generally, BOCPD (Bayesian online changepoint detection -- it is differentiable: you can backprop through it):
MOCA (metalearning with changepoint analysis).

If there is no explicit task, learn a representation of the data (unsupervised: BiGAN, DeepCLuster),
arbitrarily cluster the data (in different ways),
an ask metalearning to learn to distinguish those classes.

If two gradients conflict ($g_1 \cdot g_2 < 0 $), project each onto the normal hyperplane of the other
(PCGrad: projecting conflicting gradients).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generative deep learning}\\
  D. Foster (2019)
\end{book}
\textbf{1.}
\emph{Naive Bayes} models are \emph{generative};
they may work with simple tabular data, but will fail with pixels.

\textbf{2.}
To classify images (\textsc{cifar}-10), one could try dense layers (200, 150, 10, softmax; ReLU),
but convolutions (3$\times$3, 32, 32$_1$, 32$_2$, 64$_1$, 64$_1$, dense(128), dropout, dense; leaky ReLU,
batchnorm after each layer -- I indicate the stride with indices) work better.

\textbf{3.}
To generate images (\textsc{mnist}), we could try an \emph{autoencoder},
but the distribution in the latent space is not centered, far from Gaussian, and unbalanced.
Instead, a \emph{variational autoencoder} (VAE, or $\beta$-VAE) adds noise to the latent space and forces the distribution 
to be close to $N(0,1)$.
Check the 1-dimensional disributions in the latent space: after training, they should look Gaussian.
You can interpolate between images, or change some attribute, e.g., by adding
$\lambda \cdot \bigl[ \text{avg}(\text{smile}) - \text{avg}(\neg \text{smile}) \bigr]$ (CelebA).

\textbf{4.} GANs are notoriously difficult to train:
they suffer from mode collapse,
oscillating loss, uninformative loss
(the loss function of the generator changes with time:
if the loss increases with time, this may not be worrying,
the quality may be improving) and hyperparmeters are tricky to select.

The \emph{Wasserstein GAN} (WGAN) removes the log in the objective, makes the critic 1-Lipschitz with weight clipping,
and trains the critic to convergence (e.g., 5 steps for each generator step).

Instead of clipping the weights, the \emph{WGAN-GP} uses a gradient penalty,
\( \bigl(
  \n{ \nabla_{\!\text{input}} \text{critic}  } - 1 
\bigr) ^2, \)
where the gradient is evaluated at random averages between generated and real images,
and removes the critic batchnorm.
Examples include ``Quick, draw!'', \textsc{cifar}-10 horses and CelebA.

\textbf{5.} A \emph{CycleGAN} uses two generators and two discriminators,
with a UNet or ResNet architecture,
% Residual block
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2.5,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (A) at (0,5) {Conv2D};
  \node (B) at (0,4) {Instance Normalization};
  \node (C) at (0,3) {ReLU};
  \node (D) at (0,2) {Conv2D};
  \node (E) at (0,1) {Instance Normalization};
  \node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (P) at (0,-.8) {$+$};
  \draw[->] (0,7) -- (A);
  \draw[->] (E) -- (P);
  \draw[->] (0,6.3) -- (-1,6.3) |- (P);
  \draw[->] (P) -- (0,-2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
instance normalizaion instead of batch normalization,
and a \emph{PatchGAN} discriminator: it does not output a single number,
but an $8\times8$ matrix indicating if each patch is real or generated
(what is consistent across patches is the style, rather than the contents:
that is what we want to capture).

