\emph{Neural style transfer} combines three losses:
for the contents (MSE of the features),
the style (MSE of the GRAM matrices of the features, for several layers),
and the total variation (actually $L^{1.25}$ instead of $L^1$);
the model uses a pretrained VGG19 and is fitted with L-BFGS-B
[why not gradient descent?].

\textbf{6.} To generate text, try an LSTM RNN, or a GRU,
or their bidirectional variants, or stack them, after an embedding layer;
train with \emph{teacher forcing}; sample with \emph{temperature}
[and try \emph{beam search}]
(Maluuba NewsQA, to generate answer-question pairs from text).

\textbf{7.} To generate monophonic music (Bach cello suites),
use \texttt{music21} to process the \textsc{midi} files,
and stacked LSTMs, outputting pitch-duration pairs one by one.

\emph{MuseGAN} deals with polyphonic music (Bach chorales);
the generator has 4 componentss,
whose output change (or not) with each bar (or track);
the discriminator uses 3-dimensional convolutions.

\textbf{8.} The \emph{WorldModel} for reinforcement learning (RL)
in OpenAI Gym's CarRacing game has three components:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A VAE to process the image:
$\text{img}_t \mapsto h_t \mapsto \text{img}_t$;
\item An RNN with a \emph{mixture density network} (MDN-RNN),
\ie, an LSTM followed by an MDN (which outputs a mixure of 5 Gaussians in each dimension,
\ie, parameters $p_1,\dots,p_5,\mu_1,\dots,\mu_5,\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_5 \in \RR^n$),
which tries to predict the next state,
$(a_{t-1}, h_t) \mapsto \hat h_{t+1}$;
\item A controller
$(a_{t-1}, h_t, \hat h _{t+1} ) \mapsto ( a_t, \hat r_{t+1})$,
fully connected, trained with \emph{CMA-ES},
in parallel (\texttt{estool}).
\end{compactitem}
One can train using the MDN-RNN as a model (\emph{in-dream training}) --
to reduce overfitting,
add a temperature parameter to control model uncertainty
and steer the controller towards safer, better understood actions.

