\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine learning for asset managers}\\
  M. L\'opez de Prado (2020)
\end{book}
\textbf{1.} Applications of machine learning (ML) infinance include
existence (of a phenomenon, of information in data),
variable importance,
causation,
dimension reduction,
visualization,
latent representation,
outlier detection, etc.

\textbf{2.}
Use random matrix theory (RMT) to denoise correlation matrices,
setting the noise eigenvalues to their average (to preserve the trace).

For clustering, try \emph{detoning} -- removing the market component
(the matrix is then singular, but it is not an issue).

\textbf{3.} Correlation can be turned into a distance
as $d = \sqrt{1-\rho}$ or $d=\sqrt{1-\va\rho}$
(it is the Euclidean distance between the standardized vectors).
The \emph{variation of information} VI is another distance.
\begin{align*}
  H(X) &= \E_x [ - \log p_x ] &&\hspace*{-1.5cm}\text{(entropy)}\\
  &= \sum_x - p_x \log p_x \\
  H(X,Y) &= \E_{x,y} [ - \log p_{xy} ] \\
  H(X|Y) &= H(X,Y) - H(Y) \\
  \text{KL}(p\|q) &= \E_{ x \sim p } \left[ - \log \dfrac{ q_x }{ p_x } \right] &&\hspace*{-1.5cm}\text{(KL divergence)}\\
  &= \sum_x p_x \log \dfrac{ p_x }{ q_x } \\
  H_c(p\|q) &= \E_{ x \sim p } [ - \log q_x ] &&\hspace*{-1.5cm}\text{(cross-entropy)}\\
  &= H(p) + \text{KL}(p\|q) \\
  I(X;Y) &= H(X) - H(X|Y) &&\hspace*{-1.5cm}\text{(mutual information)}\\
  &= H(Y) - H(Y|X) \\
  &= \sum_{xy} p_{xy} \log \dfrac{ p_{xy} }{ p_x p_q } \\
  &= \text{KL}( p_{xy} \| p_x p_y ) \\
  &= \E_y \bigl[ \text{KL}\bigl( p(x|y) \bigm\| p(x) \bigr) \bigr] \\
  &= \E_x \bigl[ \text{KL}\bigl( p(y|x) \bigm\| p(y) \bigr) \bigr] \\
  \text{VI}(X,Y) &= H(X|Y) + H(Y|X) \\
  &= H(X) + H(Y) - 2 I(X;Y) \\
  &= 2(H(X,Y) - H(X) - H(Y) \\
  &= H(X,Y) - I(X;Y) \\
  \widetilde{\text{VI}}(X,Y) &= \dfrac{ \text{VI} (X,Y) }{ H(X,Y) }\\
  &= 1 - \dfrac{ I(X;Y) }{ H(X,Y) } \\
  \widetilde{\smash{\widetilde{\text{VI}}}\vrule width 0pt height .8em} &= 1 - \dfrac{ I(X;Y) }{ \max\bigl\{ H(X), H(Y) \bigr\} } 
\end{align*}

\textbf{4.} Use $k$-means clustering \emph{on the columns}
of the distance matrix $\sqrt{1-\rho}$
(or $\sqrt{1-\va\rho}$);
choose $k$ to maximize the \emph{clustering quality} $q$
(optimal number of clusters, ONC)
\begin{align*}
  q &= \dfrac{ \E[S] }{ \text{Var}[S] } \\
  S_i &= \dfrac{ b_i - a_i }{ \max( a_i, b_i ) }
\end{align*}
where $a_i$ (resp. $b_i$) is the average distance between observation $x_i$ and the elements of its cluster (resp., of the nearest cluster).
Compute the clustering quality for each cluster;
recluster those below average (just once) and keep the new clustering if quality improves.

\textbf{5.} Instead of forecasting labels based on time, tick, volume or price bars,
\[
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
+1 & \text{if } r > \tau \\
0 & \text{if } \va r \leq \tau \\
-1 & \text{if } r < - \tau,
\end{array}
\right.
\]
use a \emph{tripple barrier}
\[
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
+1 & \text{profit-taking barrier} \\
-1 & \text{stop-loss barrier} \\
0 & \text{time-out barrier}
\end{array}
\right.
\]
or \emph{trend scanning}:
whether each observation is in an up/down trend or not,
by looking at the minimum $p$-value of linear regressions on $[t,t+T]$, for various $T$.

For bet sizing:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Assume the Sharpe ratio is a standard Gaussian and bring it back to $U(-1,1)$;
\item Given $n$ binary predictors, their average under $H_0: p = \tfrac12$ is known;
  bring it back to $U(-1,1)$.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{6.} To measure feature importance, check:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $p$-values;
\item Shapley values;
\item Accumulated local effects (ALE);
\item Mean increase impurity: weighted information gain, from a random forest;
\item Mean decrease accuracy: drop in accuracy after shuffling the values of a variable.
\end{compactitem}
Replace the accuracy with:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The negative log-likelihood of the true labels;
\item The probability-weighted accuracy,
  \[
  \dfrac{
    \sum\limits_n \, \textbf{1} _{\text{correct}} \times ( p_n^{\text{max}} - 1/K )
  }{
    \sum\limits_n ( p_n^{\text{max}} - 1/K )
  }
  \]
  where $K$ is the number of classes and $p_n^{\text{max}}$ the probability, from the model, of the most likely class.
\end{compactitem}
To deal with multicolinearity, cluster the features.

\textbf{7.} Numeric instability, in portfolio construction,
is due to the condition number of the precision matrix $V^{-1}$;
it is worse when there are highly correlated assets.
It is usually dealt with by regularization (Black-Litterman),
constraints,
or better estimates of the concentration matrix.
\emph{Nested clustered optimization} (NCO)
denoises (RMT) and clusters (ONC) the covariance matrix,
computes optimal portfolios withing each cluster,
and then the inter-cluster optimal portfolio.

\textbf{8.} To limit selection bias under multiple testing,
estimate the FDR and the FWER from
\begin{align*}
  \rlap{\text{FPR}}\qquad\qquad \llap{$\alpha$} &= \dfrac{ \text{FP} }{ \text{TN} + \text{FP} } \\
  \rlap{\text{FNR}}\qquad\qquad \llap{$\beta$} &= \dfrac{ \text{FN} }{ \text{TP} + \text{FN} } \\
  \theta &= \dfrac{ \text{TP} + \text{FN} }{ \text{TN} + \text{FP} }
\end{align*}
Approximations of the distribution of
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The sample Sharpe ratio; 
\item The maximum sample Sharpe ratio after $k$ trials.
\end{compactitem}
help define a \emph{deflated Sharpe ratio}.

Use clustering to estimate the effective number of trials.

\textbf{9.} To limit overfitting, test on synthetic data, using
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Resampling: cross-validation, (sequential) bootstrap and their variants;
\item Monte Carlo: parametric, GAN, VAE.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic foundations\\ of statistical network analysis}\\
  H. Crane (2018)
\end{book}

A \emph{random graph} is a graph-valued random variable.

\textbf{3.} We rarely observe the whole graph, but only some \emph{sample} of it:
it is not always straightforward to infer properties of the population graph from those of the sample graph (\emph{consistency}),
and this depends a lot on how the network was sampled --
\emph{vertex sampling} (which tends to generate empty subgraphs, if the population graph is large and sparse),
\emph{edge sampling} (a list of edges, e.g., when you intercept communications),
\emph{snowball sampling} (pick $k$ nodes at random and consider their radius-$r$ egonets),
\emph{hyperedge sampling} (collaboration, e.g., co-author networks)
or \emph{path sampling} (traceroute, to study the internet).

\textbf{4.} Common models include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Erdos-Renyi: $P(Y_{ij} = 1 ) = p$;
\item Barabasi-Albert (preferential attachment);
\item Exponential random graph model (ERGM),
  \[ P(Y = y) \propto \exp \sum_i \theta_i T_i(y) \]
  where the $T_i$ are features (sufficient statistics);
  ERGMs do not work well with sampled networks;
\item Graphons, $P(Y_{ij} = 1 ) = \phi(U_i,U_j)$, where $U_i \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} U(0,1)$
  and $\phi: [0,1]^2 \to [0,1]$
  or, in closed form
  \[
  \!\!
  P(Y=y) =
  \!\!
  \int\limits_{[0,1]^N}
  \!
  \prod_{i \neq j}
  \phi( u_i, u_j) ^ {y_{ij}}
  \bigl(
  1 - \phi(u_i, u_j)
  \bigr) ^ { 1 - y_{ij}}
  du
  % du_1 \dots du_N
  \]
\item Sparse graphon,
  $\phi_n(u,v) = \rho_n^{-1} w(u,v)$, for graphs of size $n$,
  where $\rho_n^{-1}$ is the edge density
  (e.g., $\rho_n = n$ -- this allows sparse graphs, which are impossible with graphons,
  but not power law degree distributions)
\item Stochastic block model (SBM)
  $ P( Y_{ij} + 1 ) = 1 - \exp \theta_i \theta_j w( k(i), k(j) ) $
  degree-corrected SBM
  $ P( Y_{ij} + 1 ) = 1 - \exp \theta_i \theta_j \log \phi_{ k(i), k(j) } ( U_i, U_j ) $
\item Graphex: an exchangeable point process on $[0,\infty)^2$
  \[ Y(A) = \# \{ a \in A \ :\ a \in Y \}, \qquad A \subset [0,\infty)^\infty, \]
    where $(t,t') \in Y$ is a link between $t$ and $t'$
    (users are identified by their registration timestamp)
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{6.} Some of those models are \emph{vertex-exchangeable}:
the distribution is invariant under permutation of the vertices.
More generally, exchangeable random graphs are continuous mixtures of graphons
(Aldous-Hoover theorem).
This generalizes de Finetti's theorem:
if $(X_n)_{n\geq1}$ is an exchangeable sequence
of random variables with values in $\{0,1\}$ then,
there exists $f : [0,1]^2 \to [0,1]$ such that $X$ has the same distribution as $Y$ defined by
\begin{align*}
  U_n &\overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} U(0,1) && n \geq 0 \\
  Y_n &= f(U_0, U_n) && n \geq 1
\end{align*}
\ie, it is a continuous mixtture of iid sequences $f_u(U_n) = f(u,U_n)$.

\textbf{8.} \emph{Relatively exchangeable} models only requires that the distribution
be invariant under some permutations of the vertices,
for instance, generalized SBM
\begin{align*}
  P( Y_{ij} = 1 ) &= \phi_{ k(i), k(j) }( U_i, U_j) \\
    k &: \NN \to \llbracket 1 , k \rrbracket \\
    \phi_{k_1,k_2} &: [0,1]^2 \longrightarrow [0,1] \\
    U_i &\overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} U(0,1)
\end{align*}
where $k$ indicates the class of each node.
Relative exchangeability can be relative to another network:
\[ P( Y_{ij} = 1 ) = \phi_{ G |_{\{i,j\}} } ( U_i, U_j). \]
More generally, consider $k$-ary relations, for various $k$'s,
$X_1,\dots,X_r$,
$X_j : \NN^{a_j} \to \mathscr X_j$,
for instance
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $a_1=1$, $\mathscr X = \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket$,
  for the SBM;
\item $a_1=2$, $\mathscr X = \{0,1\}$ for graph exchangeability;
\item $a_1=1$, $\mathscr X = \RR^d$ for latent space models.
\end{compactitem}
Under reasonable assumptions, relative exchangeable random graphs are continuous mixtures of
\[
P(Y_{ij} + 1 ) = \phi_{
  X_1 | _{\{i,j\}},
  \dots,
  X_r | _{\{i,j\}}
}
( U_i, U_j )
\qquad
U_i \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} U(0,1).
\]

\textbf{9.}
An edge-exchangeable random graph is an exchangeable sequence of ordered pairs
(exchangeable modulo vertex renaming).
If $\mathscr P$ is the set of vertices,
an \emph{interaction propensity process}
is a sequence of iid random variables with values in
$\mathscr P\times \mathscr P$,
\[ P[ X_k = (r,s) ] = f_{r,s}. \]
Examples include the vertex component model
$f_{ij} = f_i^{\text{out}} f_j^{\text{in}}$
or the (preferential attachment) \emph{Hollywood model}
(which does not generate a graph but a sequence of edges).
Edge-exchangeable graphs are continuous mixtures of interaction propensity processes.

One can also consider \emph{relative edge exchangeability}
$P[ X_i = (r,s)] = f_{r,s}^{ k(i)}$,
$k : \NN \to \llbracket 1 , k \rrbracket$.
The notion of exchangeability (and the Hollywood model)
can be extended to hyperedges
(collaboration networks (IMDb, coauthors): the interaction propensity process is a probability distribution on
$\operatorname{fin}\mathscr P$, the set of finite multisets of elements of $\mathscr P$:
$P[ X_i = 1] = f_x$, $x \in \operatorname{fin}\mathscr P$)
and paths.

\textbf{11.}
To model dynamic networks, one often makes a simplifying Markov assumption,
or even a \emph{projective Markov} assumption
($(G_t)_t$ is Markov on $\{0,1\}^{N\times N}$, and so are its restrictions).
The \emph{temporal ERGM} is
\[ P(Y_{t+1} = y' | Y_t = y ) \propto \exp \bigl[ \eta \cdot T(y,y') \bigr] . \]
A \emph{rewriring process} samples a new rewriring map
\[
W : \left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \{0,1\}^{N\times N} & \longrightarrow & \{0,1\}^{N\times N} \\
  Y_t & \longmapsto & Y_{t+1} 
\end{array}
\right.
\]
for each $t$.

A \emph{graph-valued L\'evy process} is defined by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $Y_0 = 0 \in \{0,1\}^{N\times N}$;
\item Stationary increments $ Y_{t+s} \triangle Y_t \overset{\text{d}}{=} Y_s$;
\item Independent increments: $Y_{t_0} \triangle Y_{t_1}$, $Y_{t_1} \triangle Y_{t_2}$, $\ldots$, $Y_{t_{k-1}} \triangle Y_{t_k}$ independents
\end{compactitem}

It can be constructed as $Y_0=0$,
$Y_{t+1} = Y_t \triangle Z_{t+1}$, $Z_{t+1} \sim \mu$,
  for some distribution $\mu$ on $\{0,1\}^{N\times N}$ (or using a rewriring process).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep reinforcement learning hands-on}\\
  M. Lapan (2020)
\end{book} 

\textbf{1.}
While the inputs of supervised learning algorithms are pairs $(x,y)$,
where $y$ is the value to predict from $x$,
reinforcement learning uses tuples, $(s,a,r,s')$, where $s$ is the current state,
$a$ the action chosen by the agent, $r$ the (immediate) reward and $s'$ the next state,
and tries to find the best action for any given state (an action that may not have been chosen by the agent)
for the sum of the discounted rewards (and not just the immediate reward).

Challenges of RL include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The best action is not known;
\item Delayed rewards;  
\item Non-iid observations;
\item Exploitation vs exploration.
\end{compactitem}

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=.8,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (POMDP) at (1,3) {POMDP};
  \node (MDP) at (1,2) {MDP};
  \node (MRP) at (0,1) {MRP};
  \node (B) at (2,1) {Bandit};
  \node (MC) at (0,0) {MC};
  \draw[-] (MC) to node[anchor=east] {\footnotesize +reward} (MRP);
  \draw[-] (MRP) to node[pos=.8,anchor=east] {\footnotesize +action\ \ \ } (MDP);
  \draw[-] (B) to node[pos=.8,anchor=west] {\footnotesize\ \ +state} (MDP);
  \draw[-] (MDP) -- (POMDP);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\textbf{2.} OpenAI Gym provides RL environments.
Some of the environments require the commercial MuJoCo physics simulator (PyBullet is a free alternative, slower with an unpleasant API).

There is no sklearn-like standard Python library for RL algorithms:
check OpenAI Baselines (Tensorflow, with a declarative interface), ptan (used by the book), catalyst, etc.

\textbf{4.}
The RL algorithm taxonomy distinguishes between:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \d{Policy-based} (we directly try to find the best policy, \ie, a map from states to actions -- it could be stochastic)
  vs \d{value-based} (we try to estimate the value of each state, \ie, the cummulated reward we would get if we started in a given state and acted optimally --
  from there, we can find the optimal policy);
  there are also hybrid algorithms, estimating both policy and value function, each helping improve the other;
\item \d{On-policy} (the training data was generated by the current best estimate of the policy)
  vs \d{off-policy} (the training data was generated by some other policy, e.g., an old policy, or a policy modified to explore more).
\item \d{Model-based} (we use the data to estimate the dynamics of the environment) vs \d{model-free} (we do not) --
  RL usually refers to searching for an optimal policy for an unknown MDP, only accessible through observations:
  if the MDP is known, this is an easier \d{planning} problem.
\end{compactitem}

The \d{cross-entropy method} (CEM) is the simplest policy-based RL algorithm:
it learns to repeat actions that performed well.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a random policy;
\item Act according to this policy;
\item Keep the best 30\% episodes;
\item Train a new policy to replicate what the agent did in those episodes.
\end{compactitem}

This works in simple environments, such as CartPole,
but even for FrozenLake, a few adjustments are needed:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Larger batches (successful episodes are rarer);
\item Keep successful episodes in memory for longer;
\item Decrease the learning rate; train for longer.
\end{compactitem}

% CEM, theoretical justification: the explanations do not make sense (what is "p"? what is "x"? what are we trying to compute?)
% https://people.smp.uq.edu.au/DirkKroese/ps/eormsCE.pdf

\textbf{5.}
For a known MDP, the value of a state $V(s)$,
or of a state-action pair $Q(s,a)$, satisfies the \d{Bellman equation},
\begin{align*}
  V(s) &= \max_a E[ r + \gamma V(s') ] \\
  Q(s,a) &= E[ r + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s',a') ].
\end{align*}

\textbf{6.}
For an unknown MDP, the update
\[ Q(s,a) \leftarrow r + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s',a') \]
is a bit too violent: it completely discards the previous value of $Q(s,a)$...
(Tabular) Q-learning uses:
\begin{align*}
  \text{target} &\leftarrow r + \gamma \max_{ a' } Q(s',a') \\
  Q(s,a) &\leftarrow (1-\lambda) Q(s,a) + \lambda \text{target}
\end{align*}

\d{Deep Q-learning} (DQN) minimizes
\[ \text{Loss} = [ Q(s,a) - ( r + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s',a') ) ] ^ 2. \]

As often with RL, it does not work in practice without a few adjustments:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use $\epsilon$-greedy, with $\epsilon$ decreasing, to explore enough at the begining;
\item Use a replay buffer (fixed-size, or prioritized) to have enough diversity; 
\item Training is unstable because we are ``chasing our tail'' ($Q(s,a)$ and $Q(s',a')$ are too close):
  use a \emph{target network}, and only update it from time to time;
\item To ensure the Markov property, keep the previous state(s) in memory.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{8.} DQN can (should) be improved on:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $N$-step DQN
  (this is no longer really off-policy: we assume that the intermediate action was optimal -- 
  but if you only look 2 or 3 steps ahead, it is almost off-policy)
  \begin{align*}
    Q(s_t,a) &= r_t + \gamma \max_a Q(s_{t+1},a) \\
    &= r_t + \gamma r_{t+1} + \gamma^2 \max_a Q(s_{t+1},a) \\
    &= \cdots
  \end{align*}
\item Double DQN: since DQN tends to overestimate $Q$ (because of the Max), use the other network to choose the optimal action
  \begin{align*}
    Q(s_t,a) &= r_t + \gamma \max_a Q(s_{t+1},a) && \text{Bellman} \\
    Q(s_t,a) &= r_t + \gamma \max_a Q'(s_{t+1},a) && \text{DQN (target network)} \\
    Q(s_t,a) &= r_t + \gamma Q'(s_{t+1}, \argmax_a Q'(s_{t+1},a) ) && \text{Same formula} \\
    Q(s_t,a) &= r_t + \gamma Q'(s_{t+1}, \argmax_a Q(s_{t+1},a) ) && \text{Double DQN}
  \end{align*}
\item Noisy networks:
  instead of taking actions completely at random to explore, add noise to the network weights, with learned variance (Bayesian FC layer;
  use a low-rank parametrization for the matrix of variances);
\item Prioritized replay buffer: prioritize samples with a larger loss, \ie, train more on data that surprises you
  (the observations are no longer iid: use sample weights);
\item Dueling DQNs separately estimate the value of the state and the advantage of the action,
  \[ Q(s,a) = V(s) + A(s,a); \]
  to ensure that the average advantage is zero, use
  \[ Q(s,a) = V(s) + A(s,a) - \operatorname*{Mean}_a A(s,a) \]
\item Categorical (distributional) DQN: replace expectations with distributions (histograms, 51 bins).
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{9.} To speed up the computations:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Only compute the gradient when it is needed (not for the target or to select actions) (\texttt{detach()});
\item Only build the computation graph when it is needed (\texttt{torch.no\_grad()});
\item Use larger batches: sample from several (identical) environments;
\item Play and train in separate processes.
\end{compactitem}
  
\textbf{10.}
DQN could be used to design an intraday trading strategy:
the state could be the current position and the previous 1-minute bars OHLC prices,
the actions ``buy'' or ``sell'',
the reward the after-transaction-cost (realized, or unrealized) P\&L.
Do not expect the strategy to perform well out-of-sample.

[For some reason, all the applications of RL in finance use DQN. (My report on end-to-end portfolio construction used policy-based RL.)]

\textbf{11.}
So far, we have focused on the value function, $V(s)$ or $Q(s,a)$, but what we really want is the policy.
For small, discrete action spaces, we can easily solve the optimization problem
\[ \operatorname*{Maximize}_a Q(s,a) \]
but for large or infinite action spaces, it is more problematic.

The \d{policy gradient} is
\begin{align*}
  J &= \E[ Q(s,a) ] \\
  \nabla_{\!\theta} J
  &= \E_{\pi_\theta} [ Q(s,a) \ \nabla_{\!\theta} \log \pi_\theta(a|s) ] \\
  &= \E_{\pi_{\theta_1}} [ Q(s,a) \ \nabla_{\!\theta_2} \log \pi_{\theta_2}(a|s) ] \\
  &= \nabla_{\!\theta_2} \E_{\pi_{\theta_1}} [ Q(s,a) \ \log \pi_{\theta_2}(a|s) ] \\
  \text{Loss} &= - Q(s,a) \log \pi(a|s).
\end{align*}
(Note that there is no log in the definition of $J$, but there is one in the definition of the loss:
the gradients are nonetheless the same, because the parameter $\theta$ in the expectation is fixed when we differentiate the loss,
but not when we compute the expectation of $J$.)

The cross-entropy method is a policy gradient method with $Q(s,a) = 0$ or 1.

\textsc{Reinforce} uses the returns as $Q$: 
\[  \text{Loss} = -\sum_t G_t \log \pi(a_t | s_t). \]

However:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The variance is \emph{very} high: use a baseline, e.g., a constant, such as
  the average discounted reward,
  or moving average (use a \texttt{deque}),
  or an estimate of $V(s)$;
\item Full episodes are required: we could use $Q(s,a) = r + \gamma V(s')$, with another network to estimate $V$ (Actor-Critic),
  or unroll a few steps of the Bellman equation, especially if $\gamma$ is not too high;
\item There is not enough exploration (there is some at the begining, because the policy is stochastic): add an \emph{entropy bonus}, $H(\pi)$;
\item The samples are correlated (and we cannot increase the replay buffer, because policy gradient methods are on-policy):
  use parallel environments to generate enough samples.
\end{compactitem}

In practice:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use a baseline;
\item Add an entropy bonus;
\item Unroll 10 steps of the Bellman equation;
\item Use parallel environments;
\item Clip the gradients; 
\item Monitor
  the KL divergence between the current and previous policy (spikes are a bad sign),
  the $L^\infty$ and $L^2$ norm of the gradients,
  the components of the loss.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{12.}
If all the $Q$-values are positive, the optimization of
\[ \text{Loss} = - Q(s,a) \log \pi(a|s) \]
will try to move the network towards each action encountered, 
whether good or bad, but it will move slightly less towards bad actions. 
In contrast, if the $Q$-values are sometimes positive and sometimes negative, the optimization will move 
towards the good actions, and away from the bad -- it will move less, and in more consistent directions.

This can be ensured with a \emph{baseline}.

The \d{Advantage-Actor-Critic} (A2C) method makes the baseline state-dependent, $V(s)$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{actor} computes the policy, $\pi(a|s)$;
\item The \emph{critic} computes the state value function, $V(s)$.
\end{compactitem}  
There are 3 losses (pay attention to their signs!): policy, value and entropy.

\textbf{13.}
SGD works better with iid observations, but interacting with the environment gives correlated observations:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For off-policy methods (such as DQN), use a huge replay buffer;
\item For on-policy methods (such as policy gradient), use parallel environments (this is not sample-efficient).
\end{compactitem}

There are two types of parallelism for RL
(Asynchronous advantage actor critic, \textbf{A3C}):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item In data-level parallelism, the workers are in charge of the interactions between agent and environment,
  and everything else (loss, gradient, SGD) is centralized;
\item In gradient-level parallelism, the workers also compute their contributions to the loss and the gradient.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{14.}
RL (self-critical sentence training, SCST)
can be used to generate text, e.g., in a chatbot, asking a customer more details about her query, and directing her to the correct page.
It has several advantages over maximum likelihood estimation:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item It allows for several unrelated outputs, e.g., different translations of the same sentence,
  different answers to the same question -- the ``average'' of those outputs is unlikely to be a good answer;
\item We can maximize the score we want, e.g., BLEU -- it does not need to be differentiable.
\end{compactitem}

\begin{align*}
  \textsc{mle}: & \E_{ x \sim \text{data} } [ \log p_{\text{model}} ] \\
  \textsc{reinforce}: &  \E_{\substack{ s \sim \text{data} \\ a \sim \pi }} [ Q(s,a) \log \pi(a|s) ]
\end{align*}

The rewards are too sparse to use \textsc{reinforce} directly: 
first pre-train with MLE (traditional seq2seq),
then fine-tune with \textsc{reinforce}.

As baseline, use a greedy decoder to get a BLEU score estimate of the whole sentence.

\textbf{15.}
The state space can be complex: for instance, TextWorld is a grid world of which we are only given a textual description,
which can be processed by an LSTM before being fed to a DQN.

\textbf{16.}
One could also attempt to use RL to 
generate GUI actions from a textual description such as ``Click on `Close'{}'', ``Open collapsed groups and click on the link with `foobar'{}'',
``select yesterday's date using the date picker tool'',
``tick checkboxes containing `foobar'{}'', etc.

\textbf{17.}
\textbf{A2C} has three ingredients:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \textsc{reinforce} gradient,
  \[ \nabla J = \nabla_{\!\theta} \log \pi_\theta(a|s) ( R - V_\theta(s) ); \] 
\item The baseline, $V_\theta(s)$, which minimizes the MSE with the value from the Bellman equation;
\item An entropy bonus.
\end{compactitem}
If the action space is continuous, represent the policy as a Gaussian distribution, parametrized by $\mu$ and $\sigma^2$.

(Deep) Deterministic policy gradient (\textbf{DDPG}) is similar, but with a deterministic policy --
so far, the policies in policy gradient (PG) methods were stochastic.
\[ \nabla_{\!\theta} Q(s, \mu(s) ) = \nabla_{\!a} Q(s,\mu(s)) \cdot \nabla_{\!\theta} \mu(s) \]
Note that the critic, $Q(s,a)$, is not used as in A2C: in A2C, it was optional
(it was just there to reduce the variance -- without it we get the Reinforce algorithm).
Here, it is needed: we will train end-to-end using its gradient. 
Contrary to A2C, DDPG is off-policy.

Since the policy is deterministic, something else is needed to explore,
e.g., adding iid noise to the actions,
or using an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process
\[ dx = \theta(\mu-x)dt + \sigma dW \]
(but this requires the agent to be stateful and does not seem to improve on iid noise).

(Distributed) distributional (deep deterministic) policy gradients (\textbf{D4PG})
adds the usual DQN improvements to DDPG:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Distribution of $Q$-values instead of $Q$-value, 
  with the cross-entropy loss to compare the two distributions;
\item $n$-step Bellman equation;
\item Prioritized replay buffer;
\item iid noise.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{18.}
For actual robots (you can put a PyBoard, a few sensors, a few servos, on a 3D-printed frame),
train them (with DDPG) in a simulator and hope the trained model also works on the robot.
[Meta-learning is a more recent approach, but it requires more computation power on the robot.]

\textbf{19.}
Small changes in the model can lead to large changes in the policy:
that is why we were monitoring the KL divergence between the distribution of actions between consecutive steps. 
Instead of just monitoring it, trust region methods constrain it. 

% [No, that is not the explanation he gives.]
% That chapter is not clear.

Proximal policy optimization (\textbf{PPO})
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Replaces the objective 
  \( J_\theta = \E[ \log \pi_\theta(a_t|s_t) A_t ] \)
  with \( J_\theta = \E [ r_t \cdot A_t ], \)
  where the importance weights are
  \[ r_t =
  \dfrac{ \pi_{\theta_{\text{new}}} (a_t|s_t) }{ \pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}} (a_t|s_t) }
  \]
  \[\pi_{\theta_{\text{new}}} (a_t|s_t) / \pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}} (a_t|s_t) \]
\item Clips the importance weights to $(1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)$;
\item Replaces the advantage 
  \[ \hspace*{-4mm} A_t = -V(s_t) + r_t + \gamma r_{t+1} + \cdots + \gamma ^{T-t+1} r_{T-1} + \gamma ^{T-1} V(s_T) \] 
   with
   \[ A_t = \sigma_t + (\gamma \lambda)\sigma_{t+1} + \cdots + (\gamma\lambda)^{T-t+1} \sigma_{T-1} \]
   where
   \( \sigma_t = r_t + \gamma V(s_{t+1}) - V(s_t) . \)
\end{compactitem}

Trust region policy optimization (\textbf{TRPO})
uses the discounted visitation probability
\[ \rho_\pi(s) = P[s_1=s] + \gamma P[s_2=s] + \cdots, \]
defines the loss as
\[ \E[ G ] + \sum_s \rho_\pi(s) \sum_a \tilde \pi (a|s) A(s,a) \]
where $G$ is the discounted reward, $\tilde \pi(a|s)$ the argmax policy,
adds a constraint $\text{KL}(\theta_{\text{old}} \| \theta) \leq \delta$,
and uses the conjugate gradient (CG) method to solve the resulting constrained optimization problem.

\textbf{ACKTR} (A2C using K-FAC trust region)
combines A2C, Kronecker-factored (K-FAC) second-order optimization, and trust regions.

\d{Soft actor-critic} (\textbf{SAC} -- currently the preferred approach for continuous control) uses
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Entropy regularization: add an entropy bonus to each reward, to encourage actions that will lead to more exploration in the long term;
\item Clipped double-$Q$ trick: learn two networks to predict the Bellman $Q$-value, and use the minimum, to deal with $Q$-value over-estimation.
\end{compactitem}
There are 4 networks: $\pi$, $V$, $Q_1$, $Q_2$.

\textbf{20.} Population-based optimization algorithms are not sample-efficient, but eminently parallelizable.

Genetic algorithms (GA):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Randomly perturb the current policies;
\item Keep the best ones;
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}

% Novelty search (NS): change the fitness function to explore more [no details]

Evolutionary strategies:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit a Gaussian distribution to the parameters of the current population of policies;
\item Sample from it;
\item Keep the best ones;
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}

Evolutionary strategies (\textbf{CMA-ES}):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Randomly perturb the current policy, in several ways;
\item Compute the fitness of the resulting policies; 
\item Modify the original policy weights by adding the same noise, but rescaled proportionally to the fitness --
  you can use the PyTorch optimizers (not just plain descent): they just need something that looks like a gradient.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{21.} 
So far, we achieved exploration with 
$\epsilon$-greedy policies (for value-based methods)
or an entropy bonus (for policy-based methods).
But taking isolated random actions may not be sufficient to explore other regions of the search space: several steps may be needed.

\emph{Noisy networks} (Bayesian neural nets) add noise the the weights, with a learned variance.
This may look similar to $\epsilon$-greedy, but the noise is added to the weights, not the network output.

\emph{Count-based methods} add a reward for rarely-visited states, $c/\sqrt N$
(as UCB, for bandits).
If there are many states, or if the state space is continuous, use a density estimator, or a learned state embedding, or a hashing function.

\emph{Curiosity-driven exploration} does not use the reward from the environment at all, but a measure of novelty.

\emph{Prediction-based methods} measure the novelty of a state by attempting to predict something from the environment:
if prediction is easy, the state is not that new and does not warrant more exploration.
As intrinsic reward, one could even use the ability of a (trained) neural network to predict the output from another one (untrained, randomly initialized)
(more generally, the same idea, distillation of a random neural net, can be used for outlier detection).

\textbf{22.}
Model-based methods are more sample-efficient.

For insntance, imagination-augmented agents (I2A) train an environment model $(s,a) \mapsto (r,s')$
and generate trajectories from it.
They use two policies, the learned one, and a distilled version to generate trajectories,
and use an RNN to compute a latent representation of the generated trajectories
to add them to the input of the policy.

\textbf{23.}
For two-player games (tic-tac-toe, chess, go), the minimax algorithm can rarely expand the full tree. Instead:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Expand it using the current best policy (\textbf{MCTS}: Monte Carlo Tree Search);
  only the first moves are stochastic;
\item Progressively update the value of the nodes, as in value iteration;
\item Train a model on those partially expanded trees, as if they were fully expanded;
\item From time to time, compare the performance of the two models (the trained one, and that used to expand the tree), and update the latter.
\end{compactitem}

AlphaGo Zero uses a variant of MCTS.

\textbf{24.}
Attempts to solve the Rubik's Cube with self-play (ADI, autodidactic iterations) were not convincing.

\textbf{25.}
RL also applies to (competing or collaborating) multi-agent sustems (MARL).

\end{multicols}
\clearpage
\begin{multicols}{2}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%
%% ICLR 2020
%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiplicative interactions\\ and where to find them}\\
  S.M. Jayakumar et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Try replacing concatenation with \emph{multiplication} -- gating, hypernetworks, dynamic convolutions, attention, etc.
can be expressed as multiplications. 
More generally, replace linear maps $(x,z) \mapsto W [x;z] + b$
with bilinear ones $(x,z) \mapsto z' W x + z' U + V x + b$ ($W$ is then a 3-dimensional tensor).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gradients as features\\ for deep representation learning}\\
  F. Mu et al.  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The gradient of the output wrt the parameters provides a latent representation of the input.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Enhancing adversarial defense\\ by k-winners-take-all}\\
  C. Xiao et al.  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The gradient of the loss wrt the input is used to mount adversarial attacks. 
One way to thwart those attacks is to use
``obfuscated gradients'', \ie, non-differentiable layers
(differentiable wrt the parameters, for training, but not wrt the input).

But non-differentiable layers can be approximated, in the backward pass, with differentiable ones.
Prefer non-differentable layers that cannot easily be approximated by smooth functions, such as the $k$-winners-take-all:
$ \phi_k(y)_j = y_j$ if $y_j$ is in the $k$ largest components of $y$,
$ \phi_k(y)_j = 0$ otherwise.
The loss surface, in weight space, is smooth almost everywhere (so the network is still easy to train)
but in input space, it is not (so searching for adversarial examples is more problematic).
%[NICE PLOT].


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Jacobian adversarially regularized\\ networks for robustness}\\
  A. Chan et al.  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
For images, the gradient of the loss wrt the input is itself an image, 
which can help tell how robust a model is: for robust models, it looks like the input, \ie, like an actual picture, 
while for non-robust models, it looks like noise. 
During training, adding a discriminator to make this gradient image look more like a picture improves robustness
(since the colours are off, add a 1-layer CNN to make the Jacobian look more like a real image).  
% Striking example

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Meta-learning with warped gradient descent}\\
  S. Flennerhag et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
WarpGrad learns a gradient preconditioner (as in the natural gradient, but learned, and non-linear)
in the meta-learning phase -- it is fixed when the final tasks are learned.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Truth or backpropaganda? An empirical investigation of deep learning theory}\\
  M. Goldblum et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Many commonly-held beliefs about neural networks, only proved for unrealistic architectures, such as deep linear networks, are actually wrong in general:
there are sub-optimal local minima, 
low $L^2$ norm local minima need not be better,
low-rank models need not be better or more robust (maximizing the rank actually works better).
In spite of this, neural networks work well, thanks to their initialization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Piecewise linear activations substantially shape the loss surfaces of neural networks}\\
  F. He et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
ReLU networks divide the input space into ``cells'';
in each cell, the local minima are connected, but there are spurious minima.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bounds on over-parameterization\\ for guaranteed existence of descent paths\\ in shallow ReLU networks}\\
  A. Sharifnassab et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}

We already knew that sufficiently over-parametrized shallow ReLU networks were easy to train 
(the loss landscape has the ``descent path property'').
The converse is true: when a network is not sufficiently over-parametrized,
the optimization can easily get stuck (there are ``local cup minima'').

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evaluating the search phase\\ of neural architecture search}\\
  K. Yu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Evaluating network architecture search (NAS) algorithms is tricky, and they do not seem to perform much better than random search. 
They are biased towards fast-converging cells (shallow and wide), which do not guarantee better generalization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{NAS evaluation is frustratingly hard}\\
  A. Yang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
%(Review paper)  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding architectures learnt\\ by cell-based neural architecture search}\\
  Y. Shu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Network architecture search (NAS) algorithms
are biased towards fast-converging cells (shallow and wide), which do not guarantee better generalization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding and robustifying\\ differentiable architecture search}\\
  A. Zela et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Architectures that do not generalize correspond to a sharper minimum of the loss function: 
stop when the largest eigenvalue of the Hessian (on a random validation minibatch) starts to increase.
Alternatively, increase the regularization of the inner problem, to lower the curvature.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural oblivious decision ensembles\\ for deep learning on tabular data}\\
  S. Popov et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}

Many traditional machine learning algorithms can be used as layers of deep neural networks, 
either as is, if they are differentiable, or after regularization if they involve discrete choices. 
For instance, in \emph{oblivious decision trees} (decision trees which use the same features and thresholds at each tree level), 
one can use soft thresholds, \ie,
replace $ x_i > b$ with $\sigma( ( \sum \lambda_i x_i - b ) / s ) $,
and sparsemax instead of softmax for sparsity.
Those layers can be stacked (NODE).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Meta-learning acquisition functions for transfer learning in Bayesian optimization}\\
  M. Volpp et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
In GP-based Bayesian optimization, replace the acquisition function with a neural network, trained on similar objective functions;
train with RL (no gradient needed).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning space partitions\\ for nearest neighbor search}\\
  Y. Dong et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Many classical algorithms rely on some heuristic to speed them up:
in particular this is the case of search algorithms (A*, DPLL, etc.), which explore a tree or a graph, 
and need to choose which node to explore next.
Those heuristics can be replaced with a neural net.

Approximate nearest neighbour search often relies on space partition,
often dependent on data (LSH, $k$-means (quantization), hyperplane partitions).
Use supervised learning to find better partitions (neural LSH):
compute the $k$-NN graph of the data;
find a balanced partition (combinatorial graph partitioning, KaHIP);
use a deep neural net to assign each point of $\RR^n$ to a partition.
At query time, use the neural net to find the most likely bins, and search them for the nearest neighbour.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to link}\\
  M.F. Balcan et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Use a neural net to predict the best hyperparameters (of hierarchical clustering).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural network branching\\ for neural network verification}\\
  J. Lu and M.P. Kumar (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Use a graph neural net (GNN) to find branch-and-bound heuristics.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Explanation by progressive exaggeration}\\
  S. Singla et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Counterfactuals are a way of explaining the forecasts of a model:
minimal changes to the input to change the output (adversarial examples).
They are usually computed as an optimization problem, but one could use a neural network to automatically compute them.

The counterfactual explanation should look real: use a discriminator.
Also add a self-consistency loss (if the output is the age of a person from a picture,
making it 10 years older, and then 10 years younger, should recover the initial image).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mathematical reasoning in latent space}\\
  D. Lee et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
There are three main types of logic: 
propositional logic (and, or, not operators, but no quantifiers: SAT solvers, such as \texttt{minisat}),
first-order logic (with quantifiers over variables: SMT solvers, e.g., \texttt{z3}),
and higher-order logic (with quantifiers over variables, functions and propositions: theorem provers, such as
\texttt{coq}, \texttt{agda}, \texttt{isabelle}).
\texttt{HOL Light} is an interactive theorem prover, with a database of 19,000 mathematical statements, covering algebra, topology, calculus, and measure theory.
Like most theorem provers, it relies on rewrite rules -- but it is very time-consuming to check which rules can be applied, and where they lead to.

With a graph neural net, one can learn an embedding that helps tell 
if a rewrite rule applies to a given statement (by taking the scalar product of the embeddings)
and perform approximate reasoning, by moving several steps in the latent space.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning heuristics for quantified boolean formulas through reinforcement learning}\\
  G. Lederman et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Learned constraint solver heuristics outperfom hand-crafted ones.
A formula in conjunctive normal form (CNF) can be represented as a bipartite graph, whose nodes are terms and variables,
which can be fed to a graph neural net (GNN).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep symbolic superoptimization\\ without human knowledge}\\
  H. Shi et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To simplify expressions, in a compiler, one can use reinforcement learning to select which rules to apply among a set of predefined rewrite rules.
To avoid the dependence on rules provided by humans, model the expressions as trees, 
compute node (or rather sub-tree) embeddings (with a tree LSTM), 
select a subtree (with a neural network),
decode it using a tree LSTM with attention,
replace the subtree with the new one and start again.
Train with Reinforce and curriculum learning with increasingly long expressions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CLN2INV: learning loop invariants\\ with continuous logic networks}\\
  G. Ryan et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Automated program verification struggles with loops: the loop invariants still have to be specified by humans -- once provided, the SMT solver can do its job.
One can use neural networks to guide the search for invariant formulas from recorded execution traces,
after relaxing logical formulas into continuous logic networks (CLN) (and progressively tightening the relaxation during the optimization).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Oblique decision trees\\ from derivatives of ReLU networks}\\
  G.H. Lee and T.S. Jaakkola (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
An \emph{oblique decision tree} is a decision tree whose decision nodes are linear classifiers (\ie, of the form $w'x < \alpha$ instead of $x_i < \alpha$).
Locally constant neural networks model oblique decision trees, in a parsimonious way ($N$ neurons suffice for $2^N$ nodes);
they can be obtained as the gradient of a ReLU network, further processed with another neural net.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PCMC-Net:\\ feature-based pairwise choice markov chains}\\
  Alix Lh\'eritier (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
Traditional \emph{choice models} (multinomial logit, random utility models) 
do not account for human idiosyncrasies:
independence of irrelevant alternatives, 
regularity (increasing the choice set does not change the ranking).
\emph{Pairwise choice Markov chain} (PCMC) models are more general: 
they use a Markov chain whose transition matrix models the preference between pairs of alternatives
and consider its stationary distribution.
If there are too many alternatives, use a neural net to approximate the transition matrix and compute the stationary distribution.
Application: airline itinerary choice 
%  gg: random utility models (RUM)
%  gg: Pairwise choice Markov chain (PCMC)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Higher-order function networks for learning composable 3D object representations}\\
  E. Mitchell et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Hypernetworks are neural networks computing the weights of another network. 
They can be used to design input-dependent networks,
for instance to apply a different decoder for each type of object when reconstructing it from a cloud of points.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural epitome search\\ for architecture-agnostic network compression}\\
  D. Zhou et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Hypernetwork to uncompress, on the fly, the weights of a neural network.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{You only train once:\\ loss-conditional training of deep networks}\\
  A. Dosovitskiy and J. Djolonga (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Hypernetworks can be used
to generate a family of image compression models, using
\[ \text{compression} + \lambda \cdot \text{reconstruction} \]
as loss, with the weights depending on $\lambda$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Continual learning with hypernetworks}\\
  J. von Oswald et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Hypernetworks can also be used
for continual learning, preventing catastrophic forgetting 
by computing an embedding of the task,
computing the new weights from that embedding,
with a constraint that the weights of the previous tasks to remain fixed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalization bounds\\ for deep convolutional neural networks}\\
  P.M. Long and H. Sedghi (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Neural networks may appear too expressive to lead to good generalization, 
but the training procedures do not stray far from the initialization, 
considerably reducing the risk of overfitting.
Increasing the number of parameters decreases the distance from initialization.
 
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to balance: Bayesian meta-learning for imbalanced and out-of-distribution tasks}\\
  H.B. Lee et al.   (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The goal of meta-learning, or ``learning to learn'', is to be able to learn a new task with little data (\emph{few-shot learning}) 
and only a few steps of gradient descent. 
MAML (model-agnostic meta learning) is one of the popular algorithms for this: 
it learns a good initialization, from which new tasks can be learned quickly.

To account to task imbalance, class imbalance and distribution shift, 
in meta-learning (MAML),
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use sample and task statistics to recognize these;
\item Feed them to a neural net to compute the step size and number of steps 
  (more and smaller steps for larger tasks -- the gradients are larger and there is more information to extract),
  and the shift to apply to the initial parameters (to account for distribution shift).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Meta dropout: learning to perturb\\ latent features for generalization}\\
  H.B. Lee et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To improve the decision boundaries in few-shot learning (MAML), 
learn input-dependent data augmentation (multiplicative noise, at each layer).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Empirical Bayes transductive meta-learning with synthetic gradients}\\
  S.X. Hu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
MAML learns a weight initialization that can be used for any new task.
Instead, leverage the unlabeled data of the new task to choose that initialization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rapid learning or feature reuse? towards understanding the effectiveness of MAML}\\
  A. Raghu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
MAML uses an outer loop to find a good initialization, 
and an inner loop, on a few samples, to adapt that initialization to the new task.
Comparing the representations learned across several tasks shows that only the last layer changes: MAML relies on feature reuse.
To speed things up, only train the last layer in the inner loop (ANIL).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Transferring optimality across data distributions via homotopy methods}\\
  M. Gargiani et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
In transfer learning, progressively deform the source task to the target task
(with a simple convex combination).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cross-domain few-shot classification\\ via learned feature-wise transformation}\\
  H.Y. Tseng et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
In few-shot classification, try to make the feature distribution, for the new task, more diverse, with a ``feature-wise transformation layer''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable and order-robust continual learning with additive parameter decomposition}\\
  J. Yoon et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
In continual learning, separate shared parameters from task-specific parameters.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sliced Cramer synaptic consolidation\\ for preserving deeply learned representations}\\
  S. Kolouri et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To prevent catastrophic forgetting in sequential learning, one could keep track of some of the inputs of the previous tasks, 
and add a penalty to ensure their latent representations are kept.
Instead, try to preserve the \emph{distribution} of those latent representations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An exponential learning rate schedule\\ for deep learning}\\
  Z. Li and S. Arora (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
There are countless learning rate schedules: decreasing, triangular, cosine, etc.
Even weirder ones work, such as an exponentially increasing one
(yes, increasing, not decreasing: multiply the learning rate by $1+c$, $c>0$, at each iteration) --
this assumes the presence of normalizing layers, which make the loss function scale-invariant.

The tapered exponential schedule has an exponential growth in each phase,
and a small drop at the end of each phase, before moving to a lower growth rate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Budgeted training: rethinking deep neural network training under resource constraints}\\
  M. Li et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
If your training budget is limited and known in advance, 
linearly decreasing the learning rate, down to zero, may be good enough.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rethinking the hyperparameters for fine-tuning}\\
  H. Li et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
What matters is not really the learning rate, but the \emph{effective learning rate}, $\eta/(1-m)$, which combines learning rate and momentum.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Revisiting self-training\\ for neural sequence generation}\\
  J. He et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Semi-supervised learning often works as follows: 
train a teacher network on real data; 
train a student network on data labeled by the teacher; 
fine-tune the student on real data; 
iterate (the student becomes teacher).

Dropout is important (noisy self-training).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Contrastive representation distillation}\\
  Y. Tian et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Distillation is similar, but instead of matching the output of the teacher network, \ie, the class labels, 
the student network tries to match the logits. 
Instead, one could try to match the features, one layer before, after mapping them to the same vector space (they have different dimensions),
using the cosine distance. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepHoyer: learning sparser\\ neural network with differentiable\\ scale-invariant sparsity measures}\\
  H. Yang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Instead of the lasso sparsifying regularizer, try the \d{Hoyer-square} regularizer
$ H(w) = \n{ w } _1^2 / \n{ w }_2^2 $ (or the group Hoyer).
It is differentiable (like $L^1$) and scale-invariant (like $L^0$);
it still trims small values, but preserves large ones.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pad\'e activation units: end-to-end learning\\ of flexible activation functions\\ in deep networks}\\
  A. Molina et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The activation functions (ReLU, sigmoid, tanh, etc.) are usually fixed.
Instead, we can choose a flexible parametrization, that allows a large variety of shapes, 
and learn the activation functions.
The \emph{Padé approximations}, \ie, rational functions (quotients of polynomials)
\[ g_\theta(x) = \dfrac{ \sum\limits_{i=0}^m \theta_i x^n }{ 1 + \va{ \sum\limits_{j=1}^n \theta_{m+j} x^j } } \]
are often used by computers in numerical computations (e.g., for special functions).
A random variant adds a small uniform noise to $\theta$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Effect of activation functions on the training of overparametrized neural nets}\\
  A. Panigrahi et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Most theoretical studies assume ReLU activations.
Experiments suggest that non-smooth activation functions (e.g., ReLU, ELU) lead to faster convergence (than swish, tanh).
The situation improves with depth.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Order learning\\ and its application to age estimation}\\
  K. Lim et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Here is another way of adding (robustness to) nonlinearities to a neural net: 
do not output an absolute value (age, distance, pitch, score, etc.), 
but compare inputs, with a siamese network;
at test time, the numeric value can be computed by interpolation.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (x) at (0,2) {$x$};
  \node (y) at (0,0) {$y$};
  \node[thick,draw,inner sep=4pt,fill=blue!40] (A) at (1,2) {};
  \node[thick,draw,inner sep=4pt,fill=blue!40] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[thick,draw,inner sep=4pt,fill=red!40] (C) at (2,1) {};
  \node[anchor=west] (o) at (3,1) {$\left\{\begin{array}{l}
    x \succ y \\
    x \approx y \\
    x \preccurlyeq y
    \end{array}\right.$};
  \draw[->] (x) -- (A);
  \draw[->] (y) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (A) -| (C);
  \draw[->] (B) -| (C);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (o);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
This approach can be combined with clustering, 
if there are distinct groups in the input requiring different models: 
train separate models for random clusters, update cluster membership from the model performance, iterate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometric analysis\\ of nonconvex optimization landscapes\\ for overcomplete learning}\\
  Q. Qu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Overcomplete dictionary learning looks for $A \in \RR^{n\times m}$ and $X \in \RR^{m\times p}$ such that $Y = AX$.  
Since the problem has many symmetries, it is non-convex, but if all minima are equivalent, and if the saddle points are not flat, it can be solved efficiently.
We can require $A$ to be row-orthonormal, and column-near-orthogonal, 
\ie, impose an upper bound on the absolute value of the correlation (\ie, cosine) of any two columns.
To get one column of A, solve 
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} &           q \in \SS^{n-1} \\
  \text{To minimize} &    -\n{ q' Y }_4^4
\end{array} \]
(in the over-complete case, use the spikiness, $L^4$, rather than $L^1$).

With pre-conditioning, this can be generalized to \emph{convolutional dictionary learning} ($A$ is no longer near-orthogonal):
$ y_i = \sum_k a_k \oast x_{ki} $,
where $y_i, a_k, x_{ki} \in \RR^n$, 
with $x_{ki}$ sparse 

Examples include phase retrieval, low-rank matrix recovery, phase synchronization, shallow/linear neural net, sparse blind deconvolution, etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Short and sparse deconvolution:\\a geometric approach}\\
  Y. Lau et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding l$^4$-based dictionary learning: interpretation, stability, and robustness}\\
  Y. Zhai et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The $L^4$ norm, or ``spikiness'', can be used instead of the $L^2$ or $L^1$ norms,
for instance, for dictionary learning:
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & A \in O_n \\
  \text{To maximize} & \n{ A Y }_4^4
\end{array}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A closer look at the optimization landscapes\\ of generative adversarial networks}\\
  H. Berard et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
%GAN training, rotational behaviour around equilibria.

Consider the cosine similarity between 
the \emph{game vector field} $v$, \ie, the concatenation of the gradients of the generator and discriminator,
and the linear path $(\theta_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$ between the initial and final parameters. 
\[ c_t = \cos( \theta_1 - \theta_0, v(\theta_t) ) \]
Plotting $c_t$ (and $\n{ v(\theta_t) }$) for $t \in [0.5, 1.5]$ 
reveals two possible behaviours: attraction (decreasing) and rotation (bump at 1).

\[
<<GameVectorField,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=4,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
g <- function(f = function(x,y) c(-x,-y) ) { 
  s <- .2
  xs <- seq(-1,1,length=10)
  ys <- seq(-1,1,length=10)
  ts <- seq(0,2,length=10)
  par( mar = c(0,0,0,0)+1 )
  plot( NA, xlim = range(xs), ylim = range(ys), axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
  for( i in seq_along(xs) ) {
    for( j in seq_along(ys) ) {
      v <- f(xs[i], ys[j])
      arrows( xs[i], ys[j], xs[i] + s * v[1], ys[j] + s * v[2], length=.06, angle=20, lwd = 1, col = "grey" )
    }
  }
  
  A <- c(-.3,-1)
  B <- c(.2,.1)
  # Replace B with the projection of O on (AB)
  lambda <- - sum( A * (B-A) ) / sum( (B-A)^2 )
  B <- A + lambda * (B-A)
  
  for( i in seq_along(ts) ) {
    u <- ts[i]
    M <- A + u * (B-A)
    v <- f(M[1], M[2])
    v <- v / sqrt(sum(v^2))
    arrows(M[1],M[2], M[1]+s*v[1], M[2]+s*v[2], length=.06, angle=20, lwd=2)
  }
  M1 <- A + (-2)*(B-A)
  M2 <- A + 5*(B-A)
  segments( M1[1], M1[2], M2[1], M2[2], lwd = 2, col = 'grey50' )
  box()

  ts <- seq(0,2,length=100)
  zs <- NA * ts
  d <- B-A
  d <- d / sqrt(sum(d^2))
  for( i in seq_along(ts) ) {
    u <- ts[i]
    M <- A + u * (B-A)
    v <- f(M[1], M[2])
    v <- v / sqrt(sum(v^2))  
    #zs[i] <- atan2( v[2], v[1] ) - atan2( d[2], d[1] )
    zs[i] <- sum( v * d )
  }

  par( mar = c(3,4,1,1) )
  #plot( ts, (zs+pi) %% (2*pi), type = "l", lwd = 3, col = 'dark blue' )
  plot( ts, zs, type = "l", lwd = 3, col = 'dark blue', xlab = "", ylab = "Cosine", ylim = c(-1,1) )
  abline( h = 0, v = 1, lty = 2, lwd = 1, col = 'grey' )
  lines( ts, zs, type = "l", lwd = 3, col = 'dark blue' )
}
par( mfcol=c(2,3), las = 1 )
g()
g( function(x,y) c( -y, x ) )
g( function(x,y) .6 * c( -x-y, -y+x ) )
@
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An inductive bias for distances: neural nets that respect the triangle inequality}\\
  S. Pitis et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
When learning a distance function, we want it to satisfy the triangle inequality.
Deep metric learning often learns an embedding and then computes the Euclidean distance in that latent space -- 
but not all metrics can be computed in that way.

A \emph{DeepNorm} network is
\begin{align*}
  h_i &= g_i( W_i h_{i-1} + U_i x ) \\
  \n x &= h_k
\end{align*}
where $h_0=0$, $W_1=0$, the $W_i$ are non-negative,
$g$ is convex and positive homogeneous, e.g.,
\begin{align*}
  g(u) &= \alpha \text{relu}(u) + \beta u \\
  \text{maxReLU}(x,y) &= [ \text{max}(x,y), \alpha \text{relu}(x) + \beta \text{relu}(y) ]
\end{align*}
where $\alpha,\beta\geq0$.

\emph{Wide Mahalanobis norms} are of the form
\begin{align*}
  \n x &= \operatorname*{MaxMean}_i ( \n{ W_i x } _2 ) \\
  \operatorname{MaxMean}(x_1\dots,x_n) &= \alpha \max(x_1,\dots,x_n) + \\&\hphantom{{}={}}(1-\alpha) \operatorname{mean}(x_1,\dots,x_n).
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep batch active learning by diverse, uncertain gradient lower bounds}\\
  J.T. Ash et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Active learning approaches can be uncertainty-based (for small batches) or diversity-based (for larger batches).
They can be combined: 
use the gradient of the loss (for the most likely label) wrt the parameters as a representation of the unlabeled examples,
and sample using a determinantal point process (DPP) or (less expensive) $k$-means++.  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Accelerating SGD with momentum\\ for over-parameterized learning}\\
  C. Liu and M. Belkin (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Nesterov momentum provably accelerates (full) gradient descent, but not stochastic gradient descent.
Instead, try
\begin{align*}
  w_{t+1} &\leftarrow u_t - \eta_1 \nabla f(u_t)  \\
  u_{t+1} &\leftarrow (1+\gamma) w_{t+1} - \gamma w_t + \eta_2 \nabla f(u_t)        
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Variance reduction with sparse gradients}\\
  M. Elibol et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The SpiderBoost gradient,
\[ g_{n+1} = g_n + \nabla f_n( \theta_n ) - \nabla f_n(\theta_{n-1}), \]
where $f_n$ is the loss on the $n$th batch,
can be approximated with sparse updates, only computing a random subset of the coordinates with the largest variance
(sparse forward and backward passes).
          
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Coherent gradients: an approach to understanding generalization in gradient descent-based optimization}\\
  S. Chatterjee (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Models trained with gradient descent (GD) generalize well (even though they could just memorize their input)
because strong gradient directions (common to many observations) are kept and weak gradients cancel out.
It is possible to perturb GD to suppress weak gradients (cf. random forests).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic weight averaging in parallel: large-batch training that generalizes well}\\
  V. Gupta et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Start training with large batches, but stop early;
then, train on small batches, in parallel;
finally average the weights.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Why not to use zero imputation? correcting sparsity bias in training neural networks}\\
  J. Yi et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Replacing missing values with 0 changes the scale of the output of the first layer;
rescale the input accordingly (as with dropout).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ProxSGD:\\ training structured neural networks\\ under regularization and constraints}\\
  Y. Yang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Although the $L^1$ norm is a sparsifying constraint, SGD cannot push the weights to exactly zero -- 
it cannot enforce hard constraints.
Replace the gradient step
\begin{align*}
  g &\leftarrow \nabla _{\!x} f(x) + r(x)  \\
  \hat x &\leftarrow \hat x - \alpha g
\end{align*}
with a proximal step
\begin{align*}
  g &\leftarrow \nabla_{\!x} f(x) \\
  \hat x &\leftarrow \argmin_{ x \in X } ( x - \hat x ) ' g + \tfrac12 \n{ x - \hat x }^2 + r(x)
\end{align*}
When the penalty $r$ is the $L^1$ norm, the proximal operator is soft-thresholding.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identity crisis:\\ memorization and generalization\\ under extreme overparameterization}\\
  C. Zhang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To study how overparametrized models generalize, look at extreme situations,
such as learning with just one sample (learning the constant map)
and learning the identity map.
Shallow networks are better at learning the identity map, deeper networks are better at learning the constant map.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep double descent:\\ where bigger models and more data hurt}\\
  P. Nakkiran et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The \emph{double descent} phenomenon is visible for:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item test error vs model size;
\item test error vs epoch.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gradient descent maximizes\\ the margin of homogeneous neural networks}\\
  K. Lyu and J. Li (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Deep neural nets are too expressive and should overfit.
They work because gradient methods have an implicit bias towards maximum-margin solutions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unbiased contrastive divergence\\ algorithm for training\\ energy-based latent variable models}\\
  Y. Qiu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
%  [interesting, but not clear. to read]
Contrastive divergence (CD), used to train energy-based models (EBM) such as restricted Boltzman machines (RBM),
uses a biased gradient estimator (a small, fixed, number of MCMC steps) and may diverge. 
\emph{Unbiased MCMC} can provide an unbiased gradient estimator (by using a random number of MCMC steps).
%  gg: ``unbiased MCMC''
%  We want to estimate $\mu = \lim_{ n \to \infty } E[ X_k ]$. 
%  Write it as a telescoping term.
%  Throw in a random time, and another sequence of r.v. with the same marginals (???)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mixout: effective regularization to finetune large-scale pretrained language models}\\
  C. Lee et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Drop-connect sets some of the weights to zero.
Instead, set them to the weights of a pre-trained network.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Self-supervised learning of appliance usage}\\
  C.Y. Hsu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To detect causality in weakly-related data streams,
such as total energy consumption of a home over time,
and people's location in the home (approximate, from Wifi perturbations),
try to predict one from the other (the cause from the consequence: location given energy event):
when prediction is possible, the two are related.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SELF: learning to filter noisy labels\\ with self-ensembling}\\
  D.T. Nguyen et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
With low-quality (crowd-sourced) training data, some of the labels may be incorrect.
Only train on reliable labels: to identify they, compare the predictions of the current model
and that a few epochs ago: more reliable labels have more stable predictions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DivideMix: learning with noisy labels\\ as semi-supervised learning}\\
  J. Li et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To identify noisy observations, one can model that noise: 
the per-sample loss is then a two-component mixture (noisy samples have a larger loss).
One can then discard the noisy labels and use semi-supervised learning. 
To avoid confirmation bias, train two networks, each filtering the other's noise.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Simple and effective regularization methods for training on noisily labeled data with generalization guarantee}\\
  W. Hu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To deal with label noise,
one could add a penalty for the distance to the initialization parameters,
or actually model the noise by adding trainable auxiliary variables.
For a wide neural net, these are equivalent to kernel \emph{ridge} regression with a neural tangent kernel.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{AugMix: a simple data processing method\\ to improve robustness and uncertainty}\\
  D. Hendrycks et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Traditional data augmentation applies one transformation to the image. 
Instead, apply several, and mix the results (with a weighted DAG); 
add a consistency penalty to the loss to ensure that different augmentations yield the same result.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ReMixMatch: semi-supervised learning\\ with distribution matching\\ and augmentation anchoring}\\
  D. Berthelot et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
MixMatch performs semi-supervised learning
by training on the labeled data, 
running the model on many augmentations of the unlabeled data, 
and using the resulting label distribution.
ReMixMatch
enforces consistency among augmentations,
makes the label distribution close to that of the training set,
and adds a self-supervised loss (rotation prediction).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust training with ensemble consensus}\\
  J. Lee and S.Y. Chung (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To make a network more robust, we can train it on the $(100-e)$\% observations with the smallest loss
(as with a truncated regression).

Identify noisy examples among those small-loss examples
by perturbing the network: if an image was not noisy (learned by generalization), the prediction does not change,
but if it was noisy (learned by memorization), it changes.

An ensemble of neural nets, 
obtained by adding noise, at test time, to the input or to the network (e.g., with droupout), 
or by taking the same network from previous epochs (a ``self-ensemble'')
is more robust than a single network. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Self-labelling via simultaneous clustering\\ and representation learning}\\
  Y.M. Asano et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Jointly learn a representation and a clustering (or labeling) of the data,
with a constraint that each label be used the same number of times,
by iteratively computing the optimal labeling (optimal transport)
and training a classifier.

Use data augmentation.
You can learn several clusterings (classification heads -- cf. DeepCluster) at the same time.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RaPP: novelty detection\\ with reconstruction along projection pathway}\\
  K.H. Kim et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Autoencoders can be used to detect anomalies: their reconstruction error is large. 
Do not only compare the input and the output of the network, feed the output back to the input, 
and also compare the intermediate layers: if the autoencoder is
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\node (x0) at (0,1) {$x$};
\node (x1) at (1,1) {};
\node (x2) at (2,1) {};
\node (x3) at (3,1) {};
\node (x4) at (4,1) {};
\node (x5) at (5,1) {};
\node (x6) at (6,1) {$\smash{\hat x}\vphantom{x}$};
\node (y0) at (0,0) {$\smash{\hat x}\vphantom{x}$};
\node (y1) at (1,0) {};
\node (y2) at (2,0) {};
\node (y3) at (3,0) {};
\node (y4) at (4,0) {};
\node (y5) at (5,0) {};
\node (y6) at (6,0) {$\smash{\Hat{\Hat x}}\vphantom{x}$};
\draw[->] (x0) to node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize$g_1$} (x1);
\draw[->] (x1) to node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize$g_2$} (x2);
\draw[->] (x2) to node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize$g_3$} (x3);
\draw[->] (x3) to node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize$f_3$} (x4);
\draw[->] (x4) to node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize$f_2$} (x5);
\draw[->] (x5) to node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize$f_1$} (x6);

\draw[->] (y0) to node[anchor=north]{\footnotesize$g_1$} (y1);
\draw[->] (y1) to node[anchor=north]{\footnotesize$g_2$} (y2);
\draw[->] (y2) to node[anchor=north]{\footnotesize$g_3$} (y3);
\draw[->] (y3) to node[anchor=north]{\footnotesize$f_3$} (y4);
\draw[->] (y4) to node[anchor=north]{\footnotesize$f_2$} (y5);
\draw[->] (y5) to node[anchor=north]{\footnotesize$f_1$} (y6);

\draw[<->,very thick,dotted,gray] (x0) -- (y0);
\draw[<->,very thick,dotted,gray] (x1) -- (y1);
\draw[<->,very thick,dotted,gray] (x2) -- (y2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
also compare 
$g_1(x)$     and $g_1(\hat x)$,
$g_2(g_1(x))$ and $g_2(g_1(\hat x))$
etc.
            
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust subspace recovery layer\\for unsupervised anomaly detection}\\
  C.H. Lai et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Auto-encoders can be used for anomaly detection: the reconstruction error is larger than usual.
But auto-encoders are not robust to outliers:
add a \emph{robust subspace recovery} layer
$ z \mapsto Az $ between the encoder and the decoder, 
which projects to an even lower-dimensional subspace, 
with $\n{AA'-I}$ and $\n{z-A'Az}_2$ as penalties. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Classification-based anomaly detection\\ for general data}\\
  L. Bergman and Y. Hoshen (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
A similar idea can be applied to many other models: for out-of-distribution samples, 
the error is often larger. For instance, GOAD picks $M$ random affine transformations, 
transforms the data with them, and tries to recover which transformation was used: 
the prediction accuracy is an anomaly score.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Input complexity\\ and out-of-distribution detection\\ with likelihood-based generative models}\\
  J. Serr\`a et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Likelihood can be used to identify out-of-distribution datasets -- 
but the distribution of those likelihoods can be higher than for the training set (it is easier), or lower (more difficult).
The likelihood is correlated with the complexity of the data (Kolmogorov complexity, approximated by a lossless compression algorithm).
Compensate for this relation: 
\[ S(x) = - \text{LogLik}(x) - \text{Complexity}(x). \]
This can be interpreted as a likelihood ratio test.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Novelty detection via blurring}\\
  S. Choi and S.Y. Chung (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Random network distillation (RND) takes two networks: 
a randomly-initialized, fixed network $g$,
and a network $f$ trained to replicate $g$ on the training data.
Since they only match on the training data, 
their difference  $\n{f(x) - g(x)}$ is larger for out-of-distribution samples.
Blurring the data (RND trained on both raw and blurred data) helps.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{White noise analysis of neural networks}\\
  A. Borji and S. Lin (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Titration (progressively adding noise to images and checking the output, or the confusion matrix) can help understand classification biases

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Feature interaction interpretability: a case\\ for explaining ad-recommendation systems\\ via neural interaction detection}\\
  M. Tsang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Detect interactions via feature perturbation, and use them for sparse feature crossing.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting extrapolation with local ensembles}\\
  D. Madras et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To see if a model is extrapolating (and if the forecasts are unreliable), train several models (with the same structure) and check if they agree.
This can be done with a single model as well: at the local minimum, 
changing the weights in the direction of the smallest eigenvalues of the Hessian of the loss does not change the loss much, 
and provides a ``self-ensemble'' of models. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conservative uncertainty estimation\\ by fitting prior networks}\\
  K. Ciosek et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To estimate the uncertainty of a neural network, train several networks, with different random initializations, and check if they match.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards neural networks\\ that provably know when they don't know}\\
  A. Meinke and M. Hein (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Our models do not really model $P[y|x]$, 
but $P[y|x,\text{in-distribution}]$.
We can actually estimate $P[y|x]$ with Bayes's formula.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantifying point-prediction uncertainty\\ in neural networks\\ via residual estimation with an i/o kernel}\\
  X. Qiu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Given a trained neural net, train another neural net to predict the uncertainty of the forecasts
% with a (SV)GP (?)
\[ (x,\hat y) \mapsto \n{ y - \hat y} ^2. \] 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distance-based learning from errors\\ for confidence calibration}\\
  C. Xing et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To estimate the uncertainty of a prediction from a classifier, 
cluster the observations in each class, and use the distance to the cluster center.
Use a neural network to approximate those uncertainty estimates.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ensemble distribution distillation}\\
  A. Malinin et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
%  [interesting] Already read? I remember making the same plots...
There are two types of uncertainty:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Data uncertainty: the classes overlap, or we are too close to the decision boundary;
\item Knowledge uncertainty: out-of-distribution observation, \ie, we are too far from the training data.
\end{compactitem}

\[
<<UncertaintyTypes,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=4,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
f <- function() {
  set.seed(1)
  n <- 200
  s <- .5
  xy <- matrix( s * rnorm(2*n), nc = 2 )
  A <- c(0,1)
  B <- c(-1/sqrt(2),-.5)
  C <- c(+1/sqrt(2),-.5)
  par( mar = c(0,0,0,0)+1 )
  plot( NA, xlim = 2*c(-1,1), ylim = 2*c(-1,1), axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
  points( t(A + t(xy)), col = '#FF444444', cex = 1.5, pch = 16 )
  points( t(B + t(xy)), col = '#44FF4444', cex = 1.5, pch = 16 )
  points( t(C + t(xy)), col = '#4444FF44', cex = 1.5, pch = 16 )
}

par( mfrow=c(2,3) )
f()
points( -1/sqrt(2), -.5, pch = 4, cex = 5, lwd = 10 )

f()
points( 0, 0, pch = 4, cex = 5, lwd = 10 )

f()
points( -1.5, 1.5, pch = 4, cex = 5, lwd = 10 )

ternary_plot <- function(x) {
  A <- c(0,1)
  B <- c(-1/sqrt(2),-.5)
  C <- c(+1/sqrt(2),-.5)
  xy <- cbind(
    x[,1] * A[1] + x[,2] * B[1] + x[,3] * C[1],
    x[,1] * A[2] + x[,2] * B[2] + x[,3] * C[2]
  )
  xy <- x %*% rbind(A,B,C)
  plot( rbind(A,B,C,A), type = "l", axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "", asp=1 )
  points(xy, pch = 16, col = "#00000022", cex = 2)  
}

ternary_plot( MCMCpack::rdirichlet(100,c(1,30,1)))

# Data uncertainty
ternary_plot( MCMCpack::rdirichlet(100,30*c(1,1,1)) )

# Knowledge uncertainty
ternary_plot( MCMCpack::rdirichlet(100,c(1,1,1)) )
@
\]

The situation can be estimated from an ensemble of models:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item If the models are all unsure, we are close to the decision boundary;
\item If the models are sure but disagree, the data is out-of-distribution;
\item If the models are sure and agree, that is fine.
\end{compactitem}

Do not distill a single teacher model into a student one, 
nor the mean of an ensemble of teacher models, 
but both mean and diversity of the ensemble, using Dirichlet distributions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A constructive prediction of the generalization error across scales}\\
  J.S. Rosenfeld et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Predict the generalization error by fitting a model
\[ \text{test error} \sim \log( \#\text{samples} ) + \log( \#\text{parameters} ). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pitfalls of in-domain uncertainty estimation and ensembling in deep learning}\\
  A. Ashukha et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Before comparing (ensembles of) models (with the log-likelihood, or the Brier score), ensure the models are calibrated.
To assess an ensembling technique (which may produce dependent models), compute the equivalent number of independent models
(for instance, an ensemble of 100 dropout variants of a models could be equivalent to 2 independently-trained models).
Test-time augmentation works well.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unrestricted adversarial examples\\ via semantic manipulation}\\
  A. Bhattad et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Computer vision neural networks can be trained to be robust to adversarial attacks, 
in the sense that images close to the input will give the same output, where closeness 
is measured with the $L^\infty$ or $L^2$ norm. 

But this leaves enough room for photo-realistic attacks, 
manipulating colour and texture or adding barely perceptible shadows. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Breaking certified defenses:\\ semantic adversarial examples\\ with spoofed robustness certificates}\\
  A. Ghiasi et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The robustness certification radius is not a good measure of robustness:
we can find attacks that look like natural images, get misclassified, but have a large certification radius.
The shadow attack changes the luminosity of each pixel, as if there were a shadow on the image;
add a penalty to make it smooth (no edges), and another to keep the change small.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A target-agnostic attack on deep models: exploiting security vulnerabilities\\ of transfer learning}\\
  S. Rezaei and X. Liu (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Many models are built from publically-available pre-trained networks: only the head of the network is unknown to the attacker.
Inputs with a single very large activation provide good adversarial attacks. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Transferable perturbations\\ of deep feature distributions}\\
  N. Inkawhich et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
Using the latent feature distributions, from the intermediate layers, leads to more transferable attacks. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Thieves on sesame street!\\ model extraction of BERT-based APIs}\\
  K. Krishna et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
If a model, trained by fine-tuning a publically-available pre-trained model (e.g., BERT -- 
the availability of the pre-trained model is important) on proprietary data,
has a public API (e.g., Google Translate), it is (economically) possible to extract it, 
by feeding it data (e.g., random words, with no grammatical structure, or Wikipedia excerpts), 
collecting the results, and training a new model. 
This model stealing attack allows intellectual property theft, (white-box) adversarial attacks, and data leakage.

Defences include 
  API watermarking (for 0.1\% of the queries, return an incorrect answer and store the input/answer pair)
  and membership classification (recognize out-of-distribution inputs and return random data),
but they only work on naive adversaries. 
%            [Example tasks: sentiment classification (SST2), natural language inference (NLI), question answering (SQuQD, BoolQ)
%             The availability of the pre-trained model is important.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Prediction poisoning: towards defenses\\ against DNN model stealing attacks}\\
  T. Orekondy et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To protect a trained model $ f : x \mapsto y $ against stealing attacks, try to perturb the attacker's gradient:
do not return $ y = f(x) $, but $y+\delta$,
where $\delta$ is small, $\va \delta < \epsilon$
and maximizes the angle between the gradients $\nabla \text{Loss}(y)$ and $\nabla \text{Loss}(y+\delta)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{EMPIR: ensembles of mixed precision\\ deep networks for increased robustness\\ against adversarial attacks}\\
  S. Sen et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Low-precision networks are less accurate but more robust to adversarial attacks than full-precision ones: 
use an ensemble of both.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Enhancing transformation-based defenses against adversarial attacks\\ with a distribution classifier}\\
  C. Kou et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
To defend against adversarial attacks, one can use data augmentation at test time, 
\ie, feed several altered images to the classifier, and use a majority vote -- 
but accuracy degrades.
Instead, train the model that will actually be used, the model including the data augmentation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Defending against physically realizable attacks on image classification}\\
  T. Wu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
Training against rectangular occlusion attacks improves robustness to physically realizable attacks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Skip connections matter: on the transferability of adversarial examples generated with resnets}\\
  D. Wu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Replace the skip connection $ z_1 = z_0 + f(x_0) $ 
gradient $1+f'$ with $1+\gamma f'$, $\gamma<1$:
the resulting network is more robust to transferred (black-box) adversarial attacks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adversarial Lipschitz regularization}\\
  D. Terjék (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Lipschitz regularization is usually implemented with a gradient penalty.
The Lipschitz regularization,
$\E[ \va{f(y)-f(x)} / \va{y-x} ]$,
tends to diverge when estimated using pairs of points from a minibatch:
instead, use adversarial examples, $y=x+r$, $\va r \leq \epsilon$
(one could also use a gradient penalty, as in WGANs).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Consistency regularization\\ for generative adversarial networks}\\
  H. Zhang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
The gradient penalty (Lipschitz) adds a computational overhead.
Use \emph{consistency regularization} (as in semi-supervised learning -- deformed images should have the same output)
on the discriminator instead of the Lipschitz constraint (gradient penalization, spectral normalization) (CR-GAN)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Intriguing properties\\ of adversarial training at scale}\\
  C. Xie et A. Yuille (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Removing clean images from the training set makes the model more robust to adversarial attacks.
Clean images and adversarial examples seem to come from different distributions:
in the BatchNorm layers, use different statistics for the clean and adversarial inputs (mixture batch normalization),
or use a non-batch normalization (e.g., group norm).

Deeper networks are more robust.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Universal approximation\\ with certified networks}\\
  M. Baader et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
  
%  Certified Defenses for Adversarial Patches
%  P.Y. Chiang et al. (ICLR 2020)
%  https://openreview.net/forum?id=HyeaSkrYPH

%  Towards Stable and Efficient Training of Verifiably Robust Neural Networks
%  H. Zhang et al. (ICLR 2020)
%  https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.06316

\emph{Interval analytics} can provide $\ell^\infty$ robustness certification (robustness to $\ell^\infty$ adversarial examples) but, 
for arbitrarily-trained networks, the intervals are often too large.
However, for any function, there exists a ReLU network approximating it and whose $\ell^\infty$ robustness can be proved by interval analysis.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adversarially robust representations\\ with smooth encoders}\\
  T. Cemgil et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
VAEs are not robust to adversarial perturbations;
% [striking example -- is it real?];
the encoder is to blame: train it adversarially.
The adversarial training searches for an input image, close to the initial input, 
but with latent representation as far away (for the Wasserstein distance) as possible.
To check that the output is also robust, use a classifier (detecting the presence of glasses, of a smile, etc.) and try to change the predicted class.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sign bits are all you need for black-box attacks}\\
  A. Al-Dujaili and U.M. O'Reilly (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Most attacks require an estimate of the gradient of the network wrt to its input, which requires $O(n)$ queries. 
Looking for the sign of the gradient (an element of $\{\pm1\}^n$ instead of $\RR^n$) still requires $O(n)$ queries, 
but an adversarial attack is often found much earlier.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sign-OPT:\\ a query-efficient hard-label adversarial attack }\\
  M. Cheng et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding the limitations\\ of conditional generative models}\\
  E. Fetaya et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Discriminative models are not robust to outliers and adversarial attacks.
Generative models do not necessarily fare better:
with MNIST digits on CIFAR background,
images interpolated between digits have a higher log-likelihood 
(because there is more content in the background, more entropy unrelated to the class).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data-independent neural pruning via coresets}\\
  B. Mussay et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Prune the weights of a neural network by
keeping those with the highest importance (if there are several outputs, take the maximum of the importances)
and then fine-tuning.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Provable filter pruning\\ for efficient neural networks}\\
  L. Liebenwein et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Pruning is usually empirical (no guarantees), and only looks at the weights, not the data.
Instead, look at the contributions of a neuron to the activations of the next layer.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lookahead: a far-sighted alternative\\ of magnitude-based pruning}\\
  S. Park et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Magnitude-based pruning (discarding low weights) does not take into account the connectivity of the network: 
if a large-weight edge is only connected to small-weight edges, it may not be that important, and could also be discarded --
taking into account the previous and next layer can change which edges seem important:
prune weights that are small and/or connected to small weights.
More formally, do not prune $W_k$ into $\tilde W_k$ 
to minimize     $\|         W_k x         -         \tilde W_k x         \|_2$
but to minimize $\| W_{k+1} W_k W_{k-1} x - W_{k+1} \tilde W_k W_{k-1} x \|_2$.    

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Comparing rewinding and fine-tuning\\ in neural network pruning}\\
  A. Renda et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
After pruning a network, one usually fine-tunes it,
but, since the network is now different, the optimal hyperparameters are no longer the same: 
they have to be re-estimated.
\emph{Rewinding} the weights (\ie, using old weights) or just the larning rate, before fine-tuning, 
works as well as hyperparameter tuning.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The intriguing role of module criticality\\ in the generalization of deep networks}\\
  N.S. Chatterji et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Rewind a layer (only one) back to its initialization:
the impact is sometimes visible, sometimes not.
This suggests that some layers are more important than others;
this can be explained by the loss landscape, 
the path from the initialization to the final weights, 
which looks like a tunnel for some layers (less important), or like a funnel (more important).
The width of that funnel (network criticality) is related to generalization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic model pruning with feedback}\\
  T. Lin et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Pruning can be done before training, after training, several times during training, 
or dynamically during training, potentially reactivating prematurely pruned weights).
This can be done with a binary mask on the weights (use the mask in the forward pass, but not in the backward pass; the mask can change at each step)
or a threshold vector (layer-wise, filter-wise or neuron-wise).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic sparse training:\\ find efficient sparse network from scratch\\ with trainable masked layers}\\
  J. Liu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Instead of training, pruning and fine-tuning a network, 
learn both the weights and a threshold vector (layer-wise, filter-wise or neuron-wise).
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Signal propagation perspective for pruning neural networks at initialization}\\
  N. Lee et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Surprisingly, it is possible to prune the network before training, 
by looking at the change in loss when removing a parameter,
which can be computed as a gradient,
$ \nabla_{\!c} \text{Loss}( c \odot w ) $
evaluated at $c=\mathbf 1$, where $c \in \{0,1\}^m$ is the mask.
This works better if the singular values of the weights matrices of each layer 
(strictly speaking, the singular values of the jacobian of each layer) are close to 1.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{One-shot pruning of recurrent neural networks by jacobian spectrum evaluation}\\
  S. Zhang and B.C. Stadie (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
For efficient backward/forward propagation in a RNN, we want the eigenvalues of 
$J_t = \partial h_t / \partial h_{t+1}$ to be close to 1,
but they are initially small: 
only keep the weights likely to help maximize $\chi = \sum_t \E \n{ J_t }_F$, 
\ie, those with the largest value of $\va{ \partial _\theta \chi }$ (set the others to 0, permanently).
(the weights are pruned before training, the first time they see the data: it is a sparse initialization)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Batch-shaping for learning\\ conditional channel gated networks}\\
  B.E. Bejnordi et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To reduce the energy consumption of a neural net, 
only use part of it,
with a gating mechanism to choose which features to compute.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Depth-adaptive transformer}\\
  M. Elbayad et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Sequence-to-sequence models have more and more parameters; they can be shrunk by distillation or structured pruning.
Instead, reduce the model in a sample-specific way: add exits at each level of the decoder, and a halting mechanism.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Triple wins: boosting\\ accuracy, robustness and efficiency together\\ by enabling input-adaptive inference}\\
  T.K. Hu (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Multi-output networks seem more robust to adversarial attacks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gradient $L^1$ regularization\\ for quantization robustness}\\
  M. Alizadeh et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Neural networks can be trained to be more robust to post-training quantization
(a bounded, additive perturbation)
by adding an $L^1$ penalty for the gradient of the loss (wrt the weights).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust learning with jacobian regularization}\\
  J. Hoffman et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Additive powers-of-two quantization:\\ an efficient non-uniform discretization\\ for neural networks}\\
  Y. Li et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Quantization (after clipping) can be uniform (write the number with $k$ bits), power-of-two (less precision for larger numbers),
or sum of powers of two, $q = 2^x + 2^y + 2^z$ (with $k$ terms).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learned step size quantization}\\
  S.K. Esser et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To learn layer-specific quantization, with gradient descent,
use the floor function in the forward pass, and relax it to the identity in the backward pass
(using a different function in the forward and backward pass, to deal with locally constant functions, is a common trick).
Rescale the step size with the size of each bin.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mixed precision DNNs:\\ all you need is a good parametrization}\\
  S. Uhlich et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
In mixed precision DNN, the bitwidth is different for each layer, for the weights and the activations. 
For the uniform and pow-2 quantization schemes, do not directly estimate the bitwidth: parametrize the quantization with the maximum and the stepsize
(the gradients are better-behaved).
To train the model, add a penalty for the total memory required for the weights and activations; 
for the backward pass, set the derivative of the floor function to 1.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Precision gating:\\ improving neural network efficiency\\ with dynamic dual-precision activations}\\
  Y. Zhang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Dynamic quantization computes most features in low precision and a few (input-dependent) important ones in high precision,
using a gating mechanism (precision gating).  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BinaryDuo: reducing gradient mismatch\\ in binary activation network\\ by coupling binary activations}\\
  H. Kim et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Binary neural nets, \ie, neural nets whose weights and activations are $\pm1$,
are difficult to train. Approaches include coupling them with a higher-precision network,
or using a surrogate, such as a stochastic binary neural net. 
To reduce the gradient mismatch problem: train coupled binary and ternary models; then decouple them and fine-tune the binary one.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Critical initialisation in continuous approximations of binary neural networks}\\
  G. Stamatescu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Train a continuous surrogate for the binary neural net: a stochastic binary neural net.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Kaleidoscope: an efficient, learnable representation for all structured linear maps}\\
  T. Dao et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To reduce the number of parameters in a neural net, one can use structured matrices 
instead of arbitrary, dense matrices: low-rank, sparse, Kronecker-factored, DFT, etc.
Those matrices are products of sparse matrices, 
and they can be obtained from \d{butterfly matrices} (block matrices, whose blocks are diagonal);
the construction encodes a recursive divide-and-conquer structure.
The sparsity pattern is fixed, and learnable with gradient descent.

\[
<<BufferflyMatrices,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=10,fig.height=2,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
library(Matrix)
bf <- function(k) {
  B <- diag(k)
  rbind( cbind(B,B), cbind(B,B) )
}
b <- function(a,b) {
  B <- bf(a)
  B <- Matrix::bdiag( rep( list(B), b ) )
  B <- as.matrix(B)
  B
}
f <- function(x) {
  DBQR::myimage(0:32, 0:32, x[32:1,], axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "")
  abline( v = 0:32, h = 0:32, col = "grey" )
}
par( mar = .5 * c(1,1,1,1), mfrow=c(1,5) )
f( b(1,16) )
f( b(2,8) )
f( b(4,4) )
f( b(8,2) )
f( b(16,1) )
@
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Reformer: the efficient transformer}\\
  N. Kitaev et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The reformer is a memory-efficient variant of the transformer, 
using \d{reversible residual} layers (there is no longer any need to keep all the activations in memory),
and locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) to locate and compute the largest elements of softmax($Q'K$) (which is almost sparse).

% Reversible residual network
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.7,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\node (x2) at (0,2) {$x_2$};
\node (x1) at (0,0) {$x_1$};
\node[outer sep=0pt, inner sep=0pt] (A) at (1,2) {\small$\bullet$};
\node[draw] (F) at (2,1) {$F$};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (P1) at (3,0) {\footnotesize$+$};
\node[outer sep=0pt, inner sep=0pt] (B) at (4,0) {\small$\bullet$};
\node[draw] (G) at (5,1) {$G$};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (P2) at (6,2) {\footnotesize$+$};
\node (y2) at (7,2) {$y_2$};
\node (y1) at (7,0) {$y_1$};
\draw[->] (x2) -- (P2);
\draw[->] (P2) -- (y2);
\draw[->] (x1) -- (P1);
\draw[->] (P1) -- (y1);
\draw[->] (A) -- (F);
\draw[->] (F) -- (P1);
\draw[->] (B) -- (G);
\draw[->] (G) -- (P2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lite transformer\\ with long-short range attention}\\
  Z. Wu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The attention matrix can be decomposed into local attention (dense, close to the diagonal) and global attention (sparse, off-diagonal):
one can use a lower-dimensional attention mechanism for the global part, and a CNN for the local part.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ELECTRA: pre-training text encoders\\ as discriminators rather than generators}\\
  K. Clark (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Instead of masked pretraining, pretrain with replaced token detection.
The replacements come from a small BERT, trained at the same time.
(The model only requires 4 GPU$\cdot$day.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ALBERT: a lite BERT for self-supervised learning of language representations}\\
  Z. Lan et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Albert is a smaller BERT-like model,
with a projection between the 1-hot word encodings and their vector embeddings, to reduce the number of parameters,
shared parameters across layers,
a sentence-order prediction loss,
no dropout (there is enough data: it is not needed),
and more data. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FSPool: learning set representations\\ with featurewise sort pooling}\\
  Y. Zhang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Transformer-based language models present visible patterns (vertical or horizontal lines, rectangles) in their self-attention heatmaps,
which can be used to construct parse trees.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Poly-encoders: transformer architectures\\ and pre-training strategies\\ for fast and accurate multi-sentence scoring}\\
  S. Humeau et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To decide if two sentences follow one another, the \emph{bi-encoder} feeds each to a transformer and uses the scalar product of the outputs as score.
The \emph{cross-encoder} (BERT) feeds both sentences to a transformer (and can use cross-sentence attention) and directly outputs a score.
The bi-encoder is less accurate, but much faster.
The \emph{poly-encoder} feeds the candidate sentence to a transformer that outputs a query, 
feeds the context to a transformer that outputs a sequence of states, 
and uses an attention mechanism to combine query and states.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Are transformers universal approximators\\ of sequence-to-sequence functions?}\\
  C. Yun et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Transformers are universal approximators of continuous, permutation-equivariant sequence-to-sequence functions.
Transformers with positional encoding are universal approximators of continuous sequence-to-sequence functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tree-structured attention\\ with hierarchical accumulation}\\
  X.P. Nguyen et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The attention mechanism can be generalized to hierarchical structures, e.g., parsed sentences.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the relationship between\\ self-attention and convolutional layers}\\
  J.B. Cordonnier et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Transformers (self-attention with positional encoding) also work for images: 
they can compute convolutions (you need as many heads as there are pixels in the receptive field).  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-scale representation learning for spatial feature distributions using grid cells}\\
  G. Mai et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The (1-dimensional) positional encoding used in transformers can be generalized to 2 dimensions,
and it can be learned: Space2Vec is an embedding $(x,y)\mapsto \text{latent representation}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural text generation\\ with unlikelihood training}\\
  S. Welleck et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Standard language models produce too many frequent tokens and too few rare tokens.
This may be due to maximum likelihood estimation: 
add an \emph{unlikelihood} penalty to explicitly penalize negative tokens, defined as tokens previously used in the sentence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The curious case of neural text degeneration}\\
  A. Holtzman et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Text-generation models (e.g., GPT2), relying on beam search, generate text less surprising than humans do.
Instead of beam search, which is an approximation of maximum likelihood,
use sampling, but discard the most unlikely words, 
not by retaining the top-$k$ proposals, but by retaining the top-$p$ (probability mass) ones ($p=0.95$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards verified robustness\\ under text deletion interventions}\\
  J. Welbl et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Language models suffer from undersensitivity:
deleting parts of a sentence can make a model more confident,
because it exploits shallow clues (negation, premise-only entailment, etc.).
To avoid those problems, ensure that removing words does not increase the probability, 
e.g., with interval bound propagation (IBP).  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BERTScore:\\ evaluating text generation with BERT}\\
  T. Zhang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Text generation evaluation metrics are often based on $n$-grams (BLEU, ROUGE, METEOR, chrF), 
but they struggle with synonyms. 
More recent metrics (Meant, YiSi, BERTScore) are based on embeddings. 
BERTScore computes the BERT embeddings of the two sentences (generated and reference), 
then all the cosine similarities;
it then greedily matches the words (in each directions, to separate precision from recall) 
averages the similarities, and computes an $F_1$ score.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving neural language generation\\ with spectrum control}\\
  L. Wang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The word embeddings of current language models do not span the whole available space; this can be seen in the distribution of the singular values. 
Instead of learning an embedding $ x \mapsto W x $ from 1-hot-encoded vectors, 
learn its SVD decomposition $W=U\Sigma V'$, with penalties to enforce the orthogonality of $U$ and $V$
and to make the $k$th singular value close to $c k^{-\gamma}$ or $c_1 \exp( - c_2 k ^ \gamma )$

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mirror-generative neural machine translation}\\
  Z. Zheng et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Non-parallel (\ie, monolingual) data can help train better NMT systems, e.g., using back-translation (translated sentences should sound idiomatic).
The 4 models (source and target language models, and the translation models in both directions) 
can be linked with a VAE whose latent variable models the contents of the sentence, 
trained iteratively (one direction at a time).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Logic and the 2-simplicial transformer}\\
  J. Clift et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Attention can be generalized to higher-order relations (between 3 objects instead of 2): 
use two keys instead of one, and replace the scalar product of query and key with 
the tetrahedron volume
\[ \langle a, b, c \rangle = (a\cdot b) c - (a \cdot c ) b + ( b \cdot c ) a. \]
% Clifford algebras

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Strategies for pre-training\\ graph neural networks}\\
  W. Hu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To limit negative transfer in graph neural nets, 
pre-train both node embedding (predict (masked) node labels, or context)
and graph embeddings (various supervised tasks: attribute prediction, structural similarity).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Composition-based multi-relational\\ graph convolutional networks}\\
  S. Vashishth et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To process knowledge graphs with GNN, learn both node and edge embeddings.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{InfoGraph: unsupervised and semi-supervised\\ graph-level representation learning\\ via mutual information maximization}\\
  F.Y. Sun et al.  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
There are two common representations of graphs:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Graph kernels decompose graphs into substructures/motifs and counts them;
\item Graph2vec compute node embeddings, and aggregates them (cf. word2vec, doc2vec).
\end{compactitem}
InfoGraph uses graph convolutions, and a discriminator, fed with pairs of graph and subgraph representations,
to decide if they are from the same graph.
For semi-supervised learning, use two encoders, one with the supervised loss, one with the unsupervised one, 
and keep them close to one another by maximizing the mutual information between their activations --
this can be done by a discriminator, fed with a representation from each encoder, and deciding if they correspond to the same graph
(cf. student/teacher networks).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What graph neural networks cannot learn: depth vs width}\\
  A. Loukas (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Message-passing GNNs are not universal, unless nodes have uniquely identifying features, and 
$\text{depth} \times \text{width} = \Omega(n^2)$
(the exponent is lower for some tasks).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PairNorm: tackling oversmoothing in GNNs}\\
  L. Zhao and L. Akoglu (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
GNNs achieve best performance with 1 or 2 layers -- adding more layers leads to vanishing gradients, overfitting, and ``oversmoothing''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning deep graph matching\\ with channel-independent embedding\\ and Hungarian attention}\\
  T. Yu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The attention mechanism can also be generalized to graph neural nets (GNN).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to retrieve reasoning paths over wikipedia graph for question answering}\\
  A. Asai et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
When searching for the answer of a question in a text database (e.g., Wikipedia), 
the paragraph containing the answer may not have a lot of overlap (often measured at the word level, e.g., tf-idf):
multi-hop reasoning may be needed.
Use an RNN to learn the path in the graph.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Differentiable reasoning\\ over a virtual knowledge base}\\
  B. Dhingra et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
% very clear. empty? ``we can do everything efficiently, with sparse matrices products and approximate k-NN queries''...

Question answering over text often requires combining information from several sources.
The traditional approach (retrieve the relevant documents, understand them, answer) struggles with such multi-hop questions:
we do not know which documents to retrieve until we have partially answered the question.

  %% Example: 
  %%   Question: who voices the dog in the TV show ``Family Guy''?
  %%   Entity: ``Family Guy''
  %%   Relation 1: ``dog in show''
  %%   Relation 2: ``voiced by''
  %%   We expect to retrieve the entities ``Brian'' and ``Seth MacFarlane''.
    
Encode entities as vectors and relations as sparse matrices;
use BERT to measure the similarity between relations and mentions;
information retrieval can then be done 
with sparse matrices products and approximate $k$-NN queries.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{You can teach an old dog new tricks!\\ On training knowledge graph embeddings}\\
  D. Ruffinelli et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Implementation details (knowledge graph embedding) matter:
small tricks can make old models (whose training did not use them) look as good as recent models (whose training did).
% [interesting, clear, but the presentation is rather empty -- I do not know those models]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph constrained reinforcement learning\\ for natural language action spaces}\\
  P. Ammanabrolu and M. Hausknecht
\end{book}
Reinforcement learning algorithms struggle with the discrete but very large action space 
of text adventure games such as Zork.
Use a knowledge graph representing (what we know of) the game world, with a graph attention network (GAT),
to trim down the search space (Verb Object1 Object2).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Query2box: reasoning over knowledge graphs in vector space using box embeddings}\\
  H. Ren et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To answer first-order logic queries on a knowledge graph, 
model entities as points (in latent space), 
and queries as boxes (or unions of boxes);
relations map entities to boxes, e.g., (S,V,?) or (?,V,O).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tensor decompositions\\ for temporal knowledge base completion}\\
  T. Lacroix et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The ComplEx model 
\[  X_{i,j,k} = \langle U_i, V_j, \bar U_k \rangle \]
is a low-rank tensor completion model, for knowledge bases, 
often used with a nuclear 3-norm regularizer.
It can be generalized to temporal knowledge bases, 
\[  X_{i,j,k,t} = \langle U_i, V_j \bar U_k, T_t \rangle \]
or (temporal and non-temporal predicates)
\[ X_{i,j,k,t} = \langle U_i, \tilde V_j + V_j \odot T_t, \bar U_k \rangle, \]
with a temporal regularizer 
\[ \sum \n{ T_{t+1} - T_t }_p^p. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{LambdaNet: probabilistic type inference\\ using graph neural networks}\\
  J. Wei et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Computer code (untyped Python, TypeScript) can be parsed into a tree (an abstract syntax tree, AST),
which can be fed to a graph neural net and a pointer network, for various prediction tasks: 
type inference, bug detection, etc.

The compiler can provide more links to add to the graph.
% 38925746-global-relational-models-of-source-code
% To model source code, gated graph neural nets (GGNN) use an RNN or transformer, 
% with links coming from the compiler.
% The global relational embedding attention transformer (GREAT) learns those links.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hoppity: learning graph transformations\\ to detect and fix bugs in programs}\\
  E. Dinella et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
A (Javascript) program can be represented as an abstract syntax tree (AST), which a graph neural net can turn into a latent representations.
A bugfix is a graph edit, represented by a sequence of operations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards a deep network architecture\\ for structured smoothness}\\
  H. Habeeb and O. Koyejo (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
CNNs gather information from fixed neighbourhoods: instead, the ``fixed grouping layer'' (FGL)
uses neighbourhoods defined by data, e.g., 
with some clustering algorithm (fMRI data) or external data (industries, in finance), 
and ensures that the receptive fields do not span group boundaries. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep 3D pan\\ via adaptive t-shaped convolutions\\ with global and local adaptive dilations}\\
  J.L.G. Bello and M. Kim (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The filters used by convolutions need not be square: form some applications, T-shaped ones make sense.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Denoising and regularization via exploiting\\ the structural bias of convolutional generators}\\
  R. Heckel and M. Soltanolkotabi (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
An auto-encoder (U-Net) trained on a single image ($n=1$) first learns the image, then the noise -- 
early stopping provides denoising.
This comes from the linear upsampling, which fits the smooth part (low frequency) before the noise.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adjustable real-time style transfer}\\
  M. Babaeizadeh and G. Ghiasi (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
% [nice pictures]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust and interpretable blind image denoising via bias-free convolutional neural networks}\\
  S. Mohan et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Bias-free CNNs are better at denoising. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural symbolic reader: scalable integration\\ of distributed and symbolic representations\\ for reading comprehension}\\
  X. Chen et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Language models (BERT) cannot easily answer simple questions requiring arithmetic computations.
Adding specialized modules (addition, subtraction, count, span, negation, etc.) does not allow compositionality and does not generalize well to new problems. 
The neural semantic reader extracts the structure of the text and the question, turns the question into a program, and runs it on the parsed text.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural arithmetic units}\\
  A. Madsen and A.R. Johansen (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Neural networks are good at interpolation, but not at extrapolation: arithmetic expressions perform better. 
For instance, the neural arithmetic logic unit (NALU) is defined as 
\begin{align*}
  w &= \tanh \times \text{sigmoid}                  && \text{(sign and scale)} \\
  a &= \sum w_i x_i                                 && \text{(sum)} \\
  b &= \exp \sum w_i \log ( \va{ x_i } + \epsilon ) && \text{(product)} \\
  g &= \text{sigmoid}                               && \text{(gate)} \\
  y &= g a + (1-g) b                                && \text{(output)}
\end{align*}
But it does not consistently find the correct solution (and negative numbers are a problem).
Instead, the neural arithmetic unit, biases w towards -1, 0 and +1, 
and uses an actual multiplication, with a gating mechanism to select what to multiply.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What can neural networks reason about?}\\
  K. Xu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
A given neural network architecture (e.g., GNN) has an implicit prior on the struture of the computations/algorithm it learns:
it will more easily learn algorithms aligned with that structure.
The GNN nested loops (loop over the nodes, loop over the neighbours, apply some function) are aligned with dynamic programming algorithms:
this explains their success.
They do not work for NP-complete problems, though: try ``neural exhaustive search'' (NES) instead.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learn to explain efficiently\\ via neural logic inductive learning}\\
  Y. Yang and L. Song  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Neural networks can be used to explain an image by mapping its elements (e.g., car, wheel, window, etc.) to vectors,
and relations to matrices, and using an attention mechanism to find first-order-logic formulas matching the scene.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scale-equivariant steerable networks}\\
  I. Sosnovik et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
CNNs are translation-equivariant, but not scale-equivariant.
Use a predefined basis of filters, at different scales, and learn a (scale- and rotation-independent) linear combination of them.

\[
<<ScaleEquivariantSteerableNet,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=8,fig.height=6,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
par( mfrow=c(3,4), mar = c(1,1,1,1) )

col_blue <- colorRampPalette(c("white", tint(  dbColours[1], 80) ), space="Lab")(255)
col_red  <- colorRampPalette(c("white", shade( dbColours[3], 80) ), space="Lab")(255)
colours <- c( rev(col_red), col_blue )
g <- function(z) {
  xs <- seq(0,nrow(z))
  myimage(xs,xs,z, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "", col = colours, zlim = max(abs(z)) * c(-1,1) )
  abline(h=xs,v=xs)
  box()  
}
k <- 11
xs <- seq(-1,1,length=k)
xy <- expand.grid(xs,xs)
f <- function(x) dnorm( sqrt(sum(x^2)) )
z4 <- matrix( apply( 2*xy, 1, f ), k, k )
z3 <- matrix( apply( 3*xy, 1, f ), k, k )
z2 <- matrix( apply( 4*xy, 1, f ), k, k )
z1 <- matrix( apply( 6*xy, 1, f ), k, k )
g(z1)
g(z2)
g(z3)
g(z4)

f <- function(x,n=1,m=1) {
  x <- pi*(x+1)/2
  y <- sin(n*x[1]) * sin(m*x[2])
  sign(y) * sqrt(abs(y))
  y
}
h <- function(n=1, m=1, k=5, N=11) {
  xs <- seq(-1,1, length=k)
  xy <- expand.grid(xs,xs)
  z <- apply( xy, 1, f, n, m )
  z <- matrix( z, k, k )
  Z <- matrix(0, N, N)
  a <- floor( (N-k)/2 )
  Z[ (a+1):(a+k), (a+1):(a+k) ] <- z
  Z
}
g( h(1,2,5,11) )
g( h(1,2,7,11) )
g( h(1,2,9,11) )
g( h(1,2,11,11) )

g( h(2,1,5,11) )
g( h(2,1,7,11) )
g( h(2,1,9,11) )
g( h(2,1,11,11) )
@
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{B-spline CNNs on Lie groups}\\
  E.J. Bekkers (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepSphere: a graph-based spherical CNN}\\
  M. Defferrard et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
        %% 38926130-deepsphere--a-graph-based-spherical-cnn
        %%   Input data: not $\RR^n$, but $\SS^2\times\RR^n$
