        %%   gg: spherical harmonic transform

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Co-attentive equivariant neural networks: focusing equivariance on\\ transformations co-occurring in data}\\
  D.W. Romero and M. Hoogendoorn
\end{book}
Group equivariant CNNs apply the same filter after the various actions of a (discrete) group.
Add an attention mechanism to identify co-occurring actions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On universal equivariant set networks}\\
  N. Segol and Y. Lipman (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
$\mathfrak S_n$-equivariant affine maps are of the form $X \mapsto XA + \mathbf 1 \mathbf 1' XB + \mathbf 1 c'$ (DeepSets).
If $B=0$, each row of the input is transformed in the same way (PointNet).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pure and spurious critical points:\\ a geometric study of linear networks}\\
  M. Trager et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The geometry of the weight space differs from that of the function space:
one can distinguish between critical points in function space (``pure'') and those only in parameter space (``spurious'').
For linear networks and quadratic loss, spurious critical points are saddle points and there are no bad minima: all local minima are global,
even if the network has a bottleneck layer -- in this case, the map is not full rank,
and the space of such maps, the \emph{determinantal variety}, is non-convex and non-smooth.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient Riemannian optimization on the Stiefel manifold via the Cayley transform}\\ 
  J. Li et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
If a matrix $W$ is skew symmetric, then $(I+W)(I-W)^{-1}$ is orthogonal (Cayley transform).
The paths 
$ Y(\alpha) = (1-\tfrac12 \alpha W)^{-1} (1 + \tfrac12 \alpha W) X $,
or their iterative approximation 
$ Y(\alpha) = X + \tfrac12 \alpha W ( X + Y(\alpha) ) $,
allow for Riemannian optimization on the Stiefel manifold (the space of orthogonal matrices) -- 
orthogonal weight matrices improve learning, accuracy and robustness.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the need for topology-aware generative models for manifold-based defenses}\\
  U. Jang et al.  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Generative models assume that the distribution in the latent space is a standard Gaussian -- in particular, the data manifold is contractible. 
In reality, it could be more complex -- in particular, there could be several connected components.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{AE-OT: a new generative model based\\ on extended semi-discrete optimal transport }\\
  D. An et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The optimal transport map from a uniform distribution to a multi-modal one 
can be discontinuous and can lead to mode collapse.
It is the gradient of the Brenier potential, which is continuous and convex: 
directly learn the Brenier potential (in a latent representation),

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep orientation uncertainty learning\\ based on a Bingham loss}\\
  I. Gilitschenski et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The Bingham distribution, on the hypersphere, is 
\[ p(x) \propto \exp( x' M Z M' z ) \]
where $x \in \SS^{d-1}$, $M\in O(d)$ and $Z \in \RR^{d\times d}$ is diagonal.
To estimate the orientation of an object, as a unit quaternion,
have a neural network estimate $M$ (with Gram-Schmidt orthonormalization) and $Z$, and use the log-likelihood as loss function. 
The normalizing constant is difficult to compute: use a pre-computed look-up table and a radial basis function (RBF) interpolator.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural Tangents: fast and easy\\ infinite neural networks in Python}\\
  R. Novak et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
\texttt{Neural-Tangents} provide infinitely-wide layers, 
as a drop-in replacement for \texttt{stax}, the \texttt{jax} neural network library.
Since these are Gaussian processes, they do not scale well with dataset size.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Harnessing the power\\ of infinitely wide deep nets on small-data tasks}\\  
  S. Arora et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The neural tangent kernel (NTK) works better than random forests on the UCI dataset,
and better than ResNets on small datasets (10 to 100 samples).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DDSP: differentiable digital signal processing}\\
  J. Engel et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
\texttt{DDSP} provides digital signal processing operations (oscillators, filters, etc. -- everything you would do in an 
audio editor such as Audacity or with a music programming language sucg as ChucK), as Tensorflow layers
(non-reproducible) examples include removing the reverb (or extracting it to add it to another recording)
or changing the timbre (violin$\to$flute, human$\to$violin).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BackPACK: packing more into backprop}\\
  F. Dangel et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
\texttt{BackPACK} is a PyTorch library to compute more quantities during back-propagation:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item the individual gradients from a mini-batch (for instance to identify which samples are informative and which are not, for importance sampling),
\item the variance of the gradients in a mini-batch (to monitor the signal-to-noise ratio and increase the batch size if needed),
\item a Fisher information matrix approximation.
\end{compactitem}
Also check: \texttt{PyHessian}, \texttt{JAX} (or \texttt{Zygote}, in Julia)
(both forward and backward AD, AD through arbitrary programs: loops, conditionals). 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DiffTaichi: differentiable programming\\ for physical simulation}\\
  Y. Hu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{At stability's edge: how to adjust hyperparameters to preserve minima selection in asynchronous training of neural networks?}\\
  N. Giladi et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To ensure stability in (centralized) asynchronous SGD, rescale the learning rate:  
\( \text{learning rate} \propto 1 / \text{delay}. \)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gap aware mitigation of gradient staleness}\\
  S. Barkai et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Distributed stochastic gradient descent can rely on:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Ignoring synchronization problems;
\item Waiting until all the workers have finished;
\item Staleness awareness: divide the learning rate by the delay: $\eta \mapsto \eta / (\tau+1)$,
  but this over-penalizes old gradients;
\item Gap-awareness: use the gap (minimum number of updates needed to move from the previous to the current parameters): 
  \[ 1 + \dfrac{ \n{ \theta_t - \theta_{t-\tau} } }{ \eta \cdot \E[ \nabla \text{loss} ] }. \]
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Decentralized deep learning\\ with arbitrary communication compression}\\
  A. Koloskova et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
ChocoSGD combines decentralized SGD with (lossy) communication compression:
do not send everything, do not send accurate numbers, but keep track of the cummulated errors, to send them later, once they have accumulated.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SQIL: imitation learning\\ via reinforcement learning with sparse rewards}\\
  S. Reddy et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Imitation learning suffers from distribution shift: if the agent ends up away from the trajectory, it no longer knows what to do.
Adversarial approaches (e.g., GAIL) use a discriminator, separating expert from agent trajectories, but GAN training is unstable.
Instead of learning a reward function, set the reward of the expert actions to 1, those of the agent to 0, 
put both in the replay buffer, and use off-policy learning (e.g., soft actor critic).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimistic exploration\\ even with a pessimistic initialisation}\\
  T. Rashid et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Optimistic initialization does not work well with function approximation.
Use optimistic Q-values:   
\[  Q^+(s,a) = Q(s,a) + C / ( N(s,a) + 1 ) ^ M.  \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamical distance learning\\ for semi-supervised and unsupervised\\ skill discovery}\\
  K. Hartikainen et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
RL problems often have sparse rewards: the agent only receives a reward when it reaches the goal.
Learn a distance function, measuring how many time steps away two states are, on trajectories for the current policy. 
Then, use minus this distance as a reward, to improve the policy. 

This generalizes the discounted state value function, which can be seen as a measure of the distance to the final goal --
instead of the distance between arbitrary states.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Imitation learning\\ via off-policy distribution matching}\\
  I. Kostrikov et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
ValueDICE is an alternative to DAC, GAIL, based on the \emph{Donsker-Varadhan} representation of the KL divergence
\[ \text{KL}( p \| q ) = \max_{ f : X \to \RR } \E_{ x \sim p } [ f(x) ] - \log \E_{ x \sim q } [ e^{ f(x) } ]. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Implementation matters in deep RL:\\ a case study on PPO and TRPO}\\
  L. Engstrom et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Apparently insignificant code-level optimization (value-clipping, reward-clipping, Adam annealing, orthogonal initialization, etc.)
have a large impact -- more than the choice of algorithm (e.g., PPO vs TRPO).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Explain your move:\\ understanding agent actions\\ using specific and relevant feature attribution}\\
  N. Puri et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To explain the action chosen by an agent, perturb the state features and re-compute the $Q$-value: 
if it changes a lot (in absolute value, and/or relatively to the $Q$-values of the other actions), the feature was important.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Disagreement-regularized imitation learning}\\
  K. Brantley et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To limit the compounding error problem in behavioural cloning, 
add a penalty term to stay close to the the expert distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RIDE: rewarding impact-driven exploration for procedurally-generated environments}\\
  R. Raileanu and T. Rocktäschel (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Reward actions that have a large impact on the environment, measured by the 
$L^2$ norm of the difference between the latent state representations.
To focus on unforeseen/surprising consequences, train a model to predict the next (latent) state from the current state and the action.
To focus on changes we can actually control, train a model to predict the action from the current and next state.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Toward evaluating robustness of deep reinforcement learning with continuous control}\\
  T.W. Weng et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
Threat models in reinforcement learning include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Observation manipulation (before the agent receives it);
\item Action manipulation (after the agent chooses it).
\end{compactitem}
To prevent those threats, one could learn the dynamics, identify unsafe states, and avoid them -- but performance degrades a lot.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Keep doing what worked: behavior modelling priors for offline reinforcement learning}\\
  N. Siegel et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
When learning from a fixed replay buffer, with no new trajectories, 
avoid actions that would lead away from the explored portions of the state-action space,
by putting a prior on the policy, to make it close to those in the buffer -- 
or, better, close to the actions that worked.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model-based reinforcement learning\\ for biological sequence design}\\
  C. Angermueller et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To find biological sequences $x$ maximizing $f(x)$ (the result of some wet-lab experiment result),
use reinforcement learning, where the actions select the next character of the (fixed-length) sequence, 
and we only receive a reward, $f(x)$, at the end.
Add a penalty to increase diversity.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Harnessing structures for value-based planning and reinforcement learning}\\
  Y. Yang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Represent the $Q$-value as a matrix, state$\times$action (after discretization, if needed): quite often, it is low-rank.
Use such a low-rank matrix in planning or reinforcement learning.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The ingredients of real-world robotic reinforcement learning}\\
  H. Zhu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Real-world reinforcement learning faces three problems:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item No resets (the robot could get stuck -- or could reach the goal, from which the optimal action is to do nothing): 
  learn a perturbation agent;
\item High-dimensional observations (images) instead of low-dimensional state: learn a low-dimensional representation of the state, with a VAE;
\item No reward: train a classifier to recognize the goal (from a set of example images).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Behaviour suite for reinforcement learning}\\
  I. Osband et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
\texttt{bsuite} provides a set of experiments and metrics (exploration, generalization, credit assignment, scalability, etc.) 
to test and compare RL algorithms (like MNIST for RL).
It can also generate a 1-page \LaTeX summary, to add to your paper.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evolutionary population curriculum\\ for scaling multi-agent reinforcement learning}\\
  Q. Long et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
In multi-agent reinforcement learning, 
start with small populations, and progressively increase the size.
The model, for each agent, should be applicable to an arbitrary number of inputs (number of enemies, ressources).
When increasing the population size, we do not only want agents that perform well when the population size is $N$: 
they should also be good initializations for population size $2N$ -- 
use an evolutionary strategy, with $k$ groups of agents, and keep those performing best at size $2N$.
Application: grassland game (grass, sheep, wolves).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Demystifying inter-class disentanglement}\\
  A. Gabbay and Y. Hoshen (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To disentangle class (e.g., identity, in a face recognition system) from content (pose), 
learn separate class and content embeddings but, to avoid information leakage, 
have the class embedding be the same for all members of that class.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning representations for binary-classification without backpropagation}\\
  M. Lechner (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Backpropagation is biologically implausible.
Alternatives include: Hebbian learning, Target propagation,
Feedback alignment (FA), direct feedback alignment (FDA), monotone DFA.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Reconstructing continuous distributions of 3D protein structure from Cryo-EM images}\\
  E.D. Zhong (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Each Cryo-EM (electron microscopy) image contains only one molecule, in a random orientation and location, 
but they may not all be in the same configuration. We usually try to cluster them.
Instead, assume the configurations form a 2-dimensional manifold.
For the orientation and location of the molecule in each image, use an optimization over $\text{SO}(3)\times\RR^2$

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Intrinsically motivated discovery of diverse patterns in self-organizing systems}\\
  C. Reinke et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
To find interesting and diverse patterns in a self-organizing system (snowflakes, animal skin patterns,
or, as in this example, the continuous game of life -- time and space are still discrete, but the values are in [0,1]),
start with a random initial state and look after 200 steps.

To ensure diversity in the output, 
learn a latent representation (8-dimensional VAE) of the outputs;
pick a point at random in this latent space;
find an input whose output is close to that point;
iterate (and retrain the VAE once in a while).

Since random noise tends to generate global patterns rather than local ones, 
use patterns from a CPPN (compositional pattern-producing network) as inputs.

% Check the videos: https://automated-discovery.github.io/

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning compositional Koopman operators for model-based control}\\
  Y. Li et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
\emph{Koopman operator theory} takes a non-linear dynamical system 
$ x_{n+1} = F( x_n ) $
and finds an embedding into a higher-dimensional space $y = g(x)$
such that the dynamics become linear $ y_{n+1} = K y_n $.
Systems made of many parts are often modeled (and controlled) with interaction graphs.
Those two approaches can be combined, the graph structure corresponding to a block Koopman matrix.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SNODE: spectral discretization\\ of neural ODEs for system identification}\\
  A. Quaglino et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Separate the neural ODE
``find $\theta$ so that $x$ defined by $\dot x(t) = f_\theta(t, x(t))$, $x(t_0)=y_0$
minimizes $\int \text{Loss}(t, x(t)) dt$'',
into two:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Find a function $x$ minimizing the loss;
\item Find $\theta$ so that this $x$ approximately solves the ODE.
\end{compactitem}
Estimate the derivative $\dot x$ by expressing $x$ with Legendre polynomials.
This is much faster than using an ODE solver.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On robustness\\ of neural ordinary differential equations}\\
  H. Yan et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Neural ODEs are more robust to noise than CNNs with residual connections
\begin{align*}
  \dot z(t) &= f_\theta( z(t), t ) \\
  z(0) &= z_{\text{in}} \\
  z(1) &= z_{\text{out}}
\end{align*}
For time-invariant steady neural ODE (TisODE)
\[ \dot z(t) = f_\theta( z(t) ) \]
one can add a further robustness penalty.
%[NOT CLEAR]  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep audio priors emerge from\\ harmonic convolutional networks}\\
  Z. Zhang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Auto-encoders (U-Net) can denoise images, but not sound: replace the convolutions with \d{harmonic convolutions}
(a form of dilated convolutions):
do not use $[ (1-\epsilon)f, (1+\epsilon)f ]$ as neighbourhood of frequency $f$, 
but $\{f, 2f, 3f, \dots, 12f\}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the steerability\\ of generative adversarial networks}\\
  A. Jahanian et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
One can learn transformations (rotation, translation, zoom, colour changes, etc.) in the latent space of GANs, 
to some extent (not beyond the training distribution).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Controlling generative models\\ with continuous factors of variations}\\
  A. Plumerault et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Yet another attempt to control the output of a GAN: size, orientation, colour, etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Real or not real, that is the question}\\
  Y. Xiangli et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The Realness GAN considers that samples are not fully real or fully fake, 
and optimizes \[ \max_G \min_D \E_{x \sim \text{data}} \text{KL}(A_1 \| D(x) ) + \E_{ x \sim \text{fake}} \text{KL} (A_0 \| D(x) ) \]
where $A_0$ and $A_1$ are random variables (e.g., 
$A_0 \sim \text{Beta}(5,1)$,
$A_1 \sim \text{Beta}(1,5)$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic time lag regression:\\ predicting what \& when}\\
  M. Chandorkar et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The dynamic time-lag regression $ y( t + g(x_t) ) = f(x_t) $
(with $f$ and $g$ unknown) has a closed-form log-likelihood.
It has applications in astronomy (solar wind prediction).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Intensity-free learning\\ of temporal point processes}\\
  O. Shchur et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
When modeling temporal point processes (TPP),
instead of using an RNN to compute the estimate the intensity function $\lambda_t$, 
estimate the density function of the time until the next event,
with normalizing flows (to transform a Gaussian density into the desired density) or a Gaussian mixture.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Variational autoencoders for highly multivariate spatial point processes intensities}\\
  B. Yuan et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Instead of modeling the intensity function $\lambda$, model the density $h(x) = \lambda(x) / \int \lambda$ 
(this is how one can sample from the process: first sample the number of points $N$, from a Poisson distribution, 
then sample $N$ points, iid, from $h$), 
with a VAE.
% Example: data from Gowalla, which was a location-based social network.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On solving minimax optimization locally:\\ a follow-the-ridge approach}\\
  Y. Wang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The nonconvex-nonconcave minimax problem 
\[ \min_x \max_y f(x,y) \]
can be solved with the follow-the-ridge algorithm, 
which requires second-order information (the gradient is zero along the ridge).
\begin{align*}
  x_{t+1} &\leftarrow x_t - \eta \nabla _{\!x} f( x_t, y_t ) \\
  y_{t+1} &\leftarrow y_t - \eta \nabla _{\!y} f( x_t, y_t ) + \eta H_{yy}^{-1} H_{yx} \nabla _{\!x} f( x_t, y_t )  
\end{align*}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gradientless descent:\\ high-dimensional zeroth-order optimization}\\
  D. Golovin et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
When the gradient of the function to minimize is not available,
gradient-less methods (moving in a random direction if it improves the objective)
outperform gradient-approximating ones such as 
accelerated random search (ARS),
\[ x \leftarrow x - \epsilon \dfrac{  f(x+hv) - f(x) }{ h } v \]
for a random unit vector $v$.  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On mutual information maximization\\ for representation learning}\\
  M. Tschannen et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
When learning a latent representation, we want the latent representation to simpler (lower-dimensional) 
but to keep as much information as possible from the input. This can be achieved by maximizing the mutual information $\text{MI}(x,g(x))$ between 
the input x and the latent representation $g(x)$ (InfoMax).
Modern variants maximize $\text{MI}(g(x_1),g(x_2))$, where $x_1$ and $x_2$ are different parts of the same image (e.g., top and bottom).
The mutual information is maximum if $g$ is bijective, and is invariant by bijections -- it is unlikely to find good representations.
However, the approximations of MI used (NCE, noise contrastive estimation, or NWJ) are not invariant under bijections, 
and prefer (hard-to-invert maps that are) good representations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding the limitations of variational mutual information estimators}\\
  J. Song and S. Ermon (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning robust representations\\ via multi-view information bottleneck}\\
  M. Federici et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
If we know that two images correspond to the same label (but do not know the label), 
we can still learn a latent representation by maximizing the mutual information between the latent representations of the two images
(to keep what is common and discard what is not)
and minimizing the divergence between the distributions of those latent representations (in the case of a VAE).
For isolated images, use data augmentation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rényi fair inference}\\
  S. Baharlouei et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Try the Rényi correlation 
\[ 
\rho(X,Y) = \sup_{\substack{
    f, g \\
    \E\! f(X) = \E\! g(Y) = 0 \\
    \E\! f(X)^2 = \E\! g(Y)^2 = 1
}}
\bigl[ f(X) g(Y) \bigr]
\]
instead of mutual information (for discrete variables, it is tractable, especially when one is binary).
To make a model fairer (classifier or clustering), add a penalty for the Renyi correlation between the output and the protected attribute.

%% \nextbook
%% \begin{book}
%%   \emph{Expected Information Maximization: Using the I-Projection for Mixture Density Estimation}\\
%%   P. Becker et al. (ICLR 2020)
%% \end{book}
%% Information projection vs maximum likelihood
%%     $\argmin_q$ KL(q||p)        KL(model||data)   Information projection, largest mode
%%     $\argmin_q$ KL(p||q)        KL(data||model)   Moment projection, maximum likelihood
%% Expected information maximization (EIM)
%% KL(model||data) can be minimized with an EM algorithm. [a bit technical -- that is what is new in the paper]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Overlearning reveals sensitive attributes}\\
  C. Song and V. Shmatikov  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Sensitive attributes can be censored using
adversarial training (it should be impossible to predict the sensitive attribute from the latent representation)
or information theory (by minimizing the mutual information between the latent representation and the sensitive attribute).
Unfortunately, such censoring is either inefficient or harmful to the model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Training individually fair ML models\\ with sensitive subspace robustness}\\
  M. Yurochkin et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
A model is \emph{individually fair} if it is robust to sensitive perturbations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding why neural networks\\ generalize well through GSNR of parameters}\\
  J. Liu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The one-step generalization ratio is the ratio of the change in test loss over the change in training loss.

The gradient signal-to-noise ratio is the ratio of the
squared norm of the mean gradient
over the variance of the gradients computed on individual samples;
it measures if the gradients of the different samples agree.

If the gradients are consistent across samples, the GSNR and OSGR are large,
and the model generalizes well.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stable rank normalization for improved generalization in neural networks and GANs}\\
  A. Sanyal et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The generalization gap depends on (the product of) the spectral norms of the weight matrices (Lipschitzness)
and (the sum of) their stable ranks.

The \emph{stable rank} of a matrix $W$ with singular values $\sigma_i$'s is 
\[ \text{srank}(W) = \dfrac{ \n W _F^2 }{ \n W _2^2 } = \dfrac{\sum \sigma_i^2}{\sigma_1^2} \leq \text{rank}(W) \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep probabilistic subsampling\\ for task-adaptive compressed sensing}\\
  I.A.M. Huijben et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
Gumbel max sampling
\[ \argmax_i \log p_i + g_i, \text{ for } g_i \sim \text{Gumbel}(0,1) \]
can be made differentiable by replacing the argmax with a softmax (with temperature),
and trained to decide which Fourier frequencies to sample to best help classify or reconstruct
an input image.  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A function space view of bounded norm infinite width ReLU nets: the multivariate case}\\
  G. Ongie et al.  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The $r$-norm measures the representation cost of a function 
\[ R(f) = \lim_{ \epsilon \to 0 } \inf_{ \theta } \{ \n \theta ^2 \ :\ \n{ h_\theta - f } \leq \epsilon \} \]
where $h_\theta$ is a 1-hidden-layer infinite-width ReLU network.
It can be characterized as $ R(f) = c \cdot \n{ \mathscr R ( - \Delta ) ^{ (d+1)/2 } f } _1 $
where $\Delta$ is the Laplacian and $\mathscr R$ the Radon transform.
In dimension 1: $R(f) = \int \va{ f'' } $.
It is not a RKHS norm (as with the NTK).
Some functions have infinite $R(f)$, even though they are well approximated with 2-hidden-layer networks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Knowledge consistency\\ between neural networks and beyond}\\
  R. Liang et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book} 
Given two trained networks performing the same task, compare their latent representations $x_A$, $x_B$ as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Try to express $x_B$ as a linear transformation of $x_A$;
\item Try to express the residuals as a 1-layer non-linear transformation of $x_A$;
\item Try to express the residuals as a 2-layer non-linear transformation of $x_A$;
\item The residuals are then noise.
\end{compactitem}
This gives a decomposition of the latent representation, 
which can help spot unreliable features, measure overfitting, and compress networks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Functional vs. parametric equivalence\\ of ReLU networks}\\
  M. Phuong and C.H. Lampert  (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The function computed by a (ReLU) network is invariant under: permutation of neurons, rescaling (of two layers, with inverse scaling factors).
These are the only function-preserving transformations: the \emph{fold-sets} of a ReLU network provide enough constraints on the weights.

\[
<<FoldSets,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
n0 <- 2
n1 <- 3
n2 <- 5
n3 <- 7

f <- function(z, ...) {
  contour( z, drawlabels=FALSE, levels=0, axes = FALSE, xlab="", ylab="", lwd = 3, ... )
  box()
}        

par( mfrow=c(1,3), mar=c(1,1,1,1) )
i <- 161
set.seed(i)

k <- 100
xs <- seq(-1,1,length=k)
xy <- as.matrix(expand.grid(xs,xs))

W <- matrix( rnorm(6), n1,n0 )
b <- rnorm(n1)
Z1 <- t( W %*% t(xy) + b )
f( matrix(Z1[,1],k,k), col="#193296" )
f( matrix(Z1[,2],k,k), col="#193296", add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z1[,3],k,k), col="#193296", add=TRUE)

Z2 <- pmax(Z1,0)
W <- matrix( rnorm(6), n2,n1 )
b <- rnorm(n2)
Z2 <- t( W %*% t(Z2) + b )
f( matrix(Z1[,1],k,k), col = 'grey' )
f( matrix(Z1[,2],k,k), col = 'grey', add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z1[,3],k,k), col = 'grey', add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z2[,1],k,k), col="#193296", add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z2[,2],k,k), col="#193296", add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z2[,3],k,k), col="#193296", add=TRUE)

set.seed(i)
Z3 <- pmax(Z2,0)
W <- matrix( rnorm(6), n3,n2 )
b <- rnorm(n3)
Z3 <- t( W %*% t(Z3) + b )
f( matrix(Z1[,1],k,k), col = 'grey' )
f( matrix(Z1[,2],k,k), col = 'grey', add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z1[,3],k,k), col = 'grey', add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z2[,1],k,k), col = 'grey', add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z2[,2],k,k), col = 'grey', add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z2[,3],k,k), col = 'grey', add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z3[,1],k,k), col="#193296", add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z3[,2],k,k), col="#193296", add=TRUE)
f( matrix(Z3[,3],k,k), col="#193296", add=TRUE)
@
\]


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic AUC maximization\\ with deep neural networks}\\
  M. Liu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
AUC maximization can be formulated as a min-max problem, non-convex and concave, solvable with the approximate proximal point method (APPM).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Playing the lottery with rewards and multiple languages: lottery tickets in RL and NLP}\\
  H. Yu et al. (ICLR 2020)
\end{book}
The ``lottery ticket hypothesis'' is not limited to image classification: 
it also seems to hold in NLP and RL.

\end{multicols}
\clearpage
\begin{multicols}{2}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%
%% NeurIPS 2019
%% https://slideslive.com/neurips#!feed=latest
%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep equilibrium models}\\
  S. Bai et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
A deep neural network with \emph{weight-tied}, input-injected layers computes the repeated application
of a function $ z_{i+1} = f_\theta ( z_i, x ) $; it converges (in practice) to an equilibrium point
$z^* = f_\theta( z^*, x )$.
\d{Deep equilibrium} (DEQ) models find this equilibrium by root finding rather than iteration.
They can replace deep neural networks (TrellisNet, universal transformer) with 1-layer networks:
they are more memory-efficient.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{List-decodable linear regression}\\
  S. Karmalkar et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\d{List-decodable models} are robust methods returning, not one estimator, but a list of estimators,
with a good one ($\n{\hat \alpha - \alpha^*} < \epsilon$) with high probability.
They allow for adversarial outliers, and contamination beyond 50\%.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Legendre memory units: continuous-time representation in recurent neural networks}\\
  A. Voelker et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Strided recurrent neural nets can account for long-term dependencies, with Legendre polynomials:
they are an alternative to LSTMs.
[They also do hardware, and have implemented their RNN as a spiking neural net.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural networks\\ with cheap differential operators}\\
  R.T.Q. Chen and D. Duvenaud
  (2019)
\end{book}
The divergence $\div \cdot f = \sum \partial f_i / \partial x_i$ of a neural net
$f : \RR^d \to \RR^d$ only requires the diagonal of the Jacobian.
It can be efficiently computed by \emph{hollow networks}

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt,baseline=1cm]
\node (x1) at (0,2) {$x_1$};
\node (x2) at (0,1) {$x_2$};
\node (x3) at (0,0) {$x_3$};
\node (h1) at (1,2) {$h_1$};
\node (h2) at (1,1) {$h_2$};
\node (h3) at (1,0) {$h_3$};
\node (f1) at (2,2) {$f_1$};
\node (f2) at (2,1) {$f_2$};
\node (f3) at (2,0) {$f_3$};  


\draw[->,gray] (x1) -- (h2);
\draw[->,gray] (x3) -- (h2);
\draw[->,gray] (h2) -- (f2);
\draw[->,gray] (x2) to[out=30, in=150, looseness=1]  (f2);

\draw[->,gray] (x1) -- (h3);
\draw[->,gray] (x2) -- (h3);
\draw[->,gray] (h3) -- (f3);
\draw[->,gray] (x3) to[out=30, in=150, looseness=1]  (f3);

\draw[->,very thick] (x2) -- (h1);
\draw[->,very thick] (x3) -- (h1);
\draw[->,very thick] (h1) -- (f1);
\draw[->,very thick] (x1) to[out=30, in=150, looseness=1]  (f1);
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad\qquad
\begin{aligned}
  h_i &= c_i( x_{-i} ) \\
  f_i &= \tau_i ( x_i, h_i )
\end{aligned}
\]
(the Jacobian can easily be separated into diagonal and non-diagonal parts,
your deep learning framework can be asked to only compute the gradient of the diagonal).

Applications include fixed point iterations, normalizing flows.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Kernel instrumental variable regression}\\
  R. Singh et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To learn a causal relation in presence of confounding variables, regression is biased: it gives a prediction, not a \emph{counterfactual prediction}.

% Instrumental variables (IV)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\node (Z) at (0,0) {$Z$};
\node (X) at (1,0) {$X$};
\node (Y) at (2,0) {$Y$};
\node (e) at (1.5,.866) {$e$};
\draw[thick,->] (X) -- node[anchor=north] {\footnotesize $h$} (Y);
\draw[thick,->] (Z) -- (X);
\draw[thick,->] (e) -- (X);
\draw[thick,->] (e) -- (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2.5,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\node (Z) at (0,0) {\footnotesize Gas price};
\node (X) at (1,0) {\footnotesize Ticket price};
\node (Y) at (2,0) {\footnotesize Ticket sales};
\node (e) at (1.5,.866) {\footnotesize Other market forces};
\draw[thick,->] (X) -- (Y);
\draw[thick,->] (Z) -- (X);
\draw[thick,->] (e) -- (X);
\draw[thick,->] (e) -- (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2.5,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\node[align=center] (Z) at (0,0) {\footnotesize Random\\[-1mm]\footnotesize assignment};
\node[align=center] (X) at (1,0) {\footnotesize Medical\\[-1mm]\footnotesize treatment};
\node[align=center] (Y) at (2,0) {\footnotesize Health\\[-1mm]\footnotesize outcome};
\node[align=center] (e) at (1.5,.866) {\footnotesize Income};
\draw[thick,->] (X) -- (Y);
\draw[thick,->] (Z) -- (X);
\draw[thick,->] (e) -- (X);
\draw[thick,->] (e) -- (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Use an instrumental variable $Z$ (which influences $X$ but not directly $Y$) and 2SLS: 
\begin{align*}
  X &\sim Z \\
  Y &\sim \hat X(Z) \qquad \text{estimated on disjoint samples.}
\end{align*}
Replace the linear regressions with kernel ridge regressions. 
Asymmetric sample splitting is important, with more observations in the first stage, and even more if the relation $Z \to X$ is less smooth.
DeepIV is preferable if there is enough data (10,000 observations) and the relation is complex.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adversarial samples are not bugs,\\ they are features}\\
  A. Ilyas et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
  Take a dataset with cats and dogs, train a model, replace each sample with an adversarial example, 
  and flip the labels accordingly, and learn a new model: 
  it works well on the test set.
  The new model uses non-robust features that generalize well.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantum entropy scoring for fast robust mean estimation and improved outlier detection}\\
  Y. Dang et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
PCA is a good way to find outliers,
$\text{score}(x_i) = \langle x_i , \text{PCA}_k \rangle$,
but it only detects outliers in one direction at a time:
it is not ideal in high dimension.
\emph{Quantum entropy scoring} (QUE) replaces PCA with regularized PCA
\begin{align*}
  W &= \argmax_{ \tr U = 1, \ U \succcurlyeq 0 }
  \alpha \langle U, \Sigma \rangle + \tr ( U \log U ) \\
  \text{score}(X) &= \langle X X' , W \rangle 
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Uniform convergence may be unable to explain generalization in deep learning}\\
  V. Nagarjan and J.Z. Kotler
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To explain why overparametrized models generalize well,
we can use uniform convergence to find bounds of the form
\begin{align*}
  \text{generalization gap} &\leq O\left( \dfrac{ \text{effective model size} }{ \text{training set size} } \right)  
\end{align*}
where the effective model size accounts for the bias of the training algorithm (SGD).
Those bounds are too loose or require unreasonable assumptions.
They do not even behave like the generalization gap:
they increase with the training set size
(it is in the denominator but also hidden in the numerator, e.g., with the norm of the weights),
while the generalization gap decreases.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=1,inner sep=0pt, outer sep=1pt, baseline=0]
  \draw[very thick,green!50] (-.5,1) -- (2.5,1);
  \draw[very thick,blue!50] (-.5,0) -- (2.5,0);
  \draw[very thick,red!75] (-.5,.5) --
  (-.2,.5) to[out=0,in=180,looseness=.3]
  (0,1.5)  to[out=0,in=180,looseness=.3]
  (.2,.5) --
  (1.8,.5) to[out=0,in=180,looseness=.3]
  (2,-.5)  to[out=0,in=180,looseness=.3]
  (2.2,.5) -- (2.5,.5);
  \node[circle,fill,inner sep=3pt,green!50] at (2,1) {};
  \node[circle,fill,inner sep=3pt,blue!50] at (0,0) {};
  \node[circle,draw,inner sep=3pt,blue!50] at (1,0) {};
  \node[circle,draw,inner sep=3pt,blue!50] at (2,0) {};
  \node[anchor=east] at (-.5,1) {\footnotesize\color{green!50!black}class 1};
  \node[anchor=east] at (-.5,.5) {\footnotesize\color{red!50!black}learned boundary};
  \node[anchor=east] at (-.5,0) {\footnotesize\color{blue!50!black}class 1};
  \node[anchor=north,align=center] at (1,-.5) {%
    \scriptsize Most new points\\[-1mm]
    \scriptsize would be correctly\\[-1mm]
    \scriptsize classified};
  \node[anchor=north,align=center] at (2,-.5) {%
    \scriptsize but those \\[-1mm]
    \scriptsize to close to\\[-1mm]
    \scriptsize the training set\\[-1mm]
    \scriptsize would not};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Deep learning models can learn complex boundaries, which hurt uniform convergence bounds
[and look like a bonanza for adversarial attackers] but not generalization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation of the Lipschitz constant\\ of deep neural networks}\\
  (M. Fazlyab et al.)
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Computing the Lipschitz constant of a neural net is NP hard:
we can only hope to find approximations or bounds.
Taking the product of the (matrix) norms of the weights is overly conservative.
The Lipschitz constant can be expressed as the solution of a nonconvex optimization problem
whose relaxation
% with "incremental quadratic constraints" (IQC), a tool from convex optimization,
is a semi-definite program (solved with CVX and Mosek).

Robustness is one application:
low-Lipschitz networks are more robust --
conversely, networks trained with robust algorithms
% LP, PGD
have a lower Lipschitz constant.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data-dependent sample complexity of deep neural networks via Lipschitz augmentation}\\
  C. Wei et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Regularizers can be designed in a principled way,
based on theoretical upper bounds of the generalization error,
but those bounds are loose.
The following data-dependent bound
\begin{align*}
  \text{generalization} &\leq
  \dfrac{
    \text{jacobian norm} \times \text{hidden layer norm}
  }{
    \text{margin} \times \sqrt{\text{training set size}}
  }
  \\
  &\hphantom{\leq{}}
  + \text{lower-order terms}
\end{align*}
where
the Jacobian norm is the $L^\infty$ norm of the Jacobian wrt the weights,
the hidden layer norm is the $L^\infty$ norm of the activations (on the training set),
the margin is minimum, over the training set, of the difference between the first two logits,
suggests to use the \emph{Jacobian} as a regularizer
(for the hidden layer norm, just use BatchNorm or LayerNorm).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable global optimization\\ via local Bayesian optimization}\\
  D. Erikson et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Thompson sampling} can be used as an acquisition function for Bayesian optimization:
sample a function from the posterior and estimate its minimum [?].
It is parallelizable: just sample $k$ functions.
In higher dimensions (beyond 10),
do not try to find a global approximation of the objective,
but just a \emph{local} one. Expand/shrink the region based on progress
(trust-region-based Bayesian optimization, TuRBO).

Code available.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Uncertainty\\ on asynchronous time event prediction}\\
  M. Bilo\v s et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The forecast for the next event should change with time and account for uncertainty
(it should not be a point in the simplex, but a distribution on it).
Model continuously evolving distributions over the simplex with:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Dirichlet distribution parameters evolving with a basis function decomposition;
\item Logistic-normal parameters evolving with a weighted Gaussian process.
\end{compactitem}

<<Dirichlet-GP,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
f <- function(d) { 
  g <- ggtern(data=d,aes(A,B,C)) + 
    stat_density_tern(
        geom='polygon',
        aes(fill=..level..),
        bins=5,
        color='grey') +
  labs(x="", y="", z="") +
  theme_void() + guides(fill=FALSE)	  
}
g <- function(a,b,c, N=10000) {
  d <- data.frame(
    A = rgamma(N,a),
    B = rgamma(N,b),
    C = rgamma(N,c)
  )
  d <- d / rowSums(d)
  d  
}
library(ggplot2)
library(ggtern)
grid.arrange(
f( g(10,1,1) ),
f( g(7,3,1) ),
f( g(3,7,1) ),
f( g(1,10,1) ),
f( g(1,6,1) ),
f( g(1,3,1) ),
f( g(1,1,1) ),
nrow = 1)
@

Applications include anomaly detection.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Variational Bayesian\\ optimal experimental design}\\
  A. Foster et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
An informative experimental design reduces entropy.
The expected information gain
\(
\text{EIG}(d) = E_y \bigl[
  H[P(\theta)] - P[ p(\theta | y, d ) ]
\bigr]
\)
is intractable: use variational inference.
% Implementation in Pyro

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Poisson mini-batching for Gibbs sampling\\ with convergence rate guarantees}\\
  R. Zhang and C. DeSa
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
When using Gibbs sampling on subsets of variables,
\ie, when sampling from $x_i | x_J$ instead of $x_i | x_I$, $J \subsetneq I$,
a Metropolis-Hastings rejection step is needed to correct the bias.
Instead, add an \emph{auxiliary Poisson variable} for each factor to control if it is used --
it is unbiased, even without MH.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parameter estimation\\ in particle Gibbs sampling}\\
  A. Wigren et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}

