\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to control\\ self-assembling morphologies:\\ a study of generalization via modularities}\\
  D. Pathak et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Dynamic graph networks} combines several identical neural nets (with shared weights) into a graph,
the output of one being sent to the input of the next ones.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graded meta policy search}\\
  R. Mendonca et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
In meta-learning, learn \emph{local policies}, for the training tasks,
and combine them with supervised learning to deal with the whole tasks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using a logarithmic mapping to enable lower discount factors in reinforcement learning}\\
  H. van Seijen et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
In practice (with function approximation)
only discount factors close to 1 work well.
Poor performance seems to come from large differences in the action gaps
between states
\[ \Delta(s) = Q^*(s,a^*) - Q^*(s,\tilde a), \]
where $a^*$ is the optimal action and $\tilde a$ that chosen by the algorithm.

\emph{Logarithmic Q-learning} replaces $Q$ with $\log Q$;
it is possible to get the correct optimum in spite of
$E[ \log X ] \leq \log EX$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hindsight credit assignment}\\
  A. Harutyunyan et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Do not use time for credit assignment, estimate
$P(a|x, f(\tau))$,
where $a$ is a past action, $x$ a past state and $f(\tau)$ a future outcome.
\[
  Q(x,a) = r(x,a) + \E_\tau \left[
    \sum \gamma^k \dfrac{
      P(a|x,X_k)
    }{
      \pi(a|x)
    }
    R_k
  \right]
\]

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
\node[circle,draw] (x) at (0,0) {$x$};
\node[circle,draw] (y) at (1,0) {$y$};
\node[circle,draw] (z) at (2,0) {$z$};
\draw[->] (x) -- node[anchor=south,pos=.2] {\footnotesize $a$} (y);
