Unsupervised object segmentation is possible:
add a random shift to the foreground and mask:
the reconstructed image should still look real.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Faster width-dependent algorithm\\ for mixed packing and covering LPs}\\
  D. Boob et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
A mixed packing-covering linear program (MPC-LP) is of the form
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
\text{Find} & x \in [0,1]^n \\
\text{Such that} & P x \leq \mathbf 1 \\
& C x \geq \mathbf 1 & \text{where } P, C \geq 0.
\end{array}
\]
The corresponding \emph{saddle point problem} is
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_{ x \in [0,1]^n }
\max_{\substack{ y \in \Delta_p \\ z \in \Delta_q }}
L(x,y,z).
\]
It is often solved by minimizing the \emph{primal-dual gap} $f$,
with a strong convex penalty $\phi$,
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_w f(w) + \phi(w)
\]
with \emph{Nesterov's dual extrapolation}.
Replace strong convexity
\[
  \phi\left( \dfrac{ u + v }2 \right) \leq \dfrac12 \bigl[ \phi(u) + \phi(v) \bigr] - \dfrac12 \n{ u - v}
\]
with a weaker notion, area convexity,
\[
  \phi\left( \dfrac{ u + v + t }3 \right) \leq \dfrac13 \bigl[ \phi(u) + \phi(v) + \phi(t) \bigr] -
  \dfrac1{3\sqrt3} (v-u) '  M (u-t).
\]
For instance,
$ \gamma(x,y) = x y \log x + 2 y \log y $, on $[0,1]^2$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse logistic regression learns\\ all discrete pairwise graphical models}\\
  S. Wu et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Logistic regression, with an $\ell_1$ (or $\ell_{2,1}$) penalaty,
can recover the structure of a graphical model, such as the Ising (binary) model
\[
P(z) \propto \exp \biggl[ \sum_{i,j} A_{ij} z_i z_j + \sum_i \theta_i z_i \biggr]
\qquad z \in \{ \pm 1 \}^n.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Smoothing structured decomposable circuits}\\
  A. Shih et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Circuits (aka \emph{sum-product networks}) describe probabiliy distributions
and allow efficient computation of likelihood partition function, marginals, etc.
provided they are smooth.

A circuit is \emph{smooth} if the children of any $\oplus$ gate use the same variables.
It is easy to ensure, but the algorithm is quadratic;
it can be sped up by representing groups of variables as intervals.

