\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast and accurate least mean squares}\\
  A. Maalouf et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The average of $n$ points in $\RR^d$ is a convex combination of $d+1$ of those points;
such a representation can be obtained recursively, by putting the points in $d+1$ (random) groups
and computing their means (Caratheodory theorem).

Transform the least mean square (LMS) problem
$ \argmin_x \n{ A x - b } _2 ^2 + g(x) $:
\[
\n{ Ax - b }_2^2 = [ x \ -1 ] \begin{bmatrix} A' \\ b' \end{bmatrix} [ A \ b ] \begin{bmatrix} x \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} = [ x \ -1 ] P' P \begin{bmatrix} x \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}.
\]
The covariance matrix
$\tfrac1n P' P = \tfrac1n \sum p_i p_i' $ is the average of $n$ points, $p_i p_i'$ in
$\RR^{d^2}$:
if can be replaced with a convex combination of $d^2+1$ points (useful of $d^2+1 \ll n$)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast structure learning\\ with modular regularization}\\
  G. VerSteeg et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To recover the dependence structure among a large number of variables
from a small number of observations,
assume that each observed variable has a single latent parent.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.6,inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6}
  \node[very thick,draw,circle] (O-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\foreach \i in {1,3,5}
  \node[inner sep = 2.5pt,very thick,draw] (L-\i) at (\i,1) {};
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6}{
  \foreach \j in {1,3,5}{ 
    \draw[thick] (O-\i) -- (L-\j);
  }
}
\node at (3,-.5) {\footnotesize unconstrained model};
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.6,inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2}
  \node[very thick,draw,circle,fill=red!30] (O-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\foreach \i in {3,4}
  \node[very thick,draw,circle,fill=blue!30] (O-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\foreach \i in {5,6}
  \node[very thick,draw,circle,fill=green!30] (O-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\node[inner sep=2.5pt, very thick,draw,fill=red!30] (L-1) at (1,1) {};
\node[inner sep=2.5pt, very thick,draw,fill=blue!30] (L-3) at (3,1) {};
\node[inner sep=2.5pt, very thick,draw,fill=green!30] (L-5) at (5,1) {};
\draw[thick] (O-0) -- (L-1);
\draw[thick] (O-1) -- (L-1);
\draw[thick] (O-2) -- (L-1);
\draw[thick] (O-3) -- (L-3);
\draw[thick] (O-4) -- (L-3);
\draw[thick] (O-5) -- (L-5);
\draw[thick] (O-6) -- (L-5);
\node at (3,-.5) {\footnotesize latent factor model};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

The \emph{total correlation}
\begin{align*}
  \text{TC}(\mathbf X )
  &= \sum_i H(X_i) - H(\mathbf X) \\
  &= \text{KL}( \text{joint} \| \text{product of marginals} )
\end{align*}
aka multivariate mutual information
(it generalizes $H(X;Y) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X,Y)$)
can be used to characterize graphical models such that
\begin{align*}
  & \forall i \neq j \ \ Z_i \indep Z_j && \text{(latent)} \\
  & \forall i \neq j \ \ X_i \indep X_j \,|\, \mathbf Z && \text{(observed)}
\end{align*}
by $\text{TC}(X|Z) + \text{TC}(Z) = 0$.
For a ``modular'' model (each $X_i$ depends on a single $Z_j$),
it suffices to add $\forall i \ \text{TC} ( Z | X_i ) = 0 $.

To fit those models, set
$  Z = W X + \epsilon, $
$
\epsilon \sim N \bigl(
  0 ,
  \text{diag}(\mathbf \eta)
\bigr)  
$
and solve
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize} _W \text{TC}(X|Z) + \text{TC}(Z) + \text{penalty},
\]
where the penalty makes the $\text{TC}(Z|X_i)$ small and cancels out computationally expensive terms.

This can also be applied to large correlation matrices in finance: we recover industry classifications.

Numpy an Pytorch code available:
\texttt{LinearCorex}, \texttt{T-Corex}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Principal component projection and regression in nearly linear time\\ through asymmetric SVRG}\\
  Y. Jin and A. Sidford
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Principal component projection and PCR are usually done by explicit computation of the eigenvectors
or through polynomial approximations of the sign function.
Instead, use a \emph{rational approximation} (Zolotarev).
It is also possible to reduce the variance of the estimates by expanding the eigenvalue problem to higher dimensions.
The algorithm is almost linear in $\texttt{nnz}(A)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PIDForest: anomaly detection\\ via partial identification}\\
  P. Gopalan et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The \emph{sparsity} of a region $C$ is
\[ \text{sparsity}(C) = \dfrac{ \text{volume}(C) }{ \# \text{ points in } C }. \]
Anomalies can be partially identified (PID)
with their PID score:
\[
\text{score}(x) = \max_{\substack{ x \in C \\ C \text{ cube} }} \text{sparsity}(C),
\]
which can be efficiently approximated with a random forest.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A neurally plausible model\\ learns successor representation\\ in partially observable environments}\\
  E. V\'ertez and M. Sahani
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The \d{successor representation} (SR),
the expected discounted future state occupancy,
\[ M(s,s') = E \biggl[ \sum_{k \geq ` } \gamma^k \mathbf 1_{ s_{t+k} = s' } \biggm| s_t = s \biggr] \]
is a hybrid between model-based and model-free RL.
The \emph{successor features} replace state occupancy with state features..
If the reward function changes, one can use the SR to compute the value function --
bt it does not work with noisy observations.
To represent the distribution (belief) on the current state,
use the \emph{distributed distributional code} (DDC)
\[
\mu = \E_{ s \sim p } \bigl[ \psi(s) \bigr]
\qquad
M(\mu) = \E \biggl[ \sum_{k\geq1} \gamma^k \psi* s_{t+k} \biggm| \mu_t = \mu \biggr]
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning reward machines\\ for partially observable RL}\\
  R.T. Icarte et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
A \emph{reward machine} (RM)
is an automaton-based reward function.
It can be used as a memory for POMDP problems.
Learning a RM is a discrete optimization problem.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scene representation neworks: continuous 3D structure aware neural scene representation}\\
  V. Sitzmann et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
From a 2D image, use a \emph{hypernetwork}
to learn a \emph{scene representation network},
which can be fed to a \emph{neural renderer} to generate images from other points of view.
The SRN maps positions to the properties (colour, shape, etc.)
at that position/voxel;
contrary to voxel-based representations there are no discretization artefacts.
The neural renderer uses \emph{ray marching}
(look for objects closest to the camera on each ray from the camera),
sped up by predicting the distance to the nearest object fro the camera in each direction.

