\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Poincar\'e recurrence, cycles and spurious equilibria in gradient descent-ascent\\ for non-convex non-concave zero-sum games}\\
  E. Vlatakis
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Gradient descent-ascent does not always work or non-convex non-concave zero-sum games (GANs, etc.):
they suffer from spurious equilibria, cycles, and divergence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph-based discriminators:\\ sample complexity and expressiveness}\\
  R. Livni and Y. Mansour
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To compare two distributions from their samples,
use and \emph{integral probability measure} (IPM)
\[
\text{IPM}(p_1,p_2) = \sup_{ h \in H } \va{ \E_{ x \sim p_1 } \!\! h(x) - \E_{ x \sim p_2 } \!\! h(x) },
\quad H \subset \{0,1\}^X \]
or a more powerful \emph{graph-based discriminator}
\[
\text{IPM}(p_1,p_2) = \sup_{ g \in G } \va{ \E_{ x,y \sim p_1 } \!\! g(x,y) - \E_{ x,y \sim p_2 } \!\! g(x,y) },
\]
for $G \subset \{0,1\}^{X\times X}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning by abstraction:\\ the neural state machine}\\
  D. Hudson and C. Manning
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Neural nets can get the right answer for the wrong reason.
The \emph{neural state machine} is a differentiable graph-based model outputting a \emph{scene graph},
used as a state machine, with objects (with attribute -- colour, shape, etc -- given by several embeddings)
as nodes and relations (e.g., relative positions) as edges.
A quesion about the scene (visual question answering, VQA)
is translated into a sequence of instructions,
which are then run on the state machine.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Batched multi-arm bandits}\\
  Z. Gao
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
In the batched multi-arm bandit problem,
one chooses $k$ arms, gets the results, and plays again.
The grid ($k$) can be imposed or chosen.
% Not clear

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Are sample means in multi-arm bandits positively or negatively biased?}\\
  J. Shin et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The sample mean (of a fixed arm, at a fixed time) is negatively biasd for
$\epsilon$-greedy, UCB, Thompson thresholding. But for the chosen arm and a stopping time:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Negatively biased under optimistic sampling;
\item Positively biased under optimistic stopping;
\item Positively biased under optimistic choosing.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SIC-MMAB:\\ synchronization involves communication\\ in multi-player multi-arm bandits}\\
  E. Boursier
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
In the \emph{multi-player MAB}, the reward is zero in case of a collision.
If the collisions are observed and the players can comminicate,
the regret for $M$ players is
\[ \sum_{ k > M } \dfrac{ \log T }{ \mu_M - \mu_k }. \]
If they cannot communicate, this does not change:
if they are synchronized, they can use the collisions to exchange bits of information. 
If they are not synchronized (if they do not start at the same time)
and do not observe the collisions (only the zero rewards), this is trickier.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Recovering bandits}\\
  C. Pike-Burke and S. Grunewalder
  (2019)
\end{book}
Bandits are often used for product recommendation,
but you do not want to recommend a sofa to someone who has just bought one -- you should wait.
In \emph{recovering bandits}, the reward is a function of the time since the arm was last played,
modeled as a Gaussian process.
They require some strategic thinking:
if you expect a good arm's reward to increase,
you may want to wait and play a less good arm for the time being.
UCB and Thompson sampling can be modified into $d$-step look-ahead strategies.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Strategizing against no-regret learners}\\
  Y. Deng et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Mean-based algorithms can be fooled:
play a suboptimal action long-enough to trick your opponent into thinking it is optimal for you,
then play the actual optimal solution
for as long as it takes your opponent to realize they were duped.

No-regret algorithms include \textsc{exp}3, multiplicative weights, follow the perturbed leader.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time-accuracy tradeoffs for learning a ReLU with respect to Gaussian marginals}\\
  S. Goel et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{ReLU regression} looks for $w$ such that
$y \approx \text{relu}( w' x )$.
If $x \sim N(0,1)$, a shallow neural net with stochastic gradient descent learns $w$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Small ReLU networks are memorizers:\\ a tight analysis of memorization capacity}\\
  C. Yun et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
A 2-layer fully-connected ReLU network can memorize $N$ points with $\Theta(\sqrt N )$ notes
(vs $\Theta(N)$ for a 1-layer neural net).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On making stochastic classifiers deterministic}\\
  A. Cotter et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To solve a non-convex constrained problem, e.g. with fairness constraints
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & \theta \\
\text{To minimize} & g_0(\theta) \\
\text{Such that} & \forall i g_i(\theta) \leq 0
\end{array}
\]
look for Nash equilibrium of the Lagrangian
\[ \mathscr L ( \theta, \lambda ) = g_0(\theta) + \sum \lambda_i g_i(\theta). \]
There might be no pure Nash equilibrium, but there is a mixed one,
giving rise to a \emph{stochastic classifier}
(alternatively update $\theta$ and $\lambda$, and put a uniform distribution on the trajectory).

To transform a stochastic classifier into a deterministic one, one could use thresholding
$\hat f = \mathbf 1_ { f \geq 1/2 }$, but the performance can be very different
(for instance if $f \equiv 0.51$). Instead, use \emph{hashing}
(cf the difference between thresholding and dithering for images),
which fakes stochasticity -- it replaces stochasticity with arbitrariness.

One can try to interpolate between thresholding and hashing: partition the samples
into different regions and use a different threshold in each.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cold case: the lost MNIST digits}\\
  C. Yadov and L. Bottou
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
MNIST was not the whole data: 50,000 samples were discarded (computers were less powerful, then):
aMNIST adds them back and provides some metadata.

\emph{Testing set rot} is real: MNIST classifiers perform less well on the new data than on the initial dataset -- however, the ranking of the classifiers remains unchanged.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal sparse decision trees}\\
  X. Hu et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
It is possible to compute the \emph{optimal sparse decision tree}
(with a penalty on the number of nodes),
in time (often) linear in the number of samples and features,
with a proof of optimality
(in time exponential in the number of features).
Code available.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimizing generalized rate metrics \\with three players}\\
  H. Narasimhan et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
We often need to train models with \emph{fairness constraints},
e.g.,
$ \va{ \text{precision}(G_1) - \text{precision}(G_2) } \leq \epsilon $,
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \theta \\
  \text{To minimize} & \psi\bigl( R_1(\theta) , \dots , R_k(\theta) \bigr) \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ \psi_i \bigl( R_1(\theta), \dots, R_k(\theta) \bigr) \leq 0
\end{array}
\]
where the $R_k$'s are prediction rates,
\ie, expectations of counts (FPR, etc.) on subsets of the data.
Introduce slack variables
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & \theta, \xi \\
\text{To minimize} & \psi( \xi_1, \dots, \xi_k ) \\
\text{Such that} & \forall i \ \xi_i \geq R_i(\theta).
\end{array}
\]
The Lagrangian formulation
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_{\theta, \xi}
\operatorname*{Maximize}_{\lambda \geq 0}
\psi(\xi) - \lambda' \bigl( \xi - R(\theta) \bigr)
\]
is a 3-player game: use the exact solution for $xi$ ,
SGD for $\lambda$,
and SGD with surrogates for
$R_k(\theta) \leq \tilde R_k(\theta)$.

There is a TensorFlow constrained optimization (TFCO) library.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{This looks like that: deep learning\\ for interpretable image recognition}\\
  C. Chen et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Attention is not sufficient to explain what a convnet sees: it only tells where, not what.
Add a \emph{prototype layer}, which learns prototypes and outputs how similar each prototype is to a given patch; combine the prototype scores into output logits.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Paradoxes in fair machine learning}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Fairness in machine learning is often defined as some quantity, e.g., odds,
being equal among all groups
(for some predefined protected attributes).
Fairness in \emph{fair division} relies on a few axioms, e.g.,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Resource monotonicity: adding more resources makes everyone better off;
\item Population monotonicity: adding more people makes everyone worse off.
\end{compactitem}
Adding resource monotonicity to equal odds does not impact efficiency;
adding population monotonicity does.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multicriteria dimensionality reduction\\ with applications to fairness}\\
  T. Tantipongpipat et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
PCA is not fair: the reconstruction error need not be the same for all groups
(for instance, face reconstruction for males is easier than for females).
Instead, consider optimization problems of the form
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_{ P \text{ projection}} g( f_1(P), \dots, f_k(P) )
\]
where $f_i(P)$ is the reconstruction error for group $i$
and $g$ some aggregation function (e.g., sum, for PCA).
For instance
\begin{align*}
  & \operatorname*{Minimize}_P \max_{ i \in \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket }
  \Bigl( \max_Q \n{ A_i Q }_F^2 - \n{ A_i P }_F^2 \Bigr) \\
  & \operatorname*{Maximize}_P \prod \n{ A_i P }_F^2.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Implicit regularization\\ in deep matrix factorization}\\
  S. Arora et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Deep neural networks generalize well, even with no regularization --
it is widely believed that SGD provides implicit regularization.

The matrix completion problem is often solved with a nuclear norm penalty
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_W \sum_{(i,j) \in \Omega}  ( w_{ij} - b_{ij} )^2 + \lambda \n{ W }_* \]
but a deep matrix approximation (yes, a deep \emph{linear} network)
performs better
\[
  \operatorname*{Minimize}_{W_1,\dots,W_N} \sum_{(i,j) \in\Omega}
  \bigl[ (W_N W_{N-1} \cdots W_1) _{ij} - b_{ij} \bigr] ^2
\]
(no regularization).

In deeper networks, small singular values move more slowly with gradient descent,
while larger singular values move faster:
the longer gaps between singular values give a low-rank matrix.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SGD on neural networks learns\\ functions of increasing complexity}\\
  D. Kalimeris et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Stochastic gradient descent (SGD) starts to learn an ``essentially linear' classifier
(its performance can be explained by a linear classifier) and then increasingly complex models.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{When does label smoothing help?}\\
  R. M\"uller et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Label smoothing replaces hard targets
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.15,yscale=.25]
  \fill[blue!50] (1.6,0) rectangle (2.4,1);
  \draw[thick] (0,-.15) -- (0,.15);
  \draw[thick] (1,-.15) -- (1,.15);
  \draw[thick] (2,-.15) -- (2,.15);
  \draw[thick] (3,-.15) -- (3,.15);
  \draw[thick] (-.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
with soft targets
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.15,yscale=.25]
  \fill[blue!50] (-.4,0) rectangle (.4,.3);
  \fill[blue!50] (.6,0)  rectangle (1.4,.3);
  \fill[blue!50] (1.6,0) rectangle (2.4,1);
  \fill[blue!50] (2.6,0) rectangle (3.4,.3);
  \draw[thick] (0,-.15) -- (0,.15);
  \draw[thick] (1,-.15) -- (1,.15);
  \draw[thick] (2,-.15) -- (2,.15);
  \draw[thick] (3,-.15) -- (3,.15);
  \draw[thick] (-.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
\end{tikzpicture}:
this ensures that observations are close to their prototypes (weights of the last layer)
and far from the others -- the clusters are tighter.

Neural networks are over-confident:
temperature scaling and label smoothing can help calibrate them.

Label smoothing is bad for distillation: the model no longer knows how different the classes are.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Splitting steepest descent\\ for growing neural architectures}\\
  L. Wu et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To build smaller neural nets,
progressively split neurons into several copies.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Positional normalization}\\
  B. Li et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Normalization variants:
\[
