The moments $(\mu, \sigma)$ used for the normalization contain information:
re-inject them later in the network.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Variance reduction for matrix games}\\
  Y. Carmon et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
In zero-sum games
\[ \min_{ x \in \mathscr X } \max_{ y \in \mathscr Y } f(x,y), \]
$\mathscr Y$ can be seen as constraints (GAN) or uncertainty (robust optimization).

Examples of bilinear games
\( \min_x \max_y y'Ax \)
include \emph{matrix games} ($\mathscr X = \mathscr Y = \text{simplex}$),
SVM ($\mathscr X$ Euclidean, $\mathscr Y$ simplex)
and linear regression ($\mathscr X = \mathscr Y = \text{Euclidean}$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Provably robust deep learning\\ via adversarially trained smoothed classifiers}\\
  H. Salman et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}

\emph{Randomized smoothing} transforms a classifier $f$ into
\[ g(x) = \argmax_{ y \in \mathscr Y } \operatorname*{\text{P}}_{ \delta \sim N(0, \sigma^2 I) }
\bigl[ f(x+\delta) = y \bigr]. \]
Look for adversarial examples for the smoothed classifier.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Zero-shot knowledge transfer\\ via adversarial belief matching}\\
  P. Micaelli and A. Storkey
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Model compression (pruning, quantization, distillation)
usually requires training data, but one can use a generator instead.
This works even though the generated samples do not look like actual samples --
they suffice to describe the teacher's decision boundaries.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (N) at (0,0) {noise};
  \node[draw,minimum height=1cm] (G) at (.93,0) {generator $\phi$};
  \node[draw] (T) at (2,1) {teacher};
  \node[draw] (S) at (2,-1) {student};
  \node (yt) at (2.7,1)  {$y_T$};
  \node (ys) at (2.7,-1) {$y_S$};
  \draw[->] (N) -- (G);
  \draw[->] (G.east |- T.west) -- (T);
  \draw[->] (G.east |- S.west) -- (S);
  \draw[->] (T) -- (yt);
  \draw[->] (S) -- (ys);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\[
  \operatorname*{Maximize}_\phi \operatorname*{Minimize}_\theta \text{KL}( y_T, y_S )
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning perceptual inference by contrasting}\\
  C. Zhang et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Raven's progressive matrices} (RPM) are psychological tests for spatial-temporal reasoning
(``thinking in pictures'', for humans).
With computer-generated datasets (\textsc{raven}, \textsc{pgm}),
computers can have a go at it, modeling the attributes in the images and the rules they follow
with a \emph{Gumbel softmax}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Better transfer learning\\ with inferred successor maps}\\
  T. Madarasz and T. Behrens
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Replace the $Q$-value function
\[ Q^\pi_t(s,a) = \E\bigl[ \sum \gamma^k r_{t+k} | s_t = s, a_t = a \bigr] \]
with the \emph{successor representation} (SR)
\begin{align*}
  M_t^\pi &= \E \bigl[ \sum \gamma^k \mathbf 1 _{ s_{t+k+1} = s' } \bigm | s_t = s, a_t = a \bigr] \\
  Q^\pi(s,) &= \sum_{s'} M(s,a,s') w(s')
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Infra-slow brain dynamics\\ as a marker of cognitive function and decline}\\
  S. Ajmera
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Gaussian process factor analysis} (GPFA) can extract latent signals, at various time scales,
from fMRI data.
\begin{align*}
  y &: \text{ high-dimensional signal (observed)} \\
  x &: \text{ low-dimensional signal (latent)} \\
  y &= Ax + \text{noise} \\
  x_i &\sim \text{GP}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding sparse JL for feature hashing}\\
  M. Jagadeesan
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Sparse Johnson-Lindenstrauss} is a dimension reduction method trying to preserve distances,
defined by a random transformation, with $s$ nonzero entries in each column.
Equivalently, it is the sum of $s$ feature hashing dimension reductions.
\begin{align*}
& h : \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket \twoheadrightarrow \llbracket 1, m \rrbracket \text{ hash function} \\
& \sigma_i \in \{ \pm1 \} \text{ random} \\
& f(x) = \sum_{ j \in h^{-1} (i) } \sigma_j x_j
\end{align*}
There is a tradeoff between the number of hash functions $s$, the dimension $m$,
and $\ell^2$ distance preservation with 3 regimes:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $m$ small: poor performance, regardless of the number of hash functions;
\item $m$ large: performance proportional to $\sqrt s$ ($s=4$ is a good choice);
\item $m$ very large: good performance, regardless of the number of hash functions.
\end{compactitem}
The performance is measured by looking at vectors with small $\ell_\infty$-to-$\ell_1$ norm ratio.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A non-convex approach for exact and efficient multichannel sparse blind deconvolution}\\
  Q. Qu et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Gradient descent, Huber loss andpreonditioning efficiently solve the blind deconvolution problem.

