Applications include superresolution (microscopy) and neuron spike identification.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficiently learning\\ Fourier sparse set functions}\\
  A. Amrollahi et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The \emph{Walsh-Hadamard} (or Fourier) transform of a \emph{set function} $x: 2^V \to \RR$,
seen as a function $x: \FF_2^n \to \RR$, is
\[
\hat x : \left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \FF_2^n & \longrightarrow & \RR \\
  f & \longmapsto & \displaystyle \sum\limits_{ t \in \FF_2^n } (-1) ^ {\langle f, t \rangle} x_t .
\end{array}
\right.
\]
It is \emph{sparse} if
$\n{ \hat x }_0 \leq k$,
and low-frequency if $\forall f \in \supp \hat x \ \va f \leq d$,
where $\va f$ is the Hamming weight of $f$, \ie, the number of nonzero elements.

For instance, the \emph{cut function} of a graph,
\[ x(A) = \sum_{\substack{ s \in A \\ t \in V \subset A }} w(s,t) \]
is sparse ($k=\va E$) and low-frequency ($d=2$).

Applications include hyperparameter tuning, where $x \in 2^V$ is a boolean vector describing
the hyperparameters (SGD vs Adam, number of layers, layer size, etc.) and $F(x)$ the corresponding loss.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Surfing: iterative optimization\\ over incrementally trained deep networks}\\
  G. Song et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To invert a generator (e.g. a VAE), do not start with the fully trained model,
but follow a local optimum through the training (``surfing'') --
the loss landscape is much nicer at the begining.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{R2D2: repeatable and reliable\\ detector and descriptor}\\
  J. Revaud
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To align two images, identify the ``keypoints'', compute their ``descriptors' (e.g.,  \textsc{sift}),
and match the points if they are close.
We want the keypoints to be, not only repeatable (still present on a slightly modified image),
but also reliable (\ie, useful for matching -- this excludes repeated patterns),

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Training image estimators\\ without image ground truth}\\
  Z. Xia and A. Chakrabarti
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To learn how to restore image quality, e.g., for motion deblurring of face images,
when (low-quality, high-quality) image pairs are not available, use pairs of low-quality images
corresponding to the same high-quality image,
\begin{align*}
  y_1 &= \theta_1 x + \epsilon_1 \\
  y_2 &= \theta_2 x + \epsilon_2
\end{align*}
with a \emph{swap loss}
$\n{ \hat \theta_1 \hat x_2 + \hat \epsilon_1 - y1 }$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{KernelGAN: blind SR kernel estimation\\ using an internal GAN}\\
  S. Bell-Kligler
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Deep \emph{linear} network (equivalent to a 1-layer network, but with more local minima)
to infer the kernel to use for super-resolution (SR).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Differentiable ranking and sorting\\ using optimal transport}\\
  M. Cuturi et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The 1-dimensional optimal transport can be solved by sorting.
Conversely, the entropic regularization of the OT problem provides a differentiable regularization of the sorting and ranking operations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonparametric density estimation\\ and convergence rates\\ for GANs under Besov IPM losses}\\
  A. Uppal et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Density estimation does not work well in high dimensions, but GANs solve a similar problem.
GANs minimize an \emph{integral probability metric} (IPM)
\begin{align*}
  \hat P &= \argmin_Q \sup_{ f \in \mathscr F } \E_{ X \sim Q } \bigl[ f(X) \bigr] - \E_{ X \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ f(X) \bigr] \\
  &= \argmin_Q f_{\mathscr F} ( Q, \text{data} ) \\
  d_{\mathscr F}(P,Q) &= \sup_{ f \in \mathscr F } \va{ \E_{ X \sim P } \bigl[ f(X) \bigr] -
  \E_{X \sim Q} \bigl[ f(X) \bigr]
  }
\end{align*}
\emph{Besov spaces}
$B_p^s \approx \{ f \in L^p \ :\ \n{ f^{(s)} } \text{ bounded} \}$ are generalization of Sobolev spaces ($p=2$);
they are well-approximated with neural nets with ReLU activations.

% TODO: Besov spaces vs Sobolev spaces

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wasserstein Weisfeiler-Lehman graph kernels}\\
  M. Togninalli et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Traditional graph kernels decompose the graphs into simple substructures,
compute the similarities between those substructures and aggregate them in a simplistic way,
by averaging.
Use the Wasserstein distance instead (or, rather, $e^{- \lambda d_W }$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Putting an end to end-to-end}\\
  S. L\"owe et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Greedy InfoMax (GIM) is a form of semi-supervised (or even unsupervised) learning which learns a neural network without using end-to-end back-propagation
(the forward pass is still end-to-end, though, but can be done less often; learning can be asynchronous)
by dividing it into ``modules'' (groups of layers),
each with its own ``self-supervised'' loss, InfoNCE,
the mutual information between nearby patches (for images or sound).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the downstream performance\\ of compressed word embeddings}\\
  A. May et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The \emph{eigenspace overlap score} (EOS) measures how much information is retained when compressing word embeddings
$ A_{\text{raw}} \mapsto A_{\text{compressed}} $, from the left singular vectors,
$A = U D V'$,
\[ \text{EOS} = \dfrac1d \n{ U'_{\text{compressed}} U }_F^2. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Numerically accurate hyperbolic embeddings using tiling-based models}\\
  T. Yu and C. de Sa
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Euclidean models of hyperbolic space are numerically inaccurate far from the origin:
instead of using Euclidean coordinates, consider a tiling of hyperbolic space and represent a point by a tile (an element of the tiling group) and the Euclidean coordinates of the corresponding point in the central tile (close to the origin and therefore accurate).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{XLNet for language pretraining}\\
  Z. Yang et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Autoregressive language models are usually unidirectional:
XLNet changes this order of the words, so that it becomes bidirectional
\[ P[ x_{ i_{k+1} } | x_{i_1}, \dots, x_{i_k}, x_{i_{k+1}} ]. \]
The new position $i_{k+1}$ can be used as an attention key.

