\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A step towards quantifying\\ independently reproducible ML research}\\
  E. Raff
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
A paper is \emph{independently reproducible} if it is reproducible without the author's code.
They tried to reproduce 255 papers, succeeded for 60\% of them,
and identified the features of reproducible papers.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Average individual fairness}\\
  A. Roth
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Fairness is often defined from the false negative rate for groups of interest.
Instead of looking at the negative rate for a single problem for each group,
look at the negative rate over several problems for a single subject.

%% NeurIPS Workshops

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal transport methodology \\for lineage tracing}\\
  K. Yang
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To use optimal transport for \emph{lineage tracing} (to study cancer progression or embryogenesis):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use (unbalanced, relaxed) \emph{Monge} optimal transport,
\ie, learn a transport map (instead of a coupling),
parametrized by a neural net: it is faster and provides a mapping for new samples;
\item Use optimal transport wrt the Euclidean distance in a latent space, learned by an autoencoder.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation of Wasserstein distances\\ in the spiked transport model}\\
  J. Niled-Weed
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To estimate the Wasserstein distance $W_p(\mu,\nu)$ from samples
$X_1,\dots,X_n \sim \mu$,
$Y_1,\dots,Y_n \sim \nu$,
one is tempted to use the plugin estimator $W_p( \hat \mu_, \hat\nu_n )$,
but the difference is of the order $n^{1/d}$.:
the empirical distribution $\hat \mu_n$ is a bad estimator of $\mu$.
In some cases (e.g.,when $\mu$ has a density), better estimates are available.

While it may be possible to estimate $W_p(\mu,\nu)$
directly rather than through estimates $\hat \mu$ and $\hat \nu$ of $\mu$ and $\nu$,
the difference is still at least $(n \log n)^{-1/d}$.

The \emph{spiked covariance model} is $X \sim N(0,\Sigma)$,
$\Sigma = I + \beta v v'$,
where $I$ i high-dimensional noise and
$\beta v v'$ a low-dimensional perturbation.

The \emph{spiked transport model} assumes 
\begin{align*}
& \supp X, \supp Y \subset \mathscr U \text{ (low-dimensional)} \\
& \supp Z \subset \mathscr U ^\perp \\
& \mu = \text{Law}( X + Z ) \\
& \nu = \text{Law}( Y + Z )
\end{align*}
and looks for a coupling between $X$ and $Y$.
The difference is
\[ n^{-1/k} + \sqrt{ \dfrac{ d \log n }{ n } } \]
(the second term corresponds to the search for $\mathscr U$.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalizing learning with optimal transport}\\
  S. Jegelka et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
One can align word embeddings for different languages with optimal transport after adding some
rotational invariance:
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ \Gamma \in \Pi(\mu,\nu) } \min_{ P \in \mathscr F} \sum_{ij} \Gamma_{ij} d(x_i,P y_j) \]
with $\mathscr F = \{ P \in \RR^{d\times d} \ :\ \n{ P }_p \leq k \}$,
where $\n{\cdot}_p$ is the \emph{Shatten norm} (the $L^p$ norm of the singular values).

Use entropic regularization, normalization, and stabilized Sinkhorn.

Regularize the adversary: it should be close to an orthornormal transformation (no extreme distortion),
$ \beta \min_{ P \in O_n } \n{ f_W(X) - X P' } ^2 $, computed with \emph{orthogonal Procrustes},
$P^* = U V'$, where $f_W(X') X = U \Sigma V'$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards a mathematical theory of development}\\
  G. Schiebinger
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) provides snapshots if the genes expressed at various stages of development.
Optimal transport can link those snapshots:
\emph{unbalanced} optimal transport can account for cell proliferation and entropic regularization.

