\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & \pi \\
\text{To minimize} & \displaystyle \iint c(x,y) \pi(x,y) dx dy - \epsilon H(\pi) \\
\text{Such that}
& \pi( \cdot, y ) = P_{t_2} (y ) \\
& \pi( x, \cdot ) = P_{t_1} (y ) g(x)^{ t_2 - t_1 }
\end{array}
\]

Model the time dependence as $\dot x = f(x)$:
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_f
\n{ x_{ t_2 } - f( x_{ t_1 } ) }^2 + \text{reg}(f). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unsupervised hierarchy matching\\ with optimal transport over hyperbolic space}\\
  D. Alvarez-Melis et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To match hierarchies using optimal transport,
accounting for orthonormal transformations is not enough:
the order of the branches can change. Use continuous nonlinear mappings, parametrized by a neural net.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal transport mapping\\ input convex neural networks}\\
  A.V. Makkuva
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The optimal transport can be defined as $T= \nabla f^*$,
\[ f = \argmin_{ f \in \text{CVX} } \E_{X\sim P} \bigl[ f(X) \bigr] + \E_{ Y \sim Q } \bigl[ f^*(Y) \bigr] \]
and $f$ can be parametrized as an \emph{input convex neural network} (ICNN).
% There is another, more useful formula, but the notations are bad.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wasserstein style transfer}\\
  Y. Mroueh 
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To perform \href{https://wasserstein-transfer.github.io/}{style transfer},
from a style image $I_s$ to a content image $I_c$,
learn an encoder-decoder pair,
$ I \mapsto \bigl( F_j (I) \bigr) _j \mapsto I$,
where $j$ is the position, in the image, of the features $F$,
and a Monge map $T$ between the empirical distributions
$\sum \delta_{ F_j(I_c) }$ and $\sum \delta_{ F_j(I_s) }$.

That map is learned using a Gaussian approximation
\[ W_2(\mu,\nu) \geq W_2\bigl( N( \E\mu , \var\mu ), N( \E\nu, \var\nu ) \bigr). \]
The Wasserstein distance and the Monge map for Gaussians are known in closed form.

To limit content loss, consider the McCann interpolates
$(1-t)\text{Id} + t T$ instead.
To interpolate between styles, use Wasserstein barycenters
instead of linear interpolation.

% No details. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{From entropy-regularized optimal transport\\ to Sinkhorn divergences}\\
  A. Genevay (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
There are three ways of computing ``distances'' between distributions:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Csisz\'ar $\phi$-divergence, e.g., the Kullback-Leibler divergence;
\item Maximum mean discrepacies,
\[ \text{MMD}(\alpha, \beta) = \sup_{ \n{ f }_{\mathscr F} \leq 1 }
\va{ \E_{x\sim\alpha} [ fX ] - \E_{ y \sim \beta }[ fY ] } \]
for some RKHS $\mathscr H$;
\item Optimal transport, which is slower to compute, but provides better gradients.
\end{compactitem}
With an entropic regularization
\[ H(\pi | \alpha \otimes \beta ) = \int \log \dfrac{ d\pi(x,y) }{ d\alpha(x) d\beta(y) } d\pi(x,y), \]
the dual is an unconstrained optimization problem and the Sinkhorn algorithm solves it for discrete distributions.
But this regularization assumes the data is observed with noise, and tries to remove it:
you get
\[
<<FEROTTSD1,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=2,fig.height=.5,out.width='.4\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
set.seed(1)
n <- 50
x <- runif(n,0,4)
y <- runif(n,0,1)
par( mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
xlim <- c(-.1,4.1)
ylim <- c(-.1,1.1)
plot(x,y, xlim = xlim, ylim = ylim, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "")
k <- 6
xx <- seq( 0, 4, length = k+2 )[ 2:(k+1) ]
points( xx, rep(.5,k), pch=4, cex = 2, lwd = 6 )
points( xx, rep(.5,k), pch=4, cex = 2, lwd = 3, col = 'pink' )
@
\]
when you would prefer
\[
<<FEROTTSD2,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=2,fig.height=.5,out.width='.4\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
set.seed(1)
n <- 50
x <- runif(n,0,4)
y <- runif(n,0,1)
par( mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
xlim <- c(-.1,4.1)
ylim <- c(-.1,1.1)
plot(x,y, xlim = xlim, ylim = ylim, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "")
k <- 6
xx <- seq( 0, 4, length = k+2 )[ 2:(k+1) ]
points( xx, rep(c(.33,.66),k/2), pch=4, cex = 2, lwd = 6 )
points( xx, rep(c(.33,.66),k/2), pch=4, cex = 2, lwd = 3, col = 'pink' )
@
.
\]

The regularized Wasserstein distance is not a distance:
$d_{W,\epsilon}(\alpha,\alpha) \neq 0$.
Instead, consider the \emph{Sinkhorn divergence},
\[ \text{SD}_\epsilon(\alpha,\beta) = d_{W,\epsilon}(\alpha,\beta)
- \dfrac12 d_{W,\epsilon}(\alpha,\alpha)
- \dfrac12 d_{W,\epsilon}(\beta,\beta).
\]
For $\epsilon \to \infty$, it converges to a MMD.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How does mini-batching\\ affect Curvature information \\for second order deep learning optimization?}\\
  D. Granziol et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
$\spec(A+\text{noise})$ is not an unbiased estimator of $\spec A$. In particular, the largest eigenvalue is perturbed upwards.
Random matrix theory (RMT) can correct this bias.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Acceleration through spectral modeling}\\
  F. Pedregosa and S. Scieur
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Accelerated gradient methods only use the largest (and smallest) eigenvalue(s) of the Hessian,
ignoring the rest of the spectrum:
model the spectral distribution as a \emph{Marcenko-Pastur} distribution
(fitted using the largest eigenvalue and the trace)
and use it to find the optimal first-order method.
In special cases, this is equivalent to the \emph{heavyball} method.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The importance of better models\\ in stochastic optimization}\\
  H. Asi and J Duchi
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
SGD is very sensitive to step size and can diverge.
\d{(Approximate) proximal optimization} (aProx) minimizes an approximation of the objective
with a penalty to keep the new point close to the previous. Approximations include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The function itself (\ie, no approximation, just a penalty);
\item A linear approximation;
\item A linear approximation floored at zero (it is often known that $f \geq 0$):
this prevents divergence and can be implemented by replacing
$ x \leftarrow x - \alpha g $ with
\[ x \leftarrow x = \min \Bigl\{ \alpha, \dfrac{ f(x) }{ \n g ^2 } \Bigr\} g. \]
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Beyond SGD: in search of a preconditioner}\\
  E. Hazan
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Preconditining makes the loss surface more isotropic.

LiSSA (linear time second order stochastic optimization) writes the Hessian inverse
as an infinite series (assuming the eigenvalues are below 1 in absolute value) and samples from it;
to estimate the powers, use iid samples (one sample for each, \ie, rank-1 matrices).
\begin{align*}
  H^{-1} &= \sum_{ k \geq 0 } ( I - H )^k \\
  H^{-1} &\approx ( I - H )^k p \cdot p_k^{-1} \\
  (I-H)^k &\approx \prod_{ 1 \leq i \leq k } ( I - H_i )
\end{align*}
For non-convex functions, use cubic regularization (FastCubic).
For neural nets, there is no improvement on SGD.

Replace AdaGrad
$ x \leftarrow x - \diag( \sum g_t g_t' ) ^{-1/2} g $
(you can take the full matrix instead of the diagonal)
with \emph{GGT},
$ x \leftarrow x - (G_t G_t' )^{-1/2} g_t $,
where $G_t = [ g_t | \beta g_{t-1} | \beta^2 g_{t-2} | \cdots ]$.
It can be efficiently implemented using
$(GG')^{-1/2} x = G ( G' G )^{-3/2} x$,
since $G'G$ is smaller than $GG'$.
Plot the eigenvalues of $GG'$ against the epoch.

AdaGrad keeps two gradients, Adam three.
To reduce memory usage, only keep a ``low-rank approximation'' of the gradients (first reshape them into matrices or tensors): \emph{extreme tensoring}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Symmetric and multi-secant\\ quasi-Newton methods}\\
  D. Scieur et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Quasi-Newton} methods approximate the Hessian using the gradient and the secant equation
$ f''(x)^{-1} ( f' y - f'x ) \approx y-x $.
Let $\Delta x$ and $\Delta G$
be the matrices containing the first differences of successive estimates and gradients,
and $H$ be an estimator of $(f'')^{-1}$.
The secant equation becomes $H \Delta G = \Delta X$.
Quasi-Newton methods find a matrix $H$, close (for some norm),
to some reference matrix $H_0$, such that $H \Delta G = \Delta X$.
For instance, LBFG uses $H_0 = H_k$, $N=1$ seconds, $d(H,H_0) = \n{ H - H_0 }_{ \nabla^2 f }$.
For $N>1$, the secant equation has no solution in general:
use an approximation instead (it reduces to a Procrustes problem).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic Newton and cubic Newton methods with simple local linear quadratic rates}\\
  D. Koralev et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Cubic regularization} generalizes Newton's method
%($M=0$)
\[ \langle f' x_k , x - x_k \rangle + \dfrac12 \n{ x - x_k }_{f''(x_k)} ^2 + \dfrac M6 \n{x - x_k}^3.
\]
\emph{Subsampled methods} estimate the gradient and the Hessian on different (independent) subsets;
use a very large minibatch for the Hessian.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{K-FAC: extensions,\\ improvements and applications}\\
  J. Martens
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\[
