Second-order methods should be faster, but the curvature changes very quickly:
the second-order approximation is only valid locally --
use a \emph{trust region}, either as a hard constraint or, equivalently, as an $L^2$ penalty.

One can replace the Hessian wit GGN, Fisher information or (not recommended)
empirical Fisher information.

The \d{generalized Gauss--Newton} method approximates the Hessian of
\[ h(\theta) = \dfrac1m \sum_{i=0}^m L\bigl(y_i, f(x_i, \theta) \bigr) \]
as $ G = \tfrac1m \sum J_i' H_i J_i$
where $J_i = \nabla_{\!\theta} f$, $H_i = \nabla_{\!zz} L$.
This is the Hessian of $h$ with $f$ replaced with its first-order approximation around $\theta$.
The square norm loss, $L(y,z) = \tfrac12\n{y-z}^2$
gives $H=I$ and $G=\tfrac1m\sum J_i' J_i$ (Gauss--Newton).
When $z$ is the natural parameter of an exponential family,
$G$ is the Fisher information and $G^{-1} \nabla h$ the natural gradient.
GGN works better than the Hessian in practice.

The Hessian is too large, but many approximations are available:
diagonal (Adam), block-diagonal (\textsc{tonga}), low-rank + diagonal (L-BFGS), Krylov (Newton-CG, HF).

The \d{Kronecker-factorized approximate curvture} (K-FAC)
exploits the structure in layers of the neural net
For a single linear layer,
$a \mapsto s = Wa$, and $L( y,f(x,\theta)) = -\log p(y|x,\theta)$,
$\nabla_{\!q} L = g a'$,
where $g = \nabla_{\!s} L$ and the Fished information is
$ F = \cov( \operatorname{vec} \nabla_{\!w}, \operatorname{vec} \nabla_{\!w} ) = \cdots - E[aa;] \otimes E[gg'] =  A \otimes G$.
The Kronecker product allows efficient computations:
$(B \tens C)^{-1} = B^{-1} \tens C^{-1}$, $(B\tens C) \operatorname{vec} X = \operatorname{vec} ( C X B' ).$

Shared weights, in CNNs or RNNs, complicate things.

K-FAC works better than SGD for large batch sizes.
TensorFlow implementation available.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HAWKV2: Hessian-aware trace-weighted quantization of neural networks}\\
  A. Gholami
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To choose how many bits to keep for each layer, use the trace of the Hessian, computed as
\[ \tr A = \E_{ z \sim \text{Rademacher} } \bigl[ z' A z \bigr]. \]
Implementation: \texttt{PyHessian}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{New methods for regularization path optimization via differential optimization}\\
  P. Grigas and H. Liu
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The first order condition defining the regularization path,
$\nabla_{\!x} F_\lambda \bigl( x(\lambda) \bigr) = 0$
leads to an ODE,
which can be solved with the Euler scheme (or a better one);
it involves the Hessian, but computations canbe avoided using the conjugate gradient (CG).

%% Workshop: learning with temporal point processes

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Foundations of causal inference:\\ challenges and opportunities of big data}\\
  N. Kiyavash
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Granger causality can be extended to point processes,
using \emph{directed information} as a loss function;
the resulting \emph{directed information graph} (DIG)
is a Bayesian network;
it can also, more easily, be estimated from a model of the point process, e.g.,
a \emph{multivariate Hawkes process}.
Applications include neural spike trains, twitter activity,
econometric modeling of systemic risk,
hyperlink creation between (one million) websites (showing clusters of merchant and review sites).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Insider threat detection via hierarchical\\ neural temporal point processes}\\
  X. Wu
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Detect fraudulent sessions
(from login time, email activity, web activity, device access, opening/copying/creating/deleting files)
by combining RNNs (LSTM) and masked temporal point processes (MTPP),
using encoder-decoders,
predicting activity type and time (intra-session) and session time (inter-session).

Datasets: CERT insider threat, Wikipedia vandalism.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Temporal point processes:\\ web, social media, and networking systems}\\
  N. Ganguly
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Hawkes-RNN models can be used to study:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Temporal and information influence of social media users;
  latent opinion inference;
\item Tweet-hashtag (reinforcement) and hashtag-hashtag ineractions;
\item Spikes in network traffic.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Temporal logic point processes}\\
  S. Li et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Add prior knowledge (``event A happens before B'') to temporal point processes (TPP)
using \emph{temporal logic} soft constraints (with learned weights).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The graph Hawkes network for reasoning\\ on temporal knowledge graphs}\\
  Z. Han et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
A \emph{temporal knowledge graph} stores
(S,V,O,timestamp) tuples.
In a \emph{neural Hawkes process}, the intensity of events of type $k$ is
$\lambda_k = f( w_k ' h_t )$.
However, there are too many event types, and some are concurrent.
Learn vector representations of entities and predicates;
respond to queries of the form
(S,V,?,$t$).

Data: political events, news.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep point process destructors}\\
  D. Inouye
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\d{Destructive learning} identifies patterns in the data, removes them in an invertible way, and starts again.
For point processes, use time warping.
A point process destructor is a time transformation that maps the point process studied
to the unit process $\lambda \equiv 1$.

For deep point processes destructors, e.g. if $D = D_2 \circ D_1$,
\[ \lambda(t) = \dfrac{ dD }{ dt } = \lambda_2\bigl( D_1(t) \bigr) \lambda_1(t). \]
More generally, a \emph{deep point process} replaces
the mixture $\lambda_t = \sum_i \lambda_i(t)$
with $\lambda(t) = \prod_i \lambda_i( t^{(i-1)} )$,
$t^{(0)} = t$, $t^{(i)} = D_i( t^{(i-1)} )$
(each $D_i$ can be a weak model).

%% NeurIPS 2019 Workshop: causal inference

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bandits, estimation and hypothesis testing}\\
  S. Athey
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Bandits (aka \emph{adaptive experiments} in statistics)
are more data-efficient than traditional experiments.
Bandits create unbalanced data (they are not iid);
naively estimating the value of an arm is potentially biased and non-Gaussian.
\emph{Inverse propensity weighting} addresses those problems
but increases variance;
there are other weighting schemes.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Preserving causal constraints in counterfactual explanations for machine learning classifiers}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Counterfactual explanations} should be actionable (you cannot decrease your age or education level)
and satisfy some constraints (you cannot be under 20 and have a PhD):
use a structural causal model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{EconML: a machine learning library\\ for estimating heterogeneous treatment effects}\\  
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Includes: DeepIV, DoubleML, causal forests, etc.

%% NeurIPS 2019 Workshop: The optimization foundations of reinforcement learning

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive TRPO: convergence\\ and faster rates for regularized MDPs}\\
  L. Shani et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\d{Mirror descent} is the trust-region optimization algorithm
\[
  x_{k+1} \leftarrow \argmin_x \bigl\langle \nabla f(x_k) , x - x_k \bigr\rangle +
  \alpha_k B_\omega( x, x_k )
\]
where the first term is a linear approximation of the objective
and the second term is a \emph{Bregman divergence}
\[ B_\omega(x,y) = \omega(x) - \omega(y) - \langle \nabla \omega(y), x - y \rangle. \]

If $\omega$ is the Euclidean norm,
$B_\omega$ is the Euclidean distance and mirror descent is
projected gradient descent.

If $\omega$ is the negentropy,
$B_\omega$ is the Kullback-Leibler divergence,
and mirror descent is exponentiated gradient descent.

%% NeurIPS workshop: Metalearning

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How metalearning could help us\\ accomplish our grandest AI ambitions,\\ and early, exotic steps in that direction}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
% Metalearning
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=2pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node[align=center,draw] (A) at (0,0) {task\\[-1mm]+\\[-1mm]dataset};
  \node[align=center,draw] (B) at (4,0) {Model $\phi$\\new data $\overset{f_\phi}{\longmapsto}$ forecasts};
  \draw[->] (A) -- node[anchor=south] {\footnotesize $g_\theta$} (B);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\emph{Generative teaching networks} (GTN) generate data to learn on.
Backpropagate to train the generator,
but use \emph{weight normalization}. Use different model architectures.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=1,inner sep=2pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node[align=center,draw] (A) at (-.5,0) {noise};
  \node[align=center,draw] (B) at (.85,0) {fake data};
  \node[align=center,draw] (C) at (2,0) {model};
  \node[align=center,draw] (D) at (2,-1) {real data};
  \node (E) at (3,-.5) {performance};
  \draw[->] (A) -- node[anchor=south] {\footnotesize generator} (B);
  \draw[->] (B) -- node[anchor=south] {\footnotesize learn} (C);
  \draw[->] (C.east) to[out=0, in=135,  looseness=1] (E.north);
  \draw[->] (D.east) to[out=0, in=-135, looseness=1] (E.south);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

In RNNs, do not only store information in the activations, but also in the weights,
using \emph{Hebbian learning}:
``neurons that fire together wire together''.
\[ w_{ij}^{t+1} = w_{ij}^t + \eta x_i^t x_j^t. \]
Train $w$ and $\alpha$ (``differentiable plasticity'') with SGD:
\begin{align*}
  y_i &= \tanh \sum_i ( w_{ij} + \alpha_{ij} H_{ij}^t ) y_i \\
  H_{ij}^{t+1} &= \eta y_i y_j + (1-\eta) H_{ij}^t.
\end{align*}
In \emph{modulated Hebbian learning},
the Hebbian trace $H_{ij}$ is only updated if some neuron $M$ fires:
\[ H_{ij}^{t+1} = H_{ij}^t + M_t y_i^{t-1} y_j^t. \]

To prevent \emph{catastrophic forgetting},
use a mask to select which par of the network to use for the current task.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.8,yscale=.5,inner sep=2pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (A) at (-.4,0) {input};
  \node[minimum width=1.5cm,draw] (B1) at (2,1) {};
  \node[minimum width=1.5cm,draw] (B2) at (2,0) {};
  \node[circle,draw,inner sep=0pt] (C) at (3.8,0) {$\times$};
  \node (D) at (5.3,0) {output};
  \draw[->] (.6,0) |- (B1);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B2);
  \draw[->] (B1) -| (C);
  \draw[->] (B2) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (D);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

One-ended algorithms (natural selection, human culture)
generate their own problems and solve them.
The ``paired open-ended trailblazer'' (\textsc{poet}) generates environments of varying difficulties
(e.g., landscape, for a robot to navigate in);
trains an agent on each of them in parallel;
re-evaluate the agents on all models and copy them accordingly
(this works better than linearly interpolating between easy and difficult environments --
curriculum learning is hard, and a linear curriculum is rarely optimal).

% TODO: ICML tutorial on population-based methods (2019)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Better model-based RL through meta-RL}\\
  P. Abbeel
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Use \emph{domain randomization}:
simulated environments do not look like the real world,
but they may be as different between them as they are different from the real world --
instead of using just one simulated environment,
use many, and measure the performance on unseen environments.

Overfitting plagues model-based RL much more than supervised learning:
the agent would try to exploit regions of the state space where the model is imprecise but promising.
Use domain randomization,
e.g., with an ensemble of models.

Train asynchronously.

To speed up meta-RL: train separate RL agents for all the tasks,
then train a global agent to match those local agents (imitation learning)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Abstraction and meta-RL}\\
  D. Abel
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{State abstraction} tries to make reinforcement learning easier
by replacing the state space with a smaller, discrete set,
with no drop n the quality of the solution learned.

\emph{Action abstraction} replaces the action space with a larger one, by adding \emph{options},
\ie, long-term actions (defined by a start condition, an end condition, and a policy).
Finding options that minimize planning time is NP-hard,
and hard to approximate (at least in the worst case).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable metalearning}\\
  R. Hadsell
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{WarpGrad} learns to precondition the gradient -- it learns the geometry of the space while
optimizing the model.

Vanilla transformers do not work well in RL: add (GRU-like) gating.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Metalearning with warped gradient descent}\\
  S. Flennerhag
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Learn how to warp the loss surface into something nice;
this is equivalent to learning a gradient preconditioner.

%% NeurIPS 2019 workshop: Robust AI in financial services: data, fairness, explainability, trustworthiness and privacy

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fairness assessment for AI\\ in the finance industry}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\texttt{AIF360} is a Python library for fairness measures and mitigation algorithms.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting firm material event sequences from SEC 8k reports}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Seq2seq transformer model to map past events to future events;
the attention maps provide explanations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Explainable small business credit scoring}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Accounting software companies have access to detailed accounting data and can use it to decide whether to grant a loan (xgboost, monotonicity constraints, Shapley values).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adversarial learning of ``deep fakes''\\ in accounting}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Adversarial autoencoders} impose a structure on the latent representation,
e.g., a mixture of clearly separated Gaussians, to disentangle.

Auditors use ERP data: a fraudulent company could use an adversarial attack to hide anomalous transactions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Imperceptible attacks on tabular data}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Tabular data is also suceptible to adversarial attacks,
Since not all features are equally important
(you can lie more about your number of pets than about your salary),
define a notion of ``perceptability''
and minimize it to mount your attack
(LowProFool, DeepFool, FGSM).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding equilibria in multiagent systems}\\
  M. Woolridge
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
While Siri does not (yet) talk to Siri,
\emph{multiagent systems} are already there,
e.g., with algorithmic trading -- but those systems are unstable and can lead to flash crashes.

1. To understand this instability,
consider it as a bug, and use \emph{model checking}:
build the state transition graph of the system and use \emph{temporal logic} (LTL,
an extension of propositional logic with temporal operators)
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \Diamond \phi & \text{eventually} \\
  \Box \phi & \text{always} \\
  \phi \mathscr U \psi & \text{until} \\
  \Box \Diamond \phi\ & \text{infinitely often} \\
  \Diamond \Box \phi & \text{always after some point}
\end{array}
\]
to formulate and check the desired properties of the system, in particular
reachability $\Diamond \phi$ and invariance $\Box \phi$.

2. This is too restrictive:
an undesirable state could be reachable only through a sequence of irrational decisions.
Use the \emph{Reactive Modules} language
to describe the agents and their goals,
and check reachability under Nash equilibrium.

3. This approach only works on small systems:
for large systems, use \emph{agent-based models},
calibrated on real data, and simulations.

The flash crash prevention measures (stop trading if the prices drop too much)
seem ineffective and even counterproductive:
if investors need to sell ad cannot, they will find something else to sell --
the flash crash is contagious.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Putting ethical AI to the vote}\\
  A. Procaccia
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
When there are several stakeholders for a given problem
(e.g., food donations allocation),
if the problem is recurring,
build a model for each stakeholder
and aggregate their preferences (\emph{social choice}).
Since the output of the model is noisy, the aggregation mechanis, should be robust to noise:
Borda count is robust,
pairwise-majority consistent (PMC) methods are not.
[This is currently used as a decision-support tool; it is more efficient than humans
in terms of diversity and cost.]

Other applications of this \emph{virtual democracy} (the models vote, or the humans)
include ethical decisions for self-driving cars (aggregating the ethical choices of one million Americans in a fraction of a second), or ESG investment (when several people, e.g., a family, want to invest together, but do not have the same views on ESG).

%% NeurIPS 2019 Workshop: Bayesian deep learning

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gaussian process behaviour\\ in wide deep neural networks}\\
  A.G.G. Matthews
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
As the width of a 1-layer neural net tends to infinity,
it converges to a Gaussian process (natural tangent kernel).
This is still the case for $k$-layer neural nets, if deeper layers grow faster.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The natural tangent kernel}\\
  T.G.J. Rudner et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
In continuous time, gradient descent is just an ODE,
$\dot \theta = - \eta \nabla_{\!\theta} \mathscr L (\theta)$;
it also gives us the dynamics of the output of the neural net during gradient descent:
a diffusion, for the \emph{neural tangent kernel}
\[ \Theta(x,y) = \nabla_{\!\theta} f(\theta) \nabla_{\!\theta} f(\theta)'. \]

For natural gradient descent, this becomes
\[ \Theta^{\text{nat}}(x,y) = \nabla_{\!\theta} f(\theta) F(\theta)^{-1} \nabla_{\!\theta} f(\theta)', \]
where $F(\theta)$ is the Fisher information matrix.
Linearize those dynamics and solve the resulting ODE, instead of SGD to train the network.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep ensembles: a loss landscape perspective}\\
  S. Fort et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Ensembles work better than Bayesian neural nets:
ensemble can cover several modes;
Bayesian approaches struggle to (since the modes are connected, they should be able to).

Average the weights around each mode,
and the predictions across modes.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MIM Mutual information machine}\\
  M. Livne et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Replace the VAE loss
\[\ell(\theta) = \text{KL} \bigl(
  q_\theta(z|x) p(x) 
\bigl\|
  p_\theta(x|z) p(z)
\bigr)
\]
where $x$ is the observed variable and $z$ the latent representation,
with the more symmetric Jensen-Shannon divergence,
and add a regularizer
\[
  H\bigl(
  q_\theta(z|x) p(x) 
  \bigr)
  +
  H\bigl(
  p_\theta(x|z) p(z)
  \bigr)
\]
(entropy of the two joint distributions on $(x,z)$)
to help maximize the mutual information between $x$ and $z$
\[ H(x,z) = H(x) + H(z) - I(x;z). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pitfalls of in-domain uncertainty estimation and ensembling in deep learning}\\
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Metrics used for uncertainty estimation (log-likelihood, Brier score, calibration error, AUC)
have pitfalls, but can be fixed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural tangents: fast and easy\\ infinite neural networks in Python}\\
  R. Novak et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Infinite-width neural nets (Gaussian processes) in JAX/stax.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using loss surface geometry\\ for practical Bayesian deep learning}\\
  A.G. Wilson
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\url{http://losslandscape.com/}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple baseline\\ for Bayesian uncertainty in deep learning}\\
  W. Maddox et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
SWAG fits SGD iterates with a low-rank + diagonal Gaussian distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Can you trust your model's uncertainty?}\\
  J. Snoek et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Measures of uncertainty include
the expected calibration error (ECE), which can be gamed,
and \emph{proper scoring rules} such as the negative log-likelihood, which emphasizes tails too much,
or the Brier score.

Methods include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Post hoc calibration, with temperature scaling;
\item Ensembling;
\item Monte Carlo dropout;
\item Stochastic variational inference;
\item Bayesian only in the last layer.
\end{compactitem}

Under distribution shift, post hoc calibration is the worst,
ensembling (with small ensembles: 5) is the best.

