\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Function space prior in Bayesian deep learning}\\
  R. Grosse
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The \emph{automatic statistician} searches for compositional GP kernels
describing the data (and can output an English text describing the model):
having a better prior makes it better at extrapolation.

The \emph{neural kernel network} (NKN) is a neural network computing
$(x,y) \mapsto k(x,y)$;
each layer combines primitive kernels (or kernels from previous layers),
using additions and multiplications
(good for extrapolation and exploration).

\emph{Functional variational BNNs} specify a prior on the stochastic process, not on the weights;
variational inference can be done on function (not weight) space.
[GPs are currently still preferable.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Try depth instead of weight correlation}\\
  S. Farquhar et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The widespread belief that the approximate posterior for Bayesian neural nets
needs correlations between weights only holds for shallow networks.
Even for linear networks, $n\geq3$ layers gives a full correlation matrix for the outputs.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep generative models\\ for genetic variation and drug design}\\
  D. Marks
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
The alignment and evolution of biological sequences (mutation, insertion, deletion)
can be modeled with Bayesian VAEs or Bayesian seq2seq models, and be used to sample from possible future flu viruses to make vaccines.

