\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measure-valued derivatives\\ for approximate Bayesian inference}\\
  M. Rosca et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Besides the \textsc{reinforce} (score function) and reparametrization estimators of the gradient of an expectation
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{d}{d\theta} \E_{ x \sim p_\theta } f(x) &=
  \dfrac{ d }{ d \theta } \int f(x) p_\theta(x) dx \\
  &= \int f(x) \dfrac{ d p_\theta }{ d\theta } dx \\
  &= \int f(x) \dfrac{ d p_\theta }{ d\theta } \dfrac{ p_\theta(x) }{ p_\theta(x) } dx \\
  &= \int f(x) \dfrac{ d \log p_\theta }{ d\theta } p_\theta(x) dx \\
  &= \E_{ x \sim p_\theta } \bigl[ f(x) \nabla_{\!\theta} \log p_\theta(x) \bigr]
  \\
  \dfrac{d}{d\theta} \E_{ x \sim p_\theta } f(x) &=
  \dfrac{d}{d\theta} \E_{ x \sim p } f \bigl( g_\theta(x) \bigr) \\
  &\hphantom{{}={}}
  \text{if }
  z \sim p \implies g_\theta(z) \sim p_\theta  
\end{align*}
the \emph{measure-valued derivative} is less known
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{ d }{ d\theta } \E_{ x \sim p_\theta } &=
\int f(x) \dfrac{ d p_\theta }{ d\theta } dx \\
&= c_\theta \bigl( \E_{ x \sim p_\theta^+} f(x) - \E_{ x \sim p_\theta^-} f(x) \bigr)
\end{align*}
[when sampling from $p_\theta^+$ and $p_\theta^-$,
use the same random numbers, to lower variance]
where $dp_\theta / d\theta = c_\theta ( p_\theta^+ - p_\theta^- )$
is the decomposition of the signed measure
$dp_\theta / d\theta$ into  difference of probability measures,
up to a multiplicative factor (e.g., \emph{Hahn-Jordan decomposition})
[the authors provide a table of such decompositions].
For instance, for the Gaussian distribution,
\[
  \dfrac{ d }{ d\mu } \phi_{\mu,1} (x) =
  \dfrac{ 1 }{ \sqrt{ 2 \pi } }
  \bigl(
  W_{2,.5} ( x - \mu ) -
  W_{2,.5} ( \mu - x)
  \bigr)
\]
where $W$ is the Weibul distribution.

This requires no strong assumptions
(it works with discrete distributions and discontinuous $f$),
has low variance,
but requires $2\va \theta$ evaluations of $f$
(in terms of clock time, the score function with baseline is faster for $\va\theta \geq 100$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Monte Carlo gradient estimation\\ in machine learning}\\
  S. Mohamed et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Survey article:
why we need the gradient 
\[ \nabla_{\!\theta} \E_{ x \sim p_\theta } \bigl[ f_\theta(X) \bigr] \]
and three ways of computing it: score function (\textsc{reinforce}), reparametrization (``pathwise'')
and measure-valued.

%% NeurIPS workshop: information theory and machine learning

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Information in the weights and emergent properties of deep neural networks}\\
  S. Soatto and  A. Achille
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.5,yscale=.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node (x) at (0,0) {$x$};
  \node (z) at (1,0) {$z$};
  \node (y) at (2,0) {$y$};
  \node (n) at (1,-1) {$n$};
  \draw[->] (x) -- (z);
  \draw[->] (z) -- (y);
  \draw[->] (n) -- (x);
  \node at (0,1) {\footnotesize data};
  \node at (1,1) {\footnotesize representation};
  \node at (2,1) {\footnotesize task};
  \node at (1,-2) {\footnotesize nuisance};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Supervised learning looks for a representation $z$, as small as possible,
\ie, minimizing $I(x;z)$, containing as much information on the task as the input, \ie,
$I(z;y) = I(x;y)$.
The Lagrangian of the constrained optimization problem is
\[ \mathscr L\bigl( p(z|x) \bigr) = \underset{\text{\footnotesize crossentropy}}{H( y | z )} + \underset{\text{\footnotesize regularizer}}{\beta \cdot I(z;x).} \]

Troublingy, setting $z$ to the index of the image in the training set is close to optimal
(16 bits on \textsc{cifar}):
the Lagrangian should be minimized, not on the training data, but on the data we do not have yet.

Information is also stored in the weights.
However, since the weights are real, the information is infinite, and most if it is irrelevant:
the relevant information can be measured by how much noise can be added to the weights without changing
the result (or the drop in accuracy as a function of noise).
More precisely,
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Minimize} & \text{KL}\bigl( q(w|\text{data}) \| p(w)  \bigr) \\
  \text{Such that} & \displaystyle \E_{ w \sim q(w|\text{data}) } \bigl[ \mathscr L_{\text{data}}(w) \bigr] \leq t 
\end{array} \]
with Lagrangian
\begin{align*}
  \mathscr L &= \!\!\!
  \displaystyle
  \underset{
    \text{\footnotesize expected loss}
  }{
    \E_{ w \sim q(w|\text{data}) } \bigl[ \mathscr L_{\text{data}}(w) \bigr]
  }
  + \beta \cdot
  \underset{
    \clap{\text{\footnotesize information in the weights}}
  }{
    \text{KL}\bigl( q(w|\text{data}) \| p(w)  \bigr)
  }
\end{align*}

SGD minimizes the Fisher information of the weights.

The distance between two tasks (for transfer learning)
can be defined as
\[ d( \mathscr D_1 \to \mathscr D _2 ) =
\underset{
  \substack{
    \text{\footnotesize complexity of}\\
    \text{\footnotesize learning together}
  }
}{
  I(\mathscr D _1 \mathscr D _2 ; w )
}
-
\underset{
  \substack{
    \text{\footnotesize complexity of}\\
    \text{\footnotesize learning one}
  }
}{
  I( \mathscr D_1 ; w )
}
\]
Alternatively, on can represent a task with the diagonal of its Fisher information matrix (task2vec),
\[ d(\mathscr D_1, \mathscr D_2) = \cos(F_1, F_2). \]

