\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GAIT:\\ a geometric approach to information theory}\\
  J. Gallego et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Shannon entropy considers that all symbols are different in the same way.
Information theory can be extended to account for the geometry of the space --
like the Wasserstein distance, but in closed form.
Consider a \emph{similarity space} $(\mathscr X, \kappa)$,
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \forall x, y & 0 \leq \kappa(x,y) \leq 1 \\
  \forall x & \kappa(x,x) = 1
\end{array} \]
and a probability distribution $P$ on $\mathscr X$.
\begin{align*}
  KP(x) &= \E_{ y \sim p } \bigl[ \kappa(x,y) \bigr] && \text{ordinariness of $x$} \\
  1/KP(x) &&& \text{rarity of $x$} \\
  H^k_1[p] &= \E_{x \sim p} \bigl[ -\log KP(x) \bigr].
\end{align*}
It is conjectured that $H_1^k$ is concave (under reasonable conditions):
we can use $-H_1^k$ to define a similarity-sensitive Bregman divergence.
\[
D^k(P\|Q) = 1 +
\E_{ x \sim P } \biggl[ \log \dfrac{ KP(x) }{ KQ(x) } \biggr] -
\E_{ x \sim Q } \biggl[ \log \dfrac{ KP(x) }{ KQ(x) } \biggr]
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Contrastive predictive coding }\\
  A. van den Oord
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Contrastive predictive coding (InfoNCE, noise contrasting encoding)
learns to distinguish pairs with the same label,
e.g., (dog$_1$,dog$_2$) from pairs with different labels, e.g., (dog$_1$,cat$_2$).
Labels are not strictly necessary to learn a useful representation:
just assume that nearby patches (of the sound dignal, etc.) are from the same class (phoneme, speaker, etc.) while other matches from the dataset are unrelated.

