  \node (I2) at (2,0) {Image$_t$};
  \draw[thick,->,green!50!black] ([xshift=-1pt]I0.north) -- node[anchor=east]{\footnotesize\color{green!50!black}perception} ([xshift=-1pt]W0.south);
  \draw[thick,->,green!50!black] ([xshift=-1pt]I1.north) -- ([xshift=-1pt]W1.south);
  \draw[thick,->,green!50!black] ([xshift=-1pt]I2.north) -- ([xshift=-1pt]W2.south);
  
  \draw[thick,->,blue!50!black] ([xshift=+1pt]W0.south) -- node[anchor=west]{\footnotesize\color{blue!50!black}optics} ([xshift=+1pt]I0.north);
  \draw[thick,->,blue!50!black] ([xshift=+1pt]W1.south) -- ([xshift=+1pt]I1.north);
  \draw[thick,->,blue!50!black] ([xshift=+1pt]W2.south) -- ([xshift=+1pt]I2.north);

  \draw[thick,->,red!50!black] (W0) -- node[anchor=south]{\footnotesize\color{red!50!black}dynamics} (W1);
  \draw[thick,->,red!50!black] (W1) -- (W2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
It can be used to keep track of 3D objects nder occlusion.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CvxNet: learnable convex decomposition}\\
  B. Deng et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Vision is inverse graphics: a complex shape can be described using simple primitives, e.g., boxes, ellipsoids or, more generally, convex shapes, defined implicitly as intersections of half-spaces
\[ \Phi(x) = \text{LogSumExp}\bigl( \delta ( Nx + d ) \bigr) \text{ (softmax)}. \]
A (union) of convex shapes can be learnt with a (multi-headed) auto-encoder.

To convert the implicit representation into an explicit one (extreme points), compute the convex hull in the dual (cf. F-Rep, \emph{function representation})

\[
\raisebox{-2em}{%
<<CVXNet1,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=1,fig.height=1,out.width='.2\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Primal
par( mar = .1 + c(0,0,0,0) )
plot( NA, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "", xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1) )
box()
XY <- structure(c(-0.9, -0.525, 0.244444444444444, 0.54, -0.533333333333333, 0.6, -0.9, -0.9, 0.43, 0.966666666666667), .Dim = c(5L, 2L))
polygon(XY, border=NA, col='light blue')
abline(1.5,1, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline(-.9,0, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline(.7,-.5, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline( -3,-4, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline(-1.5,1, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline( -2,4.5, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
@
}
\hspace*{-2mm}
{\rightarrow}
\hspace*{-1mm}
\underset{\text{dual}}{
\raisebox{-2em}{%
<<CVXNet2,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=1,fig.height=1,out.width='.2\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Dual
par( mar = .1 + c(0,0,0,0) )
plot( NA, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "", xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1) )
box()
set.seed(2)
xy <- matrix( runif(12,-1,1), nc=2 )
points(xy, pch=16, col = 'dark blue')
@
}
}
\hspace*{-2mm}
{\rightarrow}
\hspace*{-1mm}
\underset{\text{convex hull}}{
\raisebox{-2em}{%
<<CVXNet3,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=1,fig.height=1,out.width='.2\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Convex hull
draw_line <- function(A,B, ...) {
  b <- (B[2] - A[2]) / (B[1] - A[1])
  a <- A[2] - b * A[1]
  abline(a,b, ...)
}
par( mar = .1 + c(0,0,0,0) )
plot( NA, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "", xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1) )
box()
ij <-  chull(xy)
ij <- cbind( ij, c(ij[-1],ij[1]) )
for( k in 1:nrow(ij) ) {
  draw_line( xy[ij[k,1],], xy[ij[k,2],], lwd = 3, col = 'light blue' )
}
points(xy, pch=16, col = 'dark blue')
@
}
}
\hspace*{-2mm}
{\rightarrow}
\hspace*{-1mm}
\underset{\text{primal}}{
\raisebox{-2em}{%
<<CVXNet4,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=1,fig.height=1,out.width='.2\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Back to the primal
par( mar = .1 + c(0,0,0,0) )
plot( NA, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "", xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1) )
box()
abline(1.5,1, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline(-.9,0, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline(.7,-.5, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline( -3,-4, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline(-1.5,1, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
abline( -2,4.5, col = 'dark blue', lwd = 3)
int <- function( a1, b1, a2, b2, ... ) {
  x <- - (a1-a2) / (b1-b2)
  y <- a1 + b1 * x
  points(x, y, pch = 16, cex = 1.5, col = 'light blue')
  c(x,y)
}
int( 1.5,1, -3,-4 )
int( -3,-4, -.9,0 )
int( -.9,0, -2,4.5 )
int( -2,4.5, .7,-.5 )
int( .7,-.5, 1.5,1 )
@
}
}
\]


%% NeurIPS workshop 2019: Graph representation learning

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Variational graph convolutional networks}\\
  E.V. Borilla
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Graph convolutional networks (GCN) are not robust to noise in the graph structure:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Add a prior on the graph structure;
\item Use a \emph{low rank} parametrixation of the variational posterior
\begin{align*}
  q(A) &= \prod_{ij} q( A_{ij} ) \\
  q_{ij} &= \text{Bernoulli}( A_{ij} , \rho_{ij} ) \\
  \rho & \text{: low rank}
\end{align*}
\item Relax the discrete Bernoulli distribution into a ``binary concrete'' distribution. % Not defined
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Representation and generation\\ of molecular graphs}\\
  W. Jin
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
(Permutation-invariant) graph neural nets (GNN) are not expressive enough:
they cannot predict girth, circumference, diameter, total number of cycles, etc.
In practice, we add cycle-related features, e.g., whether an atom is in an aromatic ring.

View molecules at multiple levels, using a dictionary of structures,
