\]
which define a coarser graph, and use message passing,
e.g., to forecast molecule properties
(data from the ZINC database).

For \emph{de novo molecule optimization},
modify an existing molecule into a new one, usable as a drug,
using an encoder-decoder network, and incrementally construct the hierarchical graph of the output.
Use the EM algorithm to satisfy the specifications:
the E step generates candidates and reweights them,
the M step  maximizes the weighted log probability.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2.5,yscale=.8,inner sep=-3pt, outer sep=1pt, baseline=0]
  \node (A) at (0,1) {\footnotesize\begin{tabular}{c}input\\ molecule\end{tabular}};
  \node (B1) at (1,2) {\footnotesize\begin{tabular}{c}target\\ specification\end{tabular}};
  \node (B2) at (1,1) {\footnotesize\begin{tabular}{c}latent\\ representation\end{tabular}};
  \node (B3) at (1,0) {\footnotesize\begin{tabular}{c}noise\\ (for diversity)\end{tabular}};
  \node (C) at (2,1) {\footnotesize\begin{tabular}{c}output\\ molecule\end{tabular}};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B2);
  \draw[->] (B1) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (B2) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (B3) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Proposal for an open graph benchmark}\\
  J. Leskovec
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
PyTorch code available (\texttt{dgl}, \texttt{pytorch\_geometric}): \url{http://ogb.stanford.edu/}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph representation learning\\ for optimization on graphs}\\
  B. Dilkina
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
1.
Machine learning components can be used inside optimization algorithms,
in particular when we have to repeatedly solve the same problem with new data.
For instance, one can use reinforcement learning to learn the scoring function ($Q$-value function)
of a greedy algorithm to solve combinatorial optimization problems on graphs such as
minimal cover (the degree is often used as a score, but one can do better),
maximum cut or TSP, from the features of the graph -- the graph representation (features)
is learnt by structure2vec.

2.
Two-stage processes are often suboptimal:
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=3,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt, baseline=0]
  \node[anchor=mid] (A) at (0,0) {data};
  \node[anchor=mid] (B) at (1,0) {prediction};
  \node[anchor=mid] (C) at (2,0) {decision.};
  \draw[->] (A) -- node[anchor=north] {\footnotesize predict} (B);
  \draw[->] (B) -- node[anchor=north] {\footnotesize optimize} (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

A pure end-to-end approach is possible, but combinatorial optimization
complicates things.
A simpler approach is to find a related problem, easier to solve,
and learn a mapping from the original problem to the simpler one.


\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=3.2,yscale=1.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt, baseline=0]
  \node[align=center] (A1) at (0,1) {incomplete\\ graph};
  \node[align=center] (B1) at (1,1) {graph};
  \node[align=center] (C1) at (2,1) {graph\\ partitioning};
  \draw[->] (A1) -- node[anchor=north,align=center] {\footnotesize add missing\\[-1mm]\footnotesize edges} (B1);
  \draw[->] (B1) -- node[anchor=north,align=center] {\footnotesize community\\[-1mm]\footnotesize detection} (C1);
  \node[align=center] (A2) at (0,0) {incomplete\\ graph};
  \node[align=center] (B2) at (1,0) {graph\\ representation};
  \node[align=center] (C2) at (2,0) {graph\\ partitioning};
  \draw[->] (A2) -- node[anchor=north,align=center] {\footnotesize node\\[-1mm]\footnotesize embedding} (B2);
  \draw[->] (B2) -- node[anchor=north,align=center] {\footnotesize $k$-means} (C2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

(For the backward pass, only unroll one of the $k$-means iterations.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep graph library}\\
  Z. Zhang
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
PyTorch library for (message-passing) neural nets.
Also check \texttt{pytorch\_geometric}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph networks for learning physics}\\
  P. Battaglia
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
\emph{Graph networks} are a formalism for graph neural networks based on \emph{learned message passing}.
\begin{align*}
  v_i, v_j &: \text{node features} \\
  e_{ij} &: \text{edge features} \\
  \phi &: \text{neural networks} \\
  e_{ij} ' &= \phi(e_{ij}, v_i, v_j ) && \text{edge function} \\
  e_i &= \sum_j e_{ji} && \text{aggregation} \\
  v_i' &= \phi( e_i, v_i ) && \text{node function}  
\end{align*}
They can learn physical systems from observations,
e.g., $n$-body problems,
balls bouncing in a box, springs;
the learned model generalizes to larger systems.

Once they have learned thephysical laws, onee can replace the Euler integrator
(computing the state of the system at the next time)
with a better one.

Code: \texttt{graph\_net};
also check \texttt{dgl}, \texttt{pytorch\_geometric}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning DAGs and trees with box embeddings and hyperbolic embeddings}\\
  A. McCallum
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Knowledge can be represented as raw (indexed) text, a trained deep learning model (BERT -- type 1 reasoning) or a knowledge base (KB -- type 2 reasoning).

From text, extract entities and relations;
put them in an entity pair $\times$ relation matrix, and use classical matrix completion,
or ``multi-hop reasoning'' to infer the missing relations.

Instead of using actual entity pairs and relations as rows and columns, use a latent representation:
it will accommodate entities and relations not seen during training.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node[draw,fill=blue!20,align=center,minimum height = 1cm] (EP) at (0,0) {\footnotesize entity\\[-1mm]\footnotesize pairs};
  \draw[fill=red!20] (1.5,-.5) rectangle (1.9,.5);
  \draw[->] (EP) -- node[anchor=south]{\scriptsize encoder} (1.45,0);
  \draw[fill=green!20] (2,-.5) rectangle (5,.5);
  \draw[fill=red!20] (2,.6) rectangle (5,1);
  \node at (3.5,.8) {\footnotesize latent representation};
  \node (L) at (3.5,1.5) {\footnotesize LSTM};
  \node[draw,fill=blue!20,minimum width=3cm,inner sep=3pt] (R) at (3.5,2.2) {\footnotesize relations};
  \draw[->] (R) -- (L);
  \draw[->] (L) -- (3.5,1.05);
  \foreach \i in {-4,...,4} 
    \draw (2,.1*\i) -- (5,.1*\i);
  \foreach \i in {20,...,50}
    \draw (.1*\i,-.5) -- (.1*\i,+.5);   
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Add types to those entities, with a hierarchical structure
(e.g., president $\Rightarrow$ human), learned with a \emph{bilinear model}
($c_1 ' A c_2 > 0 $ iff $1 \Rightarrow 2$, \ie, 1 \texttt{is\_a} 2, \ie, $1 \subset 2$).

To add probabilities to those hierarchical relations
(e.g., $P[\text{president} \Rightarrow \text{writer}] = .8$),
do not represent concepts as points in a vector embedding, but as Gaussian densities, cones or boxes.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
\begin{scope}  
  \draw (-.1,0) -- (2.1,0);
  \draw (0,-.1) -- (0,2.1);
  \node at (.8,1.5) {\footnotesize\color{green!50!black}herbivore};
  \node at (1.8,1.8) {\footnotesize\color{red!50!black}deer};
  \node at (.5,.5) {\footnotesize\color{blue!50!black}rabbit};
  \node at (1.5,1) {\footnotesize mammal};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(3,0)}]
  \draw (-.1,0) -- (2.1,0);
  \draw (0,-.1) -- (0,2.1);
  \draw[green!50!black,thick,fill=green!50] (.5,1) ellipse (4mm and 8mm);
  \draw[thick, fill=black!60, fill opacity = .3] (1.2,.5) ellipse (8mm and 4mm);
  \draw[blue!50!black, thick, fill=blue!50] (.6,.4) circle (.5mm);
  \draw[red!50!black, thick, fill=red!50]   (.6,.6) circle (.5mm);
  \node at (1.4,1.8) {\footnotesize\color{green!50!black}herbivore};
  \node at (1.7,1.1) {\footnotesize mammal};
  \node[anchor=north,align=center] at (1,-.1) {\footnotesize not closed under\\[-1.5mm]\footnotesize intersections};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(0,-3)}]
  \draw (-.1,0) -- (2.1,0);
  \draw (0,-.1) -- (0,2.1);
  \fill[fill=green!50] (.3,1) rectangle (2.1,2.1);
  \fill[fill=black!60, fill opacity = .3] (1,.3) rectangle (2.1,2.1);
  \fill[fill=blue!50] (1.5,1.7) rectangle (2.1,2.1);
  \node[circle,fill=green!50!black] at (.3,1) {};
  \node[circle,fill=black] at (1,.3) {};
  \node[circle,fill=blue!50!black] at (1.5,1.7) {};
  \draw[very thick,green!50!black] (.3,1) -- (.3,2.1);
  \draw[very thick,green!50!black] (.3,1) -- (2.1,1);
  \draw[very thick] (1,.3) -- (2.1,.3);
  \draw[very thick] (1,.3) -- (1,2.1);
  \draw[very thick,blue!50!black] (1.5,1.7) -- (1.5,2.1);
  \draw[very thick,blue!50!black] (1.5,1.7) -- (2.1,1.7);
  %\node at (.8,1.5) {\footnotesize\color{green!50!black}herbivore};
  %\node at (1.8,1.8) {\footnotesize\color{red!50!black}deer};
  %\node at (.5,.5) {\footnotesize\color{blue!50!black}rabbit};
  %\node at (1.5,1) {\footnotesize mammal};
  \node[anchor=north] at (1,-.1) {\footnotesize not disjoint};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(3,-3)}]
  \draw (-.1,0) -- (2.1,0);
  \draw (0,-.1) -- (0,2.1);
  \draw[green!50!black,thick,fill=green!50] (.1,.2) rectangle (.9,1.75);
  \draw[thick, fill=black!60, fill opacity = .3] (.4,.1) rectangle (2,.9); 
  \draw[blue!50!black, thick, fill=blue!50] (.55,.35) rectangle (.65,.45);
  \draw[red!50!black, thick, fill=red!50]   (.65,.6) rectangle (.75,.7);
  \node at (1.4,1.9) {\footnotesize\color{green!50!black}herbivore};
  \node at (1.7,1.1) {\footnotesize mammal};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Those boxes can be transformed with a neural net once you define addition and multiplication of boxes.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-3pt]
  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
  \draw[green!50!black,thick,fill=green!50] (-.8,-.8) rectangle (+.4,+.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
\oplus
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-3pt]
  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
  \draw[blue!50!black,thick,fill=blue!50] (+.8,+.8) rectangle (-.4,-.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
=
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-3pt]
  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
  \draw[red!80!black,thick,fill=red!50] (-.6,-.6) rectangle (.6,.6);
  \draw[dotted,green!50!black,thick] (-.8,-.8) rectangle (+.4,+.4);
  \draw[dotted,blue!50!black,thick] (+.8,+.8) rectangle (-.4,-.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-3pt]
  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
  \draw[green!50!black,thick,fill=green!50] (-.8,-.8) rectangle (+.4,+.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
\otimes
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-3pt]
  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
  \draw[blue!50!black,thick,fill=blue!50] (+.8,+.8) rectangle (-.4,-.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
=
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-3pt]
  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
  \draw[red!80!black,thick,fill=red!50] (-.4,-.4) rectangle (.4,.4);
  \draw[dotted,green!50!black,thick] (-.8,-.8) rectangle (+.4,+.4);
  \draw[dotted,blue!50!black,thick] (+.8,+.8) rectangle (-.4,-.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

To learn those hierarchies from scratch, embed concepts in \emph{Poincar\'e space}:
this gives a continuous representation of trees.

%% NeurIPS 2019 Tutorial

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Representation learning and fairness}\\
  S. Koyejo
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Fairness can be ensured by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pre-processing: representation learning, feature adjustment, metric learning;
\item In-processing: learning with fairness penalties;
\item Post-processing: adjustments.
\end{compactitem}

For the preprocessing approach, clearly separate the roles:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{data regulator} defines the fairness criteria and audits the sanitized data and the final model;
\item The \emph{data producer} turns the ra data into a fair representation;
\item The \emph{data user} uses the sanitized data to fit a model.
\end{compactitem}

There are many, often incompatible, fairness criteria.
\emph{Individual fairness} requires that ``similar'' individuals be treated ``similarly'' --
this requires a metric.
\emph{Group fairness} asks that some statistics be equalized across predefined groups, e.g.,
\emph{statistical parity} (\textsc{TP}+\textsc{FP}),
\emph{equalized odds} (\textsc{TP}, \textsc{FP}),
\emph{equality of opportunity} (\textsc{TP}).
To choose the statistics to equalize, present the end user with several confusion matrices and ask her which ones she prefers (metric elicitation).
Group fairness does not imply individual fairness;
fairness for attributes $A$ and $B$ does not imply joint fairness for $(A,B)$.

For individual fairness, the regulator can provide sets of examples
which should be treated in the same way, and the data producer trains a model using
\[ x_1 \sim x_2,\ x_1 \not\sim x_3 \implies \n{x_1 - x_2} \leq \n{x_1 - x_3}. \]
For group fairness one can replace the observations with prototypes ensuring statistical parity.

\d{Disentanglement} trains a VAE which separates the sensitive attributes from the non-sensitive ones.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=3,yscale=1.3,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt, baseline=0]
  \node[align=center] (A) at (0,1) {latent\\[-1mm] non sensitive};
  \node[align=center] (B) at (1,1) {latent\\[-1mm] sensitive};
  \node[align=center] (C) at (0,0) {data\\[-1mm] non sensitive};
  \node[align=center] (D) at (1,0) {data\\[-1mm] sensitive};
  \draw[->] (B) -- (D);
  \draw[->] ([xshift=-1pt]A.south) -- ([xshift=-1pt]C.north);
  \draw[->] ([xshift=+1pt]C.north) -- ([xshift=+1pt]A.south);
  \draw[->] ([xshift=-1pt,yshift=+1pt]C.north east) -- ([xshift=-1pt,yshift=+1pt]B.south west);
  \draw[<-] ([xshift=+1pt,yshift=-1pt]C.north east) -- ([xshift=+1pt,yshift=-1pt]B.south west);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

There is a trade-off between accuracy and fairness.

Pay attention to inappropriate data, e.g., feedback effects.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Synthetic control}\\
  A. Abadie et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
To estimate the effects of an intervention from \emph{observational} data,
in particular when the intervention is applied to large units (cities, countries) --
this is sometimes needed because of fairness considerations
or possible interactions --
match each treated unit, not with a single control,
but with a weighted average of controls.
The synthetic control is computed by constrained optimization,
matching the features of the unit and the pre-intervention values.

It may be useful to denoise the time series first,
e.g., with a low-rank approximation,
minimizing
$\n{ \hat Y - Y }^2 _{ 2,\infty}$
rather than $\n{ \hat Y - Y }_F ^2$.
This can be generalized to multiple interventions
(``what if unit $i$ had received intervention $j$ instead of $i$?''),
with a low-rank tensor decomposition.

Examples include:
German GDP after reunification,
smoking regulations in California,
UK GDP after Brexit,
cricket results if it had not rained.

