\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep learning with Bayesian principles}\\
  M.E. Khan
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
Deep learning scales well with data and model complexity;
Bayesian learning estimates uncertainty and can be incrementally updated.
Bayesian updates are global; deep learning updates are local.

Consider an exponential family,
\[ q(\theta) = \exp \bigl[ \lambda ' T(\theta) \bigr] \qquad \mu = E\bigl[ T(\theta) \bigr] \]
where $\lambda$ are the natural parameters, $T$ the sufficient statistic,
and $\mu$ the expectation parameters.
Deep learning 
\[
  \operatorname*{Minimize}\limits_\theta \ell(\theta) 
\]
\[
  \theta \leftarrow \theta - \rho H_\theta ^{-1} \nabla_{\!\theta} \ell(\theta)
\]
and Bayesian learning are eerily similar.
\[
  \operatorname*{Minimize}_q \E_{ \theta \sim q } \bigl[ \ell(\theta) \bigr] - H(q)
\]
\[
  \lambda \leftarrow \lambda - \rho \nabla_{\!\mu}
  \biggl( \E_{\theta\sim q} [\ell(\theta)] - H(q) \biggr)
\]

For instance, Bayesian learning with $q(\theta) = N(m,1)$
and a ``global-to-local'' approximation $E\bigl[\ell(\theta)\bigr] \approx \ell(\theta)$,
gives gradient descent;
$q(\theta) = N(m,S^{-1})$ gives Newton's method.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Interpretable comparison\\ of distributions and models}\\
  A. Gretton et al.
  (NeurIPS 2019)
\end{book}
1.
Integral probability metrics (IPM) compare measures $p$, $q$ by looking at their difference
\[ \text{IPM}(p,q) = \sup_{ g \in H }
\Bigl| \E_{ x \sim p } g(x) - \E_{ y \sim q } g(y) \Bigr|
\]
while $\phi$-divergences look at their ratio
\[ D(p,q) = \int q(x) \phi\left( \dfrac{ p(x) }{ q(x) } \right) dx . \]
If $H$ is the unit ball in a RKHS, the IPM is a maximum mean discrepancy (MMD)
\begin{align*}
  f(x) &= \bigl\langle f, \phi(x) \bigr\rangle = \sum f_k \phi_k(x) \\
  \E_{x \sim p } \bigl[ f(x) \bigr] &=
  \Bigl\langle
  f , \E_{ x \sim p } \bigl[ \phi(x) \bigr]
  \Bigr\rangle
  = \langle f , \mu_p \rangle  \\
  \text{MMD}(p,q) &=
  \sup_{ \n f \leq 1 } \E_{x \sim p} f(x) - \E_{ x \sim q } f(x) \\
  &= \sup_{ \n f \leq 1 } \langle  f, \mu_p - \mu_q \rangle \\
  &= \n{ \mu_p - \mu_q }
\end{align*}

2.
To test if two datasets (e.g., \textsc{cifar}-10 and \textsc{cifar}-10.1)
come from he same distribution, compute their MMD.
\begin{align*}
  \text{MMD}(p,q) &= \n{ \mu_p - \mu_q }^2 \\
  &= \langle \mu_p  , \mu_p \rangle - 2 \langle \mu_p, \mu_q \rangle
  + \langle \mu_q , \mu_q \rangle \\
  &= \E \bigl[
  \langle \phi X , \phi X' \rangle
  - 2 \langle \phi X , \phi Y \rangle
  + \langle \phi Y , \phi Y' \rangle 
  \bigr] \\
  &= \E \bigl[
  k(X,X') - 2 k(X,Y) + k(Y,Y')
  \bigr].
\end{align*}
When $p \neq q$, the distribution of $\widehat{\text{MMD}} (p,q) $
is asymptotically Gaussian,
but when $p = q$, it depends on the kernel and the distribution of the data.
Use \emph{permutation testing} to select the test threshold;
for the kernel, use a relevant representation
$\varphi : \mathscr X \to \RR^d$, e.g., a late hidden layer of a pretrained related classifier,
\[ k(x,y) = k_{\text{top}} \bigl( \varphi(x), \varphi(y) \bigr); \]
train the kernel to maximize the power
$ \text{MMD}^2(p,q) / \sigma_{H_1}(p,q) $.
