A classifier to predict if a sample comes from $p$ or $q$ is a special case.
\begin{align*}
  & k(x,y) = \dfrac14 \mathbf 1_{ f(x) > 0 } \mathbf 1 _ { f(y) > 0 } \\
  & f : \mathscr X \to \{ \pm 1 \} \text{ classifier} \\
  & \text{MMD}(p,q) = \va{ \text{accuracy} - \tfrac12 }
\end{align*}

3.
The \emph{witness function} (difference between kernel density estimates of the two distributions)
defines the \emph{unnormalized mean embedding}
\begin{align*}
\mu_p(v) &=E_{ x \sim q } \bigl[ k(x,v) \bigr] \\
\mu_q(v) &=E_{ y \sim q } \bigl[ k(y,v) \bigr] \\
\text{witness}(v) &= \mu_q(v) - \mu_p(v) \\
\text{UME}(p,q)^2 &= \dfrac1J \sum \bigl( \mu_q(v_j) - \mu_p(v_j) \bigr)^2.
\end{align*}
Choose the $v_j$'s to maximize the (normalized) mean embedding.

4.
The \emph{Stein operator}
\begin{align*}
  [ T_p f ](x) &= \dfrac{ 1 }{ p(x) } \dfrac{ d }{ dx } \bigl[ f(x) p(x) \bigr] \\
  \E\limits_p[ T_p f ] &= 0
\end{align*}
defines the \emph{kernel Stein discrepancy}
\begin{align*}
\text{KSD}_p(q) &= \sup_{ \n f \leq 1 } \biggl|
  \E\limits_q [ T_p f ] - \E\limits_p[ T_p f ]
\biggr| \\
&= \sup_{ \n f \leq 1 } 
  \E\limits_q [ T_p f ],
\end{align*}
which can be computed as the norm of the \emph{Stein witness function}
\begin{align*}
g(v) &= \E_{ x \sim q } \biggl[
\dfrac{ 1 }{ p(x) } \dfrac{ d }{ dx } \bigl[ k(x,v) p(x)\bigr]
\biggr]
\end{align*}
which gives a goodness-of-fit test between a model $p$, known up to a normalization factor,
and samples from a distribution $q$.
\begin{align*}
  \text{KSD}^2_p(q) &= \n{ g } ^ 2 = \E_{ q \sim q } \E_{ y' \sim q } h_p( y , y ' ) \\
  h_p(x,y) &= s_p(x) ' s_p(y) k(x,y) \\
  & \hphantom{={}} + s_p(x)' \nabla_{\!y} k(x,y)
                   + s_p(y)' \nabla_{\!x} k(x,y) \\
  & \hphantom{={}} + \tr \nabla_{\!xy} k(x,y) \\
  s_p(x) &= \nabla_{\! x} \log p(x) \text{ score function}
\end{align*}
To select the test threshold, use the \emph{wild bootstrap}
\[
\dfrac{ 1 }{ n^2 } \sum_{ij} w_i w_j h_p(y_i,y_j)
\]
where $w_i = \pm1$ is Bernoulli($\tfrac12$).

5. The \emph{finite set Stein discrepancy} test evaluates the Stein witness function
at several points.
\[
  \text{FSSD}^2 = \dfrac1d \dfrac1J \sum \n{ g(v_j) }^2
\]

