%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometric algebra}\\
  E. Chisolm (2012)
\end{book}
Vector algebra, \ie, vector manipulations in $\RR^3$, with scalar and cross products,
looks unsatisfactory: it seems limited to dimension 3
(what about space-time?) nd some operations look unnatural (the cross-product needs
a ``handedness'' convention and is not associative).
Geometric algebra (Clifford algebras) can replae and generalize it.

A \d{geometric algebra} is a graded $k$-vector space
$G = G_0 \oplus G_1 \oplus \cdots$
with a (non-graded, non-commutative) $k$-algebra structure satisfying properties (i) to (iv) below.
A \d{scalar} is an element of $G_0$.
A \d{vector} is an element of $G_1$.
An \d{$r$-blade} is a product of $r$ anti-commuting vectors.
An \d{$r$-vector} is a sum of $r$-blades.
An \d{$r$-versor} is a product of $r$ vectors.
A \d{rotor} is a product of two invertible vectors.

(i) $G_0 = k$.\\
(ii) The square of every vector is a scalar.\\
(iii) The symmetrized product of two vectors
$(u,v) \mapsto \tfrac12(uv+vu) = \tfrac12 ( (u+v)^2 - u^2 - v^2 )$
is a non-degenerate bilinear form.\\
(iv) $G_r = \operatorname{span}\{ \text{$r$-blades} \}$,
\ie, the $r$-vectors are the homogeneous elements of degree $r$.

Let $\langle \cdot \rangle _r : G \rightarrow G_r$ denote the projection.
The product of $A_r \in G_r$ and $B_s \in G_s$
is a sum of terms of degrees
$\va{r-s}$, $\va{r-s}+2$,\dots,$r+s$.
One can define inner ad outer products as the first and last terms:
\begin{align*}
  A_r \righthalfcup B_s &= \langle A_r B_s \rangle _{s-r} \\
  A_r \lefthalfcup B_s &= \langle A_r B_s \rangle _{r-s} \\
  A_r \wedge B_s &=   \langle A_r B_s \rangle _{r+s}
\end{align*}
Those operations have a simspler form form vectors, or vectors and versors.
\begin{align*}
  & a \righthalfcup b = b \lefthalfcup a = \tfrac12( ab + ba) \\
  & a_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge a_r = \dfrac1{r!} \sum_\sigma \text{sign}(\sigma) a_{\sigma(1)} \cdots a_{\sigma(r)} \\
  & a A_r = \langle a A_r \rangle_{r-1} + \langle a A_r \rangle _{r+1} =
    a \righthalfcup A_r + a \wedge A_r \\
  & a \righthalfcup A_r = \tfrac12 \bigl( a A_r - (-1)^r _r a \bigr) \\
  & a \wedge A_r = \tfrac12 \bigl( a A_r + (-1)^r _r a \bigr) \\
  & A_r \lefthalfcup a = (-1)^{r-1} a \righthalfcup A_r \\
  & A_r \wedge a = (-1)^r a \wedge A_r
\end{align*}
Those operations have geometric interpretations:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $a_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge a_r \neq 0$ iff $a_1$,\dots,$a_r$ are linearly independent;
\item $a \in \operatorname{Span}\{ a_1,\dots,a_r \}$ iff
  $a \wedge a_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge a_r = 0$;
\item $a \perp \operatorname{Span}\{ a_1,\dots,a_r \}$ iff
  $a \righthalfcup ( a_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge a_r ) = 0$;
\item $\operatorname{Span}\{ a_1,\dots,a_r \} = \operatorname{Span}\{ b_1,\dots,b_r \}$ iff
$\exists \lambda \ a_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge a_r = \lambda b_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge b_r$;
\item $\operatorname{Span}\{ a_1,\dots,a_r \} \subset \operatorname{Span}\{ b_1,\dots,b_r \}$ iff
$\exists c \ c \wedge a_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge a_r = b_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge b_r$.
\end{compactitem}

Blades correspond to subspaces:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $A \righthalfcup B = 0$ iff $\exists a \in A \ a \neq 0,\ a \perp B$;
\item $A \righthalfcup B$ is the orthogonal complement of $A$ in $B$;
\item $A^2 \in k$;
\item $A^{-1} = A / A^2$;
\item $A \subset B$ iff $AB = A \righthalfcup B$;
\item $A \perp B$ iff $AB = A \wedge B$.
\end{compactitem}

One can define many more operations
($I$ is the volume element).
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Grade involution} &&     a^* = -a \\
  &                        && A_r ^* = (-1)^r A_r \\
  &\text{Reversion}	  && a^\dagger = a \\
  &                        && (AB)^\dagger = BA \\
  &\text{Clifford conjugation} && A^\ddagger = A^{*\dagger} \\
  &\text{Scalar product}    && A * B = \langle A ^\dagger B \rangle \\
  &\text{Norm}  && \n{A}^2 = A * A \text{ (can be negative)} \\
  &\text{Dual} && A^\perp = A \righthalfcup I^{-1} = A I^{-1} \\
  &\text{Commutator} && A \times B = \tfrac12 ( AB - BA )
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometric algebra for computer science}\\
  L. Dorst et a. (2009)
\end{book}
\d{Geometric algebra} represents geometric objects
(points, lines, planes, circles spheres) and their transformations
(translations, rotations, symmetrices, Euclidean transformations, and even
conformal transformations)
as elements of a single set endowed with a dizzying array of products corresonding to geometric operation.
There are three flavours, based
on $\RR^3$, for linear geometry,
on $\RR^4$, for affine geometry,
on $\RR^{4,1}$, for conformal geometry.

The book refuses to give clear, concise definitions of the operations and sets it uses
and only provides verbose lists of special cases from which you are expected to get the general picture.

The \d{geometric product} is defined on $\bigwedge \RR^n$ by \\
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x^2 = G(x,x)     & \text{if $x$ is a vector} \\
\alpha \beta     & \text{if $\alpha$, $\beta$ are scalars} \\
\alpha x         & \text{if $\alpha$ is a scalar and $x$ a vector} \\
\end{array}
\]
and extended by distributivity, linearity and associativity (it is not commutative).
For vectors, one can recover the scalar and external products as the symmetric and anti-symmetric parts
of the geometric product,
\[ xy = x \cdot y + x \wedge y. \]
Contrary to the outer product, the geometric product produces elements of mixed grade.

A \d{$k$-vector} is an element of $\bigwedge ^k \RR^n$.
A \d{$k$-blade} is an outer product of $k$ vectors (a $k$-vector is alinear combination of $k$-blades).
A \d{versor} is a product of invertible vectors.
A \d{rotor} is the product of and even number of unit vectors (\ie, reflexions);
in $\RR^{n,0}$ and $\RR^{n,1}$, it is the exponential of a bivector.
Reflexions are $ x \mapsto a \hat x a^{-1} $;
rotations are $x \mapsto R x R^{-1}$,
where $R$ is a rotor, e.g., $R=b/a$.

For \emph{affine geometry},
let $e_0,e_1,\dots,e_n$ be a basis of $\RR^{n+1}$
and use the embedding
\[
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{rcl}
    \RR^n & \longrightarrow & \RR^{n+1} \\
    \sum\limits_{i \geq 1} a_i e_i & \longmapsto & e_0 + \sum\limits_{i \geq 1} a_i e_i.
  \end{array}
  \right.
\]

For \emph{conformal geometry},
let $e,e_1,\dots,e_n,\bar e$ be a basis of $\RR^{n+1,1}$,
set $o = \tfrac12(e + \bar e)$, $\infty = e - \bar e$ and use the embedding
\[
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{rcl}
    \RR^n & \longrightarrow & \RR^{n+1,1} \\
    p = \sum\limits_{i \geq 1} a_i e_i & \longmapsto & o + p + p^2 \infty.
  \end{array}
  \right.
\]
Points in $\RR^n$ are represented by null vectors in $\RR^{n+1,1}$
(\ie, $p\cdot p= 0$; $o$ and $\infty$ are also null vectors),
and their product corresponds to the Euclidean distance
\[
  \dfrac{ p }{ - \infty \cdot p } \cdot \dfrac{ q }{ - \infty \cdot q } =
  - \dfrac12 ( p - q ) ^ 2.
\]
A vector $v \in \RR^{n+1,1}$ can be interpreted in a dual way,
$\{ x \ : \ v \cdot x = 0 \}$:
null vectors are points;
vectors with no $o$ component are (dual) planes;
in general, they are (dual) spheres.

Euclidean transformations are versors preservinf $\infty$.
Lines are elements of the form
$\text{point} \wedge \text{point} \wedge \infty$
or $\text{point} \wedge \text{direction} \wedge \infty$.
Planes are of the form $\text{point} \wedge \text{point} \wedge \text{point} \wedge \infty$;
replacing $\infty$ with a finite point gives circles and spheres.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A primer on reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces}\\
  A.H. Manton and P.O. Amblard (2015)
\end{book}
A \d{finite-dimensional RKHS} is a subspace $V \subset \RR^n$
endowed with an inner product.
Its \d{kernel} is the unique matrix
$k = (k_1,\dots,k_n) \in \RR^{n \times n}$
satisfying the following three equivalent properties.\\
(i) The $k_i$ are in $V$ and $\forall v \in V \quad \langle v , k_i \rangle = e_i' v$,
\ie, $\langle v, k_i \rangle$ is the $i$th coordinate of $v$;\\
(ii) $k = u_1 u_1' + \cdots + u_r u_r'$
where $u_1, \dots, u_r$ is an orthonormal basis of $V$; \\
(iii) The $k_i$'s span $V$ and $k_{ij} = \langle k_i, k_j \rangle$.

[Note that we do not need the scalar product of $\RR^n$, only the coordinates.]

Graphically, a RKHS can be represented as the ellipsoid
$\{ v \in V \ :\ \langle v, v \rangle = 1 \}$.

The columns of the kernel can be recovered as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Let $H_i = \{ z \in \RR^n \ :\ e_i ' z = 1 \} = \{ z \in \RR^m \ :\ z_i = 1 \}$;
\item If $V \inter H_i = \nothing$, let $k_i = 0$;
\item Otherwise, let $\tilde k_i = \argmin\limits_{z \in V \inter H_i}$ and
  $k_i = \langle \tilde k_i, \tilde k_i \rangle ^{-1} \tilde k_i$.
\end{compactitem}

A \d{RKHS} is a subspace $V \subset \RR^X$,
with an inner product making it complete (a Hilbert space),
such that the evaluation functionals
\[
  \text{ev}_x : 
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{rcl}
    V & \longrightarrow & \RR \\
    f & \longmapsto & f(x),
  \end{array}
  \right.
\]
for $x \in X$, be bounded (\ie, continuous).
Since $f \mapsto f(y)$ is continuous, from the Riesz representation theorem,
there exists $k(\cdot,y) \in V$ such that
$f(y) = \langle f, k(\cdot,y) \rangle$.
The \d{kernel} $k : X \times X \to \RR$ is positive semidefinite
(often, when $V$ is large enough, it is positive definite).
Conversely, a positive semidefinite function
$k : X \times X \to \RR$ defines a RKHS
$V = \operatorname{Span} \{ k(\cdot,y), \ y \in X \} \subset \RR^X$.

Here are a few examples.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \d{Paley-Wiener space} is the space of band-limited functions $\RR \to \RR$,
\[ f(t) = \dfrac1{2\pi} \int_{-a}^a F(\omega) e^{i \omega t} d\omega, \]
$F \in L^2$, for the scalar product $\langle f, g \rangle = \int_{-\infty}^\infty fg$;
the kernel is
\[ k(s,t) = \dfrac{ \sin\bigl( a (s-t) \bigr) }{ \pi (s-t) }. \]
\item The space of absolutely continuous functions $f : [0,1] \to \RR$,
  with $f(0) = 0$, whose derivative is square integrable,
  with the inner product $\langle f, g \rangle = \int_0^1 f' g'$;
  the kernel is $k(s,t) = \min(s,t)$;
\item The \d{Bregman space} is the space of analytic and square-integrable functions
  on a domain $\Omega \subset \CC$
  with the inner product $\langle f, g \rangle = \int_\Omega f\bar g$;
  if $\Omega$ is the unit disk, the kernel is
  \[ k(z,w) = \dfrac1\pi \dfrac1{ ( 1 - z \bar w ) ^2 }. \]
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Ramanujan machine:\\ automatically generated conjectures\\ on fundamental constants}\\
  G. Raayoni et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Given a fundamental constant (e.g., $\pi$, $e$, etc.),
find 4 polynomials $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$, $\delta$ such that
\[ \dfrac{ \gamma(c) }{ \delta(c) } = \text{GCF}(\alpha,\beta), \]
where the generalized continued fraction is
\[ \text{GCF}(\alpha,\beta) = \alpha + \dfrac{ \beta_1 }{
  \alpha_1 + \dfrac{ \beta_1 }{
    \alpha_2 + \cdots
  }
}. \]
The meet-in-the-middle algorithm estimates the RHS at low precision,
puts the result in a hash table, and check which LHS match;
the precision is progressively increased.

Surprisingly, gradient descent to minimize
\[
  \n{ \dfrac{ \gamma(c) }{ \delta(c) } - \text{GCF}(\alpha, \beta) }
\]
can also lead to a solution:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The minima are not isolated points but $(d-1)$-dimensional manifolds;
\item All the minima seem to be global, and their error is zero;
\item Start with a large number of point (500);
\item Alternate gradient descent steps and ``Coulomb repulsion'' steps [?]
\item To arrive at grid points, use gradient descent for the function $\sin^2 (\pi x)$.
\end{compactitem}

One can also allow $\alpha$ and $\beta$ to be interlaced polynomials (e.g.,
different polynomials for even and odd terms)
or transform the continued fraction with a simple function (e.g.,
$x \mapsto 1/x$).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms for reinforcement learning}\\
  C. Szepesv\'ari (2009)
\end{book}

\d{2. Known MDP.}
A \d{Markov decision process} (MDP) $(X,A,P_0)$ is the datum of a state space $X$,
a set of actions $A$, and a mapping $P_0 : X \times A \rightarrow \mathscr P( X \times \RR )$
from state-action pairs to probability distributions on the next state and the reward.
If $(X_{n+1}, R_{n+1}) \sim P_0(\cdot|X_t,A_t)$,
the discounted reward is
$R = \sum_{ t \geq 0 } \gamma^t R_{t+1} $.

An \emph{episodic} MDP has a terminal (absorbing) state.

A \emph{bandit} is an 1-state MDP.

A deterministic (resp. stochastic) \emph{policy} is the choice of an action for each state,
$A_t = \pi(X_t)$, resp. $A_t \sim \pi(\cdot|X_t)$.

A \d{Markov reward process} (MRP) is an MDP without actions -- or, equivalently,
with a stationary policy.

The value function of a MRP
\begin{align*}
  V^\pi(x) &= E \bigl[ \sum \gamma^t R_{t+1} \bigm| X_0 = x \bigr] \\
  Q^\pi(x,a) &= E \bigl[ \sum \gamma^t R_{t+1} \bigm| X_0 = x , A_0 = a \bigr]
\end{align*}
satisfies the Bellman equation
\begin{align*}
  V^\pi(x) &= r(x, \pi(x) ) + \gamma \sum_{ y \in X } P(x,\pi(x),y) V^\pi(y) \\
  &= ( T^\pi V^\pi)(x).
\end{align*}
Likewise, the optimal value function $V^*$ (or $Q^*$) of an MDP satisfies
\begin{align*}
  V^*(x) &= \sup_{ a \in A } r(x, a ) + \gamma \sum_{ y \in X } P(x,a,y) V^*(y) \\
  &= ( T^*  V^*)(x).
\end{align*}
It can be obtained by \d{value iteration}, 
$V_{k+1} = T^* V_k$,
or $Q_{k+1} = Q^* V_k$.

\d{Policy iteration} uses the state-action value function $Q$ instead of the state value function $V$
and iterates the following two steps:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the value $Q^{\pi_k}$ of the policy $\pi_k$;
\item Compute the greedy policy $\pi_{k+1}$ for this value function $Q^{\pi_k}$.
\end{compactitem}

\d{3. Unknown MDP}.
The value of a state can be of independent interest:
probability of reaching a state, or expected time until we reach a state.

\d{TD(0)} moves the estimation of the value of the current state $X_t$ towards a target.
\begin{align*}
  \text{target} &= R_{t+1} + \gamma V_t ( X_{t+1} ) \\
  \delta_{t+1}  &= \text{target} + \gamma V_t ( X_{t+1} ) \\
  V_{t+1}(x) &= \begin{cases}
    V_t(X_t) + \alpha \delta_{t+1}  \text{ if } x = X_t \\
    \text{unchanged otherwise}
  \end{cases}
\end{align*}
The step size $\alpha_t$ should satisfy the Robbins-Monroe condition,
e.g., $\alpha_t \propto t^{-\eta}$, $\tfrac12 < \eta \leq 1$,
with $\eta=1$ for good asymptotic behaviour,
and $\eta$ close to $\tfrac12$ for good small sample properties --
and $\eta=0$ in practice.

TD(0) can be used for off-policy learning.

\d{Every-visit-Monte-Carlo} is similar, but the target is the discounted reward
until the end of the episode. TD(0) converges faster, but struggles with delayed rewards.

It is actually not necessary to wait until the end of the episode:
one can keep track of the eligibility f each state for the next rewards;
the eligibility is increased by 1 eachd time the state is reached and then exponentially decays.
TD($\lambda$) lets it decay faster than the discounting faster; TD(1) is every-visit MC.
\begin{align*}
  \delta_{t+1} &= R_{t+1} + \gamma V_t( X_{t+1} ) - V_t( X_t )  \\
  z_{t+1} (x) &= \mathbf 1_{ x = X_t } + \gamma \lambda z_t(x) \\
  V_{t+1}(x) &= V_t(x) + \alpha \delta_{t+1} z_{t+1} (x)\\
  z_0(x) &= 0
\end{align*}

Variants of the eligibility traces include
$z_{t+1}(x) = \max\bigl( \mathbf 1_{ x = X_t } , \gamma \lambda z_t(x) \bigr)$,
corresponding to \emph{first-visit Monte Carlo} -- it may perform better.
The optimal value of $\lambda$ is determined by trial and error.

For large state spaces, the value function can be approximated from state features, $\phi(x)$,
e.g., $V_\theta(x) = \theta ' \phi(x)$, with
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\phi_i(x) = \exp - \eta \n{ x - x^{(i)} }^2$
  for manually-chosen points $x^{(i)}$
  (if these points are model parameters, this is an \d{RBF network});
\item $\phi_i$'s rescaled so that $\sum \phi_i \equiv 1$ (\emph{averager});
\item Kernel smoothing, spline smoothing, Gaussian process (GP) regression, regression tree.
\end{compactitem}

With function approximation, the eligibility traces are for the coordinates of $\theta$
instead of the states, and they use the gradient $\nabla _\theta V_\theta$
instead of the indicator function
$\mathbf 1_{ X_t = x }$.
\begin{align*}
  \delta_{t+1} &= R_{t+1} + \gamma V_{\theta_t} ( X_{t+1} ) - V_{\theta_t} \\
  z_{t+1} &= \left. \nabla_\theta V_\theta(X_t) \right|_{ \theta = \theta_t } + \gamma \lambda z_t \\
  \theta_{t+1} &= \theta_t + \alpha_t \delta_{t+1} z_{t+1}
\end{align*}

There is no convergence guarantee for TD($\lambda$) with function approximation
if the model is not linear
or for off-policy learning.

\emph{Gradient TD learning} directly mininizes the loss function
\[ J(\theta) = \n{ V_\theta - \Pi T V_\theta }_2 ^2 \]
where $T$ is the Bellman operator and $\Pi$ the best approximation of its argument in the set of functions considered.
The gradient of $J$ is complicated: write it using $\theta$ and some other quantity $w(\theta)$;
successively update $\theta$ (assuming $w$ fixed) and $w$.
There are several ways of choosing such a decomposition (GTD2, TDC, LS, etc.).

\textbf{4.}
There are several types of reinforcement learning problems:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item No interaction: the data has already been collected;
\item \emph{Active}: we can explore freely -- the system is running, but not live yet;
\item \emph{Online}: we already want good rewards -- the system is already live -- we can explore, but not as freely as we want.
\end{compactitem}

For online learning of bandits, \emph{$\epsilon$-greedy} (with decreasing $\epsilon$)
or \emph{Boltzman exploration}
$\pi(a) \propto \exp \beta Q(a)$ are popular.

UCB$_1$ takes the action with the best upper confidence bound.
\begin{align*}
  U(a) &= r(a) + R \sqrt{ \dfrac{ 2 \log t }{ n(a) } } \\
  r(a) &= \text{average reward for action $a$} \\
  n(a) &= \text{number of times action $a$ was chosen} \\
  R &= \text{variance or range of the rewards for $a$}
\end{align*}

For Bernouli rewards, we can explicitly compute the policy maximizing the expected reward (Gittins index).
\begin{align*}
  R(a) &\sim \text{Bernoulli}( p_a ) \\
  p_a &\sim \text{Beta}( \alpha_a, \beta_a ) \\
  \alpha_a, \beta_a &\sim \text{some prior}
\end{align*}

For activelearning of bandits, compute upper and lower bounds for each action
and discard an action $a$ if
\[ U_t(a) < \max_{\substack{ a' \in \mathscr A \\ a' \neq a }} L_t(a'). \]

While it is possible to recover a deterministic MDP (find the closest state with an unexplored action),
there is no known (reasonable) algorithm to recover a stochastic MDP.

The UCRL2 algorithm, for online learning n MDPs,
constructs confidence intervals for the transition probabilities and the reward function,
defining a set $C_t$ of plausible MDPs,
and updates the policy when those estimates are precise enough,
to maximize the discounted reward.
\[ \pi^*, M^* = \argmax_{\substack{ \pi \text{ policy} \\ M \in C_t }} \rho^\pi(M) \]

\textbf{4.3 Q-learning} is TD(0) for $Q$ instead of $V$, for off-policy learning
(TD($\lambda$) is on-policy), for $\epsilon$-greedy or Boltzman exploration.
The MDP can sometimes be simplified by separating the deterministic effects of an action
from its stochastic effect (``after-effect'').

Q-learning with function approximation is not guaranteed to converge for non-linear approximation or off-policy learning.

\textbf{4.4 Actor-critic} methods generalize policy iteration
(the actor is the policy, the critic the value function):
they update the policy before it has been completely evaluated;
they may oscillate: they keep track of the best performing policy.
\d{SARSA} is an extension of TD(0) to $Q$ instead of $V$.
\begin{align*}
   \text{target} &= R_{t+1} + \gamma \max_{ a' \in \mathscr A } Q( y_{t+1}, a' ) && \text{Q-learning} \\
   \text{target} &= R_{t+1} + \gamma Q( y_{t+1}, A'_{t+1} ) && \text{SARSA}   
\end{align*}

The other value estimation algorithms for $V$ can be extended to $Q$:
TD($\lambda$), LSTD($\lambda$), etc.

The actor is updated less often, either as a greedy policy (which can be computed on the fly)
or, for parametrized stochastic policies, e.g., Boltzman
\[ \pi_\omega( a | x ) \propto \exp w' \xi(x,a), \]
where $\xi$ are features, or Gaussian,
\[ \pi_\omega(a,x) \sim N( g_\omega(x,a), \beta_\omega(x,a)^2 I), \]
with gradient ascent
\begin{align*}
  \nabla_\omega \rho &= E[ G(\omega) ] \\
  G(\omega) &= \bigl( Q^{\pi_\omega}(X,A) - h(X) \bigr) \psi_\omega(X,A) \\
  \psi_\omega&= \nabla_\omega \log \pi_\omega \quad \text{score function} \\
  h(X) &= V(X) \quad \text{or an arbitrary function,} \\
  &\hphantom{{}= V(X) \quad} \text{for variance reduction}
\end{align*}

\textsc{Reinforce} updates the parameters at the end of the episodes.

The score function can be used as features,
\[ Q_\theta(x,a) = \theta' \psi_\omega(x,a). \]

The natural actor-critic uses the natural gradient but has simpler updates.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distral:\\ robust multitask reinforcement learning}\\
  Y.W. Teh et al.
\end{book}
For multitask learning, learn the tasks separately,
with a penalty to make them closer to an average task, computed by distillation.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Large-scale optimal transport\\ and mapping estimation}\\
  V. Seguy et al.
\end{book}
The dual of the regularized Kantorovich relaxation
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_{\substack{
    \pi \\
    \operatorname{pr}_{1*} \pi = \mu \\
    \operatorname{pr}_{2*} \pi = \nu
}}
\E_{ (X,Y) \sim \pi }
c(X,Y) + \epsilon R(\pi) 
\]
with
\(
R(\pi) = \int \left( \log \dfrac{ p \pi }{ d ( \mu \times \nu ) } - 1 \right) d\pi
\)\\
or \( 
R(\pi) = \int \left( \dfrac{ p \pi }{ d ( \mu \times \nu ) } \right)^2 d\mu d\nu
\)\\
is an unconstrained optimization problem
\[
\operatorname*{Maximize}_{u,v}
\E_{\substack{ X \sim \mu \\ Y \sim \nu }}
u(X) + v(Y) - F_\epsilon\bigl( u(X) , v(Y) \bigr) 
\]
which can be solved by stochastic gradient descent,
if $u$ and $v$ are modeled by neural nets
(the Sinkhorn algorithm is quadratic and only works with discrete distributions);
the coupling $\pi$ can then be turned into a map $f$ (modeled with a neural net and estimated by SGD)
by \emph{barycentric projection}
\[
f(x) = \argmin_{ y \in \mathscr Y } \E_{ Y \sim \pi(X,\cdot) } \bigl[ d(z,Y) \bigr].
\]

Machine learning applications include \emph{domain adaptation}
(transfer nearning: aligning different datasets),
shape matching,
colour transfer,
data assimilation (aligning biased model(s) and data).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sliced Gromov-Wasserstein}\\
  T. Vayer et al.
\end{book}
The \d{sliced Wasserstein distance}
is the average of the Wasserstein distance
of projections on random 1-dimensional subspaces.

The \d{Gromov-Wasserstein distance}
between
$\mu = \sum a_i \delta_{x_i} \in \mathscr P(\RR^p)$ and
$\nu = \sum b_j \delta_{y_j} \in \mathscr P(\RR^q)$
for the dissimilarity measures
\begin{align*}
  c_X : \RR^p \times \RR^p & \longrightarrow \RR_+ \\
  c_Y : \RR^q \times \RR^q & \longrightarrow \RR_+
\end{align*}
is
\[
  \text{GW}_2 = \min_{ \pi \in \Pi(a,b) }
  \sum_{ijk\ell}
  \va{
    c_X(x_i,x_k) - c_Y(y_j,y_\ell)
  }^2
  \pi_{ij} \pi_{k\ell}.
\]
The 1-dimensional uniform case,
with squared Euclidean distances, is easy:
sort the $x_i$'s and $y_j$'s (in increasing or decreasing order, whichever is better).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pushing the right boundaries matters!\\ Wasserstein adversarial training\\ for label noise}\\
  B.B. Damodaran et al.
\end{book}
For an observation $(y,x)$,
\d{virtual adversarial training} adds a regularization term
$D\bigl( p_\theta(x) \| p_\theta(x+r) \bigr)$
where $p_\theta$ are the predicted class probabilities (when computing the gradient wrt $\theta$,
fix the first arguement and only differentiate the second) and $r$,
with $\n r \leq \epsilon$, is the direction in which the divergence increases the most
(the dominant eigenvector of the Hessian, $D\bigl( p(x) \| p(x+r) \bigr) \sim \tfrac12 r ' H r$,
obtained by power iteration -- one step is enough).

Replace the KL divergence with the Wasserstein distance (with entropic regularization),
with a cost reflecting similarities between classes (e.g., from word2vec).

The regularization is then more important for classes that should not be confused,
that are less likely to be affected by noise.

Other ways of dealing with noisy labels include
robust methods,
data cleaning (with another network),
adding a noise layer on top of the softmax,
and learning the \emph{noise transition matrix}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Wasserstein transform}\\
  F. M\'emoli et al (2019)
\end{book}
A point cloud $X \subset \RR^n$ can be seen as a uniform distribution $\alpha$ on the metric space $(X,d)$.
The local $\epsilon$-truncation of $\alpha$ is the probability measure
\[ m_\alpha^\epsilon (x) \propto \alpha|_{ B_\epsilon(x) }. \]
The \d{Wasserstein transform} is a new distance on $X$
\[ d_\alpha^\epsilon(x,y) = d_{W,1}\bigl( m_\alpha^\epsilon(x), m_\alpha^\epsilon(y) \bigr) \]
where $d_{W,1}$ is the $\ell^1$ Wasserstein distance.
The iterated Wasserstein transform generalizes the mean-shift algorithm and has a denoising effect.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to topological data analysis: fundamental and practical aspects\\ for data scientists}\\
  F. Chazal and B. Michel (2017)
\end{book}
To turn persistence diagrams into features, use persistence landscapes,
or build a persistence diagram \emph{kernel}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Information-geometric\\ optimization algorithms:\\ a unifying picture via invariance principles}\\
  Y. Ollivier et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Gradient descent on $F(\theta) = \E_{X \sim p_\theta} \bigl[ f(\theta) \bigr]$
(where the $p_\theta$ are, e.g., Gaussians)
converges to a Dirac mass at the minimum of $f$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Rewrite $f$ using quantiles, to make the algorithm invariant to monotonic transformations;
  % Incorrect notations...
\item Use the natural gradient;
\item Approximate the expectation using quantiles (as CMA-ES or CEM).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Riemann manifold Langevin\\ and Hamiltonian Monte Carlo}\\
  M. Girolami et al.
\end{book}
The Metropolis-adjusted Langevin algorithm is a Metropolis-Hastings Markov chain
with a drift term (the Langevin diffusion is unbiased when the step size tends to zero --
for finite step sizes, a MH accept-reject step isneeded -- HMC has the same problem).

One-step HMC updates are Langevin updates.

On a Riemannian manifold, one can leverge the metric:
\begin{align*}
  d\theta &= \tfrac12 \nabla_{\!\theta} \ell(\theta) \, dt + db && \text{flat} \\
  d\theta &= \tfrac12 \tilde \nabla \ell \, dt + d \tilde b && \text{Riemann} \\
  \tilde \nabla \ell &= G^{-1} \nabla \ell && \text{Natural gradient} \\
  d\tilde b &= \cdots dt + G^{-1/2} db.
\end{align*}
In the Riemannian Brownian motion, the second term accounts for the curvature,
and the first for the change in curvature.

The Hamiltonian is simpler,
\begin{align*}
  H(\theta, p) &= - \ell(\theta) + \tfrac12 \log (2\pi)^D \va G + \tfrac12 p' G^{-1} p \\
  \dfrac{ d \theta_i }{ d \tau } &=   \dfrac{ \partial H }{ \partial p_i } \\
  \dfrac{ d p_i      }{ d \tau } &= - \dfrac{ \partial H }{ \partial \theta_i } 
\end{align*}
but the naive discrete integrator is not volume-preserving:
use an implicit integrator instead.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Topological denoising:\\ strengthening the topological signal}\\
  J. Kloke and G. Carlsson (2018)
\end{book}
A few steps of the \d{mean-shift} algorithm have a denoising effect on a point cloud $X$.
If there is more noise, take a subset $S_0 \subset X$ (e.g., 10\%)
and progressively move them in the direction of the gradient of
\[ F_n(x) = f_D(x) + \omega f_{S_n} (x) \]
where $f_D$ and $f_{S_n}$ are density estimators
(Gaussian, with the same bandwidth $\sigma$) of $D$ and $S_n$
and $\omega \in [.1,.5]$.
The first term is from the mean-shift algorithm;
the second keeps the points of $S_n$ away from each other.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A topological regularizer for classifiers\\ via persistent homology}\\
  C. Chen et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The \d{topological complexity} of the decision boundary $f^{-1}(0)$
of a classifier $f : X \to \RR$
is $\sum_c \rho(c)^2$
where the \d{robustness} $\rho(c)$
of  connected component $c$ of $f^{-1}(0)$ is
\[ \min \bigl\{
\va{ f(p_c) }, \va{ f(q_c) }
\bigr\}, \]
where $p_c$ and $q_c$ are the critical points corresponding to
the birth and death of the comonent (0th dimensional persistent homology --
the \emph{persistence} is $f(q_c) - f(p_c)$).
It can be used as a regularizer (TDA usually yields features);
to compute its gradient,
use a piecewise approximation of the classifier function.

<<TopologicalRegularizer,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
op <- par( mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
s <- function(x) 1 / ( 1 + exp(-x) )
f <- function(x) 1 - ( exp(-x^6) + 1 * exp( -(x+2)^2 ) * s(-x-2) + exp( -((x-2)*2)^2 ) )
curve( f(x), xlim = c(-3,3), lwd = 3, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )

## Solutions of f(x) = .6
z <- list(
  uniroot( function(x) f(x) - .6, c(-3,-2.5) ),
  uniroot( function(x) f(x) - .6, c(-2.5,-1.5) ),
  uniroot( function(x) f(x) - .6, c(-1.5,-.5) ),
  uniroot( function(x) f(x) - .6, c(0,1) ),
  uniroot( function(x) f(x) - .6, c(1,2) ),
  uniroot( function(x) f(x) - .6, c(2,3) )
)
z <- sapply( z, `[[`, 'root' )
segments( z[c(1,3,5)], f(z[c(1,3,5)]), z[c(2,4,6)], f(z[c(2,4,6)]), col = dbColours[1], lwd = 5 )
text( mean(z[1:2]), .6, "1", adj=c(.5,-1) )
text( mean(z[3:4]), .6, "2", adj=c(.5,-1) )
text( mean(z[5:6]), .6, "3", adj=c(.5,-1) )

## Local minima
mi <- list(
  optimize(f, z[1:2]),
  optimize(f, z[3:4]),
  optimize(f, z[5:6])
)
ma <- list(
  optimize(f, z[2:3], maximum=TRUE),
  optimize(f, z[4:5], maximum=TRUE)
)
mi <- sapply( mi, `[[`, "minimum" )
ma <- sapply( ma, `[[`, "maximum" )
points( mi, f(mi), pch=16, cex = 1.5, col = dbColours[1] )
points( ma, f(ma), pch=16, cex = 1.5, col = dbColours[1] )

text( ma[1], f(ma[1]), expression(q[1] * ", " * q[2]), adj=c(.5,-1) )
text( ma[2], f(ma[2]), expression(q[3]), adj=c(.5,-1) )
text( mi[1], f(mi[1]), expression(p[1]), adj=c(.5,2) )
text( mi[2], f(mi[2]), expression(p[2]), adj=c(.5,-1) )
text( mi[3], f(mi[3]), expression(p[3]), adj=c(-1, .5) )
par(op)
@

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Connectivity-optimized representation learning via persistent homology}\\
  C.D. Hofer et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Use connectivity-optimized representation learning via persitent homology.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On characterizing the capacity of neural networks using algebraic topology}\\
  W.H. Gus and R. Salakhutdinov
\end{book}
The minimum number of neurons depends on the ``geometric complexity'' of the dataset.
Conversely, the ``topological capacity' of a neural net can be estimated with
topological data analysis (TDA).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural persistence: a complexity measure for deep neural networks using algebraic topology}\\
  B. Rieck et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The \emph{neural persistence} of a layer is the $L^p$ norm of the 0th homology persistence diagram of the corresponding bipartite graph for the filtration induced by the weights.
Regularized networks (batchnorm, dropout) have a higher (normalized) persistence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Weisfeiler-Lehman graph kernels}\\
  N. Shervashidze et al. (2011)
\end{book}
The \d{Weisfeiler-Lehman test} for graph isomorphism between $G$ and $G'$
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Augments the node labels with the (sorted) labels of the neighbours;
\item Compresses the label multisets into shorter labels (e.g., with a hash function,
  or with a bijection with $\llbracket 1, k \rrbracket$);
\item Repeats until the label sets of $G$ and $G'$ differ or a timeout is reached.
\end{compactitem}
Those new labeled graphs
$G_0,G_1,G_2,\dots$, $G'_0, G'_1,,\dots$
can be used to define graph kernels as
\[ \sum_{i=0}^h k(G_i,G'_i) \qquad \text{or} \qquad k(G_h,G'_h), \]
where $k$ is an easy-to-compute kernel, e.g., the number of matching node labels,
or the number of edge pairs whose extremities have matching labels.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A persistent Weisfeiler-Lehman procedure\\ for graph classification}\\
  B. Rieck et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Label propagation (Weisfeiler test) defines a distance between adjacent vertices
\[ d^h(u,v) = \mathbf 1_{ \ell_u^{h-1} \neq \ell_v^{h-1} } + d( \ell_u^h, \ell_v^h ) + 1 \]
where $\ell_u^h$ is the label multiset of node $u$ after $h$ steps
and the distance between multisets is the $L^p$ distance between their count vectors.
The distance $d^h$ defines a filtration on the graph,
for which we can compute the persistence diagram,
which can be added (at each step)
to the WL features (to define a graph kernel).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A tutorial on variational Bayesian inference}\\
  C. Fox and S. Roberts
\end{book}
We want to approximate a probability distribution $P(x)$,
e.g., from a graphical model,
$P(x) = \prod_I \Phi(x_I)$,
with a \d{mean-field approximation},
\ie,
$Q(x,\theta) = \prod_i Q_i( x_i, \theta)$,
by minimizing $\text{KL}( Q \| P )$.
\begin{align*}
  \text{KL}(Q \| P) &=
  \int Q \log \dfrac QP \\
  &= \int Q(x) \log \dfrac{ Q(x) }{ P( x | \text{data} ) } dx \\
  &= \int Q(x) \log \dfrac{ Q(x) P(\text{data}) }{ P( x , \text{data} ) } dx \\
  &= \int Q(x) \log Q(x) dx -
  \int Q(x) \log P(x,\text{data}) dx +
  \\ &\hphantom{={}}
  \int Q(x) dx P(\text{data}) \\
  &= - H(Q) - \int E(x,\text{data}) Q(x) dx + P(\text{data}) \\
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
  H(Q) &= - \int Q(x) \log Q(x) dx && \text{Shannon entropy} \\
  E(x,\text{data}) &= \log P(x, \text{data}) && \hspace*{-1cm}\text{Energy} \\
  L &= \langle E \rangle + H && \hspace*{-1cm}\text{Lower bound on $P(\text{data})$}  
\end{align*}
Since $Q(x) = \prod_i Q(x_i)$,
we have $H(Q) = \sum_i H(Q_i)$ and
\begin{align*}
  \langle E \rangle
  &= \int Q(x_i) \int Q(\bar x_i) E(x, \text{data}) d\bar x_i d x_i \\
  &= \int Q(x_i) \log Z Q^* (x_i) d x_i \\
  &= \int Q(x_i) \log Q_i^*(x_i) d x_i + \log Z 
\end{align*}
where
\[ Q_i^* = \dfrac1Z \exp \int Q(\bar x_i) E(x, \text{data}) d\bar x_i. \]
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
L &= \langle E \rangle + H \\
&= \int Q(x_i) \log Q_i^*(x_i) dx_i + \log Z
- \sum_j \int Q(x_j) \log Q(x_j) dx_j \\
&= \int Q(x_i) \log \dfrac{ Q_i^*(x_i) }{ Q_i(x_i) } + \log Z
- \sum_{j\neq i} H(Q_j) \\
&= - \text{KL} ( Q_i \| Q^*_i ) + \log Z - \sum_{j\neq i} H(Q_j).
\end{align*}
The lower bound $L$ is maximal when
$\text{KL}(Q_i\|Q_i^*) = 0$,
\ie, when $Q_i = Q_i^*$.
This gives an EM-like algorithm
\[
  Q_i(x_i) \leftarrow \dfrac1Z \exp \bigl\langle
  \log P( x_i, \bar x_i | \text{data} )
  \bigr\rangle_{Q(\bar x_i)}
\]
where $\bar x_i = x_{I\setminus \{i\}}$ or
$\bar x_i = \text{MarkovBlanket}(x_i)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Automatic differentiation\\ variational inference}\\
  A. Kucukelbir et al.
\end{book}
Variational inference (VI) requires model-specific derivations and implementations,
but the gradient of the VI objective can be written as an expectation
(involving $\nabla_\theta p_\theta(x)$, which can be computed by automatic differentiation (AD))
over the variational distribution $q_\phi$
and approximated with Monte Carlo methods (ADVI).
ADVI is not limited to mean-field approximations:
full-rank approximations do not under-estimate the posterior variance as much.

Examples include nonnegative matrix factorization,
e.g., Gamma-Poisson
\begin{align*}
  \theta_{uk} &\sim \text{Gamma}(a,b) \\
  \beta_{ik} &\sim \text{Gamma}(c,d) \\
  y_{ui} &\sim \text{Poisson}( \theta_u ' \beta_i )
\end{align*}
or Dirichlet-exponential-Poisson
\begin{align*}
  \theta_u &\sim \text{Dir}(\alpha) \\
  \beta_{ik} &\sim \text{Exp}(\lambda) \\
  y_{ui} &\sim \text{Poisson} ( \theta_u ' \beta_i )
\end{align*}
and models with a sparsifying \emph{automatic relevance determination} (ARD) prior,
e.g., a linear model
\begin{align*}
  \sigma &\sim \text{InvGamma}(a,b) \\
  \alpha_i &\sim \Gamma(c,d) \\
  w_i &\sim N( 0, \sigma^2 / \alpha_i ) \\
  \epsilon &\sim N(0, \sigma^2) \\
  y &= w' x + \epsilon
\end{align*}
or probabilistic PCA (PPCA),
or supervised PPCA.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep set prediction networks}\\
  Y. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
To provide sets as input to a neural network,
use an invarint function $\{x_i\}_i \mapsto \sum_i g(x_i)$,
where $g$ is a neural net.

To compare sets, use an optimal alignment
\[ \ell( \hat y , y ) = \min_{ \sigma \in \mathfrak S_n } \n{ \hat y - \sigma \cdot y }^2. \]

To output sets, decode the latent representation into the set whose encoding is
closest to the desired latent representation (this requires an optimization).
For variable-sized sets, pad the sets to a fixed size and add a mask feature.

This can also be used for an autoencoder.
\[
\begin{array}{rcccl}
  \{x_i\}_i & \longmapsto & z = \sum_i g(x_i) & \longmapsto & \argmin_{ \{y_i\} } \n{ x - \sum g(y_i) }^2 \\
  && x = \sum_i g(y_i) & \longmapsfrom & \{ y_i \}_i
\end{array}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Provably robust deep learning\\ via adversarially trained smoothed classifiers}\\
  H. Salman
\end{book}
\d{Randomized smoothing} transforms a classifier to retain the most likely class in a neighbourhood of the query
\[ g(x) = \argmax_c \P_{ \epsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2I) } \bigl[ f(x+\epsilon) = c \bigr] ;\]
it makes the classifier robust to random (non-adversarial) perturbations.

It can be generalized to \emph{soft classifiers},
which output a probability distribution on classes rather than a single class.

\d{Adversarial training} uses the gradient at adversarial perturbations
instead of the actual observations;
It can be applied to smoothed (soft) classifiers.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Kervolutional neural networks}\\
  C. Wang et al.
\end{book}
The convolution $f(x) = x * w = ( \langle x_{(i)}, w \rangle )_i$,
where $x_{(i)}$ is the circular shift of $x$ by $i$ elements,
can be kernalized
\[ g(x) = x \oast w =
\bigl(
  \langle \phi(x_{(i)}), \phi(w) \rangle
\bigr)_i =
\bigl(
  \kappa( x_{(i)}, w )
\bigr)_i
\] 
where $\kappa$ is a fixed kernel (polynomial or RBF).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepRED: deep image prior powerd by RED}\\
  G. Mataev et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The \d{deep image prior} (DIP) addresses inverse imaging problems
(denoising, deblurring, inpainting, super-resolution, tomographic reconstruction)
with a neural net $T_\theta$:
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \text{ reconstructed image} \\
  & \theta \text{ model parameters} \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ Hx - y }_2^2 \\
  \text{Such that} & x = T_\theta(z) 
\end{array}
\]
where $H$ is the (known) corruption (the identity for denoising, a convolution for deblurring, etc.),
$y$ is the corrupted image
and $z$ a fixed random vector.

While this already provides an implicit regularization,
one can add an explicit regularization and minimize
\[ \n{ Hx - y }_2^2 + \lambda x' ( x - f(x) ) \]
where $f$ is a fixed denoiser
(for a reasonable choice of $f$, the gradient of the regularizer has a simple form,
$x-f(x)$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Visualizing and measuring\\ the geometry of BERT}\\
  A. Coenen et al.
\end{book}
BERT provides contextual word embeddings whose (squared) distance matches the distance on the parse tree;
the attention matrices contain similar information.
Any tree has a power-2 embedding
(\ie, $\n{ f(x) - f(y) } ^2 = d(x,y)$) in $\RR^{n-1}$:
\begin{align*}
  f(t_0) &= 0 \\
  f(t_i) &= e_1 + f\bigl( \text{parent}(t_i) \bigr)
\end{align*}
where the $e_i$'s form an orthonormal basis (or are random unit vectors in a high-dimensional space).
The semantic information of the embeddings and the polysemy of the words
can be visualized by selecting a word,
picking 1000 Wikipedia sentences containing it,
computing the embeddings, and plotting them with UMAP.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MixMatch: a holistic approach\\ to semi-supervised learning}\\
  D. Berthelot et al.
\end{book}
To leverage unlabeled data, semi-supervised learning can use
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \d{Consistency regularization},
  to ensure the output of the classifier remains the same after data augmentation (image patches, orientation, defrmations, noise, etc.),
\item \d{Entropy minimization},
  to prevent the decision boundary from going through high-density regions, by minimizing the entropy
  $p_{\text{model}}(y|x)$ for unlabeled data,
\end{compactitem}
in addition to the traditional (supervised) regularizations:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $L^2$ regularization, often implemented as a \d{weight decay}, to prevent the model from
  memorizing the training data;
\item \d{MixUp}, which trains the model on convex combinations of inputs and labels.
\end{compactitem}
\d{MixMatch} combines those ideas:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Data augmentation on both labeled and unlabeled data;
\item Label distribution geuss for unlabeled observations by averaging the predicted distributions after data augmentation;
\item Distribution sharpening, $q_i \propto p_i ^{1/T}$, $T < 1$;
\item Train the model on convex combinations of labeled and/or unlabeled observations,
  but with a different loss function depending on whether the labeled or unlabeled observation is closer.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Phase transitions of spectral initialization \\for high-dimensional nonconvex estimation}\\
  Y.M. Lu and G. Li
\end{book}
\d{Phase retrieval} is the problem of estimating $\xi$ given noisy measurements
$y_i = ( a_i ' \xi ) ^2 + \epsilon_i$;
more generally, one may want to estimate $\xi$ from measurements
$y_i \sim f( \cdot | a_i ' \xi )$.

\emph{Spectral initialization} suggests to initialize $\xi$ to the first eigenvector
of the variance matrix of the $a_i$'s [\ie, where the information is the ``densest'':
that is not where the solution is, but that is where you will see the most clearly where to go --
provided there is enough data].


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic bouncy particle sampler}\\
  A. Pakman et al.
\end{book}
The \d{Bouncy particle sampler} (BPS) samples from
$p(w) \propto e^{-U(w)}$,
$ w \in \RR^f$,
by adding a random velocity vector $v \in \SS^{d-1}$
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $w$ follows the direction $v$ (straight lines);
\item $v$ is relected along $\nabla U^\perp$, following an inhomogeneous Poisson process of intensity
  $\lambda = ( v \cdot \nabla U )_+$:
  \[
  v \leftarrow v - 2 \dfrac{ v \cdot \nabla U }{ \n{ \nabla U } ^2 } \nabla U;
  \]
\item Occasionally resample $v$ to ensure ergodicity.
\end{compactitem}
With noisy gradients, the Poisson intensity becomes stochastic (doubly stochastic process,
aka Cox process) -- the bounces are more frequent and mixing is slower.
To sample from it, find an upper bound on $\lambda$, e.g., using a local regression $\lambda \sim t$.

(\d{Zig-zag Monte Carlo} is similar, with $v \in \{\pm 1 \}^d$, andonly changes one coordinate at a time.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The bouncy particle sampler:\\ a nonreversible rejection-free\\ Markov chain Monte Carlo method}\\
  A. Bouchard-C\^ot\'e et al. (2018)
\end{book}
% More details; implementation advice

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive hard thresholding\\ for near optimal consistent robust regression}\\
  A.S. Suggala et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Robust linear regression by discarding observations with ``large'' residual:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Iteratively estimate the regression coefficients on the set of uncorrupted observations;
\item Use an adaptive threshold to detect outliers;
\item Add some noise when decising whether to include an observations.
\end{compactitem}
The (slower) Huber loss also works well.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The cult of statistical significance}\\
  S.T. Ziliak and D.N. McCloskey (2009)
\end{book}
The $p$-value is easy to compute,
but it is not the \emph{loss function} you want --
prefer something involving the effect size.
For instance,
$X_1 \sim N(\mu=5, \sigma=0.5)$
has a better $p$-value than $X_2 \sim N(\mu=20, \sigma=10)$,
$10^{-23}$ versus $10^{-1}$ (for $H_0: X > 0$),
but $P(X_1 > X_2) = 0.07$...

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Uncertainty propagation\\ with functionally correlated quantities}\\
  M. Giordano (2016)
\end{book}
The \texttt{Mesurements.jl}
\emph{uncertainty propagation} package
handles \emph{functional correlations}, \ie, deterministic relations $y=f(x)$;
for instance, given $x = x_0 \pm \sigma$,
it estimates $x-x = 0 \pm 0$, not
$x-x = 0 \pm 2\sigma$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On cross-validation\\ for sparse reduced-rank regression}\\
  Y. She and H. Tran (2018)
\end{book}
Consider a jointly sparse and low-rank linear model
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-4pt]
  \node[draw,minimum height=1.5cm, minimum width=.5cm,align=center] (A) {$Y$};
  \node[left = 0mm of A, anchor=east] {\footnotesize $n$};
  \node[above = 0mm of A, anchor=south] {\footnotesize $m$};
\end{tikzpicture}
=
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-4pt]
  \node[draw,minimum height=1.5cm, minimum width=1cm,align=center] (A) {$X$};
  \node[left = 0mm of A, anchor=east] {\footnotesize $n$};
  \node[above = 0mm of A, anchor=south] {\footnotesize $p$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\times
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt, baseline=-4pt]
  \node[draw,minimum height=1cm, minimum width=.5cm,align=center] (A) {$B$};
  \node[left = 0mm of A, anchor=east] {\footnotesize $p$};
  \node[above = 0mm of A, anchor=south] {\footnotesize $m$};
\end{tikzpicture}
+ \epsilon
\]
where $B$ is both low-rank and row-sparse (some of the rows are zero).

To choose the regularization parameters,
e.g., $\lambda$ for the lasso,
one can use cross-validation
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item On $\lambda$, the scale of the penalty
  $\lambda \n \beta _1$,
  but a different number of variable may be selected in different folds;
\item Or on $c$, the corresponding constraint
  $\n \beta _1 \leq c$, but this is difficult to solve.
\end{compactitem}
Instead, use cross-validation on both
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Which variables are retained by the model;
\item The (low-rank) subspace spanned by the coefficient matrix
\end{compactitem}
(each can be encoded as a matrix, $S$ and $U$, which can be ecovered from their product $SU$).

[I am not sure how to use that in practice: with $\lambda$, we can just try a few values,
on a grid, but here, we have a combinatorial choice ($p!$ subsets of predictors)
and a subspace.]

The optimal rank and sparsity can be estimated using
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Some information criterion (AIC, BIC, etc. -- but it is not clear which one to use);
\item Or cross-validation.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Natural analysts in adaptive data analysis}\\
  T. Zrnic and M. Hardt
\end{book}
Data analysis is no longer limited to 1- or 2-step procedures
(statistical tests, post-selection inference):
by progressively interrogating the data, the analyst's knowledge evolves --
it is a discrete dynamical system.
\emph{Differential privacy} can help limit overfitting.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Minimax optimal rates\\ for Mondrian trees and forests}\\
  J. Mourtada et al. (2018)
\end{book}
A \emph{Mondrian process} is a distribution on infinite nested trees (partitions of $[0,1]^d$)
defined from iid sequences of random variables
$e \sim \text{Exp}(1)$ used to select the dimension to split,
by comparing it with the side lengths of the box to split
\[
\tau_i = \dfrac{ e_i }{ \text{length}_i }
\qquad
i = \argmin_i \tau_i
\qquad
\tau = \min_i \tau_i,
\]
and $u \sim U(0,1)$ usd to choose the threshold to split.
A \emph{Mondrian forest}
is a \emph{purely random forest}
whose trees are sampled from a Mondrian process.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Implicit generation and modeling\\ with energy-based models}\\
  Y. Du and I. Mordatch
\end{book}
Energy-based models,
$p(x) \propto e^{-E(x)}$,
$E$ unknown, can be estimated by MCMC
\begin{align*}
  \ell(\theta)
  &= \E_{ x \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ - \log p_\theta(x) \bigr] \\
  &= \E_{ x \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ E_\theta(x) - \log Z(\theta) \bigr] \\
  \nabla \ell(\theta) &=
  \E_{ x^+ \sim \text{data} } \bigl[ \nabla _\theta E_\theta(x^+) \bigr] -
  \E_{ x^- \sim p_\theta } \bigl[ \nabla _\theta E_\theta(x^-) \bigr]
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Meta-learning how to forecast time series}\\
  T.S. Talagala et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Train a random forest to forecast the best time series forecasting algorithm
(SARIMA, exponential smoothing, random wallk)
using time series features (length, trend, seasonality, linearity, curvature, spikiness, autocorrelation,
stability, lumpiness, spectral entropy, Hurst exponent, nonlinearity test, ETS model parameters,
unit root tests).
Implementation: \texttt{seer}.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On fast convergence of proximal algorithms\\ for SQRT-lasso optimization:\\
    don't worry about its nonsmooth loss function}\\
  X. Li et al.
\end{book}
The optimal regularization parameter for the lasso regression depends on the noise variance
\[
\argmin_\theta \dfrac1n \n{ y - X \theta }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ \theta }_1
\qquad
\lambda \approx \sigma \sqrt{ \dfrac{ \log d }{ n } }
\]
while that for the \emph{\textsc{sqrt}-lasso} (whose loss function is the \emph{unsquared} $\ell^2$ norm)
does not
\[
\argmin_\theta \dfrac1{\sqrt n} \n{ y - X \theta }_2 + \lambda \n{ \theta }_1
\qquad
\lambda \approx \sqrt{ \dfrac{ \log d }{ n } }.
\]

The \d{proximal Newton} algorithm solves this problem by using
a second order expansion of the loss function (leaving the penalty intact)
\begin{align*}
  \ell(\theta) &=
  \dfrac1{\sqrt n} \n{ y - X \theta }_2 + \lambda \n{ \theta }_1 \\
  Q(\theta, \theta_t) &= \ell(\theta_t) + \nabla \ell(\theta_t) ' (\theta - \theta_t)
  + \tfrac12 (\theta - \theta_t)' \nabla^2 \ell(\theta_t) (\theta - \theta_t) \\
  \theta_{t + 0.5} &= \argmin_\theta Q( \theta, \theta_t ) \text{ (coordinate descent)} \\
  \theta_{t+1} &= \theta_t + \eta_t ( \theta_{t+0.5} - \theta_t ) \text{ (line search)}
\end{align*}
where the line search halves $\eta_t$ until
$\ell(\theta_{t+1}) \leq \ell(\theta_t)$.

The \d{proximal gradient} algorithm uses a coarser quadratic approximation 
\[
\ell(\theta) = \ell(\theta_t) + \nabla \ell(\theta_t) ' (\theta - \theta_t )
+ \tfrac12 L \n{ \theta - \theta_t }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ \theta }_1
\]
where $L$ is obtained by line search.

\d{Pathwise optimization}, \ie,
a decreasing sequence of regularization parameters ending at the desired one,
helps yield sparse solutions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Two-player games for efficient\\ non-convex constrained optimization}\\
  A. Cotter et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To solve a constrained, non-convex optimization problem,
with non-differentiable (step function) constraints
(e.g., in fair machine learning)
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \theta \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(\theta) \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ g_i(\theta) \leq 0,
\end{array}
\]
consider the Lagrangian
\[
\mathscr L ( \theta, \lambda ) = f(\theta) + \sum_i \lambda_i g_i(\theta)
\quad (\lambda_i \geq 0)
\]
and a diferentiable proxy
\[
\tilde{\mathscr L} ( \theta, \lambda ) = f(\theta) + \sum_i \lambda_i \tilde g_i(\theta)
\]
and alternate
\begin{align*}
  \theta &\leftarrow \argmin_\theta \tilde{\mathscr L} ( \theta, \lambda ) \\
  \lambda &\leftarrow \argmin_{ \lambda \geq 0, \ \n\lambda \leq R } \mathscr L( \theta, \lambda )
\end{align*}
(we can compute the partial gradient
$\partial \mathscr L / \partial\lambda$ of the originalLagrangian,
even if the $g_i$'s are not differentiable:
only $\partial \mathscr L/ \partial \theta$ is problematic).

Keep the whole optimization history:
\[
\E_{ \theta \sim \text{Unif}(\{ \theta_1,\dots,\theta_T\}) } \bigl[ g(\theta) \bigr]
\]
is close to the minimum of $g$
(there is no \emph{pure} Nash equilibrium: all we canhope for is an approximation of a mixed Nash equilibrium).

(It is possible to reduce the number of points to $m+1$, where $m$ is the number of constraints, with a siimple linear program.)

Implementation in Tensorflow

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Accelerated extra-gradient descent:\\ a novel accelerated first-order method}\\
  J. Diakonikolas and L. Orecchia 
\end{book}
Nesterov accelerated gradient descent can be interpreted as an explicit Euler discretization
followed by a correction of the discretization error;
insted, one can use an \emph{implicit} Euler discretization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learned primal-dual reconstruction}\\
  J. Adler and O. \"Oktem
\end{book}
In the primal-dual optimization used to solve ill-posed problems (such as tomography)
with a regularizing prior (total variation, TV),
replace the proximal operator
with a learned reconstruction operator,
thereby combining deep-learning and model-based reconstruction.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bridging the gap between constant step size stochastic gradient descent and Markov chains}\\
  A. Dieuleveut et al.
\end{book}
Averaged (Ces\`aro) constant-step-size stochastic gradient descent (SGD, Robins-Monro)
benefits from Richardson-Romberg extrapolation.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Provable certificates for adversarial examples: fitting a ball in the union of polytopes}\\
  M. Jordan et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{pointwise robustness} of a classifier is the radius of the largest $\ell^p$ ball centered
at an input point $x_0$ for which the model output reains unchanged.

The \emph{Chebychev center} of a polytope is the cener of the largest $\ell^p$ ball fitting inside it.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spectral normalization\\ for generative adversarial networks}\\
  T. Miyato et al. (2018)
\end{book}
  Ensure the gradient of the discriminator remains bounded
  by normalizing each layer:
  divide each weight matrix $W$ by its largest singular value (spectral norm, $\sigma(W)$);
  the Lipschitz constant of the nonlinearities is often 1.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Face aging\\ with conditional generative adversarial networks}\\
  G. Antipov et al.
\end{book}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item 
Train a conditional GAN,
\[ ( \text{age category}, \text{noise} ) \longmapsto \text{image} \]
(they use 6 age categories);
\item Train an auto-encoder to learn the inverse mapping
\[ (\text{age}, \text{latent}) \longmapsto \text{image} \longmapsto (\text{age},\text{latent}) \]
minimizing the Euclidean distance between the latent representations;
\item Improve the latent representation by minimizing the distance between the
embedding of the original and reconstructed image for a face recognition net (FaceNet);
\item Change the age and re-generate the image.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Boosted generative models}\\
  A. Grover and S. Ermon
\end{book}
Boosting (multiplicative rather than additive) applied to the generator and/or the discriminator of a GAN.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GANSynth: adversarial neural audio synthesis}\\
  J. Engel et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Divide time into frames and generate, for each of them, a spectrogram,
with log-amplitudes and (unwrapped) phases
(the phase is needed to ensure a continuous signal; the unwrapped phase is smooth);
train on the NSynth dataset (musical notes).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Harmonizing maximum likelihood with GANs for multimodal conditional generation}\\
  S. Lee et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Conditional GANs are often trained with a loss combining minimax
\[
  \min_G \max_D
  \E_{ x,y \sim \text{data} } \log D(x,y) +
  \E_{\substack{ x \sim \text{label} \\ y \sim \text{noise} }}
  \log \bigl(
    1 - D( x, G(x,z) )
  \bigr)
\]
and reconstruction
\[
  \E_{\substack{ x,y \sim \text{data} \\ z \sim \text{noise} }}
  \n{
    y - G(x,z)
  }_p^p
\]
which worsens mode collapse.

Replace the reconstruction loss with a \d{moment reconstruction loss}:
have the generator generate a \emph{set} of samples,
compute its moments $\mu$, $\sigma$,
and use a Gaussian (or Laplace) MLE loss
\[
  \E \left[
    \dfrac{ (y - \mu)^2 }{ \sigma^2 } + \dfrac12 \log \sigma^2
  \right].
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adversarial examples are not bugs,\\ they are features}\\
  A. Ilyas et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{usefulness} and \emph{robustness} of a features $f$ are
\begin{align*}
  \rho(f) &= \E_{x,y} \cor\bigl( y, f(x) \bigr) \\
  \gamma(f) &= \E_{x,y} \cor\bigl( y, f(x+\delta_x) \bigr)
\end{align*}
where $\delta)x$ is an adversarially chosen perturbation,
\[ \delta_x = \argmin_{ \delta \in \Delta } y \cdot f(x+\delta) \]
and $y \in \{0,1\}$.

A robust model can be trained by minimizing the dversarial loss
\[ \E_{x,y} \max_{\delta \in \Delta} \text{Loss}( x + \delta, y) \]
instead of $\E_{x,y} \text{Loss}(x,y)$.

Build a dataset of similar images using only useful robust features
\[ x \mapsto x_r \in \argmin \n{ g(x_r) - g(x) } \]
where $g$ is the penultimate layer f a robust model and the optimization starts with noise.

Build a dataset of similar images using only useful non-robust features
\[ x \mapsto x_a = \argmin_{ x' \ :\ \n{x' - x} \le \epsilon } \text{Loss}(x',y') \]
where the loss is non-robust and the new class $y'$ is chosen at random.

Non-robust models trained on those datasets have good performance:
the existence of adversarial examples is a property of the dataset,
not of the model -- adversarial examples often transfer to other,
independently-trained models.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Riemannian geometry\\ of deep generative models}\\
  H. Shao et al.
\end{book}
Stochastic gradient descent can compute (discretized) geodesics (and parallel transport)
of the data manifold of variational autoencoders (VAE)
(the gradient of the decoder is expensive to compute, but that of the encoder suffices):
it appears flat.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimizing the latent space\\ of generative networks}\\
  P. Bojanowski et al.
\end{book}
Learn a GAN without a discriminator (or an autoencoder with an unconstrained encoder):
\[
\argmin_\theta \operatorname*{Mean}_{ x \in \text{Data} } \min_{ z \in \text{Ball} }
\text{Loss} \bigl( g_\theta(z) , x \bigr).
\]
To avoid blurry images,
use the \emph{Laplacian pyramid} Lap$_1$ loss.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Good semi-supervised learning\\ that requires a bad GAN}\\
  Z. Dai et al. (2017)
\end{book}
GANs can be used for semi-supervised learning:
the discriminator tries to forecast the class the sample comes from for labeled or unlabeled data,
and a $(k+1)$st class for generated samples -- the objective function has three terms,
labeled, unlabeled and generated.
\begin{align*}
  & \E_{ (x,y) \sim \text{labeled} }
  \log P_D[ y | x, y \leq k ] + {} \\
  & \E_{ x \sim \text{unlabeled} }
  \log P_D[ y \leq k | x ] + {} \\
  & \E_{ x \sim P_G }
  \log P_D[ y = k+1 | x ]  
\end{align*}
With a perfect generator, the GAN performs no better than supervised learning.
Instead, use a discriminator of the form
\( P_D ( k | X ) \propto \exp \bigl( w_k  f(x) \bigr) \)
for some features $f$,
with support in
$\{ z  :\ \forall k \ p_k(z) \leq \epsilon_k \}$
where $p_k$ is the distribution, on feature space, of class $k$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Approximation and convergence properties\\ of generative adversarial learning}\\
  S. Liu et al. (2017)
\end{book}
GAN-style algorithms are of the form
\[
\inf _\nu \sup_{ f \in \mathscr F }
\E_{\substack{ x \sim \mu \\ y \sim \nu }}
\bigl[ f(x,y) \bigr] ,
\]
\ie, $\inf_\nu \tau( \mu \| \nu )$ for an \emph{adversarial divergence} on $X$
\[ \tau( \mu \| \nu ) = \sup_{ f \in \mathscr F } \E_{ \mu \otimes \nu } [ f ], \]
for some $\mathscr F \subset \mathscr C_b(X^2)$.

For limited-capacity discriminators,
the set of distributions $\mu^*$ minimizing
$\tau( \mu \| \mu^* )$ is the set of distributions with generalized moments
(depending on $\tau$) matching those of $\mu$.

