\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The measure of intelligence}\\
  F. Chollet
\end{book}

Intelligence is not ``skill'',
but ``skill-acquisition efficiency'':
the ability to turn prior knowledge and training data into skill.

For humans, priors (\emph{Core Knowledge}) include
objectness (elementary physics: the world is made of objects),
agentness (goal-directedness),
numbers and geometry.

Intelligence could be defined, for a fixed level of skill, as
\[
  \text{Intelligence} =
  \dfrac{
    \text{Skill} \times \text{Generalization Difficulty}
  }{
    \text{Prior} \times \text{Experience}
  },
\]
averaged over training datasets (curriculum) and tasks.
The factors can be defined from information theory (Kolmogorov complexity).
\begin{align*}
\text{Difficulty} &=
\dfrac{ H( \text{Solution} \,|\, \text{Optimal solution} ) }{  H( \text{Solution}  ) } \\
\text{Prior} &= 1 - \dfrac{ H( \text{Solution} ) }{ H( \text{Solution} \,|\, \text{Initial State} ) } \\
\text{Experience} &= H( \text{Solution} \,|\, \text{IS} ) - H( \text{Solution} \,|\, \text{Data} ).
\end{align*}

The abstraction and reasoning corpus (ARC) contains 1000 tasks, each with 3 or 4 examples, one test, and allows for 3 trials. The task is to complete a grid, using geometric principles not explicitly stated, from a few examples. Humans can solve the problems without any training -- the geometric prior suffices.

Alternatives include video games (train on a level and test on others, train on a gome and test on other similar games) or data science competitions (train on a few Kaggle dataset, test on others).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Universal intelligence:\\ a definition of machine intelligence}\\
  S. Legg and M. Hutter (2007)
\end{book}
Definite the intelligence of a reinforcement learning algorithm $\pi$ 
as its expected value,
\[ \Upsilon(\pi) = \sum_{ \mu \in E } 2^{ - K(\mu) } V_\mu^\pi, \]
where $E$ is the set of environments to test
and $K(\mu)$ the Kolmogorov complexity
(approximated by the minimum description length and the running time)
of environment $\mu$ 
(simple environments are more probable)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep lattice networks\\ and partial monotonic functions}\\
  S. You et al (2017)
\end{book}
Lattices (interpolated lookup tables, which can easily enforce monotonicity constraints)
and calibrators (1-dimensional lattices)
can be used as layers and activation functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scaling limits of wide neural networks\\ with weight sharing: Gaussian process behaviour, gradient independence\\ and neural tangent kernel derivation}\\
  G. Yang
\end{book}
Infinitely large, 1-layer neural nets with random weights are Gaussian processes.
% New, more general derivation of a well-known fact

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identity crisis:\\ memorization and generalization\\ under extreme overparametrization}\\
  C. Zhang
\end{book}
When asked to reconstruct a \emph{single} example,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Some networks (FC) learn the constant function (memorization);
\item Some (shallow CNN) learn the identity function (generalization).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spectrally-normalized margin bounds\\ for neural networks}\\
  P.L. Bartlett et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{spectral complexity} of a network is
\[
  R = 
  \left(
  \prod \rho_i \n{ A_i }_\sigma
  \right)
  \left(
  \sum \dfrac{
    \n{ A_i ' - M_i ' }_{2,1}^{2/3} 
  }{
  \n{ A_i }_\sigma^{2/3}
  }
  \right) ^ { 3/2 }
\]
where
$\rho_i$ is the Lipschitz constant of the nonlinearity of layer $i$,
$\n{ A_i }_\sigma$ is the spectral norm of the weight matrix $A_i$,
$M_i$ is a reference matrix (e.g., $M_i = 0$, or $M_i=I$ for ResNets),
$\n{A}_{p,q}$ is the $(p,q)$ matrix norm
\[
  \n{ \bigl( \n{A_{\bigcdot 1}}_p, \dots, \n{A_{\bigcdot m}}_p \bigr) }_q.
\]
It is lower for networks that generalize better.
For multiclass classification,
we expect the normalized marin
\[
  \dfrac{
    f(x) _y - \max\limits_{i \neq y} f(x)_i 
  }{
    R \cdot \n{x} _2 / n
  }
\]
to increase as generalization improves.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Not all samples are created equal:\\ deep learning with importance sampling}\\
  A. Katharopoulos and F. Fleuret
\end{book}
When training a neural net, \emph{importance sampling} helps focus
the computations on informative examples,
but the importance, the per-sample gradient, is expensive to compute --
try $\bigl\| \nabla_{\! x_i^L} \text{Loss} \bigr\|$ instead,
where $i$ is the sample and $x^L$ the activation of the last layer.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep, skinny neural networks\\ are not universal approximators}\\
  J. Johnson (2019)
\end{book}
Skinny networks (the dimension of the hidden layers never exceeds that of the input)
cannot approximate functions with a level set containing a bounded connected component.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PaperRobot:\\ incremental draft generation of scientific ideas}\\
  Q. Wang et al.
\end{book}
Extract entities (gene, disease, chemical) and relations (marker, therapeutic, increased expression, etc.)
from research papers to build a knowledge graph (KG);
use both graph structure and contextual (text) embeddings of the nodes to forecast missing links;
use them to automatically write papers:
entities+relations $\rightarrow$ title $\rightarrow$ abstract $\rightarrow$ conclusion and future work $\rightarrow$ next article $\rightarrow$ etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Attention is not explanation}\\
  S. Jain and B.C. Wallace
\end{book}
Attention weights need not be correlated with importance measures: % the plots say otherwise
it is possible to change them to meaningless ones without impacting performance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Are sixteen heads really better than one?}\\
  P. Michel et al.
\end{book}
Multiple attention heads (in transformer networks, e.g., BERT)
are useful during training but most layers can be pruned to a single head
without a significant drop in performance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fair is better than sensational:\\ man is to doctor as woman is to doctor}\\
  M. Nissim et al. (2019)
\end{book}
The code used to compute analogies of the form
``$\text{doctor} + \text{woman} - \text{man} = \text{nurse}$''
excludes one of the inputs (``doctor'') and even similar items (``gynecologist'', etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A kernel for time series\\ based on global alignments}\\
  M. Cuturi at al. (2006)
\end{book}

Dynamic time-warp (DTW), Smith-Waterman local alignments and edit distance
do not readily lead to a positive definite kernel.

Gaussian and polynomial kernels are not satisfactory either:
they cannot deal with time series of different lengths
and ignore the time series structure.

Taking the best alignment
(with no gaps but possible repetitions: gaps make more sense for biological sequences,
repetitions for time series, e.g., speech)
does not give a positive definite kernel,
but combining them all often does:
\begin{align*}
  S(\pi) &= \text{score of alignment $\pi$} \\
  \kappa(x,y) &= \sum_\pi e^{ S(\pi) } \\
  &= \sum _\pi \exp \sum_i \phi( x_{\pi_1(i)}, y_{\pi_2(i)} ) \\
  &= \sum _\pi \prod_i \exp \phi( x_{\pi_1(i)}, y_{\pi_2(i)} ).
\end{align*}
The kernel can be computed in $\va x \cdot \va y$ iterations by dynamic programming
(as for DTW).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Natural language understanding}\\
  C. Potts et al. (2019)
\end{book}

Vector space models require several choices:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Design matrix: word$\times$document, word$\times$word, word$\times$context, etc.;
\item Reweighting, to ``de-emphasize the mundane and the quirky'':
probabilities, length normalization, TF-IDF, PMI, PPMI, T-test (prefer PMI);
\[
\text{PMI} =
\log \dfrac{ \text{observed} }{ \text{expected} } =
\log \dfrac{ X_{ij} }{ X_{i{\bigcdot}} X_{{\bigcdot}j} / X_{{\bigcdot}{\bigcdot}} }
\]
\item Dimension reduction: PCA, LSA, PLSA, LDA, NMF, etc.
\item Vector comparison: Euclidean, cosine, Dice, Jaccard, KL, etc. (prefer cosine).
\end{compactitem}

Large, flat windows capture semantic information,
small, scaled windows syntactic (collocational) information.

GloVe learns word vectors such that
\[ \langle u \mid v \rangle \propto P(u,v); \]
more precisely, they minimize
\[ \sum_{ij} f(X_{ij}) ( w_i ' \tilde w_k + b_i + \tilde b_k - \log X_{ik} ) \]
where $f(x) = \min\{ (x/x_{\text{max}} )^\alpha, 1 \}$,
$x_{\text{max}} = 100$, $\alpha = .75$.

Word2Vec learns word embedings such that
\[ P(b|a) \propto \exp( w_a w_b ). \]
Maximizing the likelihood is problematic because of the denominator,
\[ \argmax_{ x,w} \sum_{a \sim b} \log \dfrac{ \exp( x_a w_b ) }{ \sum_c \exp( x_a w_c ) }. \]
Instead, use \emph{noise contrastive estimation}
\[
\sum_{a \sim b} - \log \sigma( x_a w_b ) +
\sum_{a,b} \log \sigma( x_a w_b ).
\]

\emph{Retrofitting} modifies the word vectors to incorporate information (e.g., sentiment, WordNet) from a \emph{knowledge graph}.
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}\limits_q
\sum_{ i \in V } \n{ q_i - \hat q_i }^2 +
\sum_{ (i,j) \in E } \beta_{ij} \n{ q_i - q_j }^2
\]

To compare models, use the Wilcoxon signed rank test, if you run several experiments,
or the \emph{McNemar}, to compare confusion matrices, if you cannot.

Features for sentiment analysis could include: 
bag of subparts (subtrees with at most $k$ leaves),
negation (add \textsc{\_neg} to all the tokens until the end of the subtree
-- idem for other modifiers: might, etc.),
lexicon-derived features, 
modal adverbs (totally, quite possibly, etc.),
non-literal language,
character $n$-grams, tf-idf, part-of-speech, sentence length, GloVe, etc.
Use BERT (pretrained and fine-tuned) or a BiLSTM with subtree labels, or manual features

\emph{Distant supervision} builds training data for the relation extraction problem
from a knowledge base (database of known relations),
assuming that, for each known relation, any sentence mentioning both entities expresses that relation.

For natural language inference (entailment), try the following features:
words tat appear in both clauses,
words that appear in either clause,
wordnet features (pairs of words, in the first and second clause, with
$w_1 \succcurlyeq w_2$ or $w_1 \preccurlyeq w_2$,
edit distance, negation, models, quantifiers (every, some), named entities.

Given the hidden states for the words in the first, resp. second, clause,
$h_1$, $h_2$, $h_3$, resp. $h_A$, $h_B$, $h_C$,
the \d{attention mechanism} is
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Scores}    & \tilde \alpha &= [ h_C' h_1 ; h_C' h_2 ; h_C' h_3 ] \\
  &\text{Attention} & \alpha &= \operatorname{softmax} \tilde \alpha \\
  &\text{Context}   & \kappa &= \operatorname{mean}( \alpha_1 h_1, \alpha_2 h_2, \alpha_3 h_3 ) \\
  &\text{Combination} & \tilde h &= \tanh\bigl( [ \kappa ; h_C ] W \bigr) \\
  &\text{Classifier} & y&= \operatorname{softmax}( \tilde h W + b ).
\end{align*}
Other score functions are possible:
$h_C' h_i$, $h_C' W h_i$ or $W [ h_C ; h_i ]$.

Language understanding requires \emph{grounding}:
many utterances depend on the context (``there aren't any''),
so do answers (``Where are you from?'')
and even modals (is ``The door must be open'' a rule or a deduction?).

In the \d{rational speech act} (RSA) model,
the listener has a model of how the speaker thinks, and conversely.
\begin{align*}
\ell_0( \text{world} | \text{text} ) &\propto \text{language}( \text{text}, \text{world} ) \cdot P( \text{world} ) \\
s_1 ( \text{text} | \text{world} ) & \propto \left( \dfrac{ \ell_0 }{ C(\text{word}) } \right) ^ \lambda \\
\ell_1 ( \text{world} | \text{text} ) & \propto s_1 ( \text{text} | \text{world} ) P( \text{world} ) 
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\text{literal listener} &= \text{lexicon} \times \text{prior} \\
\text{pragamatic speaker} &= \text{literal speaker} - \text{message costs} \\
\text{pragamatic listener} &= \text{pragmatic speaker} \times \text{prior} 
\end{align*}

\emph{Semantic parsing} transforms text into a formal language,
suitable for constraint satisfaction solvers.
% Relies on grammar induction, EM (the correct parse tree is unknown) -- no details

Thr $F_\beta$ evaluation metric is a weighted harmonic average of recall (weight $\beta^2$)
and precision (weight 1); use $\beta=1$ or $0.5$;
for multiclass classification, the \emph{macro $F_\beta$} is the (unweighted)
average of the $F_\beta$'s for each class.

Check if your model perfroms well in an adversarial setting:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Add a misleading sentence to the input;
\item Replace a word with  synonym, a more general word, a comparable word (co-hyponym);
\item Swap subject and object;
\item Move an adjective to another noun phrase;
\item Add negations; add a negation and swap subject and object (contrapositive).
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Easy data augmentation} uses simple changes:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Synonym replacement;
\item Random insertion: take a word in the sentence and put another copy of it somewhere else;
\item Random swap;
\item Random deletion.
\end{compactitem}
\emph{Back-translation} also provides data augmentation.

Language models provide \emph{contextual embeddings}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item ELMo, a biLSTM with highway layers to forecast the next word
\item Transformers use muti-headed self-attention, poition embedding, dropout, residual connections and layer normalization
\item BERT is a masked language model, with a small vocabulary (subwords), position embedding, using CNNs with several perceptive fields and max pooling.
\end{compactitem}

To probe black-box models, take the word or sentence representation and
check if you can predict, with a shallow network:
sentence length,
which word is present,
tense,
POS, named entities, semantic information (anything from WordNet, sentiment, entailment, etc.),
tense.
Alternatively, change something in the sentence (tense, word order by swapping two consecutive words)
and see how the representation changes.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Linear algebraic structure of word senses, with applications to polysemy}\\
  S. Arora et al.
\end{book}
Use \emph{sparse coding} (and alternating minimization)
to decompose a given word embedding
$ w \mapsto v_w$ into disambiguated embeddings, $\alpha_{w,i} A_i$,
\[ v_w = \sum_i \alpha_{w,i} A_i + \text{noise} \]
where the $\alpha_{w,\cdot}$ are sparse; the $A_i$'s are the contexts (``atoms of discourse'').

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Contextual string embeddings\\ for sequence labelings}\\
  A. Akbik et al.
\end{book}
A character-level (BiLSTM) language model provides \emph{contextualized} word embeddings
(somehow aggregate the hidden states corresponding to the word letters),
which can be fed to another BiLSTM for named entity recognition (NER) or some other labeling task.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine translation\\ with weakly paired bilingual documents}\\
  (2019)
\end{book}
Use weakly-aligned documents
(e.g., matching Wikipedia articles)
to train a machine translation model:
find matching sentences from the cosine similarity of their bag-of-word embeddings,
from a multilingual word embedding (\textsc{muse}).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A syntactic neural model\\ for general-purpose code generation}\\
  P. Yin and G. Neubig
\end{book}
When translating natural language into code,
do not directly use a sequence-to-sequence model:
translate into an AST (abstract syntax tree) instead,
to ensure the code is syntactically correct.

Data from manually-annotated Django code, a card game, and an automation app.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural machine translation\\ and sequence-to-sequence models: a tutorial}\\
  G. Neubig (2017)
\end{book}
Tutorial, with exercises, covering count-based $n$-gram language models
\begin{align*}
  P[ e_{1:n} ] &= \prod_i P[ e_i | e_{1:i-1} ] \\
  &\approx \prod_i P[ e_i | e_{i-k : i-1} ],
\end{align*}
with modified Kneser-Ney smoothing,
log-linear models (with features $\phi$ and a softmax)
\[ P[e_{1:n}] \approx \prod_i P[ e_i | \phi( e_{ i-k : i-1 } ) ] , \]
neural networks (idem, but non-linear), RNNs, Seq2seq and attention.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A regularized framework for sparse\\ and structured neural attention}\\
  V. Niculae and M. Blondel
\end{book}
The maximum
\begin{align*}
  \max\nolimits^* y &=
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
    0 & \text{if } y \in \Delta \\
    \infty & \text{otherwise}
  \end{array}
  \right. \\
  \max x &= \max\nolimits^{**} x = \sup_{ y \in \Delta} y' x
\end{align*}
can be regularized
\begin{align*}
  \max\nolimits_\Omega^* y &=
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \gamma \Omega(y) & \text{if } y \in \Delta \\
    \infty & \text{otherwise}
  \end{array}
  \right. \\
  \max\nolimits_\Omega x &= \max\nolimits_\Omega^{**} x = \sup_{ y \in \Delta} y' x - \gamma \Omega(y).
\end{align*}
The softmax can be generalized to
\[
  \Pi_\Omega (x) = \argmax_{ y \in \Delta } y' x - \gamma \Omega(y) = \nabla \max\nolimits_\Omega (x).
\]
The softmax attention mechanism yields dense weights:
sparsemax gives sparse weights,
fusedmax sparse continuous weights,
oscarmax clustered weights regardless of position.
\begin{align*}
  \Omega(y) &= \sum y_i \log y_i
  &&\hspace*{-3cm}\text{negentropy}\\
  \Pi_\Omega(x) &= \dfrac{
    \exp( x/\gamma )
  }{
    \sum \exp( x_i / \gamma )
  }
  && \hspace*{-3cm}\text{softmax}
  \displaybreak[0]\\
  \Omega(y) &= \tfrac12 \n{ y }_2^2
  \\
  \Pi_\Omega(x) &= \argmin_{ y \in \Delta } \n{ y - x / \gamma }^2
  && \hspace*{-3cm}\text{spacemax}
  \displaybreak[0]\\
  \Omega(y) &= \tfrac12 \n{ y }_2^2 + \lambda \sum \n{ y_{i+1} - y_i }
  && \hspace*{-3cm}\text{fused lasso} \\
  \Pi_\Omega(x) &= \argmin_{ y \in \Delta } \n{ y - x / \gamma }^2
  + \lambda \sum \n{ y_{i+1} - y_i }
  && %\text{fusedmax}
  \displaybreak[0]\\
  \Omega(y) &= \tfrac12 \n{ y }_2^2 + \lambda \sum_{i<j} \max\bigl\{ \n{ y_i}, \n{y_j} \bigr\} \\
  \Pi_\Omega(x) &= \argmin_{ y \in \Delta } \n{ y - x / \gamma }^2
  + \lambda \sum_{i<j} \max\bigl\{ \n{ y_i}, \n{y_j} \bigr\}
  && %\text{oscarmax}
\end{align*}
%% \begin{align*}
%%   \Omega(y) &= \sum y_i \log y_i
%%   &&\text{negentropy}
%%   & \Pi_\Omega(x) &= \dfrac{
%%     \exp( x/\gamma )
%%   }{
%%     \sum \exp( x_i / \gamma )
%%   }
%%   && \text{softmax}
%%   \\
%%   \Omega(y) &= \tfrac12 \n{ y }_2^2
%%   &&
%%   & \Pi_\Omega(x) &= \argmin_{ y \in \Delta } \n{ y - x / \gamma }^2
%%   && \text{sparsemax}
%%   \\
%%   \Omega(y) &= \tfrac12 \n{ y }_2^2 + \lambda \sum \n{ y_{i+1} - y_i }
%%   && \text{fused lasso}
%%   & \Pi_\Omega(x) &= \argmin_{ y \in \Delta } \n{ y - x / \gamma }^2
%%   + \lambda \sum \n{ y_{i+1} - y_i }
%%   && \text{fusedmax}
%%   \\
%%   \Omega(y) &= \tfrac12 \n{ y }_2^2 + \lambda \sum_{i<j} \max\bigl\{ \n{ y_i}, \n{y_j} \bigr\}
%%   && 
%%   & \Pi_\Omega(x) &= \argmin_{ y \in \Delta } \n{ y - x / \gamma }^2
%%   + \lambda \sum_{i<j} \max\bigl\{ \n{ y_i}, \n{y_j} \bigr\}
%%   && \text{oscarmax}
%% \end{align*}

<<SoftMax,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=3,fig.height=3,out.width='.49\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Softmax (rescale)
library(DBQR)
op <- par( mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
plot( NA, xlim = c(-.1,1.5), ylim = c(-.1,1.5), axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
abline( h=0, v=0 )
segments( 0,1,1,0, lwd=5, col = dbColours[4] )
xs <- c(.1, .5, 1.1, 1.5 )
ys <- c( 1.2, .7, .3, .2 )
points( xs, ys, pch=16, cex = 2, col = dbColours[1] )
points( xs/(xs+ys), ys/(xs+ys), pch = 16, cex = 2, col = dbColours[1] )
segments( xs, ys, 0, 0, lty = 3, lwd = 3, col = dbColours[1] )
segments( xs, ys, xs/(xs+ys), ys/(xs+ys), lwd = 3, col = dbColours[1] )
par(op)
@
<<SparseMax,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=3,fig.height=3,out.width='.49\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Sparsemax (project)
op <- par( mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
plot( NA, xlim = c(-.1,1.5), ylim = c(-.1,1.5), axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
abline( h=0, v=0 )
segments( 0,1,1,0, lwd=5, col = dbColours[4] )
xs <- c(.1, .5, 1.1, 1.5 )
ys <- c( 1.2, .7, .3, .2 )
xxs <- ( 1 + xs - ys )/2
yys <- 1 - xxs
xxxs <- ifelse( xxs < 0, 0, ifelse( xxs > 1, 1, xxs ) )
yyys <- ifelse( yys < 0, 0, ifelse( yys > 1, 1, yys ) )
points( xs, ys, pch=16, cex = 2, col = dbColours[1] )
points( xxxs, yyys, pch = 16, cex = 2, col = dbColours[1] )
segments( xs, ys, xxs, yys, lwd = 3, col = dbColours[1] )
segments( xxs, yys, xxxs, yyys, lwd = 3, col = dbColours[1] )
par(op)
@
\hbox to \hsize{\hspace*{.8cm} Softmax (rescale) \hspace*{1.2cm} Sparsemax (project)\hss}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Searching for activation functions}\\
  P. Ramachandran et al.
\end{book}
Automated search for new activation functions, by combining 30+ common unary and binary functions,
using an RNN trained with RL to explore the search space,
% I would have usd a genetic algorithm
suggests
$\operatorname{swish}(x) = x \cdot \operatorname{sigmoid}(x)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning explanatory rules from noisy data}\\
  R. Evans and E. Grefenstette (2017)
\end{book}
\d{Inductive logic programming} (ILP) turns a set of positive and negative examples, e.g.,
\begin{align*}
  P &= \{ 0,2,4,6,8,\dots\} \\
  N &= \{ 1,3,5,7,9,\dots\}
\end{align*}
into rules, e.g.,
\begin{align*}
  \operatorname{even}(X) &\leftarrow \operatorname{zero}(X) \\
  \operatorname{even}(X) &\leftarrow \operatorname{even}(Y) \wedge \operatorname{succ2}(Y,X) \\
  \operatorname{succ2}(X,Y) &\leftarrow \operatorname{succ}(X,Z) \wedge \operatorname{succ}(Z,Y).
\end{align*}
The consequences of a set of rules can be obtained by breadth-first search (\emph{forward chaining}).

The top-down approach to ILP,
which generates programs and tests them on the positive and negative examples,
can be turned into a satisfiability problem,
and relaxed into a continuous one (replace boolean values with number in $[0,1]$),
amenable to gradient descent (forward chaining is differentiable: only perform $T$ steps of it).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Image search using multilingual texts:\\ a cross-modal learning approach\\ between image and text}\\
  M. Portaz et al.
\end{book}
Embed text (\textsc{muse},  a multilingual FastText extension)
and images in the same latent space.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep learning for image super-resolution:\\ a survey}\\
  Z. Wang et al.
\end{book}
The different approaches include
pre-upsampling ($\text{up} \cdot \text{conv}^k$),
post-upsampling ($\text{conv}^k \cdot \text{up}$),
progressive upsampling ($(\text{conv}^k \cdot \text{up})^n$),
iterative up-and-down-sampling
($(\text{conv} \cdot \text{up} \cdot \text{down})^n \cdot \text{up}$).

The upsampling can be nearest-neighbour, linear, bilinear,
or a fractionally-strided convolution (``deconvolution'').

The network can have residual connections, dense connections, and parallel paths (as in inception modules).

Loss functions include pixel loss, content loss (hidden layer of some classifier),
texture loss (Gram matrix of such a layer, as in style transfer),
adversarial loss,
cycle-consistency loss.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{EfficientNet: rethinking model scaling\\ for convolutional neural networks}\\
  M. Tan and Q.V. Le (2019)
\end{book}
Scale your network as
\begin{align*}
  &\text{depth} & d = \alpha ^ \phi \\
  &\text{width} & w = \beta ^ \phi \\
  &\text{resolution} & r = \gamma ^ \phi
\end{align*}
where $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$ are determined by grid search
and satisfy $\alpha^2 \beta^2 \gamma^2 = 2$, $\alpha,\beta,\gamma\ge1$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Interactive sketch \& fill: multiclass sketch-to-image translation}\\
  A. Ghosh et al. (2019)
\end{book}
GAN to convert partial sketches to complete sketches, and then complete sketches to images.
The desired object class is enforced using channel-wise gates -- conditional GANs tend to forget the input class.
The data comes from occluded contours, automatically extracted from images.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring the tendency of CNNs to learn surface statistical regularities}\\
  J. Jo and Y. Bengio 
\end{book}
CNNs to not recognize high-level features,
but focus on statistical regularities:
for instance, they are not robust to Fourier filtering.

% TODO: PLOT

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep normal estimation for automatic shading of hand-drawn characters}\\
  M. Hudon et al.
\end{book}
Use a CNN (U-net) to infer normals from (and automatically shade) hand-drawn 2D characters;
training data from Blender's \emph{FeeStyle}
(an edge- and line-based non-photorealistic (NPR) rendering engine).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deformable convolutional networks}\\
  J. Dai et al.
\end{book}
\d{Deformable convolution} networks use deformed grids as filters
(like \emph{active convolution}),
whose offsets are position-dependent,
computed from the features of the previous layer.

When a neural net generates both a list of regions of interest (ROI)
and (localized) features,
\emph{ROI pooling} pools the features in each ROI
(the pooling regions do not have a fixed size),
usually dividing the ROI into a regular grid.
It can be deformed: deformable ROI pooling.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{In defence of the triplet loss\\ for person re-identification}\\
  A. Hermans et al.
\end{book}
Person re-identification often uses the triplet loss
\[ \sum_{\substack{ i, j, k \\ y_i = y_j \\ y_i \neq y_k }} \bigl( m + d_{ij} - d_{ik} \bigr)_+ , \]
but since the number of triplets grows quadratically,
\emph{hard triplet mining} is necessary. Instead,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Build a batch of $P$ classes, with $K$ images in each,
  and compute the loss for all resulting triplets,
  or only for the hardest positive and hardest negative for each anchor;
\item Use the (non-squared) Euclidean distance;
\item Use a soft margin (softplus).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Variational image compression\\ with a scale hyperprior}\\
  J. Ball\'e et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Variational autoencoders (VAE)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
\matrix (m) [
  matrix of math nodes,
  nodes in empty cells,
  nodes={anchor=center},
  row sep={6ex,between origins},column sep={10ex,between origins}
]{
  x & y & x \\ & p(y) \\
};
\draw[|->] (m-1-1) -- (m-1-2);
\draw[|->] (m-1-2) -- (m-1-3);
\draw[->] (m-2-2) -- (m-1-2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
can be used for image compression
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
\matrix (m) [
  matrix of math nodes,
  nodes in empty cells,
  nodes={anchor=center},
  row sep={6ex,between origins},column sep={15ex,between origins}
]{
  x & y & \hat y & \hat x \\ & & p(\hat y) \\
};
\draw[|->] (m-1-1) -- (m-1-2);
\draw[|->] (m-1-2) -- node [above] {\footnotesize quantization} (m-1-3);
\draw[|->] (m-1-3) -- (m-1-4);
\draw[->] (m-2-3) -- (m-1-3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
and trained by replacing the quantization with uniform additive noise
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
\matrix (m) [
  matrix of math nodes,
  nodes in empty cells,
  nodes={anchor=center},
  row sep={6ex,between origins},column sep={10ex,between origins}
]{
  x & y & \tilde y & \tilde x \\ & & p(\tilde y) \\
};
\draw[|->] (m-1-1) -- (m-1-2);
\draw[|->] (m-1-2) -- node [above] {\footnotesize $+{}$noise} (m-1-3);
\draw[|->] (m-1-3) -- (m-1-4);
\draw[->] (m-2-3) -- (m-1-3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Have the variational distribution $p(\tilde y)$ depend on the image $x$
(or on the latent representation $y$).
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
\matrix (m) [
  matrix of math nodes,
  nodes in empty cells,
  nodes={anchor=center},
  row sep={6ex,between origins},column sep={10ex,between origins}
]{
  x & y & \tilde y & \tilde x \\ & \psi & p_\psi(\tilde y) \\
};
\draw[|->] (m-1-1) -- (m-1-2);
\draw[|->] (m-1-2) -- (m-1-3);
\draw[|->] (m-1-2) -- (m-2-2);
\draw[|->] (m-1-3) -- (m-1-4);
\draw[->] (m-2-3) -- (m-1-3);
\draw[->] (m-2-2) -- (m-2-3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SegNet: a deep convolutional encoder-decoder architecture for image segmentation}\\
  V. Badrinarayanan et al. (2016)
\end{book}
SegNet performs pixel-wise image segmentation by using
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item An encoder (VGG16);
\item Followed by a decoder, which upsamples the features using the \emph{pooling indices} from the encoder's pooling layers;
\item Followed by a pixelwise classification layer.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ENet: a deep neural network architecture\\ for real-time semantic segmentation}\\
  A. Paszke et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The EfficientNet (ENet) combines the following ideas to reduce the number of floating point operations when compared with VGG16:
SegNet (pooling indices),
early downsampling.
decoder smaller than the encoder,
\emph{PReLU} instead of ReLU,
pooling and convolution in parallel,
\emph{factorizing filters} ($5\times1$ and $1\times5$ instead of $5\times5$),
dilated convolutions,
\emph{spatial dropout} (dropout all channels at random locations).
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (I) at (1,2) {input};
  \node (A) at (0,1) {$3\times3$};
  \node (B) at (2,1) {MaxPool};
  \node (C) at (1,0) {Concat};
  \draw[->] (I) -- (A);
  \draw[->] (I) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (C);
