\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ShuffleNet: an extremely efficient convolutional neural network\\ for mobile devices}\\
  X. Zhang et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The $1\times1$ convolutions are dense matrix multiplications:
they are costly and can be replacced with block-diagonal matrices
(``pointwise group convolutions'') and (random) permutation matrices (``channel shuffle'').
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ShuffleSeg:\\ real-time semantic segmentation network}\\
  M. Gamal et al. (2018)
\end{book}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RepPoint point set representation\\ for object detection}\\
  Z. Yang et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Rectangular bounding boxes do not account for an object's shape and pose.
Instead, use a set of (9) points, inside the object,
computed with a feature pyramidal network (FPN) with deformable convolutions,
trained with a localization loss (it should give the samae bounding box) and 
a recognition loss. % no details?

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Complex-YOLO: an Euler-region-proposal forreal-time 3d object detection on point clouds}\\
  M. Simon et al. (2019)
\end{book}
To feed Lidar data to a \textsc{yolo} network to find 3D bounding boxes,
discretize the cloud of points into a top-view image, with 3 channels: 
maximum height, maximum intensity, number of points.
For the orientation of the boxes, output a complex number of modulus 2 instead of an angle 
to avoid singularities.
% Bad notations/terminology

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Oriented response networks}\\
  Y. Zhou et al.
\end{book}
To detect rotation-invariant patterns, and keep track of their orientation,
consider $N$ rotations of a given filter (sharing the same parameters).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Limitations\\ of the empirical Fisher approximation}\\
  F. Kunstner et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{empirical Fisher}
\[
  \sum_n \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta( y_n | x_n ) \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta( y_n | x_n ) '
\]
is not a good approximation of the Fisher information matrix
\[
  \sum_n \E_{ y \sim p_\theta(\cdot | x_n) }
  \bigl[ \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta( y_n | x_n ) \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta( y_n | x_n ) '  \bigr]
\]
Do not use it for natural gradient descent [but it is still better than plain gradient descent].

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model compression\\ by entropy penalized reparametrization}\\
  D. Oktay et al.
\end{book}
To compress the weights of a neural net, \d{scalar quantization} discretizes each coordinate independently,
while \d{vector quantization} uses some clustering algorithm (e.g., $k$-means).

Learn another network to generate weights from a low-dimensional, discrete (scalar quantization)
latent representation (hypernetwork) with a penalty for the length of the Shannon-encoded latent
representation (entropy).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning relational representations\\ with auto-encodding logic programs}\\
  S. Duman\v cic et al.
\end{book}
An auto-encoder, with (Prolog) facts as input and latent representation,
and (inverse) logical (Prolog) programs as encoder and decoder
can be learnt using constraint satisfaction tools
(large neighbourhood search: BUSL learner for Markov logic networks (MLN)),
minimizing the reconstruction error with constraints on
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The average number of facts per predicate;
\item Redundancy (if $c_1 \vDash c_2$, include at most one);
\item Coverage (reconstruct at least one of each predicate).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Joint semantic analysis and\\ morphological analysis of the derived word}\\
  R. Cotterell and H. Sch\"utze
\end{book}
Estimate word embeddings by modeling the derivation of words, e.g,
\[
\text{questionably = question:stem + able:suffix + ly:suffix}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Relational knowledge distillation}\\
  W. Park et al.
\end{book}
To distill a (teacher) model (into a student model),
do not just try to reproduce the individual outputs, but also the distances and angles.
% This will not help in high dimensions: the distances are already very similar
% Application: metric learning

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Interacting conceptual spaces I:\\ grammatical composition of concepts}\\
  J. Bolt (2016)
\end{book}
Many grammars can be modeled as a monoial category $\mathfrak G$, with reduction rules
(e.g., $\text{noun} \otimes \text{verb} \otimes \text{noun} \rightarrow \text{sentence}$)
as morphisms.
To model the meaning of sentences from that of wors, learn:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A word embedding $\text{Words} \rightarrow V$;
\item A monoidal functor $\mathfrak G \rightarrow \mathfrak{Vect}$.
\end{compactitem}
The category of vector spaces can be replaced by that of convex sets and relations
(more generally: convex algebras and convex relations).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Byzantine-tolerant machine learning}\\
  P. Blanchard et al.
\end{book}
Distributed stochastic gradient descent
is not tolerant to Byzantine failure:
use the vector closest to the average of its $n-k$ neighbours.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Solving ill-posed inverse problems\\ using iterative deep neural networks}\\
  J. Adler and O. \"Oktem (2017)
\end{book}
Use deep neural networks to find an approximate inverse of $T: X \to Y$, minimizing
\[
  \E_{\substack{ x \sim \text{data} \\ y = Tx }}
  \bigl[
    \text{Loss}( T T_\theta^{-1} y, y ) +
    \text{Penalty}( T_\theta^{-1} y )
  \bigr].
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Snapshot ensembles: train 1, get $M$ for free}\\
  G. Hunag et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Use a cyclic learning rate and ensemble the local minima found during the optimization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Personalized re-ranking for recommendation}\\
  C. Pei et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Reranking models, to transform a list of search results, sorted by their closeness to a query
(or the estimated probability that the user will click on it),
should be reranked to account for interaction (e.g., to ensure diversity and avoid redundant results).
The reranking model takes, as input, features of the items, their positions,
possibly user-specific item features (a learned user-specific linear transformation of the item features).
It is often an RNN generating a sequence of scores, along which to sort the items,
but it can be replaced with a transformer with attention.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FreezeOut: accelerate training\\ by progressively freezing layers}\\
  A. Brock et al.
\end{book}
Freeze the first layers of the network after a while:
they converge faster, to simple configurations (e.g., edge detectors).

\d{Stochastic depth}, in a resnet,
drops whole layers at a time.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On connected sublevel sets in deep learning}\\
  Q. Nguyen (2019)
\end{book}
If there are enough hidden neurons (half as many in the first hidden layer as training samples),
then the sublevel sets of the loss function are connected --
in particular, the set of global minima is connected.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A constructive approach\\ for one-shot training of neural networks\\ using hypercube-based topological coverings}\\
  W.B. Daniel and E. Yeung
\end{book}
Decision trees are special cases of neural nets (and canbe usd to unitialize them or choose their topology): decision boundaries are ReLU operations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Anchors:\\ high-precision model-agnostic explanations}\\
  M.T. Ribeiro et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To explain a model forecast,
find a single rule of thr form
$a_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge a_k$,
with beam search,
which applies to the observation of interest (local),
but with high (global) precision and decent coverage.

Contrary to purely local explanations (\textsc{lime}),
anchors give correct forecasts with high probability whenever they apply.
% They are still local, but "local" means "whenever they apply"

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Axiomatic attribution for deep neural networks}\\
  M. Sundararajan et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Attribution of the output of a neural net to input features should be
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sensitive: if $x_1$ and $x_2$ only differ by one feature and $f(x_1) \neq f(x_2)$,
then this feature's contribution should be non-zero;
\item Implementation-invariant: two different neural nets computing the same function should have the same contributions.
\end{compactitem}
\d{Integrated gradients} (along a rectilinear path, between a baseline input, e.g., a black image, and the image of interest) satisfy those conditions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BOHB: robust and efficient\\ hyperparameter optimization at scale}\\
  S. Falkner et al. (2018)
\end{book}
\emph{Hyperband} uses \emph{successive halving} for hyperparameter tuning:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pick $N$ configurations at random;
\item Train them for a while;
\item Discard the worst half;
\item Train the remaining models for a while;
\item Continue until only one is left.
\end{compactitem}
BOHB uses Bayesian optimization to replace the configurations at the begining of each HB iteration.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fixup initialization:\\ residual learning without normalization}\\
  H. Zhang et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Initialize the classification layer, the last layer of the residual branches, and the biases to zero;
rescale the other residual layers by $\sqrt L$, where $L$ is the number of layers (or $L^{1/2(m-1)}$,
if there are $m$ layers in the residual blocks).

% FixUp Initialization
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.2,yscale=.7,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt]
  \node[inner sep=0pt,circle,draw] (P) at (0,4.7) {$+$};
  \node (M) at (1,4) {Multiplier};
  \node (C2) at (1,3) {Conv};
  \node (R) at (1,2) {ReLU};
  \node (C1) at (1,1) {Conv};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (P);
  \draw[->] (P) -- (0,5.5);
  \draw[->] (0,.3) -| (C1);
  \draw[->] (C1) -- (R);
  \draw[->] (R) -- (C2);
  \draw[->] (C2) -- (M);
  \draw[->] (M) |- (P);
  \node at (2.5,5) {Initialization};
  \node at (2.5,4) {1};
  \node at (2.5,3) {0};
  \node at (2.5,1) {$\cdot/\sqrt L$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometric matrix completion\\ with recurrent multi-graph networks}\\
  F. Monti et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Matrix completion is often done with a low-rank approximation,
or its relaxation with the nuclear nurm (sum of the singular values)
\[
\begin{array}{l}
  \operatorname*{Minimize}\limits_X \text{rank}(X) + \lambda \n{ \Omega \odot ( X - Y ) }_F^2 \\
  \operatorname*{Minimize}\limits_X \n{ X }_* + \lambda \n{ \Omega \odot ( X - Y ) }_F^2.
\end{array}
\]
Given a user-item matrix, consider the corresponding row (user) and column (item)
similarity graphs,
and their Laplacians
\begin{align*}
  \Delta_r &=  \Phi_r \Lambda_r \Phi_r '\\
  \Delta_c &=  \Phi_c \Lambda_c \Phi_c '.
\end{align*}
The \emph{geometric completion} problem is
\[
\operatorname*{Minimize}_X
\n{ X }_{\Delta_r} ^2 +
\n{ X }_{\Delta_r} ^2 +
\lambda \n{ \Omega \odot ( X - Y ) }_F^2
\]
where the \d{Dirichlet norm} is
$ \n{ X }_\Delta = \text{trace}( X' \Delta X ) $.
Define the Fourier transform and the convolution as
\begin{align*}
  \hat X &= \Phi_r ' X \Phi_c\\
  X * Y &= \Phi_r ( \hat X \odot \hat Y ) \Phi_c '
\end{align*}
and approximate the filter $Y$ with Chebychev polynomials
\[
  \tilde X = \sum_{ 0 \leq i,j \leq p } \theta_{ij} T_i( \tilde \Delta_r ) X T_j( \tilde \Delta_c )
\]
where $\tilde \Delta = 2 \lambda_n ^{-1} \Delta - I$
are the rescaled Laplacians, with eigenvalues in $[-1,1]$.

Train such a \emph{multi-graph CNN} (MGCNN) for geometric matrix completion,
and feed its features to an RNN.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic matrix factorization\\ for automated machine learning}\\
  N. Fusi et al.
\end{book}
AutoML can be framed as a recommendation problem,
matching datasets (users) to ML pipelines (items),
with matrix factorization to recommend pipelines,
or with \emph{probabilistic matrix factorization}
to optimize an acquisition function (as in Bayesian optimization).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic matrix factorization}\\
  R. Salakhutdinov and A. Mnih
\end{book}
Matrix factorization
$R \approx U' V$
can be made Bayesian
\[
\argmax_{ U, V, \sigma } \prod_{\substack{
    i \text{ user} \\
    j \text{ item} \\
    i \text{ rated } j
}}
\phi( R_{ij} , \mu = U_i' V_j, \sigma^2 )
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph2Seq: graph to sequence learning\\ with attention-based neural networks}\\
  K. Xu et al. 
\end{book}
Estimate a node embedding by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Aggregating neighbour features
  (with mean,
  LSTM on random permutations of the neighbours, separating inbound and outbound neighbours,
  or elementwise max of a 1-layer neural net);
\item Concatenate with the node features;
\item Feed to a 1-layer neural net to get new node features;
\item Iterate 3 to 5 times (with different aggregators and neural nets).
\end{compactitem}
Convert it to a graph embedding with an elementwise max/min/mean,
and feed this graph representation to a sequence generator with attention.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CayleyNets: graph convolutional neural networks with complex rational spectral filters}\\
  R. Levie et al. (2017)
\end{book}
In the (spectral) graph convolution
$f \mapsto \Phi g(\Lambda) \Phi' f$,
where $\Delta = \Phi \Lambda \Phi'$ is the eigen decomposition of the Laplacian $\Delta$
and $f \mapsto \hat f = \Phi' f$ the Fourier transform,
do not model $g$ with Chebychev polynomials
(a high degree is needed to produce narrow-band filters --
in presence of communities, eigenvalues tend to cluster)
but with \emph{Cayley ``polynomials''}
$\lambda \mapsto \operatorname{Re} C(h \lambda)^k$
where the Cayley transform is
\[
\left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
  \RR & \longrightarrow & e^{i\RR} \setminus \{1\} \\
  \lambda & \longmapsto & \dfrac{ \lambda - i }{ \lambda + i }
\end{array}
\right.
\]
and $h$ a zoom parameter.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Amplifiers and suppressors of selection\\ for the Moran process in undirected graphs}\\
  G. Giakkoupis (2018)
\end{book}
The \d{Moran process} models the spread of genetic mutations on a graph:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a single mutant node;
\item Pick a node at random, with probability proportional to its fitness (1 for non-mutants, $r>1$ for mutants);
\item The node copies itself to one of its neighbours.
\end{compactitem}
There exist families of (directed or undirected) graphs for which
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The probability of fixation tends to 1 (strong amplifier);
\item The probability of extinction tends to 0 (strong suppressor).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A unified framework\\ for structured graph learning\\ via spectral constraints}\\
  S. Kumar et al.
\end{book}
Using the Laplacian, add constraints to the graphical lasso
(for graph reconstruction from a covariance matrix)
to make the graph bipartite, regular,
or to specify the number of connected components.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-dimensional count sketch:\\ dimension reduction\\ that retains efficient tensor operations}\\
  Y. Shi and A. Anandkumar
\end{book}
The \d{count sketch}
\[ \text{CS}(x) = \Biggl(
  \sum_{ h(i) = j } s_i x_j
\Biggr)_j = ( s \odot x )' H \]
can be generalized to tensors
\[
\text{MS}(T) = \bigl( (s_1 \otimes \cdots \otimes s_p ) \circ T \bigr) ( H_1,\dots, H_p )
\]
where
$ T \in \RR^{ n_1 \times \cdots \times n_p } $,
$ s_i \in \{ \pm 1 \} ^{n_i} $,
$H_i \in \{0,1\}^ { n_i \times m_i} $
with exactly one 1 in each row.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gorilla: a fast, scalable, in-memory\\ time series database}\\
  T. Pelkonen et al. (2015)
\end{book}
In 2013, Facebook moved from an HBase-backed OpenTSDB-like time series database
to an in-memory one (for the last 26 hours, HBase for the rest), using
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Delta of delta (variable length encoding) compression for timestamps: observations are almost equally spaced;
\item XOR encoding (and VLE compression) for numeric values:
  nearby values are often close, and share the begining of the mantissa, the exponent, and sometimes the precision.
\end{compactitem}
Also check
InfluxDB (no Hadoop required, distributed, metadata),
OpenTSDB (HBase, metadata),
Whisper (aka Graphite, regularly-spaced data, RDD, on-disk),
M3DB (from Uber).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards understanding generalizations\\ of deep learning: perspective of loss landscapes}\\
  L. Wu et al.
\end{book}
Minima whose bassins of attraction have a larger volume generalize better
(and are more likely to be reached after random initialization).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Loss landscapes\\ of regularized linear autoencoders}\\
  D. Kunin et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Linear autoencoders are $\textrm{GL}_k$-invariant: they learn the subspace spanned by the first $k$
principal directions, but not the separate directions.
$L^2$-regularized linear encoders do.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A PCA-like autoencoder}\\
  S. Ladjal et al. (2019)
\end{book}
To make the coordinates of the latent space of an autoencoder more interpretable and independent:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Iteratively increase the dimension of the latent space, keeping the already learned dimensions fixed
  (discard and learn the decoder anew each time);
\item Add a penalty for the (off-siagonal) covariances.  
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fader networks:\\ manipulating images by sliding attributes}\\
  G. Lample et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To train an autoencoder some of whose latent space coordinates have a pre-specified interpretation
(e.g., presence of glasses, beard, etc.),
add the interpretable feature to the latent space,
and ensure it is not present in the rest of the latent representation with a discriminator
$D : z \mapsto y$ and an adversarial loss.
\[
