\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Early visual concept learning\\ with unsupervised deep learning}\\
  I. Higgins et al.
\end{book}
In the latent factor model
\[
\operatorname*{Maximize}_{ \phi, \theta } \E_{ z \sim q_\phi(z|x) }
\bigl[ \log p_\theta( x | z ) \bigr]
\]
the \d{$\beta$-VAE} adds a constraint or penalty to make the latent representation $q_\phi$
close to a prior, e.g., $p \sim N(0,1)$, to ``disentangle'' it.
\[
\operatorname*{Maximize}_{\phi, \theta}
\E_{ z \sim q_\phi(z|x) }
\bigl[
  \log p_\theta(x|z)
\bigr]
- \beta \cdot \text{KL}
\bigl(
  q_\phi(z|x) \| p(z) 
\bigr)
\]
The original VAE corresponds to $\beta=1$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CorrGAN: sampling realistic financial correlation matrices usinggenerative adversarial networks}\\
  G. Marti (2019)
\end{book}
GAN to generate \emph{reordered} (with hierarchical clustering) correlation matrices.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation of theory-implied\\ correlation matrices}\\
  M. L\'opez de Prado (2019)
\end{book}
A hiearchical classification (e.g., GICS) can improve the estimation of the correlation matrix:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the sample correlation matrix;
\item Represent the hierarchy as a tree and use the correlations to define distances between nodes,
  using $ d = \sqrt{ \tfrac12 ( 1 - \rho ) } $;
\item Compute the distances between all pairs of leaves, and convert them back to ``correlations'',
  using $\rho = 1 - 2 d^2$;
\item Clean the resulting matrix (it may not even be positive definite) using random matrix theory (RMT).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepTrax:\\ embedding graphs of financial transactions}\\
  C.B. Bruss et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Project the credit card (bipartite) transaction graph to only keep merchants
and compute a random-walk based vector embedding (DeepWalk, Node2vec);
use as additional features in fraud detection systems.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Testing for multiple bubbles 1:\\
  historical episodes of exhuberance\\ and collapse in the S\&P 500}\\
  P.C.B. Phillips et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The SADF test,
\[ \text{SADF} = \sup_{0 < t_0 \leq t \leq 1 } \text{ADF}_{[t_0,1]}, \]
to detect bubbles (explosive behaviour: bubble-less log-prices should be $I(1)$, not more)
can be generalized to account for multiple bubbles by using a moving window (reset after each bubble)
instead of an expanding one.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Decomposing value}\\
  J. Gerakos and J.T. Linnainmaa (2016)
\end{book}
Decompose the $B/P$ into a component explained by past (5-year) size changes,
and a esidual -- only the former leads to excess returns.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time-dependent Black-Litterman}\\
  M. van der Schans and H. Steehouwer (2016)
\end{book}
Black-Litterman and Kalman filter can be combined:
both are (linear, Gaussian) Bayesian updates.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Listening to chaotic whispers:\\ a deep learning framework\\ for news-oriented stock trend prediction}\\
  Z. Hu et al (2017)
\end{book}
Attention-based RNN, not for sequences of words, but for sequences of news (bags of (vector embeddings of) words), to forecast trends.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Practical volume computation of structured convex bodies, and an application to modeling portfolio dependencies and financial crises}\\
  L. Cal\`es et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The \emph{cross-sectional score} of a portfolio is the probability that a random (uniform in $\Delta^d$)
portfolio has worse returns (or is worse for some other potfolio metric).
It can be computed with a quasi Monte Carlo method.
[This is a special vase of P.Burns's random portfolios, which also account for portfolio constraints;
the uniform distribution dodes not give insightful results if there are too many assets.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Notes on Fano ratio and portfolio optimization}\\
  Z. Kakushadze and W. Yu
\end{book}
Daily and monthly Sharpe ratios are not comparable (because of the square root of time rule),
but the mean-to-variance ratios (Fano ratios) are comparable accross investment horizons. Fano-optimal portfolios are more diversified.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonlinearity in stock networks}\\
  D. Hartman and J. Hlinka (2018)
\end{book}
Differences between stock networks computed from correlation matrices and mutual information
(which requires joint density estimation)
may not come from nonlinearities but from non-stationarity and non-Gaussian margins.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conic CPPI}\\
  I. Marquet and W. Schoutens (2017)
\end{book}
% NOT CLEAR
A 1-asset investment strategy can be seen as a reinforcement learning problem
in which the state is described by the VIX and the current wealth.
Insead of maximizing the expected future reward,
for the real or the risk-free measure,
one can maximize
\[ \inf_{ Q \in \mathscr M } \text{E}^Q[ \text{future rewards} ] \]
which can be computed as the expectation under a concave \emph{distortion function}
\[ \psi : [0,1] \to [0,1] , \qquad \psi(0) = 0, \qquad \psi(1) \]
(\emph{Choquet expectation},
expectation under a \emph{non-additive probability},
to emphasize tail loss events and de-emphasize tail gain events)
\[
\text{E}^\psi[Y] =
- \int_{-\infty}^0 \psi\bigl( F_Y(y) \bigr) dy +
\int_0^\infty \bigl( 1 - \psi\bigl( F_Y(y) \bigr) \bigr) dy.
\]
The \emph{min-max-var} family of distortion functions is
\[ \psi(u) = 1 - ( 1 - u^{ 1 / (1+\lambda) } ) ^{1+\lambda} . \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Equity portfolio risk (volatility) estimation using market information and sentiment}\\
  L. Mitra et al. (2008)
\end{book}
Implied volatility and change in news sentiment (Ravenpack)
have a predictive power in future volatility,
and can be used as part of a factor risk model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Indirect influences in international trade}\\
  R. D\'iaz and L. G\'omez
\end{book}
To compute the influence of a country on the trade network, use
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item PageRank
\item The incidence matrix $D$ (direct influence);
\item MicMac: $D^k$;
\item The heat kernel: $\exp \lambda(D-I)$;
\item PWP: $\text{expm1}(\lambda D) / \text{expm1}(\lambda)$
\end{compactitem}
with the trade network
\[ D_{A \to A} = \dfrac{
  \text{Exports}_{ A \to B } + \text{Imports}_{ B \to A }
}{
  \text{Exports}_A + \text{Imports}_A
} \]
or the offer network
\[ D_{A \to A} = \dfrac{
  \text{Exports}_{ A \to B } + \text{Imports}_{ B \to A }
}{
  \text{GDP}_A + \text{Imports}_A.
} \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dissecting characteristics nonparametrically}\\
  J. Freyberger et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Spline regression, with a group lasso penalty,
performs better than a linear model (with a lasso penalty) on uniformized variables.
[I use GAM boosting to the same effect.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Taming the factor zoo}\\
  G. Feng et al. (2017)
\end{book}
To check if a new factor adds value,
to forecast returns, to an already large set of factors $(f_j)_j$,
use the \emph{double-selection lasso}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Select a small set of factors using the lasso, $(g_i)_i$;
\item For each selected factor $g_i$, select factors $f$ whose covariance with the returns $r$
is correlated with $\cov(g_i,r)$, using the lasso:
$ \cov(g_i,r) \sim \cov(f,r)$;
\item Use all those factors to test the new candidate.
\end{compactitem}

