% Empty, superficial
The integrated cdf, used to define stochastic dominance,
is the expected shortfall (seen as a function of the threshold):
a portfolio $\lambda$ dominate a portfolio $\tau$ if its expected shortfall is better,
for all threhsolds -- equivalently,
if it is better for all (increasing, concave) utility functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The dynamic factor model\\ with an application to global credit risk}\\
  F. Br\"auning and S.J. Koopman (2016)
\end{book}
Model the financial network (Granger causality tests for CDSs of US and European banks) as
\begin{align*}
  f_t &: \text{ factors (global, not observed)} \\
  z_{ij} &: \text{factor loadings (constant)} \\
  \theta_{ijt} &= z_{ij} ' f_t \\
  \phi_{ijt} &= \text{logistic}( \theta_{ijt} ) \\
  y_{ijt} &\sim \text{Bernoulli}(\phi_{ijt}) \text{ incidence matrices}
  \displaybreak[0]
  \\[2mm]
  \gamma_i &\sim N(\mu, \Sigma) \\
  \pi_i & \propto \exp(\gamma_i)     \qquad \text{$k$-dimensional} \\
  \delta_{ij} &= \pi_i \otimes \pi_j \qquad \text{$k^2$-dimensional} \\
  z_{ij} &\sim \text{Multinomial}( \delta_{ij} )
  \displaybreak[0]
  \\[2mm]
  f_{t+1} &= \Phi f_t + \xi_t \\
  \xi_t &\sim N(0,\Sigma) \\
  f_1 & \sim N(0,V) \\
  V &= \Phi V \Phi' + \Sigma
\end{align*}

