\[
  \text{loss}( u , v ) = - \sum_k \phi\biggl( 
  \dfrac{ u_k - v_k }{ \sigma }
  \biggr)
\]
gives robust autoencoders.

\emph{Feature fusion} is a synonym for dimension reduction (PCA, LDA, kernel PCA, MDS, Isomap, Laplacian eigenmaps, RBM)



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Analysis of multivariate time-series\\ using the MARSS package} \\
  E.E. Holmes et al. (2018)
\end{book}
\begin{align*}
  & \text{hidden} & x_t &= B x_{t-1} + u + C c_t + w_t & w_t &\sim N(0,Q)\\
  & \text{observed} & y_t &= Z x_t + a + D d_t + v_t & v_t &\sim N(0,R)
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Matrix profile I:\\ all pairs similarity joins for time series:\\ a unifying view that includes motifs,\\ discords and shapelets} \\
  C.C.M. Yeh et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The \d{matrix profile} of a time series is the time series of distances to the nearest neighbour.
\[
\text{mp}_t = \min_{ \va{ t - s } > a }  
d( x_{t : t+n} , x_{s : s + n} )
\]
It summarizes the information in the distance matrix 
$\bigl( d(x_t,x_s) \bigr)_{t,s} $.

To be robust to outliers, take the $k$th nearest neighbour.

To convert DNA to a numeric sequence, add 2, 1, $-1$, $-2$
for A, G, C, T.

Use weights to account for prior information 
(regions more or less likely to contain interesting motifs).
\[
\text{cmp}_i = \text{mp}_i + (1 - \text{av}_i) \max( \text{mp} )
\]

Applications include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Motif detection: low values;
\item \d{Discords} (outliers): high values;
\item Segmentation, by looking at where the nearest neighbours are and finding the point with the fewest crossings.
\end{compactitem}

The matrix profile can be computed efficiently 
($n \log n$, thanks to the FFT, if we use the $z$-normalized Euclidean distance, \ie, the correlation).

Implementations in Python (\texttt{stumpy}, etc.) and R (\texttt{tsmp}); 14 more papers in the series.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A complexity-invariant distance measure\\ for time series} \\
  G.E. Batista et al.
\end{book}
Pairs of complex objects tend to be more distant, for most distance measures,
than pairs of simple objects.
There are already distance measures invariant to offset, scale (correlation), warping (DTW), phase, occlusion (missing subsequence): 
\[
  \text{CID}(f,g) = \n{ f - g }_2 \dfrac{
    \max\bigl\{ \n{ f' }_2 , \n{ g' }_2 \bigr\}
  }{
    \min\bigl\{ \n{ f' }_2 , \n{ g' }_2 \bigr\}
  }
\]
is invariant to complexity changes, 
if the complexity of $f$ is measured as 
$\n{ f' }_2$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A brief introduction to machine learning\\ for engineers} \\
  O. Simeone (2017)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.} 
There are several approached to statistics: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Frequentist (no prior; point estimate);
\item Bayesian (prior; posterior distribution);
\item MAP (prior distribution, \ie, regularization; point estimate);
\item MDL (penalty for complexity; point estimate).
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{2. No free lunch theorem.} 
One cannot learn rules that generalize to unseen examples 
without making assumptions (\emph{inductive bias})
about the data generation mechanism.

Supervised learning looks for a predictive algorithm minimizing generalization loss.

\d{Discriminative models} directly model $p(y|x)$.

\d{Generative models} model $p(x,y)$ (sometimes by separately modeling $p(y)$ and $p(x|y)$), from which we can compute $p(y|x)$ -- 
there is a higher risk of incorrect assumptions, 
but it is easier to deal with missing values.

The Bayesian approach allows model selection without validation,
with the \d{marginal likelihood}.
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  f_\alpha & \text{prior} \\ 
  \theta \sim f_\alpha & \text{parameters} \\
  g_\theta & \text{model likelihood} \\
  x \sim g_\theta & \text{data} \\
  g(x) := E_\theta\bigl[ g_\theta(x) \bigr] & \text{marginal likelihood}
\end{array}
\]

\d{Empirical Bayes} estimates the parameter prior from the data.

The \d{Kullback-Leibler divergence} 
is the expectation of the log-likelihood-ratio between two distributions, 
$\log p(x)/q(x)$, wrt the distribution in the numerator.
It is non-negative; it is zero iff $p=q$.
The log-likelihood is $\text{KL}(\text{data}\|\text{model})$.
\begin{align*}
  \text{KL}( p \| q ) &= \E_{x \sim p } \biggl[ \log \dfrac{ p(x) }{ q(x) } \biggr] \\
  H( p \| q ) &= \E_{x \sim p } \bigl[ - \log q(x) \bigr] & \text{cross-entropy} \\
  H( p ) &= \E_{x \sim p } \bigl[ - \log p(x) \bigr] & \text{entropy}   
  \end{align*}

% 3. Probabilistic models for learning
\textbf{3.}
\d{Exponential family} (or \d{log-linear}) models are of the form 
\begin{align*} 
  p(x | \eta ) & \propto \exp\bigl( \eta' u(x) \bigr) m(x) \\
  \log p(x | \eta ) &= \eta' u(x) + \log m(x) - A(\eta) 
\end{align*}
where 
\[
\begin{array}{ll} 
  \eta & \text{natural parameters} \\
  u & \text{sufficient statistics} \\
  m & \text{base measure} \\
  \mu = \E\limits_{ x \sim p( \cdot | \eta ) } \bigl[ u(x) \bigr] & 
  \text{mean parameters.}
\end{array}
\]

The Gaussian model $N(\mu, \sigma^2)$ is exponential, 
with 
\[
  u(x) = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ x^2 \end{pmatrix}
  \quad
  \eta = \begin{pmatrix} \mu / \sigma^2 \\ - 1 / 2 \sigma^2 \end{pmatrix} 
  \quad
  m = 1.
\]

The natural and mean parameters are related through the gradient of the partition function.
\begin{align*}
  \nabla _{\!\eta} A(\eta) &= \mu \\
  \nabla^2 _{\!\eta} A(\eta) &= \cov u(x) = I_\eta  
\end{align*}

The exponential family $p(x|\eta)$ is the \d{maximum entropy} distribution 
under the constraints 
$E \bigl[ u(x) \bigr] = \mu$
(and the natural parameters are the corresponding Lagrange multipliers).

The gradient of the log-likelihood is 
$\nabla _\eta \ell = \hat \mu - \mu$.

In Bayesian models, one often chooses \emph{conjugate priors},
\ie, priors such that priors and posteriors belong to the same family
(Beta-Bernoulli, Dirichlet-Categorical, Gaussian-Gaussian, etc.).

One can vary the prior, to see, for instance, how strong it should be to change the MAP estimator.

\d{Energy-based models} are of the form 
\[ p(x | \eta ) \propto \exp - \sum_c E_x( x_c | \eta ); \]
they generalize the exponential families (for which $E_c$ is linear).
For instance, 
$E(x | \eta ) = \log \bigl( 1 + (\eta ' x )^2 \bigr)$
gives a Student $T$ distribution; 
Markov models are another example.

A \d{generalized linear model} (GLM) 
is a model from an exponential family whose 
parameter $\eta$ depends linearly on the predictors, 
$\eta = W x$ or $\eta = W \phi(x)$.

\textbf{4. Classification}

\emph{Discriminative deterministic} (classification) models (SVM, $k$-NN) 
output binary results.

\emph{Discriminative probabilistic} models (GLM) 
output probabilities $P[\text{label} | \text{feature} ]$.

\emph{Generative probabilistic} models model the joint probability 
$P(\text{label}, \text{feature}) $,
often separating $P(\text{label})$
and $P[\text{features} | \text{label}]$; 
examples include QDA 
\begin{align*}
  t &\sim \text{Bernoulli}(\pi) \\
  x | t = k & \sim N(\mu_k, \Sigma_k)
\end{align*}
or LDA (when $\Sigma_k$ does not depend on $k$).

\textbf{5. Statistical learning theory.}
We want to minimize the \emph{generalization error}
\[ 
  \E_{ (x,y) \sim p } 
  \text{loss}\bigl( t, \hat t(x) \bigr)
\]
but the data distribution $p$ is not known.
Instead, we can use the sample distribution and minimize 
the \emph{empirical risk} 
\[ 
  \E_ { (x,y) \sim \text{Data} } 
  \text{loss}\bigl( y, \hat t(x) \bigr).
\]

A learning rule is \d{probably approximately correct} (PAC) if 
\[
  \forall p \in \mathscr H \quad
  \P_{\substack{ 
    \text{Data} \sim p \\
    \text{size}(\text{Data}) \geq N 
  }}
  \bigl[
    \text{loss}_p(\hat t) \leq \text{loss}_p(t^*) + \epsilon
  \bigr ]
  \geq 1 - \alpha
\]
where $N$, the \emph{sample complexity}, only depends on $\epsilon$ and $\alpha$ (not $p$).

Finite hypothesis classes $\mathscr H$
are \d{PAC-learnable} via empirical risk minimization (ERM)
with sample complexity 
\begin{align*}
  N &= \dfrac{
    2 \log \va{ \mathscr H } + 2 \log( 2/\delta )
  }{
    \epsilon^2
  } \\
  N &= \dfrac{
    \log \va{ \mathscr H } + \log( 1/\delta )
  }{
    \epsilon
  } (?) \\
  N &= \Theta\left(
    \dfrac{
      \dim_{\text{VC}}  \mathscr H + \log( 1 / \delta )
    }{
      \epsilon^2
    }
  \right).
\end{align*}
Dimension-$d$ (quantized) models have sample complexity proportional to $d+1$.

The \emph{minimax redundancy} is another measure of model capacity.
\[
  \Delta R( \mathscr H )  = \min_{ q \in \mathscr H }
  \max_{ p \in \mathscr H } 
  \text{KL}( p \| q )
\]

\emph{Structural risk minimization} combines model selection and hypothesis testing by minimizing an upper bound on the generalization loss 
\[
  \text{loss}_p ( \hat t ) \leq
  \text{loss}_{\text{Data}} ( \hat t ) + 
  \sqrt{\dfrac{
    \log 2 \va{ \mathscr H} / \delta
  }{
    2 N
  }
  }.
\]

\textbf{6.}
The \d{EM algorithm}
\begin{align*}
  x &: \text{observed} \\
  z &: \text{latent} \\
  \theta &: \text{parameters} 
\end{align*}
alternates between two steps 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item M step: point estimate of $\theta | x , z $;
\item E step: distribution estimate of $z | x , \theta$;
\end{compactitem}
It can also be motivated using the \d{evidence lower bound}(ELBO)
\begin{align*}
  \log p(x | \theta ) 
  &= \log \sum_z p(x, z | \theta ) \\
  &= \log \sum_z q(z) \dfrac{ p(x, z | \theta ) }{ q(z) } 
  && \substack{
    \displaystyle\text{\rlap{Importance}\hphantom{Importance}}\\
    \displaystyle\text{\rlap{sampling}\hphantom{Importance}}
    } \\
  &> \sum_z q(z) \log \dfrac{ p(x, z | \theta ) }{ q(z) } 
  && \text{Jensen} \\
  &= \mathscr L( q, \theta )
\end{align*}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item E step:
  $q \leftarrow \argmax \mathscr L( q, \theta ) $
  (with $\theta$ fixed),
  \ie, $q(z) = p(z | x, \theta )$;
\item M step: 
  $\theta \leftarrow \argmax_\theta \mathscr L(q, \theta)$.
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Probabilistic PCA} (PPCA) 
can be estimated with the EM algorithm.
\begin{align*}
  z &\sim N(0,1) \\
  x | z &\sim N(Wx + \mu, \sigma^2 I )
\end{align*}

In unsupervised learning, the loss is often the divergence between the model and the data.
The Kullback-Leibler divergence is not always the best choice: 
minimizing $\text{KL}( \text{data} \| \text{model} )$
tends to produce blurry estimates. 

\d{Generative adversarial networks} (GAN) 
generalize generative directed models: they
learn at the same time a divergence and a generative model,
\[ \min_\theta \max_T \E_{ x \sim p } \bigl[ T(x) \bigr] -
\E_{ x \sim q_\theta } \bigl[ g \bigl( T(x) \bigr) \bigr] 
\]
with $T$ a neural net and $g$ fixed
\begin{align*}
  g(t) &= - \log( 1 - e^t ) && \text{GAN} \\
  g(t) &= 1 - t && \text{WGAN}.
\end{align*}

\emph{Helmoltz machines} are multilayer extensions of generative directed models.

\textbf{7.}
Probabilistic graphical models,
directed (Bayesian networks) 
or not (Markov random fields, MRF)
encode conditional independence relations.

\textbf{8. Variational inference} (VI) approximates the posterior as 
an I-projection.
\begin{align*}
  p(z | x ) 
  &= \argmin_{ q(z) } \text{KL}\bigl( q(z) \bigm\| p(z|x) \bigr) \\
  &= \argmin_{ q(z) } \text{KL}\bigl( q(z) \bigm\| p(z,x) \bigr) + \log p(x) \\
  &= \argmin_{ q(z) } \text{KL}\bigl( q(z) \bigm\| p(z,x) \bigr) \\
  &= \argmin_{ q(z) } - \E_{ z \sim q } \bigl[ \log p(z,x)  \bigr] - H(q) \\
  &= \argmin_{ q(z) } - \mathscr L(q) \\
  &\approx \argmin_{ q \in \mathscr F } - \mathscr L(q)
\end{align*}
The I-projection is mode-seeking, 
the M-projection zero-avoiding.
\begin{align*}
\text{pr}_I(p) &= \argmin_{ q \in \mathscr F } \text{KL}( q \| p ) \\
\text{pr}_M(p) &= \argmin_{ q \in \mathscr F } \text{KL}( p \| q) 
\end{align*}

The $\alpha$-divergence 
\[
  D_\alpha(p\|q) =
  \dfrac{\displaystyle
    \sum_x \alpha p(x) + (1-\alpha) q(x) - p(x)^\alpha q(x) ^ {1-\alpha}
  }{
    \alpha ( 1 - \alpha )
  }
\]   
interpolates between 
$\text{KL}(p\|q)$ (for $\alpha=1$)
and
$\text{KL}(p\|q)$ ($\alpha=0$).



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Most likely transformations: the mlt package} \\
  T. Hothorn (2018)
\end{book}
The regression function of $Y|X$ is often defined as 
$f(x) = E[ Y | X = x ]$, 
without any distributional assumtion,
but it could be written, with a Gaussian model,
\[ Y | X = x   \sim   N\bigl( f(x), \sigma^2 \bigr) \]
or (this immediately allows censored data)
\[ P[ Y \leq y | X = x ] = F \biggl( 
\dfrac{ y - f(x) }{ \sigma } \biggr) \]
or, more generally 
\[ P[ Y \leq y | X = x ] = F \bigl( 
h(y) - f(x) \bigr) \]
where $h(y) = \theta' a(y) $, with $\theta$ to be estimated
and $a$ basis functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive gradient methods\\ with dynamic bound of learning rate}\\
  L. Luo et al. (2019)
\end{book}
Adaptive optimization algorithms (Adam, RMSProp, etc.) 
can choose extreme learning rates, leading to poor performance --
instead, bound the learning rates to a shrinking interval, \ie, progressively move towards plain stochastic gradient descent.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Bloom clock} \\
  L. Ramabaja
\end{book}
The \emph{vector clock} provides a partial order on the events
in a distributed system: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Each node keeps a vector of size $n$, with one coordinate for each node;
\item When a node sends an event, it increments its coordinate and broadcasts its vector;
\item When a node receives a vector, it increments its coordinate 
and replaces its vector with the elementwise maximum of the two.
\end{compactitem}
The vector clock can be bloomified.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{$f$-GAN: training generative neural samplers using variational divergence minimization} \\  
\end{book}
An $f$-GAN looks for a generator $g_\theta$ minimizing the 
$f$-divergence 
\[ D_{\!f}( p_{\text{data}} \| q_\theta ) = \E_{ x \sim q } 
\biggl[ f \biggl( \dfrac{ p(x) }{ q(x) } \biggr) \biggr].
\]
Since 
\begin{align*}
  D_{\!f}( p \| q ) &\geq \sup_T 
  \E_{ x \sim p } \bigl[ T(x) \bigr] - 
  \E_{ x \sim q } \bigl[ f^*\bigl( T(x) \bigr) \bigr] \\
  &= 
  \E_{ x \sim p } \bigl[ T(x) \bigr] - 
  \E_{ x \sim q } \bigl[ f^*\bigl( T(x) \bigr) \bigr]
\end{align*}
for $T(x) = f' \bigl( p(x) / q(x) \bigr)$,
we can instead try the saddle points of 
\[ 
F(\omega,\theta) = 
\E_{ x \sim p_{\text{data}} }\bigl[ T_\omega(x) \bigr] - 
\E_{ x \sim q_\theta } \bigl[ f^*\bigl(T_\omega(x) \bigr)\bigr]
\]
which can be approximated with samples from $p$ and $q$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Holographic embeddings of knowledge graphs} \\
  M. Nickel et al.
\end{book}
Knowledge graphs (RDF triples, SVO triples)
can be embedded in a vector space by learning vector representations
of subject, object and predicate, 
\begin{align*}
  \text{score}( s, p, 0 ) &= - d( e_s + r_p, e_o ) \\
  \text{or}\quad P[s, p, o ] &= \sigma\bigl( r' ( e_s \circ e_o ) \bigr).
\end{align*}
The composition operator $\cdot \circ \cdot$ can be one of 
\begin{align*}
  a \circ b &= a \otimes b \\
  a \circ b &= \psi \bigl( W ( a \oplus b ) \bigr) 
  \ \ \ \substack{
    %\displaystyle
    \text{\small concatenation, projection,}\\
    %\displaystyle
    \rlap{\small\text{non-linearity}}\hphantom{\text{\small concatenation, projection,}}
  } \\
  a \circ b &= a \star b = 
  \biggl( \sum_i a_i b_{ i + k \text{ mod } d} \biggr)_k
\end{align*}
(for the tensor product, since $r'(a \otimes b) = a' R b$, 
one can ask that $R$ be diagonal,
but this makes all relations symmetric).

The \d{circular correlation} $\star$
can be seen as a ``compression'' of the tensor product.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distributed convex optimization\\ with many non-linear constraints} \\
  J. Giesen and S. Laue
\end{book}
ADMM 
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x, z \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(x) + g(z) \\
  \text{Such that} & Ax + Bz = c,
\end{array} \]
often used 
(on a distributed infrastructure)
to minimize separable functions
$f(\theta) = \sum_i f_i(\theta)$, 
\[ \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x, z \\
  \text{To minimize} & \sum_i f_i(x_i) \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ x_i = z
\end{array} \]
can also handle nonlinear convex \emph{in}equalities
(they keep the problem convex).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Link prediction based on graph neural networks} \\
  M. Zhang and Y. Chen (2018)
\end{book}
Link prediction heuristics (common neighbours, Katz index)
make strong assumptions on when two nodes are linked;
instead, learn a function mapping subgraph patterns
to link existence (SEAL).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Discovering and deciphering relationships across disparate data modalities} \\
  Y.T. Vogelstein et al.
\end{book}
To test if $X \indep Y$: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the distance matrices (between observations)
for $X$ and $Y$; center them: $A$, $B$;
\item Compute the corresponding $k$-NN graphs: $G_k$, $H_k$ (binary matrices);
\item Compute the local correlations
\[ t_{k\ell} = \sum_{ij} A_{ij} B_{ij} G_{kij} H_{\ell ij}; \]
\item Smooth $(k,\ell) \mapsto t_{k \ell}$
and compute its maximum $t^*$;
\item Use a permutation test to see if it is significant.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Minimum R\'enyi entropy portfolios} \\
  N. Lassance and F. Vrins (2018)
\end{book}
R\'enyi entropy combines variance, skewness and kurtosis 
(as shown by a Gram-Charlier expansion): 
it can be used as a risk measure.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DGM: a deep learning algorithm\\ for solving partial differential equations} \\
J. Sirignano and K. Spiliopoulos (2018)
\end{book}
Deep learning tools can provide mesh-free solutions to PDEs -- 
meshes quickly become unusable as dimension increases.

