\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DARTS: differentiable architecture search} \\
  H. Liu et al.
\end{book}
Search for computation cells with two inputs (previous two layers in a non-RNN, input and previous state in an RNN)
by modeling them as DAGs on $n$ nodes ($n$ fixed), 
each connected to the previous ones, 
where the edges implement a linear combination of simple operations (zero, convolution, max pooling, etc.).
Alternate between gradient steps in the architecture and weight spaces;
for each edge, keep the most likely operation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Differentiable learning-to-normalize\\ via switchable normalization} \\
  P. Luo et al.
\end{book}
Successive normalization layers need not be identical:
try linear combinations of normalizations
(batch, layer, channel)
with learned weights.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient neural architecture search\\ with neural morphism} \\
  H. Jin et al.
\end{book}
Search for a good network architecture ``morphing'' it,
by adding or removing layers or skip-connections,
by changing the layer sizes, etc.
(training is fast if we start from the previous, trained architecture).

For Bayesian optimization with Gaussian processes, use a kernel of the form 
$\kappa(a,b) = \exp - d(a,b)$,
for some measure of distance $d$ between the candidate architectures.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Regularization learning networks} \\
  I. Shavitt and E. Segal
\end{book}
Use a different regularization coefficient for each weight and replace 
the loss 
\( 
  \text{Loss}(w, \lambda) = \text{Loss}[w] + \exp(\lambda) ' \va{w} 
\)
with 
\[ \text{Loss}\bigl[ 
  w - \eta \nabla_{\!w} \text{Loss}(w,\lambda)
\bigr]. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{AutoAugment:\\ learning augmentation policies from data} \\
  E.D. Cubuk et al.
\end{book}
Parametrized data augmentation procedures can be learnt.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Explainable neural networks\\ based on additive index models} \\
  J. Vaughan et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Generalized additive models 
$ f(x) = \sum f_i(x_i) $
and \d{additive index models} 
\[ f(x) = \sum g_i( \beta_i ' x ) \]
are interpretable and can be modeled by neural networks.
