\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How many random seeds?\\
  Statistical power analysis\\ in deep reinforcement learning experiments} \\
  C. Colas et al.
\end{book}
The significance (often, $\alpha=0.05$) of a statistical test
only controls the type I errors.
Compute the sample size needed to achieve the desired power
(e.g., type II error $\beta=0.20$),
for the desired effect size $\epsilon$,
and the estimated variances.


In practice, to compare deep learning or reinforcement learning algorithms, 
run them at least 20 times with different random seeds.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring scientific broadness} \\
  T. Price and S. Hossenfelder
\end{book}
To measure the ``broadness'' of a researcher: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Retrieve Arxiv abstracts using the API;
\item Extract ``keywords'': sequences of at most 10 words appearing in at least 20 papers;
clean them (remove s's, spaces; stem);
\item Compute the ``rank'' of the keywords: 
\begin{align*}
  %\operatorname{rank} k = 
  &\text{KL} \bigl(
    P[ \text{category} | \text{keyword}=k ]
    \,\|\,
    P[ \text{category} ]
  \bigr)
  \times \\
  &\qquad
  \left(
  1 - \exp - \dfrac{ P[ \text{keyword} ] }{ r }
  \right)
\end{align*}
\item Estimate an LDA model on the top 40,000 keywords (50 topics, 5 passes);
\item Define the ``broadness'' of an author as the entropy of the topic distribution of her papers.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A network approach to topic models} \\
  A. Gerlach et al.
\end{book}
Topic modeling is similar to community detection
on the bipartite graph of documents and words.
The \emph{stochastic block model} (DBM) is equivalent to 
pLSI (frequentist LDA, \ie, LDA without a prior),
but has too many parameters to be used without a prior.
The \emph{hierarchical SBM} provides such a prior 
and removes the unimodality assumption inherent in LDA.

Implementation in \texttt{graph-tool} and \texttt{TopSBM}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A high-reproducibility and high-accuracy method for automated topic classification} \\
  A. Lancichinetti et al.
\end{book}
\emph{TopicMapping} is an initialization method for LDA: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Eliminate non-discriminative words by looking at all the pairwise cosine similarities;
\item Run a clustering (community detection) algorithm,
  e.g., InfoMap, on the bipartite graph of words and documents 
  (if the vocabularies used by the topics were disjoint, the topics would be the connected components);
\item Use the clusters to estimate a PLSA model;
\item Use this model as a starting point to estimate an \emph{asymmetric LDA} model 
(LDA with a penalty for topic distribution entropy).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust PLSA performs better than LDA} \\
  A. Potapenko and K. Vorontsov (2013)
\end{book}
Variants of LDA can be estimated in the same way:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item LDA uses a Dirichlet prior and defines topics as distributions 
on words and documents as mixtures of topics;
\item PLSA replaces the Bayesian priors with maximum likelihood estimation (the priors can be seen as a penalty);
\item SWB (specific words and background) is similar, 
but models documents as a mixture of topics, a corpus-wide background word distribution, and a document-specific distribution.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The map equation} \\
  R. Rosvall et al. (2009)
\end{book}
\d{Infomap} looks for a community decomposition of a graph minimizing 
the Huffman encoding of a random walk using community$\times$node pairs.
The code length is easy to compute. 
\begin{align*}
  \alpha &:\ \text{node} \\
  p_\alpha &:\ \text{node visit probability} \\
  i &:\ \text{community} \\
  q _{ i \curvearrowright } &:\ \text{exit probability} \\
  q_{ \curvearrowright } &= \sum_i q_{i\curvearrowright} \\
  p_{i \circlearrowright } &= q_{i \curvearrowright} + \sum_{ \alpha \in i } p_\alpha \\
  L(M) &= q_{\curvearrowright} H(Q) + \sum_i p_{ i \circlearrowright } H(P_i)
\end{align*}
To minimize it, start with each node in its own community;
at each iteration, move a random node to the community decreasing the description length the most;
when nothing can be improved, collapse each community into a node, and start again.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Community detection and visualization\\ of networks with the map equation framework} \\
  L. Bohlin et al.  
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Incorporating lexical priors into topic models} \\
  J. Jagarlamudi et al.
\end{book}
To force some (rarer) topics to appear, model each topic
as a mixture of a regular topic (a distribution on words)
and a seed topic, \ie, 
a distribution on a user-specified set of words (SeededLDA).

% SeededLDA also modifies the document-topic distribution 
% by looking at seed words actually present in each document.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Paper abstract writing\\ through editing mechanism} \\
  Q. Wang et al.
\end{book}
Use seq2seq with attention to generate an abstract draft from a title,
and then a final abstract from the draft.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple method for commonsense reasoning} \\
  T.H. Trinh and Q.V. Le
\end{book}
To disambiguate pronouns, substitute them with all possible candidates
and use a language model (trained on a massive amount of unlabeled data)
to choose the most probable sentence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unsupervised neural machine translation} \\
  M. Artetxe et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Use a pre-trained, frozen, shared embedding and attention-based denoising decoders, 
trained with back-translation.
