\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Long short-term memory neural networks\\ for Chinese word segmentation} \\
  X. Chen et al.
\end{book}
1- or 2-layer LSTM, 
with character of $n$-grams as inputs, and character embeddings.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hyperbolic neural networks} \\
  O.E. Ganea et al.
\end{book}
Logistic regression 
\[ 
  P\bigl[ y = 1 \,|\, x \bigr] \propto 
  \exp\bigl( \langle a,x \rangle - b \bigr)
\]
can be generalized to hyperbolic space by noticing 
\begin{align*}
  \langle a, x \rangle - b &= 
  \operatorname{sign}\bigl( \langle a, x \rangle - b \bigr) 
  \n a d(x,H_{a,b}) \\
  &= \operatorname{sign} \bigl( \langle - p + x , a \rangle \bigr)
  \n a
  d(x, H_{a,b})
\end{align*}
where $b = \langle a, p \rangle$ and 
\begin{align*}
  H_{a,b} &= \bigl\{ x \ : \ \langle a, x \rangle = b \bigr\} \\
  H_{a,p} &= \bigl\{ x \ :\ \langle - p + x , a \rangle = 0 \bigr\} 
  = p + \{ a \} ^\perp
\end{align*}
and replacing $+$ with $\oplus$.

The linear (and affine, non-linear) transformations 
$x \mapsto Ax$
used in neural networks
can be generalized to hyperbolic space as 
$x \mapsto \exp_0 ( A \log_0 x )$.

There is no Riemannian Adam: use Riemannian SGD (projected SGD is less stable).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hyperbolic attention networks} \\
  C. Gulcehre et al.
\end{book}
% Technical

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using millions of emoji occurrences to learn any-domain representations for detecting sentiment, emotion and sarcasm} \\
  B. Felbo et al.
\end{book}
\emph{DeepMoji} is a 2-layer LSTM network with attention 
trained to forecast the presence 
of 64 common emojis 
from the text of tweets;
use it for transfer learning or provide sentiment-aware sentence embeddings.

Pretrained model available.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PlusEmo2Vec at SemEval-2018 task 1: 
  exploiting emotion knowledge\\ from emoji and hashtags} \\
  J.H. Park et al.
\end{book}
Use a biLSTM to forecast (emotion-related) emoji clusters.
Use the resulting sentence representation to forecast emotions, using a training set 
built from hashtags (from a sentiment lexicon).

This gives an \emph{emotional word embedding},
very different from the traditional ones (word2vec, glove),
which struggle to distinguish sentiment -- indeed, 
positive and negative words are used in the same context
(e.g., ``a good/bad movie'').

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\Japanese 日本語単語の難易度推定の試み} \\
  Y. Mizutani et al.
\end{book}
Measure the difficulty of a word by that of its context, initially estimated from vocabulary lists for middle, junior high and senior high school.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What is... a spectrahedron?} \\
  C. Vinzant
\end{book}
A \emph{spectrahedron} is a set of the form 
\[ \{ x \in \RR^n \ :\ A_0 + x_1 A_1 + \cdots + x_n A_n \succcurlyeq 0 \} \] 
where the $A_i$'s are symmetric matrices;
they generalize polytopes.

\emph{Semidefinite programming} 
maximizes a linear function over a spectrahedron;
it generalizes linear programming.

The \emph{elliptope} is the spectrahedron of correlation matrices of size $n$.

Spectrahedra are not closed under projections.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fr\'echet distance based approach\\ for searching online handwritten documents} \\
  R. Sriraghavendra et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{Fr\'echet distance} between two curves $P$ and $Q$ is 
\[ \min_{ \alpha, \beta} \max_{ t \in [0,1] }
\n{ P \circ \alpha(t) - Q \circ \beta(t) }_2 \]
where the reparametrizations $\alpha, \beta : [0,1] \to [0,1]$
are nondecreasing and surjective.
(Think of a man and his dog, walking along $P$ and $Q$, never going backwards -- the distance is the minimum leash length.)

The discrete Fr\'echet distance can be computed with dynamic programming.

The \emph{prefix Fr\'echet distance}
can be used to retrieve handwriting, \ie, 
time series $(t,x,y)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{R Journal (2018-2)} \\
\end{book}

The generalized autoregressive score (\textbf{GAS}) model 
models time-varying distributions $p(\cdot,\theta_t)$ whose parameters $\theta_t$
follow an AR(1) procecss 
driven by the score function 
\begin{align*}
  \nabla_t &= \left. \dfrac{ \partial \log p(y,\theta) }{ \partial \theta }
  \right|_{ y_t, \theta_t } \\
\theta_{t+1} &= \kappa + A ( \var _{t-1} \nabla _t ) ^{ -\gamma } \nabla_t + B \theta_t, 
\quad \gamma \in \{0, \tfrac12, 1\}.
\end{align*}
The maximum likelihood estimation is straightforward.

The \texttt{dynamac} package estimates and tests \d{autoregressive distributed lag models} (ARDL) 
\begin{align*}
  y \text{ or } \Delta y &\sim
  f(\text{time}) + x + \Delta x +  
  \text{lag}(y) + \text{lag}(x) + {}\\
  &\hphantom{{}\sim{}}
  \text{lag}(\Delta y) + \text{lag}(\Delta x),
\end{align*}
where the series can be stationary or (co)integrated.

In a semi-Markov model, sojourn times are arbitrarily distributed
(in a Markov model, they are exponential or geometric).
Check \texttt{SMM} (discrete-time SMMs),
\texttt{semiMarkov}, \texttt{hsmm}, \texttt{mhsmm}.

The \texttt{lmridge} package provides ridge regression diagnostics.

The \texttt{BNSP} and \texttt{bamlss} packages provide Bayesian GAMLSS with spike-and-slab priors for variable selection.

\d{Differential item functioning} looks for unfairness in item response theory (IRT) models:
eveb if they have the same ability, different groups may respond differently to the same distractor.

The \texttt{nsROC} package provides confidence bands for the ROC curve (\texttt{ROCbands})
and compares ROC curves (\texttt{compareROCdep}).
Also check 
\texttt{pROC}, \texttt{ROCR} (estimators, with smoothing, and confidence intervals for the AUC);
\texttt{plotROC}; \texttt{fbroc} (bootstrap); etc.

The $k$-means algorithm, can be generalized to mixed data types ($k$-prototypes): 
\[
d(x,y) = 
\sum_{ i \text{ continuous} } (x_i - y_i)^2 + 
\lambda \sum_{i \text{ discrete} } \mathbf 1_{ x_i = y_i }.
\]
Check 
\texttt{clustMixType}, \texttt{gower}, 
\texttt{cluster} (\texttt{daisy}, \texttt{hclust}, \texttt{agnes}),
%\texttt{cluster::daisy(method="gower")},
%\texttt{cluster::hclust},
%\texttt{cluster::agnes},
\texttt{CluMix},
%\texttt{flexclust::kcca}, 
\texttt{flexclust}
%\texttt{fpc::flexmixruns},
\texttt{fpc},
\texttt{clustMD},
\texttt{kamila}, 
\texttt{klaR}.

Instead of forward or backward variable selection,
the \emph{feasible solutions algorithm} (FSA)
starts with a random set of variables 
and replaces them, one at a time, to increase the criterion (AIC, etc.),
until this is no longer possible (repeat several times with different starting points).

There are several implementations of FastICA in 
\texttt{fICA}, \texttt{fastICA}, \texttt{ica}.

The \texttt{testforDEP} package provides more independence tests,
beyond correlation, rank correlation, Kendall's $\tau$ 
and Hoeffding's test (comparing $F_{X,Y}$ and $F_X F_Y$),
not limited to linear or monotonic dependence.

The \texttt{clikcorr} estimates bivariate correlation with censored or missing data.
The \texttt{revengc} package estimates the parameters of a Poisson or binomial distribution from censored data.

Two-dimensional density estimation is available in \texttt{kde2d}, 
\texttt{bkde2D} (binned -- significantly faster),
\texttt{pointdensityP}.

Uncertainty can be propagated using Monte Carlo simulations or Taylor expansions: 
\texttt{errors}, \texttt{propagate}, \texttt{metRology}.

The \texttt{wrapr} package generalizes \texttt{matrittr}'s pipe.

The \texttt{qqplotr} package provides confidence intervals for quantile plots 
(computed from  normal approximation, a KS test, bootstrap, etc.).\

The \texttt{utilml} and \texttt{mldr} packages deal with \emph{multilabel classification}
(each observation can have several labels);
the algorithms are built using decision trees, $k$-nearest neighbours, 
naive Bayes classifiers, support vector machines (SVM) or xgboost.

Many packages provide interpretations of complex, black-box models: 
\texttt{lime} (simple, interpretable model estimate on similar observations),
\texttt{iml} (Shapley values, \ie, contributions of a (local) linear model on binary variables),
\texttt{DALEX},
\texttt{live} (locally interpretable visual explanations, \ie, \textsc{lime} after perturbing the instance one coordinate at a time), 
\texttt{breakDown} (additive contribution of each variable,
\begin{align*}
  f(x,y) &= 
  \langle f(\cdot,\cdot) \rangle + {}\\
  &\phantom{{}={}} 
  \langle f(x,\cdot) \rangle - \langle f(\cdot,\cdot) \rangle +{}\\
  &\phantom{{}={}} 
  \langle f(x,y)    \rangle - \langle f(x,\cdot)     \rangle,
\end{align*}
ordering the variables from the least to the most important, 
as measured by 
$ \langle f(x,\cdot) \rangle - \langle f(\cdot,\cdot) \rangle$),
\texttt{pdp} (partial dependency plots, ICE),
\texttt{ALEPlot}, 
\texttt{FactorMerger}.

The \texttt{bnclassify} package learns (structure and parameters of)
\d{Bayesian network classifiers} (\d{augmented naive Bayes} models);
it is similar to \texttt{bnlearn},
but focuses on $p(c|x)$ rather than $p(c,x)$, 
and drops variables irrelevant for the classification task.

