\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning scalable deep kernels\\ with recurrent structure} \\
  M. Al-Shedivat et al. (2017)
\end{book}
To get a deep Gaussian process (GP) kernel
with a recurrent structure, 
transform the input with an LSTM
and build a kernel 
in the transformed space. 
The loss function no longer factorizes over the data: 
either pretrain the network separately and fine-tune with full batches, 
or use semi-stochastic alternating gradient descent.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unsupervised neural machine translation} \\
  M. Artetxe et al.
\end{book}
Denoising autoencoders can provide decent translations
even in the absence of any crosslingual data.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.5,xscale=2.5]
\node (A1) at (0,0) {language 2};
\node (A2) at (1,0) {language 1};
\node (A3) at (2,0) {language 2};
\node (B1) at (0,1) {language 1};
\node (B2) at (1,1) {language 2};
\node (B3) at (2,1) {language 1};
\draw[thick,->] (A1) -- (A2);
\draw[thick,->] (A2) -- (A3);
\draw[thick,->] (B1) -- (B2);
\draw[thick,->] (B2) -- (B3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Differentiable plasticity: training plastic neural networks with backpropagation} \\
  T. Miconi et al. (2018)
\end{book}
In a \d{Hebbian network}, connections between neurons that tend to fire together are reinforced.
Allow for both baseline and \emph{plastic} (Hebbian) behaviour,
\[ \text{weight}_{ij} = w_{ij} + \alpha_{ij} \text{Hebb}_{ij} \]
where the \emph{Hebbian trace}, reset at the beginning of each episode,
(when switching to a new problem)
is one of
\begin{align*}
  \text{Hebb}_{ij} (t\!+\!1) &= \text{EWMA}_\eta [ x_i(t) x_j(t) ] \\
  \text{Hebb}_{ij} (t\!+\!1) &= \text{EWMA}_\eta [ x_i(t-1) x_j(t) ] \\
  \text{Hebb}_{ij} (t\!+\!1) &= \text{Hebb}_{ij}(t) \!+\! \eta x_j(t) [ x_i(t\!-\!1) \!-\! x_j(t) \text{Hebb}_{ij}(t) ]   
\end{align*}
and $w_{ij}$, $\alpha_{ij}$ and $\eta$ are learnt by gradient descent.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Variance networks: when expectation\\ does not meet your expectations} \\
  K. Neklyudov et al.
\end{book}
Stochastic neural nets (e.g., Bayesian neural nets) replace
deterministic weights with probability distributions;
often, at test time, only the means are used.
Try a \d{variance layer}, \ie, stochastic weights with zero mean and learned variances.

\[
% Variance network
