\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Max-Mahalanobis\\ linear discriminant analysis networks} \\
  T. Pang et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The last layer of a neural net for classification is usually a (multi)logistic regression: 
try linear discriminant analysis (LDA) instead .
% after forcing the learned features to be a mixture of Gaussians (?)


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A flexible approach to automated RNN architecture generation} \\
  M. Schrimpf et al.
\end{book}
A DSL for automated RNN architecture search
with reinforcement learning suggests an alternative (BC3) to LSTM and GRU cells.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Progressive neural architecture search} \\
  C. Liu et al.
\end{book}
Bayesian optimization (SMBO), on a hierarchical cell-based search space,
to choose the structure of a CNN.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to\\ learn by gradient descent\\ by gradient descent} \\
  A. Andrychowicz et al.
\end{book}
Momentum-like learning rules of the form 
\[ \theta_{t+1} \leftarrow \theta_n + \text{RNN}\bigl( \nabla f(\theta_t) \bigr), \]
where $f$ is a random function,
can be learnt by stochastic gradient descent. 
Use coordinate-wise LSTM units, 
with a few ``global averaging cells'', 
whose output is averaged across all dimensions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural optimizer search\\ with reinforcement learning} \\
  I. Bello et al. (2017)
\end{book}
% Similar to "learning to `learn by gradient descent' by gradient descent", with better pictures.
Idem.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to optimize} \\
  K. Li and J. Malik 
\end{book}
Gradient-descent-like optimization algorithms can be learnt by reinforcement learning (guided policy search): 
the state space contains the previous 25 gradients and improvements in the objective; the action is the step size.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Guided policy search} \\
  S. Levine and V. Koltun (2013)
\end{book}
The gradient of the policy can be estimated from sample trajectories
(``likelihood ratio methods'').
Importance sampling (with some regularization)
allows off-policy learning.
A guiding distribution can be obtained as the Gaussian $i$-projection 
of the Boltzmann distribution $\rho$ on trajectories $\zeta$
with the negative reward as energy,
\[
  \argmin_{ q \text{ Gaussian} } \text{KL} ( q \| \rho ) = 
  \argmin_{ q \text{ Gaussian} }  \E_{ \zeta \sim q } [ - r(\zeta) ] - H(q)
\]
twisted with the current policy 
$ \bar r(x,a) = r(x,a) + \log \pi_\theta( a | x ) $.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning neural network policies with guided policy search under unknown dynamics} \\
  S. Levine and P. Abbeel
\end{book}
Sample trajectories from the current policy;
use them to compute a linear Gaussian approximation of the model;
compute the corresponding optimal policy (``iterative linear Gaussian regulator'', iLGR),
but constrain them to be close (for the KL divergence) to the previous ones. 


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An intriguing failing of convolutional neural networks and the CoordConv solution} \\
  R. Liu et al.
\end{book}
Add the coordinates to the inputs of a convolutional layer.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{IGLOO: slicing the feature space\\ to represent long sequences} \\
  V. Sourkov
\end{book}
To achieve long-term memory with a CNN,
compress the output of a convolutional layer with 
\emph{sparse reservoir computing}
($L$ times, pick a channel and $p=4$ points in time, concatenate, feed to a fully-connected layer).



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{K-beam minimax: efficient optimization\\ for deep adversarial learning} \\
  J. Hamm and Y.K. Noh (2018)
\end{book}
When $f(\cdot,\cdot)$ is convex in its first argument and concave in the second, 
saddlepoints coincide with minimax points: 
\[
f(u^*,v^*) = \max_v \min_u f(u,v) = \min_u \max_v f(u,v).
\]
Use alternating gradient descent,
but track $k$ candidate solutions, to account for the discontinuities 
and multiple solutions to the maximization problem.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Averaging weights leads to wider optima\\ and better generalization} \\
  P. Izmailov et al.
\end{book}
A cyclical, linearly decreasing learning rate\,\,%
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.25,xscale=.25]
\draw (0,1) -- (1,0);
\draw (1,1) -- (2,0);
\draw (2,1) -- (3,0);
\draw (3,1) -- (4,0);
\draw[densely dotted] (1,0) -- (1,1);
\draw[densely dotted] (2,0) -- (2,1);
\draw[densely dotted] (3,0) -- (3,1);
\draw[densely dotted] (4,0) -- (4,1);
%\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] at (1,0) {};
%\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] at (2,0) {};
%\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] at (3,0) {};
%\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] at (4,0) {};
\end{tikzpicture}
yields an ensemble of solutions:
one can average their forecasts or,
since they tend to be dispersed on the edge of a low-cost region, their weights --
wider optima generalize better (cf. entropy-SGD).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cyclical learning rates\\ for training neural networks} \\
  L.N. Smith (2015)
\end{book}
The \emph{learning rate (LR) range test}
estimates minimum and maximum values for the learning rate by computing the accuracy of the model
for various learning rates (e.g., from 0 to 5 -- you may go beyond 1)
after just a few epochs.

The \emph{cyclical learning rate} schedule linearly increases and decreases
the learning rate between those bounds, with 2 to 10 epochs per half-period:
the learning rate will be close to the optimal value most of the time,
but higher values will help escape saddle points
(as in simulated annealing).

The idea is very similar to stochastic gradient descent with restarts,
and related to adaptive learning rate methods
(AdaDelta, AdaGrad, RMSProp, Adam, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Super-convergence: very fast training\\ of neural networks using large learning rates} \\
  L.N. Smith and N. Taupin
\end{book}
The cyclical learning rate method can speed up training by up to an order of magnitude.

The optimal learning rate can be estimated as
\[ \epsilon^* = \epsilon \dfrac{ \theta_{i+1} - \theta_i }{ 2 \theta_{i+1} - \theta_i - \theta_{i+2} }. \]




\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Constant step size stochastic gradient descent for probabilistic modeling} \\
  D. Babichev and F. Bach
\end{book}
Stochastic gradient descent with constant step size does not converge but,
for exponential families, averaging the moment parameters (e.g., the probability $p \in [0,1]$
for a Bernoulli distribution, \ie, a logistic regression),
rather than the natural parameters (the log-odds ratio)
restores convergence.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dropout is a special case of the stochastic delta rule: faster and more accurate deep learning} \\
  N. Frazier-Logue and S.J. Hanson
\end{book}
The \emph{stochastic delta rule} (SDR) represents the weights of 
a neural net as Gaussian random variables
$w_{ij} \sim N(\mu_{ij}, \sigma_{ij}^2 )$,
sampled on each forward iteration, 
whose parameters are updated.
Dropout uses (fixed-parameter) Bernoulli distributions.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring the intrinsic dimension\\ of objective landscapes} \\
  C. Li et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Train your model on a random subspace of the space of weights
(the model remains the same, but the weights are constrained to a linear subspace)
and progressively increase the dimension:
that at which the performance reaches 90\% of the full network
is the \emph{intrinsic dimension}.
It may depend on the network structure:
for images, CNNs give a lower dimension.

Those random projections can also be used to compress networks.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural networks should be wide enough to learn disconnected decision regions} \\
  Q. Nguyen et al. (2018)
\end{book}
If no hidden layer (in a network with ReLU activations)
is wider than the input,
the decision regions are connected.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The lottery ticket hypothesis:\\ finding small, trainable networks} \\
  K. Frankle and M. Carbin
\end{book}
A large network is an ensemble of smaller overlapping networks:
some of those subnetworks have won the ``initialization lottery'' and, once pruned,
can be trained --
they work better than the initial, large network.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Harmonic analysis of neural networks} \\
  E.J. Cand\`es (1996)
\end{book}
Continuous and discrete wavelets can be seen as (infinite and finite) 1-layer neural networks --
they model functions as
\begin{align*}
  f &= \int \langle f, \psi_\gamma \rangle \psi_\gamma \, \mu(d\gamma) \\
  \text{or } f &= \sum \alpha_\gamma \psi_\gamma,
  \text{ where } \psi_\gamma (x) = \alpha^{-1/2} \psi \left(
  \dfrac{ \langle u, x \rangle - b }a \right)\!.
\end{align*}



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Manifold regularization with GANs\\ for semisupervised learning} \\
  B. Lecouat et al. (2018)
\end{book}
GANs can be leveraged for semi-supervised learning: 
have the discriminator determine 
the class of the input 
or whether it was generated.
(Self-training is also sometimes used: 
label the unlabeled data with a classifier trained on the labeled data,
then re-train on the expanded dataset.)

\d{Manifold regularization} ensures the classifier does not change as we move away from the data (orthogonally),
with a penalty of the form
\begin{align*}
& \int \n{ \nabla_{\!\!\mathscr M} f } dp \approx \dfrac1n \sum_i \nabla_{\!z} f( g( z^{(i)} ) ) \\
& z^{(i)}: \text{ latent generator variables} \\
& g :\text{ generator} \\
& f:\text{discriminator, classifier} \\
& \mathscr M: \text{ data namifold} \\
& p: \text{distribution of the data on $\mathscr M$.}
\end{align*}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The relativistic discriminator: a key element missing from standard GAN} \\
  A. Jolicoeur-Martineau 
\end{book}
The generator and discriminator of a GAN minimize their loss functions 
\begin{align*}
 \text{loss}_D &= \E_{ x \sim \text{Data} } \bigl[ f_1( D(x) ) \bigr] + 
 \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } \bigl[ f_2( D G z ) \bigr] \\
 \text{loss}_G &= \E_{ x \sim \text{Data} } \bigl[ g_1( D(x) ) \bigr] + 
 \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } \bigl[ g_2( D G z ) \bigr], \\
\end{align*}
often with $g_1 = f_1$, $g_2 = - f_2$, 
\ie, the loss is the same, up to the sign (saturating GAN)
or with 
$g_1 = f_2$, $g_2 = f_1$, 
\ie, the loss is the same, but the real and simulated data have been swapped
(non-saturating GAN).

The ``relativistic GAN'' uses 
\begin{align*}
\text{loss}_D &= - \E_{\substack{
  x \sim \text{Data} \\
  z \sim \text{Noise}
  } } [
  \log \sigma( Dx - DGz)  
  ] \\
\text{loss}_D &= - \E_{\substack{
  x \sim \text{Data} \\
  z \sim \text{Noise}
  } } [
  \log \sigma( DGz - Dx)  
  ]   
\end{align*}
to ensure that the probability of real data decreases as that of the fake data increases.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{$\chi^2$ generative adversarial network} \\
  C. Tao et al. (2018)
\end{book}
GANs can be trained with: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $f$-divergences
\[ P(p \| q ) = \int f\left( \dfrac{ p(x) }{ q(x) } \right) p(x) dx \]
where $p$ is the data and $q$ the generator -- 
but this is numerically unstable;
\item Integral probability metrics (IPM), such as Wasserstein 1 (earth mover's distance)
\[
\sup_{ \n D \leq 1 }
\E_{ X \sim \text{Data} } D(X) - 
\E_{ X \sim q } D(X)
\]
\item RKHS distances, 
\[ \text{MMD}(p,q) = \n{ 
  \E_{ X \sim p } \kappa(\cdot,X) - 
  \E_{ X \sim q } \kappa(\cdot,X) 
}_H.
\]
\end{compactitem}
The $\chi^2$ GAN is a special case of all three.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{First order generative adversarial networks} \\
    C. Seward et al. 
\end{book}
An \emph{adversarial divergence}
between probability distributions $p$, $q$ on $X$
is a function of the form 
\[ \tau( p \| q ) = \sup_{ g \in \mathscr G } E_{p\otimes q} [ g ] \]
for dome $\mathscr G \subset \mathscr C^0( X \times X )$.

A \emph{critic-based adversarial divergence}
is of the form 
\[ \tau( p \| q ) = \sup_{ f \in \mathscr F } E_{p\otimes q} [ m_f - r_f ] \]
where $\mathscr F \subset \mathscr C^0(X)$ (e.g., 1-Lipschitz), 
$m_f(x,y) = m_1(f(x)) - m_2(f(x))$, 
$m_1, m_2 : \RR \to \RR$
and $r : \mathscr F \to \mathscr C^0(X \times X)$.

The Wasserstein divergence between Dirac masses 
\[ \tau( \delta_a \| \delta_b ) = \sup_{ f \in \mathscr F } f(a) - f(b) = \va{a-b} \]
can be written 
\[ 4\tau( \delta_a \| \delta_b ) = \sup_{ f \in \mathscr F } 
 f(a) - f(b) - \va{a-b} \left(
 \dfrac{ 
   f(a) - f(b)
 }{
   a - b 
 }
 \right)^2;
\]
this motivates the \emph{penalized Wasserstein divergence},
\[ 
\tau( p \| q ) = 
\sup_{ f \in \mathscr F } 
\E_{ x\sim p} \!\! f(x) - \E_{y\sim q} \!\!f(y) 
- \lambda \E_{\substack{ x \sim p \\ y \sim q }} \!\!
\dfrac{
  \bigl( f(x) - f(y) \bigr)^2
}{
  \n{x-y}
}
\]
(one can also add another penalty, involving $\nabla f$).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evolutionary generative adversarial networks} \\
  C. Wang et al.
\end{book}
Evolve a population of generators to adapt to the environment (discriminator),
mutating them by running a few gradient descent steps for the following loss functions
\begin{align*}
  \text{loss}(G) &= \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } \log( 1 - D G z ) \\
  \text{loss}(G) &= \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } - \log( D G z ) \\
  \text{loss}(G) &= \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } ( D G z - 1 )^2
\end{align*}
where the first (second) minimizes (maximizes) the log-probability of the discriminator being correct (mistaken).

To promote diversity and avoid mode collapse,
note that the discriminator labels collapsed points as fake,
with obvious counter-measure, \ie, big gradients,
and use those gradients as a diversity measure.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Large scale GAN training for high-fidelity natural image synthesis} \\
  A. Brock et al. 
\end{book}
To train large-scale GANs: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Increase the batch size;
\item Train with Gaussian noise but sample using a truncated Gaussian;
\item Use orthogonal regularization,
$\n{ W' W - I }_F^2$, without the diagonal 
$\n{ W' W \odot (\mathbf 1 - I) }_F^2$.
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Synthesizing programs for images\\ using reinforced adversarial learning} \\
  Y. Ganin et al.
\end{book}
GAN to generate programs (sequences of graphical primitives)
to generate images, instead of raw pixels
(deep reinforced adversarial learning).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Globally and locally consistent\\ image completion} \\
  S. Iizuka et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Use a GAN for image completion, 
with both a global and a local discriminator.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to see in the dark} \\
  C. Chen et al.
\end{book}
Instead of using separately learned algorithms for denoising, deblurring, etc.,
learn an end-to-end image processing pipeline.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepMasterPrints: generating MasterPrints for dictionary attacks\\ via latent variable evolution} \\
  P. Bontrafer et al.
\end{book}
Use a GAN to generate fingerprints, 
then explore the latent space with CMA-ES
to find a master print.

Small fingerprint sensors (on mobile phones),
which only image part of the finger, are very vulnerable to that attack.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning deep generative models of graphs} \\
  Y. Li et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To sequentially generate random graphs (molecules, parse trees, graphical model structures),
use a GRU graphnet to decide, at each step, where to add an edge or a node.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Meta-learning for semi-supervised\\ few-shot classification} \\
  M. Ren et al. (2018)
\end{book}
A \d{prototypical network}, for a classification problem,
learns an embedding,
defines prototypes as the averages of the embeddings of the observations in each class,
and uses the squared distances to the prototypes in a softmax, 
$p \propto \exp (-d^2)$.

For semi-supervised learning, compute the prototypes from the labeled data, estimate the class of the unlabeled items
(soft-$k$-means, with a ``distraction cluster'' for potential unknown labels,
or soft-masking of far-away observation)
and refine the prototypes.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to generate classifiers} \\
  N. Guttenberg and R. Kanai
\end{book}
Meta-learning (\ie, learning a mapping from datasets of predictors and labels to classifiers)
with attention outperforms SVM, random forests, $k$-NN, or xgboost.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fastfood -- approximating kernel expansions\\ in loglinear time} \\
  Q. Le et al.
\end{book}
The decision function, in an SVM, is of the form 
\[ f(x) = \sum_1^N \alpha_i k(x_i,x), \]
where $N$ is the number of observations and the nonzero $\alpha_i$ 
correspond to the support vectors.
We usually prefer to compute the kernel directly, 
without explicitly computing the embedding, 
\( k(x,y) = \langle \phi(x), \phi(y) \rangle, \)
but, for large datasets, a random embedding (\emph{random kitchen sink})
may be preferable: 
\begin{align*}
  Z_{ij}  &\sim N(0,\sigma^{-2}) \\
  \phi_j(x) &= \dfrac1{\sqrt n} \exp [ i ( Z x ) _j ]
\end{align*}
is an approximation of a RBF (radial basis function) kernel.
Instead of a fully Gaussian kernel, consider 
\[ Z = \dfrac1{\sigma \sqrt d} S H G \Pi H B \]
where $H=\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix} ^ {\!\! \tens d } $,
$B$ is a random diagonal matrix with entries $\pm1$, 
$G$ is a random diagonal Gaussian, 
and $S$ is diagonal.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using matrices to model symbolic relationships} \\
  I. Sutskever and G. Hinton
\end{book}
A \d{linear relational embedding} (LRE)
learns vector and matrix representations of objects and relations 
such that $x \mathscr R y$ corresponds to a matrix product 
$\mathscr R x = y$; 
a \emph{matrix relational embedding} uses matrices for both objects and relation:
the relation matrices are smaller, and one can also consider higher-order relations
(relations between relations).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Monotonic calibrated interpolated\\ look-up tables} \\
  M. Gupta et al. (2016)
\end{book}
\d{Lattice regression} approximates a function by interpolating it on a grid -- 
the values on the vertices are chosen to minimize the loss, 
\emph{after interpolation},
at the points already observed.
Multilinear interpolation uses $2^D$ points, 
e.g., 
\[ 
f(x,y) \!=\!
(1-x)(1-y) \theta_{00} + 
x(1-y) \theta_{10} + 
(1-x)y \theta_{01} + 
xy \theta_{11}
\]
(for $D=2$),
where the weights 
$
x^\delta(1-x)^{1-\delta}
y^\epsilon(1-y)^{1-\epsilon}
$, $\delta,\epsilon \in \{0,1\}$,
are known, and the model is linear in its parameters $\theta$.
Monotonicity is easy to check.
One could use any other way of writing $(x,y)$ as a barycenter of 
$(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(1,1)$.
\d{Simplex interpolation}
uses only $D+1$ vertices, 
corresponding to a partition of the hypercube along the hyperplanes 
$x_k = x_\ell$.
The simplex containing a point can be obtained by sorting its coordinates
(in $O(D \log D)$ time).
The model is not rotationally invariant: 
all the simplices contain $(0\cdots0)$ and $(1\cdots1)$;
for a better fit, ensure all the constraints go in the same direction, e.g., everything increasing.

Add penalties such as 
\[ 
  \sum \left( \dfrac{ \partial f }{ \partial x_i } \right)^2,
  \quad
  \sum \left( \dfrac{ \partial^2 f }{ \partial x_i^2 } \right)^2,
  \quad\text{or}\quad
  \sum \left( \dfrac{ \partial^2 f }{ \partial x_i \partial x_j} \right)^2  
\]
to make the function flatter or more linear.

Add coordinatewise, piecewise (but still monotonic) transformations, 
with $k$ knots, at equally spaced quantiles.

To deal with missing values, replace the lattice 
$\{0,1\}^D$ with $\{0,1,\textsc{na}\}^D$
and interpolate on 
$\bigl( [0,1] \sqcup \{\textsc{na}\} \bigr) ^ D$.

There are many constraints and penalties: 
try sampling from them at each iteration
to speed up the computations. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lattice regression} \\
  E.K. Garcia and M.R. Gupta
\end{book}
% Initial paper on lattice regression

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast and flexible monotonic functions\\ with ensembles of lattices} \\
  K. Canini et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Build an ensemble of (monotonic) lattice regression models, 
each using a small number of variables: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Train lattices on all pairs of features and compute their ``torsion'',
  $[ (x_{11} - x_{10} ) - (x_{01} - x_{00}) ]^2$, 
  a measure of nonlinear interaction;
\item Assign the features to the different models, 
with each feature used at least once, 
and the feature count otherwise proportional to the median torsion, 
and maximize the weighted torsion of the ensemble (where the weight decreases as a pair is repeated).
\end{compactitem}



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep gradient compression:\\ reducing the communication bandwidth\\ for distributed training} \\
  Y. Lin et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Most of the gradient exchanges in distributed 
stochastic gradient descent are not needed: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Only send large gradients;
\item Accumulate them locally, and clip them, before sending;
\item Do not use momentum for old (unsent) gradients -- it would be stale;
\item During training, use little sparsification and a low training rate.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{meProp: sparsified backpropagation\\ for accelerated deep learning\\ with reduced overfitting} \\
  X. Sun et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Sparsified backpropagation, \ie, only backpropagating the $k$ largest gradients (1\% to 4\%) does not slow down training.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep image prior} \\
  D. Ulyanov et al.
\end{book}
Use untrained networks (reservoir computing, random projections).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Secure ML: a system for scalable privacy-preserving machine learning} \\
  P. Mohassel and Y. Zhang
\end{book}
Machine learning models can be trained even if the data is (and should remain) on separate servers
(e.g., financial and medical data).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Trainable calibration measures for neural networks from kernel mean embeddings} \\
  A. Kumar et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Neural networks for classification problems are not calibrated: 
the scores cannot be interpreted as probabilities -- 
we would like 
$P[\text{correct}] = \max_k p_k(x)$.
The calibration error could be added to the loss function, 
but it is discontinuous.
Instead, use 
\[ 
\text{MMCE} = \sum_{ij} \dfrac{
  (c_i - s_i)(c_j - s_j) \kappa(s_i,s_j)
}{ N^2 }
\]
where $c_i = \mathbf 1_{\text{correct}} = \mathbf 1 _{ y_i = \argmax_k p_k(x_i) }$
and $s_i = \max_k p_k(x_i)$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine learning for trading} \\
  G. Ritter (2017)
\end{book}
Reinforcement learning on simulated data 
can help devise trading strategies with arbitrary transaction cost models 
(e.g., given by an algorithmic blackbox).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms for inverse reinforcement learning} \\
  A.Y. Ng and S. Russell 
\end{book}
The reward function of a known, finite-state MDP can
be recovered from its (deterministic) optimal policy,
via a linear program;
to avoid degenerate solutions, e.g., $R \equiv 0$,
maximize the difference between the optimal policy and the others, 
\[ \sum_{ s \in S } Q\bigl( s, \pi(s) \bigr) - 
\max_{a \neq \pi(s) } Q(s,a). \]
For large state spaces, approximate the reward function as a linear combination of basis functions,
$R(s) = \sum \alpha_i \phi_i(s)$, 
$\phi_i$ fixed.
This approach is still applicable if the optimal policy is only known through sample trajectories
(but requires iteratively solving linear programs, to progressively improve the fit).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Solving the Rubik's cube\\ without human knowledge} \\
  S. McAleer et al.
\end{book}
Only one state, the final state, has a reward: 
start there and estimate the value and optimal policy for states farther and farther away from it.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Independent interpretable lasso: \\
  a new regularizer for sparse regression\\ with uncorrelated variables} \\
  M. Takada et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To avoid selecting correlated variables, ease interpretation,
and recover correct signs, 
use a penalty of the form 
\( \lambda \n{ \beta }_1 + \mu \va{ \beta }' R \va{ \beta }, \)
where $R_{ij} \geq 0$ measures the similarity between $i$ and $j$.
\[
\raisebox{-2em}{%
<<IILasso,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=1,fig.height=1,out.width='.17\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
lambda <- 1
mu <- .5
R <- matrix(c(0,1,1,0),2,2)
f <- function(x,y) {
  beta <- c(x,y)
  lambda * sum(abs(beta)) + mu * t(abs(beta)) %*% R %*% abs(beta)
}
N <- 101
x <- y <- seq(-2,2,length=N)
z <- outer( x, y, Vectorize(f) )
op <- par( mar = 0 * c(1,1,1,1) )
contour(z, drawlabels=FALSE, axes = FALSE)
par(op)
@
}
\qquad
\lambda=1, \ \mu=\tfrac12, \ R=\begin{pmatrix}0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0\end{pmatrix}
\]


% Can be estimated with coordinate descent


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{High-dimensional regression in practice:\\ an empirical study of finite-sample prediction, variable selection and ranking} \\
  F. Wang et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Prefer the lasso.
Other penalized regression, variable selection and variable ranking algorithms
include the elastic net, ridge regression, SACD (an $\ell^1$ penalty for small values, 
  \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.3,yscale=.2,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt,outer sep = 0pt,baseline=-1pt]
    \draw (-1.1,0) -- (1.1,0);
    \draw (0,-.5) -- (0,1.1);
    \draw[ultra thick] (-1,1) -- (-.5,1) -- (0,0) -- (.5,1) -- (1,1);
  \end{tikzpicture}), 
the Dantzig selector
\[ \argmin_{ \beta : \n{ X' (Y-X\beta) }_\infty \leq \lambda } \n{\beta}_1,
\]
stability selection (run a variable selection on resampled datasets and keep the variables selected at least $\tau$\% of the time).

R implementations in 
\texttt{glmnet}, 
\texttt{ncvreg} (SACD), 
\texttt{flare} (Dantzig),
\texttt{c060} (stability selection).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identifying groups\\ of strongly correlated variables\\ through smoothed ordered weighted $\ell_1$ norms} \\
  R. Sankaran et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The ordered weighted $L^1$ norm (OWL) is 
\[ \Omega(w) = \sum c_i \va{ w }_{(i)} \]
where $\va{ w }_{(i)}$ is the $i$th largest absolute value of the coordinates of $w$
and $c_i$ is a fixed non-increasing sequence 
(e.g., an arithmetic sequence: \textsc{oscar}).

As a penalty, it has a sparsifying effect, 
selecting groups of variables with the same coefficients
\[
<<OWLBalls,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=1,fig.height=1,out.width='.17\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
# OWL balls
# Ordered, weighted L^1 norm
f <- function(A,B) {
  theta <- pi/2
  R <- matrix( c(cos(theta), sin(theta), -sin(theta), cos(theta)), 2, 2 )
  for( i in 1:4 ) {
    A <- R %*% A
    B <- R %*% B
    segments( A[1], A[2], B[1], B[2], lwd = 2 )
  }
}
op <- par( mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
plot( NA, xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1), axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
for( k in c(1, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6) ) { 
  A <- c(1,0)
  B <- k * c(1,1) / 2
  C <- c(0,1)
  f(A,B)
  f(B,C)
}
par(op)
@
\]
It is the Lov\'asz extension of the cardinality-based modular function 
$P(A) = f(\va A)$, where $f(i)=c_1+\cdots+c_i$.

The $\ell^2$ relaxation is similar, but does not force the coefficients to be exactly equal.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Finite-time analysis\\ of the multiarmed bandit problem} \\
  P. Auer et al. (2002)
\end{book}
For the multiarm bandit problem, 
UCB$_1$, which plays the machine $j$ maximizing 
\[ x_j + \sqrt{ \dfrac{ 2 \log n }{ n_j } }, \]
UCB$_2$ (similar, with a division in epochs),
and $\epsilon$-greedy,
with $\epsilon \propto 1/n$, 
achieve logarithmic regret.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-fidelity blackbox optimization\\ with hierarchical partitions} \\
  R. Sen et al. (2018)
\end{book}
UCB-style multi-arm bandit algorithms can be applied to black-box optimization
of expensive functions for which cheap, 
coarse approximations (early stopping) are available, 
by hierarchically partitioning the domain.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Contextual memory trees} \\
  W. Sun et al. 
\end{book}
Store key-value pairs in the leaves of a (balanced, binary) tree,
with a classifier at each node; 
at query time, update the classifiers to improve retrieval.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The case for learned index structures} \\
  T. Kraska et al.
\end{book}
Many data structures can be replaced by learned models,
leveraging GPUs/TPUs.

B-trees (range indices, in databases)
map a key to a position in a sorted array, \ie,
estimate a cummulative distribution function (CDF);
they can be replaced with a neural net.
The maximum error measured on the whole dataset defined the size of the window to examine
(the B-tree is actually similar: it retrieves a whole ``page'',
instead of the single value it actually needs).
It is possible to replace only part of the data structure with a neural net,
or arrange several neural nets in a tree.

Hash functions can also be replaced by learning the CDF of the key,
and dilating it to get a hash map.

A good hash function for a Bloom filter
would have many key/key and non-key/non-key collisions,
but few key/non-key collisions.
Bloom filters can be replaced by a key/non-key classifier,
with a smaller Bloom filter for the set of false negatives, to keep the false negative rate to zero.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Clustering by passing messages\\ between data points} \\
  B.J. Frey and D. Dueck 
\end{book}
Use exemplars: 
\begin{align*}
  & s_{ij} : \text{ similarity} \\
  & r_{ik} \leftarrow s_{ik} - \max_{k' \neq k} a_{ik'} + s_{ik'} \\
  & a_{ik} \leftarrow \min
  \Bigl\{ 
    0,\ 
    r_{kk} + \sum _{ i' \neq i,k} (r_{ik})^{\mathstrut}_+ 
  \Bigr\} \\
  & a_{kk} \leftarrow \sum_{ j' \neq k} ( r_{ik} )^{\mathstrut}_+ \\
  & \text{exemplar}(i) = \argmax_k a_{ik} + r_{ik}
\end{align*}
with damping (\ie, updates of the form 
$x \leftarrow (1-\lambda) x + \lambda x_{\text{new}}$)
and only exchanging messages between nearby or similar points.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distributional regression forests\\ for probabilistic precipitation forecasting\\ in complex terrain} \\
  L. Schlosser et al.
\end{book}
Linear regression models the mean of the response variable as a function of the predictors.

Non-homogeneous Gaussian regression (NGR) models both mean and variance.

GAMLSS uses a GAM for location, scale and shape
(\texttt{gamlss}, \texttt{gamboostLSS}).

To account for interactions and non-smooth dependencies,
one can use a random forest of regression trees
with GAMLSS leaves (\texttt{disttree}).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical detection\\ of systematic election irregularities} \\
  P. Klimek et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Ballot stuffing (only one type of election irregularity)
can be detected by looking at:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The winner votes vs turnout density plot,
  which should not show an abnormal high-turnout, high rate cluster;
\item The cumulative number of votes as a function of turnout, which should end with a plateau.
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A fast and objective multidimensional\\ kernel density estimation method: fastKDE} \\
  T.A. O'Brien et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The choice of kernel and bandwidth, for kernel density estimation (KDE), 
is often arbitrary.
Use $\kappa$, where 
\begin{align*}
  \check \kappa(t) &= \dfrac{ n }{ 2(n-1) } \left[
    1 + \sqrt{
      1 - \dfrac{ 4(n-1) }{ n^2 \va{ \mathscr C(t) }^2 }
    } I(t) 
  \right] \\
  \mathscr C(t) &= \dfrac1n \sum e^{ i x_j \cdot t } 
  \text{ empirical characteristic function} \\
  \check \kappa &= \mathscr F^{-1} \kappa \text{ inverse Fourier transform}
\end{align*}
and $I$ filters out frequencies $t$ 
with $\va{ \mathscr C(t) } \leq 2 \sqrt{n-1} / n = \mathscr C_{\text{min}}$
and possibly a few more 
(e.g., outside $[-t^*,t^*]$,
where for half the $t$ in $[-t^*,t^*]$,
$\mathscr C(t) \geq \mathscr C_{\text{min}}$.
The optimal kernel can be computed with the nuFFT (non-uniform FFT).

This can be generalized to multivariate KDE.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Controllable conformal maps\\ for shape deformation and interpolation} \\
  O. Weber and C. Gotsman (2010)
\end{book}
To deform a (simply-connected) shape $\Omega$,
use a conformal transformation $f$.
It suffices to specify the \emph{angular factor}
$\theta =  ( \log f' )|_{\partial \Omega}^{\mathstrut}$.
the conformal transformation can be recovered as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Extend $\theta$ harmonically on $\Omega$;
\item Compute its harmonic conjugate $\phi$ (Hilbert transform);
\item Consider the holomorphic function $g = \phi + i \theta$;
\item Solve $f' = \exp g$ -- since $f'$ does not vanish, $f$ is conformal.
\end{compactitem}

Generalized Cauchy coordinates use the Cauchy formula,
a polynomial discretization of $\partial \Omega$,
a quadratic approximation of $f$ on each edge,
with two values at each corner.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An invitation to noncommutative algebra} \\
  C. Walton
\end{book}
Noncommutative algebra studies \emph{representations} 
of noncommutative algebras, 
e.g., the quaternions, or the \emph{Weyl albebra}
\[ \dfrac{ k\langle x,y \rangle }{ (yx - xy - 1) } \]
(which models differential operators, with $y = d/dx$, 
and does not have finite-dimensional representations -- 
to see it, take the trace),
\emph{deformations} of commutative structures, e.g., 
\[
  \CC_q[x,y] = \dfrac{ k\langle x, y \rangle }{ yx - qxy }, \qquad q \in \CC^\times,
\]
and deformations of group actions 
(into Hopf algebra actions), e.g., 
\[ H_q = \dfrac{
  \CC \langle g, g^{-1}, h \rangle
}{
  ( g g^{-1} - 1, g^{-1} g - 1, g h - q^2 h g )
} \]
(as $q \to 1$, $g, g^{-1} \to 1$, $h \to \partial_y$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An empirical evaluation of\\ generic convolutional and recurrent networks\\ for sequence modeling} \\
  S. Bai et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Use \emph{temporal convolutional networks} (TCN)
to model sequences: 
no pooling, causal dilated convolutions and residual connections.

% Temporal convolution network (TCN)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.4,yscale=.8,outer sep=0pt, inner sep=2pt,baseline={(0,.75)}]
\begin{scope}
 \clip(-.3,-.3) rectangle (8.3,2.3);
 \foreach \i in {-1,...,18} {
  \draw[gray] (\i,2) -- (\i,1);
  \draw[gray] (\i,2) -- (\i-3,1);
  \draw[gray] (\i,2) -- (\i-6,1);
  \draw[gray] (\i,1) -- (\i,0);
  \draw[gray] (\i,1) -- (\i-1,0);
  \draw[gray] (\i,1) -- (\i-2,0);
  \draw[gray] (\i,0) to [bend right=45] (\i,2);
 }
\end{scope}
\draw[very thick] (8,0) to [bend right=45] (8,2);
\foreach \i in {0,...,8} { 
  \node[circle,draw,gray,fill=white] at (\i,0) {};
  \node[circle,draw,gray,fill=white] at (\i,1) {};
  \node[circle,draw,gray,fill=white] at (\i,2) {};
}
\node[circle,draw,very thick] (C) at (8,2) {};
\foreach \i in {2,5,8} {
  \node[circle,draw,very thick] (B-\i) at (\i,1) {};
  \draw[very thick] (B-\i) -- (C);
  \foreach \j in {-2,-1,0} {
    \node[circle,draw,very thick] (A-\i-\j) at (\i+\j,0) {};
    \draw[very thick] (A-\i-\j) -- (B-\i);
  }
}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting malicious PowerShell commands using deep neural networks} \\
  D. Hendler et al.
\end{book}
To detect malicious commands in the logs,
use an ensemble of:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item 3-grams tf-idf logistic regression;
\item bag-of-words tf logistic regression;
\item (CNN,MaxPool)$^2$CNN$^4$MaxPool(FC,Dropout)$^4$FC;
\item CNN, MaxPool, (FC, Dropout)$^2$,
\end{compactitem}
on a dataset of 100,000 commands, 10\% malicious.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BERT: pre-training of deep bidirectional transformers for language understanding} \\
  J. Devlin et al.
\end{book}
Most language models only rely on the left context, \ie,
model $P[ w_{n+1} | w_{1:n} ]$;
BERT uses \emph{transformers} (\ie, attention-based networks)
conditioned on both the left and right context,
$P[w_n | w_{1:n-1}, w_{n+1:N} ]$.
ELMo is similar, but uses separate left-to-right and right-to-left LSTMs,
which are only concatenated at the end.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Are distributional representations ready\\ for the real world?
    Evaluating word vectors\\ for grounded perceptual meaning} \\
  L. Lucy and J. Gauthier
\end{book}
Word embeddings cannot recover \emph{semantic norms},
\ie, entailments such as
aligator $\rightarrow$ is green, animal, reptile, eats people, has teeth, etc.
% Data from the McRae lab


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Morph-fitting: fine-tuning word vector spaces with simple language-specific rules} \\
  I. Vuli\'c et al.
\end{book}
To modify a trained word embedding to account for known synonym and antonym pairs
(e.g., different word endings in morphologically rich languages), use the following loss function
\begin{align*}
  &\sum_{ x_\ell \sim x_r }
    \text{ReLU} ( \delta + x_\ell t_\ell - x_\ell x_r ) 
  + \text{ReLU} ( \delta + x_r    t_r   - x_\ell x_r ) \\
  & \rlap{\!\!+}\sum_{ x_\ell \not\sim x_r }
    \text{ReLU} ( \delta + x_\ell t_r - x_\ell x_\ell ) 
    + \text{ReLU} ( \delta + x_\ell t_r - x_r    x_r   ) \\
  & \rlap{\!\!+}\ \ \lambda \sum_x \n{ x^{\text{init}} - x }_2^2
\end{align*}
where $t_i$ is the word closest to $x_i$ (in the current minibatch).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BLEU: a method for automatic evaluation\\ of machine translation} \\
  K. Papineni et al.
\end{book}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TextRank: bringing order into texts} \\
  R. Mihalcea and P. Tarau
\end{book}
To extract keywords from a text, apply undirected PageRank to the cooccurrence graph (vary the window size)
between words or sequences of words (try different POS, e.g., nouns and adjectives).

For sentence extraction (summarization), use sentences as nodes
and similarity (number of common words, divided by the log of the lengths) as weights.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multimodal word distributions} \\
  B. Athiwaratkun and A.G. Wilson
\end{book}
Extend the skipgram model by replacing the point representation of words with Gaussian mixtures,
allowing for both polysemy and entailment
(e.g., $\text{jazz} \subset \text{music}$).
% TensorFlow code available. Can be trained in "just" days.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Enriching word vectors\\ with subword information} \\
  P. Bojanowski et al.
\end{book}
\d{FastText} fits a skipgram model to $n$-grams and represents words as the sum of their $n$-grams --
it is aware of word morphology and can deal with unknown words.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple but tough-to-beat baseline\\ for sentence embeddings} \\
  S. Arora et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Turning word embeddings into sentence embeddings 
by naively averaging the embeddings of the words in the sentence 
does not work that well; 
try the following simple modification: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use a weighted average, $w = \dfrac{ a }{ a + P(\text{word}) }$;
\item  Remove the first principal component (it contains stopwords and other non-informative words).
\end{compactitem}

In the word2vec (\textsc{cbow}) model
\[
  P[w | w_1 \cdots w_5 ] \propto
  \exp\biggl(
  v_w \cdot \dfrac15  \sum_i v_{w_{i}}
  \biggr)
\]
frequent words are under-sampled when computing the gradient, 
to speed up training and give more regular embeddings.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Supervised learning\\ of universal sentence representations\\ from natural language inference data} \\
  A. Conneau et al. (2018)
\end{book}
\emph{InferSent} uses a supervised task, 
\emph{natural language inference}
(recognizing if a sentence entails, contradicts or is unrelated to another)
with a bidirectional LSTM RNN with max pooling 
to compute sentence embeddings.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Skip-thought vectors} \\
  R. Kiros et al.
\end{book}
To compute sentence embeddings, forecast a sentence from nearby sentences
(as in word2vec's \textsc{cbow})
using a GRU RNN trained on the BookCorpus dataset (unpublished authors).

For out-of-vocabulary words,
learn a mapping from the word2vec space (using unpenalized least squares).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Vader: a parsimonious rule-based model\\ for sentiment analysis of social media text} \\
  C.J. Hutto and E. Gilbert (2014)
\end{book}
Vader is an MIT-licensed sentiment lexicon,
targeted at short, Twitter-like sentences,
augmented with a few simple rules for all-caps, punctuation (exclamation marks),
degree modifiers (extremely, marginally, etc.),
contrastive conjuctions (but) and negations, 
available in NLTK.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep contextualized word representations} \\
  C. Clark et al. (2018)
\end{book}

ELMo uses, as sentence embedding, all the internal layers (not just the first and the last ones)
of a deep bidirectional LSTM network trained as a language model
(\ie, to predict the next/previous/missing word).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Universal sentence encoder} \\
	C. Cer et al. (2018)
\end{book}

Train a model, e.g., a \d{transformer} (attention-based network, instead of an LSTM)
or a \d{deep averaging network} (DAN:
the embeddings of the inputs words and bigrams are averaged and fed to a deep neural network --
the word embeddings are trained so that their averages be useful as sentence embeddings)
on several tasks (skip-thought, conversation input-response, classification).

Pretrained models are available on TFHub.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Semantic specialization of distributional\\ word vector spaces using monolingua\\l and cross-lingual constraints} \\
N. Mrk\v si\'c et al. (2017)
\end{book}

Attract-Repel modifies a word embedding to account for synonyms and antonyms
(some come from cross-lingual sources, which can also provide multilingual embeddings)
using a margin loss on positive (actual synonyms or antonyms) or negative pairs.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Morph-fitting: fine-tuning word vector spaces with simple language-specific rules}\\
  I. Vuli{\'{c}} et al. (2017)
\end{book}

Modify a vector embedding to account for synonyms (inflections, e.g., V, V-ed, V-ing, in English)
and antonyms (derivations, e.g., negative English prefixes: dis, un, in, im, il, ir, mis, non, anti)
with  margin loss.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Concatenated power mean word embeddings\\ as universal cross-lingual\\ sentence representations} \\
  A. R\"uckle et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To compute a sentence embedding, concatenate several ``averages'' (arithmetic, maximum, minimum) of several pretrained word embeddings (GloVe, word2vec, Attract-Repel, MorphSpecialized).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A neural probabilistic language model} \\
  J. Bengio et al. (2003)
\end{book}

The first layer of an $n$-gram neural language model (predicting the next work from the previous $n$)
can be used as a word embedding.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep-learning-based\\ cryptocurrency sentiment construction} \\
  S. Nasekin and C.Y.S. Chen (2018)
\end{book}
Word2vec and RNN-based sentiment index, trained with StockTwits data -- users often label their messages with ``bullish'' or ``bearish''.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling snow crystal growth I} \\
  J. Gravner and D. Griffeath (2006)
\end{book}
Packard's digital snowflake model is a cellular automaton on a hexagonal lattice
in which a site with one (or at least one, or between 1 and 3, etc.)
occupied neighbour becomes occupied. 


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A deep learning framework for financial\\ time series using stacked autoencoders\\ and long-short-term memory} \\
  W. Bao et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Try to forecast six equity indices from prices, volumes, technical indicators
and macro variables, denoised with a wavelet transform
[doesn't this introduce some look-ahead bias?],
dimension-reduced with an auto-encoder,
and fed to an LSTM.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal timing and tilting of equity factors} \\
  H. Dichtl et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To time factors,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take 
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Some (US) macroeconomic indicators;
  \item Some technical indicators of those factors;
  \item Some cross-sectional factor characteristics, 
  e.g., earning yields of the long-short quintile portfolios, 
  momentum, volatility [these are technical indicators],.
  centrality in the (weighted) complete correlation graph 
  [they use the MST],
  distance to market [just a beta];
  \end{compactitem}
\item Perform some dimension reduction (only keep the first principal component)
in each group;
\item Find the solution of the mean-variance optimization problem 
of the form $w = \theta z'$
where $w$ are the (unknown) portfolio weights,
$z$ are the principal components (and an intercept)
and $\theta$ are the new unknowns
(Brandt--Clara parametric portfolio policy).  
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Big data and AI strategies: \\ML and alternative data approach to investing} \\
  M. Kolanovic (JPM, 2017)
\end{book}
The big data revolution is made possible by the combination of data, algorithms and hardware.
Alternative data can be classified from their origin: humans, business processes or machines (sensors).
Beware of seasonality, sampling bias and short history. 
Examples include
sentiment (for instance, stocks, regions, sectors, indices), 
Twitter, news, Wikipedia, company websites,
web traffic (Alexa), search (Google trends), 
job postings (Glassdoor, LinkedIn), purchase receipts.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Twitter sentiment, for 100 stocks in the S\&P 500, 
  aggregated every minute and smoothed with a 10-day exponential moving average, 
  to forecast the S\&P 500 returns for the next 2 days, with time-changing betas (Kalman), 
  for a long-short strategy;
\item Ravenpack news data,
  average daily sentiment score (ESS) for currencies, commodities, countries, where relevance$>75$, 
  for a daily long-short strategy;
\item Email receipts, for 30 companies in the S\&P 500: 
  weekly change of dollar spent, number of orders, number of buyers;
\item Mobile phone location, assuming sales are proportional to traffic, to predict if a company will beat expectations;
\item Satellite images to monitor retail traffic, real estate traffic, metal production and storage, factory employment
  (Bollinger bands on quarterly traffic);
\item Penalized regression to forecast S\&P, 10y, DXY, gold returns (daily), 
  from their 1m, 3m, 6m, 12m lagged values, on a 2-year window;
\item $k$-NN to forecast risk premia from macro indicators
  % List of the components of their aggregated macro indicators on page 68
\item Kalman filter (time-varying beta) for pairs trading;
\item Xgboost to forecast daily sector returns from macro factors;
\item Logistic regression to forecast if call overwriting will outperform a long-only strategy, from 10 investment factors;
\item SVM to forecast if the P\&L of a rolling long 1m ATM EURUSD will be $>20$bp, or $<-20$bp,
from the first 50 to 200 principal components of 400 predictors 
(volatility, skew, spot, basis, interest rate, equity/commodity/bond indices, economic activity, IMM position; their levels and 1w, 1m changes);
\item Random forest to forecast 1-month returns from investment factors;
\item 2-class hidden Markov model (HMM) to identify up and down states
  (low and high volatility):
  buy the S\&P 500 in up markets, keep cash in down markets;
\item Hierarchical risk parity;
\item PCA on daily changes in the USDJPY implied volatility to help describe its variations;
\item Statistical risk models;
\item Multilayer perceptron to forecast next day sector returns from 8 macro factors (just 8 predictors: 
oil, gold, dollar, bonds, economic surprise, 10y-2y spread, IG and HY credit spreads);
\item LSTM to forecast monthly returns from the past 3 years (does not work);
\item SVM to forecast daily FX returns from 20 features,
obtained by dimension reduction with a restricted Boltzman machine (RBM) using 10 days of daily returns for 10 commodities (100 predictors);
\item Reinforcement learning (combined with supervised learning to forecast returns), for trading.
\end{compactitem}
The report also includes R and Python sample code (web scraping and simple models), a long list of data providers, and 16 pages of references.

%% 2019-01
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% ICML
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Essentially no barriers\\ in neural network energy landscape} \\
  F. Draxler et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Neural networks have many local minima. 
Linear interpolation between them suggests they are separated by high-loss barriers, 
but the AutoNEB algorithm can find paths of almost constant loss.

The \emph{elastic band} model finds points %p_0 = \theta_1, \dots, p_n = \theta_2$, 
minimizing 
$\sum \text{loss}(p_i) + \tfrac12 \sum k \n{ p_{i+1} - p_i }^2 $
(where $k$ is the spring stiffness).
In the \emph{nudged elastic band} model, 
the loss force only acts perpendicularly to the path, 
and the spring force parallelly, 
$ g = \nabla\text{loss}|^{} _\perp + \nabla \text{spring} | ^{} _{/\!\!/} $,
so that the spring force redistribute the points on the path without straightening it.
Instead of choosing the spring stiffness $k$, one can remove the springs altogether and simply redistribute the points on the path at each iteration.
The \emph{AutoNEB} algorithm progressively increases the number of points.

While the algorithm finds \emph{local} minimum energy paths, 
the loss may increase too much: 
this may be remedied by considering a graph of low-energy paths between several local minima.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quickshift++: provably good initializations\\ for sample-based mean shift} \\
  H. Jiang et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The \d{MeanShift} clustering algorithm is a gradient ascent on a kernel density estimator $f$.

The \emph{QuickShift} algorithm is a discretized variant, moving each sample to the closest one with a higher density in a radius $\tau$ ball.

The \emph{QuickShift++} algorithm is a variant stopping when it reaches a cluster core, rather than a local mode.

A \emph{cluster core} $M$ of a topological space $X$ is a connected component of 
\[ \Bigl\{ x \in X \ :\ f(x) \geq ( 1-\beta) \max_{ y \in M} f(y) \Bigr\} . \]

The \emph{$k$-NN density estimator} in $\RR^d$ is 
$f(x) \propto d_k (x) ^{-d}$, 
where $d_k(x)$ is the distance to the $k$th nearest neighbour.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Representation tradeoffs\\ for hyperbolic embeddings} \\
  C. De Sa et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To embed a tree in the Poincar\'e ball (Sarka's construction),
start by placing the root $a$ at the origin,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Apply a reflection, if needed, to put $a$ at the origin;
\item Add $a$'s children on a radius $\tau$ circle centered on $a$, equally-spaced, but as far away from $a$'s parent as possible;
\item Apply the reflexion to go back to the original position (we still have a circle and equal angles, but the circle is no longer centered on $a$);
\item Iterate with $a$'s children.
\end{compactitem}
In higher dimensions, 
use \emph{spherical encoding} to put the points on the sphere (e.g., using vertices of a hypercube).

For a general graph, add \emph{Steiner nodes}.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.5,inner sep=1.5pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=-3pt]
\foreach \i in {0,60,120,180,240,300}
  \node[circle,draw,very thick] (A-\i) at (\i:1) {};
\foreach \i in {0,60,120,180,240,300}
  \foreach \j in {0,60,120,180,240,300}
    \draw (A-\i) -- (A-\j);
\end{tikzpicture}
\rightsquigarrow
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.5,inner sep=1.5pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=-3pt]
\node[circle,draw,very thick] (O) at (0,0) {};
\foreach \i in {0,60,120,180,240,300}
  \node[circle,draw,very thick] (A-\i) at (\i:1) {};
\foreach \i in {0,60,120,180,240,300}
    \draw (A-\i) -- (O);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Reconstructing approximate tree metrics} \\
  I. Abraham et al. (2007)
\end{book}
Instead of embedding your data (e.g., a graph) in a Euclidean space, try embedding it in a tree.

To embed a graph in a tree: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Choose a ``root'' node $r$;
\item Find nodes $p$, $q$ maximizing the \emph{Gromov product} 
\[ 
  ( p | a ) _r = \tfrac12 \bigl( 
    p(p,q) + d(q,r) - d(p,q)
  \bigr) 
\]
and add a \d{Steiner node}
\(
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.2,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=-3pt]
\node (O) at (0,0) {};
\node[circle,draw,thick] (A) at (90:1)   {};
\node[circle,draw,thick] (B) at (-30:1)  {};
\node[circle,draw,thick] (C) at (-150:1) {};
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (A);
\end{tikzpicture}
\rightsquigarrow
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.2,inner sep=1pt,outer sep=0pt,baseline=-3pt]
\node[circle,draw,thick] (O) at (0,0) {};
\node[circle,draw,thick] (A) at (90:1)   {};
\node[circle,draw,thick] (B) at (-30:1)  {};
\node[circle,draw,thick] (C) at (-150:1) {};
\draw (O) -- (A);
\draw (O) -- (B);
\draw (O) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
\)
at distances $(q|r)_p$, $(p|r)_q$, $(p|q)_r$ of $p$, $q$, $r$;
\item Remove $q$ and iterate.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning continuous hierarchies\\ in the Lorentz model of hyperbolic geometry} \\
  M. Nicket and D. Kiela (2018)
\end{book}
Graphs, especially trees, are more naturally embedded in hyperbolic spaces, 
e.g., the Poincar\'e disk, 
or the \emph{hyperboloid model}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Anonymous walk embeddings} \\
  S. Ivanov and E. Burnaev (2018)
\end{book}
An \d{anonymous walk} on a graph is a random walk (of length $\ell$)
with the nodes replaced with numbers 
(for instance, both ABCBC and CDBDB map to 12323).
They give a sparse embedding and, with word2vec 
(anonymous walks are words, sets of walks starting at the same node sentences, graphs documents), a dense one.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian optimization\\ of combinatorial structures} \\
  R. Baptista and M. Poloczek (2018)
\end{book}
To maximize a function $f$ on $\{0,1\}^d$, 
model it using only interactions of order up to $k$, e.g., 
\[ f(x) = \alpha_0 + \sum_i \alpha_i x_i + \sum_{i<j} \alpha_{ij} x_i x_j \qquad (k=2), \]
estimate $\alpha$ from the points already evaluated with a sparse Bayesian regression (and a Horseshoe prior),
sample a value for $\alpha$,
and approximately maximize $f_\alpha$ (with an $L^1$ or $L^2$ penalty)
with a SDP relaxation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Accurate uncertainties for deep learning\\ using calibrated regression} \\
  V. Kuleshov et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Bayesian uncertainties are often inaccurate (variational inference is an approximation):
to recalibrate a Bayesian deep learning model,
\ie, a model $x \mapsto F_x = \text{cdf of $y$}$,
learn an auxiliary model (isotonic regression)
$R : [0,1] \to [0,1]$
such that $R \circ F_x$ be calibrated.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Large-scale sparse inverse covariance estimation via thresholding\\ and max-det matrix completion} \\
  R.Y. Zhang et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The \emph{graphical lasso} 
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ X \succ 0 } \tr CX - \log \det X + \lambda \sum_{ij} \va{ X_{ij} } \]
to find a sparse concentration matrix $X$ from a sample covariance matrix $C$ can be approximated with a simple heuristic,
first soft-thresholding
\raisebox{-1.5mm}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=.2,xscale=.08]
\draw (-3,0) -- (3,0);
\draw (0,-1) -- (0,1);
\draw[ultra thick] (-3,-1) -- (-1,0) -- (1,0) -- (3,1);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}
the covariance $C \mapsto C_\lambda$,
then solving the 
\emph{maximum determinant matrix completion} (MDMC) problem.
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & X \succ 0 \\
  \text{To minimize} & \tr C_\lambda X - \log \det X \\
  \text{Subject to} & X_{ij} = 0 \text{ if } (C_\lambda)_{ij} = 0, \text{ \ie, if } \va{ C_{ij} } \leq \lambda
\end{array}
\]
(There is a recursive closed form solution if the graph is chordal
but, in practice, that rarely happens unless it is a tree.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conditional neural processes} \\
  M. Garnelo et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Neural nets can replace Gaussian processes to model functions $y = f(x)$: 
\[
  h_\theta: 
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{r@{\ }c@{\ }l}
  \RR^N\!\! \times\! \RR & \rightarrow & \RR^d \\
  (x,y) & \mapsto & r 
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \quad
  a : 
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{r@{\ }c@{\ }l}
  \RR^d \times \cdots \times \RR^d & \rightarrow & \RR^d \\
  (r_1,\dots,r_d) & \mapsto & r 
  \end{array}
  \right.
\]
\[  
  g_\theta:
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{r@{\ }c@{\ }l}
  \RR^N \!\!\times\! \RR^d & \rightarrow & \RR^p \\
  (x,r) & \mapsto & \phi
  \end{array}
  \right.  
\]
where 
$h_\theta$ and $g_\theta$ are neural nets,
$a$ is some aggregation function, 
$r$ summarizes the information so far,
$\phi$ are the parameters of the distribution of $y$, 
e.g., $\phi=(\mu,\sigma^2)$ for a Gaussian.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimization, fast and slow:\\ optimally switching between\\ local and Bayesian optimization} \\
  M. McLeod et al. (2018)
\end{book}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item 
Use Bayesian optimization, with \emph{predictive entropy search} 
(PES: maximize the expected change in information content),
to find a convex region likely to contain the global minimum; 
\item 
Use Bayesian optimization, 
with a different acquisition function, 
to ensure the region indeed contains a global minimum;
\item Find the minimum, using local search (L-BFGS).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive three operator splitting} \\
  F. Pedregosa and G. Gidel (2018)
\end{book}
To minimize a function of the form $f+g+h$, 
where $f$ is smooth (with a known gradient),
and $g$ and $h$ have a closed form proximal operator 
(even though $g+h$ does not),
use the three operator splitting (TOS) method,
\begin{align*}
  x & \leftarrow \operatorname{prox}_{ \gamma g } \bigl( z - \gamma u - \gamma \nabla f(x) \bigr) \\
  z & \leftarrow \operatorname{prox}_{\gamma h} ( x + \gamma u ) \\
  u & \leftarrow u + \gamma^{-1} ( x - z )
\end{align*}
where the step size $\gamma$ is sufficiently small: 
\[
  f(x) \leq f(z) + \langle \nabla f(z) , x - z \rangle + 
  \dfrac1{ 2 \gamma } \n{ x - z }^2 
\]
(\ie, such that the rhs be a quadratic majorizer of $f$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Shampoo:\\ preconditioned stochastic tensor optimization} \\
  V. Gupta et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Preconditioning ignores the matrix or tensor structure of the parameters; 
Shampoo is a generalization of AdaGrad (a diagonal preconditioner)
using a separate (dense) preconditioning matrix for each dimension of the gradient tensor.
For matrices: 
\begin{align*}
  & W_1 = 0 \quad L_0 = \epsilon I \quad R_0 = \epsilon I \\
  & G_t = \nabla f(W_t) && \text{gradient} \\
  & L_t \leftarrow L_{t-1} + G_t G'_t && \text{preconditioners} \\
  & R_t \leftarrow R_{t-1} + G'_t G_t \\
  & W_{t+1} \leftarrow W_t - \eta L_t^{-1/4} G_t R^{-1/4} && \text{parameters}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fairness without demographics\\ in repeated loss minimization} \\
  T.B. Hashimoto et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Minority groups contribute less to the loss function and suffer higher loss
and higher attrition -- disparity increases.
\emph{robust optimization} 
(minimizing the worst case risk over distributions close to the empirical ones
for the $\chi^2$ divergence, 
\[
D_{\chi^2} ( P \| Q ) = \int  \Bigl( \dfrac{ dP }{ dQ } - 1 \Bigr )^2 dQ,
\]
using the dual formulation)
prevents \emph{disparity amplification}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Augmented CycleGAN: learning many-to-many mappings from unpaired data} \\
  A. Almahairi et al. (2018)
\end{book}
CycleGAN learns 1-to-1 mappings.
For 1-to-many mappings, add a latent space 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=3pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
\node[draw] (A) at (0,0) {\vbox{\hsize=4mm\parindent=0pt $\mathstrut Z_b$\\$A$}};
\node[draw] (B) at (1,0) {\vbox{\hsize=4mm\parindent=0pt $\mathstrut Z_a$\\$B$}};
\draw[->] (A) to [out=45, in=140, looseness=1]  (B);
\draw[->] (B) to [out=-135, in=-40, looseness=1]  (A);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
and consider two generators and two encoders
\begin{align*}
  A \times Z_b & \longrightarrow B \\
  B \times Z_a & \longrightarrow A \\
  A \times B & \longrightarrow Z_a \\
  A \times B & \longrightarrow Z_b
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Is generator conditioning\\ causally related to GAN performance?} \\
  A. Odena et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To evaluate the quality of a GAN, check the \emph{inception score}
\[ \exp \E_{ \text{image} \sim \text{generator} } \text{KL} \bigl(
  p(\text{label} | \text{image}) \,\|\, p(\text{label})
\bigr) \]
or the \emph{Fr\'echet inception distance}, \ie, the Wasserstein distance $W_2$
between Gaussian fits of the activations, 
\[ W_2 = \n{ \mu_1 - \mu_2 }_2^2 + \tr \bigl( V_1 + V_2 - 2 (V_1V_2)^{1/2} \bigr). \]

Poor quality is linked to the condition number of the Jacobian of the generator.
\emph{Jacobian clamping} feeds two minibatches to the generator,
differing by small perturbations,
computes the relative changes 
$ \n{ \Delta G(z) } / \n{ \Delta z } $,
and adds a penalty if they are not all in some predefined interval 
$[\lambda_{\text{min}}, \lambda_{\text{max}}]$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MAGAN: aligning biological manifolds} \\
  M. Amodio (2018)
\end{book}
To align two domains, use two GANs

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2.5,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=3pt,outer sep=1pt,baseline=0]
\node at (0,1.7) {Domain 1};
\node at (1,1.7) {Domain 2};
\node (D1) at (0,1) {\mathstrut data};
\node (G2) at (1,1) {\mathstrut generated};
\node (G1) at (0,0) {\mathstrut generated};
\node (D2) at (1,0) {\mathstrut data};
\draw[|->] (D1) -- node[auto,pos=.5,inner sep = 1pt] {\footnotesize $G_{12}$} (G2);
\draw[|->] (D2) -- node[auto,pos=.5,inner sep = 1pt] {\footnotesize $G_{21}$}(G1);
\draw[->] (D1) -- (-.4,1) -- (-.4,.5) -- (-.5,.5);
\draw[->] (G1) -- (-.4,0) -- (-.4,.5) -- (-.5,.5);
\draw[->] (D2) -- (1.4,0) -- (1.4,.5) -- (1.5,.5);
\draw[->] (G2) -- (1.4,1) -- (1.4,.5) -- (1.5,.5);
\node[anchor = south east,inner sep = 1pt] at (-.4,.55) {\footnotesize discriminator};
\node[anchor = south west,inner sep = 1pt] at (1.4,.55) {\footnotesize discriminator};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
and add reconstruction losses for 
$G_{12} \circ G_{21} \approx \text{Id}$ and
$G_{21} \circ G_{12} \approx \text{Id}$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning longer-term dependencies in RNNs with auxiliary losses} \\
  T.H. Trinh et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To have RNNs learn longer-term dependencies, use truncated BPTT with auxiliary losses, 
to forecast past (and/or future) observations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Attention-based deep multiple instance learning} \\
  M. Ilse et al. (2018)
\end{book}
In \emph{multiple instance learning} (MIL), labels are not assigned to instances but to bags of instances
(labels may exist for instances, though, aggregated in each bay with a max or a sum).
A function 
$S : \mathscr P( \text{Data} ) \to \RR $
is \emph{symmetric}  if it can be written 
$S(X) = g( \sum_{ x \in X } f(x) )$, 
for some functions $f$ and $g$;
it can then be approximated arbitrarily close as 
$S(x) \approx g \bigl( \max_{ x \in X } f(x) \bigr )$.
For a binary output, model $f$ and $g$ as neural nets and maximize the Bernoulli likelihood.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mixed batches and symmetric discriminators for GAN training} \\
  T. Lucas et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To reduce \emph{mode collapse} in GANs, 
feed the discriminator a batch of true and generated samples 
and have it forecast the proportion of true samples.

Equivariant and invariant layers can be obtained as 
\begin{align*}
  (x_1,\dots,x_n) & \longmapsto (f x_1, \dots, f x_n ) \\
  (x_1,\dots,x_n) & \longmapsto A \rho(f x_1, \dots, f x_n )  
\end{align*}
where $\rho$ is the (elementwise) mean, standard deviation or maximum (or a basis of symmetric polynomials).
The following layers suffice: 
\[
  (x_1,\dots,x_n) \mapsto 
  \bigl( 
  \sigma( \beta + A x_1 + B \bar x ), 
  \dots,
  \sigma( \beta + A x_n + B \bar x )
  \bigr).
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CoVeR: learning covariate-specific vector representations with tensor decompositions} \\
  K. Tian et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The GloVe vector embedding can be generalized to deal with covariates
\begin{align*}
  A &: \text{ co-occurrence matrix} \\
  i,j &: \text{ words} \\
  k &: \text{ covariates (context)} \\
  f &: \text{ weight function, e.g., 1 or } \left( \dfrac{ \min(100,x) }{ 100 } \right)^{.75} 
\end{align*}
\[
\argmin_{v,c,b} \sum_{i,j,k} \bigl(
  ( c_k \odot v_i )' (c_k \odot v_j ) + b_{ik} + b_{jk} - \log A_{ijk}
\bigr) ^2
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning in integer latent variable models\\ with nested automatic differentiation} \\
  D. Sheldon et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The forward algorithm, for hidden Markov models,
\begin{align*}
  k &: \text{ time} \\
  n_k &: \text{ population size} \\
  z_{ik} &: \text{ offspring of individual $i \in \llbracket 1, n_{k-1} \rrbracket$ } \\
  m_k &: \text{ immigration} \\
  n_k &= \sum_{i=1} ^{ n_k - 1 } z_{k,i} + m_k \text{ (hidden)} \\
  y_k &\sim \text{Binomial}( n_k, p ) \text{ (observed)}
\end{align*}
can be formulated with probability generating functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Born-again neural networks} \\
  T. Furlanello et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Knowledge distillation trains a small (student) network to mimic a larger (teacher) network;
try with two similarly-sized networks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Differentiable dynamic programming\\ for structured prediction and attention} \\
  A. Mensch and M. Blondel (2018)
\end{book}
To make dynamic programming (Viterbi, dynamic time warp (DTW)) differentiable,
replace the $(\max,+)$ semiring with $(\max_\Omega,+)$
\begin{align*}
  \max\nolimits_\Omega(x) &= \max_{ q \in \Delta^D } \langle q, x \rangle - \Omega(q) \\
  \nabla \max\nolimits_\Omega(x) &= \argmax_{ q \in \Delta^D } \langle q, x \rangle - \Omega(q)
\end{align*}
where $\Omega$ is the negentropy $-H$
($\max_\Omega = \text{log\,sum\,exp}$, $\nabla\max_\Omega = \text{softmax}$), 
or the squared $\ell^2$ norm
($\nabla \max_\Omega$ is then sparse).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Analysis of minimax error rate\\ for crowdsourcing and its application\\ to worker clustering model} \\
  H. Imamura et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The \d{Dawid and Skene} model of crowdsourcing uses the EM algorithm to estimate the confusion matrix of the workers 
\( P[ \text{worker $i$ gives label $k$ instead of $\ell$} ] \)
and the true labels.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural networks should be wide enough\\ to learn disconnected decision regions} \\
  Q. Nguyen et al. (2018)
\end{book}
If no layer has more units than the input, 
the decision regions are connected (for ReLU activations).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A hierarchical latent vector model\\ for learning long-term structure in music} \\
  A. Roberts et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The second term in the ELBO
\[
  E[ \log p(x|z) ] - \text{KL} \bigl( q(z|x) \,\|\, p(z) \bigr) \leq p(x) 
\]
can be seen as a penalty and replaced with $\lambda \cdot \text{KL}$
or $(\text{KL} - \tau)_+$.
