\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Network global testing by counting graphlets} \\
  J. Jin et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The degree-corrected mixed membership model generalizes the 
\emph{stochastic block model} (SBM) 
\[ 
P[A_{ij} = 1 ] = \theta_i \theta_j \sum_{ 1 \leq k, \ell \leq K } \pi_{ik} \pi_{j\ell} P_{k\ell},
\]
where $\theta$ is the degree heterogeneity
and $\pi$ measures the mixed membership
of the $K$ communities;
it can be written as 
$A = \Omega - \diag \Omega + \text{noise}$, 
with $\Omega = \Theta \Pi P \Pi ' \Theta $.

The \emph{graphlet count} statistic tests for 
$H_0: K = 1 $.
\begin{align*}
  L_m &: \text{ density of length-$m$ self-avoiding paths} \\
  C_m &: \text{ density of length-$m$ self-avoiding cycles} \\
  \chi_m &= C_m = ( L_{ m-1 } / L_{m-2} ) ^m \qquad (m\geq3)
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Online convolutional sparse coding\\ with sample-dependent dictionary} \\
  Y. Wang et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Sparse coding is a sparse matrix decomposition.
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
  \text{Given} & X \in \RR^{n \times N} &\text{(columns = observations)} \\
  \text{Find} & Y \in \RR^{n\times d} &\text{(columns = filters)} \\
  & W \in \RR^{d\times N} &\text{(columns = weights)} \\
  \text{To minimize} & \rlap{$\displaystyle\n{ X - Y W }_2 ^2 + \lambda \sum _i \n{ W_{\cdot i } }_1$}
\end{array}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GAIN: missing data imputation\\ using generative adversarial nets} \\
  J. Yoon et al. (2018)
\end{book}
GANs can be used to impute missing data: 
the generator fills in missing values, 
while the discriminator tries to find which values were imputed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Inter and intra topic structure learning\\ with word embeddings} \\
  H. Zhao et al. (2018)
\end{book}
\emph{Hierarchical topic models} generalizing \emph{Poisson factor analysis}
model texts as mixtures of subtopics, subtopics as mixtures of topics, and topics as distributions on words.
\begin{align*}
  X & \sim \text{Poisson}( \Phi_1 \Theta_1 ) \qquad\text{(words)} \\
  \Theta_1 &\sim \text{Gamma}( \Phi_2 \Theta_2) \\
  \Theta_2 &\sim \text{Gamma}( \Phi_3 \Theta_3) \\
  & \ \,\vdots \\
  \Phi_i &\sim \text{Dir} ( \beta_i )   
\end{align*}
They can also use a vector embedding $f$: 
\[ \beta \sim \text{Gamma} ( \alpha, e^{ f' w } ). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving regression performance\\ with distributional losses} \\
  E. Imani and M. White (2018)
\end{book}
Do not forecast values $E[y|x]$ but distributions $y|x$, e.g., 
forecast the histogram density $q_x$
to minimize 
$D( p_y || q_x )$,
where $p_y = N(y, \sigma^2)$, with $\sigma$ fixed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Comparison-based random forests} \\
  S. Haghiri et al. (2018)
\end{book}
A \d{comparison tree} is a decision tree whose boundaries are obtained by choosing two points $x_1$, $x_2$
with different labels and splitting the data with $d(x,x_1) < d(x,x_2)$.

To use a comparison random forest, 
one only needs to be able to actively tell if the new item $A$ is closer to items $B$ or $C$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{QuantTree: histograms for change detection\\ in multivariate data streams} \\
  G. Boracchi et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To detect distributional changes in multivariate time series, 
use histogram statistics,
but build those histograms on kd-trees rather than grids.

%% 2018-10-21
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep learning} \\
  I. Goodfellow et al. (2016)
\end{book}

\textbf{3.} The probability theory needed to deal with probabilistic graphical models
boils down to two rules.
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Sum rule} & P(x) &= \sum_y P(x,y) \\
  &\text{Product rule} & P(x,y) &= P(x) P(y|x)
\end{align*}

The Kullback-Leibler divergence is the extra amount of information needed to send a message from $P$ using a code for $Q$.
\begin{align*}
  & \text{Information} & I(x) &= - \log p(x) \\
  & \text{Entropy} & H(X) &= E[ - \log p(X) ] \\
  & \text{KL divergence} & D(P \| Q) &= \E_{ x \sim P } \log \dfrac{ P(x) }{ Q(x) } \geq 0 \\
  & \text{Cross-entropy} & H(P,Q) &= \E_{ x \sim P } \bigl[ - \log Q(x) \bigr] \\
  & & &= H(P) + D( P \| Q )
\end{align*}

Log-likelihood and cross-entropy are the same thing, and the Kullback-Leibler divergence only differs by a constant: minimizing $D(\text{data} \| \text{model})$ is equivalent to minimizing $H(\text{data},\text{model})$.

<<KLDivengenceAsymmetry,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=3,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
KL <- function(p,q) {
  xlim <- range(p,q)
  xlim <- xlim + .1 * diff(xlim) * c(-1,1)
  p <- density( p, from = xlim[1], to = xlim[2] )
  q <- density( q, from = xlim[1], to = xlim[2] )
  r <- p$y * log( p$y / q$y )
  r[ ! is.finite(r) ] <- 0
  sum(r)
}
f <- function( mu, sigma ) mu + sigma * qnorm( ppoints(500) )

set.seed(1)
a <- 4
x <- c( a + rnorm(300), -a + rnorm(200) )

op <- par( mfrow = c(1,2), mar = c(1.5,.5,0,.5) )
dbColours <- c("#193296", "#0092D0", "#FFA000", "#D70032", "#2D962D", "#0055AA")
ylim <- c(0,.25)
r <- optim( c(0,a), function(p) KL( x, f(p[1],p[2]) ) )
plot( density(x), lwd = 3, col = dbColours[1], main = "", xlab = "", ylab = "", axes = FALSE, ylim = ylim )
curve( dnorm( (x-r$par[1])/r$par[2] ) / r$par[2], add = TRUE, lwd = 3, col = dbColours[3], lty = 2 )
mtext( "Minimizing KL(p,.)", font = 2, side = 1 )

r <- optim( c(a,1), function(p) KL( f(p[1],p[2]), x ) )
plot( density(x), lwd = 3, col = dbColours[1], main = "", xlab = "", ylab = "", axes = FALSE, ylim = ylim )
curve( dnorm( (x-r$par[1])/r$par[2] ) / r$par[2], add = TRUE, lwd = 3, col = dbColours[3], lty = 2 )
mtext( "Minimizing KL(.,p)", font = 2, side = 1 )

par(op)
@


% 4. Numeric computation
\textbf{4.} 
Gradient descent fails to exploit the curvature information in the Hessian --
it ``wastes time repeatedly descending canyon walls because they are the steepest feature''.

\[
<<GradientDescentFailsToExploitCurvature,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=2,fig.height=1.8,out.width='.5\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Gradient descent fails to exploit curvature information
f <- function(x, y=NULL) {
  if( missing(y) || is.null(y) ) {
    if( is.vector(x) )
      x <- t(x)
    y <- x[,2]
    x <- x[,1]
  }
  (x-2*y)^2 + .2*x^2
}
df <- function(x, h=1e-20) {
  c(
    Re( ( f(x+ c(1i,0)*h) - f(t(x)) ) / ( 1i*h ) ),
    Re( ( f(x+ c(0,1i)*h) - f(t(x)) ) / ( 1i*h ) )
  )
}
xs <- seq(0,2,length=100)
ys <- xs
A <- outer(xs, ys, f)
A[] <- rank(A)
set.seed(1)
op <- par( mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
#myimage(xs, ys, A, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "")
contour(xs, ys, A, drawlabels = FALSE, axes = FALSE, lwd = 3, col = "grey")
x <- runif(2) * 4 - 2
x <- c(1,2)
s <- .17
for( i in 1:20 ) {
  previous <- x
  x <- x - s * df(x)
  segments( previous[1], previous[2], x[1], x[2], lwd = 3 )
}
par(op)
@
\]

% 5. Machine learning basics
\textbf{5.} 
Balancing over- and under-fitting requires to both make the training
error small and the gap between training and test error small.

The \d{Bayes error} is the error made by the best predictor possible, \ie,
that knowing the true data generation process
(the error is not zero because of the error in the data generation process): 
it is the minimum test set error.

To fight the no free lunch theorem, incorporate preferences into the learning algorithm
(prior, regularization).

There are implicit priors in many machine learning algorithms:
locally constant ($k$-means), locally smooth, 
but deeper algorithms allow for more complex priors -- 
non-local generalization, hierarchical structures.

% 6. Deep feed-forward networks
\textbf{6.}
You may need to clip gradients for \emph{mixture density networks}
(MLP with Gaussian mixtures in their output).

From the \emph{universal approximation theorem}, one layer is enough,
but deeper networks require fewer units.

% 7. Regularization for deep learning
\textbf{7.} 
Regularization trades bias for variance.
Neural networks may use a separate penalty for each layer.

$L^2$ regularization shrinks towards zero, but more in the ``imprecise'' directions
(where the Hessian eigenvalues are large).
Early stopping, for a linear model, is equivalent to $L^2$ regularization.

<<EarlyStoppingIsL2Regularization,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## L^2 regularization shrinks the parameters in the "imprecise" directions
## Early stopping is equivalent to L^2 regularization
a <- 2
b <- 2
lambda <- 3
plot_1 <- function(r, ...) {
  thetas <- seq(0,2*pi,length=100)
  lines( r * cos(thetas), r * sin(thetas), ... )
}
plot_2 <- function(s, ...) { 
  thetas <- seq(0,2*pi,length=100)
  lines( a + lambda * s * cos(thetas), b + s * sin(thetas), ... )
}
f <- function(x)
  lambda^2 * b * x / ( (lambda^2-1) * x + a )

dbColours <- c("#193296", "#0092D0", "#FFA000", "#D70032", "#2D962D", "#0055AA")

op <- par( mfrow = c(1,2), mar = c(0,0,0,0) )
xlim <- c(-2.5,4)
ylim <- c(-2.5,3)
plot( NA, xlim = xlim, ylim = ylim, type = "n", xlab = "", ylab = "", axes = FALSE )
for( x in c(.1, .3, .7) ) { 
  y <- f(x)
  r <- sqrt(x^2+y^2)
  s <- sqrt( (x-a)^2/lambda^2 + (y-b)^2 )
  plot_1(r, lty = 3, lwd = 3)
  #plot_2(s)
}
for( x in c(.7, 1, 1.5) ) { 
  y <- f(x)
  r <- sqrt(x^2+y^2)
  s <- sqrt( (x-a)^2/lambda^2 + (y-b)^2 )
  #plot_1(r)
  plot_2(s, lwd = 3)
}
x <- .7
y <- f(x)
points( x, y, cex = 2, lwd = 3, col = "orange" )

plot( NA, xlim = xlim, ylim = ylim, type = "n", xlab = "", ylab = "", axes = FALSE )
for( x in c(.7, 1, 1.5) ) { 
  y <- f(x)
  r <- sqrt(x^2+y^2)
  s <- sqrt( (x-a)^2/lambda^2 + (y-b)^2 )
  #plot_1(r)
  plot_2(s, lwd = 3)
}
curve( f(x), add = TRUE, xlim = c(0,a), lwd = 3, col = dbColours[1] )
x <- .7
y <- f(x)
points( x, y, cex = 2, lwd = 3, col = "orange" )
par(op)
@

$L^1$ regularization has a sparsifying effect.

You can add noise to: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Input (denoising auto-encoder);
\item Activations (dropout corresponds to multiplicative noise);
\item Weights (this makes the network Bayesian);
\item Output (label smoothing replaces 0 and 1 with 
$\epsilon/(k-1)$ and $1-\epsilon$).
\end{compactitem}

Parameter sharing forces parameters to be equal (CNN) 
or close (transfer learning).

% Bagging, boosting

To predict with dropout, you could average over several masks;
alternatively, just rescale the weights 
(there is no theoretical justification for nonlinear models: 
it is an approximation, but it works well).
Multiplying the weights by a random number $\mu \sim N(1,1)$
is another form of dropout, 
but since $E[\mu]=1$, no rescaling is necessary.

If you do not have enough data,
prefer Bayesian neural nets or semisupervised learning.

To generalize well, 
neural nets should be locally constant around 
(orthogonally to) the data manifold -- instead, they tend to be linear.
Try data augmentation with adversarial examples, 
or a penalty for 
$\n{ \nabla\! _\theta f }$.

% 8. Optimization for training deep models
\textbf{8.}
The ideal objective function, 
the expected loss over the data generation process,
is not available:
instead, we minimize the \emph{empirical risk}, \ie, 
the average loss over the training set.
Non-differentiable loss functions (e.g., 0-1 loss)
are often replaced by differentiable surrogates (e.g., negative log-likelihood).
Optimization algorithms often converge faster with \emph{mini-batches} 
($n\geq100$ for gradient-based methods, $n\geq10,000$ for Hessian-based ones),
\ie, with frequent though imprecise updates.
The mini-batches should be selected randomly.

Gradient descent may not arrive at a critical point --
plot the gradient norm: it often fails to decrease.
The concern that we may end up at a local minimum or a saddle point is moot.

Models with latent variables are not identifiable: 
they have many (sometimes uncountably many) local minima --
but most minima have a low cost value.

Cliffs in the loss function landscape can be avoided with \emph{gradient clipping}.

\emph{Momentum} addresses the high variance of the stochastic gradient 
and bad conditioning of the Hessian.
\emph{Nesterov momentum} computes the gradient after the momentum step.

Weight initialization is usually random (to break symmetries) 
and normalized.

AdaGrad and \emph{RMSProp} decrease the learning rate using the sum or exponential moving average of 
past gradient norms;
\emph{Adam} combines momentum and RMS\-Prop.

Second-order methods often assume the Hessian is positive definite,
which is not the case around saddle points:
the Hessian can be regularized, $ H \rightsquigarrow H + \lambda I$ 
(Levenberg-Marquardt).
With gradient descent (and line search), each gradient direction is perpendicular to the previous one,
resulting in a zigzag motion.
\emph{Conjugate gradient} (CG) replaces this orthogonality condition 
$d_t ' d_{t-1} = 0$ with 
$d_t ' H d_{t-1} = 0$, 
where $d_t = \nabla J + \beta_t d_{t-1}$
($\beta_t=0$ gives the gradient).
Some approximations of $\beta_t$ do not require the Hessian, e.g.,
\begin{align*}
  \beta &= \dfrac{ 
    \text{current } ( \nabla J )' ( \nabla J ) 
  }{
    \text{previous } (\nabla J)' (\nabla J)
  } \\
  \text{or } \beta &= \dfrac{
    ( \text{change in } \nabla J )' ( \text{current } \nabla J ) 
  }{
    \text{previous } (\nabla J)' (\nabla J)
  }.
\end{align*}

The gradients tell how to update each parameter if the others remain fixed;
\emph{batch normalization} helps decouple the layers.

Polyak averaging (Ces\`aro summation) can help convergence.

\emph{Greedy supervised pretraining}
builds up an increasingly deeper network.

