\]

\emph{FitNets} train a wide and shallow ``teacher'' network,
and then a deeper ``student'' network, 
to forecast both the output and the middle layer of the teacher network,
providing hints for its middle layers.

Gradients flow more easily through linear functions: ReLU are easier to train than sigmoids.

\emph{Skip-connections} (ResNets) reduce the length of the shortest path to the output.

\emph{Auxilliary heads} are an alternative to pre-training.

% Auxilliary heads
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.5,yscale=.8,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt,
h/.style={draw,inner sep=2pt,text width=1cm}
]
\node    (x)  at (0,0) {\footnotesize input};
\node[h] (h1) at (0,1) {};
\node[h] (h2) at (0,2) {};
\node[h] (h3) at (0,3) {};
\node    (y)  at (0,4) {\footnotesize output};
\node    (y2) at (1,2) {\footnotesize output};
\node (y3) at (1,3) {\footnotesize output};
\draw[->] (x) -- (h1);
\draw[->] (h1) -- (h2);
\draw[->] (h2) -- (h3);
\draw[->] (h3) -- (y);
%\draw[->] (h1) -- (y2);
%\draw[->] (h2) -- (y3);
\draw[->] (h1) to [out=90, in=-90, looseness=1] (y2);
\draw[->] (h2) to [out=90, in=-90, looseness=1] (y3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\emph{Continuation methods} use a sequence of increasingly more difficult loss functions
(blurring non-convex functions makes them more convex);
\emph{curriculum learning}  (e.g., stochastic curriculum learning, with a mixture of easy and hard samples),
is an example.

% 9. Convolutional networks
\textbf{9.}
Convolution (same notion as in math, but without flipping the kernel)
can be expressed as multiplication by a structured matrix (circulant and sparse).
CNNs are sparsely conected: 
each neuron is only linked to nearly neurons (small receptive field -- but it grows with depth).

% Parameter sharing
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.5,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=2pt, outer sep=1pt,
col1/.style={thick,red!80!black},
col2/.style={thick,green!80!black},
col3/.style={thick,blue!80!black}
]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7} 
  \node[thick,draw,circle] (A-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\foreach \i in {1,2,3,4,5,6}
  \node[thick,draw,circle] (B-\i) at (\i,1) {};
