\draw[->] (y2) -- (h3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\rightsquigarrow
\cdots
\]

For separable kernels, the convolution can be computed separately in each dimension.

To speed up training, skip it, and use random features, 
hand-crafted features, features from $k$-means clustering of image patches,
greedy layer-wise pretraining.

Real-world convnets use more branching.

% 10. Sequence models: recurrent and recursive nets
\textbf{10.}
One-dimensional convolution provides parameter sharing, but it is shallow:
each output only depends on the recent past.
Recurrent neural nets (RNN) use a different form of sharing.
Back-propagation through time (BPTT) is backpropagation on the unrolled network.

% RNN
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt,
node/.style={circle,draw,inner sep=1pt}
]
\node (input)  at (0,0) {\footnotesize input};
\node (hidden) at (0,1) {\footnotesize hidden};
\node (output) at (0,2) {\footnotesize output};
\draw[->] (input) -- (hidden);
\draw[->] (hidden) -- (output);
\draw[->] (hidden) to [out=-15, in=15, looseness=5] node[anchor=center,fill,inner sep=2pt] {}  (hidden);
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad
% Another RNN
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt,
node/.style={circle,draw,inner sep=1pt}
]
\node (input)  at (0,0) {\footnotesize input};
\node (hidden) at (0,1) {\footnotesize hidden};
\node (output) at (0,2) {\footnotesize output};
\draw[->] (input) -- (hidden);
\draw[->] (hidden) -- (output);
\draw[->] (hidden) to [out=-15, in=15, looseness=5] node[anchor=center,fill,inner sep=2pt] {}  (hidden);
\draw[->] (output) to [out=-45, in=45, looseness=1] node[anchor=center,fill,inner sep=2pt] {}  (hidden);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Instead of feeding $\hat y_t$ to the network at time $t+1$,
\emph{teacher forcing} uses $y_t$ (during training -- otherwise it is not available)
and progressively switches to actual forecasts.

Deep recurrent networks can stack layers vertically or horizontally,
use skip connections, or remove connections.

