\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} 
  \foreach \j in {1,3}
    \draw (C-\i) -- (B-\j);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

In general, computing the marginal probability of a probabilistic graphical model
is \#P-hard (NP problems are decision problems, \#P problems are the corresponding counting problems);
they can be approximated by variational inference.

Loopy belief propagation is not supposed to work with arbitrary graphs (non-trees),
but works well with sparsely-connected graphs -- neural networks are densely connected.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.25,yscale=.5,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt, baseline=5pt,
n/.style={circle, fill}
  ]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} 
  \node[n] (A-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} 
