\]

Restricted (\ie, bipartite) Boltzman machines (RBM) can be efficiently sampled from (block Gibbs sampling).

% 17. Monte Carlo methods
\textbf{17.}
Expectations can be approximated with averages:
\[ \E_{x\sim p} f(x) \approx \dfrac1n \sum_{x_i \sim p} f(x_i) \]
or weighted averages (\emph{importance sampling})
\begin{align*}
\E_{x\sim p} f(x) &=
\int f(x) p(x) dx =
\int \dfrac{ f(x) p(x) }{ q(x) } q(x) dx \\
& =
\E_{ x \sim q } \dfrac{ f(x) p(x) }{ q(x) } \\
&\approx
\dfrac1n \sum _{ x_i \sim q } \dfrac{ f(x_i) p(x_i) }{ q(x_i) }
\end{align*}
whose variance can be lower
(it is minimal for $q \propto p \cdot \va f$)
or \emph{biased importance sampling}
\[
\E_{ x \sim p } f(x) \approx
\sum_{ x_i \sim q } \dfrac{ \tilde p(x_i) }{ \tilde q(x_i) } f(x_i)
\bigg/
\sum _{ x_i \sim q } \dfrac{ \tilde p(x_i) }{ \tilde q(x_i) }
\]
(with unnormalized probabilities,
$p \propto \tilde p$, $q \propto \tilde q$ --
the estimator is only asymptotically unbiased).

For MCMC, use 100 chains.
Mixing time is determined by the second eigenvalue (the first is 1 -- Perron-Frobenius).

Gibbs sampling has mixing problems in presence of several modes:
increase the temperature (\emph{tempering}).
MCMC is less useful when the distribution has a manifold structure:
good learning leads to high $\text{MI}(h,x)$, low entropy $p(x|h)$,
which is hard to sample from and leads to bad mixing

% 18. Confronting the partition function
\textbf{18.}
To compute the gradient of the likelihood, we need that of the partition function
(\emph{negative phase} -- the other tem, the unnormalized log-likelihood, is the positive phase).
\[
p = \dfrac{ \tilde p }{ Z }
\qquad
\nabla\!_\theta \log p = \nabla\!_\theta \log \tilde p - \nabla\! _\theta \log Z
\]
It can be estimated with Monte Carlo:
\begin{align*}
  \nabla\!_\theta \log Z &=
  \dfrac{ \nabla\! _\theta Z }{ Z } \\
  &= \dfrac{ \sum \nabla\!_\theta \tilde p(x) }{ Z } \\
  &= \dfrac{ \sum \nabla\!_\theta \exp \log \tilde p (x) }{  Z } \\
  &= \sum p(x) \nabla\!_\theta \log \tilde p(x) \\
  &= \E_{ x \sim p } \nabla\! _\theta \log \tilde p(x) .
\end{align*}

The positive phase increases $\tilde p$ for $x$ drawn from the data;
the negative phase decreases $\tilde p(x)$ for $x$ drawn from the model.

% TODO: DIAGRAMS p. 607

While $\nabla\!_\theta \log Z = \E \nabla\! _\theta \log \tilde p(x)$
could be estimated with MCMC, with a burn-in period, this would have to be done anew
at each gradient step.
\emph{Contrastive divergence} (CD) uses $k$ Gibbs steps, with no burn-in,
after initialization from the data -- but it is unaware of high-probability regions
away from the data.
It only works for shallow models (e.g., RBM) --
the latent variables are not in the data and require a burn-in period.
CD is not the gradient of any function
(there could be cycles)
% TODO: DIAGRAM

\emph{Stochastic maximum likelihood} is CD,
but it initializes the chain from the previous gradient;
the gradient steps should be small and/or the chain long.
\emph{Parallel tempreing} can help.

\emph{Pseudo-likelihood} uses
\[
\log p(\mathbf x) = \sum \log p(x_i | x_1 \cdots x_{i-1} ) \approx
\sum \log p(x_i | \mathbf x_{-i} )
\]
(generalized pseudo-likelihood puts the variables in groups).

\emph{Score matching} minimizes the expectation of
\begin{align*}
  L(x,\theta) &= \dfrac12 \n{ \nabla\!_x \log p_{\text{model}}(x,\theta) -
  \nabla\!_x \log p_{\text{data}} (x) }_2^2 \\
  &= \sum_i \dfrac{ \partial^2 }{ \partial x_i^2 } \log p(x,\theta) +
  \dfrac12 \biggl( \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial x_i } \log p(x,\theta) \biggr) ^2
\end{align*}
($Z$ does not depend on $x$ and disappears; $p_{\text{data}}$ is not known).

For binary data, \emph{ratio matching} minimizes the average of
\[ L(x,\theta) = \sum_i \biggl( 1 + \dfrac{ p(x,\theta) }{ p\bigl( f_j(x), \theta \bigr) } \biggr)^2 \]
where $f_i$ flips the $i$th bit.

\emph{Noise contrastive estimation} (NCE)
$\log p(x) = \log \tilde p_\theta(x) + c$
considers the partition function $c = - \log Z$
as another parameter to estimate,
not with maximum likelihood, but with a binary classifier,
data vs noise (e.g., word2vec).
For \emph{self-contrastive estimation},
the noise samples are generated from the model --
the model learns to distinguish reality from its own evolving beliefs.

To evaluate or compare models,
with the likelihood or the BIC,
the whole likelihood, with the partition function, is needed.
The partition function can be estimated by \emph{importance sampling},
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{ Z_1 }{ Z_0 } &=
\dfrac1{Z_0} \int \tilde p_1 =
\dfrac1{Z_0} \int p_0 \dfrac{ \tilde p_1 }{ p_0 } =
\int p_0 \dfrac{ \tilde p_1 }{ \tilde p_0 } \\
&=
\E_{ x \sim  p_0 } \dfrac{ \tilde p_1(x) }{ \tilde p_0(x) } \\
& \approx
\dfrac{1}{N} \sum_{ x_i  \sim p_0 } \dfrac{ \tilde p_1(x_i) }{ \tilde p_0(x_i) }.
\end{align*}

If the distributions are very different,
\emph{annealed importance sampling} (AIS) adds more distributions between
$p_0$ (tractable) and $p_1$ (interesting),
e.g., weighted geometric averages
$p_0 ^{ \eta_i } p_1 ^{ 1 - \eta_i }$,
and computes the successive ratios of partition functions.

% 19. 
\textbf{19.} 
The \d{evidence lower bound} (ELBO) is
\begin{align*}
  \mathscr L( v, \theta, q ) &=
  \E_{h \sim q} \bigl[ \log p(h,v) \bigr] + H(q) \\
  &= \log p(v) - D_{\text{KL}} \bigl( q(h|v) \| p(h|v) \bigr)
  \leq \log p
\end{align*}
where $v$ are the observed variables,
$h$ the latent variables,
$q$ some distribution on $h$
and $D_{\text{KL}} \geq0$.

% Unclear explanations of EM

MAP estimation is equivalent to variational inference (VI) with $q$ Dirac.

The \emph{mean field approximation}
uses a factorized $q$,
$q(h|v) = \prod_i q( h_i | v )$
(it is easy for discrete variables but requires calculus of variations for continuous ones).

% 20. Deep generative models
\textbf{20.} Learning in a Boltzman machine
\[ E(v,h) = -
\begin{pmatrix} v \\ h \end{pmatrix} '
\begin{pmatrix} R & \tfrac12 W \\ \tfrac12 W & S \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} v \\ h \end{pmatrix} -
\begin{pmatrix} b \\ c \end{pmatrix}'
\begin{pmatrix} v \\ h \end{pmatrix}
\]
is local.
Conditional distributions for RBM are factorial and easy to compute and sample from.
\begin{align*}
  p(h|v) &= \prod_j \sigma \bigl( (2h-1) \odot (c + W'v) \bigr)_j \\
  p(v|h) &= \prod_i \sigma \bigl( (2v-1) \odot (b + Wh ) \bigr)_i
\end{align*}
A \emph{deep belief network} is a RBM with additional directed layers;
a deep Boltzman machine only has undirected connections.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.5,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt,
n/.style={circle,fill}
]
\foreach \i in {0,2,4,6,8}
  \node[n] (A-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\foreach \i in {1,3,5,7}
  \node[n] (B-\i) at (\i,1) {};
\foreach \i in {2,4,6}
  \node[n] (C-\i) at (\i,2) {};
\foreach \i in {1,3,5,7}
  \node[n] (D-\i) at (\i,3) {};
\foreach \i in {1,3,5,7}
  \foreach \j in {2,4,6}
    \draw (D-\i) -- (C-\j);
\foreach \i in {2,4,6}
  \foreach \j in {1,3,5,7}
    \draw[->] (C-\i) -- (B-\j);
\foreach \i in {1,3,5,7}
  \foreach \j in {0,2,4,6,8}
    \draw[->] (B-\i) -- (A-\j);
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.5,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt,
n/.style={circle,fill}
]
\foreach \i in {0,2,4,6,8}
  \node[n] (A-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\foreach \i in {1,3,5,7}
  \node[n] (B-\i) at (\i,1) {};
\foreach \i in {2,4,6}
  \node[n] (C-\i) at (\i,2) {};
\foreach \i in {1,3,5,7}
