\foreach \i in {1,3,5,7}
  \foreach \j in {0,2,4,6,8}
    \draw (B-\i) -- (A-\j);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

RBMs can be generalized to continuous variables:
with \emph{Gaussian-Bernoulli RBMs},
the mean (or both the mean and the variance) of the Gaussian output depends on the binary latent variables.
The \emph{mean-product of Student's $T$ distribution} (mPoT)
model replaces the Bernoulli latent variables with Gamma ones.
% There are many more RBM variants

To compute the gradient in presence of random operations,
e.g., $\partial _\mu$ or $\partial_\sigma$ in $ y \sim N(\mu, \sigma^2)$,
rewrite the model as a transformation of samples from a fixed distribution,
e.g.,
\begin{align*}
\epsilon &\sim N(0,1) \\
y &= \mu + \sigma \epsilon
\end{align*}
and consider the random Gaussian input as other inputs
(``reparametrization trick'', ``stochastic back-propagation'').
For discrete distributions, it is trickier,
but doable with reinforcement learning's \textsc{reinforce} algorithm.

\emph{Variational autoencoders} (VAE) combine a generator with an inference network.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2.5,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=1pt,
