\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{R in Finance 2018} \\
\end{book}


\textbf{Bitcoin.}
Random forests and gradient boosting can help forecast Bitcoin prices
from price, volume, users, FX, VIX and search data.

\d{Dynamic model averaging} (DMA) combines several models
(here, univariate cryptocurrency forecasts,
from stock indices, commodities, interest rates, CDS, volatility),
with time-varying weights.
Implementation in \texttt{eDMA}.

Bittrex is the 10th largest bitcoin exchange (4th in terms of number of trades or number of currencies),
with a 2\% market share 
%(\url{https://cryptocoincharts.info/markets/info});
it has a REST API and an R package.
There are almost 300 cryptocurrencies (on this exchange), but their \d{cointegration dimension} is at most 4.

The \texttt{rbtc} package lets you run a Bitcoin node from R.

\textbf{Time series models.}
The finite tick size makes prices discrete:
they can be modelled with a \d{Skellam process} (a difference of Poisson processes) or,
more generally, an integer-valued Lévy process.
An integer-valued \d{trawl process} can be sampled as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take points at random on $\RR\times[0,1]$ (L\'evy basis -- it could be a random signed measure, instead, if you are familiar with Lévy processes);
\item Progressively move the ``trawl'' (a shape, e.g., a rectangle, or an exponential) and return the number of points in it.
\end{compactitem}
The non-rectangular trawl makes changes more fleeting.

<<TrawlProcess,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=3,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Trawl process
M <- 10
N <- 100
f <- function(x) ifelse( x <= 0, exp(x), 0 )
inside <- function(x,y) sum( y <= f(x) )
x <- runif(N, 0, M)
y <- runif(N)
ts <- seq(0,M,length=1e4)
ns <- NA * ts
for( i in seq_along(ts) )
  ns[i] <- inside( x - ts[i], y )
myLag <- function(x,k=1) { 
        dn <- names(x)
        if (k > 0) {
            x <- c(rep(NA, k), x)[1:length(x)]
        } else if (k < 0) {
            k <- -k
            x <- c(x[-(1:k)], rep(NA, k))[1:length(x)]
        }
        names(x) <- dn
	x
}
i <- which( ns == myLag(ns) & ns == myLag(ns,-1) )
ns <- ns[-i]
ts <- ts[-i]
op <- par( mfrow = c(2,1), mar = c(.5,1,.5,1) )
plot(x,y, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "", type = "n")
xs <- seq(0,M, length=200)
polygon( c(xs,rev(xs)), c( f(xs-2),  rep(0,length(xs)) ), col = "pink", border = NA )
polygon( c(xs,rev(xs)), c( f(xs-6),  rep(0,length(xs)) ), col = "light blue", border = NA )
polygon( c(xs,rev(xs)), c( f(xs-10), rep(0,length(xs)) ), col = "light green", border = NA )
points(x,y, lwd = 2)
box()
plot( ts, ns, type = "s", axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "", lwd =3 )
box()
par(op)
@

Avoid the \d{Hodrick-Prescott} (HP) filter:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item It creates spurious dynamic relations (autocorrelation, predictability),
  not only in the ``trend'' component (that is intended and desirable),
  but also in the ``cycle'' (noise) one;
\item The results at the extremities of the interval are not usable --
  problematic if we want to use it on a moving or expanding window;
\item It is often estimated with a Kalman filter
\begin{align*}
    & x_t = y_t + \epsilon_t                && \text{Observation} \\
    & y_t - 2 y_{t-1} + y_{t-2} = \eta_t    && \text{Evolution}
\end{align*}    
  but the estimated smoothing parameter is at odds with common practice.
\end{compactitem}  
Instead, just estimate a linear regression
\[ y_{t+h} \sim y_t + y_{t-1} + y_{t-2} + y_{t-3} \]
(with $h=8$ for quarterly data).
% [1] Why you should never use the Hodrick-Prescott filter
%     J.D. Hamilton (2016)

The time evolution of the value-at-risk (VaR) can be modeled directly,
in an autoregressive way to account for volatility clustering, e.g., 
\[ 
  \text{VaR}_t = 
  \beta_0 + 
  \sum_i \beta_i \text{VaR}_{t-i} + 
  \sum_j \gamma_i \va{ x_{t-j} } 
\]
(or some other function of $\text{VaR}_{t-i}$, $x_{t-j}$ and exogenous variables)
and estimated by quantile regression.

The correlated idiosyncratic volatility shock model is yet another multivariate GARCH (factor) model.

A tidyverse for time series may be emerging:
\texttt{tidyquant} (stock and portfolio analysis),
\texttt{anomalize} (anomaly detection),
\texttt{tibbletime} (\texttt{collapse\_by}, \texttt{rollify}, \texttt{filter\_time}, \texttt{as\_period}),
\texttt{timek} (time series machine learning),
\texttt{sweep},
\texttt{furrr} (\texttt{future} + \texttt{purrr}),
\texttt{flyingfox} (Quantopian's Zipline Python library via \texttt{reticulate}).

The \emph{stationary bootstrap} is a block bootstrap with random block lengths.

\textbf{Robust statistics.}
% A new edition of R.D. Martin's book, \emph{Robust statistics: theory and methods}, is due later this year.
We may want to try to replace some of our location and dispersion estimators
% (e.g., the median in my \texttt{median\_per\_quantile()} function)
with those in the \texttt{RobStatTM} package:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \texttt{RobStatTM::MLocDis(x)\$mv} (location and dispersion),
\item \texttt{RobStatTM::lmrobdetMM},
\item \texttt{RobStatTM::lmrobdetDCML} (regression),
\item \texttt{RobStatTM::MultiRobv(x, type="auto")} (covariance matrix).
\end{compactitem}
Replacing the Fama-French cross-sectional regressions with robust regressions:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Confirms the relation between B/P, earnings, size and future returns;
\item Reveals a negative relation between beta and future returns;
\item Suggests interactions (especially with size) are stronger than earlier thought.
\end{compactitem}
Given a robust estimator $\theta_{\text{rob}}$,
with high breakdown point and good \emph{asymptotic efficiency}
\( \var[ \text{MLE} ] / \var[ \theta_{\text{rob}} ] \)
but poor finite-sample efficiency,
the \d{distance-constrained maximum likelihood} (DCML) estimator
shrinks it towards the maximum likelihood estimator.
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find}        & \theta \\
  \text{To minimize} & \text{KL} ( \text{data} \| f_\theta ) \text{\qquad\quad log-likelihood} \\
  \text{Such that}   & \text{KL} ( f_{\theta_{\text{rob}}} \| f_\theta ) \leq \delta
\end{array}
\]

Shrinkage estimators of the variance matrix are not robust:
instead of shrinking the sample variance matrix towards the identity,
shrink the (robust) MCD variance matrix
(the variance matrix estimated on the 50\% or 75\% of the data giving the lowest determinant),
just enough for the matrix to be well-conditioned
($\lambda_{\text{max}} / \lambda_{\text{min}} < 1000$).
Implementation in \texttt{robustbase::covMcd}, \texttt{rrcov}, \texttt{RiskPortfolios::covEstimation}.

\textbf{Error estimation.}
The \d{influence function} of an estimator $T$ on a distribution $F$ is
\[
  \text{IF}(r,T,F) =
  \left.
  \dfrac{
    d[ (1-\gamma)F + \gamma \delta_r ]
  }{
    d \gamma
  }
  \right| _{\gamma=0}
\]
The variance of the estimator can be estimated as
\[
  \var[ T(F_n) ] \approx \dfrac1n \sum \text{IF}^2 (r_t,T,F_n)
\]  
The \d{Newey-West} standard error estimator gives the standard error of the sample mean
in presence of autocorrelation
\[
  \hat \sigma_n [ \hat \mu_n ] = \dfrac1{\sqrt n} 
  \biggl[ 
    C_0^2 + 2 \sum_{k\geq1} \biggl( 1 - \dfrac kn \biggr) C_k 
  \biggr]
\]
where $C_k$ is the lag-$k$ autocovariance ($C_0$ is the variance);
the sum is often truncated to
\[
  \sum_{k=1}^m \biggl( 1 - \dfrac km \biggr) C_k,
\]
for $m \ll n$.
These results can be combined:
\[
  \var T(F_n) = E[ \text{IF}^2(r_0) ] +
   2 \sum_{k\geq1} E[ \text{IF}(r_0) \text{IF}(r_k) ].
\]
Applying this formula to get an approximate finite-sample standard error
of performance measures (value-at-risk, expected shortfall, Sharpe ratio) 
is problematic,
but it turns out to be the spectral density at frequency zero,
\[
  \var T(F_n) = S_{\text{IF}}(0),
\]
which can be estimated from the periodogram,
as the intercept of a polynomial regression with exponential noise
(the periodogram is exponentially-distributed),
with a sparsity penalty (elastic net).

To test if a risk (VaR) model is correct,
test if the \emph{probability integral transform} (PIT)
\[
  P_t = F_t( \text{Loss}_t ),
\]
\ie, the running quantile, is $U(0,1)$.
Since accuracy in the ``bad tail'' (losses) is more important, test instead
\[ H_0: \nu(P_t) \sim \nu( U(0,1) ), \]
where $\nu$ is monotonic.
Implementation in \texttt{spectralBacktest}.

The negative effects of errors in mean returns on the mean-variance efficient frontier
increase with risk tolerance and number of assets.

\textbf{Bayesian statistics.}
Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC) cannot sample from discrete variables,
but Stan is flexible and lets you implement
the forward (filtering), forward-backward (smoothing) and Viterbi (MAP) algorithms yourself.
A \d{hierarchical hidden Markov model} (HHMM) is a HMM with two types of nodes,
emitting and non-emitting, arranged in layers.
% HHMM
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.4,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt,
  emit/.style={%
      draw,
      inner sep=1pt,
      outer sep=1pt,
      text width = 1.3cm,
      text centered
  },
  non1/.style={%
    draw, ellipse,
    inner sep = 2pt,
    outer sep = 2pt
  },
  non2/.style={%
    draw, circle,
    inner sep = 6pt,
    outer sep = 2pt
  }
]
\node[non1] (top) at (2.5,2) {\footnotesize start here};
\node[non1] (bull) at (1,1) {\footnotesize bullish};
\node[non1] (bear) at (4,1) {\footnotesize bearish};
\node[emit] (bull-neg) at (-.2,0) {\footnotesize\baselineskip=0pt negative zigzag\par};
\node[emit] (bull-pos) at (1,0) {\footnotesize\baselineskip=0pt positive zigzag\par};
\node[non2] (bull-end) at (2,0) {};
\node[emit] (bear-pos) at (2.8,0) {\footnotesize\baselineskip=0pt positive zigzag\par};
\node[emit] (bear-neg) at (4,0) {\footnotesize\baselineskip=0pt negative zigzag\par};
\node[non2] (bear-end) at (5,0) {};
\tikzstyle{every path}=[thick,->]
\draw (top) -- (bull);
\draw (top) -- (bear);
\draw (bull) -- (bull-neg.north);
\draw (bull-end) -- (bull);
\draw (bear) -- (bear-pos.north);
\draw (bear-end) -- (bear);
\draw (bull-neg.270) to [out=330, in=210, looseness=1]  (bull-end.270);
\draw (bear-pos.270) to [out=330, in=210, looseness=1]  (bear-end.270);
\draw (bull.5) -- (bear.175);
\draw (bear.185) -- (bull.355);
\draw (bull-neg.10) -- (bull-pos.170);
\draw (bull-pos.190) -- (bull-neg.350);
\draw (bear-pos.10) -- (bear-neg.170);
\draw (bear-neg.190) -- (bear-pos.350);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
An \emph{input-output hidden Markov model} is an HMM whose transition and emission probabilities depend on an input (``control'') signal.
Useful features, to forecast prices with a HHMM, include the presence of a local maximum or minimum, trends ($x_{n-4} < x_{n-2} < x_{n} \wedge x_{n-3} < x_{n-1}$), volume changes ($\log(v_n/v_{n-1})$, $\log (v_{n-1}/v_{n-2})$, $\log(v_n/v_{n-2})$, $\cdot>\alpha$, $\cdot<-\alpha$, $\va\cdot\leq\alpha$).
 
Stan can fit Gaussian processes.

Jags can estimate vine copulas.

\emph{Multilevel Monte Carlo} (MLMC) combines Monte Carlo simulations with different timesteps $2^{-k} T$:
\[  E[ f_n(T) ] = E[ f_0(T) ] + \sum_{k=1}^n E[ f_k(T) - f_{k-1}(T) ]. \]

\textbf{IT.}
The \texttt{partools} package provides MPI-like distributed computing with a higher-level interface.

The \texttt{foreach} package can not only parallelize your code
via \texttt{multicore} or \texttt{parallel},
but also HPC schedulers (LSF, OpenLava, Slurm, with \texttt{foreach::registerdoFuture})
or Azure (\texttt{registerAzureParallel}).

Postgres lets you define functions ($\Gamma$),
aggregate functions (Weibull distribution fit, Shapiro-Wilk test,
\texttt{moments::skewness}, GARCH fit, etc.) and window functions (ARIMA forecast) in R.

The \texttt{hdf5r} package replaces the \texttt{h5} package presented two years ago [which did not work well].

