\textbf{Options and trading.}
Here are a few volatility investing strategies:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Long vol (VXX) when SMA(VIX/VXV, 10) > 1, short vol (XIV/SVXY) otherwise
\item Switch between the highest 83-day momentum between XIV/SVXY, ZIV, VXZ, and VXX
\item Long XIV/SVXY when SMA(VIX $-$ 10day SPY vol, 5) > 0, long VXX otherwise
\item Long XIV/SVXY when VIX/VXV < .917; long VXX when VIX/VXV > 1.083
\item Long XIV/SVXY when ratio < SMA(ratio, 60) and ratio < 1, idem for VXX, where ratio=VIX6M=VIX3M/VXMT.
\end{compactitem}

To trade variance and jump risks around macroeconomic announcements,
use long VIX positions and SPX straddles.

The present value of an uncertain stream of cash flows, for an investor with time-separable power utility,
\[ 
  \sum _{ t \geq 0 } 
  \delta^t 
  \E \biggl[ \dfrac{ X_t ^{1-\gamma} }{ 1 - \gamma } \biggr] 
\]
is (Rubinstein's model, \d{stochastic discount factor})
\[ \text{PV} = \sum_{t\geq1} \dfrac{
  \E \bigl[ X_t / ( 1 + r_t^{\text{mkt}} )^\gamma \bigr]
}{
  \E \bigl[ ( 1 + r_t^{\text{mkt}} )^{1-\gamma} \bigr]
} \]
This can help price options on illiquid assets, such as private equity.

%\texttt{blotter:::txnsim()} samples ``round turn trades'' [undefined] bound by the constraint of the ``stylized facts'' [undefined] of the observed strategy.

To build an ESG strategy:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Survey Americans to identify the most important issues (workers, customers, products, environment, community, US jobs, management, shareholders);
\item Collect 100 company-level ESG metrics (customer reviews, fines, emissions, etc.); 
\item Build a portfolio (top half in each industry, market capitalized).
\end{compactitem}

Daily fund flows depend on the Morningstar click rate (raw, trend, change) 
for some funds, even after controlling for 
Net asset, Benchmark return, Excess return, Alpha, 
Market beta, Size beta, Value beta, Momentum beta 
[but they did not control for multiple testing].

\textbf{Seasonality.} 
[Several empty talks]

\textbf{Default models.}
The \d{Q-Gaussian} distribution maximizes the Tsallis entropy
(the Gaussian maximizes the Shannon entropy);
its fatter tails lead to a more realistic Merton distance to default for investment-grade companies. 

\d{Bayesian additive regression trees} (BART) are forests of regression trees,
with a prior on tree structure -- monotonicity complicates the definition of the prior and the sampling procedure.
Monotonically-constrained BART can help forecast bankruptcies.

The \texttt{mvord} package fits models of the form
\[
  \text{rating} \sim \text{firm} + \text{rater} + \text{time}
\]
accounting for correlation between raters and autocorrelation over time.

\textbf{Graphs.}
Country modularity, in the graph of non-US companies and directors / analysts / news mentions is higher than sector modularity.
Companies sharing directors/analysts/news mentions tend to be more correlated.

\d{Stochastic block models} are an alternative to modularity for community detection;
they can be generalized to bipartite graphs. Implementation in \texttt{dbisbm}.

\textbf{Text.}
The \texttt{sentometrics} and \texttt{quanteda} packages provide lexicon-based sentiment analysis.

To estimate the sentiment (or ``tone'') of a document (10k filings) from lists of positive and negative words, one can
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item count them;
\item weigh them using tf-idf weights;
\item weigh them using weights estimated from subsequent market returns.
\end{compactitem}
\emph{Abnormal tone} is defined by analogy with abnormal returns, as the residuals of 
\[
  \text{tone} \sim \text{market tone} + \text{industry tone}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Exploring and measuring\\ non-linear correlations: copulas,\\ lightspeed transportation and clustering} \\
  G. Marti et al.
\end{book}
Many measures of correlation can be seen as a ``distance'' between the data copula and some reference copula, e.g., the Fr\'echet-Hoeffding bounds (comonotonic and counter-monotonic) or the independence copula.

Common divergences (KL, etc.) are not very relevant when dependence is high
(given two probability densities, with disjoint supports, they can just tell that the supports are disjoint, not how far apart they are).
The \d{Wasserstein distance} (optimal transport, earth mover's distance (EMD)) addresses this. 
Its exact computation is cumbersome, $O(m^3 \log m)$, 
but its entropic relaxation, the \emph{Sinkhorn distance}
(an upper bound of the EMD) 
can be computed in linear time.
The Sinkhorn distance can also be used to compute barycenters of copulas, 
or to cluster copulas ($k$-means).

The \emph{target-forget dependence coefficient}
of a copula is computed from its Sinkhorn distance to a set of target (e.g., independence) copulas $(C_k^+)_k$,
and a set of forget (e.g., Fr\'echet-Hoeffding) copulas $(C^-_\ell)_\ell$.
\[
\text{ TFDC} (C) = \dfrac{
  \min d( C^-_\ell, C ) 
}{
  \min d( C^-_\ell, C ) +  
  \min d( C^+_k, C )
}
\]

The \emph{mutual information} $\text{MI}(X,Y)$
is the Kullback-Leibler divergence between the joint distribution 
$(X,Y)$ and the corresponding independent distribution 
$p_X p_Y$, 
\[ 
  \text{MI}(X,Y) = \text{KL}( p_{XY} \| p_X p_Y ).
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Equitability, mutual information\\ and the maximal information coefficient} \\
  J.B. Kinney and G.S. Atwal
\end{book}
Mutual information satisfies the \emph{data processing inequality}: 
$ I(X;Z) \leq I(Y;Z)$ 
if $X \leftrightarrow Y \leftrightarrow Z$ is a Markov chain
(\ie, information is lost, never gained, when transmitted through a noisy channel).


A dependence measure $D(X;Y)$ is \emph{self-equitable}
if $D[X;Y] = D[f(X);Y]$
whenever 
$X \leftrightarrow f(X) \leftrightarrow Y$ is a Markov chain
(in particular, if $f$ is bijective).

Mutual information is self-equitable, MIC is not.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A robust-equitable copula dependence measure for feature selection} \\
  Y. Chang et al. (2016)
\end{book}
A dependence measure $D(X;Y)$ is \emph{robust equitable}
if $D(X;Y) = p$ 
when $(X,Y)$ has copula 
$p C + (1-p) \Pi$, where $C$ is a singular copula and $\Pi$ the independence copula
(self-equitability deals with additive noise rather than mixture noise;
both try to be agnostic to the type of relation they detect).

The \emph{robust copula dependence} $\text{CD}_1$ is the $L^1$ distance 
between the copula density and the uniform density 
\[ \text{CD}_\alpha = \iint_{[0,1]^2} \va{ c(u,v) - 1 } ^\alpha du dv;
\]
it can be estimated with a kernel density estimator.
It is robust equitable; mutual information and $\text{CD}_2$ are not.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Copula correlation:\\ an equitable dependence measure\\ and extension of Pearson's correlation} \\
  A.A. Ding and Y. Li 
\end{book}
More details on the copula correlation (aka robust copula dependence),
with comparisons with other measures of dependence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sinkhorn distances: lightspeed computation\\ of optimal transportation distances} \\
  M. Cuturi (2013)
\end{book}
The \emph{optimal transport distance} (earth mover's distance (EMD), 
Wasserstein distance)between two histograms $r,c \in \Delta_d$,
wrt a cost matrix $M \in \RR^{d\times d}$, is 
\[ d(r,c) = \min_{ p \in U(r,c) } \langle P, \rangle M \]
where 
the transportation polytope
$U(r,c)$ is the set of joint probabilities whose margins are $r$ and $c$, 
\[ U(r,c) = \{ \, P \in \RR_+^{d \times d} \ : \ P \mathbf 1 _d = r , \ P' \mathbf 1 = c \. \} \]

The \d{Sinkhorn distance} only considers the joint probabilities 
close to the independence one $rc'$
\begin{align*}
  d_\alpha(r,c) &= \min\limits_{ P \in U_\alpha(r,c) } \langle P, M \rangle \\
  U_\alpha(r,c) &= \{ \, P \in U(r,c) \ :\ \text{KL}( P \| rc' ) \leq \alpha \, \}.
\end{align*}
The dual problem (obtained by replacing the hard constraint with a penalty)
can be solved efficiently with a fixed point algorithm.

In R: \texttt{Barycenter::SinkhornDistance} (buggy).

% TODO: Picture?

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast computation of Wasserstein barycenters} \\
  M. Cuturi and A. Doucet (2014)
\end{book}
The Sinkhorn distance (entropic regularization of the Wasserstein distance)
makes the computation of Wasserstein barycenters efficient.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The randomized dependence coefficient} \\
  D. Lopez-Paz et al.
\end{book}
The Hirschfeld-Gebelein-R\'enyi \d{maximum correlation coefficient} is 
\[ \text{hgr}(X,Y) = \sup_{f,g} \cor( fX, gY ). \]
To estimate it, uniformize $X$ and $Y$,
transform them in several random ways (e.g., $\cos(w'X+b)$)
and compute the corresponding 
\emph{canonical correlation}
(\ie, the maximum correlation between a linear combination of the transforms of $X$ and one of those of $Y$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating optimal transformations\\ for multiple regression and correlation} \\
  L. Breiman and J.H. Friedman (1985)
\end{book}
The \emph{alternating conditional expectation} (ACE) algorithm 
looks for transformations $\phi$, $\theta$ of random variables $X$, $Y$
maximizing the correlation $\cor( \phi X, \theta Y )$
(not unlike generalized additive models).
\begin{align*}
&\text{While } E[ \theta Y - \phi X ]^2 \text{ decreases:} \\
&\quad \phi(X) \leftarrow E[ \theta(Y) | X ] \\
&\quad \theta(Y) \leftarrow \dfrac{ 
  E[ \phi(X) | Y ]
}{ \n{
  E[ \phi(X) | Y ]
  }}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A kernel two-sample test} \\
  A. Gretton et al. (2012)
\end{book}
A \emph{reproducing kernel Hilbert space} (RKHS) 
is  Hilbert space of functions $X \to \RR$ (for some unspecified 
scalar product)
such that the evaluation functions
\[
  \text{ev}_x : \left\{ 
  \begin{array}{rcl}
    \mathscr H & \longrightarrow & \RR \\
    f & \longmapsto & f(x)
  \end{array}
  \right. 
\]
be continuous;
they are then (Riesz) of the form 
$f(x) = \langle f, k(x,\cdot) \rangle$,
for some function (``kernel'') $k$.
Conversely, a symmetric positive definite kernel $k$ on $X$ defines a unique RKHS.

For universal RKHS ($k$ continuous and $\mathscr H$ dense in $\mathscr C^0_b$ for $L^\infty$),
each probability distribution $p$ has a \emph{mean embedding}
\[ \E_{x \sim p } [ f(x) ] = \langle f , \mu_p \rangle, \]
and the \d{maximum mean discrepancy} (MMD) distance is the distance between those embeddings.
\[
  \text{MMD}(p,q) = \sup_{ \n f \leq 1 } 
  \E_{ x \sim p } f(x) - \E_{ y \sim q } f(X) 
  = \n{ \mu_p - \mu_q }
\]
The plugin estimator of $\text{MMD}^2$ (from the kernel) 
is biased, but it is easy to obtain an unbiased one, 
and even a linear-complexity one
\begin{align*}
  \text{MMD}^2 &= \dfrac 1{m/2} \sum_1^{m/2} h( z_{2i-1} , z_{2i} ) \\
  h(z_i, z_j ) &= k(x_i, x_j ) + k( y_1, y_j ) 
  - k(x_i, y_j) - k(x_j, y_i);
\end{align*}
those estimators lead to statistical tests to determine if two samples come from the same distribution.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal copula transport\\ for clustering multivariate time series} \\
  G. Marti et al.
\end{book}
Replace $d$-dimensional time series by their copulas,
and use the earth mover's distance to cluster them.

Define a measure of dependence as the relative distance between 
the independence copula and one or more target copulas;
this also reveals which type of dependence was detected.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2.5,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=0pt, outer sep=0pt]
\node[draw,thick] (I) at (0,0) {%
  \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
  \fill[lightgray] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (1,1) -- (0,1) -- cycle;
  \end{tikzpicture}
};
\node[draw] (D) at (1,0) {%
