\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A proposal of a methodological framework\\ with experimental guidelines to investigate clustering stability on financial time series} \\
  G. Marti et al.
\end{book}
To check the stability of a clustering algorithm for time series,
look at how the \emph{adjusted Rand index} (ARI) 
changes under perturbations: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sliding windows of different sizes;
\item Odd vs even observations;
\item Economic regime (for some regime detection algorithm);
\item Whether market returns are in the interquartile range or not;
\item Different sampling frequencies;
\item Different universes;
\item For fixed income: different maturities, or all the maturities together (Hellinger distance between the probability distribution functions 
$\partial \text{PD} / \partial t$).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On clustering financial time series: a need for distances between dependent random variables} \\
  G. Marti et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{hierarchical clustering correlation matrix estimator}
is consistent for the 
\emph{hierarchical correlation block model} (HCBM).

% TODO: Nice plot.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cluster analysis for portfolio optimization} \\
  V. Tola et al.
\end{book}
Original paper on the ultrametric correlation matrix from hierarchical clustering.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Toward a generic representation of random variables for machine learning} \\
  G. Marti et al.
\end{book}
The $L^2$ distance between random variables
$d^2(X,Y) = E(X-Y)^2$
mixes dependence and distribution information:
it is often unsuitable to cluster iid sequences.
Instead, use a convex combination of the \emph{correlation distance} 
(of the copula $(X,Y)$, for the dependence side)
and of the \emph{Hellinger distance}
(between the marginals, for the distribution side).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Copula-based kernel dependency measures} \\
  B. P\'oczos et al. (2012)
\end{book}
The \d{maximum mean discrepancy} (MMD) between two probability distributions 
$P$, $Q$ is 
\[ M = \sup_{ f \in \mathscr F } 
\E_{ X \sim P } [f(X)] -
\E_{ Y \sim Q } [f(Y)]
\]
where $\mathscr F$ is the unit ball in a universal reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS)
(universal means dense in $\mathscr C_b^0$, e.g., with the Gaussian or Laplace kernels);
$M^2$ can be estimated as 
\[
  \E_{ X,X' \sim P } [ k(X,X') ] 
  - 2 \E_{\substack{ X \sim P \\ Y \sim Q }} [ k(X,Y) ]
  + \E_{ Y,Y' \sim Q } [ k(Y,Y') ] .
\]

The \emph{dependence} $I^2$ between random variables $X_1,\dots,X_d$
is the MMD distance 
between their joint copula and the uniform distribution;
it can be estimated as 
\[
\dfrac1{m(m-1)}
\sum_{i,j} k(Z_i,Z_j) + k(U_i,U_j) - k(Z_i,U_j) - k(U_i,Z_j)
\]
or 
\[
\dfrac1{m^2} \sum_{ij} k(Z_i,Z_j) 
+ \dfrac1{n^2} \sum k(U_i,U_j) 
- \dfrac2{mn} \sum k(Z_i,U_j)
\]
where $U_i \sim U(0,1)^d$ 
and $Z_i$ is the empirical copula of the data $X_i$.

This dependence measure can be used for \emph{feature selection}: 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & S \subset \llbracket 1, d \rrbracket \\
  \text{To minimize} & \va S \\
  \text{And maximize} & I(X_s \union Y)
\end{array}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal transport vs Fisher-Rao distance\\ between copulas\\ for clustering multivariate time series} \\
  G. Marti et al.
\end{book}
The Fisher-Rao metric and its tractable approximations
(Kullback-Leibler and other divergences -- computing geodesics requires solving ODEs)
are unsuitable to compute distances between copulas, especially when the dependence is high:
the Gaussian copula with correlation 0.99 appears closer to that with correlation 0.50 than to that with correlation 0.9999 -- 
the monotonic copula, $\rho=1$, does not have a density and is not on the manifold -- it is at infinity.
The Wasserstein distance is preferable.

Cluster multivariate time series by replacing them with their copulas.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Autoregressive convolutional neural networks for asynchronous time series} \\
  M. Bi\'nkowski et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Irregular time series can be made regular by considering 
$y_n = (x_{t_n}, t_n - t_{n-1})$;
for multivariate asynchronous time series
(e.g., quotes for the same asset from different market makers),
put all the values together and add a 1-hot encoding of the source. 

The significance-offset CNN (SOCNN)
\[ 
  x_n = \sum_{m=1} ^ M W_{\cdot,m} \odot [ x_{n-m} + \epsilon(x_{n-m}) ] \odot
  \sigma S_{\cdot,m} ( x_{n-1},\dots,x_{n-m})
\]
where $\epsilon$ is dense, shallow, $S$ convolutional and $\sigma$ the softmax,
combines autoregression (for $\sigma=1$, $\epsilon=0$,
the recent observations are remembered and used as is)
and gated RNN (older observations are summarized, in a nonlinear way).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hausdorff clustering of financial time series} \\
  N. Basalto et al. (2005)
\end{book}
The \d{Hausdorff distance} gives an alternative to single and complete linkage clustering.
\begin{align*}
  d(A,B) &= \inf_{a \in A} \sup_{b \in B} d(a,b) \\
  d(A,B) &= \sup_{a \in A} \sup_{b \in B} d(a,b) \\
  d(A,B) &= \max \bigl\{ 
  \sup_{a \in A} \inf_{b \in B} d(a,b) \\
  & \hphantom{= \max \bigl\{ {}}
  \sup_{b \in B} \inf_{a \in A} d(a,b) \bigr\}
\end{align*}
One could also switch Sup and Inf (minimax clustering).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hierarchical clustering with prototypes\\ via minimax linkage} \\
  J. Bien and R. Tibshirani (2011)
\end{book}
The \emph{minimax radius} of a cluster $C$ is 
\[ r(C) = \min_{x \in C} \max_{y \in C} d(x,y) ; \]
the minimizer is a \emph{prototype} of $C$.
\emph{Minimax linkage} hierarchical clustering uses 
$d(A,B) = r(A\union B)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HCMapper: an interactive visualization tool\\ to compare partition-based flat clustering extracted from pairs of dendrograms} \\
  G. Marti et al. 
\end{book}
Compare the partitions implied by two dendrograms with a \emph{Sankey diagram} (D3) going from the root of the first one, to the leaves, to the root of the second one, displaying the size of the intersections.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{NetGAN: generating graphs\\ via random walks} \\
  A. Bojchevski et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Consider random walks of length $T$ on a graph as sequences 
and train a Wasserstein GAN on them: 
stop training when the (validation set) link prediction performance drops,
or when the edge overlap between the generated graph and the data reaches a user-specified threshold.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometry score: a method for comparing generative adversarial networks} \\
  V. Khrulkov and I. Oseledets (2018)
\end{book}
To assess the quality of a GAN, compare the data manifold and the generated manifold using: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The Wasserstein distance between the birth-death diagrams;
\item The distance between the persistence landscapes;
\item The proportion of time $\beta_1=i$ [they do not seem to notice this discards the persistence].
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unsupervised representation learning with deep convolutional generative adversarial networks} \\
  A. Radford et al. (2016)
\end{book}
To train deep convolutional GANs (DCGAN), 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Replace the pooling layers with strided convolutions;
\item Use batch normalization;
\item Remove the fully-connected layers;
\item Use ReLU in the generator (and tanh for the output)
  and leaky ReLU in the discriminator.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{StackGAN:\\ text to photo-realistic image synthesis\\ with stacked generative adversarial networks} \\
  H. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
To generate high-resolution images from text descriptions using GANs,
stack two of them.
If there is not enough training text data, 
add noise to the sentence embedding.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{AlignedReID: surpassing human-level performance in person re-identification} \\
  X. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
To detect of two pictures (from surveillance cameras, to track visitors in a mall) are of the same person,
compute both global and local features, 
where the local features are from horizontal bands in the image,
and are aligned between the two pictures.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Re-ranking person re-identification\\ with $k$-reciprocal encoding} \\
  Z. Zhong et al.
\end{book}
Retrieval systems return a ranking, but do not always use all the information available:
in particular, true positives are more similar among themselves.
Replace the original distance with a linear combination of the 
original distance and the (weighted) Jaccard distance
between the set of $k$-reciprocal neighbours 
($x$ and $y$ are \emph{$k$-reciprocal neighbours}
if each is among the $k$ nearest neighbours of the other),
which can be computed on binary vectors,
with the $1$s replaced with $e^{-d}$, where $d$ is the distance to the probe.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep mutual learning} \\
  Y. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
Model distillation first trains a deep neural net on data,
then a shallow one to mimic it.

\emph{Deep mutual learning} uses an ensemble of shallow networks, 
trained with two losses,
a supervised one, 
and a mimicry one.

%% 2018-08-15
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep learning}\\
  A. Ng (Coursera, 2017)
\end{book}

% Dev/test sets; bias and variance

\textbf{1.}
With big data, the dev and test sets can be much smaller: 
10,000 observations is more than enough, but that
can be less than 1\% of the data.

High bias is bad performance on the training data; 
high variance is bad performance on the test set.

Early stopping is a crutch to prevent overfitting: 
it mixes two objectives, reducing bias (improving in-sample performance) 
and reducing variance (avoiding overfitting) -- 
other regularization methods (penalties, dropout, etc.) give more control on the bias-variance tradeoff.

Rather than opposing bias and variance, we should oppose 
``avoidable bias'' (or ``Bayes error'': the difference between the error 
and the lowest possible error, estimated, e.g., with the human-level error) 
and variance.

If the training and dev sets do not come from the same distribution, 
the difference in performance can come 
either from an excessive variance or the difference itself. 
To disentangle those effects, use a ``training-dev set'' --
another dev set, carved out of the training set, to measure variance 
(i.e., whether the model generalizes to unseen data from the same distribution).

%

\textbf{2.}
Auto-encoders can be used for anomaly detection: for anomalies (inputs from a never-seen class), 
the reconstruction error is higher.

End-to-end learning needs more data (10 to 100 times more) than traditional multi-step pipelines. 
Do not use if you have more data for the individual steps than for the whole pipeline.

ResNets can easily learn the identity function (while deep ``plain'' nets cannot): 
that is why we can make them as deep as possible. 
We can also increase their depth by adding new residual layers, initialized as the identity, in the middle.

% Images, CNNs

\textbf{3.}
For images, prefer Inception-v4 -- VGG16 and VGG19 are larger and have a slightly lower accuracy.

In a CNN, the size progressively decreases, but the number of channels increases.

Average pooling is rarely used, except sometimes at the end, 
to reduce the size of the final result, 
e.g., $7 \times 7 \times 1000 \rightarrow 1 \times 1 \times 1000$. 
Max-pooling is more common.

$1 \times 1$ convolution (aka ``network in network'')
is actually a fully-connected network acting on each pixel (the same network for each pixel). 
(It should not be written $1 \times 1$ but $1 \times 1 \times n$,
where $n$ is the number of input channels.)
There are usually several of them: 
they are specified by a $1 \times 1 \times n \times m$ tensor, 
where $m$ is the number of filters, \ie, the number of output channels. 
Those \emph{bottleneck layers} ($1 \times 1$ convolutions)
are often used to reduce the number of channels before a convolution, 
to reduce the number of operations (and parameters). 
For instance, the inception units have a $1 \times 1$ convolution before their $5 \times 5$ convolutions.

If you do not know if a $1 \times 1$, $3 \times 3$, $5 \times 5$ convolution 
or a maxpooling operation is better, 
you can do them all, and concatenate the results 
(inception module: in Keras, \texttt{merge([a,b,c,d],mode='concat')}).
To reduce the computations, you may want to first reduce the number of layers 
with a $1 \times 1$ convolution. 
Inception modules also have a maxpool operation (which does not change the number of channels): 
the $1 \times 1$ convolution comes after.
The inception network also has a few side outputs, trying to forecast the same output -- 
this helps learning and has a regularizing effect.

To recognize faces with neural nets, 
feed the neural network pairs of images (faces, extracted from wider images) 
and have it predict if the two images are of the same person.

% Images (again)

To recognize faces, learn a dissimilarity function 
$d(\text{img}_1,\text{img}_2)$
(siamese network -- deepface). 

The \emph{triplet loss} (facenet)
tries to enforce $d(x_i, x_j) - d(x_i,x_k) + \alpha \leq 0$ 
(with a margin, $\alpha$, as for SVMs),
using the positive part of the lhs as loss. 
Choose triplets that are hard to train on; train on 1m to 100m images.

For object localization, have the convnet output: 
a boolean indicating if an object is present, 
a 1-hot encoding of the object class, 
the coordinates of the center of the object bounding box, 
its width and height (if no object is present, the loss function ignores the
predicted class and bbox).

For object detection (there can be several objects), use a sliding window. 
It can be implemented as a single convolutional neural net 
(keep the same weights, just change the input size -- and rewrite the FC layer as a convolutional one, 
outputing a $1 \times 1 \times 1$ tensor).

YOLO is similar, with no overlap between the windows, 
and a localization network (not just a classification one).

For object detection, the ratio of intersection over union (IoU) is a possible loss function.

To avoid detecting the same object in nearby locations, 
keep the object with the largest object presence probability (non-max suppression),
discard the detected objects with a large overlap (it is the same object), 
and iterate with the remaining (different) objects;
do this independently for each class.

To deal with overlapping objects, predict the presence of several objects 
with different bbox aspect ratios
(anchor boxes): if there are $k$ aspect ratios, the output is $k$ times larger.

Do not run the object detection on the whole region but only on ``promising'' regions
(R-CNN)
identified as ``blobs'' by some segmentation algorithm (classical or CNN  -- ``faster R-CNN'').

To do well in ML competitions 
use ensembling, and multi-crop at test time
(useless in production).

% RNN

\textbf{4.}
The \emph{BLEU} (bilingual evaluation understudy) score, for machine translation,
counts the number of 1-, 2- and 3-grams in the computer-generated sentence 
that are also present in one of the reference translations; 
it also includes a brevity penalty.

When generating a sequence with an RNN, one needs to select words one by one. 
The greedy algorithm is suboptimal -- it will favour common words. 
\emph{Beam search} takes the $b$ best words at each step. 
Use $b=10$ in production, $b=100$ to 10,000 for research.

Beam search is biased towards shorter sentences:
use the average instead of the sum of the conditional log-probabilities of the successive words
(length normalization).

To analyze the error, 
compare the score of a correct (human-generated) sentence 
with that of the sentence actually generated, 
to check if the problem comes from beam search (increase $b$) or the RNN.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-convex multi-objective optimization}\\
  P.M. Pardalos et al. (2017)
\end{book}

A multiobjective optimization problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \\
  \text{To minimize} & f_1(x), \dots, f_n(x) \\
  \text{Such that} & g(x) \geq 0 
\end{array}
\]
asks for the \emph{Pareto set} 
(non-dominated solutions -- the Pareto \emph{front} is the set of corresponding values).
For convex problems, local Pareto optimality implies global optimality,
and the solutions are the minimizers of $\sum \alpha_i f_i$ for some $\alpha$,
for various values of $\alpha$.

\d{Scalarization} replaces the objective functions with a single function
(this need not give all solutions, and may also produce non-solutions):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Weighted sum: $ \sum w_i f_i(x) $ (for convex problems);
\item Weighted sum after transformation into a convex problem;
\item Tchebychev: minimize $ \max_i w_i ( f_i(x) - u_i )$, 
  where $u$ is a utopian point, $u_i < \min f_i$;
  the problem can be reformulated as 
  \[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{x,t} t \text{ such that } w_i \bigl( f_i(x) - u_i \bigr) \leq t, \]
  and one can add a penalty for the Manhatan distance to the utopian point;
\item Achievement scalarization: idem, but with an arbitrary point instead of a utopian one;
\item Minimize only one objective and use the others as constraints;
the constraints may combine several objective functions 
(normalized normal constraint, normal boundary intersection);
\item Pascoletti and Serafini Scalarization
  \[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{t,x} t \text{ such that } u + tv - f(x) \geq 0 \]
where $u$ and $v$ are arbitrary vectors.
\end{compactitem}
The parameters in those methods can rarely be chosen beforehand: select them adaptively -- 
for instance, with two objectives, and weights $(w_1,w_2)$ as parameters, 
start with uniformly-distributed weights, and select which interval to refine/subdivide/bisect.

The bi-objective shortest path problem on a graph (with two weightings) 
can be reduced to the 0-1 knapsack problem: it is NP-complete.
The size of an $\epsilon$-solution is polynomial,
but it may be exponential in the number of objectives.

Global optimization methods include 
Bayesian optimization (under other names: P-algorithm, $\pi$-algorithm,
efficient global optimization (EGO), kriging)
and branch-and-bound 
(under a Lipschitz assumption, one can find lower bounds of the objectives 
in each region, and progressively refine promising regions -- 
probabilistic variants use extreme value theory to estimate the minimum in each region).

% The applications are all at least 15 years old, and the references are to pay-walled papers.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generative adversarial nets} \\
  I.J. Goodfellow et al.
\end{book}
Generative models can be estimated via an adversarial process, 
corresponding to a minimax two-player game, 
\[
  \min_G \max_D 
  \E_{ x \sim \text{Data} } 
  \bigl[ \log D(x) \bigr] 
  + 
  \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } 
  \Bigl[ 
  \log \bigl( 1 - D(G(z)) \bigr)
  \Bigr].
\]
Replacing $\log(1-DGz)$
with $-\log (DGz)$ helps learning early on.

The objective function is related to the Jensen-Shannon metric: 
\begin{align*}
  &\E_{ x \sim \text{Data} } 
  \bigl[ \log D(x) \bigr] 
  -
  \E_{ z \sim \text{noise} } 
  \Bigl[ 
  \log D\bigl(G(z)\bigr)
  \Bigr]
	\leq \\
	&\hspace*{3cm}
	2 \text{JS} \bigl( \text{Data}, G(\text{noise}) \bigr) - 2 \log 2
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Image-to-image translation with conditional adversarial networks} \\
  P. Isola et al. 
\end{book}
The adversarial approach of GANs
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=3,yscale=.6,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
\node (R) at (0,1) {random};
\node (F) at (1,1) {fake data};
\node (D) at (1,0) {real data};
\draw[->] (R) -- (F) node[auto,pos=.5] {\footnotesize generator};
\draw[->] (F.east) -- (1.3,.5) -- node[auto,pos=.5] {\footnotesize discriminator} (2,.5);
\draw[->] (D.east) -- (1.3,.5) -- (2,.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
can be merged with autoencoders (with skip-connections)
for image translation.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2.65,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
\node (I) at (0,1) {input};
\node[text width = 2cm, text centered] (R) at (1,1) {latent \goodbreak representation};
\node (F) at (2,1) {output};
\node[text width = 1.2cm, text centered] (D) at (2,0) {desired output};
\draw[->] (I) -- (R) node[auto,pos=.5] {\footnotesize encoder};
\draw[->] (R) -- (F) node[auto,pos=.5] {\footnotesize decoder};
\draw[->] (F.east) -- (2.3,.5) -- node[auto,pos=.5] {\footnotesize discriminator} node[below,pos=.5] {\footnotesize \ie, loss} (3,.5);
\draw[->] (D.east) -- (2.3,.5) -- (3,.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wasserstein GAN} \\
  M. Arjovsky et al.
\end{book}
The Wasserstein distance is more relevant than the Kullback-Leibler divergence
for distributions supported by low-dimensional manifolds: KL just informs us that the supports are disjoint, while Wasserstein also tells us how far apart they are.
\begin{align*}
  \text{TV}(P,Q) &= \sup_A \va{ P[A] - Q[A] } \\
  \text{KL}(P,Q) &= \int p \log \dfrac pq \\
  \text{JS}(P,Q) &= 
  \text{KL} \biggl( P, \dfrac{P+Q}2 \biggr) + 
  \text{KL} \biggl( Q, \dfrac{P+Q}2 \biggr) \\
  W(P,Q) &= \sup_{\substack{\gamma \\ \text{pr}_1 \gamma = P \\ \text{pr}_2 \gamma = Q}} \E_{ (x,y) \sim \gamma } \bigl[ \n{x-y} \bigr]
\end{align*}

If $X$ and $Z$ and random variables, and $g_\theta(z)$ is continuous in $\theta$, then $W( g\theta(Z), X )$ is continuous in $\theta$;
if $g$ is locally Lipschitz, then $W$ is differentiable almost everywhere.

The Wasserstein distance is weaker (it has more converging sequences): 
on compact spaces, 

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.2,yscale=.8,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
\node (KL) at (0,1) {KL};
\node (JS) at (1,1) {JS};
\node (TV) at (1,0) {TV};
\node (W)  at (2,1) {W};
\node (D)  at (2,0) {$\mathscr D$.};
\draw[->] (KL) -- (JS);
\draw[<->] (JS) -- (TV);
\draw[->] (JS) -- (W);
\draw[<->] (W) -- (D);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

The Kantorovich-Rubinstein duality
\[ 
W(P,Q) = \sup_{ \n{f}_{\text{Lipschitz}} \leq 1 }
\E_{ x \sim P } \bigl[ f(x) \bigr] - 
\E_{ x \sim Q } \bigl[ f(x) \bigr] 
\]
leads to a GAN-like optimization problem
\[
\min_G \max_D 
\E_{ x \sim \text{Data} } \bigl[ D(x) \bigr] - 
\E_{ z \sim \text{Noise} } \bigl[ D\bigl(G(z)\bigr) \bigr] 
\]
where the discriminator $D$ should be 1-Lipschitz
(otherwise, the objective is unbounded); 
this can be enforced, 
for instance, by using neural nets with clipped weights.

Contrary to traditional GANs,
the gradient remains meaningful: the discriminator can be trained to optimality,
and the corresponding Wasserstein distance can help monitor convergence.
WGANs do not seem to suffer from mode collapse, 
but they are unstable when trained with momentum -- prefer RMSProp.

The same idea can be used for \emph{integral probability metrics} (IPM): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bounded functions give the total variation (TV) distance and energy-based GANs (EBGAN);
\item The $L^\infty$ ball in a reproducible kernel Hilbert space 
$\mathscr H = \{ k(x, \cdot) : x \in \mathcal X \}$
for some kernel $k: \mathcal X \times \mathcal X \to \RR$
gives the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) metric and 
generative moment matching networks (GMMN).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved training of Wasserstein GANs} \\
  I. Gulrajani et al.
\end{book}
Replace the weight clipping with a penalty on the gradient,
ensuring its norm is close to 1 (rather than below~1)
on straight lines between the data and the generated data,
\[ 
  \lambda 
  %\E_{ x \in \text{Conv}( \text{Data}, G(\text{Noise}) ) }
  \E\nolimits_x
  \bigl(
  %\n{ \left. \nabla \! D \right|_x } - 1
  \bigl\| \left. \nabla \! D \right|_x \bigr\| - 1
  \bigr)^2,
  \qquad \lambda = 10.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fully convolutional networks\\ for semantic segmentation} \\
  J. Long et al.
\end{book}
To turn a classification network into a segmentation one,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a classifier;
\item Replace the fully-connected layers with $1\times1$ convolutions;
\item Add up-sampling and skip-layer connections.
\end{compactitem}
\[
