\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tiled convolutional neural networks} \\
  Q.V. Le et al. (2010)
\end{book}
A \d{topological independent component analysis} (TICA) network
is a 2-layer network whose first layer weights 
are local and learned,
whose second layer is a pooling layer, 
with square and square root activations respectively,
loss function 
\[
\sum_{ t \in \text{Training} }
\sum_{ i \in \text{Output} }
\sqrt{
  \sum_{k \in \text{Hidden} }
  \Biggl(
    \sum_{ i \in \text{Input} }
    w_{kj} x_{jt}
  \Biggr) ^ {\!\!2\mathstrut}
}
\]
and an orthogonality constraint $WW'=I$.

% TICA
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.4,yscale=.6,>=stealth,inner sep=2pt, outer sep = 1pt]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6} 
  \node[thick,draw,circle] (A-\i) at (\i,0) {};
\foreach \i in {1,2,3,4,5} 
  \node[thick,draw,circle] (B-\i) at (\i,1) {};
\foreach \i in {2,3,4} 
  \node[thick,draw,circle] (C-\i) at (\i,2) {};
  
\foreach \i in {1,2,3,4,5} 
  \draw (A-\i) -- (B-\i); 
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \j using int(\i+1)] \i in {1,2,3,4,5} 
  \draw (A-\j) -- (B-\i); 
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \j using int(\i-1)] \i in {1,2,3,4,5} 
  \draw (A-\j) -- (B-\i); 
  
\foreach \i in {2,3,4} 
  \draw (B-\i) -- (C-\i); 
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \j using int(\i+1)] \i in {2,3,4} 
  \draw (B-\j) -- (C-\i); 
\foreach[evaluate=\i as \j using int(\i-1)] \i in {2,3,4} 
  \draw (B-\j) -- (C-\i); 

\node[anchor = west] at (-3.5,0)  {\footnotesize input};
\node[anchor = west] at (-3.5,1)  {\footnotesize square};
\node[anchor = west] at (-3.5,2)  {\footnotesize square root};
\node[anchor = west] at (5.5,.7)  {\footnotesize learned: $w$};
\node[anchor = west] at (4.5,1.7) {\footnotesize pooling};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

The hidden layer is a sparse representation of the data.

One can obtain an over-complete representation of the data (hidden layer larger than the input)
by using local receptive fields
(each hidden node only depends on a few input coordinates)
and imposing orthogonality only for hidden nodes sharing the same input.

In a \emph{tiled convolutional neural net},
hidden units $k$ steps away 
share their weights
(for a CNN, $k=1$).
They can be pre-trained with TICA,
and capture invariances beyond translation (rotation, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A closed-form solution\\ to photorealistic image stylization} \\
  Y. Li et al.
\end{book}
For style transfer, start with an autoencoder with 
unpooling layers

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.5,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=2pt, outer sep = 1pt]
\node[draw,text height=1cm] (A1) at (0,0) {};
\node[draw,text height=6mm] (B1) at (1,0) {};
\node[draw,text height=1mm] (C) at (2,0) {};
\node[draw,text height=6mm] (B2) at (3,0) {};
\node[draw,text height=1cm] (A2) at (4,0) {};
\draw[->] (A1) -- (B1);
\draw[->] (B1) -- (C);
\draw[->] (C) -- (B2);
\draw[->] (B2) -- (A2);
\draw[->] ([yshift=-1mm]A1.north east) -- ([yshift=-1mm]A2.north west);
\draw[->] ([yshift=-1mm]B1.north east) -- ([yshift=-1mm]B2.north west);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

and VGG19 as the encoding part,
apply a \emph{whitening and colouring transform} (WCT) to the VGG features 
\begin{align*}
 & H_c \mapsto H = P_s P_c H_c \\
 & P_c = E_c \Lambda_c^{-1/2} E_c \\
 & P_s = E_s \Lambda_s^{+1/2} E_s 
\end{align*}
where $\Lambda_c$, $\Lambda_s$ are the eigenvalues of 
$H_cH_c'$ and $H_sH_s'$ 
and $E_c$, $E_s$ their eigenvectors,
which makes the correlations of the transformed features $HH'$ 
match the style ones, $H_s H_s'$,
and smooth the result 
\[
\argmin_r \sum_{ij} w_{ij} \n{ 
\dfrac{ r_i }{ \sqrt{ w_{i\bigcdot} ' \mathbf 1 } } - 
\dfrac{ r_j }{ \sqrt{ \smash{w_{j\bigcdot} '}\vphantom{w_i'} \mathbf 1 } }
}^2 + \lambda \sum_i \n{ r_i - y_i }^2
\]
where $y$ is the WCT image, $r$ the final image,
and $w_{ij}$ the \emph{matting affinity} of pixels $i$ and $j$
(based on the means and variances of the pixels in a local window) --
it is a quadratic optimization problem, with a closed form solution.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Metric learning\\ with adaptive density discrimination} \\
  O. Rippel et al. (2016)
\end{book}
To help separate the classes in distance metric learning (DML),
do not only use a triplet loss,
model the distribution of each class in the new space and use a \emph{magnet loss} to pull them apart.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pixel recurrent neural networks} \\
  A. van den Oord et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Recurrent neural networks can be generalized to 2-dimensional structures -- 
for instance, a bidiagonal bi-LSTM combines two grids of LSTMs, one going right and down, the other left and down.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using natural language processing techniques for stock return predictions} \\
  M.L. Chew et al. (2017)
\end{book}
% (Student project)
Many studies use a vector embedding and a CNN to forecast post-event price movements [one could also use a bidirectional LSTM with attention, instead of a CNN].
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use the Stanford NER tool in NLTK to identify company names in news headlines;
\item Heuristically map them to tickers
(for each company, build a list of possible names, but remove frequent words);
\item Infer the structure of the sentence with 
\texttt{textacy} (Spacy) of StanfordPOStagger;
\item Compute Glove embeddings of verb-object pairs;
\item Cluster them into 100 event types.
\end{compactitem}
% [No deep learning, except in the pre-trained vector embeddings.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An interpretable\\ and sparse neural network model\\ for nonlinear Granger causality discovery} \\
  A. Tank et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Adding a group lasso (or a hierarchical lasso) penalty to the first layer weights of a neural net 
$x_{i,t} \sim x_{\bigcdot,<t}$
can help detect (nonlinear) causality and select the number of lags.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Clustering correlated, sparse data streams\\ to estimate a localized housing price} \\
  Y. Ren et al.
\end{book}
House prices can be modeled at the census level.
\begin{align*}
g_t &: \text{global market trend} \\
x_{it} &: \text{value of census tract $i$} \\
y_{it\ell} &: \text{value of house $\ell$} \\
U_\ell &: \text{hedonics (house features)} \\
x_{it} &= g_t + a_i ( x_{i,t-1} - g_{t-1} ) + \epsilon_{it} \\
y_{it\ell} &= x_{it} + f_i(U\ell) + \eta_{it\ell} \\
\epsilon &\sim N(0,\Sigma), \text{ $\Sigma$ block diagonal}
\end{align*}
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A unified framework for missing data and cold start prediction for time series data} \\
  C. Xie et al.
\end{book}
Matrix factorization (for time series) can be combined with matrix completion
(from collaborative filtering): 
\[ Y_i = L R_i + H U \phi_i + b + \epsilon_i \]
where $R_i$ are latent time series, $\phi_i$ features,
$U \phi_i$ dimension-reduced features.


% ICLR 2017
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quasi-recurrent neural networks} \\
  J. Bradbury et al. (2017)
\end{book}

Model sequential data with \emph{masked convolutions} $*$
(\ie, convolutions using only past data)
outputting LSTM elements
\begin{align*}
  Z &= \tanh( W_z * X ) \\
  F &= \sigma( W_f * X ) \\
  O &= \sigma( W_o * X )
\end{align*}
combined as 
$h_t = f_t \odot h_{t-1} + ( 1 - f_t) \odot z_t $
or 
\begin{align*}
  c_t &= f_t \odot c_{t-1} + ( 1 - f_t) \odot z_t \\
  h_t &= o_t \odot c_t .
\end{align*}
Add skip connections (dense convolution),
dropout (zoneout),
attention.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lie access neural Turing machine} \\
  G. Yang (2016)
\end{book}
In addition to random access, allow translations from one memory cell to another,
or the action of some other Lie group
($\RR^2$, $\text{SO}(2)$, $\RR^2\rtimes\text{SO}(2)$ acting on $\RR^2$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to optimize} \\
  K. Li and J. Malik
\end{book}
Optimization algorithms can be learnt as reinforcement learning policies,
with \emph{guided policy search}.  % no details

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extrapolation and learning equations} \\
  G. Martius and C.H. Lampert (2016)
\end{book}
To learn small, interpretable analytical expressions, amenable to extrapolation,
use a neural network whose activation functions are the desired building blocks (the identity, $\sin$, $\cos$, sigmoid -- one could also add products and divisions)
with a sparsity constraint.
% Application: double pendulum.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adversarially learned inference} \\
  V. Dumoulin et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Combine generative adversarial networks (GAN, \ie, generator and discriminator) and variational autoencoders (VAE, \ie, inference and generation): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Encoder, from real data $x$ to latent representation $z$: $p(x,z) = p(x) p(z|x)$; 
\item Decoder (generator), from a latent representation $z$ to simulated data $x$: 
$p(x,z) = p(z) p(x|z)$, with $p(z) \sim \text{Gaussian}$;
\item Discriminator to guess if $(z,x)$ is real or simulated.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to discover sparse graphical models} \\
  E. Belilovsky (2017)
\end{book}
Incorporating a prior (e.g., small world) into the graphical lasso 
can be difficult: instead, use a neural net 
(CNN, for an arbitrary order on the nodes)
to recover the graph from the sample correlation matrix, trained on synthetic data generated by the desired prior.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep learning with sets and point clouds} \\
  S. Racanbakhsh et al. (2017)
\end{book}
If the input of a layer is a set (if its order is irrelevant).
use equivariant transformations, e.g., 
\begin{align*}
f(\mathbf x) &= \sigma\bigl( \lambda \mathbf x + \gamma ( \mathbf 1 ' \mathbf x ) \mathbf 1 \bigr) \\
f(\mathbf x) &= \sigma\bigl( \lambda \mathbf x - \gamma ( \max \mathbf x ) \mathbf 1 \bigr) \\
f(\mathbf x) &= \sigma\bigl( \beta + \mathbf x \Gamma - \mathbf 1 ( \max \mathbf x ) \Gamma \bigr)
\end{align*}
Applications include MNIST digit summation (the order of the digits is irrelevant) and point cloud classification,
or situations with an existing clustering.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Normalizing the normalizers: comparing and extending network normalization} \\
  M. Ren et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Batch normalization, layer normalization (for RNNs)
and \emph{divisive normalization} (normalize neurons' activities by their neighbours') are the same operation along different dimensions of a tensor.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Regularizing CNNs\\ with locally constrained decorrelation} \\
  P. Rodr\'igez et al. (2017)
\end{book}
To ensure the features learnt are as diverse as possible,
add a penalty $\cor(\theta_i, \theta_j)^2$
if $i$ and $j$ are neurons in the same layer.
To preserve negative correlations, replace $\cor^2$ 
with $\text{softplus}(\cor)$ 
or $\text{softplus}\bigl( \lambda ( \cor - 1 ) \bigr)$.

Orthogonal weights are also sometimes used (with Xavier scaling) to initialize neural nets.
Multibias neural nets have neurons sharing weights but with different biases.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hierarchical multiscale\\ recurrent neural neuworks} \\
  J. Chung et al. (2017)
\end{book}
LSTM with sparse updates, triggered by a boundary detector.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometry of polysemy} \\
  J. Mu et al.
\end{book}
Using traditional word embeddings, represent a context (10 words before and after the target word) as a cloud of (20) points.
The span of the first $n=4$ principal components form a subspace, 
\ie, a point in the Grassmanian $G_{4,300}$.
The different senses of a word can be recovered by clustering 
(the $k$-means algorithm can be generalized to the Grassmanian)
its contexts; 
each cluster corresponds to a line -- a point in $G_{1,300}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Exponential machines} \\
  A. Novikov et al. (2017)
\end{book}
A $d$-dimensional tensor $\mathscr A$ has 
\d{tensor train} (TT) rank at most $r$ if its elements
can be written 
\[ \mathscr A_{ i_1 \cdots i_d } = G_1[i_1] \cdots G_d[i_d] \]
where $G_1[i_1] $ is $1 \times r$, 
$G_k[i_k]$, for $1<k<d$, is $r\times r$,
and $G_d[i_d]$ is $r\times1$.
Operations on tensor trains are efficient and return other tensor trains.
\[
