The linear model 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{find} & w, b \\ 
  \text{To minimize} & \sum \ell \bigl( \langle x , w \rangle + b , y \bigr) + \lambda \n{ w } _2^2
\end{array}
\]
can be generalized to account for interactions 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \mathscr W \\
  \text{To minimize} & \sum \ell\bigl( \langle \mathscr X, \mathscr W \rangle , y \bigr) + \lambda \n{ \mathscr W } _2^1 \\
  \text{Such that} & \operatorname{TT-rank} \mathscr W = r \\
  \text{Where} & \mathscr X_{ i_1 \cdots i_d } = \prod_k x_k^{i_k}
\end{array}
\]
and estimated with Riemannian optimization
(tensor trains of fixed rank form a Riemannian manifold).

% ICLR 2017
%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Decoupled neural interfaces\\ using synthetic gradients} \\
  M. Jaderberg et al.
\end{book}
To avoid backpropagating the gradients all the way down the computation graph,
use neural networks to forecast them from local information.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Why and when can deep (but not shallow) networks avoid the curse of dimensionality:\\ a review} \\
  T. Poggio et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) work well,
not because of weight sharing, 
but because they are hierarchical compositions
of \emph{local} (\ie, low-dimensional) functions.
% Badly written; they do not know whant the "curse of dimensionality" is

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved variational inference\\ with inverse autoregressive flow} \\
  D.P. Kingma et al. (2016)
\end{book}
\d{Variational inference} looks for a simple distribution  $q$ approximating the posterior distribution $p$
minimizing $D_{KL}(q\|p)$.
\emph{Normalizing flows} allow more flexibility through reparametrization 
$z_0 \sim q$, 
$z_t = f_t(z_{t-1})$,
where the $f_t$'s combine a linear transformation and a nonlinearity.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convolutional neural networks on graphs\\ with fast localized spectral filtering} \\
  M. Defferrard et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Convolutional neural nets can be generalized from regular grids to arbitrary networks: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Consider the Laplacian $L=D-W$ or the normalized Laplacian $L=I-D^{-1/2} W D^{-1/2}$;
\item Diagonalize it, $L = U \Lambda U'$;
\item Define the Fourier transform as $x \mapsto U' x$
and the convolution as $x*y = U \bigl( (U'x) \odot (U'y) \bigr)$;
\item Consider filters of the form 
$x \mapsto g_\theta(L) x$, 
where $g_\theta(L) = \sum \theta_k L^k$
or $g_\theta(L) = \sum \theta_k T_k(L)$ (Chebychev polynomials).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Seven neurons memorizing sequences\\ of alphabetic images\\ via spike-timing dependent plasticity} \\
  T. Osogami and M. Otsuka
\end{book}
A \d{dynamic Boltzman machine} (DyBM) 
is a Boltzman machine unrolled through time.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning dynamic Boltzman machines\\ with spike-timing dependent plasticity} \\
  T. Osogami and M. Otsuka (2015)
\end{book}  
Although a DyBM can be interpreted as an RNN with memory, it can be learnt using only local information (no vanishing gradient problem).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonlinear dynamic Boltzman machines\\ for time series prediction} \\
  S. Dasgupta and T. Osogami (2016)
\end{book}   
A \d{Gaussian Boltzman machine} is a Boltzman machine (BM) in which the Bernoulli distributions 
\begin{align*}      
E_j &= b_j + \sum_{i \neq j} w_{ij} x_i &&
P[X_j=1] \propto e^{-E_j} \\
\intertext{have been replaced with Gaussian distributions}
\mu_j &= b_j + \sum_{i \neq j} w_{ij} x_i &&
X_j \sim N(\mu_j, \sigma_j). 
\end{align*}
A Gaussian dynamic Boltzman machine (DyBM) is an unfolded Gaussian BM.
It can be interpreted as a VAR model, 
with eligibility traces (exponentially weighted moving averages) 
as additional variables.
The input of a Gaussian DyBM can be preprocessed 
by an echo state RNN (\ie, an RNN with random, rather than learnt, weights).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic Boltzman machines\\ for second order moments\\ and generalized Gaussian distribution} \\
  R. Raymond et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Gaussian dynamic Boltzman machines can be generalized,
by making the variance time-dependent, as in a GARCH model (but using eligibility traces);
and/or by replacing the Gaussian distribution, e.g., with 
  $p(x) \propto \exp[ -\beta |x-\mu|^\rho ]$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Training deep and recurrent networks\\ with Hessian-free optimization} \\
  J. Martens and I. Sutskever (2015)
\end{book}
\d{Hessian-free} (HF) optimization (or \d{truncated Newton})
approximates the objective function with a quadratic
\[ h(\theta + \delta) \approx h(\theta) + G' \delta + \tfrac12 \delta' H \delta \]
but computes the update 
$\theta \leftarrow \theta - H^{-1} G$ using a few \emph{conjugate gradient} (CG) steps:
there is no need to invert the curvature matrix, or even to compute it.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Hessian-vector multiplications are directional derivatives of the gradient: 
they can be computed with forward differentiation.
\item Replace the Hessian with the \emph{generalized Gauss-Newton matrix} (GGN),
which assumes $f''=0$, (\ie, replaces $f$ with its first order approximation);
for scalar functions:
\begin{align*}
  h(\theta) &= L\bigl( f(\theta) \bigr) \\
  h'(\theta) &= f'(\theta) L' \bigl( f(\theta) \bigr) \\
  h''(\theta) &= f''(\theta) L'\bigl( f(\theta)\bigr) + 
  f'(\theta) L''\bigl(f(\theta) \bigr) f(\theta) \\
  & \approx f'(\theta) L''\bigl(f(\theta) \bigr) f(\theta);
\end{align*}
for supervised learning: 
\begin{align*}
  h(\theta) &= \dfrac1{\va S} \sum_{ (x,y) \in S } L\bigl( y, f(x,\theta) \bigr) \\
  h''(\theta) &\approx \dfrac1{\va S} \sum_{ (x,y) \in S } J' H_L J, 
  \quad\! J = \nabla_z L,
  \quad\! H_L = \nabla_{zz} L;
\end{align*}
\item Use \emph{Tikhonov damping} ($L^2$ regularization)
and make it scale-sensitive by using the diagonal of the Hessian 
(Levenberg-Marquardt);
\item Use \emph{structural damping}, \ie, a penalty, not just to changes in the parameters,
but to some intermediate quantities
(e.g., hidden activations -- just add them to the output of the network and feed them to the loss function);
\item Increase or decrease the scale of the penalty by looking at the ratio 
$\Delta \text{objective} / \Delta \text{penalty} $
(Levenberg-Marquardt heuristic);
\item Instead of a penalty, use a \emph{trust region};  % harder
\item Use early CG stopping;
\item As a last resort, use a line search;
\item Initialize CG with the previous direction (slightly scaled down) rather than 0;
\item Use a preconditioner: diagonal preconditioners are effective with deep neural nets,
because the scale of the gradients can grow/shrink exponentially between layers -- 
but less so for RNNs, because the weights are reused.
You may want to blend the diagonal matrix with the identity, 
$P = \bigl( \text{diag}(d) + \kappa I \bigr) ^\xi$.
There is no efficient algorithm to compute the diagonal of the Hessian: 
use the diagonal of the \emph{empirical Fisher information}
\begin{align*}
\bar F &= \dfrac1{ \va S } \sum_{ (x,y) \in S } 
\nabla L\bigl( y, f(x, \theta) \bigr)
\,
\nabla L\bigl( y, f(x, \theta) \bigr)' \\
d &= \text{diag} \bar F = \dfrac1{\va S} \sum \operatorname{sq} 
\nabla L\bigl( y, f(x, \theta) \bigr) \\
\text{sq} &= \text{elementwise square}
\end{align*}
(but it is biased) or, 
for an unbiased estimator,
\[ d = \E_{ v \sim N(0,1) } \bigl[ \operatorname{sq} J' H_L^{1/2} v \bigr]
\]
\item  If possible, estimate the gradient and the Hessian on the same (large) minibatch.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse portfolios\\ for high-dimensional financial index tracking} \\
  K. Benidis et al. (2017)
\end{book}

Passive investors try to reproduce the performance of some reference index
with a simpler portfolio: fewer and more liquid assets.
This can be formulated as an optimization problem:
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
  \text{Find} &          w  &          \text{Portfolio weights} \\
  \text{To minimize} &   \text{TE}[w,b]  & \text{Tracking error} \\ % of benchmark $b$} \\
  \text{Such that} &     w \geq 0, \ w'1=1 % & \text{Long-only, fully-invested}
\end{array}
\]  
For a sparse portfolio, one can add an $L^0$ penalty, 
but the resulting problem is too difficult
to be solved exactly -- there are heuristics,
but they do not provide good guarantees on the quality of the results.
To make it easier to solve, one can relax the constraint.
The usual $L^1$ relaxation does not work here: the $L^1$ norm is already constrained to be 1: $\n{w} _1 = w' 1 = 1.$
Instead, one can use a finer relaxation: 
\[
  \rho_{p,\gamma}( \mathbf w) = \dfrac{ 
    \log( 1 + \va{ \mathbf w } / p )
  }{ 
    \log( 1 + \gamma / p )
  };
\]
it is not convex,
but the optimization problem can be solved by 
\emph{majorization-minimization} (MM),
which reduces non-convex optimization problems to sequences of convex optimization problems.
Those optimization problems can be reformulated (again using MM)
into a sequence of quadratic programs with a closed form solution --
we are left with a simple and very fast iterative algorithm,
which no longer requires a solver.

The use of MM to deal with cardinality constraints is more general:
for instance, adding a minimum weight constraint
(either the weight is zero, or it is above a threshold)
leads to a similar algorithm.
One can also use other tracking errors, e.g., only pay attention to the downside risk.

To find the minimum of a function $f$, \d{majorization-minimization} (MM)
looks for a simpler function $g$ such that
\begin{align*}
  \forall & x, y & g(x,y) &\geq f(x) \\
  \forall & y    & g(y,y) &= g(y)
\end{align*}
and iterates $x_{n+1} \leftarrow \argmin_x g(x,x_n)$.

\[
<<MajorizationMinimization,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=3,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
dbColours <- c("#193296", "#0092D0", "#FFA000", "#D70032", "#2D962D", "#0055AA")
f <- function(x) 1 - 1 / (x^2 + 1)
df <- function(x) 2*x / ( x^2 + 1 )^2
g <- function(x, x0, a=1) {
  b <- df(x0) - 2 * a * x0
  c <- f(x0) - a * x0^2 - b * x0
  a * x^2 + b*x + c
}
par( mar = 0 * c(1,1,1,1), xaxs = "i" )
curve( f(x), lwd = 5, col = dbColours[1], xlim = c(-1,3), ylim = c(0,1.1), axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
for( x0 in seq(-3,3,by=.5) )
  curve( g(x,x0, 1 / ( .5 * (abs(x0) + .5) )^2 ), col = "grey", lwd = 2, add = TRUE, n = 200 )
curve( f(x), lwd = 5, col = dbColours[1], add = TRUE )
curve( g(x,1,1), col = dbColours[2], lwd = 3, add = TRUE )
points( 1, f(1), pch = 3, cex = 2, lwd = 2 )
text( 2.5, .8, "f(x)", col = dbColours[1] )
text( 1.05, .43, expression(` `(x[0],f(x[0]))), adj = c(0,0) )
text( 1.6, 1.05, expression(g(x,x[0])), adj=c(0,0), col = dbColours[2] )
xs <- seq(-3,3,length=1000)
x1 <- xs[ which.min( g(xs,1) ) ]
points( x1, g(x1,1), pch = 4, cex = 2, lwd = 2 )
points( x1, f(x1), pch = 3, cex = 2, lwd = 2 )
segments( x1, g(x1,1), x1, f(x1), lty = 3, lwd = 5 )
text( x1+.05, f(x1)-.07, expression(` `(x[1],f(x[1]))), adj=c(0,0) )
@
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Successive convex optimization methods\\ for risk parity portfolio design} \\
  Y. Feng and D.P Palomar (2015)
\end{book}
Investors usually have to choose between risk parity portfolios and Markowitz-efficient portfolios, 
but it is possible to combine both loss functions into the same objective, yielding a portfolio close to risk parity, with high returns, with low risk.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Regularized robust estimation\\ of mean and covariance matrix\\ under heavy-tailed distributions} \\
  Y. Sun et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Estimate variance matrices, in the presence of outliers, fat tails and/or insufficient data, with M-estimators (several of them) and a majorization-minimization algorithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Minimal criterion coevolution:\\ a new approach to open-ended search} \\
  J.C. Brant and K.O. Stanley (2017)
\end{book}
Open-ended evolution (non-objective search) forgoes the objective function
and only uses a minimum criterion, 
which should be satisfied for reproduction.
The idea extends to coevolution, e.g., with mazes and maze-solving neural nets,
the criteria being that each maze is solved by at least one neural net and each neural net solves at least one maze.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic dual coordinate ascent methods\\ for regularized loss minimization} \\
  S. Shalev-Shwartz and T. Zhang (2013)
\end{book}
Instead of solving the primal problem with (stochastic) gradient descent,
solve the dual problem -- 
with (stochastic) coordinate ascent (SDCA).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Submodular risk allocation} \\
  S. Ghamami and P. Glasserman (2017)
\end{book}
The problem of choosing which counterparty to use is of the form 
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_{ A \subset S } F(A) + G(S \setminus A) \]
where $S$ is the set of trades to execute,
$F$ and $G$ are the collateral costs for counterparties 1 and 2,
and $A$ and $S \setminus A$
the trades sent to them.
The costs could be of the form 
$\sigma(A) = \text{Sd} \bigl( \sum_{i \in A} X_i \bigr),$
which is submodular if $\var X$ is close to diagonal.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A hitchhiker's guide\\ to automatic differentiation} \\
  P.H.W. Hoffmann (2016)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-reversible Metropolis-Hastings} \\
  J. Bierkens
\end{book}
A Markov chain on a discrete space $S$, with transition probabilities $P$,
and a probability distribution $\pi$ on $S$,
satisfy the \emph{detailed balance equation}
if $\Gamma=0$, where $\Gamma(x,y) = \pi(x) P(x,y) - \pi(y) P(y,x)$;
the Markov chain is then said to be \emph{reversible}
and $\pi$ is an invariant distribution.
But the condition for $\pi$ to be invariant is more general: 
$\Gamma \mathbf 1 = 0$ (\emph{global balance}).

A \emph{vorticity matrix} is a skew-symmetric matrix $\Gamma$ 
such that $\Gamma \mathbf 1 = 0$.
Given a reversible chain $k$ with invariant distribution $\pi$ 
and a vorticity matrix $\Gamma$,
\[ P(x,y) = k(x,y) + \dfrac{ \Gamma(x,y) }{ 2 \pi(x) } \]
defines a (non-reversible) 
chain with invariant distribution $\pi$ 
(provided $P \geq 0$: since $\Gamma$ has negative entries, this is not guaranteed).

The \emph{non-reversible Metropolis-Hastings} (NRMH) chain
\begin{align*}
  R(x,y) &= \dfrac{ \Gamma(x,y) + \pi(y) Q(y,x) }{ \pi(x) Q(x,y) } \\
  A(x,y) &= \max \bigl\{ 1, R(x,y) \bigr\} \\
  P(x,y) &= Q(x,y) A(x,y) \text{ if } x \neq y   
\end{align*}
provided $Q(x,y) = 0 \Leftrightarrow Q(y,x) = 0$
and $\Gamma(x,y) \geq - \pi(y) Q(y,x)$, 
can be generalized to continuous spaces.

Non-reversible chains have better theoretical properties (lower variance, fewer large deviations).

The Langevin diffusion 
$dX = \nabla \log \pi dt + \sqrt 2 dW$ 
(for instance, the OU diffusion,
$dX = -V^{-1} X dt + \sqrt2 dW$, which samples from $N(0,V)$)
can be made non-reversible 
\[ dX = (I+S) \nabla \log \pi + \sqrt2 dW , \]
where $S$ is skew-symmetric;
the MH acceptance step  to correct the discretization error 
can be replaced by a NRMH step.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepWalk:\\ online learning of social representations} \\
  B. Perozzi et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Embed the vertices of a graph in $\RR^n$ (e.g., for automated text layout, or community discovery)
as you would words (with skipgrams),
considering random walks (of vertices) as ``sentences'' (or words).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling dependence with C- and D-vine copulas: the R package CDVine} \\
  E.C. Brechmann and U. Schepsmeier (2013)
\end{book}
A multivariate distribution $f(x_1,x_2,x_3)$ can be decomposed as 
\begin{align*}
  f(x_1,x_2,x_3) &= 
  f(x_1) f( x_2 | x_1 ) f( x_3 | x_1, x_2 ) \\
  f( x_2 | x_1 ) &= \dfrac{ f(x_1,x_2) }{ f(x_1) } \\
  &= \dfrac{
    c_{12}( F_1(x_1), F_2(x_2) ) f(x_1) f(x_2) 
  }{
    f(x_1)
  } \\
  &= f(x_2) c_{12} ( F_1(x_1) , f_2(x_2) ) \\
  f(x_3 | x_1, x_2 ) &= 
  \dfrac{ f(x_2,x_3 | x_1 ) }{ f(x_2 | x_1 ) } \\
  &= \dfrac{
    c_{23|1} ( F( x_2 | x_1 ), F(x_3 | x_1 ) ) 
    f( x_2 | x_1 )
    f( x_3 | x_1 )
  }{
    f(x_2 | x_1 )
  } \\
  &= c_{23|1} ( F( x_2 | x_1 ) , F( x_3 | x_1 ) ) f( x_3 | x_1 ) \\
  &= c_{23|1} ( F( x_2 | x_1 ) , F( x_3 | x_1 ) ) f( x_3 ) 
  c_{13}( F(x_1), F(x_3) ) \\
  f_{123} &= f_1 f_2 f_3 c_{12} c_{13} c_{23|1}
\end{align*}
where $F(x_2|x_1)$ and $F(x_3|x_1)$
are known from $c_{12}$ and $c_{13}$, 
and we assume that the copula density $c_{23|1}$ does not depend on $x_1$.

There are many such decompositions. 
The $d(d-1)/2$ factors in those formulas
can be arranged into $d-1$ trees -- a \d{vine}.
Special cases include stars (C-vines)
and chains (D-vines), 
both defined by an ordering of the variables. 


