\cellcolor{bgred} $f_1$ & \cellcolor{bgblue} $c_{12}$ & \cellcolor{bggreen}$c_{13}$ & \cellcolor{bgpurple}$c_{14}$ & \cellcolor{bgorange}$c_{15}$  \\
   & \cellcolor{bgred}$f_2$ &  \cellcolor{bgblue}$c_{23|1}$ & \cellcolor{bggreen}$c_{24|1}$ & \cellcolor{bgpurple}$c_{25|1}$ \\
   & & \cellcolor{bgred}$f_3$ & \cellcolor{bgblue}$c_{24|12}$ & \cellcolor{bggreen}$c_{35|12}$ \\
   & & & \cellcolor{bgred}$f_4$ &\cellcolor{bgblue} $c_{45|123}$ \\
   & & & & \cellcolor{bgred}$f_5$ 
\end{tabular}
\]

The \texttt{CDVine} package provides functions for 
BB copulas (which allow both upper and lower tail dependence, contrary to most archimedian copulas),
bivariate copula analysis
(\texttt{BiCop*} for contoutrlot, Kendall's plot, $\chi$-plot, $\lambda$ function, independence tests, gof tests, 
estimators (MLE, method of moments using Kendall's $\tau$)),
and C- and D-vine estimators
(but you need to provide the variable order and the copula family).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum likelihood estimation of mixed C-vines with applications to exchange rates} \\
  C. Czado et al. (2012)
\end{book}
To select the variable ordering in a C-vine copula, 
start with the most correlated variable,
\[ i^* = \argmax_i \sum_j \va{ \tau_{ij} }, \]
renumber them so that $i^*=1$, 
estimate the corresponding copulas $C_{ij}$,
transform the variables 
$v_{j|1} = F(u_1 | u_j )$, 
where 
$F(u|v) = \partial C_{uv} / \partial v$ and iterate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Social media mining: an introduction} \\
  R. Zafarani et al. (2014)
\end{book}

% Graphs
\textbf{2.}
The \emph{Steiner tree problem}, 
finding the smallest tree, in a graph, 
containing a given subset of nodes, is NP-hard.
% Regular graph: all the nodes have the same degree
% Ford-Fulkerson maximum flow: find a path with unused capacity, use it, iterate until there is no such path.
% Maximum bipartite matching: add source and target nodes and apply Ford-Fulkerson

Signed graphs can model friend-foe relations; 
signed directed graphs are used in social status theory.

% Network measures
\textbf{3.}
There are many node \emph{centrality} measures: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Eigenvector: $ c_i = \lambda^{-1} \sum a_{ij} c_j$, \ie, $c$ is an eigenvector for eigenvalue $\lambda$
(from the Perron-Frobenius theorem, the largest eigenvalue gives positive centralities);
\item Katz and PageRank are variants of eigenvector centrality;
\item Betweenness: number of shortest paths a node is on;
\item Closeness: (harmonic) average distance to the other nodes.
\end{compactitem}
There are a few more node metrics: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Clustering: proportion of triangles 
  (for signed graphs, one can distinguish 
  between balanced and unbalanced triangles);
\item Reciprocity (for directed graphs).
\end{compactitem}

There are many node similarity measures: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Number of nodes in common, 
  $\va{N_x \inter N_y}$;
\item Jaccard similarity, 
  $ \dfrac{\va{ N_x \inter N_y } }{ \va{N_x \union N_y} };$
\item Cosine similarity, 
  $ \dfrac{\va{ N_x \inter N_y } }{ \sqrt{ \va{N_x} \va{N_y} } };$
\item Regular similarity, 
  $\displaystyle
  \sigma_{ij} = \alpha \sum_{k\ell} A_{ik} A_{i\ell} \sigma_{k\ell};$
\item Adamic-Adar,
  $\displaystyle \sum_{ z \in N_x \inter N_y } \dfrac1{ \log \va{ N_z } };$
\item Preferential attachment,  
  $\va{ N_x } \va{ N_y } ;$
\item Katz (weighted average path length);
\item Hitting time (average time a random walk takes to reach $y$ from $x$), 
commute time;
\item Rooted PageRank;
\item SimRank, \(
  \displaystyle
  \sigma_{xy} = \lambda \dfrac1{\va{N_x} \va{N_y}}
  \sum_{x' \in N_x}
  \sum_{ y' \in N_y }
  \sigma_{x'y'}.
\)
\end{compactitem}

% Network models
\textbf{4.}
Network models try to reproduce the following stylized facts: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Power law degree distribution (\emph{scale-free} network);
\item High clustering coefficient;
\item Low average path length (\emph{small world}).
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Random graphs} models, $G(n,p)$ ($n$ nodes, edge probability $p$) 
and $G(n,k)$ ($n$ nodes, $k$ edges)
show a phase transition (apparition of a giant component)
when the average degree reaches 1. The degree distribution is binomial (asymptotically Poisson -- not a power law)
and the clustering coefficient, $p$, is low.
The average path length is 
$\log \# \text{nodes} / \log \langle \text{degree} \rangle$.

The \emph{small world} model starts with a regular (ring) lattice
and rewrites each edge (replaces one extremity at random)
with probability $\beta$. The degree distribution is still incorrect,
but the clustering coefficient and the average path length are fine.

The \emph{preferential attachment} (Barab\'asi-Albert) model
adds one node at a time, connects it to $m$ nodes, 
chosen with probabilities proportional to their degrees.
The resulting graph is scale-free, \ie, its degree distribution is a power law,
but its exponent is always the same (3);
the average path length is small enough,
but the clustering coefficient is too low.

The \emph{stochastic block model} was not mentioned.

% Data mining
\textbf{5.}
The \emph{weighted vote relational neighbour} (wvRN) classifier
is similar to $k$-NN:
\[ P[ y_i = 1 ] = \dfrac1{ \va{N_i } } \sum_{ j \in N_i } P[ y_i = 1 ] \]
(since not all $y_i$'s are observed, iterate to find a fixed point).

To evaluate the result of a clustering algorithm: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \text{Cohesiveness}: 
$\displaystyle \sum_{ i \in \text{Clusters} } \sum_{ j \in C_i } 
\n{ x_j - c_i }^2  $
\item Separateness: 
$\displaystyle \sum_i \n{ c_i - c }^2$
\item Silhouette: 
$\displaystyle \dfrac1n \sum_x \dfrac{ b(x) - a(x) }{ \max\{ b(x), a(x) \} }$,
where $a(x)$ is the average distance to the other elements of the cluster
and $b(x)$ the minimum average distance to the elements of another cluster.
\end{compactitem}

% Community analysis
\textbf{6.}
Communities could be defined as \emph{cliques}, 
(but they are rare in real networks),
$k$-plexes (subgraph with $n$ nodes, each of degree at least $n-k$)
or $k$-cliques (subgraphs in which all shortest paths have length at most~$k$).

The clique percolation method (CPM) uses cliques as seeds: 
communities are the connected components of the clique graph, 
whose vertices are cliques of size $k$ and with edges when cliques share $k-1$ nodes.

Classical clustering algorithms can be used with node similarity.

Minimum cuts tend to generate singletons. Instead, one can look for 
partitions $P=(P_1,\dots,P_k)$ minimizing 
\begin{align*}
  \text{RatioCut}(P) &= \dfrac1k \sum \dfrac{ \text{Cut}(P_i, \bar P_i ) }{ \va{ P_i}} \\
  \text{or NormalizedCut}(P) &= \dfrac1k \sum \dfrac{ \text{Cut}(P_i, \bar P_i ) }{ \text{vol}( P_i ) }   
\end{align*}
where the volume is the sum of the degrees.
Those problems are NP-hard, but can be relaxed (spectral clustering).

The \emph{modularity} of a community is the difference between the number of edges inside it and the expected number of edges for a random graph with similar degrees,
\[ \text{Modularity}(P) = \sum_i \sum_{ j,k \in P_i } A_{jk} - \dfrac{ d_j d_k }{ 2m }. \]

The \emph{Girwan-Newman} divisive hierarchical communities are obtained by progressively removing edges with the largest edge betweenness.

Evolving networks have a few more stylized facts: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Segmentation into giant component, stars and singletons;
\item Densification: the number of edges increases faster than the number of nodes, 
$\va{ E_t } \propto \va{ V_t }^\alpha$, $\alpha > 1$ 
(often, $\alpha \approx 1.6$);
\item Diameter shrinkage.
\end{compactitem}

To find evolving communities, consider a single optimization problem,
for the communities of $G_t$ for all $t$'s, 
with a penalty to keep the communities of $G_t$ and $G_{t+1}$ close.

% Information diffusion in social media
\textbf{7.}  
The information cascade model (ICM) models information diffusion on a known network, in a sender-centric fashion: 
at each time step, 
each activated node (potential client who has already heard of our product)
activates nearby nodes
(talks to them 
and succeeds if his conviction $p_{ij}$ exceeds their threshold $t_j$).
Choosing the seed nodes to maximize the speed of cascades 
is a submodular optimization problem.

The diffusion of innovation (on a complete or unknown network)
can be modeled as 
\[ A' = ( \alpha + \beta A ) ( \text{Population} - A ) \]
(where $A$ is the number of adopters, 
$\alpha$ the external influence and $\beta$ the internal influence)
or with epidemic models (SIRS -- SI, SIR, SIS are obtained with 
$\lambda = \gamma = 0$, $\lambda = 0$ and $\lambda = \gamma$, $R=0$)
\begin{align*}
  S' &= \lambda R - \beta IS \\
  I' &= \beta IS - \gamma I \\
  R' &= \gamma I - \lambda R \\
  N &= S + I + R
\end{align*}

% Influence and homophily 
\textbf{8.} \emph{Assortativity} 
measures the propensity of similar nodes to be linked.
For categorical features, 
it can be assessed by modularity; 
for continuous ones, by covariance or correlation.

It can be explained by \emph{influence} 
(the presence of links changes node attributes)
or \emph{homophily}
(feature similarity creates edges).

\[ 
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1,yscale=1,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep =1]
\node (G1) at (0,1) {$G_t$};
\node (G2) at (1,1) {$G_{t+1}$};
\node (G3) at (2.5,1) {$G_t$};
\node (G4) at (3.5,1) {$G_{t+1}$};
\node (X1) at (0,0) {$X_t$};
\node (X2) at (1,0) {$X_{t+1}$};
\node (X3) at (2.5,0) {$X_t$};
\node (X4) at (3.5,0) {$X_{t+1}$};
\draw[->] (G1) -- (X2);
\draw[->] (X3) -- (G4);
\node at (.5,-.5) {\footnotesize influence};
\node at (3,-.5) {\footnotesize homophily};
\node[anchor=east] at (-.5,1) {\footnotesize graph};
\node[anchor=east] at (-.5,0) {\footnotesize features};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Influence can be measured by centrality, 
or the size of the cascade (in the ICM or SIR model), 
e.g., the number of nodes affected,
the average number of hops, 
or the rate at which the population is infected.
Specific examples include
  the number of in-links,
  the inverse of the number of outlinks (novelty),
  the number of comments,
  the length
    for blogposts,
or
  the number of followers (popular but imprecise), retweets and mentions
    for Twitter.

Contrary to sender-centric cascade (epidemic) models, 
influence (or threshold) models are receiver-centric.
  
In the \emph{linear threshold model} 
(LTM, e.g., Schelling's model of racial segregation),
node $i$ becomes active if $\sum_{ j \in N_i } w_{ji} \geq \theta_i $.
 
The \emph{linear influence model} (LIM)
\begin{align*}
  & \va{ P_t } = \sum_{ u \in P_t } I( u, t - t_u ) 
  & \text{population influenced} \\
  & I(u,t-t_u) = c_u( t - t_u ) ^ { - \alpha_u } 
  &\text{influence function} \\
  & \va{ P_t } = \sum_{u,t} A_{ut} I_{ut}, \ I \geq 0
\end{align*}
can be solved with nonnegative least squares. 

Several tests disentangle influence and homophily: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Shuffle test: % influence
compare the \d{social correlation}
(the slope in the logistic regression 
$\text{activation} = \alpha + \beta \times \text{\#\,active friends} + \epsilon$)
before and after shuffling the activation times;
\item Edge reversal test: % influence
  the social correlation should not change when reversing the edges;
\item Randomization test: look at the assortativity gain
\begin{align*}
  \text{Gain}_{ \text{influence} } (t) &= A( G_t , X_{t+1} ) - A(G_t,X_t) \\
  \text{Gain}_{ \text{homophily} } (t) &= A( G_{t+1} , X_t ) - A(G_t,X_t).
\end{align*}
\end{compactitem}

% Recommendations
\textbf{9.} 
Recommendation engines can average the scores for similar items and/or users,
but most rely on matrix factorizations, finding embeddings $U$ (for users) and $I$ (items) such that 
\( \text{similarity}(i,j) \approx \cos( U_i, I_j ) \),
sometimes with a penalty 
$\text{sim}(i,j) \n{ U_i - U_j }^2$
to ensure nearby nodes have nearby feature vectors. 

% Behaviour analytics
\textbf{10.} 
\emph{Link prediction} can be based on node similarity.
User migration can be forecasted from user activity, network size and rank (in-degree).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Error correcting codes} \\
  F. Lemmermeyer (2005)
\end{book}
Given a finite set $A$ (alphabet, e.g., $\{0,1\}$),
a \emph{code} is a subset $C \subset A^n$.

A \emph{linear code} is a linear subspace of $\FF_q^n$.

The Hamming $[7,4]$ code, in $\FF_2^7$, 
adds 3 parity bits to a 4-bit message.

% Hamming [7,4] code
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.3,yscale=.6,>=stealth,inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
\node (A1) at (1,1) {1};
\node (A2) at (3,1) {2};
\node (A3) at (0,0) {3};
\node (A4) at (5,1) {4};
\node (A5) at (2,0) {5};
\node (A6) at (4,0) {6};
\node (A7) at (6,0) {7};
\draw (A1) -- (A3);
\draw (A1) -- (A5);
\draw (A1) -- (A6);
\draw (A2) -- (A3);
\draw (A2) -- (A6);
\draw (A2) -- (A7);
\draw (A4) -- (A5);
\draw (A4) -- (A6);
\draw (A4) -- (A7);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

A code of type $[n,k,d]_q$ is a subset of $\FF_q^n$ of size $q^k$, 
with minimum distance $d$.

The \emph{minimum distance} of a code is the minimum Hamming distance between any two codewords. 
The minimum distance of a linear code is the minimum number of non-zero bits in a codeword (the minimum weight).
A code with minimum distance $d$ can detect $d-1$ errors 
and correct $\lfloor (d-1) / 2 \rfloor$.

[The linear algebra notation for linear codes often uses \emph{row} vectors.]
A \emph{generator matrix} of a linear code $C$ 
is a matrix $G$ whose rows form a basis of $C$;
it can be used to encode messages, 
\[
\left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
\FF_q^k & \longrightarrow & \FF_q^n \\
x & \longmapsto & xG.
\end{array}
\right.
\]
A \emph{parity matrix} of $C$ is a generator matrix $H$ of $C^\perp$;
it can be used to detect the presence of errors: 
\[ C = \{ c \in \FF_q^n \ :\ c H' = 0 \}. \]
Codewords are elements $c$ whose \emph{syndrome} $cH'$ is zero.

The Hamming code $\text{Ham}(r)$
is defined by a parity matrix whose columns are 
the nonzero vectors in $\FF_2^r$; 
it has type $[2^r-1, 2^r-1-r, 3]_2$.

For a code $C \subset \FF_q^n$ with minimum distance $2t+1$, 
the \emph{sphere packing bound} is 
\[ \va C \sum_{i=0} ^t \binom ni (q-1)^i \leq q^r. \]
For a \emph{perfect code}, it is an equality.
The perfect codes are Hamming ($t=1$), 
repetition codes of odd length $n=2t+1$
and the binary Golay code $G_{23}$.

For linear codes of type $[n,k,d]$, 
the \emph{singleton bound} is 
$d \leq n - k + 1 $.

If the generator matrix is in standard form, $G=(I|A)$,
a corresponding parity check matrix is $H = (-A' | I )$.

To decode a message $c$ encoded with a general linear code $C \subset \FF_q^n$,
notice that two words $x$, $y$ are in the same coset $v + C$
iff they have the same syndrome.
The \emph{coset leader} $e$ of a coset $v+C$
is the word in it with minimal weight:
subtracting it from the encoded message corrects the errors
(for efficient decoding,
one can store a mapping table from syndromes to coset leaders).

The \emph{Plotkin} product of two codes $C_1, C_2 \subset \FF_2^n$
of the same length is 
\[ C_1 * C_2 = \{ \, (u, u+v) \ :\ u \in C_1,\ v \in C_2 \,\}. \]

\emph{Cyclic linear codes} are linear codes stable by cyclic permutations
\[ (c_1 c_1 \cdots c_{n-1}) \longmapsto (c_{n-1} c_0 c_1 \cdots c_{n-2}); \]
they correspond to ideals of 
$\FF_q[X] / ( X^n - 1 )$.
Those ideals are principal and generated by the divisors $g$ of $X^n-1$.
If $X^n-1=gh$, then $h$ is a parity check polynomial.
To decode a message, compute its euclidean division by $h$: 
the syndrome polynomial $s(X)$ is the remainder.
The error is the word of minimum weight among the cyclic shifts $X^is(X) \mod g(X)$.

If $r$ consecutive powers of an $n$th root of unity are roots of $g(X)$,
then $d \geq r+1$.

Golay's $G_{23}$ code, of type $[23,12,7]_2$, corresponds to 
\[ X^{11} + X^9 + X^7 + X^6 + X^5 + X + 1 \mid X^{23} - 1. \]


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A tutorial on conformal prediction} \\
  G. Shafer and V. Vovk (2013)
\end{book}
Conformal prediction regions are 
defined by $y \in R_\epsilon$ if 
\[ p_y = \dfrac{ \# \{ I : \alpha_i > \alpha_n \} }{n} > \epsilon \]
where
$\alpha_i = d\bigl( \lbag z_1, \dots, z_n \!\rbag \setminus \lbag\!\! z_i \rbag, z_i \bigr)$,
$z_k = (x_k, y_k )$ for $k < n$,
$z_n = (x_n, y)$,
$d$ is some \emph{non-conformity measure}
and $\lbag \cdots \rbag$ denotes multisets.

They are valid, 
\ie, they contain the true value the advertized proportion of the time,
provided the sequence of random variables is \emph{exchangeable}.

The bags of observations can be seen as an \emph{online compression model},
with summarizing and updating operators,
\begin{align*}
\{ z_1, \dots, z_n \} &\longmapsto \lbag z_1, \dots, z_n \rbag \\
\lbag z_1, \dots, z_n \rbag, z_{n+1} &\longmapsto 
\lbag z_1, \dots, z_n, z_{n+1} \!\rbag.
\end{align*}

Relaxations of exchangeability include 
within-label exchangeability
or the online Gaussian linear model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Advances in financial machine learning} \\
  M. Lopez de Prado (2018)
\end{book}

\textbf{2.} Financial data is often irregularly-spaced and should be transformed into tabular format,
often as \emph{bars}, \ie, summaries (open, high, low, close, VWAP) over intervals: 
time bars, tick bars (every 1000 ticks), volume bars (number of shares), dollar bars (value traded),
tick/volume/dollar imbalance bars (cumulated order imbalance), 
\[  b_t = \begin{cases} b_{t-1} & \text{if } \Delta\text{price} = 0 \\ \text{sign}(\Delta\text{price}) \end{cases} \]
tick/volume/dollar runs bars (length of the longest run in $b_t$).

[The book tends to use ratio returns instead of log-returns (ratio returns are skewed and have extreme positive values)
and cumulated products instead of cumulated sums of logarithms (this is numerically problematic).]

\textbf{3.} Instead of forecasting the returns, or their sign, or whether they will excess some threshold,
$h$ bars ahead (with dollar bars, otherwise $h$ should vary), use a \emph{triple barrier}:
forecast whether the price with go above or below (stop-loss) a threshold before time $h$ --
there are three outcomes: profit-taking, stop-loss and time-loss.

Consider using a \emph{cascade} of models: a first model with high recall, 
followed by another with high precision 
(e.g., separately predicting the sign of the returns 
and whether the time barrier will be hit)
[he calls that ``metalabeling''].

\textbf{4.} Using bars leads to overlaps: weigh the observations accordingly.

\textbf{5.} Returns may not be the only way of turning log-prices into stationary time series, as required by many statistical procedures: 
\emph{fractional integration}, which accounts for long memory, may be a better choice.

\textbf{6,7.} Use ensemble methods, cross-validation (with no overlap, and perhaps even an embargo).

\textbf{8.} Feature importance can be assessed by mean decrease impurity (in-sample) or mean decrease accuracy (out-of-sample).
These are single-feature importance measures: to limit the substitution effect,
try to orthogonalize the features.

\textbf{9.} [The discussion of hyperparameter tuning only mentions grid and random search, 
not low-discrepancy sequences or Bayesian optimization.]

\textbf{10.} Size your position to account for the possibility that the signal will strengthen further
using, e.g., another model to estimate the probability of a correct forecast.

\textbf{11.} Backtesting can be dangerous: 
compute the \emph{probability of backtest overfitting}.

\textbf{12.} While $k$-fold cross-validation suffers from look-ahead bias, 
it should not be completely discarded: a model able to predict the future from the past 
but not the past from the future would be suspicious.

Account for multiple testing with FWER or FDR.

\textbf{13.} Backtesting on synthetic data, 
and comparing the strategy with a random one, 
can help check if the model overfits.

\textbf{14.} Backtest statistics should include
time (different regimes should be present), capacity, leverage, frequency of bets, 
average holding period, annual turnover, 
correlation to the underlying (to check if the strategy is just long or short);
usual performance measures,
P\&L from hits, from misses;
Herfindahl index of the time between bets,
size of the tails (e.g., $\langle r_+ \rangle$, $\langle r_- \rangle$);
Sharpe ratio variants (correction for short time series, skewness, kurtosis, multiple testing); 
attribution.

\textbf{15.} The \emph{strategy risk} 
is the sensitivity of the Sharpe ratio of a strategy 
(say, paying $\pi$ with probability $p$ and $-\pi$ with probability $1-p$, with $n$ bets per year)
to the hit ratio $p$, 
or by how much the hit ratio should drop to wipe out the profit.

\textbf{17.} Structural breaks can be detected with \textsc{cusum} tests,
looking if the cumulated normalized residuals of $y \sim x$
are consistent with a constant slope, 
or Dickey-Fuller-like tests. 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item To test for explosive behaviour in $y_t = \rho y_{t-1} + \epsilon_t$,
  \ie, $\rho=1$ if $t\leq t_0$ and $\rho>1$ if $t>t_0$,
  test for a non-zero slope in $\Delta y_t \sim y_{t-1} \mathbf 1_{ t > t_0}$;
\item The ADF test uses more lags
  $\Delta y_t \sim y_{t-1} \mathbf 1_{t>t_0} + \sum_\ell \Delta y_{t-\ell}$;
\item The supremum ADF test uses 
  $\sup_{t_0} \text{ADF}_{t_0,t}$,
  but there are quantile and ``conditional'' (cf. CVaR) variants.
\end{compactitem}

Sub- and super-martingale tests test $H_0: \beta = 0$ versus $H_1 : \beta \neq 0$
on 
\begin{align*}
  y &= \alpha + \gamma t + \beta t^2 + \epsilon \\
\log y &= \alpha + \gamma t + \beta t^2 + \epsilon \\
\log y &= \alpha + \beta t + \epsilon \\
\log y &= \alpha + \beta \log t + \epsilon
\end{align*}
and use $\sup_{t_0} \va{ \hat \beta / \sigma_\beta }$.

% \textbf{18.} Entropy features

\textbf{19.} There have been three generations of microstructure features: 
first-generation models assume the mid-price is a random walk, or use high and low values to estimate volatility, spread or imbalance; 
second-generation models focus on illiquidity,
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Kyle} &       \Delta \text{price} &\sim \text{signed volume} \\
  &\text{Amihud} &     \va{ \Delta \log \text{price} } &\sim \text{volume} \\
  &\text{Hasbrouk} &   \Delta \log \text{price} &\sim \pm \sqrt{ \text{volume} };
\end{align*}
third-generation models (PIN, VPIN) focus on trades.

Other microstructural features include 
the distribution of order sizes, cancellation rates, limit orders, market orders, presence of TWAP traders,
options markets (option quotes contain little information, but option trades are informative),
serial correlation of the signed order flow.

\emph{Microstructural information} can be defined 
by building a model to forecast a market maker's profit from those features
and looking at its performance (e.g., cdf of the log-likelihood).

\textbf{22.} To process big data, scientists tend to use HPC clusters
rather than the cloud: 
the cloud has distributed storage, 
relies on virtualization (flexible, but with a performance impact),
and is less scalable (because of IO and network virtualization) 
and more expensive than HPC.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convergence\\ of the stochastic six-vertex model to the ASEP} \\
  A. Aggarwal
\end{book}
In the asymmetric simple exclusion process (ASEP) model,
particles are initially placed on the integers (not all of them)
and move left or right,
if the new position is not occupied,
at times given by random exponential clocks with rates $0 \leq L < R$.

The \emph{six-vertex model} is a probability distribution on the set of paths in the first quadrant 
$\ZZ_{\geq 0} ^ 2 $
starting from a set of points on the $x$ or $y$ axis, obtained by extending the paths from 
