\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modified profile likelihood inference\\ and interval forecast\\ of the burst of financial bubbles} \\
  V. Filimonov et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The LPPL model can be justified by a geometric random walk with jumps
\[ \dfrac{ dp }p = \mu_t dt + \sigma_t dW - \kappa dJ \]
where the hazard rate of the jumps $J$ grows exponentially with log-periodic oscillations 
\[ h(t) = \alpha (t_c - t )^ { m-1 } \bigl[ 1 + \beta \cos \bigl( \omega \log(t_c-t) - \phi' \bigr) \bigr]. \]
If the excess return $\mu_t$ is proportional to the hazard rate,
then the expected log-price is 
\[ E[ \log p ] = A + B ( t_c - t)^m + C (t-t_c)^m \!\cos\bigl( \omega \log(t_c-t) - \phi \bigr). \]
We expect 
\[ 
0.1 \leq m \leq 0.9, \ 
6 \leq \omega \leq 13, \ 
B < 0, \ 
D = \dfrac{ m \va{B} }{ \omega \va{C} } \geq 0.8.
\]
In the likelihood $L$, one can separate the critical time $t_c$ from the nuisance parameters $\eta$, 
\[ \hat t = \argmax_t \max_\eta L(t,\eta) \]
where $L_p(t) = \max_\eta(t,\eta)$ is the \d{profile likelihood}.
The likelihood can sometimes be factored into \emph{marginal} and
\emph{conditional} likelihoods,
$L(t,\eta)=L(t)L(\eta)$, but not here.

The profile likelihood ignores the uncertainty on the nuisance parameters.
The \emph{modified profile likelihood}
adds a penalty using the Fisher information matrix of the nuisance parameters.

\begingroup
\allowdisplaybreaks
\begin{align*}
& t: \text{ parameter of interest} \\
& \eta: \text{ nuisance parameters} \\
& L(t, \eta): \text{ likelihood} \\
&\ell(t, \eta) = \log L(t,\eta) \text{ log-likelihood} \\
&\ell_p(t) = \max_\eta \ell(t,\eta) \text{ profile log-likelihood}  \\
&\hat \eta_t = \argmax_\eta \ell(t,\eta) \\
&\hat t, \hat \eta = \argmax_{t,\eta} \ell(t,\eta) \\
& I( \hat\eta_t ) = - \left. \dfrac{ \partial^2 \ell }{ \partial \eta \partial \eta' }
\right|_{ \eta = \hat \eta_t }
\text{ Fisher information} \\
&\va A = \va{ \det A } \\
&\ell_m(t) = -\tfrac12 \log \va{ I(\hat\eta_t) } + \log J(t) + \ell_p(t) 
%\text{ modified profile likelihood} 
\\
&J(t) : \text{ Jacobian term, to make the modified profile} \\
&\hphantom{J(t):{}} \text{ likelihood invariant to a reparametrization of}\\
&\hphantom{J(t):{}} \text{ the nuisance parameters (difficult to evaluate)} \\
& J(t) \approx \dfrac{
  \va{ I( \hat\eta_t ) }
}{
  \va{ \Sigma( t, \hat\eta_t ; \hat t, \hat\eta ) }
} \\
&\Sigma(t_1,\eta_1; t_2, \eta_2) = E_2 \biggl[
  \left. \dfrac{ \partial \ell }{ \partial \eta } \right|_{ t_1,\eta_1 }
  \!\!
  \left. \dfrac{ \partial \ell }{ \partial \eta' } \right|_{ t_2,\eta_2 }
\biggr] \\
& \dfrac{ \partial \ell }{ \partial \eta } : \text{ score function} \\
&E_2:{}\text{ expectation wrt the distribution}\\
&\hphantom{E_2:{}} \text{ of the error term $\epsilon(t_2,\eta_2)$}
\end{align*}
\endgroup

The \emph{multiscale modified profile likelihood}
plots those confidence intervals in the 
$t_2-t_1$ vs $t_c-t_2$ space, 
highlighting when the constraints are satisfied.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dissection of Bitcoin's\\ multiscale bubble history} \\
  J.C. Gerlach et al. (2018)
\end{book}
To identify Bitcoin bubbles ex post: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Look for ``drawups'', successions of positive returns interrupted by negative returns no larger than $\epsilon$, immediately followed by a similarly-defined drawdown, with $\epsilon = \epsilon_0 \sigma$, where $\sigma$ is the volatility over the past $w$ days, and keep dates identified as a peak by 95\% of the parameters $(\epsilon,w) \in [0.1, 5 ] \times [10,60]$
(``epsilon drawdown method'');
\item Define the begining of  the bubbles by fitting an LPPL model with various starting points, 
and keeping that with the lowest root mean square error, 
detrended by removing the effect of the estimation period $t_2-t_1$
(``Lagrange regularization'');
\item Define the end of the post-bubble correction as the minimum before the next bubble.
\end{compactitem}
For real-time bubble prediction, fit the LPPL model on different window sizes and cluster the 
$(t_2-t_1, t_c-t_1)$, 
with $k$-means and the silhouette to choose the number of clusters, 
to have one or several scenarios.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Blockchain: data malls,\\ coin economies and keyless payments} \\
  Z. Kakushadze and R.P. Russo (2018)
\end{book}
The Iota data marketplace is a decentralized data lake
whose coin allows users to buy and sell data from each other.
Augur is a decentralized prediction market based on Ethereum, using a ``reputation'' token.
Golem is a network to rent spare computing power.
MicroRaiden provides off-blockchain micropayments on Ethereum.
The Everipedia (a Wikipedia competitor) token is mined by editors by making valuable contributions.

Data provenance, keyless payments (sending BTC to an email address, in a decentralized way)
and voting are other applications.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cryptocurrency portfolio management\\ with deep reinforcement learning}\\
  Z. Jiang and J. Liang
\end{book}
End-to-end portfolio construction to find the weights maximizing the after-cost returns,
on 12 (liquid) cryptocurrencies,
from the prices on 50 30-minute periods, 
with a convolutional network (\textsc{conv}+\textsc{fc}).
[There is no ``reinforcement learning''; 
the convolution kernels are $12\times 4$, \ie, they also convolve in the ``asset'' direction.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Are Bitcoin bubbles predictable?\\
  Combining a generalized Metcalfe's law\\ and the LPPLS model} \\
  S. Wheatley et al. (2018)
\end{book}
\d{Metcalfe's law} states that the value of a network is proportional to the square of its number of nodes.
The Bitcoin market value is proportional to some power (1.7) of the number of users
(or the number of transactions, from \texttt{bitcoininfocharts.com}, smoothed): 
deviations from this generalized Metcalfe's law is a bubble signal;
it can be combined with the LPPL model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Classification of crypto-coins and tokens\\ from the dynamics\\ of their power law capitalization distributions} \\
  K. Wu et al. (2018)
\end{book}
The distribution of the market capitalization of coins (resp. tokens),
from \texttt{coinmarketcap.com},
is well-described by a Pareto (power law) distribution
with exponent 0.6 (resp. 1.1).
This can be explained by modeling the market capitalization of each coin
with independent geometric Brownian motions
(with the same parameters),
with new coins created by a Poisson process
with exponentially growing intensity,
and existing coins dying with a constant hazard rate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pricing options\\ with exponential Levy neural network} \\
  J. Huh (2018)
\end{book}
For Fourier-transform-based option pricing, 
model the L\'evy density 
\[ \dfrac{ d\nu }{ dx } (x) = e^{-x} \mathscr F ^{-1} [ h(w) ] (x) \]
with a 1-hidden-layer neural net $h$
whose hidden nodes are in two groups, 
whose outputs are multiplied.
