\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generating virtual scenarios\\ of multivariate financial data\\ for quantitative trading applications} \\
J. Franco-Pedroso et al. (2018)
\end{book}
Simulated returns from traditional models are not realistic (Gaussian returns)
and/or not scalable (multivariate GARCH, e.g., BEKK).
To reproduce ``multivariate stylized facts'' (kurtosis, skewness, volatility clustering,
increased correlation in periods of high volatility, number of ``trends'',
distribution of the ``trend ratios'' $\mu/\sigma$), take a more empirical approach:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Split the data into up- and down-trends for the market index;
\item In each of those trends, estimate the variance matrix on a moving window;
\item Sample Gaussian returns from those distributions
  [I suspect they reject the samples disagreeing with the trend].
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting directional changes\\ in financial markets} \\
  A. Bakhach et al (2015)
\end{book}
An upward or downward trend can be defined as a price change beyond a threshold $\theta$;
trends go from one extreme to another;
a confirmation point is the first point, after an extreme, exceeding the threshold.
The overshoot is the difference between an extreme and the previous confirmation point (rescaled by $\theta$).
One can predict of the overshoot of the current trend will exceed some threshold $d$
using decision trees and three variables:
the Aroon up and down indicators, \ie, the number of up (or down) trends ago
the maximum (or minimum) extremum (or confirmation point) was (among the past $n=20$ up/down trends)
and the difference between the last two confirmation points.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The role of the dynamic conditional\\ quartic beta and the capital markets} \\
  G. Corvasce
\end{book}
Define a (cross-sectional, time-varying) \emph{quartic beta} 
from the quartic loss
$\sum ( y_i - \beta x_i )^4$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Imaging time series\\ to improve classification and imputation} \\
  Z. Wang and T. Oates (2015)
\end{book}
Convert a time series to an image,
using a \emph{Gramian angular field}: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Rescale $x$ to $[-1,1]$;
\item Let $\phi_t = \arccos x_t$;
\item Consider $\cos(\phi_t+\phi_s)$ or $\sin(\phi_t - \phi_s)$
\end{compactitem}
or a \emph{Markov transition field}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bin the observations, $y = \text{discretize}(x)$;
\item Compute the transition matrix $P$;
\item Spread it out to preserve the temporal dependence
$M_{t\to s} = P_{ y_t \to y_s }$
\end{compactitem}
and use a (tiled) CNN.


%% 2018-07
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 2018-04

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep reinforcement learning bootcamp}\\
  P. Abbeel et al. (2017)
\end{book}
 
\textbf{1.}
\emph{Planning} or \emph{optimal control} 
is the search of the optimal policy on a \emph{known} 
Markov decsion problem (MDP).

\d{Value iteration} iterates the Bellman equation 
\begin{align*}
  V_0(s) &= 0 \\
  V_{k+1}(s) &= \max_a \E_{s'} \bigl[ R(s,q,s') + \gamma V_k(s') \bigr] \\
  &= \max_a \sum_{s'} P(s' | a, s ) \bigl[ R(s,a,s') + \gamma V_k(s') \bigr]
\end{align*}

\d{Q-value iteration} is similar, but computes 
$Q(s,a)$, the expected value, if we start in state $s$, take action $a$, and act optimally thereafter.
\[ Q_{k+1}(s,a) = \sum_{s'} P(s' | a,s) \bigl[ R(s,a,s') + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s',a') \bigr] \]

These are just systems of equations but, because of the maximum, they are not linear.
If the policy $\pi$ is fixed, however, it is just a linear system, which can be solved iteratively or directly
(\d{policy evaluation}).
\[ V^\pi(s) = \sum_{s'} P(s' | a,s ) \bigl[ R(x,a,s') + \gamma V^\pi(s') \bigr] \]

\d{Policy iteration} alternates two steps: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the value $V^\pi$ of the policy $\pi_k$;
\item Improve the policy by taking the best 1-step action 
\[ \pi_{k+1}(s) = \argmax_a P(s'|a,s) \bigl[ R(s,a,s') + \gamma V^\pi(s') \bigr] \]
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{2.}
If the MDP is not known, we can act at random and estimate the expected $Q$-value
(\d{tabular $Q$-learning}).
\begin{align*}
 & Q(s,a) \leftarrow (1-\alpha) Q(s,a) + \alpha \cdot \text{target} \\
 & \text{target} = R(s,a,s') + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s',a')
\end{align*}
This is \emph{off-policy} learning: 
the first term comes from the policy actually followed (e.g., $\epsilon$-greedy),
the second from the optimal policy found so far.
The learning rate $\alpha$ should satisfy 
$\sum \alpha_t = \infty$, $\sum \alpha_t^2 < \infty$.

\d{Approximate Q-learning} replaces the table $Q(s,a)$
with a function (e.g., a linear combination of hand-selected features), and a gradient update 
\[ \theta \leftarrow \theta - \dfrac12 \alpha \nabla_\theta \bigl( Q_\theta(s,a) - \text{target} \bigr)^2 . \]

\textbf{3. Deep Q networks (DQN)} use a neural network to model $Q_\theta$, but 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The target is not stationary; 
\item The data is not iid;
\end{compactitem}
To address those concerns: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use batches (\emph{experience replay});
\item Use an older set of weights for the target (\emph{target network});
\item Use Huber loss instead of square loss;
\item Use RMSProp instead of standard SGD;
\item Anneal the exploration rate.
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Neural fitted Q-iteration} is a batch version of DQN: generate a lot of $\epsilon$-greedy episodes; fit the resulting target for a while; iterate.

\emph{Double DQN} uses two sets of weights, to select the best action, and to estimate it 
(otherwise $\max_a Q_\theta(s,a)$ is biased upwards)

\emph{Prioritized experience replay} uses the Bellman error as transition weights.
\[ \va{ r + \gamma \max_{a'} Q_{\theta_1}(s',a) - Q_{\theta_2}(s,a') } \]

In a \emph{duelling DQN}, the neural network adds structure to the $Q$-value 
and separately forecasts state value and advantage,
\( Q(s,a) = V(s) + A(s,a). \)

\emph{Noisy nets} add noise to the parameters for better exploration.

\textbf{4.}
\d{Policy gradient} methods directly look for an optimal stochastic policy $\pi_\theta(s)$
(a deterministic policy is a combinatorial object, more difficult to optimize).
This is on-policy (less exploration) and less sample-efficient, but can deal with large action spaces 
(with $Q$-learning, $\argmax_a Q(s,a)$ can be difficult to compute).

Consider a whole episode and let
\[ \tau = (s_0, u_0, s_1, u_1, \dots, s_N, u_N) \]
be a state-action sequence. We want to maximize the expected utility 
$U(\theta) = \sum P_\theta(\tau) R(\tau)$.
\begin{align*}
\nabla_\theta U &= \sum \nabla_\theta P_\theta(\tau) R(\tau) \\
&= \sum P_\theta(\tau) \dfrac{ \nabla_\theta P_\theta(\tau) }{ P_\theta(\tau) } R(\tau) \\
&= \sum P_\theta(\tau) \nabla _\theta \log P_\theta(\tau) R(\tau) \\
&= \E\bigl[ \nabla _\theta \log P_\theta(\tau) R(\tau) \bigr] \\
&\approx \dfrac1m \sum_i \nabla_\theta \log P_\theta(\tau_i) R(\tau_i) \\
%% Allow a beak here
\nabla_\theta \log P_\theta(\tau) 
&= \nabla_\theta \log \prod_t P(s_{t+1} | s_t, a_t) \pi_\theta(s_t|s_t) \\
&= \sum_t \nabla_\theta \log P(s_{t+1} | s_t, a_t) + \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta( a_t, s_t) \\
&= \sum_t \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta( a_t | s_t )
\end{align*}
This can also be derived using \emph{importance sampling}: 
\begin{align*}
  U(\theta) &= \E_{ \tau \sim \theta } \bigl[ R(\tau) \bigr] \\
&= \E_{ \tau \sim \theta_{\text{old}} } \left[ \dfrac{ p_\theta(\tau) }{ p_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\tau) } R(\tau) \right] \\
\left. \nabla U(\theta) \right|_{\theta = \theta_{\text{old}}} &= 
\E_{ \tau \sim \theta_{\text{old}} } \left[ 
  \dfrac{ \nabla_\theta p_\theta(\tau) |_{\theta = \theta_{\text{old}} } }{ p_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\tau) } R(\tau) 
\right] \\
&= \E_{\tau \sim \theta_{\text{old}}} \bigl[ \nabla_\theta \log P_\theta(\tau) |_{\theta = \theta_{\text{old}}} R(\tau) \bigr]
\end{align*}

To reduce variance, one can add a baseline 
\begin{align*}
  \nabla U(\theta) &\approx \dfrac1m \sum_i \nabla_\theta \log P_\theta( \tau_i ) R(\tau_i ) \\
&\approx \dfrac1m \sum_i \nabla_\theta \log P_\theta(\tau_i) \bigr( R(\tau_i) - b \bigr)
\end{align*}
and discard terms that do not depend on the current action 
\begin{align*}
  \nabla U(\theta) &\approx \dfrac1m \sum_i \sum_t \nabla _\theta \log \pi_\theta( a_{it}, s_{it} )
  \left( R(\tau_i)-b \right) \\
  &\hspace*{-8mm}= \dfrac1m \sum_i \sum_t \nabla_{\theta} \log \pi_\theta(a_{it}, s_{it} ) 
\biggl( \sum_{k\geq t} R(s_{ik}, a_{ik}) - b \biggr).
\end{align*}

The difference 
$A_{it} = \smash{\sum\limits_{k\geq t} R(s_{ik}, a_{ik}) - b}$ is the \d{advantage}.

Here are possible choices for the baseline: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Constant: $b = \E\bigl[ R(\tau) \bigr] \approx \dfrac1m \sum R(\tau_i)$;
\item Optimal constant, 
\[ b = \dfrac{ \sum \bigl( \nabla \log p(\tau_i) \bigr)^2 R(\tau_i) }{ \sum \bigl( \nabla \log p(\tau_i) \bigr)^2 } ; \]
\item Time-dependent: 
\[ b = \E\bigl[ R(\tau^{\strut}_{\geq t})\bigr] \approx \dfrac1m \sum_i \sum_{k\geq t} R(s_{ik},a_{ik}) ; \]
\item State-dependent (\d{actor-critic} -- in particular, if the current state is hopeless, regardless of the actions taken, there is no valuable information and the gradient is zero): 
\[ b(s_t) = \E[r_t + r_{t+1} + \cdots ] = V^\pi(s_t). \]
\end{compactitem}

To estimate the value function $V^\pi(s)$, we can use supervised learning, e.g., 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Monte-Carlo: minimize 
\[ \Bigl[ V_\phi^\pi(s_{it}) - \sum_{k\geq t} R(s_{ik}, u_{ik}) \Bigr] ^2 \]
(we end up with two neural nets: one for the policy, one for the value function);
\item Temporal difference (TD): use the Bellman equation for $V^\pi$
\begin{align*}
\phi_{i+1} = \argmin_\phi & \sum 
\n{ R + \gamma V_{\phi_i}^\pi(s_{t+1}) - V_\phi^\pi(s_t) }_2^2 + {} \\
& \lambda \n{ \phi - \phi_i }^2
\end{align*}
(there are now two hyperparameters, $\gamma$ and $\lambda$).
\end{compactitem}

To reduce the variance: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Discount more; 
\item Use function approximation:
\begin{align*}
  Q^\pi(s,u) &= \E[ r_0 + \gamma r_1 + \gamma^2 r_2 + \cdots ] && = Q_0 \\
&= \E[ r_0 + \gamma V^\pi(s_1)] && =Q_1 \\
&= \E[r_0 + \gamma r_1 + \gamma^2 V^\pi(s_2) ] && = Q_2 \\
&= \E[ r_0 + \gamma r_1 + \cdots + \gamma^4 r_4 + \gamma^5 V^\pi(s_5) ]. \hspace*{-3cm}
\end{align*}
\end{compactitem}
The \d{Async advantage actor critic} (A3C) uses $Q_5$; 
the \emph{generalized advantage estimation} (GAE) averages all of those:
\[ Q^\pi = (1-\lambda) \sum_{ n \geq 0 } \lambda^n Q_n^\pi. \]

\textbf{5.}
Notice that the policy gradient step is also the gradient step of some optimization problem: 
we can replace that step with a few iterations of an optimization algorithm solving that problem, 
with a constraint or penalty to limit the step size. 

More precisely, the gradient 
$E_t[ \nabla_\theta \log \pi(a|s) A ] $
could come from the surrogate loss $E_t [ \log \pi(a|s) A ]$ 
or to the importance sampling loss 
\[ E_t \left[ \dfrac{ \pi_\theta( a|s ) }{ \pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(a|s) } A \right]. \]

\d{Trust region policy optimization} (TRPO)
uses a constraint to define the ``trust region'' 
(the region where we trust our approximation to be close enough)
\[ 
  E\bigl[ \text{KL}\bigl( \pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\cdot|s), \pi_\theta(\cdot|s) \bigr) \bigr] \leq \delta.
\]
In practice, use a linear approximation of the objective, 
a quadratic approximation of the constraint
(the Kullback-Leibert divergence becomes the Fisher information matrix --
it is a \d{natural gradient})
and solve using the conjugate gradient (Hessian-free optimization).

\d{Proximal policy optimization} (PPO) uses a penalty instead of a constraint -- 
but adjusting the level of the penalty is harder than adjusting the size of the trust region -- 
a constant value does not work.

Those methods do not work well with CNNs, RNNs and complicated architectures.

Variants include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item KFAC: Natural policy gradient blockwise approximation of the Fisher information matrix;
\item ACKTR: A2C + KFAC natural gradient;
\item PPO with clipped objective.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{6.} Practical advice: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use several baselines: cross-entropy, policy gradient, Q-learning SARSA;
\item Use more samples per batch;
\item Check the sensitivity to all parameters -- the method should be robust;
\item Try different random seeds;
\item Standardize: $\text{clip}\bigl( (x - \mu)/\sigma, -10, 10\bigr)$;
\item Use $\text{KL}(\pi_{\text{old}}, \pi_{\text{new}})$ as diagnostic; spikes indicate a drop in performance.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{7.}
%  Stochastic computation graphs. Pathwise derivative policy gradient methods
When computing the gradient of an expectation,
the parameter can be in the distribution, or in the integrand, or in both.
\begin{align*}
\nabla_\theta \E_{ s \sim p_\theta } \bigl[ f(X) \bigr] &= \E \bigl[ f(x) \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(x) \bigr] \\
\nabla_\theta \E_{ x \sim N(0,1) } \bigl[ f_\theta(x) \bigr] &= \E\bigl[ \nabla _\theta f_\theta(x) \bigr]
\end{align*}

A \d{stochastic computation graph} is a DAG 
some of whose nodes are stochastic, \ie, of the form 
``sample~$x$ from $p_{\text{input}}$'' instead of ``$x = f(\text{input})$''; 
we want the gradient of the expectation of the final output.

Examples include dropout or hard attention.
%% Not explained clearly

\textbf{8.}
The \d{cross-entropy method} (CEM, aka CMA-ES) works well for Tetris 
(for a small number of parameters: 22).
It is a generalization of the finite difference method, obtained 
with a 2-element population, defined with mirrored (antithetic) noise.

In practice, transform the reward $R(\tau)$ to its percentile or to an indicator variable for the best $k$\% policies.

\d{Derivative-free methods} are not sample-efficient, but very easy to parallelize.

\textbf{9. Model-based reinforcement learning} is sample-efficient, 
and can even be used on real (physical) robots:
model $p(s' | s,a)$ using Gaussian processes, neural nets of Gaussian model mixtures; 
optimize $\pi_\theta(a|s)$; iterate a few times (otherwise the distribution of states can be very different).

The global (DP, NN, GMM) can use used as a prior, 
with a local (Bayesian linear) model in the neighbourhood of the current trajectory,
using a constraint 
% linear quadratic regulator, LQR
% Guided policy search
\[ \text{KL} \bigl( p(\tau) \mathbin\| p_{\text{old}}(\tau) \bigr) < \epsilon . \]

For high-dimensional data (e.g., the goal could be specified by an image, with no explicit reward),
learn in latent space.

\textbf{10a.}
If an agent has \emph{rational preferences} (\ie, it cannot be taken advantage of),
its preferences can be summarized by a \d{utility} function, 
and its behaviour described by \emph{expected utility maximization} 
(von Neuman and Morgenstern theorem).

Humans are not rational (Allais's paradox).

\textbf{10b.}
\d{Inverse reinforcement learning} (IRL) infers a reward function from demonstrations --
but it is not uniquely defined, and the demonstrations are ont truely optimal.
Do not learn a single reward function, 
but a distribution of reward functions -- the maximum entropy one
(the partition function is problematic, but the corresponding gradient descent algorithm
can be seen as a generative adversarial network (GAN)).

\textbf{11.}
The main challenges in RL are sample efficiency and exploration (we are still relying on random actions).

\d{Distributional RL} (C51) does not learn a value \emph{function} but value \emph{distributions}.

\d{Unsupervised RL} deals with sparse rewards by learning more about the environment 
through auxilliary prediction and control tasks (e.g., predicting that some pixels will change colours, or inducing those changes -- 
for images, use CNN features instead of pixels).

\d{Imagination-augmented agents} learn an imperfect model, and use samples from that model as additional trajectories to learn from.

%\d{Neural episodic control} combines parametric and tabular Q-learning.

\d{Hierarchical RL} improves exploration by learning subtasks and combining them to reach the desired goal.
\d{Feudal networks} use two agents, a manager, who decides on the subtasks (and is rewarded if they help get close to the goal), 
and a worker, who tries to perform the subtasks (and is not aware of the ultimate goal).

For real robots, train the agent in a simulated environment, and fine-tune it in thee physical world.
\d{One-shot imitation learning} can then learn a new task from a single example.

\textbf{12.}
Current reinforcement learning focuses on a single task, 
in contrast to machine learning, where generalization matters.

To improve generalization, train on different physical robots.

If the problem has some structure, if you can decompose the task into different ``modules'', 
have separate neural nets learn them, and only combine them at the end
(we already saw that with duelling DQNs).

Given a single demonstration, decompose it into intermediate steps 
(e.g., the latent representation from a CNN), and use them as intermediate goals.
\emph{Translation} from the demonstration context to the current context
(e.g., the position of the objects) may be needed.

\emph{Model-based RL} learns about the physical world 
and can use that knowledge to forecast the outcome of an action 
and act towards a new goal.

\emph{Multi-task learning} is a form of regularization and data augmentation -- 
some aspects of the world, useful to plan a task, may be easier to learn when performing another task.

\d{Model-agnostic meta learning}
looks for a policy that can easily be adjusted to new tasks by policy gradient -- 
for instance, a robot that could be steered in any direction after just one gradient step
(\emph{one-shot learning}).

\textbf{13.} The programming exercises use Chainer.

