\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Can't decide? Undecide!}\\
  C. Goodman-Strauss (2010)
\end{book}

Turing machines can be encoded in seemingly innocuous mathematical objects --
the undecidability of the stopping problem leads to more concrete undecidable problems:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Given a finite collection of tiles and a ``seed'' pattern, can we tile the whole plane?
\item Collatz-like functions, in particular \d{Fractran} programs --
given a list of fractions and a starting integer $n$,
multiply it with the first fraction $p/q$ in the list so that $np/q$ is integer;
continue with that integer --
for instance 
\[
  \dfrac3{11} \quad 
  \dfrac{847}{45} \quad
  \dfrac{143}{6} \quad
  \dfrac73 \quad
  \dfrac{10}{91} \quad 
  \dfrac37 \quad 
  \dfrac{36}{325} \quad 
  \dfrac12 \quad 
  \dfrac{36}6
\]
generates all prime powers of 10, in order;
\item Post's tag production systems: start with a sequence of zeroes and ones,
remove the first three digits and add 00 or 1101 at the end, depending on whether 
the first digit was 0 or 1;
\item SAT (with statements such as ``the $k$th cell contains $a$ at time $t$'', 
``the machine is reading cell $k$ at time $t$ in state $s$'', etc.
\item Conway's game of life;
\item Wolfram's rule 110.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Facets of entropy}\\
  R.W. Yeung (2012)
\end{book}
The entropy function of a collection of discrete random variables 
$(X_i)_{1 \leq i \leq n}$ is 
\[
H : 
\left\{
\begin{array}{rcl} 
\mathscr P( \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket ) & \rightarrow & \RR \\
\alpha & \mapsto & H(X_\alpha)
\end{array}
\right.
\]
where $H(X_\alpha)$ is the joint entropy of 
$(X_i)_{i \in \alpha}$.
It satisfies the polymatroidal axioms
\begin{align*}
& H(\nothing) = 0 \\
& \alpha \subset \beta \implies H(\alpha) \leq H(\beta) \\
& H(\alpha) + H(\beta) \geq H(\alpha \inter \beta ) + H( \alpha \union \beta ) .
\end{align*}
In addition to entropy, the \emph{Shannon information measures}
are 
\begin{align*}
%&\text{Conditional entropy} &
H(X|Y) &= H(X,Y) = H(Y) \\
%&\text{mutual information} &
I(X;Y) &= H(X) + H(Y) - H(X,Y) \\
%&\text{conditional MI} &
I(X;Y|Z) &= H(X,Z) + H(Y,Z) - H(Z,Y,Z) - H(Z).
\end{align*}
Joint entropy and mutual information behave like set intersection and union.
\begin{align*}
  & H(X) && A \\
  & H(Y) && B \\
  &H(X,Y) && A\inter B \\
  &I(X;Y) && A \union B
\end{align*}
%TODO: PLOT?

The polymatroidal axioms are equivalent to the basic inequalities:
\begin{align*}
  \text{entropy} &\geq 0 \\
  \text{conditional entropy} &\geq 0 \\
  \text{mutual information} &\geq 0 \\
  \text{conditional mutual information} &\geq 0 
\end{align*}
Those inequalities are not sufficient to describe entropy functions: 
\begin{align*}
  \mathscr H_n &= \{ H : \mathscr P( \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket ) \to \RR \} \\
  \Gamma_n^* &= \{ H \in \mathscr H_n \ :\ H \text{ is entropic} \} \\
  \Gamma_n &= \{ H \in \mathscr H_n \ :\ H \text{ polymatroidal} \} \\
  \Gamma_n^* &\subset \Gamma_n \\
  \Gamma_2^* &= \Gamma_2 \\
  \Gamma_3^* &\subsetstr \Gamma_3 \text{ but } \overline{ \Gamma_3^*} = \Gamma_3
\end{align*}
For $n\geq 3$, 
$\Gamma_n^*$ is neither closed nor convex, 
but $\overline{ \Gamma_n^*}$ is a cone; 
for $n\geq 4$, there are many more non-Shannon-type inequalities: 
$\overline{ \Gamma_4^*} \subsetstr \Gamma_4$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonlinear time series analysis with R}\\
  R. Huffaker et al. (2017)
\end{book}

Given a dynamical system, \ie, a system of differential equations,
we can study its orbits -- they are sometimes chaotic, in the sense that
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Nearby trajectories diverge exponentially fast (chaos);
\item The trajectories tend to accumulate around an ``attractor'' with non-integral dimension (strangeness).
\end{compactitem}
For continuous (autonomous) systems, chaotic behaviour is only possible in dimension at least 3,
but discrete systems (e.g., the \emph{Poincar\'e map} of a continuous system, \ie,
the intersections of its trajectories with a plane) in dimension 1 or 2 can be chaotic.

Conversely, given just one coordinate of one of those trajectories, observed with noise,
is it possible to reconstruct the other coordinates, the whole attractor, and the original
dynamical system? This is the goal of nonlinear time series analysis.

The \emph{embedding} of a univariate time series $(x_t)_{t \geq 0}$
with delay $\tau$ and dimension $m$ is the multivariate time series
$(x_t, x_{t+\tau}, \dots, x_{t+(m-1)\tau})_{t\geq0}$.
Under reasonable assumptions, it can be used to describe the dynamics of the system:
one coordinate is indeed enough (Takens).

To estimate the delay $\tau$, use the first minimum of the \emph{automutual information} (AMI --
but there is no theoretical justification, and even no guarantee that a minimum exists).

To estimate the embedding dimension, look at the proportion of \emph{false near neighbours} (FNN),
\ie, the proportion of points close, in phase space, at time $t$, but not at time $t+1$ -- sign that some information has been left out. PCA can also be used.

For this (and for the Lyaponov exponent),
we want the points to be close in phse space, but not because they are close in time --
they should be somehow ``intependent''. The \emph{Theiler window} specifies a minimum time separation.
To estimate it, plot the proportion of FNN as a fuction of both space and time separation,
or use the first zero of the autocorrelation function (ACF).

The dimension of the attractor can be defined using box counting
\[ \lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \dfrac{ \log N(\epsilon) }{ \log( 1/\epsilon ) } \]
where $N(\epsilon)$ is the number of balls (or boxes) of size $\epsilon$ needed to cover the set
or the \emph{correlation dimension}
\begin{align*}
  D_2 &= \lim_{ \epsilon \to 0 } \dfrac{ \log C(\epsilon) }{ \log \epsilon } \\
  C(\epsilon) &= \lim_{ n \to +\infty } \dfrac1{n^2} \# \bigl[ \va{ y_i - y_j } \leq \epsilon \bigr]
\end{align*}
(this is a different notion: it gives more weight to areas visited more often).
The embedding dimension should be at least $2D+1$.

The \emph{recurrence plot} shows the distance (in phase space) between
the observation at time $t$ and that at time $s$;
it can be thresholded to give a binary image, and reduced to a few numeric quantities:
emph{recurrent quantitative analysis} (RQA) looks, among other things,
at the proportion of points in vertical or diagonal segments.

Singular spectrum analysis (SSA) can help separate signal from noise --
but it is manual, and some of the components have to be grouped.

To test for stationarity, use Schreiber's nonlinear cross-prediction stationarity test
(divide the time series into non-overlapping segments;
fit a nonlinear model on each, e.g., $k$-NN on an embedding;
measure the performance of model $i$ on segment $j\neq i$
versus that on segment $i$).

A linear system is a differential equation of the form $\dot y = A y$.
With real eigenvalues, there can be a stable, unstable or saddle point;
with complex eigenvalues, it can be a stable or unstable focus equilibrium, or a centre point.
If you see exponentially damped or exploding trajectories, use a linear model.
\[
<<DynamicalSystem2D,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
library(deSolve)
g <- function( A = matrix( rnorm(4), 2, 2 ) ) { 
  f <- function(x,y,p) list( A %*% y )
  plot( NA, xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1), axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
  for( i in 1:100 ) 
    lines( ode( rnorm(2), seq(0,1000, by=.1), f, NULL )[,-1] )
  box()
}
op <- par( mfrow = c(1,3), mar = 1 + c(0,0,0,0) )
for( i in c(40,66,26) ) {
  set.seed(i)
  try( g() )
}
par(op)
@
\]

To test for nonlinearity,
use the BDS test,
or the \emph{Hellinger distance} $S_\rho(k)$ between $f_{X_t, X_{t+k}}$ and $f_{X_t} f_{X_{t+k}}$
(which can be seen as a ``nonlinear correlation'')
or the moving block bootsrap 
(in a time series of 0s and 1s, estimate $\hat p$ and check if its variance is
$\sqrt{ \hat p (1-\hat p) / n }$ as it would for an iid sequence).

Non-linearity can also be assessed using \emph{surrogate data}, \ie,
time series with the same ``linear properties'', e.g.,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item ARMA surrogates: Fourier-transform the time series, randomize the phases, transform it back;
\item Amplitude-adjusted Fourier transforms (AAFT) surrogates: idem, but transform the output to recover the same distribution;
\item PPS surrogates (for aperiodic oscillations): (first coordinate of a) random walk on the reconstructed shadow attractor
\end{compactitem}
and looking at some statistic, e.g.,
correlation dimension, maximum Lyapunov exponent, nonlinear prediction error or permutation entropy.

Granger causality only works for stochastic systems. For deterministic ones,
try to predict future values of $y$ using $x$'s attractor (convergent cross-mapping, CMM):
if $x(t_a), x(t_b), \dots$ are neighbours of $x(t_0)$,
then $y(t_a), y(t_b), \dots$ should be close to $y(t_0)$,
and increasingly so as more data arrives.
Delayed cross-mapping check how the forecast skill changes with the lag between $x$ and $y$.

To detect changepoints, compute the SSA decomposition before and after a possible changepoint,
compute the scalar product (\ie, correlation) between the first eigenvectors,
and compare with surrogate data to have a $p$-value.

To detect changepoints, one can also estimate the distribution of values on a moving window:
it will usually have one peak, but may have two (or be a mixture distribution)
during transition periods 

\emph{Phenomenological modeling} estimates the coefficients of an ODE from data,
e.g., with regression, assuming the coefficients are polynomial,
approximating the derivatives with high (4th) order centered finite differences,
with the lasso or adaptive lasso ($\va{\beta}^\nu$ penalty, $0<\nu<1$)
to deal with multicolinearity.

Implementations in
\texttt{tseriesChaos} (\texttt{mutual}, \texttt{d2}, \texttt{false.nearest}, \texttt{lyap\_k}),
\texttt{nonlinearTseries} (\texttt{RQA}),
\texttt{tseries\allowbreak Entropy} (\texttt{Srho.test.ts}, \texttt{surrogateAR}, \texttt{Trho.test.AR}, \texttt{Trho.\allowbreak test.SA}),
\texttt{Rssa},
\texttt{fractal} (\texttt{surrogate}),
\texttt{multispatialCCM},
\texttt{MESS},
\texttt{extRemes}.






\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A differential equation for modeling Nesterov's accelerated gradient method:\\ theory and insight} \\
  E. Su et al.
\end{book}
\d{Nesterov momentum}
\begin{align*}
  x_k &= y_{k-1} - s \nabla f( y_{k-1} ) \\
  y_k &= x_k + \dfrac{ k-1}{k+2} ( x_k - x_{k-1} )
\end{align*}
by a can be modeled by an ODE
\[ \ddot x + \dfrac3t \dot x + \nabla f(x) = 0. \]
The $O(1/t^2)$ convergence rate is guaranteed if the damping term $\tfrac3t \dot x$ 
is sufficiently large: 3 is the smallest constant guaranteeing it but, 
for large $t$, the system is over-damped.
This suggests resetting $t$ whenever 
$\langle \dot x , \ddot x \rangle = 0 $ or, 
for Nesterov momentum, 
resetting $k$ whenever $\Delta x$ starts decreasing.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning gradient descent:\\ better generalization and longer horizons} \\
  K. Lv et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The optimization algorithm can be added to the model,
to have the computer choose the best stochastic gradient descent variant.
For instance, 
one can use the gradient as input and the updates as output of a coordinate-wise  LSTM
cell whose latent variables are the first and second moments of the previous gradients (momentum and AdaDelta's normalizing factors).
To help make the algorithm scale-invariant: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Randomly scale the functions to minimize at training time;
\item Add a random convex function, 
$g(x) = \tfrac1n \sum ( x_i - v_i )^2$ 
to make the objective better-behaved;
\item Do not feed the raw gradient and momentum to the LSTM, but rescale them with the AdaDelta factor.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fighting biases with dynamic boosting} \\
  A.V. Dorogush et al. 
\end{book}
Boosting uses the same model to build the data and to compute the gradient:
this data reuse introduces bias in the gradient.
CatBoost avoids it.

\d{Oblivious decision trees} use the same criterion across an entire level of the tree.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Escaping from saddle points -- online stochastic gradient for tensor decomposition} \\
  R. Ge et al.
\end{book}
Even for non-convex functions, SGD converges to a local minimum in a polynomial number of iterations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Failures of gradient-based deep learning} \\
  S. Shalev-Shwartz et al
\end{book}
Gradient-based optimization fails when: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The gradient is too flat and/nor not informative enough 
about the position of the minimum;
\item The sample gradient gas a low signal-to-noise ratio;
\item The condition number is high.
\end{compactitem}

Possible solutions include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Preconditioning;
\item Intermediate losses in end-to-end architectures;
\item ``Forward-only update rule'': 
if a derivative is zero, in intermediate computations, replace it with 1.
\end{compactitem}

