\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Batched high-dimensional Bayesian optimization via structural kernel learning}\\
  Z. Wang et al. (2017) \\
\end{book}
Bayesian optimization can scale to high-dimensional functions by assuming a latent additive structure,
and learning it with Gibbs sampling 
(Dirichlet prior for the mixing proportions: 
$\theta \sim \text{Dir}(\alpha)$, 
$z_j \sim \text{Multi}(\theta)$).
Function evaluations can be batched 
using a determinantal point process (DPP) and processed in parallel.




%% Conformal inference
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distribution-free predictive inference\\ for regression} \\
  J. Lei et al. (2017)
\end{book}
\d{Conformal inference} provides distribution-free, finite-sample prediction sets.
With a naive approach, fitting the model on 
$(x_1,y_1),\dots,(x_n,y_n)$
and using the quantiles of the residuals to form a prediction interval
$\hat \mu ( x_{ n+1 } ) \pm q_\alpha$,
the prediction intervals are too small.

Instead, fit the model on $(x_n,y_1)\dots,(x_n,y_n), (x_{n+1},y)$,
for all values of $y$: 
the prediction set is the set of $y$'s in their 
$\hat\mu ( x_{n+1} ) \pm q_{y,\alpha} $ interval.

To reduce the computations, do not refit the model for all values of $y$, 
but split the data into equal-sized parts, use the first to fit the model, and the second to estimate the residual quantiles.
Using $N$ splits, each at level $\alpha/N$, and taking the intersection of the corresponding intervals, reduces randomness but enlarges the interval -- the Bonferroni effect dominates.
The Jackknife (using quantiles of the leave-out-one residuals)
relies on a similar idea,
but is more fragile,
unless we impose strong conditions on the estimator.
%% Not read in detail -- they apply those ideas to wide data (lasso, etc.)


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate quantiles\\ and multivariate L-moments} \\
  A. Decurninge (2014)
\end{book}
Univariate \d{L-moments} can be defined from order statistics: 
\begin{align*}
\lambda_1 &= E[X] \\
\lambda_2 &= \tfrac12 E[ X_{(2)} - X_{(1)} ] \\
\lambda_3 &= \tfrac13 E[ X_{(3)} - 2X_{(2)} + X_{(1)} ]  \\
\cdots \\
\lambda_r &= \dfrac1r \sum (-1)^r \binom{r-1}{k} E[ X_{ r-k : k } ]
\end{align*}
or by projecting the quantile function $Q$ 
on the shifted Legendre polynomials 
(an orthogonal basis of $L^2([0,1];\RR)$ for the scalar product 
$\langle f, g \rangle = \int_0^1 fg$ -- Legendre polynomials use $\int_{-1}^1 fg$).

Multivariate L-moments can be defined using: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The monotone transport between the distribution of interest and 
$\text{Unif}[0,1]^d$ to define the quantiles, $Q(F(u)) = T(u)$;
\item The basis of products of univariate Legendre polynomials.
\end{compactitem}
Other target distributions (the min-copula, a standard Gaussian)
and other transports (e.g., Rosenblatt),
or other orthogonal polynomials (Hermite)
can be used.

The \d{monotone transport} from $\mu$ to $\nu$ is the only measurable map $T$ such that $T_\# \mu = \nu$ and $T = \nabla \phi$, for some convex function $\phi$
(it is guaranteed to exist if $\mu = \text{Unif}[0,1]^d$ or, more generally, if it gives zero measure to sets of Hausdorff measure at most $d-1$)
[Brenier's theorem].

The \d{Rosenblatt transport} is 
\[
T(x_1,\dots,x_d) = \begin{pmatrix}
  F_{X_1}  (x_1) \\
  F_{ X_2 | X_1 = x_1 } (x_2) \\
  F_{ X_3 | X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2 } (x_3) \\
  \vdots
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

The \d{power Voronoi diagram} 
is defined using the ``distance''
\[ d(x_i,u) = \n{ u - x_i }^2 + w_i . \]
The convex piecewise linear function 
\[ \phi_h(u) = \max_i u \cdot x_i + h_i, \]
for $h_i = -\tfrac12 ( \n{ x_i }^2 + w_i )$
has constant gradient $x_i$ on each Voronoi cell.
The monotone transport between $\mu$ and 
the empirical distribution of a sample $x_1,\dots,x_n$
is $T = \nabla \phi_h$ with 
\[ h = \argmin \int \phi_h(u) du - \dfrac1n \sum h_i \]
(which can be computed by Newton's method).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecaster's dilemma:\\ extreme events and forecast evaluation} \\
  S. Lerch et al.
\end{book}
Forecasts are often only evaluated when an extreme event occurs
(earthquake, financial crisis, etc.):
this encourages forecasters to always predict disaster -- 
whenever they will be examined, they will be right.
There is no fix for point forecasts,
but probability forecasts are more flexible.
The joint distribution of forecast $F$ and observation $Y$ can be decomposed as 
\[ [F,Y] = [F] [Y | F] = \text{sharpness} \times \text{calibration}. \]
The quality of a forecast distribition $F$ for an observation $y$ can be measured by the logarithmic score 
\[ \text{LogS}(F,y) = - \log f(y) \]
or by the continuously ranked probability score 
\[ \text{CRPS}(F,y) = \int_{-\infty} ^{+\infty} \bigl( F(y) - \mathbf 1_{y \leq z } \bigr)^2 dz . \]
There are weighted variants: 
\begin{align*}
  \text{CL} (F,y) &= - w(y) \log \dfrac{ f(y) }{ \int \! w f } \\
  \text{twCRPS}(F,y) &= \int w(z) \bigl( F(z) - \mathbf 1_{ y \leq z} \bigr) ^ 2 dz.
\end{align*}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Cram\'er distance\\ as a solution to biased Wasserstein gradients} \\
  M.G. Bellemare et al.
\end{book}
In machine learning, the Kullback-Leibler divergence (relative entropy)
is often used to measure the ``distance'' between the data and the fitted model.
Instead, one can use the \emph{Wasserstein metric},
which does not only consider probabilities,
but also proximities.

It is scale-invariant 
\[ d( cX, cY) \leq \va{ c } ^ \beta d(X,Y) \]
and sum-invariant
\[ d( A+X, A+Y ) = d(X,Y) \]
but does not have unbiased sample gradients 
\[ 
\nabla_{\!\theta\,} d(P, Q_\theta) \neq 
\E_{ x_1,\dots,x_m \sim p } 
\nabla_{\!\theta\,} d\Bigl( \sum \delta_{x_i}, Q_\theta \Bigr).
\]

Prefer the \d{Cram\'er distance}
\[ 
d_2(P,Q) = \biggl(
  \int \bigl( F_P(x) - F_Q(x) \bigr) ^2 dx 
\biggr)^2,
\]
which is scale-invariant, sum-invariant, and has unbiased sample gradients.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A note on the evaluation of generative models} \\
  L. Theis et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The \emph{Jensen-Shanon divergence} (JSD) is a symmetrized KL divergence: 
\[ \text{JSD}(p,q) = 
\dfrac12 \text{KL}\Bigl( p \Bigm\| \dfrac{p+q}2 \Bigr) + 
\dfrac12 \text{KL}\Bigl(q \Bigm\| \dfrac{p+q}2 \Bigr) 
\]
The \emph{maximum mean discrepancy} (MMD) id 
\[
\text{MMD}(p,q) = \E_{\substack{
  x, x' \sim p \\ y, y' \sim q }} \bigl[
    k(x,x') - 2 k(x,y) + k(y,y')
  \bigr] ^ { 1/2 }.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random projection\\ through multiple optical scattering:\\ approximating kernels at the speed of light} \\
  A. Saade et al.
\end{book}
Analogue, optical devices can efficiently compute random projections.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Alpha-beta divergences discover\\ micro and macro structures in data} \\
  K. Narayan et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The Kullback-Leibler divergence 
\[ D(P \| Q ) = \sum_{i \neq j} P_{ij} \log \dfrac{ P_{ij} }{ Q_{ij} } \]
minimized by t-SNE
can be replaced by the $\alpha$-$\beta$-divergence
\[
D(P \| Q ) = 
\dfrac1{\alpha\beta} \sum_{i \neq j} 
-P_{ij}^\alpha Q_{ij}^\beta + 
\dfrac{\alpha}{\alpha + \beta} P_{ij}^{\alpha + \beta} + 
\dfrac{\beta}{\alpha+\beta} Q_{ij}^{\alpha+\beta}.
\]
Different values of $(\alpha, \beta)$ focus on macro-structures, 
micro-structures or hard-to-classify observations 
($\alpha \approx 1$, $\beta \approx 0$, $\alpha + \beta \approx 1$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Entropic graph-based posterior regularization} \\
  M.W. Libbrecht et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To encourage variables associated to nodes in a graph to have similar posterior distributions when those nodes are linked,
add a penalty for their KL divergence.
More precisely, 
\emph{posterior regularization}
introduces an auxiliary joint distribution $q$,
adds a regularizer to it, and a penalty to make it close to the the posterior $p$,
\[ \text{Penalty}(p) = \max_q - D( q \| p ) + \text{Penalty}(q). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistence topology of syntax} \\
  A. Port et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Persistence homology}
 shows that language evolution cannot be summarized by a tree,
but often needs a more general phylogenetic \emph{network} -- 
for Indo-European,
the first homology generator comes from ancient Greek.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spin class models of syntax\\ and language evolution} \\
  K. Siva et al.
\end{book}
Spin model to model and forecast the evolution of a weighted graph with binary feature vectors at each node.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph grammars, insertion Lie algebras\\ and quantum field theory} \\
  M. Marcolli and A. Port
\end{book}
\emph{Graph grammars} generalize context-free and context-sensitive grammars,
and model parallelism: 
the production rules replace a subgraph (a single, non-terminal node, in the case of context-free grammars)
with a new graph.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Prevalence and recoverability of syntactic parameters in sparse distributed memories} \\
  J.J. Park et al.
\end{book}
A \emph{Kanerva network} (or \emph{sparse distributed memory})
learns a mapping 
$\FF_2^N \to \{ \pm 1 \}$
from a dataset $(x_i, y_i)_i$ as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pick $k$ points 
$\hat x_i, \dotsm \hat x_k \in \FF_2^N$, each associated with a count variable 
$\hat y_1, \dots, \hat y_k$, initialized at $0$;
\item For each observation $(x_i,y_i)$, find the points within distance $d$ of $x_i$, 
and increment/decrement their counts.
\end{compactitem}
Here, they are used to check which language features (SVO order, etc. -- from the SSWL (now TerraLing) or WALS databases)
can be recovered.
They can also learn the identity map 
$\FF_2^N \to \{\pm1\}^N$, \ie, learn a dataset.

Implementation in \texttt{msbrogli/sdm}.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Submodularity in data subset selection\\ and active learning} \\
  K. Wei et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To select a small sample of data on which to train a classifier with minimal performance loss,
consider the data log-likelihood, 
\[ \ell(S) = \sum_{i \in V} \log p\bigl(x_i, y_i | \theta(s) \bigr) \]
\ie, the likelihood of the whole data $V$ when the model is estimated
on a subset $S \subset V$.
For the naive Bayes or the nearest neighbour classifiers,
this is a difference of submodular functions but, 
under the constraints $\va S = k$
and $S$ balanced (same label distribution as $V$),
it reduces to a modular function,
which can be approximately maximized with the lazy greedy algorithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Langevin diffusions and\\ the Metropolis-adjusted Langevin algorithm} \\
  T. Xifara et al.
\end{book}
Given a diffusion $dX_t = \mu(X_t) dt + \sigma(X_t) dW_t$,
the probability density function $p(x,y)$ 
satisfies the Fokker-Plank equation 
\[ 
\dfrac{ \partial p }{ \partial t } = 
- \dfrac{ \partial ( \mu p ) }{ \partial x } + 
\dfrac12 \dfrac{ \partial ( \sigma^2 p ) }{ \partial x^2 }
\]
or \[
\dfrac{ \partial p }{ \partial t } = -
\mathbf 1' \nabla_x ( \mu p ) + \dfrac12 \mathbf 1'
\nabla_x^2 ( \sigma \sigma' p ) \mathbf 1
\]
in dimension $n$.
One can therefore build a diffusion with a prescribed stationary distribution, e.g., 
\[
d X_t = \dfrac12 \nabla \log \pi dt + dW_t 
\]
or 
\[ dX_t = 
\dfrac12 A \nabla \log \pi dt + \Gamma dt + \sqrt A d W_t \]
(with $\Gamma(x) = 0$ if $A(x)$ does not depend on $x$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FairTest: discovering unwarranted associations in data-driven applications} \\
  F. Tram\`er et al.
\end{book}
\d{Unwarranted associations} are statistically significant associations, 
in a subpopulation,
between a protected attribute 
and an output, 
with no accompanying explanatory factor.
Examples include
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Unintended side effects (e.g., discounted prices if there is a competitor nearly exclude low-income areas); 
\item Large errors affecting a subpopulation (e.g., future health prediction for the elderly).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The dual-sparse topic model: mining focused topics and focused terms in short text} \\
  T. Lin et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Spike-and-slab prior for sparse topic mixtures and sparse word usage.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A unified model\\ for unsupervised opinion spamming detection\\ incorporating text generality} \\
  Y. Xu et al.
\end{book}
Hierarchical Bayesian model for spam detection, 
combining text, user and item features,
estimated with Gibbs-EM, \ie, alternating between collapsed Gibbs sampling and variational inference gradient descent.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian post-selection inference\\ in the linear model} \\
  S. Panigrahi et al.
\end{book}
In \emph{selective inference} (adaptive data analysis),
the analyst looks at the data before deciding which question to ask.
This can be modeled by \emph{conditioning} on selection, \ie, by using a \emph{truncated} log-likelihood, for a sequence model ($k$ largest statistics, BY correction) or a (generalized) linear model 
(through a convex approximation of the truncated likelihood).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning the nonlinear geometry of high-dimensional data: models and algorithms} \\
  T. Wu and W.U. Bajwa (2015)
\end{book}
\emph{Union-of-subspace} (UoS) models have trouble when the subspaces are close:
constrain the subspaces to be close, using a distance on the Grassmanian, e.g.,
$d(S_1,S_2) = \n{ D_1 - P_{S_2} D_1 }_F $
where $D_i$ is an orthonormal basis of $S_i$ and $P_{S_2}$ is the projection on $S_2$
-- it can also be defined from the \emph{principal angles} $\theta^k$ as 
$d(S_1,S_2) = \sqrt{ s - \sum \cos^2 \theta_{12}^k }$,
where $s$ is the subspace dimension (for nonlinear data, use a kernel).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable Gaussian processes for characterizing multidimensional change surfaces} \\
  W. Herlands et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Mixture models 
\[ f(x) = \sum p_i f_i(x), \quad
\mathbf p = \text{softmax}( \mathbf w(x) ), \]
with a Gaussian process prior on $\mathbf w$,
can be seen as generalizations of changepoint models.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deterministic independent component analysis} \\
  R. Huang et al.
\end{book}
Variants of the HKICA algorithm
(FastICA, \ie, finding directions maximizing some measure of non-Gaussianity, only has theoretical guarantees in the noiseless case): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sample $\phi, \psi \sim N(0,I)$;
\item Compute 
  $m_p(\eta) = E[ (\eta'X)^p]$, 
  $f = \tfrac{1}{12} ( m_4 - 3 m_2^2 )$, 
  $\nabla^2 \hat f$;
\item Compute the eigenvectors of 
  $( \nabla^2 \hat f \phi) ( \nabla^2 \hat f \psi) ^{-1}$, 
  $A = ( \mu_1 | \cdots | \mu_d )$;
\item We then have $\mathbf x \approx A \mathbf s + \text{Gaussian noise}$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On restricted nonnegative matrix factorization} \\
  D. Chistikov et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The \emph{nonnegative rank} of an $n\times m$ nonnegative matrix $M$ is the smallest $d$ for which we can find nonnegative $n \times d$ and $d \times m$ matrices $W$ and $H$ such that $M = WH$.
If $M$ is rational, $W$ and $H$ need not be so, \ie, 
$\text{rank}_+^{\QQ} > \text{rank}_+^{\RR}$ in general (in dimensions beyond 3).
The \emph{restricted NMF} requires $\operatorname{rank} M = \operatorname{rank} W$,
defining a \emph{restricted nonnegative rank}.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distributional rank aggregation\\ and an axiomatic analysis} \\
  A. Prasad et al. (2015)
\end{book}
\emph{Rank aggregation} is the problem of combining several rankings (e.g., from search engines) into one.
Distributional rank aggregation  only uses the distribution (histogram) of the rankings. The normative axioms of ``social welfare theory'',
non-dictatorship, universality, transversality, Pareto efficiency
and independence to irrelevant alternatives (Arrow's impossibility theorem)
can be relaxed and satisfied.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parallel resampling in the particle filter} \\
  L.M. Murray et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The propagation and weighting steps of sequential Monte Carlo (SMC, particle filters)
are easy to parallelize, but the resampling step is less so -- try: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Rejection sampling, if an upper bound on the weights is known: 
\[ 
j \sim \text{Unif} \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket
\text{ while } 
\text{Unif}(0,1) > w_1 / w_{\text{max}}
;
\]
\item Metropolis: run $N$ identical Markov chains in parallel, for $B$ steps, sampling from 
$\text{Multinom}(\mathbf w)$ -- only the weight ratios $w_i / w_j$ are needed.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable nearest neighbor algorithms\\ for high-dimensional data} \\
  M. Muja and D.G. Lowe (2014)
\end{book}
The FLANN library for approximate nearest neighbour search
automatically selects the algorithm among: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Randomized $k$-d forests}: 
multiple $k$-d trees searched in parallel, in which the split dimension is chosen randomly from the top 5 with highest variances -- non-axis-aligned variants exist but do not perform significantly better;
\item Priority search \emph{$k$-means trees}: 
find $k$ clusters and process each cluster recursively until they reach a minimum size -- 
it is just another way of hierarchically partitioning the space.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Six myths\\ of polynomial interpolation quadrature} \\
  L.N. Trefethen
\end{book}
Equispaced interpolation may diverge (Runge phenomenon)
but \emph{Chebychev interpolation},
\ie, interpolation on $[-1,1]$
at $x_k = \cos( j \pi / n )$, 
converges for Lipschitz continuous functions.

It can be numerically evaluated with the \emph{barycentric formula}
\begin{align*}
  \ell_j (x) &= \prod_ { i \neq j } \dfrac{ x - x_i }{ x_j - x_i } \\
  \ell(x) &= \prod ( x - x_i ) \\
  \ell'(x_j) &= \prod_{ i\neq j} ( x_i - x_j ) \\
  \ell_j(x)  &= \dfrac{ \ell(x) }{ x - x_j } \dfrac1{\ell'(x_j)} = \dfrac{ \ell(x) }{ x - x_j } w_j \\
  g(x) &= \sum \ell_j(x) y_j = \ell(x) \sum \dfrac{ w_j }{ x - x_j } y_j \\
  1 &= \ell(x) \sum \dfrac{ w_j }{ x - x_j } 1 \\
  g(x) &= \dfrac{ g(x) }1 = \dfrac{ 
  \displaystyle
  \sum \dfrac{ w_j }{ x - x_j } y_i 
  }{ 
  \displaystyle
  \sum \dfrac{ w_j \mathstrut }{ x - x_j }
  }
\end{align*}
The monomials $x^k$ are well-suited to find roots on the circle;
for roots on $[-1,1]$, prefer Chebychev polynomials.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fixed points of belief propagation\\ an analysis via polynomial continuation} \\
  C. Knoll et al.
\end{book}
\d{Homotopy continuation} is a way of numerically finding the solutions of a system of equations,
by following a homotopy from an easy-to-solve system to the desired one.
In the case of a polynomial system, 
the \emph{polyhedral homotopy method} finds all the solutions
(it scales better than Gr\"obner bases).

Non-free implementation in Hom4PS-3.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Transfinite game values in infinite chess} \\
  C.D.A. Evans and J.D. Hamkins
\end{book}
A position has value (at most) $n$ if there is a strategy leading to a check-mate in (at most) $n$ moves,
whatever the opponent does.

A position with value $\omega$, the first infinite ordinal,
is a position with Black to play resulting in a mate-in-$n$ position for White, with $n$ a large as Black wants.

A position with value $\omega+n$ is $n$ moves away from a position with value $\omega$.

A position with value $n\omega$ is a position in which Black can announce $n$ times 
``I will make an announcement in $k_i$ moves'' or, for the last one, 
``I will lose in $k_n$ moves'', with the $k_i$'s as large as desired.

In a position with value $k \omega$, Black can play to be in a position with value $k\omega$, with $k$ as large as desired. 
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A position in infinite chess with game value $\omega^4$} \\
  C.D.A. Evans et al.
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What is a Leavitt path algebra?} \\
  G. Abrams
\end{book}
Given a momoid $M$, it is possible to find a ring $R$ so that 
$(\mathfrak{Mod}\, R, \oplus) \simeq M$.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Highly comparative time-series analysis:\\ the empirical structure of time series\\ and their methods} \\
  B.F. Fulcher et al. (2013)
\end{book}
  The \texttt{hctsa} Matlab library (GPL) provides 1000 time series features for classification, clustering and biclustering, to be used with the UCR time series dataset for nearest neighbour search.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{{FATS}: Feature analysis for time series} \\
  I. Nun et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The \textsc{fats} Python library computes 30 time series features (to classify astronomy time series).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Feature-based time series analysis} \\
  B.D. Fulcher (2017)
\end{book}
  Look at global features:
  \begin{compactitem}[--]
  \item Distribution of the values ($\mu$, $\sigma$, etc.);
  \item Stationarity, e.g., \( \text{StatAv} = \dfrac{ \text{sd}( \bar x_{1:w}, \bar x_{w+1:2w}, \dots )}{ \text{sd}(x)} \)
  \item  ACF, Fourier and wavelet coefficients;
  \item Nonlinear time series analysis: Lyapunov coefficients, correlation dimension, correlation entropy;
  \item Entropy: approximate entropy, sample entropy, permutation entropy;
  \item Scaling (fractality): Hurst exponent (DFA);
  \item Time series models: parameters and goodness-of-fit statistics, properties of the residuals;
\end{compactitem}
and subsequence features:
  \begin{compactitem}[--]
  \item  If the time series are aligned, consider $\text{Mean}(x_{i:j})$, $\text{Sd}(x_{i:j})$,
  $\text{Slope}(x_{i:j})$ -- these are families of features: select them greedily, using the entropy gain (as in decision trees);
  \item \emph{Shapelets} are small patterns that may appear in a time series,
  \[ \min_i d(x_{i-w : i+w }, \text{shapelet} ) \]
  selected by genetic algorithms -- you can also look at their position or the number of occurrences.
  \end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Automatic time series phenotyping\\ using massive feature extraction} \\
  B.D. Fulcher and N.S. Jones (2016)
\end{book}
  Normalize the time series with a ``robust sigmoid'',
  \[ x \leftarrow \left( 1 + \exp - \dfrac{ x - \text{median} }{ 1.35 \times \text{IQR} } \right)^{-1} \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Large-scale unusual time series detection} \\
  R.J. Hyndman et al. (2015)
\end{book}
A few more time series features:
  \begin{compactitem}[--]
  \item Lumpiness: variance of the variances in 24-observation blocks;
  \item Spikiness: variance of the l.o.o. variances of the STL residuals;
  \item Level and variance change: maximum change in mean or variance in consecutive 24-observation blocks;
  \item KL score: maximum Kulback-Leibler divergence
  between kernel density estimates of consecutive 48-observation blocks;
  \item Flat spots: maximum run length of the (10-value) quantized time series.
  \end{compactitem}
  Some features are not defined: peak, trough, curvature.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecastable component analysis} \\
  G.M. Goerg (2013)
\end{book}
Find orthogonal directions, maximizing \emph{forecastability}, measured by
  $\Omega = 1 - H / \log 2\pi$, where $H$ is the \d{spectral entropy}, \ie, the entropy of the spectral density (the spectrum, rescaled to be a probability density on $\SS^1$). Implementation in \texttt{ForeCA::Omega}.
  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A scalable method for time series clustering} \\
  X. Wang et al. (2004)
\end{book}
Cluster time series using the following features:
  \begin{compactitem}[--]
  \item \( \text{Trend} = 1 - \dfrac{ \var( \text{detrended} ) }{ \var( \text{raw} ) } \)
  \item \( \text{Seasonality} = 1 - \dfrac{ \var( \text{deseasoned} ) }{ \var( \text{raw} ) } \)
  \item Periodicity: position of the first peak in the ACF, it it is positive, and significantly higher than the previous trough;
  \item Serial correlation: Box-Pierce statistic;
  \item Non-linearity: Ter\"asvirta test statistic;
  \item Skewness, kurtosis, Hurst exponent, maximum Lyapunov exponent.
  \end{compactitem}
  All those features have values in $[0,+\infty)$:
  rescale them parametrically
  \[ q \mapsto \dfrac{ e^{aq} - 1 }{ b + e^{aq} } \]
  so that quantiles 10\% and 90\% correspond to time series with and without the features
  (respectively an example from the literature, and white noise).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{{TSClust}: an {R} package\\ for time series clustering} \\
  P. Montero and J.A. Vilar (2014)
\end{book}
The \texttt{TSclust} package defines the following dissimilarity measures:
  \begin{compactitem}[--]
  \item $L^p$ distance;
  \item Dynamic time warp (DTW) distance,
  \[ d(x,y) = \min_r \sum_{ (i,j) \in r } \va{ x_i - y_j } ;\]
  \item Fr\'echet distance, 
  \[ d(x,y) = \min_r \max_{ (i,j) \in r } \va{ x_i - y_j } ;\]
  \item Correlation:
  \[ \sqrt{ 2 ( 1 - \cor(x,y) ) }, \qquad
  \left( \dfrac{ 1 - \cor(x,y) }{ 1 + \cor(x,y) }  \right) ^ {\beta/2} ;
  \]
  \item $L^2$ distance between the ACF, with constant or exponentially decaying weights;
  \item $L^2$ distance between the periodograms, the normalized periodograms, the integrated periodograms (Cram\'er--von Mises distance between the spectral densities);
  \item Distance between the wavelet coefficients, the $AR(\infty)$ coefficients
  or the cepstral coefficients;
  \item Statistics from ARMA tests checking if the two time series come from the same model;
  \item Distance between the SAX representations (obtained by aggregating, and then quantizing with quantiles);
  \item  Divergence between the distributions of the permutations induced by an $m$-dimensional embedding;
  \item Normalized compression distance and variants:
  \[ \dfrac{ C(xy) - \min \{ C(x), C(y) \} }{ \max \{ C(x), C(y) \} } , \qquad \dfrac{ C(xy) }{ C(x) C(y) } ; \]
  \item Distance between the $k$-step-ahead forecast distributions,
  from a \emph{sieve bootstrap} (\ie, bootstrap on the residuals of an AR or similar model).
  \end{compactitem}
  Those measures can be corrected, for correlation or complexity,
  by multiplying by
  \[ \dfrac1{ 1 + \exp k \cor(\Delta x, \Delta y) }
  \ \text{ or }\ 
  \dfrac{ \max\{ \text{CE}(x), \text{CE}(y) \} }{ \min\{ \text{CE}(x), \text{CE}(y) \} } ,
  \]
  where $\text{CE}(x) = \n{ \Delta x}_2^2$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{{pdc}: an {R} package\\ for complexity-based clustering of time series} \\
  A.M. Brandmaier (2015)
\end{book}
To cluster time series,
use an $m$-dimensional embedding, replace the $m$-dimensional vectors with the corresponding permutation (their ranks), 
and use the \emph{Hellinger distance} 
(a metric approximation of the KL divergence) 
between the permutation distributions as a dissimilarity measure. 
Choose the embedding dimension $m$ and the delay $\tau$ 
to minimize the (average) normalized 
\emph{permutation entropy}
\[ - \dfrac1{m!} \sum_{ \sigma \in \mathfrak S_n } p_\sigma \log p_\sigma. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Visualising forecasting algorithm performance using time series instance spaces} \\
  Y. Kang et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Time series features
  (spectral entropy, trend and seasonality strength from the STL decomposition, period (if known), ACF, optimal Box-Cox transformation to make the time series linear) can be used to
  \begin{compactitem}[--]
  \item Check how diverse the M3 dataset (in the \texttt{MComp} package) is;
  \item Add synthetic time series in its gaps, using a genetic algorithm;
  \item Visualize which forecasting algorthms perform well with which type of time series.
  \end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{{JIDT}: an information-theoretic toolkit\\ for studying the dynamics of complex systems} \\
  J.T. Lizier (2014)
\end{book}
The JIDT Java library provides several information-theoretic measures for time series, centered on \emph{transfer entropy}, and several estimators.
% in particular the ``Kraskov-St\"ogbauer technique for continuous variables''.
  \begin{align*}
%  &\text{Entropy} && H(X) = - \sum p(x) \log_21 p(x) = E[ - \log_2 p(x) ] \\
%  &\text{Conditional entropy} && H(Y|X) = H(X,Y) - H(X) \\
%  &\text{Mutual information} && I(X;Y) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X,Y) \\
%  &\text{Conditional MI} && I(X;Y | Z) = H(X|Z) + H(Y|Z) - H(X,Y|Z) \\
  &\text{Entropy rate} && H(X_{n+1} | X_n ) \\
  &\text{Active information storage} && I(X_n ; X_{n+1} ) \\
  &\text{Transfer entropy} && I(Y_{n+1}; X_{n+1} | X_n ) \\
  &\text{Conditional TE} && I(Y_{n+1}; X_{n+1} | X_n, Z_n )
  \end{align*}
  (you can replace $X_n$ with $(X_n,\dots,X_{n+k-1})$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiscale entropy analysis} \\
  M. Costa et al. (2000)
\end{book}
Compute the average of the datapoints on non-overlapping windows of size $\tau$, and then the sample entropy; plot the sample entropy versus $\tau$ -- for $1/f$ noise, it is flat.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introducing nonlinear time series analysis\\ in undergraduate courses} \\
  M. Perc (2004)
\end{book}
\emph{Nonlinear time series analysis} studies stationary, deterministic time series,
  from data alone, \ie, without knowledge of the underlying dynamical system.
\par
The dynamical system can often be recovered from the \emph{Takens embedding},
\ie, the cloud of points $(x_t, x_{t+\tau}, \dots, x_{t + (m-1)\tau}) \in \RR^m$.
To choose the delay $\tau$, check when the ACF decreases to $1/e$,
or when the AMI (auto-mutual information) reaches its first minimum --
though there is no reason such a minimum should exist.
To choose the embedding dimension $m$, notice that two points close in the embedding should remain close at the next time step -- if not, \ie, if
\[ \n{p_t - p_s } \leq \sigma \ \text{ but }\  \dfrac{ \va{ x_{t+m\tau} - x_{s + m\tau} } }{ \n{p_t - p_s } } > 10, \]
  they are \emph{false nearest neighbours} (FNN) -- we are missing some information about them.
  Plot the proportion of FNN as a function of $m$ to help select $m$.
\par
To test for stationarity, split the time series into non-overlapping segments and compare
the mean and standard deviation on each segment,
or look at the \emph{cross-prediction error} $\delta_{ij}$ when forecasting the next observation in segment $j$ with a $k$-NN model fitted on segment~$i$.
\par
To recover the dynamical system,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Split the embedding space into boxes;
\item Whenever a trajectory crosses a box, compute its average direction, as a unit vector;
\item Average the vectors in each (non-empty) box -- if the directions are consistent, the average will still have unit norm.
\end{compactitem}
\par
The \emph{Lyaponov exponent} measures how fast  nearby trajectories diverge; it can be estimated by choosing a point $p_s$ close to $p_t$
and averaging \[ \dfrac1\nu \log \dfrac{ \n{ p_{t+\nu} - p_{s + \nu} } }{ \n{ p_t - p_s } } \]
with $\nu = m\tau$, and replacing the point $p_s$ at each step, trying to keep the same direction.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonlinear time series analysis revisited} \\
  E. Bradley and H. Kantz (2015)
\end{book}
Nonlinear time series analysis reconstructs the state space of a dynamical system, from data alone, to compute fractal dimension, Lyapunov exponents (instability), Kolmogorov-Sinai entropy (unpredictability), etc. -- but noise and the finiteness of the data call for caution.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The fraction of pairs of data points at distance at most $\epsilon$ (the \emph{correlation sum})
scales as $\epsilon^{D_2}$, where $D_2$ is the \emph{fractal (Renyi) dimension}.
\item The time series should be long enough: as a rule of thumb, $N > 42^{D_2}$.
\item 
To test if the values are significantly different from a null model,
use \emph{surrogate time series}:
Fourier transform the data, randomize the phases, transform back, map onto the original values (only keeping the rank), and somehow recover the correlations.
\item 
Permutation entropy.
\item 
Recurrence plots and \emph{recurrence quantitative analysis} (RQA):
proportion of black points in the plot, in diagonals, in vertical lines, length distribution of those lines.
\item 
Network characteristics: interpret the recurrence matrix as a graph and look at some of its metrics: centrality, shortest paths, clustering coefficients, etc.
\end{compactitem}
Distinguishing noise from chaos is difficult.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Complex network approach\\ for recurrence analysis of time series} \\
  N. Marwan et al. (2009)
\end{book}
The following five RQA  features
  \begin{compactitem}[--]
  \item Maximum diagonal length;
  \item Laminicity: proportion of points in vertical lines;
  \item Link density (for the network whose adjacency matrix is the recurrence plot);
  \item Clustering coefficient;
  \item Average minimum path length
  \end{compactitem}
  can identify different regimes of the logistic map
  $x_{n+1} \leftarrow \alpha s_n (1-x_n)$ as $\alpha$ varies,
  or (when estimated on a moving window) the different regimes of a real-world time series.







\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Characterizing the structural diversity\\ of complex networks across domains} \\
  K. Ikehara and A. Clauset (2016)
\end{book}
To compare networks, look at scale-invariant features: 
clustering coefficient, 
degree assortativity
and network motifs (compared with a random graph with the same degree distribution).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time series classification with COTE: the collective of transformation-based ensembles} \\
  A. Bagnall et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Classify time series using ensemble methods (random forests or \d{rotation forests} -- random forests with a PCA on a random subset of the variables at each node),
on data transformed into the time (shapelets), frequency (periodogram) or autocorrelation (ACF, PACF, AR coefficients) domains.

The \d{shapelet features} are the distances to smaller time series $s$ (``shapelets''),
\[ d(s,x) = \min_{\substack{ y \subset x \\ \text{len}(x) = \text{len}(s) }} d(s,y); \]
those smaller time series are taken from subsequences of the training data, pruned to retain only the most discriminating ones (small distances to one class, large distance to the others)
and to avoid redundancy.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting at scale} \\
  S.J. Taylor and B. Letham
\end{book}
To forecast time series, Facebook uses a GAM-like model, with trend, seasonality (Fourier) and (irregular) holiday components -- curve fitting is easier than time series modeling.
The trend is either piecewise linear (with a large number of potential changepoints, 
but a Laplace prior keeps them sparse),
or a logistic growth model, with changepoints accounting for changes in market size
(e.g., using the population from the World Bank).

R/Python implementation, via Stan, in \texttt{fbprophet}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distributed and parallel time series feature extraction for industrial big data applications} \\
  M. Christ et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Select time series features (from \texttt{hctsa}) using tests for 
\( \text{feature} \indep \text{outcome} \)
(Fisher, Kolmogorov-Smirnov or Kendall rank, depending on whether the variables are binary or continuous)
and the Benjamini-Yekutieli (BY) procedure to control the false discovery rate (FDR).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The all-relevant feature selection\\ using random forest} \\
  M.B. Kursa and W.R. Rudnicki (2010)
\end{book}
To find all the relevant features (instead of just a non-redundant set),
add a ``shadow'' variable for each feature, by shuffling its values, fit a random forest, and mark as relevant the features more important than the best random one; remove them, and iterate until nothing left is relevant.

R implementation in \texttt{Boruta}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Surrogate time series} \\
  T. Schreiber and A. Schmitz (1999)
\end{book}
To test if a time series is linear, e.g., 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $H_0$: iid;
\item $H_0$: ARMA (``Gaussian linear'');
\item $H_0$: Nonlinear transformation of an ARMA process;
\end{compactitem}
(these are \emph{composite} null hypotheses), look at statistics such as 
\emph{time reversibility} $E[(X_n - X_{n-\tau})^3]$
or the nonlinear prediction error (nlpe).
The $p$-value can be computed with simulations. 
The bootstrap would estimate an ARMA model on the data and generate new data from the fitted model.
\d{Constrained randomization} generates data with the same ARMA estimates, \ie, with the same ACF.
For instance, one can Gaussianize the data, compute its FFT, randomize the phase, transform back, and restore the distribution, 
but this tends to give a flatter power spectrum. 
Instead, start with a shuffled time series and iterate: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Force the spectrum to be correct, by rescaling it to the correct amplitudes, keeping the phases;
\item Force the distribution to be correct (resampling).
\end{compactitem}
Conserving the Fourier amplitudes preserves the periodic ACF, not the ACF: 
to limit the problem, measure the corresponding artefacts and remove a few observations at the beginning or the end to minimize them.
Alternatively, use simulated annealing to find a permutation of the original time series with a small discrepancy between the ACFs (not exactly the minimum discrepancy: that would be the original time series).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A large set of audio features for sound description (similarity and classification)\\ in the Cuidado project} \\
  G. Peeters (2004)
\end{book}
Audio features include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Spectral shape (linear or exponential fit; properties of the spectral density);
\item ACF, zero crossing rate;
\item Length of the ADSR (envelope) phases;
\item Fundamental frequency, inharmonicity (weighted average distance between successive spectral peaks and multiples of the fundamental),
harmonic deviation (deviation of the harmonics from the spectral envelope), odd/even harmonic ratio, tristimulus (relative energy in harmonics 1, 2--4 and 5+).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time-varying market beta:\\ does the estimation methodology matter?} \\
  B. Nieto et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Comparison of several time-varying beta estimators (prefer Kalman or GARCH):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Regression on a moving window, with constant or decreasing weights;
\item Multivariate GARCH
  \begin{align*}
    \text{BEKK:}&& H_{t+1} &= C' C + A' \epsilon_t \epsilon_t' A + B' H_t B \\
    \text{DCC:} && H_t     &= D_t R_t D_t
  \end{align*}
  (where $D_t$ is a diagonal GARCH model)
  and asymmetric variants;
\item Kalman filters
  \begin{align*}
    \text{random walk:}&& \beta_{t+1} &= \beta_t + \text{noise} \\
    \text{random observations:} && \beta_t &= \beta_0 + \text{noise}
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Arbitrated ensemble for time series forecasting} \\
  V. Cerqueira et al.
\end{book}
To combine time series forecasts, model how each model performs for different types of inputs 
and how this performance changes with time
(the metamodel forecasts the model error).



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph-theoretic properties of the darkweb} \\
  V. Griffith et al.
\end{book}
The Darkweb is only loosely connected: most sites have no outgoing links.
Data from the tor2web proxy \texttt{onion.link},
crawled with \texttt{scrapinghub.com} (commercial),
starting from \texttt{directoryvi6plzm.onion} and \texttt{ahmia.fi/onions}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A divide-and-conquer framework\\ for distributed graph clustering} \\
  W. Yang and H. Xu (2015)
\end{book}
The generalized stochastic block model divides the nodes into clusters and outliers;
the edge probability is $p$ for notes inside the same cluster and $q<p$ otherwise 
(edges between clusters or involving an outlier).
It can be fitted, in parallel, 
with a divide-and-conquer approach:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Randomly partition the nodes into groups;
\item Find clusters in each of those subgraphs;
\item Build a ``fused'' graph, whose nodes are the subgraph clusters, 
and with an edge between two clusters if the edge density exceeds some threshold $t$ ($q<t<p$);
\item Find clusters in the fused graph.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Real-time community detection\\ in large social networks on a laptop} \\
  B. Chanberlain et al.
\end{book}
The \d{minhash} of a subset $A \subset \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket$ is 
$h_\sigma(A) = \min \{ \sigma(a), a \in A \}$
for a (fixed) random permutation $\sigma \in \mathfrak S_n$.
The \emph{Jaccard similarity} can be approximated with minhashes: 
\[ 
J(A,B) = \dfrac{ \va{ A \inter B } }{ \va{ A \union B } } = 
\P_{ \sigma \in \mathfrak S_n } [ h_\sigma(A) = h_\sigma(B) ]. 
\]
Given a large graph, 
the neighbours of a node can be compressed (lossily) using minhash signatures 
(e.g., 1000 hash functions for 1,000,000 nodes),
and locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) can be used to find approximate nearest neighbours: 
with the Jaccard similarity, 
they form a weighted graph,
on which we can compute (local) communities.
The Jaccard similarity also helps solve coverage problems 
(``find the smallest number of athletes that have influence over half of Twitter'') -- 
$\va{ A \union B } = \va A + \va B - \va{ A \inter B}$
links the number of neighbours of a community and of a node and their Jaccard similarity.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Querrying $k$-truss community\\ in large and dynamic graphs} \\
  X. Huang et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The \emph{$k$-truss} of a graph $G$ is the largest subgraph in which each edge is in at least $k-2$ triangles.

A \emph{$k$-truss community} is a maximal $k$-truss subgraph in which any two edges are reachable through a series of adjacent triangles.

After putting the $k$-truss subgraphs in a suitable index, one can retrieve the $k$-truss community of a vertex in linear time.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian dynamic modeling and analysis\\ of streaming network data} \\
  X. Chen and K. Irie (2015)
\end{book}
To model the flow of visitors on a graph (website), 
consider a non-Gaussian state space model 
\begin{align*}
  i&: \text{node} \\
  t &: \text{time} \\
  n_{it} &: \text{number of occupants of node $i$ at time $t$} \\
  x_{ijt} &: \text{flow from $i$ to $j$ between $t-1$ and $t$} \\
  x_{0it} & \sim \text{Pois}\phi_{it} \text{ new visitors} \\
  x_{i \cdot t} &\sim \text{Mult}(n_{i,t-1}, \theta_{i \cdot t}) \text{ movement between nodes} \\
  \phi_{it} &: \text{arrival rate} \\
  \theta_{ijt} &: \text{transition probabilities} \\
  \theta_{ijt} &= \dfrac{ \phi_{ijt} }{ \sum \phi_{i \cdot t }} \\
  \phi_{ijt} &= \mu_t \alpha_{ij} \beta_{jt} \gamma_{ijt} \\
  \beta_{jt} &: \text{attractivity of node $j$ at time $t$}
\end{align*}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unpaired image-to-image translation\\ using cycle-consistent adversarial networks} \\
  J.Y. Zhu et al.
\end{book}
CycleGAN translates (unpaired) images between two domains (paintings and photographs, horses and zebras, summer and winder, aerial photographs and maps, etc.)
by learning two mappings 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2,>=stealth,inner sep=2pt, outer sep = 1pt]
\node (A) at (0,0) {$\mathscr X$};
\node (B) at (1,0) {$\mathscr Y$};
\draw[->] ([yshift=2pt]A.east) -- node[auto,above=0pt,pos=.5]{\footnotesize$G$} ([yshift=2pt]B.west);
\draw[->] ([yshift=-2pt]B.west) -- node[auto,below=0pt,pos=.5]{\footnotesize$B$} ([yshift=-2pt]A.east);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
with an adversarial loss to make the distribution of $G(\mathscr X)$ 
indistinguishable from that of $\mathscr Y$,
and to ensure 
$F \circ G \simeq \text{id}$ (cycle-consistency).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{InfoGAN: interpretable representation learning by information maximizing\\ generative adversarial nets} \\
  X. Chen et al.
\end{book}
To make the latent space of GANs more interpretable (in an ICA-like way),
split the noise into an incompressible part $z$ and a ``code'' $c$,
and solve the minimax problem 
\begin{align*}
& \min_G \max_D V_{\text{GAN}} (D,G) - \lambda I\bigl( c ; G(z,c) \bigr) \\
& V_{\text{GAN}}(D,G) = \E_{ x \sim \text{Data} } [ \log D(x) ] + {}\\
& \hphantom{V_{\text{GAN}}(D,G) = {}} 
\E_{ (c,z) \sim \text{Noise} } \bigl[ \log\bigl( 1 - DG(c,z) \bigr) \bigr]
\end{align*}
after replacing the mutual information with a (variational) lower bound.

\nextbook
