\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple neural network module\\ for relational learning} \\
  A. Santoro et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Relation networks} (RN) are built from modules of the form 
\[ x \longmapsto f_\phi\biggl( \sum_{ij} g_\theta( x_i, x_j ) \biggr) \]
where $f$ and $g$ are multi-layer perceptrons
(but it is the same $g_\theta$ for all pairs).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast algorithms\\ for convolutional neural networks} \\
  A. Lavin and S. Gray
\end{book}
The \emph{Winograd minimal filtering algorithm} 
computes 1- or 2-dimensional filters with the minimal number of multiplications -- for the small filters used on CNNs, it is faster than the FFT.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HyperNetworks} \\
  D. Ha et al.
\end{book}
Hypernetworks are networks generating the weights of another network;
they can be trained end-to-end with backpropagation. 
They can generate non-shared LSTM (or CNN) weights.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep feature interpolation\\ for image content changes} \\
  P. Upchurch et al.
\end{book}
Deep neural networks can describe images with features in a linear (not curved) space: 
linear interpolation in a direction (age, gender, glasses, smile, etc.) produces high-resolution image transforms. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fitted learning:\\ models with awareness of their limits} \\
  N. Kardan and K.O. Stanley
\end{book}
For classification problems, 
use $k$ neurons for each class in the output layers (instead of 1),
train to set their values to 1, but use their \emph{product} at test time -- 
this limits overly-confident generalizations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unsupervised learning for physical interaction through video prediction} \\
  C. Finn et al.
\end{book}
Predict motion, at the pixel level, assuming that pixels do not move large distances,
and that pixels are only transformed in a small number of ways
(pixels belonging to the same object move in the same way).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Residual networks behave like\\ ensembles of relatively shallow networks} \\
  A. Veit et al.
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Aggregated residual transformations\\ for deep neural networks} \\
  S. Xie
\end{book}
\[
