\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Input convex neural networks} \\
  B. Amos et al.
\end{book}
Neural nets can be constrained to be convex functions of their input,
by requiring the weights to be positive
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1,yscale=.5,>=stealth,inner sep=2pt, outer sep = 1pt]
\node[draw] (y) at (0,0) {$y$};
\node[draw] (z1) at (1,0) {$z_1$};
\node[draw] (z2) at (2,0) {$z_2$};
\node[draw] (z3) at (3,0) {$z_3$};
\node       (z4) at (4,0) {$\cdots$};
\node[draw] (z5) at (5,0) {$z_k$};
\draw[->] (y) -- (z1);
\draw[->] (z1) -- (z2);
\draw[->] (z2) -- (z3);
\draw[->] (z3) -- (z4);
\draw[->] (z4) -- (z5);
\draw[->] (y) -- (0,-1) -- (2,-1) -- (z2);
\draw[->] (y) -- (0,-1) -- (3,-1) -- (z3);
\draw[->] (y) -- (0,-1) -- (5,-1) -- (z5);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
(skip connections are useful for the network to remain expressive),
or of part of the input, 
\[ z_{i+1} \sim z_i \odot u_i + z_i \odot y + u_i \]
by requiring part of the input (the $z \odot u$ term) to be positive.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1,yscale=.75,>=stealth,inner sep=2pt, outer sep = 1pt]
\node[draw] (y)  at (0,0) {$y$};
\node[draw] (z1) at (1,0) {$z_1$};
\node[draw] (z2) at (2,0) {$z_2$};
\node       (z3) at (3,0) {$\cdots$};
\node[draw] (z4) at (4,0) {$z_{k-1}$};
\node[draw] (z5) at (5,0) {$z_k$};
\node[draw] (x)  at (0,1) {$x$};
\node[draw] (u1) at (1,1) {$u_1$};
\node[draw] (u2) at (2,1) {$u_2$};
\node       (u3) at (3,1) {$\cdots$};
\node[draw] (u4) at (4,1) {$u_{k-1}$};
\draw[->] (y) -- (z1);
\draw[->] (z1) -- (z2);
\draw[->] (z2) -- (z3);
\draw[->] (z3) -- (z4);
\draw[->] (z4) -- (z5);
\draw[->] (x) -- (z1);
\draw[->] (u1) -- (z2);
\draw[->] (u2) -- (z3);
\draw[->] (u4) -- (z5);
\draw[->] (x) -- (u1);
\draw[->] (u1) -- (u2);
\draw[->] (u2) -- (u3);
\draw[->] (u3) -- (u4);
\draw[->] (y) -- (0,-.6) -- (2,-.6) -- (z2);
\draw[->] (y) -- (0,-.6) -- (4,-.6) -- (z4);
\draw[->] (y) -- (0,-.6) -- (5,-.6) -- (z5);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
Such networks can be used in optimization problems, 
\[ \argmin\limits_y f(x,y; \theta). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wide and deep learning\\ for recommender systems} \\
  H.T. Cheng et al.
\end{book}
