\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Memory networks} \\
  J. Weston et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Add (classical) memory cells to a neural network: 
at each time step, the neural network decides which cells to update and how, 
using their contents together with the new data.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DeepCoder: learning to write programs} \\
  M. Balog et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Inductive program synthesis} (IPS) for a simple array manipulation language
(only linear control flow: head, last, take, drop, access, min, max, rev, sort, sum; map, filter, count, zip, scan; (+1), $(-1)$, $(\times2)$, $(/2)$, ($\times-1$), 
$({}^2)$, $(>0)$, $(<0)$, odd, even).
The neural network is only used to guide more traditional approaches: stochastic local search, depth-first search, etc.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{mixup: beyond empirical risk minimization} \\
  H. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
To help the network generalize better to examples 
slightly outside the training distribution, 
augment the data with convex combinations 
$(1-\lambda) (x_1,y_1) + \lambda (x_2,y_2)$
(use one-hot-encoding for $y$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Coherent line drawing} \\
  H. Kang et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{edge tangent flow} of an image $I$ 
is computed iteratively: 
\begin{align*}
  t_x & \leftarrow \nabla I(x) ^ \perp \\
  t_x & \leftarrow \dfrac1k \sum_{ \n{ x - y } \leq r }
  \sigma \bigl(
    \n{ \nabla I(x) } - \n{ \nabla I(y) }
  \bigr)
  (t_x \cdot t_y ) t_y.
\end{align*}
Applying a difference of Gaussians (DoG) in the direction 
orthogonal to the edge flow gives a nice line drawing.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Simplex noise demystified} \\
  S. Gustavson (2005)
\end{book}
Simplex noise is similar to Perlin noise, but replaces hypercubes with simplices, which facilitates interpolation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SATGraf: visualizing the evolution\\ of SAT formula structures in solvers} \\
  Z. Newsham et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To visualize how SAT solvers transform a boolean formula,
look at the 
\emph{clause-variable incidence graph} -- its community structure
is related to the running time.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Network risk and financial crisis} \\
  Y.A. Chen (2015)
\end{book}
Build a parametric portfolio from the Katz centrality
\[ \mathbf x = \gamma A \mathbf x + \beta \mathbf 1 \]
of the weighted network defined by the variance of residual returns.

Compute the beta of each stock wrt that portfolio; 
decompose the idiosyncratic volatility 
into ``network volatility'' and a remainder.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Media network and return predictability} \\
  L. Guo and Y. Tao (2017)
\end{book}
The connection score of two stocks $i$, $j$, is 
\[ \text{CS}_{ij} = \sum_k \text{tone}_{ik} \times \text{tone}_{jk} \]
where the sum if over all news mentioning both $i$ and $j$ and the tone is in $[-1,1]$.

The media connection index
\[ 
\dfrac{ 
  \sum_{i\neq j} \text{CS}_{ij} 
}{ 
  \sum_{ij} \text{CS}_{ij} 
}
\]  
can be used as a risk measure.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep stock representation learning: from candlestick charts to investment decisions} \\
  G. Hu et al.
\end{book}
Feed candlestick charts (\ie, images), for the FTSE 100 stocks,
to a convolutional autoencoder;
cluster them using the resulting latent representation, with a community detection algorithm (no need to specify the number of clusters);
invest in the stocks with the largest Sharpe ratio in the cluster. 
% Comparison with the top stocks in random clusters, or in sectors?


%% Smart beta

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Local explosion modelling\\ by noncausal processes} \\
  C. Gouri\'eroux and J.M. Zakoian (2016)
\end{book}
Non-causal AR(1) processes can model bubbles growing and bursting;
aggregating them allows for different rates of increase (e.g., a continuous distribution);
one can also add a (causal, Gaussian) AR(1).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The myths and limits\\ of passive hedge fund replication} \\
  N. Amenc et al. (2007)
\end{book}
There are two approaches to replicate hedge funds (neither works well): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the exposure of hedge fund returns to a set of pre-selected factors;
\item Build an option, on a reference asset (anything, e.g., S\&P 500)
  and choose the payoff function giving the desired payoff distribution 
  (``moment matching'').
  The price of that option can be used as a measure of performance.
  This ``replicating'' strategy has comparable returns, but not at the same times. 
  The first moment is not captured: the returns are lower.
  [There is a bivariate, copula-based variant but it only models 
  (portfolio, underlying), not (portfolio, fund).]
\end{compactitem}

Possible improvements to capture nonlinearities, dynamic trading, 
non-stationarity and time series properties include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Heuristic dynamic (rule-based) strategies;
\item Instrumental variables for time- and state-dependence;
\item Future factor loading forecasts;
\item Time-varying models (Kalman filter, particle filter, Markov switching);
\item Ad hoc options portfolios (e.g, ATM and OTM puts and calls on S\&P\,500);
\item Statistical techniques to estimate the options (and underlyings) needed.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An alternative approach to alternative beta} \\
  T. Toncalli and J. Te\"iletche (2007)
\end{book}
Use the Kalman filter to estimate time-dependent factor exposures.
It also decomposes the returns (for one period) into traditional (\ie, average) beta, 
alternative (difference with the average) beta and alpha (residual, intercept).
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tracking problems, hedge fund replication\\ and alternative beta} \\
  T. Roncalli and G. Weisang (2008)
\end{book}
The Kalman filter provides (Gaussian, linear) factor exposures.
The unscented Kalman filter (UKF) allows for non-Gaussian innovations.
One can add non-linear assets to the list of factors;
if they depend on a parameter (say, a strike), 
it can be modeled as a random walk, as part of the hidden state, but the model becomes nonlinear -- use a particle filter (PF, sequential Monte Carlo, SMC).
Hedge fund performance can be decomposed into: 
traditional beta, alternative beta, lag (alternative beta one period ahead), 
alternative alpha (the rest -- this includes illiquid assets).
% Details on the Kalman filter and particle filter

