\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Proofs are programs:\\ 19th century logic and 21st century computing} \\
  P. Wadler (2000)
\end{book}
\d{Frege's logic} considers judgments (e.g., $\vdash A$), 
axioms (e.g., $\vdash A \rightarrow A$) and deduction rules, e.g., 
\[ \dfrac{ \vdash B \rightarrow A \quad \vdash B }{ \vdash A }. \]

\d{Gentzen's logic} adds assumptions to the judgments
(e.g., $B_1,\dots,B_n \vdash A$ instead of $\vdash A$)
and to the deduction rules, e.g., 
\[ \dfrac{ \Gamma \vdash B \rightarrow A \quad \Delta \vdash B }{ \Gamma, \Delta \vdash A }. \]
While this may look more complex (in particular, the three if-then notions: 
$\rightarrow$ for propositions, $\vdash$ for judgments and 
$\tfrac{\ \cdot\ }\cdot$ in the inference rules), the deduction rules end up simpler and more intuitive.

\d{Typed $\lambda$-calculus} has typing judgments
(e.g., \[ x_1:B_1, \dots, x_n:B_n \vdash t:A, \]
meaning that if the $x_i$'s have types $B_i$, then $t$ has type $A$)
and the same deduction rules as Gentzen's system, e.g., 
\[ \dfrac{ \Gamma \vdash t: B \rightarrow A \quad \Delta \vdash u: B }{ \Gamma, \Delta \vdash t(u): A }. \]
(Unless we explicitly add recursion, all functions terminate and the calculus is not Turing-complete.)
Term reduction corresponds to proof simplification (Curry-Howard correspondance).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Physics, topology, logic and computation:\\ a Rosetta stone} \\
  J.C. Baez and M.M. Stay (2009)
\end{book}
Variants of monoidal categories
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item braided: 
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.25,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt,baseline=1pt]
  \draw[->] (0,1) to [out=270, in=90, looseness=1] (1,0);
  \node[fill=white] at (.5,.5) {};
  \draw[->] (1,1) to [out=270, in=90, looseness=1] (0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
\item symmetric: 
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.25,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt,baseline=1pt]
  \draw[->] (0,1) to [out=270, in=90, looseness=1] (1,0);
  \node[fill=white] at (.5,.5) {};
  \draw[->] (1,1) to [out=270, in=90, looseness=1] (0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
=
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.25,yscale=.4,inner sep=1pt, baseline = 1pt]
  \draw[->] (1,1) to [out=270, in=90, looseness=1] (0,0);
  \node[fill=white] at (.5,.5) {};
  \draw[->] (0,1) to [out=270, in=90, looseness=1] (1,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
\item cartesian: ${\otimes} = \times$
\item closed: $\otimes \dashv \ihom$
\item compact: $\underline{\text{Hom}}(X,Y) = X^* \otimes Y$
\item dagger (with a functor $\dagger: \mathfrak C \rightarrow \mathfrak C^{\textrm{op}}$, 
the identity on $\text{Ob}\, \mathfrak C$, with $f^{\dagger\dagger} = f$)
\end{compactitem}
appear in many domains, e.g., 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item cobordisms
\item tangles in $\RR^n$
% TODO
\item finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces (the inner product gives a dagger structure)
\item finite-dimensional representations of a Lie (resp. quantum) group form a compact symmetric (braided) monoidal category.
\end{compactitem}

\d{Propositional calculus} 
forms a monoidal category
with propositions as objects, 
a single morphism $X \to Y$ 
whenever $X \Rightarrow Y$, 
and $\otimes = \wedge$, $\ihom(X,Y) = X \Rightarrow Y$.
It is a \d{Heyting algebra}, \ie, a preorder 
$\mathfrak C$ such that $\mathfrak C$ and $\mathfrak C^{\textrm{op}}$ 
be cartesian closed.

\d{Linear logic} replaces $\wedge$, $\Rightarrow$, $\top$ with $\otimes$, $\multimap$ and $I$
and only requires a symmetric monoidal category, not a cartesian closed one:
there are no natural morphisms $X \to X \otimes X$ or $X \to I$, \ie, 
we cannot duplicate or delete information 
(as in quantum physics or chemistry).

Alternatively, one can consider propositions as objects and proofs as morphisms,
where the proofs are obtained from inference rules, \ie, 
natural or \emph{dinatural} transformations,
most of which come from the monoidal/braided/cartesian structure.

In \d{$\lambda$-calculus}, everything is a function. 
For instance, \d{Church numerals} are 
\begin{align*}
  0 &= \lambda f \mapsto \lambda x \mapsto x && f \mapsto \text{id} \\
  1 &= \lambda f \mapsto \lambda x \mapsto f(x) && f \mapsto f \\
  2 &= \lambda f \mapsto \lambda x \mapsto f(f(x)) && f \mapsto f^2  
\end{align*}
and addition and multiplication are 
\begin{align*}
  \text{times} &= \lambda a\  \lambda b\  \lambda f\ a(b(f)) \\
  \text{plus} &= \lambda a\  \lambda b\  \lambda f\  \lambda x \ a(f) \bigl( b(f)(x) \bigr).
\end{align*}
(I write $\lambda x \mapsto t$ or $\lambda x\ t$ instead of $(\lambda x . t)$
and dispense with many of the parentheses.)

\d{Typed $\lambda$-calculus}, where every term has a type,
forms a cartesian closed category 
with types as objects and equivalence classes of terms as morphisms
(if we do not focus on \emph{what} is computed but \emph{how} it is computed,
we get a 2-category, with terms as morphisms and equivalence classes to rewrites as 2-morphisms).

\d{Linear type theories} correspond to symmetric monoidal categories.
They can also be described by \d{combinators}. The usual ones 
\begin{align*}
  I &= \lambda x \mapsto x \\
  K &= \lambda x \mapsto \lambda y \mapsto x \\
  S &= \lambda x \mapsto \lambda y \mapsto \lambda z \mapsto x(z) \bigl( y(z) \bigr)
\end{align*}
cannot do because $K$ forgets information and $S$ duplicates it.
Each term $t$ is equivalent to 
$\text{cp}(t) \, \text{vp}(t)$, 
where $\text{cp}(t)$ is the combinator part and $\text{vp}(t)$ the variable part.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Internal set theory} \\
  E. Nelson (2002)
\end{book}
% Unfinished book on non-standard analysis
There are two ways of presenting non-standard analysis: 
either explicitly, by constructing the hyperreals,
or by tweaking the ZFC axioms (\emph{internal set theory}):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The subset axiom, to define $\{ x \in X \ :\ A(x) \}$,
  is only valid for internal formulas $A$ (those which do not use the ``standard'' predicate);
\item Transfer: $\forall^{\textrm{st}} t_1 \cdots \forall^{\textrm{st}} t_n \, (\forall^{\textrm{st}} x A \Leftrightarrow \forall x A)$, for any internal formula $A$;
\item Idealization: 
$\forall^{\textrm{stfin}} X \, \exists y \, \forall x\!\in\!X \, A \Leftrightarrow 
\exists y \, \forall^{\textrm{st}} x\!\in\!X \, A$ (for $A$ internal);
\item Standardization: 
\[ \forall^{\textrm{st}} X \  \exists^{\textrm{st}} Y \  \forall^{\textrm{st}}z \ 
   (z \in Y \Longleftrightarrow z \in X \wedge A(z) )
\] 
for any formula $A$ (internal or external),
which allows us to define 
${}^S\{ z \in X \ :\ A(z) \}$
the standard set whose standard elements are the standard elements $z$ of $X$ such that $A(z)$.
\end{compactitem}
The ``standard'' predicate is no longer limited to real numbers,
but applies to sets, functions, topological spaces, points in topological spaces, etc.

Includes exercises. 
Check \emph{Radically elementary probability theory}, 
by the same author, on probability theory with non-standard finite probability spaces. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Division by three} \\
  P.G. Doyle and J.H. Conway (1994)
\end{book}
Proving $A \times 3 \approx B \times 3 \implies A \approx B$
does not require the axiom of choice.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Developing bug-free machine learning systems with formal mathematics} \\
  S. Selsam et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The \texttt{lean} theorem prover tries to combine program verification 
(\`a la Coq) and formal mathematics (\`a la Mizar).
Here, it is used for a provably correct gradient descent implementation.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The ring of algebraic functions\\ on persistence bar codes} \\
  A. Adcock et al. (2013)
\end{book}
To turn a barcode $[x_1,y_1],\dots,[x_n,y_n]$ 
into features,
for machine learning,
use multisymmetric polynomials
$p_{ab} = \sum_i x_i^a y_i^b$, for instance
\begin{align*}
& \sum x_i (y_i-x_i) \\
& \sum (y_{\text{max}} - y_i) (y_i-x_i) \\
& \sum x_i^2 ( y_i-x_i)^4 \\
& \sum (y_{\text{max}} - y_i)^2 (y_i-x_i)^4.
\end{align*}
Barcodes form an affine (semi)algebraic ind-variety, 
with ring of algebraic functions 
$\Lambda_2 = \varinjlim k[x_1,y_1,\dots,x_n,y_n] ^ {\mathfrak S_n}$
(multisymmetric polynomials),
quotiented by an ideal 
$D = \varinjlim D_n$ corresponding to the identification of intervals of length zero.
$\Lambda_2$ is freely generated by the multisymmetric power sums
$p_{ab} = \sum_i x_i^a y_i^b$,
and $D$ by the $p_{a+1,b} - p_{a,b+1}$.

An alternative is to use the bottleneck or Wasserstein distance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistent homology} \\
  H. Edelsbrunner and D. Morozov\\
  (Handbook of discrete\\ and computational geometry, 2017)
\end{book}
The implementation details are not that complicated.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{High-dimensional topological data analysis} \\
  F. Chazal
  (Handbook of discrete\\ and computational geometry, 2017)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.}
%  Reconstruction
A subset $X \subset \RR^n$ can be represented by its distance function
\[ \phi: \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl} \RR^n & \rightarrow & \RR_+ \\ x & \mapsto & d(x,X) \end{array} \right. \]
(it is proper and $x \mapsto \n x ^2 - \phi(x)^2$ is convex).
To increase the robustness to outliers, 
consider measures instead of subsets, 
e.g., $\mu = \sum_{ x \in X } \delta_x$ if $X$ is discrete,
and the functions 
\[ \delta_{\mu,\ell}: \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl} 
\RR^n & \rightarrow & \RR_+ \\ 
x & \mapsto & \inf \bigl\{ r > 0 \ :\ \mu\bigl( B(x,r) \bigr) > \ell \bigr\}
\end{array} \right. \]
They are not smooth, but the \d{distance to measure} $d_{\mu m}^2$ is
\[ d_{\mu m}^2: \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl} 
\RR^n & \rightarrow & \RR_+ \\ 
x & \mapsto & \dfrac1m \displaystyle \int_0^m \delta_{\mu,\ell} (x) ^2 d\ell
 \end{array} \right. \]
(it is the average of the squared distances from $x$ to its $k$ nearest neighbours).
If two measures $\mu$ and $\nu$ are close
(for the Wasserstein distance),
then the sublevel sets of their distance-to-measure functions are homotopy equivalent 
(\d{reconstruction} theorem). 

\textbf{2.}
%  Homology
For many tasks, full reconstruction is not needed
and a few homotopy or \d{homology} invariants suffice.
In particular, the \d{nerve} of an open cover $X = \bigcup_i U_i$
all of whose finite intersections are contractible is homotopy equivalent to $X$ (nerve theorem).

\textbf{3.}
%  Persistence
\d{Persistent homology} keeps track, not only of the dimensions of the homology groups (Betti numbers)
of $\bigcup_{ x \in X} B(x,\epsilon)$ as $\epsilon$ increases,
but also of individual connected components, cycles and cavities --
the result, the $(\text{birth}_i, \text{death}_i)$ pairs, 
forms the \d{persistence diagram}.
The \d{bottleneck distance} between two persistence diagrams $D$ and $D'$ 
is the smallest $\delta > 0$ for which we can match all points of $D$ at distance at least $\delta$ from the diagonal with a point of $D'$ at distance at most $\delta$.
It is bounded by twice the Gromov-Hausdorff distance.

\textbf{4.}
% Mapper
The \d{Mapper} algorithm visualizes a dataset $X \subset \RR^n$ 
and a function $f : X \to \RR$
by choosing an open cover $\bigcup I_i$ of $f(X) \subset \RR$
by intervals, clustering the points in each $f^{-1}(I_i)$
into a (finite) cover,
and plotting the 1-skeleton of the corresponding cover of $X$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Topological analysis of financial time series: landscapes of crashes} \\
  M. Gidea and Y. Katz (2017)
\end{book}
The \d{persistence landscape} is obtained from the $(\text{birth}, \text{death})$
persistence diagram by flipping it 45\textdegree, associating a piecewise function to each 
birth-death pair, and considering the sequence of functions given by the $k$th largest values.
While persistence diagrams form a (non-complete) metric space for the Wasserstein distance,
persistence landscapes form a Banach space for the $L^p$ norm.
(Measure theory on an infinite dimensional Banach space is a little more complicated: 
for instance, the notions of weak (Pettis) and strong (Bochner) integrability differ.)

\[
<<PersistenceLandscape,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
A <- matrix( c(1, 2, 3, 3.5,
               2.5, 4.5, 4.25, 4), nc = 2 )
op <- par( mar = c(1,1,1,1), mfrow = c(1,3) )
plot(A, pch = 16, cex = 2, xlim = range(A), ylim = range(A), axes = FALSE, asp = 1 )
abline(0,1, lwd = 1)
abline( h=.9, v=.9 )
text( 4.5, 1, "birth", adj = c(0,-.2) )
text( 1, 4.5, "death", adj = c(1.2,.4), xpd = NA )
segments( A[,1], A[,1], A[,1], A[,2], lwd = 3 )
segments( A[,1], A[,2], A[,2], A[,2], lwd = 3 )

f <- function(x,y=x) x+y
g <- function(x,y=x) y-x
plot( f(A[,1],A[,2]), g(A[,1],A[,2]), cex = 2, pch = 16, xlim = c(2,9), ylim = c(0,3), axes = FALSE, asp = 1 )
abline( h=0 )
segments( f(A[,1]), g(A[,1]), f(A[,1],A[,2]), g(A[,1],A[,2]), lwd = 3 )
segments( f(A[,1],A[,2]), g(A[,1],A[,2]), f(A[,2]), g(A[,2]), lwd = 3 )

f <- function(x,y=x) x+y
g <- function(x,y=x) y-x
plot( NA, xlim = c(2,9), ylim = c(0,3), axes = FALSE, asp = 1 )
abline( h=0 )
segments( f(A[1:2,1]), g(A[1:2,1]), f(A[1:2,1],A[1:2,2]), g(A[1:2,1],A[1:2,2]), lwd = 3 )
segments( f(A[1:2,1],A[1:2,2]), g(A[1:2,1],A[1:2,2]), f(A[1:2,2]), g(A[1:2,2]), lwd = 3 )

segments( 4,0, 4.5,.5, lwd = 3.1, col = "blue" )
segments( 4.5,.5, 5,0, lwd = 3.1, col = "blue" )
segments( f(A[3,1]), g(A[3,1]), f(A[3,1],A[3,2]), g(A[3,1],A[3,2]), lwd = 3, col = "blue" )
segments( f(A[3,1],A[3,2]), g(A[3,1],A[3,2]), f(A[3,2]), g(A[3,2]), lwd = 3, col = "blue" )

segments( f(A[4,1]), g(A[4,1]), f(A[4,1],A[4,2]), g(A[4,1],A[4,2]), lwd = 3, col = "orange" )
segments( f(A[4,1],A[4,2]), g(A[4,1],A[4,2]), f(A[4,2]), g(A[4,2]), lwd = 3, col = "orange" )
par(op)
@
\]

The $L^1$ or $L^2$ norm of the persistence landscape of the daily returns of a few indices 
(S\&P 500, DJIA, Nasdaq, Russel 2000)
on a 50-day window may help forecast crises.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical topological data analysis\\ using persistence landscapes} \\
  P. Bubenik (2015)
\end{book}
Initial paper on persistence landscapes.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A persistence landscapes toolbox\\ for topological statistics} \\
  P. Bubenik and P. D\l otka
\end{book}
Standalone, file-based 
C++ implementation (computing persistence landscapes, their averages, their $L^p$ distances).
Also check the \texttt{TDA} R package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Topology-based data analysis identifies\\ a subgroup of breast cancers with a unique mutational profile and excellent survival} \\
  M. Nicolau et al. (2011)
\end{book}
The \d{mapper} algorithm takes a cloud of points $\mathscr X$
and a (Morse) function $f : \mathscr X \to \RR$
(e.g., a random projection, a measure of density, a distance to a baseline, an eccentricity measure),
applies a clustering algorithm to the points in each 
$f_a = f^{-1}( \left] a - \epsilon, a + \epsilon \right[ )$,
and builds a graph with those clusters as nodes
and edges between $x \in f_a$ and $y \in f_b$ if $x \inter y \neq \nothing$
(partial clustering, discrete Morse theory). In R, check \texttt{TDAmapper}.

It is another way of ``filtering'' a cloud of points into a graph,
keeping most of its features (loops, appendages, etc.).

% Nice figure 1

% The Mapper algorithm was also explained in G. Carlsson's Topology and Data
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Topological methods for the analysis of high dimensional datasets and 3D object recognition} \\
  G. Singh et al.
\end{book}
In the Mapper algorithm, using several Morse functions defines a simplicial complex
instead of a graph -- 
and letting $\epsilon$ vary leads to persistence homology.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Topology Toolkit} \\
  J. Tierny et al.
\end{book}

% The report is too dense...

Topological data analysis (TDA) is not a single algorithm (Mapper, or persistence homology,
depending in who you ask), but a smogasbord of techniques.
TTK is a library (C++, BSD, accessible from Python)
and graphical toolkit (built on Paraview) 
using low-dimensional TDA (mostly discrete Morse theory)
to visualize scientific data (PDE solutions and other scalar fields).

The data is given as a Morse function $f : \mathscr M \to \RR$, \ie, a scalar field
(or several of them);
in contrast with high-dimensional TDA,
the PL manifold $\mathscr M$ is often uninteresting ($\RR^2$ or $\RR^3$).

TDA studies the transitions  of the Betti numbers 
$\beta_k f^{-1}\{x\}$ or 
$\beta_k f^{-1}\left] -\infty, x \right]$ or 
$\beta_k f^{-1}\left[ x, +\infty \right[$ 
as $x$ varies.

A point is \emph{critical} if one of 
\begin{align*}
  \text{Link}^+(p) &= \{ q \in \text{Neigh}(p) \ :\ f(q) > f(p) \} \\
  \text{Link}^-(p) &= \{ q \in \text{Neigh}(p) \ :\ f(q) < f(p) \}   
\end{align*}
is not simply connected.
% Bivariate f: Jacobi set
Critical points can be paired into birth-death pairs,
defining a \emph{persistence diagram}.
To simplify $f$, one can prune short-lived pairs 
(those whose persistence, 
$f(\text{death}) - f(\text{birth})$ is small).
The \emph{persistence curve}
is the plot of the number of critical points with persistence at least $x$, as $x$ increases.

The \emph{Reeb graph} segments $\mathscr M$ into regions where 
$\pi_0f^{-1} c$ does not change.

The \emph{Morse complex} is defined by the attraction bassins of $f$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Subsampling methods for persistent homology} \\
  F. Chazal et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Compute the persistence landscape on several subsamples and average them.




\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Equilibrated adaptive learning rates\\ for non-convex optimization} \\
  Y.N. Dauphin et al.
\end{book}
% Clear explanation of preconditioning
In the presence of different curvatures, in particular around a saddle point, 
gradient descent oscillates a lot. 
\d{Preconditioning} uses a change of variables, 
\begin{align*}
  \theta &= D^{-1/2} \hat \theta \\
  \hat f( \hat \theta ) &= f( D^{-1/2} \hat\theta ) = f(\theta) \\
  \nabla \hat f &= D^{-1/2} \nabla \\
  \nabla^2 \hat f &= D^{-1/2\prime} ( \nabla^2 f ) D^{-1/2} \\
  \theta_{t+1} &\leftarrow \theta_t - \eta D^{-1} \nabla f( \theta_t ).
\end{align*}
The \emph{absolute Hessian} 
(defined from the eigen-decomposition of the Hessian, by taking the absolute value of the eigenvalues)
gives a perfect conditioner (the curvature is the same in all directions), $D = \va{H}$,
but it is computationally expensive.

The \emph{Jacobi conditioner} is the diagonal of the Hessian. It could be estimated as 
\[ \diag H = \E_{ v \sim \text{Unif}(\{ \pm 1 \} ^n ) } [ v \odot Hv ]. \]
AdaGrad, AdaDelta, RMSProp are other diagonal conditioners.

The \emph{equilibrium conditioner} is the diagonal of the absolute Hessian; 
it can be defined as the $L^2$ norm of the rows of the Hessian; 
it can be estimated as 
\[ \n{ H_{i \cdot} }^2 = \E_{ v_i \sim N(0,1) } [ (Hv)^2 ]. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A smart stochastic algorithm\\ for nonconvex optimization\\ with applications to robust machine learning} \\
  A. Aravkin and D. Davis
\end{book}
Machine learning minimizes 
$\sum\limits_{1 \leq i \leq n} f_i(x)$.
Trimmed machine learning minimizes 
$\sum\limits_{1 \leq i \leq k} f_{(i)}(x)$,
where $f_{(i)}(x)$ are the order statistics of $\bigl( f_i(x) \bigr)_i$.

The problem can be formulated as 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w \in \RR^n, \  x \in \mathscr H \\
  \text{To minimize} & \dfrac1n \sum_i w_i f_i(x) + r_1(w) + r_2(x) \\
  \text{Where} & r_1 = \mathbf1_{ \Delta_h } \\
  & \Delta_h = \{ w \in [0,1]^n \ :\ w' \mathbf 1 \leq h \}.
\end{array}
\]
and solved by proximal alternating minimization 
($ w \leftarrow \operatorname{prox}_{r_1}(\cdots)$, 
$ x \leftarrow \operatorname{prox}_{r_2}(\cdots)$).
With mini-batches, use variance reduction (SVRG).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model calibration with neural networks} \\
  A. Hernandez (2016)
\end{book}
Given enough training data, optimization can be replaced with a trained neural net.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pymanopt: a Python toolbox for optimization on manifolds using automatic differentiation} \\
  J. Townsend et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Automatic differentiation  can also compute (first and second) derivatives on Riemannian manifolds.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to learn\\ for global optimization of black box functions} \\
  Y. Chet et al.
\end{book}
A recurrent neural network (RNN: LSTM or differentiable computer)
can learn to reproduce what a Bayesian blackbox optimizer does
(it uses its memory to store information about the previous function evaluations).
It is much faster than GP-based algorithms, 
and competitive in case of model mis-specification.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding deep learning requires rethinking generalization} \\
  C. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
We still do not know why deep neural networks work well:
VC dimension, 
Rademacher complexity,
uniform stability, explicit/implicit regularization, model capacity
fail to explain why they generalize so well in some cases (real data) but not others.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A tutorial on Bayesian optimization\\ of expensive cost functions, with applications to active user modeling and hierarchical reinforcement learning} \\
  E. Brochu et al. (2010)
\end{book}
% Clear, detailed. Good if you only want to read one paper on Bayesian optimization

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An entropy search portfolio\\ for Bayesian optimization} \\
  B. Shahriari et al.
\end{book}
Use a portfolio of acquisition functions 
and follow that giving the largest decrease in the uncertainty of the location of the minimizer
(or sample accordingly).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A theoretical analysis of\\ optimization by Gaussian continuation} \\
  H. Mobahi and J.W. Fisher (2015)
\end{book}
Optimization by continuation [or mollifying] starts to optimize an easy simplification (e.g., a convex relaxation) of the problem, and progressively transforms it into the actual task.

\[
<<GaussianContinuation,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
set.seed(1)
n <- 100
x <- seq(0,1, length=n)
y <- cumsum( rnorm(n) )
y <- y - x * y[n]
y <- y + 10 * ( 2*x - 1 )^ 2
f <- function( span = .75 )
  predict( loess( y ~ x, span=span ) )
g <- function(y) {
  plot( x, y, type = "l", lwd = 3, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
  i <- which.min(y)
  points( x[i], y[i], pch=16, cex = 3, col = "#193296" )
  box()
}
op <- par( mfrow=c(1,4), mar = c(1,1,1,1) )
g( f(.75) )
g( f(.2) )
g( f(.1) )
g( y )
par(op)
@
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The irace package: iterated racing\\ for automatic algorithm configuration} \\
  M. L\'opez-Ib\'a\~nez et al.
\end{book}
Implementation of the iterated F-race algorithm, with a few extensions
(other statistical tests, restarts, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Guaranteed non-convex optimization: submodular maximization\\ over continuous domains} \\
  A.A. Bian et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The notion of \emph{submodularity} can be generalized to continuous domains:
for $f: \RR^n \to \RR$, the following conditions are equivalent.
\begin{compactenum}[(i)]
\item $f(x) + f(y) \geq f(x\wedge y) + f(x \vee y)$,
  where $\wedge$ and $\vee$ are the coordinate-wise min and max;
\item $\forall x \ \forall i\neq j \ \dfrac{ \partial^2 f }{ \partial x_i \partial x_j } \leq 0 $;
\item $f(\lambda e_i + a) - f(a) \geq f(\lambda e_i + b ) - f(b)$,
  whenever $a\leq b,\ a_i = b_i, \ \lambda \geq 0$,
  where $e_i$ is the $i$th basis vector.
\end{compactenum}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Best subset selection\\ via a modern optimization lens} \\
  D. Bertsimas et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Best subset selection for linear regression can be done exactly for $p \leq 30$ (\texttt{leaps}),
with the lasso, or with \emph{non-convex penalized regression};
the minimax concave penalty (MCP), implemented in \texttt{Sparsenet},
is quadratic for $\beta$ small and then constant;
the smoothly clipped absolute deviation (SCAD) penalty is linear, then quadratic, then constant.

Progress in mixed integer programming makes solving the exact problem reasonable for 
$p=100$, $n=1000$.

Hard thresholding computes 
$ H_k(x) = \argmin\limits_{ \n{ \beta }_0 \leq k } \n{ \beta - c }_2^2. $

If $g$ is Lipschitz and convex, 
\[ g(\eta) \leq g(\beta) + \dfrac L2 \n{ \eta - \beta }_2^2 + \langle \nabla g(\beta), \eta - \beta \rangle. \]

To solve 
\[ \operatorname*{Minimize}_\beta g(\beta) \text{ subject to }
\n{ \beta }_0 \leq k \]
for $g$ lipschitz and convex, 
iterate 
\[ \beta_{m+1} \leftarrow H_k\Bigl( \beta_m - \dfrac1L \nabla g(\beta_m ) \Bigr) \]
(discrete projected gradient)
until 
$\n{ \Delta \beta }_2 \leq \epsilon$;
then solve the continuous problem for the corresponding non-zero coordinates.

A line search $\beta_{m+1} = (1-\lambda) \beta_m + \lambda H$ gives better empirical performance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Subgroup discovery} \\
  M. Atzmueller (2005)
\end{book}
\emph{Subgroup discovery} (SD) is a frequent-itemset-based supervised clustering 
technique: 
after extracting frequent association rules for the target variable, 
select a small number of them 
with good quality (measured by some statistical test: $\chi^2$, gof, etc.)
so that they hardly overlap and have good coverage.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Anytime discovery of a diverse set of patterns with Monte Carlo Tree search} \\
  G. Bosc et al.
\end{book}
Pattern mining is a form of structured learning 
(progressively build patterns, one element at a time): 
Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) produces more diverse rules than greedy approaches.
% Subgroup discovery, Learning to search

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On $b$-bit min-wise hashing for large-scale regression and classification with sparse data} \\
  R.D. Shah and N. Meinshausen (2016)
\end{book}
Large-scale datasets (large $p$, large $n$, but sparse)
can be tackled with random projections
($X \mapsto XA$, $A$ random) or sketches ($X \mapsto AX$).
\emph{$b$-bit min-wise hashing} reduces a large sparse binary matrix $X$ as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pick a random permutation of the columns;
\item In each row, find the index (in the permuted matrix) of the first non-zero value;
\item Use the last $b$ bits of those indices as $b$ new columns in the compressed matrix;
\item Repeat a few times with more permutations.
\end{compactitem}
``Similarity'' between rows is preserved.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse solutions to nonnegative linear systems and applications} \\
  A. Bhaskara et al.
\end{book}
To solve $Ax=b$, where $A$, $x$, $b$ have nonnegative coefficients and $\n{b}_1 = 1$,
increment $x$ coordinatewise while keeping the ``potential'' 
$\Phi(x) = \sum_j b_j( 1 + \delta ) ^ { (Ax) _j / b_j }$ small;
a small number of iterations leads to a sparse approximate solution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Phase recovery from a Bayesian point of view: the variational approach} \\
  A. Dr\'emeau and F. Krzakala
\end{book}
The phase recovery problem, 
finding $x \in \CC^n$ such that $y = \va{ D x }$, can be solved by alternating projections, 
convex relaxation, or variational Bayes.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Katyusha: the first direct acceleration\\ of stochastic gradient methods} \\
  Z. Allen-Zhu (2016)
\end{book}
Katyusha momentum combines variance reduction and momentum.
\begin{align*}
  x_{n+1} & \leftarrow \lambda z_n + \mu x^* + (1-\lambda -\mu) y_n \\
  i &\sim U( \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket ) \\
  \nabla_{n+1} &\leftarrow \nabla f(x^*) + \nabla f_i(x_{n+1}) - \nabla f_i(x^*) \\
  y_{n+1} &\leftarrow x_{n+1} - \beta \nabla_{n+1} \\
  z_{n+1} &\leftarrow z_n - \alpha \nabla_{n+1} \\
  x^* &\text{ updated from time to time}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Incorporating Nesterov momentum into Adam} \\
  T. Dozat
\end{book}
% Clear motivation/explanation of NAG


