\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Strongly-typed recurrent neural networks} \\
  D. Balduzzi and M. Ghifary (2016)
\end{book}
Physicists' dimension analysis suggests RNN, LSTM, GRU variants.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning scalable deep kernels\\ with recurrent structure} \\
  M. Al-Shedivat et al.
\end{book}
Adding a Gaussian process (GP) layer to a recurrent neural net (RNN)
and using the negative log-marginal likelihood
as objective is problematic: 
the objective no longer factorizes over the data and stochastic optimization cannot be used -- 
but semi-stochastic alternating gradient descent
(full gradient for the GP parameters, stochastic gradient for the RNN) is good enough.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Professor forcing: a new algorithm\\ for training recurrent networks} \\
  A. Lamb et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Recurrent neural networks (RNN) generate sequences one character at a time, each conditional on the previous ones.
During training, one can use: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The previous characters of the ground truth sequence (``teacher forcing'', maximum likelihood);
\item The previous outputs;
\item A mixture of both;
\item An adversarial approach, training another model to distinguish between true and generated sequences, and using its performance as a penalty.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved semantic representations\\ from tree-structured\\ long short-term memory networks} \\
  K.S. Tai et al.
\end{book}
LSTMs can be generalized from chains to trees.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep recursive neural networks\\ for compositionality in language} \\
  O. \.Irsoy and C. Cardie
\end{book}
