\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{AtomNet: a deep convolutional neural network for bioactivity prediction in structure-based drug discovery}\\
  I. Wallach et al.
\end{book}
CNNs can leverage molecule shapes (on a 3-dimensional grid) for drug discovery.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unsupervised representation learning with deep convolutional generative adversarial networks}\\
  A. Radford et al. (2016)
\end{book}

\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Replace pooling layers with strided convolutions (discriminator) or fractionally strided convolutions (generator);
\item Remove fully-connected layers;
\item Use batch normalization;
\item Use ReLU (generator), tanh (generator output) and leaky ReLU (discriminator).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convolutional networks on graphs\\ for learning molecular fingerprints} \\
  D. Duvenaud et al.
\end{book}
To fingerprint molecules (or graphs),
for each node, compute a hash of its neighbours, 
then a hash of the neighbours' hashes, and so on (circular fingerprints).
Alternatively, use a graph convnet with large random weights and tanh activations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometric deep learning on graphs\\ and manifolds using mixture model CNNs} \\
  F. Monti et al.
\end{book}
CNNs can be defined on arbitrary graphs or manifolds, not just lattices in Euclidian space.
For instance, for each vertex $x$, and each neighbouring vertex $y \in \mathscr N(x)$,
define pseudo coordinates 
$\mathbf u(x,y)$
(e.g., geodesic polar coordinates $(\rho,\theta)$ on a surface, or the degree on a graph),
apply a weight function 
$\mathbf w \bigl( \mathbf u(x,y) \bigr)$
(e.g., a Gaussian or triangular kernel, possibly with a learnable parameter)
and set 
\[
  D_j(x) f = \sum_{ y \in \mathscr N(x) } 
  \mathbf w \bigl( \mathbf u(x,y) \bigr) f(y).
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph stream algorithms: a survey} \\
  A. McGregor
\end{book}
Many graph problems can be solved, at least approximately, with streaming data, \ie, without storing the whole graph in memory 
(but you still need $\Omega(\#V)$ storage):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Test connectivity by building a spanning forest (a set of edges): 
  add the next edge $\{u,v\}$ if there is currently no path from $u$ to $v$;
\item Approximate distances by building a subgraph: add the next edge $\{u,v\}$ if the current $d(u,v)$ is above some threshold (\ie, if the new edge closes a small loop);
\item Minimum spanning tree (MST);
\item Approximately count triangles by considering the vectors with coordinates
$x_T = \#\text{edges in }T$ where $T \in \mathscr P_3(V)$;
\item Greedy matching;
\item Random walks ($\sqrt t$ passes on the data suffice: stitch $\sqrt t$ walks of length $\sqrt t$).
\end{compactitem}
Many streaming algorithms use a \emph{linear sketch}, \ie, a random projection 
(of the adjacency or some related matrix).
Algorithms for sliding window graphs (the last $w$ edges in an infinite stream)
often keep the $k$ most recent edges satisfying some property.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Darwini:\\ generating realistic large-scale social graphs} \\
  S. Edunov et al.
\end{book}
To generate a (large) graph with prescribed degree and clustering coefficient distributions (conditional on degree):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Assign a target degree and clustering coefficient to each node;
\item Group the nodes in clusters, according to $cd(d-1)$;
\item Build and Erd\"os-R\'enyi graph on each cluster, choosing the probability (and cluster size) to get the desired expected clustering coefficient (and a degree inferior to the target);
\item Add edges between clusters to get the desired degree distribution [tricky for large-degree nodes], trying to keep the joint degree distribution.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How to partition a billion-node graph} \\
  L. Wang et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To partition a graph (for distributed processing, or sparse matrix ordering):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Coarsen it by finding a maximum match (a set of edges sharing no vertices)
and collapsing its edges, until the graph is sufficiently small;
\item Assign a unique label to each vertex; set the label of each vertex to the majority label in its neighbourhood;
\item If there are too many labels, collapse vertices with the same label, and propagate labels anew.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graphons, mergeons and so on} \\
  J. Eldridge et al. (2016)
\end{book}
A \d{graphon} is a symmetric measurable function 
$w : [0,1]^2 \to [0,1]$.
It can be seen as a weighted graph on the uncountable vertex set $[0,1]$.
It defines a sequence of random graphs (graph-valued random variable) $(G_n)_n$,
where $G_n$ samples $n$ points $x_i$ uniformly in $[0,1]$ and then an edge $i$--$j$ with probability $w(x_i,x_j)$.
It is \emph{consistent} (the random graph obtained by removing the $(n+1)$st vertex from $G_{n+1}$ 
has the same distribution as $G_n$)
and \emph{local} (if $S,T \subset [n]$ are disjoint,
then $\left. G_n \right|_S \indep \left. G_n \right|_T$);
these properties characterize graphons.
A subset $A \subset [0,1]$ is \emph{disconnected} at level $\lambda$ 
if there exists $S \subset A$
such that 
$0 < \mu(S) < \mu(A)$ and
$w < \lambda$ on $S \times (A\setminus S)$.
Define an equivalence relation $\sim$ on the set $\mathscr A_\lambda$
of $\lambda$-connected subsets by $A_1 \sim A_2$ iff 
$\exists A \in \mathscr A_\lambda \quad A \supset A_1 \union A_2$.
The clusters at level $\lambda$
are the (essential) largest elements in each equivalence class.
A \d{mergeon} describes how clusters merge as $\lambda$ decreases: 
it is a graphon $M$ such that 
\[ M^{-1}[\lambda,1] = \bigcup_{ C \text{ $\lambda$-cluster} } C\times C. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GraphChi-DB: simple design for a scalable graph database system -- on just a PC} \\
  A. Kyrola and C. Guestrin
\end{book}
Adapt the \emph{log-structured merge tree} (LSM tree)
to store edges: 
partition them on the destination vertex, 
but sort each partition along the source vertex.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time series analysis of the S\&P 500 index:\\ a horizontal visibility graph approach} \\
  M.D. Vamvakaris et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The \emph{horizontal visibility graph} is a variant of the visibility graph:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit the degree distribution with a power law: 
if the exponent is greater than $\log 3/2$,
the time series is auto-correlated; if it is less, it is chaotic;
\item The \emph{Hellinger distance} between the in- and out-degree distributions measures irreversibility.
\end{compactitem}

\[
<<HorizontalVisibilityGraph,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
op <- par( mfrow = c(1,2), mar = .1 * c(1,1,1,1) )

## Visibility graph
set.seed(1)
x <- cumsum( rnorm(20) )
plot(x, type = "o", lwd = 3, pch = 16, cex = 1, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
n <- length(x)
for( i in 1:n ) 
for( j in 1:n ) 
if( i < j )
if( all( x[i:j] <= approx(c(i,j), c(x[i],x[j]), i:j)$y ) )
  segments( i, x[i], j, x[j], col = "blue", lwd = 2 )
lines( x, type = "o", lwd = 3, pch = 16 )

## Horizontal visibility graph
set.seed(1)
x <- cumsum( rnorm(20) )
x <- x - min(x) + 1
par( lend = 1 )
plot(x, type = "h", lwd = 5, pch = 16, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
n <- length(x)
for( i in 1:n ) 
  for( j in 1:n ) {
    ij <- (i+1) : (j-1)
    m <- min( x[i], x[j] )
    if( i < j - 1 && all( x[ij] <= m ) || i+1 == j )
        segments( i, m, j, m, col = "orange", lwd = 2 )
  }
lines(x, type = "h", lwd = 5)
par(op)
@
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Process systems engineering\\ as a modeling paradigm for analyzing systemic risk in financial networks} \\
  R. Bookstaber et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The financial system can be modeled as a \emph{signed-directed graph} (SDG): 
the directed arcs represent causal relations, 
and the sign of $x \to y$ is that of $\partial y / \partial x$.
\emph{Process hazard analysis} can help identify feedback loops,
which amplify shocks --
many agents act in a locally stabilizing but globally destabilizing way.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diffusion-convolutional neural networks} \\
  J. Atwood and D. Towsley (2016)
\end{book}
On a graph, one can forecast node labels $Y$ from node features $X$
by using the $A^nX$ as predictors, where $A$ is the adjacency matrix.
Averaging over the nodes, this can be generalized to edge or graph labels.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A machine learning perspective\\ on predictive coding with PAQ} \\
  B. Knoll and N. de Freitas (2011)
\end{book}
\emph{Prediction by partial matching} (PPM)
is a lossless compression algorithm well-suited to text:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a prior probability distribution on the letters;
\item Encode the first character with the Huffman code or the arithmetic code for this distribution;
\item Somehow update the probability distribution; iterate.
\end{compactitem}

For instance, one could use an $n$-gram model, \ie, keep track of all the substrings seen so far,
and use the longest matches to predict (the probability) of the next letter.
With noisy inputs, approximate matching helps.
For images, scan by row or column,
or along a space-filling curve (Hilbert) --
you can beat \textsc{jpeg} but not \textsc{jpeg2000}.

\textbf{PAQ8} uses 500 such models, for bit-level predictions, ensembled with a 
\emph{mixture of experts} (implemented as a neural net).

Compression can be used to define distances
\begin{align*}
d_C(x,y) &= \dfrac{ C(x|y) + C(y|x) }{ C(xy) } \\
d_{\text{CDM}}(x,y) &= \dfrac{ C(xy) }{ C(x) + C(y) } \\
d_e(x,y) &= \dfrac12 \bigl[ E[x|y] + E[y|x] \bigr] \\
d_{\text{NDM}}(x,y) &= \dfrac{ C(x,y) - \min \{ C(x) , C(y) \} }{ \max \{ C(x), C(y) \} } 
\end{align*}
where 
$C(x)$ is the compressed size of $x$ (approximation of its Kolmogorov complexity),
$C(x|y)$ is the compressed size of $x$ if the compressor is trained on $y$,
$E[x|y]$ is the corresponding cross-entropy of $x$.

\d{Arithmetic coding} encodes a text given a sequence of probability distributions on letters, 
as a single rational number: 
divide the interval $[0,1]$ into segments, one for each possible letter, of length their probabilities;
pick the segment corresponding to the current letter; 
repeat the process on that segment, with the next letter; 
iterate and return any rational number in the final segment.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A brief review\\ of the ChaLearn AutoML challenge} \\
  I. Guyon et al. (2016)
\end{book}

AutoML is a competition for automated (black-box, with no human intervention) supervised learning,
combining the following ideas: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Decision trees, $k$-NN, naive Bayes, neural nets;
\item Uniformize, replace missing values, group modalities of categorical variables, 
feature selection, dimension reduction (PCA, kPCA, ICA, MDS, LLE, Laplacian eigenmaps), clustering ($k$-means)
\item Hyperparameter tuning (SMAC, hyperopt)
\item Model selection ($k$-fold cross-validation, l.o.o, o.o.b., bilevel optimization).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Structure discovery in nonparametric regression through compositional kernel search} \\
  D. Duvenaud et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The ``automated statistician'' performs a search in a space of kernel structures,
built from sums and products of linear, square exponential, periodic, etc. bricks, in a Gaussian process. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine learning techniques: reductions between prediction quality metrics} \\
  A. Beygelzimmer et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Reduction} is the set of techniques converting a loss-minimization problem into a well-studied machine-learning one, 
typically binary classification or regression.
Examples include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Importance-weighted classification (different cost for false positives and negatives) by reweighting the training set;
\item Multiclass classification with one-against-all (inconsistent), error correcting codes (ECOC, inconsistent),
  probabilistic ECOC, all-pairs;
\item Cost-sensitive multiclass classification, with weighted all-pairs;
\item Quantile regression with importance weighted classification;
\item Ranking (of binary data).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-view machines}\\
  B. Cao et al.
\end{book}
Sparse variant of factorization machines.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FastDBT: A speed-optimized\\ and cache-friendly implementation\\ of stochastic gradient-boosted decision trees\\ for multivariate classification
} \\
  T. Keck
\end{book}
May be faster than XGBoost. Also check LightGBM and CatBoost.
% By/for physicists

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Coresets for scalable\\ Bayesian logistic regression} \\
  J.H. Higgins et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Replace large datasets with a \emph{coreset},
\ie, a weighted (smaller) subset
(obtained, e.g., from an approximate streaming clustering algorithm)
to approximate Bayesian posterior likelihood (for logistic regression).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Domain-adversarial training\\ of neural networks} \\
  Y. Ganin et al. (2016)
\end{book}
\d{Domain adaptation} (DA) is the transfer of a model from one domain to another,
sometimes by a mapping (alignment) between the two domains.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2,yscale=1.2,>=stealth]
\node (X1) at (0,1) {$X_1$};
\node (Y) at (1,1) {$Y$};
\node (X2) at (0,0) {$X_2$};
\draw[thick,->] (X1) -- (Y);
\draw[thick,dotted,->] (X2) -- (X1);
\node [left  = -2mm of X1, anchor=east, text width=1.15cm, inner sep = 0pt] {\footnotesize\raggedleft source domain};
\node [left  = -2mm of X2, anchor=east, text width=1.15cm, inner sep = 0pt] {\footnotesize target domain};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

One can also use an adversarial approach to build domain-invariant features.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.5,yscale=1.2,>=stealth]
\node (source) at (0,1) {\llap{source domain}};
\node (target) at (0,0) {\llap{target domain}};
\node (features1) at (1,1) {features};
\node (features2) at (1,0) {features};
\node (output1) at (2,1) {\rlap{output}};
\node (output2) at (2,0) {\rlap{no training data}};
\draw[thick,->] (source) -- (features1);
\draw[thick,->] (target) -- (features2);
\draw[thick,->] (features1) -- (output1);
\draw[thick,dotted,->] (features2) -- (output2);
\draw[blue,thick,<->] (features1) -- (features2);
\node (A) at (1,.5) {};
\node [right = -1.5mm of A, anchor=west] {\footnotesize discriminator};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Progressive neural networks} \\
  A.A. Rusu et al.
\end{book}
To learn a task related to an already-learnt one, 
consider a new neural net, parallel to the first one, initialized with random weights, with lateral connections.
To keep the model scalable (task $n+1$ has connections from tasks 1, 2, $\cdots,$ $n$),
use dimension reduction (single-layer network) for those lateral connections.
\[ % Progressive neural net
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  >=stealth,yscale=.6,xscale=1.3,
  neuron/.style={draw,thick,inner sep=2pt,text width=15pt}
]
\footnotesize
\node[inner sep = 0pt] (A) at (.5,0) {input};
\node[neuron,gray] (B1) at (0,1) {};
\node[neuron,gray] (C1) at (0,2) {};
\node[neuron,gray] (D1) at (0,3) {};
\node[neuron] (B2) at (1,1) {};
\node[neuron] (C2) at (1,2) {};
\node[neuron] (D2) at (1,3) {};
\node[inner sep = 1pt] (E1) at (0,4) {\color{gray}output$_1$};
\node[inner sep = 1pt] (E2) at (1,4) {output$_2$};
\draw[->,thick,gray] (A) -- (B1);
\draw[->,thick,gray] (B1) -- (C1);
\draw[->,thick,gray] (C1) -- (D1);
\draw[->,thick,gray] (D1) -- (E1);
\draw[->,thick] (D2) -- (E2);
\draw[->,thick] (A) -- (B2);
\draw[->,thick] (B2) -- (C2);
\draw[->,thick] (C2) -- (D2);
\draw[->,thick] (B1) -- (B2);
\draw[->,thick] (C1) -- (C2);
\draw[->,thick] (D1) -- (D2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Net2Net: accelerating learning\\ via knowledge transfer} \\
  T. Chen et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Transform an already trained network into a deeper one by inserting a layer initialized as the identity function,
or into a wider one by duplicating some of the nodes (keep their input weights and halve their output weights).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sampling generative networks} \\
  T. White (2016)
\end{book}
In high dimensions, linear interpolation goes through atypical points (too chose to the origin):
prefer \emph{spherical interpolation}.

To study analogies, e.g., $\text{king} - \text{man} + \text{woman}$,
generate images in a grid having those three points as corners.

In the latent space, find the directions corresponding to ``smile'', ``open mouth'', ``gender''
or even ``blurry''
and generate a 1- or 2-dimensional grid of images by moving along those directions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient convolutional auto-encoding\\ via random convexification\\ and frequency-domain minimization} \\
  M.C. Oveneke et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Use random projections for the non-linear encoding part of the auto-encoders, and only learn the (linear) decoding part.
[cf echo networks, reservoir learning]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Universal adversarial perturbations} \\
  S.M. Moosavi-Dezfooli et al.
\end{book}
There exist almost universal adversarial perturbations:
a small perturbation which, 
when added to almost any image, 
tricks a neural network into misclassifying it.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Matrix neural networks} \\
  J. Gao et al. (2016)
\end{book}
If your neural network has matrices as input (instead of vectors), 
try \emph{bilinear layers}: 
$ Y = \sigma( U X V' + B) $
($U$, $V$ and $B$ are matrices).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FastText.zip:\\ compressing text classification models} \\
  A. Joulin et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Product quantization} decomposes the space into $k$ orthogonal subspaces
$ \RR^n = \bigoplus_{i=1}^k V_i,$
computes $2^b$ (with $b=8$) $k$-means centroids in each of them,
and approximates points as sums of those centroids
$ x \approx \sum q_i(x)$.

Use when you want to approximate scalar products -- e.g., for vector embeddings.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tensorizing neural networks} \\
  A. Novikov et al.
\end{book}
Dimension reduction (TensorTrain) for dense weight matrices (of fully-connected layers).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural network based clustering\\ using pairwise constraints} \\
  Y.C. Hsu and Z. Kira (2016)
\end{book}
Cluster data by training a (softmax) network on similar and dissimilar \emph{pairs}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Composing graphical models\\ with neural networks for structured\\ representations and fast inference} \\
  M.J. Johnson et al.
\end{book}
Use neural nets to add non-linear transformations to your graphical models;
inference is tricky: SVAE (structural variational encoders) use stochastic gradients 
of a variational approximation.
% Not read in detail

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{mgm: Structure estimation\\ for time-varying mixed graphical models\\ in high-dimensional data} \\
  J.M.B. Haslbeck and L.J. Waldorp
\end{book}
To estimate a Gaussian graphical model, identify the neighbourhood of each nodel with a lasso regression.
For mixed data (exponential family), use GLMs.
For time-varying models, use (Gaussian) weights.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Inference compilation\\ and universal probabilistic programming} \\
  T.A. Le et al.
\end{book}
Neural networks can be trained to provide better proposal distributions for sequential importance sampling (SIS).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Memory efficient kernel approximation} \\
  S. Si et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Approximate large kernel matrices as a direct sum of low-rank matrices, where the blocks correspond to clusters.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-class generative adversarial networks with the $L^2$ loss function} \\
  X. Mao et al.
\end{book}
Replace the sigmoid cross-entropy loss in the discriminator with an $L^2$ loss.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GANs for sequences of discrete elements\\ with Gumbel-softmax distribution} \\
  M. Kusner and J.M. Hern\'andez-Lobato
\end{book}
The Gumbel softmax distribution is the distribution of
\[ y = \text{softmax} \dfrac{ 1 }{ \tau ( h + g ) } \]
where $g_i \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \text{Gumbel}(0,1)$.
When $\tau \to 0$,
this becomes the one-hot encoding of $h_i + g_i$,
\ie, $y \sim \text{Multinomial}(p)$,
where $p = \text{softmax}\,h$,
\ie, $p_i \propto \exp h_i$.
It can be used as a differentiable approximation of the multinomial distribution,
e.g., in GANs.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Augmenting supervised neural networks\\ with unsupervised objectives\\ for large-scale image classification} \\
  Y. Zhang et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Augment supervised networks with autoencoder losses.
