\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Infinite-dimensional word embedding} \\
  E.T. Nalisnick and S. Ravi (2016)
\end{book}
Make word embeddings ``infinite dimensional'' by initializing them 
with high-dimensional (not really infinite) vectors, 
using only the first $z$ components, with a penalty for $z$,
and a sparsity penalty on the vectors
[unrelated to Dirichlet processes, iHMM and other finite-dimensional models].



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pointer networks} \\
  O. Vinyals et al.
\end{book}
Attention-based networks for TSP, convex hull, Delaunay triangulation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Structured prediction energy networks} \\
  D. Belanger and A. McCallum (2016)
\end{book}
\emph{Structured prediction} can be performed indirectly, 
by learning an energy function and minimizing it.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,thick,yscale=.8, xscale=1.7,inner sep = 1pt]
\node (A) at (0,0) {\footnotesize $x$};
