Propagating over the ``promising'' subset of the graph may be sufficient.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A deep and autoregressive approach\\ for topic modeling of multimodal data} \\
  Y. Zheng et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Represent images as bags of visual words (e.g., SIFT features)
to use topic models (LDA, DocNADE).
\begin{align*}
  &v_1,\dots,v_d \text{ words} \\
  &p(\mathbf v) = \prod p( v_i | \mathbf v_{ <i } ) \text{ or a balanced binary tree} \\
  &\mathbf h_i( \mathbf v_{ <i} ) = g\Bigl( c + \sum _{k < i } W_{\bullet, v_k} \Bigr) \\
  &p(v_i = w | \mathbf v _{<i} ) \propto \exp\bigl( b_w + V_{w,\bullet} \mathbf h_i (\mathbf v_{<i} ) \bigr)
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Simplicial mixtures of Markov chains: distributed modelling of dynamic user profiles} \\
  M. Girolami and A. Kab\'an
\end{book}
Variant of latent Dirichlet allocation (LDA: a document is a mixture of topics, a topic is a distribution on words) 
for mixtures of Markov chains:
user activity is a mixture of behaviours, 
a behaviour is a distribution on sequences (Markov chains).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Solving heterogeneous estimating equations with gradient forests} \\
  S. Athey et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Random forests are (high-dimensional) adaptive nearest neighbour functions: 
they can be used as an alternative to kernel-based weighting,
where the weight of an observation is the proportion of trees in which it is in the same leaf as the point of interest.
The choice of the splits can be taylored to the model (quantile regression, etc.)
by minimizing the after-cut squared error, or some gradient-based approximation.

Implementation in \texttt{gradientForest}, \texttt{ranger}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{bartMachine: machine learning\\ with Bayesian additive regression trees} \\
  A. Kapelner and J. Bleich
\end{book}
Forests of regression (not classification) trees, with a prior on tree structure and leaf parameters: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Nodes at depth $d$ are non-terminal with probability $\alpha (1+d)^{-\beta}$, 
$\alpha = .95$, $\beta = 2$,
\item Splitting variables are selected uniformly at random,
\item Splitting values are sampled from the multiset of values taken,
\item The parameters are Gaussian,
\item The noise is inverse Gamma,
\end{compactitem}
estimated with Metropolis-withing-Gibbs sampling 
(Metropolis for the tree structure, Gibbs for the rest),
in Java.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the generalized ratio of uniforms\\ as a combination of transformed rejection\\ and extended inverse of density sampling} \\
  L. Martino et al.
\end{book}
Let $p$ be a monotonic probability density (e.g., a half-Gaussian)
and 
\[
  \mathscr A = \Bigl\{ 
    (u,v) \in \RR^2 \ :\ 0 \leq u \leq \sqrt{ p(v/u) } 
  \Bigr\},
\]
If $(U,V) \sim \text{Unif}\ \mathscr A$,
then $V/U \sim p$.

This can be generalized:
let $g: \RR_+ \to \RR$, $\mathscr C^1$, strictly increasing, $g(0)=0$,
\[ 
\mathscr A_g = \biggl\{
  (u,v) \in \RR^2 \ :\ 0 \leq u \leq g^{-1} \Bigl[ c \cdot p\Bigl( \dfrac{ v }{ \dot g(u) } \Bigr) \Bigr] 
\biggr\},
\]
if $(U,V) \sim \text{Unif}\ \mathscr A_g$, then $V/\dot g(U) \sim p$.

\emph{Inverse of density} sampling, for monotonic probability density functions, uses 
\[ 
(X,Y) \sim \text{Unif} \, \bigl\{
  (x,y) \ :\ 0 \leq y \leq p(x)
\bigr\}
\]
or, if it is easier to sample from $p^{-1}$,
\[
Y \sim p^{-1}, \quad X \sim \text{Unif}\, [0, p^{-1} Y].
\]
In both cases, $X \sim p$.

For non-monotonic densities, 
decompose them into intervals on which they are either increasing or decreasing.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pareto smoothed importance sampling} \\
  A. Vehtari et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Importance sampling estimates 
\[
  \int f dP = \int f \dfrac{ dP }{ dQ } dQ \approx \dfrac1N \sum f(x_i) \dfrac{ p(x_i) }{ q(x_i) },
\]
where $x_i \sim Q$,
when it is easier to sample from $Q$ than $P$.
If $p$ is only known up to a normalization factor, 
\[
  \int f dP \approx \dfrac{
    \displaystyle
    \sum f(x_i) \dfrac{ p(x_i) }{ q(x_i) }
  }{
    \displaystyle
    \sum \dfrac{ p(x_i) }{ q(x_i) }
  }.
\]
To stabilize this ratio estimate, 
one can truncate the weights 
$w_i = p(x_i) / q(x_i)$,
but this introduces bias.
Alternatively, one can fit a Pareto distribution to their tail
and replace the tail values with the corresponding Pareto quantiles.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Constrained maximum correntropy\\ adaptive filtering} \\
  S. Peng et al. (2016)
\end{book}
\emph{Maximum correntropy} estimators use a kernel as a loss function, e.g., 
\begin{align*}
\text{Loss}( \hat y, y ) &= - \sum \kappa( \hat y, y ) \\
\kappa(x,y) &= \exp - \dfrac12 \Bigl( \dfrac{x-y}{\sigma} \Bigr) ^2
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning design patterns\\ with Bayesian grammar induction} \\
  J.O Talton et al. (2012)
\end{book}
It is not possible to learn a (deterministic) grammar from positive examples, 
but it is possible for probabilistic languages 
(grammar induction).
A Bayesian approach would be: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Build a grammar that recognizes all exemplars and nothing else;
\item Modify it (MCMC), to reduce its length while keeping the likelihood high.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A case for robust Bayesian priors\\ with applications to binary clinical trials} \\
  J.A. F\'uquene et al.
\end{book}
Conjugate Bayesian analysis is not robust to the prior.
The Cauchy prior (for the normal log-odds model, or the binomial model for binary data)
is robust but does not lead to a closed form posterior.
\emph{Berger's prior} (a continuous mixture of Gaussians) is also robust and does.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RUSBoost: a hybrid approach\\ to alleviating class imbalance} \\
  C. Seiffert et al. (2010)
\end{book}
Class imbalance can be addressed by: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Adding more samples to the minority class: oversampling, boosting, extrapolating (SMOTE);
\item Removing samples from the majority class: undersampling (RUS).
\end{compactitem}
Those methods can be combined.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Orthant probabilities for robust correlation and structural performance enhancement} \\
  M. Anderson et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To capture non-linear effects with correlations, notice that if $(X,Y)$ is Gaussian
\begin{align*}
  P_{ij} &= P[ (-1)^i X > 0 \wedge (-1)^j Y > 0 ] && i,j \in \{0,1\} \\
  &= \dfrac14 + \dfrac{ (-1)^{i+j} }{ 2\pi } \arcsin \rho
\end{align*}
and convert those orthant probabilities into 4 \d{quadrant correlations}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient thresholded correlation using truncated singular value decomposition} \\
  J. Baglama et al. (2016)
\end{book}
To find all pairs of variables $(X_i,X_j)$
with correlation above a threshold $0<t<1$
without examining them all, notice that, if $\forall i\ \n{ x_i}=1$,
\[ \n{ x_i - x_j }^2 > 2(1-t) \implies \cor(x_i,x_j) < t \]
and 
$ \n{ Px_i - Px_j } \leq \n{ x_i - x_j }$ if $P$ is a projection.
Only examine pairs of vectors whose projections on the first few singular vectors are sufficiently close.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A survey of discretization techniques:\\ taxonomy and empirical analysis\\ in supervised learning} \\
  S. Garc\'ia et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Comparison of 80 discretization algorithms (equal width, equal frequency, $\chi^2$, etc.), 
without any detail.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient thresholded correlation using truncated singular value decomposition} \\
  J. Baglama et al. (2016)
\end{book}
To find the most correlated (\ie, the closest) pairs of unit vectors,
project them on the first singular value dimensions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model selection for Gaussian process regression by approximation set coding} \\
  B. Fischer et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Approximation set coding} (ASC) is a model selection procedure 
maximizing the posterior agreement $\eta_\theta$
\begin{align*}
  \theta &:\text{ hyperpatameters} \\
  \alpha &: \text{ parameters} \\
  p_\theta(\alpha) &: \text{ prior} \\
  D &= D_1 \sqcup D_2 \text{ random partition of the data} \\
  \eta_\theta &= 
  \int p_\theta( \alpha | D_1) p_\theta( \alpha | D_2 ) p_\theta(\alpha) d\alpha.
\end{align*}
The likelihood can be:
\begin{align*}
  p(\alpha | D ) &\propto p_\theta(D|\alpha) p_\theta(\alpha) && \text{Bayesian} \\
  (\alpha | D ) &\propto p_\theta(D|\alpha)^\beta && \text{Entropy ($\beta=1$)}.
\end{align*} 
It can be used to select the kernel of a Gaussian process: 
square exponential $e^{-x^2}$, rational quadratic $(1+x^2)^{-\alpha}$,
exponential $e^{-\va x}$, periodic $e^{-\sin^2 x}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
