\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Doubly stochastic\\ neighbor embedding on spheres} \\
  Y. Lu et al.
\end{book}
Normalizing the t-SNE similarity matrix to make it doubly stochastic limits the crowding around the origin.
The resulting approximately spherical embedding can be plotted on $\SS^2$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal inference and the data-fusion problem} \\
  E. Bareinboim and J. Pearl (2016)
\end{book}
The rules of \emph{do-calculus} (for causal inference) can be expressed in terms of conditional independence and graphical models. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Principled detection of out-of-distribution examples in neural networks} \\
  S. Liang et al.
\end{book}
To help detect out-of-distribution observations, 
increase the temperature of the softmax
\[ p_i(x) \propto \exp \dfrac{ f_i(x) }{ T } \]
and add noise 
\( x \leftarrow x - \epsilon \cdot \text{sign} - \nabla_x \log p_{\hat y} (x). \)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HiCS: high contrast subspaces\\ for density-based outlier ranking} \\
  F. Keller et al.
\end{book}
Outlier detection algorithms often compare the density of a point with that of its neighbours --
they suffer from the curse of dimensionality.
Instead, compute the LOF (local outlier factor) for high-contrast subspaces,
defined as axis-aligned subspaces on which the conditional distributions $Y | X = x$
(estimated on slices $Y | x_1 \leq X x_2$)
and the marginal distribution $Y$ are significantly different,
as measured by a Welsh test ($T$ test with unequal variances)
or a Kolmogorov-Smirnov test.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Programming with models:\\ writing statistical algorithms\\ for general model structures with Nimble} \\
  P. de Valpine et al.
\end{book}
Nimble lets you describe a computational graph in R,
and compiles it into C++, for use in Bugs;
it also lets you change how the data is sampled
(importance sampling, Metropolis Hastings).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{sgof: an R package\\ for multiple testing problems} \\
  O. Castro-Conde and J. de U\~na-\'Alvarez
\end{book}
For a large number of tests, SGoF (sequential goodness of fit, 
which compares $\#\{p\ :\ p<\gamma\}$ with its expected value $\gamma$ under $H_0$)
is more powerful than FDR or FWER approaches.
Also check: \texttt{qvalue} (the q-value is the proportion of false positives),
\texttt{HybridMTest},
\texttt{multtest},
\texttt{mutoss},
\texttt{multcomp},
\texttt{stats::p.adjust}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{conting: an R package for Bayesian analysis of complete and incomplete contingency tables} \\
  A.M.  Overstall and R. King (2014)
\end{book}
Log-linear models, for contingency tables, are of the form 
\begin{align*}
  y_1 &\sim \text{Poisson}\, \mu_i \\ \mu_i &= x_i' \theta
\end{align*}
where $i$ is a multiindex and $\mathbf x$ a design matrix describing which interactions the model allows.
For instance, 
$\mu_{ijk} = a + b+i + c_j + d_k$
or 
$\mu_{ijk} = a + b+i + c_j + d_k + e_{ij} + f_{ik} + g_{jk}$.
In a Bayesian context,
the model $\mathbf x$ is also unknown.
The \emph{generalizaed hyper-g-prior}
for $\theta|x$
is the posterior from a locally uniform prior and an imaginary sample of size $1/\sigma^2$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Counterfactual:\\ an R package for counterfactual analysis} \\
  M. Chen et al.
\end{book}
Given two populations 0 and 1 (e.g., men and women),
counterfactual analysis tests if the differences in the distributions of the same variable $y$ (e.g., salary) 
comes from different characteristics $x$ (are the marginal distributions $x$ the same?)
or a different treatment (are the conditional distributions $y|x$ the same?).
\begin{align*}
  F_{ \langle ij \rangle} (y) &= \int_{ \mathscr X _j } F_{ Y_i | X_j } (y|x) d F_{X_j}(x), 
  \quad\ i,j \in \{0,1\} \\
  F_{ \langle 11 \rangle }^{-1} - F_{ \langle 00 \rangle }^{-1} &= 
  \bigl( F_{ \langle 11 \rangle }^{-1} - F_{ \langle 01 \rangle }^{-1} \bigl) + 
  \bigl( F_{ \langle 01 \rangle }^{-1} - F_{ \langle 00 \rangle }^{-1} \bigr)
\end{align*}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multilabel classification with R package mlr} \\
  P. Probst et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Multiple label classification} 
assigns several labels (or ``tags'')
to each observation.
One could train a classifier for each label, sepeartely, 
but this assumes they are independent.
Instead, one can add all or some of the labels, either the true or forecasted values, to the predictors. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{coxphMIC: an R package for sparse estimation of Cox proportional hazards models via approximate information criteria} \\
  R. Nabi and X. Su
\end{book}
Best subset selection models add a penalty of the form 
$\lambda \sum \mathbf 1_{ \beta_i \neq 0 }$, 
with $\lambda = 2$ (AIC) or $\lambda = \log n$ (BIC).
It can be relaxed by replacing 
$\mathbf 1_{ \beta \neq 0}$
with 
$\tanh \beta^2$ (or $\tanh( a \beta^2)$), 
but since it is smooth in 0, it does not give sparse solutions.
Instead, replace 
$\beta = \beta \mathbf 1_{ \beta \neq 0 }$ with 
$\beta = \gamma \tanh \gamma^2$ and use $\tanh^2 \gamma$ as a penalty.

\[
<<PenaltycoxphMIC,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
gamma <- function(beta) {
  f <- function(x) ( x * tanh(x)^2 - beta ) ^ 2
  r <- optimize(f, c( beta - .5, beta + .5 ) )
  r$minimum
}
x <- seq(-2,2, length = 101)
y <- sapply( x, gamma )
op <- par( mfrow = c(1,2), mar = c(1,1,2,1) )
plot( x, tanh(x)^2, type = "l", lwd = 3, axes = FALSE, main = expression( tanh( beta^2 ) ) )
plot( x, tanh(y)^2, type = "l", lwd = 3, axes = FALSE, main = expression( tanh( gamma^2 ) ) )
par(op)
@
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GsymPoint: an R package to estimate\\ the generalized symmetry point,\\ an optimal cut-off point\\ for binary classification\\ in continuous diagnostic tests} \\
  M. L\'opez-Rat\'on et al.
\end{book}
To find the best cutoff point on a ROC curve,
one can use the \emph{symmetric point},
\ie, the intersection with the diagonal $y=1-x$, 
if the costs of false positives and negatives are the same or, more generally, 
the solution of 
$\rho( 1 - \text{specificity} ) = 1 - \text{sensitivity}$,
where \[ \rho = \dfrac{ \text{cost FP} }{ \text{cost FN} }, \]
which corresponds to 
$E[ \text{cost FP} ] = E[ \text{cost FN} ]$.
If the scores are Gaussian,
\begin{align*}
  Y_{\text{healthy}} &\sim N( \mu_0, \sigma_0 ) \\
  Y_{\text{diseased}} &\sim N( \mu_1, \sigma_1 ) ,
\end{align*}
then $\text{ROC}(x) = \Phi\bigl( a + b \Phi^{-1}(x) \bigr)$,
$a = (\mu_1 - \mu_0 ) / \sigma_1 > 0$, 
$b = \sigma_0 / \sigma_1$.
If there is uncertainty on $\mu_i$, $\sigma_i$, $\rho$,
one can also compute confidence intervals for the generalized ($\rho \neq 1$) symmetry point.
% There is also a non-parametric, empirical-likelihood estimator [not clear]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{spcadjust: an R package for adjusting\\ for estimation error in control charts} \\
  A. Gandy and T. Kval\o y
\end{book}
Use the bootstrap to estimate the threshold to use in \emph{control charts}
to spot a move from $N(\mu,\sigma^2)$ to $N(\mu+\Delta, \sigma^2)$.
\begin{align*}
  & \text{Shewhart}  & S_t &= \dfrac{ X_t - \hat \mu }{ \hat \sigma } \\
  & \text{CUSUM} & S_t &= \max \biggl\{ 0, 
    S_{t-1} + \dfrac{ X_t - \mu - \tfrac12 \Delta }{ \sigma } 
  \biggr\}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Weighted effect coding\\ for observational data with wev} \\
  R. Nieuwenhuis et al.
\end{book}
With unbalanced classes, consider \emph{weighted effects} to put categorical variables into the design matrix.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{IsoGeneGUI: multiple approaches\\ for dose-response analysis\\ of microarray data using R} \\
  M. Otava et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Isotonic regression} estimates means $\mu_1,\dots,\mu_n$ under a monotonicity constraint.
The corresponding test is 
$H_0\!\!: \forall i \neq j \ \mu_i = \mu_j$ 
versus 
$H_1\!\!: \forall i<j\  \mu_i \leq \mu_j$ or
$H_1\!\!: \forall i<j\  \mu_i \geq \mu_j$.
The constraint can also be applied to clustering 
(e.g., by using the isotonic means instead of the raw data).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{milr: multiple-instance logistic regression\\ with Lasso penalty} \\
  P.Y. Chen et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Multiple instance learning} considers bags of observations rather than observations: 
\begin{align*}
  i&: \text{ bag of instances} \\
  (i,j)&: \text{ instance} \\
  x_{ij} &: \text{ covariates} \\
  p_{ij} &= g( x_{ij}' \beta ) \text{ defect rate of instance $(i,j)$} \\
  Y_{ij} &\sim \text{Bernoulli}( p_{ij} ) \\
  \pi_i &: \text{ defect rate of bag $i$} \\
  \pi_i &\propto \sum_j p_{ij} e^{ a p_{ij} } \text{ or } 1 - \prod(1-p_{ij}) 
  \\ & \hphantom{{}\propto{}}
  \text{or } h\Bigl( \sum h( Y_{ij} = 1 ) \Bigr).
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{phtt: Panel data analysis\\ with heterogeneous time trends in R} \\
  O. Baba and D. Liebl (2014)
\end{book}
% Not read in detail
Panel models are usually of the form 
\[ y_{it} = \sum_j x_{ijt} \beta_j + \nu_t + \epsilon_{it}. \]
Big data allows the time-varying component $\nu_t$ to be subject-specific
and described by a factor model 
\begin{align*}
  y_{it} &= \sum_j x_{ijt} \beta_j + \nu_{it} + \epsilon_{it} \\
  \nu_{it} &= \sum_\ell \lambda_{i\ell} f_{\ell t} ;
\end{align*}
neither $\lambda$ nor $f$ is observed, 
the $f_{\bullet t}$ are orthonormal, so are the $\lambda_{i \bullet}$.
The factors can be assumed to be smooth (splines, \texttt{KSS})
or ARIMA (\texttt{Eup})

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian state-space modelling\\ on high-performance hardware using LibBi} \\
  L.M. Murray
\end{book}
LibBi is a BUGS-like language for parallel (multicore CPU, GPU) Bayesian estimation 
of state space models,
using particle filters (sequential Monte Carlo, SMC) instead of Gibbs sampling (as Jags)
or Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC, as Stan).

