\begin{book}
  \emph{Visualizing dependence\\ in high-dimensional data:\\ an application to S\&P 500 constituent data
} \\
  M. Hofert and W. Oldford (2016)
\end{book}
Scatterplot matrices contain each plot twice;
it is possible to show each plot only once, while ensuring that each plot shares its vertical (resp. horizontal) axis with a neighbour.
This is implemented in the \texttt{zenplot} package.

Given a measure of interestingness, one can reduce the number of plots further,
and arrange them along an \emph{Eulerian path} (\text{PairViz} package).

This can be applied to stock returns, 
de-GARCHed (ARMA-GARCH residuals, 
from \path{qrmtools::fit_ARMA_GARCH}, \texttt{rugarch::ugarchfit})
and their tail dependence indices 
(\texttt{copula::fitCopula(tCopula..., method="itau.mpl")}).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic data analysis\\ with probabilistic programming} \\
  F. Saad and V. Mansinghka
\end{book}
Composable generative population models (CGPM) 
are a generalization of CrossCat for probabilistic modeling in BayesDB (and its Bayesian query language, BQL);
they can be written VentureScript (a probabilistic programming language) or even Stan.
% Interesting but too formal

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Edward: a library for probabilistic modeling, inference and criticism} \\
  D. Tran et al. (2016)
\end{book}
% Clear, simple examples
Edward is a 
% Turing-complete?
Python probabilistic programming library,
built on TensorFlow, 
based on \emph{variational inference}:
describe the model, the priors, a family of posterior approximations,
and find the posterior approximation minimizing the KL divergence with the true posterior --
this also includes MAP (the posterior approximation of a point mass)
and MCMC (an empirical distribution).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RankPL: a qualitative\\ probabilistic programming language} \\
  T. Rienstra
\end{book}
Spohn's \dh{rank theory}
is a qualitative alternative to probability theory,
replacing probabilities with degrees of surprise (ranks).
A ranking function 
$\kappa : \Omega \to \NN \union \{\infty\}$
is extended to $\mathscr P(\Omega)$
as $\kappa(A) = \min \{ \kappa(a), a \in A \}$.
(One usually assumes $\kappa(\Omega) = 0$: 
replace $\kappa$ with 
$A \mapsto \kappa(A) - \kappa(\Omega)$ if needed.)
The conditional rank is 
$\kappa(A|B) = \kappa(A\inter B) - \kappa(B)$ if $\kappa(B) \neq \infty$.
There is a formal similarity:
\[ 
\begin{array}{lccccc}
  \text{Probability} & 0 & 1 & + & / & \times \\
  \text{Rank} & \infty & 0 & \min & - & +
\end{array}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TRX: A formally verified parser interpreter} \\
  A. Koprowski and H. Binsztok (2010)
\end{book}
\d{PEG}s (parsing expression grammars) are very similar to \emph{context-free grammars} (CFG) but
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The choice operator $e_1 | e_2$ is prioritized: $e_2$ is only tried if $e_1$ fails;
\item The repetition operators $e^*$ and $e^+$ are greedy;
\item (There are also predicates, $\&e$ and $!e$, which do not consume any input, and provide unlimited look-ahead and limited backtracking).
\end{compactitem}
PEGs are unambiguous; 
they formalize what \emph{recursive descent parsers} do
(\emph{packrat parsers} are recursive descent parsers with memoization).

\begin{book}
  \emph{Is there an optimal forecast combination?}\\
  C. Hsiao and S.K. Wan (2011)
\end{book}

There are many ways of combining forecasts $f_{1t},\dots,f_{nt}$
of a time series $y_y$ into $\beta' f_{\bigcdot t}$.

With a small sample, the (unweighted) \emph{simple average} $\bar y_t$ works well.
With more data, one can correct the bias $\mu + y_t$, or both bias and scale $\mu + c \bar y_t$.

Minimizing the mean square error 
\begin{align*}
  \phi(\beta) &= E[ ( \beta' x - y )^2 ] \\
  &= E[ \beta' x \beta' x ] - 2 E[ \beta' x y ] + E[y^2] \\
  &= E[ \beta' x x' \beta ] - 2 E[ \beta' x y ] + E[y^2 ] \\
  &= \beta' E[ x x' ] \beta - 2 \beta' E[xy] + E[y^2] \\ 
  \phi(\beta + h ) &= \phi(\beta) + 2  h' \bigl( E[xx'] \beta - E[xy] \bigr) + O(\n{h}^2)
\end{align*}
gives $\beta = E[xx'] ^{-1} E[xy]$. There are many variants:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Linear regression;
\item Linear regression with no intercept;
\item Constrained linear regression, imposing $\beta' \mathbf 1 = 1$,
  \ie, a convex combination of the forecasts;
\item Inverse-mean-square combination (Bates-Granger), \ie,
  using a diagonal estimator of $E[xx']$;
\item Best subset regression, using the AIC, AICC or BIC to select the subset of forecasts to use;
\item Bayesian model averaging, \ie,
  $w_i \propto \exp -\tfrac12 \Delta \text{BIC}_i$,
  where $\Delta \text{BIC}_i$ is the difference between in BIC between model $i$ and the best model.
\end{compactitem}

One can try to replace the constraint $\beta' \mathbf 1 = 1$ with $\n \beta _2 = 1$.
This gives the eigenvector $e_i$ for the smallest eigenvalue $\lambda_i$,
but it has to be rescaled to give an unbiased estimate: $e_i / e_i' \mathbf 1$.
The paper suggests to use the eigenvector minimizing
$\lambda_i / ( e_i ' \mathbf 1 )^2$ instead of the first.
It is possible to improve the resulting estimator
by estimating and correcting for the bias,
and by discarding the worst performing forecasts
(half of them, if the performance looks stable, or just a quarter),
based on their variance or their mean square error.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Prediction with expert advice}\\
  N. Cesa-Bianchi and G. Lugosi (2006)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.}
\emph{Sequence prediction} tries to forecast the future values of a sequence without
any underlying stochastic model: the sequence could be deterministic, stochastic,
or even devised by an adversary, dynamically adpting to our behaviour.

Consider the following setup.
\begin{align*}
  N &: \text{number of experts} \\
  n &: \text{number of observations} \\  
  i &: \text{expert} \\  
  t &: \text{time} \\  
  y_t &: \text{time series to forecast} \\  
  f_{it} &: \text{forecast of $y_t$ by expert $i$} \\  
  \hat p_t &: \text{combination of the experts' forecasts} \\  
  \ell(f_{it},y_t) &: \text{loss for expert $i$} \\  
  \ell(\hat p_t,y_t) &: \text{loss for the forecaster} \\  
  h(f,y) &: \text{payoff, }- \ell(f,y)
\end{align*}
The regret of not having followed expert $i$ is 
\begin{align*}
  r_{it} &=\ell(\hat p_t, y_t) - \ell(f_{it},y_t)\\
  R_{in} &= \sum\limits_{t=1}^n r_{it} \quad \text{(cummulated regret).}
\end{align*}
We want to minimize the \emph{cummulated regret} wrt the best expert, 
\[
  \hat L_n - \min_{ 1 \leq i \leq N } L_{in}.
\]

\textbf{2.}
Here are a few weighted average predictors, 
\[ \hat p_t \propto \sum_{i=1}^N w_{ i, t-1} f_{it}. \]
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Polynomial} & w_{it} &\propto ( R_{it} )_+^{p-1}  \\
  &\text{Exponential} & w_{it} &\propto \exp \eta R_{it} \propto \exp - \eta L_{it} \\
  && \eta &= \sqrt{ 8 \log N / n } \\
  &\text{Gradient} & w_{it} &\propto \exp - \eta \sum_{s<t} \nabla_{\hat p_s} \ell( \hat p_s, y_s ) \cdot f_{is} \\
  &\text{Multilinear} & w_{it} &\leftarrow \bigl( 1 + \eta \cdot h(f_{it},y_t ) \bigr) \cdot w_{i,t-1}
\end{align*}

The polynomial and exponential predictors can be written
$w_{it} \propto \nabla \Phi( R_{\bigcdot t} )_i$, 
for some potential $\Phi(u) = \psi( \sum \phi(u_i) )$ or, 
equivalently, 
$w_{it} \propto \phi'( R_{i,t-1} )$.
(While $\psi$ does not affect the weights, 
different choices lead to different inequalities.)

The multilinear predictor increases or decreases the weights depending on the sign of the payoff.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Methods for pastcasting, nowcasting and forecasting using Factor-MIDAS,\\
with an application to Korean GDP} \\
  H.H. Kim and N.R. Swanson (2016)
\end{book}
Given two (or more) time series with different frequencies, $Y$ (slow) and $X$ (fast),
the \d{MIDAS} model is a regression
\[ Y_{y+k} = \beta_0 + \sum_{i=0}^\ell \beta_i X_{t-i} + \text{noise} \]
where the $\beta_i$ have a sparse parametrization, e.g.,
the \emph{exponential Almon lag model}
$\beta_i \propto \exp[ \theta_1 i + \theta_2 i^2 ]$.

Given a large number of time series, ``diffusion indices'' are latent factors.
To estimate them (by PCA),
the time series have to be aligned, e.g., by AR interpolation --
other methods are available (and preferable): EM, state space model, etc.

A \emph{factor-MIDAS} model is a MIDAS model with those latent factors as predictors.

The many revisions the data undergoes
(the preliminary GDP is revised several times, often drastically)
complicate the picture -- each value has two timestamps:
when it was published, and the period it refers to.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep symbolic representation learning\\ for heterogeneous time series classification} \\
  S. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
To classify heterogeneous (continuous and categorical) time series, discretize them, consider the values at a point in time as a word, and learn (end-to-end) 
word embeddings --
or character embeddings, representing words as the direct sums of their characters.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PSF: introduction to R package for pattern sequence based forecasting algorithm} \\
  N. Bokde et al.
\end{book}
The pattern sequence-based forecasting (PSF) algorithm 
forecasts time series by clustering ($k$-means)
historical data into ``patterns''.
Only use if patterns are actually present (weather, energy, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Interpreting finite automata\\ for sequential data} \\
  C.A. Hammerschmidt et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Finite automata can model sequence data (model each word with an automaton; progressively merge them, selecting the ``best'' merge each time; stop when no good merge can be found); even large ones may be interpretable (use some graph layout algorithm and domain knowledge to interpret the clusters, bridges, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Comparison of pattern detection methods\\ in microarray time series\\ of the segmentation clock} \\
  M.L. Dequ\'eant et al. (2008)
\end{book}
To detect patterns: 
uniformize the data and compute
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\sigma / ( \sigma_1 + \cdots + \sigma_4 )$, 
where $\sigma_i$ is the standard deviation of the $i$th quarter of the data;
\item The number (or proportion) of permutations with the same total variation;
\item The persistence diagram of the sublevel sets 
$[ x \leq a ]$, for $0 \leq a \leq 1$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting periodic patterns\\ in unevenly spaced gene expression time series\\ using Lomb-Scargle periodograms} \\
  E.F. Glynn et al. (2005)
\end{book}
The \d{Lomb-Scargle periodogram} 
generalizes the discrete Fourier transform to unevenly spaced observations.
\begin{align*}
  2 \sigma^2 P(\omega) &= 
  \dfrac{
    \sum ( y_i - \bar y) \cos^2( \omega( t_i - \tau) )
  }{
	  \sum \cos^2( \omega( t_i - \tau) )
  } {}+{} \\
  & \phantom{{}={}} \dfrac{
    \sum ( y_i - \bar y) \sin^2( \omega( t_i - \tau) )
  }{
	  \sum \sin^2( \omega( t_i - \tau) )
  } \\
  \tau &= \dfrac1{2\omega} \arctan \dfrac{ 
  \sum \sin 2\omega t_i
}{
  \sum \cos 2\omega t_i
} \\
H_0&: P(\omega) \sim \text{Exp}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The cyclohedron test for finding periodic genes in time course expression studies} \\
  J. Morton et al. (2007)
\end{book}
The \emph{cyclohedron test} tests if a time series 
$(v_1,\dots,v_n)$ looks cyclic by computing its \emph{signature}
$\{\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_{n-1}\}$
\begin{align*}
\sigma_1 &= \{ \text{index of the largest element} \} \\
\sigma_i &= \{ \text{index of the $i$th largest element} \} 
\union \sigma_{k_{\text{left}}}
\union \sigma_{k_{\text{right}}}
\end{align*}
where $\sigma_{k_{\text{left}}}$ is the latest $\sigma_k$ containing $\delta_i - 1 (\text{mod } n )$.
The test statistic is the \emph{permutation count},
the number of permutations
with the same signature.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Factor models for matrix-valued high-dimensional time series} \\
  D. Wang et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Time series of matrices $X_t$ (e.g., 
$\text{country}\times\text{variable}\times\text{time}$)
can be described by a factor model $X_t = R F_t C' + E_t$
(it is not identifiable, but the column spaces of $R$ and $C$ are).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the relation between Gaussian process quadratures and sigma-point methods} \\
  S. S\"arkk\"a et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Sigma-point quadrature}
approximates integrals as 
\[ \int g(x) p(x) dx \approx w_i g(x_i), \]
where the $w_i$ are predefined (from $p$)
and the $x_i$'s are chosen according to some criterion, e.g., 
giving exact results on low-degree polynomials
or minimizing the error on some class of functions.
They are used in non-linear Kalman filters
and can be interpreted as Gaussian process (GP) quadratures.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lattice methods for multiple integration} \\
  I.H. Sloan and S. Joe (1994)
\end{book}
% Review of the book, not the book itself
The \emph{Fibonacci rule} estimates a 2-dimensional integral by evaluating it on a lattice
\[
  \iint_{[0,1]^2]} f(x,y)\, dx dy \approx \dfrac1{F_k} 
  \sum_{ j=0 }^{F_{k-1}} f\Bigl( \Bigl\{ \dfrac{j}{F_k} (1,F_{k-1}) \Bigr\} \Bigr) 
\]
where $F_n$ is the $n$th Fibonacci number and $\{x\}$ is the fractional part of $x$.
In dimension 2, they are the lattices with the best convergence rate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Monte Carlo\\ with determinantal point processes} \\
  R. Bardenet and A. Hardy (2016)
\end{book}
Determinantal point processes (DPP) can be used to build repulsive particle systems,
and give an alternative to quasi-Monte-Carlo integration or \emph{scrambled sets} (randomized QMC).
A \emph{point process} is a random variable $S$ 
whose values are finite subssets $[-1,1]^d$.
It may have an \emph{$n$-correlation function} $\rho_n$, such that for all bounded measurable 
$\phi: [-1,1]^d \to \RR$, 
\begin{align*}
&
  E \Biggl[
  \sum_{ \substack{ 
    x_1, \dots, _n \in S \\
    \text{all different}
  } }
  \phi(x_1,\dots,x_n)
  \Biggr]
  = {} \\
& \quad
  \int_{[-1,1]^d} 
  \phi(x_1,\dots,x_n) 
  \rho_n(x_1,\dots,x_n) 
  \mu(dx_1) \cdots \mu(dx_n).  
\end{align*}
It is \emph{determinantal} 
if the $n$-correlation functions exist and are given by a kernel,
\[ \rho_n(x_1,\dots,c_n) = \det k(x_i, x_j) ^{\mathstrut} _{ 1 \leq i,j \leq n }. \]
For the orthogonal polynomial (OP) ensemble,
the kernel is given by 
\[ k_N(x,y) = \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \phi_k(x) \phi_k(y), \]
where the $\phi_k$ are the Gram-Schmidt orthonormalization of $x_1^{\alpha_1} \cdots x_d ^{\alpha_d} $,
where the monomials are in the sup-graded lexicographic order.
\[ \int f d\mu = E \biggl[ \sum_{i=1}^N \dfrac{ f(x_i) }{ k_N(x_i,x_i) } \biggr] \]
(However, as $N$ increases, the $\phi_k$ become difficult to compute and sampling from the DPP time-consuming.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Why interval arithmetic is so useful} \\
  Y. Hijazi et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Affine arithmetic} is a generalization of interval arithmetic.
\[
\begin{array}{c}
  a \leq x \leq b \\
  c \leq y \leq d
\end{array}
\qquad\text{vs}\qquad
\begin{aligned}
a \leq \alpha x &+ \beta y \leq b \\
c \leq \gamma x &+ \delta y \leq d
\end{aligned}
\]
Applications in computational geometry include computing surface intersections
(ensuring that no bit of the intersection is lost), ray tracing, implicitly-defined surfaces.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of three high-precision quadrature schemes} \\
  D.H. Bailey et al. (2005)
\end{book}
The author's ARPEC C++/F90 multiprecision library also contains an implementation of PSLQ and quadrature schemes, $\int_{-1}^1 f \approx \sum w_j f(x_j)$: 
\begin{align*}
&\text{Gauss}: & x_j &= \text{Roots of Legendre polynomials} \\
&& w_j &= \dfrac{ -2 }{ (n+1) P'_n(x_j) P_{n+1}(x_j) } \\
&\text{Erf}: & x_j &= \text{erf}( h j ) \\ 
&& w_j &= \dfrac{ 2 }{ \sqrt \pi } e^{ - h^2 j^2 } \\
&\text{Tanh-Sinh}: & x_j &= \tanh u_2 \\
&& w_j &= \dfrac{ u_1 }{ \cosh^2 u_2 } \\
&& u_1 &= \dfrac\pi2 \cosh (h j) \\
&& u_2 &= \dfrac\pi2 \sinh (h j)
\end{align*}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic trading\\ with predictable returns and transaction costs} \\
  N. G\^arleanu and L.H. Pedersen (2013)
\end{book}

In presence of transaction costs:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Do not rebalance all the way to the target portfolio;
\item The target portfolio is not the current optimal portfolio, but that in a few periods:
  the weights of all assets are shrunk, with that of those present because of fast signals shrinking faster.
\end{compactitem}
The dynamic optimization problem can be solved exactly (Bellman equation).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Weighted elastic net penalized mean-variance portfolio design and computation} \\
  M. Ho et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Add an elastic net penalty to portfolio optimization problems
to control overfitting and portfolio size.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio optimization based on stochastic dominance and empirical likelihood} \\
  T. Post and S. Karabati (2017)
\end{book}
The \d{empirical likelihood} (EL) tweaks the empirical distribution 
(from uniform on the sample to multinomial)
to ensure some known properties of the population not satisfied by the sample 
(moment conditions) or add some prior information.
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
  \text{Find} & \pi_1,\dots,\pi_n \\
  \text{To maximize} & \sum \log \pi_i & \text{(MaxEnt)}\\
  \text{Such that} & \sum \pi_i = 1 \\
  & \forall j \ \sum_i \pi_i g_j(x_i) = 0 & \text{(Moments)}
\end{array}
\]
The moment conditions can also be inequalities; the maximum entropy objective is the multinomial log-likelihood.
It is often used in econometrics, as an alternative to the generalized method of moments, 
when the moment conditions depend on a parameter $\theta$,
to be estimated with the $\pi_i$'s.

To account for dynamic patterns (ARMA, GARCH), blockwise empirical likelihood (BEL) 
uses overlapping data blocks instead of individual observations.

The resulting distribution can be used for second-order stochastic dominance portfolio optimization 
(a big linear problem).
For instance, one can build a sector rotation strategy, maximizing the expected returns, using the 12-month momentum
as an estimate of future returns,
and moment conditions truncating the Fama-French-Cahart factors to their long-term 25\%--75\% quantiles.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{All that glitters is not gold: comparing backtest and out-of-sample performance\\ on a large cohort of trading algorithms} \\
  T. Wiecki et al.
\end{book}
% Quantopian 
The in-sample information ratio (IR) of a maximim IR strategy 
is not a good predictor of the out-of-sample IR,
but other measures of performance fare better (volatility, maximum drawdown).

Instead of using the in-sample IR as an estimate, let machine learning build one, using 
a long list of performance and risk measures % See appendix
as features.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic quantile models of rational behavior} \\
  L. de Castro and A.F. Galvao (2017)
\end{book}
Instead of \emph{expected} utility, maximize \emph{quantile} utility.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The capacity of trading strategies} \\
  A. Landier et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Using the Garleanu-Pedersen model, \ie, 
an AR(1) investment signal with persistence parameter $\phi$,
\begin{align*}
  \Delta s_{t+1} &= - \phi s_t + \epsilon_{t+1} \\
  s_{t+1} &= ( 1 - \phi ) s_t + \epsilon_{t+1} 
\end{align*}
with quadratic transaction costs $\tfrac12 \lambda (\Delta w)' \Sigma (\Delta w)$
and trading rate $\tau$
\[ x_t = (1-\tau) x_t + \tau x_t^* \]
and with a few approximations,
one can compute the relation between portfolio size 
and Sharpe ratio
\[ 
\text{Size} = \dfrac{ \text{SR} }{ \lambda \phi^2 } 
\left[ \biggl( \dfrac{ \text{SR}^* }{ \text{SR} } \biggr) ^{2/3} - 1 \right] ^2.
\]
For instance, if you are willing to accept a 30\% drop in the Sharpe ratio,
\ie, $\text{SR} = 0.7 \times \text{SR}^*$,
$\text{Size} = \text{SR}^* / 10 \lambda \phi^2$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A Sharpe ratio neutral prior\\ for Bayesian portfolio selection} \\
  R. Croessmann
\end{book}
In Bayesian portfolio selection, 
\[
  \operatorname*{Maximize}_w
  \E_{\substack{
    X \sim N(\mu, \Sigma) \\
    (\mu, \Sigma) \sim \text{Prior}
  }}
  \bigl[ U( w' X ) \bigr],
\]
the uninformative (improper) Jeffrey prior
$p(\mu,\Sigma) \propto \va{ \Sigma }^{ -(N+1)/2 }$
does not account for known information: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Very high Sharpe ratios are unlikely, while Jeffrey's prior gives 
\begin{align*}
  \text{SR}_{\text{max}} &= \sqrt{ \mu' \Sigma^{-1} \mu } \\
  p( \text{SR}_{\text{max}} ) &\propto \text{SR}_{\text{max}} ^{N-1} 
\end{align*}
\item High returns are a compensation for higher risks,
  while the prior assumes $\mu \indep \Sigma$.
\end{compactitem}
Instead, use
\begin{align*}
  X &\sim N( \beta \lambda, \beta \beta' + \Sigma ) \\ 
  \Sigma &= \diag(\tau) \, \Omega \, \diag(\tau) \\
  p[\Omega] &\propto \va \Omega ^{ N(N-1)/2 } \prod_i \va{ \Omega_{-i,-i} }^{ -(N+1)/2 } \\
  \beta, \lambda, \tau &\ \text{flat};
\end{align*}
this gives uniform marginal posteriors for $\cor(X_i,X_j)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal selection of large portfolios:\\ aggregation is better than\\ ignoring the return constraint} \\
  G.Y. Ban and C. Chen
\end{book}
The out-of-sample Sharpe ratio deteriorates as the number of assets increases.
Instead of optimizing a portfolio with all assets, group them, randomly, into ``super-assets'';
replace each group with the equal-weighted portfolio of its constituents; optimize.

Instead of randomly grouping the assets, group them in pairs with high correlation (or low covariance)
and put the elements of each pair in separate super-assets.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimizing optimal portfolio choice} \\
  Y. Jin (2017)
\end{book}
Instead of looking for better estimates of $\Sigma$, $\Sigma^{-1}$, or $\mu$,
one can directly look for better estimates of the optimal (unconstrained) weights, $\Sigma^{-1} \mu$.
Since they can be defined by an optimization problem, 
$\argmin_w \n{ \Sigma w - \mu }_2^2$,
one can add an $L^1$ penalty
to get a sparse portfolio, 
\[ \hat w = \argmin_w \dfrac12 \n{ \hat\Sigma w - \hat \mu }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ w }_1. \]
Under reasonable assumptions (e.g., a shrinkage estimator for $\hat \Sigma$,
$\n{ \hat w - w^* } = O_p( \sqrt{ S \log(N) / T } )$,
where $S = \min\{T,N\}$ and $O_p(\cdot)$ means $O(\cdot)$ with high probability -- 
the best possible bound is $O( \sqrt{ S \log(N) / T } )$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian emulation\\ for multi-step portfolio decisions} \\
  K. Irie and M. West (2016)
\end{book}

Optimization problems can often be recast as estimation problems, 
by interpreting the loss function as a log-likelihood and the penalties as priors.
For instance, multi-period optimization problems become state space models, 
which can be estimated with a ``forward filtering backward smoothing'' (FFBS) algorithm 
(e.g., Kalman filter, Viterbi algorithm).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio construction by mitigating error amplification: the bounded-noise portfolio} \\
  L. Zhao et al.
\end{book}
Eigenvectors can only be reliably estimated if their eigenvalues are isolated (\emph{eigengap}):
this is only the case for large eigenvalues.

The minimum variance portfolio is a linear combination of that for large eigenvalues (``signal'')
and that for small ones (``noise''), 
$w = \alpha w_S + (1-\alpha) w_N$.
The noise portfolio can be replaced with a robust alternative,
minimizing an upper bound on volatility.
The resulting optimization problem has the usual form, 
with the variance $\Sigma$
replaced with $\Sigma + mNN'$,
where the columns of $N$ are the noise eigenvectors
(the number of noise eigenvectors is estimated by bootstrap).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamical system theory\\ of periodically collapsing bubbles} \\
  V.I. Yukalov et al.
\end{book}
Interactions between a stock and a bond can be modeled as 
\begin{align*}
  \dot x &= x - x^2 e^{ - b x z } \\
  \dot z &= z - z^2 e^{ - a x },
\end{align*}
with $a \in [-.5,.5]$, $b \in [0,1]$.
The presence of bifurcations explains periodically exploding bubbles.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring uncertainty} \\
  K. Jurado et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Measure the uncertainty of a macroeconomic series as the standard deviation of the forecasted error,
\begin{align*}
  Y_t & \sim \sum_i X_{it} && \text{factors} \\
  X_{it} & \sim \text{Time series model} && \text{factor evolution}
\end{align*}
and use a stochastic volatility model for the innovations in both models.
Finally, aggregate (weighted average) the uncertainties of several series.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate geometric expectiles} \\
  K. Herrmann et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Univariate quantiles can be defined from an asymmetric absolute value loss; they generalize the median.
\begin{align*}
  & \rho_\alpha(t) = \begin{cases} \alpha t & \text{if } t \geq 0 \\ (1-\alpha) t & \text{if } t \leq 0 \end{cases} \\
  & F^{-1}(\alpha) = \argmin_{ c \in \RR } E \rho_\alpha( X - c ).
\end{align*}
The univariate \d{expectiles} are defined from an asymmetric square loss; they generalize the mean.
\begin{align*}
  & \lambda_\alpha(t) = \begin{cases} \alpha t^2 & \text{if } t \geq 0 \\ (1-\alpha) t^2 & \text{if } t \leq 0 \end{cases} \\
  & E(\alpha) = \argmin_{ c \in \RR } E \lambda_\alpha( X - c ).
\end{align*}
In higher dimensions (implementation in \texttt{qrmtools}):
\begin{align*}
\Phi_u(t) &= \n t _2 + \langle u, t \rangle && u \in B(0,1) \\
\text{VaR}_\alpha X &= \argmin_{ c \in \RR^d } E \Phi_\alpha ( X - c ) \\
\Lambda_u(t) &= \n t _2 \bigl( \n t _2 + \langle u, t \rangle \bigr) \\
e_\alpha(X) &= \argmin_{ c \in \RR^d } E \Lambda _\alpha (X-c).
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A generalized contagion process\\ with an application to credit risk} \\
  A. Dassios and H. Zhao (2016)
\end{book}
A \d{Cox process} (doubly stochastic model)
is a Poisson process in which the intensity 
$\lambda(t)$ is itself stochastic.
A \emph{dynamic contagion process with diffusion} (DCPD)
is a Hawkes process $(T_i) _{i \geq 0}$
with both self-excited and external jumps, 
with noise. 
\begin{align*}
  \lambda(t) &= a && \text{\hspace*{-1cm}mean-reverting level} \\
  &\quad + \sum_{ T_i < t } Y_i e^{ - \delta ( t - T_i ) }  && \text{\hspace*{-1cm}internal jumps} \\
  &\quad + \sum_{ S_i < t } X_i e^{ - \delta ( t - S_i ) }  && \text{\hspace*{-1cm}external jumps} \\
  &\quad + \sigma \int_0^t e^ { - \delta ( t-s) } \sqrt{ \lambda(s) } dW_s && \text{ noise}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data-driven partition-of-unity copulas\\ with applications to risk management} \\
  D. Pfeifer et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The following procedure defines a bivariate density: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take two 1-parameter families $\phi$, $\psi$ of (discrete) distributions on $\NN$, indexed by $[0,1]$, e.g., negative binomial or Poisson;
\item Take a discrete distribution on $\NN \times \NN$ whose marginals are the averages of $\phi$ and $\psi$: 
  \[ p_{i,\Cdot} = \int_0^1 \phi_i(u) du \qquad p_{\Cdot j} = \int_0^1 \psi_i(v) dv; \]
\item Set $\displaystyle c(u,v) = \sum_{ij} p_{ij} \dfrac{ \phi_i(u) }{ p_{i\Cdot} } \dfrac{ \psi_j(u) }{ p_{\Cdot j} }$.  
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust return risk measures} \\
  F. Bellini et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The \emph{Orlicz premium} of a positive random variable $X$
is the only solution $H_\Phi(X)$ of 
\[ E \biggl[ \Phi \Bigl( \dfrac{ X }{ H_\Phi(X) } \Bigr) \biggr] = 1, \]
where $\Phi: [0,+\infty) \to [0,+\infty)$ is convex,
$\Phi(0) = 0$, $\Phi(1) = 1$, $\Phi(\infty) = \infty$.

The utility-based \emph{shortfall risk} of a real random variable $Y$ is 
$\rho_\ell(Y) = \inf \{ m \,:\, E[ \ell(Y-m) ] \leq 0 \}$
where $\ell : \RR \to \RR$ is non-decreasing and $\ell(-\infty) < 0 < \ell(+\infty)$.
These notions are related: 
\begin{align*}
  \Phi(x) &= 1 + \ell( \log x ) \\
  H_\Phi(X) &= \exp\bigl( \rho_\ell( \log X ) \bigr) \\
  \rho_\ell(Y) &= \log\bigl( H_\Phi( \exp Y ) \bigr).
\end{align*}

They can be robustified, e.g., by replacing $E[\cdot]$ with 
$\sup_{ Q \in \mathscr S} E_Q[ \cdot ]$
(one can also consider a set of Young functions $\Phi$).

The \emph{Hazendonck-Goovaert risk measure} is 
\[ \Pi_\Phi(X) = \inf_{x \in \RR} x + H_\Phi \bigl( (X-x) _+ \bigr). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multifactor risk models and heterotic CAPM} \\
  Z. Kakushadze and W. Yu (2016)
\end{book}
Build your own risk model: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Separately model variances and correlations;
\item Use a factor model for the correlation;
\item Use (6-digit) industries as factors,
but estimate the model in a russian-dolls fashion.
\end{compactitem}
% Horrible notations; R code

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiportfolio time consistency for set-valued convex and coherent risk measures} \\
  Z. Feinstein and B. Rudloff
\end{book}
Intuitively, a \emph{set-valued risk measure} is a collection of portfolios covering the risk of the asset of interest.
A risk measure is \emph{time-consistent} if a portfolio covering the risk at time $t$ 
also covers the risk at any earlier time $s < t $.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Latent common return volatility factors: capturing elusive predictive accuracy gains when forecasting volatility} \\
  M. Cheng et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Stock volatility forecasts are usually computed independently for each stock, ignoring dependencies.
Instead: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the realized volatility (RV: multipower, multiscale, etc.) of each stock;
\item Use the lasso to find stocks whose RV is close to that of the target;
\item Compute a sparse PCA on them, to extract ``volatility factors'' (or just use a factor model);
\item Fit a heteroskedastic autoregressive (HAR) model 
$ y_{[t,t+1]} \sim y_{[t-1,t]} + y_{[t-4,t]} + y_{[t-21,t]} $
to the RV of the stock of interest, with the factors as exogenous variables.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to noise-reduced correlations using singular spectrum analysis} \\
  J.W. Dash et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The signal-to-noise ratio of a correlation matrix can be defined as 
\[ 
\text{SNR} = \dfrac{ 
  \sum\limits_{ i < i_c} \lambda_i
}{ 
  \sum\limits_{ i \geq i_c} \lambda_i
}
\]
where the cutoff $i_c$ is determined by finite (not asymptotic) random matrix theory (RMT).

To estimate a correlation matrix, start with log-prices, normalize them, smooth them with
singular spectrum analysis (SSA), % not defined
compute the returns, and then the correlations.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The 7 reasons\\ most machine learning funds fail} \\
  M. L\'opez de Prado (2017)
\end{book}
Do not compute returns by differentiating prices: \emph{fractional differentiation} ($\alpha=.5$)
is sufficient to yield a stationary process.

Do not use clock time but market time
(subordinated process using, not time, but volume or dollar bars).

Do not forecast ``up'' or ``down'' for a fixed horizon, but use \emph{three barriers}: 
up and down thresholds, and a time limit;
the labels to forecast may overlap: weigh them accordingly.

To avoid overfitting, make sure there is no overlap between training and test sets,
and use the \emph{deflated Sharpe ratio}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A tug of war:\\ overnight versus intraday expected returns} \\
  D. Lou et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Some investment signals realize their profits overnight 
(momentum, reversal), some intraday (the rest).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{101 formulaic alphas} \\
  Z. Kakushadze (2015)
\end{book}
101 technical indicators.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extracting consumer demand:\\ credit card spending and post-earnings returns} \\
  S. Agarwal et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Consumer spending (credit card data, email invoice data (Slice)) 
and spending surprises predict future earnings, sales surprises and future returns
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What makes stock prices move?\\ Fundamentals vs investor recognition} \\
  S. Richardson et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Stocks with low investor recognition 
(number of institutional shareholders)
have a lower price and higher expected returns;
realized returns as higher when they gain more recognition.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Facts about formulaic value investing} \\
  U.W. Kok et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Formulaic value investing (P/E and other financial ratios)
only highlights companies with temporarily high inflated earnings.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A framework for value investing} \\
  S. Chee et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The discounted dividend model computes an intrinsic value from dividend forecasts and an ``appropriate discount rate''.
Instead, the Ohlson model estimates this discount rate (prospective yield) 
so that the value equals the current market price, 
\[ P = \sum_{ t \geq 1 } \dfrac{ E[ \text{Div}_t ] }{ (1+y)^t }. \]
Estimating the dividends as 
$\text{Div}_t = \text{Earnings}_t - \Delta \text{BV}_t$,
\[ y \approx \biggl[ \dfrac{ E[ Z_T ] }{ P } + 1 \biggr] ^{1/T} - 1 \]
where $Z_T$ is the expected aggregate cum-dividend earnings for the next $T$ periods. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Accounting anomalies, risk and return} \\
  S.H. Penman and J. Zhu (2011)
\end{book}
Cash accounting recognizes revenue when it is actually received.

Accrual accounting recognizes revenue when it becomes certain.

One could go one step further and recognize revenue when it can be forecasted:
this explains many accounting ``anomalies'' -- higher returns are a compensation for the uncertainty of future profits.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A dynamic model of characteristic-based return predictability} \\
  A. Alti and S. Titman (2017)
\end{book}
Model firms as a combination of assets and projects, 
each project yielding a deterministic cash flow from inception to termination 
and liquidation cash flows (salvage value),
with younger firms having more, and more profitable projects.
\begin{align*}
  z_i &\in \{ \text{early}, \text{mature}, \text{dying}, \text{dead} \} &&\text{state} \\
  dk_{it} &= k_{z_i} ( 1-\lambda) (dt + dM_t) && \text{capital} \\
  df_{it} &= (a_{z_i} k_{z_i} - \lambda f_{it} ) ( dt + dM_t ) && \text{profitability}  \\
  dM_t &= \mu_t dt + \sigma_M d \omega_t^M && \hspace*{-1cm}\text{systematic disruption} \\
  d \mu_t &= - \rho \mu_t dt + \sigma d\omega_t^\mu && \hspace*{-1.7cm}\text{disruption climate (hidden)} \\
  ds_t &= \eta d\omega_t^M + \sqrt{1-\eta^2}  d\omega_t^s && \hspace*{-1cm}\text{soft interaction signal}
\end{align*}

Investors' overconfidence on the precision of their estimates creates mispricing.

Having a model of the economy (knowing the soft information $s$ instead of just the performance of the sorted portfolios)
helps (it doubles the information ratio).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Structure in the Tweet haystack: uncovering the link between text-based sentiment signals and financial markets} \\
  A. Gro\ss-Klu\ss mann et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Manually identify 200 ``expert'' Twitter users 
(from web pages: ``top 50 Twitter influencers'', ``who to follow'', etc.); 
measure tweet sentiment using the Harvard general inquirer and Loughran-McDonald lists
(in the latter, adapted to finance, ``tax'' is no longer negative),
without stemming (this can mix up some positive and negative words).
Sentiment and sentiment disagreement have some predictive power on market returns and volatility.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock return predictability:\\ consider your open options} \\
  F. Farazmand and A. de Souza (2017)
\end{book}
The put-call open interest ratio (aggregated put open interest, divided by aggregated call open interest, for liquid options, \ie, $K/S \in [.8,1.2]$ and $\text{maturity} \in [30\text{d}, 90\text{d}]$),
for high-volatility (arbitrage-constrained) stocks
is a good predictor of future market returns.
This can be explained by investors' limited attention.
% We already use that for index options, but these are stock options.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Beta dispersion and market timing} \\
  L.C. Kuntz (2016)
\end{book}
After an exogenous shock to the market,
stocks with a high beta are in trouble;
if there are many of them, \ie, in markets with high beta dispersion (measured by the 10\%-90\% interquantile range),
this could trigger a second, endogenous shock.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Investor attention and sentiment:\\ risk or anomaly?} \\
  M.C. Bucher (2017)
\end{book}
Google searches to measure investor attention (index, stock tickers)
and sentiment (panic words, Fears index).

%% 2017-12
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithmic differentiation in finance explained}\\
  M. Henrard (2017)
\end{book}
\nextbook

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{R in Finance 2017} \\
\end{book}

\textbf{1.}
% Time series models, in particular generalizations of GARCH models
\d{GAS} (generalized autoregressive score) models generalize GARCH models (a Gaussian GAS is a GARCH) but can be made less sensitive to outliers, leading to better VaR estimation.

Markov switching GARCH models (implementated in \texttt{MSGARCH}) give better VaR forecasts than single-regime ones. 
Prefer GJR, with skewed Student innovations; prefer MCMC to MLE to account for parameter uncertainty.

Continuous GARCH models (\d{COGARCH}) generalize GARCH models to non-regularly-spaced observations. 
In high dimensions, they can be combined with ICA (independent component analysis).

The yuima package estimates and simulates SDEs; it now has an HTML GUI.

Two time series $X$, $Y$ are \d{partially cointegrated} if some linear combination 
$Y-\beta X$ is partially auto-regressive, \ie, the sum of a random walk and an AR process.

For many multivariate diffusions, an arbitrarily close approximation (in terms of Hermite polynomials) of the transition function is known in closed form: the MLE is easy to compute. (If not all components are observed, further approximations are available.)
The \texttt{MLEMVD} package implements this for many for many 1- or 2-dimensional diffusions
\begin{align*}
  dX_t &= \mu(X_t) dt + \Sigma(X_t) dW_t \\
  \mu &: \RR^m \to \RR^n \\
  \Sigma &: \RR^n \to \RR^{n\times n}
\end{align*}

\textbf{2.} 
% Risk measurement
The standard error of performance measures $T$ (Sharpe, Sortino, etc.) can be estimated from the sample 
cumulative distribution function $F_n$ by bootstrap (or \d{block bootstrap}, in presence of serial correlation) 
or with \d{influence functions} 
\[ \text{IF}(r,T,F_n) = \left. \dfrac{d}{d\gamma} T\bigl( (1-\gamma) F_n + \gamma \delta_r \bigr) \right|_{\gamma=0} \]
(for many performance measures, they can be explicitly computed)
since the asymptotic variance is 
\[ \var[ T(F_n) \underset{ n \to \infty }{\sim} E\bigl[ \text{IF}(r_1,T,F) \bigr]. \]
With serial correlation,
\begin{align*}
  \var\bigl[ T(F_n) \bigr] &= \sum_{ \ell \in \ZZ } E\bigl[ \text{IF}(r_1,T,F_n) \text{IF}(r(1+\ell),T,F_n) \bigr] \\
                 &= \sum_{ \ell \in \ZZ } C(\ell)   &&   \hspace*{-4.5cm}\text{autocovariance density} \\
                 &= S(0)                           &&   \hspace*{-4.5cm}\text{spectral density}
\end{align*}                 
and $S(0)$ can be estimated from a polynomial fit to the periodogram of the IF time series $\text{IF}(r_t,T,F_n)$.

Whenever possible, use the \emph{two-scale estimator} (TSRV) of volatility.

The (block) rearrangement algorithm (BRA) takes a matrix and reorders the elements within each column to minimize the variance of row sums.
It can be used to estimate the worst-case portfolio VaR when only the asset return distributions are known (and not their copula).
When applied to $(X_1,\dots,X_n,-S)$, where $S=X_1+\cdots+X_n$, 
it gives a dependence structure close to the maximum entropy one 
(the correlation matrix of the reordered $(X_1,\dots,X_n)$ has maximum determinant), 
\ie, answers the question: ``if we know the marginal distribution of $X_1,\dots,X_n$ and $S$, 
what is the most likely dependence structure?''

When estimating sample comoment tensors,
do not re-compute entries already computed (the tensors are symmetric), 
and use shrinkage, towards a factor model 
\begin{align*}
    X &= BF + \epsilon \\
    \Sigma &= B \Sigma_F B' + \Delta \\
    \Phi &= B \Phi_F (B\tens B)' + \Omega \\
    \Psi &= B \Psi_F (B\tens B\tens B)' + \Gamma.
\end{align*}

Covariance matrix estimators can be more or less structured: 
\begin{align*}
 (1) \ R_t &\sim N(0,\Sigma_t)              &  (3) \ R_t &= F_t \beta_t' + \epsilon_t \\ 
 &                                          &    \beta_t &= \beta_{t-1} + \eta_t \\
 (2) \ R_t &= F_t \beta_t' + \epsilon_t     & \epsilon_t &\sim N(0,\Sigma_t) \\
 \epsilon_t &\sim N(0,\Sigma_t)             & \epsilon_t &\sim N(0,\Gamma_t)
\end{align*}

\textbf{3.}
% Portfolio construction
Do not just prefer the ``best'' strategy: also look at its \emph{sensitivity} to parameter estimation. 
This is the usual bias-variance tradeoff: you may prefer a slightly worse performance is it is more certain.

It is difficult to select a good manager, especially if there are many of them and/or there is not enough history. 
Prefer \emph{ensemble methods}: instead of selecting a single manager, select several.

The following stochastic, continuous-time model of \d{private equity} cash flow and value, 
which can accommodate fees, should replace the Yale model (deterministic, with no fees). 
\begin{align*}
    dV &= V ( \mu dt + \beta \sigma dB + \sigma dB ) + dD - dR  & \text{Value} \\
    dD &= \delta (I-D) dt                    & \llap{\text{Cumulative drawdowns}} \\
    dR &= vVdt                               & \llap{\text{Cumulative distributions}} \\
    \delta &= \delta_0 + \sigma B            & \llap{\text{Drawdown rate}} \\
    v &= v_0 t  + \sigma B                   & \llap{\text{Distribution rate}} \\
    B &                                      & \llap{\text{Different Brownian motions}} \\
    \sigma  &                                & \llap{\text{Different volatilities}} \\
    dC &= C r dt - dD + Rdt                  & \llap{\text{Cash flow}}
\end{align*}    
This leads to several measures of risk: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Market: $\text{VaR}[ \text{P\&L} ]$, where 
      \[ \text{P\&L}_{[t,t+h]} = d_{[t,t+h]} P_{t+h} - P_t, \]
      $d$ is the discount factor
      and $P = V + C$;
\item Liquidity: VaR of the liquidity-adjusted P\&L, 
  \[ (1-\pi_{t+h}) V_{t+h} + C_{t+h} - P_t, \] where 
  \( d\pi = \kappa ( \theta - \pi ) + \sigma dB$ 
  is the secondary market discount rate;
\item Cash flow: $\text{VaR}[ C_{t+h} - C_t ]$.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{4.}
% Optimization
For a sparse solution to your portfolio optimization problems, add an $L^1$ constraint;
this also improves performance and can be generalized to structured sparsity.

\d{Risk parity} can be generalized to higher moments. 
The \texttt{mrcp} package minimizes the discrepancy between the contribution to second-, third- or fourth-order risk or the assets, or some linear combination of those discrepancies. 
\begin{align*}
  v &= E (r_p - \mu_p )^2 \\
    &= E\bigl[ r_p-\mu_p) \tens (r_p-\mu_p) \bigr] \\
    &= E\Bigl[ \sum w_i (r_i - \mu_i) \tens (r_p - \mu_p) \Bigr] \\
  s &= E\Bigl[ \sum w_i (r_i - \mu_i) \tens (r_p - \mu_p) ^{\tens2} \Bigr] \\
  \kappa &= E\Bigl[ \sum w_i (r_i - \mu_i) \tens (r_p - \mu_p) ^{\tens3} \Bigr]
\end{align*}
\[
\dfrac{ \partial v }{ \partial w } = 2 M_2 w, 
\ 
\dfrac{ \partial s }{ \partial w } = 3 M_3 (w\tens w), 
\ 
\dfrac{ \partial \kappa }{ \partial w } = 4 M_4 (w\tens w \tens w)
\]
\[ M_i = E \bigl[ (r-\mu)^{\tens i} \bigr] \]
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & w \\ 
\text{To minimize} & 
\lambda_1 \var w \otimes \dfrac{ \partial v }{ \partial w } + 
\lambda_2 \var w \otimes \dfrac{ \partial s }{ \partial w } + {}
\\ &  
\lambda_3 \var w \otimes \dfrac{ \partial \kappa \vrule height 1.1em width 0pt }{ \partial w \mathstrut }  \\
\text{Such that} & w' \mathbf 1 = 1, \ w \geq 0
\end{array}
\]

\texttt{PortfolioAnalytics} can optimize (small) portfolios
and generate random portfolios.

\textbf{5.}
% Markets
The score difference, in a soccer match, can be modeled as a difference of Poisson processes 
(a \d{Skellam process}) and calibrated using the odds provided by the bookie (dynamically); 
one can also define an ``implied volatility''
$ \sqrt{ \lambda^A_t + \lambda^B_t } \sqrt{1-t}$, $t \in [0,1]$.

Ratings (credit ratings, Morning Star stars, etc.) are often estimated independently in different categories, on a curve (\ie, not calibrated) and therefore not comparable. Yet, people compare them.

The overnight gap on ex-div date is different from the dividend, because of taxes.

The \texttt{obmodeling} package models the order book (VPIN, etc.).

Not all brokers are liquidity providers; high-frequency traders seem to be liquidity-takers.

\textbf{6.}
% Options
The \texttt{sn} package provides a skew-T distribution.

The risk-neutral density can be modeled as a piecewise constant function, generating the correct call and put prices (minimizing the squared relative error).

\textbf{7.}
% Machine learning
\texttt{MXNet} is available from R; it also works on Windows and can leverage your GPU.

Email length, and perhaps also sentiment was a better predictor of future returns than new sentiment in the two years leading to Enron's demise. \d{Topic analysis} (LDA) may also be interesting.

The \texttt{mlr} package now provides the same interface for machine learning (regression, etc.) and time series forecasting (ARIMA, GARCH, etc.)

Several talks compared ML algorithms (Adaboost, MARS, Neural networks and error correcting models (ECM) are very similar -- prefer Adaboost and MARS).

Projection methods turn big data into data.
A random orthogonal projection on a 1-dimensional subspace gives a lower bound for pairwise distances; 
\d{random projections} can be used to find the most correlated columns in a large matrix (\texttt{tcor}). 
Sparse SVD (\texttt{irlba}) can help compute the most central nodes of a large graph or bicluster large matrices (\texttt{s4vd}).

\textbf{8.}
% Bayesian statistics, MCMC, Stan
If the prior is not a single distribution but a family of distribution, then so is the posterior: 
it is a \emph{regularization path}, and can be used to choose the ``best'' prior and also for prior sensitivity analysis.

To model the time evolution of multivariate yield curves $y_{\text{market}, t}(\text{tenor})$, 
write them as linear combinations of a few ``principal'' yield curves, 
modeled with splines, with weights changing as random walks. 
This is implemented in the \texttt{FDLM} package 
[but this could probably be implemented in Stan, and HMC should be better than Gibbs sampling].

\textbf{9.}
% IT
Microsoft R server lets you expose your code as an API (not unlike OpenCPU). You can also put your R code in a stored procedure (as with Postgres).

The \texttt{roll} package provides a few (fast, parallelized with \texttt{RcppParallel}) rolling statistics: 
regression, PCA, correlation matrices, etc.

\texttt{ztsdb} is a time series database (C++, either file-based or client-server), append-optimized, with an R interface, and an R-like language.

Syberia is a workflow framework for data science in~R.

The \texttt{rTRNG} package provides random numbers for parallel MCMC computations

\emph{You do not have big data.} If you want speed, try VowpalWabbit and xgboost. If you really want something distributed, try H2O (it often turns out to be faster than Spark).

\textbf{10.}
% Plots, Tidyverse
Some of the presentations showcased analyses with 
\texttt{plotly}, \texttt{shiny}, \texttt{flexdashboard}, \texttt{dygraph}, \texttt{rnaturalearth}, etc.

The tidyverse assumes data is in dataframes, while financial data tends to be in xts objects. The \texttt{tidyquant} package provides a ``tidy'' interface to manipulate data in xts objects.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Five stages\\ of accepting constructive mathematics} \\
  A. Brauer (2016)  
\end{book}
\textbf{1.}
\d{Constructive mathematics} is mathematics without the law of the excluded middle
(and, therefore, without the axiom of choice):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item There are still proofs by negation 
($P \Rightarrow \bot$ is the definition of $\neg P$),
but no proofs by contradiction 
(proving $\neg P \Rightarrow \bot$ only gives $\neg \neg P$, not $\neg P$).
\item The notions of non-empty set ($A \neq \nothing$) and inhabited set ($\exists x \in A$) are different.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{2.} Since we have removed an axiom, we can add more,
which sometimes leads to surprising consequences, e.g., 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item All functions are continuous; 
\item Subsets of finite sets need not be finite (\ie, in bijection with some $\llbracket 1, n \rrbracket$);
\item There is an injection $\NN ^ \NN \hookrightarrow \NN$;
\item There is an unbounded continuous map $[0,1] \to \RR$;
\item $\RR$ has zero measure;
\item There is an unbounded, increasing sequence without an accumulation point.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{3.}
Models of constructive mathematics include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Classical mathematics;
\item Computer programs (realizability);
\item Sheaves on a topological space $X$ (the truth values are the open sets of $X$), a topos;
\item \emph{Locales} (partially-ordered sets with properties similar to those of the open sets of a topological space; 
  points are not needed: the regular open subsets of~$\RR$, \ie, subsets $U$ such that 
  \( U = \mathring{ \bar U } \), 
  is a sublocale of $\RR$ with no points).
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{4.} Some results need to be adapted 
(e.g., the intermediate value theorem remains true with altered conclusions or additional assumptions,
e.g., an approximate solution, a monotonic function, or isolated roots)
and new objects can be defined: for instance, 
the set of random reals of $\RR$ minus all subsets of measure zero -- 
it has full measure, but no points. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What is the best fractional derivative\\ to fit data?} \\
  R. Almeida 
\end{book}
Phenomena traditionally modeled by differential equations such as 
\[ 
y' = k, \qquad y' = ky, \qquad y' = k(y-a), \qquad y' = f(t)
\]
may be more accurately described by fractional differential equations 
\[ 
D^\alpha y = k, \quad D^\alpha y = ky, \quad D^\alpha y = k(y-a), \quad D^\alpha y = f(t)
\]
for some $\alpha \in (0,1)$.
The fractional differential and integral are,
for $\alpha \in (n-1,n)$,
\begin{align*}
  D^\alpha f(x) &= \dfrac1{\Gamma(n-\alpha)} \int_a^x (x-t) ^ {n-\alpha-1} f^{(n)} (t) \, dt \\
   %D^{\alpha,\psi} f(x) &= \dfrac1{\Gamma(n-\alpha)} \int_a^x \psi'(t) \bigl( \psi(x) - \psi(t) \bigr)^{n-\alpha-1} 
   %\left( \dfrac1{\psi'(t)} \dfrac{d}{dt} \right)^n f(t) dt \\
   D^{\alpha,\psi} f(x) &= \dfrac1{\Gamma(n-\alpha)} \times \\
   & \hspace*{-8mm} \int_a^x \psi'(t) \bigl( \psi(x) - \psi(t) \bigr)^{n-\alpha-1} 
  \left( \dfrac1{\psi'(t)} \dfrac{d}{dt} \right)^n f(t) dt \\
  I^{\alpha,\psi} f(x) &= \dfrac1{\Gamma(\alpha)} \int_a^x \psi'(t) \bigl( \psi(x) - \psi(t) \bigr) ^{\alpha-1} f(t) dt.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian inference of log determinants} \\
  J. Fitzsimons et al.
\end{book}
To compute log-determinants of large kernel matrices
as a Bayesian inference problem,
use 
\begin{align*}
  \log \det A &= \tr \log A \\
  \log( I-A) &= \sum_{ k \geq 1 } A^k \\
  \tr A &= \E_{ \mathbf r \sim N(0,1) } \mathbf r' A \mathbf r
\end{align*}
and use probabilistic numerics for the 
\emph{stochastic trace estimation}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Exploiting gradients and Hessians in Bayesian optimization and Bayesian quadrature} \\
  A. Wu et al.
\end{book}
In Bayesian optimization,
if the gradient or the Hessian is readily available, use it:
the joint process $(f, \nabla f)$ is still Gaussian, with covariance function 
\[ \begin{pmatrix} k(x,y) & \nabla_{\!x} k(x,y) \\ \nabla_{\!y} k(x,y) & \nabla_{\!x} \nabla_{\!y} k(x,y) \end{pmatrix}. \]
The algorithms do not change, they no not use directly the derivatives, 
but conditioning on this additional information helps.

The kernel becomes ill-conditioned much more quickly:
if the bandwidth is small, rescaling can help; 
if it is large, try moving to the spectral domain:
\[ f \sim \text{GP} \implies \mathscr F f \sim \text{GP}. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the construction of probabilistic Newton-type algorithms} \\
  A.G. Wills and T.B. Sch\"on
\end{book}
Model the Hessian as a Gaussian process, updated with 1-dimensional information at each step (quasi-Newton),
and use this expectation for the Newton step in optimization problems.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{No spurious local minima in nonconvex low rank problems: a unified geometric analysis} \\
  R. Ge et al.
\end{book}
Many nonconvex optimization problems (even asymmetric ones) have a well-behaved loss landscape: 
local minima are also global
(and saddle points have at least one negative eigenvalue in their Hessian).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A probabilistic linear genetic programming with stochastic context-free grammar\\ for solving symbolic regression problems} \\
  L.F. Dal Piccol Sotto and V.V. de Melo
\end{book}
Use a stochastic grammar as prior for symbolic regression.



%% Change points
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Strucural change in (economic) time series} \\
  C. Kleiber (2016)
\end{book}
To test for the presence of structural change
\begin{align*}
&\text{Univariate signal}  & y_t &= \mu_t        + \epsilon_t & \mu_t &= \text{constant?} \\
&\text{Regression}         & y_t &= x'_t \beta_t + \epsilon_t & \beta_t &= \text{constant?}
\end{align*}
one can look at the cummulated residuals (\d{empirical fluctuation process}, EFP, Rec-\textsc{Cumsum})
\begin{align*}
  \hat\mu_k &= \dfrac1k \sum_1^k y_t \\
  e_t &= y_t - \hat \mu_{t-1} \\
  S_T &= \dfrac{1}{ \hat\sigma \sqrt T} \sum e_t
\end{align*}
which can be approximated by a Brownian motion, and look for excessive fluctuations (crossing certain boundaries).

Instead, one can look at the OLS-\textsc{Cumsum} process
\begin{align*}
e_t &= y_t - \hat \mu_T \\
S_k &= \dfrac1{ \hat\sigma \sqrt T} \sum_1^k e_t
\end{align*}
which can be approximated by a Brownian bridge;
fluctuations can be identified by looking at $\max_t \va{ S_t }$.

The location of the breakpoints can be estimated by minimizing the sum of squared residuals, with dynamic programming;
the BIC can help choose the number of breakpoints.

In R, check the \texttt{strucchange}, \texttt{ecp} and \texttt{wbs} packages.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A nonparametric approach for multiple changepoint analysis of multivariate data} \\
  D.S. Matteson and N.A. James (2013)
\end{book}
To detect a changepoint in a multivariate time series $X_{1:n}$, 
compute
\[
\argmax_k \dfrac{ k(n-k) }{ n } \mathscr E( X_{1:n}, X_{k+1:n} ) 
\]
where
\[
  \mathscr E(X,Y) = 2 E \va{ X-Y }^\alpha - E \va{ X-X' }^\alpha - E \va{ Y-Y' }^\alpha
\]
is the distance correlation
(which can be seen as a divergence between characteristic functions).

In presence of multiple changepoints, prefer 
\[ \argmax_{k,\ell} \dfrac{k\ell}{k+\ell} \mathscr E(X_{1:k}, X_{k+1:k+\ell}) \]
and proceed recursively.
In R: \texttt{ecp::e.divisive}.

Use permutation tests to asses the significance of the breakpoints and decide when to stop.

The agglomerative variant of the algorithm, merging the adjacent clusters minimizing 
\[ \dfrac{ \va{C_i} \va{C_{i+1}} }{ \va{C_1} + \va{C_{i+1}} } \mathscr E( C_i, C_{i+1} ), \]
is faster. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wild binary segmentation\\ for multiple change-point detection} \\
  P. Fryzlewicz (2014)
\end{book}

\emph{Binary segmentation} splits a time series at the point maximizing the \textsc{Cusum} statistic
(equivalently, it fits a piecewise constant function with exactly one jump to the data),
and proceeds recursively to find more changepoints.
It does not work well in presence of several breakpoints.

\d{Wild binary segmentation} is similar, but works on randomly-selected subsequences, of various lengths.
R~implentation in \texttt{wbs}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal detection of changepoints\\ with a linear computational cost} \\
  R. Killick et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Optimal partitioning uses dynamic programming to find the breakpoints minimizing some criterion, 
with a penalty $\beta$ to control this number 
\begin{align*}
  F(s) &= \min_{\substack{ \tau \text{ partition} \\ \text{of } 1:s }} \sum_i \text{Cost}( y_{ \tau_1 + 1 : \tau_{i+1} } ) + \beta \\
  &= \min_{ t < s } F(t) + \text{Cost}( y_{t+1 : s } ) + \beta. 
  % Missing base case
\end{align*}
The computations can be pruned (pruned exact linear time, PELT) using the fact that,
under reasonable assumptions, 
if $F(t) + \text{Cost}( y_{t+1 : s} ) + k \geq F(s)$,
then $t$ cannot be the last optimal changepoint for any $T>s$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Local extremes, runs, strings\\ and multiresolution} \\
  P.L. Davies and A. Kovac (2001)
\end{book}
% Approximating data with a function with few extrema
After smoothing a time series, one expects the residuals to be similar to white noise; 
in particular, their signs should not present long \emph{runs}.

To detect extrema in a noisy time series (to approximate a time series with a function with few extrema),
one can try to minimize the number of extrema among approximations of the time series 
for which the maximum length of the runs is below some threshold.
This can be generalized to a multiresolution condition, where $2^k$ consecutive residuals are aggregated, 
for all~$k$.

Given upper and lower bounds on the unknown values of a time series observed with noise, 
the \emph{taut string method} looks for the shortest curve between those bounds
(it can be computed in linear time, see
\texttt{ftnonpar::mintvnorm(..., method=1)}).






%% IBCC
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic Bayesian combination\\ of multiple imperfect classifiers} \\
  E. Simpson et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Consider $k$ judges infering the labels of $N$ objects: 
\begin{align*}
  \kappa &: \text{label distribution (multinomial)} \\
  t_i &: \text{label of object }i \\
  c_{ik} &: \text{label assigned to object $i$ by judge $k$} \\
  \pi_{k\ell j} &: P[ \text{ judge $k$ says $\ell$ when the true label is $j$ } ] \\
  \alpha &: \text{Dirichlet prior on $\pi$} \\
  \nu &: \text{Dirichlet prior on $\kappa$}
\end{align*}
The \d{IBCC} (independent Bayesian combination of classifiers) model can be estimated by 
MAP (maximum a posteriori), Gibbs sampling or, better, variational Bayes (VB).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian classifier combination} \\
  H.C. Kim and Z. Ghahramani (2012)
\end{book}
% Initial paper in IBCC
Contrary to IBCC, Bayesian model averaging (BMA) assumes the classifiers are somewhat correct (unbiased).

\emph{Stacking} is an alternative to ensembling in which we do not simply average the classifiers, 
but learn how to combine them -- for IBCC, the combiner is a graphical model.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=.8,inner sep = 1pt]
\node (in) at (0,0) {input};
\node (m1) at (-3,1) {model$_1$};
\node (m2) at (-1,1) {model$_2$};
\node (m3) at (1,1) {$\cdots$};
\node (m4) at (3,1) {model$_n$};
\node (c) at (0,2) {combiner};
\node (o) at (0,3) {output};
\draw[thick,->] (in) -- (m1);
\draw[thick,->] (in) -- (m2);
\draw[thick,->] (in) -- (m4);
\draw[thick,->] (m1) -- (c);
\draw[thick,->] (m2) -- (c);
\draw[thick,->] (m4) -- (c);
\draw[thick,->] (c) -- (o);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation and\\ large-scale simultaneous significance testing} \\
  B. Efron
\end{book}
Multiple testing procedures assume the tests are independent:
if not, they can be too conservative or liberal.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Convert the test statistic to $z$ scores;
\item Fit a Gaussian to the middle of the distribution;
\item Use this empirical null distribution instead of $N(0,1)$ to compute the $p$-values.
\end{compactitem}
In R, check the \texttt{locfdr} package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Homotopy parametric simplex method\\ for sparse learning} \\
  H. Pang et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Many sparse learning problems, 
\begin{align*}
  &\argmin_\theta L(\theta) + \lambda \n{ \theta } _1 \\
  \text{or } & \argmin_\theta \n{ \theta }_1 \text{ st } \n{ \nabla L(\theta) }_\infty \leq \lambda 
\end{align*}
(Dantzig selector, sparse regression, etc.)
can be formulated as linear optimization problems.
The \emph{parametric simplex method} (PSM, or homotopy optimization)
generalizes the simplex algorithm to solve those problem, 
while keeping the regularization parameter $\lambda$ unspecified -- 
the result is not a single solution, but a whole regularization path.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Voronoi treemaps} \\
  M. Balzer and O. Deussen (2005)
\end{book}
In a \emph{centroidal Voronoi tessellation} (CVT), the generators are the centers of mass of their Voronoi cells.
The ``distances'' 
\begin{align*}
  d(p_i,q) &= \n{ p_i - q } - w_i \\
  d(p_i,q) &= \n{ p_i - q }^2 - w_i \\
\end{align*}
define weighted Voronoi tessellations;
the latter (power-weighted Voronoi diagram) has straight boundaries.
To build a \emph{Voronoi tree map},
start with a CVT (replace each generator with the center of mass of its cell and iterate a few times),
adjust the weights in the direction suggested by the desired and current areas, and iterate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Computing Voronoi treemaps} \\
  A. Nocaj and U. Brandes (2012)
\end{book}
The power (weighted Voronoi) diagram can be computed using a 3-dimensional convex hull algorithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Certification of approximate roots of exact polynomial systems} \\
  A. Szanto (2016)
\end{book}
Bertini (non-free, but Bertini2, in development, will be GPL)
solves large systems of algebraic equations (numerical algebraic geometry).
An approximate zero $z$ of $f$ is a point such that Newton's iteration started at $z$ 
converges to a root $\xi \in \CC^n$ of $f$, with an upper bound $R$ on $\n{z-\xi}$
and the guarantee that there is no other root in $B(z,R)$.

As an application, one can prove that, in $\RR^3$, seven (infinite) cylinders can touch each other.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Level set method} \\
  T.H. Colding and W.P Minicozzi (2016)
\end{book}
If the level sets of $v: \RR^2 \to \RR$ 
flow by mean curvature,
then \[ v_t = \va{ \nabla v } \div \left( \dfrac{ \nabla v }{ \n{ \nabla v } } \right). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hodge theory of matroids} \\
  K. Adiprasito et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The coefficients of the chromatic polynomial of a graph $\chi_G(q)$
(number of proper colourings of $G$ using $q$ colours
form a log-concave sequence
\[ a_i ^2 \geq a_{i-1} a_{i+1}. \]
More generally,
the number $f_i$ of independent subsets of size $i$ in a matroid
is log-concave -- this can be proved by defining a Hodge theory for matroids.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TimescaleDB:\\ SQL made scalable for time series} \\  
\end{book}
Postgres extension to store time series as tables 
$(\text{time}, \text{id}_1, \dots, \text{id}_n, v_1, \dots, v_m)$,
partitioned on $(\text{time},\text{id})$. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Implementing operational calculus\\ on programming spaces\\ for differentiable computing} \\
  \v Z. Sajovic (2017)
\end{book}
Automatic differentiation (AD) in C++, using templates.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{High-dimensional discriminant analysis} \\
  C. Bouveyron et al.
\end{book}
Quadratic discriminant analysis (QDA) requires estimates of the variance matrix for each class: 
in high dimension, use regularized estimators, or make further assumptions, e.g., 
that the variance matrix only has two distinct eigenvalues. 




%% TDA in linguistics
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistent topology of syntax} \\
  A. Port et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Application of TDA to language classification.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spin glass models of syntax\\ and language evolution} \\
  K. Siva et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Modeling language interactions (from the number of bilingual editors for each pair of languages)
using a \emph{spin glass model} (aka \emph{Potts model}, \ie, the \emph{Ising model} with more than two states).
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Prevalance and recoverability of syntactic parameters in sparse distributed memories} \\
  J.J. Park et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Features to classify human languages (e.g., SVO order) can be found in: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item SSWL: syntactic structures of the world's language;
\item WALS: world atlas of language structures.
\end{compactitem}




\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Manopt, a matlab toolbox\\ for optimization on manifolds} \\
  N. Boumal et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Describe the manifold (from a few predefined ones and a few operations: projection, quotient, product); 
provide the (Euclidean) derivatives;
choose the method (trust region or conjugate gradient).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Langevin and Hamiltonian based sequential MCMC for efficient Bayesian filtering\\ in high-dimensional spaces} \\
  F. Septier and G.W. Peters (2015)
\end{book}
Sequential Monte Carlo (SMC, particle filters) does not work well in high dimensions:
instead of using importance weights, apply a few MCMC moves after each step.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Information-geometric\\ Markov chain Monte Carlo methods\\ using diffusions} \\
  S. Livingstone and M. Girolami (2014)
\end{book}
The Metropolis adjusted Langevin algorithm (MALA) can be generalized to Riemannian manifolds.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast Langevin based algorithm\\ for MCMC in high dimension} \\
  A. Durmus et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Other discretizations of the Langevin diffusion (SDE) 
give variants of MALA (Metropolis-adjusted Langevin algorithm) 
with faster convergence in higher dimensions.
% Not read.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Langevin-Euler moves} \\
\end{book}
% TODO: Move this somewhere else (it is not a paper)

If $\pi(x) \propto \exp - U(x) $
then the solution of the Langevin diffusion SDE
\[ dx = -\dfrac12 \dfrac{ \partial U }{ \partial x } dt + dW \]
follows the target distribution $\pi$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{PyCaMa: Python for cash management} \\
  F. Salas-Molina et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Given a set of bank accounts, desired transactions (from customers to suppliers), and transaction costs
($A\to B \to C$ may be cheaper than $A \to C$),
finding the cheapest path is a linear problem.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Downside risks\\ and the cross-section of asset returns} \\
  A. Farago and R. T\'edongap (2017)
\end{book}
The cross-section of returns can be explained with the covariance between stock returns and: 
market returns, change in market volatility, 
down times (boolean variable indicating either a fall in price or a rise in volatility),
market returns${}\times \mathbf{1}_{\text{down}}$, 
change in market volatility${}\times \mathbf{1}_{\text{down}}$.
Contrary to the Fama-French-Cahart model (which works better for stocks),
this applies to all asset classes, including options and currencies.
% Disappointment aversion

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Systematic tail risk} \\
  R.D.F. Harris et al. (2017)
\end{book}
The beta 
$\beta = \cov(R, R_m) / \var R_m$
and the correlation $\cor(R, R_m)$ can be turned into downside risk measures
by conditioning on $R_m \leq \mu_m$, or replacing market returns $R_m$
with their below-average part $\min\{ R_m - \mu_m, 0 \}$,
or by replacing both asset and market returns with their below-average parts.

The extreme downside betas (EDB) and correlation (EDC) are obtained by replacing the means 
$\mu$, $\mu_m$ with the 5\% quantile.
The extreme downside hedge (EDH) is the sensitivity of the forthcoming change in market value at risk, 
\( \beta( R_t \sim \Delta \text{VaR}_{ t \to t+1} ^{ \text{market, 5\%} } ), \)
estimated from an AR(1)-GJR-GARCH(1,1) model with skewed Student innovations, on a 5-year moving window.
There is a tail risk premium, visible if you compare the returns in $[t, t+1]$ and the risk estimated at time $t+1$, 
but it is not investible. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conditional asset pricing\\ in international equity markets} \\
  T. Huynh (2017)
\end{book}
The returns of an equal-weighted momentum-value portfolio can be esplained using the ``lagged component'' betas as instrumental variables. 

The time-varying betas of a portfolio (on a moving window) can be estimated directly,
by considering the portfolio as a single asset (``lagged portfolio'' betas)
or as a portfolio-weighted average of stock-level betas (``lagged component'' betas).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Contagion in financial systems:\\ a Bayesian network approach} \\
  C. Chong and C. Kl\"uppelberg (2017)
\end{book}
Tightly interconnected firms are robust to small shocks, but the network amplifies large ones (phase transition, non-monotonic effect).
Model firms as: 
\begin{align*}
  X_i & \sim N(\mu_i, \sigma^2) && \text{assets} \\
  L_{ij} &&& \text{liabilities} \\
  E_i &= X_i + \sum_{j \text{ survives}} L_{ij} - \sum_j L_{ji} && \text{equity.}
\end{align*}
If the redemption graph has no cycles, the defaulting and surviving firms are well-defined but, if not, 
there are multiple solutions with non-zero probability.
For instance, with 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1,xscale=2,inner sep = 1pt]
\node[circle,draw] (A) at (0,0) {\$5};
\node[circle,draw] (B) at (1,0) {\$5};
\draw[thick,->] (A) to [bend left=30] node[auto,pos=.5] {\footnotesize\$10} (B);
\draw[thick,->] (B) to [bend left=30] node[auto,pos=.5] {\footnotesize\$10} (A);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
both survive if netting is possible, but both default if not.

Two solutions are obtained by progressively building the list of defaulting (resp. surviving) firms. 
Using those rules, one can extend the redemption graph into a directed acyclic graph (DAG), 
by creating $N$ copies of each vertex, corresponding to the iterations of the algorithm.
Bayesian network tools are then available: independence detection, 
systemic impact measures, default probabilities, etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Instrumented principal component analysis} \\
  B. Kelly et al. (2017)
\end{book}
Factor models combine stock-specific but constant loadings $\beta$ with common, dynamic factors $f$:
\[ y_{nt} = \sum_k \beta_{nk} f_{kt} + \epsilon_{nt}. \]
Dynamic factor models usually assume a simple model for the changes of $\beta$, e.g., a random walk:
\begin{align*}
  y_{nt} &= \sum_k \beta_{nk} f_{kt} + \epsilon_{nt} \\
  \beta_{n,k,t} &= \beta_{n,k,t-1} + \eta_{n,k,t}
\end{align*}
Instead, one can use additional data (time-changing, stock-specific ``instrumental'' variables $Z$) to predict $\beta$:
\begin{align*}
  y_{nt} &= \sum_k \beta_{nk} f_{kt} + \epsilon_{nt} \\
  \beta_{n,k,t} &= \sum_\ell Z_{k\ell,t} \Gamma_{\ell,k} + \eta_{n,k,t}.
\end{align*}

The model can be estimated as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Orthonormalize the instruments so that, for each $t$, $N^{-1} Z_t ' Z_t = I_L$;
\item Set $x_{\ell,t} = N^{-1} \sum_n Z_{n,\ell,t-1} y_{n,t}$;
\item Let $\Gamma_{\!\cdot,1},\dots,\Gamma_{\!\cdot,k}$ be the $k$ eigenvectors of $N^{-1} X' X$ for the $K$ largest eigenvalues, normalized ($\Gamma' \Gamma = I_K$) and signed (so that the first non-zero entry of each column be positive);
\item Set $f_{k,t} = \sum_\ell \Gamma_{\ell,k} x_{\ell,t}$.
\end{compactitem}

If $y_{n,t}$ are stock returns and $z_{n,\ell,t}$ stock characteristics, then $x_{\ell,t}$ are the returns of portfolios defined using the (raw) instrumental variables $Z$ as weights -- factor-mimicking portfolios usually discretize them first.

