\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Second-order optimization\\ over the multivariate Gaussian distribution} \\
  L. Malag\`o and G. Pistone (2016)
\end{book}
On a Riemannian manifold, the gradient and the Hessian, 
used in first- and second-order optimization algorithms
(e.g., gradient descent, Newton) become the \emph{natural gradient}
and the \emph{Riemannian Hessian}.
For statistical manifolds and their three connections 
(Levi-Civita, exponential, mixture), there are actually three Hessians.

\d{Stochastic relaxation} replaces
\[ x^* = \argmin_{x \in \RR^n} f(x) \]
with 
\[
  (x^*,0) = 
  \argmin_{\mu,V} 
  \E_{ X \sim N(\mu,V) }
  %\operatorname*{E}\limits_{ X \sim N(\mu,V) }
  %E_{ X \sim N(\mu,V) }^{\mathstrut} 
  \bigl[ f(X) \bigr].
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The landscape of empirical risk\\ for non-convex losses} \\
  S. Mei et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Given enough observations ($\Omega(d \log d)$ in dimension $d$),
non-convex loss functions, e.g., 
\[ \dfrac1n \sum \bigl( y_i - \sigma \langle w , x_i \rangle \bigr)^2 \]
for binary classification, have a unique minimizer.

<<LandscapeEmpiricalRisk,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
op <- par( mar = c(2,1,0,1), mfrow = c(1,3) )
f <- function(x) 1 / ( 1 + exp(-x) )
x <- seq( -3, 3, length = 100 )
curve( f(x^2), xlim = c(-3,3), ylim = c(.3,1.2), col = "purple", lwd = 2, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
lines( x, f(x^2) - .05 * cos(10*x), lwd = 3 )
mtext( "Not enough observations", side = 1, line = -.5 )
curve( f(x^2), xlim = c(-3,3), ylim = c(.3,1.2), col = "purple", lwd = 2, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
lines( x, ( 1 - (.1*x)^2) * f(1.1*(x-.3)^2) - .02, lwd = 3 )
mtext( "Enough observations", side = 1, line = -.5 )
curve( f(x^2), xlim = c(-3,3), ylim = c(.3,1.2), col = "purple", lwd = 2, axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" )
mtext( "Population", side = 1, line = -.5 )
par(op)
@

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Information geometry of neural networks} \\
  S.I. Amari
\end{book}
Natural gradient descent, for neural nets, with an approximation of the Riemannian metric, avoids plateaus.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hyperparameter optimization\\ with approximate gradient} \\
  F. Pedregosa (2016)
\end{book}
Hyperparameter optimization is a bilevel optimization problem
\[ 
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \lambda, \beta^* \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(\lambda) = g( \beta^*\!, \lambda ) \\
  \text{Such that} & \beta^* = \argmin_\beta h(\beta, \lambda)
\end{array}
\]
where 
\begin{align*} 
  \lambda &: \text{hyperparameters} \\
  \beta &: \text{parameters} \\
  h &: \text{loss} \\
  g &: \text{cross-validation loss}.  
\end{align*}
It can be reformulated as a constrained optimization 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \lambda, \beta \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(\lambda) = g( \beta, \lambda ) \\
  \text{Such that} & \nabla_{\!\!\beta}^{\mathstrut} h( \beta, \lambda ) = 0.
\end{array}
\]
The gradient can be computed:
\begin{align*}
  & \dfrac{ df }{ d\lambda } = 
  \dfrac{ \partial \beta }{ \partial \lambda } 
  \dfrac{ \partial g }{ \partial \beta } + 
  \dfrac{ \partial g }{ \partial \lambda } \\
  & \dfrac{ \partial}{ \partial \beta } \bigl[
    \nabla_{\!\!\beta}^{\mathstrut} h( \beta, \lambda )
  \bigr] = 0 \\
  & \nabla f = 
  \nabla_{\!\!\lambda}^{\mathstrut} g - ( \nabla_{\!\!\beta,\lambda}^{\mathstrut} g ) ' ( \nabla_{\!\!\beta,\beta}^{\mathstrut} g ) ^ {-1} \nabla_{\!\!\beta}^{\mathstrut} g.
\end{align*}
Approximations for $\beta^*$, $\nabla g$ and $\nabla^2 g$ are good enough.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mapping estimation\\ for discrete optimal transport} \\
  M. Perrot et al. (2016)
\end{book}
\d{Optimal transport}, in particular the \emph{Wasserstein distance},
is used in machine learning, as a divergence to compare distributions, 
to interpolate between distributions, 
to compute means, barycenters or PCA in spaces of probabilities.

Given probability spaces $(X,\mu)$ and $(Y,\nu)$
and a cost function $c : X \times Y \to \RR$,
the \d{Monge problem} 
asks for a function $T: X \to Y$
such that $T_\# \mu = \nu$
($T_\#$ is the push-forward)
minimizing $ \int_X c( x, Tx) d\mu(x)$.
The \d{Kantorovitch} relaxation asks instead 
for a probability distribution 
$\gamma$ on $X \times Y$
with marginals $\mu$ and $\nu$ 
minimizing $\int_{X \times Y} c(x,y) d\gamma(x,y)$.
For discrete distributions, 
in particular, for empirical distributions, 
this is a linear program.

Jointly learn the \emph{probabilistic coupling} $\gamma$ (from the empirical distributions)
and the \emph{barycentric mapping} $T$ (in a limited set of functions, e.g., linear, or using some kernel)
with a penalty (e.g., to ensure $T$ is close to the identity).

While transfer learning transforms a model $X \to Y$ into a model $X \to Y'$ with a new target (codomain),
\d{domain adaptation} transforms it to a model $X' \to Y$ with a different source space (domain). 

In image processing, before inpainting (Poisson image editing),
one can force the gradient distribution of the source image to match that of the target image 
(using a random sample of 500 gradients on each side).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to learn, from transfer learning\\ to domain adaptation: a unifying perspective}\\
  N. Patricia and B. Caputo
\end{book}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Supervised word mover's distance} \\
  G. Huang et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Combine the word mover's distance (the distance between two documents 
is the cost of the optimal transport between their (vector-embedded) bags of words) 
with metric learning (use the distance minimizing 
the leave-out-one (LOO) $k$-nearest neighbour error when predicting document labels). 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SAGA: a fast incremental gradient method with support for non-strongly convex composite objectives} \\
  A. Defazio et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To minimize a smooth convex function $f(x) = \tfrac1n \sum_i f(x)$,
\emph{variance-reduced stochastic gradient descent} uses updates of the form 
\begin{align*}
x &\leftarrow x - \gamma \dfrac1n \Bigl[ f'_i(x) - f'_i(x^*) + \sum f'_j(x^*) \Bigr] &&\text{(SAG)} \\
\text{or } x &\leftarrow
x - \gamma \Bigl[ f'_i(x) - f'_i(x^*) + \dfrac1n\sum f'_j(x^*) \Bigr] &&\text{(SVRG)}
\end{align*}
where $x^*$ and $\sum f'_i(x^*)$ are updated once in a while.

SAGA keeps track of $n$ points 
$(x_i)_{1 \leq i \leq n}$ and the derivatives of the corresponding terms: 
\[ x \leftarrow
x - \gamma \Bigl[ f'_i(x) - f'_i(x^*) + \dfrac1n\sum f'_j(x_j) \Bigr] ;
\]
one of them is updated at each step with the current value of $x$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Accelerating stochastic gradient descent\\ using predictive variance reduction} \\
  R. Johnson and T. Zhang (2013)
\end{book}
To reduce variance in stochastic gradient descent (SVRG), 
approximate the gradient of $f = \sum f_i$ as
\[ 
  \nabla f(x) \approx \nabla f(^*) - \nabla f_i(x^*) + \nabla f_i(x).
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Faster asynchronous SGD} \\
  A. Odena
\end{book}
Distributed stochastic gradient descent (SGD) ignores synchronization problems,
but keeps track of gradient staleness,
either with the number of updates skipped,
or with the difference between the gradient computed and the gradient that would have been computed
(on the same minibatch) 
with the latest parameters,
and uses it to fine-tune the learning rate
(not unlike RMS\-prop).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast and provably good seedings for $k$-means} \\
  O. Bachem et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Kmeans++ seeding does not scale to large datasets.
Instead of sampling each point exactly from 
$p(x) \propto d(x,C)^2$,
use MCMC,
 with the exact distribution after the first point
(mixed with the uniform distribution) as proposal distribution,
instead of a uniform distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Phased LSTM: accelerating recurrent network training for long or event-based sequences} \\
  A. Neil et al. (2016)
\end{book}
By allowing neurons to update at different frequencies (by adding a ``time gate''),
LSTMs can deal with irregularly-spaced signals, e.g., event streams.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sequential neural models with stochastic layers} \\
  M. Fraccaro et al. (2016)
\end{book}

Recurrent neural nets (RNN) and state space models (SSM) are eerily similar.

