\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rethinking LDA: why priors matter} \\
  H.M. Wallach et al.
\end{book}
Prefer asymmetric Dirichlet priors for the document-topic distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic topic models} \\
  D.M. Blei and J.D. Lafferty (2006)
\end{book}
Time-dependent topic models, 
in which the logarithms of the parameters of the Dirichlet distributions
follow random walks,
can be estimated via variational inference.
(The topics are constant, but their vocabulary changes: 
for instance, in the ``atomic physics'' topic, 
``matter'' declined and ``quantum'' rose in the 20th century.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{From word embeddings to document distances} \\
  M. J. Kusnet et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To compute the distance between two sentences or texts, 
(e.g., to retrieve the document closest to a given one),
replace them with clouds of points using a vector embedding
and use the \emph{earth moving distance} (EMD) between the two clouds.
Approximations of the EMD include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The distance between the centroids (to quickly identify potential candidates);
\item A relaxed (greedy) EMD, in which the whole mass of each word is moved towards the closest word.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Trans-gram,\\ fast cross-lingual word embeddings} \\
  J. Coulmance et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The skipgram model can also deal with multilingual aligned sentences
by defining the context of a word as the set of words in the aligned sentences.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Partially collapsed Gibbs samplers:\\ theory and methods} \\
  D.A. van Dyk and T. Park (2008)
\end{book}
In some of the steps of a Gibbs sampler, integrating out (marginalizing) some of the variables
instead of conditioning on them can improve convergence. 
Care should be taken to preserve the stationary distribution: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Move some of the variables from being conditioned to being sampled;
\item Re-order the steps so that those new varaibles are not used;
\item Marginalize them.
\end{compactitem}
Example: 
\[
  \begin{array}{r@{|}l} 
  W & XYZ \\
  X & WXY \\
  Y & WXZ \\
  Z & WXY
  \end{array}
  \!\!\!\leadsto\!\!\!
  \begin{array}{r@{|}l} 
  W & XYZ \\
  X & WXY \\
  WY & XZ \\
  WZ & XY
  \end{array}
  \!\!\!\!\!\leadsto\!\!\!\!
  \begin{array}{r@{|}l} 
  WY & XZ \\
  WZ & XY \\
  W & XYZ \\
  X & WXY 
  \end{array}
  \!\!\!\!\leadsto\!\!\!
  \begin{array}{r@{|}l} 
  Y & XZ \\
  Z & XY \\
  W & XYZ \\
  X & WXY 
  \end{array}  
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Metropolis-Hastings\\ within partially collapsed Gibbs samplers} \\
  D.A. van Dyk and X. Jiao (2014)
\end{book}
Naive use of a Metropolis Hastings step in a PCG sampler 
(when no closed form distribution is available)
often changes the stationary distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A field guide to forward-backward splitting with a Fasta implementation} \\
  T. Goldstein et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To minimize $f+g$, 
where $f$ is convex and smooth and $g$ convex,
the \d{proximal gradient} (forward-backward splitting, FBS) 
method is 
\begin{align*}
  \hat x _{k+1} &\leftarrow x_k - \tau \nabla f( x_k ) \\
  x_{k+1} &\leftarrow \operatorname{prox}_g( \hat x_{k+1}, \tau )
\end{align*}
where 
\[
\operatorname{prox}_g(x,\tau) = \argmin_z \tau g(z) + \tfrac12 \n{ x - z }^2.
\]
If $g$ is differentiable, 
the second step is a ``backward gradient step'',
$ x_{k+1} \leftarrow \hat x_{n+1} - \tau \nabla g( x_{k+1} ) $: 
the gradient is evaluated at the new point.

Applications include
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Constrained optimization 
  $\argmin_{ x \in C } f(x) $;
  the proximal operator of the indicator function of $C$ is the projection, 
  and the algorithm is the projected gradient;
\item Lasso: $\argmin\limits _{ \n{ x } _1 \leq \lambda } \tfrac12 \n{ Ax - b }^2 $;
\item  Penalized regression
\[ \argmin_x \tfrac12 \n{ Ax-b}^2 + \mu \n{x}_1; \]
the proximal operator is soft-thresholding,
\[ \operatorname{prox}_{\n{\cdot}_1} (x,\tau) = \operatorname{sign}(x) ( \va{x} - \tau )_+; \]
\item Democratic representation 
\[ \argmin_x \tfrac12 \n{ Ax-b }^2 + \mu \n{ x }_\infty; \]
\item Low rank approximation 
\[ \argmin_x \n{ X - Y} _F + \mu \n{X}_*; \]
the proximal operator of th nuclear norm is the soft-thresholding of the singular values;
\item Phase retrieval.
\end{compactitem}

Implementation details are important:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item To choose the step size, approximate $f$ as a quadratic function 
  $f(x) \approx \tfrac12 a \n{x}^2 + \langle x, b \rangle + x $, and set $\tau = 1/a$;
\item Fista acceletation: after the proximal step, continue to move the the same direction;
\item Backtracking line search: if the objective does not decrease (or if it increases more than some threshold, for non-monotone line search), half the step size;
\item Stop when the relative residual 
\[
  \dfrac{
    \n{ \nabla f + \nabla g }
  }{
    \epsilon + \max \{ \n{ \nabla f }, \n{ \nabla g } \}
  }
  \text{ or }
  \dfrac{
    \n{ \nabla f + \nabla g }    
  }{
    \epsilon + \n{ \nabla f(x_1) + \nabla g(x_1) }
  }
\]
is below some threshold.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Frank-Wolfe algorithm} \\
  % No author?
\end{book}
To minimize a convex function $f$ on a bounded \emph{polygonal} region: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start in $x_k$;
\item Linearize the objective $f$ at $x_k$;
\item Solve the resilting linear program: $c_k^*$;
\item The direction 
%$\overrightarrow{ x_k x_k^* }$ 
$\vv{ x_k x_k^* }$ % From the esvect package
is a descent direction: 
use a linear search to select the next point
\[ x_{k+1} = \argmin_{ x \in [ x_k, x_k^*] } f(x); \]
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{No free lunch theorems for optimization} \\
  D.H. Wolpert and W.G. Macready (1997)
\end{book}
Without any assumption on the function to be minimized, no algorithm can be guaranteed to be good.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Copositive programming} \\
  J. Matou\v sek and B. G\"artner
\end{book}
Copositive matrices form a convex cone 
\[ X = \{ M \text{ symmetric : } \forall x \geq 0 \ \ x' M x \geq 0 \}. \]
Its dual is the set of completely positive matrices
\[ P = \{ M \ :\ \exists x_i \geq 0 \ \ M = \sum x_i x_i' \}. \]
Copositive programming is NP-hard.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extending formulations\\ in mixed-integer convex programming} \\
  M. Lubin et al.
\end{book}
To solve mixed integer \emph{convex} programs,
prefer polyhedral (but still integral) relaxations to branch-and-bound non-linear programming (NLP): 
MILP solvers are fast.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unit tests for stochastic optimization} \\
  T. Schaul et al.
\end{book}
Build unit tests for stochastic optimization algorithms (variants of stochastic gradient descent):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with prototypes 
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.25] % Quadratic
\draw[ultra thick] (-1,1)--(-0.9,0.81)--(-0.8,0.64)--(-0.7,0.49)--(-0.6,0.36)--(-0.5,0.25)--(-0.4,0.16)--(-0.3,0.09)--(-0.2,0.04)--(-0.1,0.01)--(0,0)--(0.1,0.01)--(0.2,0.04)--(0.3,0.09)--(0.4,0.16)--(0.5,0.25)--(0.6,0.36)--(0.7,0.49)--(0.8,0.64)--(0.9,0.81)--(1,1);
\end{tikzpicture},
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.25]
\draw[ultra thick] (-1,1) -- (0,0) -- (1,1);
\draw[thick,densely dotted] (0,-.1) -- (0,1.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\!,
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.25]
\draw[ultra thick] (-1,1) -- (.5,0) -- (1,1);
\draw[thick,densely dotted] (.5,-.1) -- (.5,1.1);
\end{tikzpicture},
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.25,yscale=.3]
\draw[ultra thick] (-1,-.5) -- (-.5,0) -- (.5,0) -- (1,.5);
\end{tikzpicture},
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.25]
\draw[ultra thick] (-1,0.989)--(-0.9,0.974)--(-0.8,0.944)--(-0.7,0.89)--(-0.6,0.802)--(-0.5,0.675)--(-0.4,0.513)--(-0.3,0.333)--(-0.2,0.165)--(-0.1,0.044)--(0,0)--(0.1,0.044)--(0.2,0.165)--(0.3,0.333)--(0.4,0.513)--(0.5,0.675)--(0.6,0.802)--(0.7,0.89)--(0.8,0.944)--(0.9,0.974)--(1,0.989);
\end{tikzpicture} (Gaussian),
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.25]
\draw[ultra thick] (-1,0.95)--(-0.9,0.933)--(-0.8,0.909)--(-0.7,0.878)--(-0.6,0.835)--(-0.5,0.777)--(-0.4,0.699)--(-0.3,0.593)--(-0.2,0.451)--(-0.1,0.259)--(0,0)--(0.1,0.259)--(0.2,0.451)--(0.3,0.593)--(0.4,0.699)--(0.5,0.777)--(0.6,0.835)--(0.7,0.878)--(0.8,0.909)--(0.9,0.933)--(1,0.95);
\end{tikzpicture} (Laplace);
\item Concatenate them; 
\item Add noise to the gradient (additive Gaussian or Lapalce (for outliers), multiplicative, dropout);
\item Compose them into higher-dimensional signals, e.g., with 
  $(x,y) \mapsto ( x^p + y^p ) ^ { 1/p }$, 
  rotate;
\item Add some curl (in reinforcement learning (RL), those algorithms are fed non-gradients);
\item Let the function slowly change with time (also common in RL).
\end{compactitem}
Implementation in \texttt{lua-torch}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A tutorial on spectral clustering} \\
  U. von Luxburg (2007)
\end{book}

Given a cloud of points, one can build similarity graphs (prefer connected ones): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\epsilon$-neighbourhood graph (set $\epsilon$ to the length of the longest edge in the minimum spanning tree);
\item $k$-nearest neighbour graph, $k = \log n$;
\item Mutual $k$-nearest neighbour graph, $k > \log n$;
\item Fully-connected graph, 
with Gaussian similarity, 
\[ s( x_i, x_j) = \exp - \dfrac{ \n{ x_i - x_j }^2 }{ 2 \sigma^2 } \]
(set $\sigma$ to $\epsilon$, or to the mean distance to the $k$th nearest neighbour, $k=\log n$).
\end{compactitem}

Given a graph, with weight and degree matrices $W$ and $D$,
one can consider its Laplacians (there are three of them): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $L = D - W$;
\item $L_{\text{sym}} = D^{-1/2} L D^{-1/2}$;
\item $L_{\text{rw}} = D^{-1} L$.
\end{compactitem}

\d{Spectral clustering} is $k$-means (or any other clustering algorithm)
on the span of the first $k$ eigenvectors of $L$, 
$L_{\text{sym}}$ (requires normalization) or $L_{\text{rw}}$;
prefer $L_{\text{rw}}$.

The ratio and normalized cut problems, finding a graph partition that minimizes 
\begin{align*}
  \operatorname{RatioCut}(A_1,\dots,A_n) &= \dfrac12 \sum \dfrac{ W( A_i, \bar A_i ) } { \va{ A_i } } \\
  \text{or }
  \operatorname{NCut}(A_1,\dots,A_n) &= \dfrac12 \sum \dfrac{ W( A_i, \bar A_i ) } { \operatorname{vol} A_i  }  
\end{align*}
where $\operatorname{vol} A = \sum_{i \in A} d_i$, 
are NP-hard, 
but their convex relaxations are equivalent to spectral clustering 
(for a partition $V = A \sqcup \bar A$, 
consider the vector $f_i = \sqrt{ \va{ \bar A } / \va A }$ or 
$\sqrt{ \vphantom{\va {\bar A}} \operatorname{vol} \bar A / \operatorname{vol} A }$ 
if $i \in A$, and the inverse otherwise; 
notice that 
\begin{align*}
  f' L f &= \va{ V } \cdot \operatorname{RatioCut}( A, \bar A ) & \text{and } f &\perp \mathbf 1 \\
  \text{or }
  f' L f &= \operatorname{vol}( V ) \cdot \operatorname{RatioCut}( A, \bar A ) & \text{and } Df &\perp \mathbf 1.
\end{align*}
Both $L$ and $L_{\text{rw}}$ promote dissimilarity between clusters (good cuts),
but $L_{\text{rw}}$ also promotes similarity within clusters.

The transition matrix of a random walk on a graph is 
$P = D^{-1} W = I - L_{\text{rw}}$;
random walks are related to cuts: 
$\operatorname{NCut}(A, \bar A) = P( \bar A | A ) + P( A | \bar A)$.

The \emph{commute distance} $c_{ij}$ is the expected time for the random walk to go from $i$ to $j$ and back, 
\[ c_{ij} = \operatorname{vol}(V) ( e_i - e_j )' L^\dag (e_i - e_j ); \]
therefore $\sqrt{ c_{ij} } $ can be used as a Euclidian distance. 

The commute time embedding and the unnormalized spectral embedding (via $L$) are related but different; for connected graphs, they are very similar.

The graph Laplacian is close to that of a graph in which the communities are disconnected; 
therefore, the eigenvalues and eigenvectors are also close 
(Davis-Kahan theorem -- how close depends on the \emph{spectral gap}).
This is more useful for $L$, $L_{\text{rw}}$ than for $L_{\text{sym}}$, $S$ (similarity matrix) or $W$ (weights),
because the eigenvalues are ordered, bounded away from zero, and (contrary to $L_{\text{sym}}$, the eigenvectors are unscaled. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Laplacian spectrum of graphs} \\
  B. Mohar (1991)
\end{book}
The Laplacian of a graph is $Q = \diag(D) - A$, 
where $A$ is the adjacency matrix 
and $D = A \mathbf 1$ is its degree vector;
\[ \langle Qx , x \rangle = \sum_{ (u,v) \in E } a_{uv} ( x_u - x_v ) ^2. \]
Equivalently, $Q = CC'$, where $C$ is the oriented incident $\va V \times \va E$-matrix with entries
\[
  c_{ve} = 
  \begin{cases}
  +1 & \text{if } e = * \rightarrow v \\
  -1 & \text{if } e = v \rightarrow * \\
  0  & \text{otherwise.}
  \end{cases}
\]
On a lattice, $C$ is a discretization of the gradient, $Q$ of the Laplacian.

Bounds on the eigenvalues $0 = \lambda_1 \leq \lambda_2 \cdots \leq \lambda_n$
are known.
The second eigenvalue, 
\[ 
  \lambda_2 = \min_{\substack{x \perp \mathbf 1 \\ x \neq 0}} 
  \dfrac{
    \langle Qx , x \rangle 
  }{
    \langle x , x \rangle 
  }
\]
is a measure of connectivity.
It is related to (appears in inequalities involving) expander graphs,
diameter, number of edges in a maximal cut, isometric number, mean distance, independence number, genus, vertex connectivity, edge connectivity, etc.

The eigenvectors $y$ for $\lambda_2$ can be used for 1-dimensional dimension reduction
(heuristically, they reduce the length of the jumps $\va{i-j}$ for edges $i$--$j$);
$\{ v : y_v > c \}$ and
$\{ v : y_v < c \}$ are connected (if $\forall v \ y_v \neq c$).

The number of spanning trees in $G$ is the coefficient of $x$ in the characteristic polynomial of its Laplacian.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spectral clustering of graphs\\ with the Bethe Hessian} \\
  A. Saade et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The \emph{non-backtracking matrix} of a graph is the (symmetric) binary matrix,
indexed by edges, identifying patterns of the form
$i \rightarrow j \rightarrow k$ with $i \neq k$:
$B_{i\rightarrow j, \ell \rightarrow k} = \delta_{j\ell} ( 1 - \delta_{ik} ). $

The \emph{Bethe Hessian}, a smaller, symmetric matrix, is a deformation of the Laplacian, 
$ H(r) = (r^2-1) \mathbf1 - r A + D $.

The eigenvalues of $B$ are the roots of $\det H(r)$.
The eigenspaces of $B$, and $H(r)$, for $r = \rho(B)$ 
(the spectral radius of $B$ can be approximated as 
$\langle d^2 \rangle / \langle d \rangle - 1$;
the approximation can then be refined by looking for the closest root of $\det H(r)$)
can help separate communities.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An overview of low-rank matrix recovery\\ from incomplete observations} \\
  M.A. Davenport and J. Romberg (2016)
\end{book}

Low-rank matrix recovery can be approximated as follows.
\begin{align*}
&\operatorname{Minimize}\limits_X \n{ X - X_0 }_F^2 \text{ subject to } \rank X = r \text{ (SVD)}\\
&\operatorname{Minimize}\limits_X \n{ X - X_0 }_F^2 + \lambda \n{ X }_* \\
&\operatorname{Minimize}\limits_X \n{ AX - y }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ X }_* \\
&\operatorname{Minimize}\limits_{L,R} \n{ ALR' - y }_2^2 + \tfrac12\lambda \n{ L }_* + \tfrac12\lambda \n{ R }_* \\
&\operatorname{Minimize}\limits_X \n{ X }_* \text{ subject to } AX = y
\end{align*}
One can use \emph{iterative hard thresholding} (IHT, projected gradient:
move in the direction of $\nabla \n{ AX-y}_2^2$, 
use the SVD to reduce the rank to $r$, iterate)
or alternating projections
\begin{align*}
R &\leftarrow \argmin_R \n{ ALR' - y} _2^2 \\
L &\leftarrow \argmin_L \n{ ALR' - y} _2^2
\end{align*}
Instead of the nuclear norm $\n{ \cdot }_*$,
one can consider the max-norm,
or the log-determinant (it is not convex, 
but it is a smooth rank proxy for positive semi-definite matrices).

Solving systems of quadratic equations is equivalent to finding a rank-1 matrix
$X = v v'$ satisfying linear constraints $\langle X, A_i \rangle = b_i$
(\emph{lifting})
\[
  \operatorname{Minimize}\limits_X \n{ X }_* \text{ subject to } 
  \forall i \ \langle X, A_i \rangle = b_i.
\]
Applications include \emph{phase retrieval}:
recovering $v$ from the magnitudes
$\va{ \langle v, a_i \rangle } = y_i$,
\[
  \operatorname{Minimize}\limits_X \n{ X }_* \text{ subject to } 
  \forall i \ \langle X, a_i a_i' \rangle = y_i^2.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Compressive PCA\\ for low-rank matrices on graphs} \\
  N. Shahid et al. 
\end{book}
Given a matrix $Y \in \RR^{p \times n}$,
build the $k$-nearest graph on its columns (resp. rows),
with Gaussian weights, 
compute its Laplacian and its eigenvalue decomposition.
The matrix $Y$ is $(k_r,k_c)$-\emph{low rank on graphs}
if its columns (resp. rows)
are in the span of the first $k_c$ (resp. $k_r$)
eigenvectors.
(For big data, sample the rows and columns of $Y$.)
Low rank matrices on graphs capture non-linear low-rank structures.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pursuits in structured\\ non-convex matrix factorizations} \\
  R. Khanna et al.
\end{book}
At each step, greedily add the best rank-1 matrix for the linearized objective.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Riemannian pursuit for big matrix recovery} \\
  M. Tan et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The matrix lasso, for low rank matrix reconstruction
\[ X = \argmin_X \tfrac12 \n{ b - \mathscr A X }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ X }_* \]
does not scale well,
because of the high-dimensional SVD needed to compute the nuclear norm $\n X _*$.
Instead, one can use \emph{fixed rank} methods: 
rank $r$ matrices form a Riemannian manifold
\begin{align*}
  \mathscr M_n &= \{ X \in \RR^{n \times n} \ :\ \rank X = r \} \\
  &= \{ U \diag(\sigma) V' \,:\, U \in \text{St}_r^m,\, V \in \text{St}_r^n,\, \n{\sigma}_0 = r \} \\
  \text{St}_r^m &= \{ U \in \RR^{m\times r} \ :\ U' U = I \} \quad \text{(Stiefel manifold)}.
\end{align*}
The rank $r$ in unknown: progressively increase it.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convex banding of the covariance matrix} \\
  J. Bien et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Banded covariance matrices can be recovered (if the order on the variables is known)
with a \emph{hierarchical group lasso} penalty 
(sum of weighted unsquared $L^2$ norms --
the weights are needed to avoid overpenalizing overlapping regions).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{3D shape reconstruction from 2D landmarks: a convex formulation} \\
  X. Zhou et al. (2014)
\end{book}

To reconstruct a 3D shape from a set of 2D landmarks $W$,
assume that the shape is an unknown linear combination of simpler shapes
$W = \Pi( R \sum c_i B_i + T )$,
where $\Pi$ is the camera matrix, $R$ the rotation, $T$ a translation, and $B_i$ the simpler shapes.
This can be simplified to 
$W = \bar R \sum c_I B_i$, $\bar R \bar R ' = I$.

Since the convex hull of the Stiefel manifold
$\{ X \ : \ X' X = I \}$
is the unit (spectral norm) ball,
one can consider the convex relaxation 
\[ 
\argmin_{R_i} \n{ W - \sum R_i B_i }_F ^2 + \lambda \sum \n{ R_i }_2.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-scale structure and topological anomaly detection via a new network statistic:\\ the onion decomposition} \\
  L. H\'ebert-Dufresne et al.
\end{book}
To build the \emph{$k$-core decomposition} of a network: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Remove isolated nodes;
\item Remove nodes connected to 1 (later, $k$) fewer nodes;
  iterate (because this lowers the degree of the remaining nodes);
\item Increase $k$ and iterate.  
\end{compactitem}

The \emph{onion decomposition} keeps track of the nodes in each inner iteration.
The \emph{onion spectrum} is the number of nodes in each layer of the onion decomposition;
various micro/meso/macro characteristics are visible on it
(assortativity, loops/trees, core/periphery, centrality).

It is easy to generate random graphs with prescribed onion spectrum,
and they have the same characteristics (e.g., shortest path length distribution)
as real-world networks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical models for cores decomposition\\ of an undirected random graph} \\
  V. Karwa et al. 
\end{book}
The \emph{$k$-core} of a graph is the maximal subgraph in which every vertex has degree at least $k$.
The \emph{shell index} of a node indicates the highest core in which it is.
Erd\"os-R\'enyi random graphs specify the degree distribution,
but the degree is only a coarse measure of centrality:
instead, one can use the shell index distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A fast quartet tree heuristic\\ for hierarchical clustering} \\
  R. Cilibrasi and M.B. Vit\'anyi (2014)
\end{book}
In a tree, each subset of 4 nodes has one of 3 possible layouts (``quartet topologies''),
\[
\def\a#1#2#3#4{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  xscale=.2,yscale=.4,
  inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt
]
%\footnotesize
\node (A) at (-2,1)  {#1};
\node (B) at (-2,-1) {#2};
\node (C) at (2,1)   {#3};
\node (D) at (2,-1)  {#4};
\node (E) at (-1,0) {};
\node (F) at (1,0) {};
\draw (A) -- (-1,0) -- (B);
\draw (-1,0) -- (1,0);
\draw (C) -- (1,0) -- (D);
\end{tikzpicture}
}
\a1234 \qquad \a1324 \qquad \a142{3\rlap{.}}
\]
Randomized hill-climbing (C implementation in the CompLearn library)
can find the tree maximizing the number of consistent quartet topologies.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistically-consistent k-mer methods\\ for phylogenetic tree reconstruction} \\
  E.S. Allman et al.
\end{book}
Hierarchical clustering of sequences from $n$-gram Euclidean distance can be inconsistent:
prefer model-based distances.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable force-directed graph layout algorithms using fast multipole methods} \\
  E. Yunis et al.
\end{book}

Barnes-Hut is $O(n \log n )$ and considers particle-cluster interactions;
the fast multipole method (FMM) considers cluster-cluster interactions to reduce the computations
and is $O(n)$.
The \texttt{ExaFMM} implementation can also be used for graph layout.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient and high-quality\\ force-directed graph drawing} \\
  Y. Hu
\end{book}
Force-directed graph-drawing algorithms include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fruchterman-Reigold: edges are springs; there is a repulsive force for each pair of nodes;
\item Kamada-Kawai: springs between all pairs of vertices, with length proportional to the distance in the graph;
\item Walshaw: to avoid local minima, start with a coarse graph and progressively refine it;
only consider forces from neighbouring vertices;
\item Combine this multilevel approach with Barnes-Hut oct-trees and progressive cooling.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The hidden hyperbolic geometry\\ of international trade:\\ world trade atlas 1870-2013} \\
  G. Garc\'ia-P\'erez et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Plot the world trade map in the hyperbolic plane (Poincar\'e disk),
not the Euclidian one.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Kriging of financial term structures} \\
  A. Cousin et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Adding monotonicity constraints to a Gaussian process makes it non-Gaussian but, after discretization,
it remains tractable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Trading networks, abnormal motifs\\ and stock manipulation} \\
  Z.Q. Jiang et al. (2012)
\end{book}
The order book has the (encrypted) identity of the trader:
look for abnormal motifs such as 
\!\!\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.2,>=stealth]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A) at (0,0) {};
  \draw[->,thick] (A) to [out=135, in=45,looseness=15] (A);
\end{tikzpicture}\!\!
(self-buy),
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.5,yscale=.2,>=stealth]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node (B) at (1,0) {};
  \draw[->,thick] (A) to [out=45, in=135,looseness=1] (B);
  \draw[->,thick] (B) to [out=-135, in=-45,looseness=1] (A);
\end{tikzpicture}
(stock pool) or
\raisebox{-.4ex}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.5,yscale=.2,>=stealth]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node (B) at (1,0) {};
  \draw[->,thick] (A) -- (B);
  \draw[->,thick] (A) to [out=60, in=120,looseness=2] (B);
  \draw[->,thick] (A) to [out=-60, in=-120,looseness=2] (B);
\end{tikzpicture}}
(preferred counterparty).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring nonlinear dependence\\ in time series, a distance correlation approach} \\
  Z. Zhou (2014)
\end{book}
The \emph{distance correlation}
can be applied to time series
\begin{align*}
  V(X,Y) &\propto \iint \dfrac{f_X(x) f_Y(y)}{\va{x}^{p+1} \va{y}^{p+1}} dxdy \\
  V_k(X) &= V( X_n, X_{n+k} ) \\
  R_k(X) &= \sqrt{ \dfrac{ V_k(X) }{ V_0(X) } } 
\end{align*}
where $f_X$ is the characteristic function of $X$.

It can be estimated as 
\begin{align*}
  \hat V_k(X) &\propto \sum A_{r\ell} B_{r\ell} \\
  A_{r\ell} &= a_{r\ell} - a_{r \bullet} - a_{\bullet \ell} + a_{\bullet\bullet} \\
  a_{r\ell} &= \va{ x_r - x_l }.
\end{align*}

The distance correlation tests need to be adjusted for dependence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Clustering of time series subsequences\\ is meaningless:\\ implication for previous and future research} \\
  E. Keogh and J. Lin 
\end{book}
Before clustering 1-dimensional time series,
one may be tempted to embed them in a $d$-dimensional space
(using a sliding window): 
the resulting clusters do not depend on the dataset; they are just an artefact of the embedding
(the cluster centers are translation-invariant and turn out to be (approximately) sine waves).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The ladder: a reliable leaderboard\\ for machine learning competitions} \\
  M. Hardt and A. Blum (2015)
\end{book}

Compare the new loss with the best loss so far,
with a 1-sided paired $T$-test,
and only update the performance if it is significantly better 
(you need to remember the whole loss vector).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How to scale up kernel methods\\ to be as good as deep neural nets} \\
  Z. Lu et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Kernel-based algorithms, e.g., support vector machines (SVM),
tend to scale quadratically with the number of observations,
making them unsuitable for large datasets.

Translation-invariant continuous positive definite kernels, 
$k(x,y) = k(x-y)$,
transform the Dirac measure into the Fourier transform of a non-degenerate probability density function,
$k(\delta) = \mathscr F p$,
\ie,
\[ k(x-y) = \int p(\omega) e^{i \omega' (x-y)} d\omega = E_\omega [ e^{i \omega' x} e^{- i \omega' y} ]. \]
They can be approximated by random features
\begin{align*}
k(x-y) &\approx \dfrac1{D} \sum_{i=1}^D \phi_{\omega_i}(x) \phi_{\omega_i}(y)  \\
\phi_\omega(x) &= \cos( \omega' x + b_i ) \\
\omega &\sim N(0, \sigma^{-1} I ) &&\hspace*{-1.5cm}\text{for a rbf kernel} \\
\omega &\sim \text{Cauchy}(\sigma) &&\hspace*{-1.5cm}\text{for a Laplace kernel} \\
b_i &\sim U(0,2\pi)
\end{align*}

Computations can be parallelized: train a model on a subset of 25,000 random features;
combine the resulting models, e.g., by averaging the log-probabilities of the classes. 

Those kernels can be added
(multiple kernel learning, MKL: either concatenate the random features of all the models,
or fit the models separately and combine the forecasts with non-negative weights),
multiplied (just add the corresponding $\omega_i$'s)
and even sometimes composed -- the resulting kernel is then 
equivalent to a neural net, with one hidden layer and random weights
(echo state network, extreme learning machine, reservoir learning, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep residual learning for image recognition} \\
  K. He et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The skip-layer connections in a residual net
lead to additive (rather than multiplicative) gradient back-propagation,
thereby avoiding vanishing or exploding gradients.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=1.1,xscale=1.5]
\node (x) at (0,3) {$x$};
\node (y) at (0,-.8) {$\llap{$x$}+\rlap{$f(x)$}$};
\node[draw] (A) at (0,2) {$3 \times 3 \times 64$};
\node[draw] (B) at (0,1) {$3 \times 3 \times 64$};
\node[circle,inner sep = 1pt,draw] (C) at (0,0) {$+$};
\draw[thick,->] (x) -- (A);
\draw[thick,->] (A) -- (B);
\draw[thick,->] (A) -- node[auto,left=0pt,pos=.5] {\footnotesize relu} (B);
\draw[thick,->] (B) -- node[auto,left=0pt,pos=.5] {\footnotesize $f$} (C);
\draw[thick,->] (C) -- (y);
\draw[thick,->,rounded corners=3mm] (0,2.6) -- (1,2.6) -- (1,0) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
They are also easy to initialize: $f=0$ or $f$ small.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deconstructing the ladder network architecture} \\
  M. Pezeshki et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Ladder networks, for semi-supervised learning, 
combine a denoising auto-encoder (for the unsupervised part)
with a neural net (for the supervised part),
with the decoder and the neural net constrained (or penalized) to share weights.
\[
