\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GPfit: an R package\\ for fitting a Gaussian process model\\ to deterministic simulator outputs} \\
  B. MacDonald et al. (JSS, 2015)
\end{book}
% (duplicated summary)
%The \texttt{GPfit} package fits a (Gaussian kernel) Gaussian process to deterministic functions, 
%with a nugget to avoid numerical instability 
%(its size is computed from the condition number).
%Also check \texttt{mlegp}, \texttt{tgp}.

When fitting a Gaussian process on non-noisy data, with a Gaussian correlation function $R_{ij}=\cor(y_i,y_j) = \prod_k \exp - \theta_k \va{ x_{ik} - x_{jk} }^2$, $\theta_k>0$, the correlation matrix can be ill-conditioned (some of the points are too close), and the likelihood has local extrema very close to zero. One can replace $R$ with $R + \delta I$, with the smallest nugget $\delta$ that makes the matrix well-conditioned, and reparametrize $\theta$ as $\theta = \exp\phi$.

Check the R packages \texttt{GPfit} (no noise),
\texttt{tgp} (noise)
or \texttt{mlegp} (noise, numerically unstable).

Also mentions \texttt{lhs::maximinLHS} (random but well-dispersed points).

