\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The {CMA} evolution strategy: a tutorial} \\
  N. Hansen (2011)
\end{book}

CMA-ES is a population-based optimization algorithms
that models the current population as a Gaussian, 
samples from this model,
keeps the best candidates and iterates.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gaussian processes for machine learning}\\
  C.E. Rasmussen and C.K.I. Williams (2006)
\end{book}

A Gaussian process is an infinite family of random variables
$(X_t)_{t \in \RR}$;
the mean and covariance functions 
\[ m(t) = E[X_t], \qquad k(s,t) = \cov(X_s,X_t) \]
suffice to define it.
The joint distribution of
$X_{t_1},\dots,X_{t_n}$ is Gaussian,
and the conditional distribution 
$X_{s_1},\dots,X_{s_m} | X_{t_1},\dots,X_{t_n}$,
obtained in the usual way (Shur complement)
is the posterior distribution.

To train a Gaussian process, one can use a hierarchical prior, 
\ie, posit a parametrization of the mean and covariance functions,
e.g.,
\begin{align*}
  m(t) &= a t^2 + b t + c \\
  k(s,t) &= \sigma_1^2 \exp - \dfrac{ (s-t)^2 }{\ell^2} + \sigma_2^2 \delta_{ij}
\end{align*}
(the $\sigma_2^2 \delta_{ij}$ term accounts for noisy observations)
and select the hyperparameters via log-marginal likelihood.

