\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms for hyper-parameter optimization}\\
  J. Bergstra et al. (2011)
\end{book}

A \d{Parzen estimator} of a conditional probability distribution $p(x|y)$
is an estimator of the form 
\[ 
p(x \mid y ) = \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  \ell(x) & \text{if } y < y^* \\
  g(x) & \text{if } y \geq y^*
\end{array}
\right.
\]
where $\ell$ and $g$ are kernel estimators (possibly with a Gaussian, etc.\ prior)
and the threshold $y^*$ is some quantile
of the sample data $p(y < y^*) = \gamma$.

The expected improvement ($x=\text{parameter}$, $y=\text{loss}$)
is (an increasing function of) $\ell(x)/g(x)$:
to get a new candidate solution, 
one can sample many points from~$\ell$ and take that with the highest $\ell(x)/g(x)$.

The parameter space of hyperparameter optimization problems often has a tree structure:
the Parzen estimator can be generalized to a \emph{tree-structured Parzen estimator} (TPE).

Sequential search can be parallelized with the \d{constant liar} approach: 
once a candidate point has been chosen,
provisionally set its loss to the average loss.

Check the \texttt{hyperopt} Python package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sequential model-based optimization\\ for general algorithm configuration}\\
  F. Hutter et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Gaussian processes (GP) can be generalized to mixed continuous-categorical variables: 
\[ k(x,y) = \exp \sum_j -\lambda_j d( x_j, y_j )^2, \]
where $d$ is the Euclidean or Hamming distance (note that the coordinates are independent).

Sequential model-based optimization (SMBO, aka Bayesian optimization) 
can use \emph{random forests} (of regression trees) instead of GPs.

Depending on the problem, you may want to transform the objective function 
(e.g., replacing the algorithm running time with its logarithm).

Instead of estimating the performance of an algorithm as a sample mean
\[ \operatorname*{Mean}\limits_{i \in \text{Instances}} \text{Performance}(i,\theta) \]
on a small set of instances, one can learn the mapping 
\( (\text{instance}, \theta ) \mapsto \text{performance} \),
using instance features, and use it to estimate 
\( E[ \text{Performance}( \cdot, \theta ) ] \).
(You also need a distribution on instance features. 
SATzilla uses a similar idea to choose the most promising algorithm (DPLL, LP, etc.) for a given instance.)

To maximize the expected improvement (EI), 
and produce a diverse set of configurations with high EI, use multi-start local search.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Preliminary evaluation\\ of hyperopt algorithms on HPOLib}\\
  J. Bergstra et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Testing the hyperopt algorithms on the HPOLib benchmarking suite 
suggests that vanilla Gaussian processes 
(with RBF, rather than Mat\'ern kernels) are good enough.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Making a science of model search}\\
  J. Bergstra et al. (2013)
\end{book}
TPE is slightly better than random search.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time-bounded\\ sequential parameter optimization}\\
  F. Hutter et al. (2010)
\end{book}  
  
A few improvements on SPO: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Instead of fitting a \emph{noise-free} Gaussian process to smoothed data (if there are several, contradictory measurements for the same parameter values, you need to somehow reconcile/smooth them), include noise in the model;
\item (When minimizing running time) stop the computations early if they take too long:
  a \emph{censored} running time is still informative;
\item Use an approximate Gaussian process, the \emph{projected process} (PP) approximation:
  instead of considering (inverting) the whole $n\times n$ kernel matrix, 
  select (without replacement) $p$ instances and make do with the $n\times p$ and $p\times p$ submatrices.
\end{compactitem}

