\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{F-Race and iterated F-Race: an overview} \\
  M. Birattari et al. (2009)
\end{book}
To minimize an expensive function, 
e.g., some expected cost, estimated by Monte Carlo simulations:
take a set of candidates (e.g., a grid);
evaluate them in parallel;
regularly test if the (more and more precise) estimated values are significantly different
(e.g., with a non-parametric ANOVA test);
if so, discard the worst candidate; 
iterate until only one candidate remains.

This can be combined with CMA-ES: 
when the number of candidates has been sufficiently reduced,
replace them with new, nearby candidates.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ParamILS: an automatic\\ algorithm configuration framework} \\
  F. Hutter et al.
\end{book}

Iterated local search (ILS)
builds a sequence of local minima: 
start with a point;
use local search to find a nearly local minimum; 
add some noise to escape its bassin of attraction; 
iterate, keeping the new minimum if it is better (other acceptance criteria are possible).

If the objective function is estimated from a Monte Carlo simulation, 
there is no need to always use the same number of iterations.

If the objective function is the running time of some algorithm,
censored values (``more than $x$'', \ie, we stopped after $x$ seconds) are sometimes good enough.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GGA: a gender-based genetic algorithm\\ for the automatic configuration of algorithms} \\
  K. Tierney 
\end{book}
Gender-based genetic algorithms
keep two subpopulations (genders),
using different fitness functions.
For instance, if the standard fitness is time-consuming to compute, one could use it for one gender 
and use the other as a ``variety store''.

% Could this be used for multi-objective optimization?

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{R in Finance 2016} \\
\end{book}

The \texttt{ForecastCombinations} package provides various ways of combining predictions:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Equal-weighted average;
\item Precision-weighted average;
\item Regression;
\item Regression with $L^1$ loss;
\item Constrained regression: $\sum \beta_i = 1$, $\beta_i \geq 0$;
\item Average of the best 10\% subset regressions;
\item BIC-weighted average of all subset regressions.
\end{compactitem}

Stan, a C++-like language to describe Bayesian models, in the spirit of BUGS, 
is not limited to hierarchical models, but can also be used to model time series: 
stochastic volatility, mixtures, hidden Markov models, hierarchical stochastic volatility.
% https://github.com/michaelweylandt/R-Finance-2016-Tutorial-Materials/blob/master/RFin2016.pdf
% http://tharte.github.io/mbt/mbt.html

Deep learning libraries are no longer limited to Python and Lua: MxNet (a C++ library) can be used from R.
% http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/QiangKou.pdf

There is now an R package for automatic differentiation (AD): \texttt{madness}.
% There are many ways, for a computer, to estimate the derivatives (or gradient) of a function:
% - Numeric differentiation, by looking at f(x+h)-f(x), with h small;
% - Numeric differentiation with complex numbers: f(x+ih)-f(x), with i²=-1 and h very small;
% - Dual numbers: f(x+ε)-f(x), with ε²=0;
% - Symbolic differentiation.
% Automatic differentiation is another method: it takes compiled computer code (rather than a formula), possibly with loops and conditionals, keeps track of all the floating point operations, and figures out how they affect the gradient, using the chain rule (the same idea underlies back-propagation in neural nets).
% It is often used in deep learning and for maximum likelihood estimation of complex models. 
%   http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/StevenPav.pdf

%As usual, there were a few talks about Rcpp.
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/workshop/DirkEddelbuettel.pdf
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/MattDziubinski.pdf

Several talks dealt with portfolio optimization:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Multi-objective portfolio construction, with NSGA2 (\texttt{mco::nsga2}; 
risk parity, \texttt{cccp::rp}, was also mentioned)
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/BernhardPfaff.pdf
\item The \texttt{ROML} package provides portfolio optimization functions, a bit like a limited version of disciplined convex optimization, with predefined functions for many objectives. 
[R finally has a package for disciplined convex optimization: \texttt{cvxr}.]
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/LauraVana.pdf
%  https://github.com/anqif/cvxr
\item Portfolio optimization with PortfolioAnalytics and random portfolios
%  http://rossb34.github.io/PortfolioAnalyticsPresentation2016/
\end{compactitem}

Most talks were about finance:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Do not use the ADF test to identify trading opportunities: 
it highlights mean reversion (Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process), while you want oscillations.
  Try a model of the form
\[ x_{t+1} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \times \text{level}_t + \beta_2 \times \text{slope}_t + \text{noise}_{t+1}; \]
  filter for $\beta_1 < 0$, $\beta_2 > 0$; keep the best fits. 
  One could also try a Hilbert transform.
  % This is very similar to our state-space-based momentum (we had level, slope and acceleration).
  % http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/PaulTeetor.pdf
\item SVM for stock selection (on the S\&P100): comparison of 15 different kernels 
  [one could also combine them -- this is called multiple kernel learning (MKL)].
  %http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/PedroAlexander.ppt
\item One can measure the connectedness of the bank network by fitting a VAR model 
  (use an adaptive lasso penalty to deal with the large number of banks, 
  and an OHLC-based volatility estimator) and computing the $H$-step ahead contribution of bank $i$ to bank $j$.
  %http://www.ssc.upenn.edu/~fdiebold/papers2/DDLYpaper.pdf
\item Leveraged ETFs give less return that you may think:
  \[  f(x) = \text{log1p}\bigl( k \times \text{expm1}(x) \bigr) \leq k \times x. \]
  This becomes more visible when volatility is high, and with compounding:
  \[ \sum f(x_t) \leq k \sum x_t . \]
  % http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/MatthewGinley.pdf
\item Building an index from GoogleTrends and a list of search terms
  % http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/SileLi.pdf
\end{compactitem}  

A few talks covered data management issues, e.g., 
\texttt{h5}, to read and write hdf5 files 
(a portable file format to store arrays of numbers, data.frames, etc.), 
\texttt{feather} (to store columnar data and read it from R or Python) 
or \texttt{ff} to manipulate datasets larger than memory.
R can be used in other environments, such as Postgres or Hadoop.
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/PatrickHowerter.pdf
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/BryanLewis.html
%  https://github.com/bwlewis/feathercache
%  http://slides.com/hafen/rinfinance2016
%  http://tessera.io/

A few talks focused on efficient or extensible computations.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \texttt{Arborist} package for random forests also provides quantile regression 
  (i.e., the leaves do not only contain the mean, but quantiles) 
  and monotonicity constraints (reject proposals that breach the constraints with probability $p$ -- not necessarily 1).
  % http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/MarkSeligman.pdf
\item The \texttt{roll} package provides more rolling functions 
  (\texttt{mean}, \texttt{var}, \texttt{sd}, \texttt{cor}, \texttt{cov}, \texttt{lm}, \texttt{eigen}, \texttt{pcr}, \texttt{vif});
  the computations use \texttt{Rcpp} and are parallelized.
  % http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/JasonFoster.pdf
\item The \text{pirls} package re-implementats glmnet; 
  it is slower but allows arbitrary link functions.
  % http://slides.com/michaelkane/deck-13
\end{compactitem}

Many talks showcased Shiny, interactive (Javascript) plots and the use of R to teach finance or options.
%  http://github.com/FinancialMarkets/5MinuteFinance
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/RobertMcDonald.pdf
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/KjellKonis.pdf
%  https://backtestgraphics.shinyapps.io/slides/#8
%  http://hafen.github.io/rbokeh/

There were a few talks about options and stochastic processes: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The illiquidity of a market can be estimated by comparing the price of an ETF and the NAV of the underlying portfolio; 
  this can be interpreted as a bid-ask spread by noticing that bid and ask prices are American option prices.
  % http://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=1782295
\item Computation of the returns of the dual moving average (MACD) strategy, under a Brownian motion assumption
  % http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/DougService.pdf
\item Convertible bonds, divergence swaps, stochastic local volatility model, etc.
  % https://github.com/brianboonstra/ragtop
  % http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/PiotrOrlowski.pdf
  % http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/KlauSpanderen.pdf
\end{compactitem}

A few talks were only remotely related to finance.
For instance, the \emph{rearrangement problem} is the problem of 
finding the intra-column rearrangement of a matrix that minimizes row sum variability 
(variance, maximum, minus minimum).
Here are a few heuristics:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pick a column at random and re-order it so that it be in the order opposite to the sum of the columns;
\item Idem with a subset of columns versus the others;
\item This subset of columns can be chosen so that its sum has as similar a variance as the other columns.
\end{compactitem}
%  http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2016/talk/KrisBoudt.pdf

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Why should I trust you?\\ Explaining the predictions of any classifier} \\
  M.T. Ribeiro et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To interpret the forecasts of a complex (black box) model,
approximate it, locally (as in local regression)
with simpler models:
sparse linear model (e.g., for a small number of binary features),
decision trees,
falling rule lists, etc.
The explanation is \emph{local}: 
each observation gets a different explanation.
% "For observations like that, the algorithm looks at this and that..."
To explain the whole model, 
provide a diverse, representative set of observations and explanations;
it is a (weighted) \emph{set cover problem}: 
given an instance$\times$feature binary (or weight) matrix, 
select a small number of rows that cover as many columns as possible 
(a greedy algorithm is good enough).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Falling rule lists} \\
  F. Wang and C. Rudin (2014)
\end{book}
A \emph{falling rule list} is a set of rules of the form 
``if patient satisfies condition 1, then risk = $x$\% else\dots'',
where the risk is decreasing.
From a set of candidate conditions $B$ (frequent item set mining: FP-growth, Eclat, Apriori),
one can build a Bayesian model (greedily selected decision rules do not perform well).
\begin{align*}
  B &: \text{set of candidate rules} \\
  \lambda &: \text{expected number of rules} \\
  w_j &: \text{prior probability of $j \in B$} \\
  \Gamma(\alpha_j,\beta_j) &: \text{prior for the risk of rule $j$} \\
  \Gamma(\alpha_\infty,\beta_\infty) &: \text{prior for the risk of the default rule} \\
  L &\sim \text{Pois}(\lambda) \quad \text{number of rules} \\
  c_1\dots,c_L&: \text{drawn from $B$, without replacement,}\\
  &\hphantom{{}:{}} \text{with probabilities proportional to $w_j$} \\
  \gamma_j &\sim \Gamma( \alpha_j, \beta_j ) \mathbf{1}_{\geq1} \\
  \gamma_\infty &\sim \Gamma( \alpha_\infty, \beta_\infty ) \\
  \text{risk}_j &= \sum\limits_{i\geq j} \log \gamma_i
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Interpretable classifiers using rules\\ and Bayesian analysis:\\ building a better stroke prediction model} \\
  B. Letham et al. (2015)
\end{book}
\emph{Bayesian rule lists} generalize falling rule lists to multinomial outcomes.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable Bayesian rule lists} \\
  H. Yang et al. (2016)
\end{book}
% Implementation details

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The alternating decision tree\\ learning algorithm} \\
  Y. Freund and L. Mason (1999)
\end{book}
An \d{alternating decision tree}
alternates between prediction nodes and splitting modes;
the value of a leaf is the sum of the predictions on its path;
the value of a sample is the sum of the values of the paths leading to it.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1,yscale=1,>=stealth,
  prediction/.style={fill=blue!20,inner sep=3pt},
  splitting/.style={ellipse,fill=red!30,inner sep=1pt, minimum width=5.5ex}
]
\node[prediction] (A) {$+.5$};
\node[below of= A] (A0) {};
\node[splitting, left = 0pt of A0] (B) {$a<4$};
\node[splitting, right = 0pt of A0] (C) {$b>0$};
\node[prediction, below of=B] (E) {$+.2$};
\node[prediction, below of=C] (F) {$+.3$};
\node[prediction, left = 1em of E] (D) {$-.7$};
\node[prediction, right = 1em of F] (G) {$-.6$};
\node[below of=D] (D0) {};
\node[splitting, left = 0pt of D0] (H) {$b>1$};
\node[splitting, right = 0pt of D0] (I) {$a>1$};
\node[prediction, below of=H] (K) {$-.2$};
\node[prediction, below of=I] (L) {$-.1$};
\node[prediction, left = 1em of K] (J) {$+.4$};
\node[prediction, right = 1em of L] (M) {$+.1$};
\draw[->] (A)--(B);
\draw[->] (A)--(C);
\draw[->] (B)--(D);
\draw[->] (B)--(E);
\draw[->] (C)--(F);
\draw[->] (C)--(G);
\draw[->] (D)--(H);
\draw[->] (D)--(I);
\draw[->] (H)--(J);
\draw[->] (H)--(K);
\draw[->] (I)--(L);
\draw[->] (I)--(M);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
They can be learnt by boosting;
their performance is similar to boosted decision trees (C5.0),
but they are easier to interpret.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimizing the induction\\ of alternating decision trees} \\
  B. Pfahringer et al.
\end{book}
Alternating decision trees are not scalable: 
heuristics are needed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ensemble samples with affine invariance} \\
  J. Goodman and J. Weare (2010)
\end{book}
The affine-invariant Nelder-Mead optimization algorithm
motivates an affine-invariant \emph{ensemble} of MCMC samplers,
using a ``stretch move'' (adjust the acceptance probability accordingly)
\[
  x ^{\text{candidate}} _k = x_j + Z ( x_k - x_j ), \qquad j \neq k \text{ random} 
\]
\[
  Z \sim p, \qquad p(z) \propto \dfrac1{\sqrt z} \mathbf1_{[1/a,a]}, \qquad a=2
\]
Similarly, CMAES suggests a ``walk move'',
\[ 
  x ^{\text{candidate}} _k \sim N( \mu_S, \Sigma_S ) 
\]
where $S$ is a random subset of particles, $\va{S} \geq 2$, 
$k \not\in S$, 
and $\mu_S$ and $\Sigma_S$ are their mean and variance.

This is implemented in the \texttt{emcee} Python package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Integration in finite terms} \\
  M. Rosenlicht (1972)
\end{book}
Elementary proof of Liouville's 1834 theorem,
which gives a criterion for an indefinite integral to be expressible in closed form.
For instance, 
$\int e^{-x^2} dx$ has no closed form 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An invitation to integration in finite terms} \\
  E.A. Marchisotto et G.A. Zakeri (1994)
\end{book}
More elementary presentation of Liouville's theorem.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Smooth numbers:\\ computational number theory and beyond} \\
  A. Granville (2008)
\end{book}
Smooth numbers are numbers whose prime factors are small.
A number if \emph{$y$-smooth} 
if its prime factors are at most $y$.
Let $\psi(x,y)$ be the number of $y$-smooth integers up to $x$.
Then 
\[
  \dfrac{ \psi( x, x^{1/u} ) }{ x } 
  \underset{x\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}
  \rho(u) > 0 
\]
where 
\begin{align*}
  \rho(u) &= 1 && u \in [0,1] \\
  \rho(u) &= 1 - \log u && 1 \leq u \leq 2 \\
  \rho(u) &= \dfrac1u \int_{u-1}^u \rho(t) dt && u > 1.
\end{align*}
They play a role in cryptography and computational number theory.
% Not read

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalizing dynamic time warping\\ to the multidimensional case\\ requires an adaptive approach} \\
  M. Shokoohi-Yekta et al.
\end{book}
Dependent and independent multidimensional time warp (DTW) give different results.
Which is best may be problem-, class- or exemplar-specific, and can be learned.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Clustering time series\\ using unsupervised shapelets} \\
  J. Zakaria et al.
\end{book}
(After normalizing the time series)
apply clustering algorithms to the matrix of distances between 
the time series and ``shapelets'' (shorter time series);
a small number of discriminative shapelets suffices.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Foster-Hart optimal portfolios} \\
  A. Anand et al.
\end{book}
For a bounded random variable $X$ such that $E[X] > 0$
and $P[X<0]>0$,
the Foster-Hart risk 
is the positive number $r$ such that 
$ E \log ( 1 + X/r ) = 0 $.
It is not coherent, not convex, not time-consistent, not guaranteed to exist.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Option-implied equity premium predictions\\ via entropic tilting} \\
  K. Metaxoglou et al. (2016)
\end{book}
\d{Entropy tilting} 
transforms a probability distribution $\pi$ to change some of its moments $g$.
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \pi^* \\
  \text{To minimize} & \text{KL}( \pi^* \| \pi ) = \displaystyle\int \pi^* \log \dfrac{ \pi^* }{ \pi } \\
  \text{Such that} & \displaystyle\int g(x) \pi^*(x) dx = \bar g
\end{array}
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using semantic fingerprinting in finance} \\
  F. Ibriyamova et al.
\end{book}
Rather than cosine similarity between bags of words,
use vector embeddings (here, Numenta's ``semantic fingerprint'')
to identify similar companies.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pure quintile portfolios} \\
  D. Liu
\end{book}
Let $r$ be the vector of stock returns (at a given date),
$X$ the matrix of (normalized) factor exposures (stock$\times$factor);
the \emph{factor returns} are $(X' X)^{-1} X' r$; 
the \emph{factor mimicking portfolios} are the rows of 
$(X' X)^{-1} X'$.
The \emph{factor quintile portfolios} 
are the minimum variance portfolios subject to the constraints
\begin{compactitem}
\item[(i)] $n/5$ stocks, long-only;
\item[(ii)] The exposure to the factor of interest is the same as that of the quintile portfolio;
\item[(iii)] The exposure to the other factors is zero.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling and forecasting\\ (un)reliable realized covariances\\ for more reliable financial decisions} \\
  T. Bollerslev et al. (2016)
\end{book}
The realized covariance is the sum of a (time-dependent but persistent)
unobserved variance,
and a noise term (market microstructure, Epps effect, non-synchronous observations).
Estimating the distribution of this noise term leads to a better (exponentially-weighted) covariance estimator.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Equivalence\\ of robust VaR and CVaR optimization} \\
  S. Lotfi and S.A. Zenios (2016)  
\end{book}
Robust optimization 
\[
  \operatorname*{Minimize}_\alpha \max_{\beta \in B} \text{Loss}(\alpha,\beta)
\]
optimizes the worst case situation, 
when the values of some parameters are constrained to remain in some box or ellipsoid.
Robust CVaR and robust VaR optimization turn out to be equivalent.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to ROC analysis} \\
  T. Fawcett (2005)
\end{book}
Precision-recall curves are affected by class skew; ROC curves are not.

Given a set of classifiers, look at the convex hull of their ROC curves;
given two classifiers $A$ and $B$, the random mixtures $pA + (1-p)B$ form the $[AB]$ segment.

The AUC can be interpreted as the probability, given two observations from the two classes,
of correctly distinguishing them.

To estimate the ``precision'' of a ROC curve, use bootstrapped curves and look at the variation of \textsc{tp} given \textsc{fp},
or $(\textsc{fp},\textsc{tp})$ given the score.

For multiclass problems, use 
$\sum p(c_i) \text{AUC}(c_i)$
or
\[
\dfrac{2}{ \va C ( \va C - 1 ) } \sum_{i < j } \text{AUC}(c_i,c_j)
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-linear dimensionality reduction:\\ Riemannian metric estimation\\ and the problem of geometric recovery} \\
  D. Perrault-Joncas and M. Meil\u a
\end{book}
By inverting the Laplace-Beltrami operator, 
one can add a Riemannian metric to manifold algorithms, showing how distances, angles and areas are distorted.
[One could also try to minimize those distortions.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian model choice and information criteria in sparse generalized linear models} \\
  R. Foygel and M. Drton
\end{book}
The \d{extended BIC} uses a prior favouring sparser models.
\begin{align*}
  P(J) &\propto \binom{p}{\va J} ^{\!\!-\gamma} \mathbf{ \va J \leq q } \qquad J \subset \llbracket 1, p \rrbracket \\
  \text{BIC}_\gamma(J) &= - 2 \operatorname{loglik}( \hat \theta _J ) + \va J \log n + 2 \gamma \va J \log p
\end{align*}
% Technical. Consistency results

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extended Bayesian information criteria\\ for model selection with large model spaces} \\
  J. Chen and Z. Chen
\end{book}
Original paper on the eBIC.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A new method for constructing\\ networks from binary data} \\
  C.D. van Borkulo et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The structure of a Gaussian Markov random field (MRF, \ie, an undirected graphical model)
can be estimated by a graphical lasso
(estimate the inverse of the covariance matrix, 
whose nonzero entries correspond to conditional independencies)
and approximated by lasso regressions, one for each variable.
This approximation can be generalized to binary MRF (Ising model)
and lasso logistic regressions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{High-dimensional graphical model selection using $\ell_1$-regularized logistic regression} \\
  M.J. Wainwright et al.
\end{book}
% Idem

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Confidence bounds of recurrence-based complexity measures} \\
  S. Schinkel et al. (2009)
\end{book}
Recurrence plots highlight periodic or chaotic behaviour in time series.
\[
R_{ij} = 
\begin{cases}
  1 & \text{if } \n{ x_i - x_j } \leq \epsilon \\
  0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
\d{Recurrence quantification analysis} (RQA)
turns those plots into numbers (features):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Proportion of dark points (recurrence rate);
\item Proportion of points in diagonal segments of length at least $\ell_{\text{min}}$ (determinism);
\item Length of the longest diagonal segment;
\item Average length of the diagonal segments;
\item Similar quantities for vertical segments (laminarity, trapping time).
\end{compactitem}
To compute confidence intervals on those quantities, resample, with replacement,
the diagonal (resp. vertical) segments (including those of length one).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The PP-TSVD algorithm\\ for image restoration problems} \\
  P.C. Hansen et al.
\end{book}
Besides the penalized 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & z \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ Az -b }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ z }_2
\end{array}
\]
and constrained problems
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & z \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ Az -b }_2^2 \\
  \text{Such that} & \n{ z }_2 \leq c
\end{array}
\]
one can consider the truncated one
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & z \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ z }_2 \\
  \text{Such that} & \n{ Az -b }_2^2 \leq c.
\end{array}
\]
As usual, one can replace $\n z _2$
with $\n z _1$, 
$\n{\smash{ \nabla^2 z }}_2$,
$\n{ \nabla z }_2$,
$\n{ \nabla z }_1$,
etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The evolution of popular music:\\ USA 1960-2010} \\
  M. Mauch et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To study a musical corpus: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Extract timbre features (12 Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCC),
  $\Delta\text{MFCC} = \text{MFCC}_0(t) - \text{MFCC}_0(t-1)$,
  zero-crossing count)
  using some Vamp plugin;
  extract the 12 chroma features from NNLS Chroma, for each frame;
\item Apply random sampling (20 frames), PCA, model-based clustering (Gaussian mixture, 35 clusters)
  to build the timbre lexicon;
\item Use the common chord transitions as a harmony lexicon;
\item Extract topics with latent Dirichlet allocation (LDA, with the \texttt{topicmodels} package).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An end-to-end neural network\\ for polyphonic music transcription} \\
  S. Sigtia et al.
\end{book}
Automatic music transcription (AMT) can benefit from the same tools as speech recognition:
language models, hidden Markov models, recurrent neural nets, convolutional nets.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Exploiting spillovers to forecast crashes} \\
  F. Gresnigt et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Add cross-excitations to Hawkes processes, to model contagion.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{fastFM: a library for factorization machines} \\
  I. Bayer
\end{book}
Factorization machines
\[ y = \alpha + \sum \beta_i x_i + \sum_{i<j} \langle \gamma_i, \gamma_j \rangle x_i x_j \]
are not limited to recommendation systems.

Implementations include fastFM, libFM, libffm, GraphLab.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A neural algorithm of artistic style} \\
  L.A. Gatys et al.
\end{book}
Compute the correlations between different features in different layers of the CNN
and try to match them to those of a ``target style'' image.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Compressing neural networks\\ with the hashing trick} \\
  W. Chen et al.
\end{book}
Use Vowpal Wabbit's hashing trick for weight sharing in (large) neural nets.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalized communities in networks} \\
  M.E.J. Newman and T.P. Peixoto
\end{book}
In the stochastic block model, 
each node belongs to a community,
and the link probability only depends on the endpoints' communities.
It can be generalized: 
each node is assigned a random number, and the link probability only depends on those numbers. 
\begin{align*}
x_u & \sim U(0,1) \\ 
p_{uv} &= \omega( x_u, u_v )
\end{align*}
To account for non-Poisson degree distributions,
consider instead 
\[ p_{uv} = \dfrac{ d_u d_v }{ 2 \times \text{\#\,edges} } \omega( x_u, x_v ). \]
The model can be efficiently estimated
(EM with belief propagation, 
expansion of $\omega$ in the basis of Bernstein polynomials -- they are nonnegative over $[0,1]$)
and uncovers both community structures and spatial structures.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gradient-free Hamiltonian Monte Carlo\\ with efficient kernel exponential families} \\
  H. Strathmann et al.
\end{book}
Metropolis-Hastings MCMC samples from a probability distribution $\pi$,
known only up to a multiplicative factor.
\begin{align*}
  x^* & \sim Q( \cdot | x_t ) \text{ proposal distribution} \\
  x_{t+1} &\leftarrow x^* \text{ with probability }
  \min\left\{
    1, 
    \dfrac{ \pi(x^*) }{ \pi(x_t) } \dfrac{ Q(x_t|x^*) }{ Q(x^*|x_t) }
  \right\} \\
  x_{t+1} &\leftarrow x_t \text{ otherwise}
\end{align*}
The proposal distribution is usually Gaussian,
with covariance
\[
  \Sigma_t \propto I = E[ ( \nabla \log \pi )^2 ] = - E[ \nabla^2 \log \pi ].
\]
If $\nabla \log \pi$ is intractable,
the information matrix can be estimated from the history of the Markov chain,
with a kernel embedding if there are nonlinear effects (kernel adaptive MH, KAMH).
These ideas can be generalized to Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A Riemannian framework\\ for tensor computing} \\
  X. Pennec et al. (2005)
\end{book}
Many algorithms from statistics or numerical analysis 
can be generalized to the manifold of positive definite matrices $S_+$
(``tensors''), endowed with the Riemannian structure 
(Fisher information metric) induced by 
$\exp: \text{Sym} = T_{\text{Id}} S_+ \to S_+$,
invariant by 
\[
\left\{
  \begin{aligned}
  \text{GL} \times S_+ & \longrightarrow  S_+ \\
  (X,A) & \longmapsto  X A X'.
  \end{aligned}
\right.
\]
For instance: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Mean: $\sum_i \log_{\bar x} x_i = 0$;
%  (the mean $\bar x$ is unknown, but you get it after a few iterations)
\item Gradient descent: 
 $ x_{t+1} \leftarrow \exp_{ x_t} [ - \epsilon \nabla C(x_t) ] $;
\item Linear interpolation (geodesic):
\[ x(t) = \exp_{x_1}[ t \, \overrightarrow{x_1 x_2} ] = \exp_{x_1}[ t \log_{x_1} \! x_2 ] \]
\end{compactitem}
where 
\begin{align*}
  \exp_x(y) &= x^{1/2} \exp( x^{-1/2} \, y \, x^{-1/2} ) \, x^{1/2} \\
  \log_x(y) &= x^{1/2} \log( x^{-1/2} \, y \, x^{-1/2} ) \, x^{1/2}.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Laplace-Beltrami eigenvalues\\ and topological features of eigenfunctions\\ for statistical shape analysis} \\
  M. Reuter et al. (2009)
\end{book}
Use the spectrum (``shape DNA'')
of the Laplace operator,
and its eigenfunctions (level sets, critical points)
as features for shape classification or comparison.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Geometric understanding of point clouds\\ using Laplace-Beltrami operator} \\
  J. Liang et al.
\end{book}
The Laplace operator can be approximated, from a cloud of points, using local least squares.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the origin of burstiness in human behavior: the Wikipedia edits case} \\
  Y. Gandica et al. (2016)
\end{book}

For inter-event times, compute the burstiness
\[ B = \dfrac{ \sigma - \mu }{ \sigma + \mu } \in [-1, 1] \]
(periodic if $\sigma \ll \mu$, bursty if $\sigma \gg \mu$)
and the autocorrelation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What is a paraproduct?} \\
  A. B\'enyi et al. (2010)
\end{book}
A generalization of the operator 
\[ \Pi_0(f,g) (s) = \int_{-\infty}^s f' g . \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Surprising patterns for the call duration distribution of mobile phone users} \\
  P.O.S. Vaz de Melo et al.
\end{book}
The call duration distribution has heavier tail and head than the log-normal,
but can be modeled as a log-logistic,
\[
  P[ X \leq x ] = \dfrac1{ 1 + e^{-z} },
  \qquad
  z = \dfrac{ \log x - \mu }{ \sigma }.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast moment-based estimation\\ for hierarchical models} \\
  P.O. Perry (2014)
\end{book}
Moment methods can fit large hierarchical models (e.g., a recommender system 
with 5 predictors, 1000 groups, 100,000 observations).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Differential geometric least angle regression:\\ a differential geometric approach\\ to sparse generalized linear models} \\
  L. Augugliaro and A.M. Mineo (2010)
\end{book}
The GLM $L^1$ regularization path is not piecewise linear but, 
on the statistical manifold of an exponential family, the least angle regression algorithm 
(keep the angle between the ``score'' of the active variables and the residual tangent vector constant) 
yields a sparse regularization path.
This is implemented in the \texttt{dglars} package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convolutional neural networks\\ for visual recognition}\\
  A. Karpathy and J. Johnson (2016)
\end{book}

Simple classifiers such as $k$-NN, softmax (multinomial logistic) or multiclass SVM (all with regularization)
do not perform well on image classification tasks (MNIST, Cifar-10, ImageNet),
though image features (colour histogram, bag of visual words, HOG, SIFT, GIST, LBP, Texton, SSIM, etc.) help:
prefer deep convolutional nets, with ReLU units. 

To address the vanishing/exploding gradient problem,
initialize the weights so that the activations be approximately standard Gaussian:
for instance, Xavier initialization suggests
  $\texttt{rand()}/\sqrt{\text{fan-in}}$, for linear (or tanh) units
and 
  $\texttt{rand()}/\sqrt{\text{fan-in}/2}$ for ReLU.
Alternatively, insert \emph{batch normalization layers} to rescale the activations --
it is a differentiable operation, therefore amenable to back-propagation.
The rescaling is mini-batch-specific: the jittering effect is similar to that of dropout.
To use the network, after training, use the (fixed) $\mu$ and $\sigma$ from the whole training set.

To debug your network:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The initial loss should be $\log(\#\text{classes})$ without regularization, more with
\item The unregularized network can overfit a small dataset
\item Plot loss and test accuracy versus epoch and weight update;
  $\va{\text{weight update}} / \text{weight magnitude}$ should be around $10^{-3}$
\item Use random hyperparameter search; worry if the best ones are on the boundary.
\end{compactitem}

Among the many optimization algorithms,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Stochastic gradient descent is slow
  \[  x \mathrel{{+}{=}} -\alpha  dx \]
\item Momentum tends to overshoot
\begin{align*}
    v &= \mu v - \alpha dx \\
    x &\mathrel{{+}{=}} v
\end{align*}
\item Nesterov momentum (aka Nesterov accelerated gradient, NAG)
  evaluates the gradient after the momentum step
\item With Adagrad, the step sizes tend to zero too fast
\begin{align*}
    N &\mathrel{{+}{=}} (dx)^2 \\
    x &\mathrel{{+}{=}} - \alpha  dx / \sqrt{N}
\end{align*}
\item RMSprop is similar to Adagrad, but uses an exponential moving average of the second moment
  instead of a sum;
\end{compactitem}
prefer Adam, which combines momentum and RMSprop, \ie, 
the first and second moments of the gradients.

Ensembling helps: even if you have a single model, you
can simply average some of its snapshots, taken during training, or
take an exponential moving average of its weights over
training.

Dropout suggests to drop half the neurons at random, at train time,
and shrink the activations at test time
Instead, one could drop connections (DropConnect).

Zero-padding is necessary, otherwise the image shrinks at each step,
which forbids deep nets.
The $1\times1$ filters make sense -- they are actually $1\times1\times\text{depth}$.

Pooling layers are often inserted after some of the convolutional layers: 
they downsample the image, usually with max pooling (average does not work as well).

Conv nets usually end with a fully connected layer, e.g., a softmax.

Historical convnets include LeNet, AlexNet, ZFNet, VGGNet and, more recently,
GoogLeNet (no FC layers),
ResNet (152 layers, with skip-layer connections) -- 
pretrained models are available in the 
Caffe model zoo.

Pre-trained convnets can tackle many computer vision tasks 
by replacing the ``classification head'' with another one: localization, detection, etc.

To understand what a convnet does:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pick a neuron, see what images excite it;
\item Only the first-layer weights are interpretable:
  they are always Gabor-like features;
\item The output of the FC7 layer (last before the classifier) gives a ``code'' for the image:
  visualize the cloud of images with t-SNE.
\item Occlude a part of the image and check how the probability changes, as a function of the region occluded,
  to see where the object is;
%\item Deep visualization toolbox https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AgkfIQ4IGaM http://yosinski.com/deepvis 
\item Deconv computes the gradient of a specific neuron wrt all the
  pixels to see what it sees;
  in guided backprop: ReLU units let the gradient pass if 
  gradient, or both input and gradient are positive (instead of input only) -- this gives
  nicer interpretations of the neurons;
\item Choose a neuron and build an image that maximizes its output; regularize with an $L^2$ penalty
  or blur the image after each step to avoid accumulation of high-frequency features.
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Deep dream} starts with an image and tries to amplify the features in a given layer
  (forward pass until the target layer, set the gradient to the activations, backprop the gradient back to the image, update the image, iterate).
\emph{NeuralStyle} (deep art) looks for an image close to the contents of one image (measured by the activations of a layer)
and the style of another (measured by the Gram (covariance) matrix of one or several layers).

With \emph{recurrent networks},
do not backprop through the whole sequence, but only 25 characters at a time;
to avoid exploding gradients, cut them off.
The LSTM or GRU (a slight simplification) equations look complicated:
changing them at random does not affect performance
% LSTM, a search space odyssey).

CNNs do not necessarily need a lot of data:
take a pre-trained net and freeze all but the last few layers -- 
it becomes a feature extractor and you can forget that the features come from a neural net.

To reduce the number of parameters and increase expressivity, 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Replace large convolutions ($5\times5$, $7\times7$) with stacks of $3\times3$ convolutions;
\item Apply a $1\times1$ convolution to halve the number of filters, then a $3\times3$ convolution, 
  then a $1\times1$ to restore the number of filters (bottleneck convolution);
\item Replace a $3\times3$ convolution with a $1\times3$ followed by a $3\times1$.
\end{compactitem}

To increase the training set (data augmentation), use
  horizontal flips,
  crops (train the network on crops; use crops at test time and average several crops),
  or ajitter the colour.

Convolutions can be expressed as matrix multiplications and sped up by linear algebra libraries
(to the price of some data duplication);
FFT only helps for large filters.

Double precision is not needed: single or half is enough.

Semantic segmentation is the task of labeling each pixel (``cow'', ``tree'', ``person'', etc.);
instance segmentation also distinguishes different instances (first cow, second cow, etc.)
Semantic segmentation networks usually end with an upsampling operation,
which can be learnt (fractionally-strided convolution, often improperly called ``deconvolution'').

For image segmentation, generate a set of proposals (regions of interest, ROI),
and refine them (grey out pixels far away and predict which of the remaining pixels are in the region).

For video, detect feature points at different scales; track
them using optical flow; extract features (HOG, HOF, MBH) in the
corresponding stabilized coordinates.
Spatio-temporal convnets use 3-dimensional convolutions,
but single-frame CNNs (on just one frame -- discard the others) perform well, and video does not add much.

\d{Variational auto-encoders} add a Bayesian flavour to auto-encoders:
  start with data, $x$; 
  the encoder generates $\mu_z$, $\sigma_z$;
  sample $z \sim N(\mu_z, \Sigma_z)$;
  the decoder generates $\mu_x$, $\sigma_x$;
  sample $x \sim N(\mu_x, \Sigma_x)$;
the loss function combines the 
  reconstruction loss on $x$ 
and a 
  penalty to ensure that $N(\mu_z,\Sigma_z)$ is close to the prior on $z$.

\d{Generative adversarial networks} train two networks together: 
  the generator turns random noise into a candidate image;
  the discriminator is given an image and must tell whether it comes from the data or was generated.
They work well on simple datasets (MNIST, faces);
a multiscale variant also works on more complex ones (CIFAR).

\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep learning for natural language processing}\\
  R. Socher (Stanford, 2016)
\end{book}

The current trend in natural language processing is to 
somehow replace words and phrases with
vectors, and apply deep learning tools.

For instance, one could take the co-occurrence matrix (from Wikipedia or CommonCrawl), 
look at the previous and next $k$ words ($k = 5$ to $10$ -- you can also give more weight to closer words),
and reduce the dimension to 25 to 1000, e.g., with SVD.
Polysemy, non-symmetry, common words and new words pose problems.

Instead, \d{Word2vec} tries to predict the previous and next $k$ words
\[ P( \text{output} \mid \text{input} ) \propto \exp( u_{\text{output}} ' v_{\text{input}} ^{\vphantom{\prime}} ). \]
Each word is represented by two vectors, $u_w$ and $v_w$ 
(concatenate or average them if you want a single one),
maximizing the likelihood of the observed data.
One can add \emph{negative sampling}: maximize 
\[ \sigma (u ' v) - \sum_k \sigma( -u_k ' v ) \]
where the $u_k$ are random words,
sampled with probability $ P(w) \propto U(w)^{3/4}$, where $U$ is the unigram distribution.

The \emph{continuous bag of word} (CBOW) model predicts the center word from the surrounding words.

\d{GloVe} uses the (sparse) co-occurrence matrix $p$ and minimizes
\[ \sum f(p_{ij})( u_i ' v_j - \log p_{ij} )^2, \] 
where $f(x) = \min\{x,100\}$
(to avoid giving too much weight to very common words).
% Works well on small corpora.

One can predict the sentiment of a word
using its context (the vector embedding of $w_{t-2}$, $w_{t-1}$, $w_t$, $w_{t+1}$, $w_{t+2}$),
with a max-margin (hinge) loss, bidirectional 
\d{recurrent neural network} (RNN) with LSTM units, clipped gradients, 

Use $F_1$, the harmonic mean of precision and recall, 
if the classes are unbalanced.

\d{Recursive neural networks} provide embeddings for phrases:
their input is the vector embedding of two words (or nodes)
and the output is the vector embedding (same dimension) and the score
if we merge the two nodes;
it can be as simple as one tanh layer,
or a different network for each tree depth or phrase type (NP, VP, etc.);
it can parse sentences greedily.
A PCFG can help reduce the number of candidate trees to examine.

ConvNets are similar to recursive neural nets, but merge all possible trees.
Instead of using several layers, use only one, for 2-grams (and 3-gram, 4-grams 5-grams),
and add a max-pooling layer, \ie, take the maximum of those $n$-gram vectors.

%% 2016-07
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%5

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DiceKriging, DiceOptim: two R packages for the analysis of computer experiments by Kriging-based metamodeling and optimization} \\
  O. Roustant et al. (JSS, 2012)
\end{book}
\d{Kriging} is another name for Gaussian process modeling 
(the differences lie in the way the trend is modeled: 
constant, linear, more complex, 
known, to be estimated, to be integrated out)
but it is often limited to 2 or 3 dimensions 
and Gaussian kernels,
which is unsuitable to Bayesian optimization
(``computer experiment'' is just another name for blackbox (expensive) function).

\texttt{DiceKriging::km} fits a GP model, 
\texttt{DiceOptim::max\_EI} returns the next candidate according to the expected improvement (EI) criterion.
These functions can be used with 
\texttt{lhs::optimumLHS} for Latin square designs
and
\texttt{sensitivity::fast99} for \d{sensitivity analysis}.

% Sensitivity analysis
Given random variables 
$X_1,\dots,X_n$,
any ($L^2$) function $f : \RR^n \to \RR$
can be decomposed as 
\begin{align*}
  Y &= f(X_1,\dots,X_n) \\
  &= \mu_0 + 
     \sum_i \mu_i(X_i) + 
     \sum_{i<j} \mu_{ij}(X_i,X_j) + \cdots \\
  &= \sum_{ I \subset \llbracket 1 , n \rrbracket } \mu_I( X_I )
\end{align*}
with $E[ \mu_I(X_I) | X_J ] = 0$ for $J \varsubsetneq I$,
which gives a variance decomposition 
$\var Y = \sum_I \var \mu_I(X_I).$
The \emph{Sobol indices} are 
$S_I = \var \mu_I(X_I) / \var Y$;
$S_i$ and $\sum_{I \ni i} S_I$
are of special interest.

Also check: \texttt{BACCO}, \texttt{mlegp}, \texttt{tgp}.

%% Stats, hts
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal combination forecasts\\ for hierarchical time series} \\
  R.J. Hyndman et al. (2010)
\end{book}
For an additive hierarchy of time seties
\[
  y_t = \sum_i y_{it}, \qquad
  y_{it} = \sum_j y_{ijt}, \qquad
  y_{ijt} = \sum_k y_{ijkt},
\]
independent time series forecasts need not be consistent.
They are traditionally reconciled, either in a top-down or a bottom-up fashion.
Let $Y_t$ be the vector of all the forecasts 
and $Y_{tK}$ be that of the low-level ones: 
$Y_t = S Y_{tK}$ (where $S$ is a binary matrix). % stacked block-diagonal matrices
Existing reconciliation methods are of the form 
$\tilde Y = S P \hat Y$.
The minimum variance reconciled estimator
solves the optimization problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & P \\
  \text{To minimize} & S P ( \var \hat Y ) P' S' \\
  \text{Such that} & SPS = S.
\end{array}
\]
Equivalently, one can consider the regression 
$\hat Y = S \beta + \epsilon$, 
where $\beta$ are the (unknown, consistent) low-level forecasts.

%% TS
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The great time series bake off: an experimental evaluation of recently proposed algorithms} \\
  A. Bagnall et al.
\end{book}
Comparison of time series classification algorithms: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item 1-NN (nearest neighbour) DTW (dynamic time warp) and weighted variants of the DTW distance, e.g., replacing 
$(x_i-x_j)^2$ with $w(\va{i-j})(x_i-x_j)^2$;
\item 1-NN for distances using both the series and their first differences;
\item Shapelets, \ie, localized patterns (subsequences) used in a decision tree;
\item Random-forest based on simple statistics (mean, standard deviation) on subintervals;
\item Ensemble methods, in the time, power spectrum, autocorrelation, shapelet, etc. space.
\end{compactitem}
Ensemble methods (and NN DTW) work best.

%% Stat: AIC
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding predictive information criteria for Bayesian models} \\
  A. Gelman et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The AIC and its variants (deviance information criterion, Watanabe-Akaike information criterion)
estimate the out-of-sample performance 
\begin{align*}
  \text{AIC} &= - 2 \log P( \text{date} | \hat\theta_{\text{MLE}} ) + 2 k \\
  \text{DIC} &= - 2 \log P( \text{date} | \hat\theta_{\text{Bayes}} ) + 2 p_{\text{Bayes}} \\
  \text{WAIC} &= 2 \log P_{\text{post}}( \text{date} ) + 2 p_{\text{WAIC}}
\end{align*}
where   
\begin{align*}
  \hat \theta_{\text{Bayes}}   &= E_{\text{post}}[ \theta ] \\
  p_{\text{Bayes}} &=  2 ( \log P(\text{data} | \hat \theta_{\text{Bayes}} ) - 
  E_{\text{post}} [ P( \text{data} ) ] ) \\
  \text{or }  p_{\text{Bayes}} &=  2 \var_{\text{post}}[ \log P(\text{data} | \theta ) ] \\
  \log P_{\text{post}}(\text{data}) &= \sum_i E_{\text{post}}[ p(y_i) ] \\
  p_{\text{WAIC}} &= 2 \sum_i \log E_{\text{post}} p(y_i) - E_{\text{post}} \log p(y_i) \\
  \text{or } p_{\text{WAIC}} &= \sum_i \var _{\text{post}} [ \log p(y_i) ]
\end{align*}
and $E_{\text{post}}[\cdot] = E_{\text{post}}[\cdot | \theta]$, $\var_{\text{post}}$ are computed via simulations.
(The BIC, $- 2 \log P(\text{data} | \hat \theta_{\text{MLE}}) + k \log n$,
a more conservative measure,
was designed to assess model quality, not forecast quality.)

The effective number of parameters in the DIC and the WAIC 
recognize that hierarchical models have fewer effective than actual parameters.

%% Stats L^1
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Why $\ell^1$ is a good approximation to $\ell^0$:\\ a geometric explanation} \\
  C. Ramirez et al. (2012)
\end{book}
The $\ell^1$ norm is the ``best'' convex relaxation of the $\ell^0$ pseudo-norm
(or, rather, $\ell^\epsilon$, with $\epsilon>0$ small):
look at natural, exchangeable, archimedian partial orders on $\RR^n$;
they are determined by $B_\preccurlyeq = \{ x \,:\, x \preccurlyeq (1,0,\cdots,0)\}$;
notice that the $\ell^1$ ball $[ \n{x }_1 \leq 1 ]$ is the convex hull of the $\ell^0$ ball $[\n{x}_0\leq1]$.

%% Stats, dim reduction
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Local ordinal embedding} \\
  Y. Terada and U. von Luxburg (2014)
\end{book}
Local ordinal embedding (LOE) recovers the coordinates of a cloud of points from a set of constraints 
of the form $d_{ij} \leq d_{k\ell}$, $(i,j,k,\ell)\in\mathscr A$,
by minimizing (by majorization)
\[ \sum_{\mathscr A} ( \delta + d_{ij} - d_{k\ell} )_+ ^2. \]

Local information is sufficient to recover the data. 
Contrary to other embeddings (t-SNE, etc.),
LOE also recovers the density of the data.

%% Stat, DML, triplets
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards making high-dimensional distance metric learning practical} \\
  Q. Qian et al.
\end{book}
Distance metric learning (DML) looks for 
a metric for which 
points in the same class are close and 
points in different classes are far away.
For high-dimensional problems, one often uses a low-rank parametrization 
$M = L L'$ or dimension reduction (PCA, random projection)
preliminary step. 
One could look for a positive demi-definite matrix $M$ satisfying (most of the) triplet constraints: 
for $x \sim y$ and $x \not\sim z$,
\[ (x-y)' M (x-y) + 1 \leq (x - z )' M ( x-z ) \]
\ie, $d(x,y) + 1 \leq d(x,z)$.
To find $M \in S_d$ that minimizes 
\[
\lambda \n{ M }_F ^ 2 + 
\dfrac1N \!
\sum_{\substack{x,y,z\\ x \sim y\\ x \not\sim z}}
\text{loss}\bigl( M, (x-z)(x-z)' - (x-y)(x-y)'\bigr),
\]
solve the dual;
to reduce the dimension, use a random projection (on the dual);
to ensure positive semi-definiteness, project onto the positive semidefinite code only once, at the end.

%% NN, triplets
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FaceNet: a unified embedding\\ for face recognition and clustering} \\
  F. Schroff et al.
\end{book}
To recognize faces regardless of orientation and lighting,
train your neural net to learn an embedding where 
$\n{x_1 - x_2}^2 + \alpha \leq \n{ x_1 - x_3} ^ 2$
for all triplets $x_1 \simeq x_2$, $x_1 \not\simeq x_3$;
progressively increase the difficulty of the triplets
by selecting those with the largest loss in the current minibatch [not unlike boosting].

%% Recurrent NN (good)
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A critical review of recurrent neural networks for sequence learning} \\
  Z.C. Lipton (2015)
\end{book}
Current neural networks can be trained with: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Newton: $ h = - f''(x)^{-1} f'(x) $;
\item Approximate Newton: approximate the Hessian, e.g., by assuming it is diagonal, or by updating it every $k$ steps;
\item Truncated Newton (Hessian-free): approximate the inverse $f''(x)^{-1}$, e.g. by running conjugate gradient a (very) small number of steps (this does not require the Hessian, just the map $h \mapsto f''(x) \cdot h$);
\item Saddle-free: do not rescale the gradient by $1/\lambda_i$ but $1/\va{\lambda_i}$
\end{compactitem}
and variants of stochastic gradient descent: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Momentum: $\text{step}_t \propto \text{gradient} + \alpha \cdot \text{step}_{t-1}$
\item Nesterov momentum: idem, but the gradient is taken ``half a step'' later, \ie, after the momentum step;
\item Adagrad: $\text{step}_t \propto \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{ \sum\limits_{k \geq 0} \va{ 
  \dfrac{ \partial \text{loss}_{t-k} }{ \partial w }
  }^2 } }$
\item RMSProp: $\text{step}_t \propto \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{ \sum \alpha^k \va{ 
  \dfrac{ \partial \text{loss}_{t-k} }{ \partial w }
  }^2 } }$
\item AdaDelta: $\text{step}_t \propto \dfrac{\sqrt{
  \sum \alpha^k \n{ \text{step}_{t-k} }^2 
  }}{\sqrt{ \sum \alpha^k \va{ 
  \dfrac{ \partial \text{loss}_{t-k} }{ \partial w }
  }^2 } }$
\item Adam: RMSprop + momentum.
\end{compactitem}
ReLU (restricted linear units) enforce sparsity in the hidden layers.

Recurrent structures are usually of the form: 
% Recurrent network
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  >=stealth,xscale=.7,yscale=.7,
  baseline=6mm,
  neuron/.style={circle,draw=blue!50!black,fill=blue!20,thick,inner sep=3pt},
]
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}
  \node[neuron] (i-\m) at (\m-1,0) {};
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}
  \node[neuron] (h-\m) at (\m-1,1) {};
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}
  \node[neuron] (o-\m) at (\m-1,2) {};
\node at (7.8,0) {$\cdots$};  
\node at (7.8,1) {$\cdots$};  
\node at (7.8,2) {$\cdots$};  
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}
  \draw[->] (i-\m) -- (h-\m);
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}
  \draw[->] (h-\m) -- (o-\m);
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7} {
  \pgfmathtruncatemacro{\n}{\m + 1}
  \draw[->] (h-\m) -- (h-\n);
}
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}
  \draw[->] (\m-1,-.5) -- (i-\m);
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}
  \draw[->] (o-\m) -- (\m-1,2.5);
%\node at (3.5,-1) {\footnotesize input sequence or time series};
%\node at (3.5,3) {\footnotesize output sequence or time series};
\node at (3.5,-1) {\footnotesize input sequence};
\node at (3.5,3) {\footnotesize output sequence};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Bidirectional RNN (BRNN) are of the form: 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,
  neuron/.style={circle,draw=blue!50!black,fill=blue!20,thick,inner sep=3pt}
]
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5} {
  \node[neuron] (i-\m) at (\m,0) {};
  \node[neuron] (h1-\m) at (\m+.25,.75) {};
  \node[neuron] (h2-\m) at (\m-.25,1.25) {};
  \node[neuron] (o-\m) at (\m,2) {};
}
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5} {
  \draw[->] (i-\m) -- (h1-\m);
  \draw[->] (i-\m) -- (h2-\m);
  \draw[->] (h1-\m) -- (o-\m);
  \draw[->] (h2-\m) -- (o-\m);
}
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5} {
  \draw[->] (\m,-.4) -- (i-\m);
  \draw[->] (o-\m) -- (\m,2.4);
}
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4} {
  \pgfmathtruncatemacro{\n}{\m + 1}
  \draw[->,ultra thick] (h1-\m) -- (h1-\n);
  \draw[->,ultra thick] (h2-\n) -- (h2-\m);
}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

LSTM (long short term memory) units 
introduce two changes in the hidden layer, 
to allow for long memory and avoid vanishing or exploding gradients: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Self-connected (recurrent) cell with weight 1 (``constant error carousel'');
\item Multiplicative nodes, to set or delete the ``memory'' cell.
\end{compactitem}

%% NN
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Draw: a recurrent neural network\\ for image generation} \\
  K. Gregor et al.
\end{book}
Recurrent autoencoders can progressively draw images.

%% NN
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to transduce with unbounded memory} \\
  E. Grefenstette et al.
\end{book}
One can devise differentiable analogues of computer elements 
(e.g., memory cells: LSTM)
and data structures (stack, deque, etc.).
For instance, a \emph{differentiable stack}
is a stack of weighted elements, with weights in $(0,1)$;
the weights decrease when we pop an ``element''
(but nothing is actually removed);
reading the ``element on top'' returns a weighted sum of the stack elements, 
with the weights summing to 1, 
and the higher elements hiding the lower ones as much as possible.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Highway networks} \\
  R.K. Srivastava et al. 
\end{book}
Use LSTM-like units (gates) in non-recurrent networks to make them deeper but still trainable by SGD:
replace $x_{i+1} = H(x_i, w_i)$
with $\lambda = T(x_i,w_i)$, $x_{n+1} = (1-\lambda) x_i + \lambda H(x_i,w_i)$.

%% Stats, RF
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extremely randomized trees} \\
  P. Geurts et al. (2006)
\end{book}
The splits in the trees in a random forest usually come from the data -- 
they can actually be random.
[The result can be seen as a smooth and robustified $k$-NN classifier.]

%% RF
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep neural decision forests} \\
  P. Kontschieder et al.
\end{book}
Decision trees (and therefore random forests) are amenable to backpropagation: 
replace the deterministic decision at each node with a stochastic one (a Bernoulli random variable)
and define the output of the tree as the expected leaf value. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Inverse graphics\\ with probabilistic CAD models} \\
  T.D. Kulkarni et al.
\end{book}
Computer vision is the inverse of computer graphics.
It can be approached  as a Bayesian inference problem
with, as prior, a (random) 3D scene, with lathed objects (using Gaussian processes for their shapes) 
and human shapes (deformed along their armature), affinely transformed.
% What about a simpler prior: boxes, spheres, cylinders, cones, to mimick the way artists draw?

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep convolultional inverse graphics network} \\
  T.D. Kulkarni et al.
\end{book}
Computer vision can be seen as the inverse of computer graphics 
and tackled with an auto-encoder, de-rendering an image into a scene description, and then re-rendering it.
The middle layer can be made interpretable by using mini-batches identical except for one aspect (lighting, pose, shape, etc.)
and only updating the nodes we want to reflect that aspect.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Path-SGD: path-normalized optimization\\ in deep neural networks} \\
  B. Neyshabur et al.
\end{book}
Networks with ReLU units are scale-invariant:
multiplying the weights of one layer by $\lambda$ and dividing those of the next layer by $\lambda$ leaves the output unchanged. 
Use scale-invariant optimization algorithms.

%% NN
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Weight uncertainty in neural networks} \\
  C. Blundell et al. (2015)
\end{book}
\d{Bayesian neural networks} (neural nets whose weights are not numbers but probability distributions)
can be seen as a regularization scheme (like early stopping or dropout);
in reinforcement learning, 
they also allow Thompson sampling.
A diagonal Gaussian Bayesian neural net (this only doubles the number of parameters)
can be trained by back-propagation.

%% NN
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evolving neural networks\\ through augmenting topologies} \\
  K.O. Stanley and R. Miikkulainen (2002)
\end{book}
Genetic algorithms to choose the structure of a neural net.

%% NN
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MADE:\\ masked autoencoder for distribution estimation} \\
  M. germain et al. (2015)
\end{book}
An autoencoder can be made autoregressive, \ie, one can ensure that there is no path between input $i$ and output $j$ if $i \geq j$, by using binary mask: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Connect each hidden node to the first $k$ input nodes,
  where $k$ is sampled randomly;
\item Connect the $\ell$th output node to a hidden node if $\ell > k$.
\end{compactitem}
This can be generalized to deeper networks.

%% TDA
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Topological methods\\ for the analysis of high dimensional data sets\\ and 3D object recognition} \\
  G. Singh et al. (2007)
\end{book}
Start with a covering $X = \bigcup_{\alpha \in A} U_\alpha$;
cluster the points in each component,
$U_\alpha = \bigsqcup_{\beta \in B_\alpha} V_{\alpha\beta}$;
consider the nerve of the resulting covering $X = \bigcup V_{\alpha\beta}$;
let the initial covering vary (e.g., balls of radius $\epsilon$)
to have a multiresolution approximation of the dataset.

%% Manifold learning
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Megaman:\\ manifold learning with millions of points} \\
  J. McQueen et al. (2016)
\end{book}
\texttt{megaman} is an alternative to Scikit-learn's \texttt{manifold} submodule,
focusing on performance ans scalability (sparse matrices, \texttt{pyamg}, FLANN,
\texttt{arpack}, LOBPCG, etc.): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Spectral embedding (Laplacian eigenmaps -- fastest);
\item Local tangent space adjustment (LTSA)
\item Local linear embedding (LLE)
\item Isomap
\item Diffusion maps
\end{compactitem}
The output also includes an estimate of the distorsion.

All those algorithms approximate the manifold with a graph
($k$-nearest neighnours or, better, $\epsilon$-radius graphs)
and compute its adjacency and/or Laplacian matrix.

%% ML, tensors, manifolds
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Riemannian preconditioning\\ for tensor completion} \\
  H. Kasai and B. Mishra
\end{book}
A Tucker decomposition of an order 3 tensor $X \in \RR^{n_1 \times n_2 \times n_3}$
is 
\(
  \def\mytimesA#1{\underset{\smash{\raisebox{3pt}[0pt][0pt]{\ensuremath{\scriptscriptstyle #1}}}}{\times}}
  \def\mytimes#1{\mathbin{\smash{\mytimesA{#1}}}}
  X = G \mytimes1 U_1 \mytimes2 U_2 \mytimes3 U_3
\),
where $G \in \RR^{r_1 \times r_2 \times r_3}$ anb $U_i \in \RR^{n_i \times r_i}$
has orthogonal columns.
The multilinear rank $(r_1, r_2, r_3)$ of $X$ is the rank of its unfolding matrices
(consider $X$ as a cube, cut it into 2-dimensional slices, stack them, and take the rank).
Tucker decompositions form a (Stiefel) manifold $\mathscr M$,
but they are usually only considered up to rotations: the \emph{Tucker manifold} 
is $\mathscr M / O(r_1) \times O(r_2) \times O(r_3)$.

It can be endowed with a Riemannian metric
induced by the Hessian of the loss function of the optimization problem considered, 
e.g., reconstruction from a small number of entries,
under a rank constraint, minimizing the Frobenius norm of the entries available.
This is computationally expensive:
one can make do with an approximation of the Hessian, assuming that all the entries are available,
and only keeping its diagonal blocks.
The usual Riemannian optimization framework then applies. 

%% Tensors
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast and guaranteed tensor decomposition\\ via sketching} \\
  Y. Wang et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Tensor operations can be sped up by replacing the tensor with a random projection.

%% SVM
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ensemble of exemplar-SVMs\\ for object detection and beyond} \\
  T. Malisiewicz
\end{book}
Transfer metadata from the closest examplar to the observation being classified.

%% tSNE
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Visualizing large scale\\ and high-dimensional data} \\
  J. Tang et al. (2016)
\end{book}
LargeVis is inspired by t-SNE but scales better: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute an approximate $k$-nearest neighbourhood graph (t-SNE uses an exact one)
  with a couple of \emph{random projection trees}
  (pick two points, split the space along their perpendicular bisector hyperplane, iterate)
  and neighbour exploring (neighbours of neighbours are neighbour candidates) -- 
  k-D trees do not work well in high dimensions;
\item Use the same weights 
  $p_{j|i} \propto \exp( - \n{ x_i - x_j }^2 / 2 \sigma_i^2)$
  as t-SNE and symmetrize,
\item For up to $10^6$ points, one can use graph layout algorithms: ForceAtlas2 and Openord are $O(n \log n)$;
\item Consider a probabilistic model relating the distance between the points in the low-dimensional projection $d_{ij}$ and the graph weights,
\begin{align*}
  \text{log lik} &= 
  \!\!\! \sum_{\text{$i$--$j$ edge}} \!\!\! w_{ij} \log f(d_{ij}) 
  - \gamma \sum_{\quad\clap{\scriptsize $i$--$j$ not edge}\quad} \log\bigl( 1 - f(d_{ij}) \bigr) \\
  f(x) &= \dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \text{ or } \dfrac1{1 + e^x}
\end{align*}
\item Replace the $O(n^2)$ negative edges with a smaller number of random edges;
\item Optimize with asynchronous stochastic gradient descent.
\end{compactitem}

%% Optim
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unconstrained optimization\\ of real functions in complex variables} \\
  L. Sorber et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Traditional optimization algorithms are not directly applicable to real-valued complex functions 
$\CC^n \to \RR$
because they are not (complex) differentiable.
Instead of separating the real and complex parts, which would hide some of the structure of the problem,
one can use \emph{Wirtinger calculus}, \ie, the complex differentials 
\[ 
\dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial z } = \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial x } - i \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial y },
\qquad
\dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial \bar z } = \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial x } + i \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial y }.
\]
Gradient- or Hessian-based algorithms (L-BFGS, CG, Gauss-Newton, Levenberg-Marquardt) then apply.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Recursive decomposition\\ for nonconvex optimization} \\
  A.L. Friesen and P. Domingos
\end{book}
\emph{Fully decomposable functions}, 
$f(x) = \sum g_i(x_i)$, are easy to minimize, but rare.
A function is \emph{globally decomposable}
if it can be written 
$f(x) = g_1(x_0,x_1) + g_2(x_0,x_2)$,
where $x = (x_0, x_1, x_2)$ is a partition of the coordinates.
A function $f$ is \emph{locally decomposable} of that partition depends on $x_0$.
It is \emph{approximately locally decomposable} if 
\[ \n{ x_0 ' - x_0 } \leq \delta \Rightarrow \n{ f(x') - g_1(x_0', x_1') - g_2( x_0', x_2') } \leq \epsilon. \]
Examples include 
protein folding (pairwise interactions are negligible if the distance is sufficiently high)
or structure from motion (inferring a 3D structure from a set of 2D images).
Those problems can be solved by recursive partitioning. 

%% Optim
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parallel algebraic modeling\\ for stochastic optimization} \\
  J. Huchette et al.
\end{book}
The \texttt{StochJuMP} Julia package solves 
\emph{2-stage stochastic optimization problems with recourse};
contrary to previous implementations, building the model is significantly faster than solving the problem.

%% SAT, SMT
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quick introduction to SAT/SMT solvers\\ and symbolic execution} \\
  D. Yurichev (2016)
\end{book}
SAT solvers tackle (boolean) satisfiability problems expressed in conjunctive normal form, 
with \textsc{or}s  ($\bigwedge_i \bigvee_j x_{ij}$, minisat)
or \textsc{xor}s ($\bigwedge_i \bigoplus_j x_{ij}$, cryptominisat -- \textsc{xor}s are more common than \textsc{or}s in cryptography).

SMT solvers tackle more general (constraint) satisfiability provlems, 
involving finite sets, integers, reals, arrays, quantifiers.
Examples include Z3 (Microsoft, non-free, usable from Python or via a standardized lisp-like language, \textsc{smt-lib}),
\textsc{stp}, \textsc{cvc4} (check the SMT competition for a more up-to-date list).

Klee is an LLVM-based symbolic virtual machine, 
designed to reason about actual programs,
generate unit tests,
prove code equivalence (e.g., coreutils vs busybox)
or correctness,
run code backwards -- 
it can also be coaxed into solving more general satisfiability problems.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Klee: unassisted and automatic generation\\ of high-coverage tests for complex systems} \\
  D. Dunbar et al.
\end{book}
Symbolic (LLVM) virtual machine, to automatically generate tests or identify bugs.

%% IT, nearest neighbours
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Faster cover trees} \\
  M. Izbicki and C.R. Shelton (2015)
\end{book}
A simplified cover tree (for nearest neighbour queries)
is a tree, with one data point in each node,
satisfying 
\begin{align*}
  \text{level}(\text{child}) &= \text{level}( \text{parent} ) - 1 \\
  d( \text{parent}, \text{child} ) & \leq 2 ^ { \text{level}(\text{parent}) } \\
  d( \text{child}, \text{child}_2 ) & > 2 ^ { \text{level}(\text{child}) }
\end{align*}  

%% IT
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bloofi: multidimensional Bloom filters} \\
  A. Crainiceanu and D. Lemire (2015)
\end{book}
The bitwise \textsc{or} between Bloom filters
(same length, same hash function) is still a Bloom filter.
By arranging Bloom filters in a B-tree,
one can efficiently find in which Bloom filters an element is.

%% IT
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ZeroDB white paper} \\
  M. Egorov and M. Wilkison (2016)
\end{book}
Encrypted databases expose their B-trees to their clients.

%% Crypto
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A review of homomorphic encryption\\ and software tools\\ for encrypted statistical machine learning} \\
  L.J.M. Aslett et al.
\end{book}
Homomorphic encryption schemes allow some arithmetic operations on the ciphertext,
usually additions and multiplications: 
\[ \text{dec}\bigl( \text{enc}(x) \mathbin{\raisebox{-1pt}{\ensuremath{\boxplus}}} \text{enc}(y) \bigr) = x + y. \]
They currently have the following limitations.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item They typically add noise to a deterministic function, 
  but not too much, to allow for non-ambiguous decryption -- 
  but operations on the ciphertext increase that noise. 
  It is possible to reset the level of noise during the computations, but this is complex and slow.
\item They are not field morphisms $\RR \to k$; in particular, they do not provide a division: 
  if needed, numerator and denominator can be sent back to the client. 
  Some operations become very expensive: for instance, matrix inversion can be done with cofactors.
\item There are no equality or inequality tests: no conditional code flow is possible.
\item The ciphertext can be large, -- 10,000 times larger than the plaintext.
  It is possible to use a traditional encryption scheme for the data and homomorphically encrypt the key -- but homomorphic AES decryption is very slow.
\item Large integers or real numbers have to be encoded, e.g., with the Chinese remainder theorem, or as fractions.
\end{compactitem}
In R, check the \texttt{HomomorphicEncryption} package; 
in C++, check the HeLib library.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Encrypted statistical machine learning:\\ new privacy-preserving methods} \\
  L.J.M. Aslett (2015)
\end{book}
The limitations of homomorphic encryption, 
in particular, the absence of division, exponential and comparisons,
requires a redesign of most statistical procedures; 
the \texttt{EncryptedStats} package provides Naives Bayes and extreme random forests.
Limited forms of comparison and division are actually available: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item By quantizing the values and replacing them with indicator variables, comparisons such as $x=y$
can be expressed as a scalar product $\sum x_k y_k = 1$;
no branching is possible, but \texttt{t ? a : b} can be written $ta + (1-t)b$;
\item Approximate division can be performed using (encrypted) random number generation, 
  relying on $X \sim \text{Geom}(p) \implies E[X] = 1/p$.
\end{compactitem}

%% R
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Smoothing spline ANOVA models:\\ R package \texttt{gss}} \\
  C. Gu (JSS, 2014)
\end{book}
Classical Anova
$ \mu_{ij} = \mu + \alpha_i + \alpha_j + \epsilon_{ij} $
can be generalized to functions 
\begin{align*}
  f(x_1,x_2) &= (1 - A_1 + A_1) ( 1 - A_2 + A_2 ) f \\
  &= A_1 A_2 f + (1-A_1)A_2 f + (1-A_2)A_1 f + {} \\
  &\hphantom{{}={}} (1-A_1)(1-A_2) f \\
  &= \phi_0 + \phi_1(x_1) + \phi_2(x_2) + \phi_{12}(x_1,x_2)
\end{align*}
where $A_1$ and $A_2$ are averaging operators,
e.g., 
$ (A_1 f)(x_2) = \int_a^b f(x_1,x_2) dx_1 / (b-a) $
and the problem is usually penalized, e.g., 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \phi_0, \phi_1, \phi_2, \phi_{12} \\
  \text{To minimize} & 
  ( \phi_0 - A_1 A_2 f )^2 + {} \\
  & \int \bigl(\phi_1 - (1-A_1) A_2 f \bigr) ^2 + {}\\
  & \int \bigl(\phi_2 - (1-A_2) A_1 f \bigr) ^2 + {} \\
  & \iint \bigl(\phi_{12} - (1-A_1)(1-A_2) f \bigr)^2 + {}\\
  & \lambda_1 \int \va{ \phi_1'' }^2 +     
  \lambda_2 \int \va{ \phi_2'' }^2 +     
  \lambda_{12} \iint \n{ \phi_{12}'' }^2.  
\end{array}
\]
If there are no interactions, this is just a generalized additive model (GAM).
The same framework also applies to log-density estimation (with less overfitting than \texttt{density}).
In particular, check the \texttt{ssanova} and \texttt{ssden} functions.
\begin{verbatim}
  ssanova(y~x)      # Smoothing
  ssanova(y~x1+x2)  # GAM
  ssanova(y~x1*x2)  # GAM with interactions
  ssden(~s)         # Plot with dssden()
\end{verbatim}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hierarchical archimedian copulae:\\ the \texttt{HAC} package} \\
  O. Okhrin and A. Ristig (JSS, 2014)
\end{book}
Archimedian copulas,
\[ C_\phi(u_1,\dots,u_d) = \phi\bigl( \phi^{-1}(u_1) + \cdots + \phi^{-1}(u_d)\bigr) \]
are exchangeable.
By introducing pseudo-variables $C(u_i,u_j)$, one can consider hierarchical archimedian copulas, e.g., 
% Hierarchical copulas
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.5]
\footnotesize
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (A) at (0,0) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (B) at (-1,-1) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (C) at (0,-1) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (D) at (1,-1) {};
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw (B) -- (A) -- (D);
\node[below = 0mm of B] {$u_1$};
\node[below = 0mm of C] {$u_2$};
\node[below = 0mm of D] {$u_3$};
\node at (0,-2.5) {$C(u_1,u_2,u_3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.25,yscale=.5]
\footnotesize
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (A) at (0,0) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (B) at (-2,-1) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (C) at (2,-1) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (D) at (-3,-2) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (E) at (-1,-2) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (F) at (1,-2) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (G) at (3,-2) {};
\draw (D) -- (B) --(A) -- (C) -- (G);
\draw (B) -- (E);
\draw (C) -- (F);
\node[below = 0mm of D] {$u_1$};
\node[below = 0mm of E] {$u_2$};
\node[below = 0mm of F] {$u_3$};
\node[below = 0mm of G] {$u_4$};
\node at (0,-3.5) {$C(C(u_1,u_2),C(u_3,u_4))$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.25,yscale=.5]
\footnotesize
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (A) at (0,0) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (B) at (-1,-1) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (C) at (-2,-2) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (D) at (-3,-3) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (E) at (-1,-3) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (F) at (0,-2) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep = 1pt] (G) at (1,-1) {};
\draw (G) -- (A) --(B) -- (C) -- (D);
\draw (B) -- (F);
\draw (C) -- (E);
\node[below = 0mm of D] {$u_1$};
\node[below = 0mm of E] {$u_2$};
\node[below = 0mm of F] {$u_3$};
\node[below = 0mm of G] {$u_4$};
\node at (0,-4.5) {$C(C(C(u_1,u_2),u_3),u_4)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
The hierarchical structure can be inferred 
by finding the two closest variables, 
replacing them with $C(u_i,u_j)$, and iterating;
consecutive nodes with a very similar generator $\phi$ can be collapsed.
Check the \texttt{estimate.copula} and \texttt{[rpd]HAC} functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{copulaedas: an R package for estimation\\ of distribution algorithms based on copulas} \\
  Y. Gonzalez-Fernandez and M. Soto (2014)
\end{book}
EDA (estimation of distribution algorithms, CMA-ES)
optimizes a black-box function as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a random set of candidates
\item Improve them by local search;
\item Keep the best ones;
\item Estimate their distribution;
\item Sample more candidates from this distribution
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}
The distribution is often estimated as a Gaussian,
but one can also use copula-based models:
independence copula, Gaussian copula,
hierarchical archimedian copula,
regular vine, % No details...
etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{General purpose convolution algorithm\\ in S4 classes by means of FFT} \\
  P. Ruckdeschel and M. Kohl (JSS, 2014)
\end{book}
The \texttt{distr} package provides operations ($+$, $\times$, etc.)
on independent random variables, e.g., 
\[ X \sim N(0,3) \text{ and } Y \sim N(0,4) \implies X + Y \sim N(0,5) \]
by discretizing and convolving the distributions when no closed form is available.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\texttt{mediation}:\\ R package for causal mediation analysis} \\
  D. Tingley et al. (JSS, 2014)
\end{book}
Given random variables $T$ (treatment, binary), 
$M$ (suspected mediator) and $Y$ (outcome),
one can measure how much of a role $M$ plays in the causal process from $T$ to $Y$ 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2,yscale=.5]
\node (T) at (-1,0) {$T$};
\node (M) at (0,0) {$M$};
\node (Y) at (1,0) {$Y$};
\draw[->] (T) -- (M);
\draw[->] (M) to node[auto,pos=.5,below=0pt] {\footnotesize$\delta$} (Y);
\draw[->] (T) to [bend left=60] node[auto,pos=.5] {\footnotesize$\zeta$} (Y);
\node[ below = -5pt of T ] {\footnotesize treatment};
\node[ below = -5pt of M ] {\footnotesize mediator};
\node[ below = -5pt of Y ] {\footnotesize outcome};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
by modeling $M \sim T$ and $Y \sim T + M$ and considering 
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Total treatment}  & \tau      &= Y\bigl( 1, M(1) \bigr) + Y\bigl( 0, M(0) \bigr) \\
  &\text{Causal mediation} & \delta(t) &= Y\bigl( t, M(1) \bigr) + Y\bigl( t, M(0) \bigr) \\
  &\text{Direct effect}    & \zeta(t)  &= Y\bigl( 1, M(t) \bigr) + Y\bigl( 0, M(t) \bigr),
\end{align*}
which gives decompositions 
\[ 
  \tau = \delta(t) + \zeta(1-t),\quad 
  t \in \{0,1\}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\texttt{hmmm}: an R package for hierarchical multinomial marginal models} \\
  R. Colombi et al. (JSS, 2014)
\end{book}
\emph{Marginal models} model the joint distribution of qualitative variables as 
\begin{align*}
  P(X_1,\dots,X_n) &= P(X_1) P(X_2 | X_1) P(X_3 | X_1,X_2) \cdots  \\
  &\hphantom{{}={}} \cdots P(X_n | X_1,\dots,X_{n-1}),
\end{align*}
with constraints on some of those factors to enforce conditional independencies (equalities) 
or stochastic dominance (inequalities) -- not unlike graph-less graphical models.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\texttt{structSSI}:\\ simultaneous and selective inference\\ for grouped or hierarchically structured data} \\
  K. Sankaran and S. Holmes (JSS, 2014)
\end{book}

Given $m$ $p$-values 
$p_1 \leq \cdots \leq p_m$,
multiple testing procedures usually look at 
the \emph{family-wise error rate} (FWER), \ie, 
try to ensure 
\[ P[ \text{at least one false positive} ] \leq \alpha \]
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bonferroni: reject $H_0(k)$ if $p_k \leq \alpha / m$;
\item \v Sid\'ak: reject $H_0(k)$ if $p_k \leq 1 - (1-\alpha)^{1/m}$;
\item Holm--Bonferroni: reject $H_0(k)$ as long as $p_k \leq 1/(m+1-k)$;
\item Holberg:  reject $H_0(k)$ as long as there exists $\ell \geq k$ such that $p_\ell \leq 1 / (m+1-\ell)$
\end{compactitem}
or the \emph{false discovery rate} (FDR), \ie, try to ensure 
\[ E \left[ \dfrac{ \text{false positives} }{ \text{positives} } \right] \leq \alpha \]
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Benjamin--Hochberg (BH): reject $H_0(k)$ as long as $p_k \leq k \alpha / m$;
\item BHY:  reject $H_0(k)$ as long as $p_k \leq k \alpha / \sum_{i=1}^m (1/i)$.
\end{compactitem}
But they tend to be optimal for independent tests, 
overly conservative for positively dependent tests, 
and incorrect for negatively dependent tests -- 
those that remain valid account for an arbitrary dependence structure, 
but that is too general an assumption.

Some extensions of those tests account for a hierarchical structure: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Group BH (GBH) estimates the proportion of $H_1$'s in each group and reweighs the $p$-values accordingly;
\item Hierarchical FDR (HFDR) accepts or rejects the null hypothesis at the group level,
and only looks at individual tests in groups whose null hypothesis was rejected.
\end{compactitem}

%% R
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{LDAvis: a method for visualizing\\ and interpreting topics} \\
  C. Sievert and K.E. Shirley
\end{book}
To find the words most relevant to a given topic, one can rank them according to
$P(\text{term}|\text{topic})$ 
  (but this gives too much weight to common, non-discriminative words),
or $P(\text{term}|\text{topic}) / P(\text{term})$ (lift -- 
  but this gives too much weight to rare, non-discriminative words),
or, better, a weighted average of their logarithms (``relevance'').  

%% R
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{fitdistrplus:\\ an R package for fitting distributions} \\
  M.L. Delignette-Muller and C. Dutang (2015)
\end{book}
One can fit a distribution to data using: maximum likelihood (MLE), moment matching, quantile matching, maximum goodness of fit (gof);
the gof can be assessed with the Kolmogorov-Smirnov, Cramer-von Mises or Anderson-Darling distance
(AD is a variant of CvM with more weight on the tail -- there are 1-sided variants, and variants with more weight).
% The fitdistrplus package also deals with censored data.

%% R
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SDD:\\ an R package for serial dependence diagrams} \\
  L. Bagnato et al. (JSS, 2015)
\end{book}
The autocorrelation function (ACF) relies on linear correlation.
Instead, the \emph{dependogram}
uses the independence $\chi^2$
(or its normalization, Cramer's $V$)
between $X_\cdot$ and $X_{\cdot - k}$,
discretized into $n$ quantile bins,
or the Kullback-Leibler divergence between (kernel) density estimators 
of $X_\cdot$, $X_{\cdot-k}$
and $(X_\cdot, X_{\cdot-k})$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fitting heavy tailed distributions:\\ the \texttt{poweRlaw} package} \\
  C.S. Gillespie (JSS, 2015)
\end{book}
To fit a power law distribution 
$p(x) \propto x^{-\alpha} \mathbf 1_{ x \geq x_{\text{min}} }$
on the tail of the data,
use the maximum likelihood estimator
\[ \hat \alpha = 1 + n \bigg/ \!\!\! \sum_{ x \geq x_{\text{min}} } \log \dfrac{ x }{ x_{\text{min}} }; \]
choose the $x_{\text{min}}$ minimizing the Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) distance; 
boostrap to estimate the uncertainty on $x_{\text{min}}$;
to estimate the goodness of fit, 
look at the distribution of the KS statistic on data sampled from 
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.2]
  \draw(-.5,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw(0,0) -- (0,1);
  \draw[ultra thick] (-.5,1) -- (0,1) -- 
  (0.05,0.864)--(0.1,0.751)--(0.15,0.658)--(0.2,0.579)--(0.25,0.512)--(0.3,0.455)--(0.35,0.406)--(0.4,0.364)--(0.45,0.328)--(0.5,0.296)--(0.55,0.269)--(0.6,0.244)--(0.65,0.223)--(0.7,0.204)--(0.75,0.187)--(0.8,0.171)--(0.85,0.158)--(0.9,0.146)--(0.95,0.135)--(1,0.125);
\end{tikzpicture}.

%% R
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{abctools: an R package for tuning\\ approximate Bayesian computation analyses} \\
  M.A. Nunes and D. Prangle
\end{book}
Approximate Bayesian computation (ABC)
is a Bayesian method that uses simulations instead of likelihoods.
Rejection ABC proceeds as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Draw a parameter $\theta$ from the prior;
\item Simulate data $x \sim p(\cdot | \theta )$; compute statistics $s(x)$;
\item If $d(s^{\text{obs}},s) \leq \epsilon$, accept $\theta$;
\item Iterate until you have enough samples.
\end{compactitem}
The \texttt{abctools} package complements the \texttt{abc} package
and can help choose the statistics from a set of candidates,
e.g., by approximate sufficiency 
(greedily add the statistics that produce the largest change in the posterior distribution;
stop when it becomes insignificant)
or by finding the subset minimizing some information criterion
(there are also projection methods) and help choose $\epsilon$.

%% R
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\text{ngspatial}: a package for fitting the centered autologistic and sparse spatial generalized linear mixed models for areal data} \\
  J. Hughes
\end{book}
The autologistic model 
\[
  \log \dfrac{ P[ z_i = 1 ] }{ P[ z_i = 0 ] } = 
  x_i ' \beta + \eta \sum_{ j \in \text{Neigh}(i) } Z_j 
\]
is confounded;
one can center the autocovariate with 
\begin{align*}
  \log \dfrac{ P[ z_i = 1 ] }{ P[ z_i = 0 ] } &= 
  x_i ' \beta + \eta \sum_{ j \in \text{Neigh}(i) } ( Z_j - \mu_j )  \\
  \mu_j &= ( 1 + e^{ - x_j \beta } ) ^{-1}.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MVN: an R package\\ for assessing multivariate normality} \\
  S. Korkmaz et al.
\end{book}
To check multivariate normality: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Look at the 1-dimensional margins: histograms, qqplots, normality tests;
\item Plot the 2-dimensional densities (\texttt{persp}, \texttt{contour});
\item Plot the sample Mahalanobis distance versus $\chi^2$ quantiles;
\item Try multivariate normality tests: 
  Mardia (based on skewness and kurtosis), 
  Henze--Zirkler
  (combines $\sum e^{-\alpha D_{ij}}$ and $\sum e^{-\beta D_{i}}$,
  where $D_{ij} = d_{\text{Mahalanobis}}(x_i,x_j)$ 
  and $D_{i} = d_{\text{Mahalanobis}}(x_i,\bar x)$),
  Royston (Shapiro--Wilk);
\item Remove outliers (robust Mahalanobis distance, from minimum covariance determinant estimators instead of sample covariances).
\end{compactitem}

%% R
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\texttt{paircompviz}: and R package for visualization of multiple pairwise comparison test results} \\
  M. Burda
\end{book}
The result of multiple pairwise tests is often represented by a line diagram, or a letter diagram.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.7,yscale=.3]
\foreach \m in {2,3,4,5}
  \draw[fill,gray] (\m-1,0) -- (\m,0) -- (\m,.5) -- (\m-1,.5) -- cycle;
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4}
  \draw[fill,gray] (\m-1,1) -- (\m,1) -- (\m,1.5) -- (\m-1,1.5) -- cycle;
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5}
  \draw (\m-1,0) -- (\m,0) -- (\m,.5) -- (\m-1,.5) -- cycle;
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5}
  \draw (\m-1,1) -- (\m,1) -- (\m,1.5) -- (\m-1,1.5) -- cycle;
\foreach \m in {1,2,3,4,5}
  \node at (\m-.5,2.5) {$T_\m$};
\node at (6.5,.7) {$T_1 \neq T_5$};  
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
Instead, one can consider the relation 
$T_i \succ T_j$ if $i$ is significantly different from $j$,
hope it is a partial order (this happens surprisingly often)
and draw its \emph{Hasse diagram}
(\ie, remove the edges that can be inferred by connectivity)
after compressing complete bipartite subgraphs
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep = 1pt,baseline=4mm]
\node (A) at (0,1) {A};
\node (B) at (1,1) {B};
\node (C) at (2,1) {C};
\node (D) at (0,0) {D};
\node (E) at (1,0) {E};
\node (F) at (2,0) {F};
\draw[->] (A) -- (D);
\draw[->] (A) -- (E);
\draw[->] (A) -- (F);
\draw[->] (B) -- (D);
\draw[->] (B) -- (E);
\draw[->] (B) -- (F);
\draw[->] (C) -- (D);
\draw[->] (C) -- (E);
\draw[->] (C) -- (F);
\end{tikzpicture}
\ \mapsto\ 
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1,yscale=1,inner sep = 1,baseline=4mm]
\node (A) at (0,1) {A};
\node (B) at (1,1) {B};
\node (C) at (2,1) {C};
\node (D) at (0,0) {D};
\node (E) at (1,0) {E};
\node (F) at (2,0) {F\rlap{.}};
\node (X) at (1,.5) {};
\draw[->] (A) -- (X);
\draw[->] (B) -- (X);
\draw[->] (C) -- (X);
\draw[->] (X) -- (D);
\draw[->] (X) -- (E);
\draw[->] (X) -- (F);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

%% R
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\texttt{logcondens}: computation related to univariate log-concave density estimation} \\
  L. D\"umbgen and K. Rufibach
\end{book}
The log-concave density estimator is parameter-free,
and log-concavity is a plausible assumption for many datasets: 
consider using its smoothed version 
(it is piecewise log-linear and bas discontinuities at both ends of the support) instead of a kernel density estimator.

For a multivariate estimator, check \texttt{LogConcDEAD}.

%% Graphs, games
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Online graph pruning for pathfinding\\ on grid maps} \\
  D. Harabor and A. Grastien
\end{book}
To account for symmetry and avoid examining nodes unnecessarily,
path finding algorithms on grids try to recognize
rectangular rooms, dead-ends, swamps, 
neighbours to prune and ``jump points''.

%% Finance, graphs
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Metabolic paths in world economy\\ and crude oil price} \\
  F. Picciolo et al. (2015)
\end{book}

Measure paths in the world trade web (WTW) with:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Trade imbalance
\(\displaystyle
  b = \dfrac{
    \sum_i \min( w_{\cdot i}, w_{i\cdot} ) 
  }{
    \sum_{ij} w_{ij}
  }
\)
\item Reciprocity
\(\displaystyle
  r = \dfrac{
    \sum_{ij} \min( w_{ij}, w_{ji} )
  }{
    \sum_{ij} w_{ij}
  }
\)
\item Cycling index
\begin{align*}
  \Gamma^{(s)} &= \dfrac{
    \sum s_{i \cdot} \Gamma_i^{(s)}
  }{
    \sum w_{i\cdot}
  } \\
  \Gamma_i^{(s)} &= \dfrac{ u_{ii}^{(s)} - 1 }{ u_{ii}^{(s)} } \\
  U^{s} &= I + M + M^2 + \cdots + M^s \\
  m_{ij} &\propto w_{ij} \text{ (stochastic matrix)}
\end{align*}
  where $\Gamma_i^{(s)}$ is the fraction of trade that goes back to~$i$ within $s$ steps.
\end{compactitem}

Look at the corresponding time series, the contribution of each country, the correlation with oil, etc.

%% Finance
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting stock returns\\ during good and bad times} \\
  D. Huang et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Log-prices can be modeled as the sum of a random walk (permanent shocks) and an AR(1) process (temporary shocks)
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix} p_t \\ q_t \\ z_t \end{pmatrix} &= 
\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \mu \\ 0  \end{pmatrix}  +
\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & \lambda \end{pmatrix} 
\begin{pmatrix} p_{t-1} \\ q_{t-1} \\ z_{t-1} \end{pmatrix} +
\epsilon
\\
\var \epsilon &= 
\begin{pmatrix} 
  0 & 0 & 0  \\
  0 & \sigma_1^2 & \rho \sigma_1 \sigma_2 \\
  0 & \rho \sigma_1 \sigma_2 & \sigma_2^2
\end{pmatrix} 
\end{align*}
where the correlation $\rho$ between the random walk and the AR(1) innovations is a market state indicator: 
$\rho \geq 0$ corresponds to good times, overconficence, low risk premium, reversal, $\cov(r_{t+1},r_t) < 0$.
Empirically, use 
$ \text{MRI} = ( \text{12-month return} - \mu ) / \sigma $, 
(where $\mu$ and $\sigma$ are the long-term mean and standard deviation of the 12-month return)
as momentum,
and $\text{IMA} = \mathbf1_{ \text{log-price} > \text{MA}(\text{log-price},200\text{d}) }$
or $\text{ISR} = \mathbf1_{ \text{MA}(\text{Sharpe},\text{6m}) > \text{30\% long-term quantile} }$
as market state indicator.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk premia:\\ asymmetric tail risks and excess returns} \\
  Y. Lemp\'eri\`ere et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{ranked P\&L skewness}
is the area under the curve of the cummulated standardized values, 
sorted by increasing absolute values
(there are other low-moment estimators of skewness, e.g., the normalized mean-minus-median).
The risk premium present in many markets seems to be due to skewness rather than volatility -- 
investors do not fear small Gaussian fluctuations, but rather large tail events.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting the equity risk premium:\\ the role of technical indicators} \\
  C.J. Neely et al. (2010)
\end{book}
To forecast index returns,
use both macroeconomic variables 
% Complete list pages 5-6
(dividend yield, robust excess return volatility, term spread)
and technical indicators 
(MACD, momentum, MACD of the signed volume).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial volatility and economic activity} \\
  F. Fornari and A. Mele (2010)
\end{book}
Volatility
(robust MAD estimator, 
$\sqrt{\frac{\pi\vphantom{\raisebox{.5pt}{\scriptsize a}}}2}\langle \va{\text{ret}_t} \rangle$)
and term spread can predict industrial production growth.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Macroeconomic determinants\\ of stock volatility and volatility premiums} \\
  V. Corradi et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Conversely, the business cycle can predict volatility.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Short interest and aggregate stock returns} \\
  D.E. Rapach (2014)
\end{book}
Aggregate short interest 
(as a proportion of shares outstanding, detrended) is informative.

%% Hist
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The vector algebra war: a historical perspective} \\
  J.M. Chappell
\end{book}
Different formulations can be used to compactly write Maxwell's field equations: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Gibbs vectors, \ie, vectors in $\RR^3$ with dot and cross product $(\RR,\cdot,\times)$;
\item Quaternions $\mathbf{H}$, a 4-dimensional albegra with $i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = ijk = -1$;
\item Clifford algebra, $\mathscr C\ell (\RR^3)$, an 8-dimensional algebra, 
  with $e_i e_j = - e_j e_i$, $e1^2 = e_2^2 = e_3^2 = +1$, 
  with the natural embedding $e_i \mapsto e_i$ from $\RR^3$;
  the dot and cross product can be recovered as 
  $vw = v \cdot w + e_1 e_2 e_3 v \times w$.
\end{compactitem}
With the Clifford (or geometric) algebra, Maxwell's equations become 
$\partial (E + j c B ) = \rho / \epsilon - \mu c J$.
[No mention of differential forms or screw calculus.]

%% PML
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Recent results on pattern maximum likelihood} \\
  J. Acharya et al. (2009)
\end{book}
Given an iid sequence of symbols, e.g., 
HHTHTTH,
where the list of possible symbols is not known, what can we say about the multiset of symbol probabilities?
Standard maximum likelihood (estimate the probabilities of each symbol and forget the order)
only works well for large samples. \emph{Pattern maximum likelihood} (PML) replaces the symbols by numbers, 
starting with the most frequent, and reorders them, e.g., 
ababca$\to$121231$\to$111223.
A pattern probability $p$ is a non-increasing sequence 
$p_1 \geq p_2 \geq \cdots \geq 0$
with $\sum p_i \leq 1$;
the remainder $1 - \sum p_i$ is the continuous part of $p$.
The PML of a pattern $\psi$ is 
\[ \text{PML}( \psi ) = \argmax_p p(\psi). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algebraic computation\\ of pattern maximum likelihood} \\
  J. Acharya et al. (2011)
\end{book}
The PML can be computed analytically with Gr\"obner bases. 
For instance, 
$\text{PML}(1112234)=(\tfrac15,\tfrac15,\tfrac15,\tfrac15,\tfrac15)$.

%% 2016-06
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Analytic combinatorics} \\
  P. Flajolet and R. Sedgewick (2009)
\end{book}
The traditional approach to combinatorics,
to estimate the number $a_n$ of a certain type of objects 
(e.g., graphs of size $n$ with a certain property)
is the following: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Find a recurrence relation involving $a_n$
  (if you can solve it, you are done);
\item Consider the generating function $f(x) = \sum a_n x^n$ 
  or (if $a$ grows too fast) $f(x) = \sum a_n x^n / n!$;
\item Transform the recurrence relation for $a$ into 
  a functional (or differential) equation for $f$;
\item Solve the equation or, somehow, use it to infer the asymptotic behaviour of $a$.
\end{compactitem}

\d{Analytic combinatorics} goes directly from the combinatorial
description of the problem to the functional equation for the
generating function, and uses complex analysis to estimate the
asymptotic behaviour of its coefficients.

\textbf{1.} A \emph{combinatorial class} 
$(\mathscr A, \va\cdot)$ is a set $\mathscr A$
with a size function $\va\cdot : \mathscr A \to \NN$
such that, for all $n$,
$a_n = \# \{ x \in \mathscr A \ :\ \va x = n \} $
is finite. Its \emph{ordinary generating function} is 
$A(z) = \sum a_n z^n \in \RR \llbracket z \rrbracket$.
The coefficients are denoted 
$[z^n] A(z) = a_n$.

Simple operations on combinatorial classes 
translate to their generating functions as follows. 

\begin{align*}
& \mathscr A \sqcup \mathscr B &
C(z) &= A(z) + B(z) \\
& \mathscr A \times \mathscr B &
C(z) &= A(z) B(z) \\
& \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} \mathscr B &
C(z) &= 1 / ( 1 - B(z) ) \\
& \operatorname{\textsc{Cyc}} \mathscr B &
C(z) &= \sum_{k\geq1} \dfrac{ \phi(k) }{ k } \log\dfrac1{1-B(z^k)} 
\displaybreak[0] \\
& \operatorname{\textsc{MSet}} \mathscr B &
C(z) &= \prod_{n\geq1} ( 1 - z^n ) ^{ - B_n } \\
&&&= \exp \Biggl( \sum_{k \geq 1} \dfrac1k B(z^k) \Biggr) 
\displaybreak[0] \\
& \operatorname{\textsc{PSet}} \mathscr B &
C(z) &= \prod_{n\geq1} ( 1 + z^n ) ^{  B_n } \\
&&&= \exp \Biggl( \sum_{k \geq 1} \dfrac{(-1)^{k-1}}k B(z^k) \Biggr)
\end{align*}
The last threee formulas are the P\'olya logarithm, exponential, 
and modified exponential.
The sequences $\operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} \mathscr B = 
\{\epsilon\} \sqcup \mathscr B \sqcup 
\mathscr B \times \mathscr B \sqcup \cdots = 
\coprod_{n \geq 0} \mathscr B ^n$
are only defined if $b_0$ = 0; 
the cycles are 
$\operatorname{\textsc{Cyc}} \mathscr B =
( \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} \mathscr B 
\setminus \{ \epsilon \} ) / \mathfrak S$; 
the multisets are 
$\operatorname{\textsc{MSet}} \mathscr B =
( \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} \mathscr B 
) / \mathfrak S$.

Here are a few examples. 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $E$: single object of size 0;
\item $Z$: single object of size 1;
\item $I = \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}}_{\geq1} Z$: 
  non-zero integers;
\item $\operatorname{\textsc{Seq}}( Z \sqcup Z \times Z($:
  coverings of $\llbracket 0, n \rrbracket$ 
  by intervals of length 1 or 2 
  (Fibonacci numbers);
\item $T = E \sqcup T \times Z \times T$: 
  triangulations (Catalan numbers);
\item $G = Z \times \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} G$: 
  rooted plane trees -- a tree is a root and a sequence of trees
  (shifted Catalan numbers shifted);
\item $H = Z \times \operatorname{\textsc{MSet}} H$: 
  non-plane (rooted) trees;
\item $C = \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} I$: 
  compositions (ways of writing an integer as a sum of integers);
\item $P = (\operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} I ) / \mathfrak S = 
  \operatorname{\textsc{MSet}} I$: partitions;
\item $A = m Z$: $m$-letter alphabet;
\item $W = \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} A$: words.
\end{compactitem}
The language recognized by a finite automaton 
has generating function
$L(z) = \mathbf u ' ( I - z T ) \mathbf v$,
where $T$ is the incidence matrix (it is not binary because there can be several edges, with different labels, between the same vertices), and $\mathbf u$ and $\mathbf v$ the indicator vectors 
of the initial and final states. 
Rational languages have a rational generating function; 
context-free languages have an algebraic generating function.

\textbf{2.} A \emph{labelled combinatorial class}
is a combinatorial class 
$(\mathscr A, \va\cdot)$
with an action of the symmetric group $\mathfrak S_n$
on each $\mathscr A_n = \{ x \in \mathscr A \ :\ \va x = n \}$.
Informally, 
think of the elements of $\mathscr A$ as graphs 
(perhaps with some added structure)
whose vertex set is $\llbracket 1,n \rrbracket$
-- the notion of \emph{species of structure} 
makes this rigorous and unifies the labelled and unlabelled cases.
Its \emph{exponential generating function} is 
$A(z) = \sum a_n x^n / n!$.
Operations on labelled combinatorial classes translate 
to their exponential generating functions.
\begin{align*}
& \mathscr A \sqcup \mathscr B &
C(z) &= A(z) + B(z) \\
& \mathscr A \star \mathscr B &
C(z) &= A(z) B(z) \\
& \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} \mathscr B &
C(z) &= 1 / ( 1 - B(z) ) \\
& \operatorname{\textsc{Set}} \mathscr B &
C(z) &= \exp B(z) \\
& \operatorname{\textsc{Cyc}} \mathscr B &
C(z) &= \log\dfrac1{1-B(z)}
\end{align*}

Given two labelled objects 
$\alpha \in \mathscr A$, 
$\beta \in \mathscr B$, 
one can build labelled objects $(\alpha',\beta')$ 
by relabelling $\alpha$ and $\beta$ while keeping their order 
(there are many such relabelings). The set of all such labelled objects form the \emph{labelled product}
$\mathscr A \star \mathscr B$.

Examples include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $R = \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}} \operatorname{\textsc{Set}}_{\geq1} Z$: surjections;
\item $S = \operatorname{\textsc{Set}} \operatorname{\textsc{Seq}}_{\geq 1} Z$: partitions;
\item $P = \operatorname{\textsc{Set}} \operatorname{\textsc{Cyc}} Z$: permutations;
\item $I = \operatorname{\textsc{Set}} \operatorname{\textsc{Cyc}}_{1,2} Z$: permutations;
\item $D = \operatorname{\textsc{Set}} \operatorname{\textsc{Cyc}}_{>1} Z$: derangements;
\item $T = Z \star \operatorname{\textsc{Set}} T$, 
  $T(z) = z e^{T(z)}$: a (non-plane) rooted tree is a root and a set of rooted trees;
\item $T = U^\bullet$: a rooted tree is a pointed unrooted tree;
\item $F = \operatorname{\textsc{Set}} \operatorname{\textsc{Cyc}} T$, $F(z) = (1-T(z))^{-1}$: 
the graph of an endomorphism is a set of cycles, with a tree attached to each element of those cycles.
\end{compactitem}

\textbf{3.} This can be generalized to combinatorial classes with parameters  
$(\mathscr A, \va\cdot, \chi)$,
$\chi : \mathscr A \to \NN^d$,
and multivariate generating functions,
$A(z,\mathbf u) = 
\sum a_{n,\mathbf k} z^n \mathbf u ^ {\mathbf k}$,
where 
$a_{n,\mathbf k} = \#\{ a \in \mathscr A \ : \ \va x = n \text{ and } \chi(a) = \mathbf k \}$.
This leads, for instance, to the expected number of cycles in a permutations of size $n$, $\mu_n = \sum_{k=1}^n 1/k \sim \log n$.

\textbf{4.} Complex analysis, starting with Cauchy's formula
\[
[z^n] f(z) = \dfrac1{2\pi i} \oint_\Gamma \dfrac{ f(z) dz }{ z^{n+1} },
\]
can help study the asymptotic behaviour of those power series.

If $f$ is analytic at $0$, with radius of convergence $R$, 
then $f_n = [z^n] f(z)$ 
is of exponential order $R^{-n}$, 
$f_n \bowtie R^{-n}$, \ie, 
$\varlimsup \va{ a_n} ^ {1/n} = 1/R$.

Saddlepoint bounds are often accurate: 
\[ 
[z^n] f(z) \leq 
\inf_{ r \in (0,R) } r^{-n} 
\sup_{ \va z = r } \va{ f(z) }. 
\]

If $f$ is meromorphic on $[\va z = R]$,
then 
\[ [z^n] f(z) = \sum P_i(n) \alpha_i^{-n} + O(R^{-n}), \]
where $\alpha_i$ are the poles of $f$ on the circle 
$[\va z = R]$ and $P_i$ are polynomials of degree at most 
the order of the poles minus 1.

%\textbf{5.} [Technical chapter]

\textbf{6.} If $f$ is meromorphic on 
$D=[\va z \leq R]$, with radius of convergence $R$, 
and dominant singularities (\ie, on the boundary of the convergence disk) $\zeta_1,\dots,\zeta_k$,
then $g(z) = f(z) - \sum c_i ( 1 - z / \zeta_i )^{-\alpha_i}$
is holomorphic on a neighbourhood of $D$,
and the asymptotic behaviour of $[z^n] f(z)$ 
is that of $\sum c_i ( 1 - z / \zeta_i )^{-\alpha_i}$.
For a more precise asymptotic estimate, repeat the process with~$g$.

In particular, if there is only one dominant singularity, 
\[
f(x) \sim ( 1 - z / \zeta ) ^ { - \alpha }
\implies
f_n \sim \dfrac1{\Gamma(\alpha)} \rho^{-n} n ^ { \alpha - 1 }.
\]

This can be generalized to singularities of the form
\[ (1-z)^{-\alpha} \biggl( \dfrac1z \log \dfrac1{1-z} \biggr)^\beta. \]

%\textbf{7.} [Applications]

\textbf{8.}
If there are no singularities,
or if the closest singularity is essential, 
the saddle point method
can give the asymptotic behaviour of the coefficients.

Given an analytic function $f$ on $\Omega \subset \CC$, 
the surface $z \mapsto \va{ f(z) }$ is not arbitrary: 
its points are either 
ordinary ($f \neq 0$, $f' \neq 0$),
zeroes ($f=0$)
or saddle points ($f\neq 0$, $f'=0$) --
in particular, the zeroes are the only extrema
(this can be used to prove the fundamental theorem of algebra).

A  trivial bound for the integral 
$\int_A^B f(z) dz$ is 
\[ \va{ \int_A^B f(z) dz } \leq 
\text{length}(\Gamma) \times \sup_\Gamma \va{f}. 
\]
To have a tighter bound, one can choose a path $\Gamma$ that goes through a saddle point.
In particular (if $G$ is analytic at zero, not a polynomial, with nonnegative coefficients and $G(R)=+\infty$), 
\[ 
[z^n] G(z) = \dfrac1{2\pi i} \oint \dfrac{ G(z) dz }{ z^{n+1} } 
\leq
\dfrac{ G(\zeta)  }{ \zeta^n } \]
where $\zeta G'(\zeta) / G(\zeta) = n+1$
(the saddle point, $\zeta$, and the circle of integration depend on $n$).

The \emph{saddle point method} 
is a refinement of this bound using the Laplace method: 
(under a few assumptions)
\begin{align*}
  & [z^n] G(z) \sim \dfrac{ G(\zeta) }{ \zeta^n \sqrt{ 2\pi b(\zeta)}} \\
  & b(z) = z^2 \dfrac{ d^2 }{ dz^2 } \log G(z) + z \dfrac{ d }{ dz } \log G(z) \\
  & \zeta \dfrac{ G'(\zeta) }{ G(\zeta) } = n.
\end{align*}

\textbf{9.} The multivariate power series 
$ F(x,u) \!=\! \sum f_{n,k}^{} u^k x^n  $
can be seen as a deformation of a univariate series
$F(x,1)$.
For $n$ fixed, it gives the probability generating function
of a discrete distribution
\[f_n^{-1} [x^n] F(x,u) = f_n^{-1} \sum_k f_{n,k} u^k
\]
and can help study the limit law, the convergence speed (Berry-Esseen) and the tail probabilities.

[For discrete laws, if $p_n(u) = E[u^X_n]$
pointwise converges to $p(u) = E[u^X]$ 
for all $u \ A \subset \CC$, where $A$ has an accumulation point in the open unit disk, then $X_n$ converges to $X$ in distribution.
For continuous laws ($\mu_n, \sigma_n \to \infty$ -- 
rescaling is needed),
pointwise convergence of the characteristic function (on $\RR$)
or of the Laplace function (on a neighbourhood of $0$) 
implies convergence in distribution.]

Under some conditions
(involving the Laplace transform,
but reducing to easy-to-check conditions depending on the type of singularity),
the distribution is asymptotically Gaussian.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to the theory\\ of species of structures} \\
  F. Bergeron et al. (2013)
\end{book}
% Draft of the first 3 chapters of the new edition of Combinatorial species and tree-like structures (1998)

A \emph{species of structures} is a functor $F$ 
from the category of finite sets and bijections 
$\mathfrak{set}$ to itself.

To a species $F$, one can associate formal power series
\begin{align*}
  F(x) &= \sum_{n \geq 0} \# F[n] \, \dfrac{x^n}{n!} \\
  \tilde F(x) &= \sum_{n \geq 0} \# ( F[n] / {\simeq} ) \, x^n \\
  Z_F(x_1,\dots) &= \sum_{n \geq 0}  \dfrac1{n!} \sum_{ \sigma \in \mathfrak S_n } \operatorname{fix} F[\sigma] \, x_1^{\sigma_1} x_2^{\sigma_2} \cdots
\end{align*}
where 
$[n] = \{1,2,\dots,n\}$,
$\# ( F[n] / {\simeq} ) = \# ( F[n] / \mathfrak S_n )$ 
is the number of isomorphism classes in $F[n]$,
$\sigma_k$ is the number of cycles of length $k$ in the cycle decomposition of $\sigma \in \mathfrak S_n$,
$\operatorname{fix} F[\sigma] = \# \{ u \in F[n] \ :\ \sigma u = u \} $ is the number of points of $F[n]$ fixed by $F[\sigma]$.

The \emph{exponential generating series} $F(x)$ counts (labelled) $F$-structures; 
the (isomorphism) \emph{type generating series} $\tilde F(x)$ counts isomorphism classes of $F$-structures, \ie, unlabelled $F$-structures; 
the \emph{cycle index series} $Z_F$ contains more information.

Bijections $FU \simeq GU$ for all $U$ (equipotence) do not 
necessarily define an isomorphism: 
for instance, the species  of linear orders Lin and that of permutations Perm are equiponent, but not isomorphic
(the bijections do not form a natural transformation); 
in particular, 
$\operatorname{Lin}(x) = \operatorname{Perm}(x)$, 
but 
$\widetilde{\operatorname{Lin}}(x) \neq
\widetilde{\operatorname{Perm}}(x)$.

The following operations on species correspond to more or less complex operations on their power series.
\begin{align*}
  &\text{Sum} & 
  (F+G)U &= FU \sqcup GU \\
  &\text{Product} & \displaystyle
  (F \cdot G)U &= 
  \!\!\!
  \sum_{ U = U_1 \sqcup U_2} 
  \!\!\!
  F U_1 \times F U_2 \\
  &\text{Substitution} & \displaystyle
  (F \circ G) U &= 
  \!\!\!\!\!\!
  \sum_{ \pi \text{ partition of } U }
  \!\!\!\!\!\!
  F[\pi] \times \prod_{ p \in \pi } G[p] \\
  &\text{Derivative} & F'U &= F( U \sqcup \{*\} ) \\
  &\text{Pointing} & F^\bullet U &= F[U] \times U \\
  &\text{Superposition} & (F \times G)U &= FU \times GU \\
  &\text{Composition} & (F \mathbin{\square} G) U &= F( GU )
\end{align*}

Here are a few examples. 

A permutation can be decomposed into cycles: 
the cycles of length greater than 1 form a derangement, 
the cycles of length 1 form a set. 
This corresponds to a product of species, 
$\operatorname{Perm} = \text{Der} \cdot  U$,
where $U:E \mapsto \{*\}$ is the species of sets,
and gives $1/(1-x) = \operatorname{Der}(x) e^x$.
\[
\operatorname{Perm}(E) = 
\coprod_{ U = E_1 \sqcup E_2 } 
\operatorname{Der}(E_1) \times U(E_2)
\]

Since a map is a surjection onto its image, we can relate maps and surjections by
$\text{Set}(I,\cdot) = \text{Epi}(I,\cdot) \cdot U$:
\[
\text{Set}(I,J) = 
\coprod_{J = J_1 \sqcup J_2} \text{Epi}(I,J_1) \times U(J_2).
\]

A map $E \to E$ is defined by 
(iterate the map until you get cycles, and look at the bassin of attraction of each element in those cycles): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A partition $\pi$ of $E$;
\item A rooted tree on each block of $\pi$;
\item A permutation on the blocks of $\pi$.
\end{compactitem}
This is a composition of species,
$\operatorname{End} = \operatorname{Perm}(A)$.
\[
% Code from: 
%   http://tex.stackexchange.com/questions/27171/padded-boundary-of-convex-hull
% Also check: 
%   http://tex.stackexchange.com/questions/249860/compute-and-draw-a-convex-hull
\newcommand{\convexpath}[2]{
  [   
  create hullcoords/.code={
    \global\edef\namelist{#1}
    \foreach [count=\counter] \nodename in \namelist {
      \global\edef\numberofnodes{\counter}
      \coordinate (hullcoord\counter) at (\nodename);
    }
    \coordinate (hullcoord0) at (hullcoord\numberofnodes);
    \pgfmathtruncatemacro\lastnumber{\numberofnodes+1}
    \coordinate (hullcoord\lastnumber) at (hullcoord1);
  },
  create hullcoords
  ]
  ($(hullcoord1)!#2!-90:(hullcoord0)$)
  \foreach [
  evaluate=\currentnode as \previousnode using \currentnode-1,
  evaluate=\currentnode as \nextnode using \currentnode+1
  ] \currentnode in {1,...,\numberofnodes} {
    let \p1 = ($(hullcoord\currentnode) - (hullcoord\previousnode)$),
    \n1 = {atan2(\y1,\x1) + 90},
    \p2 = ($(hullcoord\nextnode) - (hullcoord\currentnode)$),
    \n2 = {atan2(\y2,\x2) + 90},
    %\n{delta} = {Mod(\n2-\n1,360) - 360}
    \n{delta} = {mod(720+\n2-\n1,360) - 360}
    in 
    {arc [start angle=\n1, delta angle=\n{delta}, radius=#2]}
    -- ($(hullcoord\nextnode)!#2!-90:(hullcoord\currentnode)$) 
  }
}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  >=stealth,
  scale = .6
]
