% Chapter 2 Lasso for linear models
\textbf{2.}
The lasso 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \beta \\
  \text{To minimize} & \dfrac{1}{2N\mathstrut} \n{ y - X \beta }_2^2 \\
  \text{Such that} &  \n{ \beta }_1 \leq t
\end{array}
\]
can be put in \emph{Lagrangian} form 
\[ \argmin_\beta 
\dfrac{1}{2N} \n{ y - X \beta }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ \beta }_1.
\]
The $1/2N$ coefficient makes models of different sizes comparable (e.g., for cross-validation).

For a single predictor, 
the fit can be obtained by \emph{soft-thresholding} 
the least squares estimate
\[ 
S_\lambda(\beta) = 
\operatorname{sign}(\beta) 
\bigl( \va\beta - \lambda \bigr)_+ = 
\raisebox{-1.5mm}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=.2,xscale=.12]
\draw (-3,0) -- (3,0);
\draw (0,-1) -- (0,1);
\draw[ultra thick] (-3,-1) -- (-1,0) -- (1,0) -- (3,1);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}.
\]
Cyclical \emph{coordinate descent} 
updates the coefficients one at a time, 
keeping the others fixed, by soft-thresholding.

The \emph{degrees of freedom} of an estimator for an additive error model 
$ y_i = f(x_i) + \epsilon_i$, 
$f$ unknown, 
$\epsilon_i \sim N(0,\sigma^2)$, 
is 
\[
\text{df}( \hat y ) = \dfrac{1}{\sigma^2} \sum_i \cov( \hat y_i, y_i ).
\]

One could also consider $\ell^p$ penalties or constraints, 
with $0 \leq p < 1$, but the problem would no longer be convex.

\begin{centering}
<<LpBallsLassoBook,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## The ℓᵖ balls
op <- par( mfrow = c(1,4), mar = .5 * c(1,1,1,1), lend = 1 )

plot_level_curves <- function( levels = seq(0,sqrt(5),length=5)^2 ) { 
  f <- function(x,y) {
    theta <- pi/8
    u <- matrix( c( cos(theta), sin(theta), -sin(theta), cos(theta) ), 2, 2 ) %*% c(y-2,x-2)
    8 * u[1]^2 + u[2]^2
  }
  xs <- seq(-1,3,length=100)
  ys <- seq(-1,3,length=100)
  z <- outer( xs, ys, Vectorize(f) )
  contour( xs, ys, z, levels = levels, drawlabels=FALSE, add = TRUE, lwd=3, col="navy" )
}

## ℓ^2
thetas <- seq( 0, 2*pi, length=129 )
xs <- cos(thetas)
ys <- sin(thetas)
plot( NA, xlim = c(-1,2), ylim = c(-1,2), type="n", asp = 1, axes = FALSE )
polygon( xs, ys, col = "grey", lwd=3 )
abline( h=0, v=0 )
plot_level_curves( levels = seq(0,2.17,length=5)^2 )
theta <- 1.27
points( cos(theta), sin(theta), pch=15, cex=2, col="orange", lwd=3 )
text( 1,-1, expression( L ^ 2 ) )

## ℓ^1
plot( NA, xlim = c(-1,2), ylim = c(-1,2), type="n", asp = 1, axes = FALSE )
xs <- c(1,0,-1,0)
ys <- c(0,1,0,-1)
polygon( xs, ys, col = "grey", lwd=3 )
abline( h=0, v=0 )
plot_level_curves()
points( 0, 1, pch=15, cex=2, col="orange", lwd=3 )
text( 1,-1, expression( L ^ 1 ) )

## ℓ^.5
p <- 1/2
rs <- 1 / ( abs( cos(thetas) ) ^p + abs( sin(thetas) ) ^p ) ^ ( 1/p )
xs <- rs * cos(thetas)
ys <- rs * sin(thetas)
plot( NA, xlim = c(-1,2), ylim = c(-1,2), type="n", asp = 1, axes = FALSE )
polygon( xs, ys, col = "grey", lwd=3 )
abline( h=0, v=0 )
plot_level_curves()
points( 0, 1, pch=15, cex=2, col="orange", lwd=3 )
text( 1,-1, expression( L ^ .5 ) )

## ℓ^0
plot( NA, xlim = c(-1,2), ylim = c(-1,2), type="n", asp = 1, axes = FALSE )
xs <- c(1,0,0,0,-1,0,0,0)
ys <- c(0,0,1,0,0,0,-1,0)
polygon( xs, ys, col = "grey", lwd=3 )
abline( h=0, v=0 )
plot_level_curves()
points( 0, 1, pch=15, cex=2, col="orange", lwd=3 )
text( 1,-1, expression( L ^ 0 ) )

par(op)
@
\end{centering}

% Chapter 3: Generalized linear model
\textbf{3.} Generalized linear models (logistic, survival, Poisson, etc.) are estimated 
by minimizing the log-likelihood;
one can simply add an $\ell^1$ penalty to them.

Thanks to the $\ell^1$ penalty, 
overspecified models are not a problem: 
there is no need to encode factors as ``contrasts''.

%If a group of variables should be either all included or all excluded, 
%one can add an unsquared $\ell^2$ penalty for the group
%(\emph{group lasso})

The $\ell^1$ regularized SVM path presents jumps:
to avoid them, replace the hinge loss with its square;
it becomes very similar to logistic regression.
\[
\argmin_\beta \dfrac1N \sum \bigl( 1 - y_1 f_i(x_i | \beta ) \bigr)_+ ^2 + \lambda \n \beta _1.
\]

% Chapter 4: Generalizations of the lasso
\textbf{4.}
The \d{elastic net} combines $\ell^1$ and $\ell^2$ penalties.
An $\ell^1$ penalty alone does not work well when there are identical variables (the solution is not unique).

To have groups of variables enter the model or remain unselected in block, the \d{group lasso} adds their unsquared $\ell^2$ norm as a penalty.
Larger groups are more likely to be selected: 
to avoid that, weigh the penalties with the group size, e.g., 
$\sqrt p$ or (better) 
$\n Z _F$ (Frobenius norm of the design matrix of those factors).

% Computations: not read

To enforce sparsity inside each group, 
the \emph{sparse group lasso} 
adds an $\ell^1$ penalty in addition to the unsquared $\ell^2$ one.

Sometimes, those groups of variables may overlap 
(e.g., gene pathways).
The \emph{overlap group lasso} can also enforce 
a hierarchical structure, e.g., 
to allow interactions only if the main effects 
are already present.

Generalized additive models (GAM) are usually estimated by backfitting,
\( 
  f_j \leftarrow \text{Smooth}( y - 
\smash{\sum\limits_{k \neq j} \hat f_k} )
\);
they solve the optimization problem 
\[
(\hat f_1, \dots, \hat f_J) = 
\argmin_{f_1,\dots,f_J} E
\biggl[ 
  Y - \sum_j f_j( X_j ) 
\biggr]
^2.
\]
\d{Sparse GAM} (SPAM) adds an unsquared $\ell^2$ penalty 
and uses sparse backfitting: 
\[
\argmin_{f_1,\dots,f_J} E
\biggl[ 
  Y - \sum_j f_j( X_j ) 
\biggr]^2
+ \lambda \sum_j \n{ f_j }_2
\]
\begin{align*}
\tilde f_j & \leftarrow \text{Smooth} \biggl( y - \sum_{k\neq j } \hat f_k \biggr) \\
\hat f_j & \leftarrow \biggl( 1 - \dfrac{ \lambda }{ 
  \bigl\| \tilde f \bigr\| _2 
} \biggr)_+ \tilde f_j 
\end{align*}
% Remark: At the population lavel, this solves the problem; 
% on a sample, it is a good heuristic.

% COSSO Component selection and smoothing operator. 
% Not read. Based on RHHS. Older than SPAM.

The \d{fused lasso} approximates a time series with
a piecewise constant function 
\[ \argmin_\theta 
\dfrac12 \sum ( y_i - \theta_i) ^2 + \lambda \sum \va{ \theta_i - \theta_{i-1} }.
\]
It can be generalized to images,
non-equispaced observations,
regression with ordered predictors
($\va{\beta_i - \beta_{i-1}}$ -- nearby predictors tend to have the same coefficient),
piecewise affine or piecewise polynomial functions
(\emph{trend filtering}),
nearly isotonic regression 
$\n{ (\Delta \theta )_- }_1$.

Coordinate descent does not work for the fused lasso.
In the 1-dimensional case, one can start with the unpenalized estimate ($\lambda = 0$) 
and progressively fuse neighbooring intervals. 
One can also solve the dual (lifted) problem 
or use dynamic programming. 

% Chapter 5: Optimization
\textbf{5.}
The \emph{nuclear norm} (the sum of the singular values)
is a generalization of the $\ell^1$ norm: 
\( \n{ \diag x } _* = \n{ x }_1. \)
It is also a convex relaxation of the rank.

\emph{Descent methods} are iterative optimization algorithms: 
choose a direction 
(usually not far from the gradient, \ie, 
$\langle \nabla f, \text{dir} \rangle < 0 $);
make a small step in this direction; 
iterate.
Common direction choices include
\begin{align*}
  \text{dir} &= - \nabla f &&\text{(gradient descent)} \\
  \text{dir} &= - D^{-1} \nabla f &&\text{for some diagonal matrix $D$} \\
  \text{dir} &= - ( \nabla^2 f )^{-1} \nabla f &&\text{(Newton).}
\end{align*}
The step size is important: estimate it with a 1-dimensional optimization,
or with Armijo's rule.

In the presence of constraints,
the \emph{projected gradient} method 
simply projects the solution back onto the feasible set 
after each step (gradient descent is similar, with $\beta \in\RR^n$ instead of $\beta \in C$):
\[
\beta_{n+1} \! = \! \argmin_{ \beta \in C} 
f(\beta_n) + 
\bigl\langle \nabla f(\beta_n) , \beta - \beta_n \bigr\rangle + 
\dfrac{1}{2s} \n{ \beta - \beta_n} _2 ^2.
\]

If the objective is a sum of convex functions
$f = g + h$, one differentiable, one not, 
the \emph{generalized gradient update} 
linearizes $g$ but leaves $h$ as is.
\begin{align*}
\beta_{n+1} &= \argmin_\beta
g(\beta_n) + 
\bigl\langle \nabla g(\beta_n) , \beta - \beta_n \bigr\rangle + {}
\\
&\hphantom{= \argmin_\beta {}}
\dfrac{1}{2s} \n{ \beta - \beta_n} _2 ^2
+ h(\beta) \\
&= \operatorname{prox}_{sh} ( \beta_n - s \nabla g \beta_n )
\end{align*}
The \d{proximal} map generalizes the projection:
\begin{align*}
\operatorname{prox} _h z &= 
\argmin_\theta \tfrac12 \n{ z-\theta }_2^2 + h(\theta) \\
\operatorname{prox} _{I_C} z &= \text{projection of $z$ onto $C$} \\
\operatorname{prox} _{\lambda \n\cdot_1} z &= 
S_\lambda(z) \text{ elementwise soft-thresholding} \\
\operatorname{prox} _{\n\cdot_*} Z &= \text{singular value soft-thresholding}.
\end{align*}
Convergence is faster if $g$ is strongly convex.

The accelerated gradient descent, e.g., conjugate gradient, 
or \d{Nesterov momentum}
\begin{align*}
  \beta_{n+1} &= \theta_n - s \nabla f( \theta_n ) \\
\theta_{n+1} &= \beta_{n+1} + \dfrac{n}{n+1} ( \beta_{n1} - \beta_n )
\end{align*}
is faster (but non-monotonic).

\emph{Coordinate descent} 
(optimizing one coordinate at a time)
works if the non-differentiable part of $f$ is separable or, 
more generally if, when the directional derivatives along the axes are non-negative, then so are all the directional derivatives
(for a situation where it does not happen, 
take $f$ on $\RR^2$ with polygonal level curves:
coordinate descent cannot escape 
(acute) corners that remain inside one of the quadrants).

For linear regression, 
if the variables are centered and standardixed,
the lasso and the elastic net estimates 
can be computed from the OLS estimates
by shrinkage and soft-thresholding.

% not read

Coordinate descent is faster than the proximal gradient 
or Nesterov momentum.

\emph{Least angle regression} (LAR)
adds the variables one at a time but, 
contrary to stepwise regression, 
it only increases the coefficients until another variable becomes more promising.
If the algorithm also removes a variable 
from the active set when its coefficient drops to zero, 
it computes the lasso regularization path. 

For the alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM),
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & \beta , \theta \\
\text{To minimize} & f(\beta) + g(\theta) \\
\text{Such that} & A \beta + B \theta = c
\end{array}
\]
the augmented Lagrangian is (with $g$ small)
\begin{align*} 
L_\rho( \beta, \theta, \mu ) &= 
 f(\beta) + g(\theta) + 
\langle \mu, A \beta + B \theta - c \rangle 
+ {} \\
&\hphantom{={}}
 \dfrac{\rho}{2} 
\n{ A \beta + B \theta - c }_2^2.
\end{align*}
It can be solved by iterating 
\begin{align*}
  \beta_{n+1} &= \argmin_\beta L_\rho( \beta, \theta_n, \mu_n ) \\
\theta_{n+1} &= \argmin_\theta L_\rho( \beta_{n+1}, \theta, \mu_n )  \\
\mu_{n+1} &= \mu_n + \rho( A \beta_{n+1} + B \theta_{n+1} - c ).
\end{align*}
For the lasso
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \beta, \theta \\
  \text{To minimize} & \tfrac12 \n{ y - X \beta }_2^2 + \lambda \n{ \theta }_1 \\
  \text{Such that} & \beta - \theta = 0,
\end{array}
\]
$\beta$ is updated by a ridge regression, 
$\theta$ by soft thresholding
and $\mu$ linearly.

\d{Minorization-maximization} (MM) minimizes 
a (possibly non-convex) function $f$ by finding a function 
$\Psi: \RR^m \times \RR^n \to \RR$ such that 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \forall \beta, \theta  & f(\beta) \leq \Psi(\beta,\theta) \\
  \forall \beta          & f(\beta) = \Psi(\beta,\beta)
\end{array}
\]
and iterating
$ \beta_{n+1} = \argmin_\beta \Psi( \beta, \beta_n ). $

The proximal gradient can be seen as a MM (a Lipschitz gradient gives $\Psi$); 
in a different context, so can the expectation maximization (EM) algorithm
($\Psi$ comes from Jensen's inequality).

A function $f(\cdot,\cdot)$ is \emph{biconvex}
if $f(\cdot,\beta)$ and $f(\alpha,\cdot)$ are convex
(for instance, $f(\alpha,\beta) = (1-\alpha\beta)^2$);
a set $C \subset A \times B \subset \RR^n$ 
is biconvex if the sections 
$C_{\alpha, \cdot}$,
$C_{\cdot, \beta}$
are convex.
\emph{Alternate convex search} (ACS) 
successively optimizes each block;
the function values will converge, but the solution need not;
if it does, it is only to a \emph{partial optimum}.
For instance, the maximal singular vectors and values of a matrix
can be computed as (this is a generalization of the power method)
\begin{align*}
  f(\alpha, \beta, s ) &= \n{ X - s \alpha \beta' }_F^2 \\
\alpha_{n+1} &= \dfrac{ X \beta_n }{ \n{ X \beta_n }_2 } \\
\beta_{n+1} &= \dfrac{ X' \alpha_n }{ \n{ X' \alpha_n }_2 }.
\end{align*}

When there are millions of variables, 
one can use screening rules
(dual polytope projection (DPP), sequential DPP,
global string rule, sequential strong rule)
to reduce their number;
the less conservative rules make mistakes, 
but they can be corrected by checking the KKT conditions and adding the variables that violate them.

% Chapter 6 Statistical inference
\textbf{6.}
It is possible to design statistical tests for the lasso: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bayesian (MCMC) simulations, after choosing a prior
  (for instance, the number of variables in the model 
  is often assumed uniform over 
  $\llbracket 0, k \rrbracket$, with $k \ll p$)
  [this is unrelated to the fact that the lasso is the posterior model (MAP estimator) for a Laplace prior];
\item Bootstrap (even slower, but it scales better);
\item \d{Covariance test}: the change in $\cov(\hat y,y)$
  as a variable enters the model is asymptotically $\text{Exp}(1)$;
contrary to stepwise regression, adaptivity and shrinkage (asymptotically) compensate each other;
\item Exact (finite sample) tests for adaptive models 
  whose selection event can be written $Ay \leq b$
  (this includes lasso and stepwise regression)
  % Not read in detail
\end{compactitem}
One can also debias the lasso estimate to compute confidence intervals.

% Chapter 7 Matrix decompositions
\textbf{7.}
The singular value decomposition (SVD) 
provides the best rank-$r$ approximation 
of a matrix $Z$: 
\[ U D_r V' = \argmax_{ M \ :\ \rank M = r } \n{ Z - M }_F \]
but missing values complicate the situation.

The \emph{iterated SVD} (fill in the missing values, 
compute the rank $r$ approximation from the SVD, iterate)
tends to overfit.

The \emph{soft-thresholding iterated SVD}
(fill in the missing values, 
compute $U S_\lambda(D) V'$, iterate; 
you can also let $\lambda$ decrease at each step)
solves the problem 
\[ 
\argmin_M \tfrac12 \n{ Z - M }_F^2 + \lambda \n{ M } _*
\]
where the Frobenius norm is taken 
over the non-missing entries of $Z$.

The biconvex problem 
\[
\argmin_{A,B}
\n{ Z - A B' }_F^2 + 
\lambda ( \n A _F^2 + \n B _F^2 )
\]
gives a 2-dimensional family of matrix factorizations,
indexed by $(r,\lambda)$
($r$ is the number of columns of $A$ and~$B$).

A vector-valued regression 
$Y = X \Theta + E$
can be estimated with the group lasso,
which sets whole rows of $\Theta$ to zero,
or with a nuclear norm penalty, to make it low rank.
\[ \argmin_\Theta \n{ Y - X \Theta }_F^2 + \lambda \n\Theta_* \]

These models (matrix completion, low rank regression)
can be written 
$y_i = \tr( X_i ' \Theta ) + \epsilon$
for various choices of the matrices $X_i$, 
and estimated as
\[ \argmin_\Theta \dfrac1N \sum \bigl( y_i - \tr( X_i'\Theta) \bigr)^2 + \lambda \n\Theta_*. \]

The penalized SVD,
\[
\argmin_{ U,D,V} 
\n{ Z - UDV'} _F^2 + \lambda_1 \n{U}_1 + \lambda_2 \n{V}_1
\]
gives sparse singular vectors
(the rank-1 problem is biconvex 
and can be solved by alternating soft-thresholding;
for the rank-$r$ problem, start again with 
$Z - u d v'$).

Additive matrix decompositions express a matrix $Z$ as a sum, 
e.g., of low-rank, sparse and small (noise) matrices: 
\[ 
\argmin_{L,S} 
\n{ Z - (L+S) }_F^2 + \lambda_1\n L _* + \lambda_2 \n S _1.
\]
For row-wise sparsity
(\ie, to model row-wise corruption),
replace 
$\n S _1$ with $\sum \n{ S_{i \cdot } }_2$. 

Factor analysis
models the data as 
$y_i = \mu + \Gamma u_i + \epsilon_i$,
$\epsilon_i \sim N(0,S)$,
which gives 
$\var y = \Gamma \Gamma' + S$.
If $S$ is scalar, principal component analysis (PCA) recovers $\Gamma$; if not, one can use the low rank + sparse + noise decomposition. 

% Chapter 8 Sparse multivariate methods
\textbf{8.}
Most statistical procedures can be formulated as an optimization problem: 
adding an $\ell^1$ penalty (or both $\ell^1$ and $\ell^2$ penalties) sparsifies them.
It may be necessary to reformulate the problem to get a convex, 
or biconvex, or less nasty problem. 
Different reformulations give different results. 

% PCA
\textbf{8a.}
The first principal component is
the direction in which the variance is maximal
\[ 
  \argmax_{ v \ :\ \n v _2 = 1 }
  \var X v = 
  \argmax_{ v \ :\ \n v _2 = 1 }
  v' X' X v;
\]
it can be sparsified as 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & u, v \\
\text{To maximize} & u' X v \\
\text{Such that} & \n u _2 = \n v _2 = 1 , \quad \n v _1 \leq t
\end{array}
\]
(solvable with SVD and thresholding) or 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & M \succcurlyeq 0 \\
\text{To maximize} & \tr( X' X M ) \\
\text{Such that} & \tr M = 1 , \quad \tr( \va M E ) \leq t^2.
\end{array}
\]

The first principal component can also be defined as the direction minimizing the reconstruction error, and sparsified as 
\[
\argmin _{ v, \theta \ : \ \n \theta _2 = 1 }
\dfrac1N \sum \n{ x_i - u u' x_i }_2 ^2 
+ \lambda_1 \n v _1
+ \lambda_2 \n v _2 ^2.
\]
Auto-encoders also minimize the reconstruction error 
and are easy to sparsify.

For the next principal component,
since orthogonality may conflict with sparsity, 
one may consider 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & u_k, v_k \\
  \text{To miximize} & u' _k X v_k  \\
  \text{such that} & \n{ v_k }_2 \leq 1, \quad \n{u_k}_2 \leq 1 \\
  & \n{ v_k }_1 \leq c \\
  & \forall j \in \llbracket 1, k-1 \rrbracket \ 
  u_k ' u_k = 0.
\end{array}
\]

Classical PCA is consistent
when $N \to \infty$, $p/N \to 0$,
but not (at all) if $N, p \to \infty$,
$p/N \to c > 0$.
Thresholding the diagonal of the sample variance matrix
and computing the PCA in this lower-dimensional space 
is actually consistent. 

% CCA
\textbf{8b.}
Canonical correlation analysis (CCA) solves the problem
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \beta, \theta \\
  \text{To maximize} & \cov ( X \beta, Y \theta ) \\
  \text{Such that} & \var X\beta = \var Y \theta = 1
\end{array}
\]
e.g., via SVD or ALS;
it can be sparsified by adding $\ell^1$ and $\ell^2$ 
constraints and solved by alternating soft-thresholding.

% LDA 
\textbf{8c.}
Linear discriminant analysis (LDA) 
can be presented in three different ways, 
which can be sparsified as usual: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Model the classes with Gaussian distributions, 
  with different means but the same variance 
  (the naive Bayes classifier is the special case 
  when the variance matrix is diagonal);
\item Fisher: find a low-dimensional projection 
  in which the between-class variance is large wrt
  the within-class variance, \ie, 
  \[ \argmax_\beta \dfrac{\beta' \Sigma_b \beta}{\beta' \Sigma_w \beta}; \]
\item Optimal scoring: 
  LDA with two classes is a linear regression 
  of the binary response;
  with more classes, it is still a linear regression, 
  provided we assign a number to each class, in an optimal way.
\end{compactitem}

% Clustering
\textbf{8d.}
Hierarchical clustering does not work well in the presence of many uninformative variables: 
\emph{sparse hierarchical clustering} assigns (sparse) weights to the variables and applies the usual clustering algorithms on the resulting weighted dissimilarity matrix.

\d{Convex clustering} looks for prototypes, 
close to the points, 
and close to one another 
(with an $\ell^1$ loss, so that many are actually equal),
\[
\argmin_{u_1,\dots,u_n}
\sum \n{ x_1 - u_i }_2 ^2 + 
\lambda \sum_{i<j} \n{ u_i - u_j }_q,
\quad q \in \{ 1,2 \}.
\]

$K$-means can be sparsified by maximizing the between-cluster 
sum of squares
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & 
  \mathscr C_1 \sqcup \cdots \sqcup \mathscr C_K = 
  \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket, \quad w \in \RR^p \\
  \text{To maximize}\!\!\! & \displaystyle
    \sum_{j=1}^p \! w_j 
    \biggl(
    \dfrac1N \!\sum_{i, i' = 1}^N  \! d_{i i' j} - 
    \!\sum_k \dfrac1{N_k} 
    \!\!\sum_{ i, i' \in \mathscr C_k } \!\!
    d_{i i' j}
  \biggr) \\
  \text{Such that} & \n w _2 \geq 1 , \quad \n w _1 \leq s , \quad w \geq 0
\end{array}
\]
and solved with an alternating algorithm (soft-thresholding and weighted $k$-means).

% Chapter 9: Graphical models
\textbf{9.}
Undirected graphs can encode dependence properties between 
random variables (one per vertex) 
in the following equivalent ways 
(\emph{Hammersley-Clifford theorem}):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The joint probability distribution function factorizes 
  over the cliques 
  \[ P(x_1,\dots,x_p ) \propto \prod_{ C \in \text{Cliques} }
  \psi_C( x_C ); \]
\item The random variable $X$ has the Markov property over the graph: for all cut set $S$ separating the graph into connected components $A$ and $B$, 
$X_A \!\indep\! X_B \!\mid\! X_S$. 
\end{compactitem}

For instance, one can model how politicians vote as follows
\begin{align*}
  X_i & : \text{vote of politician $i$} \\
  \operatorname{sign} \theta_i &: \text{whether $i$ is likely to vote yes} \\
  \operatorname{sign} \theta_{ij} &: \text{whether $i$ and $j$ tend to agree} \\
  P & \propto \exp \Bigl( \sum \theta_i x_i + \sum \theta_{ij} x_i x_j \Bigr)
\end{align*}
(the graph is complete, but only cliques up to size 2 are used).

A multivariate Gaussian model is a graphical model, with only vertex and edge factors; 
the adjacency matrix of the graph is the sparsity pattern of the concentration (or precision) matrix $\Theta = \Sigma^{-1}$.

The Gaussian likelihood can be penalized 
(\d{graphical lasso})
\[
\argmax_{ \Theta \succcurlyeq 0 }
\log \det \Theta - \tr \hat \Sigma \Theta - \lambda \rho_1(\Theta) \]
where $\rho_1$ is the $\ell^1$ norm of the off-diagonal elements
and $\lambda = 2 \sqrt{ \dfrac{ \log p }{ N } }$.
The problem is convex and can be solved efficiently 
by blockwise coordinate descent 
(fix everything except a row (and the corresponding column); 
each step is a lasso regression).
If the solution has a block structure, it can be identified before the optimization 
($
%\va{ \hat \Theta _{ij} }
\bigl| \hat \Theta _{ij} \bigr|
\leq \lambda$
whenever $i$ and $j$ are in different blocks)
and the blocks can be solved independently.

Alternatively, one can estimate the neighbourhood of a random variable as a small set of variables that gives a good prediction, using the lasso
(and the \textsc{and} or \textsc{or} rule: 
keep an edge $(s,t)$ if both/either 
$s \in \mathscr{N} (t)$
and/or
$t \in \mathscr N (s)$).
For discrete variables, use the logistic lasso.

It is possible to mix continuous and discrete variables
\begin{align*} 
  \log P 
  &= \sum \gamma_s x_s - \tfrac12 \sum \theta_{st} x_s x_t 
  && \text{continuous} \\
  & \hphantom{={}} + \sum \rho_{sj} (y_j) x_s 
  && \text{mixed} \\
  & \hphantom{={}} + \sum \psi_{jr} ( y_j, y_r )
  && \text{discrete}
\end{align*}
through the \emph{pseudo-likelihood}
(the product of the neighbourhood likelihoods).

Omitting hidden variables makes a sparse model look dense.
In this case, the concentration matrix can be written as a sum $K = \Theta + L$, 
with $\Theta$ sparse and $L$ low rank (from the block inverse formula):
\[
\argmin_{\substack{
  \Theta, L \\
  \Theta - L \succ 0 \\
  L \succ 0
}}
\tr \hat \Sigma ( \Theta - L ) 
- \log \det ( \Theta - L )
+ \lambda \n{ \Theta }_1
+ \tr L.
\]

% Chapter 10: Compressed sensing
\textbf{10.}
The best sparse approximation,
in an orthonormal basis (Fourier, wavelets)
is obtained by keeping the $k$ largest coefficients.
One can also combine several bases, 
e.g., Fourier $\Phi$ to capture periodic patterns and smooth changes, and Haar $\Psi$ for sharp changes, with a lasso constraint
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \alpha, \beta \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ \theta - \Phi \alpha - \Psi \beta }_2 \\
  \text{Such that} & \n{ \alpha }_1 + \n{ \beta }_1 \leq R.
\end{array}
\]
The exact reconstruction problem is even simpler: 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \alpha, \beta \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ \alpha }_1 + \n{ \beta }_1 \\
  \text{Such that} &  \theta = \Phi \alpha - \Psi \beta,
\end{array}
\]

\d{Compressed sensing} reconstructs a sparse signal $\theta$ 
(e.g., the signal of interest, in a basis in which we expect it to be sparse) from a small number of random projections $z_i ' \theta$. The random projection matrix can have Gaussian iid entries, Bernoulli $\pm1$ iid entries, or be a random submatrix of the Fourier matrix 
$ \bigl( \exp( 2 \pi i k \ell / n ) \bigr)_ { 1 \leq k, \ell \leq n }$.

% [not read] Conditions under which $\ell^0$ and $\ell^1$ reconstructions are equivalent.

% Chapter 11 Theoretical results. Not read

In R, check the following packages: 
\texttt{glmnet} (GLM),
\texttt{genlasso} (fused lasso),
\texttt{monomvn::blasso} (Bayesian lasso, bootstrap),
\texttt{softImpute} (SVD with missing values),
\texttt{penalizedLDA}, \texttt{sparseLDA},
\texttt{cvxcluster} (convex clustering).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Selected applications of convex optimization} \\
  L. Li (2015)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.} Given the primal problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \\ 
  \text{To minimize} & f(x) \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ g_i(x) \leq 0 \\
  & \forall j \ h_j(x) = 0,
\end{array}
\]
define the Lagrangian and the dual function as
\begin{align*}
  L(x,\alpha,\beta) &= f(x) + \sum \alpha_i g_i(x) + \sum \beta_j h_j(x) \\
  q(\alpha,\beta) &= \inf_x L(x, \alpha, \beta);
\end{align*}
the dual problem is then 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \alpha, \beta \\ 
  \text{To maximize} & q(\alpha,\beta)\\
  \text{Such that} & \alpha \geq 0.
\end{array}
\]
% The KKT (sufficient) conditions are 
% \[ L( x^*, \alpha, \beta) \leq L(x^*, \alpha^*, \beta^* ) \leq L( x, \alpha^*, \beta^* ). \]

\textbf{2.} A support vector machine (SVM) can be fit by 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w, b \\ 
  \text{To maximize} & 1 / \n{w}_2^2 \text{\quad(width of the band)} \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \in L^+ \ w' x_i + b \geq 1 \\
                   & \forall i \in L^- \ w' x_i + b \leq -1,
\end{array}
\]
which can be written as a convex problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w, b \\ 
  \text{To mimimize} & \n{w}_2^2 \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ y_i ( w' x_i + b ) \geq 1
\end{array}
\]
where $y_i = +1$ if $i \in L^+$ and $-1$ otherwise.
To find the dual problem, 
write the Lagrangian 
\[ 
L(z,b,\alpha) = \dfrac12 \n{ w }_2^2 - \sum \alpha_i \bigl( y_1 ( w' x_i + b ) - 1 \bigr);
\]
use the KKT conditions,
$\partial L / \partial w = 0$ and
$\partial L / \partial b = 0$,
\ie, $w = \sum \alpha_i y_i x_i$ 
and $\sum \alpha_i y_i = 0$
to compute the dual function $q(\alpha)$:
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \alpha\\ 
  \text{To maximize} & 
  \displaystyle
  \sum^{}_{} \alpha_i - \dfrac12 \sum \alpha_i \alpha_j y_i y_j x_i' x_j \\
  \text{Such that} & \alpha \geq 0 \\
                   & \displaystyle \sum_{}^{} \alpha_i y_i = 0.
\end{array}
\]

There are many variants, such as the soft-margin SVM
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w, b, \xi \\ 
  \text{To mimimize} & \n{w}_2^2 + c \sum \xi_i^2 \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ y_i ( w' x_i + b ) \geq 1 - \xi_i \\
  & \forall i \ \xi_i \geq 0
\end{array}
\]
or the multiclass SVM (with $t_i = [x_i, 1]$)
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & v_1,\dots,v_k,\xi \\ 
  \text{To mimimize} & \tfrac12 \sum v_k' v_k + c \sum \xi_i^2 \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i,k \ 
  v'_{y_i} t_i - v_k' t_i \geq \delta_{y_i, k} - \xi_i.
\end{array}
\]

\emph{Sequential minimal optimization} (SMO) tries to reduce memory usage by optimizing two $\alpha_i$'s at a time.

One can also regularize SVMs,
by replacing $\sum \xi_i$ with 
$\sum \xi_i^2$ (LS-SVM), 
or by a constraint such as 
$\sum \xi_i^2 = L \sigma^2$ (Morozov) 
or $w' w = \pi^2$ (Ivanov).

\textbf{3.}
A mixture model 
\begin{align*} 
  f(x,\theta) &= \sum \pi_k f(x, \theta_k) \\
  z_i(k) &= \mathbf 1 _{ \text{$x_i$ comes from the $k$th component} }
\end{align*}
can be estimated by expectation maximization (EM): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item E-step: estimate the distribution 
  (not the values -- that is a common mistake) 
  of $z_i(k)$ from $(\pi,\theta)$: 
  \begin{align*}
    \gamma_i(k) &= P[ \text{$x_i$ comes form the $k$th component} ] \\ 
    &= E[ z_i( k ) ] \\
    &= \dfrac{
      \pi_k f(x_i, \theta_k) 
    }{
      \sum_j \pi_j f(x_i, \theta_j)
    }
  \end{align*}
\item M-step: estimate $(\pi,\theta)$ from $\gamma$ via maximum likelihood.
\end{compactitem}

The general EM algorithm estimates a parameter of interest $\theta$ and integrates out nuisance parameters (latent variables or missing values) $z$, by estimating their posterior distribution $q_i(z_i)$. The log-likelihood can be written 
\begin{align*}
  \log L(\theta) 
  &= \sum_i \log p(x_i, \theta) \\
  &= \sum_i \log \sum_{z_i} p(x_i, z_i, \theta) \\
  &= \sum_i \log \sum_{z_i} q_i(z_i) \dfrac{ p(x_i, z_i, \theta) }{ q_i(z_i) } \\
  &\geq \sum_i \sum_{z_i} q_i(z_i) \log \dfrac{ p(x_i, z_i, \theta) }{ q_i(z_i) } \qquad \text{(Jensen)}\\
  &= J(q,\theta)
\end{align*}
and maximized by coordinate descent: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item E-step: $q_m = \argmax_q J(q,\theta_m)$
  (we do not estimate the nuisance parameters $z_i$ 
  but their distributions $q_i$;
  after this step, the inequality is actually an equality)
\item M-step: $\theta_{m+1} = \argmax_\theta J(q_m,\theta)$.
\end{compactitem}

The book then gives probabilistic principal component analysis (PCA with missing values) as an example, but they make the common mistake of filling in the missing values.

\textbf{4.} Adding a penalty to the objective, 
e.g., $\lambda \n{ x }_1$, is equivalent to adding
a constraint $\n{ x }_1 \leq C$, where $C=\n{ x^*}_1$ 
and $x^*$ is the solution of the penalized problem.

% \textbf{5.} % SDP. Application to Lyapunov stability of ODEs.

\textbf{6.} The SDP (semidefinite programming) relaxation 
of a non-convex quadratically constrained quadratic program 
(QCQP, e.g., $x\in\{\pm1\}$ can be written $x^2=1$) is obtained 
by introducing a matrix $X=xx'$ and relaxing that equality to 
$X \succcurlyeq xx'$, \ie, 
\[ \begin{pmatrix}1&x'\\x&X\end{pmatrix} \succcurlyeq 0. \]

The \emph{reformulation-linearization technique} (RLT)
also relaxes $X=xx'$, but start with the inequalities 
$\ell_i \leq x_i \leq u_i$, 
\ie, $x_i - \ell_i \geq 0$ and $u_i - x_i \geq 0$,
multiples them, and replaces $x_i x_j$ with $x_{ij}$.

\textbf{7.} The minimum volume ellipsoid covering $n$ points 
$x_1,\dots,x_n \in \RR^d$ is defined by 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & A, \mu \\ 
  \text{To mimimize} & \det Q \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ (x_i-\mu)' Q^{-1} (x_i-\mu) \leq 1 \\ 
  & Q \succ 0
\end{array}
\]
and can be recast as a convex problem
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & M, m \\ 
  \text{To mimimize} & - \log \det M \\
  \text{Such that} & \forall i \ (M x_i-m)' (M x_i-m) \leq 1 \\ 
  & M \succ 0
\end{array}
\]
where $M = Q^{-1/2}$ and $m = Q^{-1/2}\mu$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parsing algorithms} \\
\end{book}

\d{Recursive descent} is the most straightforward 
  top-down parsing algorithm:
  start with the root of the tree, 
  try the first possible derivation, and continue,
  backtracking after each failure
  (it does not work with left recursive (LR) grammars, 
  \ie, those with 
  a rule of the form 
  $S \to^+ S \alpha$, where $\to^+$ 
  means a series of derivations).
\emph{Parser combinators} (Parsec, Spirit) 
  are a way to implement recursive descent parsing 
  in functional languages,
  from simple building blocks 
  (parsers, \ie, functions that convert strings to parse trees)
  and glue -- higher order functions).

\d{Parsing expression grammars} (PEG) are an alternative to context-free grammars (CFG): 
  the choice operator \verb+|+ is ordered; 
  the \verb+*+ operator is greedy and does not backtrack after failure; 
  there are \verb+&+ (\textsc{and}) and \verb+!+ (\textsc{not}) operators
    (they do not consume any input and allow unlimited look-ahead).
\emph{Packrat} is a linear-time recursive descent algorithm with memoization
for PEG.

The \d{Earley} algorithm is similar to recursive descent, 
but explores the set of possible parse trees breadth-first, not depth-first.

The \d{CYK} algorithm is a bottom-up dynamic programming based parsing algorithm, 
building a boolean table in $O(n^3)$: $T_{ijk}$ indicates if $s_{i..j}$ 
can be derived from rule $R_k$
(it assumes the grammar is in Chomsky normal form (CNF)
\ie, the derivation rules are of the form 
$A\to\alpha$ or $A\to BC$).

A grammar is LL($k$) if, whenever we hesitate between two derivation rules, 
we can decide by looking $k$ tokens ahead. 
An \d{LL} parser (e.g., ANTLR) precomputes those decisions and puts them in a table
(the table is exponential in the worst case, but not in practice).

\d{LR} parsers are a bottom-up analogue of the top-down LL parsers: 
they use a transition table
that maps 
  parser state and next token(s)
to 
  a next state and an action; 
the action can be ``shift'' (consume one token) 
or ``reduce'' (do not, but apply one of the rules).
Variants include LALR(1)  
(look-ahead LR(0), yacc/bison), 
SLR (simple LR), 
GLR (generalized LR).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{QuantifQuantile: an R package\\ for performing quantile regression \\through optimal quantization} \\
  I. Charlier et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Nonparametric quantile regression $Y \sim X$ can be estimated
with a variant of the $k$ nearest neighbours: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Find a set (or grid) $\gamma$ of $N$ points to minimize 
  $E[ \va{ X - X^\gamma } ^p ] ^ {1/p}$,
  where $X^\gamma$ is the projection of $X$ onto $\gamma$
  (start with a random grid $\gamma$ and 
  move each grid point towards the average 
  of the observations assigned to it); 
\item Approximate the conditional quantile of $Y | X$ as 
  \[ 
    \hat q_\alpha( y | x ) = 
    \argmin_{ a \in \RR }
    E[\, \rho_\alpha (Y-a) \mid X^\gamma = x^\gamma \, ]
  \]
\item Bootstrap to choose $N$;
\item Bootstrap for a smoother estimate.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{treeClust: an R package\\ for tree-based clustering dissimilarities} \\
S.E. Buttrey and L.R. Whitaker
\end{book}

Tree-based distances
are insensitive to scaling and provide 
automatic variable selection: 
use the proportion of trees 
(predict each variable with the others) 
in which the two observations are in the same leaf;
refine using tree quality and node distance. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An R package for the panel approach method for program evaluation: pampe} \\
A. Vega-Bayo
\end{book}

To estimate the effect of a policy (``program evaluation'') 
from observed panel data, with treated and control groups,
consider the effect of treatment/non-treatment 
on the control/treated group 
as missing values, 
and model the panel data with a dynamic factor model. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A significance test for the lasso} \\
  R. Lockhart et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To test if the inclusion of a variable along the regularization path is significant, use 
\[
  T_k = \dfrac{
    \langle 
      y, 
      X \hat \beta( \lambda_{k+1} ) - 
      X_{A_{k-1}} \tilde\beta_{A_{k-1}}( \lambda_{k+1}) 
    \rangle
  }{
    \sigma^2
  }
  \xrightarrow{\ d\ }
  %\longrightarrow 
  \text{Exp}(1)
\]
where $\hat \beta(\lambda)$ is the lasso estimator, 
$A_{k-1}$ is the set of variables in the model just before $\lambda_{k}$,
$\tilde \beta_A(\lambda)$ is the lasso estimator on the variables in $A$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Automatically improving accuracy\\ for floating point expressions} \\
  P. Panchekha (2015)
\end{book}
\emph{Herbie} is a Racket library that rewrites floating point
expressions to improve their precision 
(e.g., $e^x - e^{-x}$ for $x$ small, 
or $\log( 1 + e^x )$ for $x$ large): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sample the possible inputs, 
  uniformly among floating point numbers 
  (not uniformly over some inverval of $\RR$);
\item Estimate the precision of each intermediate result, 
  by comparing it with an arbitrary precision computation 
  (progressively increase the precision until it is sufficient);
\item Apply (more than a hundred) rewrite rules
  (e.g., associativity, but also $\sqrt{x+1}-\sqrt{x} \mapsto 1/(\sqrt{1+x}+\sqrt{x})$);
  simplify;
\item Also try series expansions;
\item Choose which rewritten formula to use depending on the values of the inputs.
\end{compactitem}

% Does not work for softplus: 
% softplus(x) = log(1+exp(x))  # Overflows for x large (x > 800)
% By hand, other people found: 
% softplus(x) = x < 18.0 ? log1p(exp(x)) : x < 33.3 ? x + exp(-x) : x

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TOL manual} \\
  (2013)
\end{book}
TOL (time-oriented language) is a programming language with predefined types to describe time series: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Date and Time (confusingly, it is the same type);
  % Fortunately, there are no time zones
\item Infinite sets of dates (e.g., the first day of each month),
  with operations on them (union, intersection, set difference, membership, next-element, restriction, offset);
\item Unlimited time series, with a few building blocks (indicator, step function, affine function, random number generator)
and operations ($+$, $-$, $\times$, $/$, coalesce, lag, arima, etc.).
\end{compactitem}
The package repository has a few dozen items, but many are only documented in Spanish. 

It is not clear why this DSL was not built on an existing language (Haskell, OCaml, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mathematica: a problem-centered approach} \\
  R. Hazrat (2010)
\end{book}
Here are a few examples of the syntax of the language.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Square brackets are used for function arguments, 
  curly braces for lists; matrices are lists of lists; 
\item Function definitions use \texttt{:=}, 
  while \texttt{=} creates frozen assignments: 
  compare \texttt{x:=Random[]} and \texttt{x=Random[]};
\item Function arguments are indicated by \texttt{\_}, 
  and they can be given an optional name, e.g., \verb|f[n_]:=n^2|;
\item Function application can be written \texttt{f[x]}, 
  \texttt{f@x} or \texttt{x//f}; 
  the pipe operator is often used to format the output, e.g., 
  \texttt{Inverse[A] // MatrixForm};
\item Anonymous functions end with \texttt{\&} 
  (it is not always clear where they start)
  and their arguments are called \verb|#1|, \verb|#2|, etc.
  (or \verb|#| of there is only one),
  e.g., 
  \verb|(#^2+1)&[5]| or \verb|5//(#^2)&|;
\item The map function is \texttt{/@}, e.g., \texttt{f /@ Range[5]}; the function is often anonymous, e.g., 
\verb|#^2&| \texttt{/@} \texttt{Range[5]};
\item The fold function is \texttt{Apply} or \texttt{@@}, e.g., 
  \texttt{Plus @@ Range[5]}
  (it actually replaces the head of the rhs with the lhs); 
  its vectorized form, for lists of lists, is \texttt{@@@};
\item The \texttt{Table} function provides a weak form of 
  list comprehension, e.g., \verb|Table[3n^5+11, {n,1,20}]|;
\item The substitution operator is \texttt{./}, e.g., 
  \texttt{x+y ./ x->2};
  \texttt{.//} applies the rules until nothing changes, e.g., 
\texttt{x+y .// \{x->y,y->z\}};
\item It is possible to specify the type and properties of the function arguments or substitution, e.g., 
\verb|f[x_Integer?EvenQ]:=x/2|; 
  \verb|__| matches a non-empty sequence, 
  \verb|___| a sequence;
\item \texttt{If} can be written \texttt{/;}, e.g., 
\verb|f[n_]:=Sqrt[n] /; n>0|.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A thread-safe arbitrary precision\\ computation package} \\
  D.H. Bailey (2015)
\end{book}
Implementation details for a multiprecision library -- 
usefil for ODEs (e.g., the Lorentz attractor loses 
16 digits in each period) and experimental mathematics.
Alternatives include: 
\textsc{gmp}, \textsc{mpfr} 
(and languages that use it, e.g., Julia), 
Pari/GP, mpmath (Python), Sage.

Use Newton iteration for square roots
\( x \leftarrow x + \tfrac12( 1 - x^2 a ) x, \)
$n$th roots, inverses
\( x \leftarrow x + ( 1 + xb) x, \)
logarithm 
\( x \leftarrow x - ( e^x - a ) / e^x, \)
arcsine; 
Taylor for exponential (for $x$ small), 
sine (for $x$ small -- use the double angle formula to bring it back to its initial value in $(-\pi/4,\pi/4]$).
For multiplication, use the elementary school algorithm (in base $2^{48}$)
or the FFT 
(the digits of $ab$, before accounting for carries, 
are the convolution of those of $a$ and $b$).

For much higher precision (thousands to millions of digits),
algorithms based on the \emph{arithmetic-geometric mean} (AGM),
being quadratically convergent (the number of correct digits doubles at each iteration), become competitive.
% They are also used for constants, e.g., $\pi$.
% A few special functions, $J_\nu, $\Gamma(a)$, 
% $\Gamma(a,x)$, $\zeta(s)$ are also detailed. 

