Applications of the PSLQ algorithms to situations where
the numeric estimation of the number of interest is non-trivial.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using integer relation algorithms\\ for finding relationships among functions} \\
  M. Chamberland (2007)
\end{book}
The PSLQ algorithm can also find integer relations between functions: evaluate them at some random point; apply PSLQ; try another point to make sure.
% List of examples with Maple code.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A=B} \\
  M. Petkov\v sek et al. (1997)
\end{book}
A geometric series is a series $\sum t_n$ in which the ratio of two consecutive terms $t_{n+1}/t_n$ is constant.
A \d{hypergeometric series} is a series $\sum t_n$ in which the ratio of two consecutive terms $t_{n+1}/t_n$ is a rational function of $n$.
If 
\[ \dfrac{ t_{n+1} }{ t_n } = 
  \dfrac{ 
    (n+a_1) \cdots (n+a_p) 
  }{
    (n+b_1) \cdots (n+b_q)
  } \dfrac{ x }{ n+1 },
\]
it is usually written 
\[
  {}_pF_{\!q} \left( 
    \substack{a_1\dots a_p \\ b_1 \dots b_q} ; 
    x 
  \right) 
  = 
  {}_pF_{\!q}( \mathbf a, \mathbf b; x ) 
  = \sum_{n \geq 0} t_n.
\]
There are lists of hypergeometric identities, 
but expecting to be able to evaluate arbitrary hypergeometric series from those lists is hopeless. 

\textbf{1.} Given a double hypergeometric term $F(n,k)$, \ie, 
both $F(n+1,k)/F(n,k)$ and $F(n,k+1)/F(n,k)$ 
are rational functions, 
\d{Sister Celine's algorithm} looks for a recurrence relation 
\[ \sum_{i=0}^I \sum_{j=0}^J a_{ij}(n) F(n-j,k-i) = 0 \]
as follows: 
divide the recurrence relation by $F(n,k)$; 
simplify; only rational functions remain:
put them on the same denominator; 
the numerator is a polynomial in $k$ and its coefficients should be zero: solve the corresponding system; 
if it does not have any non-trivial solution, 
increase $I$ and $J$ and try again.
By summing over $k$, we get a recurrence relation for 
$f(n) = \sum_k F(n,k)$; 
solve it with the Hyper algorithm.

(The algorithm does not scale well: prefer the equivalent Zeilberger algorithm.)

\textbf{2.} Given a hypergeometric term $(t_k)_k$, 
\ie, $r_k = t_{k+1} / t_k$ is a rational function of $k$,
\d{Gosper's algorithm} looks for another hypergeometric term $(z_k)_k$
such that $\Delta z = t$, \ie, $z_{n+1} - z_n = t_n$; 
this gives a simple formula for the partial sums 
$s_n = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} t_k = z_n - z_0$.

Since it computes an antiderivative of a hypergeometric series, 
it can be seen as an analogue of 
the Risch algorithm (differential Galois theory) 
for hypergeometric series. 

The details are technical: 
write 
\[ r+n = \dfrac{ a_n }{ b_n } \dfrac{ c{_n+1} }{ c_n } \]
with $a,b,c$ polynomials and 
$\forall k \ \text{gcd}(a_n,b_{n+k}) = 1$; 
find a polynomial $x_n$, if it exists, such that
$a_n x_{n+1} - b_{n-1} x_n = c_n$; 
let \[ z_n - \dfrac{ b_{n-1} x_n }{c_n} t_n. \]

\textbf{3.} \d{Zeilberger's algorithm} (aka creative telescoping)
is a (faster) variant of Sister Celine's algorithm,
to find a telescoping recurrence relation 
\[ F(n+1,k) - F(n,k) = G(n,k+1) - G(n,k) \]
or, more generally, 
\[ \sum_{j=0}^J a_j(n) F(n+j,k) = G(n,k+1) - G(n,k). \]
It is guaranteed to exist if $F$ is a proper hypergeometric term: 
\[ F(n,k) = P(n,k) \dfrac{
  \displaystyle
  \prod_{i=1}^U ( a_i n + b_i k + c_i ) !
}{
  \displaystyle
  \prod_{j=1}^{V\vrule width 0pt height 1.3ex} ( u_i n + v_i k + w_i ) !
} x^k, \]
where $P$ is a polynomial, $U$, $V$, $a_i$, $b_i$, $u_j$, $v_j$ are integers ($c_i$ and $w_j$ are arbitrary).

Summing over $k$ gives a recurrence relation for 
$s_n = \sum_k F(n,k)$
(the rhs cancels out if $G$ has finite support in $k$ for all $n$).

\textbf{4.} To prove hypergeometric identities, 
e.g., $\sum_k F(n,k) = 1$,
the \d{Wilf-Zeilerber (WZ) algorithm}
finds $G$ 
(with compact support in $k$ for all $n$)
such that 
$F(n-1,k) - F(n,k) = G(n,k+1) - G(n,k)$, e.g., 
by applying Gosper's algorithm.

\textbf{5.} The \d{Hyper algorithm}
finds the closed form solution, if it exists, 
of any recurrence with polynomial coefficients 
(\emph{holonomic sequence})
by looking for a hypergeometric solution and reducing the problem
to finding polynomial solutions of another recurrence 
(here, ``closed form'' means sum of hypergeometric series).

Note that the zeroes of the coefficients of the recurrence 
can make the dimension of the space of solutions
larger that expected: 
checking that two solutions of a recurrence of degree $k$ 
agree on just $k$ points may not be sufficient
to prove they are equal.

There are implementations of those algorithms 
in Maple (\texttt{EKHAD}) 
and Mathematica (\texttt{Gosper}, \texttt{Hyper}, \texttt{WZ}). 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep reinforcement learning\\ with double Q-learning} \\
  H. van Hasselt et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Imprecise action values lead to overestimated values. Double $Q$ learning, which learns two sets of weights, $\theta$ and $\theta'$,
randomly updating one of them at each step, 
and replaces the $Q$-learning target 
\begin{align*}
Y_t^Q &= R_{t+1} + \gamma \max_a Q( S_{t+1}, a; \theta_t ) \\
	&= R_{t+1} + \gamma Q\bigl( 
S_{t+1},
\argmax_a Q( S_{t+1}, a, \theta_t \bigr),
\theta_t ) \\
%\intertext{with}
\text{with } Y_t^Q &=
R_{t+1} + \gamma Q\bigl( 
S_{t+1},
\argmax_a Q( S_{t+1}, a, \theta_t ),
\theta'_t \bigr),
\end{align*}
reduces the problem.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tensorizing neural networks} \\
  A. Novikov et al.
\end{book}
To reduce the memory footprint of large neural nets,
one can use lower numeric precision,
hashing,
or low rank tensor approximations
such as Tucker, CP or tensor train (TT).
A TT decomposition of a tensor $\mathscr A$
is a decomposition of each element as a product of matrices
(the first and last factors are row and column matrices)
\[ 
\mathscr A ( j_1, \dots, j_d ) =
G_{1 j_1} G_{2 j_2} \cdots G_{d j_d}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation neural networks} \\
  S. Chandar et al. 
\end{book}
Canonical correlation analysis (CCA -- for a C++ 
implementation, check \texttt{dlib}) 
solves the following problem: 
given two (vector) random variables $X$, $Y$,
find $x$ and $y$ to maximize $\cor( x' X, y' Y)$;
$x'X$ and $y'Y$ can be seen as a common representation of $X$ and $Y$.
An auto-encoder can be used for the same purpose, 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.7,yscale=.9]
\node[fill=black!20,text width=4em,text centered] (X1) at (.5,0) {$X$};
\node[fill=black!20,text width=4em,text centered] (Y1) at (1.5,0) {$Y$};
\node[fill=black!20,text width=4em,text centered] (X2) at (.5,2) {$X$};
\node[fill=black!20,text width=4em,text centered] (Y2) at (1.5,2) {$Y$};
\node[fill=black!20,text width=4em,text centered] (H) at (1,1) {$h(X,Y)$};
\draw[->] (X1) -- (H);
\draw[->] (Y1) -- (H);
\draw[->] (H) -- (X2);
\draw[->] (H) -- (Y2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
minimizing the sum of 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Reconstruction error of $(X,Y)$ given $(X,Y)$;
\item Reconstruction error of $(X,Y)$ given $(X,0)$;
\item Reconstruction error of $(X,Y)$ given $(0,Y)$;
\item Minus $ \cor\bigl( h(X,0), h(0,Y) \bigr)$.
\end{compactitem}
[One could also use a noisy auto-encoder: 
during training, replace either $X$ or $Y$ with noise.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Semi-supervised learning with ladder networks} \\
  A. Rasmus et al.
\end{book}
A \emph{ladder network} is an auto-encoder with skip connections, to preserve details.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scheduled sampling for sequence prediction with recurrence neural networks} \\
  S. Bengio et al.
\end{book}
Neural networks outputting a sequence usually 
predict the next token from the current (hidden) state and the previous token -- there may be a discrepancy between training and inference, 
the former using the true token 
while the latter only has the precious predicted token.
Instead, during training, one can randomly choose to use the true or predicted token,
with the probability of the true token decreasing as training progresses. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning both weights and connections\\ for efficient neural networks} \\
  S. Han et al.
\end{book}
For sparse neural nets: 
train; prune unimportant connections; iterate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Minimum HGR correlation principle:\\ from marginals to joint distribution} \\
  F. Farnia et al.
\end{book}
The HGR correlation is 
\begin{align*}
\rho(X,Y) = \sup \bigl \{ & E[ f(X) g(Y) ] \ :\ 
f, g \text{ measurable such}\\
& 
\text{that }E fX = EgX = 0 \text{ and } \\
& E(fX)^2 = E(gX)^2 = 1
\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
It is between 0 and 1, and is 0 (resp. 1) 
iif $X$ and $Y$ are independent (strictly dependent).
The distribution with the minimum HGR correlation for given first and second moments is the Gaussian -- 
this is an analogue of the maximum entropy property of the Gaussian distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable Bayesian optimization\\ using deep neural networks} \\ J. Snoek et al.(2015)
\end{book}
\d{Bayesian neural networks}, 
\ie, distributions over the weights of a neural net
(instead of point estimates)
are computationally expensive.
They can be approximated by \emph{adaptive basis regression},
\ie, neural nets in which only the last, linear layer is Bayesian.
Bayesian optimization with Gaussian processes scales cubically in the number of observations; 
adaptive basis regression is a linear alternative
(random forests are another).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gradient-based hyperparameter optimization through reversible learning} \\
D. Mclaurin et al.
\end{book}
Backpropagation through the entire learning procedure,
back to the hyperparameters,
allows the use of gradient-based 
(instead of gradient-free Bayesian optimization)
hyperparameter tuning.
Storing all the intermediate results needed 
to compute the gradients is unreasonable 
(it would mean remembering all the iterations 
of the inner loop,
on the mini-batches).
It is however possible to run 
the optimization procedure backwards, 
provided we remember all the bits that fall off 
in floating point computations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Topological data analysis of contagion maps for examining spreading processes on networks} \\
  D. Taylor et al.
\end{book}
The Watts threshold model (WTM) map is obtained by simulating the WTM (a node becomes infected if the proportion of infected neighbours  exceeds some threshold) and using the infection times (for various (singleton) infection seeds) as coordinates; dimension reduction may be needed.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Compressing neural networks\\ with the hashing trick} \\
  W. Chen et al.
\end{book}
To reduce the size of a neural net: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Train a 1-layer net on the log-output of a deep net;
\item Do not learn the full weight matrices but low rank approximations;
\item Use feature hashing to reduce the weight matrices to a reasonable number or parameters.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Analyzing big data\\ with dynamic quantum clustering} \\
  M. Weinstein et al.
\end{book}
The \emph{mean-shift} clustering algorithm 
starts with a cloud of points,
computes a density estimator,
moves each point uphill,
and iterates.
Dynamic quantum clustering (DQC) is a quantum-flavoured variant: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a cloud of points $x_1,\dots,x_n$;
\item Consider the density estimator 
  $\phi(x) = \sum \phi(x_i)$, 
$\phi(x_i) = \exp - \tfrac12 (x-x_i)' (x-x_i) / \sigma^{2} $;
\item Consider the potential $V$ defined by $H\phi=0$, 
  where the Hamiltonian operator is 
  \[ H = - \dfrac{1}{2\sigma^2} \nabla^2 + V(x); \]
\item Move the densities towards this potential
  $\phi_1 \mapsto e^{ - i \delta H } \phi_i$;
\item Compute the new position of the points;
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Input warping for Bayesian optimization\\ of non-stationary functions} \\
  J. Snoek et al.
\end{book}
Gaussian processes (GP) usually model stationary processes: 
the covariance function $k(x,y)$ only depends on $x-y$.
For non-stationary processes, 
one can use a hierarchical Bayesian model, 
including a transformation of the input space 
to make the process stationary.
For instance, the cummulative distribution function of the beta distribution can model 
simple monotonic transformations:
use a logarithmic prior on $\alpha$ and $\beta$ to encode the prior belief in an exponential, logarithmic, linear, sigmoid or logit transformation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A sufficient and necessary condition\\ for global optimization} \\
  D.H. Wu et al. (2010)
\end{book}
If $f$ is Lebesgue-integrable,
then 
\begin{align*}
\operatorname{essinf} f &= 
\sup \{ \alpha \ :\ V_{m,f}(\alpha ) = 0 \},
\displaybreak[0] \\
V_{m,f}(\alpha) &= \int \bigl( \alpha - f(x) \bigr) _+ ^m d\mu \\
V_{m,f}^{(m)} (\alpha) &= m! \mu [ f(x) \leq \alpha ].
\end{align*}
Use Monte Carlo simulations to estimate those integrals 
and turn the formula into a glocal optimization algorithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Chemical equation balancing:\\ an integer programming approach} \\
  S.K. Sen et al. (2006)
\end{book}
Balancing a chemical equation is a linear optimization problem: 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find}        & x \\
\text{To minimize} & x' \mathbf 1 \\
\text{Such that}   & Ax = 0, \ 
                     x \geq \mathbf 1, \ 
                     x \in \ZZ^n.
\end{array}
\]
The quadratic problem
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find}        & x \\
\text{To minimize} & x' x \\
\text{Such that}   & Ax = 0, \ 
                     x \geq \mathbf 1, \ 
                     x \in \ZZ^n
\end{array}
\]
may be easier to solve and often has the same solution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A new approach\\ to balancing chemical equations} \\
  I.C. Risteski
\end{book}
Balancing a chemical equation means finding positive vectors,
$x$ and $y$, if they exist,
so that $Ax = By$, where $A$ and $B$ have nonnegative entries.
Try: 
\begin{align*}
  y &= ( I - G^+ G ) u \\
  x &= A^+ B y + ( I - A^+ A ) v \\
  G &= ( I - A A ^+ ) B
\end{align*}
for arbitrary vectors $u$ and $v$.
(You need to compute a pseudo inverse in $\QQ$, exactly, or in $\ZZ/n\ZZ$, with $n$ sufficiently large.)
% Only part of the result claimed is proved.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mastering the game of Go\\ with deep neural networks and tree search} \\
  D. Silver et al. (2016)
\end{book}
Consider a Go board as a $19\times19$ image
and feed it to two deep convolutional neural nets,
for forecast the next move 
(supervised learning from human games),
and to estimate the value of a position;
combine with Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS).

