\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ball arithmetic} \\
  J. van der Hoeven (2011)
\end{book}

For reliable computing, consider replacing 
interval arithmetic with ball arithmetic
(sometimes called midpoint-radius arithmetic): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item While interval arithmetic require full precision for both ends of the intervals, ball arithmetic only needs full precision for the centers;
\item On $\CC$ or $\RR^{n\times m}$, 
  intervals tend to grow faster than balls 
  (e.g., $z \mapsto z^2$ around $z = 1 + i$).
\end{compactitem}
Mathemagix if a (C++) software environment for certified numeric computations. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Arb: a C library for ball arithmetic} \\
  F. Johansson
\end{book}
Accurate special functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bitcoin and cryptocurrency technologies} \\
  A.\,Narayanan\,et\,al.\,(Princeton\,\&\,Coursera,\,2015)
\end{book}
Non-technical but clear and comprehensive 
exposition of cryptocurrencies and their applications.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Curve and surface reconstruction algorithms with mathematical analysis} \\
  T.K. Dey (2007)
\end{book}

The \d{skeleton} of a shape (curve, surface) 
$\Sigma \subset \RR^k$ 
is the set of centers of balls that do not contain 
any point of $\Sigma$ and 
cannot be enlarged while keeping this property.
The \d{medial axis} $M_\Sigma$ is its closure.
It is also the closure of the set of points 
that have at least two closest points in $\Sigma$.
[Those notions also appear in \emph{mathematical morphology}, 
a branch of image analysis.]
The \d{local feature size} of $x \in \Sigma$
is $f(x) = d(x, M_\Sigma)$.
A sample $P \subset \Sigma$ is an \emph{$\epsilon$-sample}
if 
\(
  \forall x \in \Sigma \ 
  \exists p \in P \ 
  \n{ x - p } \leq \epsilon f(x);
\)
if $\epsilon < 1$, the sample is \emph{dense}.

Given an $\epsilon$-sample $P$ of a closed plane curve $\Sigma$
whose local feature size is always positive,
one can find a piecewise approximation of $\Sigma$,
as a subset of the Delaunay triangulation, as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the Voronoi tessellation of $P$ 
  and the voronoi vertices $V$; 
  they approximate the median axis;
\item Compute the Delaunay triangulation of $P \union V$; 
  keep the edges connecting two points of $P$.
\end{compactitem}

\begin{centering}
<<CurveAndSurfaceReconstructionDey2007,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=1.5,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
## Curve reconstruction (from T.K. Dey, Curve and surface reconstruction, 2007)
set.seed(2)
n <- 10
N <- 50
theta0 <- seq(0,2*pi,length=n+1)[-1]
r0 <- .5 + runif(n)
x0 <- r0 * cos(theta0)
y0 <- r0 * sin(theta0)
#plot(x0,y0)
theta <- seq(0,2*pi, length=N+1)[-1]
r     <- spline( 1:n, r0,     N, "periodic" )$y
x <- r * cos(theta)
y <- r * sin(theta)
#plot(x,y)
library(deldir)
op <- par( mfrow=c(1,4), mar=c(1,.5,1,.5), oma=c(0,.5,0,.5) )

# Voronoi tessellation
r <- deldir(x,y)
plot( x, y, pch=16, lwd=3, cex=1.5, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="")
segments( r$dirsgs$x1, r$dirsgs$y1, r$dirsgs$x2, r$dirsgs$y2, col="#880000" )

# The Voronoi vertices approximate the medial axis
plot( c(x,x[1]), c(y,y[1]), type="o", pch=16, lwd=3, cex=1.5, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="")
points( r$dirsgs$x1, r$dirsgs$y1, pch=16, col="#880000" )

# Delaunay triangulation: the segments reconstructing the curve are there,
# but there are a lot of spurious segments.
plot( x, y, pch=16, lwd=3, cex=1.5, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="")
segments(
  r$delsgs$x1, r$delsgs$y1,
  r$delsgs$x2, r$delsgs$y2,
  pch=16, col="navy"
)

# Delaunay triangulation of the initial points and the Voronoi vertices:
# the edges joining two of the initial points reconstruct the curve.
xx <- c( x, r$dirsgs$x1 )
yy <- c( y, r$dirsgs$y1 )
s <- deldir(xx, yy)
plot( xx, yy, pch=15, col = "#880000", cex=1, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="")
points( x, y, pch=16, cex=1.5 )
segments(
  s$delsgs$x1, s$delsgs$y1,
  s$delsgs$x2, s$delsgs$y2,
  pch=16, col="navy"
)
par(op)
@
\end{centering}

Alternatively, link each point $p \in P$ 
to it nearest neighbour $q$
and its half-neighbour $s$ -- 
the closest point $s$ 
such that 
$\angle{ s p q } \geq \pi/2$.
Both algorithms are $O(n \log n)$
and work if $\epsilon < 1/5$, resp. $\epsilon < 1/3$.

One can reconstruct a compact surface 
(without boundary) $\Sigma \subset \RR^3$
from a sample $P$ of points
and its Voronoi tessellation: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The normal at a point $p \in P$ 
  can be approximated by the direction in which 
  the Voronoi cell $V_p$ is elongated; 
  more formally, if $V_p$ is unbouded,
  use the average direction $v_p$ of the unbounded edges; 
  if it is bounded, take the point $p^+ \in P$
  farthest from $p$, 
  then the point $p^-$ farther from $p$ among 
  the points such that $\angle{ p^- p p^+} > \pi/2$
  and set $v_p = p^- - p$;
\item The surface in the neighbourhgood of a point $p \in P$
  can be approximated by the \emph{cocone}: 
  the complement of the double cone, centered on $p$, 
  in the direction $v_p$, 
  of angle $3 \pi / 8$, 
  restricted to $V_p$;
\item Take the Voronoi edges that intersect the cocones; 
  their duals are triangles in the Voronoi tessellation
  (but there are still too many); 
\item Remove triangles adjacent to ``sharp'' edges 
  ($\text{angle} < 3 \pi/2$);
\item ``Pockets'' (blisters) remain on the approximate surface: remove them by walking on the outside.
\end{compactitem}

