  M.G. Gartley and W. Basener (2009)
\end{book}
Outliers form the small connected components 
of the truncated graph.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Return to the Riemann integral} \\
  R.G. Bartle (1996)
\end{book}
A \emph{tagged partition} of an interval $[a,b]$ is the datum of 
\[ a = x_0 < x_1 < \cdots < x_n = b \]
and, for each $i$, $t_i \in (x_{i-1}, x_i)$.
The \emph{Riemann sum} of a function $f : [a,b] \to \RR$
on a tagged partition 
$ ( (x_i)_i, (t_i)_i ) $ is
\[ R( f, (x_i)_i, (t_i)_i ) = \sum_i f(t_i) ( x_i - x_{i-1} ). \]
A function $f$ is \emph{Riemann integrable}, of integral $S$, if 
\begin{align*}
  &
  \forall \epsilon > 0 \quad
  \exists \delta > 0 \quad 
  \forall (x_i)_i, (t_i)_i
  \\ &
  ( \forall i \  \va{ x_i - x_{i-1} } < \delta )
  \implies
  \va{ R( f, (x_i)_i, (t_i)_i ) - S } < \epsilon.
\end{align*}
The \d{generalized Riemann integral}
replaces $\delta$
with a function $\delta : [a,b] \to \RR^{>0}$, evaluated in $t_i$.
The following functions are integrable:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Riemann integrable functions: 
  $ \mathscr R \subset \mathscr R^* $;
\item $1_\QQ$;
\item Lebesgue-integrable functions:
  \[
  \mathscr L = \{\, f \in \mathscr R^* \ :\ \va f \in \mathscr R^* \,\};
  \]
\item The derivative of (continuous) functions differentiable except at a countable number of points.
\end{compactitem}
There is no need to add improper integrals: 
such functions are already integrable.

The convergence theorems from Lebesgue integration 
(uniform, monotone, dominated convergence)
are still valid.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Regulated functions:\\ Bourbaki's alternative to the Riemann integral} \\
  S.K. Berberian (1979)
\end{book}
Another alternative (not a generalization) of the Riemann integral.
\emph{Regular functions} are functions with one-sided limits at each point; equivalently, they are uniform limits of step functions. For every regulated function $f$, there is a function $F$, differentiable except perhaps at countably many points, with $F' = f$ (the converse is not true). 
Regulated functions are Riemann integrable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A friendly introduction to RGP} \\
  O. Flasch (2014)
\end{book}
The \texttt{rgp} package provides genetic programming 
(symbolic regression)
and typed genetic programming 
(idem, with higher-order functions);
it can be used with 
\texttt{emoa} for multiobjective optimization 
and \texttt{SPOT} for hyperparameter tuning.
% SPOT seems to be just CMA-ES
Alternatives include 
ECJ (Java),
GPTIPS (Matlab) and,
on the commercial side, 
DataModeler, Discipulus and Eureqa.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gradient boosting machines, a tutorial} \\
  A. Natekin and A.Knoll (2013)
\end{book}

Boosting builds an ensemble model by fitting simplistic models
(base learners, weak learners) to the data,
with more weight on the observations currently misclassified
by the ensemble.
\d{Gradient boosting} gives an interpretation of this algorithm
as a gradient descent, not in parameter space, 
but in function space, \ie, 
we do not look at 
\[
  \sum_i \dfrac{
    \partial \text{Loss}\bigl( y_i, h(x_i, \theta) \bigr)
  }{
    \partial \theta
  }
\text{\ \ but\ \ }
  \left.
  \sum_i 
  \dfrac{
    \partial \text{Loss}( y, \hat y )
  }{
    \partial \hat y
  }
  \right|_{
  \raisebox{5pt}{%
  \ensuremath{
  \substack{
    \mathrlap{y = y_i} \\
    \mathrlap{\hat y = \hat f( x_i ).}
  }
  }
  }
  %\substack{
  %  \mathrlap{y = y_i} \\
  %  \mathrlap{\hat y = \hat f( x_i ).}
  %}
  %\scriptsize\begin{array}{l}
  %  y = y_i \\
  %  \hat y = \hat f( x_i ).
  %\end{array}
  }
  \qquad
\]
More precisely, we iterate: 
\begin{align*}
  \displaystyle
  (\rho_t, \theta_t) &= \argmin_{ 
    \rho, \theta
  }
  \!
  \sum_i 
  \left[
  - \!\!
  \left.
  \vphantom{\sum_i}
  \dfrac{
    \partial \text{Loss}( y, \hat y )
  }{
    \partial \hat y
  }
  \right|_{
  \raisebox{5pt}{%
  \ensuremath{
  \substack{
    \mathrlap{y = y_i} \\
    \mathrlap{\hat y = \hat f_{t-1}( x_i ).}
  }
  }
  }
  }
  \hspace{7mm}
  - \!
  \rho h( x_i, \theta )
  \right] ^2 \\
  f_t &\leftarrow f_{t-1} + \rho_t h( \cdot, \theta_t )
\end{align*}

The weak learners can be: 
linear regression, ridge regression, mixed model, 
splines, radial basis functions, GAM, 
tree stumps, 
trees with a maximum interaction depth (5 is good enough),
wavelets (Viola-Jones face detection algorithm), etc.

The loss function can be: $L^2$, $L^1$, Huber, quantile loss,
binomial loss $\log( 1 - e^{- y \hat y})$,
Adaboost loss $e^{- y \hat y}$, etc.

Generalizations include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item If there are many variables, fit the base learners 
  on a random subset of the variables, several times, 
  and keep the best fit --
  this gives a sparser model;
\item Subsampling (bagging);
\item Shrinkage: 
  $ f_t \leftarrow f_{t-1} + \lambda \rho_t h(\cdot, \theta_t) $;
\item Early stopping -- this is needed; 
  the optimal number of iterations depends 
  on the step size $\lambda$ 
  and can be estimated by cross-validation.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Laplacian eigenmaps for dimensionality reduction and data representation} \\
  M. Belklin and P. Niyogi (2002)
\end{book}
Given a cloud of points $x_1,\dots,x_N$,
build a graph,
with those points as vertices,
and an edge when the distance between two points 
is below some threshold, 
or when one is among the $k$ nearest neighbours of the other;
use 1 or $w_{ij} = \exp - \n{x_i - x_j}^2/t$
as weights (similarities).

\d{Spectral dimension reduction}
solves the generalized eigenvalue problem 
$ L f = \lambda D f $, 
where $D = \diag( W \mathbf 1 ) $ 
and $L = D - W $, 
\[ 
  L f_i = \lambda_i D f_i \qquad
  0 = \lambda_0 \leq \lambda_1 \leq \cdots \leq \lambda_m,
\]
and uses $f_1,\dots,f_m$ as coordinates.

In dimension 1 (Fiedler vector),
this can be justified as follows: 
we want a 1-dimensional embedding 
$(y_1,\dots,y_N) \in \RR^N$
so that points that should be close ($W_{ij}$ large)
be close,
e.g., by minimizing $\sum_{ij} (y_i - y_j) ^ 2 W_{ij}$,
with some normalization constraint, 
e.g., $\n y ^2 = 1$ or $y' D y = 1$
(in order to avoid the trivial solution $y = \mathbf 1$, 
we also add $y \perp \mathbf 1$, \ie, $y' D \mathbf 1 = 0$ -- 
this will give the smallest eigenvalue, skipping zero).
Noticing that $\sum (y_i - y_j)^2 W_{ij} = y' L y$, 
the optimization problem becomes 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & y \in \RR^N \\
\text{To minimize} & y' L y \\
\text{Such that} & y' D y = 1 \text{ and } y' D \mathbf 1 = 0
\end{array}
\]
and can be solved with Lagrange multipliers 
\begin{align*}
  f(y) &= y' L y \\
  g(y) &= y' D y \\
  \nabla f &= \lambda \nabla g,
\end{align*}
\ie, $L y = \lambda D y$ -- a generalized eigenvalue problem.

This interpretation remains valid in a continuous setting, \ie,
if we replace the cloud of points with a compact manifold $M$: 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & f : M \longrightarrow \RR, \ \mathscr C^2 \\
  \text{To minimize} & \int \n{ \nabla f }^2 \\
  \text{Such that}   & \n{f}^2 := \int_M f^2 = 1 \\
                     & \langle f, 1 \rangle := \int_M f = 0
\end{array}
\]
where $\n{ \nabla f}$ estimates how far appart $f$ maps nearby points.
The objective can be written 
$ 
\int \n{ \nabla f }^2 = 
\int \langle \nabla f, \nabla f \rangle = 
\int \langle \nabla^* \nabla f, f \rangle = 
\int \mathscr L f \cdot f,
$
where $\mathscr L = \nabla^* \nabla = - \div \nabla$
is the Laplace-Beltrami operator 
(that is the name of the Laplace operator 
on a Riemannian manifold);
it is positive semidefinite and 
(if $M$ is compact) 
its spectrum is discrete 
$0 = \lambda_0 \leq \lambda_1 \leq \cdots$.
The first eigenvalues give a low-dimensional embedding.

To find two clusters in a weighted graph,
one can try to find a partition 
$V = A \amalg B$
that minimizes 
\[ 
  \text{Cut}(A,B) = 
  \sum_{\substack{ u \in A \\ v \in B }} W_{uv},
\]
but this tends to cut off weakly-connected outliers.
Instead, one can minimize the normalized cut 
\[ 
  \operatorname{NCut}(A,B) = \operatorname{Cut}(A,B)
  \left(
    \dfrac 1{\operatorname{vol} A} +
    \dfrac 1{\operatorname{vol} B}
  \right),
\]
where $\operatorname{vol} A = 
\sum\limits_{\substack{u \in A \\ v \in V}} W_{uv}$.
By setting 
\[
  x_1 = 
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  1 / \operatorname{vol} A & \text{if } i \in A \\
  1 / \operatorname{vol} B & \text{if } i \in B,
\end{array}
\right.
\]
one can see that 
\begin{align*}
  \operatorname{NCut}(A,B) &= \dfrac{x' L x}{x' D x} \\
  x' D \mathbf 1 &= 0;
\end{align*}
the relaxed minimum normalized cut problem, 
aka \d{spectral clustering},
is a generalized eigenvalue problem.

The local linear embedding (LLE) algorithm builds an unweighted graph from a cloud of points;
the barycentric coordinates $W_{ij}$ minimize 
$ \sum_i \n{ x_1 - \sum_j W_{ij} x_j } ^ 2 $, 
and are normalized by $\forall i \ \sum_j W_{ij} = 1$;
the embedding is given by the $k$ lowest eigenvalues of 
$E = (I-W)' (I-W)$. 
One can see that $E \approx \tfrac12 L^2$
(under some conditions); 
the eigenvectors are the same as those of $L$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Constructing Laplace operator\\ from point clouds in $\RR^d$} \\
  M. Belkin et al.
\end{book}
One can approximate the Laplace operator $L$ 
on a submanifold $M \subset \RR^d$
of (known) dimension $k$ from a finite sample of points 
$P \subset M$ as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Approximate the tangent space at $p \in P$,
  for instance by the $k$ plane $Q^*$ through $p$
  best fitting $P_r = P \inter B(p,r)$,
  \ie, minimizing the 1-sided Hausdorff distance 
  $d_H(P_r, Q^*) = \sup_{ p \in P_r } \inf_{ q \in Q^* } d(p,q); $
  an approximation \smash{$\hat T_p$} of $Q^*$ is sufficient;
\item Build the Delaunay triangulation $K_\delta$ 
  of the projection $\pi(P_\delta)$ or $P_\delta$ on $\hat T_p$;
\item For a function $f : P \rightarrow \RR$, 
  set $L f(p)$ to
\[
  % = \dfrac{1}{ (4\pi)^{t/2} t }
  \!\!
  \sum_{\substack{ \sigma : \text{$k$-dim} \\ \text{face of } K_\delta }}
  \!\!\!\!
  \dfrac{ \operatorname{vol} \sigma }{ k+1 }
  \!
  \sum_{ q \in V(\sigma) }
  [ f(p) - f(\pi^{-1} q) ] 
  e^{ - \tfrac{\n{ f(p) - f(\pi^{-1}q)}^2}{4t \mathstrut} }.
\]
\end{compactitem}
Contrary to other implementations
(e.g., the adjacency matrix with Gaussian weights),
it does not assume that the points are drawn uniformly from $M$
% (but just that they form an $\epsilon$-sample)
and does not require a global mesh
(given a mesh, one can use the same formula, with the whole mesh instead of $K\delta$, 
and the tangent space is not needed ($\pi = \operatorname{id}$) -- 
this is the \d{mesh-Laplace operator}).

\begin{centering}
<<ChladniPatterns,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=3.6,out.width='\\hsize',fig.show='hold'>>=
# Chladni patterns: nodal sets of the eigenfunctions of the Laplace operator,
# staring with the smallest eigenvalues. 

# On a square or a disk
library(rARPACK)
library(Matrix)
for( disk in c( FALSE ) ) { 
n <- 100
f <- function(i,j) i + (j-1) * n
L <- matrix( 0, nrow = n^2, ncol = n^2 )
for( i in 1:n ) {
  for( j in 1:n ) {
    if( disk )
    if( (i-n/2)^2 + (j-n/2)^2 > (n/2)^2 ) next
    L[ f(i,j), f(i,j) ] <- 4
    if( i > 1 ) L[ f(i,j), f(i-1,j)] <- -1
    if( j > 1 ) L[ f(i,j), f(i,j-1)] <- -1
    if( i < n ) L[ f(i,j), f(i+1,j)] <- -1
    if( j < n ) L[ f(i,j), f(i,j+1)] <- -1
  }
}
k <- 15
#e <- eigen(L) # Too long if we want a decent resolution, because it computes ALL the eigenvectors
#e <- eigs_sym( L, k, which = "SM" ) # Smallest eigenvalues
e <- eigs_sym( L, k, which = "LM", sigma = 0 ) # Smallest eigenvalues; probably faster and more numerically stable
if( any( ! is.finite( e$values ) ) ) # Why?
  e <- eigs_sym( L, k, which = "SM" )

k <- length(e$values)
col <- colorRampPalette(c("black", "white"), space="Lab")(10)
col <- c( rev(col), col )
op <- par( mfrow = c(3,5), mar = c(0,0,0,0), oma = .1 + c(0,0,0,0) )
for( i in 1:k ) {
  image( matrix( e$vectors[,k + 1 - i ], n, n ), axes = FALSE, col = col, useRaster = TRUE )
  box(col="white")
}
par(op)

# Not pretty
if( FALSE ) {
op <- par( mfrow = c(5,5), mar = c(0,0,0,0), oma = .1 + c(0,0,0,0) )
for( i in 1:k ) {
  A <- matrix( e$vectors[,k + 1 - i ], n, n )
  contour(A, levels = 0, drawlabels = FALSE, lwd=3, axes = FALSE )
  box()
}
par(op)
}
}
@
\end{centering}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convergence, stability and discrete approximation of Laplace spectra} \\
  T.K. Dey et al.
\end{book}
The mesh-Laplace operator gives a good approximation 
of the Laplace operator (pointwise convergence)
and a good (accurate, robust) approximation of its spectrum.

The spectrum of the Laplacian gives some information 
on the manifold, e.g., its volume 
(Weyl's law: $\lambda_n \sim 4\pi n / \operatorname{Vol} M$) 
or its total curvature.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Differential representation for mesh processing} \\
  O. Sorkine (2006)
\end{book}
The graph Laplacian of a mesh in $\RR^3$
can be interpreted as the linear transformation 
from coordinates to $\delta$-coordinates
(useful, e.g., for mesh editing)
\begin{align*}
L : v_i \longmapsto \delta_i &= 
v_i - \dfrac{1}{ \va{ N(i) } } 
\sum_{ j \in N(i) } 
v_i
\\ &
= 
\dfrac{1}{ \va{ N(i) } } 
\sum_{ j \in N(i) } 
v_i  - v_j,
\end{align*}
\ie, 
$L = I - D^{-1} A$,
symmetrized $L_{\text{sym}} = DL = D - A$, 
$L_{\text{sym}} x = D \delta_x$.
There are many variants, e.g., 
\begin{align*}
  \delta_i &= 
  \dfrac 1{\va{\Omega_i}}
  \sum_j \dfrac12 ( \cot \alpha_{ij} + \cot \beta_{ij} ) (v_i - v_j ) \\
  \delta_i &= 
  \dfrac 1{\va{\Omega_i}}
  \sum_j 
  \dfrac{ 
    \tan( \theta^1_{ij} / 2 ) + 
    \tan( \theta^2_{ij} / 2 ) 
  }{
    \n{ v_i - v_j }
  }
  (v_i - v_j )
\end{align*}
where $\va{ \Omega_i }$ is the volume of the Voronoi cell of $i$,
$\alpha_{ij}$, $\beta_{ij}$ are the angles opposite the edge $(ij)$,
$\theta^1_{ij}$, $\theta^2_{ij}$ are the angles at $i$ along $(ij)$.

Reconstructing the coordinates from the $\delta$-coordinates
(with a few positional ``constraints'' on some of the vertices)
can be approached as a least squares problem.

If the mesh is known (e.g., a character in a video game -- the mesh is just deformed as it moves),
we can use the eigenfunctions of the Laplacian to encode the coordinates (it is not really necessary to compute the eigenfunctions: the reconstruction can also be reduced to a least squares problem).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distilling the knowledge in a neural network} \\
  G. Hinton et al.
\end{book}
To transfer the information in a complex model to a smaller model
(model compression, model distillation),
one often trains the smaller model to reproduce 
the output of the complex one on a very large (unlabeled) dataset.
In the case of a multiclass classification problem,
since there is information in the low probabilities,
one can, instead of reproducing the output classes of the complex model, reproduce its probabilities or, rather, their logits. 
Even better, one can increase the temperature in the learning phase (to help learn smaller probabilities), 
and set it back to 1 afterwards.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A SQP method for general nonlinear programs using only equality constrained subproblems} \\
  P. Spellucci
\end{book}
% non-free
The \texttt{donlp2} algorithm solves a nonlinear program
with equality and inequality constraints
by taking a second order approximation of the objective,
linearizing the constraints, replacing some inequalities with equalities and discarding the others.
The subtleties reside in the choice of the inequalities to keep as equalities (those violated or near-active) or to discard, and how to deal with inconsistent quadratic programs.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Q-learning and SARSA:\\ a comparison between two intelligent stochastic control approaches to financial trading} \\
M. Corazza and A. Sangalli
\end{book}
Misguided attempt to apply reinforcement learning (RL) to trading,
using the past returns as state: 
they use the \emph{past} Sharpe ratios as rewards -- 
by construction, this will give a momentum strategy.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Universal value function approximators} \\
  T. Schaul et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The value function approximator $V(s;\theta)$ learnt 
in reinforcement learning can be generalized to account for
various goals (e.g., a single desired final state), $V(s,g;\theta)$,
and used for planning (do not try to get to the final goal from the start; try to reach intermediate milestones first).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Weighted Voronoi stippling} \\
  A. Secord (2002)
\end{book}
Stippling (non-photorealistic rendering)
with weighted \emph{centroidal Voronoi diagrams}, 
\ie, Voronoi diagrams in which the generators are 
the centroids of their cell.

