\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sampling for inference in probabilistic models with fast Bayesian quadrature} \\
  T. Gunter et al.
%  \emph{Active learning of model evidence using Bayesian quadrature} \\  
%  M.A. Osborne et al. 
\end{book}
Bayesian quadrature is a faster variant of MCMC.
For positive integrands (e.g., a likelihood),
use a Gaussian process prior on its square root
or its logarithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic line searches\\ for stochastic optimization} \\
  M. Mahsereci and P. Hennig
\end{book}
Stochastic gradient descent (SGD) has two problems: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The gradient (even if it were not noisy)
  is not the best direction: use momentum, AdaGrad, etc.;
\item One must choose the learning rate or resort to an expensive line search: instead of a line search, try Bayesian optimization.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sequential Bayesian prediction\\ in the presence of changepoints and faults} \\
  R. Garnett (2009)
\end{book}
List of covariance structures 
to detect change points
with Gaussian processes (GP):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Drastic changepoint: the observations 
  before and after are independent -- the result is discontinuous;
\item Continuous drastic changepoint: the observations before and after are independent conditionally on the value at the changepoint;
\item Change in input and/or output scales, 
\[ 
  K(x,y)  = 
  \lambda^2_{\text{out}} 
  \kappa 
  \left(
    \dfrac{
      \va{y-x}
    }{
      \sigma_{\text{in}}
    }
  \right);
\]
\item Faults: bias, stuck value, drift, etc.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RRegrs: an R package for computer-aided model selection with multiple regression models} \\
G. Tsiliki et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To estimate, compare and choose between 10 regression models: linear, lasso, elastic net, SVM, neural net, random forest,
recursive feature elimination
(fit the full model,
rank the features, 
take the top $k$, re-fit the model, 
keep the best of those smaller models),
% This looks worse than a stepwise approach...
etc., via \texttt{caret}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bellman's GAP:\\ a 2nd generation language and system\\ for algebraic dynamic programming} \\
  G. Sauthoff (2011)
\end{book}
Textbook dynamic programming (DP) is straightforward, 
but real-life DP can involve more tables,
more recurrence relations,
more indices 
(for instance, 
sequence alignment with affine gap penalty requires several tables).
Algebraic dynamic programming (ADP) simplifies DP by describing the search space (e.g., the set of all possible alignments)
with a grammar -- this removes all the indices.
``Bellman's GAP'' is a Java-like programming language for ADP. Alternatives include Haskell- or Ocaml-based DSLs and/or code generators and may not scale well.

% (This is about an implementation of ADP: it is not the right place to learn it.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Categorification of persistent homology} \\
P. Bubenik and J.A. Scott (2014)
\end{book}
Persistent homology can be expressed in categorical terms.
An \emph{$\epsilon$-interleaving} between 
$F,G:(\RR,\leq) \rightarrow \mathfrak D$
is a pair of natural transformations
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[
  row sep = tiny,
  /tikz/every node/.style={inner xsep = 0pt, inner ysep = 2pt}
]
  (\RR,\leq) 
  \arrow[rd,"F",""{name=U, below}]
  \arrow[dd,"T_\epsilon"'] 
  \\
  & \mathfrak D \\
  (\RR,\leq) 
  \arrow[ru,"G"']
  \arrow[Rightarrow, from=U]
\end{tikzcd}
\qquad
\begin{tikzcd}[
  row sep = tiny,
  /tikz/every node/.style={inner xsep = 0pt, inner ysep = 2pt}
]
  (\RR,\leq) 
  \arrow[rd,"G",""{name=U, below}]
  \arrow[dd,"T_\epsilon"'] 
  \\
  & \mathfrak D \\
  (\RR,\leq), 
  \arrow[ru,"F"']
  \arrow[Rightarrow, from=U]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
where $T_\epsilon$ is the translation,
such that 
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[
  row sep = tiny,
  column sep = small,
  /tikz/every node/.style={inner xsep = 0pt, inner ysep = 1pt}
]
  F(a) \ar[rrr] \ar[rd] \ar[dd] &&& 
  F(b) \ar[ld] \ar[dd] \\
  & G(a+\epsilon) \ar[ld] \ar[r] \ar[dd] & G(b+\epsilon) 
  \ar[rd] \ar[dd] 
  \\
  F(a + 2 \epsilon) \ar[rrr] \ar[rd] &&& F(b+2\epsilon) \ar[ld]\\
  & G(a+3\epsilon) \ar[r]  &   G(b+3\epsilon).
\end{tikzcd}
\]
This defines an \emph{interleaving distance} 
\[ 
  d(F,G) = \inf \{ \epsilon \ :\  
    F, G \text{ $\epsilon$-interleaved} 
  \}.
\]
For any functor $H$, 
$d(HF, HG) \leq d(F,G)$.
In $\mathfrak{Vec} ^ {(\RR,\leq)} $,
\emph{tame} diagrams
(constant on a neighbourhood of each $t \in \RR$,
except a finite number of them)
are of \emph{finite type}; 
they can be described as \emph{barcodes}.
The \emph{bottleneck distance}
on barcodes
defines an isometric embedding 
$ \{ \text{barcodes} \} \hookrightarrow 
\mathfrak{Vec} ^ {(\RR,\leq)} $.
Given $f,g : X \rightarrow \RR$ (not necessarily continuous),
consider their lower level sets
$F,G: (\RR,\leq) \rightarrow \mathfrak{Top}$;
let $H : \mathfrak{Top} \rightarrow \mathfrak D$ 
be a functor; 
then, $d(HF,HG) \leq \n{ f - g }_\infty$, 
\ie, $H$ is stable to perturbations;
this applies to persistent homology or relative homology.
If $\mathfrak D$ is abelian,
the category of $\epsilon$-interleavings of diagrams 
$(\RR,\leq) \rightarrow \mathfrak D$ is also abelian.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Econometrics: methods and applications} \\
  P.H. Frances et al. (Coursera, 2015)
\end{book}
Linear models assume that the predictive variables are not stochastic. 
In the model $Y = X_1 \beta_1 + X_2 \beta_2 + \epsilon$,
the predictive variable $X_1$ is \d{endogenous} 
if $\cor(X_1, \epsilon) \neq 0$: the ordinary least squares 
estimate $\hat \beta_1$ is then inconsistent.
This can happen if a variable correlated with $X_1$ has been omitted, if there is a feedback mechanism between the predictive variables (e.g., when predicting sales from price and demand, it may seem that higher prices are good for sales, but higher prices may just come from higher demand), 
or if there are measurement errors (we do not observe $X_1$ but $X_1 + \eta$).

To address endogeneity, \d{2-stage least squares} (2SLS)
looks for \d{instrumental variables} (IV) $Z$, 
\ie, variables $Z$ that affect $y$ only through $X$: 
$\cor(Z,X) \neq 0$, $\cor(Z,\epsilon)=0$
and uses them to find $\beta$: 
$\cov(Z,y) = \cov(Z,X)\beta$.
The computations can also be done using two linear regressions
($X \sim Z$, then $y \sim \hat X$).
% do not use the residuals of this second regression

The method is consistent if 
$\tfrac1nZ'\epsilon \to 0$ ($Z$ and $\epsilon$ are uncorrelated),
$\lim \tfrac1nZ'Z$ invertible ($Z$ is not multicolinear),
$\lim \tfrac1nZ'X$ has rank $k$ (the number of variables -- $Z$ and $X$ are sufficiently correlated).
In particular, you need at least as many instruments as predictive variables (use the intercept and the exogenous variables as instruments: there will be fewer to find).

The \d{Sargan test} checks if $H_0: \cor(Z,\epsilon)=0$
by approximating the residuals using 2SLS: 
estimate $\hat X = Z (Z'Z)^{-1} Z' X$ (1S), 
estimate $b = (\hat X ' \hat X )^{-1} \hat X' y$ (2S),
compute the residuals 
$e = y - Xb$ (this is $X$, not $\hat X$),
compute the $R^2$ of $e \sim Z$: 
under the null hypothesis, it is small, 
and $n R^2 \sim \chi^2(m-k)$
(where $m$ is the number of instruments and $k$ the number of variables in $X$).
 
The \d{Hausmann test} for ``$H_0$: $X_1$ is actually exogenous''
uses the $R^2$ of 
$\operatorname{res}( y \sim X) \sim X + \operatorname{res}( X_1 \sim Z )$: 
it should be small and 
$n R^2 \sim \chi^2(k_1)$.

The course also covered 
variable selection (forward or backward, using $T$ or $F$ tests,
AIC, BIC, $L^2$ (RMSE) or $L^1$ (MAE) norm of the residuals),
pseudo-$R^2$ for logistic regression (McFadden, Nagelkerke),
time series (ARMA, unit roots, cointegration, Granger causality)
and many regression tests: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Regression specification (\textsc{Reset}): 
  add the powers of the fitted values 
  $ y_1 = x_i' \beta + \sum_{j=1}^p \gamma_j { \hat y_i }^{j+1} + \epsilon_i $
  (instead of the powers of $x$) and perform 
  an $F$ test for $H_0: \forall j \ \gamma_j = 0$
  (it is approximate because the $\hat y_i$ are not fixed predictors);
\item Chow break test: split the data in two,
  at a potential break point, and use an $F$ test for 
  $H_0: \beta_{\text{before}} = \beta_{\text{after}}$; 
\item Chow forecast test: linear before the break, perfect fit after: 
  $y_i = x_i'\beta + \sum\limits_{j=n_1+1}^{n_1+n_2} \gamma_j \delta_{ji} + \epsilon_i$, 
  $H_0: \forall j \gamma_j=0$;
\item Jarque-Bera normality test (for the residuals): 
  some combination of the sample skewness and kurtosis 
  of a Gaussian variable is approximately $\chi(2)$-distributed.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Integer relation detection} \\
  D.H. Bailey (2000)
\end{book}
Given $x_1,\dots,x_n$ (high-precision) floating-point numbers,
the \d{PSLQ} algorithm looks for integers 
$k_1,\dots,k_n$,
not all zero, so that $\sum_i k_i x_i = 0$.
Given a number $\alpha$ of interest, apply it to:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $1,\alpha , \alpha^2, \dots, \alpha^n$ 
to show that $\alpha$ is a root of a simple polynomial; 
\item $\alpha$, $\zeta(1)$, $\zeta(2)$, $\dots$, $\log 2$, $\log 3$, $\dots$,
$\pi$, $\pi^2$, $\dots$, $\operatorname{Li}_k(\ell)$,
$\pi^k \zeta(\ell)$, etc. to find interesting relations.
\end{compactitem}

It was also used to find a base-16 expansion of $\pi$.

For $n$ numbers and integer coefficients with at most $d$ digits, you need at least $nd$ significant digits.

The LLL algorithm has similar applications.

It is available in a C++ library, in GAP, 
perhaps also from SAGE via mpmath and/or GMP.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The decompositional approach\\ to matrix computation} \\
G.W. Stewart (2000)
\end{book}
The ``big six'' matrix decompositions are: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Cholesky: $A = RR'$ (or $A=LDL'$), 
  where $A$ is positive definite and $R$ upper-triangular; 
  used to solve $A'x=b$ or compute $x' A^{-1} x$;
\item LU: $P'AQ=LU$,
  where $A$ is square, $L$ lower-triangular, 
$U$ upper-triangular,
$P$ and $Q$ permutations;
used to solve $Ax=b$;
\item QR: $A = QR$, 
where $Q$ is orthogonal
and $R$ upper-triangular with non-negative diagonal elements; 
used in least-squares estimation: $QQ'$ is the projection on $\im A$;
\item Spectral decomposition: $A = V \Lambda V'$, 
where $A$ is symmetric$V$ orthogonal and $\Lambda$ diagonal;
\item Schur: $A = U T U^*$,
where $A$ is square, $U$ unitary, $T$ upper-triangular;
sometimes an intermediate step in eigenvalue problems; 
\item SVD: $A = U \Sigma V'$,
  where $U$ and $V$ have orthogonal columns and $\Sigma$ is diagonal.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using multi-complex variables for automatic computation of high-order derivatives} \\
G. Lantoine et al (2012)
\end{book}
% Computation of higher-order derivatives using the multi-complex step method, A. Verheyleweghen (2014) [student paper; comparison with AS and symbolic differentiation, but not with dual numbers]
The first derivative of an analytic function $f: \RR \to \RR$
can be approximated as 
$f'(x) = \im \dfrac{ f(x + ih ) }{ h } + o(h) $.
For higher derivatives,
use \d{multicomplex numbers},
\[
\CC_n = \dfrac{
  \RR[ i_1, \dots, i_n ]
}{
  ( i_1^2 + 1, \dots, i_n^2 + 1 )
}
\]
\[ 
f^{(n)} (x_0 ) \approx 
\im\nolimits_{1,2,\dots,n} \dfrac{ f(x_0 + h i_1 + \cdots + h i_n ) }{ h^n }
\]

For higher derivatives, some have also suggested 
to use Cauchy's formula,
\[
  \oint _C \dfrac{ f(x) }{ z - z_0 } dz = 2 \pi i f(z_0),
\]
which generalized to 
\[
  f^{(n)}(z_0) = 
  \dfrac{ n! }{ 2 \pi i }
  \oint _C 
  \dfrac{ f(x) }{ ( z - z_0 ) ^n } dz.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to discover\\ efficient mathematical identities} \\
  W. Zaremba et al.
\end{book}
The set of equivalent reformulations of a mathematical expression
(e.g., \verb|sum((A*B)^6)|, 
where \texttt{A} and \texttt{B} are matrices) form a graph, 
with edges corresponding to valid transformations.
Computer algebra systems (Maple, Mathematica)
use a set of heuristic rules to explore this graph and return a simpler expression
(either a shorter one, or one with a lower algorithmic complexity).
One can also use random exploration.

Instead, machine learning (e.g., $n$-grams, or RNN)
can guide the exploration.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A contextual-bandit approach\\ to personalized news article recommendation} \\
  L. Li et al.
\end{book}
There are 3 classes of bandit algorithms: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\epsilon$-greedy (with decaying $\epsilon$);
\item Upper confidence bounds (UCB);
\item Bayesian approaches (Gittins index).
\end{compactitem}
LinUCB generalizes UCB  to contextual bandits: 
if the reward is linear, \ie,
$E[ r_a | x_a ] = x_a ' \theta_a $, 
$\theta_a$ unknown, $a=\text{arm}$,
a confidence interval can be computed in closed form.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Frequentism and Bayesianism:\\ a Python-driven primer} \\
  J. VanderPlas (2014)
\end{book}
Frequentist confidence intervals can be problematic: 
for instance, for the truncated exponential 
$P(x|\theta) = e^{\theta-x} \mathbf{1}_{x > \theta}$
and the observed data 
$\{10, 12, 15\}$, 
the 95\% confidence interval is $(10.2, 12.2)$, 
even though we know that $\theta < 10$ -- 
the corresponding bayesian \d{credible interval}
is $(9.0, 10.0)$.

The paper also details Bayesian linear regression, 
with \texttt{emcee} (affine invariant ensemble MCMC),
\texttt{PyMC} (Metropolis-Hastings)
and \texttt{PyStan} (Hamiltonian MCMC),
and explains how to choose an uninformative prior: 
we want it to be invariant under the transformations 
$(x,y) \mapsto (y,x)$ 
and $(x,y) \mapsto (\lambda x, \lambda y)$ -- 
this second transformation 
gives the \d{Jeffreys prior}
for the variance, $P(\sigma) \propto 1/\sigma$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An object-oriented framework\\ for robust multivariate analysis} \\
  V. Todorov and P. Filzmoser (2009)
\end{book}
The \texttt{rrcov} package provides robust estimators of scale and location: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \d{Minimum covariance determinant} (MCD): 
  mean and variance of the $k$ observations 
  whose covariance matrix
  has the smallest determinant 
  (heuristically: 
  start with $k$ points, compute $\hat \mu$ and $\hat V$, 
  $d_i^2 = (x_i-\hat\mu)' \hat V^{-1} ( x_i - \hat\mu )$,
  take the $k$ points with the smallest distance, iterate);
\item \d{Minimum volume ellipsoid} (MVE) that contains at least half the data; 
\item \d{Stahel-Donoho}: weighted mean and covariance, 
  \begin{align*}
    w_i &= \min \biggl\{ 
      1, \Bigl( \dfrac{ c }{ r_i } \Bigr) ^ 2 
    \biggr\} \\
  r_i &= \max_a r(x_i, a ) && \!\!\!\text{outlyingness} \\
  \hspace{10mm}\llap{\ensuremath{r_i(x_i,a)}} &= \dfrac{
    \va{ x_i' a - m( a' X ) }
  }{
    s( a' X )
  }
  && \!\!\!\text{outlyingness in direction $a$} \\
  m, s &:\rlap{\text{ robust 1-dimensional location and scale;}}
  \end{align*}
\item Orthogonalized 
  Gnanadesikan-Kettenring (\d{OGK}): 
  given a robust 1-dimensional scale $\sigma$,
  define robust covariances
  \[
    s_{ij} = \dfrac14 \left[
      \sigma \left( \dfrac{X_i}{\sigma X_i} + \dfrac{X_j}{\sigma X_j} \right) ^2 -
      \sigma \left( \dfrac{X_i}{\sigma X_i} - \dfrac{X_j}{\sigma X_j} \right) ^2
    \right]
  \]
  and tweak the resulting covariance matrix to make it positive semidefinite;
\item \d{S-estimator}: solution of the optimization problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \mu, V \\
  \text{To minimize} & \sigma( d_1, \dots, d_n ) \\
  \text{Such that} & \det V = 1
\end{array}
\]
where $d_i = (x_i-\mu)' V^{-1} (x_i-\mu)$,
and $\sigma$ is an M-estimator of scale, \ie,
$ \tfrac1n \sum \rho(z/\sigma) = \delta, $
$\delta \in (0,1)$,
$\rho : \RR_+ \to [0,1] $ increasing from 0 to 1.
\end{compactitem}

It also provides \d{robust PCA}: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item PCA using a robust variance matrix; 
\item  Projection pursuit, 
  \ie, finding the direction in which some quantity
  (a robust 1-dimensional estimator of scale) is maximal;
\item Huber's method: consider all the directions defined by pairs of points, and project the data on those directions;
  for each point and each of those directions, 
  compute the normalized distance to the center, 
  using 1-dimensional robust estimators of location and scale; 
  use the $k$ points with the lowest distance. 
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust principal component analysis?} \\
  E.J. Cand\`es (2009)
\end{book}
If a matrix is the sum of a (dense) low-rank and a sparse matrix, 
$M = L + S$, the components can be recoverd by solving the convex optimization problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & L, S \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ L }_* + \lambda \n{ S }_1 \\
  \text{Such that} & L + S = M,
\end{array}
\]
where $\n{ \cdot }_*$ is the nuclear norm 
(sum of the singular values).
Depending on the application, $L$ or $S$ may be of interest.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to numerical methods\\ in differential equations} \\
  M.H. Holmes (2007)
\end{book}
% Book with many exercises

% Chapter 1. Initial value problems
\textbf{1.} 
To solve an \emph{initial-value problem} (IVP), 
\ie, an ordinary differential equation (ODE) 
of the form $y' = f(y,x)$, $y(0)=\alpha$,
one can discretize the derivative.
There are many ways of doing that 
(forward Euler, backward Euler, centered, leapfrog, etc.)
and the resulting numeric schemes have different
stability properties. 

A numeric scheme is \d{A-stable}
if the numeric solution of $y' = - r y$, $y(0)=\alpha$
remains bounded (the exact solution decays exponentially).
This equation is chosen because, near a stable (constant) solution, all ODEs look like that. 
A scheme is \emph{conditionally A-stable} if it is stable 
when the step size is sufficiently small. 
It is \emph{stricly A-stable} if the solution converges to zero.
It is \emph{monotone A-stable} if the solution is monotonic.
The forward (Euler) scheme is conditionally (monotone) A-stable and explicit; the backward scheme is (monotone) A-stable but implicit; the leapfrog method is unstable.

One can also integrate the equation, 
$y_{i+1} - y_i = \int_{t_i} ^{t_{i+1}} f(y,t) dt$
and use numeric integration, e.g., 
left box, right box, midpoint, 
trapezoidal rule 
  (implicit, A-stable, $O(h^2)$, conditionally monotone), 
Simpson's rule, etc.
The Adams method approximates $f$ with a function that can be integrated exactly 
(for instance, a quadratic approximation gives Simpson's rule).

The trapezoidal method is $O(h^2)$, but it is implicit. 
\begin{align*}
  y_{i+1} &= y_i + \tfrac12 h ( f_i + f_{i+1} ) \\
  f_i &= f( t_i , y_i ) \\
  f_{i+1} &= f( t_{i+1} , y_{i+1} ).
\end{align*}
It can be turned into an explicit method by replacing 
$f_{i+1}$, which we do not know yet, by its Euler approximation:
the second order \emph{Runge-Kutta} (RK) method (Heun) is 
$O(h^2)$, explicit, conditionally A-stable. 
\begin{align*}
  y_{i_1} &= y_i + \tfrac12 ( k_1 + k_2 ) \\
  k_1 &= h f( t_i , y_i ) \\
  k_2 &= h f( t_{i+1}, y_i + k_1 )
\end{align*}
%More generally, one can consider 
%\[
%y_{i+1} = y_i + h (
%  a f( t_i , y_i ) + 
%  b f( t_i + \alpha h , y_i + \beta h f( t_i, y_i ) )
%).
%\]
The popular RK4 method uses a similar idea, with Simpson's rule.

Newton's equation, $m y'' = F(y)$, conserves energy, 
$H(t) = \tfrac12 m y'^2 + V(y)$, where $V'(y) = - F(y)$, 
but not all numeric schemes do. The trapezoidal method does,
but it is implicit. The \emph{velocity Verlet method}
simply replaces it with its Euler approximation
(it is prefered to RK4 in physics).
It does not exactly conserve energy, but almost: 
it is \emph{symplectic}.

% Topics not addressed: p-step methods, stiff equations, L-stability, adaptive methods, bifurcations, etc.

% Chapter 2. Two-point boundary-value problems
\textbf{2.} 
A \emph{boundary value problem} (BVP) is an ODE of the form 
$y'' = f(x)$, $y(0)=\alpha$, $y(1)=\beta$.

If the equation is linear, 
$y'' + p(x) y' + q(x) y = f(x)$, 
the centered approximations of $y'$ and $y''$
give a tridiagonal system
(choose a step size $h < 2 / \n{ p }_\infty$).
If the condition number is $10^n$ 
(it is $O(N^2)$, where $N$ is the number of steps),
do not expect more than $15-n$ significant digits.

The discretization of non-linear BVP gives 
a non-linear system of equations, 
which can be solved by Newton's method 
(the Jacobian is tridiagonal);
the solution need not be unique.

\emph{Residual methods} look for solutions 
of the form $y(x) = \sum a_k \phi_k(x)$, 
for some basis functions $\phi_k$
(B-splines, Fourier) so that the residual 
be zero on a grid (\emph{collocation}) 
or minimal in $L^2$ norm (least squares).

\emph{Shooting methods} solve the IVP 
$y(0) = \alpha$, $y'(0) = s$, and adjust $s$
so that $y(1)=\beta$,
hoping the problem is not ill-conditionned.

% Topics not addressed: Sturm-Liouville theory (eigenvalue problems), finite elements (rewrite the equation in integral form and use basis functions).

% Chapter 3. Diffusion problems (parabolic PDEs)
\textbf{3.} 
The heat equation 
\begin{align*}
  D \dfrac{ \partial^2 u }{ \partial x^2 } &= 
  \dfrac{ \partial u }{ \partial t } \\
  u(0,t) &= u_{\text{left}}(t) \\
  u(1,t) &= u_{\text{right}}(t) \\
  u(x,0) &= g(x)
\end{align*}
is a parabolic equation: 
it is a \emph{diffusion} 
(it tends to smooth the initial condition -- 
$g$ need to be continuous, but $u$ will), 
it satisfies the \emph{maximum principle} 
(the maximum and minimum of $u$ are on the boundary)
and has the \emph{instant messaging} property
(even if $u(\cdot,0)$ is zero on some interval, it immediately becomes non-zero).

The explicit method 
(forward difference for time, centered for space), 
with \emph{stencil}
\[
% Stencil, explicit method
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
\draw (0-\x,1) -- (2+\x,1);
\draw (0-\x,0) -- (2+\x,0);
\draw (0,0-\x) -- (0,1+\x);
\draw (1,0-\x) -- (1,1+\x);
\draw (2,0-\x) -- (2,1+\x);
\node at (0,-.8) {$x_{i-1}$};
\node at (1,-.8) {$x_{i}$};
\node at (2,-.8) {$x_{i+1}$};
\node at (-1,0) {$t_j$};
\node at (-1,1) {$t_{j+1}$};
\fill[thick,black!60] (0,0) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (1,0) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (2,0) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (1,1) circle (.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
does not satisfy the instant messaging property 
and has stability problems as $t$ increases.
(PDEs have ad hoc notions of stability, e.g., 
by looking at how an oscillatory initial condition evolves;
the explicit method requires $k = O(h^2)$, where $k$ is the time step and $h$ the space step.)
The implicit method (backward difference) 
\[
% Stencil, implicit method
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
\draw (0-\x,1) -- (2+\x,1);
\draw (0-\x,0) -- (2+\x,0);
\draw (0,0-\x) -- (0,1+\x);
\draw (1,0-\x) -- (1,1+\x);
\draw (2,0-\x) -- (2,1+\x);
\node at (0,-.8) {$x_{i-1}$};
\node at (1,-.8) {$x_{i}$};
\node at (2,-.8) {$x_{i+1}$};
\node at (-1,0) {$t_j$};
\node at (-1,1) {$t_{j+1}$};
\fill[thick,black!60] (1,0) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (0,1) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (1,1) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (2,1) circle (.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
has the instant messaging property and is stable. 
Numerical simulations suggest that the exact solution often lies between the explicit and implicit ones. The \emph{theta method} mixes a proportion $\theta$ of explicit and $1-\theta$ of implicit. 
\[
% Stencil, Crank-Nicolson
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
\draw (0-\x,1) -- (2+\x,1);
\draw (0-\x,0) -- (2+\x,0);
\draw (0,0-\x) -- (0,1+\x);
\draw (1,0-\x) -- (1,1+\x);
\draw (2,0-\x) -- (2,1+\x);
\node at (0,-.8) {$x_{i-1}$};
\node at (1,-.8) {$x_{i}$};
\node at (2,-.8) {$x_{i+1}$};
\node at (-1,0) {$t_j$};
\node at (-1,1) {$t_{j+1}$};
\fill[thick,black!60] (0,0) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (1,0) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (2,0) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (0,1) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (1,1) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (2,1) circle (.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
For $\theta=\tfrac12$ (\emph{Crank-Nicolson} -- 
the most widely used method for parabolic equations), 
the error is $O(h^2) + O(k^2)$
(instead of $O(h^2) + O(k)$).
The trapezoidal rule (for time) also gives the Crank-Nicolson method. It is not robust to the presence of jumps.

The \emph{method of lines}
discretizes only the space: this gives a family
of coupled ODEs.

Residual methods, 
approximating the solution as 
$u(x,t) = \sum q_k(t) B_k(x)$,
can also be used. 

% Chapter 4. Advection equation
\textbf{4.} 
The advection equation is 
\[
\dfrac{ \partial u }{ \partial t } + 
a 
\dfrac{ \partial u }{ \partial x } = 0,
\quad
u(x,0) = g(x), 
\quad 
a>0.
\]
It can be solved eactly with a change of variables: 
$u(x,y) = g(x - at)$.
Contrary to the heat equation,
jumps in the initial condition $g$ are preserved 
(strictly speaking, this is a weak solution).
The upwind and downwind explicit schemes (forward difference in time, backward or forward in space)
\[
% Stencil, upwind
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
\draw (0-\x,1) -- (2+\x,1);
\draw (0-\x,0) -- (2+\x,0);
\draw (0,0-\x) -- (0,1+\x);
\draw (1,0-\x) -- (1,1+\x);
\draw (2,0-\x) -- (2,1+\x);
\node at (1,-.9) {upwind};
\node at (-1,0) {$t_j$};
\node at (-1,1) {$t_{j+1}$};
\fill[thick,black!60] (0,0) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (1,0) circle (.3);
%\fill[thick,black!60] (2,0) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (1,1) circle (.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\quad
% Stencil, downwind
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
\draw (0-\x,1) -- (2+\x,1);
\draw (0-\x,0) -- (2+\x,0);
\draw (0,0-\x) -- (0,1+\x);
\draw (1,0-\x) -- (1,1+\x);
\draw (2,0-\x) -- (2,1+\x);
\node at (1,-.9) {downwind};
\node at (-1,0) {$t_j$};
\node at (-1,1) {$t_{j+1}$};
%\fill[thick,black!60] (0,0) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (1,0) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (2,0) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (1,1) circle (.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
have different \emph{domains of dependence}.

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
%\fill[black!40] (1,2) -- (1,0) -- (-1,0);
%\draw[black!80,line width=4pt] (-1,0) -- (1,0);
%\fill[black!40] (1,2) -- (1,0) -- (3,0);
%\draw[black!80,line width=4pt] (3,0) -- (1,0);
\draw[->] (-\x,0) -- (1+\x,0);
\draw[->] (0,-\x) -- (0,2+\x);
\draw[thick] (1,2) -- (0,0);
\fill (1,2) circle (.1);
\fill (0,0) circle (.1);
\draw[black!80,line width=4pt] (-3pt,0) -- (3pt,0);
\node at (1+.5*\x,.6*\x) {$x$};
\node at (-\x/2,2+.5*\x) {$t$};
\node at (.5,-1.5*\x) {\mathstrut exact};
\end{tikzpicture}
\quad
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
\fill[black!40] (1,2) -- (1,0) -- (-1,0);
%\fill[black!40] (1,2) -- (1,0) -- (3,0);
%\draw[black!80,line width=4pt] (3,0) -- (1,0);
\draw[->] (-1-\x,0) -- (1+\x,0);
\draw[->] (0,-\x) -- (0,2+\x);
\draw[black!80,line width=4pt] (-1,0) -- (1,0);
\draw[thick] (1,2) -- (0,0);
\fill (1,2) circle (.1);
%\fill (0,0) circle (.1);
\node at (0,-1.5*\x) {\mathstrut upwind};
\end{tikzpicture}
\quad
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
%\fill[black!40] (1,2) -- (1,0) -- (-1,0);
%\draw[black!80,line width=4pt] (-1,0) -- (1,0);
\fill[black!40] (1,2) -- (1,0) -- (3,0);
\draw[->] (-\x,0) -- (3+\x,0);
\draw[->] (0,-\x) -- (0,2+\x);
\draw[black!80,line width=4pt] (3,0) -- (1,0);
\draw[thick] (1,2) -- (0,0);
\fill (1,2) circle (.1);
%\fill (0,0) circle (.1);
\node at (1.5,-1.5*\x) {\mathstrut downwind};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
The downwind scheme will not work. 
For the upwind one, the exact domain of dependence
is included in the numeric one 
(Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy (CFL) condition -- 
this did not happen with the heat equation)
if $ak/h \leq 1$.
More generally, one could look at methods of the form 
$ u_{i,j+1} = A u_{i+1,j} + B u_{i,j} + C u_{i-1,j}, $
compute a Taylor expansion of the error and set to zero 
as many terms as possible. % Lax-Wendroff
Implicit methods tend to have an infinite domain of dependence,
which is not desirable for advection equations.

% Chapter 5. Numeric wave propagation (hyperbolic equations)
\textbf{5.} 
The wave equation is 
\begin{align*}
  c^2 \dfrac{\partial ^2 u }{ \partial x^2 } &= 
  \dfrac{ \partial^2 u }{ \partial t^2 } \\
  u(0,t) &= u(1,t) = 0 \\
  u(x,0) &= f(x) \\
  u_t(x,0) &= g(x).
\end{align*}
It can be solved by separation of variables 
(Fourier series) or by a change of variable. 
Jumps in the initial conditions are preseved.
The domain of dependence is 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.4, xscale=.4]
\footnotesize
\fill[black!40] (1,2) -- (-.5,0) -- (2.5,0);
\fill (1,2) circle (.15);
\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (3,0);
\draw[->] (0,-.5) -- (0,2.5);
\draw[black!80,line width=4pt] (-.5,0) -- (2.5,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
To study the stability of hyperbolic equations
(or of their discretizations), look for solutions of the form 
$u(x,t) = e^{ i(kx-\omega t)}$. 
For the wave equation, this gives
$\omega = \pm ct$.
For other equations 
(Klein-Gordon, $c^2 u_{xx} = u_{tt}$; 
modified advection, $u_t + a u_x + b u = 0$; 
beam, $u_{xxxx} + u_{tt} = 0$; 
advection-diffusion, 
$D u_{xx} = u_t + a u_x$; etc.), 
it may be complex, 
$u(x,t) = e^{at} e^{ i( kx-bt) }$. 
The equation is 
\emph{stable} 
  if $\im \omega \leq 0$ (for all $k$), 
\emph{non-dispersive} 
  if $\re \omega \propto k$ 
  (the speed of the wave does not depend on the wavelength), 
\emph{non-dissipative}
  if $\im \omega = 0$ (for all $k$).
The explicit method (centered differences)
\[
% Stencil for hyperbolic PDEs
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
\draw (0-\x,2) -- (2+\x,2);
\draw (0-\x,1) -- (2+\x,1);
\draw (0-\x,0) -- (2+\x,0);
\draw (0,0-\x) -- (0,2+\x);
\draw (1,0-\x) -- (1,2+\x);
\draw (2,0-\x) -- (2,2+\x);
\node at (-1,0) {$t_{j-1}$};
\node at (-1,1) {$t_j$};
\node at (-1,2) {$t_{j+1}$};
\fill[thick,black!60] (1,0) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (0,1) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (1,1) circle (.3);
\fill[thick,black!60] (2,1) circle (.3);
\node at (0,-1) {$x_{i-1}$};
\node at (1,-1) {$x_{i}$};
\node at (2,-1) {$x_{i+1}$};
\draw[thick] (1,2) circle (.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
satisfies the CFL condition if $\lambda = ck/h \leq 1$; 
it is non dissipative for $\lambda \leq 1$
but dispersive for $\lambda < 1$
(problematic for short wave lengths).

% Chapter 6. Elliptic problems
\textbf{6.} 
The Laplace equation $\Delta u = 0$,
the prototypal elliptic PDE,
is the steady state of the wave equation: 
it has similar smoothing properties. 
It can be solved, with a finite difference approximation
(centered differences: 5-point scheme).
\[
% 5-point stencil for elliptic PDEs
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\footnotesize
\def\x{.5}
\draw (0-\x,2) -- (2+\x,2);
\draw (0-\x,1) -- (2+\x,1);
\draw (0-\x,0) -- (2+\x,0);
\draw (0,0-\x) -- (0,2+\x);
\draw (1,0-\x) -- (1,2+\x);
\draw (2,0-\x) -- (2,2+\x);
\node at (-1,0) {$y_{j-1}$};
\node at (-1,1) {$y_j$};
\node at (-1,2) {$y_{j+1}$};
\node at (0,-1) {$x_{i-1}$};
\node at (1,-1) {$x_{i}$};
\node at (2,-1) {$x_{i+1}$};
\draw[thick] (1,0) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (0,1) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (1,1) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (2,1) circle (.3);
\draw[thick] (1,2) circle (.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
The resulting matrix has the following sparsity pattern,
where $T$ is tridiagonal and $D$ diagonal.
\[
\left(\!\!\!  
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline={([yshift=-.8ex]current bounding box.center)}]
%\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=(current bounding box.center)]
\matrix (m) [
  matrix of math nodes,
  nodes in empty cells
%  right delimiter={]},
%  left delimiter={[} 
]{
T & D & & 0\\
D & &  & \\
&  & & D \\
0 &  & D & T \\
};
\draw (m-1-1)-- (m-4-4);
\draw (m-1-2)-- (m-3-4);
\draw (m-2-1)-- (m-4-3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\!\!\!\right)
\]

One can solve $Ax=b$, without inverting $A$, 
if $A$ is symmetric positive definite, 
by gradient descent, 
minimizing $F(x) = \tfrac12 x' A x - b x$: 
\begin{align*}
  x_{k+1} & \leftarrow x_k + \alpha_k d_k \\
  d_k &:\text{ descent direction} \\
  r_k &= b - A x_k = r_{k-1} - \alpha_{k-1} q_{k-1} \\
  q_k &= A d_k \\
  \alpha_k &= \argmin_\alpha F( x_k + \alpha d_k ) 
  = \dfrac{ d_k \cdot r_k }{ d_k \cdot q_k }.
\end{align*}
Steepest descent, $d_k = - \nabla F(x_k) = r_k$, 
may converge very slowly if the level surfaces of $F$ 
are eccentric ellipsoids, \ie, $\operatorname{cond} A \gg 1$
(but $\operatorname{cond} A \approx 4 N^2 / \pi^2 \gg 1$).
\emph{Conjugate gradient} addresses this problem by using 
$d_k = r_k + \beta_{k-1} d_{k-1}$,
where $\beta$ is such that 
$\forall i \neq j$, $r_i \perp r_j$, 
\ie, $\beta = \n{ r_{k+1} }^2 / \n{ r_k }^2$;
the solution is reached in (at most) $N$ steps.

To address the conditioning problem, find $B$ invertible 
such that $\operatorname{cond} BA \approx 1$ 
and solve $BA x = Bb$. 
To apply the conjugate gradient method, 
we need a symmetric (positive definite) matrix:
just rewrite the equation as 
$(BAB') B'^{-1} x = Bb$.
Popular choices for this \emph{preconditioner} 
include 
$M = B^{-1} B'^{-1} = \diag A$, 
$\operatorname{tridiag} A$ 
and $(D+L)D^{-1}(D+U)$
where $A=D+L+U$, $D$ diagonal, $L$ and $U$ strictly upper and lower triangular.

% Not detailed: multigrid (start with a coarse grid, find an approximate solution, refine the grid, iterate); finite elements.

\end{multicols}
\clearpage
\begin{multicols}{2}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic numerics\\ and uncertainty in computations} \\
  P. Hennig et al. (2015)
\end{book}

Many numeric (non-probabilistic) algorithms can be given a Bayesian flavour: Bayesian optimization is the best known, 
but not the only one.

In \d{Bayesian quadrature},
to estimate an integral, e.g., $\int_0^1 f$,
one models the integrand as a Gaussian process 
$f \sim \text{GP}$: since integration is linear, 
$( \int_0^1 f, f(t_1), \dots, f(t_n) )$ is Gaussian,
and one can estimate the conditional distribution 
$ \int_0^1 f \mid f(t_1), \dots, f(t_n) $.
This gives an estimate of the integral 
and of its precision -- but it depends a lot
on (the smoothness of) the prior.
One can also choose the point where to evaluate 
the function next to increase the precision the most.
The prior (the covariance function of the Gaussian process)
specifies the smoothness of the integrand 
(usually $\mathscr C^\infty$)
and perhaps other properties 
(e.g., the sign -- but the distributions would no longer be Gaussian).
Different priors lead to different quadrature rules. 

\[
  f \sim \text{GP}(m,C) 
  \implies 
  \textstyle \int f \sim N( \int m, \iint C )
\]

Bayesian quadrature can be generalized to 
\d{Bayesian ODE}s or PDEs.

\d{Bayesian decision theory} uses Bayesian quadrature 
to estimate and maximize expected utility.
(Bayesian optimization uses Bayesian decision theory 
to choose the next point to evaluate.)

\d{Bayesian linear algebra} tries to solve $Ax=b$
where $A$ to too large to be inverted, 
using the value of some products 
$A s_1 = y_1, \dots, A s_n = y_n$
to gain some knowledge about $A^{-1}$ and $A^{-1}b$.
The inverse $A^{-1}$ is not know but can be modeled as a Gaussian variable; 
$( A^{-1} b, A^{-1} y_1, \dots,  A^{-1} y_n ) $
is then Gaussian, 
and so is the conditional distribution 
$ A^{-1} b \mid s_1 = A^{-1} y_1, \dots, s_n = A^{-1} y_n $.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sampling for inference in probabilistic models with fast Bayesian quadrature} \\
  T. Gunter et al.
\end{book}
Bayesian quadrature,
to estimate expectations 
$E[\ell] = \int \ell(x) \pi(x) dx$,
with a Gaussian process (GP) prior on the integrand $\ell$,
is problematic when $\ell$ is a likelihood: 
the prior does not enforce non-negativity, 
and struggles to model the high dynamic range of most likelihoods.
A Gaussian prior on the log-likelhood is better, 
but the integral is far from Gaussian.
A square root transform,
$\ell(x) = \alpha _ f(x)^2$, $f \sim \text{GP}$,
is a good compromise; 
the integral is not Gaussian 
but can be approximated by a Gaussian, 
either by linearization 
or moment matching
(warped sequential active Bayesian integration, WSABI).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Projection pursuit} \\
  P.J. Huber (1985)
\end{book}
Given a random variable $X \sim f$ on $\RR^n$,
\d{projection pursuit}
looks for a projection 
$X \mapsto AX$ on a low-dimensional subspace 
(dimension 1, 2 or 3)
that maximizes (or minimizes) some 
\emph{projection index} $Q(AX)$.
This projection index may be: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Equivariant (mean or some other measure of location);
\item Location-invariant and scale-equivariant
  (standard deviation or some other measure of dispersion -- 
  this gives PCA and robust PCA);
\item Affine-invariant, e.g., normality test statistics
  (uninteresting projections tend to be more Gaussian 
  than those with idiosyncraties): 
  $\va{\text{skewness}}$, excess kurtosis 
  (or other standardized absolute cumulants -- 
  but they tend to be very outlier-sensitive),
  standardized Fisher information 
  \[ Q(X) = \sigma^2(X) \int \left( \dfrac{ f' }{ f } - \dfrac{ \phi' }{ \phi } \right)^2 f, \]
  standardized negative Shannon entropy 
  \[ Q(X) = - \int \log \left( \dfrac \phi f \right) f , \]
  Kolmogorov-Smirnov, Durbin-Watson, etc. 
  (this is now called independent component analysis, ICA).
\end{compactitem}

The $k$-dimensional projection can be approximated in a greedy (stepwise) fashion
(if you want a more precise result, use \emph{backfitting}: 
once you have a $k$-dimensional projection, remove one vector, find its optimal replacement, and iterate until convergence).

Given two random variables  $X$, $Y$ in $\RR^n$,
a discriminating hyperplane can be found by using the $T$-statistic as a projection index, or 
\[
  \dfrac{ 
    \operatorname{ave}[ a' X ] -
    \operatorname{ave}[ a' Y ] 
  }{
    \operatorname{sd}[ a' ( X \union Y ) ] 
  }
\]
or
\[
  \dfrac{ 
    \operatorname{med}[ a' X ] -
    \operatorname{med}[ a' Y ] 
  }{
    \operatorname{mad}[ a' ( X \union Y ) ] 
  }.
\]
The same idea gives a measure of outlyingness
\[ 
r_i = \sum_a \dfrac{
  a' x_i - \operatorname{med}[ a' X ]
}{
  \operatorname{mad}[ a' X ]
} 
\]
which can be used to define weights 
and robust (weighted) estimators of location and scale.

Regression is the estimation of 
$ f(x) = E[ Y | X = x ] $.
\d{Projection pursuit regression} (PPR)
estimates it as a sum of \emph{ridge functions}
\[ f(x) \approx \sum g_j ( a_j ' x ) \]
(note that the ridge functions are constant on hyperplanes).
% This is a generalization of GAM.
The ridge functions can be estimated greedily:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the residuals 
  $\displaystyle 
  r_i = y_i - \sum\limits_{1}^{m-1} g_j ( a_j ' x_i )$;
\item Smooth the scatterplot $r_i \sim a' x_i$: 
\[ r_i = g( a' x_i ) + \text{noise} \]
  (this depends on $a$);
\item Find the projection $a$ that minimizes 
\[ \sum_i \bigl( r_i - g( a' x_i ) \bigr)^2; \]
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}
Not all functions can be represented exactly 
as a finite sum of ridge functions
(but they can be approximated -- and, even if it is possible, 
the decomposition need not be unique.

\d{Projection pursuit density approximation} (PPDA) 
uses the same idea to approximate probability distribution functions, with a multiplicative decomposition (to ensure non-negativity)
\[ f(x) \approx f_0(x) \prod_1^k h_j( a_j' x ), \]
for some reference distribution $f_0$.
One can either look for 
\[ 
  f_k(x) = f_0(x) \prod_1^k h_j( a_j' x ) 
  \underset{k\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}
  f(x)
\]
or, dually, 
\[ 
  f_{-k}(x) = f(x) \prod_1^k h_j( a_j' x ) 
  \underset{k\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}
  f_0(x)
\]
(this removes the features of $f$, one by one, until we are only left with the reference (featureless) $f_0$).
The quality of the approximation can be measured with the 
\emph{relative entropy}
$ E(f,g) = \int \log(f/g), $
the \emph{Hellinger distance}
$ H(f,g) = \int ( \sqrt f - \sqrt g )^2, $
the Prohorov distance 
\begin{align*}
\pi(\mu,\nu) = \inf \bigl\{ 
  \epsilon > 0 
  \ :\ 
  \forall A \quad
  &\mu(A) \leq \nu(A_\epsilon)
  \text{ and }
  \\ &
  \nu(A) \leq \mu(A_\epsilon)
  \bigr\},
\end{align*}
the bounded Lipschitz metric
\begin{align*}
d(\mu,\nu) = \sup 
\Biggl\{
&
\va{ \int f d\mu - \int f d\nu} 
\ :\ 
f \in \mathscr C^0 , \\ &
\sup \va f \leq 1,
\quad
\sup_{x\neq y} \va{\dfrac{
  f(x) - f(y)
}{
  x - y
}} \leq 1 
\Biggr\},
\end{align*}
etc. 
One can proceed iteratively:
find $a$ minimizing $E(f_a, g_a)$
(where $f_a$ and $g_a$ are the marginal distributions
and $g = f_0$);
replace $f$ with $f g_a / f_a$ 
(resp. $g$ with $g f_a / g_a$);
iterate.

\d{Projection pursuit density estimation} (PPDE) 
applies the same idea to density estimation (from samples):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Standardize, using robust location and dispersion estimates;
\item Choose $f_0$ with fat tails; fit it to the cloud of points;
\item Proceed as above; 
  if the marginal densities are too time-consuming to estimate, 
  use sample-based estimates.
\end{compactitem}
(Only use when there is not enough data 
or too many dimensions for a kernel estimator.)

Deconvolution tries to recover a signal $x$
from a convolution $y = f * x$ with an unknown filter $f$.
\emph{Minimum entropy deconvolution}
considers segments of length $d$, 
$(y_t,\dots,y_{t+d-1})$,
as points of $\RR^d$,
and finds the least Gaussian 1-dimensional projection 
$q' \cdot y_{ t : t + d - 1 }$: 
$q$ is an estimate of $f^{-1}$.

If a time series $X_t$ is a sum of periodic signals with independent periods,
they can be recovered by finding $p$ maximizing 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $Q(p) = \operatorname{ave}_t[ Z_{p,t}^2 ]$,
  where $Z_{p,t} = \operatorname{ave}_k[ X_{t+kp} ]$;
\item Or 
  $\va{ C(p) }^2 = \va{
    \operatorname{ave}_t [ e^{ 2\pi i t / p } X_t ]
  }^2.$
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Reinforcement Learning} \\
  D. Silver (2015)
\end{book}
% https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL5X3mDkKaJrL42i_jhE4N-p6E2Ol62Ofa

% 1. Introduction
\textbf{1.}
Reinforcement learning (RL) is the search for the optimal policy for a Markov decision process (MDL) observed only through its history (past states, actions and rewards).
A RL agent can model one or several of: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The MDP itself;
\item The value of each state;
\item The policy.
\end{compactitem}
\emph{Planning} refers to the special case where the MDP is known.

% 2. MDP
\textbf{2.}
A \d{Markov reward process} is a Markov process (a Markov chain)
with rewards. The value of a state is 
\[ v(s) = E[ R_{t+1} + \gamma v(S_{t+1}) \mid S_t = s ], \]
\ie, $v = R + \gamma P v$.

A \d{Markov decision process} (MDP) adds actions.
We still have $v = R + \gamma P v$, 
but $v$, $R$ and $P$ depend on the policy $\pi$. 
The value of a state, or of a state-action pair, 
for the optimal strategy
(there exists an optimal deterministic strategy)
satisfies the Bellman equation: 
\begin{align*}
  v_*(s) &= \max_a q_*(s,a) \\
  q_*(s,a) &= R_s^a + \gamma \sum_{s'} P_{ss'}^a v_*(s').
\end{align*}

% 3. Planning: Dynamic programming
\textbf{3.} To find the optimal strategy for a known MDP
(planning), \d{policy iteration} starts with an arbitrary policy, evaluates the values of the states for this policy, computes the greedy policy for those values, and iterates.
Each step improves the policy and, in the limit, 
the Bellman equation is satisfied: 
it converges to the optimal policy.

\d{Value iteration} is a variant 
in which we do not compute the values exactly, 
but just iterate 
$v \leftarrow R + \gamma P v$ once.
There is actually no need to keep track of the policy, 
it suffices to iterate on the values: 
\[ 
  v(s) \leftarrow 
  \max_a R_s^a + \gamma \sum_{s'} P_{ss'}^a v(s). 
\]

There are many variants: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Update the states one at a time, in a random order;
\item Only update the most promising states; 
\item Use a sample of the transition matrix;
\item Focus on states close to the agent.
\end{compactitem}

% 4. Model-free prediction.
\textbf{4.}
To estimate the value function of a given policy 
on an unknown MDP (\emph{prediction}), 
\d{Monte Carlo RL} uses the average return 
(the sum of the discounted rewards)
from complete episodes (\ie, you have to let the MDP
run until it reaches a final state to know the return).
\d{Temporal difference learning} (TD) uses incomplete episodes: 
\begin{align*}
  \text{MC: } v(s_t) & \leftarrow
  v(s_t) + \alpha\bigl( G_t - v(s_t) \bigr) \\
  \text{TD: } v(s_t) & \leftarrow
  v(s_t) + \alpha\bigl( R_{t+1} + \gamma v(s_{t+1}) - v(s_t) \bigr).
\end{align*}
MC is unbiased but has high variance; 
TD is biased but has lower variance.
Instead of looking one step ahead,
we can look $n$ steps ahead and replace $G_t$ with 
\[
  G_t^{(n)} = R_{t+1} + \gamma R_{t+2} + \cdots + 
  \gamma^{n-1} R_{t+n} + \gamma^n v(s_{t+n}).
\]
\d{Forward-view TD($\mathbf\lambda$)} combines them all:
\begin{align*}
  G_t^\lambda &= (1-\lambda) \sum_{ n\geq1} \lambda^{n-1} G_t^{(n)} &&\text{($\lambda$-return)} \\
  v(s_t) & \leftarrow v(s_t) + \alpha \bigl( G_t^\lambda - v(s_t) \bigr).
\end{align*}
\d{Backward-view TD($\mathbf\lambda$)} only uses information from the past,
by assigning credit for the reward 
to the most recent and frequent states,
using \d{eligibility traces}.
\begin{align*}
  E_0(s) &= 0 \\
  E_t(s) &= \gamma \lambda E_{t-1}(s) + \mathbf1_{s_t=s} \\
  \delta_t &= R_{t+1} + \gamma v(s_{t+1}) - v(s_t) && \text{1-step-ahead error} \\
  v(s) &\leftarrow v(s) + \alpha \delta_t E_t(s)
\end{align*}
Backward- and forward-view TD($\lambda$) are equivalent.

% 5. Model-free control
\textbf{5.} \emph{Control} looks for 
the optimal value (or policy) of an unknown MDP.
It can be \emph{on-policy} 
(learn about the optimal policy while following it)
or \emph{off-policy}
(learn about the optimal policy while following another one).
Since the model is unknown, the \emph{$q$-value function}
is more useful than the value function to compute the policy.
\begin{align*}
  \pi(s) &= \argmax_a R_s^a + P_{ss'}^a v(s') && \text{($P$ is unknown)} \\
  \pi(s) &= \argmax_a q(s,a)
\end{align*}

\d{MC-$\epsilon$-greedy} control 
uses MC to evaluate the $q$-value function
and $\epsilon$-greedy exploration
(with probability $1-\epsilon$, 
choose the greedy (optimal) policy;
with probability $\epsilon$, act at random;
update the strategy at the end of each episode).

\d{SARSA} control uses SARSA (TD for the $q$-value function)
and $\epsilon$-greedy exploration
\[
  q(s,a) \leftarrow q(s,a) + 
  \alpha \bigl(
    R + \gamma q(s',a') - q(s,a)
  \bigr).
\]
As before, these can be generalized to 
forward-view SARSA($\lambda$)
\begin{align*}
 q_t^{(n)} &= R_{t+1} + \gamma R_{t+2} + \cdots + \gamma^{n-1} R_{t+n} + \gamma^n v(s_{t+n}) \\
 q^\lambda_t &= (1-\lambda) \sum \lambda^{n-1} q_t^{(n)} \\
 q(s_t,a_t) &\leftarrow q(s_t,a_t) + \alpha\bigl(
   q_t^\lambda - q(s_t,a_t)
 \bigr)
\end{align*}
and backward-view SARSA($\lambda$)
\begin{align*}
  E_0(s,a) &= 0 \\
  E_t(s,a) &= \gamma \lambda E_{t-1}(s,a) + \mathbf1_{s_t = s, a_t=a} \\
  \delta_t &= R_{t+1} + \gamma q(s_{t+1}, a_{t+1}) - q(s_t,a_t) \\
  q(s,a) &\leftarrow q(s,a) + \alpha \delta_t E_t(s,a)
\end{align*}

The step sizes usually decrease, but not too quickly
(Robbins-Monro sequence: $\sum \alpha_t = \infty$, 
$\sum \alpha_t^2 < \infty$).

Off-policy algorithms learn about a policy $\pi$ while following another policy $\mu$.
\d{Importance sampling MC}
\begin{align*}
  G_t^{\pi/\mu} &= 
  \dfrac{ \pi(a_t|s_t) }{ \mu(a_t|s_t) }
  \dfrac{ \pi(a_{t+1}|s_{t+1}) }{ \mu(a_{t+1}|s_{t+1}) }
  \cdots
  \dfrac{ \pi(a_T|s_T) }{ \mu(a_T|s_T) }
  G_t \\
  v(s_t) &\leftarrow v(s_t) + \alpha \bigl(
    G_t^{\pi/\mu} - v(s_t)
  \bigr)
\end{align*}
has a high variance.

For \d{importance sampling TD},
\[
  v(s_t) \leftarrow v(s_t) + \alpha \left(
  \dfrac{ \pi(a_t|s_t) }{ \mu(a_t|s_t) }
  \bigl( R_{t+1} + \gamma v(s_{t+1}) \bigr) - v(s_t)
  \right),
\]  
the variance remains reasonable.

\d{Q-learning} does not use importance sampling, 
but uses $\pi$ (not $\mu$)
in the target
\[
  q(a_t,s_t) \leftarrow 
  q(a_t,s_t) + \alpha \bigl(
  R_{t+1} + \gamma q(s_{t+1},a') - q(s_t,a_t)
  \bigr)
\]
where $a'$ comes from $\pi$ and $a_{t+1}$ from $\mu$.
In particular, 
when $\pi=\text{greedy}$ 
and $\mu=\text{$\epsilon$-greedy}$,
this becomes SARSAMAX (or ``Q-learning''): 
\[
  q(s,a) \leftarrow q(s,a) + \alpha \bigl(
  R + \gamma \max_{a'} q(s',a') - q(s,a)
  \bigr).
\]

\end{multicols}\begin{center}

% Summary table
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
  Algorithm & Model & Policy & Aim & on-/off-policy \\
  \midrule[1pt]
  Policy iteration & \multirow{2}{*}{known} & \multirow{2}{*}{unknown} & \multirow{2}{*}{$\pi^*$} & \\
  Value iteration \\
  \midrule
  MC & \multirow{2}{*}{unknown} & \multirow{2}{*}{known} & \multirow{2}{*}{$v$ or $q$} & \multirow{2}{*}{on} \\
  TD($\lambda$) \\
  \midrule
  MC + $\epsilon$-greedy & \multirow{4}{*}{unknown} & \multirow{4}{*}{unknown} & \multirow{4}{*}{$q^*$} & \multirow{2}{*}{on} \\
  TD($\lambda$) + $\epsilon$-greedy
  % (SARSA) 
  \\
  \cmidrule{1-1}
  \cmidrule{5-5}
  IS MC or TD &&&& \multirow{2}{*}{off} \\
  Q-learning
  % (SARSAMAX)        
\end{tabular}

\end{center}\begin{multicols}{2}

% 6. Value function approximation
\textbf{6.}
If the number of states is large, we cannot use a table lookup to model the value function $v(s)$ or $q(s,a)$:
one can summarize the state as a set of features and use them in a linear (or nonlinear) model.
This model attempts to minimize 
\[ J(w) = E_\pi \bigl( v_\pi(s) - v(s,w) \bigr)^2, \]
e.g., with stochastic gradient descent (SGD),
\[ \Delta w = \alpha \bigl( v_\pi(s) - v(s,w) \bigr) \nabla J(w), \]
where,
for a linear model $v(s,w) = x(s)'w$
with features $x(s)$,
the gradient is $\nabla J(w) = x(s)$,
\ie, 
\[
  \Delta w = 
  \text{step size} \times
  \text{prediction error} \times
  \text{feature value}.
\]

Since $v_\pi(s)$ is unknown, we use a target instead: 
the return $G_t$ for MC, 
$R_{t+1} + \gamma v(s_{t+1},w)$ for TD(0),
the forward or backward $\lambda$-return $G_t^\lambda$ for TD($\lambda$).

\d{Deep Q-networks} (e.g., those that play Atari games)
keep all the history and replay it (random mini-batches); 
they compute the Q-learning targets with some old, fixed 
(slowly updated) estimate of the $q$-function.

% 7. Policy gradient methods
\textbf{7.} 
Instead of the value function, one can directly learn a parametrized policy 
$\pi_\theta(s,a) = {}$probability 
of taking action $a$ in state $s$.
This allows stochastic policies.
The \emph{policy gradient theorem} links the gradient of the loss function 
to the \emph{score function} of the policy, 
$\nabla \log \pi$: 
\[ 
  \nabla J(\theta) = E_{ \pi_\theta } \bigl[
    \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta (s, a) q^{\pi_\theta}(s,a)
  \bigr].
\]
For instance, for a softmax, 
\begin{align*}
  \pi_\theta(s,a) &\propto \exp\bigl( \phi(s,a)' \theta \bigr) \\
  \nabla \log \pi &= \phi(s,a) - E_{\pi_\theta} [ \phi(s,\cdot) ]
\end{align*}
and for a Gaussian policy
\begin{align*}
  a & \sim N( \mu(s), \sigma^2 )
  , \qquad \mu(s) = \phi(s) ' \theta \\
  \nabla_\theta \log \pi &= \dfrac{\bigl(a-\mu(s)\bigr) \phi(s) }{ \sigma^2 }.
\end{align*}

The \d{Monte Carlo policy gradient} (``\textsc{reinforce}'')
algorithm estimates this expectation using one MC sample, \ie, 
$q^{\pi_\theta}(s,a) \approx r_t $.

The \d{actor-critic} method learns both the action-value function and the policy 
(it may be biased, but reduces the variance)
\begin{align*}
  &q_w(s,a) \qquad\text{critic} \\
  &\rlap{$\pi_\theta(s,a)$}\hphantom{Q_w(s,a)} \qquad\text{actor} \\
  &\Delta w = \text{as above} \\
  &\Delta\theta = \alpha \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta(s,a) q_w(s,a).
\end{align*}

The \d{advantage function} critic replaces $q_v(s,a)$
with the advantage function 
\[
  A^{\pi_\theta} (s,a) = q^{\pi_\theta}(s,a) - v^{\pi_\theta}(s)
\]
(this does not change the expectation but reduces the variance).
The \d{TD error} is an unbiased estimate of the advantage function 
\[ \delta = r + \gamma V_v(s') - V_v(s) \]
that only requires an estimate of $v$ instead of $q$.
(As before, you can replace the TD(0) target 
$r + \gamma v(s')$ with the return 
(MC target),
the $\lambda$-return (TD($\lambda$) target)
or use eligibility traces (backward-view TD($\lambda$)).)

% 8. Learning and planning
\textbf{8.} 
\d{Model-based reinforcement learning} 
learns the model (an MDP: the state and action spaces are known, 
the reward $(s,a) \mapsto r$ is a regression problem,
the transitions $(s,a) \mapsto s'$ a density estimation problem,
possibly with a Bayesian prior)
and uses it to estimate the value function or policy.

\d{Dyna} learns the model from experience
and the value function from both real experience and simulated experience.

\emph{Forward search} focuses on the sub-MDP starting at the current state.
In particular, \d{Monte Carlo tree search} (MCTS)
applies MC control to simulated experience 
(at first, we do not know the $Q(s,a)$, so we act at random until the end; this gives an estimate of some of the $Q(s,a)$; iterate with more and more paths -- this is how computers play go).

\d{TD search} applies SARSA to the sub-MDP starting at the current state.

\d{Dyna2} uses two sets of weights, long-term (TD learning, from real experience) and short-term (TD search), and adds them.

% 9. Exploration and exploitation

\textbf{9.}
A \d{multi-arm bandit} is a 1-state, 1-step MDP.
\begin{align*}
  & \text{action value} & q(a) &= E[ R \mid A = a ] \\
  & \text{optimal value} & v^* &= \max_a q(a) &&\llap{(unknown)}\\
  & \text{regret} & \ell_t &= E[ v^* - q(A_t) ] \\
  & \text{total regret} & L_t &= E 
  \Bigl[ 
  \sum_{t \leq \tau \leq T} v^* - q(A_\tau) 
  \Bigr] \\
  & \text{gap} & \Delta_a &= v^* - q(a) && \llap{(unknown)}
\end{align*}
We want to play the arms (actions) with a low gap more often.
For the \emph{greedy} (no exploration) and 
\emph{$\epsilon$-greedy} strategies,
the loss $L_t$ is linear in $t$.
\emph{Optimistic initialization} initializes $q(a)$ to a high value; it is still linear.
\emph{Decaying $\epsilon$-greedy} is sublinear, 
but choosing the schedule requires knowledge of the gaps.

``Optimism in the face of uncertainty'' 
uses an upper bound on $q(a)$: 
\(
  q(a) \leq \hat q_t(a) + \hat u_t(a) 
\)  
with high probability.
For instance, \d{UCB1} uses 
\emph{Hoeffding's inequality}
(which assumes $q$ bounded):
\[
a_t = \argmax_a \hat q(a) + \sqrt{
  \dfrac{ 2 \log t }{ N_t(a) }
}.
\]

\emph{Bayesian bandits} put a prior on $q$ and use some quantile of the posterior, e.g., $\hat q(a) + c \sigma(a)$.

\emph{Probability matching} tries to estimate 
\[ P[ \forall a' \ q(a) > q(a') ] \] 
from the posterior.
\d{Thompson sampling} crudely estimates this probability
by sampling from $q(a)$ (once for each action) and choosing the best action.

\emph{Information state search} extends the state space 
to include all the information so far: 
the bandit becomes an MDP (with an infinite state space).
For instance, a Bernoulli bandit gives an infinite tree of Beta distributions, and the values can be computed exactly (Gittins index) or with MCTS.

A \d{contextual bandit} is a 1-step MDP (with several states).

For MDPs, UCB is trickier: try optimistic initialization or the information state approach (with MCTS).

% 10. Classic games
\textbf{10.}
Let us consider 2-player zero-sum, perfect information (``Markov'') games.
Nash equilibria (\emph{joint} strategies) exist (provided you allow mixed strategies)
but need not be unique.
\d{Minimax} (depth-first) search 
\begin{align*}
  \pi &= \langle \pi_1, \pi_2 \rangle \\
  v_\pi(s) &= E_\pi[ G_t \mid S_t = s ] \\
  v_*(s) &= \max_{\pi_1} \min_{\pi_2} v_\pi(s) 
\end{align*}
explores the tree of possible actions -- 
usually truncated, with the leaf values estimated with some evaluation function.

\d{Simple TD} learns the value function with TD and uses it with minimax search.
\d{TD root} uses the minimax value (of the root of the tree) as the target.
\d{TD leaf} does not update the root but the leaf of the search tree (run a search from $s_t$, take a decision, end up in $s_{t+1}$, run a search from $s_{t+1}$, update the leaf of the first search towards the value of the second search).
\d{Treestrap} updates all the values in the search (not just the root or the leaf),
\ie, updates a shallow search from a deep one.
MCTS (simulate games until the end, from the current state, and apply RL to those self-play games) works well. 
UCB can be generalized (UCT).

