\begin{book}
  \emph{Phase plots of complex functions:\\ a journey in illustration}\\
  E. Wegert and G. Semmler (2011)
\end{book}

To plot a function $f$ of a complex variable, one often considers the surface
$z \mapsto \va{ f(z) } $
(or $\log \va{ f(z) }$),
sometimes with a colour
for the phase $ f(z) / \va{ f(z) } \in \SS^1$.
\emph{Phase plots} only plot the phase,
as a colour in the plane
(one can also change the brightness, using a sawtooth function of
$\log \va{ f(z) }$ or $ f(z) / \va{ f(z) }$
or a product of them).
Most of the information is still visible,
sometimes more clearly than with the surface:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Zeroes and poles are clearly visibles\;
  the colours changes on a simple loop give the difference between the number of zeroes and poles (counted with multiplicity) inside the loop;
\item The zeroes of $f'$ (that are not zeroes of $f$) are ``colour saddle points'', \ie, intersections of isochromatic lines;
\item Essential singularities are obvious;
\item Some results have a striking illustration, e.g.,
  the zeroes of the partial sums of a converging power series with finite convergence radius $R$ cluster at every point on $[\va z = R]$.
\end{compactitem}

\begin{centering}
<<WegertPhasePlotFunctions,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width='8cm',fig.show='hold'>>=
library(colorspace)

phase_plot <- function( 
  f, 
  xlim, ylim, nx = 500, ny = 500, k=20,
  which = 1
) {
  z <- expand.grid(
    x = seq( xlim[1], xlim[2], length=nx ),
    y = seq( ylim[1], ylim[2], length=ny )
  )
  z  <- matrix( z[,1] + 1i * z[,2], nx, ny )
  fz <- matrix( f(z), nx, ny )
  phase <- Arg( fz )
  if( which == 0 ) { 
    col <- "lightblue"
    if( k > 1 )
    col <- hex(HSV( seq(0,360,length=k+1), 1, 1 ))[ 1 + floor( k * (phase/(2*pi) + .5) ) ]
    persp( 
      log(abs(fz)), 
      col = col,
      axes = FALSE,
      box = FALSE,
      border = NA,
      #theta = 0, phi = 15,  # Default
      theta = 15, phi=15,
      shade = .5
    )
  } else if( which == 1 ) { 
    image(
      phase,
      col = hex(HSV( seq(0,360,length=k+1)[-1], 1, 1 )),
      breaks = seq(-pi-1e-6, pi+1e-6, length=k+1),
      axes=FALSE, useRaster = TRUE
    )
  } else if( which %in% c(2,3,4) ) { # Shade with a sawtooth function for the modulus, the phase, or both
    sawtooth <- function(x) x - floor(x)
    hue    <- phase/(2*pi) * 360 + 180
    value1 <- sawtooth( log(abs(fz)) )
    value2 <- sawtooth( phase / (2*pi) * 12 )   
    if( which == 2 ) { # Only the modulus
      value <- value1
    } else if( which == 3 ) { # Both modulus and phase
      value <- value1 * value2
    } else if( which == 4 ) { # Only phase
      value <- value2
    } 
    value <- value^.25
    im <- matrix(  hex(HSV( as.vector(hue), 1, as.vector(value) )), nx, ny )
    im <- im[, ncol(im):1]
    im <- t(im)
    im <- as.raster(im)
    plot(im)
  } else if( which == 5 ) { # Same as Mathematica: zeroes in black, poles in white
    h <- phase/(2*pi) * 360
    h <- ifelse( h < 0, h + 360, h )
    s <- 1/( 1   + .3 * log( 1 + abs(fz) ) )
    v <- 1 - 1/( 1.1 + 5  * log( 1 + abs(fz) ) )
    im <- matrix(  hex(HSV( as.vector(h), as.vector(s), as.vector(v) )), nx, ny )
    im <- im[, ncol(im):1]
    im <- t(im)
    im <- as.raster(im)
    plot(im)   
  } else { 
    stop( "Not implemented" )
  }
  invisible(fz)
}

# One simple root, one double root, one triple pole
f1 <- function(z) (z-1) * (z+1)^2 / (z-1i)^3

# Function with many roots
k <- 10
z1 <- runif(k,-1,1) + 1i * runif(k,-1,1)
z1 <- z1[ abs(z1) <= 1 ]
f2 <- Vectorize( function(z) prod(z-z1) )

# Power series with finite convergence radius
f3 <- Vectorize( function(z) 1+sum(z^(1:30)) )

# Blaschke product (function from the unit disk to itself, 
# with a finite (or countable) number of prescribed zeroes 
# (and no poles). For a countable number of zeroes, you 
# need sum(1-abs(z2)) < \infty.
k <- 50
z2 <- runif(k,-1,1) + 1i * runif(k,-1,1)
z2 <- z2[ abs(z2) <= 1 ]
f4 <- Vectorize( function(z) ifelse( abs(z) > 1, NA, prod( z-z2 ) / prod( 1 - Conj(z2) * z ) ) )
@

\vspace{-2mm}

<<WegertPhasePlotPersp,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=6,out.width='4cm',fig.show='hold'>>=
op <- par( mfrow=c(1,1), mar=.1*c(1,1,1,1), xaxs="i", yaxs="i" )
phase_plot(f1, xlim = c(-2,2), ylim = c(-1.5,2.5), which = 0, k = 0)
phase_plot(f1, xlim = c(-2,2), ylim = c(-1.5,2.5), which = 0, k = 1000)
par(op)
@

\vspace{-12mm}

<<WegertPhasePlotPhase,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width='8cm',fig.show='hold'>>=
op <- par( mfrow=c(1,3), mar=.1*c(1,1,1,1) )
phase_plot(f1, xlim = c(-2,2), ylim = c(-1.5,2.5), which = 5, k = 1000)
phase_plot(f1, xlim = c(-2,2), ylim = c(-1.5,2.5), which = 2, k = 1000)
phase_plot(f1, xlim = c(-2,2), ylim = c(-1.5,2.5), which = 3, k = 1000)
par(op)

op <- par( mfrow=c(1,3), mar=.1*c(1,1,1,1) )

# Simple examples
phase_plot(f1, xlim = c(-2,2), ylim = c(-1.5,2.5))
phase_plot(f2, xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1))
# Essential singularity
phase_plot(function(z) exp(1/z), xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1))

# Periodic functions
phase_plot(exp, xlim = c(-10,10), ylim = c(-10,10))
phase_plot(sin, xlim = c(-10,10), ylim = c(-10,10))
phase_plot(function(z) elliptic::P( z, g=c(5,1) ), xlim = c(-5,5), ylim=c(-5,5))

# Zeta function (only accurate for abs(z)<100)
phase_plot(pracma::zeta, xlim = c(-40,2), ylim = c(-2,40))
# Power series and its convergence radius
phase_plot(f3, xlim = c(-1.2,1.2), ylim = c(-1.2,1.2))
# Blaschke product, restricted to the unit disk
phase_plot(f4, xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1))

par(op)

op <- par( mfrow=c(1,3), mar=.1*c(1,1,1,1) )
phase_plot( function(z) z, k=100, xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1) )
phase_plot( function(z) z^2, k=100, xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1) )
phase_plot( function(z) z^3, k=100, xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1) )

phase_plot( function(z) -sqrt(z), k=100, xlim = c(-1,1), ylim = c(-1,1) )
phase_plot( log, xlim = 2*c(-1,1), ylim = 2*c(-1,1), k=50  ) 
phase_plot( function(z) exp(-z^2/2), xlim = 5*c(-1,1), ylim = 5*c(-1,1) )
par(op)

@
\end{centering}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introducing \texttt{elliptic}, an R package\\ for elliptic and modular functions} \\
  R.K.S. Hankin
\end{book}
The \texttt{elliptic} R package provides tools to deal with 
(and plot) complex functions, 
in particular elliptic functions 
(doubly periodic meromorphic functions)
such as Weierstrass's $\wp$ and related 
($\zeta'\! = - \wp,\, \sigma'/\sigma = \zeta$)
functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Topology and data} \\
  G. Carlsson (2009)
\end{book}

\textbf{1.}
\d{Topological data analysis} estimates the 
\d{Betti numbers} 
$\beta_k(X,F) = \dim H_k(X,F)$
of a topological (or metric) space $X$ 
(often, a subspace of $\RR^n$),
over a field $F$ (often $\FF_2$), 
from a finite number of points sampled from $X$.

An abstract \emph{simplicial complex}
is a pair $(V,\Sigma)$,
where $V$ is a finite set
and $\Sigma \subset \mathscr P(V)$
such that 
$\sigma \in \Sigma , \  \tau \subset \sigma
\implies \tau \in \Sigma. $
Its topological realization is 
\[ 
\bigcup_{ \sigma \in \Sigma } 
\operatorname{chull}( \{ e_i \ :\ i \in \sigma \} ) 
\]
where $(e_i)_{i \in V}$
is the canonical basis of $\RR^V$.

The \emph{homology} of a simplicial complex 
$X = (V,\Sigma)$
is defined as
\begin{align*}
  &\Sigma_k = \{ \sigma \in \Sigma \ : \ \# \sigma = k+1 \} && \text{$k$-simplices} \\
  & C_k = \ZZ^{\Sigma_k} && \text{$k$-chains} \\
  & (V, \leq) && \text{total order} \\
  & d_i(\sigma) = \sigma \setminus \text{$i$th element of $\sigma$} \\
  &d_i : \Sigma_k \longrightarrow \Sigma_{k-1} \\
  &\partial_k = \sum (-1)^i d_i : C_k \longrightarrow C_k \\
  &\partial_k \circ \partial_{k+1} = 0 
  \text{, therefore }
  \im \partial _{k+1} \subset \ker \partial_k \\
  &H_k(X,\ZZ) = \dfrac{\ker \partial_k}{\im \partial_{k+1}}.
\end{align*}

Let $\mathscr U = ( U_\alpha )_{ \alpha \in A }$
be an open covering of a topological space $X$.
The \d{nerve} $N \mathscr U$ 
is the abstract simplicial complex $(A, \Sigma)$
where $\sigma = \{ \alpha_0,\dots,\alpha_k \} \in \Sigma$
iff $\bigcap_{\alpha \in \sigma} U_\alpha \neq \nothing$.
If
all intersections of elements of $\mathscr U$ 
are empty or contractible,
it is homotopy equivalent to $X$.

The \d{\v Cech complex}
$\check C(V, \epsilon)$
is the nerve of the covering 
of $X$ with balls of radius $\epsilon$ 
with centers in a finite subset $V \subset X$.
If $X$ is compact, Riemannian, $\epsilon$ sufficiently small
and $V$ well chosen, 
then $\check C(V,\epsilon)$
is homotopy equivalent to $X$.

The \d{Vietoris-Rips complex}
$\text{VR}(V,\epsilon)$
is $(V,\Sigma)$,
with $\sigma = \{x_0,\dots,x_k\} \in \Sigma$
iff $\forall i,j \ d(x_i,x_j) \leq \epsilon$, \ie, 
all $(x_i,x_j)$ span a \v Cech 1-simplex.
(Its 1-skeleton is the ``truncated graph'' 
from the distance matrix.)

The \d{Delaunay complex}
is the nerve of the covering by Voronoi cells 
\[
  V_\lambda = \{ x \! \in \! X 
  \ :\ 
  \text{$x$ closer to $\lambda$ 
  than to any other $\lambda'\! \in \!V$}
  \}.
\]

The \d{strong witness complex} of $X$, 
wrt a finite set of points (landmarks)
$\mathscr L \subset X$,
is a fattening of the Delaunay complex,
$W^s(X,\mathscr L, \epsilon) = ( \mathscr L, \Sigma)$
with $\{ \ell_0, \dots, \ell_k \} \in \Sigma$
iff 
\[ 
\exists x \in X \ :\ \forall i \  d(x, \ell_i) \leq d(x, \mathscr L) + \epsilon. 
\]
The \d{weak witness complex} 
$W^w(X,\mathscr L, \epsilon)$ is $( \mathscr L, \Sigma)$
with $\{ \ell_0, \dots, \ell_k \} \in \Sigma$
iff 
\begin{align*}
&
\forall \Lambda \subset \{ \ell_0,\dots,\ell_k\} \quad
\exists x \in X \quad
\forall \ell \in \mathscr L \setminus \Lambda \quad
\forall \ell_i \in \Lambda  \\
&
d(x,\ell) + \epsilon \geq d(x,\ell_i).
\end{align*}
There are Vietoris-Rips variants 
$W_{\text{VR}}^s$ and $W_{\text{VR}}^w$.

All those complexes are \emph{functorial} in $\epsilon$:
the inequality $\epsilon \leq \epsilon'$
induces an inclusion 
$\check C(V,\epsilon) \hookrightarrow \check C(X,\epsilon)$,
which in turn induces a morphism of homology groups 
$H_k \check C(V,\epsilon) \longrightarrow 
H_k \check C(V,\epsilon')$.

The \d{$\RR$-persistence homology}
is the functor
\[
\left\{
\begin{array}{ccl}
(\RR_+^{>0}, \leq) & \longrightarrow & \mathfrak{Mod}\, \ZZ \\
\epsilon & \longmapsto & H_k \check C(V,\epsilon).
\end{array}
\right.
\]

$\RR$-persistence $\ZZ$-modules are complicated objects, 
but there is a classification theorem 
for finitely-generated $\NN$-persistence $F$-vector spaces,
\[
  (V_n)_{n \in \NN} \equiv 
  \bigoplus_{i=0}^N U(m_i,n_i),
\]
where \[
U(m,n) _t = \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
F & \text{if $m \leq y \leq n$} \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{array}
\right.
\]
and $U(m,n)_s \longrightarrow U(m,n)_t$
is the identity for $m \leq s \leq t \leq n$.
Note that 
\(
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=.06,xscale=.12]
\draw (-3,1) -- (3,1);
\draw (-3,-1) -- (3,-1);
\draw[line width=2.5pt] (-2.5,1) -- (0,1);
\draw[line width=2.5pt] (0,-1) -- (2.5,-1);
\end{tikzpicture}
\neq
\raisebox{1.8pt}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=.06,xscale=.12]
\draw (-3,0) -- (3,0);
\draw[line width=2.5pt] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}
\)
even though all the dimensions are the same.

The $\NN$-persistence $F$-homology 
of a finite set $X$ 
(choose a subset $\{ \epsilon_n,\  n \in \NN \} \subset \RR$)
can be represented as a \d{barcode}.

The actual computations 
require linear algebra
(diagonalizing $\partial$ -- Smith normal form)
over the principal ideal domain $F[t]$
($\NN$-persistence vector spaces are equivalent to
graded $F[t]$-modules).

\textbf{2.}
Those ideas have been applied to image data: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Consider $3\times3$ patches (points in $\RR^9$);
  only keep those with high contrast (top 20\%);
\item Center and normalize (points in $\SS^7$); sub-sample;
\item Estimate the density with the distance to the nearest neighbours ($k=15$)
and keep the top $T$\% densest points ($T=20\%$);
\item Build the witness complex on 50 landmark points 
  chosen by \d{archetypal analysis}.
\end{compactitem}
The barcodes suggest 
$\beta_0 = 1$, $\beta_1 = 5$, 
which can be realized as the union of 3 circles,
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.5,yscale=.5]
\draw[black!60!blue,ultra thick] (0,0) circle (1.25);
\draw[black!60!red,ultra thick] (0,0) ellipse (.5 and 1.25);
\draw[black!60!green,ultra thick] (0,0) ellipse (1.25 and .5);
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt]
\node (A) at ++(0:1.25) {};
\node (A) at ++(90:1.25) {};
\node (A) at ++(180:1.25) {};
\node (A) at ++(270:1.25) {};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
corresponding to edges (or gradients), 
horizontal lines and vertical lines
(consistent with the presence of horizontal 
and vertical artefacts: horizon, buildings, people, trees, etc.).
Looking at $\beta_2$ suggests (unconvincingly) 
that those cricles may be embedded in a Klein bottle.
