For datasets in which one observation is a cloud of points
(e.g., a few statistics estimated on a moving window -- say, 
the number of spikes for the 5 neurons firing the most, 
in brain activity data),
you can use Betti numbers 
(or the most frequent Betti signatures) as features.

\textbf{3.}
Given a map $\rho : X \to Z$
and a covering $\mathscr U$ of $Z$
(with $Z = \RR$ or $\RR^n$ or $\SS^1$),
$\rho^* \mathscr U$ is a covering of $X$;
consider the \v Cech complex of its connected components
$\check C ^{\pi_0} ( \rho^* \mathscr U )$, 
e.g., for $\mathscr U = \mathscr U[R,e] = \{
 [k R - e , (k_1) R + e ], \ k \in \ZZ
\}. $
For point clouds, 
replace the connected components $\pi_0$ 
with single linkage clustering:
points less than $\epsilon$ apart are in the same component
(these are the Vietoris-Rips connected components).
The map $\rho$ could be a density estimator, 
data depth 
\[
\rho_p(x) = \dfrac1{\va X} \sum_{y \in X} d(x,y)^p
\]
or eigenvectors  of the graph Lapalcian of the 1-skeleton of the Vietoris-Rips complex, seen as (eigen) functions.
To avoid choosing $\epsilon$, 
consider the simplicial complex $SS(X, \rho, \mathscr U)$
whose vertices are $(\alpha, I)$,
with $\alpha \in A$ and $I = (\epsilon_i, \epsilon_{i+1})$
a (maximal) interval on which the barcode of 
$H_0 \check C ( \rho^{-1} U_\alpha, \epsilon ) )_\epsilon$
does not change 
and whose $k$-simplices are the 
$\{ (\alpha_0,I_0), \dots, (\alpha_k, I_k) \}$
such that 
$A_{\alpha_0} \inter \cdots \inter A_{\alpha_k}
\neq \nothing$
and 
$I_0 \inter \cdots \inter I_k \neq \nothing$.
Choose a section of the projection, 
$s : \check C \mathscr U \rightarrow SS$,
$s(\alpha) = (\alpha, I_\alpha)$,
and choose $\epsilon_\alpha \in I_\alpha$.
[The results are similar to Isomap. 
I am not convinced this is significantly different 
from a minimum spanning tree.]

\textbf{4.}
Persistent homology can be generalized to other index posets.
For instance, 
$\text{VR}( X[T], \epsilon) _{ \epsilon, T} $
where $X[T] = \{ x \in X \ :\ \phi(x) \geq T \} $
and $\phi$ is a density estimator
on the finite set $X$ 
(or some other function -- this is 
\emph{Morse theory}),
gives $\RR\times \RR$- (or $\NN\times\NN$-) persistent homology.
It can be described with multigraded modules over 
$k[X_1,\dots,X_n]$ (here, $n=2$).
The classification of those modules is too complex to be useful
(but may be amenable to \emph{Gr\"obner bases}),
but simpler invariants are more accessible, e.g., 
$\dim M_{t_1,\dots,t_n}$ or 
\[ 
\rank\bigl( 
  M_{t_1,\dots,t_n}
  \longrightarrow
  M_{t_1',\dots,t_n'}
\bigr) 
\]
(for $n=1$, the ranks contain all the information).

Zigzag diagrams (\emph{quiver representations})
appear in the following sotuations: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pairwise comparisons of subsamples (bootstrap)
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = 2ex,row sep=0pt,inner sep=1pt]
   & S_0 \union S_1 && S_1 \union S_2 && \phantom{S_2 \union S_3} \\
   &&&&& \cdots \\
   S_0 \arrow[uur] && S_1 \arrow[uul] \arrow[uur] && S_2 \arrow[uul] \arrow[uur]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
\item Samples from a moving window;
\item Upper level sets ($T$\%) of a density estimators 
  with varying bandwidths $\epsilon_i$
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[
  column sep = {1.8cm,between origins},
  row sep = 1ex,
  inner sep = 1pt
]
   & X[T,\epsilon_{i-1}] \union X[T,\epsilon_{i}] && X[T,\epsilon_{i}] \union X[T,\epsilon_{i+1}] && \phantom{X[T,\epsilon_{i-1}] \union X[T,\epsilon_{i}]} \\
   \cdots&&&\cdots \\
   X[T,\epsilon_{i-1}] \arrow[uur] && X[T,\epsilon_{i}] \arrow[uul] \arrow[uur] 
\end{tikzcd}
\] 
\item Witness complexes as the set of landmarks change
\[
\hspace*{-8mm}
\begin{tikzcd}[
  column sep = {1.8cm,between origins},
  row sep = 1ex,
  inner sep = 1pt
]
   \phantom{W(X, \mathscr L_{i}, \epsilon)}
   &&
   W(X, \mathscr L_{i}, \epsilon)
   &&
   \phantom{W(X, \mathscr L_{i}, \epsilon)}
   \\
   \cdots&&&&\cdots \\
   & W(X, \mathscr L_{i-1}, \mathscr L_i, \epsilon)
   \arrow[uul] \arrow[uur] &&
   W(X, \mathscr L_{i}, \mathscr L_{i+1}, \epsilon)
   \arrow[uul] \arrow[uur] 
\end{tikzcd}
\] 
\end{compactitem}
There is a classification theory for finite zigzag persistence vector spaces: the elementary blocks are of the form
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[
  column sep = {8mm,between origins},
  row sep = {5mm,between origins},
  inner sep = 1pt
]
  0 && F && F && 0 \\
  \cdots && && && \cdots \\
  &  F \arrow[uul] \arrow[uur] 
  && F \arrow[uul] \arrow[uur] 
  && 0 \arrow[uul] \arrow[uur] 
\end{tikzcd}
\] 

\textbf{5.}
The paper ends with a discussion of functorial clustering algorithms
(functors from the category of finite metric spaces with isometries, 
embeddings, non-increasing embeddings, non-increasing maps,
to the catogory of sets.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Barcodes: the persistent topology of data} \\
  R. Ghrist (2008)
\end{book}
Another (shorter) clear review article.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Computational topology for point data:\\ Betti numbers of $\alpha$-shapes} \\
  V. Robins (2002)
\end{book}
To estimate the Betti numbers
(intuitively, $\beta_k(X)$ is the number of $k$-dimensional 
holes in $X$)
of $X \subset \RR^d$ 
from a finite set of points $S \subset X$, 
attach spheres of increasing radius $\alpha$ at each point: 
\begin{align*}
  S_\alpha &= \bigcup_{ x \in X } B( x, \alpha ) \\
  \beta_k^\alpha(S) &= \dim H_k ( S_\alpha )
\end{align*}
for $\alpha \geq d_{\text{Hausdorff}}(S,X)$.
The persistent Betti number
$\beta_k^{\alpha,\beta}(S) = 
\rank \bigl(
i_* : H_k(S_\alpha) \rightarrow H_k(S_\beta)
\bigr),
$
for 
$\alpha \leq \beta$
and
$i : S_\alpha \hookrightarrow S_\beta$,
is, intuitively,
the number of $k$-dimensional holes in $S_\alpha$ that are not filled in $S_\beta$.

The number of connected components $\beta_0^\alpha(S)$
can be obtained from the minimum spanning tree of $S$.
Higher Betti numbers can be computed 
from the Delaunay complex of $S_\alpha$ in $S$ 
(the dual of the Voronoi complex of $S_\alpha$ in $S$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{JavaPlex tutorial} \\
  H. Adams and A. Tausz (2015)
\end{book}
JavaPlex is a Java/Matlab library to compute 
the persistent homology of filtered simplicial complexes. 
The tutorial ends with the 3-circle structure of image patches.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A roadmap for the computation\\ of persistent homology} \\
  N. Otter et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Comparison of software to compute persistent homology: 
JavaPlex (well documentated), 
Dionysus (C++/Python, documented),
Dipha (C++, MPI, command line interface, fast),
Gudhi (C++, fast),
Perseus, 
Simpers,
jHoles,
pHat,
CHomP.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Three examples of applied\\ and computational homology} \\
R. Ghrist (2008)
\end{book}
Concrete applications of algebraic topology include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Euler characteristic integration}
  (constructible sheaves) to aggregate sensor data:
  the Euler characteristic is a measure
  since, under some circumstances,
  $\chi(A \union B) = \chi(A) + \chi(B) - \chi(A \inter B)$;
\item \emph{Persistent homology}
  to understand the shape of and identify the structures in 
  high-dimensional datasets, e.g., 
  natural images or brain activity;
\item \emph{Conley index theory} 
  (a generalization of Morse theory)
  to distinguish between chaotic and noisy 
  (experimental or simulated) data.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How to write a 21st century proof} \\
  L. Lamport (2012)
\end{book}
Advocacy for the replacement of ``informal proofs''
(which we have been writing since the 17th century)
with ``structured proofs'': 
sequences of statements, each with a proof 
(itself structured, if needed). 
It is still too early for mathematicians to write formal (computer-verified) proofs, 
but familiarity with them can be helpful; 
the appendix shows a proof in TLA$^+$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An extensible SAT-solver} \\
  N. E\'en and N. S\"orensson (2004)
\end{book}
Walk through the MiniSAT code -- 
should you want to implement your own SAT solver

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conflict-driven clause learning SAT solvers} \\
J. Marques-Silva et al. (2008)
\end{book}
DPLL is the basic SAT solving algorithm: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Write the formula as $\bigwedge_i \bigvee_j x_{ij}$;
\item If one of the clauses has only one term, set it to true;
\item If one of the variables always appears with the same sign,
  e.g., always $x$, or always $\neg x$, set it to true (or false);
\item When you can no longer apply the previous steps, 
  choose a value for one of the variables; 
  backtrack when needed.
\end{compactitem}

But this is suboptimal: 
when we reach a contradiction, 
we backtrack and try another value for some variable,
but if it is unrelated to the contradiction, nothing will change, 
and we will explore the same tree again and again.
\emph{Clause learning} identifies the cause of the contradiction
and adds it as a new clause, to help prune the search tree.

To find one (or several) clauses to add, 
represent the current state of the exploration as a graph, 
whose nodes are the possible values of the variables
% (e.g., ``9f'' for $x_9=0$) 
and with the clauses used for the deductions as arrows (all inbound edges have the same label),
and find a cut between the latest decision variable and the conflict node.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Practical applications of Boolean satisfiability} \\
  J. Marques-Silva 
\end{book}
SAT applications include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Hardware verification: checking that two circuits are equivalent;
\item Circuit testing: automatic test generation, for boolean circuits, assuming the failure is that of a single value stuck at 0 or 1;
\item Planning: checking if a state is reachable in $k$ steps, in a deterministic transition system, with several transition functions, corresponding to the decisions; temporal logic;
\item Bioinformatics, e.g., inferring haplotypes from genotypes: 
  a genotype is a string over the alphabet $\{0,1,2\}$ (wild, mutant, heterozygous); a haplotype is a string over $\{0,1\}$; each genotype comes from two haplotypes; the genotypes are known but the haplotypes are not; we want the minimum number of haplotypes to explain the genotypes.
\end{compactitem}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% Shorter articles

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian structural time series models} \\
  S.L. Scott (2015)
\end{book}
Structural models (level, trend and seasonality) 
can be put in state-space form and used 
for ad campain effectiveness measurement. 
The model can be extended to allow 
non-Gaussian innovations (by expressing them as an
(infinite) mixture of Gaussians)
and long-term trends (by using a mean-reverting AR(1) slope
instead of a random walk).

In R, check the \texttt{bsts} and \texttt{CausalImpact} packages.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Experiments in the internet age:\\ a modern look at the multi-armed bandit} \\
  S.L. Scott (2014)
\end{book}
Planning an experiment and running it until the end is suboptimal
(in terms of regret): 
you spend a lot of time estimating precisely options 
that are suboptimal.
In the 2-arm bandit setup,
$P(\theta_1 > \theta_2)$
can be estimated analytically,
or estimated by sampling from the posteriors of $\theta_1$ and $\theta_2$: \emph{Thompson sampling} uses a single sample and works well.
Full factorial experiments are rarely possible, 
but one can design fractional factorial experiments 
to estimate the coefficients as precisely as possible 
[this is not unlike Bayesian optimization].

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Big data, statistics and the internet} \\
  S.L. Scott (2014)
\end{book}
Gibbs sampling
(sample from $\beta | \mu,V$; sample from $\mu,V|\beta$; iterate)
cannot be directly, efficiently implemented with MapReduce
(there is too much communication and virtually no CPU usage -- 
the complexity of most MapReduce algorithms is 
the volume of communications, not the computations).
Instead, \d{consensus Monte Carlo} 
gives different bits of data to various workers,
which work intependently
(sample from $\beta | \mu,V, \text{data}_w$; 
sample from $\mu,V|\beta, \text{data}_w$; 
iterate) and the results are combined.
Contrary to distributed optimization (ADMM)
or horizontal strategies for stochastic optimization
(progressive hedging),
it is a simple consensus -- there are no increasing penalties
to force convergence.

This can be applied to hierarchical Bayesian Poisson regression
($2\times 10^7$ observations, $10^4$ groups, $10$ variables).
% Also mentions BART (Bayesian additive regression trees).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to RKHS\\ and some simple kernel algorithms} \\
  A. Gretton (2015)
\end{book}
Given a kernel $k : X \times X \rightarrow \RR$
on a finite set $X$,
one can consider the Hilbert space 
\begin{align*}
  & H = \operatorname{Span} \{ k(x,\cdot), \ x \in X \} \subset \mathscr F( X, \RR ) \\
  & \Bigl\langle 
  \sum_x \alpha_x k(x, \cdot)\ ,\ \sum_y \beta_y k(y, \cdot)
  \Bigr\rangle 
  := \sum_{x,y} \alpha_a \beta_y k(x,y).
\end{align*}
It satisfies: 
\begin{align*}
  & H \subset \mathscr F( X, \RR ) \\
  & \forall x \in X \quad k(x, \cdot) \in H \\
  & \forall x \in X \quad \forall f \in H \quad \langle f, k(x,\cdot) \rangle = f(x) \\
  & \n{ \delta_x } = k(x,x)^{1/2} < \infty, 
\end{align*}
where 
\[
  \delta_x : \left\{ 
  \begin{array}{rcl}
    H & \longrightarrow & \RR \\
    f & \longmapsto & f(x) 
  \end{array}
  \right.
\]
is the evaluation function.

Those results generalize to infinite $X$, and $H$ is called a 
\emph{reproducible kernel Hilbert space} (RKHS) for $k$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable Bayesian optimization\\ using deep neural networks} \\
  J. Snoek et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Gaussian-process-based optimization scales 
cubically with the number of observations. 
Instead, one can train a neural net on the data 
\[
\text{input}
\xrightarrow{\tanh}
\cdots
\xrightarrow{\tanh}
\phi_1 \cdots \phi_D 
\xrightarrow{\text{linear}}
\text{output}
\]
(prefer tanh to ReLU)
and fit a Bayesian linear model using the last layer 
\( y  = \sum \beta_i \phi_i + \epsilon \)
(\emph{adaptive basis regression}).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast exact summation\\ using small and large superaccumulators} \\
  R.M. Neal (2015)
\end{book}
Naive computation of long sums can lead to rounding error accumulation.
There are approximate algorithms (Kahan-Babu\v ska),
but it is actually possible to compute those sums exactly, 
by using a ``superaccumulator'',
\ie, 4096 64-bit numbers,
one for each possible exponent
(all but one bit overlap: carry propagation is rarely needed).
For small sums, 
a smaller number of 64-bit numbers (e.g., with 32-bit overlaps) is sufficient.
The overhead is twofold.
[A big fixpoint number, with no overlap, looks simpler, but would force us to reimplement addition and could end up slower.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantifying creativity in art networks} \\
  A. Elgammal and B. Saleh (2015)
\end{book}
To measure creativity (originality and influence):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Build a graph of paintings,
  with a directed edge between two paintings if the first precedes the second, weighted by similarity;
\item Subtract some reference value from the weights;
\item Invert the negative edges;
\item Compute the eigenvector centrality (PageRank).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An R package \texttt{flare}\\for high dimensional linear regression\\ and precision matrix estimation} \\
  X. Li et al.
\end{book}

Variants of the lasso where the square loss is replaced with the absolute value, the $L^2$ norm (not its square), or more generally the $L^p$ norm, $1 \leq p \leq 2$,
and applications to sparse precision matrix estimation 
(\textsc{tiger}, \textsc{clime}).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tiger: a tuning-insensitive approach\\ for optimally estimating\\ Gaussian graphical models} \\
H. Liu and L. Wang (2012)
\end{book}
Fitting a Gaussian graphical model, 
\ie, estimating a sparse precision matrix, 
can be done column by column,
with sparse regressions 
$ X_i \sim X_{ \setminus i } $:
the lasso and many variants (Dantzig selector, scaled lasso, etc.)
have been suggested, but the optimal tuning parameters 
cannot be computed in practice.
The \d{\textsc{sqrt} lasso}
(minimize 
$\n{\text{residuals}}_2 + \lambda \n{\beta}_1$ -- this is the $L^2$ norm, not its square) is tuning-insensitive.

In R, check the \texttt{bigmatrix} and \texttt{flare} packages.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identification of structured dynamical\\ systems in tensor product\\ reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces} \\
  M. Signoretto and J.A.K. Suykens
\end{book}
Factorization machines (or tensor machines)
with a low rank constraint:
add a ``multilinear spectral penalty'',
built from the SVDs of the unfoldings of the model parameters.
% They use a hard (0/\infty) penalty

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving distributional similarity\\ with lessons learned from word embeddings} \\
  O. Levy et al.
\end{book}
Traditional and neural word embeddings use the same bag-of-contexts representation:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Positive pointwise mutual information; 
\[ 
\text{PPMI}( \text{word}, \text{context} ) = 
\left( 
\log \dfrac{
  \hat P( \text{word}, \text{context} ) 
}{
  \hat P( \text{word} ) \hat P \text{context} ) 
}
\right)_+
\]
\item Its SVD-based low-rank approximation;
\item Skipgram with negative sampling (SGNS, wordvec): 
  maximize $s(\text{word} \cdot \text{context})$
  and minimize 
  $s(\text{word} \cdot \text{corrupted context})$,
  where $s$ is the sigmoid function 
  and $\cdot$ the scalar product -- 
  this can be seen as a matrix factorization
  $\text{PMI} \approx \text{Words} \cdot \text{Contexts} ' + 
  \log k$, with a robust (sigmoid) loss;
\item Global vectors (GloVe), 
  a factorization 
  $
  M \approx \text{Words} \cdot \text{Contexts} ' + 
  b_w \cdot \mathbf 1' + 
  \mathbf 1 \cdot b_c'
  $,
  where $M_{w,c} = \log \#(w,c)$.  
\end{compactitem}
The difference in performance is mostly due to 
better (or learned) hyperparameters in the ``neural'' networks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Auto-WEKA:\\ combined selection\\ and hyperparameter optimization\\ of classification algorithms} \\
  C. Thornton et al.
\end{book}
Use Bayesian optimization, 
e.g., SMAC (random-forest-based Bayesian optimization)
or TPE (tree-structured Parzen estimators),
to choose both algorithm and hyperparameters in Weka.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Real-time prediction and post-mortem analysis of the Shanghai 2015 stock market bubble} \\
D. Sornette et al. (2015)
\end{book}
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit the log-periodic power law (LPPL) 
  model on several windows, 
  from 750 to 125 trading days;
\item Filter the results (they give a long list of empirical conditions 
  the model should satisfy);
\item The ``confidence'' is the proportion of models 
  (\ie, window sizes)
  that pass those filters; 
  it estimates crash risk;
\item The ``trust'' is a resampling-based variant 
  of that goodness-of-fit test: 
  resample the residuals, 
  add them to the fit, 
  re-estimate the model,
  check if it still passes the filters;
\item Compute the probability distribution function of 
  the crash time, or of the bubble start time.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generative adversarial nets} \\
  I.J. Goodfellow et al. 
\end{book}
% That is actually the begining of the summary of 
% "Conditional generative adversarial nets"
\d{Generative adversarial nets} simultaneously learn two models 
\[
\arraycolsep=1.4pt
\begin{array}{llrcl}
  \text{generator} & G: & \text{noise} & \longmapsto & \text{data} \\
  \text{discriminator} & D: & \text{noise or data} & \longmapsto & \text{true or false}
\end{array}
\]
via the minimax game with value 
\begin{align*}
&V(D,G) = 
E_{ x \sim \text{data}  } \log D(x) + 
E_{ x \sim \text{noise} } \log ( 1 - D G (x) )
\\
&\text{value}( \text{game} ) = 
\min_G \max_D V(D,G).
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conditional generative adversarial nets} \\
  M. Mirza and S. Osindero
\end{book}
GANs can be generalized to conditional models
(models of $x|y$ instead of $x$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep generative image models 
  using\\ a Laplacian pyramid of adversarial networks} \\
  E. Denton et al.
\end{book}
Stack GANs to generate finer and finer images.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A neural conversational model} \\
  O. Vinyals and Q.V. Le
\end{book}
Build a conversational model with a sequence-to-sequence (seq2seq) RNN-LSTM network, trained on IT helpdesk chats or movie subtitles.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning to understand phrases\\ by embedding the dictionary} \\
  F. Hill et al.
\end{book}
Train a RNN with LSTM to map dictionary definitions 
to word vector representations (word2vec)
and use as a crossword solver (or a reverse dictionary, \ie, 
to find the word on the tip of your tongue from its definition).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple way to initialize\\ recurrent networks of rectified linear units} \\
  Q.V. Le et al.
\end{book}
To avoid the vanishing/exploding gradient problems when training recurrent neural networks (RNN) with stochastic gradient descent (SGD):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use Hessian-free optimization instead of SGD;
\item Or use SGD, with momentum, careful initialization, and clipped gradients;
\item Or use a LSTM network (the model is complicated, but its gradients well-behaved);
\item Or use ReLU and careful initialization.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards large-scale continuous EDA:\\ a random matrix theory perspective} \\
  A. Kab\'an et al. 
\end{book}
In high dimension, one can still use CMA-ES after imposing some structure on the covariance matrix (e.g., diagonal).
Instead, one can apply CMA-ES on random (low-dimensional) subspaces and combine the resulting subpopulations.
(EDA, ``estimation of distribution algorithms'', refers to CMA-ES-like evolutionalry algorithms.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Centrality of the supply chain} \\
  L. Wu
\end{book}
Supplier and consumer centralities (\textsc{hits} algorithm).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Discovering hidden factors of variation\\ in deep networks} \\
  B. Cheung et al.
\end{book}
Train a semi-supervised auto-encoder 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.7]
\draw[black!70,line width=4pt] (0,2) -- (5,2);
\draw[black!70,line width=4pt] (1,1) -- (3,1);
\draw[black!70,line width=4pt] (3.2,1) -- (4,1);
\draw[black!70,line width=4pt] (0,0) -- (5,0);
\draw[->] (2.5,.2) -- (2.5,.8);
\draw[->] (.5,.2) --  (1.5,.8);
\draw[->] (4.5,.2) -- (3.5,.8);
\draw[<-] (2.5,1.8) -- (2.5,1.2);
\draw[<-] (.5,1.8) --  (1.5,1.2);
\draw[<-] (4.5,1.8) -- (3.5,1.2);
\node at (2.5,-.3) {$x$};
\node at (2.5,2.3) {$x$};
\node at (.6,1) {$z$};
\node at (4.4,1) {$y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
(we want a latent representation $(z,y)$ of $x$, where $y$ is known, and $z$ should be as independent of $y$ as possible)
with a penalty on the average squared covariance 
$\bigl\langle \cov( z_i, y_i ) ^2 \bigr\rangle$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A MiniZinc tutorial} \\
  K. Marriott and P.J. Stuckey
\end{book}
MiniZinc is a discrete optimization modeling language: 
it translates the problem to feed it to various optimizers
(constraint propagation, linear programming, etc.)
and provides global constraints (alldifferent, etc.).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling discrete optimization} \\
  P.J. Stuckey and C. Coffrin (Coursera, 2015)
\end{book}
Another MiniZinc tutorial, with exercises.

%% 2015-10
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Wiener-Askey polynomial chaos\\ for stochastic differential equations} \\
  D. Xiu and G.E. Karniadakis (2001)
\end{book}
The \d{polynomial chaos} expansion of a stochastic process
(with finite variance) is the approximation 
\begin{align*}
  X_t &= a_0(t) H_0 + {}\\
  &\hphantom{={}} \sum_{i_1 \geq0} a_{i_1}(t) H_1(X_{i_1}) + {}\\
  &\hphantom{={}} \sum_{i_1,i_2 \geq0} a_{i_1i_2}(t) H_2(X_{i_1},X_{i_2}) + {}\\
  &\hphantom{={}} \cdots
\end{align*}
where $X_1,X_2,\dots$ are $N(0,1)$ iid,
\[
  H_n(x_1,\dots,x_n) = 
  e^{\textstyle - \frac12 x' x }_{\mathstrut}
  (-1)^n
  \dfrac{
    \partial^n
  }{
    \partial x_1 \cdots \partial x_n 
  }
  e^{\textstyle - \frac12 x' x }_{\mathstrut}
\]
are Hermite polynomials
and $a_{i_1,\dots,i_k}$ are functions to be determined.
It can be generalized to other families of orthogonal polynomials
and non-Gaussian random variables.
These expansions can be used to solve SDEs,
in the same way one would use power series with ODEs.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time-dependent polynomial chaos} \\
  P. Vos
\end{book}
% Very clear
Worked out examples showing how to 
numerically solve stochastic ODEs
(ODEs with random variables in their coefficients 
or their initial conditions)
with generalized polynomial chaos (gPC) expansions
and to circumvent numeric instability 
(by reinitializing the expansion from time to time).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Polynomial chaos: a tutorial and critique\\ from a statistician's perspective} \\
  A. O'Hagan (2013)
\end{book}
Polynomial chaos (PC) describes a random variable $X$ as 
$X \smash{{}\overset{d}={}} f(\Xi)$
(equality in distribution),
where $\Xi$ is a random variable following a known, simple distribution.
Such a function $f$ is not unique: 
one can look for it in the finite-dimensional space of polynomials (of degree at most $n$) 
orthogonal wrt the probability distribution function of $\Xi$
(Legendre for $U(-1,1)$, Hermite for $N(0,1)$, Laguerre for $\text{Exp}(1)$ -- choose depending on the boundedness of $X$).
Typically, $\Xi$ has dimension at least that of $X$.
The Karhunen-Lo\`eve expansion is a degree-1 PC expansion.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random geometry on the sphere} \\
  J.F. Le Gall (2014)
\end{book}
% There is a presentation and a paper: prefer the presentation.
To obtain a random metric on the sphere, 
draw a random planar graph on the sphere, 
consider the graph distance 
(suitably rescaled),
refine it by adding more nodes and edges 
until, in the limit,
you have a metric on the whole sphere.
More precisely, the ``random graphs'' are defined as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Consider \emph{planar maps}, 
  \ie, embeddings of graphs into the sphere $\SS^2$, 
  up to homeomorphism;
\item Consider only $p$-angulations 
  (\ie, the faces have $p$ edges); 
\item Sample uniformly from 
  \[ M_n^p = \{ \text{$p$-angulations with $n$ faces} \}, \]
  for $p=3$ or $4$ and $n$ large.
\end{compactitem}
For the limit, notice that a planar map is an element of 
\[ K = \{ \text{compact metric spaces} \} / \text{isometries}, \]
which is complete when equiped with 
the Gromov-Hausdorff distance 
\begin{align*}
  d(E_1,E_2) &= \inf \{ 
    d_{\text{H}}( \psi_1E_1,\psi_2E_2) 
    \ :\ \\
  &\qquad\quad
    \psi_i : E_i \hookrightarrow E
    \text{ isometric embeddings}
  \} \\
  d_{\text{H}}(X,Y) &= 
  \max \Bigl\{
    \sup_{x \in X} \inf_{y \in Y} d(x,y) ,\ 
    \sup_{y \in Y} \inf_{x \in X} d(x,y)
  \Bigr\} \\
  &= \inf \{ 
    \epsilon \geq 0 
    \ :\ 
    X \subset Y_\epsilon
    \text{ and }
    Y \subset X_\epsilon
  \}
\end{align*}
where $X_\epsilon$ is the $\epsilon$-enlargement of $X$.

The \d{Brownian map} is the random variable, with values in $K$, 
obtained in the limit;
% Convergence in distribution
it is a sphere, but it has Hausdorf dimension 4 (a.s.).

\emph{Quadrangulations} can be described as 
\emph{well-labeled trees} (Schaeffer's bijection).

A \d{continuous random tree} (CRT)
is obtained from a \emph{Brownian excursion}
(a Brownian motion $B$ such that $B_0=B_1=0$ and 
$\forall t \ B_t \geq 0$),
as $[0,1] / {\sim}$
where $s \sim t$ if $d(s,t)=0$ and 
\[d(s,t) = B_s + B_t - 2 \min_{u \in [s,t]} B_u; \]
is a distance on the tree.
Schaeffer's construction can be applied to a CRT with Brownian labels 
(there are two levels of randomness: in the tree, 
and in the labels).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Aspects of random maps} \\
  G. Miermont (2014)
\end{book}
All the details on the Brownian map.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A relation between Brownian bridge\\ and Brownian excursion} \\
  W. Vervaat (1979)
\end{book}
A \d{Brownian excursion}
is a Brownian motion $E$ on $[0,1]$
conditioned by $E_0 = E_1 = 0 $ and 
$\forall s \in [0,1]$, $E_s \geq 0$.
It can be built from a Brownian bridge 
by finding its minimum and shifting it (modulo 1)
to start and end at that minimum: 
\begin{align*}
  W_t &&& \text{Brownian motion} \\
  B_t &= W_t - t W_1 && \text{Brownian bridge} \\
  \tau &= \argmin_t B_t \\
  E_t &= B_{ \tau + t \mathop{\text{mod}} 1 } - B_\tau && \text{Brownian excursion}.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Automatic construction\\ and natural language description\\ of nonparametric regression models} \\
  J.R. Lloyd et al.
\end{book}
The \d{automatic statistician} models time series 
as Gaussian processes (GP) 
whose kernel is described via a formal language,
using elements such as 
  WN (white noise), 
  C (constant), 
  Lin (linear), 
  SE (square exponential), 
  Per (periodic), 
  CP (change points) 
and $+$ and $\times$ operators.
The models are not unlike \emph{symbolic regression} and include
  linear regression ($\text{C}+\text{Lin}+\text{WN}$),
  GP smoothing ($\text{SE}+\text{WN}$),
  cyclical decomposition 
    ($\sum \text{SE} + \sum \text{Per} + \text{WN}$),
  Fourier decomposition ($\text{C} + \sum\cos + \text{WN}$), 
etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Intelligible models\\ for classification and regression} \\
  Y. Lou et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Comparison of variants of GAM: 
the shape functions can be splines (in R: \texttt{mgcv}),
regression trees, 
tree ensembles (bagged, boosted, or both) 
with a fixed or adaptive depth;
the model can be learnt via penalized least squares
(wiggliness penalty, $\int \va{f''}^2$),
gradient boosting
or backfitting (learn $f_k$ on the residuals 
$y - \sum_{i\neq k} f_i(x_i)$, iterate until convergence):
boosted bagged trees, preferably with adaptive depth, perform better.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Accurate intelligible models\\ with pairwise interactions} \\
  Y. Lou et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Interactions can easily be added to GAMs (GA$\hskip -1pt{}^2$M),
but since the number of pairs of variables can be huge, 
some form of feature selection is needed.
For instance, one can greedily add the most promising
interaction to the model,
using 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Anova ($p$-value of a test comparing the model with no interactions and the model with the $(x_i,x_j)$ interaction);
\item Independence $\chi^2$ test between the sign of the GAM residuals and 
$(x_i > a_i, x_j > a_j)$, $a_i = \text{median}(x_i)$;
\item Comparison of the RMSE of a full model 
  (e.g., a random forest)
  $ y \sim \text{rf}(x_1,\dots,x_n)$
  with a model without the $(x_i,x_j)$ interaction,
  $ 
  y \sim 
  \text{rf}(x_1,\dots,\widehat{x_i},\dots,x_n) + 
  \text{rf}(x_1,\dots,\widehat{x_j},\dots,x_n)
  $;
\item RSS of the best split for $(x_i,x_j)$ -- it can be computed efficiently.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convexifying the set of matrices of bounded rank: applications to the quasiconvexification and convexification of the rank function} \\
  J.B. Hiriart-Urruty and H.Y. Le (2011)
\end{book}
The convex hull of 
\[
  \{ M \ :\ \rank M \leq k \text{ and } \n M \leq 1 \} 
\]
is
\[
  \{ M \ :\ \n{M}_* \leq k \text{ and } \n M \leq 1 \} 
\]
where 
$\n M = \sum_{x \neq 0} \n{ M x}_2 / \n{x}_2 = \sigma_1(M) $
is the spectral (operator) norm
and $\n{M}_* = \sum_i \sigma_i(M)$
is the nuclear (trace) norm
(those norms are dual).

This generalizes a similar result for the $\ell^0$ pseudo-norm: 
the convex hull of 
\(
  \{ x \ :\ \n{x}_0 \leq k \text{ and } \n{x}_\infty \leq 1 \}
\)
is 
\(
  \{ x \ :\ \n{x}_1 \leq k \text{ and } \n{x}_\infty \leq 1 \}
\)
and $\n{\cdot}_1$ and $\n{\cdot}_\infty$ are dual.

A function $f$ is \d{quasi-convex}
if its sublevel sets 
$[f \leq \alpha]$ are convex.
The \emph{(quasi-)convex hull} of a function is the largest 
(quasi-)convex function minorizing it.
The restricted rank is 
\[
\rank_r : M \mapsto \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  \rank M & \text{if } \n M \leq r \\
  +\infty & \text{otherwise.}
\end{array}
\right.
\]
Its quasi-convex hull is 
\[
M \mapsto \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  \lceil \tfrac1r \n{M}_* \rceil & \text{if } \n M \leq r \\
  +\infty & \text{otherwise}
\end{array}
\right.
\]
and its convex hull is 
\[
M \mapsto \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  \tfrac1r \n{M}_* & \text{if } \n M \leq r \\
  +\infty & \text{otherwise.}
\end{array}
\right.
\]

In other words, the spectral norm 
$\n{\cdot}_*$ (sum of the singular values)
is a convex relaxation of the rank.
For instance, the non-convex
\d{robust PCA} problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & L, S, N \\
\text{To minimize} & \rank L + \lambda \n{S}_0 \\
\text{Such that} & \n{N}_F \leq \epsilon \\
\text{and} & X = L + S + N
\end{array}
\]
can be relaxed to 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & L, S, N \\
\text{To minimize} & \n{L}+* + \lambda \n{S}_1 \\
\text{Such that} & \n{N}_F \leq \epsilon\\
\text{and} & X = L + S + N.
\end{array}
\]

Robust models often look for a decomposition 
\begin{align*} 
  \text{data} &= 
  \text{model} + \text{outliers} + \text{noise} \\ 
  &= \text{low rank} + \text{sparse} + \text{small}.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust rotation synchronization\\ via low-rank and sparse matrix decomposition} \\
  F. Arrigoni et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Robust PCA and matrix completion are both low-rank approximations:
% (the GoDec algorithm applies to both)
they can be combined.

Rotation matrices $R_1,\dots,R_n \in \text{SO}(3)$
can be recovered from noisy relative alignments 
$R_{ij} = R_i R_j^{-1} + \text{noise}$
%(e.g., from the ``iterative closest point'' algorithm)
by minimizing 
\[ 
  \sum_{ R_{ij} \text{ available} }
  \n{ R_{ij} - R_i R_j^{-1} }_F ^2.
\]

Letting 
$X = \begin{pmatrix}
  1 & R_{12} & \cdots & R_{1m} \\
  R_{21} &&& \vdots \\
  \vdots &&&  \\
  R_{n1} & \cdots && 1
\end{pmatrix},
$
the problem becomes
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & R = \begin{pmatrix}R_1\\\vdots\\R_n\end{pmatrix} \in \text{SO}(3)^n \\
\text{To minimize} & \n{ p(X - RR') }_F^2
\end{array}
\]
where $p$ is the projection on the available coordinates.
Since $R \in \text{SO}(3)^n$,
we can add the constraints 
$\rank X \leq 3$ and $X \succcurlyeq 0$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient computation of sparse Hessians\\ using coloring and automatic differentiation} \\
  A.H. Gebremedhin et al. (2009)
\end{book}
To recover a sparse, symmetric matrix $A$,
from its sparsity structure and a small number of products $Ax_1,\dots,Ax_n$,
one can use a colouring of the graph whose adjacency matrix is the sparsity structure 
to define $S=(\mathbf1_{\text{node $i$ has colour $j$}})_{ij}$;
for a \emph{star-colouring} 
(each 4-vertex path has at least 3 colours;
consequently, the subgraph induced by 2 colours 
is a collection of stars),
one can efficiently recover $A$ from $AS$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Compressed sensing recovery\\ via nonconvex shrinkage penalties} \\
  J. Woodworth and R. Chartrand (2015)
\end{book}
Compressed sensing, \ie, $\ell^0$ minimization 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & w \\
\text{To minimize} & \n{ w }_0 \\
\text{Such that} & Aw = b 
\end{array}
\]
is often relaxed to $\ell^1$ minimization,
and many algorithms rely on the \emph{proximal mapping}
of the $\ell^1$ norm
\[
S(x) = 
\raisebox{-1.4mm}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=.2,xscale=.12]
\draw (-3,0) -- (3,0);
\draw (0,-1) -- (0,1);
\draw[ultra thick] (-3,-1) -- (-1,0) -- (1,0) -- (3,1);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}
= 
\argmin_w \lambda \n w _1 + \tfrac12 \n{ w-x}_2^2.
\]
The $\ell^p$ quasi-norms, $0 < p < 1$,
are a better approximation of the $\ell^0$ penalty,
but their proximal mapping has no known closed form expression.
Instead,
one can start with a proximal mapping (in closed form), 
e.g., 
\[
