\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Entropy-based financial asset pricing} \\
  M. Ormos and S. Zibriczky (2014)
\end{book}
In portfolio construction, one can use entropy 
instead of the standard deviation as a risk measure 
(it requires a density estimator):
one can compute and plot efficient portfolios 
in the entropy$\times$return space 
and decompose the entropy into 
systematic (mutual information) 
and specific (conditional entropy) components.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Permutation-information-theory approach\\ to unveil delay dynamics\\ from time series analysis} \\
  L. Zunino et al. (2010)
\end{book}

Given a probability distribution $p$ 
on $\llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$, 
one can define the normalized entropy $H_S$
and the complexity $C_{JS}$ as
(here, $p_e$ denotes the uniform distribution)
\begin{align*}
  S[p]   &= - \sum p_i \log p_i 
    %&& \text{Shanon entropy} 
    \\
  S[p_e] &= \log N 
    %&&\text{Uniform distribution}
    \\
  H_S[p] &= \dfrac{ S[p] }{ S[p_e] } 
    %&& \text{Normalized entropy} 
    \\
  J(p|p_e) &= S[ \tfrac12 ( p + p_e ) ] 
    - \tfrac12 S[p] - \tfrac12 S[p_e] 
    %&& \text{Jensen-Shannon divergence} 
    \\
  \max_p J(p|p_e) &= \log 2N - (N+1) \log(N+1) \\
  Q_J(p|p_e) &= \dfrac{ J(p|p_e) }{ \max_q J(q|p_e) } 
    %&& \text{disequilibrium} 
    \\
  C_{JS}[p] &= Q_J(p|p_e) H_S[p] 
    %&& \text{statistical complexity}
\end{align*}


To transform a time series into a discrete distribution, choose 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item an embedding dimension $D > 1$;
\item an embedding delay $\tau$
\end{compactitem}
and consider the permutation defined by 
\[ (x_t, x_{t+\tau}, \dots, x_{t+(D-1)\tau} ). \]
This defines a discrete distribution on $\mathfrak S_D$
($D$ should not be too large: $D! \ll n$.)
The corresponding entropy and complexity 
are the \emph{permutation entropy} and the 
\emph{permutation statistical complexity}.

As an example, one can look at the Mackey-Glass oscillator 
\begin{align*}
  & \dot x = - x + \dfrac{
    a x ( t - \tau_0 )
  }{
    1 + x^c (t - \tau_0)
  } \\
  & 
  a = 2, \quad
  c = 10, \quad
  \tau_0 = 60 
\end{align*}
in the $(H_S,C_{JS})$ space, as $\tau_0$ varies.
% A small amount of additive noise is usually beneficial.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data manipulation detection\\ via permutation information theory quantifiers} \\
  A.F. Bariviera (2015)
\end{book}
Application to financial time series.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Representing numeric data in 32 bits\\ while preserving 64-bit precision} \\
  R.M. Neal (2015)
\end{book}
Storing data as decimals (rather than floating point)
is space-efficient, but a time-consuming conversion is usually needed to use the data.
Instead, one can use a table lookup
(implemented in pqR).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A generalized\\ Kahan-Babu\v ska-summation algorithm} \\
  A. Klein (2005)
\end{book}
When naively computing a (long) sum of floating point numbers, the rounding errors accumulate.
It is possible to compensate for them, e.g., by re-ordering the terms 
(if they are all positive, use the Huffman code order,
starting with the smallest numbers -- 
unfortunately, with arbitrary signs, finding the optimal order
is an NP-hard problem)
or by estimating the error and correcting for it:
the Kahan algorithm estimates the error and corrects it immediately; 
the more accurate Kahan-Babu\v ska-Neumaier (KBN) algorithm corrects it at the end.
There are dozens of variants of those algorithms.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Printing floating-point numbers quickly\\ and accurately with integers} \\
  F. Loitsch (2010)
\end{book}
Printing floating-point numbers (\ie, converting them to decimal) 
is not a trivial task. The previous algorithm, Dragon4, required arbitrary-precision arithmetic. Grisu, only requires integer arithmetic (but occasionally needs to fall back to Dragon4).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Kylix: a sparse allreduce\\ for commodity clusters} \\
  H. Zhao and J. Canny
\end{book}
The all-reduce pattern usually involves a tree network
\[ 
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=-.3,xscale=-.75,>=stealth]
