\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Edge compression techniques\\ for visualization of dense directed graphs} \\
  T. Dwyer et al. (2013)
\end{book}
To display a directed graph with a lot of edges 
(e.g., the dependencies between software components),
one can try to group them into modules,
with edges between modules instead of between nodes: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item One can find, in linear time (via hashing), 
  nodes that have the same set of neighbours, 
  and group them (the nodes inside a module are 
  either disconnected or form a clique, 
  depending on whether we include a node in its neighbourhood);
\item One can also allow for some internal structure 
  in the modules
  (but the algorithm is trickier);
\item One can also allow for some module-crossing edges
  (powergraph), e.g., with a greedy algorithm:
  compute a hierarchical clustering of the nodes,
  for some node similarity measure;
  for each potential cluster, 
  compute the number of edges that would be removed; 
  choose the modules greedily.
  The results were compared with the optimal minimizer of 
  \[\#\text{modules} + w_1 \times \# \text{edges}
  + w_2 \times \# \text{crossings}, \]
  computed using MiniZinc -- 
  the greedy solution is far from optimal but, 
  in psychological tests 
  (``how much of the graph do you remember?''
  ``Can you find the shortest path from $A$ to $B$?''),
  it is an improvement on traditional layout algorithms.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved optimal and approximate\\ power graph compression\\ for clearer visualization of dense graphs} \\
  T. Dwyer et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Finding the optimal \emph{powergraph compression}
of a graph
(\ie, a module decomposition that reduce the number of edges)
using general optimization methods 
(integer propagation, integer programming) 
is too time-consuming.

The following heuristic is faster, and gives decent results: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Arrange the set of possible solutions into a tree,
  with the initial graph (1-node modules) as the root,
  and children obtained by merging two modules in the parent;
\item Use best-first-search or, better, \emph{beam search}: 
  at each iteration (\ie, for each depth in the tree),
  keep the $k$ best solutions
  ($k=1$ is greedy-best-first search).
\end{compactitem}
It is actually reasonable to explore the whole tree, 
with backtracking, after judicious pruning.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A generic algorithm\\ for layout of biological networks} \\
  F, Schreiber et al. (2009)
\end{book}
The algorithm beging \texttt{dunnart}
(for constrained graph layout):
start with a feasible but partial layout, 
find a locally optimal partial layout,
extend it into a full layout.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Google matrix analysis\\ of the multiproduct world trade network} \\
  L. Ermann and D.L. Shepelyansky (2015)
\end{book}
In the graph whose vertices are country$\times$product pairs,
also consider the \emph{Chei-rank}
 (the PageRank of the opposite graph);
use a personalized variant of PageRank to account for imbalance between products (e.g., oil vs furs) or countries;
and compute the correlations 
\begin{align*}
  \kappa &= \sum_{c,p} p_{cp} p^*_{cp} - 1 \\
  \kappa_{p_1,p_2} &= N_C \sum_c \dfrac{
    p_{c p_1} p^*_{c p_2} 
  }{
    \sum\limits_{c_1} p_{c_1p_1} \sum\limits_{c_2} p_{c_2p_2}
  } - 1
\end{align*}
where $p_{cp}$ (resp. $p^*_{cp}$) are 
the PageRank (Chei-rank) scores
(eigenvectors for $\lambda=1$, 
normalized so that $p'\mathbf1 = p^*{}'\mathbf1=1$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Randomizing bipartite networks:\\ the case of the world trade web} \\
  F. Saracco et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The common graph metrics can be generalized to bipartite graphs:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Assortativity: 
\( \cor( \deg X, \deg Y \mid \text{edge $X$---$Y$} ) \);
\item Complexity and fitness are PageRank analogues -- 
  they can be used to reorder the rows and columns 
  of the adjacency matrix;
\item As a replacement for the clustering coefficient (there are no odd cycles), one can count motifs, e.g., 
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.2]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A1) at (0,1) {};
  \node (A2) at (1,1) {};
  \node (B1) at (0,0) {};
  \node (B2) at (1,0) {};
  \draw (A1) -- (B1) -- (A2) -- (B2) -- (A1);
\end{tikzpicture},
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.2]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A1) at (-.5,1) {};
  \node (A2) at (.5,1) {};
  \node (A3) at (1.5,1) {};
  \node (B1) at (0,0) {};
  \node (B2) at (1,0) {};
  \draw (A1) -- (B1) -- (A2) -- (B2) -- (A1);
  \draw (B1) -- (A3) -- (B2);
\end{tikzpicture}\!,
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.2]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A1) at (-.5,0) {};
  \node (A2) at (.5,0) {};
  \node (A3) at (1.5,0) {};
  \node (B1) at (0,1) {};
  \node (B2) at (1,1) {};
  \draw (A1) -- (B1) -- (A2) -- (B2) -- (A1);
  \draw (B1) -- (A3) -- (B2);
\end{tikzpicture},
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.2]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A1) at (-.5,1) {};
  \node (A2) at (.5,1) {};
  \node (A3) at (1.5,1) {};
  \node (B1) at (.5,0) {};
  \draw (A1) -- (B1) -- (A2);
  \draw (B1) -- (A3);
\end{tikzpicture}\!,\,
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.2]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A1) at (-.5,0) {};
  \node (A2) at (.5,0) {};
  \node (A3) at (1.5,0) {};
  \node (B1) at (.5,1) {};
  \draw (A1) -- (B1) -- (A2);
  \draw (B1) -- (A3);
\end{tikzpicture},
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.2]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A1) at (0,1) {};
  \node (A2) at (1,1) {};
  \node (B1) at (.5,0) {};
  \draw (A1) -- (B1) -- (A2);
\end{tikzpicture}
and 
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.2]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A1) at (0,0) {};
  \node (A2) at (1,0) {};
  \node (B1) at (.5,1) {};
  \draw (A1) -- (B1) -- (A2);
\end{tikzpicture};
\item Nestedness: count the number of products (countries) two countries (products) have in common; sum; normalize.
\end{compactitem}
To construct statistical tests on a graph, 
use some maximal entropy distribution on the set of graphs 
(e.g., assuming the degree distribution is known).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the modular dynamics\\ of financial market networks} \\
  F.N. Silva et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Let $G$ be a graph (undirected, no self-loops, no multiple edges),
$A(G)$ its adjacency matrix,
$\Delta(G)$ its degree matrix (diagonal),
$L(G) = \Delta(G) - A(G)$ its Laplacian,
$\rho(G) = L(G) / \tr \Delta(G)$ its density matrix,
$\lambda_1 \geq \lambda_2 \geq \cdots \geq \lambda_n$
the eigenvalues of $\rho(G)$.
The \d{Von Neumann entropy} of $G$ is 
\[ S(G) = - \sum_i \lambda_i \log_2 \lambda_i. \]
One can look at how graph metrics or community metrics
(modularity, 
average shortest path length,
average betweenness,
degree assortativity, 
transitivity, von Neumann entropy)
vary over time (for a network built from the correlation matrix of asset returns, estimated on a moving window).

With a model-based community detection algorithm, 
one can generate random graphs from the fitted community model, 
and look at the distribution of graph metrics.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A note on the von Neumann entropy\\ of random graphs} \\
  W. Du et al. (2010)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic multi-factor clustering\\ of financial networks} \\
  G.J. Ross (2015)
\end{book}
To measure how much a qualitative variable (sector, country, etc.)
influences a hierarchical clustering (from stock return correlations):
for each pair of points $i,j$ in the same class,
find their closest common ancestor $k$,
count the proportion of points in this class among the descendants of $k$;
average over all pairs.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identifying states in a financial market} \\
  M.C. M\"unnix et al. (2012)
\end{book}
To identify market states: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the correlation matrix, 
  for daily (or hourly) returns, on a 2-month moving window;
\item Compute the normalized $L^1$ distance 
  (alternatively, the difference between the largest eigenvalues)
  between those correlation matrices, 
  and cluster them (hierarchical clustering or $k$-means) --
  alternatively, fit some regime-switching model.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting financial extremes: a network degree measure of super-exponential growth} \\
  W. Yan et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Use the degree of the \emph{visibility graph} 
of the log-price (or its opposite)
to identify super-exponential growth (or decay);
this is similar to the LPPL pattern recognition indicator.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A practical approach to financial crisis indicator based on random matrices} \\
  A. Kornprobost and R. Douady (2015)
\end{book}
The \d{Hellinger distance} between two probability distributions is 
\[ \text{Hellinger}(p,q) = \int \bigl( \sqrt p - \sqrt q \bigr) ^2 .\]
To detect market instability,
look at the following indicators: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Hellinger distance between the Marcenko-Pastur 
  distribution and the distribution of the eigenvalues 
  of the correlation matrix
  (low eigenvalues are not informative: discard everything below the tenth of the maximum theoretical eigenvalue);
\item Hellinger distance with the spectrum of the sample correlation of a constant correlation Gaussian (or Student, with 2 degrees of freedom);
\item Maximum eigenvalue of $\var X$ (spectral radius);
\item $\tr \var X$;
\item $\max \spec \cor X$, with capitalization-weighted and volume-weighted variants 
(apply a moving average filter if too noisy).
\end{compactitem}
% List of crises on page 7, until 2014-12.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Kernel spectral clustering and applications} \\
  R. Langone et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To cluster data with PCA (or kernel PCA, or spectral methods -- kPCA on a graph Laplacian),
binarize the transformed data 
$(\operatorname{sign} \text{PC}_1, 
\operatorname{sign} \text{PC}_2, \dots )$
and use the most frequent binary codes as clusters.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scale up nonlinear component analysis\\ with doubly stochastic gradients} \\
  B. Xie et al.
\end{book}
Use two stochastic approximations simultaneously in kernel PCA: 
process the data in minibatches 
(as in stochastic gradient descent)
and use random features (different in each batch,
as in randomized PCA).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Implied correlation and expected returns} \\
  M. Valenzuela
\end{book}
The option-implied correlation
\begin{align*}
\rho &= \dfrac{
  \sigma^2 - \sum\limits_i w_i^2 \sigma_i^2
}{
  \sum\limits_{i \neq j} w_i w_j \sigma_i \sigma_j
  \vrule height .95em width 0pt
} \\
\sigma^2 &= 
\dfrac{ 2 e^{rT}}{F_T} 
\left(
  \int_0^{F_T} 
  \text{put}_T(K) dK
  +
  \int_{T_F}^\infty
  \text{call}_T(K) dK
\right)
\end{align*}
computed from (30-day) put and call options on the S\&P\,100 and its constituents,
can help predict (3- to 12-month) future returns.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Market timing with a robust moving average} \\
  V. Zakamulin (2015)
\end{book}
Among the half-dozen momentum strategies studies,
the exponentially-weighted moving average (EWMA) 
``buy if $
\sum_{k\geq0} 
\lambda^k \times 
\text{returns}_{[t-k-1,t-k]} 
> 0$'',
with $\lambda=0.87$,
is the most robust to the look-back period.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Market timing with moving averages:\\ anatomy and performance of trading rules} \\
  V. Zakamulin
\end{book}
Technical analysis rules can be formulated as 
``buy if $\sum_{k\geq0} w_k \text{Price}_{\text{now}-k}$''
for different weigting schemes
(one can also use price changes, with different weights).
% Prefer the article above: it concisely summarizes this one

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Copula-based hierarchical risk aggregation} \\
  F. Derendinger (2015)
\end{book}
A \emph{mildly tree-dependent} random vector $(X_i)_{i \in I}$ is 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item a rooted tree structure on the index set $I$,
\item a univariate distribution for each leaf,
\item a copula for each node, describing the dependence of its children,
\end{compactitem}
where each node is the sum of its children.
This does not uniquely determine 
the distribution of the random vector,
unless one also assumes that, for each node,
its descendants are conditionally independent 
from the other nodes.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring financial asset return\\ and volatility spillovers,\\ with applications to global equity markets} \\
  F. Diebold and K. Yilmaz (2009)
\end{book}
To measure spillover in a $\text{VAR}(p)$ model 
$x_t = \Phi x_{t-1} + \epsilon_t$,
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Write the proces as an $\text{MA}(\infty)$ process, 
  $x_t = ( 1 - \Phi L)^{-1} \epsilon_t$; 
\item Using the Choleski decomposition
  $\var \epsilon_t = Q_t Q_t^{-1}$,
  rewrite the process as an $MA(\infty)$ process 
  with orthogonal innovations
  $x_t = ( I - \Phi L )^{-1} Q_t^{-1} \cdot Q_t \epsilon_t = 
  A(L) u_t$;
\item The 1-step-ahead error $x_{t+1} - x_{t+1|t} = A_0 u_{t+1}$
  has variance $A_0 A_0'$;
  the $K$-step-ahead error can be decomposed 
  \[ 
  \sum_{ij} \sum_{ 1 \leq k \leq K} a_{ij}^k {}^2 = 
  \sum_i \left( 
    \sum_k a_{ii}^k {}^2 + \sum_{j \neq i} \sum_k a_{ij}^k {}^2
  \right)
  \]
  where the second term is the spillover of $j$ onto $i$.
\end{compactitem}
The \emph{spillover index} is 
\[
  S = \dfrac{
    \displaystyle \sum_{i\neq j} \sum_k a_{ij}^k {}^2
  }{
    \displaystyle \sum_{ijk} a_{ij}^k {}^2
  }
\]
It depends on the order of the variables, but not too much.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the computational complexity of high-dimensional Bayesian variable selection} \\
  Y. Yang et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The Bayesian hierarchical sparse model
\begin{align*}
  & \gamma = \{ \text{variables entering the model} \} 
  \subset \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket \\
  & \pi(\gamma) \propto p^{ - \kappa \va \gamma } \mathbf 1_{ \va \gamma \leq s_0 } \\
  & \pi(\phi) \propto \phi^{-1} \text{ (improper prior)} \\
  & \beta_\gamma \sim N( 0, g \phi^{-1} I_{\va \gamma} ) \\
  & w \sim N( 0, \phi^{-1} I_n ) \\
  & Y = X+\gamma \beta_\gamma + w 
\end{align*}
(the slab-and-spike prior, \ie, 
a mixture of two Gaussians with different variances,
is another popular choice),
sampled via Metropolis-Hastings
(with the neighbourhood of $\gamma$ defined 
by removing or adding a variable;
or by removing \emph{and} adding a variable)
is an alternative to the lasso for variable selection:
it has different, sometimes better, theoretical properties. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Relevance vector machines explained} \\
  T. Fletcher 
\end{book}
\d{Relevance vector machines} (RVM)
consider the bayesian model 
\begin{align*}
  w_j &\sim N(0, \alpha_j ^{-1} ) \\
  \epsilon_i & \sim N( 0, \beta^{-1} ) \\
  t_i &= w' \phi(x_i) + \epsilon_i 
\end{align*}
and estimate its posterior
\begin{align*}
& w \mid t, \alpha, \beta \sim N(m, \Sigma) \\
& m = \beta \Sigma \Phi' t \\
& \Sigma = ( \operatorname{diag} \alpha + \beta^{-1} \Phi' \Phi ) ^{-1}
\end{align*}
iteratively,
by computing $m$ and $\Sigma$ 
for given values of $\alpha$ and $\beta$,
then computing the values of the hyperparameters $\alpha$, $\beta$
that maximize the evidence $P(t \mid \alpha, \beta)$: 
\begin{align*} 
  \alpha_i & \leftarrow \dfrac{1 - \alpha_i \Sigma_{ii}}{m_i^2} \\
  \beta &\leftarrow \dfrac{
  N - \sum_i ( 1 - \alpha_i \Sigma_{ii} )
  }{
  \n{ t - \Phi m } ^2
  }.
\end{align*}
Quite often, 
$\alpha_i \to \infty$, \ie, $w_i \to 0$: 
the corresponding parameters can be pruned.
The remaining parameters are the \emph{relevance vectors}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Shotgun stochastic search\\ for ``large $p$'' regression} \\
  C. Hans et al. (2007)
\end{book}
Shotgun stochastic search (SSS) 
is a variant of Metropolis-Hastings (MH)
to look for a sparse model: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Let $\Gamma$ be the set of the best $k$ models 
  examined so far
  and $\gamma_n$ the current model;
\item In parallel (MCMC is usually sequential), 
  examine  all the (or a large number of) neighbours 
  of $\gamma_n$;
  add them to $\Gamma$;
  discard the worst models if $\va \Gamma \> k$;
\item Choose $\gamma_{n+1}$ as with MH
  (if you allow for three types of neighbours,
  from deletion moves $\gamma^-$, 
  additions $\gamma^+$ and replacement $\gamma^0$,
  they are unbalanced, expecially in high dimensions: 
  $\va{ \gamma^0 } \gg \va{ \gamma^+} \gg \va{ \gamma^- }$ --
  you may want to sample from them separately),
  but with an acceptance probability that depends on 
  the sum of the scores of the neighbours 
  of the current point and the candidate.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical model criticism\\ using kernel two sample tests} \\
    J.R. Lloyd and Z. Ghahramani
\end{book}
The notion of $p$-value can be generalized to a Bayesian setting: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Choose a statistic $T$;
\item Estimate $P[ T \leq T_{\text{obs}} ]$
  using the posterior distribution of $T$.
\end{compactitem}
It measures how surprising the data still is,
even after observing it.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random bits regression:\\ a strong general predictor for big data} \\
  Y. Wang et al.
\end{book}
Penalized regression on random binary features performs well on big data 
(same idea as echo state networks or extreme learning machines,
which are just SVMs with a randomized (instead of universal) kernel).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random feature maps for dot product kernels} \\
  P. Kar and H. Karnick (2012)
\end{book}
SVMs with a high-dimensional embedding space tend to have too many support vectors.
One can transform the kernel to reduce the dimension,
randomly (as in the Johnson-Lindenstrauss lemma).
For instance, if the kernel is of the form 
$k(x,y) = f(\langle x, y \rangle )$,
with $f(x) = \sum_{n \geq 0} a_n x^n$, 
where the $a_n$ are nonnegative,
one can use this expression to build an embedding 
$Z : \RR^d \longrightarrow \RR^D$,
with $\langle Zx, Zy \rangle \approx k(x,y)$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Select $N$ randomly with $P[N=n] \propto 1/p^{n+1}$;
\item Select $\omega_1,\dots,\omega_n \in \{ \pm 1 \} ^d$ randomly;
\item Let $Z_1(x) = \sqrt{ a_N p^{N+1} } \prod_j \omega_j' x$;
\item Do the same for the other coordinates $Z_2,\dots,Z_D$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to random indexing} \\
  M. Sahlgren
\end{book}
LSA (latent semantic indexing) builds a word-document matrix
and computes its (truncated) SVD to produce a lower-dimensional representation of the words.
However, the initial word-document matrix can be too large.
Instead, one can use a random projection (Johnson-Lindenstrauss):
it can be built incrementally, one document at a time.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A practical guide\\ to applying echo state networks} \\
  M. Luko\v sevi\v cius
\end{book}
Implementation advice for \emph{reservoir computing}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{fastFM: a library for factorization machines} \\
  I. Bayer 
\end{book}
C library, with Python bindings (contrary to libfm).
Factorization machines are not limited to recommendation systems, 
but can add interactions to any model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tensor machines\\ for learning target-specific polynomial features} \\
  J. Yang and A. Gittens
\end{book}
Tensor machines are a generalization of factorization machines
\[
f(x) = w^0 + \langle w^1, x \rangle + 
\sum_{p=2}^q
\left\langle
\sum_{i=1}^r
w_1^{pi} \bullet \cdots \bullet w_p^i, 
x \bullet \cdots \bullet x
\right\rangle
\]
where $\cdot \bullet \cdot$ is the outer product 
and $w_1^{pi} \bullet \cdots \bullet w_p^i$ is a rank-1 tensor.
The model is fitted by minimizing 
\[ \dfrac1n \sum_i \text{loss}\bigl( f(x_i), y_i \bigr) + \lambda \n{ w }_2^2 .
\]
(Kar-Karnick features are similar, but $w$ is constrained to be in the subspace spanned by a sufficiently large number of random tensors.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Score function features for discriminative learning: matrix and tensor frameworks} \\
  M. Janzamin et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To build features: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Estimate the probability distribution $p(x)$ of the (unlabelled) data $x$;
\item Compute the score functions 
  \[
  S_m(x) = (-1)^m \dfrac{ \nabla^{m} p(x) }{ p(x) } ;
  \]
\item Given labeled data $(x,y)$,
  we want some information about 
  $G(x) = E[ y | x ]$;
  we can estimate the expectation of its derivatives: 
  $E[ \nabla^m G(x) ] = E[ y S_m(x) ]$;
\item These are (huge) tensors: 
  compute a low rank approximation (SVD, CP),
  $E[ \nabla^m G(x) ] \approx \sum_j u_j^{\tens m} $
  (each tensor product only involves a single vector 
  because the tensors are symmetric);
\item Use $\sigma( x \cdot u_j )$ as features
  where $\sigma$ is a signoid function.
\end{compactitem}
% It is not clear to me why these features should work better than random features.
% Video: Simons institute, Information Theory and Big Data, 2015

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Picture: a probabilistic programming language for scene perception} \\
  T.D. Kulkarni et al.
\end{book}
A scene description language can be turned into 
a probabilistic programming language;
do not use a fully-rendered (photorealistic) scene
to compute the model with the data pixel by pixel: 
use features instead (from computer vision or deep learning).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Slice sampling for probabilistic programming} \\
  R. Ranca (2015)
\end{book}
To sample from a probability distribution only known up to a multiplicative factor,
$p(x) \propto p^*(x)$,
\d{slice sampling} proceeds iteratively: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pick $u_t \sim U( 0, p^*(x_t) )$; 
\item Pick $x_{t+1} \sim U( \{ x \,:\, p^*(x) \geq u_t \} )$.
\end{compactitem}
StocPy is yet another Python-based 
probabilistic programming language.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Count-min-log sketch: approximately counting with approximate counters} \\
  G. Pitel and G. Fouquier (2015)
\end{book}
Count-min-sketch variant with more precision for low counts,
with applications to TD-IDF matrices.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Supervised learning from multiple experts: whom to trust when everyone lies a bit} \\
  V.C. Raykar et al. (2009)
\end{book}
Binary forecasts $y_{ij}$, for many tasks $i$ (e.g., medical diagnostic)
for several experts $j$
can be combined
(use majority voting as a baseline model)
with an EM algorithm,
alternatively estimating the forecasts and the reliability 
of each expert;
one can add a Bayesian prior 
if some experts are known to be more or less reliable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Invariant backpropagation: how to train\\ a transformation-invariant neural network} \\
  S. Demyanov et al.
\end{book}
One expects neural networks to be invariants to some transformations (rotations, translations, etc.).
The following weight updates achieve that
(you do not have to specify the group action): 
\[
  \mathbf w \leftarrow
  \mathbf w - \alpha 
  \dfrac{ \partial \text{Loss} }{ \partial \mathbf w } 
  - \beta \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial \mathbf w } 
  \n{ 
    \dfrac{ \partial \text{Loss} }{ \partial \text{Input} }
  }^2.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning classifiers from synthetic data\\ using a multichannel autoencoder} \\
  X. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
Use an autoencoder (trained on real data) on surrogate data to make it more realistic.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep transform: cocktail party source separation via probabilistic re-synthesis} \\
  A.J.R. Simpson
\end{book}
To separate two speech signals (in a mono-aural signal),
learn an auto-encoder 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2.5,yscale=.3,>=stealth]
\node (i1) at (-1,1) {Speaker 1};
\node (i2) at (-1,-1) {Speaker 2};
\node (s)  at (0,0) {Mixed};
\node (o1) at (1,1) {Speaker 1};
\node (o2) at (1,-1) {Speaker 2};
\draw[->] (i1) -- (s);
\draw[->] (i2) -- (s);
\draw[->] (s) -- (o1);
\draw[->] (s) -- (o2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
[They used a male and a female speaker: 
their auto-encoder learned to separate gender,
rather than speech.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Explaining and harnessing\\ adversarial examples} \\
  I.J. Goodfellow et al. (2015)
\end{book}
For high-dimensional linear models, 
you can make infinitesimal changes to the input
(e.g., $\pm1$ to each 8-bit pixel of an image)
that add up to a large change in the output.
To mitigate the problem: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use ``deep'' neural networks (at least one hidden layer);
\item Train on a mixture of adversarial and clean examples.
\end{compactitem}
(RBF networks are immune to those adversarial examples, but do not generalize well.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural Turing machine} \\
  A. Graves et al.
\end{book}
A differentiable Turing machine can be trained with gradient descent to learn simple algorithms.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Long short-term memory over tree structures} \\
  X. Zhu et al. (2015)
\end{book}
LSTM networks can be generalized from sequences to trees.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{LSTM: a search space odyssey} \\
  K. Greff et al.
\end{book}
In a recurrent net, there is feedback from the output to the input.
In an LSTM net, there is feedback from (some of) the inner nodes to (themselves and) the input: 
they can be trained line RNN (with the same problems).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{jvmr: integration of R, Java and Scala} \\
  D.B. Dahl et al.
\end{book}
Embed R in Scala or Java, or the reverse.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian estimation supersedes the $t$ test} \\
  J.K. Kruschke (2013)
\end{book}
For a Bayesian $T$ test, check the \texttt{BEST} R package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cubist models for regression} \\
  M. Kuhn et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Cubist models generalize decision trees: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item There is a model in each node, 
and each leaf (or node) is shrunk towards its parent;
\item One can add boosting and/or shrink each forecats
  towards its nearest neighbours (in the training set or the sample to forecast).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{R in finance 2015} \\
\end{book}

\textbf{1.}
Several presentations applied social network analysis (SNA) methods to financial networks.

PageRank on a graph weighted by cross-correlations can identify leaders.
[Some use the HITS algorithm instead, to identify leaders and followers, extracting information from leaders and investing in followers.]

If the nodes $i$ of a graph represent financial entities,
  each with a risk $C_i$, 
  one can compute the following graph metrics: 
  \begin{align*}
  \text{Fragility} &= 
  \dfrac{ E[ \text{degree}^2 ] }{ E[ \text{degree} ] } \\
  \text{Risk score} &= \sqrt{ C' A C }
  \end{align*}
where $A$ is the incidence matrix
The risk score can be decomposed with Euler's formula:
\[
  \text{Risk score} = 
  \sum \dfrac{\partial \text{Score} }{ \partial C_i } C_i. 
\]
One can then compute
\[
  \text{Criticality} = C \odot x
\]
where $x$ is the eigenvector centrality 
(not unlike Page\-Rank) and $\odot$ the elementwise product,
and 
\[
  \text{Spillover}_{ij} = 
  \dfrac{ \partial }{ \partial C_i }
  \dfrac{ \partial \text{Score} }{ \partial C_j }.
\]

A graph (e.g., interbank lending market, in some country) can be decomposed
into \emph{core and periphery}, and compute various graph metrics 
(density, betweenness centrality, closeness centrality, transitivity, 
average path length, eigenvalues, etc.) over time.

One can apply network analysis (plots, centrality) on raw (SWIFT) financial transactions.
%[It is not clear where they get the data from.]

One can compute graph metrics (centrality, etc.) on graphs learnt with \texttt{bnlearn}.

%[1a] Michael Kapler: Follow the Leader - the application of time-lag series analysis to discover leaders in S&P 500
%[1b] Sanjiv Das: Matrix Metrics: Network-Based Systemic Risk Scoring
%[1c] Gergely Daroczi: Network analysis of the Hungarian interbank lending market
%[1d] Kresimir Kalafatic: Financial network analysis using SWIFT and R
%[1e] Stephen Rush: Information Diffusion in Equity Markets

\textbf{2.} 
Higher moments (up to the fifth...) 
were mentioned a couple of times.

Use differential evolution (\texttt{DEoptim}) to optimize the expected utility of a portfolio
(using the sample distribution of past returns).
The derivatives of the utility function should alternate in sign. They all have names: 
\begin{align*}
  U'     &> 0    &&\text{Non-satiation (high returns)} \\
  U''    &< 0    &&\text{Risk aversion (low risk)} \\
  U'''   &> 0    &&\text{Prudence (positive skew)} \\
  U''''  &< 0    &&\text{Temperance (low kurtosis)} \\
  U''''' &> 0    &&\text{Edginess.}
\end{align*}
Use, for instance, 
$U(\text{wealth}) = \text{wealth} / ( c + \text{wealth} ).$

Investors have a preference for high odd moments and low even moments. 
The \texttt{HighFreq} package estimates them from OHLC data (not unlike all the volatility estimators in \texttt{TTR}).

%[2a] John Burkett: Portfolio Optimization: Price Predictability, Utility Functions, Computational Methods, and Applications
%[2b] Jerzy Pawlowksi: Are High Frequency Traders Prudent and Temperate?

\textbf{3.}
% Time series models
A \emph{partially auto-regressive} process is the sum of a stationary AR process and a random walk;
it can be estimated with a Kalman filter.
Caveats: the tests have low power; the AR component may just capture the market microstructure (e.g., the bid-ask bounce).
This is implemented in the \texttt{partialAR} package (use the cointegration residuals, from \texttt{egcm}).

To identify change points in a time series, find the partition of the indices that maximizes the sample energy distance 
\begin{align*}
  \mathscr E(X,Y) &= 
    2 E \va{ X - Y }^\alpha 
    \!- E \va{ X_1 - X_2 }^\alpha 
    \!- E \va{ Y_1 - Y_2 }^\alpha \\
  \hat{\mathscr E}(X,Y) &= 
  \dfrac{2}{\#B} 
  \sum_B \va{ x_i - x_j }^\alpha + \\
  &\hphantom{={}}
  - \dfrac1{\#W_x} \sum_{ W_x } \va{ x_i - x_j }^\alpha 
  \!- \dfrac1{\#W_y} \sum_{ W_y } \va{ y_i - y_j }^\alpha 
\end{align*}  
The e-cp3o algorithm speeds up the computations by removing points that are unlikely to be change points and reducing the size of $B$, $W_x$, $W_y$.
This is implemented in \texttt{ecp::ecp3o}.

For large but sparse VARX models (VAR with exogenous variables), add a (nested group) lasso penalty.
The Hierarchical VAR (HVAR) model also adds lasso penalties to the lags.
This is implemented in the \texttt{BigVAR} package.

By combining bits of models that are not often used together, 
one can build rather complicated models, e.g., 
a regime switching cointegration model with time-varying transition probabilities

To detect multivariate outliers, use a robust covariance matrix, e.g., the MCD (minimum covariance determinant).
Iteratively-reweighted MCD gives the correct false positive rate.
This is implemented in the \texttt{CerioliOutlierDetection} package.

%[3a] Matthew Clegg: The partialAR Package for Modeling Time Series with both Permanent and Transient Components
%[3b] Nicholas James: Efficient Multivariate Analysis of Change Points
%[3c] William Nicholson: Structured Regularization for Large Vector Autoregression 
%[3d] Matt Brigida: Markov Regime-Switching (and some State Space) Models in Energy Markets
%[3e] Chris Green: Detecting Multivariate Financial Data Outliers using Calibrated Robust Mahalanobis Distances

\textbf{4.}
% Portfolio construction
The \texttt{PortfolioAnalytics} package provides many portfolio optimization approaches, 
including Meucci's fully flexible views or Almgren and Chriss's portfolios from sorts.

Flexible asset allocation with stepwise correlation rank 
suggests to build 
a portfolio one asset at a time, adding the asset with the best
\begin{align*}
	& 
	 w_1 \times \text{momentum rank} + 
    w_2 \times \text{volatility rank} + {}\\
    & \qquad
    w_3 \times \text{correlation rank},
\end{align*}
where the correlation is the average correlation with the assets already in the portfolio.

It is safer to build a portfolio from a few (smart beta) ETFs than from a large number of stocks: 
lower estimation error and fewer parameters lead to better out-of-sample performance.

Taxes have an impact on the optimal multi-period 2-asset portfolio (Kelly principle).

%[4a] Ross Bennett: PortfolioAnalytics: Advanced Moment Estimation & Optimization
%[4b] Ilya Kipnis: Flexible Asset Allocation With Stepwise Correlation Rank
%[4c] Marjan Wauters: Characteristic-based equity portfolios: economic value and dynamic style allocation
%[4d] Sanjiv Das: Efficient Rebalancing of Taxable Portfolios

\textbf{5.}
%  Risk measurement
mVaR and mES (Cornish-Fisher value at risk and expected shortfall) 
are estimators: we can compute their variance and bias
(a computer algebra system (CAS) is useful -- they used the 
Matlab symbolic toolbox, but you may want to check SAGE, Maxima or Yacas)
and compare them with a maximum likelihood estimator 
(asymptotically no bias and lowest variance). 
Also check \texttt{qrmtools::ARA} and the \texttt{SharpeR} package.
mVaR and mES are bad for: low $\alpha$, fat tails, large samples.

One can still decompose the VaR into a sum of contributions (risk factors, managers) in the presence of non-linear assets (e.g., with the delta-gamma approximation).

One can compute an upper bound on the expected Sharpe ratio; it is unclear if/how it depends on the number of assets.

%[5a] Rohit Arora: Inefficiency of Modified VaR and ES
%[5b] Matthew Dixon: Risk Decomposition for Fund Managers
%[5c] Steven Pav: Portfolio Cramer-Rao Bounds (why bad things happen to good quants)

\textbf{6.}
% High frequency data, market microstructure, options
The \texttt{highfrequency} package provides functions to
align non-synchronous time series (wait until there has been a new observation for all time series),
compute volatility or jump estimators (realized bipower variation, MedRV, ROWVar),
tests for the presence of jumps, etc.

Here is a stylized fact for high frequency data: 
\[
  \text{Number of transactions} \propto
  \text{USD Volume} ^ {2/3} 
  \times 
  \text{Volatility}.
\]

The \texttt{creditr} package provides CDS computations, 
with a Shiny interface, not unlike the Bloomberg or Markit CDS screen/calculator.

%[6a] Kris Boudt: High-frequency Price Data Analysis in R
%[6b] Oleg Bondarenko: High-Frequency Trading Invariants for Equity Index Futures (pdf)
%[6c] Yuanchu Dang: Credit Default Swaps with R (with Zijie Zhu) (html)
%[6d] Alexander McNeil: R Tools for Understanding Credit Risk Modelling  (pdf)
%[6e] Eric Zivot: Price Discovery Share-An Order Invariant Measure of Price Discovery with Application to Exchange-Traded Funds (pptx)

\textbf{7.}
% Machine learning
The \texttt{Rborist} package is similar to \texttt{RandomForest}, but faster; it can use several cores (or a GPU).

The \texttt{irlba} package computes the fast truncated SVD.

%[7a] Mark Seligman: The Arborist: a High-Performance Random Forest Implementation (pdf)
%[7b] Bryan Lewis: More thoughts on the SVD and Finance (html)

\textbf{8.}
% R
\texttt{data.table} still has an edge on \texttt{dplyr}: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The data is indexed;
\item It provides fast aggregation, and ordered joins (aka rolling joins, locf);
\item \texttt{fread} is a fast CSV reader (but \texttt{h2o.importFile} is even faster).
\end{compactitem}

The development version of \texttt{DBI} (finally) allows for parametric queries (prepared statements).
The \texttt{readr} package provides faster functions to read CSV files (but \texttt{fread} is even faster).
The \texttt{httr}, \texttt{xml2}, \texttt{rvest}, \texttt{jsonlite} packages were also mentioned; \texttt{dplyr}, \texttt{reshape2}, etc. were not.

\texttt{Rcpp} provides better integration with RStudio,
annotations (e.g, \texttt{// [[Rcpp::export]]}), 
C++11 (closures, type inference), 
direct access to Boost even on Windows (\texttt{BH}).

\texttt{Rblpapi} is a newer inferface to Bloomberg, 
in C++ rather than Java. 
It does not work well on Windows.

Some suggest to describe the data pipeline 
in a purely declarative way; 
to facilitate deployment, this should also include 
the data cleaning part.

The \texttt{simsalapar} package can help you (easily) 
parallelize computations -- 
when you have to run the same computations/simulations many times,
with different parameter values. 
An example for VaR computations with nested archimedian copulas
(\texttt{copula::onacopula}) was given. 
[I prefer to use \texttt{doParallel} 
(and \texttt{foreach}) directly.]

%[8a] Matt Dowle: Fast automatic indexing with data.table
%[8b] Hadley Wickham: Data ingest in R
%[8c] Dirk Eddelbuettel: Hands-on Introduction to Rcpp
%[8d] Dirk Eddelbuettel: Rblpapi: Connecting R to the data service that shall not be named
%[8e] Rob Krzyzanowski: Building Better Credit Models through Deployable Analytics in R
%[8f] Marius Hofert: Parallel and other simulations in R made easy: An end-to-end study

