% Bayesian optimization

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The computation of the expected improvement in dominated hypervolume\\ of {Pareto} front approximations} \\
  M. Emmerich et al. (2008)
\end{book}

For multi-objective Bayesian optimization, replace the expected improvement (EI) with the expected increase in the hypervolume dominated by the Pareto set. While a Monte Carlo approach is possible, it can be computed exactly: decompose the space into boxes, each corresponding to a different shape for the new efficient frontier, and compute the expected improvement conditional on the new point being in a given box.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Faster computation\\ of expected hypervolume improvement} \\
  I. Hupkens et al. (2014)
\end{book}

% NOT READ

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Expensive multiobjective optimization\\ for robotics} \\
  M. Tesch et al. (2013)
\end{book}

Bayesian multiobjective optimization to steer snake robots, to optimize both speed and head camera stability: replace the expected improvement (EI) with the expected improvement in (dominated) hypervolume (EIHV). Also gives another test problem, built from the Branin function.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A tutorial on {Bayesian} optimization\\ of expensive cost functions,\\ with application to active user modeling\\ and hierarchical reinforcement learning} \\
  E. Brochu et al. (2010)
\end{book}
Good review article.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{{ParEGO}: a hybrid algorithm\\ with online landscape approximation\\ for expensive multiobjective optimization} \\
  J. Knowles (2004)
\end{book}

The following multiobjective optimization algorithm is more economical (in function evaluations) than NSGA-II: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Initialize the population with a Latin hypercube;
\item Take a weight vector $\lambda \in \Delta_n$ at random (a new one for each iteration, possibly with some of the coordinates set to zero);
\item Consider the objective function 
\[
  f(x) = \max_i \lambda_i f_i(x) + 
         \rho \sum_i \lambda_i f_i(x);
\]
\item Model it using a Gaussian process (if there are more than 80 points in the population, take 80 at random);
\item Use some optimization algorithm (say, Nelder-Mead) to maximize the expected improvement;
\item Add the new point to the population;
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}
The paper also provides a few test functions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiobjective optimization\\ on a budget of 250 evaluations} \\
  J. Knowles and E.J. Hughes (2005)
\end{book}

ParEGO performs better than a binary-search-based algorithm: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pick a point at random in the largest hypercube, and split the hypercube at that point in the direction that yields the most ``cube-like'' sub-spaces;
\item Idem with a point close to a ``good point'', for some random aggregation of the scores, e.g. $\sum_i \lambda_i f_i(x)$, $\lambda \sim U(\Delta_n)$;
\item Alternate between those steps with a predefined exploration/exploitation ratio.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiobjective optimization\\ of urban wastewater systems using {ParEGO}:\\ a comparison with {NSGA-II}} \\
  G. Fu et al. (2008)
\end{book}

Another comparison of ParEGO and NSGA-II.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient global optimization\\ of expensive black-box functions} \\
  D.R. Jones et al. (1998)
\end{book}

Not very different from DACE: start with a Latin hypercube; check the goodness of fit of the model (and transform the variables if needed); compute the expected improvement in closed form.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient global optimization ({EGO})\\ for multi-objective problem and data mining} \\
  S. Jeong and S. Obayashi (2005)
\end{book}

Another EGO example.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BOA: the {Bayesian} optimization algorithm} \\
  M. Pelikan et al. (1999)
\end{book}

Variant of CMA-ES with Bayesian networks (suitable for discrete variables) instead of a Gaussian distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian optimization algorithms\\ for multi-objective optimization} \\
  M. Laumanns and J. Ocenasek (2002)
\end{book}

The multiobjective Bayesian optimization algorithm works as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Keep an archive of best solutions (all those that $\epsilon$-dominate the solutions seen so far, but allow some dominated solutions to have at least $k$ of them);
\item Model the archive, not unlike CMA-ES, but with decision trees (one for each variable -- Bayesian networks proved too complex) instead of a Gaussian distribution;
\item Sample candidates from this model.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-objective\\ {Bayesian} optimization algorithm} \\
  N. Khan et al. (2002)
\end{book}

{BOA} can be generalized to multiobjective optimization: use non-dominated sorting and the crowding distance to select the $n$ nest solutions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiobjective evolutionary algorithm\\ test suites} \\
  D.A. van Veldhuizen and G.B. Lamont (1999)
\end{book}

