%\end{align*} 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable test problems for evolutionary multi-objective optimization} \\
  K. Deb et al. (2001)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Combining multiobjective optimization\\ and {Bayesian} model averaging to calibrate forecast ensembles of soil hydraulic models} \\
  T. W\"ohling and J.A. Vrugt (2008)
\end{book}

Bayesian optimization with Gaussian processes replaced by a (BMA) ensemble of domain-specific (PDE) models.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian optimization\\ with inequality constraints} \\
  J.R. Gardner et al. (2014)
\end{book}

Constrained Bayesian optimization,
\[
\begin{aligned}
  & \text{Find} && x \\
  &\text{To minimize} && f(x) \\
  &\text{Such that} && g(x) \geq 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
where both the objective $f$ and the constraints $g$ are expensive to compute,
is a straightforward generalization of Bayesian optimization: replace the expected improvement 
$E[ ( f(x^+) - \hat f(x) )_+ ]$ 
with the expected constrained improvement 
$E [ \mathbf 1 _{\hat g(x)} \cdot ( f(x^+) - \hat f(x) )_+ ]$.
One often assumes conditional independence: 
$\hat g(x) \indep \hat f(x) \mid x$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pareto front modeling for sensitivity analysis in multi-objective {Bayesian} optimization} \\
  R. Calandra et al. (2014)
\end{book}

In multiobjective Bayesian optimization (MOBO), do not return a discrete set of solutions but, since a model was used for the optimization, a model of the Pareto front.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Design and analysis of computer experiments} \\
  J. Sacks et al. (1989)
\end{book}

One of the first papers on Bayesian optimization.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A fast and elitist\\ multiobjective genetic algorithm: {NSGA-II}} \\
  K. Deb et al. (2002)
\end{book}

The non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm II (NSGA-II) solves multi-objective optimization problems, \ie, estimates their non-dominated front.

1. The candidate solutions are sorted by first finding the non-dominated front, removing it, and iterating. The \d{non-dominated rank} can be computed effeciently:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For each solution, compute the domination count, \ie, the number of solutions that dominate it;
\item For each solution, compute the set of solutions it dominates;
\item The first non-domination front solutions have domination count zero: remove it;
\item Update the domination counts and the domination sets;
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}

2. To preserve diversity, look at the \d{crowding distance} of each solution, defined as the average side length of the cuboid around it with (half) its vertices among the other solutions.

3. Define a partial order on the solutions:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Prefer solutions with a lower non-domination rank;
\item If the ranks are equal, prefer solutions with a higher crowding distance.
\end{compactitem}

4. One can add constraints: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Prefer feasible solutions;
\item When two solutions are infeasible, prefer that with the smaller constraint violation.
\end{compactitem}

The paper also contains reference problems.

% Bayesian optim
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{{GPfit}: an {R} package\\ for {Gaussian} process model fitting\\ using a new optimization algorithm} \\
  B. MacDonald et al. (2013)
\end{book}
When fitting a Gaussian process on non-noisy data, 
with a Gaussian correlation function 
\[
R_{ij}=\cor(y_i,y_j) = 
\prod_k \exp - \theta_k \va{ x_{ik} - x_{jk} }^2,
\]
$\theta_k>0$, 
the correlation matrix can be ill-conditioned 
(some of the points are too close), 
and the likelihood has local extrema very close to zero. 
One can replace $R$ with $R + \delta I$, 
with the smallest nugget $\delta$ 
that makes the matrix well-conditioned
[this is the idea behind ridge regression; 
this is also what you naturally do 
when the eigenvalues are too close to zero], 
and reparametrize $\theta$ as $\theta = \exp\phi$.

Check the R packages \texttt{GPfit} (no noise),
\texttt{tgp} (noise)
or \texttt{mlegp} (noise, numerically unstable).

Also mentions \texttt{lhs::maximinLHS} 
(random but well-dispersed points).


% SVAR

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{News and monetary shocks\\ at a high frequency: 
  a simple approach} \\
  T. Matheson and E. Stavrev (2014)
\end{book}
Given a bivariate time series $X$,
one can estimate a VAR(1) model
$X_t = \alpha + \beta X_{t-1} + \epsilon_t$ using OLS.

To interpret the residual 
(\ie, to estimate a structural VAR (SVAR) model),
one can try to write it as an MA(1) process,
$\epsilon_t = A \eta_t$
where $\eta \sim N(0,1)$.
The innovations $\eta$ represent independent 
(Gaussian, standard) shocks.

There is not enough information 
to estimate both $A$ and $\eta$: 
if $\Sigma = \var \epsilon$
has Choleski decomposition $\Sigma = P P'$,
then $A=P$ is a solution, 
but so is $A=PU$, for $U \in O_2$.
One typically adds linear constraints on $A$ 
to ensure that there is only one solution, 
e.g., $A$ lower-triangular.

Instead, one can impose constraints 
on the signs of (some) of the coefficients) of $A$, e.g., 
\[
  \begin{pmatrix}
    + & + \\
    + & -
  \end{pmatrix}.
\]
This is still insufficient to determine $A$, 
but it is sufficient for a Bayesian approach: 
one can compute the posterior distribution of $A$
(using, e.g., a uniform prior on $O_2$).
The authors use rejection sampling 
[but since the elements of $O_2$ admit a simple parametrization, exact computation should be possible -- 
rejection sampling poses even larger problems in higher dimensions].
Since $O_2 \approx \mathbb S^1 \amalg \mathbb S^1$,
it is not straightforwad 
to extract an estimator from the posterior distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How do fiscal and technology shocks\\ affect real exchange rates?\\
  New evidence for the United States} \\
  Z. Enders et al. (2008)
\end{book}
More details on SVAR models with sign restrictions: 
allowance for small deviations to the sign constraints,
use of the posterior distribution 
to test the sign of the non-constrained elements. 


% Stat, LLA

%% Log-linear analysis; association discovery;
%% contingency tables with thousands of variables
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scaling log-linear analysis\\ to datasets
  with thousands of variables} \\
  F. Petitjean and G.I. Webb (2015)
\end{book}
Given $M$ qualitative variables $V_1,\dots,V_M$,
\d{log-linear analysis} (LLA)
greedily builds a model 
\begin{align*}
  P[V_1=a_1,\dots,V_M=a_M] = {} & u + {}\\
  &\sum_i u_i(a_i) + {}\\
  &\sum_{i<j} u_{ij}(a_i,a_j) + {}\\
  &\cdots
\end{align*}
only adding a term if it leads to a significant difference.

At each step, it is not necessary to re-examine 
all the (tuples) of variables:
one can identify many of those that remain insignificant
and only re-evaluate the others.

Implementation: Chordalysis (Java, GPL).

%% LLA
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A statistically efficient and scalable method\\ for log-linear analysis of high-dimensional data} \\
  F. Petitjean et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Use minimum message length (MML) 
instead of goodness-of-fit $\chi^2$ tests. 

%% LLA
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scaling log-linear analysis\\ to high-dimensional data} \\
  F. Petitjean et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Considering only decomposable models
(if an interaction between $k$ variables is present,
then so are all of its subsets,
and the corresponding graph is \emph{chordal})
leads to more efficient $\chi^2$ computations.

% Stat, Matrix
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unsupervised learning of acoustic features\\
  via deep canonical correlation analysis} \\
  W. Wang et al.
\end{book}
Canonical correlation analysis (CCA)
looks for highly correlated linear combinations between two groups of variables, $X$ and $Y$: 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} &  u, v \\
  \text{To maximize} & \cor( u' X, v' Y ).
\end{array}
\]
More generally (if $X$ and $Y$ are centered),
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} &  U, V \\
  \text{To maximize} & \operatorname{trace} U' X Y' V \\
  \text{Such that} & U' X X' U = n I \\
  & V' Y Y' V = n I.
\end{array}
\]
This is equivalent to minimizing 
\[
\dfrac1n \n{ U' X - V' Y }_F^2 + 
\lambda \n{ U }_F^2 + 
\lambda \n{ V }_F^2. 
\]
One can replace $X$ and $Y$ with their embedding
in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) 
or a deep neural net.

% Matrix
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tensor factorization via matrix factorization} \\
  V. Kuleshov et al. (2015)
\end{book}
To compute an approximate CP decomposition 
of a symmetric 3-tensor,
evaluate it on a set of random vectors
to get  symmetric matrices $(M_\ell)_\ell$
and jointly diagonalize them, \ie, find $U$ orthogonal
such that $U M_\ell U'$ be almost diagonal
(use an $L^2$ penalty on the off-diagonal elements -- 
it is possible to build $U$ iteratively, 
as a product of simple (rotation) matrices).
This can be generalized 
to higher-order tensors
or to non-symmetric tensors -- consider 
\[ \begin{pmatrix}0 & M' \\ M & 0\end{pmatrix}. \]


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fastfood -- approximating kernel expansions\\ in loglinear time} \\
  Q. Le et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Forecasts from kernel methods require the evaluation of the kernel on a large number of points
(e.g., for SVM, one needs
\[
  f(x) = \sum_{y \text{ support vector}} \alpha_y k(y,x), 
\]
and the number of support vectors grows linearly with the data).

A kernel can be written 
\[ k(x,y) = \sum \lambda_i \phi_i(x) \phi_i(y). \]
It can be approximated by drawing 
$i_1,\dots,i_n$ 
with $p(i) \propto\lambda_i$
and considering 
$k(x,y) \approx
\sum_{k=1}^n \phi_{i_k}(x) \phi_{i_k}(y)
.$

For Gaussian radial basis functions (RBF),
one can use 
$\phi_j(x) = \exp(i (Zx)_j)$
as eigenfunctions, 
where $Z$ is a Gaussian random matrix.

This matrix is large, but may be replaced with 
$S H G \Pi H B$ where 
$B$ has random $\pm1$ on the diagonal,
$G$ has random Gaussian numbers on the diagonal,
$S$ is a (diagonal) scaling factor,
$\Pi$ is a random permutation matrix
and $H$ is a Walsh-Hadamard matrix: 
\[
H_2 = 
\begin{pmatrix}
  1 & 1 \\
  1 & -1 
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
H_{2d} = 
\begin{pmatrix}
  H_d & H_d \\
  H_d & -H_d 
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

% Matrix, ML
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Speeding up convolutional neural network using fine-tuned CP-decomposition} \\
  V. Lebedev et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Convolutional neural networks (CNN) 
can be sped up by replacing the 4D convolution kernel 
with a low-rank approximation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An entropy-based early-warning indicator\\ 
  for systemic risk} \\
  M. Billio et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Use the entropy 
\begin{align*}
  & - \sum p_i \log p_i && \text{(Shannon)} \\
  &\dfrac1{\alpha-1} \left( 1 - \sum p_i^\alpha \right) && \text{(Tsallis)} \\
  &\dfrac1{1-\alpha} \log \sum p_i^\alpha && \text{(R\'enyi)}
\end{align*}
of 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The marginal expected shortfall
\[ \text{MES}_i = E[ r_i \mid r_{\text{market}} < \text{VaR}_{\text{market}}^{5\%} ]; \] 
\item $\Delta\text{CoVaR}$, where
\begin{align*}
  \Delta\text{CoVaR}_{m,i}^\alpha &= 
  \text{CoVaR}_{m,i}^\alpha - \text{CoVaR}_{m,i}^{1/2} \\
  \text{CoVaR}_{m,i}^\alpha &= \text{VaR}^\alpha[ r_{\text{market}} \mid r_i = \text{VaR}_i^\alpha ];
\end{align*}
\item The number of edges in the Granger causality graph of asset returns
\end{compactitem}
to predict crises (in the Finance industry).

% Matrix
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{New ranks for even-order tensors and their application in low-rank tensor optimization} \\
  B. Jiang et al. (2015)
\end{book}
For tensors, the notion of rank is complicated.


% Fin, BL
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Black-Litterman approach:\\ original model and extensions} \\
  A. Meucci (2010)
\end{book}
The Black-Litterman model
\begin{align*}
  \pi & && \text{equilibrium market returns} \\
  \mu &\sim N(\pi, \tau\Sigma) && \text{\emph{expected} market returns} \\
  X & \sim N(\mu, \Sigma) && \text{market returns} \\
  P' \mu & \sim N(v, \Omega) && \text{view on the expected returns}
\end{align*}
is needlessly complicated
and even counter-intuitive 
(the limiting cases $\Omega \to 0$ or $\Omega \to \infty$
are not those the end-user will expect).
Instead, one can specify views on the market returns:
\begin{align*}
X & \sim N( \mu, \Sigma ) \\
P' X & \sim N( v, \Omega ).
\end{align*}
To compute the posterior distribution,
reformulate the model as 
\begin{align*}
  X & \sim N(\mu, \Sigma) && \text{returns} \\
  Z & \sim N(0, \Omega) && \text{error on the views} \\
  v &= P' X + Z && \text{views},  
\end{align*}
compute the joint distribution of 
$(X, Z, P'X + Z)$
and then the conditional distribution 
$X \mid P' X + Z = v$.


% Alg

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A fuzzy R code similarity search\\ detection algorithm} \\
  A. Bartoszuk and M. Gagilewski
\end{book}
To measure the similarity (plagiarism, copy-paste) between two functions: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compare the edit distance $\mu_1$ 
  (\texttt{utils::adist})
  between the strings (normalized with 
  \texttt{deparse(paste(}\hspace{0pt}\texttt{text=f))});
\item Consider the blocks of code as bags of functions 
  (indeed, \texttt{<-}, \texttt{:}, \texttt{+}, \texttt{for}
  are functions)
  and compute some variant of Jaccard similarity, e.g., 
  \[ \mu_2(x,y) = 
     \dfrac{ \sum \min(x_i,y_i)}{\sum x_i + y_i} \]
  where $\mathbf x$ and $\mathbf y$ are the count vectors;
\item Compute the abstract syntax tree (AST)
  (\texttt{utils::}\hspace{0pt}\texttt{getParseData}),
  ignore names and values 
  (\textsc{symbol}, 
  % short underscore...
  \textsc{num\vrule height -1pt width .8ex depth 1.35pt\relax const})
  and compute the total length $\mu_3$
  of the common substrings above some minimum length;
\item Combine those three measures using a training set 
  and logistic regression.
\end{compactitem}
Other plagiarism detection tools include: 
MOSS, JPlag, GPlag.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting similarity of R functions\\ via a fusion of multiple heuristic methods} \\
  A. Bartoszuk and M. Gagilewski
\end{book}
To measure code similarity, 
use asymmetric measures, similar to the previous three ones, 
but normalized by dividing by 
the length of the first function, e.g., 
\[ \mu_2(x,y) = \dfrac{\sum \min(x_i,y_i) }{\sum x_i}. \]

Add another measure, 
\[
\mu_4 = \dfrac{\# V( H_{ij} ) }{ \# V( G_i) } \]
where $G_i$ is the 
\emph{program dependence graph} (PDG:
the vertices are the vertices of the AST; 
there are two types of edges, 
corresponding to the control flow
and to data dependencies) of $f_i$,
and $H_{ij}$ is the largest common (isomorphic) 
subgraph of $G_i$ and $G_j$
(computed with the McGregor heuristic algorithm, 
from the Boost C++ library).

% Alg, ML

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An industrial-strength audio search algorithm} \\
  A.L.C. Wang
\end{book}
To fingerprint audio files: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the time$\times$frequency spectrogram;
\item Identify peaks; ensure they are approximately 
  uniformly distributed;
\item Use rectangular regions of this constellation 
  of peaks as fingerprint.
\end{compactitem}
Search is then an alignment problem.

% Alg

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of fractal trees\\ to log-structured merge (LSM) trees} \\
  B.C. Kuszmaul (2014)
\end{book}
A \emph{fractal tree index} is a B-tree
with a buffer at each node:
only when the buffer is full is it sent to lower nodes. 
This requires more memory, but greatly reduces disk writes. 
[Nothing seems to justify the work ``fractal'': 
``lazy'' would have been a better name.]

\emph{LSM trees} store runs of data,
\ie, lists of key-value pairs sorted in key order
(some big data databases keep recently-inserted data 
in memory, sorted, as long as it fits,
and then write it to disk, each time in a different file).
These runs can be arranged in a tree.

% Alg
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data structures for text sequences} \\
  C. Crowley (1998)
\end{book}
To store and manipulate text, with insert and delete operations (as in a text editor), check the following data structures: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Array;
\item Array with a gap in the middle (corresponding to the current position);
\item Doubly-linked list of characters, or lines, or fixed-length (partially-empty) buffers;
\item PieceTable: two buffers, one read-only (the original file), one append-only, with an array of pointers indicating which span of which buffer to use in which order.
\end{compactitem}

% Alg, NN
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An efficient natural neighbour interpolation algorithm for geoscientific modelling} \\
  H. Ledoux and C. Gold
\end{book}
Move in the space of triangulations using flipping movements
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.22,yscale=.11,baseline=-3]
\draw (-1,0) -- (0,1) -- (1,0) -- (-1,0);
\draw (-1,0) -- (0,-1) -- (1,0) -- (-1,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
$\mapsto$
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.22,yscale=.11,baseline=-3]
\draw (0,1) -- (-1,0) -- (0,-1) -- (0,1);
\draw (0,1) -- (1,0) -- (0,-1) -- (0,1);
\end{tikzpicture}
until you reach the Delaunay triangulation. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A stable and fast implementation of natural neighbor interpolation} \\
  L. Liang and D. Hale (2010)
\end{book}
Java implementation: \texttt{edu.mines.booles.jtk.interp}.

% Alg
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Differential privacy and machine learning:\\ a survey and review} \\
  Z. Ji et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Differential privacy adds noise to ensure that changes to a single individual have a negligible impact.
For instance, one can add Laplace noise to the result $f(D)$,
with the amplitude of the noise proportional to 
\[ 
\max_{ d(D_1,D_2) = 1 \vrule height .6em width 0pt} 
  \!\!\!\!\!\! \n{ f(D_1) - f(D_2) }
\text{ or}
\max_{ d(D,D') = 1 \vrule height .6em width 0pt}
  \!\!\!\!\!\! \n{ f(D) - f(D') }
\]
(in the second case, only $D'$ varies, but you need some smoothing).

Similarly, to compute 
$\argmax_a f(D,a)$
(e.g., to maximize a likelihood),
one can sample from a Laplace distribution
\[ 
  p(a) \propto \exp \dfrac{ 
    \epsilon f(D,a)
  }{
  2 \max\limits_{\text{dist}=1} \n{ \Delta f }
  }
\]
For most machine learning algorithms, 
adding noise to the data 
and/or the loss function 
and/or the result 
ensures differential privacy.

% Alg, ML

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{DREAM$_{(D)}$: an adaptive Markov chain Monte Carlo simulation algorithm to solve discrete, non-continuous, and combinatorial posterior parameter estimation problems} \\
  J.A. Vrugt and C.J.F. Ter Braak (2011)
\end{book}

Differential evolution can be used as a proposal distribution in an adaptive Metropolis simulation with several MCMC chains (DREAM).

For discrete states, one could try to round the results, 
but this only works with ordered discrete variables.
Instead, one can take two chains at random,
two coordinates, and swap them.

Discrete MCMC can also be used for optimization or satisfiability problems (e.g., Sudoku).
[How different is it from simulated annealing and genetic algorithms?]


% VaR

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A directional multivariate value at risk} \\
  R. Torres et al.
\end{book}
The value-at-risk (VaR) can be generalized 
to a multivariate setup
by considering the set 
$Q_\alpha = \{ \mathbf x \ :\ P[ X \leq x ] = \alpha \}$
or,
if we prefer a single vector,
the intersection $Q_\alpha \inter E[X] + \Span \mathbf u$,
where $\mathbf u = (1,\dots,1)'$ is the vector in the middle 
of the positive quadrant.
This can be generalized to an arbitrary quadrant.
[The authors mistakenly claim that the quadrant
is entirely determined by $\mathbf u$.]

% VaR
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Shortfall deviation risk:\\ an alternative for risk measurement} \\
  M.B. Righi and P.S. Ceretta
\end{book}
There are many, many variants of value at risk (VaR)
and expected shortfall (ES).
Here is yet another one: 
\begin{align*}
  \text{ES} &= E[ \text{loss} \mid \text{loss} \geq \text{VaR} ] \\
  \text{SDR} &= \text{ES} + \sigma[ \text{loss} \mid\text{loss} \geq \text{ES} ]
\end{align*}


% Portfolio, variance matrix

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A robust statistics approach to minimum variance portfolio optimization} \\
  L. Yang et al.
\end{book}

Estimate the variance matrix as the fixed point $V(\rho)$ of 
\[
  V(\rho) = 
  (1-\rho) \dfrac1n
  \sum_i 
  \dfrac{
    x_i x'_i
  }{
    \dfrac1n x_i' V(\rho)^{-1} x_i 
  }
  + \rho I_N
\]
and choose $\rho$ to minimize 
the (ex post) variance portfolio
(Tyler's robust $M$ estimator).

% Stoch
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tornadoes and related damage costs\\ statistical modeling with a semi-Markov approach} \\
  C. Corini et al. (2015)
\end{book}
Tornadoes can be modeled using a \emph{Markov renewal process} $(J,T)$
\begin{align*}
  J_n &= \text{(discrete) intensity of the $n$th tornado}\\
  &\hphantom{{}={}} \text{(Fujita scale)}\\
  T_n &= \text{(discrete) time of the $n$th tornado}.
\end{align*}
One can also consider 
\begin{align*}
  Z_t &= \text{intensity of the last tornado} \\
  B_t &= \text{time since the last tornado}.
\end{align*}
Both $(J,T)$ and $(Z,B)$ are Markov; 
$Z$ is a \emph{semi-Markov} process.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hawkes processes in finance} \\
  E. Bacry et al.
\end{book}
Survey: definitions, properties, applications,
estimation, simulation (either one event at a time, 
as for inhomogeneous Poisson processes, 
or by tracking the parenthood tree:
first, generate events coming from the background rate, 
then their children, 
then their grand-children, etc.).

% Stoch
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dirac processes and default risk} \\
  C. Kenyon and A. Green (2015)
\end{book}
Since the short rate $r$ is always used in an integral, 
$\int_{t_1} ^{t_2} r(s) ds$,
it does not have to be a stochastic process:
it can be a generalized process.
A \emph{Dirac process}
is a process of the form 
$\sum_i \delta_{T_i}$,
there the $T_i$ are the arrival times of a Poisson process
and $\delta_a$ is the Dirac mass in~$a$.
They can be used in option pricing.


% Stat
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Horseshoe+ estimator\\ of ultra-sparse signals} \\
  A. Bhadra et al.
\end{book}
Sparse signals can be recovered by thresholding,
using the hierarchical model
\begin{align*}
  \theta_i &\sim (1-\mu) \delta_0 + \mu N(0,\psi^2) \\
  X_i      &\sim N(\theta_i,1).
\end{align*}
The Horseshoe prior is another hierarchical model: 
\begin{align*}
  \lambda_i &\sim \text{Half-Cauchy}(\tau) \\
  \theta_i  &\sim N(0,\lambda_i^2) \\
  X_i      &\sim N(\theta_i,1).
\end{align*}
The Horseshoe+ prior is yet another one:
\begin{align*}
  \eta_i &\sim \text{Half-Cauchy}(1) \\
  \lambda_i &\sim \text{Half-Cauchy}(\eta_i\tau) \\
  \theta_i  &\sim N(0,\lambda_i^2) \\
  X_i      &\sim N(\theta_i,1).
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hierarchical Bayesian survival analysis\\ and projective covariate selection\\ in cardiovascular event risk prediction} \\
  T. Peltola et al.
\end{book}
Prefer the Horseshoe prior to the Laplace one (lasso):
it does not shrink large coefficients too much.
The resulting (Bayesian, posterior) model is not sparse,
but one can find a sparse model with similar forecasts.

% Stat
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Moments determine the tail of the distribution (but not much else)} \\
  B.G. Lindsay and P. Basak (2000)
\end{book}
\[
\sup_{\substack{
\text{$F$ and $G$ have}\\
\text{the same first}\\
\text{$p$ moments}
}}
\hspace*{-5mm}
\va{ F(x) - G(x) }
\leq w_p(x)
\]
\[
w_p(x) = 
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & x & \cdots & x^p
\end{pmatrix}
\!\!
\begin{pmatrix}
  1 & m_1 & \cdots & m_p \\
  m1 & & & \vdots \\
  \vdots & & & \vdots \\
  m_p & \cdots & \cdots & m_{2p}
\end{pmatrix}
\!\!
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\ x \\ \vdots \\ x^p
\end{pmatrix}
\]

% Stat
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distinguishing cause from effect using observational data: methods and benchmarks} \\
  J.M. Mooij et al.
\end{book}
Comparison of bivariate causal discovery algorithms,
additive noise models
(compare the complexity of the linear models 
$y \sim x$ and $x \sim y$)
and information-theoretic causal inference 
(compare the flatness or entropy of the margins -- 
for non-linear relations, the flatter is the cause)
on a benchmark dataset.

% Stat
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A fast unified algorithm for solving group-lasso penalized learning problems} \\
  Y. Yang and H. Zou (2014)
\end{book}
A faster algorithm for the \emph{group lasso}
\[ 
  \hat \beta = 
  \argmin_\beta 
  \text{Loss}(\beta) + 
  \sum_{g \in \text{Groups}} \n{\beta_g}_2
\] 
(this is $\n{\cdot}_2$, not $\n{\cdot}^2_2$),
when the loss function has a bounded second derivative
(or, more generally, a Lipschitz first derivative: 
least squares, squared hinge loss, huberized hinge loss).

Implemented in R in \texttt{gglasso}.
Also check \texttt{grplasso}.

% Not read in detail: broken English and complicated notations.

% Stat, Copulas
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Archimedian-based Marshall-Olkin distributions and related copula functions} \\
  S. Mulinacci
\end{book}
% Clear motivating examples
The generalized Marshall-Olkin copula is the copula of 
$(\min(X_1,X_3), \min(X_2,X_3))$, 
where $(X_1,X_2,X_3)$ has an archimedian copula
and (for instance) exponential margins.
The random variables $X_1$ and $X_2$
are the failure times of two machines, 
$X_3$ is a systemic failure time 
(it entails the failure of both machines),
the archimedian copula represents a common factor 
that influences all three types of failures in the same way
(e.g., the maintenance of the power plant).

% Stat, Stan
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fully automated variational inference\\
   of differentiable probability models} \\
  A. Kucukelbir et al.
\end{book}
Variational Bayes in Stan: 
let $X$ denote the observed variable, 
$Z$ the hidden variable; 
$p(X,Z)$ is known, but $p(Z|X)$ is too complicated.
One can look for $q(Z)$, Gaussian, 
close to $p(Z|X)$ for the Kullback-Leibler distance,
estimated by gradient descent.


% Optim
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\texttt{blowtorch}: an R package for on-line constrained optimization} \\
  L. Petito and S. Pollack
\end{book}
The \texttt{blowtorch} package solves the equality-constrained optimization problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & x \\
\text{To maximize} & f(x) \\ 
\text{Such that} & G(x) = 0
\end{array}
\]
using Lagrange multipliers
\[ \Lambda(x,\lambda) = f(x) - \lambda \cdot G(x) \]
and stochastic gradient descent (SGD)
to minimize $\n{ \nabla \Lambda }_2^2$.
The user must provide $f'$, $f''$, $G$, $G'$, $G''$;
it can be used as a pre-processing step before 
CG (conjugate gradient) or BFGS.

% Optim
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic dual coordinate ascent methods\\ for regularized loss minimization} \\
  S. Shalev-Shwartz and T. Zhang
\end{book}
One can replace gradient descent 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
 \text{Find} & w \\
 \text{To minimize} & 
 \displaystyle
 \dfrac1n \sum_i \phi_i( w' x_i) + \dfrac\lambda2\n w ^2
\end{array}
\]
with dual coordinate ascent
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \\
  \text{To maximize} & 
  \displaystyle
  \dfrac1n \sum - \phi_i^*( - \alpha_i) - \dfrac\lambda2 \n{\dfrac1{\lambda n} \sum \alpha_i x_i }^2
\end{array}
\]
For faster convergence, 
start with SGD,
then use stochastic DCA.


% Philo
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The role of Occam's razor\\ in knowledge discovery} \\
  P. Domingos
\end{book}
There are two versions of Occam's razor: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item[1.] If the generalization error is the same, prefer simpler models: they are easier to understand and explain; 
\item[2.] If the training error is the same, prefer the simpler model: the generalization error will be lower.
\end{compactitem}
The second is wrong:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item In a regression, one should not round the coefficients,
  even though ``$x+2$'' is simpler than ``$1.17 x + 1.98$'';
\item The VC dimension is a better indicator 
  of the generalization error than the number of parameters; 
%\item It favours a given bias-variance trade-off, 
%  which need not correspond to the data;
\item The number of models among which the final model is chosen is more important than its complexity (multiple testing); 
\item Insanely complex models work well: 
  penalized regression, 
  the kernel trick which inflates 
  the dimension of the feature space, 
  ensemble models, deep learning.
\end{compactitem}


% Fin, Alg

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating the algorithmic complexity\\ of stock markets} \\
  O. Brandouy et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The Kolmogorov complexity $K(s)$
of a binary string $s$ 
is the minimum length of a program (in some fixed language)
that outputs $s$.
An (infinite) string $s$ is \emph{random}
(this is \emph{not} a probabilistic notion) if 
\[ 
\exists c \quad \forall n \quad K(s_{1:n}) \geq n - c.
\]
One can estimate the randomness 
of a (long) string by looking at its compressibility, 
with algorithms such as Huffman, RLE or Paq8o8. 
To apply this idea to time series, 
iteratively remove the visible structure, 
try to compress, and iterate as long as 
the signal remains compressible: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Remove the visible structure from the prices: 
  compute the log-returns; 
\item Remove the visible structure 
  (the distribution of returns): 
  uniformize the returns; 
\item Remove the visible structure (volatility clustering): 
  uniformize the returns on a moving window;
\item Discretize, compress.
\end{compactitem}
What remains is not clear: 
it could be market microstructure (e.g., tick size) 
or the artefacts of the structure-removing procedures. 

% Fin
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Liquidity crises on different time scales} \\
  F. Corradi et al. (2015)
\end{book}
The notion of liquidity depends on the time scale: 
the depth and breadth of the limit order book 
around 30 seconds; 
the speed at which the order imbalance disappears, 
around 15 minutes.

