\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data science at the command line} \\
  J. Janssens (2014)
\end{book}

Traditional Unix tools (\texttt{grep}, \texttt{sort}, \texttt{uniq}, 
\texttt{head},
\texttt{seq}, 
\texttt{paste}, 
\texttt{shuf}, 
\texttt{parallel}, 
etc.
can be complemented with more 
recent or specialized tools to process data
(download, clean, explore, model) such as 
\texttt{cvskit} 
(in particular \texttt{csvlook} and \texttt{cvssql}),
\texttt{jq} (to transform JSON data, \`a la XPath),
\texttt{json2csv}, 
\texttt{scrape} (to transform XML/HTML data, 
using XPath or CSS selectors), 
\texttt{Rio} (to use R as a filter, 
to convert a CSV file into another one, or into a plot).

Some of those tools are not standard 
and were developped by the author for the book.
All are available in a (Vagrant) virtual machine.

The book suggests Drake to manage data science workflows
and gives a few examples of actual computations,
with Tapkee (a C++ library, part of Shogun, 
for dimension reduction: PCA, MDS, t-SNE, LLE, isomap),
Weka, R (via \texttt{Rio}), 
SKLL, BigML -- but no mention of 
Vowpal Wabbit, libsvm, liblinear or libfm.
Some traditional and useful Unix commands were also missing: 
\texttt{xargs} (mostly replaced by \texttt{parallel}),
\texttt{dc} (but many prefer \texttt{bc}),
\texttt{file}, 
\texttt{screen}, 
\texttt{tmux}, 
\texttt{csplit} (though \texttt{split} was mentioned).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Big data analytics summer school} \\
  (Caltech, JPL, Coursera, 2014)
\end{book}

The $k$-nearest neightbours (kNN) algorithm 
can be implented as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item In dimension 1, with binary search;
\item In dimensions 2 to 8, with a kd-tree;
\item In dimensions greater than 8, 
  with locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) -- 
  this only provides approximate neighbours.
\end{compactitem}
In much higher dimensions, kNN no longer works:
all the distances are approximately equal 
(curse of dimensionality).

The Euclidian distance is not a good choice 
for distance-based algorithms 
in presence of corrrelations or differences of scale. 
One could use the Mahalanobis distance but, 
for supervised learning, this is suboptimal.
For instance, for a classification problem, 
multiclass discriminant analysis 
(a form of \d{metric learning})
looks for the distance that best separates the classes, 
\ie, maximizes 
\[ 
  \gamma(V) = 
  \dfrac{ \va{ V' \Sigma_b V } }%
        { \va{ V' \Sigma_w V } }
\]
where 
\begin{align*}
  V & = \text{linear projection of the data} \\
  \Sigma_b &= \text{between scatter matrix} \\
  \Sigma_w &= \text{within scatter matrix}.
\end{align*}

The course also covered the following topics: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Dimension reduction:
  kNN, feature selection, PCA, metric learning, 
  kernel PCS (kPCA);
\item Bootstrap (classical, $N$ out of $M$, subsampling);
\item Visualization;
\item Random forests (the sample proximity
  is the number of trees in which 
  samples $i$ and $j$ end up in the same leaf).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mining massive datasets} \\
	J. Leskovec, A. Rajaraman, J. Ullman
	(Stanford, Coursera, 2014)
\end{book}

The complexity of a \emph{map-reduce} algorithm should be measured by the communication cost -- 
that is the main bottleneck, 
and its is reasonable to assume that 
all the data has to be moved around. 
For instance, matrix multiplication is more efficient 
with a 2-step algorithm: 
first divide the matrix into blocks (\emph{tiles}) 
and compute the partial sums, 
then aggregate them.

\emph{Page-rank} can be personalized 
by teleporting to a random page in a set of reference pages 
(e.g., the DMOZ pages for the topic of interest) 
instead of a random page;
it can be made more robust to spam farms 
by teleporting to a random page in a set of trusted pages
(e.g., edu domains).
% Also mentioned: HITS 

\d{Min-hashing} is an approximate test for set equality --
a \d{locality-sensitive hashing} (LSH) function for sets.
Let $\Omega=\{1,\dots,n\}$ be a set 
(in practice, it will be a set of words or $n$-grams);
a random permutation $\sigma \in \mathfrak S(\Omega)$ 
defines a (random) hash function 
\[ h_\sigma(C) = \inf \{\, i \ :\ \sigma(i) \in C \,\}, 
  \qquad C \subset \Omega. \]
The probability that two subsets $C_1$ and $C_2$ agree 
on this hash function is their Jaccard similarity, 
\[ 
  P[ h_{\sigma}(C_1) = h_{\sigma}(C_2) ] = 
  J(C_1,C_2) = 
  \dfrac{\va{C_1 \inter C_2}}{\va{C_1 \union C_2}}.
\]

A family $H$ of hash functions $h: S \longrightarrow X$, 
where $(S,d)$ is a metric space
and $P$ a probability on $H$, is 
said to be 
$(d_1,d_2,p_1,p_2)$-sensitive if 
\[ \forall x,y \in S \quad d(x,y) \leq d_1 \implies P[ h(x) = h(y) ] \geq p_1 \]
\[ \forall x,y \in S \quad d(x,y) \geq d_2 \implies P[ h(x) = h(y) ] \leq p_2. \]
It can be ``amplified'' by the \textsc{and} 
or \textsc{or} construction,
or an \textsc{and-or} or \textsc{or-and} combination
(the distances $d_1$, $d_2$ remain the same, 
probabilities are transformed by $p \mapsto p^r$ or $p \mapsto 1-(1-p)^r$).
\begin{align*}
  \textsc{and} &:\ \forall i,j \in \llbracket 1, r \rrbracket \quad h_i(x) = h_j(x) \\
  \textsc{or} &:\ \exists i,j \in \llbracket 1, r \rrbracket \quad h_i(x) = h_j(x)
\end{align*}

Here are some examples of LSH families:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For the cosine distance, project on a random hyperplane
  \[ 
  h_v(x) = \operatorname{sign}(v\cdot x), \quad
  P[ h(x) = h(y) ] = 1 - \dfrac\theta\pi;
  \]
\item For the Euclidian distance, 
project the points on a random line, 
and put them in buckets of length $a$.
\end{compactitem}

For approximate text similarity 
(Jaccard similarity of the bag or words, 
or the bag of $n$-grams), 
one can also look at the following: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The number of distinct words; 
\item The $N$ least common words -- 
 that gives a lower bound on the distance;
\item An index (word, position, number of remaining words), 
  where ``position'' is the position in the bag of words,
  sorted with the rarest words first.
\end{compactitem}

The apriori algorithm, for \emph{frequent itemset mining},
proceeds in two steps
(for item pairs -- $n$ steps for $n$-item sets): 
first count the number of occurrences of each item, 
and pick those that appear at least $s$ times; 
then, count the pairs of items 
(but only for those found in the first step), 
and keep those that appear at least $s$ times. 
The memory consumption can be reduced: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item PCY: in the first pass, also count pairs, 
  but after hashing them;
\item Multihash: idem, with several hash functions; 
\item Multistage: in pass 1.5, count the hashed pairs, 
  but only for the items from pass 1;
\item Toivonen: run the algorithm 
  on a random subset of the data that fits in memory; 
  enlarge the set of candidate itemsets 
  by adding the immediate subsets 
  of those selected by the algorithm; 
  count them (on the whole dataset); 
  enlarge the set of candidate itemsets if necessary; 
  iterate until convergence.
\end{compactitem}

One can model communities using a bipartite graph,
with nodes and communities as vertices, 
and a link probability $p_c$ for each community $c$; 
a link emerges between nodes $u$ and $v$ with probability 
\[ P(u,v) = 1 - \prod_{c\ :\ u,v \in c} ( 1 - p_c ) \]
(or $\max\{\epsilon, P(u,v)\}$).
The \d{BigCLAM} model 
replaces the binary community membership structure 
with a community strength matrix, 
$F_{uc} \in [0,1]$, 
and a link emerges between $u$ and $v$ 
with probability 
\begin{flalign*}
  && P(u,v) &= 1 - \prod_c ( 1 - P_c(u,v) ), &&\\
  &\text{where}&
  P_c(u,v) &= 1 - \exp( - F_{uc} \cdot F_{vc}  ),\\
  &\text{\ie,}&
  P(u,v) &= 1 - \exp( - F_u \cdot F_v' ). 
\end{flalign*}
The strength matrix can be estimated as 
\begin{align*} 
F &= 
  \argmax_F 
  \prod_{ (u,v) \in     E } P(u,v) 
  \prod_{ (u,v) \not\in E } ( 1 - P(u,v) ) \\
  &= 
  \argmax_F 
  \sum_{ (u,v) \in     E } \log\left( 1 - e^{-F_u \cdot F_v'} \right)
  \sum_{ (u,v) \not\in E } F_u \cdot F_v'.
\end{align*}

If all the nodes of a graph have the same degree $d$, 
$\mathbf 1$ is an eigenvector of its adjacency matrix 
with eigenvalue $d$;
if the graph is not connected, 
$(1\cdots10\cdots0)$ and $(0\cdots01\cdots1)$ are 
eigenvectors for eigenvalue $d$;
if there are few edges between the clusters, 
those vectors are
approximate eigenvectors for an eigenvalue close to $d$.
In the general case
(\d{spectral graph partitioning}), 
consider the Laplacian 
$L = (\operatorname{deg} G) \text{Id} - A$; 
the vector $\mathbf 1$ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $0$; 
the second smallest eigenvalue is 
\[ 
  \lambda_2 = 
  \min_x \dfrac{ x' L x }{ x' x } = 
  \min_{\substack{x\\x'x=1}} \sum_{(i,j) \in E} (x_i-x_j)^2.
\]
The coordinates of that vector give the clustering: 
if there are two clusters, of the same size, 
they are $\{ i \ :\ x_i > 0 \}$ 
and      $\{ i \ :\ x_i < 0 \}$;
in general, look for a jump in \texttt{sort(x)}
and/or look at the third eigenvector.

\d{Trawling} finds small communities 
by looking for complete bipartite subgraphs $K_{s,t}$ -- 
it is a frequent itemset problem.

One can approximately count 
the number of 1s in a stream of bits, 
on a moving window of size $k\gg1$, 
by keeping track of the counts 
for blocks of sizes $2^0$, $2^1$, $2^2,\dots$
and merging them when needed, 
but the result can be imprecise 
if the smaller blocks are empty; 
instead, one can define ``size'' as being 
the number of 1s instead of the number of positions.

To estimate the number of different elements in a stream,
look at the maximum number $R$ of leading zeroes 
in a binary hash of those elements 
(and repeat for many hash functions): 
$2^R$ is an estimator of the number of distinct elements but, 
unfortunately, $E[2^R] = \infty$. 
This problem can be fixed (\emph{HyperLogLog}).

% Also mentioned: Bloom filter

The singular value decomposition (SVD) gives 
the best rank-$k$ approximation of a matrix,
but it does not preserve sparsity. 
The \d{CUR decomposition} addresses this problem: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $C$ is obtained by taking (say) 
  $4k$ columns of $A$, at random, 
  sampled with probability 
  $P(j) \propto \n{ A_{\cdot j} }_{L^2}^2$
  (with replacement):
  $ C = A_{\cdot J} $;
\item Similarity, $R$ is obtained from rows of $A$: 
  $R = A_{I \cdot}$;
\item $U = A_{IJ}^\dag$
  (the pseudo-inverse is also computed from the SVD: 
  if $W = X S Y'$ is the SVD of $W$, 
  then $W^\dag = Y S^\dag X'$, 
  where $S=\diag(s_i)$, 
  $S^\dag=\diag(s_i^{-1})$
  and $s_i^{-1} = 0$ if $s_i=0$).
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Content-based recommendation systems} 
look at the features 
(e.g., TDIDF, for text) of items highly rated by the user.
User-user \emph{collaborative filtering} 
finds users similar to the target
(cosine similarity, or 
centered cosine similarity aka correlation);
item-item collaborative filtering is similar.
\emph{Latent factor recommendation systems} 
decompose the user$\times$item rating matrix 
(e.g., SVD with missing values).

There are big-data (1-pass) approximations of the $k$-means clustering algorithm: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Read the data in batches; build the clusters progressively; 
  for each cluster, only keep the mean, standard deviation and count;
\item CURE: Run $k$-means on a random sample of the data; 
  pick 4 points per cluster; 
  move them towards the center; 
  read all the data and assign each observation to the nearest point.
\end{compactitem}

\d{Support vector machines} (SVM)
are looking for the separating hyperplane with the largest margin
\[
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w, b \\
  \text{To maximize} & \min\limits_i ( w \cdot x_i + b ) y_i \\
  \text{Such that} & \n w = 1
  \end{array} 
\]
where $y_i \in \{ \pm 1 \} $. 
This can be written 
\[
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \text{Find} & w, b, \gamma \\
    \text{To maximize} & \gamma \\
    \text{Such that} & \forall i \  (w \cdot x+i + b ) y_i \geq \gamma \\
    & \n w = 1.
  \end{array}
\]
The \emph{support vectors} are the observations $x_i$
such that the margin 
$(w \cdot x_i + b ) y_i$ is $\gamma$.
If we remove the constraint $\n w = 1$ and impose 
$(w \cdot x_i + b ) y_i = 1$, 
the margin becomes $\n w ^{-1}$
(to prove it, consider a support vector $x_+$, 
its symmetrix $x_-$ wrt the hyperplane,
and add their margins),
and the problem
\[
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \text{Find} & w, b \\
    \text{To minimize} & \n w ^2 \\
    \text{Such that} & \forall i \  (w \cdot x+i + b ) y_i \geq 1.
  \end{array}
\]
If the data is not separable, 
add a penalty $c \xi_i$ for each misclassified point 
\[
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \text{Find} & w, b, \xi \\
    \text{To minimize} & \n w ^2 + c \sum_i \xi_i \\
    \text{Such that} & \forall i \  (w \cdot x+i + b ) y_i \geq 1 - \xi_i \\
    & \forall i \ \xi_i \geq 0.
  \end{array}
\]
This can be written with a \emph{hinge loss}
\[
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \text{Find} & w, b \\
    \text{To minimize} & \n w ^2 + c \sum\limits_i \max\{ 0, 1 - y_i ( w \cdot x_i + b ) \}.
  \end{array}
\]
The constraints have disappeared: we can use (stochastic) gradient descent.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Process mining} \\
  W. van der Aalst (Coursera, 2014)
\end{book}

Process mining is the study and modeling of logs 
(a \emph{log} is a set of traces,
a \emph{trace} is q sequences of actions
or tasks or activities, with timestamps, 
and often ancilliary data -- usually as CSV files, 
but there is an XML standard: XES). % MXML is deprecated 
The model is often represented
using the business process modeling notation (\d{BPMN}), 
with activities 
$\tikz{\node[draw]{};}$
and decision nodes 
(\textsc{xor} split
\smash{\raisebox{-1.8mm}{\tikz{\node[draw,diamond,inner sep=0pt]{$\times$};}}}, 
\textsc{xor} join, 
\textsc{and} join, 
\smash{\raisebox{-1.8mm}{\tikz{\node[draw,diamond,inner sep=0]{$+$};}}}, 
\textsc{and} split)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=3mm,>=stealth]
\tikzset{
  activity/.style={draw},
  start/.style={draw,circle},
  end/.style={draw,circle,double},
  place/.style={draw,diamond,inner sep=0pt}
}
\node[start] (Start) at (0,0) {};
\node[activity, right=of Start    ] (Request) {};
\node[place,    right=of Request ] (Check1) {$\boldsymbol+$};
\node[activity, right=5.5mm of Check1   ] (Check) {};
\node[activity, above=of Check    ] (Examine) {};
\node[activity, above=of Examine  ] (Thoroughly) {};
\node[place,    right=5.5mm of Check,   ] (Check2) {$\boldsymbol+$};
%\node[place,    left=of Examine   ] (Examine1) {$\boldsymbol\times$};
%\node[place,    right=of Examine  ] (Examine2) {$\boldsymbol\times$};
\node[place,    above=2.5mm of Check1  ] (Examine1) {$\boldsymbol\times$};
\node[place,    above=2.5mm of Check2  ] (Examine2) {$\boldsymbol\times$};
\node[activity, right=of Check2,  ] (Decide) {};
\node[end,      right=of Decide     ] (End) {};
\draw[->] (Start) -- (Request);
\draw[->] (Request) -- (Check1);
\draw[->] (Check1) -- (Check);
\draw[->] (Check1) -- (Examine1);
\draw[->] (Examine2) -- (Check2);
\draw[->] (Examine1) -- (Examine.west);
\draw[->] (Examine1) -- (Thoroughly.west);
\draw[->] (Examine.east) -- (Examine2);
\draw[->] (Thoroughly.east) -- (Examine2);
\draw[->] (Check) -- (Check2);
\draw[->] (Check2) -- (Decide);
\draw[->] (Decide) -- (End);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

To more easily keep track of the ``current state''
(the process can be in several branches at the same time),
one can use a \d{Petri net} instead: 
it is made of transitions 
$\tikz{\node[draw]{};}$
and places
\raisebox{-1.5pt}{\tikz{\node[draw,circle]{};}},
and the current state is tracked with 
one or several tokens
\raisebox{.3mm}{\tikz{\node[circle,fill,inner sep=.5mm]{};}}\,: 
a transition is enabled when there is a token in each
of its input places; 
when it fires, 
it consumes a token in each of its input places 
and produces one in each output place 
(the number of tokens is not constant).
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
\tikzset{
  transition/.style={draw},
  start/.style={draw,circle},
  end/.style={draw,circle,double},
  place/.style={draw,circle}
}
\node[start] (Start) at (0,0) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep=.5mm] (Token) at (0,0) {};
\node[transition] (Request) at (1,0) {};
\node[place] (Examine1) at (2,.5) {};
\node[place] (Check1) at (2,-.5) {};
\node[transition] (Check) at (3,-.5) {};
\node[transition] (ExamineA) at (3,.1667) {};
\node[transition] (ExamineB) at (3,.8333) {};
\node[place] (Examine2) at (4,.5) {};
\node[place] (Check2) at (4,-.5) {};
\node[transition] (Decide) at (5,0) {};
\node[end] (End) at (6,0) {};
\draw[->] (Start) -- (Request);
\draw[->] (Request) -- (Examine1);
\draw[->] (Request) -- (Check1);
\draw[->] (Examine1) -- (ExamineA);
\draw[->] (Examine1) -- (ExamineB);
\draw[->] (ExamineA) -- (Examine2);
\draw[->] (ExamineB) -- (Examine2);
\draw[->] (Check1) -- (Check);
\draw[->] (Check) -- (Check2);
\draw[->] (Check2) -- (Decide);
\draw[->] (Examine2) -- (Decide);
\draw[->] (Decide) -- (End);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

A Petri net is $k$-bounded 
  if there are at most $k$ tokens in  any given place; 
it is \emph{safe} if it is 1-bounded; 
a \emph{deadlock} is a reachable dead marking, \ie, 
  a state in which no transition is enabled; 
a \emph{live transition} is a transition that can be enabled from any reachable marking;   
a \emph{workflow net} is a Petri net, 
  with one source place, one sink place, 
  in which all nodes are on a path from source to sink; 
a workflow net is \emph{sound} 
  if it is safe,
  has no dead parts, 
  has proper completion (if there is a token in the sink,
  there are no tokens anywhere else),
  and has the option to complete.
  
If $x$ and $y$ are events appearing in the log, 
$x<y$ means that $x$ is sometimes immediately 
followed by $y$.
The \d{causal footprint} of a log is the set (matrix) 
of relations between events, 
where the possible relations are: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $x \rightarrow y$ if $x < y$ and not $y < x$;
\item $x \mathrel{\|} y $ if $x<y$ and $y<x$; 
\item $x \mathrel{\#} y$ if neither $x<y$ nor $y<x$.
\end{compactitem}

The \d{alpha algorithm} tries to build a Petri net 
from the causal footprint of a log. Intuitively,
it tries to recognize patterns for 
\textsc{xor}/\textsc{and} split/join 
(e.g., $a \rightarrow b$, $a \rightarrow c$, $b \mathrel{\#} c$
 corresponds to a \textsc{xor} split).
More formally: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $L = $ the set of traces (the log); 
  a trace is a sequence of tasks;
\item $T = $ the set of tasks;
\item $T_i = $ the set of initial tasks;
\item $T_o = $ the set of final (output) tasks;
\item $X$ is the set of candidate places, 
  where a candidate place is a pair $(A,B)$, 
  $A, B \subset T$, 
  such that 
  \begin{align*}
  \forall a \in A \quad \forall b \in B &\quad a \rightarrow b \\
  \forall a_1, a_2 \in A &\quad a_1 \mathrel{\#} a_2 \\
  \forall b_1, b_2 \in B &\quad b_1 \mathrel{\#} b_2
  \end{align*}
\item $Y \subset X$ 
  only contains the maximal elements of $X$;
\item For the set of places
  $P = Y \union \{ i, o \} $, 
  just add an initial and a final place; 
\item The set of arcs, $F$, contains
  an arc from $i$ to each element of $T_i$;
  an arc from each element of $T_o$ to $o$;
  for places $(A,B)$, 
  an arc from each element $a$ of $A$ to $(A,B)$, 
  and an arc from $(A,B)$ to each element $b$ of $B$.
\end{compactitem}

The algorithm is related to frequent itemset mining.
The model learned by the alpha algorithm is not minimal 
(one can sometimes remove places with no visible effect),
and cannot capture loops or non-local dependencies; 
it is not robust to noise: 
you can end up with a flower model, 
which allows any behaviour.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.7]
\tikzset{
  transition/.style={draw},
  start/.style={draw,circle},
  end/.style={draw,circle,double},
  place/.style={draw,circle}
}
\node[start] (Start) at (0,0) {};
\node[circle,fill,inner sep=.5mm] (Token) at (0,0) {};
\node[transition] (Start1) at (1,0) {};
\node[place] (Center) at (2,0) {};
\node[transition] (A) at (1.5,.7) {};
\node[transition] (B) at (2,1) {};
\node[transition] (C) at (2.5,.7) {};
\node[transition] (D) at (1.5,-.7) {};
\node[transition] (E) at (2,-1) {};
\node[transition] (F) at (2.5,-.7) {};
\node[transition] (End1) at (3,0) {};
\node[end] (End) at (4,0) {};
\draw[->] (Start) -- (Start1);
\draw[->] (Start1) -- (Center);
\draw[->] (Center) to [bend left=10] (A);
\draw[->] (Center) to [bend left=10] (B);
\draw[->] (Center) to [bend left=10] (C);
\draw[->] (Center) to [bend left=10] (D);
\draw[->] (Center) to [bend left=10] (E);
\draw[->] (Center) to [bend left=10] (F);
\draw[->] (A) to [bend left=10] (Center);
\draw[->] (B) to [bend left=10] (Center);
\draw[->] (C) to [bend left=10] (Center);
\draw[->] (D) to [bend left=10] (Center);
\draw[->] (E) to [bend left=10] (Center);
\draw[->] (F) to [bend left=10] (Center);
\draw[->] (Center) -- (End1);
\draw[->] (End1) -- (End);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\d{Region-based} process discovery 
tries to address some of those problems. 
First, define states from the log; for instance,
a state could be 
  the trace before a given point, 
  or the trace after a given point, 
  or the whole trace, 
  or the last (or next) $k$ actions
  (as a sequence, or as a set, or as a multiset);
  one could also use the other attributes (\emph{resources})
  of the events. 
The states form a transition system. 
A \emph{region} is a subset of states such that, 
for each activity $a$, either $a$ always enters the region, 
or $a$ always exits the region, 
or $a$ never enters nor leaves the region.
The places are the non-trivial minimal regions.

\d{Language-based regions} reduce the problem 
of finding the places to a linear algebra problem: 
a place is a solution $(x,y,c) \in \NN^3$ of 
$c \mathbf 1 + B x - A y \geq 0$,
where 
$A$ and $B$ are binary matrices, 
with one column per activity 
and one row for each prefix of a trace, 
an element of $A$ is 1 if the activity is in the prefix
an element of $B$ is 1 if the activity is in the prefix
without its last element; 
$c$ is the number of tokens, 
the nonnegativity condition ensures that 
the number of tokens in a place is never negative,
$x$ and $y$ describe the arcs, 
$x$ is the number of transitions to the place $(x,y,c)$,
$y$ is the number of transitions from the place $(x,y,c)$.

\d{Inductive process mining} decomposes the event log 
using \textsc{seq}, \textsc{xor} and \textsc{and} 
operations.
% (loops as well)
Genetic algorithms are also used in process mining.

To measure the \emph{conformance} 
of a log and a model: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the causal footprint of the log 
  and the model, and count the discrepancies; 
\item Replay the log, 
  add tokens to enable transitions when needed, 
  and count the number of tokens added 
  and the number of tokens remaining at the end
  (but once the net is flooded with tokens, 
  anything is possible);
\item Align the log and the model [no details].
\end{compactitem}

Process models can be \emph{enhanced}: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item At decision points, use statistical models 
  to guess which branch will be taken
  (data-aware Petri nets);
\item Identify bottlenecks and their causes;
\item Social networks (for instance, 
  it is easy to identify the structure of an organization); 
\item Operational support: detect problems, 
  predict the remaining time, 
  predict and recommend the next activity or resource.
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MEG decoding using Riemannian geometry and unsupervised classification} \\
  A. Barachant 
\end{book}
To forecast a binary class $k \in \{0,1\}$
from multidimensional signals 
$x_i: \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket \rightarrow \RR^c$: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the average for each class, 
$p_k: \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket \rightarrow \RR^c$;
\item For each class $k \in \{0,1\}$, 
  combine the signals 
  \begin{align*}
    & w_1 p_{k1} + \cdots + w_c p_{kc} \\
    & w_1 x_{1}  + \cdots + w_c x_{c}
  \end{align*}
  to maximize the signal-to-noise ratio
  \[
  w^{(k)} = 
  \argmax_w 
  \dfrac{ \n{ w' p_k }_2 }{ \n{ w' x }_2 } =
  \argmax_w \dfrac{w' p_k p'_k w}{w'xx'w};
  \]
\item It is an eigenvalue problem: 
  keep the first four eigenvectors
  $w^{k1},\dots,w^{k4}$;
\item As features, use the covariance matrix 
  $\Sigma_i = \tfrac1N Z_i Z_i'$ 
  of 
  \[ 
  Z_i = \begin{pmatrix}
  w^{01}{}' p_0 \\ \vdots \\ w^{04}{}' p_0 \\ 
  w^{11}{}' p_1 \\ \vdots \\ w^{14}{}' p_1 \\ 
  w^{01}{}' x_i \\ \vdots \\ w^{14}{}' x_i
  \end{pmatrix}
  \]
\item Do not use the variance matrices themselves, but project them on the tangent space (at $I$, using the logarithm) of the space $S_+$ of positive definite matrices.
\end{compactitem}
The actual Kaggle task was a form of 
\emph{transfer learning}
(the test samples were not 
from the same distribution as the training samples -- 
MEGs from a different patient):
one can use the forecasts from the trained model 
as a starting point for the $k$-means algorithm.
But this requires barycenters: 
in $S_+$, 
the average of a set of points is the point that maximizes the sum of the squared distances, 
and the distance is 
\begin{align*}
  d( \Sigma_1, \Sigma_2 ) &= 
  \n{ \log \Sigma_1^{-1/2} \Sigma_2 \Sigma_1^{-1/2} }_F \\
  & = \sqrt{ \sum_c \log^2 \lambda_c}
\end{align*}
where the $\lambda_c$ are the eigenvalues of 
$\Sigma_1^{-1/2} \Sigma_2 \Sigma_1^{-1/2}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A Riemannian geometry with\\ complete geodesics for the set of positive semidefinite matrices of fixed rank} \\
  B. Vandereycken et al. (2013)
\end{book}
It is easy to describe the geodesics of 
$S_+(n,n)$, the manifold of positive definite matrices
of size $n$, and use them in optimization problems 
(e.g., line search along a geodesic),
but the algorithms do not scale (they are $O(n^3)$).
Low-rank approximations are attractive, 
but the space of positive semidefinite matrices 
of size $n$ and rank $p$, $S_+(n,p)$, 
has no canonical metric. 
It can be described as the orbit of 
%\[ 
%\begin{pmatrix} 
%1 &        &   &   & & 0 \\
%  & \ddots \\
%  &        & 1 \\
%  &        &   & 0 \\
%  &        &   &   & \ddots \\
%0 &        &   &   &        & 0   
%\end{pmatrix}
%\]
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
  \mathbf 1 _p & \mathbf 0 _{p,n-p} \\
  \mathbf 0 _{n-p,p} & \mathbf 0 _{n-p}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
under the action of $\text{GL}_n^+$
defined by $A \cdot X = A X A^{-1}$.
The natural right-invariant metric on $\text{GL}_n$,
$g_A(\eta_A,\nu_A) = \tr( A^{-1}{}' \eta_A' \nu_A A^{-1} )$,
defines a metric on $S_+(p,n)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A differential geometric approach\\ to the geometric mean\\ of symmetric positive-definite matrices} \\
  M. Moakher
\end{book}
The matrix exponential $\exp: S(n) \to S_+(n)$ 
establishes a bijection between symmetric matrices $S(n)$
and symmetric positive definite matrices 
$S_+(n) \simeq \text{GL}_n/O_n$.

The geodesic on $S_+(n)$ from $I$ in the direction $S$ is 
$t \mapsto e^{tS}$.

The geodesic on $S_+(n)$ from $P$ in the direction 
$S \in S(n) \simeq T_P S_+(n)$ is 
$t \mapsto P^{1/2} e^{tP^{1/2}SP^{-1/2}}P^{1/2}$.

The distance on $S_+(n)$ is 
\[
d(P,Q) = \n{ \operatorname{Log}(P^{-1}Q) }_F = 
\left( 
  \sum_{\lambda \in \spec P^{-1}Q} 
  \ln^2 \lambda
\right)^{1/2}
\]
(note that $P^{-1}Q$ is not symmetric, 
but $\spec P^{-1}Q = \spec Q^{1/2}P^{-1}Q^{1/2} 
\subset \RR^\times_+$).

The Riemannian mean of positive definite matrices 
$P_1,\dots,P_n$, defined as 
$P = \argmin_P \sum_k d(P,P_k)^2$,
satisfies 
$\sum_k \operatorname{Log}( P_k^{-1} P) = 0$; 
it is a generalization of the geometric mean.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiclass brain computer interface classification by Riemannian geometry} \\
    A. Barachant et al. (2012)
\end{book}

If your data consists of positive definite matrices,
rather than arbitrary vectors,
algorithms that only use the notions of distance and mean
(e.g., kNN, k-means, etc.)
are directly applicable 
using the Riemannian structure of $S_+(n)$.
For other algorithms, project the data on the tangent space
(at the identity, or any other point, 
if one makes better sense),
using the logarithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Classification of covariance matrices using a Riemannian-based kernel for BCI applications} \\
  A. Barachant et al. (2013)
\end{book}
More details; algorithm to compute the mean; 
application to support vector machines.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MEG decoding accross subjects} \\
  E. Olivetti et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Transductive transfer learning (TTL): 
\[
\hat \theta = 
\argmin_\theta
\dfrac1n
\sum_i
\dfrac{ P_{\text{target}}(x_i) }%
      { P_{\text{source}}(x_i) }
\text{loss}(\theta, x_i, y_i).
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Factorization machines} \\
  S. Rendle 
\end{book}
A \d{factorization machine} is a linear model 
with interactions,
\[ 
   y = \alpha + \sum_i \beta_i x_i + 
       \sum_{ij} \gamma_{ij} x_i x_j 
\]
where the interaction terms have low ramk
$\gamma_{ij} = \langle v_i, v_j \rangle,$, 
$v_i \in \RR^k$,
$k$ small
(you can add higher order interactions).
It is an alternative to the ``hash trick'' 
Vowpal Wabbit uses.
It works better than support vector machines for sparse data.
(Without $\alpha$ and $\beta$, 
it would just be a matrix factorization.)

For an implementation, check \texttt{libfm}.

% FM
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning recommender systems\\ with adaptive regularization} \\
  S. Rendle (2012)
\end{book}
Simultaneously nearn the model and the regularization parameter: 
\begin{align*}
\beta_{n+1} &= \argmin_\beta 
\text{Loss}( \text{training set}, \beta, \lambda_n ) \\
\lambda_{n+1} &= \argmin_\lambda 
\text{Loss}( \text{test set}, \beta_{n+1}, \lambda ).
\end{align*}
(Here, $\argmin$ does not have to be the minimizer: taking a few steps towards the minimum is sufficient.)

% FM
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scaling factorization machines\\ to relational data} \\
  S. Rendle (2013)
\end{book}
When fitting a model on relational data
(often, a star schema,
e.g., movie ratings, with 
movie characteristics (genre, year) 
and user characteristics (age, gender, friends)),
one typically joins (denormalizes) the data. 
this needlessly inflates the volume of data to process and the running time.
Learning on data in normal form 
is possible 
(process the fact table; 
update the weights of the rows on the dimension tables;
process the dimension tables).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tensor decompositions and applications} \\
  T.G. Kolda and B.W. Bader (2009)
\end{book}
There are two generalizations of PCA to tensors
(using Eistein's summation convention):
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{CP: }     & x_{ijk} = g^r a_{ir} b_{jr} c_{kr} \\
  \text{Tucker: } & x_{ijk} = g^{pqr} a_{ip} b_{jq} c_{kr} 
\end{array}
\]
The CP (canonical decomposition/parallel factors)
expresses the tensor $x$ as a sum of rank-1 tensors.
The \emph{rank} of $x$ is 
the minimal number of terms in such a sum.
Contrary to the notion of rank for matrices: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The ranks over $\RR$ and $\CC$ may be different;
\item Among tensors of a given shape, 
  the typical rank (any rank that occurs 
  on a set of non-zero measure)
  and the maximum rank can differ;
\item ``The'' typical rank is not unique;
\item The best rank-1 approximation need not be part 
  of a best rank-2 approximation; 
\item The best rank-$k$ approximation need not exist: 
  the set of rank-$k$ tensors is not closed, 
  \ie, some tensors can be approximated arbitrarily closely 
  by a tensor of smaller rank -- 
  the \emph{border rank} of $x$ is the minimum 
  number of rank-1 tensors needed to approximate $x$
  with an arbitrarily small error.
\end{compactitem}
The CP decomposition can be estimated using 
alternating least squares (ALS).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic principal component analysis} \\
  M.E. Tipping and C.M Bishop (1999)
\end{book}
PCA is a limiting case of (isotropic) factor analysis,
$X \sim N( \mu, WW' + \sigma^2 I)$,
when $\sigma^2 \to 0$ 
($\sigma^2$ is the average variance 
due to discarded components).
PPCA allows for missing values. 

In R, check the \texttt{pcaMethods} package 
(for probabilitic and bayesian PCA).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{NICE: non-linear\\ 
  independent component estimation} \\
  L. Dinh et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Given a random variable $X: \Omega \rightarrow \RR^n$, 
a non-linear independent component decomposition 
is a transformation $f : \RR^n \rightarrow \RR^n$
such that the density of $H=f(X)$ 
factorizes: 
$ p_H(h) = \prod_d p_{H_d} ( h_d ) $.

It can be estimated by maximum likelihood, 
$p_X(x) = p_H(f(x)) \va{ \det f' } $, 
\ie, 
\[
\argmax_{f, p_{H_1}, \dots, p_{H_n}}
\sum_x
\sum_d 
\log p_{H_d} f_d x + 
\log \va{ \det f' }.
\]

We look for a transformation 
(built from three layers of building blocks)
of the form 
\begin{align*}
  y_1 &= x_1 \\
  y_2 &= x_2 + m(x_1)
\end{align*}
which ensures that $\va{\det f'}$ 
and $f^{-1}$ are easy to compute.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse matrix factorization} \\
  B. Neyshabur and R. Panigrahy (2014)
\end{book}
The sparse matrix factorization problem 
aims to write a matrix $Y$
as a product of sparse matrices 
$Y = X_1 X_2 \cdots X_n$ -- 
PCA can be seen as a special case, 
where $X_1$ has few columns and $X_2$ few rows.

Assuming the matrices are square, random, centralized, normalized, and the coefficients of $Y$ are $0$, $1$ or $-1$,
$X_1 X_1' = \operatorname{round}( YY' )$
with high probability; 
$X_1$ can be recovered from $X_1 X_1'$,
and one can then iterate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Blind denoising with random greedy pursuits} \\
  M. Moussallam et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To find a sparse reconstruction of a time series\,$y$,
choose ``atoms'' (elementary time series
$\Phi = ( \phi_1 | \cdots | \phi_n )$
and fit $y = \Phi \alpha + w$, 
where $\alpha$ is sparse: 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & \alpha \\
  \text{To minimize} & \n{ \alpha }_0 \\
  \text{Such that} & \n{ y - \Phi \alpha } \leq \epsilon.
\end{array}
\]
One can use a greedy algorithm (matching pursuit, MP)
to find an approximate solution -- 
for the stopping criterion, look at 
\[ 
\max_\phi \dfrac
{ \va{ \langle y, \phi \rangle } }%
{ \n{ y } _2 \n{ \phi }_2 }.
\]
Instead of a single sparse representation,
one can compute several 
(as with random forests: at each step, 
choose a new atom in a random subset of $\Phi$)
and average them.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dimensionality reduction for $k$-means 
  clustering and low rank approximation} \\
  M. Cohen et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To speed up approximate $k$-means or PCA,
use a sketch of the data 
(row and column sampling, approximate SVD (CUR),
Johnson-Lindenstrauss projection).
% Bound on the errors.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Alternating least squares\\ 
  for personalized ranking}\\
  G. Tak\'acs and D. Tikk (2012) 
\end{book}
Alternating least squares (ALS) are often used 
to find $\hat R = P' Q$, 
with $P$ and $Q$ rectangular with a small number of columns,
to minimize 
\[
  \sum_{ u \in \text{Users} }
  \sum_{ i \in \text{Items} }
  c_{ui} ( \hat r_{ui} - r_{ui} ) ^2.
\]
It can also be used with a ranking objective 
\[
  \sum_{uij} c_{ui} s_j 
  \bigl( 
  ( \hat r_{ui} - \hat r_{uj} ) - ( r_{ui} - r_{uj} )
  \bigr)^2.
\]
% c is given, e.g., 0 or 1 depending on whether r_{ui} is known
% s has to be found.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HyperLogLog in practice:\\ algorithmic engineering of a state of the art\\ cardinality estimation algorithm} \\
  S. Heule et al. (2013)
\end{book}

HyperLogLog is biased for small counts: 
one can (empirically) estimate the bias
and correct it.
(HyperLogLog++ introduces a few more changes,
mostly to reduce memory usage.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{In-code computation\\ of geometric centralities with HyperBall:\\ hundred billion nodes and beyond} \\
  P. Boldi and S. Vigna
\end{book}

To count the number 
$\va{B(x,r)}$
of nodes in the ball of center $x$ and radius $r$
in a graph,
use a HyperLogLog counter $c_v$ for each node
and estimate $B(x,r+1)$ from $B(x,r)$.

This can be used to compute the \emph{harmonic centrality}
\[
\operatorname{centrality}(x) = 
\sum_y \dfrac1{d(x,y)} = 
\sum_{ r \geq 1 } \dfrac{\va{B(x,r)}}r.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The power of comparative reasoning} \\
  J. Yagnik et al.
\end{book}
The winner-takes-all (WTA) 
locality-sensitive hash (LSH) is 
\[
\left\{ 
\begin{array}{rcl}
\RR^n & \longrightarrow & \{\, 1, \dots, k \,\} \\
\mathbf x & \longmapsto & 
\smash{
\argmax\limits_{1 \leq j \leq k} x_{\sigma(j)}
}
\end{array}
\right.
\vrule width 0pt depth 4mm
\]
for a random permutation $\sigma \in \mathfrak S_n$.

By using several permultations $\sigma$ 
and choosing $k=2$,
we get a binary hash. 

One can apply a kernel before computing the hash.

