\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Practically accurate floating point math} \\
  N. Toronto and J. McCarthy (2014)
\end{book}
Measure the floating point error 
when approximating a real number $r$
with a floating point number $x$ as 
\[ 
  \operatorname{error}( x, r ) = 
  \dfrac{ x - r }%
        { \operatorname{ulp}( \operatorname{fl}(r) )}
\]
where
\begin{align*}
  \operatorname{ulp}(y) &= 
  \text{distance from $y$ to the next float
  %away from 0
  } \\
  \operatorname{fl}(r) &= \text{float closest to $r$} \\
  \operatorname{ord}(x) &= \text{(signed) number of floats between $0$ and $x$} \\
  \operatorname{ord}^{-1}(n) &\hphantom{{}={}} \text{(needed to compute ulp).}
\end{align*}
It is the number of floats between $x$ and $r$.

When implementing a numeric function, 
check where it is ill-conditionned (``the badlands''),
e.g., for functions $f : \RR \rightarrow \RR$, 
check where the condition number 
$ \va{ x f'(x) / f(x) } $ exceeds 4.
% Table of common functions with their badlands; 
% multiplication and atan have none.

The examples use typed/racket (for the plots as well) 
and MPFR.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast approximate nearest neighbors\\ with automatic algorithm configuration} \\
  M. Muja and D.G. Lowe
\end{book}
Comparison of randomized kd-trees and hierarchical $k$-means
for fast approximate nearest neighbours (FANN).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CRDTs: consistency\\ without concurrency control} \\
  M. Le\c tia et al. (2009)
\end{book}
CRDTs are data structures whose operations commute:
they can be used in a distributed environment.
For instance: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A set with a single add-element operation;
\item A set with add and delete operations, 
  if no element is ever added twice:
  keep two lists, one for added elements, 
  one for deleted elements,
  and clean them from time to time;
\item An ordered set, with insert-at and delete operations:
  use a dense index space (not $\RR$, 
  but paths in a binary tree).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A comprehensive study of convergent\\
   and commutative replicated data types} \\
  M. Shapiro et al. (2011)
\end{book}
Eventual consistency, 
in a distributed/asynchronous environment,
can be guaranteed by the data structures themselves,
without synchronization. For instance: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Add-only set;
\item Distributed maximum (when the maximum changes,
  broadcast it to the other replicas);
\item Counter: broadcast $+1$ or $-1$ -- the reception order is irrelevant;
\item Register: store value-timestamp pairs 
  and only keep the value with the latest timestamp; 
\item Set with add and remove operations, 
  if removed elements cannot be added back: 
  use two add-only sets, 
  for elements added and elements removed, 
  and occasionally garbage-collect them;
\item Set with add and remove operations, 
  in which add has priority over remove 
  in case of a conflict (e.g., shopping cart):
  when adding an element, 
  also store a unique identifier 
  (a new one, if the element was already there);
  remove removes the elements with the identifiers 
  known to the replica that received the order;  
\item Graphs: like sets;
\item Directed acyclic graphs: that is a global property; 
  it cannot be ensured in general without synchronization.
\item Partial order: like sets;
\item Total order (e.g., collaborative editing): 
  either add an addRight(position,content) operation,
  which stores the content-timestamp pair in a linked list
  or an addBetween operation, 
  for which the total order is defined 
  by a dense set of identifiers 
  (trees: Logoot or Treedoc -- 
  but they have to be rebalanced (garbaged-collected) 
  from time to time).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generation and analysis of constrained random sampling patterns} \\
  J. Pierzchlewski and T. Arildsen
\end{book}
Random sampling of analog (1-dimensional) 
signals is preferable 
to regular sampling.
Simple sampling patterns include: 
a regular grid with noise, 
or adding noise to the previous point.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The algorithmic foundations\\ 
  of differential privacy} \\
  C. Dwork and A. Roth (2014)
\end{book}
To ensure plausible deniability,
use randomized algorithms
(e.g., answer truthfully with probaility $\tfrac12$, 
answer randomly otherwise -- for real-valued functions, perturb the result with the Laplace distribution).
A randomized algorithm $\mathscr M$ 
is differentially private if 
\[
  \n{ x - y } \leq 1 \implies
  P[ \mathscr M(x) \in S ] \leq
  e^\epsilon P[ \mathscr M(y) \in S ] + \delta .
\]
The privacy loss is 
$\ln \dfrac
  { P[ \mathscr M (x) = \xi ] }%
  { P[ \mathscr M (y) = \xi ] }.
$  

When answering several queries, 
the system should refuse to answer similar ones 
(e.g., the number of people with disease D, 
and the number of people not named X with disease D).

% Not read completely

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Computing arbitrary functions\\ of encrypted data} \\
  C. Gentry (2008)
\end{book}
The following encryption scheme, 
which adds noise to the cleartext,
is somewhat homomorphic -- 
the noise progressively increases and eventually
becomes too large.
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{cleartext:} & m \in \{ 0, 1 \} \\
  \text{key:} & p \in \{0,1\}^P, \  
                p \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod}\ 2) \\
  \text{ciphertext:} & c = m' + pq \\
  \text{where} & m' \in \{0,1\}^N, \ 
                 m' \equiv m \  ( \text{mod} \ 2 ) \\ 
               & q \in \{0,1\}^Q \\
  \rlap{\text{somewhat preserved: $+$, $-$, $\times$}}
\end{array}
\]

To make it homomorphic, 
try to find functions $f_{i \to i+1}$
that transform 
$\operatorname{Encrypt}(p_i,m)$ to 
$\operatorname{Encrypt}(p_{i+1},m)$
(\ie, that change the key and reset the noise),
using the somewhat preserved operations
(this is possible after a small change 
in the encryption scheme).
This is slow, there is no division, 
and no inequalities.

