When discretizing data, use $\sqrt n$ 
quantile bins,
but make sure each bin has at least 30 observations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Winograd schema challenge} \\
  H.J. Levesque et al. (2012)
\end{book}
A replacement for captchas (or the Turing test):
resolve referential ambiguity 
(e.g., ``the trophy does not fit in the suitcase 
because it is too big'', 
what is too big: the trophy or the suitcase?).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sloane's gap:\\ do mathematical and social factors explain\\ the distribution of numbers in the OEIS?} \\
  N. Gauvrit et al. (2011)
\end{book}
Plotting the number of occurrences of an integer 
in the OEIS 
shows two clouds, 
with a similar exponential decay, and a gap inbetween.
Computational complexity explains the decay, 
but not the gap -- it could be due to social factors. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Profiling R on a contemporary processor} \\
  S. Sridharan and J.M. Patel (2014) % VLDB41
\end{book}
R is slow because of cache misses, caused by: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pointer-chasing the the garbage collector (GC);
\item Linear algebra operations: 
  R should provide both row- and column-major storage
  and/or use blocking;
\item Custom C code in packages (e.g., \texttt{rpart}): 
  it should be cache-conscious.
\end{compactitem}
The GC problems are made worse by:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Unnecessary attributes 
  (e.g., row names, which are automatically added);
\item Unnecessary copies; 
  intermediate object creation 
  (arithmetic operations on vectors or matrices);
\item Inefficient implementations (e.g., subsetting
  with a boolean vector recomputes 
  the row indices for each column).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convex optimization in Julia} \\
  M. Udell et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The \texttt{Convex.jl} package 
brings disciplined convex programming (DCP) to Julia.

Besides the positive orthant, 
the second order cone,
and the semi-definite cone, 
note the \emph{exponential cone},
\[
  \{\, 
  (x,y,z) \in \RR^3 \ :\  
  y \geq 0, \quad 
  y   e^{x/y} \leq z 
  \, \},
\]
used for the convex functions 
$\exp(x)$, $-\log(x)$, $\log \sum_i \exp x_i$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Computing in operations research using Julia} \\
  M. Lubin and I. Dunning (2013)
\end{book}
\texttt{JuMP.jl} relies on macros 
rather than operator overloading
(as in CVX, \texttt{Convex.jl}, PuPL, Pyomo, etc.)
and can therefore efficiently build larger problems.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A review of goal programming\\ for portfolio selection} \\
  R. Azmi and M. Tamiz (2010)
\end{book}
``Goal programming'' is a vague notion that refers 
to the various ways of selecting a single solution
of a multi-objective optimization problem, 
``find $x$ to minimize $g_1(x),\dots,g_n(x)$''
somewhere on the efficient frontier. 
For instance: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Minimize $\sum_i \lambda_i g_i(x)$;
\item Minimize $\max_i g_i(x)$;
\item Minimize $g_1(x)$; among the solutions, minimize $g_2$;
  continue until there is only one solution left;
\item etc.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio selection with higher moments:\\ a polynomial goal programming\\ approach to ISE30 index} \\
  G. Kemalbay et al. (2011)
\end{book}
% empty but clear

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Indicator-based selection\\
  in multiobjective search} \\
  E. Zitzler and S. K\"unzli (2004)
\end{book}
Multiobjective search tries to find 
a good approximation of the Pareto set -- 
a good solution is close to the Pareto set and diversified.
These notions are rarely clearly defined,
but could be formalized, e.g., 
one could try to maximize
the volume dominated by the solution.
Here is one algorithm: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a large candidate set;
\item Compute the ``fitness'' of each point in it 
  (relative to the others);
\item Remove the worst point; update the fitness 
  of the others;
\item Iterate (until some termination criterion is met, 
  e.g., the population size).
\end{compactitem}
The \emph{indicator-based evolutionary algorithm} (IBEA) 
defines the fitness of a point $a \in A$ as 
\[
  F(a) = \sum_{ b \in A \setminus \{ a \} }
  - \exp -I( \{a\}, \{b\} )  / \kappa
\]
where $\kappa$ is a parameter (the temperature)
and $I$ is a \emph{binary quality indicator}, e.g., 
\begin{align*}
  I(A,B) = \min 
  \bigl\{ \,
  \epsilon &\ :\ 
  \forall\, b \in B \quad
  \exists\, a \in A \quad
  \exists\, i \in \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket \\
  & \hphantom{{}\ :\ {}} 
  f_i(a) - \epsilon \leq f_i(b)
  \,\bigr\}
\end{align*}
or 
\[
I(A,B) = \left\{
\begin{array}{l}
I(B) - I(A)  \quad \text{if } 
  \forall\, b \in B \ 
  \exists\, a \in A \ 
  a \succ b \\
I(A\union B) - I(A)  \quad \text{ otherwise}  
\end{array}
\right.
\]
where $I(A)$ is the volume 
%of the space 
dominated by $A$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse and stable Markowitz portfolios} \\
  J. Brodie et al. (2008)
