For sparse portfolios, 
add an $L^1$ penalty to the portfolio optimization problem.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse portfolio selection\\ 
  via quasi-norm regularization} \\
  C. Chen et al. (2013)
\end{book}

For sparse portfolios, add an $L^p$ penalty ($0<p<1$)
to the portfolio optimization problem; 
it is no longer convex, but can still be solved 
in polynomial time via interior point methods.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal risk budgeting\\ 
  under a finite investment horizon} \\
  M.\,L\'opez de prado, R.\,Vince and Q.\,Zhu (2013)
\end{book}
% Interesting but very confusing.
To decide which proportion $x_i$ 
of one's wealth to invest in asset $i$,
one can use 
$ \hat x = \argmax _x f_{Q,U}(x) $,
where $f_{Q,U}(x)$ is the expected utility 
for utility function $U$ after $Q$ periods. 
The Kelly principle is 
$\hat x = \argmax _x f_{\infty, \text{linear}} (x)$.
The \emph{leverage space} seems to be the graph of 
$x \mapsto f_{Q, \text{linear}} (x) $.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A factor model for non-linear dependences\\ in stock returns} \\
  R. Chicheportiche and J.P. Bouchaud (2013)
\end{book}
The \emph{non-linear correlation}
between two random variables $X$ and $Y$
generalizes $\cor(\va X, \va Y)$ and $\cor(X^2,Y^2)$:  

\[ 
C_p(X,Y) = \dfrac1{p^2} \log \dfrac{
  E\bigl[ \va{ XY } ^ p \bigr]
}{
  E\bigl[ \va{ X } ^ p \bigr]
  E\bigl[ \va{ Y } ^ p \bigr]
}.
\]

In the standard factor model 
$x_i = \sum_k \beta_{ki} f_k + e_i$,
the non-linear correlations
$C_p(f_k,f_\ell)$,
$C_p(f_k,e_i)$
$C_p(e_i,e_j)$
are non-zero.
The nested factor model, 
for the volatilities of the factors $f$ 
and the residuals $e$,
with two factors, $\Omega_0$ and $\Omega_1$,
can account for them: 
\begin{align*}
f_k &= \epsilon_k \exp[ A_{k0} \Omega_0 + A_{k1} \Omega_1 + \omega_k ] \\
e_j &= \eta_j \exp[ B_{j0} \Omega_0 + B_{j1} \Omega_1 + \varpi_k ].
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Commodity futures and market efficiency} \\
  L. Kristoufek and M. Vosvrda (2013)
\end{book}

To measure the efficiency of a given market (or asset), 
average the following measures:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Hurst exponent (long-term efficiency):
  local Whittle estimator, GPH estimator;
\item Fractal dimension (short-term): 
  Hall-Wood estimator, Genton estimator;
\item Entropy: Pincus approximate entropy.
\end{compactitem}
The article also gives a concise review of those estimators.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Implied Hurst exponent and fractional implied volatility: a variance term structure model} \\
  K.Q. Li and R. Chen (2014)
\end{book}

One can compute the Hurst exponent from option prices: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Using the fractional Black-Scholes formula; 
  a single option does not give $(\sigma,H)$, 
  but just $V=\sigma^2T^{2H}$: 
  use several maturities and regress $ \log V \sim \log T $;
\item  Using a model-free approach, 
  very similar to the VIX computations, 
  to compute $V$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting the NOK/USD echange rate\\
   with machine learning techniques} \\
  T. Papadimitriou et al.
\end{book}

One can forecast the exchange rate 
\begin{align*}
  \text{FX}_{t+1} & \sim \text{FX}_t
  + ( M_2 - M_2^{\text{US}} ) 
  + ( \text{GDP} - \text{GDP}^{\text{US}} ) + {}\\
  & \hphantom{{}\sim{}} 
  ( \text{IR} - \text{IR}^{\text{US}} ) 
  + ( \text{Inflation} - \text{Inflation}^{\text{US}} ) +{}\\
  & \hphantom{{}\sim{}} 
  \text{Oil} + t,
\end{align*}
where the inflation is a forecast from an ARMA model 
or from the forward rate,
using support vector regression 
\begin{align*}
  \hat \beta &= \argmin_\beta \tfrac12 \n \beta ^2 + C \sum_i \phi( y_i - \beta' x_i ) \\
  \phi &= 
  \raisebox{-.5mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.15,yscale=.15]
    \draw (-3,0) -- (3,0);
    \draw (0,-1) -- (0,2);
    \draw[very thick,] (-2.9,1.9) -- (-1,0) -- (1,0) -- (2.9,1.9);
  \end{tikzpicture}}
\end{align*}
(with the kernel trick).
The resulting model can be made more interpretable 
using a ``dynamic evolution neuro-fuzzy inference system''
(\textsc{denfis}), \ie, a set of fuzzy rules of the form 
``Rule $m$: 
if $x_1$ is $R_{m1}$ and $\ldots$ and $x_n$ is $R_{mn}$
then $y = \sum_i \beta_{mi} x_i$'',
where $R_{m1}$ is a Gaussian membership function 
\[ R_{m1} \propto \exp 
  -\dfrac{(x-c)^2}{2\sigma^2}.
\]

\nextbook
