\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Do social firms catch the drift?\\
   Social media and earnings news} \\
  V. Bhagwat and T.R. Burch (2013)
\end{book}
Twitter firms (more than 0.1 tweet per day, on average, 
since the creation of the twitter account -- 
the mapping from firm to twitter account was done manually)
have stronger post-earnings announcement drift (PEAD).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A general option valuation approach\\
   to discount for lack of marketability} \\
  R. Brooks (2014)
\end{book}
To price a non-marketable asset, 
one can model the price 
of the corresponding marketable asset 
as the sum of the unmarketable price and an option, 
e.g., an option to sell the asset 
at the current market price, 
or at the best price in the period 
(if the investor has market timing abilities)
or at the average price in the period
(European put, lookback put, average-strike put).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock market ambiguity\\ and the equity premium} \\
  P.C. Andreou et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Ambiguity (uncertainty about 
the probability distribution
of future assets)
can be measured as the dispersion 
(standard deviation or mean absolute deviation)
of the volume-weighted strike prices of 
S\&P 500 index options.
It is a better predictor of future returns 
than other ambiguity proxies 
(analysts' forecasts, stock turnover, press)
or other option-implied measures 
(slope of the volatility smirk, risk-neutral variance,
skewness or kurtosis,
hedging pressure, \ie, OTM puts / ATM calls),
and almost as good as the variance risk premium,
($\text{VRP} = \var^\QQ\! X_t - \var ^\PP\! X_t$,
where $\var^\QQ\! X_t$ is computed from option prices 
and $\var^\PP\! X_t = \var^{\text{realized}} X_{t-1}$).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Credit risk models II: structural models} \\
  A. Elizalde (2005)
\end{book}
Structural default models model 
the value of the assets of a company 
as a geometric Brownian motion;
a default occurs when the value of the assets 
is below some threshold
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item at the end of the period;
\item at any point during the period;
\item or on some sufficiently long subinterval 
of the forecast period.
\end{compactitem}
These correspond to the Merton model (European options), 
first passage models (FPM, barrier options)
and liquidation process models.
Those models do not work well.

To account for correlation and contagion, 
one can movel the value of the assets (of several companies)
as diffusions with correlated innocations 
and correlated jumps. 


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Asymmetric dependence, tail dependence\\ and the time interval\\ over which the variables are measured} \\
  B.U. Kang and G. Kim (2014)
\end{book}
Look at the asymptotic behaviour of the $n$-period copula:
\begin{align*}
  \va{ \lambda^{(n)}(q) - \lambda^{(n)}(1-q) } & \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} 0 \\
  \lambda_U^{(n)}, \lambda_L^{(n)} & \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} 0
\end{align*}
where $\lambda^{(n)}(q)$ is the $n$-period quantile
and $\lambda_U^{(n)}$, $\lambda_L^{(n)}$ the upper and lower
tail dependence indices.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock and index derivatives and markets} \\
  J. Spina (2014)
\end{book}
% Teaching material?
The main option strategies are: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Dividend spread: profit from investors 
  that fail from exercising their American options in time;
\item If you own stocks of your own firm, 
  want to keep the dividends and voting rights, 
  but want some protection: buy OTM puts
  (you may also want to sell OTM calls to finance the puts);
\item Variable prepaid forward
  (sell the stock you own in the future, 
  but get the money now);
\item  If you want to bet on the performance of a stock
 (e.g., your firm), use an option collar, 
 sell ATM puts, buy ATM calls.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Relative alpha} \\
  J.C. Jackwerth and A. Slavutskaya 
\end{book}
The relative alpha of a hedge fund is 
the excess performance wrt its peers, 
where the peers are funds for which 
the return difference has a small variance. 
More formally,
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_i &= \sum_{j \neq i} w_{ij} E[ r_i - r_j] \\
  w_{ij} & \propto k\bigl( h^{-1} \var[ r_i - r_j] \bigr) \\
  k &: \text{ kernel} \\
  h &: \text{ bandwidth (Silverman's rule of thumb).}
\end{align*}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Factor high-frequency based volatility models} \\
  K. Sheppard and W. Xu (2014)
\end{book}
Factor model to estimate large realized covariance matrices.
% Not unlike $\beta$-GARCH

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A multivariate model\\ 
  of strategic asset allocation with longevity risk} \\
  E. Bisetti et al. (2014)
\end{book}
If you use reinsurance as a cheap source of leverage
(like Berkshire),
do not forget to account for longevity risk -- 
it is negligible for short-term investors, 
but significant for long-term ones.
% risk term structure 


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Consumption-based asset pricing\\ 
  with rare disaster risk} \\
  J. Grammig and J. S\"onksen (2014)
\end{book}
High equity risk premium may be due to fears of rare disasters.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal hedging\\ 
  with the vector autoregressive model} \\
  L.T. Gatarek and S. Johansen (2014)
\end{book}
With several assets (3+) and cointegration relations (2+),
use portfolio construction tools
(e.g., minimum variance)
on the set of stationary portfolios.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Classifying as defensive or cyclical\\a bivariate wavelet analysis perspective} \\
  J. Bruzda 
\end{book}
Given two signals $x$ and $y$, 
compute the non-decimated Hilbert coefficients $u$ and $v$
and define 
\begin{align*}
  \cov_j( x_t, y_{t+\tau} ) &= u_{jt} \bar v_{j,t+\tau} \\
  \var _j x_t &= \cov_j( x_t, x_t ) \\
  \re \cov_j(x_t,y_t) &: \text{ wavelet cospectrum} \\
  \im \cov_j(x_t,y_t) &: \text{ wavelet quadrature spectrum} \\ 
   \cor_j(x_t,y_t) &= \dfrac{\cov_j(x_t,y_t)}{\sqrt{\var_j x_t \var_j y_t}} \\
   \va{\cor_j(x_t,y_t)}^2 &:\text{ wavelet coherence} \\
   \va{\cor_j(x_t,y_t)} &:\text{ wavelet coherency} \\
   \theta_j( x_t, y_t) &= \arg \cov_j ( x_t, y_y )
     \text{\ \ \ phase spectrum} \\
   \tau_j(x_t,y_t) &= - \theta_j(x_t,y_t) \cdot 
   \dfrac{ 2^{j+2} }{ 6 \pi } \text{\ \ \ time delay} \\
   \beta_j( x_t,y_t) &= \dfrac{\cov_j(x_t,y_t)}{\var_j x_t} \\
   \va{ \beta_j(x_t,y_t) } &:\text{ wavelet gain}.
\end{align*}
In R, check \texttt{waveslim::modwt.hilbert(x,"k3l3",4)}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimality of momentum and reversal} \\
  X.Z. He et al. (2014)
\end{book}
One can add momentum and reversal to a geometric Brownian motion
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{dS}{S} &= \bigl[ \phi m_t + ( 1 - \phi) \mu_t \bigr] dt + \rlap{$\sigma_S d Z_S$} \\
  d \mu_t &= \alpha ( \bar \mu - \mu_t ) dt + \sigma_\mu d Z_\mu  && \text{Ornstein-Uhlenbeck} \\
  m_t &= \dfrac1\tau \int_{t-\tau} ^t \dfrac{dS_u}{S_u}
  && \text{Momentum}
\end{align*}
and compute the optimal strategy, 
for a 2-asset universe (stock and bond)
and an investor maximizing 
the expected utility of final wealth.
The model can be fitted to historical data 
(S\&P\,500, monthly, 1 century).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Discounting the distant future} \\
  J.D. Farmer et al. (2014)
\end{book}
If the interest rate (is non-constant and) follows an
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process
$ dr = - \alpha( r - m ) dt + k dW $,
the discount factor 
$ D(t) = E [ \exp - \int _0 ^t r(s) ds ] $
satisfies
$ \log D(t) \sim_{t\to\infty} ( m - k^2 / 2 \alpha^2 ) t, $
in particular, the long-term interest rate 
$ r_\infty = m - k^2 / 2\alpha^2 $
is not the long-term average of the interest rate.
The interest rate distribution, 
$r \sim N( m, k^2 / 2\alpha )$, 
does not determine $r_\infty$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Independence of stock markets before\\
   and after the global financial crisis of 2007} \\
  B.M. Ibrahim and J. Brzeszczy\'nski (2014)
\end{book}
To measure the impact of a variable $z$ on a regression $y \sim x$,
where $x$, $y$, $z$ are time series,
assume that the coefficients of the regression 
are AR(1) processes whose coefficients depend on $Z$,
\begin{align*}
  y_t &= ( \alpha + \epsilon_t ) + ( \beta + \eta_t ) x_t + u_t \\
  \epsilon_{t+1} &= ( a + b z_t ) \epsilon_t + v_{t+1} \\
  \eta_{t+1} &= ( c + d z_t ) \eta_t + w_{t+1}
\end{align*}
For instance, $x$ and $y$ could be 
the returns in two markets 
and $z$ the difference in turnover 
of volatility between those two markets.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An empirical investigation\\
   of methods to reduce transaction costs} \\
  T. Moorman (2014)
\end{book}
To reduce transaction costs, 
define a no-trade zone around the optimal portfolio, 
using some measure of distance 
(Euclidian, cosine, etc.) % long list (37)
and an ``optimal'' threshold. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal dependency in extreme returns} \\
  K. Echaust
\end{book}
There is more long memory in return tails:
look at the cross-correlation of block maxima and minima.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Option pricing\\ with the logistic return distribution} \\
  M. Levy and H. Levy
\end{book}
Black Scholes option pricing formulas 
can be generalized to the logistic distribution 
(the distribution whose cdf is the logistic function).
A possible justification is that 
while aggregated log-returns are asymptotically Gaussian,
normal market conditions are still far away from this limit.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting crashes:
  correlated fund flows\\ 
  and the skewness in stock returns} \\
  X. Gong et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Correlated demand for liquidity (from mutual funds)
explains the negative skewness of stock returns: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use mutual fund holdings to estimate how much 
  of the trading of a fund is due to liquidity shocks 
  (inflows and outflows);
\item Assume that the stock returns are linearly related 
  to those liquidity shocks; 
\item Estimate the variance and skewness of the returns, 
  conditional on the liquidity shocks; 
\item Decompose it into contributions of the variance,
  covariance, skewness, coskewness of the liquidity shocks.
\end{compactitem}
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-linear forecasting of energy futures:\\ oil, coal and natural gas} \\
  G.G. Creamer (2014)
\end{book}
Use Brownian distance correlation 
\begin{align*}
  \nu(X,Y) &= \n{ f_X f_Y - f_{XY} } \\
  c(X,Y) &= \dfrac{\nu(X,Y)}{\sqrt{\nu(X)\nu(Y)}}
\end{align*}
as an alternative to Granger causality,
and for feature selection
(to build leading indicators).
% Also mentions, confusingly, random forests, 
% cointegration, Bai-Perron test for structural breaks, etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Agent-based models of financial markets} \\
  E. Samanidou et al. (2007)
\end{book}
Clear review of a few agent-based models of financial markets: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Rebalancers 
  (targeting $w_{\text{cash}} = 
  w_{\text{stock}} = \tfrac12$)
  and CPPI investors,
  reviewing their portfolios (and trading) at random times,
  each agent estimating the ``fair price'' 
  from the order book 
  to make her decision,
  with random deposits and withdrawals;
\item Traders with a noisy log-utility
  (so that the optimal weight of stock and bond 
  is independent of wealth, up to noise), 
  assuming the forward returns will be 
  one of the past $k$ returns 
  (the look-back $k$ is investor-dependent);
\item Gamblers, 
$\text{wealth}_{i,t+1} = \lambda \times \text{wealth}_{it}$,
with $\lambda$ random around 1,
and a welfare state ensuring 
$\text{wealth}_{i,t} \geq q \times \text{average wealth}$;
\item \emph{Percolation}: 
  traders are the occupied sites on a lattice of dimension 2 to 7,
  clusters are groups of traders making the same decision (herding), 
  $P[\text{buy}] = P[\text{sell}] = a$, 
  $P[\text{sleep}] = 1 - 2a$, 
  $\Delta \log \text{price} \propto \text{supply} - \text{demand}$;
  this gives the correct stylized facts, 
  including log-periodic oscillations 
  (if you add some rationality and hysteria);
\item The supply and demand balance 
  between noise traders and fundamentalists 
  drives price changes,
  and each trader reviews her beliefs 
  based on those price changes. 
\end{compactitem}
Only the last two models give credible prices.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Electricity price forecasting: a review of the state-of-the-art with a look into the future} \\
  R. Weron (2014)
\end{book}
Review of many methods to forecast electricity prices: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Agent models (Nash equilibrium, 
  multi-agent simulations);
\item Jump diffusion model,
  Markov-switching model;
\item Exponential smoothing, regression, 
  AR, SARMA, ARX, TAR, GARCH, etc.;
\item Neural nets (MLP, recurrent, fuzzy), SVM.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial sector tail risk and real economic activity: evidence from the option market} \\
  M. Neumann
\end{book}
An option-implied measure of tail risk can be computed 
from the price of deep OTM put options on
a financial index 
(cheaper than it should be because of 
(implicit) government guarantee) 
and the constituents of this index. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Q-learning-based financial trading systems with applications} \\
  M. Corazza and F. Bertoluzzo (2014)
\end{book}
Use reinforcement learning (e.g., Q-learning)
to design your automated trading systems.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{TrueSkill\texttrademark: 
  a Bayesian skill rating system} \\
  R. Herbrich et al. 
\end{book}
Generalization of the (already Bayesian) Elo system.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Intrinsic ratings compendium} \\
  K.W. Regan (2012)
\end{book}
To estimate Elo ratings from moves 
rather than game outcomes (there are more of them): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the value of the position before and after each move (with a chess program);
\item Assume 
  $
  P[ \text{move} ] \propto 
  p_0^{ \alpha( \text{value}, \Delta \text{value} ) },
  $
  where $\alpha$ is a function to estimate;
\item Discard openings (first 8 moves), 
  repeated positions, 
  clearly advantageous positions;
\item Estimate the Elo rating from $\alpha$.
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A different angle on fitting ROC curves to rating data using the first principal component} \\
  J.R. Vokey (2014)
\end{book}
To estimate a ROC curve from a few points,
convert the $p$-values to a Gaussian
(\texttt{qnorm} -- the points are likely to be aligned)
and take the first principal component (rather than a regression line).
% R code provided.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{From Archimedian to Liouville copulas} \\
  A.J. McNeil and J. Ne\v slehov\'a 
\end{book}

Archimedian copulas are copulas of 
$d$-dimensional $\ell_1$-norm symmetric distributions, 
\ie, copulas of random variables $X = R S_d$, 
where $S_d$ is uniform on the unit simplex 
$\Delta_d = \{\, x \in \RR_+^d \,:\, \sum x_i = 1 \,\} $
and $R$ is an independent nonnegative random variable.
% One can easily define new archimedian copula families.
Replacing the uniform distribution on $\Delta_d$ 
with a Dirichlet distribution, 
$ P(x_1,\dots,x_n) \propto \prod x_i ^ { \alpha_i - 1 }$
gives the (non-exchangeable) 
\emph{Liouville copulas}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to state space models} \\
  M. Wildi (2013)
\end{book}
Detailed presentation of state space models 
and the Kalman filter in R, with
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Detailed explanations of the formulas 
(the Kalman filter is a sequence of Bayesian updates -- 
in particular, a prior is needed);
\item The various objective functions for model estimation 
  (look at the out-of-sample residuals 
  $y_t = \hat y_{t|t-1}$,
  or the in-sample ones,
  $y_t = \hat y_{t|t}$,
  and minimize their sum of squares or their likelihood -- 
  prefer the out-of-sample maximum likelihood estimator);
\item A few examples: 
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Regression, where the coefficients 
    are the hidden state, and the covariates form the 
    (time-changing) observation matrix;
  \item Intervention studies;
  \item Missing values, dealt with 
    by skipping the observation 
    $\hat\xi_{t|t} \leftarrow \hat\xi_{t|t-1}$;
  \item Time-changing AR(1) process;
  \item Decomposition into trend and SARMA components.
  \item Smoothing was not detailed -- 
    there are many applications in signal processing,
    but few/none in time series analysis.
  \end{compactitem}
\end{compactitem}
In R, check the 
\texttt{dlm}, 
\texttt{KFAS},
\texttt{dlmodeler} packages.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parallel Markov chain Monte Carlo} \\
  D.N. VanDerwerken and S.C. Schmidler (2013)
\end{book}
Use importance sampling weights to merge parallel MCMC
simulations and get a consistent estimator much earlier,
even in case of slow mixing 
(e.g., multimodal or highly correlated distributions).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Testing the equality\\ of two positive definite matrices\\ with applications to information matrix testing} \\
  J.S. Cho and H. White (2014)
\end{book}
Build a test for equality of positive definite matrices
using
\[ A = B \iff (\det D) ^{1/k} = \dfrac1k \tr D = 1, \]
where $D = B A^{-1}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Birds of a feather flock together\\ Local learning of mid-level representations\\ for fine-grained recognition} \\
  A. Freytag et al. 
\end{book}
\emph{Local learning} is similar to local regression, 
but for arbitrary machine learning algorithms: 
given a new observation to classify, 
find the $k$-nearest ones ($k \gg 1$),
train a model on them, and use it.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate Weibull distributions\\ 
  for asset returns I} \\
  Y. Malevergne and D. Sornette (2004)
\end{book}
Asset returns $X$ can be described 
by a modified Weibull distribution,
\[
  p(x) \propto
  \va{ x } ^ { c / 2 - 1 } 
  \exp - \left( \dfrac{ \va x }{ \sigma } \right)^c
\]
(perhaps with a different exponent $c$ for $x>0$ and $x<0$),
\ie, some power of the returns, 
$\operatorname{sign}(X) \va{X} ^\alpha$,
is Gaussian.
For the correlation structure, use a Gaussian copula.
% The second part of this paper computes the moments
% and cummulants of $\sum w_i X_i$ 
% where $\mathbf X$ has generalized Weibull margins 
% and a Gaussian copula.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tweedie family densities:\\ methods of evaluation} \\
  P.K. Dunn and G.K. Smyth
\end{book}
The Tweedie distribution
is an exponential dispersion model
in which the variance is some power of the expectation 
\begin{align*}
  f(y) &= a(y,\phi) \exp 
  \dfrac{y \theta - \kappa(\theta)}\phi \\
  \var y &\propto E[Y]^p.
\end{align*}
Special cases include Gaussian ($p=0$), 
Poisson ($p=1$), 
Gamma ($p=2$)
and inverse Gaussian ($p=3$).
The pdf can be approximated 
by inverting the cummulant generating function 
(it has a simple form) or by an infinite series.

In R, check the \texttt{cplm} and \texttt{tweedie} packages.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Series evaluation of Tweedie exponential dispersion model densities} \\
  P.K. Dunn and G.K. Smyth (2005)
\end{book}
If $Y \sim \text{ED}_p(\mu,\phi)$ 
follows a Tweedie distribution
with $0<p<1$,
it can be written 
$Y = X_1 + \cdots + X_N$, 
with $X_i \sim \Gamma$, $X_i$ iid, $N \sim \text{Poisson}$.
When $p$ is close to 1, 
the distribution is multimodal 
($p=1$ gives the Poisson distribution, which is discrete).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model identification\\ using stochastic differential equation\\ grey-box models in diabetes} \\
  A.K. Duun-Henriksen et al. (2013)
\end{book}
A white-box model is a system of ODEs 
(here, phamaco-kinetics) based on domain knowledge.
A grey-box model is a noisy system of SDEs; 
it mixes domain knowledge and data.
A black-box model is entirely data-driven.

For grey-box models in R, 
check the \texttt{ctsm} package
(SDEs with noisy measurements at discrete times, 
\ie, non-linear Kalman filter).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Approximate bayesian inference\\ for latent Gaussian models by using\\ integrated nested Laplace approximations} \\
  H. Rue et al. (2009)
\end{book}
% Very technical
One can use \emph{nested} Laplace approximations 
(asymptotic expansion of $\int_a^b e^{M f(x)} dx$, 
when $M \to \infty$,
if $f$ has a unique maximum $x_0$, 
using a Taylor expansion of $f$ around $x_0$)
to estimate posterior probabilities 
in latent Gaussian models.

In R, check the \texttt{INLA} package (not on CRAN).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-linear causal inference\\ using Gaussianity measures} \\
  D. Hern\'andez-Lobato et al. (2014)
\end{book}
If $x$ causes $y$
(through a linear model with non-Gaussian noise),
the residuals of $y\sim x$ are less Gaussian 
than those of $x \sim y$.
% Proof: relation between the cumulants of the residuals in both directions.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ranking the economic importance\\ 
  of countries and industries} \\
  W. Li et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Country- and sector-level imports and exports 
(world input-output data,
\texttt{www.wiod.org})
defines a (weighted, directed) graph,
amenable to \emph{cascading failure tolerance analysis}: 
check what happens when a node fails -- 
other nodes fail if their revenue drops 
by a fraction greater than $p \in [0,1]$.
The critical value $p_c$ beyond which most nodes fail 
measures the centrality of the first failing node.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Interdependencies and causalities\\ in coupled financial networks} \\
  I. Vodenska et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To identify lead-lag relations from log-return time series,
  complexify the signals with a Hilbert transform; 
  compute the ``complex correlation matrix'';
  build a directed network using the sign 
    of the phase of the  correlation 
    to infer the direction of the edges.
One can also look at the principal components (complex PCA) 
and use random matrix theory 
(or its resampling-based equivalent, 
rotational random shuffling, RRS).    
\iffalse
library(quantmod)
library(hht)
library(stringr)
library(igraph)
# Sample data: log-returns, with no missing values
e <- new.env()
getSymbols( c("^GSPC", "^N225", "RHT", "MSFT"), env = e )
d <- as.data.frame( log1p( ROC( do.call( cbind, eapply( e, Ad ) ) ) ) )
d <- d[ complete.cases(d), ]
colnames(d) <- str_replace( colnames(d), "[.].*", "" )
# Use the Hilbert transform to get a complex signal
d <- apply( d, 2, HilbertTransform )
# Compute the "complex correlation"
d <- t( t(d) - colMeans(d) )
d <- t( t(d) / sqrt( colMeans( Mod(d)^2 ) ) )
C <- t(d) %*% Conj(d) / nrow(d)
# Directed graph
plot( graph.adjacency( sign( Arg(C) ) ) )
plot( graph.adjacency( ( Mod(C) > .3 ) * sign( Arg(C) ) ) )
# Look at the first principal component
z <- eigen(C)$vectors[,1]
z <- z * exp(- 1i * Arg(z[1]))    # Rotate, so that Arg( S&P500 ) = 0
lim <- range(c(  Re(z), Im(z) ))
lim <- range(c( lim, -lim ))
plot( Re(z), Im(z), xlim=lim, ylim=lim, pch=16  )
abline( h=0, v=0, lty=3 )
text( Re(z), Im(z), colnames(C), adj=c(1,0) )
\fi

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{SIMoNe: statistical inderence\\ for modular networks} \\
  J. Chiquet et al. (2008)
\end{book}
If you suspect your network has a modular structure, 
\ie, the adjacency matrix has a block structure, 
with dense diagonal blocks and sparse off-diagonal blocks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Maps of random walks on complex networks reveal community structure} \\
  M. Roswall and C.T. Bergstrom (2008)
\end{book}
One can describe paths on a graph by using a Huffman code 
for the outgoing edges of each node --
but there is a different code for each node.
Instead, one can use a unique code, 
and encode the target nodes instead of the outgoing edges
(Huffman code for the limiting distribution 
of a random walk on the graph).
To identify clusters, use a 2-level code: 
each cluster has a unique name, 
each node has a code unique in its cluster, 
but reused on other clusters.
The path is encoded by using the node codes, 
as long as it remains in the same cluster
and, when it changes cluster, an exit code, 
the name of the cluster, 
and the code of the new node.
The average description length is 
\begin{align*}
  &P[ \text{change cluster} ] 
  H[ \text{cluster codes} ] 
  +{}\\
  &\quad
  \sum_{\text{cluster}}
  P[ \text{same cluster} ] 
  H[ \text{node codes in that cluster} ]
\end{align*}
where $H$ is the entropy.

To find the optimal partition into clusters, 
use a greedy agglomerative algorithm, 
and refine with simulated annealing. 
Contrary to other clustering algorithms,
the edge weights are used. 
By adding teleportation (\`a la PageRank),
the algorithm can also deal with directed graphs.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Online community detection\\ for large complex networks} \\
  G. Pan et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To estimate communities online, 
do not optimize the modularity, 
but the expected modularity,
for some generative model, e.g.: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Link a new node to a community $C$ 
with probability proportional to $\deg C$; 
\item Long two nodes in communities $C$ and $C'$ 
with probability proportional to 
$\deg C \times \deg C'$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nowcasting economic and social data:\\ when and why search engine data fails,\\ an illustration using Google flu trends} \\
  P. Ormerod et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Search engine data (e.g., Google flu)
is less reliable when social influence is high: 
the \emph{Bass diffusion model}
(fitted between the two low points on both sides of a peak)
can help distinguish between 
independently-motivated and socially-motivated searches. 


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Do we need hundreds of classifiers\\ 
  to solve real-world classification problems?} \\
  M. Fern\'andez-Delgato et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Comparison of 179 classifiers
(the output is binary),
in R, Weka, C, Matlab (no Python)
on 121 datasets: 
prefer random forests and SVM, 
but do not reject neural networks, boosting, C5.0,
avNNet (caret) or ELM (extreme learning machines).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generating abbreviations\\ using Google Books library}\\
  V.D. Solovyev and V.V Bochkarev 
\end{book}
Abbreviations are prefixes (or substrings)
with the same context.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved part-of-speech tagging for online conversational text with word clusters} \\
  O. Owoputi et al. (2013)
\end{book}
TweetNLP is a POS tagger for twitterese; 
it uses a hidden Markov model (HMM) 
with Brown clustering to recognize 
new words and alternate spellings. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Selecting influential examples: active learning with expected model output changes} \\
  A. Freytag et al. 
\end{book}

\emph{Active learning} refers to the algorithms used to decide which unlabeled samples to label next
(when labeling is costly and online),
\ie, which sample is the most informative.
Strategies include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Rapid exploration: prefer samples far away from already-labeled ones -- but this gives too much emphasis on outliers; 
\item Maximum uncertainty: label the samples the model is the most uncertain about;
\item Maximize the expected model change; 
\item Reduce the estimated classification error (expected entropy minimization);
\item Maximize the expected model output change (EMOC).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Logarithmic-time online multiclass prediction} \\
  A. Choromanska and J. Langford (2014)
\end{book}
For multiclass classification problems 
with a large number of classes, 
arrange the classes in a logarithmic-depth tree, either: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Known in advance;
\item Constructed using class frequencies (Huffman coding);
\item Constructed online, 
  recycling orphan nodes when needed.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model compression} \\
  C. Bucil\u a et al. (2006)
\end{book}
To compress an ensemble of thousands of models into 
a simpler, smaller model,
fit the smaller model 
on the forecasts of the complicated model 
on a large set of unlabeled examples.
If there is no such set, use a synthetic one: 
add noise to each feature of existing observations,
where the amplitude of the noise is 
the distance to the closest observation
(for qualitative variables, replace the value 
with that of the nearest neighbour with probability $p$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Do deep nets really need to be deep?} \\
  L.J. Ba and R. Caruana (2014)
\end{book}
Shallow neural networks are as expressive as deep ones: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Learn a deep model;
\item Use it to create a huge synthetic training set 
  from unlabeled data; 
\item Train a shallow model on the synthetic dataset
  (to speed things up, 
  use a low-rank approximation of the weight matrix).
\end{compactitem}
% The shallow net is very large -- perhaps even 
% larger than the deep one.
% We need better algorithms to train shallow nets.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards automated discovery\\ 
  of artistic influence} \\
  B. Saleh et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Linguistic models can be used with non-text data: 
for instance, one can use topic models 
(latent Dirichlet analysis, LDA) on images,
by replacing the words with image features 
(first identified by the Laplace Harris detector,
then reduced to a set of 600 features (``words'')
using $k$-means).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Memory networks} \\
  J. Weston et al. (2014)
\end{book}
One can combine neural networks with a memory component: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Convert the input to features; 
\item Store them in memory (more generally, 
  update the memory from the input features 
  and the current memory);
\item Predict the output features from the memory 
  and the input text;
\item Generate the output from the output features. 
\end{compactitem}
The various components can use your favourite 
machine learning algorithm 
(SVN, decision tree, neural net, etc.).
The idea can be used on text data, 
using a bag-of-word approach, e.g., to answer
questions about a story
(store the raw input, verbatim, sequentially, 
time-stamped, in memory; 
compute the memory $m_1$ ``closest'' to the input question; 
compute the memory $m_2$ closest to both the input 
and $m_1$ and with $m_1 \prec m_2$; output a single word).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling interestingness\\ with deep neural networks} \\
  J. Gao et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Use a deep neural net, 
with a convolutional and a max-pooling layer
(often used in image processing)
to model text;
train it to forecast followed links,
\ie, 
$f(\text{text}_1, \text{text}_2) = 1$
if $\text{text}_2$ was read after $\text{text}_1$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding locally competitive networks} \\
  R.K. Srivastava et al.
\end{book}
Visualizing the nodes of a neural network 
with rectified linear units
(RLU, 
\raisebox{-.5mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.15]
    \draw (-2,0) -- (2,0);
    \draw (0,-.5) -- (0,2);
    \draw[very thick,] (-2,0) -- (0,0) -- (1.8,1.8);
  \end{tikzpicture}}),
maxout
(output the maximum of the inputs)
or local winner takes all
(in a group of nodes, 
the maximum passes its input unchanged, 
but the others output 0)
(these are often trained with droupout)
suggest that the nodes cluster -- 
only part of the network is activated for
any given input pattern.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Political ideology detection\\ using recursive neural networks} \\
  M. Iyyer et al.
\end{book}
A \d{recursive neural net} (RNN) is a model that
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Uses a vector representation of the words 
  in the sentence (initialize it with word2vec);
\item Combines them, along the parse tree of the sentence
  (all nodes share the same parameters);
\item And outputs the predicted class
  (here, liberal vs conservative) 
  using a softmax.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient programmable learning to search} \\
  H. Daum\'e III et al. (2014)
\end{book}
In structured prediction, the search space can be described
by an arbitrary imperative program (or a grammar).

Software to model sequence data 
(segmentation, tagging in NLP): 
CRF++, crfsgd, VW.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dropout: a simple way to prevent\\ neural networks from overfitting} \\
  N. Srivastava et al. (2014)
\end{book}
When iteratively training a neural net
(e.g., with stochastic gradient descent, SGD),
forget (drop) a random subset of the nodes at each iteration 
(and update this smaller set).
The resulting neural net
can be seen as an ensemble 
of (all the) $2^n$ sparser subnets.

This can be interpreted as the addition 
of noise to the hidden nodes; 
this noise can be integrated out to compute the gradient
(for regression, this is similar to ridge regression).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Monte Carlo non-local means: 
  random sampling for large-scale image filtering} \\
  S.H. Chan et al. (2013)
\end{book}
To denoise a pixel $i$ in an image, 
take the weighted average of the pixels $j$,
$\sum_j w_{ij} x_j$,
where $w_{ij}$ measures the similarity 
of the regions around $i$ and $j$
(non-local means, NLM);
the pixels $j$ can be from the same image 
or from an image database.
To speed up the algorithm, consider: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Only looking at pixels $j$ close to $i$;
\item Dimension reduction (SVD); 
\item Effective data structures (kd-trees);
\item A low rank approximation of the weight matrix $W$;
\item Random sampling.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling the shape of the scene: a holistic representation of the spatial envelope} \\
  A. Oliva and A. Torralba (2000)
\end{book}
It is possible to recognize scenes by looking 
at their texture and structure,
without any segmentation
(GIST descriptors): % TODO: Check this
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Presence of textured zones;
\item Undulating contours;
\item Uncluttered horizon line; 
\item Size of the major components;
\item How straight the horizon line is;
\item Parallels and perpendiculars;
\item Converging lines.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evaluation of GIST descriptors\\ 
  for web-scale image search} \\
  M. Douze et al. (2009)
\end{book}
Useful for duplicated image search.

