\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random search\\ for hyper-parameter optimization} \\
  J. Bergstra and Y. Bengio (2012)
\end{book}
Avoid grid search for hyper-parameter optimization:
if some of the directions are irrelevant,
random search (or a low discrepancy sequence) 
performs better.
\[
<<RandomSearchForHyperparameterOptimization,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width='4cm',fig.show='hold'>>=
set.seed(1)
n <- 100
r <- density(runif(20,-2,2), from=-2, to=2, n=n, bw=.2)
x <- r$x
y1 <- r$y
r <- density(runif(10000,-2.3,2.3), from=-2, to=2, n=n, bw=.2)
y2 <- r$y
#matplot(x, cbind(y1,y2), type="l", lwd=3, lty=1, ylim=c(0,.6), col="black")

p1 <- expand.grid( c(-1.5,0,1.5), c(-1.5,0,1.5) )
set.seed(2)
p2 <- cbind( runif(9,-2,2), runif(9,-2,2) )

f <- function( x, y1, y2, p ) { 
  xlim <- range(x)
  ylim <- range(c( 0, y1, y2 ))
  ylim[2] <- 1.1 * ylim[2]
  a <- 3
  b <- 1
  op <- par( mar=.2*c(1,1,1,1), xaxs="i", yaxs="i", oma=c(2,2,0,0) )
  layout( rbind(
    cbind( matrix(2,b,a), matrix(4,b,b) ),
    cbind( matrix(1,a,a), matrix(3,a,b) )
  ) )
  plot( p, xlim=xlim, ylim=xlim, pch=15, cex=2, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="" )
  box()
  mtext( "Important parameter", side=1, line=1 )
  mtext( "Irrelevant parameter", side=2, line=1 )
  plot( x, y1, type="n", lwd=3, ylim=ylim, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="" )
  polygon( c( x[1], x, x[n] ), c(0, y1, 0), col="grey", border=NA )
  lines( x, y1, lwd=3 )
  points( approx(x, y1, p[,1]), pch=16, cex=2 )
  plot( y2,x, type="n", lwd=3, xlim=ylim, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="" )
  polygon( c(0,y2,0), c(x[1],x,x[n]), col="grey", border=NA )
  lines( y2, x, lwd=3 )
  r <- approx(x, y2, p[,1])
  points( r$y, r$x, pch=16, cex=2 )
  plot.new()
  par(op)  
}

f(x,y1,y2,p1)
#@
#<<RandomSearchForHyperparameterOptimization2,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="4cm">>=
f(x,y1,y2,p2)
@
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Freeze-thaw bayesian optimization} \\
  K. Swersky et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Bayesian optimization searches the (global) minimum 
of an expensive, noisy function (over $[0,1]^D$).
The function is often modeled as a Gaussian process (GP).
To choose the next point on which to evaluate the function,
one can use:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The point that gives the best expected improvement 
\[ 
  a_{\text{EI}}(x) = \left( 
  f( x_{\text{best}} ) - \mu(x)
  \right)_+
\]
  (where the positive part 
  is smoothed -- they do not use 
  $u_+ \approx \log(1+e^u)$,
  but 
  $u_+ \approx u \Phi(u) + \phi(u)$ --
  and the smoothing is larger 
  when the variance $V(x)$ is large);
\item The point that reduces the expected uncertainty 
  about the location of the minimum the most (entropy search).
\end{compactitem}
It is actually not needed to precisely estimate 
the function at each point: 
one can stop early (freeze),
estimate the limiting value 
(by assuming exponential convergence -- 
this is not unlike the classical series acceleration
techniques) and, at each iteration, 
decide to either explore a new point, 
or continue (thaw) the computations for a frozen point.

For bayesian optimization in Python, 
check \path{HIPS/spearmint}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MA-SW-Chains: memetic algorithm\\ based on local search chains\\ for large scale continuous global optimization} \\
  D. Molina et al. (2010)
\end{book}
A memetic algorithm is an evolutionary algorithm,
hybridized with local search.
The chains refer to the fact that 
the local search is not run until the end,
but just a few more steps at each generation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Approximate bayesian computation\\ and particle filters} \\
  D. prangle (2014)
\end{book}
Maximum likelihood estimation can still be used when the likelihood is not computable: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Simulate data $y_{\text{sim}}$ for many values of the parameter $\theta$;
\item Select the $\theta$ for which $y_{\text{sim}}$ 
and $y_{\text{obs}}$ are the closest
  (instead of using all of $y$,
  you can use some summary statistic).
\end{compactitem}
In a bayesian framework, this likelihood-free method 
becomes ``approximate bayesian computation'' (ABC): 
\[
\begin{array}{l}
\text{Repeat $N$ times:} \\
\qquad\text{Draw $\theta$ from the prior}\\
\qquad\text{Draw $y_{\text{sim}}|\theta$ from the model}\\
\qquad\text{Accept $\theta$ if 
  $d( y_{\text{sim}}, y_{\text{obs}} ) < \epsilon$}.
\end{array}
\]

To avoid degeneracy (no samples accepted), 
repeat until there are $M$ samples
(alive ABC).

Adding noise makes the estimate consistent (noisy ABC).

For state space models, 
one can start with a \emph{particle filter}
\[
\begin{array}{l}
t=1 \\
\text{Sample $x^{(i)}$ from the prior} \\
w^{(i)} \propto \operatorname{likelihood}( 
  y_t^{\text{obs}} | x^{(i)}
) \\
\text{Increment $t$, resample, propagate the particles}
\end{array}
\]
and replace the likelihood
\[
\begin{array}{l}
  \text{Simulate $y^{(i)} | x^{(i)}, \theta$} \\
  w^{(i)} = \mathbf1_{ 
    d( y_t^{\text{obs}}, y^{(i)} ) < \epsilon
  }.
\end{array} 
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Accurate methods\\ for approximate bayesian computation filtering} \\
  L. Calvet and V. Czellar (2012)
\end{book}
In the ABC particle filter
\[ 
  p_t^{(n)} \propto \mathbf1_{ 
    d( \tilde y_t^{(n)}, y_t ) < \epsilon
  },
\]
where $y$ is the data and 
$\tilde y$ is the data sampled from the model ($N$ samples),
choosing $\epsilon$ is akin 
to selecting the bandwidth of a kernel density estimator 
(this guarantees 
%$\epsilon \underset{N \to \infty}{\longrightarrow} 0$).
$\epsilon \xrightarrow[N \to \infty]{} 0$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Relevant statistics for Bayesian model choice} \\
  J.M. Marin et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The choice of a (non-sufficient) summary statistic $T$ 
(\ie, 
using $\pi(\theta | T(y))$
instead of $\pi(\theta|y)$, 
where $\theta=$model, $y=$data, $T=$summary statistic)
in bayesian statistics (in particular ABC)
leads to incorrect Bayes factors, and therefore 
incorrect model selection.
Under some conditions on the summary statistics, 
the Bayes factor is asymptotically correct.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian computation via empirical likelihood} \\
  K.L. Mengersen et al. (2012)
\end{book}
The \emph{empirical likelihood} (in R, \texttt{emplik})
is an alternative to approximate bayesian computations (ABC):
given a set of equations 
uniquely determining the parameters of interest $\theta$, 
$E[ h(y,\theta)] = 0$,
let 
\begin{align*}
  L_{\text{el}} = \max \Bigl\{ 
    \prod_i p_i 
    \ :\ \ 
    &
    p \in [0,1]^n, \\
    & 
    \sum p_i = 1,\ 
    \sum p_i h(y_i,\theta) = 0
  \Bigr\}.
\end{align*}
% Applications: population genetics

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multidimensional scaling using majorization:
  SMACOF in R} \\
  J. de Leeuw and P. Mair 
\end{book}
To find the minimum of a function $f$, 
\emph{majorization} suggests to find a simpler function $g$
such that 
\begin{align*}
  \hspace*{2cm} & \forall x, y & g(x,y) &\geq f(x) \hspace*{2cm}\\
  & \forall y & g(y,y) &= f(y)
\end{align*}
and iterate 
$ x_{n+1} = \argmin_x g(x, x_n) $.
%(Quite often, badly-behaved objective functions are 
%of the form 
%$ f(x) = \inf_y g(x,y)$, 
%with $g$ much nicer -- if we do not discard this information, the optimization is easier.)

Multi-dimensional scaling (MDS) is the problem 
\[ 
\argmin_{x_1,\dots,x_n} 
\sum_{ij} w_{ij} ( \delta_{ij} - \n{ x_i - x_j} ) ^2 ;
\]
the objective function can be written 
\begin{align*}
  \sigma(X) &= 1 + \tr X' V X - 2 \tr X' B(X) X  \\
  & \geq 1 + \tr X' V X - 2 \tr X' B(X) Y
\end{align*}
(the rhs is now a quadratic function).

Variants include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Several dissimilarity matrices;
\item Linear constraints (they can be formulated 
  as a reparametrization: find $C$ to minimize $\sigma(ZC)$, 
  with $Z$ known);
\item Rectangular matrices: 
  given a judge$\times$object rating matrix,
  find coordinates for both judges and items
  in the same space -- 
  this is a special case of the previous setup, 
  with weights 
  $ W = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & * \\ * & 0 \end{pmatrix}$;
\item Non-metric MDS: 
\[ 
\argmin_{\substack{X,f\\\text{$f$ non-decreasing}}} \sum_{ij} w_{ij} ( 
  f( \delta_{ij} - \n{x_i - x_j}
) ^2, 
\]
the estimate of $f$ changes at each step, 
and is optained by \emph{monotone regression} 
(aka \emph{isotonic regression})
\item MDS on quadratic surfaces (mostly spheres).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hierarchical k-means clustering} \\
  A. B\"ocker et al. (2004)
\end{book}

The hierarchical $k$-means algorithm 
is a sequence of $k$-means invocations:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with the mean;
\item Split all the centroids in two, add noise, 
  and start the $k$-means algorithm with these centroids; 
\item Iterate until the number of centroids 
  equals the number of points.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Clustering with Bregman divergences} \\
  A. Banerjee et al. (2005)
\end{book}
The $k$-means algorithm
(of the $k$-medoid one -- 
the cluster centers are chosen among the data points)
can be generalized to other distances or divergences.
The \emph{Bregman divergence}
associated to a convex function 
$\phi: S \longrightarrow \RR$, with $S\subset\RR^n$,
is \[
d_\phi(x,y) = \phi(x) - \phi(y) -
\langle x - y , \nabla \phi(y) \rangle.
\]
Examples include 
$\phi(x) = \n{x}^2$, for the Euclidian distance;
$\phi(x) = \sum x_i \log x_i$,
for the Kullback-Leibler divergence on the simplex 
$S=[\,\sum x_i=1,\, \forall i \  x_i \geq 0 \,]$;
$\phi(x) = - \log x$,
for the Itakura-Saito distance, 
\[ D(p,q) = \sum_i \left( 
  \dfrac{p_i}{q_i} - 
  \log \dfrac{p_i}{q_i} - 
  1 
\right).
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Itakura-Saito nonnegative matrix factorization and friends for music signal decomposition} \\
  C. F\'evotte (2012)
\end{book}
The $\beta$-divergence, 
\[ 
  d_\beta(x|y) = 
  \dfrac1{\beta(\beta-1)}
  (x^\beta + (\beta-1)y^\beta - \beta x y^{\beta-1}),
\]
which specialized to the Euclidian distance ($\beta=2$),
the Kullback-Leibler divergence ($\beta=1$)
or the Itakura-Saito divergence ($\beta=0$, 
non-convex, but scale-invariant)
is a popular cost function 
in nonnegative matrix factorization (NMF).
It is non-convex in general,
but can be optimized by majorization-minimization (MM)
% Nice plots
by majorizing the convex and concave parts separately
(using Jensen's inequality and the tangent, respectively).
IS divergence NMF 
is actually a maximum likelihood estimator 
for a generative model of the 
short-term Fourier transform (STFT)
of the signal.

In music, NMF can recover instruments and notes.

% When we listen to music, we unknowingly perform 
% nonnegative matrix factorizations (NMF).

