\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the surprising behavior of distance metrics in high-dimensional space} \\
  C.C. Aggarwal et al. (2009)
\end{book}
The curse of dimensionality states that, 
in high dimensions, the closest point
and the farthest point 
are approximately at the same distance.
A study of the asymptotic behaviour of 
\[
\dfrac{
  \max_{1 \leq i \leq n} \n{ X_i }_{L^k}
}{
  \min_{1 \leq i \leq n} \n{ X_i }_{L^k}
}
\]
as the dimension $d$ tends to infinity suggests that the situation is worse if $k$ is large.
Prefer the $L^1$ distance
or, even better, the $L^k$ 
semi-metric for $k \in (0,1)$.
It is not a metric -- 
it does not satisfy the triangle inequality -- but, 
for algorithmic purposes 
(e.g., to look for the $k$ nearest neighbours), 
it is usually fine.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Feature selection strategies 
  for classifying high-dimensional astronomical data sets} \\
  C. Donalek et al.
\end{book}
Algorithms to select features include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fast relief: compare the distribution of 
  $f(\text{nearest hit}) - f(x_0)$ and
  $f(\text{nearest miss}) - f(x_0)$;
\item Fisher discriminant ratio: 
  $\text{FDR} = \left( 
  \dfrac{ \mu_1 - \mu_2 }{ \sigma_1 / \sigma_2 } 
  \right) ^2;$
\item Correlation-based feature selection: 
  find a subset of the variables 
  with low correlation between themselves 
  and high correlation with the target;
\item Least angle regression.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distribution's template estimate\\
  with Wasserstein metrics} \\
  E. Boissard et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The notion of mean (and barycenter) 
can be generalized to arbitrary metric spaces,
\[ 
\operatorname(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \argmin_\mu 
\sum_i d(\mu, x_i)^2 ,
\]
but unicity, or even existence, is not guaranteed in general.
Given a metric measurable space 
$(E,d,\Omega)$,
the Wasserstein distance between two probability measures
$\mu$ and $\nu$ on $E$ is
\[
  d(\mu,\nu)^2 = 
  \inf_{\substack{
  m \text{ measure on } E \times E \\
  \operatorname{pr}_1 \! m = \mu \\
  \operatorname{pr}_2 \! m = \nu 
  }} 
  \int_{E \times E}
  d(x,y) m(dx,dy).
\]
If $E=\RR^n$ and $\mu$ is absolutely continuous 
wrt the Lebesgue measure,
there exists 
$T: \RR^n \longrightarrow \RR^n$ 
(Brenier map)
such that 
$\nu = T * \mu$ 
and 
\[ d(\mu,\nu) = \int \va{ T(x) - x }^2 \mu(dx). \]
It measures the deformation needed 
to transform $\mu$ into $\nu$
and can be used to compute barycenters.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Item response models\\ to measure corporate social responsibility} \\
  N. marco et al. (2013)
\end{book}
To combine several signals into one
(here, various CSR measures, 
provided by KLD -- now MSCI ESG), one can use: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Equal weights;
\item Weights reflecting stakeholders' preferences;
\item Data envelopment analysis (DEA -- 
  the weights are company-specific 
  and determined by the data);
\item The first principal component, from PCA, ICA, NMF, etc.
  [not mentioned];
\item Item response theory (IRT):
  this automatically identifies 
  the offset for each variable 
  and how discriminative they are.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring corporate social performance:\\ an efficiency perspective} \\
  C.M. Chen and M. Delmas (2010)
\end{book}
\emph{Data envelopment analysis} (DEA)
is a way of measuring the distance 
(of a firm)
from the efficient frontier 
(of its peers).
Let 
  $i$ denote companies, 
  $k$ the measures to be maximized (profit, etc.),
  $x_{ki}$ the value of measure $k$ for firm $i$,
  $F = \{ i \,:\, \forall j \ \exists k \ x_{ki} \geq x_{ki} \}$
  the efficient frontier.
The aggregated score for company $i$ is 
$ \sum_k \lambda_{ki} x_{ki} $, 
where the optimal weights are
\[ 
  \lambda_{ \cdot i } = 
  \argmin_{\substack{
    \lambda_{\cdot i}\\
    \lambda \geq 0 \\ 
    \sum\limits_k \lambda_{ki} = 1
  }}
  \max_{ j \in F }
  \sum_k 
  \lambda_{ki} ( x_{kj} - x_{xi} ).
\]
Traditionnaly, DEA separates the measures 
into inputs $x_{ki}$ (to be minimized)
and outputs $x_{kj}$ (to be maximized): 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & \lambda_{\cdot i}, \mu_{\cdot i} \\ 
\text{To maximize} & 
  \displaystyle
  \left.
  \sum_\ell \mu_{\ell i} y_{\ell i} 
  \middle/
  \sum_k \lambda_{ki} x_{ki}
  \right. \\
\text{Such that} & 
  \displaystyle
  \forall j \ \ \  
  \left.
  \sum_\ell \mu_{\ell i} y_{\ell j} 
  \middle/
  \sum_k \lambda_{ki} x_{kj}
  \right.
  \leq 1 \\
\text{and} & 
  \lambda, \mu \geq 0.
\end{array}
\]
(To make the program linear,
rescale it by adding the constraint 
$\sum_k \lambda_{ki} x_{xi} = 1$.)

The ratio 
$
  \left.
  \sum_\ell \mu_{\ell i} y_{\ell i} 
  \middle/
  \sum_k \lambda_{ki} x_{ki}
  \right.
$ is the efficiency of the firm; 
it is $1$ iff it is on the efficient frontier.

The article uses an extension of DEA
to ordinal variables
to aggregate corporate social responsibility measures.
% Data from KLD, now MSCI ESG    

[Would the bootstrap help give more stable results?]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-linear shrinkage of the covariance matrix\\ for portfolio selection:\\ Markowitz meets Goldilocks} \\
  O. Ledoit and M. Wolf (2014)
\end{book}
Linear shrinkage estimates a covariance matrix as 
$ (1-\lambda) V + \lambda I$,
where $V$ is the sample variance matrix
and $\lambda$ is chosen to minimize some loss function.
The loss function could be the asymptotic
Frobenius distance 
between the estimator and the population variance 
(the population variance is not known
but,
to compute the \emph{asymptotic} distribution,
we do not need all of it:
the eigenvalues actually suffice).
In a financial context,
one can minimize the variance of the tangential portfolio:
the results turn out to be similar.

Instead of letting just one parameter vary, 
one can let $O(N)$ parameters vary, 
e.g., the eigenvalues of the sample covariance matrix.

The estimator is defined as follows: 
\begin{align*}
 & c = N / T && \text{concentration} \\
 & V = U \Lambda U' && \text{sample covariance matrix} \\
 & \lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n && \text{eigenvalues of } V \\
 & \tau_1,\dots,\tau_n && \text{eigenvalues of the population variance} \\
 &s(x) \in \RR \union \CC^+\!\!\!\! && \text{solution of }
 s^{-1} = \dfrac1T \sum_{i=1}^N \dfrac{\tau_i}{1 + \tau_i s} - x \\
 &d_i = \dfrac{1}{\lambda_i s(\lambda_i} && \text{new eigenvalues} \\
 &\rlap{$D = \text{diag}(d_1,\dots,d_N)$}  \\
 &S = U D U' && \text{desired estimator.}
\end{align*}

The population eigenvalues can be estimated 
by discretizing the relation between 
the asymptotic empirical distribution function $H$ 
of the population eigenvalues 
and that, $F$, of the sample eigenvalues: 
for all $x \in \CC^+$,
the Stieltjes transform of $F$, 
\[ s = \int \dfrac{dF(\lambda)}{ \lambda - z } \]
is determined by 
\begin{align*}
  - \dfrac{1-c}z + cs \in \CC^+ \\
  s = \int \dfrac{dH(\tau)}{\tau(1-c-czs)-z}.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Linear vs quadratic\\ portfolio selection models in practice} \\
  F. Cesarone et al.
\end{book}
The standard quadratic programming problem 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & x \in \RR^n \\
  \text{To minimize} & f(x) = x' Q x \\
  \text{Such that} & x \geq 0, \ x' \mathbf 1 = 1
\end{array}
\]
can be solved efficiently 
with the \emph{increasing set} algorithm.
The minimum is attained in the relative interior of a face where $f$ is strictly convex; 
furthermore, there is an increasing sequence of faces 
$F_1 \subset F_2 \subset \cdots \subset F_k$,
$\dim F_i = i$,
on which $f$ is strictly convex,
has an interior global minimizer $x_i^*$
and $x^* = x_k^*$.
The algorithm looks at all possible faces of dimension 1
satisfying the conditions, 
and tries to extend them (breadth-first).
For cardinality-constrained problems, that is even easier: 
stop early.

This can be used to find cardinality-constrained 
minimum variance portfolios.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Premium indexation:\\ 
  CVaR-lasso optimal index replication} \\
  Y.J. Jin and X. Zhong (2014)
\end{book}
To replicate an index, 
use CVaR optimization rather than mean-variance
%\[  \hat w = \argmin_w = \operatorname{CVaR}_\alpha( Y - \mathbf w' \mathbf X ) \]
and add an $L^1$ constraint 
(the authors suggest a hard constraint, 
$\sum_i \va{ w_i } \leq 2$)
to control the number of stocks in the replicating portfolio.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio selection\\ under directional predictability of returns} \\
  J. Hämäläinen (2014)
\end{book}
Forecasting return signs is easier than forecasting returns,
and the signs can still be used in a mean-variance optimization:
the retrun distributions can be computed using 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sign forecasts, $s_i$ for $\operatorname{sign} X_i$;
\item The probability that they are correct, 
  $p_i = P[ \operatorname{sign} X_i = s_i ]$;
\item The probability that they are jointly 
  correct or incorrect, 
  $p_ij = P[ (\operatorname{sign} X_i)(\operatorname{sign} X_j) = s_i s_j ]$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diversification management\\ of a multi-asset portfolio} \\
  M. Poonia (2014)
\end{book}
Comparison of various notions of diversification: 
number of stocks, diversification ratio, risk parity, 
PCA risk diversification, torsion bets. 


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Conditional Sharpe ratios} \\
  K.V. Chow and W.C. Lai 
\end{book}
When computing the Sharpe ratio, 
replace the excess returns by 0 
unless the benchmark returns 
are below their $\alpha$ quantile.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio choice in the presence of estimation error: a pricing model filter approach} \\
  M. Lozano (2014)
\end{book}
Use the sample covariance matrix of the CAPM (or some factor model) residuals.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse and robust normal and t-portfolios\\
  by penalized Lq likelihood minimization} \\
  D. Ferrari et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The lasso can be generalized to 
\[
\hat \beta = 
\argmin_{\beta, \mu, \sigma} 
- \sum_{i=1}^n
L_q f \dfrac{x'_i \beta - \mu}{\sigma}
- \sum_{j=1}^p
L_p \pi( \beta_j, \lambda)
\]
where 
\begin{align*}
  x_i &= \text{observations} \\
  f &= \text{Gaussian distribution (for instance)} \\
  L_q &= \text{generalized logarithm,} \\
  & \qquad\qquad
  L_q(z) = \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \dfrac{z^{q-1} - 1}{1 - q} & \text{if } q \neq 1 \\
  \log z & \text{if } q = 1
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \\  
  \pi &= \text{prior on the parameters, e.g., Laplace,} \\
  &\qquad\qquad 
  \pi(\beta,\lambda) = 
  \lambda e^{-\lambda\va\beta/2}.
\end{align*}
The lasso is obtained for $q=1$, $\pi=\text{Laplace}$, $f=\text{Gaussian}$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio spillovers\\ and a limit to diversification}\\
  B. Argyle (2013)
\end{book}
After a sharp price change to a stock,
investors holding the stock will rebalance their portfoklio,
but not necessarily by buying/selling this stock -- 
it may be easier, faster to buy/sell
more liquidy assets.
In the case of large institutional investors, 
and/or many investors holding a similar portfolio, 
there could be a price impact to those other stocks: 
can we detect it?


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic portfolio selection\\ by augmenting the asset space} \\
  M.W. Brandt and P. Santa-Clara (2006)
\end{book}
To compute an (approximately) optimal 
dynamic asset allocation 
when all you can do is compute static asset allocations
(Markowitz portfolio optimization),
just add a few managed portfolios
(e.g., weights proportional to some factor)
to your universe of assets,
and compute the corresponding ``static''  optimal portfolio.
Use a VAR model if you end up with too many assets.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Tilt Nickel to Diamond} \\
  G. Xiang and T. Yu (2014)
\end{book}
Take a set of risk factors (Fama-French, Carhart, etc.)
and a set of alpha factors.
Starting with a capital-weighted portfolio 
(or some other benchmark),
increase the exposure to the alpha factors
while keeping the exposure to the risk factors 
close to zero.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The deflated Sharpe ratio: correcting\\ for selection bias and backtest overfitting} \\
  M. L\'opez de Prado (2014)
\end{book}

When computing the Sharpe ratio of the best strategy
in your backtests,
you can account for multiple testing 
and non-Gaussian returns as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Estimate the return distribution, \\
  $\hspace*{1cm} X_{it} \sim F(\mu, \sigma, s, \kappa) \text{ iid}$;
\item Estimate the corresponding distribution of 
  the (sample) Sharpe ratios $S_i$;
\item Estimate the distribution of the best one,
  $\max\limits_{1 \leq i \leq n} S_i$;
\item Convert the best Sharpe ratio into a $p$-value, 
  using this distribution; 
\item Convert the $p$-value into a $z$-score --
  a deflated Sharpe ratio.
\end{compactitem}
The independence assumption is unrealistic, in this context: 
when backtesting many strategies, 
they are often just variants of the same idea,
so their returns are very similar.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evaluating trading strategies} \\
  C.R. Harvey and Y. Liu (2014)
\end{book}
To compare several strategies, 
take multiple testing into account: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item FWER (family-wise error rate, 
  e.g., Bonferroni or Holm  correction) 
  is often too conservative;
\item FDR (false discovery rate, e.g., BHY correction)
  gives more usable results.
\end{compactitem}
The discussion is needlessly complicated 
by the use of $T$ values instead of $p$-values.

In R check \texttt{bonferroni}, \texttt{holm}, \texttt{BY}, 
in the \texttt{mutoss} package.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal asset pricing} \\
  R. Turner et al. (2014)
\end{book}
\emph{Asset selling} (sometimes called ``asset pricing'')
studies the optimal price 
of a perishable asset
(airline ticket, hotel room, 
TV advertising slots)
for which customers arrive randomly
(inhomogeneous Poisson process)
with (known) decreasing price elasticity
(they are less and less price-sensitive as time passes).

Pricing policy $x_q(t)$ (price of the asset at time $t$ if there are still $q$ in inventory)
and expected revenue $v_q(t)$
are related by a system of ODEs.
For the optimal policy, 
just add 
\[ \dfrac{\partial R_q(x,t)}{\partial x} = 0, \]
where $R_q(x,t)$ is the expected revenue 
if a customer arrives at time $t$ and is quoted price $x$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Disentangling rebalancing return} \\
  W.G. Hallerbach (2014)
\end{book}
The ``rebalancing return'', \ie, 
the difference between a rebalanced portfolio 
and a buy-and-hold portfolio,
can be decomposed into a volatility return 
(``volatility harvesting'')
and a ``dispersion discount'' (a negative contribution), 
coming from the dispersion between the growth rate 
$\mu_i - \tfrac12 \sigma_i$ of the assets.

% Confusing notations: I do not understand what they are computing or how.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Signal-wise performance attribution\\ for constrained portfolio optimization} \\
  B. Durin (2014)
\end{book}
One can decompose the performance 
of a constrained portfolio
built from several signals 
into the contribution of each signal 
and that of the constraints (with no residual term).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning bayesian networks in R:\\ an example in systems biology} \\
  M. Scutari (UseR 2013)
\end{book}
The \texttt{bnlearn} package can learn the structure 
of a Gaussian or discrete bayesian network, using
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Constraint-based algorithms 
  (incremental association),
  that perform tests of conditional independence 
  (mutual information, $\chi^2$, partial correlation);
\item Score-based algorithms,
  that maximize (hill-climbing, tabu search)
  the goodness of fit 
  (likelihood, AIC, BIC, Dirichlet posterior density, etc.)
  of the network;
\item Hybrid algorithms, 
  local search using statistical tests 
  to define the neighbourhoods.
\end{compactitem}
If the data is continuous but non-Gaussian,
one can discretize it with 
\emph{Hartemink's method}:
discretize each variable into 60 quantile bins,
collapse them while reducing the mutual information 
as little as possible,
stop when each variable has 3 levels (low, average, high).

The \texttt{deal}, \texttt{catnet}, \texttt{pcalg} packages
can also learn the graph structure,
and some even accept mixed data
(but each only provides one algorithm).
All those packages can also fit the model parameters.
For inference, use \texttt{gRain} (fast) 
or \texttt{bnlearn} (flexible).

\emph{Bootstrap} can improve the stability of the result.

To model \emph{interventions},
either whitelist all the edges from the intervention node,
or blacklist all the edges to the intervention node.

\begin{verbatim}
 b <- boot.strength(d, R=200, algorithm="hc")
 g <- averaged.network(b, threshold=.85)
 plot(cpdag(g)) # Remove ambiguous directions
\end{verbatim}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning bayesian networks\\ with the bnlearn R package} \\
  M. Scutari (JSS, 2010)
\end{book}
More (but terse) details about the underlying 
algorithms, tests and scores.

