\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian networks in R\\
  with applications in systems biology} \\
  R. Nagarajan et al. (2013). 
\end{book}

% Chapter 1: empty
% Chapter 2: see the presentation, above -- it has more details
% Chapter 3: Dynamic networks

After a chapter on \texttt{bnlearn} 
(see the presentation above for a more thorough exposition),
the book reviews, 
the book quickly presents dynamic networks.
VAR models can be represented 
as dynamic baysesian networks (DBN), and estimated using 
shrinkage (\texttt{GeneNet}), 
lasso (\texttt{var}, \texttt{lars}, 
\texttt{glmnet}, \texttt{penalized}), 
low order independencies (\texttt{G1DBN}), 
change point models (\texttt{ARTIVA}),
modular assummptions (\texttt{simone}).

% Chapter 4

The inference chapter names the main algorithms 
(variable elimination, junction trees, particle methods), 
with few details, 
and gives examples with 
\texttt{gRain} (\texttt{setFinding}, \texttt{querygrain})
and \texttt{bnlearn} (\texttt{cpdist}, \texttt{cpquery}).

% Chapter 5: parallelization

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Inferring dynamic genetic networks\\ with low order independencies} \\
  S. L\`ebre (2009)
\end{book}
The structure of undirected Bayesian networks 
(random Markov fields)
can be estimated from the partial correlation 
(which can be computed from the inverse 
of the correlation matrix, or from regressions):
add an edge between $i$ and $j$ 
if $\operatorname{pCor}(X_i,X_j) \neq 0$.

When there are not enough observations ($p \gg n$), 
one can use a shrinkage estimator
(e.g., replace the correlation matrix $C$
with $(1-\lambda)C + \lambda I$
to make it invertible,
or use a Lasso regression),
or the first order partial correlation: 
add an edge between $i$ and $j$ 
if $\cor(X_i,X_j)\neq0$
and $\forall k \neq i,j \  \cor(X_i,X_j | X_k ) \neq 0$
(the $0$th order partial correlation is the correlation,
the $(q+1)$st order partial independence graph
is a subgraph of the $q$th).

This can be used to infer the structure 
of \emph{dynamic bayesian networks} (DBN).
There is an implementation in the \texttt{G1DBN} R package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Inferring causal impact\\ using bayesian structural time-series models} \\
  K.H. Brodersen et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To measure the effect of an intervention 
on a time series $y_t$,
the difference-in-difference approach just compares 
\begin{align*}
  y_{\text{after}} - y_{\text{before}} 
  & \mid \text{intervention} \\
  \intertext{with}
  y_{\text{after}} - y_{\text{before}} 
  & \mid \text{no intervention},
\end{align*}  
ignoring time series dynamics.
Instead, one can model the time series,
before the intervention,
with a state space model,
including a linear trend
(whose slope is a random walk 
or a mean-reverting time series),
seasonality, and covariates with dynamic coefficients.
A \emph{spike-and-slab} prior
\raisebox{0mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.3,yscale=.1]
    \draw (-1.5,0) -- (1.5,0);
    \draw[very thick,fill=lightgray] (-1,0) -- (-1,1) -- (0,1) -- (0,3) -- (0,1) -- (1,1) -- (1,0);
  \end{tikzpicture}}
provides automated variable selection.
This is implemented in the \texttt{CausalImpact}
R package,
and used for A/B testing.

%% DAG ANALYSIS (BAYESIAN NETWORKS)
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Credit risk spillovers among financial institutions around the global credit crisis: firm-level evidence} \\
  J. Yang and Y. Zhou (2013)
\end{book}
To study spillover risk, 
fit a VAR model to the data,
and perform a ``DAG analysis'' 
on (the correlation matrix of) the residuals,
\ie, fit a bayesian network,
and check if the direction of the edges is well-defined.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Reducing bias through directed acyclic graphs} \\
  I. Shrier and R.W. Platt (2008)
\end{book}
Identifying causal relations in observational data is tricky.
Introducing a confounding variable in the regression
can reduce or increase the bias, e.g., 
\[
\tikzset{
  commutative diagrams/.cd, 
  arrow style=tikz, 
  diagrams={>=stealth}
}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = 2ex,row sep=3ex,inner sep=1pt]
  & Z \arrow{rd}{} \arrow{ld}{} \\
  X \arrow[dashed]{rr}[below]{?} && Y
\end{tikzcd}
\text{ versus }
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = 2ex,row sep=3ex,inner sep=1pt]
  & Z \arrow{rd}{} \\
  X \arrow{ru}{} \arrow[dashed]{rr}[below]{?} && Y.
\end{tikzcd}
\]
Real world causal networks are more complicated.
If the DAG is known, one can find 
whether the variable will increase or decrease the bias 
as follows: 
the covariates should not be descendants of $X$; 
keep only ancestors of $X$, $Y$ or $Z$; 
delete arcs leaving $X$; moralize, convert to undirected;
delete $Z$; if $X$ and $Y$ are no longer connected,
then $Z$ can be used as a confounder in $Y \sim X$.

There are potential problems: 
the DAG need not be known (consider all DAG candidates), 
or may be incomplete; 
the method does not account for synergistic 
or antagonistic actions. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal diagrams for epidemiologic research} \\
  S. Greenland et al. (1999)
\end{book}
Earlier article on the same topic.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using directed acyclic graphs to guide analyses of neighbour health effects: an introduction} \\
  N.L. Fleischer and A.V. Diez Roux (2008)
\end{book}
Algorithms to decide whether a set of confounders is
sufficient, necessary, minimally sufficient.

For implementations, check: 
DAGitty (browser-based), 
Tetrad (Java)
or dagR (undocumented).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adjustment criteria in causal diagrams:\\
  an algorithmic perspective} \\
  J. Textor and M. Li\'skiewicz (2011)
\end{book}
Efficient algorithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A tool for filtering information\\ 
  in complex systems} \\
  M. Tumminello et al. (2005)
\end{book}
The minimum spanning tree 
(take the edges one by one, 
starting with the most important ones, 
and keep them if they do not introduce a loop)
discards too much information:
instead of looking for an acyclic graph,
one can look for planar graphs
(giving the ``planar maximally filtered graph'', PMFG)
or, more generally, graphs of genus $g$.
Instead of highlighting edges, this highlights 
3- and 4-cliques,
\raisebox{-1mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.3,yscale=.15]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A) at (0,1) {};
  \node (B) at (.5,-.866) {};
  \node (C) at (-.5,-.866) {};
  \draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (A);
  \end{tikzpicture}}
and   
\raisebox{-1mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.3,yscale=.15]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (O) at (0,-.2) {};
  \node (A) at (0,1) {};
  \node (B) at (.5,-.866) {};
  \node (C) at (-.5,-.866) {};
  \draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (A);
  \draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (A);
  \draw (O) -- (A);
  \draw (O) -- (B);
  \draw (O) -- (C);
  \end{tikzpicture}}.
  
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Spread of risk across financial markets:\\ better to invest in the peripheries} \\
  F. Pozzi et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Stocks in the periphery of the minimum spanning tree (MST)
or the planar maximally filtered graph (PMFG)
provide more diversification benefit.
Those graphs can be used to represent the contents 
of a portfolio.
Try a long-short strategy, 
periphery vs center,
with minimum variance portfolios on each side,
using the following measures of centrality: 
degree, betweenness, 
eccentrality, closeness,
eigenvector centrality.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\texttt{import sage.graphs.genus}} \\
\end{book}

To compute the genus of a graph 
$G=(V,E)$,
consider all possible 
\emph{combinatorial embeddings}, \ie,
all possible cyclic orderings at each vertex. 
Formally, a combinatorial embedding is an action 
of the free group $\ZZ * \ZZ = \langle v, e \rangle$
on the set of half-edges,
with the orbits for $\langle v \rangle$ corresponding 
to the vertices 
and those for $\langle e \rangle$, to the edges
($e$ is an involution).
The genus of the embedding is given by $2 - 2g = V - E + F$,
where $V$, $E$ and $F$ are the number of orbits for 
$v$, $e$ and $ve$.
% Doesn't this assume the surface is oriented?

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Determining the genus of a graph} \\
  A. Perez (2009)
\end{book}
% Student article
A graph is planar iff it does not contain (a subdivision of)
$K_5 = 
\raisebox{-.8mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.15]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
 \node (A) at (0,1) {};
 \node (B) at (0.951,0.309) {};
 \node (C) at (0.588,-0.809) {};
 \node (D) at (-0.588,-0.809) {};
 \node (E) at (-0.951,0.309) {};
 \draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (E) -- (A);
 \draw (A) -- (C) -- (E) -- (B) -- (D) -- (A);
  \end{tikzpicture}}
$
or 
$K_{3,3} = 
\raisebox{-.8mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.3]
\tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
 \node (A1) at (0,0) {};
 \node (B1) at (1,0) {};
 \node (C1) at (2,0) {};
 \node (A2) at (0,1) {};
 \node (B2) at (1,1) {};
 \node (C2) at (2,1) {};
 \draw (A1) -- (A2) -- (B1) -- (B2) -- (C1) -- (C2) -- (A1) -- (B2);
 \draw (B1) -- (C2);
 \draw (C1) -- (A2);
  \end{tikzpicture}}
$ (Kuratowski theorem).

The \emph{genus} of a graph is the minimum $g$ 
such that the graph can be embedded in a surface of genus $g$.

The \emph{maximum genus} of a graph is 
the maximum $g$ such that the graph 
can be embedded in a surface of genus $g$ and, 
cutting the surface along the graph gives 2-cells,
\ie, pieces homeomorphic to the open disk.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The use of correlation networks\\ in parametric portfolio policies} \\
  H. Lohre et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Nothing new: the authors suggest to look at the minimum spanning tree (MST)
built from the correlation matrix of stock or sector returns,
and look at graph metrics such as
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The graph diameter (a small stock network diameter, 
  or a \emph{large} sector network diameter, 
  is a sign of market instability);
\item The betweenness centrality, 
  which can be used in a parametric portfolio
  (a parametric portfolio is a portfolio 
  whose weights are a linear combination of
  some investment signals: 
  since only the coefficients have to be estimated,
  the quadratic optimization problem is much simpler).
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Finding community structure\\ in very large networks} \\
  A. Clauset et al. 
\end{book}
To find communities in a graph, 
one can use a greedy agglomerative algorithm: 
start with each node in its own cluster;
merge the two clusters that produce 
the largest increase in modularity 
(the \emph{modularity} is the difference between
the fraction of edges inside clusters
and 
the expected fraction);
iterate.
Instead of keeping track of the adjacency matrix $A_{ij}$ 
as you merge the clusters
(it is sparse, but finding the pair 
that increases the modularity the most 
creates dense matrices),
keep track of $\Delta Q_{ij}$, 
the increase in modularity if we merge $i$ and $j$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quadrature rule-based bounds\\ for functions of adjacent matrices} \\
  M. Benzi and P. Boito (2010)
\end{book}
Many graph metrics can be computed using matrix functions
(often, the exponential)
and the adjacency matrix $A$, e.g.: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Degree;
\item Subgraph centrality of a node $i$: $[e^A]_{ii}$;
\item Estrada index, 
  $\operatorname{EE}(G) = \sum e^{\lambda_k} = \tr e^A$;
\item Comminicability between $i$ and $j$, 
$[e^A]_{ij}$; average communicability from $i$;
\item Communicability betweenness of $k$: 
\[
  \sum_{ijk \text{ different}}
  \dfrac{
    [e^A]_{ij} - [e^{A-E_k}]_{ij}
  }{
    [e^A]_{ij}
  } 
\]
where $E_k$ is the adjacency matrix of the graph 
without the edges linked to node $k$;
\item Resolvent subgraph centrality, etc.: 
replace $e^x$ with 
\[ 
  f(x) = \left( 1 - \dfrac{x}{N-1} \right)^{-1}.
\]
\end{compactitem}
They can be estimated by Gaussian quadrature
(\ie, 
$\int_{-1}^1 f(x) dx \approx \sum w_i f(x_i)$;
choose $n$ and $w_i$; 
compute the optimal $x_i$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Ranking hubs and authorities\\ using matrix functions} \\
  M. Benzi et al.
\end{book}
Graph metrics defined by matrix functions 
applied to the adjacency matrix $A$ 
can be generalized to directed graphs 
by considering the undirected bipartite graph 
$\begin{pmatrix} 0 & A \\ A' & 0 \end{pmatrix}$.
The node metrics then have two variants: 
the hub and authority ones.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Functions of matrices} \\
  N.J. Higham (2005)
\end{book}
A complex function $f$ can be evaluated 
on a matrix $A$ as follows.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item If $f(z) = \sum a_n z^n$ has convergence radius $R$, 
  and $\spec A \subset B(0,R)$,
  then $f(A) = \sum a_n A^n$.
\item If $f$ is $\mathscr C^{\infty}$ 
  on a neighbourhood of $\spec A$, 
  and $Z^{-1} A Z = \diag(J_1,\dots,J_p)$ 
  is the Jordan decomposition of $A$,
  then $f(A) = Z \diag(f(J_1),\dots,f(J_p)) Z^{-1}$,
  where 
  \[ J_k = 
  \left( 
  \begin{tikzcd}[column sep = 1ex,row sep=1ex,inner sep=1pt]
  \lambda \ar[-]{rrrddd}{} & 1 \ar[-]{rrdd}{} &\hphantom{\lambda} & 0 \\
  \vphantom{\lambda}\\
  &&& 1 \\
  0 &&& \lambda
  \end{tikzcd}
  \right)
  \]
  \[
  f(J_k) = \left(
  \begin{tikzcd}[column sep = 2ex,row sep=2ex,inner sep=1pt]
    f(\lambda) \ar[-]{rrrddd}{} & f'(\lambda) \ar[-]{rrdd}{} \ar[-,dotted]{rr}{} &\hphantom{\lambda} & \dfrac{f^{m-1}(\lambda)}{(m-1)!} \ar[-,dotted]{dd}{}\\
  \vphantom{\lambda}\\
  &&& f'(\lambda) \\
  0 &&&   f(\lambda)
  \end{tikzcd}%
  \right)
  \]
\item If $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_s$ are the eigenvalues of $A$,
$n_1,\dots,n_s$ the sizes of the largest Jordan blocks for each of them,
$r$ the polynomial of degree at most $\sum n_i$
such that 
\[ 
  \forall i \in \llbracket 1, s \rrbracket
  \quad
  \forall j \in \llbracket 0, n_i-1 \rrbracket
  \quad
  r^{(j)} ( \lambda_i ) = f^{ (j) }( \lambda_i ),
\]
then $f(A) = r(A)$.
\item $\displaystyle 
  f(A) = \dfrac{1}{2\pi i} \oint_\Gamma
  f(z) ( z I - A ) ^{-1} dz$,
  where 
  $f$ is analytic inside $\Gamma$ and 
  $\Gamma$ encloses $\spec A$.
\end{compactitem}
Common examples include exponential, logarithm, sine, 
cosine, square root (not always defined),
sign (only defined if $i \RR \inter \spec A = \nothing$).

Numeric methods include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Polynomial or rational approximations
  (for exp, log, sin, cos: use Pad\'e's approximation and scaling);
\item Factorization: write $A = X^{-1} B X$, 
  where $f(B)$ is easier to compute;
  for instance, if $B$ is upper triangular, 
  the diagonal of $f(B)$ is $f(b_{ii})$,
  the coefficients immediately above the diagonal 
  can be computed by solving $f(B) B = B f(B)$,
  and one can iterate for the rest 
  (Parlett recurrence -- if only works 
  if the eigenvalues are different, 
  but there is a block variant if they are not).
\item Iterative methods, 
  $X_{n+1} = g(X_n)$,
  often coming from Newton's method 
  for some equation satisfied by $f(z)$
  (but beware of numeric instability).
  For instance, 
  \begin{align*}
    X_0 &= A \\
    Y_0 &= I \\
    X_{k+1} &= \tfrac12 ( X_k + Y_k^{-1} ) \\
    Y_{k+1} &= \tfrac12 ( Y_k + X_k^{-1} )
  \end{align*}
  for the square root or 
  \begin{align*}
  X_0 &= A \\
  X_{k+1} &= \tfrac12 ( X_k + X_k^{-1} )
  \end{align*}
  for the sign.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantitative easing and volatility spillovers across countries and asset classes} \\
  Y. Zhou
\end{book}
% [Interesting, but badly written.]
One can use contemporaneous causal inference to study 
the relations between various volatility indices.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamical macro-prudential stress testing using network theory} \\
  S. Levy-Carciente et al. (2014)
\end{book}
There are two main channels of risk contagion: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Direct interbank liability linkages;
\item Changes in bank asset values.
\end{compactitem}
One can account for the latter 
with a bipartite bank-asset network.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Global portfolio investment network\\ and stock market co-movement} \\
  T. Chuluun (2014)
\end{book}
Cross-border capital flows 
(data from the IMF CPIS
(coordinated portfolio investment survey), 2001--present)
define a network of countries.
Central countries 
(high degree, indegree (better),
outdegree (worse) or
eigenvector centrality)
are more correlated with global indices.
Other flows define other networks: 
trade flows, foreign direct investment,
bank loans, etc.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic flow diagrams} \\
  N.J. Calkin and M. L\'opez de Prado (2014)
\end{book}
The authors have re-invented graphical models 
and/or recurrent neural networks,
with a slightly different graphical notation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The topology of macro financial flows} \\
  N.J. Calkin and M. L\'opez de Prado (2014)  
\end{book}
Instead of building a minimum spanning tree
(or more general graph)
from a correlation matrix, 
one can build a graphical model from an SVAR$(p)$ model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Structural analysis\\ with independent innovations} \\
  H. Herwartz (2014)
\end{book}
Often, multi-variate time series models 
(e.g., SVAR, structural vector autoregressive model)
are not identified: 
independent component analysis (ICA) can help.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Emergence of statistically validated financial intraday lead-lag relationships} \\
  C. Curme et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Compute the cross-correlation matrix of stock returns,
convert the correlations to $p$-values,
adjust them for multiple testing,
truncate them,
and build the corresponding graph.
As the lag increases (from 5 minutes to 1 hour),
the graph becomes sparser.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimizing directed acyclic graph\\ support vector machines} \\
  F. Takahashi and S. Abe
\end{book}
When using support vector machines (SVM) 
on multiclass problems
with the one-against-all or the pairwise approach,
there are unclassified regions.
To avoid them, one can use a smaller number 
of pairwise models, arranged in a tree,
either by eliminating one element at each level of the tree
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[
  column sep = -3ex, %between origins
  row sep = 5ex, inner sep = 1pt,
  cells={nodes={align=center,text width = 7ex,draw,ellipse}} 
]
  && \text{1 or 2?} 
  \arrow{rd}[above]{\text{not 1}} 
  \arrow{ld}[above]{\text{not 2}} \\
  & \text{1 or 3?} 
  \arrow{ld}[above]{\text{not 3}} 
  \arrow{rd}[above]{\text{not 1}} 
  && \text{2 or 3} 
  \arrow{ld}[above]{\text{not 2}} 
  \arrow{rd}[above]{\text{not 3}} 
  \\
  |[draw=none]|{1} && 
  |[draw=none]|{3} && 
  |[draw=none]|{2}
\end{tikzcd}
\]
or using a tournament,
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[
  column sep = -3ex, %between origins
  row sep = 5ex, inner sep = 1pt,
  cells={nodes={align=center,text width = 7ex,draw,ellipse}} 
%  column sep=5pt, row sep=5ex, inner sep = 1pt,
%%  cells={nodes={fill=black!10}} 
%  cells={nodes={draw,ellipse}} 
]
  \text{1 or 2?} \arrow{rd}[below]{\displaystyle a}
  && \text{3 or 4?} \arrow{ld}{\displaystyle b} \\
  & a\text{ or }b? \arrow{d}{} \\
  & |[draw=none]|{}
\end{tikzcd}
\]

But the performance depends 
on the directed acyclic graph (DAG) chosen.
It is better if easy-to-classify class pairs 
(low support vectors/observations ratio)
appear in the top layers of the graph,
and the hard-to-clasify ones in the leaves.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of methods\\ for multiclass support vector machines} \\
  C.W. Hsu and C.J. Lin (2002)
\end{book}
Among the multiclass SVM algorithms, 
prefer the pairwise or DAG ones
to the (more complicated) ``all together'' methods.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms for the multi-armed bandit problem} \\
  V. Kuleshov and D. Precup (2000)
\end{book}
Simple multi-arm bandit algorithms
(to manage the exploration-exploitation trade-off 
in \emph{reinforcement learning})
work well: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\epsilon$-greedy: 
  select the arm with the highest empirical mean reward 
  $\hat \mu_i$ with probability $1-\epsilon$, 
  and a random arm with probability $\epsilon$;
\item Boltzmann: $p_i \propto \exp( \hat \mu_i / T ) $,
  where the temperature $T$ slowly decreases;
\item Pursuit: 
  \[
  p_i \leftarrow \left\{ 
  \begin{array}{ll}
    (1-\beta) p_i + \beta \cdot 1 & 
    \text{if } i = \argmax_j \hat \mu_j \\
    (1-\beta) p_i + \beta \cdot 0 & 
    \text{otherwise;}
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \]
\item Reinforcement comparison:
  \begin{align*}
    p_i & \propto \exp \pi_i \\
    \pi_j & \leftarrow \pi_j + \beta (r - \bar r) \\
    \bar r &\leftarrow (1-\alpha) \bar r + \alpha r
  \end{align*}
  where 
  \begin{align*}
    \pi_i &= \text{preference} \\
    j &= \text{arm selected} \\
    r &= \text{reward} \\
    \bar r &= \text{average reward;}
  \end{align*}
\item Upper confidence bound
  \[
    j = \argmax_i \left( 
      \hat \mu_i + \sqrt{ \dfrac{2 \ln t}{n_i}}
    \right)
  \]
  where 
  \begin{align*}
  \hat\mu_i &= \text{average reward for arm } i \\
  n_i &= \text{number of times $i$ was chosen} \\
  t &= \sum n_i = \text{number of steps}.
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}
Applications include clinical trials and online advertising.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hierarchical temporal memory\\ including HTM cortical learning algorithms} \\
  Numenta (2011)
\end{book}
Recurrent (convolutional?)
deep neural network,
with connections \emph{within} layers, 
binary weights, 
and sparsity constraints.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A survey on transfer learning} \\
  S.J. Pan and Q. Yang (2010)
\end{book}
Transfer learning studies what to do 
when the training and test sets are significantly different.
For instance:
how to extend a document classification model to a new domain
(say, a sentiment model, 
from camera reviews to hotel reviews)?
How to deal with a time-changing domain?
Some new learning will be needed, 
but we want to reuse as much information as possible --
we want to automatically identify the bits of the model
that can be kept and those that should be discarded.
Approaches include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Instance transfer: reweight the source data 
  (Ada\-boost, importance sampling);
\item Feature transfer: reweight/select the features 
  to reduce the distance between 
  the source and the target domains;
\item Semisupervised learning;
\item Use the source model as a prior for the target one.
\end{compactitem}
Markov logic networks (MLN) and
inductive logic programming (ILP) 
were also mentioned, 
with no details.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MEG decoding across subjects} \\
  E. Olivetti et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Application of transfer learning (instance transfer,
\ie, importance sampling): decoding an MEG
when training and test data are from different subjects.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation and large-scale simultaneous 
  significance testing} \\
  B. Efron
\end{book}
In presence of high correlation, 
the $p$-values are still $U(0,1)$ under $H_0$, but not iid:
their sample distribution can be: 
wider (if many correlations are large and negative); 
narrower and skewed 
(if many correlations are large and positive).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{High-dimensional variable selection\\ for survival data} \\
  H. Ishwaran et al. (2010)
\end{book}
To gauge the importance of a variable 
in a random forest model,
look at (the distribution of) its minimum depth.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A factor model to analyze heterogeneity\\ in gene expression} \\
  Y. Blum et al. (2010)
\end{book}
Approaches to multiple testing often assume independence --
FAMT (factor analysis for multiple testing) 
relaxes that assumption [no details].
In R, check the \texttt{FAMT} package.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring and testing dependence\\ by correlation of distances} \\
  G.J. Sz\'ekely et al. (2008)
\end{book}
Original article on distance covariance
\[
V^2(X,Y) = \int_{\RR^{p+q}} \dfrac{
  \va{
    f_{XY}(t,s) - f_X(t) f_Y(s)
  }^2
}{
  \n{t}_p^{1+p} \n{s}_q^{1+q}
}
dt \, ds.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A non-iterative bayesian approach\\ to statistical matching} \\
  S. R\"assler (2003)
\end{book}
Statistical matching (aka ``data fusion'')
can be seen as a missing data problem,
and tackled by 
\emph{stochastic regression imputation} (NIBAS):
compute the regressions $X \sim Z$, $Y \sim Z$ 
(where $Z$ are the common variables),
$\Sigma_X$, $\Sigma_Y$, $\cor(X,Y)$ 
(use the identity if you have no information),
and sample 
$\Sigma$, 
$\beta_X$, 
$\beta_Y$, 
$X|y,\beta,\Sigma$, 
$Y|x,\beta,\Sigma$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Coherent mortality forecasting:\\ the product-ratio method\\ with functional time series} \\
  R.J. Hyndman et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Forecasts (using functional PCA,
and time series models for the coefficients 
of the principal components)
of the mortality rates of two subpopulations
(say, males and females) often diverge.
Instead, forecast the sum and the difference 
of the (log) rates 
(if the variances are equal, they are uncorrelated:
$\cov(X+Y,X-Y) = \var X - \var Y$).
For more than two populations, consider the average 
(of the log-rates) and the difference with the average.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting elections\\ with non-representative polls} \\
  W. Wand et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Non-representative polls are just stratified surveys: 
after adjustment,
they are perfectly usable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Super Learner} \\
  M.J. van der Laan et al. (2007)
\end{book}
Use \emph{cross-validation} to choose among various models.
Besides the usual models (GLM, GLMnet, GAM, etc.), 
the \texttt{SuperLearner} R package also wraps
\texttt{DSA} (deletion, substitution, addition), 
\texttt{LogicReg} (logic regression -- ``logic'', 
not ``logistic''),
\texttt{polspline} (adaptive regression splines, aka MARS).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Subsemble: an ensemble method\\
  for combining subset-specific algorithms fits} \\
  S. Sapp et al. (2013)
\end{book}
\emph{Bagging} fits models on samples of the data, 
and averages them.
Instead of an unweighted average, 
one can use cross-validation to find the best weights.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian estimation supercedes the t-test} \\
  M. Meredith and S. Kruschke (2014)
\end{book}
The \texttt{BEST} package replaces the $T$ test 
with Bayesian statistics 
(with a Student $T$ distribution to allow for outliers).
\begin{verbatim}
  library(BEST)
  r <- BESTmcmc(y1,y2)
  r
  summary(r)
  plot(r)
  plotPostPred(r)
  s <- r$sigma1^2 / r$sigma2^2
  hdi(s)
  plotPost(s)
\end{verbatim}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The OpenCPU system: 
  towards a universal interface 
  for scientific computing 
  through separation of concerns} \\
  J. Ooms (2014)
\end{book}
OpenCPU is a REST interface 
(with a well-defined API, 
inviting other implementations) 
to R;
it is
stateless (function results are stored on the server, 
and a key to access them is returned to the client).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The stringdist package\\ for approximate string matching} \\
  M.P.J. van der Loo 
\end{book}
The \texttt{stringdist} package
implements many string distances: 
  Hamming (limited to same-length strings),
  longest common substring,
  Levenstein (number of insersions, deletions, substitutions),
  optimal string alignment (idem, with transpositions 
    of adjacent characters -- not a distance),
  Damerau-Levenstein (idem, but corrected 
    to make it a distance),
  $n$-gram distance ($L^1$ distance between 
    the $n$-gram count vectors),
  cosine distance (
  $1 - \langle u,v \rangle$ is not a distance on the sphere --
  but $\arccos \langle u, v \rangle$ is),
  Jaccard distance (number of common $n$-grams 
    divided by the number of $n$ grams),
  heuristics (Jaro and Jaro-Winkler distances 
    count ``matches'' and penalize ``non-matches'').


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ScagExplorer: exploring scatterplots\\ by their scagnostics} \\
  T.N. Dang and L. Wilkinson
\end{book}
To examine a large number of scatterplots,
one can summarize them with a small number of metrics
(outlying, skewed, clumpy, sparse, striated, 
convex, skinny, stringy, monotonic),
look at the resulting parallel-plot-with-violins
and/or cluster them.
The authors suggest the \emph{leader algorithm}
(start with an empty leader list 
and add a new scatter plot 
if the nearest leader is beyond 
some Euclidian distance threshold; 
only display the leaders, 
or only display the scatterplots attached to a given leader).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{New approaches in visualization\\ of categorical data: R package extracat} \\
  A. Pilh\"ofer and A. Unwin (2013)
\end{book}
If you have more than two qualitative variables, check: 
\begin{verbatim}
  # Relative multiple barchart
  extracat::rmb( ~ V1 + V2 + V2 ) 
  # Categorical parallel coordinate plot
  extracat::cpcp(d) 
  vcd::mosaic(xtabs( freq ~ V1 + V2 + V2 ))
  vcd::doubledecker( xtabs(...) )
\end{verbatim}
The \texttt{extracat::optile} function, 
used by \texttt{cpcp} to reorder 
the levels of the qualitative variables 
to reduce overplotting,
may also be of interest.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rainbow color map (still) considered harmful} \\
  D. Borland and R.M. Taylor (2007)
\end{book}
It is not perceptually uniform,
it is not naturally ordered, 
its luminance does not vary monotonically.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{RnavGraph: an R package to visualize high-dimensional data using graphs as navigational infrastructure} \\
  A. Waddell and W. Oldford (2013)
\end{book}
Not unlike ggobi: 
move from one (2D) scatterplot to another by a rotation
(in 3D if they share a variable, in 4D otherwise).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dealing with stochastic volatility in time series using the R package stochvol} \\
  G. Kastner
\end{book}
Bayesian estimation of stochastic volatility models.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parallel computing for data science,\\
  with examples in R and beyond} \\
  N. Matloff (2013)
\end{book}
This book reviews, with detailed examples, 
the main types of parallel systems,
mostly in R (clusters, multicore systems, GPUs):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \texttt{parallel::clusterApply},
  \texttt{parallel::cluster\-Apply\-LB} (with load balancing);
\item \texttt{parallel::mclapply};
\item \texttt{foreach}, \verb|%dopar%|;
\item \texttt{Rmpi} 
  (low-level programming on clusters, via message passing);
\item \texttt{Rdsm} (based on \texttt{bigmemory}): 
  shared memory (you have to manage the locks yourself);
\item OpenMP, to automatically parallelize loops in C
  (yes, it is that easy);
\item TBB (Threads building blocks): 
  thread-safe C++ containers,
  with a few parallel algorithms;
\item Cilk++: adds \texttt{spawn} 
  and \texttt{sync} keywords to C
  (I am not sure how relevant this is, 
  given that C++ now has futures);
\item \texttt{gputools}
\item Thurst: similar to TBB, for the GPU
  (it can also use OpenMP or TBB if you do not have a GPU);
\item \texttt{Rth}: to use Thrust from R;
\item OpenBLAS.
\end{compactitem}
(The author wrote \texttt{Rdsm} and \texttt{Rth}.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of programming languages\\ in economics} \\
  S.B. Aruoba (2014)
\end{book}
Julia, Python+Numba, Matlab+MEX, Mathematica 
have speeds comparable to C++/Fortran/Java,
but functional languages (OCaml, Haskell, Scala) 
are worth a look.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A reliable effective terascale\\ linear learning system}\\
  A. Agarwal et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Most machine learning algorithms can be implemented on a 
\emph{data-centric computing platform}
such as Hadoop using the \emph{AllReduce} operation 
(arrange the nodes in a tree, 
use it to compute sums or weighted averages, 
\ie, to aggregate the partial results),
which has much less overhead than MapReduce.
However, their implementation bypasses Hadoop's MapReduce
(the nodes communicate between themselves via TCP),
which may lead to reliability issues 
(do not expect the system to run for more than a few hours
without failures).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Asynchronous functional reactive programming 
  for GUIs} \\
  E. Czaplicki and S. Chong (2013) 
\end{book}
FRP is programming with signals, \ie, time-changing values
(in Haskell, that would be a functor, 
\texttt{fmap: (a$\rightarrow$b)$\rightarrow$\,Signal\,a\,$\rightarrow$\,Signal\,b}). 
Most FRP languages assume the signal changes continuously,
and sample from it.
Elm (a Haskell DSL)
works with discrete signals (asynchronous FRP) 
and therefore avoids needless recomputations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MDF} \\
  \url{https://github.com/ahlmss/mdf} (2013)
\end{book}
% SecDB graph
Data-flow programming in Python.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Clash of the lambdas} \\
  A. Biboudis et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Lambdas in Java 8 are mature and efficient.
Scala is inefficient, e.g., because of garbage collection or
boxing/unboxing 
(collections are not specialized for primitive types).
Optimizing frameworks (ScalaBlitz) help,
but not enough: the gap is reduced 
from 2 orders of magnitude to 1.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph drawing in Tikz} \\
  T. Tantau (2013)
\end{book}
There are now graph layout algorithms in Tikz,
implemented in Lua.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Managing schema evolution\\ in NoSQL data stores} \\
  S. Scherzinger et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Schema migration in schema-less databases (NoSQL) 
are often ad hoc (dangerous, untested) data transformations,
either eager (convert everything in one go) 
or lazy (old and new entries cohabit, 
they are only converted when accessed).
A system-agnostic data migration language would be useful.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Locality-sensitive hashing scheme\\ 
  based on $p$-stable distributions} \\
  M. Datar et al. (2004)
\end{book}
To solve the $k$-nearest neighbour problem probabilistically,
in high dimension: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Find hash functions with a higher probability of collision when the points are close: 
\begin{align*}
  \text{distance} \leq r_1 & \implies P[ \text{collision} ] \geq p_1 \\
  \text{distance} \geq r_2 & \implies P[ \text{collision} ] \leq p_2;
\end{align*}
\item Apply many such hash functions to the data 
and examine the points with the most collisions.
\end{compactitem}

Such hash functions can be obtained 
by projecting the points in a random direction 
and binning them: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Choose $r>0$;
\item Pick $b \sim U(0,1)$ and
  $a \sim F$, where $F$ is an $n$-dimensional 
  $p$-stable distribution;
\item Let $h_{a,b}(v) = 
  \left\lfloor 
  \dfrac{ a \cdot v + b }{r} 
  \right\rfloor$.
\end{compactitem}
A distribution $F$ is $p$-stable if, 
for all $n$, for all $v_1,\dots,v_n \in \RR$,
if $X_1,\dots,X_n,X \sim F$ are iid,
then $\sum_i v_i X_n$ and 
$ ( \sum \va{v_i}^p )^{1/p} X $
have the same distribution.
The Cauchy distribution is $1$-stable; 
the Gaussian distribution is $2$-stable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The development of hyperdual numbers\\ for exact second-derivative calculations} \\
  J.A. Fike and J.J. Alonso (2011)
\end{book}
The first derivative of a function $f$ 
is often approximated using
\begin{align*}
  f'(x) &= \dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} + O(h) \\
  f'(x) &= \dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x-h)}{2h} + O(h^2),
\end{align*}
but this is not numerically stable: 
there is a \emph{subtractive cancellation error} problem,
which prevents $h$ from being too small 
(often, $10^{-4}$ to $10^{-6}$ is the best we can do). 
The complex step approximation
\[ f'(x) = \dfrac{\im f(x+ih)}{h} + O(h^2) \]
addresses this problem: $h$ can be very small ($10^{-14}$).
But the problem reappears if you want the second derivative.

Instead of complex numbers, 
one can use other extensions of $\RR$, 
such as the dual numbers $\RR[\epsilon]/(\epsilon^2)$,
\[ f(x+\epsilon) = f(x) + f'(x) \epsilon \]
or the \emph{hyperdual numbers},
$\RR[\epsilon,\eta]/(\epsilon^2,\eta^2,\epsilon^2\eta^2)$,
\[
  f( x + \epsilon + \eta ) = 
  f(x) + f'(x) \epsilon + f'(x) \eta + f''(x) \epsilon\eta.
\]
The derivatives are exact,
and there is no step size to choose.

That is easy to implement 
in languages with operator overloading (C++, Julia), 
but 10 times slower than the difference method.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Unbounded spigot algorithms\\ for the digits of pi} \\
  J. Gibbons (2005)
\end{book}
Good job interview question: 
implement a streaming algorithm to compute 
the digits of $\pi$,
ad infinitum, using 
\[ 
\pi = \sum_{ n \geq 0 }
\dfrac{
  (n!)^2 2^{n+1}
}{
  (2n+1)!
}.
\]
The formula comes from 
\[ 
\dfrac\pi4 = 
\arctan 1 = 
\int_0^1 \dfrac{dx}{1+x^2} = 
\sum \dfrac{(-1)^n}{2n+1}.
\]
The series converges very slowly, 
but it is alternating, 
and taking the average of two consecutive partial sums 
gives a faster converging series --
but it is still alternating, 
so we can use the same trick, again and again.
This is the \emph{Euler convergence accelation transform}.

This transformation can also be motivated as follows: 
given 
\[ f(x) = \sum_{n \geq 0} (-1)^n a_n x^n, \]
show that 
\[ 
g(y) = f\left( \dfrac{y}{1-y}\right) 
     = \sum_{n\geq 0} (-1)^n \Delta^n a_0 y^n,
\]
where $\Delta a$ is the forward difference of $a$,
and compute $g(\tfrac12) = f(1).$

Here is a straightforward algorithm: 
find an upper and lower bound on the error, 
\[ s_n - \ell_n \leq \pi \leq s_n + u_n, \]
and use it to infer if the $k$th decimal of $s_n$
is that of $\pi$ -- for this, 
you need arbitrary precision rationals.
% The algorithm presented is slightly different.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Computing extremely accurate quantiles\\ using $t$-digests} \\
  T. Dunning
\end{book}
To compute a quantile of a datastream, approximately, one can:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sample from it, online, 
  and take the quantile of the sample -- 
  for extreme quantiles, you only need to keep 
  a sample of the tails;
\item Bin the data into a set of centroids and counts, 
  and collapse the centroids when there are too many 
  of them ($t$-digest). 
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The PH-tree -- a space-efficient storage structure and multi-dimensional index} \\
  T. Z\"aschke et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Indexes for spacial data are often 
KD-trees (split the space in half, 
along an axis-aligned hyperplace, at each node),
quad-trees (split the space in $2^n$ at each node)
or R-trees (trees of bounding boxes).
One can also use \emph{prefix trees} (tries),
by interleaving the different dimensions 
into a single bitstring. There is no need for rebalancing.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nepotism and equity prices} \\
  G. Zhu (2014)
\end{book}
Nepotism (several family members being directors 
in the same company -- 
in the US, this has to be reported in text form; 
the information can be extracted by looking 
for keywords such as ``brother'', ``father'', etc., 
used to build a social network 
and compute the usual network metrics)
is detrimental to firm value,
except when there is a large outside block holder.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Textual classification of SEC comment letters} \\
  J.P. Ryans (2014)
\end{book}
SEC comment letters are a good predictor 
of future negative abnormal returns 
(use a naive Bayes classifier),
especially if they have been accessed 
by many investors (number of downloads).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient online summarization\\ of microblogging streams} \\
  A. Alariu (2014)
\end{book}
A \emph{word graph} (the nodes are words,
the edges are bigrams, with weights)
can be used to summarize a corpus 
(look for the highest-weight path).
One can also use trigrams and let the weights decay (multiply by $1-\epsilon$ at each step).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Looking for risk in words:\\ a narrative approach to measuring\\ the pricing implications of finance constraints} \\
  M.M.M. Buehlmaier and T.M. Whited (2014)
\end{book}
Financially constrained firms 
(those having difficulty raising funds)
can be identified from the text in the MD\&A section 
of their 10-K filing, 
using the naive Bayes algorithm
(building the training set is problematic, though).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Finance constraints} \\
  M.M.M. Buehlmaier (2014)
\end{book}
One can estimate how financially constrained a company is 
by the textual analysis of its regulatory filings, using a Naive Bayes classifier.
The training sets are questionable: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A small, subjective manually-generated training set;
\item Companies identified by a set of words known 
  to be associated with delays resulting 
  from the difficulty of issuing new equity;
\item Idem for debt.
\end{compactitem}
One could also check for the effect 
of pairs, triplets, quadriplets of words.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The price of silence: when no one\\ asks questions during conference calls} \\
  S. Chen et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The number of questions asked by investors 
in earnings-related conference calls 
measures the quality of a firm's communication.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{News-implied volatility and disaster concerns} \\
  A. Manela and A. Moreira (2013)
\end{book}
Use SVM regression to predict the VIX 
from front-page articles of the Wall Street Journal.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio selection\\ with options and transaction} \\
  S. Malamud (2014)
\end{book}
% Interesting but very technical
Introducing options in your investment universe 
dramatically increases the number of assets, 
and the variance matrix becomes untractable -- and, 
option traders would argue, largely irrelevant.
Instead of a mean-variance portfolio, 
one can look for greek-efficient portfolios -- 
with a high value for the greeks you want, 
and a small value for those you do not.
[I would still use the mean-variance framework, 
and include the greeks as constraints, 
setting those we want to $\pm1$, 
and those we do not to 0.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Volatility and directional\\ information-based trading in options} 
  %\\ Anonymous (2014)
\end{book}
Generalization of the PIN model to option markets, 
accounting for two types of information, direction and volatility.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Why does the option to stock volume ratio predict stock returns?
} \\
  L. Ge
\end{book}
Most option-implied measures only use the price: 
the volume, normalized by the stock volume, 
and decomposed into call/put, buy/sell, open/close, 
is also a predictor of future returns.
Opening and closing of long call positions 
are more informative than synthetic shorts.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CDS inferred stock volatility} \\
  B. Guo (2014)
\end{book}
Since both CDSs and OTM puts can be used 
to protect against downside risk, 
one can define a CDS-implied volatility.
It is different from the option-implied one, 
and is a better predictor of future stock volatility.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Option listing and information asymmetry} \\
  J. Hu (2014)
\end{book}
Option listing improves market efficiency and reduces information asymmetry.
(The study uses propensity score matching.)


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock market ambiguity\\ and the equity premium} \\
  A. Kagkadis (2014)
\end{book}
The dispersion of volume-weighted strike prices 
is a measure of market ambiguity
(high ambiguity leads to high hedging demand 
with different beliefs, 
which leads to a higher dispersion of strikes).
It is related to, but different 
from, other option-implied quantities 
(variance risk premium (VRP), smirk slope, 
risk-neutral variance, skewness, kurtosis, 
open-interest ratio (OTM puts/ATM calls, 
a measure of hedging pressure)).
It complements the VRP.
(Analyst forecast dispersion is another measure of ambiguity.)


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wealth transfers via equity transactions} \\
  R.G. Sloan and H. You (2013)
\end{book}
The option of a company to issue new shares or buy back shares
when its stock is over- or under-priced
should be included in its valuation.
It corresponds to a wealth transfer 
from new to old investors.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Demand for crash insurance, intermediary constraints and stock return predictability} \\
  H. Chen et al. (2013)
\end{book}
New deep out-of-the-money S\&P 500 put options
are a good (negative) predictor of future (monthly) returns.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An option to cheat: an application of option theory to realize flipping in underpricing} \\
  J. Stojkovic (2013)
\end{book}
IPO flipping is the sale of an (undervalued) IPO stock
on day one if/when the price jumps.
It can be modeled as a perpetual American call option,
with the IPO price as strike.
The option price can be decomposed into 
the potential immediate profit,
and the time value of waiting.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Does VIX truly measure return volatility?} \\
  K.V. Chow et al. (2014)
\end{book}
% Not as clear as it should be
The current VIX computations
(price of a portfolio of OTM put or call options,
with weights inversely proportional 
to the square of the strike)
are not really model-free
(they assume a diffusion).
It is a biased estimator of expected volatility, 
but it can be corrected.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Merton's model, credit risk and volatility skews} \\
  J. Hull et al. (2004)
\end{book}
Merton's model assess the credit risk of a company
by considering its equity as an option on the assets --
the spot is the value of the assets, 
the strike is the value of the debt,
the expiry its maturity.
Options on equity are therefore 
\emph{compound options} 
on the assets -- this explains the skew.
One can use the option market to estimate 
the model parameters and compare the results 
with CDS spreads.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Where would the EUR/CHF exchange rate be without the SNB's\\ minimum exchange rate policy?} \\
  M. Hanke et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Capped exchange rates (e.g., the floor on EUR/CHF)
can be modeled as American options
on the latent (uncapped) FX.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Option-implied volatility measures\\ and stock return predictability } \\
  X. Fu et al.
\end{book}
The following option-derived quantities 
have some predictive power
on future stock returns:
\begin{align*}
  % Call-put implied volatility spread
  \text{IVSpread} &= 
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Call}} - 
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Put}} \\
  % Implied volatility skew
  \text{IVSkew} &= 
  \text{IV}_{\text{OTM,\,Put}}  -
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Call}} \\
  % Above-minus-below
  \text{AMB} &= 
  \tfrac12 ( 
  \text{IV}_{\text{ITM,\,Put}} +
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Call}} 
  ) - \\
  &\hphantom{{}={}}
  \tfrac12 ( 
  \text{IV}_{\text{ITM,\,Call}} +
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Put}} 
  ) \\
  % Call out-minus-at
  \text{COMA} &= 
  \text{IV}_{\text{OTM,\,Call}} - 
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Call}} \\
  % Put out-minus-at
  \text{POMA} &= 
  \text{IV}_{\text{OTM,\,Put}} - 
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Put}} \\
  % Realized-implied volatility spread
  \text{RVIV} &= 
  \text{RV} - \tfrac12(
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Put}} +
  \text{IV}_{\text{ATM,\,Call}}
  ).
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Jump and variance risk premia\\ in the S\&P 500} \\
  M. Neumann et al. (2014)
\end{book}
By estimating the physical  
and risk-neutral (option prices) 
SVCJ (stochastic volatility
with contemporaneous jumps in returns and variance)
model,
\begin{align*}
  ds &= \mu\, dS dt + S \sqrt V d W_1 + Z_1 dN \\
  dV &= \kappa( \theta - V) dt + 
        \sigma \sqrt V d W_2 + Z_2 dN  ,
\end{align*}
one can define the following risk premia: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Diffusive variance risk premium: 
  $\kappa^{\QQ} - \kappa^{\PP}$;
\item Price jump risk premium: 
  $\mu^{\QQ} - \mu^{\PP}$;
\item Variance jump risk premium
  $\theta^{\QQ} - \theta^{\PP}$.
\end{compactitem}
They are related to macroeconomic uncertainty.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk-adjusted option-implied moments} \\
  F. Brinkmann and O. Korn (2014)
\end{book}
The (model-free) risk-neutral density
can be computed from option prices;
the physical density (and the physical moments)
can be computed in the same way,
using physical expected discounted payoffs 
instead of prices.
Those physical prices can be computed from market prices 
and the utility function of a representative investor.
A CRRA utility function can explain 
the variance risk premium (VRP), 
\[ \text{VRP} \overset{\text{def}}{=}
  E^{\PP}[ \text{variance} ] - 
  E^{\QQ}[ \text{variance} ],
\]
but not both the VRP and the skewness risk premium.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pricing interest rate derivatives\\ and computing pathwise Greeks\\ in the extended Libor market model} \\
  T. Lee (2013)
\end{book}
There is no longer a single yield curve; 
the Libor market model (LMM) can be extended 
to this new multicurve environment.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Expected bond returns\\ and the credit risk premium} \\
  Z. Afik and S. Benninga (2014)
\end{book}
Do not confuse yield to maturity 
(computed from the promised cash flows)
and expected bond returns 
(computed from the expected cash flows, 
\ie, accounting for rating, rating transition matrix
and industry recovery rate).
The difference between the two,
the credit risk premium,
is (empirically) a simple function of rating and duration.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Asymmetry in stock returns:\\ an entropy measure} \\
  L. Jiang et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Asymmetry tests usually rely on exceedance correlations
\begin{align*}
  \rho_c^+ &= \cor( X, Y \mid X, Y \geq c ) \\
  \rho_c^- &= \cor( X, Y \mid X, Y \leq -c ).
\end{align*}
Tests based on exceedance densities
\begin{align*}
  f_c^+ (x,y) &= f(x, y, \mid x,y \geq c ) \\
  f_c^- (x,y) &= f(x, y, \mid x,y \leq -c )  
\end{align*}
and the ``entropy'' 
\[
  S_c = \tfrac12 \iint_{RR^2} \left( 
  \sqrt{ f_c^+ } - 
  \sqrt{ f_c^- } 
  \right) ^ 2  
\]
are more powerful.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to nonextensive statistical mechanics and thermodynamics} \\
  C. Tsallis et al. (2003)
\end{book}
Tsallis entropy (or $q$-entropy, or non-extensive entropy)
generalizes entropy.

The exponential function $e^x$ 
is a solution of $y' = y$; 
its inverse, $\ln x$, is additive:
$\ln(xy) = \ln x + \ln y$;
the entropy is 
\[ 
S = \sum_i p_i \ln \dfrac1{p_i} 
= \left\langle \ln \dfrac1{p_i} \right\rangle.
\]

The $q$-exponential $e_q^x$ 
is a solution of $y' = y^q$; 
its inverse, 
\[ \ln_q x = \dfrac{x^{1-q} - 1}{1-q}, \]
is pseudo-additive:
\[ \ln_q(xy) = \ln_q x + \ln_q y + (1-q) \ln_q x \ln_q y; \]
the $q$-entropy is 
\[ 
S_q = \sum_i p_i \ln_q \dfrac1{p_i} 
= \left\langle \ln_q \dfrac1{p_i} \right\rangle.
\]

The characterizations of entropy (Shannon or Khinchin
theorem) generalize to $q$-entropy, with one more term or exponent, e.g., 
\begin{align*}
  S(A+B) &= S(A) + S(B) + (1-q) S(A) S(B) 
  \text{ if } A \!\!\indep\!\! B  \\
  S(A+B) &= S(A) + S(B|A) + (1-q) S(A) S(B|A) 
\end{align*}

Tsallis entropy is related to other generalized entropies, 
e.g., 
\[ S_q^{\text{Renyi}} = \dfrac1{1-q} \ln \sum_i p_i^q = 
\dfrac{
\ln[ 1 + (1-q) S_q ]
}{1-q}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Realized coskewness of the VIX and S\&P 500 and the equity premium} \\
  Z. Liu et al. (2014)
\end{book}
In factor models, besides volatility, 
idiosyncratic volatility, 
$\beta(\text{stock},\text{market})$, 
one can also look at 
$\operatorname{coskew}(\text{stock},\text{market})$
(but it can be tricky to estimate -- 
if you have high-frequency data, 
you can try the realized skewness).
$\operatorname{Coskew}(\text{VIX},\text{S\&P 500})$
is related to, 
but different from,
the variance risk premium (VRP).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Which beta is best? On the information content of option-implied beta} \\
  R. Baule et al. (2014)
\end{book}
One can define many option-implied betas: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Using implied volatility (IV) 
  and historical correlation;
\item Using IV and assuming constant correlation;
\item Using IV and assuming some specific 
  option-pricing model;
\item Using IV and cross-correlation derivatives 
  (if available);
\item Using the implied moments: 
  if $ y = \alpha + \beta x + \epsilon$, 
  then 
  \[ \beta = 
  \sqrt{1 - \dfrac{\var\epsilon}{\var y}} 
  \left( \dfrac{\var y}{\var\epsilon} \right)^{\!\!1/2} 
  \!\!\! = 
  \left( 
  \dfrac{\operatorname{Skew} y}{\operatorname{Skew}\epsilon}
  \right)^{\!\!1/3}
  \]
\end{compactitem}
Option-implied betas based on the variance,
and those that ensure that the market beta is one 
(for this, jointly estimate the betas for all the stocks)
are more accurate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extremal dependence and contagion} \\
  R. Fry-McKibbin and C.Y.L. Hsiao (2014)
\end{book}
During crises, not only do the moments change
(returns drop, volatility jumps),
so do the \emph{comoments}:
for instance, 
$\cor(\text{ret},\text{vol})$
changes sign, 
from negative (volatility skew) in normal times,
to positive (volatility smile) in crises.
One can devise \emph{contagion tests}
by looking at 
$\cor( \text{ret}_i, \text{vol}_j)$,
$\cor( \text{ret}_i, \text{skew}_j)$,
$\cor( \text{vol}_i, \text{vol}_j)$.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Modelling dependence structure\\ and forecasting portfolio value-at-risk\\ with dynamic copulas} \\
  M. Cerrato et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Look at the asymmetric dependence, e.g., 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Threshold correlation,
  \[ \rho^- = \cor( X_1, X_2 \mid X_1 \leq x_1, X_2 \leq x_2 ), \]
  where $x_1$, $x_2$ are the $p$-quantiles of $X_1$ and $X_2$,
  and 
  \[ \rho^+ = \cor( X_1, X_2 \mid X_1 \geq x_1, X_2 \geq x_2 ), \]
  where $x_1$, $x_2$ are the $(1-p)$-quantiles;  
\item Quantile dependence
  \[ \lambda ^p = P[ X_1 \leq x_1 \mid X_2 \leq x_2 ] \] 
  or 
  \[ \lambda ^p = P[ X_1 \geq x_1 \mid X_2 \geq x_2 ] ; \] 
\item Tail dependence 
  \[ \lambda^{\text{U}} = \lim_{p\to1} \lambda^p \]
  \[ \lambda^{\text{L}} = \lim_{p\to0} \lambda^p \]
\end{compactitem}
between the long and short portfolios 
built from the following signals
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\beta(\text{stock}, \text{market})$;
\item $\operatorname{coskew}(\text{stock}, \text{market}, \text{market})$;
\item $\operatorname{cokurt}(\text{stock}, \text{market}, \text{market}, \text{market})$.
\end{compactitem}
To estimate the VaR, 
use the GAS (generalized autoregressive score) model.
% Not enough details.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Asymmetry in tail dependence\\ of equity portfolios} \\
  E. Jondeau
\end{book}
To estimate tail dependence, prefer parametric methods
(unless you really have a lot of data),
e.g., using the multivariate non-central $t$ distribution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cornish-Fisher versus Gramm-Charlier:\\ an appraisal} \\
  D. Maillard (2014)
\end{book}
Cornish-Fisher is a transformation 
of a Gaussian random variable
\[ Y = X + (X^2-1) \dfrac{S}{6} + (X^3-3X) \dfrac{K}{24} 
- ( 2X^3-5X) \dfrac{S^2}{36}; \]
if $S$ and $K$ are small, 
they are close to the skewness and excess kurtosis of $Y$.

Gram-Charlier is a transformation 
of the Gaussian density $\phi$: 
\begin{align*}
  \psi(x) &= \phi(x) \left( 
    1 + \dfrac S6 H_3(x) + \dfrac K{24} H_4(x)
  \right) \\
  H_n(x) &= (-1)^n e^{-x^2/2} \dfrac{d^n}{d x^n} e^{-x^2/2}
\end{align*}
(this time, 
$S$ and $K$ are really the skewness and excess kurtosis).
The domain of valitidy of the Cornish-Fisher transformation 
(the set of skewness-kurtosis pairs attainable)
is much larger.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Expansion of probability density functions\\ as a sum of gamma densities\\ with applications in risk theory} \\
  N.L. Bowers (1966)
\end{book}
The idea behind the Gram-Charlier expansion of a pdf,
\[ p(x) = \phi(x) + \sum_{n\geq1} \cdots = 
\sum_{n \geq 0} a_n H_n(x) \phi(x), \]
where the $H_n$ are orthogonal polynomials 
wrt the Gaussian distribution $\phi$,
can be generalized to other reference distributions, 
e.g., for positive random variables, 
the Gamma distribution (and Laguerre polynomials).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust series expansions\\ for probability density estimation} \\
  M. Welling
\end{book}
Use robust moments 
\[ \left\langle 
  (\alpha x)^n e^{\frac12 (1 - \alpha^2) x^2} 
\right\rangle, \qquad 1 \leq \alpha^2 \leq 2, 
\]
to define robust Gram-Charlier or Hedgeworth expansions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Joining risks and rewards} \\
  H.J. Stein (2014)
\end{book}
When you need both the $\PP$ and $\QQ$ measures 
(e.g., for risk and pricing),
use the same samples, but with different weights
(\ie, use importance sampling,
as when pricing deep OTM options).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identifying jumps in financial assets:\\ comparison between nonparametric jump tests} \\
  A.M. Dumitru and G. Urga (2011)
\end{book}
Review of many jump estimators or tests, e.g., those based on 
\begin{align*}
  &\text{BV} = \sum \va{ r_t r_{t-1} } \\
  & \sum \min( r_t, r_{t-1} )^2 \\
  & \sum \operatorname{median}( r_t, r_{t-1}, r_{t-2} ) ^2 \\
  & \max \dfrac{ \va{ r_t} }{ \text{BV} } \\
  & \dfrac{\displaystyle
    \sum_{ t \equiv 0 \ (\text{mod } \delta ) }
    \va{
      r_t + \cdots + r_{t+\delta-1}
    } ^m
    }{ 
      \sum \va{ r_t }^{ m \mathstrut }
    } \\
  &\sum R_t - r_t \text{ (ratio vs log-returns), etc. }
\end{align*}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Monitoring housing markets\\ for episodes of exhuberance} \\
  E. Pavlidis et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The ADF test can be generalized to distinguish between 
integrated $I(1)$ and explosive behaviours:
\[
  \Delta y_t = \alpha + \beta y_{t-1} + 
  \sum_{i=1}^k \gamma_i \Delta y _{t-i} + \epsilon,
  \quad t \in [T_1,T_2] 
\]
\begin{align*}
  \text{ADF}_{T_1T_2} &= \dfrac{\beta_{T_1 T_2}}{ \sigma(\beta_{T_1 T_2} ) } 
  \\
  \text{SADF}_{T_1} &= \sup_{ T_2 \geq T_1 } \text{ADF}_{T_1T_2} 
  && \text{expanding window} \\
  \text{GSADF}_w &= 
  \!\!\! \sup_{ T_2 - T_1 \geq w } \!\!\!
  \text{ADF}_{T_1 T_2}
  && \text{window size at least $w$.}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A compound multifractal model for high-frequency asset returns} \\
  E.M. Aldrich et al. (2014)
\end{book}
High-frequency returns 
(E mini S\&P 500 futures contract),
in trade space,
away from prescheduled news announcements,
are Gaussian.
Intertrade duration can be modeled
with an exponential distribution,
conditional on a hidden state
($k$ binary state variables, 
with varying degrees of persistence).
\begin{align*}
d_t &\sim \text{Exp}\left( \lambda \prod_i M_{it} \right) \\
M_{i,t+1} &= \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  M_{it} & \text{with probability } 1 - p_i \\
  2 - M_{it} & \text{with probability } p_i
\end{array}
\right. \\
M_{i0} &= m_0 \in (0,2)
\end{align*}
A truncated version of this Markov 
switching multifractal duration (MSMF) model,
$\min( d_t, d_t^0)$, 
where $d_t^0 \sim \text{Exp}(\lambda^0)$,
gives a better fit.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{When to sell Apple and the Nasdaq? Trading bubbles with a stochastic disorder model} \\
  A. Shiryaev et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Consider a geometric brownian motion 
with a change point in the trend, 
\[ 
  d S_t = ( 
    \mu_1 \mathbf 1_{t < \theta} + 
    \mu_2 \mathbf 1_{t \geq \theta}
  ) S_t dt + 
  \sigma dB_t
\]
(the breakpoint, $\theta$, is called a ``moment of disorder'' in quality control).
Close a long (resp. short( position at 
\begin{align*}
  \tau_{\text{long}} &= 
  \argmax_{\substack{
    \tau \leq T \\
    \tau \text{ stopping time}
  }} E[ S_\tau ] \\
  \tau_{\text{long}} &= 
  \argmin_{\substack{
    \tau \leq T \\
    \tau \text{ stopping time}
  }} E[ S_\tau ].
\end{align*}
It can be estimated explicitly.
% \begin{align*}
%   \tau &= \inf \{ t \geq 0 \ :\ \psi_t \geq a(t) \} \\
%   \psi_t &= \dfrac1T e^{ - \mu X_t - \mu^2 \frac{t^2}2 }
%   \int_0^t e^{ 
%     \mu X_s - \mu^2 \frac{ s^2 }2
%   } ds \\
%   X_t &= \dfrac1\sigma \log \dfrac{ S_t }{ S_0 } - 
%   (\mu_1 - \tfrac12 \sigma^2) t \\
%   \mu &= \dfrac{ \mu_1 - \mu_2 }{ \sigma } \\
%   a(t) &= \,? \\ % Decreasing, a(T) = 0
%   \sigma^2 &= \dfrac1{(n-1) \Delta t} 
%   \sum_k ( \xi_k - \bar\xi )^2 \\
%   \xi_k &= \log \dfrac{ P_k }{ P_{k-1} } \\
%   P_k &= S_{ k \cdot \Delta t } \\ 
%   \bar xi &= \dfrac1n \sum \xi_k  \\
%   n &= T / \Delta t \\
%   \mu_1 &= \dfrac{ \bar\xi }{ \Delta t } + \tfrac12 \sigma^2 \\
%   \mu_2 &= - \mu_1 \text { or } \mu_2 = - 2 \mu_1.
% \end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Empirical asset pricing: Eugene Fama,\\ Lars Peter Hansen and Robert Shiller} \\
  J.Y. Campbell (2014)
\end{book}
Around the notion of stochastic discount factor.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The systematic risk of private equity} \\
  A. Buchnet and R. Stucke (2014)
\end{book}
To estimate the risk of a private equity fund, 
model its dividend payments as a stochastic process.
\begin{align*}
  \text{Dividend yield} &= 1 - e^{-\delta_t} \\
  \delta_t &= \delta_0 + z_t t \\
  z_t & \sim N(0,\sigma)
\end{align*}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The valuation of M\&A targets\\ by relative indifference pricing} \\
  C. Mauch and S. Rostek (2013)
\end{book}
The \emph{indifference price}
is the price at which a given investor 
(initial wealth and utility function)
is indifferent between holding and not holding the asset.
The buying and selling (\ie, long and short) 
indifference prices are different.
Indifference pricing is relevant for incomplete markets,
e.g., for real options.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Return and risk of pairs trading\\ using a simulation-based bayesian procedure\\ for predicting stable ratios of stock prices} \\
  L.T. Gatarek et al. (2014)
\end{book}
When testing for cointegration, 
only the subspace of cointegration relations is well-defined.
To single out a cointegration relation, 
some ``normalization'' is needed,
e.g., 
$\beta = \begin{pmatrix}\beta_1 \\ \beta_2\end{pmatrix} = 
\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ \beta_2\end{pmatrix}$,
or $\beta' \beta = I$, \ie, 
$\beta = 
\begin{pmatrix}\beta_1 \\ \sqrt{1-\beta_1^2} \end{pmatrix}$
(only the leverage changes).
There are several rules to choose the size of the position on the spread 
$s_t = \beta_1 y_{1t} + \beta_2 y_{2t}$,
e.g., $-s_t$, 
$\operatorname{sign} E_t[\Delta s_{t+1}]$
or 
$
E_t[\Delta s_{t+1} ] \times
\operatorname{sign} E_t[\Delta s_{t+1} ]$
or a buy-and-hold strategy.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial contagion risk\\ and the stochastic discount factor} \\
  L. Piccotti (2014)
\end{book}
Contagion risk (in the financial sector) can be estimated as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with bank returns, $r_{it}$;
\item Remove common risk factors (e.g., Fama-French,
  estimated with a Kalman filter,
  assuming that the betas are random walks),
  and only look at the residuals $e_{it}$;
\item Regress those residuals against the sector residuals,
  with the bank of interest removed,
  $e_{it} = \beta_{it} e_{\hat\imath t} + \epsilon_{it}$;
\item Decompose the variance of the sector excess returns 
  $\var \sum_i w_i e_{it}$
  into a contagion contribution
  (the $\beta_{it}$, \ie, the off-diagonal elements 
  of $\var e_{\cdot t}$)
  and a residual term.
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Explosive bubble modelling\\ by noncausal process} \\
  C. Gouriéroux and J.M. Zakoian (2013)
\end{book}
Instead of a causal linear autoregressive process
\[ y_{t+1} = \rho y_t + \epsilon_{t+1}, 
   \qquad \epsilon_t \ \text{iid} 
\]
one can try to model financial time series 
with a \emph{non-causal} AR process 
\[ y_{t} = \rho y_{t+1} + \epsilon_{t+1}, 
   \qquad \epsilon_t \ \text{iid, Cauchy.} 
\]
[An AR(1) model is not convincing:
try to add terms for 1-day, 1-week, 1-month, 1-year, etc.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic areas of diffusions\\ and applications in risk theory} \\
  Z. Cui
\end{book}
The area swept by a stochastic process 
below some threshold can be used to model default.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extending time-changed L\'evy asset models through multivariate subordinators} \\
  E. Luciano and P. Semeraro (2007)
\end{book}
To construct a multivariate L\'evy process 
from a Gaussian one,
use a multivatiate subordinator, not a univariate one.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FARVaR} \\
  C.X. Cai et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To compute the value-at-risk,
estimate the intraday density of returns
(with a kernel estimator)
and model the evolution of this density,
from day to day,
as a \emph{functional autoregressive} (FAR) process.

%\nextbook
%\begin{book}
%  \emph{Risk-adjusted time series momentum} \\
%  S. Malamud
%\end{book}
%Momentum can be defined with risk-adjusted returns, rather than raw returns.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk-adjusted time series momentum} \\
  M. Dudler et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Define risk-adjusted momentum as 
\( \displaystyle
\sum \dfrac{
  \text{log-returns}_t
}{
  \text{volatility}_t
}.
\)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A Market-based funding liquidity measure} \\
  Z. Chen and A. Lu (2014)
\end{book}
The BAB (betting against beta) factor is the spread between
(leveraged) low-beta stocks and (deleveraged) high-beta stocks.
Its performance (low beta anomaly) can be explained 
by the inability of many investors to leverage their portfolio.
But the beta is not a measure of funding liquidity: 
instead, one can combine size, idiosyncratic volatility, 
institutional ownership and analyst coverage -- the BAB premium
is higher for high-margin stocks.
Appendix A lists 14 funding liquidity proxies.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Corporate news releases and equity vesting} \\
  A. Edmans et al. (2014)
\end{book}
CEOs time the release of corporate news to match their vesting equity.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Does liquidity beta predict mutual-fund alpha?} \\
  X. Dong et al. (2014)
\end{book}
The outperformance of a fund with a liquidity beta is not due to the liquidity premium,
but to more trading opportunities in liquid times.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Policy announcements in FX markets} \\
  P. Mueller et al (2014)
\end{book}
Interest rate is a good FX investment signal,
but only on scheduled FOMC announcement days.
This can be explained by an agent model, 
in which agents learn the announcement effects in a Bayesian fashion.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Alliances and return predictability} \\
  J. Cao (2014)
\end{book}
Use alliance partners in your pairs trading strategy.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Post-split drift and post-earnings announcement drift: one anomaly or two?} \\
  L. Fengfei (2014)
\end{book}
Both stock splits and earnings announcements have a lasting effect on future returns, 
but the two phenomena may be distinct: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The effects are real;
\item They have the same duration, but
\item A trading strategy using both outperforms one using only one.
\end{compactitem}
[The conclusion is incorrect: the fact that the trading strategy performs better 
could come from the lower noise in the combined signal.]


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{International funding illiquidity} \\
  A. Vedolin (2014)
\end{book}
Country-specific funding illiquidity 
can be measured by the difference between 
market bond prices and theoretical bond prices 
(using the Svensson, Nelson-Siegel, or cubic spline model): 
large discrepancies mean that arbitrageurs 
could not profit from the arbitrage opportunity.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Can information be locked-up? Informed trading ahead of macro-news announcements} \\
  Y. Tang (2014)
\end{book}
US macroeconomic announcements are often 
pre-released to the media under embargo agreements (lockup).
Evidence (abnormal returns, order flow imbalance, 
from high-frequency data on index futures and ETFs, 
in the 30 minutes preceding the announcement)
suggests that, for the FOMC (Federal Open Market Committee), 
this embargo is routinely breached -- other government 
agencies manage to enforce it, though.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{General purpose technologies,\\
   international technology diffusion,\\
   and the cross section of stock returns} \\
  W. Yang (2014)
\end{book}
Patents can be classified as general-purpose 
(computers, telecom, electronics)
or special-purpose (chemical, medical, mechanical, etc.)
The difference between the growth rate of general- 
and special-purpose patents 
is a non-diversifiable risk factors, 
that can be added to factor models (Fama-French, etc.)


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Macro disagreement and the cross-section\\ of stock returns} \\
  W. Li (2014)
\end{book}
High-beta stocks are more prone to speculative mispricing, 
and high dispersion (in market forecasts) 
leads to lower returns.
This effect also exists for macroeconomic factors (industrial growth, inflation, etc.)


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Liquidity risk and expected stock returns} \\
  L. P\'astor and R.F. Stambauch (2003)
\end{book}
Stock liquidity can be defined as the volume coefficient
in the regression 
\[
\text{return}_{t+1} \sim 
\text{return}_t + \operatorname{sign}( \text{return}_t )
\text{volume}_t.
\]
The market liquidity is the equal-weighted average 
of the stock liquidities.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{In search of distress risk} \\
  J.Y. Campbell et al. (2008)
\end{book}
Yet another bankruptcy model.
[Why not a survival model? 
They already look at various horizons
and the distance to default from Merton's model.]


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The world price of political uncertainty} \\
  J. Brogaard et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Global political uncertainty
(data from \href{http://www.policyuncertainty.com/}{www.policy\-uncertainty.com}
affects the implied cost of capital of 
countries integrated with the global economy;
local political uncertainty has the opposite effect 
(it can be defined as a binary variable 
indicating elections in a given year -- 
data from the World Bank database of political institutions).
One can also use text data to measure political uncertainty: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Proportion of articles about economic uncertainty;
\item Residual of the regression of 
  the domestic proportion against the global one.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A leverage-based measure\\ of financial instability} \\
  A. Tepper and K.J. Borowiecki (2014)
\end{book}
Credit constraints, margin requirements 
and leveraged investors (their demand curve can become
upward-sloping -- to estimate it, 
assume they are optimistic and reinvest everything they gain,
as much as possible)
can make markets unstable.
The stability condition 
(for a 4-agent model: 
leveraged investors, unleveraged investors, 
bank, central bank) can be written 
explicitly. % Complicated expression / market size <= 1


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Aggregate short interest\\ and return predictability} \\
  D.E. Rapach et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Detrended aggregate short interest helps predict 
future market returns.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Predictive systems under economic constraints} \\
  M. Bonelli et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Expected returns are not constant and can be modeled as 
\begin{align*}
  r_{t+1} &= \mu_t + u_{t+1} \\
  x_{t+1} &= (1-\lambda) x_\infty + \lambda x_t + w_{t+1} \\
  \mu_{t+1} &= (1-\beta) \mu_\infty + \beta \mu_t + v_{t+1} \\
  \Sigma & = \var ( u_t, v_t, w_t )'.
\end{align*}
Since the expected returns $\mu_t$ are unlikely to be negative, one can add this constraint, 
either with a Bayesian prior,
or by reparametrizing the model, e.g.,
by using a square root process for $\mu_t$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model uncertainty and expected return proxies} \\
  C. J\"ackel (2013)
\end{book}
The implied cost of capital 
(internal rate of return in a dividend discount model -- 
if you know the future dividends)
is often used as a proxy for expected returns,
but it is as imprecise.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Predicting the spread of financial innovations: an epidemiologic approach} \\
  I. Hull (2013)
\end{book}
The spread of financial innovations 
(new products: CDS, MBS, ABS, CDO, ETF, etc.)
can be modeled with the SIR model. 
A 2-host SIR model (banks and creditors) 
can help monitor solvency problems.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk vs anomaly:\\ a new methodology applied to accruals} \\
  J. Ohlson and P. Bilinski
\end{book}
Low accruals increase/decrease 
the probability of large positive/negative returns.
(The ``new methodology'' is logistic regression...)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Strategic allocation\\ to commodity factor premiums} \\
  D. Blitz and W. de Groot (2013)
\end{book}
Consider tercile commodity portfolios, for the following factors: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Momentum (past 12-month return);
\item Carry (annualized nearby future price / next nearby future price);
\item Volatility (of daily returns, over the past 3 years)
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The cross-sectional variation\\ of volatility risk premia} \\
  A. Gonz\'alez-Urteaga and G. Rubio (2014)
\end{book}
Instead of the index VRP (realized vol, minus model-free implied vol (MFIV)),
look at the stock-specific one,
and $\beta(
  \text{VRP}_{\text{stock}} \sim 
  \text{VRP}_{\text{index}} 
)$.  


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Convenient liquidity measure\\ for financial markets} \\
  O. Danyliv et al.
\end{book}
There are already many definitions of liquidity: 
bid-ask spread, $\va{\text{returns}}/\text{ADV}$,
long-term return variance~/ short-term return variance,
\[
\left.
\dfrac{\text{high} - \text{low}}{ \text{low} } 
\middle/
\dfrac{\text{volume}}{\text{MCap}}
\right. .
\]

Here is one more: 
\[ 
\log \dfrac{\text{Volume} \times \text{Close}}%
           { \text{High} - \text{Low} }.
\]


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Colog asset pricing,\\ evidence from emerging markets} \\
  Y. Dranev and S. Fomkina 
\end{book}
Yet another measure of risk: 
\[ \operatorname{Colog} X = E[ X \log X] - E[X] E[\log X], \]
where $X$ measures the loss beyond some threshold.
One can also consider the colog utility: 
\[ U(x) = x - \lambda \, x \log x. \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Machines vs machines: high-frequency trading and hard information} \\
  Y. Huh (2014)
\end{book}
Hawkes's self-exciting model can be used 
to measure liquidity from high-frequency data
(\ie, how fast the intensity of the Poisson process 
reverts to its long-term value).

High-frequency trading both takes and provides liquidity,
but the balance depends on information asymmetry --
measure it by the proportion of transactions 
in the stock (say, AAPL)
caused by a change in some index (sat QQQ).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Predictive power of aggregate short interest} \\
  E.J. Yu (2014)
\end{book}
Aggregate short interest can help predict cyclical GDP changes, up to four quarters ahead.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Exployee satisfaction, labor market flexibility, 
  and stock returns around the world} \\
  A. Edmans et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Employee satisfaction only leads to higher returns 
in countries with a flexible job market 
(US, UK, but not Germany).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-scale representation\\ of high-frequency market liquidity} \\
  A. Golub et al. (2014)
\end{book}
One can define another time scale 
(besides wall clock, volume clock,
number-of-transactions clock)
by looking at price changes beyond some threshold;
time scales corresponding to different thresholds can be combined to define a multiscale market state, in
$ \{ \text{up}, \text{down} \} ^{ \sharp \text{thresholds} }$.

% For a long, exhaustive list of liquidity measures, see
% ``Measuring market liquidity, an introductory survey''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Predictability of bank stock returns\\ during the recent financial crisis} \\
  W.S. Leung et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To predict the future returns of a bank, look at: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Earnings;
\item Non-performing loans;
\item Loan-to-deposit ratio;
\item Tier-1 capital ratio;
\item Exposure to the structured finance market 
  (off-balance sheet activities, ABX AAA index);
\item Funding illiquidity risk (maturity mismatch).
\end{compactitem}
The data for US banks comes from the bank regulatory database (``FRY5C form'').


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Technical market indicators: an overview} \\
  J. Fang et al. 
\end{book}
Technical analysis does not work -- review of 93 indicators...

%\nextbook
%\begin{book}
%  \emph{Exploring macroeconomic sensitivities:
%  how investments respond to different economic environments} \\
%  A. Ilmanen et al. (2014)
%\end{book}
%Look at the Sharpe ratio of various strategies (asset classes, styles, etc.)
%in various environments (e.g., growth, inflation, 
%yield, volatility, liquidity, etc.
%% Plot( cor(strategy, risk_factor_2) ~ 
%%       cor(strategy, risk_factor_1) )

\end{multicols}

\clearpage

\begin{multicols}{2}
\begin{book}
  \emph{Discretionary accrual models\\ and the accounting process} \\
  X.G. G\'omez et al. (2000)
\end{book}
Accruals should be included in the earnings:
the cash flow alone is very noisy
(its autocorrelation is negative),
and accruals help smooth it.
However, there is too much discretion in their computation: 
only part of them is informative.
\[ 
  \text{Earnings} = 
  \text{Cash flows} +     % Noisy if used alone
  \text{Good accruals} +  % Forecast of future cash flows
  \text{Bad accruals}     % Manipulation, GAAP discretion
\]
The \emph{non-discretionary accruals} have been defined as:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Last year's accruals; 
\item The average of the previous accruals;
\item The fitted value of the following regression 
  (divide by the previous assets and fit the models for each industry$\times$year)
  \begin{align*}
    \text{Accruals} &\sim \Delta ( \text{Revenue} - \text{Receivables} ) + \text{PPE} \\
    \text{Accruals} &\sim \Delta\text{Revenue} + \text{PPE} + \text{CFO} \\
    \text{Accruals} &\sim \text{Previous short-term accruals} + {}\\
    &\hphantom{{}\sim{}} \text{Previous long-term accruals} + \Delta\text{CF}
  \end{align*}
  where
  \begin{align*}
    \text{Short-term accruals} &= 
    \Delta \bigl( ( \text{Current assets} - \text{Cash} ) -
    \\ &\hspace*{-2cm} ( \text{Current liabilities} - \text{Financing items} ) \bigr) \\
    \text{Long-term accruals} &= \\
    &\hspace*{-2cm}- \text{Depreciation not in inventory} - \\
    &\hspace*{-2cm}  \Delta \text{Allowances}. \ (?)
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Foreign ownership and real earnings management: evidence from Japan} \\
  J. Guo et al. (2014)
\end{book}
After the tightening of accounting rules in Japan 
(``big bang accounting reform'', in the late 1990s),
companies have moved from accrual earnings management 
to real earnings management.
This can be measured with the residuals 
of the following regression 
(divide by the previous assets and estimate 
for each industry$\times$year pair, 
if there are at least 15 companies)
\begin{align*}
  \text{Cash flow from operations} &\sim 
  \\ &\hspace*{-2cm} \text{Previous assets} + \text{Sales} + \Delta \text{Sales} \\
  \text{Discretionary expenses} &\sim \text{Previous assets} + \text{Sales} \\
  \text{Production costs} &\sim 
  \\ &\hspace*{-2cm} \text{Previous assets} + \text{Sales} + \Delta \text{Sales} + 
  \\ &\hspace*{-2cm} \text{previous }\Delta \text{Sales}
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
  \text{Discretionary expenses} &= \text{Advertising} + \text{R\&D} + \text{SGA} \\ 
  \text{Production costs} &= \text{COGS} + \text{Inventory}.
\end{align*}
One can also add them: 
\begin{align*}
  & \text{abnormal CFO} + 
  \text{abnormal discretionary expenses} - \\
  & \hspace*{1cm}
  \text{abnormal production costs}.
\end{align*}
Firms with more foreign ownership are less manipulative.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Performance matched\\ discretionary accrual measures} \\
  S.P. Kothari et al. (2001)
\end{book}
Discretionary accrual measures, e.g., the residuals of the following regressions
(divide by the previous assets, fit for each year$\times$industry combination)
\begin{align*}
  \text{Accruals} &\sim \Delta \text{Sales} + \text{PPE} \\ 
  \text{Accruals} &\sim \Delta ( \text{Sales} - \text{Receivables} ) + \text{PPE} \\ 
  \text{Accruals} &\sim \Delta \text{Sales} + \text{PPE} + \text{Previous ROA} \\ 
  \text{Accruals} - \text{Depreciation} \sim \Delta \text{Sales}, \hspace*{-4cm}
\end{align*}
rarely account for the relation between performance and accruals 
(a trend in performance leads to systematically positive/negative accruals).
One can use \emph{matching} (on year, industry, and previous ROA).

%%
%% 2014-10
%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%%
%% Momentum
%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Kinetic component analysis} \\
  M. L\'opez de Prado and R. Rebonato (2014)
\end{book}

The authors have rediscovered structural models.
\begin{align*}
  \text{acceleration}   && a_{n+1} &=                      a_n + \text{noise} \\
  \text{velocity}       && v_{n+1} &=       v_n +          a_n + \text{noise} \\
  \text{true price}     && p_{n+1} &= p_n + v_n + \tfrac12 a_n + \text{noise} \\
  \text{observed price} &&    q_n  &= p_n + \text{noise}
\end{align*}
(I think there is no $\tfrac12 a_n$ term in structural models, 
and R's \texttt{StructTS} function only uses a first-order Taylor expansion, 
\ie, assumes that the acceleration is zero.)

Those models can be fitted with a Kalman filter and, 
by choosing the amplitude of the noise, 
one can fine-tune the amount of smoothing.

Contrary to other smoothing methods, such as 
Fourier transform, wavelets, Hodrick-Prescott filter
or local regression (lowess), which do not behave well
at the ends of the sample, they are forward-looking,
and provide forecasts and confidence intervals.
For instance, market makers could use those forecasts 
to compute the acceptable interval for their quotes.

By looking if the acceleration is significantly different from zero,
one can decide whether to follow a ``momentum'' strategy
(strictly speaking, it should be called ``inertia'' strategy).

As usual, the article includes Python code.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Reward-risk momentum strategies using classical tempered stable distribution} \\
  J. Choi et al. (2014)
\end{book}

Momentum strategies look at past performance but, 
instead of returns, one can consider other performance measures: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item VaR, CVaR, here estimated from and ARMA(1,1)-GARCH(1,1) model
  with classical tempered stable (CTS) innovations;
\item Sharpe ratio, $\mu / \sigma$;
\item Stable tail-adjusted return ratio (STARR), $\mu / \text{CVaR}$, 
  or an additive version of it, $\mu - \text{CVaR}$;
\item Rachev ratio $ \text{CVaR}(\text{gain})/\text{CVaR}(\text{loss})$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum drawdown, recovery and momentum} \\
  J. Choi (2014)
\end{book}

The returns over the past 6 months (or 6 weeks)
can be decomposed into the sum of pre-peak,
maximum drawdown, and recovery log-returns.
In a momentum strategy, one can replace 
the past returns with various linear 
combinations of those three terms.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Persistent doubt: an examination of the performance of hedge funds} \\
  M. de la O Gonz\'alez et al. (2014)
\end{book}

Hedge fund performance seems to be persistent:
to check it, form quintile portfolios 
on various performance measures, such as
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Alpha, adjusted for everything you can think of 
  (they used developed and emerging markets, size,
  bonds, credit, FX, commodities);
\item The manipulation-proof performance measure (MPPM),
  $\Theta(A)$, \ie, the certainty-equivalent monthly return
  (of the past 2 years of returns) for an investor 
  with constant relative risk aversion (CRRA) $A=3$;
\item The excess MPPM, $\Theta_{\text{fund}}(A) - \Theta_{\text{Russell 2000}}(A)$;
\item The doubt ratio, $\text{DR} = 2 + \Theta(2) / ( \Theta(2) + \Theta(3) )$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Asymetric risks of momentum strategies} \\
  V. Dobrynskaya (2014)
\end{book}

The momentum anomaly can be explained by the CAPM with upside and downside betas:
past winners have a lower $\beta_+$ and a higher $\beta_-$.

%% Bubbles, regime switching
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian analysis of bubbles in asset prices} \\
  A. Fulop and J. Yu (2014)
\end{book}
Bubbles can be described by regime-switching models
(choose two among mean-reverting, unit root (random walk) 
and explosive root).
Structural break models are similar, but their breakpoint is deterministic.

The authors suggest to model the price/dividend ratio
(rather than the price) 
as a mean-reverting process, 
whose long-term mean follows a random walk,
with an AR coefficient $>1$ or $<1$ depending on the regime,
estimated with a particle filter.

%%
%% Algorithms
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{emcee: the MCMC hammer} \\
  D. Foreman-Mackey et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The \d{Goodman-Weare sampler}
uses the detailed balance rule,
but the candidate distribution 
is inspired by differential evolution (DE): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Consider $n$ chains, $X_1,\dots,X_n$;
\item The candidate for $X_k$ is 
  $X_j + Z(X_k - X_j)$, 
  where $Z \sim g$ is random,
  e.g., $g(z) \propto z^{-1/2} \mathbf{1}_{[a^{-1},a]}(z)$
  and $j \sim U(\llbracket 1, n \rrbracket \setminus \{k\}).$
\end{compactitem}

The algorithm can be parallelized 
(but this changes the detailed balance condition)
and is affine-invariant: 
it is not sensitive to different scales in the parameters.

\texttt{emcee} is a Python implementation.

%% Algorithms
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GraphX:\\
  a resilient distributed graph system on Spark}\\
  R.S. Xin et al. (2013)
\end{book}
In a distributed environment,
graph algorithms tend to be inefficient 
because data partitioning is often done on 
\emph{edge cuts} (vertices are assigned to machines, 
edges span machines -- 
it is a vertex-centric view of the graph).
does not preserve locality.
But graph partitioning with (random) \emph{vertex cuts}
(edges are assigned to machines, 
vertices span multiple machines -- 
an edge-centric view)
fares better.

GraphX (part of Spark) can easily implement 
the Pregel API (send-combine) 
or the Power Graph API (gather-apply-scatter).

%% Algorithms
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Extreme learning machines: a survey} \\
  G.B. Huang et al. (2011)
\end{book}
``Extreme learning machines'' are single-hidden-layer
feed-forward neural networks
in which the hidden layer is fixed  and arbitrary -- 
it seems to be another name for \emph{echo state networks}, 
which can be seen as discrete support vector machines 
(with a random, rather than universal, kernel).

%% Algorithms, Machine learning
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Variable selection using random forests} \\
  R. Genuer et al. (2012)
\end{book}
To account for groups of highly correlated 
explanatory variables,
consider 
\emph{nested random forests}, 
with an increasingly large number of variables.

%%
%% Machine learning
%%
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning the parts of objects\\
  by non-negative matrix factorization} \\
  D.D. Lee and H.S. Seung (Nature, 1999) 
\end{book}
Principal component analysis (PCA), 
vector quantization (VQ), 
nonnegative matrix factorization (NMF),
independent component analysis (ICA)
are all decomposition 
$V \approx W H$, 
with different constraints: 
orthogonal columns (rows) in $H$ ($W$) for PCA;
boolean $W$ with exactly one 1 in each row for VQ;
$W,H\geq0$ for NMF.
When used to reduce the dimension of a set of images 
(say, faces),
they give qualitatively different results: 
PCA gives eigenfaces (not faces), designed to be combined;
VQ gives prototypes (faces);
NMF gives parts of images 
(the non-negative constraints only allow 
for additive reconstruction).
On text data, the decompositions define topics:
VQ only allows a single topic per text;
PCA allows several, but these are ``relative'' topics, designed to be combined, and difficult to interpret;
the independence assumption in ICA is 
inappropriate in this context.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{When does\\
  the nonnegative matrix factorization\\
  give the correct decomposition into parts?} \\
  D. Donoho and V. Stodden 
\end{book}
The nonnegative matrix factorization (NMF) $X = A \Psi$
can be interpreted geometrically: 
the rows of $X$ are
in the simplicial cone generated by the rows of $\Psi$, 
and this cone is in the first orthant.
In particular, unless the cone is generated by rows of $X$ 
``touches'' the frontier of the orthant
(e.g., the orthant itself, 
or an ice-cream cone tangent to the orthant),
the decomposition is not unique: 
one can replace $\Psi$ with a slightly wider cone.
Separability and complete factorial sampling ensure unicity.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms\\ for nonnegative matrix factorization} \\
  D.D. Lee and H.S. Seung
\end{book}
A nonnegative matrix factorization (NMF) 
of an $n \times m$ matrix $V$
(with nonnegative entries)
is a decomposition $V \approx W H$ 
where $W$ is $n \times k$ 
and   $H$    $k \times m$,
with $k$ small,
and $W$ and $H$ have non-negative entries.
By iterating 
\begin{align*}
  H &\leftarrow H \odot (W' V) \oslash ( W' W H ) \\
  W &\leftarrow W \odot (V H') \oslash ( W H H' )
\end{align*}
(where $\odot$ and $\oslash$ are the elementwise multiplication and division),
one can find a local minimum of 
$\n{ V - VH }_2 $
(it is actually a gradient descent with an adaptive learning rate).
One can find a similar update rule 
to minimize the divergence 
$D( V \mathbin{||} WH )$, 
where \[ 
D(A \mathbin{||} B ) = 
\sum_{ij} \bigl( 
  A_{ij} \log \dfrac{ A_{ij} }{ B_{ij} } - A_{ij} + B_{ij}
\bigr).
\]

Alternating least squares (ALS) were not mentionned.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Big data in asset management} \\
  T. Roncalli (2014)
\end{book}
Machine learning methods can be applied to finance: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Lasso regression can be used for hedge fund replication, 
  or to hedge positions;
\item Nonnegative matrix factorization (NMF) can be used
  to decompose returns into factors, with positive weights
  (this is not the classical NMF: we want a decomposition
  $R = WF$, with $W \geq 0$, but no sign constraint
  on $R$ or $F$; no algorithm is mentionned, 
  but alternating least squares (ALS) should work);
\item There is no satisfactory \emph{sparse Kalman filter},
  but the model
  \begin{align*}
    y_t &= x_t' \beta_t + \epsilon_t \\
    \beta_t &= \beta_{t-1} + \eta_t
  \end{align*}
  can be estimated with a penalty, 
  as in the  Hodrick-Prescott (HP) filter,
\begin{align*}
  (\hat\beta_1,\dots,\hat\beta_n) = 
  \argmin_\beta 
  &\sum_t \n{ y_t - x_t' \beta_t }^2 + {}\\
  &\,\lambda \sum_{t,j} h(\beta_{tj} - \beta_{t-1,j}) +{}\\
  &\,\mu \sum_{t,j} h(\beta_{tj})
\end{align*}
  with $h(\epsilon) = \n{\epsilon}_1$ or 
  $h(\epsilon) = \n{\epsilon}_2^2$.
\end{compactitem}

%% Machine learning
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Finding large average submatrices\\ in high dimensional data} \\
  A.A. Shabalin (2009)
\end{book}
Biclustering algorithms look for homogeneous submatrices
(e.g., approximately equal rows, or columns, or elements,
or rank statistics in rows, or in columns, 
or elements above a threshold, or well-fit by a 2-way anova, 
etc.)
and can be seen as a form of 
\emph{unsupervised exploratory data analysis}.
The LAS model assumes that the data is constant on 
(possibly overlapping) submatrices), with additive noise.
The log-likelihood of a $k \times \ell$ submatrix
of the whole $m\times n$ matrix is 
\[ 
  - \log \left[ 
  \binom mk \binom n\ell 
  \Phi\left( - \text{average} \times \sqrt{l\ell} \right).
  \right] 
\]
To account for overlap, 
the submatrix with the best score 
is subtracted from the data, 
and the algorithm is iterated.
To find a good submatrix 
with prescribed size $k \times \ell$,
take $k$ rows at random, 
take the best $\ell$ columns, 
then the best $k$ rows, 
and iterate until convergence; 
then fine-tune $(k,\ell)$.

%%
%% Statistics
%%

%% Causality
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measures of causality in complex datasets\\
  with applications to financial data} \\
  A. Zaremba and T. Aste (2014)
\end{book}
Granger causality is a comparison of 
$P(X_t | X _{\leq t-1} )$
and 
$P(X_t | X _{\leq t-1}, Y_{\leq t-k} )$.
For a VAR model, one can use \emph{Geweke's measure},
\[
  F_{Y \to X} = \log \dfrac{
    \var [ X_t | X_{\leq t-1} ]
  }{
    \var [ X_t | X_{\leq t-1}, Y_{\leq t-k} ]
  },
\]  
or apply the kernel trick to it.

The \emph{transfer entropy} is 
\[ 
  T_{Y \to X} = 
  H( X_t | X _{\leq t-1} ) -
  H( X_t | X _{\leq t-1}, Y_{\leq t-k} )
\]  
where
\[
  H(U|V) = \sum_{uv} p_{uv} \log \dfrac{ p_v }{ p_{uv} }
\]
is Shannon's conditional entropy.

%% Bubbles, SVAR
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Are there bubbles in stock prices?\\
  Testing for fundamental shocks} \\
  A. Velinov and W. Chen (2014)
\end{book}
A $\text{VAR}(p)$ model, for a stationary time series $y_t$
(if it is not stationary, try with $\Delta y_t$),
\[ y_t = \nu + A_1 y_{t-1} + \cdots + A_p y_{t-p} + u_t, \]
can be written 
\[ A(L) y_t = \nu + u_t \]
where $L$ is the lag operator and
(if $A(L)$ is invertible) as an $\text{MA}(\infty)$ process
(Wold decomposition)
\[ 
  y_t = 
  \mu + \sum_{s \geq 0} \Phi_s u_{t-s} = 
  \mu + \Phi(L) u_t.
\]
While the VAR parametrization is well-defined,
the MA one is not identified: 
one can multiply $u_t$ by an invertible matrix:
\[ 
  y_t = 
  \mu + \Phi(L) B \epsilon_t = 
  \mu + \Psi(L) \epsilon_t.
\]
The article uses two variables, 
\[ 
  y_t = \Delta \begin{pmatrix}
    \log( \text{production} ) \\
    \log( \text{stock prices} )
  \end{pmatrix} 
\]
and (to identify the model) the restrictions 
\[
  E[ \epsilon_t \epsilon_t' ] =
  \begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},
  \quad
  \Psi = \begin{pmatrix}*&0\\{}*&*\end{pmatrix}
\]
(this is a \d{structural VAR} (SVAR) model).
The two types of shock can be interpreted as 
fundamental and non-fundamental, 
and give a decomposition of the log-price 
into fundamental and non-fundamental components.

% They also look at a Markov-switching VAR model (MS-VAR).

%%
%% Volatility
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Downside volatility timing} \\
  I. Nolte and Q. Xu (2014)
\end{book}
Add (high-frequency, realized) downside risk 
to your alpha model or your risk forecasts
(e.g., a heterogeneous autoregressive (HAR) model, 
$ \sigma_{t,t+1} \sim 
\sigma_{t-1,t}^+ + 
\sigma_{t-1,t}^- + 
\sigma_{t-4,t} + 
\sigma_{t-22,t}
$): 
it is more informative than volatility.

You can also try to decompose the risk into its jump 
and diffusion components (with the bipower variation).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The economic value of realized jumps:\\
  an asset allocation perspective} \\
  I. Nolte and Q. Xu (2014)
\end{book}

Separating jumps from diffusion gives better volatility forecasts;
they can be used in portfolio construction (``volatility timing''),
either in the variance matrix
in an optimization problem,
or in a ``parametric portfolio construction'' 
(the portfolio weights are a linear function 
of some state variable, linked to the volatility components).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Volatility indices and state-preference pricing} \\
  Z.F. Liu (2014)
\end{book}
The VIX was originally computed from ATM options on the SP100
and the Black-Scholes formula.
It is now computed from OTM SP500 options, 
but, although it no longer relies 
on the Black-Scholes formula,
it is not completely model-free,
ignores trading volume and includes illiquid options.

The article suggests a model-free approach
(by constructing a ``variance'' option, 
that pays the squared log-returns) 
using only liquid vanilla options.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Effects of financial crises on the long memory volatility dependency of foreign exchange rates: the Asian crisis vs the global crisis} \\
  Y.W. Han (2014)
\end{book}
Long-memory, \ie, the slow decay of the autocorrelation
(processes between stationary and integrated)
can be estimated with ARFIMA models 
(more precisely, for volatility, ARMA-FIGARCH)
or by the local Whittle estimator
(the asymtotic behaviour of the spectrum (periodogram) 
when $\omega \to 0^+$).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Omega risk model with tax} \\
  Z. Cui (2014)
\end{book}
Ruin theory models, 
such as the first hitting time 
\[ \tau = \inf \{ t \geq 0 \ :\ X_t \leq 0 \}, \]
the deterministic grace period (Chapter 11)
\[ \tau = \inf \{ t \geq 0 \ :\  
  \forall s \in [ t - \epsilon, t ] \ 
  X_s \leq 0 
  \} 
\]
or the stochastic grace period (omega risk model,
$w(X_s) \mathbb1_{X_s \leq 0}$ is the intensity 
of a Poisson process)
\[ 
\tau = \inf \{ 
  t \geq 0 \ :\  
  \int_0^t w(X_s) \mathbb1_{X_s \leq 0} \, ds > e
\} 
\]
can be augmented with taxes (paid at rate $\gamma$ 
when $X_t = \bar X_t$).
\begin{align*}
  X_t &= \text{pre-tax value} \\ 
  U_t &= \text{after-tax value} \\
  d U_t &= d X_t - \gamma \bar X_t d \bar X_t \\
  \bar X_t &= \text{running maximum}
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On a new index of riskiness:\\
  theoretical results and some applications} \\
  R. Resta and M.E. Marina (2013)
\end{book}

The riskiness of a gamble $g$ can be measured by finding 
$\alpha = R_{\textsc{as}}(g)$
such that $E[ e^{-g/\alpha} ] = 1$;
this is monotonic wrt stochastic dominance.
This can be generalized to $\alpha = I_\theta(g)$
such that 
\[ \dfrac{ E[ e^{-g_-/\alpha} - 1 ] }{ E[ 1 - e^{-g_+/\alpha} ] } = \theta. \]
%(only for gambles such that $ E[g_-]/E[g_+] = E[\text{loss}]/E[\text{gain}] < \theta \in [0,1] $ )

%% Risk
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model risk of risk models} \\
  J. Danielsson et al. (2014)
\end{book}
To measure model risk, use several risk models 
(MA, EWMA, GARCH, t-GARCH, EVT, historical, etc.)
and look at the ratio 
\[
  \dfrac{ \text{highest VaR (or ES)} }{ \text{lowest} }.
\]

The co-expected-shortfall is 
\[ \operatorname{CoES} = E[ X | M \leq M_\alpha ], \]
where $X$ are the stock returns, 
$M$ the market returns, 
$M_\alpha$ the $\alpha$-quantile of the market returns. 
The CoVaR is similarily defined.

%% Risk
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the measurement of economic tail risk} \\
  S. Kou and X. Peng (2014)
\end{book}
\emph{Median shortfall} is the only ``economically sound''
(notion defined with a set of axioms)
tail risk measure (yes, it is just a VaR).

The main problem (``elicitability'') 
with the expected shortfall (ES) is that,
at least theoretically,
you cannot check that your ES estimate is correct:
something very bad could happen vmuch farther in the tail,
that would have no effect on the VaR, 
but a huge effect on the ES.

%% Physics, time series
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Power law and evolutionary trends\\ in stock markets} \\
  P.V.S. Balakrishnan et al. (2007)
\end{book}
The power law exponent of stock trading volume 
has been increasing since 1965,
suggesting a higher concentration of trades.

%% Risk
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Rollover risk and volatility risk\\ in credit spread models: a unified approach} \\
  S. Perrakis and R. Zhong (2013)
\end{book}
Credit risk is often estimated as a barrier option on the value of the firm, modeled as a random walk. The model can be improved by adding: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The capital structure;
\item Illiquidity risk -- an illiquid bond market 
  increases the \emph{rollover risk};
\item Volatility risk -- volatility is not constant
  (the article uses a CEV process).
\end{compactitem}

%% Volatility
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating stochastic volatility models using realized measures} \\
  J. Bekierman and B. Gribisch (2014)
\end{book}
Add the realized volatility to your GARCH 
or stochastic volatility model: 
\begin{align*}
  \text{return}_t &= e^{\lambda_t / 2 } \epsilon_t \\
  \log( \text{realized volatility} _t ) &= 
  \xi + \lambda_t + \sigma_1 u_t \\
  \text{latent volatility} = \lambda_t &= \gamma + \delta \lambda_t + \sigma_2 \eta_t \textbf{ ???} \\
  \epsilon_t, u_t, \eta_t &\sim N(0,1).
\end{align*}

%% Volatility, Higher moments
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A user's guide\\ to the Cornish-Fisher expansion} \\
  D. Maillard (2012)
\end{book}
The Cornish-Fisher expansion is often 
considered as a black box: 
``compute the skewness and kurtosis of your distribution,
plug them into the formulas,
and retrieve the VaR and CVaR''.
The expansion is actually very simple: 
a Gaussian random variable $Z \sim N(0,1)$
can be transformed into a non-Gaussian one:
\[ 
X = f(X) = 
Z 
+ \dfrac S6 ( Z^2 - 1 ) 
+ \dfrac K{24} ( Z^3 - 3Z ) 
- \dfrac{ S^2 }{ 36 } ( 2 Z^2 - 5 Z )
\]
(understanding where this specific 
degree-3 polynomial comes from is less simple).

There are two common mistakes.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item To easily compute the VaR and CVaR of $X$ 
  from those of $Z$, $f$ should be bijective: 
  this imposes restrictions on the $S$ and $K$ parameters.
\item The moments of $X$ are not $(0,1,S,3+K)$,
  but only asymptotically so, when $S,K \to 0$. 
  In particular, what needs to be put in the formula 
  is not the skewness and kurtosis, 
  but the skewness and kurtosis \emph{parameters} 
  (the article fails to stress that 
  the variance is affected by the same problem).
  The actual moments can be numerically transformed 
  into the needed parameters.
\end{compactitem}

%%
%% Higher moments, options
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forward-looking measures\\
  of higher-order dependencies\\
  with an application to portfolio selection} \\
  F. Brinkmann et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Option prices can be used to estimate higher moments
(skewness, kurtosis).
In a multi-asset situation, 
the variance, skewness and kurtosis tensors
can be modeled in a parsimonious way
by assuming that the ``higher correlations''
(normalized covariance, coskewness, cokurtosis) 
are all equal, \ie, besides the univariate parameters,
there are only three parameters to estimate 
($\rho^{\text{cov}}$,
$\rho^{\text{coskew}}$,
$\rho^{\text{cokurt}}$).
Those higher moments can be used in portfolio optimization
(take a 4th order Taylor expansion of the expected utility).

%%
%% Options
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FVA explained: is there a solution?} \\
  D. Lu and F. Juan
\end{book}
When marking to market derivatives, 
one should also consider 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item CVA (credit value adjustment), \ie, the credit rist of the counterparty (CDS spread);
\item DVA (debit value adjustment), \ie, the credit risk of the bank itself;
\item FVA (funding value adjustment), 
  \ie, the cost of funding the collateral 
  for the derivative -- 
  uncollateralized derivatives are becoming rarer and rarer
  and the funding cost is not exactly the CDS spread.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Recovering from derivatives funding:\\
   a consistent approach\\ to DVA, FVA and hedging} \\
  C.J. Gunnesson and A.F. Mu\~nos de Morales (2014)
\end{book}
CVA, DVA, FVA can be priced by replication.
% The asset needed does not exist, but can be priced.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{KVA: capital valuation adjustment} \\
  A. Green et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Besides CVA, DVA, FVA, 
one can also include the cost of regulatory capital (KVA)
in derivative valuation.

%% Option pricing (FI)
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On multicurve models for the term structure} \\
  L. Morino and W.J. Runggaldier (2014)
\end{book}
To price forward rate agreements (FRA)
and other fixed income products, 
you need a \emph{multicurve model},
with one curve for discounting, 
and one for the cash flows.
[It had always shocked me 
to see people use the same curve.]

%% Options and portfolio construction
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Equity portfolio management\\ using option price information} \\
  P. Christoffersen and X.N. Pan (2014)
\end{book}
Clear review of the use of options data 
for equity portfolio management: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\text{Smile slope} = 
  \restr{\text{ivol}}{K/S=0.95} -
  \restr{\text{ivol}}{K/S\approx1}$;
\item $\left\langle 
  \restr{\text{ivol}}{\text{Call}} -
  \restr{\text{ivol}}{\text{Put}}
  \right\rangle$;
\item Model-free volatility, skewness, kurtosis;
\item  Exposure to VIX innovations, oil VIX (OVX),
  gold VIX (compute it yourself), copper VIX;
\item Exposure to market skew;
\item Exposure to VRP;
\item Option-implied covariance matrix or, even, 
  option-implied joint return distribution.
\end{compactitem}

%% Options strategy
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dispersion trading in South Africa} \\
  S. Maze (2012)
\end{book}
A \emph{dispersion trade} is long an index call, 
and short constituent calls; 
it is profitable if the constituents of the index 
are more volatile than the index.

%% Options
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Option and accounting information: empirical evidence in stock and derivative markets} \\
  C.J. Garc\'\i a Mart\'\i n et al. 
\end{book}
When forecasting the post-earnings announcement drift (PEAD),
use options data 
(options volume/stock volume, open interest, etc.)
if available.

%% Options
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The price impact of option hedging\\
   and the anomalous weekly reversal} \\
  T. Wang (2014)
\end{book}
Hedging by option writers impacts prices, 
even for liquid underliers,
especially around expiry, creating weekly reversals.

%%
%% Portfolio construction
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identifying the drivers of predicted beta} \\
  J. Wand and J. menchero (MSCI, 2014)
\end{book}
One can compute betas from a factor model
\begin{align*}
  r &\sim N( \mu_0, V ) \\
  V &= e v e' + \Delta  \\
  \beta_i &= \beta( r_i, w' r) = 
  \dfrac{ \cov(r_i, w'r) }{ \var(w'r) } = 
  \dfrac{ V_{i,\cdot} w }{ w' V w }
\end{align*}
and decompose it into factor contributions.
One can do the same thing 
for the cross-sectional dispersion of the $\beta$'s.  

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalized risk-based investing} \\
  E. Jurczenko et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Minimum variance, maximum diversity, risk parity, etc.
are special cases of the following optimization problem: 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & w \\
\text{To minimize} & 
\displaystyle\operatorname*{dispersion}_i 
\dfrac{w_i ^ \gamma}{ \sigma_i ^ \delta}
\dfrac{ \partial \sqrt{ w' V w } }{ \partial w_i} \\
\text{Such that} & w' \mathbf 1 = 1
\end{array}
\]
\ie, trying to make those asset contributions
equal, if possible.

%% Portfolio construction
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Equal-risk bounding is better than risk parity for portfolio selection} \\
  F. Cesarone and F. Tardella (2014)
\end{book}
The equal risk bounding (ERB) portfolio is the minimum risk portfolio among the risk parity portfolios built on a subsets of the assets: 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & w \\
\text{To minimize} & \max_i w_i (Vw)_i \\
\text{Such that} & w' \mathbb1 = 1, \ w \geq 0;
\end{array}
\]
it can also be written 
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & w, \lambda \\
\text{To minimize} & \lambda \\
\text{Such that} & \forall i \ w_i (Vw)_i \leq \lambda \\
& w' \mathbb1 = 1, \ w \geq 0.
\end{array}
\]

%% Portfolio construction
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficiently combining multiple sources of alpha\\
  in portfolio construction} \\
  J. Menchero and J.H. Lee (MSCI, 2014)
\end{book}
Given a risk (or alpha) factor, one can define several portfolios.
Let $w_i$ be the weight of asset $i$ in the market portfolio,
with $\sum w_i = 1$.
Let $\beta_{ik}$ be the exposure of stock $i$ to risk factor $k$,
normalized: 
$\sum_i w_i \beta_{ik} = 0$, 
$\sum_i w_i \beta_{ik}^2 = 1$.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{simple factor portfolio} for factor $k$
  has weights $w_i \beta_{ik}$.
  (Thanks to the normalization) 
  the factor returns can also be obtained by 
  (univariate, time series) regression: 
  $X_i = \alpha_i + 
         \beta_{ik} F_k^{\text{simple}} + \epsilon_i$.
\item The \emph{pure factor portfolio} for factor $k$
  has exposure~1 to factor $k$ and exposure 0 
  to the other factors; 
  the weights  are the rows of 
  $\beta^+ \operatorname{diag}(w)$,
  where $\beta^+$ is the pseudo inverse of $\beta$
  ($\beta^+ = (\beta' \beta )^{-1} \beta'$
  if the columns of $\beta$ are linearly independent --
  it is a little more complicated otherwise, e.g.,
  if you have world, country and sector factors).
\item The \emph{minimum volatility portfolio}
  among those with unit exposure to factor $k$.
\end{compactitem}

%% Portfolio construction
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Alpha-risk factor misalignment:\\
  does it pose a problem?} \\
  J.H. Lee et al. (MSCI, 2013)
\end{book}
Portfolio optimization tends to maximize 
the exposure to the \emph{residual alpha} -- 
the part of the alpha not accounted for by the risk model --,
assuming that it bears no risk.
Since
it does contain risk,
and less information than the alpha, 
one can add a penalty for the exposure 
to this residual alpha to the optimization problem --
but, quite often, this adjustment is not needed.

%% Portfolio construction
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diversified minimum variance portfolios} \\
  G. Coqueret (2014)
\end{book}
Regularization path of the minimum variance portfolio 
with an $L^2$ constraint.

%% Portfolio construction?, Accounting
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Trilateral foreign exchange exposure} \\
  T.J. O'Brian (2014)
\end{book}
To compute (and hedge) the FX exposure of a firm 
in a trilateral situation 
(e.g.,
the firm and the customers use different currencies, 
   and the price is in a third currency; 
two firms, from different countries, 
   compete in a third country; 
firm, factory and market are in three different countries),
consider unilateral shocks (\ie, shocks, up or down, 
in one currency, leaving the other two fixed).

%%
%% Empirical finance
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pseudo-mathematics and financial charlatanism: the effects of backtest overfitting on out-of-sample performance} \\
  D.H. Bailet et al. (Notices of the AMS, 2014)
\end{book}
The dangers (and omnipresence) of overfitting in finance.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Significance testing in empirical finance:\\
  a critical review and assessment} \\
  J.H. Kim and P.I. Ji (2014)
\end{book}
Empirical finance is still in the statistical dark ages: 
people use conventional (arbitrary) significance thresholds,
disregarding: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sample size;
\item Economic significance 
  (often, tests do not answer the question 
  ``is there   something'' but 
  ``do I have enough data to see it?''):
  do not test 
  $H_0: \beta = 0$ against $H_1: \beta>0$, 
  but against $H_1: \beta > \beta_1$ -- 
  if you do not want to choose $\beta_1$, 
  report a confidence interval for $\beta$;
\item Test power;
\item Heteroskedasticity, autocorrelation, outliers;
\item Expected losses.
\end{compactitem}

One could use: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Smaller thresholds ($10^{-3}$ is fine in psychology);
\item Bayesian methods, \ie, check if 
\[
  \dfrac{ P(H_1 | \text{data} ) }{ P(H_0 | \text{data} ) }  = 
  \dfrac{ P(\text{data} | H_1) }{ P(\text{data} | H_0) } 
  \dfrac{P(H_1)}{P(H_0)} > 1.
\]
\end{compactitem}
In addition, the publication bias is high 
and replicated studies almost inexistent.
[I disagree: I see a lot of replicated studies --
but they remain unpublished.]

%%
%% Economy
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive learning and survey data} \\
  A. Markiewicz and A. Pick (2013)
\end{book}
Homo economicus and his exact subjective probabilities 
should be replaced with 
\emph{homo econometricianus},
who uses time series models fitted on real data 
(``adaptive learning'')
and macroeconomic data.

%% Economy, accounting
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantifying differential interpretation\\
   of public information} \\
  X.S. Sheng
\end{book}
By modeling analysts as Bayesian learners, 
blending their prior beliefs with their interpretation of new information,
one can decompose the dispersion in their forecasts into a prior dispersion and the dispersion due to new information, 
\begin{align*}
  \var_{\text{new information}} &= 
  \dfrac{
    \var X_i^{\text{before}} - 
    \lambda^2 
    \var X_i^{\text{after}}
  }{
    1 - \lambda^2
  } \\ 
  X_i^{\text{after}} - X^{\text{after}} &= 
  \alpha + \lambda 
  ( X_i^{\text{before}} - X^{\text{before}} ) 
  + \epsilon
\end{align*}

%%
%% Lottery
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Investor behavior and financial innovation:\\
  a case study on callable bull/bear contracts} \\
  X. Li et al. (2014)
\end{book}
Knock-out barrier options are cheap, volatile, and skewed -- 
prospect theory can explain why investors like them 
(as a lottery),
even though they lose money.

%% Lottery
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bankruptcy sells stocks...\\
  but who's buying and why?} \\
  L. Coelho et al. 
\end{book}
Bankrupt companies are actively traded, and often overpriced:
they are used by retail investors as lottery stocks
(high probability of a small loss, 
low probability of a large gain, 
\ie, high skewness).

%%
%% Pairs trading
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pairs trading with copulas} \\
  W. Xie et al. (2014)
\end{book}
When implementing a pairs trading strategy
(once you have identified the pair, e.g., 
via cointegration tests),
one often just looks at the difference in normalized prices,
\begin{align*}
  & p_X(0) = p_Y(0) = 1 \\
  & \Delta(t) = p_X(t) - p_Y(t) \\
  & \text{Trade when } 
  \va{ \Delta(t) } > 2 \sigma_{ \Delta(t) }.
\end{align*}
Instead, one can estimate the copula of the returns,
and the conditional probabilities 
(``mispricing indices'')
\begin{align*}
  \text{MI}_t^{X|Y}\!\! &= P[ X_t < x_t | Y_t = y_t ] \\
  \text{MI}_t^{Y|X}\!\! &= P[ Y_t < y_t | X_t = x_t ]
\end{align*}
(where $X_t$ are the returns and $x_t$ the realized returns)
and trade when 
$\sum_{s\leq t} \text{MI}_s^{X|Y}$
or 
$\sum_{s\leq t} \text{MI}_s^{Y|X}$
exceeds some threshold.

%% Pairs trading
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving pairs trading} \\
  T.R. Almeida (2011)
\end{book}
Pairs opening after a 1-sided shock are less profitable.

%%
%% Accounting
%%

%% Accounting, time series, bubbles
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Liquidity-adjusted price-dividend ratios\\
  and expected returns} \\
  B.G. Jang et al.
\end{book}
The price-dividend ratio is often assumed to be stationary:
if can therefore be defined as a cointegration relation for 
$(\log(\text{price}), \log(\text{dividends}))$.
But evidence suggests that it may not always be stationary: 
one can try to replace it with a cointegration relation
for
\[ 
  \bigl(
  \log(\text{price}),
  \log(\text{dividends}),
  \log(\text{liquidity})
  \bigr),
\]
where the USD trading volume can be used 
as a proxy for liquidity.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random walks in dividend yields and bubbles} \\
  F. Bidian (2014)
\end{book}
A non-stationary dividend yield is not necessarily 
evidence of a bubble.

%% Portfolio construction, accounting
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating private equity market beta using cash flows: a cross-sectional regression of fund-market paired internal rates of return} \\
  Y. Jiang and J. S\'aenz (2014)
\end{book}
To estimate the beta of private equity (PE):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the internal rate of return (IRR) 
  of the PE investments 
  (you need several of them, and you get an IRR for each);
\item Compute the corresponding market IRRs
  (using the same cash flows, 
  but invested in the market, with a non-zero final value);
\item Regress.
\end{compactitem}

[I am skeptical about the stability of this procedure: 
the market IRRs are weighted averages of market returns -- 
they will be very, very similar, 
so we are almost regressing against a constant...]

%% Accounting
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Peering inside the analyst ``black box'':\\
  how do equity analysts model companies?} \\
  A. Markou and S. Taylor (2014)
\end{book}
Analysts value firms using 
the discounted cashflow model (DCF),
with explicit cashflow forecasts 
(subjective,
 or from subjective forecasts of balance sheet items,
or from subjective/historical growth rates thereof).
The discount factor 
(weighted average cost of capital, WACC)
is computed from the cost of debt, 
the cost of equity (equity risk premium, ERP),
the tax advantage of debt,
and the capital structure --
some include preference shares 
and pension liabilities in the capital structure.
The ERP is difficult to estimate -- most use the CAPM.
The terminal value and the time after which it is reached
influence the result.
The DCF is complemented by other approaches: 
EV/EBITDA, P/E, etc.

For more details, check Damodaran's books.

%% Accounting
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Changes in cash:\\ persistence and pricing implications} \\
  J.Z. Chen and P.B. Shane (2010)
\end{book}
The changes in earnings can be decomposed into 
\begin{align*}
\Delta \text{Earnings} 
&= \text{Accruals} + \Delta \text{FCF} \\
\Delta \text{FCF} &= \Delta \text{Cash} + 
\text{net\,distribution\,to\,debt\,holders} \\
&\hphantom{{}= \Delta \text{Cash}} {}+
\text{net\,distribution\,to\,shareholders}.
\end{align*}
The change in cash can be decomposed into a normal and an abnormal part, 
\[ 
\Delta \text{Cash} = 
\widehat{ \Delta \text{Cash} } + \text{residual}
\]
where the forecast $\widehat{ \Delta \text{Cash} }$
comes from the model
\begin{align*}
\Delta \text{Cash} 
& \sim \sigma_{\text{industry}}( \text{FCF} ) 
+ {} \\
& \hphantom{{}\sim{}}
\Delta \dfrac{ 
  \text{assets} - \text{BV}(\text{equity}) + \text{MV}(\text{equity})
}{
  \text{assets}
} + {} \\
& \hphantom{{}\sim{}}
\Delta \log(\text{assets}) + \Delta \text{FCF} + {} \\
& \hphantom{{}\sim{}}
\dfrac{
  \text{working capital} - \text{cash}
}{
  \text{assets}
} + {} \\
& \hphantom{{}\sim{}}
\Delta \dfrac{ \text{debt} }{ \text{assets} } + 
\Delta \dfrac{ \text{R\&D} }{ \text{assets} } +
( \Delta \text{dividends} > 0 ) + {} \\
& \hphantom{{}\sim{}}
\Delta \dfrac{ \text{cash outflow on acquisitions} }{ \text{assets} }  + {} \\
& \hphantom{{}\sim{}}
\Delta \text{Cash}_{t-1} + \text{Cash}_{t-1}.
\end{align*}
Negative abnormal cash changes are persistent, 
and do not bode well for the company.
Positive abnormal cash changes are not persistent,
but such hubris is ignored by the market.

%% Accounting, text mining
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{When the use of positive language backfires:\\
  the joint effect of language sentiment,\\
  readability and investor sophistication\\ 
  on earnings judgements} \\
  H.T. Tan et al. (2013)
\end{book}
When readability is low, 
sentiment (\ie, word choice) matters,
but in opposite directions for naive 
and sophisticated investors.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{R\&D spillover and predictable returns} \\
  Y. Jiang et al. (2012)
\end{book}
R\&D spending is an externality: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Low R\&D firms lag high R\&D firms in the same industry;
\item The surprises are higher for low R\&D firms 
  (in the same industry) that are not often found
  next to the leaders (in news, portfolios or analysts' reports).
\end{compactitem}

%% Accounting
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What do we learn from two new accounting-based stock market anomalies?} \\
  S. Basu (2004)
\end{book}
Accounting anomalies (buy low NOA, 
sell short firms receiving a going-concern audit opinion,
etc.) 
are consistent with \emph{minimally rational markets}:
the prices are inconsistent with 
all investors being rational, 
but transaction costs are too high 
for rational investors to make a profit. 

%% Accounting
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The roles of receivables and deferred revenues\\
  in revenue management} \\
  J. Zha (2014)
\end{book}
To detect manipulations, look at: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\Delta\text{receivables} \gg 0$ 
  (fictitious sales, bill-and-hold);
\item $\Delta \text{deferred revenue} \ll 0$
  (get the cash early, ship the goods late).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Disentangling the accruals mispricing\\ in Europe:
  is it an industry effect?} \\
  E. Basilico and T. Johnsen (2013)
\end{book}
Also look at: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\dfrac{\Delta\text{accounts receivables}}{\text{net operating assets}}$;
\item $\dfrac{\Delta\text{inventory}}{\text{net operating assets}}$;
\item $
\text{Days of sales outstanding} = 
\dfrac{\Delta
  \dfrac{\text{receivables}}{\text{sales}}
}{
  \dfrac{\text{receivables}}{\text{sales}}
}; $
\item $
\text{Days of inventory outstanding} = 
\dfrac{\Delta
  \dfrac{\vrule height 1em width 0pt\text{inventory}}{\text{COGS}}
}{
  \dfrac{\text{inventory}}{\text{COGS}}
}.
$
\end{compactitem}

