%%
%% R Engine
%%

\textbf{R engine.}
% Luke Tierney: Some Performance Improvements for the R Engine (pdf)
Recent or forthcoming performance improvements include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bytecode compilation;
\item Shallow duplication (since R 3.1.0);
\item Reference counting (perhaps in R 3.2.0);
\item Large vectors ($>16$\,GB);
\item Parallelization: 
  explicit parallelization works;
  C/C++ parallelization via OpenMP only works on Linux;
  implicit parallelization only works for a few functions;
\item The \texttt{proftools} package will soon be available.
\end{compactitem}

%%
%% Portfolios
%%

\textbf{Portfolio construction.}
% Ross Bennett: Complex Portfolio Optimization with PortfolioAnalytics (pdf)
The \texttt{PortfolioAnalytics} package performs portfolio optimization 
  (with \texttt{ROI} (\texttt{Rglpk}, \texttt{Rsymphony}, \texttt{quadprog}), 
  random portfolios, 
  \texttt{DEoptim}, \texttt{pso}, \texttt{GenSA}), 
  for various types of objectives (mean, value at risk (VaR), expected shortfall (ES), 
  standard deviation, expected utility)
  and constraints; the types of objective and constraints are apparently limited.

% Bernhard Pfaff: The R package cccp: Solving Cone Constrained Convex Programs (pdf)
The \texttt{cccp} package will solve cone-constrained convex programs.

% Avery Moon: Tax Efficient Portfolios
One should include taxes (in particular, ``tax harvesting'' effects in case of losses) 
in portfolio optimization: 
it can be very profitable to replace a stock with an equivalent one.
% The presentation ends with a vague slide about "R in the cloud".

% Steven Pav: Portfolio Inference with this One Weird Trick
  Let $\mathbf x$ be the (random variable of) asset returns 
  and $\tilde{ \mathbf x } = (1 \ \mathbf x')'$.
  The inverse of 
  \[ 
    \Theta = E[ \tilde{ \mathbf x } \tilde{ \mathbf x } ' ] = 
    \begin{pmatrix}
      1 & \mu' \\ \mu  & \Sigma + \mu \mu'
    \end{pmatrix}
  \]
  is 
  \[
    \Theta^{-1} = 
    \begin{pmatrix} 
      1 + \mu' \Sigma \mu & - \mu' \Sigma^{-1} \\
      - \Sigma^{-1} \mu & \Sigma^{-1}
    \end{pmatrix} 
    = 
    \begin{pmatrix} 
      1 + \text{sharpe}^2 & - \mathbf w' \\ 
      - \mathbf w         & \Sigma^{-1}
    \end{pmatrix}
  \]
  where $\mathbf w$ is the markowitz portfolio.
  The distribution of $\vech \Theta^{-1}$ is asymptotically gaussian,
  and $\var \vech \Theta^{-1}$ is easy to compute 
  (from $\var \vech \Theta$):
  it can be used to perform tests or compute confidence intervals
  on the Shape ratio or the portfolio weights.
  This can be generalized to constrained portfolios
  (e.g., the optimal portfolio with zero covariance wrt some reference portfolio).

% Yang Lu: Re-Evaluation of the Low-Risk Anomaly in Finance via Matching (pdf)
  To check if a signal has some predictive power on future returns,
  build the top and bottom quintiles, and match them (on industry, size, trading volume): 
  if the effect disappears, it was caused by the imbalance between the portfolios.
  Coarsened exact matching is implemented in the \texttt{cem} package.

% Matthew Clegg: On the Persistence of Cointegration in Pairs Trading (pdf)
Cointegrated pairs do not remain so (use the \texttt{egcm} package for the tests).

%%
%% Higher moments
%%

\textbf{Higher moments.}
% John Burkett: Portfolio Optimization: Utility, Computaton, Equities Applications (pdf)
\texttt{DEoptim} can account for higher moments in portfolio optimization.

% Kris Boudt: Asset Allocation with Higher Order Moments and Factor Models
  The factor model $r_i = \sum_k b_{ik} f_k + e_i$
  (with $E[r_i]=E[f_k]=E[e_i]=0$, $f_k \indep e_i$, $e_i \indep e_j$ if $i \neq j$)
  can be used to decompose the higher moments of the portfolio returns.
  \begin{align*}
    E[ (w' r)^2 ] &= \sum_{ijkl} w_i w_j b_{ik} b_{jl} E[f_kf_l] + \sum_i w_i^2 E[e_i^2] \\
    E[ (w' r)^3 ] &= \!\!
      \sum_{\substack{i_1, i_2, i_3 \\ k_1, k_2, k_3}} 
      \!\!
      w_{i_1} w_{i_2} w_{i_3} 
      b_{i_1 k_1} b_{i_2 k_2} b_{i_3 k_3}
      E[ f_{k_1} f_{k_2} f_{k_3} ] \\
      &\hphantom{={}} +
      \sum_i w_i^3 E[e_i^3] \\
    E[ (w' r)^4 ] &= \cdots.
  \end{align*}
  (I leave the fourth moment as an exercise to the reader: 
  contrary to the second and third moments, the cross-products do not all disappear -- 
  you should have three more terms.)
  Those higher-order tensors can be written as matrices using the Kronecker product 
  \begin{align*}
    E[ (w' r)^2 ] &= w' ( B S B'                         + \Delta ) w \\
    E[ (w' r)^3 ] &= w' ( B G (B' \otimes B'           ) + \Omega ) (w \otimes w) \\
    E[ (w' r)^4 ] &= w' ( B P (B' \otimes B' \otimes B') + Y      ) (w \otimes w \otimes w). \\
  \end{align*}

%%
%% Volatility
%%

\textbf{Volatility models.}
% Alexios Ghalanos: Twinkle, twinkle little STAR: Smooth Transition AR Models in R (pdf)
The 2-state STAR model 
  \[ 
    y_t = 
    [ \lambda_t \phi_1 + (1-\lambda_t) ]' 
    \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ y_{t-1} \\ \vdots \\ y_{t-p} \end{pmatrix} +
    \epsilon_t 
  \]
smoothly switches between two AR models, $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$;
the state transition function $\lambda_t = \lambda(z_t)$ depends
(via a logistic link) on exogenous variables $z_t$.
It can be extended to a STARMAX model by allowing autoregressive dynamics in the state $z_t$.
% library(twinkle)

% Rohini Grover: The Imprecision of Volatility Indexes (pdf)
One  can use the bootstrap to estimate the precision of volatility indices 
(VIX, vega-weighted VIX (VVIX), liquidity- or elasticity-weighted VIX);
check the \texttt{ifrogs} package for an implementation.

% Gregor Kastner: stochvol: Dealing with Stochastic Volatility in Time Series (pdf)
The \texttt{stochvol} package uses MCMC to fit stochastic volatility (SV) models: 
for time series modeling, regression with SV noise or factor models.
%  Vignette: http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/stochvol/vignettes/article.pdf
%  Dealing with Stochastic Volatility in Time Series Using the R Package stochvol, G. Kastner

% Matthew Dixon: gpusvcalibration: Fast Stochastic Volatility Model Calibration using GPUs (pdf)
The \texttt{gpusvcalibration} package uses GPUs to calibrate stochastic volatility models;
it relies on \texttt{nloptr} and \texttt{DEoptim}.
%  library(nloptr) # Interface to NLoptr http://ab-initio.mit.edu/wiki/index.php/NLopt_Introduction , which provides many optimization algorithms (local/global, derivative-free/gradient-based, etc.)
%  library(DEoptim)
%  library(gpusvcalibration)

%%
%% Risk models
%%

\textbf{Risk models.}
% Yi-An Chen: Estimating Factor Models and Managing Risk with FactorAnalytics (pdf)  
The \texttt{factorAnalytics} package can estimate risk factor models 
  (time series, fundamental, statistical)
  and decompose the risk (standard deviation, VaR, ES) 
  into risk factor contributions;
  
% Michael Kapler [systematicinvestor]: Average Correlation and Adaptive Shrinkage Estimators (pdf)
To estimate correlation, one can use shrinkage or, even better, weighted averages of shrinkage estimators.

% Steven Greiner: Stress Testing your way to Better Portfolio Management
When monitoring the volatility (or the VaR) of a portfolio, 
$\text{risk} = \sqrt{ w' V w }$ where
$V = \beta v \beta' + \Delta$,
one may want to decompose its changes into contributions of
changes in weights $w$,
changes in $\beta$,
changes in $v$,
changes in $\Delta$.
Graphically, one can look at: 
\begin{align*}
  \operatorname{risk}( \text{portfolio}_{\text{now}}, \text{model}_t            ) \sim t \\
  \operatorname{risk}( \text{portfolio}_t,            \text{model}_{\text{now}} ) \sim t \\
  \text{VIX}_t \sim t \\
  \text{VaR}_t^{99\%} - \text{VaR}_t^{95\%} \sim t
\end{align*}
For stress-testing: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Model the joint distribution of the risk factors and the stress factors 
    (the stress factors may be in your risk model), either as a Gaussian 
    or a mixture of Gaussians;
\item Sample from the conditional distribution 
  \[ \text{risk factors}  \mid \text{stress factors} \]
  for given values of the stress factors ($-10\%$, $-20\%$, $-30\%$, etc.)
\end{compactitem}

%  Stephen Rush: Twenty Years of VPIN (pdf)
One can add the volume synchronized probability of informed trading (VPIN) 
to the Fama-French, momentum and liquidity factors;
look at the beta of stock VPIN vs Market VPIN.
% They show plots of the "VPIN network", but I have no idea what it is...

%%
%% Performance
%%

\textbf{Performance measurement.}
% Karl Polen: Private Equity Performance Analytics Implemented in R (pdf)
To measure the performance of private equity (PE), 
use the cash flows to and from the fund, apply them to the benchmark, and look at
$
  \operatorname{log1p}( \text{IRR}_{\text{PE}} ) -
  \operatorname{log1p}( \text{IRR}_{\text{Market}} )
$
or 
\[
  \dfrac{ \text{final value}_{\text{PE}} }{ \text{final value}_{\text{Market}} }.
\]

%%
%% Time series
%%

\textbf{Time series.}
% David Matteson: Identifying Recessions in Real-Time Using Time-Frequency Functional Models
% http://www.rinfinance.com/agenda/2014/talk/DavidMatteson.pdf
To predict economic recessions, one can: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take high-frequency time series (e.g., daily index returns);
\item Compute their spectrograms on a moving window (short-term Fourier transform (STFT), sometimes also called Gabor transform);
    one can notice more low-frequencies in expansion periods, and more mid-frequencies in recessions;
\begin{verbatim}
library(quantmod)
getSymbols( "^GSPC", from="1980-01-01" )
x <- log1p( as.vector( ROC( Ad( GSPC ) ) ) )
dates <- index(GSPC)

library( e1071 )
y <- stft(x, inc=1)$values
dates <- index(GSPC)
dates <- tail( dates, nrow(y) )

par(mar=c(2,1,1,1), xaxs="i")
image( 
  dates, 1:ncol(y), y, 
  col = rev(topo.colors(10)), 
  axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" 
)
axis.Date( 1, dates )
box()
par(new = TRUE)
plot( 
  dates, rowSums(y), 
  type = "l", lwd = 3, axes = FALSE 
)

# The patterns we see are just due 
# to the volatility:  they completely 
# disappear if we normalize the signal 
# in each window...
image( 
  dates, 1:ncol(y), y/rowSums(y), 
  col = rev(topo.colors(10)), 
  axes = FALSE, xlab = "", ylab = "" 
)
axis.Date( 1, dates )
box()
par (new = TRUE )
plot( 
  dates, rowSums(y), 
  type = "l", lwd = 3, axes = FALSE 
)
\end{verbatim}
\item Consider those spectrograms as images, and model them as a 2-dimensional Gaussian process;
\item Compute the \emph{empirical orthogonal functions} (EOF) of the sequence of images (basically, a PCA):
    this gives variables with a potential predictive power on the current or future market state;
\item Add low-frequency variables (GDP, perhaps also yield, etc.);
\item Use \emph{stochastic search variable selection} (SSVS) on those variables to find a parsimonious probit model,
    to predict the current or future state of the economy.
\end{compactitem}
%For more details: 
%  An approach for identifying and predicting economic recessions in real-time using time–frequency functional models, S.H. Holan et al. (2012, Applied Stochastic Models in Business and Industry)
%  http://apps.olin.wustl.edu/conf/SBIES/Files/pdf/2014/105.pdf

% Tobias Setz: BCP Stability Analytics: New Directions in Tactical Asset Management (pdf)
The \texttt{bcp} package detects change points (different means) 
in multivariate (Gaussian) time series, using bayesian methods. 
Use with log-returns (I would also try with their absolute value) 
for a few indices or currency pairs.
% The presentation also described a Shiny interface.

\textbf{Tests.}
% Doug Martin: Tests for Robust versus Least Squares Factor Model Fits (pptx)
\texttt{robust::lsRobTest} provides tests on robust models
(I often say that you should start to worry and pay attention 
to robust methods (e.g., \texttt{robust::lmRob}) when they yield significantly different results).
%  More details (those articles are apparently NOT available (yet?)):
%  A Hausman type test of robust versus least-squares regression fits, Maravina and Martin (2014)
%  A Wald type test of robust versus least-squares regression fits

\textbf{Text mining.}
% James Thewissen: Sentiment Dynamics and Information Content within CEO Letters (pdf)
When extracting sentiment from text, the position of the words is relevant: 
for instance, if a text starts with a lot of negative words and ends with a lot of positive words, 
it does not mean it is neutral. 
Use weighted measures of sentiment, with 
  \raisebox{0mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.3]
    \draw (-.2,0) -- (1.2,0);
    %\draw (0,-.1) -- (0,1);
    \draw[thick] (0,1) -- (1,1);
  \end{tikzpicture}},
  \raisebox{0mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.3]
    \draw (-.2,0) -- (1.2,0);
    %\draw (0,-.1) -- (0,1);
    \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (1,1);
  \end{tikzpicture}},
  \raisebox{0mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.3]
    \draw (-.2,0) -- (1.2,0);
    %\draw (0,-.1) -- (0,1);
    \draw[thick] (0,0) --
(0.1,0.36) --
(0.2,0.64) --
(0.3,0.84) --
(0.4,0.96) --
(0.5,1) --
(0.6,0.96) --
(0.7,0.84) --
(0.8,0.64) --
(0.9,0.36) --
(1,0);
  \end{tikzpicture}}
($1$, $x$, $x(1-x)$),  
  etc.
as weights
(they like $P_c(u) - ( 1 - u^c ) u^{3-c}$).

\textbf{Networks.}
% Bryan Lewis: New Ideas for Large Network Analysis, Implemented in R (pdf)
From the adjacency matrix $A$ of a graph, one can compute the number of paths
of length $m$ between $i$ and $j$ as $[A^m]_{ij}$. 
The total number of paths between $i$ and $j$,
$[\sum_{m\geq0} A^m]_{ij}$, may be infinite: 
instead, one can give less importance to longer paths, 
$[\sum_{m\geq0} w_m A^m]_{ij}$, e.g., $[\exp A]_{ij}$.
Many graph notions (centrality, communicability, etc.), 
usually computed from the adjacency matrix $A$, 
can be generalized and computed from $f(A)$ (with, e.g., $f=\exp$):
the $f$-centrality $e_i' f(A) \mathbb 1$ generalizes the degree, 
the $f$-communicability $f(A)_{ij}$ generalizes the number of paths.
Check the \texttt{irlba} package (implicitly-restricted Lanczos method) 
to help compute those quantities on large, sparse matrices.
%The rest (the irlba package, and the implicitly-restricted Lanczos method) is too technical/superficial: check the papers.

\textbf{Infrastructure.}
% Bill Cleveland: Divide and Recombine for the Analysis of Large Complex Data with R (pdf)
The \texttt{RHIPE} package is an interface to Hadoop;
\texttt{datadr} provides a simpler divide-map-reduce API,
using either Hadoop (via \texttt{RHIPE}) or and in-memory key-value store.
\texttt{treilliscope} applies the same ideas to treillis/lattice/faceted plots; 
it relies on ``cognostics'' (e.g., scagnostics, i.e., scatterplot cognostics) 
to navigate the huge number of panels.

% Dirk Eddelbuettel: Building Simple Redis Data Caches (pdf)
The \texttt{rredis} package serializes/deserializes R objects 
to/from strings with \texttt{paste}/\texttt{substitude}/\texttt{deparse}:
it is slow for large objects (e.g., time series).
\texttt{RApiSerialize} does the serialization/deserialization in C++; 
\texttt{RcppRedis} uses it,
and can therefore efficiently deal with streaming data:
for instance, C++ or Python would put the data to process in Redis,
and R would read and processe it.

% Maria Belianina: Multi-Factor Models and Analytics with R, OneTick, and OneQuantData (pdf)
OneTick is a tick data database, with online aggregation
and complex event processing (CEP) in a GUI; 
the node can be arbitrary R/Python/Java/C++ code: 
for instance, one could fit a state-space model (well, PCA) on a moving window to predict future returns.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CEM: Software for coarsened exact matching} \\
  S.M.Iacus et al. (2009)
\end{book}
 
Coarsened Exact Matching (CEM) removes the sampling bias in observational studies:
it temporarily coarsens each control variable (\ie, bins the observations and builds a histogram)
and prunes the observations in the (multivariate) bins that do not have at least one control and one treated unit.
The statistical analysis can then proceed on the remaining data
(with weights inversely proportional to the bin sizes).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Asset Allocation with Higher Order Moments
  and Factor Models} \\
  K. Boudt et al (2014)
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An approach for identifying and predicting 
  economic recessions in real-time\\ 
  using time–frequency functional models} \\
  S.H. Holan et al. (2012)
\end{book}

%%
%% 2014-07
%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

% HFT
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Apparent criticality and calibration issues 
    in the Hawkes self-excited point process model: 
    application to high-frequency financial data} \\
  V. Filimonov and D. Sornette (2013)
\end{book}
Hawkes processes (Poisson processes in which 
each event increases the background Poisson intensity,
with an exponential or power law decay -- there are several 
commonly-used such ``kernels'')
can be interpreted as branching processes
(each event is either a mother event, exogenous,
coming from the background intensity,
or a daughter event, endogenous, 
coming from a previous event).
The \emph{branching ratio}
is the average number of daughter events.

Goodness of fit can be assessed with a change of time, 
$\hat \Lambda = \int \hat \lambda$,
which should give a Poisson process with 
$\lambda \equiv 1$.

The calibration of Hawkes processes is plagued by the following problems: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sensitivity to outliers (use a contamination model);
\item Edge effects: missing mother events before the sample,
  missing daughter events after;
\item Sensitivity to kernel misspecification: 
  we often only care about the tail of the kernel, 
  but its short-term behaviour also matters;
\item Multiple extrema in the log-likelihood.
\end{compactitem}

The microstructure of high-frequency data 
brings more problems: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Overnight trading is not neglibible,
  and it is different;
\item The exchange only sends 1-second timestamps, 
  and groups the ticks (to send fewer messages);
  The data provided tries to add miliseconds 
  using the reception time 
  (ignoring latencies -- problematic if they fluctuate),
  but there are still ties
  (one can try to add noise to reduce the bias);
\item The data is not stationary,
  $\mu(t)$ is not constant: 
  there are daily patters ($U$ shape)
  (estimate the patter and remove it to get 
  $\lambda \equiv 1$ -- but this distorts the kernel)
  and announcement days are completely different 
  (remove them);
\item Regime switches 
  (between different branching ratios, 
  or different background intensities)
  are interpreted as excess clustering 
  and bias the estimation.
\end{compactitem}

% HFT, liquidity
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An investigation of trades that move the BBO} \\
  Y. Huang et al. 
\end{book}
The bid-ask spread is insufficient to measure liquidity: 
one can look into the depth of the order book 
by considering \emph{strings},
\ie, series of trades with non-increasing prices
and computing the (average) 
number of trades, total volume, duration, log-return,
volatility, begining spread, 
begining trade size, begining price.

To test for variations between days or securities,
one can use the 
intra-class correlation coefficient (ICC).

%%
%% Networks
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Investor networks in the stock market} \\
  R. Bilkdik et al. (2013)
\end{book}
While it is possible to develop 
an information network model 
and estimate it using (approximate) maximum likelihood 
from transaction data,
this is not scalable.
In a very empirical way, one can estimate the network 
by linking agents $i$ and $j$ 
if they trade the same asset, in the same direction,
in a window of size $\tau$,
at least $M$ times.

Agents with higher centrality trade earlier 
and earn higher returns.

% Could we augment the PIN model with a network?

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A longitudinal analysis of asset return,\\
  volatility and corporate news network} \\
  G. Creamer et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Use a topic model (LDA, latent Dirichlet analysis)
on a newsfeed,
build a bipartite network of topics and companies 
(mentionned in the news items),
reduce it to a network of companies
(with the number of articles on the same topic as weights)
and test for Granger causality 
between graph metrics (centrality, etc.)
and returns or volatility.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A link mining algorithm\\
  for earnings forecast and trading} \\
  G. Creamer and S. Stolfo (2008)
\end{book}
Build a bipartite graph with directors 
(or directors and analysts) and firms as nodes,
reduce it to a graph of firms
(with the number of directors or analysts in common 
as weights):
the network metrics (centralities, clustering coefficient)
can help predict earnings surprises.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Trading networks} \\
  L. Adamic et al. (2010)
\end{book}
A simple limit-order book model 
($n$ brokers, a long liquidity taker, a short liquidity taker)
suggests that order flow imbalance and size 
are visible on the network.
Using transaction-level data,
one can build a time-varying transaction network
(e.g., on a 600-transaction window),
look at network metrics
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Centrality ($\text{in-degree} - \text{out-degree}$);
\item Absolute centrality;
\item Assortativity (degree correlation);
\item Clustering coefficient 
  (comparison of the number of triangles 
  and the number of connected triples);
\item Size of the largest strongly connected component
\end{compactitem}
and financial variables
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Amihud price impact;
\item Bid-ask spread;
\item Volatility (absolute value of the returns, 
  squared returns or $\text{high} - \text{low}$;
\item Intertrade duration;
\item Trading volume;
\end{compactitem}
Network metrics Granger-cause volume and intertrade duration.

% Networks?
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The two faces of interbank correlation} \\
  K. Schaeck et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The correlation between banks can be decomposed 
into a diversification component
(if the banks are very diversified, 
they are doing all possible banking activities, 
\ie, they are all doing the same things -- 
they are therefore exposed to the same sources of risk)
and a residual component 
(containing, among others, the endogenic risk 
resulting from the relations between the banks).
\begin{align*}
  \text{Commonality}_A &= 
  \cor( \text{returns}_A, 
        \text{returns}_{\text{sector}} ) \\
  \text{Diversification}_A &= 
  \cor( \text{returns}_A, \text{returns}_{\text{market}} ) \\
  \text{Excess commonality}_A &= \\
  \rlap{\ensuremath{
    \text{residuals}( 
    \text{Commonality} \sim \text{Diversification} )_A.
  }}\hspace*{2cm}
\end{align*}

If one knew the bank holdings, 
one could define commonality (resp. diversification) 
as the distance between 
the weights of the portfolio of bank $A$ 
and the aggregate portfolio of the banking sector 
(resp., the market portfolio),
and the excess commonality 
as the diffrence between 
the commonality and 
the minimum commonality possible 
for that level of diversification.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Filtering noise from correlation matrices} \\
  A. Izmailov and B. Shay (2013)
\end{book}
Random matrix theory (RMT) is often used 
to clean sample correlation matrices: 
since the asymptotic 
($T \to \infty$, $N\to\infty$, $Q=T/N$ constant) 
distribution of the eigenvalues 
of the sample correlation matrix 
of a standard Gaussian random variable
is known and bounded, 
one can discard all eigenvalues 
beyond the (asymptotic) maximum 
\[ \lambda_{\text{max}} = ( 1+Q^{-1/2} )^2. \]

The article suggests a finite sample correlation 
(but this increases $\lambda_{\text{max}}$,
which is already very high)
and measures the noise present in the correlation matrix 
by the ``entropy''
\[ 
  \sum_i 
  \dfrac{ \lambda_i }{ n } 
  \log_2 \dfrac{ \lambda_i }{ n }. 
\]

Since, on the examples, 
the effect of filtering seems to be 
an increase in the ``contrast'' of the correlation matrix,
one may want to compare with 
a simple coefficient-wise transformation, 
e.g., $x \mapsto x^\alpha$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Determining the number of clusters/segments in hierarchical clustering/segmentation algorithms} \\
  S. Salvador and P. Chan 
\end{book}
To estimate the number of clusters,
consider some performance metric as a function of the number of clusters,
and try to find the ``knee'' in its plot: 
$\argmax_i x_{i+1} - x_i$,
$\argmax_i x_{i+1} / x_i$, 
$\min \{ i \ :\ D^2(x)_i = x_{i+1} + x_{i-1} - 2 x_i \geq c \}$,
$\argmax_i D^2(x)_i$,
$\argmax \text{res}( x \sim i )$,
or the changepoint of a broken line fit to the data.
If there is a lot of data,. use the method again on
$\llbracket 1, 2 \hat\i \rrbracket$ and iterate until convergence.

%%
%% CAPM
%% 

% CAPM
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The relative asset pricing model:\\ 
  towards a unified theory of asset pricing} \\
    A. Muraalidhar et al.
\end{book}
Advocacy for liability-driven investing (LDI):
institutional investors
(pension funds form a growing proportion of all investors)
first define a ``liability proxy portfolio'',
describing their future liabilities 
(most do not do that seriously and settle 
for ``60\% equity, 40\% bonds'')
and then try to track or beat it.
This can be modeled in a CAPM-like way, 
\[ E[X-X_0] = \beta E[ M - L - X_0 ], \]
where 
  $X$ are the asset returns, 
  $X_0$ are the risk-free returns,
  $M$ are the market returns,
  $L$ are the liability portfolio returns.
The model can be augmented with more factors, 
\`a la Fama-French,
and the presence of a reference point $L$ 
accounts for prospect theory.

% CAPM
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A robust capital asset pricing model} \\
  D. Ruffino (2014)
\end{book}
In mean-variance portfolio optimization
\[
  \hat w = 
  \argmax_w \operatorname{CE}[ w' X ]
  \approx
  \argmax_w w' \mu - \tfrac12 \lambda_1 w' V_1 w,
\]
only the returns $X \sim N(\mu,V_1)$ are uncertain.
In robust mean-variance optimization, $\mu$ and $V_1$
are also uncertain. It is possible to linearize 
the certainly equivalent into
\[
  \hat w \approx
  \argmax_w 
    w' \mu_0 
    - \tfrac12 \lambda_1 w' V_1 w 
    - \tfrac12 \lambda_2 w' V_2 w
\]
where $\mu \sim N(\mu_0, V_2)$.
% Actually, we do not need that all these distributions
% be Gaussian, we just need the expectation and the variance.
(Note that the uncertainty on $V_1$ does not appear
in this approximation.)

This is equivalent to the initial problem
with $V_1$ replaced with 
$V_1 + (\lambda_2 / \lambda_1) V_2$ -- 
\ie, this looks like a shrinkage estimator.

% CAPM, FF
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Fama-French three factors\\
   in the Chinese stock market} \\
    J. Xu and S. Zhang (2013)
\end{book}
The Fama-French model works well in China 
(higher returns for small or high-value stocks,
higher $R^2$ when compared with the US),
but pay attention to a few pitfalls: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A large proportion of the shares are (still?) non-tradable;
\item There are 4 markets 
  (main Shanghai board, main Shenzhen board, 
  \textsc{sme}, \textsc{geb} -- 
  the last two, also in Shenzhen, are similar to the Nasdaq:
  you may, or not, want to include them);
\item There are multiple share classes (A, B, H, foreign) -- 
  do not forget them when computing per-share quantities.
\end{compactitem}

% CAPM, FF, factor models
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The determinants of convenience yields} \\
  M. Prokpczuk and Y. Wu (2013)
\end{book}
The carry cost is 
the difference between the future and spot prices.
It corresponds to interest foregone, storage costs, 
inventory, insurance against unexpected demand, 
insurance against supply shocks, speculation, etc. -- 
it can be interpreted as a convenience yield.

Rather than the spread 
$\text{future} - \text{spot}$, 
the convenience yield can be estimated 
from a model: 
the spot is a diffusion, the convenience yield
is an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process and it determines
the spot trend).
The convenience yield can be explained by: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Global inventory levels;
\item Hedging pressure 
  (relative net/gross position for commercial traders);
\item Volatility;
\item Macroeconomic conditions: industrial production 
  and inflation forecasts (from the BCEI).
\end{compactitem}

% CAPM, beta
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Systematic tail risk} \\
  M.R.C. van Oordt (2013)
\end{book}
The tail beta,
the sensitivity to extreme 
(say, beyond the 5\% value at risk) % EVT, 5 years of daily returns
market returns 
is persistent and a good predictor of losses during crises.
However, there is no tail beta premium.

