% Patents
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Patent- and innovation-driven performance\\
   in venture capital-backed IPOs} \\
  J. Cao et al (2013)
\end{book}
Patents (use the NBER patent database)
are still a good sign.
% Data sources: 
% - Thomson Reuters VentureXpert database: VC-backed IPOs or not
% - NBER patent database (need to match patents and companies)

% Patents
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{General purpose technologuies\\
  and the cross-section of stock returns} \\
  P.H. Hsu and W. Yang 
\end{book}
%Incorporate patent data in your models.
Classify patents as 
general-purpose (computers, telecom, electronics)
or special-purpose (chemical, medical, mechanical, etc.).
The difference between the growth rate of general 
and special purpose patents predicts industrial growth; 
it is a non-diversifiable risk factor,
that can be added to your factor risk models 
(Fama-French, etc.).

%%
%% Cost of capital 
%%

% Cost of capital
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Blockholder exit threats\\
  and financial reporting quality} \\
  Y. Dou (2014)
\end{book}
The presence of blockholders
(measured by the Herfindahl index of shareholders above 5\%),
increases financial reporting quality
(measured by abnormal accruals,
production costs, discretionary expenses,
operating cash flow,
where ``abnormal'' 
means ``absolute value of the residuals of a regression
agains other financial variables, cross-sectionally, 
by industry), 
at least for liquid stocks.

% Cost of capital
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Environmental disclosure\\
  and the cost of capital:
 evidence\\ from the Fukushima nuclear accident} \\
  P. Bonetti et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Environmental disclosure (CO$_2$ emissions, etc.)
is associated to a lower cost of capital 
(and a lower rise in cost of capital after accidents).
It can be measured by: 
the existence of an environmental report, 
the inclusion of CO$_2$ emissions,
the inclusion of a CO$_2$ emissions target,
lower CO$_2$ emissions than the industry.
The effect on the cost of capital can be estimated with
\emph{propensity score matching} wrt 
size, % log(assets)
leverage, % liabilities / equity
ROA, B/P
industry.
