High state-level property crime leads 
to a higher cost of capital.

% Liquidity
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Linguistic complexity in firm disclosures:
  obfuscation or information?} \\
  B.J. Bushee et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Linguistic complexity
(measured by some linear combination of
  the average number of words per sentence,
  the average number of syllables per word,
  the proportion of ``complex'' words:
  Gunning fog index, Fleisch-Kincaid index)
in manager-driven discourse 
is a sign of obfuscation,
but it is informative in analyst-driven discourse.

One can assess the relation with \emph{information asymmetry},
measured by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\text{Amihud liquidity} = 
  \dfrac{
    \va{\text{daily returns}}
  }{
    \text{dollar volume}
  };$
\item $
  \lambda_{GH} = 
  2( \lambda_1 + \lambda_2 \times \text{average size} )
  $,
  where
  \begin{align*}
  \Delta p_t &= 
  \psi_1 \Delta D_t + 
  \psi_2 ( \Delta D_t \cdot \text{Size} ) + \\
  &\hphantom{{}={}}
  \lambda_1 D_t + \lambda_2 D_1 \cdot \text{Size} +
  \text{noise} \\
  p_t &= \text{price} \\
  D_t &= \operatorname{sign}( \text{trade} _t );
  \end{align*}
\item $\lambda_{\text{MRR}}$ in 
 \begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ \Delta p_t }{ p_t } &= 
  \psi \Delta D_t + 
  \lambda ( D_t - \rho D_{t-1} ) +
  \text{noise} \\
  D_t &= \text{noise} + \rho D_{t-1}.
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}

% Liquidity
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Liquidity risk in credit default swap markets} \\
  B. Junge and A.B. Trolle (2013)
\end{book}
Market illiquidity can be measured 
by the difference between the price of an index CDS 
and its theoretical price, 
from the CDSes of its constituents.
The corresponding index arbitrage portfolio
is a \emph{tradable liquidity factor}.
It can be used in a multi-factor model, as we do for equities.

