\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{ParadisEO-MOEO: 
  a framework\\ for evolutionary 
  multi-objective optimization} \\
  A. Liefooghe et al. (2007)
\end{book}
ParadisEO is a C++ framework
% just a framework
for multiobjective optimization, \ie, 
for the estimation of the Pareto frontier.
Here are some of the ideas used in those algorithms: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pareto ranking: a solution's rank is 
  the number of solutions dominating it
  (rank-1 solutions are optimal, at least in the sample);
\item Pareto front: one can group the candidate solutions 
  into ``fronts'' of increasing Pareto rank, 
  to preserve diversity;
\item One can eep both an archive of Pareto optimal solutions 
  and a current population;
  the strength of an archive solution is the number 
  of population solutions it dominates;
  the strength of a population solution is 
  the sum of the strengths of the archive solutions 
  dominating it.
\end{compactitem}
















% AUC
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The area under the ROC curve\\ and its competitors} \\
  J. Hilden (1991)
\end{book}
There are a few variants of the ROC curve: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item OROC (ordinary ROC),
\[ 
  P( \text{positive} |      D ) \sim 
  P( \text{positive} | \neg D );
\]
\item FROC (frequency-scaled ROC),
\[ 
  P( \text{positive} ,      D ) \sim 
  P( \text{positive} , \neg D );
\]
\item EUROC (expected utility ROC),
\[ 
  E[ U(\text{treatment}) | x,      D ] \sim
  E[ U(\text{treatment}) | x, \neg D ]
\]
\end{compactitem}
The AUC can be interpreted as the probability 
of correctly distinguishing between 
a diseased ($D$) and a non-diseased ($\neg D$) patient, 
but it is not helpful for a single patient.
The power of a diagnostic test ($x$) is better measured
by its \emph{diagnasticity}:
\begin{align*}
  \text{Prior regret} &= - \max \{
    E[ U(      T ) ], 
    E[ U( \neg T ) ]
  \} \\
  \text{Posterior regret} &= % Function of x
  - \max \{
    E[ U(      T ) | x ], 
    E[ U( \neg T ) | x ]
  \} \\
  \text{Diagnosticity} &= 
    \text{Prior regret} - E[ \text{Posterior regret} ]
\end{align*}

% AUC
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Use and misuse of the receiver operating characteristic curve in risk prediction} \\
  N.R. Cook (2007)
\end{book}
Do not use the AUC (or its generalization for survival data, 
the $c$ statistic)
for model selection

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Visualizing distributions\\ of covariance matrices} \\
  T. Tokuda et al.
\end{book}
To visualize a distribution of covariance matrices (e.g., a prior),
look at: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Univariate distributions of the log-volatilities and the correlations
  (since distributions used as priors are often exchangeable, there are few of them to consider: $\log \sigma_1$ and $\rho_{12}$);
\item Bivariate scatterplots;
\item Trivariate scatterplot of correlations
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
  1 & \rho_{12} & \rho_{13} \\
  \rho_{12} & 1 & \rho_{23} \\
  \rho_{13} & \rho_{23} & 1
\end{pmatrix};
\]
\item A hundred 50\% equiprobability matrices 
  for $N( 0, \var(X_1,X_j))$;
\item Histogram of the \emph{effective variance} 
   $\va{V}^{1/n}$
   and the \emph{effective dependence}
   $ 1 - \va{ \text{cov2cor}(V) }^{1/n} $
   (highlight samples with a high or low 
   effective dependence in the other plots);
\item The effective dependence of 
  $ V_{1:k,1:k} $ versus $k$.   
\end{compactitem}

Commonly-used priors are not as uninformative as we would like: 
for instance, for the inverse Wishart, 
$\rho_{ij}$ is uniform, 
but $(\rho_{ij},\rho_{k\ell})$ is not;
even for a uniform correlation, the effective dependence
depends on the size and converges to $1-e^{-1}$.

Those plots are implemented in the \texttt{VisCov} R package.

