\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Trade interpretation and trade imbalances\\
  in the European union: a network perspective} \\
  G.M. Krings et al. (2013)
\end{book}

Flow, in a trade network,
can be decomposed into symmetric,
cyclic (often negligible)
and acyclic components.
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=5pt, row sep=5ex, inner sep = 1pt, cells={nodes={fill=black!10}} ]
  & A \arrow[bend left]{rd}{30} \\
  C \arrow{ur}{10} && B \arrow{ll}{20} \arrow{ul}{10}
\end{tikzcd}
\ = \ 
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=5pt, row sep=5ex, inner sep = 1pt, cells={nodes={fill=black!10}} ]
  & A \arrow[bend left=15]{rd}{10} \\
  C && B \arrow[bend left=15]{ul}{10}
\end{tikzcd}
\ + \ 
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=5pt, row sep=5ex, inner sep = 1pt, cells={nodes={fill=black!10}} ]
  & A \arrow{rd}{10} \\
  C \arrow{ur}{10} && B \arrow{ll}{10}
\end{tikzcd}
\ + \ 
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=5pt, row sep=5ex, inner sep = 1pt, cells={nodes={fill=black!10}} ]
  & A \arrow{rd}{10} \\
  C && B \arrow{ll}{10}
\end{tikzcd}
\]

There are usually several acyclic flow graphs:
choose the most diverse 
(maximize the sum of squared flows -- 
Herfindahl index, \ie, a generalized entropy).
The acyclic graph can be decomposed into 
(single-source, single-destination) elementary flows:
start with the nodes with no inflows as sources 
and no outflows as destinations (as in a topological sort),
compute the maximum flow 
(not unique if there is a bottleneck),
and choose the most diverse one.

Integration between two countries can be defined as 
trade/GDP.

One can also consider the random walk 
of a dollar on the trade network 
(PageRank without teleportation):
the average commute time 
% Formula given
can be used as a distance; 
after multi-dimensional scaling (MDS),
one can watch the time evolution of the network.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Political risk spreads} \\
  G. Bekaert (2013)
\end{book}
The political risk spread
is the proportion of the sovereign spread explained 
by the \emph{political risk rating} 
from the ICRG (international country risk grade),
in a regression that also includes 
global macroeconomic conditions (high-yield spread),
local macroeconomic conditions 
  (GDP, GDP growth, inflation, budget, current account)
and liquidity (incidence of zero daily bond returns).

One can also look at the components 
of the political risk rating.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cluster PIN: a new estimation method\\ for the probability of informed trading} \\
  Q. Gan et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The PIN (probability of informed trading) model 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.2, yscale=.9]
  \tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A) at (0,1) {};
  \node (B) at (1,1.5) {};
  \node (C) at (2,2) {};
  \node (D) at (2,1) {};
  \node (E) at (2,0) {};
  \draw (A) -- (B) -- (C);
  \draw (A) -- (B) -- (D);
  \draw (A) -- (E);
  \tikzstyle{every node}=[inner sep=0pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node[right of=C, node distance=1mm, anchor=south west] {$\text{Buy}_t \sim \text{Pois}(\epsilon_B+\mu)$};
  \node[right of=C, node distance=1mm, anchor=north west] {$\text{Sell}_t \sim \text{Pois}(\epsilon_S)$};
  \node[right of=D, node distance=1mm, anchor=south west] {$\text{Buy}_t \sim \text{Pois}(\epsilon_B)$};
  \node[right of=D, node distance=1mm, anchor=north west] {$\text{Sell}_t \sim \text{Pois}(\epsilon_S+\mu)$};
  \node[right of=E, node distance=1mm, anchor=south west] {$\text{Buy}_t \sim \text{Pois}(\epsilon_B)$};
  \node[right of=E, node distance=1mm, anchor=north west] {$\text{Sell}_t \sim \text{Pois}(\epsilon_S)$};
  \node (AB) at (.5,1.25) {};
  \node (AE) at (1,.5) {};
  \node (BC) at (1.5,1.75) {};
  \node (BD) at (1.5,1.25) {};
  \node[above of=AB, node distance=.5mm, anchor=south east] {\footnotesize news};
  \node[below of=AB, node distance=.5mm, anchor=north west] {\footnotesize $\alpha$};
  \node[below of=AE, node distance=.5mm, anchor=north east] {\footnotesize no news};
  \node[above of=BC, node distance=.5mm, anchor=south east] {\footnotesize \smash{good}};
  \node[below of=BD, node distance=.5mm, anchor=north east] {\footnotesize bad};
  \node[above of=BD, node distance=.5mm, anchor=south west] {\footnotesize $\delta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\] 
\[ 
  \text{PIN} = \dfrac{ 
    \alpha \mu 
  }{ 
    \alpha \mu + \epsilon_B + \epsilon_S
  }
\]
is difficult to fit.
One can approximate it by considering the order imbalance 
$X_t = \text{Buy}_t - \text{Sell}_t$; 
its distribution is a mixture of three 
\emph{Skellam distributions}
(differences of independent Poisson variables),
corresponding to the three regimes 
(no news, bad news, good news).
It is not easier to fit,
but one can use hierarchical clustering 
to approximate the three regimes;
the resulting parameters can then be used 
as starting points for a more exact algorithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Short-horizon CEO incentives\\ and abnormal stock returns,\\ insider trading and earnings management} \\
  J.D. Chi et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Firms with short-horizon CEO incentives 
(data from S\&P ExecuComp)
and high short sales constraints 
(\ie, with inefficient information dispersal, 
as measured by idiosyncratic volatility, 
short interest and size)
are different: 
share price inflation and correction, 
greater eranings surpries,
greater responses to earnings surprises,
higher accruals,
higher revenue for the CEO selling his shares.

To control for exogenous variables, 
the study uses 
\emph{propensity score matching}:
first, predict the probability that a firm has 
short-horizon CEO incentives, with a logit model from: 
  incentive ratio 
    (stock and option compensation, 
    divided by total compensation),
  $P/B$,
  total assets (size),
  ROA,
  volatility of cash flow from operations,
  capital intensity (fixed assets / total assets),
  governance
  (institutional ownership, 
  Gompers's governance index,
  percentage of independent directors on the board,
  board size);
then, compare the long- and short-horizon CEO incentive firms
with a similar predicted probability of short horizon.

