\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Asset volatility} \\
  M. Correia et al. (2013)
\end{book}

Structural bankruptcy models (Merton)
can be improved by better estimates of asset volatility:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\sigma = \lambda \sigma_{\text{Market Value}}$,
  where $\lambda = \text{MV} / ( \text{MV} + \text{debt} )$;
\item 
  \vrule height 1em width 0pt%
  $\sigma = \sqrt{
  \lambda^2 \sigma_{\text{MV}}^2 + 
  (1-\lambda)^2 \sigma^2_{\text{debt}} + 
  2 \rho \sigma_{\text{MV}} \sigma_{\text{debt}}
  }$,
  where $\rho$ is shrunk towards to industry average
  and $\sigma_{\text{debt}}$
  is estimated from a representative bond;
\item Replace $\sigma_{\text{MV}}$
  with the implied volatility 
  (average the put and call volatilities),
  which is more forward-looking;
\item The volatility of the 
  RNOA (return on net operating assets),
  estimated from the inter-quartile range,
  from the quantile regression 
  \begin{align*}
    \text{RNOA}_{t+1} 
    &\sim 
    \text{RNOA}_+ + 
    (\text{RNOA}>0) + 
    \text{RNOA}_- + \\
    &\hphantom{{}\sim{}}
    \text{accruals} + 
    \text{dividends} + 
    (\text{dividends}>0)
  \end{align*}
\item The volatility of the seasonality-adjusted RNOA,
  $\text{RNOA}_t - \text{RNOA}_{t - 4\text{Q}}$.
\end{compactitem}

Instead of a probit model 
\begin{align*}
  \text{bankruptcy} &\sim 
  \text{leverage} + 
  \text{past returns} + \\
  &\hphantom{{}\sim{}}
  \text{market capitalization} + 
  \text{volatility}, 
\end{align*}
a \emph{survival model} (predicting the time to bankruptcy)
makes better use of the data.

% I do not understand what they do with the OAS.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Buffet's alpha} \\
  A. Frazzini et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Berkshire Hathaway's returns 
(at least the public stock portfolio part -- 
the private equity part
and the value of Buffet himself are difficult to replicate)
can be replicated using 
  market, 
  size,
  value,
  low-beta
  and quality factors,
with a 1.6 leverage --
Berkshire have a low financing rate,
thanks to their AAA rating,
the use of insurance as liabilities, 
deferred taxes due to accelerated depreciation (free loans from the IRS),
and the sale of derivatives.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A five-factor asset pricing model} \\
  E.F. Fama and K.R. French (2013)
\end{book}
The Fama-French model
to explain excess returns can be extended
by adding two more factors: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Market excess returns;
\item Returns of the quintile long-short size portfolio;
\item Returns of the quintile long-short $B/P$ portfolio;
\item Returns of the quintile long-short 
  profitability portfolio,
  where
  \[ 
    \text{profitability} = \dfrac{
      \text{revenue} 
      - \text{COGS} - \text{interest} - \text{SGA}
    }{
      \text{book equity}
    };
  \]
\item  Returns of the quintile long-short 
  investment portfolio,
  where 
  \[ 
    \text{investment} = \dfrac{
      \Delta \text{assets}
    }{
      \text{previous assets}
    }.
  \]
\end{compactitem}
The model is not sufficient to describe returns: 
the intercept is significantly different from zero --
however, it is small, 
so it may not matter after all.

