%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Insider trading in Hong Kong: 
  concentrated ownership versus the legal environment} \\
  J. Zhu et al. (2002)
\end{book}
Looking at Jensen's alpha (the intercept in the CAPM model)
or comparing firms with their peers 
(build $3\times3\times3$ tercile portfolios on 
size, $B/P$ and momentum)
suggests that insider purchases are informative, 
while their sales are not.
In contrast with the US,
the effect is more pronounced for large (not small) companies.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio concentration\\
   and the geometry of co-movement} \\
  W. Phoa (JPM, 2013)
\end{book}

\emph{Diffusion maps}
are another dimension reduction technique 
(the article fails to compare it with 
principal component analysis (PCA),
multi-dimensional scaling (MDS),
independent component analysis (ICA),
isomap,
t-SNE, 
Kohonen maps, etc.):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the similarity matrix $1+\cor(X)$;
\item Divide each row by its sum, 
  to have a probability transition matrix;
\item Compute its first $k$ eigenvectors,
  rescale them by the eigenvalues,
  and use their coefficients as coordinates.
\end{compactitem}

One can estimate the \emph{portfolio concentration}
by computing the variance matrix $\Sigma$ 
of the resulting cloud of points
(using the portfolio weights)
and looking at $\sqrt{ \tr \Sigma }$.
One can estimate the \emph{local concentration}
with a kernel estimator.

This can be generalized to other measures of dependence 
(rank correlation, tail dependence, etc.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to diffusion maps} \\
  J. de la Porte et al. (2008)
\end{book}

For the purpose of dimension reduction, 
small distances are more informative than large distances.
Since multi-dimensional scaling (MDS)
weighs them in the same way, 
it struggles at identifying non-linear, 
low-dimensional structures.
Isomap uses the geodesic distance on a graph, 
but is not very robust to perturbations.
Instead, one can consider a diffusion process 
(a random walk -- this is very similar 
to the PageRank algorithm)
on the complete graph, with transition probabilities 
\[ 
  p(x \to y ) \propto 
  \exp - \dfrac{ \n {x - y} ^2 }{ 2 \sigma^2}.
\]
Diffusion maps are projections to a lower-dimensional
space coming from the spectral analysis 
of the transition matrix $P$ (or $P^t$, after $t$ steps) -- 
note that this matrix is not symmetric.
Those projections approximate the \emph{diffusion distance}
\[ D_t(i,j) = \sum_k \va{ P_{ki}^t - P_{kj}^t}^2 \]
(two points are close if they are similarily distant 
from any other third point).

Here are the computations:
let $K$ be the kernel matrix,
$D=\operatorname{diag}(K\mathbf1)$
the (normalizing) density, 
$P=D^{-1}K$ the transition matrix;
$Q = D^{1/2} P D^{-1/2}$ is symmetric
and can be diagonalized 
$Q = S \Lambda S'$; 
$P = (D^{-1/2} S) \Lambda (D^{-1/2} S)^{-1}$
is also diagonalizable and
$D^{-1/2}S=[ \psi_0 | \psi_1 | \cdots | \psi_n ]$
are its right eigenvectors; 
send observation $i$ 
to $(\lambda_1^t \psi_1(i), \dots, \lambda_k^t \psi_k(i))$
for some 
$k \in \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket$
and $t > 0$.

% The article also contains proofs and formulas

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What is... Data Mining} \\
  M. Maggioni (2012)
  % Notices of the AMS
\end{book}

Gentler presentation of diffusion maps.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Speaker identification using diffusion maps} \\
  Y. Michalevsky et al. (2011)
\end{book}
To use diffusion maps for classification tasks
(e.g., speaker identification),
one needs to project \emph{new} observations
on the submanifold, e.g., 
using the \emph{Nystr\"om extension}.
\begin{align*}
  k(x,y) &= \exp - \dfrac{ \n{ x - y }^2 }{ 2 \sigma^2 } \\
  d(i) &= \sum_j k(x_i, x_j) \\
  p(i,j) &= \dfrac{ k(x_i,x_j) }{ d(i) }
\end{align*}
The right eigenvectors $\psi_k$ 
satisfy
\(
  P \psi_k = \lambda_k \psi_k
\), \ie
\[ \psi_k(i) = \dfrac1{\lambda_k} \sum_j p(i,j) \psi_k(j). \]
That formula can be generalized to project any point $z$,
not just one of the $x_i$: 
\begin{align*}
  d(z) &= \sum_j k(z, x_j) \\
  p(z,x_j) &= \dfrac{ k(z,x_j) }{ d(z) } \\
  \psi_k(z) &= \dfrac1{\lambda_k} \sum_j p(z,j) \psi_k(j).
\end{align*}
% Confusing notations.
[There may be a confusion, in this article or in others,
between eigenvectors and singular vectors,
and/or between the matrices $D^{-1}K$ and $D^{-1/2}KD^{-1/2}$.]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diffusion maps} \\
  R.R. Coifman and S. Lafon (2006)
\end{book}
Original article on diffusion maps, in a continuous context.
One can define a \emph{family} of diffusions
(a diffusion is a continuous analogue of a random walk) as
\begin{align*}
  k_\sigma(x,y)&= \exp - \dfrac{\n{x-y}^2}{2\sigma^2} \\
  d_\sigma(x) &= \int k_\sigma(x,y) \, dy \\
  k_{\sigma, \alpha}(x,y) &= 
    \dfrac{
      k_\sigma(x,y)
    }{ 
      d_\sigma(x) ^\alpha
      d_\sigma(y) ^\alpha
    } \\
  d_{\sigma,\alpha}(x) &= \int k_{\sigma,\alpha}(x,y) \, dy \\
  p_{\sigma, \alpha}(x,y) &= 
    \dfrac{
      k_{\sigma,\alpha}(x,y)
    }{ 
      d_{\sigma,\alpha}(x) 
    },
\end{align*}
with $\alpha=0$, $\tfrac12$ or $1$
(there are three parameters: 
$\alpha$, $\sigma$ and $t$, 
the number of steps in the random walk).
With $\alpha=1$, the distribution of the points on the submanifold has no influence.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diffusion maps and coarse-graining:\\
  a unified framework for dimensionality reduction,
  graph partitioning\\
  and data set parametrization} \\
  S. Lafon and A.B. Lee (2006)
\end{book}
For large datasets, one can aggregate the data 
and control the discrepancy on the diffusion distances.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diffusion maps for signal processing} \\
  R. Talmon et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Diffusion maps can be used in signal processing: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For single-channel source localization,
  one can record white noise from various angles,
  compute the corresponding diffusion map,
  notice that its first component $\psi_1$ corresponds 
  to the location (angle),
  and project any new recording onto it.
\item To identify repeating, transient interference
  (keyboard noises, etc.),
  one can compute the diffusion map 
  of the frames of the signal
  and notice that they form two clusters:
  a compact one, with the interference,
  and a more diffuse one, with the signal (speech).
  The interference samples can be averaged 
  (the speech samples are too different:
  averaging them is destructive).
\item Non-local filtering averages a signal $x$ 
  with nearby signals: 
  \( \hat x \propto \sum_i \bar k(x,x_i) x_i; \)
  the kernel $\bar k$ used can come 
  from the diffusion distance: 
  \[ \bar k(x,y) = \exp - \dfrac{D_t^2(x,y)}{2\epsilon^2}. \]
\end{compactitem}

To build diffusion maps,
the Euclidian distance is not always the best choice.
Consider: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Euclidian distance in feature space;
\item Intrinsic distance: 
  cluster the data, compute the covariance $C_i$ 
  of each cluster $i$,
  use the Mahalanobis distance 
  \[ 
    d(x,y) = 
    \dfrac12
    (x-y)' 
    ( 
      C_{\text{cl}(x)}^{-1} +
      C_{\text{cl}(y)}^{-1}
    )
    (x-y).
  \]
%  ($C_i$ may not be invertible'' use the pseudo-inverse.)
\item Local PCA models: 
  use dimension reduction, but only locally,
  to remove the noise when computing small distances
  (they matter more than large distances).
\end{compactitem}

The diffusion map can be computed 
from the singular value decomposition
(not checked).
Let $K$ be the kernel matrix,
$D=\operatorname{diag}(K\mathbf1)$
the (normalizing) density, 
$P=D^{-1}K$ the transition matrix,
$P=U \Lambda V'$ its singular value decomposition,
$V=[ \psi_0 | \psi_1 | \cdots | \psi_n ]$
its right singular vectors,
$\Lambda = \diag(\lambda_0,\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n)$
its singular values;
send observation $i$ 
to $(\lambda_1^t \psi_1(i), \dots, \lambda_k^t \psi_k(i))$
for some 
$k \in \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket$
and $t > 0$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Applications of machine learning to finance} \\
  Z. Cazalet and R.L. Dao
\end{book}

\emph{Lasso regression} can be used 
to replicate hedge funds with a parsimonious portfolio.

The \emph{non-negative matrix factorization} (NMF)
\[ A = B C \]
with $\forall i,j,k \  A_{ij}, B_{ik}, C_{kj} \geq 0$, 
$A$ square, $B$ and $C$ rectangular and small
can be used as an alternative to 
principal component analysis (PCA) 
or independent component analysis (ICA),
with log-prices (not price returns);
stocks can be clustered using the corresponding factors.

When fitting classification models
(probit, etc.),
try ensemble methods such as 
\emph{bagging} or \emph{boosting}.

The \emph{Hodrick-Prescott} (HP) filter is a low-pass filter
with an $L^2$ penalty on the second derivative, 
\[
  \argmin_y 
  \dfrac12 \n{ y - x }_{L^2}^2 + 
  \lambda \n{ D_2 x }_{L^2}^2.
\]
One can use an $L^1$ penalty instead:
the $L^2$ penalty tends to ``oversmooth'' the signal
and the $L^1$ penalty tolerates isolated singularities.

Add support vector machines (SVM),
with cross-validation,
to your toolbox.
They can also be used to smooth a signal
[not clearly explained].

