\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Liquidity planning tools and strategy capacity 
  for equity markets} \\
  D. diBartolomeo
  (Northfield, 2013)
\end{book}

The risk of a portfolio increases with size. 
To estimate it, decide on a \emph{liquidation policy}
(cost of liquidating $x$\% in $n$ days 
in a normal/crisis situation),
compute the volatility of the portfolio, 
convert it to a value at risk (VaR),
add the liquidity costs (from your transaction cost model), 
and convert the corrected VaR back to a volatility.

