%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data stream mining: a practical approach}\\
  A. Bifet et al. (2011)
\end{book}

\textsc{moa} is a Java library for machine learning
on streaming data, an online analogue of Weka.
Contrary to Vowpal Wabbit, 
it only allows a small number of features (hundreds),
focuses on algorithms with theoretical guarantees,
(mainly decision trees and ensemble methods),
and can deal with time-changing models
(\emph{concept drift}).

\textbf{1.}
\d{Hoefding trees} (VFDT, very fast decision trees)
only look at each observation once, 
and split a node if the information gain 
and/or the difference between the best and the next best variable to split on 
exceeds some threshold (computed from \emph{Hoefding's inequality});
this requires storing sufficient statistics at each node.
To save memory, some nodes can be disactivated.
To save time, the information gains are only computed every 200 observations.
Quantitative variables can be approximated by histogram (quantile) summaries or a Gaussian approximation.

For prediction, one can use the majority class of a leaf,
or use the sufficient statistics stored in that leaf in a naive Bayes classifier,
or use whichever method worked best for that leaf in the past.

To estimate performance, use measures robust to unbalanced classes, 
such as \emph{Cohen's kappa}.

\textbf{2.}
\d{Online bagging} estimates several models in parallel
(a small number, to keep resource usage low),
including each observation $\text{Pois}(1)$ times
(offline bagging corresponds to a binomial distribution,
whose limit is a Poisson distribution).
To improve online bagging: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Include each observation $\text{Pois}(\lambda)$ times,
  with $\lambda > 1$, to increase weight diversity;
\item Output a random class (sampled according to the predicted probabilities), instead of the majority one.
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Online boosting} is similar, but with $\text{Pois}(\lambda_{i,m})$ instead, 
for observation $i$ and model $m$, with $\lambda_{i,1} = 1$,
and $\lambda_{i,m+1}$ computed from $\lambda_{i,m}$ 
depending on whether observation $i$ is classified properly by model $m$.
Online boosting does not perform as well as online bagging, 
especially in the presence of changes.

Instead of separate models, \emph{option trees}, 
\ie, trees with option (non-deterministic) nodes (both branches are taken)
are more memory-efficient.

Ensembles of classifiers of different sizes
(say, $2^n$, for various values of $n$ -- 
when the size exceeds the threshold, 
either delete the root (the oldest node)
and all its children except one -- 
or delete everything and start afresh)
perform better: smaller trees adapt quickly,
and larger trees perform better when there is no change
(use weights proportional to the inverse of the square of the error rate, 
estimated with an exponential moving average).

To avoid overfitting, one can use depth-limited trees,
or trees built on distinct subsets of the attributes
(all $k$-element subsets)
and combined with a (softmax) neural network,
trained alongside the trees.

\textbf{3.}
To account for concept drift, one can: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit the model on a moving window;
\item Review the previous decisions, at each node, and check if we would still make the same decision;
\item Check if the error rate decreases (it should, as long as the data-generation process does not change).
\end{compactitem}
The data in the moving window can be used,
not only to detect change, but also to update or refit the model.
The windows can be: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A fixed window of size $W$ (reference) and the last $W$ observations;
\item The last $W$ observations and the $W$ observations immediately before;
\item All the possible partitions of the previous $W$ observations into two windows.
\end{compactitem}
This last idea can be improved (\textsc{adwin}):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Only consider $\log W$ windows (the past $\lfloor c\rfloor$, $\lfloor c^2\rfloor$, 
  $\lfloor c^3\rfloor$, etc. observations);
\item Use an approximate data structure (exponential histogram) to store the data needed;
% It is actually exact, but for slightly different windows
\item Instead of an actual statistical test, use a threshold,
  derived from Hoefding's inequality.
\end{compactitem}
This can be combined with a Kalman filter.
% (use the window size to estimate the state noise and the observation noise)
For Hoefding trees, look for changes at each node, but adjust the threshold for multiple testing.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to stream:\\ a framework for data stream mining}\\
  J. Forrest et al.
\end{book}
\texttt{stream} is an R package for machine learning on data streams,
focusing on clustering
(cluster the data into a large number of micro-clusters, online, 
then cluster the micro-clusters into macro-clusters, offline).
The interface allows for other mining tasks (classification, itemset mining), 
but there is no implementation yet.
The clustering algorithms from \textsc{moa} are available.
% Uses the following R packages: clue, fpc, birch, mclust, flexmix, proxy (similarity computations)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A general framework for observation-driven time-varying parameter models}\\
  D. Creal et al. (2008)
\end{book}
GAS (generalized autoregressive score) models are a unified framework to describe GARCH-like models
(observation-driven models, \ie, the time-dependence of the paramters comes from lagged, observed values -- 
as opposed to, say, stochastic volatility)
\begin{align*}
  y_t &\sim p( y | y_1, \dots, y_{t-1} ; f_1, \dots, f_{t-1} ; x_1, \dots, x_t ; \theta ) \\
  f_t & = \omega + \sum_i A_i s_{t-i} + \sum_j B_j f_{t-j} \\
  s_t &= S_{t-1} \dfrac{ \partial \log p }{ \partial f_{t-1} } \\
  S_{t-1} &= I \text{ or } 
  S_{t-1} = E_{t-1} \left[ 
    \dfrac{ \partial \log p }{ \partial f_t } 
    \dfrac{ \partial \log p' }{ \partial f_t } 
  \right] ^{-1}.
\end{align*}
(This can be seen as a kind of smoothed or shrunk Kalman filter.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Structural and toppological phase transitions on the German stock exchange}\\
  A. Sienkiewicz et al. (2013)
\end{book}
The minimum spanning tree (MST) computed from the correlation of stock returns 
% use $d_{ij} = \sqrt{ 1 - \cor_{ij} }$ as distance
changes with time, 
sometimes a scale-free network (the degree distribution is a power law),
sometimes a star-like network (idem, with one outlier).
% They looked at other markets and wrote an article for each...

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Coupling between time series: a network view}\\
  S. Mehraban et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Some of the properties of a time series can be read from its visibility graph:
the vertices are the timestamps, and there is an edge between $i$ and $j$
if $\forall k \in \mathopen\rrbracket i, j \mathclose\llbracket$,
\[ y_k < y_i + \dfrac{ k - i }{ j - i } (y_j - y_i). \]
% For instance: the degree distribution, its exponent if it is a power law.
It can be generalized to pairs of time series (normalize them both first): 
edge between $i$ and $j$
if 
\[ \forall k \in \mathopen\rrbracket i, j \mathclose\llbracket \quad y_k \leq y_i + \dfrac{ k - i }{ j - i } (x_j - x_i) \]
or 
\[ \forall k \in \mathopen\rrbracket i, j \mathclose\llbracket \quad y_k \geq y_i + \dfrac{ k - i }{ j - i } (x_j - x_i). \]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamics of episodic transient correlations\\ in currency exchange rate returns\\ and their predictability}\\
  M. \v Zukovi\v c (2013)
\end{book}
One can look for predicability in financial time series 
by testing 
% with a statistical test
if the correlation $C_{xx}(s)$ 
or the bicorrelation $C_{xxx}(r,s)$ of the log-returns
\begin{align*}
  C_{xx}(r) &= E [ x_t x_{t-r} ] \\
  C_{xxx}(r,s) &= E[ x_t x_{t-r} x_{t-s} ],
\end{align*}
estimated on a moving window,
is zero.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dancing links}\\
  D.E. Knuth (2000)
\end{book}
Dancing links, \ie, the remark that it is easy to remove 
\emph{and add back} an element in a doubly-linkes list,
can be used to efficiently keep track of the current state 
in depth-first (backtracking) search.
For instance, in the \emph{exact set cover} problem
(given a boolean matrix, find a set of rows
with exactly one 1 in each column), one can encode the matrix
as two doubly-linked lists (rows and columns),
and descending in the tree just removes a column and one or several rows.
This can be used in many satisfaction problems: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Packing pentominoes on a chessboard: 
  one row for each possible position of each individual pentomino, 
  one column for each chessborad square,
  1 if it is occupied;
\item The $n$-queen problem: one row for each possible queen position,
  one column for each row, column or diagonal, 
  1 if it is controlled;
  % This looks like a maximum cover problem, not an exact cover one.
\item Sudoku: one row for each possible decision of the form ``digit $i$ in $(k,l)$'',
  columns for constraints: ``there is a number in $(k,l)$'',
  ``there is an $i$ in row $k$'',
  ``there is an $i$ in column $l$'',
  ``there is an $i$ in square $m$''.
% Also check: 
%   Dancing links: solving sudoku, Xi Chen
%     http://cgi.cse.unsw.edu.au/~xche635/dlx_sodoku/
%     http://uaa.wtf.im/?page_id=27
\end{compactitem}

