\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Deep learning via hessian-free approximation}\\
  J. Martens (2010)
\end{book}

Newton's method to minimize $f$ 
approximates the objective as 
$q_\theta(p) = f(\theta) + \nabla f(\theta) ' p + \tfrac12 p' B p$
and minimizes it by solving $\nabla f(\theta) + B p = 0$.
Instead of exactly minimizing this second order Taylor expansion,
minimize it approximately by a few conjugate gradient (CG) steps -- 
the whole hessian $B$ is not needed,
only a few products $Bp$ are (\emph{truncated Newton}).

\[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  title={Gradient descent},
  view={-5}{30},
  width=5cm, height=3.5cm,
  zmin=0, zmax=1, 
  colormap/blackwhite,
  colormap={blackwhite}{gray(0cm)=(.5); gray(1cm)=(1)},
  axis lines=none
]
\addplot3[surf, domain=-3:3] {x^2/(1+x^2)};
%\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(-2,2,.8) (2,1,.8) (-2,0,.8) (2,-1,.8) (-2,-2,.8)};
\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(-2,2,.8) (2,1,.8)};
\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(2,1,.8) (-2,0,.8)};
\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(-2,0,.8) (2,-1,.8)};
\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(2,-1,.8) (-2,-2,.8)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\qquad
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  title={Newton},
  view={-5}{30},
  width=5cm, height=3.5cm,
  zmin=0, zmax=1, 
  %colormap/blackwhite,
  colormap={blackwhite}{gray(0cm)=(.5); gray(1cm)=(1)},
  axis lines=none
]
\addplot3[surf, domain=-3:3] {x^2/(1+x^2)};
%\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(-2,2,.8) (-.5,2,.2) (0,1,0) (0,0,0) (0,-1,0) (0,-2,0)};
\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(-2,2,.8) (-.5,2,.2)};
\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(-.5,2,.2) (0,0,0) };
\addplot3[->,ultra thick] coordinates {(0,0,0) (0,-2,0)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

The article also lists a few modifications, useful for neural networks:
damping (do not move too far: the quadratic approximation should remain good),
back-propagation to compute products with the (Gauss-Newton approximation of the) hessian,
mini-batches (large, and constant during the CG runs),
termination condition for the CG,
\emph{no} backtracking (in the CG runs, keeping the parameter corresponding to the best value of $f$, 
as opposed to the last value, \ie, the best value of $q_\theta$),
preconditioning (change of variables),
start each CG iteration in the direction of the previous one.

