\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Beyond implied volatility:\\ extracting information from option prices}\\
  R. Cont (1997)
\end{book}
In an incomplete market, the \emph{state price density} (SPD) is not unique;
to choose one, 
take that that minimizes hedging risk (for a given option)
among all probability densities that give arbitrage-free prices 
(or among all densities, if hedging risk is more important than absence of arbitrage),
or that closest to the historical density.

To estimate the SPD, use the option prices (or the volatility smile): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Taylor expansion of the logarithm of the Fourier transform (cummulants, Edgeworth expansion);
\item Hermite expansion of both the SPD and the payoff; % easy to implement
\item Kernel estimator of the call option prices $C$ to compute the density
\[ e^{ r ( T - t ) } \dfrac{ \partial ^ 2 C }{ \partial K ^ 2 } \]
\item Maximum entropy distribution under the constraints that the market prices are fair
  (without further constraints, it is often multimodal);
\item Implied binomial trees (unstable);
\item Mixture of log-normals (thin tails).
\end{compactitem}

As an application, one can assume that the volatility is a function of the price,
$\sigma = \sigma(S)$, 
estimate this function,
compute the corresponding SPD,
compare with another estimator (say, a kernel estimator),
and use the difference as an investment signal.

The article also stresses the difference between expectation pricing 
(what accountants do) and no-arbitrage pricing.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring capital market efficiency:\\ global and local correlations structure}\\
  L. Kristoufek and M. Vosvrda (2012)
\end{book}
A low fractal dimension indicates herding;
fractal dimension $D$ and Hurst exponent $H$ can be combined to form a ``market efficiency measure''.
\begin{align*}
  r_{xx}(0) - r_{xx}(\tau) & \underset{\tau \to 0}{\propto} \tau^{ 4 - 2D } \\
  r_{xx}(\tau) & \underset{ \tau \to \infty }{\propto} \tau^{ 2H - 2 } \\
  r_{xx}(\tau) &= \operatorname*{Mean}\limits_t [ x_t x_{t+\tau} ]
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Calibration of optimal execution\\ of financial transactions\\ in the presence of transient market impact}\\
  E. Busseti and F. Lillo (2012)
\end{book}
% Very clear
Finding the trading strategy (how much to trade in each 5-minute period, 
using the information available at that time, to trade a total quantity $X$ during the day)
to ``minimize'' the execution cost 
(the difference between the profit if everything had been traded at the open price 
and the actual profit -- 
it is a random variable, 
so by looking at its expectation and variance,
one can build a whole efficient frontier of trading strategies)
can be formulated and solved as a quadratic problem.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to nloptr:\\ an R interface to NLopt}\\
  J. Ypma (2013)
\end{book}
\href{http://ab-initio.mit.edu/wiki/index.php/NLopt_Algorithms}{NLOpt}
is a set of optimization algorithms,
for constrained or unconstrained, global or local, gradient-free or not:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
% Global
\item Dividing rectangles (\textsc{direct}, StoGO):
  not unlike Lipschitz optimization (if $f$ is Lipschitz with constant $k$, then 
  $\forall x \in [a,b] \  f(x) \geq f(a) - k (x-a)$, 
  $f(x) \geq f(b) - k (b-x)$; by refining the intervals one can build a piecewise lower approximation of $f$),
  but without the knowledge of the Lipschitz constant;
%\item Locally-biased \textsc{direct};
\item Controlled random search (\textsc{crs}) with local mutation,
improved stochastic ranking evolution strategy (\textsc{isres}):
population-based random Nelder-Mead;
\item Multi-level single linkage (local search, 
  with many random or low-discrepancy restarts,
  that tries to avoid points leading to already-visited extrema);
% Local, Gradient-free
\item Constrained optimization by linear approximations;
\item Bounded optimization by quadratic approximations;
\item Principal axis (Brent);
\item Nelder-Mead, Subplex;
% Gradient-based
\item Method of moving asymptotes (\textsc{mma});
\item Sequential quadratic programming (\textsc{slsqp});
\item \textsc{l-bgfs};
\item Augmented Lagrangian (to turn a constrained optimization problem into a sequence of unconstrained ones).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An effective implementation of the Lin-Kernighan traveling salesman heuristic}\\
  L. Helsgaun
\end{book}
The 2-opt move for the traveling salesman problem 
(remove 2 edges and rewire in the only possible way)
can be extended to $k$-opt moves
(remove $k$ edges and rewire in one of the many possible ways).
The Lin-Kernighan  heuristic uses the same idea,
but extends a $(k-1)$-opt move into a $k$-opt move,
but limits the choice of the $k$-th edge to the edge after the $(k-1)$st edge.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The traveling salesman provlem:\\ a case study in local optimization}\\
  D.S. Johnson and L.A. McGeoch (1995)
\end{book}
Among other algorithms, here are two heuristics to address the problem.
The Clarke-Wright savings heuristics starts with a star path 
(return to the starting point after visiting each city) and progressively joins ``nearby'' cities.
The Christofides heuristics constructs a minimum spanning tree, 
finds a minimum-length matching of its vertices of odd-degree
(producing a graph all of whose vertices have even degree),
and finds a Euler tour.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The late-acceptance hill-climbing heurstic}\\
  E. Burke and Y. Bykov (2012)
\end{book}
A variant of hill-climbing, as fast as, easier to implement than, and more robust than simulated annealing: 
keep a queue of past values; 
accept a candidate if it is better than the current solution or the solution in the queue $L$ steps ago; 
put the candidate in the queue if it is better than the current solution.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The late-acceptance hill-climbing algorithm\\ for the magic square problem}\\
  Y. Bykov
\end{book}
Another application of LAHC,
to \emph{magic frames} 
(to build a large magic square, 
% Onyl works for large squares
constrain the problem some more, 
and look for nested magic squares -- 
the magic frame problems are smaller and independent).

(But there are constructive magic square algorithms: 
Siamese method, LUX method.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The vehicle routing problem}\\
  T.G. Hinton (2010)
\end{book}
Heuristics to solve the traveling salesman problem (TSP) include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The minimum spanning tree (MST) (pre-traversal order, after choosing a root);
\item The \emph{Christofides heuristic}:
  build the MST,
  find a minimum matching of the odd-degree vertices,
  find a Eulerian tour (\ie, it visits all edges) of the resulting graph (all the vertices now have even degree),
  prune it to have a tour;
\item Local search with $k$-opt neighbourhoods 
  (remove $k$ edges and rewire the graph into a tour in all possible ways);
  to speed up the search, use a neighbourhood list 
  (only consider the $k=40$ closest nodes of each node);
  with simulated annealing, select the starting temperature 
  so that the average probability of accepting a detrimental move be 0.75.
\end{compactitem}
Here are a few heuristics to solve the vehicle routing problem (VRP):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Local search with $\lambda$-interchange neighbourhoods 
  (swap $\lambda$ vertices between two routes);
\item $n$-opt-$k$: pick $k$ routes, remove $n$ edges, rewire in all possible ways;
\item Iterated tour partition: 
  solve the TSP problem, pick a node, start there, follow the tour until full capacity, start another tour, iterate until the end;
\item Clarke-Wright (CW) heuristic (agglomerative greedy algorithm):
  start with 1-city routes,
  merge two routes, iterate -- the routes to merge are chose with the CW savings: 
\[ s(i,j) = c_{0,i} + c_{0,j} - c_{i,j} \]
  or their modified version
\[ s(i,j) = c_{0,1} + c_{0,j} - \lambda c_{i,j} + \mu \va{ c_{0,i} - c_{0,j} } + \nu \dfrac{ d_i + d_j }{ \bar d } \]
where $\bar d$ is the average demand
and $\lambda, \mu, \nu \in [0,2]$ are chosen by exhaustive search
(they try to avoid ``ring routes'' and favour large demands).
It can be randomized: sort the possible moves from best to worst
and take the $i$th one with probability $\tfrac1q \textbf{1}_{ i \leq q }$ or $p(1-p)^i$.
\end{compactitem}

% NP-hard: not necessarily in NP (many optimization problems)
% NP-complete: in NP (many satisfyability probems)

\begin{book}
  \emph{Factors on demand}\\
  A. Meucci (2010)
\end{book}
Factor risk models are often used to both estimate and decompose risk.
One can use different factors for estimation and attribution:
given the joint distribution of $(F,Z)$ or $(X,Z)$
(often, simulated data),
find $d$ to minimize the discrepancy 
(variance, $R^2$, VaR, etc.)
between $R(X)$ and $d'Z$.

\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=small,]
  F : \text{estimation factors} 
  \arrow[-]{r}{}
  \arrow{d}{}
  & Z : \text{attribution factors} 
  \arrow{ddd}{} \\
  \epsilon = BF + U \text{ invariants}
  \arrow{d}{\text{dim reduction}} \\
  X : \text{risk factors} 
  \arrow{d}{\text{pricing}} \\
  R(X) : \text{portfolio returns}
  \arrow[-]{r}{}
  & d' Z
  %\RR^n \arrow{rr}{h} & & \RR^m \\
  %& \Omega \arrow{ul}{X} \arrow[swap]{ur}{Y=h(X)}
\end{tikzcd}
\]

For instance: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Statictical estimation factors (random matrix theory, RMT)
  and industry attribution factors.
\item $X = Z = \text{stock returns}$ and
  $R(X) = $ price of an option on this stock 
  defines \ ``non-linear delta''
  \[ 
    %\Delta_{\text{non-linear}} = 
    \argmin_d 
    \operatorname{CVaR} 
    \left( 
      \text{option returns} - d \times \text{stock return}
    \right) 
  \]
\item Use detailed, regional (numerous) estimation factors and (fewer) global attribution factors;
\item Style analysis (maximize the $R^2$ between the portfolio returns and a linear combination of reference strategies)
  % But for this, you need to know the portfolio weights
\item Risk attribution to the alpha components: 
  use portfolios associated to the components of the alpha as attribution factors
  and minimize the variance of the residuals.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk management\\ for central bank foreign reserves}\\
  ECB (2004)
\end{book}

%1. Strategic asset allocation for foreign exchange reserves (P. Cardon and J. Coche)
%Use a 3-tier governance structure: strategic asset allocation (SAA), tactical asset allocation (TAA), portfolio management (PM).

%2.Thoughts on investment guidelines for institutions with special liquidity and capital preservation requirements (B.H. Putnam)
%Foreign reserves are often separated into two sub-portfolios, 
%managed independently: liquidity constrained, and not liquidity-constrained.

%*3. A framework for strategic foreign reserves risk management (S. Claessens and J. Kreuser)
3. Foreign reserves management is similar to assets and liabilities management (ALM), 
evaluated on a tree modeling the evolution of short rate and exchange rates, 
with a few differences: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The portfolio is often separated into liquid and non-liquidity-constrained parts;
\item Choosing a base currency is problematic;
\item Constraints of the form 
\[ \text{reserves} \geq  \text{12-month debt repayments} + \text{imports} \]
  are imposed on the liquid portfolio;
\item If the distribution of final wealth is not satisfactory, 
it can be ``shaped'' by adding (several) value at risk (VaR) constraints.
\end{compactitem}

%4. Asset allocation for central banks: optimally combining liquidity, duration, currency and non-government risk (S.J. Fisher and M.C. Lie)
4. Foreign reserves are often excessively constrained.
If you can tolerate short-term underperformance, add corporate bonds;
relaxing the (liquidity, currency, duration, credit) constraints, 
and allowing for a larger universe,
moves the efficient frontier:
you can gain 1.5\% in expected returns or risk -- 
assuming that your estimates of the various market parameters are correct.
%5.
%6. The risk of diversification (P. Ferket and M. Zwanenburg)
However, as you diversify your portfolio, measuring risk becomes trickier.

%*7. Currency reserve management by dual benchmark optimization (A. Gintschel and B. Scherer)
7. The conflicting targets, wealth preservation (in the home currency) 
and liquidity preservation (ability to pay in foreign currencies),
call for different benchmarks.
One can use robust (worst-case) optimization 
\[ 
  \max_{\substack{ w \geq 0 \\ w' 1 = 1}} 
  \min_{s,b} 
  \bigl[ 
    w' \mu - \lambda_{s,b} (w - w_b) ' V_s (w-w_b) 
  \bigr] 
\]
to address multiple benchmarks $b$,
multiple scenarios $s$ (normal and hectic, with the same correlation, but different volatilities,
estimated with robust (shrinkage) estimators),
and different ``weights'' (risk aversions) $\lambda_{s,b}$ in each case.
The problem can be reformulated as a linear problem with quadratic constraints.
For non-Gaussian returns, directly optimize the expected utility with a set of scenarios.

%8. Risk systems in central bank reserves management (M. Dwyer and J. Nug\'ee)
%VaR

%9. Corporate bonds in central bank reserves portfolios: a strategic asset allocation perspective (R.L. Grava)
%If you can tolerate short-term underperformance, add corporate bonds.

%*10. Setting counterparty credit limits for the reserves portfolio (S. Ramaswamy)
10. To account for counterparty risk (credit risk), do not simply look at the credit ratings, but check: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The probability of default
\(  P[ \text{default} ];  \)
\item The expected and unexpected loss (given default), 
\begin{align*}
  \text{EL} &= E     [ \text{loss} | \text{default} ] \\
  \text{UL} &= \sigma[ \text{loss} | \text{default} ];
\end{align*}
\item The portfolio expected loss
\( \sum_i \text{EL}_i, \)
  the portfolio unexpected loss 
\[  \text{UL}' \, \rho \, \text{UL} \]
  where $\rho$ is either the default correlation (``default'' is a Bernoulli random variable)
  or the correlation of the asset returns.
\end{compactitem}
As usual, the expected and unexpected portfolio loss can be decomposed into a sum of contributions
and/or used in portfolio optimization.

%11. Multi-factor risk analysis of bond portfolios (L. Dynkin and J. Hyman)
%Use risk factors directly interpretable by portfolio managers (even if they are not independent.
%For a more modern approach, check Factors on Demand, A. Meucci, 2010.

%12. Managing market risks: a balance sheet approach (B. Boertje and H. van der Hoorn)
%A portfolio optimization problem can be written in accounting terms
%(long positions are in the asset side, short positions in the liability side of the balance sheet).

%*13. Ex post risk attribution in a value-at-risk framework (E. Puschkarski)
13. Instead of decomposing the risk (ex ante),
one can decompose the \emph{change} in risk (ex post), either as 
\begin{align*}
  \Delta \text{VaR}
  &= \text{VaR}( w_t,     p_{t-1}, t\!-\!1 ) \!-\! \text{VaR}( w_{t-1}, p_{t-1}, t\!-\!1 ) \\
  &\hphantom{={}}   + \text{VaR}( w_{t-1}, p_t,     t\!-\!1 ) \!-\! \text{VaR}( w_{t-1}, p_{t-1}, t\!-\!1 )\\
  &\hphantom{={}}   + \text{VaR}( w_{t-1}, p_{t-1}, t   ) \!-\! \text{VaR}( w_{t-1}, p_{t-1}, t\!-\!1 ) \\
  &\hphantom{={}}   + \text{rest}
\end{align*}
or 
\begin{align*}
  \Delta \text{VaR} &= \text{VaR}( w_t,     p_t, t   ) \!-\! \text{VaR}( w_{t-1}, p_t,     t\!-\!1 )  \\
  &\hphantom{={}}   + \text{VaR}( w_{t-1}, p_t, t\!-\!1 ) \!-\! \text{VaR}( w_{t-1}, p_{t-1}, t\!-\!1 ),
\end{align*}
where $w$ are the portfolio weights, $p$ the market data (prices) and $t$ the time.
This helps understand why the risk limits are breached -- to avoid blindly selling positions.

%14. Ruin theory revisited (P. Embrechts et al.)
%Introduction to \emph{extreme value theory} (EVT) and \emph{ruin theory}.
%Also check:
%  Calculation of accregate loss distributions (P.V. Shevchenko, 2010)
%  Bayesian methods for measuring operational risk (C. Alexander, 2002)
%  Modeling and evaluating insurance risk using actuar (RMetrics workshop, 2009)

%15.

%16. Management of currency distribution and duration (K, Bauer et al.)
%Example of 2-asset portfolio optimization, with VaR.
%Also check what happens to the portfolio in some scenarios (large payment in one currency; entering the Eurozone).
%Treat currency and duration separately.
%A constant-duration benchmark is not constant, it depends on the short rates.

%*17. Foreign reserves risk management in Hong Kong (C. Ho)
17.
To decompose the risk of a portfolio, use 
risk factors directly interpretable by portfolio managers (even if they are not independent --
for a more modern approach, check Meucci's \emph{factors on demand}).
For instance, for a fixed income portfolio, 
use use shift, slope and curvature factors; 
for an equity portfolio, use 
size,
value (P/E, P/B, dividend yield)
and momentum (earnings revisions, price reversals, earnings torpedo (high expected earnings and price drop))
%  (more momentum factors than I am used to)
factors.

%*18. Performance attribution analysis -- a homemade solution (A. Simicak and M. Zajac)
18. The performance of a fixed income portfolio can be decomposed 
into time, curve and spread (\ie, benchmark) components: 
\begin{align*}
  \Delta &= P(t,t) - P(t-1,t-1) \\
  &= B(t,t)   - B(t,t-1)     & \text{time} \\
  &\hphantom{={}} + B(t,t-1) - B(t-1,t-1) & \text{curve} \\
  &\hphantom{={}} + \text{rest} & \text{spread}
\end{align*}
where 
\begin{align*}
  P(\text{curve}, \text{time}) &= \text{portfolio value} \\
  B(\text{curve}, \text{time}) &= \text{benchmark value}
\end{align*}

%*19. Performance attribution for fixed income portfolios in central bank of Brazil international reserves management (A.F. de Almeida)
19. The PnL of fixed income portfolios can be decomposed into 
benchmark, duration, allocation and selection components,
where ``duration'' captures the effect of the difference 
between the duration of the benchmark and that of the portfolio.
% [The article gives formulas with no justification.]
The factors used to explain the PnL should be portfolio-dependent. 
%[a few examples]
%The attribution depends on the frequency.

%*20. Management of the international reserve liquidity portfolio (D. Delgado Ruiz et al.)
20. Liquidity crises can be modeled as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Random inflows and outflows occur daily, and have to be addressed by the overnight tranche of the portfolio;
\item If the overnight tranche exceeds some threshold ON*, the excess is moved to the weekly tranche;
\item If the weekly    tranche exceeds some threshold  W*, the excess is moved to the optimized portfolio;
\item Every week, the overnight tranche is replenished, up to the target ON*, from the weekly tranche.
\end{compactitem}
Using simulations (e.g., the cashflows follow an auto-regressive (AR) process)
% [it is not clear how they model the inflows or outflows -- an AR process?],
one can estimate the probability of a liquidity crisis, as a function of the target levels ON* and W*.

%21. Determining neutral duration in the Bank of Israel's dollar portfolio (J. Assouline)
21. Choose your benchmark wisely: if it only depends on one parameter, duration,
and if high duration means high returns,
choose the maximum duration so that 
\[  \text{VaR}_{95\%} \geq \tfrac12 \times \text{risk-free returns}  \]
(or some other threshold -- the reference rate could be a constant rate, or a short-term rate minus a spread).

%% 2013-08, 2013-09

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%%
%% 1. Expectation-Maximization
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The expectation maximization algorithm:\\ a short tutorial}\\
  S. Borman (2009)
\end{book}

Let $x$ denote the observed data, $z$ the hidden data and $\theta$ the parameters to estimate.
We want to maximize
\[ L(\theta) = \log p(x|\theta) = \log \sum_z p(x, z | \theta), \]
iteratively.
The improvement brought by $\theta$ on the previous estimate $\theta_n$ is 
\begin{align*}
  \Delta(\theta|\theta_n) &=
  L(\theta) - L(\theta_n) \\
  &= \log \sum_z p(x, z | \theta) - \log p(x|\theta_n) \\
  % Multiply and divide by:  p(z|x, \theta_n)
  &= \log \sum_z p(z|x, \theta_n) \dfrac{ p(x, z | \theta) }{ p(z|x, \theta_n) } - \log p(x|\theta_n) \\
  % Jensen's inequality
  &\stackrel{\text{\footnotesize Jensen}}{\geq}  \sum_z p(z|x, \theta_n) \log \dfrac{ p(x, z | \theta) }{ p(z|x, \theta_n) } - \log p(x|\theta_n) \\
  &= \sum_z p(z|x, \theta_n) \log \dfrac{ p(x, z | \theta) }{ p(z|x, \theta_n) p(x|\theta_n) }.
\end{align*}
Since $L(\theta) \geq L(\theta_n) + \Delta(\theta | \theta_n)$, 
with equality if $\theta = \theta_n$,
a value of $\theta$ that improves the right handside will also improve the left.
After dropping the terms constant with respect to $\theta$, 
we end up maximizing
\[ \sum_z p(z|x, \theta_n) \log p(x, z | \theta) = E_{ Z|X,\theta_n } [ \log p(x,z | \theta ) ]. \]

The single formula 
\[ \theta_{n+1} = \argmax_\theta \sum_z p(z|x, \theta_n) \log p(x, z | \theta) \]
is traditionally (and confusingly) decomposed into E and M steps:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the conditional probabilities $p(z|x, \theta_n)$, one for each value of $z$ (E step);
\item ``Compute'' the function $\sum_z p(z|x, \theta_n) \log p(x, z | \theta) $ -- it is a function of $\theta$ (E step);
\item Find the value $\theta_{n+1}$ of $\theta$ that maximizes it (M step).
\end{compactitem}

The generalized expectation maximization (GEM) algorithm
does not use 
$ \theta_{n+1} = \argmax_\theta \Delta(\theta|\theta_n) $
but merely 
$\theta_{n+1} \in \{ \theta \ :\ \Delta(\theta|\theta_n) > 0 \}; $
this is sufficient to ensure convergence.
% No applications.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A quasi-Newton acceleration\\ for high-dimensional optimization algorithms}\\
  H. Zhou et al. (2009)
\end{book}
The EM (expectation-maximization) iteration can be written 
$ x_{n+1} = F(x_n) $
and (hopefully) converges to a fixed point of $F$.
One can replace it with Newton's method to solve $G(x)=0$, where $G(x) = x - F(x)$:
$ x_{n+1} = x_n - G'(x_n) ^{-1} G(x_n) $,
\ie,
\[ x_{n+1} = x_n - ( I - F'(x_n) ) ^ {-1} G(x_n). \]
To facilitate the inversion, one can use a low-rank approximation of $F'(x_n)$.
The \emph{quasi-Newton method}
uses another approximation of $F'(x)$: 
sufficiently close to the fixed point $x_\infty$,
\[ F^2 x_n - F x_n \approx F'(x_\infty) ( F x_n - x_n ). \]
The smallest matrix $\widehat{ F'(x_\infty) }$ satisfying those equations for the last $q$ observations
is an estimate of $F'(x_\infty)$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The speed of Shor's algorithm}\\
  J.V. Burke et al. (2007)
\end{book}
Shor's $r$-algorithm is a generalization of steepest descent ($\gamma=0$) 
and conjugate gradient ($\gamma=1$)
to find a minimizer of a (non-necessarily smooth) function $f$
\begin{align*}
  x_{n+1} &= x_n - t_n B_n B_n' f'(x_n) \\
  t_n &= \argmin_t f( x_{n+1} ) \\
  r_{n+1} &= B_n' ( f'(x_{n+1}) - f'(x_n) ) \\
  B_{n+1} &= B_n ( I - \gamma r^{}_{n+1} r'_{n+1} )
\end{align*}
with good performance for non-smooth functions (in this case, $f'$ is a subgradient).

%% 
%% 2. R
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The R Journal (2013)}\\
\end{book}
This issue contains more articles than usual; among others:
\texttt{RTextTool} provides a unified interface to supervised classification algorithms for text processing
  (\texttt{svm}, \texttt{glmnet}, \texttt{maxent}, \texttt{sldca}, \texttt{ipred::bagging}, 
  \texttt{caTools::LogitBoost}, \texttt{randomForest}, \texttt{nnet}, \texttt{tree});
``generalized simulated annealing'' (\texttt{genSA}) may be faster than \texttt{DEOptim} or \texttt{rgenoud};
\texttt{ftsa} analyzes functional time series (e.g., a yield curve, a volatility surface);
qualitative comparative analysis (\texttt{QCA}) is another name for 
  association rules analysis in the social sciences (small datasets), 
  often with fuzzy sets;
many packages to manipulate maps (\texttt{ggmap}, \texttt{osmar}, \texttt{OpenStreetMap}, \texttt{RGoogleMaps}); 
applications of sparse matrices (\texttt{Matrix}) to fit GEE models and spatial probit models; 
etc.

To promote/enable reproducibility and productionalization, the R package manager 
should account for versioning (version dependencies are currently declared, checked, but not used),
like Gentoo or NPM -- 
but this would require the ability to install and load, simultaneously, several versions of the same package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The generalized simulated annealing algorithm in the LEED search problem}\\
  E.R. Correia et al.
\end{book}
% Much clearer than the original article
\emph{Generalized simulated annealing} (GSA)
is simulated annealing on $\RR^D$,
with the following visiting distribution 
\[ g(\Delta x ) \propto \dfrac{
  1
}{
  \left(
    1 + (q-1) \dfrac{(\Delta x)^2}{ T^{2(3-q)}}
  \right)
  ^{ \tfrac{1}{q-1} + \tfrac{D-1\mathstrut}{2} }
},
\]
cooling scheme 
\( T(t) \propto t^{1-q} \)
and acceptance probability
\[ p(\Delta E) = \left( 1 + (q-1) \dfrac{\Delta E}{T} \right) ^{- \tfrac{1}{q-1}}. \]
Fast simulated annealing ($q=2$, Cauchy visiting distribution) performs well.

%%
%% 3. R in Finance
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{R in finance 2013}\\
\end{book}

Here are some of the topics mentionned: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Instead of finding the portfolio weights maximizing the expected utility, 
  one can smooth the expected utility and 
  look for the center of a region (in the portfolio weights space) 
  where it is high: 
  \emph{probabilistic utility} formalizes this idea 
  and computes the expected weights $w$ for the distribution 
  \[ p(w) \propto \exp E[ \text{utility}( \text{wealth}( w ) ) ]; \]
\item Part of the volatility smile is due to numeric imprecisions 
  (far from the strike, the price is almost independent of the implied volatility);
\item On a toy model of asset returns (say, ARMA), 
  one can explicitly compute the Sharpe ratio
  of simple strategies (say, a moving average);
\item Markov chains can model the evolution (survival) of exchange-traded derivatives, by looking at their ($\log_{10}$-binned) volume;
\item Performance attribution;
\item Implied expected returns can be computed from a \emph{set} of mean-variance optimal portfolios, 
  e.g., capital-weighted (market), equal-weighted ($1/N$), equal risk contribution, 
  maximum diversification ($w' \sqrt{\diag V} / \sqrt{ w' V w }$), 
  and risk efficient (the tangency portfolio, under the assumption that the returns are proportional to the downside deviation);
%\item The \emph{risk efficient portfolio} is defined as:
%  \begin{align*}
%  & \xi_1, \dots, \xi_{10}: \text{ deciles of the semi-deviation of stocks} \\
%  & J_{ij} = \mathbf1_{\text{stock $i$ is i decile $j$}} \\
%  & w = \argmax \dfrac{ w' J \mathbf\xi }{ \sqrt{ w' V w } };
%  \end{align*}
\item The high-frequency-based volatility (HEAVY) model
\begin{align*}
  h_t &= \var r_t = \omega_1 + \alpha_1 V_{t-1} + \beta_1 h_{t-1} + \lambda r_t^2 \\
  \mu_t &= E[V_t] = \omega_2 + \alpha_2 V_{t-1} + \beta_2 \mu_{t-1} \\
  r_t &: \text{ (daily) returns} \\
  V_t &: \text{ realized volatility, measured} \\
  &\hphantom{{}:{}} \text{ from high-frequency data;}
\end{align*}
\item Change-point analysis on a time series of independent observations 
  can be performed using cluster analysis (agglomerative or divisive)
  with time constraints (to ensure that the clusters are contiguous -- 
  this is not unlike key detection in music),
  by maximizing 
  \[ \mathscr E(X,Y,\alpha) = 2 E \va{X-Y}^\alpha - \va{X-X'}^\alpha - \va{Y-Y'}^\alpha;\]
  this is implemented in the \texttt{ecp} package;
\item Cluster risk portfolios assign the same risk contribution to each cluster
  (either user-defined clusters, e.g., asset classes, or those resulting from some clustering algorithm),
  and then to each asset inside each cluster; % Cluster ERC
\item Many models predict the yield curve or the S\&P\,500 from economic variables;
  combining them, with weights inversely proportional to the past performance
  (mean squared error, with older values discounted more), improves performance -- 
  but penalized models, with all the variables, do even better;
\item Network analysis of interbank overnight loans, from SEC filings, can identify those whose failure can lead to a crisis (high centrality);
\item Communities % (defined by maximizing modularity) 
  in a venture capital (VC) network influence performance;
\item Remortgaging can be formulated as a game between lender and borrower;
  % (and most of the crisis comes from the fact that the player do not / cannot (legally) act in their best interest
\item Crashes can be predicted with the log-linear power law (LPPL) model;
\item Behavioural portfolios (or goal-based optimal portfolios) 
  are mean-variance optimal portfolios, with a constraint on the value-at-risk.
\end{compactitem}

The estimation of covariance matrices was a recurring topic:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Factor models can also be used to decompose the cross-sectional volatility; % Standard deviation of the returns at a given date
\item When estimating a variance matrix, rescale the volatility with the (smoothed) VIX to make it stationary;
\item Use robust correlation estimators
  (MCD for market outliers, robust pairwise correlation for specific risk outliers): 
  to avoid believing in diversification when it is not there;
  for dynamic alerts (plot the Mahalanobis distance versus time with a (say) $\pm3\sigma$ band);
\item Principal volatility component (PVC) analysis looks for no-ARCH portfolios 
  (not unlike independent component analysis, which looks for non-Gaussian portfolios);
% ARCH-dimension
\end{compactitem}

A few new packages were presented: 
\texttt{RND} to estimate the risk-neutral density from option prices,
\texttt{modopt} (not currently available) to specify optimization (and even stochastic optimization) problems as strings
(unfortunately not as expressions, as Matlab's CVX allows),
\texttt{greeks},
\texttt{MTS} (unavailable) for sparse modeling (sparse parametrization or penalized estimation) of multivariate time series (VAR, VARMA, etc.).

From a programming point of view, 
besides \texttt{Rcpp} 
(to access QuantLib functions or objects not available in \texttt{RQuantLib}, % Many code samples
to use linear algebra libraries such as Armadillo),
\texttt{quantstrat} (to build and test investment strategies),
or \texttt{data.table},
\texttt{rzmq} (an interface to ZeroMQ, an easy-to-use and language-agnostic networking library)
and
\texttt{scidb} were also mentionned.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient indexation\\An alternative to cap-weighted indices}\\
  N. Amenc et al. (2010)
\end{book}
Compute the \emph{tangency portfolio} 
by assuming that the returns are proportional to the (rank of the) downside risk 
(since investors require higher returns to invest in riskier stocks)
and using principal component analysis to estimate the variance matrix;
shrink towards the $1/N$ portfolio by adding minimum and maximum weight constraints, 
$1/2N \leq w \leq 2/N$;
do not rebalance every quarter but only when the absolute value of the changes in weights exceed 50\%.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scalable robust covariance\\ and correlation estimates for data mining}\\
  F.A. Alqallaf et al. (2002)
\end{book}
Robust estimators of the variance matrix are often computationnally intensive ($O(e^dn^2)$):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Minimum covariance determinant (MCD): 
  take 90\% of the data (at random),
  compute the covariance matrix,
  iterate, keep the matrix with the smallest determinant;
\item Minimum volume ellipsoid (MVE): 
  take 90\% of the data,
  find the smallest ellipsoid containing  those points, 
  iterate, keep the ellipsoid with the smallest volume
  (MCD can be seen as a constrained MVE: the ellipsoids are those defined by the sample mean and variance);
\item Fast MCD: instead of taking a different, random, independent sample at each iteration, 
  take the 90\% of the data with the smallest Mahalanobis distance.
\end{compactitem}
Faster estimates can be obtained by using robust pairwise correlation estimators (but you need to fix the correlation matrix afterwards): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Rank correlation;
\item Find the robust scale $s_j$ and location $m_j$ of each variable $j$, 
  \[ \sum_i \psi \left( \dfrac{ x_{ij} - m_j }{ s_j } \right)= 0, \]
  where 
  $\psi$
  is the Huber function
  \raisebox{-1mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.15]
    \draw (-2,0) -- (2,0);
    \draw (0,-1) -- (0,1);
    \draw[thick] (-2,-1) -- (-.8,-1) -- (.8,1) -- (2,1);
  \end{tikzpicture}},
  \( \psi(x) = \min( \max( -c, x ), x ) \)
  or the sign function 
  \raisebox{-1mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.15]
    \draw (-2,0) -- (2,0);
    \draw (0,-1) -- (0,1);
    \draw[thick] (-2,-1) -- (0,-1) -- (0,1) -- (2,1);
  \end{tikzpicture}}
  and take the correlation $\cor(y_{\cdot j}, y_{\cdot k})$ of the transformed data
  $y_{ij} = \psi \left( \dfrac{ x_{ij} - m_j }{ s_j } \right)$
  (Huber correlation, quadrant correlation).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Propagation of outliers in multivariate data}\\
  F. Alqallaf et al.
\end{book}
The contamination models used to develop robust estimators rarely include 
\emph{componentwise outliers} --
the resulting estimators have a poor breakdown, in high dimension, in presence of such outliers.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio optimization with mental accounts}\\
  S. Das et al. (2010)
\end{book}
It is easier for investors to specify, for each of their goals (retirement, bequest, etc.)
the minimum success probability than the overall risk aversion.
Each goal corresponds to a subportfolio, 
optimized with a value-at-risk (VaR) constraint.
Under a Gaussian assumption, those portfolios are mean-variance efficient;
if short sales are allowed, the resulting portfolio is still optimal (if they are not, you only lose a few basis points).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Options and structured products\\ in behavioural portfolios}\\
  S. Das and M. Statman (2013)
\end{book}
Options have a role to play in behavioural portfolios (VaR-constrained portfolios), 
even if they are overpriced (by up to 20\%).

%%
%% 4. Finance
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Threshold cointegration:\\ overview and implementation in R}\\
  M. Stigler (2012)
\end{book}
The cointegration relation can be written in VECM (vector error correction model) form,
\[ \Delta Z_{t+1} = c + \lambda' Z_t a + B \Delta Z_t + \epsilon, \]
where $\lambda' Z_t$ are the cointegration relations
(e.g., 
\[ 
  \lambda = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -\beta \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
  Z_t = \begin{pmatrix} X_t \\ Y_t \end{pmatrix}
\]
and $a$ is the mean-reversion speed).
It can be extended to a threshold model 
by having the mean-reversion speed $a$ depend on the current regime
(the regime could correspond to the position of 
$\lambda' Z_t$ wrt some thresholds).
% I have not read the details.
This is implemented in the \texttt{tsDyn} package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The misleadig value of measured correlation}\\
  B.M. Damghani et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Correlation is rarely a good measure of how linked two assets are, 
and cointegration should often be preferred. 
For instance, 
its assumptions (linear, synchronous, no outliers) are seldom satisfied; 
it is not valid if the spread between two underlyers follows a mean-reverting process (e.g., WTI and Brent -- if you have a notion of ``spread'', correlation is a bad idea),
or for companies sharing the same, fixed-size market,
or for commodities with an equity mirror (e.g., oil and BP),
or for assets with contagion/domino effects (credit derivatives),
or for overlays.

Instead of replacing the correlation model
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{dx}x &= \sigma_1 d W_1 \\
  \dfrac{ dy }{ y } &= \sigma_1 \rho d W_1 + \sigma_2 \sqrt{ 1 - \rho } \, dW_2  \qquad dW_1 \indep dW_2
\end{align*}
with the cointegration (Ornstein-Uhlenbeck) one
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{dx}x &= \sigma_1 d W_1 \\
  \dfrac{ dy }{ y } &=  \theta ( \mu + x - y ) dt + \sigma_2 d W_2 \qquad dW_1 \indep dW_2
\end{align*}
one could try a hybrid model
\begin{align*}
  &\dfrac{dx}x = \sigma_1 d W_1 \\
  &\dfrac{ dy }{ y } =  \theta ( \mu + x - y ) dt + \sigma_2 d W_2 \\
  &\cor( dW_1, dW_2 ) = \rho.
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving portfolio selection using option-implied volatility and skewness}\\
  V. DeMiguel (2012)
\end{book}
With a continuum of option prices,
one can compute the model-free implied volatility (MFIV)
\[ \text{MFIV}_{[t,T]} = \sqrt{ E^Q [ e^{ - r (T-t) } R^2_{[t,T]} ] } \]
(or the other impled moments) and,
assuming that $\text{MFIV} / \text{realized volatility}$
is constant,
forecast the future realized volatility --
it is a better forecast of future volatility than historical volatility.
Use implied volatility and implied skewness 
(either the model-free implied skewness, defined as the MFIV,
or the spread between the IV computed from puts and calls)
in your alpha to increase performance (Sharpe ratio, etc.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{High-quality topic extraction\\ from business news explains\\ abnormal financial market volatility}\\
  R. Hisano (2012)
\end{book}
Analysis of a huge set of business news suggests that news do have an impact on trade \emph{volume}:
for the news associated to a given company,
discard all stock-related news, use \emph{latent Dirichlet analysis} (LDA) for form 100 topics
(sets of words, each word is in exactly one topic),
and use penalized regression to try to predict future returns.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Factor-risk-constrained mean-variance portfolio selection: formulation\\ and global optimization solution approach}\\
  S. Zhu (2011)
\end{book}
Constraints on the contribution of risk factors to the portfolio risk (a quadratic term) 
make the portfolio optimization problem non-convex.
For up to a few hundred assets, it is amenable to branch-and-bound, with a semi-definite relaxation.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Measuring financial market stress}\\
  K.L. Kliesen and D.C. Smith (2010)
\end{book}
One can build a ``financial stress index'' by looking at (weekly) interest rates
(government bonds with various maturities, TIPS, corporate bonds with various credit ratings),
spreads (Libor-OIS, TED),
and indices (emerging market bonds, VIX, bond volatility, equities)
and taking the first principal component (of the demeaned, normalized time series)
(the coefficients (``loadings'') are only estimated once, when the index is first constructed; 
they do not change with time).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stability analysis of financial contagion\\ due to overlapping portfolios}\\
  F. Caccioli et al. (2012)
\end{book}
When banks hold diversified portfolios, those portfolios overlap;
a bankruptcy triggers a flash sale and a devaluation of the assets,
thereby impacting other banks.
Contagion is made worse by overcrowding 
(ratio of the number of banks by the number of assets).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cascading failures in bipartite graphs:\\ model for systemic risk propagation}\\
  X. Huang et al. (2013)
\end{book}
Idem.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A model of market limit orders\\ by stochastic PDEs, parameter estimation,\\ and investment optimization}\\
  Z. Zheng and R.B. Sowers (2012)
\end{book}
The limit order book can be modeled with a (system of) \emph{stochastic} PDEs 
for mid-price (a function of time)
and volume (a function of price and time -- positive volumes for supply, negative for demand, and the volume at the mid-price is zero).
% Interesting, but not read in detail

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Keynes meets Markowitz: the tradeoff\\ between familiarity and diversification}\\
  P. Boyle et al. (2009)
\end{book}
Knightian uncertainty (uncertainty about the parameters of probability distributions)
can be introduced in portfolio construction as follows: 
asset returns are Gaussian, $X \sim N(\mu_0,V)$;
their variance $V$ is known but their mean $\mu_0$ is not; 
we only have an estimate $\mu \sim  N(\mu_0,A)$,
whose precision (ambiguity, $A$) is known; 
the investor wants to maximize the expected returns,
with penalties for risk and ambiguity.
The article uses a robust optimization instead: 
\[ 
  \max_w \min_{\mu_0} \left\{
    w' \mu_0 - \lambda_1 w' V w, \ 
  \dfrac{(\mu - \mu_0)^2}{\diag A} \leq \lambda_2
  \right\}.
\]
% Some of the conclusions may be artefacts of the fact that they only look at the diagonal of $A$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved performance by constraining portfolio norms: a generalized approach to portfolio optimization}\\
  V. DeMiguel et al. (2007)
\end{book}
Adding an $L^2$ penalty to the portfolio optimization problem 
\[ \min _w \{ w' V w + \lambda \n{ w }_2 ^2 , \ w' \mathbf 1 = 1 \} \]
is equivalent to shrinking the covariance matrix $V$ to $V + \lambda I$;
one can equivalently use a hard constraint
\[ \min_w \{ w' V w, \ w' \mathbf 1 = 1 , \ \n{ w }_2 \leq \nu \}. \]
Adding an $L^1$ constraint
$\n{ w }_1 \leq \ell$ restricts the leverage to $\ell$ ($\ell \geq 1$, e.g., $1.3$ for 130/30 portfolios).

Applying the conjugate gradient algorithm to the $1/N$ portfolio to minimize the variance
produces a sequence of $N-1$ ``partial minimum variance portfolios''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock return serial dependence and out-of-sample portfolio performance}\\
  V. DeMiguel et al. (2010)
\end{book}
Use a VAR (or NAR -- non-parametric auto-regressive -- 
\emph{nearest neighbour regression}, using the 50 days closest to today's performance) 
model
to build a momentum portfolio (use the conditional variance and mean, $V,\mu | X_{t-1}$;
it will also capture lead-lag relations;
you may want to add an $L^1$ constraint (or penalty) to reduce turnover.

%%
%% 5. Statistics
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The adaptive lasso and its oracle properties}\\
  H. Zou (2006)
\end{book}
Prefer the adaptive lasso 
(the coefficients of the $L^1$ penalties are variable-specific,
e.g., $\lambda / \hat \beta_{\text{ols}} ^ \gamma$, with $\gamma > 0$)
to the lasso:
it makes variable selection consistent.
In~R, it is implemented in \texttt{lqa::adaptive.lasso}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A kernel statistical test of independence}\\
  A. Gretton et al. 
\end{book}
Covariance is not sufficient to test for independence, 
but in a universal reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS),
it is.
``Universal'' means dense in $\mathscr C_b(X,\mathbb R)$ -- in practice, 
you will choose a kernel and hope that it is not too far from universal.
The norm of the covariance operator is
\begin{align*}
  \text{HSIC} = {}& E[ k(x_1,x_2) \ell(y_1,y_2)] + E[ k(x_1,x_2) ] E[ \ell(y_1,y_2) ] \\
  & - 2 E_{x_1,y_1}[ E_{x_2}[k(x_1,x_2)] E_{y_2}[\ell(y_1,y_2)] ] 
\end{align*}
and it can be estimated as 
\[ \dfrac 1{ m^2 } \tr( K H L H ) \]
where 
\begin{align*}
  m &= \text{number of observations} \\
  k_{ij} &= k(x_i,x_j) \\
  \ell_{ij} &= \ell(x_i,x_j) \\
  H &= I = \dfrac1m \mathbf 1 \mathbf 1' \\
  k, \ell &: \text{kernels on $X$ and $Y$.}
\end{align*} 
One can compute the asymptotic distribution of this estimator under
the null hypothesis $P_{xy} = P_x P_y$
and estimate the critical value for a statistical test
(or to convert the test statistic to a $p$-value).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{New approaches in visualization\\ of categorical data: R package extracat}\\
  A. Pilh\"ofer and A. Unwin (2013)
\end{book}

Use barcharts overlaid with mosaic plots (\texttt{rmb})
and interactive (iWidgets, iPlots)
parallel graphs (\texttt{cpcp}),
with the variables and values reordered to reduce overplotting.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Visualizing association rules: introduction\\ to the R-extension package arulesViz}\\
  M. Hahsler and S. Chelluboina
\end{book}
Here are a few ways to visualize association rules: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Plot confidence, support, lift, number of items using two variables as coordinates and a third as colour;
\item Plot the rules as a matrix, with the antecedant (lhs) on the $x$ axis and the consequence on the $y$ axis,
  reordered with the \texttt{seriation} package and/or clustered with the Jaccard distance
  \[ d(X_i,X_j) = 1 - \dfrac{\va{X_i \inter X_j}}{\va{X_i \union X_j}}; \]
\item Graphs (with itemsets, or items, as nodes);
\item Parallel plots, etc.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time-frequency dynamics\\ of the biofuels-fuels-food system}\\
  L. Vacha et al. (2012)
\end{book}
To study \emph{one} time series $x$,
one can look at its continuous wavelet transform
\[ W_x(u,s) = \int x(t) \dfrac1{\sqrt s} \bar \psi \left( \dfrac{t-u}{s} \right) dt. \]
To study two time series $x$, $y$,
one can look at the cross-wavelet transform
\[ W_{xy}(u,s) = W_x(u,s) \overline{ W_y(u,s) } \]
or, separately at the \emph{wavelet coherence coefficient} (``time and frequency correlation'')
\[ R^2(u,s) = \dfrac{ 
  \va{ S[s^{-1} W_{xy}(u,s) ] }^2
}{
  S[s^{-1} \va{ W_x(u,s) }^2 ] 
  S[s^{-1} \va{ W_y(u,s) }^2 ]
}
\]
where  $S$ is some (2-dimensional) smoothing operator,
and the wavelet phase 
\[ \phi_{xy}(u,s) = \arg W_{xy}(u,s). \]

% Nice plots, but I do not understand the role of the smoothing operator -- and it is apparently needed: if you remove it, you get R^2=0...

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GillespieSSA: Implementing\\ the stochastic simulation algorithm in R}\\
  M. Pineda-Krch (2008)
\end{book}
Population dynamics (predator-prey, SIR, etc.)
are often modeled as coupled ordinary differential equations (ODE),
but this is only valid for large populations.
For small populations, a stochastic model is needed -- 
\emph{stochastic simulation algorithms} (SSA) 
are a generalization of the simulation of a Poisson process,
modeling the state changes (alive/dead, suceptible/infected/resistant)
of the individuals;
they can be exact or (faster) approximate.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{GrassmannOptim: an R package\\ for Grassmann manifold optimization}\\
  K.P. Adragano et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Gradient-based optimization can be used on manifolds other than $\RR^n$ -- 
for instance, many constrained optimization problems looking for a set of orthogonal vectors
are unconstrained optimization problems on the Grassmannian
(e.g., the first eigenvectors, independent components, or other dimension reduction problems).


%%
%% 6. Graphs
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graphical models with R: Tutorial}\\    % Just a tutorial, not the book with the same title
  S. H\o jsgaard (2012)
\end{book}
The \texttt{gRbase}, \texttt{gRain}, \texttt{gRim} packages
provide functions to build, use, fit graphical models (or discrete variables, \ie, log-linear models).
\iffalse
% Build:
\texttt{ug}, \texttt{as(...,"graphNEL")}, \texttt{dg};
% Manipulate:
\texttt{subGraph}, \texttt{isComplete}, \texttt{maxClique}, \texttt{separates}, \texttt{parents}, \texttt{children}, \texttt{moralize},
\texttt{triangulate}, \texttt{ancestralSet}, \texttt{ancestralGraph};
% Explicitely compute the joint distribution:
\texttt{parray}, \texttt{tableMult}, \texttt{tableMargin};
% Computations using a junction tree factorization:
\texttt{cptable}, \texttt{computeCPT}, \texttt{grain}, \texttt{querygrain(...type="join")},
\texttt{setFindings}, \texttt{predict};
% Fitting:
\texttt{loglin}, 
\texttt{loglm}, % Idem, with a formula interface
\texttt{stepwise}\verb|(dmod(~.^.,data=d))|;  % Model selection
% Tests:
\texttt{ciTest} % Conditional independence
\fi

\iffalse
\begin{verbatim}
  ug  as(...,"graphNEL")  dg
  subGraph  isComplete  maxClique  separates 
  parents  children  moralize
  triangulate  ancestralSet  ancestralGraph
  parray  tableMult  tableMargin
  cptable  computeCPT  grain  
  querygrain(...type="join")
  setFindingd  predict
  loglin  loglm  stepwise(dmod(~.^.,data=d))
  ciTest
\end{verbatim}
\fi

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An improved algorithm\\ for matching large graphs}\\
  L.P. Cordella et al. 
\end{book}
To find an isomorphism between two graphs, 
grow an isomorphism between subgraphs 
(adding edges outgoing from the subgraph, or incoming if there are no outgoing edges, 
or other vertices of there are no edges to/from the subgraph),
backtracking when needed.
The VF2 algorithm is implemented, for instance, in Python's NetworkX module.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{McKay's canonical graph labeling algorithm}\\
  S.G. Hartke and A.J. Radcliffe
\end{book}
To test if two graphs are isomorphic, one could try all possible permutations,
or the permutations (of the vertices) that preserve some vertex invariant,
such as the degree (or the centrality, etc.).
The degree is too coarse an invariant, but it can be propagated:
first, partition the edges by degree;
then, for each node, look at the number of edges to each part of the partition -- this gives a finer partition;
iterate until the partition stabilizes (equitable partition).
When you reach an equitable partition, 
split by removing an element (non-deterministically) from one of the parts (deterministically).
The smallest leaf of the resulting search tree (the partitions are ordered) is a canonical isomorph of the graph.

The algorithm is implemented in \texttt{nauty}.

It is not known if the graph isomorphism is NP-complete.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Clustering with qualitative information}\\
  M. Charikar et al. (2004)
\end{book}
Here are some algorithms to cluster the vertices of a graph whose edges are labeled ``agree'' or ``disaggree'':
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take a binary decision variable $x_{ij}$ for each edge, 
  with $x_{ij}=0$ if $i$ and $j$ are in the same cluster; 
  the condition $x_{ij} = x_{jk} = 0 \implies x_{ik} = 0$ can be written $x_{ik} \leq x_{ij} + x_{jk}$;
  minimize
  \[ \sum_{i,j \text{ aggree}} x_{ij} + \sum_{i,j \text{ disaggree}} ( 1 - x_{ij}); \]
  truncate the solution of the linear relaxation at $2/3$ and $1/3$, one cluster at a time;
  % 1/3 is not optimal, but it gives the idea
\item Associate a vector $v_i$ to each vertex: maximizing 
  \[ \sum_{i,j \text{ aggree}} v_i v_j + \sum_{i,j \text{ disaggree}} ( 1 - v_i v_j) \]
  subject to $v_i v_i = 1$ and $v_i v_j \geq 0$
  is a semi-definite relaxation of the previous problem.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{On the identifiability\\ of the post-nonlinear causal model}\\
  K. Zhang and A. Hyv\"arinen (2009)
\end{book}
The PNL (post-nonlinear) model 
$ x_2 = f_2( f_1 (x_1) + e_1 ) $
is not identifiable in general (e.g., in the Gaussian case)
but is, in many special cases (e.g., if $f_1$ is not invertible).
It is not directly useable beyond two variables: 
first use conditional independence tests to find the Markov equivalence class of directed acyclic graphs (DAG),
then use the PNL model two variables at a time, to identify the unresolved causal relations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic decentralized any-time\\ hierarchical clustering}\\
  H.V.D. Parunak et al.
\end{book}
\emph{Ant clustering} (each ant picks up items and drops them when it is close to similar items)
can be seen as a distributed variant of $k$-means.
It can be generalized to produce a hierarchical clustering: 
start with a random tree (with the data in the leaves)
and move nodes (if they are too dissimilar to their siblings)
or merge them (if their children are very dissimilar).
(This is just a local search algorithm, but an easy-to-parallelize one.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parable: a parallel random partition based\\ hierarchical clustering algorithm\\ for the MapReduce framework}\\
  S. Wang and H. Dutta
\end{book}
To cluster a large dataset on Hadoop:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Split the data at random and compute a hierarchical clustering on each node;
\item Choose one of the trees as a template;
\item Align all the other trees to it: 
  swap two children of the root if doing so makes them more similar 
  to the two children of the root of the template, continue recursibely with the other nodes;
\item Output the resulting tree: the shape is that of the template, 
  but the leaves contain \emph{sets} of observations rather than individual observations.
% But you could run a hierarchical clustering algorithm on each of those sets
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic latent variable models\\ for distinguishing between cause and effect}\\
  J.M. Mooij
\end{book}
The direction of a causal relation between two random variables $X$ and $Y$ 
can be inferred by comparing the two models 
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = tiny, row sep = small]
  & E \arrow{rd}{} \\
  X \arrow{rr}{} && Y 
\end{tikzcd}
\text{ and }
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = tiny, row sep = small]
  & E \arrow{ld}{} \\
  X && Y \arrow{ll}{} 
\end{tikzcd}
\]
($E$ is not observed) using Bayesian model selection.

%%
%% 7. CLP
%% 

\nextbook
\def\ECLiPSe{ECL$^\text{\!i}$\!PS$^\text{e}$\xspace}
\begin{book}
  \emph{A quick and gentle guide\\ to constraint logic programming via \ECLiPSe}\\
  A. Niederli\'nski (2011)
\end{book}
The book starts with a clear description of the differences between 
constraint logic programming (CLP) 
and its ancestor, Prolog:
they are syntactically similar, they explore the same tree, 
but CLP prunes it more efficiently.
Prolog only gives up a branch
(a partial instantiation, or grounding, of the decision variables)
when a constraint (involving ground variables) is violated.
CLP keeps track of the domain of each variable
(usually, as an interval of integers -- but it could also be an arbitrary finite set of integers)
and, after each instantiation, propagates the constraints -- 
for instance, if you know that 
$x+y=z$, 
$x, y \in \llbracket 0,3 \rrbracket$,
$z \in \llbracket 5,9 \rrbracket$,
you can deduce that 
$x, y \in \llbracket 2,3 \rrbracket$,
$z \in \llbracket 5,6 \rrbracket$ -- this prunes a lot of branches.
In addition, CLP offers more choice for the search strategy: 
branch-and-bound (Prolog is designed for satisfaction problems
and requires dirty, non-declarative tricks),
variable choice heuristic (e.g., most constrained),
value choice heuristic (e.g., minimum, maximum, middle, random),
random restarts, timeout, etc.
CLP also differs from operations research (OR), 
which is limited to continuous and binary variables.

The rest of the book is a long list of examples for the 
\ECLiPSe open source CLP system, 
but most of the language features 
are used without any explanation.
\begin{verbatim}
   =  <  >  =<  >= is ; , not => <= :- \==
  #= #< #> #=< #>= :: .. &= &:: ~ ! -> =\=
  $= $< $> $=< $>= fail retractall assert
  labeling findall minimize bb_min search
  indomain ic_symbolic:indomain 
  ground suspend delete  
\end{verbatim}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Similarity evaluation on tree-structured data}\\
  R. Yang et al. (2005)
\end{book}
The edit distance between trees (number of relabeling, delete and insert operations 
needed to transform one into the other)
can be computed with dynamic programming.
Alternatively, one can use the string edit distance between the preorder (or post-order) traversal sequences,
or various histogram distances (height, degree, label).
Here is another histogram: 
transform the tree into a binary tree 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.8,baseline=-3pt]
  \tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A) at (3,1) {};
  \node (B) at (0,0) {};
  \node (C) at (2,0) {};
  \node (D) at (4,0) {};
  \node (E) at (6,0) {};
  \node (F) at (.5,-1) {};
  \node (G) at (2,-1) {};
  \node (H) at (3.5,-1) {};
  \node (I) at (5.25,-1) {};
  \node (J) at (6.75,-1) {};
  \draw (A)--(B);
  \draw (A)--(C);
  \draw (A)--(D);
  \draw (A)--(E);
  \draw (C)--(F);
  \draw (C)--(G);
  \draw (C)--(H);
  \draw (E)--(I);
  \draw (E)--(J);
\end{tikzpicture}
\ \leadsto\ 
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=.2,yscale=.8,baseline=-3pt]
  \tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A) at (3,1) {};
  \node (B) at (0,0) {};
  \node (C) at (2,0) {};
  \node (D) at (4,0) {};
  \node (E) at (6,0) {};
  \node (F) at (.5,-1) {};
  \node (G) at (2,-1) {};
  \node (H) at (3.5,-1) {};
  \node (I) at (5.25,-1) {};
  \node (J) at (6.75,-1) {};
  \draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--(E);
  \draw (C)--(F)--(G)--(H);
  \draw (E)--(I)--(J);
  \tikzstyle{every node}=[inner sep=0pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node[right of=J, node distance=1mm, anchor=north west] {,};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
make it complete,
and consider the histogram (empirical distribution)
of the binary branches
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.2]
  \tikzstyle{every node}=[circle, fill=black, inner sep=1pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node (A) at (1,2) {};
  \node (B) at (0,0) {};
  \node (C) at (2,0) {};
  \draw (B) -- (A) -- (C);
  \tikzstyle{every node}=[inner sep=0pt, outer sep=0pt]
  \node[above right of=A, node distance=1mm, anchor=south west] {\rlap{$a$}};
  \node[left of=B, node distance=1mm, anchor=south east] {\llap{$b$}};
  \node[right of=C, node distance=1mm, anchor=south west] {\rlap{$c$}};
\end{tikzpicture}
\] 
(they can be seen as 3-grams for binary trees).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{OpenTURNS reference guide}\\
\end{book}
OpenTURNS is a C++ \emph{reliability} library, often used from Python, \ie, 
a list of methods used by engineers to propagate uncertainty in their computations 
and identify the main sources of those uncertainties.

First, the input variables can be described, as a joint probability distribution,
using empirical cumulative distribution functions (ecdf),
kernel smoothing,
correlation, rank correlation,
common parametric distributions, 
copulas,
mixtures (via the Fourier transform),
classical or Bayesian estimators, 
tests and plots for goodness of fit, etc.
%% TODO: Kendall plot

Then, one propagates uncertainty: 
either as [min,max] intervals,
analytically, or by solving optimization problems, 
or by simulations with quasi-random numbers 
(low discrepancy sequences, or Latin squares (design of experiments) -- 
Latin squares are fixed-length multivariate low discrepancy sequences);
or as location-dispersion pairs, or quantiles,
estimated by Monte carlo simulations or Taylor expansions.

To approximate the probability of some event $[g(X) \leq 0 ]$, 
transform $X$ to make it rotationally invariant 
(isoprobabilistic (Nataf, Rosenblatt) transformation -- 
if $X \sim N(\mu,C'C)$, just use $X \mapsto C^{-1} ( X - \mu )$)
and approximate the event $[g(X) \leq 0 ]$
with a half-plane (FORM: first order reliability method)
or a ball (SORM). The probability can also be approximated with 
Monte Carlo simulations.
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline,xscale=.6,yscale=.6,>=stealth]
  % n <- 40; theta <- seq(0,2*pi,length=n+1)[-1]; r <- 1 + .2 * cos(8*theta); x <- r*cos(theta); y <- r*sin(theta); plot(x,y, type="o"); x <- 2 + round(x,2); y <- round(y,2); paste0("(", x, ",", y, ")", collapse=" ")
  \fill[black!10] plot [smooth,tension=1.5] coordinates{
(3.05,0.17) (2.8,0.26) (2.75,0.38) (2.86,0.62) (2.85,0.85) (2.62,0.86) (2.38,0.75) (2.26,0.8) (2.17,1.05) (2,1.2) (1.83,1.05) (1.74,0.8) (1.62,0.75) (1.38,0.86) (1.15,0.85) (1.14,0.62) (1.25,0.38) (1.2,0.26) (0.95,0.17) (0.8,0) (0.95,-0.17) (1.2,-0.26) (1.25,-0.38) (1.14,-0.62) (1.15,-0.85) (1.38,-0.86) (1.62,-0.75) (1.74,-0.8) (1.83,-1.05) (2,-1.2) (2.17,-1.05) (2.26,-0.8) (2.38,-0.75) (2.62,-0.86) (2.85,-0.85) (2.86,-0.62) (2.75,-0.38) (2.8,-0.26) (3.05,-0.17) (3.2,0)
};
  \node (Z) at (2,.3) {\footnotesize$g(X) \leq 0$};
  \draw (-1,0) -- (3.2,0);
  \draw (0,-2) -- (0,2);
  \draw[ultra thick] (0,0) -- (.8,0);
  \node[text width=1cm, anchor=north west, inner sep=0, outer sep=0] (A) at (1,-2) {\rlap{reliability}\hbox to 1cm{} \rlap{index}};
  \node (B) at (.4,0) {};
  \draw[->] (A.north west) -- (B);
\end{tikzpicture}
\quad
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline,xscale=.5,yscale=.5]
  \fill[black!10] (1,-2) -- (1,2) -- (3,2) -- (3,-2);
  \draw (-1,0) -- (3,0);
  \draw (0,-2) -- (0,2);
  \draw[ultra thick] (0,0) -- (1,0);
  %\draw (1,-2) -- (1,2);
  \node (A) at (1,-3) {FORM};
\end{tikzpicture}
\quad
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline,xscale=.5,yscale=.5]
  \fill[black!10] (2,0) circle (1);
  \draw (-1,0) -- (3,0);
  \draw (0,-2) -- (0,2);
  \draw[ultra thick] (0,0) -- (1,0);
  \node (A) at (1,-3) {SORM};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

Finally, the importance of each input variable on the uncertainly of the output 
can be estimated, for instance, if $Z = h(X)$, with a Taylor expansion
\begin{align*}
  \var Z & \approx \nabla h(\mu_X) \, \var X \, \nabla h( \mu_X )' \\
  &= \sum_i \nabla_i h(\mu_X) \, \var X \, \nabla h(\mu_X)'
\end{align*}
or, simply, 
with $\cor(Z,X_i)$, \verb|lm(Z~X|$_{\texttt{i}}$\texttt{)},
$\text{pCor}(Z,X_i)$, rank correlation,
$\partial \text{RI} / \partial \theta$, etc.

Instead of the Taylor expansion,
given
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=small,]
  \RR^n \arrow{rr}{h} & & \RR^m \\
  & \Omega \arrow{ul}{X} \arrow[swap]{ur}{Y=h(X)}
\end{tikzcd}
\]
one can use the \emph{functional chaos expansion}:
the expansion of $h$ over a basis of orthogonal polynomials (``polynomial chaos'') wrt $f_X$
(only keep the ``first'' coefficients -- 
since there are many indices,
you can tweak the notion of ``first'', e.g., 
using the $L^p$ pseudonorm, for $p < 1$, of the indices).

