breadth-first and depth-first.
Eclat can be implemented by storing the transactions as rows in a sparse bitmatrix
and by using the columns to compute the intersections.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Simple algorithms for frequent itemset mining }\\
  C. Borgelt (2010)
\end{book}
The split-and-merge algorithm for frequent itemset mining preprocesses the data: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the item frequencies;
\item Remove the 
  %(transactions containing) 
  items below the desired threshold;
\item Sort the items in each transaction by increasing frequency;
\item Aggregate identical transactions (keep track of their count).
\end{compactitem}
The dataset is then processed recursively (divide and conquer):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Take the least frequent item;
\item Recursively process the itemsets that do not contain it,
  by removing it from the transactions and reaggregating them (some may become equal);
\item Recursively process the transactions that contain it: discard the other transactions, 
  and remove the item from the remaining transactions.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A comprehensive assessment of methods\\ for de novo reverse engineering\\ of genome-scale regulatory networks}\\
  V. Nerendra et al. (2010)
\end{book}
Algorithms to identify (undirected) causal relations include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Relevance networks: 
  add an edge between variables with a significant mutual information 
  \[ \text{MI}(x,y) = \iint p(x,y) \log \dfrac{ p(x,y) }{ p(x) p(y) } dx dy \]
  (use a kernel estimator for the densities,
  and change the order of the nested loops to acoid recomputing the 1-dimensional densities $p(x)$, $p(y)$ each time);
\item Add an edge between variables with a mutual information above some threshold;
\item Prune an MI-derived graph by removing the edge with the lowest mutual information in each triangle (Arcane);
\item Methods based on higher order mutual information 
  \begin{align*}
    H(X) &= \int - p(x) \log p(x) dx \\
    H(X,Y) &= \iint - p(x,y) \log p(x,y) dx dy \\
    H(X,Y,Z) &= \iiint - p(x,y,z) \log p(x,y,z) dx dy dz \\
    H( X ; Y, Z ) &= H(X) + H(Y,Z) - H(X,Y,Z)
  \end{align*}
  to test if 
  $ Y \rightarrow X \leftarrow Z $;
\item Add an edge between variables with a significant correlation;
\item Add an edge between variables with a significant partial correlation;
  the partial correlations can be computed from the concentration matrix (the inverse of the variance);
\item The graphical lasso, \ie, and $L^1$-penalized estimator of the concentration matrix;
\item Limited partial correlation ($qp$-graphs);
\item Hierarchical clustering;
\item Local methods.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast calculation\\ of pairwise mutual information\\ for gene regulatory network reconstruction}\\
  P. Qiu et al.
\end{book}
To compute the mutual information matrix 
\[ \text{MI}_{k\ell} = \iint p_{k\ell} (x,y) \log \dfrac{ p_{k\ell}(x,y) }{ p_k(x) p_\ell(y) } dx dy \]
one often uses a kernel estimator of the probability densities,
which gives something like 
$ \text{MI}_{k\ell} = \sum_i \log \sum_j f_{ijk\ell} $.
This is often implemented with nested loops
(\texttt{for k ... for l ... for i ... for j ...}),
\iffalse
  for k in 1..N:
    for l in 1..N:
      MI[k,l] = 0
      for i in 1..M:
        L = 0
        for j in 1..M:
          L += f[i,j,k,l]
        MI[k,l] += log L
\fi
but one can change the order of the loops (to $i$, $j$, $k$, $\ell$)
\iffalse
  for i
    for j 
      for k
        for l
\fi
and factor out some of the computations 
(bits of the kernel estimators of $p_k(x)$, $p_\ell(x)$,
which were recomputed each time).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A robust procedure\\ for Gaussian graphical model search\\ from microarray data with $p$ larger than $n$}\\
  R. Castelo and A. Roverato (2006)
\end{book}
\emph{Relevance networks} infer the structure of a graphical model by testing 
if $\cor(X_i,X_j)=0$,
but this ignores confounders, e.g.,
if $B \leftarrow A \rightarrow C$,
then $\cor(B,C) \neq 0$.
Instead, one can look at the conditional independences, 
which can be tested with partial correlations
\[ \cor( X_i, X_j \ |\ X_k, k \neq i,j ) \]
or, equivalently, the zero pattern of the concentration matrix (the inverse of the variance).
When it cannot be estimated, e.g., if $p \gg n$, 
one can use the \emph{limited partial order correlations}
instead, 
\[ \cor( X_i, X_j \ |\ X_{k_1}, \dots, X_{k_q} ). \]
% (usually, $q=1$ or $2$: there are $\binom{p}{q+2}$ of them).
The \emph{non-rejection rate} is the proportion of 
subsets $\{ k_1, \dots, k_q \}$
such that 
\[ H_0: \ \cor( X_i, X_j \ |\ X_{k_1}, \dots, X_{k_q} ) = 0 \]
is not rejected (using the $T$ test for zero regression coefficients) -- 
if $q$ is large, use a random subset of the $\binom{p}{q+2}$ subsets.
The \emph{qp-procedure} 
produces the graph whose edges have a non-rejection rate below some threshold.
To choose $q$ and assess its adequateness, 
look at the histogram of the non-rejection rate
and the qp-clique-plot:
\begin{verbatim}
  plot( max_clique_size ~ threshold | q,
        cex = number_of_edges )
  abline( h = min(p,n) )
\end{verbatim}
(you want enough edges to capture all the information, but a small maximum clique size).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Local causal and Markov blanket induction\\ for causal discovery\\ and feature selection for classification}\\
  C.F. Aliferis et al. (2010)
\end{book}
% Too long...
Review of a few algorithms to infer \emph{local causality} 
in a graphical model,
viz computing the parents and children (PC) of a node $X \in \mathbf V$ 
and its \emph{Markov blanket} (MB) -- a minimal element of 
\[ \{ \mathbf A \subset \mathbf V \setminus \{ X \} \ :\ 
  \forall\, Y \in \mathbf A \setminus \{ x \} 
  \quad X \indep Y \mid \mathbf A \}. \]
For a \emph{faithful graph}
(all dependence and independence relations come from the graph), 
\[ \text{PC}(X) = \{ Y \in \mathbf V
  \ :\ 
  \forall\, \mathbf Z \subset \mathbf V \setminus \{ X, Y \}
  \quad X \not \indep Y \mid Z 
\} \]
and $\text{MB}(X)$ is uniquely defined 
and contains parents, children and childrens' parents (spouses) of $X$.

By stitching the $\text{PC}(X)$ for all $X$, 
one can estimate the whole (global) graphical model.

Local causality learning can also be used for \emph{feature selection}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Hash kernels for structured data}\\
  Q. Shi et al. (2009)
\end{book}
The idea behing the count-min sketch (use a hash table, with several hash functions, and ignore collisions)
can be used to approximate scalar products in high-dimensional spaces (e.g., after the ``kernel trick'')
or to compress sparse feature vectors (in text processing)
\begin{align*}
  k(x,y) &= \langle \phi(x), \phi(y) \rangle
  \approx \left\langle \bar \phi(x), \bar \phi(y) \right\rangle \\
  \bar \phi_j(x) &= \sum_{ i \ :\ h(i) = j } \phi_i(x).
\end{align*}
If the features are duplicated,
the drop in performance is not that bad.
Hashing can be combined with random sampling -- for instance, if the features are the number of subgraphs of size $k$ in each isomorphism class.
This idea is implemented in Vowpal Wabbit.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{New methods for separating causes\\ from effects in genomics data}\\
  A. Statnikov et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Finding the direction of a causal link from observational data alone
(no intervention analysis)
looks hopeless: in terms of graphical models, 
$A \rightarrow B$ and $A \leftarrow B$
are in the same Markov equivalence class.
However, comparing the complexities of both models, 
\ie, of the decompositions of $P(A,B)$ into 
$P(A) P(B|A)$ or $P(B) P(A|B)$,
or checking if the conditional distribution is constant, \ie, $B|A \indep A$,
% You need to center $A$.
can sometimes give the answer -- many algorithms have been proposed.
% IGCI looks good

Also check the ``Cause-effects pairs'' Kaggle competition.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A linear non-Gaussian acyclic model\\ for causal discovery}\\
  S. Shimizu et al. (2006)
\end{book}
Variables $x_1,\dots,x_n$, 
related by cause/consequence relations described by a directed acyclic graph (DAG),
and observed with a non-Gaussian, additive noise $\epsilon$,
can be modeled as 
\( x = B x + \epsilon, \)
where $B$ is strict lower triangular.
But since $x = (I-B)^{-1} \epsilon$,
independent component analysis (ICA) can recover
$ B = I - \textsc{ICA}(x) $,
up to a permutation of the rows and columns.
% (LiNGAM)
The resulting DAG can be pruned with statistical tests for the strength of the causal relations.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Distinguishing causes from effects using non-linear acyclic causal models}\\
  K. Zhang and A. Hyv\"arinen (2007)
\end{book}
The LiNGAM algorithm (ICA-based causality discovery)
can be extended to allow for non-linearities
\[ x_{k+1} = f_{k+1} ( g_{k+1} ( x_1, \dots, x_k ) + \epsilon_{k+1} ), \]
which can be reformulated as 
\[ x = f( B s ) \]
with $B$ strict lower triangular.
% With 2 variables: 
%   x1 = f1( epsilon1 )
%   x2 = f2( g2( x1 ) + epsilon2 )
% Write x2 = f2( s1 + s2 ),
% where s2 = epsilon2 and s1 = g2(x1), i.e., x1 = g2^-1(s1).
% The model becomes
%   x1 = g2^-1( s1 )
%   x2 = f2( s1 + s2 )
In the case of two variables,
one can find $g_1$ and $g_2$ 
so that $x_1$ be as independent as possible from $g_2(x2) - g_1(x_1)$, 
e.g., by minimizing their mutual information with a multilayer perceptron (MLP)
and using a statistical test to check the independence. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonlinear causal discovery\\ with additive noise models}\\
  P.O. Hoyer et al. 
\end{book}
Given a non-linear noise with \emph{additive} non-Gaussian noise
$y = f(x) + \epsilon$,
plot the density of $y|x$ for several values of $x$:
it should be the same curve, up to translations.
This property is unlikely to hold for $x|y$
and strengthens the belief that $x \rightarrow y$.

\begin{center}
  \setkeys{Gin}{width=4cm}%
<<NonLinearCausalDiscovery1,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="4cm",fig.show="hold">>=
# It should not look like that: the conditional distributions should overlap much more...
n <- 1e4
x <- runif(n)
y <- x ^ 2 + runif(n)
#library(lattice)
#densityplot( ~ y | floor( 4 * x ) )
library(ggplot2)
d <- data.frame(
  x, y,
  gx = factor( floor( 10 * (rank(x)-1) / length(x) ) ),
  gy = factor( floor( 10 * (rank(y)-1) / length(y) ) )
)
ggplot(d, aes(x=x, colour=gy)) + geom_density(size=2, adjust=5) + theme(legend.position = "none")  + xlab( "x|y" )
#@
#<<NonLinearCausalDiscovery2,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="4cm">>=
ggplot(d, aes(x=y, colour=gx)) + geom_density(size=2, adjust=5) + theme(legend.position = "none")  + xlab( "y|x" )
@
\end{center}

The actual test checks that $x \not \indep y$ and 
$\text{res}( y \sim f(x) ) \indep y$,
where $y \sim f(x)$ is a non-linear regression 
and $\text{res}(\cdot)$ its residuals.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Inferring deterministic causal relations}\\
  P. Daniu\v sis et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Causal inferrence often relies on the assumption that the noise is additive and non-Gaussian.
This breaks down in the case of a deterministic relation $Y = f(X)$, 
especially if $f$ is invertible.
However, one can expect $ X \indep f$ and $Y \not\indep f$,
where $\indep$ denotes \emph{algorithmic independence} -- 
the shortest description of $p(x,y)$ is a separate description of $p(x)$ and $f$.


\begin{center}
  \setkeys{Gin}{width=4cm}%
<<InferringDeterministicCausalRelations,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="4cm">>=
f <- function(x) x + .010*sin(30*abs(x)^2)
#curve(f(x), xlim=c(0,1))
library(numDeriv)
df <- function(x) numDeriv::grad(f,x)
i_f <- Vectorize( function(x) uniroot( function(u) f(u) - x, c( 0, 2 ) )$root )
pY <- function(x) 1/df( i_f(x) )
#curve(df(x), xlim=c(0,1))
#curve(i_f(x), xlim=c(0,1))
#curve(pY(x), xlim=c(0,1))
op <- par(oma=.1+c(0,0,0,0), mar=c(0,0,0,0),xpd=NA)
a <- .2
par(fig=c(a,1,a,1))
curve(f(x), xlim=c(0,1), col="navy", lwd=3, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="")
box()
par(fig=c(a,1,0,a),new=TRUE)
curve(1+0*x, xlim=c(0,1), ylim=c(0,1.1), col="navy", lwd=3, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="" )
mtext( expression(p[X]), side=2, line=0, las=1 )
box()
par(fig=c(0,a,a,1),new=TRUE)
x <- seq(0,1,length=100)
plot( pY(x), x, type="l", lwd=3, col="navy", axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="" )
mtext( expression(p[Y]), side=1, line=0 )
box()
par(op)
@
\end{center}

Given a family of distributions $\mathscr E$
well-suited to model a probability density $p$,
one can measure the complexity of $p$ as 
\[ D(p \| \mathscr E ) = \min_{ q \in \mathscr E } D( p \| q ), \]
where
\[ D( p \| q ) = \int p(x) \log \dfrac{ p(x) }{ q(x) } dx \]
is the Kullback-Leibler divergence.
The information-geometric causal inference (IGCI) method computes 
\[ C_{ X \to Y } = D ( p_X \| \mathscr E_X ) - D( p_Y \|  \mathscr E_Y ) \]
and concludes that $X \to Y$ if $C_{X \to Y} < 0$.
If $\mathscr E_X = \mathscr E_Y$ only contains the uniform distribution $U(0,1)$,
\begin{align*}
  C_{X \to Y} &= \int_0^1 \log \va{ f'(x) } p(x) dx \\
  &\approx \dfrac1{m-1} \sum_{i=1}^{m-1}  \log \va{\dfrac{ y_{i+1} - y_i }{ x_{i+1} - x_i }},
\end{align*}
where $x_1 \leq \cdots \leq x_m$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The architecture of SciDB}\\
  M. Stonebraker (2011)
\end{book}
SciDB is a database for scientific data, not unlike a column store, but with arrays instead of columns.
It can be queried with a SQL-like language (AQL) or a more precedural one (AFL).
In addition to relational operators (join, etc.), it provides scientific operations (matrix multiplication, singular value decomposition (SVD), regression, machine learning).
Data is stored in \emph{overlapping} chunks, because many operations (on time series, images, geographical data, etc.) require neighbouring values.
It also supports versioning (the data is never deleted, but stored to optimize queries with current data).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Latent Dirichlet allocation:\\ towards a deeper understanding}\\
  C. Reed (2012)
\end{book}
A \emph{topic} is a probability distribution on a collection of words.
Latent Dirichlet allocation (LDA) is similar to $k$-means, but with bags of words instead of vectors in $\RR^n$:
it models a ``mixture'' of topics, one step of the algorithm assigning each text to the nearest topic,
the next refining the topics by ``averaging'' the texts associated with it.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The R package metaLik\\ for likelihood inference in meta-analysis}\\
  A. Guolo and C. Varin (2012)
\end{book}
Meta-analysis (combining the result of many studies, without access to the raw data of each),
boils down to a seemingly simple random-effects model: estimate $\beta$ in 
\begin{align*}
  Y_i &= \beta_i + e_i \\
  \beta_i &= \beta + \epsilon_i \\
  e_i &\sim N(0, \sigma_i^2) && \text{precision of study } i \\
  \epsilon_i &\sim N(0,\tau^2) && \text{heterogeneity of the studies.}
\end{align*}
Unfortunately, most statistical procedures (tests, confidencen intervals, etc.)
rely on asymptotic results: the small size of the sample (the number of studies aggregated) 
makes them invalid. 
In this context, higher-order expansions (of the log-likelihood) are preferable.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-parametric kernel distribution function estimation with \texttt{kerdiest}:
  an R package\\ for bandwidth choice and applications}\\
  A.Quintela-del-R\'io\,and\,G.Est\'evez-P\'erez\,(2012)
\end{book}
If you are more interested in the cumulative distribution function (cdf) 
than the probability distribution function (pdf),
e.g., probability of exceedance, mean return period, quantiles, etc.,
the optimal bandwidth for kernel density estimation is different.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Spherical $k$-means clustering}\\
  K. Hornik et al. (2012)
\end{book}
Spherical $k$-means, often used in text clustering, 
refers to $k$-means for the cosime dissimilarity, \ie, after projecting the data on the sphere,
\( \min_{p,c} \sum_i 1 - \cos( x_i, p_{c(i)} ), \)
\ie, \[ \min_{M,p} \sum_{ij} \mu_{ij} ( 1 - \cos(x_i, p_j) ), \]
with $\mu_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$ and $M \mathbf 1 = \mathbf 1$.
In the fixed point algorithm, local improvements are possible
(change the membership of a single observation; this also changes the prototypes $p$).
Extended spherical $k$-means minimize 
\[ \sum_{ij} w_{ij} \mu_{ij}^m ( 1 - \cos(x_i, p_j) ), \]
with $\mu_{ij} \in [0,1]$ and $M \mathbf 1 = \mathbf 1$
($m$ and $w$ are given).
% library(skmeans)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{ClustOfVar:\\ an R package for clustering of variables}\\
  M. Chavent et al. (2012)
\end{book}
PCAMIX is a generalization of principal component analysis (PCA)
that allows both quantitative and qualitative variables.
The first component is 
% n = number of observations
\[ 
  \argmax_{ \mathbf u \in \RR^n} 
  \sum_{\substack{j \text{ quantitative}\\\text{variable}}} \cor^2( \mathbf u, \mathbf x_j ) 
  +
  \sum_{\substack{j \text{ qualitative}\\\text{variable}}} \corr^2( \mathbf u, \mathbf y_j ) 
\]
where $\corr(\mathbf u, \mathbf y)$ is the \emph{correlation ratio},
\ie, the proportion of the variance of $\mathbf u$ explained by $\mathbf y$.
It can be computed from the singular value decomposition (SVD).
It can also be used to cluster the variables, by considering 
\[
  \argmax_{\substack{
    \mathbf u_1, \dots, \mathbf u_k \in \RR^n \\
    \mathscr C_1, \dots, \mathscr C_k \text{ partition} \\
    \text{of the variables}
  }}
  \sum_{\ell=1}^k 
  \sum_{j \in \mathscr C_\ell \inter \text{Quant}} 
  \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!
  \cor( \mathbf u, \mathbf x_j )
  \,
  +
  \!\!\!\!\!\!
  \sum_{j \in \mathscr C_\ell \inter \text{Qual}} 
  \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!
  \cor( \mathbf u, \mathbf y_j ).
\]
This can be approximated by hierarchical clustering of the variables, 
or a $k$-means-like algorithm.

% Clustering in R:
%   cluster::pam
%   cluster::agnes   Agglomerative clustering (hclust)
%   cluster::diana   Divisive hierarchical clustering
\nextbook

\begin{book}
\emph{The devil is in the tails: actuarial mathematics and the subprime mortgage crisis}\\
  C. Donnelly and P. Embrechts (2010)
\end{book}
The default times, needed to price CDOs, are often modeled with a 1-parameter Gaussian copula
and (say) exponential margins. This is inadequate: it implies asymptotic independence -- 
one should use another copula or, at least, stress the model with other copulas.
% There are other models for CDOs, mentionned with no details: 
% - Structural models (KMV)
% - Hazard rate models (CreditRisk+)

