\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An efficient algorithm\\ for automatic peak detection\\ in noisy periodic and quasi-periodic signals}\\
  F. Scholkmann et al. (2012)
\end{book}

The \emph{local maxima scalogram} (LMS) 
\[ m_{k,i} = \textbf{1} _{ x_i < x_{i-k},\ x_i > x_{i+k} } \]
may help detect peaks in a signal.
% Some details of the algorithm look fishy.
% In my implementation, I have to cheat to select the window radius
% (and that is the whole point of the algorithm...).
% Even if it wirks, it is not efficient: O(n^2)...

\begin{center}
  \setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm}%
<<LocalMaximaScalogram,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=4,out.width="8cm">>=
lms <- function(y) { 
  n <- length(y)
  L <- ceiling(n/2) - 1
  m <- matrix(0, nr=L, nc=n)
  k <- row(m)
  i <- col(m)
  f <- function(u) ifelse(u <= 0 | u > n, NA, u)
  m[] <- y[f(i-1)] > y[f(i-k-1)] & y[f(i-1)] > y[f(i+k-1)]
  m[ pmin( i-1, i-k-1, i-1, i+k-1 ) <= 0 ] <- NA
  m[ pmax( i-1, i-k-1, i-1, i+k-1 ) > n ] <- NA
  m
}
m <- lms(sunspots)
mm <- apply(m, 2, cumprod) > 0
# Where to truncate the scalogram: the article's suggestion does not seem to work
gamma <- apply(ifelse(is.na(m),1,m),1,sum)
# plot( gamma, type="l" )
j <- which.min(gamma)
j <- 11*6 # Half a period
op <- par()
layout(matrix(c(1,1,2,3),nc=1))
# Scalogram
par(mar=c(0,1,2,1))
y <- sunspots
image(1:length(y), 1:dim(m)[1], t(m), col=c("white","grey"), main = "Local maxima scalogram", axes=FALSE, useRaster=TRUE)
points( 1:length(y), apply(mm,2,sum,na.rm=TRUE), type="h", lwd=2 )
box()
abline( h = (1:2)*j, lty=3:2 )
# Zoom
par(mar=c(0,1,0,1))
image(1:length(y), 1:(2*j), t(m[1:(2*j),]), col=c("white","grey"), axes=FALSE, useRaster=TRUE)
points( 1:length(y), apply(mm,2,sum,na.rm=TRUE), type="h", lwd = 2 )
abline(h=j, lty=3)
box()
# Time series
par(mar=c(1,1,0,1))
plot(as.vector(sunspots), type="l", axes=FALSE)
x <- which( apply( m[1:j,], 2, all ) )
points( x, sunspots[x-1], pch=16, col="maroon" ) # Why "x-1"?
box()
par(op)
@
\end{center}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Graph databases}\\
  I. Robinson et al. (2013)
\end{book}

Relational databases are cumbersome and inefficient when dealing with graphs
(self-joins, recursive joins), and nullable columns complicate queries even further.
NoSQL databases can fake foreign keys, but all the work has to be done by the application.
% Strictly speaking, there are 4 types of NoSQL databases: 
% key-value stores, document stores, column stores and graph databases
Graph databases use index-free adjacency
(the relations are not stored in a global, separate index, but locally, bidirectionally, at each node),
often separate graph structure from property data (not unlike column stores),
and provide efficient graph-theoretic operations 
(depth-first search, breadth-first search, shortest path (Dijkstra), A*, etc.).
They can be used for predictive modeling, e.g.,
identify missing links 
(\emph{triadic closure}: if A--B and A--C, then B--C is likely)
% (the type and direction of B--C can be different, e.g., MANAGES vs WORKS_WITH)
or important links (a \emph{local bridge} is a relation that leads to a different part of the network).

Applications include social data
(find colleagues with similar interests, colleagues of colleagues interested in a given topic, etc.),
email forensics,
recommendations, 
geographical data (parcel delivery and shortest path queries, R-trees),
master data management, 
network and data center management (machines, applications, etc.), 
access control (dealing with complex organization structures and product hierarchies 
requires recursive joins in SQL),
bioinformatics (protein networks).

Data modeling is very similar to entity-relation (ER) modeling:
use a node for each entity (and for most relations:
% Example: "Email" as a relation between sender and reciepient is difficult to manage.
%          Considering "email" as another type of node simplifies things.
only encode simple relations as relationships),
avoid attributes on relationships (prefer fine-grained relationships).
% e.g., [:DELIVERY_ADDRESS] vs [:ADDRESS] {type: 'delivery'}
Time can be stored in linked lists (previous/next)
or date nodes (or, even, a timeline tree: each day points to its month, each month points to its year).
Optimizing a graph database just means
adding direct links for relations that would otherwise require several links.
% (Since you just add, you do not lose any of the expressivity of the previous model.)

Neo4j provides several APIs: 
a core API (in Java, with \texttt{Node} and \texttt{Relationship} objects),
a traversal API (more declarative), 
and Cypher (a SQL-like language -- the book has a lot of examples);
SPARQL (for RDF)
% http://www.w3.org/TR/rdf-sparql-query/ 
and Gremlin
% https://github.com/tinkerpop/gremlin/wiki/
are other query languages.
It can be embedded (either persisted on disk, or in-memory), 
used as a server (REST, JSON -- the server can be extended: 
use JAX-RS annotations to indicate to which URIs a class/method responds),
supports transactions, recovery, master-slave replication.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data-intensive text processing\\ with MapReduce}\\
  J. Lin and C. Dyer (2010)
\end{book}

% Introduction
\textbf{1.}
With the help of infrastructures such as MapReduce, 
the ``unreasonable effectiveness of data''
has made data-intensive scientific discovery
the ``fourth paradigm of science'' (after theory, experiment and simulations).

Contrary to most books on MapReduce or its implementation Hadoop,
which hide actual contents (if any) under complicated installation instructions
(this is Java) and boilerplate code (this is Java),
this one focuses on MapReduce \emph{algorithm} design -- 
no actual code, just clear Python-like pseudo-code.

% Basics
\textbf{2.}
MapReduce assumes that failures are common, moves processing closer to the data
and processes data sequentially (no random access).
It can be seen as a cloud analogue of the map and fold primitives of functional programming.
\begin{align*}
  \text{map:}&\ (k_1,v_1) \longrightarrow [(k_2,v_2)] \\
  \text{reduce:}&\ (k_2,[v_2]) \longrightarrow [(k_3,v_3)]
\end{align*}
The Hadoop infrastructure works as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The input data is stored in a distributed file system (HDFS),
  often serialized (ProtocalBuffers, Avro, Thrift);
  the files are stored on \emph{data nodes} (each file is replicated three times);
  the metadata is on the \emph{name node}.
\item The \emph{mapper} receives the data, as key-value pairs, and emits more key-value pairs.
\item The \emph{combinator} (optional) pre-aggregates the data:
  for instance, when counting words, emitting pairs $(w,1)$ is inefficient
\begin{verbatim}
def mapper(id, terms):
  for term in terms:
    emit term, 1
def reducer(term,counts):
  emit term, sum(counts)
\end{verbatim}
  and the counts can be pre-aggregated.
\item The \emph{partitionner} (optional) groups the data,
  usually by key but, for some applications, you may want to use only part of the key.
\item The framework shuffles and sorts the data (transparently);
\item The \emph{reducer} receives (key,list) pairs and emits more key-value pairs.
\item The \emph{jobtracker} oversees the MapReduce jobs (on the job submission node),
  the \emph{tasktrackers}, on each data node, run the code.
\end{compactitem}

% MapReduce algorithm design
\textbf{3.}
Here are a few MapReduce design patterms: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{In-mapper recombiner}: while it is possible to pre-aggregate the data in a combiner,
  it is often more efficient to do it in the mapper -- 
  not in its \texttt{map} method (called for each key-value pair), 
  but in its \texttt{close} method (called when the node has finished processing all the data).
  You may need to flush the accumulator if it gets too large.
  For some operations, e.g., the mean, the combiner and the reducer do different things.
\begin{verbatim}
def mapper(t,x): 
  emit t, (x,1)
def combiner(t, xcs):
  xs, cs = zip(*xcs) # unzip
  emit t, (sum(xs), sum(cs))
def reducer(t, xcs):
  xs, cs = zip(*xcs) # unzip
  emit t, sum(xs)/sum(cs)
\end{verbatim}
\item \emph{Pairs}: to compute co-occurrences, use pairs of words as keys.
\item \emph{Stripes}: to compute co-occurrences, use one word as key, and a hash table as value.
\item \emph{Order inversion}: if you need the result of the aggregation before it is ready, e.g., to compute relative frequencies, 
  duplicate the data (e.g., one copy for the word count, another for the word pair count),
  make sure the intermedite result (word count) is computed first (set the sort order),
  and partition the data accordingly.
\begin{verbatim}
 emit (w1,*):1
 emit (w1,w2):1
 emit (w1,w3):1
 partition on w1
\end{verbatim}
\item \emph{Value-to-key conversion}: 
  when the reducer must produce sorted data, 
  ask the framework to sort it before, by putting the value to sort in the key.
\item \emph{Reduce-side join}: 
  for a 1-to-1 join of $\mathcal T$ and $\mathcal S$,
  emit $k:(t,T)$ and $k:(s,S)$
  (where $s$ and $t$ are the primary keys of $\mathcal S$ and $\mathcal T$, 
  $k$ the column to join on,
  and $T$, $S$ the other columns),
  for a 1-to-many join, 
  emit $(k,s):S$ and $(k,t):T$,
  define a custom partitioner, ensure that the values of the small table come first and cache them.
\item \emph{Map-side join}: if the data has already been partitionned on disk, 
  e.g., as a result of a previous MapReduce process, 
  map over the larger set after reading the corresponding chunk of the smaller one in the mapper -- 
  no reducer is needed.
\item \emph{Memory-backed join}: 
  put the smaller dataset into memory 
  (or some distributed key-value store: Memcached, etc.)
  in every napper -- if it does not fit into memory, partition it into $n$ pieces
  and perform $n$ in-memory joins.
\end{compactitem}

% Inverted indexing
\textbf{4.}
An \emph{inverted index} maps words to sorted lists of document ids
(there may be some data (``payload'') with the id:
term frequency, position, style (title or not), \textsc{html} link, linguistic function (\textsc{pos}, type of entity: place, person), etc.).
For efficient set operations (intersection, union),
the list should be sorted:
do not emit \texttt{term:(id,freq)}, but \texttt{(term,id):freq}
to have the framework sort the ids
(you need a custom partitionner to ensure that all the messages for a given term are sent to the same reducer).

The volume of data is huge: the process can benefit from compression.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Only store the differences ($d$-gaps) between document ids -- since they are sorted, the numbers are smaller.
\item Variable length \emph{integer coding}: set the 8th bit to zero as long as the number is not finished.
\item Group varInt: group the numbers by 4 and prefix each group with a byte indicating (on 2 bits) the length of each number. 
\item Simple-9: in each 32-bit word, use 4 bits to indicate how the remaining 28 bits are split into equal-sized parts.
\item Unary code: encode $n$ as $n-1$ \texttt{1}s and one \texttt{0}.
\item $\gamma$-code: encode $n$ as $\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor$ \texttt{1}s, a \texttt{0}, and the binary encoding of the number, without the leading 1.
\item $\delta$-code: similar to the $\gamma$ code, but the first part is not unary-encoded but $\gamma$-encoded.
\item  Golomb code: choose $b$, encode $q = \lfloor (n-1)/q \rfloor$ in unary, 
  encode the remainder $r = n-1-qb$ in truncated binary
  % unclear
  (the smaller numbers are encoded on $k$ bits, the others on $k+1$ -- unassigned $k$-bit codes followed by 0 or 1).
\end{compactitem}

MapReduce is a poor solution for document retrieval,
but you could partition the documents, and cache the most-requested results.

% Graph algorithms
\textbf{5.} 
Many sequential graph algorithms rely on some global data structure
(e.g., a priority queue): they are not straightforward to implement in MapReduce.
For instance, breadth-first search (Dijkstra's algorithm) can be implemented
as a sequence of MapReduce iterations:
store the estimated distance from the source in each node,
process each node in each iteration,
stop when the distances no longer change -- there are as many iterations as the longest shortest path
(you also need to keep track of the adjacency list, e.g., by emitting it as well).
\begin{verbatim}
def mapper(id,d):
  for a in neigh(id):
    emit a, d+w
def reducer(id,ds):
  emit id, min(ds)
\end{verbatim}

PageRank (a random walk on the graph, with teleportation, 
computing a score (equilibrium probability) for each node),
\textsc{hits} (random walk on a bipartite graph of hubs and authorities,
computing two scores for each node on the initial graph -- 
the graph is usually query-dependent: pages containing the search terms and their neighbours),
\textsc{salsa} (idem)
can be computed in the same way,
each iteration spreading the probability mass 
(but pay attention to dangling nodes).
Combiners can improve performance,
if you partition the data with some heuristic 
(sort by zipcode, school, language, domain name, etc.).
To avoid underflow, use log-probabilities: they can be added as 
\[ 
  a \oplus b = 
  \begin{cases}
    b + \operatorname{log1p}( e^{a-b} ) & \text{if } a < b \\
    a + \operatorname{log1p}( e^{b-a} ) & \text{if } a \geq b.
  \end{cases}
\]

% EM algorithms
\textbf{6.}
If a statistical model contains parameters 
$\theta$ (unobserved, to be estimated),
hidden variables $y$ (unobserved, but we do not care much about their values),
and data $x$ (observed),
maximum likelihood estimators
\[ (\hat \theta, \hat{ \mathbf y }) = \argmax_{ (\theta, \mathbf y) } P( X = \mathbf x, Y = \mathbf y ; \theta ) \]
give a very noisy estimator of $\theta$: 
prefer the \emph{marginal likelihood} estimator (\ie, integrate $y$ out):
\begin{align*}
  \hat \theta 
  &= \argmax_\theta P(X = \mathbf x ; \theta ) \\
  &= \argmax_\theta \prod_i \sum_y P(X = x_i, Y = y_i ; \theta ).
\end{align*}
The \emph{expectation-maximization} (EM) algorithm 
is a hill-climbing algorithm that attempts to maximize the marginal log-likelihood:
\[ \theta_{n+1} \leftarrow \argmax_\theta \sum_{x,y} P(x,y;\theta_n) \log P(x,y;\theta). \]
This single formula is often, confusingly, presented as two steps: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Estimation of the probability distribution $X,Y\mid\theta=\theta_n$
  in the E step (it is a probability distribution: 
  in the discrete case, it is a set of probabilities);
\item Computation of the expected log-probability
  \[ \sum_{x,y} P(x,y;\theta_n) \log P(x,y;\theta) \]
  (it is a function of $\theta$: if it is explicitly computed, it is also part of the E step);
\item Maximization, in the M step.
\end{compactitem}

Given a hidden Markov Model (HMM), the problems of
computing the probability that the model generated the data 
  (used for clustering or outlier detection)
and computing the most probable sequence of hidden states that could generate the observed data 
can be solved with dynamic programming 
  (the forward algorithm
    computes the probability that the process is in state $q$ at time $t$;
  the Viterbi algorithm
    computes the probability of the most probable sequence of states leading to state $q$ at time $t$):
they can be implemented with MapReduce, 
by processing one column of the dynamic programming table (one time $t$) in each iteration;
each mapper receives a small part of the data;
each reducer computes a cell in the dynamic programming table.
Similarly, estimating the parameters of a HMM (forward-backward algorithm)
can be implemented with EM.

HMM can be used, for instance, to align texts in different languages:
\begin{align*}
  &P( \text{target}, \text{alignment} \mid \text{source} ) \\
  &\qquad= 
  \prod P( t_{i+1} | t_i ) \times \prod P( t_i | s_{a_i} ) \\
  &\qquad= \text{language model} \times \text{translation model}.
\end{align*}

MapReduce can also be used for parallel but non-data-intensive tasks.

The book also mentionned, with few details, other technologies in the MapReduce/Hadoop ecosystem: 
Mahoot (machine learning), 
HBase (tables in HDFS), 
Cassandra (key-value store),
Giraph (bulk-synchronous parallel (BSP) framework),
GPU programming (e.g., for streaming data),
Pig, Hive, Hadapt (data processing).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial risk modelling\\ and portfolio optimization with R}\\
  B. Pfaff (2013)
\end{book}

Each chapter is structured in the same way:
terse presentation of a few mathematical notions
%(insufficient if you do not know them, useless if you do)
(consider it as a mere check-list),
list of relevant R packages (not unlike the CRAN task views),
and (more interesting) examples.

The topics covered are
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Risk measures: VaR, ES, mVaR, mES, coherent measures;
\item Efficient frontier;
\item Distributions to model returns: generalized lambda distribution (GLD), GHD
  (\texttt{ghyp}, \texttt{lmomco::pargld}, \texttt{fBasics::gldFit});
\item Extreme value theory: block maxima, peaks over thresholds,
  exceedance declustering
  (\texttt{evir::gev}, \texttt{esmev::gev.fit}, \texttt{fExtremes::gevFit}, \path{ismev::rlarg.fit}, \texttt{fExtremes::mrlPlot}, \texttt{gpdFit}, \texttt{de\-Cluster});
\item Volatility: GARCH models 
  (\texttt{fGarch::garchFit}, \texttt{rugarch});
\item Dependence: correlation, rank correlation, Kendall's $\tau$, lower tail dependence,
  copula GARCH model (fit a GARCH model to individual time series, and then a copula to the (joint) residuals),
  mixture of copulas
  (\texttt{QRM::fit.tcopula}, \texttt{QRM::rcopula.t}, \texttt{copula::dcopula});
\item Robust estimation: 
  M-estimator, MM-estimator, MVE, MCD, S-estimator, SDE, OGK estimator 
  (\texttt{rrcov::Cov*});
\item Robust optimization:
  scenario-based, box or elliptic uncertainty set
  (\texttt{FRAPO::Socp}, \texttt{Rsocp});
\item Diversification: risk contribution, diversification ratio, 
  defined with the volatility or some downside risk measure 
  such as the tail dependence coefficient
  (\texttt{FRAPO::P*}, \texttt{FRAPO::dr}, \texttt{FRAPO::cr}, \texttt{Portfolio::Analytics::optimize.portfolio});
\item  Portfolio optimization with VaR, ES or drawdown in the objecive or the constraints
  (\texttt{fPortfolio::minRiskPortfolio}, \texttt{FRAPO::P*});
\item Time series models: ARMA, VAR, VECM, SVAR, SVEC (\texttt{urca}, \texttt{vars});
\item Tactical asset allocation: Black-Litterman, copula opinion pollomg, entropy polling (\texttt{TTR}, \texttt{fTrading}, \texttt{BLCOP}).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Lectures on modern convex optimization}\\
  A. Ben-Tal and A. Nemirovski (2012)
\end{book}
Convex programming is too general to be amenable to practical, efficient methods,
but many special cases are -- 
linear, conic quadratic, semi-definite programming are all examples of \emph{conic programming}.

% Linear programming, conic programming
\textbf{1.}
To find a lower bound of 
\[ x^* = \min_x \{ f(x) \ :\ \forall i \ g_i(x) \geq b_i \}, \]
consider a linear combination of the constraints 
\[ \sum_i y_i g_i(x) \geq \sum_i y_i b_i \]
(with $y \geq 0$). 
If $ f(x) \geq \sum_i y_i g_i(x) $,
then 
$\sum y_i b_i$ is a lower bound.
The best such lower bound is 
\[ \max_{ y \geq 0 } \biggl\{ \sum_i y_i b_i \ :\ \forall x \ f(x) \geq \sum_i y_i g_i(x) \biggr\}. \]
This is the \emph{dual problem};
its optimal value is less than that of the primal (weak duality).
If $f$ and $g$ are linear, 
set $x$ to $+1$ and $-1$: the inequalities become equalities, 
and the dual of 
\[ \min_x \{ c' x \ :\ Ax \geq b \} \]
is 
\[ \max_y \{ b' y \ :\ y \geq 0,\  A' y = c \}. \]
For linear problems, there is a \emph{strong duality}: the primal has an optimal solution iff the dual has,
and the optimal values are the same -- 
but there are two ways in which a problem can fail to have a solution: 
it can be infeasible, or unbounded;
if the primal is unbounded, then the dual is infeasible, but the converse if false: both can be infeasible.

Applications of linear programming include 
compressed sensing ($\ell^1$ penalty as an appoximation of a (non-convex) $\ell^0$ one), 
support vector machines (SVM),
discrete-time linear dynamic systems.

Replacing the linear objective function by an arbitrary (convex) function is not the only way of
generalizing linear programming into non-linear programming -- 
one can keep the objective function linear 
but replace the constraints $Ax - b \geq 0$
with $Ax-b \succcurlyeq_K 0$,
where $\succcurlyeq_K$
denotes the partial order induced by a closed, pointed convex cone with non-empty interior $K$.
In the dual problem, $y \geq 0$ gets replaced by 
$y \succcurlyeq_{K^*} 0$, \ie, $y \in K^*$, the dual cone.
This is \d{conic programming}.

To formulate the dual of 
\[ \min_x \{ c' x \ :\ Ax \succcurlyeq_K b \}, \]
we are looking for a lower bound on the objective 
\[  c' x \geq \langle \lambda, Ax \rangle \geq \langle \lambda, b \rangle \]
for some $\lambda$.
But an arbitrary $\lambda$ does not preserve inequalities:
the dual of $K$ is the set of $\lambda$ that always do:
\[ K^* = \{ \lambda \ :\ \forall x \succcurlyeq_K 0 \ \langle \lambda, x \rangle \geq 0 \}. \]

For the dual, consider 
\[ \max_\lambda \{ \langle \lambda, b \rangle \ :\ \lambda \succcurlyeq_{K^*} 0,\ \forall x \langle c, x \rangle \geq \langle \lambda, Ax \rangle \}. \]
The last condition can be written as 
\[ \forall x \ \langle c, x \rangle \geq \langle A* \lambda, x \rangle. \]
By setting $x$ to a basis vector and its opposite, 
we see that this is equivalent to $c = A^* \lambda$.
The dual is therefore: 
\[ \max_\lambda \{ \langle \lambda, b \rangle \ :\ \lambda \succcurlyeq_{K^*} 0,\ A^* \lambda = c \}. \]
The primal can be formulated in the same way
(intersection of a cone and an affine subspace) by adding a variable $y = Ax - b$.
More compactly: 
\begin{align*}
  \text{primal}:&\ 
  \min_y \{ \langle d, y \rangle \ :\ y \in \mathscr L - b , \ y \succcurlyeq_K 0 \} \\
  \text{dual}:&\ 
  \max_\lambda \{ \langle b, \lambda \rangle \ :\ \lambda \in \mathscr L^\perp + d, \ \lambda \succcurlyeq_{K^*} 0 \}.
\end{align*}

\emph{Strong duality} holds if the primal is strictly feasible,
\ie, $\exists x \ Ax - b \succ_K 0$ (strict inequality),
\ie, 
$\accentset{\circ}{K} \inter ( \mathscr L - b ) \neq \nothing$.
(Without strict feasibility, anything can happen: primal solvable but dual infeasible, etc.)

% Conic quadratic programming
\textbf{2.}
The Lorentz cone
\[ L^m = \left\{ x \in \RR^m \ :\ x_m \geq \sqrt{x_1^2 + \cdots + x_{m-1}^2} \right\}; \]
is self-dual.
A \d{second order cone program} (SOCP) is 
\[ \min_x \{ c' x \ :\ Ax - b \in L^{m_1} \times \cdots \times L^{m_k} \}. \]
The condition 
$Ax - b \in L^m$ can be written, 
\[ \begin{pmatrix} Dx \\ p' x \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} d \\ q \end{pmatrix} \in L^m, \]
where
\[ \begin{pmatrix} D & d \\ p' & q \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} A & b \end{pmatrix}, \]
\ie, $p' x - q \geq \n{Dx - d}_2$.

The dual of 
\[ \min_x \{ c' x \ :\ \forall i \ \n{ D_i x - d_i }_2 \leq p_i' q - q_i \} \]
is 
%\begin{align*}
%\max_{\mu, \nu} \biggl\{ \sum_i \mu_i' d_i + \nu_i q_i \ :\ &
%  \sum_i D_i' \mu_i + \nu_i p_i = c,
%  \\ & \forall i \ \n{ \mu_i}_2 \leq \nu_i \biggr\}. 
%\end{align*}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Find} & \mu, \nu \\
\text{To maximize} & \displaystyle \sum\limits_i \mu_i' d_i + \nu_i q_i\\
\text{Such that} & \displaystyle \sum\limits_i D_i' \mu_i + \nu_i p_i = c\\
& \forall i \ \n{ \mu_i}_2 \leq \nu_i. 
\end{array}
\]

Optimization problems rarely arise in this form: they have to be somehow transformed.
For the objective, replace ``minimize $f(x)$''
with ``minimize $t$ such that $t \geq f(x)$''.
For the constraints, there is a very, very long list of functions or constructions that can be used in SOCPs; here are a few of them.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The Euclidian norm: $\n{x}_2 \leq t$;
\item Its square: 
  \begin{align*}
    \n{x}_2^2 \leq t 
    &\iff x' x + \tfrac14( t+1)^2 \leq \tfrac14(t-1)^2 \\
    &\iff \n{\begin{pmatrix} x \\ \tfrac12(t-1)\end{pmatrix}}_2 \leq \dfrac{t+1}2
  \end{align*}
\item Convex quadratic forms (\ie, quadratic programming): 
  \begin{align*}
  & x' C'C x + q' x + r \leq t \\
  \iff& \n{\begin{pmatrix} Cx \\ \tfrac12(t + q'x - r) \end{pmatrix}}_2 \leq \tfrac12(t-q'x-r);
  \end{align*}
\item Many (rational) powers;
\item The $L^p$ norm;
\item etc.
\end{compactitem}

Robustifying a linear program with elliptic (or, more generally, conic-quadratic representable) constraints gives a SOCP.
For instance, the robustification of the constraint $ax - b \geq 0$
is 
\[ \min_{a^*, b^*} \left\{ a^* x - b^* \ :\ \n{\begin{pmatrix} a^* - a \\ b^* - b \end{pmatrix}}_2 \leq \epsilon \right\} \geq 0 \]
\ie,
\[ ax - b + \min_{u,v} \left\{ \begin{pmatrix} x \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}' \begin{pmatrix} u \\ v \end{pmatrix} \ :\ \n{\begin{pmatrix} u \\ v \end{pmatrix}}_2 \leq \epsilon \right\} \geq 0 \]
\ie,
\[ ax -b - \epsilon \n{ \begin{pmatrix} x \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} }_2 \geq 1. \]
(To robustify the objective $c'x$,
replace it with a new variable $t$,
add the constraint $c' x \leq t$,
and robustify it.)
The robustified problem can be used to assess the stability of the objective, the solution, the feasibility status of a linear problem.

% Semi-definite programming.
\textbf{3.}
\d{Semi-definite programming} (SDP)
is another special case of cone programming, 
where the (self-dual) cone is that of positive semi-definite symmetric matrices.
One can assume there is only one inequality -- otherwise, 
put them in a single block-diagonal matrix.
It is a generalization of conic quadratic programming: 
\[ \begin{pmatrix} x \\ t \end{pmatrix} \in L^k \iff \begin{pmatrix} t I_{k-1} & x \\ x' & t \end{pmatrix} \succcurlyeq 0. \]
In addition, SDP can use
  the largest eigenvalue (or singular value),
  the sum of the $k$ largest eigenvalues (or singular values),
  the spectral norm,
  (some rational powers of) the determinant,
  non-negative polynomials, 
  trigonometric polynomials, etc.
Applications include 
the stability of dynamical systems (eigenvalues)
and robust cone programming.

Semi-definite programming provides \emph{relaxations} of combinatorial problems, 
finer than LP relaxations, as follows.
Formulate the problem as a quadratic program with quadratic constraints
(e.g., $x \in \{0,1\}$ is equivalent to $x^2-x = 0$ or, 
since we prefer inequalities, $x^2-x \leq 0$, $x - x^2 \leq 0$).
\[ x^* = \argmin_x \{ f(x) \ :\ g(x) \leq 0 \} \]
Consider the dual problem, \ie, transform the constraints to penalties,
with coefficients to be determined,
\[ f_\lambda(x) = f(x) + \lambda g(x), \ \lambda \geq 0; \]
this provides a lower bound on $f(x^*)$
\[ \zeta^* = \inf_x f_\lambda(x) \leq f(x^*). \]
But, since $f_\lambda(x)$ is a quadratic form, 
$\forall x \ \zeta \leq f_\lambda(x)$
means that 
$f_\lambda(\cdot) - \zeta \succcurlyeq 0$.
Finding the best bound is therefore a semi-definite program: 
\[ 
  (\zeta^*, \lambda^*) = 
  \argmax_{\zeta, \lambda} 
  \{ 
    \zeta \ :\ 
    f_\lambda(\cdot) - \zeta \succcurlyeq 0, \ 
    \lambda \geq 0 
  \}. 
\]

%Applications to graph theory: 
%  Shannon capacity of a grap, maxcut
%Application: 
%  robust cone programming -- 
%  assessing the stability of a SOCP

The SDP relaxation of a quadratically-constrained quadratic  program 
can also be formulated as follows: 
replace inhomogeneous quadratic forms 
\[ g(x) = x' A x + 2 b' x + c \]
with homogeneous ones
\[ 
  G(x,t) = 
  x' A x + 2 t b' x + c t^2 = 
  \begin{pmatrix} t \\ x  \end{pmatrix}' 
  \begin{pmatrix} c & b' \\ b & A  \end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix} t \\ x \end{pmatrix},
\]
then, replace the quadratic forms $x' G x$ 
with $\tr(GX)$. The relaxation corresponds to the embedding
\[
\left\{
\begin{array}{r@{\,}c@{\,}l}
  \RR^n & \longrightarrow & S^{n+1}_+ \\
  x & \longmapsto & \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ x \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ x \end{pmatrix}'.
\end{array}
\right.
\]

Applications of SDP relaxation include graph problems
(Shannon capacity, maxcut),
other combinatorial problems, chance constraints.

Finding the best inner ellipsoidal approximation of a polytope defined by inequalities (or, more generally, of an intersection of ellipsoids) is a SDP.

Finding the best outer ellipsoidal approximation of a polytope defined as the convex hull of its vertices (or, more generally, of a union of ellipsoids) is a SDP.

% Interior point methods
\textbf{4.}
Convex programming is a polynomial problem: 
the \emph{ellipsoid method} needs $O(n^2 \log \epsilon^{-1})$ steps 
(and each steps needs $O(n^2)$ operations) and goes as follows.
Start with an ellipsoid containing the optimal set.
If its center is not feasible, there exists a separating hyperplane
between the center and the (convex) feasible set: use it to cut the ellipsoid.
If it is feasible, the (sub) gradient of the objective defines a hyperplane,
on one side of which the optimal set lies: use it to cut the ellipsoid.
In both cases, take an outer approximation of the cut elllipsoid and iterate.

\d{Interior point methods} are interior penalty methods, 
\ie, to solve 
\[ \min_x \{ c' x \ : \ x \in \mathscr X \}, \]
they solve a sequence of unconstrained problems 
\[ x_*(t) = \argmin_x  t c' x + K(x) \]
where $K$ is a \emph{barrier function}
(if $x_n \to x$ and $x \in \partial \mathscr X$,
then $K(x_n) \to +\infty$)
and $t$ increases,
$(x_*(t))_{t \geq 0}$ traces the \emph{central path}
(each is solved with the Newton method, 
which converges quadratically if sufficiently close to the solution).

With conic programming, a clever choice of the barrier function
\begin{align*} 
  x \geq 0 &\leadsto - \log x \\
  X \succcurlyeq 0 &\leadsto - \log \det X \\
  x \in L^k &\leadsto -\log( x_k^2 - x_1^2 - \cdots - x_{k-1}^2 )
\end{align*}
and $t_{n+1} = 1.1 t_n$, a single Newton step suffices for each value of $t$
and the algorithm converges in $O(\log \epsilon^{-1})$ steps.
It is easier to monitor convergence and stay close to the central path
if one traces both the primal and dual central paths;
they are related by the augmented \emph{complementary slackness} relation
\begin{align*}
  x^*(t) &= - t^{-1} \nabla K( s^*(t)) \\
  s^*(t) &= - t^{-1} \nabla K( x^*(t)) 
\end{align*}
(since the cone is self-dual, the barrier is the same for both problems).

% Large-scale problems
\textbf{5.}
Interior point methods are polynomial, 
but for \emph{large-scale problems} $O(n^3)$ or $O(n^2)$ will not do:
if we want the algorithm to terminate in a reasonable amount of time, 
we can afford to evaluate the objective, its gradient, but nothing fancier.

There are information-theoretic bounds on the number of steps needed 
to achieve a given precision $\epsilon$,
e.g., $O(\log n / \epsilon^2)$ as $n \to \infty$ for ball constraints 
(and much worse for box constraints) -- it is almost independent of the dimension.

\d{Mirror descent} is a generalization of the projected gradient 
\[ x_{n+1} \leftarrow \text{Prox}_{ x_n } ( \gamma_t f'(x_t) ), \]
where one replaces the norm, distance and gradient to better suit the problem. 
For instance, 
\begin{itemize}[--]
\item Over an $L^2$ ball, $\n\cdot_2$, $\omega(x) = \tfrac12 x' x$
  and the proxy mapping 
  \begin{align*}
    \text{prox}_x \xi &= \argmin_{ y \in X } \omega(y) + \langle \xi - \omega'(x), y \rangle \\
    &= \argmin_{ y \in X } \n{ x - \xi - y }_2
  \end{align*}
  is the projection of $x-\xi$ on $X$;
\item Over the simplex $\Delta_n$, 
  use $\n\cdot_1$ and $\omega(x) = \sum x_i \log x_1$;
\item Over the \emph{spectahedron}
  $\{ x \in S^n \ :\ x \succcurlyeq 0, \ \tr x \leq 1 \}$,
  use $\n{ \lambda(x) }_1$ and $\omega(x) = \sum_i \lambda_i(x) \log \lambda_i(x) $.
\end{itemize}

A convex-concave saddle point problem 
(e.g., equilibrium, in a zero-sum game)
\[ \min_{ x \in X } \max_{ y \in Y } \phi(x,y), \]
where $\phi$ is convex in $x$, concave in $y$,
can be solved with mirror descent by considering the vector field 
$(\phi_x, \phi_y)$ instead of the gradient.
% The dual just interchanges max and min: $ \max_{ y \in Y } \min_{ x \in X } \phi(x,y). $

There are a few variants: 
stochastic mirror descent if the gradient is noisy (the expected error converges in the same way),
bundle mirror descent (momentum, to address plateau problems), 
etc.

Most non-smooth optimization problems are of the form 
\[ \min_{ x \in X } \max_{ y \in Y } \phi(x,y) \]
and can be smoothed as (Nesterov smoothing)
\[ \min_{x \in X} \max_{ y \in Y} \phi(x,y) + d(y). \]
The \emph{mirror prox} algorithm (a variant of saddle point mirror descent) 
can be used to solve those problems.
Convergence, for a non-smooth optimization problem, using only first-order information,
is at best $O(1/sqrt t)$,
but it can be brought to $O(1/t)$ after such a transformation.
%For instance, least squares 
%\[ \min_{ \n x _2 \leq R \n{ B x - b } _2 \]
%can be written 
%\[ \min_{ \n x \leq R } \max_{ \n y \leq 1 } y' (Bx - b) \]
%and smoothed as 
%\[ \min_{ \n x \leq R } \max_{ \n y \leq 1 } y' (Bx - b) + \tfrac12 \delta y' y. \]
Examples of non-smooth functions that can be used in this wayinclude:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The $L^p$ norm, $\n x _p = \max_{ \n y _q \leq 1 } \langle y, x \rangle$.
\item The $L^p$ norm of the singular vectors of a matrix;
\item The $L^p$-norm of the positive part, 
  \[ \n{ x^+ }_p = \max_{\substack{ \n y _q \leq 1 \\ y \geq 0}} \langle y, x \rangle \]
\item The maximum entry of a vector, or the sum of the $k$ largest entries 
  \[ s_k(x) = \max_{ y \in \Delta_{n,k} } \langle y, x \rangle \]
where $\Delta_{n,k} = [ \mathbf 1' y = k, \ y \geq 0 ]$;
\item The sum of the $k$ largest eigenvalues
  \[ S_k(x) = \max_{\substack{ y \succcurlyeq 0 \\ \tr y = k }} \tr(yx). \]
\end{compactitem}

%%
%% 2013-07
%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum likelihood estimation\\ of a multi-dimensional log-concave density}\\
  M. Cule et al. (2010)
\end{book}
The log-concave maximum likelihood density estimator is well-defined, 
and asymptotically finds the log-concave density that minimizes
the Kullback-Leibler divergence with the true density.
Its support is the convex hull of the sample data 
(log-concave distributions have thin tails).

The estimator can be used for data visualization
(e.g., checking for the presence of elliptic contours),
classification (estimate the density of each class),
clustering (fit a mixture of log-concave distributions with the EM algorithm,
as you would a mixture of Gaussians),
monte Carlo estimation of functionals of the density 
(probabilities, moments, expectations, entropy, etc.),
mixing detection (compare the log-concave estimator with a mixture of log-concave densities,
or with $f(x) \propto \exp( \phi(x) _ c \n{x}^2 )$, 
with $\phi$ concave and fixes values $c>0$ (this also accounts for fat tails),
or with a permutation test comparing the data with samples from the estimated distribution).

Contrary to kernel-based estimators, 
this does not require the choice of a bandwidth matrix
(problematic in high dimensions).

The density is found by minimizing 
\[ \sigma(y_1,\dots,y_n) = - \dfrac1n \sum y_i + \int_C \exp h_y(x) dx \]
where 
  $y_i = \log \hat f(x_i)$ are the log-densities evaluated at the data points $x_i$,
  $h_y$ is the smallest concave function with $\forall i \  h_y(x_i) \geq y_i$,
  the first term is the (negated) log-likelihood,
  the second term can be thought of as a Lagrangian term %(???)
  (we want the density to integrate to 1),
  $C$ is the convex hull of the points (the support of the density).
Since the concave function $h_y$ is affine on each triangle of a triangulation of the points
(obtained as a side result of the QuickHull algorithm),
the integral is easy to compute.
The objective fuction is convex, but non-differentiable:
one could use Newton's algorithm with a subgradient, but convergence would be slow
(linear or worse, versus quadratic for differentiable functions),
or \emph{Shor's $r$ algorithm} 
(space dilation in the direction of the difference of two consecutive gradients -- 
empirically faster)

There is an R implementation in the \texttt{LogConcDEAD} package
(in dimension 1, also check \texttt{logconden}).
\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient rank reduction\\ of correlation matrices}\\
  I. Grubi\v si\'c and R. Pietersz (2005)
\end{book}
A constrained optimization problem 
\[ \max_{x \in \RR^n} \{ f(x) \ :\ g(x) = 0 \} \]
can be formulated and solved as an unconstrained one
if the feasible set $M = [ g(x) = 0 ] \subset \RR^n$ 
is a manifold $M$ 
on which you can explicitly compute the gradient
and (for the Newton and conjugate gradient methods) the hessian:
the gradient comes from the isomorphism between 
$T_y M$ and $T^*_y M$ induced by the Riemannian structure,
\[ F_y (u) = \langle (\grad F)_y, u \rangle_{T_yM}, \]
the hessian from the Levi-Civita connection
and the updates to the solution (``moving in some direction'')
are given by parallel transport alomg geodesics.

In the case of rank-constrained correlation matrices, 
the feasible set is not a manifold 
(it is a stratified manifold, each stratum corresponding to a value for the rank),
but it can be described as the quotient of a manifold (the ``Cholesky manifold'')
by the orthogonal group.
The resulting algorithm is equivalent to optimization using a parametrization with spherical coordinates.

\nextbook

%%
%% Sparse inverse covariance
%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse inverse covariance selection via alternating linearization methods}\\
  K. Scheinberg et al. (2010)
\end{book}
There are many algorithms for sparse inverse covariance selection (SICS), 
\ie, sparse estimators of the inverse covariance matrix
corresponding to sparse graphical models
($ \Sigma^{-1}_{ij} = 0$ iff $ X_i \indep X_j | X_{k, k \neq i,j} $): 
\[ \max_{ X \succ 0 } \log \det X - \langle X, \Sigma \rangle + \lambda \n{X}_1. \]
The alternating linearization method solves the optimization problem 
\[ \min_x f(x) + g(x) \]
with $f$ and $g$ convex,
by rewriting the problem as 
\[ \min_{x,y} \{ f(x) + g(y) \ :\ x = y \} \]
and separately updating $x$ and $y$.
It can be adapted to solve the SICS problem.
% The functions are not well-behaved: not defined everywhere, not Lipschitz differentiable.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Model selection\\ through sparse maximum likelihood estimation\\ for multivariate gaussian or binary data}\\
  O. Banerjee et al. (2006)
\end{book}
The sparse inverse covariance matrix
\[ \argmax_{X\succ0} \log \det X - \tr SX - \rho \n{X}_1, \]
where $S$ is the sample variance matrix, can be estimated by solving the dual problem, 
which is of the form
\[ \max_W \{ \log \det W \ :\ \n{W-S}_{\infty} \leq \lambda \}. \]
It can be estimated one coordinate at a time 
(block-coordinate descent)
\[ w_{12} \leftarrow \argmin_y \{ y' W_{11}^{-1} y \ :\; \n{ y - s_{12} }_\infty \leq \rho \}. \]
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse inverse covariance estimation\\ with the graphical lasso}\\
  J. Friedman et al. (2007)
\end{book}
This problem can in turn be solved though its dual 
\[ \min_\beta \tfrac12 \n{ W_{11}^{1/2} \beta - b }^2 + \rho \n{ \beta}_1, \]
which is a lasso problem.
The \texttt{glasso} package provides an implementation.
\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Covariance selection and estimation\\ via penalized normal likelihood}\\
  N. Liu et al. 
\end{book}
The coefficients of the Cholesky matrix can be interpreted as regression coefficients -- 
penalized (lasso, ridge) regression gives a penalized Cholesky matrix, 
and a penalized variance matrix.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Covariance selection for non-chordal graphs via chordal embedding}\\
  J. Dahl et al.
\end{book}
The sparsity pattern of a variance matrix forms a chordal graph.
One can easily estimate the inverse covariance matrix under a chordal soarsity constraint and,
with more work, an arbitrary sparsity constraint.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse permutation invariant\\ covariance estimation}\\
  A.J. Rothman et al. (2008)
\end{book}
The $L^1$-penalized inverse-variance (concentration) estimator 
\[ \argmin_{\Omega \succ 0} \tr(\Omega \hat \Sigma) - \log \va\Omega + \lambda \n{ \Omega^- }_1 \]
(where $\Omega^-$ are the off-diagonal elements of $\Omega$) 
can be estimated by parametrizing $\Omega$ as $ \Omega = T' T $, 
with $T$ lower-triangular, to ensure positivity,
and using a quadratic approximation of the absolute value: 
\[ \va{ u_{n+1} } \approx 
\dfrac12 \dfrac{ u_{n+1}^2 }{ \va{ u_n } } + \dfrac12 \va{u_n} 
\text{ or }
\dfrac12 \dfrac{u_{n+1}^2}{ \va{u_n} + \epsilon} + \dfrac12 \va{u_n}.
\]

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient estimation\\ of covariance selection models}\\
  F. Wong et al. (2003)
\end{book}
Bayesian approach for covariance selection (sparse estimation of the inverse covariance),
with a $\Gamma$ prior on the diagonal elements,
and a zero-inflated prior on the correlations,
estimated with Gibbs sampling,
updating one partial correlation at a time 
(to keep the matrix positive definite).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Shrinkage algorithms\\ for MMSE covariance estimation}\\
  Y. Chen et al. (2009)
\end{book}
How to choose the shrinkage coefficient when estimating a covariance matrix
(the optimal coefficient depends on the unknown variance matrix and cannot be used,
the Ledoit-Wolf coefficient can be improved on).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Identifying small mean-reverting portfolios}\\
  A. d'Aspremont (2008)
\end{book}
When looking for cointegrated assets to form mean-reverting portfolios,
sparse portfolio are more investable.
For instance, one could look for a portfolio whose prices $P_t = S_t x$ maximize 
the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck mean reversion parameter $\lambda$
\[ d P_t = \lambda ( \bar P - P_t ) dt + \sigma d Z_t. \]
As a proxy for the mean-reversion,
one can use the Box-Tiao \emph{predictability}
\[ \nu = \dfrac{ \var_{t-1} [ P_{t-1} ] }{ \var_{t-1} [ P_t ] } = \dfrac{ \var [ P_{t-1} ] }{ \var [ P_t | P_{t-1} ] } \]
(maximizing mean-reversion corresponds to minimizing predictability, \ie, momentum).
If the asset prices follow a VAR(1) process 
$ S_t = S_{t-1} A + Z_t $,
then 
$P_t = S_t x = S_{t-1} A x + Z_t x$ 
and \[ \nu(x)  = \dfrac{ x' A' \Gamma A x }{x' \Gamma x} \]
where $\Gamma = \var S_t$ -- it is a generalized eigenvalue problem.

To estimate the matrices $A$ and $\Gamma$, one can use 
%$\ell^1$-penalized 
penalized 
methods
(covariance selection for $\Gamma$, lasso for $A$).

The sparse generalized eigenvalue problem 
\[ 
  \max_x 
  \left\{ 
    \dfrac{x'Ax}{x'Bx},\ \n{x}_0,\ \n x = 1
  \right\}
\]
can be approximately solved greedily 
(progressively increase the number of non-zero coefficients,
assuming that those sets of indices are increasing)
or by semi-definite relaxation.

%%
%% Sparse inverse covariance (end)
%%









%%
%% CS
%%

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{An open-source implementation of the critical line algorithm for portfolio optimization}\\
  D.H. Bailey and M. L\'opez de Prado (2013)
\end{book}
When people compute the efficient frontier
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
  \text{Find} & w \\
  \text{To minimize} & w' \Sigma w \\
  \text{and maximize} & w' \mu \\
  \text{Such that} & w' \textbf 1 = 1 \\
  & l \leq w \leq u
\end{array}
\]
they often solve a series of optimization problems, each with a different target return, and interpolate.
This is imprecise and computationally wasteful.

The \emph{turning points} on the efficient frontier are efficient portfolios such that nearby efficient portfolios contain different assets -- 
points, on the efficient frontier, at which an asset enters or leaves the portfolio.
Between two turning points, the efficient portfolios are the solution of an unconstrained problem 
(only equality constraints: $w' \mu = \mu_{\text{target}}$ and $w' \textbf1 = 1$)
on a subset of the assets, 
for which an analytic expression can be derived (via Lagrange multipliers);
they are also convex conbinations of the turning points (2-fund theorem).
The efficient frontier can be computed by starting with the maximum return portfolio and moving down, 
from turning point to turning point.

The authors provide an implementation in Python.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Loglog counting of large cardinalities}\\
  M. Durand and P. Flajolet (2003)
\end{book}

To approximately count the number of different elements in a stream of words
(words in a text, IP addresses in an intrusion detection system, values in a column in a database for query optimization, etc.)
one can: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Distribute the hashed (randomized) values in $N$ buckets, in a bitmap,
  and count the occupied buckets (there are adaptive and hierarchical variants);
\item Keep a proportion $p \ll 1$ of the data (by looking at the first bits of the hashed value) and count it, exactly;
\item Look at the maximum position $\rho(x)$ of the first non-zero bit in the hashed data: $\log_2 N \approx \max \rho(x)$.
\end{compactitem}
The log-log algorithm uses this idea, after splitting the data into $m$ buckets, and averages the estimates.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{HyperLogLog: the analysis of a near-optimal cardinality estimation algorithm}\\
  P. Flajolet et al. (2007)
\end{book}
In the loglog counting algorithm, one can replace the arithmetic mean of the 
$\hat N_i$ with the harmonic mean of the $2^{\hat N_i}$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Firefly algorithm, stochastic test functions\\ and design optimisation}\\
  X.S. Yang (2010)
\end{book}
The firefly algorithm is similar to particle swarm optimization (PSO),
but each particle moves randomly (no momentum)
and is more attracted by nearby fitter particles (it is not attracted by less fit particles at all).
If the local minima are hardly distinguishable, the particles can end up in several of them.

