%  Semantically secure under a strange, strong assumption.
%  Variants are secure under a more natural assumption.
% (twin ElGamal):
%the public key is $(g, g^a, g^b)$; the private key is $(a, b)$;
%to encrypt, take $c$ at random, 
%encrypt with $\text{key}=\text{hash}(g^a,g^b,g^c)$, send $g^c$ and the cyphertext.
%????

%One-way function: difficult to invert. ($\text{existence} \implies \text{P} \neq \text{NP}$).
%  Examples: secure PRG, discrete log ($f(x+y)=f(x)f(y)$), $e$-th root (property: $f(xy)=f(x)f(y)$, and it has a trap door).

