To avoid overfitting, use regularization
(add a $\lambda \n{w} ^2$ penalty or,
equivalently (by duality), a constraint $\n{w} \leq C$)
and (10-fold) cross-validation
(to select the regularization parameters and/or the model complexity).

The examples covered included
singular value decomposition (SVD, for recommendation systems);
the perceptron,
logistic regression,
neural networks;
soft-margin support vector machines (SVM),
with details of the corresponding 
quadratic optimization problem and its dual,
which gives the support vectors 
(the number of support vectors measures the complexity of the model, 
not unlike the VC dimension);
the kernel trick
($k(x,y)=(1+x'y)^k$, which gives polynomials of degree up to $k$,
$k(x,y) = \exp -\gamma \n{x-y}$);
radial basis functions
(apply $k$-means on the inputs, 
regress $y \sim \sum \exp -\gamma \n{x-\mu_i}$,
choose $\gamma$ and $k$ by cross-validation -- 
this is very similar to SVM with a gaussian kernel,
where the $\mu_i$ are support vectors instead of $k$-means centers.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to logic}\\
  M. Genesereth and E. Kao (Coursera 2012)
\end{book}

\d{Propositional logic} is the logic of truth tables:
given propositional constants $p$, $q$, $r$, etc.
and a \emph{truth assignment},
you can check if complex sentences 
($p \wedge q$, $(p \vee q) \Rightarrow r$, etc.)
are true or false.
A sentence is \emph{valid} if it is always true, for all truth assignments;
\emph{contingent} if it is sometimes true, sometimes false;
\emph{unsatisfiable} if it is never true;
\emph{satisfiable} if it is sometimes true (valid or contingent);
\emph{falsifiable} if it is sometimes false (unsatisfiable or contingent).
A set of sentences $\Delta$ \d{entails} a sentence $\phi$,
written $\Delta \vDash \phi$, if, 
for all truth assignments for which $\Delta$ is true,
so is $\phi$.
Entailment is a satisfiability problem: 
$\Delta \vDash \phi$ iff $\Delta \union \{ \neg \phi \}$ is unsatisfiable.

\d{Satisfiability} (SAT)
is an NP-complete problem,
but there are some improvements on the naive algorithm (checking all the truth tables):
arrange the possible truth assignments in a tree (each level corresponds to a literal,
each node corresponds to a partial truth assignment),
simplify the problem at each node by partially evaluating the formula,
and prune the tree whenever possible.
Besides deterministic algorithms (DPLL),
there are a few heuristics (WalkSAT).

The \d{Fitch system} is a set of rules of inference,
to draw conclusions from premisses (notation: $\Delta \vdash \phi$).
Since it allows subproofs
\[
  \dfrac{ \phi \vdash \psi }{ \phi \Rightarrow \psi }
\]
(if you are not familiar with this notation: the premises are above the bar, 
the conclusions below),
its proofs are shorter that those of other systems (Mendelson, etc.).
A proof system is \emph{sound} when:
if $\Delta \vdash \phi$, then $\Delta \vDash \phi$
(\ie, if something is provable, it is true);
it is \emph{complete} when the converse holds.
Fitch for propositional logic is sound and complete.

\d{Propositional resolution} provides a more algorithmic way of checking
entailment:
distribute ($\phi \vee(\psi\wedge \chi) = (\phi\vee\psi)\wedge(\phi\vee\chi)$)
all the premisses to put them in \emph{clausal form}
$ \bigwedge_i \bigvee_j p_{ij} $, 
often written as 
\begin{align*}
  &\{ p_{11},\dots,p_{1 k_1} \} \\
  &\cdots\\
  &\{ p_{n1},\dots,p_{n k_n} \}
\end{align*}
and apply the propositional resolution principle
\[
  \dfrac{
    \substack{
      \displaystyle \{ p, q_1,\dots,q_m \} \\
      \displaystyle \{ \neg p, r_1, \dots, r_n \}
    }
  }{
    \{ q_1,\dots,q_m, r_1, \dots, r_n \}.
  }
\]
The empty clause is a contradiction.
The propositional resolution principle is not complete
(e.g., $\{p\},\{q\}\nvdash\{p,q\}$),
but unsatisfiable clauses can be proven to be so:
to prove $\Delta \vDash \phi$,
show $ \Delta \union \{\phi\} \vdash \{\} $.

\d{Relational logic} adds quantifiers, 
variables (needed if you want to use quantifiers),
relations and functions.
(You will often need an equality relation,
syntactic sugar for an equivalence relation with the substitution property:
$\forall x \forall y \ p(x) \wedge (x=y) \Rightarrow p(y)$.)
We can still define the notion of truth assignment 
and logical entailment but,
in presence of functions, the set of sentences for which you have to provide 
a truth value (the \d{Herbrand base}) is infinite.
Here are a few examples: 
$\ZZ/4$ can be described with 4 literals and relations ``same'', ``next'', ``plus'';
$\NN$ (Peano arithmetic) can be described with one literal 0, a ``successor'' function
and ``same'', ``next'', ``plus'' relations;
lists can be represented with a ``nil'' literal, a ``cons'' function and an ``append'' relation;
BNF grammars; propositional logic itself (\emph{meta-level logic}).

\emph{Finite relational logic} (\ie, the Herbrand base is finite, in particular, 
there are no functions) is equivalent to propositional logic: 
to see it, write everything in \emph{prenex form}, 
\ie, with the quantifiers on the outside.
It is \emph{compact}: every unsatisfiable set of sentences 
contains a finite unsatisfiable subset.
\emph{Omega relational logic} (infinitely many literals but no functions)
is not compact, but is \emph{semi-decidable}: 
if a finite set of sentences is unsatisfiable, it can be shown in finite time
(using the prenex transformation).

The Fitch proof system is still applicable to relational logic
(with a few more rules: universal and existential introduction and elimination,
for which you have to pay attention to free variables;
domain closure; induction):
if $\Delta \vDash \phi$, then there exists a finite proof $\Delta \vdash \phi$
(but if $\Delta \nvDash \phi$, the search for a proof will take forever).

\emph{Relational resolution} is useful, but incomplete: it is based 
on satisfiability, but relational logic is not decidable.
To put an expression in clausal form, 
existential elimination,
$
  \exists a \ p(x) \longrightarrow p(a(y)),
$
adds a new function (\emph{Skolem function}) of the enclosing universal variables~$y$.
Once you are only left with universal quantifiers, on the outside, you can drop them.
Unification is also trickier: you may need to substitute some of the variables and
drop part of the clauses.

Relational resolution can be used for \emph{answer extraction}:
to find $x$ such that $p(x)$,
add the clause $\{ \neg p(x), \text{goal}(x) \}$, 
\ie, $p(x) \Rightarrow \text{goal}(x)$,
and continue 
until you get $\{\}$ (no solution),
or $\{\text{goal}(a)\}$ ($a$ is a solution),
or $\{\text{goal}(a_1),\dots,\text{goal}(a_n)\}$ (one of the $a_i$ is a solution).
It may also take forever (relational logic is not decidable).
The algorithm can be sped up by removing tautologies 
($\{p, \neg p, \dots\}$), duplications ($\Psi$ if $\Phi \sigma \subset \Psi$)
or clauses containing a literal that is never negated.

In \d{Herbrand semantics}, the universe of discourse contains
what the logic describes and nothing else;
in \d{Tarskian semantics}, the universe of discourse can be larger
(e.g., the reals and the hyperreals (non-standard numbers) 
are both models of the reals).
Propositional logic and relational logic are examples of Herbrand semantics.
\d{First order logic} is the tarskian equivalent of relational logic
(you can emulate first order logic with relational logic: just add a new unary function).
Fitch without domain closure and induction is sound and complete for 
first order logic: 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  >=implies,
  double shafted/.style={double,double equal sign distance}
  %  shorten >=2pt, shorten <=3pt}
]
  \matrix (m) [matrix of math nodes, row sep=1em, column sep=3em]
    { \Delta \vdash_{\text{Fitch} \backslash \{\textsc{dc}, \textsc{ind}\} } \phi
      &
      \Delta \vDash_{\textsc{fol}} \phi \\
      \Delta \vdash_{\text{Fitch}} \phi
      &
      \Delta \vDash_{\textsc{rl}} \phi \\
    };
    \path[<->] (m-1-1) edge[double shafted] (m-1-2);
    \path[->] (m-1-1) edge[double shafted] (m-2-1);
    \path[->] (m-2-1) edge[double shafted] (m-2-2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{R in finance 2012}\\
\end{book}

To design a real-time (\ie, you cannot look into the future) finite-sample filter
approximating some idealized filter,
\begin{align*}
    y_t      &= \sum_{k\in\ZZ}     \gamma_k x_{t-k} \\
    \hat y_t &= \sum_{k=0}^n   \hat\gamma_k x_{t-k},
\end{align*}
express it in frequency space
\[
  \Gamma(\omega) = \sum_k \gamma_k e^{- i k \omega}
\]
and find the $\hat \gamma_k$ that minimize
\[
  \int_{\va\omega=1} \va{ \Gamma(\omega) - \hat\Gamma(\omega) }^2
\]
You can decompose this quantity into 
a penalty for the delay between the real and idealized filter
and a penalty for the amount of remaining noise.
% Details: http://blog.zhaw.ch/idp/sefblog/

The singular value decomposition (SVD) 
can be used to quickly test for cointegration among a large number of assets.
\iffalse
  # Data
  x <- matrix(rnorm(120), nc=6)
  x <- apply(x, 2, cumsum)
  x[,1] <- x[,2] + .1*rnorm(nrow(x))
  x[,3] <- x[,4] + .1*rnorm(nrow(x))
  n <- nrow(x)
  # Computations
  X0 <- apply(x,2,diff)
  X1 <- x[-n,]
  s0 <- svd(X0)
  s1 <- svd(X1)
  Z <- t(s1$u) %*% s0$u
  s <- svd(Z)  # We want the smallest values of s$d
  epsilon <- .1
  beta <- sqrt(n) * s1$v %*% diag(ifelse(s1$d<epsilon,0,1/s1$d)) %*% s$u
  alpha <- 1/sqrt(n) * s0$v %*% diag(s0$d) %*% t(Z) %*% s$u
  # Result: one relation per column...
  apply( beta %*% t(alpha), 2, function(u) { i <- order(-abs(u)); u[i>2] <- 0; u} )
\fi

The \texttt{dlm} package can fit, filter (Kalman), smooth
state space models: ARMA, stochastic volatility, 
regression with time-varying parameters (\texttt{dlmModReg}), etc.
and help you cross-validate the result.

The \texttt{Rcpp} package allows you to transparently 
manipulate R objects 
in C++, eschewing the gory macros you have to put up with 
when using \texttt{.Call} directly -- 
and the \texttt{inside} package
simplifies things even further.
But you are left with those template-related error messages...
It is even more useful with the advent of reference classes (R5).
In the other direction, you can use \texttt{Rinside} to, 
say, include R in a GUI or a web application 
(e.g., with the Wt framework).

To mine textual data,
use Python and NLTK,
of the \texttt{tm} package and its many plugings
(\nolinkurl{tm.plugin.webmining}, \nolinkurl{tm.plugin.sentiment}, 
\nolinkurl{tm.plugin.tags}).

\texttt{CppBugs} is faster than \texttt{MCMCpack}, and 
(almost?) as flexible as JAGS.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Evaluating the design of the R language}\\
  F. Morandat et al.
\end{book}

Unflattering but objective evaluation of R:
the language is designed to be used interactively (named and optional arguments);
the main data type is the vector (with support for missing values);
it encourages vectorized operations.
It mixes seemingly incompatible programming language paradigms:
functional and lazy, but dynamic, imperative and reflexive
(\texttt{parse}, 
\texttt{eval},
\texttt{quote},
\texttt{bquote},
\texttt{substitute},
\texttt{deparse}).
The current implementation is ``massively inefficient'',
mainly because of the laziness of the language 
(which also fails to deliver the performance improvements it should bring),
immoderate memory usage (garbage collection is more expensive, frequent and slow 
than it should).
The two class systems (S3, S4 -- there is also R5 and a few more in separate packages)
look ``like an afterthought''.
It is ``hopelessly non-thread-safe'' and lacks standard data structures
(growable arrays, hash maps).
``It is not the ideal language to develop robust packages.''
``As a language, R is like French;
it has an elegant core, but every rule comes with a set of ad hoc exceptions 
that directly contradict it''.

The article also provides a \emph{formal semantics} 
of the language,
and analyzes large amounts of code,
to see how the language is actually used.

The article does not mention the ability to easily describe statistical models 
(\texttt{formula}), the data manipulation (\texttt{reshape2}, \texttt{plyr})
and plotting capabilities -- but these are add-ons, 
rather than parts of the core language.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The nature of alpha}\\
  A.M. Berd (2011)
\end{book}

Even when they do not explicitly trade options,
there is often a negative correlation between the returns of a hedge fund 
and market volatility, at least for ``convergence'' (mean-reverting) strategies:
indeed, those strategies are essentially a short strangle
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline,scale=.25]
  \draw (-1,.4) -- (1,.4);
  \draw (0,-.3) -- (0,1);
  \draw[thick] (-1,-.3) -- (-.5,.7) -- (.5,.7) -- (1,-.3);
\end{tikzpicture}.
It is the opposite for momentum strategies.
Measure this risk, diversify your strategies and,
if it is not enough, add some explicit volatility hedging.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The feedback effect of hedging\\ in portfolio optimization}\\
  P. Henry-Labord\`ere (2004)
\end{book}

Delta hedging can have a destabilizing market impact:
to hedge a call,
you buy when the market rises.
This is the opposite of portfolio optimization,
which suggests to buy when the price falls.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Circadian patterns and burstiness\\ in human communication activity}\\
  H.H. Jo et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Human activities (phone calls, tweets, etc.) show both periodicities 
(circadian, weekly) and bursts.
The bursts can be modeled with a \d{cascading Poisson process},
\ie, a Poisson process $(X_t)_t$ with intensity
\[ \lambda_t = \lambda_0 + \lambda_1 \sum_{s<t} e^{-(t-s)/\tau} X_s. \]
The periodicity can be modeled with a change of time
$Y_t = X_{T_t}$, 
with $dT_t/dt$ periodic (and positive).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust pricing and hedging\\ of double no-touch options}\\
  A.M.G. Cox and J. Ob\l\'oj (2009)
\end{book}

Given a set of calls and digital call prices, with no arbitrage oportunities
(there are a few conditions to be satisfied:
$C(0)=S_0$, $C(\infty)=0$, $C'(0)\geq-1$, $D(k)=-C'(k)$),
how much latitude do we have to extend the market model to include other options?
In the case of double-no-touch options, one can devise a few super- and sub-hedges
and show that the corresponding bounds are as tight as possible.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Conquering the Greeks in Monte Carlo:
  efficient calculation of the market sensitivities and hedge ratios 
  of financial assets\\ by direct numeric simulation}\\
  M. Avellaneda and R. Gamba (2000)
\end{book}

When pricing options via Monte Carlo simulations,
you do not get exactly the market prices;
however, you can put weights on the sample paths to exactly recover them
(use the maximum entropy principle to have uniquely defined weights).
Those weighted paths can then be used 
to compute sensitivities (``Greeks'').

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Vibrato Monte Carlo\\ and the computation of Greeks}\\
  S. Keegan (2008)
\end{book}

Option sensitivities (``greeks'') can be computed via
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Finite differences 
  \[
    \dfrac{\partial \text{Price}}{\partial \theta} = 
    \dfrac{\text{Price}(\theta+\Delta\theta) - \text{Price}(\theta-\Delta\theta)}%
          {2\Delta\theta}
  \]
\item Likelihood ratio
  \begin{align*}
    \text{Price} &= E[ \text{Payoff} ] = \int \text{Payoff}(S) p(S)\, dS \\
    \dfrac{\partial \text{Price}}{\partial \theta} 
    &= \int \text{Payoff} \times \dfrac{\partial p}{\partial \theta} dS \\
    &= \int \text{Payoff} \times \dfrac{\partial \log p}{\partial \theta} p dS \\
    &= E \left[ \text{Payoff} \times \dfrac{\partial \log p}{\partial \theta} \right]
  \end{align*}
  (but the variance is too high);
\item Pathwise sensitivity
  \[
    \dfrac{\partial \text{Price}}{\partial \theta} = 
    E \left[
      \dfrac{\partial \text{Payoff}}{\partial \text{Spot}} 
      \dfrac{\partial \text{Spot}_T}{\partial \theta} 
    \right]
  \]
\item Adjoint method: discretize the PDE
  \begin{align*}
    S_{n+1} &= S_n + a(S_n,t_n) h + b(S_n,t_n) Z_{n+1} \\
            &= f_{n+1}(S_n,Z_{n+1}) \\
    S_N &= f_N \circ \cdots \circ f_1( S_0);
  \end{align*}
  we can compute 
  $\partial S_N / \partial \theta$ 
  from the $f_i$ and the $\partial f_i / \partial \theta$
  (not unlike the backpropagation algorithm, 
  to compute the gradient of the loss function in a neural network),
  for $(Z_n)_n$ fixed; and then integrate $Z$ out;
\item Conditional expectation:
  simulate the price until the penultimate time step $S_{T-\Delta T}$,
  consider the gaussian distribution 
  $S_T | S_{T-\Delta T} = S_T | Z_1,\dots,Z_{T-\Delta T}$,
  use the likelihood ratio method,
  and integrate $Z$ out
  \begin{align*}
    &\dfrac{\partial \text{Price}}{\partial \theta} = \\
    &\qquad
    E_{Z_1,\dots,Z_{T-\Delta T}} E_{Z_T} 
    \left[
      \text{Payoff}
      \dfrac{\partial \log p_{S_T | Z_1,\dots,Z_{T-\Delta T}} }{\partial\theta}
    \right]
  \end{align*}
\item The Vibrato method decomposes this expression further.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Smoking adjoints:
  fast Monte Carlo Greeks}
  % No author name on the first page (Risk, 2006)
\end{book}

More details on the adjoint method to compute Greeks.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Web search queries can predict\\ stock market volumes}\\
  I. Bordino et al. (2012)
\end{book}

It could be useful as a warning signal,
but they do not seem to check for confounding variables 
(e.g., the day of the week).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Pricing stocks with yardsticks and sentiments}\\
  S.M. Bustos et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Another variant of the CAPM:
\[
  E[ \text{return}_i ] = 
  \dfrac{ 
    \sigma(\text{return}_i) 
  }{ 
    \sigma(\text{market without }i) 
  }
  E[ \text{market without }i ]
\]
rather than
\[
  E[ \text{return}_i ] = 
  \dfrac{ 
    \cov(\text{return}_i,\text{market}) 
  }{ 
    \sigma^2(\text{market}) 
  }
  E[ \text{market} ].
\]

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Memory effects in stock price dynamics: evidence of technical trading}\\
  F. Garzarelli et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Technical trading is thought to be a self-fulfilling prophecy;
one can test how efficient it is as follows.
A ``support'' (``resistance'') is a local minimum (maximum) 
in a window of width $\tau$ ticks;
estimate the probability that prices rebound on
(rather than cross) the latest support or resistance 
if they enter a band of width $\epsilon$ around them; 
fine-tune $\tau$ and $\epsilon$ to maximize this probability
(and validate on a test dataset). 

\nextbook

% 2012-06
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% 
% 2012-05

\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving recommendation quality 
    by merging collaborative filtering and social relationships}\\
  P. De Meo et al. (2011)
\end{book}

The user rating matrix $R$ can be written as a product of
the user preference matrix, and the item characteristic matrix,
$R = PQ'$ (``non-negative matrix factorization'').
The singular value decomposition (SVD) provides such a decomposition, 
but does not deal well with missing values.
Tikhonov regularization can help:
\[ (P,Q) = \argmin \n{R-PQ'}^2 + \lambda( \n{P}^2 + \n{Q}^2 ). \]
You can add another penalty 
\[ 
  \mu 
  \sum_{x\ \text{user}} 
  \sum_{\substack{ y \text{ neighbour}\\ \text{of } x}}
  \n{ P_{x,\cdot} - P_{y,\cdot} }
\]
to account for social relationships.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-conservative diffusion and its application to social network analysis}\\
  R. Gosh et al. (2011)
\end{book}
\emph{PageRank} is the steady state probability distribution
of a random walk on a network.
The \emph{alpha-centrality} can be defined in the same way, 
by using a non-conservative diffusion:
the process does not move from one node to a single other node, 
but broadcasts to each neighbouring node,
as an epidemic or a fad would do
(the number of viruses or of infected nodes is not conserved) --
this is not unlike the difference between deterministic 
and non-deterministic finite automata.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Information filtering via preferential diffusion}\\
  L. L\"u and W. Liu (2011)
\end{book}

Most recommendation algorithms are based on similarities
between users or items or both.
One could also use a diffusion process on the user-object bipartite graph.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum entropy random walks 
    in complex networks with limited information}\\
  R. Sinatra et al. (2011)
\end{book}
You can construct a maximum entropy random walk on a graph using
only local information: 
make the transition probabilities 
proportional to some power 
of the degree of the target node.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{From brain to earth and climate systems: 
  small-world interaction networks or not}\\
  A. Bialonski et al. (2011)
\end{book}
The omnipresent ``small world property''
(the average shortest path length grows at most logarithmically 
with the number of nodes)
could be an artefact of the way we sample real-world networks.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Rewiring world trade I:\\ A binary network analysis}\\
  T. Squartini et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Local properties (node degree) 
are sufficient to describe the international trade network 
(ITN, \href{http://comtrade.un.org/}{world trade web}, WTW),
when viewed as a non-weighted network,
but not when viewed as a weighted 
(trade volume) network.
To see it, you can build a family of randomized variants,
by \d{local rewiring}
and look at the distribution of some global quantities, 
such as degree correlation or clustering coefficient.
There is a less computation-intensive method,
using the maximum entropy probability distribution 
on the set of graphs satisfying the degree constraints.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The network of global corporate control}\\
  S. Vitali et al. (2011)
\end{book}

The presence of cycles in the cross-holding network 
of transnational corporations complicates 
the estimation of the control structure (who controls who).
(It is not clear how they address the problem:
they seem to just mitigate it.)
The network has a bow tie structure:
financial companies (left half) 
control transnational companies (knot),
transnational companies have tight circular crossholding relations
and control smaller companies (right half).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Propagation of cascades in complex networks:
  from supply chains to food webs}\\
  R.D. Smith (2011)
\end{book}

Modelling the bullwhip effect, on a graph (supply chain, food web),
using birth-death processes.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The blogosphere as an excitable social medium:
  Richter's and Omori's law in media coverage}\\
  P. Klimek et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Word frequencies have statistical properties similar to earthquake energies, 
before and after an event.
\begin{align*}
  w_i &\approx (t-t_0) ^ {-\alpha} & \text{before} \\
  w_i &\approx (t_0-t) ^ {-\beta } & \text{after}
\end{align*}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{iSAX: indexing and mining\\ terabyte-sized time series}\\
  J. Shieh and E. Keogh
\end{book}
To index large databases of time series, 
store the time series in a tree,
whose nodes correspond to increasingly finer 
and/or more precise discretizations.
This is not unlike wavelet coefficients, 
R-trees (used to index spatial databases)
or $k$-d trees.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A decision-theoretic formulation\\ of Fisher's approach to testing}\\
  K. Rice (2010)
\end{book}
Statistical tests can be interpreted in a decision-theoretic framework:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item
  A frequentist test minimizes the type II error rate (``inaccuracy'')
  under a constraint on the type I error rate (``embarrassment'');
\item
  A bayesian test assigns a cost to type I and type II errors 
  and minimizes the expected cost.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A gentle introduction to quantile regression for ecologists}\\
  B.S. Cade and B.R. Noon (2003)
\end{book}
Quantile regression addresses (and measures) heteroskedasticity.
It can highlight changes in the distribution
that are significant, but not visible on the mean.
It allows you to focus on the tail, where interesting things happen.
In ecology, it can help identify limiting factors
(corresponding to distribution truncation:
\(
