\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Machine learning markets}\\
  A. Storkey (2011)
\end{book}
There are many ways of aggregating statistical models
(``ensemble methods'': boosting, mixtures, etc.).
Here is another one, inspired by financial markets.
Statistical models are agents in a market, 
each with a utility function,
trading ``bets'' (options on the next data point).
The price formation mechanism is similar to loopy belief propagation.
Linear, logarithmic, exponential utility functions
lead to the median, (weighted) mean or geometric mean
of the models.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Value-at-risk in portfolio optimization:
  properties and computational approach}\\
  A.A. Gaivoronski and G. Pflug (2004)
\end{book}

The value-at-risk (VaR), computed from a set of scenarios, 
is neither smooth nor convex -- there are local extrema everywhere.
Since the VaR can be expressed as 
\[
  \max_i {}^{k+1} f_i(x) \propto 
  \sum_{\substack{ I \subset \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket \\ \va I = k}}
  \sum_{i \in I} \mathbf{1} ^{} _{\!\!\substack{ 
      \{x \,:\,\forall i \in I \, f_i(x) \leq f_j(x) \\
      \hphantom{\{x \ :\ }\forall i \not\in I \, f_i(x) \geq f_j(x) \}
  }}
  f_i(x),
\]
one can replace 
\( \mathbf{1}_{\{ x \ : \ f_i(x) \leq f_j(x)  \}}  \)
with \( \phi_\epsilon( f_i(x) - f_j(x) ) \)
for some smooth $\phi_\epsilon$ approximating $1_{\{ x \leq 0\}}$.
The resulting smoothed VaR (SVaR)
is smooth and has fewer local extrema.
(The authors do not seem to be worried by the size of the sum.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Transaction costs, trading volume,\\and the liquidity premium}\\
  S. Gerhold (2011)
\end{book}

In presence of transaction costs, 
even if you want to hold a constant-weight portfolio,
you cannot rebalance it continuously.
This suboptimality corresponds to a liquidity premium:
\[ 
  \text{liquidity premium} \propto 
  \text{bid-ask spread} \times \text{share turnover}.
\]
(The liquidity premium is the difference in expected returns 
between a risky asset with transaction costs
and a risky asset with the same utility but no transaction costs.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic market efficiency}\\
  O. Peters and A. Adamou (2011)
\end{book}
In a stochastically efficient market,
it is not possible to beat the market
(in terms of expected growth rate)
by holding the market portfolio 
and playing with the leverage:
the optimal leverage, from the Kelly principle, is always 1.
This notion is different from that of price efficiency, 
which is static (at one point in time).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{How efficiency shapes market impact}\\
  J.D. Farmer et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Market impact is concave.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Record statistics for biased random walks,\\
  with an application to financial data}\\
  G. Wergen et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Asymptotic behaviour of the record rate
\( P_n = P[ X_n > \sup_{k<n} X_k ] \)
for a random walk with drift $X$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantitative Trading}\\
  E.P. Chan (2009)
\end{book}
Elementary and superficial book 
on how to become an independent quantitative trader --
in short: 
do not forget to backtest your strategies, 
with clean, survivor-bias-free data.
The book ends with a list of possible strategies
(earnings or macro announcements;
% PEAD: Post-earnings announcement drift
gold vs gold miners pairs trading;
Fama-French factor model;
calendar trades: January effect, gasoline May futures in April,
natural gas June futures from January to April)
and a frighteningly incorrect interpretation 
of the $p$-value of a cointegration test.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial Risk Forecasting}\\
   J. Dan\'{\i}elsson (Wiley 2011)
\end{book}
Clear book on the value at risk (VaR),
at a very elementary level.

\nextbook

% 2012-05
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% 
% 2012-04

\begin{book}
  \emph{Reconstruction of financial networks\\
    for robust estimation of systemic risk}\\
  I. Mastromatteo (2012)
\end{book}

The credit network between banks can be described by
a liability matrix, $L_{ij}$ indicating the funds lent
by bank~$j$ to bank~$i$.

Only some entries (those above a regulatory threshold,
reported to a regulator) and the aggregate values
(total debt and credit, from the balance sheet) are
known, and the matrix is often reconstructed using a
maximum entropy estimator, \ie,
by maximizing the Kullback-Leibler divergence 
between $L$ and a uniform distribution: 
$$ \sum_{i,j} L_{ij} \log \dfrac{L_{ij}}{Q} $$
But this assumes that the credit relations 
are as evenly spread out as possible:
the real credit network is more heterogeneous, 
the real liability matrix is sparse.

One can try to maximize the sparsity (number of zero
entries) under those constraints, and then compute the
corresponding maximum entropy matrix.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Mean-variance portfolio optimization\\
    when means and covariances are unknown}\\
  T.L. Lai et al. (2011)
\end{book}
Mean-variance portfolio optimization assumes that the
expected returns $\mu$ and variance matrix $V$ are
known: often, some estimators 
(sample estimators, factor models, shrinkage, Bayes,
Black-Litterman, etc.)
are simply plugged into the optimization problem.
This 2-step procedure (estimation, then optimization)
is suboptimal.
One can replace the optimization problem 
$$ \text{Maximize } E[w' r_{n+1}|\mu,V] - \lambda \var[w' r_{n+1}|\mu,V] $$
with a stochastic optimization problem 
$$ 
  \text{Maximize } 
  E[w' r_{n+1}|r_1,\dots,r_n] - 
  \lambda \var[w' r_{n+1}|r_1,\dots] 
$$
% TODO: Explain how to do this
The corresponding ``non-parametric empirical Bayes'' frontier
is higher than the shrinkage or bootstrap ones.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk minimization in stochastic volatility models:
  model risk and empirical performance}\\
  R. Poulsen et al. (2009)
\end{book}
In a complete market with a risk-free asset $B$ 
and a risky asset $S$,
any European option $X$ can be replicated 
with a self-financing strategy $(\alpha, \beta)$.
\begin{align*}
  X_t  &= \alpha_t B_t +  \beta_t S_t \\
  dX_t &= \alpha_t dB_t + \beta_t dS_t
\end{align*}
If the market is not complete 
(e.g., a stochastic volatility model),
you can still build a replicating strategy, 
but it will not be self-financing:
$$ \text{Cost}_t = \int dX_t - \int \alpha_t dB_t - \int \beta_t dB_t $$
is not constant.
Some people just use a strategy that would be self-financing
in a complete market, delta-hedging, and hope for the best.
Alternatively, one could try to minimize some measure of risk 
associated to this cost, e.g., 
$ 
  E[ ( 
    \text{Cost}_T - \text{Cost}_t
  )^2 | \mathscr F_t ]
$.
The problem need not have a global solution, 
but a local extremum is often sufficient.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A fuzzy pay-off method\\ for real option valuation}\\
  A. Collan et al. (2009)
\end{book}
A \emph{fuzzy subset} $A$ of $X$ is a map 
$\mu : X \to [0,1]$,
interpreted as $\mu(x) = P(x \in A)$
(it corresponds to a probability distribution on $\mathscr P(X)$
with the property that 
$ ( x \in A ) \indep ( y \in B ) $ whenever $x \neq y$).
For instance, $\mu(x) = \delta_{x, x_0}$ corresponds to a single point
and 
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline,scale=.3]
  \draw (-1,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw (0,-.3) -- (0,1);
  \draw[thick] (-1,0) -- (-.3,0) -- (0,.8) -- (.3,0) -- (1,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
or 
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline,scale=.3]
  \draw (-1,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw (0,-.3) -- (0,1);
  \draw[thick] (-1,0) -- (-.6,0) -- (-.3,.8) -- (.3,.8) -- (.6,0) -- (1,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
are \emph{fuzzy numbers}.
They can be used, instead of probability distributions, 
as the payoff of an investment,
when computing its value (via MCMC) 
as the risk-neutral expected present value.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The US stock market leads\\
    the federal funds rate and treasury bond yields}\\
  K. Guo et al. (2011)
\end{book}

To find which, of two time series $x$ and $y$, 
leads the other, find a path in their \d{recurrence plot}
(the matrix of squared distances $d_{s,t} = (x_t-y_s)^2$)
from one corner to the other,
that minimizes the sum of squared distances.
The algorithm can be made more robust to noise by replacing 
the dynamic programming equation 
$$ C_{s,t} = d_{s,t} + \min \{ C_{s-1,t}, C_{s-1,t-1}, C_{s,t-1} \} $$
with 
$$ G_{s,t} = ( G_{s-1,t} + G_{s-1,t-1} + G_{s,t-1} ) \exp( - d_{s,t} / T ) $$
leading to the ``thermal optimal path'' 
($T$ can be interpreted as a temperature).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Econophysics -- complex correlations 
    and trend switchings in financial time series}\\
  T. Preis (2011)
\end{book}

% Section 3, plot page 40
\d{Pattern conformity} measures how well we can predict 
the evolution of a time series for the next $\Delta t ^+$ instants
from the previous $\Delta t ^-$ instants,
using pattern matching on previous intervals of size 
$\Delta t ^-$ (the patterns are normalized with their range, 
to account for changes in volatility).
It can be normalized (in a complicated way) 
and plotted against
$\Delta t ^-$ and $\Delta t ^+$.
It is a non-linear measure of autocorrelation.

% Section 4, plot page 45
Pattern matching can also be used to study trends
and crashes,
on various time scales.
Consider the time series between two local extrema
(extrema on centered intervals of size $2\Delta t$),
reparametrize them so that the time varies between 0 and 1,
find matching time series, 
look at the volume or intertrade time 
time series for those matching price time series, 
and average them. 

% Section 5: order book model

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Switching processes in financial markets}
  T. Preis et al.(2011)
\end{book}
Shorter article on the same subject.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic generalized Hurst exponent\\
    as a tool to monitor unstable periods\\
    in financial time series}\\
  R. Morales et al. (2011)
\end{book}
``Scaling behaviour'' refers to the relation between
the volatility of the returns on some asset 
and the horizon~$\tau$ over which it is computed.
The Hurst exponent $H$ satisfies 
$$ \text{Volatility} \propto \text{Horizon} ^H. $$
More generally, one can look at other moments:
$$ q\text{th moment} \propto \text{Horizon} ^{q H(q)}. $$
If the \d{generalized Hurst exponent} $H(q)$ depends on $q$,
the times series is said to be \d{multifractal}.

The generalized Hurst exponent $H(1)$ 
can be computed on a moving window, 
with exponentially decreasing weights, 
and used as a bubble detector.

Similarly, one can study the tail of the returns distribution:
$$ 
  P[ \text{log-returns} \geq r ] 
  \underset{n \to\infty}{\propto} 
  r^{-\alpha}. 
$$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Is there a bubble on LinkedIn's stock price?}\\
  R. Jarrow et al. (2011)
\end{book}
One can apparently detect bubbles by checking
if the squared volatility increases more than linearly with the price:
$$ \int _a ^\infty \dfrac{\text{Price}}{\text{Volatility}^2} < \infty. $$
(If this condition is satisfied, 
the price process is only a local martingale under the risk-free measure.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{How to detect an asset bubble}\\
  R. Jarrow et al. (2011)
\end{book}
More details about the (kernel-based) variance estimators used.

\nextbook

%\begin{book}
%  \emph{About the non-random content\\ of financial markets}\\
%  L. Schoeffel (2011)
%\end{book}
%The distribution of the returns $x$ in financial time series 
%depends on the time horizon $\tau$:
%$$ f_\tau(x) \propto [ 1 - \beta_\tau ( x - \bar x _\tau )^2 ]_+ ^\alpha. $$
%\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Why is order flow so persistent?}\\
  B. T\'oth et al. (2011)
\end{book}
The \d{order flow} 
(signed traded volume, 
the sign indicating whether the trade 
was initiated by the seller or the buyer),
computed from transaction data, 
is persistent over several days,
and its autocorrelation can be decomposed into 
``order splitting'' and herding components
(you need to know which performed each transaction):
order-splitting dominates,
and anti-herding (from market-makers) 
becomes visible at longer time-scales.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Investment volatility: 
    a critique of standard beta estimation and a simple way forward}\\
  C. Tofallis
\end{book}
Should the usual beta 
\begin{align*}
  \beta &= \dfrac{\cov(\text{Portfolio},\text{Market})}{\var{\text{Market}}} \\
  &= 
  \cor(\text{Portfolio},\text{Market})
  \times
  \dfrac{\sd(\text{Portfolio})}{\sd{\text{Market}}}
\end{align*}
be replaced by the simpler and more stable 
$$
  \text{sign}\bigl(\cor(\text{Portfolio},\text{Market})\bigr)
  \times
  \dfrac{\sd(\text{Portfolio})}{\sd{\text{Market}}}
  ?
$$
It corresponds to the slope of a line between the 
portfolio-vs-market and market-vs-portfolio 
regression lines,
and minimizes the sum of the products of the deviations
in the horizontal and vertical directions
(triangle areas).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Hedging the smirk}\\
  D.S. Bates (2005)
\end{book}
Some of the greeks ($\Delta$, $\Gamma$) can be computed 
without any model, 
only relying on the fact that option prices are homogeneous
(of degree 1) in the underlying and the strike
(actually, some models do not satisfy this property):
indeed, from Euler's theorem,
$$ 
\text{Price} = 
\text{Spot} \dfrac{\partial \text{Price}}{\partial \text{Spot}} +
\text{Strike} \dfrac{\partial \text{Price}}{\partial \text{Strike}}.
$$
(For $\Gamma = \partial^2 \text{Price} / \partial \text{Spot}^2 $,
use the fact that 
$ \partial \text{Price} / \partial \text{Spot} $
and 
$ \partial \text{Price} / \partial \text{Strike} $
are themselves homogeneous.)

Since the Black-Scholes implied volatility
fluctuates much less than 
the option prices (which move with the underlying),
it can be used instead of the price, from 
\begin{align*}
  \text{Price} &= \text{BS}( \text{Strike}, \text{Volatility} ) \\
  \dfrac{ \partial \text{Price} }{ \partial \text{Strike} } &=
  \dfrac{\partial \text{BS}}{\partial \text{Strike}}
  +
  \dfrac{\partial \text{BS} }{\partial \text{Volatility} }
  \dfrac{\partial \text{Volatility} }{\partial \text{Strike} }
\end{align*}
% Use a smoothed implied volatility to compute the derivatives

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the assessment of Monte Carlo error\\
    in simulation-based statistical analyses}\\
  E. Koehler et al. (2009)
\end{book}
Monte Carlo simulations provide noisy estimators,
but many studies do not report the error 
coming from the finiteness of the sample --
and many use a frighteningly small number of replications.
To estimate this Monte Carlo error (MCE),
plot the estimate versus the number of samples
(ideally for several chains);
bootstrap the Monte Carlo samples you have 
(bootstrap after bootstrap);
you can also use the fact that 
$ \text{MCE} \propto R^{-1/2} $
to justify the number of replications you chose.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Factor analysis for multiple testing (FAMT): 
  an R package for large-scale significance testing under dependence}\\
  D. Causeur et al. (JSS 2011)
\end{book}
Knowledge of the dependency structure of the data 
can help improve the correction for multiple testing (FDR, etc.), 
which usually assumes the tests are independent.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{topicmodels: an R package for fitting models}\\
  B. Gr\"un and K. Hornik (JSS 2011)
\end{book}
Latent Dirichlet allocation (LDA)
models the occurrence of words as follows: 
for each topic, 
choose a word distribution (Dirichlet prior -- 
the Dirichlet distribution is a distribution on 
$[ \sum_i x_i = 1,\ \forall i \ x_i \geq 1 ]$);
for each text, choose the distribution 
of the topics present in the text (another Dirichlet);
to generate each word, select a topic from the topic distribution, 
and select a word from the word distribution of that topic.
The correlated topic model (CTM) also allows for dependence between topics.
Fit the model with Gibbs simulations 
or variational expectation maximization (VEM).
In R, check the \texttt{lda} and \texttt{topicmodels} packages
(Jags is more general, but slower).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Consistently weighted measures\\
    for complex network topologies}\\
  J. Heitzig et al. (2011)
\end{book}
One often considers small finite ``aggregated''
approximations of large or infinite graphs,
for instance, 
a finite mesh for the brain,
finite approximations of climate networks
whose vertices are points on the Earth
and with an edge between two points 
if the the time series of (say) temperatures at those points 
are significantly correlated,
network of IP address ranges when we are really interested in users,
network of households to study people,
of countries instead of consumers or companies, etc.
Those aggregations or discretizations can be biased:
for instance, 
a latitude-longitude mesh of the Earth gives undue importance
to the poles. 
Graph-theoretic statistics 
(centrality, betweeness, etc.) have to be corrected for this bias:
one can usually define weighted variants of those statistics 
by looking at how they change under node splitting.
\[
\raisebox{-.5\height+.5ex}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.3]
  \tikzstyle{node}=[shape=ellipse,draw=black,thick]
  %\tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node (A) at (-1,0) [node] {};
  \node (B) at  (0,0) [node,fill=black!20] {};
  \node (C) at  (1,0) [node] {};
  \draw (A) -- (B) -- (C);
  \draw (A) -- (-2,-1);
  \draw (A) -- (-2,0);
  \draw (A) -- (-2,1);
  \draw (C) -- (2,-1);
  \draw (C) -- (2,0);
  \draw (C) -- (2,1);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}
\longrightarrow
\raisebox{-.5\height+.5ex}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.3]
