The Sharpe ratio of an asset or portfolio is the slope 
of the line going through it and the risk-free asset: 
$( EX - r ) / \sigma(X)$. 
You should maximize your Sharpe ratio.
A stock is worth more if it increases the Sharpe ratio.
This can be quantified by the 
\d{covariance pricing theorem}:
$$ 
  \text{Price} = 
  (1+r)^{-1} EX - \alpha \cov(\text{Market}, X),
$$
\ie, the risk is \emph{not} measured by the variance, 
but by the covariance.
The price is determined by the marginal utility, 
by the utility of a marginal buyer, 
by how much diversification the asset adds
to the market portfolio -- not by something intrinsic.
(This still assumes quadratic utility.)
This can be somewhat unintuitive.
For instance, if GE and a startup 
have the same expected returns, 
which has the highest price?
You might think it is GE, 
because it has a lower risk, 
as measured by the variance.
It is actually the startup,
because it has a lower risk, 
as measured by the correlation with the market.

In particular, $(EX, \cov(\text{Market},X))$ 
should be on a line.

