In dimension 1, a low discrepancy sequence (van der Corput)
  can be constructed as follows:
  \begin{compactitem}[--]%[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Write increasing integers (0, 1, 2, 3, etc.) in base $b$ 
    (0, 1, 10, 11, 100, etc.);
  \item Write the digits in reverse order and 
    add a decimal point in front of them 
    (0, .1, .01, .11, .001, etc; \ie,
    0, $\tfrac12$, $\tfrac14$, $\tfrac34$, $\tfrac18$, etc.).
  \end{compactitem}
This is not unlike the Farey sequence 
(0, 1, 
$\tfrac12$, 
$\tfrac14$, 
$\tfrac34$, 
$\tfrac15$, 
$\tfrac25$, 
$\tfrac35$, 
$\tfrac45$, 
$\tfrac17$,
etc.), in number theory
Sobol sequences generalize this idea 
in higher dimensions. % Much more complicated
To estimate the error of a quasi Monte Carlo computation
using statistical methods, 
you may want to scramble the sequence first
(to make it random).

% 17
The discrete maximum of an (arithmetic) Brownian motion
(used by look-back options)
can be computed using the $z$-transform.

% Chapters 18, 19, 20
Agent-based models can be used to describe 
\d{electricity prices} -- 
the stylized facts are different from those of stock prices:
seasonality, spikes, strong mean reversion, 
time-dependent volatility.
The times series models used to price options on energy prices 
(these are real options: 
the optionality is the ability to 
switch the plant or generator on)
have to account for those stylized facts.
Swing contracts (a kind of American option, 
where you can buy a commodity at a given price,
in an arbitrary quantity, every day in the year) 
can be priced with dynamic programming.

% 21
Mortgage prepayment is important to value MBS 
  (mortgage-backed securities): borrowers with an improving 
  credit rating are more likely to pre-pay their debt earlier
  to switch to lower rates -- leaving only bad borrowers.
  Econometric forecast of the prepayment, using historical data,
  does not perform well, but the behaviour of the borrower,
  choosing to switch to new rates or not, can be modelled 
  as an American option, and the optimal prepayment strategy 
  can be computed via dynamic programming.

% 22
Structural models of default,
  used to price CDS (credit default swaps),
  compare the enterprise value 
  (a geometric Brownian motion or some other stochastic process)
  with the debt of the company
  $$ \tau = \inf \{ t \ :\ 
    \text{Enterprise Value}_t \leq \text{Debt} 
  \}. 
  $$
  Reduced form models of default 
  directly model the default time,
  with an intensity process.
  For basket default swaps, 
  one usually mixes those default times 
  with a 1-factor Gaussian copula
  (the factor can be interpreted (chosen?) 
  as the business cycle, some stock index, interest rates, etc.).

%23
Monte Carlo simulations, in high dimensions, 
  can be sped up and simplified as follows:
  \begin{compactitem}[--]%[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Perform a principal component analysis (PCA) 
    on the data to simulate, to reduce  the dimension;
  \item Replace the continuous distribution 
    (of the factors retained from the PCA) 
    with a small number (10 to 2, depending on the importance 
    of the factor) of points or ``scenarios'';
  \item Sample from the resulting (discrete) distribution,
  \end{compactitem}

% 24
To estimate the parameters 
  of a diffusion (or a jump-diffusion)
  via maximum likelihood, you need the transition probabilities.
  They can be estimated via Monte Carlo simulation:
  just split the intervals between two observations
  into smaller intervals, and simulate the diffusion 
  using the Euler scheme. (Simulated maximum likelihood, SML)

% 25 
The drift and diffusion coefficients 
  of a diffusion process
  are its instantaneous first moments -- 
  higher moments are zero.
  Jump diffusion processes produce 
  non-zero higher instantaneous moments.
  \begin{align*}
    dX &= \mu(X) dt + \sigma(X) dW + dJ \\
    \mu(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} \dfrac1h 
      E[X_{t+h} - X_t | X_t = x] \\
    \sigma^2(x) + \lambda(x) E[Y^2] &= 
      \lim_{h \to 0} \dfrac1h 
      E[(X_{t+h} - X_t)^2 | X_t = x] \\
    \lambda(x) E[Y^j] &= 
      \lim_{h \to 0} \dfrac1h 
      E[(X_{t+h} - X_t)^j | X_t = x] 
  \end{align*}
  Non-parametric (kernel) estimators of those moments
  give non-parametric estimates of 
  drift $\mu$, 
  diffusion $\sigma$,
  jump intensity $\lambda$ 
  and jump distribution $Y$
  (you have to make some more assumptions 
  on the distribution of jumps, e.g, Gaussian with zero mean).

% 26
GARCH models (and their generalizations)
  can be estimated from the volatility surface, 
  by considering it as a moment 
  (in the generalized method of moments, GMM).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The accountant's guide to the universe\\
  Heaven and hell by the numbers}\\
  C. Hovey (2010)
\end{book}

Could accounting be made more interesting 
by applying it to non-conventional enterprises, 
such as crime, 
or deciding whether one should go to heaven or hell?
The book presents several economic and accounting notions 
(bounded rationality, free markets, creative destruction,
present value,
double-entry book-keeping, balance sheet, income statement)
for those two examples.
The result is pleasantly funny, 
but you will not learn anything:
the notions of credit and debit are not explained
(I vaguely remember from my accounting courses that ``debit''
means the opposite of what it means for my bank account -- 
at least, half the time),
some of the numeric examples are wrong 
(the publisher dropped some of the credit or debit columns)
or have to be accepted on faith (present value computations),
and the reluctance to use negative numbers complicates things
(you never know in which direction the money is going).

Computer scientists will prefer 
M. Kleppmann's 
\href{http://martin.kleppmann.com/2011/03/07/accounting-for-computer-scientists.html}%
{graph-theoretic explanations}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantifying and modeling\\
  long-range cross-correlations\\
  in multiple time series\\
  with applications to world stock indices}\\
  D. Wang et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Random matrix theory (RMT, 
comparing the distribution 
of the eigenvalues of a sample correlation matrix 
with the distribution coming from 
the sample correlation matrix of iid gaussian variables)
can also be applied to cross-correlation.
In a financial context, you should look at both 
the correlation of returns and absolute returns -- 
the latter decay more slowly.
Using a 1-factor (sic) model, 
the authors find 10 uncorrelated indices 
that may form a low-risk portfolio.
(Other methods could be used, e.g., 
detrended cross-correlation analysis, DCCA).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Predicted and verified\\
  deviations from Zipf's law\\
  in ecology of competing products}\\
  R. Hisano et al. (2011)
\end{book}

Zipf's law (stable distribution with power law tails)
appears in the following situation:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A finite population of ``entities'' $I_t$ changes with time,
  with entities entering or leaving the universe 
  according to a Poisson process;
\item Each entity $i \in I_t$ is described 
  by a geometric Brownian motion $S_i(t)$,
  starting with some random value at time $t_i$
  (when $i$ enters $I$);
\item Then, for $t \gg 1$ fixed (and $\va{I_t} \gg 1$), 
  the distribution of the $S_i(t)$, $i \in I_t$,
  has power law tails .
  % under some reasonable assumptions.
\end{compactitem}

% plot( drift ~ diffusion )

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Utility theory front to back\\
  inferring utility from agents' choices}\\
  A.M.G. Cox et al.
\end{book}

The optimal investment problem
often assumes that the utility function is given,
but determining which utility function 
faithfully describes the preferences of a given investor 
is tricky, and usually done by looking at her choices
when faced with 1-period investment problems.
This article tries to infer the utility function 
from the actual (observed) 
consumptions choices in a continuous-time framework
(deterministic or stochastic (Black-Scholes)).
Not all consumption choices come from a utility function
(they have to satisfy some PDE),
and the utility function 
(or, even, the sign of the relative risk aversion) 
is not entirely determined by the consumption choices.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Topological isomorphism\\ of human brain 
  and financial market networks}\\
  P.E. V\'ertes et al. (2011)
\end{book}

The growing graph, minimum spanning tree
and the various graph-theoretical measures 
(modularity, etc.)
of (the variance matrix of) fMRI and financial data
are very similar.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The common component\\ of idiosyncratic volatility}\\
  J. Duarte et al. (2011)
\end{book}

One more factor should be added to the Fama-French model
$$ 
  \text{returns} \sim 
  \text{market} + \text{size} + \text{book-to-market},
$$
corresponding to the ``idiosyncratic volatility''
(the first principal component of
the variance matrix of the residual return --
it is always a good idea to add statistical factors 
to your risk model, to catch anything you may have overlooked, 
or anything that may have changed);
it can be interpreted as the position in the business cycle.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{An empirical analysis\\ of dynamic multiscale hedging\\
  using wavelet decomposition}\\
  T. Conlon and J. Cotter (2011)
\end{book}

% Rather empty

You can assess the influence of the investment horizon
on the minimum variance portfolio 
(or mean-variance, or mean-Gini --
\d{Gini's mean difference} 
is $E[\tfrac12 \va{ X - Y }] = 2 \cov(X,F_X(X))$,
where $X \indep Y$ are distributed as the portfolio returns)
by looking at the wavelet transforms of the returns time series
and expressing the variance matrix, the portfolio weights,
the higher moments of the portfolio, etc.,
as functions the the wavelet coefficients
(maximum overlap discrete wavelet transform).
Only the ``optimal hedge ratio'' case, \ie, 
the 2-asset case, is treated
(one asset is the portfolio you hold and want to hedge,
its weight is imposed to be 1,
the other is the hedge portfolio, already computed,
whose weight has to be determined).



\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Anchor modeling}\\
  L. R\"onnb\"ack et al. (2010)
\end{book}

Anchor modeling is a rebranding of entity-relation (ER) modeling,
that describes a database schema in terms of 
actors (entities), attributes (historicized or not),
knots (dimension tables, \ie, attributes with a small or fixed 
number of values),
ties (relations)
and, more importantly,
advocates that changes to the database 
be implemented as extensions 
rather than modifications -- 
this ensures backward compatibility.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial models with Levy processes\\
  and volatility clustering}\\
  S.T. Rachev et al. (2011)  
\end{book}

The main claim of the book is that 
non-gaussian distributions 
should and can be used in practice, 
to model stock prices, to price options, 
to optimize portfolios -- 
even if those distributions 
do not have an easy-to-use probability distribution function,
their characteristic function is simple,
and sufficient for all practical purposes -- 
at worst, you have to compute one or two integrals, 
via the FFT (Fast Fourier Transform), quadratures, 
or Monte Carlo simulations).

(Beware: the book has apparently not been proof-read 
and contains innumerable incorrect formulas;
many explanations are also seriously lacking -- 
the original articles are often clearer.)


% Distributions

The gamma distribution $\Gamma(c,\lambda)$ 
is the law of $Y_1+\cdots+Y_c$ 
where $Y_i \sim \textrm{Exp}(\lambda)$ iid.
The \d{variance gamma} distribution 
$\text{VG}(c,\lambda_+,\lambda_-)$ 
is the law of $G_+ - G_-$ 
where $G_\pm \sim \Gamma(c,\lambda_\pm)$.
The \d{inverse Gaussian} distribution 
is the law of the first time at which 
a random walk reaches a given value 
$ \tau = \min \{ t \ :\ X_t = m \}. $

\d{Stable} (or $\alpha$-stable) distributions 
can be defined by (and used through) 
their characteristic function.
One can define many variants of the stable distributions, 
e.g., by cutting the tails and replacing them 
with Gaussian tails (truncated stable distributions) 
or by tweaking the characteristic function 
(tempered stable distributions)
or (equivalently) by multiplying the L\'evy measure 
of the $\alpha$-stable distribution 
($\alpha$-stable distributions are infinitely divisible, 
and can therefore be expressed 
using the L\'evy-Khinchine formula)
by some tempering function (e.g., $e^{-t}$).
% Those notions are introduced with no insight whatsoever

(Tempered) $\alpha$-stable distributions can be fitted
either by fitting their characteristic function, 
or via maximum likelihood, using a numerical approximation of their density function.

% Stochastic Processes

A \d{compound Poisson process} is a process of the form 
$ X_t = \sum_{k=1}^{N_t} Y_k $,
where the $Y_k$ are iid (arbitrary distribution)
and $N_t \sim \textrm{Pois}(t\lambda)$ is a Poisson process.
A \d{pure jump process} is a limit of compound Poisson processes:
let $S \subset \RR$ be a countable set (jump sizes),
$\lambda: S \rightarrow \RR_+$ some function, 
$N_t^{\lambda(s)}$ Poisson processes of intensity $\lambda(s)$,
and $Y_t = \gamma t + \sum_{s \in S} s N_t^{\lambda(s)}. $
In the expression of the characteristic function of $Y_t$, 
one can replace the measure
$$ \nu(A) = \sum_{s \in S \inter A} \lambda(s) $$
(expected number of jumps of size in $A$ per unit of time)
with a more general measure --
% (you may need: $\int_{-infty}^\infty \min(1, x^2) \nu(dx) < \infty$)
it is the L\'evy measure of the pure jump process.

A non-Gaussian infinitely divisible random variable $X_1$
(e.g., gamma, $\alpha$-stable, inverse gaussian, variance gamma)
defines a pure jump process.
% Dubious: what if you add a Gaussian to such an $X_1$?

Non-decreasing pure jump processes 
(gamma, inverse gaussian, Poisson)
% The book also adds stable distributions with $\alpha \in [0,1]$
can be interpreted as a ``cumulated trading activity'' 
and used to define time-changed Brownian motions:
the inverse gaussian gives the \d{normal inverse gaussian} (NIG);
the gamma distribution gives the \d{variance gamma} process.

A \d{L\'evy process} is a process with 
independent stationary increments,
c\`adl\`ag and stochastically continuous
$$ 
  \forall t \qquad
  \forall \epsilon > 0 \qquad
  \lim _{s \to t} P\bigl[ \va{ X_s - X_t } \bigr] = 0.
$$
It is the sum of a Brownian motion and a pure jump process.
An exponential L\'evy process is the exponential of a L\'evy process.

It is possible to simulate a random variable from its characteristic function
(if the tails are not too fat),
using 
\begin{align*}
  f(x) &\leq \dfrac1{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \va{ \phi } \\
  f(x) &\leq \dfrac1{2\pi x^2} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \va{ \phi'' },
\end{align*}
by rejection -- it is easy to sample from 
$$ g(x) \propto \min\left( 
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \va{ \phi },
\dfrac1{x^2} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \va{ \phi'' }
\right). $$
L\'evy processes can be simulated,
by writing them as 
$$ X_t = Y_t^\epsilon + Z_t^\epsilon, $$
where $Z_t^\epsilon$ is 
the compound Poisson process containing 
the jumps of size larger than $\epsilon$,
and $Y_t^\epsilon$ is either neglected 
or replaced by an easy-to-simulate process (e.g., a Brownian motion).
There are ad hoc algorithms to simulate from the tempered $\alpha$-stable distributions 
and a few special cases (NIG, VG, etc.).

The Girsanov theorem links 
the (known) real-world probability $\mathbb{P}$ 
to the risk-neutral one $\QQ$
(needed to compute option prices).

% Dependency

For elliptical distributions, 
$$ f(x) \propto g( (x-\mu)' \Sigma^{-1} (x-\mu) ), $$
the correlation completely describes the dependence.
Elliptical distributions can account for the fat tails and tail-dependence in asset returns but,
because of radial symmetry, 
not for the larger dependence in the lower tail.
Elliptic distributions can be written as 
$$ X = \mu + R A S $$
where $S \sim U(\SS^{d-1})$,
$R$ is a random variable in $\RR^d$
and $A$ is a $d\times d$ matrix,
with $R \indep AS$. 
Instead, one can let the distribution of $R$ depend on the direction $AS$:
let $s: \RR^d \rightarrow [0,1]$
be a function, measuring the tail dependence in a given direction,
$(R_t)_{t \in [0,1]}$ a family of positive random variables,
and consider the \d{multi-tail generalized elliptic distribution}
$$ X = \mu + R_{s(AS)} AS. $$
The tail function $s$ could come, for instance, 
from the first principal component $v_1$ of $\Sigma=AA'$,
$$ s(x) = 
\alpha_+ \mathbf{1}_{ \langle x, v_1 \rangle > 0 }(x) +
\alpha_- \mathbf{1}_{ \langle x, v_1 \rangle < 0 }(x) 
.$$

% Portfolio optimization

Portfolios of non-gaussian assets can be optimized 
with a scenario-based approach
(fit the data, simulate returns, optimize of those scenarios),
which also allows for more flexibility the measure of risk.

% Option Pricing

Option prices can be computed 
for tempered stable processes,
but some integrals remain -- 
there are convergence problems 
with (non-tempered) stable processes:
that is why \d{tempered} stable distributions were introduced.

Options can also be priced with GARCH models
(e.g., with truncated stable innovations),
in discrete time, but more technical complications emerge 
(non-unique risk-free measure, etc.).
GARCH parameters, when used for option pricing, 
are usually estimated from option prices, 
completely ignoring the underlier --
models with non-gaussian residuals may be able to reconcile the underlier and the options.

The following pictures explain why 
early exercise of an American call option 
is never optimal 
(it is always more profitable 
to sell the American option as a European one
than to exercise it)
and why 
early exercise of an American put option 
is sometimes optimal 
(if it were not, 
the price would be the same as that of a European put,
but that is not possible because,
for a deep-in-the-money option, 
it is more profitable to exercise it immediately
rather than sell it at the same price as a European option --
however, this does not tell you 
\emph{when} to exercise the option).

\begin{center}
  \setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm}%
<<AmericanCallAndPut,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=8,fig.height=3,out.width="8cm">>=

blackscholes <- function(S, X, rf, T, sigma) {
    d1 <- (log(S/X)+(rf+sigma^2/2)*T)/sigma*sqrt(T)
    d2 <- d1 - sigma * sqrt(T)
    cbind(
      call = S*pnorm(d1) - X*exp(-rf*T)*pnorm(d2),
      put =  X*exp(-rf*T) * pnorm(-d2) - S*pnorm(-d1)
   )
}
sigma <- .03
x <- seq(95,105, length=101)

par(mfrow=c(1,2),mar=c(1,.5,1,.5))
par(xpd=FALSE)
plot(x, blackscholes(x,100,.01,1,sigma)[,1], 
  ylim=c(-.2,4),
  type="l", col="green3", lwd=3, 
  axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="")
abline(h=0, lty=3)
lines(x, pmax(0,x-100), col="blue", lwd=3)
lines(x,pmax(0,x-exp(-.01)*100))
legend("topleft", 
  c("European Call", "Immediate exercise"), 
  lty=1, lwd=3, col=c("green3","blue"),
  box.col="white")
par(xpd=NA)
text(100, -.1, "Strike", adj=c(0,1))
text(exp(-.01)*100, -.1, "Discounted strike", adj=c(1,1))
points(c(1,exp(-.01))*100,c(0,0),pch=16)

x <- seq(95,105, length=101)
par(xpd=FALSE)
plot(x, blackscholes(x,100,.01,1,sigma)[,2], 
  ylim=c(-.2,4),
  type="l", col="green3", lwd=3, 
  axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="")
abline(h=0, lty=3)
lines(x, pmax(0,100-x), col="blue", lwd=3)
lines(x, pmax(0,exp(-.01)*100-x))
legend("topright", 
  c("European Put", "Immediate exercise"), 
  lty=1, lwd=3, 
  col=c("green3","blue"),
  box.col="white")
#axis(1,
#  at=c(exp(-.01)*100, 100), 
#  labels=c("discounted\nstrike", "strike"))

par(xpd=NA)
text(100, -.1, "Strike", adj=c(0,1))
text(exp(-.01)*100, -.1, "Discounted strike", adj=c(1,1))
points(c(1,exp(-.01))*100,c(0,0),pch=16)
@
\end{center}

