\nextbook

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

% 2010-09

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiple testing corrections}\\
  Silicon Genetics (2003)
\end{book}

When performing multiple statistical tests (often, tens of thousands), 
$p$-values have to be corrected, 
for instance with the following methods: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bonferroni correction: $p^* = \min( 1, np )$;
\item Bonferroni step-down (Holm) correction, 
  $$p^* = \min\bigl( (n-k+1)p, 1 \bigr),$$
  where $k$ is the rank of the $p$-value (sort them in ascending order);
\item Westfall and Young correction: resample the data to have an
  estimate of the distribution of the sorted $p$-values under the null
  hypothesis, and consider the sorted $p$-values significant as long
  as they are under their resampled estimates;
\item Benjamini and Hochberg correction: 
  $$ p^* = \dfrac{ n }{ n - k } p. $$
\end{compactitem}
The first 3 methods control the familywise error rate (FWER), 
while the last controls the false discovery rate (FDR).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum entropy distribution inferred\\ from option portfolios on an asset}\\
  C. Neri and L. Schneider (2009)
\end{book}

Maximum entropy estimators make no distributional assumptions: 
given a set of option prices for the same underlier and maturity, 
one can compute the distribution of prices at expiry 
that maximizes entropy and agrees with the option prices. 
The article provides detailed computations for calls and digitals,
with theoretical justifications.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Social effects in science: modelling agents\\ 
    for a better scientific practice}\\
  A.C.R. Martin (2009)
\end{book}

Scientific discovery can be seen as an \emph{opinion dynamics} model
on a social network, endowed with two error-correcting mechanisms:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Experiments, \ie, interaction with nature, seen as an external field;
\item Retirement of old scientists.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The bivariate normal copula}\\
  C. Meyer (2009)
\end{book}

20 pages of formulas.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation of the instantaneous volatility}\\
  A. Alvarez et al. (2010)
\end{book}

Study of the speed of the convergence of 
$$ \epsilon^{ 1 - p/2} \sum_i \va{ X_{i\epsilon} - X_{(i-1)\epsilon} }^p 
\longrightarrow
m_p \int_0^t \sigma_s^p \, ds
$$
where $dX_t = a_t dt + \sigma_t dW_t$ 
is a stochastic volatility model ($\sigma_t$ is a c\`adl\`ag martingale)
and $m_p$ a constant,
$$ m_p = E \va{Z}^p, \qquad Z \sim N(0,1). $$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Most efficient homogeneous\\ volatility estimator}\\
  A. Saichev et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Common estimators of volatility based on OHLC prices, such as 
\begin{align*}
  \hat \sigma_{RS} &= \sqrt{\dfrac{(H-O)(H-C)+(L-O)(L-C)}{T}} \\
  \hat \sigma_{GK} &= \sqrt{\dfrac{
  k_1 (H-L)^2 
  - k_2( (C-O)(H+L)-2(H-O)(L-O) ) 
  - k_3 (C-O)^2
}{T}}
\end{align*}
(for some magic values of $k_1$, $k_2$, $k_3$)
are not the most efficient.
This article studies quadratic estimators (\ie, quadratic forms on
$H-O$, $L-O$, $C-O$) or more generally homogeneous estimators, and
identifies the best one, depending on the model describing the
underlying stochastic process.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Impact of the tick size\\ on financial returns and correlations}\\
  M.C. M\"unnix et al.
  (2010)
\end{book}

The distribution of stock returns (e.g., tail behaviour), the
correlation between stock returns, are known to depend on the horizon
in a non-obvious way.  This could be due to rounding, \ie, to the tick
size (either imposed by the exchange, or through psychological reasons
leading to clusters at some multiples of the tick size -- the
\emph{effetive tick size}) and can be compendated for.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A new composite index\\ of coincident economic indicators in Japan}\\
  S. Fukuda and T. Onodera (2001)
\end{book}

Coincident indicators (that give the state of the economy, \ie, the
position in the business cycle) such as industrial production, sales,
employment, income (income is used in the US but not in Japan) are
naively combined (average growth rate) by the Japanese Economic
Planning Agency to produce a ``composite index (CI) of coincident
indicators''.
It is biased towards industrial production, can be inconsistent with
leading indicators (that try to predict the state of the economy in
the coming year) or even the economic situation.
It can be replaced by a less naive model, assuming that all those
indicators come from a single hidden factor, that can be estimated
with a Kalman filter (Stock-Watson's ``single index dynamic factor
model'').

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiscaled cross-correlation dynamics\\ in financial time series}\\
  T. Colon et al. (2010)
\end{book}

Correlation matrices of stock returns have been studied in the
following situations:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Whole spectrum of the sample correlation matrix, estimated on a
  fixed period of time;
\item Evolution, with time, of the largest eigenvalue of the
  correlation matrix estimated on a moving window of fixed size.
\end{compactitem}
This article suggests to:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Look at the whole spectrum, not just the first eigen value;
\item Look at the dependence on both window position and window size,
  as in wavelet analysis, or look at the matrix of \textbf{wavelet
    correlations} instead: the correlation between the wavelet
  coefficients at a given location and scale.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The effect of discrete vs continuous-valued ratings on reputation and ranking algorithms}\\
  M. Medo and J.R. Wakeling (2010)
\end{book}

Iterative refinements of user and object reputation (\`a la PageRank)
give more accurate ratings than naive averages, but restricting the
ratings to a discrete set of values (e.g., a 5-star system)
significantly degrades the performance of the algorithm.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Using Permia and Nsp for constructing\\ a risk management benchmark\\ for testing parallel architecture}\\
  J.-P. Chancelier et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Premia is a non-free library developed by the Inria to price
derivatives.  Nsp is a free Matlab-like language developed by the
\'Ecole des Ponts, not unlike Octave, Scilab or R.  They also use MPI
for parallelism.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Recurrence networks -- a novel paradigm\\ for non-linear time series analysis}\\
  R.V. Donner et al. (2009)
\end{book}

There are many ways of constructing a graph (or network) from a time
series (or a (single) trajectory of a dynamical system):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \textbf{recurrence network}, whose adjacency matrix is given
  by the recurrence plot,
  $$ R_{ij}(\epsilon) = \mathbf{1}_{ \n{ x_i - x_j } \leq \epsilon} $$
  (you may want to remove self-loops; as you change $\epsilon$, you
  actually have a family of networks);
\item The visibility graph (whose vertices are points in time, and
  there is an edge between $t_1$ and $t_2$ if the $[x_{t_1}, x_{t_2}]$
  segment remains abive the curve);
\item The transition matrix (a weighted graph), whose vertices are
  discretized values of the time series, and whose weights are the
  transition probabilities;
\item The correlation network (whose vertices are segments $[t,t+T]$,
  and whose edge weights are $\cor( x_{t_1, t_1+T}, x_{t_2, t_2+T} )$.
\end{compactitem}
Some properties of the time series may be visible on the network (for
instance, the recurrence plot of an ergodic time series represents the
invariant density, in the phase space), and some graph-theoretic
metrics may be infomative:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Degree (number of neighbours);
\item Edge density (average degree);
\item Local clustering coefficient (testing if there are more
  triangles around a vertex than in a random network);
\item Average clustering coefficient;
\item Local degree anomaly (difference between the degree of a node
  and the average degree of its neighbours);
\item Assortativity (measuring if links tend to form between vertices
  of similar or dissimilar degree -- this is similar to the local
  degree anomaly, but based on edges rather than vertices);
\item Matching index (how many neighbours two vertices have in
  common);
\item The matrix of shortest path distances; the average path length;
  the network diameter;
\item Closeness centrality of a vertex (inverse of the average
  distance to other vertices);
\item Betweenness centrality of a vertex (on how many shortest paths it is);
\item Edge betweenness (idem, with an edge).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial crises and the evaporation of trust}\\
  A. Anand (2009)
\end{book}

Financial crises can be modeled with a \emph{credit network}: a
dynamic network, whose nodes represent lenders and whose vertices
represent loans.
From time to time, information about a node (bank) is revealed
(leaked) and other lenders may then decide (in a game-theoretic way)
to foreclose their positions with it.
In this model, the only cause of default is the breakdown of trust.
The opacity of the network increases the risk of a bank run: it makes
it easier to fall into an ``equilibrium'' leading to a bank run than
to leave it or to fall into an ``equilibrium'' that does not lead to a
bank run (there are many ``equilibria'', most of which break down when
information is leaked).

Crises are a financial example of \textbf{hysteresis} -- trust is easy
to lose, but hard to restore.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal split of orders across liquidity pools: a stochastic algorithm approach}\\
  S. Laruelle et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Application of \textbf{stochastic approximation} (a continuous-time
analogue of reinforcement learning -- used here in a discrete setting)
to splitting orders across several dark pools -- the algorithm
progressively learns which pool is the best, in terms of volume and
price.  The article details an alternative algorithm, based on
\emph{optimization under constraints}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Continuous-time trading\\ and the emergence of probability}\\
  V. Vovk (2009)\\
  \url{http://www.probabilityandfinance.com/}
\end{book}

There are two main approaches to probability theory: the axiomatic
approach (measure theory) and the frequentist approach.  This article
uses a third one: game theory.  Even without explicitely giving a
probability distribution, one can define a game-theoretic notion of
``almost surely'', \ie, identify events of probability 1
(on the space $\Omega$ of continuous functions $[0,\infty)
  \longrightarrow\RR$):
``with probability one'' means ``unless there exists a trading
  strategy (it has to be adapted to the filtration $\mathscr F_t$
  generated by $\Omega$ -- there is no measure, just a filtration)
  that increases the capital it risks manyfold''.

The article does not really introduce this framework, but it uses it
to state and prove that the process of quadratic variation exists, and
that replacing the time by this quadratic variation process gives a
brownian motion -- the result is known for martingales, \ie, if you
have a probability distribution.  (Informally, this says that in
\emph{market time} log-prices are a Brownian motion.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonparametric methods\\ for volatility density estimation}\\
  B. van Es and P. Spreij (2009)
\end{book}

In a model of the form $$ Y = X + \text{noise}, $$ if you know the
distribution of $Y$, of the noise, and want that of $X$, you have a
\emph{deconvolution problem},
$$ \mathscr D_Y = \mathscr D_X * \mathscr D_{\text{noise}}. $$
With enough independence assumptions, kernel estimators or wavelet
estimators are fine.  To estimate the density of the volatility in a
GARCH or stochastic volatility model, the lack of independance calls
for extra caution -- but kernels can still do the job.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Admissible strategies\\ in semimartingale portfolio selection}\\
  S. Biagini and A. \v Cern\'y (2010)
\end{book}

A \emph{self-financing strategy} $H$ is a predictable stochastic
process satisfying
$$ H \cdot S_t = V_0 + \int_0^t H_s \, dS_s $$ 
where $S$ is the semimartingale of discounted prices and $V_0$ is the
initial wealth.  This definition is not restrictive enough and
includes undesirable or unrealistic strategies: one usually adds
further restrictions, such as ``never be bankrupt'' or (more commonly)
``$H$ is a supermartingale''.  This article examines those
restrictions and how they arise from the choice of a utility function.
(The article uses the notion of Orlicz space: the $L^p$ spaces can be
generalized by replacing $\va\cdot^p$ by any non-decreasing function,
for instance the lower tail of a utility function $-U(-\va\cdot)$.
Strictly speaking, since the utility is not homogeneous and can even
take infinite values, there are two spaces: the \textbf{Orlicz space}
contains functions which have a scalar multiple of finite norm; the
\textbf{Moore subspace} contains functions all of whose multiples have
a finite norm.)
% [Technical, not read in detail]

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Modelling financial risk}\\
  R. Pfaff\\
  R in Finance 2010
\end{book}

Overview of the tools one may need to model risk, from one or two time series.

The \texttt{PerformanceAnalytics} package provides various estimators
of the most common measures of risk, value at risk (VaR) and expected
shortfall (ES), e.g., assuming that the distribution is gaussian, and
adding a correction for skewness and kurtosis (Cornish-Fisher).

Extreme value theory suggests to refine those estimators in one of the
following three ways:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item By looking only at the tail of the data (\ie, the observations
  above a certain threshold -- the peaks-over-threshold (POT) method)
  and fitting it to a generalized Pareto distribution (GPD).
  The mean-residual-life plot (aka conditional mean exceedance (CME) plot), 
  $E[X-a|X\geq x]\sim x$, 
  can help select the threshold: the plot will onften have a
  non-linear part, a linear part, and noise; you want to fit the GPD
  to the linear part (check the \texttt{POT} package and its
  vignette).
\item An alternative, but less efficient use of the data, is to look
  at the distribution of the maximum return for each year or month
  (the block-maxima method) and fit it to a genralized extreme value
  (GEV) distribution (under reasonable assumptions, the limit
  distribution is in this family).
\item Poisson Point Processes are mentionned, with no details.
\end{compactitem}

You may also want to check the 
\texttt{VaR}, 
\texttt{ismev},
\texttt{QRMlib} 
packages
(the last two accompany books on quantitative risk management).

To fit the whole distribution, you can use a heneralized hyperbolic distribution 
(GHD, with 3 parameters in addition to the location and scale, check the \texttt{ghyp} package), 
or a special case, such as the normal inverse gaussian (NIG), 
the hyperbolic distribution (HYP, check the \texttt{HyperbolicDist} package 
for the pdqr functions and some fitting algorithms), 
etc. (also check the \texttt{SkewHyperbolic} package). 
The \texttt{actuar} package contains common loss distributions (with fewer parameters).

Serial dependence skews the estimators or risk (VaR, ES) 
that rely on iid returns:
check the \texttt{tseries::garch} (GARCH), 
\texttt{fGarch::garchFit} (ARMA-GARCH),
\texttt{bayesGARCH} (MCMC estimation of a GARCH(1,1) model 
with Student innovations),
\texttt{rgarch} (on RForge).
packages.
There are also some multivariate GARCH packages, as well: 
\texttt{ccGARCH} (conditional correlation GARCH),
\texttt{gogarch} (generalized orthogonal GARCH).

To account for dependence, do not rely on correlation 
(there are many fallacies, 
such as the meaning of a zero correlation 
or the extreme values not being $-1$ and $1$)
but prefer the rank correlation, Kendall's tau, 
the index of upper (and lower) dependence
$$ \lambda_{\text{upper}}(X,Y) = P[ Y>F^{-1}_Y(q) \,|\, X>F_X^{-1}(q) ] $$
or a copula. 
(e.g., Gaussian, Gumbel, Clayton or Student, 
estimated by the method of moments from the rank correlation or Kendall's tau, 
or by maximum likelihood). 
Non-symmetric copulas are not mentionned; no R packages are mentionned.

A copula-GARCH model can be estimated as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fit the time series independently, 
  as GARCH(1,1) models with Student innovations, S
  unless you have a very good reason to think 
  that the serial dependence is negligible;
\item Estimate the copula of the residuals;
\item Compute the chosen risk measure (VaR, ES, drawdowns, etc.) 
  using simulations.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{RQuantLib: interfacing QuantLib from R}\\
  D. Eddelbuettel and K. Nguyen\\
  R in Finance 2010
\end{book}

QuantLib is a C++ library, 
using Boost, 
with bindings for many languages 
(Python, etc. via Swig; the R binding is manual),
for option pricing. 
implementing various products and methods 
(Black-Scholes, finite differences, binomial, Monte Carlo, 
Monte Carlo with low discrepancy sequences, etc.).
\begin{verbatim}
  example(EuropeanOption)
  example(BinaryOption)
  demo(OptionSurfaces)
\end{verbatim}
Fixed income products were recently added: 
you can build a discount curve 
(\texttt{DiscountCurve}, \texttt{FittedBondCurve})
and price bonds (\texttt{ZeroCouponBond}, \texttt{FixedRateBond}, etc.).
A GUI (\texttt{RQuantLibGUI}), currently only for bonds, 
based on the \texttt{traitr} package
(inspired by Python's traits UI module)
has also been added.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Extending and embedding R with C++}\\
  D. Eddelbuettel \\
  R in Finance 2010
\end{book}

To speed up computations in R, you can: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use faster functions, that only compute 
  the data needed and none of the diagnostics, 
  for instance \texttt{lm.fit} instead of \texttt{lm}
\item Use \texttt{.C} to call a C function: 
  you have to cast the arguments to the right type in R,
  to compile the shared library (\texttt{R CMD SHLIB}) 
  and load it (\texttt{dyn.load});
  the C code only deals with pointers;
\item Use \texttt{.Call} to call a C function: 
  it is more transparent on the R side,
  you can use arbitrarily complicated types, 
  but you have to cast the arguments in C from 
  the \texttt{SEXP} type using macros,
  leading to hard-to-read code;
  you still have to compile and load the shared library;
\item Use \texttt{Rcpp}, that relies on the STL to replace those macros 
  and enhance code readability, at the expense of speed 
  (it will be faster than pure R code, but slower than \texttt{.C});
\item Use the \texttt{Inline} package, that uses any of those three approaches, 
  but transparently compiles and loads the shared library;
\item Combine any of the above with optimized libraries, such as 
  \href{http://www.gnu.org/software/gsl/}{GSL}
  or the C++ 
  \href{http://arma.sourceforge.net/}{Armadillo}
  linear algebra classes
  (the Gnu Scientific Library (GSL) is a C library, 
  with no platform-specific optimizations 
  and a sometimes awkward C-like syntax;
  it is not limited to linear algebra;
  Armadillo is a (LGPL) C++ wrapper around Lapack and Atlas, 
  with (compile-time) delayed evaluation 
  of some operations to more efficiently combine them, using templates).
\end{compactitem}
\texttt{RInside} works in the other direction: 
it allows you to call R from C++.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical finance for investors\\ 
    unfamiliar with quantitative methods\\ 
    using \texttt{stockPortfolio} in R}\\
  N. Christou and D. Diez\\
  R in Finance 2010
\end{book}

Three functions to download stock returns from Yahoo; 
estimate the variance matrix and the expected returns;
and compute ``the'' optimal portfolio 
(they mean the tangential portfolio), with or without short-selling.
They provide several (mostly undocumented) estimators 
of the variance matrix and the expected returns: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sample variance matrix;
\item Constant correlation model: 
  the correlation of any two stocks is always the same;
\item Multigroup model: the correlation can take two values, 
  depending on whether the stocks are in the same industry or not; 
  industry membership has to be provided;
\item Single index model (a 1-factor model, 
  where the risk factor has to be provided).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Business objectives\\ and complex portfolio optimization}\\
  P. Carl et al.\\
  R in Finance (2010)
\end{book}

Simple optimization problems (linear, quadratic, conical) 
are well addressed by the \texttt{fPortfolio} package,
but for real-world problems and less trivial business objectives, 
such as the expected shortfall (aka conditional value at risk (CVaR), 
often the Cornish-Fisher CVaR computed from historical data) 
or the conditional drawdown at risk (mean of the worst $p$\% drawdowns),
and constraints, 
such as position limits 
(no single asset should be more than $x$\% of the portfolio)
or CVaR limits (no single asset should have a contribution 
to the CVaR over $x$\%),
the \texttt{PerformaceAnalytics} package
provides an ``approximatively correct [solution] rather than 
[a] precisely wrong [one]'', using two methods:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Random portfolios: start with random weights 
  and modify them so that the constraints are met
  (this is an optimization problem with constraints 
  but no objective functions); 
  this will give a set of portfolios on the boundary 
  of the feasibility domain and some in the interior; 
\item Differential Evolution, 
  \ie, a genetic algorithm used to evolve a population of portfolios 
  to optimize several objective functions (maximize return and minimize risk), 
  so as to cover the (efficient) boundary of the feasibility domain.
\end{compactitem}
Since those computations deal with many (independent) portfolios, 
they can easily be parallelized.

The package also provides many plots: 
portfolios in the risk$\times$return space
(you can compare your portfolio with an equal-weighted 
or \emph{equal-risk} (equal-contribution-to-CVaR) benchmark);
cumulative returns;
monthly returns (and expected shortfall) over time; 
drawdown;
portfolio weights by asset (or asset type) over time;
contribution to the portfolio espected shortfall 
(or value at risk) by asset, over time.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{RProtoBuf: Protocol Buffers for R}\\
  R. Fran\, cois and D. Eddelbuettel \\
  R in Finance 2010
\end{book}

\href{http://code.google.com/p/protobuf/}{Protocol Buffers} is a serialization format 
(like JSON, BSON (binary JSON, used by some unstructured databases, such as MongoDb), YAML, ASN, or even XML)
and corresponding C++ library, developed by Google.
The RProtoBuf packages allows you to read and write data in this format,
and can be used when your data does not easily fit in a CSV file.
It is possible to (manually, for the moment) use Rcpp to speed up data access.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Testing, monitoring and dating\\ 
  structural changes in FX regimes}\\
  A. Zeilis\\
  R in Finance 2010
\end{book}

The \texttt{strucchange} package can be used to assess when a country changed the control on its currency: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Consider FX returns with respect to a currency not involved in the affair, e.g., CHF;
\item Regress the returns against those of currencies potentially involved (USD, EUR, GBP, JPY);
\item Test if the coefficient for the dollar is significantly different from 1, 
  if the other coefficients are significantly different from 0;
\item Look for a regine change.
\end{compactitem}

[duplicated?]

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio optimization\\ with CVaR budgets}\\
  K. Boudt et al.\\
  R in Finance 2010
\end{book}

The risk budget (risk is measured by the expected shortfall (ES, CVaR), 
estimated from historical moments via the Cornish-Fisher estimator)
can be used in the portfolio construction process
(with the \texttt{DEOptim} package): 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item As an objective: minimize the CVaR of the portfolio, 
  or minimize the maximum contribution to CVaR;
\item As a constraint: equal-risk portfolio (all assets are required to have the same contribution to the portfolio CVaR, to downweigh ``hot spots'')
  or 60/40 allocation between (the contribution to CVaR of) equities and bonds.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

% 2010-06

\begin{book}
  \emph{Entropy correlation distance method\\ 
  applied to study correlations\\ 
  between the gross domestic product\\ 
  of rich countries}\\
  A. Ausloos and J. Mi\'skiewicz (2009)
\end{book}

Another distance between positive time series (for clustering, minimum spanning tree, etc.): 
the correlation between their moving-window entropies.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Spiraling towards market completeness\\ 
  and financial instability}\\
  M. Marsili (2009)
\end{book}

There seems to be a singularity (like S. Hawking's black holes) in the space of possible financial markets,
as we increase the complexity of financial instrumetns to increase market completeness -- 
this also increases any market imperfection (lack of transparency, asymmetric information, etc.).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The premium of dynamic trading}\\
  C.H.Chiu and X.Y.Zhou (2009)
\end{book}

The efficient frontier of a set of assets is usually computed for a single period.
It can be generalized to a multiperiod (or even continuous-time) setting. 
The efficient frontier is (unsurprisingly) higher but is still a line if there is a risk-free asset.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Characterizing individual\\ communication patterns}\\
  R.D. Malmgren et al. (2009)
\end{book}
Human-generated events (e.g., sending emails) can be modeled as a 2-state Markov chain (active/inactive), 
the agent emitting a signal (Poisson process) with a different rate in each state.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Personalized recommendation 
  via integrated diffusion 
  on user-item-tag tripartite graphs}\\
  Z.-K. Zhang et al.
\end{book}

Many recommendation algorithms are just diffusions (random walks) on bipartite (user-item and user-tag) or tripartite (user-tag-item) (hyper)graphs.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Hypergraph topological quantities\\ 
  for tagged social networks}\\
  V. Zlati\'c et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Another article on the same subject -- nothing new.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding\\ 
  the Lee-Carter mortality forecasting model}\\
  F. Girosi and G. King (2007)
\end{book}

The Lee-Carter mortality model approximates the matrix $m_{a,t}$
of the logarithms of mortality rates in (age) group $a$ and year $t$
as a rank-1 matrix (use the singular value decomposition (SVD) to find it)
$$ m = \beta \gamma' $$
where $\beta$ is the base mortality in each group
and the mortality index $\gamma$ models the time evolution of the mortality rates, often as a random walk.
The resulting process $(m_{\cdot,t})_t$ is a multi-dimensional random walk with strong constraints on the variance matrix.
If the rank-1 assumption is not valid (look at the eigenvalues),
you may want to consider a rank-$k$ approximation or an unconstrained random walk.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The Lee-Carter model\\ 
  for forecasting mortality, revisited}\\
  S.H. Li and W.S. Chan
\end{book}

The Lee-Carter model is not robust.
You may want to add a contamination model that looks for:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Additive outliers:
