\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The role of a matchmaker\\ 
  in buyer-vendor interactions}\\
  L. L\"u et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Around the stable mariage problem: match men/women, employers/job seekers, landlords/tenants, buyers/sellers
to maximize some total utility 
$$ u = ( x^y + y^k )^{1/k} $$
(changing $k$ creates more or less inequality).
The ``matchmaker'' is just a personification of the perfect knowledge of all utilities, 
as opposed to auctions or the (potentially never-ending) quest for a sufficiently good match.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A dynamic model of\\ 
  time-dependent complex networks}\\
  S.A. Hill and D. Braha (2009)
\end{book}

We have acceptable models of (random, static) networks, but few of \emph{dynamic} networks.
Here is one, in which the hubs (highly connected nodes) can change quickly, as observed in daily email datasets:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a (static) underlying network;
\item For each day, build a subnetwork via a node-reinforced random walk: 
  choose the next node of the random walk among the neighbours of the current node, with probabilities 
$$ p \propto 1 + \lambda \times \text{number of visits}. $$
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Effective and efficient similarity index\\ 
  for link prediction of complex networks}\\
  L. L\"u et al. (2009)
\end{book}
One can try to predict missing or future links in a network 
(the recommendation system of an online shop is a link prediction system in the bipartite client$\times$item graph;
there are applications in gene$\times$protein networks as well)
by looking at node similarity measures, such as: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The number of common neighbours $(A^2)_{xy}$;
\item The \emph{Katz index}, 
  \ie, the weighted number of paths between two nodes, 
  with less weight on longer paths, 
  $$\biggl( \sum_{k\geq2}\beta^k A^k \biggr) _{xy};$$
\item The first two terms of this series, 
  $(A^2 + \epsilon A^3)_{xy}$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Limits of declustering methods 
  for disentangling exogenous from endogenous events 
  in time series with foreshocks, 
  main shocks and aftershocks}\\
  D. Sornette and S. Utkin (2009)
\end{book}

A time series of events (earthquakes, financial crises, etc.) can be modeled as a \emph{Hawkes process}
(aka ETAS (epidemic-type aftershock) model)
$$ \lambda( t, M, \mathscr H_t ) = \mu( t, M ) + \sum_{t_i < t} h( t, M, t_i, M_i ) $$
where $M$ is the magnitude of the event, the first term is the background density (alone, it would lead to a Poisson process),
the second term is the response function to past events (measuring their propensity to trigger aftershocks).
Algorithms to disentangle those events (\ie, label them as ``exogenous'' (background)
or ``endogenous'' (aftershock)) do not perform well.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting network communities\\ 
  by propagating labels under constraints}\\
  M.J. Barber and J.W. Clark (2009)
\end{book}

The label propagation algorithm (LPA) to identify communities
(assign a label to each vertex at random; change the label of a vertex to that of the majority of its neighbours, iterate until convergence)
finds a local optimum of some optimization problem -- it turns out that the global solution is the situation in which all vertices have the same label...
A penalty can be added to bring the optimization closer to what we expect.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Structure of shells in complex networks}\\
  J. Shao et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Exercise on generating functions and networks; the shell (wrt a node) is the set of nodes $\ell$ edges away from it.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Market bubbles and crashes}\\
  T. Kaizoji and D. Sornette
\end{book}

A readable, non technical, superficial review article: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Bubbles are frequent and follow the following pattern: new oportunity 
  (beware when the media start to talk about ``new ...'' -- 
  you could try to build a bubble index by counting the number of occurrences of the word ``new'' in the press -- add synonyms, as well),
  rising prices, investments in increasingly illiquid assets, crash.
\item Markets are not efficient: current prices are not the present value of future cash flows.
\item Evidence that this can be explained by limits on arbitrage (e.g., short sales restrictions) are mixed: 
  some look convincing (such restrictions should lead to overvaluation), 
  but so is the evidence that those limits can be circumvented 
  (by using options instead of short sales: the put-call parity, 
  from tick data (rather than close prices, which are quotes on which one cannot trade), is valid);
\item Rational investors do ride bubbles; by anticipating them, they actually create them: they know that noise (irrational) traders will follow them;
\item Rational investors do not arbitrage bubbles out, because then do not know \emph{when} to do so -- the bubbles therefore continues to grow;
\item Physicists use statistical mechanics to model markets as the aggregation of many ``agents'' and describe bubble bursts as phase changes;
\item Bubbles are characterized by faster-than-exponential growth
$$ \log( \text{price} ) = \alpha + \beta ( t_0 - t ) ^\gamma + \text{noise}, \quad \gamma < 1, \quad \beta < 0 $$
(this is often visible on a 6-month window; you could use this to build a bubble detector)
\item ODE with ``explosive'' behaviour can be generalized to SDEs (``stochastic finite time singularities'');
\item The log-periodic power law model is just mentionned.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial bubbles, real estate bubbles, derivative bubbles and the financial\\ and economic crisis}\\
  D. Sornette and R. Woodard (2009)
\end{book}

Another similar review article, with histical data, 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item To explain the causes of the crises, do not spend your time identifying the first domino to fall: instead, try to understand why the dominoes were laid out like that.
\item Technical and rational mechanisms (what is rational for a single investor need not be so for the market as a whole)
  play a small role, behavioral mechanisms dominate.
\item More details on the log-periodic power law, and actual examples
$$ \log( \text{price} ) = \alpha + \beta ( t_0 - t ) ^ \gamma ( 1 + \epsilon \cos ( \omega \log(t_0-t) + \phi ) ) $$ 
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Complex systems: from nuclear physics\\ 
  to financial markets}\\
  J. Speth et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Log-periodic oscillations (\`a la Sornette) can result from 
a \textbf{Weierstrass random walk}: 
take a step of length $b^j a$ with probability proportional to $1/M^j$ ($b>1$, $M>1$, vary $j \in \NN^\times$).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The Markov modulated Poisson process\\ and Markov Porsson cascade\\ with applications to web traffic modelling}\\
  S.L. Scott and P. Smyth\\
  (Bayesian Statistics, 2003)
\end{book}

A Markov modulated Poisson processe (MMPP, 
or Markov Poisson cascade, when the states are ordered to make the model identifiable)
is a Poisson process whose rate varies according to a (finite-state, hidden) Markov process,
used to model events with irregular bursts of activity 
(an $n$-state MMPP is equivalent to a superposition (sum) of $n$ (fixed-rate) Poisson processes,
some of which may be inactive, the inactivity being controled by an $n$-state Markov chain).
They can be fitted with the EM algorithm or MCMC data augmentation.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Causal links between US economic sectors}\\
  G.H.T. Lee et al. (2009)
\end{book}

To analyze a bubble or crash (after the fact), 
take hourly sector log-returns, 
segment each of them by recursive regime change tests 
(likelihood ratio test of a gaussian distributions versus two gaussian distributions;
you can reoptimize the segment boundaries at each step),
stop when some threshold is reached,
assign the segments to either a ``high'' or a ``low'' volatility regime,
plot all segmented time series (the segments are different for each sector)
with a level of grey indicating the volatility level,
try to spot the begining of the crisis and the recovery (a month of high, resp low, volatility),
compare the start and end of the crisis for each sector --
often, the first to enter is also the last to leave.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The StressVaR: a new risk concept\\ 
  for superior fund allocation}\\
  C. Coste et al.  (2009)
\end{book}

The risk of a (portfolio of) hedge fund(s) can be estimated as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Select several good 1-factor risk models (rather than a single multi-factor model -- this can be seen as a form a bayesian model averaging (BMA) or a regularized multifactor model);
\item Take the \emph{maximum} of the value-at-risk (VaR) predicted by those models (rather than the VaR of their linear combination).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive model for recommendation of news}\\
  M. Medo et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Most recommendation systems use a \emph{global} rating system,
and only narrow down the centers of interest of the users by categories or keywords --
some distinguish between short- and long-term interests; 
some use implicit ratings (based on access or reading time).
The authors use an epidemic-like process to propagate relevant news among users with a similar interest profile;
the timeliness of the news is ensured by the exponential spreading of epidemics (if the users find the news relevant)
and a continuous time decay.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial bubble analysis\\ with a cross-sectional estimator}\\
  F. Abergel et al. (2009)
\end{book}

The authors claim that the proportion of stocks with performance beyond some threshold $z$ since ``some'' reference date 
(and its asymptotic behaviour $\sim_{z\to\infty} z^{-\alpha}$) can be used as a bubble indicator --
but then, they only check that the cross-sectional variance can be used as a bubble indicator.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stability analysis 
  with applications of a two-dimensional dynamical system 
  arising from a stochastic model for an asset market}\\
  V. Belitsky and A.L. Pereira (2009)
\end{book}

To examine the stability of stock markets, 
you can build a 2-dimensional dynamical system 
for the price and the excess demand (for a single asset).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Joint modelling of\\ gas and electricity spot prices}\\
  N. Frikha and V. Lemaire (2009)
\end{book}

Gas and electricity prices are important for power plants and energy companies: 
they represent their costs and revenues.
They exhibit the following features: seasonality, trend, spikes, heavy tails, auto-correlation, long-memory, cross-correlation.
(After removing the trend and seasonality), they are often modeled by 
Ornstein processes
\begin{align*}
  X(t)  &= \sum_i Y_i(t) \\
  d Y_i &= - \lambda_i Y_i dt + d L_i
\end{align*}
where the $L_i$ are brownian motions (leading to Ornstein-Uhllenbeck processes) 
or L\'evy processes (to model the spikes -- one could also use OU processes with very quick mean reversion);
some of those $L_i$ appear in both the gas and electricity models, to account for cross-correlation.
The authors replace the L\'evy processes by considering OU processes and more general diffusions
$$ d Y = - \lambda Y dt + \sigma(Y) dW $$
where $\sigma$ is chosen to give the observed stationary distribution (if you know the drift and the stationary distribution of a diffusion, you can compute its volatility),
modeled as a (quasi-saddlepoint approximation of a) normal inverse gaussian (NIG) distribution.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Eroding market stability by proliferation\\ 
  of financial instruments}\\
  F. Caccioli et al. (2009)
\end{book}

The proliferation of financial instruments make the markets more efficient, more complete, 
but it also brings them closer to an unstable state -- a phase transition.
This comes from the interplay of supply and demand and the price impact of transactions.

The article also contains a concise definition of financial markets (markets that allow intertemporal exchanges fo wealth)
and a clear description of the 1-period asset pricing framework:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item There are two times, $t=0$ (today) and $t=1$ (tomorrow);
\item There is a finite set $\Omega$ of possible states $\omega$ at time $t=1$, each probabilities $p_\omega$;
\item There are $K$ risky assets, that cost 1 today and pay $r_{k,\omega}$ tomorrow;
\item There is one risk-less asset, that costs 1 today and pays 1 tomorrow;
\item There is no arbitrage, \ie, there is no portfolio whose return is positive or 0 in all states and positive in at least one state;
\item This implies the existence of an \emph{equivalent martingale measure} $q$ such that 
  $$ \forall k \qquad E_q[r_k] = \sum_\omega q_\omega r_{k,\omega} = 0 $$
\item A contingent claim is a contract that pays $f_\omega$ in stale $\omega$;
  if there exists a replicating portfolio $\theta$, \ie, a portfolio $\theta$ such that 
  $$ \forall \omega \in \Omega \qquad f_\omega = f_0 + \sum_k \theta_k r_{k,\omega} $$
  the contingent claim is said to be \emph{marketable}
  and its price is $V_F = E_q[f] = \sum_\omega q_\omega f_\omega$;
\item The market is complete if there are at least $\#\Omega$ independent vectors among $r_1,\dots,r_K$; 
  then, all claims are marketable and the risk-neutral measure is unique.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A general ``bang-bang'' principle for predicting the maximum of a random walk}\\
  P.C. Allart (2009)
\end{book}

The best predictor (adapted stopping time) 
of the maximum (on $[0,T]$) 
of a random walk with drift 
is obtained with $\tau=0$ if the drift is negative 
and $\tau=T$ if it is positive.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk concentration and diversification: 
  second-order properties}\\
  M. Degen et al. (2009)
\end{book}

First order (I would say zeroth order) approximations of the \textbf{risk concentration}
$$ C(\alpha) = \dfrac{ \text{VaR}_\alpha \sum_i X_i }{ \sum_i \text{VaR}_\alpha X_i } $$
or the risk diversification $1 - C(\alpha)$, for instance, 
$$ 
  \dfrac{F(tx)}{F(t)} \longrightarrow_{t\to\infty} x^{-1/\xi} 
  \quad\implies\quad 
  C(\alpha) \longrightarrow_{\alpha\to1} n^{\xi-1}
$$
are not sufficient: the convergence is too slow. 
One can devise a second-order (first-order) approximation.

In the case of non-coherent risk measures (such as the value-at-risk), the diversification benefit can even be negative.
\begin{verbatim}
  N <- 1e6
  library(actuar)
  x <- rburr(N,1,1)
  y <- rburr(N,1,1)
  f <- function(z) quantile(z,.99)
  f(x+y)/(f(x)+f(y)) # > 1...
\end{verbatim}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive model for recommendation of news}\\
  M. Medo et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Most recommendation systems use a \emph{global} rating system,
and only narrow the centers of interest of the user by categories or keywords --
some also distinguish between short- and long-term interests;
some use implicit ratings (based on reading time or access).
The authors use an epidemics-like process 
to propagate relevant news among users with a similar rating profile; 
the timeliness of the news is ensured by the exponential spreading of epidemics 
(if the users find it relevant) 
and a continuous time-decay.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A coupled markov chain approach 
  to risk analysis of credit default swap index products}\\
  R. Hochreiter and D. Wozabal (2009)
\end{book}

Confusing attempt to add some dependency (sector membership) to the transition matrix generating rating changes:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Each assets's rating is a Markov chain (with states AAA, AA, etc.) whose transition matrix is known;
\item If two assets are in the same sector, their rating are not independant and can be modeled by a Bernoulli mixing model.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Universal power laws\\ 
  in the threshold network model:\\ 
  a theoretical analysis\\ 
  based on extreme value theory}\\
  A. Fujihara et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Asymptotic analysis of the \textbf{threshold network model} -- 
random networks on a set of nodes obtained by randomly assigning a weight to each node 
(according to a given probability distribution) 
and linking any two nodes when the sum of their weights exceeds a given threshold.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Leverage causes fat tails\\ and clustered volatility}\\
  S. Thurner et al. (2010)
\end{book}

Limits on leverage cause funds to sell in falling markets, amplifying the fall. 
Limits based on volatility (allow a high leverage when the volatility is low) are even worse.
(The article also recall what a \emph{margin call} is: what you have to pay to a lender when the value of your collateral drops below an agreed limit.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Atmospheric complexity\\ or scale by scale simplicity?}\\
  S. Lovejoy et al.\\
  Geophysical Research Letters (2009)
\end{book}

The fractal nature of the atmosphere (similar structures are present at different scales) could simplify weather forecast --
the article only presents evidence of these fractal structures, but no applications.
[This structure can be seen as a form of symmetry, 
$\ZZ$ acting by 
$n: \mathbf x \mapsto \lambda^n \mathbf x$,
which should lead to a conservation law (Noether's theorem),
that could simplify the PDEs -- 
or simply allow macroscopic forecasts without looking at the microscopic data.]

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation breakdown,\\ 
  copula credit default models and arbitrage}\\
  R. Tzani and A.P. Polychronakos (2009)
\end{book}

Complete nonsense:
when the market and the model (Gaussian copula for CDSes...) disagree, the market is wrong and the model is right -- and you are bankrupt.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A computational view of market efficiency}\\
  J. Hasanhodzic et al.\\
  AlphaSimplex, MIT (2009)
\end{book}

In a market with periodic returns, modified by the impact of bounded-memory traders,
traders with more memory make more profits.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Market impact and trading profile\\ of large trading orders in stock markets}\\
  E. Moro et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Market impact of hidden orders (large trading orders, executed incrementally)
increases as the square root of the order size; 
once the order is finished, the impact reverts to half its peak value

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The scale of market quakes}\\
  T. Bisig et al. (2009)
\end{book}

The multiscale structure of log-price time series can be described by the ``term structure of volatility''
(volatility of the time series of returns over intervals of size $\tau$, as a function of $\tau$);
it is a function, but its values can be combined in some ad hoc way to produce a single numeric ``scale of market shocks''.
The authors present (somewhat confusingly) a similar measure based on the irregular time series of 
``directional changes of amplitude greater than $\lambda$'', for various values of $\lambda$.
This is similar to the term structure of volatility, but measured on \emph{market time}, 
rather than clock time.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling scientific citation patterns\\ 
  and other triangle-rich acyclic networks}\\
  Z.X. Wu and P. Holme (2009)
\end{book}

Empty article presenting a model of network evolution, whose parameters are: 
the out-degree distribution; 
the aging of the relevance of papers (nodes);
the formation of triangles.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the relationship between\\ 
  trading network and WWW network:\\ 
  a preferential attachment perspective}\\
  A. Mirzal (2009)
\end{book}

The \textbf{page rank} algorithm 
(the rank of a page depends on the rank of the pages pointing to it, 
with less weight for pages with too many links),
$$ \text{page rank}_i = \sum_{j \to i} \dfrac{\text{page rank}_j}{\text{out-degree}_j} $$
is an eigenvalue problem 
\begin{align*}
  p &= p \cdot O^{-1} L \\
  p &: \text{vector of ranks}\\
  O &: \text{diagonal matrix of out-degrees}\\
  L &: \text{incidence matrix}
\end{align*}
often solved iteratively (after some rescaling to ensure convergence).

The HITS (hypertext-induces topic search) algorithm considers two types of ranks, ``hub'' and ``authority'' 
(you can see this as a hidden fuzzy bipartite structure)
\begin{align*}
  \text{authority}_i &= \sum_{j \to i} \text{hub}_j \\
  \text{hub}_j &= \sum_{j \to i} \text{authority}_i
\end{align*}
is another eigenvalue problem
\begin{align*}
  \text{authority} &= \text{hub} \cdot L \\
  \text{hub} &= \text{authority} \cdot L
\end{align*}
\ie, the vectors of authority and hub scores are eigenvectors of $L'L$ and $LL'$
(to ensure unicity and convergence, it may be necessary to rescale and/or regularize those matrices, as with ridge regression).

The same ideas can be used to study \emph{trading networks}, \ie,
networks of buyers, sellers and resellers.
[There may be a link between the price discovery process and the convergence of those algorithms.]

\nextbook

% 2010-04

\begin{book}
  \emph{A penalized matrix decomposition, with applications to sparse principal components and canonical correlation analysis}\\
  D.W. Witten et al.
\end{book}

The first $r$ components of the singular value decomposition of a matrix $X$,
$$ X = U D V', \quad U'U = 1, \quad V'V = 1, \quad D \geq 0, $$
give its best rank-$r$ approximation (for the Frobenius norm)
$$ 
  \sum_{k=1}^r d_k \mathbf{u}_k \mathbf{v}_k' = 
  \argmin _{Y,\ \rank Y \leq r} \n{ X - Y }_F ^2. 
$$
This can be generalized by adding a penalty for $\mathbf u$ and $\mathbf v$, 
leading to a penalized rank-$r$ approximation,
and a \textbf{penalized matrix decomposition} (PMD).
The suggested penalty combines the lasso (an $L^1$ penalty, $\sum_u \va{u_i}$)
with the \emph{fused lasso} ($\sum \va{u_i - u_{i-1}}$).

The PMD can be used to define \textbf{sparse principal components}
(other formulations exist, based on regression properties 
or the variance minimization property of the principal components).

The article also provides (efficient) algorithms 
to compute those decompositions.
% I have not read the section on penalized canonical correlation analysis.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Sparse inverse covariance estimation\\ with the lasso}\\
  J. Friedman et al. (2007)
\end{book}
A sparse variance matrix estimator, based on the lasso ($L^1$ penalty);
the sparsity of the matrix gives an undirected (sparse) graph between the variables.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Ant-based clustering and topographic mapping}\\
  J. Handl et al.
\end{book}

\textbf{Ant-based sorting} is a topographic mapping algorithm
(\ie, an algorithm that maps observations 
from a high-dimensional space 
to a plane or some other low-dimensional space, 
such as multi-dimensional scaling (MDS) 
or self-organizing maps (SOM))
in which agents (ants) pick up observations (randomly), 
transport and drop them (randomly) 
where there are similar observations around.
The algorithm can be improved as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Increase the penalty for dissimilarity, 
  to help separate the clusters;
\item Add some short-term memory, 
  so that each ant knows if a newly picked up observation 
  is similar to those around a previous drop point;
\item Progressively increase the radius of of perception of the ants;
\item For a limited time, 
  take into account 
  the number of occupied cells (the density), 
  to help spread out the clusters
  (only for a limited time, 
  because we want the clusters to be globular, cluster-like);
\item The article also suggests some empirical 
  changes to the pickup and drop probabilities, 
  and gives some advice on the choice of the parameters);
\item If you want clustering, and not only topographic mapping, 
  apply some clustering algorithm 
  such as agglomerative clustering to the result.
\end{compactitem}

The performance of the algorithm was examined with the $F$ measure
(from the ideas of \emph{precision} and \emph{recall} in \emph{information retrieval})
\begin{align*}
  G_i &= \text{desired groups} \\
  C_i &= \text{clusters produced} \\
  p_{ij} &= \dfrac{\# G_i \inter C_j}{\# C_j} \\
  r_{ij} &= \dfrac{\# G_i \inter C_j}{\# G_i} \\
  F_{ij} &= \dfrac{2 p_{ij} r_{ij}}{p_{ij} + r_{ij}} \\
  F &= \sum_i \dfrac{\# G_i}{\# X} \max_j F_{ij},
\end{align*}
the Dunn index
$$ D = \min \dfrac{\text{inter-cluster distance}}{\max\text{cluster diameter}} $$
and the intra-cluster variance.

For clustering, the algorithm fails to recognize 
the clusters in presence of a hierarchical structure 
(it only recognizes the top of the hierarchy),
fails to recognize small clusters in general;
but scales much better than competing algorithms.
For topographic mapping, MDS performs better in low dimensions, SOM in high dimensions.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A hybrid particle swarm ant colony optimization for design of truss structures}\\
  A. Kaveh et al. (2008)
\end{book}

(This is just a random article on the subject, among hundreds of others: it does not bring anything new.)
\textbf{Particle swarm optimization} (PSO) is a population-based optimization algorithm,
in which the particles (candidate solutions) move, influenced by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Their momentum;
\item The best solution they have found so far;
\item The position and momentum of the particles around them,
  \ie, (indirectly) the best solution their neighbours have found so far;
\item In the \textbf{ant colony optimization} (ACO) variant,
  they are also influenced by (pheromones left around,
  indicating the momentum of) particles that were there some time ago.
\end{compactitem}
This may not work well in high dimensions,
because of the curse of dimensionality,
except if, as here,
\emph{constraints} drastically reduce the search space.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.perceptualedge.com/articles/b-eye/choosing_colors.pdf}{\emph{Choosing colors for Data Visualization}}\\
  M. Stone (2006)
\end{book}

% In short: assign color according to function; use contrast to highlight, analogy to group; and control value contrast for legibility.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item We perceive contrasts of colours in three dimensions: hue, value and chroma; try to use all three.
\item Excessive hue variations create clutter: also use contrasts of value and chroma.
\item Avoid saturated colours (only use them to highlight).
\item To be legible, graphical (or textual) elements should have a contrast of \emph{value} with the background (contrast of hue or chroma is insufficient);
smaller sizes require more contrast. Indeed, some artists first ``block in the values'' and only add colour at a later stage.
\item Only use 2 or 3 hues, e.g., analogous or complementary or split-complementary (\ie, one colour and two colours close to its complementary).
\item A white background is preferable: it provides a reference for colours (the ``white balance'' of your digital camera).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.perceptualedge.com/articles/b-eye/dot_plots.pdf}{\emph{Dot Plots: A Useful Alternative to Bar Charts}}\\
  N.B. Robbins (2006)
\end{book}

Barcharts can become cluttered when there are many observations 
(the ink/information ratio is high), 
when there are several variables 
(stacked bar charts are almost always a bad idea), 
or when the zero is not meaningful 
or too far from the bulk of the data: 
in those cases, you can use dotplots instead.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.perceptualedge.com/articles/visual_business_intelligence/rules_for_using_color.pdf}{\emph{Practical rules for using colors in charts}}\\
  S. Few (2008)
\end{book}

A list of simple rules, with examples and counterexamples:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use a consistent background, to allow a reliable comparison of the colour or value of the plot elements
  (think of grey squares of the same intensity, on a grey \emph{gradient});
\item The colour of the objects should contrast with the background 
  (counterexample: a heatmap, \ie, a table whose cells are coloured, where the important values sometimes end up in black on a dark background);
\item Only use colour with a purpose in mind;
\item The differences in colour should be meaningful (the viewer will instinctively try to interpret those differences: at the very least, this would distract him from what he should be looking at; the typical counterexample is a barchart whith a different colour for each bar);
\item Use neutral colours (greys, browns, pastels) for most information, and bright/dark saturated colours for what requires more attention;
\item Do not cover large areas with saturated colours: that looks garish, unsophisticated, unprofessional
  (the Brewer palettes are fine for large areas);
\item Small elements (thin lines, dots in a scatterplot) require brighter/darker and more saturated colours to be easily distinguished; 
  this is the reason why the Brewer palettes (designed for maps) are inadequate for line plots or scatterplots;
\item Use a single hue to encode sequential values (or two for diversing ones, \ie, if there is a clearly defined ``zero'');
\item Use a dull, hardly visible colour for less important elements (axes and borders could be grey, the background could be white);
\item Do not use red and green together, even if they seem widely understood as ``traffic light colours'': 
  most colourblind people cannot distinguish them (red and blue is fine);
\item Choose a few palettes in advance (and for each, a variant with more saturated colours for scatterplots and lineplots) and keep them at hand;
\item Do not use visual effects (3D, reflections, etc.).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{\href{http://www.perceptualedge.com/articles/guests/intro_to_cycle_plots.pdf}{Introduction to cycle plots}}\\
  N.B. Robbins (2008)
\end{book}

For progressively changing periodic patterns:
\begin{verbatim}
  xyplot( x ~ week | day_of_week )
\end{verbatim}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Sequential implementation\\ of Monte Carlo tests\\ with uniformly bounded resampling risk}\\
  A. Gandy
\end{book}

The power of a Monte Carlo test can be improved by replacing the sum 
$$ \sum_1^n \mathbf{1}_{T_i \geq T} $$
with the whole path of the partial sums
$$ \sum_1^k \mathbf{1}_{T_i \geq T}, \qquad k \geq 1, $$
and comparing them with boundary paths.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple forward selection procedure\\ based on the false discovery rate control}\\
  Y. Benjamini and Y. Gavrilov (2009)
\end{book}

Variable selection, in a regression model, can be seen as a penalized regression with an $L^0$ penalty;
this can be generalized to other penalties, 
including non-linear ones
(a non-linear function of the $L^0$ or $L^1$ norm of the coefficients).
Variable selection algorithms often perform statistical tests to decide whether to add a new variable, 
all with the same threshold for the $p$-value; 
instead, one can use the false discovery rate, 
$$ 
\text{FDR} = \dfrac{
  \# \text{true rejected null hypotheses}
}{
  \text{rejected null hypotheses}
}
$$
to progressively change this threshold.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian methods\\ for measuring operating risk}\\
  C. Alexander (2002)
\end{book}
If you do not have enough loss data to fit an extreme distribution,
add a bayesian prior, in the form of a bayesian network 
(\ie, more variables, with known dependency relations between them), 
to incorporate domain knowledge.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Retrospective exact simulation\\ of diffusion sample paths with applications}\\
  A. Beskos et al. (2006)
\end{book}

Most algorithms sampling from the paths of a diffusion do not sample from the stochastic process itself, 
but from a different but hopefully close discrete process.
The (confusing) article presents an exact simulation algorithm, based on rejection sampling.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Exact simulation of diffusions}\\
  A. Beskos and G.O. Roberts (2006)
\end{book}

Still not understandable, in spite of the examples.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{MCMC methods for multi-response\\ generalized linear mixed models:\\ the MCMCglmm R package}\\
  J. Hadfield\\
  Journal of Statistical Software (2006)
\end{book}

The \texttt{MCMCglmm} package fits generalized linear mixed models; 
it is more restricted than Bugs/Jags, but faster.
Presenting the same example in the Bugs language (which is straightforward to understand) 
would help understand the syntax of the function.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Exploratory data analysis leading towards\\ the most interesting binary association rules}\\
  A. Iodice D'Enza et al. 
\end{book}

% Interesting and clear
Given a binary dataset, e.g., a basket$\times$item boolean matrix $Z$ representing the sales of an online shop,
\textbf{association rules} are pairs of items, $A \implies B$, 
with a large enough \textbf{support} $P(A\inter B)$
and a large enough \textbf{confidence} $P(B|A)$ --
this can be generalized to tuplets.
Association rule mining algorithms, such as the \emph{Apriori} algorithm,
usually select all pairs with a large enough support, and among them, take those with the largest confidence.
The computations can be formulated in linear algebraic terms, 
\begin{align*}
  \text{Support} &= \dfrac1n Z' Z \\
  \text{Confidence} &= Z' Z \text{diag}(\text{Support})^{-1}
\end{align*}
but the matrices are too large.
Dimension reduction through \textbf{incremental $k$-means}
(add the baskets one by one)
can make (those matrices block-diagonal and) the computations amenable.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Volatility forecasts and the at-the-money implied volatility:
  a multi-components ARCH approach and its relation with market models}\\
  G. Zumbach\\
  Risk Metrics (2007)
\end{book}

A \textbf{multiscale ARCH} process is 
an ARCH($\infty$) process of the form 
\begin{align*}
  r_{t+1}    &= \sigma_t \epsilon_{t+1} \\
  \epsilon_t &\sim N(0,1) \\
  \sigma_t^2 &= \sum_{k=1}^n w_k \sigma_{t, \tau_k} ^2 \\
  \sigma_{t,\tau}^2 &= e^{- 1 / \tau} \sigma_{t-1, \tau} + ( 1 - e^{- 1 / \tau}) r_t^2
\end{align*}
\ie, the squared volatility is some linear combination of exponential moving averages, 
with different time scales, of the squared returns. 
These factors play a role similar to the risk factors of a risk model.
One or two different time scales (say, 3 weeks and 3 years) may suffice.
The long-memory ARCH uses a large number of components, but the characteristic times and the weights have simple forms (geometric increasing and logarithmic decreasing),
\begin{align*}
  \tau_t / \tau_{k-1} &= \rho \\
  w_k & \propto 1 - \dfrac{\log \tau_k}{\log \tau_0}
\end{align*}
This can be generalized to a continuous-time model.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stability of graph communities\\ across time scales}\\
  J.-C. Delvenne et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Gauging the quality of a clustering (community structure) in a graph is difficult:
here is another measure to consider.
Let $(X_t)_t$ be the random walk on the graph and 
$T_{i,t} = \mathbf{1}_{X_t \in C}$ the boolean variable denoting membership to community $C$ (it is no longer Markov).
Its autocorrelation function (plot it with a log-scale for the lag) is a measure of the stability of the community.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Mapping change in large networks}\\
  M. Rosvall and C.T. Bergstrom (2009)
\end{book}

You can assess the significance of a clustering or partition of a network (a kind of dimension reduction)
by comparing with that of bootstrap samples;
the evolution of those clusters can be represented in an 
\textbf{alluvial diagram}.
This is exemplified by the emergence of neuroscience from molecular biology, psychology, psychiatry, neurology, medicine.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Random hypergraphs and their applications}\\
  G. Ghoshal et al. (2009)
\end{book}

Folksonomies (sets of $\langle$user, ressource, tag$\rangle$ triplets,
in collaborative tagging websites such as 
flickr,
citeUlike,
delicious,
bibsonomy)
or RDF databases (sets of $\langle$subject, object, verb$\rangle$ triplets)
can be represented by \textbf{tripartite hypergraphs}.
Some care is needed to extend classical graph-theoretic notions:
the degree of a vertex is the number of hyperedges containing it (rather than the number of neighbours of a given colour);
there is a degree distribution for each vertex type (colour) and the three are linked (they cannot be chosen independently: they must imply the same number of hyperedges);
there are various kinds of projections (onto one or two colours, using vertices of one or two colours).
Some properties of hypergraphs can be studied by looking at the generating functions of the three degree distributions:
degree distribution of the projections, existence of a giant component, percolation (\ie, proportion of vertices you can remove without breaking the giant component).
A random tripartite graph can be defined by specifying the degree distribution (they have to be compatible, \ie, they should all suggest the same number of hyperedges),
choosing hyperedge stubs ar random for each vertex, and joining them at random.
The proportions of those random tripartite hypergraphs do not match those of real-world ones, mainly because they fail to account for multiple tagging (the same user giving different tags to the same ressource).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The empirical properties\\ of large covariance matrices}\\
  G. Zumbach (2009)
\end{book}

When estimating a covariance matric from time series, you may want to estimate it on a moving window (or with decaying weights) and consider the evolution of:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The top eigenvalues (spectrum);
\item The distribution of the eigenvalues (spectral density);
\item The subspace spanned by the top eigenvalues (it can change, even if the eigenvalues look stable);
\end{compactitem}
In the spectrum, there is no clear separation between noise and non-noise eigenvalues as suggested by random matrix theory (RMT).
The dynamics of the covariance and the correlation matrix are very different (some people suggest they have significantly different characteristic time scales 
and suggest to model correlation and volatility separately, as in the constant correlation GARCH model, 
with a quickly moving volatility and a slowly changing correlation.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Executing large orders\\ in a microscopic market model}\\
  A. Weiss (2009)
\end{book}

Comparison of a microscopic market model 
(that explicitely models the limit order book, 
or even a generalized order book, 
which contains the opinion of a fair price for all investors, 
to account for hidden liquidity) 
and a macroscopic model 
(that models the limit order book bore implicitely, 
e.g., with two parameters, ``shape'' and ``resilience''):
how do \textbf{optimal trading strategies} for one model fare in the other?
This is an (imperfect) way of measuring the robustness (to model specification) of those strategies.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Computational modeling of collective human behavior: example of financial markets}\\
  A. Kirou et al. (2008)
\end{book}
A market model in which traders use information both global and local (from their social network).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Studies of the limit order book\\ around large price changes}\\
  B. Toth et al. (2009)
\end{book}

% Empty?
After a large price jump (a single-stock event, not a market crash), all quantities progressively go back to normal, following a power law.
This relaxation can be simulated by a multiagent model.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Production copula}\\
  H. Iyetomi et al. (2009)
\end{book}

The \emph{production equation}
$$ \text{Production} = F( \text{Labour}, \text{Capital} ) $$
(e.g., the Cobb--Douglas model, 
$\text{Production} \propto \text{Labour}^\alpha \text{Capital}^\beta$)
should be replaced by (a statistical model or, more generally)
the probability distribution of 
$$ (\text{Production}, \text{Labour}, \text{Capital}) $$
to account for the heterogeneity of economic agents.
The article models the marginal distributions and the copula separately -- but their model has fatter tails than the data...
Their copula is a \textbf{non-exchangeable} (\ie, hierarchical) Gumbel copula.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Smoothing of multivariate data\\Density estimation and visualization}\\
  J. Klemel\"a\\
  Wiley (2009)
\end{book}

In this multivariate data analysis book, 
the author uses two types of plots to display information, in very different contexts:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \textbf{Rooted trees}, whose nodes are labeled by numbers, such as the heights of the nodes
  (for nice plots it should increase as you move away from the root) 
  or the $n$th coordinate of the point (if it came from the dataset);
\item \textbf{Volume plots}, built from a rooted tree 
  whose nodes are labeled with two numbers interpreted as 
  the altitude of the node and its width; 
  the tree plot looks like stacked slabs of tree.
  (It is not uniquely defined: each time there is a branching,
  you can choose the order of the branches.)
  This is a 1-dimensional analogue of a treemap;
\end{compactitem}

Here are a few examples.

%  the connected components of 
%  the level sets of the probability distribution 
%  form the nodes of a tree, 
%  the node altitudes are the levels,
%  the node widths are the volumnes of the components 
Plotting the barycenter of each connected component 
of each level set $[f=\lambda]$ 
or upper level set $[f \geq \lambda]$ 
of a density $f$ versus $\lambda$
gives a tree.
Plotting a segment for each connected component of each level set $[f=\lambda]$ 
(of length the volumes of the level sets, suitably renormalized,
e.g., by using the radius of a ball with the same volume),
and stacking those segments, gives a \textbf{volume plot}:
it displays the modes and spreads of the original distribution
(the notion of ``mode-preserving transformation'' or \emph{mode isomorphism} can be formalized).
The shape of the peaks gives information about the skewness and the fatness of the tails.
The volume plot is actually a 1-dimensional analogue of the treemap.

%Appendages (tails) of a shape: 
%Given a shape $S$ (say, a single level set) and a central point $P$,
%  the connected components of $S \setminus B(P,r)$, 
%  when the radius $r>0$ varies, form a tree;
%  the radius can be used for the node altitudes, 
%  the (renormalized) volume as the width of the nodes;
In a volume plot, you can replace the volume of the levelsets 
by any interesting function.
You can also replace the density by any function built from the data at hand -- 
think of all the functions you use in scagnostics plots.
For instance, if you have a shape (say, a single level set), 
the distance from a reference point gives a foliation, 
called the \emph{radius transform} of this shape.
The corresponding tree, the \textbf{shape tree}, 
formed from the connected components of $S \setminus B(P,r)$ when $r$ varies,
shows the \textbf{appendages} of the shape.
You can change the reference point to hide or reveal more appendages; 
you can also change the metric.
This is called a \textbf{tail plot} (or a \textbf{radius plot}).

With a cloud of points, 
  you can remove and plot the most central point, and iterate,
  until the cloud ceases to be $\delta$-connected
  (a set is $\delta$-connected iif for any two points, 
  there is a chain of points going from one to the other, 
  so that the distance between two consecutive points 
  be less than $\delta$ -- the problem is that you have to choose $\delta$ -- 
  instead, you could cluster the remaining observations);
  you then process the components separately.
When plotting this \textbf{tail tree} 
(e.g., using the distance from the center versus
the $i$th coordinate of the last point removed),
it is more insightful to use the observations removed 
rather than the barycenters of the $\rho$-connected components.
This can help guess the number of tails and their shapes: 
the book provides a large list of examples, in dimension 2, for common copulas.
The number of tails concerns me: in dimension $d$, $2^d$ tails is not uncommon.
Since a tail tree is just a tree structure on a set of points,
one could consider other tree structures, 
such as the minimum spanning tree (MST) or the shortest-path tree (a rooted analogue of the MST).
  
With a density (or its copula),
the tail plots of the level sets 
can be arranged into a persepctive plot.
  
With a cloud of points, you can estimate the density and look at the mode plot.
  However, density estimates usually depend on a smoothing parameter: 
  by letting it vary, you have a family of volume plots,
  that can be represented as a perspective plot.

To visualize the effect of the smoothing parameter,
you can also take the modes of the density estimate 
for different values of the smoothing parameters
and arrange them into a tree

\begin{center}
  \setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm}%
<<VolumePlot,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width="8cm">>=
library(denpro)
library(MASS)
library(RColorBrewer)
x <- sim.data(n=100,type="mulmod")
op <- par(mfrow=c(1,3),mar=c(0,0,2,0),oma=.1*c(1,1,1,1))
k <- kde2d(x[,1], x[,2],n=500)
#image(k, col=brewer.pal(9, "Oranges"),axes=FALSE)
#image(k, col=brewer.pal(9, "YlOrRd"),axes=FALSE)
image(k, col=brewer.pal(9, "YlGn")[1:6],axes=FALSE)
#contour(k,add=T)
points(x)
box()
pcf <- pcf.kern(x,h=1,N=c(32,32))
lst <- leafsfirst(pcf)             
td  <- treedisc(lst,pcf,ngrid=30)
plottree(td,ptext=0.002,modelabel=TRUE,symbo="L",colo=TRUE,paletti=brewer.pal(8,"Set3")[-2],axes=FALSE)
box()
plotvolu(td,ptext=0.002,modelabel=TRUE,symbo="L",colo=TRUE,paletti=brewer.pal(8,"Set3")[-2],xaxt="n",yaxt="n")
par(op)
mtext("Modes of a multivariate density", side=3,line=3)
@
\end{center}

\begin{center}
  \setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm}%
<<TailPlotOfAShape,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width="8cm">>=
op <- par(mfrow=c(1,3),mar=c(0,0,2,0),oma=.1*c(1,1,1,1))
    # Code from the book
    func <- "clayton"
    N <- c(100,100) 
    marginal <- "student"
    yla <- 5.8
    ala <- -yla
    support <- c(ala,yla,ala,yla)
    g <- 4 
    t <- c(4,4)

    pcf <- pcf.func(func,N,support=support,marginal=marginal,g=g,t=t)

    dp <- draw.pcf(pcf)

    st.big <- leafsfirst(pcf,refe=c(0,0),propor=0.005)
    st <- treedisc(st.big,pcf,ngrid=80)

    paletti <- c("red","blue","green","orange","navy","darkgreen",
     "orchid","aquamarine","turquoise","pink","violet","magenta","chocolate","cyan",
     colors()[50:657],colors()[50:657])

    st.big2 <- prunemodes(st.big,modenum=3)
    ts <- tree.segme(st.big2,pcf=pcf,paletti=paletti)

    # 1st frame
    draw.levset(pcf,propor=0.005,col=ts,xaxt="n",yaxt="n")
    text(-5.6,-5.6,"M1")
    text(5.6,1.0,"M2")
    text(0.8,5.5,"M3")  

    # 3rd frame
    plotbary(st,modelabel=TRUE,ptext=0.4,xaxt="n",yaxt="n")

    # 2nd frame
    plotvolu(st,modelabel=FALSE,colo=TRUE,ptext=0.4,xaxt="n",yaxt="n")  #,proba=TRUE,
    text(5.5,7.3,"M1")
    text(16.9,4.9,"M2")
    text(18.9,4.9,"M3")
par(op)
mtext("Appendages (tails) of a shape", side=3,line=3)
@
\end{center}

\begin{center}
  \setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm}%
<<TailPlotOfACloudHORRIBLYLONGforNOVALIDREASON,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2,out.width="8cm">>=
op <- par(mfrow=c(1,3),mar=c(0,0,2,0),oma=.1*c(1,1,1,1))
    # Code from the book
    library(copula)
    d <- 2
    family <- "clayton"
    n <- 2000
    param <- 4
    c.cop <- archmCopula(family=family,param=param,dim=d)
    set.seed(3)
    dendat <- rCopula(n, c.cop)
    df <- 4
    dendat <- qt(dendat, df=df)

    rho <- 1.1
    tt <- leafsfirst(dendat=dendat,rho=rho)

    paletti <- c("red","blue","green","orange","navy","darkgreen",
      "orchid","aquamarine","turquoise","pink","violet","magenta","chocolate","cyan",
      colors()[50:657],colors()[50:657])

    # frame 1
    ru <- 11
    rl <- -7
    ts <- tree.segme(tt,paletti=paletti)
    plot(dendat,col=ts,xlim=c(rl,ru),ylim=c(rl,ru),
         xlab="",ylab="",pch=16, axes=FALSE)
    box()         
    text(-4.8,-6,labels="T1",cex=1.5)
    text(-0.3,9,labels="T2",cex=1.5)
    text(10,3,labels="T3",cex=1.5)

    # frame 2
    plottree(tt,modelabel=TRUE,colo=TRUE,paletti=paletti,#col=col,linecol=col,
    ptext=0.6,symbo="T",cex=1.5,
    axes=FALSE,
    xmarginright=0.05,xmarginleft=0.05)
    box()
    
    # Frame 3
    plotbary(tt,coordi=2,modelabel=TRUE,symbo="T",ptext=0.6,xaxt="n",yaxt="n") 

par(op)
mtext("Tails of a cloud of points", side=3,line=3)
@
\end{center}

The R code is available in the \texttt{denpro} (for the plots,
which are very tricky to fine-tune -- no \texttt{...} argument,
half-missing contours)
and \texttt{delt} (for the density estimation algorithms) packages.

% Part 1: Visualization

% Chapter 1 Visualization of data
The first part of the book also reviews many types of statistical plots
%(e.g., the \textbf{tailplot})
% Tailplot: x <- -sort(-x[x>median(x)]); plot(x,1:length(x),log="y")
and descriptive statistics for high-dimensional datasets.
The \textbf{depth} of a point (in a cloud of points, or wrt a density)
can be defined as $(1+d)^{-1}$, 
where $d$ is the Mahalanobis or Euclidian $L^2$ or $L^1$ distance to the ``center'';
as the half-space depth (the minimum proportion of points in a half space containing your point);
as the simplicial depth 
(the probability that your point is inside 
a simplex whose vertices are random points from the cloud or the distribution);
as the convex hull peeling depth
(points on the convex hull have depth 1; remove them; 
points on the convex hull of the remaining points have depth 2; etc.).
% Also mentionned: mode, dispersion, PCA, projection pursuit, SOM, MDS

% Chapter 2 Visualization of functions

Functions or probability densities can be examined by 
pp-plots, qq-plots, ff-plots;
perspective or contour plots (but these are not adequate to visualize tails);
slices, projections (marginal densities), or more generally Radon transforms (tomography).

The spread can be examined by looking at the volume of the upper level sets
$$ \lambda \sim \text{Volume}( \{ x \ :\ f(x) \leq \lambda \} ). $$
You may want to normalize those volumes 
(replace them by the radius of a ball of the same volume); 
you may want to replace the sequence of nested sets $f \leq \lambda$ 
(the inspiration comes from \textbf{Morse theory})
by $[f = \lambda]$ 
or by depth regions
or by minimum volume sets 
(sets of minimum volume with a given probability).

\textbf{Multivariate quantiles} are defined by replacing 
$ p (X - u)^+ + (1-p) (X-u)^- $
in the definition of a quantile by 
$$ \n{ X - u } + p \langle v, X - u \rangle, \quad p \in \left]0,1\right[, v \in \SS^d. $$

% Chapter 3 Visualizations of trees

% Chapter 4 Level set trees
%Barycenterplot: levelset tree where the horizontal coordinate is one of the original coordinates and each level set is replaced by the barycenters of its components.

% Chapter 5 Shape trees

% Chapter 6 Tail trees

% Chapter 7 Scales of density estimates

% Chapter 8 Cluster Analysis
The book suggests a few plots to examine clusters, 
but they were not convincing. 
For instance, you can plot the depth of a point against its $i$th coordinate, 
and add edges. In a parallel level plot, 
you choose the number of clusters and plot the clusters separately in this way, 
on top of each other -- it will always look informative, 
even if your choice of cluster number is wrong.
\textbf{Graphical matrices} present the same problem.

\begin{center}
  \setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm}%
<<Clusters,results='hide',echo=FALSE,fig.width=6,fig.height=2.5,out.width="8cm">>=
source("www.rni.helsinki.fi__jsk_denpro_denpro.R")
# http://www.rni.helsinki.fi/~jsk/denpro/denpro.R
op <- par(mfrow=c(1,3),mar=c(0,0,2,0),oma=.1*c(1,1,1,1))
  # 1
  siemen<-5
  n<-300  
  dendat<-sim.data(n=n,seed=siemen,type="mulmod")
  dis<-dist(dendat)
  method<-"average" 
  hc2 <- hclust(dis, method=method)
  tr2<-dend2parent(hc2,dendat)
  plotbary(tr2,modelabel=F,coordi=1,colometh="cluster",colothre=3,xaxt="n",yaxt="n")
  # 2
  algo<-"complete"         #"hclust"   #"kmeans"  
  k<-4
  levelmethod<-"center"    #"random" 
  range<-"local"           #"other"  
  paletti<-c("green","orange","blue","red")
  paraclus(dendat,algo=algo,k=k,method=method,scatter=FALSE,paletti=paletti,
  range=range,levelmethod=levelmethod,terminal=FALSE,coordi=2,xaxt="n",yaxt="n")
  # 3 
  dis<-dist(dendat)
  method<-"complete" 
  hc3 <- hclust(dis, method=method)
  luokat=cutree(hc3,k=4)
  pale<-c("green","orange","blue","red")
  col<-pale[luokat]
  permu<-order(luokat)
  graph.matrix(dendat,permu=permu,col=col,ystart=-10,yaxt="n")
par(op)
rm(support)    
@
\end{center}

% Part 2: Analytical tools
The second part of the book is very technical
and explains how to prove inequalities 
and establish convergence results for density estimators.

% Chapter 9 Density estimation
%Technicalities. Locally identically distributed observations. Locally stationnary time series.

Among the interesting technicalities are 
the correlation between a real-value and a vector valued random variable,
$$ \cor(X,\mathbf Y) = \max_\phi \cor(Y,\phi'\mathbf X),$$
the \textbf{mixing coefficient}, 
$ \beta(X,Y) = 
  \tfrac12 
  \int \va{ f_{X,Y} - f_X f_Y } $,
can also be computed as 
$$ 
  %\beta(X,Y) = 
  \tfrac12 
  \sup_{\mathscr A, \mathscr B}
  \sum_{\substack{A \in \mathscr A \\ B \in \mathscr B}}
  P(X \in A, Y \in B) - P(X \in A) P(Y \in B)
$$
where $\mathscr A$, $\mathscr B$ are partitions,
which can be used to define asymptotic (or ``local'') 
analogues of  the assumptions of
``indentically distributed'' or ``stationary''.

%Add some measure of dependency to the definition of ``locally identically distributed''.
%(the mixing coefficients of the distance between the copulas).

It is always a good idea to preprocess your data, 
either by sphering it or lookting at its copula
(it works fine with financial data).
%Density estimation. Preprocessing: sphering; copula preprocessing (useful for financial data)

% Radon transform, tomography %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

% Chapter 10 Density classes
Densities can often be well approximated by simple density classes, 
such as mixtures, products (as in independent component anlysis) or copulas
(the tail plots can help choose an adequate family of copulas).

% Chapter 11 Lower bounds
%Lower bounds, speed of convergence, $\delta$-nets

% Chapter 12 Empirical processes
%Empirical processes? 

% Chapter 13 Manipulation of density estimates
Density estimation algorithms can benefit from efficient data structures, 
such as those used in the video game industry: 
for instance, an \emph{evaluation tree} is a binary search tree 
that represents a setwise constant function, often enhanced by bounding boxes; 
it can be used to store a density estimate.

% Part 3: toolbox of density estimators
The third part of the book presents density estimators.
% Chapter 14 Local averaging
Local averaging estimators include histograms;
average shifted histograms (ASH), 
which approximate a kernel estimator with a triangular kernel; 
kernel estimators.
You can use volume plots to check that the density estimation is correct -- 
even in high dimensions, when you cannot really ``plot'' it.

Series estimators include 
Fourier series, wavelets, 
projections on a lower-dimensional subspace,
including shrinkage estimators, 
which also tweak the coefficients -- the projection is not orthogonal.

% Chapter 15 Minimization estimators
Those estimators often minimize some empirical risk (MLE, etc.)
and reduce the problem to a finite (combinatorial) one: 
choose the best density among a finite (huge) set of candidates ($\delta$-net),
or a lower-dimensional subspace, 
or a (countable, infinite) dense set in a lower-dimensional subspace
(unless direct computation is possible, 
these are usually approximated by increasingly finer finite sets)

% Chapter 16 Wavelets estimators

% Chapter 17 Multivariate adaptive estimators
%Adaptive density estimators. Histogram = locally constant density.
A greedy \textbf{adaptive density estimator} 
starts with a uniform density on a rectangle, 
splits it in two in the best possible way 
(to minimize some measure of empirical risk), and iterates.
A \textbf{CART histogram}
(a histogram is just a locally constant density)
proceeds greedily, 
but also penalize for the complexity of the density, and prunes the result.
\textbf{Dyadic histograms} only split the rectangles in their middle: 
they are actually Haar wavelet decompositions.
Bootstrap aggregation, \ie, 
averaging the density estimates on several bootstrap samples,
can improve the result.

% Chapter 18 Best basis selection: dyadic histograms, aka Haar wavelet 

% Chapter 19 Stagewise minimization
Stagewise minimization
models the data as a mixture, 
but increases the penalty as more terms are added.

Boosting can be interpreted as a gradient search.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian methods in finance}\\
  S.T. Rachev et al.\\
  Wiley (2008)
\end{book}

(The book is printed on low quality, nose-irritating, dust-mite-friendly paper.)

Clear and detailed introduction to bayesian statistics 
and its applications to portfolio management --
nothing new.

% Chapters 1-5: bayesian statistics
% p. 84
% For the logistic regression, the posterior is not tractable, 
% but can be approximated via a Taylor(-like) gaussian approximation

% Chapter 6
Mean-variance portfolio allocation instability problems can be fixed by 
robust methods, portfolio resampling or bayesian methods.
Bayesian methods take a non-informative or informative prior, 
mix it with the sample data, and output a posterior distribution of expected returns and variance matrix;
in the non-informative prior case, the returns are the sample returns, 
while in the informative prior case they are shrunk towards the prior;
in the non-informative prior case, the variance matrix is scaled \emph{up};
for gaussian returns, the posterior distribution is a Student distribution.

% Chapter 7 Prior beliefs and asset pricing models (CAPM, Fama-French 3-factor model, APT)
More generally, instead of a gaussian model $N(\mu,\Sigma)$, one can ``bayesianize'' a regression -- 
in an asset management context, regressions are called \textbf{asset pricing models}
and the main examples are 
the CAPM ($r \sim f_1$, where $f_1$ are the market returns), 
the Fama-French 3-factor model ($r \sim f_1 + f_2 + f_3$
where the $f_i$ are the market excess returns, 
the returns difference between large and small caps, 
and the returns difference between high and low price-to-book stocks),
and the arbitrage pricing theory (APT,
$r \sim f_1 + \cdots + f_n$, where you can freely choose the $f_i$).
You end up with a posterior distribution for the regression coefficients.
You can blend several models with bayesian model averaging (BMA).

% Chapter 8 Black--Litterman
The \textbf{Black-Litterman} framework allows an investor 
to start with an asset pricing model 
(e.g., the CAPM, in particular the expected return of all assets is the same)
and progressively incorporate more information, 
in the form of forecasts on the returns of assets or portfolios of assets,
each with a confidence level.

% Chapter 9 Market efficiency and return predictability
A market is said to be \textbf{efficient} if one cannot predict future price changes
from past prices (weak efficiency),
from current public information (semistrong efficiency)
or from current public or private information (strong efficiency).
In other words, tests of efficiency compare the following models.
\begin{align*}
  \text{returns}_t &\sim \text{factors}_t \\
  \text{returns}_t &\sim \text{factors}_t + \text{information}_{t-1}
\end{align*}
Economists often use a 2-step procedure: 
first estimate the beta of each asset, using time series regressions 
$r_{i,t} \sim f_t$,
then estimate a cross-sectional regression, (\ie, for a single date $t$)
and test if the intercept is zero. 
In a bayesian setup, you can look at the posterior distribution of this intercept.

But this two 2-step process is incorrect 
(this is the ``error in variables'' problem). 
Instead, one can look for consequences of the efficient market hypothesis (EMH):
for instance, under the CAPM, 
the market portfolio (or your benchmark portfolio) should be efficient
(under the APT, some combination of the factor portfolios should be efficient).
The tests then consider some measure of inefficiency, 
such as the difference in returns between the benchmark 
and the efficient portfolio with the same risk 
(vertical distance to the efficient frontier),
or the difference in risk-adjusted returns $\mu/\sigma$ (Sharpe ratio)
between the Benchmark and the tangent portfolio 
(a kind of ``angular distance'' to the efficient frontier)
or the difference in certainty-equivalent returns 
(the returns of a risk-free portfolio with the same utility,
\ie, the difference in utility converted into returns).
In a bayesian context, one can sample from the posterior distribution of 
the inefficiency measure (it is not tractable: you have to use simulations).
Adding constraints (they change the efficient frontier) is straightforward.

On can also directly test for returns predictability, 
\begin{align*}
  \text{return}_t &\sim \text{factor}_{t-t} \\
  \text{factor}_t &\sim \text{factor}_{t-1}.
\end{align*}
In a bayesian setup, one can estimate 
the posterior distribution of expected returns, 
and look at how it changes as the horizon increases: 
the impact of estimation uncertainty grows, 
making stocks less and less attrative.
[Isn't this in contradiction with the received wisdom that 
stocks are good for the long term 
and bonds better for the short term?]

The \emph{Bayes factor} is not mentionned.

% Chapter 10 Volatility models
The estimation of \textbf{stochastic volatility} (SV) models, 
contrary to GARCH models, is not straightforward.
Approximations, such as the quasi-maximum likelihood,
or biased methods, such as the method of moments,
are not reliable.
The \textbf{efficient method of moments} uses 
an auxiliary, easy-to-fit model, such as the GARCH model:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item You have a complicated model $\mathscr M(\theta)$, 
  e.g., a stochastic volatility model;
\item You have a less satisfactory but tractable model $\mathscr M_0(\zeta)$;
\item Estimate the parameter $\hat\zeta$ of the tractable model $\mathscr M_0$ on the data;
\item For a value $\theta$ of the parameters of the complicated model, simulate data 
  and estimate the parameters $\hat \zeta_\theta$ of the tractable model 
  on the simulated data;
\item Minimize $\n{\hat\zeta - \hat\zeta_\theta}$
  (you can use a maximim-likelihood-based distance instead).
\end{compactitem}

% Chapter 11 Bayesian estimation of ARCH models
The fact that the sum of the GARCH parameters $\alpha+\beta$ is often almost 1
(it should be less than one for the model to be stationary
and anything you do with it meaningful)
could be an artefact of a misspecified model, e.g., time-dependent parameters.
The linear model with student GARCH noise can be estimated via Gibbs sampling
and can easily be generalized to a Markov regime-switching model (MSGARCH),
by adding latent variables.

% Chapter 12 Bayesian estimation of SV models
The stochastic volatility models are also latent-variable models: 
in this case, the number of unknown parameters 
(the model parameters and the unobserved volatility)
is of the same order as the sample.
They can be fitted by a Gibbs single-move sampler 
(\ie, one updates a single parameter or non-observed variable at a time),
but this converges very slowly if there are autocorrelations between the latent variables
(and there are in the case of time series).
% Technicalities:
% The conditional densities $p(\sigma^2_t|\sigma^2_{-t})$
% where $\sigma^2_{-t}$ is the vector of volatilities without time $t$
% are proportional to a product of inverse gamma and log-normal distribution; 
% and use a product of two inverse gamma distributions, which is still inverse gamma.
This can be remediated by Kalman-filter-based \textbf{multimove samplers}.
% NOT READ

The models can be extended to allow for jumps:
add Bernoulli variables $q_t$ to denote the presence of a jump
and log-normal variables $j_t$ for the amplitude of the jump;
you can add jumps in the volatility as well: 
there are then fewer jumps and they are easier to interpret.

% Chapter 13 Advanced techniques
The distribution of asset returns are not gaussian: 
one can model them with a mixture of gaussians, 
an asymmetric Student $T$ distribution, 
a stable distribution, 
an extreme value distribution (EVD), 
a skew-gaussian distribution (mixture of a gaussian and a truncated gaussian).
Copulas are mentionned and the authors are aware of 
the need for and lack of tools to handle and estimate copulas in high dimensions;
they can be used to generalize the Black--Litterman framework 
(copula opinion pooling).
\textbf{Coskewness} (third moment between a stock and the market or a benchmark)
or alternative measures of risk 
(expected shortfall (ES), aka conditional value at risk (CVaR))
can be incorporated into portfolio optimization.

% Chapter 14 Multifactor risk models
A \textbf{risk model} is a concise way of describing the dependency between stock returns: 
it is sometimes written as a linear model,
$$ \mathbf{r} = \boldsymbol{\alpha} + \mathbf{f} \mathbf{e} + \text{noise}, $$
where $\mathbf f$ are the factor returns,
sometimes as a decomposition of the variance matrix of returns $V$ into 
factor exposures $e$ and factor variance $v$: $V = \mathbf{e} v \mathbf{e}' $.
Bayesian methods can be used when estimating risk models.
The risk or variance of a stock can be decomposed into the contribution of each factor (and their interactions); 
this can be generalized to other measures of risk.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Simulation and inference\\ for stochastic differential equations}\\
  S.M. Iacus\\
  Springer (2008)
\end{book}

The first chapter of the book reviews 
stochastic processes and stochastic differential equations (SDE):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Random number generation (Mersenne Twister);
\item Monte Carlo simulations :
  the convergence of $\tfrac1n\sum g(x_i)$ to $Eg(X)$ is very slow, in $\sqrt n$, 
  but is independent of the smoothness of $g$;
\item Variance reduction:
  preferential sampling, antithetic sampling 
  (low discrepancy sequences, \ie, quasi-random numbers, are not covered),
  control variables 
  (find any relation between the quantity of interest 
  and any other quantity, easier/faster to compute, 
  for instance the call-put parity);
\item
  (Total) variation,
  quadratic variation
  $$ [X,X]_t = \limsup_{0=t_0<\cdots<t_N=t} \va{X(t_{k+1})-X(t_{k})}; $$
\item
  Conditional expectation; 
\item
  Martingales;
\item
  Brownian motion, geometric brownian motion,
  brownian bridge (to simulate a brownian motion);
\item
  The \textbf{Karhunen-Lo\`eve} expansion of the brownian motion 
  expresses it as a \emph{countable} sum:
  \begin{align*}
    W(t)(\omega) &= \sum_{i=0}^\infty Z_i(\omega) \phi_i(t) \\
    \phi_i(t)    &= \dfrac{ 2\sqrt{2T} }{ (2i+1)\pi } \sin\dfrac{ (2i+1)\pi t }{2T}\\
    Z_i       &\sim N(0,1) \text{ iid};
  \end{align*}
\item
  Diffusion processes, \ie, SDEs of the form 
  $$  dX_t = b(t,X_t) dt + \sigma(t,X_t)dW_t,  $$
  their stationary distribution, 
  infinitesimal generator 
  (only the formula is given, 
  we are not told what it is nor why it is called like that);
\item
  Ito's formula;
\item
  If the diffusion coefficient $\sigma$ of a SDE 
  only depends on the state variable $X_t$ (and not on $t$), 
  the \textbf{Lamperti transform} turns it, via the Ito formula, 
  into a SDE with unit diffusion coefficient.
\item
  \textbf{Girsanov's theorem} is a change-of-measure theorem, 
  that can be used to compute a Radon-Nikod\'ym derivative, \ie, a likelihood ratio.
\end{compactitem}

This chapter ends with a list of (17) useful families of stochastic processes 
(Ornstein--Uhlenbeck (aka Vasicek), etc.)
with unproven formulas (explicit solution, invariant law $X_{\infty}$,
covariance function $\cov(X_t,X_s)$,
conditional covariance function $\cov(X_t,X_s|X_0=x_0)$,
conditional law $X_t|X_0=x_0$, 
etc.) and properties.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item 
  The \textbf{Jacobi diffusion process}
  $$ dX_t = - \theta \left( X_t - \dfrac12 \right) dt + \sqrt{\theta X_t(1-X_t)} dW_t $$
  is reminiscent of copulas: its invariant distribution is uniform $U(0,1)$;
  in particular, you can use it to build a process 
  with a prescribed invariant distribution $F$ as 
  $Y_t=F^{-1}(X_t)$.
\item 
  Social scientists often consider feedback models
  (for population dynamics, political polarization, etc.), 
  \ie, models with a drift of the form $b(x)=r(\theta-x)$,
  whose diffusion coefficient $\sigma^2$ is constant, linear or polynomial in $x$,
  leading to a gaussian, Gamma or Beta stationary distribution.
\item
  Epidemiologists often consider ordinary differential equations (ODE), 
  to which you can just add a noise term $\sigma(X_t)dW_t$
  to get a SDE.
\end{compactitem}

The second chapter examines numerical simulations. 
The quality of a simulation $Y_\delta$ (where $\delta$ is the step size)
of $Y$ is assessed by looking at how the distance 
$E\va{Y_\delta(t)-T(t)}$ changes, 
for $t$ fixed and $\delta$ getting smaller --
$O(\delta^{1/2})$ is passable, $O(\delta)$ is better.

The \textbf{Euler scheme} is the naive discretization of the SDE: 
replace the $d$ signs with $\Delta$ signs.
Note that the discrete paths you get are 
not discretizations of paths you would get from the SDE itself: 
except for the brownian motion itself, there is a bias, 
you are sampling from a distribution of discrete paths 
close to but different from the desired one.
For instance, the paths of the Cox-Ingersoll-Ross model 
$$ dX_t = (\theta_1=\theta_2X_t)dt+\theta_3\sqrt{X_t}dW_t $$
are (a.s.) positive and therefore well-defined 
(if $2\theta_1 >\theta_3^2$), 
but those of the corresponding Euler scheme is not.

The \textbf{Milstein scheme} uses a second order Taylor expansion (via Ito's theorem).
$$ Y_{i+1} = Y_i + b \Delta t + \sigma \Delta W_t + \dfrac12 \sigma \sigma_x ((\Delta W_t)^2 - \Delta t) $$

If the diffusion coefficient $\sigma$ 
only depends on the state variable $X_t$ (and not on $t$), 
you can remove it with the Lamberti transform; 
things then magically improve: 
the (simpler) Euler scheme of the transformed process 
is the Milstein scheme of the initial one;
in particular, its order of convergence is $1$.

Higher order Taylor expansions (KPS scheme, second Milstein scheme) 
further improve the convergence;
they are comparable to the Runge-Kutta method for ODE.

In some rare cases, 
the conditional distribution $X_t|X_s$ is known
and exact simulation is possible:
for the brownian motion, it coincides with the Euler scheme,
but it is also available 
for the geometric brownian motion 
(GBM, aka Black-and-Scholes model),
the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (Vasicek) process 
and the CIR process.

The Euler scheme replaces the SDE by one 
whose drift and diffusion are constant
(this approximation is constant between any two consecutive steps, 
but it changes at each step):
instead, one can approximate the drift by a linear function of $X$:
the \textbf{Ozaki} (and Shoji-Ozaki, if the drift also depends on time) method 
uses such a local linearization.

\textbf{Diffusion bridges} can be simulated for ergodic processes 
(\ie, those with no ``arrow of time''): 
simulate two paths, one forwards, one backwards, 
and combine them if/when they intersect.

Exact sampling via rejection sampling is alluded to; 
it can be extended to diffusion bridges.

The third chapter addresses the estimation problem: 
you have a discretized sample path from a (time-homogeneous) diffusion process, 
you know (assume) it belongs to some family of SDEs (e.g., geometric brownian motion), 
and you want to estimate the corresponding parameters.
As often, we will only have asymptotic results, in various setups:
large sample ($\Delta$ constant, $T\to\infty$);
high frequency ($\Delta\to0$, $T$ constant);
rapidly increasing ($\Delta\to0$, $T\to\infty$).

In real situations, even though your sample path looks fine 
(two or three parameters, hundreds of observations, the path looks smooth, 
sufficiently rich to present (not one but) many ``patterns'' 
that numerologists/chartists would be eager to interpret)
there is not enough data and the asymptotic results are not valid.

In the ideal ergodic case, 
if we have a complete (continuous, not discretized) path,
we know the invariant distribution;
the (parameters intervening in the) diffusion coefficient can be estimated 
from the quadratic variation
$$ \langle X, X \rangle_t = \int_0^t \sigma^2(X_s,\theta)\,ds; $$
the remaining parameters can be estimated by maximizing the log likelihood 
(the likelihood ratio is the Radon-Nikod\'ym derivative)
$$ \int \dfrac{b}{\sigma^2}dX_s - \dfrac12 \int\dfrac{b^2}{\sigma^2}ds. $$

In the discrete case, you could discretize those continuous estimators 
(they are not that efficient).

You can obtain discrete estimators directly, 
by replacing the integral by a sum -- 
but you have to know the transition probabilities:
it works for the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck model, 
the geometric brownian motion, the CIR model.

If the transition probabilities are not known, 
use one of the discrete schemes used to simulate trajectories of the SDE
(Euler, Milstein, KPS, Ozaki, etc.):
their transition probabilities are known. 
If $\Delta \ll 1$ and $N\Delta \gg 1$, then the Euler scheme is sufficient,
if not, then none of those \textbf{pseudo likelihoods} is satisfactory.

The likelihood of $x_{t+\Delta}|x_t$ can also be estimated by simulating
(with the Euler scheme and a step size $\delta$ smaller than $\Delta$)
trajectories starting at $x_t$.

Various expansions (Taylor, Hermite polynomials, etc.) of the likelihood 
can also be used, leading to \textbf{approximated likelihood} estimators.

Bayesian methods (MCMC simulations) can also be used.

\textbf{Estimating functions} (aka moments) 
are functions $f$ such that $Ef(X,0)=0$ iff $\theta=\theta_0$.
If you choose the function wisely
(e.g., apply the \textbf{infinitesimal generator} of the diffusion to any function $h$),
this is analytically tractable and often consistent and asymtotically gaussian.
You can also use the first and second moments of the transition density:
the resulting estimators are consistent and robust to model misspecification.

The \textbf{generalized method of moments} is almost identical, 
but suggests you use more moments than needed: the model
is overidentified.

The last chapter examines miscellaneous topics. 
The AIC (the log-likelihood, corrected for the dimension of the parameter space) 
could be used to compare models -- but beware: it does not work.

Diffusions are amenable to non-parametric estimation.
For instance, you can estimate the stationary density (with a usual kernel estimator),
estimate the drift parameters (if this part of the process is parametric)
and use the Kolmogorov equations, 
which link stationary distribution, drift and diffusion, 
to estimate the (non-parametric) diffusion.
In a high-frequency setup, 
you can estimate the drift and diffusion as 
Nadaraya-Watson kernel regression estimators
\begin{align*}
  b(x)        &= \lim_{t\to0} \dfrac1tE[X_t-x|X_0=x] \\
  \sigma(x)^2 &= \lim_{t\to0} \dfrac1tE[(X_t-x)^2|X_0=x]. \\
\end{align*}

Change-point models can also be estimated with the Euler scheme.

The book is interesting but unpolished: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item
  Some formulas are wrong (not misleading, just meaningless); 
  others are badly typeset, for instance, 
  the limit in probability is written ``$p$ minus lim'': 
  why not use ``plim'' or ``p-lim'' (with a hyphen and consistent typeface) instead?
\item
  The R code, most of which is available in the \texttt{sde} package
  (check the \texttt{sde.sim}, \texttt{DBridge}, \texttt{dcEuler}, 
  \texttt{ksmooth}, \texttt{cpoint} functions),
  tends to return invisible objects:
  invisible objects are useful for functions called for their side effects 
  and whose return values are rarely used, such as plotting functions; 
  but if the return value is just too complicated or long to be printed, 
  it is less misleading for the end-user to implement a \texttt{print} method;
\item
  Some theoretical notions are missing or confusing: 
  the author talks of weak and strong solutions of a SDE but never defined them;
  the definitions of strong and weak order of convergence look fishy 
  (the weak seems stronger than the strong).
\end{compactitem}

%QUESTIONS: Example of non-diffusions? (jump processes) And with a.s. continuous paths?

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Beautiful Data}\\
  T. Segaran et al.\\
  O'Reilly (2009)  
\end{book}

Empty.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Recent advances in harmony search}\\
  Z.W.Geem et al. (2008)
\end{book}

\textbf{Harmony search} (HS) is a music-inspired optimization algorithm, comparable to genetic algorithms, 
but it does not assume the genes are 1-dimensional:
choose the value of each variable independently (no cross-overs, genes are not linear), 
either at random of from the \emph{harmony memory}, \ie,
the current population (you can end up with more than two parents),
and apply some \emph{pitch adjustment} (mutation).
This can be refined by putting back some dependency between the variables (ensemble HS (EHS))
and/or progressively changing the  parameters 
(e.g., lower the mutation probability, as in simulated annealing: improved HS (IHS)).

There have been some applications in music (organum composition) 
and engineering (designing various structures, such as geodesic domes).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{AI methods for algorithmic composition:\\ a survey, a critical view and future prospects}\\
  G. Papadopoulos and G. Wiggins
\end{book}
The following classes of composition algorithms have been investigated:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Stochastic processes, such as Mozart's dice game 
  (a musical analogue of R.~Queneau's \emph{Cent mille milliards de po\`emes}),
  Markov chains (this requires a corpus, in which melody and rythm are often separated);
  artificial neural networks, machine learning,
  stochastic grammars 
  (which try to reflect large structures but fail to accout for ``ambiguity'' 
  or the 2-dimensional structure of music -- 
  this is also my main criticism of Haskore: 
  musical structures cannot be represented by a tree, 
  we need a directed (but still acyclic) graph);
\item Non-linear systems, iterated functions 
  (but there is no way to evaluate the quality of the result);
\item Knowledge-based systems: constraint satisfaction problems (CSP);
\item Genetic algorithms (GA)
  (since it is almost impossible to design a good fitness function,
  you end up using humans: interactive GA).
\end{compactitem}
Few of those use a geometric model of \emph{pitch space}
(pitches can be arranged in a \emph{lattice}, 
e.g., with thirds in one direction and fifths in the other).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Frankensteinian methods\\ 
  for evolutionary music composition}\\
  P.M. Todd and G.M.Werner
\end{book}
One can also consider \textbf{coevolutionary algorithms}, 
with two populations, of composers and critics.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Group Theory and SAGE: a primer}\\
  R.A.Beezer (2009)
\end{book}
SAGE is a common interface to several computer algebra systems (CAS)
(Gap for group theory, Maxima for symbolic computations, etc.)
but fails to follow the evolution of the components -- 
that would be easy if they had an exentensive test suite, 
such as all the computations in one of the many online documentstions.

For finite groups (mainly subgroups of $\mathfrak S_n$ for $n \leq 50$),
it looked fine, but symbolic computations (Maxima) and plotting (MatPlotLib)
are broken (this may be Ubuntu-specific).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Adventures in group theory:\\
  Rubik's cube, Merlin's machine\\
  and other mathematical toys}\\
  D. Joyner (2008)
\end{book}

Applied group theory with Sage.

To solve the Rubik's cube
(many other puzzles are covered): 
try to find sequences of moves 
(of the form $aba^{-1}$ (conjugation by internal automorphism), 
$aba^{-1}b^{-1}$ (commutator), $(ab)^n$)
that keep a lot of the facets unchanged;
use them to first put the corners in the right places,
then the edges, 
then the corners in the right orientations,
then the edges.
This can be formalized as a decomposition 
$ g_0 = g_1 g_2 g_3 g_4 $
where $g_0 \in G$ is the permutation 
representing the current configuration;
$$ \{1\} = G_4 \subset G_3 \subset G_2 \subset G_1 \subset G; $$
$G_1$ is the subgroup that fixes the corner positions;
$G_2$, the subgroup that fixes the corner and edge positions;
$G_3$, the subgroup that fixes the corner and edge positions,
and the corner orientations;
\begin{align*}
  g_0 & \in g_1 G_1 \\
  g_1^{-1} g_0 & \in g_2 G_2 \\
  g_2^{-1} g_1^{-1} g_0 & \in g_3 G_3 \\
  g_3^{-1} g_2^{-1} g_1^{-1} g_0 & \in g_4 G_4.
\end{align*}

The Merlin machine 
(an $n \times m$ array of buttons with lights;
pressing a button switches its light and that of its neighbours;
your goal is to switch all lights on (or off) 
from an arbitrary initial position)
is a matrix inversion problem:
the initial state $b$ is a vector if size $nm$
(with coefficients in $\ZZ/2\ZZ$);
the possible moves are also vectors of size $nm$,
which can be arranged into a matrix $A$;
solving the puzzle is equivalent to solving $Ax=b$
(this is not always possible: $A$ need not be invertible).

The \d{Cayley graph} of a group with prescribed generators
$ G = \langle g_1, \dots, g_n \rangle $
has the elements of $G$ as vertices
and an edge $x \to y$ if $y = g_i x$ for some generator $g_i$.
A solution to a puzzle is 
a path in the Cayley graph of its group. 
The diameter of the Cayley graph
gives (a bound on) the minimum number of moves 
required to solve the puzzle.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Differential calculus and Sage}\\
  W. Granville and D. Joyner
\end{book}
Sage relies on Maxima, which is woefully unstable
(but Maxima is written in Lisp: how can they get a segmentation fault in Lisp?).

Sage notebooks are web-based equivalents of Maple's workbooks:
you can use \LaTeX{} for mathematical expressions;
they rely on Twisted for the access from a web browser.

Sage functions can be written in Python
(but, of course, Python function cannot be symbolically differentiated).

Here is some sample code (press \textsc{tab} after the name of a function
such as \texttt{expand} or \texttt{simplify}): 
\begin{verbatim}
  x=var('x')
  integral(x^2,x,0,1)
  n(integral(x^2,x,0,1))
  integrate(x^2,x)
  a,b,c,d,e,f,x,y=var('a,b,c,d,e,f,x,y')
  solve(ax^2+bx+x==0,x)
  solve([ax+by+c==0,dx+ey+f==0],x,y)
  tan?
  diff(sin(x),x,4)
  partial_fraction(1/(1-x^2),x)
  limit(1/t,t=Infinity)
  limit(1/t,t=0,dir="plus")
  show(plot(x^2,-2,2))
  maxima("sum(3*k+1,k,2,5)")
  sum([f(i) for i in range(20)])
\end{verbatim}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Sage for Newbies}\\
  T. Kosan (2008)
\end{book}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Integral calculus and Sage}\\
  D. Hoffman et al. (2009)
\end{book}

A calculus book relying on Sage:
most of the code is actually Python
and the pictures are awful (low resolution, lossily-compressed bitmaps, 
scaled by a different (random?) and ever changing factor 
in the horizontal and vertical directions).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Asymptote: the vector graphics language}\\
\end{book}

Asymptote is a variant of Metapost
(a language to describe and produce PDF figures 
to be included in \LaTeX{} files) 
with a C++-like syntax:
semi-colons, parentheses around function arguments;
standard data types (real, triple, vector, matrix, string);
standard libraries (operations on strings, arrays, vectors, matrices -- 
including mathematical operations such as matrix inversion or the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT));
plotting libraries (any kind of plot with axes, even with logarithmic or broken scales, 
3-dimensional plots, diagrams, etc.);
ability to include code directly in your \LaTeX{} files 
(in the \texttt{asy} environment; use the \texttt{asy} command to produce the plots).
The labels are typeset in \LaTeX{}, with some Unicode if needed
(cyrillic fonts, Asian languages through the old \texttt{CJK} package -- 
no mention of \XeTeX).
They seem to have kept all the good features of Metapost, \emph{except} the constraint-solving capabilities -- 
but given the emphasis on ``scientific'' (\ie, numeric) plots, this may not be an issue.

For an extensive gallery of examples, 
including many showing that the constraint-solving feature was not that important,
check \url{http://www.piprime.fr/asymptote}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Primes in P}\\
  M. Agrawal et al.
\end{book}

There exists a polynomial algorithm to determine whether a number is prime.
The algorithm is very simple (but not that fast: $O((\log n)^{21/2})$)
and based on a generalization of Fermat's little theorem: 
$$
    a \text{ prime} \iff (X+a)^n \equiv X^n + a \ (n)
$$
for $a \in \ZZ$, $n \in \NN$, $n \geq 2$, $a \wedge n = 1$.

The complexity classes are the following: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item P: there exists a polynomial algorithm to determine if a number is prime
\item co-NP: to prove that a number is not prime, 
  it suffices to provide a ``non-primality certificate'' 
  (the decomposition into a product of prime factors); 
  the verification (product) can be done in polynomial time; 
\item NP: to prove that a number is prime, 
  it suffices to provide a certificate 
  from which one can check that the number is indeed prime 
  (since Prime is P, the certificate can be empty);
\item NP-Complete: a problem is NP-complete 
  if all NP problems can be reduced to it in polynomial time.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of\\
  random forest and its Gini importance\\
  with standard chemometric methods\\
  for the feature selection and classification of spectral data}\\
  B.H. Menze et al\\
  BMC Bioinformatics (2009)
\end{book}

Regularized methods can tackle large numbers of predictors, 
but do not identify and discard irrelevant variables 
(variables are often called ``features''):
in chemometry, one often applies them after some feature selection algorithm,
e.g., with the \emph{random forest Gini importance}
(total change in entropy brought by a given variable 
or set of variables in a random forest).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Planet: massively parallel learning\\ of tree ensembles with MapReduce}\\
  B. Panda et al.\\
  VLDB 2009
\end{book}

Classification trees or regression trees (a ``common data mining task" at Google) can be parallelized.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A course in credibility theory\\ and its applications}\\
  H. B\"uhlmann and A. Gisler\\
  Springer Verlag (2005)
\end{book}

On the use of bayesian models, or more generally hierarchical models, 
in actuarial science (\ie. ``insurance'') -- 
they use a different vocabulary: 
``credibility theory'' means ``hierarchical model''.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamical bias in the coin toss}\\
\end{book}

Coin tossing is not random: with probability 0.51, the coin lands as it started.

Coin spinning is even worse: depending on the age of the coin, the probability of tails can be as high as 0.9.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Tag cloud drawing:\\ algorithms for cloud visualization}\\
  O. Kaser and D. Lemire
\end{book}

Tag clouds, in most websites, are often just an alphabetic list of words of varying sizes,
with no attention given to esthetics, ease of use (the alphabetic order is far from optimal)
or space usage. Here are a few ideas to improve them: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Keep the alphabetic order but use a decent 
  (\TeX-like, dynamic-programming-based algorithm
  (compute the badness $b_{ij}$ of a line containing words $i$ to $j$)
  instead of the greedy, Word-like one) 
  line-breaking algorithm;
  you may want to shuffle the words a dozen times and keep the best layout;
\item Sort the tags by decreasing height and add them one by one, 
  greedily, to the first available line; create a new line if needed;
\item Take the relations between the tags into account (model them as a weighted graph)
  and use some heuristic from the \emph{placement problem} 
  in EDA (Electronic Design Automation), 
  e.g.,  force-directed methods (view the graph edges as springs),
  simulated annealing,
  or heuristics for the (NP-hard) \emph{min-cut placement} problem,
  which recursively cuts the set of tags in two (left/right or top/bottom)
  so that the cut size (total weight of the edges between the two parts; 
  also add the influence of external edges) be small
  and the bipartition balanced;
  the result can be implemented with nested HTML tables 
  (but it looks very tabular).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Using trees to depict a forest}\\
  B. Liu and H.V. Jagadish\\
  VLDB 2009
\end{book}
When a database query returns too many results, 
instead of ranking them, 
present a ``representative'' subset, obtained by clustering.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A demonstration of SciDB:\\ a science-oriented DBMS}\\
  VLDB 2009
\end{book}

Scientific database applications have the following requirements:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Non-tabular data-types: 
  $n$-dimensional arrays, ragged arrays, nested arrays, sequences, graphs, meshes;
\item \textbf{Provenance} tracking, 
  snapshots (``named versions'', \ie, you can modify the data 
  without tampering with other users' work and without having to copy it), 
  no-overrite storage manager (just an ``update time'' dimension);
\item Grid storage (because of the volume of data: 100 tetabytes);
\item Ability to work on data without having to load it (HDF-5, NetCDF file formats);
\item Database operations (join, etc.) should account for imprecision, 
  e.g., by 
  adding a ``precision'' to a column and 
  considering values differing by less than this threshold as equal; 
  more elaborate models could assume the data in a column is gaussian, 
  estimate its mean and variance, and output results with a confidence interval;
\item User-defined functions (e.g., in C++).
\end{compactitem}
SciDB does not exist: it is just a wishlist.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{ConvergenceConcepts: an R package to
  investigate various modes of convergence}\\
  P. Lafaye de Micheaux and B. Liquet\\
  R Journal (2009)
\end{book}

If you have a sequence of random variables, 
or random number generators
(or several samples -- 
it may even work with a single path, 
if it is sufficiently ``representative'' and ``ergodic''),
you can study their convergence as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Plot several samples paths $(X_n)_n$ and check if they leave $[-\epsilon,\epsilon]$ for $n>N$:
  if most do not, then $X_n \to 0$ a.s.;
\item Estimate the proportion of paths that leave this interval,
  $$ a_N = P[ \exists n \geq N \ :\ \va{ X_n } > \epsilon ], $$
  if it converges towards zero (in most (textbook) examples, it is really clear-cut), 
  the sequence converges almost surely;
\item In the plot of the sample paths, 
  instead of the $\left[N,+\infty\right[\times[-\epsilon,\epsilon]$ infinite rectangle,
  just consider the narrower $\{n\}\times[-\epsilon,\epsilon]$ rectangle:
  if the proportion of paths outside it 
  $$ p_N = P[ \va{x_N} > \epsilon ] $$
  decreases to 0 as $N$ increases, 
  there is convergence in probability;
\item For the convergence in law, you can plot the cumulative distribution function (cdf)
  and check if it moves towards the supposed limit;
  one could also use an animated quantile-quantile plot (or ppplot, or histogram, etc.)
  or a plot of the $\va{F_n(x)-F(x)}\sim x+n$ surface;
\item For $L^p$ convergence, just compute an estimator of 
  $E\va{X_n-X}^r$,
  $$ \hat e_n = \dfrac1{\va\Omega}\sum_\omega\va{x_n(\omega)-x(\omega)}^r. $$
\end{compactitem}
The GUI is ugly but interactive.

\begin{center}
<<NotionsOfConvergence,echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
cex <- 1.3
op <- par(mfrow=c(2,2),mar=c(0,0,0,0),oma=.1*c(1,1,1,1))
curve(1/x^2,xlim=c(1,5),ylim=c(0,1.2),axes=F,xlab="",ylab="",lwd=3,col="blue")
box()
text(3,1,"Almost Sure Convergence to 0",cex=cex)

epsilon <- .05
x <- seq(1e-4,5,by=1e-4)
jumps <- ifelse(abs(x-round(x))<epsilon, abs(x-round(x))/epsilon, 0 )
y <- (20*(x-1))^.5
jumps <- ifelse(abs(y-round(y))<epsilon, 1-abs(y-round(y))/epsilon, 0 )
plot(x, 1/x^2 + pmax(.1,.3*(x-1)) * jumps, type="l", lwd=3, col="blue", ylim=c(0,1.2), xlim=c(1,5), xlab="", ylab="", axes=F )
box()
text(3,1.05,"Convergence in probability",cex=cex)
text(3,.9,"The peaks are rarer and rarer",cex=cex)

f <- function(samples, n) {
  ifelse( sample(c(0,1),samples,replace=TRUE), rnorm(samples, -2/n^.5), rnorm(samples, 2/n^.5) )
}
N <- 5
plot(NA,type="n",xlim=c(1,N+.8),ylim=c(-5,5),xlab="",ylab="",axes=FALSE)
box()
for(i in 1:N) {
  r <- density( f(1e3, 2*i), probability=TRUE )
  polygon( i+2*r$y, r$x, col="light blue" )
  lines( i+2*dnorm(r$x), r$x, col="blue", lty=2, lwd=3 )
}
text((N+1)/2,4,"Convergence in law",cex=cex)

plot(x, 1/x^2 + pmin(.2,1/(x-1)) * jumps, type="l", lwd=3, col="blue", ylim=c(0,1.2), xlim=c(1,5), xlab="", ylab="", axes=F )
box()
text(3,1,"Convergence L^p",cex=cex)
text(3,.85,"The peaks are not growing higher",cex=cex)
par(op)
@
\end{center}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://faculty.washington.edu/ezivot/econ583/convergenceConcepts.pdf}%
  {\emph{Understanding convergence concepts: \\
  a visual-minded and
  graphical\\ simulation-based approach}}\\
  P. Lafaye de Micheaux and B. Liquet\\
  The American Statistician (2009)
\end{book}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{asympTest: 
  a simple R package \\
  for classical parametric statistical tests\\
  and confidence intervals in large samples}\\
  J.-F. Coeurjolly et al.\\
  R Journal (2009)
\end{book}

Mean comparison tests are robust to non-gaussianity,
but variance comparison tests
(the 1-sample $\chi^2$ variance test;
or the 2-sample Fisher ratio test, \texttt{var.test})
are not, even for large samples.

\begin{center}
<<VarianceComparisonTestsRobustness,echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
Replications <- 500 
Sample_Size  <- 1e4
p <- matrix(NA,nr=Replications,nc=4)
colnames(p) <- c("Gaussian","Student, df=4","Uniform","Exponential")
for(i in 1:Replications) {
  x <- rnorm(Sample_Size, mean = 0, sd = 1)
  y <- rnorm(Sample_Size, mean = 0, sd = 1)
  x <- rt(Sample_Size,df=4)
  y <- rt(Sample_Size,df=4)
  p[i,"Gaussian"     ] <- var.test( rnorm(Sample_Size),   rnorm(Sample_Size)   )$p.value
  p[i,"Student, df=4"] <- var.test( rt(Sample_Size,df=4), rt(Sample_Size,df=4) )$p.value
  p[i,"Uniform"      ] <- var.test( runif(Sample_Size),   runif(Sample_Size)   )$p.value
  p[i,"Exponential"  ] <- var.test( rexp(Sample_Size),    rexp(Sample_Size)    )$p.value
}
for(i in 1:dim(p)[2])
  p[,i] <- sort(p[,i])
matplot(ppoints(Replications),p,type="l",lty=1,las=1,lwd=3,
  xlab="Theoretical p-value",ylab="Computed p-value",main="Variance comparison tests are not robust (N=10,000)")
abline(0,1,lty=3)
legend("topleft",colnames(p),col=1:dim(p)[2],lty=1,lwd=3,bg="white")
@
\end{center}

Robust tests and confidence intervals can be obtained 
by considering asymptotically gaussian quantities such as 
$$ \dfrac{S_n^2 - \sigma^2}{\hat\sigma_{S_n^2}}. $$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{copas: An R package\\ 
  for fitting the Copas selection model}\\
  J. Carpenter et al.\\
  R Journal (2009)
\end{book}

The Copas model accounts for publication bias
(more significant or smaller studies (with more extreme results) 
are more likely to be published)
and is used to improve meta-analyses in medical studies.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/}{Use R 2009 Conference}}
\end{book}

\textbf{Examples} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Year after year, there are fewer and fewer examples 
that boil down to a simple linear regression: 
they are replaced by 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Cheng+Sheu.pdf}%
{non-linear} (sometimes non-parametric) 
and/or hierarchical linear (or 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Hofner+Hothorn+Kneib.pdf}%
{survival}, logictic, 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Allignol+Schumacher+Beyersmann.pdf}%
{Markov},
spatial, even 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Ferreira+Bertolde+Holan.pdf}%
{spatio-temporal multiscale} (country/micro-region/region))
penalized models, sometimes with added boosting or a bayesian prior;
some presenters use the
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Sharp+Friskin+Hosking+Logie.pdf}%
{bootstrap} to have more robust confidence intervals.

Examples included
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Non-linear models in 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Radivoyevitch.pdf}%
  {biochemistry},
  to estimate 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Harbron.pdf}%
  {drug interactions},
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Theussl+Feinerer+Hornik.pdf}%
  {dose-response}
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Thorin+Mallem+Noireaud+Desfontis.pdf}%
  {curves};
\item \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Katina.pdf}{Shape analysis};
\item Text mining (for 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Friedrich+Gundel.pdf}%
  {gene names} in biology);
\item \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Marot+Foulley+Mayer+Jaffrezic.pdf}%
  {Meta-analysis}
  (aggregating several studies, e.g., with microarray data);
\item Time series 
  (building a business cycle (recession) indicator from NBER data: 
  transform and/or filter the data, 
  use non-linear and/or 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Fontdecaba+SanchezEspigares+Munoz.pdf}%
  {Markov switching} (MS) models);
\item Forecasting multivariate time series 
  (to optimize a \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Despagne.pdf}%
  {frozen goods supply chain}
  or forecast 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Walz+Ziemer+Amberti.pdf}%
  {electricity comsumption});
\item \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/LeRay+Molto+Husson.pdf}{Multivariate analysis} 
  (with \texttt{FactoMineR}); 
  % 2 examples  (ade4, vegan were hardly mentionned)
\item 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Gromping.pdf}%
  {Design of experiments} (DOE),
  survey design
  (compute the sample size, in each stratum, 
  for a given desired precision, to estimate 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/VanIseghem+Demaneche+Daures+Leblond.pdf}%
  {fisheries revenues})
\item Subject randomization system
  (subjects for a randomized trial arrive one by one; 
  we then learn in which strata (gender, age, etc.) they are;
  we decide to give them treatment A or B by flipping a coin;
  we can hasten the convergence 
  by keeping a running difference $n_A - n_B$ for each stratum 
  and using a 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Henry+Wood+Narasimhan.pdf}%
  {biased coin} to redress any unbalance).
\end{compactitem}

%\begin{verbatim}
%  R+Eclipse for reporting
%        http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Chard+Gibbs+Dunn+Shao.pdf
%  Non-linear models (there were better examples)
%        http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Haldermans+Shkedy.pdf
