%\end{verbatim}

The large-scale 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Wijffels.pdf}%
{ThomasCook} example was interesting: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item They store the data in an SQLite table 
  (modified to hold 30,000 columns --
  with PL/R, they had ended up writing
  R code that called \href{http://www.joeconway.com/plr/}{PostgreSQL} that called R: 
  it was a nightmare to debug);
  %but a \emph{column store} might be a better idea); [NO: it would be for few columns and many rows]
\item They use R for ETL (Extract, Transform, Load), 
  with some Python (BeautifulSoup) for screenscrapping;
\item
  \texttt{glmpath} to select variables;
\item
  \texttt{randomForest} (on the selected variables) 
  to predict the speed at which the planes will fill;
\item
  The prices are optimized with the random forest model;
\item
  They add a fuzzy inference engine (based on the \texttt{sets} package) 
  to prevent canibalization (you lose clients to yourself) 
  and allow the business to add their imput to the model;
\item
  They provide a graphical interface with wxPython/RPy2 
  (and py2exe to create Windows executables 
  that do not require Python on the clients);
  R remained on a central server 
  (some call it SaaS: Software as a Service).
\end{compactitem}

\medskip
\textbf{Plots} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\textbf{Kaleidoscope plots} are those impressive, 
dense, polar coordinate plots,
judiciously using transparent colours,
you sometimes see in bioinformatics, 
often produced by 
\href{http://mkweb.bcgsc.ca/circos/}{Circos} 
(GPL, unrelated to R).
There was one on the title page of 
\href{http://www.renci.org/wp-content/pub/tutorials/BeautifulCode.pdf}{Beautiful code, Compelling Evidence},
which explains how to use Haskell and OpenGL to produce graphics.
The basic idea stems from the graph layout problem: 
if you are completely clueless about how to plot a graph,
just put the vertices in a circle; 
if the graph is weighted, draw the edges as varying-width ribbons;

\begin{center}
<<KaleidoscopePlot1,echo=FALSE,results='hide',fig.width=6,fig.height=6>>=
# Return a line in the Poincare disk, \ie,
# a circle arc, perpendicular to the unit circle, through two given points.
poincare_segment <- function(u1, u2, v1, v2) {
    # Check that the points are sufficiently different
    if( abs(u1-v1) < 1e-6 && abs(u2-v2) < 1e-6 )
        return( list(x=c(u1,v1), y=c(u2,v2)) )
    # Check that we are in the circle
    stopifnot( u1^2 + u2^2 - 1 <= 1e-6 )
    stopifnot( v1^2 + v2^2 - 1 <= 1e-6 )
    # Check it is not a diameter
    if( abs( u1*v2 - u2*v1 ) < 1e-6 )
        return( list(x=c(u1,v1), y=c(u2,v2)) )
    # Equation of the line: x^2 + y^2 + ax + by + 1 = 0 (circles orthogonal to the unit circle)
    a <- ( u2 * (v1^2+v2^2) - v2 * (u1^2+u2^2) + u2 - v2 ) / ( u1*v2 - u2*v1 )
    b <- ( u1 * (v1^2+v2^2) - v1 * (u1^2+u2^2) + u1 - v1 ) / ( u2*v1 - u1*v2 ) # Swap 1's and 2's
    # Center and radius of the circle
    cx <- -a/2
    cy <- -b/2
    radius <- sqrt( (a^2+b^2)/4 - 1 )
    # Which portion of the circle should we draw?
    theta1 <- atan2( u2-cy, u1-cx )
    theta2 <- atan2( v2-cy, v1-cx )
    if( theta2 - theta1 > pi )
        theta2 <- theta2 - 2 * pi
    else if( theta2 - theta1 < - pi )
        theta2 <- theta2 + 2 * pi
    theta <- seq( theta1, theta2, length=100 )
    x <- cx + radius * cos( theta )
    y <- cy + radius * sin( theta )
    list( x=x, y=y )
}

par(mar=c(1,1,2,1))
plot(0,0, type="n", axes=FALSE, main="A directed graph", xlab="", ylab="")
radius_start <- 1
radius_end   <- .95
n <- 20
colours <- rainbow(n, alpha=.3)
half_width <- .1
p <- .05
d <- matrix(sample(0:1, n*n, replace=TRUE, p=c(1-p,p)), nr=n, nc=n)
theta <- seq(0, 2*pi, length=n+1)[1:n]
for (i in 1:n) {
    th <- seq( theta[i] - half_width, theta[i] + half_width, length=20 )
    polygon( 
        c(1.01 * cos(th), rev(1.05 * cos(th))),
        c(1.01 * sin(th), rev(1.05 * sin(th))),
        col=rgb(t(col2rgb(colours[i])/255)),  # Opaque
        border=NA
    )
}
for(from in 1:n) {
    for(to in 1:n) {
        if( d[from,to] && to != from ) {
            r1 <- poincare_segment(
                radius_start * cos(theta[from]-half_width), radius_start * sin(theta[from]-half_width), 
                radius_end * cos(theta[to]),              radius_end   * sin(theta[to]) 
            )
            r2 <- poincare_segment(
                radius_start * cos(theta[from]+half_width), radius_start * sin(theta[from]+half_width), 
                radius_end * cos(theta[to]),              radius_end   * sin(theta[to]) 
            )
            th <- seq( theta[from] - half_width, theta[from] + half_width, length=20 )
            polygon(
                c( radius_start * cos(th), r1$x, rev(r2$x) ),
                c( radius_start * sin(th), r1$y, rev(r2$y) ),
                col=colours[from], border=NA
            )
        }
    }
}
@
\end{center}

finally remark that a contingency table is a (bipartite: rows and columns) 
weighted graph

\begin{center}
<<KaleidoscopePlot2,echo=FALSE,results='hide',fig.width=6,fig.height=2>>=
## Non-polar example
n <- 3 # First dimension
m <- 2 # Second dimension
d <- matrix( c(.5,.3,.2, .1,.4,.5), nr=n, nc=m )
#n <- 10
#m <- 7
#d <- matrix( abs(rnorm(n*m)), nr=n, nc=m )
colour <- matrix( 
    sample( c(rgb(1,0,0,.3), rgb(0,1,0,.3), rgb(0,0,1,.3)), n*m, replace=TRUE), 
    nr=n, nc=m 
)
border <- ifelse( is.na(d),NA,NA)
w1 <- apply(d,1, sum)
w2 <- apply(d,2, sum)
w1 <- w1/sum(w1)
w2 <- w2/sum(w2)
ww1 <- d / apply(d,1,sum)
ww2 <- t(t(d)) / apply(d,2,sum)
f <- function(x) c(0,cumsum(x)[-length(x)])
A <- f(w1) + t(apply(ww1*w1,1,f))
B <- f(w1) + t(apply(ww1*w1,1,cumsum))
C <- t( f(w2) + t(apply(t(ww2)*w2,1,f)) )
D <- t( f(w2) + t(apply(t(ww2)*w2,1,cumsum)) )

par(mar=c(1,1,2,1))
plot(0,0, xlim=c(0,1), ylim=c(-.1,1.1), type="n", axes=FALSE, main="Two qualitative variables", xlab="", ylab="")
for(from in 1:n) {
    for (to in 1:m) {
        polygon(
            c(A[from,to], B[from,to], D[from,to], C[from,to]),
            c(1,1,0,0),
            col=rainbow(n,alpha=.3)[from], #colour[from,to],
            border=border[from,to]
        )
    }
}
#segments( f(w1), 1, cumsum(w1), 1, col=rainbow(n,alpha=1), lwd=10 )
#segments( f(w2), 0, cumsum(w2), 0, col=gray(ppoints(m)),   lwd=10 )
for(i in 1:n)
  polygon( 
    c( f(w1)[i],  f(w1)[i], cumsum(w1)[i], cumsum(w1)[i] ),
    1 + .1 * c(0,1,1,0),
    col=rainbow(n,alpha=1)[i]
  )
for(i in 1:m)
  polygon( 
    c( f(w2)[i],  f(w2)[i], cumsum(w2)[i], cumsum(w2)[i] ),
    0 - .1 * c(0,1,1,0),
    col=gray(ppoints(m))[i]
  )
@
\end{center}

and draw it in polar coordinates 
(pay attention to the order of the segments and the ribbons,
label the segments, 
add axes).
Use it if you want artistic plots
(that make the user wonder what the plot is about)
or if your data is too large to be amenable to more traditional plots.

\begin{center}
<<KaleidoscopePlot3,echo=FALSE,results='hide',fig.width=6,fig.height=6>>=
## Polar example
library(RColorBrewer)
n <- 10
m <- 7
segment_weight <- abs(rnorm(n))
segment_weight <- segment_weight / sum(segment_weight)
d <- matrix(abs(rnorm(n*n)),nr=n, nc=n)
diag(d) <- 0 # No loops allowed
# The weighted graph comes from two quantitative variables
d[1:m,1:m] <- 0
d[(m+1):n,(m+1):n] <- 0
ribbon_weight <- t(d) / apply(d,2,sum) # The sum of each row is 1; use as ribbon_weight[from,to]
ribbon_order <- t(apply(d,2,function(...)sample(1:n))) # Each row contains sample(1:n); use as ribbon_order[from,i]
segment_colour <- rainbow(n)
segment_colour <- brewer.pal(n,"Set3")
transparent_segment_colour <- rgb(t(col2rgb(segment_colour)/255),alpha=.5)
ribbon_colour <- matrix(rainbow(n*n), nr=n, nc=n) # Not used, actually...
ribbon_colour[1:m,(m+1):n] <- transparent_segment_colour[1:m]
ribbon_colour[(m+1):n,1:m] <- t(ribbon_colour[1:m,(m+1):n])
gap <- .01
x <- c( segment_weight[1:m], gap, segment_weight[(m+1):n], gap )
x <- x / sum(x)
x <- cumsum(x)
segment_start <- c(0,x[1:m-1],x[(m+1):n])
segment_end   <- c(x[1:m],x[(m+2):(n+1)])
start1 <- start2 <- end1 <- end2 <- ifelse(is.na(ribbon_weight),NA,NA)
x <- 0
for (from in 1:n) {
  x <- segment_start[from]
  for (i in 1:n) {
    to <- ribbon_order[from,i]
    y <- x + ribbon_weight[from,to] * ( segment_end[from] - segment_start[from] )
    if( from < to ) {
      start1[from,to] <- x
      start2[from,to] <- y
    } else if( from > to ) {
      end1[to,from] <- x
      end2[to,from] <- y
    } else {
      # no loops allowed
    }
    x <- y
  }
}

par(mar=c(1,1,2,1))
plot(
  0,0, 
  xlim=c(-1,1),ylim=c(-1,1), type="n", axes=FALSE, 
  main="Two qualitative variables in polar coordinates", xlab="", ylab="")
for(from in 1:n) {
  for(to in 1:n) {
    if(from<to) {
      u <- start1[from,to]
      v <- start2[from,to]
      x <- end1  [from,to]
      y <- end2  [from,to]
      if(!is.na(u*v*x*y)) {
            r1 <- poincare_segment( cos(2*pi*v), sin(2*pi*v), cos(2*pi*x), sin(2*pi*x) )
            r2 <- poincare_segment( cos(2*pi*y), sin(2*pi*y), cos(2*pi*u), sin(2*pi*u) )
            th1 <- 2*pi*seq(u,v,length=20)
            th2 <- 2*pi*seq(x,y,length=20)
            polygon(
                c( cos(th1), r1$x, rev(cos(th2)), r2$x ),
                c( sin(th1), r1$y, rev(sin(th2)), r2$y ),
                col=transparent_segment_colour[from], border=NA
            )
      }
    }
  }
}
for(i in 1:n) {
  theta <- 2*pi*seq(segment_start[i], segment_end[i], length=100)
  r1 <- 1
  r2 <- 1.05
  polygon( 
    c( r1*cos(theta), rev(r2*cos(theta)) ),
    c( r1*sin(theta), rev(r2*sin(theta)) ),
    col=segment_colour[i], border="black"
  )
}
@

\end{center}

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Theus.pdf}%
{\textbf{Visual analytics}} are
statistical graphics made by artists or good communicators:
they should capture the audience's attention 
and convey the speaker's ideas.
The most salient such example is 
\href{http://www.ted.com/talks/hans_rosling_shows_the_best_stats_you_ve_ever_seen.html}%
{GapMinder}
(a flash-based application 
to display multivariate time series, as animations)
and its narrative power
(it helps you efficiently tell a story,
but might not be the best tool to \emph{discover} that story).
As statisticians, we can try to 
reduce the information displayed to what is relevant
and add model information 
(distribution, prior, expected values under H$_0$, etc.,
for instance a map of cholera cases 
can be complemented by well positions 
and their Voronoi tessellation).
R provides graphics for diagnostics, 
presentation (but no animations or interactivity), 
and not much for data exploration (iplot, iplot eXtreme, ggobi) -- 
redundant functions do not help 
(for instance, you can draw a histogram in any of the following ways: 
\texttt{hist}, \texttt{truehist}, \texttt{histogram}, 
\texttt{qplot(...,geom="histogram")}, \texttt{iplot}).

An \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Porzak.pdf}%
{\textbf{information dashboard}} (ID) 
presents all the information needed in a single, easy-to-read page;
it can use bargraphs, stacked bargraphs, linegraphs, 
scatterplots, boxplots, sparklines, treemaps,
\href{http://www.perceptualedge.com/articles/misc/Bullet_Graph_Design_Spec.pdf}%
{\textbf{bullet graphs}}
(a thick horizontal line from the origin to represent a single value, 
with a tick for a reference point (target, last year's value, etc.),
and a coloured background to represent how good the value is 
(3 values, from dark (bad) to light (good) grey; do not rely on colours);
do not forget a textual label, with units (smaller), and a scale)

\begin{center}
<<BulletPlot,echo=FALSE,results='hide',fig.height=1.5>>=
  bullet <- function( value, target, xlim, ranges, name="", unit="" ) {
    plot.new()
    plot.window(xlim=xlim, ylim=c(0,1))
    axis(1)
    greys <- gray(c(50,70,85,92,97)/100 )
    for( i in length(ranges):2 ) {
      rect( 0, 0, ranges[i], 1, border=NA, col=greys[i-1] )
    }
    segments( target, .25, target, .75, lwd=3 )
    rect( 0, .4, value, .6, border=NA, col="black" )  
    op <- par(xpd=NA)
    text(-5, .6, name, adj=c(1,0), font=2, cex=1.2 ) # Should not be hard-coded...
    text(-5, .1, unit, adj=c(1,0), col=gray(.1) )
    par(op)
  }
  par(mar=c(3,5,1,1))
  bullet( 270, xlim=c(0,300), target = 230, ranges = c(0, 100, 200, 300), 
    name="Bullet Plot", unit="arbitrary units" )
@
\end{center}

or \href{http://www.perceptualedge.com/blog/?p=390}{\textbf{horizon plots}}
(to plot a time series, 
represent both positive and negative values above the axis, 
with a different color (blue/red),
put the possible values into three bands, of darker colours, and overlay them)
-- no \href{http://www.perceptualedge.com/articles/08-21-07.pdf}{piecharts}.

\begin{center}
<<HorizonPlot,echo=FALSE,results='hide',fig.width=6,fig.height=4>>=
set.seed(2)
library(RColorBrewer)
f <- function(x,low,high)
ifelse(x>=0,ifelse(x>=low&low>=0,pmin(high,x)-low,0),ifelse(x<=high&high<=0,high-pmax(x,low),0))
colours <- brewer.pal(7,"RdYlBu")[-4]
N <- 1000
bounds <- c(-9,-6,-3,0,3,6,9) * sqrt(N/100)
op<- par(mfrow=c(5,1),mar=c(0,0,0,0),oma=.1*c(1,1,1,1))
for(i in 1:5) {
  x <- cumsum(rnorm(N))
  x <- x - x[N] * seq(0,1,length=N) # Brownian bridges
  plot(NA,xlim=c(0,N),ylim=c(0,max(diff(bounds))),axes=FALSE)
  for(i in c(4,5,6, 3,2,1)) 
    polygon( c(1,1:length(x),length(x)), c(0,f(x,bounds[i],bounds[i+1]),0), col=colours[i], border=NA)
  box()
}
par(op)
@
\end{center}

The \texttt{iPlot} package adds \textbf{interactive graphics} to R, 
but cannot be used for large datasets: 
there is no OpenGL in Java and 
the data is duplicated between R and the JVM.
The soon-to-be-released 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Urbanek.pdf}%
{iPlot eXtreme}, in C++, 
should address those problems.

The 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Hothorn+Zeileis.pdf}%
{\texttt{PairViz}} package uses \texttt{graph} and \texttt{igraph}
to order the variables in a \textbf{parallel coordinate plot} 
using graph-based algorithms (hamiltonial or eulerian path);
this can be combined with \textbf{scagnostics} 
if there are many variables.

R can be linked to external visual exploratory analysis tool: 
ggobi, 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Boubela+Filzmoser+Piringer.pdf}%
{Visplore} (opensource, but early alpha), etc.

To plot the results of a 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Leisch.pdf}%
{clustering}, 
\begin{verbatim}
  bwplot( value ~ variable | cluster )
\end{verbatim}
you can add a reference point (the global average of each variable), 
fine-tune the order of the clusters, of the variables,
highlight important variables 
(or grey out unimportant ones -- this will depend on the cluster). 
% Interesting plots; uses: flexclust, gcExplorer, symbol, flexmix

\medskip
\textbf{Statistical Tests} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\textbf{Multiple testing} often assumes the tests are independent 
and then controls the \emph{family-wise error rate} 
(FWER, probability of having at least one wrongly rejected null hypothesis),
the \emph{false discovery rate}
(FDR, proportion of wrongly rejected null hypotheses)
or the \emph{false non-discovery rate}
(FNDR, proportion of wrongly non-rejected null hypotheses).
If you simulate data similar to yours, 
you can have an idea of the distribution of the FDR and FNDR
and see how it changes as the correlation of the data increases.
It is possible to include some knowledge of the corelation structure 
into the testing procedure: 
these ``factor-adjusted test statistics'', 
implemented in the \texttt{FAMT} package 
(\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Kloareg+Friguet+Causeur.pdf}%
{factor analysis for multiple testing}),
can improve both FDR and NDR.

When estimating a \textbf{$p$-value} 
by Monte Carlo simulations (bootstrap, permutations, etc.), 
some people do not want the $p$-value 
but are happy with just knowing if $p \leq \alpha$
for some predefined threshold $\alpha$.
Because of this, we can do with slightly less data 
(than if we wanted $p$, \ie, all values of $\alpha$)
by looking at the path 
$(\sum_{1 \leq i\leq n} T_i)_{n\geq1}$
of partial sums of the test statistic
and checking if it remains in or leaves 
some box- or tunnel-shaped region
(\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Gandy.pdf}%
{sequential Monte Carlo $p$-values}).

The \texttt{car::Anova} function implements 
nested 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Monette+Fox.pdf}%
{\textbf{linear tests}}
(type II tests, \ie, tests of $H_1|H_2$, where $H_1 \implies H_2$)
and unconditional linear tests (type III tests)
with an $F$ statistic.

\medskip
\textbf{Matching} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

When comparing the two models 
  $y \sim x$ 
and 
  $y \sim \text{treatment} + x$,
where the treatment is a binary variable,
some people like to have two observations 
for each possible value of the covariates, 
one for each value of the treatment.
This is usually not the case, 
but we can instead ``fabricate'' new observations:
\textbf{matching} is very similar to regression, 
but it is not linear
(it could be seen as a ``non-parametric regression developed by non-statistitians'').
This looks like an imputation 
(or inference with missing data) problem, 
but it is apparently addressed with ad hoc methods:
coarsened exact matching 
(\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Porro+Iacus+King.pdf}%
{CEM}) is one of them -- 
there are 10 matching packages for R...
An application to program evaluation 
(``program'' means sanitation improvement, etc.) 
was presented.

\medskip
\textbf{Penalized Estimators} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Penalized estimators (such as non-parametric regression) 
are good but biased by construction: 
you can try to estimate and reduce the bias;
the \texttt{ibr} package implements 
such an \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Cornillon+Hengartner+Matzner-Lober.pdf}%
{\textbf{iterative bias reduction}} -- 
but there were too few details in the presentation.

Penalized regression minimizes 
$\text{RSS} + \sigma^2 k \lambda$, 
where RSS is a sum of squares, $k$ is the model size, 
and $\lambda$ the penalty for complicated models.
AIC (Akaike information criterion) 
uses $\lambda=2$ but overfits large datasets;
BIC (bayesian information criterion) 
uses $\lambda = \log n$.
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Galili+Gavrilov+Benjamini.pdf}%
{\textbf{Adaptively penalized regression}} minimizes 
$\text{RSS} + \sigma^2 \phi(k,n)$,
where $\phi$ is motivated by the FDR (false discovery rate), 
\ie, the expected number of wrongly rejected $H_0$ 
in a multiple test
(it has a higher power than the too conservative FWER 
(family-wise error rate), 
which controls the probability of 
one or more incorrectly rejected $H_0$;
with the FDR, you do not have a single $p$-value, 
but a \emph{sequence} (for the first, second, etc. test): 
$p/m$, $2q/m$, $3q/m$).

A full \textbf{regularization path} 
(\ie, a penalized estimator, for all values of $\lambda$:
lasso, with its $L^1$ penalty;
ridge, with its $L^2$ penalty;
elastic net, with both: 
$\tfrac12(1-\alpha) \va{\beta}^2 + \alpha \va{\beta}$)
can be efficiently computed by 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Hastie.pdf}%
{\textbf{coordinate descent}}
(discretize the path, 
estimate one coordinate at a time until convergence, 
repeat for the next point on the path, etc.).
Coordinate descent should work any time 
your optimization if of the form 
$\text{something} + \lambda \times \text{penalty}$,
for instance to build undirected graphical models 
(it gives you a sequence of graphs)
or to complete a matrix 
(e.g., a $\text{movies} \times \text{rates}$ matrix, 
not observed completely).

In \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Muller.pdf}%
{\textbf{credit rating}},
to estimate the probability of default, 
you can replace the (linear) logit model 
by a generalized additive model (GAM).
(There are (too) many such estimators in R, 
not only \texttt{gam} and \texttt{mgcv}; 
if you hesitate, use \texttt{gam} (more stable for small datasets), 
unless you have a lot of relatively clean data.)

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Nieuwenhuis+Pelzer+TeGrotenhuis.pdf}%
{\texttt{Influence.ME}} package provides 
regression diagnostics (influential observations, etc.) 
for mixed models;
the \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Ruckdeschel+Spangl.pdf}%
{\texttt{merBoot}} package uses bootstrap 
to provide empirical $p$-values for mixed models.

\medskip
\textbf{Non-linear models} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Baty+Charles+Flandrois+Delignette-Muller.pdf}%
{\texttt{nlstools}} package provides 
a few functions to help fit non-linear models:
\texttt{preview} to help you (graphically) 
  choose the starting values;
\texttt{nls} to fit the data;
\texttt{overview} as an alternative to \texttt{summary};
\texttt{plotfit} to display the data and the fitted curve
-- also check \texttt{nlsResiduals}, \texttt{test.nlsResiduals},
\texttt{nlsContourRSS} (to identify ill-conditioning),
\texttt{nlsConfRegions}, \texttt{nlsJack}, \texttt{nlsBoot}.

\medskip
\textbf{Univariate data} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

To estimate the 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Burger+Dhorne.pdf}%
{\textbf{mode}} of unimodal data,
you can compute density estimators with wider and wider kernels,
look at their local maxima and 
remember their positions when they disappear: 
you get a dendogram-like plot --
this can actually ge generalized to higher dimensional data
(apply some dimension reduction algorithm afterwards,
if needed): check the \texttt{denpro} and \texttt{delt} packages.

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/RiouFranca+Genolini.pdf}%
{\texttt{R2lUniv}} package provides graphical summaries of multivariate data, in \LaTeX,
but is not as nice and compact as \texttt{Hmisc::describe}.

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Moreira+DeUnaAlvarez+Crujeiras.pdf}%
{\texttt{DTDA}}
 package provides functions 
to analyze truncated data 
(estimator of the cumulated distribution function (cdf), etc.).

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Delignette-Muller+Pouillot+Denis.pdf}%
{\texttt{fitdistrplus}} package helps you fit distributions 
(as \texttt{MASS::fitdistr}), 
but allows for \emph{censored} observations,
provides skewness/kurtosis plots 
(with the dataset, bootstrap replications, 
and the regions attainable by the models you are considering)
and goodness-of-fit tests 
(Anderson-Darling test to compare the data and the fitted model -- 
it accounts for estimated parameters).

The Tobit model 
\begin{align*}
  y_1 &= \beta x + \epsilon \\
  y   &= y_1 \cdot \mathbb{1}_{y_1>0}\\
\intertext{can be generalized to \textbf{multiple hurdle} models}
  \mathbf{y} &= \beta \mathbf{x} + \mathbf{\epsilon} \\
  \mathbf{\epsilon} &\sim N(\mathbf{0},V) \\
  y   &= y_1 \cdot \mathbb{1}_{y_1>0,y_2>0,\dots,y_m>0}
\end{align*}
with the 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Carlevaro+Croissant+Hoareau.pdf}%
{\texttt{mhurdle}} package.

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Croissant.pdf}%
{\texttt{mlogit}} package fits multinomial logit models 
(wasn't there already an implementation in \texttt{nnet::multinom}?).

\medskip
\textbf{Time Series} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\textbf{Markov Switching models} can be fitted 
by expectation maximization (EM): 
estimate the state at each time;
then the parameters in each state; iterate.

You can 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Varadhan+Subramaniam.pdf}%
{\textbf{mine}} a large number of time series 
by computing a few metrics for each of them 
(average, volatility, minimum and maximum of the derivative, etc. --
use the methods of \emph{functional data analysis} (FDA)) 
and plot the resulting multivariate dataset 
(1- or 2-dimensional plots, PCA (principal component analysis), etc.).
This is the idea behind 
\href{http://www.rgrossman.com/dl/proc-094.pdf}{\textbf{scagnostics}}, 
applied to time series.

In a \emph{threshold autoregressive model} (TAR) model,
the autogression (AR) coefficient depends on the previous value 
(often, one allows two values depending on the sign;
sometimes three values corresponding to 
``significantly positive'', ``significantly negative'', ``small'').
The idea can be generalized to \textbf{threshold cointegration}:
two time series are 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Stigler.pdf}%
{threshold cointegrated} 
if they have a threshold stationary linear combination $y$,
\ie, $y_{t+1} = \alpha(y_t) y_t + \epsilon_{t+1}$, 
with $\va{ \alpha(y_t) } < 1$ if $\va{ y_t }$ is large.

The Kalman filter can be 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Ruckdeschel+Spangl.pdf}%
{robustified} 
to resist to either outliers or regime changes
(or even both, but with a delay) 
with the rLS and ACM algorithms (not detailed enough).

With the \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Sueur+Aubin+Simonis.pdf}%
{\texttt{seewave}} package, R can process sound samples
(the plots are nice and the PDF presentation 
even contained an animation) -- 
of course, since R tends to store data in memory, 
this is only limited to sound \emph{samples}.

\textbf{Breakpoint models} are used in bioinformatics 
to model the ratio 
$$ 
  \dfrac{ 
    \text{number of copies of a gene} 
  }{ 
    \text{number of copies in the reference} 
  } 
$$
as a locally constant function of the position on the chromosome; 
the breakpoints can be estimated with \emph{dynamic programming} -- 
could dynamic programming be applied to other breakpoint problems?
I rarely see it outside bioinformatics.

The usual RMetrics presentation(s) focused on 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Chalabi+Wuertz.pdf}%
{date arithmetics}
(the \texttt{timeDate} and \texttt{timeSeries} packages 
are aware of weekdays, businessdays, 
for a given financial center -- 
but I did not really understand 
their explanation of the \texttt{recordIDs} slot)
and \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Wuertz+Chalabi+Ellis+Hanf.pdf}%
{\emph{portfolio optimization}}.

\medskip
\textbf{%
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Antoni+Dhorne+LeGuyadec.pdf}%
  {Spatial Models}
  and 
  \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Zambrano+Bigiarini.pdf}%
  {GIS}
}

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Laurent+RuizGazen+ThomasAgnan.pdf}%
{\texttt{GeoXp}} package provides a few plots for spatial data: 
\emph{driftmap} (not defined), 
\emph{angle map},
$$ f(x1)-f(x2) \sim \text{angle}(x2-x1),$$ 
where the angle is with respect to the horizontal axis),
\emph{neighbourhood map} 
(number of neighbours versus distance to the nearest neighbour),
\emph{Moran plot} (to check for spatial autocorrelation,
plot the value of the variable of interest at a point 
versus the average value in a neighborhood of this point -- 
you could also use two different variables),
outliers 
(Mahalanobis distance between pairs versus 
Euclidian distance between pairs);
those plots could be be linked to a map.

\medskip
\textbf{Multivariate Data} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

To the list of \emph{multidimensional scaling} algorithms, 
\texttt{cmdscale}, \texttt{isoMDS}, \texttt{sammon},
you can add 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Epifanio.pdf}%
{\texttt{isomap}}, which tries to find a (non-linear) 
2-dimensional submanifold, smooth but close to the data.
As always, try all algorithms and 
try to replace the dissimilarities by their ranks
(it does not matter if the matrix is not metric).

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Kaiser+Leisch.pdf}%
{\texttt{biclust}} package implements many 
\textbf{biclustering} algorithms 
(for binary, quantitative, ordinal or qualitative variables).
% Check the Quest algorithm (among those implemented)

Hierarchical clustering can be performed on principal components 
(some claim that discarding the last factors 
of a factor analysis makes the clustering more robust, 
others claim the opposite); 
the clusters can be defined by 
representative individuals (actual data points), 
variables (as in biclustering) 
or axes (as in a biplot).

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Bordier+Dojat+LafayedeMicheaux.pdf}%
{fMRI} data can be analyzed through spatial 
and even (spatio-)temporal ICA (independent component analysis)
if you have a sparse description 
of the singular value decomposition (SVD);
also check the \texttt{PTAk} package for principal tensor analysis --
a similar idea was presented a few years ago at 
\href{http://vision.ai.uiuc.edu/~wanghc/research/siggraph05.html}%
{SigGraph}.
%  Out-of-Core Tensor Approximation of Multi-Dimensional Matrices of Visual Data

\emph{Canonical correspondance analysis} (CCA) 
and \emph{partial least squares} (PLS) are 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Dejean+Gonzalez+LeCao.pdf}%
{very similar}: 
the former looks for linear combinations 
$\alpha'X$ and $\beta'Y$ of $X$ and $Y$ 
that maximize $\cor(\alpha'X,\beta'Y)$, 
the latter maximizes $\cov(\alpha'X,\beta'Y)$.
Both can be regularized: 
the ridge (replace $(XX')^{-1}$ with $(XX'-\lambda I)^{-1}$)
is popular with CCA, 
the lasso (an $L^1$ penalty) with PLS.

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Noncheva+Mendes+daSilva.pdf}%
{\textbf{Data envelopment analysis}} (DEA, \ie, 
the construction of \emph{efficient frontiers}
in the $\text{input} \times \text{output}$ 
or $\text{risk} \times \text{reward}$ space)
can use CCA as a first step, to select the variables to use;
beware of outliers: DEA focuses on extremes.
DEA can also be applied to 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/vanEikeren+DowHygelund.pdf}%
{medicine}.

\textbf{Distributed quantile estimation}
(compute a few quantiles on each client, 
convert them into an estimated cumulated distribution function (cdf), 
send it to the server which then averages those cdfs)
can be generalized to higher dimensions with PCA 
(only compute the quantiles on the first principal components);
this idea can be applied to 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Baier+Neuwirth.pdf}{\emph{monitoring}}
(e.g., to spot when a coffee machine 
is about to run out of coffee by listening to it).

\textbf{Multiple tests} and variable selection 
can be improved by accounting for correlation 
(with a \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Ahdesmaki+Strimmer.pdf}%
{correlation-adjusted $T$ score}).
For instance, in genetics, 
you can group genes in the same pathway 
(or, if you do not have this information, 
in the same correlation neighbourhood).
For feature selection, 
you should control the FNDR (false non-discovery rate) 
rather than the FDR (false discovery rate).

Several presentations focused on \textbf{multiple imputation}: 
exploring/visualizing the structure of missing values 
with the 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Templ+Alfons+Filzmoser.pdf}%
{\texttt{VIM}} 
package (there is also a GUI);
fixing the separation problem 
(more variables than observations would 
remove all the randomness from the imputation procedure)
by adding a bayesian prior with the 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Su+Gelman+Hill.pdf}%
{\texttt{mi}} package;
reducing the bias in variance estimates
after multiple imputation with a misspecified model 
with ``\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Reilly.pdf}%
{estimating equations}'' (no details).

\medskip
\textbf{Trees} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Hothorn+Zeileis.pdf}%
{\texttt{partykit}} package unifies the various tree packages 
(\texttt{rpart}, \texttt{knnTree}, \texttt{Rweka}, \texttt{party}, 
\texttt{randomForest}, \texttt{gbm}, \texttt{mboost}) 
and should facilitate the implementation of other algorithms; 
it also provides a PMML interface 
(PMML is an XML file format to exchange statistical models 
between applications, 
mainly used in data mining).

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Michaelson+Ackermann+Beyer.pdf}%
{\emph{Random forests}}
are not only used for \emph{prediction}
or \emph{feature selection}, 
but also to measure how important each variable is;
contrary to univariate measures, 
they can take interactions into account.
The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Strobl+Zeileis.pdf}%
{\textbf{permutation importance}} of $X_j$ is 
the average decrease in classification accuracy 
after permuting $X_j$.
This is problematic because a variable can appear important 
because it is independent from the variable to predict, 
or just from the other variables. 
Conditional permutation importance 
(implemented in the \texttt{party} package)
can help focus on the former.

The standard benchmark to measure 
the performance of a tree algorithm is C4.5 --
its licence does not allow you to use it, 
but it has been reimplemented as J4.8 in Weka, 
available through \texttt{RWeka}.
Other algorithms include: 
CART (classification and regression trees) 
  in the \texttt{rpart} package;
unbiased recursive partitioning 
  (QUEST in the \texttt{LohTools} package)
and conditional inference trees 
  (CTree in the \texttt{party} package).
They all perform well, except C4.5/J4.8 without cross validation 
(cross-validation improves \emph{all} those algorithms 
and is already included in some of them: 
if not, do not forget to add it).

Classification trees (CART, in the \texttt{rpart} package) 
can be generalized to a multivariate gaussian outcome 
(\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Stewart+Abdolell+LeBlanc.pdf}%
{\texttt{longRPart}}).

After a hierarchical classification, 
one usually cuts the dendogram,
but you do not have to do it horizontally -- 
\emph{permutation tests} can help you find 
a good, \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Vistocco+Bruzzese.pdf}%
{non-horizontal cut}.

\medskip
\textbf{Graphs} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

While the \emph{graphical model} R packages landscape 
is getting less empty, 
it remains more heterogeneous than other domains, 
and is still plagued with 
external, Windows-only and/or closed source solutions, 
rarely useable in a professional context: 
WinBugs, 
MIM, 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Klinger+Friedrich.pdf}%
{Genie/Smile} (closed-source, but free to use, even commercially), etc.
Here are a few examples besides the classical 
\texttt{gR} (actually a combination of several packages) 
and \texttt{gRbase}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Denis+Delignette-Muller+Pouillot.pdf}%
  {\texttt{ReBaStaBa}} package describes 
  an already constructed bayesian network
  (\emph{bayesian networks} provide a concise description 
  of a joint distribution);
\item The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/FernandezdelPozo+Bielza.pdf}%
  {\texttt{IdR}} (influence diagrams in R) package, 
% for probabilistic graphical networks,
  can apparently learn the graph structure from the data -- 
  too few details in the presentation.
\end{compactitem}

Graph (or link) data abound, 
from the web (links between Wikipedia articles) 
to linguistic databases. 
An \textbf{ontology} is a list of words, with links between them; 
contrary to a taxonomy, 
those relations are not limited to speciation/generalization 
but can be arbitrary complicated; 
polysemy and synonyms are often prohibited 
(to avoid the mess of gene and protein names);
ontologies can be used to represent knowledge and make deductions 
(an ontology can be seen as a graph theorist's reinvention of Prolog).
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Quesada.pdf}%
{OWL} (Web Ontology Language) may appear powerful,
but is not robust at all; 
good old RDF (resource description language: 
it lists triplets (subject, predicate, object), 
or equivalently (node$_1$, edge type, node$_2$),
\ie, describes a graph with coloured edges -- 
contrary to OWL, it does not allow for 
constraints on the predicates such as cardinality, transitivity) 
is sufficient for most practical purposes.

The \texttt{igraph} and \texttt{pagerank} packages 
provide graph-theoretic algorithms; 
for instance,
they can be used to create a 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Klinke.pdf}%
{\textbf{tag cloud}} 
using multidimensional scaling (MDS) for the positions. % (and LaTeX for the hyphenation)
You may also want to check 
\href{http://www.wordle.net/}%
{Wordle} (non-free, but free to use),
which takes the shape of the words into account,
or 
\href{http://manyeyes.alphaworks.ibm.com/manyeyes/page/Bubble_Chart.html}%
{Many Eyes}'s bubble chart (apparently no licence), 
which uses a 
\href{http://www.ams.org/notices/200311/fea-stephenson.pdf}%
{circle packing} algorithm 
and amounts to a square root transform.

The \texttt{sna} package (social networks)
can be combined with the \texttt{tm} package (text mining)
to analyze \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Bohn+Feinerer+Hornik+Theussl.pdf}%
{mailing lists}.
% plot(termFreq(tm))

The result of a centroid-based clustering algorithm 
($k$-means, PAM (partition around medoids), etc.) 
can be assessed with: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{silhouette plot}, 
  $d(x,c_2(x)) \sim d(x,c_1(x))$, 
  where $c_1(x)$ and $c_2(x)$ are 
  the centroids closest and second closest to obervation $x$;
\item Topology-representing networks (TRN) 
  or \emph{neighbourhood graphs},
  weighted graphs with the centroids as vertices
  and the weight between $c_1$ and $c_2$ is the number of 
  points $x$ such that $c_1(x)=c_1$ and $c_2(x)=c_2$
  or the average mean average distance.
\end{compactitem}
The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Scharl+Leisch.pdf}%
{\texttt{gcExplorer}} package relies on 
\texttt{Rgraphviz}, \texttt{graph} 
and \texttt{symbols} 
(to add plots (barplots, etc.) in the graph vertices) 
to display these, interactively.

%\begin{verbatim}
%???- RAFT (Random Forests graphics tool): 
%    java-based stand-alone application (uses ImageJ)
%    Not much in the presentation 
%    (it promotes random forests, MDS, 
%    and stresses the importance of the parameters 
%    of the algorithms), 
%    no screnshots: just R graphics
%    (move to the GUI section?)
%    www.math.usu.edu/~adele/forests/cc_graphics.htm
%    www.ssec.wisc.edu/~billh/visad.html
%    http://rsb.info.nih.gov/ij
%    What is the link between ImageJ and graphs?
%\end{verbatim}

\medskip
\textbf{Machine Learning} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/IodiceDEnza.pdf}%
{\textbf{Association rule mining}} can be seen as a form of 
``non-symmetric correspondance analysis''
for binary data: 
it can rely on brute force 
or multiple correspondance analysis (MCA); 
some algorithms also use exogenous data 
(e.g., socio-demographic data for a 
$\text{client} \times \text{item}$ dataset).

The \texttt{set} package provides (finite) set operations, 
that can easily be generalized to \textbf{fuzzy sets}
(just replace the topos of truth values $\{0,1\}$ 
by the interval $[0,1]$);
applications include 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Meyer+Hornik.pdf}%
{relations} 
(order, partial order, equivalence, etc.) 
and how to average them
(e.g., \textbf{voting systems}) -- 
this leads to non trivial optimization problems.

%\medskip
%\textbf{Ordinal data} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Schimek+Budinska+Lin+Mysickova.pdf}%
{\texttt{TopkLists}} package implements algorithms 
to average \textbf{ordinal data}, \ie, rankings,
when $m$ raters provide the top $k$ elements in a list of $N$
(more voting systems).

%- ??? Comparing ROC curves

\medskip
\textbf{Dynamical Systems} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Surprisingly, R is sufficiently fast 
to simulate \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Soetaert.pdf}%
{dynamical systems} (e.g., in ecology), 
\ie, large systems of differential equations;
check the following packages: 
\texttt{desolve} 
  (rather than \texttt{odesolve}, 
  which could only tackle small/toy problems) 
  for dynamical systems;
\texttt{rootSolve} for the steady state solution;
\texttt{linSolve} 
  for least square solutions
  (when your linear system is undetermined, 
  just add $\min \n{x}^2$ to turn it into a quadratic program);
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Petzoldt.pdf}%
  {\texttt{simecol}}
  for applications in ecology.

\medskip
\textbf{High Performance Computing} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Emerson+Kane.pdf}%
{\texttt{bigmemory}}, which uses the Boost C++ interprocess library,
paliates the lack of multithreading in R 
through shared memory or file-based matrices;
it also provides 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Smith.pdf}%
{iterators} 
to hide (or make implicit) the parallelism
provided  by \texttt{multicore}, \texttt{snow}, \texttt{nws}.
Also check 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Oehlschlagel+Adler.pdf}%
{\texttt{ff}} 
(matrices limited to $2^{31}-1$ elements), 
\texttt{ffdf} (as fast as bigmemory),
\texttt{sqlite} (sufficiently fast), 
\texttt{BufferedMatrix} (inefficient), 
\texttt{filehash} (sufficiently fast).
  
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Eddelbuettel.pdf}%
{\texttt{RCpp}} allows (eases) the use of C++ and STL containers; 
conversely, \texttt{RInside} allows you to call R from C++;
also check 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Adler+Philipp.pdf}%
{\texttt{rdyncall}} to use C libraries from R.

Several packages provide some 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Schmidberger.pdf}%
{parallelism}: 
\texttt{multicore}; 
\texttt{snowfall}, \texttt{nws}, \texttt{Rmpi}; 
\texttt{gridR}
(with parallel computations, beware of random number generators: 
the random numbers on each node should look independent).

Threads (as opposed to parallelism) are needed 
to process real-time 
(\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Winston.pdf}%
{streaming}) data.
R will remain mono-threaded for the foreseable future;
in the meantime, 
use several process (with some C/C++ where needed)
and shared memory (with bigmemory) between processes.

R can be used in simple web applications 
(the user fills in a form and a plot is updated),
thanks to the 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Ooms.pdf}%
{\texttt{RApache}} module,
with some Ajax (Prototype, Scritaculous, YUI, jQuery, Dojo, Ext), 
XML or JSON (better than XML for large datasets),
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Pau+Huber.pdf}%
and {\texttt{hwriter}} (for static reports, I use \texttt{Sweave}).

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Nakano+Nakama.pdf}%
{\texttt{RdWeb}} is a (still immature) 
web interface to parallel computing in R
(\texttt{RApache} and \texttt{Rserve} do not focus on parallelism);
it requires a batch system (\texttt{at}, openPBS, Torque, etc.)

A \textbf{task scheduler} is a hybrid between 
\texttt{make} and \texttt{at}/\texttt{crontab}:
it automatically executes tasks after a given time, 
when some conditions (file availability, etc.) are met
and allows tasks to be dependent on one another 
(you can write a quick and dirty task scheduler 
with \texttt{make} and \texttt{crontab}: 
from the crontab,
launch \texttt{make -k -j 100} in a directory every minute;
tasks simply fail if their starting conditions are not met;
track which tasks have run or are currently running 
by creating empty files with predefined names).
The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Etienne+Corvazier+Legros.pdf}%
{Coalition} task manager
(in Python, 
with \href{http://twistedmatrix.com/}{Twisted Matrix}, 
on \href{http://code.google.com/p/coalition/}{GoogleCode})
handles dependencies, load balancing; 
it does not seem to provide repeated tasks (``every day''),
or tasks starting after a given time.
There is nothing specific to R (and there should not be): 
it just launches executables, which can be R scripts.

A \textbf{workflow engine} allows you to write 
(the equivalent of) Makefiles 
(\ie, describe dependencies between tasks) graphically 
and run them on a grid, such as 
%Taverna (general, old, perhaps unmaitained) or 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Risselada+Friedrich+Ebeling+Klinger.pdf}%
{Knime}, which focuses on \emph{data pipelines}: 
it provides all the operations you can want on tables of data, 
the most common data mining algorithms and plots (Weka),
and interfaces with R and BIRT (a J2EE reporting system).

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Diamond+Abramson+Peachey.pdf}%
{Nimrod} toolkit combines a workflow system (Kepler), 
a design of experiments framework 
(DOE, more precisely 
``computer experiments'', whatever that means -- simulations?) 
and \textbf{gaussian processes} 
(with the \texttt{mlegp} package).

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Chine.pdf}%
{Biocep-R} is a javaesque application server: 
huge, exhaustive, unwieldy and fully buzzword-compliant.

The \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Theussl+Feinerer+Hornik.pdf}%
{\texttt{hive}} package uses Hadoop 
(a distributed computed framework, with a distributed file system,
trying to run the computations near the data,
initialy developped by Yahoo and 
currently maintained by the Apache foundation)
for text mining tasks (with the \texttt{tm} package).

Most of those cloud computing frameworks (Amazon EC2, etc.)
can generate or use models in the 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Guazzelli+Lin+Zeller.pdf}%
{PMML} format
(designed to exchange statistical models between applications).

\medskip
\textbf{GUI} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Francois+Chine.pdf}%
{JEdit} can be used as an IDE for R
(many people advise \textbf{Eclipse/StatET}):
it interfaces with the \texttt{codetools} package 
(warnings are presented 
as spelling mistakes would be in a text editor);
provides a tree view of the code (based on the R parser),
completion popups 
(for colours or plotting symbols, 
you have the actual colour or symbol),
an object browser, a debugger.

The building blocks for 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Grosjean+Francois+Barton.pdf}%
{SciViews} (an R GUI) 
are available as individual, reuseable packages; 
some were used to build other GUIs such as 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/abstracts/pdf/Denis+Irigoien+Francois+Grosjean.pdf}%
{Zoo/PhytoImage}, an R-based graphical software for image analysis;
it also uses the 
\href{http://rsbweb.nih.gov/ij/}{ImageJ} 
image processing Java library (public domain).

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Battke+Symons+Nieselt.pdf}{Mayday} 
is a visualization platform 
(in Java, interactive, aware of meta data, 
with the ability to write plugins)
for numeric matrices; 
it now has an interactive R shell, 
based on RLink, a replacement (or a layer above) rJava.

\medskip
\textbf{Windows} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Many mentions of 
\texttt{rcom} and \texttt{statconnDOM} (R--C\# link)
to interface R and 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Neuwirth.pdf}{Excel}
(there is even a 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Heiberger.pdf}%
{book} on the subject -- frightening) 
or generate 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Jones.pdf}%
{powerpoint files}.

\medskip
\textbf{R Ecosystem} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In many domains, 
R provides very similar functions in different packages 
each with its own syntax.
Some (well needed but) isolated \textbf{unification} works have begun, 
for instance \texttt{Zelig} (statistical models), 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Hothorn+Zeileis.pdf}%
{\texttt{partykit}} (recursive partitioning algorithms)
or \href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Nash+Varadhan.pdf}%
{\texttt{optimx}} (for optimization: 
it provides a single interface and compares the various algorithms, 
to help the user choose the best for the problem at hand).

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Dalgaard.pdf}%
{Instructional} presentations included 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Maechler+Bates.pdf}%
{\textbf{sparse matrices}}
with the \texttt{Matrix} package 
(used for linear mixed models by \texttt{lme4}) 
and spatial autocorrelated (SAR) models;
\textbf{S4 classes} 
(the dispatch is based on the type of all arguments, 
not just the first as with S3 classes);
implementing new statistical 
\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Scott+Wuertz+Dong.pdf}%
{distributions} 
(the dpqr functions, and a few others), 
as exemplified in the \texttt{distr} 
and \texttt{VarianceGamma} packages,
with some good general \textbf{software engineering} advice.

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Silles+Runnalls.pdf}%
{\textbf{Provenance tracking}} tries to answer the question
``where does this variable come from? how was it modified?'';
it is implemented in CXXR (a C++ reimplementation of R, 
designed to easily produce 
experimental versions of the R interpreter):
the \texttt{pedigree} function is similar to \texttt{history}, 
but the \emph{audit trail} it returns
only gives the commands that affected a given object.
The Open Provenance model aims at 
provenance tracking across systems.

\href{http://www.agrocampus-ouest.fr/math/useR-2009/slides/Wessa+vanStee.pdf}%
{\textbf{Reproducible computing}} can be easily obtained
by recording 
(``\href{http://www.freestatistics.org}%
{blogging}'') everything you type, including outputs and plots, 
in a way reminiscent of Maple worksheets.
Social data networks 
(\href{http://manyeyes.alphaworks.ibm.com/}{Many Eyes},
\href{http://www.swivel.com/}{Swivel},
\href{}{thedata.org},
etc.)
are starting to appear
(but they often present pure coincidences as hard evidence).
%Check 
%\url{freestatistics.org},   % Blog your statistical computations
%\url{moodle.org},           % Learning Management System
%\url{gopublish.org},        % Peer-reviewed version of freestatistics?
%\url{wessa.net},            % The author's website, with the software he wrote
%\url{thedata.org},          % To archive the data you used

%UNSORTED
%- HDF data (hierarchical data file)

\nextbook


%From the RMetrics 2009 seminar (http://www.rmetrics.org/meielisalp.htm):
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate copulas at work}\\
  M. Fischer\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

While binomial copulas are galore,
``higher dimensional'' ones 
(dimension 3 or 4, not the 200 or 2000 I needed a few years ago)
used to be rare. 
The situation is slowly changing:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Elliptical copulas;
\item Archimedian copulas
  $$ C(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \phi^{-1}( \phi(x_1) + \cdots + \phi(x_n) ) $$
  which can be written multiplicatively 
  (set $\theta(x) = \exp - \phi(x)$)
  $$ C(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \theta^{-1} \biggl( \prod_i \theta(x_i) \biggr) $$
\item \textbf{Generalized archimedian copulas}: 
  use archimedian copulas to group variables two at a time,
  in a fully-nested way
  $$
  \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.25,yscale=.5]
    \node (x1) at (0,0) {$x_1$};
    \node (x2) at (1,0) {$x_1$};
    \node (x3) at (2,0) {$x_1$};
    \node (x4) at (3,0) {$x_1$};
    \node (C1) at (   .5,1) {$C_1$};
    \node (C2) at ( 1.25,2) {$C_2$};
    \node (C3) at (2.125,3) {$C_3$};
    \draw[-] (x1) -- (   0, 1) -- (C1);
    \draw[-] (x2) -- (   1, 1) -- (C1);
    \draw[-] (C1) -- ( 0.5, 2) -- (C2);
    \draw[-] (x3) -- (   2, 2) -- (C2);
    \draw[-] (C2) -- (1.25, 3) -- (C3);
    \draw[-] (x4) -- (   3, 3) -- (C3);
  \end{tikzpicture}
  $$
  or using an arbitrary tree;
  $$ 
  \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.25,yscale=.5]
    \node (x1) at (0,0) {$x_1$};
    \node (x2) at (1,0) {$x_1$};
    \node (x3) at (2,0) {$x_1$};
    \node (x4) at (3,0) {$x_1$};
    \node (C1) at ( .5,1) {$C_1$};
    \node (C2) at (2.5,1) {$C_2$};
    \node (C3) at (1.5,2) {$C_3$};
    \draw[-] (x1) -- (   0, 1) -- (C1);
    \draw[-] (x2) -- (   1, 1) -- (C1);
    \draw[-] (x3) -- (   2, 1) -- (C2);
    \draw[-] (x4) -- (   3, 1) -- (C2);
    \draw[-] (C1) -- (  .5, 2) -- (C3);
    \draw[-] (C2) -- ( 2.5, 2) -- (C3);
  \end{tikzpicture}
  $$
\item \textbf{Generalized multiplicative archimedian copulas}: 
  replace the independent copula $\prod$ by another copula $C_0$
  $$ C(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \theta^{-1}( C_0( x_1, \dots, x_n ) ); $$
  this can be seen as a ($\mathfrak{S}_n$-symmetric) deformation of $C_0$;
\item \textbf{Liebscher copulas} combine 
  several multiplicative archimedian copulas 
  $$ 
    C(x_1,\dots,x_n) = 
    \Psi\biggl( \dfrac1m \sum_j \prod_i \theta_j( u_i ) \biggr); 
  $$
\item The \textbf{Koehler-Symanowski copulas} are build as follows: 
  take $n$ iid random variables 
  $$ Y_1, \dots, Y_n \sim \text{Exp}(\lambda = 1 ) ; $$
  choose random variables to model bivariate associations 
  $$ G_{ij} \sim \Gamma( \alpha_{ij}, 1 ), \quad i < j ; $$
  (and trivariate associations if you want: 
  $G_{ijk} \sim \Gamma(\alpha_{ijk},1)$)
  and try to put them all in a formula, e.g.,
  $$ 
    U_i = 
    \left( 
      1 + \dfrac{ Y_i }{ \sum\limits_j G_{ij} }
    \right) 
    ^ { \sum\limits_j \alpha_{ij} }
  $$
\item Starting from a (graphical model) decomposition 
  $$ f(x_1,x_2,x_3) = f(x_3) f(x_2|x_3) f(x_1|x_2,x_3) $$
  one can express a copula as a (complicated) product of bivariate copulas;
  this is the \textbf{pair copula decomposition} (PCD).
\end{compactitem}
A maximum likelihood fit of those on several financial datasets 
suggests to forget plain archimedian copulas:
elliptic (Student) or pair copulas provide a better fit.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Pair-copula constructions\\ of multiple independence}\\
  K. Aas et al.
\end{book}

Detailed presentation of pair copulae.
The decompositions are not described 
by a single graphical model 
but  by a (maximal) nested set of (tree) 
graphical models -- a $D$-vine.
Imposing some conditional independence 
greatly simplifies the model.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Package development in R: \\
  Implementing GO-GARCH models}\\
  B. Pfaff\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

The univariate GARCH model can be extended to a multivariate model 
by direct generalization (VEC, BEKK, factor models:
keep the same formulas, with matrices instead of real numbers,
and impose some conditions on them to keep 
the number of parameters reasonable);
by linear combinations of univariate GARCH models
(GO-GARCH (generalized orthogonal), latent factor GARCH)
or even non-linear combinations
(dynamic conditional correlation, general dynamic covariance models).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Consistent return and risk forecasting\\
  for portfolio optimization\\
  using kernel regressions}\\
  J. Hindebrand and T. Poddig\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

Separately estimating expected returns and risk model (variance matrix)
is suboptimal (when your only source of information is the time series of returns).
The authors suggest to estimate both from past returns
using \emph{kernel regression}
(apparently sometimes called 
``general regression neural network'' (GRNN))
which can be seen as a smoothed  $k$ nearest neighbours algorithm.
The risk can be estimated ``explicitely'', from the residuals
$$ \hat \sigma^2 = \dfrac1k \sum (\hat y_i - y_i )^2 $$
or ``implicitely''
\begin{align*}
  \hat y &= \dfrac1k \sum y_i \\
  \hat \sigma_i &= \dfrac1k \sum (\hat y - y_i)^2
\end{align*}
(the sums are over the $k$ nearest neighbours;
for kernel regression, replace those averages by weighted averages).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial crises\\
  and the exchange rate volatility\\
  for Asian economies}\\
  T. Oga and W. Polasek\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

Markov switching AR-GARCH models are amenable to 
bayesian MCMC estimation.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Markowitz and two managers}\\
  K. Rheinberger\\
  RMetrics Workshop 2009
\end{book}

In many companies, portfolio managers work independently 
and their strategies or portfolios are combined by higher authorities
(\ie, they decide on the capital allocated to each).
This is suboptimal: if the asset classes are correlated, 
the optimal global portfolio need not be obtainable 
as a combination of efficient subportfolios.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Simple parallel computing 
  in R with Hadoop}\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

\textbf{MapReduce} 
(also called \textbf{Cloud Computing} 
or \textbf{SAAS} (software as a service))
is a trendy name for ``distributed divide and conqueer''
where the task division step is aware of 
the location of the data
on the \emph{distributed file system} (DFS) 
to limit network usage.
R can use 
\href{http://developer.yahoo.com/hadoop/tutorial/}%
{\emph{Hadoop}}, a (Java) MapReduce implementation,
initially developped by Yahoo
and now maintained by the Apache foundation.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling and evaluation \\
  of insurance risk
  using \texttt{actuar}}\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

The \texttt{actuar} package provides 
some functionalities for actuarial science, 
which is just ``risk management'' with a different vocabulary:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Distributions often used to model losses 
  (on top of the usual dpqr functions,
  you also have moments $E[X^k]$,
  limited moments $E[\min(X,x)^k]$
  and moment generating functions 
  $E[e^{tX}]$);
\item \emph{Minimum distance estimators} (MDE) 
  for the Cramer-von Mises, modified $\chi^2$ 
  or layer average severity distances
  (these are not clearly defined);
\item Handling (plotting, fitting) 
  of censored data (both sides);
\item Various estimators of the distribution of 
  $X_1 + \cdots + X_N$
  from those of $N$ and the $X_i$ 
  (assumed iid):
  Panjer algorithm (uses discretized distributions),
  simulations, 
  normal approximations 
  (these algorithms are not detailed);
\item Ruin models, \ie, estimation of 
  $$ P\bigl[ \exists t > 0 \ :\ U(t) < 0 \bigr] $$
  where
  \begin{align*}
    U(t) &: \text{surplus at time } t \\
    u    &: \text{initial surplus} \\
    c_k  &: \text{premium collected at time } k \\
    N(t) &: \text{number of claims in } [ 0, t ] \\
    X_n  &: \text{value of the $n$th claim} \\
    U(t) &= u + \sum_{k \leq t} c_k - \sum_{n \leq N(t)} X_n
  \end{align*}
  from the distributions of $N$ and $X$;
\item Simulations of hierarchical models.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{An overview of random number generation}\\
  C. Dutang\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

The default random number generator (RNG) in R 
(the \texttt{runif} function)
is the \textbf{Mersenne twister} (MT), 
a linear congruential RNG
modified by some bitwise operations.
The \texttt{randomtoolbox} package implements other RNG
(mainly generalized MT: WELL, SFMT)
and quasi-random number generators 
(aka \textbf{low discepancy sequences}):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For the \textbf{Van der Corput} sequence, 
  choose a prime number $p$, 
  write successive numbers $1, 2, 3, \dots, n$ in base $p$, 
  $n = \sum_j a_j p^j$ 
  and return $\phi_p(n) = \sum_j a_j / p^{j+1}$;
  if you choose several prime numbers, 
  you have a multidimensional low discrepancy sequence, 
  the \textbf{Halton sequence};
  in R, the \texttt{halton(n,k)} function
  returns the first $n$ elements of the $k$-dimensional 
  Halton sequence;
\item The \textbf{torus algorithm} (or \textbf{Kronecker sequence}) is 
  $$ 
    u_n = ( 
      \mathop{\text{frac}} n \sqrt{p_1}, 
      \cdots,
      \mathop{\text{frac}} n \sqrt{p_d}
    )
  $$
  where $\mathop{\text{frac}} x = x - \lfloor x \rfloor$
  is the fractional part.
\end{compactitem}
RNGs can be tested by looking at
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The histogram of generated data;
\item The autocorrelation function (ACF);
\item The order test, which checks
  in which order $x_{i-1}$, $x_i$ and $x_{i+1}$ are);
\item The Poker test: 
  take $d$ consecutive numbers from your sequence, 
  count how many there are in each 
  $\left[ (k-1)/d, k/d \right[$ interval
  ($k \in \llbracket 1, d \rrbracket$),
  compare with the expected values using a $\chi^2$ test.
\end{compactitem}
\emph{Discrepancy} can be defined as 
\begin{align*}
  D_n(x,A) &= \dfrac1n \text{\#} \bigl\{\, 
    i \in \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket \ :\ i \in A 
  \,\bigr\} \\
  D_n^\infty(x, \mathscr P) &= \sum_{ A \in \mathscr P } \va{ D_n( x, A ) }
\end{align*}
for some $\mathscr P \subset \mathscr P( [0,1]^d )$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{GMM and GEL with R}\\
  P. Chauss\'e\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

The \emph{generalized method of moments} (GMM)
fits a model to your data by considering
several quantities or \emph{moments}
(e.g., average, variance, median, quantiles, 
higher moments, truncated moments, etc.)
and fine-tuning the parameters so that the 
theoretical quantities be as close from the 
observed ones as possible.
More precisely, find several moments $g = (g_1,\dots,g_n)$
so that $E[g(\theta,X)] = 0$ and compute 
\begin{align*}
  \hat \theta &= \argmin_\theta \n{ \bar g(\theta) }^2 \\
  \bar g( \theta ) &= \dfrac1T \sum_t g(\theta, x_t).
\end{align*}

Since the moments are not ``independent'', 
the Euclidian distance is not the best choice:
you will prefer 
$$ 
  \hat \theta = \argmin_\theta \bar g(\theta) ' W \bar g(\theta) 
$$
for some $W$.
The most efficient estimator is obtained with 
$$
  W = \sum_{ s \in \ZZ } \cov( g(\theta, x_t), g(\theta, x_{t+s})
$$
which can be estimated 
(with an HAC (heteroscedasticity and autocorrelation consistent) estimator) 
as 
\begin{align*}
  \hat \Omega( \theta^* ) &= 
    \sum_{ \va s \leq T-1 } k_h(s)\hat \Gamma_s( \theta^* ) \\
  \hat \Gamma_s( \theta^* ) &= 
    \dfrac1T g(\theta^*, x_t) g(\theta^*, x_{t+s})' \\
  k_h &: \text{some kernel}
\end{align*}
For instance, one could try any of the following:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Estimate $\theta^*$ with $W=1$; 
  compute $\hat \Omega( \theta^* )$; 
  estimate~$\hat\theta$;
\item Iterate the above until convergence;
\item Optimize properly:
  $$
    \hat \theta = \argmin_\theta 
    \bar g(\theta)' \hat\Omega(\theta) \bar g(\theta).
  $$
\end{compactitem}
The limiting distribution is known.

In finance or economy, you can use the CAPM, 
the utility function and other meaningful quantities
to define moments.

In R, just provide your moments and your data to the \texttt{gmm} function.

I did not understand what GEL (generalized empirical likelihood) was.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A tale of two theories: \\
  Reconciling random matrix theory\\ 
  and shrinkage estimation \\
  as methods for covariance matrix estimation}\\
  B. Rowe\\
  \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/Meielisalp2009/Presentations/}{RMetrics Workshop 2009}
\end{book}

Both methods try to remove the noise 
in the spectrum of the sample covariance matrix: 
random matrix theory (RMT) violently shrinks the low values to zero; 
shrinkage smoothly shrinks all values to their mean 
(or to a predefined value).
Combining the two methods turns out to be a bad idea.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The structure and function\\ of complex networks}\\
  M.E.J. Newman\\
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat/0303516}
\end{book}

Given a network, one can compute the following 
numeric quantities (invariants):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Harmonic mean geodesic distance between all pairs
  (the arithmetic mean would have problems with
  non-connected vertices);
\item Clustering coefficient:
\begin{align*}
  C &= \dfrac1n \sum C_i \\
  C_i &= \dfrac{
    \text{Number of triangles connected to vertex $i$}
  }{
    \text{Number of triples centered on vertex $i$}
  };
\end{align*}
\item Degree distribution;
\item Network resilience:
  progressively remove vertices, 
  staring with the highest degree ones,
  and plot of the mean distance between edges 
  versus the fraction of vertices removed;
\item \textbf{Assortativity} coefficient,
  to measure \textbf{mixing patterns} between vertex types
  $$ r = \dfrac{ \tr e - \n{ e^2} }{ 1 - \n{ e^2 } } $$
  where $e_{ij}$ is the fraction of edges
  between vertices of types $i$ and $j$;
  those types can be numeric
  (e.g., the degree,
  leading to the \textbf{degree correlation})
  and even continuous.
\end{compactitem}

Here are a few models of randon graphs:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Poisson random graphs}
  (choose the degree of each vertex; join the vertices at random),
  aka \emph{Erd\H os--R\'enyi graphs};
\item Configuration model
  (replace the Poisson distribution by another one);
  they fail to account for \textbf{transitivity};
\item Exponential random graphs:
  %\begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  %\item
    choose a few numeric properties of graphs,
    $\epsilon_1,\dots,\epsilon_k$,
    e.g., number of vertices, number of vertices of a given degree, etc.;
  %\item
    choose a few numbers $\beta_1,\dots,\beta_k$
    (inverse temperatures);
  %\item
    sample graphs with probability
    $$ P(G) \propto \exp - \sum _i \beta_i \epsilon_i(G). $$
  %\end{compactitem}
  These are amenable to (Monte Carlo) simulations,
  but not analytical derivations,
  except in the case of \textbf{Markov graphs}
  (the absence/presence of edges are independent unless the edges share a vertex).
  Unfortunately, they model transitivity in an unnatural way:
  there are too many complete cliques.
\item \emph{Small world model}:
  put all the vertices on a circle
  (or any other low-dimensional lattice),
  connect each vertex to its $k$ nearest neighbours,
  rewire edges at random with probability $p$
  (a variant adds edges with probability $p$:
  this can be seen as the average
  of a lattice and a random graph);
\item \emph{The Barab\'asi-Albert model} is a model of
  network growth, \ie, it tries to explain where
  the graph properties come from:
  add vertices one by one,
  link them to previous nodes,
  with a higher probability for nodes with a high degree
  (preferential attachment);
  the earlier Price model was similar but used directed edges.
\end{compactitem}
\textbf{Fully mixed} epidemiological models,
such as SIR (suceptible-infected-recovered:
after catching the disease,
people (die or) become immune)
\begin{align*}
  \dot S &= - \beta I S \\
  \dot I &= \beta I S - \gamma I \\
  \dot R &= \gamma I
\end{align*}
or SIS (reinfections are possible)
\begin{align*}
  \dot S &= - \beta I S + \gamma I\\
  \dot I &=   \beta I S - \gamma I
\end{align*}
can be generalized to a network.
Other processes on networks include
\textbf{percolation} and search.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{strucchange: an R package\\
  for testing for structural change\\
  in linear regression models}\\
  A. Zeilis et al.
\end{book}

The \texttt{strucchange} package implements tests
based on the following \textbf{empirical fluctuation processes}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item OLS-CUSUM: cumulated sum of the residuals (a brownian bridge);
\item recursive CUSUM: cumulated sums of the residuals,
  where the $i$th residual comes from the model
  estimated on the first $i$ observations;
\item Recursive MOSUM: idem, on a moving window;
\item Fluctuations: parameters of the model estimated on an expanding window, 
  suitably renormalized (contrary to the residuals, 
  this is a multidimensional process);
\item Moving estimate: idem, on a moving window.
\end{compactitem}
In case of a structural change,
these processes ``fluctuate more'';
they can be compared with boundaries of the form
$b(t)=\lambda$ (MOSUM: it is stationary),
$b(t)=\lambda\sqrt{t}$ (brownian motion) or
$b(t)=\lambda\sqrt{t(1-t)}$.
However, the crossing probability is easier to compute for linear boundaries:
we lose some power at the begining and end of the interval.
%  library(strucchange)
%  x <- rnorm(100)
%  y <- x + .2 * rnorm(100) + 
%       ifelse( 1:100 > 60, 0, - 2 * x )
\begin{verbatim}
  r <- epf(y ~ x, type="fluctuation")
  plot(r)
  sctest(r)
\end{verbatim}
Instead of those \emph{significance tests},
which have no clear alternative hypothesis,
you may prefer an $F$ test,
which tests against the alternative that
there are two different models,
one before and one after an (unknown) breakpoint;
they compare the sum of squared residuals for the
model with and without a breakpoint at observation $\tau$ 
with an $F$ statistic $F_\tau$
and aggregate them as
$\sup F_\tau$,
$\mean F_\tau$ or
$\log \mean \exp F_\tau$.
\begin{verbatim}
  f <- Fstats(y~x)
  sctest(f)
\end{verbatim}

The package also provides \textbf{monitoring}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A unified approach\\ to structural change tests\\ 
  based on $F$ statistics,\\ OLS residuals and ML scores}\\
  A. Zeileis
\end{book}

The following classes of structural change tests are actually
generalized $M$-fluctuation tests,
built from the empirical fluctuation process (efp):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item CUSUM
  (the absolute maximum of the cumulative sums of the residuals
  rescaled by an estimate $\hat \sigma^2$ of the error variance --
  it is not a real test, there is no clear alternative hypothesis,
  but it is often used as an exploratory tool);
\item 
  $ \text{SupLM} = 
  \sup\limits_{t \in [0,1] } 
  \dfrac{ \n{ \text{efp}_t }_2^2 }{ t(1-t) }  $
  (in the alternative, the model parameters have two values, 
  before and after some (unknown) breakpoint);
\item  The Nyblom-Hansen statistic, where in the alternative $H_1$, 
  the parameters follow a random walk, boils down to
  $$ \dfrac1n \sum_{i=1}^n \n{ \text{efp}_{i/n} }_2^2. $$
\end{compactitem}
To detect multiple breakpoints,
you may want to use a moving sum (MOSUM) instead of a cumulative sum.

These tests can be extended to a \textbf{monitoring} setup,
where the observations arrive one by one:
estimate the model on $[0,1]$
and compute the empirical fluctuation process beyond 1;
the critical values become a curve $b(t)_{t>1}$ instead of a constant.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Permutation tests for structural change}\\
  A. Zeileis and. T. Hothorn
\end{book}
The SupF (or SupLM: the test statistic can be transformed 
to look like an F or a Student T one)
tests whether the average of a series of random variables
$(X_{k/n})_{k \in \llbracket 1,n \rrbracket}$
is a constant $\mu$
against the alternative hypothesis $H_\pi$ that 
it is $\mu_1$ before some breakpoint $\tau \in [0,1]$ and $\mu_2$ after,
by considering the statistic
$$ \dfrac{\text{RSS}_\pi}{\text{RSS}_0}, $$
suitably renormalized
$$ Z_\pi = \sqrt{ (n-1) \left( 1 - \dfrac{\text{RSS}_\pi}{\text{RSS}_0} \right) }, $$
where $\text{RSS}_0$ and $\text{RSS}_\pi$ are the sums of squared residuals 
under $H_0$ and $H_\pi$,
and aggregating them as
$$ D = \max_\pi Z_\pi. $$
This statistic can be compared with the following distributions:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\mathscr D_\infty$: the \emph{unconditional limiting distribution},
  \ie, the limiting distribution, for $n \to \infty$;
  it is 
  $$\sup\limits_{\pi \in [0,1]} \pi (1-\pi) B(\pi),$$
  where $B$ is a brownian bridge;
\item $\mathscr D _{ \sigma | Y }$: the \emph{conditional distribution},
  obtained by randomly permuting the observations
  (usually computed via simulations; 
  only in very rare cases is it analytically tractable);
\item $\mathscr D_{\infty|Y}$, the \emph{asymptotical conditional distribution},
  which can be computed analytically:
  \begin{align*}
    Z & \sim N( 0, \Sigma ) \\
    Z_{\pi,\tau} &= 
    \dfrac{ \pi (1-\tau) }{\sqrt{ \pi (1-\pi) \tau (1-\tau) }} 
    \qquad (\pi \leq \tau)
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}
This can be generalized to structural changes in binary variables,
multivariate series, stratified observations, parametric models.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Implementing a class of structural change tests: 
  an econometric computing approach}\\
  A. Zeileis
\end{book}

Structural change tests can be performed as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compute the residuals of your model, $(r_i)_{i\in\llbracket1,n\rrbracket}$;
\item Robustify those residuals, 
  $\bigl(\psi(r_i)\bigr)_{i\in\llbracket1,n\rrbracket}$,
  where $\psi$ is an $M$-estimation scale function;
  we only want the sum of the robustified residuals to be zero;
\item Compute their cumulative sum process $(Z_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$
  (the article is not very clear 
  if we take the cumulative sum of the residuals
  or build a process from the model coefficients estimated on an expanding window:
  we cannot get a multidimensional process with just the residuals);
\item Estimate the asymptotic covariance matrix of the scores $\hat J$;
  we then have
  $$ \cov( Z_t, Z_s ) = \min(t,s) \hat J; $$
  you can (should) use an HAC 
  (heteroskedasticity and autocorrelation consistent)
  estimator for $J$;
\item Compute the decorrelated fluctuation process
  $$ \text{efp}_t = \hat J ^{-1/2} Z_t; $$
  it converges to a brownian bridge
  (since the residuals sum up to zero, we have $Z_0 = Z_1 = 0$);
  you may choose to use the whole matrix $\hat J$ or just its diagonal elements;
\item Aggregate this multidimensional process, for instance:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Take the maximum of the components at each point in time,
    then take the maximum accross time (maxBB);
  \item Take the $L^2$ norm of the components at each point in time;
    then take the average over time (Cramer von Mises statistic);
  \item Take the range (\ie, $\max - \min$) over time, for each component;
    then take the maximum of those ranges (range test).
  \end{compactitem}
\end{compactitem}
This is implemented in the \texttt{strucchange} package
and will work for any model for which you can compute the residuals
(Poisson, logistic, beta regressions, etc.).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Minority games}\\
  C.H. Yeung and Y.-C. Zhang\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0811.1479}
\end{book}
A readable review article.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Copula-based\\ non-linear quantile autoregression}\\
  X. Chen et al.
\end{book}

A copula-based Markov model
(for a stationary time series $(Y_t)_t$)
just specifies the copula $C$ of $(Y_{t-1},Y_t)$
and the marginal distribution of $Y_t$.
This is often estimated by a 2-step precedure:
first, estimate the marginal distribution,
then, estimate the copula
(using either the empirical marginal distribution or the fitted one).
\par
One can look at the $\tau$th quantile of $Y_t | Y_{t-1}=x$:
$$ F^{-1} \bigl( C(\tau,\cdot)^{-1} (F(x)) \bigr). $$
The article considers the case of
a parametric copula and marginal distribution,
but those parameters depend on the quantile $\tau$
This is more robust to model misspecification.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantile autoregression}\\
  R. Koenker and Z. Xiao (2006)
\end{book}

Quantile autoregression (QAR) is a special case of
\textbf{random coefficient autoregression} (RCAR)
\begin{align*}
  y_t &= \theta_0(U_t) + \theta_1(U_t) y_{t-1} + \cdots + \theta_p(U_t) y_{t-p} \\
  U_t &\sim U(0,1).
\end{align*}
For instance
\begin{align*}
  \theta_0(\tau) &= \sigma \Phi^{-1}(\tau) \\
  \theta_1(\tau) &= \min( \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \tau, 1 )
\end{align*}
leads to a mixture of unit root or explosive behaviour (when $\theta_1=1$)
and mean-reverting behaviour (when $\theta_1 \in (0,1)$) --
this is actually sufficient to make the process stationary.
\par
Since the functions $\theta$ are unknown, this is a non-parametric model.
(Most of the theoretical troubles come from the requirement 
that those functions be monotonic.)
You can test for \emph{asymmetric dynamics} 
by checking if $\theta_1$ depends on $\tau$ at all.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{March madness, quantile regression bracketology and the Hayek hypothesis}\\
  R. Koenker and G.W. Bassett
\end{book}

More details on this example.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Issues on quantile autoregression}\\
  J. Fan and Y. Fan
\end{book}

Quantile autoregression (QAR) is related to 
\textbf{functional coefficient autoregression} (FCAR) models
$$ y_t = \alpha_0(U_t) + \alpha_1(U_t) Y_{t-1} + \cdots + 
         \alpha_p(U_t) Y_{t-p} + \epsilon_t 
$$
where $U_t$ is observed.
The special case $U_t = Y_{t-d}$ is known as
\textbf{functional autoregression} (FAR).
Identifiability is a real problem,
and in case of lack of monotonicity, the estimators are not consistent.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Feature selection in ``omics'' prediction problems
  using cat scores and false non-discovery rate control}\\
  M. Ahdesm\"aki and K. Strimmer\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0903.2003}
\end{book}

Linear discriminant analysis (LDA) is a supervised learning algorithm
that assumes the data is a mixture of gaussian distributions
with the same variance matrix $\Sigma$;
the LDA discriminant score of a new observation $x$ for cluster $k$ is
$$
  d_k(x) = \log P[ k | x ] =
  \mu_k' \Sigma^{-1} x - \tfrac12 \mu_k \Sigma^{-1} \mu_k + \log \pi_k
$$
where $\mu_k$ is the center of cluster $k$
and $\pi_k$ its weight (or a priori probability).
Estimating the inverse $\Sigma^{-1}$ is problematic:
\textbf{diagonal discriminant analysis}
(DDA, aka naive Bayes classification)
assumes it is diagonal.
The nearest shrunken centroids (NSC, 
aka PAM, partitioning around medoids) algorithm
is a regularized version of it.
\par
The article suggests a regularized LDA
(regularize the variances (shrink to the median)
and the correlation matrix (ridge regularization) separately)
to account for correlations, \ie, for non-spherical clusters.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Monitoring networked applications with incremental quantile estimation}\\
  J.M. Chambers et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0708.0302}
\end{book}

Sequential quantile estimation algorithms compute quantiles
in a ``cumulative'' (online) way, as data arrives:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Exact algorithm exist (e.g., based on the quick sort)
  to track a single quantile, 
  but they have to keep a potentially large buffer of data;
  they can be modified to track a set of quantiles;
\item Stochastic approximation is similar, 
  but assumes the data has a continuous distribution
  to approximate between the quantiles.
\end{compactitem}
Incremental quantile (IQ) estimation is a quick-and-dirty algorithm
that allows both online quantile estimation
and \emph{aggregation} of quantiles from distributed agents;
it uses cumulative distribution functions (CDF), 
which are easy to aggregate, rather than quantiles:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item On each agent, wait until you have enough data;
  turn it into a cumulative distribution function (CDF);
  blend it with the previous CDF estimate;
\item From time to time, send it to the server, 
  and start the computation afresh;
\item On the server, aggregate the CDFs received from the agents 
  and compute the desired quantiles.
\end{compactitem}
The CDFs can be stored and sent as a set of quantiles
(for increased precision, we may want more quantiles 
than the final users actually receive).
\par
This can be useful in distributed QoS (quality of service) measurement.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Text mining infrastructure in R}\\
  I. Feinerer et al.\\
  Journal of statistical software (2008)
\end{book}

The \texttt{tm} package provides a framework to manage collections of texts
(with their metadata; to avoid memory problems,
you can just keep the metadata in memory and leave the texts on disk)
and relies on other packages for stemming (\texttt{Rstem}, \texttt{snowball} (Weka)),
synonym substitution (\texttt{wordnet}),   %% Does this require a separate installation?
part-of-speech tagging (\texttt{openNLP}), %% Requires Java
term-document matrix computation (\texttt{Matrix}, for sparse matrices),
machine learning algorithms
(for unsupervised learning (document clustering):
hierarchical clustering, $k$-means;
supervised learning (document classification):
$k$-nearest neighbours (kNN), support vector machines (SVM);
or more text-specific tasts: information retrieval, keyword extraction),
string kernels (\texttt{kernlab}).

The \textbf{string kernel} of two strings $x$ and $y$
is the scalar product $\llbracket \Phi(x), \Phi(y) \rrbracket$
of their embeddings in some high-dimensional space:
knowing this scalar product is sufficient for most linear-algebra-based algorithms,
the actual coordinates are not needed. Examples include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\displaystyle k(x,y) = \sum_{s \sqsubseteq x, y} 
  \mathbb{1}_{ s^{\mathstrut}_0 = s^{\mathstrut}_{\va s} = \textvisiblespace }$ (bag of words)
\item $\displaystyle k(x,y) = \sum_{s \sqsubseteq x, y} \mathbb{1}_{ \va s \leq n }$ (full string kernel)
\item $\displaystyle k(x,y) = \sum_{s \sqsubseteq x, y} \mathbb{1}_{ \va s = n }$ (string kernel)
\item $\displaystyle k(x,y) = \sum_{s \sqsubseteq x, y} \lambda^{\va s}$ (substring kernel).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting the term structure\\ of government bond yields}\\
  F.X. Diebold and C. Li (2003)
\end{book}

The forward rate can be approximated as
$$ 
  f_t(\tau) = \alpha_t + 
              \beta_t e^{-\lambda_t \tau} + 
              \gamma_t \lambda_t e^{-\lambda_t \tau }
$$
where the three time series
$\alpha$ (level), $\beta$ (slope) and $\gamma$ (curvature)
can be estimated with an AR model
(why did $\lambda$ disappear?).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Comparing smooth transition and\\ 
  Markov switching autoregressive models\\
  of US unemployment}\\
  P.J. Deschamps (2007)
\end{book}

Comparison of a Markov switching autoregressive model (MSAR)
and a logistic smooth transition autoregressive model (LSTAR)
\begin{align*}
  y_{t+1} &= ( \alpha_0 + \alpha_1 y_t ) + G_t( \beta0 + \beta_1 y_t ) + \epsilon_t \\
  G_t     &= \dfrac1{1 + e^{-(a s_t + b)}}
\end{align*}
where $s_t$ is \emph{observed}, e.g., $s_t = y_t - y_{t-12}$.
There are variants with other shapes for the transition function $G$
(it need not be symmetric) and/or more than two states.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Two-dimensional correlation\\ 
  optimized warping algorithm\\
  for aligning GC$\times$GC-MS data}\\
  D. Zhang et al.
\end{book}
% Empty
Two-dimensional time warping can be applied to spectrometry.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast text mining using kernels in R}\\
  I. Feinerer and A. Karatzoglou
\end{book}

String kernels
$$ k(x,x') = \sum_{s \in A^*} \text{num}_s(x) \text{num}_s(y) \lambda_s $$
usually require $O\bigl( (\va x + \va y )^2 \bigr)$ operations,
but they can be computed from the \textbf{suffix tree} 
of the texts in linear time;
using a \textbf{suffix array} (not defined), 
as implemented in the \texttt{kernlab} package
is (still linear but) more efficient.

\textbf{Spectral clustering} is just a complicated name for clustering
(using traditional techniques) 
after PCA-dimension reduction using the kernel trick.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Invariant coordinate selection}\\
  D.E. Tyler et al.
\end{book}

Most multivariate data analysis methods are based on the variance matrix,
or more generally \emph{one} measure of dispersion.
\textbf{Invariant coordinate selection} (ICS) 
uses \emph{two} measures of dispersion,
$V_1$ and $V_2$,
and looks at the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of one wrt the other,
\ie, looks for $p \in \RR$ and $h \neq 0$ such that $V_2 h = \rho V_1 h$;
these are also the eigenvalues of $V_1^{-1/2} V_2 V_1^{-1/2}$.
For elliptical data, any two measures of dispersion are proportional
$V_2 = \lambda V_1$:
ICS highlights departure from gaussianity.

Examples of scatter (or dispersion) matrices include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The variance matrix;
\item The weighted variance (the weights could depend on the Mahalanobis distance);
\item $M$-estimates of the variance, \ie, an adaptively-weighted variance;
\item Higher breakdown robust scatter matrices: 
  MVE, MCD, $S$-estimates, $\tau$-estimates, etc.
  (they are computationnally expensive);
\item A dispersion matrix wrt to the origin 
  (\ie, the location estimator is constant);
\item A dispersion matrix of the pairwise differences,
  $\text{Disp}\,(x_i - x_j)_{i,j}$
\item Fourth central moments;
\item Tyler's shape matrix.
\end{compactitem}
Outliers are often spotted by looking at 
the Mahalanobis distance for one measure of dispersion:
besides (before) a plot of the first eigencoordinates,
a scatterplot of those distances for several measures 
of dispersion may also be helpful.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Tools for exploring multivariate data:\\ the package ICS}\\
  K. Nordhausen et al.\\
  Journal of Statistical Software (2008)
\end{book}

A more application-oriented presentation of invariant coordinate selection (ICS).
The ICS transformation can be described as:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Whiten the data wrt the first measure of dispersion:
  $x \mapsto (x - 1' \bar x) \text{Disp}^{-1/2}$
  (stopping after that step is not satisfactory:
  the square root is not unique
  and the whitening transformation is not affine-equivariant);
\item Perform a principal component analysis (PCA) on the whitened data, 
  wrt the second measure of dispersion.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Scala by Example}\\
  M. Odersky
\end{book}

Scala is a functional and object-oriented language,
with static typing and type inference
(somewhat inferior to Haskell),
whose main advantages are:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item It is a script-like language, easy to write and read;
\item It runs on the \textbf{Java Virtual Machine} (JVM) and
  can therefore leverage all existing Java libraries and
  the Java infrastructure and knowledge you may already have;
\item It provides a rather exhaustive implementation of parallelism patterns:
  Erlang's \textbf{actors}, lazy variables, explicit parallelism, Java threads, etc.;
\item Method names can contain weird characters (*:, etc.),
  facilitating the creation of libraries that look like
  domain-specific languages (DSL).
\end{compactitem}

Its main drawbacks are:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The type inference is not as good as Haskell's 
  (e.g., you have to provide the return type of recursive functions);
\item Function types can be unreadable, 
  with the name of the variable (function) in the middle of the type, as in C;
\item There are still too many parentheses.
\end{compactitem}

Here is a summary of the syntax.

When you define a variable,
it can be left unevaluated 
(\texttt{def}, this is useful for functions, which are just blocks),
immutable (\texttt{val}),
mutable (\texttt{var}),
lazy (\texttt{lazy val})
or evaluated in the background
(\texttt{var x = future(f(1))}).
\begin{verbatim}
  def f(
    g : Int => Int, // Function argument
    a : Int,
    b : => Int, // Not evaluated immediately
  ): Int = { ... }
  class Foo(a:Int, b:Int) {...} // private members
  class Bar(...) Extends Foo {
    ... 
    override def toString ...
  }
  val x = new Foo(1,2);
\end{verbatim}

A \textbf{trait} is a Java interface,
designed to be added to a class,
\ie, designed for multiple inheritance;
abstract classes are designed for single inheritance.

\textbf{Case classes} provide 
implicit constructors,
implicit getter methods 
and pattern matching,
\ie, implicit tests on the type of objects --
this is just syntactic sugar, but it increases code readability.
\begin{verbatim}
  abstract class A
  case class A1(n:Int)        extends A
  case class A2(n:Int, m:Int) extends A
  ...
  e match { case A1(n)   => n
            case A2(n,m) => n+m }
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Type parameters} (aka templates or generic types)
can also specify lower and upper bounds on types.
\begin{verbatim}
  class Set[A]
  // A implements the Ordered trait:
  class Set[A <: Ordered[A]] 
  // A can be converted into 
  // something that implements Ordered
  class Set[A <% Ordered[A]] 
\end{verbatim}
Inheritance relations can be automatically \textbf{lifted} to generic types,
so that if \verb+A<:B+, then \verb+Stack[A]<:Stack[B]+.
In some cases, this mapping is not covariant but contravariant:
e.g., if \verb+A<:B+, then \verb+Fun[B,C]<:Fun[A,C]+.
\begin{verbatim}
  class Stack[+A]
  class Fun[-A,+B]
\end{verbatim}
Functions are just objects with an \texttt{apply} method;
\texttt{f(x)} is just a shorthand for \texttt{f.apply(x)};
arrays implement the same interface and use the same syntax.

Tuples, written \texttt{(a,b)}, are just a shorthand for the \texttt{Tuple2[A,B]} type.

Besides \texttt{Array} (Java's mutable arrays),
Scala provides a \texttt{List} type
(homogeneous, immutable, unsuitable for random access),
as in Lisp, with the usual \texttt{map}, \texttt{foreach},
\texttt{forall}, \texttt{exists}
(called \texttt{all} and \texttt{any} in some other languages)
methods.
The \texttt{for} comprehension is a shorthand for those list operations.
\begin{verbatim}
  for(x <- xs if f(x)) yield g(x)
\end{verbatim}
\emph{Streams} are lazy (potentially infinite) lists; 
they have (almost) the same methods.
Iterators, \ie, classes with \texttt{hasNext} and \texttt{next} methods,
are an imperative analogue of streams;
prefer them to streams if you know previous values can be discarded.

Methods can have implicit arguments
(actually, even the method name can be implicit:
these are implicit conversions (casts)),
but this does not look as useful 
the named and implicit arguments
of most scripting languages.

Detailed examples include
an auction service (actors),
type inference,
electric circuit simulation,
$N$-queen problem (list comprehensions),
building $\mathbb{N}$,
Newton's method and more general fixed point problems,
stacks (type parameters).

This introduction does not mention some important features
of Scala, such as the ability to include XML directly in the code
(not as strings), with the \verb|<>| delimitors,
in the same way most languages allow you to enter strings directly
(not as arrays of characters), with the \verb|"| delimitor.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Regularized discriminant analysis}\\
  J.K. Friedman\\
  Journal of the American Statistical Association (1989)
\end{book}

Your data comes from a mixture of distributions,
$\sum_k \lambda_k f_k(x)$,
and you want to find from which class $k$
a given observation $x$ comes from.
If you knew the prior probabilities $\lambda_k$ 
and the conditional distributions $f_k$,
you would just use the Bayes rule.
If those conditional distributions are multivariate gaussian
with identical (resp. different) variance matrices,
obtained from the plug-in (aka sample) estimators,
this is linear discriminant analysis (LDA)
(resp. quadratic discriminant analysis (QDA)).
Some people also use diagonal discriminant analysis (DDA).

You can use a regularized estimator of those variance matrices.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Partial least squares: 
  a versatile tool for the analysis of high-dimensional genomic data}\\
  A.-L. Boulesteix and K. Strimmer (2006)
\end{book}

Should you want formulas to compute partial least squares (PLS) yourself,
here they are.
Let $X$ be the predictive variables 
(one observation per row, few rows, many columns)
and $Y$ the variable to predict. 
The data is assumed to come from latent components $T$,
\begin{align*}
  X &= T P' + \text{noise} \\
  Y &= T Q' + \text{noise}
\end{align*}
Once you have those latent components $T$, 
you can estimate $\hat Y$ by least squares.
The latent components are of the form $T = X W$,
where $W$ is defined by some iterative optimization procedure
(there are many of them: PLS1, PLS2, NIPALS, Kernel-PLS, SIMPLS, etc.)
such as
\begin{align*}
  \forall i \quad \text{Maximize } & w_i' X' Y Y' X w_i \\
  \text{such that } &  w_i' w_i = 1 \\
  \text{and } & \forall j < i \quad w_i'X' X w_j = 0.
\end{align*}
In R, check the \texttt{pls}, \texttt{pls.pcr},
\texttt{plsgenomics}, \texttt{gpls} packages.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-objective optimization\\ using genetic algorithms: a tutorial}\\
  A. Konak et al.\\
  Reliability engineering and system safety (2006)
\end{book}

Multiobjective optimization do not lead to a \emph{single}
but to a \emph{set} of solutions (the Pareto set, aka efficient frontier) --
transforming the problem into a single-objective one is rarely satisfactory:
it involves arbitrary choices (weights, utility function)
that often have a huge (and badly understood) effect on the solution.

Genetic algorithms (GA) naturally produce a population of solutions,
but care should be taken:
the solutions found should
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item be uniformly distributed
  (genetic algorithms tend to produce clusters (genetic drift):
  to prevent this, you can try to decrease
  the fitness of densely populated areas)
\item explore the whole spectrum of the efficient frontier
  (genetic algorithms tend to focus on the middle and miss the extremities --
  you may want to store non-dominated solutions in a separate list,
  to avoid losing them).
\end{compactitem}
There is a very large number of multi-objective genetic algorithms:
the authors provide a comparison table
(check PAES, NSGA-II, SPEA-2).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multi-objective generic algorithms: 
  problem difficulties and construction of test problems}\\
  K. Deb
\end{book}

A set of test problems for multi-objective genetic algorithms.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Spectral graph theory and its applications}\\
  D.A. Spielman (2004)
\end{book}

An interesting applied linear algebra course,
that explains how to read the properties of a graph
from the eigenvalues and vectors of its
incidence matrix or Laplacian matrix.
The Laplacian of an edge is $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -1 \\ -1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$
and the Laplacian of a graph is the sum of those of its edges.
Applications include
graph colouring, error correcting codes,
random matrices and random graphs,
graph approximation
(find a sparser graph
whose Laplacian matrix is close, in spectral norm --
this can be used to speed up linear system solving).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum overhang}\\
  M. Paterson et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0909.0093}, \texttt{arXiv:0910.2357}
\end{book}

Funny...

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{An evaluation of mathematics competitions using item response theory}\\
  J. Gleason\\
  Notices of the AMS (2008)
\end{book}

An introduction to item response theory (IRT).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Little B: Biological modeling}\\
\end{book}

Little B is a Lisp-like biological simulation language --
basically, to define large systems of ordinary differential equations (ODE)
from simple blocks.
Only have a look if you think Lisp has too few parentheses...

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{OpenModelica Users Guide}\\
  P. Fritzson et al. % (2008)
\end{book}
Modelica is a Pascal-like, object-oriented, equation-based,
domain-specific language (DSL),
targeted at physical (event-based) simulations,
probably similar to Matlab' Simulink;
there are interfaces to/from Matlab or Maple.
OpenModelica is a free implementation
with an Eclispe-based IDE (integrated development environment).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{WaveScript User Manual}\\
\end{book}

A (functional) language to describe (and run) data flow graphs.
The compiler is written in Scheme
and converts the code to C or MLtron
(there is also an unsupported C++ backend through XStream).

It is not a ``higher order data flow language'':
the graph does not change at runtime.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Scale theory, serial theory and voice leading}\\
  D. Tymoczko (2007)
\end{book}

When turning a harmonic progression into a set of melodies
(\ie, you have a chord voicing, often spread over several octaves,
you have chosen the next chord, but you still have to decide on the voicing),
composers try to use \emph{efficient voice leading},
\ie, try to limit how far each voice moves;
this facilitates the perception of independent musical lines
(in addition, you may want those voice leadings to avoid crossings and be bijective).
The number of voice leadings may look daunting
(especially for large chords, e.g., hexachords),
but the problem is not.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Freer than Max -- porting FTM to Pure Data}\\
  I. m Zm\"olnig (2007)
\end{book}

The FTM library, that provides complex data structures to data flow languages,
is now available for Pure Data --
it is an alternative to Pure Data's ``graphical data structures''
and the PDContainer (C++ STL) library.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Analytical features\\ 
  to extract harmonic or rythmic  information}\\
  G. Cabral et al.
\end{book}

Description of an automated musical feature 
(note, chord, rythm, timbre) extraction tool;
it just combines known algorithms:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Time-based pitch recognition algorithms compare
  (by looking at the correlation or just the distance)
  the signal with its lags; it works better for low frequency signals;
\item Frequency-based pitch recognition algorithms
  (e.g., the harmonic product spectrum (HPS))
  try to find a frequency $f$ for which the spectrum
  has peaks at $2f$, $3f$, $4f$, etc.
  (I expect this to fail for lamellophones);
  the cepstrum is mentionned but not defined;
\item For better pitch recognition, you can combine several algorithms;
\item Chord recognition algorithms split the spectrum into regions,
  compute the energy in each, assign it to the 12 notes,
  normalize the resulting vectorm and apply some machine-learning algorithm.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Can the Red Queen help catch the Snark?\\
  A co-evolutionary waveform\\
  transformation approach}\\
  M. Caetano et al.\\
  SBCM 2007
\end{book}

One can generate constantly changing sounds
with optimization algorithms
such as genetic algorithms:
those algorithms tend to converge,
but you can use them to model two populations,
predators and preys,
and have them \emph{coevolve}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Applications of group theory\\ on granular synthesis}\\
  R. Fabbri and A. Maia\\
  SBMC 2007
\end{book}

Granular synthesis is an extreme case of additive synthesis:
create a sound by combining (adding) hundreds or thousands of sound particles,
defined as small areas or clouds in the time$\times$frequency space
(with a gaussian envelope and a width of 10\,ms to 20\,ms).
The evolution of those grains with time is often defined 
by stochastic methods or finite automata.
The article suggests to use group theory instead,
with the symmetric group or some of its subgroups
(alternating, dihedral and cyclic groups).
The group action is not clearly defined; it could be:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The action of the symmetric group on the set of all sound particles,
  not just those currently used;
\item Some action on the parameters of the particles (frequency, duration, amplitude).
\end{compactitem}
At each step, a random element from the group is selected 
to transform the set of particles.

The implementation relies on
\href{http://www.sagemath.org/}{Sage},
\href{http://www.scipy.org/}{NumPy},
PyAUdiolab,
\href{http://matplotlib.sourceforge.net/}{MatplotLib},
wxPython.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Methods for evaluating clustering algorithms\\
    for gene expression data\\
    using a reference set of
    functional classes}\\
  S. Datta and S. Datta\\
  BMC Bioinformatics (2006)
\end{book}

How good are the results of your hierarchical clustering?
In a biological context, you can use gene ontology (GO) databases to compute
a biological homogeneity index
and a biological stability index.

This could be generalized to a non-biological setup:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For the homogeneity index, find another source of data
  and check if the clusters are consistent with it;
\item For the stability, just resample and compare.
\end{compactitem}

Also check the \texttt{pvclust} package.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Phantom probability}\\
  Y. Izhakian and Z. Izhakian\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0901.0902}
\end{book}

To model the fact that the probability of an event, $P(A) \in [0,1]$,
is not known precisely,
you can try to replace $[0,1]$ by something else.
I would have tried the ring of probability distributions on $[0,1]$,
but you can follow the lead of
synthetic geometry,
or algebraic geometry and its dual numbers,
which adds a nilpotent element $\epsilon$ to the real line
$\RR[\epsilon]/\langle\epsilon^2\rangle$,
and add an idempotent (phantom) element $\wp$:
$\RR[\wp]/\langle \wp^2 = \wp \rangle$.
Intuitively, use $a + b \wp$ to mean
``I estimated the probability as $a + \wp b$,
where $\wp$ is the probability of some other event
on which I know nothing''.
This can be generalized to
$$ 
  \dfrac{
    \RR[ \wp_1, \dots, \wp_n, \dots ] 
  }{
    \bigl\langle
      \wp_i \wp_j = \wp^{\mathstrut}_{\max(i,j)} 
    \bigr\rangle
  } 
$$
where the $\wp_i$ are more and more hidden --
you could have made them independent,
$\RR[\wp_1,\dots]/\rangle\wp_i^2=\wp_i\langle$.
Almost everything in probability theory (limit theorems, etc.)
can be generalized to probabilities with values in a phantom ring.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Synthetic differential geometry}\\
  M. Schulman (2006)
\end{book}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Decision making in phantom spaces}\\
  Y. Izhakian and Z. Izhakian 
\end{book}

Classical decision theory assumes that the probabilities of all events are known:
it addresses risk (we do not know which event will be realized)
but not uncertainty.
With phantom probability theory,
this can be generalized to situations where some but not all probabilities are known
(each unknown probability is a nilpotent phantom probability).
Plots of the efficient frontier have one more dimension for each phantom dimension.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A survey of product-integration 
  with a view toward application in survival analysis}\\
  R.D. Gill and S. Johansen\\
  The annals of statistics (1990)
\end{book}

If $X$ is a real (\ie, the matrix coefficients are signed measures)
matrix-valued measure on $(0,t]$,
then its \textbf{product integral} is
\begin{align*}
  Y(t) &=
  \mathop{\mathscr{P}}_{s \in (0,t]} \bigl( \textbf{1} + X(ds) \bigr) \\
  &=
  \lim_{\substack{
    0 = t_0 < t_1 < \cdots < t_n = t \\
    \max \va{ t_i - t_{i-1} } \to 0
  }}
  \prod \biggl(
    \textbf{1} + X\bigl( (t_i,t_{i-1}] \bigr)
  \biggr)
\end{align*}
It could also be defined by the Voltera equation 
(aka Kolmogorov forward equation)
$$ Y(t) = \textbf{1} + \int_{s \in (0,t]} Y(s^-) X(ds). $$
Here are some applications:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Link between survival and hazard functions:
  \begin{align*}
    T &: \text{positive random variable (survival time)} \\
    S(t) &= P[T>t] \text{ (survival function)} \\
    \lambda(t) &= - \dfrac{d\log S(t)}{dt} \text{ (hazard rate) }\\
    \Lambda(A) &= \int_A \lambda(u) du \text{ (survival measure)} \\
    S(t) &= \mathop{\mathscr{P}}_{(0,t]} ( 1 - d \Lambda )
  \end{align*}
\item In a censored context,
  the product-limit (Kaplan-Meier) estimator
  is the product integral of
  the empirical commulative hazard function
  (Nelson-Aalen estimator):
  this formulation can help derive properties of those estimators;
\item For multivariate censored data, there is no classical estimator
  (the naive one would depend on a choice of coordinates)
  but the product-integral can provide a
  (complicated but) well-defined one;
\item All this can be generalized to Markov processes
  (the survival analysis model uses 3 states: alive, censored, dead)
  by using matrices of size the number of states --
  it even works for countable state space Markov chains
  (e.g., Markov branching processes).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Current State of Java for HPC}\\
  B. Amedro et al.\\
  Inria (2008)
\end{book}

Java is useable for high performance computing (HPC),
e.g., with the ProActive library (LGPL, developed at the Inria)
as an MPI replacement,
but the performance greatly depends on the virtual machine (JVM) used
and communications can be a bottleneck.
\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Versatile and declarative dynamic programming using pair algebras}\\
  P. Steffen and R. Giegerich\\
  BMC Bioinformatics (2005)
\end{book}

When you need to implement several dynamic programming algorithms
(they are everywhere in bioinformatics),
you may want to factor out the algorithm
into an algebraic dynamic programming (ADP) algorithm;
what remains is then almost purely declarative:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The alphabet used by the input sequence;
\item The set of candicate solutions: they can be expressed as a set of trees, described by a \emph{grammar};
\item A way to compute the score of a candidate tree (recursively).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Least angle regression}\\
  B. Efron et al. (2003)
\end{book}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to the Kalman filter}\\
  G. Welch and G. Bishop (2001)
\end{book}

\nextbook

%% Above: 2009-08-31

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio optimization in R}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

Most portfolio optimization problems can be solved within R:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item For mean-variance optimization with no constraints (except perhaps a long-only one),
  use the \texttt{portfolio.optim} function in the \texttt{tseries} package;
\item For the whole efficient frontier, call the function inside a loop;
\item For the maximum Sharpe ratio portfolio, search on that efficient frontier;
\item For mean-variance optimization with linear constraints
  (linear transaction costs, maximum weights, sectors or countries, etc.),
  use \texttt{solve.QP} in the \texttt{quadprog} package;
\item Expected shortfall (ES, aka conditional value at risk, CVaR)
  optimization, is only a linear problem
  (but beware: it is a \emph{scenario-based} optimization) is just a
  linear problem:
  you can use the \texttt{Rglpk\_solve\_LP} function in the
  \texttt{Rglpk} package
  (it can also do mixed integer linear programmin (MILP), but not
  quadratic programming);
\item For more general constraints and/or performance/risk measures,
  such as 
  $$ \Omega(r) = 
  \dfrac
  { \displaystyle \int _r          ^\infty (1-F(u)) \, du }%
  { \displaystyle \int _{-\infty} ^r       F(u)     \, du } 
  $$
  the drawdown (which can be plotted as an \textbf{underwater graph},
  with the \texttt{chart.Drawdown} function),
  the \textbf{Rachev ratio} 
  $R = \text{CVar}^+_\alpha / \text{CVar}^-_\alpha$,
  you can check the \texttt{DEoptim} package.
\end{compactitem}
A few remarks:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Drawdown optimization looks very good in-sample,
  but it is a scenario-based optimization:
  how does it perform out-of-sample?
\item There is no mention of constraints on the number of assets or of mixed integer quadratic programmins (MIQP):
  what about \texttt{Rsymphony} (it is open-source)?
\item There is no mention of stochastic programming -- but it is not reasonable (yet?) for more than 2 or 3 assets.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Working with xts and quantmod}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

The \texttt{xts} package relies on \texttt{zoo} to provide \emph{time-based indexing}:
\begin{verbatim}
  x <- xts(rnorm(N, Sys.Date()-1:N))
  x["2009-01-01"]
  x[index(x) >= Sys.Date()-5]
  x["2009-01-01/2009-01-05"] # Interval
  x["2009-01"] # One month of data
\end{verbatim}
You may also want to check the
\texttt{to.period} (convert to OHLC (open-high-low-close)),
\texttt{endpoints} (to extract weekly, monthly, etc. data),
\texttt{period.apply}
functions.

The \texttt{quantmod} package abstracts data access,
from local (RData, CSV, SQLite, MySQL)
or public sources 
(\href{http://www.google.com/finance}{Google}, 
\href{http://finance.yahoo.com/}{Yahoo}, 
\href{http://www.oanda.com/}{Oanda}, 
\href{http://research.stlouisfed.org/fred2/}{FRED}).
The \texttt{getSymbols} functions assigns to local variables of the same name.
\begin{verbatim}
  getSymbols("AAPL;IBM")
  getSymbols("USD/EUR", src="oanda")
\end{verbatim}
You may also want to check:
\texttt{getDividends}, \texttt{getQuote},
\texttt{getSplits}, \texttt{getFX},
\texttt{getMetals}, \texttt{getOptionChain}.

There are also a few plotting functions.
\begin{verbatim}
  candleChart(
    GSPC, subset="2009-01-01", 
    theme="white", TA=NULL
  )
  chartSeries(IBM)
  addBands()
  ?newTA # to add your own TA indicators
  AddMACD(32,50,12)
  reChart(
    subset="2009", 
    theme="white", type="candles"
  )
  ?chartSeries3d # For yield curves, etc.
\end{verbatim}
Data can be loaded on-demand (lazily) and cached in memory or in a file.
\begin{verbatim}
  attachSymbols(
    DB=DDB_Yahoo(), 
    pos=2, prefix="E."
  )
  search()
  str(ls("DDB:Yahoo"))
\end{verbatim}
For more, check \texttt{TTR}, \texttt{blotter}, 
\texttt{PerformanceAnalytics}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistics for Financial Engineering:\\ Some R Examples}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

After clearly explaning the difference between linear, 
non-linear and non-parametric regression,
the author presents three applications of statistics in finance.

The \textbf{probability of default} (PD) is often modeled as
$$ P[ \text{default} \,|\, \text{rating} ] = \exp( \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{rating} ); $$
which can be transformed via a \textbf{Box-Cox transformation}
$ p \mapsto ( \kappa + p ) ^ \lambda $
(unless the problem dictates another family of transformations);
you cannot naively take the logarithm, because the probability can be zero
(many textbooks do this, and have to throw observations away,
resulting in a biased estimator). Whatever transformation you choose,
do not forget the \textbf{residual analysis}.
(The presenter wrote a book about transformations.)

Most stylized facts about an interest rate time series $(R_t)_t$ are visible in the following plots:
$$
  R_t \sim t,
  \quad
  \Delta R_t \sim t,
  \quad
  \Delta R_t \sim R_{t-1}
  \quad
  (\Delta_t)^2 \sim R_{t-1}.
$$
They can be modeled as a \textbf{diffusion process}
$$ \Delta_t = \mu( R_{t-1} ) + \sigma( R_{t-1} ) \epsilon_t $$
where the drift $\mu$ and the volatility $\sigma$
can be non-linear (e.g., $\sigma(r)=\beta_0r^\beta_1$)
or \textbf{non-parametric}.
With 50 years of data, non-parametric regression
(e.g., with the \texttt{SemiPar} package --
the presenter wrote a book on non-parametric estimation with the package author),
non-parametric regression performs better:
the parametric model will be outside the confidence interval of the non-parametric one.
In this case, the residuals look GARCH
\begin{verbatim}
  garch(x=res^2, order=c(1,1))
\end{verbatim}
but the GARCH residuals still have some AR(1) noise
\begin{verbatim}
  garchFit(
    formula = ~arma(1,0)+garch(1,1), 
    data = res
  )
\end{verbatim}
and are far from Gaussian
\begin{verbatim}
  garchFit(
    formula = ~arma(1,0)+garch(1,1), 
    data = res, 
    cond.dist = "std"
  )
\end{verbatim}
This 2-step process may be suboptimal.

The last example tries to predict future returns (starting with the worst):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The average of the previous returns;
\item The CAPM,
  $ \text{forecast} = \beta \cdot \text{previous returns} $;
\item Bayesian shrinkage:
  $$ \text{forecast} = \lambda \cdot \text{previous returns} + (1-\lambda) \cdot \text{market returns} $$
\item The previous market returns (this is the best forecast, but all stocks end up with the same forecast).
\end{compactitem}
The \textbf{bayesian shrinkage} can be implemented with WinBUGS/R2WinBUGS
(OpenBugs/BRugs is no longer on CRAN, 
and OpenBugs was written in a obscure, commercial, 
non-textual, Pascal-like language 
(the source file is a binary file);
\href{http://www-ice.iarc.fr/~martyn/software/jags/}{JAGS}
is not mentionned):
\begin{align*}
  \mu_i   & \sim N(\alpha, \sigma_1^2) \\
  r_{i,t} & \sim N(\mu_i,  \sigma_2^2);
\end{align*}
this is a data-driven shrinkage: the amount of shrinkage is
$ \sigma_1^2 / \sigma_2^2 $.

% Not enough code: not everything is directly reproducible.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk Capital for Interacting Market and Credit Risk: VEC and GVAR models}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

% Not as clear as it should be...

International financial regulations (Basel II) assume that market and credit risk are independent
and consider that assumption conservative:
actually, the interaction between market and credit risk can be benign or malign.
For small fluctuations, this can be seen from a Taylor expansion:
let $x$ be the risk factors (market, credit);
let $f(x)$ be the value of your portfolio,
then
$$ f(x + \epsilon ) = f(x) + f'(x) \cdot \epsilon + f''(x) \cdot \epsilon \cdot \epsilon + o(\va{\epsilon}^2), $$
where the bilinear form $f''(x)$ can be positive, negative, or neither.
This shows the problem for small fluctuations: 
we would like a decomposition
$$ f(x+\epsilon) = f(x) + 
g_{\text{market}}(x, \epsilon_{\text{market}}) + 
g_{\text{credit}}(x, \epsilon_{\text{credit}}) $$
for large fluctuations as well --
this works iff the portfolio is \emph{separable}, \ie,
$f(x) = f_{\text{market}}(x) + f_{\text{credit}}(x)$.

The rest of the presentation is a confusing example 
(the GVAR in the title is apparently 
a vector autoregression (VAR) with fewer parameters).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{ICA for multivariate financial time series}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

Since independent component analysis (ICA) is an optimization problem, you can easily add constraints;
you could define a \textbf{constrained PCA} in the same way.

This presentation also tries to give technical details on how ICA is estimated
(but it quickly becomes a list of formulas with no explanations)
and how a time dimension can be added
(but their ``time-varying mixing matrix'' looks like a moving window estimator).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Random portfolios: Practice and Theory}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

\textbf{Random portfolios} can be seen 
as a kind of bootstrap or permutation test.

The basic idea is the following: since we add so many constraints, many things only depend on the constraints;
random portfolios can measure the performance or risk coming from the constraints
and isolate the value added by the portfolio manager.

Random portfolios (and matched portfolios)
can replace a benchmark or a peer group:
they will be less biased and lead to tests with a higher \textbf{power};
they can also incorporate more information,
such as the position at the begining of the period
and the turnover constraint.

Random portfolios can also help measure the (sometimes unwanted) impact of constraints:
you can compare the performance and risk of random portfolios for several sets of constraints
(e.g., loose or tight constraints on sector weights). [This was new.]

Generating random portfolios is problematic. [This is also new.]
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A rejection strategy is not reasonable (there are too few portfolios satisfying the constraints);
\item Algorithms to sample inside a polytope do not apply if there are non-linear constraints;
\item You can compute an optimal portfolio for random returns, but this may be biased;
\item You can use a random search:
  start with a random,
  unconstrained portfolio and minimize a penalty for broken constraints;
  however,
  you will be close to a binding constraint
  (this does not worry me: we are in high dimension, everything is close to the boundary)
  and the sampling will not be uniform
  (this is not very worrying: this is almost exactly what a portfolio manager does,
  except that he starts with non-random returns).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Matching portfolios}\\
  D. Kane\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

To measure the performance of a portfolio,
one may look at the average capitalization, the momentum,
and the book market value of its constituents
and compare with the corresponding quintile portfolio:
but you have a single portfolio and
the more factors you add, the emptier those quintile portfolios become.
\emph{Matching} views those portfolio characteristics
(except those in which the portfolio manager claims expertise)
as constraints and build a portfolio matching those constraints.

I do not see the difference with \emph{random portfolios}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantile regression in R}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

The definition of a quantile can be formulated as a linear optimization problem
and this generalizes readily to a regression setup
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_t &= \argmin_{\alpha \in \RR} 
               \sum_{i=0}^n \rho_\tau ( y_i - \alpha ) \\
  \rho_\tau( u ) &= (\tau - \mathbf{1}_{u<0}) u.
\end{align*}
Even with a linear model, quantile regression can highlight non-linear features,
such as the conditional distribution of the data
(this is not as general as the distribution of $y|x$, but almost:
there is a model, which assumes a linear relation 
between the quantiles of $x$ and (a linear combination of) $y$,
but that relation can depend on the quantile;
in particular, we can end up with non-gaussian, and even multimodal, distributions)
or the dependence of the regression coefficients on the quantile
(for an additive gaussian model,
we would expect the intercept to be the inverse cumulative distribution function of the quantile
and the other coefficients to be constant).

Two plots: conditional densities (this is similar to a violin plot, but with an underlying linear model); coef $\sim$ quantile.

In a time series setup, you can use quantile regression to estimate a
\textbf{quantile autoregression} (QAR):
it it will be non-gaussian, but still partly parametric.

Quantile regression is actually equivalent to CVaR (conditional value at risk) portfolio optimization;
by approximating the weight function of a spectral risk measure by a locally constant function,
quantile regression can be used to optimize portfolios for any ``pessimistic'' risk measure
(this has nothing to do with quantile regression: it applies to linear programming in general).

The presentation ends up with a non-gaussian regression
$$ \text{score} \sim \text{team 1} + \text{team 2} + \text{home advantage} $$
(all the predictors are qualitative variables, the variable to predict is a count variable:
this would classically be estimated by a Poisson model --
but we would have to check this distributional assumption).
With quantile regression, we keep the linearity of the model, but remove any distributional assumption;
the model can be estimated as a (not unreasonably) large but sparse linear program
and one can \emph{sample} from the distribution of scores,
in a half-bayesian fashion.

Quantile regression can be seen as a
prior-less bayesian regression
with no distributional assumptions.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Using R for hedge fund of funds management}\\
  E. Zivot\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

When faced with non-gaussian data, use the
\href{http://www.thejournalofrisk.com/public/showPage.html?page=jor_v11n2a4}{Cornish-Fisher expansion}
to compute the value at risk (VaR) or the expected shortfall (ES, CVaR).

If the time series of returns do not have the same length:
select the best risk factors;
compute the exposure of the funds to those factors;
simulate the missing returns;
use those simulated returns to estimate the
marginal contribution to risk (MCTR)
or risk attribution for VaR or ES.
This is available in the \texttt{PerformanceMetrics} package.

This reminds me of the problem of estimating a variance matrix
$\var(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ when there are many missing values:
the maximum-likelihood estimator can be computed explicitely
(this is ``just'' linear algebra, albeit horrible),
but an MCMC simulation is easier to implementat
(and less likely to be buggy).
However, even for toy examples, the convergence is horribly slow.

The presentation mentions but does not tackle the following problems:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Biases (survivorship, reporting, backfill);
\item Serial correlation (performance smoothing; illiquid positions).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Particle learning}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

Not understandable. None of the articles they refer to are available.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Performance Analysis in R}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

Clear presentation of what the \texttt{PerformanceAnalytics} package can do:
measure, compare, decompose the performance or risk of portfolios.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Market Microstructure Tutorial}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

Market microstructure is the study of why markets are not efficient and how to profit from it
(or, equivalently, help make them more efficient);
this includes behavioural effects.

The \emph{Glosten-Milgrom model} considers two traders:
one (informed) always buys (or sells, this is fixed at the begining);
the other (non-informed) buys or sells with probability $1/2$
(they just buy or sell, the model does not consider the need to find a counterparty);
only one trader (selected at random) trades at each point in time.
We define the \emph{bid} (respectively, \emph{ask}) 
as the probability that the informed trader is
buying (resp. selling)
given that the last trade was a buy (resp. sell).
The bid and ask converge towards the true price (0 or 1)
(\ie, the market goes where the informed trader wants:
market manipulation is indistinguishable from price discovery).

The \emph{Kyle model} considers a market maker facing informed and non-informed traders
and sets the price to the expected true price given the total order size he sees;
the informed trader chooses the order size to maximize profit.
There again, information leaks very quickly.


\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Event study: a change-point model approach}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

Hidden variable (aka latent variable, or regime switching if that variable is discrete)
models can be estimated via Monte Carlo simulations.
In this example, we consider spells of iid gaussian random variables

[[PLOT]]

and estimate the model by Gibbs sampling, with a bayesian prior:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Estimate the state at each point in time;
\item Estimate the parameters in each spell;
\item Estimate the transition probabilities;
\item Iterate.
\end{compactitem}
(At each step, we consider the whole time series.)
This can be repeated with a different number of regimes,
and the best model can be selected by looking at the \emph{Bayes factor}.

In a financial setting, breakpoints need not coincide with announcements:
they can occur before.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting structural breaks in tail behavior}\\
  W.H. Liu\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

The \emph{Hills estimator} of the tail index (and the tail index itself)
only makes sense for power law tails and, even in that case, is known to be very noisy.
Replacing the power law by a general extreme value (GEV) distribution
does not solve those problems: tests for structural breaks
(I do not know them:
SupLN,
OLS-based CUSUM,
Nyblom-Hansen,
generalized M-fluctuation)
return a surprisingly high number fo potential regime changes.

For risk management purposes, estimators of GEV models are as problematic as the Hills estimator.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio Optimization with R/RMetrics}\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

Quick overview of what is available in RMetrics for portfolio optimization --
there is an \href{http://www.rmetrics.org/ebook.htm}{ebook} with the same title
(it excludes: quadratic (non-linear) constraints, integer constraints,
non-linear objectives, Black-Litterman copula pooling (BLCP),
quadratic lower partial moments (QLPM), copula tail risk:
these will be covered in a second volume).
The list of packages looks exhaustive:
\texttt{Rglpk},
\href{http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/Rsymphony/index.html}{\texttt{Rsymphony}},
\texttt{Rlpsolve},
\texttt{quadprog},
\texttt{Ripop},
\texttt{Rsocp},
\texttt{Rdonlp2},
\texttt{Rnlminb}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Econometrics and practice: mind the gap!}\\
  Y. Chalabi and D. W\"urtz\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

There is an unreasonable number of variants of the GARCH model, 
but fitting them poses many practical issues 
(bugs, parameter initialization, optimization schemes, etc.).

The presentation slides contain some sample code:
it is surprisingly straightforward
(it would be trickier to estimate a stochastic volatility model, 
\ie, a model with two sources of randomness: 
you would need to consider a time series of (hidden) innovations).

The stationary assumption is often violated.

We might be tempted to use \emph{robust} methods to throw outliers away, 
but we cannot afford it: we would underestimate risk. 
Instead, you can include them in the model, 
either as isolated points or as a different regime: 
\textbf{MS-GARCH} (Markow-Switching GARCH) is one such example.

\textbf{Indirect inference} can help fit a difficult-to-fit model $g$
by using an (easier-to-fit) model $f$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Estimate the auxiliary model $f$ in the data $y$: $\hat\theta$;
\item For all possible values of the parameters $\rho$ of the model $g$,
  generate a sample $\tilde y$, 
  and estimate the auxilliary model $f$ on it: 
  $\tilde \theta( \rho )$.
\item Find the parameters $\rho$ that minimize the distance 
  $\n{ \hat \theta - \tilde \theta( \rho ) }.$
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to high-performance\\ computing with R}\\
  D. Eddelbuettel\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

You can profile your code with
the \texttt{system.time}, \texttt{Rprof}, \texttt{Rprofmen}, \texttt{tracemem} functions;
the \texttt{profr} and \texttt{proftools} package;
the \texttt{prof2dot.pl}, \texttt{valgrind}, \texttt{kcachegrind}, 
\texttt{sprof}, \texttt{oprofile}, Google PerfTools tools.

Vectorization, with the \texttt{*apply} or \texttt{outer} functions,
or just-in-time compilation with Ra 
(a set of patches for R, available for Debian/Ubuntu -- 
a very search-engine-unfriendly name)
and the \texttt{jit} package, can speed things up.

For the best performance, you can rewrite the most time-consuming parts of the code in C/C++:
with \texttt{.C}, the C function uses C types;
with \texttt{.Call}, it uses R types (\texttt{SEXP});
with \texttt{Rcpp}, the conversion between R and C++ types (templates) 
is transparent.
The \texttt{inline} package and the \texttt{cfunction} function 
allow you to put the C code 
(with \texttt{.Call} or \texttt{.C} conventions) 
directly inside the R code -- 
as with Perl's \texttt{Inline::C} module.

In the other direction, 
\texttt{RInside} allows you to evaluation R code from a C/C++ program.

Other high-performace topics were not mentionned:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Parallel computing: MPI, nws, snow;
\item Out-of-memory computations: 
  \texttt{biglm}, \texttt{ff}, \texttt{big\-matrix9};
\item Scripting and automation.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A latent-variable approach\\ to validate credit rating systems with R}\\
  B. Gr\"un et al.\\
  \href{http://rinfinance.quantmod.com/presentations/}{R in Finance 2009}
\end{book}

Credit rating of several firms (obligors) 
by several raters (banks, rating agencies)
can be modeled as a mixed model:
let the observed score $S_{i,j}$ be 
the probit of the announced probability of default
for obligor $i$ according to rater $j$:
$$ S_{i,j} = S_i + \mu_j + \sigma_j Z_{i,j}, $$
where $S_i$ is the latent (consensus) rating 
and $\mu_j$ the bias of rater $j$.

This can be generalized into a dynamic model by assuming the latent probability is AR(1)
and examined, by Gibbs sampling,
with the 
\href{http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/rjags/index.html}{\texttt{rjags}} 
and 
\href{http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/coda/index.html}{\texttt{coda}} 
packages.
In particular, you can look at: bias and variance of raters, consensus rating;
and the residuals can be further analyzed.

In practice, you have to map the ordinal rating of the rating agencies (AAA, etc.) into probabilities:
this hides the bias.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{RMeCab}
\end{book}
\href{http://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/MeCab}{MeCab} 
is a Japanese morphological analyzer,
\ie, a tool to cut sentences into words
(there are no spaces between words in Japanese).
It looks more robust than its competitors (ChaSen, CaboCha) 
and can now be used from R.

After installing R, MeCab, MeCab-ipadic 
(beware, they apparently only provide a \emph{binary} package, 
even for Linux):
\\\verb+  install.packages(+
\\\verb+    "RMecab.tar.gz",+
\\\verb+    destdir=".", repos=NULL+
\\\verb+  )+
\\\verb+  library(RMecab)+
\\
To split a sentence into words, and get the POS (part-of-speech) of those words:
\\{\ttfamily\mbox{}\ \ RMeCabC("{\Japanese 猫が鼠を食べた。}\!\!\!")}\\
The default output of the command-line tool
(word, POS0, POS1, POS2, POS3 (more and more detailed),
conjugated form (?), dictionary form,
pronunciation, pronunciation (yes, twice)):
\\\verb+  RMeCabText("text.txt")+\\
The frequency dataframe, with primary key word-POS0-POS1 (sort it before use):
\\\verb+  RMeCabFreq("text.txt")+\\
The term-document matrix:
\\{\ttfamily\mbox{}\ \ docMatrix("corpus/")}
\\\verb+  d <- docMatrix(+
\\\verb+    "corpus/",+
\\\verb+    pos=c("+{\ttfamily{\Japanese 名詞}","{\Japanese 形容詞}"))}
\\{\ttfamily\mbox{}\ \ cor(d)}\\
N-grams:
\\\verb+  Ngram("text.txt", N=4)+\\
However, my current (NLP) interest is lexicography
and I am skeptical about the ability of R/MeCab to
handle very large datasets (say, all of Wikipedia).

I still have to read the book,
\emph{R\,{\Japanese\itshape によるテキストマイニング入門}}
({\Japanese 石田基広}, 2008).

\nextbook

 \begin{book}
  \emph{Pessimistic Portfolio Allocation\\ and Choquet Expected Utility}\\
\end{book}

Details on the relation between
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Choquet expected utility (very similar to \emph{cumulated prospect theory}:
  when computing the expected utility, we give more or less importance
  (we distort the probability of) favourable or unfavourable events);
\item coherent (or spectral, or pessimistic) risk measures.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation and decomposition of downside risk for portfolios with non-normal returns}\\
  K. Boudt et al (2007)
\end{book}

Some people are apparently still using gaussian parametric value at risk (VaR) estimators...
 
After recalling the Edgeworth expansion of a cumulative distribution function (around the Gaussian)
$$ G( z ) = \Phi( z ) + \sum_{i=1}^r P_i(z) $$
and the Cornish-Fisher (CF) expansion of the corresponding quantile function
\begin{align*}
  G^{-1}( \alpha ) &= z_\alpha + \phi(z_\alpha) \sum_{i=1}^r P_i^*(z_\alpha) \\
  z_\alpha &= \Phi^{_1}( \alpha )
\end{align*}
the authors compute the \textbf{modified VaR} (mVaR) and the
\textbf{modified expected shortfall} (mES),
corresponding to the second order Cornish-Fisher expansion,
\ie, a Gaussian distribution modified to have non-trivial skew and kurtosis.
 
By differentiating the mVaR or mES with respect to portfolio weights, one can compute risk attribution.
 
Since the estimation of higher moments is noisy,
the authors suggest to clean the data before computing skewness and kurtosis:
they do not remove or truncate outliers,
but just \emph{shrink} them
(the cleaning threshold should be beyond the desired VaR threshold).
 
\nextbook
 
\begin{book}
  \emph{SemiPar, an R package\\ for semiparametric regression}\\
  M.P Wand et al. (2005)
\end{book}

The main R functions for semiparametric regression are
\texttt{gam::gam}, \texttt{SemiPar::spm}, \texttt{mgcv::gam};
you may also want to check the \texttt{lmeSplines}
and \texttt{fields} packages.
\texttt{SemiPar} is younger than \texttt{gam}, but allows for bivariate terms and better plots.
\begin{verbatim}
  r <- spm( y ~ f(x) )
  plot(r)
  points(x, y)
  plot(r, drv=1) # First derivative
\end{verbatim}
You can impose the smoothing parameter (e.g., \texttt{spar=3}),
the number of degrees of freedom (\texttt{df} for a single term,
\texttt{adf} (approximate degrees of freedom) if there are several terms)
or the basis (e.g., \texttt{basis="trunc.poly"}, \texttt{degree=5} --
by default, cubic radial basis functions).
\begin{verbatim}
  u ~ x + f(y)     # semi-parametric
  u ~ f(x) + f(y)  # additive model
  u ~ f(x,y)       # bivariate smoothing
\end{verbatim}
For bivariate smoothing (used in spatial statistics, you may want to check and/or alter the nodes: there may be too few or too
many). For higher dimensions, you can use a \emph{geoadditive} model
\begin{verbatim}
  u ~ f(x,y) + f(z)
\end{verbatim}
The function also accepts binary data (\texttt{family="binom\-ial"}), count data (\texttt{family="poisson"}), or mixed models (with
the usual horrible syntax).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Unit root quantile autoregression inference}\\
  R. Koenker and Z. Xiao
\end{book}

Financial time series are often autoregressive with a root near unity:
for quantile auto-regression, this changes the asymptotic distributions,
but the correct tests are more powerful (for non-gaussian innovations)
than tests based on a gaussian model.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Conditional quantiles of volatility\\ in equity index and foreign exchange data}\\
  J.W. Galbraith (2001)
\end{book}

Quantile regression $r_t \sim r_{t-1} + \sigma_t$
can highlight asymmetric behaviour 
in time series of foreign exchange returns.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{VAR, SVAR and SVEC Model: Implementation within R package vars}\\
  B. Pfaff\\
  Journal of Statistical Software (2008)
\end{book}

The \texttt{vars} package estimates vector autoregressive (VAR) models
$$ y_t = A_1 y_{t-1} + \cdots + A_p y_{t-p} + u_t $$
or structural VAR (SVAR) models
$$ A y_t = A_1^* y_{t-1} + \cdots + A_p^* y_{t-p} + B \epsilon_t $$
(for the model to be identifiable, you have to add restrictions (prior knowledge) on $A$ and/or $B$)
or structural vector error correction (SVER) models
(not explained clearly).
The package should be used in conjuction with the \texttt{urca} package,
whose author systematically refuses to output $p$-values and only provides
test statistics and a couple of critical values.
\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalized linear mixed model analysis\\
  via sequential Monte Carlo sampling}\\
  Y. Fan et al. (2007)
\end{book}

Generalized linear mixed models (GLMM) are notoriously more complicated to estimate than linear mixed models or generalized linear
models.
\textbf{Sequential Monte Carlo} (SMC) sampling 
is an alternative to Monte Carlo Markov Chain (MCMC) sampling:
it is a \emph{static analogue of particle filters}.
You have to add a time dimension to the problem, for instance,
by adding the observations one by one
(the time-indexed family of posterior distributions 
is more and more precise)
or by sampling from the family of posterior distributions
$( \pi_0^{1-t} \pi^t )_{t\in[0,1]}$,
where $\pi$ is the desired posterior and $\pi_0$ the prior
or a gaussian distribution centered on an approximate fit.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Parsimonious classification\\ via generalized mixed models}\\
  G. Kauermann et al. (2007)
\end{book}

Supervised classification can be performed via a generalized additive logistic model
(logictic GAM)
$$ P( Y = 1 ) = f_1(x_1) + \cdots + f_p( x_p ) $$
where the unknown functions $f_i$ are estimated as generalized splines or,
equivalently, as a mixed model
\begin{align*}
  f_i(x) &= \sum_j u_{i,j} b_j(x) \\
  u_{ij}   &\sim \text{some prior}
\end{align*}
where the $b_j$ are the basis functions and the $u_j$ their coefficients.\\
See also the \texttt{gam.step} function for a similar approach.

\nextbook

% older
\begin{book}
  \emph{Anomalous returns\\ in a neural network equity-ranking predictor}\\
  J.B. Satinover and D. Sornette (2008)
\end{book}
A nonlinear AR(10) model 
fitted on quaterly retruns 
with a neural returns 
can beat the S\&P\,500 (or would have, in the 1990s),
even if you include trading costs 
(but with a much higher volatility).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting speculative bubbles\\ created in experiments\\ via decoupling in agenbt-based models}\\
  M. Roszcynska et al. (2008)
\end{book}

Bubbles are periods when everyone makes the same investment decisions (one could argue that short-selling helps prevent bubbles,
by allowing people to make opposite investment decisions).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Decomposition of proper scores}\\
  J. Br\"ocker (2008)\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0806.0813}
\end{book}
 
\emph{Scores} are a way of measuring the quality of estimators of probabilities (or estimators of probability distributions).
 
An estimator $\hat p$ of the probability of a binary random variable $Y$ is \emph{reliable} if
$$ \forall p \quad P[ Y = 1 \,|\, \hat p = p ] = p. $$
 
Given an observation $y \in [0,1]$ of $Y$, 
the \textbf{Brier score} of $\hat p$ is
$(y-\hat p)^2$; with several observations and forecasts, 
you would take the average of those quantities. 
This sum of squares is used in decision theory.
 
This can be generalized to non-binary (discrete or continuous) random variables (the article is not always clear);
some properties of the Brier score lead to its decomposition into \emph{reliability} and \emph{sharpness} (information content).
 
\nextbook
 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model of information diffusion}\\
  D.V. Lande
\end{book}
Cellullar automata (the game of life) on a 2-dimensional grid can be used to model information diffusion.
(I am skeptical about the lack of influence of the graph structure: a 2-dimensional graph and a small-world graph are very
 different -- physicists are already used to seeing differences between 2- and 3-dimensional phenomena...)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{``Bubbles in society''\\The example of the United States\\ Apollo program}\\
  M. Gisler and D. Sornette
\end{book}

Bubbles, \ie, collective over-enthusiasm, are (unavoidable and) beneficial: they foster innovation and allow the development of
new technologies; examples include wars, cold wars, space programs, etc.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The $w$-index:\\a significant improvement on the $h$-index}\\
  Q. Wu (2008)
\end{book}

One can reduce the number of citations papers of a researcher get 
(or any finite sequence of positive numbers) 
to a single index,
$$ \sup \{ w \,:\, \text{he has $w$ papers cited at most 10 times} \}. $$
If you plot the number of citations against the paper number $w$ 
(after ranking the papers in decreasing citation order), 
this is the intersection of the curve with the $n = 10 w$ line. 
Of course, this ``10'' is arbitrary...

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Probing the improbable:\\ 
  methodological challenges for risks\\
  with low probabilities and high stakes}\\
  T. Ord et al.
\end{book}

When estimating very low probabilities (e.g., asteroid impact), you should consider that the argument could be incorrect, for many
reasons:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Theory (e.g., choice of Newtonian mechanics or general relativity);
\item Model: is it broad/detailed enough (which celestial bodies to include, influence of their shape or structure, etc.);
retraction rates of academic papers suggests that the proportion of inadequate models is at least $10^{-3}$;
\item Calculations: intentional simplification (if a physicist is doing the computations) or errors: the probability of
computational errors varies between 1\% (drug doses in hospitals) and 90\% (if you use spreadsheets).
\end{compactitem}
(The problem is sometimes obvious in the conclusion: claims that something ``is impossible'' are almost always wrong.)
 
The estimate can be improved by providing several models.
 
Contrary to what the title suggests, the article only focuses on the probabilities, not on the stakes: there can be unpleasant
situations where
$ \int \text{Stake} \, dP = \infty. $

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonlinear dimension reduction with kernel sliced inverse regression}\\
  Y.-R. Yeh et al. (2007)
\end{book}

The sliced inverse regression (SIR), a linear dimension reduction technique (not explained clearly: in a regression setup $y \sim
x$, look for $y = f(\beta x)$, where $\beta x$ has much fewer columns than $x$ and $f$ is a linear or nonlibnear function, by
looking at $\Sigma_{x|y}$ and $\Sigma_x$) can be extended with the ``kernel trick''.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The Fast-$\tau$ estimator for regression}\\
  M. Salibian et al.
\end{book}

A $\tau$-estimator is defined as
\begin{align*}
  & \hat \beta = \argmin_\beta s^2(\beta) \tfrac 1n \sum_i \rho_2\left( \dfrac{y_i - \beta x_i}{s(\beta)}\right) \\
  & \tfrac1n \sum_i \rho_1\left( \dfrac{y_i - \beta x_i}{s(\beta)}\right) = 1.
\end{align*}
(The second equation defines $s$.)
If $\rho_1 = \rho_2$, it is an $S$-estimator (and the first equation simplifies to $\hat\beta=\argmin_\beta s(\beta)$).
The \emph{breakdown} is determined by $\rho_1$; the \emph{efficiency} by $\rho_2$.
 
Contrary to common belief, 
$\tau$-estimators can be efficiently implemented.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust performance testing\\ with the Sharpe ratio}\\
  O. Ledoit and M. Wolf\\
  Journal of empirical finance (2008)
\end{book}

The tests traditionally used to compare Sharpe ratios 
are not robust to fat tails, autocorrelation or volatility clustering.
One can use the same test statistics, but ``assume'' that their limiting distribution is $N(0,\Psi)$, with $\Psi$ unknown (instead
of known) and estimated with an HAC (heteroskedasticity and autocorrelation robust) estimator. The resulting $p$-values are
higher, \ie, apparent differences are less significant.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation, hierarchies and networks\\ in financial markets}\\
  M. Tumminello et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0809.4615}
\end{book}

Hierarchical clustering from a correlation matrix (minimum spanning tree (MST), average linkage MST, etc. -- try several algorithms) 
can also be used to \emph{filter} the correlation matrix: if $C_1$ and $C_2$ are two separate subtrees joined by an
edge (or an internal node),
$$ \exists c \quad \forall i \in C_1 \quad \forall j \in C_2 \quad \cor(i,j) = c $$
(as a result, there are as many (potentially) different values in the correlation matrix as internal nodes: $n-1$).
The structure of the filtered correlation matrix can replace the traditional factor model $V = e v e'$.
This \textbf{hierarchical nested factor model} has one factor for each internal node, but a \emph{bootstrap} will often suggest that
only a few are significant: fuse the non-needed nodes with their parent.
 
To check the goodness-of-fit  of this hierarchical nested factor model,
compare the Kullback--Leibler distance between the filtered matrix
and the (raw) observed one with what you would expect of the observed matrix if the filtered matrix was the right one
(with a multivariate Gaussian or Student distribution).
 
To check the stability of the filtering algorithm, check the average Kullback--Leibler distance bewteen filtered bootstraped
matrices: random matrix theory (RMT) is bad, shrinkage and hierarchical nested factor models are good.
 
\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock market volatility:\\
  an approach based on the Tsallis entropy}\\
  S.R. Bentes et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0809.4570}
\end{book}

Entropy is often used in a cross-sectional way, on the distribution of prices (or market capitalizations) of stocks at a given
date, but you can also use it in a time series fashion, on the distribution of the returns of a single stock, as yet another
measure of volatility.
 
They authors fail to properly define and motivate the Tsallis entropy
$$ S_q(p) = \dfrac{ 1 - \int p^q }{ q - 1 } $$
they prefer over the Shannon entropy
$$ H(p) = \int - p \ln p. $$
 
\nextbook
 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Smile dynamics:\\ a theory of implied leverage effect}\\
  S. Ciliberti et al. (2008)\\ 
  \href{http://arxiv.org/abs/0809.3375}{arXiv:0809.3375}
\end{book}

The volatility surface, 
if you only want a Taylor expansion around the money, 
can be studied without 
a complete model of the returns of the underlying
(e.g., local volatility 
(the volatility is a deterministic function of the price),
stochastic volatility, GARCH, jumps and L\'evy processes,
multiscale fractals, SABR, etc.):
the \emph{term structure} of the skewness (and higher moments) suffices
\begin{align*}
  \Sigma(K, T) &= \sigma \left( 1 + \dfrac{\zeta(T)}{6}M + \dfrac{\kappa(T)}{24}(M^2-1) + O(M^3) \right) \\
  M &= \dfrac{1}{\sigma \sqrt T}\left( \dfrac{K e^{-rT}}{S} - 1 \right)
\end{align*}
where $K$ is the strike, $T$ the maturity, $M$ the moneyness,
$\zeta(T)$ the skewness (of the returns of the underlying over intervals of size $T$),
$\kappa(T)$ the kurtosis.
The \emph{term structure} of the skewness
$$ \zeta(T) = \dfrac{\zeta(1)}{\sqrt T} + \dfrac{3}{\sqrt T} \sum_{t=1}^T \left( 1 = \dfrac tT \right) \rho_{\text{leverage}}(T)
$$
can be computed from the \emph{leverage correlation function} (that quantifies the \emph{leverage effect}, \ie,
the correlation between past returns and future squared returns)
$$ \rho_{\text{leverage}} (t) = \dfrac{E[ r_i r_{i+t}^2]}{\sigma^3} $$
which can be approximated as
$$ \rho_{\text{leverage}} (t) = - A e^{-t/\tau}. $$
 
The \emph{dynamics} of the implied volatility are between those suggested by the \emph{sticky strike}
($\Sigma = \Sigma(K,T)$ (does not change as time passes), an upper bound, correct for short maturities)
and the \emph{sticky delta} ($\Sigma = \Sigma(M,T)$, lower bound, good approximation for longer maturities).
 
In the same way a 1-factor model of the returns of a stocks allows the volatility to be decomposed into a market volatility and an
idiosyncratic volatility, it also allows for the decomposition of the skewness (into three terms).
 
Similarly, long-range volatility clustering would induce a non-trivial term structure on the kurtosis.
 
\nextbook
 
\begin{book}
  \emph{An extensive set of scaling laws\\ and the FX coastline}\\
  J.B. Glattfelder et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0809.1040}
\end{book}

The laws are all of the form
$\text{something} \propto (\Delta x)^k$
or
$\text{something} \propto (\Delta t)^k$
where $\Delta x$ is the price change (that could be interpreted as an ``intrinsic time''),
$\Delta t$ a time interval
and ``something'' is the $L^p$ average ($p \in \{1,2\}$) of:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Absolute returns in $\Delta t$;
\item Number of ticks in $\Delta t$, or $\Delta x$;
\item Number of direction changes in $\Delta t$, or $\Delta x$;
\item Maximum price move (drawdown) in $\Delta t$;
\item Waiting time for a price move of $\Delta x$ (in absolute value or not);
\item Total variation in $\Delta t$, or $\Delta x$,
\end{compactitem}
 
\nextbook
 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Solvable stochastic dealer models\\ for financial markets}\\
  K. Yamada et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0807.0481}
\end{book}

Yet another agent-based model, whose agents have different horizons and try to predict future prices with moving averages.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian analysis of value-at-risk\\
  with product partition models}\\
  G. Bormetti et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0809.0241}
\end{book}

Empty article suggesting to use gaussian mixtures 
(instead of a gaussian model) to estimate value-at-risk (VaR).
(``Product partition'' is the physicalese word 
for ``gaussian mixture''.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Minimal agent based model\\ for financial markets I:\\ origin and self-organization of stylized facts}\\
  V. Alfi et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0808.3562}
\end{book}

Stylized facts could be a \emph{finite size effect}:
they could disappear as the number of agents grow.
 
The authors build their (uninteresting) model on:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Short-horizon investors;
\item Herding
\item The only available information is the price time series,
\ie, everyone is a chartist, with either a long or short horizon, and looks at what others are doing.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards a common framework\\ for statistical analysis and development}\\
  K. Imai et al.\\
  Journal of computational and graphical statistics (2008)
\end{book}

What about ``refactoring statistical analysis'' (or, equivalently, on ``ontology of statistical analysis'')
in the way you refactor  (statistical) software?
The article advocates the \texttt{zelig} package
(a unfified interface to models implemented in other packages),
explains how R's formulas can be generalized 
to accomodate constants and more complex models,
presents Zelig's dynamically-generated GUI
and the \href{http://thedata.org/}{\emph{dataverse network}}
(a data-sharing/publishing platform).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient emulators for multivariate deterministic functions}\\
  J. Rougier (2007)
\end{book}

Climate models can be seen as (deterministic) functions
$f:\RR^N \longrightarrow \RR^M$, $x \mapsto y = f(x)$ 
with $N$ very large.
Since they are expensive to evaluate, they are often replaced by statistical approximations
or \emph{emulators}, \ie, regressions $y \sim x$ from a small number of evaluations,
where both $x$ and $y$ are vectors and have a spatial dependency structure modeled by a gaussian process.
 
In case the output is a vector 
and you are more familiar with scalar outputs, you can:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Forecast the coordinates independently
\item Perform a principal component analysis (PCA) or, better, a canonical correlation analysis (CCA) on the output space;
\item uncurrify the function to be evaluated, \ie, replace
  $x \mapsto (y_1,\dots,y_n)$
  by
  $(x,i) \mapsto y_i$
  (this assumes that the $y_i$ are commensurate);
\item Replace $x \mapsto f(x)$ by $x \mapsto N(\hat f(x), V(x))$ where $\hat f(x)$ is the predicted value and $V(x)$ its precision. 
\end{compactitem}
The article explains how to use
\href{http://www.ee.ic.ac.uk/hp/staff/dmb/matrix/intro.html}{linear algebra}
to approximate and speed up the estimation of this gaussian process.
% I have not read the details.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Residual-based shadings for visualizing (conditional) independence}\\
  A. Zeileis et al.
\end{book}

\emph{Mosaic plots} (that display contingency tables) 
and \emph{association plots} 
(that display the Pearson residuals of a log-linear model
$r_{ij} = ( n_{ij} - \hat n_{ij} ) / \sqrt{n_{ij}}$)
can be augmented by shadings or colours to display the significance of
(\ie, the $p$-value of a statistical test for) departure from
independence:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The usual $\chi^2$ of independence, 
  $\chi^2 = \sum_{ij} r_{ij}^2$
  can be replaced by $M = \max_{ij} \va{ r_{ij} }$,
  so that the whole test is significant iff a cell is significant;
\item The critical values of this test 
  (used to decide whether to colour or not) 
  are data-dependent: 
  they can be computed by \emph{permutation tests};
\item The traditional colourings rely 
  on the HSV (hue, saturation, value) \emph{colour space} 
  (a cone, whose vertex is black, 
  whose base has a fully saturated rim and a white center) 
  and can be misleading 
  (saturation is not uniform accross hues);
  it can be replaced by the HCL (hue, chroma, luminance) space 
  (a double cone, with a white vertex on one side 
  and a black vertex on the other, 
  and a fully saturated rim in the middle -- 
  that rim is slightly broken: 
  admissible chroma and luminance values depen on the hue);
\item Instead of changing just the saturation, 
  you can also change both chroma and luminance;
\item When there is nothing significant, use a medium grey (not white);
  as significance increases, increase the range of hues
  (a more colorful picture looks more significant).
\end{compactitem}
Those ideas are implemented in R in the \texttt{vcd} package: 
check the \texttt{mosaic}, \texttt{assoc} 
and \texttt{cotabplot} functions.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Singular value decomposition\\ and its visualization}\\
  L. Zhang et al. (2005)
\end{book}
To stuy a periodic signal (of known periodicity, e.g., for hourly data you would expect a daily pattern), you can put the data in
a matrix (dates in rows, time in columns -- you can already plot this matrix as a surface to see intraday and interday patterns)
and perform a singular value decomposition (SVD): it expresses the matrix as a sum of rank-1 matrices. Plotting the data
corresponding to matrices 1, 1+2, 1+2+3, etc. can highlight patterns in the data.
Potential applications are similar to those of functional data analysis (FDA).
 
\nextbook
 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Delineation of irregularly shaped disease clusters through multiobjective optimization}\\
  L. Duczmal et al.\\
  Journal of computational and graphical statistics (2008)
\end{book}
To detect a spatial cluster, in epidemiology, compute the \emph{Kulldorff scan statistic}
$$ \dfrac{\text{incidence inside}^{\text{cases inside}}}{\text{incidence outside}^{\text{cases outside}}} $$
for each (connected) potential cluster
and select the one with the highest values
(for circular clusters, this statistic is well approximated by a \emph{Gumbel distribution}).
This will select non-significant tree-like ``clusters'': you can add a penalty, such as the \emph{compactness}
$$ \dfrac{\text{Area}}{\text{Parameter}^2} $$
and maximize
$$
\left( \dfrac
  {\text{incidence inside}^{\text{cases inside}}}%
  {\text{incidence outside}^{\text{cases outside}}}
\right)
^{
\displaystyle
\left(
  \dfrac{\text{Area}}{\text{Parameter}^2}
\right)^\alpha
}
$$
for some value of $\alpha$ 
(higher values favour circular shapes,
while the actual cluster may be around a road, 
river, shore or plume or air pollution).
 
Instead, one can use a genetic algorithm 
to find clusters in the scan statistic vs compactness \emph{Pareto set}
(\ie, efficient frontier).
(Most machine learning algorithms 
can be generalized to multi-objective optimization.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Modeling spatial-temporal binary data\\ using Markov random fields}\\
  J. Zhu et al.
\end{book}

% (not read, not Earth-shattering)
The spatial linear model
$$ x_{i,j} \sim y + x_{i+1,j} + x_{i-1,j} + x_{i,j-1} + x_{i,j+1} $$
can ge generalized for spatio-temporal data
$$ x_{i,j,t} \sim y + x_{i+1,j,t} + x_{i-1,j,t} + x_{i,j-1,t} + x_{i,j+1,t} + x{i,j,t-1}.$$
The article does this in the context of a binary (autologistic) model (infected or not)
an suggests to estimate it properly (previous attempts were estimating the spatial and temporal components separately),
via Gibbs sampling.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Autologistic regression analysis of spatial-temporal binary data via Monte Carlo maximum likelihood}\\
  J. Zhu et al. (2008)
\end{book}

Terser but illustrated article on the spatio-temporal autologistic model

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Hierarchical structure and the prediction\\ of missing links in networks}\\
  A. Clauset et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0811.0484}
\end{book}

% Interesting
A \textbf{hierarchical random graph} is build from a dendogram with a probability assigned to each internal node, by linking two
vertices with the probability coming from their closest common ancestor.
 
Conversely, one can use a maximum likelihood (MCMC) approach to find the hierarchical random graph closest to a given network.
 
Once found, this hierarchical organization can also be used to predict missing connections.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical properties of information flow\\
  in financial time series}\\
  C. Eom et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0811.0448}
\end{book}
The information flow between stocks can be studied by looking at
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item the Granger causality within all pairs of stock retruns.
\item the minimum spanning tree of the correlation matrix of their returns.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Serial corelation and heterogeneous volatility in financial markets:\\
  beyond the LeBaron effect}\\
  S. Bianco et al.\\
  \texttt{0810.4912}
\end{book}

The \emph{LeBaron effect}, 
\ie, the negative correlation between volatility and autocorrelation,
$$ \langle x_t^3 x_{t-1} \rangle < 0, $$
exists in high-frequency data.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Look-ahead benchmark bias in portfolio performance evaluation}\\
  G. Daniel et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0810.1922}
\end{book}

If you are assuming that 
the composition of your benchmark (often, an index)
is constant 
and use the constituents from the end of the test period, 
you have a textbook example of a \emph{look-ahead bias} 
and/or \emph{survivor bias} 
(many indices are defined as the top $N$ stocks in some market). 
The authors measure that bias: 
it can be as large as 8\% per annum for the S\&P 500.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Volatility effects on the escape time in financial market models}\\
  B.  Spagnolo and S. Valenti\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0810.1625}
\end{book}

One can use the \emph{probability distribution of escape times} 
to analyze (or build a more formal statistical test of) 
the goodness of fit of a financial time series model.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Scale free effects in world currency network}\\
  A.Z. G\'orski et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0810.1215}
\end{book}

Yet another article on the minimum spanning tree of the correlation matrix of exchange rates;
this one also looks at what happens if one changes the reference currency.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{From time series to complex networks:\\ the visibility graph}\\
  L. Lacasa et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0810.0920}
\end{book}

Here is an algorithm to turn a time series into a graph
(feel free to imagine yours and check if/how time series properties translate into graph properties):
one vertex for each observation,
two vertices are linked if one can ``see'' the other
in the flatland world \texttt{plot(.,type='h')}.
A periodic time series becomes a random graph; a fractal time series becomes a scale-free network.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio optimization under habit formation}\\
  R. Naryshkin and M. Davidson\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0810.0678}
\end{book}

The classical 1-period portfolio optimization problem
$$ \text{Maximize } E[ U(W_T) ] $$
(maximize the expected utility of final wealth)
can be replaced by the (also classical) multiperiod (or continuous time)
optimal investment/consumption/bequest problem
$$ E \int_0^T e^{-\rho t} U(C_t) dt + e^{-\rho T} B( W_T ) $$
(maximize the discounted utility of consumption and utility of bequest,
where $T$ is the terminal time, $C_t$ the consumption, $W_t$ the wealth, $U$ the utility of consumption, $B$ the utility of
bequest).
This model has several drawbacks:
the utility of consumption remains constant (or predefined). it should actually depend on the previous consumption
$$ \bar C_t = \dfrac1t \int_0^t C_u du$$
to account for habit formation or intolerance to a decline in the standard of living.

The article suggests a utility of the form
$$ U = T \left( \dfrac{ C_t }{ C_0 + \beta \bar C_t } \right), $$
for which the solution can be approximated with a discrete stochastic programming method
(for two assets, it is $O(n^3)$, but it is exponential in the number of assets).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Distributed multilevel modeling}\\
  D. Afshartous and G. Michailidis
\end{book}

Because of confidentiality reasons or storage limitations, you may want to estimate a multilinear model on distributed data (one
database for each group) without exchanging too much data between the nodes. This is possible; it is not an approximation.
The article also contains a terse refresher on multilevel models, with formulas for all the estimators you may want.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to statistical learning theory}\\
  O. Bousquet et al.
\end{book}

Good tutorial about the \emph{theory} of supervised classifiers and the general \emph{inequalities} you use to study them  (these
are inequalities with few assumptions: you are already familiar with the Markov and Chebychev ones, but there are many others).
 
No fishy arguments to sell you some magical algorithm as in some other articles on the subject.
 
\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Should risk managers rely\\
  on maximum likelihood estimation method\\
  while quantifying operational risk?}\\
  B. Ergashev (2007)
\end{book}

Operational risk is usually estimated 
with a loss distribution approach (LDA):
for each risk cell $C$ (line of business, event type, etc.),
estimate the distribution of the severity of losses 
and the frequency of losses
(exponential, log-normal, log-normal-gamma, log-logistic, Weibull, generalized Pareto, $g$-and-$h$, log-???;
Poisson, binomial, negative binomial);
these are then aggregated to produce the distribution of the total loss.
To complicate things, the data we are given are often stripped of ``small'' losses (say, under USD\,10,000);
some risk cells are almost empty; and the model need not be specified properly.
 
The model can be estimated using a maximum likelihood estimator (MLE)
or some kind of ``distance'' between distributions (between the empirical distribution of the data and that of the model)
such as the \textbf{Cramer von Mises statistic}
$$ \text{CvM}(F, G) = \int [ G(x) - F(x) ]^2 f(x) dx $$
or the \textbf{Anderson--Darling statistic}, almost identical, but with more weight on the tails
$$ \text{AD}( F, G ) = \int \dfrac{ [ G(x) - F(x) ]^2 }{ F(x) [ 1 - F(x) ] } f(x) dx $$
or a quantile-based distance
$$ \text{QD}( F, G ) = \sum_p \left[ F^{-1}(p) - G^{-1}(p) \right] ^2 $$
where the sum is over a set of quantiles $p \in [0,1]$, such as R's \texttt{ppoints(N)}
or the author's ``equally spaced'' (sic)
$$ p = \dfrac{ 1 - \epsilon }{ k }, \qquad 
k \in \llbracket 1,N \rrbracket. $$
The estimation procedure should be robust to local extrema, e.g., simulated annealing (SA).
The distance-based methods estimate the severity of kisses distribution and the frequency of losses separately,
while the MLE approach can estimate the joint model.
The article finds the quantile distance estimation superior, with a log-$T$/Poisson model, simulated data in an ideal situation,
simulated data with insufficient data in some cells, simulated data from a mixture of log-gaussians (I am not sure the data from
such mixtures is bad enough).
 
You may also look at other metrics:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Kolmogorov:
  $\displaystyle d_K( F, G ) = \sum_x \va{ F(x) - G(x) } $
\item L\'evy:
  $ d_L(F,G) = \inf \{ \epsilon > 0 \ :\ \forall x \ F(x - \epsilon) \leq G(x) \leq F(x+\epsilon) \} $
\item Total variation:
  $\displaystyle d_{TV}( F, G ) = \tfrac12 \int \va{ f(x) - g(x) } dx $
\item Kullback--Leibler (aka entropy divergence)
  $$ K(F, G) = - \int \log( g / f ) f(x) dx $$
\item Hellinger distance:
  $\displaystyle H(F,G) = \sqrt{ \int ( \sqrt f - \sqrt g )^2 dx } $
\item Fisher information:
  $$ I( F || G ) = \int \n{ \nabla \log f - \nabla \log g } f(x) dx. $$
\end{compactitem}
 
\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Activity spectrum\\ from waiting-time distribution}\\
  M. Politi and E. Scalas\\
  \texttt{arXiv:08013043}
\end{book}

Waiting times in high-frequency financial data do not follow an exponential distribution,
but can be modeled by a continuous mixture of exponentials
$$ 
  \psi( \tau ) = 
  \int_0^\infty g(\lambda) \lambda e^{-\lambda \tau} d\lambda. 
$$
Finding the \textbf{activity spectrum} $g(\lambda)$ from the density of durations $\psi$
or the more directly accessible survival function (an inverse Laplace transform)
is a numerically unstable task and can be tackled by a regularization method
such as Tikhanov's (this could be another name for \emph{ridge regression}).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Personal recommendation via modified collaborative filtering}\\
  R.-R. Liu et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0801.1333}
\end{book}

An application of bipartite networks.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Voter models on heterogeneous networks}\\
  V. Sood et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0712.4288}
\end{book}

Two examples of dynamical systems on \textbf{heterogeneous graphs}
(\ie, graphs whose node degree distribution is ``broad'' --
the results can be qualitatively different from those obtained on a lattice):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Voter model: each note has a state (0 or 1), a randomly-chosen node imports its new state from a randomly chosen neighbour;
\item Invasion model: idem, but replace ``import'' by ``export''.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Critical comparison of several order-book models for stock-market fluctuations}\\
  F. Slanina\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0801.0631}
\end{book}

There are  many order book models 
but none reproduces the features of empirical data: 
return distribution, absolute return
autocorrelation, Hurst exponent:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Stigler: limit orders (buy, sell) arrive at random times 
  and are executed if possible, or stay;
\item BPS: buy and sell particles are introduced 
  from both ends of the allowed price interval $[p_1,p_2]$,
  diffuse (follow a random walk), and annihilate when they meet.
\end{compactitem}
Those models can be augmented with cancellations (evaporation); the other models mentionned (Genoa, Maslov) are more promising but
not detailed.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Fluctuating epidemics on adaptive networks}\\
  L.B. Shaw and I.B. Schwartz\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0801.0606}
\end{book}

Classical epidemic models (SIR, SIRS) can be studied on a fixed network
or on a network changing to avoid contagion.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Asymptotic theory of statictics and probability}\\
  A. DasGupta (Springer, 2008)
\end{book}

A reference book, but a readable one 
(the style is lively and you can open it at any chapter, 
the author will not assume you have already read the previous 500 pages), covering everything you may want to know about 
convergence or limits in probability and statistics.
 
I only read the two chapters available on the publisher's website.
 
Chapter 2 presents various \textbf{metrics} 
on the space of probability distributions
(Kolmogorov, L\'evy, total variation, Kullback--Leibler
(\emph{minimum distance estimation} is a synonym for KL estimation),
Hellinger, etc.);
reviews the various notions of convergence and the relations between them;
recalls the \emph{Poisson approximation} (\ie, convergence to a Poisson distribution);
explains that gaussian distributions are extremal (if you fix the variance) for the
maximum entropy and the minimum Fisher information problems.
 
Chapter 29 explains what ``the bootstrap works'' means, and reminds us that it could fail
(infinite variance, non-differentiable functions, support of $F_\theta$ that depends on $\theta$,
$\theta \in \partial \Theta$, tangency problems, etc.) -- in that case,
the $m/n$ bootstrap may still work.
For time series prefer fixed block sizes (there is an optimal block size that depends on the spectrum of the series);
the bootstrap will not work for long-memory time series.
%try a parametric boot

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Comparison of volatility measures:\\ a risk management perspective}\\
  C.T. Brownlees and G.M. Gallo (2008)\\
  \texttt{http://ssrn.com/abstract=107321}
\end{book}

\emph{Volatility} refers to any estimator 
that converges to the \emph{integrated variance}
$$ \lim_{\tau \to 0} E \left[ \dfrac1\tau \int_T^{T + \tau} (r_t - \bar r)^2 dt \right]. $$
This assumes that you have an underlying \emph{continuous} model of stock prices. Ideally, the estimator should be robust to the
misspecification of this model.
 
Volatility estimators can be assessed by comparing them with a reference volatility, an implied volatility (VIX) and by checking
the value at tisk (VaR) and the expected shortfall (ES).
 
The authors find that the \emph{daily range} is preferable to the realized volatility (which does not look at the overnight
returns -- the article lists several realized volatility estimators), which is better than a GARCH model.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Modern Multivariate Statistical Techniques}\\
  A.J. Izenman (Springer, 2008)
\end{book}

The sample chapter looked empty.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical Design}\\
  G. Castella (2008)
\end{book}

Not understandable.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multifractal analysis of Chinese stock volatilities based on partition function approach}\\
  Z.-Q. Jiang and Z.-X. Zhou\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0801.1710}
\end{book}

The \textbf{multifractal analysis} of a positive time series (the realized volatility) can be carried out as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Put the time series into boxes of size $s$;
\item Sum the volatilities in each box;
\item Normalize, \ie, divide by the sum of all boxes;
\item Compute:
\begin{align*}
  \chi_q(s) &= \sum_{\text{boxes}} ( \text{normalized volatility} )^q \\
  \chi_q(s)  &\sim s^{ \tau(q) } \\
  \alpha    &= \dfrac{ d\tau }{ dq } \\
  f(\alpha) &= q \alpha - \tau( q ).
\end{align*}
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Effect of Asian currency crisis\\ on multifractal spectra}\\
  G. Oh et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0801.1475}
\end{book}

% Empty

The multifractal detrended fluctuation analysis (MF-DFA) of a time series (or returns) is performed as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Make sure the mean is 0;
\item For each $s$,
  divide the data into boxes of size $s$;
  remove a linear (or polynomial of degree $m$, or spline) trend;
  compute the average of the squared residuals;
  take the $L^p$ average $F_p(s)$ of the value in each box;
\item Fit $F_p(s) \sim s^{h(q)}$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian core: a practical approach\\ to computational bayesian statistics}\\
  J.M. Marin and C.P. Robert\\
  Springer (2008)
\end{book}

Interesting.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalizing Swendsen-Wang\\ for image analysis}\\
  A. Barbu and S.-C. Zhu
\end{book}

To sample from probability distributions on graphs (graph colourings),
the Gibbs sampler performs poorly if the variables are linked
(e.g., if they form a grid, as in image analysis).
The Swendsen-Wang (SW) method paliates this by clustering the nodes at random
(turn all the edges between nodes of different colours off,
turn some of the remaining edges off at random,
take the connected components -- you have a different clustering each time)
and changing the colour of whole clusters at a time.
However, since there is no Metropolis-Hastings (MH) rule,
it only works for the Ising-Potts model.
It can be seen as a \emph{data-augmentation} procedure: 
we actually sample from the joint
distribution
$$ \text{Potts model} \times \text{Random Cluster Model} $$
by moving, \`a la Gibbs,
in only one marginal chain at a time.
 
This can be improved as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use the data to get an a priori probability 
  that two adjacent nodes have the same colour
  (say, a measure of pixel intensity similarity, 
  if you are trying to segment an image)
  and use that probability when deciding which edges to turn off;
  this gives more meaningful clusters
  (if the probabilities are completely wrong, no real harm is done:
  the constant probability of the SW model is wrong as well);
\item Allow the number of colours to increase or decrease: 
  the new colour of the cluster can be a new one or an existing one.
\item Use a Metropolis-Hastings (MH) acceptance probability.
\end{compactitem}
The algorithm can be applied to \emph{image segmentation} 
(identify the various elements of an image: 
car, sky, building, tree, lion, etc.).
 
Large graphs can be handled as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Multigrid clustering: only apply the algorithm to a subset of the data
  (e.g., a slice of a cube, a single image in a video, etc.);
\item Multilevel clustering (for instance, in motion segmentation, this would give you a hierarchy of clustering:
  pixels, pixels moving in the same way (cheetah limbs), actual objects (cheetah)).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum spanning trees, asset graphs and random matrix denoising in the analysis ofdynamics of financial networks}\\
  T. Heimo\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0806.4714}
\end{book}

You might want to denoise your correlation matrix using random matrix theory (RMT)
before computing a minimum spanning tree (MST) (useful but notoriously unstable).
This is actually a bad idea: the denoised matrices are even less stable
and have lost most of the clustering information.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Global recessions as a cascade phenomenon with heterogeneous, interacting agents}\\
  P. Ormerod (2008)
\end{book}

Which countries are in recession can be modeled on a (small world) graph:
each node will go in recession the next year with probability $\alpha$,
will go out of recession with probability $\beta$,
and will also go in recession if sufficiently many of its neighbours go in recession.
As usual, the physicist's intuition that the shape of the network is irrelevant
and can be assumed a lattice breaks down for small world graphs.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{How to quantify the influence of correlations on investment diversification}\\
  M. Medo et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805.3397}
\end{book}

A definition of the \textbf{effective portfolio size} of a basket:
the number of stocks, with iid returns 
(and therefore equal expected returns and risk)
for which the minimum variance portfolio has the same risk.
You can use the tangential portfolio instead.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Evolutionary Monte Carlo methods\\ for clustering}\\
  G. Goswami et al.\\
  Journal of computational and graphical statistics (2007)
\end{book}

Clustering algorithms 
either try to solve an optimization problem, more or less greedily.
For instance, $k$-means tends to get stuck in a local optimum;
you can compensate for this by running them several times 
and choosing the best solution;
and/or you can start with a partition coming from a hierarchical clustering.
MClust is similar to $k$-means but uses a mixture of gaussians
and the BIC (bayesian information criterion) to choose the number of clusters.
 
You can avoid that local optimum problem altogether 
by sampling from some posterior distribution.
 
This article suggests an improvement over MCMC sampling
based on \textbf{parallel tempering} (PT)
(you have several MCMC chains, each with its temperature,
sampling from distributions of the form
$g(z) \propto \exp( - H(z) / T ) $,
and we want to sample from the coldest)
by adding the following operators:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Crossover: select two chains (parent 1 and 2), consider the intersection of the clusterings
