\begin{book}
  \emph{L\'evy processes}\\
  Cont \& Tankov (2004)
\end{book}

[This summary is very incomplete: I have not finished reading the book...]

% Chapter 1: Financial models beyond brownian motion

Some of the following classical models of stock prices
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Brownian motion (independent, stationnary, gaussian increments --
  the book calls \emph{Wiener process}
  the standardized (identity covariance matrix) brownian motion);
\item Geometric brownian motion (GBM, Black--Scholes),
  $$ \dfrac{ dS }{ S } = \sigma dW + ( \mu + \tfrac12 \sigma^2 ) dt ; $$
\item Local volatility, \ie, 1-dimensional diffusion
  $$ \dfrac{ dS }{ S } = \sigma(t, S) dW + \mu dt ; $$
\item Stochastic volatility
  (two sources of randomness, one for the returns, one for the volatility),
  \begin{align*}
    \dfrac{ dS }{ S } &= \sigma dW + \mu dt \\
    \sigma &= f( Y ) \\
    dY &= \alpha dt + \gamma dW^2 ;
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}
can reproduce the heavy tails observed in the data,
but in a very unnatural way: the diffusion coefficient $\sigma_t$
varies too much -- the volatility of volatility is unrealistically high.

An \emph{option pricing model} is an interpolation
(extract information from vanilla option prices)
and extrapolation
(use it to price (illiquid) exotic options)
tool.

The \emph{implied volatility} $\sigma$ is
the volatility to put in the Black--Scholes model
to produce the option prices actually observed --
since the model is not valid,
it does depend on the option type (call, put, etc.),
the maturity and the strike --
it can be seen as a change of coordinates to replace
the \emph{moneyness}, \ie, the ratio price / strike.

The \emph{implied volatility surface} is the relation
$$ \sigma \sim \text{maturity} + \text{strike}. $$

When seen as a function of the strike,
the implied volatility often exhibits
a skew (that can be interpreted as an asymmetric jump fear, as observed in equities)
or a smile (symmetric jump fear, in currencies).

Diffusion models struggle to reproduce the high skew of short maturities.

Local volatility vs diffusion models.
%TODO: 
% What is the difference 
% between "local volatility" and "diffusion" models?
%   Local volatility: can reproduce any 
%     ($C^2$, arbitrage-free) volatility surface.
%   Diffusion: can reproduce the skew or smile 
%     at a given maturity,
%     but cannot reproduce the \emph{time structure} 
%     of implied volatility.

In a \emph{perfect} market, perfect hedging is possible and options are redundant
(\emph{delta hedging} is robust to the gaussian assumptions,
provided the volatility is bounded -- this excludes jump models --
this explains why option markets exist
and why vanilla options are sometimes used to hedge other options when they seem redundant
(they are not always redundant:
in stochastic volatility, there are two sources of randomness,
so you need two assets, e.g., the underlying and a vanilla option, to get a perfect hedge).

% Chapter 2: Basic tools

Measure and probability theory
(with a clear explanation of what the set of scenarios $\Omega$ really is);
notions of convergence;
characteristic, moment-generation, cumulant-generating (or log-characteristic) functions;

\emph{Cadlag} (continues \`a droite, limites \`a gauche) functions
have unpredictable jumps and can be used for prices
(caglad functions have predictable jumps and can be used to describe trading strategies;
the \emph{Skorokhod space} $D([0,T],\RR)$ is the set of cadlag functions.

The notion of a \emph{Wiener measure},
a gaussian Borel measure on
$(C^\infty( [0,T], \RR^d ), \n{\cdot}_\infty)$,
is mentionned but not detailed.

Filtration,
non-anticipating process (often called ``adapted'').

A \emph{stopping time} is a non-anticipating random time (e.g., hitting time, exit time).

Martingale; sampling property (the martingale property still holds if you replace the times by stopping times).

Optional processes,
Predictable processes.

Infinite divisibility

Poisson process, compensated poisson process,

Random measures:
If $\tau_i$ are iid exponential random variables,
and $T_k = \tau_1 + \cdots + \tau_k$;
the \emph{Poisson process}
$$ N_t = \sharp \{ \, i \ :\  T_i \in [0,t] \, \} $$
defines a measure $M(\omega,\cdot)$
$$ M(\omega, A) = \sharp \{ \, T_i(\omega) \in A  \, \}. $$
This \emph{random measure} (we have a mesure for each $\omega \in \Omega$)
can be seen as the ``derivative'' of $N_t(\omega)$.

More generally, for any Radon measure $\mu$ on $\RR^d$,
a \emph{Poisson random measure}
is an integer-valued measure
with $M(\cdot,A) \sim \text{Pois}( \mu(A) )$
such that if $A_1,...,A_n$ are disjoint
then $M(A_1),...,M(A_n)$ are independent.

A \emph{compound Poisson process} is a Poisson process with random jump sizes;
it can be seen as a random walk undergoing a time change specified by a Poisson process.
A \emph{compensated Poisson process} is a Poisson process minus a linear drift,
so that the expectation be zero.

% Chapter 3: Levy Processes

A \emph{L\'evy process} is a stochastic process with independent, stationary increments
(one also requires \emph{stochastic continuity},
\ie, there are no deterministic jumps, \ie, calendar effects);
this can be seen as a continuous-time analogue of random walks (sums of iidrv).

L\'evy processes sampled are regular time intervals are random walks:
for all $t$, $X_t$ is \emph{infinitely divisible};
conversely, specifying an infinitely divisible distribution for (say) $X_1$
suffices to define a L\'evy process.

The jumps of a realization of a L\'evy process can be seen as points
$(t,\Delta_t) \in \RR_+ \times \RR^d \setminus \{ 0 \}$;
the corresponding counting measure (it depends on the realization: it is a random measure) is
a random Poisson measure, called the \emph{jump measure}.

Since a L\'evy process has stationary increments,
we can remove the time dimension;
since it is a Poisson measure,
we can take the expectation (to get its parameter and) remove the randomness:
the \emph{L\'evy measure} is the measure on $\RR^d \setminus \{ 0 \}$
defined by
$$ \nu( A ) = E [ \sharp \{ \, t \in [0,1] , \  \Delta X_t \neq 0, \  \Delta X_t \in A \, \} ]. $$

The \emph{L\'evy--It\=o decomposition} expresses a L\'evy process as a sum of
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A deterministic drift;
\item A brownian motion;
\item a compound Poisson process with jumps greater than 1;
\item A limit of compensated Poisson processes with jumps smaller than 1.
\end{compactitem}
If you change the threshold (1), the drift changes;
you cannot choose zero because the process can have infinitely many jumps.

%TODO: Formula

In particular, a L\'evy process is entirely determined by
it drift $\gamma$ (beware, it depends on the arbitrarily-chosen truncation, often 1),
the covariance $A$ of its brownian motion component
and the L\'evy measure $\nu$.

The characteristic function of a L\'evy process is of the form
$$ E[ e^ {} ] = e^{ t \psi( z ) } $$
where $\psi$ is given by the \emph{L\'evy--Khinchin} formula
$$
  \psi(z) = -\tfrac12 z \cdot A z + i \gamma \cdot z +
  \int_{\RR^d} ( e^{i z \cdot x} - 1 - i z \cdot x 1_{\va x \leq 1} ) \nu( dx )
$$

% TODO: FINISHED?

% Chapter 4: Examples of 1-dimensional Levy processes

A L\'evy process is determined by its L\'evy measure
or by the infinitely divisible distribution of $X_\Delta$
(for a given time scale $\Delta$);
here are other ways of building 1-dimensional L\'evy processes:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item A \emph{jump-diffusion} is a diffusion with added jumps from a compounded Poisson process,
  such as the \emph{Merton model} (gaussian jumps --
  the probability distribution of $X_t$ is them a rapidly converging sum of gaussians,
  and option prices therefore involve series of Black--Scholes formulas),
  the Kou model (double exponential jumps),
  or the Bates model (stochastic volatility with jumps);
\item A \emph{subordinated Brownian motion},
  \ie, a Brownian motion after a change-of-time specified by
  an increasing L\'evy process (the \emph{subordinator}),
  such as the \emph{variance gamma process},
  the \emph{normal inverse gaussian process};
\item The \emph{generalized hyperbolic (GH) distribution}
  is a generalization of the distribution of $X_t$
  obtained from subordinated brownian motions;
  the class of GH distributions is not stable by convolutions:
  you will have to choose one time scale to be GH.
\end{compactitem}

% Chapter 5: Multi-dimensional models

Time changing a multivariate brownian motion with a 1-dimensional subordinator
only produces limited dependence patterns (in particular, no independence:
the jumps will be synchronous -- but the sign of the jumps can be independent and
the processes can therefore be uncorrelated)
and all the components will be of the same type.

You can add a compound Poisson process to a brownian motion:
the jumps will be synchronous but you can specify their distributions separately
for each coordinate and model the dependence of their amplitude with a copula;
this can be generalized to add several types of schocks
(say, sectors, countries, etc.).

Copulas cannot be used directly to deal with L\'evy processes:
if the marginals are infinitely divisible, the joint need not;
infinite divisibility is not preserved by increasing transformations;
the notion of dependence has to be changed (if $X$ is a prore jump process,
we would like to call $X=\sum\Delta_x$ and $Y=\sum(\Delta_x)^3$ dependent).

The \emph{copula} of a (pure-jump, increasing) L\'evy process is that if its L\'evy measure;
since the L\'evy measure need not be finite,
replace the cumulated distribution function by the tail integral
$ \int_{x_1}^\infty\cdots\int_{x_n}^\infty $,
the copula is a function $[0,\infty]^d \rightarrow [0,\infty]$
(for arbitrary-sign L\'evy processes,
replace $\int_{x_i}^\infty$ by $\int_{-\infty}^{x_i}$ if $x_i < 0$
and the copula is a function
$[-\infty,\infty]^d \rightarrow [-\infty,\infty]$).
(Since the quandrants are treated separately, you end up with several dependence copulas.)

% Chapter 6: Simulation

We need to simulate L\'evy processes, e.g., to price options in high dimensions,
where PIDE methods become intractable.

For jump-diffusion processes just simulate the diffusion and jump parts separately;
to compute expectations (say, the price of an up-and-out call option)
that depend on the whole path
you can use an \emph{exact} Monte Carlo approach:
the value of the function on the sample path can be computed exactly instead of approaximated on a discretized path.

In some cases, you can simulate the increments --
this is easier if the class of distributions you chose is stable by convolution
(gaussian, $\alpha$-stable).

For infinite-activity L\'evy processes, you can use the L\'evy--It\=o formula
to decompose the process as the sum of
a brownian motion, a compound Poisson process
and a limit of compensated Poisson processes;
and replace the small jumps by their expectation or by a brownian motion --
depending on the process, the convergence can be very good or very bad.

A subordinator (increasing L\'evy process) $Z$ can be simulated on $[0,1]]$ as
\begin{align*}
  Z_s       &=    \sum_{i \geq 0} U^{-1} ( \Gamma_i ) \textbf 1 _{V_i \leq s }              \\
  U(x)      &=    \int_x^\infty \nu(d\xi) \text{tail integral of the L\'evy measure of $Z$} \\
  U^{-1}(y) &=    \inf\{ x > 0 \ :\ U(x) < y \}           \\
  \Gamma_i  &:    \text{jump times of a Poisson process} \\
  V_i       &\sim \textrm{U}(0,1) \text{, iid}.
\end{align*}
Truncate the series by keeping
$N(\tau) = \inf\{i \ :\ \Gamma_i \geq \tau \}$ terms, for a fixed $\tau$;
\ie, jumps smaller that $U^{-1}(\tau)$ are truncated.

Those series representations can be generalized to other L\'evy processes. % NOT READ

All this (compound Poisson approximation, series representations) remains valid
in a multidimensional setting -- but do not hope for direct simulation of increments
except in very simple (gaussian) cases.

% TODO: 
Simulating multidimensional L\'evy processes

% Chapter 7: Estimation, Statistical properties of Levy processes

The statistical properties of asset returns at different sampling frequencies can very different:
if you need several very different frequencies (minutes to years),
you will find time series (\ie, discrete) methods limiting --
even worse, common time series models are not stable under time aggregation:
e.g., if hourly returns are GARCH, then daily returns need no longer be GARCH.
Continuous-time models, such as \emph{exponential L\'evy models}
(just the exponential of a L\'evy process) can fare better.

