\nextbook
\fi

\begin{book}
  \emph{Time series decomposition and analysis\\ 
  in a study of oxygen isotope records}\\
  M. West (1995)\\
\end{book}

The \emph{bayesian periodogram}, \ie, 
the posterior distribution of $\omega$ in a simple model 
$$ x_t = a \cos \omega t + b \sin \omega t + \epsilon_t, $$
can help identify quasi-cyclical components.
Being a whole distribution, it has a finer resolution than
the classical, discrete (FFT (Fast Fourier Transform)) periodogram, 
which can miss frequencies between the Fourier frequencies.

The author suggests to fit an $AR(p)$ model 
(or an autoregressive state space model, \ie,
an $AR(p)$ model observed with noise)
to the data, in a bayesian way:
you can look at the distribution of 
the roots of the characteristic polynomial 
to test 
whether the data is stationary
(the roots are in the $[ \va z < 1 ]$ disc),
you can also identify noise 
(roots with a small real part, \ie, an argument close to $\pm\pi/2$);
after somehow sorting those roots, 
you can look at the distribution of the arguments of the first ones: 
they correspond to the frequency of the \emph{quasi-cyclical component} of the dataset
(it is not extremely clear if they directly look 
at the roots or at the bayesian periodogram of the fitted $AR(p)$).

In a bayesian context, you can use a mixture distribution 
to account for or model outliers with a \emph{contamination model}:
replace $\epsilon \sim N(0,v_0)$ by
$$ \epsilon \sim (1-\pi) N(0,v_0) + \pi N(0,v_1) $$
with $\pi$ small and $v_1 > v_0$.

Regime switching can be studied in a non-bayesian setting:
cut the data in 2 or 3.

An apppendix explains how to sample from (simulate) the posterior distribution 
of an $AR(p)$ model or a more general state space model (SSM).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock price jumps:\\ news and volume play a minor role}\\
  A. Joulin et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0803.1769}
\end{book}

Empirical observations suggest that
price jumps are not cause by news
(they are followed by increased volatility 
while news are followed by a lower volatility)
or large transaction volumes, 
but rather by very small-scale \emph{liquidity squeezes} --
market microstructure is a nonlinear system with feedback
and has a non-mean-reverting effect.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Feasibility of portfolio optimization\\ under coherent risk measures}\\
  I. Kondor and I. Varga-Haszonits\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0803.2283}
\end{book}

The authors remark that (unconstrained) optimization of coherent risk measures 
(expected shortfall (ES), etc.) is sometimes unfeasible,
the risk measure not being bounded below.

Shouldn't the conclusion be that constraints are necessary?
The article has a more worrying shortcoming: 
the authors assume that the returns are elliptical (fine) 
and use a naive estimator of the dispersion matrix -- 
unifying risk model estimation and optimization is good 
unless you botch the first step and end up with a singular matrix...

To their defense, they correctly identify the cause of the problem:
if you rely on the \emph{empirical} returns distribution, 
an asset will \emph{strongly dominate} the others with non-zero probability.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Log-normal continuous cascades:\\ aggregation properties and estimation.\\
    Application to financial time series}\\
  A. Bacry et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:9894.0185}
\end{book}

The article is not understandable,
but the introduction gives a definition of \emph{multifractality} 
as a generalization of the ``square root of time rule'' 
to non trivial term structures:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Square root of time: $E[X_t^2] \propto t^{1/2}$;
\item Scaling behaviour:  $E \va{ X_t^q } \propto t^{\zeta q}$;
\item Multifractal: $E \va{ X_t^q } \propto t^{\zeta(q)}$,
  where $\zeta$ is concave but not linear.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{How much random a random network is:\\ a random matrix analysis}\\
  S. Jalan and N. Bandyopadhyay\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805.4343}
\end{book}

To quantitatively measure the randomness of a network,
one can compare the distribution of the eigenvalues of its adjacency matrix 
with those of a random matrix, e.g., using the $\Delta_3$ statistic 
(approximately, the sum of square residuals of a linear fit 
of the cumulated distribution function (a staircase function) 
of the (empirical) distribution of the eigenvalues).

The article does not explain why we should expect 
the adjacency matrix of a random network to look like a random matrix.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian approach to clustering real value,\\ 
  hypergraph an bipartite graph data:\\ 
  solution via variational methods}\\
  A. Vasquez\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805.2689}
\end{book}

A physicist remarks that mixture models 
(or rather bayesian model averaging (BMA) of mixture models, 
since you rarely know the number of clusters) can be generalized to biclustering, 
\ie, simulataneous clustering among both subjects and variables.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Note on two-phase phenomena\\ in financial markets}\\
  S.M. Jiang et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0801:0108}
\end{book}
Looking for a regime change (a ``bifurcation'') 
from a unimodal to a multimodal distribution 
in 1-minute index returns: 
the authors find that this is linked to the return tails -- 
this looks like an artefact of the way they are modeling those distributions.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A brief history of generative models\\
  for power law and lognormal distributions}\\
  M. Mitzenmacher
\end{book}

Power law distributions, aka fat tail distributions, 
\ie, $P[ X \geq x ] \sim_{x \to \infty} c x^{-\alpha}$, 
\ie, $\log P[ X \geq 0 ]$ is well approximated by an affine function
in $\log x$ for large $x$, are very similar to log-normal distributions
$$ \log f(x) = - \dfrac{ (\log x)^2}{ 2 \sigma^2 }
  + \left( \dfrac{ \mu }{ \sigma^2 } \log x \right)
  - \log \sqrt{ 2 \pi } \sigma 
  - \dfrac{ \mu^2 }{ 2 \sigma^2 }
$$
(especially if $\sigma$ is large) and can be used interchangeably.

Power laws can be generated by \emph{preferential attachment}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Start with a 1-mode graph;
\item Add a new node, link it to a uniformly-chosen node with probability $\alpha$
  or, with probability $1-\alpha$ to a node chosen randombly and proportionnally to its degree.
\end{compactitem}
This can describe the world wide web or, say, 
the distribution of species (degrees) among genera (nodes).

The relation between word frequency and word length can stem from an optimization problem (Huffman coding) -- but simpler models, using randomness without optimization,
yield similar power law distributions.

Multiplicative processes lead to log-normal distributions (e.g., Black-Scholes),
but if $X_t$ is a geometric random walk and $T$ an exponential random variable,
then $X_T$ follows a power law.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Topological structures\\ in the equities market network}\\
  G. Leibon et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805.3470}
\end{book}

A \emph{multiresolution clustering} of $N$ time series (think: stock returns)
can be obtained as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Cluster the time series, using your prefered clustering algorithm 
  (the article suggests ``hierarchical spectral clustering'' 
  but assumes the reader already understands what it is);
\item Compute the average time series in each cluster (think: sector returns);
\item Project the time series on the subspace spanned by those average time series 
  (this allows each time series to be considered as a member of several clusters,
  with weights);
\item Start again with the residuals: 
  if you are lucky, the next step can be interpreted (think: countries);
\item Iterate until the eigenvalues of the correlation matrix of the residuals 
  look like those of a random matrix, 
  \ie, until the residuals are indistinguishable from the Gaussian ensemble.
\end{compactitem}

The introduction of the article contains ideas that could be developed further.
For instance, random matrix theory (RMT) studies the distribution of the eigenvalues 
of the correlation matrix of gaussian iid variables: 
in statistical terms, this can be seen as the null hypothesis of a test. 
One could use the same ideas with another null hypothesis: 
a given correlation matrix, e.g., coming from a clustering 
(this could be used to \emph{test} if sectors are sufficient 
to explain return correlations).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Comparison of detrending methods\\ for fluctuation analysis}\\
  A. Bashan et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805.4081}
\end{book}

\emph{Short-range correlation} in a time series is characterised 
by the exponential decay of the autocorrelation function
$$ \rho(s) \sim \exp( - s / \tau ) ,$$
where the decay time $\tau$ can be obtained as 
$$ \tau = \int_0^\infty \rho(s) \, ds \qquad(?).$$
When this integral diverges, there are 
\emph{long-term correlations};
quite often, the decay can be approximated by a power law
$$ \rho(s) \sim s^{-\gamma}, \qquad \gamma \in (0,1).$$
The estimation of this exponent $\gamma$ 
directly from the autocorrelation function $\rho$ is very noisy: 
one can do better.

One can study the \emph{scaling behaviour} 
(in finance, this could be called the ``term structure of volatility''),
\ie, how the properties of the time series (e.g., the standard deviation)
change with the sampling frequency 
(e.g., whether the economist's ``square root of time'' rule is valid).
More specifically:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Somehow detrend the time series;
\item Compute the \emph{fluctuation function} $F(s)$ on scale~$s$, \ie,
  the standard deviation of the time series resampled at period~$s$;
\item Try to estimate $\alpha$ such that 
  $$ F(s) \sim_{s \to \infty} s^\alpha; $$
  this is linked to the power law decay in the autocorrelation function by
  $\alpha = 1 - \gamma / 2$;
  when $\alpha = \tfrac12$, there is no long-range dependency;
  when $\alpha \in (\tfrac12,1)$, there is;
  when $\alpha \in (0,\tfrac12)$, there is \emph{negative} long-range dependency;
  when $\alpha \geq 1$, the series is not stationary.
\end{compactitem}

There are many ways of detrending the time series:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Do not do anything: this is (almost) Hurst's $R/S$;
\item Substract the moving average on a window of size~$s$; 
  always check that your chosen detrending method does not introduce bias:
  here, use a centered window;
\item Fit a linear function on a moving window of size~$s$ and take the residuals;
  people often take non-overlapping windows 
  and then worry about the discontinuities this introduces;
\item Fit a polynomial of degree~$d$ on a moving window of size~$s$ 
  and take the residuals; 
  this is the \emph{detrended fluctuation analysis} (DFA)
\item Use a local (linear or polynomial) regression;
\item Use Fourier analysis to remove known frequencies; 
  this is limited to periodic, regular, known trends;
\item Embed the time series in a higher-dimensional vector space,
  perform some kind of dimension reduction to identify the ``trend''
  and take the residuals;
\item Apply digital high-pass filters (Butterworth, Chebychev, 
  elliptical filters (?)).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Agents for traffic simulation}\\
  A. Kesting et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0300}
\end{book}

Funny article on simulating road traffic
with heterogeneous agents operating in a shared environment;
some of the visualization methods are... interesting --
for instance, the coffee-meter to measure the driving comfort.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient navigation in scale-free networks 
    embedded in hyperbolic metric spaces}\\
  D. Krioukov et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805.1266}
\end{book}

Routing algorithms on a graph (e.g., the internet) 
usually work by laborious information exchanges between neighbouring nodes --
but if the graph is embedded in a metric space,
if each node knows its ``coordinates'', 
those of its neighbours and those of the target
(strictly speaking, there are no coordinates in a metric space:
each node only needs to know the distance between each of 
its neighbours and the target),
one can use a \emph{greedy} routing strategy:
send packets to the neighbour closest to the destination.
This could explain the 
\href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_World_Experiment}{\emph{Milgram experiment}}
(sending letters to unknown persons, identified by name, location and job,
by sending it to the friend ``closest'' to the target,
leading to the ``six degrees of separation'').

Real-world network suggest a \emph{negatively curved} space.

However, determining the metric embedding will likely require 
laborious information exchanges 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Accurate estimator of correlations\\ between asynchronous signals}\\
  B. Toth and J. Kertesz\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805:2310}
\end{book}

Asynchronicity biases the naive correlation estimator towards zero.
Just model the asynchronicity, for instance $x_t$ and $y_t$
could be AR(1) processes with missing values, 
or Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) processes 
(not random walks:  we want stationary processes)
observed at discrete (different) 
times and try to manufacture an estimator from the cross-correlation 
of $\text{locf}(x)$ and $\text{locf}(y)$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Power law in customer's expenditures\\ in convenience stores}\\
  T. Mizuno\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0802.0105}
\end{book}

Yet another example of a power law distribution,
in point-of-sale (POS) databases.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The structural role of weak and strong links\\ in a financial market}\\
  A. Garas et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805.2477}
\end{book}

Apparently, one can cluster (or apply modern/trendy tools of graph theory:
scaling theory, percolation, fractal analysis, 
community detection (this one is not mentionned), etc.) 
using the correlation or their daily returns. 
Removing the 99\% links with the largest correlation keeps the network connected.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{New approach\\ to community detection in networks}\\
  A.D. Medus and C.O. Dorso\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0808.0375}
\end{book}

\emph{Community detection} (this is the graph-theoretical word for clustering,
a \emph{community} being a subgraph 
``whose nodes are more connected between themselves than to external nodes'';
it is often easier to look for non-overlapping clusters, \ie, partitions;
it can be used for ``dimension'' (graph size) reduction)
algorithms usually try to maximize some \emph{merit factor} 
such as the \emph{modularity} of a partition of the graph
$$
\sum_{\text{subgraphs}}
\dfrac{
  \text{\# external links}
}{
  E[\text{\# external links} \,|\, \text{degrees}]
}.
$$
This is an NP-complete problem, mainly because the modularity 
is a \emph{non-local} merit factor; 
furthermore, evidence suggests that modularity cannot find 
clusters smaller than a certain size.

There are local alternatives:
a \emph{strong community} is a subgraph $C$ such that 
$$ \forall i \in C \quad k_i^{\text{in}} > k_i^{\text{out}} $$
(where $k_i^{\text{in}}$ is the number of edges between node $i$ and 
other nodes in $C$, and $k_i^{\text{out}}$ is the number of edges between
node $i$ and nodes outside $C$);
a \emph{weak community} is a subgraph $C$ such that
$$ \sum_{i \in C} k_i^{\text{in}} > \sum_{i \in C} k_i^{\text{out}}. $$
One can find a partition into communities by maximizing
$$
  \sum_C \dfrac{
    \sum\limits_{i \in C} k_i^{\text{in}} - k_i^{\text{out}}
  }{
    \sum\limits_{i \in C} k_i
  }
$$
under the constraints
$$ \forall C \quad \forall i \in C \quad k_i^{\text{in}} > k_i^{\text{out}} $$
or
$$ \forall C \quad \sum_{i \in C} k_i^{\text{in}} > \sum_{i \in C} k_i^{\text{out}}. $$
You may want to replace the strict inequalities $>$ by~$\leq$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multifactor analysis of multiscaling\\ in volatility return intervals}\\
  F. Wang et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0808.3200}
\end{book}

Many articles study the distribution of \emph{return intervals}
$$ \inf \{\,  t \,:\, \text{Volatility}_t > q  \,\}.$$
This one is empty.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The distribution of first-passage times 
  and durations in FOREX and future markets}\\
  N. Sazuka et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0808.0372}
\end{book}

The distribution of the first passage time 
$$ T_p = \inf\{\, t \,:\, \text{Price}_t \geq p \,\} $$
is well approximated by a Weibull distribution
with a power law tail.

Since inter-trade durations are not exponentially-distributed, 
we cannot use L\'evy processes to model tick data.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A model for interevent times with long tails and multifractality in human communications: an application to financial trading}\\
  J. Perello et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0805.1353}
\end{book}

Interevent times are not Poisson;
the can be modeled by mixture distributions (Poisson and something else).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Higher-order potential forces 
  observed in bubbles and crashes in financial markets}\\
  K. Watanabe et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0808.3339}
\end{book}

Bubbles and crashes can be identified from a non-linear AR(1) model
$$ y_{t+1} = f(y_t) + \text{noise} $$
where $f$ moves from
\raisebox{-1mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.2]
  \draw (-1,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw (0,-1) -- (0,1);
  \draw[thick] (-1,1) to[out=-60,in=180] (0,0) to[out=0,in=-120] (1,1);
\end{tikzpicture}}
through
\raisebox{-1mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.2]
  \draw (-1,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw (0,-1) -- (0,1);
  \draw[thick] (-1,-1) to[out=060,in=180] (0,0) to[out=0,in=-120] (1,1);
\end{tikzpicture}}
to
\raisebox{-1mm}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.2]
  \draw (-1,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw (0,-1) -- (0,1);
  \draw[thick] (-1,-1) to[out=60,in=180] (0,0) to[out=0,in=120] (1,-1);
\end{tikzpicture}}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved subset autoregression}\\
  A.I. McLeod and Y. Zhang\\
  Journal of Statistical Software (2008)
\end{book}

Some time series, e.g., those with periodic features,
have a complicated autoregression structure:
for instance, only the first, second and eleventh
partial autocorrelations are significanatly non-zero
(an AR(2) process with 11-year periodic patterns).
You can fit this model more parsimoniously than an AR(11)
with the \texttt{FitAR} package.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Power law distributions in empirical data}\\
  A. Clauset et al. (2007)\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0706.1062}
\end{book}

Some interesting and clear remarks about power law estimators:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Whether the data is continuous or discrete does matter: 
  when using estimators for continuous data with discrete data 
  (because you do not like zeta functions), 
  beware of the bias they introduce;
\item With fat-tailed data, least-squares-based estimators
  such as the classical histogram regression
  $$ \log p(x) = \alpha \log x + \beta, $$
  should be avoided; 
  prefer maximum likelihood estimators such as the 
  \emph{Hills estimator};
\item To find the cutoff beyond which your data follows a power law, 
  you can compare the data with the corresponding quantiles 
  (with a \emph{Kolmogorov--Smirnov test}): 
  if the threshold is too low, they do not match, 
  if it is too high, they do not match either 
  because there is too much noise; 
  use the bootstrap if you want 
  a confidence interval on this threshold.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Boom and bust in continuous time\\ evolving economic model}\\
  L. Mitchell and G.J. Ackland (2008)
\end{book}

Economic systems (modelled here by companies competing on prices --
lower prices mean higher sales but lower margins)
need not converge to an equilibrium,
but can exhibit \emph{cycles};
this is similar to the Lotke--Voltera oscillations in ecology.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Heavy-tailed statistics\\ in short-message communications}\\
  W. Hong et al. (2008)
\end{book}

Not all event patterns are Poisson.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Fat tails, long memory, maturity and ageing 
  in open-source software projects}\\
  D. Challet and S. Valverde\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0802.3170}
\end{book}

You can do a lot of interesting data analysis 
from a version control system (CVS, SVN, etc.) log, 
e.g., look at the distribution of the number of lines changed, 
or inter-commit time, 
for a given file, a given developer or the whole project; 
look at the cross-correlation 
between changeset size and intercommit time.
This highlights different periods in the lifecycle of the project -- 
if you manage programmers, you can use this idea to monitor your employees.

CVS's deficiencies (no tracking of file moves or renames) 
create visible artefacts in some of the plots.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The log-periodic-AR(1)-GARCH(1,1) model\\ for financial crashes}\\
  L. Gazola et al.
\end{book}

The oscillations before a crash, 
with slow rises and faster drops,
accelerating before the singularity,
can be modeled as
$$
  p_t = A + (t_c - t) ^ \beta 
  ( 
    B 
    + 
    C 
    \cos(
      w \log( t_c - t ) + \phi
    )
  )
  +
  u_t.
$$
The article suggests to use an AR(1) model
with GARCH innovations for the noise term $u_t$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A non-parametric investigation of risk premia}\\
  C. Peroni
\end{book}

Simplistic credict risk models, of the form
$$ \text{corporate rate} = \text{government rate} + \text{risk premium} $$
\ie,
$ \text{corporate spread} = \text{risk premium} $
are not confirmed by historical data:
there are too few defaults to account for such a high risk premium.
\emph{Affine multifactor models},
$$ \text{corporate spread} \sim \text{government term structure}, $$
(you can use some form of dimension reduction on the term structure:
principal component analysis (PCA), 
factor analysis, kernel PCA, etc.)
can be improved by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item allowing non-linearities (use a generalized additive model (GAM) on the (discrete) time structure);
\item adding macroeconomic variables (inflation).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic volatilities and correlations,\\
  extreme values and modeling\\ 
  the macroeconomic environment\\
  under which Brazilian banks operate}\\
  T. Barnhill and M. Souto (IMF)
\end{book}
 
Most estimators of volatility and correlation perform equally poorly -- 
stick to the simplest one, the exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA).

When estimating how well those estimators perform, 
do not compare their immediate results (volatility forecasts)
but their P\&L impact, as they are used in portfolio management.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Credit derivatives and risk management}\\
  M.S. Gibson (2007)
\end{book}

Empty article, stressing that credit derivatives do not eliminate risk;
the following risk sources remain and may be tricky to quantify:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item credit risk;
\item counterparty risk;
\item model risk;
\item rating agency risk;
\item settlement risk.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial Modeling\\ under non-gaussian distributions}\\
  E. Jondeau et al.\\
  Springer Finance (2007)
\end{book}

This is a reference book
(if you want details about any statistical \emph{test} useful
(or misleading, misused) in finance, it should be there),
but not a very good one:
it emphasizes the value of the statistic of the tests rather than the $p$-value;
the plots are often uninsightful
(for instance, the differences between a gaussian density and a non-gaussian density
are invisible unless you use a logarithmic scale on the vertical axis)
and lack axis names and units;
it compares the log-likelihood of non-nested models with a different
number of parameters (better use the BIC instead);
etc.

% Chapter 2: Stylized facts

Financial returns present the following \emph{stylized facts}:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Fat tails;
\item Asymmetry (negative skew);
\item Aggregated normality;
\item Absence of serial correlation;
\item Volatility clustering (all measures of volatility are serially correlated);
\item Time-varying cross-correlation.
\end{compactitem}

There are tests for all of those
(but beware: some are only asymptotic;
others assume that the mean and variance are known,
not estimated).

% Chapter 3: Microstructure

Those stylized facts can be explained
by the \emph{microstructure} of financial markets
(the book only considers \emph{order-driven markets}),
leading to either:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Stable distributions
  (amenable to computations, through their \emph{characteristic function} --
  the density can then be approximated via numeric integration);
\item \emph{Subordinated} processes, \ie, the prices are geometric random walks
  in \emph{market time} (aka event time or theta time),
  reflecting intermittent information arrival
  (this could be an interview question:
  if $X_t$ is a brownian motion and the market time $I_t$ has log-normal increments,
  what is the distribution of $X_{I_t}$?)
\item Mixture of distributions
  (estimated via the generalized method of moments (GMM),
  with the corresponding $\chi^2$ test);
\end{compactitem} 

Current models of the \emph{order book}
(there is a detailed description of the \emph{EKO model}
of the quote arrival process)
are not satisfactory.

\emph{GARCH} models, and their generalizations,
for instance, those based on \emph{duration} between trades,
such as ACD (autoregressive conditional duration) model (?)
or CAR (compount autoregressive) model (?)
can model some of the stylized facts.

% Chapter 4: GARCH and generalizations, GARCH aggregation, realized volatility

GARCH models have an \emph{aggregation} problem:
if daily returns are GARCH, then weekly returns need not be GARCH;
if stock returns are GARCH, then portfolio returns need not be GARCH.
You can get both temporal and cross-sectional (portfolio) aggregation
by enlarging the class of GARCH processes to \emph{weakly GARCH} processes
(which includes GARCH-like process with ARMA, non-gaussian innovations).

% Surprisingly clear presentation of stochastic volatility:

The GARCH model is over-simplified:
it uses a single source of randomness, for the returns;
\begin{align*}
  \epsilon_t &= \sigma_t z_t \\
  \sigma_t^2 &= \omega + \alpha \epsilon_{t-1}^2 + \beta \sigma_{t-1}^2;
\end{align*}
\emph{stochastic volatility} models have another one, for the volatility;
\begin{align*}
  \epsilon_t &= \sigma_t z_t \\
  \sigma_t^2 &= \exp h_t \\
  h_t &= \omega + \beta \sigma_{t-1}^2 + v_t;
\end{align*}
they can be estimated with a \emph{Kalman filter}.

\emph{Realized volatility},
\ie, volatility estimated from high-frequency data,
is a discrete analogue of the \emph{quadratic variation}
(which leads to some more tests).

You can also test for the presence of jumps in the data.

% Chapter 5: higher moments

Time-varying volatility is not sufficient to explain fat tails:
the innovations seem to be genuinely non-gaussian.

You can still use GARCH estiamtors that assume the innovations are gaussian,
even if they are not, and plug in estimates of the first two moments;
these \emph{quasi-maximum likelihood estimators} (QMLE)
are often consistent but the resulting tests have to be robustified.

% p.151
Not all values of skewness and kurtosis are attainable
(the (wrongly stated) condition is simple:
a set of determinants have to be positive);
if you want a distribution inside some amenable family,
the range of possible values is even smaller.

% Zoo of non-gaussian distributions

Beyond a few ad hoc non-gaussian distributions
(say, the skewed Student $T$ distribution),
you can easily get one by a series expansions around the gaussian distribution:
multiply the gaussian probability distribution function $\phi$ by a polynomial --
if you are not careful, you end up with a non-positive ``density''.
Common choices are the \emph{Gram--Charlier expansion}
$$ 1 + \dfrac a6 H_3(x) + \dfrac b{24} H_4(x) $$
and the \emph{Edgeworth expansion}
$$ 1 + \dfrac a6 H_3(x) + \dfrac b{24} H_4(x) + \dfrac{a^2}{72} H_6(x)$$
where the \emph{Hermite polynomials} are
$$ H_i( x ) = (-1)^i \dfrac 1{\phi(x)} \dfrac{\partial^i \phi}{\partial x^i}.$$

% I read an article about this and other methods, a few years ago
The probability distribution function (pdf) $g$
of a skewed version of a distribution
with pdf $f$ and cumulative distribution function (cdf) $F$
can be obtained by \emph{hidden truncation},
$$ g(z) = 2 f(z) F( \xi z ). $$

The \emph{entropy distribution} is that with the lowest entropy
among the distributions with the desired moments;
if you only specify mean and variance, you get a gaussian distribution,
\ie, $p(x) \propto \exp P(x)$ for a degree 2 polynomial $P$;
if you ask for the first $k$ moments,
$P$ just becomes a polynomial of degree (at most) $p$.
% We are given details on the estimation of entropy distributions
Entropy distributions tend to be multimodal.

You can build gaussianity or goodness of fit tests from the moments,
the whole density function or its restriction on some intervals.

GARCH models can be generalized by considering several time-varying moments --
but beware, not all possible values of those higher moments come from an actual density function
(but the GARCH-like specification of the time dependency of the higher moments
can still be used to \emph{test} for their time-dependence).
More generally, you can consider
\emph{autoregressive conditional density} (ARCD) models.

% Chapter 6: Multivariate time series, copulas

There are several multivariate GARCH models, such as:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item the \emph{vech} model,
  where each element of the covariance matrix $V_t$
  is a linear combination of
  the previous squared residuals $\epsilon_{i,t-k} \epsilon_{j,t-k}$
  and the elements of the previous covatiance matrices $V_{t-\ell}$
\item the \emph{diagonal vec model} is the special case
  where $V_i,j,t$ only depends
  on $\epsilon_{i,t-k} \epsilon_{j,t-k}$
  and $V_{i,j,t-\ell}$;
\item the \emph{BEKK} model ensures that $V_t$ is positive definite
  (add more time lags if you want):
  $$ V_{t+1} = \Omega + A' \epsilon_t \epsilon'_t A + B' V_t B; $$
\item In higher dimensions, you can use
  an \emph{orthogonal GARCH} (PCA-GARCH) model,
  a \emph{factor GARCH} model,
  or a \emph{flexible GARCH model} (?).
  % THIS LOOKED INTERESTING BUT WAS NOT CLEAR AT ALL
  % p.204
\end{compactitem}

It is often easier to model the correlations and the volatilities separately
(most of the previous models do not guarantee
that the variance matrix remains positive definite):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{constant conditional correlation} (CCC) model:
  the correlation matrix is constant,
  only the volatilities are time-dependent;
\item The \emph{dynamic conditional correlation} (DCC) model,
  \begin{align*}
    u_t          &= \left( \dfrac{ \epsilon_{it} }{ \sigma_{it} }\right)_{ i \in \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket } \\
    \text{Cor}_t &= (\diag Q_t)^{-1/2} Q_t (\diag Q_t)^{-1/2} \\
    Q_t          &= (1-\delta_1-\delta_2) \bar Q + \delta_1 u_{t-1} u'_{t-1} + \delta_2 Q_{t-1} \\
    \bar Q       &= \cov u
  \end{align*}
\item The ARMA-like \emph{time-varying correlation} (TVC) model,
  $$ R_t = (1 - \theta_1 - \theta_2) R + \theta_1 R_{t-1} + \theta_2 \Psi_{t-1} $$
  where $\Phi_t$ is the sample correlation matrix of the normalized residuals;
\item The \emph{general dynamic covariance} (GDC) model
  (which nests most of the above).
\end{compactitem}

Many 1-dimensional distributions do not have a natural multivariate generalization
(the gaussian, Student or more generally elliptical distributions are exceptions);
there are (several) skewed generalizations of the multivariate gaussian or Student distributions.

Choosing a copula family imposes restrictions on the various measures of concordance
(Kendall's tau, Spearman' rho, etc.);
for instance, many popular copulas only allow for positive dependence.

% Zoo of copulas
% (to plot a copula, produce 3 contour plots, with margins U(0,1), N(0,1), T(2 or 3)):

Here are a few copulas:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Gaussian;
\item Student;
\item Frank, \ie, archimedian with
  $$\phi(t) = \log \dfrac{ e^{-\theta} - 1 }{ e^{-\theta t} - 1 } ;$$
\item Clayton, \ie, archimedian with
  $$\phi(t) = (t^{-\theta}-1)/\theta ;$$
\item Gumbel, \ie, archimediam with
  $$\phi(t) = (-\log t)^\theta ;$$
\item Plackett;
\item Marshall-Olkin.
\end{compactitem}

Margins and copula can be estimated together or separately
and you can mix parametric and non-parametric models.

You can test the goodness of fit of a copula.

You can build a GARCH-like model by replacing the variance
by the parameter(s) of the family of copulas you have chosen.

With copulas, moment computations often become analytically intractable.

% Chapter 7: EVT, i.e., models for "distribution tails",
% meaning either "distribution of maxima over subsamples"
% or "distribution of exceedances over a threshold".

There are two approaches of \emph{extreme value theory} (EVT),
that lead to estimators of the same quantities:
you can focus on the distribution of maxima over subsamples,
or on the tail (or \emph{peaks over thresholds} (POT)),
\ie, the distribution of exceedances over a threshold.

If the (rescaled) maximum of $X_1,...X_n$ converges,
then it converges to a \emph{generalized extreme value distribution}
(it emcompasses the Frechet (fat tails), Gumbel (thin tails) and Weibull distributions)
$$ H_\xi(x) = \exp( - (1+\xi y)^{-1/\xi} ) $$
(for $\xi=0$, take the limit).
The parameter $\xi$ is the \emph{tail index};
its inverse $1/\xi$ is the \emph{shape index}.
You can graphically estimate this parameter
with a \emph{quantile-quantile plot}
and formalize this with a regression.

If the distribution of $X$ is in the domain of attraction
of the extreme value distribution $H_\xi$,
then the extreme distribution function
$$ F_u(x) - P[ X - u \leq y | X > u ] $$
can be approximated by the \emph{generalized Pareto distribution}
$$G_{\xi,\psi}(x) = 1 - \left( 1 + \dfrac{\xi}{\psi} \right)^{-1/\xi}$$

You can also plot the \emph{mean excess function},
$$ e(u) = E[ X - u | X > u ]. $$

The tail index can be estimated
with the \emph{Pickands estimator},
the \emph{Hills estimator}
(popular, but it assumes we are
in the domain of attracion of the Frechet distribution)
or the Deckers--Einmahl--de Haan (DEdH) estimator
(a variant of the Hills estimator,
that corrects for this problem, and appears more stable).

There is no satisfactory way of determining
``where'' the tail starts for those estimations.

For (non-iid, stationary) time series (under mild conditions),
$H_\xi$ gets replaced by $H_\xi^\theta$ (for the same $\xi$),
where $\theta \in [0,1]$ is called the \emph{extremal index}.

On the other hand, the subsample maxima approach is
(asymptotically) unaffected by the dependence structure.

High quantiles can be estimated from the extrema or the tail approach.

Most of the results only hold for 1-dimensional variables:
the EVT limit theorems (analogues of the central limit theorem)
only hold in one dimension...

With several (say, two) variables,
if you get the copula wrong (and you sure will),
the tail distribution will be wrong.
There are three ingredients to the tail:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The tail of the margins;
\item The \emph{asymptotic dependence},
  which is unrelated to the dependence of the bulk of the data;
  it can be measured by
  $$ \chi = \lim_{u\to1} P[ V > u | U > u ] $$
  $$ \bar\chi = \lim_{u\to1} \dfrac{ 2 \log P[ U > u ] }{ \log P[ U > u, V > u ] }-1 $$
  (and can be estimated from (1-dimensional) tail index estimators);
\item The time dependency.
\end{compactitem}

To plot the tail of a bivariate random variable,
consider the \emph{Frechet transformation}
$$ S = - \dfrac{ 1 }{ \log F_X( X ) }. $$

% Chapter 8: VaR

Prefer \emph{expected shortfall} (ES) to value at risk (VaR)
(not coherent, \ie, does not recognize that diversification reduces risks;
ignores extreme risks)
or variance (symmetric, ignores extreme risks).

VaR can be computed from a ``historical simulation'', with moving window
artefacts (the VaR estimates jump);
the expected shortfall does not erase those artefacts.
EVT-based estimators do not have this problem.

The \emph{GARCH-EVT} model separates the conditional volatility and the tail distribution
(compute the GARCH residuals via quasi-maximum likelihood (QML), \ie,
assuming gaussian innovations (this is consistent even if they are not gaussian);
estimate the tail of the residuals;
filter (?) the initial data).

The book mentions \emph{quantile regression},
purportedly to define the conditional autoregressive value at risk (CAViaR),
but their presentation is confusing and further clouded by typos.

\emph{RiskMetrics} estimates volatility by an exponential (autoregressive)
moving average of squared returns.

% Flexible GARCH (p.239)

Computing the VaR of a portfolio can be tricky;
if you have derivatives,
you should take into account the non-linearity of those instruments.

% Chapter 9: Portfolio construction

In portfolio construction,
you can approximate the investor's utility
with mean, variance and higher moments;
you can also add downside risk (VaR, ES) constraints.

% Chapter 10: Option pricing: Black-Scholes-Merton (DSM)

There are several approaches to option pricing:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Binomial lattice;
\item Replicating portfolio;
\item No-arbitrage:
  build a risk-free portfolio with the option and the underlying;
  since it is risk-free and there is no arbitrage, its dynamics,
  \ie, a stochastic differential equation (SDE), are known;
  since we also know the dynamics (SDE) of the underlying,
  we get that of the option;
  this eventually leads to a partial differential equation (PDE)
  which can be solved with the \emph{Feynmann--Kac formula} (?);
\item Martingales, \ie, \emph{risk-neutral density}
  (this can be seen as a ``change of base'',
  performed to simplify the computations --
  and indeed, some parameters,
  such as the investor's preferences
  and the growth of the stock even disappear);
\item Pricing kernel (?).
\end{compactitem}

% Chapter 11: Non-structural option pricing

The risk-neutral density (RND) can be estimated from the option prices.
This is a non-parametric approach:
there is no model of the underlying prices evolution.
The authors have apparently never heard of \emph{functional data analysis} (FDA)
and happily present estimates of a density function with negative values;
mixtures of distributions;
Edgeworth expansions
(this is the second time they explain this, but it became abominously confusing);
\emph{maximum entropy} (good to estimate density functions);
splines (FDA),
kernel (local) regression
can give acceptable results, though, but not in the tails.

The book also explains the relation between risk-neutral ans objective density:
the latter incorporates the investor's preferences and the trend (?).

% Chapter 12: Structural option pricing

The last chapter presents option pricing in a stochastic volatility model,
\ie, with time-dependent volatility,
and the volatility, modeled by a \emph{square root process},
comes from a second source of randomness;
and in jump models
(L\'evy processes are often amenable if you use the characteristic function).

% Appendixes (not read)
I have not read the appendices on stochastic calculus.
% Chapter 13: Brownian motion, stochastic calculus
% Chapter 14: Martingales, change of measure
% Chapter 15: Characteristic functions, Fourier transform
% Chapter 16: Jump processes
% Chapter 17: L\'evy processes

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Music constraint programming}\\
  T. Anders (2007)
\end{book}
\emph{Computer aided composition} (CAC) is often based
on stochastic processes such as Markov chains, automata, grammars
(cellular automata, fractals, L-systems, etc.)
or on \emph{constraint programming} -- the topic of these presentations.

\emph{Music theory} studies structures inside musical pieces, such as
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item melody (how notes follow one another, in time);
\item harmony (how notes can be combined, at the same time, to form a ``chord'',
  and how chords follow one another);
\item counterpoint (how melodic lines fit together);
\item harmonic counterpoint (counterpoint on an underlying harmonic progression)
\item musical form (macrostructure)
\item orchestration (choice of instruments)
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Musical representation} is inherently bidimensional:
a musical piece can be seen as
a set of melodies, played at the same time;
a set of chords, played in succession;
a set of measures, played in succession; etc.
List-based (or tree-based) representations (e.g., Haskore) 
will choose one of those representations
and accessing the others will be extremely clumsy.
Instead, those inclusion relations can be represented by an acyclic graph:
an event (note) is both in a melody and a chord,
the chords and bits of melodies are in measures,
melodies and measures are in the piece.

Constraint programming can be used to compose music;
traditionnal programming concepts 
(abstract data types, object-oriented programming) come in handy,
and even functional programmin becomes useful:
the constraints (rules) are functions returning a boolean value,
and you may want to transform them,
\ie, to specify (dynamically) where to apply them.

The \emph{constraint store} is the set of possible values of the variables,
often expressed as a cartesian product of their domains.
The search algorithm progressively reduces it:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Propagate} the constraints,
  while keeping the set of potential solutions a box,
  \ie, $\textbf x \in \prod \llbracket a_i, b_i \rrbracket $;
  for instance, given
  $x_1 = 3$,
  $x_2 \in \llbracket 1, 10 \rrbracket$
  and $x_1 \geq x_2$,
  the search space can be reduced to
  $\{ 3 \} \times \llbracket 1, 3 \rrbracket$;
\item \emph{Distribute} the constraints:
  when you can no longer propagate any constraint,
  choose a variable, and split its domain:
  for instance, $x_1 \in \llbracket 1, 10 \rrbracket$
  can be decomposed into
  $x_1 = 1$ and $x_1 \in \llbracket 2, 10 \rrbracket$,
  or
  $x_1 \in \llbracket 1, 5 \rrbracket$ and $x_1 \in \llbracket 6, 10 \rrbracket$;
  the algorithm then proceeds recursively, on each branch of this tree.
\end{compactitem}
When distributing the constraints,
you have to choose which variable to distribute (\emph{variable ordering})
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{First-fail principle}: start wil the variables that are the most likely to fail
  (it the search in a branch has to fail, it had better fail early),
  e.g., variables with a small domain or with a lot of constraints;
\item left-to-right variable ordering: earlier notes first, longer notes first --
  variable ordering is used in manual composition:
  you classically write the harmonic progression before the actual pitches,
  the melody before the accompaniement;
\end{compactitem}
and how to distribute it (\emph{value ordering})
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item take the first (lowest) value;
\item take a value at random
  (for musical purposes, this is better: the result will be more ``varied'');
\item use some domain-specific heuristic.
\end{compactitem}

Some rules might be too complicated to be evaluated with the information currently available
(e.g., harmonic constraints when the rythm is still undetermined):
their \emph{context} is \emph{inaccessible} and should be resolved as early as possible.

Constraint programming is easy to parallelize.

Should you want to play with constraint programming in general,
you can have a look at
Mozart (an implementation of the Oz multi-paradigm programming language)
or Gecode (C++).

Check \href{http://strasheela.sourceforge.net/documents/TorstenAnders-PhDThesis.pdf}{the author's thesis}
for more details on 
\href{http://strasheela.sourceforge.net/}{Strasheela}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.librecours.org/documents/5/521.pdf}{\emph{Concepts, techniques and\\ models of computer programming}}\\

  P. Van Roy and S. Haridi (2003)
\end{book}

% \texttt{!!} read-only (unbindable copy of a variable)

% Chapter 1 - Introduction

This book about programming languages paradigms,
more recent than \href{ftp://ftp.aw.com/cseng/authors/finkel/apld/}{Finkel's},
is based on Oz (and its implementation \href{http://www.mozart-oz.org/}{Mozart/Oz}),
a \emph{multi-paradigm} programming language --
or rather, Oz was designed to illustrate all the paradigms presented in the book --
it is more a paedagogical language than a real-world one.
This multiplicity is apparent in the variety of ``equal'' operators:
\texttt{==} (test),
\texttt{=} (binding),
\texttt{:=} (assignment),
\texttt{=:} (constraint propagation).
But if you want a language with more rarely-present features, 
such as \emph{dataflow variables} or
\href{http://www.mozart-oz.org/documentation/fdt/index.html}{\emph{constraint programming}},
it might be a good choice.

A word of caution: Mozart/Oz is not portable -- it only runs on 32-bit machines.

The book is based on a series of \emph{kernel languages},
the basic features of Oz, plus the topic of the current chapter,
without any form of syntactic sugar or \emph{linguistic abstraction} (loops, etc.):
these are simpler to reason with.

The book also contains some Fortran-bashing
(replacing a comma by a dot created another valid program
that contributed to the loss of a satellite)
and some shared-state concurrency bashing
(because of a race condition in its stateful concurrent software,
a medical radiation machine actually killed people).

% Chapter 2 - Declarative computation model
\textbf{Declarative computation model}

The various scoping, typing, evaluation policies are presented:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item static (or lexical) scoping
  (a function, or \emph{closure},
  can use variables from the environment in which it was defined --
  this can be used to hide implementation details,
  since that environment is often no longer directly accessible);
\item dynamic scoping
  (a function can use variables from the environment from which it is called,
  e.g., Vision's special (carret) variables);
\item weakly typed
  (you cannot get type errors:
  the computer will silently convert everything --
  even if you are trying to compare, say,
  a real number with a list of strings);
\item strongly typed (you can get type errors);
\item dynamically typed
  (some type checks are made at runtime,
  you may get type errors during the execution
  even though the program compiled fine);
\item statically typed
  (the type checks are made at compile time,
  you cannot get type errors at run-time;
  Alice is apparently a variant of Oz with static typing;
  also note that statically typed languages need not be \emph{explicitely typed}:
  some let the compiler infer the type of each variable);
\item \emph{eager} (supply-driven) evaluation (execute code thouroughly, when it appears);
\item \emph{lazy} (demand-driven) evaluation (only execute code when and if it is needed --
  this allows infinite data structures, such as ``the list of all integers'',
  because they will never be evaluated completely;
  in Oz, define lazy functions with \texttt{fun lazy} instead of \texttt{fun});
  the authors suggest that languages should be eager by default 
  and allow some functions to be lazy;
\item \emph{non-strict} evaluation may perform more operations
  than lazy evaluation (which is guaranteed not to do any non-needed work)
  in the hope that they will be needed later (Haskell is not lazy but non-strict).
\end{compactitem}

Kernel language semantics: 
interesting discussion on how the environment store can be implemented.

Variables can only be assigned once; they are then \emph{bound} to a value;
before that, they are \emph{unbound}, or bound to other (unbound) variables.
This is the \emph{single-assignment store}.

As a result, there is no difference between input and output variables in a procedure:
a procedure just adds information (bindings) to the store;
variables that were unbound and become bound can be seen as outputs,
while variables that were already bound can be seen as inputs.
Functions are mere syntactic sugar:
\verb+x=f(y,z)+ is equivalent to \verb+f(?x,y,z)+
(the question mark is an indication for the programmer:
it is ignored by the compiler).

% Chapter 3 - Declarative programming techniques
\textbf{Declarative programming techniques}

A (superficial) chapter is devoted to declarative programming techniques:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item difference between recursive computation and \emph{iterative computation}
  (it looks recursive, but it uses a \emph{bounded} stack);
\item state transformations, such as \emph{accumulators},
  to turn recursive computations into iterative ones --
  this can be seen as a functional programming design pattern,
  that becomes invisible when the language provides it (states or mutable variables);
\item currying;
\item foldl, foldr, map, filter;
\item \emph{difference lists};
\item complexity
and amortized complexity,
with the \emph{banker's method} (put ``credits'' aside, for each operation, 
use them, if available, when needed, to perform costly operations)

and the \emph{physicist's method}
(use a \emph{potential function} to measure how good the current state is
and whose differences represent the cost of the cleaning operations,
e.g., the number of operations required to rebalance a tree, or $\Theta(1)$ thereof);
\item Serialization (pickling).
\end{compactitem}

For instance, most of the description of a graphical user interface (GUI)
should be declarative (see a later chapter).

A \emph{functor} is a parametrized module,
that depends on some ``interfaces'' (aka \emph{abstract data types} or ADT --
there is apparently nothing in Oz to define types: 
they will only be in the programmer's mind, as C++'s traits),
for which the user will have to choose concrete implementations.
A \emph{module} is an instance of a functor.
More generally, a functor is a function that returns a module --
no language extension is needed, but Oz provides a linguistic abstraction.

An \emph{open program} is a program that interacts with other programs (or humans), 
only known at run time;
its execution is therefore not deterministic.

% Chapter 4 - Declarative concurrency
\textbf{Declarative concurrency}

Because of single-assignment,
Oz's threads (similar to a shell's background processes)
do not lead to observable non-determinism or race conditions.
The \emph{causal order} is the partial order between the execution steps.
Threads can be \emph{ready} or \emph{suspended};
a \emph{scheduler} decides which to run,
trying not to \emph{starve} them.

If you add \emph{exceptions} to the concurrent model, it is no longer declarative,
because the state ``before the exception was thrown'' is execution-dependent.

Dataflow variables can be used to communicate between threads:
synchronization is implicit (no locks, etc.),
dependencies can be dynamic (dictated by the data, not harcoded),
they also allow computations with partial values.
A dataflow variable can be seen as both
stateful (it can change state, \ie, become bound -- but only once)
and stateless (its binding is monotonic: 
information can be added but neither removed nor altered).

Single-assignment variables are related to
\emph{futures} (an unbound object whose contents are being computed in separate thread)
and \emph{I-structures} (arrays of single-assignment variables).

Instead, you can use \emph{streams} (analogue to Unix pipes).
They can be demand-driven (lazy),
supply-driven (eager, but if you generate data faster than you can consume it,
you will run out of memory)
or with a bounded buffer.

This chapter details applications such as
logic gates (\emph{lift} boolean operators to streams)
and \emph{coroutines}.

Mozart/oZ has a parser generator tool, \texttt{gump},
to extend the language with linguistic abstractions.

To accomodate demand-driven concurrency,
the kernel language is augmented with a \emph{trigger store}
and a \texttt{ByNeed} primitive (that asks for its argument to be computed).
(There is a problem, though: in \texttt{X==Y},
if neither \texttt{X} or \texttt{Y} is bounded or needed,
they both remain unbound and unneeded;
this may be a deadlock.)

Declarative concurrency can be used 
to let the computer determine the order of the computations,
in a data-dependent way -- for instance, to draw a tree, you can could let the computer
compute the coordinates of the points, without bothering exploring the tree yourself;
or to solve the \emph{Hamming problem},
\ie, to enumerate all integers of the form $2^a3^b5^c$,\\
\verb+H=1|{Merge3{Times 2 H}{Times 3 H}{Times 5 H}}+.

Logic programming generalizes this:
the constraint propagation is no longer local
and the constraints are no longer equalities.

Laziness can have beneficial effects on complexity:
it can turn an $O(f(n))$ \emph{average} complexity algorithm
(say, a queue implementation,
which is problematic for (pure) functional languages)
into a \emph{worst-case} $O(f(n))$ complexity.

There is however a mismatch between computers (optimized to modify data) and
the declarative model (which does not modify data).
You can overcome some of those efficiency problems with
\emph{state},
\emph{memoization} (but this changes the signature of the function --
  think Haskell \emph{monads} -- it looks awkward because it is),
\emph{instrumentation} (counting function calls -- 
this sounds like \emph{aspect oriented programming} (AOP)).
Furthermore, the stateful model is more expressive: complex algorithms are simpler --
in particular, for graph algorithms -- my big problem with Haskell...

Interaction with the outside/real world brings observable nondeterminism:
communicating with (independent) clients;
displaying a video stream, skipping frames when needed;
interacting with stateful components (hardware, libraries, UI);
implementing specifications, protocols expressed in terms of state.

\emph{Impedence matching} suggests to mix several computation models, for instance:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item a sequencial component in a concurrent model
  (you need a ``thread-safety enhancing'' layer);
\item a declarative component in a stateful model;
\item a centralized component in a distributed system;
\item a non-security-conscious component in a security-conscious environment
  (you need a ``protector'' -- sandbox, jail, chroot, etc.);
\item a non-failure-safe component in a failure-safe system
  (you need a fault-tolerance layer).
\end{compactitem}

% Chapter 5 - Message passing concurrency
\textbf{Message passing concurrency}

\emph{Message-passing concurrency} can be obtained
by adding \emph{ports} to the kernel language;
they generalize streams: several threads can send messages to a port,
a stream knows where the data is coming from.

Message passing can be used to implement
RMI (remote method invocation -- this can probably be seen as a synonym of message passing)
and multi-agent systems (MAS,
such as
\href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elevator_algorithm}{lift control systems} --
also used for hard disk heads).
The message passed can be a \emph{continuation},
\ie, a block of code or an indication of the computations 
to run after the message has been processed --
the reciepient of the message is responsible for the next steps of the program.

This chapter also contains an introduction to
\href{http://www.erlang.org/}{Erlang/OTP}
(Erlang is the language, OTP is the imlpementation or, rather, a set of libraries) --
if you need that in a mainstream language, 
check the POE (Perl) and Twisted (Python) frameworks.

Here are a few concurrent message-passing patterns:
several ports within a single thread (but you have to do the scheduler's job);
a queue (two ports, clients can send data to it, or retrieve data, in a FIFO manner);
thread and subthread termination detection.

The \emph{non-deterministic concurrent} model (aka concurrent logic programming)
is between the declarative concurrent and the message passing one:
it allows you to merge two streams
(if you want more, it becomes awkward and inefficient;
you would expect compiler to have been developped to make this efficient,
but you would be wrong).

A port can be seen as a state: it is a list of values,
which can be generated and used anywhere (contrary to a stream);
if you only look at the latest value available,
it behaves like a (stateful) variable.
\emph{Cells} implement the same idea, without concurrency: 
the model remains \emph{sequential}.

% Chapter 6 - Explicit State
\textbf{Explicit State}

State can be implicit (only present in the programmer's mind, as with accumulators)
or explicit:
the kernel language can be extended with \emph{cells},
that can be written to.
Thus, Oz ends up with three equality operators:
\texttt{==} (equality test),
\texttt{=} (mathematical equality, binding),
\texttt{:=} (assignment).
Also beware of the difference between equality between
cells (\verb+X==Y+)
and their contents (\verb+@X==@Y+).

The authors try to motivate the need for states by encapsulation and compositionnality -- 
unconvincingly.
\emph{Invariants} (and encapsulation) can help reason with stateful programs (or components).

\emph{Component-based programming} (encapsulation):
procedures (functions and blocks of code);
functors (compilation units, modules);
threads;
object-oriented programming adds another way of composing components: inheritance
(but is adds unwanted dependencies;
design patterns are the art of avoiding the resulting problems).

An \emph{abstract data type} (ADT) can be:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item open (its structure is visible and tweakable);
\item secure (encapsulated;
  security can be obtained through unforgeable tokens provided by the compiler or the VM,
  or just with lexical scoping)
\item declarative (immutable, but you quickly end up creating a large number of instances;
  those instances have to be passed around)
\item stateful (single instance; it can be a state in the scope of the procedures 
  that need it and need not be passed around)
\item unbundled (the data and the operations on the data are stored separately --
  the compiler and/or virtual machine (VM) can ensure that the ADT remains secure, 
  if needed);
\item bundled.
\end{compactitem}
Declarative languages (Scheme, Prolog) tend to provide open, declarative, unbundled ADTs;
object-oriented ones (Java, Smalltalk) tend to provide secure, stateful, bundled ADTs.

You can use state to implement \emph{revocable capabilities}
(functions you may no longer be allowed to call after some time --
just redefine them to raise an exception).

The book distinguishes the following (irrelevant?) parameter-passing mechanisms:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item call by reference
\item call by variable (a special case of call-by-reference, 
  when the argument is a cell -- 
  in procedural languages, this is often called call-by-reference);
\item call by value (the procedure cannot change the value of it argument);
\item call by value-result (call-by-variable, 
  when the argument is supposed to be modified to hold the result, 
  e.g., a procedure that would increment its argument);
\item call by name (the argument is a \emph{function} that returns a reference; 
  a procedure with such arguments is a \emph{thunk});
\item call by need (call-by-name, but the argument is only evaluated once).
\end{compactitem}

Explicit state allows the implementation of
stateful (mutable, and even extensible) \emph{collections} (arrays, dictionnaries, etc.).

\emph{Contracts} (or \emph{invariants}: 
what should be true before and after each operation --
you can use \emph{assertions})
can be used to formally prove \emph{partial correctness};
to show that loops are not infinite,
find some integral positive quantity that decreases at each iteration.

The state chapter contains a few digressions on software design:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item do not dilute responsabilities;
\item share knowledge;
\item document the \emph{interfaces};
\item start small;
\item the components can communicate with
  procedures,
  coroutines,
  synchronously,
  asynchronously,
  via mailboxes (asynchronously with pattern matching)
  or a \emph{tuple space} (a mailbox with several reciepients);
\item the \emph{interfaces} should be independent of the computation model 
  used for the implementation
\end{compactitem}
and on the future of programming:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item ``Components will make programming accessible to application users'';
  this is already the case for statistical or digital signal processing applications
  (R, matlab, \href{http://puredata.info/}{puredata}, etc.);
\item Professionnal programmers will combine larger components --
  currently, they are still too complicated (Java Beans)
  and their interfaces are too vaguely documented.
\end{compactitem}

State does not blend well with concurrency:
whenever possible, use message-passing concurrency.

% Chapter 7 - Object-Oriented Programming
\textbf{Object-Oriented Programming}

\emph{Inheritance} allows ADTs to be built incrementally, avoiding code duplication,
but spreading the implementation.
(``Early on, it was believed that inheritance would solve the problem of software reuse.
This has not worked out in practice.'')
%The failure of inheritance as a reuse technique is clear from the success of
%other techniques such as components, frameworks, and design patterns.''

In Oz, a class is a record containing a set of attribute names and a set of methods;
methods have named and optional arguments;
dynamic binding (virtual methods) and static binding (non-virtual method)
are decided  when the method is called, not when it is defined;
it allows \emph{encapsulation} and
access control (private, public)

\emph{Reflection}
(e.g., examining, and perhaps even changing inheritance relations at runtime),
through a \emph{meta-object protocol},
can be used for debugging, customization, separation of concerns (AOP?):
method wrapping (intercepting each method call, 
looking at and possibly modifying its arguments),
serialization (creation of a \emph{chunk} -- 
a few chapters earlier, it was a \emph{pickle}),
cloning, etc.

You should \textbf{not} use inheritance to structure your program, but only to create types.
(Will this be exemplified in the chapter on GUIs?)
In particular, inheritance should not break invariants of the parent class;
you should \textbf{not} violate the \emph{substitution property}.
Some people also subclass to fix (patch) parent classes,
needlessly complicating the inheritance graph.

Avoid multiple inheritance when the classes inherited from have something in common.

A \emph{mixin} is a class often used in multiple inheritance,
that does not need to know anything about the classes it will be mixed with;
for instance, a \texttt{Batcher} class,
with a single \texttt{batch} method,
that takes a list of messages (or 0-argument methods)
and sends them to \texttt{self}.

Class diagrams (UML) are useful but not a panacea:
they do specify the functionality (in particular, \emph{invariants}) of the class;
they do not reflect the dynamic behaviour of the application;
they display the structure of the application at a rather low level.

The OOP chapter contains an introduction to Java, should you need one,
and gives a design pattern example:
the \emph{composite} (just a \emph{tree} type).

\emph{Object-based programming} is object-oriented programming without inheritance.:
it provides encapsulation, state shared by several methods.

If you need to define several functions $f_1,...,f_n$
that can operate on several types $T_1,...,T_m$,
you can opt for a \emph{type decomposition}
  (define a virtual class $T$, let $T_1,...,T_m$ inherit from it, 
  and implement all the methods;
  you can easily add a new type,
  but adding a new function requires modifying all the classes)
or a \emph{functional decomposition}
  (within each function, check for the type of the argument and act accordingly;
  it is very easy to add a new function, but cumbersome to add a new type --
  you have to modify all the functions).
S3 classes in~R are a functional decomposition --
I call this \emph{method-oriented programming}.

An \emph{active object} is a port object which is an instance of a class,
\ie, an instance of a class, listening for (concurrent) messages
and launching a method for each received message.
The \href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josephus_problem}{\emph{Flavius Josephus problem}}
illustrates active objects:
$n=20$ soldiers are standing in a circle are committing a sequential collective suicide,
by killing every $k=3$rd (remaining) soldier -- 
what should be your position if you want to remain alive?

Active objects can be used to implement a simple concurrent event manager.

% Chapter 8 - Shared state concurrency
\textbf{Shared state concurrency}

% gg: Dekker's algorithm

Coroutines can be seen as a manual scheduling of threads in a sequential environment.

Prefer the message-passing model for multi-agent programs,
consider the shared-state approach for data-centric programs
(programs that access and update a large, central database).

The \emph{maximally concurrent model} uses one thread per instruction;
only data dependencies affects their execution;
you do not have to explicitely create threads --
you might not want to use it, though.

The kernel language provides an atomic \texttt{Exchange} operation
that can be used to implement concurrent ADTs (ADTs whose operations
can be run simultaneously).

% TODO: Token passing

Shared-state concurrent programming uses various forms of locks to create atomic actions:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{simple lock};
\item \emph{reentrant lock}
  (when a thread is inside a lock, it can enter it again 
  (e.g., by calling the same function recursively)).
  \emph{monitor} (wait points, to wait before, not after, entering a lock;
  e.g., a thread filling a bounded buffer would wait
  for enough room to be available in the buffer
  before requesting a lock to add an element;
  monitors use \texttt{wait} and \texttt{notify} operations;
  often, methods are \emph{guarded},
  \ie, they wait until some condition is satisfied before starting);
\item light (ACI) transactions (abortable, atomic, not persistent)
  or full ACID transactions --
  but how to add them to a \emph{programming} language is still an open question,
  you will implement them by hand or rely on some library (or database) --
  databases can use
  \emph{optimistic concurrency control}
  with \emph{two-phase locking}
  (first grant locks without releasing any lock;
  then release locks without granting any lock;
  in strict 2-phase locking, all the locks are released at the same time)
  and \emph{deadlock avoidance} (using the \emph{wait-for} graph).
\end{compactitem}

%
% Other books:
% Concurrent programming in Java (it assumes that threads are expensive -- as in Java).
% Concurrent programming in Erlang.
% Concurrent Programming: principles and practice.
% Transaction Processing: Concepts and Techniques.

A \emph{Tuple space} (aka \emph{Linda})
is a (concurrent) multiset with non-blocking pattern matching capabilities
(you can add objects to the tuple space;
you can extract one object of a given ``type'' to the tuple space,
in a blocking or non-blocking way).

% Chapter 9 - Relational programming
\textbf{Relational programming}

A \emph{procedure} is a map 
(in the mathematical sense: it takes inputs and returns outputs);
it can be replaced by a \emph{relation}
(in mathematical terms, 
you are replacing the category of sets by that of correspondances):
the ``function'' can be multi-valued (or even sometimes fail to return a value)
and the input and output arguments need not be the same for each call.
You can do that in the declarative model, 
but you would end up with a lot of unbound variables:
the relational model adds a \texttt{search} operation, that tries to assign a value
to all currently unbound variables, while respecting all the constraints.
There is often also a \texttt{fail} statement,
which indicates that the current choice is wrong
(and that another one should be tried):
this allows for constraints that cannot be expressed by bounding variables,
such as inequalities.

The search space can be huge
and \emph{generate-and-test} programs exhaustively examine it:
this kind of \emph{non-algorithmic programming},
without fine-tuning,
can be used in databases (the amount of data is huge, but the search space small)
or for toy (but perhaps instructive) examples.

\emph{Encapsulated search} runs the program in an environment
that controls how the search tree is explored (depth first, breadth first, etc.)
and how many solutions are returned
(just one, all of them, on-demand generation of new solutions, etc.);
it also hides multiple bindings to the rest of the application.
It can be obtained by adding a \texttt{Solve} function to the model.

\emph{Propositional logic} only uses $\vee$, $\wedge$, $\neg$
(you can use those to define $\Rightarrow$, $\Leftrightarrow$) and can be used
to express, prove or disprove \emph{tautologies}.
\emph{First-order predicate logic} adds quantifiers ($\forall$, $\exists$)
and allows atoms to have arguments; for instance, it can be used to express
genealogical relations and questions.
For performance reasons, languages based on first-order predicate logic (Prolog)
restrict the form of the axioms, e.g., to \emph{Horn clauses}
$$ \forall x_1,\dots,x_k \quad a_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge a_n \implies a $$
and allow the user to provide \emph{operational knowledge}
(e.g., instead of specifying what a sort algorithm should do,
specify what an efficient sort algorithm should do).

\emph{Pure Prolog} does not allow higher-order programming,
full prolog
(which adds useful constructs with no corresponding logical semantics)
only partially supports it.

Logic programming can be seen as a new ``control structure''
that can be added to other models
(but the more stateful your program, the trickier it becomes).

Relational programming was traditionally used for
natural language processing (NLP) -- or, more generally,
to parse ambiguous languages.

Relational programming can also be used to store relations in a database;
a \emph{deductive database} can deduce tuples not explicitely stored
(e.g., paths in a graph).

This chapter also contains an introduction to Prolog;
most Prolog applications actually solve algorithmic problems
(expert systems, deductuve databases, parsing --
most of the program is declarative,
but the programmer somehow gives algorithmic cues to the computer).

Prolog is limited to \emph{backtracking}, \ie, depth-first search.

Relational programming still evolves:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Pure declarative, higher-order languages
  (\href{http://www.mercury.csse.unimelb.edu.au/}{Mercury});
\item Multi-paradigm languages that allow a clearer separation
  of the declarative and non-declarative parts of the program (Oz);
\item Constraint programming, either as a language feature (Oz, SICStus Prolog)
  or as a separate library (\href{http://www.gecode.org/}{GeCode}, Ilog).
\end{compactitem}

% Chapter 10 - Graphical User Interface Programming
\textbf{Graphical User Interface Programming}

GUI design is usually restricted to a single computation model such as:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item imperative (Tcl/Tk);
\item object-oriented (Java);
\item functional (Haskell and its fudgets);
\item declarative (HTML);
\item graphical.
\end{compactitem}

The book advocates a multi-paradigm approach,
with a declarative base augmented by objects and threads
(it does not look very different from what is traditionnally done;
except that the widget description really \emph{is} a data structure
and (can but) does not have to be built in a procedural way):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The structure of the widgets, their types, their initial values, 
their behaviour after simple events (resize) are purely declarative;
\item Actions (procedures run when external events occur) are procedural;
\item Handlers (objects that change the interface) are also procedural.
\end{compactitem}
This allows you, for instance, to completely change the GUI 
(say, from Gtk to QT) while it is being used.

% Chapter 11 - Distributed programming
\textbf{Distributed programming}

There are various types of \emph{distributed} systems:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Shared memory multiprocessor;
\item Distributed memory multiprocessor;
\item Distributed memory multiprocessor with partial failure
  (so far, these are
  HPC (high-performance computing)
  and/or
  HA (high availability)
  \emph{clusters});
\item Open distributed system
  (with naming and security problems).
\end{compactitem}

Distributed programming is more complicated than concurrent programming:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item the processes are completely separated
  (they cannot share state or dataflow variables;
  the data has to be converted
  (\emph{marshaled}/serialized/pickled and
  unmarshaled/unserialized/unpickled)
  and sent around);
\item ressources (disk, memory, licence of commercial software, etc.) are localized;
\item networks are several orders of magnitude slower than memory;
\item failures are more common;
\item not all components of the network may be trustworthy;
\item nodes may be added or removed at runtime.
\end{compactitem}

In the \emph{network-transparent approach}
(which assumes that none of those problems occur),
the objects are local to a given node or machine
(that synchronizes their usage and ensures their consistency)
and respond to messages, whatever their origin --
this is equivalent to concurrent programming.

Streams and ports can be readily used
(the two ends of a stream can be launched in different processes).
Other locking mechanisms can be used
(e.g., distributed mutual exclusion using token passing).

Objects can be \emph{stationary}
(they remain on the same node --
this is desireable if they access some local ressources, e.g., disk)
or \emph{mobile}
(the data is copied around (this is a \emph{cache})
and the right to update the state is also moved around --
this can be complemented by an \emph{invalidation} mechanism
that allows several nodes to hold a copy of the object).

Dataflow objects can be shared in an asynchronous way.

Distributed binding and distributed garbage collection can be slightly trickier.

This approach can be augmented:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Network awareness (explicit use of message-passing for performance)
%\item Openness (?)
\item \emph{Naming}
  (language entities can be refered to by an (unforgeable) reference, name or ticket --
  a ticket exists outside the system --
  in particular, you will not be able to reclaim the memory used by an object
  if a ticket has been issued for it --
  tickets therefore often have an expiry time);
%\item Localized ressources
\item Partial failure tolerance: you can detect
  (permanent) process failure (\emph{fail-silent})
  and (temporary) network failure,
  either in a synchronous, lazy way
  (you only notice the component is unavailable when you try to access it)
  or in an asynchronous, eager way
  (with a heart-beat mechanism to check what is alive and what is not;
  you may want to either wait or raise an exception
  when trying to access a failed ressource);
  your application design should allow faults to be \emph{confined};
  fault tolerance is extremely difficult with shared states:
  prefer message-passing concurrency;
\item Active fault tolerance (through replication and failover)
\item Security (resilience to malicious failures), at several levels:
  application,
  language
    (check the \href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E_programming_language}{\emph{E} programming language}),
  language implementation
    (you should not be able to tamper with compiled binaries
    or the virtual machine (VM)),
  operating system,
  network,
  hardware.
\end{compactitem}

% Chapter 12 - Constraint programming
\textbf{Constraint programming}

Alternate the following two steps:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Local deduction:
  combine the constraints to reduce
  the space of possible solutions (the \emph{computation space}),
  while keeping it manageable
  (e.g., a box $\prod \llbracket a_i, b_i \rrbracket$);
  to facilitate this,
  the constraints are completed by \emph{propagators}, \ie,
  function that explain how the constraint can help reduce the computation space
  (Oz already provides some of them;
  they can be seen as a generalization of the use of Horn clauses in Prolog);
\item Search: split the problem $P$ into $P \wedge C$ and $P \wedge \neg C$,
  where $C$ is a cleverly-chosen new constraint; this choice is called a
  \emph{distribution strategy};
  the \emph{search strategy} is the way the resulting tree is explored
  (depth-first, breadth-first or some domain-specific heuristic).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \href{http://soundlab.cs.princeton.edu/publications/chuck_icmc2003.pdf}{\emph{ChucK: a concurrent, on-the-fly,\\ audio programming language}}\\
  G. Wang and P.R. Cook\\
  %Proceedings of the
  International Computer Music Conference (2003)
\end{book}
ChucK is a \emph{strongly-timed} audio programming language, based on data flows:
data and time are clearly separated.
The real-time problems are the computer's job:
you just code in frozen time and say
``proceed to the next sample'' -- or beat, or user-generated event (Midi, network, etc.).
It features lightweight threads (``shreds'')
and a massively overloaded operator  \texttt{=>} (assignment, dataflow, etc.).
Contrary to its predecessors,
it does not seem to have escaped from the 1950s
(\href{http://www.csounds.com/}{Csound},
\href{http://supercollider.sourceforge.net/}{SuperCollider})
and is a real programming language, not a graphical one
(\href{http://puredata.info/}{Pure Data}).

Also check \href{http://www.iua.upf.es/~gcoleman/chuck/tutorial/tutorial.html}{G. Coleman's tutorial}.

Beware: ChucK is not portable, it only runs on 32-bit machines.

\nextbook

