%  G. Wang and P.R. Cook\\
%  ACM Multimedia 2004, Open Source Software Competition
%\end{book}
%A shorter article on ChucK.

%\nextbook

%\begin{book}
%  \emph{Designing and implementing the ChucK programming language}\\
%  G. Wang, P.R. Cook, A. Misra
%\end{book}
%A shorter article on ChucK and what is meant by \emph{stronly-timed}.

%\nextbook

%\begin{book}
%  \emph{Yeah, chuck it! => Dynamic, contrallable interface mapping}\\
%  G. Wang, A. Misra, A. Kapur, P.R. Cook\\
%  NIME 2005
%\end{book}
%Another short article on ChucK.

%\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://soundlab.cs.princeton.edu/publications/uana_icmc2007.pdf}{\emph{Combining analysis and synthesis\\ in the ChucK programming language}}\\
  G. Wang, R. Fiebrink, P.R. Cook (2007)\\
\end{book}
The ChucK language has been extended with analysis operations (FFT, etc.);
contrary to synthesis operations,
they are not automatically computed for each sample
(but usually less often)
and have to be called (slightly) more explicitely;
consequently, a new operator \verb+=^+ (upchuck)
was introduced besides \texttt{=>}  (chuck).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \href{http://soundlab.cs.princeton.edu/publications/audicle_icmc2004.pdf}{\emph{The Audicle: A Context-Sensitive, On-the-fly Audio Programming Environ/mentality}}\\
  G. Wang and P.R. Cook \\
  %Proceedings of the
  International Computer Music Conference 2004
\end{book}
The \emph{Audicube} is a 3-dimensional IDE for ChucK,
exploiting the real-time and on-the-fly nature of the language --
think Compiz/Fusion (the faces of the cube) and VRML (inside each face),
to visualize data-flow relations,
time relations (and program execution),
parent-child thread relations,
thread or process information \`a la \texttt{top}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.maths.abdn.ac.uk/~bensondj/html/music.pdf}{\emph{Music, a mathematical offering}}\\
  D. Benson (2006)
\end{book}

Elementary (in its prerequisites) but excellent book
that disspells a lot of misconceptions
about the links between music and mathematics
(in spite of a couple of overly vague mathematical statements such as
``if the function $f$ is absolutely integrable, \ie, $L^1$,
then $f(t) \to 0$ as $\va t \to \infty$
except perhaps on a set of measure zero'',
the book is eminently readable).

Here are some of the topics covered.

Sine waves are considered ``pure'' not because of Fourier analysis
(you could use other bases)
but because they are solutions of differential equations of the form 
$y'' + ay' + by = 0$,
which describe the vibration of our eardrums (hence, the sounds we hear)
and most ``simple'' systems (string and wood instruments).

\emph{Bessel functions}, \ie the Fourier  decomposition
$$ \sin( \phi + z \sin \theta ) = \sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty} J_n(z) \sin( \phi + n z ) $$
appear in the description of percussion and brass instruments,
\emph{FM synthesis} and planetary motion (this last point was unclear).

Whether two sounds are consonnant or dissonant 
does not only depend on the ratio of their fundamental frequencies, 
but also on their \emph{timbre} (\ie, spectrum):
when the spectrum has peaks (\emph{partials})
at integral multiples of the fundamental frequency 
(string and wood instruments -- such partials are called \emph{harmonics}),
the common belief that simple ratios are consonnant is correct;
but when the partials are not integral multiples of the fundamental
(percussions and brass instruments), this is no longer true --
you can even tweak the spectrum so that 
any interval you choose be consonnant or dissonnant.
Pure sine wave (no partials at all) are not dissonnant 
if they are sufficiently far apart.
The ``consonnance function'' for a given spectrum can be obtained as
a linear combination of that of pure sine waves
(which are obtained experimentally).

You cannot define an ``instantaneous spectrum''
(you have to have a non-zero window size;
the smaller the window the more smeared the spectrum --
this is actually \emph{Heisenberg's uncertainty principle}),
but the \emph{Hilbert transform} can give you an
\emph{instantaneous frequency}.

The harmonics of a sound can be seen as a form of periodicity in its spectrum,
which could be studied by another Fourier transform: on a logarithmic scale,
this is called the \emph{cepstrum},
$ \mathscr F \ln \mathscr F f$;
it is used in voice recognition to separate the partials
(which are then called \emph{formants}).

The book also presents a few musical paradoxes:
\emph{Shepard scale} (a scale whose pitch seems to be always increasing, ad infinitum);
\emph{virtual pitch} (the pitch we hear is that of the fundamental,
even if it is not present and only some of its integral multiples are there);
\emph{combination tones}, \ie, hearing
$f_1 + f_2$, $f_1 - f_2$, $2f_1 - f_2$, $f_1 - 2 f_2$
when frequencies $f_1$ and $f_2$ are sounded -- 
this is not the \emph{beat} phenomenon (we would hear
$\tfrac12(f_1+f_2)$ and $\tfrac12(f_1-f_2)$
but results from the non-linearities (quadratic and cubic terms) in our ear --
probably in the neural feedback.

When you compose music,
limiting yourself to a set of allowable pitches or \emph{scale}
simplifies your work.
For instance, you can start with a note and add ``pleasant'' multiples of it,
such as octaves (2/1) and fifths (3/2). The \emph{pythagorean scale} does just this:
start with a note, add a fifth, another, and so on,
but each time you exceed one octave, remove one octave;
stop when you get close enough from your starting point.
You never reach exactly the starting frequency, because $\log 3 / \log 2$ is irrational --
its increasingly accurate rational approximations (via \emph{continued fractions})
give rise to increasingly large scales (5, 12, 41, 53, etc.).

This set of notes is already a compromise, but we often want more:
we want other simple ratios to be there. We have the octave 2/1, the fifth 3/2,
the fourth 4/3 (this is an octave minus a fifth, so we already have it),
the third 5/4 -- more generally, \emph{harmonics} brought down to the same octave $a/2^b$.
For instance, we could use the 3-note scale
1:5/4:3/2 (often written 4:5:6 to emphasize 
that we are using the 4th, 5th and 6th harmonics --
this is called a \emph{major third}).
\emph{Just intonation} is obtained by taking three major thirds
(CEG (I), the previous one FAC (IV) and the next one GBD (V)),
giving 7 notes -- should you want 12, you can fill in the 5 remaining ones as you want.

Classical harmony tends to move from triad to triad:
the \emph{mean-tone scale} asks for exact major thirds 5:4
and wants the note in the middle to be exactly in the middle:
CDE, FGA and GAB are eaxctly
$ 1 : \sqrt5/2:5/4 $
(the two remaining semitones are made equal).
The problem is that the farther you move on the circle of fifths,
the worse the fifths become (\emph{wolf fifth})

The \emph{well-tempered scale} (or irregular temperament, or circulating temperament)
tries to correct this.

\emph{Equal temperament} is the easiest scale: 12 equally-spaced notes.
This is the most common nowadays. The number of notes comes from the
continued fraction approximating $\log 3 / \log 2$.
Regarding continued fractions, you might want to notice that
the golden ratio $[1,1,1,1,\dots]$ is as far away as possible from the rationals.

In all those scales, the octave was pivotal:
but you can remove it
(especially with instruments with only odd harmonics,
such as open-ended wood instruments like the clarinet)
and consider, for instance,
13 notes in one octave and a half (Bohlen and Pierce)
or focus on the ratios 3/2 and 4/3 instead of 2/1 and 3/2 (W.~Carlos).

Since the Fourier transform of a \emph{digital} signal is periodic,
it can be written as a function of
$z = \exp 2 \pi i \nu \Delta t$
instead of $\nu$:
this is the \emph{$z$-transform}.

Sound synthesis is slightly touched upon:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{additive synthesis}: this is how cathedral organs work;
\item \emph{AM synthesis} (amplitude modulation): 
the \emph{ADSR enveloppe} (attack-decay-sustain-release)
is actually an example of AM synthesis;
\item FM synthesis (frequency modulation),
whose Fourier coefficients are \emph{Bessel functions};
the book contains a small introduction to Csound, qualified of ``tedious'' --
I would have said ``antiquated'';
\item Granular synthesis (no details)
\item Phase vocoder: transform the windowed discrete Fourrier transform;
\item Chebychev polynomials: applying the function
$T_2: x \mapsto x^2 - 1$ doubles the frequency, \ie,
$T_2( \cos \nu t ) = \cos 2 \nu t$;
the other Chebychev polynomials are defined similarly
and you can combine them, e.g., to turn sime waves into square waves.
\end{compactitem}

The book ends with a chapter on symmetry -- or \emph{group theory}.
Musical pieces exhibit some form of symmetry, in the rythm (temporal)
or the time$\times$pitch space:
you can recognize the seven \emph{frieze types}.
(To avoid boredom, this is often just an approximate symmetry:
for instance, the same motive can be transposed a step higher,
while being constrained to remain in the same scale --
some intervals will subtly change.)
\emph{Change ringing} (or \emph{campanology}) is an
\href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oulipo}{Oulipo}-like constraint
on Church (bell) music composition, involving constrained paths
in the symmetric group $\mathfrak S_n$.

In spite of its qualities, the book almost looks unfinished:
when you reach the end, you still want more,
you have the impression that only the surface has been scratched,
that only the starting points have been exposed --
in particular, there is hardly anything about
composition, harmony, counterpoint, sonification or stochastic processes.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimization methods in portfolio management and option hedging}\\
  H. Pham (2007)
\end{book}

Very clear (and complete) introduction to \emph{continuous-time portfolio management}.
After recalling the von Newmann--Morgenstern utility theory,
with a clear motivation for the absolute and relative risk aversions
and details on the \emph{Allais paradox}
(expected utility cannot reflect human preferences)
\begin{align*}
  P_{X_1} &= 0.33 \delta_{2500} + 0.66 \delta_{2400} + 0.01 \delta_0 \\
  P_{X_2} &= \delta_{2400} \\
  P_{Y_1} &= 0.34 \delta_{2500} + 0.66 \delta_0 \\
  P_{Y_2} &= 0.33 \delta_{2500} + 0.67 \delta_0 \\
\end{align*}
$$ X_2 > X_1, \qquad Y_2 > Y_1 $$
the author presents,
on a discrete example,
the two approaches to
final-utility-maximizing
multi-period portfolio management:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Dynamyc programming;
\item The margingale approach:
  \begin{compactitem}[\ensuremath{\cdot}]
  \item Find the \emph{risk-neutral measure},
    \ie, a measure $\QQ$ for which the stock prices are a martingale;
    the price of a claim $H$ is then $x = E^{\QQ}[H]$;
  \item Find the claim $H$ solving the optimization problem
    \begin{align*}
      \text{Maximize}  & E[ U(H) ] \\
      \text{such that} & E^{\QQ}[H] = x
    \end{align*}
  \item Find a portfolio strategy leading to this wealth $H$.
  \end{compactitem}
\end{compactitem}
The continuous-time analogues are then detailed,
together with a few examples:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Optimal strategy in a 1-stock Black-Scholes world 
  (actively-rebalanced with constant weights);
\item \emph{Superreplication cost}, \ie,
  minimum initial capital of a strategy
  whose payoff almost surely dominates a given payoff $g(X_T)$,
  in a Black-Scholes world with non-constant volatility
  (no assumption whatsoever on the volatility, beyond measurability)
  \[ \dfrac{d X_t}{X_t} = \alpha_t d W_t \]
\item Optimal strategy for a diffusion model
  \[ \dfrac{dS_t}{S_t} = \mu_t dt + \sigma_t dW_t \]
\item \emph{Quantile hedging}: since superhedging
  \[ P[\, \text{Payoff} \geq \text{Desired payoff} \,] = 1 \]
  can be expensive,
  one might prefer \emph{VaR (value at risk) hedging}
  \[ P[\, \text{Payoff} \geq \text{Desired payoff} \,] \geq 1 - \alpha; \]
  conversely, one can choose the initial investment $x$ and look for the strategy that maximizes
  \[ P[\, \text{Payoff} \geq \text{Desired payoff} \,]. \]
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic control theory\\ for optimal investment}\\
  M.T. Castillo and G. Parrocha
\end{book}
The \emph{Cramer--Lundberg model}, in non-life insurance, is
\begin{align*}
  \text{Surplus} &= \text{Initial capital} + \text{Income} - \text{Outflows} \\
  R(t) &= r + ct - \sum_{k=1}^{N(t)} X_k \\
  dR &= c dt - dS
\end{align*}
where the aggregate claim $S(t) = \sum_{k=1}^{N(t)} X_k$
is a \emph{compounded Poisson process}
(or a \emph{Cox process} or a \emph{general renewal process} -- those notions are not defined).

In order to control (diversify) the risk,
an insurance company can invest part of its assets:
the articles considers that a constant amount $a$ is invested,
a proportion $b(t)$ in a risky asset,
a proportion $1-b(t)$ in a riskless asset.
\begin{align*}
  d \text{Risk} &= c dt - dS \\
  \dfrac{ d \text{Price}_0 }{ \text{Price}_0 } &= \rho dt \\
  \dfrac{ d \text{Price}_1 }{ \text{Price}_1 } &= \mu dt + \sigma dW \\
  dI &= a (1-b) \dfrac{ d \text{Price}_0 }{ \text{Price}_0 } + ab \dfrac{ d \text{Price}_1 }{ \text{Price}_1 } \\
  dU &= dR + dI \\
  U(0) &= u \\
\end{align*}
\[
  \text{Maximize}\ P[\, \nexists t \ U(t) < 0 \, ]
\]
Finding the investment process $b$ is a stochastic control problem,
which can be solved with the Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman (HJB) equation.

(The mathematical details in the article look fishy:
before knowing anything about the solution,
not even its existence,
they start to assume it is twice differentiable...)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Some applications and methods of large deviations in finance and insurance}\\
  H. Pham (2007)
\end{book}
Clear but rather technical presentation of \emph{large deviation theory} and its applications in finance.

Large deviation theory is concerned with the asymptotic expansion of the probability of large events:
for instance, the central limit theorem states that
\[
  P \left[
    \va{ \dfrac1n \sum X_i - \mu } \geq a
  \right]
  \longrightarrow_{n\to\infty} 0
\]
and \emph{Cramer's theorem} states that
\begin{align*}
  P \left[ \dfrac1n \sum X_i \geq a \right] & \sim_{n\to\infty} e^{ - n \Gamma^*(a) } \\
  \Gamma( \theta ) &= \ln E[ e^{\theta X} ] \\
  \Gamma^*(x) &= \sup_{ \theta \in \RR } \left[ \theta x - \Gamma(theta) \right].
\end{align*}
$\Gamma$ is called the \emph{cumulant generating function} (cgf);
it is the logarithm of the Laplace transform;
$\Gamma^*$ is called the \emph{Fenchel--Legendre transform} of $\Gamma$;
you also encounter it as the dual of a convex function in variational analysis.

The idea behind the proof is that of \emph{importance sampling}:
change the probability distribution
(in a tractable way, using an \emph{exponential family})
to make the rare events more probable,
\[ \mu_\theta(dx) = \exp \left[ \theta x - \Gamma(\theta) \right] \mu(dx). \]
Indeed, large deviation theory can be used to prove theoretical convergence results for importance sampling.

Cramer's theorem can be generalized:
the \emph{G\"artner--Ellis theorem} relaxes the independence assumption;
\emph{Freidlin--Wentzell theory} estimates the probability that a diffusion
(a solution of a stochastic differential equation)
leaves some domain,
when the noise is small,
given that it remains in it when there is no noise at all
(so that the event is indeed rare).

More generally, \emph{large deviation principles} (LPD)
state (more formally) that
\[ P[ Z_\epsilon \in dx ] \sim_{\epsilon \to 0} e^{ - I(x) / \epsilon } dx \]
for some \emph{rate function} $I$.
In this context, \emph{Varadhan's theorem}
generalizes the \emph{Laplace method}
\[ \lim_{n\to\infty} \tfrac1n \ln \int_0^1 e^{ n \phi(x) } dx = \max_{ x \in [0,1] } \phi(x) \]
to
\[
  E \left[ e^{ \phi( Z_\epsilon / \epsilon ) } \right]
  \sim_{\epsilon \to 0}
  C \exp \left[
    \dfrac1\epsilon \sup_x \left[ \phi(x) - I(x) \right]
  \right].
\]

Applications include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{Cramer--Lundberg approximation} of the ruin probability of an insurer
  (initial reserve, regular premiums, iid claims arriving at the jump times of a Poisson process)
  when the reserve is large and its importance sampling extimator;
\item Generalization to insurance-finance: if there is a drift in the asset prices,
  the insurer should invest and follow a buy-and-hold strategy --
  replacing the minimum ruin probability criterion with a less conservative one
  would lead to more complex/dynamic optimal strategies.
\item Importance sampling to price very out-of-the-money options (change the drift to make exercise more probable);
\item value at risk VaR) estimation of a credit risk portfolio,
  in a 1-factor gaussian copula model,
  when the portfolio is large or the probability of default small.
\item VaR of a passive strategy in the long term,
  when it gets closer and closer to the index it is tracking.
\end{compactitem}

