\begin{book}
  \emph{Specialising simulator generators for high-performance Monte-Carlo methods}\\
  G. Keller et al.
\end{book}

A scientific application of the
\href{http://www.pragprog.com/}{pragmatic programmer}'s tip number 29,
``Write code that writes code'' --
this could also be applied to (MCMC simulations of) mixed models (WinBugs,
\href{http://www-fis.iarc.fr/~martyn/software/jags/}{Jags}).

This approach shifts part of the compiler design problem towards the end programmer;
it looks similar to lex/yacc (or flex/bison) and
the use of \emph{hardware description languages}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The need for open source software\\ in machine learning}\\
  S. Sonnenburg et al.\\
  Journal of machine learning (2007)
\end{book}

Open source tools are mature and suitable for large-scale real-world problems;
their wider use would result in greater reuseability and interoperability --
this is already the case in bioinformatics.

The article contains summary tables (definition, advantages, licences)
screaming to be reused in bullet-point/Powerpoint/executive summary presentations.

The authors trace free software back to Sir Isaac Newton:
\emph{If I have seen further, it is by standing on the shoulders of giants.}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Proceedings of the\\ Linux Audio Conference 2007}
\end{book}

A few articles about \emph{ambisonic} recording
and \emph{wave field synthesis} (WFS),
scripting languages (\texttt{PySndObj})
and the usual suspects (Pure Data, etc.).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=1005894}{\emph{High-dimensional modelling and simulation with asymetric normal mixtures}}\\
  A. Tsanakas and A. Smith (2007)
\end{book}

A \emph{gaussian mixture} is a random variable of the form
$X = \sqrt H Z$,
where $Z \sim N(0,1)$ and $H>0$
(for instance, $H$ could follow a discrete distribution
with two values $\sigma_1^2$ and $\sigma_2^2$).

In higher dimensions, this becomes $X = \sqrt H L X$,
where $\Sigma = LL'$ is the desired dispersion matrix
and $E[H] = 1$ ($H$ is still 1-dimensional).

This can be generalized by also randomizing the mean,
yielding \emph{asymmetric gaussian mixtures},
\begin{align*}
  & X = \gamma^{-1} (H-1) u + \sqrt H L Z \\
  & Z \sim N(0,\textbf{1}) \\
  & H > 0, \quad E[H] = 1, \quad \var H = \gamma^2 \\
  & u \in \RR^n, \quad u' \Sigma^{-1} u \leq 1 \\
  & \Sigma \text{ positive definite.}
\end{align*}

In high dimension, the dispersion matrix $\Sigma$
can be parametrized by a Kronecker product.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Markov chain Monte Carlo convergence diagnostocs: a comparative review}
  M.K. Cowles and B.P. Carlin\\
  J. Amer. Statist. Assoc. 91 (1996)
\end{book}

Presentation and comparison of 13 convergence diagnostics -- beware, all can fail.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Constructing free energy approximations and generalized belief propagation algorithms}\\
  J.S. Yedidia et al. (2004)
\end{book}

Surprisingly clear presentation of the belief propagation algorithm (BP) for factor graphs.

Instead of looking for the optimal probability distribution $P_{\text{opt}}$,
the free energy approximation looks for a simpler distribution $Q$
(say, one where all the variables are independent
(this could be seen as an ``average'' of one-variable modells),
or more generally a simply connected graphical model)
that minimizes the Kullback--Leibler distance
$D(P_{\text{opt}}||Q)$ --
the magic is that it can be computed without knowing $P_{\text{opt}}$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Variational approximations between mean field theory and the junction tree algorithm}\\
  W. Wiegerinck (2000)
\end{book}

Another article on the same subject.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Understanding belief propagation\\ and its generalizations}\\
  J.S. Yedidia et al. (2002)
\end{book}

Earlier article on generalized belief propagation.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A differential approach to inference\\ in bayesian networks}\\
  A. Darwiche
\end{book}

The author associates a polynomial (in several variables) to a bayesian network
so that operations in the network
(parameter estimation, marginal probability computations, etc.)
are evaluations of the polynomial or its derivatives.
The size of the polynomial is exponential,
but manipulations on the parsing tree of the polynomial
(which the author calls an ``arithmetic circuit'')
can make those evaluations can be amenable.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A logical approach to factoring belief networks}\\
  A. Darwiche
\end{book}

A more formal article on the subject.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to variational methods\\ for graphical models}\\
  M.I. Jordan et al.\\
  Machine Learning 37 (1999)
\end{book}

Variational methods come from the following remark:
a convex function $f$ is characterized by the affine functions above it,
\[ f(x) = \max \{\, \phi \ :\ \phi'x \geq f^*(\phi) \,\}. \]
To prove an assertion about $f$,
it might suffice to prove it for all or one of those affine functions --
in particular, this can lead to upper bounds of quantities involving~$f$.

This idea can be applied to the likelihood of a graphical model:
under mild assumptions (e.g., all the probabilities are from the exponential family),
a graphical model is characterized by the set of independent models
(graphical models with a discrete, edge-less graph -- these are called
\emph{mean-field} models) more (or less) likely than it.

In particular, a graphical model can be fit by finding an independent model
close to the optimal model -- this is different from finding the independent
model best fitting the data.

The fit can be improved by allowing more structure
on the approximate graphical models:
for instance, you can progressively complexify it,
towards the graphical model being studied,
until it becomes untractable.

% In my copy of the article, all the equations were unreadable: the mathematical symbols had been replaced by <= or (...

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Model-independent mean field theory\\ as a local method for approximate propagation\\ of information}\\
  M. Haft et al. (1997)
\end{book}

Another presentation of \emph{mean field theory} (MFT),
that suggests to average several mean field solutions
(this really sounds like bayesian model averaging (BMA):
for instance, you could approximate a grid by horizontal chains
and vertical chains and hope to get a reasonable result by averaging
those two simply connected models).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The bayesian structural EM algorithm}\\
  N. Friedman
\end{book}

The structure and parameters of a bayesian network
can be learned ``\` la Gibbs'',
with an EM (expectation-maximization) algorithm:
find the best structure for the current parameters,
find the best parameters for the current structure,
iterate until convergence.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Learning bayesian network models from incomplete data using importance sampling}\\
  C. Riggelsen and A. Feelders
\end{book}

\emph{Data augmentation} (DA) is a simulation-based EM (expectation minimization) algorithm.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic Graphical Models}\\
  M. Hauskrecht (2005)
\end{book}

Lecture notes, with a very useful list of references.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Probabilistic reasoning over time}\\
  in \emph{Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach}\\
  S. Russell and P. Norvig
\end{book}

A readable introduction to dynamic bayesian networks (DBN):
backward-forward message passing (for hidden Markov models (HMM)),
application to the Kalman filter and extended Kalman filter (EKF),
transient failure model, persistent failure model, particle filter,
with applications to speech recognition
(it also mentions $A^*$-search, with no details) and exercises.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Graphical models\\ and automatic speech recognition}\\
  J.A. Bilmes\\
  in \emph{Mathematical foundations of speech\\ and language processing} (2003)
\end{book}

Yet another introduction to graphical models,
this time targeting automatic speech recognition (ASR).

Among the examples:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Chain graphs (partly directed, partly undirected);
\item Decision trees (yes, this is how trees look like, when turned into a bayesian network)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=1.5]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node[observed] (A) at (0,0) {};
  \node[observed] (B) at (1,0) {};
  \node[observed] (C) at (2,0) {};
  \node[observed] (D) at (3,0) {};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (D);
  \draw[->] (B) to [out=-25, in=-155] (D);
  \draw[->] (A) to [out= 25, in= 155] (C);
  \draw[->] (A) to [out= 30, in= 150] (D);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\item A multivariate gaussian distribution is an undirected graphical model:
  the (blocks of) zeroes in the inverse of the variance matrix
  correspond to edges removed from the clique formed on the variables
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=circle,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick,inner sep=1pt]
  \node[observed] (X1) at (0,1) {$X_1$};
  \node[observed] (X2) at (1,1) {$X_2$};
  \node[observed] (X3) at (0,0) {$X_3$};
  \node[observed] (X4) at (1,0) {$X_4$};
  \draw (X3) -- (X1) -- (X2) -- (X3) -- (X4) -- (X2);
  \node[label=right:{$X_1 \perp\!\!\!\!\perp X_4 \,|\, X_2,\, X_3$}] at (1.5,.5) {};
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\item A multivariate gaussian distribution is a directed graphical model,
  the Choleski matrix yielding a ``tree'', its zero entries pruning it;
\item Principal component analysis (PCA):
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.5]
  \tikzstyle{parameter}=[shape=ellipse,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{mvariable}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node[mvariable]  (X)   at (0,0) {$X$};
  \node[mvariable]  (Y)   at (0,1) {$Y$};
  \node[parameter] (C)   at (135:1cm) {$C$};
  \node[parameter] (mu)  at (180:1cm) {$\mu$};
  \node[parameter,
        label=right:{$\qquad\qquad X = C Y + \mu + \epsilon$}]
                   (eps) at (45:1cm)  {$\epsilon$};
  \node[parameter] (OI)  at (0,2)  {$0,\textbf{1}$};
  \node[parameter] (D)   at (1.414214,1.414214) {$0,\sigma^2\textbf{1}$};
  \draw[->] (mu)  -- (X);
  \draw[->] (C)   -- (X);
  \draw[->] (Y)   -- (X);
  \draw[->] (eps) -- (X);
  \draw[->] (OI)  -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (D)   -- (eps);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\item Factor Analysis (FA):
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=1.5]
  \tikzstyle{parameter}=[shape=ellipse,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{mvariable}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node[mvariable]  (X)   at (0,0) {$X$};
  \node[mvariable]  (Y)   at (0,1) {$Y$};
  \node[parameter] (C)   at (135:1cm) {$C$};
  \node[parameter] (mu)  at (180:1cm) {$\mu$};
  \node[parameter,
        label=right:{$\qquad\qquad X = C Y + \mu + \epsilon$}]
                   (eps) at (45:1cm)  {$\epsilon$};
  \node[parameter] (OI)  at (0,2)  {$0,\textbf{1}$};
  \node[parameter] (D)   at (1.414214,1.414214) {$0,\Delta$};
  \draw[->] (mu)  -- (X);
  \draw[->] (C)   -- (X);
  \draw[->] (Y)   -- (X);
  \draw[->] (eps) -- (X);
  \draw[->] (OI)  -- (Y);
  \draw[->] (D)   -- (eps);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
\end{compactitem}

This chapter also mentions
\href{http://ssli.ee.washington.edu/~bilmes/gmtk/}{GMTK} (graphical model toolkit),
``open source'' but with no source code available (not under active development).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to Monte Carlo methods}\\
  D.J.C. MacKay
\end{book}

A presentation of \emph{importance sampling}, \emph{rejection sampling},
\emph{Gibbs sampling} and the \emph{Metropolis algorithm},
with some of their improvements:
\emph{hybrid Monte Carlo} (augment the state space with momentum variables),
\emph{overrelaxation} (in Gibbs sampling, do not sample from the conditional distribution, but bias it away from the current value),
\emph{simulated annealing}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{An introduction to MCMC\\ for machine learning}\\
  C. Andrieu et al.\\
  Machine Learning (2003)
\end{book}

Concise presentation of MCMC (Markov Chain Monte Carlo), terser than Liu's book,
with insightful pictures to represent \emph{rejection sampling}
\iffalse
  x <- round(qnorm(ppoints(200)),3)
  y <-
    .2  * dnorm(x, mean=-.7, sd=.5)  +
    .1  * dnorm(x, mean=.5,  sd=.15) +
    .05 * dnorm(x)
  {
    cat(paste( "    (", x, ",", round(dnorm(x),3)-.008, ")", sep="", collapse=" --\n" ))
    cat("\n")
  }
  {
    cat(paste( "    (", x, ",", round(y,3), ")", sep="", collapse=" --\n" ))
    cat("\n")
  }
\fi
$$
