\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Graphical models and their role in databases}\\
  A. Deshpande \\
  VLDB 2007 Tutorial
\end{book}

A \emph{graphical model} is a decomposition of a joint distribution
on many (typically millions) variables
as a product of distributions on fewer (two or three) variables.

In a \emph{directed graphical model} or \emph{bayesian network},
the decomposition is described by a directed acyclic graph (DAG),
and the factors, called \emph{potentials}, are conditional probabilities:
\[ p(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \prod _x p \bigl( x \,|\, \text{parents}(x) \bigr). \]

In an \emph{undirected graphical model} or \emph{Markov random field} (MRF),
the decomposition is described by the \emph{cliques} (complete subgraphs)
of a graph, and the potentials need not be probabilities
(hence the need for the normalization factor $Z$):
\[ p(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \dfrac1Z \prod_{ C \text{\ Clique} } \psi_C( x_C ). \]

Those directed or undirected graphs model
\emph{conditional independence} relations between sets of variables.

One can then compute marginal probabilities
or the most likely labels of the remaining variables:
\begin{align*}
  p( x_B ) &= \int p( x_A, x_B ) \, d P_B \\
  x_B | x_A &= \argmax_{ x_B} p(x_A, x_B).
\end{align*}

Inference on a chain is easy:
\begin{footnotesize}
\begin{align*}
  p(x_5)
  &= \sum_{ x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4 } p(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4) \\
  %&= \sum_{ x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4 } p(x_1) p(x_2 | x_1) p( x_3 | x_1, x_2 ) p( x_4 | x_1, x_2, x_3 ) p( x_5 | x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4 ) \\
  &= \sum_{ x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4 } p(x_1) p(x_2 | x_1) p( x_3 | x_2 ) p( x_4 | x_3 ) p( x_5 | x_4 ) \\
  &= \sum_{ x_1 } p(x_1) \sum_{ x_2 } p(x_2 | x_1) \sum_{ x_3} p( x_3 | x_2 ) \sum_{x_4} p( x_4 | x_3 ) p( x_5 | x_4 ) \\
  &= \sum_{ x_1 } p(x_1)
     \underbrace{ \sum_{ x_2 } p(x_2 | x_1)
                  \underbrace{ \sum_{ x_3} p( x_3 | x_2 )
                               \underbrace{ \sum_{x_4} p( x_4 | x_3 ) p( x_5 | x_4 ) }_{ B_3(x_3) }
                             }_{ B_2( x_2 ) }
                }_{ B_1( x_1 ) } \\
\end{align*}
\end{footnotesize}

This can be generalized to simply-connected graphs,
under the name \emph{variable elemination algorithm}:
e.g., for a hidden markov model (HMM), this is the \emph{Viterbi algorithm}.

For more a general directed acyclic graph, just consider its \emph{junction tree}
(some articles claim this is an approximate algorithm,
others (this one) that it is exact but NP-hard):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Form the \emph{moral graph}: join the parents of each node and forget the orientation;
\item Triangulate the graph;
\item The junction tree has vertices the maximal cliques of the triangulated (or \emph{chordal}) graph
  and edges their separators.
\end{compactitem}
But this is NP-hard, because the cliques can be very large:
you can try with approximate junction trees,
whose nodes are arbitrary clusters in the triangulated graph
and use \emph{loopy belief propagation} (not detailed) if loops arise.
When all else fails, MCMC (Gibbs) sampling still works.

Depending on the application, the structure of the graph can be imposed
or learnt (this is NP-hard); similarly, the parameters can be imposed
(from domain knowledge) or learnt.

A time dimension can be added: for instance,
the hidden variable model 
\[
  \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.7]
    \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=circle,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
    \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
    \node (A) at (0,0) [observed] {};
    \node (B) at (0,1) [hidden] {};
    \node (C) at (0,2) [hidden] {};
    \draw[->] (B) -- (A);
    \draw[->] (C) -- (B);
  \end{tikzpicture}
\]
becomes a Kalman filter
\[
  \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=3,>=stealth]
    \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=ellipse,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
    \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
    \node (V1) at (0,2) [hidden] {speed$_{t-1}$};
    \node (V2) at (1,2) [hidden] {speed$_t$};
    \node (X1) at (0,1) [hidden] {position$_{t-1}$};
    \node (X2) at (1,1) [hidden] {position$_{t}$};
    \node (Y1) at (0,0) [observed] {observed$_{t-1}$};
    \node (Y2) at (1,0) [observed] {observed$_{t}$};
    \draw[->] (V1) -- (V2);
    \draw[->] (X1) -- (X2);
    \draw[->] (V1) -- (X1);
    \draw[->] (V2) -- (X2);
    \draw[->] (X1) -- (Y1);
    \draw[->] (X2) -- (Y2);
    \draw[->] (-.7,2) -- (V1);
    \draw[->] (-.7,1) -- (X1);
    \draw[->] (-.7,0) -- (Y1);
    \draw[->] (V2) -- (1.7,2);
    \draw[->] (X2) -- (1.7,1);
    \draw[->] (Y2) -- (1.7,0);
  \end{tikzpicture}
\]
or more general \emph{dynamic bayesian networks} (DBN) --
this example is only partially oriented (this is called a \emph{chain graph})
\[
  \begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=.5,>=stealth]
    \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=circle,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
    \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
    \node (A)   at (0,3) [hidden]   {};
    \node (B)   at (1,5) [hidden]   {};
    \node (C)   at (2,4) [hidden]   {};
    \node (A')  at (0,0) [observed] {};
    \node (B')  at (1,2) [observed] {};
    \node (C')  at (2,1) [observed] {};
    \node (A2)  at (3,3) [hidden]   {};
    \node (B2)  at (4,5) [hidden]   {};
    \node (C2)  at (5,4) [hidden]   {};
    \node (A2') at (3,0) [observed] {};
    \node (B2') at (4,2) [observed] {};
    \node (C2') at (5,1) [observed] {};
    \draw (A)  -- (B)  -- (C)  -- (A);
    \draw (A2) -- (B2) -- (C2) -- (A2);
    \draw[->] (A)  -- (A');
    \draw[->] (B)  -- (B');
    \draw[->] (C)  -- (C');
    \draw[->] (A2) -- (A2');
    \draw[->] (B2) -- (B2');
    \draw[->] (C2) -- (C2');
    \draw[->] (A) -- (A2);
    \draw[->] (B) -- (B2);
    \draw[->] (C) -- (C2);
    \draw[->] (-.5,3) -- (A);
    \draw[->] ( .5,5) -- (B);
    \draw[->] (1.5,4) -- (C);
    \draw[->] (A2) -- (3.5,3);
    \draw[->] (B2) -- (4.5,5);
    \draw[->] (C2) -- (5.5,4);
  \end{tikzpicture}
\]

General applications include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Error correcting codes: \emph{turbo codes};
\item Medical diagnostic: \href{http://www.openclinical.org/aisp_qmr.html}{QMR} (Quick medical reference);
\item Vision (Potts model or, for binary variables, Ising model).
\[
  \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.5]
    \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=circle,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
    \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
    \node (A1) at (0,0) [observed] {};
    \node (A2) at (1,0) [observed] {};
    \node (A3) at (2,0) [observed] {};
    \node (B1) at (0,1) [observed] {};
    \node (B2) at (1,1) [observed] {};
    \node (B3) at (2,1) [observed] {};
    \node (C1) at (0,2) [observed] {};
    \node (C2) at (1,2) [observed] {};
    \node (C3) at (2,2) [observed] {};
    \draw (A1) -- (A2) -- (A3);
    \draw (B1) -- (B2) -- (B3);
    \draw (C1) -- (C2) -- (C3);
    \draw (A1) -- (B1) -- (C1);
    \draw (A2) -- (B2) -- (C2);
    \draw (A3) -- (B3) -- (C3);
  \end{tikzpicture}
\]
\end{compactitem}

Database-specific applications include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Approximate aggregate over some of the many columns of a table
  (\ie, marginal of the joint distribution of the columns);
\item Estimate intermediate result sizes (e.g., that of
  \texttt{income$>$90\,000 and homeowner=yes})
  for query plan optimization;
\item Join uniformity estimation
  (how likely is a tuple from one relation to join with tuples from another);
\item \emph{Probabilistic relational models} (PRM,
  sometimes called \emph{statistical relational models}),
  to extend or replace the relational model to
  represent uncertainties in the data;
\item Record matching;
\item Deduplication (with a logistic regression
  or a support vector machine (SVM), this can end up
  as a simple linear combination of the differences
  of the features of the records):
  duplication can appear in several columns and
  duplicates found in one can help find more in another (joint deduplication);
  deduplication can look for similarities
  in attributes (cells in a table) or records (rows).
\end{compactitem}

Information extraction (e.g., named entity recognition (NER)) applications include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Protein and gene names from publications;
\item Room attributes from hotel websites;
\item Addresses and qualifications from resumes for human ressources databases;
\item Job postings in newsgroups;
\item Emails in help centers;
\item News monitoring.
\end{compactitem}

\emph{Sensor networks} present more challenges: the data are not only large, incomplete and imprecise,
they are streaming (calling for dynamic bayesian networks),
spacial (calling for added space dimensions, as we added time)
and distributed -- the part of the network describing the spacial distribution of the sensors
can also describe the location of the databases and help devise distributed,
\emph{in-network} algorithms.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Representing and querying correlated tuples\\ in probabilistic databases}\\
  P. Sen and A. Deshpande
\end{book}

Graphical models can be used to estimate tuple dependencies in a database,
for query plan optimization.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian networks without tears}\\
  E. Charniak\\
  AI Magazine (1991)
\end{book}

An early introductory article on bayesian networks,
that allow to model probability distributions
on many discrete variables
without requiring ``absurdly many numbers'':
construct a directed acyclic graph (DAG)
whose vertices are the variables and such that
two variables $x$ amd $y$ are conditionnally independent
given a set of variables $Z$
if and only if $x$ and $y$ are $d$-separated given $Z$, \ie,
there is no $d$-connecting path between them -- that is a path
whose every interior node is either
(i) linear
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,fill=black,inner sep=1pt]
  \node (A) at (0,0) [observed] {};
  \node (B) at (1,0) [observed,fill=red!50!white] {};
  \node (C) at (2,0) [observed] {};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
or diverging
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.09]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,fill=black,inner sep=1pt]
  \node (A) at (0,0)  [observed,fill=red!50!white] {};
  \node (B) at (1,1)  [observed] {};
  \node (C) at (1,-1) [observed] {};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (B);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
and not in $Z$,
or (ii) converging
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5,yscale=.09]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,fill=black,inner sep=1pt]
  \node (A) at (0,1)  [observed] {};
  \node (B) at (0,-1) [observed] {};
  \node (C) at (1,0)  [observed,fill=red!50!white] {};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (C);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
and in $Z$ or with a descendant in $Z$
(this notion can also be explained with the \emph{ten rules of Bayes ball}.)

The computation of every node's belief is NP-hard in general --
but linear for \emph{polytrees} (simply-connected graphs).

Applications include:
medical diagnostic (often with some decision theory),
map learning,
story understanding.

