\end{book}

After giving a few examples of graphical models, such as factor analysis,
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=circle,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node (x) at (0,1) [hidden,label=left:{(hidden)},label=right:{$N(0,I)$}] {$x$};
  \node (y) at (0,0) [observed,label=left:{(observed)},label=right:{$N(\mu+Wx,\Sigma)$}] {$y$};
  \draw[->] (x) -- (y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
mixture of gaussians:
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=circle,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=circle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node (i) at (0,1) [hidden,label=right:{discrete}] {$i$};
  \node (x) at (0,0) [observed,label=right:{$N(\mu_i,\Sigma_i)$}] {$x$};
  \draw[->] (i) -- (x);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
linear dynamic models (Kalman filters)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2]
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=ellipse,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node (X1) at (0,1) [hidden]   {$x_{i-1}$};
  \node (X2) at (1,1) [hidden,label=above right:{$N(Ax_{i-1}+b,V)$}]   {$x_i$};
  \node (Y1) at (0,0) [observed] {$y_{i-1}$};
  \node (Y2) at (1,0) [observed,label=below right:{$N(Cx_i+d,W)$}] {$y_i$};
  \draw[->] (X1) -- (X2);
  \draw[->] (-.5,1) -- (X1);
  \draw[->] (X2) -- (1.5,1);
  \draw[->] (X1) -- (Y1);
  \draw[->] (X2) -- (Y2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
or Pearl's classical artificial intelligence example
(whether the sprinkler is switched on depends on whether it is cloudy;
when I come back the grass is wet: does it means it rained?)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=2]
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=ellipse,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node (C) at (1,2) [observed] {cloudy};
  \node (S) at (0,1) [hidden] {sprinkler};
  \node (R) at (2,1) [hidden] {rain};
  \node (G) at (1,0) [observed] {wet grass};
  \draw[->] (C) -- (S);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (R);
  \draw[->] (S) -- (G);
  \draw[->] (R) -- (G);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
the article mentions, without any detail,
inference algorithms,
exact (variable elimination, with memoization (dynamic programming) if you need several marginals)
or approximate (sampling (Monte Carlo) methods,
variational methods (mean field approximation),
loopy belief propagation (Pearl's algorithm, e.g., used to decode \emph{turbo codes})).

Learning a bayesian network can be more or less complicated,
depending on whether you know the graph structure
(if not, you can greedily add edges)
and on the presence of hidden variables --
adding a hidden variable can simplify a graph:
\[
\raisebox{-.5\height+.5ex}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5]
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=ellipse,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node (A) at (0,1) [observed] {};
  \node (B) at (1,1) [observed] {};
  \node (C) at (2,1) [observed] {};
  \node (D) at (0,0) [observed] {};
  \node (E) at (1,0) [observed] {};
  \node (F) at (2,0) [observed] {};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (D);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (E);
  \draw[->] (A) -- (F);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (D);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (E);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (F);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (D);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (E);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (F);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}
\longmapsto
\raisebox{-.5\height+.5ex}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,xscale=.5]
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=ellipse,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=ellipse,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node (A) at (0,1) [observed] {};
  \node (B) at (1,1) [observed] {};
  \node (C) at (2,1) [observed] {};
  \node (D) at (0,0) [observed] {};
  \node (E) at (1,0) [observed] {};
  \node (F) at (2,0) [observed] {};
  \node (I) at (1,.5) [hidden] {};
  \draw[->] (A) -- (I);
  \draw[->] (B) -- (I);
  \draw[->] (C) -- (I);
  \draw[->] (I) -- (D);
  \draw[->] (I) -- (E);
  \draw[->] (I) -- (F);
