\]

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Graphical models}\\
  M.I. Jordan
\end{book}

\emph{Hierarchical bayesian models} (as in BUGS) are a special case of graphical models.

%TODO: Example...

The decomposition of the joint probability distribution
\[ p(x_1,\cdots,x_n) = \dfrac1Z \prod_C f_C(x_C) \]
can also be represented with the associated \emph{factor graph}:
a bipartite graph, whose round edges are the variables and square edges the potentials $f_C$.

Marginal distributions can be computed (exactly) with the variable elimination algorithm:
just write the humongous sum and shift the summation signs as far right as possible;
this amounts to chosing an \emph{elimination order} on the variables --
chosing the best one, that will lead the the fewest operations, is NP-hard.
If you need several marginals, just add memoization to the elimination algorithms --
this is the \emph{sum-product algorithm}.

One can replace sums by maximums
(simply because maximum and product still for a commutative semi-ring)
to get the most probable value of a set of variables.

Sampling algorithms can be used for approximate computations:
for Gibbs sampling, you just have to condition on the \emph{Markov blanket}
of a node (its neighbours in the unoriented case;
its parents, children and co-parents in the oriented case).

\emph{Variational inference} proceeds as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Formulate the problem as an optimization problem;
\item ``Perturb'' it, to make it simpler,
  typically by adding or removing constraints
  (inner or outer approximation)
\item Solve the new problem.
\end{compactitem}

Applications include:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Phylogenetic trees:
  graphical models can account for dependence between sites
  and lateral gene transfer
  (the graph structure is unknown:
  we can relax the requirement that it be a tree);
\item Multi-locus linkage analysis in pedigrees
  (large pedigrees often contain many loops);
\item Error correcting codes, such as \emph{low-density parity check} (LDPC) codes,
  whose factor graphs look like (the codes actually used are selected at random)
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,yscale=.7]
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=circle,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=rectangle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \node (f1) at (0,2)   [observed] {};
  \node (f2) at (1,2)   [observed] {};
  \node (f3) at (2,2)   [observed] {};
  \node (f4) at (3,2)   [observed] {};
  \node (X1) at (0,1)   [hidden]   {};
  \node (X2) at (1,1)   [hidden]   {};
  \node (X3) at (2,1)   [hidden]   {};
  \node (X4) at (3,1)   [hidden]   {};
  \node (fa) at (.5,0)  [observed] {};
  \node (fb) at (1.5,0) [observed] {};
  \node (fc) at (2.5,0) [observed] {};
  \node (f0) at (3.1,2) [label=right:{transmitted message}] {};
  \node (X0) at (3.1,1) [label=right:{message}] {};
  \node (fz) at (3.1,0) [label=right:{parity bits}] {};
  \draw (f1) -- (X1);
  \draw (f2) -- (X2);
  \draw (f3) -- (X3);
  \draw (f4) -- (X4);
  \draw (X1) -- (fa);
  \draw (X1) -- (fb);
  \draw (X2) -- (fa);
  \draw (X2) -- (fc);
  \draw (X3) -- (fa);
  \draw (X3) -- (fc);
  \draw (X4) -- (fb);
  \draw (X4) -- (fc);
\end{tikzpicture}%
\]
\item Computational linguistics, with a host of generalizations of hidden Markov models (HMM):
higher order Markov chains,
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \tikzstyle{hidden}=[shape=circle,draw=red!50,fill=red!20,thick]
  \tikzstyle{observed}=[shape=rectangle,draw=blue!50,fill=blue!20,thick]
  \clip (1.5,-.45) rectangle (5.5,.45);
  \node (X0) at (0,0) [observed] {};
  \node (X1) at (1,0) [observed] {};
  \node (X2) at (2,0) [observed] {};
  \node (X3) at (3,0) [observed] {};
  \node (X4) at (4,0) [observed] {};
  \node (X5) at (5,0) [observed] {};
  \node (X6) at (6,0) [observed] {};
  \node (X7) at (7,0) [observed] {};
