\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Recursive conditioning}\\
  A. Darwiche
\end{book}

\emph{Cutset conditioning} is an inference algorithm for graphical models
that replaces the problem by several simpler ones,
obtained by conditioning on (fixing the values of) a set of nodes (a cutset):
this can discard loops.

\emph{Recursive conditioning} applies the same idea
but really cuts the graph in two at each step
(you still have to choose the elimination order on the nodes).
You can tune the amount of memoization used by the algorithm
to control the tradeoff between its space and time complexity --
it is an \emph{any-time, any-space} algorithm.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bounded RD}\\
  S. Monti and G.F. Cooper
\end{book}

Another algorithm derived from \emph{recursive decomposition}
(cutset conditioning)
that progressively refines interval bounds on marginal probabilities. 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Inducing features of random fields}\\
  S. Della Pietra et al. (1995)
\end{book}

The authors build \emph{stepwise maxent models} as follows.
Start with a graph, whose vertices are the variables to be included
and whose edges represent time of spacial relations --
or just take a complete graph.
First add atomic features and then features of the form
$$ \text{atomic feature} \times \text{active feature} $$
where the support of the candidate feature is connected in the underlying graph.
To estimate the gain of a candidate feature, you can freeze the weights
of the active ones: this allows the quick evaluation of thousands of features.

The article also presents the \emph{improved iterative scaling} (IIS) algorithm.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of algorithms for maximum entropy parameter estimation}\\
  R. Malouf
\end{book}

Presentation of the \emph{improved iterative scaling} (IIS) algorithm.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive smoothing of digital images:\\ the R package \texttt{adimpro}}\\
  J. Polzehl and K. Tabelow\\
  Journal of statistical software (2007)
\end{book}

Image smoothing can be adaptive and edge-preserving:
the intensity of the smoothing  varies locally,
with more smoothing in ``homogeneous'' (for some model) regions.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Interactive multivariate data analysis in R\\ with the \texttt{ade4} and \texttt{ade4TkGUI} packages}\\
  J. Thioulouse and S. Dray\\
  Journal of statistical software (2007)
\end{book}

Just a GUI for the \texttt{ade4} package,
that also provides factor analysis of a mixture of quantitative and qualitative variables
(\texttt{dudi.hillsmith} and \texttt{dudi.mix} functions).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Numerical computing and graphics\\ for the power method transformation\\ using Mathematica}\\
  T.C. Headrick et al.\\
  Journal of statistical software (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{power method} constructs a non-gaussian random variable $Y$ from a gaussian one $Z$,
$$ Y = \sum_{k=0}^n c_k Z^k.$$
It is actually used in the other direction,
to transform non-gaussian data into gaussian-looking data;
the coefficients are chosen so as to match the cumulants of the data --
this is a method of moments estimator.

This can be generalized to multivariate data, often as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Each $Y_i$ is a polynomial function of a single gaussian variable $Z_i$,
  chosen to have the right cumulants;
\item The $Z_i$ are jointly gaussian, with a correlation matrix
  chosen to produce the second cross-moments of $Y$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient training of conditional random fields}\\
  H. Wallach (2002)
\end{book}

\emph{Graphical models} are a way of describing a joint distribution
as a product of functions of fewer variables.
For instance, with independent variables
$$ p(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \prod_i p( x_i ); $$
or with a Markov chain
\begin{align*}
  p(x_1,\dots,x_n)
  = {}&p(x_n | x_{n-1}, \dots, x_1 ) \times \\
  & p(x_{n-1} | x_{n-2}, \dots, x_1 ) \times \\
  & \cdots \\
  & p(x_2|x_1) p(x_1) \\
  = {}& p(x_n | x_{n-1}) p(x_{n-1} | x_{n-2}) \cdots \\
  &\cdots p(x_2|x_1) p(x_1) \\
  = {}& p(x_1) \prod_{i>1} p(x_i|x_{i-1}).
\end{align*}
More generally, an oriented graph (with no cycles),
defines an \emph{oriented graphical model} (or \emph{bayesian network})
with the decomposition
$$ p(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \prod_i p( x_i | x_{ \textrm{parents}(i) } ). $$

\begin{tabular}{c@{\qquad\quad}c}
