$$

Graphical models are usually estimated
by \emph{iterative scaling} (see somewhere below)
or by heuristic algorithms such as \emph{belief propagation}:
form the \emph{junction tree} of the graph, \ie, the graph
whose vertices are the maximum cliques
and whose edges are their separators
(it is a tree iff the graph is chordal: triangulate it first);
and propagate beliefs as for a Markov chain.

The article also contains a clear
(and correct -- many people still think it is a data imputation algorithm)
explanation of the \emph{expectation-maximization} (EM) algorithm,
to estimate a model with hidden or missing values:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item In the E-step, estimate the distribution of
  $\text{hidden} | \text{observed}$;
  if the hidden variables are discrete, you compute their frequency table;
  if the hidden variables are jointly gaussian, you compute their mean and variance;
  often, there is often no actual computation at this step,
  the distribution is just a formula that will be used in the next step;
\item In the M-step, consider the joint distribution
  $$ \text{observed}, \text{hidden} | \text{parameter}, $$
  integrate out the hidden variables using the distribution from the E-step,
  and maximize the corresponding expected log-likelihood;
  you may want to add a prior on the parameters to avoid overfitting;
\item Repeat ad libidum.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A simple introduction to maximal entropy models for natural language processing}\\
  A. Ratnaparkhi (1997)
\end{book}

Given a pair of discrete random variables $ z = (x, y) $ and a data sample,
we want to estimate the probability distribution $P(z)$ --
but the random variables $x$ and $y$ have so many possible values
($y$ can be the part-of-speech (pos) of a word and $x$ the word and its context)
that even with a large dataset, not all $(x,y)$ combinations are observed.

The maximum entropy (maxent) estimator is the probability distribution with
the largest entropy among those ``compatible'' with the data.
One selects \emph{features} $z \mapsto f_i(z)$ (for instance,
boolean indicator that the word has a given prefix or is preceded by a given word)
and ask that their expected value equal their observed mean.
In other words, a maximum entropy estimator is
an underdetermined generalized method of moments (GMM) estimator;
it is the solution of a constrained optimization problem.

It can be shown that that estimator is
$$
\left\{ \argmax_{ p \in P } H(p) \right\} =
\left\{ \argmax_{ p \in Q } L(p) \right\} =
P \inter Q
$$
where
\begin{align*}
  P &= \{\, p \ :\ \forall i \ E_p f_i = E_{\tilde p} f_i \, \} \\
  p &: \textrm{ model probabilities} \\
  \tilde p &: \textrm{ observed probabilities} \\
  Q &= \left\{\, p \ :\ p(z) \propto \prod \alpha_i ^ { f_i(z) }, \ \alpha_i > 0 \, \right\} \\
  H(p) &= \sum p(z) \log p(z) \qquad \textrm{ entropy} \\
  L(p) &= \sum \tilde p(z) \log \tilde p(z) \qquad \textrm{ log-likelihood},
\end{align*}
\ie, the maximum entropy estimator is also the maximum likelihood estimator
for probability distributions of the form
$ p(z) \propto \prod \alpha_i ^ { f_i(z) } $;
these are sometimes called \emph{log-linear models},
\emph{Gibbs models}, \emph{exponential models},
\emph{multinomial logit models}.

This duality between maximum entropy and maximum likelihood leads to the
\emph{generalized iterative scaling} (GIS) algorithm to find the $\alpha_i$.
It assumes that
\begin{align*}
  \forall z                 &\quad \sum_i f_i(z) = C \\
  \forall z \quad \exists i &\quad f_i(z) = 1 \\
  \forall i \quad \forall z &\quad f_i(z) \geq 0
\end{align*}
and proceeds as follows
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_i^{(0)}   &= 1 \\
  \alpha_i^{(n+1)} &= \alpha_i^{(n)} \left( \dfrac{ \tilde E f_i }{ E^{(n)} f_i } \right) ^ { 1 / c } \\
  E^{(n)} f_i      &= \sum_x p^{(n)} (z) f_i(x) \\
  \tilde E f_i     &= \sum_x \tilde p(z) f_i(z)
                    = \dfrac{ 1 }{ \va{ \textrm{Sample} } } \sum_{ x \in \textrm{Sample} } f_i(z).
\end{align*}
While $\tilde E$ is easy to compute (it is just a sum over the sample),
$E^{(n)}$ usually involves an exponentially large number of terms;
if $z = (x,y)$, it can be approximated as a sum over the sample
$\{ (x_1,y_1), \dots, (x_N,y_N) \}$:
$$
  E^{(n)} f_i \approx
  \sum_j \tilde p ( x_j ) \sum_y p^{(n)} ( y | x_j ) f_i( y, x_j ).
$$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Reduction of maximum entropy models\\ to hidden Markov models}\\
  J. Goodman
\end{book}

Maximum entropy models can be reformulated as hidden Markov models (HMM):
just turn the formula in the GIS (generalized iterative scaling) algorithm into a graph
(the HMM depends on the sample, though).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A gaussian prior\\ for smoothing maximum entropy models}\\
  S.F. Chen and R. Rosenfeld (1999)
\end{book}

To avoid overfitting a maximum entropy model, one can:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Add more constraints (it is already a constrained optimization);
  but even reasonable constraints such as forbidding zero probabilities
  can make the problem unfeasible;
\item Shrink the $n$-gram model towards the $(n-1)$-gram model,
  \ie, avoid zero probabilities in $p(w_{n+1}|x_n\cdots w_1)$
  by shrinking it towards $p(w_{n+1}|x_n\cdots w_2)$;
\item Replace the constrained optimization
  $$ \textrm{Minimize }\  D(q \, || \, p_{\textrm{unif}}) \ \textrm{ such that }\  U(q) = 0 $$
  by a penalized entropy maximization
  $$ \textrm{Minimize }\  D(q \, || \, p_{\textrm{unif}}) + \tau U(q) $$
  where $U(q)$ is often a weighted average of the squared constraint breaches
  $$ U(q) = \sum_i \dfrac1{\sigma_i^2} \left( \sum_x q(x) f_i(x) - \tilde p(x) f_i(x) \right)^2; $$
\item Replace the constraints $E f_i = \tilde E f_i$ by fat constraints
  $ \va{ E f_i - \tilde E f_i } < \epsilon $;
  this is equivalent to an entropy penalized by a step function;
\item Apply the smoothing to the equivalent maximum likelihood problem: this can be interpreted as a gaussian prior.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximum entropy estimation in economic models with linear inequality restrictions}\\
  R.C. Campbell and R.C. Hill
\end{book}

You can define a maximum entropy estimator for linear regression, \ie, the estimation of $\beta$ in $y = x \beta + \epsilon$,
as follows: impose $\beta_i \in [ \beta_i ^{\textrm{min}}, \beta_i ^{\textrm{max}} ]$, and write it as
$\beta_i = p_{1i} \beta_i^{\textrm{min}} + p_{2i} \beta_i^{\textrm{max}}$ where $p_{1i} + p_{2i} = 1$; $p$ can be interpreted as a probability;
proceed similarly for the error terms (some suggest $\epsilon_j \in [ - 3\hat\sigma, 3\hat\sigma ]$, where $\hat\sigma$ comes
from some other estimator); solve the optimization problem
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Maximize}\ \  & \sum p \ln p \\
  \textrm{such that}\ \ & y = x \beta + \epsilon \\
                        & p_1 + p_2 = 1.
\end{align*}

This can also be used for the general linear model (GLM).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maxent models,\\ conditional estimation and optimization}\\
  D. Klein and C. Manning\\
  HLT-NAACL, ACL (2003)
\end{book}

\emph{Generative models} (or joint models,
such as naive Bayes classifiers, hidden Markov models (HMM),
probabilistic context-free grammars, $n$-gram models)
predict joint probabilities $p(y,x)$,
while \emph{discriminative models} (or conditional models,
such as logictic regression or support vector machines (SVM) --
logistic regression is actually the conditional analogue of
the naive Bayes classifier)
only predict conditional probabilities $p(y|x)$.
Since the latter do not have to predict the marginal distribution $p(x)$,
they usually perform better.

An \emph{exponential} (or log-linear, or maxent) model is a model of the form
$$ p_\lambda ( y | x ) \propto \exp \sum_i \lambda_i f_i(x,y) $$
where the $f_i$ are called \emph{features}
(often binary variables such as
``the word is capitalized'',
``the previous word is a verb'',
``the word is `car'{}'',
``the word ends in `-ic'{}'',
``the word starts with `re-'{}'',
``the word is in a dictionary of place names'',
etc. -- often, there are hundreds of thousands of them,
but only a handful are non-zero).

Those models tend to overfit the data and need to be smoothed, e.g., with a prior
(adding virtual data or dropping features with low counts are bad ideas --
actually, including features that are always zero in the training set
makes wrong answers less likely;
along the same idea, redundant features, such as 2-grams and 1-grams,
are an insurance against the lack of data).

This presentation also clearly presents general-purpose optimization algorithms:
line search,
gradient ascent (a line search in the direction of the gradient;
the search path is then a succession of orthogonal segments),
\emph{conjugate gradient} (modify the gradient towards the previous search direction
to avoid those right angles),
Lagrange multipliers for constrained optimization.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bivariate option pricing\\ using dynamic copula models}\\
  R.W.J. van den Goorbergh et al.\\
  Insurance mathematics and economics (2005)
\end{book}

\emph{Rainbow options}, \ie, options on several underlyings
(call-on-max, put-on-min, call-on-min, put-on-max)
can be priced by modelling the (two) underlyings as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Each underlying follows a GARCH process with gaussian innovations;
\item The dependence between the innovations is described by a copula,
  from a family of copulas parametrized by Kendall's $\tau$, depending on time:
  $$ \tau_t = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \log \max( \sigma_{1,t}^2, \sigma_{2,t}^2 ). $$
\end{compactitem}

Wouldn't it be better to model dependence using \emph{cointegration}
instead of copulas?

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{One size fits all\\
  Part 1: An idea whose time\\ has come and gone\\
  Part 2: Benchmarking results}\\
  M. Stonebraker et al. (2005, 2007)
\end{book}

\href{http://www.sai.msu.su/~megera/postgres/history/rel-db-hist.png}{Traditional database management systems (DBMS)}
try to provide a single solution to all your data problems; but now that some of those problems have received more attention, specialized systems outperform them by one or two orders of magnitude:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Traditional DBMS cater to \emph{datawarehousing} with bitmap indices (instead of B-trees), materialized views and optimizer tactic for
\href{http://www.amazon.co.uk/Data-Warehouse-Toolkit-Complete-Dimensional/dp/0471200247}{star schema} queries are outperformed by \emph{column stores}
(Vertical, MonetDB), that do not retrieve whole rows, allow for compression and better indexing or sorting; and remember that the TPC-H datawarehouse benchmark is biased to overlook problems of generalist DBMS...
\item Traditional DBMS tackle \emph{stream processing} (monitoring traffic congestion and suggesting alternative routes; tracking military vehicles and personel; financial feeds) in a 2-stage process (update the database, then use the data); furthermore log-based recovery and synchronization needs do not fit well in a real-time setup; Stream engines (Streambase, Coral8, Apama) do not have that legacy;
\item Text search (Google with GFS, BigTable and \href{http://lambda-the-ultimate.org/node/2091}{MapReduce});
\item Scientific databases require specialized storage (dense or sparse matrices or higher dimensional arrays, compression (MPEG, etc.)) and optimized operations on them (matrix multiplication, pivot, interpolation, concatenation, approximate search for a small subarray); some of the authors are developing such a system, called ASAP.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The end of an architectural era:\\ It's time for a complete rewrite}\\
  M. Stonebraker et al\\
  VLDB 2007
\end{book}

Traditional DBMS are also beaten in the OLTP arena (they authors' \emph{H-store} prototype wins the TPC-C benchmark by almost two orders of magnitude):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Log-based recovery goes against high availability (HA);
\item Dynamic locking is outperformed by optimistic transactions (run the transaction as if there were no locking problems, unroll if and when a problem occurs -- this is called transactional memory in multi-threaded programming languages), especially with short-lived OLTP transactions;
\item Traditional DBMS were designed at a time when data could not fit in memory: \emph{main memory} databases can outperform them;
\item The multithreading overhead is not needed, since OLTP transactions are extremely short (larger transactions should be sent to a different, OLAP system);
\item Traditional DBMS are optimized for a shared memory or shared disk environment, not shared-nothing grids (with peer-to-peer communications), which provide higher reliability and hot upgrades; 
\item Traditional DBMS were designed in a time when hardware was expensive and people cheap and therefore require a lot of human fine-tuning: self-tuning, self-healing -- self-everything -- systems are eventually cheaper;
\item JDBC increases the overhead and complicates or hides the program logic.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{It's time to stop calling circuits ``hardware''}\\
  F. Vahid (2007)
\end{book}

The software-hardware frontier is getting blurrier: sequential programming, event-based programming, data flow programming, \emph{FPGA} (field programmable gate array, \ie, ``programmable chip'') cover the whole spectrum from \emph{temporal modelling} (formerly software) to \emph{spacial modelling} (formerly hardware).
FPGAs are still underused -- some people talk of using them to speed up financial computations.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.jstatsoft.org/v21/i07}{\emph{\texttt{ks}: Kernel density estimation\\ and kernel discriminant analysis\\ for multivariate data in R}}\\
  T. Duong\\
  Journal of statistical software (2007)
\end{book}

A \emph{kernel density estimate} if
$$ \hat f _H (x) = \dfrac1n \sum_i K_H( x - X_i ) $$
where $X_i$ are the observations,
$$ K_H = \va H ^{-1/2} K ( H ^{-1/2} x ), $$
$K$ is a kernel (such as $K(x) = (2\pi)^{-d/2} \exp( -\tfrac12 x' x )$, its choice is not crucial),
and $H$ is a positive definite matrix.

The article explains how to choose $H$, beyond the naive choice of ``sphering the data'' (replacing $X$ by $(\var X)^{-1/2} X$ and choosing a diagonal $H$, check the \texttt{sm} and \texttt{KernSmooth} packages),
based on the \emph{(asymptotic) mean integrated square error}
$$ \textrm{MISE}(H) = E \int_{\RR^d} \bigl( \hat f_H(x) - f(x) \bigr)^2 dx. $$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.jstatsoft.org/v21/i05}{\emph{Self- and super-organizing maps in R:\\ the \texttt{kohonen} package}}\\
  R. Wehrens and L.M.C. Buydens\\
  Journal of statistical software (2007)
\end{book}

To map a high-dimensional data set to two dimensions, one can use:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Principal component analysis (PCA);
\item Multidimensional scaling (MDS):
similar to PCA, but starts directly from the distance matrix, not the coordinates --
there are also non-linear variants;
\item self-organizing maps (SOM):
a variant of the $k$-means algorithm that takes into account proximity between cluster centers.
\end{compactitem}

SOM can also be used for supervised classification:
learn two maps in parallel, one with the predictive variables, the other with the variable(s) to predict;
often each will have its own distance and the algorithm will use a linear combination of these.
\emph{Super-organizing maps} generalize this to more than two layers.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Copulas: a personal view}\\
  P. Embrechts
\end{book}

A review article on current topics, uses, misuses, problems and workarounds in copula theory, with interesting references:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Copulas were designed to study continuous variables (or rather: variables with continuous margins): with non-continuous variables, everything that could possibly go wrong will;
\item Fitting a copula to your data is only half the story: do not forget goodness of fit tests!
\item Correlation is not always a good measure: given two continuous marginal distributions $F_1$ and $F_2$, the set of attainable correlations is a closed interval that can be much smaller than $[-1,1]$ -- e.g., with $\textrm{LN}(0,1)$ and $\textrm{LN}(0,4^2)$, it is $[-2.5\,10^{-4},1.4\,10^{-2}]$...
\item There is no good answer to the question ``which copula to use?'': at the very least, you should have a look at elliptic and extreme value copulas, but do not expect them to suffice -- our copula catalogue is still mostly empty (some would even say useless);
\item Even if you do not know which copula to choose, there is hope: given several sources of risk $X_1,\dots,X_n$ (market risk, operational risk, etc.), the knowledge of their marginal distribution, and a risk aggregation function $\psi(X_1,\dots,X_n)$, we can find bounds of this aggregated risk
$$ \psi_{\textrm{min}} \leq \psi(X_1,\dots,X_n) \leq \psi_{\textrm{max}} $$
For instance, $\inf \{ \textrm{VaR}_\alpha( \sum X_i), X_i \sim F_i \}$; one sometimes also assumes properties for the copula, instead of optimizing on the set of all copulas.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A primer on copulas for count data}\\
  C. Genest and J. Ne\v slehov\'a\\
  Astin bulletin (2007)
\end{book}

The definition of a copula assumes that the margins are continuous:
otherwise, the ``copula function'' $C(u,v)$ is no longer uniquely defined --
and even worse, naive definitions are never a copula.
Given two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$,
one can consider the \emph{set} of copulas $C$ such that
$$ \forall x, y \qquad F_{X,Y} (x,y) = C( F_X(x), F_Y(y) ). $$
(One of those copulas has good properties, though:
the \emph{bilinear extension}, \ie,
the simplest piecewise affine, continuous copula.)

Even if the variables are independent of monotonic-dependent
(this notion becomes tricker than functional monotonic dependence),
this set is rather large:
one can compute pointwise bounds of those copulas;
these lead to bounds on Spearman's correlation and Kendall's $\tau$
(the difference between those bounds is at least one).
The definitions of Kendall's $\tau$ and Spearman's $\rho$ do not account for ties;
in particular, depending on the marginal distribution, they may not span all of $[0,1]$;
they can be replaced by Kendall's $\tau_b$
$$ \tau_b(X,Y) = \dfrac{ \tau(X, Y) }{ \sqrt{ P(X_1 \neq X_2) P(Y_1 \neq Y_2) } } $$
and Spearman's grade correlation coefficient (many other concordance measures have been suggested);
dependence in the copula (positive quadrant dependence,
left tail dependence, positive likelihood ratio dependence)
leads to dependence in the initial variables (for the bilinear extension, this is even equivalent).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The joy of copulas:\\ bivariate distributions with uniform marginals}\\
  C. Genest and J. MacKay\\
  American Statistician (1986)
\end{book}

Elementary (but interesting) exercises leading to the definition of archimedian copulas,
an example of a singular distribution with smooth margins
\begin{align*}
  \phi(t) &= (1-t)^\alpha, \qquad \alpha \geq 1 \\
  C(x,y) &= \phi^{-1} ( \phi(x) + \phi(y) ),
\end{align*}
and a ``geometric'' interpretation of Kendall's $\tau$ for archimedian copulas
$$ \tau = 4 \int_0^1 \dfrac{ \phi(t) }{ \phi'(t) } dt + 1. $$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Copulas: tales and facts}\\
  T. Mikosch (2005)
\end{book}

Copulas do not really fit in the theory of stochastic processes and time series analysis -- this is worrying, since the banking and finance industry, responsible for the \emph{copula epidemic}, mainly use them in this context.

More work needs to be done on copula estimators and goodness of fit tests (Should we estimate margins and copula simultaneously? Should we use a parametric fit of the margins or their empirical distribution to fit the copula? Does a good fit for the margins and for the copula lead to a good fit for the multivariate distribution?).

The effects of increasing dimension (on, say, the quality of copula estimators) remains largely unstudied: most authors stick to the 2-dimensional case. For stochastic processes, you would even need infinite-dimensional copulas.

Our catalogue of copulas is still mostly empty: archimedian copulas are rarely meaningful in practical situations, elliptic copulas are bad at measuring ``tail dependence'' (I use quotes because the notion of upper and lower tail dependence is limited to the 2-dimensional case), there is no \emph{canonical} multivariate distribution on which to build an extreme value copula (the Gumbel copula is popular, but is far from being the only choice).

The author also disagrees with the need to transform the margins, and with the choice of a uniform distribution.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Discussion of\\ ``copulas: tales and facts'' by T. Mikosch}\\
  C. Genest and B. R\'emillard\\
  Extremes (2006)
\end{book}

Harsh criticism of the previous article.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Copulas: tales and facts -- rejoinder}\\
  T. Mikosch
\end{book}

After summarizing the responses to the article
(e.g., someone mentionned that pseudo-likelihood copula estimators
(use the empirical distribution of the marginals to fit a parametric copula)
are inefficient), the author also clarifies what he means by ``dependence structure'' for stochastic processes:
the whole distribution, which is determined by all the finite-dimensional marginals --
this includes the 1-dimensional margins and therefore leads to large misundertandings
with copula specialists.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Everything you always wanted to know about copula modelling but were afraid to ask}\\
  C. Genest and A.-C. Favre \\
  Journal of hydrologic engineering (2007)
\end{book}

The following graphics can help modelling with copulas:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Scatterplot of ranks, \ie, plot of the sample copula;
\item The Chi-plot plots the pairs $(\lambda_a, \chi_i)$ where
\begin{align*}
  H_i &= \dfrac{ \#\{\, j \neq i \ :\ X_j \leq X_i,\ Y_j \leq Y_i \,\} }{ n - 1 } \\
  F_i &= \dfrac{ \#\{\, j \neq i \ :\ X_j \leq X_i \,\} }{ n - 1 } \\
  G_i &= \dfrac{ \#\{\, j \neq i \ :\ Y_j \leq Y_i \,\} }{ n - 1 } \\
  \chi_i &= \dfrac{ H_i - F_i G_i }{ \sqrt{ F_i ( 1 - F_i ) G_i ( 1 - G_i ) } } \\
  \tilde F_i &= F_i - \tfrac12 \\
  \tilde G_i &= G_i - \tfrac12 \\
  \lambda_i &= 4 \, \textrm{sign}(\tilde F_i \tilde G_i) \max( \tilde F_i ^2 , \tilde G_i ^2 );
\end{align*}
  large values of $\lambda$ are outliers, large values of $\chi$ signal non-independence;
\item The \emph{K-plot} is the quantile-quantile plot of the $H_i$ versus
  the distribution coming from independent variables
  (on this plot, you can also draw the curve obtained with perfect positive dependence).
\end{compactitem}

The article also reviews a few copula estimators:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item If you are fitting a copula from a 1-parameter familly,
  you can choose this parameter so as to produce the observed Kendall's $\tau$
  or Spearman's $\rho$
  (this is a method or moments estimator); this can lead to actual tests;
\item Maximum pseudo-likelihood estimators modify the log-likelihood
  $$ \sum_i \log c_\theta ( F X_i, G X_i ) $$
  with the empirical marginal distributions
  $$ \sum_i \log c_\theta \left( \dfrac{ R_i }{ n + 1 }, \dfrac{ S_i }{ n + 1 } \right) $$
  where $R_i$ and $S_i$ are the ranks of $X_i$ and $Y_i$;
\item 2-step estimators: first estimating the maginal distributions, then the copula (this is another pseudo-likelihood);
\item Kernel (non-parametric) estimators.
\end{compactitem}

When modelling data with copulas, people often forget to check how good the fit is:
the article presents an actual test; some graphics can also help:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Compare the plot of the sample copula and that of simulated data from the fitted copula;
\item The quantile-quantile plot of
$$ \hat C \left(  \dfrac{ R_i }{ n+1 }, \dfrac{ S_i }{ n+1 } \right)$$
against $C_{\hat\theta}(U,V)$ where $(U,V) \sim C_{\hat\theta}$.
\end{compactitem}

The authors also remark that most measures of dependence are of the form
$$ \iint J(u,v) \, dC(u,v) $$
where $J$ can be chose optimally if you know the familly of copulas your data comes from;
$J(u,v) = uv$ gives Spearman's $\rho$, 
$J(u,v) = 4 C(u,v) - 1$ gives Kendall's $\tau$,
$J(u,v) = \Phi^{-1}(u) \Phi^{-1}(v)$ gives the van der Waerden statistic.

Also note that, for gaussian distributions,
correlation, Spearman's $\rho$ and Kendall's $\tau$ are very similar.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation and dependence in risk management: properties and pitfalls}\\
  P. Embrechts et al. (1999)
\end{book}

Correlation is a misleading measure of dependence for non-elliptic distributions,
such as derivative returns in finance or loss in insurance;
the article lists some of those fallacies:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Marginal distributions and correlation need not determine the joint distribution, in particular, the value at risk (VaR) of linear portfolios is not determined by the marginal distributions and the correlation;
\item Given the marginal distributions, not all correlations in $[-1,1]$ are attainable;
\item The worst-case VaR (quantile) of $X+Y$ need not occur when $\rho(X,Y)$ is maximal, \ie, when $X$ and $Y$ are comonotonic (the variance will be maximal, but not the quantiles): the best you can say is
$$ \textrm{VaR}_\alpha( X + Y ) \leq \inf_{ C_\ell(u,v) = \alpha } F_1^{-1}(u) + F_2^{-1}(v); $$
this is not surprising since VaR is not a subadditive risk measure; consequently, low correlation need not mean high diversification.
\end{compactitem}

The article also recalls:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item the link between correlation and regression
  \begin{align*}
    \rho(X,Y) &= \dfrac{ \var Y - \min\limits_{a,b} E[ ( Y - (a + bX) )^2 ] }{ \var Y } \\
    b &= \dfrac{ \cov(X,Y) }{ \var X } \\
    a &= E[Y] - b E[X].
  \end{align*}
\item other measures of dependence: rank correlation, Schweizer and Wolff
\begin{align*}
  \rho_S(X,Y) &= 12 \iint ( C(u,v) - uv ) du dv \\
  \delta_1(X,Y) &= 12 \iint \va{ C(u,v) - uv } \, du dv \\
  \delta_2(X,Y) &= \left( 90 \iint \va{ C(u,v) - uv }^2 \, du dv \right)^{1/2} \\
  \delta_3(X,Y) &= 4 \sup_{u,v} \va{ C(u,v) - uv }
\end{align*}
  comonotonicity, positive quadrant dependence
  $$ \forall x,y \quad P[ X > x, Y > y ] \geq P[ X > x ] P[ Y > y ], $$
  positive association
  \begin{align*}
    & \forall g_1, g_2 \textrm{ increasing} \\
    & E[ g_1(X,Y) g_2(X,Y) ] \geq E[ g_1(X,Y) ] E[ g_2(X,Y) ].
  \end{align*}
\end{compactitem}

If all correlations are either
$\rho_{\textrm{min}}(F_i,F_j)$
or
$\rho_{\textrm{max}}(F_i,F_j)$
and satisfy some compatibility conditions,
then the joint distribution is uniquely determined;
it is called an \emph{extremal distribution}.

It is not known if all linear correlation matrices are rank correlation matrices,
but a sufficient condition is that the matrix of the
$2 \sin ( \pi \rho_{ij} / 6 )$ be a linear correlation matrix
(this is then the correlation matrix of the gaussian distribution
with the prescribed rank correlation matrix, which you can use in simulations).

The article does not tackle serial and cross-correlation and estimators (of correlation, rank correlation and copulas).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On Blest's measure of rank correlation}\\
  C. Genest and J.F. Plante\\
  Canadian journal of statistics (2003)
\end{book}

Many measures of concordance, for a copula $C$, are of the form
$$ \int_{[0,1]^2} f(u,v) \, dC(u,v). $$
For instance:
\begin{align*}
  \rho(X,Y) &= 12 \int_{[0,1]^2} uv \, dC(u,v) - 3 \\
  \tau(X,Y) &= 4  \int_{[0,1]^2} C(u,v) \, dC(u,v) - 1 \\
\end{align*}
This article examines the asymptotic distribution of Blest's measure of rank correlation, an estimator of
$$ \nu(X,Y) = 2 - 12 \int_{[0,1]^2} (1 - u^2) v \, dC(u,v) $$
and suggest to symetrize it as
\begin{align*}
  \xi(X,Y) &= \dfrac{ \nu(X,Y) + \nu(Y,X) }{ 2 } \\
           &= - 4 + 6 \int_{[0,1]^2} uv (4 - u - v) \, dC(u,v).
\end{align*}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation in a bayesian framework}\\
  A. DasGupta et al.\\
  Journal of multivariate analysis (2000)
\end{book}

Asymptotic distribution of $\cor( \theta, \hat\theta )$, where $\theta$ is a parameter in a model (seen as a random variable) and $\hat\theta$ an estimator of $\theta$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A characterization of quasi-copulas}\\
  C. Genest et al.\\
  Journal of multivariate analysis (1999)
\end{book}

A \emph{quasi-copula} is a function $Q: [0,1]^2 \longrightarrow [0,1]$ so that
for each track $B$ (a path from (0,0) to (1,1), non-decreasing in both coordinates)
there exist a copula $C_B$ that coincides with $Q$ on $B$.

Not all quasi-copulas are copulas.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting multifractal volatility}\\
  L. Calvet and A. Fisher (2001)
\end{book}
The \emph{multifractial model of asset returns} (MMAR) is a brownian motion $B$ compounded with a random trading time $\theta$
$$ \log P_t - \log P_0 = B [ \theta(t) ] $$
where the trading time $\theta$ is the cumulative distribution of a \emph{multifractal measure}.

Given two sequences of positive real numbers $m_0$ and $m_1$ such that
$$ \forall t \qquad m_{0,t} + m_{1,t} = 1, $$
define a sequence of probability measures on $[0,1]$ as follows:
$\mu_0 = \lambda$ is the Lebesgue measure;
$\mu_1$ has weight $m_{0,1}$ on $[0,\tfrac12)$ and weight $m_{1,1}$ on $[\tfrac12,1)$;
$\mu_2$ splits each of the intervals $[0,\tfrac12)$ and $[\tfrac12,1)$ in two,
and assigns a fraction $m_{0,2}$ (resp. $m_{1,2}$) to the left (resp. right) one; etc.
In formulas,
$$ \mu_n = \left( \sum_{i_1=0}^1 \cdots \sum_{i_n=0}^n m_{i_1,1} \cdots m_{i_n,n} \textbf{1}_{[x, x+2^{-n}]} \right) \lambda$$
where $x = \sum_{k=1}^n i_k 2^{-k} $.
This is a \emph{binomial measure} on $[0,1]$. Instead of splitting each interval in two, one can uniformly split them into $b$ subintervals.
Instead of using deterministic sequences $m_{k,t}$,
one can use a discrete random variable $M$
taking values $m_0,\dots,m_{b-1}$
with probabilities $p_0,\dots,p_{b-1}$:
this is a \emph{random multifractal measure}.
One can ask that the weight be exactly preserved at each step or only on average: $E[M] = 1/b$.

The \emph{Poisson multifractal} also randomizes the splitting points (with a Poisson process).
The authors have yet to study option pricing and stochastic portfolios in this context.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multifractality in asset returns:\\ theory and evidence}\\
  L. Calvet and A. Fisher (1996, 2001)
\end{book}

Earlier article on the MMAR.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Volatility comovement:\\ a multifrequency approach}\\
  L.E. Calvet et al. (2004)
\end{book}

Multifractal volatility can be generalized to bivariate time series;
the model can be estimated via maximum likelihood (in closed form) or a particle filter.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Theoretical foundations of asset allocation and pricing models with higher-order moments}\\
  E. Jurczenko and B. Maillet (2006)
\end{book}

A random variable $X$ $D_4$-dominates $Y$ if it is prefered over $Y$ for each utility function in
\[
  D_4 = \{\,
    U : \RR \rightarrow \RR
    \ :\
    U' > 0, \ 
    U'' < 0, \ 
    U''' > 0, \
    U''''< 0
  \,\}.
\]

(This chapter was not interesting.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Community structure\\ in social and biological networks}\\
  M. Girvan and E.J. Newman (2001)
\end{book}

To the usual stylized facts of social networks
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Small world: the average distance between two nodes is small, often logarithmic in the number of vertices;
\item Power law degree distribution;
\item Network transitivity (or clustering):
  the probability that two nodes are connected is higher if they are connected to a third,
  measured by the \emph{clustering coefficient}
  \[
    C = 3 \dfrac{\text{Number of triangles}}{\text{number of connected triples of vertices}}
  \]
\end{compactitem}
the authors add one more: those graphs can be partitionned into communities.
The following algorithm performs better than hierarchical clustering.
The \emph{betweenness} of an edge is the number of shortest paths that run through it;
if there are several shortest paths between two nodes, they are given the same weight
so that those weights sup up to one.
Communities appear when you remove edges with the highest betweenness.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A general approach to integrated risk management with skewed, fat-tailed risks}\\
  J.V. Rosenberg and Tl. Schuermann (2005)
\end{book}

Recipes to aggregate different sources of risk (market, credit, operational),
measured by the value-at-risk (VaR),
without really falling in the gaussian trap.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian analysis  for penalized spline regression using WinBUGS}\\
  C.M. Crainiceanu et al.\\
  Journal of statistical software (2005)
\end{book}

Penalized models can be seen as bayesian models
(indeed, the penalty can be turned into a prior distribution).
As a result, one can tweak bayesian inference software  (e.g., Bugs, Jags)
into fitting penalized models.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A software tool for the exponential power distribution: the \texttt{normalp} package}\\
  A.M. Mineo and M. Ruggieri\\
  Journal of statistical software (2005)
\end{book}

\emph{General error  distributions} (GED),
aka \emph{exponential power distributions} (EPD),
aka normal distributions of order $p$,
are a family of distributions generalizing the gaussian distribution
and designed to be used as error distributions.
They have three parameters: mean,
shape ($p=2$ for the gaussian,
$p=1$ for the Laplace and
$p=\infty$ for the uniform distribution)
and dispersion ($\sigma_p = ( E \va{ X - \mu } ^ p )^{1/2}$).

Along with the usual \texttt{d}, \texttt{p}, \texttt{q}, \texttt{r} functions,
the package also provides an \texttt{lmp} function for regression with EPD errors.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{\texttt{EbayesThresh}:\\ R programs for empirical Bayes thresholding}\\
  I.M. Johnstone and B.W. Silverman\\
  Journal of statistical software (2005)
\end{book}

When fitting data with wavelets,
we want most of the coefficients to be zero --
to this end, we have to threshold them.
This article suggests to model the coefficients as a mixture of
a Dirac density in zero (coefficients to be thresholded)
and a Laplace distribution (aka \emph{double exponential}), to which noise has been added.
They use bayesian methods to perform the thresholding.

This can be applied to other situations where you have a series of random variables,
measured with noise, most of which are zero.

The same idea is also used for discrete distributions,
e.g., with the \emph{zero-inflated Poisson distribution} (ZIP) --
check the \texttt{zicounts} package in R.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A boosting approach for automated trading}\\
  G. Creamer and Y. Freund\\
  Workshop on data mining for business applications, KDD 2006
\end{book}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Monkeying with the goodness-of-fit test}\\
  G. Marsaglia\\
  Journal of statistical software (2005)
\end{book}

To test if qualitative variables $x_1,\dots,x_n$  are iid and follow a given distribution, one can:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Look at $Q_1 = \sum \dfrac{ ( \text{observed} - \text{expected} )^2 }{ \text{expected} }$;
\item Look at the same thing, $Q_2$, for pairs $(x_1,x_2)$, $(x_2,x_3),\dots$;
\item Look at $Q_2 - Q_1$.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A \texttt{clue} for cluster ensembles}\\
  K. Hornik\\
  Journal of statistical software (2005)
\end{book}

What to do when a clustering algorithm is not stable.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of immune\\ and genetic algorithms for two real-life tasks\\ of pattern recognition}\\
  A.O. Tarakanov and Y.A. Tarakanov\\
  Journal of unconventional computing (2005)
\end{book}

\emph{Immunocomputing} (IC),
\ie, $k$-nearest neighbours
after dimension reduction
via singular value decomposition (SVD),
perform better than neural nets,
which perform better than genetic algorithms (GA) --
on those datasets, chosen because IC was working on them.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Negative selection algorithm\\ for aircraft fault detection}\\
  A. Dasgupta et al.
\end{book}

Negative selection algorithms (NSA) are just
a $k$-nearest neighbours algorithm
after a principal component analysis (PCA)
and/or $k$-means dimension reduction step.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On-line negative databases}\\
  F. Esponda et al.
\end{book}

Negative selection (the immune system recognizes the self by storing information about the non-self)
can obscure information and help design privacy-enhancing negative databases.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Squashing flat files flatter}\\
  W. DuMouchel et al.
\end{book}

Instead of adapting statistical methods to large datasets,
you can make large datasets smaller:
random sampling is an option,
but \emph{surrogate data} can perform better:
cluster the data in a large number of clusters
($k$-means or simply bins, with a weight on each bin),
examine the statistical properties of the cluster centers, e.g., moments,
and generate random (or non random) data with similar properties.

The caveat is that the ``statistical properties'' you replicate
must capture the information you want.


\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Downside consumption risk and expected returns}\\
  V. Polkovnichenko (2006)
\end{book}

Yet another article showing that investors are averse to dowside risk:
with \emph{rank-dependent expected utility} (RDEU), utility is weighted
by \emph{decision weights} which are transformations
of the cumulative objective probabilities of ranked events --
\emph{cumulative prospect theory} is a special case of RDEU.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Equilibrium underdiversification and the preference for skewness}\\
  T. Mitton and K. Vorkink\\
  Review of financial studies (2007)
\end{book}

Preference for skewness, \ie, maximization of
\[
  E[X] - \dfrac{ 1 }{ 2 \tau } \var X + \dfrac{ 1 }{ 3 \phi } \text{Skew}\, X,
\]
as with ``Lotto investors'',
explains underdiversified portfolios.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Opinion divergence and post-earnings announcement drift}\\
  K.L. Anderson et al. (2007)
\end{book}

Opinion divergence can be measured as
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The \emph{Herfindhal} measure of divergence,
\[ \textrm{HI} = \sum_{i=1}^N \left( \dfrac{ V_i }{ V_{\text{total}} } \right)^2 \]
where $V_i$ is the volume traded by market maker $i$ on that day;
\item The standardized unexplained volume (SUV),
\ie, standardized residual (on the day of interest)
of the regression (over the past two months)
\[ \text{volume} \sim \text{returns}_+ + \text{returns}_- \]
\item The standardized price volatility (SPV),
\ie, the standardized residuals of the regression
of the daily volatility against volume and returns.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the interaction between ultra-high frequency measures of volatility}\\
  G.M. Gallo and M. Velucchi (2007)
\end{book}

Comparison of realized variance
\( \sum x_t^2 \)
and \emph{bipower variance}
\[ \sum \va{ x_t x_{t-1} } \]
(the latter is supposed to be robust to jumps)
in the context of \emph{multiplicative error models} (MEM),
\ie, GARCH-like models used to model positive quantities (volume, duration, range, etc.):
they are almost undistinguishable.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Neoclassical factors}\\
  L. Zhang and L. Chen (2007)
\end{book}

The Fama--French model can be replaced by a 3-factor model:
market, investment/assets, earnings/assets;
this also captures \emph{momentum} effects.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Asset based style analysis for equity strategies: the role of the volatility factor}\\
  D.E. Kuenzi and X. Shi\\
  Journal of alternative investments (2007)
\end{book}

When trying to explain or replicate hedge fund returns, do not forget to include a volatility factor
(calls and puts (the authors' advice), change in VIX, straddle, convertible bonds),
lest you remain blind to all non-linear effects, such as Lo's ``capital decimation'' strategy.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Who moves stock prices? Monthly evidence}\\
  B. A. \O degaard (2007)
\end{book}

Change in institutional ownership might help predict monthly returns.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A better tool than Allen's relations for expressing temporal knowledge in interval data}\\
  F. Morchen\\
  Theory and practice of temporal data mining (2006)
\end{book}

Allen's relations between intervals,
used in temporal reasoning
(before, meets, overlaps, starts, finished, equals),
are not \emph{robust} enough for data mining.
The \emph{time series knowledge representation} (TSKR)
uses a musical analogy (tones, chords, phrases) and
only considers the interaction of participating intervals
and accounts for noise.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantile trees for marketing}\\
  C. Perlich and S. Rosset\\
  DMBA 2006
\end{book}

\emph{Quantile regression} can be used to focus on customers with high growth potential.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Adaptive event detection\\ with time-varying Poisson processes}\\
  A. Ihler et al.\\
  KDD 2006
\end{book}

(Bugs-like) graphical model example.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Data mining in the real world}\\
  F. Fogelman Souli\'e
\end{book}

Data mining is widely used for customer relation management (CRM),
fraud detection, credit scoring, etc. and
\emph{extreme data mining}, \ie, data mining in databases beyond 10TB,
is starting to emerge. It is characterized by:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Heterogeneous data;
\item Poor data quality (missing values, outliers -- or even ``unlabeled data'', \ie, ``massively missing values'');
\item Large volume (billions of rows, thousands of columns), that prevents the user from hand-chosing the variables and calls for \emph{data duplication avoidance};
\item Fast model calibration (ideally hours, should be linear in the number of rows or columns);
\item Automated model quality assessment (they use a robustness indicator, KR, from Vapnik's structural risk minimization theory);
\item Real-time model application;
\item Industrialization (we would say ``productionalization'') within the same project, not as a separate, long-term project;
\item Model control (check deviations on the data distribution);
\item Data mining standards (JDM, PMML) to export the results;
\item Process control and workflow;
\item Exploratory modeling (the end-user will want to \emph{understand} the model);
\item Predictive modeling;
\item Unskilled users.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A review of some semiparametric regression models with application to scoring}\\
  J.L. Berthet and V. Patilea
\end{book}

``Scoring'' means predicting a binary variable, whose values are interpreted
as ``bad'' (``default'') or ``good'' (``non-default'');
the problem is usually asymetric: while the ``good'' observations are homogeneous,
there are many ways of being ``bad''.
Between parametric methods (logistic regression, linear discriminant analysis (LDA))
and non-parametric methods ($k$-nearest neighbours (kNN), classification trees,
neural networks, support vector machines), one can also consider semiparametric methods,
such as logistic generalized additive model (GAM):
\begin{align*}
  P[ \text{non-default} ] &= m_1(X_1) + \cdots + m_n(X_n) \\
  P[ \text{non-default} ] &= m( \lambda_1 X_1 + \cdots + \lambda_n X_n )
\end{align*}
This is very similar to a dimension reduction before the logistic or GAM reduction,
but the dimension reduction step takes the variable to predict into account
(this sounds like the difference between principal component regression (PCR)
and partial least squares (PLS)).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic skew in currency options}\\
  P. Carr and L. Wu\\
  Journal of financial economics (2007)
\end{book}

Traditional jump-diffusion stochastic volatility models
fail to account for the time-varying skew
of the risk-neutral distribution.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Time reversal invariance in finance}\\
  G. Zumbach (2007)
\end{book}

Most time-series models are time-reversal invariant -- price time series are not... % Forgotten stylized fact

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Group dynamics of the Japanese market}\\
  W.S. Jung et al. (2007)
\end{book}

When building a minimum spanning tree (MST)
on the correlation matrix of stock returns
to find sectors,
you might want to remove the influence of
the global market (here, S\&P\,500) first.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Information flow between composite stock index and individual stocks}\\
  O. Kwon and J.-S. Yang (2007)
\end{book}

Individual stocks are influenced by the market.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiagent cooperative search for portfolio selection}\\
  D.C. Parkes and B.A. Huberman
\end{book}

Investment strategies can be averaged
(as in bayesian model averaging (BMA) or ``multi-alpha'').

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Information diffusion based explanations of asset pticing anomalies}\\
  A. Bolmatis and E.G. Sekeris (2007)
\end{book}

For not-so-liquid stocks, you might want to replace the Fama-French factors by
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Age (number of days since the IPO);
\item proportion of non-trading days in the past year.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The supervisor's portfolio: the market price risk of German banks from 2001 to 2003 -- analysis and models for risk aggregation}\\
  C. Memmel and C. Wehn\\
  Bundesbank (2005)
\end{book}

The value-at-risk (VaR) of a portfolio
can be estimated from the \emph{time series}
of the VaR of its constituents.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-linearities in exchange rate dynamics: chaos?}\\
  V. Vandrovych (2006)
\end{book}

The hypothesis that exchange rates are chaotic is rejected by the
\emph{correlation dimension} and the
\emph{Lyapunov exponent}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Shortfall: a tail of two parts}\\
  R. Martin and D. Tasche\\
  Risk (2007)
\end{book}

Expected shortfall can be decomposed into a systematic and a specific part:
\begin{align*}
  Y &= X \beta + \epsilon \\
  E[ Y | Y > y ] &= E[ X | Y > y ] \beta + E[ \epsilon | Y > y ].
\end{align*}
Under gaussian (or some other distributional) assumptions,
this can be explicitely computed.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Cointegration and exchange market efficiency: an analysis of high frequency data}\\
  A. Trapletti et al. (1999)
\end{book}

Were markets efficients, a triangle of high frequency exchange rates would be cointegrated
and the cointegration term would be a \emph{martingale difference}
(testing properties of the cointegration term instead of the cointegration itself is easier:
it does not require a long period -- just a lot of data, but with tick data, there is enough).

Markets are not efficient; arbitrage opportunities, profitable trading strategies do exist.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Predicting tick-by-tick price fluctuations by evolutionary computations}\\
  M. Tanaka-Yamawaki
\end{book}

Genetic algorithms (GA) can predict the direction of the next tick
given previous tick directions.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The volatility effect:\\ lower risk without lower return}\\
  D.C. Blitz and P. van Vliet (2007)
\end{book}

Stocks with low volatility earn higher risk-adjusted returns: equity investors overpay for risk.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal hedge ratio estimation\\ and effectiveness using ARCD}\\
  E. Kostika and R.N. Markellos
\end{book}

A GARCH-like model (auto-regressive conditional density)
for generalized Student distributions,
allowing time-varying variance, skewness and kurtosis.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient time series matching by wavelets}\\
  K.P. Chan and A.W.C. Fu
\end{book}

The Haar wavelet transform can be used to redure the dimension of time series, before indexing with an R-tree.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Similarity search over time series data\\ using wavelets}\\
  I. Popivanov and R.J. Miller\\
  ICDE (2002)
\end{book}

Wavelet transforms can be used as a data reduction rechniaue for time series data --
contrary to popular belief, the Haar wavelet is not always the best choice. 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Time series feature extraction\\ for data mining using DWT and DFT}\\
  F. M\"orchen (2003)
\end{book}

When using the discrete Fourrier transform (DFT, as in JPEG)
or the discrete wavelet transform (DWT, as in JPEG2000)
for time series dimension reduction, you can select
the largest coefficients instead of the first ones.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{An econometric model of serial correlation and illiquidity in hedge fund returns}\\
  M. Getmansky et al.\\
  Journal of financial economics (2004)
\end{book}

Time-varying leverage and high water mark incentive fees
cannot account for all the autocorrelation in hedge fund returns:
illiquidity (e.g., non-synchronous trading)
and/or return smoothing play a larger role.

This calls for an econometric model of smoothed returns:
observed returns are a moving average of true economic (unobserved) returns.
a hidden Markov chain 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Mimicking portfolios with conditioning information}\\
  W. Ferson et al.\\
  Journal of financial and quantitative analysis (2006)
\end{book}

To replicate a non-investible asset,
you can look for the linear combination of assets in your universe
whose returns have the largest correlation -- this is an example of
\emph{partial least squares} (PLS).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Option pricing with aggregation of physical models and nonparrametric statistical learning}\\
  J. Fan and L. Mancini (2007)
\end{book}

Traditional option pricing models can be combined with nonparametric (machine learning) approaches to correct their errors.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Asian options versus vanilla options}\\
  G. Ye
\end{book}

Asian options need not be cheaper than vanilla options.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Market timing\\ with candlestick technical analysis}\\
  B.R. Marshall et al. (2007)
\end{book}

Technical analysis (TA) is not profitable....

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The impact of option strategies in financial portfolios performance: mean-variance and stochastic dominance approaches}\\
  F. Abid et al. (2007)
\end{book}

\emph{Stochastic dominance} is very rare:
given to investment strategies,
you should not expect one to dominate the other.
However, if the strategies are related
(say, a 1-parameter family of strategies,
or a strategy with or without an overlay),
all hope is not lost.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Individual vs aggregate preferences:\\ the case of a small fish in a big pond}\\
  D.W. Blackburn and A.D. Ukhov (2006)
\end{book}

The risk preferences (utilities) of individual investors
sometimes aggregate in a counterintuitive way:
an economy of risk-seeking agents
can be risk-averse as a whole.
The notion of a ``representative agent''
is therefore not well defined...

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Long memory modeling\\ of realized covariance matrices}\\
  R. Chiriac and V. Voev (2007)
\end{book}

A time-dependent variance matrix can be modeled with an ARMA
(here, a fractionally integrated ARMA) model on its Cholesky matrix.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating high-frequency based (co-)variancces: a unified approach}\\
  I. Nolte and V. Voev (2007)
\end{book}

Microstructure noise and non-synchronous observation
complicates the estimation of the covariance of diffusion processes.
Those effects can be studied and remedied by examining
the effects of changes in the sampling frequency.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantile estimation\\ with adaptive importance sampling}\\
  D. Egloff and M. Leippold (2007)
\end{book}

Importance sampling can also be used to estimate quantiles, not just expectations.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{How well do financial and macroeconomic variables predict stock returns: time series\\ and cross-sectional evidence}\\
  A.S. Reng Rasmussen (2006)
\end{book}

Yet another list of variables to predict future returns.

