\end{multicols}
\clearpage
\begin{multicols}{2}

\begin{book}
  \emph{What happened to the quants in August 2007?}\\
  A.E. Khandani and A.W. Lo (2007)
\end{book}

This may have been caused by the unwind of a large portfolio.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The paradox of quantitative investing\\ and ways to deal with it}\\
  T. Suwabe et al.\\
  Goldman Sachs (2007)
\end{book}

The authors build a BMA-CAPM (\emph{bayesian model averaging} capital
asset pricing model) on a hundred variables, to decompose the momentum
(in excess of the industry average) into common and specific
components: the latter can be used as a reversal (it used to works),
the former as a momentum (noisier, only works in the past three
years).

One could also try:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item a time-series (Northfield-like) risk model (this means one regression per date and per stock);
\item support vector machines (SVM) or regularization path regression (least angle regression (LAR), lasso, etc.) instead of BMA.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Costly short-selling and stock price\\ adjustment to earnings announcements}\\
  A.V. Reed (2007)
\end{book}
Costly short-selling, or short-selling constraints,
reduces the speed at which the price adjusts to private information,
increases volatility (around earnings announcements),
reduces the information content of price and volume.
(This looks like a textbook example of the omitted variable syndrome...)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A stochastic volatility model with fat tails, skewness and leverage effects}\\
  D.R. Smith (2007)
\end{book}
One can try to reproduce the \textbf{leverage effect},
$\cor( r_t, v_{t+1} ) < 0$, 
\ie, shocks are correlated with future volatility, as follows.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item This first attempt exhibits leverage, but not negative skewness; 
  \begin{align*}
    y_t     &= \mu + e^{ x_t / 2 } + \epsilon_t \\
    x_{t+1} &= \omega + \phi_t x_t + \eta_t \\
    \begin{pmatrix} \epsilon_t \\ \eta_t \end{pmatrix} &\sim 
    N\left( 
      \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, 
      \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \rho \sigma \\ \rho \sigma & \sigma^2 \end{pmatrix} 
    \right)
  \end{align*}
\item If you ask that shocks be correlated with contemporaneous volatility,
  this is no longer a martingale;
\item Use the first model with a copula instead of a gaussian distribution.
\end{compactitem}


\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Who needs hedge funds?\\A copula-based approach\\ to hedge fund return replication}\\
  H.M. Kat and H.P. Palaro (2005)
\end{book}

Hedge fund replication usually tries to mimic the distribution of the
returns of a hedge fund, but hedge funds are often used as an overlay
to an already existing portfolio: the authors suggest to also
replicate the dependency between the hedge fund and the existing
portfolio, as follows.
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Infer the joint distribution of the hedge fund and the existing
  portfolio using copulas (gaussian, Student, Gumbel, Cook-Johnson
  (aka Clayton), Frank, symmetrised Joe-Clayton (SJC)) and marginal
  distributions (gaussian, Student, Johnson $S_U$);
\item Among all the payoff functions, find the cheapest that produces
  this distribution (this is Dybvig's \emph{payoff distribution
    pricing model} (PDPM) generalized to a 2-dimensional payoff
  distribution); it suffices to consider path-independent payoff
  function (any payoff distribution generated by a path-dependent
  payoff function can also be generated by a path-independent payoff
  funcion); if the Sharpe ratio of the underlying asset is high enough
  and the correlation with the investor's portfolio low enough, the
  payoff function will be non-decreasing;
\item Price and find a replicating strategy for this payoff function.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimation of value at risk using Johnson's $S_U$-normal distribution}\\
  P. Choi (2001)
\end{book}

Value at risk (VaR) estimation can be univariate (you only have the
returns of a portfolio) or multivariate (you know the returns of the
constituents of your portfolio, \ie, you want to estimate a quantile
of $\sum_i w_{it} r_{it}$, where $r$ and perhaps also $w$ is a random
variable); it can be conditional (e.g., conditional
heteroskedasticity, but you could also have models with conditional
third or fourth moments; the article also mentions exponential
smoothing (RiskMetrics), which is a special case of IGARCH) or
unconditional (extreme value theory (EVT)).

The article studies VaR estimation in a GARCH model with $S_U$-normal
innovations.

A random variable $Y$ is $S_U$-normal if it is of the form 
$$ Y = \sinh( \lambda + \theta X ) $$
where $\theta > 0$ and $X$ is standard gaussian. Those distributions
can be skewed and have fat tails.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Reinforcement learning: a survey}\\
  L.P. Kaelbling et al.\\
  Journal of artificial intelligence research (1996)
\end{book}

In \emph{reinforcement learning}, the agent has to make choices and
receives some feedback on those choices. Contrary to supervised
learning, he is not told which action would have been the best;
furthermore, the consequences of an action need not be immediate.

The agent typically tries to maximize one of the following quantities
(they do not lead to the same optimal policies):
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $N$-step optimal control
$$ E \left[ \sum_{t=n+1}^N r_t \right], \qquad n \in \llbracket 0, N \rrbracket $$
\item $N$-step receding horizon control
$$ E \left[ \sum_{t=n+1}^{n+N} r_t \right] $$
\item Infinite-horizon discounted model
$$ E \left[ \sum_{t=1}^\infty  \gamma^t  r_t  \right] $$
\item average reward model (this one does not penalize for long learning times)
$$ \lim _{N \to \infty} E \left[ \dfrac1N \sum_{t=1}^N r_t  \right] $$
\end{compactitem}

Asymptotic convergence results are useless: a fast convergence to a
near-optimal solution is better than a sluggish convergence to the optimal. 

Algorithms have to make a trade-off between exploration (of the
possible choices and their consequences) and exploitation.

\emph{Dynamic programming} can be used to maximize those performance measures.

With a 1-step, immediate-reward model, one can use greedy strategies,
\emph{Boltzman exploration} or \emph{interval-based techniques} (for
each action, store the number of trials and the number of successes
and compute a confidence interval on the probability of success).
These algorithms can be adapted to multi-step, immediate-reward
problems.

\emph{Markov decision processes} (MDP) can be used to model delayed
reward. If the decision process were known (we do assume that the
states and transition probabilities are known, though), we could
compute the value of each state for the discounted model and, from
there, the optimal policy.

\emph{Value iteration} estimates the value of each node and derives a
strategy (since a strategy is a finite object, it converges in a
finite number of steps); \emph{policy iteration} directly estimates
the policy. 

But we do not know the model.

The \emph{adaptive heuristic critic} algorithm is similar to policy
iteration, but the value function is computed iteratively, from the
$TD(0)$ algorithm:
$$ V(s) \leftarrow V(s) + \alpha ( r + \gamma V(s') - V(s) ) $$
where $r$ is the reward, $s$ the current state, $s'$ the next state. 
The $TD(\lambda)$ algorithm also updates states that were recently visited. 

The \emph{Q-learning} algorithm (if you do not know which algorithm to
implement, use this one) estimates $Q(s,a)$, the expected discounted
reinforcement of taking action $a$ while in state $s$, with the
$TD(0)$ or $TD(\lambda)$ algorithm, with a decreasing $\alpha$. You
must first explore the states (but the algorithm is robust) and, after
convergence, you can act greedily.

Other algorithms (\emph{certainty equivalence}, \emph{Dyna},
\emph{prioritized sweeping} learn the model at the same time as the
optimal policy: they make a better use of the data and converge
faster.

In case of very large state spaces, you can aggregate some states and
solve a coarser problem (\emph{multi-grid methods}, \emph{state
  aggregation}); you can also replace the mappings (transition
probabilities, value functions, policies, etc.) by a simpler
representation obtained by \emph{supervised learning}.There are other
\emph{generalization} algorithms.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Computer science and game theory}\\
  J.Y. Halpern\\
  \texttt{arXiv:cs/0703148}
\end{book}

Complexity plays a role in game theory: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Agents can have \emph{bounded rationality}, \ie, have limited computational power (e.g., they can be finite automata); this can lead to cooperation in the prisoner's dilemma;
\item Many problems about \emph{Nash equilibrium} are NP-hard;
\item One cannot design a non-dictatorial voting scheme immune to
  manipulation by voters, but this manipulation can be made
  computationnally unreasonable; there are similar problems in
  \emph{combinatorial auctions} (auctions where you can bet on bundles
  of objects; but pricing $2^N$ objects is too long);
\item Agent-based games (e.g., \emph{Byzantine agreement} are very similar to distributed computing, networking protocols, fault-tolerance, mediator-less cryptography.   
\end{compactitem}

This (concise) article also mentions random graphs, bayesian and
Markov networks, reinforcement learning.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{What every investor should know \\ about commodities\\part 1: univariate return analysis}\\
  H.M. Kat and R.C.A. Oomen (2006)
\end{book}

Commodities do not offer a risk premium: the average return is not
significantly different from zero; the returns depend (slightly) on
the business cycle, on monetary conditions, on the futures curve
(upward sloping (contango) or downward sloping (backwardation)).

Commodities are not more volatile than stock, as measured by the
2.5\%--97.5\% range, the standard deviation, the GARCH(1,1) $\gamma =
\alpha + \beta$ parameter (it measures the persistence of volatility);
they are more volatile in backwardation; energy is more volatile in
recessions and less in expansion phases; volatility shocks tend to
persist ($\gamma > 0.9$).

Commodity returns are not skewed, when skewness is measured as
normalized third moment, as $L$-moment
$$ 
\dfrac{
  X_{.75} - 2 X_{.50} + X_{.25}
}{
  X_{.75} - X_{.25}
},
$$
as the normalized difference between mean and median
$$
\dfrac{
  \bar X - X_{.50}
}{
  \dfrac1T \sum\limits_{i=1}^T \va{ x_i - X_{.50} }
}
$$
or as the GARCH skew parameter.

All commodity returns have fat tails, as measured by the fourth moment, the $L$-moment 
$$
\dfrac{
  X_{.875} - X_{.625} + X_{.375} - X_{.125}
}{
  X_{.75} - X_{.25}
},
$$
a ratio of fractile ranges
$$
\dfrac{
  X_{.975} - X_{.025}
}{
  X_{.75} - X_{.25}
}
$$
or the number of degrees of freedom of a GARCH(1,1) model with Student innovations.

Commodity returns are autocorrelated.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{What every investor should know\\ about commodities\\part 2: multivariate return analysis}\\
  H.M. Kat and R.C.A. Oomen (2006)
\end{book}

Commodity returns are correlated within groups but not between groups, except ``softs''.

Commodity returns are weakly correlated with stock or bond returns, as
measured by correlation or Kendall's tau, but this depends on the
business cycle; there is little persistence in correlation dynamics,
as measured by a GARCH(1,1)-DCC model, except metals.

There is tail dependence, as measured by the symmetrized Joe Clayton
copula (SJC), which has two parameters for tails and head dependence,
within groups but not between groups; there is no difference between
head and tail dependence: correlation is sufficient.

There is no tail dependence between commodities and stocks or bonds.

Commodity futures are correlated with unexpected inflation, as
measured by inflation minus interest rate or change in inflation.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On more robust estimation of skewness\\ and kurtosis: simulation and application to the S\&P500 index}\\
  T.H. Kim and H. White (2003)
\end{book}

One can define quantile-based measures of skewness:
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{SK}_1(\alpha) &= \dfrac{
    X_{1-\alpha} -  2 X_{.5} + X_\alpha
  }{
    X_{1-\alpha} -  X_\alpha
  } \\
  \textrm{SK}_2 &= \dfrac{
    \displaystyle \int_0^{1/2} (X_{1-\alpha} -  2 X_{.5} + X_\alpha) d \alpha
  }{
    \displaystyle \int_0^{1/2} (X_{1-\alpha} -  X_\alpha) d \alpha
  } \\
  &= \dfrac{ E[X] - X_{.5} }{ E[ \va{ X - X_{.5} } ] } \\
  \textrm{SK}_3 &= \dfrac{ E[X] - X_{.5} }{ \sigma(X) }
\end{align*}
(SK$_3$ is not compatible with ``skewness ordering'' -- but this notion is not defined)
and kurtosis (those measures are usually corrected by substracting the value for a gaussian distribution):
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{KR}_1 &= \dfrac{ 
    (X_{7/8} - X_{5/8}) + (X_{3/8} - X_{1/8}) 
  }{ 
    X_{3/4} - X_{1/4} 
  } \\
  \textrm{KR}_2(\alpha, \beta) &= 
  \dfrac{
    E[ X | X > X_{1-\alpha} ] - E[ X | X < X_\alpha ] 
  }{
    E[ X | X > X_{1-\beta}  ] - E[ X | X < X_\beta  ] 
  } \\
  \textrm{KR}_2(\alpha, \beta) &= 
  \dfrac{ X_{1-\alpha} - X_\alpha }{ X_{1-\beta} - X_\beta }
\end{align*}
(with $\alpha=0.05$, $\beta=.5$ in KR$_2$ 
and $\alpha=0.025$, $\beta=.25$ in KR$_3$).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Significance tests harm progress in forecasting}\\
  J.S. Armstrong (2005)
\end{book}

Significance tests should be avoided: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item readers and authors misinterpret them;
\item the choice of the null hypothesis is problematic;
\item there is a publication bias agains non-rejection of the null;
\item it distracts the authors from other issues: they often fail to
  assess importance (with \emph{effect sizes} or confidence (with
  \emph{confidence intervals}).
\end{compactitem}

Furthermore, if you have enough data, everything is statistically
significant.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting with many predictors}\\
  J.H. Stock and M.W. Watson (2005)
  Handbook of economic forecasting
\end{book}

The authors review several classes of forecasting algorithms: forecast
combination (without any discussion of bagging or boosting), bayesian
model averaging (BMA, rarely used, but better than an equal-weighted
combination of predictors), \emph{empirical Bayes methods} (the prior
is not a prior, it comes from the data) and dynamic factor models (no
mention of biased estimators or regularization paths).

In a \emph{dynamic factor model} (DFM), unobserved AR factors $\textbf{f}$ and
their lags explain the observed variables $X_{it}$:
\begin{align*}
  & \textbf{f}_t + \Gamma_1 \textbf{f}_{t-1} + \cdots + \Gamma_k \textbf{f}_{t-k} = \boldsymbol{\eta}_t \\
  & X_{it} = \lambda_{i0} \textbf{f}_t + \cdots + \lambda_{i\ell} \textbf{f}_{t-\ell} + u_t \\
\end{align*}
while a (static) factor model would be 
$$ X_{it} = \lambda_i f_t + u_{it}. $$

Dynamic factor models can be reformulated as static factor models and
estimated by maximum likelihood; there are also
principal-component-analysis-based approximations.

The article interprets this model in terms of the \emph{spectral
  density matrix} without bothering to define it.

Dynamic principal component analysis (PCA) is similar; the
corresponding latent variables can be obtained by performing a PCA
dimension reduction on the spectral density.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Principal components at work: the empirical analysis of monetary policy with large datasets}\\
  C.A. Favero et al. (2002)
\end{book}

Comparison of two estimators of \emph{dynamic factor models}, based on
static (time-domain) and dynamic (frequency-domain) PCA (principal
component analysis): the performance is similar, but dynamic PCA is
more parsimonious.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Moment problems\\ via semi-definite programming:\\ applications in probability and finance}\\
  P. Popescu and D. Bertsimas (2000)
\end{book}

The Markov, Chebychev, Chernoff inequalities provide bounds on some
probabilities $P[X \in \Omega]$ given some moments or joint moments of
arbitrary random variables. More generally, one can try to solve the
following optimization problem:
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Maximize }  & E[ \phi(X) ] \\
  \textrm{such that } & \forall i \quad E[ f_i(X) ] = q_i
\end{align*}
for all random variable $X : \Omega \longrightarrow \RR^m $.

For instance, in finance, one can look for bounds on the price of an
option given the moments of the underlying and/or other option prices
(we only have to assume that the option price is the expected
discounted payoff under the risk-neutral measure; we do not know
anything about that measure except that it is a probability measure).

The \emph{feasibility} of that optimization problem can be expressed
in terms of positive semi-definitiveness -- e.g., in a
multi-dimensional context, the second centered moment (the variance
matrix) should be positive semi-definite.

In the \emph{dual} problem (under reasonable conditions, there is a
strong duality theorem), the expectation disappears but we have an
inifite number of constraints:
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Minimize }  & y' q \\
  \textrm{such that } & \forall x \in \Omega \quad y' f(X) \geq \phi(x).
\end{align*}

Fortunately, those constraints can often be expressed as semi-definite
constraints and the problem can be solved.

In higher dimensions, beyond the second moment, the problem of finding
optimal bounds is NP-hard.

This is a review article: there are more detailed publications by the same authors.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Second order cone programming approaches for handling missing and uncertain data}\\
  P.K. Shivaswamy et al.\\
  Journal of machine learning research (2006)  
\end{book}

In the support vector machine (SVM) binary classification optimization problem
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Minimize }  & \tfrac12 \n z ^2 + C \sum_i \xi_i \\
  \textrm{such that } & \forall i \quad y_i ( \langle w, x_i \rangle + b ) \geq 1 - \xi_i \\
                      & \forall i \quad \xi_i \geq 0
\end{align*}
uncertainty can be introduced by transforming the constraints into 
$$ 
P \bigl[ 
  y_i ( \langle w, x_i \rangle + b ) \geq 1 - \xi_i 
\bigr]
\geq 1 - \kappa_i.
$$
The \emph{Chebychev inequality} gives a worst-case bound on this probability,
provided you know the first two moments of $x$.

This can be generalized to classification into more than two groups
and to regression.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Applications of second order cone programming}\\
  M.S. Lobo et al. (1998)
\end{book}

Many optimization problems can be recast as second order cone programs
(SOCP): quadratically-constrained quadratic programs (QCQP),
optimization problems in which the objective is a sum or max of norms;
FIR (finite impulse response) filter design, robust linear
programming, robust least squares, robust portfolio optimization.

\emph{Interior-point} methods adapted to SOCP are faster than those
adapted to the more general semidefinite programs (SDP).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Second-order cone programming}\\
  F. Alizadeh and D. Goldfarb (2002)
\end{book}

More detailed article on the same subject.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Introducing Mr. Risk}\\
  A. Meucci
\end{book}

Very simple (but readable and rigorous) article about portfolio
construction: variance matrix (\ie, risk model) construction,
optimization, warning on the confusion between linear and log-returns,
myopic allocation (multi-period asset allocation in the special case
where the weights are constant), portfolio insurance via delta
hedging, decomposition of homogeneous risk measures (e.g., the value
at risk) using \emph{Euler's formula}. 

The last chapter presents \emph{sensibility analysis}: after choosing a
few risk factors, you can express the future price of an asset as a
function of those risk factors; this can be approximated by a Taylor
expansion,
\begin{align*}
  \Delta \textrm{price} &= 
  \dfrac{ \partial \textrm{price} }{ \partial \textrm{risk} }
  \Delta \textrm{risk} 
  +
  \tfrac12
  \Delta \textrm{risk}'   
  \dfrac{ \partial ^2 \textrm{price} }{ \partial \textrm{risk} ^2 }
  \Delta \textrm{risk}.
\end{align*}

For instance, in fixed income, you can use time and yield as risk
factors; the corresponding derivatives are called \emph{carry} and
\emph{duration}
$$ 
C = \dfrac{ \partial \pi }{ \partial t }
\qquad
D = - \dfrac{ 1 }{ \pi } \dfrac{ \partial \pi }{ \partial y };
$$
this leads to the \emph{duration approximation}
$$
\Delta \pi \approx C \Delta t - \pi D \Delta y.
$$
Similarly, the \emph{delta approximation} is a first-order
approximation of the price change of a (vanilla) option using stock
indices.

Second-order approximations can be used to compute the value at risk
(VaR) of a portfolio.

The article ends with a detailed example: the risk of a portfolio of
mortgages.

