\end{book}

Benefits of functional languages in finance.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial econometrics}\\
  A.W. Lo (2007)
\end{book}

This soon-to-be-published book (this was only the preface) is a
collection of old articles. The table of contents is interesting,
though:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Models of asset returns: time-varying moments, fat tails, long memory, regime shifts, cointegration;
\item Asset pricing models: CAPM and its \href{http://www.google.co.uk/search?q=CAPM+regression+tests+elmar+merten}{tests}, multifactor models (APT), performance measurement, tests of market timing or security selection ability;
\item Dynamic asset pricing models: variance bounds controversy (?), equity premium puzzle, consumption-based CAPM, stochastic discounted factor model, incomplete markets, term structure;
\item Market microstructure: price discovery (walrasian tatonnement, etc.), price discreteness, irregular trading intervals, bid-ask spread, binomial ``trees'', state-space density estimation, price jumps, serial correlation, functional central limit theory (?)'
\item Non-standard finance: survivorship bias, data snooping, bayesian methods, event study, generalized method of moments, robust statistics, non-linear dynamical systems, neural networks, technical analysis, random matrix theory.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A jigsaw puzzle of basic risk-adjusted performance measures}\\
  H. Scholz and M. Wilkens\\
  Journal of performance measurement (2005)
\end{book}

Performance measures based on total risk 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Sharpe ratio;
\item RAP (risk-adjusted performance or \emph{Modigliani's $M^2$}): expected returns of the portfolio, leveraged so that its volatility equals that of the market portfolio;
\item DR$^{\textrm{RAP}}$ (differential returns based on RAP): difference in expected returns between the market portfolio and the portfolio, leveraged to have the same volatility as the market portfolio;
\item TRA (total risk alpha): difference in expected returns between the portfolio and the market portfolio, leveraged to have the same volatility as the portfolio studied;
\end{compactitem}
can be modified by replacing the total risk $\sigma$ by the market risk $\beta$: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Treynor ratio;
\item MRAP (market-risk-adjusted performance);
\item DR$^{\textrm{MRAP}}$ (differential returns based on MRAP);
\item Jensen's alpha.
\end{compactitem}

The authors explain those definitions with pictures.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Symmetric versus asymmetric conditional covariance forecasts: does it pay to switch?}\\
  S. Thorp and G. Milunovich\\
  Journal of financial research (2007)  
\end{book}

Multidimensional, asymmetric GARCH models in portfolio optimization can lower realized portfolio risk.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Model comparison of coordinate-free multivariate skewed distributions with an applucation to stochastic frontiers}\\
  J.T.A.S. Ferreira and M.F.J. Steel\\
  Journal of econometrics (2007)
\end{book}

Most multivariate families of distributions used to model skewness are
not coordinate-free: the coordinate axes play a special role, skewness
is only alloed in some (coordinate-dependent) directions -- those
families are not closed under orthodonal transformations.

Coordinate-free multivariate skew distributions can be obtained as
follows: choose $n$ univariate skewed distributions, apply an
invertible $n$-dimensional affine transformation.

Univariate skewed distributions can be obtained as follows, from a
symmetric distribution with pdf (probability distribution function)
$f$ and cdf (cumulative distribution function) $F$:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Hidden truncation: 
  $ s(x) \propto f(x) F(\alpha x) ;$
\item Inverse scale factor (scale the distribution by a different factor depending on the sign): 
  $ s(x) \propto f( \gamma^{-\textrm{sign}\, x} x) ;$
\item More generally, one can consider 
  $ s(x) = f(x) p( F(x) ) $
  for various choices for $p$, e.g., 
  $$ p(u) = \dfrac{ u ^{\tau - 1} (1-u) ^{1/\tau - 1} }{ B(\tau, 1/tau) }. $$  
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Enjoy the joy of copulas}\\
  J. Yan (2006)
\end{book}

Presentation of the \texttt{copula} R package to sample from, plot or
fit gaussian, Student, Clayton, Frank or Gumbel copulas in any
dimension.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=972731}{\emph{Analyst recommendations, mutual fund herding and overreaction in stock prices}}\\
  N.C. Brown et al. (2007)
\end{book}

Mutual funds herd after consensus analyst changes -- exploiting their overreaction can earn you 6\% per year.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A robust version of the KPSS test based on indicators}\\
  R.M. de Jong\\
  Journal of econometrics (2006)
\end{book}

In the KPSS test, instead of demeaning the data 
$x_i \leftarrow x_i - \bar x$, just use 
$x_i \leftarrow \textrm{sign}(x_i - \textrm{median}\,x)$:
this does not change the asymptotic distribution, relaxes assumptions
about the moments and increases the power of the test in presence of
fat tails (but reduces it otherwise).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate archimedian copulas, d-monotone functions and $\ell_1$-norm symmetric distributions}\\
    A.J. McNeil and J. Ne\v slehov\'a
\end{book}

Archimedian copulas are exactly the $\ell_1$-symmetric copulas that avoid the origin. 

The article also characterizes functions that generate $d$-dimensional archimedian copulas. 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=988998}{\emph{On the interaction between ultra-high frequency measures of volatility}}\\
  G.M. Gallo and M. Velucchi (2007)
\end{book}

The realized variance (\ie, variance estimated from tick data) 
$\sum x_t^2$ and the \emph{bipower variance}
$ \sum \va{ x_t x_{t-1} } $, robust to jumps,
are almost undistinguishable in the context of \emph{multiplicative
  error models} (MEM), \ie, GARCH-like models used to model positive
quantities (volume, duration, range, etc.).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating copula functions from limited data}\\
  D. Allwright et al. (2006)
\end{book}

The problem of estimating the value at risk (VaR) of a random variable
without any distributional assumtion (or when the distribution has too
many parameters, say, a 2-parameter Pareto distribution or a mixture
of gaussians or exponentials) remains the same in higher dimension: it
is impossible when $p^n > \alpha$, where $p$ is the quantile to
estimate, $1-\alpha$ is the confidence level and $n$ the number of
observations (say, $p=0.99$, $\alpha=0.05$).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=970358}{\emph{Zero-intelligence realized variance estimation}}
  J. Gatheral and R.C.A. Oomen (2007)
\end{book}

Realized variance estimators (\ie, variance estimators using high
frequency data) should avoid market microstructure artefacts such as
the \emph{bid-ask bounce} -- one can do better than aggregate the
data, \ie, throw away 99\% of it. The article compares twenty such
estimators on simulated data (using the \emph{zero-intelligence limit
  order book model}: orders arrive at random, following a Poisson
distribution, and are randomly cancelled).
Their conclusions are:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Do not forget to correct for any bias in your estimator;
\item Prefer mid-quotes to trade prices, they are much less noisy;
\item Use subsampling or kernel estimators (a \emph{kernel variance
    estimator} is a linear combination of $\cov(x_t,x_t)$,
  $\cov(x_t,x_{t-1})$, $\cov(x_t,x_{t-2})$, etc., with well-chosen
  decreasing coefficients).
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=968576}{\emph{Issues in creating a sentiment measurement based on internet stock message boards}}\\
  Y.C. Zhang and P.E. Swanson
\end{book}

Internet message boards (thelion.com, Yahoo finance, the motley fool,
raging bull, etc.) can be used to build a sentiment index; reply
messages should be given a lower weight (0.125); the \emph{maximum
  entropy text classifier} (not clearly presented) performs better
than the naive Bayes one.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Some methods for training mixtures of experts}\\
  P. Moerland (1997)
\end{book}

\emph{Ensemble learning} algorithms typically combine several learning
algorithms (or \emph{experts}) using fixed weights (the weights were
determined from the training sample but they do not change and depend
on each new observation).

On the contrary, you could have several experts controlled by a
\emph{gating expert} assigning weights to each of them, depending on
the observation to classify.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Voting with a parametrized veto strategy: solving the KDD cup 2006 problem by means of a classifier committee}\\
  D. Tikk et al.\\
  SIGKDD Explorations (2006)
\end{book}

Combine several (neural-network-based) supervised learning techniques
with a committee vote, using weights from the average performance of a
10-fold cross-validation test and the confidence value of each
algorithm, with a veto to control the maximum allowed error rate.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximizing classifier utility when training data is costly}\\
  G.M. Weiss and Y. Tian\\
  SIGKDD Explorations (2006)   
\end{book}

Formalization of what emergency medicine already does -- for instance,
this is one of the motivations of \emph{decision trees}, which use
\emph{few} variables.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio management using value at risk\\
  A comparison between genetic algorithms\\ and particle swarm optimization}\\
  V.A.D. Filho (2006)
\end{book}

Non-linear, multi-modal optimization problems, such as value-at-risk
(VaR) portfolio optimization (the article is limited to 50 assets)
can be solved by \emph{particle swarm optimization} (PSO) 
or \emph{genetic algorithms} (GA).

In PSO, particles (portfolio weights vectors) fly around and their
velocity is updated from their previous velocity (with a linearly
decreasing inertia), the best position encountered so far by the
particle and the whole swarm:
\begin{align*}
  v_{i, t+1} &= w v_{i,t} + c_1 r_1 (\textrm{best}_i - x_i)
                          + c_2 r_2 (\textrm{best}_{\textrm{swarm}} - x_i) \\
  r_1, r_2 &\sim U(0,1).
\end{align*}

Constraints can be taken care of in different ways: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{bumping}: when a particle hits a boundary, its velocity is
  set to zero (but the particles may become trapped near local extrema
  near the boundary);
\item \emph{random positioning}: when a particle hits a boundary, it
  is put somewhere else, randomly;
\item \emph{amnesia}: when a particle hits a boundary, it continues as
  if there were no boundary but stops recording the best position so
  far;
\item \emph{penalty} function.  
\end{compactitem}

In the GA, individuals are selected for reproduction by a
\emph{roulette wheel} (with a probability proportional to their
fitness) or by a \emph{tournament} (paris of individuals are selected
ar random and the best one is chosen); the crossover can be
\emph{basic}
$$
  (x,y) \mapsto (x_{1..k} \sqcup y_{k+1..n},\, y_{1..k} \sqcup x_{k+1..n})
$$
or \emph{arithmetic}
$$
  (x,y) \mapsto \bigl(px + (1-p)y,\, (1-p)x + py\bigr);
$$
the mutation operator is 
\begin{align*}
  & (x_k, x_\ell) \leftarrow (x_k + r,\, x_\ell - r) \\
  & k, \ell \sim U(\llbracket 1, n \rrbracket) \\
  & r \sim U(0,1).
\end{align*}

PSO is faster but GA are more robust.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic analysis of an agent-based model}\\
  A. Veglio and M. Marsili\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0705.4025}
\end{book}

An agent-based herding model.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Extending Black--Litterman analysis beyond the mean-variance framework}\\
  L. Martellini and V. Ziemann\\
  Journal of portfolio management (2007)
\end{book}

The authors extend the Black--Litterman framework to take into account
skewness and kurtosis: the simply replace the benchmark portfolio
(used to compute the implied alpha) by the minimum VaR (value at risk)
portfolio, where the VaR is computed from a Cornish--Fisher expansion
and the CAPM (capital asset pricing model) is replaced by a 4th moment
CAPM. The classifical Black--Litterman formula is then applied.

This is an approximation of Meucci's generalization: he considered the
whole returns distribution, not just its first moments. 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On dynamic measures of risk}\\
  J. Vcitani\'c and I. Karatzas (1998)
\end{book}

An agent has some initial capital $x$, some liability $C$ to be met at
time $T$ and tries to find a strategy $\pi$ so that his wealth at time
$T$ exceeds the liability: 
$$ X^{x,\pi} (T) \geq C. $$
He can do so privided the initial capital $x$ is large enough, 
$$ x \geq E_{\textrm{risk-neutral}} \left[ \dfrac{ C }{ S_0(T) } \right] $$
(where $S_0$ is the price of the risk-free asset).
If not, its risk can be measured as 
$$ \inf_{ \pi } E_{\text{real-world}} \left[ \dfrac{ C - X^{x,\pi}(T) }{ S_0(T) } \right]^+ $$
or even, to account for model uncertainty, 
$$ 
\sup_{ P \in \mathscr P }
\inf_{ \pi } 
E_P \left[ \dfrac{ C - X^{x,\pi}(T) }{ S_0(T) } \right]^+.
$$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The Epps effect revisited}\\
  B. T\'oth and J. Kert\'esz\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0704.1099}
\end{book}

Stock correlations decrease as the sampling frequency increases: this
is the \emph{Epps effect}. The classical explanations: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item the maximum cross-correlation is the same but is not attained at a zero time lag;
\item asynchronicity of ticks
\end{compactitem}
are not sufficient. The article provides a physicist's explanation for
the remaining effect.

Also check \texttt{arXiv:0704.3798}, by the same authors.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Price discovery in the US stock and stock options markets: a portfolio approach}\\
  R. Holowczak et al. (2006)
\end{book}

Many articles try to guess the direction of the information flow
between the stock and option markets, \ie, in which market price
discovery occurs (with inconclusive results).

To remove the non-linear effects of options, the authors consider
synthetic forwards, \ie, portfolios made of a put-call pair, with a
linear payoff.

Price discovery seems to occur in stock markets, except in case of
unusual option trading activity.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Structural breaks in financial time series}\\
  E. Andreou and E. Ghysels (2006)
\end{book}

Not accounting for structural changes can lead to overcomplicated models.

Here are some partial-sum-based (CUSUM) statistics to test for a structural break:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $
\textrm{Fluctuation} = \dfrac{ 
  \bar X_{\llbracket m+1, n \rrbracket} - \bar X_{\llbracket 1, m \rrbracket}
}{ \sigma / (n-m) }
$;
\item $
\textrm{PS} = \sum\limits_{i=m+1}^{m+k} \left(X_i - \bar X_{\llbracket 1,m \rrbracket}\right)
$;
\item $
\textrm{PG} = \sum\limits_{i=1}^n X_i - \min\limits_{1 \leq i \leq n} \sum\limits_{i=1}^n X_i
$.  
\end{compactitem}

The asymptotic framework developped by Kuan, Hornik and Leisch to
detect structural changes in linear regressions can be adapted to
non-linear processes (GARCH, etc.) and can help determine various
thresholds beyond which those partial sum statistics should be
considered suspicious.

To spot multiple breaks, one can use \emph{penalized likelihood}
methods or, for stochastic volatility (GARCH, etc.) models,
\emph{minimum description length} (MDL)-based tests (no details given).

The bulk of the article is devoted to checking the conditions under
which those tests are valid.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Notes sur les mesures de performance}\\
  D. Herlemont\\
  YATS (2004)
\end{book}

The \emph{Sharpe ratio} is not compatible with \emph{strong dominance}
and can be manipulated (just write out-of-the-money puts);
non-gaussian arbitrage oportunities can have an arbitrarily small
Sharpe ratio.

A \emph{Bonnet--Nagot risk measure} $\pi$ satisfies:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $
  X \perp\!\!\!\perp Y 
  \implies 
  \max\limits_{\alpha, \beta} \pi(\alpha X + \beta X) 
  = 
  \pi(X) + \pi(Y)
  $
\item The maximum is reached for $\alpha = \lambda(X)$ and $\beta =
  \lambda(Y)$ where $\lambda(Z)$ only depends on $Z$.
\end{compactitem}

Up to an affine transformation, a Bonnet--Nagot measure of risk
coincides with the Sharpe ratio for gaussian distributions.

They can be characterized in terms of the log-Laplace transform
$H_X(\lambda) = - \ln E e^{-\lambda X}$ as 
$$ \pi(X) = \sup\limits_\lambda \mathscr{I} ( H_{\lambda X} ) $$
where $\mathscr I : \mathscr C^\infty(\RR, \RR) \longrightarrow \RR$
is linear and continuous.

Examples include: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The Sharpe ratio:
  $$\mathscr I_{\textrm{SR}} (H) = H'(0) + \dfrac12 H''(0)$$
\item The \emph{Hodges} (or \emph{Stutzer}) measure:
  $$ \mathscr I_{\textrm{Hodges}} (H) = H(1) $$
\item The Bonnet--Nagot measure: 
  $$ \mathscr I_{\textrm{BN}} (H) = H(1) + a H(\alpha). $$
\end{compactitem}

(The link with Acerbi's \emph{coherent} measures of risk is mentionned
but not explained.)

The Stutzer measure is actually the rate of convergence to zero of the
ruin probability (studied in \emph{large deviation theory}); the
Stutzer criterion is a (riskier) alternative to the Kelly criterion,
which maximizes the rate of growth instead.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Information uncertainty and stock returns}\\
  X.F. Zhang (2004)
\end{book}

Greater information uncertainty produces higher returns following good
news and lower returns following bad news; ``news'' are measured by
forecast revisions or short-term momentum; uncertainty is measures by
firm size, fir age, analyst coverage, analyst forecast dispersion,
returns volatility, cash flow volatility. In other words: the more
volatile or uncertain the stock, the better momentum works.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The underlying dynamics of credit correlations}\\
  A. Berd et al.\\
  Journal of credit risk (2007)
\end{book}

The default of a basket of companies is usually modelled as follows:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Consider boolean, time-dependent \emph{loss variables} $Y_{it} =
  \textrm{1}_{ R_{it} \geq d_{it} }$, where $R_{it}$ are \emph{latent}
  variables and $d_{it}$ thresholds (e.g., $R_{it} = R_t$ is the time
  of default of company $i$ and $d_{it} = t$);
\item For fixed $t$, study the \emph{copula} of the latent variables (use a 1-factor gaussian copula);
\item Consider the \emph{loss distribution}, \ie, the distribution of $\sum_i \ell_i Y_i$.
\end{compactitem}

The \emph{correlation surface} $\rho(K,p)$ is the correlation
parameter of the gaussian copula that produces the same expected loss
for the tranche $[0,K]$ and default probability $p$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Pricing forward contracts in power markets by the certainty equivamence principle: explaining the sign of the market risk premium}\\
  F.E. Benth et al. (2006)
\end{book}

Explanation of the risk premium for commodities, from a 2-agent model
(consumer and producer), each with a different utility, and an
underlying with a stochastic price.

% Not read

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=982473}{\emph{Asset price dynamics with heterogeneous groups}}\\
  G. Caginalp and H. Merdan\\
  Physica D (2007)
\end{book}

Asset price dynamics can be modeled by a system of differential
equations, similar to the predator-prey systems in biology.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Generalized canonical regression}\\
  A. Estrella (2007)
\end{book}

Linear regression assumes that there is a single dependent variable
$$ y = \sum_i \beta_i x_i + \epsilon; $$
with several dependent variables, one can use \emph{multiple regression}
$$ \forall j \quad y_j = \sum_i \beta{ij} x_i + \epsilon_j $$
(often with some added structure to account for dependencies between
the $y_j$)
or \emph{canonical regression}:
$$ \sum_j \alpha_j y_j = \sum_i \beta_i x_i + \epsilon $$
(the left-hand side is ``the most predictable linear combination'').

The traditional assumptions of canonical regression are too
restrictive: this article relaxes them and derives the asymptotic
distribution of the coefficients, in order to design statistical
tests.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Forwards and European options on CDO tranches}\\
  J. Hull and Al. White\\
  Journal of credit risk (2007)
\end{book}

Options on CDOs, once the parameters (default probability, expected
recovery rate) have been estimated, are not that different from
vanilla European options.

The article also clearly recalls what CDS and CDO are:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item In a \emph{credit default swap} (CDS), the buyer makes periodic
  payments (quarterly in arrears) until default or maturity; in case
  of default (credit rating change, delayed payment, bankruptcy,
  etc.), the sellers pays the notional value times one minus the
  recovery rate (and the buyer makes a final accrual payment);
\item A \emph{collateralized debt obligation} (CDO) is a claim on a
  portfolio of CDS; the portfolio is cut into \emph{tranches} (the
  order statistics of the CDS);
\item iTraxx are CDO tranches on 125 European firms;
\item CDX are CDO tranches on 125 US firms.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Modelling asymetric exchange rate dependence}\\
  A.J. Patten (2005)
\end{book}

\emph{Conditional copulas}, \ie, the colula of $XY|Z$, can be used to assess
how the relation between two variables depends on a third.

(Instead of considering a copula depending on a an exogenous variable,
one could consider a higher-dimensional copula -- but the exogenous
variable likely plays a very different role.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The performance of model-based\\ option trading strategies}\\
  B. Eraker
\end{book}

Option pricing models should not be assessed by comparing predicted
and actual prices but by looking at the profitability of the
corresponding strategy (in efficient markets, these would be equivalent).

The weights in the resulting strategy depend on the price difference
and its sensitivity to estimation errors -- this is an optimization
problem.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Improved option pricing using artificial neural networks and bootstrap methods}\\
  P.R. Lajbcygier anf J.T. Connor\\
  International journal of neural systems (1997)
\end{book}

If you are unsatisfied with your option pricing model, just try to
predict its residuals with a neural network, a bootstrap or a bagging
algorithm.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-parametric determination of real-time\\ lag structure between two time series:\\ the ``optimal thermal causal path'' method}\\
  D. Sornette and A.-X. Zhou\\
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat/0408166}
\end{book}

The time-warping algorithm can be generalized in a fuzzy (Boltzmann) way to make it robust to noise.

The authors apply these results to the study of causal relations between macroeconomic time series.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On developing a financial prediction system: pitfalls and possibilities}\\
  S. Zemke (2002)
\end{book}

A bullet-point list of \emph{machine learning} ideas to be applied to finance.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Model uncertainty and forecasting,\\ a practitioner point of view}\\
  B. Bellone and E. Michaux (2006)
\end{book}

The article examined several ways of tackling model uncertainty:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Linear regression bayesian model averaging (BMA), good in the short term;
\item Dynamic factor models (DFA, \ie, regression on the factors of a
  factor analysis and their lags), good in the long term;
\item Median BMA (model obtained from the variables with an inclusion probability beyond 1/2);
\item General-to-specific (GETS) algorithm (a stepwise variable selection, with multiple steps and tests).
\end{compactitem}

The authors use a Scilab econometric toolbox, ``Grocer'', developped at the ENSAE.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Momentum, reversal\\ and the trading behaviors of institutions}\\
  R.C. Gutierrez and C.A. Prinsky\\
  Journal of financial markets (2007)
\end{book}

Cumulated residual returns (from a 5-year CAPM model) can help devise
a long-term profitable strategy, while the other component of the
momentum (``relative momentum'') reverts after one year.

The article builds a long-short portfolio on the signal 
$$ 
\alpha = \dfrac{
  \textrm{cumulated residual returns}
}{\sqrt{
  \textrm{cumulated variance of the residual returns}
}}.
$$

This effect is linked to institutional ownership.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Macroeconomic factors\\ and Japan's industry risk}\\
  P. Nguyen\\
  Journal of multinational financial management (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{risk} of an industry can be defined as 
the average cash flow, weighted by firm size, divided by the (equal-weighted) volatility of those cash flows.
\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Analyzing digits for portfolio formation\\ and index tracking}\\
  P.N. Posch and W.A. Kreiner
\end{book}

The Newcomb--Benford law (or \emph{first-digit law}),
$$
  P[X_1 = d] = \log_{10}\left( 1 + \dfrac1d \right), \qquad d \in \llbracket 1,9 \rrbracket
$$
is already used to
detect fraud in tax sheets and company reports.

The authors put this distribution in a 1-parameter family
$$
  P_d(\lambda) \propto (d+1)^{1-\lambda} - d^{1-\lambda}
$$
and compute this parameter, the \emph{digital-fit factor} (DFF) for
the price of the members of an index: it moves in the opposite
direction as the index (the incorrectly use correlation to assess this
-- the time series are integrated).

They then try to use it to design an investment strategy, but their
strategy is merely a leverage-increasing strategy, and they even
forget to use their DFF...

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A statistical derivation\\ of the significant-digit law}\\
  T.P. Hill\\
  Statistical science (1996)
\end{book}

(Theoretical article on Benford's Law.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Synchronizing multivariate\\ financial time series}\\
  F. Audrino and P. B\"uhlmann (2001)
\end{book}

When combining data from markets in different time zones, care should
be taken: two time series $X$, $Y$ from (say) New York and London can
be seen as sampled at two different times, London close and New York
close, with every other value missing. This is a missing value
problem: models can be fitted using the EM algorithm.

The article applies this idea to the CCC-GARCH(1,1) model.

Alternatively, one can use one data point per day at a time when both
markets are opened.

With weekly data, the problem disappears.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlations and volatitilities\\ of asynchronous data}\\
  P. Burns et al. (1998)
\end{book}

Earlier article on the same subject.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Why risk and return are uncorrelated:\\ a relative status approach}\\
  E. Falkenstein (2006)
\end{book}

Expected utility models where utility is a function of the wealth
relative to other market participants predict there is no risk
premium.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Long-term dependence in stock returns}\\
  J.T. Barkoulas and C.F. Baum
\end{book}

Long memory (or ``fractal dynamics'') can be tested using:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Hurst's rescaled-range (R/S) analysis;
\item Lo's modified R/S analysis;
\item Geweke's spectral regression: in an ARFIMA model, 
  \begin{align*}
    & \Phi(L) (1-L)^d (Y_t - \delta) = \Theta(L) \epsilon_t \\
    & (1-L)^d = \sum_{k\geq 0} \dfrac{ \Gamma(k-d) }{ \Gamma(-d) \Gamma(k+1) } L^k
  \end{align*}
  the fractional integration parameter $d$ can be estimated as the
  opposite of the slope in the regression
  $$ \ln I(\xi) \sim \ln \sin^2 \dfrac{ \xi }{ 2 } $$
  where $I(\xi)$ is the periodogram of $y$ at frequency $\xi$.  
\end{compactitem}

Some stocks exhibit long memory, but the effects disappear when they
are aggregated into stock indices.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Residual-based tests for fractional cointegration: a Monte Carlo study}\\
  I. Dittmann (1998)
\end{book}

Comparison of many \emph{fractional} cointegration tests (Geweke,
modified rescaled range, ADF, etc.): prefer the Phillips--Perron
$T$-test, it is usually more powerful.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Symbolic representation of long time-series}\\
  G. H\'ebrail and B. Huhueney
\end{book}

To plot a very large and somewhat periodic time series, perform a fast
Fourier transform (FFT) to find the largest period, cut the time
series accordingly, cluster the chunks with a self-organizing map
(SOM) and use a different icon for each cell.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic portfolio selection\\ by augmenting the asset space}\\
  M.W. Brandt and P. Santa-Clara\\
  Journal of finance (2006)
\end{book}

You can replace the (difficult) choice of a dynamic strategy by a
static choice of managed portfolios; the authors use a constant linear
combination of ``state variables'' and maximize
$$ E \left[ (\theta z)' r - \dfrac \gamma 2 (\theta z)' r r' (\theta z)  \right] $$
where $z$ are the state variables, 
$\theta$ their weights, 
$\theta z$ the weight of the stocks, 
$r$ the forward returns,
$\gamma$ the risk aversion.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On default correlation:\\ a copula function approach}\\
  D.X. Li\\
  RiskMetrics (2000)
\end{book}

(Non-gaussian) copulas should be used to model the joint distribution
of time-until-default (some people use a binary variable indicating a
default in the next year, which is very imprecise) instead of the
correlation (as CreditMetrics does).

This can be used to price credit default swaps or first-to-default swaps.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Analyzing active investment strategies}\\
  M. Ammann et al.\\
  Journal of portfolio management (2006)
\end{book}

Analysis of variance (anova) can help find out which strategy (``style'') an asset manager is following.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Managing guarantees}\\
  M.A.H. Dempster\\
  Journal of portfolio management (2006)
\end{book}

The methods of asset-liability management (ALM), \ie, dynamic stochastic programming,
can be used to implement guaranteed strategies, thereby competing with portfolio insurance.

The technical details might be ``a challenge even for sophisticated users''.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Pairs trading: the new generation}\\
  S. Hartman et al.\\
  ABN Amro (2006)
\end{book}

A pairs trading strategy can be improved by selecting pairs with
attractive fundamentals (or even playing them against pairs with poor
fundamentals).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A liquidity-augmented\\ capital asset pricing model}\\
  W. Liu\\
  Journal of financial economics (2006)
\end{book}

The CAPM (capital asset pricing model) overlooked the \emph{liquidity premium}. 

To account for all the dimensions of liquidity (quantity, speed, cost,
price impact -- speed is often forgotten), the author develops yet
another measure:
$$ 
\textrm{LM}_x = 
\left( n_x + \dfrac{ \lambda_x }{ \textrm{turnover}_x } \right) 
\dfrac{ 21 x }{ N_x }
$$
where 
$x=1,2,12$ months, 
$n_x$ is the number of zero daily trading volume in the past $x$ months,
$N_x$ is the number of trading days in the past $x$ months,
$\textrm{turnover}_x$ is the sum of the daily turnover 
(number of shares exchanged divided by the number of outstanding shares)
over the past $x$ months, 
$\lambda_x$ is chosen so that 
$$ 0 < \dfrac{ \lambda_x }{ \textrm{turnover}_x } < 1 $$
for all stocks.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Equity portfolios generated by functions\\ of ranked market weights}\\
  R. Fernholz (Intech, 2000)
\end{book}

Functionnally-generated portfolios have weights of the form
$$ w_i = f(\textrm{MCap}_i). $$
They can be generalized to 
$$ w_i = f(\textrm{rank}(\textrm{MCap})_i). $$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=962329}{\emph{Parameters for estimation of entropy to study price manipulation in stock market}}\\
  Y.V. Reddy and A. Sebastin
\end{book}

Confusing article presenting various entropy estimators 
(approximate entropy, sample entropy) and applying them 
to price, time, quantity data.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio choice with jumps:\\ a closed form solution}\\
  Y. A\"it-Sahalia et al. (2006)
\end{book}

By imposing a factor structure on the asset returns, one can derive a
closed form solution to the portfolio choice problem for Levy returns.

(The article is technical, I have not read the details.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting exchange rates:\\ a robust regression approach}\\
  A. Preminger and R. Franck (2005)
\end{book}

Robust regression can also be used to fit autoregressive models. 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The PIN anomaly \\ around M\&A announcements}\\
  N. Aktas et al.\\
  Journal of financial markets (2006)
\end{book}

The \emph{probability of informed trading} (PIN) can be estimated from
the following model (add a few independence assumptions), via maximum
likelihood:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item $\alpha$: probability of information availability on a given day;
\item $\delta$: probability of a positive price change after an event;
\item $\mu$: rate of arrival of uninformed buyers and sellers;
\item $\epsilon$: rate of arrival of informed traders;
\item $\textrm{PIN} = \dfrac{\alpha \mu}{\alpha \mu + 2 \epsilon}$
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Fund manager use of public information:\\ new evidence on managerial skills}\\
  M. Lacperczyk and A. Seru\\
  Journal of finance (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{reliance on public information} (RPI), defined as the $R^2$
of the regression of holdings changes against analyst recommentation
changes, is a measure of a portfolio manager's lack of skill.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=968052}{\emph{On the use of data envelopment analysis in assessing local and global performances of hedge funds}}\\
  H. Nguyen-Thi-Thanh (2006)
\end{book}

Yet another article on the applications of data envelopment analysis
(DEA) to hedge fund comparison -- previous ones forgot the fees.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the relationship between changes in stock prices and bond yield in the G7 countries: wavelet analysis}\\
  S. Kim and F. In\\
  Journal of international financial markets, institutions and money (2007)
\end{book}

\emph{Wavelet correction analysis} (a time series of length $N$ yields
wavelet coefficients that can be arranged in a pyramid, $N/2$
coefficient on the first row, $N/4$ on the second, etc.; one can then
compute the variance (and covariance, if you have several time series)
of each of these rows) allows the authrs to examine short- and
long-term relations between stock and bind returns: except in Japan,
they are negatively related.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Mean-semivariance behavior: downside risk and capital asset pricing}\\
  J. Estrada\\
  International review of economics and finance (2007)
\end{book}

The CAPM (capital asset pricing model) can be augmented by a downside risk: 
$$ 
\textrm{return} = 
\alpha 
+ \beta \cdot (\textrm{market returns})
+ \beta_- \cdot (\textrm{market returns})_-
$$
The authors prefer to \emph{replace} the beta $\beta$ by the downside beta $\beta_-$

