Stealth traders tend to use medium-sized \emph{rounded} (500, 1000,
5000) transaction in an attempt to disguise their trades.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimizing benchmark-based\\ portfolios with hedge funds}\\
  I. Popova et al.\\
  Journal of alternative investments (2007)
\end{book}

To maximize the expected utility, the probability of outperforming a
benchmark, possible corrected with the expected shortfall, in a
non-gaussian set-up:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item generate $N$ scenarios; the authors model hedge fund returns as
  a mixture of two gaussians, designed to fit the first four moments,
  corrected for various biases (survivorship, autocorrelation, etc.);
\item optimize; this gives an upper bound on the true optimal value;
\item estimate the expected utility of the resulting weights on a new
  random sample of $N$ scenarios; you get a lower bound;
\item if the confidence interval given by those bound is too small,
  increase $N$.
\end{compactitem}

