  D.J. Disatnik and S. Benninga\\
  Journal of portfolio management (2007)
\end{book}

Comparison of several covariance matrix estimators (sample variance,
shrinkage with an optimized shrinkage coefficient, equal-weighted
portfolio of variance estimators, diagonal matrix): the shrinkage
estimators give better risk forecasts for the unconstrained minimum
variance portfolio but no estimator leads to a good forecast of the
standard deviation of the constrained (long-only) minimum variance
portfolio.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{How do performance measures perform?\\ Examining precision and stability}\\
  G. H\"ubner\\
  Journal of portfolio managemenr (2007)
\end{book}

Performance or risk measures such as \emph{beta}, \emph{Jensen's
  alpha} (the intercept of the regression of the returns against the
market returns), \emph{Treynor ratio} 
$$ \textrm{GTR}_i = \alpha_i B_\textrm{market} R / B_i R$$ 
where $r_i = \alpha_i + B_i R + \epsilon_i$, 
\emph{information ratio} (IR),
\emph{Sortino ratio}, Modigliani's $M^2$ (not defined), $\Omega$
can be compared with:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item the rank correlation;
\item the \emph{concordance correlation coefficient}
$$\rho = \dfrac{ 2 \sigma_{XY} }{ \sigma_X^2 + \sigma_Y^2 + (\mu_X - \mu_Y)^2 };$$
\item Cohen's Kappa, after binning the stocks into winner and losers
  for each measure (it estimates the number of matching pairs not due
  to chance),
$$ \kappa = 2 \dfrac{\textrm{WW} + \textrm{LL}}{N} - 1. $$
\end{compactitem}

The author prefers the Treynor ratio.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=985127}{\emph{Where do alphas come from?\\ A new measure of the value of active investment management}}\\
  A.W. Lo (2007)
\end{book}

Performance is traditionnally measured by ratios, 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Sharpe ratio} &= \dfrac{ E[ \textrm{excess returns} ] }{ \sigma(\textrm{excess returns}) } \\
  \textrm{Treynor ratio} &= \dfrac{ E[ \textrm{excess returns} ] }{ \beta } \\
  \textrm{Information ratio} &= \dfrac{ \alpha }{ \sigma(\textrm{noise}) }
\end{align*}
where 
$$ 
\textrm{excess returns} =
\alpha + \beta \cdot ( \textrm{market excess returns} ) + \textrm{noise} 
$$
(for our market-neutral strategies, $\beta = 0$, so the Sharpe ratio
and the information coefficient coincide).

The author suggests a decomposition of the expected portfolio returns
into a passive and a non-passive components:
\begin{align*}
  E[ \textrm{excess returns} ] 
  &= E\left[ \sum_i w_i r_i \right] \\
  &= \sum_i E\left[ w_i r_i \right] \\
  &= \sum_i \left( \cov(w_i, r_i) + E[w_i] E[r_i] \right) \\
  &= \sum_i \cov(w_i, r_i) + \sum_i E[w_i] E[r_i] \\
  \textrm{Active component}  &= \sum_i \cov(w_i, r_i) \\
  \textrm{Passive component} &= \sum_i E[w_i] E[r_i]
\end{align*}
$$
\textrm{Active ratio} = \dfrac{ 
  \textrm{active component} 
}{
  \textrm{active component} + \textrm{passive component}
}
$$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Exchange rates and the conversion of currency-specific risk premia}\\
  A. Eisenberg and M. Rudolf\\
  European financial management (2007)
\end{book}

In an economy without arbitrage oportunities, there is a positive
\emph{stochastic discount factor} (SDF) that prices all assets
$$ P_{i,t} = E_t \left[ \dfrac{ M_T }{ M_t } X_{i,T} \right] $$
where $X_{i,T}$ is the payoff of asset $i$ and $M_T/M_t$ is the SDF.

In an international setup, there is one SDF for each currency.  In a
complete market, the exchange rate can conveert stochastic discount
factors accross currencies (in incomplete markets, the SDF is not
unique).

