\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=958037}{\emph{Implied volatility from Asian options\\ via Monte Carlo methods}}\\
  C.-O. Ewald et al.
\end{book}

Many people calibrate the volatility surface using European options
and then use it with Asian ones (e.g., to compute their vega), 
oblivious to the dependence of the
volatility surface on the type of option.

The article uses the ``logarithm trick''
$$
\dfrac{ \partial E[ h(X_\sigma) ] }{ \partial \sigma } =
E \left[ h(X_\sigma) \dfrac{ \log f(X_\sigma, \sigma) }{ d \sigma } \right]
$$
(where $f(\cdot, \sigma)$ is the density of the law of $X_\sigma$)

to express the vega of an Asian option as a weighted expectation of
$h(X_\sigma)$, which can then be computed via Monte Carlo methods.

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimizing the retirement portfolio: asset allocation, annuitization and risk aversion}\\
  W.J. Horneff (2006)
\end{book}

Investment advice for retirement is either \emph{life annuity} (\ie,
the payment of the same sum every year until death) or \emph{phased
  withdrawal plan} (a normal portfolio, say 60\% stock, 40\% bond,
from which we take a fixed proportion for consumption every year --
the proportion can also be chosen as $1/T$, where $T$ is the maximum
or expected number of remaining years of life).

The article suggests a blend of the two. They do not seem to mention
\emph{portfolio insurance}.

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=981127}{\emph{A multivariate commodity analysis and applications to risk management}}\\
  R.H. B\"orger et al.
\end{book}

Asset (here, commodity futures) returns are not gaussian and can be modeled
by a \emph{generalized hyperbolic distribution}, the mixture of a
gaussian and a \emph{generalized inverse gaussian}:
$$ X = \mu + W \gamma  + \sqrt W A Z $$
where $Z \sim N(0_k,I_k)$, 
$W \sim GIG(\lambda, \chi, \psi)$, 
$\mu, \lambda \in \RR^d$, 
$Z, A \in \RR^{d \times k}$.

This gives better value-at-risk and espected shortfall estimations.

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=956813}{\emph{The $m$ out of $n$ provisional call: an auxiliary reversed binomial tree approximation}}\\
  Q. Liu (2006)
\end{book}

A \emph{convertible bond} (CB) is a bond, with am option to transform it into a stock.

A \emph{hard call} is a CB which may be exercised anytime after (say) the first two years.

A \emph{soft-call} is a CB which may only be exercised if the
underlying stock closes above a prespecified trigger $m$ out of $n$
consecutive days.

Even though this is a path-dependent option, it can be priced with a
binomial ``tree'' (a lattice, really) without keeping all the path
histories in memory.

\begin{book}
  \emph{Composing contracts:\\ an adventure in financial engineering}\\
  S. Peyton Jones et al. \\
  Functional Pearl, ICFP (2000)
\end{book}

Haskell (or any other functional language) can be used to
\emph{describe} complicated financial contracts (options on options
on\dots on options, with complicated cash flows) -- the industry lack
such a precise notation -- and then to price them (but there are still
optimizations to be made; and one might prefer to use C for the final
computations).

