%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% 
%% 
%% Before my resignation (below)
%%

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Universal portfolios}\\
  T.M. Cover\\
  Mathematical finance (1991)
\end{book}

Given the history of total returns of a set of stocks $x_{tj}$, one
can compute, a posteriori, the best constant-weight (rebalanced)
strategy and try to beat it or at least to attain the same asymptotic
rate of growth, without peering into the futire but by allowing for
changing weights.

The author suggests, at each time $k$, to average all the possible
constant-weight strategies $\textbf{b}$, weighing them by their past
performance $S_k(\textbf{b})$:
\begin{align*}
  b(1)   &= \left(\dfrac1m, \cdots, \dfrac1m\right) \\
  b(k+1) &= \dfrac{
    \int_B \textbf{b} S_k(\textbf{b}) d\textbf{b}
  }{
    \int_B S_k(\textbf{b}) d\textbf{b}
  } \\
  S_k(\textbf{b}) &= \prod_{t=1}^i \textbf{b} ' \textbf{x}_t
\end{align*}
where $B = \{\, \textbf{b} \in \RR^m \ :\ \textbf{b} \geq 0,\ \textbf{b}' \textbf{1} = 1 \,\}$
is the set of long-only fully-invested strategies. 

This works, simply because the wealth of the strategy is the average
wealth of the experts, all of which have an exponential growth rate,
and an average of exponentials has (under suitable assumptions) the
same asymptotic growth rate as the maximum. Buy-and-hold strategies
are beaten because they are not diversified enough.

For $m=2$ stocks, the computations are easy. Using 
\begin{align*}
  \int_{\substack{b_1, b_2 \geq 0 \\ b_1 + b_2 = 1}} f(b_1,b_2) db &= \int_0^1 f(u,1-u) du \\
  B(1+x,1+y) &= \int_0^1 u^x (1-u)^y du
\end{align*}
one gets
\begin{align*}
  b_1(n) = \dfrac{
    \sum  \limits _{ P_1 \sqcup P_2 = \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket }
    \prod \limits _{t \in P_1} x_{t1} 
    \prod \limits _{t \in P_2} x_{t2} 
    B(2 + \va{P_1}, 1 + \va{P_2})
  }{
    \sum  \limits _{ P_1 \sqcup P_2 = \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket }
    \prod \limits _{t \in P_1} x_{t1} 
    \prod \limits _{t \in P_2} x_{t2} 
    B(1+ \va{P_1}, 1 + \va{P_2})
  }
\end{align*}
where $P_1 \sqcup P_2 = \llbracket 1, n \rrbracket$ are the partitions of 
$\llbracket 1, n \rrbracket$ in two parts. The beta function is
related to the binomial coefficients and can be computed in the same
way.

In dimension $m$, the computations are similar, with a ``multivariate
beta function'' whose computations require an $m$-dimensional array.
Since the sums have $m^n$ terms, the complexity is
$O(m^n + n^m)$. Last, but not least, the important terms in those huge 
sums are very close to one: numerical instability prevents us from
doing the computations in this way. (Since these are high-dimensional
integrals, monte Carlo methods (importance sampling) might be useful.)

There are no assumptions for this strategy (this is a worst-case
scenario), but the constant-weight benchmark is only optimal if there
is no time-dependence in the data, \ie, if the data are iid.

This is an expanding window strategy: with enough data, the weights
will hardly move -- this is not a dynamic strategy.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient algorithms for universal portfolios}\\
  A. Kalai and S. Vempala\\
  Journal of machine learning research (2002)
\end{book}

The definition of Cover's universal portfolio is exactly the Monte
Carlo Markov Chain (MCMC) setup:
$$ 
w_{\textrm{universal}} = \dfrac{
  \int w \, \textrm{Performance}(w) \, dw
}{
  \int   \textrm{Performance}(w) \, dw
}.
$$
To prove the convergence of the chain, the authors do not integrate
over the simplex of all portfolios but limit themselves to those all
of whose weights are at least $\delta_0$ and they slightly modify the
performance function.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Nonparametric kernel-based sequential investment strategies}\\
  L. Gy\"orfi et al. (2005)
\end{book}

In contrast with Cover's approach, the authors consider a stationary
and ergodic market: there can be time-dependency; their benchmark is
the \emph{log-optimal portfolio}
$$ b_n = \argmax E \bigl[ \log (b'_n x_n) \,|\, x_{-\infty  \to n-1} \bigr]. $$
A \emph{universal strategy} is a strategy that attains the same
(asymptotic) rate of return without knowing the distribution.

The \emph{histogram-based strategy} is a performance-weighted average
of elementary strategies or \emph{experts}; an expert is the best
strategy, over windows of size~$k$, according to the past history,
corresponding to a discretization $\ell$ of the past returns. They
consider all possible window sizes and the discretizations are defined
from nested partitions of the space of returns, so that the size of
the cells uniformly converge to zero.

(This is not implementable: most of the cells would be empty, unless
you have a humongous amount of data; it will converge very slowly.)

The \emph{kernel-based strategy} replaces the rigid partitions by a
moving window:
$$
  b^{(k,\ell)} ( x_{ 1 \to n-1 }, s ) = 
  \argmax_{\substack{b \geq 0 \\ b' 1 = 1}}
  \prod\limits_{\substack{ k < i < n \\ \n{ x_{ i-k \to i-1 } - s } \leq c / \ell }}
  b' x_i
$$
where $x_{ 1 \to n-1 }$ is the whole history and 
$s = x_{ n-k \to n-1 }$ is the recent history.

The \emph{general kernel-based strategy} is 
\begin{align*}
  b^{(k,\ell)} ( x_{ 1 \to n-1 }, s ) &= 
  \argmax_{\substack{b \geq 0 \\ b' 1 = 1}}
  \dfrac{
    \displaystyle\sum_i w_i^{(k,\ell)} \log b' x_i
  }{
    \displaystyle\sum_i w_i^{(k,\ell)}
  }
  \\
  w_i^{(k,\ell)} &= K_k( \ell \cdot (x_{ i-k \to x_{i-1}} - s))
\end{align*}
where $K_k$ is a kernel (on $\RR^{kd}$, where $d$ is the number of assets), such as 
$K_k = \mathbb{1}_{ \n{x} \leq c }$.

This is an expanding window strategy; the stationarity assumption
makes it non-robust to structural changes.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Universal portfolios with side information}\\
  T.M. Cover\\
  IEEE transactions on information theory (1996)
\end{book}

In the definition of Cover's universal portfolio,
$$ 
w_{\textrm{universal}} = \dfrac{
  \int w \, \textrm{Performance}(w) \, dw
}{
  \int   \textrm{Performance}(w) \, dw
}
$$
one can replace the uniform distribution $dw$ by a
Dirichlet($\tfrac12,\dots,\tfrac12$) prior -- the worst-case
performance is slightly better and, this being \ (bayesian)
\emph{conjugate} distribution, the computations are marginally faster.

Uniform or Dirichlet universal portfolios attain their worst-case
performance for \emph{Kelly market sequences}, \ie, when, in each
period, all stocks but one go bankrupt (similar to horse races, where
a single horse wins).

This article also explains how to use side information: the benchmark
no longer has constant weights but the weights are the function 
$$ \textrm{side information} \longmapsto \textrm{weights} $$
that maximizes the total returns on the period. The corresponding
universal weights are
$$ 
w_{\textrm{universal}}(y) = \dfrac{
  \int w \, \textrm{Performance}(w|y) \, dw
}{
  \int   \textrm{Performance}(w|y) \, dw
}
$$
where $y$ is the side information.

Universal portfolios can also be used to combine experts: if the
experts have constant weights, just use them instead of all the
constant reweighted strategies (the integrals become sums); even
better, consider them as new assets, add them to the universe and
compute the universal portfolio of this enlarged universe.

The article ends with some details about the exact computation of
those portfolios, in the Dirichlet case -- this is still exponential.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Gestion de portefeuille et croissance optimale}\\
  D. Herlemont (2004)\\
  \url{http://yats.com/}
\end{book}

The \emph{Kelly criterion} invests the same fraction of your wealth in
a game (e.g., a coin-tossing game where you receive twice the value of
your bet when you win); the fraction is chosen so as 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item to maximize the long-term growth rate 
  $ \dfrac1t \log \dfrac{W_t}{W_0} $;
\item to maximize the expectation of the logarithmic utility $u(W) = \log W$
  (log-optimal portfolios are sometimes called \emph{Kelly portfolios});
\item to maximize the median wealth;
\item to minimize the expected time to reach a given wealth;
\item to minimize the entropy (?).
\end{compactenum}

The Kelly criterion can be generalized to any binomial setting (you
earn $\alpha$ on tails and $\beta$ on heads, as in \emph{bracket
  tracking}, \ie, profit taking and stop-loss rules) or for any
distribution of returns.

Here are some more generalizations of the Kelly strategy: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item use power utility: this is the \emph{fractional Kelly
    criterion};
\item impose a constraint on the maximum acceptable drawdown and only
  reinvest a constant fraction of the wealth in excess of the drawdown;
\item maximize the growth rate of the utility: 
  $$ \liminf \dfrac 1t E\bigl[ \textrm{utility}\, W(t) \bigr] ; $$
\item add transaction costs -- but the non-trading intervals can be
  very large and the rebalancing frequency very low (several years);
\end{compactenum}

The Kelly criterion might still work if the asset has a negative
trend: it only requires that
$E[ \textrm{returns} ] > 0$, 
while a positive trend is the more restrictive condition
$E[ \log \textrm{returns} ] > 0$; 
the Kelly criterion is a constantly-rebalanced strategy while the
asset alone is a buy-and-hold strategy.

The drawdown of a Kelly strategy follows a power law with exponent 2
(under a gaussian assumtion); even worse, assuming gaussian returns
when they are skewed or have fat tails leads to an excessive leverage
and larger losses.

The Kelly strategy is sometimes referred to as a \emph{volatility
  pump}: for a log-normal process without drift, the optimal growth is
proportional to the variance. D.~Farmer likens this phenomena to
tapping energy from waves or tides.

Cover's \emph{universal portfolios} are a non-parametric analogue of the Kelly criterion: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Cover averages the constant reweighted strategies, weighing them with their performance;
\item Gy\"orfi et al. average experts, \ie, non-parametric functions
  that use the returns in the past $k$ periods to produce suggested
  weights;
\item One could also average statistical experts, \ie, parametric
  statistical models (regression, GARCH, etc.) that map previous
  returns to weights.
\end{compactenum}

This article (mentions but does not) present a generalization of
Gy\"orfi et al.'s non-parametric experts without a long-only constraint.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal gambling systems for favorable games}\\
  L. Breiman (1961)
\end{book}

In a favorable game, the \emph{Kelly principle} bets each time the
same fraction of the current wealth, chose so as to maximize the rate
or growth or, equivalently, the expected logarithmic utility.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A new interpretation of information rate}\\
  J.L. Kelly (1956)
\end{book}

Kelly's original, information-theoretic article.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Can we learn to predict the best stock?}\\
  A. Borodin et al.\\
  Journal of artificial intelligence research (2004)
\end{book}

The authors add a new strategy to the list of portfolio selection
algorithms:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item \emph{exponentiated gradient}: greedily choose the best
  portfolio for yesterday's market with a penalty for moving far from
  yesterday's portfolio;
\item Cover's universal portfolio:
$$  
  b(k+1) = \dfrac{
    \int_B \textbf{b} \, \textrm{Performance}_k(\textbf{b}) \, d\textbf{b}
  }{
    \int_B \textrm{Performance}_k(\textbf{b}) \, d\textbf{b}
  } 
$$
where $d\textbf{b}$ is the prior distribution on the weights, for
instance a uniform or Dirichlet distribution;
\item algorithms that predict the best stock, such as the prediction
  component of the Lempel-Ziv compression algorithm.
\end{compactenum}

Choose a window size $w$, consider the stock log-returns in the
previous two non-overlapping windows $x_{-1}$ and $x_{-2}$ and compute
the cross-correlation matrix $\cor(x_{-2}, x_{-1})$. If stock $i$
outperformed stock $j$ in the most recent period and if the
log-returns of $i$ in period $-2$ are correlated with those of $j$ in
period $-1$, shift your investment from stock $i$ to stock $j$ by a
proportion
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{claim}_{i \to j} &= 0 
  \quad \textrm{unless } \mu_{-1}(i) > \mu_{-1}(j) \\
  &\hphantom{\ensuremath{=0\quad}\textrm{unless }} \llap{\textrm{and }}   
  \cor( x_{-2}, x_{-1} )(i,j) \\
  \textrm{claim}_{i \to j} &= \cor( x_{-2}, x_{-1} ) (i,j) > 0 \\
                           & \quad + \va{ \cor( x_{-2}, x_{-1} )(i,i) _- } \\
                           & \quad + \va{ \cor( x_{-2}, x_{-1} )(j,j) _- }
\end{align*}

The strategy is too sensitive to the choice of the window size $w$,
This can be improved on:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Consider an equal-weighted average of all those strategies for 
$w \in \llbracket 2, 50 \rrbracket$;
\item Instead of applying the strategy on stocks
  $\textsc{Anticor}_w(x_1,\dots,x_n)$, apply it on strategies:
  $\textsc{Anticor}_w(\textsc{Anticor}_2,\dots,\textsc{Anticor}_{50})$.  
\end{compactitem}

I would also try to change ``correlation'' into ``cointegration''.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the competitive theory and\\ practice of portfolio selection}\\
  A. Borodin et al.\\
  Proceedings of the 4th Latin American\\ Symposium on Theoretical Informatics (2000)
\end{book}

After an information-theoretic review of the portfolio selection problem, 
the authors present several prediction-based algorithms: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item the \emph{add-beta prediction rule}, used to predict the next
  element of a binary sequence
  $$\hat P[ X_{T+1} ] = \dfrac{ \beta + \sharp \{ X_i = 0, \ 1 \leq i \leq T \} }{ 2 \beta + T }$$
  can be used to predict the best performing stock - it is competitive with 
  Cover's universal portfolio, but not universal (bad worst-case
  performance) and the convergence is likely to be even slower,
  especially for large markets (for which a lot of information is discarded);
\item Lempel-Ziv trading.
\end{compactitem}

Transaction costs do not affect the theoretical, asymptotic results,
but on real data, they are a real problem; you can sometimes have
decent results by rebalancing every month instead of every day,
though.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On-line portfolio selection\\ using multiplicative updates}\\
  D.P. Helmbold et al.\\
  Mathematical finance (1998)
\end{book}

One can greedily choose the best portfolio for yesterday's market with a
penalty for moving far from yesterday's portfolio, according to the
\emph{entropy distance}. The exact optimization problem 
\begin{align*}
&\mathop{\textrm{Maximize}}\limits_{ \textbf{w}_{t+1} } 
\eta \log ( \textbf{w}_{t+1} ' \textbf{x}_t ) -
d( \textbf{w}_{t+1} , \textbf{w}_{t} ) \\
& d(\textbf u, \textbf v) = \sum_i u_i \log \dfrac{ u_i }{ v_i }
\end{align*}
would be too long to solve at each step; instead, it is approximated as 
$$ 
w_{i, t+1} = \dfrac{
  w_{i,t} \exp \dfrac{ \eta x_{i,t} }{ \textbf w _t ' \textbf x _t }
}{
  \displaystyle \sum_j w_{j,t} \exp \dfrac{ \eta x_{j,t} \mathstrut }{ \textbf w _t ' \textbf x _t }
}
$$
(start with an equal-weighted portfolio). 
This \emph{exponentiated gradient} portfolio selection strategy is
linear in time and space and seems to have better
small-sample properties than Cover's.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient universal portfolios for past-dependent target classes}\\
  J.E. Cross and A.R. Barron\\
  Mathematical finance (2003)
\end{book}

Very technical article explaining how universal portfolios in
continuous time, without any stochastic model (just a couple of
regularity assumptions about the paths), has a formulaic expression
computable in $O(m^2)$ where $m$ is the number of stocks.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multivariate realized stock market volatility}\\
  G.H. Bauer and K. Vorkink (2006)
\end{book}

High frequency data allows us to compute the \emph{realized volatility
  matrix} without using a moving, overlapping window or a statistical
model.

Many variance matrix estimators are plagued by the
positive-definiteness condition, which is non-trivial to impose: the
article suggests to estimate the matrix logarithm of the variance
matrix instead -- the \emph{matrix exponential} of a real symmetric
matrix is positive definite and every positive definite matrix can be
uniquely obtained in this way.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Construction of multivariate copulas\\ and the compatibility problem}\\
  C. Laforge
\end{book}

Examples of bivariate copulas abound but examples of multivariate
copulas are rare, besides the \emph{archimedian copulas} 
$$ C(x_1, \dots, x_n) = \phi^{-1} ( \phi(x_1), \dots, \phi(x_2) ). $$
The \emph{copula product}
$$ C_1 * C_2 (x,y) = \int_0^1 
\dfrac{ \partial C_1 (x,t) }{ \partial t }
\dfrac{ \partial C_2 (y,t) }{ \partial t }
dt
$$
can be generalized to produce a multivariate copula from $n$ bivariate copulas
$$
C(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \int_0^1 \prod_{i=1}^n 
\dfrac{ \partial C_i(x_i, t) }{ \partial t } dt
$$
or, in terms of copula densities,
$$ 
c(x_1,\dots,x_n) 
= \dfrac{ \partial^n C(x_1,\dots,x_n) }{ \partial x_1 \cdots \partial x_n }
= \int_0^1 \prod_{i=1}^n c_1(x_i, t) dt.
$$
For instance, the Gumbel copulas 
$$ C_i(x,y) = x y e^{ \theta_i \ln x \ln y } $$
give rise to an $n$-parameter copula
$$
C(x_1,\dots,x_n) = 
\dfrac{ 
  \prod\limits_{i=1}^n x_i ( 1 - \theta_i \ln x_i )
}{
  1 - \sum\limits_{i=1}^n \theta_i \ln x_i
}.
$$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Selecting copulas for risk management}\\
  E. Kole et al. (2006)
\end{book}

To choose a copula among gaussian, Student (another elliptic copula,
with more tail dependence and the strange property that the
zero-correlation Student copula is not independent) and Gumbel (an
archimedian copula (in particular, it is $\mathfrak S_n$-invariant)
with even more tail dependence), one can use the traditional
goodness-of-fit tests for distributions, Kolmogorov--Smirnov and
Anderson--Darling (a copula is a distribution on the cube $[0,1]^N$):
\begin{align*}
  D_{\textrm{KS}}^{\textrm{max}} &= \max _t \va{ F(\mathbf x_t) - F_0(\mathbf x_t) } \\
  D_{\textrm{KS}}^{\textrm{avg}} &= \int_{\mathbf x} \va{ F(\mathbf x) - F_0(\mathbf x) } dF_0(\mathbf x) \\
  D_{\textrm{AD}}^{\textrm{max}} &= 
    \max _t \dfrac{
      \va{ F(\mathbf x_t) - F_0(\mathbf x_t) } 
    }{
      \sqrt{ F_0(\mathbf x_t) ( 1 - F_0(\mathbf x_t) )}
    } \\
  D_{\textrm{AD}}^{\textrm{avg}} &= 
    \int_{\mathbf x} \dfrac{
      \va{ F(\mathbf x) - F_0(\mathbf x) } 
    }{
      \sqrt{ F_0(\mathbf x) ( 1 - F_0(\mathbf x) )}
    } dF_0(\mathbf x) \\
\end{align*}

(The \emph{tail dependence} can be defined as an asymptotic expansion of the copula in $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Why use QuantLib?}\\
  N.P. Firth (2004)
\end{book}

After recalling what free or open source software is, the author
reviews a few ``open source'' financial libraries: Premia (X, Inria,
non-free, closed to external contributions), Financial numerical
recipes (C++, GPL, weak project management), Martingale (C++ and Java,
GPL, weak project management) and Quantlib (C++, BSD licence, more
active mailing list but dominated by a single company).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=981016}{\emph{Hedging contingent claims with constrained portfolios and nonlinear wealth dynamics}}\\
  D. Ebmeyer (2007)
\end{book}

A \emph{super-replicationg strategy} for a contingent claim is a
strategy whose payoff dominates (\ie, is almost surely greater than)
the contingent claim. The initial wealth of this strategy is an upper
bound on the price of the contingent claim (one can similarly get
lower bounds) and can be used to \emph{hedge} the contingent claim.

This article examines what happens when the superreplicating strategy
has to satisfy some constraints (e.g., short-selling constraints).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal replication of contingent claims under portfolio constraints}\\
  M. Broadie et al.
\end{book}

Article on the same subject.

% Not read

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Extreme observations and non-normality\\ in ARCH and GARCH}\\
  R. Bali and H. Guirguis\\
  International review of economics and finance (2007)
\end{book}

Traditional GARCH estimators are extremely sensitive to outliers, which
produce biased coefficients and fat-tailed residuals; this article
explains how to correct for outliers.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Predictive systems:\\ living with imperfect predictors}\\
  L. P\'astor and R.F. Stambaugh (2006)
\end{book}

When trying to predict future returns, do not forget the time
dimension: also use the lagged returns and predictors.

See also: mixed data sampling (MIDAS).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=954249}{\emph{The information content\\ in implied idiosyncratic volatility\\ and the cross-section of stock returns:\\ evidence from the option markets}}\\
  D. Diavatopoulos et al. (2007)
\end{book}

Realized idiosyncratic risk (after removing market, size and
book-to-market) is a bad predictor of future returns; option-implied
idiosyncratic risk is better.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A note on the mean-variance analysis\\ of self-financing portfolios}\\
  Z. Bai et al. (2006)
\end{book}

Beware of optimization as a drop-in replacement for a statistical
procedure: even with large samples, it tends to create biased
\emph{estimators}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{You don't have to bother\\ Newton for implied volatility}\\
  M. Li (2006)
\end{book}

Implied volatility can be seen as a \emph{special function} (like
sine, exponential, Bessel functions, etc.) and computed in the same
way, e.g., with rational approximation.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=965649}{\emph{Stock market return, order flow\\ and financial market linkages}}\\
  J.M. Moberg and G. Sucarrat (2007)
\end{book}

Order flow imbalance (OFI) still works in small markets.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The similarity between\\ mean-variance and mean-Gini:\\ testing for equality of Gini correlations}\\
  E. Schechtman et al.
\end{book}

Here is one more risk measure one can use instead of the standard
deviation in a portfolio optimization: the \emph{Gini mean difference}
(GMD) of a random variabe $X$ is
$$ \textrm{GMD}\, X = E \va{ X_1 - X_2 } $$
where $X_1$ and $X_2$ are iid with the law of $X$. 

The article uses 
$$ \cov\bigl( X, F_X (X) \bigr) $$
instead; considers the (non-symetric) Gini matrix $\cov(X_i,F_X(X_j))$
where $X_i$ and $X_j$ are the returns of assets $i$ and $j$ and $F_X$
is the cumulative distribution of the pooled returns of all the
assets; and derives conditions under which ``mean Gini optimization''
reduces to a quadratic program.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=962359}{\emph{Random intersection graphs with tunable degree distribution and clustering}}\\
  M. Deijfen and W. Kets (2007)
\end{book}

An \emph{intersection graph} is a graph whose vertices are subsets of
a set $X$, with an edge between two subsets whenever they intersect.

A \emph{random intersection graph} can be generated as follows: start
with the set of vertices, assign a random weight (following a law you
can choose/tune) to each vertex, assign a subset to each vertex, whose
size is proportional to the weight, consider the corresponding
intersection graph.

This produces a wide range of random graphs. 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Primer on using neural networks\\ for forecasting market variables}\\
  S.A. Hamid (2004)
\end{book}

Neural networks, trained on futures and spot prices, are better
predictors of realized volatility (standard deviation of the daily
log-returns over the next two months) than implied volatility is.

The article also contains a literature review and a list of financial
applications of neural networks.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The value of transaction cost forecasts: another source of alpha}\\
  A. Coppejans and A. Madhavan\\
  Journal of investment management (2007)
\end{book}

Predicting transaction costs can increase your capacity.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=983209}{\emph{Predicting hedge fund failure:\\ a comparison of risk measures}}\\
  H. Park (2005, 2007)
\end{book}

Downside risk is a better predictor of failure than standard deviation.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=974421}{\emph{Hedge fund investment through piecewise linear regression and optimization}}\\
  F. Pan and B. Zeng (2007)\\
\end{book}

The size of a hedge fund has a non-linear impact on its return; it can be
investigated by a piecewise linear regression.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Timing ability\\ in the focus market of hedge funds}\\
  Y. Chen \\
  Journal of investment management (2007)
\end{book}

Hedge funds do time the market.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{How hedge fund beat the market}\\
  C. French and D. Ko\\
  Journal of investment management (2007)
\end{book}

Hedge funds do not time the market.


\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=937972}{\emph{Hedge fund risk dynamics:\\ implications for performance appraisal}}\\
  N.P.B. Bollen and R.E. Whaley
\end{book}

Computing the ``alpha'' of a strategy (say, a hedge fund) by removing
the linear effect of a set of standard risk factors (market,
book-to-market, size, etc.) cannot account for non-linearities or
structural breaks: this article uses \emph{optimal change point
  regression} to account for the latter (for the former, just use
non-linear risk factors, as below).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=965425}{\emph{Detecting structural breaks and identifying\\ risk factors in hedge fund returns:\\ a bayesian approach}}\\
  L. Meligkotsidou and I.D. Vrontos
\end{book}

Another article on the same subject.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Can hedge fund returns be replicated?\\ the linear case}\\
  J. Hasanhodzic and A.W. Lo\\
  Journal of investment management (2007)
\end{book}

Linear clones of hedge funds often have inferior performance, but they
are transparent, scalable and cheaper.

\begin{book}
  \emph{Hedge fund replication strategies:\\ implications for investors and regulators}\\
  W. Fung and D.A. Hsieh (2007)
\end{book}

Hedge fund performance can be replicated, in a linear way, from a
small set of \emph{primitive trading strategies} (PTS) such as (since
these are tradable, they are sometimes called \emph{asset-based style}
(ABS) factors):
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Call option on the market (to replicate a market timer);
\item Lookback straddles (to replicate trend followers, who buy at the low and sell at the high: a \emph{lookback call option} allows the owner to buy at the lowest price over the life of the option);
\item BAA corporate bonds minus 10-year treasuries (to replicate fixed income hedge funds); 
\item ``The merger fund'' (to replicate a merger arbitrage strategy -- no details given);
\item Swap spread (?), yield curve spread (?), mortgage spread (?);
\item US stock market;
\item Small minus large stocks (to replicate long-short equity strategies);
\item IFC emerging market index;
\item CSFB high yield bond index (to replicate distressed securities hedge funds).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Will hedge funds regress\\ towards index-like products}\\
  W. Fung and D.A. Hsieh (2007)
\end{book}

Detailed and badly typeset version of the above.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  Statistical properties of short term price trends in high frequency
  stock market data\\
  P. Sieczka and J.A. Ho\l yst\\
  \texttt{arXiv:physics/0703208}
\end{book}

High frequency data fails the \emph{runs test} (unsurprising, given
their long memory): with the amount of data, one can even study the
\emph{distribution} of the run lengths.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=972783}{\emph{Return predictability, economic profits\\ and model mis-specification:\\ how important are the better-specified models?}}\\
  Y. Han (2007)
\end{book}

Simulations show that having the ``right'' return prediction model
does not allow you to beat a good old VAR (vector auto-regressive
model) -- the choice of variables is important, though.

Furthermore, return \emph{sign} forecasts, (rather than return
prediction) are more profitable.

This article is a pledge for simple models.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=996080}{\emph{Implied correlation from VaR}}\\
  J. Cotter and F. Longin (2007)
\end{book}

In a gaussian setup, one can compute the correlation of two variables
$X$ and $Y$ from the value-at-risk (VaR) at level $\alpha$ of $X$, $Y$
and $xX + yY$ (\ie, a portfolio of $X$ and $Y$ with weights $x$ and
$y$): this is the \emph{implied correlation} -- it depends on the
level $\alpha$, the weights $x$ and $y$ (and also the frequency of the
data).

The article examines the distribution of the implied correlation (this
is just a family of estimators of the correlation) -- beware of the
bias.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=996484}{\emph{An empirical study of multi-objective algorithms for stock ranking}}\\
  Y.L. Becker et al.
\end{book}

Genetic algorithms can be used to simultaneously optimize several
goals: put the solutions on separate ``islands'', one for each goal,
and have some migrate from time to time.

This is very similar to \emph{data envelopment analysis} (DEA) but I
do not expect the algorithm to converge towards a uniquely defined (or
meaningful) solution; if the migration rate is well chosen, it should
converge to a (set of) point(s) on the efficient frontier, not far
away from the optimal solutions of the one-goal problems.

The algorithm is applied to a stock selection problem with the
following goals: information ratio (IR), information coefficient (IC)
and intra-fractile hit rate (IFHR), \ie, proportion of stocks in the
top (resp. bottom) decile that outperform (resp. underperform) the
average.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://ssrn.com/abstract=970486}{\emph{Portfolio selection using evolutionary computational techniques}}
\end{book}

This (author-less) article suggests to replace each return estimate by
three values (say, the median and the quantiles) and optimizes three
goals: maximize the middle value of the portfolio, minimize the
distance between middle and low, maximize the distance between middle
and high, subject to VaR-like constraints. The author is oblivious of
the dependency between the stocks; his use of \emph{fuzzy logic} looks
bogus, his use of \emph{genetic algorithms} is irrelevant (he combines
the three goals into one).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stable models\\ for the distribution of equity capital}\\
  R. Fernholz\\
  Intech (2004)
\end{book}

Stochastic portfolio theory can be used to model the evolution of the
distribution of market capitalization over time.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Diversity and relative arbitrage\\ in equity markets}\\
  R. Fernholz et al.\\
  Intech (2004)
\end{book}

A market is \emph{diverse} if the weight of its largest stock satisfies 
$$ 
  \exists \delta > 0 
  \quad 
  \forall t \in [0,T] 
  \quad 
  \mu_{(1)} (t) < 1 - \delta; 
$$
it is \emph{weakly diverse} if 
$$ 
  \exists \delta > 0 
  \quad 
  \dfrac1T \int_0^T \mu_{{1}} (t) \, dt < 1 - \delta; 
$$
it is \emph{asymptotically weakly diverse} if 
$$ 
  \exists \delta > 0 
  \quad 
  \lim_{T \to \infty} \dfrac1T \int_0^T \mu_{{1}} (t) \, dt < 1 - \delta. 
$$

In a weakly diverse market, there are arbitrage oportunities, e.g., invest 
$$ \dfrac{ p }{ w_1(0) ^ p } + (p-1) $$

in the market and $-p$ in stock 1, where $p \gg 1$. The weights are
so large that the strategy is not implementable; furthermore, there
are morket models, such as the \emph{Atlas model} (defined in a later
article by the same authors) that are almost diverse but with no
arbitrage opportunity.

The article also investigates the consequences on long-term option pricing.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Relative arbitrage in volatility-stabilized markets}\\
  R. Fernholz and I. Karatzas\\
  Intech (2004)
\end{book}

A more general condition than diversity for the existence of a
relative arbitrage.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic portfolio theory: an overview}\\
  R. Fernolz and I. Karatzas\\
  Intech (2006)
\end{book}

Clear and exhaustive review of stochastic portfolio theory.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stocks as lotteries: the implications of probability weighting for security prices}\\
  N. Barberis and M. Huang (2007)
\end{book}

\emph{Prospect theory} differs from expected (concave) utility theory:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The utility function is concave over gains and convex over
  losses; it has a kink (\ie, left and right derivatives are
  different) at the origin;
\item The investor does not maximize the expected utility but a
  weighted utility: the probabilities are transformed (but these are
  not subjective probabilities, just decision weights: the investor is
  perfectly aware of the objective probabilities); as a result, low
  probabilities are overweight: the investor wants both lottery and
  insurance. 
\end{compactenum}

Prospect theory is incompatible with first-order dominance, but can be
modified into \emph{cumulative prospect theory}, which applies the
weights to the cumulative distribution function. Often, one chooses
$$ v(x) = \left\{ 
\begin{aligned} 
  & x^\alpha             & \textrm{if } x \geq 0 \\
  & -\lambda (-x)^\alpha & \textrm{if } x < 0
\end{aligned}
\right.
$$
for the value function and 
$$
  w(p) = \dfrac{ 
    p^\delta 
  }{ 
    (p^\delta + (1-p)^\delta)^{1/\delta} 
  }
$$
for the weighting function. Psychological studies suggest 
$ \alpha = 0.88$, $\lambda = 2.25$, $\delta = 0.65$.

Under gaussian assumptions (more generally, in the absence of
skewness), cumulative prospect theory is consistent with the CAPM
(capital asset pricing model); however, the weighting function creates
mispricing for assets with skewed returns -- but the authors are not
convinced that it can be arbitraged away.

Cumulative prospect theory can explain that assets with a high
idiosyncratic skewness, such as IPOs, private equity, distressed
stocks, deep out-of-the-money options, are overpriced and earn a low
average return -- this leads to the \emph{volatility smile}.
It also explains why household portfolios lack diversification. 

To test (and use) that positively skewed stocks earn lower average
returns, one would need to forecast future skewness: past skewness
does not work, but cross-sectional industry skewness does.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Probability elicitation, scoring rules and competition among forecasters}\\
  K.C. Lichtendahl and R.L. Winkler (2007)
\end{book}

Competitive forecasters tend to report more extreme probabilities.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A forecasting model for stock market diversity}\\
  F. Audrino et al.\\
  Intech (2006)
\end{book}

To predict changes in market diversity and switch between a
capitalization-weighted and a diversity- (or, better, equal-) weighted
portfolio, one can use: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item an AR(1)-GARCH(1,1) model;
\item a \emph{generalized regime-switching} (GRS) model, 
  \ie, a mixture of AR(1)-GARCH(1,1)'s;
\item a \emph{generalized tree-structured} (GTS) model, \ie, 
  the analogue of a decision or regression tree whose leaves contain
  AR(1)-GARCH(1,1) models.
\end{compactenum}
The predictive variables are:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item 1-month US treasury bill rate;
\item 60-month zero-coupon bind rate;
\item CPI;
\item PPI of finished goods;
\item Index of "help wanted" advertising in newspapers;
\item Unemployment;
\item Growth rate of industrial production;
\item S\&P 500 monthly log-returns;
\item Large/small $\times$ low-/mid-/high-value portfolio returns;
\item Market returns minus 1-month treasury bill;
\item SMB (large minus small) and HML (high minus low value) returns;
\item Industry portfolios.
\end{compactenum}

They find three regimes, that can be interpreted with the Fama--French factors.

The \emph{diversity timing} strategy provides a ``significant'' 17bp
increase in annual returns (after transaction costs) when compared
with a monthly-rebalanced equal-weighted strategy.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Tree-structured GARCH models}\\
  F. Audrino and P. B\"uhlmann (2000)
\end{book}

The regression-tree idea can be applied to times series modeling:
put GARCH(1,1) or AR(1)-GARCH(1,1) models in the leaves.

The authors use S-Plus but do not provide any code.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating and predicting multivariate volatility thresholds in global stock markets}\\
  F. Audrino and F. Trojani (2003)
\end{book}

The tree-structured AR-GARCH model can be applied to national stock
indices, the conditional variables being local and US market returns.
There are usually three regimes: two driven by local information, one
by US information.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Growth optimal investment strategy efficacy:\\ an application on long run\\ Australian equity data}\\
  B.F. Hunt\\
  Investment management and financial innovations (2005)
\end{book}

The \emph{growth optimal portfolio} is not diversified: it only
contains 2 or 3 stocks. To increase diversity, one can regulalize the
sample variance matrix as in a ridge regression:
$$ V = V_{\textrm{sample}} + \lambda I. $$

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Incorporating estimation errors into portfolio selection: robust portfolio construction}\\
  S. Ceria and R.A. Stubbs\\
  Journal of asset management (2006)
\end{book}

Robust optimization usually considers the worst-case scenario in
ellipsoidal or box-shaped uncertainty regions, meaning that all the
errors have a negative effect. This article suggests to consider the
subset of those uncertainty sets where the errors cancel out.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Experiments in robust portfolio optimization}\\
  D. Bienstock (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{uncertainty sets} used in robust portfolio optimization are
often boxes or ellipses (some add the further constraint that the
deviations sum up to zero); this article presents two new shapes for
the uncertainty shares: one using fractiles (just specify how bad the
situation can be in each decile) and another using the
\emph{adversarial value-at-risk} (AVaR), that uses mixtures to model
the higher correlation during crashes (I am not sure it is different
from the VaR of a mixture).

The article also details how to efficiently implement those
optimizations.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Equity-style timing: a multi-style rotation model for the Russel large-cap and small-cap growth and value style indexes}\\
  B.G. Arshanapalli\\
  Journal of asset management (2007)
\end{book}

\emph{Market timing} often uses logistic regression to switch between
two market segments -- beyond two, use \emph{multinomial regression}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio choice\\ beyond the traditional approach}\\
  F. Pe\~naranda (2007)
\end{book}

Not very readable (if you do not already know what he talks about, you
will not understand it) but correct and fairly complete survey of
asset allocation, with all the formulas you may need. The topics include:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item the portfolio weights as a statistic, whose distribution can be
  studied (surprisingly, this was missing from Meucci's book);
\item the difference between \emph{posterior distribution} and
  \emph{predictive distributopm} (the posterior distribution is for
  the present, the predictive distribution is its projection to the
  investment horizon)
\item \emph{lower partial moments} (LPM) are linked to stochastic
  dominance (no details given) but are more computable and yield a
  complete order; mean-semi-variance optimization is a convex problem;
\item generalizations of the Sharpe ratio: \emph{Sortino ratio} (with
  the semi-variance), \emph{Omega}, \emph{rate of decay} $I$ (this
  comes from \emph{large deviation theory})
  $$ P [ \textrm{returns} _{ 0 \to T } \leq 0 ] \sim e^{ - I \cdot T } $$
  (but the Sharpe ratio gives a ranking similar to most alternatives);
\item log-utility is indifferent to the investment horizon;
\item the \emph{Black--Litterman} approach can be used to tilt a
  long-term strategy (strategic asset allocation or SAA) towards a
  short-term view (tactical asset allocation or TAA);
\item dynamic asset allocation (with insufficient details) and the
  \emph{intertemporal CAPM} (ICAPM).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The impact of EMU\\ on the equity cost of capital}\\
  G.A. Hardouvelis et al.\\
  Journal of international money and finance
\end{book}

European monetary integration can be modeled as a \emph{time-weighted CAPM}
\begin{align*}
r_{it} &= \lambda_{i,t-1} \beta^{\textrm{market}}_i r^{\textrm{market}}_t +
          (1 - \lambda_{i,t-1}) \beta^{\textrm{local}}_i r^{\textrm{local}}_t \\
\lambda_{i,t-1} &= \exp - \va{\gamma_i' X_{i,t-1}}
\end{align*}
where $X$ is a set of predictive variables: country effects are
becoming less important.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Performance and distress indicators\\ of new public companies}\\
  N. Beneda\\
  Journal of asset management (2007)
\end{book}

To choose among IPOs (initial public offerings), use Ohlsen's \emph{O
ratio} (a measure of bankrupcy risk, apparently the result of a
logistic regression, whose coefficients were set in stone 30 years
ago), the market-to-book ratio and the market share of the underwriter.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Long-term economic relationships\\ from cointegration maps}\\
  R. Vicente et al.
  \texttt{arXiv:physics/0701062}
\end{book}

Correlation is a cross-sectional distance, blind to dynamic phenomena:
it can be replaced by a measure of cointegration.

The author suggests to estimate the cointegration from the model 
\begin{align*}
  a_1 x_{1,t} + a_2 x_{2,t} + b &= \epsilon_t \\
  \epsilon_{t+1} &= \gamma \epsilon_t + \eta_t \\
  \langle \epsilon_t \rangle &= 0 \\
  \langle \epsilon_t ^2 \rangle &= \sigma \\
  0 \leq \gamma &< 1
\end{align*}
using bayesian computations and physicist's approximations:
\begin{align*}
  \hat\gamma &= \textrm{Argmax} \log \int p(\gamma | \epsilon) p(\epsilon | x1, x2) \, d\epsilon \\
  p(\gamma | \epsilon) & \propto 1_{[0,1]}(\gamma) \left[ \sum_{t=1}^{T-1} (\epsilon_{t+1} - \gamma \epsilon_t)^2 \right] ^{ - \frac{T-2}{2}} \\
  p(\epsilon | x_1, x_2) & \propto \epsilon_t - x_{1,t} \sin \hat\theta + x_{2,t} \cos \hat\theta \\
  \hat\theta &= \tfrac12 \arctan \left( 2 \dfrac{\langle x_1 x_2 \rangle}{ \langle x_1^2 \rangle - \langle x_2^2 \rangle}\right)
\end{align*}

The estimator $\hat \gamma$ can be seen as a measure of dissimilarity
and can be transformed (this can make the plots more or less
readable):
$$ d_\alpha(x_1, x_2) = \hat\gamma^\alpha. $$

The dissimilarity matrix is sometimes modified further (two times
series are similar if they interact with the other time series in the
same way -- this idea is also used in \emph{spectral clustering}):
$$ D_{i,j} = \sqrt{ \sum_{k=1}^N (d_\alpha(x_i, x_k) - d_\alpha(x_j,x_k))^2 }. $$

Before plotting (this is the \emph{heatmap}, common with bioarrays),
the rows and columns of the dissimilarity matrix $D$ can be reordered:
a greedy algorithm looks for a permutation matrix $P$ that minimizes
$$ \mathscr{F}(P) = \tr ( P D P' W ) $$
where the weight matrix $W$ can be chosen as 
$W_{ij} = \exp( \va{i-j} \sigma )$ (neighbourhood weights) 
or 
$W = XX'$ for some vector $X$ such that $X_i > X_j$ if $i>j$ 
(side-by-side weights): this is the SPIN algorithm.

Finally, one can apply a clustering algorithm such as SPC
(\emph{superparamagnetic clustering} -- I have no idea what it means).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Information dynamics\\ and origins of uncertainty}\\
  P. Garbaczewski\\
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat/0703147}
\end{book}

An uncertainty principle can be expressed in information-theoretic
terms, between $S(\rho)$ and $S(\tilde\rho)$ where
$\rho$ is a probability density,
$-\ln \rho(x)$ the \emph{surprise level},
$S(\rho) = \langle - \ln \rho \rangle$ the \emph{Shanon entropy}
and 
$\tilde\rho = \mathscr{F} \rho$ is the Fourier transform of $\rho$.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal execution of portfolio transactions:\\ a review}\\
  E. Kochieva \\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}

The market impact has a long-term component (information leakage) and
a short-term one (liquidity-demand-related price jumps), which add to
the background price dynamics.

Trade execution is a trade-off between \emph{market impact} (which decreases
with time) and \emph{timing risk} (which increases with time); it can be
evaluated with respect to a benchmark such as \emph{pre-trade} price (the
most often used), VWAP or post-trade price.

The \emph{VWAP strategy} trades a fixed percentage of the market value
in each period; the optimal trading rate can be estimated by a
quadratic optimization (\emph{efficient trading frontier}).

This static strategy can be replaced by a dynamic one, where the
trading rate is a function of the price, or by a full-fledged
\emph{stochastic programming} approach.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Continuing work on optimal trade execution}
  D. Bienstock\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}
One can consider a market impact model of the form
$$
  p_{t+1} / p_t - 1 = \alpha * \textrm{size} ^ \pi
$$
with a fixed (very small) $\alpha$ and $\pi$ varying in $(0,1)$
according to a Markov chain. The optimal trade execution strategy can
be obtained by dynamic programming (or \emph{approximate dynamic
programming}). This strategy is not robust to a misspecification of the
Markov transition probabilities -- assuming that $\pi$ is constant is
preferable.

One can also assume that $\pi$ is not chosen at random but by an
adversary (under some constraints to keep it around a certain value
$\bar\pi$; the corresponding optimal strategy is more robust.

One can also consider what happens when two traders compete.

Someone also suggested to try with a hidden Markov model (HMM), \ie,
with unknown transition probabilities.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  Design of an FX trading system using\\ adaptive reinforcement learning\\
  M.A.H. Dempster\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}

\emph{Recurrent reinforcement learning} (RRL), \ie, a recurrent
1-layer neural network, can be used to turn returns time series into a
buy/sell/wait order, so as to maximize a \emph{moving-average Sharpe
  ratio},
$$
  \dfrac{ \textrm{EWMA} ( \textrm{returns} ) }
        { \textrm{EWMA} ( \textrm{returns}^2 ) }.
$$
This used to work, one decade ago. One can try to improve the model as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Do not only consider past returns: add in technical indicators
  -- actually, this does not add anything: RRL already extracts all
  the relevant information;
\item Replace the transaction costs parameter by something larger than
  the bid-ask spread, so as to favour trades with larger returns;
\item Fix the instability in the neural network weights by shrinking them;
\item Update the weights twice at each step, \ie, replace
$
  w_t = f( x_t, w_{t-1} )
$
with
$
  w_t = f( x_t, f( x_t, w_{t-1} ) ).
$
\item Add a risk and performance management layer in the algorithm,
  with a stop-loss and a shutdown procedure (to stop investing when
  the algorithm becomes unstable after a regime switch); the suggested
  risk measure takes into account both total loss and the size of the
  individual losses (this is similar to Omega):
$$
  \Sigma = \dfrac{ \sum r_i {}_- ^2 }{ \sum  r_i {}_+ ^2 };
$$
\item Update the neural network weights at each step, but do not
  update the meta-parameters (transaction cost, trading threshold,
  etc. -- there are five of them) that often;
\end{compactenum}
The following improvements have not been tested yet:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Add other information, such as the order flow or the limit order
  book;
\item Use the algorithm on several currency pairs; generalize the risk
  control accordingly;
\item Apply the algorithm to market making instead of trading.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal liquidation against\\a markovian limit order book}\\
  P. Hewlett\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}
This technical talk combined limit order book models (cf. D. Farmer's
work -- I attended one of talks last month) with dynamic programming
to find the optimal way of executing a trade; some of the tools
required are similar to those used to price American options (?); the
robustness of the resulting strategy could be examined as in D.
Bienstock's talk.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Automated new content and algorithmic trading}
  P. Gagner (RavenPack)\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}
They turn news into numbers using a Bayesian spam filter -- unconvincing.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio optimization\\ with drawdown constraints}\\
  S. Uryasev\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}

Maximum drawdown, average drawdown and conditional drawdown at risk
(CDaR) can be expressed using piecewise linear functions and can
therefore be optimized with a linear program (scenario-based) solver.
The actual risk measure is not that important.

His company (AOrDa) sells an optimizer and he claims to run his own
money with it, using sample returns as an alpha (and Monte Carlo
simulations to estimate the risk); surprisingly, he is aware that this
momentum strategy does not perform that well because of reversal
effects.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Applying stochastic programs\\ to improve investor performance}\\
  J.M. Mulvey\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}
The title sounded interesting, but the talk was mostly empty:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item do not forget to rebalance (equal weights are good);
\item look for volatility (or diversity);
\item do not forget transaction costs;
\item do not forget to diversify your portfolio (using \emph{overlays}, if
  needed): with enough diversity and volatility, a \emph{momentum} strategy
  can be quite successful.
\end{compactenum}

The stressed difference between \emph{dynamic stochastic control}
(discretization of the state space, \ie, of the investor's decisions)
and \emph{multi-stage stochastic program} (scenario tree) was not
clear. The author suggests to analyze the result of those
optimizations to derive \emph{rules} that can then be back-tested.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Theory of acceptability indices}\\
  D. Madan\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}

To compare and choose among strategies, one can use \emph{scale-free
acceptability indices}, such as the Sharpe ratio and its
generalizations: these are functions $\alpha(X)$ of the distribution of
the returns $X$ of the strategy. An acceptability index is \emph{coherent} if
it satisfies the following properties:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item convexity: 
  $
  \alpha(X) \geq x, 
  \alpha(Y) \geq x  
  \implies 
  \forall \lambda \in (0,1) 
  \alpha( \lambda X + (1-\lambda) Y ) \geq x
  $
\item monotonicity, \ie, compatibility with strong dominance: 
  if $X \geq Y$ almost surely, then $\alpha(X) \geq \alpha(Y)$;
\item scale invariance:  
  $\forall \lambda > 0  \quad \alpha(\lambda X) = \alpha(X)$
\item continuity: 
  if $\va{X_n} \leq 1$, 
  $\alpha(X_n) \geq x$ 
  and $X_n$ converges to $X$ in probability, 
  then $\alpha(X) \geq x$.
\end{compactenum}
We would also like:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item second order monotonicity;
\item arbitrage consistency: 
  if $X \geq 0$ almost surely, 
  then $\alpha(X) = \infty$
\item expectation consistency: 
  if $E[X] < 0$, then $\alpha(X) = 0$;  
  if $E[X] > 0$, then $\alpha(X) > 0$.
\end{compactenum}

Here are a few examples:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The Sharpe ratio: 
  $$
  \textrm{SR}(X) = \dfrac{ E[X] }{ \textrm{Sd}(X) } \textrm{ if } E[X] > 0;
  $$ 
  it is not monotonic and not arbitrage consistent;
\item The gain-loss ratio: 
  $$
  \textrm{GLR}(X) = \dfrac{ E[X_+] }{ E[X_-] } - 1 \textrm{ if } E[X] > 0;
  $$
  it is coherent, but small and large losses are considered equally bad;
\item $\textrm{RAROC} = \dfrac{ E[X] }{ \textrm{VaR}(X) } $
\item The \emph{tilt coefficient} is the highest risk aversion that will say ``yes'' to the strategy (for the exponential utility):
  $$
  \textrm{TC}(X) = \inf \{ \lambda \geq 0  \ :\   E[ X e^{ - \lambda X } ] < 0 \}
  $$
\item The expected shortfall can be turned into a scale-free acceptability measure:
  $$
  \textrm{AIT}(X) = 
  \dfrac1{\inf \{ \lambda \in [0,1] \ :\  \textrm{ES}_\lambda(X) \geq 0 } - 1
  $$
where the expected shortfall is
  $$
  \textrm{ES}_\lambda(X) = E [ X \,|\, X \leq F_X^{-1}(\lambda) ]
  $$
and $F_X^{-1}$ is the quantile function of $X$.
\end{compactenum}

Actually, coherent acceptability measures can be characterized as follows
(this is the same notion of coherence and the same characterization as
for the coherent indices of satisfactions, see A. Meucci's book,
section 5.6): generalize the expected shortfall to
$$
  u(X) = \int_0^1 \psi'(s) F_X^{-1}(s) ds
$$
where $\psi: [0,1] \longrightarrow [0,1]$

is concave, non-decreasing, with $\psi(0)=0$ and $\psi(1)=1$; it can
be seen as a deformation to be applied to the quantile function
$F_X^{-1}$ before computing the expected shortfall, equivalently,
$\psi'$ can be seen as weights (for the expected shortfall, the weight
is constant on $[0,\lambda]$ and zero on $[\lambda,1]$). To get the
index of satisfaction, we actually need an increasing continuous
family $(\psi_x)_{x\geq0}$ of such functions $\psi$, with
$\psi_0(y)=y$ and $\psi_x \to \delta_0$ when $x \to \infty$. The
corresponding index of satisfaction is then (this is the same formula
as for the expected shortfall, with $x = 1/\lambda - 1$):
$$
  \textrm{AIW}(X) = \inf \{ x \geq 0 \ :\  u_x(X) < 0 \}
$$
For instance (these can be interpreted in terms of order statistics):
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item MinVaR:    $\psi(y) = 1 - (1-y)^{x+1}$
\item MaxVaR:    $\psi(y) = y ^ { 1 / (1 + x) }$
\item MaxMinVaR: $\psi(y) = (1 - (1-y)^{x+1}) ^ { 1 / (1 + x) }$
\item MinMaxVaR: $\psi(y) = 1 - (1 - y ^ { 1 / (1 + x) })^{x+1}$
\end{compactenum}

Those results were applied to options and hedge funds; for the latter,
the influence of the kurtosis was decomposed into \emph{peakedness} and
\emph{tailweightedness}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithmic trading of hedge funds}\\
  N. Christofides\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}
A complete trading platform can be built as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Gather the data (some variables as daily, others monthly)
\item Fill in the data, using the EM algorithm (contrary to what the
  speaker seems to think, this is \emph{not} a data imputation
  algorithm: using it as such would lead to biased estimators,
  especially for dispersion estimators)
\item Build a risk model, using independent component analysis (ICA)
  (principal component analysis (PCA) fails to get rid of
  cross-kurtosis)
\item Model the (daily) time-series behaviour of the independent
  components (more complicated than that of the inital assets), using
  \emph{neural networks} (fitted with \emph{bionomic} (?) algorithm)
  or \emph{wavelets}
\item Discretize the market model into a \emph{state transition graph}
  (STG); merge similar vertices to keep their number under control
  (but you can still have millions of them); make sure there are no
  arbitrage opportunities (it suffices to check for 1-period
  opportunities from each vertex);
\item Proceed with dynamic programming (the model is daily but your
  investment horizon is longer); you may want to use a \emph{state
    space relaxation} technique to replace this single large problem
  into several smaller ones.
\end{compactenum}
The speaker also mentionned \emph{real options}, but I still have no idea what it is.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Long-short portfolio optimization under the mean-variance-CVaR framework}\\
  G. Mitra\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}

Current optimizers are not limited to the variance as a risk measure:
they can use mean absolute deviation (MAD), semi-variance, lower
partial moments, value at risk (VaR), conditional value at risk (CVaR,
also called expected shortfall (ES) or tail VaR (TVaR)) -- the
presenter gives more details as to how this can be expressed as a
linear problem, provided you add more variables and stick to
\emph{scenario-based optimization}; he also explains how to write the
long-short, leverage and number-of-assets constraints.

You can also use several risk measures, such as the variance and the
CVaR: the efficient frontier is then 2-dimensional.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Independent component analysis}\\
  A. Robinson (APT)\\
  Carisma (2007)
\end{book}

\emph{Principal component analysis} (PCA) models data as a linear mixture of
gaussian variables; the principal components are linear combinations
(more precisely, rotations) of the observed variables that maximize
the variance.

\emph{Independant component analysis} (ICA) models the data as a
linear mixture of independant non-gaussian variables: the independent
components are linear combinations of the observed variables that
maximize some measure of non-gaussianity, such as \emph{kurtosis} or
\emph{negentropy} -- a couple of years ago, APT were extremely careful
never to use the ``ICA'' words when explaining their product.

Similarly, \emph{second-order blind identification} (SOBI) models the
data as a linear mixture of autocorrelated variables -- one could
generalize this by considering the volatility process.

The audience raised a few questions:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item how robust is it?
\item can we combine ICA and SOBI?
\end{compactenum}
D. Madan raised a few objections:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item the assumption of a linear mixture of non-gaussian variables is
  not valid for financial data: it would create dependence between the
  necks, while we also/mainly observe (and worry about) dependence in
  the tails;
\item furthermore, the independent components we get are not
  independent.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Alpha budgeting\\ cross-sectional dispersion decomposed}\\
  W. Yu and Y.M. Sharaiha\\
  Journal of asset management (2007)
\end{book}

Anova (analysis of variance) can be used to decompose cross-sectional
variance.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving returns-based style analysis}\\
  Daniel Mostovoy\\
  (Northfield, 2007)
\end{book}

Returns-based style analysis (RBSA) tries to replicate the returns of
a fund, or at least to explain its risk (for variance decomposition or
to use it in a (fund-of-funds) portfolio optimization), from a handful
of assets or \emph{spanning indices}, in a linear way. The naive
\emph{constrained linear regression} (constrained because the weights
have to be positive) can be improved:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item you can \emph{compute confidence} intervals on the weights;
\item for hedge funds, you can allow negative weights, add a cash
  asset and constrain on the leverage;
\item you can allow regime shifts by choosing the estimation period
  using \emph{CUSUM} -- they have a
  \href{http://flashvideo.northinfo.com/cusum-700.cfm}{CUSUM} video on
  their website;  
\item plot the residuals against time (everyone should already be
  doing that) to spot slow style changes and use exponential weights
  to get rid of them; alternatively, use a \emph{Kalman particle
    filter} (because of the constraints, you have to use Monte Carlo
  Markov Chain (MCMC) simulations)
\item Correct for heteroskedasticity, lest the more volatile periods
  be counted more heavily: define the dispersion between the spanning
  indices, at a given date, as their (cross-sectional) average
  absolute returns difference; weigh the observations with the inverse
  of the square root of the dispersion;
\item Be sure to include volatility-base indices; you can choose the
  spanning indices by \emph{forward stepwise regression};
\item the proxy portfolio will be too diversified: replace it by its
  constituents and ask the optimizer to reduce the number of assets;
\item You still cannot capture all the stock-specific risk: this can
  be fixed by adding cash and changing the leverage; you will also
  have to tweak the systematic and unsystematic risk aversion
  parameters (RAP) to keep the same factor variances; you might also
  want to add a ``pure stock-specific risk'' asset to the risk model.
\end{compactenum}

However, the more ``hedged'' the fund, the less it works.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Who should you listen to?\\ A sector decomposition of surprise stock returns}\\
  G. de Rossi (UBS)
  Northfield (2007)
\end{book}
A long-short strategy based on analysts' earnings estimates upgrades
and downgrades is profitable in some sectors but not in others,
regardless of the quality of the forecasts (?). To explain this, one
can start with a discounted cash flow model
$$
  \textrm{Price} = E\left[ \sum_{t\geq1} \dfrac{ D_t }{ 1 + r_{0 \to t} } \right]
$$
and try to separate the influence of the numerator (cash flow news)
and the denominator (discount-rate news).

(I am not the best person to talk about discounted cash flow models:
if you want more information, check the article itself.)

In a nutshell: in your models, use sector-specific weights.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Alpha scaling revisited}
  Amish Shah (Northfield, 2007)
\end{book}
The \emph{Grinold formula} can be derived as follows: let
\begin{align*}
  y:       &\textrm{forward returns} \\
  g:       &\textrm{investment signal (our alpha -- or even its components)} \\
  \hat g:  &\textrm{a realization of }g
\end{align*}
The ordinary least squares (OLS) estimate of  $y$  given  $g = \hat g$  is 
\begin{align*}
  \hat y &= E[y] + \cov(y,g) \cov(g,g)^-1 (\hat g - E[g])             \\
         &= E[y] + \cor(y,g) \sd(y) \sd(g) \sd(g)^-2 (\hat g - E[g])  \\
         &= E[y] + \cor(y,g) * \sd(y) * (\hat g - E[g]) / \sd(g)
\end{align*}
Where 
\begin{align*}
  \cor(y,g):                      &\textrm{ information coefficient} \\
  \sd(y):                         &\textrm{ volatility}\\
  \dfrac{\hat g - E[g]}{\sd(g)}:  &\textrm{ score}
\end{align*}
This remains valid for time series regressions or cross-sectionnally
(for the whole universe or within sectors).

The Northfield alpha scaling tool processes the client's alpha as follows: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item gaussianize it to get a score
\item use the cross-sectional Grinold formula: 
$$
  \textrm{return forecast} = \textrm{IC} \times \textrm{volatility} \times \textrm{score}
$$
\end{compactenum}

The IC is provided by the user (perhaps on a sector basis); the
volatility was not clearly explained (you could take the volatility
from the risk model or compute a cross-sectional volatility for the
whole market or for each sector).

This talk also mentionned the Black--Litterman framework. J. Sefton
explained that this helps alleviate the ``error maximization problem'':
the risk factors used in the risk model are also present in the alpha
and the optimizer is asked to minimize the former and maximize the
latter -- it ends up maximizing the misalignment between the two.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio construction\\ in a regression framework}\\
  J. Sefton (formerly UBS)\\
  Northfield (2007)
\end{book}

Mean-variance optimization is often implemented as a 2-step process:
first estimate the returns and the variance, then optimize. This can
be replaced by a 1-step procedure, that goes directly from the data to
the portfolio weights: constrained linear regression.
\begin{align*}
  \gamma 1_T &= R w + \epsilon \\
  1_N' w = 1
\end{align*}
where 
\begin{align*}
  1_T &= (1 \cdots 1)' \\
  1_N &= (1 \cdot 1)' \\
  R &= \begin{pmatrix}
      r_{11} & \cdots & r_{1N} \\
      \vdots &        & \vdots \\
      r_{T1} & \cdots & r_{TN}
  \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
and $\gamma$ is the risk apetite. 

As a bonus, you get all the machinery surrounding regression:
confidence intervals, robust regression, regression diagnostics
(leverage, etc.) -- the \emph{audit trail} from data to weights is
easier to follow.

For instance, looking at the \emph{leverage} over time shows that,
because of the recent drop in volatility, recent observations have a
lower leverage, \ie, a lower influence on the final weights, than
older observations -- this is not what you want.

Other example: the F-test can be used to test the difference between a
portfolio $P$ and an efficient portfolio $E_\gamma$; it simplifies to
$$
Z(\gamma) = 
\dfrac{ 
  \textrm{IR}( E_\gamma )^2 - \textrm{IR}(P)^2 
}{ 
  1 + \textrm{IR}(P)^2 
}
$$
and the ``distance'' between portfolio $P$ and the efficient frontier can be defined as 
$$
  Z = \min_\gamma Z(\gamma).
$$

I am not convinced that all the classical regression tools and
formulas remain valid for constrained regression, though. For
confidence intervals, Dan di Bartolomeo wrote an article, 10 years
ago.

The constrained regression can include priors for the returns and the risk matrix:
$$
\gamma (1 \cdots 1 1 0 \cdots 0 ) =
\begin{pmatrix}
  & R & \\
  \alpha_1 & \cdots & \alpha_N \\
  & V_{\textrm{prior}} ^{1/2} & 
\end{pmatrix}
w + \epsilon
$$
$$
\var \epsilon = \sigma^2 \textrm{diag}(1, t_0^{-1}, t_0^{-1})
$$
where $t_0$ is the ``weight'' (in days) of the prior.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Distinguishing between being unlucky and unskillful}\\
  Dan diBartolomeo (Northfield, 2007)
\end{book}

Alpha or information ratios (IR) are rarely statistically significant,
unless you have several centuries of data -- and increasing the
frequency of the data does not help: not only do the data becaome
statistically more complicated (autocorrelation, kurtosis, jumps), but
it does not increase the sample size, \ie, the number of investment
decisions taken. To account for regime switches, the size of the
sample period can be estimated using the \emph{CUSUM} method.

Grinold's fundamental law of active management,
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{IR} &= \textrm{IC} \sqrt{\textrm{breadth}} \\
  \textrm{IR} &= \textrm{Information Ratio} 
               = \dfrac{\alpha}{\textrm{tracking error}} \\
  \textrm{IC} &= \textrm{Information coefficient}   \\
              &= \cor(\textrm{forecast}, \textrm{future returns}) \\
  \textrm{breadth} &= \textrm{number of independant bets}
\end{align*}
makes unreasonable assumptions (no constraints, no transaction
costs) and can be modified as
$$
  \textrm{IR} = \textrm{IC} \times \textrm{TC} \times \sqrt{\textrm{breadth}}
$$
where the transfer coefficient $\textrm{TC} < 1$ measures portfolio
construction efficiency.

Using the \textrm{IR} in the first place is not that good an idea: it
only corresponds to a utility function if you are very, very risk
averse -- in which case, you should not approach stocks.

One can measure performance in a cross-sectional way (the sample size
grows quickly) using the \emph{effective information coefficient}
(EIC, you can also see it as an \emph{ex post transfer coefficient}):
the correlation between the implied alpha (\ie, the alpha for which
the portfolio actually held would be optimal) and the realized
returns.

Going the other way round, you can use the EIC to evaluate a risk model:
$$
  \textrm{excess returns} = \textrm{EIC} \times \textrm{dispersion} + \textrm{residuals}
$$
where the dispersion is computed cross-sectionally and the dispersion
of the residuals measures the quality of the risk model.

Good fund returns can have two causes: either a good \emph{batting
  average} (the proportion of forecasts with the correct sign) or a
good \emph{skew} (third moment of the product weights * returns),
\ie, larger gains than losses -- the skew appears with
trend-following strategies or portfolio insurance.

Someone remarked that those measures did not take \emph{capacity}
problems into account.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A market impact model that works}\\
  Dan diBartolomeo (Northfield, 2007)
\end{book}
Northfield now have a market impact model:
$$
  M = A + B * X + C * X^{1/2}.
$$

Since large trades are extremely rare in the data (they are split into
smaller trades), those model typically do not predict their impact
well -- the impact can exceed 100\%. To avoid this, they add boundary
constraints on the value of the coefficients, using a worst-case
scenario (a takeover with information leakage). They also overweigh
large trades (using their dollar value), in order to have better
forecasts for them. Their current model works well for North America,
but not for the UK or Japan -- this may be due to a data problem.

In the Carisma seminar, Dan had more time and gave more details: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item When estimating the worst-case market impact, they take the liquidity into account
\item Their market impact model has a liquidity exhaustion term
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{The market efficiency in stock markets}\\
  J.S. Yang et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:physics/0701179}
\end{book}

Yet another econophysics article.
For each asset of interest (here, indices: S\&P 500 and Kospi) plot,
over time (using high-frequency (1-minute) log-returns; only intraday
to avoid discontinuities):
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The \emph{tail index};
\item The ``variance of the autocorrelation'', \ie, the sum of the
  (day) 10 first autocorrelations, \ie, the \emph{$Q$-statistic} (used
  in the Ljung--Box test);
\item The scaling property of the standard deviation, \ie, $\mu$ in 
  $ \sigma( \Delta t) \sim (\Delta t)^\mu $.
\end{compactenum}
Discretize the returns and compute: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Their \emph{entropy} $H(1) = \sum_i - p_i \ln p_i$;
\item The entropy $H(L)$ of the sequences of $L$ returns, or rather
  the marginal entropy $H(L) - H(L-1)$;
\item The \emph{statistical complexity} (?) of the corresponding Markov chain (it will be zero if the data is either regular or completely random).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A valuation-based test of market-timing}\\
  W.B. Elliott et al.\\
  Journal of corporate finance (2007)
\end{book}

Whe deciding whether to issue stock or debt, CFOs try to time the
market. To test this hypothesis, one can compute the correlation
between the \emph{leverage} (debt-to-equity ratio) and a misvaluation
measure, such as the prite-to-book ratio (P/B) -- but the P/B contains
more information than simple over-valuation: growth opportunities,
asymetric information, debt overhang, etc. Instead, the authors use
the \emph{residual income model} (RIM) to study the capital structure
--CFOs do try to time the market.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{CFA Institute magazine}\\
  March-April 2007
\end{book}

It does not make sense for a company not to pay dividends -- it
prevents investors from valuing it, unless you are satisfied with a
null value.

Fat tails, power laws, drawdown can be used as a risk measure: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item APT models take fat tails into account;
\item higher moments can be used in portfolio theory and lead to a \emph{multi-moment efficient frontier};
\item The tails are different for market risk (thin) and credit and
  operational risk (fat); furthermore, they are not independent.
\end{compactenum}

%There was a very clear article about the yield curve:
%TODO

The article about the \emph{quantitative investment process} (what we
are doing) stressed the importance of sector-dependant, dynamic
models; it also mentions \emph{fundamental indices}, such as RAFI
(Ressearch Associates Fundamental Index), which weigh stock using
(additive) variables other than market capitalization.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Fundamental indexation}\\
  R.D. Arnott et al.\\
  Financial analysts journal (2005)
\end{book}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Extreme value problems in random matrix theory and other disordered systems}\\
  G. Biroli et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat:0702244}
\end{book}

There are several variants or generalizations of the central limit theorem:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The asymptotic behaviour of $\sum_{i=1}^N x_i$ is gaussian if
  $x$ is $L^2$, stable (if defined) otherwise: only the tails matter;
\item The asymptotic behaviour of $\max_{i=1}^N x_i$ is Weibull (if $X$ is bounded), Gumbel (if $X$ has thinner than power law tails) or Fr\'echet (if $X$ has power law tails);
\item The asymptotic behaviour of 
$$ S_q(N) = \sum_{i=1}^N x_i^q $$
depends on $\mu = \sqrt{2\ln N} / q \sigma$ and 
exhibit phase transition phenomena;
\item etc.
\end{compactenum}

The article examines what happens to the \emph{Tracy--Widom
  distribution} (the distribution of the largest eigenvalue of a symmetric random
matrix with gaussian entries) when the entries have power law tails.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the distribution of the largest principal component}\\
  I.M. Johnstone (2000)
\end{book}

The largest singular of a random (gaussian) matrix (or the largest
eigenvalue of a Wishart matrix) approaches the Tracy-Widom
distribution, which is computable in terms of the Painlev\'e
differential equation.

(Random matrix theory (RMT) is just a limit theorem for matrices,
similar to other limit theorems (central limit, stable distributions,
extreme distributions, large deviations, etc.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Correlation functions, cluster functions and spacing distributions for random matrices}\\
   C.A. Tracy and H. Widom\\
   \texttt{arXiv:solv-int/9801004}
\end{book}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Exponential weighting and matrix-based filtering of financial covariance matrices for portfolio optimization}\\
  S. Pafka et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat/0402573}
\end{book}

Random matrix theory (RMT) noise undressing can be combined with
exponential weighting to account for heteroskedasticity. 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Non-hermitian random matrices}\\
  B. Khoruzhenko (2001)
\end{book}

The tools used to study the (non-real) eigenvalues of non-hermitian matrices are very different. 

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Records in a changing world}\\
  J. Krug\\
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat/0702136}
\end{book}

Records (\ie, entries larger than all the previous ones) in a sequence
of iid random variables asymptotically follow an extreme value theory
(EVT) distribution and the distribution of record times do not depend
on the distribution of the random variables.

One can also consider independant non-iid variables:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item $X_n = \max \{ Y_{1,n},\dots,Y_{N_n,n} \}$, with $Y_{i,j}$ iid, can be iused to mode sports records where $N_n$ (usually increasing) is the population size;
\item $X_n = Y_n + cn$;
\item $X_n = \lambda_n Y_n$.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Random but not so much\\A parametrization for the returns and correlation matrix of financial time series}\\
  A.C.R. Martins\\
  \texttt{arXiv:physics/0701025}
\end{book}

Random matrix theory (RMT) can be seen as a statistical test of $H_0:
V = \textbf{1}_{nn}$ using $\textrm{Spec}\, \hat V$, with no
well-defined alternative hypothesis.

One can extend the Mar\u cenko--Pastur model by:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item allowing for a more general variance matrix $V$, e.g., one with
  a handful of non-zero eigenvalues (this is how RMT is used, to
  compare $H_0: \lambda_k=0$ against $H_1: \lambda_k\neq0$; but people
  use results valid for $k=1$ for any value of $k$, and feel free to
  perform as many tests as possible);
\item allowing for non-stationarity effects (the subject of the
  article, but their model is not very clear).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock market distributions:\\ from past to present}\\
  S. Dro\.zd\.z et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:0704.0664}
\end{book}

Earlier studies claimed that market fluctuations had power laws with a
scaling index $\alpha>3$, regardless of the horizon. This index now
depends on the horizon: 3 for high frequency data (minute), while
weekly data are gaussian.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Mean-variance portfolio analysis under parameter uncertainty}\\
  T. Bodnar and W. Schmid
\end{book}

Going from sample returns to market parameters to the (minimum variance or tangency) efficient portfolio to an estimation of the portfolio return and risk are estimators. 
$$
\xymatrix{
  &\txt{data} \ar[d] \\
  &\hat \mu, \hat V \ar[rd] \ar[ld] \\
  \txt{minimum variance\\portfolio} \ar[d] && \txt{tangency\\portfolio} \ar[d] \\
  \hat\mu_{\textrm{MV}}, \hat\sigma^2_{\textrm{MV}} &&
  \hat\mu_{\textrm{T}}, \hat\sigma^2_{\textrm{T}}  
}
$$
Under gaussian assumptions, one can compute, in closed form, the
distribution of those estimators, and perform \emph{tests} on them.

Note that 
\begin{align*}
  E\left[ \va{ \hat\mu_{\textrm{MV}} } \right] & = \infty \\
  E\left[ \va{ \hat\sigma^2_{\textrm{MV}} }^{1/2} \right] &= \infty.
\end{align*}

The minimum variance portfolio is more robust.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{SNP: a program for non-parametric time series analysis}\\
  A.R. Gallant and G. Tauchen (1990--2007)
\end{book}

\emph{Hermite probability distribution functions} (pdf) generalize the
gaussian distribution: 
$$ h(z) \propto P(z)^2 \phi(z) $$
where $P$ is a polynomial and $\phi$ the (multivariate, standard) gaussian pdf.

\emph{Semi-non-parametric} distributions are the distributions of
affine transformations of Hermite random variables.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Conditional properties of hedge funds: evidence from daily returns}\\
  Y. Li and H. Kazemi\\
  European financial management (2007)
\end{book}

Non-parametric GARCH(1,1) (or more genarally SNP models) can be used
to model daily hedge fund returns and accounts for asymetries.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Investor attention\\ and time-varying comovements}\\
  L. Pend et al.\\
  European financial management (2007)
\end{book}

Comovement can be measures as 
$$ \dfrac{
  \textrm{stock-specific risk}
}{
  \textrm{total risk}
}. $$
The authors use realized log-volatility from 5-minute returns and regress stock returns against S\&P 500 returns.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparative study of portfolio insurance}\\
  S. Basak (2001)
\end{book}

Portfolio insurance can be cast into a utility framework: as the
wealth approaches the floor, the marginal utility tends to infinity,
e.g., one can use 
$$ 
  \dfrac{ ( \textrm{wealth} - \textrm{floor} )^\gamma }{ \gamma }
$$
instead of $\textrm{wealth}^\gamma / \gamma$.

The article uses continuous-time processes

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{A shrinkage approach to model uncertainty and asset allocation}\\
  Z. Wang (2003)
\end{book}

To account for model uncertainty, shrink stock returns and variance
towards those implied by the CAPM or Fama--French model.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Valuation in US commercial real estate}\\
  E. Ghysels et al.\\
  European financial management (2007)
\end{book}

Real estate (and real estate investment trusts (REIT)) is very similar
to equity: properties (especially commercial properties) can be valued
with the \emph{discounted rent model}, analogue of the discounted cash
flow model; the \emph{cap rate} (rent-to-price ratio) is the analogue
of the P/E, etc.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Is coskewness a better measure of risk in the downside that downside beta?\\ Evidence in emerging market data}\\
  D.U.A. Galagedera and R.D. Brooks\\
  Journal of multinational financial management (2007)
\end{book}

The various \emph{downside betas}
\begin{align*}
  \beta^{\textrm{HW}} &= \dfrac{
    E \left[ (r - r_{\textrm{free}})  (r_{\textrm{market}} - r_{\textrm{free}})_- \right]
  }{
    E \left[ (r_{\textrm{market}} - r_{\textrm{free}})_- ^2 \right]
  } \\
  \beta^{\textrm{HR}} &= \dfrac{
    E \left[ (r - \bar r)  (r_{\textrm{market}} - \bar r_{\textrm{market}})_- \right]
  }{
    E \left[ (r_{\textrm{market}} - \bar r_{\textrm{market}})_- ^2 \right]
  } \\
  \beta^{\textrm{E}} &= \dfrac{
    E \left[ (r - \bar r)_-  (r_{\textrm{market}} - \bar r_{\textrm{market}})_- \right]
  }{
    E \left[ (r_{\textrm{market}} - \bar r_{\textrm{market}})_- ^2 \right]
  } \\
\end{align*}
can be generalized into \emph{downside skewnesses}:
\begin{align*}
  \gamma^{\textrm{HW}} &= \dfrac{
    E \left[ (r - r_{\textrm{free}})  (r_{\textrm{market}} - r_{\textrm{free}})_- ^2\right]
  }{
    E \left[ (r_{\textrm{market}} - r_{\textrm{free}})_- ^3 \right]
  } \\
  \gamma^{\textrm{HR}} &= \dfrac{
    E \left[ (r - \bar r)  (r_{\textrm{market}} - \bar r_{\textrm{market}})_- ^2\right]
  }{
    E \left[ (r_{\textrm{market}} - \bar r_{\textrm{market}})_- ^3 \right]
  } \\
  \gamma^{\textrm{E}} &= \dfrac{
    E \left[ (r - \bar r)_-  (r_{\textrm{market}} - \bar r_{\textrm{market}})_- ^2 \right]
  }{
    E \left[ (r_{\textrm{market}} - \bar r_{\textrm{market}})_- ^3 \right]
  } \\
\end{align*}

This is appropriate when the returns distribution is skewed.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio selection with higher moments}\\
  C.R. Harvey et al.
\end{book}

Traditional portfolio selection has two drawbacks: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item It assumes that the expected utility (the authors confuse utility and expected utility) only depends on the first two moments;
\item It assumes that the market parameters are known, \ie, that the \emph{estimation risk} will remain negligible.
\end{compactenum}


To tackle the first problem, one ca: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item consider the expected utility as a function of the first three moments, leading to a 2-dimensional efficient frontier;
\item replace the variance by an asymetric measure of risk.
\end{compactenum}


To tackle the second problem, one can:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item shrink the market parameters;
\item use bayesian estimators of the market parameters (this is a non-linear shrinkage);
\item use robust estimators of the market parameters;
\item shrink the ``optimal'' portfolio (Black--Litterman);
\item add constraints on the weights/
\end{compactenum}

The authors use bayesian estimators from a multivariate \emph{skew
  gaussian} (the sum of a truncated gaussian and a (smaller) gaussian,
up to affine transformations) and a higher-dimensional efficient
frontier to account for both skewness and estimation risk.

Their presentation of utility theory, with their undefined notion of
\emph{predictive utility} (something between utility and expected
utility), is confusing.

As often, there is also some ``resampled frontier bashing''.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Robust mean-variance portfolio selection}\\
  C. Perret-Gentil and M.P. Victoria-Feser (2003)
\end{book}

``Robust portfolio construction'' refers to two different notions:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item either a worst-case optimization;
\item or a classical mean-variance optimization using robust estimates
  of the mean and variance matrices.
\end{compactenum}
This article focuses on the latter and proves that it suffices to
robustify the inputs of the mean-variance machinery to get a robust
mean-variance portfolio.

However,
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item it does not work complicated (non-convex) constraints -- there
  are similar limitations to the equivalence between robust
  optimization (first sense) and bayesian optimization;
\item in high dimensions (the article remains in dimension 3), robust
  variance matrices are not very robust.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.clrc.rhul.ac.uk/events/TCJ.pdf}{\emph{Hedging predictions in machine learning}}\\
  A. Gammerman and V. Vovk (2007)  
\end{book}

While statistical prediction procedures can provide confidence
intervals (or, even better, full posterior distributions, for bayesian
statistics), machine learning algorithms only provide a single, naked
prediction.

\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Find a measure of strangeness, using a machine learning (ML)
  algorithm, in an ad hoc way, e.g.:
  \begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
  \item absolute value of the residuals in a regression;
  \item Lagrange multipliers in a support vector machine (SVM) (it is
    zero for non-support vectors);
  \item for the $k$ nearest neighbours,
    $$ 
    \alpha = \dfrac{ 
      \sum\limits_{j=1} ^k d_{ij}^+ 
    }{ 
      \sum\limits_{j=1} ^k d_{ij}^- 
    } 
    $$
    where $d_{ij}^+$ (resp. $d_{ij}^-$) is the $j$th shortest distance
    (euclidian or other) between $i$ and the observations with the
    same (resp. a different) label
    (this can be seen as a ``truncated anova'');
  \end{compactenum}
\item The \emph{empirical $p$-value} $p_{x,y}$ of an observation
  $(x,y)$ is the proportion of observations stranger than it (this
  should remind you of bootstrap $p$-values);
\item For regression problems, after choosing a confidence level
  $\epsilon$, the prediction of $x$ is the \emph{set}
  $$ \{ y \in Y \,:\, p_{x,y} > \epsilon \}; $$
\item For classification problems, 
  \begin{align*}
    \hat y &= \mathop{\textrm{Argmax}}_y p_{x,y} \\
    \textrm{credibility}(\hat y) &= p_{x, \hat y} \\
    \textrm{confidence}(\hat y) &= 1 - \max_{y \neq \hat y} p_{x,y}
  \end{align*}
  (this should remind you en \emph{entropy-based estimators}: we are
  looking for the model under which the data looks as random as
  possible, \ie, the model that removes as much regularity as possible
  from the data); the credibility should be high, unless the training
  set is not iid and/or not representative of the data and/or the new
  observation is an outliera.
\end{compactenum}

More formally, the measure of strangeness $\alpha$ is called a
\emph{non-conformity measure} and is just a family of maps $(X \times
Y) ^ n / \mathfrak S_n \longrightarrow \RR_+$.

The empirical $p$-value is an approximate \emph{random test}: ideally, it 
should be a function $t: \ZZ^\ast \longrightarrow [0,1]$, where $\ZZ^\ast$ is
the set of all finite sequences of integers, such that
for all $\epsilon > 0$,
for all $n \in \NN^\times$,
for all probability distribution $P$ on $\ZZ^n$,
$$
P(\{ z \in \ZZ^n \,:\, t(z) \leq \epsilon \}) \leq \epsilon.
$$

The article mentions \emph{ridge regression} but seems unaware of the
fact that this is not a single estimator but a family of estimators (a
\emph{regularization path}), depending on the value of the ridge
parameter.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{The style consistency of hedge funds}\\
R. Gibson and S. Gyger\\
European financial management (2007)
\end{book}

PAM (partitionning around medoids -- this is similar to $k$-means
clustering but the center of the clusters are chosen among the
observations) can recognize hedge fund styles as well as principal
component analysis (same $R^2$).

Fuzzy clustering algorithms find the clusters and assign a membership
probability for each observation-cluster pair. The authors use an
\emph{entropy-based} fuzzy clustering:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Entropy-based estimators find the probability distribution
$(p_1,\dots,p_n)$ that maximizes the entropy $H = \sum - p_i \ln p_i$,
subject to all the information available expressed as constraints.
\item The cost of a configuration (\ie, a set of membership
  probabilities) is
$$ \textrm{cost}(Y) = \sum_{ij} p_{ij} d(r_i,y_j) $$
where the $r_i$ are the observations and the $y_j$ the cluster
centers. If the cost is known, we can find the membership
probabilities that maximize the entropy under the constraint above.
\item For the cost defined by each hard clustering configuration (yes,
  there are many of them), compute the entropy-maximizing membership
  probabilities and average them all (\ie, you have no prior
  preference on the hard clustering configurations).
\item Finally, with those membership probabilities, find the cluster
  centers by maximum likelihood.
\end{compactenum}

After fuzzy clustering, style consistency of hedge funds can be
measured as \emph{mean time consistency} (average (over time)
membership probability), \emph{consistency deviation} (standard
deviation of time consistency) or their ratio (\emph{consistency
  ratio}); style consistency does not affect returns.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Calibration risk for exotic options}\\
  K. Detlefsen and W.K. H\H ardle\\
  Journal of derivatives (2007)
\end{book}

Derivative models have to be calibrated by minimizing some error
function, but the calibration depends on the choice of this error
function, leading to \emph{calibration risk} -- on top of \emph{model
  risk}.

Popular error functions include: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item absolute price difference;
\item relative price difference;
\item absolute implied volatility difference;
\item relative implied volatility difference.
\end{compactenum}

This article compares them for the \emph{Heston model} 
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ d S_t }{ S_t } &= \mu dt + \sqrt{V_t} d W_t^1 \\
  dV_t &= \xi (\eta - V_t) dt + \theta \sqrt{V_t} dW_t^2
\end{align*}
(well-defined if $\xi \eta > \theta^2/2$, but in simulations, negative variances are truncated to zero) and the \emph{Bates model}, which accounts for jumps in the stock prices,
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ dS_t }{ S_t } &= \mu dt + \sqrt{V_t} dW_t^1 + dZ_t \\
  dV_t &= \xi (\eta - V_t) dt + \theta \sqrt{V_t} dW_t^2 \\
  Z & \sim \textrm{Poisson}(\lambda, k) \\
  \log(1+k) &\sim N\left( \log(1+\bar k) - \dfrac{\delta^2}2, \delta^2  \right).
\end{align*}

The models are calibrated with vanilla options (using the
characteristic function of those models -- yet another application of
the charcateristic function) and then used to price exotic options:
barrier (un and out call)
$$ \textrm{Payoff} = \textbf{1}_{\max S < B} (S_T - K)^+ $$
and cliquet
$$ \textrm{Payoff} = \sum_{i=1}^N \dfrac{ S_{t_i} - S_{t_{i-1}} }{ S_{t_{i-1}} } $$
(the cliquet pays the sum of the \emph{linear} returns; it is actually
more complicated: in each period, the returns are truncated (above and
below), and so is their sum).

The article suggests to calibrate with respect to absolute prices to
mitigate model risk -- or relative implied volatilities, if the model
has already been chosen.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Hedge fund activism, corporate governance and firm performance}\\
  A. Brav et al.
\end{book}

Some hedge funds can acquire more than 5\% of a company (this has to
be filed to the SEC (schedule 13D)) and ask it to change (e.g., sell
part of its business, fire its CEO, accept a takeover). The following
variables can help predict targeted companies:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item size:
  \begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
  \item market value;
  \end{compactenum}
\item value:
  \begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
  \item $Q = \dfrac{ \textrm{debt} + \textrm{market value} }{ \textrm{debt} + \textrm{book value} };$
  \item book-to-market;
  \end{compactenum}
\item performance:
  \begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
  \item sales growth;
  \item $\textrm{ROA} = \dfrac{ \textrm{EBITDA} }{ \textrm{assets} };$
  \end{compactenum}
\item capital structure:
  \begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
  \item debt-to-capital;
  \item new equity issuance;
  \item dividend yield;
  \item payout ratio = $\dfrac{\textrm{dividends}
    }{\substack{\displaystyle\textrm{net income before}\\\displaystyle\textrm{extraordinary items}}}$;
  \end{compactenum}
\item governance:
  \begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
  \item number of Ginex takeover defenses;
  \item institutional ownership;
  \end{compactenum}
\item liquidity:
  \begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
  \item Amihud illiquidity (daily values, averaged over 1 year): 
  $$E\left[ 
    0.001 \cdot \sqrt{ \dfrac{ 
                          \textrm{price} \cdot \textrm{volume} 
                       }{ 
                          \va{ \textrm{returns} } } }
  \right]$$
  \item idiosyncratic volatility: monthly standard deviation of the daily returns in excess of the industry mean.
  \end{compactenum}
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{A breakdown of the valuation effects of international cross-listing}\\
  A. Bris et al.\\
