  M. Najand et al.\\
  Journal of asset management (2007)
\end{book}

CAPM-GARCH (\ie, CAPM with GARCH noise) is better than CAPM.

Managers with more than 5 acquisitions in the past 3 years are overconficent.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk and asset allocation}\\
  A. Meucci (2006)
\end{book}

% Part I: Statistical preliminaries

% Chapter 1: Univariate statistics
\textbf{Chapter 1: Univariate statistics}

There are three equivalent ways of describing a univariate
distribution: the probability distribution function (pdf, $f_X$), the
cumulative distribution function (cdf, $F_X$) and the
\emph{characteristic function} ($\phi_X(\omega) = E e^{i\omega X}$) --
there is also the \emph{quantile function} ($F_X^{-1}$), but it does
not usefully generalize to higher dimensions.

A \emph{location parameter} (mean, median, mode) is defined by affine
equivariance; so are \emph{dispersion parameters}: for instance, the
standard deviation, the MAD, the interquartile range or the
\emph{modal dispersion}
$$ \textrm{MDis} = 
\left(
  \left. 
  \dfrac{d^2 \ln f_X}{dx^2} 
  \right|_{x = \textrm{Mod}\,X}
\right) ^{-1}.
$$

The \emph{$Z$-score} for a location and a dispersion parameters is 
$$ Z = \dfrac{ X - \textrm{Loc}\,X }{ \textrm{Dis}\,X }. $$

The first chapter ends with a taxonomy of univariate distributions:
uniform, gaussian, Cauchy, Student, log-normal, Gamma (a
generalization of the $\chi^2$ distribution, often used as a prior for
variance).

% Chapter 2: Multivariate statistics
\textbf{Chapter 2: Multivariate statistics}

In a multivariate setting, there are still three ways of representing
a distribution: pdf, cdf and characteristic function. A multivariate
distribution can be factored into 1-dimension distributions (the
marginals) and a ``purely joint component'' -- the \emph{copula},
defined as the joint definition of its grades (the \emph{grade} of a
univariate distribution being its uniformization).

The book fails to give a taxonomy of copulas, but provides a few examples: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The copula of a log-normal distribution is a gaussian copula;
\item The Student T copula is not independant;
\item The copula between prices and log-prices, or between ratio-returns and log-returns, is trivial;
\item The copula between a call option and its underlying is trivial.
\end{compactenum}

The copula is invariant under monotonic increasing transfromations --
e.g., replacing a stock by a call option has no effect.

A \emph{location parameter} (mean, mode) is characterized by
\emph{affine equivariance}:
$$ \textrm{Loc}(a + BX) = a + B \, \textrm{Loc}\,X $$
for all invertible affine transformations $x \mapsto a + B x$.

In dimension greater than 1, the median is not a location parameter:
it depends on a choice of coordinates.

A \emph{dispersion matrix} is a symetric, positive matrix satisfying the
affine equivariance property:
$$ \textrm{DisSq}(a + B x) = B \, \textrm{DisSq}(X) \, B' $$
for all invertible affine transformations $x \mapsto a + B x$, 
such as the covariance matrix or the \emph{modal dispersion}:
$$ \textrm{MDis}\, X = - 
\left(
  \left.
  \dfrac{ \partial^2 \ln f_X }{ \partial x \partial x' }
  \right|_{x = \textrm{Mod}\,X}
\right)^{-1}.
$$

The $Z$-score is then: 
$$ 
Z = \sqrt{
  (X - \textrm{Loc}\,X)'
  (\textrm{DisSq}\, X)^{-1}
  (X - \textrm{Loc}\,X)
}.
$$

Any measures of location and dispersion define a family of
\emph{location-dispersion ellipsoids} -- for non-elliptical
distribution, this information is far from sufficient.

\begin{center}
<<IsoProbContNonEll,echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
library(MASS)
op <- par(mar=c(1,1,3,1))
x <- y <- seq(0,3,length=20)
contour(x, y, outer(x, y, function(a,b) dlnorm(a) * dlnorm(b)))
title(main="Isoprobability contour of a log-normal distribution")
par(op)
@ 
\end{center}

For a more empirical/non-parametric/procedural analogue of the
location-dispersion ellispoid, check the notions of \emph{bag plot}
and \emph{half-space depth}.

The expected value and the variance are equivariant with respect to
not-necessarily invertible transformations: you can obtain the
location-dispersion ellipsoid of a portfolio from that of the market.

\emph{Higher-order statistics} (HOS) can also be defined in the
multidimensional case, but they are \emph{tensors}; however, the
\emph{coskewness} and the \emph{cokurtosis} tensors can be summarized
in one overall index of symetry (for coskewness) or tail thickness
(for kurtosis) -- but the book does not tell us how...

The \emph{Schweizer--Wolff} measure of \emph{dependence} of two
(univariate) random variables is the $L^p$ distance between their
copula and the independant copula, normalized to take values in
$[0,1]$; it is invariant under monotonic (increasing or decreasing)
transformations.

Measures of \emph{concordance}, such as \emph{Kendall's $\tau$} or
\emph{Spearman's $\rho$} are only invariant under increasing
transformations and take values in $[-1,1]$; they change sign under
decreasing transformations; furthermore, being zero is only a
necessary condition for independance.

\emph{Correlation} mixes marginal and joint features: it is only an
``affine measure of concordance'', it is changed by monotonic
transformations of the marginals: ``One might wonder why correlation
is such a popular tool: (...) for an important class of distributions
[elliptical distributions], the correlation completely defines the
dependance structure.''

The taxonomy of multivariate distributions includes: matrix-variate
gaussian and $T$ distributions, Wishart (generalization of the
$\chi^2$: it is the distribution of $X_1 X_1' + \cdots + X_\nu
X_\nu'$, where $X_i \sim N(0,\Sigma)$ with $\nu \geq n$).

\emph{Elliptical distributions} are affine transformations of
spherically symetric distributions, which can be described by a single
univariate function: 
$X \sim \textrm{El}(\mu, \Sigma, g)$
has density 
$$ 
f(x) = \va{\Sigma}^{-1/2} 
g 
\left(
  (x - \mu)' \Sigma^{-1} (x - \mu)
\right)
$$
where $g$ is a \emph{probability density generator}: 
$g\geq0$ and 
$\int_0^\infty v^{N/2-1} g(v)\, dv < \infty.$
For instance: 
\begin{align*}
  g(z) &\propto e^{-z/2} & \textrm{(gaussian)} \\
  g(z) &\propto (1+z)^{-(1+N)/2} & \textrm{(Cauchy)} \\
  g(z) &\propto \left(1+\dfrac z\nu\right)^{-(\nu+N)/2} & \textrm{(Student)} \\
\end{align*}
(Notice that the gaussian pdf has an exponential decay, while the
Student or Cauchy pdf have a \emph{power law} decay.)

Alternatively, elliptic distributions can be recognized from their characteristic function, 
$\phi(\omega) = e^{i \omega' \mu} \psi(\omega' \Sigma \omega),$
for some suitable real-valued function $\psi$.

When a \emph{stable distribution} describes a phenomenon (log-returns,
risk, etc.) at horizon $T$, the compounded distribution at horizon
$2T$, $3T$, etc. is still in the same family.
\emph{Symetric-alpha-stable} (s$\alpha$s) distributions, 
$ X \sim \textrm{SS}(\alpha, \mu, m_\Sigma) $, are defined as
$$
\phi_X(\omega) = e^{ i \omega'\mu} \exp 
\left(
- \int_{\RR^N} \va{\omega' s} ^ \alpha m_\Sigma(s) ds
\right)
$$
where $m_\Sigma$ is a symmetric measure on the 
ellipsoid $s' \Sigma^{-1} s = 1$.

Beware, stable distributions are dangerous: they violate the central
limit theorem (they have no second moment).

When an \emph{infinitely divisible} distribution describes some
phenomenon at horizon $T$, you can (under an independance assumption)
get the distribution at horizons $0<t<T$.

% Chapter 3: Market invariants
\textbf{Chapter 3: Market invariants}

To model a market, the author suggests to:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Look for \emph{market invariants}, \ie, iid random variables
  built from market data (if you see the market as a machine to
  produce prices from iid noise, the noise is a market invariant --
  some people speak of \emph{innovations}), recognized by looking
  (graphically) at their autocorrelation and comparing their
  distribution in the first and last half of the sample; examples
  include: log-returns for the equity market, changes in yield to
  maturity for the fixed income market, changes in (ATMF) implied
  volatility for the options market;
\item Model their distribution;
\item Project their distribution to the investment horizon (e.g., we
  could use daily data to invest on a monthly horizon) -- this can be
  done using the \emph{characteristic function} and is even easier
  with additive invariants (such as log-returns);
\item Transform the projected market invariant distribution into a
  market price distribution -- even if that transformation is not
  analytically tractable, one can easily get the moments of the
  distribution of prices -- but be sure to keep track of the
  propagation of estimation errors.
\end{compactenum}

The quest for market invariants can involve \emph{dimension reduction}
or \emph{variable selection}, but this part of the book is a bit
confusing and the author fails to warn the reader of the dangers of
model selection.

\emph{Cointegration} is mentionned, for the equity and fixed income
markets, but not detailed.

% Part II: Classical asset allocation
% Chapter 4: Estimators
\textbf{Chapter 4: Estimators}

Depending on the amount of information available, you will prefer
shrinkage or bayesian estimates (very little data), maximum likelihood
estimators (MLE) (more data) or non-parametric estimators (a lot of
data).

The quality of an estimator can be measured as its \emph{error} (mean
square error, MSE), its \emph{bias} (average distance to the correct
value) and its \emph{inefficiency} (standard deviation, \ie,
dispersion around its expected value):
$$ \textrm{Error}^2 = \textrm{Bias}^2 + \textrm{Inefficiency}^2. $$
One can also consider the \emph{loss}, \ie, the squared distance to the
correct value (with respect to some quadratic form) -- contrary to the
error, the bias or the inefficiency, this is not a number but a random
variable. One can look at these quantities and distributions for a
family of ``stress-test distributions'', to gauge estimation risk.

\begin{center}
\setkeys{Gin}{width=6cm,height=8cm}
<<MeucciStressTestCorr,echo=FALSE,results='hide',fig.width=6,fig.height=8,out.width="8cm">>=
Replications <- 1000
SampleSize   <- 10
Parameters   <- seq(0, 1, by=0.1)
library(MASS)
GetSample    <- function (parameter) { 
  mvrnorm(SampleSize, 
          mu = c(0,0), 
          Sigma = matrix(c(  1,                 sqrt(2)*parameter,
                             sqrt(2)*parameter, 2),
                         2, 2))
}
Estimator    <- function (x) {
  .9 * cor(x[,1],x[,2])  # I wanted a biased estimator...
}
TrueValue    <- rep(0, length(Parameters))
TrueValue    <- Parameters
EstimatorSample <-
  LossSample    <- matrix(NA, nr=Replications, nc=length(Parameters))
error2 <- bias2 <- ineff2 <- rep(NA, length=length(Parameters))
colnames(EstimatorSample) <- colnames(LossSample) <-
  names(error2) <- names(bias2) <- names(ineff2) <-
    as.character(Parameters)
for (j in seq(along= Parameters)) {
  y <- rep(NA, length=Replications)
  for (i in 1:Replications) {
    x <- GetSample(Parameters[j])
    y[i] <- Estimator(x)
  }
  EstimatorSample[,j] <- y
  LossSample[,j] <- (EstimatorSample[,j] - TrueValue[j])^2
  error2[j] <- mean((EstimatorSample[,j] - TrueValue[j])^2)
  bias2[j]  <- mean(EstimatorSample[,j] - TrueValue[j])^2
  ineff2[j]    <- (Replications - 1) / Replications * var(EstimatorSample[,j])
}
range(error2 - bias2 - ineff2)
op <- par(mfrow=c(3,1), oma=c(0,0,2,0))

library(UsingR)
simple.violinplot(as.vector(EstimatorSample) ~ 
                  colnames(EstimatorSample)[col(EstimatorSample)], 
                  col = "pink")
title(main = "Estimator distribution")

simple.violinplot(as.vector(LossSample) ~ 
                  colnames(LossSample)[col(LossSample)],
                  col = "pink")
abline(h=0, lty=2)
title(main = "Loss distribution")

x <- rbind(ineff2,bias2)
barplot(x, 
        col = c("light blue", "blue"), 
        las = 1,
        main = "Error, inefficiency and bias",
        xlab = "Different stress distributions",
        ylab = expression(Error^2))
legend("topright", 
       rev(c(expression(Inefficiency^2), expression(Bias^2))),
       fill = rev(c("light blue", "blue")))
par(op)
mtext("Estimator of correlation, shrunk towards zero", line=3, font=2, cex=1.2)
@
\setkeys{Gin}{width=6cm,height=6cm}
\end{center}

The \emph{Glivenko--Cantelli} theorem states that the empirical
cumulative distribution function (ecdf) is a consistent estimator of
the cdf; this yields the \emph{benchmark (non-parametric) estimator}:
just replace the cdf in the definition of the quantity of interest by
the ecdf; those estimators are often a good starting point and can be
improved on (e.g.: sample mean and OLS benchmark estimators are
non-biased, while the variance benchmark estimator is).

\emph{Kernel estimators} are obtained from the benchmark estimators by
replacing the Dirac masses in the epdf by gaussian kernels -- \ie, we
use a smoothed estimator of the pdf.

MLE relies on the assumption that the distribution from which the data
is drawn is in a very small set of (known) distributions (sometimes
called the \emph{stress distributions}): the MLE is the mode of the
distribution of the parameters given the data; it is asymptotically
unbiased, asymptotically gaussian, and asymptotically attains the
Cramer--Rao bound (\ie, it is the best unbiased estimator -- the book
assumes you are already familiar with those notions).

The MLE of the parameters of an elliptic distribution (with a known
probability density generator $g$) is a weighted mean and weighted
variance; the weights can be computed (iteratively) using the
Mahalanobis distance and the density generator.
The book does not consider the (semi-parametric) situation of an
elliptic distribution with an unknown probability density generator.
Linear regression or principal components are still amenable in the
case of elliptic distributions.

In the gaussian case, the \emph{condition number}, \ie, the ratio of
the smallest to the largest eigen value, measures how close to a
sphere the cloud of points is: if it is close to~1, the problem is
\emph{well-conditionned}, if it is close to~0, it is
\emph{ill-conditionned}.

An estimator is \emph{admissible} if there does not exist another
estimator with a lower error for all the stress-test distributions.
Benchmark estimators or MLE estimators tend not to be admissible:
their bias is low but their inefficiency large (especially when the
condition number is close to one); shrinkage estimators have a larger
bias and often a much smaller inefficiency, resulting in a lower
error.

For intance, the sample mean, with gaussian data, in dimension at
least 2, is not admissible: the \emph{James--Stein shrinkage
estimator}
\begin{align*}
  \hat{\boldsymbol\mu} ^ {\textrm{Stein}} &= 
    (1 - \alpha) \hat{\boldsymbol\mu} + \alpha \textbf{b} \\
  \alpha &= \dfrac1T 
  \dfrac{ 
    N \bar \lambda - 2 \lambda_1 
  }{
    (\hat{\boldsymbol\mu} - \mathbf{b})' 
    (\hat{\boldsymbol\mu} - \mathbf{b}) 
  }
\end{align*}
where $T$ is the number of observations, $N$ the dimension,
$\lambda_1$ the largest eigenvalue (we do not know it: it  will have
to be computed from an estimator of the variance matrix),
$\bar\lambda$ the average eigen value and $\mathbf{b}$ \emph{any}
vector, is admissible.
The prior $\mathbf{b}$ can be chosen arbitrarily (e.g., $0$), or using
``prior'' information, or as the \emph{grand mean}
$$ b = \dfrac{ \mathbf{1}' \hat{\boldsymbol\mu} }{N} \mathbf{1} $$
or as the volatility-weighted grand-mean (\emph{Jorion estimator})
$$ \mathbf{b} = \dfrac{
  \mathbf{1} ' \hat\Sigma^{-1} \hat{\boldsymbol\mu}
}{
  \mathbf{1} ' \hat\Sigma^{-1} \mathbf{1}
} \mathbf{1}.
$$
(Strictly speaking, this is not prior information, since it is
extracted from the same data set; but it is much less volatile.)

Dispersion estimators can be assessed with the \emph{Frobenius loss}
$$ \textrm{Loss}( \hat\sigma, \sigma ) = \tr \left( \hat\Sigma -
\Sigma \right)^2 . $$

The sample covariance matric scatters the eigen values away from $\bar
\lambda$ (this is easily seen when the true eigen values are all
equal: the sample eigen values will be more dispersed, the largest
eigen values will be too large and the smallest eigen values too
small): it squeezes and stretches the location-dispersion ellipsoid;
the estimation worsens the condition number of the market invariants.
One can shrink the eigenvalues towards their mean (Ledoit):
\begin{align*}
  \hat\Sigma^s &= (1-\alpha) \hat\Sigma + \alpha \hat C\\
  \hat C &= \dfrac1N \sum_{n=1}^N \hat\lambda_n \\
  \alpha & = \min\left(1,
  \dfrac1T \dfrac{
    \dfrac1T \sum_t \tr( x_t x_t' - \hat\Sigma)^2
  }{
    \tr (\hat\Sigma - \hat C)^2
  }
  \right)
\end{align*}
\emph{Ledoit shrinkage} also works for regression.

% Robustness
\textbf{Robustness}

Here are a few measures of robustness:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item the \emph{leave-out-one Jackknife};
\item the \emph{sensitivity curve} (add an observation instead of
  removing it; this is a function of the added observation; in
  particular, we are interested whether this function is bounded);
\item the \emph{influence function} (the infinite-sample limit of the
  sensitivity curve): generalize your estimator so that it be a
  function of a distribution:
  $$ \hat G = G[ \sum_t \delta^{(x_t)} ] $$
  and consider its \emph{G\^ateaux derivative} (or \emph{directional
    derivative}: contrary to the usual (Fr\'echet) derivative, 
    we do not require it to be linear, \ie, the
  derivative in the direction 
  $\vec u + \vec v$ need not be the sum of the derivatives in the directions 
  $\vec u$ and $\vec v$):
  $$ 
  \textrm{IF}(x,f,\hat G) = 
  \lim_{\epsilon \to 0}
  \dfrac1\epsilon \left(
    \hat G [ (1-\epsilon) f + \epsilon \delta^{(x)} ] - \hat G[f]
  \right).
  $$
\end{compactenum}

For maximum likelihood estimators (MLE), one can show that 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{IF}(x, f, \hat\theta) &=
  A \left. \dfrac{
    \partial \ln f_\theta
  }{
    \partial \theta
  }
  \right|_{ \theta = \hat\theta[f] } \\
  A &= - \left[
  \left.
    \int \dfrac{
      \partial^2 \ln f_\theta(x)
    }{
      \partial \theta \, \partial \theta'
    }
  \right|_{\theta = \hat\theta[f]}
  f(x) \, dx
  \right]^{-1}.
\end{align*}
The influence functions of the gaussian MLE of location and dispersion are not bounded: they are not robust; for other elliptical distribution, they are.

More generally, \emph{M-estimators} (or \emph{generalized MLE}) are
obtained by modifying the log-likelihood so that the influence be
bounded; M-estimators of location and dispersion are actually weighted
means and variances.

% Outliers
\textbf{Outliers}

The \emph{breakdown point} of an estimator is the proportion of
outliers it can sustain while maintaining a bounded influence
function.

The \emph{minimum volume ellipsoid} (MVE: find the smallest ellipsoid
containing $x$\% of the data, for various values of $x$; if there is a
jump in the volume, we know there are outliers and we know how many)
and the \emph{minimum covariance determinan} (MCD: the minimum value
of the determinant of the sample covariance matrix containing $x$\% of
the data)
have a high breakdown point and are usually computed in a greedy and
approximate way, by discarding the observations one at a time.

% Missing data
\textbf{Missing data}

To compute location and dispersion parameters in presence of
\emph{missing values}, one can do better than discarding incomplete
observations. In dimension 2, if the time series are complete but have
different lengths, there is an explicit formula (Stambaugh). In the
general case, use the \emph{expectation-maximization} (EM) algorithm.

Beware: the EM algorithm is not a data imputation algorithm. Indeed,
since the values are replaced by their expected values, they will have
a much lower dispersion; in particular, you cannot naively use them to
compute a dispersion parameter (the EM algorithm explains how to
compensate for that in the case of the variance -- some books forget
that compensation in their presentation of the algorithm).

% Weighted estimates
\textbf{Weighted estimates}

Since more recent observations contain less stale information, they
can be given a linear weight, either with a \emph{moving window} or
\emph{exponential smoothing} -- to find the \emph{devcay factor}, just
put it in the log-likelihood formula for $\hat\mu$ and $\hat\Sigma$ --
this is actually consistent with a GARCH model.

If the location is known to be close to zero (with respect to the
dispersion), you can assume it actually is zero: this is a
\emph{shrinkage estimator}.

One can also estimate location, dispersion or any other parameter
using a \emph{pricing model}, \ie, choosing the parameters so that the
model-implied price be as close as possible from the market price.

% Chapter 5: Evaluation allocations
\textbf{Chapter 5: Evaluation allocations}

The \emph{investor's objective} can be expressed in monetary terms:
usually the final wealth or the change in wealth (when the absolute
value of wealth does not matter) or the difference between the final
wealth and a benchmark.

% Dominance
\textbf{Dominance}

An allocation $\alpha$ \emph{strongly dominates} an allocation $\beta$
if $\Psi_\alpha \geq \Psi_\beta$ a,s, where $\Psi_\alpha$ is the
investor's objective coming from allocation $\alpha$ (it is a random
variable). Strong dominance (also called \emph{zeroth-order
  dominance}) rarely happens and relies on the \emph{join}
distribution $(\Psi_\alpha, \Psi_\beta)$.

\emph{Weak dominance} (or \emph{first-order dominance}) is defined as
$$ 
\forall \psi \in \RR 
\qquad
F_{ \Psi_\alpha } (\psi) \leq F_{ \Psi_\beta } (\psi) 
$$
where $F$ is the cumulative distribution function.

\begin{center}
\setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm,height=3cm}
<<MeucciWeakDominance,echo=FALSE,results='hide',fig.width=8,fig.height=3,out.width="8cm">>=
area_plot <- function (x, y, border=NA, ...) {
  polygon(c(x[1], x, x[length(x)]), c(0, y, 0), border=border, ...)
  lines(x, y)
}
library(RColorBrewer)
cols <- brewer.pal(3, "Set3")
op <- par(mfrow=c(1,2), mar=c(1.5,1,0,1), oma=c(0,0,2,0))
x <- seq(-3,3,length=500)
y1 <- dnorm(x, -.5, .5) / dnorm(0,0,.5)
y2 <- dnorm(x, .5, .5) / dnorm(0,0,.5)
plot.new()
plot.window(xlim=c(-3,3), ylim=c(0,1))
area_plot(x, y1, col=cols[1])
area_plot(x, y2, col=cols[2])
abline(h=0, lty=3)
title(xlab="pdf", line=0)

y1 <- pnorm(x, -.5, .5)
y2 <- pnorm(x, .5, .5)
plot.new()
plot.window(xlim=c(-3,3), ylim=c(0,1))
area_plot(x, y1, col=cols[1])
area_plot(x, y2, col=cols[2])
abline(h=0, lty=3)
title(xlab="cdf", line=0)
par(op)

mtext("Weak dominance", line=3, font=2, cex=1.2)
@
\setkeys{Gin}{width=6cm,height=6cm}
\end{center}

Equivalently: 
$$ \forall p \in [0,1]
\qquad
Q_{ \Psi_\alpha } (\psi) \geq Q_{ \Psi_\beta } (\psi) 
$$
where $Q$ is the quantile function.

\emph{Second-order stochastic dominance} (SSD) is defined as 
$$ \forall \psi \in \RR
\qquad
E[ \Psi_\alpha - \psi) ^- ] \geq
E[ \Psi_\beta  - \psi) ^- ],
$$
\ie, for every benchmark $\psi$, the underperformance of $\alpha$ is
not as bad as that of $\beta$.
Equivalently: 
$$ \forall \psi \in \RR \qquad
I^2 [ f_{\Psi_\alpha} ] (\psi)
\geq
I^2 [ f_{\Psi_\beta}  ] (\psi)
$$
where 
$$
I^2 [ f_\Psi ] (\psi) =
I   [ F_\Psi ] (\psi) =
\int_{-\infty} ^\psi F_\Psi (s) ds.
$$
These definitions can be generalized to \emph{order-$q$ dominance} --
but these are not easily interpretable and still fail to yield a total
order.

% Satisfaction
\textbf{Satisfaction}

Instead of trying to assess the whole distribution of the investor's
objective $\Psi_\alpha$, one can try to summarize an allocation
$\alpha$ into a single number $S(\alpha)$, an \emph{index of
  satisfaction}. We might want it to satisfy some of the following properties: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item money-equivalence: it should be measured in units of money;
\item sensibility, \ie, consistence with strong dominance;
\item consistence with stochastic dominance;
\item constancy: if the distribution of the investor's objective is
  concentrated in a single point, $\Psi_\alpha = \delta_{ \psi }$, then
  $S(\alpha) = \psi$;
\item positive homogeneity of degree 1 (the investor's objective already is):
  $$ 
  \forall \alpha       \quad
  \forall \lambda > 0  \quad
  S(\lambda \alpha) = \lambda S(\alpha)
  $$
  in particular, this gives the \emph{contribution} of each asset
  (Euler's formula):
  $$ 
  S(\alpha) = \sum_{n=1}^N \alpha_n \dfrac{ \partial S(\alpha) }{ \partial \alpha_n }
  $$
\item translation invariance (translation by a deterministic allocation): if 
  $\Psi_b = \delta_{\psi_b}$, then $S(\alpha + b) = S(\alpha) + \psi_b$;
\item super-additivity (if you focus on risk and not satisfaction, you
  will call this \emph{subadditivity}):
  $$ \forall \alpha,\,\beta \qquad S(\alpha + \beta) \geq S(\alpha) + S(\beta) $$
\item comonotonic additivity (two allocations $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are
  \emph{comonotonic} if their objectives are increasing functions of
  one another, for instance a stock and a call option on it): if
  $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are comonotonic, then $S(\alpha + \beta) =
  S(\alpha) + S(\beta)$, \ie, the index of satisfaction is option-proof;
\item concavity (results from homogeneity and superadditivity) , \ie,
  the index of satisfactionpromotes diversification;
\item risk aversion: 
  if $\Psi_b = \delta_\psi$ and $E\Psi_\alpha = 0$ then
  $S(b) \geq S(b+\alpha)$.
\end{compactenum}

Examples include:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item the expected value of the objective, $S(\alpha) = E \Psi_\alpha$;
\item the \emph{Sharpe ratio} 
  $$\textrm{SR}(\alpha) = \dfrac{ E \Psi_\alpha }{ \textrm{Sd}\, \Psi_\alpha }$$
  (it is not expressed in monetary units; it is homogeneous of degree 0, not 1);
\item the certainty-equivalent;
\item the value at risk;
\item expected shortfall.
\end{compactenum}

% Certainty-equivalent
\textbf{Certainty-equivalent}

Given a (continuous) increasing \emph{utility function} $u$, the \emph{certainty equivalent} is 
$$ \textrm{CE}(\alpha) = u^{-1} E [ u( \Psi_\alpha ) ]; $$
this is the value of the investor's objective of a deterministic
investment having the same expected utility as $\alpha$ -- \ie, this
is the expected utility expressed in monetary units thanks to the
(inverse of the) utility function:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item it is only homogeneous for \emph{power utility}, 
  $u(\psi) = \psi^{ 1 - 1/\gamma }$, $\gamma \geq 1$;
\item it is not comonotonic additive (except for a linear utility);
\item it is not superadditive (except for a linear utility);
\item in general, it is neither nor convex (in particular, the
  convexity of the utility function is not linked to the convexity of
  the certainty equivalent);
\item if the utility is concave, it is risk-averse.
\end{compactenum}

For theoretical purposes, the utility function can be written 
$$ 
u(\psi) = \int g(\theta) H^{(\theta)}(\psi) \, d\theta,
\quad g \geq 0, 
\quad \int g = 1
$$
where $H$ is the Heaviside function (this generates all increasing
functions) or
$$ 
u(\psi) = \int g(\theta) \min(\psi,\theta) \, d\theta,
\quad g \geq 0, 
\quad \int g = 1
$$
(which generates all concave functions).

(I do not understand the explanations about the investor's
\emph{subjective probability}.)

A utility function is entirely determined (up to a positive affine
transformation) by its \emph{Arrow--Pratt absolute risk aversion},
$$ A(\psi) = - \dfrac{ u''(\psi) }{ u'(\psi) }. $$
The \emph{Pearson specification} includes the following particular cases:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item hyperbolic absolute risk aversion (HARA, always concave):
  $$ A(\psi) = \dfrac1{\gamma \psi + \zeta} $$
\item exponential utility: $ u(\psi) = - \exp - \dfrac \psi \zeta $
\item quadratic utility (beware, it is not sensible for $\psi > \zeta$):
  $$ u(\psi) = \psi - \dfrac{ \psi^2 }{ 2 \zeta } $$
\item power utility: $ u(\psi) = \psi^{ 1 - 1/\gamma } $
\item logarithmix utility: $ u(\psi) = \ln \psi $
\item linear utility: $ u(\psi) = \psi. $
\end{compactenum}

The Arrow--Pratt risk aversion also yields approximations of the
certainty equivalent and the \emph{risk premium}: 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{CE}(\alpha) 
  &\approx E \Psi_\alpha - \tfrac12 A(E\Psi_\alpha) \var \Psi_\alpha \\
  \textrm{RP}(\alpha) 
  &= E[\Psi_\alpha] - S(\alpha) \\
  &= E[\Psi_\alpha] - \textrm{CE}(\alpha) \\
  &\approx \tfrac12 A(E\Psi_\alpha) \var \Psi_\alpha
\end{align*}

In \emph{prospect theory}, utility functions are S-shaped: concave
(risk-averse) for profits and convex (risk-prone) for losses.

% Quantile (VaR)
\textbf{Quantile (VaR)}

The \emph{value at risk} (VaR), defined as 
$$Q_c(\alpha) = Q_{\Psi_\alpha}(1-c),$$ is 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item not consistent with second-order dominance (the definitions of
  second-order dominance and of the expected shortfall are very
  similar);
\item consistent with first-order dominance;
\item not super-additive: it fails to promote diversity;
\item comonotonic additive (it is not fooled by derivatives);
\item positive homogeneous;
\item neither concave not convex;
\item not risk-averse.
\end{compactenum}

The VaR can be computed with: a lot of data, or a gaussian assumption,
or the \emph{Cornish-Fisher expansion} (which is an approximation of
the quantile function, but it need not be particularly accurate for
the extreme values we are interested in), or \emph{extreme value
  theory} (EVT) (which requires enough data in the tails -- but in the
1\% tail, you only have 1\% of your data).

% Coherent indices, spectral indices, expected shortfall
\textbf{Coherent indices, spectral indices, expected shortfall}

An index of satisfaction is \emph{coherent} if it is
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item consistent with strong dominance;
\item positive homogeneous;
\item translation invariant;
\item superadditive.
\end{compactenum}
This implies money-equivalence and concavity. The \emph{one-sided
  moments} are coherent (but they are not comonotonic additive).

A coherent index of satisfaction is \emph{spectral} if it is estimable
(?) and comonotonic additive. This implies weak stochastic dominance
and risk aversion.

Spectral indices are of the form 
$$ \textrm{Spc}_\phi(\alpha) = \int_0^1 \phi(p) Q_{\Psi_\alpha}(p) \, dp $$
where $\phi$, the \emph{spectrum}, is decreasing, $\phi(1)=0$ and
$\int_0^1\phi=1$.

% Chapter 6: Optimizing allocations
\textbf{Chapter 6: Optimizing allocations}

Portfolio construction proceeds as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Define the investor's objective;
\item Define his index of satisfaction;
\item Model the market invariants;
\item Project the market invariants to the investment horizon;
\item Gather other information: legal constraints, transaction costs;
\item Maximize the investor's satisfaction -- if there are several
  indices of satistaction, maximize the first subject to bounds on the
  others.
\end{compactenum}

The author details an example with a closed form solution: total
wealth, certainty equivalent for the exponential utility, gaussian
prices (the certainty equivalent is then quadratic), linear
transaction costs, constraint on the value at risk.

The classes of optimization problems that can be efficiently solved
include, from the most specific to the most general:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Linear programming (LP);
\item Quadratic programming (QP);
\item Quadratically-constrained linear programming (QCLP);
\item Second-order cone programming (SOCP: the \emph{ice cream} constraints);
\item Semi-definite programming (SDP);
\item Cone programming (up to here, interior point methods are available);
\item Convex programming.
\end{compactenum}

Most of the time, e.g., with value at risk or certainty equivalent, we
are not that lucky: the optimization problem is not convex. The
\emph{mean-variance approximation} can make this problem amenable:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Express the satisfaction as a function of the moments of the market distribution, 
  $$ S(\alpha) = H( E[\Psi_\alpha], 
                    \textrm{CM}_2 (\Psi_\alpha), 
                   \textrm{CM}_3 (\Psi_\alpha), 
                    \dots ) $$
(this is obtained from a Taylor expansion of the utility function 
or the Cornish--Fisher expansion of the value at risk); 
the problem is now infinite-dimensional: 
$$ 
\entrymodifiers={!!<0pt,0.7ex>+}
\xymatrix @!0 @R=1.2em @C=7pc {
  \RR^n \ar[r] & \RR^{\NN} \ar[r] & \RR \\
  \alpha \ar@{|->}[r] & 
  (E[\Psi_\alpha], \textrm{CM}_2 (\Psi_\alpha), \dots) \ar@{|->}[r] & 
  S(\alpha)
}$$
\item Assume that the index of satisfaction is well approximated by
  the first two moments
$$ S(\alpha) \approx \tilde H( E[\Psi_\alpha], \textrm{CM}_2 (\Psi_\alpha)) $$
\item Since the index of satisfaction is consistent with weak
  stochastic dominance, the optimal allocation is on the
  \emph{efficient frontier}
  $$ 
  \alpha(v) = \mathop{\textrm{Argmax}}\limits_{\substack{
    \alpha \in \mathscr{C} \\ 
    \var \Psi_\alpha = v 
  } }
  E[\Psi_\alpha],
  v \geq 0
  $$
\item You now just have to maximize the index of satisfaction on the efficient frontier.
\end{compactenum}

The approximation of $S(\alpha)$ from the first two moments is valid
in the following cases:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The market prices $\Psi_\alpha$ are elliptical (e.g., gaussian),
  so that their distribution is entirely determined by the first two
  moments (regardless of the index of satisfaction); this is wrong for
  derivatives and even for stock prices (it has to be prices, not
  their logarithms) -- but for short horizons, this is good enough an
  approximation;
\item The index of satisfaction really depends only on the first two
  moments (regardless of the distribution of the market invariants).  
\end{compactenum}

Contrary to what many people believe, the risk aversion parameter
$\lambda$ in $ E[ \Psi_\alpha ] - \lambda \var \Psi_\alpha $ is not a
feature of the investor and it does not define an index of
satisfaction: its value also depends on the market -- in some extreme
cases, it only depends on the market and not on the investor; choosing
it beforehand yields allocations inconsistent with strong dominance.

The mean-variance approximation is a two-step process: first compute
the (approximate) efficient frontier, then maximize the index of satisfaction
on this frontier. The one-step mean-variance approximation, \ie, fixing
the ``Lagrange multiplier'' $\lambda$, is only valid under very strong
assumptions, e.g., gaussian prices and exponential utility.

The following problems are usually considered equivalent, but this is
only the case for affine constraints:
\begin{align*}
\textrm{Maximize } E   [\Psi_\alpha] &\textrm{ such that } \var \Psi_\alpha = v \\
\textrm{Maximize } E   [\Psi_\alpha] &\textrm{ such that } \var \Psi_\alpha \leq v \\
\textrm{Minimize } \var \Psi_\alpha  &\textrm{ such that } E   [\Psi_\alpha] \geq e.
\end{align*}

% Section 6.7 applies what we have seen so far.

% Part III: Estimation error

% Chapter 7: Estimators
\textbf{Chapter 7: Bayesian estimators}

Bayesian statistics differs from classical inference in two regards:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item we provide some \emph{prior} information as input;
\item the output is not a single number of vector but a whole
  distribution -- the \emph{posterior}.
\end{compactenum}

A \emph{classical-equivalent} estimator is the single number (or
vector) obtained as a location parameter of the posterior distribution
-- e.g., \emph{maximum a posteriori} (MAP) estimators, Bayes--Stein
estimators, shrinkage estimators.

For some prior and model distributions (called \emph{conjugate
  distributions}), the posterior is computable in closed form; for
instance, the \emph{normal inverse Wishart} distribution specifies the
joint distribution of $(\mu, \Sigma)$ as
\begin{align*}
  \mu | \Sigma & \sim N \left( \mu_0, \dfrac{ \Sigma }{ T_0 } \right) \\
  \Sigma^{-1}  & \sim W \left( \nu_0, \dfrac{ \Sigma_0^{-1} }{ \nu_0 } \right) \\
\intertext{and the data is}
  X_t \,|\, \mu, \Sigma & \sim N(\mu, \Sigma).
\end{align*}

Similar computations can be performed with factor models.

The prior distribution (or its location parameter) can be defined by
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item inverting the unconstrained allocation function 
  $\theta \mapsto \alpha(\theta) = \textrm{Argmax}\, S_\theta(\alpha)$, 
  \ie, finding the $\theta$ for which the market weights maximize the
  satisfaction index (the Black--Litterman prior is of this kind) --
  you might want to add a few constraints, though;
\item a (constrained) maximim likelihood estimator.
\end{compactenum}

% Chapter 8: Evaluating allocations
\textbf{Chapter 8: Evaluating allocations under uncertainty}

An \emph{allocation} is not a single set of weights but a function (a random variable)
$$ \textrm{available information} \longmapsto \textrm{weights}. $$
The \emph{cost of randomness} is the difference between the
satisfaction of the best portfolio given perfect insight, \ie, the
portfolio with the highest ex-post returns (it is likely to contain a
single security) and the optimal (diversified) portfolio.

The \emph{opportunity cost} (OC) is the difference between the
satisfaction of the optimal allocation (assuming perfect knowledge of
the market distribution) and that of the allocation actually chosen;
constraint violations should be expressed in monetary terms (often in
an ad hoc way).

Cost of randomness and opportunity cost are random variables. 

\emph{Prior allocation}, \ie, allocation that does not use the
information available, is the analogue of a fixed estimator: it is
extremely biased.

\emph{Sample-based allocations} are not too biased but have a very
scattered opportunity cost (they are inefficient): the optimal
allocation function is very sensitive to its inputs and
\emph{leverages} estimation error.

% Chapter 9: Optimizing allocations
\textbf{Chapter 9: Shrinkage allocation decisions}

\emph{Bayesian allocation} maximizes the expected utility of the
investor's objective, but the expectation is computed with respect to
the posterior distribution; this is the analogue of a
classical-equivalent bayesian estimator and the opportunity costs are
less scattered than with sample-based allocation. Bayesian methods are
\emph{non-linear shrinkage} methods.

\emph{Black--Litterman allocation} shrinks, not the market parameters,
but the market distribution, towards the investor's prior. The
investor provides a random variable $V$ that depends on the as-yet
unknown market invariants $X$, for instance, $V \sim N(w'X, \phi^2)$,
where $X$ are the market returns, $w'$ a portfolio on which the
investor has a view and $\phi^2$ the confidence of this view. Given a
realization $v$ of $V$ and the knowledge of the distribution of $V|X$,
we can compute the Black--Litterman distribution $X\,|\,V=v$ and then
the corresponding Black--Litterman allocation decision. The prior $X$
need not be the market invariants, but can be an ``official'' model.
In the gaussian, linear case, the computations are straightforward.

The Mahalanobis distance between the market expectations $\mu$ and the
Black--Litterman expectations $\mu_{\textrm{BL}}$ follows a $\chi^2$
distribution, which can be turned into a $p$-value, to spot views in
contradiction with the prior. To identify which view is responsible,
just differentiate this $p$-value with respect to each view.

\emph{Resampled allocation} proceeds as follows: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Estimate the market parameters $\hat\theta$ from the data;
\item Create nes samples, by parametric bootstrap;
\item For each sample $q$, estimate the market parameters $\theta_q$
  and the corresponding optimal allocation $\alpha_p$;
\item Average those optimal allocations.
\end{compactenum}
However, resampled allocation can violate investment constraints
(e.g., the maximum number of securities) and are difficult to
stress-test -- see Scherer's book for more arguments against it.

\emph{Robust allocation} replaces point estimates of market parameters
by \emph{uncertainty regions}:
$\mathop{\textrm{Argmax}}\limits_\alpha S(\alpha, \theta)$
becomes 
$$ 
\mathop{\textrm{Argmax}}_\alpha 
\min_{\theta \in \Theta}
S(\alpha, \theta).
$$
This depends on the choice (size, shape) of the uncertainty regions;
with elliptical uncertainty regions, the problem is a second order
cone program (SOCP).

Since bayesian methods output a whole distribution instead of a single value, 
\emph{robust bayesian allocation} uses the corresponding location-dispersion
ellipsoids as uncertainty sets; the radius of the chosen ellipsoid is the
investor's avertion to estimation risk. Under gaussian assumptions, the 
robust, bayesian, (two-step) mean-variance framework is amenable to explicit
computations.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Beyond Black--Litterman:\\ views on non-normal markets}\\
A. Meucci\\
Risk (2006)
\end{book}

The Black--Litterman framework can be generalized to a non-gaussian framework using the \emph{copula-opinion pooling} (COP) methodology: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Choose a prior distribution (e.g., by an equilibrium argument,
  as in the traditiional BL framework, or by backward-looking
  estimation, or with forward-looking market-implied values -- the
  prior could be a company-wide model on which individual managers
  would add their views),
  $$ V \sim (f_M, F_M, \phi_M) $$
  ($f_M$ is the probability distribution function (pdf), 
  $F_M$ the cumulative distribution function (cdf),
  $\phi_M$ the characteristic function);
\item Choose the views $V = P M$ (each row of $P$ is a portfolio;
  those portfolios should be independant) and the (marginal)
  distribution of each of them,
  $$ \hat V_k \sim (f_{\hat V_k}, F_{\hat V_k}, \phi_{\hat V_k}) $$
\item Blend these distributions with those,
$(f_{V_k}, F_{V_k}, \phi_{V_k}) $
induced by the market (separately for each view):
$$ \tilde V_k \sim f_{ \tilde V_k } = (1 - c_k) f_{V_k} + c_k f_{\hat V_k} $$
\item Compute the joint posterior distribution of the views, by combining the marginal posterior distribution with the copula induced by the market distribution;
\item Complete $P$ into a basis:
$$ 
  \begin{pmatrix} V \\ W       \end{pmatrix} = 
  \begin{pmatrix} P \\ P^\perp \end{pmatrix} M
\qquad \textrm{or} \qquad
  M = 
  \begin{pmatrix} P \\ P^\perp \end{pmatrix} ^{-1}
  \begin{pmatrix} V \\ W       \end{pmatrix}
$$
and compute the posteror distribution
$$ \tilde M = 
  \begin{pmatrix} P        \\ P^\perp \end{pmatrix} ^{-1}
  \begin{pmatrix} \tilde V \\ W       \end{pmatrix}.
$$
\end{compactenum}
The computations can be carried out explicitely with a skewed $T$
prior and uniform views; the result can be used in an expected
shortfall optimization.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Beyond Black--Litterman in practice}\\
A. Meucci\\
Risk (2006)
\end{book}

The COP approach can be applied even if the prior is not amenable to
computations, by representing it by a large number (100,000) of Monte
Carlo simulations:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Generate Monte-Carlo samples from the market prior: $M$;
\item Turn those samples into marginal view priors: $V = P M$;
\item Compute the view posterior distributions, using the cdf of the investor's views;
\item Compute the market copula;
\item Combine the view posterior marginal distributions with the market copula;
\item Compute the market distribution: 
$$ 
M = 
\begin{pmatrix} P        \\ P^\perp   \end{pmatrix} ^{-1} 
\begin{pmatrix} \tilde V \\ P^\perp M \end{pmatrix}.
$$
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Convex vs concave dynamic allocation}\\
A. Meucci (2002)
\end{book}

In a 2-asset world (cash and a stock), the author compares
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item a buy-and-hold strategy;
\item a call option (or the corresponding replicating portfolio);
\item portfolio insurance (CPPI -- the article clearly explains what it is);
\item a power-utility maximizing strategy.
\end{compactenum}
Portfolio insurance and call options yield a very similar payoff
(indeed, the strategies are almost identical), while the power-utility
maximizing strategy has a concave payoff (you would prefer a convex
one).

The plots of the payoff of those four strategies are impressive -- if
you want to promote CPPI or badmouth portfolio optimization (but the
results are specific to power-utility).

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Broadening horizons}\\
A. Meucci\\
Risk Magazine (2004)
\end{book}

Care should be taken when estimating the market distribution at a
short (1 month) horizon and using it at a longer (severa years) one:
use the characteristic function. The article details this switch
between logarithmic and linear returns in a gaussian world and applies
the results to portfolio optimization.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Robust bayesian allocation}\\
A. Meucci
\end{book}

Robust bayesian allocation yields an efficient frontier parametrized
by 3 parameters: the target variance, the size of the confidence
ellipsoid on the mean, the size of the confidence ellipsoid on the
variance matrix -- but the efficient frontier is actually
1-dimensional, those 3 parameters, mixing market risk and estimation
risk, can be combined in a single parameter.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Bayesian diagnostics for portfolio allocation}\\
F. Corielli and A. Meucci
\end{book}

To decide whether to or how much to rebalance your portfolio towards
the optimal portfolio, do not compare the composition of the
portfolios (this is not robust) but their utility.

This articles actually compares the investor's views and the
equilibrium portfolio, in the Black--Litterman framework, and gauges
how far from the equilibrium one can go; this will be different for
each view: check the derivative of the utility wrt the confidence in
each view.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{A common pitfall\\ in mean-variance asset allocation}\\
A. Meucci\\
Wilmott (2001)
\end{book}

When using mean-variance asset allocation, investors often mix linear returns
and log-returns: the risk matrix is estimated using log-returns (the
variance of the ratio returns is then: 
\begin{align*}
  \mu_{\textrm{ratio}} &= 
  \exp( \mu + \tfrac12 \textrm{diag}\, \Sigma ) - 1 \\
  \Sigma_{\textrm{ratio}} &=
  \exp( \mu + \tfrac12 \textrm{diag}\, \Sigma )' 
  (\exp \Sigma - 1)
  \exp( \mu + \tfrac12 \textrm{diag}\, \Sigma )
\end{align*}
but people rarely bother computing it -- furthermore, the
mean-variance framework assumes that the ratio returns follow an elliptical
distribution), the forecasted alphas are ratio returns but are
projected to a longer horizon as if they were log-returns. In the
short term, those mistakes are harmless, but lead to very different
allocations for long-term strategies -- the author suspiciously finds
that long-term investors should perfer less volatile assets.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Assessing views}\\
G. Fusai and A. Meucci\\
Risk (2003)
\end{book}

When using the Black--Litterman framework to blend a general model
(usually, universal equilibrium) with views, you may want to check how
far from the model those views are: this can be measured with the
Mahalanobis distance between the model alpha and the Black--Litterman
alpha (they use a RiskMetrics variance matrix, \ie, an
exponentially-weighted sample non-central variance matrix). The distance can
be brought back to $[0,1]$ assuming a $\chi^2_N$ distribution ($N$ is
the number of assets). If the distance is too large and has to be
reduced, you can choose which view to alter by differentiating the
distance with respect to the returns of the views and selecting the
view with the highest derivative.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Pitfalls in linear models for style analysis}\\
F. Coriellu and A. Meucci\\
Statistical methods and applications (2004)
\end{book}

Debunking bad econometrics (a case study for an econometrics (OLS)
course): it is usually a bad idea to consider returns and is
preferable to use prices and numbers of shares; some models that are
often assumed linear are actually not linear and should be tackled
with non-linear regression. There is no loss of parsimony: this is
simply ``the model you had in mind'', with exactly the same
parameters, instead of an approximation. Using non-linear regression
might not be that good an idea if you want reliable estimates, but is
preferable if you want to compare models.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Anomalies in the foundations\\ of ridge regression}\\
D.R. Jensen and D.E. Ramirez\\
\texttt{arXiv:math.ST/0703551}
\end{book}

Many people claim, without proof, that \emph{ridge regression}, \ie,
replacing the ordinary least squares (OLS) estimator
$$ \hat \beta = (X'X)^{-1} X'Y $$
by the biased estimator
$$ \hat \beta = (X'X + \lambda I)^{-1} X'Y $$
is equivalent to a constrained regression
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Minimize }  & (Y - X\beta)' (Y - X\beta) \\
  \textrm{such that } & \beta' \beta \leq c^2
\end{align*}
or
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Minimize }  & (Y - X\beta)' (Y - X\beta) \\
  \textrm{such that } & \beta' \beta = c^2.
\end{align*}
This is actually wrong.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Forecasting the CATS benchmark with the double vector quantization method}\\
G. Simon et al.\\
\texttt{arXiv:math.ST/0703143}
\end{book}

Double vector quantization (DVQ) can be used to predict a time series
(the data comes from the
\href{http://www.cis.hut.fi/~lendasse/competition/competition.html}{competition
  on artifitial time series (CATS)} of 
the IJCNN 2004 (international joint
conference on neural networks)):
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The data is modelled as a non-linear auto-regressive (NAR) time
  series whose order is computed from the \emph{correlation dimension}
\begin{align*}
p &= 2 D_{\textrm{cor}} + 1 \\
D_{\textrm{cor}} &= \lim_{r \to 0} \dfrac{ \ln C(r) }{ \ln r } \\
C(r) &= \lim_{n\to\infty} \dfrac2{n(n-1)} \sum_{1\leq t < t'\leq n} 
\textbf{1}( \n{x_t - x_{t'}} < r );
\end{align*}
$C(r)$ is the (normalized) number of points in a hypersphere of radius
$r$ centered on $x_t$; $D_{\textrm{cor}}$ can be estimated by plotting
$C(r)$ versus $r$ and looking at the slope of the curve in the middle
of the plot;
\item Compute a self-organizing map (SOM -- any other
  \emph{quantization} algorithm would do) for the
  $\textbf{x}_t = (x_t,\dots,x_{t-p+1})$ 
  and another one for the \emph{deformation regressors}
  $\textbf{y}_t = \textbf{x}_t - \textbf{x}_{t-1}$;
\item Compute the transition probabilities from the cells of the first
  map to those of the second; use them to \emph{simulate} (do not just
  take the most probable values) the missing values.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Measuring financial contagion:\\ a copula approach}\\
J.C. Rodriguez\\
Journal of empirical finance (2007)
\end{book}

You can model pairs of exchange rates, around crises, using switching copulas: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item the marginals are SWARCH (switching ARCH) models;
\item the copula is a mixture of Frank, Clayton and Gumbel copulas;
  the parameters do not depend on the state variable but the weights
  do.
\end{compactenum}
The model is compared with a switching Student copula with SWARCH
marginals using the AICC (the AIC corrected for small sample size;
this is better than the BIC is you are more interested in model
selection that actual forecasts). Tests are then performed on the
upper (and lower) \emph{tail dependence}:
\begin{align*}
\lambda_u 
&= \lim_{u\to1} P \left[ Y > G^{-1}(u) \,|\, X > F^{-1}(u) \right] \\
&= \lim_{u\to1} \dfrac{ 1 - 2 u + C(u,u) }{ 1 - u } \\
\lambda_l
&= \lim_{u\to0} P \left[ Y < G^{-1}(u) \,|\, X < F^{-1}(u) \right] \\
&= \lim_{u\to0} \dfrac{ C(u,u) }{  u }
\end{align*}
where $F$ and $G$ are the cdf of $X$ and $Y$ and $C$ their copula.

Asian currency crises exhibit tail dependance and asymetry, while
latin American ones do not.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Specification and estimation of discrete time quadratic stochastic volatility models}\\
H. Kawakatsu\\
Journal of empirical finance (2007)
\end{book}

The log-quadratic stochastic volatility model is: 
\begin{align*}
  y_t &= \sigma_t u_t \\
  \ln \sigma_t^2 &= a_0 + a_1 x_t + a_2 x_t^2 \\
  x_{t+1} &= \phi x_t + \sqrt{1-\phi^2} v_{t+1}
  \begin{pmatrix} u_t \\ v_{t+1} \end{pmatrix} \sim 
  N \left(0, \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \rho \\ \rho & 1 \end{pmatrix} \right)
\end{align*}

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{A closed form approach to the valuation and hedging of basket and spread options}\\
S. Borovkova et al.\\
Journal of derivatives (2007)
\end{book}

Option pricing often relies on a log-normal distribution. When the
underlying is a linear combination of assets, sometimes with negative
coefficients (\emph{commodity basket options}, e.g., \emph{spread
  options}: spark spread (difference between electrivity and gas
price), crack spread (difference between crude oil and refined
products), soybean crush spread), this no longer works: instead, one
can use a shifted and/or reflected log-normal distribution.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{An algorithm for simulating bermudan option prices on simulated asset prices}\\
B.N. Huge and N. Rom-Poulsen\\
Journal of derivatives (2007)
\end{book}
How to price options when the underlying (e.g., an already complicated
option) has itself to be priced by Monte Carlo simulation -- in
particular, the simulated prices will be too volatile and this bias
should be corrected.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Algebraic statistical models}\\
M. Drton and S. Sullivant\\
\texttt{arXiv:math.ST/0703609}
\end{book}

An \emph{algebraic exponential family} is a semi-algrbraic subset (up
to diffeomorphism) of a regular exponential family. It is
``well-behaved'': its maximum likelihood estimators are asymptotically
gaussian.

Using \emph{semi}-algebraic sets, \ie, allowing for inequalities, has
applications in phylogenetics with \emph{conditional independance
  models}, e.g., $X \perp\!\!\!\perp Y | Z \vee U \perp\!\!\!\perp Z$.

The article is very abstract: for instance, regular algebraic families
are defined in a non-constructive way, using the Laplace transform.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Transitional densities of diffusion processes:\\ a new approach to solving\\ the Fokker--Planck equation}\\
A.S. Hurn et al.\\
Journal of derivatives (2007)
\end{book}
Numerically computing the transition probability distribution function
(pdf) of a diffusion process via the Fokker--Planck PDE is tricky: the
initial condition is a Dirac measure. Instead, one can consider the
cumulative distribution function (cdf) and numerically differentiate
it.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{The implied volatility term structure\\ of stock index options}\\
S. Mixon\\
Journal of empirical finance (2007)
\end{book}
$
\left(
\dfrac{ 
  \partial \textrm{implied volatility} 
}{
  \partial \textrm{maturity}
}
\right)_{\textrm{ATM}}
$ can help predict future implied volatility.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{When is inter-transaction time informative?}\\
C. Furfine\\
Journal of empirical finance (2007)
\end{book}
Faster arriving trades move prices more than slower ones; the effect
is weaker for actively traded stocks; faster arriving stocks in the
same direction are more informative.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{The performance of hedge fund strategies and the asymetry of return distributions}\\
B. Ding and H.A. Shawky\\
European financial management
\end{book}
To study hedge funds, do not use returns but residual returns, \ie,
remove the effect of the market, the Fama--French factors and the
square of the market (the corresponding coefficient of the regression
is the \emph{coskewness}: that added term yields a higher-moment CAPM).

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Risk measures for hedge funds:\\ a cross-sectional approach}\\
B. Liang and H. Park\\
European financial management
\end{book}

To measure risk, prefer expected shortfall (ES) or \emph{tail risk}
(TR) (\ie, $E[ (X - \bar X)^2 \,|\, X < \textrm{VaR} ]$) to value at
risk or semi-variance.

The Cornish-Fisher expansion is preferable to a non-parametric
estimation of ES or TR -- the authors do no consider parametric
(extreme value theory, EVT) estimation -- the non-parametric approach
should become preferable with more (daily) data.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Hedge fund indices:\\ reconciling investability and representativity}\\
F. Goltz et al.\\
European financial management
\end{book}
It is possible to build investable and representative hedge fund
indices (classical HF indices are not investable).

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Sources of contrarian profits\\ in the Japanese stock market}\\
P.-H. Chou et al.\\
Journal of empirical finance (2007)
\end{book}

Reversal (with short (1 month) or long (2 years) horizons) works in
Japan, because of the \emph{lead-lag} effect, \ie, because of
\emph{cross-correlations} between the stock returns.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Industry information diffusion and the lead-lag effect in stock returns}\\
K. Hou (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{lead-lag} effect (past large-firm returns and future
small-firm returns are related) is an intra-industry phenomenon, more
pronounced in small (size, volume, market share), less competitive and
neglected (analyst overage, analyst dispersion) industries
(institutional ownership also plays a role).

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Portfolio selection with heavy tails}\\
N. Hyung and C.G. de Vries\\
Journal of empirical finance (2007)
\end{book}

For portfolio construction, first order tail expansions are not
precise enough: they lead to extreme allocations, with all the weight
in the asset with the thinest tail -- in fact, the tail thickness of a
portfolio is well approximated by that of its component with the
fatest tail.

The article derives asymptotic expansions of $P(X_1 + X_2 > s)$
assuming that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent and satisfy
$$ P(X > s) = A s^{-\alpha} \left(
  1 + B s^{-\beta} + o(s^{-\beta})
\right).
$$

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Are IPOs really overpriced?}\\
S.X. Zheng\\
Journal of empirical finance (2007)
\end{book}
Studies claiming that IPOs are overpriced often overlook their growth
potential (omitted variable problem).

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Interaction of stock return momentum\\ with earnings measures}\\
I. Figelman\\
Financial analysts journal (2007)
\end{book}
Among companies with poor past returns, companies with high ROE or low
quality (accruals) underperform.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Migration}\\
E.F. Fama and K.R. French\\
Financial analysts journal (2007)
\end{book}
The size premium can be explained by small caps becoming large caps
and conversely. This remains valid for the value premium -- but these
are ex post explanations.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Bayesian learning in financial markets: testing the relevance of information precision and price discovery}\\
N. Hautsch and D. Hess\\
Journal of financial and quantitative analysis (2007)
\end{book}

Investors are not blind to heteroskedasticity: use a ``quality''
factor in your models.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Small-world MCMC and convergence to multi-modal distributions: from slow-mixing to fast mixing}\\
Y. Guan and S.M. Krone\\
\texttt{arXiv:math.PR/0703021}
\end{book}

To reduce mixing time in an MCMC (Markov Chain Monte Carlo) simulation
on a multimodal space, you can use, as a proposal distribution, a
mixture of a thin-tailed and a fat-tailed distribution. This can be
interpreted in graph-theoretic terms as a small-world effect -- some
people will also see a Levy process.

The article also contains a theoretical (functional analysis)
presentation of continuous Markov chains.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{A generative model for feedback networks}\\
D.R. White et al. (2005)
\end{book}

There are many models of \emph{random networks}, bit not is
satisfactory. Here is yet another one, that mimicks kinship or
corporate alliances networks:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Select a node $i$, 
  $$ P(i) \propto \textrm{degree}(i)^{\alpha} $$
\item Select a distance $d$, 
  $$ P(d) \propto d^\beta $$
\item Generate a search path, from $i$, of length $d$, iteratively,
  randomly choosing a neighbour $l$, without going through the same
  node twice,
  $$ P(l) \propto 1 + u(l)^\gamma $$
where $u(l)$ is the unused degree of $l$
\item If this works (we do find a path), tha we add an edge between
  $i$ and the end of the path; otherwise, we add a new node and link
  it to $i$.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Times-series behavior of\\ share repurchases and dividends}\\
B.S. Lee and O.M. Rui\\
Journal of financial and quantitative analysis (2007)
\end{book}

The authors compare two hypotheses to explain the fall in the number
of companies paying dividends and the rise of buy-backs: either taxes
or temporary cash flows -- the latter is more likely.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Giving content to investor sentiment:\\ the role of media in the stock market}\\
P.C. Tetlock\\
Journal of Finance (2007)
\end{book}

The author processes a daily Wall Street Journal editorial over 16
years through a \emph{content analysis} software (General Inquirer,
non-free, non-commercial -- check 
\url{http://www.wjh.harward.edu/~inquirer/inqtabs.txt}
and 
\url{http://textanalysis.info}), counting the words in 100 predefined
categories; a principal component analysis (PCA) of the result yields
a \emph{pessimism measure}.

It predicts volume, volatility, depth, but not returns -- though it is
correlated with downwards pressure followed by a reversal.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{An examination of alternative portfolio rebalancing strategies applied to sector funds}\\
Journal of asset management (2007)
\end{book}

You should rebalance your portfolio (to make it equal-weighted), if
only to limit the effect of bubbles, but there are no significant
differences between rebalancing on a trigger or at regular intervals
(once a year on average for a portfolio made of index funds).

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Event studies\\ with a contaminated estimation period}\\
N. Aktas et al.\\
Journal of corporate finance (2007)
\end{book}

In event studies, the learning period can be contaminated, \ie, can already contain events (e.g., if you are looking for potential M\&A bidders). This can be taken into account with \emph{Markov switching models}, distinguishing between high and low periods. 

The article also reviews the tests classically used in event studies.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{All events induce variance:\\ analyzing abnormal returns\\ when effects vary accross firms}\\
S.E. Harrington and D.G. Shrider\\
Journal of financial and quantitative analysis (2007)
\end{book}

Beware of heteroskedasticity in event studies -- prefer robust tests. 

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{A theory for long-memory\\ in supply and demand}\\
F. Lillo et al. (2005)
\end{book}

The long memory of the sign of trades can be explained by
\emph{market clearing} and hidden orders.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Statistical mechanics of\\ liquidity providers and liquidity takers}\\
J.D. Farmer\\
Barclays Capital (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{market impact} is not a ``second order effect'' as some claim,
but the main price formation mechanism (buyer-initiated trades raise
the price, seller-initiated ones lower it).

The statistical properties of the price impact can be examined as
follows: plot the price impact (logarithm of the ratio of prices)
aggregated over 2 (resp. 4, 8, 16, \dots, 1024) trades versus the
corresponding aggregated signed volume. After rescaling, you get:

\begin{center}
<<SMLPLT1,results='hide',echo=FALSE>>=
  sigmoid <- function (x) {
    ifelse(x < 500, exp(x) / (1 + exp(x)), 1)
  }
  curve( sigmoid(100 * x), col="black", lwd=2,
         xlim=c(-2,2), ylim=c(0,1), axes = FALSE, 
         xlab = "Aggregated signed volume",
         ylab = "Aggregated price impact")
  box()
  curve( sigmoid(3   * x), col="red",   lwd=2, add=TRUE )
  curve( sigmoid(1   * x), col="blue",  lwd=2, add=TRUE )
  legend("topleft", c("1", "16", "512"), col=c("black", "red", "blue"), lwd=3, lty=1)
@ 
\end{center}

This shape can be explained by the following model: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item the volume follows a power law: $p(v) = 1/v^{\alpha+1}$;
\item the impact is $f(v) = \textrm{sign}\, v \cdot \va v ^\beta$
\end{compactenum}
which also gives the slope of the limiting curve: 
$$ 
\dfrac{
  \textrm{aggregated returns}
}{
  \textrm{aggregated signed}
}
\sim ~ N^{-\kappa}
$$
where $N$ is the number of aggregated transactions and $\kappa$
depends on $\alpha$ and $\beta$.

However, this does not give the whole picture: it does not account for
the following two facts:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item the orders that arrive are not iid;
\item the volume sign has \emph{long-memory}: if you plot the log
  autocorrelation versus the log of the lag, you get a line, \ie, the
  autocorrelation decreases very slowly: even after two weeks
  (remember, these are high-frequency data), autocorrelation is still
  statistically significant. (As far as statistical estimation is
  concerned, this is bad news: the error of most estimators decrease
  as $1/N^{1-H}$, where $H$ is the \emph{Hurst exponent} (expect it to be around
  3/4), instead of the usual $1/\sqrt N$.)
\end{compactenum}

