  log_acf <- function (x) {
    x <- na.omit(x)
    x <- acf(x, lag.max = floor(length(x)/10), plot=FALSE)
    plot(ifelse(x$acf > 0, x$acf, NA), log="xy", xlab="lag", ylab="autocorrelation", type="l")
  }
  log_acf(x)
@ 
\end{center}

The main cause of this long memory seems to be, not herding, but the
slicing of large trades into smaller ones: indeed, the autocorrelation
of the volume signs is more persistent if you restrict the data to a
single broker and the long memory disappears if you consider different
brokers.

\begin{center}
<<SMLPLT3,results='hide',echo=FALSE>>=
  curve(1-x/3, xlim=c(0,1), ylim=c(.1,1.2), col="black", lwd=2, axes=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="") 
  box()
  curve(.8 - x/1.8, add=TRUE, col="red", lwd=2)
  curve(.5 - x/4 - 10*x^5, add=TRUE, col="blue", lwd=2)
  legend("topright", c("same broker", "everyone", "different brokers"),
         col=c("black", "red", "blue"), lwd=3, lty=1)
@ 
\end{center}

Long memory does not contradict market efficiency thanks to the following (equivalent) phenomena: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item the price impact is temporary and cancels the long memory;
\item there is liquidity imbalance.
\end{compactenum}

One can model the non-iid nature of the orders by assuming that: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item large, \emph{hidden orders} are split into smaller ones;
\item large orders follow a power law;
\item the market participants know when a large order starts but not
  when it stops -- because of the power law, the probability that the
  next order will be of the same sign increases with the size of the
  hidden trade);
\item the market is efficient.
\end{compactenum}

This suffices to show that 
$$ \textrm{impact} \sim \log N $$
where $N$ is the number of chunks (their size is fixed). This is
confirmed by the data -- on the other hand, the hypothesis that order
flows are anonymized (market participants do not know when a hidden
trade starts) do not fit the data.

The life of a hidden order looks like this (the model also predicts
clustered volatility; it can take more than four transactions for the
market participants to realize that the hidden trade has ended):

\begin{center}
<<SMLPLT4,results='hide',echo=FALSE>>=
  f <- function (x) {
    ifelse(x < 1, log(x),
    ifelse(x < 1.05, 10 * (x - 1),
    ifelse(x < 1.1, 10 * (1.1 - x),
    - .5 * (x - 1.1) + .1 * rnorm(length(x)))))
  }
  curve(f(x), xlim=c(.3, 1.7), xlab="", ylab="", axes=FALSE)
  box()
@ 
\end{center}

In a nutshell: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item power laws are everywhere
\item they can be explained by the slicing of large (hidden) orders
\item the market participants can recognize those hidden orders, but they do not immediately notice when they have stopped
\item the logarithm law, 
  $\textrm{impact} \sim \log N$
  suggests that the \emph{capacity} of your strategy could be higher than you think.
\end{compactenum}

The speaker likens the financial world to an \emph{ecosystem},
with \emph{competition} (if A grows bigger then B loses money; if B grows
bigger, then A loses money) and \emph{prey/predator} relations (if A grows
bigger, then B earns money; if B grow bigger, then A loses money) --
you can also imagine relations not encountered in wildlife
ecosystems.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{The key role of liquidity fluctuations in determining large price changes}\\
F. Lillo and J. D. Farmer\\
Fluctuation and noise letters (2005)
\end{book}

In a \emph{double continuous auction market}, such as the LSE (London
stock exchange) or the NYSE (New York Stock Exchange), impatient
traders send \emph{market orders}, to be executed immediately, at the
best possible price, while patient investors send \emph{limit orders}
or \emph{quotes}, to be executed at a given price, which are stored in
a (publically visible) queue, the \emph{limit order book}. (At the
begining and the end of the day, there is also an auction, where
everyone sends quotes for 10 minutes, and they are only matched at the
end. And this is only the \emph{on-book market} (SETS in London,
downstairs market in New York): there is an \emph{off-book market}
(SEAQ, upstars market), where trades are decided bilaterally, on the
phone, and only made public afterwards.)

Large price changes are determined by holes in the order book and can
be triggered by small volumes. Not only the first \emph{gap} (the
closest to the best price) matters, but also the others --
\emph{sparse} order books call for large price changes.

(The gap size or the sparsity of the book can be measured from the
tick data we already have.)

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{There's more to volatility than volume}\\
L. Guillemot et al. (2005)
\end{book}

Volatility clustering cannot be explained by a change-of-time either
using the volume clock or the number-of-transactions clock.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{An empirical behavioral model of price formation}\\
S. Mike and J. D. Farmer (2005)
\end{book}

An \emph{agent-based model} of price formation, comprised of
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item An order placement model, using a Student $T$ distribution with fewer than 2 degrees of freedom;
\item A cancellation model, taking order imbalance and the position
  wrt the best price
\end{compactenum}
can reproduce the observed distributions of returns and bid-ask
spreads.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
