A. Davies\\
Journal of fixed income (2007)
\end{book}

There is \emph{cointegration} (``integration'') between national bond
markets; the common trend presents structural changes.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Simple forecasts and paradigm shifts}\\
H. Hong et al.\\
Journal of finance (2007)
\end{book}

The following agent-base model: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item $A$ and $B$ are two independant AR processes (think of $A$ as value and $B$ as growth);
\item (Log-)dividend payments are given by a linear regression: 
  $D_{t+1} = A_t + B_t + \textrm{noise}$;
\item Investors choose between the two incorrect models
  $D_{t+1} = A_t + \textrm{noise}$
  and
  $D_{t+1} = B_t + \textrm{noise}$;
  they change their model when the difference in log-likelihood
  exceeds a certain threshold (this is the confidence level of the
  statistical (LR) test); that threshold varies from investor to
  investor;
\item The combined behaviour of those investors drives the prices
\end{compactenum}
explains the stylized facts:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item book-to-market effect;
\item volatility and skewness are stochastic.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\href{http://www.newscientist.com/channel/being-human/mg19426021.100-life-choices--can-science-help.html}{\emph{How to make better choices}}\\
K. Douglas and D. Jones\\
New Scientist 2602, May 2007, p.35--43
\end{book}

This article lists several \emph{behavioural biases} that could be
applied to bahavioural finance:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item loss aversion
\item information overload; to many choices (e.g., in a supermarket,
  when faced with two queues, you have a 50\% chance of chosing
  the fastest one; with 100 queues, this probability drops to 1\%)
\item emotions (anger or disgust are bad, depression is good)
\item confirmation bias
\item anchoring effect (\ie, bayesian shrinkage with a meaningless prior)
\item sunk cost
\item framing effect (the half full/empty glass)
\item social (or peer) pressure.
\end{compactenum}

See also: 
\emph{Intuition, its powers and perils} (D.G. Myers), 
\emph{The paradox of choice} (B. Schwartz),
\emph{Why choose this book?} (R. Montague),
\emph{Why smart people make big money mistakes and how to correct them} 
(G. Belsky and T. Gilovich). 

