\end{book}

The authors implemented the following strategy for the 
\emph{Penn--Lehman automated trading} (PLAT) competition 
(a competitor of the university of Michigan's 
\emph{trading agent competition} (TAC)), that simulates 
ISLAND, one of the majors ECNs:
\begin{itemize}[--]
\item Using Rmetrics, compute technical indicators over the past 90
  days (they are detailed in an appendix);
\item Apply \emph{boosting} on the trading rules (\emph{decision trees}) derived from those indicators;
\item Use a constantly rebalanced portfolio (50\% cash, 50\% long; 
  or 125\% cash, 25\% short); 
  peek into the \emph{limit order} book and add limit orders just
  beyond the best ones; 
  only use \emph{market orders} if this fails.
\end{itemize}

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Stupid data miner tricks:\\ overfitting the S\&P\,500}\\
D.J. Leinweber\\
Journal of investing (2007)
\end{book}

Data mining, \ie, the extraction of patterns from large data sets, can
go awry and become \emph{data snooping}. To prevent this, the authors
suggest to use:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item temporal and cross-sectional out-of-sample;
\item a measure of data snooping, such as \emph{White's reality check};
\item random data.
\end{compactenum}

This sounds outdated: no mention of the bootstrap as an alternative to
the in- and out-of-sample splitting; no mention of sensitivity
measures (influence function, etc.) to measure how dependant on data
variations the results are or robust methods to reduce this
dependance; no mention of precedures that can cope with an excess of
predictive variables (shrinkage, support vector machines (SVM), etc.).

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Value and growth, theory and practice}\\
J.S. Brush\\
Journal of portfolio management (spring 2007)
\end{book}

Value ($V$) and growth ($\Delta V$) are two (negatively correlated
but) different signals, measuring \emph{static} and \emph{dynamic}
value. Growth beats value in the short term, while value persists for
years. Thanks to the negative correlation, combining them lowers
volatility (plot various blends of value and growth in the
returns$\times$volatility plane).

The author uses the following value factors: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item dividend to price;
\item earnings to price;
\item cash flow to price;
\item expected earnings to price;
\item book value to price
\end{compactenum}
and the following growth factors:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item short-term change in earnings-to-price
\item long-term change in earnings-to-price
\item estimate revisions (number of upwards revisions, minus number of downwards ones, divided by the total number of revisions, time-weighted);
\item earnings surprises (reported earnings minus consensus predicted earnings, at the time of the last earnings report);
\item price momentum.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 
