\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{How to generate random matrices\\ from the classical compact groups}\\
F. Mezzadri\\
Notices of the AMS, May 2007
\end{book}

To get a random unitary matrix (since $U(N)$ is a compact group, we
use its Haar measure), one could think of using the
\emph{Gram--Schmidt orthonormalization} -- but it is numerically
unstable. Instead, one can use the \emph{QR decomposition}, accounting
for its non-unicity, as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Take a random, gaussian, complex matrix $Z$ (this probability space is called the \emph{Ginibre ensemble});
\item Compute the $QR$ decomposition: $Z = QR$;
\item Set $Q' = Q \cdot
  \textrm{diag}\left( 
    \dfrac{ r_{11} }{ \va{ r_{11} } },
    \dots,
    \dfrac{ r_{NN} }{ \va{ r_{NN} } }
  \right)$
\end{compactenum}

The article gives actual code (in Python, using \emph{scipy}) and
examples from applications of random matrix theory (RMT) in quantum
mechanics, approximating infinite-dimensional operators with large
matrices.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Enhanced index investing\\ based on goal programming}\\
L.-C. Wu et al\\
Journal of portfolio management (2007)
\end{book}

\emph{Goal programming} can be used to tackle dual-objective problems,
such as active management (\ie, follow the market and beat it, \ie,
have a low tracking error with the market and high expected returns):
just set a goal for each objective (say, 3\% tracking error and 7\%
returns) and minimize the sum of the distances to those goals.

The authors fail to see than the objectives should be expressed in the
same (monetary) unit, lest the user want to tweak the weight given to
each.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Robust portfolio optimization:\\ recent trends and future directions}\\
F.J. Fabozzi et al.\\
Journal of portfolio management (2007)
\end{book}

The article is an informal presentation of robust optimization -- or
an advert for the authors' forthcoming book.

Robust optimization provides more stable weights and a better use of
the turnover budget than classical mean-variance optimization.

Robust optimization is only equivalent to shrinkage towards the
minimum variance portfolio in some very special cases (no uncertainty
on the variance matrix, stock variance and estimation variance are
proportional, no constraints).

Robust portfolio optimization can also be used to find a portfolio
``statistically equivalent'' to the optimal one that minimizes
transaction costs.

Robust portfolio optimization is essentially a worst-case
optimization: \emph{zero net alpha adjustment} changes the shape of the
uncertainty set to reflect the fact that estimation errors are not
systematically in the wrong direction.

Robust \emph{multi-period} problems can surprisingly prove more
tractable than their non-robust analogues.

Robust optimization is not limited to portfolio construction, but also
has applications in optimal trading, optimal hedging, econometric
model estimation, model selection, etc.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Does size matter? Assets under management\\ a questionable question}\\
G.C. Allen\\
Journal of portfolio management (2007)
\end{book}

Funds with large AUM (assets under management) are less risky but have
lower returns; the effect is less marked with large capitalizations.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Optimal execution for portfolio transitions: minimizing oportunity cost}\\
M. Kritzman et al.\\
Journal or portfolio management (2007)
\end{book}

To find the best trade order for a \emph{portfolio transition} (\ie, a
drastic rebalance), look at the partial derivative of the tracking
error (TE) (the distance between the current and desired portfolios)
and choose trades that reduce the TE most. (Generally, people use any
order that preserves sector neutrality.) You can also add \emph{market
  impact}, proportional to
$$ \sqrt{ \dfrac{ \textrm{trade size} }{ \textrm{average daily volume} } } $$
to the TE.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Measuring portfolio performance\\ using a modified measure of risk}\\
C. Adcock\\
Journal of asset management (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{Rubinstein-Leland beta}, defined as
$$
\beta ^{\textrm{RL}} = \dfrac{
  \cov( r  , -(1+r_m)^{-b} )
}{
  \cov( r_m, -(1+r_m)^{-b} )
}
$$
(where $r$ are the portfolio ratio-returns, $r_m$ the market
ratio-returns, the investor's utility is
$U(x) \propto x^{1-b} / (1-b)$ and $b$ can be estimated as
$b = \tfrac12 + (E\ln(1+r_m) - \log(1+r_f))/\sigma_m^2$)
does not seem to add anything to the CAPM beta: this can be proven for
elliptical distributions, and empirically tested for asymetric ones.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
\emph{Can robust portfolio optimization help\\ to build better portfolios?}\\
B. Scherer\\
Journal of asset management (2007)
\end{book}

Robust optimization is no better than bayesian shrinkage -- it
actually is a shrinkage towards the mimimum variance portfolio, with a
difficult-to-interpret-and-predict coefficient.
Furthermore, it decouples model uncertainty and risk, while they are
inseparable.

(``Robust optimization'' is actually a misnomer: it should be called
``worst case optimization''.)

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
\emph{Attribution:\\ Modeling asset characteristics as portfolios}\\
R. Grinold\\
Journal of portfolio management (2006)
\end{book}

The author explains how to analyze a portfolio using only covariances
between portfolio returns.

Let $X$ be the random variable of stock returns, with $EX = \alpha$,
$\var X = V$; let $p$ be the portfolio weights and $P = p' X$ its
returns. The ideal portfolio has weights $q = \lambda ^{-1} V^{-1}
\alpha$ and returns $Q = q' X$, where $\lambda$ is the risk aversion.
One then has:
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_P &= \lambda \cov (P,Q) \\
  \textrm{IR}_P &= \dfrac{\alpha}{\sd P} = \lambda \sd(Q) \cor(P,Q) \\
  \textrm{TC}_P &= \dfrac{\textrm{IR}_P}{\textrm{IR}_Q} = \cor(P,Q) \\
  U_Q - U_P &= \dfrac\lambda2 \var(Q-P)
\end{align*}
(The transfer coefficient (TC) is a measure of implementation efficiency.)

By decomposing the portfolio weights into several explanatory
portfolios (it is up to you to define them),
$$ p = \sum _j \beta_j p_j + \epsilon $$
(in case of multicolinearity problems, you might want to use some
shrinkage; also not the absence of intercept: if some relevant factors
are missing, they will be spread out over all the terms instead of
being put in the residual portfolio), you can decompose the alpha (it
is linear in $p$) into contributions for each source.

For an ex post analysis, just replace the alphas by realized returns.

The article suggests a report as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Portfolio IR: $\lambda \sd Q \cor(P,Q)$
\item Portfolio risk: $\sd P$
\item Portfolio alpha: $\lambda \cov(P,Q)$
\item Portfolio TC: $\cor(P,Q)$
\item For each explanatory portfolio:
\begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
\item Exposure: $\psi_j = \beta_j \dfrac{ \sd P_j }{ \sd P }$
\item Risk: $\psi_j \cor(P_j, P)$?
\item TC: $\cor(P_j, Q)$
\item $\textrm{IR} \sd P \textrm{TC}_j$
\item Contribution: $\psi_j \cdot \textrm{IR} \cdot \sd P \cdot \textrm{TC}_j$
\end{compactenum}
\item Residual exposure: $\cor(P,E)$
\item Residual risk: $\cor(E,P)^2$?
\item TC$_{\textrm{residual}}$: $\cor(E,Q)$
\item Residual alpha: $\textrm{IR} \cdot \sd P \cdot \cor(P,E) \cdot \cor(E,Q)$
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio constraints and the fundamental law of active management}\\
  R. Clarke et al. (2002)
\end{book}

(Another article on the transfer coefficient.)

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Maximize the Sharpe ratio}\\
  Axioma Advisor Newsletter, March 2007
\end{book}

The problem 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Maximize }  & \dfrac{ w' \alpha}{ \sqrt{w' Q w} } \\
  \textrm{such that } & w' 1 = 1 \\
  & w \geq 1 \\
  & \forall i \quad l_i \leq w' s_i \leq u_i
\end{align*}
can be replaced by 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Minimize }  & w' Q w \\
  \textrm{such that } & w' \alpha = \bar \alpha \\
  & w \geq 1 \\
  & \forall i \quad \tau l_i \leq w' s_i \leq \tau u_i
\end{align*}
The unknowns are $w$ and $\tau$ and the desired weights are $w /
\tau$, but since you do not know $\bar \alpha$, you will have to
perform several iterations to fine-tune it.
This does not easily generalize to more complicated constraints or
penalties (transaction costs, etc.)

