\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.ssc.wisc.edu/~bhansen/notes/notes.htm}{\emph{Econometrics}}\\
  B. Hansen (2000--2007)
\end{book}

% Chapter 1: Introduction
There are several differences between econometrics and mainstream statistics: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The use of \emph{observational data} means that one cannot redo an expetiment after changing something: \emph{causality} cannot be inferred;
\item Econometricians make as few hypotheses as possible, in particular, they rarely assume that the distributions are gaussian (or even known) or that the variables of interest are independant;
\item They do not only want forecasts from their models, they also want inferences on the parameters of those models.
\end{compactenum}

% Chapter 2: Linear projection model
One can consider several \emph{regression models}:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item \emph{Linear regression}:
  \begin{align*}
    & y = x' \beta + e \\
    & E[e|x] = 0
  \end{align*}
\item \emph{Homoskedastic linear regression}:
  \begin{align*}
    & y = x' \beta + e \\
    & E[e|x] = 0 \\
    & E[e^2|x] = \sigma^2
  \end{align*}
\item \emph{Linear projection} (we assume that the formula for the coefficient of the regression is valid at the population level and we try to see what we can say from a sample):
  \begin{align*}
    & y = x' \beta + e \\
    & E[e|x] = 0 \\
    & \beta = E[xx']^{-1} E[xy]
  \end{align*}
\item Homoskedastic linear projection;
\item Gaussian linear regression.
\end{compactenum}

% Chapter 3: Least Squares
The following estimators coincide: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Take the formula for the linear projection model and replace expectations by means (this is called a \emph{moment});
\item Minimize 
  $\n{ y - x' \beta } ^ 2$: this is the \emph{ordinary least squares} (OLS or LS) estimator;
\item Assume that the predictors $x$ and the noise $e$ are independant, that the noise is iid gaussianm and maximize the likelihood: this is the \emph{maximum likelihood estimator} (MLE);
\item Assume that $(x,y)$ only take a finite number of values and compute the corresponding (multinomial) MLE;
\item Maximize the $R^2$ (not mentionned in this book).
\end{compactenum}

In the linear projection model, the LS estimator is the \emph{best
linear unbiased estimator} (BLUE) and \emph{semi-parametric efficient}
(\ie, it has the smallest mean square error (MSE) among the feasible
estimators; a \emph{feasible} estimator is an estimator that does not
use parameters we do not know, e.g., it cannot use $\sigma$).

The regression can be done in two steps: 
$$ 
  \textrm{res}( y \sim x_1 + x_2 ) =
  \textrm{res}( 
    \textrm{res}( y \sim x_2 )
    \sim 
    \textrm{res}( x_1 \sim x_2 )
  ).
$$

In case of \emph{multicolinearity}, \ie, redundant predictive
variables, it will be difficult to disentangle the effects of the
predictors.

To measure the influence of isolated observations, you can compute
their \emph{leverage}, the \emph{leave-out-one} (LOO) $\hat \beta$ and
residuals.

The quality of a regression model can be measured by estimators of 
$\rho^2 = 1 - \sigma^2 / \sigma_y^2$: 
\begin{align*}
R^2 &= 1 - \dfrac{ \hat \sigma ^2 }{ \hat \sigma _y ^2 } \\
R^2_{\textrm{adj}} &= 1 - \dfrac{ s ^2 }{ \hat s _y ^2 }
\end{align*}
where 
\begin{align*}
\hat \sigma^2 &= \tfrac1n \sum \hat e_i^2 \\
s^2 &= \tfrac1{n-k} \sum \hat e_i^2 \\
\hat \sigma_y^2 & = \tfrac1n \sum (y_i - \bar y)^2 \\
\hat s_y^2 &= \tfrac1{n-1} \sum (y_i - \bar y)^2
\end{align*}

% Chapter 4: Inference
The least squares estimator is \emph{consistent}: it converges, in probability, to
the true value of the parameter. More precisely, 
$ \sqrt n ( \hat \beta - \beta ) \longrightarrow^d N(0,V) $
where 
\begin{align*}
  Q &= E[x x'] \\
  \Omega &= E[ (x_i e_i) (x_i e_i)' ] = E[ x_i x_i' e_i^2 ] \\
  V &= Q^{-1} \Omega Q^{-1}.
\end{align*}
For the homoskedastic projection model, the variance is 
$V^0 = Q^{-1} \sigma^2$.

Since the true value of $V$ is not knowable, we estimate it: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Traditional: $\hat V^0 = \hat Q ^{-1} s^2 $;
\item Heteroskedasticity-robust covariance matrix:
  $$ \hat V = \hat Q^{-1} \hat \Omega \hat Q^{-1}; $$
\item MacKinnon--White jackknife estimator:
  $$ \hat V ^ * = (n-1) \sum 
  ( \hat \beta_{(-i)} - \bar \beta )
  ( \hat \beta_{(-i)} - \bar \beta )' ;$$
\item Andrew's cross-validation estimator.
\end{compactenum}

The \emph{$t$-statistic} 
$$ t_n(\theta) = \dfrac{ \hat \theta - \theta }{ s(\hat\theta) }, $$
with $\theta = h(\beta)$ some function of the parameters,
\begin{align*}
s(\hat\theta) &= n^{-1/2} \sqrt{ \hat H'_\beta \hat V \hat H_\beta } \\
H_\beta &= \dfrac{ \partial h }{ \partial \beta }(\beta),
\end{align*}
converges in distribution to $N(0,1)$ 
(it is asymptotically \emph{pivotal}, \ie, the limit distribution no
longer depends on the parameters) and can be used for (asymptotic tests) or 
(asymptotic) confidence intervals.

The \emph{Wald test} is a higher-dimensional analogue: 
$$ 
W_n = n 
(h(\beta) - \theta_0) 
( \hat H _\beta ' \hat V \hat H _\beta ) ^ {-1}
(h(\beta) - \theta_0)';
$$
it converges in distribution to 
$\chi^2_q$ where 
$q = \textrm{rank}\, H_\beta$.

The \emph{$F$-test} is a special test of the Wald test where one
checks if one or several of the parameters is zero.

With \ gaussian linear model, one knows the distribution of the
$T$-statistic and can replace the Wald test by a more precise
\emph{likelihood ratio} (LR) test -- the former is a first-order
approximation of the latter.

The $T$- and Wald tests do not work well with non-linear hypotheses --
in that case (if you cannot transform the hypothesis into a linear
one), or if the sample is small prefer Monte Carlo simulations,

% Chapter 5: Additional topics

OLS is efficient in the projection model (and in the gaussian one, of
course), but not in the linear regression model. However, the
\emph{generalized least squares} (GLS) estimator is efficient: 
\begin{align*}
  \tilde \beta &= (X' D^{-1} X)^{-1} (X' D^{-1} y) \\
  D &= \textrm{diag}(\sigma_1^2,\dots,\sigma_n^2) \\
 \sigma_i^2 &= \sigma^2(x_i) = E[e_i^2 | x_i ].
\end{align*}
If you want a feasible estimator, replace $\sigma_i^2$ by some
estimator $\hat \sigma_i^2$, e.g., from a \emph{skedastic regression}
$\sigma_i^2 = \alpha' z_i$.

GLS is less robust than OLS: only use it if you really, really need it.

Heteroskedasticity can be tested with a Wald test on the skedastic
regression; the White and Breusch--Pagan tests actually test for more:
second-order independance (the $z_i$ include the $x_i$, their squares
and cross-products).

I have not read section 5.3 on forecast intervals. 

\emph{Non-linear least squares} estimate a (prescribed) non-linear
expression for the conditional mean, still minimizing the sum of
squared errors. Non-linear models need not be \emph{identifiable}:
e.g., if $y = \beta x ^\gamma$ and $\beta=0$, then $\gamma$ is not
identified.

\emph{Least absolute deviations} (LAD) minimizes the sum of the absolute
values of the errors:
$$ \theta_0 = \mathop{\textrm{argmin}}_\theta E \va{y - \theta} ;$$
equivalently: 
$$P(y \leq \theta_0) = P(y \geq \theta_0) = \tfrac12$$
or
$$E [ \textrm{sgn}(y-\theta_0) ]= 0.$$

LAD works better when there are many observations bear the median,
\ie, when $f(0)$ is large. This is the case with many skewed
distributions (there are more observations around the median than
around the mean) and fat-tailed distributions (they are ``pointy'').

LAD is a special case of \emph{quantile regression}, usually
implemented as a linear problem.

To test for non-linearity, just add non-linear functions of the
regressors and perform a Wald test: the \emph{RESET test} uses $\hat y
^2, \hat y ^3, \dots, \hat y ^m$ and is particularly powerful at
detecting \emph{single-index models}:
$y = G(x'\beta) + e$. 

Omission of a relevant variable leads to the \emph{omitted
variable bias}.
When the error is conditionally heteroskedastic, 
$E[e_i^2 | x_i] = \sigma^2$,
the inclusion of irrelevant correlated variables reduces efficiency;
without homoskedasticity, there is no clear rule.

Statistical tests do not help for \emph{model selection} (they are not
symetric); AIC (an estimator of the Kullback--Leibler distance)
selection is inconsistent (it overfits): prefer the BIC.

% Chapter 6: The bootstrap
A statistic can be written as $T_n(F)$ where $F$ is the (population) distribution function of the data and $n$ the number of observations. To estimate the cumulative distribution function (cdf) 
$ G_n(u,F) = P(T_n \geq u \,|\, F ) $, the \emph{non-parametric bootstrap} makes two approximations: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Use a consistent estimator of the cdf $F$, such as the \emph{empirical distribution function} $F_n$;
\item Use a Monte Carlo simulation to estimate $G_n^*(u) = G_n(u,F_n)$ -- in other words, we sample \emph{with replacement} (to simulate the multinomial distribution $F_n$) from the sample.
\end{compactenum}

The \emph{bias-corrected} bootstrap estimator is 
$\hat \theta - ( \bar{\hat{\theta}}^* - \hat\theta) = 2 \hat\theta - \bar{\hat{\theta}}^*$
where 
$\hat\theta$ is the value of the estimator $T$ on the sample,
$\hat\theta^*$ is the value of $T$ on a bootstrap sample, 
$\bar{\hat{\theta}}^*$ is the average value value of $T$ on the bootstrap samples, 
$( \bar{\hat{\theta}}^* - \hat\theta)$ is an estimator of the bias.

Variance estimators are not very useful for the bootstrap: prefer
\emph{confidence intervals}:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The oft-used \emph{Efron percentile interval}, 
$[ \hat\theta - q(\alpha/2), \hat\theta - q(1-\alpha/2) ]$,
where $q$ are the quantiles of $\hat\theta^* - \hat\theta$,
is biased (unless the distribution of symetric) and should be avoided;
\item The \emph{percentile interval}, 
$[ \hat\theta - q(1-\alpha/2), \hat\theta - q(\alpha/2) ],$
is preferable;
\item The \emph{percentile-t interval} uses the quantiles of 
$$ \dfrac{
  \hat\theta^* - \hat\theta
}{
  s(\hat\theta^*)
};
$$
\item The \emph{symetric percentile-t interval} uses the quantiles of 
$$ \dfrac{
  \va{ \hat\theta^* - \hat\theta }
}{
  s(\hat\theta^*)
};
$$
\end{compactenum}

The convergence of bootstrap estimates is $O(n^{-1/2})$, but the
$n^{-1/2}$ term is even and the $n^{-1}$ term odd: this can lead to
cancellations in certain situations -- notably, 
the percentile-$t$ interval is in $O(n^{-1})$ and the
the symetric percentile-$t$ interval is in $O(n^{-3/2})$.

To bootstrap a regression $y = x'\beta + e$, sample the noises $e_i^*$
from the residuals $\hat e_i$ and resample the predictors (or leave
the predictors as-is) -- but this assumes that predictors $x$ and
noise $e$ are independant.

The \emph{wild bootstrap} uses a 2-point distribution for each $e_i^*$
so that the first three (conditional) moments of $e_i^*$ coincide with
those of $\hat e_i$:
$$ 
P \left[
  e_i^* = \dfrac{1\pm\sqrt5}{2\sqrt5}
\right] =
\dfrac{1\mp\sqrt5}{2\sqrt5}.
$$

% Chapter 8

\emph{Empirical likelihood} (EL) maximizes the likelihood of the sample,
seen as a multinomial model,
$ \sum \log p_i $,
subject to $ \sum p_i = 1 $ and all the \emph{estimating equations} you have.
For instance: 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Maximize } & \sum \log p_i \\
  \textrm{such that } & \sum p_i = 1 \\
  & y - x' \beta = 0
\end{align*}
The actual optimization requires \emph{Lagrange multipliers} and is
not straightforward. The EL estimator is asymptotically efficient and
can be used to perform likelihood ratio (LR) tests.

% Rest of the book
The rest of the book is less clear and/or more superficial.

% Chapter 7
The presentation of the \emph{generalized method of moments} (GMM)
(B. Hansen invented it in the 1980s) is by far the least readable chapter.

% Chapter 9
An \emph{endogeneous} model is a model $y = z' \beta + e$ with $E(ze)
\neq 0$. For instance:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item You want to estimate $\beta$ in $y=x'\beta + e$ but you only
  observe the predictor $x$ with noise: $z = x + u$ (with OLS, the
  forecasts of $y$ will be fine, but the estimation of $\beta$ will be
  biased);
\item There is a \emph{feedback} in the system you are modeling: 
\begin{align*}
  q & = \beta_1 p + e_1 \\
  p & = \beta_2 q + e_2;
\end{align*}
  naive projection then suffers for the \emph{simultaneous equations bias}:
  $\beta_1^* = (\beta_1 - \beta_2) / 2$
\end{compactenum}

One solution to the endogeneity problem is to add variables for which
$E(xe)=0$ -- these are called \emph{instrument variables} (IV). The
estimation can be done by GMM or EL.

The presentation of the \emph{IV estimator} and the \emph{indirect
  least squares} (ILS) is confusing.

In case of homoskedasticity,
$E(e_i^2|x_i) = \sigma^2$, 
one can use the \emph{two-stage least squares} (2SLS) estimator:
regress $z$ on $x$, then $y$ on $\hat z$.
The 2SLS bias increases with the number of IV and decreases with sample size.

% Chapter 10
% Chapter 11
The time series chapters are too superficial but cover AR, unit roots,
cointegration and Granger causality.

% Chapter 12

\emph{Limited dependant variables} (LDV), \ie, binary (logit,
probit; which can be interpreted as a \emph{latent variable model}),
multinomial, integral (Poisson regression), censored
are mentionned, but only estimated with parametric maximum
likelihood, even though semi-parametric methods are superior.

The \emph{Tobit model} is the censored regression 
\begin{align*}
  y_i &= (y_i^* > 0) \,?\, y_i^* : 0 \\
  y_i^* &= x'_i \beta + e_i \\
  e_i & \sim N(0,\sigma^2)
\end{align*}

The \emph{sample selection} problem is similar: 
\begin{align*}
  y_i &= x'_i \beta + e_i \\
  T &= \textbf{1}(z_i' \gamma + f_i)
\end{align*}
and $y$ is only observed when $T=1$. 
Since $E[e\,|\,T=1] = \rho \lambda(z'\gamma)$, where 
$\rho = \cov(e,f)$,
$\lambda = E[ \epsilon \,|\, \epsilon > -x ] = \phi(x) / \Phi(x)$,
$\epsilon \sim N(0,1)$
($\lambda$ is called the \emph{Mills ratio}),
it is natural to estimate $\beta$ and $\rho$ using 
\begin{align*}
  & y = x' \beta + \rho \lambda(z' \gamma) + u \\
  & E[ u \,|\, T = 1 ] = 0
\end{align*}
where $\gamma$ is estimate with a probit model -- this 2-step
procedure is the \emph{Heckit estimator} and it has a few robustness
problems.

% Chapter 13
Only two \emph{panel} data models are presented: 
\begin{align*}
  y & \sim ( 1 \,|\, \textrm{subject} ) + x \\
  y & \sim ( 1 \,|\, \textrm{subject} ) + \textrm{lag}(y) + x
\end{align*}

% Chapter 14
The quality of a \emph{kernel density estimation} can be measured by the
bias, the variance, their sum (asymptotic MSE or AMSE) (but all this
depends on the point) or the integral of that sum (integrated AMSE or AMISE)
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Bias}(x) &= \tfrac12 f''(x) h^2 \sigma_K^2 \\
  \var \hat f(x) &\approx  \dfrac{ f(x) R(K) }{ nh } \\
  \textrm{AMSE}(x) &= \textrm{Bias}(x) + \var \hat f(x) \\
  \textrm{AMISE}(x) &= \int\textrm{AMSE}(x)\,dx
\end{align*}
where $K$ is the kernel, $R(K) = \int K^2$ its \emph{roughness}
and $h$ the bandwidth. This can be used to choose the bandwidth.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Bootstrap methods and permutation tests}\\
  T. Hersterberg et al.
\end{book}

This is part of the S-Plus documentation but remains software
agnostic. It gives some good advice (use bootstrap confidence
intervals corrected for bias and skewness; permutation tests are not
valid if, under the null hypothesis, the two samples do not have the
same spread and shape), but fails to give any technical detail (you
will not know how \emph{BCa} and \emph{tilting} confidence intervals
are computed.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.cs.arizona.edu/people/rts/tdbbook.pdf}{\emph{Developing time-oriented\\ database applications in SQL}}\\
  R.T. Snodgras\\
  Morgan Kaufman (2000)
\end{book}

This is an SQL cookbook for temporal problems.  Its examples are
complicated, anti-pedagogic (lots of numeric magic numbers, numeric
codes, abbreviations and cryptic acronyms as column names, only chunks
of the database at a time so as to prevent a global view of the data,
examples intentionally ``quite difficult to decipher'', etc.) and
dusty (some references to Cobol).

There are three temporal types (instants, intervals and periods
(anchored intervals)); three types of times (user-defined (a normal
piece of data), valid time (time in the real world), transaction time
(time in the database)); three types of statements (current,
sequenced, non-sequenced). Time in SQL is \emph{granular} (\ie,
discrete, e.g., with a 1-day or 1-minute precision).

\texttt{NOW} is an unsolved problem in SQL: you can either use
\texttt{NULL}, but this has side effects for comparisons and clashes
with other uses of \texttt{NULL} or use a magic value far in the
future, which, besides its arbitrariness, leads to a confusion between ``until further notice'' 
and ``forever''. 

A temporal table is a table with \texttt{start} and \texttt{end}
columns. A \emph{temporal partition} splits this table into current
(the \texttt{end} column contains \texttt{NOW}) and past tables (this
is actually a solution to the \texttt{NOW} problem).  A \emph{snapshot
table} is a slice of a temporal table, at a given date. A
\emph{transaction table} contains a log of the changes to a table
(similar to a set of patches in a version control system).  You might
want to vacuum transaction tables or even temporal tables, if old data
becomes irrelevant.

A \emph{bitemporal table} contains both valid time and transaction
time: there are two \texttt{start} and two \texttt{end} columns. A
bitemporal table can be partitionned along valid time, transaction
time or both.

Most of the book is devoted to the handling, in SQL, of temporal or
bitemporal tables: sequenced \texttt{JOIN},
\begin{verbatim}
  SELECT A.x, B.y, ...
         last(A.start, B.start) AS start,
         first(A.end, B.end) AS end
  FROM A, B
  WHERE ...
  AND last(...) < first(...);
\end{verbatim}
sequenced \texttt{EXCEPT},
sequenced \texttt{DISTINCT} (trickier: procedural),
sequenced \texttt{PRIMARY KEY},
sequenced \texttt{UPDATE},
etc. -- if in doubt, draw all the possible relative positions of the
intervals and write a huge query full of \texttt{UNION} or
\texttt{CASE}.

Bitemporal tables complicate things, but not as much as one would
fear. Bitemporal diagrams are bidimensional.

The recipe to build a temporal database goes as follows: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Build a non-temporal ER (entity-relation) model;
\item Add time;
\item Convert it into a relational model (no more entities, only relations);
\item Convert it to a physical model (with tables, taking into account efficiency problems, e.g., temporal partitioning).
\end{compactenum}

To deal with time in an application using the database, choose among:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Avoid time;
\item Write unreadable SQL code;
\item Use a middleware to convert SQL/Temporal (a rarely implemented part of the SQL standard) to SQL.
\end{compactenum}

The book ends with a presentation of SQL/Temporal: it adds a
\texttt{PERIOD} data type, 
\begin{verbatim}
  PERIOD '[2001-01-01 2002-01-01)';
\end{verbatim}
with a few operators 
(\texttt{OVERLAPS},
\texttt{PRECEDES},
\texttt{SUCCEEDS},
\texttt{MEETS},
\texttt{BEGIN},
\texttt{END},
\texttt{LAST},
\texttt{PRIOR},
\texttt{NEXT},
\texttt{P\_UNION},
\texttt{P\_INTERSECT},
\texttt{P\_EXCEPT},
\texttt{CAST} (to change the granularity)), 
a \texttt{VALIDTIME} keyword to handle temporal tables
\begin{verbatim}
  ALTER TABLE T1 ADD VALIDTIME(DATE);
  ALTER TABLE T1 ADD 
    VALIDTIME PRIMARY KEY (x,y);
  ALTER TABLE T1 ADD 
    VALIDTIME UNIQUE (x,y);
  ALTER TABLE T1 ADD 
    VALIDTIME FOREIGN KEY (f) REFERENCES T2;
  VALIDTIME SELECT ... FROM ...;
  NONSEQUENCED VALIDTIME SELECT ... 
    WHERE VALIDTIME(T1) 
    OVERLAPS DATE '2001-01-01';
\end{verbatim}
a \texttt{TRANSACTIONTIME} keyword
\begin{verbatim}
  VALIDTIME AND NONSEQUENCED TRANSACTIONTIME 
  SELECT ...
\end{verbatim}
\texttt{EXPAND} and \texttt{NORMALIZE} (for sets of potentially
overlapping or abutting periods) operators and a \texttt{NORMALIZE ON}
construct (similar to \texttt{GROUP BY}).

You may note that SQL/Temporal fails to address the \texttt{NOW}
problem.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.burns-stat.com/pages/Tutor/bootstrap_resampling.html}{\emph{The statistical bootstrap\\ and other resampling methods}}\\
  P. Burns (2007)
\end{book}

A short introduction to the bootstrap.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.burns-stat.com/pages/Working/dart_to_heart.pdf}{\emph{Dart to the heart}}\\
  P. Burns (2007)
\end{book}

A advert for random portfolios, comparing them to benchmarks and peer groups.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Total return strategies\\ for multi-asset portfolios:\\ dynamically managing portfolio risk}\\
  U. Herold et al.\\
  Journal of portfolio management (2007)
\end{book}

This article review various dynamic asset allocation (DAA) strategies:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Portfolio insurance (stop loss, synthetic put, CPPI);
\item Rainbow options (best-of-two, best-of-$n$ plus floor);
\item Optimization with a constraint on the value at risk (VaR) or the expected shortfall (ES, CVaR), e.g., require that the probability of going below a predetermined floor be below 1\%.
\end{compactenum}

The VaR-based strategies seem preferable, provided one controls the turnover.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{A Cardan's discriminant approach\\ to predicting currency crashes}\\
  S.K. Koh, W.M. Fong and F. Chan\\
  Journal of international money and finance (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{generalized normal} (GEN) distribution,
$$ f(u) \propto \exp \left( \theta_1 u + \theta_2 \dfrac{u^2}2 - \dfrac{u^4}4 \right), $$
allows for bimodality and can be used to model large jumps.
Bimodality can be measured with \emph{Cardan's determinant}
$$ \textrm{CD} = \dfrac{\theta_1^2}4 - \dfrac{\theta_2^3}{27} $$
which can help predict currency crises -- both type~I and type~II error rates are low.

The same idea could be generalized to any other jump prediction situation.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards reliable efficient frontiers}\\
  K. Sch\"ottle and R. Werner\\
  Journal of asset management (2006)
\end{book}

One sould use \emph{robust optimization}:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item For the portfolio to change slowly with time, when the inputs are changed;
\item For the portfolio to change slowly when changing the risk target;
\item To account for parameter estimation error.
\end{compactenum}

Robust optimization outperforms \emph{Michaud's resampled portfolio}, which outperforms classical mean-variance optimization.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Incorporating estimation errors into portfolio selection: robust portfolio construction}\\
  S. Ceria and R.A. Stubbs\\
  Journal of asset management (2006)
\end{book}

The list of alternatives to mean-variance portfolio optimization:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item \emph{James--Stein estimators} shrink the expected returns of
  each asset to the average expected returns, depending on the
  volatility of the asset;
\item \emph{Jorion estimators} shrink the expected returns estimate
  towards the minimum variance portfolio;
\item The \emph{Black--Litterman} model blends views on some
  portfolios to implied market returns;
\item \emph{Michaud's resampled portfolios} are computed as follows:
  on bootstrap samples, estimate average returns and variance matrix,
  compute the optimal portfolio, average those portfolios;
\item Adding constraints might improve (or worsen) the sensitivity of
  the portfolio to parameter changes;
\item Robust optimization, where we only give the optimizer intervals
  containing the alpha;
\end{compactenum}
the authors add a new one.

One can estimate the error on the expected returns of the portfolio and maximize this return with a penalty for the estimation error:
\begin{align*}
  & \textrm{Maximize} && \textbf{w}' \boldsymbol{\alpha} - \lambda \n{ \Sigma^{1/2} \textbf{w} } \\
  & \textrm{st}       && \textbf{w}' V \textbf{w} \leq v \\
  &                   && \textbf{w}' \textbf{1} = 1 \\
  &                   && \textbf{w} \geq 0
\end{align*}
where $\lambda$ is the penalty for the estimatio error, $\Sigma$ is the variance matrix of the estimation errors, $V$ is the variance matrix of the returns, $\boldsymbol{\alpha}$ are the forward returns, $v$ is the risk target and $\textbf{w}$ are the portfolio weights the optimizer should find.

This is not a quadratic problem, but a \emph{second-order cone program}.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Improving investment performance\\ for pension plans}\\
  J.M. Mulvey et al.\\
  Journal of asset management (2006)
\end{book}

We do not live in a one-period world;
traditional risk-reward mesures such as the Sharpe ratio are not directly applicable to a multi-period setup;
one should consider multi-period strategies, stochastic programming, etc.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Investing in hedge funds: adding value through active style allocation decisions}\\
  L. Martellini et al.
  Edhec, SGAM (2005)
\end{book}

The Black--Litterman framework can be generalized to allow views on higher moments;
the optimization can then use a fourth-order approximation of the utility function and the (Cornish-Fisher) VaR as a risk measure.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{An analysis of performance measures\\using copulae}\\
  S. Hwang and M. Salmon (2001)
\end{book}

The following performance measures
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item \emph{Jensen's alpha}, \ie, the intercept in the regression of the returns (we always consider the returns minus the risk-free rate) against the market returns (this is the alpha in the CAPM);
\item The \emph{Treynor and Mazuy measure}, the intercept and the quadratic term in a quadratic CAPM,
\begin{align*}
  r_t &= \beta_1 r^{\textrm{market}}_t + \beta_2 (r^{\textrm{market}}_t) ^2 + \epsilon_t \\
 \textrm{TM} &= \alpha + \beta_2 E \left[ (r^{\textrm{market}}_t) ^2 \right]
\end{align*}
\item The higher moment performance measure (?)
\begin{align*}
\textrm{HM} &= \mu + \lambda_1 \mu_{\textrm{M}} +
               \lambda_2 ( \beta_{\textrm{P},\textrm{M}} - \gamma_{\textrm{P},\textrm{M}} ) \\
\mu &= \textrm{E} [r_t] \\
\mu_{\textrm{M}} &= \textrm{E} [r^{\textrm{Market}}_t] \\
\sigma_{\textrm{M}} ^2 &= \textrm{Var}(r^{\textrm{Market}}_t) \\
\gamma_{\textrm{M}} &= \dfrac{
  \textrm{Cov} (r^{\textrm{Market}}_t, r^{\textrm{Market}}_t, r^{\textrm{Market}}_t)
}{ \sigma_{\textrm{M}}^4 } \\
\theta_{\textrm{M}} &= \dfrac{
  \textrm{Cov} (r^{\textrm{Market}}_t, r^{\textrm{Market}}_t,
                r^{\textrm{Market}}_t, r^{\textrm{Market}}_t)
}{ \sigma_{\textrm{M}}^4 } \\
\beta_{\textrm{P},\textrm{M}} &= \dfrac{
  \textrm{Cov}(r_t, r^{\textrm{Market}}_t)
}{ \sigma_{\textrm{M}}^2 } \\
\gamma_{\textrm{P},\textrm{M}} &= \dfrac{
  \textrm{Cov}(r_t, r^{\textrm{Market}}_t, r^{\textrm{Market}}_t)
}{ \sigma_{\textrm{M}}^3 } \\
\lambda_1 &= \dfrac{
  \gamma_{\textrm{M}} ^2 \gamma_{\textrm{P,M}} - \theta_{\textrm{M}}
}{
  \gamma_{\textrm{M}} ^2 - (\theta_{\textrm{M}} - 1)
} \\
\lambda_2 &= \dfrac{
  \gamma_{\textrm{M}} \sigma_{\textrm{M}}
}{
  \gamma_{\textrm{M}} ^2 - (\theta_{\textrm{M}} - 1)
}
\end{align*}
\item The positive weighted weighting (PPW) measure (?)
\end{compactenum}

The Sharpe ratio is very different from the others and, worryingly,
for extreme positive values, those performance measures are
\emph{asymptotically independent}.

To this end, the article reviews various measures of tail or
asymptotic dependence, around
$$
\lambda = \lim_{u \to 1}
P \left[ Y > F_Y^{-1}(u) \,|\, X > F_X^{-1}(u) \right].
$$

To obtain a more reliable measure of performance, one can use a
\emph{copula}, describing the relation between those measures, to
combine them, yielding the following distribution (the choice of the
copula is left to the reader, e.g., a gaussian copula to simplify the
computations):
$$ f(x | f_1, f_2) = f_1(x) f_2(x) c( 1 - F_1(x), 1 - F_2(x) ). $$

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock returns and volatility:\\ pricing the short-run and long-run\\ components of market risk}\\
  T. Adrian and J. Rosenberg (2006)
\end{book}

Volatility can be modeled by a GARCH-like model where
$\log v_t$
is the sum of two AR(1) processes.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Market reactions to tangible\\ and intangible information}\\
  K. Daniel and S. Titman\\
  Journal of finance (2006)
\end{book}

The \emph{intangible return}, \ie, the component of the past returns unexplained by the firm's past performance,
defined as the residuals of the regression (over 5 years; the book-value growth is actually corrected for dividend payments)
$$
  \textrm{forward returns} \sim \log(B/P) + \textrm{growth}(B)
$$
is negatively related to future returns.

The article also explains why the B/P helps predict future returns: it can be written as
$$
  \log(P/B)_{\textrm{now}} = \log(B/P)_{\textrm{before}}
  + \textrm{growth}(B)
  - \textrm{growth}(P)
$$
(where $\textrm{growth}(x) = \log ( x_{\textrm{now}} / x_{\textrm{before}} )$)
and only
$ \textrm{growth}(B) - \textrm{growth}(P) $
has a predictive power.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Predicting the time-varying covariance matrix of electricity forwards}\\
  K. Aas and K.F. K\aa resen (2003)
\end{book}

The ARCH model can be generalized to describe the covariance between similar, short-lived, overlapping time series (forwards).

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Trimability and fast optimization\\ of long-short portfolios}\\
  B.I. Jacobs et al.\\
  Financial analysts journal (2006)
\end{book}

The authors explain how to transform most long-short optimization problems into a long-only problem, suitable for long-only optimizers.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Feedback and the success\\ of irrational investors}\\
  D. Hirshleifer et al.\\
  Journal of financial economics (2006)
\end{book}

It is good to be an early irrational investor and bad to be a late one -- provided many share your irrationality.
Early irrational investors have private information about the future order flow of investors with similar biases.
This effect causes prices to follow a random walk...

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{A comparison of financial duration models\\ via density forecasts}\\
  L. Bauwens et al. (2000)
\end{book}

\emph{Duration} data are irregular time series represented as bidimensional time series,
$(x_n, t_n - t_{n-1})$, where $x_n$ is the value measured at time $t_n$. Examples include price or volume in high-frequency financial data. They exhibit properties similar to those of GARCH data, such as \emph{duration clustering}: hence, one can model them along the same lines by replacing the (unobserved) volatility by the (observed) duration -- GARCH becomes ACD (autoregressive conditional duration), T-FARCH becomes TACD (threshold ACD), log-GARCH becomes log-ACD and stochastic volatility models become SVD (stochastic volatility duration).

The authors compare those methods using \emph{density forecasts}: prediction methods do not simply provide a forecast value $\hat x_i$, but a whole forecast distribution $\mathscr{F}_i$: for each observation $i$, you can compute
$p _{\mathscr{F}_i} (x_i) $, where $x_i$ is the actual observation and
$p _{\mathscr{F}_i} $ is the $p$-function (cumulative distribution function) of the predictive distribution.
If the forecasts are correct, the
$p _{\mathscr{F}_i} (x_i) $
should be uniformly distributed -- this can be tested.
This method accounts for models that change over time.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Equity style timing using\\ support vector regressions}\\
  G. Nalbantov et al.
\end{book}

Support vector machines (SVM) can be used to \emph{time} growth vs value and large vs small strategies.

The variables used are:
value-growth spread,
small-large spread,
S\&P\,500 volatility,
12-month forward S\&P\,500 P/E,
6-month S\&P\,500 momentum,
EPS change over the past year in the S\&P\,500,
P/E differences between value and growth,
P/E differences between large and small caps,
dividend yield difference between value and growth,
dividend yield difference between large and small caps,
yield spread Baa vs Aaa,
12-month change in the US CPI (consumer price index),
difference between the forward E/P of the S\&P\,500 and the 10-year T-bond yield,
yield spread between 10-year T-bonds and 3-month T-bills,
10-year T-bond yield adjusted for the inflation rate,
seasonally-adjusted US industrial production,
and a couple more.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.fbe.hku.hk/doc/seminars/multimedia/siyi%20road%20021603.pdf}{\emph{Applying financial statement analysis\\ to forecast earnings growth\\ and evaluate P/E ratios}}\\
  S. Li (2003)
\end{book}

If investors buy future earnings, the P/E should be explained by the earnings (or operating income) growth, which can be obtained by a big cross-sectional regression with the variables from the financial statement. The author suggests to combine this estimate with that from the analysts.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Essential portfolio theory}\\
  J. Mulvey\\
  Rydex investments (2005)
\end{book}

Empty article: it just reminds us that we do not live in a long-only, equity-and-bonds-only, 1-period, static, unleveraged world.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{You want to diversify risk?\\ Consider economic derivatives}\\
  R. Dubil\\
  Journal of wealth management (2007)
\end{book}

Individual investors should use \emph{economic derivatives} (consumer inflation, currencies, real estate prices, gas-at-the-pump, etc.) to hedge their non-stock assets (house, purchasing power, etc.).

Contrary to devivatives with a tradable underlying, economic derivatives are valued with \emph{probabilistic insurance pricing}.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www2.warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/wbs/research/wfri/wpaperseries/wp99-16.pdf}{\emph{An introduction to\\ differential geometry in econometrics}}\\
  P. Marriott and M. Salmon\\
  in \emph{Applications of differential geometry\\ to econometrics}, Chapter 1 (CUP, 2000)
\end{book}

A parametric (exponential) family of probability distributions can be seen
as a manifold (generalizations to the manifold of ``all''
distributions are possible, but infinite-dimensional manifolds
pose further theoretical problems)
and statistical properties of distributions, tests or estimators
can be translated into geometric properties, for instance:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The \emph{score} statistic is the length of a certain cotangent vector measured with respect to the metric defined by the expected Fisher information;
\item The Kullback--Leibler divergence defines a (non-metric) \emph{connection};
\item The \emph{curvature} of the manifold is linked to information loss (on a flat statistical manifold, expected and observed information coincide).
\end{compactenum}

The authors do not mention the interpretation of \emph{robust} estimators as continuous functions on a statistical manifold.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Region vs industry effects\\ and volatility transmission}\\
  P. Soriano and F. Climent\\
  Financial analysts journal (2006)
\end{book}

This article studies \emph{volatility transmission} (anova) between regions, between industries, between regions within industries, between industries within regions, and confirms the dominance of region effects over industry effects.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Common failings:\\ how corporate defaults are correlated}\\
  S.R. Das et al.
  Journal of finance (2007)
\end{book}

Defaults cluster in time, but this cannot be explained only by the exposure to common risk factors (the authors develop a test for this): \emph{contagion} plays a role.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Cluster analysis:\\ two macro themes dominate Eurozone markets}\\
  A. Harmstone\\
  Bear Stearns (2005)
\end{book}

One can cluster stocks (with the PAM algorithm) using their exposure to risk factors and then use the resulting (two) groups as \emph{macro themes} (depending on the market conditions, these could be value and growth, or something entirely different).

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Information transmission\\ across global markets}\\
  P. Singh\\
  Bear Stearns (2006)
\end{book}

Uncertainty (volatility) is transmitted both ways between Asia (Japan, Hong Kong, Taiwan, South Korea) and the West (US and Europe), while returns are mainly transmitted from Asia to the West with a surprising 1-day lag. As a result, intra-day US strategies should use Asian information.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Implied macro returns:\\ profiting from over-reaction}\\
  A. Harmstone\\
  Bear Stearns (2006)
\end{book}

Yet another article advocating the use of cumulated (time series) residuals.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Multilayer modeling of\\ a market covariance matrix}\\
  R. Staub\\
  Journal of Portfolio management (2006)
\end{book}

Rolling (or weighted rolling) volatility is a bad estimate of future volatility: prefer GARCH modeling.

One can generalize strict factor models (``risk models'') in a mutilayer way: estimate the covariance between asset groups; then, within each group, between the subgroups; etc..

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Liquidity and stock returns:\\ evidence from a pure order-driven market\\ using a new liquidity proxy}\\
  B.R. Marshall\\
  International review of financial analysis (2006)
\end{book}

The \emph{weighted order value} (WOV) is a new liquidity proxy: in 30-minute windows, bin the bids (and asks) with respect to the best one (e.g., 1\% from the best, 2\%, 5\%, 10\%, etc.); compute the bid (resp. ask) execution rate (number or executed orders divided by the number of orders) in each bin; pool the bids in each bin to get the bid order value (the sum of the bid prices times the bid volumes); pool the bins: the weighted bid value is the sum of the products of the bid order values and the bid execution rates; the WOV is then the geometric mean of the bid and ask weighted values.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=302815}{\emph{Sharpening Sharpe Ratios}}\\
  W. Goetzmann et al. (2004)
\end{book}

Sharpe ratios are amenable to manipulations, with options (non-linear payoffs), dynamic strategies (that also modify the distribution of returns: truncate it on the right and fatten the left tail to move the mean to the right) or smoothed returns.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Futures trading volume as a determinant of prices in different momentum phases}\\
  A. Hodgson et al.\\
  International review of financial analysis (2006)
\end{book}

Options trading volume has predictive power on the short-term (15 minutes) stock price; this effect changes with the current price trend.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Advanced frequency and time domain filters for currency portfolio management}\\
  C. Dunis and J. Miao\\
  Journal of asset management (2006)
\end{book}

Many FX traders use a combination of several MACDs (say, 1 and 32 days, 1 and 61 days, 1 and 117 days, equal-weighted), but this strategy does not perform well.

Spectral analysis (the periodogram) can help identify cyclical spells in FX time series (you can try to use it as a trading strategy, but it will not work).

The article suggests to follow the MACD strategy but to modify it in cyclical and/or volatile (as measured by the RiskMetrics model, \ie, an exponentially-weighted non-central variance) periods, by not trading or trading in the opposite direction.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the maximum drawdown\\ of a brownian motion}\\
  M. Magdon-Ismail et al. (2003)
\end{book}

The (asymptotic) expected maximum drawdown (MDD) of a brownian motion is
\begin{align*}
  X(t) &= \sigma W(t) + \mu t \\
  E[D] &= \sqrt{\dfrac\pi2} \sigma \sqrt T               & \text{if } \mu = 0\\
  E[D] &\sim_{T \to\infty} \dfrac{\sigma^2}{2\mu} \log T & \text{if } \mu > 0\\
  E[D] &\sim_{T \to\infty} - \mu T                       & \text{if } \mu < 0.
\end{align*}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://alumnus.caltech.edu/~amir/mdd-risk.pdf}{\emph{Maximum drawdown}} \\
  M. Magdon-Ismail et al. (2003)\\
  Risk (October 2004)
\end{book}

Using the previous article, one can study the term structure of the \emph{Calmar} or \emph{Sterling} ratios.
\begin{align*}
\textrm{Calmar}   &= \dfrac{\text{returns on }[0,T]}{\text{MDD on }[0,T]} \\
\textrm{Sterling} &= \dfrac{\text{returns on }[0,T]}{\text{MDD on }[0,T] - 10 \%}
\end{align*}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{The misuse of expected returns}\\
  E. Hughson et al.\\
  Financial analysts journal (2006)
\end{book}

Yet another article against ratio returns...

At the very least, if you have to deal with ratio returns, prefer their median to their mean.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio selection with parameter and model uncertainty: a multi-prior approach}\\
  L. Garlappi et al.\\
  Review of financial studies (2007)
\end{book}

The article interprets the robust portfolio optimization framework
\begin{align*}
  &\textrm{Maximize } & \min_\alpha w' \alpha - \lambda w' V w \\
  &\textrm{st }       & \alpha_{\textrm{min}} \leq \alpha \leq \alpha_{\textrm{max}}
\end{align*}
in a bayesian setting, as a generalization of the Black--Litterman model, in terms of \emph{uncertainty aversion}.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Common and country-specific components\\ in national stock prices}\\
  O. Hu\\
  Journal of multinational financial management (2006)
\end{book}

\emph{Market integration} can be measured by cointegration -- more precisely, the presence of a \emph{single} cointegration relation.

G7 countries are not integrated: diversification benefits still exist.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Testing for multiple regimes in the tail behavior of emerging currency returns}\\
  B. Candelon and S. Straetmans\\
  Journal of international money and finance (2006)
\end{book}

The \emph{tail index} of a distribution is the parameter $\alpha$ such that
$$
\forall x > 0
\quad
\lim_{ t \to \infty }
\dfrac{ 1 - F(tx) }{ 1 - F(t) }
= x ^ {- \alpha},
$$
\ie, such that the tail of the distribution be a first order approximation of a Pareto distribution
($F_{\textrm{Pareto}}(x) = 1 - a x^{-\alpha}$).

The \emph{Hills estimator} of the tail index in a sample of size $n$ is
$$
\hat \alpha =
\left(
  \dfrac1m
\sum _{j=0} ^{m-1}
\log \dfrac{ X_{(n-j)} }{ X_{(n-m)} }
\right) ^ {-1}
$$
where the $X_{(k)}$ are the order statistics and $m$ is chosen so that $m/n$ be small (the estimator has good asymptotic properties for $m/n \to 0$ and $n \to \infty$).

The authors then perform \emph{recursive tests} for \emph{structural breaks}: when you find a break, split the sample in two and look for new breaks in each chunk.

There are structural breaks in the tail index of emerging market currency returns.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Are international value premiums driven by the same set of fundamentals?}\\
  A.J. Black et al.\\
  International review of economics and finance (2007)
\end{book}

Yet another article that gauges the benefits of diversification (of a value strategy, across G7 countries) using cointegration: cointegration is present.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Using dynamic programming\\ to optimally rebalance portfolios}\\
  W. Sun et al.\\
  Journal of trading (2006)
\end{book}

Dynamic trading strategies include: periodic rebalancing, \emph{tolerance band rebalancing}, \emph{no trade zone} (identical to the tolerance band rebalancing, but the band is not arbitrarily defined and we do not rebalance all the way to the optimal portfolio but just back into the band).

The authors measure, in USD, the tracking error of a portfolio as the risk-free return giving the same utility (\emph{certainty equivalent}) and use this measure in a dynamic program to control rebalancing -- this can be seen as a dynamic equivalent of tolerance band rebalancing. The problem is amenable up to 8 or 9 assets.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://labs.accenture.com/kdd2006_workshop/dmba_proceedings.pdf}{\emph{Forecasting online auctions\\ using dynamic models}}\\
  W. Jank et al.\\
  \href{http://labs.accenture.com/kdd2006_workshop/}{KDD 2006 Workshop on Data mining\\ for Business Applications}
\end{book}

The authors use \emph{functional data analysis} (FDA) to forecast irregular time series (ebay auction prices) taking into account the influence of opening bid, auction length and seller's reputation on the price dynamics (accelaration, velocity and eventually final price).

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://labs.accenture.com/kdd2006_workshop/dmba_proceedings.pdf}{\emph{Data mining in the real world:\\ what do we need and what do we have?}}\\
  F. Fogelman Souli\'e\\
  \href{http://labs.accenture.com/kdd2006_workshop/}{KDD 2006 Workshop on Data mining\\ for Business Applications}
\end{book}

Data mining is widely used for CRM (client relation management), fraud detection, credit scoring, etc. and \emph{extreme data mining}, \ie, data mining with databases beyond 10\,TB is starting to emerge. It is characterized by:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Heterogeneous data;
\item poor data quality (outliers, missing data and even \emph{unlabelled data}, \ie, ``massively missing values'');
\item Large volumes (millions of rows, thousands of variables) that prevent the user from hand-chosing the variables and calls for \emph{data duplication avoidance};
\item Fast model calibration (hours; linear in the number of rows or columns);
\item Automated model quality assessment (they use a \emph{robustness indicator}, $KR$, from Vapnik's \emph{structural risk minimization theory});
\item Real-time model application;
\item Industrialization (``productionalization'') within the same project, not as a separate, long project;
\item Model control (checks deviations in the data distribution);
\item Data export using data mining standards (\href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_Data_Mining}{JDM}, \href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PMML}{PMML});
\item Process control and workflow;
\item Exploratory modeling (the end-user needs to understand the model);
\item Predictive modeling (the end-user want accurate forecasts);
\item Unskilled users.
\end{compactenum}

The article failed to mention the following:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Asynchronicity (we might want real-time results even though some data sources might not be real-time; bursts of data may delay the delivery of the results);
\item Distributed analysis (the volume and location of the data, with the requirement that the data should not be duplicated, will call for the computations to be run where the data is, possibly in different locations).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.eecs.berkeley.edu/Pubs/TechRpts/2006/EECS-2006-183.pdf}{\emph{The landscape of parallel computing research: a view from Berkeley}}\\
  2006
\end{book}

The next big thing in computer science could be \emph{distributed computing}: this article enumerates benchmarks (called ``\emph{dwarfs}'') or challenges for such systems.

Among the potential ``next big thing'', one could add, in no particular order:
declarative programming,
functional languages,
virtualization,
virtual machines.

DNA computers, quantum computers are still decades (or centuries) away, though programming paradigms are already being developped for those architectures.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.algorithm.com.au/talks/concurrency-erlang/}{\emph{Concurrency and Erlang}}\\
  A. Pang\\
%   LCA 2007
\end{book}

Another article about distributed programming and the need for adequate tools -- not threads: in the hands of most programers, they make \texttt{i = i + 1} non-deterministic...

On the same subject:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Some real-time systems already use lock-free programming, e.g., the
\href{http://lac.zkm.de/2005/papers/letz_et_al.pdf}{Jack audio server}, as explained in
the \href{http://lac.zkm.de/2005/proceedings.shtml}{Linux Audio Conference 2005}.
\item \href{http://lambda-the-ultimate.org/search/node/STM}{Software Transactional Memory (STM)} is getting more widely discussed, and is already in the \href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concurrent_Haskell}{Haskell libraries}.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \href{http://www.sci.utah.edu/~shachar/Publications/rmls.pdf}{\emph{Robust moving least squares fitting\\ with sharp features}}\\
  S. Fleishman et al.\\
  \href{http://www.cs.brown.edu/~tor/sig2005.html}{SIGGRAPH 2005}
\end{book}

This article applies an \emph{outlier detection} algorithm (the \emph{forward search algorithm paradigm}: start with a small set of robustly chosen points, progressively add more points, while monitoring some statistic) for \emph{surface reconstruction}, accounting for noise and singularities (\ie, sharp edges).

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Everything you know about\\ dynamic time warping is wrong}\\
  C.A. Ratanamahatana and E. Keogh\\
  SDM 2005
\end{book}

This article disspells a few DTW myths:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item DTW's ability to deal with sequences of different lengths is not an advantage (but not a disadvantage either): you would get the same results after resampling the sequences;
\item The 10\% constraint on warping paths inherited from the speech processing community is not too low but actually much too high for most data mining applications;
\item As a result, DTW is essentially $O(n)$.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio selection in stochastic environments}\\
  J. Liu\\
  Review of financial studies (2007)
\end{book}

The \emph{dynamic portfolio choice} problem can be expressed as a partial differential equation (Merton, 1971), which does not lead to a general, amenable solution. This article derives an explicit solution in the case of \emph{quadratic returns}, generalizing similar results for \emph{Ornstein--Uhlenbeck processes}.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Emergent effective collusion in an economy of perfectly rational competitors}\\
  R.K. Standish and S. Keen\\
  \href{http://www.arxiv.org/abs/nlin.AO/0411006}{\texttt{arXiv:nlin.AO/0411006}}
\end{book}

Some agent-based models predict the emergence of monopolies; irrational agents or delays can help avoid them.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio optimization\\ with drawdown constraints}\\
  A. Chekhlov et al. (2003)
\end{book}

In portfolio optimization, one can replace the standard deviation by other risk measures such as the expected shortfall (ES, conditional value at risk, CVaR) or the conditional drawdown at risk (CDaR).

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Investor sentiment and\\ the cross-section of stock returns}\\
  M. Baker and J. Wurgler\\
  Journal of finance (2006)
\end{book}

You might want to completely reverse your trading strategy (usually: large, mature, low-volatility, profitable, dividend-paying, non-distressed, value stocks) when the investor sentiment is low.

The \emph{investor sentiment} can be computed from the following (annual) proxies (do not forget to remove the systematic risk):
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Opposite of the closed-end fund discount (CEFD), \ie, the average difference between the net asset value (NAV) of closed-end stock funds shares and their market prices;
\item Detrended NYSE share turnover (or any measure of liquidity);
\item Number of IPOs;
\item Average first-day returns on IPOs;
\item Equity share in new issues;
\item Dividend premium, \ie, difference of the P/B of dividend payers and non-payers.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Re-examining the profitability of technical analysis with White's reality check\\ and Hansen's SPA test}\\
  P.H. Hsu and C.M. Kuan (2005)
\end{book}

To avoid data snooping, one can:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Claim it is not a problem (but it is!);
\item Split the data into in- and out-of-sample (but this is arbitrary and discards part of the data);
\item Explicitely test all the models and correct the $p$-values accordingly (but when there are too many tests, the power of the corrected tests drops);
\item Use the bootstrap to compute the $p$-value:
  \begin{compactenum}[$\cdot$]
  \item Build a universe of all trading strategies you might be interested in;
  \item Choose a performance measure (\href{http://citeseer.ist.psu.edu/554873.html}{White} uses the annualized returns, \href{http://www-econ.stanford.edu/faculty/workp/swp05003.pdf}{Hansen} uses the information ratio or $0$ when it is negative);
  \item Estimate the distribution of the maximum performance on bootstrap samples;
  \item Compare the performance of your model with the distribution of the best performing model.
  \end{compactenum}
\end{compactenum}

The article applies this methodology to technical analysis (TA): it works, and works better in less mature markets.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Data Envelopment Analysis}\\
  G.N. Gregoriou and J. Zhu\\
  Journal of portfolio management (2007)
\end{book}

The notion of \emph{efficient frontier} can be generalized to higher
dimensions: several risk measures or inputs (standard deviation,
downside deviation, maximum drawdown), several outputs (returns,
proportion of profitable months, maximum consecutive gain).

If you have enough data points, they provide an approximation of the
efficient frontier (?).

The \emph{efficiency} (closeness to the efficient frontier) can be
defined as the solution of a linear problem:
\begin{align*}
  & \textrm{Minimize } \theta \textrm{ such that} \\
  & \sum_k \lambda_k x_{ik} \leq \theta x_{ik_0} \\
  & \sum_k \lambda_k y_{jk} \geq        y_{jk_0} \\
  & \sum_k \lambda_k = 1 \\
  & \lambda_k \geq 1 \\
  & i : \textrm{input} \\
  & j : \textrm{output} \\
  & k : \textrm{funds} \\
  & k_0 : \textrm{fund whose efficiency is being computed} \\
  & x_{ik} : \textrm{input $i$ of fund $k$} \\
  & y_{jk} : \textrm{output $j$ of fund $k$}.
\end{align*}

See \url{http://people.brunel.ac.uk/~mastjjb/jeb/or/dea.html} for a picture.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimization of the largest US mutual funds using data envelopment analysis}\\
  G.N. Gregoriou\\
  Journal of asset management (2006)
\end{book}

Another article on data envelopment analysis (DEA, sometimes also called \emph{frontier analysis}).
It defines the \emph{classical efficiency} as
$$
\textrm{CCR}_{k_0} = \max
\left\{
  \dfrac{
    \sum_j \mu_j     y_{j k_0}
  }{
    \sum_i \lambda_i x_{i k_0}
  }
  \textrm{ st }
  \forall k
  \
  \dfrac{
    \sum_j \mu_j     y_{j k}
  }{
    \sum_i \lambda_i x_{i k}
  }
  \leq 1
\right\}
$$
and the \emph{super-efficiency} (which is no longer bounded by 1) as
$$
\textrm{CCR}_{k_0} = \max
\left\{
  \dfrac{
    \sum_j \mu_j     y_{j k_0}
  }{
    \sum_i \lambda_i x_{i k_0}
  }
  \textrm{ st }
  \forall k \neq k_0
  \
  \dfrac{
    \sum_j \mu_j     y_{j k}
  }{
    \sum_i \lambda_i x_{i k}
  }
  \leq 1
\right\}.
$$

They also mention the \emph{cross-efficiency model}, but not clearly.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Asset allocation for robust portfolios}\\
  T. Farrelly\\
  Journal of investing (2006)
\end{book}

This article is a variation on Michaud's \emph{resampled portfolios}:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Start with your risk model and your alpha; choose a risk threshold and
a ``sensitivity to underperformance'' $\lambda$;
\item Compute $N=500$ simulated returns from $N(0,V)$ (or $N(\alpha,V)$, the article is not clear) and let
  \begin{align*}
    r_{ij} &= \textrm{return of stock $i$ in situation $j$}\\
    w_{ij} &= \textrm{weight of stock $i$ in} \\
           &\qquad \textrm{the efficient portfolio }(r_{\bullet,j},V)
  \end{align*}
  (the article is not clear here either: there sometimes seems to be
  one optimal portfolio per scenario, sometimes one for the set of all
  scenarios)
\item Minimize 
  $$ \sum_{ij} \left|(w_{ij} - w_i) r_{ij}\right|^\lambda $$
  under the condition that the risk be below the threshold.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Automatic bayesian model averaging\\
        for linear regression and applications\\
        in bayesian curve fitting}\\
  F. Liang et al.\\
  Statistica Sinica 11 (2001) 1005--1029
\end{book}

The article suggests an ``automatic prior'' for bayesian model average
(BMA) -- in situations where it has an edge on model selection, BMA
\emph{is} sensitive to the prior, for instance, to the expected number
of factors in the model.

The inclusion probability still has to be provided by the user; the
authors only provide a prior for $\beta$ and $\sigma$ (equations 10
and 11).

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Evolutionary Monte Carlo: applications to $C_p$ model sampling and change point problem}\\
  F. Liang and W.H. Wong \\
  Statistica Sinica 10 (2000) 317--342
\end{book}

Check \emph{Monte Carlo Strategies in Scientific Computing}, J.S. Liu,
Springer Verlag, 2002, for a 1-page summary of this article.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Are alternatives the next bubble?}\\
  J. Loeys and N. Panigirtzoglou\\
  Journal of alternative investment (2006)
\end{book}

One can use high returns, expensive valuation (low risk premium),
speculative acivity (trading volume, credit, leverage) as a bubble
indicator.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Profitability of price momentum strategies:\\
        surprising evidence from\\
        Pacific-Bassin countries}\\
  P. Ryan and R. Curtin\\
  Journal of investing (2006)
\end{book}

Momentum does not work in Asia -- but have a look at reversal.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Do asset prices reflect fundamentals?\\ 
        Freshly squeezed evidence from\\
        the orange juice market}\\
  J. Boudoukh et al.\\
  Journal of financial economics (2006)
\end{book}

Example of the misuse of linear regression to study a non-linear phenomenon.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Mean-variance versus full-scale optimisation: in- and out-of-sample}\\
  T. Adler and M. Kritzman\\
  Journal of asset management (2007)
\end{book}

The authors suggest to replace quadratic utility (or even power
utility: $U(x) = x^\gamma / \gamma$, which translates \emph{constant
  relative risk aversion} (CRRA)) by \emph{kinked utility} (for
investors whishing to brech a threshold, e.g., the minimum level of
wealth to maintain a certain standard of living) or \emph{S-shaped
  utility} (to reflect asymetric risk behaviour).
\begin{align*}
  U_{\textrm{kinked}} (x) 
  &= \ln(1+x) & \textrm{if } x \geq \theta \\
  &= 10 (x-\theta) + \ln(1+\theta) & \textrm{if } x < \theta \\
  U_{\textrm{S-shaped}}(x) 
  &= -A(\theta-x)^{\gamma_1} &\textrm{if } x \leq \theta \\
  &= -B(x-\theta)^{\gamma_2} &\textrm{if } x > \theta
\end{align*}

\begin{center}
<<KinkedAndSShapedUtility,echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
op <- par(mfrow=c(2,1), mar=c(5,4,4,2))
U <- function (x, theta=-5/100) {
  ifelse(x >= theta, log(1+x), 10*(x-theta) + log(1+theta))
}
curve(U, xlim=c(-.1,.1), las=1,
      xlab="Return", ylab="Utility", 
      main="Kinked utility, theta=-5%")
U <- function (x, theta=-5/100, A=2.25, B=2.25, gamma1=.1, gamma2=.1) {
  ifelse(x <= theta, 
         - A * (theta - x)^gamma1, 
         + B * (x - theta)^gamma2)
}
curve(U, xlim=c(-.1,.1), las=1,
      xlab="Return", ylab="Utility", 
      main="S-shaped utility, theta=-5%, A=B=2.25, gamma1=gamma2=.1")
par(op)
@ 
\end{center}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting financial time series\\using artificial market models}\\
  N. Gupta et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:physics/0506134}
\end{book}

Stock market simulations by \emph{agent-based models} suggest there
are \emph{pockets of predictability}; once one such pocket is closed,
investment strategies trained on previous data become worthless.

In other words: agent-based market simulations confirm the
predictability of stock markets and the presence of structural breaks.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Are you about to handcuff\\ your information ratio?}\\
  R. Staub\\
  Journal of Asset Management (2007)
\end{book}

If you want yet another explanation/application of the FLAM
(fundamental law of active management)...

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Alphas with attitude:\\ revving up your factor model}\\
  B. Lau and G. Platt\\
  Macquarie Research Equities (2007)
\end{book}

Yet another article promoting dynamic, IC-based weights: 
$w = (\var \textrm{IC})^{-1} \cdot \textrm{IC}$, 
using an expanding window and imposing constraints on the weights 
($0 \leq w \leq 0.5$).

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Do industries lead stock markets?}\\
  H. Hong et al.\\
  Journal of Financial Economics (2007)
\end{book}

One can predict market returns from lagged industry returns; this is
due to the \emph{bounded rationality} of investors: they cannot
process all the information about all the stocks immediately when they
receive it; the effect is present in all developped markets; the
predicting power is comparable to that of inflation, default spread
and dividend yield.

Others had already noticed that large or liquid stocks lead small or
illiquid ones.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Noise trader risk:\\evidence from the Siamese twins}\\
  J.T. Scruggs\\
  Journal of Financial Markets (2007)
\end{book}

Identical stocks on different exchanges do not move in parallel: up to
half the weekly volatility is due to noise trading.

Thus, arbitrage is limited and the risk of pairs trading is high.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Behavioral finance: frame-dependant\\decision making and factor anomalies}\\
  T. Suwabe et al.\\
  Goldman Sachs (2006)
\end{book}

Investors look at their \emph{unrealized gains} when deciding to leave
a position: this can be exploited by comparing the current price of a
stock with its average buying price
$$
R_{t+1} = V_t P_t + (1-V_t) R_t
$$
where $P_t$ is the price and $V_t$ the turnover.

This is actually a momentum computed with an exponential moving
average of the price (instead of the lagged price), where the
coefficient depends on the volume.

\begin{book}
  \emph{Profiting from momentum}\\
  R. de Souza\\
  Macquarie Research, Equities (2006)
\end{book}

Another article on the same subject, under the name \emph{capital gains overhang}.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Alpha repair: a factor competition\\approach to stock selection}\\
  J.J. Mezrich and J. Feng\\
  Nomura (2007)
\end{book}

To select the factors to put in your model, regard them as assets,
evaluate their long-term (5-year) returns, their variance matrix, and
feed them to your prefered optimizer to find an optimal ``portfolio of
5 factors'' (you can fine-tune the number of factors).

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Refinements to the Sharpe ratio:\\comparing alternatives for bear markets}\\
  H. Scholz\\
  Journal of Asset Management (2007)
\end{book}

Sharpe ratios (excess returns divided by risk) cannot be used to
compare funds when the returns are negative (they rarely give the
right order).

The \emph{normalized Sharpe ratio} tries to correct this. The Sharpe ratio can be computed as
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{er}_t  &= \textrm{excess returns} \\
  \textrm{mer}_t &= \textrm{market excess returns} \\
  \textrm{er}_t &= \alpha + \beta \, \textrm{mer}_t + \epsilon_t \\
  \textrm{SR} &= \dfrac{
    \alpha + \beta \, \overline{\textrm{mer}}
  }{
    \sqrt{ \beta^2 + \sigma(\textrm{mer})^2 + \sigma(\epsilon)^2 }
  }
\end{align*}
The authors simply use 20 years for 
$\overline{\textrm{mer}}$ 
and 
$\sigma(\textrm{mer})$.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Momentum, reversal\\and the trading behaviors of institutions}\\
  R.C. Gutierrex and C.A. Prinsky\\
  Journal of Financial markets (2007)
\end{book}

Cumulated residual returns (for a 5-year CAPM model) can help devise a
long-term stragegy, while the other component of the momentum
(``relative momentum'') reverts after one year.

More precisely, the article builds a strategy from the signal :
$$ 
\alpha = \dfrac{ 
  \textrm{cumulated residual returns} 
}{
  \textrm{cumulated variance of the resisuals}
}
$$

This effect is linked to institutional ownership.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Countries versus industries in emerging markets: 
    a normative portfolio approach}\\
  J. Estrada et al., Journal of investing (2006)
\end{book}

The authors compare dispersion, information ratio 
($\mathrm{IR} = \mu / \sigma$), 
utility ($U = \log(1 + \mu) - \tfrac12 \sigma^2 / (1 + \mu)^2$),
option price (an option that allows its owner to swap the performance
of the top (or bottom) quartile with the median performance)
of random country portfolios and random industry portfolios 
and conclude that country effects prevail.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the use of hypothesis testing and\\ other problems in financial research}\\
  F. G\'omez-Bezares et al.\\
  Journal of investing (2006)
\end{book}

The authors notices the following problems: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Not enough ``case studies'' -- \ie, you should look at the data;
\item Unnecessary mathematical formulation;
\item Rarely applicable results: when they become familiar with a
  method (say, regression), researchers try to apply it
  everywhere;
\item Bad null hypothesis: it may even
  overlap with the alternative hypothesis -- the use of
  \emph{confidence intervals} besides tests would mitigate the
  problem.
\item Data snooping: be extremely suspicious of ``tables of
  $p$-values'';
\item Publication bias, \ie, unpublished ``tables of
  $p$-values'';
\item Errors in the data -- you should look
  at the data and use \emph{robust methods};
\item Simplifying hypotheses (no transaction costs, no bid-ask spread,
  etc.);
\item Insufficient data (sample too small);
\item Non-parametric tests -- only use them when needed: their power is much lower;
\item Some studies turn qualititive variables into quantitative ones
  in a rather arbitrary way.
\end{compactenum}

The authors fail to notice the following problems:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Unnecessary data binning;
\item Use of linear or gaussian methods without checking if it makes sense.
\end{compactenum}

