\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Was there a Nasdaq bubble in the late 1990s?}\\
  L. P\'astor and P. Veronesi\\
  Journal of financial economics (2006)
\end{book}

The Nasdaq bubble can be explained by uncertainty about future profitability which, by Jensen's formula, increases a firm's value:
\begin{align*}
  \dfrac{ \textrm{Price} }{ \textrm{Dividends} } &=
  \dfrac1{ \textrm{discrount rate} - \textrm{growth} } \\
  \textrm{E}[P/D] & > \dfrac1{ \textrm{discrount rate} - \textrm{E}[\textrm{growth}] }
\end{align*}

