\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{On the aggregation of local risk models for global risk management}\\
  G. Anderson et al. (2005)
\end{book}

To build an integrated risk model, \ie, a risk model covering several
markets, from a model for each market and a global model, with the
drill-down property, \ie, such that the restriction of the integrated
model be the local ones, one can write the global risk model as
$V=BB'$ and replace $B$ by $LB$ where $L$ is to be determined.

The possible values are 
$L = \bigoplus_i \tilde \Theta _i ^{1/2} O_i \Theta_i ^{-1/2}$
with $O_i \in O(n_i)$, 
the $\Theta_i$ are the diagonal blocks of the global model
and $\tilde \Theta_i$ are the desired diagonal blocs -- the local risk models.

Choose the $O_i$ to minimize $\n{ BB' - LBB'L' }^2$ -- or rather, the
distance between the corresponding correlation measures.

This is the \emph{double Procrustes problem}. 

