\begin{book}
  \emph{Subordinated binomial option pricing}\\
  C.W. Chang et al.\\
  Journal of financial research (2006)
\end{book}

Binomial trees can easily be generalised to account for stochastic
volatility, by describing the price evolution with successive binomial
trials up/down and trade/non-trade (this is a \emph{trinomial tree},
the spot price $S$ becoming $uS$, $S$ or $dS$ with probabilities $gh$,
$1-g$ and $g(1-h)$), corresponding to a time change from calendar time
to operational time.

