\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Downside risk}\\
  A. Ang et al.
  Review of Financial Studies (2006)
\end{book}

Stocks with a high sensitivity to downside market movement ($\beta_-$)
have higher returns.

This effect does not (entirely) come from stock with high coskewness
risk having higher returns.

Downside beta can be used as an alpha, but not for high-volatility
stocks.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Transmission of information\\accross international equity markets}\\
  J. Wongswan\\
  Review of Financial Studies (2006)
\end{book}

Macroeconomic announcements in the US or Japan have short-lived (30 to
60 minutes) effects on volatility and volume (not returns) in Korea
and Thailand.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Lehman suite of PCA risk models}\\
  J. Ruiz-Mata (2006)
\end{book}

They provide statistical risk models, for Europe, US, developped Asia
and emerging markets, built as follows: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Using daily (or weekly, monthly) \emph{excess returns} over 40
  (long-only) to 60 (long-short) days, they perform a factor analysis
  with 4 to 8 factors;
\item The number of factors is determined by \emph{random matrix
    theory} (RMT), \ie, by comparing the distribution of the
  eigenvalues with that of the variance matrix of iid gaussian
  variables (they do not seem to realize that RMT uses principal
  component analysis (PCA), not factor analysis, \ie, it assumes there
  is no stock-specific risk: this might bias the methodology towards
  an unduly high number of factors -- they are lucky to have so few);
  they fail to note that the number of factors to retain also depends
  on the size of the universe: 4 or 5 factors might be necessary if
  you have several thousand US stocks, but with just 100, one might be
  sufficient;
\item The training period is determined by computing the distance
  between the factor model variance matrix and the \emph{forward}
  sample variance matrix (they do not use the distance between those
  matrices, by the difference between the forecasted risk and the
  realized risk, for a large number of long-short portfolios -- this
  is very similar to the definition of a \emph{matrix norm}, but we do
  not need to have time series of forward returns, one forward return
  for each stock suffices).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Is fundamental analysis effective\\ for growth stocks?}\\
  P.S. Mohanram (2003)
\end{book}

This article lists financial variables likely to help predict future
returns for growth stocks. There are classical fundamental variables:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item ROA
\item ROA growth
\item Cash flow ROA
\item Cash flow $-$ Net income
\item Asset turnover (sales / begining-of-period assets) growth
\item Operating margin (gross margin / sales) growth
\item Leverage reduction
\item Current ratio (?) growth
\item Equity issuance in previous year
\end{compactenum}
and growth-specific variables (all sectorwise): 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Earnings stability: 5-year ROA variance
\item Growth stability: 5-year sales growth variance
\item Earnings growth $-$ Sales growth
\item R\&D / Begining-of-period assets
\item Capital expenditures / Assets
\item Advertising / Assets
\end{compactenum}

