\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Using Economic and financial information\\ for stock selection}\\
  I. Roko and M. Gilli (2006)
\end{book}
  
Instead of building a model using linear regression on a moving
window, they use a \emph{classification tree}.

They do not produce a single tree, but a set of trees (a \emph{bag},
a \emph{forest}), each estimated on a bootstrap sample, to lower the
variance of the predictor -- this should be reminiscent of the set of
models produced by bayesian model averaging.

They do not predict the future returns of the stocks but just whether
they outperform, underperform or remain neutral -- but the method can
be generalized to \emph{regression trees} (and forests).

They discretize the predictive variables, thereby discarding
information: this is a mistake.

