\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Investment strategies using options on ETFs}\\
  A. Seddik Meziani (2006)
\end{book}

On the use of \emph{covered calls} (calls written when you own the
underlying, as opposed to \emph{naked calls}): if you think the price
will drop for a stock you own, you can write a call: if the price goes
up, you just fail to cash on it, if it goes down, you do not lose
anything and even pocket the option price.

The author also mentions \emph{short straddles} (write a put and a
call, if you think the price will not move) and \emph{long straddles}
(buy a call and a put, if you think the price will move but have no
view on its direction).

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{The other January effect}\\
  M.J. Cooper et al.\\
  Journal of Financial Economics (2006)
\end{book}

The \emph{January Effect} states that small and low-priced stocks that lose a
lot at the end of the year rebound in January.

The (completely unrelated) \emph{Other January Effect} states that
January returns help predict the returns of the remaining 11 months.
The article confirms this effect, for capitalization-weighted or
equal-weighted portfolios, large or small stocks, value or growth
stocks.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Applied Functional Data Analysis\\Methods and Case Studies}\\
  J.O. Ramsay and B.W. Silverman\\
  Springer Verlag (2002)
\end{book}

% Chapter 2: Criminology

One can reliably smooth a function, defined by a noisy set of points,
by expanding it over a basis (e.g., splines -- in case of periodic
phenomena, you can also consider a Fourier expansion) and by adding a
penalty: if you want the $k$th derivative to be smooth, add a penalty
on the $(k+2)$th derivative. The smoothing parameter can be estimated
by \emph{cross-validation}.

The principal component analysis (PCA) of smoothed data is still noisy
and not interpretable: \emph{functional PCA} (fPCA) solves the problem
by putting the smoothing inside the PCA algorithm -- e.g., for the
first component, you maximize a penalized variance. More precisely,
the usual conditions 
\begin{align*}
  & \forall i        & \int \xi_i^2 &= 1 \\
  & \forall i \neq j & \int \xi_i \xi_j &= 0 \\
\intertext{become}
  & \forall i        & \int \xi_i^2 + \lambda \int \xi_i '' {}^2 &= 1 \\
  & \forall i \neq j & \int \xi_i \xi_j + \lambda \int \xi_i'' \xi_j'' &= 0.
\end{align*}
Thanks to the use of basis functions, the computations boil down to
linear algebra.

To plot and interpret the principal components, just plot the mean function
plus and minus some multiple of those components.

The book uses fPCA to study crime careers -- but the same ideas could
be applied to parallel plots.

% Chapter 8

Classical methods (e.g., Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)) can
either be applied on the first functional principal components or
``penalized'' as PCA was.

% Chapter 3: Non-durable goods index

A \emph{phase-plane plot}, \ie, a plot of the acceleration against the
velocity (since you want to use the second derivative, use a penalty
on the fourth), can be interpreted in terms of energy and momentum;
one should look for cycles, their radius, their center, changes in
their shape, etc. The book examines a consumption index phase plane
plot.

% Chapters 4, 8: Bone shapes (paleopathology)

To expand plane curves (here, bone shapes) over a basis of functions,
one can use \emph{landmarks} or parametrize the curves with
\emph{arc-length}.  

If the principal components are not directly interpretable, you can
perform a \emph{varimax rotation}: this is a rotation in the subspace
spanned by the first few principal components that maximizes the
variance of the coordinates (``loadings''); it makes the loadings
either large or close to zero and therefore eases the interpretation
of the components. With the \emph{promax rotation}, one first performs
a varimax rotation, takes the power 2 or 4 of the loadings, so as to
simplify them further, and looks for an invertible (not necessarily
orthogonal) matrix $Q$ that approximates those loadings: $Q b \approx
(R_{\textrm{varimax}} b) ^ 2$. (In R, check the \texttt{varimax} and
\texttt{promax} functions.) This sounds like a poor man's
\emph{independant component analysis} (ICA).

% Chapter 5: Distributions

One can use the same ideas to study families of sample distributions,
by smoothing the sample distribution functions. Those distributions
can then be used in, say, hierarchical models.

% Chapters 6 and 7: Human growth, registering handwriting, registering weather data

The mean curve may fail to exhibit the features of the individual
curves, especially if (as in the example of human growth) they differ
in location -- they are averaged out. Before computing the mean, you
can align the functions: this is called \emph{registration} or
\emph{(dynamic) time warping}. This accounts for phase changes -- but
one can also allow for amplitude changes -- this can be expressed and
implemented as an eigenvalue problem.

Registration transforms the set of curves to study into a set of pairs
of curves: the aligned curves and the warping functions $h(t)$ (or the
\emph{time deformation functions} $h(t) - t$). 

Depending on the data, you may want to register the functions
themselves of their first derivative.

\emph{Monotone curve smoothing} (e.g., human growth or time warping
functions) can be performed as follows: instead of looking directly
for a smooth increasing function $H$, look for the \emph{relative
  acceleration} $w = H'' / H'$, \ie, look for a differential equation
$H'' = w H'$. As usual, smooth $w$ using a penalty on 
$\int \va{ w'' }$.

% Chapter 9: Item Response Theory

\emph{Item Response Theory} (IRT) studies answers to ability tests. The
\emph{ability curve} is a curve in $[0,1]^N$, where $N$ is the number
of questions, whose $k$th coordinate is the probability or correctly
answering the $k$th question -- we assume that the test allows us to
rank the candidates, \ie, that they are indeed described by a
1-dimensional subspace, \ie, that the test only measures one thing --,
it goes from \textbf{0} (bad candidate) to \textbf{1} (excellent
candidate) and wriggles in between depending on the \emph{difficulty}
and \emph{discriminability} of the questions.

\begin{center}
<<IRTDiffDiscDef,echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
op <- par(mfrow=c(2,2), mar=c(4,4,2,2))
f <- function () {
  plot(NA, ylab="", xlim=c(0,1), ylim=c(-1,1), axes=FALSE, xlab="")
  box()
  abline(h=0, lty=3)
  mtext("ability", side=1, line=1)
  mtext("log-odds", side=2, line=1)
}
f()
abline(-1,4, col="blue", lwd=3)
title(main="Easy")

f()
abline(-3,4, col="blue", lwd=3)
title(main="Difficult")

f()
abline(-.2,.4, col="blue", lwd=3)
title(main="Not discriminant")

f()
abline(-4,8, col="blue", lwd=3)
title(main="Discriminant")
par(op)
@ 
\end{center}

The arclength on that curve can be used as a measure~$\theta$ of
\emph{ability}.

IRT models the probability of a correct answer to question $i$ as a
function of the ability $\theta$: logistic regression measures
difficulty and discriminability , but one can also devise models that
account for a non-negligible probability of right answer at low
ability (the candidate can answer at random).
The picture is complicated by the fact that the ability $\theta$ is
unknown. 

Functional PCA allows more freedom in the modeling of the success
probabilities.

% Chapter 10: Functional linear model

A \emph{functional linear model} is a continuous analogue of a
regression of a time series versus another, including lags:
\begin{align*}
y_i(t) &= \alpha(t) + \sum_{s=t-k}^t \beta_s(t) x_i(t) + \epsilon_i(t)\\
y_i(t) &= \alpha(t) + \int_{t-k} ^t \beta(s,t) x_i(t) \,ds + \epsilon_i(t). 
\end{align*}
The data can be visualized by a \emph{cross-correlation matrix}, the
model can be fitted after discretization by \emph{finite elements},
the fit can be assessed by looking at the MSE (mean square error) 
as a function of time.
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{SSE}(t) &= \sum_{i=1}^N \left( y_i(t) - \hat y_i(t) \right)^2 \\
  \textrm{SSM}(t) &= \sum_{i=1}^N \left( y_i(t) - \bar y_i(t) \right)^2 \\
  R^2 &= 1 - \dfrac{ \int \textrm{SSE}(t) dt }{ \int \textrm{SSM}(t) dt }
\end{align*}

% Chapter 11: Principal Differential Analysis (handwriting recognition)

One can characterize a family of functions (here, hand-written
letters) by a differential equation
$$ D^3 f_i = \alpha + \beta_0 f_i + \beta_1 D f_i + \beta_2 D^2 f_i + \epsilon_i $$
which can then be used for supervised classification: \emph{Principal
  Differential Analysis} is a functional analogue of LDA. 

% Chapter 12: Integrated least squares (juggling)

This differential equation can actually be a vector one, that allows
for \emph{coupling} between the coordinates -- especially when there
is no canonical coordinate system (here, the hand movements of a
juggler).

Those differential equations can be seen as a continuous analogue of
regression and the coefficients can be estimated by \emph{integrated
  least squares}.

\nextbook

\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock market diversity}\\
  R. Fernholz, Intech (2005)
\end{book}

Studying portfolios in a 1-period world can be misleading: by modeling
them as stochastic processes (with time-varying, \ie, stochastic,
weights), one can decompose the portfolio returns into the contribution
of its contituents and the contribution (\emph{excess growth rate}) of
the volatility and the correlation of those constituents, accounting
for rebalancing and diversification -- this is called \emph{stochastic
  portfolio theory}.

The distribution of the capitaliztion in the market changes over time;
one can measure the \emph{diversity} of this distribution with the
\emph{entropy}
$$ S = \sum_i - w_i \log w_i $$
where $w_i$ are the market capitaliztion weights, or with 
$$ D_p = \left( \sum_i w_i ^ p \right) ^ {1/p} $$
(people suggest $p=0.5$ or $p=0.76$).  Each such diversity measure is
canonically associated with a \emph{diversity-weighted portfolio} (in
our examples, its weights would be $1/N$ or (proportional to)
$w_i^{1/p}$).

One can note that when market diversity rises, active strategies
outperform passive ones and conversely.

Diversity measures are linked to the performance of ``size'' factors. 

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diversity-weighted indexing}\\
  R. Fernholz, et al., JPM (1998)
\end{book}

Capitalization-weighted indexes are never rebalanced, except when
stocks enter or leave the index, \ie, stocks with a historically high
or low price are bought or sold -- the opposite of what you should be
doing.

Diversity-weighted indexes mitigate this problem: the weights are proportional to 
some power, $p \in [0,1]$, of the market capitalization (e.g., $p=0.76$).

The returns of a diversity-weighted portfolio can be decomposed into a
contribution of the variation in the distribution of the
capitalization of the stocks and a contribution of the changes in
stock rankings (``kinetic differential'' -- strictly speaking, this
component also accounts for dividend payments and stocks leaving and
entering the index). The former is noisy and mean-reverting, the
latter brings the returns.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Volatility capture as an alpha source}\\
  L. Vasquez, SEI (2005)
\end{book}

Rebalancing a portfolio so that its weights remain constant is better
than a buy-and-hold strategy.

``Volatility capture'' is also sometimes called \emph{compound growth}
or \emph{diversity-weighted indexing}.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Diversification returns and asset contributions}\\
  D.G. Booth and E.F. Fama\\
  Financial Analysts Journal (1992)
\end{book}

A portfolio rebalanced so as to keep its weights constant performs
better than a non-rebalanced one. More precisely, the returns of a
buy-and-hold strategy are:
\begin{align*}
  \sum_i w_i \left( \prod _t ( 1 + r_{it} ) - 1 \right ) 
  & \approx 
  \sum _i w_i \left( E[r_{i\cdot}] - \tfrac12 \var [ r_{i\cdot} ] \right) \\
  & \approx
  \sum _i w_i r_i - \tfrac12 \sum_i w_i \sigma_{ii}  
\end{align*}
while those of a rebalanced one are 
\begin{align*}
  \prod _t \left( 1 + \sum _i w_i r_{it} \right) - 1 
  & \approx
  E \left[ \sum _i w_i r_{i\cdot} \right] \\
  & \qquad 
  -\tfrac12 \var \left[ \sum_i w_i r_{i\cdot} \right] \\
  & \approx
  \sum _i w_i r_i - \tfrac12 \sum_{ij} w_i w_j \sigma_{ij}.
\end{align*}
The difference between the two is called the \emph{excess growth
  rate}:
$$ \tfrac12 \left( 
  \sum_i w_i \sigma_{ii} - \sum_{ij} w_i w_j \sigma_{ij}
\right).
$$

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stochastic portfolio theory\\and stock market equilibrium}\\
  R. Fernholz and B. Shay\\
  Journal of Finance (1982)
\end{book}

A continuous-time derivation of the \emph{excess growth rate}.

\nextbook 
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model uncertainty and forecasting, a practitioner point of view}\\
  B. Bellone and E. Michaux (2006)
\end{book}

The article compares several model selection procedures: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Linear regression bayesian model averaging (BMA) is good, especially for the short term;
\item Dynamic factor models (DFA), \ie, regression on the factors from a factor analysis and their lags, is not bad, especially for the long term.
\end{compactenum}

The article also examined
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Median BMA (the model obtained from the variables whose inclusion probability is beyond 1/2);
\item The general-to-specific (GETS) algorithm (a stepwise variable
  selection, with multiple steps and tests).
\end{compactenum}

The authors use a 
\href{http://www.scilab.org/}{Scilab} econometric toolbox developped at the 
\textsc{Ensae}: 
``\href{http://dubois.ensae.net/grocer.html}{Grocer}''.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolio insurance: the extreme value approach to the CPPI method}\\
  P. Bertrand and J.L. Prigent (2001)
\end{book}

The CPPI (constant proportion portfolio insurance) method is
a simplified asset allocation strategy that tries to
guarantee a minimum terminal wealth. The \emph{floor} is the
present value of the insured terminal value; the
\emph{cushion} is the difference between the portfolio value
and the floor (it should remain positive). CPPI suggests to
invest a fixed proportion (the \emph{multiple}, e.g., 10) of
the cushion in the risky asset and keep the rest in the
risk-free asset -- if the cushion is already large, 10 times
the cushion could exceed the portfolio value: in this case,
everything would be invested in the risky asset.

The article explains how to choose the multiple, using quantiles.

Options-based portfolio insurance (OBPI) is another
portfolio insurance (PI) method.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Financially-motivated\\model performance measures}\\
  C. Friedman and S. Sandow\\
  Journal of credit risk (2006)
\end{book}

Classical performance measures (e.g., contingency
table-based measures such as the type I and type II error
rates; rank-based measures (that fail to properly account
for extreme events); calibrated measures (?); conditional
information entropy ratio (CIER) (?), etc.) do not easily
generalize beyond two states and are not consistent with
utility theory. The article suggests to compare the
performance of two models with their Kullback--Leibler
distance to the data:
%$$
%\textrm{Performance} ( \textrm{model 1}, \textrm{model 2} ) =
$$
\textrm{KL}(\textrm{model 1}, \textrm{data}) -
\textrm{KL}(\textrm{model 2}, \textrm{data}).
$$
Those Kullback--Leibler distances can be expressed in terms
of a utility function, to be chosen -- preferably of the
form
$$
U(\textrm{wealth}) = \dfrac{
  \textrm{wealth} ^ { 1 - \kappa } - 1
}{
  1 - \kappa
}.
$$
This setup can be robustified and generalized to multi-state
or continuous models. 

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{A heteroskedasticity-consistent\\
    covariance matrix estimator and\\
    a direct test for heteroskedasticity}\\
  H. White, Econometrica (1980)
\end{book}

In presence of heteroskedasticity, the coefficients of the
linear model $y \sim x$ are consistent, but their variance
matrix, usually defined as 
$$ \dfrac1n \sum_i \hat \sigma ^2  x_i' x_i $$
is not. Instead, one can replace the standard deviation 
$\hat \sigma$
by the residuals $\hat \epsilon_i$: 
$$ \hat V = \dfrac1n \sum_i \hat \epsilon_i ^2 x_i' x_i. $$

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  1/N\\
  V. DeMiguel et al. (2005--2006)
\end{book}

If you do not have much data, assign the same fraction of
your wealth to each asset in your universe; if you have more
data, use a minimum-variance portfolio; only if you have
huge amounts of data (years of tick data, centuries of daily
data) should you consider mean-variance optimization. In all
cases, imposing constraints to your optimization problem is
a good idea.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Towards a new early warning system\\ of financial crises}\\
  M. Bussiere and M. Fratzscher\\
  Journal of international money and finance (2006)
\end{book}

Early warning systems (EWS) of financial (currency) crises for
emerging market economies (EME) shoud not consider two states (crisis,
non-crisis) but three (pre-crisis, crisis, post-crisis), to avoid the
\emph{post-crisis bias}.

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
