\iffalse

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{A new European alpha model}\\
  C. Marin (SSgA, 2006)
\end{book}

Ciprian suggests a new model, containing three factors: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Residual Momentum (RM, 25\%): cumulated sum of the Fama-French residuals (residuals of the linear regression of stock returns on market returns, growth-value return spread, large-small return spread), on the last 12 months, without the previous month;
\item Implied Discount Rate (IDR, 25\%) from a residual income model (?);
\item A ``ComboStat'' (CS, 50\%) factor.
\end{compactenum}
The ComboStat factor is computed as follows: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Take one year worth of daily prices;
\item For each stock, normalize the price;
\item Perform a principal component analysis on the stock prices;
\item Retain the non-stationary components (using, e.g., the Dickey-Fuller test) among the first 20 components;
\item For each stock, regress the normalized forward returns on those components and compute a forecast of those returns;
\item Repeat with 2, 3,\ldots,10 years worth of data;
\item Average those forecasts.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Absolute-return investment analytics}\\
  AlphaSimplex and SSgACM (2006)
\end{book}

Since illiquidity produces autocorrelation, one can use
autocorrelations to gauge illiquidity, e.g., with Ljung and Box's $Q$
statistic. (One might also be able to define an illiquidity measure
using \emph{small-world networks}.)

The Sharpe ratio estimator is asymptotically gaussian, 
$$ \widehat{\textrm{SR}} \sim N\left( \textrm{SR} , 
\dfrac{
  1 + \tfrac12 \textrm{SR}^2
}{T}
\right).
$$
For iid returns, the change-of-frequency formula si straightforward, 
$$ \widehat{ \textrm{SR}(q) } \sim N \left(
\sqrt q \cdot \textrm{SR},
q \cdot \dfrac{
  1 + \tfrac12 \textrm{SR}^2
}{T}
\right).
$$
For non-iid returns, it is less straightforward -- but doable.

The variance of the returns of a long-short portfolio can be
decomposed into the variance of the long portfolio, plus that of the
short one, plus a covariance term.

One can define conditional and unconditional exposures of stocks to
risk factors: we measure the impact of a change in one factor, either
fixing all the other factors, or taking their correlation into account
-- to get one from the other, just multiply by the correlation matrix
of the factor returns.

One can define an \emph{unconditional value at risk} (UVaR) or a VaR
conditional on the value of the risk factors: this CVaR is a function
of the risk factors (to this end, one can assume that the returns
follow an amenable distribution, e.g., a gaussian one).
\begin{align*}
  P[ X \leqslant - \textrm{UVaR}(\alpha) ] &= \alpha \\
  P[ X \leqslant - \textrm{CVaR}(\alpha,\Lambda) \,|\, \Lambda] 
  &= \alpha \\
\end{align*}
Similarly, one can define unconditional and conditional loss
probabilities and expected thresholds.

A \emph{regime-switching model} is simply a regression model 
$y = B X + \textrm{noise}$ 
where $B$ and the variance of the noise depend on a
2-value hidden state.

Correlation matrices should be prefered over variance matrices: they
are more stable, lead to more parsimonious time-varying models and
allow the user to provide his own variances.

The portfolios maximally and minimally correlated with a set of risk
factors correspond to the largest and the smallest eigenvalues of $V'
E v' E$.

\fi

\nextbook 

\begin{book}
  \emph{Smoothly mixing regressions}\\
  J. Geweke and M. Keane (2005)
\end{book}

One can extend the mixture of gaussians model by allowing the
