<<aItECCFMST,echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
  set.seed(2)
  library(MASS)
  cmdplot <- function (d, i=1:2, m=ape::mst(d)) {
    y <- cmdscale(d,max(i))
    y[ !is.finite(y) ] <- 0 # This happens if the tree is flat: one dimension suffices
    x <- sammon(d, y=y, k=max(i))$points
    plot(x[,i], pch=" ", axes=F, xlab="", ylab="",
         xlim = range(x[,1]) + c(-.2,.2) * diff(range(x[,1])))
    text(x[,i], colnames(d))
    x <- x[,i]
    ii <- which(m==1, arr.ind=T)
    for (k in 1:dim(ii)[1]) {
      j <- ii[k,]
      segments( x[j[1],1], x[j[1],2], x[j[2],1], x[j[2],2], col="red" )
    }
    text(x[,i], colnames(d))
  }
  treedist <- function (d, m=ape::mst(d)) {
    n <- dim(d)[1]
    dd <- ifelse(m==1, 1, Inf)
    diag(dd) <- 0
    for (k in 1:n) {
      for (i in 1:n) {
        for (j in 1:n) {
          dd[i,j] <- min(dd[i,]+dd[,j], dd[i,j])
        }
      }
    }
    dd
  }
  library(ape)
  td <- treedist(1 - V)
  cmdplot(td + .1*rlnorm(length(td)))
@
\end{center}

The book ends with an indroduction to options: first on an
idealized market, then on a real one, that complicates the
picture (discontinuous stock returns, unknown and changing
volatility, discrete time, transaction costs, round lots,
non-gaussian distributions): replicating portfolio and
perfect hedging strategy do not exist.

Among the interesting plots:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Probability distribution function with a vertical log-scale, to assess the fatness of the tails;
\item Autocorrelation function with a log-scale on both axes;
\item Spectral density with a log-scale on both axes;
\item Probability distribution function of the stock returns over
  time (3-dimensional graph);
\item Probability of returning to the origin after $n$ steps, with a
  log-scale on both axes;
\item Histogram of the correlation matrix of the stock returns, over
  time.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantitative stock selection in Japan 
  and the United States: some past and current issues}\\
  J.B. Guerard (2006)
\end{book}

The author computes Information Coefficients (ICs) for a
dozen yearly, quarterly, monthly factors.  

They perform a robust (biweight) ``latent root regression'':
robust to account for the outliers, ``latent root'' to
account for multicolinearity (``latent root'' seems to be a
weird synonym of ``eigenvalue'' -- I do not know how
``latent root regression'' relates to PCR (Principal
Component Regression), PLS (Partial Least Squares) or ridge
regression).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian clustering of many ARCH models}\\
  L. Bauwens and J.V.K. Rombouts
\end{book}

Instead of fitting a GARCH model to each time series in you
sample, you can try to fit your data as a mixture of GARCH
models. As usual with mixtures, you can fit them with
bayesian methods (MCMC: Monte Carlo Markov Chains). 

This can be seen as a clustering method or as a poor man's
mixed GARCH model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Multiple alpha sources and active management}\\
  E.H. Sorensen at al. (2004)
\end{book}

This article builds on very unpedagogical foundations:
Grinold and Kahn's book.

They try to devise ``optimal'' (in the sense that they
maximize the rank correlation with the forward returns --
this is a form of robust regression, albeit not a very
academic one) weights to combine two factors using their ICs
-- forgetting that the ICs are relevant for non-gaussian
data while they assume theirs are gaussian.

In a later article, the same authors claim that this article
claims (or even ``proves'') that the optimal weights are
$$ \textbf{w} = (\var \textrm{IC})^{-1} \cdot \textrm{IC}. $$

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Temporal data and the relational model}\\
  C.J. Date et al. (2003)
\end{book}

The relational model can handle temporal data: just define
an ``interval'' type and a few operators to handle them --
optimizing the queries is a non-trivial and non-tackled
\emph{implementation} problem.

``Time point'' types are granular: they come with ``next''
and ``previous'' operators, e.g., ``date with daily
granularity'', ``date with monthly granularity'', ``date
with business day granularity'', etc.

\texttt{PACK} and \texttt{UNPACK} operators, that merge
overlapping/abutting intervals and that split intervals into
unions of 1-point intervals, are handy to write queries or
constraints -- e.g., primary key constraints.

Other operators can be readily generalized: unpack
everything, apply the operator, pack the results. A few
intricacies arise, because in higher dimensions, \ie, if you
have several interval columns, the packed form is not
unique.

A semi-temporal (or \emph{current}) table is a table with a
\texttt{SINCE} column.

A \emph{historical} table is a table with a \texttt{DURING}
column.

The book suggests to avoid a \texttt{NOW} special value (its
semantic would be changing...) and to avoid using the
end-of-time timestamp for open-ended intervals (indeed, that
would mean putting incorrect values in the database):
instead, use two sets of tables, one for historical data
(with a \texttt{DURING} column) another for current data
(with a \texttt{SINCE} column) -- but updating the tables
(since it may involve moving data from one set of table to
another) or writing the constraints becomes inordinately
complex.

\emph{Bitemporality} is briefly mentionned: ``The database
is not the database. The log is the database. The database
is merely an optimized access path to the most recent
version of the log.''
   
The book uses the vocabulary of the relational model
(``relation'' instead of ``contents of a table'', ``relvar''
instead of ``table'', ``attribute'' instead of ``column'',
``tuple'' instead of ``row'') and the examples use the
\emph{Tutorial D} language (recalled in the introduction).

There is an \emph{annotated} bibliography.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Needles, Haystacks and hidden factors}\\
  G. Miller (2006)
\end{book}

In a risk model, statistical factors can be used to
complement fundamental factors and help mitigate portfolio
risk under-estimation.

(The article distinguishes between three kinds of factors:
\emph{fundamental factors}, whose exposures are estimated by
hand and whose returns are computed from the exposures;
\emph{macroeconomic factors}, whose returns are known and
whose exposures are computed from the returns; and
\emph{statistical factors}.)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Independant variable selection:
  application of independant component analysis\\
  to forecasting a stock index}\\
  A. Cichocki et al. (2005)
\end{book}

The article generalizes PCR (Principal Component Regression) 
by replacing 
PCA (Principal Component Analysis) 
by
ICA (Independant Compoment Analysis) -- ICA is used for
source separation in acoustics and to build some statistical
risk models (perhaps APT).

The authors also add a bit of neural networks, because it is
trendy -- but they apparently fail to understand what ICA
is...

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Introduction to modern portfolio optimization}\\
  B. Scherer and D. Martin (2005)
\end{book}

Chapter 1 introduces linear and quadratic optimization --
but assumes you already know about scenario optimization or
the Black--Litterman model.

Quadratic optimization, aka \emph{mean-variance
  optimization} or \emph{Markowitz optimization} or
\emph{modern portfolio theory} goes as follows: you have $n$
(jointly) gaussian random variables $X=(X_1,\dots,X_n)$, you
know their mean $\boldsymbol{\alpha} = E[X]$, their variance
matrix $V=\var X$ (that matrix is also called a \emph{risk model})
and you want to find a linear combination 
$w_1 X_1 + \cdots + w_n X_n = \textbf w' X$ 
(\ie, a \emph{portfolio}) with 
$w_1 + \cdots + w_n = \textbf{w}' \textbf{1} = 1$ 
under the condition that 
$\var \textbf w ' X$ 
be below some threshold (the risk constraint the client asks
you not to breach), \ie,
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Maximize }  & \textbf{w}' \boldsymbol{\alpha} \\
  \textrm{such that } & \textbf{w}' \textbf{1} = 1 \\
  & \textbf{w}' V \textbf{w} \leq \textrm{threshold}
\end{align*}

There are variants where the risk constraint is turned into
a \emph{soft constraint}, depending on the \emph{risk
  aversion} parameter $\lambda$.
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Maximize }  & \textbf{w}' \boldsymbol{\alpha} 
  - \tfrac12 \lambda \textbf{w}' V \textbf{w} \\
  \textrm{such that } & \textbf{w}' \textbf{1} = 1
\end{align*}

Caveats: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item If two assets are correlated, the optimizer will not
  manage to distinguish them and the weights will vary a lot
  (but the sum of their weights will be better defined):
  this is the same problem as multicolinearity in
  regression.
\item The estimation of $\boldsymbol{\alpha}$ and $V$ can
  have an impact on the optimal portfolio (nowhere in the
  book do they use a risk model: always the sample variance
  matrix, or a ``robust variance matrix'').
\end{compactenum}


If you have enough data, you can dispense with the gaussian
assumption and use a \emph{scenario-based optimization}. For
instance, let $S$ be a matrix whose rows are realization of
the random variable $X$; to test for \emph{arbitrage}
situations, one could solve
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Minimize }  & \textbf{w} ' \textbf{1} \\
  \textrm{such that } & S \textbf{w} \geq 0
\end{align*}
The first line is the money you invest (ideally, none) and
the second is the requirement that the payoff be positive in
\emph{all} the realizations.

This first chapter also presents (or, rather, assumes that
you already know) the Black--Litterman model and remarks
that adding constraints to a quadratic optimization problem
so that the solution looks better is equivalent to imposing
a \emph{view} -- but a very strict one.

Chapter 2 presents the syntax of \textsc{Simple}, the
``language'' used to describe the optimization problems
(actually, it is plain S+, with a lot of placeholders, as in
template-intensive numerical C++). It is a general
optimizer: linear, quadratic, MIP (Mixed Integer
Programming), convex, non-convex; the examples given range
from Maximum Likelihood Estimators (MLE) to multistage
stochastic programming to problems with CDS.  The chapter
also presents semi-quadratic, CRRA and other utility
functions -- but without a proper background in utility
theory, this is not readily understandable.

Chapter 3 enumerates the extensions one can make to
mean-variance optimization: constraints on the MCTR
(Marginal Contribution to Risk), tracking a benchmark,
tracking several benchmarks, using several risk models,
using several risk measures, imposing lower bounds on
weights, imposing bounds on the number of assets, including
transaction costs, etc.

The more constraints you add, the weirder the efficient
frontier and the more irregular the portfolio composition: a
slight change in the acceptable risk threshold can yield
drastic changes in the optimal portfolio composition --
constraints actually chisel more edges on the simplex on the
boundary of which the optimal portolios lie...

Chapter 4 focuses on \emph{resampled portfolios}, \ie,
portfolios optimal for resampled values of 
  $\boldsymbol{\hat\alpha}$ 
and 
  $\hat V$; 
similarly, a \emph{resampled Sharpe portfolio} is the Sharpe
portfolio for resampled values of
  $\boldsymbol{\hat\alpha}$ 
and 
  $\hat V$.

That notion provides a good argument against the long-only
constraint: if you take many resampled Sharpe portfolios and
average them, you get the true Sharpe portfolio; but with a
long-only constraint, you do not -- constrained optimal
portfolios are biased...

Not bad enough? If one of your assets has zero expected
returns (say, a lottery ticket), the long-only constraint
will, on average, suggest that you buy it.

This chapter also suggests the use of the \emph{Mahalanobis
  distance} (they almost clearly recall what it is) 
$$ d _{\textrm{Mahalanobis}} (\textbf{w}_1, \textbf{w}_2) = 
(\textbf{w}_1 - \textbf{w}_2) ' 
V^{-1}
(\textbf{w}_1, \textbf{w}_2)
$$
to compare portfolios. Some people also advocate the use
of the \emph{tracking error distance},
$$ d _{\textrm{TE}} (\textbf{w}_1, \textbf{w}_2) = 
(\textbf{w}_1 - \textbf{w}_2)' 
V
(\textbf{w}_1, \textbf{w}_2).
$$
You can use them to design a statistical test to compare
portfolios -- but that test turns out not to have any power.

The chapter confusingly ends with a compendium of problems
with resampled portfolios and/or the long-only constraint
(mainly, they tend to invest in every stock) and advocates
the use of robust methods.

Actually, resampling can prevent the adverse effects of the
long-only constraint: construct resampled portfolios without
the constraint and discard the stocks whose weight is not
positive often enough -- \ie, is not significantly
positive.

Resampling, aka \emph{bootstrap}, can also give you an idea
of the distribution of Sharpe or Sortino ratios -- and
\emph{double bootstrap} (they try to explain what it is) can
help lower the bias.

Chapter 5 addresses non-gaussianity, starting with
\emph{scenario optimization} and expected utility; they
mention \emph{copulas} but do not use them; finally they
suggest replacing variance by MAD (Mean Absolute Deviation),
semi-variance or CVaR (Conditional Value at Risk, aka
Expected Shortfall or ES). The chapter closes on an
application of scenario optimization and CVaR to CDOs.

Chapter 6, devoted to robust statistics, is the gist of the
book. The most straightforward robust methods are the
\emph{trimmed} ones (trimmed mean, trimmed regression (LTS),
estimation of the volatility of a time series where you
discard the observations beyond $2.5\sigma$, etc.). Where
least squares methods minimize the sum of squared residuals,
$\sum_i r_i^2$, an \emph{$M$-estimator} minimizes
$\sum_i\rho(r_i)$, for some function $\rho$. This is
equivalent to a weighted regression.

\setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm,height=6cm}
<<ItMPOwNOSPaSPBrhopsi,echo=FALSE,fig.width=8,fig.height=6,out.width="8cm">>=
op <- par(mfrow=c(1,2))
curve( pmin(abs(x), 1.5)^2, xlim=c(-2.5, 2.5), ylim=c(0, 3),
       col="blue", lwd=3, main="M-estimator: rho", 
       ylab = "rho", xlab="residual")
abline(h=0, lty=3)
curve( approxfun( c(-100,-1.5001,-1.5,1.5,1.5001,100),
                  c(   0,      0,-1.5,1.5,0,     0  ) )(x),    
       xlim = c(-2.5, 2.5), ylim = c(-1.6, 1.6),
       col = "blue", lwd = 3, 
       main = "M-estimator: weights", 
       ylab = "weight", xlab = "x")
abline(h=0, lty=3)
par(op)
@ 
\setkeys{Gin}{width=6cm,height=6cm}

The robust Mahalanobis distance between $r_t$ (the returns
of the stocks on a given day) and its historic (robust) mean
can help spot hectic times.

When computing a historical variance, all the series need
not have the same length: it is possible to compute a
maximum-likelihood estimator of the variance matrix and the
means that do not discard the information available
(\emph{Stambaugh method}); this method, in turn, can be
robustified (they just give a recipe, with no theoretical
foundation).

The book also presents ``\emph{robust portfolio
  optimization}'', \ie, portfolio optimization with a robust
variance matrix: if the robust efficient frontier and the
classical one coincide, use the classical one; if they
differ, investigate.

In these ``robust portfolio optimizations'', we are
concerned by the fat tails of the distribution of returns --
and to solve the problem, we end up cutting those tails. To
estimate the returns, \ie, the center of the distribution,
this is legitimate, but to compute the variance matrix, \ie,
the dispersion, this is dubious...

This chapter ends with an original section on
\emph{influence functions}. The \emph{empirical influence
  function} or \emph{finite-sample influence function} of a
family of estimators $\hat\theta_n$ (e.g., 
$\hat\theta_n$ could be the mean of $n$ numbers) is
$$ \mathrm{EIF}(x, \hat\theta, \textbf{x}) = 
(n+1) \left(
\hat\theta_{n+1} (x, \textbf{x}) -
\hat\theta_n(\textbf x) 
\right),
$$
\ie, it is the change in the estimator
$\hat\theta_n(\textbf x)$ brought by a new observation $x$
-- the $n+1$ coefficient is a normalizing coefficient,
ensuring that, under mild conditions, the large-sample limit
exists.

\setkeys{Gin}{width=8cm,height=12cm}%
<<ItMPOwNOSPaSPBEIFmean,echo=FALSE,fig.width=8,fig.height=12,results='hide',out.width="8cm">>=
EIF1 <- function (x, new, statistic, ...) {
  xx <- c(x, new)
  n <- length(x)
  (n+1) * ( statistic(xx, ...) - statistic(x, ...) )
}
EIF <- function (x, new, statistic, ...) {
  n <- length(new)
  res <- rep(NA, n)
  for (i in 1:n) {
    res[i] <- EIF1(x, new[i], statistic, ...)
  }
  res
}
op <- par(mfrow=c(3,2), mar=c(2,4,4,2))
curve(EIF(qnorm(ppoints(20)), new=x, statistic=mean),
      xlim=c(-3,3), ylab="EIF(x, mean)", xlab="",
      main="Mean", col="blue", lwd=3)
abline(h=0, lty=3)
curve(EIF(qnorm(ppoints(20)), new=x, statistic=median),
      xlim=c(-3,3), ylab="EIF(x, median)", xlab="",
      main="Median", col="blue", lwd=3)
abline(h=0, lty=3)
curve(EIF(qnorm(ppoints(20)), new=x, statistic=mean, trim=.10),
      xlim=c(-3,3), ylab="EIF(x, mean, trim=.10)", xlab="",
      main="Trimmed mean (10%)", col="blue", lwd=3)
abline(h=0, lty=3)
curve(EIF(qnorm(ppoints(20)), new=x, statistic=sd),
      xlim=c(-3,3), ylab="EIF(x, sd)", xlab="",
      main="Standard deviation", col="blue", lwd=3)
abline(h=0, lty=3)
curve(EIF(qnorm(ppoints(20)), new=x, statistic=mad),
      xlim=c(-3,3), ylab="EIF(x, mad)", xlab="",
      main="MAD (Mean Average Distance)", col="blue", lwd=3)
abline(h=0, lty=3)
require(MASS)
curve(EIF(qnorm(ppoints(20)), new=x, statistic=function(a)huber(a)$s),
      xlim=c(-3,3), ylab="EIF(x, huber)", xlab="",
      main="Huber", col="blue", lwd=3)
abline(h=0, lty=3)
par(op)
@ 
\setkeys{Gin}{width=6cm,height=6cm}

In a portfolio optimization setup, one can compute the
influence of an asset (\ie, the pair risk-return) on the
risk of the optimal portfolio, ist expected return, its
Sharpe ratio, its weights -- since there are two dimensions,
the plot is actually a contour plot.

Chapter 7 is an unreadable introduction to bayesian
statistics and Monte Carlo Markov Chain (MCMC) simulations.
They insist on the importance of \emph{Composition sampling}
but fail to clearly explain that notion. The book ends on
the Black-Litterman model, including a few interesting
remarks on its generalization (use of a non-gaussian prior) and
applications (to get an idea of the distribution of the Sharpe
ratio) and the \emph{Bayes--Stein estimator}, which they
fail to clearly explain. 

If you remember something from this book, it could be:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Use robust estimators, \ie, discard the most extreme
  observations -- especially if your computations assume the
  data are gaussian;
\item The influence function;
\item The (robust) distance between today's return and the historical
average to find ``hectic times'';
\item The plot of the composition of a portfolio as a function of the
  risk (you might already be used to a similar plot with respect to
  time).
\end{compactenum}

<<PortCompwrtRisk,echo=FALSE,results='hide',fig.keep='none'>>=
EfficientFrontier <- function (alpha, V, N=500) {
  stopifnot( is.vector(alpha), is.matrix(V),
             dim(V)[1] == dim(V)[2],
             length(alpha) == dim(V)[1] )
  n <- length(alpha)
  One <- rep(1, n)
  names(One) <- names(alpha)
  A <- t(One) %*% solve(V, alpha)
  B <- t(alpha) %*% solve(V, alpha)
  C <- t(One) %*% solve(V, One)
  D <- B * C - A^2
  g <- (B * solve(V, One)   - A * solve(V, alpha)) / D 
  h <- (C * solve(V, alpha) - A * solve(V, One  )) / D 
  returns <- seq( 5*min(alpha, 0), 5*max(alpha), length=N )
  risk <- rep(NA, N)
  res.w <- matrix(NA, nr=N, nc=length(alpha))
  colnames(res.w) <- names(alpha)
  for (i in 1:N) {
    w <- g + h * returns[i]
    res.w[i,] <- w
    risk[i] <- sqrt( t(w) %*% V %*% w )    
  }
  res <- cbind(risk=risk, returns=returns)
  attr(res, "weights") <- res.w
  names(g) <- names(h) <- names(alpha)
  attr(res, "g") <- g
  attr(res, "h") <- h
  res
}

EfficientFrontier(alpha, V, N = 500)
@ 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantitative strategy: global model portfolio}\\
  A. Tsai, Bear Stearns (2004)
\end{book}

They model stock returns as ARIMA processes, with the
parameters estimated for each stock, with the AIC, on a
30-month moving window; they claim that on a large,
diversified universe (MSCI World), with a monthly
rebalancing, with a 15\% stop-loss, with a crude
optimization (only consider the top 7\% stock and minimize
the risk, regardless of the returns) it works -- with an
85\% turnover.

If most of the stocks are AR(1), this is an exponential
momentum factor whose length is stock-dependant. Their model
captures short-term momentum but fails to spot longer-term
reversal.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Separating the winners from the losers}\\
  A. Hartman et al., ABN-Amro (2003)
\end{book}

The article builds an adaptive model to predict future stock
returns, as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Take 60 factors, compute their returns (how?), compute
  their robust correlation matrix, cluster them;
\item In each group, select a factor with a high IC
  (Information Coefficient, \ie, rank correlation with the
  forward returns) and a high mean(IC)/sd(IC);
\item Model the monthly ICs of each factor as AR(1)
  processes, independently;
\item These AR(1) forecasts of the ICs are too noisy: shrink
  them towards their historical mean (use the explained
  variance of the AR(1) model and the estimated variance on
  the estimator of the historical mean);
\item Combine the factors using the following weights:
  $$ \textbf{w} = (\var \textrm{IC} )^{-1} \cdot \textrm{IC}. $$
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Neural networks in business: techniques and applications for the operations researcher}\\
  K.A. Smith and J.N.D. Gupta (2000)
\end{book}

A review articles on the history of neural networks, the
families of neural networks (multi-layer feed-forward with
back-propagation learning rule, Hopfield networks (for
quadratic programming), Kohonen maps) and their
applications.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Surrogate time series}\\
  T. Schreiber and A. Schmitz (1999)
\end{book}

A more detailed (but less readable) article about surrogate
time series, stressing what you can and cannot test with
them -- and how powerful or powerless those tests are:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item For the ARMA surrogate data, they allow for the series
  to be transformed in order to be gaussian;
\item For the Fourrier Transform-based surrogate, they use
  an iterative process to compensate for some of the
  artifacts of the naive approach.
\end{compactenum}

The non-linearity tests they suggest relie on a
non-linearity measure, such as:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The third-order statistic, 
  $E[ (X_n - X_{n-\tau})^3 ]$,
    that measures the asymetry of a time series under time
  reversal;
\item the RMS (Root Mean Square) error of a locally constant
  ($k$ nearest neighbour) predictor;
\item Coarse-grained redundancies (?);
\item Symbolic methods (?);
\item False nearest neighbour (?);
\item Unstable Periodic Orbits (UPO) (?);  
\end{compactenum}

The appendix of the article discusses an implementation of
those ideas: \textsc{Tisean} -- you should also have a look
at the tseriesChaos package, in R.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting nonlinearity in data\\
    with long coherence times}\\
  J. Theiler et al (1992)
\end{book}

Bootstrap is not as straightforward for time series as it is
for other types of data. In this context, resampled time
series are often called \emph{surrogate data}. Here are a
few ideas:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Bootstrap as with other kinds of data -- but this
  assumes that the observations are independant and destroys
  any causality information;
\item Cut the time series in chunks and reshuffle them --
  this is not as bad, but it creates structural breaks in
  the series;
\item Fit an ARMA model to your data, reshuffle or resample
  the residuals and reconstruct the ARMA series;
\item Idem, but sample the new residuals from a gaussian (or
  whatever) distribution;
\item Compute the Fourrier transform of your series, keep
  the amplitude, replace the phase by random data and take
  the inverse Fourrier transform.
\end{compactenum}

The result, however, may be quite different from the initial
time series, e.g., for the following reasons:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The signal is periodic and there is not an integral
  number of periods in the window used for the Fourrier
  transform;
\item Changing the phase at the main frequency of the signal
  and/or at its harmonics can have strange effects;
\item The time series is not stationary;
\item The time series is non-linear (surrogate data are
  often used to test for non-linearity, but one should be
  aware of these and other artifacts).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The $S^2$ tree: an index structure for 
  subsequence matching of spatial objects}\\
  H. Wang and C.-S. Perng
\end{book}

To index sequences of points (``trails'' as used, e.g., for
subsequence matching in time series databases), one can
combine ideas from sequence indexing (suffix trees) and
spatial data indexing ($R^*$-trees), as follows: build and
$R^*$-tree with the points in your trail or the MBR (Minimum
Bounding Rectangles) covering them and add the links you
would have in a suffix tree.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Efficient time series subsequence matching 
  using duality in constructing windows}\\
  Y.-S. Moon et al. (2000)
\end{book}

This article refines the previous: instead of sliding a
window on the data sequences, one can cut them into chunks;
one then has to slide a window on the query sequence,
instead of cutting it into chunks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Fast subsequence matching\\
    in time-series databases}\\
  C. Faloutsos et al.
\end{book}

To look for subsequences in a time series database,
preprocess it as follows: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item move a sliding window (of the same length as the query
  series, or smaller) in the data series;
\item For each position of the sliding window, compute a DTF
  (Discrete Fourrier Transform) or any other
  distance-preserving transform;
\item Retain the first $k$ coordinates: you get a
  \emph{trail} in a $k$-dimensional space; 
\item Since the trail contains too many points to store them
  all, cover the trail with rectangles (MBR, Minimum
  Bounding Rectangles);
\item Store those rectangles in an $R^*$-tree: it is a data
  structure to store or index geometric objects that allows
  you to efficiently look for objects that intersect a given
  query object (it is used by some spatial databases).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Forecasting transaction rates:\\
    the autoregressive
    conditional duration model}\\ 
  R.F. Engle and J.R. Russell (1994)
\end{book}

Autoregressive Conditional Duration (ACD) models are models
of irregular time series, very similar to GARCH, with the
variance replaced by the duration (\ie, the time between
two values) -- so similar that one may use GARCH software to
fit those. It can also be seen as a State Space Model (but a
non-gaussian one: durations are waiting times). The model
can be generalized to accomodate for discrete increments.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A portfolio diversification index}\\
  A.M. Rudin and J.S. Morgan\\
  Journal of Portfolio Management, 2006
\end{book}

To check if the risks in your portfolio are well diversified, you can: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Look at the variance matrix of the returns of the
  assets in your portfolio (but you need a good pair of eyes
  for this: the matrix is huge);
\item Cluster the stocks in your portfolio and count the
  number of clusters (but this is somewhat arbitrary).
\end{compactenum}

This article suggests to count the number of ``independant
factors'' in your portfolio, as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Perform a Principal Component Analysis (PCA) on the
  variance matrix of the stock returns, to get those
  ``independant factors'';
\item Compute the weights $w_k$ of each factor $k$ in your
  portfolio;
\item Count the number of factors, as follows: 
  $$
  \textrm{PDI} = 2 \sum_k k w_k - 1, 
  $$
  (if there is a
  single factor, \ie, if $w=(1,0,\cdots)$, then
  $\textrm{PDI}=1$;
  (if there are $n$ factors and
  $w=(1/n,\cdots,1/n,0,\cdots)$, then $\textrm{PDI}=n$).
\end{compactenum}

To check if your portfolio is diversified as it should, you
can compare its PDI with that of random portfolios of the
same size, on the same universe.

Stepwise portfolio construction using the \emph{marginal
  PDI} does not significantly change the returns but lowers
the volatility -- when compared to random portfolio
construction.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk management for hedge funds: introduction and overview}\\
  A. W. Lo
\end{book}

VaR (Value at Risk) is not a good risk measure: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item It is not sub-additive, \ie, the VaR of a more
  diversified portfolio need not be lower;
\item It does not provide a decomposition of the risk into
  different risk sources;
\item VaR is a static measure: it does not account fo events
  (e.g., crashes), time-varying risks or dynamic trading
  strategies; it is computed unconditionally;
\item VaR is difficult to estimate: if you choose Extreme
  Value Theory, you use very few data points; if you assume
  the distributions are gaussian, you are plainly wrong.
\end{compactenum}

If you have to choose a hedge fund, do not be impressed by
their positive returns: it does not mean that hedge fund
returns tend to be positive, but simply that hedge funds
with negative returns disappear (survivorship bias). 

To show the problems of risk measurement, the article
presents an unscrupulous but impressive trading strategy:
short deep out-of-the-money put options.

To introduce events (crashes, ``phase locking''), consider
two risk models, one for normal days, another for event
days. You can then use conditional measures of risk,
\begin{align*}
  & \textrm{Risk}[ \textrm{portfolio} \,|\, \textrm{normal day} ] \\
  & \textrm{Risk}[ \textrm{portfolio} \,|\, \textrm{event} ]
\end{align*}

To account for non-linearities, do not compute sensitivities
($\beta$), but sensitivities ($\beta_+$, $\beta_-$) to
positive and negative values of the factor you are
interested in (e.g., S\&P 500) and test for their equality.

You can use Ljung \& Box's $Q$ statistic (or rather its
$p$-value) to measure the illiquidity of a fund.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time series analysis and prediction 
  using recurrent gated experts}\\
  C. Gilde (1996)	
\end{book}

Neural networks can be extended to accomodate time series,
by reusing the previous value of a node, e.g., with a
recurrent architecture.

This document adds that feedback capability to gated experts.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock market pattern recognition\\
    with neural networks}\\
  D. Vengerov (1997)
\end{book}

The gated experts look at different time scales. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Gated experts for classification\\
    of financial time series}\\
  D. Vengerov (1997)
\end{book}
To forecast future stock prices, use a Markov mixture of neural
networks:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The markovian structure recognizes the regime;
\item The neural networks fit the data.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock market prediction using\\
    artificial neural networks}\\
  B. Egeli, M. Ozturan, B. Badur
\end{book}

One can forecast future stock returns (or an emerging market index
returns) with a
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item MLP (MultiLayer Perceptron) -- the neural net everyone knows;
\item or a GFF (Generalized Feed Forward net) -- in which the signal
  can jump one or several layers.
\end{compactenum}

The input variables are: the previous value of the index
(yes, a single value), the USD exchange rate, the day of the
week.  They forget to mention the number of neurons in the
hidden layer -- probably as many as input variables -- and
just focus on the number of hidden layers.

It works best with a single hidden layer and GFF networks.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{21 nonlinear ways to beat the market}\\
  G.T. Albanis and R.A. Batchelor
\end{book}

Presentation of several Machine Learning (ML) algorithms,
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item LDA (Linear Discriminant Analysis);
\item PNN (Probabilistic Neural Network);
\item RRI (Ripper Rule Induction algorithm);
\item LVQ (Learning Vector Quantization);
\item OC1 (Oblique Classifier) 
\end{compactenum}
and suggests to combine them with some voting scheme.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Dynamic detection of change points\\
    in long time series}\\
  N. Chopin, \\
  Annals of the Institute of Statistical Mathematics (2006)
\end{book}

This article tries to account for the fact that GARCH models
poorly fit long time series, suggesting that the model does
not remain the same over time, by looking for abrupt model
changes.

This can be done with MCMC simulations (remember that MCMC
simulations may be used whenever you have an algorithmic
description of the process producing the data, with a
reasonable number of parameters), but the algorithm will be
slow (quadratic) and the convergence poor.

Instead, one can use a ``particle filter''. The differences
with MCMC are twofold:
\begin{itemize}
\item instead of generating several times series one after the
  other, one generates them at the same time;
\item For each instant, we compute the likelihood of each
  time series, and their probability of survival to the next
  step is proportional to this probability (this should
  remind you of genetic algorithms).
\end{itemize}

The discrete nature of the abrupt model changes hinder the
algorithm: the rest of the article explains how to
circumvent this problem -- the article could have been
titled ``Speeding up MCMC simulations in the presence of
discrete variables''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random portfolios\\
    for evaluating trading strategies}\\
  P. Burns (2006)
\end{book}

Traditional means of testing an investment strategy are very basic:
rank correlations (called ``information coefficients'') or return
spreads (difference between the returns of the first and the last
quintile of our signal or ``alpha'').

But they forget half the story: how the strategy will actually be
implemented. Instead of those crude measures, one can compare 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item your strategy, \ie, the transactions suggested by your
  alpha when fed to an optimizer;
\item the strategy obtained by feeding a random signal (with
  the same statistical properties as your signal -- do not forget 
  the autocorrelation) to an optimizer (with the same
  constraints and the same risk model);
\item a random investment strategy (with the same
  constraints: turnover, maximum and minimum weights, etc.).
\end{compactenum}

Repeat this for hundreds of random signals (I would even
suggest 10,000) to get an idea of how you signal fares. Feel
free to change the initial portfolio (you might want to draw
it ``at random'', but this would have to be defined).

You may want to draw the following plots, either in
back-tests or to monitor a strategy in real time:
\begin{compactenum}
\item cumulated returns of your portfolio over time,
  together with the fractiles of the cumulated returns of
  the random portfolios;
\item the $p$-value of the Stouffer test (?) over time;
\item final wealth of your strategy versus that of a random
  strategy, for the same initial portfolio.
\end{compactenum}

You can also use those ideas to assess the influence of
constraints, to compare risk models -- or to compare
optimizers.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Copulas and coherence:\\
    portfolio analysis in a non-normal
    world}\\
  K. Dowd \\
  The Journal of Portfolio management (2005)
\end{book}

They remark that correlation might not be a very good
measure of the dependance between stock returns and suggest
the use of copulas instead.  As an illustration, they consider a
two-stock portfolio: in their example, correlation-based
methods vastly underestimate the risk. The problem, is that
this is a simulated example, very far away from a gaussian
setup: are dependances between real returns that extreme?

Another untackled question is the estimation of the copulas
with a large number of stocks. In a correlation-based
framework, this estimation is unreliable and we have to make
further assumptions on the structure of the
variance-covariance matrix, e.g., that it only depends on a
handful of factors: this is a risk model. One would
therefore have to build our own copula-based risk model.
This is an ambitious undertaking.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Optimal trading strategies\\
    and supply/demand dynamics}\\
  A. Obizhaeva and J. Wang (2005)
\end{book}

Classical models to study trading strategies, 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item We want to buy $X$ shares of company \textsc{rhat}
\item We do it at times $0$, $\tau$, $2\tau$,\dots,$N\tau$
\item the price evolves as $P_{n+1} = P_n + \lambda x_n + u_n$ where 
$u_n \sim N(0, \sigma^2 \tau)$
\item We want to minimize the transaction costs
$E[ \sum P_n x_n ]$ or the risk-adjsuted transaction costs
$E[ \sum P_n x_n ] + \alpha \var[\sum P_n x_n ]$,  
\end{compactenum}
whose solution is $x_i = X / (N+1)$, are overly simplified. For
instance, in the continuous limit, they can no longer distinguish
between the strategies: the transaction costs are strategy-independant.

The article provides a richer model, based on the Limit Order Book
(LOB).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Portfolios from Sorts}\\
  R. Almgren and N. Chriss (2005)
\end{book}

This article develops a theoretical framework to define and compute an
``optimal'' portfolio from ordinal information (``this stock will
outperform this one'', etc.), e.g., from an ordering of the stocks. In
a nutshell, they look for the portfolio that performs best in the
worst situation that respects the ranking.

Surprisingly, this boils down to imposing a predefined
distribution on the returns -- but not a gaussian one.

This can easily be generalized to any partial order (e.g., a total
order in each sector but no information about one sector
outperforming another) or any set of linear equations (\ie, portfolios
outperforming other portfolios).

This article is a simplification (a special case) of \emph{Optimal
  Portfolios from Ordering Information} (2004), by the same authors.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Barra factor returns and macro indicators}\\
  M. Furukawa and H. Tamura, Nomura (2005)
\end{book}

Application of the previous article to the Barra JPE3
factors:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item momentum (contrarian);
\item value (especially when the yen is weak);
\item interest-rate sensitivity (industrial production and
  topix decline, strong yen);
\item leverage (rising long-term interest rate and topix);
\item size (contrarian, rising topix).
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The impact of the macroeconomic environment on global factor effectiveness}\\
  H. Tamura and K. Iro, Nomura (2002)
\end{book}

One expects factors to efficiently forecast future returns at some
times and poorly at others, depending on the ``macroeconomic
environment''. 

One can define the macroeconomic environment as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item select a few macroeconomic variables (interest rates, stock
  market indices, exchange rates);
\item apply a low-pass filter (there is an example on my web page;
  they seem to forget the boundary problems);
\item check when the resulting signal is up or down.  
\end{compactenum}

One can define the returns of a factor as follows: use the
(normalized) factor values as the active alpha in a mean-variance
optimization; impose that the resulting portfolio be neutral wrt the
other factors -- actually, their description is rather vague: they could be using a Black-Litterman model.

One can then study the effects of the macroeconomic
environment on the factor returns: this effect is important
in the US and for the valuation factors (P/E, P/B, P/DPS,
P/Sales). The most important macro\-economic factors are the
long-term interest rate and the stock-market indices.

The results can be represented graphically in the
returns$\times$risk plane, with a point (mean risk and
return) and two arrows (mean risk and return in the up and
down phases) for each factor. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Out-of-core tensor approximation of multidimensional matrices of visual data}\\
  H. Wang et al. (Siggraph 2005)
\end{book}

% Multilinear PCA
A \href{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tensor_decomposition}{generalization of PCA}
that acts on matrices (or higher-dimensional arrays) 
instead of vectors, based on
multilinear (aka tensor) algebra.  This is used in computer
graphics, to compress Bidirectional Texture Functions (BTF,
\ie, a texture is not given as a single image, but as a
series of images that depend on the viewing and illumination
directions). Applications in finance could revolve around
volatility surfaces.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Genetic algorithms with collective sharing for
    robust optimization in financial applications}\\
  O.V. Pictet et al. (1996)
\end{book}

One can use genetic algorithms to design trading algorithms,
as above: combine arithmetic operations, \texttt{sign},
\texttt{<}, \texttt{ifelse} and exponential moving average
to produce a result in $\{-1, 0, +1\}$. They are careful to
``normalize'' the data and modify the division in order to
avoid divisions by zero.

To stay away from non-representative peaks in the
log-likelihood landscape, one can reduce the fitness in a
region depending on the number of individuals in this
region. This notion of ``region'' can be defined with
gaussian kernels (but one has to choose the width of those
kernels) or by applying the $k$-means clustering algorithm.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Genetic programming with syntactic restrictions applied to financial volatility forecasting}\\
  G. Zumbach et al. (2001)
\end{book}

Genetic programming can be used to perform model selection
among a (huge) set of non-linear regression models, as follows:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item represent each model as a tree (the syntax tree of the
  corresponding formula);
\item define a few mutation operators (node substitution,
  subtree mutation, root splicing, node insertion, node
  deletion);
\item define a cross-over mechanism (given two trees, select
  a node in each and interchange the corresponding
  subtrees).
\end{compactenum}

The article suggests: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Do not use genetic programming to estimate the
  constants in the model, use more classical (and faster)
  algorithms;
\item Use \emph{typed} trees to preserve the symetry in the
  function to be estimated;
\item Fine-tune the mutation probabilities (they apply the
  genetic algorithm on simulated data with various values
  for the mutation and cross-over probabilities and select
  the best -- with yet another genetic algorithm);
\item Penalize the score of a given model by its
  ``complexity'' (which they define).
\end{compactenum}

They then apply those ideas to FX data to forecast
volatility (using simple arithmetic functions, absolute
value, square and exponential moving averages).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The misbehaviour of markets\\
    A Fractal View of Risk, Ruin and Reward}\\
  B. Mandelbrot (2004)
\end{book}

Besides providing a bird's eye view of the history of finance, the
author advocates three ideas.

1. Financial data exhibit fat tails, that follow a \emph{power law};
this can be measured by the corresponding exponent, $\alpha$.

2. Financial data exhibit long memory, or long-range dependance; this
can be measured by the \emph{Hurst exponent}, $H$. The interactions
between those two effects can be measured by the \emph{$R/S$
  statistic}.

3. Financial data look as though they were produced from a
``shuffled'' fractal process, with time distorsions -- but the book
fails to give any detail as to the shape of those ``time
distorsions''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The geometry of crashes: a measure 
    of the dynamics of stock market crises}\\
  T. Ara\'ujo and F. Lou\c c\~a\\
  \texttt{arxiv:physics/0506137}
\end{book}

During a crash, all the assets become correlated: this can be seen on
the correlation matrix (estimated on a 3-week window) e.g., by
comparing its first eigenvalues with those of ``random'' data. The
article suggests a measure of that difference,
$$
S = \sum_{i=1} ^6 \dfrac{ \lambda_i - \mu_i }{ \mu_i } 
$$
where the $\lambda_i$ are the actual eigenvalues and the $\mu_i$ those
of ``random'' data.

There are a few problems, though: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The article forgets to define ``random''; 
\item Between the correlation matrix computation and the eigenvalues
  computation, the authors perform a (useless, if linear)
  MultiDimensional Scaling (MDS);
\item It can only be used in retrospect, to conclude ``this was not
  just an impression, there really was a crash''.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{C++ design patterns and derivatives pricing}\\
  M. Joshi, CUP (2004)
\end{book}

This book has a very good but undeserved reputation (but again, its
competitors might be even worse). It explains how to price
derivatives, using Monte Carlo methods, in C++, with as many
\emph{design patterns} as possible.

Without \emph{any} picture. 

The author also explains how to circumvent some of the intricacies of
C++ (as Meyer's \emph{Effective C++}, but more untidy) or some of its
implementations (the \emph{only} comment in the code is ``...should be
in namespace \texttt{std} but aren't in VCPP6'') -- at times, it even
looks like an advocacy for interpreted languages (where you can write
a Monte Carlo sampler in one line instead of one page) or functionnal
languages (where a function can accept another function as argument).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A new method to estimate the noise in financial correlation matrices}\\
  T. Guhr and B. H\"alber
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-math/0206577}
\end{book}

Yet another correlation cleaning procedure: if the returns
are the sum of sector-specific returns and stock-specific
returns, the correlation matrix is block-diagonal. The
\emph{power mapping} of the sample correlation matrix,
$$
c_{ij} \leftarrow \textrm{sign}( c_{ij} ) \cdot c_{ij}^q, 
$$
for a well-chosen (well, hand-chosen) value of $q$ (this
is merely a shrinkage of the correlation coefficients
towards $+1$ or $-1$) provides a matrix that looks like the
sample correlation matrix on a much larger time scale (two
distinct peaks) and can thus be used instead.

Note that the authors used simulated data: I have tried it
with real data (500 stocks, 300 days) -- to no avail.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Financial applications of Random Matrix Theory: 
    old laces and new pieces}\\
  M. Potters et al., 
  \texttt{arXiv:physics/0507111}
\end{book}

Other correlation cleaning ideas: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Shrink all the eigenvalues of the correlation matrix towards
  zero;
\item Idem, but shrink more the small eigenvalues than the large
  ones.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Cluster analysis for portfolio optimization}\\
  V. Tola et al., 
  \texttt{arXiv:physics/0507006}
\end{book}

The article presents three ways of filtering a correlation matrix to be used to build a portfolio: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item convert the correlation matrix into a distance matrix, perform
  an average linkage hierarchical clustering, measure the distance
  along the resulting tree, convert the (ultrametric) distance back to
  a correlation matrix;
\item Idem with simple linkage;
\item Random Matrix Theory (RMT, \ie, PCA-based filtering).
\end{compactenum}

The average linkage performs better. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Parametric portfolio policies:\\
    Exploiting characteristics in the \\cross-section
    of equity returns}\\
  M.W. Brandt et al. (2004)
\end{book}

Quantitative investors do not forecast the returns of each
and every stock: instead, they devise a formula that will
predict those returns from the characteristics (financial
ratios, etc.) of the stocks; then they find the optimal
portfolio for those returns. It is a two-step process. 

Instead of predicting the stock returns, we can directly devise a
formula to find the portfolio weights and tune this formula to
optimize some ``utility function''.

It you are used to classical portfolio construction, this
looks a bit weird: the utility function you are used to
requires the expected stock returns and the
variance-covariance matrix of those returns. The article
suggests a utility function (called the Constant Relative
Risk Aversion (CRRA) function) that only depends on
the portfolio returns -- a function of a single variable.
$$ 
\textrm{Utility} = 
\dfrac{
  ( 1 + \textrm{return} ) ^ { 1 - \gamma }
}{
  1 - \gamma
}
$$

You would expect the utility to depend on the portfolio
expected return but also on its risk; you would expect the
utility of a risky portfolio to be lower than that of a less
risky one with the same expected return -- but the formula
only depends on the return -- why?

The trick is that it indeed depends on the returns, not the
expected return: we do not maximize
$$ \textrm{utility} ( E[ \textrm{return} ], \dots ) $$
but 
$$ E[ \textrm{utility} ( \textrm{return}, \dots ) ]. $$

The advantages over the classical paradigm are that: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item This is a \emph{dynamic} utility function;
\item The approach is not limited to the first two moments of the
  returns: it can also account for asymetries or fat tails
  in return distributions.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{A step-by-step guide to the\\ Black--Litterman model}\\
  T.M. Idzorek (2002)
\end{book}

Classical Mean-Variance optimization poses a few problems: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item The optimal portfolios tend to only contain a couple
  of stocks -- they are not diversified;
\item The optimal portfolios are corners (vertices) in a
  simplex: if we slightly change the inputs, we might jump
  to another corner -- the portfolio is over-sensitive to
  the inputs;
\item For the optimizer, the most important information
  often lies in the tiny differences between forecasts for
  similar assets, and it will amplify those differences,
  even if they are not significant, even if they are mere
  estimation errors.
\end{compactenum}

The \emph{implied returns} (or \emph{implied alphas}) of the market
portfolio are the expected returns for which the market portfolio is
optimal: they are the expected returns~$\boldsymbol\alpha$ such
that the market portfolio weights~$\mathbf w$ maximizes 
the utility $\boldsymbol\alpha' \mathbf w - 
\tfrac12 \lambda \mathbf w' V \mathbf w$,
where $V$ is the variance-covariance matrix of the stock returns and
$\lambda$ the risk aversion, \ie, the $\boldsymbol\alpha$ such that
$\mathbf w = (\lambda V)^{-1} \boldsymbol \alpha$, \ie,
$\boldsymbol \alpha = \lambda V \mathbf w$.

The \emph{risk aversion} coefficient $\lambda$ can be estimated as
$$
\lambda \approx \dfrac{
  \textrm{risk premium (\ie, expected excess return)}
}{
  \textrm{market excess return variance}
}.
$$
(Do not ask me why.)

The Black--Litterman model will combine those implied
returns with a few \emph{views} on the returns of a few
stocks or portfolios. We need not provide expected returns
for all the stocks (that would be error-prone) and we can
simply provide the returns of portfolios instead of
individual stocks (it is easier) -- forecasting the returns
of a few portfolios instead of those of hundreds of stocks
is sometimes called \emph{mixed optimization}.

The Black--Litterman model relies on \emph{bayesian
  statistics}: we have a prior belief (the returns will be
gaussianly distributed, with mean the implied alphas and
variance matrix $V$), we have some new information (or
views: that the portfolios we can predict will have the
returns $\nu$ we forecast, with a certain (diagonal) variance
matrix $\Omega$), and we combine all that. 

Here is the classical set-up for bayesian statistics:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item We are interested in some parameter $\lambda$ defining
  the distribution of a random variable, say $X \sim
  \textrm{Exp}(\lambda)$;
\item We have some prior knowledge about $\lambda$: it is
  sampled from a known distribution, the \emph{prior
    distribution}, say 
  $\lambda \sim \textrm{LogN}(0, 10^6)$;
\item We have some new information, usually a sample from
  the random variable $X$, say $X_1,\dots,X_n$;
\item We compute the distribution of the parameter $\lambda$
  given the new information $X$, \ie, $\lambda | X$ -- this
  is the \emph{posterior distribution}.
\end{compactenum}
This can be seen as an extension of Maximum Likelihood
Estimation (MLE), whose prior contains no information at
all, and whose result is the \emph{mode} of the posterior
distribution instead of the whole distribution itself.

If you want formulas for the probability distribution
functions, this is the \emph{Bayes formula}: 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Prior:} &\quad
  f(\lambda) \\
  \textrm{Posterior:} &\quad
  f(\lambda | X) 
  = \dfrac{
    f(X|\lambda) f(\lambda)
  }{
    \displaystyle \int f(X | \mu) f(\mu) \, d\mu
  }.
\end{align*}
If you do not like probability distribution functions and
prefer discrete probabilities, replace $f$ by $P$:
$$
P(\lambda | X) = \dfrac{
  P(X | \lambda) P(\lambda)
}{
  \displaystyle \sum_\mu P(X|\mu) P(\mu)
}.
$$

But the Black--Litterman model does not follow those lines
-- it merely reuses the same ideas, ``prior'', ``new
information'' and ``posterior'':
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Prior: the forward returns $r$ should look as though
  they were sampled from a gaussian distribution, with mean
  the implied alpha $\alpha$, with variance matrix $\tau V$,
  a scaled-down version of the variance matrix $V$ of the
  stock returns provided by the risk model. Setting $\tau=0$
  would mean that we believe that the returns will exactly
  coincide with the implied alphas; some people suggest
  $\tau = 0.3$, others $\tau=1$, others $\tau=0.01$.
\item New information: the returns $Pr$ of the portfolios we
  can predict should look like they were sampled from a
  gaussian distribution with mean our view $\nu$ and variance
  $\Omega$ -- a user-supplied matrix, usually diagonal to
  simplify things.
\item Posterior: we try to somehow combine the prior and the
  new information. Algorithmically, one could proceed as
  follows:
  \begin{compactenum}[\quad$\cdot$]
  \item sample the portfolio returns $Pr$ from their distribution $N(\nu, \Omega)$;
  \item sample the stock returns conditionnally to the
    portfolio returns, \ie, from $N(\alpha, \tau V) | Pr$;
  \item iterate and take the average.    
  \end{compactenum}
  Alternatively, you can \emph{compute} the distribution of
  $N(\alpha, \tau V) | Pr$ (with the Bayes formula) (?)
  \begin{align*}
    - 2 \log \textrm{Lik} = {}
    &(x - \alpha)' (\tau V)^{-1} (x - \alpha) + \\
    &(P x - \nu) ' \Omega^{-1} (P x - \nu) + \textrm{constant}
  \end{align*}
  and compute the Maximum Likelihood estimator. 

  Equivalently, one can solve the following ``GLS system''
  \begin{align*}
    r &= \alpha && \textrm{with variance } \tau V \\
    Pr &= \nu && \textrm{with variance } \Omega.
  \end{align*}
  This yields 
  $$ r = [ (\tau V)^{-1} + P' \Omega^{-1} P ]^{-1}
  [ (\tau V)^{-1} \alpha + P' \Omega^{-1} \nu ]. $$
\end{compactenum}

In this framework, the user has still too many parameters to
specify: the views $\nu$ (this is the only one we want to
provide the algorithm with), $\Omega$ (it says how confident
we are in our view, but it is rather tricky to set)
and~$\tau$. The article provides a simple and intuitive
means of setting up those parameters. Well, actually, only
$\Omega / \tau$ appears in the formulas, so you can set
$\tau = 1$ and only specify $\Omega$.

Their recipe is as follows. For each view $k$ (they consider
only one view at a time):
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item Compute the returns $r_{k, 100\%}$ with $\Omega = 0$ (100\% certainty);
\item Compute the corresponding unconstrained weights 
  $w_{k, 100\%} = (\lambda V)^{-1} r_{k, 100\%}$;
\item Adjust the weights according to the confidence level
  $C_k$ for the $k$th view ($C_k=0$ for 0\% confidence, 1
  for 100\%),
  $$ 
  w_k = w_{\textrm{market}} + 
  C_k ( w_{k, 100\%} - w_{\textrm{market}} );
  $$
\item Imagine that $w_k$ comes from the Black--Litterman
  formula, for some value of $\omega_k$: find the
  corresponding value of $\omega_k$, in the Least Squares
  sense.  
\end{compactenum}
Then, put all the $\omega_k$ in the diagonal matrix $\Omega$
and apply the Black--Litterman formula.



\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Analysis of longitudinal data}\\
  P.J. Diggle et al. (2002)
\end{book}

Chapter 1 contrasts \emph{cross-sectionnal data} (a single
observation for each subject) and \emph{longitudinal data}
(several observations for each subject, usually across
time): different methods are needed to account for the lack
of independence of the observations for a given subject and
to avoid the \emph{ecological fallacy}.

Here are three ways of dealing with longitudinal data: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Marginal analysis: you look for the mean and the
  variance (at each point in time) over all the subjects;
\item Random effect models: you have a model for each
  subject, $E[Y_{ij} | \beta_i] = x'_{ij}$, and the model
  parameters $\beta_i$ are themselves iid random variables;
\item Transition models: you have a model for 
  $E[Y_{ij} | Y_{i,j-1}, Y_{i,j-2},\dots]$. 
\end{compactitem}

I have not read the second chapter, \emph{Design
  considerations}, that deals with power and sample size.

Chapter 3 suggests a few plots to explore longitudinal data: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The variable to predict vs time, you can highlight
  some subjects (selected at random or not: e.g., the lowest
  final value -- or any other fractile); you may want to add
  a lowess curve;
\item The variable to predict vs one of the predictive
  variables, again with a non-parametric curve estimation; 
\item The variable to predict vs its lags, as a pairs plot;
  the Auto-Correlation Function (ACF) -- for
  irregularly-spaced and/or non-stationnary data, replace
  the ACF by a \emph{variogram} -- for binary data,
  replace the variagram by a \emph{lorelogram} (LOR stands
  for Log-Odds Ratio).
\end{compactitem}

Chapters 4 and 5, \emph{Generalized Least Squares (GLS) and
  parametric models for the variance-covariance matrix},
present three correlation structures: the uniform
correlation model, $\cor(X_i,X_j)=\rho$ if $i\neq j$
(equivalent to a subject-specific intercept), the
exponential one (the equivalent of an AR(1) model for
irregularly spaced data: $\rho(u) = e^{-\phi u}$) and the
gaussian one ($\rho(u)=e^{-\phi u^2}$); an alternative
approach is to perform a regression for each subject and
then look at the distribution of the estimated coefficients
-- or, more rigorously, a random effects model.

GLS are simply weighted least squares, with weight matrix
equal to the inverse of the variance matrix. One could use
Maximum Likelihood (MLE) to estimate this variance matrix,
but the results are biased: Restricted Maximum Likelihood
(REML) gives better results (in short: with MLE, you
estimate the regression coefficients $\beta$ and the
variance matrix $V$, but the estimators $\hat \beta$ and
$\hat V$ are not independent; with REML, you transform the
problem into estimatinh $\beta$ and another matrix $H$, so
that $\hat \beta$ and $\hat H$ be independent and that $\hat
H$ do not depend on $\beta$). For best results, estimate the
variance matrix with as saturated a model as possible (e.g.,
if no predictive variable is time-dependant, add a separate
parameter for the mean response at each time with each
treatment). The estimates de $\beta$, the confidence
intervals and the tests remain valid even if the variance
matrix is mis-specified.

When estimating the error, you can decompose it as a sum of
three terms: the random (subject-specific) effects, the
serial correlation, and the measurement (residual) error.

Chapter 6 explains how \emph{Anova} (Analysis of Variance) provides a
simple but unsatisfactory means of studying longitudinal data:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item With \emph{cross-sectionnal anova}, \ie, one anova for each
  time, you do not know how to combine the results (if they
  all agree and say there is something slightly significant,
  how do we combine them? how do we account for the
  correlation of the data in strengthening the significance
  of the result?) nor how to spot time-dependant effects
  (e.g., the slope);
\item The idea of \emph{derived variables} is to replace the
  observations for one subject by a single or a few numbers
  (say, the mean, the average rate of change, the parameters
  of a meaningful non-linear model) and perform an anova
  with this derived variable -- but with several derived
  variables, you run into the same problems as before. 
\item \emph{Repeated measures anova}, \ie, a mixed model of
  the form $ y \sim \textrm{treatment} +
  \textrm{treatment}:\textrm{time} + (\textrm{treatment} |
  \textrm{subject} ) ,$ completely forgets the time ordering
  of the data.
\end{compactitem}

Chapter 7 presents the main models used to fit longitudinal data: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{Marginal models}, \ie, GLS: you specify the
  structure of the variance and of the correlations of the
  random variables;
\item \emph{Random effects models};
\item \emph{Transition models}: you can model discrete data
  as a Markov chain whose transition probabilities follow
  logistic models.
\end{compactitem}

%TODO: Read the end of chapter 7...

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Visual Explorations in Finance with Self-Organizing Maps}\\
  G. Deboeck and T. Kohonen Eds., 1998
\end{book}

A \emph{Self-Organizing Map} (SOM) or \emph{Kohonen map} is
a hybrid between the $k$-means algorithm and
multidimensionnal scaling (it provides a clustering
\emph{and} a graphical representation of the proximity of
the clusters), implemented via a ``neural network'' (a
``non-linear, multi-layered, parallel regression technique''
-- if you already know about neural nets, bad luck, it has
nothing to do with it: it stems from the same biological
analogy, but it is a completely different species of
neurons). 

Intuitively, it takes a net and tries to cover the data
with~it.

The algorithm goes as follows: each node of the net
corresponds to a cluster center; assign them to initial
values (e.g., random values); for each observation, find the
closest cluster center, drag it \emph{and its neighbours}
towards the observation (this is the difference with the
$k$-means algorithm); iterate until convergence.

You will remark that SOM can handle missing values (however,
for the observations with too many missing values, it might
be wiser to remove them to compute the map and see
afterwards where they belong).

You can also use SOM for forecasting purposes, in a
\emph{semi-supervised} way: use only the predictive
variables to select the winning neuron, use all the
variables to update the weights.

To speed up the building of the map and avoid instability
problems with ``large'' (beyond $3\times3$) maps, you can
start with a small map and later refine it -- this is a
\emph{hierarchical SOM}. 

A few ideas to display the map:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Use a hexagonal grid with a neuron on every other
  tile; the other cells indicate if the nearby neurons are
  close (white) or not (black); this highlights frontiers
  between different regions of the map and/or areas of
  different densities;
\item The \emph{U-matrix} is similar, with a neuron on each
  tile and varying colours between the tiles;
\item Colour the tiles according to the similarity of their
  neurons with their neighbours (aka \emph{iso-contours});
\item Colour the tiles according to the number of 
  observations they contain;
\item The \emph{Sammon projection} of the net, to see
  shriveled regions of the map;
\item \emph{Atlas} (or \emph{component map}): colour the map
  according to one of the input variables (you get one
  picture for each variable, hence the name ``atlas''); this
  can help interpret the regions of the map;
\item Of course, it might be useful to display summary
  statistics for each cluster;
\item Colour the map according to the \emph{quantization
    error} (the distance between the observations and their
  neurons);
\item Colour the map according to the \emph{sensitivity} to
  changes in a component;
\item The \emph{synaptic weight map} indicates, for each
  neuron, which variable provides the greatest effect;
\item Colour the tiles according to the curvature;
\item You can imagine other ways of colouring the map: for
  instance, fit a circular (1-dimensional) SOM to the
  neurons and assign them rainbow colours;
\item Parallel plot of the observations, with a different
  colour for each cluster.
\end{compactitem}

Do not forget to estimate the quality of the map: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item its quantization error;
\item its stability (try again with resampled data, without
  the outliers, with different parameters, etc.)
\end{compactitem}

SOM are not only nice plots to look at: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item You can display the evolution of a subject (company) on the map
  (as the maps are not stable, you should reuse the same map);
\item Given a new observation, you can highlight the closest
  neuron -- or neurons;
\item You can use a SOM for asset allocation: either invest
  in a single cluster, or invest equally in all the
  clusters, or assign weights to the clusters according to
  some other variables.
\end{compactitem}

Some advice: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Choose a rectangular hexagonal map: square maps are
  less stable; funny shapes (torus, Klein bottle) are only
  relevant if you \emph{know} the data has this structure;
\item Choose the number of neurons: either slightly less
  than the number of observations, if you want a compact
  map, or up to ten times the number of observations, if you
  want a more detailed map;
\item Carefully choose the variables; you might want to
  discard some of them or even perform a Principal Component
  Analysis (PCA) and retain the first dimensions; 
\item Carefully transform the variables, e.g.:
  \begin{compactitem}[\quad$\cdot$]
  \item apply a logarithm or sigmoidal transform; 
  \item normalize them: $x \longmapsto \dfrac{x - \mu}{\sigma}$;
  \item range-normalize them: $x \longmapsto \dfrac{x -
      \min}{\textrm{range}}$;
  \item histogram-normalize them (this is a bad idea: it
    erases the clusters);
  \item Transform unordered qualitative variables into
    binary variables;
  \end{compactitem}
\item Initialize the algorithm with PCA (only start with
  random data when you want to show that the map can
  self-organize -- it works, but it is not optimal).
\end{compactitem}

The book does not tackle variants of SOM: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item DEC (Dynamically Expanding Method);
\item LSM (Learning Subspace Method);
\item ASSOM (Adaptive Subspace SOM);
\item FASSOM (Feedback-controlled ASSOM);
\item Supervised SOM;
\item LVQ-SOM (Linear Vector Quantization SOM).  
\end{compactitem}

The book provides examples in the following domains: Mutual
funds, Firm failures, Russian banks, Emerging countries,
Stocks, Real estate, Consumer preferences, Texts. You need
not buy it: most of it is available on
\url{http://www.dokus.com/PDF-files/}.

In R, have a look at \texttt{som} in package \texttt{som},
\texttt{SOM} in package \texttt{class} and \texttt{shardsplot} in
package \texttt{klaR}.

%See also \url{http://www.fenews.com/fen8/selforganizing.html}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Estimating the number of data clusters\\ 
    via the gap statistic}\\
  T. Hastie, R. Tibshirani and G. Walther (2000)
\end{book}

One can estimate the quality of a clustering with the
\emph{within cluster dispersion}, defined as the average
distance between two points in the same cluster (weighted so
that all the clusters have the same prevalence),
$$
W = \log \!\!\!\!\!\sum_{r \in \textrm{Clusters}}
\dfrac1{2 \va r} 
\sum_{a, b \in r}
d(a,b).
$$

One can compare this number with that obtained from random
data (uniformly distributed in a box aligned with the
principal components).

The following algorithm suggests the number of clusters: 
\begin{compactenum}[--] 
\item Perform the clustering assuming $k=1$, 2, 3,\dots\ 
  clusters and compute the within cluster dispersion $W_k$;
\item Do the same thing with random data and compute the
  average dispersion $\bar W_k$ and its standard
  deviation~$s_k$;
\item Define the gap as $\textrm{Gap}_k = \bar W_k - W_k$; 
\item Retain the smallest $k$ such that 
  $$\textrm{Gap}_k \geq \textrm{Gap}_{k+1} - s_{k+1}.$$ 
\end{compactenum}

Depending on the situation, the Gap statistic performs
similarly or better than the Silouette method.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Non parametric maximum likelihood estimation of features in spatial point processes using Voronoi tessalations}\\
  D. Allard
\end{book}

To denoise a spatial point process, one can model it as a
mixture of two uniform variables, one on the whole space
(the noise), one on a subset $A$ of it. The partial
log-likelihood (``partial'' means that we have cheated: we
need one more parameter, the probability of being in $A$,
but we replace it by its maximum likelihood estimator) is
$$
- n \log n + M \log \dfrac Ma + (n-M) \log \dfrac{n-M}{1-a}
$$
where
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item $n$ is the total number of points;
\item $M$ is the number of points in $A$;
\item $a$ is the volume of $A$.  
\end{compactenum}

The subset $A$ is sought as a union of tiles from the
\emph{Voronoi tessellation} -- actually, the union of the
$m$ smallest tiles, where the integer $m$ is to be
determined.

\begin{center}
<<echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
library(tripack)
n <- 50
k <- 20
x <- runif(n)
y <- runif(n)
x <- c(x, .2 + runif(k)/2)
y <- c(y, .4 + runif(k)/5)
r <- voronoi.mosaic(x,y)
op <- par(mar=c(0,0,0,0))
  plot(r, main="", xlab="")
  points(x,y, pch=3)
par(op)
@ 
\end{center}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Nearest neighbor clutter removal for estimating features in spatial point processes}\\
  S. Byers and A.E. Raftery
\end{book}

To remove noise from a cloud of points: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item For each point, compute the distance to its $k$th
  nearest neighbour (the histogram of those distances should
  be bimodal);
\item Model those points as a mixture of two Poisson
  processes, with different densities; estimate the model
  parameters with the EM algorithm (the model does not
  assume any shape);
\item Label the points as ``noise'' or ``not noise''.
\end{compactenum}

The performance is close to that of the \texttt{mclust}
algorithm, which is more computationnally intensive, and it
works surprisingly well in high dimensions.

The idea can also be used to remove outliers. 

In R, this is implemented as the \texttt{NNclean} function,
in the \texttt{prabclus} package.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MCLUST: Software for model-based clustering, density estimation and discriminant analysis}\\
  C. Fraley and A.E. Raftery (2003)
\end{book}

Documentation of the \texttt{mclust} R package, that
implements the EM model-based clustering algorithm -- and
its extensions to discriminant analysis and density
estimation.
\begin{verbatim}
  library(mclust)
  r <- clustBIC(x)
  plot(r)   # BIC
  s <- summary(r,x)
  pairs(x, col=s$classification)
\end{verbatim}
\begin{center}
<<echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
library(mclust)
x <- iris[,1:4]
r <- mclustBIC(x) # Was: EMclust(x)
s <- summary(r,x)
pairs(x, col=s$classification, pch=s$classification, gap=0)
@ 
\end{center}
\begin{verbatim}
  coordProj(x, 
            z=s$z,         # Probabilities
            mu=s$mu,       # Cluster centers
            sigma=s$sigma, # Cluster shapes
            what="classification")
\end{verbatim}
\begin{center}
<<echo=FALSE,results='hide'>>=
  coordProj(x, 
            z=s$z,         # Probabilities
            mu=s$mu,       # Cluster centers
            sigma=s$sigma, # Cluster shapes
            what="classification")
@ 
\end{center}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Model-based clustering discriminant analysis and density estimation}\\
  C. Fraley and A.E. Raftery\\
  Journal of the American Mathematical Association (2002)
\end{book}

The EM clustering algorithm can also be used in
\emph{density estimation} and \emph{discriminant analysis}
(the article has a lot of figures).

For high-dimensional data, new problems occur: the number of
parameters (in the variance-covariance matrices) to estimate
soars and calls for dimension reduction techniques (Principal
Component Analysis (which may obscure the situation),
Principal Curves, wavelets, Multidimensional Scaling (MDS))
or for extensions of the algorithms that directly deal with
distance matrices.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Chameleon: a hierarchical clustering algorithm using dynamic modeling}\\
  G. Karypis et al.
\end{book}

Chameleon is a \emph{graph-theoretic} clustering algorithm
that takes into account the fact that clusters can have
different densities and distinguishes clusters touching by a
single point or a whole facet:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item replace the data by its $k$-nearest neighbour (sparse)
  graph, whose edges are weighted by the similarity of the
  vertices;
\item find initial subclusters with the \emph{edge-cut}
  algorithm;
\item Merge the clusters whose Relative Interconnectivity
  (RI) and Relative Closeness (RC) are above a
  (user-specified) threshold, or according to the product
  $\textrm{RI} \cdot \textrm{RC}^\alpha$ (for a
  user-specified $\alpha$).
\end{compactenum}
The relative interconnectivity or closeness is defined from
the absolute and internal interconnectivity (AI, II) and
closeness (AC, IC), as follows.
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{AI}(A,B) &= 
  \sum_{\substack{a \in A\\ b \in B\\ (a,b) \in \textrm{Graph}}} 
  w(a,b) \\
  \textrm{II}(A) &= 
  \min_{\substack{C \textrm{ is a }\\\textrm{cut of }A}}
  \sum_{\substack{a, b \in A\\ (a,b) \in \textrm{Graph}}} 
  w(a,b) \\
  \textrm{RI}(A,B) &= \dfrac{
    \textrm{AI}(A,B)
  }{
    \tfrac12 
    \bigl(
    \textrm{II}(A) + \textrm{II}(B)
    \bigr)
  } \\
  \textrm{AC}(A,B) &= 
  \mean_
  {\substack{a \in A\\ b \in B\\ (a,b) \in \textrm{Graph}}} 
  w(a,b) \\
  \textrm{IC}(A) &= 
  \mean_
  {\substack{a, b \in A\\ (a,b) \in \textrm{Graph}}} 
  w(a,b) \\
  \textrm{RC}(A,B) &= \dfrac{
    \va{A \cup B} \cdot \textrm{AC}(A,B)
  }{
    \va A \cdot \textrm{IC}(A) + 
    \va B \cdot \textrm{IC}(B)
  }
\end{align*}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{How many clusters?\\Which clustering method?\\Answers via model-based cluster analysis}\\
  C. Fraley and A.E. Raftery
\end{book}

The $k$-means clustering algorithm assumes that the clusters
are symetric and have the same size -- hierarchical
clustering algorithms similarly misbehave when confronted
with clusters of varying shape, orientation or size. The EM
(Expectation-Maximization) algorithm finds the clusters by
modeling the data as a mixture of gaussians, with
potentially different variance-covariance matrices. The BIC
(Bayesian Information Criterion) can help you choose the
number of clusters and the variance-covariance matrix.

You can extend the algorithm to deal with noise: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item estimate the noise, by denoising the data (nearest
  neighbour method, Voronoi tessellations method);
\item estimate the clusters, by a hierarchical
  classification of the denoised data;
\item use the EM algorithm to fit the data as a mixture of
  gaussians (clusters) and a Poisson process (for the
  noise), initializing it with the previous two steps.
\end{compactenum}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{CURE: an efficient clustering algorithm\\ for large databases}\\
  S. Guha et al.
\end{book}

The \emph{Birch} algorithm clusters large databases as
follows: first replace dense regions by points (and discard
less dense regions), then cluster those points.

The \emph{CURE} (Clustering Using REpresentatives) algorithm
is a variant of the $k$-means algorithm that does not use a
single centroid for each cluster but \emph{several}
representatives: this allows for non-spherical clusters. You
can adapt it to large databases by running the algorithm on
a (random) sample of the data.

The article also briefly mentions other clustering
algorithms for large databases: Clarans, R$^*$-tree, DbScan.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Determining the number of clusters/segments in hierarchical clustering/segmentation algorithms}\\
  S. Salvador and P. Chan
\end{book}

There are four main categories of \emph{clustering
  algorithms}:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item partitionning (aka \emph{iterative relocation}, e.g.,
  $k$-means)
\item hierarchical (bottom-up or top-down);
\item density-based (e.g., EM (Expectation Maximization);
\item grid-based (e.g., SOM (Self-Organizing Map, aka
  Kohonen maps)).
\end{compactenum}

\emph{Segmentation algorithms} are related to clustering
algorithms: they try to approximate a time series as a
piece-wise linear function (PLR, Piece-wise Linear
Representation); the main categories of clustering
algorithms are:
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item sliding window (grow a segment until the error exceeds a
  threshold, then start a new one);
\item top-down (recursively split the entire series until the chunks
  can be approximated by segments);
\item bottom-up (start with length-2 segments, merge the
  closest pair, iterate).
\end{compactenum}

The number of clusters (resp. segments) can be guessed by: 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item cross-validation;
\item penalized likelihood:
  \begin{compactenum}[\quad$\cdot$]
  \item MML (Minimul Message Length);
  \item MDL (Minimum Description Length);
  \item BIC (Bayes Information Criterion), AIC (Akaike
    Information Criterion);
  \item SIC (Subspace Information Criterion);    
  \end{compactenum}
\item permutation tests (for segmentation: shuffle the time series);
\item resampling;
\item finding the maximum of a curve representing the quality of the
  cluster:
  \begin{compactenum}[\quad$\cdot$]
  \item gap statistic;
  \item prediction strength;
  \end{compactenum}
\item finding the \emph{knee} of the error curve.
\end{compactenum}

The \emph{L-method} finds the knee of an error curve by
fitting it as a 2-segment broken line (to get more stable
results, take a subset of the data so that there be as many
points on each side th the knee; if the curve (or the time
series) is too noisy, first smooth it by a moving maximum
over a length 2 window).

Note that this does not work of there are only 1 or 2 clusters. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Algorithms of maximum likelihood data clustering with applications}\\
  L. Giada and M. Marsili
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat/0204202}
\end{book}

To cluster a cloud of points, the authors consider the following
model: take $n$ points at random, take $k$ cluster centers at random,
assign each point to a cluster at random, drag each point towards its
cluster center. The likelihood is 
$$ \sum_{s \ :\ n_s > 1}
\log
\dfrac{n_s}{c_s} + (n_s - 1) \log \dfrac{
  n_s^2 - n_s}{n_s^2 - c_s} $$
where 
\begin{compactenum}[--]
\item $s$ is the cluster number;
\item $n_s$ is the number of points in cluster $s$;
\item $\displaystyle
  c_s = \sum_{i, j \in s} \dfrac{
    \langle x_1, x_j \rangle
  }{
    \n{x_i} \n{x_j}
  }$.  
\end{compactenum}
Contrary to most methods, you do not have to specify the number of
clusters. You can try to find the configuration with the best
likelihood by the usual means (steepest descent, simulated annealing),
which is quite time-consuming. The article suggests a faster (less
precise) hierarchical algorithm: start with $N$ 1-element clusters,
merge two clusters so as to maximize the likelihood, iterate until
there is a single N-element cluster, retain the clustering with the
best likelihood.

The hierarchical clusterings they get in their two examples are very
different: with financial data, single stocks are repeatedly added to
already formed clusters, while with biological data, larger clusters
are merged.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Data clustering and noise undressing\\ of correlation matrices}\\
  M. Marsili, \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat/0003241}
\end{book}

A correlation matrix estimated on a sample (and not on the whole
population or from the probability distribution of the random
variables) is ``dressed in noise'', \ie, not equal to the theoretical
one. To see if there is something behind this noise, one can compare
the spectrum (\ie, the eigenvalues, sorted from the largest to the
smallest) of the correlation matrix with that of the correlation
matrix of a sample of uncorrelated gaussian random variables (this is
called Random Matrix Theory (RMT) -- if your distributions are not
gaussian, just resample each vector independently) or that coming from
a given model. The proposed model is hidden behind an overly
specialized phisics's vocabulary: take $n$ points at random, take $k$
cluster centers at random, assign each point to a cluster at random,
drag each point towards its cluster center.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Noise dressing\\ of financial correlation matrices}\\
  L. Laloux et al.\\
  \texttt{arXiv:cond-mat/9810255}  
\end{book}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Marginal Contribution to Risk\\
    for GICS industries in Aegis 3.4}\\
  Barra (2004)
\end{book}

To compute and balance the risk of a portfolio of stocks, we
use the variance-covariance matrix of the stock returns. As
this matrix is huge, we do not estimate each of its
coefficients: instead, we assume that the covariances are
due to several ``factors''; we estimate the
variance-covariance matrix of those factors, we estimate the
exposure of each stock to those factors (this means: we
perform a regression of the returns of stock~$i$ against the
``factor returns'', the exposures are the coefficients
estimated by this regression), we estimate the residual
variance of each stock returns and we put everything in a
nice formula,
$$
V = e' v e + \Delta,
$$
where $V$ is the variance matrix
of the stock returns, $e$ is the exposures matrix, $v$ is
the variance matrix of the factor returns, $\Delta$ is the
(diagonal) matrix of the residual (aka stock-specific,
sytematic) variance.

If $\textbf{w}$ are the weights of a portfolio (as a
fraction of the market value of the portfolio), then the
risk (aka volatility, standard deviation) of this portfolio is 
$$ \textrm{Risk}(\textbf w) = \sqrt{ \textbf w' V \textbf w }. $$

The Marginal Contribution To Risk (MCTR) of a stock~$i$ is 
$$ \textrm{MCTR}_i = \dfrac{
  \partial \textrm{Risk}
}{
  \partial w_i
} = \dfrac{
  (V \textbf w)_i
}{
  \textrm{Risk} (\textbf w)
}.
$$

Similarly, the contribution of a sector (seen as a
portfolio) $\textbf{s}$ to the risk of a portfolio
$\textbf{w}$ is the directionnal derivative
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{MCTR}(\textbf w, \textbf s) &= 
  \lim_{\epsilon \to 0}
  \dfrac{ \textrm{Risk}(\textbf w + \epsilon \textbf s) - 
    \textrm{Risk}(\textbf w)
  }\epsilon \\
  &= \dfrac{
    \textbf s' V \textbf w
  }{
    \textrm{Risk} (\textbf w)
  }.
\end{align*}
(You can do the computations yourself: this stems from the
Taylor expansion of $\textrm{Risk}(\textbf w + \epsilon
\textbf s)$.)

But how do we turn a sector into a portfolio? We could take
each stock in this sector, with a weight equal to one; we
could also weigh them by the market capitalization.
Alternatively, if we do not have a single sector but a
\emph{partition} of the universe into several sectors, we
can try to build a portfolio with unit exposure to the
sector we are interested in and zero exposure to the others.
This can be done as follows. Let $f$ be the matrix of
exposures to your sectors (one row per stock, one column per
sector, 1 if the stock is in the sector, 0 otherwise) and
$\delta_j$ the desired exposure for the sector portfolio (1
in position $j$ and 0 elsewhere). We want a row vector
$\textbf s_j$ such that $\textbf s_j f = \delta_j$: it is not
unique, but the pseudo inverse of~$f$ gives one: $\textbf s_j
= (f'f)^{-1} f' \delta_j$.

Actually, these are \emph{not} the formulas mentionned in
the article: for a reason I do not understand, they first
compute the contribution of their factors to the portfolio
risk and then ``translate'' (yes, they use that word --
weird for something that is actually a matrix
multiplication) them to the (pseudo-inverse) sector
portfolio.

They do not seem to realize that they have forgotten the
stock-specific risk in the process. It might be correct if
your sector decomposition is coarser than their factor
decomposition, but otherwise, it is plainly wrong.

Incidentally, they also forget to suggest what to do with
missing values -- stocks whose sector is unknown -- I
suggest to set their exposures to zero.

Finally, they forget to stress the absence of a formula to
compute the risk from the contributions to risk -- which
should not be called ``contributions'', after all:
$$
\sum_{j \in \textrm{sectors}} 
(\textbf w' f)_j 
\textrm{MCTR}( \textbf w, \textbf s_j )
\neq \textrm{Risk} (\textbf w).
$$

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Japanese Equity Model}\\
  Barra (1999)
\end{book}

Risk can be decomposed into: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item \emph{specific risk}, associated with individual securities (unless you are a stock-picker, you try to avoid it);
\item \emph{common risk factor}, associated with industries and other factors (these correspond to the bets we make);
\item \emph{systematic risk}, associated with the market
  (you cannot avoid it -- well, with cash, futures or with
  no long-only constraint, you can).
\end{compactitem}

The \emph{Capital Asset Pricing Model} (CAPM) is a
no-intercept linear regression of the (excess) returns of a
stock against the (excess) returns of the market; the
coefficient is called the \emph{beta} of the stock or its
\emph{exposure} to the market.

\emph{Multi-Factor Models} (MFM) are linear regressions of
the (excess) returns of a stock against various ``factors''
(industry, market excess return, oil price, temperature,
etc. -- you have to choose your own factors).

\emph{Arbitrage Pricing Theory} (APT) is the use of MFM to
estimate the variance-covariance matrix of the stock returns
-- more generally, a \emph{risk model} is a means of
estimating a variance matrix. 

There are several types of active investment strategies:
\emph{market timing} (sell before the market goes down, buy
before it goes up); sector emphasis, stock selection. 

Risk models can be used to create a portfolio, to analyze
one (check that the risk is where the manager claims it is),
or to attribute its performance to the varrious factors.

This document does not stress the difference between the
Barra way of estimating a risk model and others: you always
have
%\begin{align*}
%&\textrm{returns of stock } i = \\
%&\qquad
%\sum_{k \in \textrm{Factors}}
%(\textrm{exposure of stock } i \textrm{ to factor } k)
%\times
%(\textrm{returns of factor } k)
%\end{align*}
$$
\textrm{returns of stock } i
=
\sum_{k \in \textrm{Factors}}
\beta_{i,k} r_k,
$$
but 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item Barra estimates the exposures $\beta_{i,k}$ of stock
$i$ to factor $k$ and computes the factor returns $r_k$;
\item Northfield estimates the factor returns and computes the 
exposures;
\item APT estimates the factor returns from the first eigen
  vectors of the variance-covariance matrix and computes the
  exposures (this is called a \emph{statistical risk model}:
  you then have to interpret the various factors -- it is
  also linked to Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and
  Random Matrix Theory (RMT));
\item USB uses a hybrid approach: they start with the factor
  returns, use them to estimate the exposures, use the
  exposures to estimate the factor returns, and so on, until
  convergence.
\end{compactitem}

The document ends with a rather comprehensive glossary. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{MNP: R package for fitting the multinomial probit model}\\
  K. Imai and D.A. van Dyk,
  Journal of Statistical Software (2005)
\end{book}

The multinomial probit model models a qualitative 
variable $Y$, with values 1, 2,\dots,$n$ as follows: 
\begin{compactitem}[  ]
\item $W_1, W_2,\dots,W_n$ are (unobserved, latent) gaussian variables, of
  unknown mean and variance,
\item $Y=\operatorname{Argmax} W_i$
\end{compactitem}
This package fits the model with an MCMC.
Before interpreting the results, the article asks us to
use the \texttt{coda} package to look at the Gelman--Rubin
convergence statistic (numerically and graphically), to
look at the time series of the sampled parameters, to look
at the distribution of those parameters.

The appendix also explains how to set-up a ``local library
directory''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{BugXLA: Bayes for the Common Man}\\
  P. Woodward,
  Journal of Statistical Software (2005)
\end{book}

This is an Excel interface to WinBugs -- but they seem to
completely forget the convergence problem...

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Calling the \texttt{lp\_solve} linear program software from R, SPlus and Excel}\\
  S.E. Buttrey,
  Journal of Statistical Software (2005)
\end{book}
There is another, faster, more reliable R package for linear and mixed integer programming (MIP), \texttt{lpSolve}; it relies on the free (LGPL) \texttt{lp\_solve} library. 

The article insists on the Excel interface and the fact that
it yields the right result, contrary to Excel.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{...}\\
  ?.?. ? et al. (????)
\end{book}

This article explains how to perform a ``regression with time-varying
parameters''. The main difference
with my current implementation is:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item They assume the parameters are AR(1) processes,
  I assume they are random walks. 
\end{compactitem}
There are other, minor differences: 
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item They take sensible initial values for the estimation of
  the coefficients, I take 0;
\item  They give the $p$-value of a Likelihood Ratio (LR) test to
  check if the parameters are indeed varying (but to compute
  it, they have to know the "number of degrees of freedom"
  of the model with time-dependant parameters -- I am not
  sure how they estimate it);
\item Their Kalman filter seems to be univariate;
\item They forget to check that their data look gaussian, that
  the residuals look gaussian and iid.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random matrix approach to\\cross-correlations in financial data} \\
  V. Plerou et al.,
  Phys.Rev.E 65 066126 (2002)
\end{book}

Another article on the same subject:
\begin{compactitem}[--]
\item The bulk of eigenvalues is consistent with RMT (they
  look at the distribution of eigenvalues, at the distance
  between two adjacent eigenvalues (nearest neighbour
  spacing), at the next nearest neighbour spacing -- and
  another quantity I do not understand);
\item There are no significant negative correlations;
\item The largest eigenvalue represents the market; 
\item The next eigenvalue represents the logarithm of the
  capitalization;
\item The next eigenvalues represent sectors (they provide a
  formula to estimate the number of significant stocks in a
  normalized eigenvector: the inverse of the sum of the
  fourth power of the coordinates);
\item The smallest, non-significant eigenvectors correspond
  to correlated pairs;
\item Eigenvectors with large eigenvalue are stable over
  time (for this, they compute the \emph{overlap matrix},
  whose $(i,j)$ element is the scalar product of the $i$th
  eigenvector at time $t$ with the $j$th eigenvector at time
  $t+T$; discarding the smallest eigenvalues, \ie, using a
  (statistical) risk model to optimize a portfolio yields
  more reliable risk and returns forecasts.
\end{compactitem}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Shortcomings in advise versus the art and science of investing in hedge funds}\\
  G. Susinno, 2005
\end{book}

\emph{Risk models} are ways of reliably estimating
variance-covariance matrices. \emph{Statistical risk models}
proceed as follows: compute the variance-covariance matrix,
diagonalize it and zero out the lowest eigen values.
\emph{Random matrix theory} tells us how many eigen vectors
to retain: compute the variance matrix obtained after
shuffling each time series, repeat several times to get an
idea of the distribution of the eigen values. 

To visualize this correlation matrix, turn it into a
distance matrix and draw the \emph{Minimum Spanning Tree}
(MST) -- when distance analysis and clustering fail to bring
any insight on the data, MST often prove enlightening and
can highlight the structure of the data. (The author tries hard
to impress the reader by using the words ``ultrametric
distance'' -- an \emph{ultrametric distance} is just a
distance measured on a tree.)

The author falls into (at least) two traps. First, even
though he stresses the non-gaussianity of the data, the lack
of \emph{power} (he does not seem to know that word) of
gaussianity tests, he uses the eigen value distributions for
gaussian random matrices, instead of the permutation-based
tests. Second, the permutation-based tests assume that in
the initial time series, consecutive observations were
independent -- a suspicious assumption.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Risk premia on european sectors}\\
  K. Reimer and H. Berster, Commerzbank, april 2005
\end{book}

One can also use the Residual Income Model (RIM) to compute the 
$$
\textrm{risk premium}
= k - \textrm{risk-free interest rate}.
$$

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{EROS (Expected Returns on Stocks)}\\
  K. Reimer and H. Berster, Commerzbank, april 2005
\end{book}

They have 3 ideas:
\begin{itemize}
\item Use the \emph{Residual Income Model} (RIM) to compute
  the ``market expectation of returns'' $k$, remark that it
  has a very high rank correlation with future returns (IC,
  Information Coefficient) and use it to optimize a
  benchmark;
\item Compute a \emph{value} measure, as a forcefully normalized
  expected \emph{divident yield} over the next 5 years,
  corrected to account for the end of the fiscal year;
\item Similarly compute a \emph{growth} measure, as the
  annual \emph{earnings growth}; Use those two measures to
  interpret the portfolio.
\end{itemize}

This ``forceful normalization'' has a drawback: it allows
you to compare indidual stocks or sectors to the whole
market, but does not give you any information for the market
as a whole -- its growth and its value are, by definition,
zero.

The succinctly recall how the Residual Income Model (RIM)
works:
\begin{itemize}
\item If the risk-free interest rate is high, investors will
  only invest in companies with a higher prospective return:
  the price of the companies will depend on the current
  risk-free interest rate -- more precisely, it will depend
  on the current ``interested rate'' or desired return, $k$,
  for investments providing a similar risk profile.
\item The price of a given company will also depend on its
  Return on Equity (RoE).
\end{itemize}
The RIM sums this up: 
$$
\textrm{Fair Value}
=
\textrm{BV}_0 + \sum_{t=0}^\infty
\textrm{BV}_{t-1}
\dfrac{
  \textrm{RoE}_t - k
}{
  (1+k)^t
}
$$
where 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{BV}_n &= \textrm{Book Value in } n \textrm{ years} \\
  \textrm{RoE}_n &= \textrm{Return on Equity in } n \textrm{ years} \\
\end{align*}
The difference $\textrm{RoE} - k$ is called \emph{abnormal
  earnings} or \emph{residual income}.

Of course, one has to provide a mode for the evolution of
the book value BV$_n$ and the returns on equity RoE$_n$ --
the article does not give any detail and simply mentions a
``three-stage model using IBES consensus forecasts''.

We know neither the fair value nor the desires return $k$,
but if we replace the fair value by the current market
price, we get a market estimation of the desired return $k$.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Longitudinal and Panel Data: Analysis and Applications for the Social Sciences},\\ 
  E.W. Frees, Cambridge University Press, 2002
\end{book}

Chapter 1 presents and motivates the subject, longitudinal
data: it is a mixture of regression and time series
analysis:
\begin{itemize}
\item Instead of studying a single time series, we study several at the same time; \ie, we have several realizations of a stochastic process.
\item We investigate a relation $y = \alpha + \beta_1 x_1 +
  \beta_2 x_2 + \textrm{noise}$ that evolves with time:
  $\alpha$, $\beta_1$, $\beta_2$ may change with time. This
  could suggest a 2-step procedure: for each $t$, estimate
  the coefficients $\alpha(t)$, $\beta_1(t)$, $\beta_2(t)$;
  then study the resulting time series. Longitudinal data
  analysis is a similar but reliable, 1-step procedure. The
  noise is often called \emph{subject-specific dynamic
    pattern}.
\end{itemize}

Chapter 2 lists the plots used to investigate longitudinal
data:
\begin{itemize}
\item density estimation (histogram, etc.) of each variable
  and evolution with time (violin plot, lattice plot);
\item $y \sim x_i$ for the whole period and its evolution
  over time (in a lattice plot or as paths on a single
  plot);
\item Time series of each variable for each subject (or
  group of subjects);
\item Added variable plots,
  discarding the effects of time
  $ y_i(t) - \bar y_i \sim x_i(t) - \bar x_i$,
  of the other variables 
  \verb|lm(y+x2+x3)$res ~ lm(x1~x2+x3)$res|, or of both
\end{itemize}
the possible temporal covariance matrices (independent,
AR(1), AR(T)) and lists a few tests: e.g., you no longer
test the \emph{influence} of an observation (as with the
Cook distance, in classical regression) but that of a whole
subject on the regression results.

Chapter 3 motivates \emph{random effects} and \emph{mixed
  effects models}: the observations are grouped (various
subjects (stocks) are studied over time, thus we have
several observations per subject) but the subjects do not
represent the whole population: we want to take into account
the variations between subjects and to be able to extend the
results to the whole population -- to this end, we provide a
model for the \emph{subject-dependant parameters}. Those
parameters (aka \emph{nuisance parameters} or \emph{random
  effects}), are themselves random variables, e.g., 
\begin{align*}
  y_{i,t} &= \alpha_i + \beta x_{i,t} + \epsilon_{i,t} \\
  \alpha_i & \sim N(\mu, \sigma) \\
  \epsilon & \sim N(0,1)
\end{align*}
Random effects can be computed as a Generalized Least
Squares (GLS: like Ordinary Least Squares (OLS), but the
noises can be correlated and/or heteroskedastic) problem
(but you have to know the variances of the parameters:
estimate them as you can).  Restricted Maximum Likelihood
(REML) provides better, less biased results than ML -- but
for tests, only use ML.

Chapter 4 stresses the difference between \emph{estimators}
(of the fixed effects) and \emph{predictors} (of the random
effects); predictors are actually \emph{bayesian} Maximum
Likelihood estimators, because we have a \emph{prior}
distribution for them -- or, at least, a model for that
distribution.

Chapter 5 generalizes the previous models to (multilevel)
hierarchical models; they are still estimated as a single
GLS problem, with a more parsimonious covariance structure.
One should beware of the tests of hierarchical models: the
usual ones are no longer valid, especially tests about the
covariance.

Predictive variables in a regression can either be \emph
{stochastic} (\ie, random variables) or deterministic (e.g.,
fixed by the experimentor). Chapter 6, very technical,
details the properties those stochastic predictive variables
should have for a mixed model estimation to be valid.
Problems appear, for instance, when you include the lagged
variables to predict as a predictor or when the predictors
are autoregressive.

Chapter 7 lists a few dangers of panel data models: 
\begin{itemize}
\item If the model is too complicated, different elements of
  the model can lead to similar effects, making the model
  \emph{unidentifyable};
\item You should account for (or beware of) ommitted
  variables (subject-dependant, sector-dependant,
  time-dependant);
\item You should account for missing data (discarding
  missing observations or imputing them can lead to
  suboptimal results).
\end{itemize}

Chapter 8 tackles dynamic models: if you want to use your
model to forecast future values, spotting changes in the
model is pivotal. Dynamic changes can be included in the
model in several ways:
\begin{itemize}
\item add the time as a predictive variable (this works fine
  if its effects are linear);
\item use the discrete derivatives of the variables;
\item Allow for serial correlations (AR(1), etc.) in the noise;
\item Allow for time-varying parameters;
\item Include the lagged variable to predict as a predictor;
\item \emph{adapt} the Kalman filter.
\end{itemize}
The authors details the Kalman filter example (with the
CAPM), unfortunately hiding the ideas behind complicated
formulas. The important thing to note is that you should
\emph{not} proceed in two steps, first computing
cross-sectionnal regression, then applying a Kalman filter
on the coefficients, but instead \emph{adapt} the Kalman
filter to the model.

Chapter 9 focuses on logistic regression (\ie, predicting
binary variables): here, for technical reasons, we need to
replace the Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) by
\emph{conditional MLE}. This chapter also succinctly
presents the Generalized Method of Moments (GMM), aka
Generalized Estimating Equations (GEE) -- if you do not want
to estimate the random effects but just their first two
moments.

Chapter 10 presents the General Linear Model (GLM) and
delves into the details of the GMM/GEE method (forgetting to
state the ideas and instead giving equation after equation).
I still have not understood what it is. As an example, they
apply the Conditionnal MLE to the Poisson regression. 

Chapter 11 tackles the case of a qualitative variable to
predict, which can be treated as a multinomial regression
problem or as a Markov chain one.  In the middle of this
chapter, they also mention the generalized Extreme Value
Distribution (EVD) -- but I do not know why. The slides do
not mention survival models, but they appear in the book. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Mixed-Effects Models in S and S-Plus}\\
  J.C. Pinheiro and D.M. Bates\\
  Springer Verlag, 2000
\end{book}

This is the official documentation of the R \texttt{nlme} package, that deals with mixed data, such as: 
\begin{itemize}
\item Grouped data: several observations of one (or several)
  variable(s) for each subject, the subjects being partionioned into
  groups (and the groups can themselves form groups of groups);
\item Longitudinal data: one (or several) variable(s)
  observed several times for each subject, at different
  points in time;
\item Panel data: idem, but the observation times are the
  same for each subject, so that you can put the data in
  tables, one subject per row, one date per column;
\item Repeated measures: several observations for each subject;
\item Blocked design: several experiments (``experiment factor'') on several subjects (``block factor'');
\item Split-splot design: idem;
\item Multilevel data: each subject is in a group, each group is in a group
  of groups, etc.
\end{itemize}

These models could be written as:
\\\verb$  y ~ x | subject/group$
\\\verb$  y ~ time | subject$
\\\verb$  y ~ 1 | subject$
\\\verb$  y ~ experiment | subject$
\\\verb$  y ~ 1 | subject/group$

Chapter 1 is an overview of mixed models. When facing a new
data set, you often assume that the observations are
independent. But this is not always the case: in particular
when you have several observations for each ``subject'' (a
stock, in finance, a patient, in medicine), the observations
for a given subject will be correlated. The same problem
occurs if the subjects form several groups: the observations
in a given group will be correlated.

One solution is to consider that the parameters to be
estimated depend on the subjects, e.g. (this is a
\emph{fixed effects model}),
\begin{align*}
  &y_{it}  = \alpha_i + \beta x_{it} + \textrm{noise}_{it} \\
  &\textrm{Estimate: } \beta,\ \alpha_1,\alpha_2,\dots,\ 
  \var[\textrm{noise}].
\end{align*}

Alternatively, we can consider that the $\alpha_i$ are not
parameters but random variables, following iid gaussian
distributions (this is a \emph{mixed effects model}):
\begin{align*}
  &y_{it}  = \alpha_i + \beta x_{it} + \textrm{noise}_{it} \\
  &\textrm{Estimate: } \beta,\ 
  \mathrm{E}[\alpha],\ 
  \var[\alpha],\ 
  \var[\textrm{noise}].
\end{align*}

This is often preferable because there are fewer parameters
and we can meaningfully extrapolate to subjects that were
not included in the study.

This is a partly bayesian point of view: we have a prior on
$\alpha$ (but not on $\beta$, and we take a Maximum
Likelihood Estimator, which is very unbayesian).

Chapter 2 presents the underlying theory. In the following
expression,
\begin{align*}
y_i &= [ X_i | Z_i ] \left[ 
  \begin{matrix}
    \beta \\ b_i
  \end{matrix}
\right] + \epsilon_i
\\
b_i & \sim N(0, \Psi) \\
\epsilon_i & \sim N(0, V)
\end{align*}
$y_i$, $X_i$, $Z_i$ are known and we want to estimate
$\beta$, $\Psi$ and~$V$. The coefficient $\beta$ is said to
be a \emph{fixed effect} while $b_i$ is a \emph{random
  effect} -- the word ``effect'' means ``estimated
parameter''.

This can be done by Maximum
Likelihood (ML), 
$$
\textrm{Likelihood}
(
\textrm{variance structure}, \textrm{parameters} \ |\ 
\textrm{data}
),
$$
which produces biased estimates, or with Restricted
Maximum Likelihood (REML): the restricted likelihood is the
likelihood with the parameters integrated out; we use it to
estimate the variance structure, and then, we plug this back
into the likelihood to estimate the parameters. 

From a computational point of view, one can first use the \emph{EM}
(Expectation--Maximization) method, that considers the
random effects as unobserved (missing) variables (it goes
very fast to the neighbourhood of the optimum, but then
converges very slowly) and then the good old \emph{Newton--Raphson}
method (it converges fast when it starts close to the
optimum).

The parameters are asymptotically gaussian, so one can use
AIC, BIC and Likelihood Ratio (LR) tests (but you had better
only use them to compare two models if the fixed effects are
the same).

Chapter 3 presents the R functions to explore grouped data,
fit mixed models and assess those models. For instance, 
\\\verb$  lme(y~x, random= ~ 1 | subject/group)$\\
fits a model in which the slope is a fixed effect, the
intercept is a random effect that depends on the two
variables \texttt{subject} and \texttt{group}.  If we wanted
both the slope and the intercept to be random, we would have
typed (remember that when you write a model, the intercept
is always implicitely included: we need not mention it)
\\\verb$  lme(y~x, random= ~ x | subject/group)$\\
or
\\\verb$  lme(y~x, random=list(subject=~x, group=~x))$

If you want your data to be easily plotted, with functions
from the \texttt{lattice} library, you can store them in a
\texttt{groupedData} object, that contains the data (as a
data frame), a display formula (e.g.,
\verb!y~time|subject)!), ``outer factors'' (\ie, groups of
subjects, e.g., \verb|outer=~sex| or
\verb|outer=~Variety*Year|) and ``inner factors'' (\ie,
other qualitative predictive variables, e.g.,
\verb|inner=~Treatment|).

You might also want to investigate the functions
\texttt{gsummary},
\texttt{intervals},
\texttt{augPred},
\texttt{plot.lme},
\texttt{summary.lme},
\texttt{VarCorr},
\texttt{lmList},
\texttt{simulate.lme},
\texttt{balancedGrouped}.

Chapter 4 suggests a model building strategy:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Fit a usual regression without taking the
  \texttt{subject} variable into account; look at the
  residuals in each \texttt{subject} (with a boxplot): it
  they are significantly different, this might warrant a
  mixed effects model with the \texttt{subject} variable.
  but we still do not know which effects should be fixed and
  which should be random.
\item Fit a regression for each subject, with the
  \texttt{lmList} function; look which coefficients vary a
  lot between subjects, with the
  \texttt{plot(intervals(...))} function: they will be your
  random effects; you may also want to look at the pairplots
  of the estimated coefficients, to get an idea of the
  covariance structure $\Psi$ of the random effects. 
\item Fit the model and examine it: boxplot of the residuals
  for each subject (to see outlying subjects), residuals vs
  fitted value (to check if there is a difference between
  groups: if so, add this new grouping variable),
  quantile-quantile plots of the residuals; also remember to
  check for heteroskedasticity and correlation problems.
\item Do not only look at the residuals: similarly, look at
  the random effects: quantile-quantile plot, pairplot (to
  see if you should add a coarser grouping variable, as
  above; and also to check if your assumptions about the
  covariance matrix were not unreasonable).
\end{enumerate}

Chapter 5 explains how the covariance of the random effects
(the $\Psi$ matrix) can be specified. There is first the
correlation structure among the random effects (investigated
with the pair plots mentionned earlier). If you do not
specify anything, the computer assumes that the covariance
matrix has no remarkable property. If they are independent:
\\\verb$  random=list(subject=pdDiag(~x1+x2+x3))$\\
Other examples: 
\\\verb$  pdIdent(~ x1+x2+x3)$
\\\verb$  pdDiag(~ Variety-1)$
\\\verb$  pdCompSymm(~ Variety-1)$
\\\verb$  pdBlocked(list(~1, ~x1+x2+x3-1))$
\\\verb$  pdBlocked(list(pdIdent(~1), pdIdent(~ Variety-1))$

You should also tackle heteroskedasticity (the $V$ matrix);
e.g., if the variance of the noise depends on the
\texttt{group} variable, you can say
\\\verb$  weights=varIdent(form= ~ 1 | group)$

But the general situation is more complicated: to simplify
it, we decompose the noise variance matrix $V$ into a
variance component (a diagonal matrix, that will account for
heteroskedasticity in the noise term) and a correlation component (a
positive definite matrix with 1s on the diagonal, that will
account for the lack of independence of the noise term), and
we specify them separately.

Here are a few examples, to be given to the \texttt{lme}
function as a \texttt{weights} argument. 

The variance increases linearly with age:
\\\verb$  varFixed(form= ~age)$
\\
The variance is constant in each group:
\\verb$  varIdent(form= ~1|group)$
\\
The variance increases linearly with the fitted values:
\\\verb$  varPower(fixed=.5)$
\\
The variance increases linearly with the fitted values for
one group only:
\\\verb$  varPower(form= ~fitted(.) | Sex,$
\\\verb$           fixed=list(Male=.5, Female=0))$
\\
There are other functions: \texttt{varExp}, 
\texttt{varConstPower}, \texttt{varComb}.

And now, the correlation structures, to be fed to the
\texttt{lme} function as a \texttt{correlation} argument. 

Compound symetry: two observations for the same subject have
a $0.3$ correlation.
\\\verb$  corCompSymm(value=0.3, form=~1 |Subject)$
\\
A general correlation matrix: 
\\\verb$  corSymm(form = ~ 1 |Subject)$
\\
An AR(1) correlation structure (by default, the order is
that in which the observations appear -- see also the
\texttt{ACF} function):
\\\verb$  corAR1(0.8, form = ~ 1 | Subject )$
\\
A continuous AR(1) correlation structure (the coefficient
will be positive):
\\\verb$  corCAR1(0.8, form = ~ time | Subject )$
\\
An ARMA correlation structure: 
\\\verb$  corARMA( c(.8, .4), p=1, q=1,$
\\\verb$           form= ~ 1 | Subject )$
\\
There are also spatial correlation structures (see also the
\texttt{plot(variogram(...))}) function:
\\\verb$  corExp( 1, form = ~ x + y )$
\\\verb$  corGauss(...)$
\\\verb$  corLin(...)$
\\\verb$  corRatio(...)$
\\\verb$  corSphere(...)$

This chapter also presents the \texttt{gls} function
(Generalized Least Squares), to fit (non-mixed)
heteroskedastic linear models -- because \emph{the
  correlation structure and the heteroskedasticity can have
  the same effects} and therefore specifying both can lead
to unidentifiability. People often wonder how to tackle
heteroskedasticity in R: this is one solution. 

Part 2 (I have not carefully read it) tackles non-linear
models: you should use them when you know the mechanism
producing the data (this is often the case in chemistry) or
when the data has natural properties, such as an asymptote.
Otherwise, stick to linear models -- with splines or local
regression, if needed.

One limitation of the \texttt{nlme} package is the fact
that, if we have several grouping variables, they have to be
nested -- but you can imagine mixed models in which they are
not.

Another limitation is that it does not include Generalized
Linear Models (GLM) such as logistic or Poisson regression:
for those, you might want to have a look at \texttt{glmmML}
in the \texttt{glmmML} package for GLM with a random
intercept; \texttt{GLMM} in the \texttt{lme4} package for
mixed GLM via REML; \texttt{glmmPQL} in the \texttt{MASS}
package (idem); \texttt{gee} in the \texttt{gee} package. 

The under-hyped \texttt{lme4} package contains other
interesting functions, such as the \texttt{lmer} function (a
revised version of the \texttt{lme} function), that allows
for nested and \emph{crossed} random factors. The syntax for
the mixed effects is the ``natural'' one:
\\\verb$y ~ (x1 | g1) + (x2 | g2)$

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Incorporating estimation errors into portfolio selection: robust portfolio construction}\\
  S. Ceria, R.A. Stubbs, 2005
\end{book}

Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT) works as follows: estimate the
 returns and the volatility of each stock, then try to
find the combination of stocks (``portfolio'') with the
highest return among those whose volatility is within the
limits imposed by your clients. 
\begin{align*}
  &\textrm{Maximize } \alpha^T w \\
  &\textrm{Subject to } w^T Q w \leqslant v
\end{align*}
where $\alpha$ are the predicted returns, 
$Q$ is the covariance matrix of the predicted returns,
$v$ is the maximum volatility allowed, 
$w$ is the portfolio composition (to be found). 

This is a \emph{quadratic optimization problem}: the set of
feasable solutions is convex, with sharp edges (sharp
because of the linear constraints we do not mention) and the
best solution will be on one of its vertices. When we
slightly change the inputs, the solution may switch from
one vertex to another.

Several solutions to overcome the imprecision of returns
forecasts have been proposed:
\begin{itemize}
\item shrink them towards the average expected return
  (James--Stein);
\item shrink them towards that of the minimum variance
  portfolio (Jorion);
\item Adopt a bayesian approach (Black--Litterman);
\item Increase the risk-aversion parameter (Horst);
\item Resample (Michaud) -- if you have time and no
  non-convex constraints, such as a limit on the portfolio
  size or a minimum investment per asset.
\end{itemize}

The idea of robust optimization is: 
\begin{align*}
  &\textrm{Maximize } 
  \min\bigl\{ \alpha^T w,\ \alpha \in B(\bar\alpha,r)\bigr\} \\
  &\textrm{Subject to } w^T Q w \leqslant v
\end{align*}
where $\bar\alpha$ are the return forecasts, 
$$
B(\bar\alpha,r) = \bigl\{ \alpha \ :\ 
(\alpha-\bar\alpha)^T 
\Sigma^{-1} 
(\alpha-\bar\alpha) \leqslant r^2 \bigr\}
$$
is an ellipsoid, centered on $\bar\alpha$, containing the
actual forward returns and $\alpha$ are the worst-case
returns in this ellipsoid. The magic is that one can
actually compute that minimum:
$$
\min \alpha^T w = \bar\alpha^Tw - r \lVert \Sigma^{-1}
  w \rVert. 
$$
We thus have a new convex optimization problem -- but it
is no longer quadratic: we need either a general convex
optimization algorithm or a ``symetric second order cone
optimizer''.

This was the general idea, but it still has to be refined.
Two problems are:
\begin{itemize}
\item We are too conservative, we have assumed that the
  errors were always in the worst direction;
\item This approach only works for long-only portfolios.
\end{itemize}

We can introduce a ``bayesian prior'' (they claim they do
not like bayesian methods, but it is what they are doing) as
a new constraint, stating that the upward and downword
errors should cancel each other (but you can refine this, to
account for the larger number of overestimated returns and
for the volatility).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Axioma, the future of risk analysis, rebalancing and
    trading analytics}\\
  D. Cashion (2005) 
  %Presentation to SSgA
\end{book}

Mean-Variance Optimization (MVO) consists in taking a set of
assets, with their predicted returns and estimated
volatility and building the portfolios with a given
volatility, the maximum expected return, satisfying a set of
constraints (e.g., long-only, at most 2\% in each stock,
etc.). The problem is that the computer assumnes that the
predicted returns are accurate while they are not: if we
change them a bit, the resulting portfolios can drastically
change. The reason is that the possible weights of the
various assets in the portfolio form a convex set, with sharp
vertices, and the result portfolio is on one of those vertices:
when we change the inputs, either we stay on the same
vertex, or we switch to another one.

Instead, Axioma incorporate uncertainty in the inputs, they
replace exact values by intervals (ellipses, actually) of
possible values; this amounts to bevel the vertices of our
convex set. 

They also add discrete heuristic algorithms for rules such
as ``maximum number of holdings'' or ``minimum holding''.
if-then-else rules, etc.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The generalized dynamic factor model: representation 
    theory}\\
  M. Forni and M. Lippi
\end{book}

Here are a few ideas to study panel data:
\begin{itemize}
\item Consider the time series one at a time -- but this
  will not account for the correlation between the time
  series;
\item Use a VAR model -- but this will only work if you have
  few subjects: we have thousands of them;
\item In the \emph{dynamic factor analytic model}, aka
  \emph{index model}, there are (latent) orthogonal
  variables $f_1(t),\dots,f_n(t)$ and, for each subject, the
  variable studied is a linear combination of those factors
  plus an \emph{idiosyncratic component} (aka
  \emph{subject-specific dynamic pattern}), orthogonal to
  the factors and pairwise orthogonal among them, 
  $$ x_i(t) = \beta_i^1 f_1(t) + \cdots + \beta_i^n f_n(t) + \epsilon_i(t) $$
\item You can also add in the lagged factors;
\item Finally, you can remove the requirement that the
  idiosyncratic factors be pairwise orthogonal to account
  for, e.g., shock propagation.
\end{itemize}
This last model is the \emph{generalized dynamic
  factor model}.

But how do yo estimate the coefficients of the model? Well,
you do not. However, a preceding article, by the same
author, explained how to infer the number $n$ of factors and
this articles states that the common component $x_i(t) -
\epsilon_i(t)$ is the projection of the signal on the first
$n$ ``\emph{dynamic principal components}'' -- but I have
not understood what it meant.

%TODO: Explain how to find $n$.

%
TODO: Explain ``dynamic Principal Component''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Linear regression with errors in both variables: a
    proper bayesian approach}\\
  T.P. Minka, 1999
\end{book}

Linear regression focuses oin the relation between (say) two
variables, e.g., $y = \alpha + \beta x + \textrm{noise}$: we
assume that there is a simple, deterministic relation
between $x$ and $y$ and that $y$ is observed with errors.
But what if $x$ is also measured with errors? You can:
\begin{itemize}
\item Forget about those errors if you just want to predict $y$ from the imprecisely measured $x$;
\item Try more symetric methods, such as Principal Component
  Analysis (PCA): it gives you some insight as tp the
  relation between $x$ and $y$, put focuses on the joint
  distribution $p(x,y)$, not the conditional distribution
  $p(y|x)$ -- do not use it for predictions;
\item Account for those errors with an EIS (Error In
  Variables) regression: we have a relation $y_0 = \alpha +
  \beta x_0$ but we can only measure $y=y_0+\textrm{noise}$
  and $x=x_0+\textrm{noise}$ and we want to find $\alpha$
  and $\beta$ -- we do not want to make forecasts, just to
  study the mechanisms behind the data.
\end{itemize}

The article presents three EIS estimators:
\begin{itemize}
\item TLS (Total Least Squares): write the likelihood (which
  depends on $\alpha$, $\beta$ and $x_0$), replace $x_0$
  with its most probable value and find $\alpha$ and $\beta$
  by the values that maximize the likelihood;
\item Maximum Likelihood (ML): integrate $x_0$ out of the
  likelihood and maximize this marginal likelihood;
\item Posterior Mean: the posterior distribution tends to be
  bimodal: the ML estimator is not stable; we replace it by
  the expectation of the posterior distribution of
  $(\alpha,\beta)$.
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Liquidity and autocorrelations in individual stock
    returns}, D. Avramov et al., 2005
\end{book}

When an investor wants to buy or sell a large amount of a
given stock, the price will rise or drop for a short moment
and then revert to normal. Seemingly profitable contrarian
trading strategies can stem from this phenomenon. To check
wether they are profitable, the article combines
\begin{itemize}
\item Past returns;
\item Illiquidity (defined as
$$ \dfrac{ \va{ \textrm{returns} } }{\textrm{traded value}} $$
or as the average ask/bid spread);
\item Turnover
\end{itemize}
to define their strategy, equally weighted or ``relative
strength''-weighted (more weight on the stocks whose recent
returns were high) and estimates the transaction costs (in
three ways, but the only detailed one is the ask/bid
spread). In the end, the strategy is not profitable, the
Efficient Market Hypothesis is not violated.

In other words: ``non-informed trading is accompanied with
high trading volume, informed trading is accompanied with
low trading volume'' -- ``changes accompanied with high volume
revert'', ``changes accompanied with low volume need not
revert''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Panel data models for stock returns:\\
    the importance of industries}\\
  R. Bauer et al, LIFE, 2003
\end{book}

In finance, to study a factor, \ie, a variable
suspected of providing some insight into future
returns, one usually uses the non-parametric
(``non-parametric means that it does not rely on
statistical assumptions that one could forget to
check -- it is fool-proof) \emph{portfolio
  method}: build five portfolios, each containing
the stocks in one quintile of the variable and monitor
their returns. This can be generalized to two
factors -- but not more: beyond that, many
fractiles would be empty...

Instead, in order to include more variables,
people have tried regression (often forgetting
that there are assumptions to be checked, at least
graphically, before performing the analysis, and
forgetting to assess the quality of the results,
e.g., with diagnostic plots). But financial data
is inherently bidimensional, \ie, the variables we
play with do not form vectors but rather
2-dimensional arrays, one stock per row, one date
per column. One can forget this 2-dimensional
structure and compute the usual (``Ordinaly Least
Squares'' or OLS) regression. But by doing so we
discard some of the information available.

Instead, we can slice the data and consider one
date (column) at a time, compute the regression
coefficients for each date (people say
``cross-sectionnally'') and take the mean of those
regression coefficients: this is the
\emph{Fama-McBeth regression}; it takes into
account the unbalancedness of the data.

But this suggests that the quality (variance) of
the estimation of the regression coefficients is
the same for each date -- this need not be the
case. We can estimate this variance and take it
into account by computing a weighted average of
the coefficients (the weight of a given
coefficient at a given date is the inverse of its
estimated standard deviation): this is called
\emph{weighted regression} or \emph{weighted least
  squares}.

But we still have a problem: we assume that these
regression coefficients are constant over time --
this is surely wrong. To take this effect into
account, we must leave the realm of DIY statistics
and enter that of \emph{mixed models}. We can then
consider regressions of the form
$$
\textrm{return} = \alpha + \beta \cdot
\textrm{factor} + \textrm{noise} $$
where the
regression coefficients $\alpha$ and $\beta$ may
themselves depend on other data, say:
\begin{align*}
  \alpha &\sim \textrm{stock} + \textrm{industry} \times \textrm{time} \\
  \beta & \sim \textrm{industry} \\
  \textrm{noise variance} & \sim \textrm{time}.
\end{align*}
This is the example detailed in the article (with
two differences: they have a dozen factors instead
of one and they assume that the variance is
constant), but you may change it as you want. 

The numerical estimation of the parameters of
those mixed models used to be very intricate (that
is why some people still use ``Fama-McBeth
regression'' or ``weighted least squares'') but,
luckily, R can now do that for us.

The article also remarks that statistical tests
with a 5\,\% confidence level are useless in this
context, because the huge volume of data leads to
very small $p$-values. Instead, they use the
Schwartz Information Criterion (SIC). We already
mentionned this problem when we studied Bayesian
Model Averaging (BMA) -- we had used the Bayesian
Information Criterion (BIC), aka Schwartz Bayesian
Criterion (SBC).


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{VWAP reversion}\\
  S. Usui, Nomura, 2004
\end{book}

They propose the following (intraday) investment strategy:
\begin{itemize}
\item If index $\gg$ 15-minute VWAP $\gg$ 60-minute VWAP,
  then long the index;
\item If index $\ll$ 15-minute VWAP $\ll$ 60-minute VWAP,
  then short the index.
\end{itemize}
where VWAP is the volume-weighted average price -- it is a replacement
of the moving average, better-suited for intra-day data. This is just
a refinement of the strategy:
\begin{itemize}
\item If price $\gg$ Moving Average, then buy;
\item If price $\ll$ Moving Average, then sell.
\end{itemize}
that relies on the belief that the price is mean-reverting -- the
refinement selects periods when the price has a significant upward
(respectively downward) trend, visible both on the 15-minute VWAP and
the 60-minute VWAP.

They motivate this strategy by a linear relation between (price $-$
15-minute VWAP) and (15-minute VWAP $-$ 60-minute VWAP). Here, of
course, regression is a bad idea: first the data are not normal (they
are fat-tailed), second, regression is asymetric (you try to predict
one variable from the other) while the strategy is symetric (if you
want a plot, draw the first principal component, if you want a test, a
simple correlation test would do).

They try to tune their strategy by modifying the thresholds above
which those inequalities trigger the trades, but:
\begin{itemize}
\item They do not tune the time parameters (15 minutes and 60
  minutes);
\item Their tuning is not adaptive.
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Trading Securities Using Trailing Stops}\\
  P.W. Glynn and D.L. Iglehart,\\
  Management Science, 41:6 (1995)
\end{book}

To avoid losses, one can sell whenever the price (of a stock you have
bought) goes to far away (say, \$5) below the previous maximum. The
article investigates, from a theoretic point of view, the time you
hold the asset, the return, the ``best'' limit to choose -- under the
unrealistic assumption that the price follows a random walk with a
constant linear trend, oblivious of the fact that those stop-loss
rules are designed to get rid of stocks that do not follow our model
or to spot a change in the model.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{High frequency data filtering}\\
  T.N. Falkenberry, TickData Inc. (2002)
\end{book}

High-frequency data may be unclean, even more so than
traditional data because the sheer volume of data prevents
their efficient automatic (or manual) detection.

The ``errors'' can be: isolated bad ticks, bad ticks in
succession or real outliers (to be retained, otherwise we
would build a model on data with an unrealistically low
volatility). 

The errors may be obvious at some scale but not at larger
scale.

To automatically detect some of those errors, you may remark
that:
\begin{itemize}
\item The tick frequency depends on the capitalization and
  on the volume;
\item The tick frequency depends on the time of the day and
  the capitalization (constant accorss the day for
  small-caps, constant accross the day except for an opening
  peak for mid-caps, U-shaped for big-caps).
\end{itemize}

The filter they provide takes into account: those different
tick frequency profiles, changing volatility, overnight
gaps.

They do not give any detail, but the filter could look like:
\begin{itemize}
\item Compute a MA and a moving standard deviation; start
  anew every morning to accommodate overnight gaps; if the
  standard deviation is too low, set it to 2 ticks;
\item If a point is more than 3 standard deviations from the
  moving average, tag it as a bad tick; you may want to
  change the threshold;
\item If the last tick was a bad tick and you would like the
  current tick to be a bad tick as well, don't, and
  reinitialize the moving average and the moving volatility;
  you may want to modify this rule to accomodate repeated
  bad ticks (they would be equal);
\item Replace bad ticks with ticks with the same volume and
  the preceding price.
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Predicting Returns with Financial Ratios}\\
  J. Lewellen, 2003
\end{book}

To test if a factor $x$ has a predictive power on the
returns $r$, one usually uses Ordinary Least Squares (OLS,
aka ``classical regression''), even if the method's
hypotheses are not satisfied. But the model is often:
\begin{align*}
  r_n &= \alpha + \beta x_n + \epsilon_n \\
  x_{n+1} &= \phi + \rho x_n + \eta_n \\
  \cor(\epsilon, \eta) &= \gamma.
\end{align*}
With just the first two equations and independent noises,
OLS regression and tests give correct answers, but with the
last equation, the results are biased towards
predictability.

The article, oblivious of other methods, such as Maximum Likelihood
Estimators (MLE), tries to \emph{force} OLS into that framework and
derive reliable tests.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Natural Selection$^{\textrm{TM}}$: Matching strategies to assets}\\
  Evolutionary Finance Ltd, June~2003
\end{book}

They present an investment strategy design software, based on
``patented'' genetic algorithms (it seems to be a UK company: software
patents are illegal in Europe...), that proceeds as follows.
\begin{itemize}
\item Take a set of possible investment strategies, each yielding a
  ``buy/sell'' signal (they give an extensive list of such signals);
\item Combine those strategies to find the ``best mixed strategy'' at
  a given time, for a given universe, for a given horizon;
\item Given the history of those ``best'' strategies, try to predict
  the next best strategy.
\end{itemize}

There are a few problems:
\begin{itemize}
\item They do not explain how to \emph{combine} strategies -- this is the heart of the algorithm.
\item Using \emph{the} previous best investment strategy to predict
  the next best one means that you discard all the information about
  strategies almost as good as the best -- the actual best strategy is
  sample-dependant, you surely do not want to discard all the
  information contained in the near-best strategies.
\item They do not explain how they use genetic algorithms in the
  second step, to predict the next best strategy; one could use
  straightforward bayesian methods or a Markov chain: is there more
  than that?
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Momentum Morons} \\
  Evolutionary Finance Ltd, Biweekly~report, June~2003
\end{book}

Backtesting a strategy often suffers from a serious bias: it selects a
strategy adapted to the backtest period, not to the present or to the
future. The author advocates methods able to detect \emph{regime
  shifts} -- such as their evolutionary models.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Detecting a currency's dominance or dependance using 
    foreign exchange network trees} \\
  M. McDonald et al.
  \url{arXiv:cond-mat/0412411}
\end{book}

This article applies ``\emph{distance analysis}'' fo financial data.
There are a few differences fromn the classical, idealistic situation
you might be used to.
\begin{itemize}
\item Oftentimes, distance analysis simply does not work. In order to
  actually see something, you can compute and plot the \emph{Minimum
    Spanning Tree} (MST) of the data. This is actually a crude,
  1-dimensional form of ``local distance analysis'' -- if you want
  more about this, have a look at the \emph{isomap} algorithm.
\item There are more missing data than usual. The distances are
  computed from a correlation matrix (there are several ways of doing
  so, e.g., $d = \sqrt{1-\textrm{cor}}$ or $d =
  \sqrt{1-\va{\textrm{cor}}}$).  When you compute a correlation
  matrix, you have to remove all the observations (here, the dates)
  for which at least one value is missing -- it you just remove the
  pairs for which one value is missing, you might not get a positive
  definite matrix.
\item As the data are exchange rates, both USD/EUR and EUR/USD are
  present: with the chosen distance, the two points will be very far
  apart.
\item The fact that the product of the exhange rates of any three
  currencies, say USD/EUR, EUR/JPY, JPY/USD, is 1 imposes some
  structure on the tree. To see if this structure dominates or if the
  resulting tree contains more information, they suggest to resample
  one (only one) of the currencies and to compare the two trees.
\item They also check, visually, that the relations between the
  various exchange rate returns are linear.
\item They check if lagged correlations contain some information: for
  hourly exchange rate data, there is none. If there were, we would
  depict the corresponding relations by a \emph{directed} graph.
\item They also investigate the sability of the tree over time (by
  looking at the edges that remain).
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical Computing: an Introduction to Data Analysis using S-Plus} \\
  M.J. Crawley, John Wiley and Sons, 2002
\end{book}

I did not read the book but just three extra ``chapters'' available on
the Web
(\url{http://www.bio.ic.ac.uk/research/mjcraw/statcomp/chapters.html}).
The first one is about \emph{gamma regression}, \ie, linear regression
with (non normal) gamma-distributed noise: you use it when the
relation looks linear but the noise is asymetric -- however, the
motivation and example they give could be dealt with a simple
transformation (\verb|1/y~1/x|) -- quite confusing.

The second chapter, about Generalized Additive Models (GAM) was
superficial but fine.

The third chapter was the most confusing: they state that PCA and
Factor Analysis are different but give the same interpretation (why,
then, are the results different? Which method should we choose in
which situation?); they mix up \emph{supervised learning} (in
\emph{discriminant analysis} we know the classes beforehand we do not
try to find if there are classes, how many there are or how to
interpret them, as the author sometimes (but not always) seems to
think) and \emph{unsipervised learning}.

Neither buy nor even read that book!

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Global Financial Data Products} \\
  M. Bulsing and A. Scowcroft \\
  UBS Investment Research, April 2002
\end{book}

A badly written article (the abstract is too long, contains too many
technical terms; there is no distinction between the data that come
from the data sources and the data that has been computed from the
data sources; etc. -- the preceding article used marginal notes to
improve readability: this was a good idea) that tries to explain what
can go wrong when you handle financial data:
\begin{itemize}
\item multiple data sources, with multiple conventions;
\item data cascades (you take data from one source, perform some
  computations, combine the result with another data source, perform
  some more computations, etc.);
\item mis-aligned data: ``monthly data'' can be ``data at the end of
  the month'', ``data at mid-month'', ``data smoothed across the
  month'' -- furthermore, corporate events might occur within the
  month;
\item Market capitalization requires the ``number of shares'', which
  fluctuates daily (because of options, for instance): data sources
  update this more or less continuously;
\item Currency problems (not read);
\item Models: as data providers, they try to add some value to the
  data they provide, by performing some computations, based on models;
  they must include enough variables in those models but not too many.
\end{itemize}
They favour a model with:
\begin{itemize}
\item the market return
\item a factor (``yield''), for which they provide several formulas,
  each corresponding to a given ``style'' -- they state the problems posed by each factor);
\item the sector;
\item the region (considering the country would lead to too many
  classes).
\end{itemize}

They do not mention the problem of missing data, of outliers, of
errors and sanity checks, of unsatisfied normality or linearity
assumptions in their regressions.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Active Return is Ambiguous} \\
  M. Partridge at al. \\
  UBS Investment Research, August 2003
\end{book}

There are several definitions of ``active return'' (\ie,
``difference'' between the return of your portfolio and that of a
benchmark), but the most widely used have a few problems:
\begin{itemize}
\item They are not transitive: if they say that portfolio A is better
  that portfolio B and that portfolio B is better than portfolio C,
  they need not say that portfolio A is better than portfolio C;
\item The can be incoherent and state that portfolio A is better than
  portfolio B \emph{and} that portfolio B is better than portfolio A;
\item They do not give the same result if you use monthly or daily
  data: they can say that portfolio A is better that portfolio B if you
  look at monthly historical prices and the opposite if you look at
  daily prices (these are fixed portfolios, not investment strategies:
  the portfolios are not rebalanced).
\end{itemize}

The definitions are as follows (active return, index difference,
compounded return, log-return, index ratio):
\begin{align*}
  \alpha_{\text{active}}(A-B) &= 
  \sum_t \left( \dfrac{A_{t+1}}{A_t} - \dfrac{B_{t+1}}{B_t} \right) \\
  \alpha_{\text{diff.}} (A-B) &= 
  \prod_t \dfrac{A_{t+1}}{A_t} - 
  \prod_t \dfrac{B_{t+1}}{B_t} \\
  &= 
  \dfrac{A_{\text{end}}}{A_{\text{begin}}} - 
  \dfrac{B_{\text{end}}}{B_{\text{begin}}} \\
  \alpha_{\text{comp.}} (A-B) &= 
  \prod_t \left(
    1 + \dfrac{A_{t+1}}{A_t} - 
    \prod_t \dfrac{B_{t+1}}{B_t} \right) \\
  \alpha_{\text{log}} (A-B) &= 
  \sum_t \log 
  \dfrac{A_{t+1}}{A_t} - 
  \sum_t \log \dfrac{B_{t+1}}{B_t} \\
  &= 
  \log 
  \left(
  \dfrac{A_{\text{end}}}{A_{\text{begin}}} /
  \dfrac{B_{\text{end}}}{B_{\text{begin}}} 
  \right)
  \\
  \alpha_{\text{ratio}} (A-B) &= 
  \left(
    \prod_t  
    \dfrac{A_{t+1}}{A_t} 
  \right)
  /
  \left(
    \dfrac{B_{t+1}}{B_t} 
  \right) - 1 \\
  &= 
  \dfrac{A_{\text{end}}}{A_{\text{begin}}} /
  \dfrac{B_{\text{end}}}{B_{\text{begin}}} 
\end{align*}


The log returns or the index ratio have no problems (except, of
course, being non-additive accross portfolios), and should be used
instead of the others. The article pairs the pathologies and the
indices (table 10 page 19).

The conclusion could be: if you want to add returns month after month,
choose the log-returns (but do not add the log-returns of individual
stocks to hope to get that of a portfolio), if you want to ``add'' the
returns of stocks to get that of a portfolio, use the active returns
(but \emph{never} add them month after month). If you hesitate, choose
the log-returns.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wavelet compression, determinism\\
    and time series forecasting}\\
  I. Kaplan, 2003
\end{book}

The author presents a new way of assessing the noisiness (or
volatility) of a time series: the percentage of compression (say, on a
64-element moving window) obtained by \emph{lossless} wavelet
compression.

Let us first recall what wavelet compression is. We start with a time
series and we want to approximate it with a ``simpler'' function (yes,
it sounds like regression). We first try to approximate it by a
constant (a horizontal line); then, we take the residuals, cut them
into two parts (the first half of the series and the last half) and
try to approximate each part by a constant. And we iterate.

At each step, instead of approximating the data with a horizontal
line, we can use a more complicated function (a line, a single sine
wave -- there are other less classical functions, called wavelets,
with better theoretical properties).

This can be interpreted as: at each scale, a good forecast of $x_n$ is 
$\tfrac12( x_{n-1} + x_{n+1} )$.

The idea of \emph{lossless} compression is to start with an
integer-valued time series, allow only integer coefficients and retain
all the coefficients.

One can measure the ``complexity'' or the ``compressibility'' by
counting the number of bits in the resulting set of \emph{integer}
coefficients (the binary code of an integer~$n$ requires $\lceil
\log_2 n \rceil$ bits). Low values means low noise and high
predictability.

Remark: lossy compression removes the low-amplitude noise and thus
cannot estimate the overall amount of noise.

Remark: the author notes that the compressibility is reliably
approximated by the number of bits of the first difference, which is
easier to compute (\verb|sum(log(diff(x))/log(2))|, in R).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Signal processing for everyone}\\
  G. Strang (2001)
\end{book}

There are three kinds of Fourier transforms:
\begin{itemize}
\item The Fourier series expansion, that turns a (periodic) function
  into a sequence of coefficients;
\item The Fourier transform, that turns a function (in $L^2$) into another function;
\item The Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT), that turns a discrete
  signal (a sampled function, a sequence) into a periodic function (a
  function on the circle 
  $\mathbf{S}^1$),
  $$
  (x_n)_{n \in \ZZ} \longmapsto X(\omega) = \sum_n x_n e^{-in\omega}.
  $$
\end{itemize}
The sampled signal and its DFT contain the same information, they are
just two different ways of looking at the same data; when we look at the
sampled signal, we say that we are in the \emph{time domain}, when we
look at its DFT, we are in the \emph{frequency domain}.

In the real world, you never study a continuous signal: you sample it
at discrete points and study the resulting sequence -- hence our
interest in the DFT. But we might lose information if the sampling
frequency is too low: for instance, the following sampled signal
\begin{center}
\setkeys{Gin}{width=3cm,height=3cm}
<<Aliasing1,echo=FALSE,fig.width=2,fig.height=2,out.width="3cm">>=
par(mar=c(0,0,0,0))
x <- seq(-5,5) * 3*pi/2
xlim <- 3*pi*c(-1,1)
plot( cos(x) ~ x, axes=F, pch=16, xlim=xlim, col="blue" )
abline(h=0, lty=3)
@ 
\end{center}
could come from one of the following continuous signals
\begin{center}
\setkeys{Gin}{width=3cm,height=3cm}
<<Aliasing2,echo=FALSE,fig.width=2,fig.height=2,out.width="3cm">>=
par(mar=c(0,0,0,0))
plot( cos(x) ~ x, axes=F, pch=16, xlim=xlim, col="blue" )
abline(h=0, lty=3)
xx <- seq(-5,5, length=1000) * 3*pi/2
lines(cos(xx) ~ xx)
@ 
\qquad
<<Aliasing3,echo=FALSE,fig.width=2,fig.height=2,out.width="3cm">>=
par(mar=c(0,0,0,0))
plot( cos(x) ~ x, axes=F, pch=16, xlim=xlim, col="blue" )
abline(h=0, lty=3)
xx <- seq(-5,5, length=1000) * 3*pi/2
lines(cos(xx/3) ~ xx)
@ 
\end{center}
this is called \emph{aliasing}. We usually assume that the signal has
no components with a frequency higher than half the sampling rate (or,
equivalently, that we sample at a frequency higher than twice the
highest frequency in the signal -- this is called the \emph{Nyquist
  frequency}: anything above that is lost by the sampling).

It would be nice if we could recover the signal from its samples, if
we could interpolate it -- under the assumption that the signal has no
component of frequency higher than the sampling frequency, this is
reasonable. One would expect a formula of the form $x(t) = \sum a_n
g_n(t)$ where the $a_n$ are coefficients computed in a complicated way
from the sampled function and the $g_n$ are functions -- actually,
things are even simpler: the coefficients in \emph{Shannon's formula}
are the sampled values themselves,
$$ x(t) = \sum_n x(nT) \cdot \sinc \dfrac{t - nT}{T/\pi} $$
where $\sinc$ is the \emph{sine cardinal} function, 
$$ \sinc t = \dfrac{\sin t}t.$$

A \emph{filter} is a way of transforming a sampled signal into another
sampled signal. To study a filter, one first applies it to simple
signals such as: a constant, a linear function, an impulse, an
alternating sequence or a pure sinusoidal signal $x_n(\omega) = e^{i
  n\omega}$.  The filters may (or not) have the following properties:
linear, time-invariant, of finite length, low-pass (the low
frequencies are (mostly) preserved, the high-frequencies are (mostly)
discarded), high-pass, causal (they do not peer into the future), they
can preserve constant signals, linear signals, etc.

Linear Time-Invariant (LTI) filters are linear filters that do not
change the frequency of pure sine waves, \ie, $x_\omega \longmapsto
H(\omega) x_\omega$, \ie, a sine wave is only changed in its amplitude
and phase, \ie, the filter is diagonalizable in the basis of pure sine
waves. The function 
$H: \mathbf{S}^1 \longrightarrow \CC$ that describes the
changes in amplitude and phase is called the \emph{frequency
  response}.

We can now examine a few filters. First, the Moving Average (MA) is a
lowpass filter,
\begin{center}
\setkeys{Gin}{width=3cm,height=3cm}
<<DSP_MA1,echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="3cm">>=
par(mar=c(4,4,1,1))
w <- seq(0,pi,length=100)
plot(cos(w/2)~w, xlab="w", ylab="|H(w)|", main="", type="l", col="blue", lwd=3)
@ 
\qquad
<<DSP_MA2,echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="3cm">>=
par(mar=c(4,4,1,1))
plot(-w/2 ~ w, ylim=c(-pi,pi), xlab="w", ylab="Arg H(w)", main="", type="l", col="blue", lwd=3)
abline(h=c(-pi, -pi/2, 0, pi/2, pi), lty=3)
@ 
\end{center}
the Moving Difference is a high-pass filter,
\begin{center}
\setkeys{Gin}{width=3cm,height=3cm}
<<DSP_HPF1,echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="3cm">>=
par(mar=c(4,4,1,1))
w <- seq(0,pi,length=100)
plot(sin(w/2)~w, xlab="w", ylab="|H(w)|", main="", lwd=3, type="l", col="blue")
@
\qquad
<<DSP_HPF2,echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="3cm">>=
par(mar=c(4,4,1,1))
plot(pi/2-w/2 ~ w, ylim=c(-pi,pi), xlab="w", ylab="Arg H(w)", main="", type="l", col="blue", lwd=3)
abline(h=c(-pi, -pi/2, 0, pi/2, pi), lty=3)
@ 
\end{center}

A linear filter can be seen as a convolution; in the frequency domain
(recall that it means that we replace the sampled signal by its
Discrete Fourier Transform), this becomes a multiplication --
multiplication by the frequency response $H(\omega)$.

The article then becomes more complex and explains how to \emph{build}
filters. Ideally, we would like to have filters that look like
\begin{center}
\setkeys{Gin}{width=3cm,height=3cm}
<<DSP_F1,echo=FALSE,fig.width=4,fig.height=4,out.width="3cm">>=
par(mar=c(4,4,1,1))
w <- seq(0,pi,length=1000)
h <- ifelse( w>pi/3 & w<2*pi/3, 1, 0 )
plot(h~w, xlab="w", ylab="|H(w)|", main="", type="l", lwd=3, col="blue")
@ 
\end{center}
but this is impossible with finite-length filters: the best filter we
can design will show ripples around the transition band, either very
high but localized ripples (Gibbs phenomenon) or (better), ripples of
the same moderate amplitude everywhere (equiripple fiilter, Remez
algorithm).

The last part (two thirds) of the article focuses on \emph{wavelet
  transforms}, that can be implemented as a \emph{filter bank}. 
$$
\xymatrix @!0 @R=1em @C=6pc {
  & \text{high-pass} \ar[rr] && \txt{} \\
  \txt{signal} \ar[ru] \ar[rd] && \txt{high-pass} \ar[r] & \txt{} \\
  & \txt{low-pass} \ar[ru] \ar[rd] \\
  && \txt{low-pass} \ar[r] & \txt{}
}
$$
The signal is divided into high and low frequencies, the low
frequencies are divided into\dots (we do not divide the high
frequencies further: they contain mainly noise -- uninteresting
information -- but we could: we would get \emph{wavelet packets}).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian model selection in social research} \\
  A.E. Raftery (1995)
\end{book}

(This is the article accompanying the \texttt{bicreg} code -- a very
good and readable introduction to bayesian methods.)

Bayseian testing has the following advantages:
\begin{itemize}
\item It works with large samples. In classical hypothesis testing,
  the choice of the significance, $\alpha$, is based on empirical
  remarks for samples of 30 to 100 observations that suggest
  $\alpha=0.01$ to $0.05$; but what if our sample is larger? We should
  decrease $\alpha$ (we never do), but how do we balance significance
  and power?
\item It is simpler: instead of having to consider two numbers,
  sigificance ($\alpha$) and power ($\beta$, which is often
  forgotten), we only have one (the Bayes factor).
\item It allows multiple tests, e.g., for model selection; with
  $p$-values, we have to transform the $p$-values, using the
  Bonferroni correction (designed by someone who assumed the
  observations were independent and who did not understand the notion
  of percentage but well-suited for people with a 4-operation (no
  ``power'' operation) calculator) or the Sidak one (that assumes that
  the observations could be independent): this is pretty awkward.
\item It handles model uncertainty, we do not have to choose a single
  best model if none stands out.
\item It can compare non-nested models.
\item It is symetric: the answer of classical hypothesis testing is
  either ``we reject $H_0$'' or ``we do not reject $H_0$ yet''; the
  answer of bayesian hypothesis testing is ``there is weak/strong/very
  strong evidence for $H$''.
\end{itemize}

The main problem with hypothesis testing is the choice of the prior
distributions. However it only contributes as $O(1/n)$ of the final
result: for large samples, it is not important.

We can compare two models with:
$$
\dfrac{
  P(\text{Model}_1 | \text{data})
}{
  P(\text{Model}_2 | \text{data})
}
=
\dfrac{
  P(\text{data} | \text{Model}_1)
}{
  P(\text{data} | \text{Model}_2)
} 
\times 
\dfrac{
  P(\text{Model}_1)
}{
  P(\text{Model}_2)
}
$$
\ie,
$$
\text{posterior odds} = \text{Bayes factor } B_{12} \times \text{prior odds}.
$$

They also detail (with heuristic non-mathematical arguments) the BIC
approximation:
$$
\log P(\text{data} | \text{model}) = 
\text{maximum likelihood} - 
\dfrac d2 \log n + O(1)
$$
where $d$ is the number of parameters in the model and $n$ the
number of observations. This yields
$$
2 \log B_{12} \equiv \chi^2_{ \text{LRT}, 12} - \text{df}_{12} \log n
$$
where $\chi^2_{\text{LRT},12}$ is the likelihood ratio test
statistic (aka \emph{deviance}, it is the difference of the
loglikelihoods of the models) and $\text{df}_{12} = d_1 - d_2$.

Actually, we have two definitions of BIC, that only differ by an
additive constant:
\begin{align*}
  \text{BIC} &= \chi^2_{
    \text{LRT with the saturated model}
  }
   - d \log n \\
  \text{BIC}' &= \chi^2_{
    \text{LRT with the null (empty) model}
  }
   - d \log n \\
\end{align*}
Here are a few rules of thumb to interpret the Bayes factor or the BIC
difference:

\begin{centering}
  \begin{tabular}{ccc}
    BIC difference & Bayes factor & Evidence\\
    \hline 
    2--6  & 3--20   & positive     \\
    6--10 & 20--150 & strong       \\
    $>10$ & $>150$  & very strong  
  \end{tabular}
\end{centering}

One can convert BIC differences into $p$-value thresholds, but this
depends on the sample size (e.g., strong evidence: $n=20$,
$\alpha=0.05$; $n=100$, $\alpha=0.01$; $n=10000$, $\alpha=0.001$ --
they give a complete table).

The article also details Occam's window for model selection (strong
version: retain the data-supported and parsimonious models; symetric
version: retain the data-supported models -- the symetric variant
performs better).

The bayesian (\ie, BIC-based) approach may also be useful when we want
to select \emph{one} best model: it works better (out-of-sample) than
$p$-values or stepwise regression.

The conclusion mentions the model uncertainty problem and BMA -- which
surpasses bootstrap (bagging).

I have not read the comments after the article. 

While reading articles about bayesian methods, you may stumble upon
the following vocabulary.
\begin{itemize}
\item Non-informative prior: When choosing the prior distribution, we
  sometimes want to include as little information as possible in it
  (because we do not have this information). We may choose, for
  instance, a Cauchy distribution with a large dispersion parameter.
\item Improper prior: Sometimes, we would even like to choose a
  ``uniform distribution on $\RR$'': this is not a probability
  distribution, but it may work. A non-$L^1$ ``density'' function is
  called an improper prior. (Actually, it does not work that well:
  prefer a proper prior.)
\item Conjugate prior: The conjugate family of priors is a family of
  probability distribution functions for which the computations are
  tractable -- this depends on the problem at hand, more precisely on
  the likelihood function -- if you do not do the computations by
  hand, forget about this notion. 
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Stock return predictability: a bayesian model selection perspective}\\
  K.J.M. Cremers
\end{book}

The author tackle the variable selection problem
(for a 15-variable regression model of stock
returns) with a bayesian approach.

Contrary to other articles that dismiss the issue
of choosing the prior distributions by taking a
``diffuse'' one, this article examines 
\begin{itemize}
\item the interpretation of those prior distributions, as the
  inverstor's belief (or not) in the predictability of the returns;
\item the effects of these prior distributions on
  the results: there are no major qualitative
  differences, in both the skeptic and confident
  case, the posterior distribution suggests the
  returns are predictable from half a dozen
  variables.
\end{itemize}
They also performed out-of-sample tests: choosing
the best model brings no reliable forecast,
while a Bayesian Model Average (BAM) has a very
small predictive power.

This could be improved by removing some
restrictions (normality, linearity, parameter
stability) and introducing lagged variables.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Choosing factors in a multifactor asset 
    pricing model: a bayesian approach}\\
  J. Ericsson and S. Karlsson
\end{book}

Bayesian modeling goes as follows: you have a
model whose parameters you want to ``estimate'';
you start with an a priori distribution of the
parameters; you use the data to compute the a
posteriori distribution of the parameters given
the data. You can have several different models if
you choose an a priori probability for each of
them. The differences with the \emph{frequentist} approach are:
\begin{itemize}
\item You have (and use) an a priori distribution
  on the parameters and/or models (if you do not
  have this informationm you may cheat and use a
  \emph{diffuse prior});
\item The result is not an estimation of the
  parameters (that would be a single number)
  but the distribution of these
  parameters -- the classical estimates may be
  obteined from this distribution, by taking the
  expectation (unbiased estimator) or the mode
  (Maximum Likelihood Estimator) -- but the whole
  distribution provides more information.
\end{itemize}

This article is less badly written than the
preceeding. They consider the variable selection
problem in the linear regression of a stock return
(or the returns of $N$ stocks) with respect to
several economic variables and several benchmark
returns. The bayesian approach (with diffuse
priors) gives a probability for each of these
models. The interesting point is not (as the
authors claim) the ability to choose the best
model, but the ability to detect \emph{model
  uncertainty}: indeed, the probability of the
best model can be pretty low. I can see several
causes to that model uncertainty:
\begin{itemize}
\item Some of the models are \emph{very} similar
  or, at least, give similar predictions, and we
  do not have enough data to distinguish between
  them (this is the theoretical foundation of
  Bayesian Model Averaging);
\item The best models are different but wrong, by
  the same amount (this is reality).
\end{itemize}

The article also details the 15 factors used in
the study.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Thick Modeling}\\
  C.W.J. Granger and Y. Jeon
\end{book}

Extremely confusing article whose authors may not
be as competent as they ought to be:
\begin{itemize}
\item In their ``portfolio example'', they do not
  say where the data is, where the model is, which
  parameters are estimated. Actually, they
  consider several stocks, each known by the time
  series of its returns (this is the data). They
  assume that the returns are normal and
  independent and that their covariance structure
  is simple and can be described by three
  parameters (this is the model). They also define
  the quantity of interest: the composition of the
  optimal portfolio one can build with these
  stocks.
  
  But their confusion goes further: this is not
  Bayesian Model Averaging (we would need
  \emph{several} models) but a classical bayesian
  method: we have an a priori distribution of the
  model parameters, we use the data to compute
  their a posteriori distribution and then the a
  posteriori distribution of the quantity of
  interest.
\item They confuse ``noise'' (what appears in a
  model) and ``residuals'' (what appears at the
  end of a regression -- the residuals are an
  \emph{estimation} of the noise.
\item They seem to think that Maximum Likelihood
  can be used to compare models: you only use it
  when facing a \emph{single} model (or for tests,
  with \emph{nested} models).
\item They suggest to perform Bayesian Model
  averaging with an equally-weighted trimmed-mean,
  as if they had never heard of MCMC.
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{FlexMix: a general framework\\ for finite 
    mixture models\\ and latent class regression in R}\\
  F. Leisch (JSS 2004)
\end{book}

The EM (Expectation Maximization) algorithm is
used to fit models with missing data, such as
finite mixture models (for instance \verb+z~x*y+,
where only $x$ and $y$ are known and $z$ is
qualitative). It works as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item Choose an a priori estimate of the model parameters;
\item (E-step) Replace themissing values by their
  expected values, assuming that the model
  parameters are correct;
\item (M-step) Estimate the parameters by MLE
  (Maximum Likelihood Estimation), assuming that
  the missing values have the value inferred at
  the previous step;
\item (E-step) ...
\item (M-step) ...
\item etc.
\end{itemize}

There are already some R packages to do that:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{mclust} (for mixtures of gaussians);
\item \texttt{fpc} (for mixtures of regressions);
\item \texttt{mmlcr} (for mixtures of latent-class regression).
\end{itemize}
FlexMix encompasses the general linear models
(Poisson regression, etc.) and is more
configurable : you can provide your own model and
write your own M-step.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{iPlots}\\
  S. Urbanek and M. Theus (DSC 2003)
\end{book}

Interactive graphics should provide \emph{colour
  brushing} and \emph{linked highlighting}: the
authors provide a framework to do so, for simple
graphics (histograms, barcharts, scatterplots),
implemented with SJava (but the user need not know
anything about Java). It might be easier to use
than GGobi (and prettier), but it lacks its
multidimensional capabilities.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Synergy, issue 3, 2000}
\end{book}

The Coil Contest is the European equivalent of the
KDD Contest (but the KDD Contest is still alive).
The conclusions of that issue are that for
real-world problems, they suggest to try different
algorithms (``look beyond your pet algorithm'') and to
use a rigorous methodology (you can afford a
crappy algorithm, but not a crappy methodology).

This issue also contains an article about
wavelets, focusing on the combination of wavelets
and ``soft'' algorithms (fuzzy logic, neural
networks, genetic algorithms).

Do not expect to learn anything reading this -- they
provide interesting real-life examples, though --
even one with high-frequency financial data.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Using Perl for Statistics: Data Processing and Statistical Computing}\\
  G. Baiocchi 
  (JSS, 2004)
\end{book}

This long and detailed article presents Perl as a
glue language for statistics, navigating between
remote data (web), statistical libraries (R,
various Perl modules) and presentation tools
(\LaTeX), performing tasks such as data retrieval,
transformation, validation and recoding. They
suggest using Windows (why?) and Emacs (or SciTE,
which also exists under Windows).

They list and assess the quality of various Perl
statistical modules: reproductibility,
reliability, benchmarking -- the main problem is
the Random Number Generator (RNG): avoid the
default one and prefer MT or TT800.

They also mention matrix handling modules, PDL (a
Perl package allowing one to manipulate matrices
as easily as if they were scalars -- like Matlab
or R --, whose large graphical capabilities are
not mentionned) and even Win32::OLE (to talk to
the R DCOM server).

As many people claim to be a Perl guru after just
a few hours, as the articles covers a lot of
subjects, one could have feared that the author
does not master some of them -- this is not the
case. It seems to be a good presentation of Perl,
aimed at statisticians.


\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Kernlab -- an S4 package for kernel methods in R}\\
  A. Karatzoglou et al. (JSS, 2004)
\end{book}

There are three R packages dealing with kernel
methods: \texttt{e1071} (which uses libsvm),
\texttt{klaR} (which uses SVMlight) and
\texttt{kernlab}, with which you can add new
kernels in R (with no need to dive into C or C++
code) and new algorithms (though many are already
implemented).

The article starts by recalling the notion of S4
classes (with S3 objects, the class is merely an
attribute, only used for method dispatching; with
S4 classes, all objects of a given class have
the same ``slots'' and methods are attached to the
class -- but if you just want to \emph{use} S4
classes, the main difference is that \verb+$+
became \verb+@+) and namespaces.  It then lists
the kernels and algorithms:
\begin{itemize}
\item SVM regression, classification (with 2 or
  more classes, but also with just one class:
  ``novelty detection'' or ``outlier detection'');
\item \emph{Relevance Vector Machine} (a bayesian, weighted SVM regression);
\item ``\emph{Gaussian processes}'' (?);
\item A Google-type ranking algorithm based on a
  network constructed with the kernel distance;
\item kernel PCA, kernel CCA, clustering based on a kernel PCA. 
\end{itemize}
The package also provides an \emph{interior point
  quadratic optimizer} (most optimization
algorithms (e.g., the \emph{simplex} algorithm)
examine the boundary of a convex subset of a
high-dimensional space, because we know that the
solution is on this boundary; but in high
dimension, the boundary is huge and some
algorithms are trying to take a ``shortcut''
\emph{through} the convex: they are called
``interior point'' algorithms).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wavelets in Statistics: a review}\\
  A. Antoniadis (1997)
\end{book}

This document is rather old and assumes that the
reader is familiar with the theory behind wavelets
(Besov spaces, etc.). Yet, the conclusion was
enlightening, presenting ``recent or future''
developments (with no details):
\begin{itemize}
\item Non-paramnetric tests;
\item Regression with irregularly spaced points
  (``\emph{lifting scheme}'': one can define
  wavelets adapted to the sample points), non gaussian noise;
\item Jump detection;
\item \emph{Wavelet packets}.
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{State Space Modeling in Macroeconomics and Finance 
    using S+FinMetrics} \\
  E. Zivot at al. (2002)
\end{book}

This is the documentation of the SsfPack part of
S+FinMetrics, containing a lot of examples
(Recursive Least Squares (RLS), CUSUM test (the
cumulative sum of the residuals should have zero
mean and a variance proportional to time; we can
plot those cumulative sums of residuals and the
confidence intervals), ARMA), with code -- but it
is rather useless if you do not have S-Plus and
S+FinMetrics.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Searching for alpha, the importance of revenues and cash flow estimate revisions in stock selection}\\
  J. Gadaut et al. (Citigroup, 2004)
\end{book}

Analysts can provide us with a few estimations for each stock:
\begin{itemize}
\item estimated earnings (very commonly used);
\item estimated revenue;
\item estimated cash flow.
\end{itemize}
The changes in those estimates (``revisions'') can help us select
outperforming stocks. Actually, it suffices to look at the
\emph{breadth}, \ie, the number of revisions. This strategy works
better with the cash flow, continental Europe and low-capitalization
values, but is is sector-dependant.

New accounting standards changes are likely to increase the earnings
estimates volatility and hence its usefulness; the other estimates may
help.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Option Pricing under Model and Parameter Uncertainty using Predictive Densities}\\
  F.O. Bunnin et al. (2000)
\end{book}

This article (which I have not read) gives another example of Bayesian
Model Averaging (BAM).

The theoretical price of an option is the expectation of its
discounted payoff. To perform this computation, we assume that:
\begin{itemize}
\item We know the model describing the price of the underlying
  (usually the Black--Scholes model, which is known to be wrong);
\item We know that the model parameters are constant (this is
  precisely the reason why the Black--Scholes model is wrong: the
  parameters are not constant (``clustered volatility''));
\item We know those parameters (while different estimations of the
  volatility, such as the implied or the historical volatility, give
  different results).
\end{itemize}
With BAM, we can simultaneously consider several models (with constant
or stochastic volatility) and take into account the uncertainty on the
parameters; as a result, we get a distribution of theoretical prices. 

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bayesian Model Averaging: A Tutorial}\\
  J.A. Hoeting et al. (1999)
\end{book}

This article is a more comprehensive, detailed presentation of
Bayesian Model Averaging (BMA) and its algorithms.

Here are a few means of averaging over the set of all models:
\begin{itemize}
\item Do not consider all the models but just the ``best'' and those
  close to it (a slightly different variable selection, a different
  error structure, etc.).
\item \emph{Occam's window} suggests not to consider all the models
  but only the parsimonious and data-supported ones, \ie, the models
  $M \in \mathscr M$ such that 
  \begin{itemize}
  \item $\displaystyle
    \pr( M | \text{Obs} ) > (1-\epsilon) \max\limits_{N\in\mathscr M}
    \pr( N | \text{Obs} )$
  \item there is no smaller model $N \subsetstr M$ that better fits
    the data, \ie, such that $\pr( N | \text{Obs} ) >
    \pr(M|\text{Obs})$.
  \end{itemize}
  Those models are enumerated by a Branch and Bound (B\&B) algorithm
  (for small, simple problems).
\item For larger problems, one can use the Markov Chain Monte Carlo
  (MCMC) algorithm to sample from the set of all models -- this is
  called \emph{Markov Chain Monte Carlo Model Composition} (MCMCMC or
  MC$^3$).
\item Orthogonalized model mixing (?).
\end{itemize}

The integrals (the posterior probability of the models) can be computed by 
\begin{itemize}
\item explicit formulas for some (simple) classes of models;
\item approximations (Laplace method);
\item replacing $\pr(y | M,\text{Obs})$ by 
  $\pr(y | M,\hat\theta_{\text{MLE}}, \text{Obs})$.
\end{itemize}
One may remark that these ideas allow us to compare completely
unrelated models. If a model is embedded in the other, we can use a
Likelihood Ratio (LR) test or the AIC, if not we can use the BIC
approximation.  Here, we have other approximations (actually, the
Laplace approximation often coincides with the BIC one).

For linear regression, the choice of a model comprises:
\begin{itemize}
\item choice of variables;
\item transformations: Box--Cox, ACE (Alternating Conditionnal
  Expectation: a family of non parametric transformations), broken
  lines (ACE often yields broken lines);
\item outliers, using a model of the form $y = b x + \epsilon$ where
  $\epsilon$ is a mixture of $N(0,\sigma^2)$ with probability $1-p$
  and $N(0,K^2\sigma^2)$ with probability $p$, with $p$ the
  proportion of outliers and $K\gg1$ the variance-inflation parameter.
\end{itemize}
They also detail survival analysis and graphical models. 

But how do we fix the prior model probabilities? One can set 
$$
\pr(M) = \prod_j \pi^{\delta_j} (1-\pi)^{\delta_j} 
$$
where $\pi$ is the probability of including a variable (with
$\pi<\tfrac12$, we put a penalty on larger models) and $\delta \in
\{0,1\}$ denotes the presence or absence of variable $j$ in model $M$.
Another solution is to ask an expert of the domain to forge
``imaginary data''.

There are other approaches to model averaging:
\begin{itemize}
\item Bootstrap both the data and the model selection (the results are
  poorer than those of BMA);
\item ``Minimax Multiple Shrinkage Stein Estimator''~(?);
\item Bagging;
\item Stacking (?);
\item Boosting (weighted bagging).
\end{itemize}

The article concludes with two very detailed real-life examples and is
followed by a few comments.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Methodology for Bayesian Model Averaging: an Update}\\
  J.A. Hoeting (2002)
\end{book}

When performing a regression (or any other statistical analysis), we
are confronted to the choice of a model (e.g., the choice of a few
variables to use as predictive variables, among dozens of variables).
Oftentimes, the data do not suggest a \emph{single} best model. To
overcome that \emph{model uncertainty}, one can do the computation
(e.g., we may want to compute a forecast) for each model, compute the
``probability of each model given the data'', and average over the
models.

More formally, let
\begin{align*}
  M \in \mathscr M : & \  \text{the models} \\
  \text{Obs} : & \  \text{the observed data} \\
  y : & \  \text{the parameter to be estimated}, \\
  & \  \text{or the variable to be predicted} \\
  \pr(M) : & \  \text{prior probability of model }M \\
  \pr(\theta | M) : & \  \text{prior distribution of parameter }
  \theta
  \text{ in model }M
\end{align*}
\begin{align}
  \pr( y | \text{Obs} ) &= \sum_{M \in \mathscr M} 
  \pr(y | \text{Obs}, M) \cdot \pr(M | \text{Obs}) \\
  \pr(M | \text{Obs} &= \dfrac{
    \pr(\text{Obs} | M) \cdot \pr(M)
  }{
    \displaystyle
    \sum_{N \in \mathscr M} 
    \pr(\text{Obs} | N) \cdot \pr(N)
  } \\
  \pr(\text{Obs} | M) &= \int_{\theta \in \Theta}
  \pr( \text{Obs} | M, \theta)  \, d\theta.
\end{align}

To use such an approach, one has to overcome some problems:
\begin{itemize}
\item How to choose the prior probabilities?
\item How to compute the integrals?
\item How to deal with the large number of models?
\end{itemize}
For the first point, I have no clue; for the second, one uses ad hoc,
model-dependent approximations; for the third, one uses Monte Carlo
Markov Chains (MCMC) to sample in the set of models.

BMA can be applied, for instance, to the following sets of models:
\begin{itemize}
\item Linear models, with a different choice of variables, a
  different choice of transformations, a different selection of outliers;
\item Non-parametric regression models;
\item Spacial models (but I do not know anything about spacial models...);
\item Generalized Linear Models (GLM);
\item Graphical models;
\item Classification trees (this is very similar to \emph{bagging});
\item Survival analysis.
\end{itemize}

The article mentions a few S-Plus packages to perform BMA (apparently,
they do not work under R in a straightforward way (they use
undocumented internal S-Plus functions) and they have not been ported to
R); a more comprehensive list is on the BMA home page,
\url{http://www.research.att.com/~volinsky/bma.html}.

To sum up, the advantages of BMA are:
\begin{itemize}
\item Takes model uncertainty into account (if there is no model
  uncertainty, it is useless; it is more useful when there are many
  variables, many useless variables and multicolinearity problems);
\item Better forecasts;
\item Less over-optimistic confidence intervals (more classical model
  selection precedures use the same data twice: first to select the
  model, then to estimate the quantities of interest).
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Time Series in Finance:\\ the array database approach}\\
  D. Shasha
  %\url{http://www.cs.nyu.edu/shasha/papers/jagtalk.html}
\end{book}

A time series database should have the following desireable properties: 
\begin{itemize}
\item Handling of regular time series, with missing values;
\item Converting a time series to a given frequency, taking its
  \emph{type} into account (for \emph{level} time series, just take
  the latest value; for \emph{flow} time series, take the sum since
  the last timestamp), in both directions (lower frequency: sampling,
  higher frequency: interpolation) -- this also includes turning an
  irregular tinme series into a regular one;
\item Perform basic computations (cummulative sum, moving average,
  correlation, regression, acf, etc.) and user-defined ones;
\item Sequences are first-class objects, \ie, you can play with them
  as easily as if they were numbers;
\item \emph{Bitemporality}: each event has two timestamps, the time at
  which it is valid and the time at which it was entered into the
  database; in other words, the databse keeps track of how the errors
  were corrected. Thus, when backtesting (\ie, when doing tests with
  historical data), we can use either the ``historical data we should
  have had at the time, had the database been error-free and
  up-to-date'' or the ``historical data we actually had at the time,
  including the errors and the delays'' -- the former could in fact
  assume that we had the information before everyone else, which is
  not very realistic...
\end{itemize}

They detail several systems: FAME (Forecasting, Analysis and
ModEling), S-Plus, SAS and KSQL (K is an array language -- it makes me
think of stored procedures in R under PostgreSQL).

The document also presents many concrete and detailed use cases; it
ends with a large (but perhaps a bit outdated) bibliography on
sequence similarity, subsequence matching, pattern matching.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Evanesce Implementation in S-Plus FinMetrics Module}\\
  Insightful Corp., 2002
\end{book}

Copulas are a mean of describing the dependance relation between two
random variables $(X,Y)$ abstracting the peculiarities of the
distribution of $X$ and $Y$ -- we only focus on the dependance
relation. The idea is to transform $X$ and $Y$ so that their
distribution be uniform in $[0,1]$. A \emph{copula} is the joint
distribution of the transformed variables.

This document lists more than a dozen copulas. Let us mention the
simplest ones.
\begin{itemize}
\item Gaussian copulas (obtained when $(X,Y)$ is gaussian, with
  correlation $\rho$);
\item Mixture-of-gaussians copulas;
\item Extreme value copulas: this is a \emph{max-stable} family of
  copula; they verify 
  $$\forall u,v \quad C(u^t,v^t)=C(u,v)^t;$$
  the Gumbel copula
  $$ 
  C(u,v) = \exp - \left(
    (-\log u)^\delta +
    (-\log v)^\delta
  \right)^{1/\delta}
  $$
  is one of them;
\item Archimedean copulas, 
  $$
  C(u,v) = \phi^{-1} \left(
    \phi(u) + \phi(v)
  \right)
  $$
  for some function $\phi$ -- this reduces the study to functions
  of a simgle variable. The Gumbel copula is archimedean.
\end{itemize}

They only present the notion of copulas for \emph{pairs} of variables:
actually, it can be generalized in higher dimensions. This is
especially easy for families of copulas defined by a function of a
single variable such as the archimedean copulas:
$$ 
C(u_1,\dots,u_n) = \phi^{-1}\left(
  \phi(u_1) + \cdots + \phi(u_n)
\right).
$$

You might also want to read \emph{Taken to the limit: simple and not
  so simple loan loss distribution}, P.G. Sch\"onbucher,
\url{http://www.wilmott.com/pdfs/040404_schon.pdf}.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Orla, a data flow programming system\\
    for very large time series}\\
  G. Zumbach and A. Trapletti\\
  (Olsen \& Associates)
\end{book}

Flow programming languages (see \emph{Advanced Programming Language
  Design}, by R. A. Finkel, for a description of the main families of
programming languages and their characteristic features) are quite
rare outside eletronics. The authors have developped a framework based
on the flow programming paradigm to process time series.  Their data
is stored in a ``time series data base'' (aka \emph{temporal
  database}) that can be queried in a very simple way, close to: 
\begin{verbatim}
  SELECT FX(USD,*)
  BETWEEN 2004-01-01 AND now;
\end{verbatim}
(actually, it does not look like SQL, but it is as straightforward --
they call that syntax SQDADL (Sequencial Data Description Language).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Iterative Incremental Clustering\\
    of Time Series}\\
  J. Lin et al.
\end{book}

Clustering algorithms such as $k$-means (that tries to finsd spherical
clusters) or EM (that tries to find elliptical clusters -- more
precisely, it is a model-based clustering algorithm that assumes the
data is a mixture of (say) gaussians -- in R, it is implemented in the
\texttt{mclust} module) require a \emph{good} first estimate of the
cluster centers, that is progressively refined.

Instead of considering the whole data set right away (it may have a
lot of variables), one may perform a clustering on the first
coefficients of the Haar wavelet transform; then on the coefficients
of levels 1 and 2, using the preceding results as initial estimates;
etc.

The results are better that with a ``batch'' algorithm (we are less
likely to find in a local extremum) -- and it is faster.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{To vary or not to vary?\\
  Weights, breaks, factors and sampling frequencies
  in predictive return models}\\
  R. Masih (Goldman Sachs, 2004)
\end{book}

Factors vary with time. They suggest two ways of accomodating those
changes.

First, you can take the same factor at several
frequencies (daily, weekly, monthly) and use all
of them in the same regression (MIDAS: MIxed DAta
Sampling).

Second, you can take several simple models (linear regression with a
simgle predictive variable, for all the possible variables) and
combine the models, with ``bayesian methods'' (they are very vague
about those).

They also speak of ``breaks'', but I know neither how they find them
nor how they use them.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Quantitative Portfolio Strategy\\ 
    Peculiarity of the Japanese Stock Market}\\
  T. Suwabe (Goldman Sachs, 2004)
\end{book}

\emph{Investor sentiment}, defined as 
$$
\text{NIS} = \dfrac{ 
  \text{Buy volume} - \text{Sell volume} 
}{
  \text{Total volume}
},
$$
estimated with intraday data, is correlated with
future return.

Incidentally, they also define the notion of ``factor return'' (the
coefficients of a factor, in a regression, when you do the same
regression at various times); remark that they are autocorrelated in
Europe and the US but not in Japan or in Asia; remark that Japanese
returns have a negative autocorrelation that tends quickly to zero
while the NIS has positive autocorrelation and tends slowly to zero.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Wavelets, Approximation and\\
    Statistical Applications}\\
  W. H\"ardle et al., 1997
\end{book}

The following figure represents a wavelet transform: the curve in the
first plot is the signal to be described; the rectangles (or vertical
segments) represent the coefficients; the lower left part tells us
that in the left part of the interval, high frequencies dominate; the
top right part tells us that in the right part of the interval, low
frequencies dominate.

\begin{center}
<<WASAH1,results="hide",echo=FALSE>>=
N <- 1024
k <- 6
x <- ( (1:N) - N/2 ) * 2 * pi * k / N
y <- ifelse( x>0, sin(x), sin(3*x) )
plot(y, type='l')
@ 

<<WASAH2,results="hide",echo=FALSE>>=
library(wavethresh)
y <- ifelse( x>0, sin(x), sin(10*x) )
plot(wd(y), main="")
@ 
\end{center}

This is very similar to the decomposition of periodic functions into
Fourier series, but we have one more dimension: the frequency spectrum
is replace by a frequency-location plot (an other advantage is that
there is almost no Gibbs effect -- very convenient when the signals
are not periodic or stationnary...)

The general idea is the following: find an orthonormal basis of
$L^2$ whose elements can be interpreted in terms of ``location'' and
``frequency''. For example (this is called the Haar basis):
\setkeys{Gin}{width=2cm,height=2cm}
<<WASAH3,echo=F,out.width="2cm">>=
  father <- function (x) { ifelse( x<0 | x>1, 0, 1 ) }
  mother <- function (x) { ifelse( x<0 | x>1, 0, ifelse(x<.5, 1, -1)) }
  jk <- function (f,x,j,k) { f(2^j * x - k) }
@
$$
  \begin{array}{rccc}
    V_0 &
\vcenter{\hbox{%
<<WASAH4,echo=FALSE,fig.width=1,fig.height=1,out.width="2cm">>=
op <- par(mar=c(0,0,0,0))
curve(jk(father,x,0,0),
  xlim=c(-.2,3.2), ylim=c(-1,1), xlab="", ylab="", axes=F, lwd=3, col="blue")
abline(h=0,lty=3); abline(v=0:4,lty=3)
par(op)
@    
}}
&
\vcenter{\hbox{%
