\setkeys{Gin}{width=6cm,height=6cm}
$$

This gives a decomposition 
$$
L^2 = V_0 \oplus \bigoplus_j W_j 
$$
where $V_0$ is generated by the father wavelet $\phi$ and its
translations and the $W_j$ are generated by the mother wavelet $\psi$,
its translations and rescalings.
\setkeys{Gin}{width=1cm,height=1cm}%
\begin{align*}
  \phi &= 
\vcenter{\hbox{%
<<WASAH16,echo=FALSE,width=.5,height=.5,out.width="1cm">>=
op <- par(mar=c(0,0,0,0))
curve(jk(father,x,0,1),
 xlim=c(-.2,3.2), 
 ylim=c(-1,1),
 xlab="", ylab="", axes=F, lwd=3, col="blue")
abline(h=0,lty=3)
#abline(v=0:4,lty=3)
par(op)
@    
}}
  && \text{father wavelet} \\
  \psi &=
\vcenter{\hbox{%
<<WASAH17,echo=FALSE,width=.5,height=.5,out.width="1cm">>=
op <- par(mar=c(0,0,0,0))
curve(jk(mother,x,0,1),
 xlim=c(-.2,3.2), 
 ylim=c(-1,1), 
 xlab="", ylab="", axes=F, lwd=3, col="blue")
abline(h=0,lty=3)
#abline(v=0:4,lty=3)
par(op)
@
}}
  && \text{mother wavelet} \\
\end{align*}%
\setkeys{Gin}{width=6cm,height=6cm}%
The choice of the basis depends on the applications: will you be
dealing with smooth ($\mathscr{C}^\infty$) functions? continuous but
non-differentiable functions, with a fractal nature? functions with
jumps? The convergence speed of the Wavelet decomposition will depend
on the adequacy of the basis and the data studied.

More technically, the construction of a basis goes as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Choose a father wavelet $\phi$.
\item Set $\phi_jk(x) = 2^{j/2} \phi(2^j x - k )$, for $j \in \NN$ and
  $k \in \ZZ$ -- this is $f$ translated by $k$ and shrinked $j$ times.
\item Set $V_j = \vect\left\{
    \phi_{jk}, k \in \ZZ
  \right\}$ and check that:
\item $(\phi_{0k})_{k \in \ZZ}$ is an orthonormal system (a Hilbert
  basis of $V_0$;
\item $\forall j \in \ZZ \qquad V_j \subset V_{j+1}$;
\item $\overline{ \bigcup _{j \geq 0} V_j } = L^2$.
\item Then, set 
  $W_j = V_{j+1} \ominus V_j$ so that 
  $L^2 = V_0 \oplus \bigoplus_{j\geq0} W_j$.

  We then want to find a mother wavelet 
  $\psi \in W_0$ such that if we set 
\item $\psi_{jk}(x) = 2^{j/2} \psi( 2^j x - k)$, 
\item then 
  $(\psi_{jk})_{k \in \ZZ}$ is a basis of $W_j$.
\item Then (finally), we have a basis of $L^2$:
  $$
  \begin{array}{ccccccc}
    \cdots & \phi_{0,-2} & \phi_{0,-1} & \phi_{0,0} & \phi_{0,1} & \phi_{0,2} & \cdots \\
    \cdots & \phi_{1,-2} & \phi_{1,-1} & \phi_{1,0} & \phi_{1,1} & \phi_{1,2} & \cdots \\
    \cdots & \phi_{2,-2} & \phi_{2,-1} & \phi_{2,0} & \phi_{2,1} & \phi_{2,2} & \cdots \\
    &&&\vdots&&& \\
  \end{array}
  $$
\end{enumerate}

When conditions 4, 5 and 6 are satisfied, we say that we have found a
MultiResolution Analysis (MRA).

For the forgetful reader, they recall some basic facts about Fourier
Analysis -- if we just want to \emph{use} wavelets, we do not need it:
it is used to check that the abovementioned properties are satisfied,
when you build your own wavelet basis.

The book then gives a first construction of wavelet bases, from a
Riesz basis (it is not that hard, but we do not really need to know
what it is): we get the Battle--Lemari\'e father wavelets.
  
Apart from the first one (the Haar father wavelet), they are not
compactly supported.

The book then goes on to the Daubechies's construction of wavelet
bases (based on Fourier analysis). This time:
\begin{itemize}
\item father and mother wavelets have a compact support;
\item the first moment of the father wavelet are vanishing.
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
<<WASAH18,echo=F>>=
N <- 1024
k <- 6
x <- ( (1:N) - N/2 ) * 2 * pi * k / N
y <- ifelse( x>0, sin(x), sin(3*x) )
r <- wd(y)
#draw(r, col="blue", lwd=3, main="") # Bug...
  filter <- r$filter
  draw.default(
    filter.number = filter$filter.number, 
    family = filter$family 
    #$
  )
abline(h=0, lty=3)
@ 
\end{center}

Let us list the main (families of) wavelet bases mentionned in the
book:
\begin{itemize}
\item Haar;
\item Battle--Lemari\'e;
\item Daubechies;
\item Coiflets (the father wavelet has vanishing moments);
\item Symlets (more symetric than the Daubechies wavelets).
\end{itemize}

(There are two technical chapters on Sobolev and Besov spaces, which I
have not read.)

Let us now mention some statistical applications of wavelets:
\begin{itemize}
\item Approximation of possibly irregular functions and surfaces
  (there is a reduced Gibbs phenomenon, but you can circumvent it by
  using \emph{translation-invariant wavelets}.)
\item Smoothing.
\item Density estimation: just try to estimate the density as a linear
  combination of wavelets, then remove/threshold the wavelets whose
  coefficients are too small (usually, the threshold is chosen as
  $0.4$ to $0.8$ times the maximum coefficient; we also use
  \emph{block thresholding}: we do not remove simgle coefficients but
  whole blocks of coefficients). For instance, density estimation of
  financial returns: we can clearly see the fat tails. Kerlel methods
  do not perform that well, unless they use adaptive bandwidth.
\item Regression: this is very similar, we try to find a function $f$
  (linear combination of wavelets, we threshold the smaller
  coefficients) such that
  $$
  \forall i \qquad y_i = f(x_i) + \text{noise}. $$
  If $x_i = i/n$
  with $n=2^k$ for some $k \in \ZZ$, it works out of the box
  otherwise, you have to scale and bin the data.
\item Gaussian White Noise Estimation: find a function $f$ such that 
  $$ dY(t) = f(t) \, dt + \epsilon dW(t) \qquad t \in [0,1]. $$
\item Jump detection.
\item Time series.
\item Diffusion Models (?).
\item Image compression, indexing, reconstruction, etc.
\end{itemize}

Problems: 
\begin{itemize}
\item The authors do not seem to know the symbols $\forall$ and
  $\exists$ -- they systematically omit them, hoping that the reader
  will pick the right one;
\item The book is rather old and therefore does not mention recent
  developments -- it is good as an introduction to the subject, but
  should be complemented by other documents.
\end{itemize}

For more about wavelets, check
\url{http://www.wavelet.org/} and its tutorials.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Computational Methods for Time Series}\\
  G. Kitagawa, T. Higuchi, S. Sato
\end{book}

Linear State Space Models (SSM) are used to model non-stationnary time
series; but when the series have jumps, outliers or involve
non-gaussian distributions, we can turn to \emph{non-linear state
  space models}:
\begin{align*}
  x_n & \sim Q_n(\cdot | x_{n-1} ) \\
  y_n & \sim R_n(\cdot | x_n ).
\end{align*}
The extended Kalman filter used in this context is only an
approximation (a Taylor series, in fact) and it still assumes that the
distributions are gaussian. If we do not want this approximation or if
our distributions are really non gaussian, we can use the exact
formulas:
\begin{align*}
  p( x_n | y_{n-1} ) &= \int p(x_n | x_{n-1}) p(x_{n-1}|y_{n-1}) \, d x_{n-1} \\
  p( x_n | y_n ) &= \dfrac{
    p(y_n | x_n) p(x_n | y_{n-1}) 
  }{
    \int p(y_n | x_n) p(x_n | y_{n-1})
  }.
\end{align*}

We have integrals when we go from $n$ to $n+1$, but actually, we want
to go from 1 to $N+1$ -- we have integrals of integrals of... of
integrals: all in all, we have to integrate over a high-dimensional
space. We resort to \emph{Monte Carlo integration}.  This is called a
\emph{Particle Kalman Filter} (the idea is similar to that of using
Monte Carlo simulations to price exotic options).

We usually estimate the model parameters by Maximum Likelihood (MLE),
but this assumes that those parameters are constant -- often, this is
not a reasonable assumption. To solve this problem, we can augment the
model by adding the parameters to the list of hidden variables: this
is called a \emph{Self-Organizing SSM}.

The articles also explains how the computations can be parallelized,
how the particles can be exchanged between the different runs
(\emph{cross-over}).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Pattern Recognition of Time Series\\Using Wavelets}\\
  E. A. Maharaj
\end{book}

The wavelets coefficients of time series can be used to estimate the
``difference'' (the distance) between two time series. One can devise
a test to check if the coefficients of two series are significantly
different: compute 
$$
\dfrac{ 
  ( \text{wavelet coefficients of the first series} )^2 
}{
  ( \text{coefficients of the other } )^2 
}
$$
and compare it with the distribution obtained by randomly swapping
the coefficients of the two series (they did the test with 256 and
1024 coefficients). One could use the resulting $p$-value to compute a
distance between time series.

Personnal remarks:
\begin{itemize}
\item One might use that to \emph{cluster} time series (into sectors,
  industries, countries, etc. -- the interpretation will be left to
  more economically-inclined people).
\item The coefficients may be similar but with delays (one could
  replace the distance between $x$ and $y$ by the minimum distance
  between delayed versions of $x$ and delayed versions of $y$).
\item Those delays can vary with time (a few days in normal market
  situations, much less in a crash -- here, I should tell more about
  time warp distance and dynamic programming -- the idea is the
  following: compute a time-warp distance for each level of the
  wavelets decomposition, then combine these distances).
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Random and Changing Coefficient Models}\\
  G.C. Chow, Handook of Econometrics (1984), 
  volume 2, chapter 21, 
\end{book}
A survey article, with too many formulas to be enlightening.
A section presents various tests on those models (such as ``are the
coefficients random or constant?'').

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{The Kalman Foundations of\\
    Adaptive Least Squares}\\
  J.H. McCulloch, 2004
\end{book}
The model underlying a regression looks like
$$ y = \beta x + \text{noise}. $$
But in a financial context, the observations are ordered
$(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\dots,(x_n,y_n)$ and the coefficient $\beta$ may
(slowly) change with time. The model is then
\begin{align*}
  \beta &= \text{random walk} \\
  y_t &= \beta_t x_t + \text{noise}.
\end{align*}
This is a State Space Model, but a non-linear one.
\begin{align*}
  \beta_{t+1} &= \beta_t + \text{noise} && \text{(hidden)} \\
  x_t &= \text{noise}                   && \text{(observed)} \\
  y_t &= \beta_t x_t + \text{noise}     && \text{(observed)} \\
\end{align*}
It is called a \emph{Random Coefficient Model} (there are variants
where $\beta$ is stationnary) and the corresponding non-linear Kalman
filter is called \emph{Adaptive Least Squares} (ALS). This is a
generalization of the Recursive Least Squares alluded to above.

The article is full of formulas and thus not very readable.

Here are some simpler alternatives to ALS:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Local Regression}, \ie, for each point we do a weighted
  regression with a null weight for future observations (because we do
  not know them yet) and an exponentially decreasing weight for past
  observations; the difference with ALS is that we have to choose the
  rate of decrease of the weights. 
\item A broken-line regression (it sounds appealing, especially if we
  look at real data, but unfortunately it is not robust at all).
\end{itemize}

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{What Investors Can Learn\\
    from a Very Alternative Market}\\
  R.N. Kahn, Financial Analysts Journal 60\ 5, p.\,17--21.
\end{book}
The article stresses the ``importance of rigorous scientific analysis
to successful investing''.

``Investors (or baseball team managers) are irrational in systematic
and predictable ways:
\begin{itemize}
\item social interaction (herding);
\item heuristic simplification (generalization from personnal
  experience, extrapolating from recent events);
\item self-deception (attributing positive outcomes to one's skill and
  negative outcomes to bad luck).''
\end{itemize}
The low liquidity of the baseball market makes the resulting
inefficiencies easier to spot and exploit.

(These ideas are presented and developed in the book \emph{Moneyball},
by M. Lewis (2003).)

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Statistical Algorithms for Models\\
    in State Space using SsfPack 2.2}
  \\
  S.J. Koopman, N. Shephard, J.A. Doornik
  \\
  Econometrics Journal (1999) 2, p.~113--166
\end{book}
This article presents SsfPack, an Ox library for State Space Model
estimations (Ox is a C-like language for matrix and time-series
analysis, commercial, closed-source and Windows-only -- there is a
limited Linux version, available only to academic users). The SPlus
module ``FinMetrics'' uses SsfPack.

The article contains a lot of examples and has a distinctive bayesian
flavor. 

The first part presents various time series models that may be
expressed in state-space form: ARMA, structured model with seasonal
and \emph{cycle} components
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
  x_{t+1} \\ y_{t+1}
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
  \cos \theta & \sin \theta \\
  - \sin \theta & \cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
  x_t \\ y_t
\end{pmatrix}
+ \text{noise},
$$
regression models (\emph{recursive least squares} -- we want to find
$\beta$ such that $y_t = \beta x_t + \text{noise}$: take a first,
random, estimation $\hat \alpha_0$ of $\beta$, consider the first
observation $(x_1,y_1)$, refine the estimate $\hat \alpha_1$, consider
the second observation, etc.), non-parametric cubic spline models (not
read). 

The second part presents the implemented algorithms: simulation,
Kalman filter, smoother (in a filter, you only use the information
available up to time $t$ to predict the hidden variable at time $t$,
in a smoother, you use all the information, even that posterior to the
prediction), a posteriori sampler (I think -- it was not that clear),
likelihood computation
$$
- \dfrac{ n N }{ 2 }
\log( 2\pi )
- \dfrac12 \left(
  \log \va{F_t} + v_t' F_t^{-1} v_t
\right)
$$
(where $n$ is the number of observations, $N$ the number of observed
variables, $\va{\cdot}$ the determinant, $v_t$ the residuals, $F_t$
the variance of the residuals) -- there is also a ``concentrated or
profile log-likelihood''.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{``We're going to be a 90\,\% Linux shop by 2006''}\\
  Linux Journal 127, November 2004, p.~48-50
\end{book}
These words are from Morgan Stanley.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Scientific Visialization with PoVRay}\\
  Linux Journal 127, November 2004, p.~62-67
\end{book}
PoVRay is a ray tracer, \ie, a program to draw 3D scenes from a
textual description. It can draw isosurfaces, \ie, surfaces of the
form $f(x,y,z)=k$, where $f$ is specified by an equation or a binary
df3 file (8-bit by default, but a patch extends this to 32-bit data
and another patch to HDF files, that contain several functions). It is
being used for meteorogogical data.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
\emph{Maximum likelihood estimation of mean-reverting processes}\\
J.C.G. Franco
\end{book}
MLE of a mean-reverting Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process (OU is a continuous
analogue of an AR(1) time series, the MR version is used to model
exchange rates or the price of products with a fixed cost of
production) requires either maximizing a function of 3 variables or
solving a (non-linear) system of 3 equations. This article mixes these
two approaches and reduces the problem to that of maximizing a
function of a single variable.

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Swingtum -- A Computational Theory of Fractal Dynamic Swings
    and Physical Cycles of Stock Market in a Quantum Price-Time Space}\\
  H. Pan
\end{book}

The market reacts to 4 types of fluctuations:
\begin{itemize}
\item dynamic swings (they have a fractal nature, have multilevel
  trends, as Elliott waves for instance);
\item physical cycles (year, month, week);
\item abrupt momentum (news impact, chart patterns);
\item random walk (the rest, what we cannot predict);
\end{itemize}

Nothing really concrete:
\begin{itemize}
\item If the prices depend on several ``forces'', then the price
  variation is the ``sum'' of these ``forces'' (he takes into account
  a stock ``mass'' , \ie, inertia);
\item The prices are piecewise log-periodic, 
  $$
  p \sim \sin \log t, 
  $$
  \ie, the rise is steeper that the fall (or conversely) and
  the waves start and end at random instants;
\item Hilbert transforms are important (I have no idea why).
\item ``Multidimensionnal embedding'' (yes, these words are mentionned
  without any further explaination).
\end{itemize}

Yet, if we remove all the nonsense from this gibberish, the article
suggest to do exactly what we are doing: look at the data, all the
data, cluster it (empirically or not, your clustering may/should be
hierarchical) and try to find a model (using the rationnal and
irrational beliefs investors are actually using, because their actions
depend on that and the market responds to those actions).

\nextbook
\begin{book}
  \emph{Bubbles, Crashes and Intermittency\\ in Agent-Based Market Models}\\
  I. Giardina and J.-P. Bouchaud\\
  arXiv:cond-mat/0206222
\end{book}
A very readable article on the same subject. They present a simplified
model of financial markets:
\begin{itemize}
\item There are only two assets, a stock and a bond (risk-free asset).
\item Each agent has a predefined set of strategies. He looks at the
  last $m$ returns, compares them with the risk-free returns, obtains
  a history binary vector of length $m$; a strategy is a function
  $\{0,1\}^m \longrightarrow \{\text{buy},\ \text{sell}\}$ (chartists
  do that). The strategies are chosen at random with a bias, $P$
  (``polarization'') towards ``trend-following'' or ``contrarian''
  strategies.
\item There is also a ``do nothing'' strategy''.
\item If the prices become too high or too low, the agents revert to
  fundamental analysis.
\item If an agent decides to invest, he only invests a fraction $g$ of his assets. 
\item Each strategy is rated with the number of ``successes'' it would have yielded in the past (weighted moving average).
\item The prices depend on the number of actors willing to buy/sell and on the market stiffness $\lambda$.
\end{itemize}
The consequences:
\begin{itemize}
\item The system has three states: oscillating (bubbles and crashes), intermittent (irregular bubbles and crashes) and stable.
\item Only two parameters are important: polarization $P$ and $g/\lambda$.
\item There are multiscale fluctuations (even though the model is not
  multiscale).
\item There is volatility clustering and long-term dependance.
\item A Tobin tax of a few basis points would have no effect; A Tobin
  tax of a few percents have a stabilizing effect.
\end{itemize}

